if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-6593"]="Sir,\nNotwithstanding your constant refusal, when I have asked leave to prefix your name to this dedication, I must still insist on my right to desire your protection of this work.\n\nTo you, Sir, it is owing that this history was ever begun. It was by your desire that I first thought of such a composition. So many years have since past, that you may have, perhaps, forgotten this circumstance: but your desires are to me in the nature of commands; and the impression of them is never to be erased from my memory.\n\nAgain, Sir, without your assistance this history had never been completed. Be not startled at the assertion. I do not intend to draw on you the suspicion of being a romance writer. I mean no more than that I partly owe to you my existence during great part of the time which I have employed in composing it: another matter which it may be necessary to remind you of; since there are certain actions of which you are apt to be extremely forgetful; but of these I hope I shall always have a better memory than yourself.\n\nLastly, It is owing to you that the history appears what it now is. If there be in this work, as some have been pleased to say, a stronger picture of a truly benevolent mind than is to be found in any other, who that knows you, and a particular acquaintance of yours, will doubt whence that benevolence hath been copied? The world will not, I believe, make me the compliment of thinking I took it from myself. I care not: this they shall own, that the two persons from whom I have taken it, that is to say, two of the best and worthiest men in the world, are strongly and zealously my friends. I might be contented with this, and yet my vanity will add a third to the number; and him one of the greatest and noblest, not only in his rank, but in every public and private virtue. But here, whilst my gratitude for the princely benefactions of the Duke of Bedford bursts from my heart, you must forgive my reminding you that it was you who first recommended me to the notice of my benefactor.\n\nAnd what are your objections to the allowance of the honour which I have sollicited? Why, you have commended the book so warmly, that you should be ashamed of reading your name before the dedication. Indeed, sir, if the book itself doth not make you ashamed of your commendations, nothing that I can here write will, or ought. I am not to give up my right to your protection and patronage, because you have commended my book: for though I acknowledge so many obligations to you, I do not add this to the number; in which friendship, I am convinced, hath so little share: since that can neither biass your judgment, nor pervert your integrity. An enemy may at any time obtain your commendation by only deserving it; and the utmost which the faults of your friends can hope for, is your silence; or, perhaps, if too severely accused, your gentle palliation.\n\nIn short, sir, I suspect, that your dislike of public praise is your true objection to granting my request. I have observed that you have, in common with my two other friends, an unwillingness to hear the least mention of your own virtues; that, as a great poet says of one of you, (he might justly have said it of all three), you\n\nDo good by stealth, and blush to find it fame.\n\nIf men of this disposition are as careful to shun applause, as others are to escape censure, how just must be your apprehension of your character falling into my hands; since what would not a man have reason to dread, if attacked by an author who had received from him injuries equal to my obligations to you!\n\nAnd will not this dread of censure increase in proportion to the matter which a man is conscious of having afforded for it? If his whole life, for instance, should have been one continued subject of satire, he may well tremble when an incensed satirist takes him in hand. Now, sir, if we apply this to your modest aversion to panegyric, how reasonable will your fears of me appear!\n\nYet surely you might have gratified my ambition, from this single confidence, that I shall always prefer the indulgence of your inclinations to the satisfaction of my own. A very strong instance of which I shall give you in this address, in which I am determined to follow the example of all other dedicators, and will consider not what my patron really deserves to have written, but what he will be best pleased to read.\n\nWithout further preface then, I here present you with the labours of some years of my life. What merit these labours have is already known to yourself. If, from your favourable judgment, I have conceived some esteem for them, it cannot be imputed to vanity; since I should have agreed as implicitly to your opinion, had it been given in favour of any other man's production. Negatively, at least, I may be allowed to say, that had I been sensible of any great demerit in the work, you are the last person to whose protection I would have ventured to recommend it.\n\nFrom the name of my patron, indeed, I hope my reader will be convinced, at his very entrance on this work, that he will find in the whole course of it nothing prejudicial to the cause of religion and virtue, nothing inconsistent with the strictest rules of decency, nor which can offend even the chastest eye in the perusal. On the contrary, I declare, that to recommend goodness and innocence hath been my sincere endeavour in this history. This honest purpose you have been pleased to think I have attained: and to say the truth, it is likeliest to be attained in books of this kind; for an example is a kind of picture, in which virtue becomes, as it were, an object of sight, and strikes us with an idea of that loveliness, which Plato asserts there is in her naked charms.\n\nBesides displaying that beauty of virtue which may attract the admiration of mankind, I have attempted to engage a stronger motive to human action in her favour, by convincing men, that their true interest directs them to a pursuit of her. For this purpose I have shown that no acquisitions of guilt can compensate the loss of that solid inward comfort of mind, which is the sure companion of innocence and virtue; nor can in the least balance the evil of that horror and anxiety which, in their room, guilt introduces into our bosoms. And again, that as these acquisitions are in themselves generally worthless, so are the means to attain them not only base and infamous, but at best incertain, and always full of danger. Lastly, I have endeavoured strongly to inculcate, that virtue and innocence can scarce ever be injured but by indiscretion; and that it is this alone which often betrays them into the snares that deceit and villainy spread for them. A moral which I have the more industriously laboured, as the teaching it is, of all others, the likeliest to be attended with success; since, I believe, it is much easier to make good men wise, than to make bad men good.\n\nFor these purposes I have employed all the wit and humour of which I am master in the following history; wherein I have endeavoured to laugh mankind out of their favourite follies and vices. How far I have succeeded in this good attempt, I shall submit to the candid reader, with only two requests: First, that he will not expect to find perfection in this work; and Secondly, that he will excuse some parts of it, if they fall short of that little merit which I hope may appear in others.\n\nI will detain you, sir, no longer. Indeed I have run into a preface, while I professed to write a dedication. But how can it be otherwise? I dare not praise you; and the only means I know of to avoid it, when you are in my thoughts, are either to be entirely silent, or to turn my thoughts to some other subject.\n\nPardon, therefore, what I have said in this epistle, not only without your consent, but absolutely against it; and give me at least leave, in this public manner, to declare that I am, with the highest respect and gratitude,--\n\nSir,\nYour most obliged,\n\nObedient, humble servant,\n\n# BOOK I.\n\nCONTAINING AS MUCH OF THE BIRTH OF THE FOUNDLING AS IS NECESSARY OR PROPER TO ACQUAINT THE READER WITH IN THE BEGINNING OF THIS HISTORY. Chapter i.\nThe introduction to the work, or bill of fare to the feast.\n\nAn author ought to consider himself, not as a gentleman who gives a private or eleemosynary treat, but rather as one who keeps a public ordinary, at which all persons are welcome for their money. In the former case, it is well known that the entertainer provides what fare he pleases; and though this should be very indifferent, and utterly disagreeable to the taste of his company, they must not find any fault; nay, on the contrary, good breeding forces them outwardly to approve and to commend whatever is set before them. Now the contrary of this happens to the master of an ordinary. Men who pay for what they eat will insist on gratifying their palates, however nice and whimsical these may prove; and if everything is not agreeable to their taste, will challenge a right to censure, to abuse, and to d--n their dinner without controul.\nTo prevent, therefore, giving offence to their customers by any such disappointment, it hath been usual with the honest and well-meaning host to provide a bill of fare which all persons may peruse at their first entrance into the house; and having thence acquainted themselves with the entertainment which they may expect, may either stay and regale with what is provided for them, or may depart to some other ordinary better accommodated to their taste.\n\nAs we do not disdain to borrow wit or wisdom from any man who is capable of lending us either, we have condescended to take a hint from these honest victuallers, and shall prefix not only a general bill of fare to our whole entertainment, but shall likewise give the reader particular bills to every course which is to be served up in this and the ensuing volumes.\n\nThe provision, then, which we have here made is no other than Human Nature. Nor do I fear that my sensible reader, though most luxurious in his taste, will start, cavil, or be offended, because I have named but one article. The tortoise--as the alderman of Bristol, well learned in eating, knows by much experience--besides the delicious calipash and calipee, contains many different kinds of food; nor can the learned reader be ignorant, that in human nature, though here collected under one general name, is such prodigious variety, that a cook will have sooner gone through all the several species of animal and vegetable food in the world, than an author will be able to exhaust so extensive a subject.\n\nAn objection may perhaps be apprehended from the more delicate, that this dish is too common and vulgar; for what else is the subject of all the romances, novels, plays, and poems, with which the stalls abound? Many exquisite viands might be rejected by the epicure, if it was a sufficient cause for his contemning of them as common and vulgar, that something was to be found in the most paltry alleys under the same name. In reality, true nature is as difficult to be met with in authors, as the Bayonne ham, or Bologna sausage, is to be found in the shops.\n\nBut the whole, to continue the same metaphor, consists in the cookery of the author; for, as Mr Pope tells us--\n\n“True wit is nature to advantage drest; What oft was thought, but ne'er so well exprest.”\n\nThe same animal which hath the honour to have some part of his flesh eaten at the table of a duke, may perhaps be degraded in another part, and some of his limbs gibbeted, as it were, in the vilest stall in town. Where, then, lies the difference between the food of the nobleman and the porter, if both are at dinner on the same ox or calf, but in the seasoning, the dressing, the garnishing, and the setting forth? Hence the one provokes and incites the most languid appetite, and the other turns and palls that which is the sharpest and keenest.\n\nIn like manner, the excellence of the mental entertainment consists less in the subject than in the author's skill in well dressing it up. How pleased, therefore, will the reader be to find that we have, in the following work, adhered closely to one of the highest principles of the best cook which the present age, or perhaps that of Heliogabalus, hath produced. This great man, as is well known to all lovers of polite eating, begins at first by setting plain things before his hungry guests, rising afterwards by degrees as their stomachs may be supposed to decrease, to the very quintessence of sauce and spices. In like manner, we shall represent human nature at first to the keen appetite of our reader, in that more plain and simple manner in which it is found in the country, and shall hereafter hash and ragoo it with all the high French and Italian seasoning of affectation and vice which courts and cities afford. By these means, we doubt not but our reader may be rendered desirous to read on for ever, as the great person just above-mentioned is supposed to have made some persons eat.\n\nHaving premised thus much, we will now detain those who like our bill of fare no longer from their diet, and shall proceed directly to serve up the first course of our history for their entertainment.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nA short description of squire Allworthy, and a fuller account of Miss Bridget Allworthy, his sister.\n\nIn that part of the western division of this kingdom which is commonly called Somersetshire, there lately lived, and perhaps lives still, a gentleman whose name was Allworthy, and who might well be called the favourite of both nature and fortune; for both of these seem to have contended which should bless and enrich him most. In this contention, nature may seem to some to have come off victorious, as she bestowed on him many gifts, while fortune had only one gift in her power; but in pouring forth this, she was so very profuse, that others perhaps may think this single endowment to have been more than equivalent to all the various blessings which he enjoyed from nature. From the former of these, he derived an agreeable person, a sound constitution, a solid understanding, and a benevolent heart; by the latter, he was decreed to the inheritance of one of the largest estates in the county.\n\nThis gentleman had in his youth married a very worthy and beautiful woman, of whom he had been extremely fond: by her he had three children, all of whom died in their infancy. He had likewise had the misfortune of burying this beloved wife herself, about five years before the time in which this history chuses to set out. This loss, however great, he bore like a man of sense and constancy, though it must be confest he would often talk a little whimsically on this head; for he sometimes said he looked on himself as still married, and considered his wife as only gone a little before him, a journey which he should most certainly, sooner or later, take after her; and that he had not the least doubt of meeting her again in a place where he should never part with her more--sentiments for which his sense was arraigned by one part of his neighbours, his religion by a second, and his sincerity by a third.\n\nHe now lived, for the most part, retired in the country, with one sister, for whom he had a very tender affection. This lady was now somewhat past the age of thirty, an aera at which, in the opinion of the malicious, the title of old maid may with no impropriety be assumed. She was of that species of women whom you commend rather for good qualities than beauty, and who are generally called, by their own sex, very good sort of women--as good a sort of woman, madam, as you would wish to know. Indeed, she was so far from regretting want of beauty, that she never mentioned that perfection, if it can be called one, without contempt; and would often thank God she was not as handsome as Miss Such-a-one, whom perhaps beauty had led into errors which she might have otherwise avoided. Miss Bridget Allworthy (for that was the name of this lady) very rightly conceived the charms of person in a woman to be no better than snares for herself, as well as for others; and yet so discreet was she in her conduct, that her prudence was as much on the guard as if she had all the snares to apprehend which were ever laid for her whole sex. Indeed, I have observed, though it may seem unaccountable to the reader, that this guard of prudence, like the trained bands, is always readiest to go on duty where there is the least danger. It often basely and cowardly deserts those paragons for whom the men are all wishing, sighing, dying, and spreading every net in their power; and constantly attends at the heels of that higher order of women for whom the other sex have a more distant and awful respect, and whom (from despair, I suppose, of success) they never venture to attack.\n\nReader, I think proper, before we proceed any farther together, to acquaint thee that I intend to digress, through this whole history, as often as I see occasion, of which I am myself a better judge than any pitiful critic whatever; and here I must desire all those critics to mind their own business, and not to intermeddle with affairs or works which no ways concern them; for till they produce the authority by which they are constituted judges, I shall not plead to their jurisdiction.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nAn odd accident which befel Mr Allworthy at his return home. The decent behaviour of Mrs Deborah Wilkins, with some proper animadversions on bastards.\n\nI have told my reader, in the preceding chapter, that Mr Allworthy inherited a large fortune; that he had a good heart, and no family. Hence, doubtless, it will be concluded by many that he lived like an honest man, owed no one a shilling, took nothing but what was his own, kept a good house, entertained his neighbours with a hearty welcome at his table, and was charitable to the poor, i.e. to those who had rather beg than work, by giving them the offals from it; that he died immensely rich and built an hospital.\n\nAnd true it is that he did many of these things; but had he done nothing more I should have left him to have recorded his own merit on some fair freestone over the door of that hospital. Matters of a much more extraordinary kind are to be the subject of this history, or I should grossly mis-spend my time in writing so voluminous a work; and you, my sagacious friend, might with equal profit and pleasure travel through some pages which certain droll authors have been facetiously pleased to call The History of England.\n\nMr Allworthy had been absent a full quarter of a year in London, on some very particular business, though I know not what it was; but judge of its importance by its having detained him so long from home, whence he had not been absent a month at a time during the space of many years. He came to his house very late in the evening, and after a short supper with his sister, retired much fatigued to his chamber. Here, having spent some minutes on his knees--a custom which he never broke through on any account--he was preparing to step into bed, when, upon opening the cloathes, to his great surprize he beheld an infant, wrapt up in some coarse linen, in a sweet and profound sleep, between his sheets. He stood some time lost in astonishment at this sight; but, as good nature had always the ascendant in his mind, he soon began to be touched with sentiments of compassion for the little wretch before him. He then rang his bell, and ordered an elderly woman-servant to rise immediately, and come to him; and in the meantime was so eager in contemplating the beauty of innocence, appearing in those lively colours with which infancy and sleep always display it, that his thoughts were too much engaged to reflect that he was in his shirt when the matron came in. She had indeed given her master sufficient time to dress himself; for out of respect to him, and regard to decency, she had spent many minutes in adjusting her hair at the looking-glass, notwithstanding all the hurry in which she had been summoned by the servant, and though her master, for aught she knew, lay expiring in an apoplexy, or in some other fit.\n\nIt will not be wondered at that a creature who had so strict a regard to decency in her own person, should be shocked at the least deviation from it in another. She therefore no sooner opened the door, and saw her master standing by the bedside in his shirt, with a candle in his hand, than she started back in a most terrible fright, and might perhaps have swooned away, had he not now recollected his being undrest, and put an end to her terrors by desiring her to stay without the door till he had thrown some cloathes over his back, and was become incapable of shocking the pure eyes of Mrs Deborah Wilkins, who, though in the fifty-second year of her age, vowed she had never beheld a man without his coat. Sneerers and prophane wits may perhaps laugh at her first fright; yet my graver reader, when he considers the time of night, the summons from her bed, and the situation in which she found her master, will highly justify and applaud her conduct, unless the prudence which must be supposed to attend maidens at that period of life at which Mrs Deborah had arrived, should a little lessen his admiration.\n\nWhen Mrs Deborah returned into the room, and was acquainted by her master with the finding the little infant, her consternation was rather greater than his had been; nor could she refrain from crying out, with great horror of accent as well as look, “My good sir! what's to be done?” Mr Allworthy answered, she must take care of the child that evening, and in the morning he would give orders to provide it a nurse. “Yes, sir,” says she; “and I hope your worship will send out your warrant to take up the hussy its mother, for she must be one of the neighbourhood; and I should be glad to see her committed to Bridewell, and whipt at the cart's tail. Indeed, such wicked sluts cannot be too severely punished. I'll warrant 'tis not her first, by her impudence in laying it to your worship.” “In laying it to me, Deborah!” answered Allworthy: “I can't think she hath any such design. I suppose she hath only taken this method to provide for her child; and truly I am glad she hath not done worse.” “I don't know what is worse,” cries Deborah, “than for such wicked strumpets to lay their sins at honest men's doors; and though your worship knows your own innocence, yet the world is censorious; and it hath been many an honest man's hap to pass for the father of children he never begot; and if your worship should provide for the child, it may make the people the apter to believe; besides, why should your worship provide for what the parish is obliged to maintain? For my own part, if it was an honest man's child, indeed--but for my own part, it goes against me to touch these misbegotten wretches, whom I don't look upon as my fellow-creatures. Faugh! how it stinks! It doth not smell like a Christian. If I might be so bold to give my advice, I would have it put in a basket, and sent out and laid at the churchwarden's door. It is a good night, only a little rainy and windy; and if it was well wrapt up, and put in a warm basket, it is two to one but it lives till it is found in the morning. But if it should not, we have discharged our duty in taking proper care of it; and it is, perhaps, better for such creatures to die in a state of innocence, than to grow up and imitate their mothers; for nothing better can be expected of them.”\n\nThere were some strokes in this speech which perhaps would have offended Mr Allworthy, had he strictly attended to it; but he had now got one of his fingers into the infant's hand, which, by its gentle pressure, seeming to implore his assistance, had certainly out-pleaded the eloquence of Mrs Deborah, had it been ten times greater than it was. He now gave Mrs Deborah positive orders to take the child to her own bed, and to call up a maid-servant to provide it pap, and other things, against it waked. He likewise ordered that proper cloathes should be procured for it early in the morning, and that it should be brought to himself as soon as he was stirring.\n\nSuch was the discernment of Mrs Wilkins, and such the respect she bore her master, under whom she enjoyed a most excellent place, that her scruples gave way to his peremptory commands; and she took the child under her arms, without any apparent disgust at the illegality of its birth; and declaring it was a sweet little infant, walked off with it to her own chamber.\n\nAllworthy here betook himself to those pleasing slumbers which a heart that hungers after goodness is apt to enjoy when thoroughly satisfied. As these are possibly sweeter than what are occasioned by any other hearty meal, I should take more pains to display them to the reader, if I knew any air to recommend him to for the procuring such an appetite.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nThe reader's neck brought into danger by a description; his escape; and the great condescension of Miss Bridget Allworthy.\n\nThe Gothic stile of building could produce nothing nobler than Mr Allworthy's house. There was an air of grandeur in it that struck you with awe, and rivalled the beauties of the best Grecian architecture; and it was as commodious within as venerable without.\n\nIt stood on the south-east side of a hill, but nearer the bottom than the top of it, so as to be sheltered from the north-east by a grove of old oaks which rose above it in a gradual ascent of near half a mile, and yet high enough to enjoy a most charming prospect of the valley beneath.\n\nIn the midst of the grove was a fine lawn, sloping down towards the house, near the summit of which rose a plentiful spring, gushing out of a rock covered with firs, and forming a constant cascade of about thirty feet, not carried down a regular flight of steps, but tumbling in a natural fall over the broken and mossy stones till it came to the bottom of the rock, then running off in a pebly channel, that with many lesser falls winded along, till it fell into a lake at the foot of the hill, about a quarter of a mile below the house on the south side, and which was seen from every room in the front. Out of this lake, which filled the center of a beautiful plain, embellished with groups of beeches and elms, and fed with sheep, issued a river, that for several miles was seen to meander through an amazing variety of meadows and woods till it emptied itself into the sea, with a large arm of which, and an island beyond it, the prospect was closed.\n\nOn the right of this valley opened another of less extent, adorned with several villages, and terminated by one of the towers of an old ruined abby, grown over with ivy, and part of the front, which remained still entire.\n\nThe left-hand scene presented the view of a very fine park, composed of very unequal ground, and agreeably varied with all the diversity that hills, lawns, wood, and water, laid out with admirable taste, but owing less to art than to nature, could give. Beyond this, the country gradually rose into a ridge of wild mountains, the tops of which were above the clouds.\n\nIt was now the middle of May, and the morning was remarkably serene, when Mr Allworthy walked forth on the terrace, where the dawn opened every minute that lovely prospect we have before described to his eye; and now having sent forth streams of light, which ascended the blue firmament before him, as harbingers preceding his pomp, in the full blaze of his majesty rose the sun, than which one object alone in this lower creation could be more glorious, and that Mr Allworthy himself presented--a human being replete with benevolence, meditating in what manner he might render himself most acceptable to his Creator, by doing most good to his creatures.\n\nReader, take care. I have unadvisedly led thee to the top of as high a hill as Mr Allworthy's, and how to get thee down without breaking thy neck, I do not well know. However, let us e'en venture to slide down together; for Miss Bridget rings her bell, and Mr Allworthy is summoned to breakfast, where I must attend, and, if you please, shall be glad of your company.\n\nThe usual compliments having past between Mr Allworthy and Miss Bridget, and the tea being poured out, he summoned Mrs Wilkins, and told his sister he had a present for her, for which she thanked him--imagining, I suppose, it had been a gown, or some ornament for her person. Indeed, he very often made her such presents; and she, in complacence to him, spent much time in adorning herself. I say in complacence to him, because she always exprest the greatest contempt for dress, and for those ladies who made it their study.\n\nBut if such was her expectation, how was she disappointed when Mrs Wilkins, according to the order she had received from her master, produced the little infant? Great surprizes, as hath been observed, are apt to be silent; and so was Miss Bridget, till her brother began, and told her the whole story, which, as the reader knows it already, we shall not repeat.\n\nMiss Bridget had always exprest so great a regard for what the ladies are pleased to call virtue, and had herself maintained such a severity of character, that it was expected, especially by Wilkins, that she would have vented much bitterness on this occasion, and would have voted for sending the child, as a kind of noxious animal, immediately out of the house; but, on the contrary, she rather took the good-natured side of the question, intimated some compassion for the helpless little creature, and commended her brother's charity in what he had done.\n\nPerhaps the reader may account for this behaviour from her condescension to Mr Allworthy, when we have informed him that the good man had ended his narrative with owning a resolution to take care of the child, and to breed him up as his own; for, to acknowledge the truth, she was always ready to oblige her brother, and very seldom, if ever, contradicted his sentiments. She would, indeed, sometimes make a few observations, as that men were headstrong, and must have their own way, and would wish she had been blest with an independent fortune; but these were always vented in a low voice, and at the most amounted only to what is called muttering.\n\nHowever, what she withheld from the infant, she bestowed with the utmost profuseness on the poor unknown mother, whom she called an impudent slut, a wanton hussy, an audacious harlot, a wicked jade, a vile strumpet, with every other appellation with which the tongue of virtue never fails to lash those who bring a disgrace on the sex.\n\nA consultation was now entered into how to proceed in order to discover the mother. A scrutiny was first made into the characters of the female servants of the house, who were all acquitted by Mrs Wilkins, and with apparent merit; for she had collected them herself, and perhaps it would be difficult to find such another set of scarecrows.\n\nThe next step was to examine among the inhabitants of the parish; and this was referred to Mrs Wilkins, who was to enquire with all imaginable diligence, and to make her report in the afternoon.\n\nMatters being thus settled, Mr Allworthy withdrew to his study, as was his custom, and left the child to his sister, who, at his desire, had undertaken the care of it.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nContaining a few common matters, with a very uncommon observation upon them.\nWhen her master was departed, Mrs Deborah stood silent, expecting her cue from Miss Bridget; for as to what had past before her master, the prudent housekeeper by no means relied upon it, as she had often known the sentiments of the lady in her brother's absence to differ greatly from those which she had expressed in his presence. Miss Bridget did not, however, suffer her to continue long in this doubtful situation; for having looked some time earnestly at the child, as it lay asleep in the lap of Mrs Deborah, the good lady could not forbear giving it a hearty kiss, at the same time declaring herself wonderfully pleased with its beauty and innocence. Mrs Deborah no sooner observed this than she fell to squeezing and kissing, with as great raptures as sometimes inspire the sage dame of forty and five towards a youthful and vigorous bridegroom, crying out, in a shrill voice, “O, the dear little creature!--The dear, sweet, pretty creature! Well, I vow it is as fine a boy as ever was seen!”\n\nThese exclamations continued till they were interrupted by the lady, who now proceeded to execute the commission given her by her brother, and gave orders for providing all necessaries for the child, appointing a very good room in the house for his nursery. Her orders were indeed so liberal, that, had it been a child of her own, she could not have exceeded them; but, lest the virtuous reader may condemn her for showing too great regard to a base-born infant, to which all charity is condemned by law as irreligious, we think proper to observe that she concluded the whole with saying, “Since it was her brother's whim to adopt the little brat, she supposed little master must be treated with great tenderness. For her part, she could not help thinking it was an encouragement to vice; but that she knew too much of the obstinacy of mankind to oppose any of their ridiculous humours.”\n\nWith reflections of this nature she usually, as has been hinted, accompanied every act of compliance with her brother's inclinations; and surely nothing could more contribute to heighten the merit of this compliance than a declaration that she knew, at the same time, the folly and unreasonableness of those inclinations to which she submitted. Tacit obedience implies no force upon the will, and consequently may be easily, and without any pains, preserved; but when a wife, a child, a relation, or a friend, performs what we desire, with grumbling and reluctance, with expressions of dislike and dissatisfaction, the manifest difficulty which they undergo must greatly enhance the obligation.\n\nAs this is one of those deep observations which very few readers can be supposed capable of making themselves, I have thought proper to lend them my assistance; but this is a favour rarely to be expected in the course of my work. Indeed, I shall seldom or never so indulge him, unless in such instances as this, where nothing but the inspiration with which we writers are gifted, can possibly enable any one to make the discovery.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nMrs Deborah is introduced into the parish with a simile. A short account of Jenny Jones, with the difficulties and discouragements which may attend young women in the pursuit of learning.\n\nMrs Deborah, having disposed of the child according to the will of her master, now prepared to visit those habitations which were supposed to conceal its mother.\n\nNot otherwise than when a kite, tremendous bird, is beheld by the feathered generation soaring aloft, and hovering over their heads, the amorous dove, and every innocent little bird, spread wide the alarm, and fly trembling to their hiding-places. He proudly beats the air, conscious of his dignity, and meditates intended mischief.\n\nSo when the approach of Mrs Deborah was proclaimed through the street, all the inhabitants ran trembling into their houses, each matron dreading lest the visit should fall to her lot. She with stately steps proudly advances over the field: aloft she bears her towering head, filled with conceit of her own pre-eminence, and schemes to effect her intended discovery.\n\nThe sagacious reader will not from this simile imagine these poor people had any apprehension of the design with which Mrs Wilkins was now coming towards them; but as the great beauty of the simile may possibly sleep these hundred years, till some future commentator shall take this work in hand, I think proper to lend the reader a little assistance in this place.\n\nIt is my intention, therefore, to signify, that, as it is the nature of a kite to devour little birds, so is it the nature of such persons as Mrs Wilkins to insult and tyrannize over little people. This being indeed the means which they use to recompense to themselves their extreme servility and condescension to their superiors; for nothing can be more reasonable, than that slaves and flatterers should exact the same taxes on all below them, which they themselves pay to all above them.\n\nWhenever Mrs Deborah had occasion to exert any extraordinary condescension to Mrs Bridget, and by that means had a little soured her natural disposition, it was usual with her to walk forth among these people, in order to refine her temper, by venting, and, as it were, purging off all ill humours; on which account she was by no means a welcome visitant: to say the truth, she was universally dreaded and hated by them all.\n\nOn her arrival in this place, she went immediately to the habitation of an elderly matron; to whom, as this matron had the good fortune to resemble herself in the comeliness of her person, as well as in her age, she had generally been more favourable than to any of the rest. To this woman she imparted what had happened, and the design upon which she was come thither that morning. These two began presently to scrutinize the characters of the several young girls who lived in any of those houses, and at last fixed their strongest suspicion on one Jenny Jones, who, they both agreed, was the likeliest person to have committed this fact.\n\nThis Jenny Jones was no very comely girl, either in her face or person; but nature had somewhat compensated the want of beauty with what is generally more esteemed by those ladies whose judgment is arrived at years of perfect maturity, for she had given her a very uncommon share of understanding. This gift Jenny had a good deal improved by erudition. She had lived several years a servant with a schoolmaster, who, discovering a great quickness of parts in the girl, and an extraordinary desire of learning--for every leisure hour she was always found reading in the books of the scholars--had the good-nature, or folly--just as the reader pleases to call it--to instruct her so far, that she obtained a competent skill in the Latin language, and was, perhaps, as good a scholar as most of the young men of quality of the age. This advantage, however, like most others of an extraordinary kind, was attended with some small inconveniences: for as it is not to be wondered at, that a young woman so well accomplished should have little relish for the society of those whom fortune had made her equals, but whom education had rendered so much her inferiors; so is it matter of no greater astonishment, that this superiority in Jenny, together with that behaviour which is its certain consequence, should produce among the rest some little envy and ill-will towards her; and these had, perhaps, secretly burnt in the bosoms of her neighbours ever since her return from her service.\n\nTheir envy did not, however, display itself openly, till poor Jenny, to the surprize of everybody, and to the vexation of all the young women in these parts, had publickly shone forth on a Sunday in a new silk gown, with a laced cap, and other proper appendages to these.\n\nThe flame, which had before lain in embryo, now burst forth. Jenny had, by her learning, increased her own pride, which none of her neighbours were kind enough to feed with the honour she seemed to demand; and now, instead of respect and adoration, she gained nothing but hatred and abuse by her finery. The whole parish declared she could not come honestly by such things; and parents, instead of wishing their daughters the same, felicitated themselves that their children had them not.\n\nHence, perhaps, it was, that the good woman first mentioned the name of this poor girl to Mrs Wilkins; but there was another circumstance that confirmed the latter in her suspicion; for Jenny had lately been often at Mr Allworthy's house. She had officiated as nurse to Miss Bridget, in a violent fit of illness, and had sat up many nights with that lady; besides which, she had been seen there the very day before Mr Allworthy's return, by Mrs Wilkins herself, though that sagacious person had not at first conceived any suspicion of her on that account: for, as she herself said, “She had always esteemed Jenny as a very sober girl (though indeed she knew very little of her), and had rather suspected some of those wanton trollops, who gave themselves airs, because, forsooth, they thought themselves handsome.”\n\nJenny was now summoned to appear in person before Mrs Deborah, which she immediately did. When Mrs Deborah, putting on the gravity of a judge, with somewhat more than his austerity, began an oration with the words, “You audacious strumpet!” in which she proceeded rather to pass sentence on the prisoner than to accuse her.\n\nThough Mrs Deborah was fully satisfied of the guilt of Jenny, from the reasons above shewn, it is possible Mr Allworthy might have required some stronger evidence to have convicted her; but she saved her accusers any such trouble, by freely confessing the whole fact with which she was charged.\n\nThis confession, though delivered rather in terms of contrition, as it appeared, did not at all mollify Mrs Deborah, who now pronounced a second judgment against her, in more opprobrious language than before; nor had it any better success with the bystanders, who were now grown very numerous. Many of them cried out, “They thought what madam's silk gown would end in;” others spoke sarcastically of her learning. Not a single female was present but found some means of expressing her abhorrence of poor Jenny, who bore all very patiently, except the malice of one woman, who reflected upon her person, and tossing up her nose, said, “The man must have a good stomach who would give silk gowns for such sort of trumpery!” Jenny replied to this with a bitterness which might have surprized a judicious person, who had observed the tranquillity with which she bore all the affronts to her chastity; but her patience was perhaps tired out, for this is a virtue which is very apt to be fatigued by exercise.\n\nMrs Deborah having succeeded beyond her hopes in her inquiry, returned with much triumph, and, at the appointed hour, made a faithful report to Mr Allworthy, who was much surprized at the relation; for he had heard of the extraordinary parts and improvements of this girl, whom he intended to have given in marriage, together with a small living, to a neighbouring curate. His concern, therefore, on this occasion, was at least equal to the satisfaction which appeared in Mrs Deborah, and to many readers may seem much more reasonable.\n\nMiss Bridget blessed herself, and said, “For her part, she should never hereafter entertain a good opinion of any woman.” For Jenny before this had the happiness of being much in her good graces also.\n\nThe prudent housekeeper was again dispatched to bring the unhappy culprit before Mr Allworthy, in order, not as it was hoped by some, and expected by all, to be sent to the house of correction, but to receive wholesome admonition and reproof; which those who relish that kind of instructive writing may peruse in the next chapter.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nContaining such grave matter, that the reader cannot laugh once through the whole chapter, unless peradventure he should laugh at the author.\n\nWhen Jenny appeared, Mr Allworthy took her into his study, and spoke to her as follows: “You know, child, it is in my power as a magistrate, to punish you very rigorously for what you have done; and you will, perhaps, be the more apt to fear I should execute that power, because you have in a manner laid your sins at my door.\n\n“But, perhaps, this is one reason which hath determined me to act in a milder manner with you: for, as no private resentment should ever influence a magistrate, I will be so far from considering your having deposited the infant in my house as an aggravation of your offence, that I will suppose, in your favour, this to have proceeded from a natural affection to your child, since you might have some hopes to see it thus better provided for than was in the power of yourself, or its wicked father, to provide for it. I should indeed have been highly offended with you had you exposed the little wretch in the manner of some inhuman mothers, who seem no less to have abandoned their humanity, than to have parted with their chastity. It is the other part of your offence, therefore, upon which I intend to admonish you, I mean the violation of your chastity;--a crime, however lightly it may be treated by debauched persons, very heinous in itself, and very dreadful in its consequences.\n\n“The heinous nature of this offence must be sufficiently apparent to every Christian, inasmuch as it is committed in defiance of the laws of our religion, and of the express commands of Him who founded that religion.\n\n“And here its consequences may well be argued to be dreadful; for what can be more so, than to incur the divine displeasure, by the breach of the divine commands; and that in an instance against which the highest vengeance is specifically denounced?\n\n“But these things, though too little, I am afraid, regarded, are so plain, that mankind, however they may want to be reminded, can never need information on this head. A hint, therefore, to awaken your sense of this matter, shall suffice; for I would inspire you with repentance, and not drive you to desperation.\n\n“There are other consequences, not indeed so dreadful or replete with horror as this; and yet such, as, if attentively considered, must, one would think, deter all of your sex at least from the commission of this crime.\n\n“For by it you are rendered infamous, and driven, like lepers of old, out of society; at least, from the society of all but wicked and reprobate persons; for no others will associate with you.\n\n“If you have fortunes, you are hereby rendered incapable of enjoying them; if you have none, you are disabled from acquiring any, nay almost of procuring your sustenance; for no persons of character will receive you into their houses. Thus you are often driven by necessity itself into a state of shame and misery, which unavoidably ends in the destruction of both body and soul.\n\n“Can any pleasure compensate these evils? Can any temptation have sophistry and delusion strong enough to persuade you to so simple a bargain? Or can any carnal appetite so overpower your reason, or so totally lay it asleep, as to prevent your flying with affright and terror from a crime which carries such punishment always with it?\n\n“How base and mean must that woman be, how void of that dignity of mind, and decent pride, without which we are not worthy the name of human creatures, who can bear to level herself with the lowest animal, and to sacrifice all that is great and noble in her, all her heavenly part, to an appetite which she hath in common with the vilest branch of the creation! For no woman, sure, will plead the passion of love for an excuse. This would be to own herself the mere tool and bubble of the man. Love, however barbarously we may corrupt and pervert its meaning, as it is a laudable, is a rational passion, and can never be violent but when reciprocal; for though the Scripture bids us love our enemies, it means not with that fervent love which we naturally bear towards our friends; much less that we should sacrifice to them our lives, and what ought to be dearer to us, our innocence. Now in what light, but that of an enemy, can a reasonable woman regard the man who solicits her to entail on herself all the misery I have described to you, and who would purchase to himself a short, trivial, contemptible pleasure, so greatly at her expense! For, by the laws of custom, the whole shame, with all its dreadful consequences, falls intirely upon her. Can love, which always seeks the good of its object, attempt to betray a woman into a bargain where she is so greatly to be the loser? If such corrupter, therefore, should have the impudence to pretend a real affection for her, ought not the woman to regard him not only as an enemy, but as the worst of all enemies, a false, designing, treacherous, pretended friend, who intends not only to debauch her body, but her understanding at the same time?”\n\nHere Jenny expressing great concern, Allworthy paused a moment, and then proceeded: “I have talked thus to you, child, not to insult you for what is past and irrevocable, but to caution and strengthen you for the future. Nor should I have taken this trouble, but from some opinion of your good sense, notwithstanding the dreadful slip you have made; and from some hopes of your hearty repentance, which are founded on the openness and sincerity of your confession. If these do not deceive me, I will take care to convey you from this scene of your shame, where you shall, by being unknown, avoid the punishment which, as I have said, is allotted to your crime in this world; and I hope, by repentance, you will avoid the much heavier sentence denounced against it in the other. Be a good girl the rest of your days, and want shall be no motive to your going astray; and, believe me, there is more pleasure, even in this world, in an innocent and virtuous life, than in one debauched and vicious.\n\n“As to your child, let no thoughts concerning it molest you; I will provide for it in a better manner than you can ever hope. And now nothing remains but that you inform me who was the wicked man that seduced you; for my anger against him will be much greater than you have experienced on this occasion.”\n\nJenny now lifted her eyes from the ground, and with a modest look and decent voice thus began:--\n\n“To know you, sir, and not love your goodness, would be an argument of total want of sense or goodness in any one. In me it would amount to the highest ingratitude, not to feel, in the most sensible manner, the great degree of goodness you have been pleased to exert on this occasion. As to my concern for what is past, I know you will spare my blushes the repetition. My future conduct will much better declare my sentiments than any professions I can now make. I beg leave to assure you, sir, that I take your advice much kinder than your generous offer with which you concluded it; for, as you are pleased to say, sir, it is an instance of your opinion of my understanding.”--Here her tears flowing apace, she stopped a few moments, and then proceeded thus:--“Indeed, sir, your kindness overcomes me; but I will endeavour to deserve this good opinion: for if I have the understanding you are so kindly pleased to allow me, such advice cannot be thrown away upon me. I thank you, sir, heartily, for your intended kindness to my poor helpless child: he is innocent, and I hope will live to be grateful for all the favours you shall show him. But now, sir, I must on my knees entreat you not to persist in asking me to declare the father of my infant. I promise you faithfully you shall one day know; but I am under the most solemn ties and engagements of honour, as well as the most religious vows and protestations, to conceal his name at this time. And I know you too well, to think you would desire I should sacrifice either my honour or my religion.”\n\nMr Allworthy, whom the least mention of those sacred words was sufficient to stagger, hesitated a moment before he replied, and then told her, she had done wrong to enter into such engagements to a villain; but since she had, he could not insist on her breaking them. He said, it was not from a motive of vain curiosity he had inquired, but in order to punish the fellow; at least, that he might not ignorantly confer favours on the undeserving.\n\nAs to these points, Jenny satisfied him by the most solemn assurances, that the man was entirely out of his reach; and was neither subject to his power, nor in any probability of becoming an object of his goodness.\n\nThe ingenuity of this behaviour had gained Jenny so much credit with this worthy man, that he easily believed what she told him; for as she had disdained to excuse herself by a lie, and had hazarded his further displeasure in her present situation, rather than she would forfeit her honour or integrity by betraying another, he had but little apprehensions that she would be guilty of falsehood towards himself.\n\nHe therefore dismissed her with assurances that he would very soon remove her out of the reach of that obloquy she had incurred; concluding with some additional documents, in which he recommended repentance, saying, “Consider, child, there is one still to reconcile yourself to, whose favour is of much greater importance to you than mine.”\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nA dialogue between Mesdames Bridget and Deborah; containing more amusement, but less instruction, than the former.\n\nWhen Mr Allworthy had retired to his study with Jenny Jones, as hath been seen, Mrs Bridget, with the good housekeeper, had betaken themselves to a post next adjoining to the said study; whence, through the conveyance of a keyhole, they sucked in at their ears the instructive lecture delivered by Mr Allworthy, together with the answers of Jenny, and indeed every other particular which passed in the last chapter.\n\nThis hole in her brother's study-door was indeed as well known to Mrs Bridget, and had been as frequently applied to by her, as the famous hole in the wall was by Thisbe of old. This served to many good purposes. For by such means Mrs Bridget became often acquainted with her brother's inclinations, without giving him the trouble of repeating them to her. It is true, some inconveniences attended this intercourse, and she had sometimes reason to cry out with Thisbe, in Shakspeare, “O, wicked, wicked wall!” For as Mr Allworthy was a justice of peace, certain things occurred in examinations concerning bastards, and such like, which are apt to give great offence to the chaste ears of virgins, especially when they approach the age of forty, as was the case of Mrs Bridget. However, she had, on such occasions, the advantage of concealing her blushes from the eyes of men; and De non apparentibus, et non existentibus eadem est ratio--in English, “When a woman is not seen to blush, she doth not blush at all.”\n\nBoth the good women kept strict silence during the whole scene between Mr Allworthy and the girl; but as soon as it was ended, and that gentleman was out of hearing, Mrs Deborah could not help exclaiming against the clemency of her master, and especially against his suffering her to conceal the father of the child, which she swore she would have out of her before the sun set.\n\nAt these words Mrs Bridget discomposed her features with a smile (a thing very unusual to her). Not that I would have my reader imagine, that this was one of those wanton smiles which Homer would have you conceive came from Venus, when he calls her the laughter-loving goddess; nor was it one of those smiles which Lady Seraphina shoots from the stage-box, and which Venus would quit her immortality to be able to equal. No, this was rather one of those smiles which might be supposed to have come from the dimpled cheeks of the august Tisiphone, or from one of the misses, her sisters.\n\nWith such a smile then, and with a voice sweet as the evening breeze of Boreas in the pleasant month of November, Mrs Bridget gently reproved the curiosity of Mrs Deborah; a vice with which it seems the latter was too much tainted, and which the former inveighed against with great bitterness, adding, “That, among all her faults, she thanked Heaven her enemies could not accuse her of prying into the affairs of other people.”\n\nShe then proceeded to commend the honour and spirit with which Jenny had acted. She said, she could not help agreeing with her brother, that there was some merit in the sincerity of her confession, and in her integrity to her lover: that she had always thought her a very good girl, and doubted not but she had been seduced by some rascal, who had been infinitely more to blame than herself, and very probably had prevailed with her by a promise of marriage, or some other treacherous proceeding.\n\nThis behaviour of Mrs Bridget greatly surprised Mrs Deborah; for this well-bred woman seldom opened her lips, either to her master or his sister, till she had first sounded their inclinations, with which her sentiments were always consonant. Here, however, she thought she might have launched forth with safety; and the sagacious reader will not perhaps accuse her of want of sufficient forecast in so doing, but will rather admire with what wonderful celerity she tacked about, when she found herself steering a wrong course.\n\n“Nay, madam,” said this able woman, and truly great politician, “I must own I cannot help admiring the girl's spirit, as well as your ladyship. And, as your ladyship says, if she was deceived by some wicked man, the poor wretch is to be pitied. And to be sure, as your ladyship says, the girl hath always appeared like a good, honest, plain girl, and not vain of her face, forsooth, as some wanton husseys in the neighbourhood are.”\n\n“You say true, Deborah,” said Miss Bridget. “If the girl had been one of those vain trollops, of which we have too many in the parish, I should have condemned my brother for his lenity towards her. I saw two farmers' daughters at church, the other day, with bare necks. I protest they shocked me. If wenches will hang out lures for fellows, it is no matter what they suffer. I detest such creatures; and it would be much better for them that their faces had been seamed with the smallpox; but I must confess, I never saw any of this wanton behaviour in poor Jenny: some artful villain, I am convinced, hath betrayed, nay perhaps forced her; and I pity the poor wretch with all my heart.”\n\nMrs Deborah approved all these sentiments, and the dialogue concluded with a general and bitter invective against beauty, and with many compassionate considerations for all honest plain girls who are deluded by the wicked arts of deceitful men.\n\nContaining matters which will surprize the reader.\n\nJenny returned home well pleased with the reception she had met with from Mr Allworthy, whose indulgence to her she industriously made public; partly perhaps as a sacrifice to her own pride, and partly from the more prudent motive of reconciling her neighbours to her, and silencing their clamours.\n\nBut though this latter view, if she indeed had it, may appear reasonable enough, yet the event did not answer her expectation; for when she was convened before the justice, and it was universally apprehended that the house of correction would have been her fate, though some of the young women cryed out “It was good enough for her,” and diverted themselves with the thoughts of her beating hemp in a silk gown; yet there were many others who began to pity her condition: but when it was known in what manner Mr Allworthy had behaved, the tide turned against her. One said, “I'll assure you, madam hath had good luck.” A second cryed, “See what it is to be a favourite!” A third, “Ay, this comes of her learning.” Every person made some malicious comment or other on the occasion, and reflected on the partiality of the justice.\n\nThe behaviour of these people may appear impolitic and ungrateful to the reader, who considers the power and benevolence of Mr Allworthy. But as to his power, he never used it; and as to his benevolence, he exerted so much, that he had thereby disobliged all his neighbours; for it is a secret well known to great men, that, by conferring an obligation, they do not always procure a friend, but are certain of creating many enemies.\n\nJenny was, however, by the care and goodness of Mr Allworthy, soon removed out of the reach of reproach; when malice being no longer able to vent its rage on her, began to seek another object of its bitterness, and this was no less than Mr Allworthy, himself; for a whisper soon went abroad, that he himself was the father of the foundling child.\n\nThis supposition so well reconciled his conduct to the general opinion, that it met with universal assent; and the outcry against his lenity soon began to take another turn, and was changed into an invective against his cruelty to the poor girl. Very grave and good women exclaimed against men who begot children, and then disowned them. Nor were there wanting some, who, after the departure of Jenny, insinuated that she was spirited away with a design too black to be mentioned, and who gave frequent hints that a legal inquiry ought to be made into the whole matter, and that some people should be forced to produce the girl.\n\nThese calumnies might have probably produced ill consequences, at the least might have occasioned some trouble, to a person of a more doubtful and suspicious character than Mr Allworthy was blessed with; but in his case they had no such effect; and, being heartily despised by him, they served only to afford an innocent amusement to the good gossips of the neighbourhood.\n\nBut as we cannot possibly divine what complection our reader may be of, and as it will be some time before he will hear any more of Jenny, we think proper to give him a very early intimation, that Mr Allworthy was, and will hereafter appear to be, absolutely innocent of any criminal intention whatever. He had indeed committed no other than an error in politics, by tempering justice with mercy, and by refusing to gratify the good-natured disposition of the mob,[*] with an object for their compassion to work on in the person of poor Jenny, whom, in order to pity, they desired to have seen sacrificed to ruin and infamy, by a shameful correction in Bridewell.\n\n[*]Whenever this word occurs in our writings, it intends persons without virtue or sense, in all stations; and many of the highest rank are often meant by it.\n\nSo far from complying with this their inclination, by which all hopes of reformation would have been abolished, and even the gate shut against her if her own inclinations should ever hereafter lead her to chuse the road of virtue, Mr Allworthy rather chose to encourage the girl to return thither by the only possible means; for too true I am afraid it is, that many women have become abandoned, and have sunk to the last degree of vice, by being unable to retrieve the first slip. This will be, I am afraid, always the case while they remain among their former acquaintance; it was therefore wisely done by Mr Allworthy, to remove Jenny to a place where she might enjoy the pleasure of reputation, after having tasted the ill consequences of losing it.\n\nTo this place therefore, wherever it was, we will wish her a good journey, and for the present take leave of her, and of the little foundling her child, having matters of much higher importance to communicate to the reader.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nThe hospitality of Allworthy; with a short sketch of the characters of two brothers, a doctor and a captain, who were entertained by that gentleman.\n\nNeither Mr Allworthy's house, nor his heart, were shut against any part of mankind, but they were both more particularly open to men of merit. To say the truth, this was the only house in the kingdom where you was sure to gain a dinner by deserving it.\n\nAbove all others, men of genius and learning shared the principal place in his favour; and in these he had much discernment: for though he had missed the advantage of a learned education, yet, being blest with vast natural abilities, he had so well profited by a vigorous though late application to letters, and by much conversation with men of eminence in this way, that he was himself a very competent judge in most kinds of literature.\n\nIt is no wonder that in an age when this kind of merit is so little in fashion, and so slenderly provided for, persons possessed of it should very eagerly flock to a place where they were sure of being received with great complaisance; indeed, where they might enjoy almost the same advantages of a liberal fortune as if they were entitled to it in their own right; for Mr Allworthy was not one of those generous persons who are ready most bountifully to bestow meat, drink, and lodging on men of wit and learning, for which they expect no other return but entertainment, instruction, flattery, and subserviency; in a word, that such persons should be enrolled in the number of domestics, without wearing their master's cloathes, or receiving wages.\n\nOn the contrary, every person in this house was perfect master of his own time: and as he might at his pleasure satisfy all his appetites within the restrictions only of law, virtue, and religion; so he might, if his health required, or his inclination prompted him to temperance, or even to abstinence, absent himself from any meals, or retire from them, whenever he was so disposed, without even a sollicitation to the contrary: for, indeed, such sollicitations from superiors always savour very strongly of commands. But all here were free from such impertinence, not only those whose company is in all other places esteemed a favour from their equality of fortune, but even those whose indigent circumstances make such an eleemosynary abode convenient to them, and who are therefore less welcome to a great man's table because they stand in need of it.\n\nAmong others of this kind was Dr Blifil, a gentleman who had the misfortune of losing the advantage of great talents by the obstinacy of a father, who would breed him to a profession he disliked. In obedience to this obstinacy the doctor had in his youth been obliged to study physic, or rather to say he studied it; for in reality books of this kind were almost the only ones with which he was unacquainted; and unfortunately for him, the doctor was master of almost every other science but that by which he was to get his bread; the consequence of which was, that the doctor at the age of forty had no bread to eat.\n\nSuch a person as this was certain to find a welcome at Mr Allworthy's table, to whom misfortunes were ever a recommendation, when they were derived from the folly or villany of others, and not of the unfortunate person himself. Besides this negative merit, the doctor had one positive recommendation;--this was a great appearance of religion. Whether his religion was real, or consisted only in appearance, I shall not presume to say, as I am not possessed of any touchstone which can distinguish the true from the false.\n\nIf this part of his character pleased Mr Allworthy, it delighted Miss Bridget. She engaged him in many religious controversies; on which occasions she constantly expressed great satisfaction in the doctor's knowledge, and not much less in the compliments which he frequently bestowed on her own. To say the truth, she had read much English divinity, and had puzzled more than one of the neighbouring curates. Indeed, her conversation was so pure, her looks so sage, and her whole deportment so grave and solemn, that she seemed to deserve the name of saint equally with her namesake, or with any other female in the Roman kalendar.\n\nAs sympathies of all kinds are apt to beget love, so experience teaches us that none have a more direct tendency this way than those of a religious kind between persons of different sexes. The doctor found himself so agreeable to Miss Bridget, that he now began to lament an unfortunate accident which had happened to him about ten years before; namely, his marriage with another woman, who was not only still alive, but, what was worse, known to be so by Mr Allworthy. This was a fatal bar to that happiness which he otherwise saw sufficient probability of obtaining with this young lady; for as to criminal indulgences, he certainly never thought of them. This was owing either to his religion, as is most probable, or to the purity of his passion, which was fixed on those things which matrimony only, and not criminal correspondence, could put him in possession of, or could give him any title to.\n\nHe had not long ruminated on these matters, before it occurred to his memory that he had a brother who was under no such unhappy incapacity. This brother he made no doubt would succeed; for he discerned, as he thought, an inclination to marriage in the lady; and the reader perhaps, when he hears the brother's qualifications, will not blame the confidence which he entertained of his success.\n\nThis gentleman was about thirty-five years of age. He was of a middle size, and what is called well-built. He had a scar on his forehead, which did not so much injure his beauty as it denoted his valour (for he was a half-pay officer). He had good teeth, and something affable, when he pleased, in his smile; though naturally his countenance, as well as his air and voice, had much of roughness in it: yet he could at any time deposit this, and appear all gentleness and good-humour. He was not ungenteel, nor entirely devoid of wit, and in his youth had abounded in sprightliness, which, though he had lately put on a more serious character, he could, when he pleased, resume.\n\nHe had, as well as the doctor, an academic education; for his father had, with the same paternal authority we have mentioned before, decreed him for holy orders; but as the old gentleman died before he was ordained, he chose the church military, and preferred the king's commission to the bishop's.\n\nHe had purchased the post of lieutenant of dragoons, and afterwards came to be a captain; but having quarrelled with his colonel, was by his interest obliged to sell; from which time he had entirely rusticated himself, had betaken himself to studying the Scriptures, and was not a little suspected of an inclination to methodism.\n\nIt seemed, therefore, not unlikely that such a person should succeed with a lady of so saint-like a disposition, and whose inclinations were no otherwise engaged than to the marriage state in general; but why the doctor, who certainly had no great friendship for his brother, should for his sake think of making so ill a return to the hospitality of Allworthy, is a matter not so easy to be accounted for.\n\nIs it that some natures delight in evil, as others are thought to delight in virtue? Or is there a pleasure in being accessory to a theft when we cannot commit it ourselves? Or lastly (which experience seems to make probable), have we a satisfaction in aggrandizing our families, even though we have not the least love or respect for them?\n\nWhether any of these motives operated on the doctor, we will not determine; but so the fact was. He sent for his brother, and easily found means to introduce him at Allworthy's as a person who intended only a short visit to himself.\n\nThe captain had not been in the house a week before the doctor had reason to felicitate himself on his discernment. The captain was indeed as great a master of the art of love as Ovid was formerly. He had besides received proper hints from his brother, which he failed not to improve to the best advantage.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\nContaining many rules, and some examples, concerning falling in love: descriptions of beauty, and other more prudential inducements to matrimony.\n\nIt hath been observed, by wise men or women, I forget which, that all persons are doomed to be in love once in their lives. No particular season is, as I remember, assigned for this; but the age at which Miss Bridget was arrived, seems to me as proper a period as any to be fixed on for this purpose: it often, indeed, happens much earlier; but when it doth not, I have observed it seldom or never fails about this time. Moreover, we may remark that at this season love is of a more serious and steady nature than what sometimes shows itself in the younger parts of life. The love of girls is uncertain, capricious, and so foolish that we cannot always discover what the young lady would be at; nay, it may almost be doubted whether she always knows this herself.\n\nNow we are never at a loss to discern this in women about forty; for as such grave, serious, and experienced ladies well know their own meaning, so it is always very easy for a man of the least sagacity to discover it with the utmost certainty.\n\nMiss Bridget is an example of all these observations. She had not been many times in the captain's company before she was seized with this passion. Nor did she go pining and moping about the house, like a puny, foolish girl, ignorant of her distemper: she felt, she knew, and she enjoyed, the pleasing sensation, of which, as she was certain it was not only innocent but laudable, she was neither afraid nor ashamed.\n\nAnd to say the truth, there is, in all points, great difference between the reasonable passion which women at this age conceive towards men, and the idle and childish liking of a girl to a boy, which is often fixed on the outside only, and on things of little value and no duration; as on cherry-cheeks, small, lily-white hands, sloe-black eyes, flowing locks, downy chins, dapper shapes; nay, sometimes on charms more worthless than these, and less the party's own; such are the outward ornaments of the person, for which men are beholden to the taylor, the laceman, the periwig-maker, the hatter, and the milliner, and not to nature. Such a passion girls may well be ashamed, as they generally are, to own either to themselves or others.\n\nThe love of Miss Bridget was of another kind. The captain owed nothing to any of these fop-makers in his dress, nor was his person much more beholden to nature. Both his dress and person were such as, had they appeared in an assembly or a drawing-room, would have been the contempt and ridicule of all the fine ladies there. The former of these was indeed neat, but plain, coarse, ill-fancied, and out of fashion. As for the latter, we have expressly described it above. So far was the skin on his cheeks from being cherry-coloured, that you could not discern what the natural colour of his cheeks was, they being totally overgrown by a black beard, which ascended to his eyes. His shape and limbs were indeed exactly proportioned, but so large that they denoted the strength rather of a ploughman than any other. His shoulders were broad beyond all size, and the calves of his legs larger than those of a common chairman. In short, his whole person wanted all that elegance and beauty which is the very reverse of clumsy strength, and which so agreeably sets off most of our fine gentlemen; being partly owing to the high blood of their ancestors, viz., blood made of rich sauces and generous wines, and partly to an early town education.\n\nThough Miss Bridget was a woman of the greatest delicacy of taste, yet such were the charms of the captain's conversation, that she totally overlooked the defects of his person. She imagined, and perhaps very wisely, that she should enjoy more agreeable minutes with the captain than with a much prettier fellow; and forewent the consideration of pleasing her eyes, in order to procure herself much more solid satisfaction.\n\nThe captain no sooner perceived the passion of Miss Bridget, in which discovery he was very quick-sighted, than he faithfully returned it. The lady, no more than her lover, was remarkable for beauty. I would attempt to draw her picture, but that is done already by a more able master, Mr Hogarth himself, to whom she sat many years ago, and hath been lately exhibited by that gentleman in his print of a winter's morning, of which she was no improper emblem, and may be seen walking (for walk she doth in the print) to Covent Garden church, with a starved foot-boy behind carrying her prayer-book.\n\nThe captain likewise very wisely preferred the more solid enjoyments he expected with this lady, to the fleeting charms of person. He was one of those wise men who regard beauty in the other sex as a very worthless and superficial qualification; or, to speak more truly, who rather chuse to possess every convenience of life with an ugly woman, than a handsome one without any of those conveniences. And having a very good appetite, and but little nicety, he fancied he should play his part very well at the matrimonial banquet, without the sauce of beauty.\n\nTo deal plainly with the reader, the captain, ever since his arrival, at least from the moment his brother had proposed the match to him, long before he had discovered any flattering symptoms in Miss Bridget, had been greatly enamoured; that is to say, of Mr Allworthy's house and gardens, and of his lands, tenements, and hereditaments; of all which the captain was so passionately fond, that he would most probably have contracted marriage with them, had he been obliged to have taken the witch of Endor into the bargain.\n\nAs Mr Allworthy, therefore, had declared to the doctor that he never intended to take a second wife, as his sister was his nearest relation, and as the doctor had fished out that his intentions were to make any child of hers his heir, which indeed the law, without his interposition, would have done for him; the doctor and his brother thought it an act of benevolence to give being to a human creature, who would be so plentifully provided with the most essential means of happiness. The whole thoughts, therefore, of both the brothers were how to engage the affections of this amiable lady.\n\nBut fortune, who is a tender parent, and often doth more for her favourite offspring than either they deserve or wish, had been so industrious for the captain, that whilst he was laying schemes to execute his purpose, the lady conceived the same desires with himself, and was on her side contriving how to give the captain proper encouragement, without appearing too forward; for she was a strict observer of all rules of decorum. In this, however, she easily succeeded; for as the captain was always on the look-out, no glance, gesture, or word escaped him.\n\nThe satisfaction which the captain received from the kind behaviour of Miss Bridget, was not a little abated by his apprehensions of Mr Allworthy; for, notwithstanding his disinterested professions, the captain imagined he would, when he came to act, follow the example of the rest of the world, and refuse his consent to a match so disadvantageous, in point of interest, to his sister. From what oracle he received this opinion, I shall leave the reader to determine: but however he came by it, it strangely perplexed him how to regulate his conduct so as at once to convey his affection to the lady, and to conceal it from her brother. He at length resolved to take all private opportunities of making his addresses; but in the presence of Mr Allworthy to be as reserved and as much upon his guard as was possible; and this conduct was highly approved by the brother.\n\nHe soon found means to make his addresses, in express terms, to his mistress, from whom he received an answer in the proper form, viz.: the answer which was first made some thousands of years ago, and which hath been handed down by tradition from mother to daughter ever since. If I was to translate this into Latin, I should render it by these two words, Nolo Episcopari: a phrase likewise of immemorial use on another occasion.\n\nThe captain, however he came by his knowledge, perfectly well understood the lady, and very soon after repeated his application with more warmth and earnestness than before, and was again, according to due form, rejected; but as he had increased in the eagerness of his desires, so the lady, with the same propriety, decreased in the violence of her refusal.\n\nNot to tire the reader, by leading him through every scene of this courtship (which, though in the opinion of a certain great author, it is the pleasantest scene of life to the actor, is, perhaps, as dull and tiresome as any whatever to the audience), the captain made his advances in form, the citadel was defended in form, and at length, in proper form, surrendered at discretion.\n\nDuring this whole time, which filled the space of near a month, the captain preserved great distance of behaviour to his lady in the presence of the brother; and the more he succeeded with her in private, the more reserved was he in public. And as for the lady, she had no sooner secured her lover than she behaved to him before company with the highest degree of indifference; so that Mr Allworthy must have had the insight of the devil (or perhaps some of his worse qualities) to have entertained the least suspicion of what was going forward.\n\n# Chapter xii.\n\nContaining what the reader may, perhaps, expect to find in it.\nIn all bargains, whether to fight or to marry, or concerning any other such business, little previous ceremony is required to bring the matter to an issue when both parties are really in earnest. This was the case at present, and in less than a month the captain and his lady were man and wife.\n\nThe great concern now was to break the matter to Mr Allworthy; and this was undertaken by the doctor.\n\nOne day, then, as Allworthy was walking in his garden, the doctor came to him, and, with great gravity of aspect, and all the concern which he could possibly affect in his countenance, said, “I am come, sir, to impart an affair to you of the utmost consequence; but how shall I mention to you what it almost distracts me to think of!” He then launched forth into the most bitter invectives both against men and women; accusing the former of having no attachment but to their interest, and the latter of being so addicted to vicious inclinations that they could never be safely trusted with one of the other sex. “Could I,” said he, “sir, have suspected that a lady of such prudence, such judgment, such learning, should indulge so indiscreet a passion! or could I have imagined that my brother--why do I call him so? he is no longer a brother of mine----”\n\n“Indeed but he is,” said Allworthy, “and a brother of mine too.”\n\n“Bless me, sir!” said the doctor, “do you know the shocking affair?”\n\n“Look'ee, Mr Blifil,” answered the good man, “it hath been my constant maxim in life to make the best of all matters which happen. My sister, though many years younger than I, is at least old enough to be at the age of discretion. Had he imposed on a child, I should have been more averse to have forgiven him; but a woman upwards of thirty must certainly be supposed to know what will make her most happy. She hath married a gentleman, though perhaps not quite her equal in fortune; and if he hath any perfections in her eye which can make up that deficiency, I see no reason why I should object to her choice of her own happiness; which I, no more than herself, imagine to consist only in immense wealth. I might, perhaps, from the many declarations I have made of complying with almost any proposal, have expected to have been consulted on this occasion; but these matters are of a very delicate nature, and the scruples of modesty, perhaps, are not to be overcome. As to your brother, I have really no anger against him at all. He hath no obligations to me, nor do I think he was under any necessity of asking my consent, since the woman is, as I have said, sui juris, and of a proper age to be entirely answerable only, to herself for her conduct.”\n\nThe doctor accused Mr Allworthy of too great lenity, repeated his accusations against his brother, and declared that he should never more be brought either to see, or to own him for his relation. He then launched forth into a panegyric on Allworthy's goodness; into the highest encomiums on his friendship; and concluded by saying, he should never forgive his brother for having put the place which he bore in that friendship to a hazard.\n\nAllworthy thus answered: “Had I conceived any displeasure against your brother, I should never have carried that resentment to the innocent: but I assure you I have no such displeasure. Your brother appears to me to be a man of sense and honour. I do not disapprove the taste of my sister; nor will I doubt but that she is equally the object of his inclinations. I have always thought love the only foundation of happiness in a married state, as it can only produce that high and tender friendship which should always be the cement of this union; and, in my opinion, all those marriages which are contracted from other motives are greatly criminal; they are a profanation of a most holy ceremony, and generally end in disquiet and misery: for surely we may call it a profanation to convert this most sacred institution into a wicked sacrifice to lust or avarice: and what better can be said of those matches to which men are induced merely by the consideration of a beautiful person, or a great fortune?\n\n“To deny that beauty is an agreeable object to the eye, and even worthy some admiration, would be false and foolish. Beautiful is an epithet often used in Scripture, and always mentioned with honour. It was my own fortune to marry a woman whom the world thought handsome, and I can truly say I liked her the better on that account. But to make this the sole consideration of marriage, to lust after it so violently as to overlook all imperfections for its sake, or to require it so absolutely as to reject and disdain religion, virtue, and sense, which are qualities in their nature of much higher perfection, only because an elegance of person is wanting: this is surely inconsistent, either with a wise man or a good Christian. And it is, perhaps, being too charitable to conclude that such persons mean anything more by their marriage than to please their carnal appetites; for the satisfaction of which, we are taught, it was not ordained.\n\n“In the next place, with respect to fortune. Worldly prudence, perhaps, exacts some consideration on this head; nor will I absolutely and altogether condemn it. As the world is constituted, the demands of a married state, and the care of posterity, require some little regard to what we call circumstances. Yet this provision is greatly increased, beyond what is really necessary, by folly and vanity, which create abundantly more wants than nature. Equipage for the wife, and large fortunes for the children, are by custom enrolled in the list of necessaries; and to procure these, everything truly solid and sweet, and virtuous and religious, are neglected and overlooked.\n\n“And this in many degrees; the last and greatest of which seems scarce distinguishable from madness;--I mean where persons of immense fortunes contract themselves to those who are, and must be, disagreeable to them--to fools and knaves--in order to increase an estate already larger even than the demands of their pleasures. Surely such persons, if they will not be thought mad, must own, either that they are incapable of tasting the sweets of the tenderest friendship, or that they sacrifice the greatest happiness of which they are capable to the vain, uncertain, and senseless laws of vulgar opinion, which owe as well their force as their foundation to folly.”\n\nHere Allworthy concluded his sermon, to which Blifil had listened with the profoundest attention, though it cost him some pains to prevent now and then a small discomposure of his muscles. He now praised every period of what he had heard with the warmth of a young divine, who hath the honour to dine with a bishop the same day in which his lordship hath mounted the pulpit.\n\n# Chapter xiii.\n\nWhich concludes the first book; with an instance of ingratitude, which, we hope, will appear unnatural.\n\nThe reader, from what hath been said, may imagine that the reconciliation (if indeed it could be so called) was only matter of form; we shall therefore pass it over, and hasten to what must surely be thought matter of substance.\n\nThe doctor had acquainted his brother with what had past between Mr Allworthy and him; and added with a smile, “I promise you I paid you off; nay, I absolutely desired the good gentleman not to forgive you: for you know after he had made a declaration in your favour, I might with safety venture on such a request with a person of his temper; and I was willing, as well for your sake as for my own, to prevent the least possibility of a suspicion.”\n\nCaptain Blifil took not the least notice of this, at that time; but he afterwards made a very notable use of it.\n\nOne of the maxims which the devil, in a late visit upon earth, left to his disciples, is, when once you are got up, to kick the stool from under you. In plain English, when you have made your fortune by the good offices of a friend, you are advised to discard him as soon as you can.\n\nWhether the captain acted by this maxim, I will not positively determine: so far we may confidently say, that his actions may be fairly derived from this diabolical principle; and indeed it is difficult to assign any other motive to them: for no sooner was he possessed of Miss Bridget, and reconciled to Allworthy, than he began to show a coldness to his brother which increased daily; till at length it grew into rudeness, and became very visible to every one.\n\nThe doctor remonstrated to him privately concerning this behaviour, but could obtain no other satisfaction than the following plain declaration: “If you dislike anything in my brother's house, sir, you know you are at liberty to quit it.” This strange, cruel, and almost unaccountable ingratitude in the captain, absolutely broke the poor doctor's heart; for ingratitude never so thoroughly pierces the human breast as when it proceeds from those in whose behalf we have been guilty of transgressions. Reflections on great and good actions, however they are received or returned by those in whose favour they are performed, always administer some comfort to us; but what consolation shall we receive under so biting a calamity as the ungrateful behaviour of our friend, when our wounded conscience at the same time flies in our face, and upbraids us with having spotted it in the service of one so worthless!\n\nMr Allworthy himself spoke to the captain in his brother's behalf, and desired to know what offence the doctor had committed; when the hard-hearted villain had the baseness to say that he should never forgive him for the injury which he had endeavoured to do him in his favour; which, he said, he had pumped out of him, and was such a cruelty that it ought not to be forgiven.\n\nAllworthy spoke in very high terms upon this declaration, which, he said, became not a human creature. He expressed, indeed, so much resentment against an unforgiving temper, that the captain at last pretended to be convinced by his arguments, and outwardly professed to be reconciled.\n\nAs for the bride, she was now in her honeymoon, and so passionately fond of her new husband that he never appeared to her to be in the wrong; and his displeasure against any person was a sufficient reason for her dislike to the same.\n\nThe captain, at Mr Allworthy's instance, was outwardly, as we have said, reconciled to his brother; yet the same rancour remained in his heart; and he found so many opportunities of giving him private hints of this, that the house at last grew insupportable to the poor doctor; and he chose rather to submit to any inconveniences which he might encounter in the world, than longer to bear these cruel and ungrateful insults from a brother for whom he had done so much.\n\nHe once intended to acquaint Allworthy with the whole; but he could not bring himself to submit to the confession, by which he must take to his share so great a portion of guilt. Besides, by how much the worse man he represented his brother to be, so much the greater would his own offence appear to Allworthy, and so much the greater, he had reason to imagine, would be his resentment.\n\nHe feigned, therefore, some excuse of business for his departure, and promised to return soon again; and took leave of his brother with so well-dissembled content, that, as the captain played his part to the same perfection, Allworthy remained well satisfied with the truth of the reconciliation.\n\nThe doctor went directly to London, where he died soon after of a broken heart; a distemper which kills many more than is generally imagined, and would have a fair title to a place in the bill of mortality, did it not differ in one instance from all other diseases--viz., that no physician can cure it.\n\nNow, upon the most diligent enquiry into the former lives of these two brothers, I find, besides the cursed and hellish maxim of policy above mentioned, another reason for the captain's conduct: the captain, besides what we have before said of him, was a man of great pride and fierceness, and had always treated his brother, who was of a different complexion, and greatly deficient in both these qualities, with the utmost air of superiority. The doctor, however, had much the larger share of learning, and was by many reputed to have the better understanding. This the captain knew, and could not bear; for though envy is at best a very malignant passion, yet is its bitterness greatly heightened by mixing with contempt towards the same object; and very much afraid I am, that whenever an obligation is joined to these two, indignation and not gratitude will be the product of all three.\n\n# BOOK II.\n\nCONTAINING SCENES OF MATRIMONIAL FELICITY IN DIFFERENT DEGREES OF LIFE; AND VARIOUS OTHER TRANSACTIONS DURING THE FIRST TWO YEARS AFTER THE MARRIAGE BETWEEN CAPTAIN BLIFIL AND MISS BRIDGET ALLWORTHY. Chapter i.\n\nShowing what kind of a history this is; what it is like, and what it is not like.\n\nThough we have properly enough entitled this our work, a history, and not a life; nor an apology for a life, as is more in fashion; yet we intend in it rather to pursue the method of those writers, who profess to disclose the revolutions of countries, than to imitate the painful and voluminous historian, who, to preserve the regularity of his series, thinks himself obliged to fill up as much paper with the detail of months and years in which nothing remarkable happened, as he employs upon those notable aeras when the greatest scenes have been transacted on the human stage.\n\nSuch histories as these do, in reality, very much resemble a newspaper, which consists of just the same number of words, whether there be any news in it or not. They may likewise be compared to a stage coach, which performs constantly the same course, empty as well as full. The writer, indeed, seems to think himself obliged to keep even pace with time, whose amanuensis he is; and, like his master, travels as slowly through centuries of monkish dulness, when the world seems to have been asleep, as through that bright and busy age so nobly distinguished by the excellent Latin poet--\n\nAd confligendum venientibus undique poenis, Omnia cum belli trepido concussa tumultu Horrida contremuere sub altis aetheris auris; In dubioque fuit sub utrorum regna cadendum Omnibus humanis esset, terraque marique.\n\nOf which we wish we could give our readers a more adequate translation than that by Mr Creech--\n\nWhen dreadful Carthage frighted Rome with arms, And all the world was shook with fierce alarms; Whilst undecided yet, which part should fall, Which nation rise the glorious lord of all.\n\nNow it is our purpose, in the ensuing pages, to pursue a contrary method. When any extraordinary scene presents itself (as we trust will often be the case), we shall spare no pains nor paper to open it at large to our reader; but if whole years should pass without producing anything worthy his notice, we shall not be afraid of a chasm in our history; but shall hasten on to matters of consequence, and leave such periods of time totally unobserved.\n\nThese are indeed to be considered as blanks in the grand lottery of time. We therefore, who are the registers of that lottery, shall imitate those sagacious persons who deal in that which is drawn at Guildhall, and who never trouble the public with the many blanks they dispose of; but when a great prize happens to be drawn, the newspapers are presently filled with it, and the world is sure to be informed at whose office it was sold: indeed, commonly two or three different offices lay claim to the honour of having disposed of it; by which, I suppose, the adventurers are given to understand that certain brokers are in the secrets of Fortune, and indeed of her cabinet council.\n\nMy reader then is not to be surprized, if, in the course of this work, he shall find some chapters very short, and others altogether as long; some that contain only the time of a single day, and others that comprise years; in a word, if my history sometimes seems to stand still, and sometimes to fly. For all which I shall not look on myself as accountable to any court of critical jurisdiction whatever: for as I am, in reality, the founder of a new province of writing, so I am at liberty to make what laws I please therein. And these laws, my readers, whom I consider as my subjects, are bound to believe in and to obey; with which that they may readily and cheerfully comply, I do hereby assure them that I shall principally regard their ease and advantage in all such institutions: for I do not, like a jure divino tyrant, imagine that they are my slaves, or my commodity. I am, indeed, set over them for their own good only, and was created for their use, and not they for mine. Nor do I doubt, while I make their interest the great rule of my writings, they will unanimously concur in supporting my dignity, and in rendering me all the honour I shall deserve or desire.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nReligious cautions against showing too much favour to bastards; and a great discovery made by Mrs Deborah Wilkins.\n\nEight months after the celebration of the nuptials between Captain Blifil and Miss Bridget Allworthy, a young lady of great beauty, merit, and fortune, was Miss Bridget, by reason of a fright, delivered of a fine boy. The child was indeed to all appearances perfect; but the midwife discovered it was born a month before its full time.\n\nThough the birth of an heir by his beloved sister was a circumstance of great joy to Mr Allworthy, yet it did not alienate his affections from the little foundling, to whom he had been godfather, had given his own name of Thomas, and whom he had hitherto seldom failed of visiting, at least once a day, in his nursery.\n\nHe told his sister, if she pleased, the new-born infant should be bred up together with little Tommy; to which she consented, though with some little reluctance: for she had truly a great complacence for her brother; and hence she had always behaved towards the foundling with rather more kindness than ladies of rigid virtue can sometimes bring themselves to show to these children, who, however innocent, may be truly called the living monuments of incontinence.\n\nThe captain could not so easily bring himself to bear what he condemned as a fault in Mr Allworthy. He gave him frequent hints, that to adopt the fruits of sin, was to give countenance to it. He quoted several texts (for he was well read in Scripture), such as, He visits the sins of the fathers upon the children; and the fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge,&c. Whence he argued the legality of punishing the crime of the parent on the bastard. He said, “Though the law did not positively allow the destroying such base-born children, yet it held them to be the children of nobody; that the Church considered them as the children of nobody; and that at the best, they ought to be brought up to the lowest and vilest offices of the commonwealth.”\n\nMr Allworthy answered to all this, and much more, which the captain had urged on this subject, “That, however guilty the parents might be, the children were certainly innocent: that as to the texts he had quoted, the former of them was a particular denunciation against the Jews, for the sin of idolatry, of relinquishing and hating their heavenly King; and the latter was parabolically spoken, and rather intended to denote the certain and necessary consequences of sin, than any express judgment against it. But to represent the Almighty as avenging the sins of the guilty on the innocent, was indecent, if not blasphemous, as it was to represent him acting against the first principles of natural justice, and against the original notions of right and wrong, which he himself had implanted in our minds; by which we were to judge not only in all matters which were not revealed, but even of the truth of revelation itself. He said he knew many held the same principles with the captain on this head; but he was himself firmly convinced to the contrary, and would provide in the same manner for this poor infant, as if a legitimate child had had fortune to have been found in the same place.”\n\nWhile the captain was taking all opportunities to press these and such like arguments, to remove the little foundling from Mr Allworthy's, of whose fondness for him he began to be jealous, Mrs Deborah had made a discovery, which, in its event, threatened at least to prove more fatal to poor Tommy than all the reasonings of the captain.\n\nWhether the insatiable curiosity of this good woman had carried her on to that business, or whether she did it to confirm herself in the good graces of Mrs Blifil, who, notwithstanding her outward behaviour to the foundling, frequently abused the infant in private, and her brother too, for his fondness to it, I will not determine; but she had now, as she conceived, fully detected the father of the foundling.\n\nNow, as this was a discovery of great consequence, it may be necessary to trace it from the fountain-head. We shall therefore very minutely lay open those previous matters by which it was produced; and for that purpose we shall be obliged to reveal all the secrets of a little family with which my reader is at present entirely unacquainted; and of which the oeconomy was so rare and extraordinary, that I fear it will shock the utmost credulity of many married persons.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nThe description of a domestic government founded upon rules directly contrary to those of Aristotle.\n\nMy reader may please to remember he hath been informed that Jenny Jones had lived some years with a certain schoolmaster, who had, at her earnest desire, instructed her in Latin, in which, to do justice to her genius, she had so improved herself, that she was become a better scholar than her master.\n\nIndeed, though this poor man had undertaken a profession to which learning must be allowed necessary, this was the least of his commendations. He was one of the best-natured fellows in the world, and was, at the same time, master of so much pleasantry and humour, that he was reputed the wit of the country; and all the neighbouring gentlemen were so desirous of his company, that as denying was not his talent, he spent much time at their houses, which he might, with more emolument, have spent in his school.\n\nIt may be imagined that a gentleman so qualified and so disposed, was in no danger of becoming formidable to the learned seminaries of Eton or Westminster. To speak plainly, his scholars were divided into two classes: in the upper of which was a young gentleman, the son of a neighbouring squire, who, at the age of seventeen, was just entered into his Syntaxis; and in the lower was a second son of the same gentleman, who, together with seven parish-boys, was learning to read and write.\n\nThe stipend arising hence would hardly have indulged the schoolmaster in the luxuries of life, had he not added to this office those of clerk and barber, and had not Mr Allworthy added to the whole an annuity of ten pounds, which the poor man received every Christmas, and with which he was enabled to cheer his heart during that sacred festival.\n\nAmong his other treasures, the pedagogue had a wife, whom he had married out of Mr Allworthy's kitchen for her fortune, viz., twenty pounds, which she had there amassed.\n\nThis woman was not very amiable in her person. Whether she sat to my friend Hogarth, or no, I will not determine; but she exactly resembled the young woman who is pouring out her mistress's tea in the third picture of the Harlot's Progress. She was, besides, a profest follower of that noble sect founded by Xantippe of old; by means of which she became more formidable in the school than her husband; for, to confess the truth, he was never master there, or anywhere else, in her presence.\n\nThough her countenance did not denote much natural sweetness of temper, yet this was, perhaps, somewhat soured by a circumstance which generally poisons matrimonial felicity; for children are rightly called the pledges of love; and her husband, though they had been married nine years, had given her no such pledges; a default for which he had no excuse, either from age or health, being not yet thirty years old, and what they call a jolly brisk young man.\n\nHence arose another evil, which produced no little uneasiness to the poor pedagogue, of whom she maintained so constant a jealousy, that he durst hardly speak to one woman in the parish; for the least degree of civility, or even correspondence, with any female, was sure to bring his wife upon her back, and his own.\n\nIn order to guard herself against matrimonial injuries in her own house, as she kept one maid-servant, she always took care to chuse her out of that order of females whose faces are taken as a kind of security for their virtue; of which number Jenny Jones, as the reader hath been before informed, was one.\n\nAs the face of this young woman might be called pretty good security of the before-mentioned kind, and as her behaviour had been always extremely modest, which is the certain consequence of understanding in women; she had passed above four years at Mr Partridge's (for that was the schoolmaster's name) without creating the least suspicion in her mistress. Nay, she had been treated with uncommon kindness, and her mistress had permitted Mr Partridge to give her those instructions which have been before commemorated.\n\nBut it is with jealousy as with the gout: when such distempers are in the blood, there is never any security against their breaking out; and that often on the slightest occasions, and when least suspected.\n\nThus it happened to Mrs Partridge, who had submitted four years to her husband's teaching this young woman, and had suffered her often to neglect her work, in order to pursue her learning. For, passing by one day, as the girl was reading, and her master leaning over her, the girl, I know not for what reason, suddenly started up from her chair: and this was the first time that suspicion ever entered into the head of her mistress. This did not, however, at that time discover itself, but lay lurking in her mind, like a concealed enemy, who waits for a reinforcement of additional strength before he openly declares himself and proceeds upon hostile operations: and such additional strength soon arrived to corroborate her suspicion; for not long after, the husband and wife being at dinner, the master said to his maid, Da mihi aliquid potum: upon which the poor girl smiled, perhaps at the badness of the Latin, and, when her mistress cast her eyes on her, blushed, possibly with a consciousness of having laughed at her master. Mrs Partridge, upon this, immediately fell into a fury, and discharged the trencher on which she was eating, at the head of poor Jenny, crying out, “You impudent whore, do you play tricks with my husband before my face?” and at the same instant rose from her chair with a knife in her hand, with which, most probably, she would have executed very tragical vengeance, had not the girl taken the advantage of being nearer the door than her mistress, and avoided her fury by running away: for, as to the poor husband, whether surprize had rendered him motionless, or fear (which is full as probable) had restrained him from venturing at any opposition, he sat staring and trembling in his chair; nor did he once offer to move or speak, till his wife, returning from the pursuit of Jenny, made some defensive measures necessary for his own preservation; and he likewise was obliged to retreat, after the example of the maid.\n\nThis good woman was, no more than Othello, of a disposition\n\nTo make a life of jealousy And follow still the changes of the moon With fresh suspicions--\n\nWith her, as well as him,\n\n--To be once in doubt, Was once to be resolvd--\n\nshe therefore ordered Jenny immediately to pack up her alls and begone, for that she was determined she should not sleep that night within her walls.\n\nMr Partridge had profited too much by experience to interpose in a matter of this nature. He therefore had recourse to his usual receipt of patience, for, though he was not a great adept in Latin, he remembered, and well understood, the advice contained in these words\n\n--Leve fit quod bene fertur onus\n\nin English:\n\nA burden becomes lightest when it is well borne--\n\nwhich he had always in his mouth; and of which, to say the truth, he had often occasion to experience the truth.\n\nJenny offered to make protestations of her innocence; but the tempest was too strong for her to be heard. She then betook herself to the business of packing, for which a small quantity of brown paper sufficed, and, having received her small pittance of wages, she returned home.\n\nThe schoolmaster and his consort passed their time unpleasantly enough that evening, but something or other happened before the next morning, which a little abated the fury of Mrs Partridge; and she at length admitted her husband to make his excuses: to which she gave the readier belief, as he had, instead of desiring her to recall Jenny, professed a satisfaction in her being dismissed, saying, she was grown of little use as a servant, spending all her time in reading, and was become, moreover, very pert and obstinate; for, indeed, she and her master had lately had frequent disputes in literature; in which, as hath been said, she was become greatly his superior. This, however, he would by no means allow; and as he called her persisting in the right, obstinacy, he began to hate her with no small inveteracy.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nContaining one of the most bloody battles, or rather duels, that were ever recorded in domestic history.\nFor the reasons mentioned in the preceding chapter, and from some other matrimonial concessions, well known to most husbands, and which, like the secrets of freemasonry, should be divulged to none who are not members of that honourable fraternity, Mrs Partridge was pretty well satisfied that she had condemned her husband without cause, and endeavoured by acts of kindness to make him amends for her false suspicion. Her passions were indeed equally violent, whichever way they inclined; for as she could be extremely angry, so could she be altogether as fond.\n\nBut though these passions ordinarily succeed each other, and scarce twenty-four hours ever passed in which the pedagogue was not, in some degree, the object of both; yet, on extraordinary occasions, when the passion of anger had raged very high, the remission was usually longer: and so was the case at present; for she continued longer in a state of affability, after this fit of jealousy was ended, than her husband had ever known before: and, had it not been for some little exercises, which all the followers of Xantippe are obliged to perform daily, Mr Partridge would have enjoyed a perfect serenity of several months.\n\nPerfect calms at sea are always suspected by the experienced mariner to be the forerunners of a storm, and I know some persons, who, without being generally the devotees of superstition, are apt to apprehend that great and unusual peace or tranquillity will be attended with its opposite. For which reason the antients used, on such occasions, to sacrifice to the goddess Nemesis, a deity who was thought by them to look with an invidious eye on human felicity, and to have a peculiar delight in overturning it.\n\nAs we are very far from believing in any such heathen goddess, or from encouraging any superstition, so we wish Mr John Fr----, or some other such philosopher, would bestir himself a little, in order to find out the real cause of this sudden transition from good to bad fortune, which hath been so often remarked, and of which we shall proceed to give an instance; for it is our province to relate facts, and we shall leave causes to persons of much higher genius.\n\nMankind have always taken great delight in knowing and descanting on the actions of others. Hence there have been, in all ages and nations, certain places set apart for public rendezvous, where the curious might meet and satisfy their mutual curiosity. Among these, the barbers' shops have justly borne the pre-eminence. Among the Greeks, barbers' news was a proverbial expression; and Horace, in one of his epistles, makes honourable mention of the Roman barbers in the same light.\n\nThose of England are known to be no wise inferior to their Greek or Roman predecessors. You there see foreign affairs discussed in a manner little inferior to that with which they are handled in the coffee-houses; and domestic occurrences are much more largely and freely treated in the former than in the latter. But this serves only for the men. Now, whereas the females of this country, especially those of the lower order, do associate themselves much more than those of other nations, our polity would be highly deficient, if they had not some place set apart likewise for the indulgence of their curiosity, seeing they are in this no way inferior to the other half of the species.\n\nIn enjoying, therefore, such place of rendezvous, the British fair ought to esteem themselves more happy than any of their foreign sisters; as I do not remember either to have read in history, or to have seen in my travels, anything of the like kind.\n\nThis place then is no other than the chandler's shop, the known seat of all the news; or, as it is vulgarly called, gossiping, in every parish in England.\n\nMrs Partridge being one day at this assembly of females, was asked by one of her neighbours, if she had heard no news lately of Jenny Jones? To which she answered in the negative. Upon this the other replied, with a smile, That the parish was very much obliged to her for having turned Jenny away as she did.\n\nMrs Partridge, whose jealousy, as the reader well knows, was long since cured, and who had no other quarrel to her maid, answered boldly, She did not know any obligation the parish had to her on that account; for she believed Jenny had scarce left her equal behind her.\n\n“No, truly,” said the gossip, “I hope not, though I fancy we have sluts enow too. Then you have not heard, it seems, that she hath been brought to bed of two bastards? but as they are not born here, my husband and the other overseer says we shall not be obliged to keep them.”\n\n“Two bastards!” answered Mrs Partridge hastily: “you surprize me! I don't know whether we must keep them; but I am sure they must have been begotten here, for the wench hath not been nine months gone away.”\n\nNothing can be so quick and sudden as the operations of the mind, especially when hope, or fear, or jealousy, to which the two others are but journeymen, set it to work. It occurred instantly to her, that Jenny had scarce ever been out of her own house while she lived with her. The leaning over the chair, the sudden starting up, the Latin, the smile, and many other things, rushed upon her all at once. The satisfaction her husband expressed in the departure of Jenny, appeared now to be only dissembled; again, in the same instant, to be real; but yet to confirm her jealousy, proceeding from satiety, and a hundred other bad causes. In a word, she was convinced of her husband's guilt, and immediately left the assembly in confusion.\n\nAs fair Grimalkin, who, though the youngest of the feline family, degenerates not in ferocity from the elder branches of her house, and though inferior in strength, is equal in fierceness to the noble tiger himself, when a little mouse, whom it hath long tormented in sport, escapes from her clutches for a while, frets, scolds, growls, swears; but if the trunk, or box, behind which the mouse lay hid be again removed, she flies like lightning on her prey, and, with envenomed wrath, bites, scratches, mumbles, and tears the little animal.\n\nNot with less fury did Mrs Partridge fly on the poor pedagogue. Her tongue, teeth, and hands, fell all upon him at once. His wig was in an instant torn from his head, his shirt from his back, and from his face descended five streams of blood, denoting the number of claws with which nature had unhappily armed the enemy.\n\nMr Partridge acted for some time on the defensive only; indeed he attempted only to guard his face with his hands; but as he found that his antagonist abated nothing of her rage, he thought he might, at least, endeavour to disarm her, or rather to confine her arms; in doing which her cap fell off in the struggle, and her hair being too short to reach her shoulders, erected itself on her head; her stays likewise, which were laced through one single hole at the bottom, burst open; and her breasts, which were much more redundant than her hair, hung down below her middle; her face was likewise marked with the blood of her husband: her teeth gnashed with rage; and fire, such as sparkles from a smith's forge, darted from her eyes. So that, altogether, this Amazonian heroine might have been an object of terror to a much bolder man than Mr Partridge.\n\nHe had, at length, the good fortune, by getting possession of her arms, to render those weapons which she wore at the ends of her fingers useless; which she no sooner perceived, than the softness of her sex prevailed over her rage, and she presently dissolved in tears, which soon after concluded in a fit.\n\nThat small share of sense which Mr Partridge had hitherto preserved through this scene of fury, of the cause of which he was hitherto ignorant, now utterly abandoned him. He ran instantly into the street, hallowing out that his wife was in the agonies of death, and beseeching the neighbours to fly with the utmost haste to her assistance. Several good women obeyed his summons, who entering his house, and applying the usual remedies on such occasions, Mrs Partridge was at length, to the great joy of her husband, brought to herself.\n\nAs soon as she had a little recollected her spirits, and somewhat composed herself with a cordial, she began to inform the company of the manifold injuries she had received from her husband; who, she said, was not contented to injure her in her bed; but, upon her upbraiding him with it, had treated her in the cruelest manner imaginable; had tore her cap and hair from her head, and her stays from her body, giving her, at the same time, several blows, the marks of which she should carry to the grave.\n\nThe poor man, who bore on his face many more visible marks of the indignation of his wife, stood in silent astonishment at this accusation; which the reader will, I believe, bear witness for him, had greatly exceeded the truth; for indeed he had not struck her once; and this silence being interpreted to be a confession of the charge by the whole court, they all began at once, una voce, to rebuke and revile him, repeating often, that none but a coward ever struck a woman.\n\nMr Partridge bore all this patiently; but when his wife appealed to the blood on her face, as an evidence of his barbarity, he could not help laying claim to his own blood, for so it really was; as he thought it very unnatural, that this should rise up (as we are taught that of a murdered person often doth) in vengeance against him.\n\nTo this the women made no other answer, than that it was a pity it had not come from his heart, instead of his face; all declaring, that, if their husbands should lift their hands against them, they would have their hearts' bloods out of their bodies.\n\nAfter much admonition for what was past, and much good advice to Mr Partridge for his future behaviour, the company at length departed, and left the husband and wife to a personal conference together, in which Mr Partridge soon learned the cause of all his sufferings.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nContaining much matter to exercise the judgment and reflection of the reader.\n\nI believe it is a true observation, that few secrets are divulged to one person only; but certainly, it would be next to a miracle that a fact of this kind should be known to a whole parish, and not transpire any farther.\n\nAnd, indeed, a very few days had past, before the country, to use a common phrase, rung of the schoolmaster of Little Baddington; who was said to have beaten his wife in the most cruel manner. Nay, in some places it was reported he had murdered her; in others, that he had broke her arms; in others, her legs: in short, there was scarce an injury which can be done to a human creature, but what Mrs Partridge was somewhere or other affirmed to have received from her husband.\n\nThe cause of this quarrel was likewise variously reported; for as some people said that Mrs Partridge had caught her husband in bed with his maid, so many other reasons, of a very different kind, went abroad. Nay, some transferred the guilt to the wife, and the jealousy to the husband.\n\nMrs Wilkins had long ago heard of this quarrel; but, as a different cause from the true one had reached her ears, she thought proper to conceal it; and the rather, perhaps, as the blame was universally laid on Mr Partridge; and his wife, when she was servant to Mr Allworthy, had in something offended Mrs Wilkins, who was not of a very forgiving temper.\n\nBut Mrs Wilkins, whose eyes could see objects at a distance, and who could very well look forward a few years into futurity, had perceived a strong likelihood of Captain Blifil's being hereafter her master; and as she plainly discerned that the captain bore no great goodwill to the little foundling, she fancied it would be rendering him an agreeable service, if she could make any discoveries that might lessen the affection which Mr Allworthy seemed to have contracted for this child, and which gave visible uneasiness to the captain, who could not entirely conceal it even before Allworthy himself; though his wife, who acted her part much better in public, frequently recommended to him her own example, of conniving at the folly of her brother, which, she said, she at least as well perceived, and as much resented, as any other possibly could.\n\nMrs Wilkins having therefore, by accident, gotten a true scent of the above story,--though long after it had happened, failed not to satisfy herself thoroughly of all the particulars; and then acquainted the captain, that she had at last discovered the true father of the little bastard, which she was sorry, she said, to see her master lose his reputation in the country, by taking so much notice of.\n\nThe captain chid her for the conclusion of her speech, as an improper assurance in judging of her master's actions: for if his honour, or his understanding, would have suffered the captain to make an alliance with Mrs Wilkins, his pride would by no means have admitted it. And to say the truth, there is no conduct less politic, than to enter into any confederacy with your friend's servants against their master: for by these means you afterwards become the slave of these very servants; by whom you are constantly liable to be betrayed. And this consideration, perhaps it was, which prevented Captain Blifil from being more explicit with Mrs Wilkins, or from encouraging the abuse which she had bestowed on Allworthy.\n\nBut though he declared no satisfaction to Mrs Wilkins at this discovery, he enjoyed not a little from it in his own mind, and resolved to make the best use of it he was able.\n\nHe kept this matter a long time concealed within his own breast, in hopes that Mr Allworthy might hear it from some other person; but Mrs Wilkins, whether she resented the captain's behaviour, or whether his cunning was beyond her, and she feared the discovery might displease him, never afterwards opened her lips about the matter.\n\nI have thought it somewhat strange, upon reflection, that the housekeeper never acquainted Mrs Blifil with this news, as women are more inclined to communicate all pieces of intelligence to their own sex, than to ours. The only way, as it appears to me, of solving this difficulty, is, by imputing it to that distance which was now grown between the lady and the housekeeper: whether this arose from a jealousy in Mrs Blifil, that Wilkins showed too great a respect to the foundling; for while she was endeavouring to ruin the little infant, in order to ingratiate herself with the captain, she was every day more and more commending it before Allworthy, as his fondness for it every day increased. This, notwithstanding all the care she took at other times to express the direct contrary to Mrs Blifil, perhaps offended that delicate lady, who certainly now hated Mrs Wilkins; and though she did not, or possibly could not, absolutely remove her from her place, she found, however, the means of making her life very uneasy. This Mrs Wilkins, at length, so resented, that she very openly showed all manner of respect and fondness to little Tommy, in opposition to Mrs Blifil.\n\nThe captain, therefore, finding the story in danger of perishing, at last took an opportunity to reveal it himself.\n\nHe was one day engaged with Mr Allworthy in a discourse on charity: in which the captain, with great learning, proved to Mr Allworthy, that the word charity in Scripture nowhere means beneficence or generosity.\n\n“The Christian religion,” he said, “was instituted for much nobler purposes, than to enforce a lesson which many heathen philosophers had taught us long before, and which, though it might perhaps be called a moral virtue, savoured but little of that sublime, Christian-like disposition, that vast elevation of thought, in purity approaching to angelic perfection, to be attained, expressed, and felt only by grace. Those,” he said, “came nearer to the Scripture meaning, who understood by it candour, or the forming of a benevolent opinion of our brethren, and passing a favourable judgment on their actions; a virtue much higher, and more extensive in its nature, than a pitiful distribution of alms, which, though we would never so much prejudice, or even ruin our families, could never reach many; whereas charity, in the other and truer sense, might be extended to all mankind.”\n\nHe said, “Considering who the disciples were, it would be absurd to conceive the doctrine of generosity, or giving alms, to have been preached to them. And, as we could not well imagine this doctrine should be preached by its Divine Author to men who could not practise it, much less should we think it understood so by those who can practise it, and do not.\n\n“But though,” continued he, “there is, I am afraid, little merit in these benefactions, there would, I must confess, be much pleasure in them to a good mind, if it was not abated by one consideration. I mean, that we are liable to be imposed upon, and to confer our choicest favours often on the undeserving, as you must own was your case in your bounty to that worthless fellow Partridge: for two or three such examples must greatly lessen the inward satisfaction which a good man would otherwise find in generosity; nay, may even make him timorous in bestowing, lest he should be guilty of supporting vice, and encouraging the wicked; a crime of a very black dye, and for which it will by no means be a sufficient excuse, that we have not actually intended such an encouragement; unless we have used the utmost caution in chusing the objects of our beneficence. A consideration which, I make no doubt, hath greatly checked the liberality of many a worthy and pious man.”\n\nMr Allworthy answered, “He could not dispute with the captain in the Greek language, and therefore could say nothing as to the true sense of the word which is translated charity; but that he had always thought it was interpreted to consist in action, and that giving alms constituted at least one branch of that virtue.\n\n“As to the meritorious part,” he said, “he readily agreed with the captain; for where could be the merit of barely discharging a duty? which,” he said, “let the word charity have what construction it would, it sufficiently appeared to be from the whole tenor of the New Testament. And as he thought it an indispensable duty, enjoined both by the Christian law, and by the law of nature itself; so was it withal so pleasant, that if any duty could be said to be its own reward, or to pay us while we are discharging it, it was this.\n\n“To confess the truth,” said he, “there is one degree of generosity (of charity I would have called it), which seems to have some show of merit, and that is, where, from a principle of benevolence and Christian love, we bestow on another what we really want ourselves; where, in order to lessen the distresses of another, we condescend to share some part of them, by giving what even our own necessities cannot well spare. This is, I think, meritorious; but to relieve our brethren only with our superfluities; to be charitable (I must use the word) rather at the expense of our coffers than ourselves; to save several families from misery rather than hang up an extraordinary picture in our houses or gratify any other idle ridiculous vanity--this seems to be only being human creatures. Nay, I will venture to go farther, it is being in some degree epicures: for what could the greatest epicure wish rather than to eat with many mouths instead of one? which I think may be predicated of any one who knows that the bread of many is owing to his own largesses.\n\n“As to the apprehension of bestowing bounty on such as may hereafter prove unworthy objects, because many have proved such; surely it can never deter a good man from generosity. I do not think a few or many examples of ingratitude can justify a man's hardening his heart against the distresses of his fellow-creatures; nor do I believe it can ever have such effect on a truly benevolent mind. Nothing less than a persuasion of universal depravity can lock up the charity of a good man; and this persuasion must lead him, I think, either into atheism, or enthusiasm; but surely it is unfair to argue such universal depravity from a few vicious individuals; nor was this, I believe, ever done by a man, who, upon searching his own mind, found one certain exception to the general rule.” He then concluded by asking, “who that Partridge was, whom he had called a worthless fellow?”\n\n“I mean,” said the captain, “Partridge the barber, the schoolmaster, what do you call him? Partridge, the father of the little child which you found in your bed.”\n\nMr Allworthy exprest great surprize at this account, and the captain as great at his ignorance of it; for he said he had known it above a month: and at length recollected with much difficulty that he was told it by Mrs Wilkins.\n\nUpon this, Wilkins was immediately summoned; who having confirmed what the captain had said, was by Mr Allworthy, by and with the captain's advice, dispatched to Little Baddington, to inform herself of the truth of the fact: for the captain exprest great dislike at all hasty proceedings in criminal matters, and said he would by no means have Mr Allworthy take any resolution either to the prejudice of the child or its father, before he was satisfied that the latter was guilty; for though he had privately satisfied himself of this from one of Partridge's neighbours, yet he was too generous to give any such evidence to Mr Allworthy.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nThe trial of Partridge, the schoolmaster, for incontinency; the evidence of his wife; a short reflection on the wisdom of our law; with other grave matters, which those will like best who understand them most.\n\nIt may be wondered that a story so well known, and which had furnished so much matter of conversation, should never have been mentioned to Mr Allworthy himself, who was perhaps the only person in that country who had never heard of it.\n\nTo account in some measure for this to the reader, I think proper to inform him, that there was no one in the kingdom less interested in opposing that doctrine concerning the meaning of the word charity, which hath been seen in the preceding chapter, than our good man. Indeed, he was equally intitled to this virtue in either sense; for as no man was ever more sensible of the wants, or more ready to relieve the distresses of others, so none could be more tender of their characters, or slower to believe anything to their disadvantage.\n\nScandal, therefore, never found any access to his table; for as it hath been long since observed that you may know a man by his companions, so I will venture to say, that, by attending to the conversation at a great man's table, you may satisfy yourself of his religion, his politics, his taste, and indeed of his entire disposition: for though a few odd fellows will utter their own sentiments in all places, yet much the greater part of mankind have enough of the courtier to accommodate their conversation to the taste and inclination of their superiors.\n\nBut to return to Mrs Wilkins, who, having executed her commission with great dispatch, though at fifteen miles distance, brought back such a confirmation of the schoolmaster's guilt, that Mr Allworthy determined to send for the criminal, and examine him viva voce. Mr Partridge, therefore, was summoned to attend, in order to his defence (if he could make any) against this accusation.\n\nAt the time appointed, before Mr Allworthy himself, at Paradise-hall, came as well the said Partridge, with Anne, his wife, as Mrs Wilkins his accuser.\n\nAnd now Mr Allworthy being seated in the chair of justice, Mr Partridge was brought before him. Having heard his accusation from the mouth of Mrs Wilkins, he pleaded not guilty, making many vehement protestations of his innocence.\n\nMrs Partridge was then examined, who, after a modest apology for being obliged to speak the truth against her husband, related all the circumstances with which the reader hath already been acquainted; and at last concluded with her husband's confession of his guilt.\n\nWhether she had forgiven him or no, I will not venture to determine; but it is certain she was an unwilling witness in this cause; and it is probable from certain other reasons, would never have been brought to depose as she did, had not Mrs Wilkins, with great art, fished all out of her at her own house, and had she not indeed made promises, in Mr Allworthy's name, that the punishment of her husband should not be such as might anywise affect his family.\n\nPartridge still persisted in asserting his innocence, though he admitted he had made the above-mentioned confession; which he however endeavoured to account for, by protesting that he was forced into it by the continued importunity she used: who vowed, that, as she was sure of his guilt, she would never leave tormenting him till he had owned it; and faithfully promised, that, in such case, she would never mention it to him more. Hence, he said, he had been induced falsely to confess himself guilty, though he was innocent; and that he believed he should have confest a murder from the same motive.\n\nMrs Partridge could not bear this imputation with patience; and having no other remedy in the present place but tears, she called forth a plentiful assistance from them, and then addressing herself to Mr Allworthy, she said (or rather cried), “May it please your worship, there never was any poor woman so injured as I am by that base man; for this is not the only instance of his falsehood to me. No, may it please your worship, he hath injured my bed many's the good time and often. I could have put up with his drunkenness and neglect of his business, if he had not broke one of the sacred commandments. Besides, if it had been out of doors I had not mattered it so much; but with my own servant, in my own house, under my own roof, to defile my own chaste bed, which to be sure he hath, with his beastly stinking whores. Yes, you villain, you have defiled my own bed, you have; and then you have charged me with bullocking you into owning the truth. It is very likely, an't please your worship, that I should bullock him? I have marks enow about my body to show of his cruelty to me. If you had been a man, you villain, you would have scorned to injure a woman in that manner. But you an't half a man, you know it. Nor have you been half a husband to me. You need run after whores, you need, when I'm sure--And since he provokes me, I am ready, an't please your worship, to take my bodily oath that I found them a-bed together. What, you have forgot, I suppose, when you beat me into a fit, and made the blood run down my forehead, because I only civilly taxed you with adultery! but I can prove it by all my neighbours. You have almost broke my heart, you have, you have.”\n\nHere Mr Allworthy interrupted, and begged her to be pacified, promising her that she should have justice; then turning to Partridge, who stood aghast, one half of his wits being hurried away by surprize and the other half by fear, he said he was sorry to see there was so wicked a man in the world. He assured him that his prevaricating and lying backward and forward was a great aggravation of his guilt; for which the only atonement he could make was by confession and repentance. He exhorted him, therefore, to begin by immediately confessing the fact, and not to persist in denying what was so plainly proved against him even by his own wife.\n\nHere, reader, I beg your patience a moment, while I make a just compliment to the great wisdom and sagacity of our law, which refuses to admit the evidence of a wife for or against her husband. This, says a certain learned author, who, I believe, was never quoted before in any but a law-book, would be the means of creating an eternal dissension between them. It would, indeed, be the means of much perjury, and of much whipping, fining, imprisoning, transporting, and hanging.\n\nPartridge stood a while silent, till, being bid to speak, he said he had already spoken the truth, and appealed to Heaven for his innocence, and lastly to the girl herself, whom he desired his worship immediately to send for; for he was ignorant, or at least pretended to be so, that she had left that part of the country.\n\nMr Allworthy, whose natural love of justice, joined to his coolness of temper, made him always a most patient magistrate in hearing all the witnesses which an accused person could produce in his defence, agreed to defer his final determination of this matter till the arrival of Jenny, for whom he immediately dispatched a messenger; and then having recommended peace between Partridge and his wife (though he addressed himself chiefly to the wrong person), he appointed them to attend again the third day; for he had sent Jenny a whole day's journey from his own house.\n\nAt the appointed time the parties all assembled, when the messenger returning brought word, that Jenny was not to be found; for that she had left her habitation a few days before, in company with a recruiting officer.\n\nMr Allworthy then declared that the evidence of such a slut as she appeared to be would have deserved no credit; but he said he could not help thinking that, had she been present, and would have declared the truth, she must have confirmed what so many circumstances, together with his own confession, and the declaration of his wife that she had caught her husband in the fact, did sufficiently prove. He therefore once more exhorted Partridge to confess; but he still avowing his innocence, Mr Allworthy declared himself satisfied of his guilt, and that he was too bad a man to receive any encouragement from him. He therefore deprived him of his annuity, and recommended repentance to him on account of another world, and industry to maintain himself and his wife in this.\n\nThere were not, perhaps, many more unhappy persons than poor Partridge. He had lost the best part of his income by the evidence of his wife, and yet was daily upbraided by her for having, among other things, been the occasion of depriving her of that benefit; but such was his fortune, and he was obliged to submit to it.\n\nThough I called him poor Partridge in the last paragraph, I would have the reader rather impute that epithet to the compassion in my temper than conceive it to be any declaration of his innocence. Whether he was innocent or not will perhaps appear hereafter; but if the historic muse hath entrusted me with any secrets, I will by no means be guilty of discovering them till she shall give me leave.\n\nHere therefore the reader must suspend his curiosity. Certain it is that, whatever was the truth of the case, there was evidence more than sufficient to convict him before Allworthy; indeed, much less would have satisfied a bench of justices on an order of bastardy; and yet, notwithstanding the positiveness of Mrs Partridge, who would have taken the sacrament upon the matter, there is a possibility that the schoolmaster was entirely innocent: for though it appeared clear on comparing the time when Jenny departed from Little Baddington with that of her delivery that she had there conceived this infant, yet it by no means followed of necessity that Partridge must have been its father; for, to omit other particulars, there was in the same house a lad near eighteen, between whom and Jenny there had subsisted sufficient intimacy to found a reasonable suspicion; and yet, so blind is jealousy, this circumstance never once entered into the head of the enraged wife.\n\nWhether Partridge repented or not, according to Mr Allworthy's advice, is not so apparent. Certain it is that his wife repented heartily of the evidence she had given against him: especially when she found Mrs Deborah had deceived her, and refused to make any application to Mr Allworthy on her behalf. She had, however, somewhat better success with Mrs Blifil, who was, as the reader must have perceived, a much better-tempered woman, and very kindly undertook to solicit her brother to restore the annuity; in which, though good-nature might have some share, yet a stronger and more natural motive will appear in the next chapter.\n\nThese solicitations were nevertheless unsuccessful: for though Mr Allworthy did not think, with some late writers, that mercy consists only in punishing offenders; yet he was as far from thinking that it is proper to this excellent quality to pardon great criminals wantonly, without any reason whatever. Any doubtfulness of the fact, or any circumstance of mitigation, was never disregarded: but the petitions of an offender, or the intercessions of others, did not in the least affect him. In a word, he never pardoned because the offender himself, or his friends, were unwilling that he should be punished.\n\nPartridge and his wife were therefore both obliged to submit to their fate; which was indeed severe enough: for so far was he from doubling his industry on the account of his lessened income, that he did in a manner abandon himself to despair; and as he was by nature indolent, that vice now increased upon him, by which means he lost the little school he had; so that neither his wife nor himself would have had any bread to eat, had not the charity of some good Christian interposed, and provided them with what was just sufficient for their sustenance.\n\nAs this support was conveyed to them by an unknown hand, they imagined, and so, I doubt not, will the reader, that Mr Allworthy himself was their secret benefactor; who, though he would not openly encourage vice, could yet privately relieve the distresses of the vicious themselves, when these became too exquisite and disproportionate to their demerit. In which light their wretchedness appeared now to Fortune herself; for she at length took pity on this miserable couple, and considerably lessened the wretched state of Partridge, by putting a final end to that of his wife, who soon after caught the small-pox, and died.\n\nThe justice which Mr Allworthy had executed on Partridge at first met with universal approbation; but no sooner had he felt its consequences, than his neighbours began to relent, and to compassionate his case; and presently after, to blame that as rigour and severity which they before called justice. They now exclaimed against punishing in cold blood, and sang forth the praises of mercy and forgiveness.\n\nThese cries were considerably increased by the death of Mrs Partridge, which, though owing to the distemper above mentioned, which is no consequence of poverty or distress, many were not ashamed to impute to Mr Allworthy's severity, or, as they now termed it, cruelty.\n\nPartridge having now lost his wife, his school, and his annuity, and the unknown person having now discontinued the last-mentioned charity, resolved to change the scene, and left the country, where he was in danger of starving, with the universal compassion of all his neighbours.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nA short sketch of that felicity which prudent couples may extract from hatred: with a short apology for those people who overlook imperfections in their friends.\n\nThough the captain had effectually demolished poor Partridge, yet had he not reaped the harvest he hoped for, which was to turn the foundling out of Mr Allworthy's house.\n\nOn the contrary, that gentleman grew every day fonder of little Tommy, as if he intended to counterbalance his severity to the father with extraordinary fondness and affection towards the son.\n\nThis a good deal soured the captain's temper, as did all the other daily instances of Mr Allworthy's generosity; for he looked on all such largesses to be diminutions of his own wealth.\n\nIn this, we have said, he did not agree with his wife; nor, indeed, in anything else: for though an affection placed on the understanding is, by many wise persons, thought more durable than that which is founded on beauty, yet it happened otherwise in the present case. Nay, the understandings of this couple were their principal bone of contention, and one great cause of many quarrels, which from time to time arose between them; and which at last ended, on the side of the lady, in a sovereign contempt for her husband; and on the husband's, in an utter abhorrence of his wife.\n\nAs these had both exercised their talents chiefly in the study of divinity, this was, from their first acquaintance, the most common topic of conversation between them. The captain, like a well-bred man, had, before marriage, always given up his opinion to that of the lady; and this, not in the clumsy awkward manner of a conceited blockhead, who, while he civilly yields to a superior in an argument, is desirous of being still known to think himself in the right. The captain, on the contrary, though one of the proudest fellows in the world, so absolutely yielded the victory to his antagonist, that she, who had not the least doubt of his sincerity, retired always from the dispute with an admiration of her own understanding and a love for his.\n\nBut though this complacence to one whom the captain thoroughly despised, was not so uneasy to him as it would have been had any hopes of preferment made it necessary to show the same submission to a Hoadley, or to some other of great reputation in the science, yet even this cost him too much to be endured without some motive. Matrimony, therefore, having removed all such motives, he grew weary of this condescension, and began to treat the opinions of his wife with that haughtiness and insolence, which none but those who deserve some contempt themselves can bestow, and those only who deserve no contempt can bear.\n\nWhen the first torrent of tenderness was over, and when, in the calm and long interval between the fits, reason began to open the eyes of the lady, and she saw this alteration of behaviour in the captain, who at length answered all her arguments only with pish and pshaw, she was far from enduring the indignity with a tame submission. Indeed, it at first so highly provoked her, that it might have produced some tragical event, had it not taken a more harmless turn, by filling her with the utmost contempt for her husband's understanding, which somewhat qualified her hatred towards him; though of this likewise she had a pretty moderate share.\n\nThe captain's hatred to her was of a purer kind: for as to any imperfections in her knowledge or understanding, he no more despised her for them, than for her not being six feet high. In his opinion of the female sex, he exceeded the moroseness of Aristotle himself: he looked on a woman as on an animal of domestic use, of somewhat higher consideration than a cat, since her offices were of rather more importance; but the difference between these two was, in his estimation, so small, that, in his marriage contracted with Mr Allworthy's lands and tenements, it would have been pretty equal which of them he had taken into the bargain. And yet so tender was his pride, that it felt the contempt which his wife now began to express towards him; and this, added to the surfeit he had before taken of her love, created in him a degree of disgust and abhorrence, perhaps hardly to be exceeded.\n\nOne situation only of the married state is excluded from pleasure: and that is, a state of indifference: but as many of my readers, I hope, know what an exquisite delight there is in conveying pleasure to a beloved object, so some few, I am afraid, may have experienced the satisfaction of tormenting one we hate. It is, I apprehend, to come at this latter pleasure, that we see both sexes often give up that ease in marriage which they might otherwise possess, though their mate was never so disagreeable to them. Hence the wife often puts on fits of love and jealousy, nay, even denies herself any pleasure, to disturb and prevent those of her husband; and he again, in return, puts frequent restraints on himself, and stays at home in company which he dislikes, in order to confine his wife to what she equally detests. Hence, too, must flow those tears which a widow sometimes so plentifully sheds over the ashes of a husband with whom she led a life of constant disquiet and turbulency, and whom now she can never hope to torment any more.\n\nBut if ever any couple enjoyed this pleasure, it was at present experienced by the captain and his lady. It was always a sufficient reason to either of them to be obstinate in any opinion, that the other had previously asserted the contrary. If the one proposed any amusement, the other constantly objected to it: they never loved or hated, commended or abused, the same person. And for this reason, as the captain looked with an evil eye on the little foundling, his wife began now to caress it almost equally with her own child.\n\nThe reader will be apt to conceive, that this behaviour between the husband and wife did not greatly contribute to Mr Allworthy's repose, as it tended so little to that serene happiness which he had designed for all three from this alliance; but the truth is, though he might be a little disappointed in his sanguine expectations, yet he was far from being acquainted with the whole matter; for, as the captain was, from certain obvious reasons, much on his guard before him, the lady was obliged, for fear of her brother's displeasure, to pursue the same conduct. In fact, it is possible for a third person to be very intimate, nay even to live long in the same house, with a married couple, who have any tolerable discretion, and not even guess at the sour sentiments which they bear to each other: for though the whole day may be sometimes too short for hatred, as well as for love; yet the many hours which they naturally spend together, apart from all observers, furnish people of tolerable moderation with such ample opportunity for the enjoyment of either passion, that, if they love, they can support being a few hours in company without toying, or if they hate, without spitting in each other's faces.\n\nIt is possible, however, that Mr Allworthy saw enough to render him a little uneasy; for we are not always to conclude, that a wise man is not hurt, because he doth not cry out and lament himself, like those of a childish or effeminate temper. But indeed it is possible he might see some faults in the captain without any uneasiness at all; for men of true wisdom and goodness are contented to take persons and things as they are, without complaining of their imperfections, or attempting to amend them. They can see a fault in a friend, a relation, or an acquaintance, without ever mentioning it to the parties themselves, or to any others; and this often without lessening their affection. Indeed, unless great discernment be tempered with this overlooking disposition, we ought never to contract friendship but with a degree of folly which we can deceive; for I hope my friends will pardon me when I declare, I know none of them without a fault; and I should be sorry if I could imagine I had any friend who could not see mine. Forgiveness of this kind we give and demand in turn. It is an exercise of friendship, and perhaps none of the least pleasant. And this forgiveness we must bestow, without desire of amendment. There is, perhaps, no surer mark of folly, than an attempt to correct the natural infirmities of those we love. The finest composition of human nature, as well as the finest china, may have a flaw in it; and this, I am afraid, in either case, is equally incurable; though, nevertheless, the pattern may remain of the highest value.\n\nUpon the whole, then, Mr Allworthy certainly saw some imperfections in the captain; but as this was a very artful man, and eternally upon his guard before him, these appeared to him no more than blemishes in a good character, which his goodness made him overlook, and his wisdom prevented him from discovering to the captain himself. Very different would have been his sentiments had he discovered the whole; which perhaps would in time have been the case, had the husband and wife long continued this kind of behaviour to each other; but this kind Fortune took effectual means to prevent, by forcing the captain to do that which rendered him again dear to his wife, and restored all her tenderness and affection towards him.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nA receipt to regain the lost affections of a wife, which hath never been known to fail in the most desperate cases.\n\nThe captain was made large amends for the unpleasant minutes which he passed in the conversation of his wife (and which were as few as he could contrive to make them), by the pleasant meditations he enjoyed when alone.\n\nThese meditations were entirely employed on Mr Allworthy's fortune; for, first, he exercised much thought in calculating, as well as he could, the exact value of the whole: which calculations he often saw occasion to alter in his own favour: and, secondly and chiefly, he pleased himself with intended alterations in the house and gardens, and in projecting many other schemes, as well for the improvement of the estate as of the grandeur of the place: for this purpose he applied himself to the studies of architecture and gardening, and read over many books on both these subjects; for these sciences, indeed, employed his whole time, and formed his only amusement. He at last completed a most excellent plan: and very sorry we are, that it is not in our power to present it to our reader, since even the luxury of the present age, I believe, would hardly match it. It had, indeed, in a superlative degree, the two principal ingredients which serve to recommend all great and noble designs of this nature; for it required an immoderate expense to execute, and a vast length of time to bring it to any sort of perfection. The former of these, the immense wealth of which the captain supposed Mr Allworthy possessed, and which he thought himself sure of inheriting, promised very effectually to supply; and the latter, the soundness of his own constitution, and his time of life, which was only what is called middle-age, removed all apprehension of his not living to accomplish.\n\nNothing was wanting to enable him to enter upon the immediate execution of this plan, but the death of Mr Allworthy; in calculating which he had employed much of his own algebra, besides purchasing every book extant that treats of the value of lives, reversions, &c. From all which he satisfied himself, that as he had every day a chance of this happening, so had he more than an even chance of its happening within a few years.\n\nBut while the captain was one day busied in deep contemplations of this kind, one of the most unlucky as well as unseasonable accidents happened to him. The utmost malice of Fortune could, indeed, have contrived nothing so cruel, so mal-a-propos, so absolutely destructive to all his schemes. In short, not to keep the reader in long suspense, just at the very instant when his heart was exulting in meditations on the happiness which would accrue to him by Mr Allworthy's death, he himself--died of an apoplexy.\n\nThis unfortunately befel the captain as he was taking his evening walk by himself, so that nobody was present to lend him any assistance, if indeed, any assistance could have preserved him. He took, therefore, measure of that proportion of soil which was now become adequate to all his future purposes, and he lay dead on the ground, a great (though not a living) example of the truth of that observation of Horace:\n\nTu secanda marmora Locas sub ipsum funus; et sepulchri Immemor, struis domos.\n\nWhich sentiment I shall thus give to the English reader: “You provide the noblest materials for building, when a pickaxe and a spade are only necessary: and build houses of five hundred by a hundred feet, forgetting that of six by two.”\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\nA proof of the infallibility of the foregoing receipt, in the lamentations of the widow; with other suitable decorations of death, such as physicians, &c., and an epitaph in the true stile.\n\nMr Allworthy, his sister, and another lady, were assembled at the accustomed hour in the supper-room, where, having waited a considerable time longer than usual, Mr Allworthy first declared he began to grow uneasy at the captain's stay (for he was always most punctual at his meals); and gave orders that the bell should be rung without the doors, and especially towards those walks which the captain was wont to use.\n\nAll these summons proving ineffectual (for the captain had, by perverse accident, betaken himself to a new walk that evening), Mrs Blifil declared she was seriously frightened. Upon which the other lady, who was one of her most intimate acquaintance, and who well knew the true state of her affections, endeavoured all she could to pacify her, telling her--To be sure she could not help being uneasy; but that she should hope the best. That, perhaps the sweetness of the evening had inticed the captain to go farther than his usual walk: or he might be detained at some neighbour's. Mrs Blifil answered, No; she was sure some accident had befallen him; for that he would never stay out without sending her word, as he must know how uneasy it would make her. The other lady, having no other arguments to use, betook herself to the entreaties usual on such occasions, and begged her not to frighten herself, for it might be of very ill consequence to her own health; and, filling out a very large glass of wine, advised, and at last prevailed with her to drink it.\n\nMr Allworthy now returned into the parlour; for he had been himself in search after the captain. His countenance sufficiently showed the consternation he was under, which, indeed, had a good deal deprived him of speech; but as grief operates variously on different minds, so the same apprehension which depressed his voice, elevated that of Mrs Blifil. She now began to bewail herself in very bitter terms, and floods of tears accompanied her lamentations; which the lady, her companion, declared she could not blame, but at the same time dissuaded her from indulging; attempting to moderate the grief of her friend by philosophical observations on the many disappointments to which human life is daily subject, which, she said, was a sufficient consideration to fortify our minds against any accidents, how sudden or terrible soever. She said her brother's example ought to teach her patience, who, though indeed he could not be supposed as much concerned as herself, yet was, doubtless, very uneasy, though his resignation to the Divine will had restrained his grief within due bounds.\n\n“Mention not my brother,” said Mrs Blifil; “I alone am the object of your pity. What are the terrors of friendship to what a wife feels on these occasions? Oh, he is lost! Somebody hath murdered him--I shall never see him more!”--Here a torrent of tears had the same consequence with what the suppression had occasioned to Mr Allworthy, and she remained silent.\n\nAt this interval a servant came running in, out of breath, and cried out, The captain was found; and, before he could proceed farther, he was followed by two more, bearing the dead body between them.\n\nHere the curious reader may observe another diversity in the operations of grief: for as Mr Allworthy had been before silent, from the same cause which had made his sister vociferous; so did the present sight, which drew tears from the gentleman, put an entire stop to those of the lady; who first gave a violent scream, and presently after fell into a fit.\n\nThe room was soon full of servants, some of whom, with the lady visitant, were employed in care of the wife; and others, with Mr Allworthy, assisted in carrying off the captain to a warm bed; where every method was tried, in order to restore him to life.\n\nAnd glad should we be, could we inform the reader that both these bodies had been attended with equal success; for those who undertook the care of the lady succeeded so well, that, after the fit had continued a decent time, she again revived, to their great satisfaction: but as to the captain, all experiments of bleeding, chafing, dropping, &c., proved ineffectual. Death, that inexorable judge, had passed sentence on him, and refused to grant him a reprieve, though two doctors who arrived, and were fee'd at one and the same instant, were his counsel.\n\nThese two doctors, whom, to avoid any malicious applications, we shall distinguish by the names of Dr Y. and Dr Z., having felt his pulse; to wit, Dr Y. his right arm, and Dr Z. his left; both agreed that he was absolutely dead; but as to the distemper, or cause of his death, they differed; Dr Y. holding that he died of an apoplexy, and Dr Z. of an epilepsy.\n\nHence arose a dispute between the learned men, in which each delivered the reasons of their several opinions. These were of such equal force, that they served both to confirm either doctor in his own sentiments, and made not the least impression on his adversary.\n\nTo say the truth, every physician almost hath his favourite disease, to which he ascribes all the victories obtained over human nature. The gout, the rheumatism, the stone, the gravel, and the consumption, have all their several patrons in the faculty; and none more than the nervous fever, or the fever on the spirits. And here we may account for those disagreements in opinion, concerning the cause of a patient's death, which sometimes occur, between the most learned of the college; and which have greatly surprized that part of the world who have been ignorant of the fact we have above asserted.\n\nThe reader may perhaps be surprized, that, instead of endeavouring to revive the patient, the learned gentlemen should fall immediately into a dispute on the occasion of his death; but in reality all such experiments had been made before their arrival: for the captain was put into a warm bed, had his veins scarified, his forehead chafed, and all sorts of strong drops applied to his lips and nostrils.\n\nThe physicians, therefore, finding themselves anticipated in everything they ordered, were at a loss how to apply that portion of time which it is usual and decent to remain for their fee, and were therefore necessitated to find some subject or other for discourse; and what could more naturally present itself than that before mentioned?\n\nOur doctors were about to take their leave, when Mr Allworthy, having given over the captain, and acquiesced in the Divine will, began to enquire after his sister, whom he desired them to visit before their departure.\n\nThis lady was now recovered of her fit, and, to use the common phrase, as well as could be expected for one in her condition. The doctors, therefore, all previous ceremonies being complied with, as this was a new patient, attended, according to desire, and laid hold on each of her hands, as they had before done on those of the corpse.\n\nThe case of the lady was in the other extreme from that of her husband: for as he was past all the assistance of physic, so in reality she required none.\n\nThere is nothing more unjust than the vulgar opinion, by which physicians are misrepresented, as friends to death. On the contrary, I believe, if the number of those who recover by physic could be opposed to that of the martyrs to it, the former would rather exceed the latter. Nay, some are so cautious on this head, that, to avoid a possibility of killing the patient, they abstain from all methods of curing, and prescribe nothing but what can neither do good nor harm. I have heard some of these, with great gravity, deliver it as a maxim, “That Nature should be left to do her own work, while the physician stands by as it were to clap her on the back, and encourage her when she doth well.”\n\nSo little then did our doctors delight in death, that they discharged the corpse after a single fee; but they were not so disgusted with their living patient; concerning whose case they immediately agreed, and fell to prescribing with great diligence.\n\nWhether, as the lady had at first persuaded her physicians to believe her ill, they had now, in return, persuaded her to believe herself so, I will not determine; but she continued a whole month with all the decorations of sickness. During this time she was visited by physicians, attended by nurses, and received constant messages from her acquaintance to enquire after her health.\n\nAt length the decent time for sickness and immoderate grief being expired, the doctors were discharged, and the lady began to see company; being altered only from what she was before, by that colour of sadness in which she had dressed her person and countenance.\n\nThe captain was now interred, and might, perhaps, have already made a large progress towards oblivion, had not the friendship of Mr Allworthy taken care to preserve his memory, by the following epitaph, which was written by a man of as great genius as integrity, and one who perfectly well knew the captain.\n\n# CAPTAIN JOHN BLIFIL.\n\nLONDON HAD THE HONOUR OF HIS BIRTH, OXFORD OF HIS EDUCATION.\n\n# LONDON HAD THE HONOUR OF HIS BIRTH, OXFORD OF HIS EDUCATION.\n\nHIS PARTS WERE AN HONOUR TO HIS PROFESSION AND TO HIS COUNTRY: HIS LIFE, TO HIS RELIGION AND HUMAN NATURE. HE WAS A DUTIFUL SON, A TENDER HUSBAND, AN AFFECTIONATE FATHER, A MOST KIND BROTHER, A SINCERE FRIEND, A DEVOUT CHRISTIAN, AND A GOOD MAN.\n\nHIS INCONSOLABLE WIDOW HATH ERECTED THIS STONE, THE MONUMENT OF HIS VIRTUES AND OF HER AFFECTION.\n\n# BOOK III.\n\nCONTAINING THE MOST MEMORABLE TRANSACTIONS WHICH PASSED IN THE FAMILY OF MR ALLWORTHY, FROM THE TIME WHEN TOMMY JONES ARRIVED AT THE AGE OF FOURTEEN, TILL HE ATTAINED THE AGE OF NINETEEN. IN THIS BOOK THE READER MAY PICK UP SOME HINTS CONCERNING THE EDUCATION OF CHILDREN. Chapter i.\n\nContaining little or nothing.\n\nThe reader will be pleased to remember, that, at the beginning of the second book of this history, we gave him a hint of our intention to pass over several large periods of time, in which nothing happened worthy of being recorded in a chronicle of this kind.\n\nIn so doing, we do not only consult our own dignity and ease, but the good and advantage of the reader: for besides that by these means we prevent him from throwing away his time, in reading without either pleasure or emolument, we give him, at all such seasons, an opportunity of employing that wonderful sagacity, of which he is master, by filling up these vacant spaces of time with his own conjectures; for which purpose we have taken care to qualify him in the preceding pages.\n\nFor instance, what reader but knows that Mr Allworthy felt, at first, for the loss of his friend, those emotions of grief, which on such occasions enter into all men whose hearts are not composed of flint, or their heads of as solid materials? Again, what reader doth not know that philosophy and religion in time moderated, and at last extinguished, this grief? The former of these teaching the folly and vanity of it, and the latter correcting it as unlawful, and at the same time assuaging it, by raising future hopes and assurances, which enable a strong and religious mind to take leave of a friend, on his deathbed, with little less indifference than if he was preparing for a long journey; and, indeed, with little less hope of seeing him again.\n\nNor can the judicious reader be at a greater loss on account of Mrs Bridget Blifil, who, he may be assured, conducted herself through the whole season in which grief is to make its appearance on the outside of the body, with the strictest regard to all the rules of custom and decency, suiting the alterations of her countenance to the several alterations of her habit: for as this changed from weeds to black, from black to grey, from grey to white, so did her countenance change from dismal to sorrowful, from sorrowful to sad, and from sad to serious, till the day came in which she was allowed to return to her former serenity.\n\nWe have mentioned these two, as examples only of the task which may be imposed on readers of the lowest class. Much higher and harder exercises of judgment and penetration may reasonably be expected from the upper graduates in criticism. Many notable discoveries will, I doubt not, be made by such, of the transactions which happened in the family of our worthy man, during all the years which we have thought proper to pass over: for though nothing worthy of a place in this history occurred within that period, yet did several incidents happen of equal importance with those reported by the daily and weekly historians of the age; in reading which great numbers of persons consume a considerable part of their time, very little, I am afraid, to their emolument. Now, in the conjectures here proposed, some of the most excellent faculties of the mind may be employed to much advantage, since it is a more useful capacity to be able to foretel the actions of men, in any circumstance, from their characters, than to judge of their characters from their actions. The former, I own, requires the greater penetration; but may be accomplished by true sagacity with no less certainty than the latter.\n\nAs we are sensible that much the greatest part of our readers are very eminently possessed of this quality, we have left them a space of twelve years to exert it in; and shall now bring forth our heroe, at about fourteen years of age, not questioning that many have been long impatient to be introduced to his acquaintance.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nThe heroe of this great history appears with very bad omens. A little tale of so LOW a kind that some may think it not worth their notice. A word or two concerning a squire, and more relating to a gamekeeper and a schoolmaster.\n\nAs we determined, when we first sat down to write this history, to flatter no man, but to guide our pen throughout by the directions of truth, we are obliged to bring our heroe on the stage in a much more disadvantageous manner than we could wish; and to declare honestly, even at his first appearance, that it was the universal opinion of all Mr Allworthy's family that he was certainly born to be hanged.\n\nIndeed, I am sorry to say there was too much reason for this conjecture; the lad having from his earliest years discovered a propensity to many vices, and especially to one which hath as direct a tendency as any other to that fate which we have just now observed to have been prophetically denounced against him: he had been already convicted of three robberies, viz., of robbing an orchard, of stealing a duck out of a farmer's yard, and of picking Master Blifil's pocket of a ball.\n\nThe vices of this young man were, moreover, heightened by the disadvantageous light in which they appeared when opposed to the virtues of Master Blifil, his companion; a youth of so different a cast from little Jones, that not only the family but all the neighbourhood resounded his praises. He was, indeed, a lad of a remarkable disposition; sober, discreet, and pious beyond his age; qualities which gained him the love of every one who knew him: while Tom Jones was universally disliked; and many expressed their wonder that Mr Allworthy would suffer such a lad to be educated with his nephew, lest the morals of the latter should be corrupted by his example.\n\nAn incident which happened about this time will set the characters of these two lads more fairly before the discerning reader than is in the power of the longest dissertation.\n\nTom Jones, who, bad as he is, must serve for the heroe of this history, had only one friend among all the servants of the family; for as to Mrs Wilkins, she had long since given him up, and was perfectly reconciled to her mistress. This friend was the gamekeeper, a fellow of a loose kind of disposition, and who was thought not to entertain much stricter notions concerning the difference of meum and tuum than the young gentleman himself. And hence this friendship gave occasion to many sarcastical remarks among the domestics, most of which were either proverbs before, or at least are become so now; and, indeed, the wit of them all may be comprised in that short Latin proverb, “Noscitur a socio;” which, I think, is thus expressed in English, “You may know him by the company he keeps.”\n\nTo say the truth, some of that atrocious wickedness in Jones, of which we have just mentioned three examples, might perhaps be derived from the encouragement he had received from this fellow, who, in two or three instances, had been what the law calls an accessary after the fact: for the whole duck, and great part of the apples, were converted to the use of the gamekeeper and his family; though, as Jones alone was discovered, the poor lad bore not only the whole smart, but the whole blame; both which fell again to his lot on the following occasion.\n\nContiguous to Mr Allworthy's estate was the manor of one of those gentlemen who are called preservers of the game. This species of men, from the great severity with which they revenge the death of a hare or partridge, might be thought to cultivate the same superstition with the Bannians in India; many of whom, we are told, dedicate their whole lives to the preservation and protection of certain animals; was it not that our English Bannians, while they preserve them from other enemies, will most unmercifully slaughter whole horse-loads themselves; so that they stand clearly acquitted of any such heathenish superstition.\n\nI have, indeed, a much better opinion of this kind of men than is entertained by some, as I take them to answer the order of Nature, and the good purposes for which they were ordained, in a more ample manner than many others. Now, as Horace tells us that there are a set of human beings\n\n“Born to consume the fruits of the earth;” so I make no manner of doubt but that there are others\n\nFeras consumere nati,\n“Born to consume the beasts of the field;” or, as it is commonly called, the game; and none, I believe, will deny but that those squires fulfil this end of their creation.\n\nLittle Jones went one day a shooting with the gamekeeper; when happening to spring a covey of partridges near the border of that manor over which Fortune, to fulfil the wise purposes of Nature, had planted one of the game consumers, the birds flew into it, and were marked (as it is called) by the two sportsmen, in some furze bushes, about two or three hundred paces beyond Mr Allworthy's dominions.\n\nMr Allworthy had given the fellow strict orders, on pain of forfeiting his place, never to trespass on any of his neighbours; no more on those who were less rigid in this matter than on the lord of this manor. With regard to others, indeed, these orders had not been always very scrupulously kept; but as the disposition of the gentleman with whom the partridges had taken sanctuary was well known, the gamekeeper had never yet attempted to invade his territories. Nor had he done it now, had not the younger sportsman, who was excessively eager to pursue the flying game, over-persuaded him; but Jones being very importunate, the other, who was himself keen enough after the sport, yielded to his persuasions, entered the manor, and shot one of the partridges.\n\nThe gentleman himself was at that time on horse-back, at a little distance from them; and hearing the gun go off, he immediately made towards the place, and discovered poor Tom; for the gamekeeper had leapt into the thickest part of the furze-brake, where he had happily concealed himself.\n\nThe gentleman having searched the lad, and found the partridge upon him, denounced great vengeance, swearing he would acquaint Mr Allworthy. He was as good as his word: for he rode immediately to his house, and complained of the trespass on his manor in as high terms and as bitter language as if his house had been broken open, and the most valuable furniture stole out of it. He added, that some other person was in his company, though he could not discover him; for that two guns had been discharged almost in the same instant. And, says he, “We have found only this partridge, but the Lord knows what mischief they have done.”\n\nAt his return home, Tom was presently convened before Mr Allworthy. He owned the fact, and alledged no other excuse but what was really true, viz., that the covey was originally sprung in Mr Allworthy's own manor.\n\nTom was then interrogated who was with him, which Mr Allworthy declared he was resolved to know, acquainting the culprit with the circumstance of the two guns, which had been deposed by the squire and both his servants; but Tom stoutly persisted in asserting that he was alone; yet, to say the truth, he hesitated a little at first, which would have confirmed Mr Allworthy's belief, had what the squire and his servants said wanted any further confirmation.\n\nThe gamekeeper, being a suspected person, was now sent for, and the question put to him; but he, relying on the promise which Tom had made him, to take all upon himself, very resolutely denied being in company with the young gentleman, or indeed having seen him the whole afternoon.\n\nMr Allworthy then turned towards Tom, with more than usual anger in his countenance, and advised him to confess who was with him; repeating, that he was resolved to know. The lad, however, still maintained his resolution, and was dismissed with much wrath by Mr Allworthy, who told him he should have to the next morning to consider of it, when he should be questioned by another person, and in another manner.\n\nPoor Jones spent a very melancholy night; and the more so, as he was without his usual companion; for Master Blifil was gone abroad on a visit with his mother. Fear of the punishment he was to suffer was on this occasion his least evil; his chief anxiety being, lest his constancy should fail him, and he should be brought to betray the gamekeeper, whose ruin he knew must now be the consequence.\n\nNor did the gamekeeper pass his time much better. He had the same apprehensions with the youth; for whose honour he had likewise a much tenderer regard than for his skin.\n\nIn the morning, when Tom attended the reverend Mr Thwackum, the person to whom Mr Allworthy had committed the instruction of the two boys, he had the same questions put to him by that gentleman which he had been asked the evening before, to which he returned the same answers. The consequence of this was, so severe a whipping, that it possibly fell little short of the torture with which confessions are in some countries extorted from criminals.\n\nTom bore his punishment with great resolution; and though his master asked him, between every stroke, whether he would not confess, he was contented to be flead rather than betray his friend, or break the promise he had made.\n\nThe gamekeeper was now relieved from his anxiety, and Mr Allworthy himself began to be concerned at Tom's sufferings: for besides that Mr Thwackum, being highly enraged that he was not able to make the boy say what he himself pleased, had carried his severity much beyond the good man's intention, this latter began now to suspect that the squire had been mistaken; which his extreme eagerness and anger seemed to make probable; and as for what the servants had said in confirmation of their master's account, he laid no great stress upon that. Now, as cruelty and injustice were two ideas of which Mr Allworthy could by no means support the consciousness a single moment, he sent for Tom, and after many kind and friendly exhortations, said, “I am convinced, my dear child, that my suspicions have wronged you; I am sorry that you have been so severely punished on this account.” And at last gave him a little horse to make him amends; again repeating his sorrow for what had past.\n\nTom's guilt now flew in his face more than any severity could make it. He could more easily bear the lashes of Thwackum, than the generosity of Allworthy. The tears burst from his eyes, and he fell upon his knees, crying, “Oh, sir, you are too good to me. Indeed you are. Indeed I don't deserve it.” And at that very instant, from the fulness of his heart, had almost betrayed the secret; but the good genius of the gamekeeper suggested to him what might be the consequence to the poor fellow, and this consideration sealed his lips.\n\nThwackum did all he could to persuade Allworthy from showing any compassion or kindness to the boy, saying, “He had persisted in an untruth;” and gave some hints, that a second whipping might probably bring the matter to light.\n\nBut Mr Allworthy absolutely refused to consent to the experiment. He said, the boy had suffered enough already for concealing the truth, even if he was guilty, seeing that he could have no motive but a mistaken point of honour for so doing.\n\n“Honour!” cryed Thwackum, with some warmth, “mere stubbornness and obstinacy! Can honour teach any one to tell a lie, or can any honour exist independent of religion?”\n\nThis discourse happened at table when dinner was just ended; and there were present Mr Allworthy, Mr Thwackum, and a third gentleman, who now entered into the debate, and whom, before we proceed any further, we shall briefly introduce to our reader's acquaintance.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nThe character of Mr Square the philosopher, and of Mr Thwackum the divine; with a dispute concerning----\n\nThe name of this gentleman, who had then resided some time at Mr Allworthy's house, was Mr Square. His natural parts were not of the first rate, but he had greatly improved them by a learned education. He was deeply read in the antients, and a profest master of all the works of Plato and Aristotle. Upon which great models he had principally formed himself; sometimes according with the opinion of the one, and sometimes with that of the other. In morals he was a profest Platonist, and in religion he inclined to be an Aristotelian.\n\nBut though he had, as we have said, formed his morals on the Platonic model, yet he perfectly agreed with the opinion of Aristotle, in considering that great man rather in the quality of a philosopher or a speculatist, than as a legislator. This sentiment he carried a great way; indeed, so far, as to regard all virtue as matter of theory only. This, it is true, he never affirmed, as I have heard, to any one; and yet upon the least attention to his conduct, I cannot help thinking it was his real opinion, as it will perfectly reconcile some contradictions which might otherwise appear in his character.\n\nThis gentleman and Mr Thwackum scarce ever met without a disputation; for their tenets were indeed diametrically opposite to each other. Square held human nature to be the perfection of all virtue, and that vice was a deviation from our nature, in the same manner as deformity of body is. Thwackum, on the contrary, maintained that the human mind, since the fall, was nothing but a sink of iniquity, till purified and redeemed by grace. In one point only they agreed, which was, in all their discourses on morality never to mention the word goodness. The favourite phrase of the former, was the natural beauty of virtue; that of the latter, was the divine power of grace. The former measured all actions by the unalterable rule of right, and the eternal fitness of things; the latter decided all matters by authority; but in doing this, he always used the scriptures and their commentators, as the lawyer doth his Coke upon Lyttleton, where the comment is of equal authority with the text.\n\nAfter this short introduction, the reader will be pleased to remember, that the parson had concluded his speech with a triumphant question, to which he had apprehended no answer; viz., Can any honour exist independent on religion?\n\nTo this Square answered; that it was impossible to discourse philosophically concerning words, till their meaning was first established: that there were scarce any two words of a more vague and uncertain signification, than the two he had mentioned; for that there were almost as many different opinions concerning honour, as concerning religion. “But,” says he, “if by honour you mean the true natural beauty of virtue, I will maintain it may exist independent of any religion whatever. Nay,” added he, “you yourself will allow it may exist independent of all but one: so will a Mahometan, a Jew, and all the maintainers of all the different sects in the world.”\n\nThwackum replied, this was arguing with the usual malice of all the enemies to the true Church. He said, he doubted not but that all the infidels and hereticks in the world would, if they could, confine honour to their own absurd errors and damnable deceptions; “but honour,” says he, “is not therefore manifold, because there are many absurd opinions about it; nor is religion manifold, because there are various sects and heresies in the world. When I mention religion, I mean the Christian religion; and not only the Christian religion, but the Protestant religion; and not only the Protestant religion, but the Church of England. And when I mention honour, I mean that mode of Divine grace which is not only consistent with, but dependent upon, this religion; and is consistent with and dependent upon no other. Now to say that the honour I here mean, and which was, I thought, all the honour I could be supposed to mean, will uphold, much less dictate an untruth, is to assert an absurdity too shocking to be conceived.”\n\n“I purposely avoided,” says Square, “drawing a conclusion which I thought evident from what I have said; but if you perceived it, I am sure you have not attempted to answer it. However, to drop the article of religion, I think it is plain, from what you have said, that we have different ideas of honour; or why do we not agree in the same terms of its explanation? I have asserted, that true honour and true virtue are almost synonymous terms, and they are both founded on the unalterable rule of right, and the eternal fitness of things; to which an untruth being absolutely repugnant and contrary, it is certain that true honour cannot support an untruth. In this, therefore, I think we are agreed; but that this honour can be said to be founded on religion, to which it is antecedent, if by religion be meant any positive law--”\n\n“I agree,” answered Thwackum, with great warmth, “with a man who asserts honour to be antecedent to religion! Mr Allworthy, did I agree--?”\n\nHe was proceeding when Mr Allworthy interposed, telling them very coldly, they had both mistaken his meaning; for that he had said nothing of true honour.--It is possible, however, he would not have easily quieted the disputants, who were growing equally warm, had not another matter now fallen out, which put a final end to the conversation at present.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nContaining a necessary apology for the author; and a childish incident, which perhaps requires an apology likewise.\n\nBefore I proceed farther, I shall beg leave to obviate some misconstructions into which the zeal of some few readers may lead them; for I would not willingly give offence to any, especially to men who are warm in the cause of virtue or religion.\n\nI hope, therefore, no man will, by the grossest misunderstanding or perversion of my meaning, misrepresent me, as endeavouring to cast any ridicule on the greatest perfections of human nature; and which do, indeed, alone purify and ennoble the heart of man, and raise him above the brute creation. This, reader, I will venture to say (and by how much the better man you are yourself, by so much the more will you be inclined to believe me), that I would rather have buried the sentiments of these two persons in eternal oblivion, than have done any injury to either of these glorious causes.\n\nOn the contrary, it is with a view to their service, that I have taken upon me to record the lives and actions of two of their false and pretended champions. A treacherous friend is the most dangerous enemy; and I will say boldly, that both religion and virtue have received more real discredit from hypocrites than the wittiest profligates or infidels could ever cast upon them: nay, farther, as these two, in their purity, are rightly called the bands of civil society, and are indeed the greatest of blessings; so when poisoned and corrupted with fraud, pretence, and affectation, they have become the worst of civil curses, and have enabled men to perpetrate the most cruel mischiefs to their own species.\n\nIndeed, I doubt not but this ridicule will in general be allowed: my chief apprehension is, as many true and just sentiments often came from the mouths of these persons, lest the whole should be taken together, and I should be conceived to ridicule all alike. Now the reader will be pleased to consider, that, as neither of these men were fools, they could not be supposed to have holden none but wrong principles, and to have uttered nothing but absurdities; what injustice, therefore, must I have done to their characters, had I selected only what was bad! And how horribly wretched and maimed must their arguments have appeared!\n\nUpon the whole, it is not religion or virtue, but the want of them, which is here exposed. Had not Thwackum too much neglected virtue, and Square, religion, in the composition of their several systems, and had not both utterly discarded all natural goodness of heart, they had never been represented as the objects of derision in this history; in which we will now proceed.\n\nThis matter then, which put an end to the debate mentioned in the last chapter, was no other than a quarrel between Master Blifil and Tom Jones, the consequence of which had been a bloody nose to the former; for though Master Blifil, notwithstanding he was the younger, was in size above the other's match, yet Tom was much his superior at the noble art of boxing.\n\nTom, however, cautiously avoided all engagements with that youth; for besides that Tommy Jones was an inoffensive lad amidst all his roguery, and really loved Blifil, Mr Thwackum being always the second of the latter, would have been sufficient to deter him.\n\nBut well says a certain author, No man is wise at all hours; it is therefore no wonder that a boy is not so. A difference arising at play between the two lads, Master Blifil called Tom a beggarly bastard. Upon which the latter, who was somewhat passionate in his disposition, immediately caused that phenomenon in the face of the former, which we have above remembered.\n\nMaster Blifil now, with his blood running from his nose, and the tears galloping after from his eyes, appeared before his uncle and the tremendous Thwackum. In which court an indictment of assault, battery, and wounding, was instantly preferred against Tom; who in his excuse only pleaded the provocation, which was indeed all the matter that Master Blifil had omitted.\n\nIt is indeed possible that this circumstance might have escaped his memory; for, in his reply, he positively insisted, that he had made use of no such appellation; adding, “Heaven forbid such naughty words should ever come out of his mouth!”\n\nTom, though against all form of law, rejoined in affirmance of the words. Upon which Master Blifil said, “It is no wonder. Those who will tell one fib, will hardly stick at another. If I had told my master such a wicked fib as you have done, I should be ashamed to show my face.”\n\n“What fib, child?” cries Thwackum pretty eagerly.\n\n“Why, he told you that nobody was with him a shooting when he killed the partridge; but he knows” (here he burst into a flood of tears), “yes, he knows, for he confessed it to me, that Black George the gamekeeper was there. Nay, he said--yes you did--deny it if you can, that you would not have confest the truth, though master had cut you to pieces.”\n\nAt this the fire flashed from Thwackum's eyes, and he cried out in triumph--“Oh! ho! this is your mistaken notion of honour! This is the boy who was not to be whipped again!” But Mr Allworthy, with a more gentle aspect, turned towards the lad, and said, “Is this true, child? How came you to persist so obstinately in a falsehood?”\n\nTom said, “He scorned a lie as much as any one: but he thought his honour engaged him to act as he did; for he had promised the poor fellow to conceal him: which,” he said, “he thought himself farther obliged to, as the gamekeeper had begged him not to go into the gentleman's manor, and had at last gone himself, in compliance with his persuasions.” He said, “This was the whole truth of the matter, and he would take his oath of it;” and concluded with very passionately begging Mr Allworthy “to have compassion on the poor fellow's family, especially as he himself only had been guilty, and the other had been very difficultly prevailed on to do what he did. Indeed, sir,” said he, “it could hardly be called a lie that I told; for the poor fellow was entirely innocent of the whole matter. I should have gone alone after the birds; nay, I did go at first, and he only followed me to prevent more mischief. Do, pray, sir, let me be punished; take my little horse away again; but pray, sir, forgive poor George.”\n\nMr Allworthy hesitated a few moments, and then dismissed the boys, advising them to live more friendly and peaceably together.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nThe opinions of the divine and the philosopher concerning the two boys; with some reasons for their opinions, and other matters.\n\nIt is probable, that by disclosing this secret, which had been communicated in the utmost confidence to him, young Blifil preserved his companion from a good lashing; for the offence of the bloody nose would have been of itself sufficient cause for Thwackum to have proceeded to correction; but now this was totally absorbed in the consideration of the other matter; and with regard to this, Mr Allworthy declared privately, he thought the boy deserved reward rather than punishment, so that Thwackum's hand was withheld by a general pardon.\n\nThwackum, whose meditations were full of birch, exclaimed against this weak, and, as he said he would venture to call it, wicked lenity. To remit the punishment of such crimes was, he said, to encourage them. He enlarged much on the correction of children, and quoted many texts from Solomon, and others; which being to be found in so many other books, shall not be found here. He then applied himself to the vice of lying, on which head he was altogether as learned as he had been on the other.\n\nSquare said, he had been endeavouring to reconcile the behaviour of Tom with his idea of perfect virtue, but could not. He owned there was something which at first sight appeared like fortitude in the action; but as fortitude was a virtue, and falsehood a vice, they could by no means agree or unite together. He added, that as this was in some measure to confound virtue and vice, it might be worth Mr Thwackum's consideration, whether a larger castigation might not be laid on upon the account.\n\nAs both these learned men concurred in censuring Jones, so were they no less unanimous in applauding Master Blifil. To bring truth to light, was by the parson asserted to be the duty of every religious man; and by the philosopher this was declared to be highly conformable with the rule of right, and the eternal and unalterable fitness of things.\n\nAll this, however, weighed very little with Mr Allworthy. He could not be prevailed on to sign the warrant for the execution of Jones. There was something within his own breast with which the invincible fidelity which that youth had preserved, corresponded much better than it had done with the religion of Thwackum, or with the virtue of Square. He therefore strictly ordered the former of these gentlemen to abstain from laying violent hands on Tom for what had past. The pedagogue was obliged to obey those orders; but not without great reluctance, and frequent mutterings that the boy would be certainly spoiled.\n\nTowards the gamekeeper the good man behaved with more severity. He presently summoned that poor fellow before him, and after many bitter remonstrances, paid him his wages, and dismist him from his service; for Mr Allworthy rightly observed, that there was a great difference between being guilty of a falsehood to excuse yourself, and to excuse another. He likewise urged, as the principal motive to his inflexible severity against this man, that he had basely suffered Tom Jones to undergo so heavy a punishment for his sake, whereas he ought to have prevented it by making the discovery himself.\n\nWhen this story became public, many people differed from Square and Thwackum, in judging the conduct of the two lads on the occasion. Master Blifil was generally called a sneaking rascal, a poor-spirited wretch, with other epithets of the like kind; whilst Tom was honoured with the appellations of a brave lad, a jolly dog, and an honest fellow. Indeed, his behaviour to Black George much ingratiated him with all the servants; for though that fellow was before universally disliked, yet he was no sooner turned away than he was as universally pitied; and the friendship and gallantry of Tom Jones was celebrated by them all with the highest applause; and they condemned Master Blifil as openly as they durst, without incurring the danger of offending his mother. For all this, however, poor Tom smarted in the flesh; for though Thwackum had been inhibited to exercise his arm on the foregoing account, yet, as the proverb says, It is easy to find a stick, &c. So was it easy to find a rod; and, indeed, the not being able to find one was the only thing which could have kept Thwackum any long time from chastising poor Jones.\n\nHad the bare delight in the sport been the only inducement to the pedagogue, it is probable Master Blifil would likewise have had his share; but though Mr Allworthy had given him frequent orders to make no difference between the lads, yet was Thwackum altogether as kind and gentle to this youth, as he was harsh, nay even barbarous, to the other. To say the truth, Blifil had greatly gained his master's affections; partly by the profound respect he always showed his person, but much more by the decent reverence with which he received his doctrine; for he had got by heart, and frequently repeated, his phrases, and maintained all his master's religious principles with a zeal which was surprizing in one so young, and which greatly endeared him to the worthy preceptor.\n\nTom Jones, on the other hand, was not only deficient in outward tokens of respect, often forgetting to pull off his hat, or to bow at his master's approach; but was altogether as unmindful both of his master's precepts and example. He was indeed a thoughtless, giddy youth, with little sobriety in his manners, and less in his countenance; and would often very impudently and indecently laugh at his companion for his serious behaviour.\n\nMr Square had the same reason for his preference of the former lad; for Tom Jones showed no more regard to the learned discourses which this gentleman would sometimes throw away upon him, than to those of Thwackum. He once ventured to make a jest of the rule of right; and at another time said, he believed there was no rule in the world capable of making such a man as his father (for so Mr Allworthy suffered himself to be called).\n\nMaster Blifil, on the contrary, had address enough at sixteen to recommend himself at one and the same time to both these opposites. With one he was all religion, with the other he was all virtue. And when both were present, he was profoundly silent, which both interpreted in his favour and in their own.\n\nNor was Blifil contented with flattering both these gentlemen to their faces; he took frequent occasions of praising them behind their backs to Allworthy; before whom, when they two were alone, and his uncle commended any religious or virtuous sentiment (for many such came constantly from him) he seldom failed to ascribe it to the good instructions he had received from either Thwackum or Square; for he knew his uncle repeated all such compliments to the persons for whose use they were meant; and he found by experience the great impressions which they made on the philosopher, as well as on the divine: for, to say the truth, there is no kind of flattery so irresistible as this, at second hand.\n\nThe young gentleman, moreover, soon perceived how extremely grateful all those panegyrics on his instructors were to Mr Allworthy himself, as they so loudly resounded the praise of that singular plan of education which he had laid down; for this worthy man having observed the imperfect institution of our public schools, and the many vices which boys were there liable to learn, had resolved to educate his nephew, as well as the other lad, whom he had in a manner adopted, in his own house; where he thought their morals would escape all that danger of being corrupted to which they would be unavoidably exposed in any public school or university.\n\nHaving, therefore, determined to commit these boys to the tuition of a private tutor, Mr Thwackum was recommended to him for that office, by a very particular friend, of whose understanding Mr Allworthy had a great opinion, and in whose integrity he placed much confidence. This Thwackum was fellow of a college, where he almost entirely resided; and had a great reputation for learning, religion, and sobriety of manners. And these were doubtless the qualifications by which Mr Allworthy's friend had been induced to recommend him; though indeed this friend had some obligations to Thwackum's family, who were the most considerable persons in a borough which that gentleman represented in parliament.\n\nThwackum, at his first arrival, was extremely agreeable to Allworthy; and indeed he perfectly answered the character which had been given of him. Upon longer acquaintance, however, and more intimate conversation, this worthy man saw infirmities in the tutor, which he could have wished him to have been without; though as those seemed greatly overbalanced by his good qualities, they did not incline Mr Allworthy to part with him: nor would they indeed have justified such a proceeding; for the reader is greatly mistaken, if he conceives that Thwackum appeared to Mr Allworthy in the same light as he doth to him in this history; and he is as much deceived, if he imagines that the most intimate acquaintance which he himself could have had with that divine, would have informed him of those things which we, from our inspiration, are enabled to open and discover. Of readers who, from such conceits as these, condemn the wisdom or penetration of Mr Allworthy, I shall not scruple to say, that they make a very bad and ungrateful use of that knowledge which we have communicated to them.\n\nThese apparent errors in the doctrine of Thwackum served greatly to palliate the contrary errors in that of Square, which our good man no less saw and condemned. He thought, indeed, that the different exuberancies of these gentlemen would correct their different imperfections; and that from both, especially with his assistance, the two lads would derive sufficient precepts of true religion and virtue. If the event happened contrary to his expectations, this possibly proceeded from some fault in the plan itself; which the reader hath my leave to discover, if he can: for we do not pretend to introduce any infallible characters into this history; where we hope nothing will be found which hath never yet been seen in human nature.\n\nTo return therefore: the reader will not, I think, wonder that the different behaviour of the two lads above commemorated, produced the different effects of which he hath already seen some instance; and besides this, there was another reason for the conduct of the philosopher and the pedagogue; but this being matter of great importance, we shall reveal it in the next chapter.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nContaining a better reason still for the before-mentioned opinions.\nIt is to be known then, that those two learned personages, who have lately made a considerable figure on the theatre of this history, had, from their first arrival at Mr Allworthy's house, taken so great an affection, the one to his virtue, the other to his religion, that they had meditated the closest alliance with him.\n\nFor this purpose they had cast their eyes on that fair widow, whom, though we have not for some time made any mention of her, the reader, we trust, hath not forgot. Mrs Blifil was indeed the object to which they both aspired.\n\nIt may seem remarkable, that, of four persons whom we have commemorated at Mr Allworthy's house, three of them should fix their inclinations on a lady who was never greatly celebrated for her beauty, and who was, moreover, now a little descended into the vale of years; but in reality bosom friends, and intimate acquaintance, have a kind of natural propensity to particular females at the house of a friend--viz., to his grandmother, mother, sister, daughter, aunt, niece, or cousin, when they are rich; and to his wife, sister, daughter, niece, cousin, mistress, or servant-maid, if they should be handsome.\n\nWe would not, however, have our reader imagine, that persons of such characters as were supported by Thwackum and Square, would undertake a matter of this kind, which hath been a little censured by some rigid moralists, before they had thoroughly examined it, and considered whether it was (as Shakespear phrases it) “Stuff o' th' conscience,” or no. Thwackum was encouraged to the undertaking by reflecting that to covet your neighbour's sister is nowhere forbidden: and he knew it was a rule in the construction of all laws, that “Expressum facit cessare tacitum.” The sense of which is, “When a lawgiver sets down plainly his whole meaning, we are prevented from making him mean what we please ourselves.” As some instances of women, therefore, are mentioned in the divine law, which forbids us to covet our neighbour's goods, and that of a sister omitted, he concluded it to be lawful. And as to Square, who was in his person what is called a jolly fellow, or a widow's man, he easily reconciled his choice to the eternal fitness of things.\n\nNow, as both of these gentlemen were industrious in taking every opportunity of recommending themselves to the widow, they apprehended one certain method was, by giving her son the constant preference to the other lad; and as they conceived the kindness and affection which Mr Allworthy showed the latter, must be highly disagreeable to her, they doubted not but the laying hold on all occasions to degrade and vilify him, would be highly pleasing to her; who, as she hated the boy, must love all those who did him any hurt. In this Thwackum had the advantage; for while Square could only scarify the poor lad's reputation, he could flea his skin; and, indeed, he considered every lash he gave him as a compliment paid to his mistress; so that he could, with the utmost propriety, repeat this old flogging line, “Castigo te non quod odio habeam, sed quod AMEM. I chastise thee not out of hatred, but out of love.” And this, indeed, he often had in his mouth, or rather, according to the old phrase, never more properly applied, at his fingers' ends.\n\nFor this reason, principally, the two gentlemen concurred, as we have seen above, in their opinion concerning the two lads; this being, indeed, almost the only instance of their concurring on any point; for, beside the difference of their principles, they had both long ago strongly suspected each other's design, and hated one another with no little degree of inveteracy.\n\nThis mutual animosity was a good deal increased by their alternate successes; for Mrs Blifil knew what they would be at long before they imagined it; or, indeed, intended she should: for they proceeded with great caution, lest she should be offended, and acquaint Mr Allworthy. But they had no reason for any such fear; she was well enough pleased with a passion, of which she intended none should have any fruits but herself. And the only fruits she designed for herself were, flattery and courtship; for which purpose she soothed them by turns, and a long time equally. She was, indeed, rather inclined to favour the parson's principles; but Square's person was more agreeable to her eye, for he was a comely man; whereas the pedagogue did in countenance very nearly resemble that gentleman, who, in the Harlot's Progress, is seen correcting the ladies in Bridewell.\n\nWhether Mrs Blifil had been surfeited with the sweets of marriage, or disgusted by its bitters, or from what other cause it proceeded, I will not determine; but she could never be brought to listen to any second proposals. However, she at last conversed with Square with such a degree of intimacy that malicious tongues began to whisper things of her, to which, as well for the sake of the lady, as that they were highly disagreeable to the rule of right and the fitness of things, we will give no credit, and therefore shall not blot our paper with them. The pedagogue, 'tis certain, whipped on, without getting a step nearer to his journey's end.\n\nIndeed he had committed a great error, and that Square discovered much sooner than himself. Mrs Blifil (as, perhaps, the reader may have formerly guessed) was not over and above pleased with the behaviour of her husband; nay, to be honest, she absolutely hated him, till his death at last a little reconciled him to her affections. It will not be therefore greatly wondered at, if she had not the most violent regard to the offspring she had by him. And, in fact, she had so little of this regard, that in his infancy she seldom saw her son, or took any notice of him; and hence she acquiesced, after a little reluctance, in all the favours which Mr Allworthy showered on the foundling; whom the good man called his own boy, and in all things put on an entire equality with Master Blifil. This acquiescence in Mrs Blifil was considered by the neighbours, and by the family, as a mark of her condescension to her brother's humour, and she was imagined by all others, as well as Thwackum and Square, to hate the foundling in her heart; nay, the more civility she showed him, the more they conceived she detested him, and the surer schemes she was laying for his ruin: for as they thought it her interest to hate him, it was very difficult for her to persuade them she did not.\n\nThwackum was the more confirmed in his opinion, as she had more than once slily caused him to whip Tom Jones, when Mr Allworthy, who was an enemy to this exercise, was abroad; whereas she had never given any such orders concerning young Blifil. And this had likewise imposed upon Square. In reality, though she certainly hated her own son--of which, however monstrous it appears, I am assured she is not a singular instance--she appeared, notwithstanding all her outward compliance, to be in her heart sufficiently displeased with all the favour shown by Mr Allworthy to the foundling. She frequently complained of this behind her brother's back, and very sharply censured him for it, both to Thwackum and Square; nay, she would throw it in the teeth of Allworthy himself, when a little quarrel, or miff, as it is vulgarly called, arose between them.\n\nHowever, when Tom grew up, and gave tokens of that gallantry of temper which greatly recommends men to women, this disinclination which she had discovered to him when a child, by degrees abated, and at last she so evidently demonstrated her affection to him to be much stronger than what she bore her own son, that it was impossible to mistake her any longer. She was so desirous of often seeing him, and discovered such satisfaction and delight in his company, that before he was eighteen years old he was become a rival to both Square and Thwackum; and what is worse, the whole country began to talk as loudly of her inclination to Tom, as they had before done of that which she had shown to Square: on which account the philosopher conceived the most implacable hatred for our poor heroe.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nIn which the author himself makes his appearance on the stage.\n\nThough Mr Allworthy was not of himself hasty to see things in a disadvantageous light, and was a stranger to the public voice, which seldom reaches to a brother or a husband, though it rings in the ears of all the neighbourhood; yet was this affection of Mrs Blifil to Tom, and the preference which she too visibly gave him to her own son, of the utmost disadvantage to that youth.\n\nFor such was the compassion which inhabited Mr Allworthy's mind, that nothing but the steel of justice could ever subdue it. To be unfortunate in any respect was sufficient, if there was no demerit to counterpoise it, to turn the scale of that good man's pity, and to engage his friendship and his benefaction.\n\nWhen therefore he plainly saw Master Blifil was absolutely detested (for that he was) by his own mother, he began, on that account only, to look with an eye of compassion upon him; and what the effects of compassion are, in good and benevolent minds, I need not here explain to most of my readers.\n\nHenceforward he saw every appearance of virtue in the youth through the magnifying end, and viewed all his faults with the glass inverted, so that they became scarce perceptible. And this perhaps the amiable temper of pity may make commendable; but the next step the weakness of human nature alone must excuse; for he no sooner perceived that preference which Mrs Blifil gave to Tom, than that poor youth (however innocent) began to sink in his affections as he rose in hers. This, it is true, would of itself alone never have been able to eradicate Jones from his bosom; but it was greatly injurious to him, and prepared Mr Allworthy's mind for those impressions which afterwards produced the mighty events that will be contained hereafter in this history; and to which, it must be confest, the unfortunate lad, by his own wantonness, wildness, and want of caution, too much contributed.\n\nIn recording some instances of these, we shall, if rightly understood, afford a very useful lesson to those well-disposed youths who shall hereafter be our readers; for they may here find, that goodness of heart, and openness of temper, though these may give them great comfort within, and administer to an honest pride in their own minds, will by no means, alas! do their business in the world. Prudence and circumspection are necessary even to the best of men. They are indeed, as it were, a guard to Virtue, without which she can never be safe. It is not enough that your designs, nay, that your actions, are intrinsically good; you must take care they shall appear so. If your inside be never so beautiful, you must preserve a fair outside also. This must be constantly looked to, or malice and envy will take care to blacken it so, that the sagacity and goodness of an Allworthy will not be able to see through it, and to discern the beauties within. Let this, my young readers, be your constant maxim, that no man can be good enough to enable him to neglect the rules of prudence; nor will Virtue herself look beautiful, unless she be bedecked with the outward ornaments of decency and decorum. And this precept, my worthy disciples, if you read with due attention, you will, I hope, find sufficiently enforced by examples in the following pages.\n\nI ask pardon for this short appearance, by way of chorus, on the stage. It is in reality for my own sake, that, while I am discovering the rocks on which innocence and goodness often split, I may not be misunderstood to recommend the very means to my worthy readers, by which I intend to show them they will be undone. And this, as I could not prevail on any of my actors to speak, I myself was obliged to declare.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nA childish incident, in which, however, is seen a good-natured disposition in Tom Jones.\n\nThe reader may remember that Mr Allworthy gave Tom Jones a little horse, as a kind of smart-money for the punishment which he imagined he had suffered innocently.\n\nThis horse Tom kept above half a year, and then rode him to a neighbouring fair, and sold him.\n\nAt his return, being questioned by Thwackum what he had done with the money for which the horse was sold, he frankly declared he would not tell him.\n\n“Oho!” says Thwackum, “you will not! then I will have it out of your br--h;” that being the place to which he always applied for information on every doubtful occasion.\n\nTom was now mounted on the back of a footman, and everything prepared for execution, when Mr Allworthy, entering the room, gave the criminal a reprieve, and took him with him into another apartment; where, being alone with Tom, he put the same question to him which Thwackum had before asked him.\n\nTom answered, he could in duty refuse him nothing; but as for that tyrannical rascal, he would never make him any other answer than with a cudgel, with which he hoped soon to be able to pay him for all his barbarities.\n\nMr Allworthy very severely reprimanded the lad for his indecent and disrespectful expressions concerning his master; but much more for his avowing an intention of revenge. He threatened him with the entire loss of his favour, if he ever heard such another word from his mouth; for, he said, he would never support or befriend a reprobate. By these and the like declarations, he extorted some compunction from Tom, in which that youth was not over-sincere; for he really meditated some return for all the smarting favours he had received at the hands of the pedagogue. He was, however, brought by Mr Allworthy to express a concern for his resentment against Thwackum; and then the good man, after some wholesome admonition, permitted him to proceed, which he did as follows:--\n\n“Indeed, my dear sir, I love and honour you more than all the world: I know the great obligations I have to you, and should detest myself if I thought my heart was capable of ingratitude. Could the little horse you gave me speak, I am sure he could tell you how fond I was of your present; for I had more pleasure in feeding him than in riding him. Indeed, sir, it went to my heart to part with him; nor would I have sold him upon any other account in the world than what I did. You yourself, sir, I am convinced, in my case, would have done the same: for none ever so sensibly felt the misfortunes of others. What would you feel, dear sir, if you thought yourself the occasion of them? Indeed, sir, there never was any misery like theirs.”\n\n“Like whose, child?” says Allworthy: “What do you mean?”\n\n“Oh, sir!” answered Tom, “your poor gamekeeper, with all his large family, ever since your discarding him, have been perishing with all the miseries of cold and hunger: I could not bear to see these poor wretches naked and starving, and at the same time know myself to have been the occasion of all their sufferings. I could not bear it, sir; upon my soul, I could not.” [Here the tears ran down his cheeks, and he thus proceeded.] “It was to save them from absolute destruction I parted with your dear present, notwithstanding all the value I had for it: I sold the horse for them, and they have every farthing of the money.”\n\nMr Allworthy now stood silent for some moments, and before he spoke the tears started from his eyes. He at length dismissed Tom with a gentle rebuke, advising him for the future to apply to him in cases of distress, rather than to use extraordinary means of relieving them himself.\n\nThis affair was afterwards the subject of much debate between Thwackum and Square. Thwackum held, that this was flying in Mr Allworthy's face, who had intended to punish the fellow for his disobedience. He said, in some instances, what the world called charity appeared to him to be opposing the will of the Almighty, which had marked some particular persons for destruction; and that this was in like manner acting in opposition to Mr Allworthy; concluding, as usual, with a hearty recommendation of birch.\n\nSquare argued strongly on the other side, in opposition perhaps to Thwackum, or in compliance with Mr Allworthy, who seemed very much to approve what Jones had done. As to what he urged on this occasion, as I am convinced most of my readers will be much abler advocates for poor Jones, it would be impertinent to relate it. Indeed it was not difficult to reconcile to the rule of right an action which it would have been impossible to deduce from the rule of wrong.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\nContaining an incident of a more heinous kind, with the comments of Thwackum and Square.\n\nIt hath been observed by some man of much greater reputation for wisdom than myself, that misfortunes seldom come single. An instance of this may, I believe, be seen in those gentlemen who have the misfortune to have any of their rogueries detected; for here discovery seldom stops till the whole is come out. Thus it happened to poor Tom; who was no sooner pardoned for selling the horse, than he was discovered to have some time before sold a fine Bible which Mr Allworthy gave him, the money arising from which sale he had disposed of in the same manner. This Bible Master Blifil had purchased, though he had already such another of his own, partly out of respect for the book, and partly out of friendship to Tom, being unwilling that the Bible should be sold out of the family at half-price. He therefore deposited the said half-price himself; for he was a very prudent lad, and so careful of his money, that he had laid up almost every penny which he had received from Mr Allworthy.\n\nSome people have been noted to be able to read in no book but their own. On the contrary, from the time when Master Blifil was first possessed of this Bible, he never used any other. Nay, he was seen reading in it much oftener than he had before been in his own. Now, as he frequently asked Thwackum to explain difficult passages to him, that gentleman unfortunately took notice of Tom's name, which was written in many parts of the book. This brought on an inquiry, which obliged Master Blifil to discover the whole matter.\n\nThwackum was resolved a crime of this kind, which he called sacrilege, should not go unpunished. He therefore proceeded immediately to castigation: and not contented with that he acquainted Mr Allworthy, at their next meeting, with this monstrous crime, as it appeared to him: inveighing against Tom in the most bitter terms, and likening him to the buyers and sellers who were driven out of the temple.\n\nSquare saw this matter in a very different light. He said, he could not perceive any higher crime in selling one book than in selling another. That to sell Bibles was strictly lawful by all laws both Divine and human, and consequently there was no unfitness in it. He told Thwackum, that his great concern on this occasion brought to his mind the story of a very devout woman, who, out of pure regard to religion, stole Tillotson's Sermons from a lady of her acquaintance.\n\nThis story caused a vast quantity of blood to rush into the parson's face, which of itself was none of the palest; and he was going to reply with great warmth and anger, had not Mrs Blifil, who was present at this debate, interposed. That lady declared herself absolutely of Mr Square's side. She argued, indeed, very learnedly in support of his opinion; and concluded with saying, if Tom had been guilty of any fault, she must confess her own son appeared to be equally culpable; for that she could see no difference between the buyer and the seller; both of whom were alike to be driven out of the temple.\n\nMrs Blifil having declared her opinion, put an end to the debate. Square's triumph would almost have stopt his words, had he needed them; and Thwackum, who, for reasons before-mentioned, durst not venture at disobliging the lady, was almost choaked with indignation. As to Mr Allworthy, he said, since the boy had been already punished he would not deliver his sentiments on the occasion; and whether he was or was not angry with the lad, I must leave to the reader's own conjecture.\n\nSoon after this, an action was brought against the gamekeeper by Squire Western (the gentleman in whose manor the partridge was killed), for depredations of the like kind. This was a most unfortunate circumstance for the fellow, as it not only of itself threatened his ruin, but actually prevented Mr Allworthy from restoring him to his favour: for as that gentleman was walking out one evening with Master Blifil and young Jones, the latter slily drew him to the habitation of Black George; where the family of that poor wretch, namely, his wife and children, were found in all the misery with which cold, hunger, and nakedness, can affect human creatures: for as to the money they had received from Jones, former debts had consumed almost the whole.\n\nSuch a scene as this could not fail of affecting the heart of Mr Allworthy. He immediately gave the mother a couple of guineas, with which he bid her cloath her children. The poor woman burst into tears at this goodness, and while she was thanking him, could not refrain from expressing her gratitude to Tom; who had, she said, long preserved both her and hers from starving. “We have not,” says she, “had a morsel to eat, nor have these poor children had a rag to put on, but what his goodness hath bestowed on us.” For, indeed, besides the horse and the Bible, Tom had sacrificed a night-gown, and other things, to the use of this distressed family.\n\nOn their return home, Tom made use of all his eloquence to display the wretchedness of these people, and the penitence of Black George himself; and in this he succeeded so well, that Mr Allworthy said, he thought the man had suffered enough for what was past; that he would forgive him, and think of some means of providing for him and his family.\n\nJones was so delighted with this news, that, though it was dark when they returned home, he could not help going back a mile, in a shower of rain, to acquaint the poor woman with the glad tidings; but, like other hasty divulgers of news, he only brought on himself the trouble of contradicting it: for the ill fortune of Black George made use of the very opportunity of his friend's absence to overturn all again.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nIn which Master Blifil and Jones appear in different lights.\n\nMaster Blifil fell very short of his companion in the amiable quality of mercy; but he as greatly exceeded him in one of a much higher kind, namely, in justice: in which he followed both the precepts and example of Thwackum and Square; for though they would both make frequent use of the word mercy, yet it was plain that in reality Square held it to be inconsistent with the rule of right; and Thwackum was for doing justice, and leaving mercy to heaven. The two gentlemen did indeed somewhat differ in opinion concerning the objects of this sublime virtue; by which Thwackum would probably have destroyed one half of mankind, and Square the other half.\n\nMaster Blifil then, though he had kept silence in the presence of Jones, yet, when he had better considered the matter, could by no means endure the thought of suffering his uncle to confer favours on the undeserving. He therefore resolved immediately to acquaint him with the fact which we have above slightly hinted to the readers. The truth of which was as follows:\n\nThe gamekeeper, about a year after he was dismissed from Mr Allworthy's service, and before Tom's selling the horse, being in want of bread, either to fill his own mouth or those of his family, as he passed through a field belonging to Mr Western espied a hare sitting in her form. This hare he had basely and barbarously knocked on the head, against the laws of the land, and no less against the laws of sportsmen.\n\nThe higgler to whom the hare was sold, being unfortunately taken many months after with a quantity of game upon him, was obliged to make his peace with the squire, by becoming evidence against some poacher. And now Black George was pitched upon by him, as being a person already obnoxious to Mr Western, and one of no good fame in the country. He was, besides, the best sacrifice the higgler could make, as he had supplied him with no game since; and by this means the witness had an opportunity of screening his better customers: for the squire, being charmed with the power of punishing Black George, whom a single transgression was sufficient to ruin, made no further enquiry.\n\nHad this fact been truly laid before Mr Allworthy, it might probably have done the gamekeeper very little mischief. But there is no zeal blinder than that which is inspired with the love of justice against offenders. Master Blifil had forgot the distance of the time. He varied likewise in the manner of the fact: and by the hasty addition of the single letter S he considerably altered the story; for he said that George had wired hares. These alterations might probably have been set right, had not Master Blifil unluckily insisted on a promise of secrecy from Mr Allworthy before he revealed the matter to him; but by that means the poor gamekeeper was condemned without having an opportunity to defend himself: for as the fact of killing the hare, and of the action brought, were certainly true, Mr Allworthy had no doubt concerning the rest.\n\nShort-lived then was the joy of these poor people; for Mr Allworthy the next morning declared he had fresh reason, without assigning it, for his anger, and strictly forbad Tom to mention George any more: though as for his family, he said he would endeavour to keep them from starving; but as to the fellow himself, he would leave him to the laws, which nothing could keep him from breaking.\n\nTom could by no means divine what had incensed Mr Allworthy, for of Master Blifil he had not the least suspicion. However, as his friendship was to be tired out by no disappointments, he now determined to try another method of preserving the poor gamekeeper from ruin.\n\nJones was lately grown very intimate with Mr Western. He had so greatly recommended himself to that gentleman, by leaping over five-barred gates, and by other acts of sportsmanship, that the squire had declared Tom would certainly make a great man if he had but sufficient encouragement. He often wished he had himself a son with such parts; and one day very solemnly asserted at a drinking bout, that Tom should hunt a pack of hounds for a thousand pound of his money, with any huntsman in the whole country.\n\nBy such kind of talents he had so ingratiated himself with the squire, that he was a most welcome guest at his table, and a favourite companion in his sport: everything which the squire held most dear, to wit, his guns, dogs, and horses, were now as much at the command of Jones, as if they had been his own. He resolved therefore to make use of this favour on behalf of his friend Black George, whom he hoped to introduce into Mr Western's family, in the same capacity in which he had before served Mr Allworthy.\n\nThe reader, if he considers that this fellow was already obnoxious to Mr Western, and if he considers farther the weighty business by which that gentleman's displeasure had been incurred, will perhaps condemn this as a foolish and desperate undertaking; but if he should totally condemn young Jones on that account, he will greatly applaud him for strengthening himself with all imaginable interest on so arduous an occasion.\n\nFor this purpose, then, Tom applied to Mr Western's daughter, a young lady of about seventeen years of age, whom her father, next after those necessary implements of sport just before mentioned, loved and esteemed above all the world. Now, as she had some influence on the squire, so Tom had some little influence on her. But this being the intended heroine of this work, a lady with whom we ourselves are greatly in love, and with whom many of our readers will probably be in love too, before we part, it is by no means proper she should make her appearance at the end of a book.\n\nContaining five pages of paper.\n\nAs truth distinguishes our writings from those idle romances which are filled with monsters, the productions, not of nature, but of distempered brains; and which have been therefore recommended by an eminent critic to the sole use of the pastry-cook; so, on the other hand, we would avoid any resemblance to that kind of history which a celebrated poet seems to think is no less calculated for the emolument of the brewer, as the reading it should be always attended with a tankard of good ale--\n\nWhile--history with her comrade ale, Soothes the sad series of her serious tale\n\nFor as this is the liquor of modern historians, nay, perhaps their muse, if we may believe the opinion of Butler, who attributes inspiration to ale, it ought likewise to be the potation of their readers, since every book ought to be read with the same spirit and in the same manner as it is writ. Thus the famous author of Hurlothrumbo told a learned bishop, that the reason his lordship could not taste the excellence of his piece was, that he did not read it with a fiddle in his hand; which instrument he himself had always had in his own, when he composed it.\n\nThat our work, therefore, might be in no danger of being likened to the labours of these historians, we have taken every occasion of interspersing through the whole sundry similes, descriptions, and other kind of poetical embellishments. These are, indeed, designed to supply the place of the said ale, and to refresh the mind, whenever those slumbers, which in a long work are apt to invade the reader as well as the writer, shall begin to creep upon him. Without interruptions of this kind, the best narrative of plain matter of fact must overpower every reader; for nothing but the ever lasting watchfulness, which Homer has ascribed only to Jove himself, can be proof against a newspaper of many volumes.\n\nWe shall leave to the reader to determine with what judgment we have chosen the several occasions for inserting those ornamental parts of our work. Surely it will be allowed that none could be more proper than the present, where we are about to introduce a considerable character on the scene; no less, indeed, than the heroine of this heroic, historical, prosaic poem. Here, therefore, we have thought proper to prepare the mind of the reader for her reception, by filling it with every pleasing image which we can draw from the face of nature. And for this method we plead many precedents. First, this is an art well known to, and much practised by, our tragick poets, who seldom fail to prepare their audience for the reception of their principal characters.\n\nThus the heroe is always introduced with a flourish of drums and trumpets, in order to rouse a martial spirit in the audience, and to accommodate their ears to bombast and fustian, which Mr Locke's blind man would not have grossly erred in likening to the sound of a trumpet. Again, when lovers are coming forth, soft music often conducts them on the stage, either to soothe the audience with the softness of the tender passion, or to lull and prepare them for that gentle slumber in which they will most probably be composed by the ensuing scene.\n\nAnd not only the poets, but the masters of these poets, the managers of playhouses, seem to be in this secret; for, besides the aforesaid kettle-drums, &c., which denote the heroe's approach, he is generally ushered on the stage by a large troop of half a dozen scene-shifters; and how necessary these are imagined to his appearance, may be concluded from the following theatrical story:--\n\nKing Pyrrhus was at dinner at an ale-house bordering on the theatre, when he was summoned to go on the stage. The heroe, being unwilling to quit his shoulder of mutton, and as unwilling to draw on himself the indignation of Mr Wilks (his brother-manager) for making the audience wait, had bribed these his harbingers to be out of the way. While Mr Wilks, therefore, was thundering out, “Where are the carpenters to walk on before King Pyrrhus?” that monarch very quietly eat his mutton, and the audience, however impatient, were obliged to entertain themselves with music in his absence.\n\nTo be plain, I much question whether the politician, who hath generally a good nose, hath not scented out somewhat of the utility of this practice. I am convinced that awful magistrate my lord-mayor contracts a good deal of that reverence which attends him through the year, by the several pageants which precede his pomp. Nay, I must confess, that even I myself, who am not remarkably liable to be captivated with show, have yielded not a little to the impressions of much preceding state. When I have seen a man strutting in a procession, after others whose business was only to walk before him, I have conceived a higher notion of his dignity than I have felt on seeing him in a common situation. But there is one instance, which comes exactly up to my purpose. This is the custom of sending on a basket-woman, who is to precede the pomp at a coronation, and to strew the stage with flowers, before the great personages begin their procession. The antients would certainly have invoked the goddess Flora for this purpose, and it would have been no difficulty for their priests, or politicians to have persuaded the people of the real presence of the deity, though a plain mortal had personated her and performed her office. But we have no such design of imposing on our reader; and therefore those who object to the heathen theology, may, if they please, change our goddess into the above-mentioned basket-woman. Our intention, in short, is to introduce our heroine with the utmost solemnity in our power, with an elevation of stile, and all other circumstances proper to raise the veneration of our reader.--Indeed we would, for certain causes, advise those of our male readers who have any hearts, to read no farther, were we not well assured, that how amiable soever the picture of our heroine will appear, as it is really a copy from nature, many of our fair countrywomen will be found worthy to satisfy any passion, and to answer any idea of female perfection which our pencil will be able to raise.\n\nAnd now, without any further preface, we proceed to our next chapter.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nA short hint of what we can do in the sublime, and a description of Miss Sophia Western.\nHushed be every ruder breath. May the heathen ruler of the winds confine in iron chains the boisterous limbs of noisy Boreas, and the sharp-pointed nose of bitter-biting Eurus. Do thou, sweet Zephyrus, rising from thy fragrant bed, mount the western sky, and lead on those delicious gales, the charms of which call forth the lovely Flora from her chamber, perfumed with pearly dews, when on the 1st of June, her birth-day, the blooming maid, in loose attire, gently trips it over the verdant mead, where every flower rises to do her homage, till the whole field becomes enamelled, and colours contend with sweets which shall ravish her most.\n\nSo charming may she now appear! and you the feathered choristers of nature, whose sweetest notes not even Handel can excell, tune your melodious throats to celebrate her appearance. From love proceeds your music, and to love it returns. Awaken therefore that gentle passion in every swain: for lo! adorned with all the charms in which nature can array her; bedecked with beauty, youth, sprightliness, innocence, modesty, and tenderness, breathing sweetness from her rosy lips, and darting brightness from her sparkling eyes, the lovely Sophia comes!\n\nReader, perhaps thou hast seen the statue of the Venus de Medicis. Perhaps, too, thou hast seen the gallery of beauties at Hampton Court. Thou may'st remember each bright Churchill of the galaxy, and all the toasts of the Kit-cat. Or, if their reign was before thy times, at least thou hast seen their daughters, the no less dazzling beauties of the present age; whose names, should we here insert, we apprehend they would fill the whole volume.\n\nNow if thou hast seen all these, be not afraid of the rude answer which Lord Rochester once gave to a man who had seen many things. No. If thou hast seen all these without knowing what beauty is, thou hast no eyes; if without feeling its power, thou hast no heart.\n\nYet is it possible, my friend, that thou mayest have seen all these without being able to form an exact idea of Sophia; for she did not exactly resemble any of them. She was most like the picture of Lady Ranelagh: and, I have heard, more still to the famous dutchess of Mazarine; but most of all she resembled one whose image never can depart from my breast, and whom, if thou dost remember, thou hast then, my friend, an adequate idea of Sophia.\n\nBut lest this should not have been thy fortune, we will endeavour with our utmost skill to describe this paragon, though we are sensible that our highest abilities are very inadequate to the task.\n\nSophia, then, the only daughter of Mr Western, was a middle-sized woman; but rather inclining to tall. Her shape was not only exact, but extremely delicate: and the nice proportion of her arms promised the truest symmetry in her limbs. Her hair, which was black, was so luxuriant, that it reached her middle, before she cut it to comply with the modern fashion; and it was now curled so gracefully in her neck, that few could believe it to be her own. If envy could find any part of the face which demanded less commendation than the rest, it might possibly think her forehead might have been higher without prejudice to her. Her eyebrows were full, even, and arched beyond the power of art to imitate. Her black eyes had a lustre in them, which all her softness could not extinguish. Her nose was exactly regular, and her mouth, in which were two rows of ivory, exactly answered Sir John Suckling's description in those lines:--\n\nHer lips were red, and one was thin, Compar'd to that was next her chin. Some bee had stung it newly.\n\nHer cheeks were of the oval kind; and in her right she had a dimple, which the least smile discovered. Her chin had certainly its share in forming the beauty of her face; but it was difficult to say it was either large or small, though perhaps it was rather of the former kind. Her complexion had rather more of the lily than of the rose; but when exercise or modesty increased her natural colour, no vermilion could equal it. Then one might indeed cry out with the celebrated Dr Donne:\n\n--Her pure and eloquent blood Spoke in her cheeks, and so distinctly wrought That one might almost say her body thought.\n\nHer neck was long and finely turned: and here, if I was not afraid of offending her delicacy, I might justly say, the highest beauties of the famous Venus de Medicis were outdone. Here was whiteness which no lilies, ivory, nor alabaster could match. The finest cambric might indeed be supposed from envy to cover that bosom which was much whiter than itself.--It was indeed,\n\nNitor splendens Pario marmore purius.\n\nA gloss shining beyond the purest brightness of Parian marble.\n\nSuch was the outside of Sophia; nor was this beautiful frame disgraced by an inhabitant unworthy of it. Her mind was every way equal to her person; nay, the latter borrowed some charms from the former; for when she smiled, the sweetness of her temper diffused that glory over her countenance which no regularity of features can give. But as there are no perfections of the mind which do not discover themselves in that perfect intimacy to which we intend to introduce our reader with this charming young creature, so it is needless to mention them here: nay, it is a kind of tacit affront to our reader's understanding, and may also rob him of that pleasure which he will receive in forming his own judgment of her character.\n\nIt may, however, be proper to say, that whatever mental accomplishments she had derived from nature, they were somewhat improved and cultivated by art: for she had been educated under the care of an aunt, who was a lady of great discretion, and was thoroughly acquainted with the world, having lived in her youth about the court, whence she had retired some years since into the country. By her conversation and instructions, Sophia was perfectly well bred, though perhaps she wanted a little of that ease in her behaviour which is to be acquired only by habit, and living within what is called the polite circle. But this, to say the truth, is often too dearly purchased; and though it hath charms so inexpressible, that the French, perhaps, among other qualities, mean to express this, when they declare they know not what it is; yet its absence is well compensated by innocence; nor can good sense and a natural gentility ever stand in need of it.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nWherein the history goes back to commemorate a trifling incident that happened some years since; but which, trifling as it was, had some future consequences.\n\nThe amiable Sophia was now in her eighteenth year, when she is introduced into this history. Her father, as hath been said, was fonder of her than of any other human creature. To her, therefore, Tom Jones applied, in order to engage her interest on the behalf of his friend the gamekeeper.\n\nBut before we proceed to this business, a short recapitulation of some previous matters may be necessary.\n\nThough the different tempers of Mr Allworthy and of Mr Western did not admit of a very intimate correspondence, yet they lived upon what is called a decent footing together; by which means the young people of both families had been acquainted from their infancy; and as they were all near of the same age, had been frequent playmates together.\n\nThe gaiety of Tom's temper suited better with Sophia, than the grave and sober disposition of Master Blifil. And the preference which she gave the former of these, would often appear so plainly, that a lad of a more passionate turn than Master Blifil was, might have shown some displeasure at it.\n\nAs he did not, however, outwardly express any such disgust, it would be an ill office in us to pay a visit to the inmost recesses of his mind, as some scandalous people search into the most secret affairs of their friends, and often pry into their closets and cupboards, only to discover their poverty and meanness to the world.\n\nHowever, as persons who suspect they have given others cause of offence, are apt to conclude they are offended; so Sophia imputed an action of Master Blifil to his anger, which the superior sagacity of Thwackum and Square discerned to have arisen from a much better principle.\n\nTom Jones, when very young, had presented Sophia with a little bird, which he had taken from the nest, had nursed up, and taught to sing.\n\nOf this bird, Sophia, then about thirteen years old, was so extremely fond, that her chief business was to feed and tend it, and her chief pleasure to play with it. By these means little Tommy, for so the bird was called, was become so tame, that it would feed out of the hand of its mistress, would perch upon the finger, and lie contented in her bosom, where it seemed almost sensible of its own happiness; though she always kept a small string about its leg, nor would ever trust it with the liberty of flying away.\n\nOne day, when Mr Allworthy and his whole family dined at Mr Western's, Master Blifil, being in the garden with little Sophia, and observing the extreme fondness that she showed for her little bird, desired her to trust it for a moment in his hands. Sophia presently complied with the young gentleman's request, and after some previous caution, delivered him her bird; of which he was no sooner in possession, than he slipt the string from its leg and tossed it into the air.\n\nThe foolish animal no sooner perceived itself at liberty, than forgetting all the favours it had received from Sophia, it flew directly from her, and perched on a bough at some distance.\n\nSophia, seeing her bird gone, screamed out so loud, that Tom Jones, who was at a little distance, immediately ran to her assistance.\n\nHe was no sooner informed of what had happened, than he cursed Blifil for a pitiful malicious rascal; and then immediately stripping off his coat he applied himself to climbing the tree to which the bird escaped.\n\nTom had almost recovered his little namesake, when the branch on which it was perched, and that hung over a canal, broke, and the poor lad plumped over head and ears into the water.\n\nSophia's concern now changed its object. And as she apprehended the boy's life was in danger, she screamed ten times louder than before; and indeed Master Blifil himself now seconded her with all the vociferation in his power.\n\nThe company, who were sitting in a room next the garden, were instantly alarmed, and came all forth; but just as they reached the canal, Tom (for the water was luckily pretty shallow in that part) arrived safely on shore.\n\nThwackum fell violently on poor Tom, who stood dropping and shivering before him, when Mr Allworthy desired him to have patience; and turning to Master Blifil, said, “Pray, child, what is the reason of all this disturbance?”\n\nMaster Blifil answered, “Indeed, uncle, I am very sorry for what I have done; I have been unhappily the occasion of it all. I had Miss Sophia's bird in my hand, and thinking the poor creature languished for liberty, I own I could not forbear giving it what it desired; for I always thought there was something very cruel in confining anything. It seemed to be against the law of nature, by which everything hath a right to liberty; nay, it is even unchristian, for it is not doing what we would be done by; but if I had imagined Miss Sophia would have been so much concerned at it, I am sure I never would have done it; nay, if I had known what would have happened to the bird itself: for when Master Jones, who climbed up that tree after it, fell into the water, the bird took a second flight, and presently a nasty hawk carried it away.”\n\nPoor Sophia, who now first heard of her little Tommy's fate (for her concern for Jones had prevented her perceiving it when it happened), shed a shower of tears. These Mr Allworthy endeavoured to assuage, promising her a much finer bird: but she declared she would never have another. Her father chid her for crying so for a foolish bird; but could not help telling young Blifil, if he was a son of his, his backside should be well flead.\n\nSophia now returned to her chamber, the two young gentlemen were sent home, and the rest of the company returned to their bottle; where a conversation ensued on the subject of the bird, so curious, that we think it deserves a chapter by itself.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nContaining such very deep and grave matters, that some readers, perhaps, may not relish it.\nSquare had no sooner lighted his pipe, than, addressing himself to Allworthy, he thus began: “Sir, I cannot help congratulating you on your nephew; who, at an age when few lads have any ideas but of sensible objects, is arrived at a capacity of distinguishing right from wrong. To confine anything, seems to me against the law of nature, by which everything hath a right to liberty. These were his words; and the impression they have made on me is never to be eradicated. Can any man have a higher notion of the rule of right, and the eternal fitness of things? I cannot help promising myself, from such a dawn, that the meridian of this youth will be equal to that of either the elder or the younger Brutus.”\n\nHere Thwackum hastily interrupted, and spilling some of his wine, and swallowing the rest with great eagerness, answered, “From another expression he made use of, I hope he will resemble much better men. The law of nature is a jargon of words, which means nothing. I know not of any such law, nor of any right which can be derived from it. To do as we would be done by, is indeed a Christian motive, as the boy well expressed himself; and I am glad to find my instructions have borne such good fruit.”\n\n“If vanity was a thing fit,” says Square, “I might indulge some on the same occasion; for whence only he can have learnt his notions of right or wrong, I think is pretty apparent. If there be no law of nature, there is no right nor wrong.”\n\n“How!” says the parson, “do you then banish revelation? Am I talking with a deist or an atheist?”\n\n“Drink about,” says Western. “Pox of your laws of nature! I don't know what you mean, either of you, by right and wrong. To take away my girl's bird was wrong, in my opinion; and my neighbour Allworthy may do as he pleases; but to encourage boys in such practices, is to breed them up to the gallows.”\n\nAllworthy answered, “That he was sorry for what his nephew had done, but could not consent to punish him, as he acted rather from a generous than unworthy motive.” He said, “If the boy had stolen the bird, none would have been more ready to vote for a severe chastisement than himself; but it was plain that was not his design:” and, indeed, it was as apparent to him, that he could have no other view but what he had himself avowed. (For as to that malicious purpose which Sophia suspected, it never once entered into the head of Mr Allworthy.) He at length concluded with again blaming the action as inconsiderate, and which, he said, was pardonable only in a child.\n\nSquare had delivered his opinion so openly, that if he was now silent, he must submit to have his judgment censured. He said, therefore, with some warmth, “That Mr Allworthy had too much respect to the dirty consideration of property. That in passing our judgments on great and mighty actions, all private regards should be laid aside; for by adhering to those narrow rules, the younger Brutus had been condemned of ingratitude, and the elder of parricide.”\n\n“And if they had been hanged too for those crimes,” cried Thwackum, “they would have had no more than their deserts. A couple of heathenish villains! Heaven be praised we have no Brutuses now-a-days! I wish, Mr Square, you would desist from filling the minds of my pupils with such antichristian stuff; for the consequence must be, while they are under my care, its being well scourged out of them again. There is your disciple Tom almost spoiled already. I overheard him the other day disputing with Master Blifil that there was no merit in faith without works. I know that is one of your tenets, and I suppose he had it from you.”\n\n“Don't accuse me of spoiling him,” says Square. “Who taught him to laugh at whatever is virtuous and decent, and fit and right in the nature of things? He is your own scholar, and I disclaim him. No, no, Master Blifil is my boy. Young as he is, that lad's notions of moral rectitude I defy you ever to eradicate.”\n\nThwackum put on a contemptuous sneer at this, and replied, “Ay, ay, I will venture him with you. He is too well grounded for all your philosophical cant to hurt. No, no, I have taken care to instil such principles into him--”\n\n“And I have instilled principles into him too,” cries Square. “What but the sublime idea of virtue could inspire a human mind with the generous thought of giving liberty? And I repeat to you again, if it was a fit thing to be proud, I might claim the honour of having infused that idea.”--\n\n“And if pride was not forbidden,” said Thwackum, “I might boast of having taught him that duty which he himself assigned as his motive.”\n\n“So between you both,” says the squire, “the young gentleman hath been taught to rob my daughter of her bird. I find I must take care of my partridge-mew. I shall have some virtuous religious man or other set all my partridges at liberty.” Then slapping a gentleman of the law, who was present, on the back, he cried out, “What say you to this, Mr Counsellor? Is not this against law?”\n\nThe lawyer with great gravity delivered himself as follows:--\n\n“If the case be put of a partridge, there can be no doubt but an action would lie; for though this be ferae naturae, yet being reclaimed, property vests: but being the case of a singing bird, though reclaimed, as it is a thing of base nature, it must be considered as nullius in bonis. In this case, therefore, I conceive the plaintiff must be non-suited; and I should disadvise the bringing any such action.”\n\n“Well,” says the squire, “if it be nullus bonus, let us drink about, and talk a little of the state of the nation, or some such discourse that we all understand; for I am sure I don't understand a word of this. It may be learning and sense for aught I know: but you shall never persuade me into it. Pox! you have neither of you mentioned a word of that poor lad who deserves to be commended: to venture breaking his neck to oblige my girl was a generous-spirited action: I have learning enough to see that. D--n me, here's Tom's health! I shall love the boy for it the longest day I have to live.”\n\nThus was the debate interrupted; but it would probably have been soon resumed, had not Mr Allworthy presently called for his coach, and carried off the two combatants.\n\nSuch was the conclusion of this adventure of the bird, and of the dialogue occasioned by it; which we could not help recounting to our reader, though it happened some years before that stage or period of time at which our history is now arrived.\n\nContaining matter accommodated to every taste.\n\n“Parva leves capiunt animos--Small things affect light minds,” was the sentiment of a great master of the passion of love. And certain it is, that from this day Sophia began to have some little kindness for Tom Jones, and no little aversion for his companion.\n\nMany accidents from time to time improved both these passions in her breast; which, without our recounting, the reader may well conclude, from what we have before hinted of the different tempers of these lads, and how much the one suited with her own inclinations more than the other. To say the truth, Sophia, when very young, discerned that Tom, though an idle, thoughtless, rattling rascal, was nobody's enemy but his own; and that Master Blifil, though a prudent, discreet, sober young gentleman, was at the same time strongly attached to the interest only of one single person; and who that single person was the reader will be able to divine without any assistance of ours.\n\nThese two characters are not always received in the world with the different regard which seems severally due to either; and which one would imagine mankind, from self-interest, should show towards them. But perhaps there may be a political reason for it: in finding one of a truly benevolent disposition, men may very reasonably suppose they have found a treasure, and be desirous of keeping it, like all other good things, to themselves. Hence they may imagine, that to trumpet forth the praises of such a person, would, in the vulgar phrase, be crying Roast-meat, and calling in partakers of what they intend to apply solely to their own use. If this reason does not satisfy the reader, I know no other means of accounting for the little respect which I have commonly seen paid to a character which really does great honour to human nature, and is productive of the highest good to society. But it was otherwise with Sophia. She honoured Tom Jones, and scorned Master Blifil, almost as soon as she knew the meaning of those two words.\n\nSophia had been absent upwards of three years with her aunt; during all which time she had seldom seen either of these young gentlemen. She dined, however, once, together with her aunt, at Mr Allworthy's. This was a few days after the adventure of the partridge, before commemorated. Sophia heard the whole story at table, where she said nothing: nor indeed could her aunt get many words from her as she returned home; but her maid, when undressing her, happening to say, “Well, miss, I suppose you have seen young Master Blifil to-day?” she answered with much passion, “I hate the name of Master Blifil, as I do whatever is base and treacherous: and I wonder Mr Allworthy would suffer that old barbarous schoolmaster to punish a poor boy so cruelly for what was only the effect of his good-nature.” She then recounted the story to her maid, and concluded with saying, “Don't you think he is a boy of noble spirit?”\n\nThis young lady was now returned to her father; who gave her the command of his house, and placed her at the upper end of his table, where Tom (who for his great love of hunting was become a great favourite of the squire) often dined. Young men of open, generous dispositions are naturally inclined to gallantry, which, if they have good understandings, as was in reality Tom's case, exerts itself in an obliging complacent behaviour to all women in general. This greatly distinguished Tom from the boisterous brutality of mere country squires on the one hand, and from the solemn and somewhat sullen deportment of Master Blifil on the other; and he began now, at twenty, to have the name of a pretty fellow among all the women in the neighbourhood.\n\nTom behaved to Sophia with no particularity, unless perhaps by showing her a higher respect than he paid to any other. This distinction her beauty, fortune, sense, and amiable carriage, seemed to demand; but as to design upon her person he had none; for which we shall at present suffer the reader to condemn him of stupidity; but perhaps we shall be able indifferently well to account for it hereafter.\n\nSophia, with the highest degree of innocence and modesty, had a remarkable sprightliness in her temper. This was so greatly increased whenever she was in company with Tom, that had he not been very young and thoughtless, he must have observed it: or had not Mr Western's thoughts been generally either in the field, the stable, or the dog-kennel, it might have perhaps created some jealousy in him: but so far was the good gentleman from entertaining any such suspicions, that he gave Tom every opportunity with his daughter which any lover could have wished; and this Tom innocently improved to better advantage, by following only the dictates of his natural gallantry and good-nature, than he might perhaps have done had he had the deepest designs on the young lady.\n\nBut indeed it can occasion little wonder that this matter escaped the observation of others, since poor Sophia herself never remarked it; and her heart was irretrievably lost before she suspected it was in danger.\n\nMatters were in this situation, when Tom, one afternoon, finding Sophia alone, began, after a short apology, with a very serious face, to acquaint her that he had a favour to ask of her which he hoped her goodness would comply with.\n\nThough neither the young man's behaviour, nor indeed his manner of opening this business, were such as could give her any just cause of suspecting he intended to make love to her; yet whether Nature whispered something into her ear, or from what cause it arose I will not determine; certain it is, some idea of that kind must have intruded itself; for her colour forsook her cheeks, her limbs trembled, and her tongue would have faltered, had Tom stopped for an answer; but he soon relieved her from her perplexity, by proceeding to inform her of his request; which was to solicit her interest on behalf of the gamekeeper, whose own ruin, and that of a large family, must be, he said, the consequence of Mr Western's pursuing his action against him.\n\nSophia presently recovered her confusion, and, with a smile full of sweetness, said, “Is this the mighty favour you asked with so much gravity? I will do it with all my heart. I really pity the poor fellow, and no longer ago than yesterday sent a small matter to his wife.” This small matter was one of her gowns, some linen, and ten shillings in money, of which Tom had heard, and it had, in reality, put this solicitation into his head.\n\nOur youth, now, emboldened with his success, resolved to push the matter farther, and ventured even to beg her recommendation of him to her father's service; protesting that he thought him one of the honestest fellows in the country, and extremely well qualified for the place of a gamekeeper, which luckily then happened to be vacant.\n\nSophia answered, “Well, I will undertake this too; but I cannot promise you as much success as in the former part, which I assure you I will not quit my father without obtaining. However, I will do what I can for the poor fellow; for I sincerely look upon him and his family as objects of great compassion. And now, Mr Jones, I must ask you a favour.”\n\n“A favour, madam!” cries Tom: “if you knew the pleasure you have given me in the hopes of receiving a command from you, you would think by mentioning it you did confer the greatest favour on me; for by this dear hand I would sacrifice my life to oblige you.”\n\nHe then snatched her hand, and eagerly kissed it, which was the first time his lips had ever touched her. The blood, which before had forsaken her cheeks, now made her sufficient amends, by rushing all over her face and neck with such violence, that they became all of a scarlet colour. She now first felt a sensation to which she had been before a stranger, and which, when she had leisure to reflect on it, began to acquaint her with some secrets, which the reader, if he doth not already guess them, will know in due time.\n\nSophia, as soon as she could speak (which was not instantly), informed him that the favour she had to desire of him was, not to lead her father through so many dangers in hunting; for that, from what she had heard, she was terribly frightened every time they went out together, and expected some day or other to see her father brought home with broken limbs. She therefore begged him, for her sake, to be more cautious; and as he well knew Mr Western would follow him, not to ride so madly, nor to take those dangerous leaps for the future.\n\nTom promised faithfully to obey her commands; and after thanking her for her kind compliance with his request, took his leave, and departed highly charmed with his success.\n\nPoor Sophia was charmed too, but in a very different way. Her sensations, however, the reader's heart (if he or she have any) will better represent than I can, if I had as many mouths as ever poet wished for, to eat, I suppose, those many dainties with which he was so plentifully provided.\n\nIt was Mr Western's custom every afternoon, as soon as he was drunk, to hear his daughter play on the harpsichord; for he was a great lover of music, and perhaps, had he lived in town, might have passed for a connoisseur; for he always excepted against the finest compositions of Mr Handel. He never relished any music but what was light and airy; and indeed his most favourite tunes were Old Sir Simon the King, St George he was for England, Bobbing Joan, and some others.\n\nHis daughter, though she was a perfect mistress of music, and would never willingly have played any but Handel's, was so devoted to her father's pleasure, that she learnt all those tunes to oblige him. However, she would now and then endeavour to lead him into her own taste; and when he required the repetition of his ballads, would answer with a “Nay, dear sir;” and would often beg him to suffer her to play something else.\n\nThis evening, however, when the gentleman was retired from his bottle, she played all his favourites three times over without any solicitation. This so pleased the good squire, that he started from his couch, gave his daughter a kiss, and swore her hand was greatly improved. She took this opportunity to execute her promise to Tom; in which she succeeded so well, that the squire declared, if she would give him t'other bout of Old Sir Simon, he would give the gamekeeper his deputation the next morning. Sir Simon was played again and again, till the charms of the music soothed Mr Western to sleep. In the morning Sophia did not fail to remind him of his engagement; and his attorney was immediately sent for, ordered to stop any further proceedings in the action, and to make out the deputation.\n\nTom's success in this affair soon began to ring over the country, and various were the censures passed upon it; some greatly applauding it as an act of good nature; others sneering, and saying, “No wonder that one idle fellow should love another.” Young Blifil was greatly enraged at it. He had long hated Black George in the same proportion as Jones delighted in him; not from any offence which he had ever received, but from his great love to religion and virtue;--for Black George had the reputation of a loose kind of a fellow. Blifil therefore represented this as flying in Mr Allworthy's face; and declared, with great concern, that it was impossible to find any other motive for doing good to such a wretch.\n\nThwackum and Square likewise sung to the same tune. They were now (especially the latter) become greatly jealous of young Jones with the widow; for he now approached the age of twenty, was really a fine young fellow, and that lady, by her encouragements to him, seemed daily more and more to think him so.\n\nAllworthy was not, however, moved with their malice. He declared himself very well satisfied with what Jones had done. He said the perseverance and integrity of his friendship was highly commendable, and he wished he could see more frequent instances of that virtue.\n\nBut Fortune, who seldom greatly relishes such sparks as my friend Tom, perhaps because they do not pay more ardent addresses to her, gave now a very different turn to all his actions, and showed them to Mr Allworthy in a light far less agreeable than that gentleman's goodness had hitherto seen them in.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nAn apology for the insensibility of Mr Jones to all the charms of the lovely Sophia; in which possibly we may, in a considerable degree, lower his character in the estimation of those men of wit and gallantry who approve the heroes in most of our modern comedies.\n\nThere are two sorts of people, who, I am afraid, have already conceived some contempt for my heroe, on account of his behaviour to Sophia. The former of these will blame his prudence in neglecting an opportunity to possess himself of Mr Western's fortune; and the latter will no less despise him for his backwardness to so fine a girl, who seemed ready to fly into his arms, if he would open them to receive her.\n\nNow, though I shall not perhaps be able absolutely to acquit him of either of these charges (for want of prudence admits of no excuse; and what I shall produce against the latter charge will, I apprehend, be scarce satisfactory); yet, as evidence may sometimes be offered in mitigation, I shall set forth the plain matter of fact, and leave the whole to the reader's determination.\n\nMr Jones had somewhat about him, which, though I think writers are not thoroughly agreed in its name, doth certainly inhabit some human breasts; whose use is not so properly to distinguish right from wrong, as to prompt and incite them to the former, and to restrain and withhold them from the latter.\n\nThis somewhat may be indeed resembled to the famous trunk-maker in the playhouse; for, whenever the person who is possessed of it doth what is right, no ravished or friendly spectator is so eager or so loud in his applause: on the contrary, when he doth wrong, no critic is so apt to hiss and explode him.\n\nTo give a higher idea of the principle I mean, as well as one more familiar to the present age; it may be considered as sitting on its throne in the mind, like the Lord High Chancellor of this kingdom in his court; where it presides, governs, directs, judges, acquits, and condemns according to merit and justice, with a knowledge which nothing escapes, a penetration which nothing can deceive, and an integrity which nothing can corrupt.\n\nThis active principle may perhaps be said to constitute the most essential barrier between us and our neighbours the brutes; for if there be some in the human shape who are not under any such dominion, I choose rather to consider them as deserters from us to our neighbours; among whom they will have the fate of deserters, and not be placed in the first rank.\n\nOur heroe, whether he derived it from Thwackum or Square I will not determine, was very strongly under the guidance of this principle; for though he did not always act rightly, yet he never did otherwise without feeling and suffering for it. It was this which taught him, that to repay the civilities and little friendships of hospitality by robbing the house where you have received them, is to be the basest and meanest of thieves. He did not think the baseness of this offence lessened by the height of the injury committed; on the contrary, if to steal another's plate deserved death and infamy, it seemed to him difficult to assign a punishment adequate to the robbing a man of his whole fortune, and of his child into the bargain.\n\nThis principle, therefore, prevented him from any thought of making his fortune by such means (for this, as I have said, is an active principle, and doth not content itself with knowledge or belief only). Had he been greatly enamoured of Sophia, he possibly might have thought otherwise; but give me leave to say, there is great difference between running away with a man's daughter from the motive of love, and doing the same thing from the motive of theft.\n\nNow, though this young gentleman was not insensible of the charms of Sophia; though he greatly liked her beauty, and esteemed all her other qualifications, she had made, however, no deep impression on his heart; for which, as it renders him liable to the charge of stupidity, or at least of want of taste, we shall now proceed to account.\n\nThe truth then is, his heart was in the possession of another woman. Here I question not but the reader will be surprized at our long taciturnity as to this matter; and quite at a loss to divine who this woman was, since we have hitherto not dropt a hint of any one likely to be a rival to Sophia; for as to Mrs Blifil, though we have been obliged to mention some suspicions of her affection for Tom, we have not hitherto given the least latitude for imagining that he had any for her; and, indeed, I am sorry to say it, but the youth of both sexes are too apt to be deficient in their gratitude for that regard with which persons more advanced in years are sometimes so kind to honour them.\n\nThat the reader may be no longer in suspense, he will be pleased to remember, that we have often mentioned the family of George Seagrim (commonly called Black George, the gamekeeper), which consisted at present of a wife and five children.\n\nThe second of these children was a daughter, whose name was Molly, and who was esteemed one of the handsomest girls in the whole country.\n\nCongreve well says there is in true beauty something which vulgar souls cannot admire; so can no dirt or rags hide this something from those souls which are not of the vulgar stamp.\n\nThe beauty of this girl made, however, no impression on Tom, till she grew towards the age of sixteen, when Tom, who was near three years older, began first to cast the eyes of affection upon her. And this affection he had fixed on the girl long before he could bring himself to attempt the possession of her person: for though his constitution urged him greatly to this, his principles no less forcibly restrained him. To debauch a young woman, however low her condition was, appeared to him a very heinous crime; and the good-will he bore the father, with the compassion he had for his family, very strongly corroborated all such sober reflections; so that he once resolved to get the better of his inclinations, and he actually abstained three whole months without ever going to Seagrim's house, or seeing his daughter.\n\nNow, though Molly was, as we have said, generally thought a very fine girl, and in reality she was so, yet her beauty was not of the most amiable kind. It had, indeed, very little of feminine in it, and would have become a man at least as well as a woman; for, to say the truth, youth and florid health had a very considerable share in the composition.\n\nNor was her mind more effeminate than her person. As this was tall and robust, so was that bold and forward. So little had she of modesty, that Jones had more regard for her virtue than she herself. And as most probably she liked Tom as well as he liked her, so when she perceived his backwardness she herself grew proportionably forward; and when she saw he had entirely deserted the house, she found means of throwing herself in his way, and behaved in such a manner that the youth must have had very much or very little of the heroe if her endeavours had proved unsuccessful. In a word, she soon triumphed over all the virtuous resolutions of Jones; for though she behaved at last with all decent reluctance, yet I rather chuse to attribute the triumph to her, since, in fact, it was her design which succeeded.\n\nIn the conduct of this matter, I say, Molly so well played her part, that Jones attributed the conquest entirely to himself, and considered the young woman as one who had yielded to the violent attacks of his passion. He likewise imputed her yielding to the ungovernable force of her love towards him; and this the reader will allow to have been a very natural and probable supposition, as we have more than once mentioned the uncommon comeliness of his person: and, indeed, he was one of the handsomest young fellows in the world.\n\nAs there are some minds whose affections, like Master Blifil's, are solely placed on one single person, whose interest and indulgence alone they consider on every occasion; regarding the good and ill of all others as merely indifferent, any farther than as they contribute to the pleasure or advantage of that person: so there is a different temper of mind which borrows a degree of virtue even from self-love. Such can never receive any kind of satisfaction from another, without loving the creature to whom that satisfaction is owing, and without making its well-being in some sort necessary to their own ease.\n\nOf this latter species was our heroe. He considered this poor girl as one whose happiness or misery he had caused to be dependent on himself. Her beauty was still the object of desire, though greater beauty, or a fresher object, might have been more so; but the little abatement which fruition had occasioned to this was highly overbalanced by the considerations of the affection which she visibly bore him, and of the situation into which he had brought her. The former of these created gratitude, the latter compassion; and both, together with his desire for her person, raised in him a passion which might, without any great violence to the word, be called love; though, perhaps, it was at first not very judiciously placed.\n\nThis, then, was the true reason of that insensibility which he had shown to the charms of Sophia, and that behaviour in her which might have been reasonably enough interpreted as an encouragement to his addresses; for as he could not think of abandoning his Molly, poor and destitute as she was, so no more could he entertain a notion of betraying such a creature as Sophia. And surely, had he given the least encouragement to any passion for that young lady, he must have been absolutely guilty of one or other of those crimes; either of which would, in my opinion, have very justly subjected him to that fate, which, at his first introduction into this history, I mentioned to have been generally predicted as his certain destiny.\n\nBeing the shortest chapter in this book.\n\nHer mother first perceived the alteration in the shape of Molly; and in order to hide it from her neighbours, she foolishly clothed her in that sack which Sophia had sent her; though, indeed, that young lady had little apprehension that the poor woman would have been weak enough to let any of her daughters wear it in that form.\n\nMolly was charmed with the first opportunity she ever had of showing her beauty to advantage; for though she could very well bear to contemplate herself in the glass, even when dressed in rags; and though she had in that dress conquered the heart of Jones, and perhaps of some others; yet she thought the addition of finery would much improve her charms, and extend her conquests.\n\nMolly, therefore, having dressed herself out in this sack, with a new laced cap, and some other ornaments which Tom had given her, repairs to church with her fan in her hand the very next Sunday. The great are deceived if they imagine they have appropriated ambition and vanity to themselves. These noble qualities flourish as notably in a country church and churchyard as in the drawing-room, or in the closet. Schemes have indeed been laid in the vestry which would hardly disgrace the conclave. Here is a ministry, and here is an opposition. Here are plots and circumventions, parties and factions, equal to those which are to be found in courts.\n\nNor are the women here less practised in the highest feminine arts than their fair superiors in quality and fortune. Here are prudes and coquettes. Here are dressing and ogling, falsehood, envy, malice, scandal; in short, everything which is common to the most splendid assembly, or politest circle. Let those of high life, therefore, no longer despise the ignorance of their inferiors; nor the vulgar any longer rail at the vices of their betters.\n\nMolly had seated herself some time before she was known by her neighbours. And then a whisper ran through the whole congregation, “Who is she?” but when she was discovered, such sneering, gigling, tittering, and laughing ensued among the women, that Mr Allworthy was obliged to exert his authority to preserve any decency among them.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nA battle sung by the muse in the Homerican style, and which none but the classical reader can taste.\n\nMr Western had an estate in this parish; and as his house stood at little greater distance from this church than from his own, he very often came to Divine Service here; and both he and the charming Sophia happened to be present at this time.\n\nSophia was much pleased with the beauty of the girl, whom she pitied for her simplicity in having dressed herself in that manner, as she saw the envy which it had occasioned among her equals. She no sooner came home than she sent for the gamekeeper, and ordered him to bring his daughter to her; saying she would provide for her in the family, and might possibly place the girl about her own person, when her own maid, who was now going away, had left her.\n\nPoor Seagrim was thunderstruck at this; for he was no stranger to the fault in the shape of his daughter. He answered, in a stammering voice, “That he was afraid Molly would be too awkward to wait on her ladyship, as she had never been at service.” “No matter for that,” says Sophia; “she will soon improve. I am pleased with the girl, and am resolved to try her.”\n\nBlack George now repaired to his wife, on whose prudent counsel he depended to extricate him out of this dilemma; but when he came thither he found his house in some confusion. So great envy had this sack occasioned, that when Mr Allworthy and the other gentry were gone from church, the rage, which had hitherto been confined, burst into an uproar; and, having vented itself at first in opprobrious words, laughs, hisses, and gestures, betook itself at last to certain missile weapons; which, though from their plastic nature they threatened neither the loss of life or of limb, were however sufficiently dreadful to a well-dressed lady. Molly had too much spirit to bear this treatment tamely. Having therefore--but hold, as we are diffident of our own abilities, let us here invite a superior power to our assistance.\n\nYe Muses, then, whoever ye are, who love to sing battles, and principally thou who whilom didst recount the slaughter in those fields where Hudibras and Trulla fought, if thou wert not starved with thy friend Butler, assist me on this great occasion. All things are not in the power of all.\n\nAs a vast herd of cows in a rich farmer's yard, if, while they are milked, they hear their calves at a distance, lamenting the robbery which is then committing, roar and bellow; so roared forth the Somersetshire mob an hallaloo, made up of almost as many squalls, screams, and other different sounds as there were persons, or indeed passions among them: some were inspired by rage, others alarmed by fear, and others had nothing in their heads but the love of fun; but chiefly Envy, the sister of Satan, and his constant companion, rushed among the crowd, and blew up the fury of the women; who no sooner came up to Molly than they pelted her with dirt and rubbish.\n\nMolly, having endeavoured in vain to make a handsome retreat, faced about; and laying hold of ragged Bess, who advanced in the front of the enemy, she at one blow felled her to the ground. The whole army of the enemy (though near a hundred in number), seeing the fate of their general, gave back many paces, and retired behind a new-dug grave; for the churchyard was the field of battle, where there was to be a funeral that very evening. Molly pursued her victory, and catching up a skull which lay on the side of the grave, discharged it with such fury, that having hit a taylor on the head, the two skulls sent equally forth a hollow sound at their meeting, and the taylor took presently measure of his length on the ground, where the skulls lay side by side, and it was doubtful which was the more valuable of the two. Molly then taking a thigh-bone in her hand, fell in among the flying ranks, and dealing her blows with great liberality on either side, overthrew the carcass of many a mighty heroe and heroine.\n\nRecount, O Muse, the names of those who fell on this fatal day. First, Jemmy Tweedle felt on his hinder head the direful bone. Him the pleasant banks of sweetly-winding Stour had nourished, where he first learnt the vocal art, with which, wandering up and down at wakes and fairs, he cheered the rural nymphs and swains, when upon the green they interweaved the sprightly dance; while he himself stood fiddling and jumping to his own music. How little now avails his fiddle! He thumps the verdant floor with his carcass. Next, old Echepole, the sowgelder, received a blow in his forehead from our Amazonian heroine, and immediately fell to the ground. He was a swinging fat fellow, and fell with almost as much noise as a house. His tobacco-box dropped at the same time from his pocket, which Molly took up as lawful spoils. Then Kate of the Mill tumbled unfortunately over a tombstone, which catching hold of her ungartered stocking inverted the order of nature, and gave her heels the superiority to her head. Betty Pippin, with young Roger her lover, fell both to the ground; where, oh perverse fate! she salutes the earth, and he the sky. Tom Freckle, the smith's son, was the next victim to her rage. He was an ingenious workman, and made excellent pattens; nay, the very patten with which he was knocked down was his own workmanship. Had he been at that time singing psalms in the church, he would have avoided a broken head. Miss Crow, the daughter of a farmer; John Giddish, himself a farmer; Nan Slouch, Esther Codling, Will Spray, Tom Bennet; the three Misses Potter, whose father keeps the sign of the Red Lion; Betty Chambermaid, Jack Ostler, and many others of inferior note, lay rolling among the graves.\n\nNot that the strenuous arm of Molly reached all these; for many of them in their flight overthrew each other.\n\nBut now Fortune, fearing she had acted out of character, and had inclined too long to the same side, especially as it was the right side, hastily turned about: for now Goody Brown--whom Zekiel Brown caressed in his arms; nor he alone, but half the parish besides; so famous was she in the fields of Venus, nor indeed less in those of Mars. The trophies of both these her husband always bore about on his head and face; for if ever human head did by its horns display the amorous glories of a wife, Zekiel's did; nor did his well-scratched face less denote her talents (or rather talons) of a different kind.\n\nNo longer bore this Amazon the shameful flight of her party. She stopt short, and, calling aloud to all who fled, spoke as follows: “Ye Somersetshire men, or rather ye Somersetshire women, are ye not ashamed thus to fly from a single woman? But if no other will oppose her, I myself and Joan Top here will have the honour of the victory.” Having thus said, she flew at Molly Seagrim, and easily wrenched the thigh-bone from her hand, at the same time clawing off her cap from her head. Then laying hold of the hair of Molly with her left hand, she attacked her so furiously in the face with the right, that the blood soon began to trickle from her nose. Molly was not idle this while. She soon removed the clout from the head of Goody Brown, and then fastening on her hair with one hand, with the other she caused another bloody stream to issue forth from the nostrils of the enemy.\n\nWhen each of the combatants had borne off sufficient spoils of hair from the head of her antagonist, the next rage was against the garments. In this attack they exerted so much violence, that in a very few minutes they were both naked to the middle.\n\nIt is lucky for the women that the seat of fistycuff war is not the same with them as among men; but though they may seem a little to deviate from their sex, when they go forth to battle, yet I have observed, they never so far forget, as to assail the bosoms of each other; where a few blows would be fatal to most of them. This, I know, some derive from their being of a more bloody inclination than the males. On which account they apply to the nose, as to the part whence blood may most easily be drawn; but this seems a far-fetched as well as ill-natured supposition.\n\nGoody Brown had great advantage of Molly in this particular; for the former had indeed no breasts, her bosom (if it may be so called), as well in colour as in many other properties, exactly resembling an antient piece of parchment, upon which any one might have drummed a considerable while without doing her any great damage.\n\nMolly, beside her present unhappy condition, was differently formed in those parts, and might, perhaps, have tempted the envy of Brown to give her a fatal blow, had not the lucky arrival of Tom Jones at this instant put an immediate end to the bloody scene.\n\nThis accident was luckily owing to Mr Square; for he, Master Blifil, and Jones, had mounted their horses, after church, to take the air, and had ridden about a quarter of a mile, when Square, changing his mind (not idly, but for a reason which we shall unfold as soon as we have leisure), desired the young gentlemen to ride with him another way than they had at first purposed. This motion being complied with, brought them of necessity back again to the churchyard.\n\nMaster Blifil, who rode first, seeing such a mob assembled, and two women in the posture in which we left the combatants, stopt his horse to enquire what was the matter. A country fellow, scratching his head, answered him: “I don't know, measter, un't I; an't please your honour, here hath been a vight, I think, between Goody Brown and Moll Seagrim.”\n\n“Who, who?” cries Tom; but without waiting for an answer, having discovered the features of his Molly through all the discomposure in which they now were, he hastily alighted, turned his horse loose, and, leaping over the wall, ran to her. She now first bursting into tears, told him how barbarously she had been treated. Upon which, forgetting the sex of Goody Brown, or perhaps not knowing it in his rage--for, in reality, she had no feminine appearance but a petticoat, which he might not observe--he gave her a lash or two with his horsewhip; and then flying at the mob, who were all accused by Moll, he dealt his blows so profusely on all sides, that unless I would again invoke the muse (which the good-natured reader may think a little too hard upon her, as she hath so lately been violently sweated), it would be impossible for me to recount the horse-whipping of that day.\n\nHaving scoured the whole coast of the enemy, as well as any of Homer's heroes ever did, or as Don Quixote or any knight-errant in the world could have done, he returned to Molly, whom he found in a condition which must give both me and my reader pain, was it to be described here. Tom raved like a madman, beat his breast, tore his hair, stamped on the ground, and vowed the utmost vengeance on all who had been concerned. He then pulled off his coat, and buttoned it round her, put his hat upon her head, wiped the blood from her face as well as he could with his handkerchief, and called out to the servant to ride as fast as possible for a side-saddle, or a pillion, that he might carry her safe home.\n\nMaster Blifil objected to the sending away the servant, as they had only one with them; but as Square seconded the order of Jones, he was obliged to comply.\n\nThe servant returned in a very short time with the pillion, and Molly, having collected her rags as well as she could, was placed behind him. In which manner she was carried home, Square, Blifil, and Jones attending.\n\nHere Jones having received his coat, given her a sly kiss, and whispered her, that he would return in the evening, quitted his Molly, and rode on after his companions.\n\nContaining matter of no very peaceable colour.\n\nMolly had no sooner apparelled herself in her accustomed rags, than her sisters began to fall violently upon her, particularly her eldest sister, who told her she was well enough served. “How had she the assurance to wear a gown which young Madam Western had given to mother! If one of us was to wear it, I think,” says she, “I myself have the best right; but I warrant you think it belongs to your beauty. I suppose you think yourself more handsomer than any of us.”--“Hand her down the bit of glass from over the cupboard,” cries another; “I'd wash the blood from my face before I talked of my beauty.”--“You'd better have minded what the parson says,” cries the eldest, “and not a harkened after men voke.”--“Indeed, child, and so she had,” says the mother, sobbing: “she hath brought a disgrace upon us all. She's the vurst of the vamily that ever was a whore.”\n\n“You need not upbraid me with that, mother,” cries Molly; “you yourself was brought-to-bed of sister there, within a week after you was married.”\n\n“Yes, hussy,” answered the enraged mother, “so I was, and what was the mighty matter of that? I was made an honest woman then; and if you was to be made an honest woman, I should not be angry; but you must have to doing with a gentleman, you nasty slut; you will have a bastard, hussy, you will; and that I defy any one to say of me.”\n\nIn this situation Black George found his family, when he came home for the purpose before mentioned. As his wife and three daughters were all of them talking together, and most of them crying, it was some time before he could get an opportunity of being heard; but as soon as such an interval occurred, he acquainted the company with what Sophia had said to him.\n\nGoody Seagrim then began to revile her daughter afresh. “Here,” says she, “you have brought us into a fine quandary indeed. What will madam say to that big belly? Oh that ever I should live to see this day!”\n\nMolly answered with great spirit, “And what is this mighty place which you have got for me, father?” (for he had not well understood the phrase used by Sophia of being about her person). “I suppose it is to be under the cook; but I shan't wash dishes for anybody. My gentleman will provide better for me. See what he hath given me this afternoon. He hath promised I shall never want money; and you shan't want money neither, mother, if you will hold your tongue, and know when you are well.” And so saying, she pulled out several guineas, and gave her mother one of them.\n\nThe good woman no sooner felt the gold within her palm, than her temper began (such is the efficacy of that panacea) to be mollified. “Why, husband,” says she, “would any but such a blockhead as you not have enquired what place this was before he had accepted it? Perhaps, as Molly says, it may be in the kitchen; and truly I don't care my daughter should be a scullion wench; for, poor as I am, I am a gentlewoman. And thof I was obliged, as my father, who was a clergyman, died worse than nothing, and so could not give me a shilling of potion, to undervalue myself by marrying a poor man; yet I would have you to know, I have a spirit above all them things. Marry come up! it would better become Madam Western to look at home, and remember who her own grandfather was. Some of my family, for aught I know, might ride in their coaches, when the grandfathers of some voke walked a-voot. I warrant she fancies she did a mighty matter, when she sent us that old gownd; some of my family would not have picked up such rags in the street; but poor people are always trampled upon.--The parish need not have been in such a fluster with Molly. You might have told them, child, your grandmother wore better things new out of the shop.”\n\n“Well, but consider,” cried George, “what answer shall I make to madam?”\n\n“I don't know what answer,” says she; “you are always bringing your family into one quandary or other. Do you remember when you shot the partridge, the occasion of all our misfortunes? Did not I advise you never to go into Squire Western's manor? Did not I tell you many a good year ago what would come of it? But you would have your own headstrong ways; yes, you would, you villain.”\n\nBlack George was, in the main, a peaceable kind of fellow, and nothing choleric nor rash; yet did he bear about him something of what the antients called the irascible, and which his wife, if she had been endowed with much wisdom, would have feared. He had long experienced, that when the storm grew very high, arguments were but wind, which served rather to increase, than to abate it. He was therefore seldom unprovided with a small switch, a remedy of wonderful force, as he had often essayed, and which the word villain served as a hint for his applying.\n\nNo sooner, therefore, had this symptom appeared, than he had immediate recourse to the said remedy, which though, as it is usual in all very efficacious medicines, it at first seemed to heighten and inflame the disease, soon produced a total calm, and restored the patient to perfect ease and tranquillity.\n\nThis is, however, a kind of horse-medicine, which requires a very robust constitution to digest, and is therefore proper only for the vulgar, unless in one single instance, viz., where superiority of birth breaks out; in which case, we should not think it very improperly applied by any husband whatever, if the application was not in itself so base, that, like certain applications of the physical kind which need not be mentioned, it so much degrades and contaminates the hand employed in it, that no gentleman should endure the thought of anything so low and detestable.\n\nThe whole family were soon reduced to a state of perfect quiet; for the virtue of this medicine, like that of electricity, is often communicated through one person to many others, who are not touched by the instrument. To say the truth, as they both operate by friction, it may be doubted whether there is not something analogous between them, of which Mr Freke would do well to enquire, before he publishes the next edition of his book.\n\nA council was now called, in which, after many debates, Molly still persisting that she would not go to service, it was at length resolved, that Goody Seagrim herself should wait on Miss Western, and endeavour to procure the place for her eldest daughter, who declared great readiness to accept it: but Fortune, who seems to have been an enemy of this little family, afterwards put a stop to her promotion.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nA story told by Mr Supple, the curate. The penetration of Squire Western. His great love for his daughter, and the return to it made by her.\n\nThe next morning Tom Jones hunted with Mr Western, and was at his return invited by that gentleman to dinner.\nThe lovely Sophia shone forth that day with more gaiety and sprightliness than usual. Her battery was certainly levelled at our heroe; though, I believe, she herself scarce yet knew her own intention; but if she had any design of charming him, she now succeeded.\n\nMr Supple, the curate of Mr Allworthy's parish, made one of the company. He was a good-natured worthy man; but chiefly remarkable for his great taciturnity at table, though his mouth was never shut at it. In short, he had one of the best appetites in the world. However, the cloth was no sooner taken away, than he always made sufficient amends for his silence: for he was a very hearty fellow; and his conversation was often entertaining, never offensive.\n\nAt his first arrival, which was immediately before the entrance of the roast-beef, he had given an intimation that he had brought some news with him, and was beginning to tell, that he came that moment from Mr Allworthy's, when the sight of the roast-beef struck him dumb, permitting him only to say grace, and to declare he must pay his respect to the baronet, for so he called the sirloin.\n\nWhen dinner was over, being reminded by Sophia of his news, he began as follows: “I believe, lady, your ladyship observed a young woman at church yesterday at even-song, who was drest in one of your outlandish garments; I think I have seen your ladyship in such a one. However, in the country, such dresses are\n\nRara avis in terris, nigroque simillima cygno.\n\nThat is, madam, as much as to say, 'A rare bird upon the earth, and very like a black swan.' The verse is in Juvenal. But to return to what I was relating. I was saying such garments are rare sights in the country; and perchance, too, it was thought the more rare, respect being had to the person who wore it, who, they tell me, is the daughter of Black George, your worship's gamekeeper, whose sufferings, I should have opined, might have taught him more wit, than to dress forth his wenches in such gaudy apparel. She created so much confusion in the congregation, that if Squire Allworthy had not silenced it, it would have interrupted the service: for I was once about to stop in the middle of the first lesson. Howbeit, nevertheless, after prayer was over, and I was departed home, this occasioned a battle in the churchyard, where, amongst other mischief, the head of a travelling fidler was very much broken. This morning the fidler came to Squire Allworthy for a warrant, and the wench was brought before him. The squire was inclined to have compounded matters; when, lo! on a sudden the wench appeared (I ask your ladyship's pardon) to be, as it were, at the eve of bringing forth a bastard. The squire demanded of her who was the father? But she pertinaciously refused to make any response. So that he was about to make her mittimus to Bridewell when I departed.”\n\n“And is a wench having a bastard all your news, doctor?” cries Western; “I thought it might have been some public matter, something about the nation.”\n\n“I am afraid it is too common, indeed,” answered the parson; “but I thought the whole story altogether deserved commemorating. As to national matters, your worship knows them best. My concerns extend no farther than my own parish.”\n\n“Why, ay,” says the squire, “I believe I do know a little of that matter, as you say. But, come, Tommy, drink about; the bottle stands with you.”\n\nTom begged to be excused, for that he had particular business; and getting up from table, escaped the clutches of the squire, who was rising to stop him, and went off with very little ceremony.\n\nThe squire gave him a good curse at his departure; and then turning to the parson, he cried out, “I smoke it: I smoke it. Tom is certainly the father of this bastard. Zooks, parson, you remember how he recommended the veather o' her to me. D--n un, what a sly b--ch 'tis. Ay, ay, as sure as two-pence, Tom is the veather of the bastard.”\n\n“I should be very sorry for that,” says the parson.\n\n“Why sorry,” cries the squire: “Where is the mighty matter o't? What, I suppose dost pretend that thee hast never got a bastard? Pox! more good luck's thine? for I warrant hast a done a therefore many's the good time and often.”\n\n“Your worship is pleased to be jocular,” answered the parson; “but I do not only animadvert on the sinfulness of the action--though that surely is to be greatly deprecated--but I fear his unrighteousness may injure him with Mr Allworthy. And truly I must say, though he hath the character of being a little wild, I never saw any harm in the young man; nor can I say I have heard any, save what your worship now mentions. I wish, indeed, he was a little more regular in his responses at church; but altogether he seems\n\nIngenui vultus puer ingenuique pudoris.\n\nThat is a classical line, young lady; and, being rendered into English, is, `a lad of an ingenuous countenance, and of an ingenuous modesty;' for this was a virtue in great repute both among the Latins and Greeks. I must say, the young gentleman (for so I think I may call him, notwithstanding his birth) appears to me a very modest, civil lad, and I should be sorry that he should do himself any injury in Squire Allworthy's opinion.”\n\n“Poogh!” says the squire: “Injury, with Allworthy! Why, Allworthy loves a wench himself. Doth not all the country know whose son Tom is? You must talk to another person in that manner. I remember Allworthy at college.”\n\n“I thought,” said the parson, “he had never been at the university.”\n\n“Yes, yes, he was,” says the squire: “and many a wench have we two had together. As arrant a whore-master as any within five miles o'un. No, no. It will do'n no harm with he, assure yourself; nor with anybody else. Ask Sophy there--You have not the worse opinion of a young fellow for getting a bastard, have you, girl? No, no, the women will like un the better for't.”\n\nThis was a cruel question to poor Sophia. She had observed Tom's colour change at the parson's story; and that, with his hasty and abrupt departure, gave her sufficient reason to think her father's suspicion not groundless. Her heart now at once discovered the great secret to her which it had been so long disclosing by little and little; and she found herself highly interested in this matter. In such a situation, her father's malapert question rushing suddenly upon her, produced some symptoms which might have alarmed a suspicious heart; but, to do the squire justice, that was not his fault. When she rose therefore from her chair, and told him a hint from him was always sufficient to make her withdraw, he suffered her to leave the room, and then with great gravity of countenance remarked, “That it was better to see a daughter over-modest than over-forward;”--a sentiment which was highly applauded by the parson.\n\nThere now ensued between the squire and the parson a most excellent political discourse, framed out of newspapers and political pamphlets; in which they made a libation of four bottles of wine to the good of their country: and then, the squire being fast asleep, the parson lighted his pipe, mounted his horse, and rode home.\n\nWhen the squire had finished his half-hour's nap, he summoned his daughter to her harpsichord; but she begged to be excused that evening, on account of a violent head-ache. This remission was presently granted; for indeed she seldom had occasion to ask him twice, as he loved her with such ardent affection, that, by gratifying her, he commonly conveyed the highest gratification to himself. She was really, what he frequently called her, his little darling, and she well deserved to be so; for she returned all his affection in the most ample manner. She had preserved the most inviolable duty to him in all things; and this her love made not only easy, but so delightful, that when one of her companions laughed at her for placing so much merit in such scrupulous obedience, as that young lady called it, Sophia answered, “You mistake me, madam, if you think I value myself upon this account; for besides that I am barely discharging my duty, I am likewise pleasing myself. I can truly say I have no delight equal to that of contributing to my father's happiness; and if I value myself, my dear, it is on having this power, and not on executing it.”\n\nThis was a satisfaction, however, which poor Sophia was incapable of tasting this evening. She therefore not only desired to be excused from her attendance at the harpsichord, but likewise begged that he would suffer her to absent herself from supper. To this request likewise the squire agreed, though not without some reluctance; for he scarce ever permitted her to be out of his sight, unless when he was engaged with his horses, dogs, or bottle. Nevertheless he yielded to the desire of his daughter, though the poor man was at the same time obliged to avoid his own company (if I may so express myself), by sending for a neighbouring farmer to sit with him.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\nThe narrow escape of Molly Seagrim, with some observations for which we have been forced to dive pretty deep into nature.\n\nTom Jones had ridden one of Mr Western's horses that morning in the chase; so that having no horse of his own in the squire's stable, he was obliged to go home on foot: this he did so expeditiously that he ran upwards of three miles within the half-hour.\n\nJust as he arrived at Mr Allworthy's outward gate, he met the constable and company with Molly in their possession, whom they were conducting to that house where the inferior sort of people may learn one good lesson, viz., respect and deference to their superiors; since it must show them the wide distinction Fortune intends between those persons who are to be corrected for their faults, and those who are not; which lesson if they do not learn, I am afraid they very rarely learn any other good lesson, or improve their morals, at the house of correction.\n\nA lawyer may perhaps think Mr Allworthy exceeded his authority a little in this instance. And, to say the truth, I question, as here was no regular information before him, whether his conduct was strictly regular. However, as his intention was truly upright, he ought to be excused in foro conscientiae; since so many arbitrary acts are daily committed by magistrates who have not this excuse to plead for themselves.\n\nTom was no sooner informed by the constable whither they were proceeding (indeed he pretty well guessed it of himself), than he caught Molly in his arms, and embracing her tenderly before them all, swore he would murder the first man who offered to lay hold of her. He bid her dry her eyes and be comforted; for, wherever she went, he would accompany her. Then turning to the constable, who stood trembling with his hat off, he desired him, in a very mild voice, to return with him for a moment only to his father (for so he now called Allworthy); for he durst, he said, be assured, that, when he had alledged what he had to say in her favour, the girl would be discharged.\n\nThe constable, who, I make no doubt, would have surrendered his prisoner had Tom demanded her, very readily consented to this request. So back they all went into Mr Allworthy's hall; where Tom desired them to stay till his return, and then went himself in pursuit of the good man. As soon as he was found, Tom threw himself at his feet, and having begged a patient hearing, confessed himself to be the father of the child of which Molly was then big. He entreated him to have compassion on the poor girl, and to consider, if there was any guilt in the case, it lay principally at his door.\n\n“If there is any guilt in the case!” answered Allworthy warmly: “Are you then so profligate and abandoned a libertine to doubt whether the breaking the laws of God and man, the corrupting and ruining a poor girl be guilt? I own, indeed, it doth lie principally upon you; and so heavy it is, that you ought to expect it should crush you.”\n\n“Whatever may be my fate,” says Tom, “let me succeed in my intercessions for the poor girl. I confess I have corrupted her! but whether she shall be ruined, depends on you. For Heaven's sake, sir, revoke your warrant, and do not send her to a place which must unavoidably prove her destruction.”\n\nAllworthy bid him immediately call a servant. Tom answered there was no occasion; for he had luckily met them at the gate, and relying upon his goodness, had brought them all back into his hall, where they now waited his final resolution, which upon his knees he besought him might be in favour of the girl; that she might be permitted to go home to her parents, and not be exposed to a greater degree of shame and scorn than must necessarily fall upon her. “I know,” said he, “that is too much. I know I am the wicked occasion of it. I will endeavour to make amends, if possible; and if you shall have hereafter the goodness to forgive me, I hope I shall deserve it.”\n\nAllworthy hesitated some time, and at last said, “Well, I will discharge my mittimus.--You may send the constable to me.” He was instantly called, discharged, and so was the girl.\n\nIt will be believed that Mr Allworthy failed not to read Tom a very severe lecture on this occasion; but it is unnecessary to insert it here, as we have faithfully transcribed what he said to Jenny Jones in the first book, most of which may be applied to the men, equally with the women. So sensible an effect had these reproofs on the young man, who was no hardened sinner, that he retired to his own room, where he passed the evening alone, in much melancholy contemplation.\n\nAllworthy was sufficiently offended by this transgression of Jones; for notwithstanding the assertions of Mr Western, it is certain this worthy man had never indulged himself in any loose pleasures with women, and greatly condemned the vice of incontinence in others. Indeed, there is much reason to imagine that there was not the least truth in what Mr Western affirmed, especially as he laid the scene of those impurities at the university, where Mr Allworthy had never been. In fact, the good squire was a little too apt to indulge that kind of pleasantry which is generally called rhodomontade: but which may, with as much propriety, be expressed by a much shorter word; and perhaps we too often supply the use of this little monosyllable by others; since very much of what frequently passes in the world for wit and humour, should, in the strictest purity of language, receive that short appellation, which, in conformity to the well-bred laws of custom, I here suppress.\n\nBut whatever detestation Mr Allworthy had to this or to any other vice, he was not so blinded by it but that he could discern any virtue in the guilty person, as clearly indeed as if there had been no mixture of vice in the same character. While he was angry therefore with the incontinence of Jones, he was no less pleased with the honour and honesty of his self-accusation. He began now to form in his mind the same opinion of this young fellow, which, we hope, our reader may have conceived. And in balancing his faults with his perfections, the latter seemed rather to preponderate.\n\nIt was to no purpose, therefore, that Thwackum, who was immediately charged by Mr Blifil with the story, unbended all his rancour against poor Tom. Allworthy gave a patient hearing to their invectives, and then answered coldly: “That young men of Tom's complexion were too generally addicted to this vice; but he believed that youth was sincerely affected with what he had said to him on the occasion, and he hoped he would not transgress again.” So that, as the days of whipping were at an end, the tutor had no other vent but his own mouth for his gall, the usual poor resource of impotent revenge.\n\nBut Square, who was a less violent, was a much more artful man; and as he hated Jones more perhaps than Thwackum himself did, so he contrived to do him more mischief in the mind of Mr Allworthy.\n\nThe reader must remember the several little incidents of the partridge, the horse, and the Bible, which were recounted in the second book. By all which Jones had rather improved than injured the affection which Mr Allworthy was inclined to entertain for him. The same, I believe, must have happened to him with every other person who hath any idea of friendship, generosity, and greatness of spirit, that is to say, who hath any traces of goodness in his mind.\n\nSquare himself was not unacquainted with the true impression which those several instances of goodness had made on the excellent heart of Allworthy; for the philosopher very well knew what virtue was, though he was not always perhaps steady in its pursuit; but as for Thwackum, from what reason I will not determine, no such thoughts ever entered into his head: he saw Jones in a bad light, and he imagined Allworthy saw him in the same, but that he was resolved, from pride and stubbornness of spirit, not to give up the boy whom he had once cherished; since by so doing, he must tacitly acknowledge that his former opinion of him had been wrong.\n\nSquare therefore embraced this opportunity of injuring Jones in the tenderest part, by giving a very bad turn to all these before-mentioned occurrences. “I am sorry, sir,” said he, “to own I have been deceived as well as yourself. I could not, I confess, help being pleased with what I ascribed to the motive of friendship, though it was carried to an excess, and all excess is faulty and vicious: but in this I made allowance for youth. Little did I suspect that the sacrifice of truth, which we both imagined to have been made to friendship, was in reality a prostitution of it to a depraved and debauched appetite. You now plainly see whence all the seeming generosity of this young man to the family of the gamekeeper proceeded. He supported the father in order to corrupt the daughter, and preserved the family from starving, to bring one of them to shame and ruin. This is friendship! this is generosity! As Sir Richard Steele says, `Gluttons who give high prices for delicacies, are very worthy to be called generous.' In short I am resolved, from this instance, never to give way to the weakness of human nature more, nor to think anything virtue which doth not exactly quadrate with the unerring rule of right.”\n\nThe goodness of Allworthy had prevented those considerations from occurring to himself; yet were they too plausible to be absolutely and hastily rejected, when laid before his eyes by another. Indeed what Square had said sunk very deeply into his mind, and the uneasiness which it there created was very visible to the other; though the good man would not acknowledge this, but made a very slight answer, and forcibly drove off the discourse to some other subject. It was well perhaps for poor Tom, that no such suggestions had been made before he was pardoned; for they certainly stamped in the mind of Allworthy the first bad impression concerning Jones.\n\n# Chapter xii.\n\nContaining much clearer matters; but which flowed from the same fountain with those in the preceding chapter.\n\nThe reader will be pleased, I believe, to return with me to Sophia. She passed the night, after we saw her last, in no very agreeable manner. Sleep befriended her but little, and dreams less. In the morning, when Mrs Honour, her maid, attended her at the usual hour, she was found already up and drest.\n\nPersons who live two or three miles' distance in the country are considered as next-door neighbours, and transactions at the one house fly with incredible celerity to the other. Mrs Honour, therefore, had heard the whole story of Molly's shame; which she, being of a very communicative temper, had no sooner entered the apartment of her mistress, than she began to relate in the following manner:--\n\n“La, ma'am, what doth your la'ship think? the girl that your la'ship saw at church on Sunday, whom you thought so handsome; though you would not have thought her so handsome neither, if you had seen her nearer, but to be sure she hath been carried before the justice for being big with child. She seemed to me to look like a confident slut: and to be sure she hath laid the child to young Mr Jones. And all the parish says Mr Allworthy is so angry with young Mr Jones, that he won't see him. To be sure, one can't help pitying the poor young man, and yet he doth not deserve much pity neither, for demeaning himself with such kind of trumpery. Yet he is so pretty a gentleman, I should be sorry to have him turned out of doors. I dares to swear the wench was as willing as he; for she was always a forward kind of body. And when wenches are so coming, young men are not so much to be blamed neither; for to be sure they do no more than what is natural. Indeed it is beneath them to meddle with such dirty draggle-tails; and whatever happens to them, it is good enough for them. And yet, to be sure, the vile baggages are most in fault. I wishes, with all my heart, they were well to be whipped at the cart's tail; for it is pity they should be the ruin of a pretty young gentleman; and nobody can deny but that Mr Jones is one of the most handsomest young men that ever----”\n\nShe was running on thus, when Sophia, with a more peevish voice than she had ever spoken to her in before, cried, “Prithee, why dost thou trouble me with all this stuff? What concern have I in what Mr Jones doth? I suppose you are all alike. And you seem to me to be angry it was not your own case.”\n\n“I, ma'am!” answered Mrs Honour, “I am sorry your ladyship should have such an opinion of me. I am sure nobody can say any such thing of me. All the young fellows in the world may go to the divil for me. Because I said he was a handsome man? Everybody says it as well as I. To be sure, I never thought as it was any harm to say a young man was handsome; but to be sure I shall never think him so any more now; for handsome is that handsome does. A beggar wench!--”\n\n“Stop thy torrent of impertinence,” cries Sophia, “and see whether my father wants me at breakfast.”\n\nMrs Honour then flung out of the room, muttering much to herself, of which “Marry come up, I assure you,” was all that could be plainly distinguished.\n\nWhether Mrs Honour really deserved that suspicion, of which her mistress gave her a hint, is a matter which we cannot indulge our reader's curiosity by resolving. We will, however, make him amends in disclosing what passed in the mind of Sophia.\n\nThe reader will be pleased to recollect, that a secret affection for Mr Jones had insensibly stolen into the bosom of this young lady. That it had there grown to a pretty great height before she herself had discovered it. When she first began to perceive its symptoms, the sensations were so sweet and pleasing, that she had not resolution sufficient to check or repel them; and thus she went on cherishing a passion of which she never once considered the consequences.\n\nThis incident relating to Molly first opened her eyes. She now first perceived the weakness of which she had been guilty; and though it caused the utmost perturbation in her mind, yet it had the effect of other nauseous physic, and for the time expelled her distemper. Its operation indeed was most wonderfully quick; and in the short interval, while her maid was absent, so entirely removed all symptoms, that when Mrs Honour returned with a summons from her father, she was become perfectly easy, and had brought herself to a thorough indifference for Mr Jones.\n\nThe diseases of the mind do in almost every particular imitate those of the body. For which reason, we hope, that learned faculty, for whom we have so profound a respect, will pardon us the violent hands we have been necessitated to lay on several words and phrases, which of right belong to them, and without which our descriptions must have been often unintelligible.\n\nNow there is no one circumstance in which the distempers of the mind bear a more exact analogy to those which are called bodily, than that aptness which both have to a relapse. This is plain in the violent diseases of ambition and avarice. I have known ambition, when cured at court by frequent disappointments (which are the only physic for it), to break out again in a contest for foreman of the grand jury at an assizes; and have heard of a man who had so far conquered avarice, as to give away many a sixpence, that comforted himself, at last, on his deathbed, by making a crafty and advantageous bargain concerning his ensuing funeral, with an undertaker who had married his only child.\n\nIn the affair of love, which, out of strict conformity with the Stoic philosophy, we shall here treat as a disease, this proneness to relapse is no less conspicuous. Thus it happened to poor Sophia; upon whom, the very next time she saw young Jones, all the former symptoms returned, and from that time cold and hot fits alternately seized her heart.\n\nThe situation of this young lady was now very different from what it had ever been before. That passion which had formerly been so exquisitely delicious, became now a scorpion in her bosom. She resisted it therefore with her utmost force, and summoned every argument her reason (which was surprisingly strong for her age) could suggest, to subdue and expel it. In this she so far succeeded, that she began to hope from time and absence a perfect cure. She resolved therefore to avoid Tom Jones as much as possible; for which purpose she began to conceive a design of visiting her aunt, to which she made no doubt of obtaining her father's consent.\n\nBut Fortune, who had other designs in her head, put an immediate stop to any such proceeding, by introducing an accident, which will be related in the next chapter.\n\n# Chapter xiii.\n\nA dreadful accident which befel Sophia. The gallant behaviour of Jones, and the more dreadful consequence of that behaviour to the young lady; with a short digression in favour of the female sex.\n\nMr Western grew every day fonder and fonder of Sophia, insomuch that his beloved dogs themselves almost gave place to her in his affections; but as he could not prevail on himself to abandon these, he contrived very cunningly to enjoy their company, together with that of his daughter, by insisting on her riding a hunting with him.\n\nSophia, to whom her father's word was a law, readily complied with his desires, though she had not the least delight in a sport, which was of too rough and masculine a nature to suit with her disposition. She had however another motive, beside her obedience, to accompany the old gentleman in the chase; for by her presence she hoped in some measure to restrain his impetuosity, and to prevent him from so frequently exposing his neck to the utmost hazard.\n\nThe strongest objection was that which would have formerly been an inducement to her, namely, the frequent meeting with young Jones, whom she had determined to avoid; but as the end of the hunting season now approached, she hoped, by a short absence with her aunt, to reason herself entirely out of her unfortunate passion; and had not any doubt of being able to meet him in the field the subsequent season without the least danger.\n\nOn the second day of her hunting, as she was returning from the chase, and was arrived within a little distance from Mr Western's house, her horse, whose mettlesome spirit required a better rider, fell suddenly to prancing and capering in such a manner that she was in the most imminent peril of falling. Tom Jones, who was at a little distance behind, saw this, and immediately galloped up to her assistance. As soon as he came up, he leapt from his own horse, and caught hold of hers by the bridle. The unruly beast presently reared himself an end on his hind legs, and threw his lovely burthen from his back, and Jones caught her in his arms.\n\nShe was so affected with the fright, that she was not immediately able to satisfy Jones, who was very solicitous to know whether she had received any hurt. She soon after, however, recovered her spirits, assured him she was safe, and thanked him for the care he had taken of her. Jones answered, “If I have preserved you, madam, I am sufficiently repaid; for I promise you, I would have secured you from the least harm at the expense of a much greater misfortune to myself than I have suffered on this occasion.”\n\n“What misfortune?” replied Sophia eagerly; “I hope you have come to no mischief?”\n\n“Be not concerned, madam,” answered Jones. “Heaven be praised you have escaped so well, considering the danger you was in. If I have broke my arm, I consider it as a trifle, in comparison of what I feared upon your account.”\n\nSophia then screamed out, “Broke your arm! Heaven forbid.”\n\n“I am afraid I have, madam,” says Jones: “but I beg you will suffer me first to take care of you. I have a right hand yet at your service, to help you into the next field, whence we have but a very little walk to your father's house.”\n\nSophia seeing his left arm dangling by his side, while he was using the other to lead her, no longer doubted of the truth. She now grew much paler than her fears for herself had made her before. All her limbs were seized with a trembling, insomuch that Jones could scarce support her; and as her thoughts were in no less agitation, she could not refrain from giving Jones a look so full of tenderness, that it almost argued a stronger sensation in her mind, than even gratitude and pity united can raise in the gentlest female bosom, without the assistance of a third more powerful passion.\n\nMr Western, who was advanced at some distance when this accident happened, was now returned, as were the rest of the horsemen. Sophia immediately acquainted them with what had befallen Jones, and begged them to take care of him. Upon which Western, who had been much alarmed by meeting his daughter's horse without its rider, and was now overjoyed to find her unhurt, cried out, “I am glad it is no worse. If Tom hath broken his arm, we will get a joiner to mend un again.”\n\nThe squire alighted from his horse, and proceeded to his house on foot, with his daughter and Jones. An impartial spectator, who had met them on the way, would, on viewing their several countenances, have concluded Sophia alone to have been the object of compassion: for as to Jones, he exulted in having probably saved the life of the young lady, at the price only of a broken bone; and Mr Western, though he was not unconcerned at the accident which had befallen Jones, was, however, delighted in a much higher degree with the fortunate escape of his daughter.\n\nThe generosity of Sophia's temper construed this behaviour of Jones into great bravery; and it made a deep impression on her heart: for certain it is, that there is no one quality which so generally recommends men to women as this; proceeding, if we believe the common opinion, from that natural timidity of the sex, which is, says Mr Osborne, “so great, that a woman is the most cowardly of all the creatures God ever made;”--a sentiment more remarkable for its bluntness than for its truth. Aristotle, in his Politics, doth them, I believe, more justice, when he says, “The modesty and fortitude of men differ from those virtues in women; for the fortitude which becomes a woman, would be cowardice in a man; and the modesty which becomes a man, would be pertness in a woman.” Nor is there, perhaps, more of truth in the opinion of those who derive the partiality which women are inclined to show to the brave, from this excess of their fear. Mr Bayle (I think, in his article of Helen) imputes this, and with greater probability, to their violent love of glory; for the truth of which, we have the authority of him who of all others saw farthest into human nature, and who introduces the heroine of his Odyssey, the great pattern of matrimonial love and constancy, assigning the glory of her husband as the only source of her affection towards him.[*]\n\n[*] The English reader will not find this in the poem; for the sentiment is entirely left out in the translation.\n\nHowever this be, certain it is that the accident operated very strongly on Sophia; and, indeed, after much enquiry into the matter, I am inclined to believe, that, at this very time, the charming Sophia made no less impression on the heart of Jones; to say truth, he had for some time become sensible of the irresistible power of her charms.\n\n# Chapter xiv.\n\nThe arrival of a surgeon.--His operations, and a long dialogue between Sophia and her maid.\n\nWhen they arrived at Mr Western's hall, Sophia, who had tottered along with much difficulty, sunk down in her chair; but by the assistance of hartshorn and water, she was prevented from fainting away, and had pretty well recovered her spirits, when the surgeon who was sent for to Jones appeared. Mr Western, who imputed these symptoms in his daughter to her fall, advised her to be presently blooded by way of prevention. In this opinion he was seconded by the surgeon, who gave so many reasons for bleeding, and quoted so many cases where persons had miscarried for want of it, that the squire became very importunate, and indeed insisted peremptorily that his daughter should be blooded.\n\nSophia soon yielded to the commands of her father, though entirely contrary to her own inclinations, for she suspected, I believe, less danger from the fright, than either the squire or the surgeon. She then stretched out her beautiful arm, and the operator began to prepare for his work.\n\nWhile the servants were busied in providing materials, the surgeon, who imputed the backwardness which had appeared in Sophia to her fears, began to comfort her with assurances that there was not the least danger; for no accident, he said, could ever happen in bleeding, but from the monstrous ignorance of pretenders to surgery, which he pretty plainly insinuated was not at present to be apprehended. Sophia declared she was not under the least apprehension; adding, “If you open an artery, I promise you I'll forgive you.” “Will you?” cries Western: “D--n me, if I will. If he does thee the least mischief, d--n me if I don't ha' the heart's blood o'un out.” The surgeon assented to bleed her upon these conditions, and then proceeded to his operation, which he performed with as much dexterity as he had promised; and with as much quickness: for he took but little blood from her, saying, it was much safer to bleed again and again, than to take away too much at once.\n\nSophia, when her arm was bound up, retired: for she was not willing (nor was it, perhaps, strictly decent) to be present at the operation on Jones. Indeed, one objection which she had to bleeding (though she did not make it) was the delay which it would occasion to setting the broken bone. For Western, when Sophia was concerned, had no consideration but for her; and as for Jones himself, he “sat like patience on a monument smiling at grief.” To say the truth, when he saw the blood springing from the lovely arm of Sophia, he scarce thought of what had happened to himself.\n\nThe surgeon now ordered his patient to be stript to his shirt, and then entirely baring the arm, he began to stretch and examine it, in such a manner that the tortures he put him to caused Jones to make several wry faces; which the surgeon observing, greatly wondered at, crying, “What is the matter, sir? I am sure it is impossible I should hurt you.” And then holding forth the broken arm, he began a long and very learned lecture of anatomy, in which simple and double fractures were most accurately considered; and the several ways in which Jones might have broken his arm were discussed, with proper annotations showing how many of these would have been better, and how many worse than the present case.\n\nHaving at length finished his laboured harangue, with which the audience, though it had greatly raised their attention and admiration, were not much edified, as they really understood not a single syllable of all he had said, he proceeded to business, which he was more expeditious in finishing, than he had been in beginning.\n\nJones was then ordered into a bed, which Mr Western compelled him to accept at his own house, and sentence of water-gruel was passed upon him.\n\nAmong the good company which had attended in the hall during the bone-setting, Mrs Honour was one; who being summoned to her mistress as soon as it was over, and asked by her how the young gentleman did, presently launched into extravagant praises on the magnanimity, as she called it, of his behaviour, which, she said, “was so charming in so pretty a creature.” She then burst forth into much warmer encomiums on the beauty of his person; enumerating many particulars, and ending with the whiteness of his skin.\n\nThis discourse had an effect on Sophia's countenance, which would not perhaps have escaped the observance of the sagacious waiting-woman, had she once looked her mistress in the face, all the time she was speaking: but as a looking-glass, which was most commodiously placed opposite to her, gave her an opportunity of surveying those features, in which, of all others, she took most delight; so she had not once removed her eyes from that amiable object during her whole speech.\n\nMrs Honour was so intirely wrapped up in the subject on which she exercised her tongue, and the object before her eyes, that she gave her mistress time to conquer her confusion; which having done, she smiled on her maid, and told her, “she was certainly in love with this young fellow.”--“I in love, madam!” answers she: “upon my word, ma'am, I assure you, ma'am, upon my soul, ma'am, I am not.”--“Why, if you was,” cries her mistress, “I see no reason that you should be ashamed of it; for he is certainly a pretty fellow.”--“Yes, ma'am,” answered the other, “that he is, the most handsomest man I ever saw in my life. Yes, to be sure, that he is, and, as your ladyship says, I don't know why I should be ashamed of loving him, though he is my betters. To be sure, gentlefolks are but flesh and blood no more than us servants. Besides, as for Mr Jones, thof Squire Allworthy hath made a gentleman of him, he was not so good as myself by birth: for thof I am a poor body, I am an honest person's child, and my father and mother were married, which is more than some people can say, as high as they hold their heads. Marry, come up! I assure you, my dirty cousin! thof his skin be so white, and to be sure it is the most whitest that ever was seen, I am a Christian as well as he, and nobody can say that I am base born: my grandfather was a clergyman,[*] and would have been very angry, I believe, to have thought any of his family should have taken up with Molly Seagrim's dirty leavings.”\n\n[*] This is the second person of low condition whom we have recorded in this history to have sprung from the clergy. It is to be hoped such instances will, in future ages, when some provision is made for the families of the inferior clergy, appear stranger than they can be thought at present.\n\nPerhaps Sophia might have suffered her maid to run on in this manner, from wanting sufficient spirits to stop her tongue, which the reader may probably conjecture was no very easy task; for certainly there were some passages in her speech which were far from being agreeable to the lady. However, she now checked the torrent, as there seemed no end of its flowing. “I wonder,” says she, “at your assurance in daring to talk thus of one of my father's friends. As to the wench, I order you never to mention her name to me. And with regard to the young gentleman's birth, those who can say nothing more to his disadvantage, may as well be silent on that head, as I desire you will be for the future.”\n\n“I am sorry I have offended your ladyship,” answered Mrs Honour. “I am sure I hate Molly Seagrim as much as your ladyship can; and as for abusing Squire Jones, I can call all the servants in the house to witness, that whenever any talk hath been about bastards, I have always taken his part; for which of you, says I to the footmen, would not be a bastard, if he could, to be made a gentleman of? And, says I, I am sure he is a very fine gentleman; and he hath one of the whitest hands in the world; for to be sure so he hath: and, says I, one of the sweetest temperedest, best naturedest men in the world he is; and, says I, all the servants and neighbours all round the country loves him. And, to be sure, I could tell your ladyship something, but that I am afraid it would offend you.”--“What could you tell me, Honour?” says Sophia. “Nay, ma'am, to be sure he meant nothing by it, therefore I would not have your ladyship be offended.”--“Prithee tell me,” says Sophia; “I will know it this instant.”--“Why, ma'am,” answered Mrs Honour, “he came into the room one day last week when I was at work, and there lay your ladyship's muff on a chair, and to be sure he put his hands into it; that very muff your ladyship gave me but yesterday. La! says I, Mr Jones, you will stretch my lady's muff, and spoil it: but he still kept his hands in it: and then he kissed it--to be sure I hardly ever saw such a kiss in my life as he gave it.”--“I suppose he did not know it was mine,” replied Sophia. “Your ladyship shall hear, ma'am. He kissed it again and again, and said it was the prettiest muff in the world. La! sir, says I, you have seen it a hundred times. Yes, Mrs Honour, cried he; but who can see anything beautiful in the presence of your lady but herself?--Nay, that's not all neither; but I hope your ladyship won't be offended, for to be sure he meant nothing. One day, as your ladyship was playing on the harpsichord to my master, Mr Jones was sitting in the next room, and methought he looked melancholy. La! says I, Mr Jones, what's the matter? a penny for your thoughts, says I. Why, hussy, says he, starting up from a dream, what can I be thinking of, when that angel your mistress is playing? And then squeezing me by the hand, Oh! Mrs Honour, says he, how happy will that man be!--and then he sighed. Upon my troth, his breath is as sweet as a nosegay.--But to be sure he meant no harm by it. So I hope your ladyship will not mention a word; for he gave me a crown never to mention it, and made me swear upon a book, but I believe, indeed, it was not the Bible.”\n\nTill something of a more beautiful red than vermilion be found out, I shall say nothing of Sophia's colour on this occasion. “Ho--nour,” says she, “I--if you will not mention this any more to me--nor to anybody else, I will not betray you--I mean, I will not be angry; but I am afraid of your tongue. Why, my girl, will you give it such liberties?”--“Nay, ma'am,” answered she, “to be sure, I would sooner cut out my tongue than offend your ladyship. To be sure I shall never mention a word that your ladyship would not have me.”--“Why, I would not have you mention this any more,” said Sophia, “for it may come to my father's ears, and he would be angry with Mr Jones; though I really believe, as you say, he meant nothing. I should be very angry myself, if I imagined--“--“Nay, ma'am,” says Honour, “I protest I believe he meant nothing. I thought he talked as if he was out of his senses; nay, he said he believed he was beside himself when he had spoken the words. Ay, sir, says I, I believe so too. Yes, says he, Honour.--But I ask your ladyship's pardon; I could tear my tongue out for offending you.” “Go on,” says Sophia; “you may mention anything you have not told me before.”--“Yes, Honour, says he (this was some time afterwards, when he gave me the crown), I am neither such a coxcomb, or such a villain, as to think of her in any other delight but as my goddess; as such I will always worship and adore her while I have breath.--This was all, ma'am, I will be sworn, to the best of my remembrance. I was in a passion with him myself, till I found he meant no harm.”--“Indeed, Honour,” says Sophia, “I believe you have a real affection for me. I was provoked the other day when I gave you warning; but if you have a desire to stay with me, you shall.”--“To be sure, ma'am,” answered Mrs Honour, “I shall never desire to part with your ladyship. To be sure, I almost cried my eyes out when you gave me warning. It would be very ungrateful in me to desire to leave your ladyship; because as why, I should never get so good a place again. I am sure I would live and die with your ladyship; for, as poor Mr Jones said, happy is the man----”\n\nHere the dinner bell interrupted a conversation which had wrought such an effect on Sophia, that she was, perhaps, more obliged to her bleeding in the morning, than she, at the time, had apprehended she should be. As to the present situation of her mind, I shall adhere to a rule of Horace, by not attempting to describe it, from despair of success. Most of my readers will suggest it easily to themselves; and the few who cannot, would not understand the picture, or at least would deny it to be natural, if ever so well drawn.\n\n# BOOK V.\n\nCONTAINING A PORTION OF TIME SOMEWHAT LONGER THAN HALF A YEAR. Chapter i.\nOf the SERIOUS in writing, and for what purpose it is introduced.\n\nPeradventure there may be no parts in this prodigious work which will give the reader less pleasure in the perusing, than those which have given the author the greatest pains in composing. Among these probably may be reckoned those initial essays which we have prefixed to the historical matter contained in every book; and which we have determined to be essentially necessary to this kind of writing, of which we have set ourselves at the head.\nFor this our determination we do not hold ourselves strictly bound to assign any reason; it being abundantly sufficient that we have laid it down as a rule necessary to be observed in all prosai-comi-epic writing. Who ever demanded the reasons of that nice unity of time or place which is now established to be so essential to dramatic poetry? What critic hath been ever asked, why a play may not contain two days as well as one? Or why the audience (provided they travel, like electors, without any expense) may not be wafted fifty miles as well as five? Hath any commentator well accounted for the limitation which an antient critic hath set to the drama, which he will have contain neither more nor less than five acts? Or hath any one living attempted to explain what the modern judges of our theatres mean by that word low; by which they have happily succeeded in banishing all humour from the stage, and have made the theatre as dull as a drawing-room! Upon all these occasions the world seems to have embraced a maxim of our law, viz., cuicunque in arte sua perito credendum est: for it seems perhaps difficult to conceive that any one should have had enough of impudence to lay down dogmatical rules in any art or science without the least foundation. In such cases, therefore, we are apt to conclude there are sound and good reasons at the bottom, though we are unfortunately not able to see so far.\n\nNow, in reality, the world have paid too great a compliment to critics, and have imagined them men of much greater profundity than they really are. From this complacence, the critics have been emboldened to assume a dictatorial power, and have so far succeeded, that they are now become the masters, and have the assurance to give laws to those authors from whose predecessors they originally received them.\n\nThe critic, rightly considered, is no more than the clerk, whose office it is to transcribe the rules and laws laid down by those great judges whose vast strength of genius hath placed them in the light of legislators, in the several sciences over which they presided. This office was all which the critics of old aspired to; nor did they ever dare to advance a sentence, without supporting it by the authority of the judge from whence it was borrowed.\n\nBut in process of time, and in ages of ignorance, the clerk began to invade the power and assume the dignity of his master. The laws of writing were no longer founded on the practice of the author, but on the dictates of the critic. The clerk became the legislator, and those very peremptorily gave laws whose business it was, at first, only to transcribe them.\n\nHence arose an obvious, and perhaps an unavoidable error; for these critics being men of shallow capacities, very easily mistook mere form for substance. They acted as a judge would, who should adhere to the lifeless letter of law, and reject the spirit. Little circumstances, which were perhaps accidental in a great author, were by these critics considered to constitute his chief merit, and transmitted as essentials to be observed by all his successors. To these encroachments, time and ignorance, the two great supporters of imposture, gave authority; and thus many rules for good writing have been established, which have not the least foundation in truth or nature; and which commonly serve for no other purpose than to curb and restrain genius, in the same manner as it would have restrained the dancing-master, had the many excellent treatises on that art laid it down as an essential rule that every man must dance in chains.\n\nTo avoid, therefore, all imputation of laying down a rule for posterity, founded only on the authority of ipse dixit--for which, to say the truth, we have not the profoundest veneration--we shall here waive the privilege above contended for, and proceed to lay before the reader the reasons which have induced us to intersperse these several digressive essays in the course of this work.\n\nAnd here we shall of necessity be led to open a new vein of knowledge, which if it hath been discovered, hath not, to our remembrance, been wrought on by any antient or modern writer. This vein is no other than that of contrast, which runs through all the works of the creation, and may probably have a large share in constituting in us the idea of all beauty, as well natural as artificial: for what demonstrates the beauty and excellence of anything but its reverse? Thus the beauty of day, and that of summer, is set off by the horrors of night and winter. And, I believe, if it was possible for a man to have seen only the two former, he would have a very imperfect idea of their beauty.\n\nBut to avoid too serious an air; can it be doubted, but that the finest woman in the world would lose all benefit of her charms in the eye of a man who had never seen one of another cast? The ladies themselves seem so sensible of this, that they are all industrious to procure foils: nay, they will become foils to themselves; for I have observed (at Bath particularly) that they endeavour to appear as ugly as possible in the morning, in order to set off that beauty which they intend to show you in the evening.\n\nMost artists have this secret in practice, though some, perhaps, have not much studied the theory. The jeweller knows that the finest brilliant requires a foil; and the painter, by the contrast of his figures, often acquires great applause.\n\nA great genius among us will illustrate this matter fully. I cannot, indeed, range him under any general head of common artists, as he hath a title to be placed among those\n\nInventas qui vitam excoluere per artes. Who by invented arts have life improved.\n\nI mean here the inventor of that most exquisite entertainment, called the English Pantomime.\n\nThis entertainment consisted of two parts, which the inventor distinguished by the names of the serious and the comic. The serious exhibited a certain number of heathen gods and heroes, who were certainly the worst and dullest company into which an audience was ever introduced; and (which was a secret known to few) were actually intended so to be, in order to contrast the comic part of the entertainment, and to display the tricks of harlequin to the better advantage.\n\nThis was, perhaps, no very civil use of such personages: but the contrivance was, nevertheless, ingenious enough, and had its effect. And this will now plainly appear, if, instead of serious and comic, we supply the words duller and dullest; for the comic was certainly duller than anything before shown on the stage, and could be set off only by that superlative degree of dulness which composed the serious. So intolerably serious, indeed, were these gods and heroes, that harlequin (though the English gentleman of that name is not at all related to the French family, for he is of a much more serious disposition) was always welcome on the stage, as he relieved the audience from worse company.\n\nJudicious writers have always practised this art of contrast with great success. I have been surprized that Horace should cavil at this art in Homer; but indeed he contradicts himself in the very next line:\n\nIndignor quandoque bonus dormitat Homerus; Verum opere in longo fas est obrepere somnum.\n\nI grieve if e'er great Homer chance to sleep, Yet slumbers on long works have right to creep.\n\nFor we are not here to understand, as perhaps some have, that an author actually falls asleep while he is writing. It is true, that readers are too apt to be so overtaken; but if the work was as long as any of Oldmixon, the author himself is too well entertained to be subject to the least drowsiness. He is, as Mr Pope observes,\n\nSleepless himself to give his readers sleep.\n\nTo say the truth, these soporific parts are so many scenes of serious artfully interwoven, in order to contrast and set off the rest; and this is the true meaning of a late facetious writer, who told the public that whenever he was dull they might be assured there was a design in it.\n\nIn this light, then, or rather in this darkness, I would have the reader to consider these initial essays. And after this warning, if he shall be of opinion that he can find enough of serious in other parts of this history, he may pass over these, in which we profess to be laboriously dull, and begin the following books at the second chapter.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nIn which Mr Jones receives many friendly visits during his confinement; with some fine touches of the passion of love, scarce visible to the naked eye.\n\nTom Jones had many visitors during his confinement, though some, perhaps, were not very agreeable to him. Mr Allworthy saw him almost every day; but though he pitied Tom's sufferings, and greatly approved the gallant behaviour which had occasioned them; yet he thought this was a favourable opportunity to bring him to a sober sense of his indiscreet conduct; and that wholesome advice for that purpose could never be applied at a more proper season than at the present, when the mind was softened by pain and sickness, and alarmed by danger; and when its attention was unembarrassed with those turbulent passions which engage us in the pursuit of pleasure.\n\nAt all seasons, therefore, when the good man was alone with the youth, especially when the latter was totally at ease, he took occasion to remind him of his former miscarriages, but in the mildest and tenderest manner, and only in order to introduce the caution which he prescribed for his future behaviour; “on which alone,” he assured him, “would depend his own felicity, and the kindness which he might yet promise himself to receive at the hands of his father by adoption, unless he should hereafter forfeit his good opinion: for as to what had past,” he said, “it should be all forgiven and forgotten. He therefore advised him to make a good use of this accident, that so in the end it might prove a visitation for his own good.”\n\nThwackum was likewise pretty assiduous in his visits; and he too considered a sick-bed to be a convenient scene for lectures. His stile, however, was more severe than Mr Allworthy's: he told his pupil, “That he ought to look on his broken limb as a judgment from heaven on his sins. That it would become him to be daily on his knees, pouring forth thanksgivings that he had broken his arm only, and not his neck; which latter,” he said, “was very probably reserved for some future occasion, and that, perhaps, not very remote. For his part,” he said, “he had often wondered some judgment had not overtaken him before; but it might be perceived by this, that Divine punishments, though slow, are always sure.” Hence likewise he advised him, “to foresee, with equal certainty, the greater evils which were yet behind, and which were as sure as this of overtaking him in his state of reprobacy. These are,” said he, “to be averted only by such a thorough and sincere repentance as is not to be expected or hoped for from one so abandoned in his youth, and whose mind, I am afraid, is totally corrupted. It is my duty, however, to exhort you to this repentance, though I too well know all exhortations will be vain and fruitless. But liberavi animam meam. I can accuse my own conscience of no neglect; though it is at the same time with the utmost concern I see you travelling on to certain misery in this world, and to as certain damnation in the next.”\n\nSquare talked in a very different strain; he said, “Such accidents as a broken bone were below the consideration of a wise man. That it was abundantly sufficient to reconcile the mind to any of these mischances, to reflect that they are liable to befal the wisest of mankind, and are undoubtedly for the good of the whole.” He said, “It was a mere abuse of words to call those things evils, in which there was no moral unfitness: that pain, which was the worst consequence of such accidents, was the most contemptible thing in the world;” with more of the like sentences, extracted out of the second book of Tully's Tusculan questions, and from the great Lord Shaftesbury. In pronouncing these he was one day so eager, that he unfortunately bit his tongue; and in such a manner, that it not only put an end to his discourse, but created much emotion in him, and caused him to mutter an oath or two: but what was worst of all, this accident gave Thwackum, who was present, and who held all such doctrine to be heathenish and atheistical, an opportunity to clap a judgment on his back. Now this was done with so malicious a sneer, that it totally unhinged (if I may so say) the temper of the philosopher, which the bite of his tongue had somewhat ruffled; and as he was disabled from venting his wrath at his lips, he had possibly found a more violent method of revenging himself, had not the surgeon, who was then luckily in the room, contrary to his own interest, interposed and preserved the peace.\n\nMr Blifil visited his friend Jones but seldom, and never alone. This worthy young man, however, professed much regard for him, and as great concern at his misfortune; but cautiously avoided any intimacy, lest, as he frequently hinted, it might contaminate the sobriety of his own character: for which purpose he had constantly in his mouth that proverb in which Solomon speaks against evil communication. Not that he was so bitter as Thwackum; for he always expressed some hopes of Tom's reformation; “which,” he said, “the unparalleled goodness shown by his uncle on this occasion, must certainly effect in one not absolutely abandoned:” but concluded, “if Mr Jones ever offends hereafter, I shall not be able to say a syllable in his favour.”\n\nAs to Squire Western, he was seldom out of the sick-room, unless when he was engaged either in the field or over his bottle. Nay, he would sometimes retire hither to take his beer, and it was not without difficulty that he was prevented from forcing Jones to take his beer too: for no quack ever held his nostrum to be a more general panacea than he did this; which, he said, had more virtue in it than was in all the physic in an apothecary's shop. He was, however, by much entreaty, prevailed on to forbear the application of this medicine; but from serenading his patient every hunting morning with the horn under his window, it was impossible to withhold him; nor did he ever lay aside that hallow, with which he entered into all companies, when he visited Jones, without any regard to the sick person's being at that time either awake or asleep.\n\nThis boisterous behaviour, as it meant no harm, so happily it effected none, and was abundantly compensated to Jones, as soon as he was able to sit up, by the company of Sophia, whom the squire then brought to visit him; nor was it, indeed, long before Jones was able to attend her to the harpsichord, where she would kindly condescend, for hours together, to charm him with the most delicious music, unless when the squire thought proper to interrupt her, by insisting on Old Sir Simon, or some other of his favourite pieces.\n\nNotwithstanding the nicest guard which Sophia endeavoured to set on her behaviour, she could not avoid letting some appearances now and then slip forth: for love may again be likened to a disease in this, that when it is denied a vent in one part, it will certainly break out in another. What her lips, therefore, concealed, her eyes, her blushes, and many little involuntary actions, betrayed.\n\nOne day, when Sophia was playing on the harpsichord, and Jones was attending, the squire came into the room, crying, “There, Tom, I have had a battle for thee below-stairs with thick parson Thwackum. He hath been a telling Allworthy, before my face, that the broken bone was a judgment upon thee. D--n it, says I, how can that be? Did he not come by it in defence of a young woman? A judgment indeed! Pox, if he never doth anything worse, he will go to heaven sooner than all the parsons in the country. He hath more reason to glory in it than to be ashamed of it.”--“Indeed, sir,” says Jones, “I have no reason for either; but if it preserved Miss Western, I shall always think it the happiest accident of my life.”--“And to gu,” said the squire, “to zet Allworthy against thee vor it! D--n un, if the parson had unt his petticuoats on, I should have lent un o flick; for I love thee dearly, my boy, and d--n me if there is anything in my power which I won't do for thee. Sha't take thy choice of all the horses in my stable to-morrow morning, except only the Chevalier and Miss Slouch.” Jones thanked him, but declined accepting the offer. “Nay,” added the squire, “sha't ha the sorrel mare that Sophy rode. She cost me fifty guineas, and comes six years old this grass.” “If she had cost me a thousand,” cries Jones passionately, “I would have given her to the dogs.” “Pooh! pooh!” answered Western; “what! because she broke thy arm? Shouldst forget and forgive. I thought hadst been more a man than to bear malice against a dumb creature.”--Here Sophia interposed, and put an end to the conversation, by desiring her father's leave to play to him; a request which he never refused.\n\nThe countenance of Sophia had undergone more than one change during the foregoing speeches; and probably she imputed the passionate resentment which Jones had expressed against the mare, to a different motive from that from which her father had derived it. Her spirits were at this time in a visible flutter; and she played so intolerably ill, that had not Western soon fallen asleep, he must have remarked it. Jones, however, who was sufficiently awake, and was not without an ear any more than without eyes, made some observations; which being joined to all which the reader may remember to have passed formerly, gave him pretty strong assurances, when he came to reflect on the whole, that all was not well in the tender bosom of Sophia; an opinion which many young gentlemen will, I doubt not, extremely wonder at his not having been well confirmed in long ago. To confess the truth, he had rather too much diffidence in himself, and was not forward enough in seeing the advances of a young lady; a misfortune which can be cured only by that early town education, which is at present so generally in fashion.\n\nWhen these thoughts had fully taken possession of Jones, they occasioned a perturbation in his mind, which, in a constitution less pure and firm than his, might have been, at such a season, attended with very dangerous consequences. He was truly sensible of the great worth of Sophia. He extremely liked her person, no less admired her accomplishments, and tenderly loved her goodness. In reality, as he had never once entertained any thought of possessing her, nor had ever given the least voluntary indulgence to his inclinations, he had a much stronger passion for her than he himself was acquainted with. His heart now brought forth the full secret, at the same time that it assured him the adorable object returned his affection.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nWhich all who have no heart will think to contain much ado about nothing.\n\nThe reader will perhaps imagine the sensations which now arose in Jones to have been so sweet and delicious, that they would rather tend to produce a chearful serenity in the mind, than any of those dangerous effects which we have mentioned; but in fact, sensations of this kind, however delicious, are, at their first recognition, of a very tumultuous nature, and have very little of the opiate in them. They were, moreover, in the present case, embittered with certain circumstances, which being mixed with sweeter ingredients, tended altogether to compose a draught that might be termed bitter-sweet; than which, as nothing can be more disagreeable to the palate, so nothing, in the metaphorical sense, can be so injurious to the mind.\n\nFor first, though he had sufficient foundation to flatter himself in what he had observed in Sophia, he was not yet free from doubt of misconstruing compassion, or at best, esteem, into a warmer regard. He was far from a sanguine assurance that Sophia had any such affection towards him, as might promise his inclinations that harvest, which, if they were encouraged and nursed, they would finally grow up to require. Besides, if he could hope to find no bar to his happiness from the daughter, he thought himself certain of meeting an effectual bar in the father; who, though he was a country squire in his diversions, was perfectly a man of the world in whatever regarded his fortune; had the most violent affection for his only daughter, and had often signified, in his cups, the pleasure he proposed in seeing her married to one of the richest men in the county. Jones was not so vain and senseless a coxcomb as to expect, from any regard which Western had professed for him, that he would ever be induced to lay aside these views of advancing his daughter. He well knew that fortune is generally the principal, if not the sole, consideration, which operates on the best of parents in these matters: for friendship makes us warmly espouse the interest of others; but it is very cold to the gratification of their passions. Indeed, to feel the happiness which may result from this, it is necessary we should possess the passion ourselves. As he had therefore no hopes of obtaining her father's consent; so he thought to endeavour to succeed without it, and by such means to frustrate the great point of Mr Western's life, was to make a very ill use of his hospitality, and a very ungrateful return to the many little favours received (however roughly) at his hands. If he saw such a consequence with horror and disdain, how much more was he shocked with what regarded Mr Allworthy; to whom, as he had more than filial obligations, so had he for him more than filial piety! He knew the nature of that good man to be so averse to any baseness or treachery, that the least attempt of such a kind would make the sight of the guilty person for ever odious to his eyes, and his name a detestable sound in his ears. The appearance of such unsurmountable difficulties was sufficient to have inspired him with despair, however ardent his wishes had been; but even these were contruoled by compassion for another woman. The idea of lovely Molly now intruded itself before him. He had sworn eternal constancy in her arms, and she had as often vowed never to out-live his deserting her. He now saw her in all the most shocking postures of death; nay, he considered all the miseries of prostitution to which she would be liable, and of which he would be doubly the occasion; first by seducing, and then by deserting her; for he well knew the hatred which all her neighbours, and even her own sisters, bore her, and how ready they would all be to tear her to pieces. Indeed, he had exposed her to more envy than shame, or rather to the latter by means of the former: for many women abused her for being a whore, while they envied her her lover, and her finery, and would have been themselves glad to have purchased these at the same rate. The ruin, therefore, of the poor girl must, he foresaw, unavoidably attend his deserting her; and this thought stung him to the soul. Poverty and distress seemed to him to give none a right of aggravating those misfortunes. The meanness of her condition did not represent her misery as of little consequence in his eyes, nor did it appear to justify, or even to palliate, his guilt, in bringing that misery upon her. But why do I mention justification? His own heart would not suffer him to destroy a human creature who, he thought, loved him, and had to that love sacrificed her innocence. His own good heart pleaded her cause; not as a cold venal advocate, but as one interested in the event, and which must itself deeply share in all the agonies its owner brought on another.\n\nWhen this powerful advocate had sufficiently raised the pity of Jones, by painting poor Molly in all the circumstances of wretchedness; it artfully called in the assistance of another passion, and represented the girl in all the amiable colours of youth, health, and beauty; as one greatly the object of desire, and much more so, at least to a good mind, from being, at the same time, the object of compassion.\n\nAmidst these thoughts, poor Jones passed a long sleepless night, and in the morning the result of the whole was to abide by Molly, and to think no more of Sophia.\n\nIn this virtuous resolution he continued all the next day till the evening, cherishing the idea of Molly, and driving Sophia from his thoughts; but in the fatal evening, a very trifling accident set all his passions again on float, and worked so total a change in his mind, that we think it decent to communicate it in a fresh chapter.\n\nA little chapter, in which is contained a little incident.\n\nAmong other visitants, who paid their compliments to the young gentleman in his confinement, Mrs Honour was one. The reader, perhaps, when he reflects on some expressions which have formerly dropt from her, may conceive that she herself had a very particular affection for Mr Jones; but, in reality, it was no such thing. Tom was a handsome young fellow; and for that species of men Mrs Honour had some regard; but this was perfectly indiscriminate; for having being crossed in the love which she bore a certain nobleman's footman, who had basely deserted her after a promise of marriage, she had so securely kept together the broken remains of her heart, that no man had ever since been able to possess himself of any single fragment. She viewed all handsome men with that equal regard and benevolence which a sober and virtuous mind bears to all the good. She might indeed be called a lover of men, as Socrates was a lover of mankind, preferring one to another for corporeal, as he for mental qualifications; but never carrying this preference so far as to cause any perturbation in the philosophical serenity of her temper.\n\nThe day after Mr Jones had that conflict with himself which we have seen in the preceding chapter, Mrs Honour came into his room, and finding him alone, began in the following manner:--“La, sir, where do you think I have been? I warrants you, you would not guess in fifty years; but if you did guess, to be sure I must not tell you neither.”--“Nay, if it be something which you must not tell me,” said Jones, “I shall have the curiosity to enquire, and I know you will not be so barbarous to refuse me.”--“I don't know,” cries she, “why I should refuse you neither, for that matter; for to be sure you won't mention it any more. And for that matter, if you knew where I have been, unless you knew what I have been about, it would not signify much. Nay, I don't see why it should be kept a secret for my part; for to be sure she is the best lady in the world.” Upon this, Jones began to beg earnestly to be let into this secret, and faithfully promised not to divulge it. She then proceeded thus:--“Why, you must know, sir, my young lady sent me to enquire after Molly Seagrim, and to see whether the wench wanted anything; to be sure, I did not care to go, methinks; but servants must do what they are ordered.--How could you undervalue yourself so, Mr Jones?--So my lady bid me go and carry her some linen, and other things. She is too good. If such forward sluts were sent to Bridewell, it would be better for them. I told my lady, says I, madam, your la'ship is encouraging idleness.”--“And was my Sophia so good?” says Jones. “My Sophia! I assure you, marry come up,” answered Honour. “And yet if you knew all--indeed, if I was as Mr Jones, I should look a little higher than such trumpery as Molly Seagrim.” “What do you mean by these words,” replied Jones, “if I knew all?” “I mean what I mean,” says Honour. “Don't you remember putting your hands in my lady's muff once? I vow I could almost find in my heart to tell, if I was certain my lady would never come to the hearing on't.” Jones then made several solemn protestations. And Honour proceeded--“Then to be sure, my lady gave me that muff; and afterwards, upon hearing what you had done”--“Then you told her what I had done?” interrupted Jones. “If I did, sir,” answered she, “you need not be angry with me. Many's the man would have given his head to have had my lady told, if they had known,--for, to be sure, the biggest lord in the land might be proud--but, I protest, I have a great mind not to tell you.” Jones fell to entreaties, and soon prevailed on her to go on thus. “You must know then, sir, that my lady had given this muff to me; but about a day or two after I had told her the story, she quarrels with her new muff, and to be sure it is the prettiest that ever was seen. Honour, says she, this is an odious muff; it is too big for me, I can't wear it: till I can get another, you must let me have my old one again, and you may have this in the room on't--for she's a good lady, and scorns to give a thing and take a thing, I promise you that. So to be sure I fetched it her back again, and, I believe, she hath worn it upon her arm almost ever since, and I warrants hath given it many a kiss when nobody hath seen her.”\n\nHere the conversation was interrupted by Mr Western himself, who came to summon Jones to the harpsichord; whither the poor young fellow went all pale and trembling. This Western observed, but, on seeing Mrs Honour, imputed it to a wrong cause; and having given Jones a hearty curse between jest and earnest, he bid him beat abroad, and not poach up the game in his warren.\n\nSophia looked this evening with more than usual beauty, and we may believe it was no small addition to her charms, in the eye of Mr Jones, that she now happened to have on her right arm this very muff.\n\nShe was playing one of her father's favourite tunes, and he was leaning on her chair, when the muff fell over her fingers, and put her out. This so disconcerted the squire, that he snatched the muff from her, and with a hearty curse threw it into the fire. Sophia instantly started up, and with the utmost eagerness recovered it from the flames.\n\nThough this incident will probably appear of little consequence to many of our readers; yet, trifling as it was, it had so violent an effect on poor Jones, that we thought it our duty to relate it. In reality, there are many little circumstances too often omitted by injudicious historians, from which events of the utmost importance arise. The world may indeed be considered as a vast machine, in which the great wheels are originally set in motion by those which are very minute, and almost imperceptible to any but the strongest eyes.\n\nThus, not all the charms of the incomparable Sophia; not all the dazzling brightness, and languishing softness of her eyes; the harmony of her voice, and of her person; not all her wit, good-humour, greatness of mind, or sweetness of disposition, had been able so absolutely to conquer and enslave the heart of poor Jones, as this little incident of the muff. Thus the poet sweetly sings of Troy--\n\n--Captique dolis lachrymisque coacti Quos neque Tydides, nec Larissaeus Achilles, Non anni domuere decem, non mille Carinae.\n\nWhat Diomede or Thetis' greater son, A thousand ships, nor ten years' siege had done False tears and fawning words the city won.\n\nThe citadel of Jones was now taken by surprize. All those considerations of honour and prudence which our heroe had lately with so much military wisdom placed as guards over the avenues of his heart, ran away from their posts, and the god of love marched in, in triumph.\n\nA very long chapter, containing a very great incident.\n\nBut though this victorious deity easily expelled his avowed enemies from the heart of Jones, he found it more difficult to supplant the garrison which he himself had placed there. To lay aside all allegory, the concern for what must become of poor Molly greatly disturbed and perplexed the mind of the worthy youth. The superior merit of Sophia totally eclipsed, or rather extinguished, all the beauties of the poor girl; but compassion instead of contempt succeeded to love. He was convinced the girl had placed all her affections, and all her prospect of future happiness, in him only. For this he had, he knew, given sufficient occasion, by the utmost profusion of tenderness towards her: a tenderness which he had taken every means to persuade her he would always maintain. She, on her side, had assured him of her firm belief in his promise, and had with the most solemn vows declared, that on his fulfilling or breaking these promises, it depended, whether she should be the happiest or most miserable of womankind. And to be the author of this highest degree of misery to a human being, was a thought on which he could not bear to ruminate a single moment. He considered this poor girl as having sacrificed to him everything in her little power; as having been at her own expense the object of his pleasure; as sighing and languishing for him even at that very instant. Shall then, says he, my recovery, for which she hath so ardently wished; shall my presence, which she hath so eagerly expected, instead of giving her that joy with which she hath flattered herself, cast her at once down into misery and despair? Can I be such a villain? Here, when the genius of poor Molly seemed triumphant, the love of Sophia towards him, which now appeared no longer dubious, rushed upon his mind, and bore away every obstacle before it.\n\nAt length it occurred to him, that he might possibly be able to make Molly amends another way; namely, by giving her a sum of money. This, nevertheless, he almost despaired of her accepting, when he recollected the frequent and vehement assurances he had received from her, that the world put in balance with him would make her no amends for his loss. However, her extreme poverty, and chiefly her egregious vanity (somewhat of which hath been already hinted to the reader), gave him some little hope, that, notwithstanding all her avowed tenderness, she might in time be brought to content herself with a fortune superior to her expectation, and which might indulge her vanity, by setting her above all her equals. He resolved therefore to take the first opportunity of making a proposal of this kind.\n\nOne day, accordingly, when his arm was so well recovered that he could walk easily with it slung in a sash, he stole forth, at a season when the squire was engaged in his field exercises, and visited his fair one. Her mother and sisters, whom he found taking their tea, informed him first that Molly was not at home; but afterwards the eldest sister acquainted him, with a malicious smile, that she was above stairs a-bed. Tom had no objection to this situation of his mistress, and immediately ascended the ladder which led towards her bed-chamber; but when he came to the top, he, to his great surprize, found the door fast; nor could he for some time obtain any answer from within; for Molly, as she herself afterwards informed him, was fast asleep.\n\nThe extremes of grief and joy have been remarked to produce very similar effects; and when either of these rushes on us by surprize, it is apt to create such a total perturbation and confusion, that we are often thereby deprived of the use of all our faculties. It cannot therefore be wondered at, that the unexpected sight of Mr Jones should so strongly operate on the mind of Molly, and should overwhelm her with such confusion, that for some minutes she was unable to express the great raptures, with which the reader will suppose she was affected on this occasion. As for Jones, he was so entirely possessed, and as it were enchanted, by the presence of his beloved object, that he for a while forgot Sophia, and consequently the principal purpose of his visit.\n\nThis, however, soon recurred to his memory; and after the first transports of their meeting were over, he found means by degrees to introduce a discourse on the fatal consequences which must attend their amour, if Mr Allworthy, who had strictly forbidden him ever seeing her more, should discover that he still carried on this commerce. Such a discovery, which his enemies gave him reason to think would be unavoidable, must, he said, end in his ruin, and consequently in hers. Since therefore their hard fates had determined that they must separate, he advised her to bear it with resolution, and swore he would never omit any opportunity, through the course of his life, of showing her the sincerity of his affection, by providing for her in a manner beyond her utmost expectation, or even beyond her wishes, if ever that should be in his power; concluding at last, that she might soon find some man who would marry her, and who would make her much happier than she could be by leading a disreputable life with him.\n\nMolly remained a few moments in silence, and then bursting into a flood of tears, she began to upbraid him in the following words: “And this is your love for me, to forsake me in this manner, now you have ruined me! How often, when I have told you that all men are false and perjury alike, and grow tired of us as soon as ever they have had their wicked wills of us, how often have you sworn you would never forsake me! And can you be such a perjury man after all? What signifies all the riches in the world to me without you, now you have gained my heart, so you have--you have--? Why do you mention another man to me? I can never love any other man as long as I live. All other men are nothing to me. If the greatest squire in all the country would come a suiting to me to-morrow, I would not give my company to him. No, I shall always hate and despise the whole sex for your sake.”--\n\nShe was proceeding thus, when an accident put a stop to her tongue, before it had run out half its career. The room, or rather garret, in which Molly lay, being up one pair of stairs, that is to say, at the top of the house, was of a sloping figure, resembling the great Delta of the Greeks. The English reader may perhaps form a better idea of it, by being told that it was impossible to stand upright anywhere but in the middle. Now, as this room wanted the conveniency of a closet, Molly had, to supply that defect, nailed up an old rug against the rafters of the house, which enclosed a little hole where her best apparel, such as the remains of that sack which we have formerly mentioned, some caps, and other things with which she had lately provided herself, were hung up and secured from the dust.\n\nThis enclosed place exactly fronted the foot of the bed, to which, indeed, the rug hung so near, that it served in a manner to supply the want of curtains. Now, whether Molly, in the agonies of her rage, pushed this rug with her feet; or Jones might touch it; or whether the pin or nail gave way of its own accord, I am not certain; but as Molly pronounced those last words, which are recorded above, the wicked rug got loose from its fastening, and discovered everything hid behind it; where among other female utensils appeared--(with shame I write it, and with sorrow will it be read)--the philosopher Square, in a posture (for the place would not near admit his standing upright) as ridiculous as can possibly be conceived.\n\nThe posture, indeed, in which he stood, was not greatly unlike that of a soldier who is tied neck and heels; or rather resembling the attitude in which we often see fellows in the public streets of London, who are not suffering but deserving punishment by so standing. He had a nightcap belonging to Molly on his head, and his two large eyes, the moment the rug fell, stared directly at Jones; so that when the idea of philosophy was added to the figure now discovered, it would have been very difficult for any spectator to have refrained from immoderate laughter.\n\nI question not but the surprize of the reader will be here equal to that of Jones; as the suspicions which must arise from the appearance of this wise and grave man in such a place, may seem so inconsistent with that character which he hath, doubtless, maintained hitherto, in the opinion of every one.\n\nBut to confess the truth, this inconsistency is rather imaginary than real. Philosophers are composed of flesh and blood as well as other human creatures; and however sublimated and refined the theory of these may be, a little practical frailty is as incident to them as to other mortals. It is, indeed, in theory only, and not in practice, as we have before hinted, that consists the difference: for though such great beings think much better and more wisely, they always act exactly like other men. They know very well how to subdue all appetites and passions, and to despise both pain and pleasure; and this knowledge affords much delightful contemplation, and is easily acquired; but the practice would be vexatious and troublesome; and, therefore, the same wisdom which teaches them to know this, teaches them to avoid carrying it into execution.\n\nMr Square happened to be at church on that Sunday, when, as the reader may be pleased to remember, the appearance of Molly in her sack had caused all that disturbance. Here he first observed her, and was so pleased with her beauty, that he prevailed with the young gentlemen to change their intended ride that evening, that he might pass by the habitation of Molly, and by that means might obtain a second chance of seeing her. This reason, however, as he did not at that time mention to any, so neither did we think proper to communicate it then to the reader.\n\nAmong other particulars which constituted the unfitness of things in Mr Square's opinion, danger and difficulty were two. The difficulty therefore which he apprehended there might be in corrupting this young wench, and the danger which would accrue to his character on the discovery, were such strong dissuasives, that it is probable he at first intended to have contented himself with the pleasing ideas which the sight of beauty furnishes us with. These the gravest men, after a full meal of serious meditation, often allow themselves by way of dessert: for which purpose, certain books and pictures find their way into the most private recesses of their study, and a certain liquorish part of natural philosophy is often the principal subject of their conversation.\n\nBut when the philosopher heard, a day or two afterwards, that the fortress of virtue had already been subdued, he began to give a larger scope to his desires. His appetite was not of that squeamish kind which cannot feed on a dainty because another hath tasted it. In short, he liked the girl the better for the want of that chastity, which, if she had possessed it, must have been a bar to his pleasures; he pursued and obtained her.\n\nThe reader will be mistaken, if he thinks Molly gave Square the preference to her younger lover: on the contrary, had she been confined to the choice of one only, Tom Jones would undoubtedly have been, of the two, the victorious person. Nor was it solely the consideration that two are better than one (though this had its proper weight) to which Mr Square owed his success: the absence of Jones during his confinement was an unlucky circumstance; and in that interval some well-chosen presents from the philosopher so softened and unguarded the girl's heart, that a favourable opportunity became irresistible, and Square triumphed over the poor remains of virtue which subsisted in the bosom of Molly.\n\nIt was now about a fortnight since this conquest, when Jones paid the above-mentioned visit to his mistress, at a time when she and Square were in bed together. This was the true reason why the mother denied her as we have seen; for as the old woman shared in the profits arising from the iniquity of her daughter, she encouraged and protected her in it to the utmost of her power; but such was the envy and hatred which the elder sister bore towards Molly, that, notwithstanding she had some part of the booty, she would willingly have parted with this to ruin her sister and spoil her trade. Hence she had acquainted Jones with her being above-stairs in bed, in hopes that he might have caught her in Square's arms. This, however, Molly found means to prevent, as the door was fastened; which gave her an opportunity of conveying her lover behind that rug or blanket where he now was unhappily discovered.\n\nSquare no sooner made his appearance than Molly flung herself back in her bed, cried out she was undone, and abandoned herself to despair. This poor girl, who was yet but a novice in her business, had not arrived to that perfection of assurance which helps off a town lady in any extremity; and either prompts her with an excuse, or else inspires her to brazen out the matter with her husband, who, from love of quiet, or out of fear of his reputation--and sometimes, perhaps, from fear of the gallant, who, like Mr Constant in the play, wears a sword--is glad to shut his eyes, and content to put his horns in his pocket. Molly, on the contrary, was silenced by this evidence, and very fairly gave up a cause which she had hitherto maintained with so many tears, and with such solemn and vehement protestations of the purest love and constancy.\n\nAs to the gentleman behind the arras, he was not in much less consternation. He stood for a while motionless, and seemed equally at a loss what to say, or whither to direct his eyes. Jones, though perhaps the most astonished of the three, first found his tongue; and being immediately recovered from those uneasy sensations which Molly by her upbraidings had occasioned, he burst into a loud laughter, and then saluting Mr Square, advanced to take him by the hand, and to relieve him from his place of confinement.\n\nSquare being now arrived in the middle of the room, in which part only he could stand upright, looked at Jones with a very grave countenance, and said to him, “Well, sir, I see you enjoy this mighty discovery, and, I dare swear, take great delight in the thoughts of exposing me; but if you will consider the matter fairly, you will find you are yourself only to blame. I am not guilty of corrupting innocence. I have done nothing for which that part of the world which judges of matters by the rule of right, will condemn me. Fitness is governed by the nature of things, and not by customs, forms, or municipal laws. Nothing is indeed unfit which is not unnatural.”--“Well reasoned, old boy,” answered Jones; “but why dost thou think that I should desire to expose thee? I promise thee, I was never better pleased with thee in my life; and unless thou hast a mind to discover it thyself, this affair may remain a profound secret for me.”--“Nay, Mr Jones,” replied Square, “I would not be thought to undervalue reputation. Good fame is a species of the Kalon, and it is by no means fitting to neglect it. Besides, to murder one's own reputation is a kind of suicide, a detestable and odious vice. If you think proper, therefore, to conceal any infirmity of mine (for such I may have, since no man is perfectly perfect), I promise you I will not betray myself. Things may be fitting to be done, which are not fitting to be boasted of; for by the perverse judgment of the world, that often becomes the subject of censure, which is, in truth, not only innocent but laudable.”--“Right!” cries Jones: “what can be more innocent than the indulgence of a natural appetite? or what more laudable than the propagation of our species?”--“To be serious with you,” answered Square, “I profess they always appeared so to me.”--“And yet,” said Jones, “you was of a different opinion when my affair with this girl was first discovered.”--“Why, I must confess,” says Square, “as the matter was misrepresented to me, by that parson Thwackum, I might condemn the corruption of innocence: it was that, sir, it was that--and that--: for you must know, Mr Jones, in the consideration of fitness, very minute circumstances, sir, very minute circumstances cause great alteration.”--“Well,” cries Jones, “be that as it will, it shall be your own fault, as I have promised you, if you ever hear any more of this adventure. Behave kindly to the girl, and I will never open my lips concerning the matter to any one. And, Molly, do you be faithful to your friend, and I will not only forgive your infidelity to me, but will do you all the service I can.” So saying, he took a hasty leave, and, slipping down the ladder, retired with much expedition.\n\nSquare was rejoiced to find this adventure was likely to have no worse conclusion; and as for Molly, being recovered from her confusion, she began at first to upbraid Square with having been the occasion of her loss of Jones; but that gentleman soon found the means of mitigating her anger, partly by caresses, and partly by a small nostrum from his purse, of wonderful and approved efficacy in purging off the ill humours of the mind, and in restoring it to a good temper.\n\nShe then poured forth a vast profusion of tenderness towards her new lover; turned all she had said to Jones, and Jones himself, into ridicule; and vowed, though he once had the possession of her person, that none but Square had ever been master of her heart.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nBy comparing which with the former, the reader may possibly correct some abuse which he hath formerly been guilty of in the application of the word love.\n\nThe infidelity of Molly, which Jones had now discovered, would, perhaps, have vindicated a much greater degree of resentment than he expressed on the occasion; and if he had abandoned her directly from that moment, very few, I believe, would have blamed him.\n\nCertain, however, it is, that he saw her in the light of compassion; and though his love to her was not of that kind which could give him any great uneasiness at her inconstancy, yet was he not a little shocked on reflecting that he had himself originally corrupted her innocence; for to this corruption he imputed all the vice into which she appeared now so likely to plunge herself.\n\nThis consideration gave him no little uneasiness, till Betty, the elder sister, was so kind, some time afterwards, entirely to cure him by a hint, that one Will Barnes, and not himself, had been the first seducer of Molly; and that the little child, which he had hitherto so certainly concluded to be his own, might very probably have an equal title, at least, to claim Barnes for its father.\n\nJones eagerly pursued this scent when he had first received it; and in a very short time was sufficiently assured that the girl had told him truth, not only by the confession of the fellow, but at last by that of Molly herself.\n\nThis Will Barnes was a country gallant, and had acquired as many trophies of this kind as any ensign or attorney's clerk in the kingdom. He had, indeed, reduced several women to a state of utter profligacy, had broke the hearts of some, and had the honour of occasioning the violent death of one poor girl, who had either drowned herself, or, what was rather more probable, had been drowned by him.\n\nAmong other of his conquests, this fellow had triumphed over the heart of Betty Seagrim. He had made love to her long before Molly was grown to be a fit object of that pastime; but had afterwards deserted her, and applied to her sister, with whom he had almost immediate success. Now Will had, in reality, the sole possession of Molly's affection, while Jones and Square were almost equally sacrifices to her interest and to her pride.\n\nHence had grown that implacable hatred which we have before seen raging in the mind of Betty; though we did not think it necessary to assign this cause sooner, as envy itself alone was adequate to all the effects we have mentioned.\n\nJones was become perfectly easy by possession of this secret with regard to Molly; but as to Sophia, he was far from being in a state of tranquillity; nay, indeed, he was under the most violent perturbation; his heart was now, if I may use the metaphor, entirely evacuated, and Sophia took absolute possession of it. He loved her with an unbounded passion, and plainly saw the tender sentiments she had for him; yet could not this assurance lessen his despair of obtaining the consent of her father, nor the horrors which attended his pursuit of her by any base or treacherous method.\n\nThe injury which he must thus do to Mr Western, and the concern which would accrue to Mr Allworthy, were circumstances that tormented him all day, and haunted him on his pillow at night. His life was a constant struggle between honour and inclination, which alternately triumphed over each other in his mind. He often resolved, in the absence of Sophia, to leave her father's house, and to see her no more; and as often, in her presence, forgot all those resolutions, and determined to pursue her at the hazard of his life, and at the forfeiture of what was much dearer to him.\n\nThis conflict began soon to produce very strong and visible effects: for he lost all his usual sprightliness and gaiety of temper, and became not only melancholy when alone, but dejected and absent in company; nay, if ever he put on a forced mirth, to comply with Mr Western's humour, the constraint appeared so plain, that he seemed to have been giving the strongest evidence of what he endeavoured to conceal by such ostentation.\n\nIt may, perhaps, be a question, whether the art which he used to conceal his passion, or the means which honest nature employed to reveal it, betrayed him most: for while art made him more than ever reserved to Sophia, and forbad him to address any of his discourse to her, nay, to avoid meeting her eyes, with the utmost caution; nature was no less busy in counterplotting him. Hence, at the approach of the young lady, he grew pale; and if this was sudden, started. If his eyes accidentally met hers, the blood rushed into his cheeks, and his countenance became all over scarlet. If common civility ever obliged him to speak to her, as to drink her health at table, his tongue was sure to falter. If he touched her, his hand, nay his whole frame, trembled. And if any discourse tended, however remotely, to raise the idea of love, an involuntary sigh seldom failed to steal from his bosom. Most of which accidents nature was wonderfully industrious to throw daily in his way.\n\nAll these symptoms escaped the notice of the squire: but not so of Sophia. She soon perceived these agitations of mind in Jones, and was at no loss to discover the cause; for indeed she recognized it in her own breast. And this recognition is, I suppose, that sympathy which hath been so often noted in lovers, and which will sufficiently account for her being so much quicker-sighted than her father.\n\nBut, to say the truth, there is a more simple and plain method of accounting for that prodigious superiority of penetration which we must observe in some men over the rest of the human species, and one which will serve not only in the case of lovers, but of all others. From whence is it that the knave is generally so quick-sighted to those symptoms and operations of knavery, which often dupe an honest man of a much better understanding? There surely is no general sympathy among knaves; nor have they, like freemasons, any common sign of communication. In reality, it is only because they have the same thing in their heads, and their thoughts are turned the same way. Thus, that Sophia saw, and that Western did not see, the plain symptoms of love in Jones can be no wonder, when we consider that the idea of love never entered into the head of the father, whereas the daughter, at present, thought of nothing else.\n\nWhen Sophia was well satisfied of the violent passion which tormented poor Jones, and no less certain that she herself was its object, she had not the least difficulty in discovering the true cause of his present behaviour. This highly endeared him to her, and raised in her mind two of the best affections which any lover can wish to raise in a mistress--these were, esteem and pity--for sure the most outrageously rigid among her sex will excuse her pitying a man whom she saw miserable on her own account; nor can they blame her for esteeming one who visibly, from the most honourable motives, endeavoured to smother a flame in his own bosom, which, like the famous Spartan theft, was preying upon and consuming his very vitals. Thus his backwardness, his shunning her, his coldness, and his silence, were the forwardest, the most diligent, the warmest, and most eloquent advocates; and wrought so violently on her sensible and tender heart, that she soon felt for him all those gentle sensations which are consistent with a virtuous and elevated female mind. In short, all which esteem, gratitude, and pity, can inspire in such towards an agreeable man--indeed, all which the nicest delicacy can allow. In a word, she was in love with him to distraction.\n\nOne day this young couple accidentally met in the garden, at the end of the two walks which were both bounded by that canal in which Jones had formerly risqued drowning to retrieve the little bird that Sophia had there lost.\n\nThis place had been of late much frequented by Sophia. Here she used to ruminate, with a mixture of pain and pleasure, on an incident which, however trifling in itself, had possibly sown the first seeds of that affection which was now arrived to such maturity in her heart.\n\nHere then this young couple met. They were almost close together before either of them knew anything of the other's approach. A bystander would have discovered sufficient marks of confusion in the countenance of each; but they felt too much themselves to make any observation. As soon as Jones had a little recovered his first surprize, he accosted the young lady with some of the ordinary forms of salutation, which she in the same manner returned; and their conversation began, as usual, on the delicious beauty of the morning. Hence they past to the beauty of the place, on which Jones launched forth very high encomiums. When they came to the tree whence he had formerly tumbled into the canal, Sophia could not help reminding him of that accident, and said, “I fancy, Mr Jones, you have some little shuddering when you see that water.”--“I assure you, madam,” answered Jones, “the concern you felt at the loss of your little bird will always appear to me the highest circumstance in that adventure. Poor little Tommy! there is the branch he stood upon. How could the little wretch have the folly to fly away from that state of happiness in which I had the honour to place him? His fate was a just punishment for his ingratitude.”--“Upon my word, Mr Jones,” said she, “your gallantry very narrowly escaped as severe a fate. Sure the remembrance must affect you.”--“Indeed, madam,” answered he, “if I have any reason to reflect with sorrow on it, it is, perhaps, that the water had not been a little deeper, by which I might have escaped many bitter heart-aches that Fortune seems to have in store for me.”--“Fie, Mr Jones!” replied Sophia; “I am sure you cannot be in earnest now. This affected contempt of life is only an excess of your complacence to me. You would endeavour to lessen the obligation of having twice ventured it for my sake. Beware the third time.” She spoke these last words with a smile, and a softness inexpressible. Jones answered with a sigh, “He feared it was already too late for caution:” and then looking tenderly and stedfastly on her, he cried, “Oh, Miss Western! can you desire me to live? Can you wish me so ill?” Sophia, looking down on the ground, answered with some hesitation, “Indeed, Mr Jones, I do not wish you ill.”--“Oh, I know too well that heavenly temper,” cries Jones, “that divine goodness, which is beyond every other charm.”--“Nay, now,” answered she, “I understand you not. I can stay no longer.”--“I--I would not be understood!” cries he; “nay, I can't be understood. I know not what I say. Meeting you here so unexpectedly, I have been unguarded: for Heaven's sake pardon me, if I have said anything to offend you. I did not mean it. Indeed, I would rather have died--nay, the very thought would kill me.”--“You surprize me,” answered she. “How can you possibly think you have offended me?”--“Fear, madam,” says he, “easily runs into madness; and there is no degree of fear like that which I feel of offending you. How can I speak then? Nay, don't look angrily at me: one frown will destroy me. I mean nothing. Blame my eyes, or blame those beauties. What am I saying? Pardon me if I have said too much. My heart overflowed. I have struggled with my love to the utmost, and have endeavoured to conceal a fever which preys on my vitals, and will, I hope, soon make it impossible for me ever to offend you more.”\n\nMr Jones now fell a trembling as if he had been shaken with the fit of an ague. Sophia, who was in a situation not very different from his, answered in these words: “Mr Jones, I will not affect to misunderstand you; indeed, I understand you too well; but, for Heaven's sake, if you have any affection for me, let me make the best of my way into the house. I wish I may be able to support myself thither.”\n\nJones, who was hardly able to support himself, offered her his arm, which she condescended to accept, but begged he would not mention a word more to her of this nature at present. He promised he would not; insisting only on her forgiveness of what love, without the leave of his will, had forced from him: this, she told him, he knew how to obtain by his future behaviour; and thus this young pair tottered and trembled along, the lover not once daring to squeeze the hand of his mistress, though it was locked in his.\n\nSophia immediately retired to her chamber, where Mrs Honour and the hartshorn were summoned to her assistance. As to poor Jones, the only relief to his distempered mind was an unwelcome piece of news, which, as it opens a scene of different nature from those in which the reader hath lately been conversant, will be communicated to him in the next chapter.\n\nIn which Mr Allworthy appears on a sick-bed.\n\nMr Western was become so fond of Jones that he was unwilling to part with him, though his arm had been long since cured; and Jones, either from the love of sport, or from some other reason, was easily persuaded to continue at his house, which he did sometimes for a fortnight together without paying a single visit at Mr Allworthy's; nay, without ever hearing from thence.\n\nMr Allworthy had been for some days indisposed with a cold, which had been attended with a little fever. This he had, however, neglected; as it was usual with him to do all manner of disorders which did not confine him to his bed, or prevent his several faculties from performing their ordinary functions;--a conduct which we would by no means be thought to approve or recommend to imitation; for surely the gentlemen of the Aesculapian art are in the right in advising, that the moment the disease has entered at one door, the physician should be introduced at the other: what else is meant by that old adage, Venienti occurrite morbo? “Oppose a distemper at its first approach.” Thus the doctor and the disease meet in fair and equal conflict; whereas, by giving time to the latter, we often suffer him to fortify and entrench himself, like a French army; so that the learned gentleman finds it very difficult, and sometimes impossible, to come at the enemy. Nay, sometimes by gaining time the disease applies to the French military politics, and corrupts nature over to his side, and then all the powers of physic must arrive too late. Agreeable to these observations was, I remember, the complaint of the great Doctor Misaubin, who used very pathetically to lament the late applications which were made to his skill, saying, “Bygar, me believe my pation take me for de undertaker, for dey never send for me till de physicion have kill dem.”\n\nMr Allworthy's distemper, by means of this neglect, gained such ground, that, when the increase of his fever obliged him to send for assistance, the doctor at his first arrival shook his head, wished he had been sent for sooner, and intimated that he thought him in very imminent danger. Mr Allworthy, who had settled all his affairs in this world, and was as well prepared as it is possible for human nature to be for the other, received this information with the utmost calmness and unconcern. He could, indeed, whenever he laid himself down to rest, say with Cato in the tragical poem--\n\nLet guilt or fear Disturb man's rest: Cato knows neither of them; Indifferent in his choice to sleep or die.\n\nIn reality, he could say this with ten times more reason and confidence than Cato, or any other proud fellow among the antient or modern heroes; for he was not only devoid of fear, but might be considered as a faithful labourer, when at the end of harvest he is summoned to receive his reward at the hands of a bountiful master.\n\nThe good man gave immediate orders for all his family to be summoned round him. None of these were then abroad, but Mrs Blifil, who had been some time in London, and Mr Jones, whom the reader hath just parted from at Mr Western's, and who received this summons just as Sophia had left him.\n\nThe news of Mr Allworthy's danger (for the servant told him he was dying) drove all thoughts of love out of his head. He hurried instantly into the chariot which was sent for him, and ordered the coachman to drive with all imaginable haste; nor did the idea of Sophia, I believe, once occur to him on the way.\n\nAnd now the whole family, namely, Mr Blifil, Mr Jones, Mr Thwackum, Mr Square, and some of the servants (for such were Mr Allworthy's orders) being all assembled round his bed, the good man sat up in it, and was beginning to speak, when Blifil fell to blubbering, and began to express very loud and bitter lamentations. Upon this Mr Allworthy shook him by the hand, and said, “Do not sorrow thus, my dear nephew, at the most ordinary of all human occurrences. When misfortunes befal our friends we are justly grieved; for those are accidents which might often have been avoided, and which may seem to render the lot of one man more peculiarly unhappy than that of others; but death is certainly unavoidable, and is that common lot in which alone the fortunes of all men agree: nor is the time when this happens to us very material. If the wisest of men hath compared life to a span, surely we may be allowed to consider it as a day. It is my fate to leave it in the evening; but those who are taken away earlier have only lost a few hours, at the best little worth lamenting, and much oftener hours of labour and fatigue, of pain and sorrow. One of the Roman poets, I remember, likens our leaving life to our departure from a feast;--a thought which hath often occurred to me when I have seen men struggling to protract an entertainment, and to enjoy the company of their friends a few moments longer. Alas! how short is the most protracted of such enjoyments! how immaterial the difference between him who retires the soonest, and him who stays the latest! This is seeing life in the best view, and this unwillingness to quit our friends is the most amiable motive from which we can derive the fear of death; and yet the longest enjoyment which we can hope for of this kind is of so trivial a duration, that it is to a wise man truly contemptible. Few men, I own, think in this manner; for, indeed, few men think of death till they are in its jaws. However gigantic and terrible an object this may appear when it approaches them, they are nevertheless incapable of seeing it at any distance; nay, though they have been ever so much alarmed and frightened when they have apprehended themselves in danger of dying, they are no sooner cleared from this apprehension than even the fears of it are erased from their minds. But, alas! he who escapes from death is not pardoned; he is only reprieved, and reprieved to a short day.\n\n“Grieve, therefore, no more, my dear child, on this occasion: an event which may happen every hour; which every element, nay, almost every particle of matter that surrounds us is capable of producing, and which must and will most unavoidably reach us all at last, ought neither to occasion our surprize nor our lamentation.\n\n“My physician having acquainted me (which I take very kindly of him) that I am in danger of leaving you all very shortly, I have determined to say a few words to you at this our parting, before my distemper, which I find grows very fast upon me, puts it out of my power.\n\n“But I shall waste my strength too much. I intended to speak concerning my will, which, though I have settled long ago, I think proper to mention such heads of it as concern any of you, that I may have the comfort of perceiving you are all satisfied with the provision I have there made for you.\n\n“Nephew Blifil, I leave you the heir to my whole estate, except only £500 a-year, which is to revert to you after the death of your mother, and except one other estate of £500 a-year, and the sum of £6000, which I have bestowed in the following manner:\n\n“The estate of £500 a-year I have given to you, Mr Jones: and as I know the inconvenience which attends the want of ready money, I have added £1000 in specie. In this I know not whether I have exceeded or fallen short of your expectation. Perhaps you will think I have given you too little, and the world will be as ready to condemn me for giving you too much; but the latter censure I despise; and as to the former, unless you should entertain that common error which I have often heard in my life pleaded as an excuse for a total want of charity, namely, that instead of raising gratitude by voluntary acts of bounty, we are apt to raise demands, which of all others are the most boundless and most difficult to satisfy.--Pardon me the bare mention of this; I will not suspect any such thing.”\n\nJones flung himself at his benefactor's feet, and taking eagerly hold of his hand, assured him his goodness to him, both now and all other times, had so infinitely exceeded not only his merit but his hopes, that no words could express his sense of it. “And I assure you, sir,” said he, “your present generosity hath left me no other concern than for the present melancholy occasion. Oh, my friend, my father!” Here his words choaked him, and he turned away to hide a tear which was starting from his eyes.\n\nAllworthy then gently squeezed his hand, and proceeded thus: “I am convinced, my child, that you have much goodness, generosity, and honour, in your temper: if you will add prudence and religion to these, you must be happy; for the three former qualities, I admit, make you worthy of happiness, but they are the latter only which will put you in possession of it.\n\n“One thousand pound I have given to you, Mr Thwackum; a sum I am convinced which greatly exceeds your desires, as well as your wants. However, you will receive it as a memorial of my friendship; and whatever superfluities may redound to you, that piety which you so rigidly maintain will instruct you how to dispose of them.\n\n“A like sum, Mr Square, I have bequeathed to you. This, I hope, will enable you to pursue your profession with better success than hitherto. I have often observed with concern, that distress is more apt to excite contempt than commiseration, especially among men of business, with whom poverty is understood to indicate want of ability. But the little I have been able to leave you will extricate you from those difficulties with which you have formerly struggled; and then I doubt not but you will meet with sufficient prosperity to supply what a man of your philosophical temper will require.\n\n“I find myself growing faint, so I shall refer you to my will for my disposition of the residue. My servants will there find some tokens to remember me by; and there are a few charities which, I trust, my executors will see faithfully performed. Bless you all. I am setting out a little before you.”--\n\nHere a footman came hastily into the room, and said there was an attorney from Salisbury who had a particular message, which he said he must communicate to Mr Allworthy himself: that he seemed in a violent hurry, and protested he had so much business to do, that, if he could cut himself into four quarters, all would not be sufficient.\n\n“Go, child,” said Allworthy to Blifil, “see what the gentleman wants. I am not able to do any business now, nor can he have any with me, in which you are not at present more concerned than myself. Besides, I really am--I am incapable of seeing any one at present, or of any longer attention.” He then saluted them all, saying, perhaps he should be able to see them again, but he should be now glad to compose himself a little, finding that he had too much exhausted his spirits in discourse.\n\nSome of the company shed tears at their parting; and even the philosopher Square wiped his eyes, albeit unused to the melting mood. As to Mrs Wilkins, she dropt her pearls as fast as the Arabian trees their medicinal gums; for this was a ceremonial which that gentlewoman never omitted on a proper occasion.\n\nAfter this Mr Allworthy again laid himself down on his pillow, and endeavoured to compose himself to rest.\n\nContaining matter rather natural than pleasing.\n\nBesides grief for her master, there was another source for that briny stream which so plentifully rose above the two mountainous cheek-bones of the housekeeper. She was no sooner retired, than she began to mutter to herself in the following pleasant strain: “Sure master might have made some difference, methinks, between me and the other servants. I suppose he hath left me mourning; but, i'fackins! if that be all, the devil shall wear it for him, for me. I'd have his worship know I am no beggar. I have saved five hundred pound in his service, and after all to be used in this manner.--It is a fine encouragement to servants to be honest; and to be sure, if I have taken a little something now and then, others have taken ten times as much; and now we are all put in a lump together. If so be that it be so, the legacy may go to the devil with him that gave it. No, I won't give it up neither, because that will please some folks. No, I'll buy the gayest gown I can get, and dance over the old curmudgeon's grave in it. This is my reward for taking his part so often, when all the country have cried shame of him, for breeding up his bastard in that manner; but he is going now where he must pay for all. It would have become him better to have repented of his sins on his deathbed, than to glory in them, and give away his estate out of his own family to a misbegotten child. Found in his bed, forsooth! a pretty story! ay, ay, those that hide know where to find. Lord forgive him! I warrant he hath many more bastards to answer for, if the truth was known. One comfort is, they will all be known where he is a going now.--`The servants will find some token to remember me by.' Those were the very words; I shall never forget them, if I was to live a thousand years. Ay, ay, I shall remember you for huddling me among the servants. One would have thought he might have mentioned my name as well as that of Square; but he is a gentleman forsooth, though he had not cloths on his back when he came hither first. Marry come up with such gentlemen! though he hath lived here this many years, I don't believe there is arrow a servant in the house ever saw the colour of his money. The devil shall wait upon such a gentleman for me.” Much more of the like kind she muttered to herself; but this taste shall suffice to the reader.\n\nNeither Thwackum nor Square were much better satisfied with their legacies. Though they breathed not their resentment so loud, yet from the discontent which appeared in their countenances, as well as from the following dialogue, we collect that no great pleasure reigned in their minds.\n\nAbout an hour after they had left the sick-room, Square met Thwackum in the hall and accosted him thus: “Well, sir, have you heard any news of your friend since we parted from him?”--“If you mean Mr Allworthy,” answered Thwackum, “I think you might rather give him the appellation of your friend; for he seems to me to have deserved that title.”--“The title is as good on your side,” replied Square, “for his bounty, such as it is, hath been equal to both.”--“I should not have mentioned it first,” cries Thwackum, “but since you begin, I must inform you I am of a different opinion. There is a wide distinction between voluntary favours and rewards. The duty I have done in his family, and the care I have taken in the education of his two boys, are services for which some men might have expected a greater return. I would not have you imagine I am therefore dissatisfied; for St Paul hath taught me to be content with the little I have. Had the modicum been less, I should have known my duty. But though the Scriptures obliges me to remain contented, it doth not enjoin me to shut my eyes to my own merit, nor restrain me from seeing when I am injured by an unjust comparison.”--“Since you provoke me,” returned Square, “that injury is done to me; nor did I ever imagine Mr Allworthy had held my friendship so light, as to put me in balance with one who received his wages. I know to what it is owing; it proceeds from those narrow principles which you have been so long endeavouring to infuse into him, in contempt of everything which is great and noble. The beauty and loveliness of friendship is too strong for dim eyes, nor can it be perceived by any other medium than that unerring rule of right, which you have so often endeavoured to ridicule, that you have perverted your friend's understanding.”--“I wish,” cries Thwackum, in a rage, “I wish, for the sake of his soul, your damnable doctrines have not perverted his faith. It is to this I impute his present behaviour, so unbecoming a Christian. Who but an atheist could think of leaving the world without having first made up his account? without confessing his sins, and receiving that absolution which he knew he had one in the house duly authorized to give him? He will feel the want of these necessaries when it is too late, when he is arrived at that place where there is wailing and gnashing of teeth. It is then he will find in what mighty stead that heathen goddess, that virtue, which you and all other deists of the age adore, will stand him. He will then summon his priest, when there is none to be found, and will lament the want of that absolution, without which no sinner can be safe.”--“If it be so material,” says Square, “why don't you present it him of your own accord?” “It hath no virtue,” cries Thwackum, “but to those who have sufficient grace to require it. But why do I talk thus to a heathen and an unbeliever? It is you that taught him this lesson, for which you have been well rewarded in this world, as I doubt not your disciple will soon be in the other.”--“I know not what you mean by reward,” said Square; “but if you hint at that pitiful memorial of our friendship, which he hath thought fit to bequeath me, I despise it; and nothing but the unfortunate situation of my circumstances should prevail on me to accept it.”\n\nThe physician now arrived, and began to inquire of the two disputants, how we all did above-stairs? “In a miserable way,” answered Thwackum. “It is no more than I expected,” cries the doctor: “but pray what symptoms have appeared since I left you?”--“No good ones, I am afraid,” replied Thwackum: “after what past at our departure, I think there were little hopes.” The bodily physician, perhaps, misunderstood the curer of souls; and before they came to an explanation, Mr Blifil came to them with a most melancholy countenance, and acquainted them that he brought sad news, that his mother was dead at Salisbury; that she had been seized on the road home with the gout in her head and stomach, which had carried her off in a few hours. “Good-lack-a-day!” says the doctor. “One cannot answer for events; but I wish I had been at hand, to have been called in. The gout is a distemper which it is difficult to treat; yet I have been remarkably successful in it.” Thwackum and Square both condoled with Mr Blifil for the loss of his mother, which the one advised him to bear like a man, and the other like a Christian. The young gentleman said he knew very well we were all mortal, and he would endeavour to submit to his loss as well as he could. That he could not, however, help complaining a little against the peculiar severity of his fate, which brought the news of so great a calamity to him by surprize, and that at a time when he hourly expected the severest blow he was capable of feeling from the malice of fortune. He said, the present occasion would put to the test those excellent rudiments which he had learnt from Mr Thwackum and Mr Square; and it would be entirely owing to them, if he was enabled to survive such misfortunes.\n\nIt was now debated whether Mr Allworthy should be informed of the death of his sister. This the doctor violently opposed; in which, I believe, the whole college would agree with him: but Mr Blifil said, he had received such positive and repeated orders from his uncle, never to keep any secret from him for fear of the disquietude which it might give him, that he durst not think of disobedience, whatever might be the consequence. He said, for his part, considering the religious and philosophic temper of his uncle, he could not agree with the doctor in his apprehensions. He was therefore resolved to communicate it to him: for if his uncle recovered (as he heartily prayed he might) he knew he would never forgive an endeavour to keep a secret of this kind from him.\n\nThe physician was forced to submit to these resolutions, which the two other learned gentlemen very highly commended. So together moved Mr Blifil and the doctor toward the sick-room; where the physician first entered, and approached the bed, in order to feel his patient's pulse, which he had no sooner done, than he declared he was much better; that the last application had succeeded to a miracle, and had brought the fever to intermit: so that, he said, there appeared now to be as little danger as he had before apprehended there were hopes.\n\nTo say the truth, Mr Allworthy's situation had never been so bad as the great caution of the doctor had represented it: but as a wise general never despises his enemy, however inferior that enemy's force may be, so neither doth a wise physician ever despise a distemper, however inconsiderable. As the former preserves the same strict discipline, places the same guards, and employs the same scouts, though the enemy be never so weak; so the latter maintains the same gravity of countenance, and shakes his head with the same significant air, let the distemper be never so trifling. And both, among many other good ones, may assign this solid reason for their conduct, that by these means the greater glory redounds to them if they gain the victory, and the less disgrace if by any unlucky accident they should happen to be conquered.\n\nMr Allworthy had no sooner lifted up his eyes, and thanked Heaven for these hopes of his recovery, than Mr Blifil drew near, with a very dejected aspect, and having applied his handkerchief to his eye, either to wipe away his tears, or to do as Ovid somewhere expresses himself on another occasion\n\nSi nullus erit, tamen excute nullum,\n\nIf there be none, then wipe away that none,\n\nhe communicated to his uncle what the reader hath been just before acquainted with.\n\nAllworthy received the news with concern, with patience, and with resignation. He dropt a tender tear, then composed his countenance, and at last cried, “The Lord's will be done in everything.”\n\nHe now enquired for the messenger; but Blifil told him it had been impossible to detain him a moment; for he appeared by the great hurry he was in to have some business of importance on his hands; that he complained of being hurried and driven and torn out of his life, and repeated many times, that if he could divide himself into four quarters, he knew how to dispose of every one.\n\nAllworthy then desired Blifil to take care of the funeral. He said, he would have his sister deposited in his own chapel; and as to the particulars, he left them to his own discretion, only mentioning the person whom he would have employed on this occasion.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\nWhich, among other things, may serve as a comment on that saying of Aeschines, that “drunkenness shows the mind of a man, as a mirrour reflects his person.”\n\nThe reader may perhaps wonder at hearing nothing of Mr Jones in the last chapter. In fact, his behaviour was so different from that of the persons there mentioned, that we chose not to confound his name with theirs.\n\nWhen the good man had ended his speech, Jones was the last who deserted the room. Thence he retired to his own apartment, to give vent to his concern; but the restlessness of his mind would not suffer him to remain long there; he slipped softly therefore to Allworthy's chamber-door, where he listened a considerable time without hearing any kind of motion within, unless a violent snoring, which at last his fears misrepresented as groans. This so alarmed him, that he could not forbear entering the room; where he found the good man in the bed, in a sweet composed sleep, and his nurse snoring in the above mentioned hearty manner, at the bed's feet. He immediately took the only method of silencing this thorough bass, whose music he feared might disturb Mr Allworthy; and then sitting down by the nurse, he remained motionless till Blifil and the doctor came in together and waked the sick man, in order that the doctor might feel his pulse, and that the other might communicate to him that piece of news, which, had Jones been apprized of it, would have had great difficulty of finding its way to Mr Allworthy's ear at such a season.\n\nWhen he first heard Blifil tell his uncle this story, Jones could hardly contain the wrath which kindled in him at the other's indiscretion, especially as the doctor shook his head, and declared his unwillingness to have the matter mentioned to his patient. But as his passion did not so far deprive him of all use of his understanding, as to hide from him the consequences which any violent expression towards Blifil might have on the sick, this apprehension stilled his rage at the present; and he grew afterwards so satisfied with finding that this news had, in fact, produced no mischief, that he suffered his anger to die in his own bosom, without ever mentioning it to Blifil.\n\nThe physician dined that day at Mr Allworthy's; and having after dinner visited his patient, he returned to the company, and told them, that he had now the satisfaction to say, with assurance, that his patient was out of all danger: that he had brought his fever to a perfect intermission, and doubted not by throwing in the bark to prevent its return.\n\nThis account so pleased Jones, and threw him into such immoderate excess of rapture, that he might be truly said to be drunk with joy--an intoxication which greatly forwards the effects of wine; and as he was very free too with the bottle on this occasion (for he drank many bumpers to the doctor's health, as well as to other toasts) he became very soon literally drunk.\n\nJones had naturally violent animal spirits: these being set on float and augmented by the spirit of wine, produced most extravagant effects. He kissed the doctor, and embraced him with the most passionate endearments; swearing that next to Mr Allworthy himself, he loved him of all men living. “Doctor,” added he, “you deserve a statue to be erected to you at the public expense, for having preserved a man, who is not only the darling of all good men who know him, but a blessing to society, the glory of his country, and an honour to human nature. D--n me if I don't love him better than my own soul.”\n\n“More shame for you,” cries Thwackum. “Though I think you have reason to love him, for he hath provided very well for you. And perhaps it might have been better for some folks that he had not lived to see just reason of revoking his gift.”\n\nJones now looking on Thwackum with inconceivable disdain, answered, “And doth thy mean soul imagine that any such considerations could weigh with me? No, let the earth open and swallow her own dirt (if I had millions of acres I would say it) rather than swallow up my dear glorious friend.”\n\nQuis desiderio sit pudor aut modus Tam chari capitis?[*]\n\n[*] “What modesty or measure can set bounds to our desire of so dear a friend?” The word desiderium here cannot be easily translated. It includes our desire of enjoying our friend again, and the grief which attends that desire.\n\nThe doctor now interposed, and prevented the effects of a wrath which was kindling between Jones and Thwackum; after which the former gave a loose to mirth, sang two or three amorous songs, and fell into every frantic disorder which unbridled joy is apt to inspire; but so far was he from any disposition to quarrel, that he was ten times better humoured, if possible, than when he was sober.\n\nTo say truth, nothing is more erroneous than the common observation, that men who are ill-natured and quarrelsome when they are drunk, are very worthy persons when they are sober: for drink, in reality, doth not reverse nature, or create passions in men which did not exist in them before. It takes away the guard of reason, and consequently forces us to produce those symptoms, which many, when sober, have art enough to conceal. It heightens and inflames our passions (generally indeed that passion which is uppermost in our mind), so that the angry temper, the amorous, the generous, the good-humoured, the avaricious, and all other dispositions of men, are in their cups heightened and exposed.\n\nAnd yet as no nation produces so many drunken quarrels, especially among the lower people, as England (for indeed, with them, to drink and to fight together are almost synonymous terms), I would not, methinks, have it thence concluded, that the English are the worst-natured people alive. Perhaps the love of glory only is at the bottom of this; so that the fair conclusion seems to be, that our countrymen have more of that love, and more of bravery, than any other plebeians. And this the rather, as there is seldom anything ungenerous, unfair, or ill-natured, exercised on these occasions: nay, it is common for the combatants to express good-will for each other even at the time of the conflict; and as their drunken mirth generally ends in a battle, so do most of their battles end in friendship.\n\nBut to return to our history. Though Jones had shown no design of giving offence, yet Mr Blifil was highly offended at a behaviour which was so inconsistent with the sober and prudent reserve of his own temper. He bore it too with the greater impatience, as it appeared to him very indecent at this season; “When,” as he said, “the house was a house of mourning, on the account of his dear mother; and if it had pleased Heaven to give him some prospect of Mr Allworthy's recovery, it would become them better to express the exultations of their hearts in thanksgiving, than in drunkenness and riots; which were properer methods to encrease the Divine wrath, than to avert it.” Thwackum, who had swallowed more liquor than Jones, but without any ill effect on his brain, seconded the pious harangue of Blifil; but Square, for reasons which the reader may probably guess, was totally silent.\n\nWine had not so totally overpowered Jones, as to prevent his recollecting Mr Blifil's loss, the moment it was mentioned. As no person, therefore, was more ready to confess and condemn his own errors, he offered to shake Mr Blifil by the hand, and begged his pardon, saying, “His excessive joy for Mr Allworthy's recovery had driven every other thought out of his mind.”\n\nBlifil scornfully rejected his hand; and with much indignation answered, “It was little to be wondered at, if tragical spectacles made no impression on the blind; but, for his part, he had the misfortune to know who his parents were, and consequently must be affected with their loss.”\n\nJones, who, notwithstanding his good humour, had some mixture of the irascible in his constitution, leaped hastily from his chair, and catching hold of Blifil's collar, cried out, “D--n you for a rascal, do you insult me with the misfortune of my birth?” He accompanied these words with such rough actions, that they soon got the better of Mr Blifil's peaceful temper; and a scuffle immediately ensued, which might have produced mischief, had it not been prevented by the interposition of Thwackum and the physician; for the philosophy of Square rendered him superior to all emotions, and he very calmly smoaked his pipe, as was his custom in all broils, unless when he apprehended some danger of having it broke in his mouth.\n\nThe combatants being now prevented from executing present vengeance on each other, betook themselves to the common resources of disappointed rage, and vented their wrath in threats and defiance. In this kind of conflict, Fortune, which, in the personal attack, seemed to incline to Jones, was now altogether as favourable to his enemy.\n\nA truce, nevertheless, was at length agreed on, by the mediation of the neutral parties, and the whole company again sat down at the table; where Jones being prevailed on to ask pardon, and Blifil to give it, peace was restored, and everything seemed in statu quo.\n\nBut though the quarrel was, in all appearance, perfectly reconciled, the good humour which had been interrupted by it, was by no means restored. All merriment was now at an end, and the subsequent discourse consisted only of grave relations of matters of fact, and of as grave observations upon them; a species of conversation, in which, though there is much of dignity and instruction, there is but little entertainment. As we presume therefore to convey only this last to the reader, we shall pass by whatever was said, till the rest of the company having by degrees dropped off, left only Square and the physician together; at which time the conversation was a little heightened by some comments on what had happened between the two young gentlemen; both of whom the doctor declared to be no better than scoundrels; to which appellation the philosopher, very sagaciously shaking his head, agreed.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nShowing the truth of many observations of Ovid, and of other more grave writers, who have proved beyond contradiction, that wine is often the forerunner of incontinency.\n\nJones retired from the company, in which we have seen him engaged, into the fields, where he intended to cool himself by a walk in the open air before he attended Mr Allworthy. There, whilst he renewed those meditations on his dear Sophia, which the dangerous illness of his friend and benefactor had for some time interrupted, an accident happened, which with sorrow we relate, and with sorrow doubtless will it be read; however, that historic truth to which we profess so inviolable an attachment, obliges us to communicate it to posterity.\n\nIt was now a pleasant evening in the latter end of June, when our heroe was walking in a most delicious grove, where the gentle breezes fanning the leaves, together with the sweet trilling of a murmuring stream, and the melodious notes of nightingales, formed altogether the most enchanting harmony. In this scene, so sweetly accommodated to love, he meditated on his dear Sophia. While his wanton fancy roamed unbounded over all her beauties, and his lively imagination painted the charming maid in various ravishing forms, his warm heart melted with tenderness; and at length, throwing himself on the ground, by the side of a gently murmuring brook, he broke forth into the following ejaculation:\n\n“O Sophia, would Heaven give thee to my arms, how blest would be my condition! Curst be that fortune which sets a distance between us. Was I but possessed of thee, one only suit of rags thy whole estate, is there a man on earth whom I would envy! How contemptible would the brightest Circassian beauty, drest in all the jewels of the Indies, appear to my eyes! But why do I mention another woman? Could I think my eyes capable of looking at any other with tenderness, these hands should tear them from my head. No, my Sophia, if cruel fortune separates us for ever, my soul shall doat on thee alone. The chastest constancy will I ever preserve to thy image. Though I should never have possession of thy charming person, still shalt thou alone have possession of my thoughts, my love, my soul. Oh! my fond heart is so wrapt in that tender bosom, that the brightest beauties would for me have no charms, nor would a hermit be colder in their embraces. Sophia, Sophia alone shall be mine. What raptures are in that name! I will engrave it on every tree.”\n\nAt these words he started up, and beheld--not his Sophia--no, nor a Circassian maid richly and elegantly attired for the grand Signior's seraglio. No; without a gown, in a shift that was somewhat of the coarsest, and none of the cleanest, bedewed likewise with some odoriferous effluvia, the produce of the day's labour, with a pitchfork in her hand, Molly Seagrim approached. Our hero had his penknife in his hand, which he had drawn for the before-mentioned purpose of carving on the bark; when the girl coming near him, cryed out with a smile, “You don't intend to kill me, squire, I hope!”--“Why should you think I would kill you?” answered Jones. “Nay,” replied she, “after your cruel usage of me when I saw you last, killing me would, perhaps, be too great kindness for me to expect.”\n\nHere ensued a parley, which, as I do not think myself obliged to relate it, I shall omit. It is sufficient that it lasted a full quarter of an hour, at the conclusion of which they retired into the thickest part of the grove.\n\nSome of my readers may be inclined to think this event unnatural. However, the fact is true; and perhaps may be sufficiently accounted for by suggesting, that Jones probably thought one woman better than none, and Molly as probably imagined two men to be better than one. Besides the before-mentioned motive assigned to the present behaviour of Jones, the reader will be likewise pleased to recollect in his favour, that he was not at this time perfect master of that wonderful power of reason, which so well enables grave and wise men to subdue their unruly passions, and to decline any of these prohibited amusements. Wine now had totally subdued this power in Jones. He was, indeed, in a condition, in which, if reason had interposed, though only to advise, she might have received the answer which one Cleostratus gave many years ago to a silly fellow, who asked him, if he was not ashamed to be drunk? “Are not you,” said Cleostratus, “ashamed to admonish a drunken man?”--To say the truth, in a court of justice drunkenness must not be an excuse, yet in a court of conscience it is greatly so; and therefore Aristotle, who commends the laws of Pittacus, by which drunken men received double punishment for their crimes, allows there is more of policy than justice in that law. Now, if there are any transgressions pardonable from drunkenness, they are certainly such as Mr Jones was at present guilty of; on which head I could pour forth a vast profusion of learning, if I imagined it would either entertain my reader, or teach him anything more than he knows already. For his sake therefore I shall keep my learning to myself, and return to my history.\n\nIt hath been observed, that Fortune seldom doth things by halves. To say truth, there is no end to her freaks whenever she is disposed to gratify or displease. No sooner had our heroe retired with his Dido, but\n\nSpeluncam Blifil dux et divinus eandem Deveniunt--\n\nthe parson and the young squire, who were taking a serious walk, arrived at the stile which leads into the grove, and the latter caught a view of the lovers just as they were sinking out of sight.\n\nBlifil knew Jones very well, though he was at above a hundred yards' distance, and he was as positive to the sex of his companion, though not to the individual person. He started, blessed himself, and uttered a very solemn ejaculation.\n\nThwackum expressed some surprize at these sudden emotions, and asked the reason of them. To which Blifil answered, “He was certain he had seen a fellow and wench retire together among the bushes, which he doubted not was with some wicked purpose.” As to the name of Jones, he thought proper to conceal it, and why he did so must be left to the judgment of the sagacious reader; for we never chuse to assign motives to the actions of men, when there is any possibility of our being mistaken.\n\nThe parson, who was not only strictly chaste in his own person, but a great enemy to the opposite vice in all others, fired at this information. He desired Mr Blifil to conduct him immediately to the place, which as he approached he breathed forth vengeance mixed with lamentations; nor did he refrain from casting some oblique reflections on Mr Allworthy; insinuating that the wickedness of the country was principally owing to the encouragement he had given to vice, by having exerted such kindness to a bastard, and by having mitigated that just and wholesome rigour of the law which allots a very severe punishment to loose wenches.\n\nThe way through which our hunters were to pass in pursuit of their game was so beset with briars, that it greatly obstructed their walk, and caused besides such a rustling, that Jones had sufficient warning of their arrival before they could surprize him; nay, indeed, so incapable was Thwackum of concealing his indignation, and such vengeance did he mutter forth every step he took, that this alone must have abundantly satisfied Jones that he was (to use the language of sportsmen) found sitting.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\nIn which a simile in Mr Pope's period of a mile introduces as bloody a battle as can possibly be fought without the assistance of steel or cold iron.\n\nAs in the season of rutting (an uncouth phrase, by which the vulgar denote that gentle dalliance, which in the well-wooded[*] forest of Hampshire, passes between lovers of the ferine kind), if, while the lofty-crested stag meditates the amorous sport, a couple of puppies, or any other beasts of hostile note, should wander so near the temple of Venus Ferina that the fair hind should shrink from the place, touched with that somewhat, either of fear or frolic, of nicety or skittishness, with which nature hath bedecked all females, or hath at least instructed them how to put it on; lest, through the indelicacy of males, the Samean mysteries should be pryed into by unhallowed eyes: for, at the celebration of these rites, the female priestess cries out with her in Virgil (who was then, probably, hard at work on such celebration),\n\n--Procul, o procul este, profani; Proclamat vates, totoque absistite luco.\n\n--Far hence be souls profane, The sibyl cry'd, and from the grove abstain.--DRYDEN.\n\n[*] This is an ambiguous phrase, and may mean either a forest well cloathed with wood, or well stript of it.\n\nIf, I say, while these sacred rites, which are in common to genus omne animantium, are in agitation between the stag and his mistress, any hostile beasts should venture too near, on the first hint given by the frighted hind, fierce and tremendous rushes forth the stag to the entrance of the thicket; there stands he centinel over his love, stamps the ground with his foot, and with his horns brandished aloft in air, proudly provokes the apprehended foe to combat.\n\nThus, and more terrible, when he perceived the enemy's approach, leaped forth our heroe. Many a step advanced he forwards, in order to conceal the trembling hind, and, if possible, to secure her retreat. And now Thwackum, having first darted some livid lightning from his fiery eyes, began to thunder forth, “Fie upon it! Fie upon it! Mr Jones. Is it possible you should be the person?”--“You see,” answered Jones, “it is possible I should be here.”--“And who,” said Thwackum, “is that wicked slut with you?”--“If I have any wicked slut with me,” cries Jones, “it is possible I shall not let you know who she is.”--“I command you to tell me immediately,” says Thwackum: “and I would not have you imagine, young man, that your age, though it hath somewhat abridged the purpose of tuition, hath totally taken away the authority of the master. The relation of the master and scholar is indelible; as, indeed, all other relations are; for they all derive their original from heaven. I would have you think yourself, therefore, as much obliged to obey me now, as when I taught you your first rudiments.”--“I believe you would,” cries Jones; “but that will not happen, unless you had the same birchen argument to convince me.”--“Then I must tell you plainly,” said Thwackum, “I am resolved to discover the wicked wretch.”--“And I must tell you plainly,” returned Jones, “I am resolved you shall not.” Thwackum then offered to advance, and Jones laid hold of his arms; which Mr Blifil endeavoured to rescue, declaring, “he would not see his old master insulted.”\n\nJones now finding himself engaged with two, thought it necessary to rid himself of one of his antagonists as soon as possible. He therefore applied to the weakest first; and, letting the parson go, he directed a blow at the young squire's breast, which luckily taking place, reduced him to measure his length on the ground.\n\nThwackum was so intent on the discovery, that, the moment he found himself at liberty, he stept forward directly into the fern, without any great consideration of what might in the meantime befal his friend; but he had advanced a very few paces into the thicket, before Jones, having defeated Blifil, overtook the parson, and dragged him backward by the skirt of his coat.\n\nThis parson had been a champion in his youth, and had won much honour by his fist, both at school and at the university. He had now indeed, for a great number of years, declined the practice of that noble art; yet was his courage full as strong as his faith, and his body no less strong than either. He was moreover, as the reader may perhaps have conceived, somewhat irascible in his nature. When he looked back, therefore, and saw his friend stretched out on the ground, and found himself at the same time so roughly handled by one who had formerly been only passive in all conflicts between them (a circumstance which highly aggravated the whole), his patience at length gave way; he threw himself into a posture of offence; and collecting all his force, attacked Jones in the front with as much impetuosity as he had formerly attacked him in the rear.\n\nOur heroe received the enemy's attack with the most undaunted intrepidity, and his bosom resounded with the blow. This he presently returned with no less violence, aiming likewise at the parson's breast; but he dexterously drove down the fist of Jones, so that it reached only his belly, where two pounds of beef and as many of pudding were then deposited, and whence consequently no hollow sound could proceed. Many lusty blows, much more pleasant as well as easy to have seen, than to read or describe, were given on both sides: at last a violent fall, in which Jones had thrown his knees into Thwackum's breast, so weakened the latter, that victory had been no longer dubious, had not Blifil, who had now recovered his strength, again renewed the fight, and by engaging with Jones, given the parson a moment's time to shake his ears, and to regain his breath.\n\nAnd now both together attacked our heroe, whose blows did not retain that force with which they had fallen at first, so weakened was he by his combat with Thwackum; for though the pedagogue chose rather to play solos on the human instrument, and had been lately used to those only, yet he still retained enough of his antient knowledge to perform his part very well in a duet.\n\nThe victory, according to modern custom, was like to be decided by numbers, when, on a sudden, a fourth pair of fists appeared in the battle, and immediately paid their compliments to the parson; and the owner of them at the same time crying out, “Are not you ashamed, and be d--n'd to you, to fall two of you upon one?”\n\nThe battle, which was of the kind that for distinction's sake is called royal, now raged with the utmost violence during a few minutes; till Blifil being a second time laid sprawling by Jones, Thwackum condescended to apply for quarter to his new antagonist, who was now found to be Mr Western himself; for in the heat of the action none of the combatants had recognized him.\n\nIn fact, that honest squire, happening, in his afternoon's walk with some company, to pass through the field where the bloody battle was fought, and having concluded, from seeing three men engaged, that two of them must be on a side, he hastened from his companions, and with more gallantry than policy, espoused the cause of the weaker party. By which generous proceeding he very probably prevented Mr Jones from becoming a victim to the wrath of Thwackum, and to the pious friendship which Blifil bore his old master; for, besides the disadvantage of such odds, Jones had not yet sufficiently recovered the former strength of his broken arm. This reinforcement, however, soon put an end to the action, and Jones with his ally obtained the victory.\n\n# Chapter xii.\n\nIn which is seen a more moving spectacle than all the blood in the bodies of Thwackum and Blifil, and of twenty other such, is capable of producing.\n\nThe rest of Mr Western's company were now come up, being just at the instant when the action was over. These were the honest clergyman, whom we have formerly seen at Mr Western's table; Mrs Western, the aunt of Sophia; and lastly, the lovely Sophia herself.\n\nAt this time, the following was the aspect of the bloody field. In one place lay on the ground, all pale, and almost breathless, the vanquished Blifil. Near him stood the conqueror Jones, almost covered with blood, part of which was naturally his own, and part had been lately the property of the Reverend Mr Thwackum. In a third place stood the said Thwackum, like King Porus, sullenly submitting to the conqueror. The last figure in the piece was Western the Great, most gloriously forbearing the vanquished foe.\n\nBlifil, in whom there was little sign of life, was at first the principal object of the concern of every one, and particularly of Mrs Western, who had drawn from her pocket a bottle of hartshorn, and was herself about to apply it to his nostrils, when on a sudden the attention of the whole company was diverted from poor Blifil, whose spirit, if it had any such design, might have now taken an opportunity of stealing off to the other world, without any ceremony.\n\nFor now a more melancholy and a more lovely object lay motionless before them. This was no other than the charming Sophia herself, who, from the sight of blood, or from fear for her father, or from some other reason, had fallen down in a swoon, before any one could get to her assistance.\n\nMrs Western first saw her and screamed. Immediately two or three voices cried out, “Miss Western is dead.” Hartshorn, water, every remedy was called for, almost at one and the same instant.\n\nThe reader may remember, that in our description of this grove we mentioned a murmuring brook, which brook did not come there, as such gentle streams flow through vulgar romances, with no other purpose than to murmur. No! Fortune had decreed to ennoble this little brook with a higher honour than any of those which wash the plains of Arcadia ever deserved.\n\nJones was rubbing Blifil's temples, for he began to fear he had given him a blow too much, when the words, Miss Western and Dead, rushed at once on his ear. He started up, left Blifil to his fate, and flew to Sophia, whom, while all the rest were running against each other, backward and forward, looking for water in the dry paths, he caught up in his arms, and then ran away with her over the field to the rivulet above mentioned; where, plunging himself into the water, he contrived to besprinkle her face, head, and neck very plentifully.\n\nHappy was it for Sophia that the same confusion which prevented her other friends from serving her, prevented them likewise from obstructing Jones. He had carried her half ways before they knew what he was doing, and he had actually restored her to life before they reached the waterside. She stretched out her arms, opened her eyes, and cried, “Oh! heavens!” just as her father, aunt, and the parson came up.\n\nJones, who had hitherto held this lovely burthen in his arms, now relinquished his hold; but gave her at the same instant a tender caress, which, had her senses been then perfectly restored, could not have escaped her observation. As she expressed, therefore, no displeasure at this freedom, we suppose she was not sufficiently recovered from her swoon at the time.\n\nThis tragical scene was now converted into a sudden scene of joy. In this our heroe was certainly the principal character; for as he probably felt more ecstatic delight in having saved Sophia than she herself received from being saved, so neither were the congratulations paid to her equal to what were conferred on Jones, especially by Mr Western himself, who, after having once or twice embraced his daughter, fell to hugging and kissing Jones. He called him the preserver of Sophia, and declared there was nothing, except her, or his estate, which he would not give him; but upon recollection, he afterwards excepted his fox-hounds, the Chevalier, and Miss Slouch (for so he called his favourite mare).\n\nAll fears for Sophia being now removed, Jones became the object of the squire's consideration.--“Come, my lad,” says Western, “d'off thy quoat and wash thy feace; for att in a devilish pickle, I promise thee. Come, come, wash thyself, and shat go huome with me; and we'l zee to vind thee another quoat.”\n\nJones immediately complied, threw off his coat, went down to the water, and washed both his face and bosom; for the latter was as much exposed and as bloody as the former. But though the water could clear off the blood, it could not remove the black and blue marks which Thwackum had imprinted on both his face and breast, and which, being discerned by Sophia, drew from her a sigh and a look full of inexpressible tenderness.\n\nJones received this full in his eyes, and it had infinitely a stronger effect on him than all the contusions which he had received before. An effect, however, widely different; for so soft and balmy was it, that, had all his former blows been stabs, it would for some minutes have prevented his feeling their smart.\n\nThe company now moved backwards, and soon arrived where Thwackum had got Mr Blifil again on his legs. Here we cannot suppress a pious wish, that all quarrels were to be decided by those weapons only with which Nature, knowing what is proper for us, hath supplied us; and that cold iron was to be used in digging no bowels but those of the earth. Then would war, the pastime of monarchs, be almost inoffensive, and battles between great armies might be fought at the particular desire of several ladies of quality; who, together with the kings themselves, might be actual spectators of the conflict. Then might the field be this moment well strewed with human carcasses, and the next, the dead men, or infinitely the greatest part of them, might get up, like Mr Bayes's troops, and march off either at the sound of a drum or fiddle, as should be previously agreed on.\n\nI would avoid, if possible, treating this matter ludicrously, lest grave men and politicians, whom I know to be offended at a jest, may cry pish at it; but, in reality, might not a battle be as well decided by the greater number of broken heads, bloody noses, and black eyes, as by the greater heaps of mangled and murdered human bodies? Might not towns be contended for in the same manner? Indeed, this may be thought too detrimental a scheme to the French interest, since they would thus lose the advantage they have over other nations in the superiority of their engineers; but when I consider the gallantry and generosity of that people, I am persuaded they would never decline putting themselves upon a par with their adversary; or, as the phrase is, making themselves his match.\n\nBut such reformations are rather to be wished than hoped for: I shall content myself, therefore, with this short hint, and return to my narrative.\n\nWestern began now to inquire into the original rise of this quarrel. To which neither Blifil nor Jones gave any answer; but Thwackum said surlily, “I believe the cause is not far off; if you beat the bushes well you may find her.”--“Find her?” replied Western: “what! have you been fighting for a wench?”--“Ask the gentleman in his waistcoat there,” said Thwackum: “he best knows.” “Nay then,” cries Western, “it is a wench certainly.--Ah, Tom, Tom, thou art a liquorish dog. But come, gentlemen, be all friends, and go home with me, and make final peace over a bottle.” “I ask your pardon, sir,” says Thwackum: “it is no such slight matter for a man of my character to be thus injuriously treated, and buffeted by a boy, only because I would have done my duty, in endeavouring to detect and bring to justice a wanton harlot; but, indeed, the principal fault lies in Mr Allworthy and yourself; for if you put the laws in execution, as you ought to do, you will soon rid the country of these vermin.”\n\n“I would as soon rid the country of foxes,” cries Western. “I think we ought to encourage the recruiting those numbers which we are every day losing in the war.--But where is she? Prithee, Tom, show me.” He then began to beat about, in the same language and in the same manner as if he had been beating for a hare; and at last cried out, “Soho! Puss is not far off. Here's her form, upon my soul; I believe I may cry stole away.” And indeed so he might; for he had now discovered the place whence the poor girl had, at the beginning of the fray, stolen away, upon as many feet as a hare generally uses in travelling.\n\nSophia now desired her father to return home; saying she found herself very faint, and apprehended a relapse. The squire immediately complied with his daughter's request (for he was the fondest of parents). He earnestly endeavoured to prevail with the whole company to go and sup with him: but Blifil and Thwackum absolutely refused; the former saying, there were more reasons than he could then mention, why he must decline this honour; and the latter declaring (perhaps rightly) that it was not proper for a person of his function to be seen at any place in his present condition.\n\nJones was incapable of refusing the pleasure of being with his Sophia; so on he marched with Squire Western and his ladies, the parson bringing up the rear. This had, indeed, offered to tarry with his brother Thwackum, professing his regard for the cloth would not permit him to depart; but Thwackum would not accept the favour, and, with no great civility, pushed him after Mr Western.\n\nThus ended this bloody fray; and thus shall end the fifth book of this history.\n\nIn our last book we have been obliged to deal pretty much with the passion of love; and in our succeeding book shall be forced to handle this subject still more largely. It may not therefore in this place be improper to apply ourselves to the examination of that modern doctrine, by which certain philosophers, among many other wonderful discoveries, pretend to have found out, that there is no such passion in the human breast.\n\nWhether these philosophers be the same with that surprising sect, who are honourably mentioned by the late Dr Swift, as having, by the mere force of genius alone, without the least assistance of any kind of learning, or even reading, discovered that profound and invaluable secret that there is no God; or whether they are not rather the same with those who some years since very much alarmed the world, by showing that there were no such things as virtue or goodness really existing in human nature, and who deduced our best actions from pride, I will not here presume to determine. In reality, I am inclined to suspect, that all these several finders of truth, are the very identical men who are by others called the finders of gold. The method used in both these searches after truth and after gold, being indeed one and the same, viz., the searching, rummaging, and examining into a nasty place; indeed, in the former instances, into the nastiest of all places, A BAD MIND.\n\nBut though in this particular, and perhaps in their success, the truth-finder and the gold-finder may very properly be compared together; yet in modesty, surely, there can be no comparison between the two; for who ever heard of a gold-finder that had the impudence or folly to assert, from the ill success of his search, that there was no such thing as gold in the world? whereas the truth-finder, having raked out that jakes, his own mind, and being there capable of tracing no ray of divinity, nor anything virtuous or good, or lovely, or loving, very fairly, honestly, and logically concludes that no such things exist in the whole creation.\n\nTo avoid, however, all contention, if possible, with these philosophers, if they will be called so; and to show our own disposition to accommodate matters peaceably between us, we shall here make them some concessions, which may possibly put an end to the dispute.\n\nFirst, we will grant that many minds, and perhaps those of the philosophers, are entirely free from the least traces of such a passion.\n\nSecondly, that what is commonly called love, namely, the desire of satisfying a voracious appetite with a certain quantity of delicate white human flesh, is by no means that passion for which I here contend. This is indeed more properly hunger; and as no glutton is ashamed to apply the word love to his appetite, and to say he LOVES such and such dishes; so may the lover of this kind, with equal propriety, say, he HUNGERS after such and such women.\n\nThirdly, I will grant, which I believe will be a most acceptable concession, that this love for which I am an advocate, though it satisfies itself in a much more delicate manner, doth nevertheless seek its own satisfaction as much as the grossest of all our appetites.\n\nAnd, lastly, that this love, when it operates towards one of a different sex, is very apt, towards its complete gratification, to call in the aid of that hunger which I have mentioned above; and which it is so far from abating, that it heightens all its delights to a degree scarce imaginable by those who have never been susceptible of any other emotions than what have proceeded from appetite alone.\n\nIn return to all these concessions, I desire of the philosophers to grant, that there is in some (I believe in many) human breasts a kind and benevolent disposition, which is gratified by contributing to the happiness of others. That in this gratification alone, as in friendship, in parental and filial affection, as indeed in general philanthropy, there is a great and exquisite delight. That if we will not call such disposition love, we have no name for it. That though the pleasures arising from such pure love may be heightened and sweetened by the assistance of amorous desires, yet the former can subsist alone, nor are they destroyed by the intervention of the latter. Lastly, that esteem and gratitude are the proper motives to love, as youth and beauty are to desire, and, therefore, though such desire may naturally cease, when age or sickness overtakes its object; yet these can have no effect on love, nor ever shake or remove, from a good mind, that sensation or passion which hath gratitude and esteem for its basis.\n\nTo deny the existence of a passion of which we often see manifest instances, seems to be very strange and absurd; and can indeed proceed only from that self-admonition which we have mentioned above: but how unfair is this! Doth the man who recognizes in his own heart no traces of avarice or ambition, conclude, therefore, that there are no such passions in human nature? Why will we not modestly observe the same rule in judging of the good, as well as the evil of others? Or why, in any case, will we, as Shakespear phrases it, “put the world in our own person?”\n\nPredominant vanity is, I am afraid, too much concerned here. This is one instance of that adulation which we bestow on our own minds, and this almost universally. For there is scarce any man, how much soever he may despise the character of a flatterer, but will condescend in the meanest manner to flatter himself.\n\nTo those therefore I apply for the truth of the above observations, whose own minds can bear testimony to what I have advanced.\n\nExamine your heart, my good reader, and resolve whether you do believe these matters with me. If you do, you may now proceed to their exemplification in the following pages: if you do not, you have, I assure you, already read more than you have understood; and it would be wiser to pursue your business, or your pleasures (such as they are), than to throw away any more of your time in reading what you can neither taste nor comprehend. To treat of the effects of love to you, must be as absurd as to discourse on colours to a man born blind; since possibly your idea of love may be as absurd as that which we are told such blind man once entertained of the colour scarlet; that colour seemed to him to be very much like the sound of a trumpet: and love probably may, in your opinion, very greatly resemble a dish of soup, or a surloin of roast-beef.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nThe character of Mrs Western. Her great learning and knowledge of the world, and an instance of the deep penetration which she derived from those advantages.\n\nThe reader hath seen Mr Western, his sister, and daughter, with young Jones, and the parson, going together to Mr Western's house, where the greater part of the company spent the evening with much joy and festivity. Sophia was indeed the only grave person; for as to Jones, though love had now gotten entire possession of his heart, yet the pleasing reflection on Mr Allworthy's recovery, and the presence of his mistress, joined to some tender looks which she now and then could not refrain from giving him, so elevated our heroe, that he joined the mirth of the other three, who were perhaps as good-humoured people as any in the world.\n\nSophia retained the same gravity of countenance the next morning at breakfast; whence she retired likewise earlier than usual, leaving her father and aunt together. The squire took no notice of this change in his daughter's disposition. To say the truth, though he was somewhat of a politician, and had been twice a candidate in the country interest at an election, he was a man of no great observation. His sister was a lady of a different turn. She had lived about the court, and had seen the world. Hence she had acquired all that knowledge which the said world usually communicates; and was a perfect mistress of manners, customs, ceremonies, and fashions. Nor did her erudition stop here. She had considerably improved her mind by study; she had not only read all the modern plays, operas, oratorios, poems, and romances--in all which she was a critic; but had gone through Rapin's History of England, Eachard's Roman History, and many French Mémoires pour servir à l'Histoire: to these she had added most of the political pamphlets and journals published within the last twenty years. From which she had attained a very competent skill in politics, and could discourse very learnedly on the affairs of Europe. She was, moreover, excellently well skilled in the doctrine of amour, and knew better than anybody who and who were together; a knowledge which she the more easily attained, as her pursuit of it was never diverted by any affairs of her own; for either she had no inclinations, or they had never been solicited; which last is indeed very probable; for her masculine person, which was near six foot high, added to her manner and learning, possibly prevented the other sex from regarding her, notwithstanding her petticoats, in the light of a woman. However, as she had considered the matter scientifically, she perfectly well knew, though she had never practised them, all the arts which fine ladies use when they desire to give encouragement, or to conceal liking, with all the long appendage of smiles, ogles, glances, &c., as they are at present practised in the beau-monde. To sum the whole, no species of disguise or affectation had escaped her notice; but as to the plain simple workings of honest nature, as she had never seen any such, she could know but little of them.\n\nBy means of this wonderful sagacity, Mrs Western had now, as she thought, made a discovery of something in the mind of Sophia. The first hint of this she took from the behaviour of the young lady in the field of battle; and the suspicion which she then conceived, was greatly corroborated by some observations which she had made that evening and the next morning. However, being greatly cautious to avoid being found in a mistake, she carried the secret a whole fortnight in her bosom, giving only some oblique hints, by simpering, winks, nods, and now and then dropping an obscure word, which indeed sufficiently alarmed Sophia, but did not at all affect her brother.\n\nBeing at length, however, thoroughly satisfied of the truth of her observation, she took an opportunity, one morning, when she was alone with her brother, to interrupt one of his whistles in the following manner:--\n\n“Pray, brother, have you not observed something very extraordinary in my niece lately?”--“No, not I,” answered Western; “is anything the matter with the girl?”--“I think there is,” replied she; “and something of much consequence too.”--“Why, she doth not complain of anything,” cries Western; “and she hath had the small-pox.”--“Brother,” returned she, “girls are liable to other distempers besides the small-pox, and sometimes possibly to much worse.” Here Western interrupted her with much earnestness, and begged her, if anything ailed his daughter, to acquaint him immediately; adding, “she knew he loved her more than his own soul, and that he would send to the world's end for the best physician to her.” “Nay, nay,” answered she, smiling, “the distemper is not so terrible; but I believe, brother, you are convinced I know the world, and I promise you I was never more deceived in my life, if my niece be not most desperately in love.”--“How! in love!” cries Western, in a passion; “in love, without acquainting me! I'll disinherit her; I'll turn her out of doors, stark naked, without a farthing. Is all my kindness vor 'ur, and vondness o'ur come to this, to fall in love without asking me leave?”--“But you will not,” answered Mrs Western, “turn this daughter, whom you love better than your own soul, out of doors, before you know whether you shall approve her choice. Suppose she should have fixed on the very person whom you yourself would wish, I hope you would not be angry then?”--“No, no,” cries Western, “that would make a difference. If she marries the man I would ha' her, she may love whom she pleases, I shan't trouble my head about that.” “That is spoken,” answered the sister, “like a sensible man; but I believe the very person she hath chosen would be the very person you would choose for her. I will disclaim all knowledge of the world, if it is not so; and I believe, brother, you will allow I have some.”--“Why, lookee, sister,” said Western, “I do believe you have as much as any woman; and to be sure those are women's matters. You know I don't love to hear you talk about politics; they belong to us, and petticoats should not meddle: but come, who is the man?”--“Marry!” said she, “you may find him out yourself if you please. You, who are so great a politician, can be at no great loss. The judgment which can penetrate into the cabinets of princes, and discover the secret springs which move the great state wheels in all the political machines of Europe, must surely, with very little difficulty, find out what passes in the rude uninformed mind of a girl.”--“Sister,” cries the squire, “I have often warn'd you not to talk the court gibberish to me. I tell you, I don't understand the lingo: but I can read a journal, or the London Evening Post. Perhaps, indeed, there may be now and tan a verse which I can't make much of, because half the letters are left out; yet I know very well what is meant by that, and that our affairs don't go so well as they should do, because of bribery and corruption.”--“I pity your country ignorance from my heart,” cries the lady.--“Do you?” answered Western; “and I pity your town learning; I had rather be anything than a courtier, and a Presbyterian, and a Hanoverian too, as some people, I believe, are.”--“If you mean me,” answered she, “you know I am a woman, brother; and it signifies nothing what I am. Besides--“--“I do know you are a woman,” cries the squire, “and it's well for thee that art one; if hadst been a man, I promise thee I had lent thee a flick long ago.”--“Ay, there,” said she, “in that flick lies all your fancied superiority. Your bodies, and not your brains, are stronger than ours. Believe me, it is well for you that you are able to beat us; or, such is the superiority of our understanding, we should make all of you what the brave, and wise, and witty, and polite are already--our slaves.”--“I am glad I know your mind,” answered the squire. “But we'll talk more of this matter another time. At present, do tell me what man is it you mean about my daughter?”--“Hold a moment,” said she, “while I digest that sovereign contempt I have for your sex; or else I ought to be angry too with you. There--I have made a shift to gulp it down. And now, good politic sir, what think you of Mr Blifil? Did she not faint away on seeing him lie breathless on the ground? Did she not, after he was recovered, turn pale again the moment we came up to that part of the field where he stood? And pray what else should be the occasion of all her melancholy that night at supper, the next morning, and indeed ever since?”--“'Fore George!” cries the squire, “now you mind me on't, I remember it all. It is certainly so, and I am glad on't with all my heart. I knew Sophy was a good girl, and would not fall in love to make me angry. I was never more rejoiced in my life; for nothing can lie so handy together as our two estates. I had this matter in my head some time ago: for certainly the two estates are in a manner joined together in matrimony already, and it would be a thousand pities to part them. It is true, indeed, there be larger estates in the kingdom, but not in this county, and I had rather bate something, than marry my daughter among strangers and foreigners. Besides, most o' zuch great estates be in the hands of lords, and I heate the very name of themmun. Well but, sister, what would you advise me to do; for I tell you women know these matters better than we do?”--“Oh, your humble servant, sir,” answered the lady: “we are obliged to you for allowing us a capacity in anything. Since you are pleased, then, most politic sir, to ask my advice, I think you may propose the match to Allworthy yourself. There is no indecorum in the proposal's coming from the parent of either side. King Alcinous, in Mr Pope's Odyssey, offers his daughter to Ulysses. I need not caution so politic a person not to say that your daughter is in love; that would indeed be against all rules.”--“Well,” said the squire, “I will propose it; but I shall certainly lend un a flick, if he should refuse me.” “Fear not,” cries Mrs Western; “the match is too advantageous to be refused.” “I don't know that,” answered the squire: “Allworthy is a queer b--ch, and money hath no effect o'un.” “Brother,” said the lady, “your politics astonish me. Are you really to be imposed on by professions? Do you think Mr Allworthy hath more contempt for money than other men because he professes more? Such credulity would better become one of us weak women, than that wise sex which heaven hath formed for politicians. Indeed, brother, you would make a fine plenipo to negotiate with the French. They would soon persuade you, that they take towns out of mere defensive principles.” “Sister,” answered the squire, with much scorn, “let your friends at court answer for the towns taken; as you are a woman, I shall lay no blame upon you; for I suppose they are wiser than to trust women with secrets.” He accompanied this with so sarcastical a laugh, that Mrs Western could bear no longer. She had been all this time fretted in a tender part (for she was indeed very deeply skilled in these matters, and very violent in them), and therefore, burst forth in a rage, declared her brother to be both a clown and a blockhead, and that she would stay no longer in his house.\n\nThe squire, though perhaps he had never read Machiavel, was, however, in many points, a perfect politician. He strongly held all those wise tenets, which are so well inculcated in that Politico-Peripatetic school of Exchange-alley. He knew the just value and only use of money, viz., to lay it up. He was likewise well skilled in the exact value of reversions, expectations, &c., and had often considered the amount of his sister's fortune, and the chance which he or his posterity had of inheriting it. This he was infinitely too wise to sacrifice to a trifling resentment. When he found, therefore, he had carried matters too far, he began to think of reconciling them; which was no very difficult task, as the lady had great affection for her brother, and still greater for her niece; and though too susceptible of an affront offered to her skill in politics, on which she much valued herself, was a woman of a very extraordinary good and sweet disposition.\n\nHaving first, therefore, laid violent hands on the horses, for whose escape from the stable no place but the window was left open, he next applied himself to his sister; softened and soothed her, by unsaying all he had said, and by assertions directly contrary to those which had incensed her. Lastly, he summoned the eloquence of Sophia to his assistance, who, besides a most graceful and winning address, had the advantage of being heard with great favour and partiality by her aunt.\n\nThe result of the whole was a kind smile from Mrs Western, who said, “Brother, you are absolutely a perfect Croat; but as those have their use in the army of the empress queen, so you likewise have some good in you. I will therefore once more sign a treaty of peace with you, and see that you do not infringe it on your side; at least, as you are so excellent a politician, I may expect you will keep your leagues, like the French, till your interest calls upon you to break them.”\n\nContaining two defiances to the critics.\n\nThe squire having settled matters with his sister, as we have seen in the last chapter, was so greatly impatient to communicate the proposal to Allworthy, that Mrs Western had the utmost difficulty to prevent him from visiting that gentleman in his sickness, for this purpose.\n\nMr Allworthy had been engaged to dine with Mr Western at the time when he was taken ill. He was therefore no sooner discharged out of the custody of physic, but he thought (as was usual with him on all occasions, both the highest and the lowest) of fulfilling his engagement.\n\nIn the interval between the time of the dialogue in the last chapter, and this day of public entertainment, Sophia had, from certain obscure hints thrown out by her aunt, collected some apprehension that the sagacious lady suspected her passion for Jones. She now resolved to take this opportunity of wiping out all such suspicion, and for that purpose to put an entire constraint on her behaviour.\n\nFirst, she endeavoured to conceal a throbbing melancholy heart with the utmost sprightliness in her countenance, and the highest gaiety in her manner. Secondly, she addressed her whole discourse to Mr Blifil, and took not the least notice of poor Jones the whole day.\n\nThe squire was so delighted with this conduct of his daughter, that he scarce eat any dinner, and spent almost his whole time in watching opportunities of conveying signs of his approbation by winks and nods to his sister; who was not at first altogether so pleased with what she saw as was her brother.\n\nIn short, Sophia so greatly overacted her part, that her aunt was at first staggered, and began to suspect some affectation in her niece; but as she was herself a woman of great art, so she soon attributed this to extreme art in Sophia. She remembered the many hints she had given her niece concerning her being in love, and imagined the young lady had taken this way to rally her out of her opinion, by an overacted civility: a notion that was greatly corroborated by the excessive gaiety with which the whole was accompanied. We cannot here avoid remarking, that this conjecture would have been better founded had Sophia lived ten years in the air of Grosvenor Square, where young ladies do learn a wonderful knack of rallying and playing with that passion, which is a mighty serious thing in woods and groves an hundred miles distant from London.\n\nTo say the truth, in discovering the deceit of others, it matters much that our own art be wound up, if I may use the expression, in the same key with theirs: for very artful men sometimes miscarry by fancying others wiser, or, in other words, greater knaves, than they really are. As this observation is pretty deep, I will illustrate it by the following short story. Three countrymen were pursuing a Wiltshire thief through Brentford. The simplest of them seeing “The Wiltshire House,” written under a sign, advised his companions to enter it, for there most probably they would find their countryman. The second, who was wiser, laughed at this simplicity; but the third, who was wiser still, answered, “Let us go in, however, for he may think we should not suspect him of going amongst his own countrymen.” They accordingly went in and searched the house, and by that means missed overtaking the thief, who was at that time but a little way before them; and who, as they all knew, but had never once reflected, could not read.\n\nThe reader will pardon a digression in which so invaluable a secret is communicated, since every gamester will agree how necessary it is to know exactly the play of another, in order to countermine him. This will, moreover, afford a reason why the wiser man, as is often seen, is the bubble of the weaker, and why many simple and innocent characters are so generally misunderstood and misrepresented; but what is most material, this will account for the deceit which Sophia put on her politic aunt.\n\nDinner being ended, and the company retired into the garden, Mr Western, who was thoroughly convinced of the certainty of what his sister had told him, took Mr Allworthy aside, and very bluntly proposed a match between Sophia and young Mr Blifil.\n\nMr Allworthy was not one of those men whose hearts flutter at any unexpected and sudden tidings of worldly profit. His mind was, indeed, tempered with that philosophy which becomes a man and a Christian. He affected no absolute superiority to all pleasure and pain, to all joy and grief; but was not at the same time to be discomposed and ruffled by every accidental blast, by every smile or frown of fortune. He received, therefore, Mr Western's proposal without any visible emotion, or without any alteration of countenance. He said the alliance was such as he sincerely wished; then launched forth into a very just encomium on the young lady's merit; acknowledged the offer to be advantageous in point of fortune; and after thanking Mr Western for the good opinion he had professed of his nephew, concluded, that if the young people liked each other, he should be very desirous to complete the affair.\n\nWestern was a little disappointed at Mr Allworthy's answer, which was not so warm as he expected. He treated the doubt whether the young people might like one another with great contempt, saying, “That parents were the best judges of proper matches for their children: that for his part he should insist on the most resigned obedience from his daughter: and if any young fellow could refuse such a bed-fellow, he was his humble servant, and hoped there was no harm done.”\n\nAllworthy endeavoured to soften this resentment by many eulogiums on Sophia, declaring he had no doubt but that Mr Blifil would very gladly receive the offer; but all was ineffectual; he could obtain no other answer from the squire but--“I say no more--I humbly hope there's no harm done--that's all.” Which words he repeated at least a hundred times before they parted.\n\nAllworthy was too well acquainted with his neighbour to be offended at this behaviour; and though he was so averse to the rigour which some parents exercise on their children in the article of marriage, that he had resolved never to force his nephew's inclinations, he was nevertheless much pleased with the prospect of this union; for the whole country resounded the praises of Sophia, and he had himself greatly admired the uncommon endowments of both her mind and person.\n\nTo which I believe we may add, the consideration of her vast fortune, which, though he was too sober to be intoxicated with it, he was too sensible to despise.\n\nAnd here, in defiance of all the barking critics in the world, I must and will introduce a digression concerning true wisdom, of which Mr Allworthy was in reality as great a pattern as he was of goodness.\n\nTrue wisdom then, notwithstanding all which Mr Hogarth's poor poet may have writ against riches, and in spite of all which any rich well-fed divine may have preached against pleasure, consists not in the contempt of either of these. A man may have as much wisdom in the possession of an affluent fortune, as any beggar in the streets; or may enjoy a handsome wife or a hearty friend, and still remain as wise as any sour popish recluse, who buries all his social faculties, and starves his belly while he well lashes his back.\n\nTo say truth, the wisest man is the likeliest to possess all worldly blessings in an eminent degree; for as that moderation which wisdom prescribes is the surest way to useful wealth, so can it alone qualify us to taste many pleasures. The wise man gratifies every appetite and every passion, while the fool sacrifices all the rest to pall and satiate one.\n\nIt may be objected, that very wise men have been notoriously avaricious. I answer, Not wise in that instance. It may likewise be said, That the wisest men have been in their youth immoderately fond of pleasure. I answer, They were not wise then.\n\nWisdom, in short, whose lessons have been represented as so hard to learn by those who never were at her school, only teaches us to extend a simple maxim universally known and followed even in the lowest life, a little farther than that life carries it. And this is, not to buy at too dear a price.\n\nNow, whoever takes this maxim abroad with him into the grand market of the world, and constantly applies it to honours, to riches, to pleasures, and to every other commodity which that market affords, is, I will venture to affirm, a wise man, and must be so acknowledged in the worldly sense of the word; for he makes the best of bargains, since in reality he purchases everything at the price only of a little trouble, and carries home all the good things I have mentioned, while he keeps his health, his innocence, and his reputation, the common prices which are paid for them by others, entire and to himself.\n\nFrom this moderation, likewise, he learns two other lessons, which complete his character. First, never to be intoxicated when he hath made the best bargain, nor dejected when the market is empty, or when its commodities are too dear for his purchase.\n\nBut I must remember on what subject I am writing, and not trespass too far on the patience of a good-natured critic. Here, therefore, I put an end to the chapter.\n\nContaining sundry curious matters.\n\nAs soon as Mr Allworthy returned home, he took Mr Blifil apart, and after some preface, communicated to him the proposal which had been made by Mr Western, and at the same time informed him how agreeable this match would be to himself.\n\nThe charms of Sophia had not made the least impression on Blifil; not that his heart was pre-engaged; neither was he totally insensible of beauty, or had any aversion to women; but his appetites were by nature so moderate, that he was able, by philosophy, or by study, or by some other method, easily to subdue them: and as to that passion which we have treated of in the first chapter of this book, he had not the least tincture of it in his whole composition.\n\nBut though he was so entirely free from that mixed passion, of which we there treated, and of which the virtues and beauty of Sophia formed so notable an object; yet was he altogether as well furnished with some other passions, that promised themselves very full gratification in the young lady's fortune. Such were avarice and ambition, which divided the dominion of his mind between them. He had more than once considered the possession of this fortune as a very desirable thing, and had entertained some distant views concerning it; but his own youth, and that of the young lady, and indeed principally a reflection that Mr Western might marry again, and have more children, had restrained him from too hasty or eager a pursuit.\n\nThis last and most material objection was now in great measure removed, as the proposal came from Mr Western himself. Blifil, therefore, after a very short hesitation, answered Mr Allworthy, that matrimony was a subject on which he had not yet thought; but that he was so sensible of his friendly and fatherly care, that he should in all things submit himself to his pleasure.\n\nAllworthy was naturally a man of spirit, and his present gravity arose from true wisdom and philosophy, not from any original phlegm in his disposition; for he had possessed much fire in his youth, and had married a beautiful woman for love. He was not therefore greatly pleased with this cold answer of his nephew; nor could he help launching forth into the praises of Sophia, and expressing some wonder that the heart of a young man could be impregnable to the force of such charms, unless it was guarded by some prior affection.\n\nBlifil assured him he had no such guard; and then proceeded to discourse so wisely and religiously on love and marriage, that he would have stopt the mouth of a parent much less devoutly inclined than was his uncle. In the end, the good man was satisfied that his nephew, far from having any objections to Sophia, had that esteem for her, which in sober and virtuous minds is the sure foundation of friendship and love. And as he doubted not but the lover would, in a little time, become altogether as agreeable to his mistress, he foresaw great happiness arising to all parties by so proper and desirable an union. With Mr Blifil's consent therefore he wrote the next morning to Mr Western, acquainting him that his nephew had very thankfully and gladly received the proposal, and would be ready to wait on the young lady, whenever she should be pleased to accept his visit.\n\nWestern was much pleased with this letter, and immediately returned an answer; in which, without having mentioned a word to his daughter, he appointed that very afternoon for opening the scene of courtship.\n\nAs soon as he had dispatched this messenger, he went in quest of his sister, whom he found reading and expounding the Gazette to parson Supple. To this exposition he was obliged to attend near a quarter of an hour, though with great violence to his natural impetuosity, before he was suffered to speak. At length, however, he found an opportunity of acquainting the lady, that he had business of great consequence to impart to her; to which she answered, “Brother, I am entirely at your service. Things look so well in the north, that I was never in a better humour.”\n\nThe parson then withdrawing, Western acquainted her with all which had passed, and desired her to communicate the affair to Sophia, which she readily and chearfully undertook; though perhaps her brother was a little obliged to that agreeable northern aspect which had so delighted her, that he heard no comment on his proceedings; for they were certainly somewhat too hasty and violent.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nIn which is related what passed between Sophia and her aunt.\nSophia was in her chamber, reading, when her aunt came in. The moment she saw Mrs Western, she shut the book with so much eagerness, that the good lady could not forbear asking her, What book that was which she seemed so much afraid of showing? “Upon my word, madam,” answered Sophia, “it is a book which I am neither ashamed nor afraid to own I have read. It is the production of a young lady of fashion, whose good understanding, I think, doth honour to her sex, and whose good heart is an honour to human nature.” Mrs Western then took up the book, and immediately after threw it down, saying--“Yes, the author is of a very good family; but she is not much among people one knows. I have never read it; for the best judges say, there is not much in it.”--“I dare not, madam, set up my own opinion,” says Sophia, “against the best judges, but there appears to me a great deal of human nature in it; and in many parts so much true tenderness and delicacy, that it hath cost me many a tear.”--“Ay, and do you love to cry then?” says the aunt. “I love a tender sensation,” answered the niece, “and would pay the price of a tear for it at any time.”--“Well, but show me,” said the aunt, “what was you reading when I came in; there was something very tender in that, I believe, and very loving too. You blush, my dear Sophia. Ah! child, you should read books which would teach you a little hypocrisy, which would instruct you how to hide your thoughts a little better.”--“I hope, madam,” answered Sophia, “I have no thoughts which I ought to be ashamed of discovering.”--“Ashamed! no,” cries the aunt, “I don't think you have any thoughts which you ought to be ashamed of; and yet, child, you blushed just now when I mentioned the word loving. Dear Sophy, be assured you have not one thought which I am not well acquainted with; as well, child, as the French are with our motions, long before we put them in execution. Did you think, child, because you have been able to impose upon your father, that you could impose upon me? Do you imagine I did not know the reason of your overacting all that friendship for Mr Blifil yesterday? I have seen a little too much of the world, to be so deceived. Nay, nay, do not blush again. I tell you it is a passion you need not be ashamed of. It is a passion I myself approve, and have already brought your father into the approbation of it. Indeed, I solely consider your inclination; for I would always have that gratified, if possible, though one may sacrifice higher prospects. Come, I have news which will delight your very soul. Make me your confident, and I will undertake you shall be happy to the very extent of your wishes.” “La, madam,” says Sophia, looking more foolishly than ever she did in her life, “I know not what to say--why, madam, should you suspect?”--“Nay, no dishonesty,” returned Mrs Western. “Consider, you are speaking to one of your own sex, to an aunt, and I hope you are convinced you speak to a friend. Consider, you are only revealing to me what I know already, and what I plainly saw yesterday, through that most artful of all disguises, which you had put on, and which must have deceived any one who had not perfectly known the world. Lastly, consider it is a passion which I highly approve.” “La, madam,” says Sophia, “you come upon one so unawares, and on a sudden. To be sure, madam, I am not blind--and certainly, if it be a fault to see all human perfections assembled together--but is it possible my father and you, madam, can see with my eyes?” “I tell you,” answered the aunt, “we do entirely approve; and this very afternoon your father hath appointed for you to receive your lover.” “My father, this afternoon!” cries Sophia, with the blood starting from her face.--“Yes, child,” said the aunt, “this afternoon. You know the impetuosity of my brother's temper. I acquainted him with the passion which I first discovered in you that evening when you fainted away in the field. I saw it in your fainting. I saw it immediately upon your recovery. I saw it that evening at supper, and the next morning at breakfast (you know, child, I have seen the world). Well, I no sooner acquainted my brother, but he immediately wanted to propose it to Allworthy. He proposed it yesterday, Allworthy consented (as to be sure he must with joy), and this afternoon, I tell you, you are to put on all your best airs.” “This afternoon!” cries Sophia. “Dear aunt, you frighten me out of my senses.” “O, my dear,” said the aunt, “you will soon come to yourself again; for he is a charming young fellow, that's the truth on't.” “Nay, I will own,” says Sophia, “I know none with such perfections. So brave, and yet so gentle; so witty, yet so inoffensive; so humane, so civil, so genteel, so handsome! What signifies his being base born, when compared with such qualifications as these?” “Base born? What do you mean?” said the aunt, “Mr Blifil base born!” Sophia turned instantly pale at this name, and faintly repeated it. Upon which the aunt cried, “Mr Blifil--ay, Mr Blifil, of whom else have we been talking?” “Good heavens,” answered Sophia, ready to sink, “of Mr Jones, I thought; I am sure I know no other who deserves--” “I protest,” cries the aunt, “you frighten me in your turn. Is it Mr Jones, and not Mr Blifil, who is the object of your affection?” “Mr Blifil!” repeated Sophia. “Sure it is impossible you can be in earnest; if you are, I am the most miserable woman alive.” Mrs Western now stood a few moments silent, while sparks of fiery rage flashed from her eyes. At length, collecting all her force of voice, she thundered forth in the following articulate sounds:\n\n“And is it possible you can think of disgracing your family by allying yourself to a bastard? Can the blood of the Westerns submit to such contamination? If you have not sense sufficient to restrain such monstrous inclinations, I thought the pride of our family would have prevented you from giving the least encouragement to so base an affection; much less did I imagine you would ever have had the assurance to own it to my face.”\n“Madam,” answered Sophia, trembling, “what I have said you have extorted from me. I do not remember to have ever mentioned the name of Mr Jones with approbation to any one before; nor should I now had I not conceived he had your approbation. Whatever were my thoughts of that poor, unhappy young man, I intended to have carried them with me to my grave--to that grave where only now, I find, I am to seek repose.” Here she sunk down in her chair, drowned in her tears, and, in all the moving silence of unutterable grief, presented a spectacle which must have affected almost the hardest heart.\n\nAll this tender sorrow, however, raised no compassion in her aunt. On the contrary, she now fell into the most violent rage.--“And I would rather,” she cried, in a most vehement voice, “follow you to your grave, than I would see you disgrace yourself and your family by such a match. O Heavens! could I have ever suspected that I should live to hear a niece of mine declare a passion for such a fellow? You are the first--yes, Miss Western, you are the first of your name who ever entertained so grovelling a thought. A family so noted for the prudence of its women”--here she ran on a full quarter of an hour, till, having exhausted her breath rather than her rage, she concluded with threatening to go immediately and acquaint her brother.\n\nSophia then threw herself at her feet, and laying hold of her hands, begged her with tears to conceal what she had drawn from her; urging the violence of her father's temper, and protesting that no inclinations of hers should ever prevail with her to do anything which might offend him.\nMrs Western stood a moment looking at her, and then, having recollected herself, said, “That on one consideration only she would keep the secret from her brother; and this was, that Sophia should promise to entertain Mr Blifil that very afternoon as her lover, and to regard him as the person who was to be her husband.”\n\nPoor Sophia was too much in her aunt's power to deny her anything positively; she was obliged to promise that she would see Mr Blifil, and be as civil to him as possible; but begged her aunt that the match might not be hurried on. She said, “Mr Blifil was by no means agreeable to her, and she hoped her father would be prevailed on not to make her the most wretched of women.”\n\nMrs Western assured her, “That the match was entirely agreed upon, and that nothing could or should prevent it. I must own,” said she, “I looked on it as on a matter of indifference; nay, perhaps, had some scruples about it before, which were actually got over by my thinking it highly agreeable to your own inclinations; but now I regard it as the most eligible thing in the world: nor shall there be, if I can prevent it, a moment of time lost on the occasion.”\n\nSophia replied, “Delay at least, madam, I may expect from both your goodness and my father's. Surely you will give me time to endeavour to get the better of so strong a disinclination as I have at present to this person.”\n\nThe aunt answered, “She knew too much of the world to be so deceived; that as she was sensible another man had her affections, she should persuade Mr Western to hasten the match as much as possible. It would be bad politics, indeed,” added she, “to protract a siege when the enemy's army is at hand, and in danger of relieving it. No, no, Sophy,” said she, “as I am convinced you have a violent passion which you can never satisfy with honour, I will do all I can to put your honour out of the care of your family: for when you are married those matters will belong only to the consideration of your husband. I hope, child, you will always have prudence enough to act as becomes you; but if you should not, marriage hath saved many a woman from ruin.”\n\nSophia well understood what her aunt meant; but did not think proper to make her an answer. However, she took a resolution to see Mr Blifil, and to behave to him as civilly as she could, for on that condition only she obtained a promise from her aunt to keep secret the liking which her ill fortune, rather than any scheme of Mrs Western, had unhappily drawn from her.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nContaining a dialogue between Sophia and Mrs Honour, which may a little relieve those tender affections which the foregoing scene may have raised in the mind of a good-natured reader.\n\nMrs Western having obtained that promise from her niece which we have seen in the last chapter, withdrew; and presently after arrived Mrs Honour. She was at work in a neighbouring apartment, and had been summoned to the keyhole by some vociferation in the preceding dialogue, where she had continued during the remaining part of it. At her entry into the room, she found Sophia standing motionless, with the tears trickling from her eyes. Upon which she immediately ordered a proper quantity of tears into her own eyes, and then began, “O Gemini, my dear lady, what is the matter?”--“Nothing,” cries Sophia. “Nothing! O dear Madam!” answers Honour, “you must not tell me that, when your ladyship is in this taking, and when there hath been such a preamble between your ladyship and Madam Western.”--“Don't teaze me,” cries Sophia; “I tell you nothing is the matter. Good heavens! why was I born?”--“Nay, madam,” says Mrs Honour, “you shall never persuade me that your la'ship can lament yourself so for nothing. To be sure I am but a servant; but to be sure I have been always faithful to your la'ship, and to be sure I would serve your la'ship with my life.”--“My dear Honour,” says Sophia, “'tis not in thy power to be of any service to me. I am irretrievably undone.”--“Heaven forbid!” answered the waiting-woman; “but if I can't be of any service to you, pray tell me, madam--it will be some comfort to me to know--pray, dear ma'am, tell me what's the matter.”--“My father,” cries Sophia, “is going to marry me to a man I both despise and hate.”--“O dear, ma'am,” answered the other, “who is this wicked man? for to be sure he is very bad, or your la'ship would not despise him.”--“His name is poison to my tongue,” replied Sophia: “thou wilt know it too soon.” Indeed, to confess the truth, she knew it already, and therefore was not very inquisitive as to that point. She then proceeded thus: “I don't pretend to give your la'ship advice, whereof your la'ship knows much better than I can pretend to, being but a servant; but, i-fackins! no father in England should marry me against my consent. And, to be sure, the 'squire is so good, that if he did but know your la'ship despises and hates the young man, to be sure he would not desire you to marry him. And if your la'ship would but give me leave to tell my master so. To be sure, it would be more properer to come from your own mouth; but as your la'ship doth not care to foul your tongue with his nasty name--“--“You are mistaken, Honour,” says Sophia; “my father was determined before he ever thought fit to mention it to me.”--“More shame for him,” cries Honour: “you are to go to bed to him, and not master: and thof a man may be a very proper man, yet every woman mayn't think him handsome alike. I am sure my master would never act in this manner of his own head. I wish some people would trouble themselves only with what belongs to them; they would not, I believe, like to be served so, if it was their own case; for though I am a maid, I can easily believe as how all men are not equally agreeable. And what signifies your la'ship having so great a fortune, if you can't please yourself with the man you think most handsomest? Well, I say nothing; but to be sure it is a pity some folks had not been better born; nay, as for that matter, I should not mind it myself; but then there is not so much money; and what of that? your la'ship hath money enough for both; and where can your la'ship bestow your fortune better? for to be sure every one must allow that he is the most handsomest, charmingest, finest, tallest, properest man in the world.”--“What do you mean by running on in this manner to me?” cries Sophia, with a very grave countenance. “Have I ever given any encouragement for these liberties?”--“Nay, ma'am, I ask pardon; I meant no harm,” answered she; “but to be sure the poor gentleman hath run in my head ever since I saw him this morning. To be sure, if your la'ship had but seen him just now, you must have pitied him. Poor gentleman! I wishes some misfortune hath not happened to him; for he hath been walking about with his arms across, and looking so melancholy, all this morning: I vow and protest it made me almost cry to see him.”--“To see whom?” says Sophia. “Poor Mr Jones,” answered Honour. “See him! why, where did you see him?” cries Sophia. “By the canal, ma'am,” says Honour. “There he hath been walking all this morning, and at last there he laid himself down: I believe he lies there still. To be sure, if it had not been for my modesty, being a maid, as I am, I should have gone and spoke to him. Do, ma'am, let me go and see, only for a fancy, whether he is there still.”--“Pugh!” says Sophia. “There! no, no: what should he do there? He is gone before this time, to be sure. Besides, why--what--why should you go to see? besides, I want you for something else. Go, fetch me my hat and gloves. I shall walk with my aunt in the grove before dinner.” Honour did immediately as she was bid, and Sophia put her hat on; when, looking in the glass, she fancied the ribbon with which her hat was tied did not become her, and so sent her maid back again for a ribbon of a different colour; and then giving Mrs Honour repeated charges not to leave her work on any account, as she said it was in violent haste, and must be finished that very day, she muttered something more about going to the grove, and then sallied out the contrary way, and walked, as fast as her tender trembling limbs could carry her, directly towards the canal.\n\nJones had been there as Mrs Honour had told her; he had indeed spent two hours there that morning in melancholy contemplation on his Sophia, and had gone out from the garden at one door the moment she entered it at another. So that those unlucky minutes which had been spent in changing the ribbons, had prevented the lovers from meeting at this time;--a most unfortunate accident, from which my fair readers will not fail to draw a very wholesome lesson. And here I strictly forbid all male critics to intermeddle with a circumstance which I have recounted only for the sake of the ladies, and upon which they only are at liberty to comment.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nA picture of formal courtship in miniature, as it always ought to be drawn, and a scene of a tenderer kind painted at full length.\n\nIt was well remarked by one (and perhaps by more), that misfortunes do not come single. This wise maxim was now verified by Sophia, who was not only disappointed of seeing the man she loved, but had the vexation of being obliged to dress herself out, in order to receive a visit from the man she hated.\n\nThat afternoon Mr Western, for the first time, acquainted his daughter with his intention; telling her, he knew very well that she had heard it before from her aunt. Sophia looked very grave upon this, nor could she prevent a few pearls from stealing into her eyes. “Come, come,” says Western, “none of your maidenish airs; I know all; I assure you sister hath told me all.”\n\n“Is it possible,” says Sophia, “that my aunt can have betrayed me already?”--“Ay, ay,” says Western; “betrayed you! ay. Why, you betrayed yourself yesterday at dinner. You showed your fancy very plainly, I think. But you young girls never know what you would be at. So you cry because I am going to marry you to the man you are in love with! Your mother, I remember, whimpered and whined just in the same manner; but it was all over within twenty-four hours after we were married: Mr Blifil is a brisk young man, and will soon put an end to your squeamishness. Come, chear up, chear up; I expect un every minute.”\n\nSophia was now convinced that her aunt had behaved honourably to her: and she determined to go through that disagreeable afternoon with as much resolution as possible, and without giving the least suspicion in the world to her father.\n\nMr Blifil soon arrived; and Mr Western soon after withdrawing, left the young couple together.\n\nHere a long silence of near a quarter of an hour ensued; for the gentleman who was to begin the conversation had all the unbecoming modesty which consists in bashfulness. He often attempted to speak, and as often suppressed his words just at the very point of utterance. At last out they broke in a torrent of far-fetched and high-strained compliments, which were answered on her side by downcast looks, half bows, and civil monosyllables. Blifil, from his inexperience in the ways of women, and from his conceit of himself, took this behaviour for a modest assent to his courtship; and when, to shorten a scene which she could no longer support, Sophia rose up and left the room, he imputed that, too, merely to bashfulness, and comforted himself that he should soon have enough of her company.\n\nHe was indeed perfectly well satisfied with his prospect of success; for as to that entire and absolute possession of the heart of his mistress which romantic lovers require, the very idea of it never entered his head. Her fortune and her person were the sole objects of his wishes, of which he made no doubt soon to obtain the absolute property; as Mr Western's mind was so earnestly bent on the match; and as he well knew the strict obedience which Sophia was always ready to pay to her father's will, and the greater still which her father would exact, if there was occasion. This authority, therefore, together with the charms which he fancied in his own person and conversation, could not fail, he thought, of succeeding with a young lady, whose inclinations were, he doubted not, entirely disengaged.\n\nOf Jones he certainly had not even the least jealousy; and I have often thought it wonderful that he had not. Perhaps he imagined the character which Jones bore all over the country (how justly, let the reader determine), of being one of the wildest fellows in England, might render him odious to a lady of the most exemplary modesty. Perhaps his suspicions might be laid asleep by the behaviour of Sophia, and of Jones himself, when they were all in company together. Lastly, and indeed principally, he was well assured there was not another self in the case. He fancied that he knew Jones to the bottom, and had in reality a great contempt for his understanding, for not being more attached to his own interest. He had no apprehension that Jones was in love with Sophia; and as for any lucrative motives, he imagined they would sway very little with so silly a fellow. Blifil, moreover, thought the affair of Molly Seagrim still went on, and indeed believed it would end in marriage; for Jones really loved him from his childhood, and had kept no secret from him, till his behaviour on the sickness of Mr Allworthy had entirely alienated his heart; and it was by means of the quarrel which had ensued on this occasion, and which was not yet reconciled, that Mr Blifil knew nothing of the alteration which had happened in the affection which Jones had formerly borne towards Molly.\n\nFrom these reasons, therefore, Mr Blifil saw no bar to his success with Sophia. He concluded her behaviour was like that of all other young ladies on a first visit from a lover, and it had indeed entirely answered his expectations.\n\nMr Western took care to way-lay the lover at his exit from his mistress. He found him so elevated with his success, so enamoured with his daughter, and so satisfied with her reception of him, that the old gentleman began to caper and dance about his hall, and by many other antic actions to express the extravagance of his joy; for he had not the least command over any of his passions; and that which had at any time the ascendant in his mind hurried him to the wildest excesses.\n\nAs soon as Blifil was departed, which was not till after many hearty kisses and embraces bestowed on him by Western, the good squire went instantly in quest of his daughter, whom he no sooner found than he poured forth the most extravagant raptures, bidding her chuse what clothes and jewels she pleased; and declaring that he had no other use for fortune but to make her happy. He then caressed her again and again with the utmost profusion of fondness, called her by the most endearing names, and protested she was his only joy on earth.\n\nSophia perceiving her father in this fit of affection, which she did not absolutely know the reason of (for fits of fondness were not unusual to him, though this was rather more violent than ordinary), thought she should never have a better opportunity of disclosing herself than at present, as far at least as regarded Mr Blifil; and she too well foresaw the necessity which she should soon be under of coming to a full explanation. After having thanked the squire, therefore, for all his professions of kindness, she added, with a look full of inexpressible softness, “And is it possible my papa can be so good to place all his joy in his Sophy's happiness?” which Western having confirmed by a great oath, and a kiss; she then laid hold of his hand, and, falling on her knees, after many warm and passionate declarations of affection and duty, she begged him “not to make her the most miserable creature on earth by forcing her to marry a man whom she detested. This I entreat of you, dear sir,” said she, “for your sake, as well as my own, since you are so very kind to tell me your happiness depends on mine.”--“How! what!” says Western, staring wildly. “Oh! sir,” continued she, “not only your poor Sophy's happiness; her very life, her being, depends upon your granting her request. I cannot live with Mr Blifil. To force me into this marriage would be killing me.”--“You can't live with Mr Blifil?” says Western. “No, upon my soul I can't,” answered Sophia. “Then die and be d--d,” cries he, spurning her from him. “Oh! sir,” cries Sophia, catching hold of the skirt of his coat, “take pity on me, I beseech you. Don't look and say such cruel--Can you be unmoved while you see your Sophy in this dreadful condition? Can the best of fathers break my heart? Will he kill me by the most painful, cruel, lingering death?”--“Pooh! pooh!” cries the squire; “all stuff and nonsense; all maidenish tricks. Kill you, indeed! Will marriage kill you?”--“Oh! sir,” answered Sophia, “such a marriage is worse than death. He is not even indifferent; I hate and detest him.”--“If you detest un never so much,” cries Western, “you shall ha'un.” This he bound by an oath too shocking to repeat; and after many violent asseverations, concluded in these words: “I am resolved upon the match, and unless you consent to it I will not give you a groat, not a single farthing; no, though I saw you expiring with famine in the street, I would not relieve you with a morsel of bread. This is my fixed resolution, and so I leave you to consider on it.” He then broke from her with such violence, that her face dashed against the floor; and he burst directly out of the room, leaving poor Sophia prostrate on the ground.\n\nWhen Western came into the hall, he there found Jones; who seeing his friend looking wild, pale, and almost breathless, could not forbear enquiring the reason of all these melancholy appearances. Upon which the squire immediately acquainted him with the whole matter, concluding with bitter denunciations against Sophia, and very pathetic lamentations of the misery of all fathers who are so unfortunate to have daughters.\n\nJones, to whom all the resolutions which had been taken in favour of Blifil were yet a secret, was at first almost struck dead with this relation; but recovering his spirits a little, mere despair, as he afterwards said, inspired him to mention a matter to Mr Western, which seemed to require more impudence than a human forehead was ever gifted with. He desired leave to go to Sophia, that he might endeavour to obtain her concurrence with her father's inclinations.\n\nIf the squire had been as quicksighted as he was remarkable for the contrary, passion might at present very well have blinded him. He thanked Jones for offering to undertake the office, and said, “Go, go, prithee, try what canst do;” and then swore many execrable oaths that he would turn her out of doors unless she consented to the match.\n\nThe meeting between Jones and Sophia.\n\nJones departed instantly in quest of Sophia, whom he found just risen from the ground, where her father had left her, with the tears trickling from her eyes, and the blood running from her lips. He presently ran to her, and with a voice full at once of tenderness and terrour, cried, “O my Sophia, what means this dreadful sight?” She looked softly at him for a moment before she spoke, and then said, “Mr Jones, for Heaven's sake how came you here?--Leave me, I beseech you, this moment.”--“Do not,” says he, “impose so harsh a command upon me--my heart bleeds faster than those lips. O Sophia, how easily could I drain my veins to preserve one drop of that dear blood.”--“I have too many obligations to you already,” answered she, “for sure you meant them such.” Here she looked at him tenderly almost a minute, and then bursting into an agony, cried, “Oh, Mr Jones, why did you save my life? my death would have been happier for us both.”--“Happier for us both!” cried he. “Could racks or wheels kill me so painfully as Sophia's--I cannot bear the dreadful sound. Do I live but for her?” Both his voice and looks were full of inexpressible tenderness when he spoke these words; and at the same time he laid gently hold on her hand, which she did not withdraw from him; to say the truth, she hardly knew what she did or suffered. A few moments now passed in silence between these lovers, while his eyes were eagerly fixed on Sophia, and hers declining towards the ground: at last she recovered strength enough to desire him again to leave her, for that her certain ruin would be the consequence of their being found together; adding, “Oh, Mr Jones, you know not, you know not what hath passed this cruel afternoon.”--“I know all, my Sophia,” answered he; “your cruel father hath told me all, and he himself hath sent me hither to you.”--“My father sent you to me!” replied she: “sure you dream.”--“Would to Heaven,” cries he, “it was but a dream! Oh, Sophia, your father hath sent me to you, to be an advocate for my odious rival, to solicit you in his favour. I took any means to get access to you. O speak to me, Sophia! comfort my bleeding heart. Sure no one ever loved, ever doated like me. Do not unkindly withhold this dear, this soft, this gentle hand--one moment, perhaps, tears you for ever from me--nothing less than this cruel occasion could, I believe, have ever conquered the respect and awe with which you have inspired me.” She stood a moment silent, and covered with confusion; then lifting up her eyes gently towards him, she cried, “What would Mr Jones have me say?”--“O do but promise,” cries he, “that you never will give yourself to Blifil.”--“Name not,” answered she, “the detested sound. Be assured I never will give him what is in my power to withhold from him.”--“Now then,” cries he, “while you are so perfectly kind, go a little farther, and add that I may hope.”--“Alas!” says she, “Mr Jones, whither will you drive me? What hope have I to bestow? You know my father's intentions.”--“But I know,” answered he, “your compliance with them cannot be compelled.”--“What,” says she, “must be the dreadful consequence of my disobedience? My own ruin is my least concern. I cannot bear the thoughts of being the cause of my father's misery.”--“He is himself the cause,” cries Jones, “by exacting a power over you which Nature hath not given him. Think on the misery which I am to suffer if I am to lose you, and see on which side pity will turn the balance.”--“Think of it!” replied she: “can you imagine I do not feel the ruin which I must bring on you, should I comply with your desire? It is that thought which gives me resolution to bid you fly from me for ever, and avoid your own destruction.”--“I fear no destruction,” cries he, “but the loss of Sophia. If you would save me from the most bitter agonies, recall that cruel sentence. Indeed, I can never part with you, indeed I cannot.”\n\nThe lovers now stood both silent and trembling, Sophia being unable to withdraw her hand from Jones, and he almost as unable to hold it; when the scene, which I believe some of my readers will think had lasted long enough, was interrupted by one of so different a nature, that we shall reserve the relation of it for a different chapter.\n\nBeing of a much more tempestuous kind than the former.\n\nBefore we proceed with what now happened to our lovers, it may be proper to recount what had past in the hall during their tender interview.\n\nSoon after Jones had left Mr Western in the manner above mentioned, his sister came to him, and was presently informed of all that had passed between her brother and Sophia relating to Blifil.\n\nThis behaviour in her niece the good lady construed to be an absolute breach of the condition on which she had engaged to keep her love for Mr Jones a secret. She considered herself, therefore, at full liberty to reveal all she knew to the squire, which she immediately did in the most explicit terms, and without any ceremony or preface.\n\nThe idea of a marriage between Jones and his daughter, had never once entered into the squire's head, either in the warmest minutes of his affection towards that young man, or from suspicion, or on any other occasion. He did indeed consider a parity of fortune and circumstances to be physically as necessary an ingredient in marriage, as difference of sexes, or any other essential; and had no more apprehension of his daughter's falling in love with a poor man, than with any animal of a different species.\n\nHe became, therefore, like one thunderstruck at his sister's relation. He was, at first, incapable of making any answer, having been almost deprived of his breath by the violence of the surprize. This, however, soon returned, and, as is usual in other cases after an intermission, with redoubled force and fury.\n\nThe first use he made of the power of speech, after his recovery from the sudden effects of his astonishment, was to discharge a round volley of oaths and imprecations. After which he proceeded hastily to the apartment where he expected to find the lovers, and murmured, or rather indeed roared forth, intentions of revenge every step he went.\n\nAs when two doves, or two wood-pigeons, or as when Strephon and Phyllis (for that comes nearest to the mark) are retired into some pleasant solitary grove, to enjoy the delightful conversation of Love, that bashful boy, who cannot speak in public, and is never a good companion to more than two at a time; here, while every object is serene, should hoarse thunder burst suddenly through the shattered clouds, and rumbling roll along the sky, the frightened maid starts from the mossy bank or verdant turf, the pale livery of death succeeds the red regimentals in which Love had before drest her cheeks, fear shakes her whole frame, and her lover scarce supports her trembling tottering limbs.\n\nOr as when two gentlemen, strangers to the wondrous wit of the place, are cracking a bottle together at some inn or tavern at Salisbury, if the great Dowdy, who acts the part of a madman as well as some of his setters-on do that of a fool, should rattle his chains, and dreadfully hum forth the grumbling catch along the gallery; the frighted strangers stand aghast; scared at the horrid sound, they seek some place of shelter from the approaching danger; and if the well-barred windows did admit their exit, would venture their necks to escape the threatening fury now coming upon them.\n\nSo trembled poor Sophia, so turned she pale at the noise of her father, who, in a voice most dreadful to hear, came on swearing, cursing, and vowing the destruction of Jones. To say the truth, I believe the youth himself would, from some prudent considerations, have preferred another place of abode at this time, had his terror on Sophia's account given him liberty to reflect a moment on what any otherways concerned himself, than as his love made him partake whatever affected her.\n\nAnd now the squire, having burst open the door, beheld an object which instantly suspended all his fury against Jones; this was the ghastly appearance of Sophia, who had fainted away in her lover's arms. This tragical sight Mr Western no sooner beheld, than all his rage forsook him; he roared for help with his utmost violence; ran first to his daughter, then back to the door calling for water, and then back again to Sophia, never considering in whose arms she then was, nor perhaps once recollecting that there was such a person in the world as Jones; for indeed I believe the present circumstances of his daughter were now the sole consideration which employed his thoughts.\n\nMrs Western and a great number of servants soon came to the assistance of Sophia with water, cordials, and everything necessary on those occasions. These were applied with such success, that Sophia in a very few minutes began to recover, and all the symptoms of life to return. Upon which she was presently led off by her own maid and Mrs Western: nor did that good lady depart without leaving some wholesome admonitions with her brother, on the dreadful effects of his passion, or, as she pleased to call it, madness.\n\nThe squire, perhaps, did not understand this good advice, as it was delivered in obscure hints, shrugs, and notes of admiration: at least, if he did understand it, he profited very little by it; for no sooner was he cured of his immediate fears for his daughter, than he relapsed into his former frenzy, which must have produced an immediate battle with Jones, had not parson Supple, who was a very strong man, been present, and by mere force restrained the squire from acts of hostility.\n\nThe moment Sophia was departed, Jones advanced in a very suppliant manner to Mr Western, whom the parson held in his arms, and begged him to be pacified; for that, while he continued in such a passion, it would be impossible to give him any satisfaction.\n\n“I wull have satisfaction o' thee,” answered the squire; “so doff thy clothes. At unt half a man, and I'll lick thee as well as wast ever licked in thy life.” He then bespattered the youth with abundance of that language which passes between country gentlemen who embrace opposite sides of the question; with frequent applications to him to salute that part which is generally introduced into all controversies that arise among the lower orders of the English gentry at horse-races, cock-matches, and other public places. Allusions to this part are likewise often made for the sake of the jest. And here, I believe, the wit is generally misunderstood. In reality, it lies in desiring another to kiss your a-- for having just before threatened to kick his; for I have observed very accurately, that no one ever desires you to kick that which belongs to himself, nor offers to kiss this part in another.\n\nIt may likewise seem surprizing that in the many thousand kind invitations of this sort, which every one who hath conversed with country gentlemen must have heard, no one, I believe, hath ever seen a single instance where the desire hath been complied with;--a great instance of their want of politeness; for in town nothing can be more common than for the finest gentlemen to perform this ceremony every day to their superiors, without having that favour once requested of them.\n\nTo all such wit, Jones very calmly answered, “Sir, this usage may perhaps cancel every other obligation you have conferred on me; but there is one you can never cancel; nor will I be provoked by your abuse to lift my hand against the father of Sophia.”\n\nAt these words the squire grew still more outrageous than before; so that the parson begged Jones to retire; saying, “You behold, sir, how he waxeth wrath at your abode here; therefore let me pray you not to tarry any longer. His anger is too much kindled for you to commune with him at present. You had better, therefore, conclude your visit, and refer what matters you have to urge in your behalf to some other opportunity.”\n\nJones accepted this advice with thanks, and immediately departed. The squire now regained the liberty of his hands, and so much temper as to express some satisfaction in the restraint which had been laid upon him; declaring that he should certainly have beat his brains out; and adding, “It would have vexed one confoundedly to have been hanged for such a rascal.”\n\nThe parson now began to triumph in the success of his peace-making endeavours, and proceeded to read a lecture against anger, which might perhaps rather have tended to raise than to quiet that passion in some hasty minds. This lecture he enriched with many valuable quotations from the antients, particularly from Seneca; who hath indeed so well handled this passion, that none but a very angry man can read him without great pleasure and profit. The doctor concluded this harangue with the famous story of Alexander and Clitus; but as I find that entered in my common-place under title Drunkenness, I shall not insert it here.\n\nThe squire took no notice of this story, nor perhaps of anything he said; for he interrupted him before he had finished, by calling for a tankard of beer; observing (which is perhaps as true as any observation on this fever of the mind) that anger makes a man dry.\n\nNo sooner had the squire swallowed a large draught than he renewed the discourse on Jones, and declared a resolution of going the next morning early to acquaint Mr Allworthy. His friend would have dissuaded him from this, from the mere motive of good-nature; but his dissuasion had no other effect than to produce a large volley of oaths and curses, which greatly shocked the pious ears of Supple; but he did not dare to remonstrate against a privilege which the squire claimed as a freeborn Englishman. To say truth, the parson submitted to please his palate at the squire's table, at the expense of suffering now and then this violence to his ears. He contented himself with thinking he did not promote this evil practice, and that the squire would not swear an oath the less, if he never entered within his gates. However, though he was not guilty of ill manners by rebuking a gentleman in his own house, he paid him off obliquely in the pulpit: which had not, indeed, the good effect of working a reformation in the squire himself; yet it so far operated on his conscience, that he put the laws very severely in execution against others, and the magistrate was the only person in the parish who could swear with impunity.\n\nIn which Mr Western visits Mr Allworthy.\nMr Allworthy was now retired from breakfast with his nephew, well satisfied with the report of the young gentleman's successful visit to Sophia (for he greatly desired the match, more on account of the young lady's character than of her riches), when Mr Western broke abruptly in upon them, and without any ceremony began as follows:--\n\n“There, you have done a fine piece of work truly! You have brought up your bastard to a fine purpose; not that I believe you have had any hand in it neither, that is, as a man may say, designedly: but there is a fine kettle-of-fish made on't up at our house.” “What can be the matter, Mr Western?” said Allworthy. “O, matter enow of all conscience: my daughter hath fallen in love with your bastard, that's all; but I won't ge her a hapeny, not the twentieth part of a brass varden. I always thought what would come o' breeding up a bastard like a gentleman, and letting un come about to vok's houses. It's well vor un I could not get at un: I'd a lick'd un; I'd a spoil'd his caterwauling; I'd a taught the son of a whore to meddle with meat for his master. He shan't ever have a morsel of meat of mine, or a varden to buy it: if she will ha un, one smock shall be her portion. I'd sooner ge my esteate to the zinking fund, that it may be sent to Hanover to corrupt our nation with.” “I am heartily sorry,” cries Allworthy. “Pox o' your sorrow,” says Western; “it will do me abundance of good when I have lost my only child, my poor Sophy, that was the joy of my heart, and all the hope and comfort of my age; but I am resolved I will turn her out o' doors; she shall beg, and starve, and rot in the streets. Not one hapeny, not a hapeny shall she ever hae o' mine. The son of a bitch was always good at finding a hare sitting, an be rotted to'n: I little thought what puss he was looking after; but it shall be the worst he ever vound in his life. She shall be no better than carrion: the skin o'er is all he shall ha, and zu you may tell un.” “I am in amazement,” cries Allworthy, “at what you tell me, after what passed between my nephew and the young lady no longer ago than yesterday.” “Yes, sir,” answered Western, “it was after what passed between your nephew and she that the whole matter came out. Mr Blifil there was no sooner gone than the son of a whore came lurching about the house. Little did I think when I used to love him for a sportsman that he was all the while a poaching after my daughter.” “Why truly,” says Allworthy, “I could wish you had not given him so many opportunities with her; and you will do me the justice to acknowledge that I have always been averse to his staying so much at your house, though I own I had no suspicion of this kind.” “Why, zounds,” cries Western, “who could have thought it? What the devil had she to do wi'n? He did not come there a courting to her; he came there a hunting with me.” “But was it possible,” says Allworthy, “that you should never discern any symptoms of love between them, when you have seen them so often together?” “Never in my life, as I hope to be saved,” cries Western: “I never so much as zeed him kiss her in all my life; and so far from courting her, he used rather to be more silent when she was in company than at any other time; and as for the girl, she was always less civil to'n than to any young man that came to the house. As to that matter, I am not more easy to be deceived than another; I would not have you think I am, neighbour.” Allworthy could scarce refrain laughter at this; but he resolved to do a violence to himself; for he perfectly well knew mankind, and had too much good-breeding and good-nature to offend the squire in his present circumstances. He then asked Western what he would have him do upon this occasion. To which the other answered, “That he would have him keep the rascal away from his house, and that he would go and lock up the wench; for he was resolved to make her marry Mr Blifil in spite of her teeth.” He then shook Blifil by the hand, and swore he would have no other son-in-law. Presently after which he took his leave; saying his house was in such disorder that it was necessary for him to make haste home, to take care his daughter did not give him the slip; and as for Jones, he swore if he caught him at his house, he would qualify him to run for the geldings' plate.\n\nWhen Allworthy and Blifil were again left together, a long silence ensued between them; all which interval the young gentleman filled up with sighs, which proceeded partly from disappointment, but more from hatred; for the success of Jones was much more grievous to him than the loss of Sophia.\n\nAt length his uncle asked him what he was determined to do, and he answered in the following words:--“Alas! sir, can it be a question what step a lover will take, when reason and passion point different ways? I am afraid it is too certain he will, in that dilemma, always follow the latter. Reason dictates to me, to quit all thoughts of a woman who places her affections on another; my passion bids me hope she may in time change her inclinations in my favour. Here, however, I conceive an objection may be raised, which, if it could not fully be answered, would totally deter me from any further pursuit. I mean the injustice of endeavouring to supplant another in a heart of which he seems already in possession; but the determined resolution of Mr Western shows that, in this case, I shall, by so doing, promote the happiness of every party; not only that of the parent, who will thus be preserved from the highest degree of misery, but of both the others, who must be undone by this match. The lady, I am sure, will be undone in every sense; for, besides the loss of most part of her own fortune, she will be not only married to a beggar, but the little fortune which her father cannot withhold from her will be squandered on that wench with whom I know he yet converses. Nay, that is a trifle; for I know him to be one of the worst men in the world; for had my dear uncle known what I have hitherto endeavoured to conceal, he must have long since abandoned so profligate a wretch.” “How!” said Allworthy; “hath he done anything worse than I already know? Tell me, I beseech you?” “No,” replied Blifil; “it is now past, and perhaps he may have repented of it.” “I command you, on your duty,” said Allworthy, “to tell me what you mean.” “You know, sir,” says Blifil, “I never disobeyed you; but I am sorry I mentioned it, since it may now look like revenge, whereas, I thank Heaven, no such motive ever entered my heart; and if you oblige me to discover it, I must be his petitioner to you for your forgiveness.” “I will have no conditions,” answered Allworthy; “I think I have shown tenderness enough towards him, and more perhaps than you ought to thank me for.” “More, indeed, I fear, than he deserved,” cries Blifil; “for in the very day of your utmost danger, when myself and all the family were in tears, he filled the house with riot and debauchery. He drank, and sung, and roared; and when I gave him a gentle hint of the indecency of his actions, he fell into a violent passion, swore many oaths, called me rascal, and struck me.” “How!” cries Allworthy; “did he dare to strike you?” “I am sure,” cries Blifil, “I have forgiven him that long ago. I wish I could so easily forget his ingratitude to the best of benefactors; and yet even that I hope you will forgive him, since he must have certainly been possessed with the devil: for that very evening, as Mr Thwackum and myself were taking the air in the fields, and exulting in the good symptoms which then first began to discover themselves, we unluckily saw him engaged with a wench in a manner not fit to be mentioned. Mr Thwackum, with more boldness than prudence, advanced to rebuke him, when (I am sorry to say it) he fell upon the worthy man, and beat him so outrageously that I wish he may have yet recovered the bruises. Nor was I without my share of the effects of his malice, while I endeavoured to protect my tutor; but that I have long forgiven; nay, I prevailed with Mr Thwackum to forgive him too, and not to inform you of a secret which I feared might be fatal to him. And now, sir, since I have unadvisedly dropped a hint of this matter, and your commands have obliged me to discover the whole, let me intercede with you for him.” “O child!” said Allworthy, “I know not whether I should blame or applaud your goodness, in concealing such villany a moment: but where is Mr Thwackum? Not that I want any confirmation of what you say; but I will examine all the evidence of this matter, to justify to the world the example I am resolved to make of such a monster.”\n\nThwackum was now sent for, and presently appeared. He corroborated every circumstance which the other had deposed; nay, he produced the record upon his breast, where the handwriting of Mr Jones remained very legible in black and blue. He concluded with declaring to Mr Allworthy, that he should have long since informed him of this matter, had not Mr Blifil, by the most earnest interpositions, prevented him. “He is,” says he, “an excellent youth: though such forgiveness of enemies is carrying the matter too far.”\n\nIn reality, Blifil had taken some pains to prevail with the parson, and to prevent the discovery at that time; for which he had many reasons. He knew that the minds of men are apt to be softened and relaxed from their usual severity by sickness. Besides, he imagined that if the story was told when the fact was so recent, and the physician about the house, who might have unravelled the real truth, he should never be able to give it the malicious turn which he intended. Again, he resolved to hoard up this business, till the indiscretion of Jones should afford some additional complaints; for he thought the joint weight of many facts falling upon him together, would be the most likely to crush him; and he watched, therefore, some such opportunity as that with which fortune had now kindly presented him. Lastly, by prevailing with Thwackum to conceal the matter for a time, he knew he should confirm an opinion of his friendship to Jones, which he had greatly laboured to establish in Mr Allworthy.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\nA short chapter; but which contains sufficient matter to affect the good-natured reader.\n\nIt was Mr Allworthy's custom never to punish any one, not even to turn away a servant, in a passion. He resolved therefore to delay passing sentence on Jones till the afternoon.\n\nThe poor young man attended at dinner, as usual; but his heart was too much loaded to suffer him to eat. His grief too was a good deal aggravated by the unkind looks of Mr Allworthy; whence he concluded that Western had discovered the whole affair between him and Sophia; but as to Mr Blifil's story, he had not the least apprehension; for of much the greater part he was entirely innocent; and for the residue, as he had forgiven and forgotten it himself, so he suspected no remembrance on the other side. When dinner was over, and the servants departed, Mr Allworthy began to harangue. He set forth, in a long speech, the many iniquities of which Jones had been guilty, particularly those which this day had brought to light; and concluded by telling him, “That unless he could clear himself of the charge, he was resolved to banish him his sight for ever.”\n\nMany disadvantages attended poor Jones in making his defence; nay, indeed, he hardly knew his accusation; for as Mr Allworthy, in recounting the drunkenness, &c., while he lay ill, out of modesty sunk everything that related particularly to himself, which indeed principally constituted the crime; Jones could not deny the charge. His heart was, besides, almost broken already; and his spirits were so sunk, that he could say nothing for himself; but acknowledged the whole, and, like a criminal in despair, threw himself upon mercy; concluding, “That though he must own himself guilty of many follies and inadvertencies, he hoped he had done nothing to deserve what would be to him the greatest punishment in the world.”\n\nAllworthy answered, “That he had forgiven him too often already, in compassion to his youth, and in hopes of his amendment: that he now found he was an abandoned reprobate, and such as it would be criminal in any one to support and encourage. Nay,” said Mr Allworthy to him, “your audacious attempt to steal away the young lady, calls upon me to justify my own character in punishing you. The world who have already censured the regard I have shown for you may think, with some colour at least of justice, that I connive at so base and barbarous an action--an action of which you must have known my abhorrence: and which, had you had any concern for my ease and honour, as well as for my friendship, you would never have thought of undertaking. Fie upon it, young man! indeed there is scarce any punishment equal to your crimes, and I can scarce think myself justifiable in what I am now going to bestow on you. However, as I have educated you like a child of my own, I will not turn you naked into the world. When you open this paper, therefore, you will find something which may enable you, with industry, to get an honest livelihood; but if you employ it to worse purposes, I shall not think myself obliged to supply you farther, being resolved, from this day forward, to converse no more with you on any account. I cannot avoid saying, there is no part of your conduct which I resent more than your ill-treatment of that good young man (meaning Blifil) who hath behaved with so much tenderness and honour towards you.”\n\nThese last words were a dose almost too bitter to be swallowed. A flood of tears now gushed from the eyes of Jones, and every faculty of speech and motion seemed to have deserted him. It was some time before he was able to obey Allworthy's peremptory commands of departing; which he at length did, having first kissed his hands with a passion difficult to be affected, and as difficult to be described.\n\nThe reader must be very weak, if, when he considers the light in which Jones then appeared to Mr Allworthy, he should blame the rigour of his sentence. And yet all the neighbourhood, either from this weakness, or from some worse motive, condemned this justice and severity as the highest cruelty. Nay, the very persons who had before censured the good man for the kindness and tenderness shown to a bastard (his own, according to the general opinion), now cried out as loudly against turning his own child out of doors. The women especially were unanimous in taking the part of Jones, and raised more stories on the occasion than I have room, in this chapter, to set down.\n\nOne thing must not be omitted, that, in their censures on this occasion, none ever mentioned the sum contained in the paper which Allworthy gave Jones, which was no less than five hundred pounds; but all agreed that he was sent away penniless, and some said naked, from the house of his inhuman father.\n\nContaining love-letters, &c.\n\nJones was commanded to leave the house immediately, and told, that his clothes and everything else should be sent to him whithersoever he should order them.\n\nHe accordingly set out, and walked above a mile, not regarding, and indeed scarce knowing, whither he went. At length a little brook obstructing his passage, he threw himself down by the side of it; nor could he help muttering with some little indignation, “Sure my father will not deny me this place to rest in!”\n\nHere he presently fell into the most violent agonies, tearing his hair from his head, and using most other actions which generally accompany fits of madness, rage, and despair.\n\nWhen he had in this manner vented the first emotions of passion, he began to come a little to himself. His grief now took another turn, and discharged itself in a gentler way, till he became at last cool enough to reason with his passion, and to consider what steps were proper to be taken in his deplorable condition.\n\nAnd now the great doubt was, how to act with regard to Sophia. The thoughts of leaving her almost rent his heart asunder; but the consideration of reducing her to ruin and beggary still racked him, if possible, more; and if the violent desire of possessing her person could have induced him to listen one moment to this alternative, still he was by no means certain of her resolution to indulge his wishes at so high an expense. The resentment of Mr Allworthy, and the injury he must do to his quiet, argued strongly against this latter; and lastly, the apparent impossibility of his success, even if he would sacrifice all these considerations to it, came to his assistance; and thus honour at last backed with despair, with gratitude to his benefactor, and with real love to his mistress, got the better of burning desire, and he resolved rather to quit Sophia, than pursue her to her ruin.\n\nIt is difficult for any who have not felt it, to conceive the glowing warmth which filled his breast on the first contemplation of this victory over his passion. Pride flattered him so agreeably, that his mind perhaps enjoyed perfect happiness; but this was only momentary: Sophia soon returned to his imagination, and allayed the joy of his triumph with no less bitter pangs than a good-natured general must feel, when he surveys the bleeding heaps, at the price of whose blood he hath purchased his laurels; for thousands of tender ideas lay murdered before our conqueror.\n\nBeing resolved, however, to pursue the paths of this giant honour, as the gigantic poet Lee calls it, he determined to write a farewel letter to Sophia; and accordingly proceeded to a house not far off, where, being furnished with proper materials, he wrote as follows:--\n\n“When you reflect on the situation in which I write, I am sure your good-nature will pardon any inconsistency or absurdity which my letter contains; for everything here flows from a heart so full, that no language can express its dictates.\n\n“I have resolved, madam, to obey your commands, in flying for ever from your dear, your lovely sight. Cruel indeed those commands are; but it is a cruelty which proceeds from fortune, not from my Sophia. Fortune hath made it necessary, necessary to your preservation, to forget there ever was such a wretch as I am.\n\n“Believe me, I would not hint all my sufferings to you, if I imagined they could possibly escape your ears. I know the goodness and tenderness of your heart, and would avoid giving you any of those pains which you always feel for the miserable. O let nothing, which you shall hear of my hard fortune, cause a moment's concern; for, after the loss of you, everything is to me a trifle.\n\n“O Sophia! it is hard to leave you; it is harder still to desire you to forget me; yet the sincerest love obliges me to both. Pardon my conceiving that any remembrance of me can give you disquiet; but if I am so gloriously wretched, sacrifice me every way to your relief. Think I never loved you; or think truly how little I deserve you; and learn to scorn me for a presumption which can never be too severely punished.--I am unable to say more.--May guardian angels protect you for ever!”\n\nHe was now searching his pockets for his wax, but found none, nor indeed anything else, therein; for in truth he had, in his frantic disposition, tossed everything from him, and amongst the rest, his pocket-book, which he had received from Mr Allworthy, which he had never opened, and which now first occurred to his memory.\n\nThe house supplied him with a wafer for his present purpose, with which, having sealed his letter, he returned hastily towards the brook side, in order to search for the things which he had there lost. In his way he met his old friend Black George, who heartily condoled with him on his misfortune; for this had already reached his ears, and indeed those of all the neighbourhood.\n\nJones acquainted the gamekeeper with his loss, and he as readily went back with him to the brook, where they searched every tuft of grass in the meadow, as well where Jones had not been as where he had been; but all to no purpose, for they found nothing; for, indeed, though the things were then in the meadow, they omitted to search the only place where they were deposited; to wit, in the pockets of the said George; for he had just before found them, and being luckily apprized of their value, had very carefully put them up for his own use.\n\nThe gamekeeper having exerted as much diligence in quest of the lost goods, as if he had hoped to find them, desired Mr Jones to recollect if he had been in no other place: “For sure,” said he, “if you had lost them here so lately, the things must have been here still; for this is a very unlikely place for any one to pass by.” And indeed it was by great accident that he himself had passed through that field, in order to lay wires for hares, with which he was to supply a poulterer at Bath the next morning.\n\nJones now gave over all hopes of recovering his loss, and almost all thoughts concerning it, and turning to Black George, asked him earnestly if he would do him the greatest favour in the world?\n\nGeorge answered with some hesitation, “Sir, you know you may command me whatever is in my power, and I heartily wish it was in my power to do you any service.” In fact, the question staggered him; for he had, by selling game, amassed a pretty good sum of money in Mr Western's service, and was afraid that Jones wanted to borrow some small matter of him; but he was presently relieved from his anxiety, by being desired to convey a letter to Sophia, which with great pleasure he promised to do. And indeed I believe there are few favours which he would not have gladly conferred on Mr Jones; for he bore as much gratitude towards him as he could, and was as honest as men who love money better than any other thing in the universe, generally are.\n\nMrs Honour was agreed by both to be the proper means by which this letter should pass to Sophia. They then separated; the gamekeeper returned home to Mr Western's, and Jones walked to an alehouse at half a mile's distance, to wait for his messenger's return.\n\nGeorge no sooner came home to his master's house than he met with Mrs Honour; to whom, having first sounded her with a few previous questions, he delivered the letter for her mistress, and received at the same time another from her, for Mr Jones; which Honour told him she had carried all that day in her bosom, and began to despair of finding any means of delivering it.\n\nThe gamekeeper returned hastily and joyfully to Jones, who, having received Sophia's letter from him, instantly withdrew, and eagerly breaking it open, read as follows:--\n\n“It is impossible to express what I have felt since I saw you. Your submitting, on my account, to such cruel insults from my father, lays me under an obligation I shall ever own. As you know his temper, I beg you will, for my sake, avoid him. I wish I had any comfort to send you; but believe this, that nothing but the last violence shall ever give my hand or heart where you would be sorry to see them bestowed.”\n\nJones read this letter a hundred times over, and kissed it a hundred times as often. His passion now brought all tender desires back into his mind. He repented that he had writ to Sophia in the manner we have seen above; but he repented more that he had made use of the interval of his messenger's absence to write and dispatch a letter to Mr Allworthy, in which he had faithfully promised and bound himself to quit all thoughts of his love. However, when his cool reflections returned, he plainly perceived that his case was neither mended nor altered by Sophia's billet, unless to give him some little glimpse of hope, from her constancy, of some favourable accident hereafter. He therefore resumed his resolution, and taking leave of Black George, set forward to a town about five miles distant, whither he had desired Mr Allworthy, unless he pleased to revoke his sentence, to send his things after him.\n\n# Chapter xiii.\n\nThe behaviour of Sophia on the present occasion; which none of her sex will blame, who are capable of behaving in the same manner. And the discussion of a knotty point in the court of conscience.\n\nSophia had passed the last twenty-four hours in no very desirable manner. During a large part of them she had been entertained by her aunt with lectures of prudence, recommending to her the example of the polite world, where love (so the good lady said) is at present entirely laughed at, and where women consider matrimony, as men do offices of public trust, only as the means of making their fortunes, and of advancing themselves in the world. In commenting on which text Mrs Western had displayed her eloquence during several hours.\n\nThese sagacious lectures, though little suited either to the taste or inclination of Sophia, were, however, less irksome to her than her own thoughts, that formed the entertainment of the night, during which she never once closed her eyes.\n\nBut though she could neither sleep nor rest in her bed, yet, having no avocation from it, she was found there by her father at his return from Allworthy's, which was not till past ten o'clock in the morning. He went directly up to her apartment, opened the door, and seeing she was not up, cried, “Oh! you are safe then, and I am resolved to keep you so.” He then locked the door, and delivered the key to Honour, having first given her the strictest charge, with great promises of rewards for her fidelity, and most dreadful menaces of punishment in case she should betray her trust.\n\nHonour's orders were, not to suffer her mistress to come out of her room without the authority of the squire himself, and to admit none to her but him and her aunt; but she was herself to attend her with whatever Sophia pleased, except only pen, ink, and paper, of which she was forbidden the use.\n\nThe squire ordered his daughter to dress herself and attend him at dinner; which she obeyed; and having sat the usual time, was again conducted to her prison.\n\nIn the evening the gaoler Honour brought her the letter which she received from the gamekeeper. Sophia read it very attentively twice or thrice over, and then threw herself upon the bed, and burst into a flood of tears. Mrs Honour expressed great astonishment at this behaviour in her mistress; nor could she forbear very eagerly begging to know the cause of this passion. Sophia made her no answer for some time, and then, starting suddenly up, caught her maid by the hand, and cried, “O Honour! I am undone.” “Marry forbid,” cries Honour: “I wish the letter had been burnt before I had brought it to your la'ship. I'm sure I thought it would have comforted your la'ship, or I would have seen it at the devil before I would have touched it.” “Honour,” says Sophia, “you are a good girl, and it is vain to attempt concealing longer my weakness from you; I have thrown away my heart on a man who hath forsaken me.” “And is Mr Jones,” answered the maid, “such a perfidy man?” “He hath taken his leave of me,” says Sophia, “for ever in that letter. Nay, he hath desired me to forget him. Could he have desired that if he had loved me? Could he have borne such a thought? Could he have written such a word?” “No, certainly, ma'am,” cries Honour; “and to be sure, if the best man in England was to desire me to forget him, I'd take him at his word. Marry, come up! I am sure your la'ship hath done him too much honour ever to think on him;--a young lady who may take her choice of all the young men in the country. And to be sure, if I may be so presumptuous as to offer my poor opinion, there is young Mr Blifil, who, besides that he is come of honest parents, and will be one of the greatest squires all hereabouts, he is to be sure, in my poor opinion, a more handsomer and a more politer man by half; and besides, he is a young gentleman of a sober character, and who may defy any of the neighbours to say black is his eye; he follows no dirty trollops, nor can any bastards be laid at his door. Forget him, indeed! I thank Heaven I myself am not so much at my last prayers as to suffer any man to bid me forget him twice. If the best he that wears a head was for to go for to offer to say such an affronting word to me, I would never give him my company afterwards, if there was another young man in the kingdom. And as I was a saying, to be sure, there is young Mr Blifil.” “Name not his detested name,” cries Sophia. “Nay, ma'am,” says Honour, “if your la'ship doth not like him, there be more jolly handsome young men that would court your la'ship, if they had but the least encouragement. I don't believe there is arrow young gentleman in this county, or in the next to it, that if your la'ship was but to look as if you had a mind to him, would not come about to make his offers directly.” “What a wretch dost thou imagine me,” cries Sophia, “by affronting my ears with such stuff! I detest all mankind.” “Nay, to be sure, ma'am,” answered Honour, “your la'ship hath had enough to give you a surfeit of them. To be used ill by such a poor, beggarly, bastardly fellow.”--“Hold your blasphemous tongue,” cries Sophia: “how dare you mention his name with disrespect before me? He use me ill? No, his poor bleeding heart suffered more when he writ the cruel words than mine from reading them. O! he is all heroic virtue and angelic goodness. I am ashamed of the weakness of my own passion, for blaming what I ought to admire. O, Honour! it is my good only which he consults. To my interest he sacrifices both himself and me. The apprehension of ruining me hath driven him to despair.” “I am very glad,” says Honour, “to hear your la'ship takes that into your consideration; for to be sure, it must be nothing less than ruin to give your mind to one that is turned out of doors, and is not worth a farthing in the world.” “Turned out of doors!” cries Sophia hastily: “how! what dost thou mean?” “Why, to be sure, ma'am, my master no sooner told Squire Allworthy about Mr Jones having offered to make love to your la'ship than the squire stripped him stark naked, and turned him out of doors!” “Ha!” says Sophia, “I have been the cursed, wretched cause of his destruction! Turned naked out of doors! Here, Honour, take all the money I have; take the rings from my fingers. Here, my watch: carry him all. Go find him immediately.” “For Heaven's sake, ma'am,” answered Mrs Honour, “do but consider, if my master should miss any of these things, I should be made to answer for them. Therefore let me beg your la'ship not to part with your watch and jewels. Besides, the money, I think, is enough of all conscience; and as for that, my master can never know anything of the matter.” “Here, then,” cries Sophia, “take every farthing I am worth, find him out immediately, and give it him. Go, go, lose not a moment.”\n\nMrs Honour departed according to orders, and finding Black George below-stairs, delivered him the purse, which contained sixteen guineas, being, indeed, the whole stock of Sophia; for though her father was very liberal to her, she was much too generous to be rich.\n\nBlack George having received the purse, set forward towards the alehouse; but in the way a thought occurred to him, whether he should not detain this money likewise. His conscience, however, immediately started at this suggestion, and began to upbraid him with ingratitude to his benefactor. To this his avarice answered, That his conscience should have considered the matter before, when he deprived poor Jones of his £500. That having quietly acquiesced in what was of so much greater importance, it was absurd, if not downright hypocrisy, to affect any qualms at this trifle. In return to which, Conscience, like a good lawyer, attempted to distinguish between an absolute breach of trust, as here, where the goods were delivered, and a bare concealment of what was found, as in the former case. Avarice presently treated this with ridicule, called it a distinction without a difference, and absolutely insisted that when once all pretensions of honour and virtue were given up in any one instance, that there was no precedent for resorting to them upon a second occasion. In short, poor Conscience had certainly been defeated in the argument, had not Fear stept in to her assistance, and very strenuously urged that the real distinction between the two actions, did not lie in the different degrees of honour but of safety: for that the secreting the £500 was a matter of very little hazard; whereas the detaining the sixteen guineas was liable to the utmost danger of discovery.\n\nBy this friendly aid of Fear, Conscience obtained a compleat victory in the mind of Black George, and, after making him a few compliments on his honesty, forced him to deliver the money to Jones.\n\n# Chapter xiv.\n\nA short chapter, containing a short dialogue between Squire Western and his sister.\n\nMrs Western had been engaged abroad all that day. The squire met her at her return home; and when she enquired after Sophia, he acquainted her that he had secured her safe enough. “She is locked up in chamber,” cries he, “and Honour keeps the key.” As his looks were full of prodigious wisdom and sagacity when he gave his sister this information, it is probable he expected much applause from her for what he had done; but how was he disappointed when, with a most disdainful aspect, she cried, “Sure, brother, you are the weakest of all men. Why will you not confide in me for the management of my niece? Why will you interpose? You have now undone all that I have been spending my breath in order to bring about. While I have been endeavouring to fill her mind with maxims of prudence, you have been provoking her to reject them. English women, brother, I thank heaven, are no slaves. We are not to be locked up like the Spanish and Italian wives. We have as good a right to liberty as yourselves. We are to be convinced by reason and persuasion only, and not governed by force. I have seen the world, brother, and know what arguments to make use of; and if your folly had not prevented me, should have prevailed with her to form her conduct by those rules of prudence and discretion which I formerly taught her.” “To be sure,” said the squire, “I am always in the wrong.” “Brother,” answered the lady, “you are not in the wrong, unless when you meddle with matters beyond your knowledge. You must agree that I have seen most of the world; and happy had it been for my niece if she had not been taken from under my care. It is by living at home with you that she hath learnt romantic notions of love and nonsense.” “You don't imagine, I hope,” cries the squire, “that I have taught her any such things.” “Your ignorance, brother,” returned she, “as the great Milton says, almost subdues my patience.”[*] “D--n Milton!” answered the squire: “if he had the impudence to say so to my face, I'd lend him a douse, thof he was never so great a man. Patience! An you come to that, sister, I have more occasion of patience, to be used like an overgrown schoolboy, as I am by you. Do you think no one hath any understanding, unless he hath been about at court. Pox! the world is come to a fine pass indeed, if we are all fools, except a parcel of round-heads and Hanover rats. Pox! I hope the times are a coming when we shall make fools of them, and every man shall enjoy his own. That's all, sister; and every man shall enjoy his own. I hope to zee it, sister, before the Hanover rats have eat up all our corn, and left us nothing but turneps to feed upon.”--“I protest, brother,” cries she, “you are now got beyond my understanding. Your jargon of turneps and Hanover rats is to me perfectly unintelligible.”--“I believe,” cries he, “you don't care to hear o'em; but the country interest may succeed one day or other for all that.”--“I wish,” answered the lady, “you would think a little of your daughter's interest; for, believe me, she is in greater danger than the nation.”--“Just now,” said he, “you chid me for thinking on her, and would ha' her left to you.”--“And if you will promise to interpose no more,” answered she, “I will, out of my regard to my niece, undertake the charge.”--“Well, do then,” said the squire, “for you know I always agreed, that women are the properest to manage women.”\n\n[*] The reader may, perhaps, subdue his own patience, if he searches for this in Milton.]\n\nMrs Western then departed, muttering something with an air of disdain, concerning women and management of the nation. She immediately repaired to Sophia's apartment, who was now, after a day's confinement, released again from her captivity.\n\nA comparison between the world and the stage.\nThe world hath been often compared to the theatre; and many grave writers, as well as the poets, have considered human life as a great drama, resembling, in almost every particular, those scenical representations which Thespis is first reported to have invented, and which have been since received with so much approbation and delight in all polite countries.\n\nThis thought hath been carried so far, and is become so general, that some words proper to the theatre, and which were at first metaphorically applied to the world, are now indiscriminately and literally spoken of both; thus stage and scene are by common use grown as familiar to us, when we speak of life in general, as when we confine ourselves to dramatic performances: and when transactions behind the curtain are mentioned, St James's is more likely to occur to our thoughts than Drury-lane.\n\nIt may seem easy enough to account for all this, by reflecting that the theatrical stage is nothing more than a representation, or, as Aristotle calls it, an imitation of what really exists; and hence, perhaps, we might fairly pay a very high compliment to those who by their writings or actions have been so capable of imitating life, as to have their pictures in a manner confounded with, or mistaken for, the originals.\n\nBut, in reality, we are not so fond of paying compliments to these people, whom we use as children frequently do the instruments of their amusement; and have much more pleasure in hissing and buffeting them, than in admiring their excellence. There are many other reasons which have induced us to see this analogy between the world and the stage.\n\nSome have considered the larger part of mankind in the light of actors, as personating characters no more their own, and to which in fact they have no better title, than the player hath to be in earnest thought the king or emperor whom he represents. Thus the hypocrite may be said to be a player; and indeed the Greeks called them both by one and the same name.\n\nThe brevity of life hath likewise given occasion to this comparison. So the immortal Shakespear--\n\n--Life's a poor player, That struts and frets his hour upon the stage, And then is heard no more.\n\nFor which hackneyed quotation I will make the reader amends by a very noble one, which few, I believe, have read. It is taken from a poem called the Deity, published about nine years ago, and long since buried in oblivion; a proof that good books, no more than good men, do always survive the bad.\n\nFrom Thee[*] all human actions take their springs, The rise of empires and the fall of kings! See the vast Theatre of Time display'd, While o'er the scene succeeding heroes tread! With pomp the shining images succeed, What leaders triumph, and what monarchs bleed! Perform the parts thy providence assign'd, Their pride, their passions, to thy ends inclin'd: Awhile they glitter in the face of day, Then at thy nod the phantoms pass away; No traces left of all the busy scene, But that remembrance says--The things have been!\n\n[*] The Deity.\n\nIn all these, however, and in every other similitude of life to the theatre, the resemblance hath been always taken from the stage only. None, as I remember, have at all considered the audience at this great drama.\n\nBut as Nature often exhibits some of her best performances to a very full house, so will the behaviour of her spectators no less admit the above-mentioned comparison than that of her actors. In this vast theatre of time are seated the friend and the critic; here are claps and shouts, hisses and groans; in short, everything which was ever seen or heard at the Theatre-Royal.\n\nLet us examine this in one example; for instance, in the behaviour of the great audience on that scene which Nature was pleased to exhibit in the twelfth chapter of the preceding book, where she introduced Black George running away with the £500 from his friend and benefactor.\n\nThose who sat in the world's upper gallery treated that incident, I am well convinced, with their usual vociferation; and every term of scurrilous reproach was most probably vented on that occasion.\n\nIf we had descended to the next order of spectators, we should have found an equal degree of abhorrence, though less of noise and scurrility; yet here the good women gave Black George to the devil, and many of them expected every minute that the cloven-footed gentleman would fetch his own.\n\nThe pit, as usual, was no doubt divided; those who delight in heroic virtue and perfect character objected to the producing such instances of villany, without punishing them very severely for the sake of example. Some of the author's friends cryed, “Look'e, gentlemen, the man is a villain, but it is nature for all that.” And all the young critics of the age, the clerks, apprentices, &c., called it low, and fell a groaning.\n\nAs for the boxes, they behaved with their accustomed politeness. Most of them were attending to something else. Some of those few who regarded the scene at all, declared he was a bad kind of man; while others refused to give their opinion, till they had heard that of the best judges.\n\nNow we, who are admitted behind the scenes of this great theatre of Nature (and no author ought to write anything besides dictionaries and spelling-books who hath not this privilege), can censure the action, without conceiving any absolute detestation of the person, whom perhaps Nature may not have designed to act an ill part in all her dramas; for in this instance life most exactly resembles the stage, since it is often the same person who represents the villain and the heroe; and he who engages your admiration to-day will probably attract your contempt to-morrow. As Garrick, whom I regard in tragedy to be the greatest genius the world hath ever produced, sometimes condescends to play the fool; so did Scipio the Great, and Laelius the Wise, according to Horace, many years ago; nay, Cicero reports them to have been “incredibly childish.” These, it is true, played the fool, like my friend Garrick, in jest only; but several eminent characters have, in numberless instances of their lives, played the fool egregiously in earnest; so far as to render it a matter of some doubt whether their wisdom or folly was predominant; or whether they were better intitled to the applause or censure, the admiration or contempt, the love or hatred, of mankind.\n\nThose persons, indeed, who have passed any time behind the scenes of this great theatre, and are thoroughly acquainted not only with the several disguises which are there put on, but also with the fantastic and capricious behaviour of the Passions, who are the managers and directors of this theatre (for as to Reason, the patentee, he is known to be a very idle fellow and seldom to exert himself), may most probably have learned to understand the famous nil admirari of Horace, or in the English phrase, to stare at nothing.\n\nA single bad act no more constitutes a villain in life, than a single bad part on the stage. The passions, like the managers of a playhouse, often force men upon parts without consulting their judgment, and sometimes without any regard to their talents. Thus the man, as well as the player, may condemn what he himself acts; nay, it is common to see vice sit as awkwardly on some men, as the character of Iago would on the honest face of Mr William Mills.\n\nUpon the whole, then, the man of candour and of true understanding is never hasty to condemn. He can censure an imperfection, or even a vice, without rage against the guilty party. In a word, they are the same folly, the same childishness, the same ill-breeding, and the same ill-nature, which raise all the clamours and uproars both in life and on the stage. The worst of men generally have the words rogue and villain most in their mouths, as the lowest of all wretches are the aptest to cry out low in the pit.\n\nContaining a conversation which Mr Jones had with himself.\n\nJones received his effects from Mr Allworthy's early in the morning, with the following answer to his letter:--\n\n“I am commanded by my uncle to acquaint you, that as he did not proceed to those measures he had taken with you, without the greatest deliberation, and after the fullest evidence of your unworthiness, so will it be always out of your power to cause the least alteration in his resolution. He expresses great surprize at your presumption in saying you have resigned all pretensions to a young lady, to whom it is impossible you should ever have had any, her birth and fortune having made her so infinitely your superior. Lastly, I am commanded to tell you, that the only instance of your compliance with my uncle's inclinations which he requires, is, your immediately quitting this country. I cannot conclude this without offering you my advice, as a Christian, that you would seriously think of amending your life. That you may be assisted with grace so to do, will be always the prayer of\n\n“Your humble servant,\n\nMany contending passions were raised in our heroe's mind by this letter; but the tender prevailed at last over the indignant and irascible, and a flood of tears came seasonably to his assistance, and possibly prevented his misfortunes from either turning his head, or bursting his heart.\n\nHe grew, however, soon ashamed of indulging this remedy; and starting up, he cried, “Well, then, I will give Mr Allworthy the only instance he requires of my obedience. I will go this moment--but whither?--why, let Fortune direct; since there is no other who thinks it of any consequence what becomes of this wretched person, it shall be a matter of equal indifference to myself. Shall I alone regard what no other--Ha! have I not reason to think there is another?--one whose value is above that of the whole world!--I may, I must imagine my Sophia is not indifferent to what becomes of me. Shall I then leave this only friend--and such a friend? Shall I not stay with her?--Where--how can I stay with her? Have I any hopes of ever seeing her, though she was as desirous as myself, without exposing her to the wrath of her father, and to what purpose? Can I think of soliciting such a creature to consent to her own ruin? Shall I indulge any passion of mine at such a price? Shall I lurk about this country like a thief, with such intentions?--No, I disdain, I detest the thought. Farewel, Sophia; farewel, most lovely, most beloved--” Here passion stopped his mouth, and found a vent at his eyes.\n\nAnd now having taken a resolution to leave the country, he began to debate with himself whither he should go. The world, as Milton phrases it, lay all before him; and Jones, no more than Adam, had any man to whom he might resort for comfort or assistance. All his acquaintance were the acquaintance of Mr Allworthy; and he had no reason to expect any countenance from them, as that gentleman had withdrawn his favour from him. Men of great and good characters should indeed be very cautious how they discard their dependents; for the consequence to the unhappy sufferer is being discarded by all others.\n\nWhat course of life to pursue, or to what business to apply himself, was a second consideration: and here the prospect was all a melancholy void. Every profession, and every trade, required length of time, and what was worse, money; for matters are so constituted, that “nothing out of nothing” is not a truer maxim in physics than in politics; and every man who is greatly destitute of money, is on that account entirely excluded from all means of acquiring it.\n\nAt last the Ocean, that hospitable friend to the wretched, opened her capacious arms to receive him; and he instantly resolved to accept her kind invitation. To express myself less figuratively, he determined to go to sea.\n\nThis thought indeed no sooner suggested itself, than he eagerly embraced it; and having presently hired horses, he set out for Bristol to put it in execution.\n\nBut before we attend him on this expedition, we shall resort awhile to Mr Western's, and see what further happened to the charming Sophia.\n\nContaining several dialogues.\n\nThe morning in which Mr Jones departed, Mrs Western summoned Sophia into her apartment; and having first acquainted her that she had obtained her liberty of her father, she proceeded to read her a long lecture on the subject of matrimony; which she treated not as a romantic scheme of happiness arising from love, as it hath been described by the poets; nor did she mention any of those purposes for which we are taught by divines to regard it as instituted by sacred authority; she considered it rather as a fund in which prudent women deposit their fortunes to the best advantage, in order to receive a larger interest for them than they could have elsewhere.\n\nWhen Mrs Western had finished, Sophia answered, “That she was very incapable of arguing with a lady of her aunt's superior knowledge and experience, especially on a subject which she had so very little considered, as this of matrimony.”\n\n“Argue with me, child!” replied the other; “I do not indeed expect it. I should have seen the world to very little purpose truly, if I am to argue with one of your years. I have taken this trouble, in order to instruct you. The antient philosophers, such as Socrates, Alcibiades, and others, did not use to argue with their scholars. You are to consider me, child, as Socrates, not asking your opinion, but only informing you of mine.” From which last words the reader may possibly imagine, that this lady had read no more of the philosophy of Socrates, than she had of that of Alcibiades; and indeed we cannot resolve his curiosity as to this point.\n\n“Madam,” cries Sophia, “I have never presumed to controvert any opinion of yours; and this subject, as I said, I have never yet thought of, and perhaps never may.”\n\n“Indeed, Sophy,” replied the aunt, “this dissimulation with me is very foolish. The French shall as soon persuade me that they take foreign towns in defence only of their own country, as you can impose on me to believe you have never yet thought seriously of matrimony. How can you, child, affect to deny that you have considered of contracting an alliance, when you so well know I am acquainted with the party with whom you desire to contract it?--an alliance as unnatural, and contrary to your interest, as a separate league with the French would be to the interest of the Dutch! But however, if you have not hitherto considered of this matter, I promise you it is now high time, for my brother is resolved immediately to conclude the treaty with Mr Blifil; and indeed I am a sort of guarantee in the affair, and have promised your concurrence.”\n\n“Indeed, madam,” cries Sophia, “this is the only instance in which I must disobey both yourself and my father. For this is a match which requires very little consideration in me to refuse.”\n\n“If I was not as great a philosopher as Socrates himself,” returned Mrs Western, “you would overcome my patience. What objection can you have to the young gentleman?”\n\n“A very solid objection, in my opinion,” says Sophia--“I hate him.”\n\n“Will you never learn a proper use of words?” answered the aunt. “Indeed, child, you should consult Bailey's Dictionary. It is impossible you should hate a man from whom you have received no injury. By hatred, therefore, you mean no more than dislike, which is no sufficient objection against your marrying of him. I have known many couples, who have entirely disliked each other, lead very comfortable genteel lives. Believe me, child, I know these things better than you. You will allow me, I think, to have seen the world, in which I have not an acquaintance who would not rather be thought to dislike her husband than to like him. The contrary is such out-of-fashion romantic nonsense, that the very imagination of it is shocking.”\n\n“Indeed, madam,” replied Sophia, “I shall never marry a man I dislike. If I promise my father never to consent to any marriage contrary to his inclinations, I think I may hope he will never force me into that state contrary to my own.”\n\n“Inclinations!” cries the aunt, with some warmth. “Inclinations! I am astonished at your assurance. A young woman of your age, and unmarried, to talk of inclinations! But whatever your inclinations may be, my brother is resolved; nay, since you talk of inclinations, I shall advise him to hasten the treaty. Inclinations!”\n\nSophia then flung herself upon her knees, and tears began to trickle from her shining eyes. She entreated her aunt, “to have mercy upon her, and not to resent so cruelly her unwillingness to make herself miserable;” often urging, “that she alone was concerned, and that her happiness only was at stake.”\n\nAs a bailiff, when well authorized by his writ, having possessed himself of the person of some unhappy debtor, views all his tears without concern; in vain the wretched captive attempts to raise compassion; in vain the tender wife bereft of her companion, the little prattling boy, or frighted girl, are mentioned as inducements to reluctance. The noble bumtrap, blind and deaf to every circumstance of distress, greatly rises above all the motives to humanity, and into the hands of the gaoler resolves to deliver his miserable prey.\n\nNot less blind to the tears, or less deaf to every entreaty of Sophia was the politic aunt, nor less determined was she to deliver over the trembling maid into the arms of the gaoler Blifil. She answered with great impetuosity, “So far, madam, from your being concerned alone, your concern is the least, or surely the least important. It is the honour of your family which is concerned in this alliance; you are only the instrument. Do you conceive, mistress, that in an intermarriage between kingdoms, as when a daughter of France is married into Spain, the princess herself is alone considered in the match? No! it is a match between two kingdoms, rather than between two persons. The same happens in great families such as ours. The alliance between the families is the principal matter. You ought to have a greater regard for the honour of your family than for your own person; and if the example of a princess cannot inspire you with these noble thoughts, you cannot surely complain at being used no worse than all princesses are used.”\n\n“I hope, madam,” cries Sophia, with a little elevation of voice, “I shall never do anything to dishonour my family; but as for Mr Blifil, whatever may be the consequence, I am resolved against him, and no force shall prevail in his favour.”\n\nWestern, who had been within hearing during the greater part of the preceding dialogue, had now exhausted all his patience; he therefore entered the room in a violent passion, crying, “D--n me then if shatunt ha'un, d--n me if shatunt, that's all--that's all; d--n me if shatunt.”\n\nMrs Western had collected a sufficient quantity of wrath for the use of Sophia; but she now transferred it all to the squire. “Brother,” said she, “it is astonishing that you will interfere in a matter which you had totally left to my negotiation. Regard to my family hath made me take upon myself to be the mediating power, in order to rectify those mistakes in policy which you have committed in your daughter's education. For, brother, it is you--it is your preposterous conduct which hath eradicated all the seeds that I had formerly sown in her tender mind. It is you yourself who have taught her disobedience.”--“Blood!” cries the squire, foaming at the mouth, “you are enough to conquer the patience of the devil! Have I ever taught my daughter disobedience?--Here she stands; speak honestly, girl, did ever I bid you be disobedient to me? Have not I done everything to humour and to gratify you, and to make you obedient to me? And very obedient to me she was when a little child, before you took her in hand and spoiled her, by filling her head with a pack of court notions. Why--why--why--did I not overhear you telling her she must behave like a princess? You have made a Whig of the girl; and how should her father, or anybody else, expect any obedience from her?”--“Brother,” answered Mrs Western, with an air of great disdain, “I cannot express the contempt I have for your politics of all kinds; but I will appeal likewise to the young lady herself, whether I have ever taught her any principles of disobedience. On the contrary, niece, have I not endeavoured to inspire you with a true idea of the several relations in which a human creature stands in society? Have I not taken infinite pains to show you, that the law of nature hath enjoined a duty on children to their parents? Have I not told you what Plato says on that subject?--a subject on which you was so notoriously ignorant when you came first under my care, that I verily believe you did not know the relation between a daughter and a father.”--“'Tis a lie,” answered Western. “The girl is no such fool, as to live to eleven years old without knowing that she was her father's relation.”--“O! more than Gothic ignorance,” answered the lady. “And as for your manners, brother, I must tell you, they deserve a cane.”--“Why then you may gi' it me, if you think you are able,” cries the squire; “nay, I suppose your niece there will be ready enough to help you.”--“Brother,” said Mrs Western, “though I despise you beyond expression, yet I shall endure your insolence no longer; so I desire my coach may be got ready immediately, for I am resolved to leave your house this very morning.”--“And a good riddance too,” answered he; “I can bear your insolence no longer, an you come to that. Blood! it is almost enough of itself to make my daughter undervalue my sense, when she hears you telling me every minute you despise me.”--“It is impossible, it is impossible,” cries the aunt; “no one can undervalue such a boor.”--“Boar,” answered the squire, “I am no boar; no, nor ass; no, nor rat neither, madam. Remember that--I am no rat. I am a true Englishman, and not of your Hanover breed, that have eat up the nation.”--“Thou art one of those wise men,” cries she, “whose nonsensical principles have undone the nation; by weakening the hands of our government at home, and by discouraging our friends and encouraging our enemies abroad.”--“Ho! are you come back to your politics?” cries the squire: “as for those I despise them as much as I do a f--t.” Which last words he accompanied and graced with the very action, which, of all others, was the most proper to it. And whether it was this word or the contempt exprest for her politics, which most affected Mrs Western, I will not determine; but she flew into the most violent rage, uttered phrases improper to be here related, and instantly burst out of the house. Nor did her brother or her niece think proper either to stop or to follow her; for the one was so much possessed by concern, and the other by anger, that they were rendered almost motionless.\n\nThe squire, however, sent after his sister the same holloa which attends the departure of a hare, when she is first started before the hounds. He was indeed a great master of this kind of vociferation, and had a holla proper for most occasions in life.\n\nWomen who, like Mrs Western, know the world, and have applied themselves to philosophy and politics, would have immediately availed themselves of the present disposition of Mr Western's mind, by throwing in a few artful compliments to his understanding at the expense of his absent adversary; but poor Sophia was all simplicity. By which word we do not intend to insinuate to the reader, that she was silly, which is generally understood as a synonymous term with simple; for she was indeed a most sensible girl, and her understanding was of the first rate; but she wanted all that useful art which females convert to so many good purposes in life, and which, as it rather arises from the heart than from the head, is often the property of the silliest of women.\n\nA picture of a country gentlewoman taken from the life.\n\nMr Western having finished his holla, and taken a little breath, began to lament, in very pathetic terms, the unfortunate condition of men, who are, says he, “always whipt in by the humours of some d--n'd b-- or other. I think I was hard run enough by your mother for one man; but after giving her a dodge, here's another b-- follows me upon the foil; but curse my jacket if I will be run down in this manner by any o'um.”\n\nSophia never had a single dispute with her father, till this unlucky affair of Blifil, on any account, except in defence of her mother, whom she had loved most tenderly, though she lost her in the eleventh year of her age. The squire, to whom that poor woman had been a faithful upper-servant all the time of their marriage, had returned that behaviour by making what the world calls a good husband. He very seldom swore at her (perhaps not above once a week) and never beat her; she had not the least occasion for jealousy, and was perfect mistress of her time; for she was never interrupted by her husband, who was engaged all the morning in his field exercises, and all the evening with bottle companions. She scarce indeed ever saw him but at meals; where she had the pleasure of carving those dishes which she had before attended at the dressing. From these meals she retired about five minutes after the other servants, having only stayed to drink “the king over the water.” Such were, it seems, Mr Western's orders; for it was a maxim with him, that women should come in with the first dish, and go out after the first glass. Obedience to these orders was perhaps no difficult task; for the conversation (if it may be called so) was seldom such as could entertain a lady. It consisted chiefly of hallowing, singing, relations of sporting adventures, b--d--y, and abuse of women, and of the government.\n\nThese, however, were the only seasons when Mr Western saw his wife; for when he repaired to her bed, he was generally so drunk that he could not see; and in the sporting season he always rose from her before it was light. Thus was she perfect mistress of her time, and had besides a coach and four usually at her command; though unhappily, indeed, the badness of the neighbourhood, and of the roads, made this of little use; for none who had set much value on their necks would have passed through the one, or who had set any value on their hours, would have visited the other. Now to deal honestly with the reader, she did not make all the return expected to so much indulgence; for she had been married against her will by a fond father, the match having been rather advantageous on her side; for the squire's estate was upward of £3000 a year, and her fortune no more than a bare £8000. Hence perhaps she had contracted a little gloominess of temper, for she was rather a good servant than a good wife; nor had she always the gratitude to return the extraordinary degree of roaring mirth, with which the squire received her, even with a good-humoured smile. She would, moreover, sometimes interfere with matters which did not concern her, as the violent drinking of her husband, which in the gentlest terms she would take some of the few opportunities he gave her of remonstrating against. And once in her life she very earnestly entreated him to carry her for two months to London, which he peremptorily denied; nay, was angry with his wife for the request ever after, being well assured that all the husbands in London are cuckolds.\n\nFor this last, and many other good reasons, Western at length heartily hated his wife; and as he never concealed this hatred before her death, so he never forgot it afterwards; but when anything in the least soured him, as a bad scenting day, or a distemper among his hounds, or any other such misfortune, he constantly vented his spleen by invectives against the deceased, saying, “If my wife was alive now, she would be glad of this.”\n\nThese invectives he was especially desirous of throwing forth before Sophia; for as he loved her more than he did any other, so he was really jealous that she had loved her mother better than him. And this jealousy Sophia seldom failed of heightening on these occasions; for he was not contented with violating her ears with the abuse of her mother, but endeavoured to force an explicit approbation of all this abuse; with which desire he never could prevail upon her by any promise or threats to comply.\n\nHence some of my readers will, perhaps, wonder that the squire had not hated Sophia as much as he had hated her mother; but I must inform them, that hatred is not the effect of love, even through the medium of jealousy. It is, indeed, very possible for jealous persons to kill the objects of their jealousy, but not to hate them. Which sentiment being a pretty hard morsel, and bearing something of the air of a paradox, we shall leave the reader to chew the cud upon it to the end of the chapter.\n\nThe generous behaviour of Sophia towards her aunt.\n\nSophia kept silence during the foregoing speech of her father, nor did she once answer otherwise than with a sigh; but as he understood none of the language, or, as he called it, lingo of the eyes, so he was not satisfied without some further approbation of his sentiments, which he now demanded of his daughter; telling her, in the usual way, “he expected she was ready to take the part of everybody against him, as she had always done that of the b-- her mother.” Sophia remaining still silent, he cryed out, “What, art dumb? why dost unt speak? Was not thy mother a d--d b-- to me? answer me that. What, I suppose you despise your father too, and don't think him good enough to speak to?”\n\n“For Heaven's sake, sir,” answered Sophia, “do not give so cruel a turn to my silence. I am sure I would sooner die than be guilty of any disrespect towards you; but how can I venture to speak, when every word must either offend my dear papa, or convict me of the blackest ingratitude as well as impiety to the memory of the best of mothers; for such, I am certain, my mamma was always to me?”\n\n“And your aunt, I suppose, is the best of sisters too!” replied the squire. “Will you be so kind as to allow that she is a b--? I may fairly insist upon that, I think?”\n\n“Indeed, sir,” says Sophia, “I have great obligations to my aunt. She hath been a second mother to me.”\n\n“And a second wife to me too,” returned Western; “so you will take her part too! You won't confess that she hath acted the part of the vilest sister in the world?”\n\n“Upon my word, sir,” cries Sophia, “I must belie my heart wickedly if I did. I know my aunt and you differ very much in your ways of thinking; but I have heard her a thousand times express the greatest affection for you; and I am convinced, so far from her being the worst sister in the world, there are very few who love a brother better.”\n\n“The English of all which is,” answered the squire, “that I am in the wrong. Ay, certainly. Ay, to be sure the woman is in the right, and the man in the wrong always.”\n\n“Pardon me, sir,” cries Sophia. “I do not say so.”\n\n“What don't you say?” answered the father: “you have the impudence to say she's in the right: doth it not follow then of course that I am in the wrong? And perhaps I am in the wrong to suffer such a Presbyterian Hanoverian b-- to come into my house. She may 'dite me of a plot for anything I know, and give my estate to the government.”\n\n“So far, sir, from injuring you or your estate,” says Sophia, “if my aunt had died yesterday, I am convinced she would have left you her whole fortune.”\n\nWhether Sophia intended it or no, I shall not presume to assert; but certain it is, these last words penetrated very deep into the ears of her father, and produced a much more sensible effect than all she had said before. He received the sound with much the same action as a man receives a bullet in his head. He started, staggered, and turned pale. After which he remained silent above a minute, and then began in the following hesitating manner: “Yesterday! she would have left me her esteate yesterday! would she? Why yesterday, of all the days in the year? I suppose if she dies to-morrow, she will leave it to somebody else, and perhaps out of the vamily.”--“My aunt, sir,” cries Sophia, “hath very violent passions, and I can't answer what she may do under their influence.”\n\n“You can't!” returned the father: “and pray who hath been the occasion of putting her into those violent passions? Nay, who hath actually put her into them? Was not you and she hard at it before I came into the room? Besides, was not all our quarrel about you? I have not quarrelled with sister this many years but upon your account; and now you would throw the whole blame upon me, as thof I should be the occasion of her leaving the esteate out o' the vamily. I could have expected no better indeed; this is like the return you make to all the rest of my fondness.”\n\n“I beseech you then,” cries Sophia, “upon my knees I beseech you, if I have been the unhappy occasion of this difference, that you will endeavour to make it up with my aunt, and not suffer her to leave your house in this violent rage of anger: she is a very good-natured woman, and a few civil words will satisfy her. Let me entreat you, sir.”\n\n“So I must go and ask pardon for your fault, must I?” answered Western. “You have lost the hare, and I must draw every way to find her again? Indeed, if I was certain”--Here he stopt, and Sophia throwing in more entreaties, at length prevailed upon him; so that after venting two or three bitter sarcastical expressions against his daughter, he departed as fast as he could to recover his sister, before her equipage could be gotten ready.\n\nSophia then returned to her chamber of mourning, where she indulged herself (if the phrase may be allowed me) in all the luxury of tender grief. She read over more than once the letter which she had received from Jones; her muff too was used on this occasion; and she bathed both these, as well as herself, with her tears. In this situation the friendly Mrs Honour exerted her utmost abilities to comfort her afflicted mistress. She ran over the names of many young gentlemen: and having greatly commended their parts and persons, assured Sophia that she might take her choice of any. These methods must have certainly been used with some success in disorders of the like kind, or so skilful a practitioner as Mrs Honour would never have ventured to apply them; nay, I have heard that the college of chambermaids hold them to be as sovereign remedies as any in the female dispensary; but whether it was that Sophia's disease differed inwardly from those cases with which it agreed in external symptoms, I will not assert; but, in fact, the good waiting-woman did more harm than good, and at last so incensed her mistress (which was no easy matter) that with an angry voice she dismissed her from her presence.\n\nContaining great variety of matter.\n\nThe squire overtook his sister just as she was stepping into the coach, and partly by force, and partly by solicitations, prevailed upon her to order her horses back into their quarters. He succeeded in this attempt without much difficulty; for the lady was, as we have already hinted, of a most placable disposition, and greatly loved her brother, though she despised his parts, or rather his little knowledge of the world.\n\nPoor Sophia, who had first set on foot this reconciliation, was now made the sacrifice to it. They both concurred in their censures on her conduct; jointly declared war against her, and directly proceeded to counsel, how to carry it on in the most vigorous manner. For this purpose, Mrs Western proposed not only an immediate conclusion of the treaty with Allworthy, but as immediately to carry it into execution; saying, “That there was no other way to succeed with her niece, but by violent methods, which she was convinced Sophia had not sufficient resolution to resist. By violent,” says she, “I mean rather, hasty measures; for as to confinement or absolute force, no such things must or can be attempted. Our plan must be concerted for a surprize, and not for a storm.”\n\nThese matters were resolved on, when Mr Blifil came to pay a visit to his mistress. The squire no sooner heard of his arrival, than he stept aside, by his sister's advice, to give his daughter orders for the proper reception of her lover: which he did with the most bitter execrations and denunciations of judgment on her refusal.\n\nThe impetuosity of the squire bore down all before him; and Sophia, as her aunt very wisely foresaw, was not able to resist him. She agreed, therefore, to see Blifil, though she had scarce spirits or strength sufficient to utter her assent. Indeed, to give a peremptory denial to a father whom she so tenderly loved, was no easy task. Had this circumstance been out of the case, much less resolution than what she was really mistress of, would, perhaps, have served her; but it is no unusual thing to ascribe those actions entirely to fear, which are in a great measure produced by love.\n\nIn pursuance, therefore, of her father's peremptory command, Sophia now admitted Mr Blifil's visit. Scenes like this, when painted at large, afford, as we have observed, very little entertainment to the reader. Here, therefore, we shall strictly adhere to a rule of Horace; by which writers are directed to pass over all those matters which they despair of placing in a shining light;--a rule, we conceive, of excellent use as well to the historian as to the poet; and which, if followed, must at least have this good effect, that many a great evil (for so all great books are called) would thus be reduced to a small one.\n\nIt is possible the great art used by Blifil at this interview would have prevailed on Sophia to have made another man in his circumstances her confident, and to have revealed the whole secret of her heart to him; but she had contracted so ill an opinion of this young gentleman, that she was resolved to place no confidence in him; for simplicity, when set on its guard, is often a match for cunning. Her behaviour to him, therefore, was entirely forced, and indeed such as is generally prescribed to virgins upon the second formal visit from one who is appointed for their husband.\n\nBut though Blifil declared himself to the squire perfectly satisfied with his reception; yet that gentleman, who, in company with his sister, had overheard all, was not so well pleased. He resolved, in pursuance of the advice of the sage lady, to push matters as forward as possible; and addressing himself to his intended son-in-law in the hunting phrase, he cried, after a loud holla, “Follow her, boy, follow her; run in, run in; that's it, honeys. Dead, dead, dead. Never be bashful, nor stand shall I, shall I? Allworthy and I can finish all matters between us this afternoon, and let us ha' the wedding to-morrow.”\n\nBlifil having conveyed the utmost satisfaction into his countenance, answered, “As there is nothing, sir, in this world which I so eagerly desire as an alliance with your family, except my union with the most amiable and deserving Sophia, you may easily imagine how impatient I must be to see myself in possession of my two highest wishes. If I have not therefore importuned you on this head, you will impute it only to my fear of offending the lady, by endeavouring to hurry on so blessed an event faster than a strict compliance with all the rules of decency and decorum will permit. But if, by your interest, sir, she might be induced to dispense with any formalities--”\n\n“Formalities! with a pox!” answered the squire. “Pooh, all stuff and nonsense! I tell thee, she shall ha' thee to-morrow: you will know the world better hereafter, when you come to my age. Women never gi' their consent, man, if they can help it, 'tis not the fashion. If I had stayed for her mother's consent, I might have been a batchelor to this day.--To her, to her, co to her, that's it, you jolly dog. I tell thee shat ha' her to-morrow morning.”\n\nBlifil suffered himself to be overpowered by the forcible rhetoric of the squire; and it being agreed that Western should close with Allworthy that very afternoon, the lover departed home, having first earnestly begged that no violence might be offered to the lady by this haste, in the same manner as a popish inquisitor begs the lay power to do no violence to the heretic delivered over to it, and against whom the church hath passed sentence.\n\nAnd, to say the truth, Blifil had passed sentence against Sophia; for, however pleased he had declared himself to Western with his reception, he was by no means satisfied, unless it was that he was convinced of the hatred and scorn of his mistress: and this had produced no less reciprocal hatred and scorn in him. It may, perhaps, be asked, Why then did he not put an immediate end to all further courtship? I answer, for that very reason, as well as for several others equally good, which we shall now proceed to open to the reader.\n\nThough Mr Blifil was not of the complexion of Jones, nor ready to eat every woman he saw; yet he was far from being destitute of that appetite which is said to be the common property of all animals. With this, he had likewise that distinguishing taste, which serves to direct men in their choice of the object or food of their several appetites; and this taught him to consider Sophia as a most delicious morsel, indeed to regard her with the same desires which an ortolan inspires into the soul of an epicure. Now the agonies which affected the mind of Sophia, rather augmented than impaired her beauty; for her tears added brightness to her eyes, and her breasts rose higher with her sighs. Indeed, no one hath seen beauty in its highest lustre who hath never seen it in distress. Blifil therefore looked on this human ortolan with greater desire than when he viewed her last; nor was his desire at all lessened by the aversion which he discovered in her to himself. On the contrary, this served rather to heighten the pleasure he proposed in rifling her charms, as it added triumph to lust; nay, he had some further views, from obtaining the absolute possession of her person, which we detest too much even to mention; and revenge itself was not without its share in the gratifications which he promised himself. The rivalling poor Jones, and supplanting him in her affections, added another spur to his pursuit, and promised another additional rapture to his enjoyment.\n\nBesides all these views, which to some scrupulous persons may seem to savour too much of malevolence, he had one prospect, which few readers will regard with any great abhorrence. And this was the estate of Mr Western; which was all to be settled on his daughter and her issue; for so extravagant was the affection of that fond parent, that, provided his child would but consent to be miserable with the husband he chose, he cared not at what price he purchased him.\n\nFor these reasons Mr Blifil was so desirous of the match that he intended to deceive Sophia, by pretending love to her; and to deceive her father and his own uncle, by pretending he was beloved by her. In doing this he availed himself of the piety of Thwackum, who held, that if the end proposed was religious (as surely matrimony is), it mattered not how wicked were the means. As to other occasions, he used to apply the philosophy of Square, which taught, that the end was immaterial, so that the means were fair and consistent with moral rectitude. To say truth, there were few occurrences in life on which he could not draw advantage from the precepts of one or other of those great masters.\n\nLittle deceit was indeed necessary to be practised on Mr Western; who thought the inclinations of his daughter of as little consequence as Blifil himself conceived them to be; but as the sentiments of Mr Allworthy were of a very different kind, so it was absolutely necessary to impose on him. In this, however, Blifil was so well assisted by Western, that he succeeded without difficulty; for as Mr Allworthy had been assured by her father that Sophia had a proper affection for Blifil, and that all which he had suspected concerning Jones was entirely false, Blifil had nothing more to do than to confirm these assertions; which he did with such equivocations, that he preserved a salvo for his conscience; and had the satisfaction of conveying a lie to his uncle, without the guilt of telling one. When he was examined touching the inclinations of Sophia by Allworthy, who said, “He would on no account be accessary to forcing a young lady into a marriage contrary to her own will;” he answered, “That the real sentiments of young ladies were very difficult to be understood; that her behaviour to him was full as forward as he wished it, and that if he could believe her father, she had all the affection for him which any lover could desire. As for Jones,” said he, “whom I am loth to call villain, though his behaviour to you, sir, sufficiently justifies the appellation, his own vanity, or perhaps some wicked views, might make him boast of a falsehood; for if there had been any reality in Miss Western's love to him, the greatness of her fortune would never have suffered him to desert her, as you are well informed he hath. Lastly, sir, I promise you I would not myself, for any consideration, no, not for the whole world, consent to marry this young lady, if I was not persuaded she had all the passion for me which I desire she should have.”\n\nThis excellent method of conveying a falsehood with the heart only, without making the tongue guilty of an untruth, by the means of equivocation and imposture, hath quieted the conscience of many a notable deceiver; and yet, when we consider that it is Omniscience on which these endeavour to impose, it may possibly seem capable of affording only a very superficial comfort; and that this artful and refined distinction between communicating a lie, and telling one, is hardly worth the pains it costs them.\n\nAllworthy was pretty well satisfied with what Mr Western and Mr Blifil told him: and the treaty was now, at the end of two days, concluded. Nothing then remained previous to the office of the priest, but the office of the lawyers, which threatened to take up so much time, that Western offered to bind himself by all manner of covenants, rather than defer the happiness of the young couple. Indeed, he was so very earnest and pressing, that an indifferent person might have concluded he was more a principal in this match than he really was; but this eagerness was natural to him on all occasions: and he conducted every scheme he undertook in such a manner, as if the success of that alone was sufficient to constitute the whole happiness of his life.\n\nThe joint importunities of both father and son-in-law would probably have prevailed on Mr Allworthy, who brooked but ill any delay of giving happiness to others, had not Sophia herself prevented it, and taken measures to put a final end to the whole treaty, and to rob both church and law of those taxes which these wise bodies have thought proper to receive from the propagation of the human species in a lawful manner. Of which in the next chapter.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nA strange resolution of Sophia, and a more strange stratagem of Mrs Honour.\n\nThough Mrs Honour was principally attached to her own interest, she was not without some little attachment to Sophia. To say truth, it was very difficult for any one to know that young lady without loving her. She no sooner therefore heard a piece of news, which she imagined to be of great importance to her mistress, than, quite forgetting the anger which she had conceived two days before, at her unpleasant dismission from Sophia's presence, she ran hastily to inform her of the news.\n\nThe beginning of her discourse was as abrupt as her entrance into the room. “O dear ma'am!” says she, “what doth your la'ship think? To be sure I am frightened out of my wits; and yet I thought it my duty to tell your la'ship, though perhaps it may make you angry, for we servants don't always know what will make our ladies angry; for, to be sure, everything is always laid to the charge of a servant. When our ladies are out of humour, to be sure we must be scolded; and to be sure I should not wonder if your la'ship should be out of humour; nay, it must surprize you certainly, ay, and shock you too.”--“Good Honour, let me know it without any longer preface,” says Sophia; “there are few things, I promise you, which will surprize, and fewer which will shock me.”--“Dear ma'am,” answered Honour, “to be sure, I overheard my master talking to parson Supple about getting a licence this very afternoon; and to be sure I heard him say, your la'ship should be married to-morrow morning.” Sophia turned pale at these words, and repeated eagerly, “To-morrow morning!”--“Yes, ma'am,” replied the trusty waiting-woman, “I will take my oath I heard my master say so.”--“Honour,” says Sophia, “you have both surprized and shocked me to such a degree that I have scarce any breath or spirits left. What is to be done in my dreadful situation?”--“I wish I was able to advise your la'ship,” says she. “Do advise me,” cries Sophia; “pray, dear Honour, advise me. Think what you would attempt if it was your own case.”--“Indeed, ma'am,” cries Honour, “I wish your la'ship and I could change situations; that is, I mean without hurting your la'ship; for to be sure I don't wish you so bad as to be a servant; but because that if so be it was my case, I should find no manner of difficulty in it; for, in my poor opinion, young Squire Blifil is a charming, sweet, handsome man.”--“Don't mention such stuff,” cries Sophia. “Such stuff!” repeated Honour; “why, there. Well, to be sure, what's one man's meat is another man's poison, and the same is altogether as true of women.”--“Honour,” says Sophia, “rather than submit to be the wife of that contemptible wretch, I would plunge a dagger into my heart.”--“O lud! ma'am!” answered the other, “I am sure you frighten me out of my wits now. Let me beseech your la'ship not to suffer such wicked thoughts to come into your head. O lud! to be sure I tremble every inch of me. Dear ma'am, consider, that to be denied Christian burial, and to have your corpse buried in the highway, and a stake drove through you, as farmer Halfpenny was served at Ox Cross; and, to be sure, his ghost hath walked there ever since, for several people have seen him. To be sure it can be nothing but the devil which can put such wicked thoughts into the head of anybody; for certainly it is less wicked to hurt all the world than one's own dear self; and so I have heard said by more parsons than one. If your la'ship hath such a violent aversion, and hates the young gentleman so very bad, that you can't bear to think of going into bed to him; for to be sure there may be such antipathies in nature, and one had lieverer touch a toad than the flesh of some people.”--\n\nSophia had been too much wrapt in contemplation to pay any great attention to the foregoing excellent discourse of her maid; interrupting her therefore, without making any answer to it, she said, “Honour, I am come to a resolution. I am determined to leave my father's house this very night; and if you have the friendship for me which you have often professed, you will keep me company.”--“That I will, ma'am, to the world's end,” answered Honour; “but I beg your la'ship to consider the consequence before you undertake any rash action. Where can your la'ship possibly go?”--“There is,” replied Sophia, “a lady of quality in London, a relation of mine, who spent several months with my aunt in the country; during all which time she treated me with great kindness, and expressed so much pleasure in my company, that she earnestly desired my aunt to suffer me to go with her to London. As she is a woman of very great note, I shall easily find her out, and I make no doubt of being very well and kindly received by her.”--“I would not have your la'ship too confident of that,” cries Honour; “for the first lady I lived with used to invite people very earnestly to her house; but if she heard afterwards they were coming, she used to get out of the way. Besides, though this lady would be very glad to see your la'ship, as to be sure anybody would be glad to see your la'ship, yet when she hears your la'ship is run away from my master--” “You are mistaken, Honour,” says Sophia: “she looks upon the authority of a father in a much lower light than I do; for she pressed me violently to go to London with her, and when I refused to go without my father's consent, she laughed me to scorn, called me silly country girl, and said, I should make a pure loving wife, since I could be so dutiful a daughter. So I have no doubt but she will both receive me and protect me too, till my father, finding me out of his power, can be brought to some reason.”\n\n“Well, but, ma'am,” answered Honour, “how doth your la'ship think of making your escape? Where will you get any horses or conveyance? For as for your own horse, as all the servants know a little how matters stand between my master and your la'ship, Robin will be hanged before he will suffer it to go out of the stable without my master's express orders.” “I intend to escape,” said Sophia, “by walking out of the doors when they are open. I thank Heaven my legs are very able to carry me. They have supported me many a long evening”--“Yes, to be sure,” cries Honour, “I will follow your la'ship through the world; but your la'ship had almost as good be alone: for I should not be able to defend you, if any robbers, or other villains, should meet with you. Nay, I should be in as horrible a fright as your la'ship; for to be certain, they would ravish us both. Besides, ma'am, consider how cold the nights are now; we shall be frozen to death.”--“A good brisk pace,” answered Sophia, “will preserve us from the cold; and if you cannot defend me from a villain, Honour, I will defend you; for I will take a pistol with me. There are two always charged in the hall.”--“Dear ma'am, you frighten me more and more,” cries Honour: “sure your la'ship would not venture to fire it off! I had rather run any chance than your la'ship should do that.”--“Why so?” says Sophia, smiling; “would not you, Honour, fire a pistol at any one who should attack your virtue?”--“To be sure, ma'am,” cries Honour, “one's virtue is a dear thing, especially to us poor servants; for it is our livelihood, as a body may say: yet I mortally hate fire-arms; for so many accidents happen by them.”--“Well, well,” says Sophia, “I believe I may ensure your virtue at a very cheap rate, without carrying any arms with us; for I intend to take horses at the very first town we come to, and we shall hardly be attacked in our way thither. Look'ee, Honour, I am resolved to go; and if you will attend me, I promise you I will reward you to the very utmost of my power.”\n\nThis last argument had a stronger effect on Honour than all the preceding. And since she saw her mistress so determined, she desisted from any further dissuasions. They then entered into a debate on ways and means of executing their project. Here a very stubborn difficulty occurred, and this was the removal of their effects, which was much more easily got over by the mistress than by the maid; for when a lady hath once taken a resolution to run to a lover, or to run from him, all obstacles are considered as trifles. But Honour was inspired by no such motive; she had no raptures to expect, nor any terrors to shun; and besides the real value of her clothes, in which consisted a great part of her fortune, she had a capricious fondness for several gowns, and other things; either because they became her, or because they were given her by such a particular person; because she had bought them lately, or because she had had them long; or for some other reasons equally good; so that she could not endure the thoughts of leaving the poor things behind her exposed to the mercy of Western, who, she doubted not, would in his rage make them suffer martyrdom.\n\nThe ingenious Mrs Honour having applied all her oratory to dissuade her mistress from her purpose, when she found her positively determined, at last started the following expedient to remove her clothes, viz., to get herself turned out of doors that very evening. Sophia highly approved this method, but doubted how it might be brought about. “O, ma'am,” cries Honour, “your la'ship may trust that to me; we servants very well know how to obtain this favour of our masters and mistresses; though sometimes, indeed, where they owe us more wages than they can readily pay, they will put up with all our affronts, and will hardly take any warning we can give them; but the squire is none of those; and since your la'ship is resolved upon setting out to-night, I warrant I get discharged this afternoon.” It was then resolved that she should pack up some linen and a night-gown for Sophia, with her own things; and as for all her other clothes, the young lady abandoned them with no more remorse than the sailor feels when he throws over the goods of others, in order to save his own life.\n\nContaining scenes of altercation, of no very uncommon kind.\n\nMrs Honour had scarce sooner parted from her young lady, than something (for I would not, like the old woman in Quevedo, injure the devil by any false accusation, and possibly he might have no hand in it)--but something, I say, suggested itself to her, that by sacrificing Sophia and all her secrets to Mr Western, she might probably make her fortune. Many considerations urged this discovery. The fair prospect of a handsome reward for so great and acceptable a service to the squire, tempted her avarice; and again, the danger of the enterprize she had undertaken; the uncertainty of its success; night, cold, robbers, ravishers, all alarmed her fears. So forcibly did all these operate upon her, that she was almost determined to go directly to the squire, and to lay open the whole affair. She was, however, too upright a judge to decree on one side, before she had heard the other. And here, first, a journey to London appeared very strongly in support of Sophia. She eagerly longed to see a place in which she fancied charms short only of those which a raptured saint imagines in heaven. In the next place, as she knew Sophia to have much more generosity than her master, so her fidelity promised her a greater reward than she could gain by treachery. She then cross-examined all the articles which had raised her fears on the other side, and found, on fairly sifting the matter, that there was very little in them. And now both scales being reduced to a pretty even balance, her love to her mistress being thrown into the scale of her integrity, made that rather preponderate, when a circumstance struck upon her imagination which might have had a dangerous effect, had its whole weight been fairly put into the other scale. This was the length of time which must intervene before Sophia would be able to fulfil her promises; for though she was intitled to her mother's fortune at the death of her father, and to the sum of £3000 left her by an uncle when she came of age; yet these were distant days, and many accidents might prevent the intended generosity of the young lady; whereas the rewards she might expect from Mr Western were immediate. But while she was pursuing this thought the good genius of Sophia, or that which presided over the integrity of Mrs Honour, or perhaps mere chance, sent an accident in her way, which at once preserved her fidelity, and even facilitated the intended business.\n\nMrs Western's maid claimed great superiority over Mrs Honour on several accounts. First, her birth was higher; for her great-grandmother by the mother's side was a cousin, not far removed, to an Irish peer. Secondly, her wages were greater. And lastly, she had been at London, and had of consequence seen more of the world. She had always behaved, therefore, to Mrs Honour with that reserve, and had always exacted of her those marks of distinction, which every order of females preserves and requires in conversation with those of an inferior order. Now as Honour did not at all times agree with this doctrine, but would frequently break in upon the respect which the other demanded, Mrs Western's maid was not at all pleased with her company; indeed, she earnestly longed to return home to the house of her mistress, where she domineered at will over all the other servants. She had been greatly, therefore, disappointed in the morning, when Mrs Western had changed her mind on the very point of departure; and had been in what is vulgarly called a glouting humour ever since.\n\nIn this humour, which was none of the sweetest, she came into the room where Honour was debating with herself in the manner we have above related. Honour no sooner saw her, than she addressed her in the following obliging phrase: “Soh, madam, I find we are to have the pleasure of your company longer, which I was afraid the quarrel between my master and your lady would have robbed us of.”--“I don't know, madam,” answered the other, “what you mean by we and us. I assure you I do not look on any of the servants in this house to be proper company for me. I am company, I hope, for their betters every day in the week. I do not speak on your account, Mrs Honour; for you are a civilized young woman; and when you have seen a little more of the world, I should not be ashamed to walk with you in St James's Park.”--“Hoity toity!” cries Honour, “madam is in her airs, I protest. Mrs Honour, forsooth! sure, madam, you might call me by my sir-name; for though my lady calls me Honour, I have a sir-name as well as other folks. Ashamed to walk with me, quotha! marry, as good as yourself, I hope.”--“Since you make such a return to my civility,” said the other, “I must acquaint you, Mrs Honour, that you are not so good as me. In the country, indeed, one is obliged to take up with all kind of trumpery; but in town I visit none but the women of women of quality. Indeed, Mrs Honour, there is some difference, I hope, between you and me.”--“I hope so too,” answered Honour: “there is some difference in our ages, and--I think in our persons.” Upon speaking which last words, she strutted by Mrs Western's maid with the most provoking air of contempt; turning up her nose, tossing her head, and violently brushing the hoop of her competitor with her own. The other lady put on one of her most malicious sneers, and said, “Creature! you are below my anger; and it is beneath me to give ill words to such an audacious saucy trollop; but, hussy, I must tell you, your breeding shows the meanness of your birth as well as of your education; and both very properly qualify you to be the mean serving-woman of a country girl.”--“Don't abuse my lady,” cries Honour: “I won't take that of you; she's as much better than yours as she is younger, and ten thousand times more handsomer.”\n\nHere ill luck, or rather good luck, sent Mrs Western to see her maid in tears, which began to flow plentifully at her approach; and of which being asked the reason by her mistress, she presently acquainted her that her tears were occasioned by the rude treatment of that creature there--meaning Honour. “And, madam,” continued she, “I could have despised all she said to me; but she hath had the audacity to affront your ladyship, and to call you ugly--Yes, madam, she called you ugly old cat to my face. I could not bear to hear your ladyship called ugly.”--“Why do you repeat her impudence so often?” said Mrs Western. And then turning to Mrs Honour, she asked her “How she had the assurance to mention her name with disrespect?”--“Disrespect, madam!” answered Honour; “I never mentioned your name at all: I said somebody was not as handsome as my mistress, and to be sure you know that as well as I.”--“Hussy,” replied the lady, “I will make such a saucy trollop as yourself know that I am not a proper subject of your discourse. And if my brother doth not discharge you this moment, I will never sleep in his house again. I will find him out, and have you discharged this moment.”--“Discharged!” cries Honour; “and suppose I am: there are more places in the world than one. Thank Heaven, good servants need not want places; and if you turn away all who do not think you handsome, you will want servants very soon; let me tell you that.”\n\nMrs Western spoke, or rather thundered, in answer; but as she was hardly articulate, we cannot be very certain of the identical words; we shall therefore omit inserting a speech which at best would not greatly redound to her honour. She then departed in search of her brother, with a countenance so full of rage, that she resembled one of the furies rather than a human creature.\n\nThe two chambermaids being again left alone, began a second bout at altercation, which soon produced a combat of a more active kind. In this the victory belonged to the lady of inferior rank, but not without some loss of blood, of hair, and of lawn and muslin.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\nThe wise demeanour of Mr Western in the character of a magistrate. A hint to justices of peace, concerning the necessary qualifications of a clerk; with extraordinary instances of paternal madness and filial affection.\n\nLogicians sometimes prove too much by an argument, and politicians often overreach themselves in a scheme. Thus had it like to have happened to Mrs Honour, who, instead of recovering the rest of her clothes, had like to have stopped even those she had on her back from escaping; for the squire no sooner heard of her having abused his sister, than he swore twenty oaths he would send her to Bridewell.\n\nMrs Western was a very good-natured woman, and ordinarily of a forgiving temper. She had lately remitted the trespass of a stage-coachman, who had overturned her post-chaise into a ditch; nay, she had even broken the law, in refusing to prosecute a highwayman who had robbed her, not only of a sum of money, but of her ear-rings; at the same time d--ning her, and saying, “Such handsome b--s as you don't want jewels to set them off, and be d--n'd to you.” But now, so uncertain are our tempers, and so much do we at different times differ from ourselves, she would hear of no mitigation; nor could all the affected penitence of Honour, nor all the entreaties of Sophia for her own servant, prevail with her to desist from earnestly desiring her brother to execute justiceship (for it was indeed a syllable more than justice) on the wench.\n\nBut luckily the clerk had a qualification, which no clerk to a justice of peace ought ever to be without, namely, some understanding in the law of this realm. He therefore whispered in the ear of the justice that he would exceed his authority by committing the girl to Bridewell, as there had been no attempt to break the peace; “for I am afraid, sir,” says he, “you cannot legally commit any one to Bridewell only for ill-breeding.”\n\nIn matters of high importance, particularly in cases relating to the game, the justice was not always attentive to these admonitions of his clerk; for, indeed, in executing the laws under that head, many justices of peace suppose they have a large discretionary power, by virtue of which, under the notion of searching for and taking away engines for the destruction of the game, they often commit trespasses, and sometimes felony, at their pleasure.\n\nBut this offence was not of quite so high a nature, nor so dangerous to the society. Here, therefore, the justice behaved with some attention to the advice of his clerk; for, in fact, he had already had two informations exhibited against him in the King's Bench, and had no curiosity to try a third.\n\nThe squire, therefore, putting on a most wise and significant countenance, after a preface of several hums and hahs, told his sister, that upon more mature deliberation, he was of opinion, that “as there was no breaking up of the peace, such as the law,” says he, “calls breaking open a door, or breaking a hedge, or breaking a head, or any such sort of breaking, the matter did not amount to a felonious kind of a thing, nor trespasses, nor damages, and, therefore, there was no punishment in the law for it.”\n\nMrs Western said, “she knew the law much better; that she had known servants very severely punished for affronting their masters;” and then named a certain justice of the peace in London, “who,” she said, “would commit a servant to Bridewell at any time when a master or mistress desired it.”\n\n“Like enough,” cries the squire; “it may be so in London; but the law is different in the country.” Here followed a very learned dispute between the brother and sister concerning the law, which we would insert, if we imagined many of our readers could understand it. This was, however, at length referred by both parties to the clerk, who decided it in favour of the magistrate; and Mrs Western was, in the end, obliged to content herself with the satisfaction of having Honour turned away; to which Sophia herself very readily and cheerfully consented.\n\nThus Fortune, after having diverted herself, according to custom, with two or three frolicks, at last disposed all matters to the advantage of our heroine; who indeed succeeded admirably well in her deceit, considering it was the first she had ever practised. And, to say the truth, I have often concluded, that the honest part of mankind would be much too hard for the knavish, if they could bring themselves to incur the guilt, or thought it worth their while to take the trouble.\n\nHonour acted her part to the utmost perfection. She no sooner saw herself secure from all danger of Bridewell, a word which had raised most horrible ideas in her mind, than she resumed those airs which her terrors before had a little abated; and laid down her place, with as much affectation of content, and indeed of contempt, as was ever practised at the resignation of places of much greater importance. If the reader pleases, therefore, we chuse rather to say she resigned--which hath, indeed, been always held a synonymous expression with being turned out, or turned away.\n\nMr Western ordered her to be very expeditious in packing; for his sister declared she would not sleep another night under the same roof with so impudent a slut. To work therefore she went, and that so earnestly, that everything was ready early in the evening; when, having received her wages, away packed bag and baggage, to the great satisfaction of every one, but of none more than of Sophia; who, having appointed her maid to meet her at a certain place not far from the house, exactly at the dreadful and ghostly hour of twelve, began to prepare for her own departure.\n\nBut first she was obliged to give two painful audiences, the one to her aunt, and the other to her father. In these Mrs Western herself began to talk to her in a more peremptory stile than before: but her father treated her in so violent and outrageous a manner, that he frightened her into an affected compliance with his will; which so highly pleased the good squire, that he changed his frowns into smiles, and his menaces into promises: he vowed his whole soul was wrapt in hers; that her consent (for so he construed the words, “You know, sir, I must not, nor can, refuse to obey any absolute command of yours”) had made him the happiest of mankind. He then gave her a large bank-bill to dispose of in any trinkets she pleased, and kissed and embraced her in the fondest manner, while tears of joy trickled from those eyes which a few moments before had darted fire and rage against the dear object of all his affection.\n\nInstances of this behaviour in parents are so common, that the reader, I doubt not, will be very little astonished at the whole conduct of Mr Western. If he should, I own I am not able to account for it; since that he loved his daughter most tenderly, is, I think, beyond dispute. So indeed have many others, who have rendered their children most completely miserable by the same conduct; which, though it is almost universal in parents, hath always appeared to me to be the most unaccountable of all the absurdities which ever entered into the brain of that strange prodigious creature man.\n\nThe latter part of Mr Western's behaviour had so strong an effect on the tender heart of Sophia, that it suggested a thought to her, which not all the sophistry of her politic aunt, nor all the menaces of her father, had ever once brought into her head. She reverenced her father so piously, and loved him so passionately, that she had scarce ever felt more pleasing sensations, than what arose from the share she frequently had of contributing to his amusement, and sometimes, perhaps, to higher gratifications; for he never could contain the delight of hearing her commended, which he had the satisfaction of hearing almost every day of her life. The idea, therefore, of the immense happiness she should convey to her father by her consent to this match, made a strong impression on her mind. Again, the extreme piety of such an act of obedience worked very forcibly, as she had a very deep sense of religion. Lastly, when she reflected how much she herself was to suffer, being indeed to become little less than a sacrifice, or a martyr, to filial love and duty, she felt an agreeable tickling in a certain little passion, which though it bears no immediate affinity either to religion or virtue, is often so kind as to lend great assistance in executing the purposes of both.\n\nSophia was charmed with the contemplation of so heroic an action, and began to compliment herself with much premature flattery, when Cupid, who lay hid in her muff, suddenly crept out, and like Punchinello in a puppet-show, kicked all out before him. In truth (for we scorn to deceive our reader, or to vindicate the character of our heroine by ascribing her actions to supernatural impulse) the thoughts of her beloved Jones, and some hopes (however distant) in which he was very particularly concerned, immediately destroyed all which filial love, piety, and pride had, with their joint endeavours, been labouring to bring about.\n\nBut before we proceed any farther with Sophia, we must now look back to Mr Jones.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nContaining several matters, natural enough perhaps, but low.\nThe reader will be pleased to remember, that we left Mr Jones, in the beginning of this book, on his road to Bristol; being determined to seek his fortune at sea, or rather, indeed, to fly away from his fortune on shore.\n\nIt happened (a thing not very unusual), that the guide who undertook to conduct him on his way, was unluckily unacquainted with the road; so that having missed his right track, and being ashamed to ask information, he rambled about backwards and forwards till night came on, and it began to grow dark. Jones suspecting what had happened, acquainted the guide with his apprehensions; but he insisted on it, that they were in the right road, and added, it would be very strange if he should not know the road to Bristol; though, in reality, it would have been much stranger if he had known it, having never past through it in his life before.\n\nJones had not such implicit faith in his guide, but that on their arrival at a village he inquired of the first fellow he saw, whether they were in the road to Bristol. “Whence did you come?” cries the fellow. “No matter,” says Jones, a little hastily; “I want to know if this be the road to Bristol?”--“The road to Bristol!” cries the fellow, scratching his head: “why, measter, I believe you will hardly get to Bristol this way to-night.”--“Prithee, friend, then,” answered Jones, “do tell us which is the way.”--“Why, measter,” cries the fellow, “you must be come out of your road the Lord knows whither; for thick way goeth to Glocester.”--“Well, and which way goes to Bristol?” said Jones. “Why, you be going away from Bristol,” answered the fellow. “Then,” said Jones, “we must go back again?”--“Ay, you must,” said the fellow. “Well, and when we come back to the top of the hill, which way must we take?”--“Why, you must keep the strait road.”--“But I remember there are two roads, one to the right and the other to the left.”--“Why, you must keep the right-hand road, and then gu strait vorwards; only remember to turn vurst to your right, and then to your left again, and then to your right, and that brings you to the squire's; and then you must keep strait vorwards, and turn to the left.”\n\nAnother fellow now came up, and asked which way the gentlemen were going; of which being informed by Jones, he first scratched his head, and then leaning upon a pole he had in his hand, began to tell him, “That he must keep the right-hand road for about a mile, or a mile and a half, or such a matter, and then he must turn short to the left, which would bring him round by Measter Jin Bearnes's.”--“But which is Mr John Bearnes's?” says Jones. “O Lord!” cries the fellow, “why, don't you know Measter Jin Bearnes? Whence then did you come?”\n\nThese two fellows had almost conquered the patience of Jones, when a plain well-looking man (who was indeed a Quaker) accosted him thus: “Friend, I perceive thou hast lost thy way; and if thou wilt take my advice, thou wilt not attempt to find it to-night. It is almost dark, and the road is difficult to hit; besides, there have been several robberies committed lately between this and Bristol. Here is a very creditable good house just by, where thou may'st find good entertainment for thyself and thy cattle till morning.” Jones, after a little persuasion, agreed to stay in this place till the morning, and was conducted by his friend to the public-house.\n\nThe landlord, who was a very civil fellow, told Jones, “He hoped he would excuse the badness of his accommodation; for that his wife was gone from home, and had locked up almost everything, and carried the keys along with her.” Indeed the fact was, that a favourite daughter of hers was just married, and gone that morning home with her husband; and that she and her mother together had almost stript the poor man of all his goods, as well as money; for though he had several children, this daughter only, who was the mother's favourite, was the object of her consideration; and to the humour of this one child she would with pleasure have sacrificed all the rest, and her husband into the bargain.\n\nThough Jones was very unfit for any kind of company, and would have preferred being alone, yet he could not resist the importunities of the honest Quaker; who was the more desirous of sitting with him, from having remarked the melancholy which appeared both in his countenance and behaviour; and which the poor Quaker thought his conversation might in some measure relieve.\n\nAfter they had past some time together, in such a manner that my honest friend might have thought himself at one of his silent meetings, the Quaker began to be moved by some spirit or other, probably that of curiosity, and said, “Friend, I perceive some sad disaster hath befallen thee; but pray be of comfort. Perhaps thou hast lost a friend. If so, thou must consider we are all mortal. And why shouldst thou grieve, when thou knowest thy grief will do thy friend no good? We are all born to affliction. I myself have my sorrows as well as thee, and most probably greater sorrows. Though I have a clear estate of £100 a year, which is as much as I want, and I have a conscience, I thank the Lord, void of offence; my constitution is sound and strong, and there is no man can demand a debt of me, nor accuse me of an injury; yet, friend, I should be concerned to think thee as miserable as myself.”\n\nHere the Quaker ended with a deep sigh; and Jones presently answered, “I am very sorry, sir, for your unhappiness, whatever is the occasion of it.”--“Ah! friend,” replied the Quaker, “one only daughter is the occasion; one who was my greatest delight upon earth, and who within this week is run away from me, and is married against my consent. I had provided her a proper match, a sober man and one of substance; but she, forsooth, would chuse for herself, and away she is gone with a young fellow not worth a groat. If she had been dead, as I suppose thy friend is, I should have been happy.”--“That is very strange, sir,” said Jones. “Why, would it not be better for her to be dead, than to be a beggar?” replied the Quaker: “for, as I told you, the fellow is not worth a groat; and surely she cannot expect that I shall ever give her a shilling. No, as she hath married for love, let her live on love if she can; let her carry her love to market, and see whether any one will change it into silver, or even into halfpence.”--“You know your own concerns best, sir,” said Jones. “It must have been,” continued the Quaker, “a long premeditated scheme to cheat me: for they have known one another from their infancy; and I always preached to her against love, and told her a thousand times over it was all folly and wickedness. Nay, the cunning slut pretended to hearken to me, and to despise all wantonness of the flesh; and yet at last broke out at a window two pair of stairs: for I began, indeed, a little to suspect her, and had locked her up carefully, intending the very next morning to have married her up to my liking. But she disappointed me within a few hours, and escaped away to the lover of her own chusing; who lost no time, for they were married and bedded and all within an hour. But it shall be the worst hour's work for them both that ever they did; for they may starve, or beg, or steal together, for me. I will never give either of them a farthing.” Here Jones starting up cried, “I really must be excused: I wish you would leave me.”--“Come, come, friend,” said the Quaker, “don't give way to concern. You see there are other people miserable besides yourself.”--“I see there are madmen, and fools, and villains in the world,” cries Jones. “But let me give you a piece of advice: send for your daughter and son-in-law home, and don't be yourself the only cause of misery to one you pretend to love.”--“Send for her and her husband home!” cries the Quaker loudly; “I would sooner send for the two greatest enemies I have in the world!”--“Well, go home yourself, or where you please,” said Jones, “for I will sit no longer in such company.”--“Nay, friend,” answered the Quaker, “I scorn to impose my company on any one.” He then offered to pull money from his pocket, but Jones pushed him with some violence out of the room.\n\nThe subject of the Quaker's discourse had so deeply affected Jones, that he stared very wildly all the time he was speaking. This the Quaker had observed, and this, added to the rest of his behaviour, inspired honest Broadbrim with a conceit, that his companion was in reality out of his senses. Instead of resenting the affront, therefore, the Quaker was moved with compassion for his unhappy circumstances; and having communicated his opinion to the landlord, he desired him to take great care of his guest, and to treat him with the highest civility.\n\n“Indeed,” says the landlord, “I shall use no such civility towards him; for it seems, for all his laced waistcoat there, he is no more a gentleman than myself, but a poor parish bastard, bred up at a great squire's about thirty miles off, and now turned out of doors (not for any good to be sure). I shall get him out of my house as soon as possible. If I do lose my reckoning, the first loss is always the best. It is not above a year ago that I lost a silver spoon.”\n\n“What dost thou talk of a parish bastard, Robin?” answered the Quaker. “Thou must certainly be mistaken in thy man.”\n\n“Not at all,” replied Robin; “the guide, who knows him very well, told it me.” For, indeed, the guide had no sooner taken his place at the kitchen fire, than he acquainted the whole company with all he knew or had ever heard concerning Jones.\n\nThe Quaker was no sooner assured by this fellow of the birth and low fortune of Jones, than all compassion for him vanished; and the honest plain man went home fired with no less indignation than a duke would have felt at receiving an affront from such a person.\n\nThe landlord himself conceived an equal disdain for his guest; so that when Jones rung the bell in order to retire to bed, he was acquainted that he could have no bed there. Besides disdain of the mean condition of his guest, Robin entertained violent suspicion of his intentions, which were, he supposed, to watch some favourable opportunity of robbing the house. In reality, he might have been very well eased of these apprehensions, by the prudent precautions of his wife and daughter, who had already removed everything which was not fixed to the freehold; but he was by nature suspicious, and had been more particularly so since the loss of his spoon. In short, the dread of being robbed totally absorbed the comfortable consideration that he had nothing to lose.\n\nJones being assured that he could have no bed, very contentedly betook himself to a great chair made with rushes, when sleep, which had lately shunned his company in much better apartments, generously paid him a visit in his humble cell.\n\nAs for the landlord, he was prevented by his fears from retiring to rest. He returned therefore to the kitchen fire, whence he could survey the only door which opened into the parlour, or rather hole, where Jones was seated; and as for the window to that room, it was impossible for any creature larger than a cat to have made his escape through it.\n\nThe adventure of a company of soldiers.\n\nThe landlord having taken his seat directly opposite to the door of the parlour, determined to keep guard there the whole night. The guide and another fellow remained long on duty with him, though they neither knew his suspicions, nor had any of their own. The true cause of their watching did, indeed, at length, put an end to it; for this was no other than the strength and goodness of the beer, of which having tippled a very large quantity, they grew at first very noisy and vociferous, and afterwards fell both asleep.\n\nBut it was not in the power of liquor to compose the fears of Robin. He continued still waking in his chair, with his eyes fixed stedfastly on the door which led into the apartment of Mr Jones, till a violent thundering at his outward gate called him from his seat, and obliged him to open it; which he had no sooner done, than his kitchen was immediately full of gentlemen in red coats, who all rushed upon him in as tumultuous a manner as if they intended to take his little castle by storm.\n\nThe landlord was now forced from his post to furnish his numerous guests with beer, which they called for with great eagerness; and upon his second or third return from the cellar, he saw Mr Jones standing before the fire in the midst of the soldiers; for it may easily be believed, that the arrival of so much good company should put an end to any sleep, unless that from which we are to be awakened only by the last trumpet.\n\nThe company having now pretty well satisfied their thirst, nothing remained but to pay the reckoning, a circumstance often productive of much mischief and discontent among the inferior rank of gentry, who are apt to find great difficulty in assessing the sum, with exact regard to distributive justice, which directs that every man shall pay according to the quantity which he drinks. This difficulty occurred upon the present occasion; and it was the greater, as some gentlemen had, in their extreme hurry, marched off, after their first draught, and had entirely forgot to contribute anything towards the said reckoning.\n\nA violent dispute now arose, in which every word may be said to have been deposed upon oath; for the oaths were at least equal to all the other words spoken. In this controversy the whole company spoke together, and every man seemed wholly bent to extenuate the sum which fell to his share; so that the most probable conclusion which could be foreseen was, that a large portion of the reckoning would fall to the landlord's share to pay, or (what is much the same thing) would remain unpaid.\n\nAll this while Mr Jones was engaged in conversation with the serjeant; for that officer was entirely unconcerned in the present dispute, being privileged by immemorial custom from all contribution.\n\nThe dispute now grew so very warm that it seemed to draw towards a military decision, when Jones, stepping forward, silenced all their clamours at once, by declaring that he would pay the whole reckoning, which indeed amounted to no more than three shillings and fourpence.\n\nThis declaration procured Jones the thanks and applause of the whole company. The terms honourable, noble, and worthy gentleman, resounded through the room; nay, my landlord himself began to have a better opinion of him, and almost to disbelieve the account which the guide had given.\n\nThe serjeant had informed Mr Jones that they were marching against the rebels, and expected to be commanded by the glorious Duke of Cumberland. By which the reader may perceive (a circumstance which we have not thought necessary to communicate before) that this was the very time when the late rebellion was at the highest; and indeed the banditti were now marched into England, intending, as it was thought, to fight the king's forces, and to attempt pushing forward to the metropolis.\n\nJones had some heroic ingredients in his composition, and was a hearty well-wisher to the glorious cause of liberty, and of the Protestant religion. It is no wonder, therefore, that in circumstances which would have warranted a much more romantic and wild undertaking, it should occur to him to serve as a volunteer in this expedition.\n\nOur commanding officer had said all in his power to encourage and promote this good disposition, from the first moment he had been acquainted with it. He now proclaimed the noble resolution aloud, which was received with great pleasure by the whole company, who all cried out, “God bless King George and your honour;” and then added, with many oaths, “We will stand by you both to the last drops of our blood.”\n\nThe gentleman who had been all night tippling at the alehouse, was prevailed on by some arguments which a corporal had put into his hands, to undertake the same expedition. And now the portmanteau belonging to Mr Jones being put up in the baggage-cart, the forces were about to move forwards; when the guide, stepping up to Jones, said, “Sir, I hope you will consider that the horses have been kept out all night, and we have travelled a great ways out of our way.” Jones was surprized at the impudence of this demand, and acquainted the soldiers with the merits of his cause, who were all unanimous in condemning the guide for his endeavours to put upon a gentleman. Some said, he ought to be tied neck and heels; others that he deserved to run the gantlope; and the serjeant shook his cane at him, and wished he had him under his command, swearing heartily he would make an example of him.\n\nJones contented himself however with a negative punishment, and walked off with his new comrades, leaving the guide to the poor revenge of cursing and reviling him; in which latter the landlord joined, saying, “Ay, ay, he is a pure one, I warrant you. A pretty gentleman, indeed, to go for a soldier! He shall wear a laced wastecoat truly. It is an old proverb and a true one, all is not gold that glisters. I am glad my house is well rid of him.”\n\nAll that day the serjeant and the young soldier marched together; and the former, who was an arch fellow, told the latter many entertaining stories of his campaigns, though in reality he had never made any; for he was but lately come into the service, and had, by his own dexterity, so well ingratiated himself with his officers, that he had promoted himself to a halberd; chiefly indeed by his merit in recruiting, in which he was most excellently well skilled.\n\nMuch mirth and festivity passed among the soldiers during their march. In which the many occurrences that had passed at their last quarters were remembered, and every one, with great freedom, made what jokes he pleased on his officers, some of which were of the coarser kind, and very near bordering on scandal. This brought to our heroe's mind the custom which he had read of among the Greeks and Romans, of indulging, on certain festivals and solemn occasions, the liberty to slaves, of using an uncontrouled freedom of speech towards their masters.\n\nOur little army, which consisted of two companies of foot, were now arrived at the place where they were to halt that evening. The serjeant then acquainted his lieutenant, who was the commanding officer, that they had picked up two fellows in that day's march, one of which, he said, was as fine a man as ever he saw (meaning the tippler), for that he was near six feet, well proportioned, and strongly limbed; and the other (meaning Jones) would do well enough for the rear rank.\n\nThe new soldiers were now produced before the officer, who having examined the six-feet man, he being first produced, came next to survey Jones: at the first sight of whom, the lieutenant could not help showing some surprize; for besides that he was very well dressed, and was naturally genteel, he had a remarkable air of dignity in his look, which is rarely seen among the vulgar, and is indeed not inseparably annexed to the features of their superiors.\n\n“Sir,” said the lieutenant, “my serjeant informed me that you are desirous of enlisting in the company I have at present under my command; if so, sir, we shall very gladly receive a gentleman who promises to do much honour to the company by bearing arms in it.”\n\nJones answered: “That he had not mentioned anything of enlisting himself; that he was most zealously attached to the glorious cause for which they were going to fight, and was very desirous of serving as a volunteer;” concluding with some compliments to the lieutenant, and expressing the great satisfaction he should have in being under his command.\n\nThe lieutenant returned his civility, commended his resolution, shook him by the hand, and invited him to dine with himself and the rest of the officers.\n\nThe adventure of a company of officers.\nThe lieutenant, whom we mentioned in the preceding chapter, and who commanded this party, was now near sixty years of age. He had entered very young into the army, and had served in the capacity of an ensign at the battle of Tannieres; here he had received two wounds, and had so well distinguished himself, that he was by the Duke of Marlborough advanced to be a lieutenant, immediately after that battle.\n\nIn this commission he had continued ever since, viz., near forty years; during which time he had seen vast numbers preferred over his head, and had now the mortification to be commanded by boys, whose fathers were at nurse when he first entered into the service.\n\nNor was this ill success in his profession solely owing to his having no friends among the men in power. He had the misfortune to incur the displeasure of his colonel, who for many years continued in the command of this regiment. Nor did he owe the implacable ill-will which this man bore him to any neglect or deficiency as an officer, nor indeed to any fault in himself; but solely to the indiscretion of his wife, who was a very beautiful woman, and who, though she was remarkably fond of her husband, would not purchase his preferment at the expense of certain favours which the colonel required of her.\n\nThe poor lieutenant was more peculiarly unhappy in this, that while he felt the effects of the enmity of his colonel, he neither knew, nor suspected, that he really bore him any; for he could not suspect an ill-will for which he was not conscious of giving any cause; and his wife, fearing what her husband's nice regard to his honour might have occasioned, contented herself with preserving her virtue without enjoying the triumphs of her conquest.\n\nThis unfortunate officer (for so I think he may be called) had many good qualities besides his merit in his profession; for he was a religious, honest, good-natured man; and had behaved so well in his command, that he was highly esteemed and beloved not only by the soldiers of his own company, but by the whole regiment.\n\nThe other officers who marched with him were a French lieutenant, who had been long enough out of France to forget his own language, but not long enough in England to learn ours, so that he really spoke no language at all, and could barely make himself understood on the most ordinary occasions. There were likewise two ensigns, both very young fellows; one of whom had been bred under an attorney, and the other was son to the wife of a nobleman's butler.\n\nAs soon as dinner was ended, Jones informed the company of the merriment which had passed among the soldiers upon their march; “and yet,” says he, “notwithstanding all their vociferation, I dare swear they will behave more like Grecians than Trojans when they come to the enemy.”--“Grecians and Trojans!” says one of the ensigns, “who the devil are they? I have heard of all the troops in Europe, but never of any such as these.”\n\n“Don't pretend to more ignorance than you have, Mr Northerton,” said the worthy lieutenant. “I suppose you have heard of the Greeks and Trojans, though perhaps you never read Pope's Homer; who, I remember, now the gentleman mentions it, compares the march of the Trojans to the cackling of geese, and greatly commends the silence of the Grecians. And upon my honour there is great justice in the cadet's observation.”\n\n“Begar, me remember dem ver well,” said the French lieutenant: “me ave read them at school in dans Madam Daciere, des Greek, des Trojan, dey fight for von woman--ouy, ouy, me ave read all dat.”\n\n“D--n Homo with all my heart,” says Northerton; “I have the marks of him on my a-- yet. There's Thomas, of our regiment, always carries a Homo in his pocket; d--n me, if ever I come at it, if I don't burn it. And there's Corderius, another d--n'd son of a whore, that hath got me many a flogging.”\n\n“Then you have been at school, Mr Northerton?” said the lieutenant.\n\n“Ay, d--n me, have I,” answered he; “the devil take my father for sending me thither! The old put wanted to make a parson of me, but d--n me, thinks I to myself, I'll nick you there, old cull; the devil a smack of your nonsense shall you ever get into me. There's Jemmy Oliver, of our regiment, he narrowly escaped being a pimp too, and that would have been a thousand pities; for d--n me if he is not one of the prettiest fellows in the whole world; but he went farther than I with the old cull, for Jimmey can neither write nor read.”\n\n“You give your friend a very good character,” said the lieutenant, “and a very deserved one, I dare say. But prithee, Northerton, leave off that foolish as well as wicked custom of swearing; for you are deceived, I promise you, if you think there is wit or politeness in it. I wish, too, you would take my advice, and desist from abusing the clergy. Scandalous names, and reflections cast on any body of men, must be always unjustifiable; but especially so, when thrown on so sacred a function; for to abuse the body is to abuse the function itself; and I leave to you to judge how inconsistent such behaviour is in men who are going to fight in defence of the Protestant religion.”\n\nMr Adderly, which was the name of the other ensign, had sat hitherto kicking his heels and humming a tune, without seeming to listen to the discourse; he now answered, “O, Monsieur, on ne parle pas de la religion dans la guerre.”--“Well said, Jack,” cries Northerton: “if la religion was the only matter, the parsons should fight their own battles for me.”\n\n“I don't know, gentlemen,” said Jones, “what may be your opinion; but I think no man can engage in a nobler cause than that of his religion; and I have observed, in the little I have read of history, that no soldiers have fought so bravely as those who have been inspired with a religious zeal: for my own part, though I love my king and country, I hope, as well as any man in it, yet the Protestant interest is no small motive to my becoming a volunteer in the cause.”\n\nNortherton now winked on Adderly, and whispered to him slily, “Smoke the prig, Adderly, smoke him.” Then turning to Jones, said to him, “I am very glad, sir, you have chosen our regiment to be a volunteer in; for if our parson should at any time take a cup too much, I find you can supply his place. I presume, sir, you have been at the university; may I crave the favour to know what college?”\n\n“Sir,” answered Jones, “so far from having been at the university, I have even had the advantage of yourself, for I was never at school.”\n\n“I presumed,” cries the ensign, “only upon the information of your great learning.”--“Oh! sir,” answered Jones, “it is as possible for a man to know something without having been at school, as it is to have been at school and to know nothing.”\n\n“Well said, young volunteer,” cries the lieutenant. “Upon my word, Northerton, you had better let him alone; for he will be too hard for you.”\n\nNortherton did not very well relish the sarcasm of Jones; but he thought the provocation was scarce sufficient to justify a blow, or a rascal, or scoundrel, which were the only repartees that suggested themselves. He was, therefore, silent at present; but resolved to take the first opportunity of returning the jest by abuse.\n\nIt now came to the turn of Mr Jones to give a toast, as it is called; who could not refrain from mentioning his dear Sophia. This he did the more readily, as he imagined it utterly impossible that any one present should guess the person he meant.\n\nBut the lieutenant, who was the toast-master, was not contented with Sophia only. He said, he must have her sir-name; upon which Jones hesitated a little, and presently after named Miss Sophia Western. Ensign Northerton declared he would not drink her health in the same round with his own toast, unless somebody would vouch for her. “I knew one Sophy Western,” says he, “that was lain with by half the young fellows at Bath; and perhaps this is the same woman.” Jones very solemnly assured him of the contrary; asserting that the young lady he named was one of great fashion and fortune. “Ay, ay,” says the ensign, “and so she is: d--n me, it is the same woman; and I'll hold half a dozen of Burgundy, Tom French of our regiment brings her into company with us at any tavern in Bridges-street.” He then proceeded to describe her person exactly (for he had seen her with her aunt), and concluded with saying, “that her father had a great estate in Somersetshire.”\n\nThe tenderness of lovers can ill brook the least jesting with the names of their mistresses. However, Jones, though he had enough of the lover and of the heroe too in his disposition, did not resent these slanders as hastily as, perhaps, he ought to have done. To say the truth, having seen but little of this kind of wit, he did not readily understand it, and for a long time imagined Mr Northerton had really mistaken his charmer for some other. But now, turning to the ensign with a stern aspect, he said, “Pray, sir, chuse some other subject for your wit; for I promise you I will bear no jesting with this lady's character.” “Jesting!” cries the other, “d--n me if ever I was more in earnest in my life. Tom French of our regiment had both her and her aunt at Bath.” “Then I must tell you in earnest,” cries Jones, “that you are one of the most impudent rascals upon earth.”\n\nHe had no sooner spoken these words, than the ensign, together with a volley of curses, discharged a bottle full at the head of Jones, which hitting him a little above the right temple, brought him instantly to the ground.\n\nThe conqueror perceiving the enemy to lie motionless before him, and blood beginning to flow pretty plentifully from his wound, began now to think of quitting the field of battle, where no more honour was to be gotten; but the lieutenant interposed, by stepping before the door, and thus cut off his retreat.\n\nNortherton was very importunate with the lieutenant for his liberty; urging the ill consequences of his stay, asking him, what he could have done less? “Zounds!” says he, “I was but in jest with the fellow. I never heard any harm of Miss Western in my life.” “Have not you?” said the lieutenant; “then you richly deserve to be hanged, as well for making such jests, as for using such a weapon: you are my prisoner, sir; nor shall you stir from hence till a proper guard comes to secure you.”\n\nSuch an ascendant had our lieutenant over this ensign, that all that fervency of courage which had levelled our poor heroe with the floor, would scarce have animated the said ensign to have drawn his sword against the lieutenant, had he then had one dangling at his side: but all the swords being hung up in the room, were, at the very beginning of the fray, secured by the French officer. So that Mr Northerton was obliged to attend the final issue of this affair.\n\nThe French gentleman and Mr Adderly, at the desire of their commanding officer, had raised up the body of Jones, but as they could perceive but little (if any) sign of life in him, they again let him fall, Adderly damning him for having blooded his wastecoat; and the Frenchman declaring, “Begar, me no tush the Engliseman de mort: me have heard de Englise ley, law, what you call, hang up de man dat tush him last.”\n\nWhen the good lieutenant applied himself to the door, he applied himself likewise to the bell; and the drawer immediately attending, he dispatched him for a file of musqueteers and a surgeon. These commands, together with the drawer's report of what he had himself seen, not only produced the soldiers, but presently drew up the landlord of the house, his wife, and servants, and, indeed, every one else who happened at that time to be in the inn.\n\nTo describe every particular, and to relate the whole conversation of the ensuing scene, is not within my power, unless I had forty pens, and could, at once, write with them all together, as the company now spoke. The reader must, therefore, content himself with the most remarkable incidents, and perhaps he may very well excuse the rest.\n\nThe first thing done was securing the body of Northerton, who being delivered into the custody of six men with a corporal at their head, was by them conducted from a place which he was very willing to leave, but it was unluckily to a place whither he was very unwilling to go. To say the truth, so whimsical are the desires of ambition, the very moment this youth had attained the above-mentioned honour, he would have been well contented to have retired to some corner of the world, where the fame of it should never have reached his ears.\n\nIt surprizes us, and so perhaps, it may the reader, that the lieutenant, a worthy and good man, should have applied his chief care, rather to secure the offender, than to preserve the life of the wounded person. We mention this observation, not with any view of pretending to account for so odd a behaviour, but lest some critic should hereafter plume himself on discovering it. We would have these gentlemen know we can see what is odd in characters as well as themselves, but it is our business to relate facts as they are; which, when we have done, it is the part of the learned and sagacious reader to consult that original book of nature, whence every passage in our work is transcribed, though we quote not always the particular page for its authority.\n\nThe company which now arrived were of a different disposition. They suspended their curiosity concerning the person of the ensign, till they should see him hereafter in a more engaging attitude. At present, their whole concern and attention were employed about the bloody object on the floor; which being placed upright in a chair, soon began to discover some symptoms of life and motion. These were no sooner perceived by the company (for Jones was at first generally concluded to be dead) than they all fell at once to prescribing for him (for as none of the physical order was present, every one there took that office upon him).\n\nBleeding was the unanimous voice of the whole room; but unluckily there was no operator at hand; every one then cried, “Call the barber;” but none stirred a step. Several cordials was likewise prescribed in the same ineffective manner; till the landlord ordered up a tankard of strong beer, with a toast, which he said was the best cordial in England.\n\nThe person principally assistant on this occasion, indeed the only one who did any service, or seemed likely to do any, was the landlady: she cut off some of her hair, and applied it to the wound to stop the blood; she fell to chafing the youth's temples with her hand; and having exprest great contempt for her husband's prescription of beer, she despatched one of her maids to her own closet for a bottle of brandy, of which, as soon as it was brought, she prevailed on Jones, who was just returned to his senses, to drink a very large and plentiful draught.\n\nSoon afterwards arrived the surgeon, who having viewed the wound, having shaken his head, and blamed everything which was done, ordered his patient instantly to bed; in which place we think proper to leave him some time to his repose, and shall here, therefore, put an end to this chapter.\n\n# Chapter xiii.\n\nContaining the great address of the landlady, the great learning of a surgeon, and the solid skill in casuistry of the worthy lieutenant.\n\nWhen the wounded man was carried to his bed, and the house began again to clear up from the hurry which this accident had occasioned, the landlady thus addressed the commanding officer: “I am afraid, sir,” said she, “this young man did not behave himself as well as he should do to your honours; and if he had been killed, I suppose he had but his desarts: to be sure, when gentlemen admit inferior parsons into their company, they oft to keep their distance; but, as my first husband used to say, few of 'em know how to do it. For my own part, I am sure I should not have suffered any fellows to include themselves into gentlemen's company; but I thoft he had been an officer himself, till the serjeant told me he was but a recruit.”\n\n“Landlady,” answered the lieutenant, “you mistake the whole matter. The young man behaved himself extremely well, and is, I believe, a much better gentleman than the ensign who abused him. If the young fellow dies, the man who struck him will have most reason to be sorry for it: for the regiment will get rid of a very troublesome fellow, who is a scandal to the army; and if he escapes from the hands of justice, blame me, madam, that's all.”\n\n“Ay! ay! good lack-a-day!” said the landlady; “who could have thoft it? Ay, ay, ay, I am satisfied your honour will see justice done; and to be sure it oft to be to every one. Gentlemen oft not to kill poor folks without answering for it. A poor man hath a soul to be saved, as well as his betters.”\n\n“Indeed, madam,” said the lieutenant, “you do the volunteer wrong: I dare swear he is more of a gentleman than the officer.”\n\n“Ay!” cries the landlady; “why, look you there, now: well, my first husband was a wise man; he used to say, you can't always know the inside by the outside. Nay, that might have been well enough too; for I never saw'd him till he was all over blood. Who would have thoft it? mayhap, some young gentleman crossed in love. Good lack-a-day, if he should die, what a concern it will be to his parents! why, sure the devil must possess the wicked wretch to do such an act. To be sure, he is a scandal to the army, as your honour says; for most of the gentlemen of the army that ever I saw, are quite different sort of people, and look as if they would scorn to spill any Christian blood as much as any men: I mean, that is, in a civil way, as my first husband used to say. To be sure, when they come into the wars, there must be bloodshed: but that they are not to be blamed for. The more of our enemies they kill there, the better: and I wish, with all my heart, they could kill every mother's son of them.”\n\n“O fie, madam!” said the lieutenant, smiling; “all is rather too bloody-minded a wish.”\n\n“Not at all, sir,” answered she; “I am not at all bloody-minded, only to our enemies; and there is no harm in that. To be sure it is natural for us to wish our enemies dead, that the wars may be at an end, and our taxes be lowered; for it is a dreadful thing to pay as we do. Why now, there is above forty shillings for window-lights, and yet we have stopt up all we could; we have almost blinded the house, I am sure. Says I to the exciseman, says I, I think you oft to favour us; I am sure we are very good friends to the government: and so we are for sartain, for we pay a mint of money to 'um. And yet I often think to myself the government doth not imagine itself more obliged to us, than to those that don't pay 'um a farthing. Ay, ay, it is the way of the world.”\n\nShe was proceeding in this manner when the surgeon entered the room. The lieutenant immediately asked how his patient did. But he resolved him only by saying, “Better, I believe, than he would have been by this time, if I had not been called; and even as it is, perhaps it would have been lucky if I could have been called sooner.”--“I hope, sir,” said the lieutenant, “the skull is not fractured.”--“Hum,” cries the surgeon: “fractures are not always the most dangerous symptoms. Contusions and lacerations are often attended with worse phaenomena, and with more fatal consequences, than fractures. People who know nothing of the matter conclude, if the skull is not fractured, all is well; whereas, I had rather see a man's skull broke all to pieces, than some contusions I have met with.”--“I hope,” says the lieutenant, “there are no such symptoms here.”--“Symptoms,” answered the surgeon, “are not always regular nor constant. I have known very unfavourable symptoms in the morning change to favourable ones at noon, and return to unfavourable again at night. Of wounds, indeed, it is rightly and truly said, Nemo repente fuit turpissimus. I was once, I remember, called to a patient who had received a violent contusion in his tibia, by which the exterior cutis was lacerated, so that there was a profuse sanguinary discharge; and the interior membranes were so divellicated, that the os or bone very plainly appeared through the aperture of the vulnus or wound. Some febrile symptoms intervening at the same time (for the pulse was exuberant and indicated much phlebotomy), I apprehended an immediate mortification. To prevent which, I presently made a large orifice in the vein of the left arm, whence I drew twenty ounces of blood; which I expected to have found extremely sizy and glutinous, or indeed coagulated, as it is in pleuretic complaints; but, to my surprize, it appeared rosy and florid, and its consistency differed little from the blood of those in perfect health. I then applied a fomentation to the part, which highly answered the intention; and after three or four times dressing, the wound began to discharge a thick pus or matter, by which means the cohesion--But perhaps I do not make myself perfectly well understood?”--“No, really,” answered the lieutenant, “I cannot say I understand a syllable.”--“Well, sir,” said the surgeon, “then I shall not tire your patience; in short, within six weeks my patient was able to walk upon his legs as perfectly as he could have done before he received the contusion.”--“I wish, sir,” said the lieutenant, “you would be so kind only to inform me, whether the wound this young gentleman hath had the misfortune to receive, is likely to prove mortal.”--“Sir,” answered the surgeon, “to say whether a wound will prove mortal or not at first dressing, would be very weak and foolish presumption: we are all mortal, and symptoms often occur in a cure which the greatest of our profession could never foresee.”--“But do you think him in danger?” says the other.--“In danger! ay, surely,” cries the doctor: “who is there among us, who, in the most perfect health, can be said not to be in danger? Can a man, therefore, with so bad a wound as this be said to be out of danger? All I can say at present is, that it is well I was called as I was, and perhaps it would have been better if I had been called sooner. I will see him again early in the morning; and in the meantime let him be kept extremely quiet, and drink liberally of water-gruel.”--“Won't you allow him sack-whey?” said the landlady.--“Ay, ay, sack-whey,” cries the doctor, “if you will, provided it be very small.”--“And a little chicken broth too?” added she.--“Yes, yes, chicken broth,” said the doctor, “is very good.”--“Mayn't I make him some jellies too?” said the landlady.--“Ay, ay,” answered the doctor, “jellies are very good for wounds, for they promote cohesion.” And indeed it was lucky she had not named soup or high sauces, for the doctor would have complied, rather than have lost the custom of the house.\n\nThe doctor was no sooner gone, than the landlady began to trumpet forth his fame to the lieutenant, who had not, from their short acquaintance, conceived quite so favourable an opinion of his physical abilities as the good woman, and all the neighbourhood, entertained (and perhaps very rightly); for though I am afraid the doctor was a little of a coxcomb, he might be nevertheless very much of a surgeon.\n\nThe lieutenant having collected from the learned discourse of the surgeon that Mr Jones was in great danger, gave orders for keeping Mr Northerton under a very strict guard, designing in the morning to attend him to a justice of peace, and to commit the conducting the troops to Gloucester to the French lieutenant, who, though he could neither read, write, nor speak any language, was, however, a good officer.\n\nIn the evening, our commander sent a message to Mr Jones, that if a visit would not be troublesome, he would wait on him. This civility was very kindly and thankfully received by Jones, and the lieutenant accordingly went up to his room, where he found the wounded man much better than he expected; nay, Jones assured his friend, that if he had not received express orders to the contrary from the surgeon, he should have got up long ago; for he appeared to himself to be as well as ever, and felt no other inconvenience from his wound but an extreme soreness on that side of his head.\n\n“I should be very glad,” quoth the lieutenant, “if you was as well as you fancy yourself, for then you could be able to do yourself justice immediately; for when a matter can't be made up, as in case of a blow, the sooner you take him out the better; but I am afraid you think yourself better than you are, and he would have too much advantage over you.”\n\n“I'll try, however,” answered Jones, “if you please, and will be so kind to lend me a sword, for I have none here of my own.”\n\n“My sword is heartily at your service, my dear boy,” cries the lieutenant, kissing him; “you are a brave lad, and I love your spirit; but I fear your strength; for such a blow, and so much loss of blood, must have very much weakened you; and though you feel no want of strength in your bed, yet you most probably would after a thrust or two. I can't consent to your taking him out tonight; but I hope you will be able to come up with us before we get many days' march advance; and I give you my honour you shall have satisfaction, or the man who hath injured you shan't stay in our regiment.”\n\n“I wish,” said Jones, “it was possible to decide this matter to-night: now you have mentioned it to me, I shall not be able to rest.”\n\n“Oh, never think of it,” returned the other: “a few days will make no difference. The wounds of honour are not like those in your body: they suffer nothing by the delay of cure. It will be altogether as well for you to receive satisfaction a week hence as now.”\n\n“But suppose,” says Jones, “I should grow worse, and die of the consequences of my present wound?”\n\n“Then your honour,” answered the lieutenant, “will require no reparation at all. I myself will do justice to your character, and testify to the world your intention to have acted properly, if you had recovered.”\n\n“Still,” replied Jones, “I am concerned at the delay. I am almost afraid to mention it to you who are a soldier; but though I have been a very wild young fellow, still in my most serious moments, and at the bottom, I am really a Christian.”\n\n“So am I too, I assure you,” said the officer; “and so zealous a one, that I was pleased with you at dinner for taking up the cause of your religion; and I am a little offended with you now, young gentleman, that you should express a fear of declaring your faith before any one.”\n\n“But how terrible must it be,” cries Jones, “to any one who is really a Christian, to cherish malice in his breast, in opposition to the command of Him who hath expressly forbid it? How can I bear to do this on a sick-bed? Or how shall I make up my account, with such an article as this in my bosom against me?”\n\n“Why, I believe there is such a command,” cries the lieutenant; “but a man of honour can't keep it. And you must be a man of honour, if you will be in the army. I remember I once put the case to our chaplain over a bowl of punch, and he confessed there was much difficulty in it; but he said, he hoped there might be a latitude granted to soldiers in this one instance; and to be sure it is our duty to hope so; for who would bear to live without his honour? No, no, my dear boy, be a good Christian as long as you live; but be a man of honour too, and never put up an affront; not all the books, nor all the parsons in the world, shall ever persuade me to that. I love my religion very well, but I love my honour more. There must be some mistake in the wording the text, or in the translation, or in the understanding it, or somewhere or other. But however that be, a man must run the risque, for he must preserve his honour. So compose yourself to-night, and I promise you you shall have an opportunity of doing yourself justice.” Here he gave Jones a hearty buss, shook him by the hand, and took his leave.\n\nBut though the lieutenant's reasoning was very satisfactory to himself, it was not entirely so to his friend. Jones therefore, having revolved this matter much in his thoughts, at last came to a resolution, which the reader will find in the next chapter.\n\n# Chapter xiv.\n\nA most dreadful chapter indeed; and which few readers ought to venture upon in an evening, especially when alone.\n\nJones swallowed a large mess of chicken, or rather cock, broth, with a very good appetite, as indeed he would have done the cock it was made of, with a pound of bacon into the bargain; and now, finding in himself no deficiency of either health or spirit, he resolved to get up and seek his enemy.\n\nBut first he sent for the serjeant, who was his first acquaintance among these military gentlemen. Unluckily that worthy officer having, in a literal sense, taken his fill of liquor, had been some time retired to his bolster, where he was snoring so loud that it was not easy to convey a noise in at his ears capable of drowning that which issued from his nostrils.\n\nHowever, as Jones persisted in his desire of seeing him, a vociferous drawer at length found means to disturb his slumbers, and to acquaint him with the message. Of which the serjeant was no sooner made sensible, than he arose from his bed, and having his clothes already on, immediately attended. Jones did not think fit to acquaint the serjeant with his design; though he might have done it with great safety, for the halberdier was himself a man of honour, and had killed his man. He would therefore have faithfully kept this secret, or indeed any other which no reward was published for discovering. But as Jones knew not those virtues in so short an acquaintance, his caution was perhaps prudent and commendable enough.\n\nHe began therefore by acquainting the serjeant, that as he was now entered into the army, he was ashamed of being without what was perhaps the most necessary implement of a soldier; namely, a sword; adding, that he should be infinitely obliged to him, if he could procure one. “For which,” says he, “I will give you any reasonable price; nor do I insist upon its being silver-hilted; only a good blade, and such as may become a soldier's thigh.”\n\nThe serjeant, who well knew what had happened, and had heard that Jones was in a very dangerous condition, immediately concluded, from such a message, at such a time of night, and from a man in such a situation, that he was light-headed. Now as he had his wit (to use that word in its common signification) always ready, he bethought himself of making his advantage of this humour in the sick man. “Sir,” says he, “I believe I can fit you. I have a most excellent piece of stuff by me. It is not indeed silver-hilted, which, as you say, doth not become a soldier; but the handle is decent enough, and the blade one of the best in Europe. It is a blade that--a blade that--in short, I will fetch it you this instant, and you shall see it and handle it. I am glad to see your honour so well with all my heart.”\n\nBeing instantly returned with the sword, he delivered it to Jones, who took it and drew it; and then told the serjeant it would do very well, and bid him name his price.\n\nThe serjeant now began to harangue in praise of his goods. He said (nay he swore very heartily), “that the blade was taken from a French officer, of very high rank, at the battle of Dettingen. I took it myself,” says he, “from his side, after I had knocked him o' the head. The hilt was a golden one. That I sold to one of our fine gentlemen; for there are some of them, an't please your honour, who value the hilt of a sword more than the blade.”\n\nHere the other stopped him, and begged him to name a price. The serjeant, who thought Jones absolutely out of his senses, and very near his end, was afraid lest he should injure his family by asking too little. However, after a moment's hesitation, he contented himself with naming twenty guineas, and swore he would not sell it for less to his own brother.\n\n“Twenty guineas!” says Jones, in the utmost surprize: “sure you think I am mad, or that I never saw a sword in my life. Twenty guineas, indeed! I did not imagine you would endeavour to impose upon me. Here, take the sword--No, now I think on't, I will keep it myself, and show it your officer in the morning, acquainting him, at the same time, what a price you asked me for it.”\n\nThe serjeant, as we have said, had always his wit (in sensu praedicto) about him, and now plainly saw that Jones was not in the condition he had apprehended him to be; he now, therefore, counterfeited as great surprize as the other had shown, and said, “I am certain, sir, I have not asked you so much out of the way. Besides, you are to consider, it is the only sword I have, and I must run the risque of my officer's displeasure, by going without one myself. And truly, putting all this together, I don't think twenty shillings was so much out of the way.”\n\n“Twenty shillings!” cries Jones; “why, you just now asked me twenty guineas.”--“How!” cries the serjeant, “sure your honour must have mistaken me: or else I mistook myself--and indeed I am but half awake. Twenty guineas, indeed! no wonder your honour flew into such a passion. I say twenty guineas too. No, no, I mean twenty shillings, I assure you. And when your honour comes to consider everything, I hope you will not think that so extravagant a price. It is indeed true, you may buy a weapon which looks as well for less money. But----”\n\nHere Jones interrupted him, saying, “I will be so far from making any words with you, that I will give you a shilling more than your demand.” He then gave him a guinea, bid him return to his bed, and wished him a good march; adding, he hoped to overtake them before the division reached Worcester.\n\nThe serjeant very civilly took his leave, fully satisfied with his merchandize, and not a little pleased with his dexterous recovery from that false step into which his opinion of the sick man's light-headedness had betrayed him.\n\nAs soon as the serjeant was departed, Jones rose from his bed, and dressed himself entirely, putting on even his coat, which, as its colour was white, showed very visibly the streams of blood which had flowed down it; and now, having grasped his new-purchased sword in his hand, he was going to issue forth, when the thought of what he was about to undertake laid suddenly hold of him, and he began to reflect that in a few minutes he might possibly deprive a human being of life, or might lose his own. “Very well,” said he, “and in what cause do I venture my life? Why, in that of my honour. And who is this human being? A rascal who hath injured and insulted me without provocation. But is not revenge forbidden by Heaven? Yes, but it is enjoined by the world. Well, but shall I obey the world in opposition to the express commands of Heaven? Shall I incur the Divine displeasure rather than be called--ha--coward--scoundrel?--I'll think no more; I am resolved, and must fight him.”\n\nThe clock had now struck twelve, and every one in the house were in their beds, except the centinel who stood to guard Northerton, when Jones softly opening his door, issued forth in pursuit of his enemy, of whose place of confinement he had received a perfect description from the drawer. It is not easy to conceive a much more tremendous figure than he now exhibited. He had on, as we have said, a light-coloured coat, covered with streams of blood. His face, which missed that very blood, as well as twenty ounces more drawn from him by the surgeon, was pallid. Round his head was a quantity of bandage, not unlike a turban. In the right hand he carried a sword, and in the left a candle. So that the bloody Banquo was not worthy to be compared to him. In fact, I believe a more dreadful apparition was never raised in a church-yard, nor in the imagination of any good people met in a winter evening over a Christmas fire in Somersetshire.\n\nWhen the centinel first saw our heroe approach, his hair began gently to lift up his grenadier cap; and in the same instant his knees fell to blows with each other. Presently his whole body was seized with worse than an ague fit. He then fired his piece, and fell flat on his face.\n\nWhether fear or courage was the occasion of his firing, or whether he took aim at the object of his terror, I cannot say. If he did, however, he had the good fortune to miss his man.\n\nJones seeing the fellow fall, guessed the cause of his fright, at which he could not forbear smiling, not in the least reflecting on the danger from which he had just escaped. He then passed by the fellow, who still continued in the posture in which he fell, and entered the room where Northerton, as he had heard, was confined. Here, in a solitary situation, he found--an empty quart pot standing on the table, on which some beer being spilt, it looked as if the room had lately been inhabited; but at present it was entirely vacant.\n\nJones then apprehended it might lead to some other apartment; but upon searching all round it, he could perceive no other door than that at which he entered, and where the centinel had been posted. He then proceeded to call Northerton several times by his name; but no one answered; nor did this serve to any other purpose than to confirm the centinel in his terrors, who was now convinced that the volunteer was dead of his wounds, and that his ghost was come in search of the murderer: he now lay in all the agonies of horror; and I wish, with all my heart, some of those actors who are hereafter to represent a man frighted out of his wits had seen him, that they might be taught to copy nature, instead of performing several antic tricks and gestures, for the entertainment and applause of the galleries.\n\nPerceiving the bird was flown, at least despairing to find him, and rightly apprehending that the report of the firelock would alarm the whole house, our heroe now blew out his candle, and gently stole back again to his chamber, and to his bed; whither he would not have been able to have gotten undiscovered, had any other person been on the same staircase, save only one gentleman who was confined to his bed by the gout; for before he could reach the door to his chamber, the hall where the centinel had been posted was half full of people, some in their shirts, and others not half drest, all very earnestly enquiring of each other what was the matter.\n\nThe soldier was now found lying in the same place and posture in which we just now left him. Several immediately applied themselves to raise him, and some concluded him dead; but they presently saw their mistake, for he not only struggled with those who laid their hands on him, but fell a roaring like a bull. In reality, he imagined so many spirits or devils were handling him; for his imagination being possessed with the horror of an apparition, converted every object he saw or felt into nothing but ghosts and spectres.\n\nAt length he was overpowered by numbers, and got upon his legs; when candles being brought, and seeing two or three of his comrades present, he came a little to himself; but when they asked him what was the matter? he answered, “I am a dead man, that's all, I am a dead man, I can't recover it, I have seen him.” “What hast thou seen, Jack?” says one of the soldiers. “Why, I have seen the young volunteer that was killed yesterday.” He then imprecated the most heavy curses on himself, if he had not seen the volunteer, all over blood, vomiting fire out of his mouth and nostrils, pass by him into the chamber where Ensign Northerton was, and then seizing the ensign by the throat, fly away with him in a clap of thunder.\n\nThis relation met with a gracious reception from the audience. All the women present believed it firmly, and prayed Heaven to defend them from murder. Amongst the men too, many had faith in the story; but others turned it into derision and ridicule; and a serjeant who was present answered very coolly, “Young man, you will hear more of this, for going to sleep and dreaming on your post.”\n\nThe soldier replied, “You may punish me if you please; but I was as broad awake as I am now; and the devil carry me away, as he hath the ensign, if I did not see the dead man, as I tell you, with eyes as big and as fiery as two large flambeaux.”\n\nThe commander of the forces, and the commander of the house, were now both arrived; for the former being awake at the time, and hearing the centinel fire his piece, thought it his duty to rise immediately, though he had no great apprehensions of any mischief; whereas the apprehensions of the latter were much greater, lest her spoons and tankards should be upon the march, without having received any such orders from her.\n\nOur poor centinel, to whom the sight of this officer was not much more welcome than the apparition, as he thought it, which he had seen before, again related the dreadful story, and with many additions of blood and fire; but he had the misfortune to gain no credit with either of the last-mentioned persons: for the officer, though a very religious man, was free from all terrors of this kind; besides, having so lately left Jones in the condition we have seen, he had no suspicion of his being dead. As for the landlady, though not over religious, she had no kind of aversion to the doctrine of spirits; but there was a circumstance in the tale which she well knew to be false, as we shall inform the reader presently.\n\nBut whether Northerton was carried away in thunder or fire, or in whatever other manner he was gone, it was now certain that his body was no longer in custody. Upon this occasion the lieutenant formed a conclusion not very different from what the serjeant is just mentioned to have made before, and immediately ordered the centinel to be taken prisoner. So that, by a strange reverse of fortune (though not very uncommon in a military life), the guard became the guarded.\n\nThe conclusion of the foregoing adventure.\n\nBesides the suspicion of sleep, the lieutenant harboured another and worse doubt against the poor centinel, and this was, that of treachery; for as he believed not one syllable of the apparition, so he imagined the whole to be an invention formed only to impose upon him, and that the fellow had in reality been bribed by Northerton to let him escape. And this he imagined the rather, as the fright appeared to him the more unnatural in one who had the character of as brave and bold a man as any in the regiment, having been in several actions, having received several wounds, and, in a word, having behaved himself always like a good and valiant soldier.\n\nThat the reader, therefore, may not conceive the least ill opinion of such a person, we shall not delay a moment in rescuing his character from the imputation of this guilt.\n\nMr Northerton then, as we have before observed, was fully satisfied with the glory which he had obtained from this action. He had perhaps seen, or heard, or guessed, that envy is apt to attend fame. Not that I would here insinuate that he was heathenishly inclined to believe in or to worship the goddess Nemesis; for, in fact, I am convinced he never heard of her name. He was, besides, of an active disposition, and had a great antipathy to those close quarters in the castle of Gloucester, for which a justice of peace might possibly give him a billet. Nor was he moreover free from some uneasy meditations on a certain wooden edifice, which I forbear to name, in conformity to the opinion of mankind, who, I think, rather ought to honour than to be ashamed of this building, as it is, or at least might be made, of more benefit to society than almost any other public erection. In a word, to hint at no more reasons for his conduct, Mr Northerton was desirous of departing that evening, and nothing remained for him but to contrive the quomodo, which appeared to be a matter of some difficulty.\n\nNow this young gentleman, though somewhat crooked in his morals, was perfectly straight in his person, which was extremely strong and well made. His face too was accounted handsome by the generality of women, for it was broad and ruddy, with tolerably good teeth. Such charms did not fail making an impression on my landlady, who had no little relish for this kind of beauty. She had, indeed, a real compassion for the young man; and hearing from the surgeon that affairs were like to go ill with the volunteer, she suspected they might hereafter wear no benign aspect with the ensign. Having obtained, therefore, leave to make him a visit, and finding him in a very melancholy mood, which she considerably heightened by telling him there were scarce any hopes of the volunteer's life, she proceeded to throw forth some hints, which the other readily and eagerly taking up, they soon came to a right understanding; and it was at length agreed that the ensign should, at a certain signal, ascend the chimney, which communicating very soon with that of the kitchen, he might there again let himself down; for which she would give him an opportunity by keeping the coast clear.\n\nBut lest our readers, of a different complexion, should take this occasion of too hastily condemning all compassion as a folly, and pernicious to society, we think proper to mention another particular which might possibly have some little share in this action. The ensign happened to be at this time possessed of the sum of fifty pounds, which did indeed belong to the whole company; for the captain having quarrelled with his lieutenant, had entrusted the payment of his company to the ensign. This money, however, he thought proper to deposit in my landlady's hand, possibly by way of bail or security that he would hereafter appear and answer to the charge against him; but whatever were the conditions, certain it is, that she had the money and the ensign his liberty.\n\nThe reader may perhaps expect, from the compassionate temper of this good woman, that when she saw the poor centinel taken prisoner for a fact of which she knew him innocent, she should immediately have interposed in his behalf; but whether it was that she had already exhausted all her compassion in the above-mentioned instance, or that the features of this fellow, though not very different from those of the ensign, could not raise it, I will not determine; but, far from being an advocate for the present prisoner, she urged his guilt to his officer, declaring, with uplifted eyes and hands, that she would not have had any concern in the escape of a murderer for all the world.\n\nEverything was now once more quiet, and most of the company returned again to their beds; but the landlady, either from the natural activity of her disposition, or from her fear for her plate, having no propensity to sleep, prevailed with the officers, as they were to march within little more than an hour, to spend that time with her over a bowl of punch.\n\nJones had lain awake all this while, and had heard great part of the hurry and bustle that had passed, of which he had now some curiosity to know the particulars. He therefore applied to his bell, which he rung at least twenty times without any effect: for my landlady was in such high mirth with her company, that no clapper could be heard there but her own; and the drawer and chambermaid, who were sitting together in the kitchen (for neither durst he sit up nor she lie in bed alone), the more they heard the bell ring the more they were frightened, and as it were nailed down in their places.\n\nAt last, at a lucky interval of chat, the sound reached the ears of our good landlady, who presently sent forth her summons, which both her servants instantly obeyed. “Joe,” says the mistress, “don't you hear the gentleman's bell ring? Why don't you go up?”--“It is not my business,” answered the drawer, “to wait upon the chambers--it is Betty Chambermaid's.”--“If you come to that,” answered the maid, “it is not my business to wait upon gentlemen. I have done it indeed sometimes; but the devil fetch me if ever I do again, since you make your preambles about it.” The bell still ringing violently, their mistress fell into a passion, and swore, if the drawer did not go up immediately, she would turn him away that very morning. “If you do, madam,” says he, “I can't help it. I won't do another servant's business.” She then applied herself to the maid, and endeavoured to prevail by gentle means; but all in vain: Betty was as inflexible as Joe. Both insisted it was not their business, and they would not do it.\n\nThe lieutenant then fell a laughing, and said, “Come, I will put an end to this contention;” and then turning to the servants, commended them for their resolution in not giving up the point; but added, he was sure, if one would consent to go the other would. To which proposal they both agreed in an instant, and accordingly went up very lovingly and close together. When they were gone, the lieutenant appeased the wrath of the landlady, by satisfying her why they were both so unwilling to go alone.\n\nThey returned soon after, and acquainted their mistress, that the sick gentleman was so far from being dead, that he spoke as heartily as if he was well; and that he gave his service to the captain, and should be very glad of the favour of seeing him before he marched.\n\nThe good lieutenant immediately complied with his desires, and sitting down by his bed-side, acquainted him with the scene which had happened below, concluding with his intentions to make an example of the centinel.\n\nUpon this Jones related to him the whole truth, and earnestly begged him not to punish the poor soldier, “who, I am confident,” says he, “is as innocent of the ensign's escape, as he is of forging any lie, or of endeavouring to impose on you.”\n\nThe lieutenant hesitated a few moments, and then answered: “Why, as you have cleared the fellow of one part of the charge, so it will be impossible to prove the other, because he was not the only centinel. But I have a good mind to punish the rascal for being a coward. Yet who knows what effect the terror of such an apprehension may have? and, to say the truth, he hath always behaved well against an enemy. Come, it is a good thing to see any sign of religion in these fellows; so I promise you he shall be set at liberty when we march. But hark, the general beats. My dear boy, give me another buss. Don't discompose nor hurry yourself; but remember the Christian doctrine of patience, and I warrant you will soon be able to do yourself justice, and to take an honourable revenge on the fellow who hath injured you.” The lieutenant then departed, and Jones endeavoured to compose himself to rest.\n\nA wonderful long chapter concerning the marvellous; being much the longest of all our introductory chapters.\n\nAs we are now entering upon a book in which the course of our history will oblige us to relate some matters of a more strange and surprizing kind than any which have hitherto occurred, it may not be amiss, in the prolegomenous or introductory chapter, to say something of that species of writing which is called the marvellous. To this we shall, as well for the sake of ourselves as of others, endeavour to set some certain bounds, and indeed nothing can be more necessary, as critics[*] of different complexions are here apt to run into very different extremes; for while some are, with M. Dacier, ready to allow, that the same thing which is impossible may be yet probable,[**] others have so little historic or poetic faith, that they believe nothing to be either possible or probable, the like to which hath not occurred to their own observation.\n\n[*] By this word here, and in most other parts of our work, we mean every reader in the world. [**] It is happy for M. Dacier that he was not an Irishman.\n\nFirst, then, I think it may very reasonably be required of every writer, that he keeps within the bounds of possibility; and still remembers that what it is not possible for man to perform, it is scarce possible for man to believe he did perform. This conviction perhaps gave birth to many stories of the antient heathen deities (for most of them are of poetical original). The poet, being desirous to indulge a wanton and extravagant imagination, took refuge in that power, of the extent of which his readers were no judges, or rather which they imagined to be infinite, and consequently they could not be shocked at any prodigies related of it. This hath been strongly urged in defence of Homer's miracles; and it is perhaps a defence; not, as Mr Pope would have it, because Ulysses told a set of foolish lies to the Phaeacians, who were a very dull nation; but because the poet himself wrote to heathens, to whom poetical fables were articles of faith. For my own part, I must confess, so compassionate is my temper, I wish Polypheme had confined himself to his milk diet, and preserved his eye; nor could Ulysses be much more concerned than myself, when his companions were turned into swine by Circe, who showed, I think, afterwards, too much regard for man's flesh to be supposed capable of converting it into bacon. I wish, likewise, with all my heart, that Homer could have known the rule prescribed by Horace, to introduce supernatural agents as seldom as possible. We should not then have seen his gods coming on trivial errands, and often behaving themselves so as not only to forfeit all title to respect, but to become the objects of scorn and derision. A conduct which must have shocked the credulity of a pious and sagacious heathen; and which could never have been defended, unless by agreeing with a supposition to which I have been sometimes almost inclined, that this most glorious poet, as he certainly was, had an intent to burlesque the superstitious faith of his own age and country.\n\nBut I have rested too long on a doctrine which can be of no use to a Christian writer; for as he cannot introduce into his works any of that heavenly host which make a part of his creed, so it is horrid puerility to search the heathen theology for any of those deities who have been long since dethroned from their immortality. Lord Shaftesbury observes, that nothing is more cold than the invocation of a muse by a modern; he might have added, that nothing can be more absurd. A modern may with much more elegance invoke a ballad, as some have thought Homer did, or a mug of ale, with the author of Hudibras; which latter may perhaps have inspired much more poetry, as well as prose, than all the liquors of Hippocrene or Helicon.\n\nThe only supernatural agents which can in any manner be allowed to us moderns, are ghosts; but of these I would advise an author to be extremely sparing. These are indeed, like arsenic, and other dangerous drugs in physic, to be used with the utmost caution; nor would I advise the introduction of them at all in those works, or by those authors, to which, or to whom, a horse-laugh in the reader would be any great prejudice or mortification.\n\nAs for elves and fairies, and other such mummery, I purposely omit the mention of them, as I should be very unwilling to confine within any bounds those surprizing imaginations, for whose vast capacity the limits of human nature are too narrow; whose works are to be considered as a new creation; and who have consequently just right to do what they will with their own.\n\nMan therefore is the highest subject (unless on very extraordinary occasions indeed) which presents itself to the pen of our historian, or of our poet; and, in relating his actions, great care is to be taken that we do not exceed the capacity of the agent we describe.\n\nNor is possibility alone sufficient to justify us; we must keep likewise within the rules of probability. It is, I think, the opinion of Aristotle; or if not, it is the opinion of some wise man, whose authority will be as weighty when it is as old, “That it is no excuse for a poet who relates what is incredible, that the thing related is really matter of fact.” This may perhaps be allowed true with regard to poetry, but it may be thought impracticable to extend it to the historian; for he is obliged to record matters as he finds them, though they may be of so extraordinary a nature as will require no small degree of historical faith to swallow them. Such was the successless armament of Xerxes described by Herodotus, or the successful expedition of Alexander related by Arrian. Such of later years was the victory of Agincourt obtained by Harry the Fifth, or that of Narva won by Charles the Twelfth of Sweden. All which instances, the more we reflect on them, appear still the more astonishing.\n\nSuch facts, however, as they occur in the thread of the story, nay, indeed, as they constitute the essential parts of it, the historian is not only justifiable in recording as they really happened, but indeed would be unpardonable should he omit or alter them. But there are other facts not of such consequence nor so necessary, which, though ever so well attested, may nevertheless be sacrificed to oblivion in complacence to the scepticism of a reader. Such is that memorable story of the ghost of George Villiers, which might with more propriety have been made a present of to Dr Drelincourt, to have kept the ghost of Mrs Veale company, at the head of his Discourse upon Death, than have been introduced into so solemn a work as the History of the Rebellion.\n\nTo say the truth, if the historian will confine himself to what really happened, and utterly reject any circumstance, which, though never so well attested, he must be well assured is false, he will sometimes fall into the marvellous, but never into the incredible. He will often raise the wonder and surprize of his reader, but never that incredulous hatred mentioned by Horace. It is by falling into fiction, therefore, that we generally offend against this rule, of deserting probability, which the historian seldom, if ever, quits, till he forsakes his character and commences a writer of romance. In this, however, those historians who relate public transactions, have the advantage of us who confine ourselves to scenes of private life. The credit of the former is by common notoriety supported for a long time; and public records, with the concurrent testimony of many authors, bear evidence to their truth in future ages. Thus a Trajan and an Antoninus, a Nero and a Caligula, have all met with the belief of posterity; and no one doubts but that men so very good, and so very bad, were once the masters of mankind.\n\nBut we who deal in private character, who search into the most retired recesses, and draw forth examples of virtue and vice from holes and corners of the world, are in a more dangerous situation. As we have no public notoriety, no concurrent testimony, no records to support and corroborate what we deliver, it becomes us to keep within the limits not only of possibility, but of probability too; and this more especially in painting what is greatly good and amiable. Knavery and folly, though never so exorbitant, will more easily meet with assent; for ill-nature adds great support and strength to faith.\n\nThus we may, perhaps, with little danger, relate the history of Fisher; who having long owed his bread to the generosity of Mr Derby, and having one morning received a considerable bounty from his hands, yet, in order to possess himself of what remained in his friend's scrutore, concealed himself in a public office of the Temple, through which there was a passage into Mr Derby's chambers. Here he overheard Mr Derby for many hours solacing himself at an entertainment which he that evening gave his friends, and to which Fisher had been invited. During all this time, no tender, no grateful reflections arose to restrain his purpose; but when the poor gentleman had let his company out through the office, Fisher came suddenly from his lurking-place, and walking softly behind his friend into his chamber, discharged a pistol-ball into his head. This may be believed when the bones of Fisher are as rotten as his heart. Nay, perhaps, it will be credited, that the villain went two days afterwards with some young ladies to the play of Hamlet; and with an unaltered countenance heard one of the ladies, who little suspected how near she was to the person, cry out, “Good God! if the man that murdered Mr Derby was now present!” manifesting in this a more seared and callous conscience than even Nero himself; of whom we are told by Suetonius, “that the consciousness of his guilt, after the death of his mother, became immediately intolerable, and so continued; nor could all the congratulations of the soldiers, of the senate, and the people, allay the horrors of his conscience.”\n\nBut now, on the other hand, should I tell my reader, that I had known a man whose penetrating genius had enabled him to raise a large fortune in a way where no beginning was chaulked out to him; that he had done this with the most perfect preservation of his integrity, and not only without the least injustice or injury to any one individual person, but with the highest advantage to trade, and a vast increase of the public revenue; that he had expended one part of the income of this fortune in discovering a taste superior to most, by works where the highest dignity was united with the purest simplicity, and another part in displaying a degree of goodness superior to all men, by acts of charity to objects whose only recommendations were their merits, or their wants; that he was most industrious in searching after merit in distress, most eager to relieve it, and then as careful (perhaps too careful) to conceal what he had done; that his house, his furniture, his gardens, his table, his private hospitality, and his public beneficence, all denoted the mind from which they flowed, and were all intrinsically rich and noble, without tinsel, or external ostentation; that he filled every relation in life with the most adequate virtue; that he was most piously religious to his Creator, most zealously loyal to his sovereign; a most tender husband to his wife, a kind relation, a munificent patron, a warm and firm friend, a knowing and a chearful companion, indulgent to his servants, hospitable to his neighbours, charitable to the poor, and benevolent to all mankind. Should I add to these the epithets of wise, brave, elegant, and indeed every other amiable epithet in our language, I might surely say,\n\n--Quis credet? nemo Hercule! nemo; Vel duo, vel nemo;\n\nand yet I know a man who is all I have here described. But a single instance (and I really know not such another) is not sufficient to justify us, while we are writing to thousands who never heard of the person, nor of anything like him. Such rarae aves should be remitted to the epitaph writer, or to some poet who may condescend to hitch him in a distich, or to slide him into a rhime with an air of carelessness and neglect, without giving any offence to the reader.\n\nIn the last place, the actions should be such as may not only be within the compass of human agency, and which human agents may probably be supposed to do; but they should be likely for the very actors and characters themselves to have performed; for what may be only wonderful and surprizing in one man, may become improbable, or indeed impossible, when related of another.\n\nThis last requisite is what the dramatic critics call conversation of character; and it requires a very extraordinary degree of judgment, and a most exact knowledge of human nature.\n\nIt is admirably remarked by a most excellent writer, that zeal can no more hurry a man to act in direct opposition to itself, than a rapid stream can carry a boat against its own current. I will venture to say, that for a man to act in direct contradiction to the dictates of his nature, is, if not impossible, as improbable and as miraculous as anything which can well be conceived. Should the best parts of the story of M. Antoninus be ascribed to Nero, or should the worst incidents of Nero's life be imputed to Antoninus, what would be more shocking to belief than either instance? whereas both these being related of their proper agent, constitute the truly marvellous.\n\nOur modern authors of comedy have fallen almost universally into the error here hinted at; their heroes generally are notorious rogues, and their heroines abandoned jades, during the first four acts; but in the fifth, the former become very worthy gentlemen, and the latter women of virtue and discretion: nor is the writer often so kind as to give himself the least trouble to reconcile or account for this monstrous change and incongruity. There is, indeed, no other reason to be assigned for it, than because the play is drawing to a conclusion; as if it was no less natural in a rogue to repent in the last act of a play, than in the last of his life; which we perceive to be generally the case at Tyburn, a place which might indeed close the scene of some comedies with much propriety, as the heroes in these are most commonly eminent for those very talents which not only bring men to the gallows, but enable them to make an heroic figure when they are there.\n\nWithin these few restrictions, I think, every writer may be permitted to deal as much in the wonderful as he pleases; nay, if he thus keeps within the rules of credibility, the more he can surprize the reader the more he will engage his attention, and the more he will charm him. As a genius of the highest rank observes in his fifth chapter of the Bathos, “The great art of all poetry is to mix truth with fiction, in order to join the credible with the surprizing.”\n\nFor though every good author will confine himself within the bounds of probability, it is by no means necessary that his characters, or his incidents, should be trite, common, or vulgar; such as happen in every street, or in every house, or which may be met with in the home articles of a newspaper. Nor must he be inhibited from showing many persons and things, which may possibly have never fallen within the knowledge of great part of his readers. If the writer strictly observes the rules above-mentioned, he hath discharged his part; and is then intitled to some faith from his reader, who is indeed guilty of critical infidelity if he disbelieves him.\n\nFor want of a portion of such faith, I remember the character of a young lady of quality, which was condemned on the stage for being unnatural, by the unanimous voice of a very large assembly of clerks and apprentices; though it had the previous suffrages of many ladies of the first rank; one of whom, very eminent for her understanding, declared it was the picture of half the young people of her acquaintance.\n\nIn which the landlady pays a visit to Mr Jones.\n\nWhen Jones had taken leave of his friend the lieutenant, he endeavoured to close his eyes, but all in vain; his spirits were too lively and wakeful to be lulled to sleep. So having amused, or rather tormented, himself with the thoughts of his Sophia till it was open daylight, he called for some tea; upon which occasion my landlady herself vouchsafed to pay him a visit.\n\nThis was indeed the first time she had seen him, or at least had taken any notice of him; but as the lieutenant had assured her that he was certainly some young gentleman of fashion, she now determined to show him all the respect in her power; for, to speak truly, this was one of those houses where gentlemen, to use the language of advertisements, meet with civil treatment for their money.\n\nShe had no sooner begun to make his tea, than she likewise began to discourse:--“La! sir,” said she, “I think it is great pity that such a pretty young gentleman should under-value himself so, as to go about with these soldier fellows. They call themselves gentlemen, I warrant you; but, as my first husband used to say, they should remember it is we that pay them. And to be sure it is very hard upon us to be obliged to pay them, and to keep 'um too, as we publicans are. I had twenty of 'um last night, besides officers: nay, for matter o' that, I had rather have the soldiers than officers: for nothing is ever good enough for those sparks; and I am sure, if you was to see the bills; la! sir, it is nothing. I have had less trouble, I warrant you, with a good squire's family, where we take forty or fifty shillings of a night, besides horses. And yet I warrants me, there is narrow a one of those officer fellows but looks upon himself to be as good as arrow a squire of £500 a year. To be sure it doth me good to hear their men run about after 'um, crying your honour, and your honour. Marry come up with such honour, and an ordinary at a shilling a head. Then there's such swearing among 'um, to be sure it frightens me out o' my wits: I thinks nothing can ever prosper with such wicked people. And here one of 'um has used you in so barbarous a manner. I thought indeed how well the rest would secure him; they all hang together; for if you had been in danger of death, which I am glad to see you are not, it would have been all as one to such wicked people. They would have let the murderer go. Laud have mercy upon 'um; I would not have such a sin to answer for, for the whole world. But though you are likely, with the blessing, to recover, there is laa for him yet; and if you will employ lawyer Small, I darest be sworn he'll make the fellow fly the country for him; though perhaps he'll have fled the country before; for it is here to-day and gone to-morrow with such chaps. I hope, however, you will learn more wit for the future, and return back to your friends; I warrant they are all miserable for your loss; and if they was but to know what had happened--La, my seeming! I would not for the world they should. Come, come, we know very well what all the matter is; but if one won't, another will; so pretty a gentleman need never want a lady. I am sure, if I was you, I would see the finest she that ever wore a head hanged, before I would go for a soldier for her.--Nay, don't blush so” (for indeed he did to a violent degree). “Why, you thought, sir, I knew nothing of the matter, I warrant you, about Madam Sophia.”--“How,” says Jones, starting up, “do you know my Sophia?”--“Do I! ay marry,” cries the landlady; “many's the time hath she lain in this house.”--“With her aunt, I suppose,” says Jones. “Why, there it is now,” cries the landlady. “Ay, ay, ay, I know the old lady very well. And a sweet young creature is Madam Sophia, that's the truth on't.”--“A sweet creature,” cries Jones; “O heavens!”\n\nAngels are painted fair to look like her. There's in her all that we believe of heav'n, Amazing brightness, purity, and truth, Eternal joy and everlasting love.\n\n“And could I ever have imagined that you had known my Sophia!”--“I wish,” says the landlady, “you knew half so much of her. What would you have given to have sat by her bed-side? What a delicious neck she hath! Her lovely limbs have stretched themselves in that very bed you now lie in.”--“Here!” cries Jones: “hath Sophia ever laid here?”--“Ay, ay, here; there, in that very bed,” says the landlady; “where I wish you had her this moment; and she may wish so too for anything I know to the contrary, for she hath mentioned your name to me.”--“Ha!” cries he; “did she ever mention her poor Jones? You flatter me now: I can never believe so much.”--“Why, then,” answered she, “as I hope to be saved, and may the devil fetch me if I speak a syllable more than the truth, I have heard her mention Mr Jones; but in a civil and modest way, I confess; yet I could perceive she thought a great deal more than she said.”--“O my dear woman!” cries Jones, “her thoughts of me I shall never be worthy of. Oh, she is all gentleness, kindness, goodness! Why was such a rascal as I born, ever to give her soft bosom a moment's uneasiness? Why am I cursed? I, who would undergo all the plagues and miseries which any daemon ever invented for mankind, to procure her any good; nay, torture itself could not be misery to me, did I but know that she was happy.”--“Why, look you there now,” says the landlady; “I told her you was a constant lovier.”--“But pray, madam, tell me when or where you knew anything of me; for I never was here before, nor do I remember ever to have seen you.”--“Nor is it possible you should,” answered she; “for you was a little thing when I had you in my lap at the squire's.”--“How, the squire's?” says Jones: “what, do you know that great and good Mr Allworthy then?”--“Yes, marry, do I,” says she: “who in the country doth not?”--“The fame of his goodness indeed,” answered Jones, “must have extended farther than this; but heaven only can know him--can know that benevolence which it copied from itself, and sent upon earth as its own pattern. Mankind are as ignorant of such divine goodness, as they are unworthy of it; but none so unworthy of it as myself. I, who was raised by him to such a height; taken in, as you must well know, a poor base-born child, adopted by him, and treated as his own son, to dare by my follies to disoblige him, to draw his vengeance upon me. Yes, I deserve it all; for I will never be so ungrateful as ever to think he hath done an act of injustice by me. No, I deserve to be turned out of doors, as I am. And now, madam,” says he, “I believe you will not blame me for turning soldier, especially with such a fortune as this in my pocket.” At which words he shook a purse, which had but very little in it, and which still appeared to the landlady to have less.\n\nMy good landlady was (according to vulgar phrase) struck all of a heap by this relation. She answered coldly, “That to be sure people were the best judges what was most proper for their circumstances. But hark,” says she, “I think I hear somebody call. Coming! coming! the devil's in all our volk; nobody hath any ears. I must go down-stairs; if you want any more breakfast the maid will come up. Coming!” At which words, without taking any leave, she flung out of the room; for the lower sort of people are very tenacious of respect; and though they are contented to give this gratis to persons of quality, yet they never confer it on those of their own order without taking care to be well paid for their pains.\n\nIn which the surgeon makes his second appearance.\n\nBefore we proceed any farther, that the reader may not be mistaken in imagining the landlady knew more than she did, nor surprized that she knew so much, it may be necessary to inform him that the lieutenant had acquainted her that the name of Sophia had been the occasion of the quarrel; and as for the rest of her knowledge, the sagacious reader will observe how she came by it in the preceding scene. Great curiosity was indeed mixed with her virtues; and she never willingly suffered any one to depart from her house, without enquiring as much as possible into their names, families, and fortunes.\n\nShe was no sooner gone than Jones, instead of animadverting on her behaviour, reflected that he was in the same bed which he was informed had held his dear Sophia. This occasioned a thousand fond and tender thoughts, which we would dwell longer upon, did we not consider that such kind of lovers will make a very inconsiderable part of our readers. In this situation the surgeon found him, when he came to dress his wound. The doctor perceiving, upon examination, that his pulse was disordered, and hearing that he had not slept, declared that he was in great danger; for he apprehended a fever was coming on, which he would have prevented by bleeding, but Jones would not submit, declaring he would lose no more blood; “and, doctor,” says he, “if you will be so kind only to dress my head, I have no doubt of being well in a day or two.”\n\n“I wish,” answered the surgeon, “I could assure your being well in a month or two. Well, indeed! No, no, people are not so soon well of such contusions; but, sir, I am not at this time of day to be instructed in my operations by a patient, and I insist on making a revulsion before I dress you.”\n\nJones persisted obstinately in his refusal, and the doctor at last yielded; telling him at the same time that he would not be answerable for the ill consequence, and hoped he would do him the justice to acknowledge that he had given him a contrary advice; which the patient promised he would.\n\nThe doctor retired into the kitchen, where, addressing himself to the landlady, he complained bitterly of the undutiful behaviour of his patient, who would not be blooded, though he was in a fever.\n\n“It is an eating fever then,” says the landlady; “for he hath devoured two swinging buttered toasts this morning for breakfast.”\n\n“Very likely,” says the doctor: “I have known people eat in a fever; and it is very easily accounted for; because the acidity occasioned by the febrile matter may stimulate the nerves of the diaphragm, and thereby occasion a craving which will not be easily distinguishable from a natural appetite; but the aliment will not be concreted, nor assimilated into chyle, and so will corrode the vascular orifices, and thus will aggravate the febrific symptoms. Indeed, I think the gentleman in a very dangerous way, and, if he is not blooded, I am afraid will die.”\n\n“Every man must die some time or other,” answered the good woman; “it is no business of mine. I hope, doctor, you would not have me hold him while you bleed him. But, hark'ee, a word in your ear; I would advise you, before you proceed too far, to take care who is to be your paymaster.”\n\n“Paymaster!” said the doctor, staring; “why, I've a gentleman under my hands, have I not?”\n\n“I imagined so as well as you,” said the landlady; “but, as my first husband used to say, everything is not what it looks to be. He is an arrant scrub, I assure you. However, take no notice that I mentioned anything to you of the matter; but I think people in business oft always to let one another know such things.”\n\n“And have I suffered such a fellow as this,” cries the doctor, in a passion, “to instruct me? Shall I hear my practice insulted by one who will not pay me? I am glad I have made this discovery in time. I will see now whether he will be blooded or no.” He then immediately went upstairs, and flinging open the door of the chamber with much violence, awaked poor Jones from a very sound nap, into which he was fallen, and, what was still worse, from a delicious dream concerning Sophia.\n\n“Will you be blooded or no?” cries the doctor, in a rage. “I have told you my resolution already,” answered Jones, “and I wish with all my heart you had taken my answer; for you have awaked me out of the sweetest sleep which I ever had in my life.”\n\n“Ay, ay,” cries the doctor; “many a man hath dozed away his life. Sleep is not always good, no more than food; but remember, I demand of you for the last time, will you be blooded?”--“I answer you for the last time,” said Jones, “I will not.”--“Then I wash my hands of you,” cries the doctor; “and I desire you to pay me for the trouble I have had already. Two journeys at 5s. each, two dressings at 5s. more, and half a crown for phlebotomy.”--“I hope,” said Jones, “you don't intend to leave me in this condition.”--“Indeed but I shall,” said the other. “Then,” said Jones, “you have used me rascally, and I will not pay you a farthing.”--“Very well,” cries the doctor; “the first loss is the best. What a pox did my landlady mean by sending for me to such vagabonds!” At which words he flung out of the room, and his patient turning himself about soon recovered his sleep; but his dream was unfortunately gone.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nIn which is introduced one of the pleasantest barbers that was ever recorded in history, the barber of Bagdad, or he in Don Quixote, not excepted.\n\nThe clock had now struck five when Jones awaked from a nap of seven hours, so much refreshed, and in such perfect health and spirits, that he resolved to get up and dress himself; for which purpose he unlocked his portmanteau, and took out clean linen, and a suit of cloaths; but first he slipt on a frock, and went down into the kitchen to bespeak something that might pacify certain tumults he found rising within his stomach.\n\nMeeting the landlady, he accosted her with great civility, and asked, “What he could have for dinner?”--“For dinner!” says she; “it is an odd time a day to think about dinner. There is nothing drest in the house, and the fire is almost out.”--“Well, but,” says he, “I must have something to eat, and it is almost indifferent to me what; for, to tell you the truth, I was never more hungry in my life.”--“Then,” says she, “I believe there is a piece of cold buttock and carrot, which will fit you.”--“Nothing better,” answered Jones; “but I should be obliged to you, if you would let it be fried.” To which the landlady consented, and said, smiling, “she was glad to see him so well recovered;” for the sweetness of our heroe's temper was almost irresistible; besides, she was really no ill-humoured woman at the bottom; but she loved money so much, that she hated everything which had the semblance of poverty.\n\nJones now returned in order to dress himself, while his dinner was preparing, and was, according to his orders, attended by the barber.\n\nThis barber, who went by the name of Little Benjamin, was a fellow of great oddity and humour, which had frequently let him into small inconveniencies, such as slaps in the face, kicks in the breech, broken bones, &c. For every one doth not understand a jest; and those who do are often displeased with being themselves the subjects of it. This vice was, however, incurable in him; and though he had often smarted for it, yet if ever he conceived a joke, he was certain to be delivered of it, without the least respect of persons, time, or place.\n\nHe had a great many other particularities in his character, which I shall not mention, as the reader will himself very easily perceive them, on his farther acquaintance with this extraordinary person.\n\nJones being impatient to be drest, for a reason which may be easily imagined, thought the shaver was very tedious in preparing his suds, and begged him to make haste; to which the other answered with much gravity, for he never discomposed his muscles on any account, “Festina lente, is a proverb which I learned long before I ever touched a razor.”--“I find, friend, you are a scholar,” replied Jones. “A poor one,” said the barber, “non omnia possumus omnes.”--“Again!” said Jones; “I fancy you are good at capping verses.”--“Excuse me, sir,” said the barber, “non tanto me dignor honore.” And then proceeding to his operation, “Sir,” said he, “since I have dealt in suds, I could never discover more than two reasons for shaving; the one is to get a beard, and the other to get rid of one. I conjecture, sir, it may not be long since you shaved from the former of these motives. Upon my word, you have had good success; for one may say of your beard, that it is tondenti gravior.”--“I conjecture,” says Jones, “that thou art a very comical fellow.”--“You mistake me widely, sir,” said the barber: “I am too much addicted to the study of philosophy; hinc illae lacrymae, sir; that's my misfortune. Too much learning hath been my ruin.”--“Indeed,” says Jones, “I confess, friend, you have more learning than generally belongs to your trade; but I can't see how it can have injured you.”--“Alas! sir,” answered the shaver, “my father disinherited me for it. He was a dancing-master; and because I could read before I could dance, he took an aversion to me, and left every farthing among his other children.--Will you please to have your temples--O la! I ask your pardon, I fancy there is hiatus in manuscriptis. I heard you was going to the wars; but I find it was a mistake.”--“Why do you conclude so?” says Jones. “Sure, sir,” answered the barber, “you are too wise a man to carry a broken head thither; for that would be carrying coals to Newcastle.”\n\n“Upon my word,” cries Jones, “thou art a very odd fellow, and I like thy humour extremely; I shall be very glad if thou wilt come to me after dinner, and drink a glass with me; I long to be better acquainted with thee.”\n\n“O dear sir!” said the barber, “I can do you twenty times as great a favour, if you will accept of it.”--“What is that, my friend?” cries Jones. “Why, I will drink a bottle with you if you please; for I dearly love good-nature; and as you have found me out to be a comical fellow, so I have no skill in physiognomy, if you are not one of the best-natured gentlemen in the universe.” Jones now walked downstairs neatly drest, and perhaps the fair Adonis was not a lovelier figure; and yet he had no charms for my landlady; for as that good woman did not resemble Venus at all in her person, so neither did she in her taste. Happy had it been for Nanny the chambermaid, if she had seen with the eyes of her mistress, for that poor girl fell so violently in love with Jones in five minutes, that her passion afterwards cost her many a sigh. This Nanny was extremely pretty, and altogether as coy; for she had refused a drawer, and one or two young farmers in the neighbourhood, but the bright eyes of our heroe thawed all her ice in a moment.\n\nWhen Jones returned to the kitchen, his cloth was not yet laid; nor indeed was there any occasion it should, his dinner remaining in statu quo, as did the fire which was to dress it. This disappointment might have put many a philosophical temper into a passion; but it had no such effect on Jones. He only gave the landlady a gentle rebuke, saying, “Since it was so difficult to get it heated he would eat the beef cold.” But now the good woman, whether moved by compassion, or by shame, or by whatever other motive, I cannot tell, first gave her servants a round scold for disobeying the orders which she had never given, and then bidding the drawer lay a napkin in the Sun, she set about the matter in good earnest, and soon accomplished it.\n\nThis Sun, into which Jones was now conducted, was truly named, as lucus a non lucendo; for it was an apartment into which the sun had scarce ever looked. It was indeed the worst room in the house; and happy was it for Jones that it was so. However, he was now too hungry to find any fault; but having once satisfied his appetite, he ordered the drawer to carry a bottle of wine into a better room, and expressed some resentment at having been shown into a dungeon.\n\nThe drawer having obeyed his commands, he was, after some time, attended by the barber, who would not indeed have suffered him to wait so long for his company had he not been listening in the kitchen to the landlady, who was entertaining a circle that she had gathered round her with the history of poor Jones, part of which she had extracted from his own lips, and the other part was her own ingenious composition; for she said “he was a poor parish boy, taken into the house of Squire Allworthy, where he was bred up as an apprentice, and now turned out of doors for his misdeeds, particularly for making love to his young mistress, and probably for robbing the house; for how else should he come by the little money he hath; and this,” says she, “is your gentleman, forsooth!”--“A servant of Squire Allworthy!” says the barber; “what's his name?”--“Why he told me his name was Jones,” says she: “perhaps he goes by a wrong name. Nay, and he told me, too, that the squire had maintained him as his own son, thof he had quarrelled with him now.”--“And if his name be Jones, he told you the truth,” said the barber; “for I have relations who live in that country; nay, and some people say he is his son.”--“Why doth he not go by the name of his father?”--“I can't tell that,” said the barber; “many people's sons don't go by the name of their father.”--“Nay,” said the landlady, “if I thought he was a gentleman's son, thof he was a bye-blow, I should behave to him in another guess manner; for many of these bye-blows come to be great men, and, as my poor first husband used to say, never affront any customer that's a gentleman.”\n\nA dialogue between Mr Jones and the barber.\n\nThis conversation passed partly while Jones was at dinner in his dungeon, and partly while he was expecting the barber in the parlour. And, as soon as it was ended, Mr Benjamin, as we have said, attended him, and was very kindly desired to sit down. Jones then filling out a glass of wine, drank his health by the appellation of doctissime tonsorum. “Ago tibi gratias, domine” said the barber; and then looking very steadfastly at Jones, he said, with great gravity, and with a seeming surprize, as if he had recollected a face he had seen before, “Sir, may I crave the favour to know if your name is not Jones?” To which the other answered, “That it was.”--“Proh deum atque hominum fidem!” says the barber; “how strangely things come to pass! Mr Jones, I am your most obedient servant. I find you do not know me, which indeed is no wonder, since you never saw me but once, and then you was very young. Pray, sir, how doth the good Squire Allworthy? how doth ille optimus omnium patronus?”--“I find,” said Jones, “you do indeed know me; but I have not the like happiness of recollecting you.”--“I do not wonder at that,” cries Benjamin; “but I am surprized I did not know you sooner, for you are not in the least altered. And pray, sir, may I, without offence, enquire whither you are travelling this way?”--“Fill the glass, Mr Barber,” said Jones, “and ask no more questions.”--“Nay, sir,” answered Benjamin, “I would not be troublesome; and I hope you don't think me a man of an impertinent curiosity, for that is a vice which nobody can lay to my charge; but I ask pardon; for when a gentleman of your figure travels without his servants, we may suppose him to be, as we say, in casu incognito, and perhaps I ought not to have mentioned your name.”--“I own,” says Jones, “I did not expect to have been so well known in this country as I find I am; yet, for particular reasons, I shall be obliged to you if you will not mention my name to any other person till I am gone from hence.”--“Pauca verba,” answered the barber;” and I wish no other here knew you but myself; for some people have tongues; but I promise you I can keep a secret. My enemies will allow me that virtue.”--“And yet that is not the characteristic of your profession, Mr Barber,” answered Jones. “Alas! sir,” replied Benjamin, “Non si male nunc et olim sic erit. I was not born nor bred a barber, I assure you. I have spent most of my time among gentlemen, and though I say it, I understand something of gentility. And if you had thought me as worthy of your confidence as you have some other people, I should have shown you I could have kept a secret better. I should not have degraded your name in a public kitchen; for indeed, sir, some people have not used you well; for besides making a public proclamation of what you told them of a quarrel between yourself and Squire Allworthy, they added lies of their own, things which I knew to be lies.”--“You surprize me greatly,” cries Jones. “Upon my word, sir,” answered Benjamin, “I tell the truth, and I need not tell you my landlady was the person. I am sure it moved me to hear the story, and I hope it is all false; for I have a great respect for you, I do assure you I have, and have had ever since the good-nature you showed to Black George, which was talked of all over the country, and I received more than one letter about it. Indeed, it made you beloved by everybody. You will pardon me, therefore; for it was real concern at what I heard made me ask many questions; for I have no impertinent curiosity about me: but I love good-nature and thence became amoris abundantia erga te.”\n\nEvery profession of friendship easily gains credit with the miserable; it is no wonder therefore, if Jones, who, besides his being miserable, was extremely open-hearted, very readily believed all the professions of Benjamin, and received him into his bosom. The scraps of Latin, some of which Benjamin applied properly enough, though it did not savour of profound literature, seemed yet to indicate something superior to a common barber; and so indeed did his whole behaviour. Jones therefore believed the truth of what he had said, as to his original and education; and at length, after much entreaty, he said, “Since you have heard, my friend, so much of my affairs, and seem so desirous to know the truth, if you will have patience to hear it, I will inform you of the whole.”--“Patience!” cries Benjamin, “that I will, if the chapter was never so long; and I am very much obliged to you for the honour you do me.”\n\nJones now began, and related the whole history, forgetting only a circumstance or two, namely, everything which passed on that day in which he had fought with Thwackum; and ended with his resolution to go to sea, till the rebellion in the North had made him change his purpose, and had brought him to the place where he then was.\n\nLittle Benjamin, who had been all attention, never once interrupted the narrative; but when it was ended he could not help observing, that there must be surely something more invented by his enemies, and told Mr Allworthy against him, or so good a man would never have dismissed one he had loved so tenderly, in such a manner. To which Jones answered, “He doubted not but such villanous arts had been made use of to destroy him.”\n\nAnd surely it was scarce possible for any one to have avoided making the same remark with the barber, who had not indeed heard from Jones one single circumstance upon which he was condemned; for his actions were not now placed in those injurious lights in which they had been misrepresented to Allworthy; nor could he mention those many false accusations which had been from time to time preferred against him to Allworthy: for with none of these he was himself acquainted. He had likewise, as we have observed, omitted many material facts in his present relation. Upon the whole, indeed, everything now appeared in such favourable colours to Jones, that malice itself would have found it no easy matter to fix any blame upon him.\n\nNot that Jones desired to conceal or to disguise the truth; nay, he would have been more unwilling to have suffered any censure to fall on Mr Allworthy for punishing him, than on his own actions for deserving it; but, in reality, so it happened, and so it always will happen; for let a man be never so honest, the account of his own conduct will, in spite of himself, be so very favourable, that his vices will come purified through his lips, and, like foul liquors well strained, will leave all their foulness behind. For though the facts themselves may appear, yet so different will be the motives, circumstances, and consequences, when a man tells his own story, and when his enemy tells it, that we scarce can recognise the facts to be one and the same.\n\nThough the barber had drank down this story with greedy ears, he was not yet satisfied. There was a circumstance behind which his curiosity, cold as it was, most eagerly longed for. Jones had mentioned the fact of his amour, and of his being the rival of Blifil, but had cautiously concealed the name of the young lady. The barber, therefore, after some hesitation, and many hums and hahs, at last begged leave to crave the name of the lady, who appeared to be the principal cause of all this mischief. Jones paused a moment, and then said, “Since I have trusted you with so much, and since, I am afraid, her name is become too publick already on this occasion, I will not conceal it from you. Her name is Sophia Western.”\n\n“Proh deum atque hominum fidem! Squire Western hath a daughter grown a woman!”--“Ay, and such a woman,” cries Jones, “that the world cannot match. No eye ever saw anything so beautiful; but that is her least excellence. Such sense! such goodness! Oh, I could praise her for ever, and yet should omit half her virtues!”--“Mr Western a daughter grown up!” cries the barber: “I remember the father a boy; well, Tempus edax rerum.”\n\nThe wine being now at an end, the barber pressed very eagerly to be his bottle; but Jones absolutely refused, saying, “He had already drank more than he ought: and that he now chose to retire to his room, where he wished he could procure himself a book.”--“A book!” cries Benjamin; “what book would you have? Latin or English? I have some curious books in both languages; such as Erasmi Colloquia, Ovid de Tristibus, Gradus ad Parnassum; and in English I have several of the best books, though some of them are a little torn; but I have a great part of Stowe's Chronicle; the sixth volume of Pope's Homer; the third volume of the Spectator; the second volume of Echard's Roman History; the Craftsman; Robinson Crusoe; Thomas a Kempis; and two volumes of Tom Brown's Works.”\n\n“Those last,” cries Jones, “are books I never saw, so if you please lend me one of those volumes.” The barber assured him he would be highly entertained, for he looked upon the author to have been one of the greatest wits that ever the nation produced. He then stepped to his house, which was hard by, and immediately returned; after which, the barber having received very strict injunctions of secrecy from Jones, and having sworn inviolably to maintain it, they separated; the barber went home, and Jones retired to his chamber.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nIn which more of the talents of Mr Benjamin will appear, as well as who this extraordinary person was.\n\nIn the morning Jones grew a little uneasy at the desertion of his surgeon, as he apprehended some inconvenience, or even danger, might attend the not dressing his wound; he enquired of the drawer, what other surgeons were to be met with in that neighbourhood. The drawer told him, there was one not far off; but he had known him often refuse to be concerned after another had been sent before him; “but, sir,” says he, “if you will take my advice, there is not a man in the kingdom can do your business better than the barber who was with you last night. We look upon him to be one of the ablest men at a cut in all this neighbourhood. For though he hath not been her above three months, he hath done several great cures.”\n\nThe drawer was presently dispatched for Little Benjamin, who being acquainted in what capacity he was wanted, prepared himself accordingly, and attended; but with so different an air and aspect from that which he wore when his basin was under his arm, that he could scarce be known to be the same person.\n\n“So, tonsor,” says Jones, “I find you have more trades than one; how came you not to inform me of this last night?”--“A surgeon,” answered Benjamin, with great gravity, “is a profession, not a trade. The reason why I did not acquaint you last night that I professed this art, was, that I then concluded you was under the hands of another gentleman, and I never love to interfere with my brethren in their business. Ars omnibus communis. But now, sir, if you please, I will inspect your head, and when I see into your skull, I will give my opinion of your case.”\n\nJones had no great faith in this new professor; however, he suffered him to open the bandage and to look at his wound; which as soon as he had done, Benjamin began to groan and shake his head violently. Upon which Jones, in a peevish manner, bid him not play the fool, but tell him in what condition he found him. “Shall I answer you as a surgeon, or a friend?” said Benjamin. “As a friend, and seriously,” said Jones. “Why then, upon my soul,” cries Benjamin, “it would require a great deal of art to keep you from being well after a very few dressings; and if you will suffer me to apply some salve of mine, I will answer for the success.” Jones gave his consent, and the plaister was applied accordingly.\n\n“There, sir,” cries Benjamin: “now I will, if you please, resume my former self; but a man is obliged to keep up some dignity in his countenance whilst he is performing these operations, or the world will not submit to be handled by him. You can't imagine, sir, of how much consequence a grave aspect is to a grave character. A barber may make you laugh, but a surgeon ought rather to make you cry.”\n\n“Mr Barber, or Mr Surgeon, or Mr Barber-surgeon,” said Jones. “O dear sir!” answered Benjamin, interrupting him, “Infandum, regina, jubes renovare dolorem. You recall to my mind that cruel separation of the united fraternities, so much to the prejudice of both bodies, as all separations must be, according to the old adage, Vis unita fortior; which to be sure there are not wanting some of one or of the other fraternity who are able to construe. What a blow was this to me, who unite both in my own person!” “Well, by whatever name you please to be called,” continued Jones, “you certainly are one of the oddest, most comical fellows I ever met with, and must have something very surprizing in your story, which you must confess I have a right to hear.”--“I do confess it,” answered Benjamin, “and will very readily acquaint you with it, when you have sufficient leisure, for I promise you it will require a good deal of time.” Jones told him, he could never be more at leisure than at present. “Well, then,” said Benjamin, “I will obey you; but first I will fasten the door, that none may interrupt us.” He did so, and then advancing with a solemn air to Jones, said: “I must begin by telling you, sir, that you yourself have been the greatest enemy I ever had.” Jones was a little startled at this sudden declaration. “I your enemy, sir!” says he, with much amazement, and some sternness in his look. “Nay, be not angry,” said Benjamin, “for I promise you I am not. You are perfectly innocent of having intended me any wrong; for you was then an infant: but I shall, I believe, unriddle all this the moment I mention my name. Did you never hear, sir, of one Partridge, who had the honour of being reputed your father, and the misfortune of being ruined by that honour?” “I have, indeed, heard of that Partridge,” says Jones, “and have always believed myself to be his son.” “Well, sir,” answered Benjamin, “I am that Partridge; but I here absolve you from all filial duty, for I do assure you, you are no son of mine.” “How!” replied Jones, “and is it possible that a false suspicion should have drawn all the ill consequences upon you, with which I am too well acquainted?” “It is possible,” cries Benjamin, “for it is so: but though it is natural enough for men to hate even the innocent causes of their sufferings, yet I am of a different temper. I have loved you ever since I heard of your behaviour to Black George, as I told you; and I am convinced, from this extraordinary meeting, that you are born to make me amends for all I have suffered on that account. Besides, I dreamt, the night before I saw you, that I stumbled over a stool without hurting myself; which plainly showed me something good was towards me: and last night I dreamt again, that I rode behind you on a milk-white mare, which is a very excellent dream, and betokens much good fortune, which I am resolved to pursue unless you have the cruelty to deny me.”\n\n“I should be very glad, Mr Partridge,” answered Jones, “to have it in my power to make you amends for your sufferings on my account, though at present I see no likelihood of it; however, I assure you I will deny you nothing which is in my power to grant.”\n\n“It is in your power sure enough,” replied Benjamin; “for I desire nothing more than leave to attend you in this expedition. Nay, I have so entirely set my heart upon it, that if you should refuse me, you will kill both a barber and a surgeon in one breath.”\n\nJones answered, smiling, that he should be very sorry to be the occasion of so much mischief to the public. He then advanced many prudential reasons, in order to dissuade Benjamin (whom we shall hereafter call Partridge) from his purpose; but all were in vain. Partridge relied strongly on his dream of the milk-white mare. “Besides, sir,” says he, “I promise you I have as good an inclination to the cause as any man can possibly have; and go I will, whether you admit me to go in your company or not.”\n\nJones, who was as much pleased with Partridge as Partridge could be with him, and who had not consulted his own inclination but the good of the other in desiring him to stay behind, when he found his friend so resolute, at last gave his consent; but then recollecting himself, he said, “Perhaps, Mr Partridge, you think I shall be able to support you, but I really am not;” and then taking out his purse, he told out nine guineas, which he declared were his whole fortune.\n\nPartridge answered, “That his dependence was only on his future favour; for he was thoroughly convinced he would shortly have enough in his power. At present, sir,” said he, “I believe I am rather the richer man of the two; but all I have is at your service, and at your disposal. I insist upon your taking the whole, and I beg only to attend you in the quality of your servant; Nil desperandum est Teucro duce et auspice Teucro”: but to this generous proposal concerning the money, Jones would by no means submit.\n\nIt was resolved to set out the next morning, when a difficulty arose concerning the baggage; for the portmanteau of Mr Jones was too large to be carried without a horse.\n\n“If I may presume to give my advice,” says Partridge, “this portmanteau, with everything in it, except a few shirts, should be left behind. Those I shall be easily able to carry for you, and the rest of your cloaths will remain very safe locked up in my house.”\n\nThis method was no sooner proposed than agreed to; and then the barber departed, in order to prepare everything for his intended expedition.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nContaining better reasons than any which have yet appeared for the conduct of Partridge; an apology for the weakness of Jones; and some further anecdotes concerning my landlady.\n\nThough Partridge was one of the most superstitious of men, he would hardly perhaps have desired to accompany Jones on his expedition merely from the omens of the joint-stool and white mare, if his prospect had been no better than to have shared the plunder gained in the field of battle. In fact, when Partridge came to ruminate on the relation he had heard from Jones, he could not reconcile to himself that Mr Allworthy should turn his son (for so he most firmly believed him to be) out of doors, for any reason which he had heard assigned. He concluded, therefore, that the whole was a fiction, and that Jones, of whom he had often from his correspondents heard the wildest character, had in reality run away from his father. It came into his head, therefore, that if he could prevail with the young gentleman to return back to his father, he should by that means render a service to Allworthy, which would obliterate all his former anger; nay, indeed, he conceived that very anger was counterfeited, and that Allworthy had sacrificed him to his own reputation. And this suspicion indeed he well accounted for, from the tender behaviour of that excellent man to the foundling child; from his great severity to Partridge, who, knowing himself to be innocent, could not conceive that any other should think him guilty; lastly, from the allowance which he had privately received long after the annuity had been publickly taken from him, and which he looked upon as a kind of smart-money, or rather by way of atonement for injustice; for it is very uncommon, I believe, for men to ascribe the benefactions they receive to pure charity, when they can possibly impute them to any other motive. If he could by any means therefore persuade the young gentleman to return home, he doubted not but that he should again be received into the favour of Allworthy, and well rewarded for his pains; nay, and should be again restored to his native country; a restoration which Ulysses himself never wished more heartily than poor Partridge.\n\nAs for Jones, he was well satisfied with the truth of what the other had asserted, and believed that Partridge had no other inducements but love to him, and zeal for the cause; a blameable want of caution and diffidence in the veracity of others, in which he was highly worthy of censure. To say the truth, there are but two ways by which men become possessed of this excellent quality. The one is from long experience, and the other is from nature; which last, I presume, is often meant by genius, or great natural parts; and it is infinitely the better of the two, not only as we are masters of it much earlier in life, but as it is much more infallible and conclusive; for a man who hath been imposed on by ever so many, may still hope to find others more honest; whereas he who receives certain necessary admonitions from within, that this is impossible, must have very little understanding indeed, if he ever renders himself liable to be once deceived. As Jones had not this gift from nature, he was too young to have gained it by experience; for at the diffident wisdom which is to be acquired this way, we seldom arrive till very late in life; which is perhaps the reason why some old men are apt to despise the understandings of all those who are a little younger than themselves.\n\nJones spent most part of the day in the company of a new acquaintance. This was no other than the landlord of the house, or rather the husband of the landlady. He had but lately made his descent downstairs, after a long fit of the gout, in which distemper he was generally confined to his room during one half of the year; and during the rest, he walked about the house, smoaked his pipe, and drank his bottle with his friends, without concerning himself in the least with any kind of business. He had been bred, as they call it, a gentleman; that is, bred up to do nothing; and had spent a very small fortune, which he inherited from an industrious farmer his uncle, in hunting, horse-racing, and cock-fighting, and had been married by my landlady for certain purposes, which he had long since desisted from answering; for which she hated him heartily. But as he was a surly kind of fellow, so she contented herself with frequently upbraiding him by disadvantageous comparisons with her first husband, whose praise she had eternally in her mouth; and as she was for the most part mistress of the profit, so she was satisfied to take upon herself the care and government of the family, and, after a long successless struggle, to suffer her husband to be master of himself.\n\nIn the evening, when Jones retired to his room, a small dispute arose between this fond couple concerning him:--“What,” says the wife, “you have been tippling with the gentleman, I see?”--“Yes,” answered the husband, “we have cracked a bottle together, and a very gentlemanlike man he is, and hath a very pretty notion of horse-flesh. Indeed, he is young, and hath not seen much of the world; for I believe he hath been at very few horse-races.”--“Oho! he is one of your order, is he?” replies the landlady: “he must be a gentleman to be sure, if he is a horse-racer. The devil fetch such gentry! I am sure I wish I had never seen any of them. I have reason to love horse-racers truly!”--“That you have,” says the husband; “for I was one, you know.”--“Yes,” answered she, “you are a pure one indeed. As my first husband used to say, I may put all the good I have ever got by you in my eyes, and see never the worse.”--“D--n your first husband!” cries he. “Don't d--n a better man than yourself,” answered the wife: “if he had been alive, you durst not have done it.”--“Then you think,” says he, “I have not so much courage as yourself; for you have d--n'd him often in my hearing.”--“If I did,” says she, “I have repented of it many's the good time and oft. And if he was so good to forgive me a word spoken in haste or so, it doth not become such a one as you to twitter me. He was a husband to me, he was; and if ever I did make use of an ill word or so in a passion, I never called him rascal; I should have told a lie, if I had called him rascal.” Much more she said, but not in his hearing; for having lighted his pipe, he staggered off as fast as he could. We shall therefore transcribe no more of her speech, as it approached still nearer and nearer to a subject too indelicate to find any place in this history.\n\nEarly in the morning Partridge appeared at the bedside of Jones, ready equipped for the journey, with his knapsack at his back. This was his own workmanship; for besides his other trades, he was no indifferent taylor. He had already put up his whole stock of linen in it, consisting of four shirts, to which he now added eight for Mr Jones; and then packing up the portmanteau, he was departing with it towards his own house, but was stopt in his way by the landlady, who refused to suffer any removals till after the payment of the reckoning.\n\nThe landlady was, as we have said, absolute governess in these regions; it was therefore necessary to comply with her rules; so the bill was presently writ out, which amounted to a much larger sum than might have been expected, from the entertainment which Jones had met with. But here we are obliged to disclose some maxims, which publicans hold to be the grand mysteries of their trade. The first is, If they have anything good in their house (which indeed very seldom happens) to produce it only to persons who travel with great equipages. 2dly, To charge the same for the very worst provisions, as if they were the best. And lastly, If any of their guests call but for little, to make them pay a double price for everything they have; so that the amount by the head may be much the same.\n\nThe bill being made and discharged, Jones set forward with Partridge, carrying his knapsack; nor did the landlady condescend to wish him a good journey; for this was, it seems, an inn frequented by people of fashion; and I know not whence it is, but all those who get their livelihood by people of fashion, contract as much insolence to the rest of mankind, as if they really belonged to that rank themselves.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nJones arrives at Gloucester, and goes to the Bell; the character of that house, and of a petty-fogger which he there meets with.\n\nMr Jones and Partridge, or Little Benjamin (which epithet of Little was perhaps given him ironically, he being in reality near six feet high), having left their last quarters in the manner before described, travelled on to Gloucester without meeting any adventure worth relating.\n\nBeing arrived here, they chose for their house of entertainment the sign of the Bell, an excellent house indeed, and which I do most seriously recommend to every reader who shall visit this antient city. The master of it is brother to the great preacher Whitefield; but is absolutely untainted with the pernicious principles of Methodism, or of any other heretical sect. He is indeed a very honest plain man, and, in my opinion, not likely to create any disturbance either in church or state. His wife hath, I believe, had much pretension to beauty, and is still a very fine woman. Her person and deportment might have made a shining figure in the politest assemblies; but though she must be conscious of this and many other perfections, she seems perfectly contented with, and resigned to, that state of life to which she is called; and this resignation is entirely owing to the prudence and wisdom of her temper; for she is at present as free from any Methodistical notions as her husband: I say at present; for she freely confesses that her brother's documents made at first some impression upon her, and that she had put herself to the expense of a long hood, in order to attend the extraordinary emotions of the Spirit; but having found, during an experiment of three weeks, no emotions, she says, worth a farthing, she very wisely laid by her hood, and abandoned the sect. To be concise, she is a very friendly good-natured woman; and so industrious to oblige, that the guests must be of a very morose disposition who are not extremely well satisfied in her house.\n\nMrs Whitefield happened to be in the yard when Jones and his attendant marched in. Her sagacity soon discovered in the air of our heroe something which distinguished him from the vulgar. She ordered her servants, therefore, immediately to show him into a room, and presently afterwards invited him to dinner with herself; which invitation he very thankfully accepted; for indeed much less agreeable company than that of Mrs Whitefield, and a much worse entertainment than she had provided, would have been welcome after so long fasting and so long a walk.\n\nBesides Mr Jones and the good governess of the mansion, there sat down at table an attorney of Salisbury, indeed the very same who had brought the news of Mrs Blifil's death to Mr Allworthy, and whose name, which I think we did not before mention, was Dowling: there was likewise present another person, who stiled himself a lawyer, and who lived somewhere near Linlinch, in Somersetshire. This fellow, I say, stiled himself a lawyer, but was indeed a most vile petty-fogger, without sense or knowledge of any kind; one of those who may be termed train-bearers to the law; a sort of supernumeraries in the profession, who are the hackneys of attorneys, and will ride more miles for half-a-crown than a postboy.\n\nDuring the time of dinner, the Somersetshire lawyer recollected the face of Jones, which he had seen at Mr Allworthy's; for he had often visited in that gentleman's kitchen. He therefore took occasion to enquire after the good family there with that familiarity which would have become an intimate friend or acquaintance of Mr Allworthy; and indeed he did all in his power to insinuate himself to be such, though he had never had the honour of speaking to any person in that family higher than the butler. Jones answered all his questions with much civility, though he never remembered to have seen the petty-fogger before; and though he concluded, from the outward appearance and behaviour of the man, that he usurped a freedom with his betters, to which he was by no means intitled.\n\nAs the conversation of fellows of this kind is of all others the most detestable to men of any sense, the cloth was no sooner removed than Mr Jones withdrew, and a little barbarously left poor Mrs Whitefield to do a penance, which I have often heard Mr Timothy Harris, and other publicans of good taste, lament, as the severest lot annexed to their calling, namely, that of being obliged to keep company with their guests.\n\nJones had no sooner quitted the room, than the petty-fogger, in a whispering tone, asked Mrs Whitefield, “If she knew who that fine spark was?” She answered, “She had never seen the gentleman before.”--“The gentleman, indeed!” replied the petty-fogger; “a pretty gentleman, truly! Why, he's the bastard of a fellow who was hanged for horse-stealing. He was dropt at Squire Allworthy's door, where one of the servants found him in a box so full of rain-water, that he would certainly have been drowned, had he not been reserved for another fate.”--“Ay, ay, you need not mention it, I protest: we understand what that fate is very well,” cries Dowling, with a most facetious grin.--“Well,” continued the other, “the squire ordered him to be taken in; for he is a timbersome man everybody knows, and was afraid of drawing himself into a scrape; and there the bastard was bred up, and fed, and cloathified all to the world like any gentleman; and there he got one of the servant-maids with child, and persuaded her to swear it to the squire himself; and afterwards he broke the arm of one Mr Thwackum a clergyman, only because he reprimanded him for following whores; and afterwards he snapt a pistol at Mr Blifil behind his back; and once, when Squire Allworthy was sick, he got a drum, and beat it all over the house to prevent him from sleeping; and twenty other pranks he hath played, for all which, about four or five days ago, just before I left the country, the squire stripped him stark naked, and turned him out of doors.”\n\n“And very justly too, I protest,” cries Dowling; “I would turn my own son out of doors, if he was guilty of half as much. And pray what is the name of this pretty gentleman?”\n\n“The name o' un?” answered Petty-fogger; “why, he is called Thomas Jones.”\n\n“Jones!” answered Dowling a little eagerly; “what, Mr Jones that lived at Mr Allworthy's? was that the gentleman that dined with us?”--“The very same,” said the other. “I have heard of the gentleman,” cries Dowling, “often; but I never heard any ill character of him.”--“And I am sure,” says Mrs Whitefield, “if half what this gentleman hath said be true, Mr Jones hath the most deceitful countenance I ever saw; for sure his looks promise something very different; and I must say, for the little I have seen of him, he is as civil a well-bred man as you would wish to converse with.”\n\nPetty-fogger calling to mind that he had not been sworn, as he usually was, before he gave his evidence, now bound what he had declared with so many oaths and imprecations that the landlady's ears were shocked, and she put a stop to his swearing, by assuring him of her belief. Upon which he said, “I hope, madam, you imagine I would scorn to tell such things of any man, unless I knew them to be true. What interest have I in taking away the reputation of a man who never injured me? I promise you every syllable of what I have said is fact, and the whole country knows it.”\n\nAs Mrs Whitefield had no reason to suspect that the petty-fogger had any motive or temptation to abuse Jones, the reader cannot blame her for believing what he so confidently affirmed with many oaths. She accordingly gave up her skill in physiognomy, and hence-forwards conceived so ill an opinion of her guest, that she heartily wished him out of her house.\n\nThis dislike was now farther increased by a report which Mr Whitefield made from the kitchen, where Partridge had informed the company, “That though he carried the knapsack, and contented himself with staying among servants, while Tom Jones (as he called him) was regaling in the parlour, he was not his servant, but only a friend and companion, and as good a gentleman as Mr Jones himself.”\n\nDowling sat all this while silent, biting his fingers, making faces, grinning, and looking wonderfully arch; at last he opened his lips, and protested that the gentleman looked like another sort of man. He then called for his bill with the utmost haste, declared he must be at Hereford that evening, lamented his great hurry of business, and wished he could divide himself into twenty pieces, in order to be at once in twenty places.\n\nThe petty-fogger now likewise departed, and then Jones desired the favour of Mrs Whitefield's company to drink tea with him; but she refused, and with a manner so different from that with which she had received him at dinner, that it a little surprized him. And now he soon perceived her behaviour totally changed; for instead of that natural affability which we have before celebrated, she wore a constrained severity on her countenance, which was so disagreeable to Mr Jones, that he resolved, however late, to quit the house that evening.\n\nHe did indeed account somewhat unfairly for this sudden change; for besides some hard and unjust surmises concerning female fickleness and mutability, he began to suspect that he owed this want of civility to his want of horses; a sort of animals which, as they dirty no sheets, are thought in inns to pay better for their beds than their riders, and are therefore considered as the more desirable company; but Mrs Whitefield, to do her justice, had a much more liberal way of thinking. She was perfectly well-bred, and could be very civil to a gentleman, though he walked on foot. In reality, she looked on our heroe as a sorry scoundrel, and therefore treated him as such, for which not even Jones himself, had he known as much as the reader, could have blamed her; nay, on the contrary, he must have approved her conduct, and have esteemed her the more for the disrespect shown towards himself. This is indeed a most aggravating circumstance, which attends depriving men unjustly of their reputation; for a man who is conscious of having an ill character, cannot justly be angry with those who neglect and slight him; but ought rather to despise such as affect his conversation, unless where a perfect intimacy must have convinced them that their friend's character hath been falsely and injuriously aspersed.\n\nThis was not, however, the case of Jones; for as he was a perfect stranger to the truth, so he was with good reason offended at the treatment he received. He therefore paid his reckoning and departed, highly against the will of Mr Partridge, who having remonstrated much against it to no purpose, at last condescended to take up his knapsack and to attend his friend.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\nContaining several dialogues between Jones and Partridge, concerning love, cold, hunger, and other matters; with the lucky and narrow escape of Partridge, as he was on the very brink of making a fatal discovery to his friend.\n\nThe shadows began now to descend larger from the high mountains; the feathered creation had betaken themselves to their rest. Now the highest order of mortals were sitting down to their dinners, and the lowest order to their suppers. In a word, the clock struck five just as Mr Jones took his leave of Gloucester; an hour at which (as it was now mid-winter) the dirty fingers of Night would have drawn her sable curtain over the universe, had not the moon forbid her, who now, with a face as broad and as red as those of some jolly mortals, who, like her, turn night into day, began to rise from her bed, where she had slumbered away the day, in order to sit up all night. Jones had not travelled far before he paid his compliments to that beautiful planet, and, turning to his companion, asked him if he had ever beheld so delicious an evening? Partridge making no ready answer to his question, he proceeded to comment on the beauty of the moon, and repeated some passages from Milton, who hath certainly excelled all other poets in his description of the heavenly luminaries. He then told Partridge the story from the Spectator, of two lovers who had agreed to entertain themselves when they were at a great distance from each other, by repairing, at a certain fixed hour, to look at the moon; thus pleasing themselves with the thought that they were both employed in contemplating the same object at the same time. “Those lovers,” added he, “must have had souls truly capable of feeling all the tenderness of the sublimest of all human passions.”--“Very probably,” cries Partridge: “but I envy them more, if they had bodies incapable of feeling cold; for I am almost frozen to death, and am very much afraid I shall lose a piece of my nose before we get to another house of entertainment. Nay, truly, we may well expect some judgment should happen to us for our folly in running away so by night from one of the most excellent inns I ever set my foot into. I am sure I never saw more good things in my life, and the greatest lord in the land cannot live better in his own house than he may there. And to forsake such a house, and go a rambling about the country, the Lord knows whither, per devia rura viarum, I say nothing for my part; but some people might not have charity enough to conclude we were in our sober senses.”--“Fie upon it, Mr Partridge!” says Jones, “have a better heart; consider you are going to face an enemy; and are you afraid of facing a little cold? I wish, indeed, we had a guide to advise which of these roads we should take.”--“May I be so bold,” says Partridge, “to offer my advice? Interdum stultus opportuna loquitur”--“Why, which of them,” cries Jones, “would you recommend?”--“Truly neither of them,” answered Partridge. “The only road we can be certain of finding, is the road we came. A good hearty pace will bring us back to Gloucester in an hour; but if we go forward, the Lord Harry knows when we shall arrive at any place; for I see at least fifty miles before me, and no house in all the way.”--“You see, indeed, a very fair prospect,” says Jones, “which receives great additional beauty from the extreme lustre of the moon. However, I will keep the left-hand track, as that seems to lead directly to those hills, which we were informed lie not far from Worcester. And here, if you are inclined to quit me, you may, and return back again; but for my part, I am resolved to go forward.”\n\n“It is unkind in you, sir,” says Partridge, “to suspect me of any such intention. What I have advised hath been as much on your account as on my own: but since you are determined to go on, I am as much determined to follow. I prae sequar te.”\n\nThey now travelled some miles without speaking to each other, during which suspense of discourse Jones often sighed, and Benjamin groaned as bitterly, though from a very different reason. At length Jones made a full stop, and turning about, cries, “Who knows, Partridge, but the loveliest creature in the universe may have her eyes now fixed on that very moon which I behold at this instant?” “Very likely, sir,” answered Partridge; “and if my eyes were fixed on a good surloin of roast beef, the devil might take the moon and her horns into the bargain.” “Did ever Tramontane make such an answer?” cries Jones. “Prithee, Partridge, wast thou ever susceptible of love in thy life, or hath time worn away all the traces of it from thy memory?” “Alack-a-day!” cries Partridge, “well would it have been for me if I had never known what love was. Infandum regina jubes renovare dolorem. I am sure I have tasted all the tenderness, and sublimities, and bitternesses of the passion.” “Was your mistress unkind, then?” says Jones. “Very unkind, indeed, sir,” answered Partridge; “for she married me, and made one of the most confounded wives in the world. However, heaven be praised, she's gone; and if I believed she was in the moon, according to a book I once read, which teaches that to be the receptacle of departed spirits, I would never look at it for fear of seeing her; but I wish, sir, that the moon was a looking-glass for your sake, and that Miss Sophia Western was now placed before it.” “My dear Partridge,” cries Jones, “what a thought was there! A thought which I am certain could never have entered into any mind but that of a lover. O Partridge! could I hope once again to see that face; but, alas! all those golden dreams are vanished for ever, and my only refuge from future misery is to forget the object of all my former happiness.” “And do you really despair of ever seeing Miss Western again?” answered Partridge; “if you will follow my advice I will engage you shall not only see her but have her in your arms.” “Ha! do not awaken a thought of that nature,” cries Jones: “I have struggled sufficiently to conquer all such wishes already.” “Nay,” answered Partridge, “if you do not wish to have your mistress in your arms you are a most extraordinary lover indeed.” “Well, well,” says Jones, “let us avoid this subject; but pray what is your advice?” “To give it you in the military phrase, then,” says Partridge, “as we are soldiers, `To the right about.' Let us return the way we came; we may yet reach Gloucester to-night, though late; whereas, if we proceed, we are likely, for aught I see, to ramble about for ever without coming either to house or home.” “I have already told you my resolution is to go on,” answered Jones; “but I would have you go back. I am obliged to you for your company hither; and I beg you to accept a guinea as a small instance of my gratitude. Nay, it would be cruel in me to suffer you to go any farther; for, to deal plainly with you, my chief end and desire is a glorious death in the service of my king and country.” “As for your money,” replied Partridge, “I beg, sir, you will put it up; I will receive none of you at this time; for at present I am, I believe, the richer man of the two. And as your resolution is to go on, so mine is to follow you if you do. Nay, now my presence appears absolutely necessary to take care of you, since your intentions are so desperate; for I promise you my views are much more prudent; as you are resolved to fall in battle if you can, so I am resolved as firmly to come to no hurt if I can help it. And, indeed, I have the comfort to think there will be but little danger; for a popish priest told me the other day the business would soon be over, and he believed without a battle.” “A popish priest!” cries Jones, “I have heard is not always to be believed when he speaks in behalf of his religion.” “Yes, but so far,” answered the other, “from speaking in behalf of his religion, he assured me the Catholicks did not expect to be any gainers by the change; for that Prince Charles was as good a Protestant as any in England; and that nothing but regard to right made him and the rest of the popish party to be Jacobites.”--“I believe him to be as much a Protestant as I believe he hath any right,” says Jones; “and I make no doubt of our success, but not without a battle. So that I am not so sanguine as your friend the popish priest.” “Nay, to be sure, sir,” answered Partridge, “all the prophecies I have ever read speak of a great deal of blood to be spilt in the quarrel, and the miller with three thumbs, who is now alive, is to hold the horses of three kings, up to his knees in blood. Lord, have mercy upon us all, and send better times!” “With what stuff and nonsense hast thou filled thy head!” answered Jones: “this too, I suppose, comes from the popish priest. Monsters and prodigies are the proper arguments to support monstrous and absurd doctrines. The cause of King George is the cause of liberty and true religion. In other words, it is the cause of common sense, my boy, and I warrant you will succeed, though Briarius himself was to rise again with his hundred thumbs, and to turn miller.” Partridge made no reply to this. He was, indeed, cast into the utmost confusion by this declaration of Jones. For, to inform the reader of a secret, which he had no proper opportunity of revealing before, Partridge was in truth a Jacobite, and had concluded that Jones was of the same party, and was now proceeding to join the rebels. An opinion which was not without foundation. For the tall, long-sided dame, mentioned by Hudibras--that many-eyed, many-tongued, many-mouthed, many-eared monster of Virgil, had related the story of the quarrel between Jones and the officer, with the usual regard to truth. She had, indeed, changed the name of Sophia into that of the Pretender, and had reported, that drinking his health was the cause for which Jones was knocked down. This Partridge had heard, and most firmly believed. 'Tis no wonder, therefore, that he had thence entertained the above-mentioned opinion of Jones; and which he had almost discovered to him before he found out his own mistake. And at this the reader will be the less inclined to wonder, if he pleases to recollect the doubtful phrase in which Jones first communicated his resolution to Mr Partridge; and, indeed, had the words been less ambiguous, Partridge might very well have construed them as he did; being persuaded as he was that the whole nation were of the same inclination in their hearts; nor did it stagger him that Jones had travelled in the company of soldiers; for he had the same opinion of the army which he had of the rest of the people.\n\nBut however well affected he might be to James or Charles, he was still much more attached to Little Benjamin than to either; for which reason he no sooner discovered the principles of his fellow-traveller than he thought proper to conceal and outwardly give up his own to the man on whom he depended for the making his fortune, since he by no means believed the affairs of Jones to be so desperate as they really were with Mr Allworthy; for as he had kept a constant correspondence with some of his neighbours since he left that country, he had heard much, indeed more than was true, of the great affection Mr Allworthy bore this young man, who, as Partridge had been instructed, was to be that gentleman's heir, and whom, as we have said, he did not in the least doubt to be his son.\n\nHe imagined therefore that whatever quarrel was between them, it would be certainly made up at the return of Mr Jones; an event from which he promised great advantages, if he could take this opportunity of ingratiating himself with that young gentleman; and if he could by any means be instrumental in procuring his return, he doubted not, as we have before said, but it would as highly advance him in the favour of Mr Allworthy.\n\nWe have already observed, that he was a very good-natured fellow, and he hath himself declared the violent attachment he had to the person and character of Jones; but possibly the views which I have just before mentioned, might likewise have some little share in prompting him to undertake this expedition, at least in urging him to continue it, after he had discovered that his master and himself, like some prudent fathers and sons, though they travelled together in great friendship, had embraced opposite parties. I am led into this conjecture, by having remarked, that though love, friendship, esteem, and such like, have very powerful operations in the human mind; interest, however, is an ingredient seldom omitted by wise men, when they would work others to their own purposes. This is indeed a most excellent medicine, and, like Ward's pill, flies at once to the particular part of the body on which you desire to operate, whether it be the tongue, the hand, or any other member, where it scarce ever fails of immediately producing the desired effect.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nIn which our travellers meet with a very extraordinary adventure.\n\nJust as Jones and his friend came to the end of their dialogue in the preceding chapter, they arrived at the bottom of a very steep hill. Here Jones stopt short, and directing his eyes upwards, stood for a while silent. At length he called to his companion, and said, “Partridge, I wish I was at the top of this hill; it must certainly afford a most charming prospect, especially by this light; for the solemn gloom which the moon casts on all objects, is beyond expression beautiful, especially to an imagination which is desirous of cultivating melancholy ideas.”--“Very probably,” answered Partridge; “but if the top of the hill be properest to produce melancholy thoughts, I suppose the bottom is the likeliest to produce merry ones, and these I take to be much the better of the two. I protest you have made my blood run cold with the very mentioning the top of that mountain; which seems to me to be one of the highest in the world. No, no, if we look for anything, let it be for a place under ground, to screen ourselves from the frost.”--“Do so,” said Jones; “let it be but within hearing of this place, and I will hallow to you at my return back.”--“Surely, sir, you are not mad,” said Partridge.--“Indeed, I am,” answered Jones, “if ascending this hill be madness; but as you complain so much of the cold already, I would have you stay below. I will certainly return to you within an hour.”--“Pardon me, sir,” cries Partridge; “I have determined to follow you wherever you go.” Indeed he was now afraid to stay behind; for though he was coward enough in all respects, yet his chief fear was that of ghosts, with which the present time of night, and the wildness of the place, extremely well suited.\n\nAt this instant Partridge espied a glimmering light through some trees, which seemed very near to them. He immediately cried out in a rapture, “Oh, sir! Heaven hath at last heard my prayers, and hath brought us to a house; perhaps it may be an inn. Let me beseech you, sir, if you have any compassion either for me or yourself, do not despise the goodness of Providence, but let us go directly to yon light. Whether it be a public-house or no, I am sure if they be Christians that dwell there, they will not refuse a little house-room to persons in our miserable condition.” Jones at length yielded to the earnest supplications of Partridge, and both together made directly towards the place whence the light issued.\n\nThey soon arrived at the door of this house, or cottage, for it might be called either, without much impropriety. Here Jones knocked several times without receiving any answer from within; at which Partridge, whose head was full of nothing but of ghosts, devils, witches, and such like, began to tremble, crying, “Lord, have mercy upon us! surely the people must be all dead. I can see no light neither now, and yet I am certain I saw a candle burning but a moment before.--Well! I have heard of such things.”--“What hast thou heard of?” said Jones. “The people are either fast asleep, or probably, as this is a lonely place, are afraid to open their door.” He then began to vociferate pretty loudly, and at last an old woman, opening an upper casement, asked, Who they were, and what they wanted? Jones answered, They were travellers who had lost their way, and having seen a light in the window, had been led thither in hopes of finding some fire to warm themselves. “Whoever you are,” cries the woman, “you have no business here; nor shall I open the door to any one at this time of night.” Partridge, whom the sound of a human voice had recovered from his fright, fell to the most earnest supplications to be admitted for a few minutes to the fire, saying, he was almost dead with the cold; to which fear had indeed contributed equally with the frost. He assured her that the gentleman who spoke to her was one of the greatest squires in the country; and made use of every argument, save one, which Jones afterwards effectually added; and this was, the promise of half-a-crown;--a bribe too great to be resisted by such a person, especially as the genteel appearance of Jones, which the light of the moon plainly discovered to her, together with his affable behaviour, had entirely subdued those apprehensions of thieves which she had at first conceived. She agreed, therefore, at last, to let them in; where Partridge, to his infinite joy, found a good fire ready for his reception.\n\nThe poor fellow, however, had no sooner warmed himself, than those thoughts which were always uppermost in his mind, began a little to disturb his brain. There was no article of his creed in which he had a stronger faith than he had in witchcraft, nor can the reader conceive a figure more adapted to inspire this idea, than the old woman who now stood before him. She answered exactly to that picture drawn by Otway in his Orphan. Indeed, if this woman had lived in the reign of James the First, her appearance alone would have hanged her, almost without any evidence.\n\nMany circumstances likewise conspired to confirm Partridge in his opinion. Her living, as he then imagined, by herself in so lonely a place; and in a house, the outside of which seemed much too good for her, but its inside was furnished in the most neat and elegant manner. To say the truth, Jones himself was not a little surprized at what he saw; for, besides the extraordinary neatness of the room, it was adorned with a great number of nicknacks and curiosities, which might have engaged the attention of a virtuoso.\n\nWhile Jones was admiring these things, and Partridge sat trembling with the firm belief that he was in the house of a witch, the old woman said, “I hope, gentlemen, you will make what haste you can; for I expect my master presently, and I would not for double the money he should find you here.”--“Then you have a master?” cried Jones. “Indeed, you will excuse me, good woman, but I was surprized to see all those fine things in your house.”--“Ah, sir,” said she, “if the twentieth part of these things were mine, I should think myself a rich woman. But pray, sir, do not stay much longer, for I look for him in every minute.”--“Why, sure he would not be angry with you,” said Jones, “for doing a common act of charity?”--“Alack-a-day, sir!” said she, “he is a strange man, not at all like other people. He keeps no company with anybody, and seldom walks out but by night, for he doth not care to be seen; and all the country people are as much afraid of meeting him; for his dress is enough to frighten those who are not used to it. They call him, the Man of the Hill (for there he walks by night), and the country people are not, I believe, more afraid of the devil himself. He would be terribly angry if he found you here.”--“Pray, sir,” says Partridge, “don't let us offend the gentleman; I am ready to walk, and was never warmer in my life. Do pray, sir, let us go. Here are pistols over the chimney: who knows whether they be charged or no, or what he may do with them?”--“Fear nothing, Partridge,” cries Jones; “I will secure thee from danger.”--“Nay, for matter o' that, he never doth any mischief,” said the woman; “but to be sure it is necessary he should keep some arms for his own safety; for his house hath been beset more than once; and it is not many nights ago that we thought we heard thieves about it: for my own part, I have often wondered that he is not murdered by some villain or other, as he walks out by himself at such hours; but then, as I said, the people are afraid of him; and besides, they think, I suppose, he hath nothing about him worth taking.”--“I should imagine, by this collection of rarities,” cries Jones, “that your master had been a traveller.”--“Yes, sir,” answered she, “he hath been a very great one: there be few gentlemen that know more of all matters than he. I fancy he hath been crost in love, or whatever it is I know not; but I have lived with him above these thirty years, and in all that time he hath hardly spoke to six living people.” She then again solicited their departure, in which she was backed by Partridge; but Jones purposely protracted the time, for his curiosity was greatly raised to see this extraordinary person. Though the old woman, therefore, concluded every one of her answers with desiring him to be gone, and Partridge proceeded so far as to pull him by the sleeve, he still continued to invent new questions, till the old woman, with an affrighted countenance, declared she heard her master's signal; and at the same instant more than one voice was heard without the door, crying, “D--n your blood, show us your money this instant. Your money, you villain, or we will blow your brains about your ears.”\n\n“O, good heaven!” cries the old woman, “some villains, to be sure, have attacked my master. O la! what shall I do? what shall I do?”--“How!” cries Jones, “how!--Are these pistols loaded?”--“O, good sir, there is nothing in them, indeed. O pray don't murder us, gentlemen!” (for in reality she now had the same opinion of those within as she had of those without). Jones made her no answer; but snatching an old broad sword which hung in the room, he instantly sallied out, where he found the old gentleman struggling with two ruffians, and begging for mercy. Jones asked no questions, but fell so briskly to work with his broad sword, that the fellows immediately quitted their hold; and without offering to attack our heroe, betook themselves to their heels and made their escape; for he did not attempt to pursue them, being contented with having delivered the old gentleman; and indeed he concluded he had pretty well done their business, for both of them, as they ran off, cried out with bitter oaths that they were dead men.\n\nJones presently ran to lift up the old gentleman, who had been thrown down in the scuffle, expressing at the same time great concern lest he should have received any harm from the villains. The old man stared a moment at Jones, and then cried, “No, sir, no, I have very little harm, I thank you. Lord have mercy upon me!”--“I see, sir,” said Jones, “you are not free from apprehensions even of those who have had the happiness to be your deliverers; nor can I blame any suspicions which you may have; but indeed you have no real occasion for any; here are none but your friends present. Having mist our way this cold night, we took the liberty of warming ourselves at your fire, whence we were just departing when we heard you call for assistance, which, I must say, Providence alone seems to have sent you.”--“Providence, indeed,” cries the old gentleman, “if it be so.”--“So it is, I assure you,” cries Jones. “Here is your own sword, sir; I have used it in your defence, and I now return it into your hand.” The old man having received the sword, which was stained with the blood of his enemies, looked stedfastly at Jones during some moments, and then with a sigh cried out, “You will pardon me, young gentleman; I was not always of a suspicious temper, nor am I a friend to ingratitude.”\n\n“Be thankful then,” cries Jones, “to that Providence to which you owe your deliverance: as to my part, I have only discharged the common duties of humanity, and what I would have done for any fellow-creature in your situation.”--“Let me look at you a little longer,” cries the old gentleman. “You are a human creature then? Well, perhaps you are. Come pray walk into my little hutt. You have been my deliverer indeed.”\n\nThe old woman was distracted between the fears which she had of her master, and for him; and Partridge was, if possible, in a greater fright. The former of these, however, when she heard her master speak kindly to Jones, and perceived what had happened, came again to herself; but Partridge no sooner saw the gentleman, than the strangeness of his dress infused greater terrors into that poor fellow than he had before felt, either from the strange description which he had heard, or from the uproar which had happened at the door.\n\nTo say the truth, it was an appearance which might have affected a more constant mind than that of Mr Partridge. This person was of the tallest size, with a long beard as white as snow. His body was cloathed with the skin of an ass, made something into the form of a coat. He wore likewise boots on his legs, and a cap on his head, both composed of the skin of some other animals.\n\nAs soon as the old gentleman came into his house, the old woman began her congratulations on his happy escape from the ruffians. “Yes,” cried he, “I have escaped, indeed, thanks to my preserver.”--“O the blessing on him!” answered she: “he is a good gentleman, I warrant him. I was afraid your worship would have been angry with me for letting him in; and to be certain I should not have done it, had not I seen by the moon-light, that he was a gentleman, and almost frozen to death. And to be certain it must have been some good angel that sent him hither, and tempted me to do it.”\n\n“I am afraid, sir,” said the old gentleman to Jones, “that I have nothing in this house which you can either eat or drink, unless you will accept a dram of brandy; of which I can give you some most excellent, and which I have had by me these thirty years.” Jones declined this offer in a very civil and proper speech, and then the other asked him, “Whither he was travelling when he mist his way?” saying, “I must own myself surprized to see such a person as you appear to be, journeying on foot at this time of night. I suppose, sir, you are a gentleman of these parts; for you do not look like one who is used to travel far without horses?”\n\n“Appearances,” cried Jones, “are often deceitful; men sometimes look what they are not. I assure you I am not of this country; and whither I am travelling, in reality I scarce know myself.”\n\n“Whoever you are, or whithersoever you are going,” answered the old man, “I have obligations to you which I can never return.”\n\n“I once more,” replied Jones, “affirm that you have none; for there can be no merit in having hazarded that in your service on which I set no value; and nothing is so contemptible in my eyes as life.”\n\n“I am sorry, young gentleman,” answered the stranger, “that you have any reason to be so unhappy at your years.”\n\n“Indeed I am, sir,” answered Jones, “the most unhappy of mankind.”--“Perhaps you have had a friend, or a mistress?” replied the other. “How could you,” cries Jones, “mention two words sufficient to drive me to distraction?”--“Either of them are enough to drive any man to distraction,” answered the old man. “I enquire no farther, sir; perhaps my curiosity hath led me too far already.”\n\n“Indeed, sir,” cries Jones, “I cannot censure a passion which I feel at this instant in the highest degree. You will pardon me when I assure you, that everything which I have seen or heard since I first entered this house hath conspired to raise the greatest curiosity in me. Something very extraordinary must have determined you to this course of life, and I have reason to fear your own history is not without misfortunes.”\n\nHere the old gentleman again sighed, and remained silent for some minutes: at last, looking earnestly on Jones, he said, “I have read that a good countenance is a letter of recommendation; if so, none ever can be more strongly recommended than yourself. If I did not feel some yearnings towards you from another consideration, I must be the most ungrateful monster upon earth; and I am really concerned it is no otherwise in my power than by words to convince you of my gratitude.”\n\nJones, after a moment's hesitation, answered, “That it was in his power by words to gratify him extremely. I have confest a curiosity,” said he, “sir; need I say how much obliged I should be to you, if you would condescend to gratify it? Will you suffer me therefore to beg, unless any consideration restrains you, that you would be pleased to acquaint me what motives have induced you thus to withdraw from the society of mankind, and to betake yourself to a course of life to which it sufficiently appears you were not born?”\n\n“I scarce think myself at liberty to refuse you anything after what hath happened,” replied the old man. “If you desire therefore to hear the story of an unhappy man, I will relate it to you. Indeed you judge rightly, in thinking there is commonly something extraordinary in the fortunes of those who fly from society; for however it may seem a paradox, or even a contradiction, certain it is, that great philanthropy chiefly inclines us to avoid and detest mankind; not on account so much of their private and selfish vices, but for those of a relative kind; such as envy, malice, treachery, cruelty, with every other species of malevolence. These are the vices which true philanthropy abhors, and which rather than see and converse with, she avoids society itself. However, without a compliment to you, you do not appear to me one of those whom I should shun or detest; nay, I must say, in what little hath dropt from you, there appears some parity in our fortunes: I hope, however, yours will conclude more successfully.”\n\nHere some compliments passed between our heroe and his host, and then the latter was going to begin his history, when Partridge interrupted him. His apprehensions had now pretty well left him, but some effects of his terrors remained; he therefore reminded the gentleman of that excellent brandy which he had mentioned. This was presently brought, and Partridge swallowed a large bumper.\n\nThe gentleman then, without any farther preface, began as you may read in the next chapter.\n\nIn which the Man of the Hill begins to relate his history.\n\n“I was born in a village of Somersetshire, called Mark, in the year 1657. My father was one of those whom they call gentlemen farmers. He had a little estate of about £300 a year of his own, and rented another estate of near the same value. He was prudent and industrious, and so good a husbandman, that he might have led a very easy and comfortable life, had not an arrant vixen of a wife soured his domestic quiet. But though this circumstance perhaps made him miserable, it did not make him poor; for he confined her almost entirely at home, and rather chose to bear eternal upbraidings in his own house, than to injure his fortune by indulging her in the extravagancies she desired abroad.\n\n“By this Xanthippe” (so was the wife of Socrates called, said Partridge)--“by this Xanthippe he had two sons, of which I was the younger. He designed to give us both good education; but my elder brother, who, unhappily for him, was the favourite of my mother, utterly neglected his learning; insomuch that, after having been five or six years at school with little or no improvement, my father, being told by his master that it would be to no purpose to keep him longer there, at last complied with my mother in taking him home from the hands of that tyrant, as she called his master; though indeed he gave the lad much less correction than his idleness deserved, but much more, it seems, than the young gentleman liked, who constantly complained to his mother of his severe treatment, and she as constantly gave him a hearing.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” cries Partridge, “I have seen such mothers; I have been abused myself by them, and very unjustly; such parents deserve correction as much as their children.”\n\nJones chid the pedagogue for his interruption, and then the stranger proceeded.\n\n“My brother now, at the age of fifteen, bade adieu to all learning, and to everything else but to his dog and gun; with which latter he became so expert, that, though perhaps you may think it incredible, he could not only hit a standing mark with great certainty, but hath actually shot a crow as it was flying in the air. He was likewise excellent at finding a hare sitting, and was soon reputed one of the best sportsmen in the country; a reputation which both he and his mother enjoyed as much as if he had been thought the finest scholar.\n\n“The situation of my brother made me at first think my lot the harder, in being continued at school: but I soon changed my opinion; for as I advanced pretty fast in learning, my labours became easy, and my exercise so delightful, that holidays were my most unpleasant time; for my mother, who never loved me, now apprehending that I had the greater share of my father's affection, and finding, or at least thinking, that I was more taken notice of by some gentlemen of learning, and particularly by the parson of the parish, than my brother, she now hated my sight, and made home so disagreeable to me, that what is called by school-boys Black Monday, was to me the whitest in the whole year.\n\n“Having at length gone through the school at Taunton, I was thence removed to Exeter College in Oxford, where I remained four years; at the end of which an accident took me off entirely from my studies; and hence, I may truly date the rise of all which happened to me afterwards in life.\n\n“There was at the same college with myself one Sir George Gresham, a young fellow who was intitled to a very considerable fortune, which he was not, by the will of his father, to come into full possession of till he arrived at the age of twenty-five. However, the liberality of his guardians gave him little cause to regret the abundant caution of his father; for they allowed him five hundred pounds a year while he remained at the university, where he kept his horses and his whore, and lived as wicked and as profligate a life as he could have done had he been never so entirely master of his fortune; for besides the five hundred a year which he received from his guardians, he found means to spend a thousand more. He was above the age of twenty-one, and had no difficulty in gaining what credit he pleased.\n\n“This young fellow, among many other tolerable bad qualities, had one very diabolical. He had a great delight in destroying and ruining the youth of inferior fortune, by drawing them into expenses which they could not afford so well as himself; and the better, and worthier, and soberer any young man was, the greater pleasure and triumph had he in his destruction. Thus acting the character which is recorded of the devil, and going about seeking whom he might devour.\n\n“It was my misfortune to fall into an acquaintance and intimacy with this gentleman. My reputation of diligence in my studies made me a desirable object of his mischievous intention; and my own inclination made it sufficiently easy for him to effect his purpose; for though I had applied myself with much industry to books, in which I took great delight, there were other pleasures in which I was capable of taking much greater; for I was high-mettled, had a violent flow of animal spirits, was a little ambitious, and extremely amorous.\n\n“I had not long contracted an intimacy with Sir George before I became a partaker of all his pleasures; and when I was once entered on that scene, neither my inclination nor my spirit would suffer me to play an under part. I was second to none of the company in any acts of debauchery; nay, I soon distinguished myself so notably in all riots and disorders, that my name generally stood first in the roll of delinquents; and instead of being lamented as the unfortunate pupil of Sir George, I was now accused as the person who had misled and debauched that hopeful young gentleman; for though he was the ringleader and promoter of all the mischief, he was never so considered. I fell at last under the censure of the vice-chancellor, and very narrowly escaped expulsion.\n\n“You will easily believe, sir, that such a life as I am now describing must be incompatible with my further progress in learning; and that in proportion as I addicted myself more and more to loose pleasure, I must grow more and more remiss in application to my studies. This was truly the consequence; but this was not all. My expenses now greatly exceeded not only my former income, but those additions which I extorted from my poor generous father, on pretences of sums being necessary for preparing for my approaching degree of batchelor of arts. These demands, however, grew at last so frequent and exorbitant, that my father by slow degrees opened his ears to the accounts which he received from many quarters of my present behaviour, and which my mother failed not to echo very faithfully and loudly; adding, `Ay, this is the fine gentleman, the scholar who doth so much honour to his family, and is to be the making of it. I thought what all this learning would come to. He is to be the ruin of us all, I find, after his elder brother hath been denied necessaries for his sake, to perfect his education forsooth, for which he was to pay us such interest: I thought what the interest would come to,' with much more of the same kind; but I have, I believe, satisfied you with this taste.\n\n“My father, therefore, began now to return remonstrances instead of money to my demands, which brought my affairs perhaps a little sooner to a crisis; but had he remitted me his whole income, you will imagine it could have sufficed a very short time to support one who kept pace with the expenses of Sir George Gresham.\n\n“It is more than possible that the distress I was now in for money, and the impracticability of going on in this manner, might have restored me at once to my senses and to my studies, had I opened my eyes before I became involved in debts from which I saw no hopes of ever extricating myself. This was indeed the great art of Sir George, and by which he accomplished the ruin of many, whom he afterwards laughed at as fools and coxcombs, for vying, as he called it, with a man of his fortune. To bring this about, he would now and then advance a little money himself, in order to support the credit of the unfortunate youth with other people; till, by means of that very credit, he was irretrievably undone.\n\n“My mind being by these means grown as desperate as my fortune, there was scarce a wickedness which I did not meditate, in order for my relief. Self-murder itself became the subject of my serious deliberation; and I had certainly resolved on it, had not a more shameful, though perhaps less sinful, thought expelled it from my head.”--Here he hesitated a moment, and then cried out, “I protest, so many years have not washed away the shame of this act, and I shall blush while I relate it.” Jones desired him to pass over anything that might give him pain in the relation; but Partridge eagerly cried out, “Oh, pray, sir, let us hear this; I had rather hear this than all the rest; as I hope to be saved, I will never mention a word of it.” Jones was going to rebuke him, but the stranger prevented it by proceeding thus: “I had a chum, a very prudent, frugal young lad, who, though he had no very large allowance, had by his parsimony heaped up upwards of forty guineas, which I knew he kept in his escritore. I took therefore an opportunity of purloining his key from his breeches-pocket, while he was asleep, and thus made myself master of all his riches: after which I again conveyed his key into his pocket, and counterfeiting sleep--though I never once closed my eyes, lay in bed till after he arose and went to prayers--an exercise to which I had long been unaccustomed.\n\n“Timorous thieves, by extreme caution, often subject themselves to discoveries, which those of a bolder kind escape. Thus it happened to me; for had I boldly broke open his escritore, I had, perhaps, escaped even his suspicion; but as it was plain that the person who robbed him had possessed himself of his key, he had no doubt, when he first missed his money, but that his chum was certainly the thief. Now as he was of a fearful disposition, and much my inferior in strength, and I believe in courage, he did not dare to confront me with my guilt, for fear of worse bodily consequences which might happen to him. He repaired therefore immediately to the vice-chancellor, and upon swearing to the robbery, and to the circumstances of it, very easily obtained a warrant against one who had now so bad a character through the whole university.\n\n“Luckily for me, I lay out of the college the next evening; for that day I attended a young lady in a chaise to Witney, where we staid all night, and in our return, the next morning, to Oxford, I met one of my cronies, who acquainted me with sufficient news concerning myself to make me turn my horse another way.”\n\n“Pray, sir, did he mention anything of the warrant?” said Partridge. But Jones begged the gentleman to proceed without regarding any impertinent questions; which he did as follows:--\n\n“Having now abandoned all thoughts of returning to Oxford, the next thing which offered itself was a journey to London. I imparted this intention to my female companion, who at first remonstrated against it; but upon producing my wealth, she immediately consented. We then struck across the country, into the great Cirencester road, and made such haste, that we spent the next evening, save one, in London.\n\n“When you consider the place where I now was, and the company with whom I was, you will, I fancy, conceive that a very short time brought me to an end of that sum of which I had so iniquitously possessed myself.\n\n“I was now reduced to a much higher degree of distress than before: the necessaries of life began to be numbered among my wants; and what made my case still the more grievous was, that my paramour, of whom I was now grown immoderately fond, shared the same distresses with myself. To see a woman you love in distress; to be unable to relieve her, and at the same time to reflect that you have brought her into this situation, is perhaps a curse of which no imagination can represent the horrors to those who have not felt it.”--“I believe it from my soul,” cries Jones, “and I pity you from the bottom of my heart:” he then took two or three disorderly turns about the room, and at last begged pardon, and flung himself into his chair, crying, “I thank Heaven, I have escaped that!”\n\n“This circumstance,” continued the gentleman, “so severely aggravated the horrors of my present situation, that they became absolutely intolerable. I could with less pain endure the raging in my own natural unsatisfied appetites, even hunger or thirst, than I could submit to leave ungratified the most whimsical desires of a woman on whom I so extravagantly doated, that, though I knew she had been the mistress of half my acquaintance, I firmly intended to marry her. But the good creature was unwilling to consent to an action which the world might think so much to my disadvantage. And as, possibly, she compassionated the daily anxieties which she must have perceived me suffer on her account, she resolved to put an end to my distress. She soon, indeed, found means to relieve me from my troublesome and perplexed situation; for while I was distracted with various inventions to supply her with pleasures, she very kindly--betrayed me to one of her former lovers at Oxford, by whose care and diligence I was immediately apprehended and committed to gaol.\n\n“Here I first began seriously to reflect on the miscarriages of my former life; on the errors I had been guilty of; on the misfortunes which I had brought on myself; and on the grief which I must have occasioned to one of the best of fathers. When I added to all these the perfidy of my mistress, such was the horror of my mind, that life, instead of being longer desirable, grew the object of my abhorrence; and I could have gladly embraced death as my dearest friend, if it had offered itself to my choice unattended by shame.\n\n“The time of the assizes soon came, and I was removed by habeas corpus to Oxford, where I expected certain conviction and condemnation; but, to my great surprize, none appeared against me, and I was, at the end of the sessions, discharged for want of prosecution. In short, my chum had left Oxford, and whether from indolence, or from what other motive I am ignorant, had declined concerning himself any farther in the affair.”\n\n“Perhaps,” cries Partridge, “he did not care to have your blood upon his hands; and he was in the right on't. If any person was to be hanged upon my evidence, I should never be able to lie alone afterwards, for fear of seeing his ghost.”\n\n“I shall shortly doubt, Partridge,” says Jones, “whether thou art more brave or wise.”--“You may laugh at me, sir, if you please,” answered Partridge; “but if you will hear a very short story which I can tell, and which is most certainly true, perhaps you may change your opinion. In the parish where I was born--” Here Jones would have silenced him; but the stranger interceded that he might be permitted to tell his story, and in the meantime promised to recollect the remainder of his own.\n\nPartridge then proceeded thus: “In the parish where I was born, there lived a farmer whose name was Bridle, and he had a son named Francis, a good hopeful young fellow: I was at the grammar-school with him, where I remember he was got into Ovid's Epistles, and he could construe you three lines together sometimes without looking into a dictionary. Besides all this, he was a very good lad, never missed church o' Sundays, and was reckoned one of the best psalm-singers in the whole parish. He would indeed now and then take a cup too much, and that was the only fault he had.”--“Well, but come to the ghost,” cries Jones. “Never fear, sir; I shall come to him soon enough,” answered Partridge. “You must know, then, that farmer Bridle lost a mare, a sorrel one, to the best of my remembrance; and so it fell out that this young Francis shortly afterward being at a fair at Hindon, and as I think it was on--, I can't remember the day; and being as he was, what should he happen to meet but a man upon his father's mare. Frank called out presently, Stop thief; and it being in the middle of the fair, it was impossible, you know, for the man to make his escape. So they apprehended him and carried him before the justice: I remember it was Justice Willoughby, of Noyle, a very worthy good gentleman; and he committed him to prison, and bound Frank in a recognisance, I think they call it--a hard word compounded of re and cognosco; but it differs in its meaning from the use of the simple, as many other compounds do. Well, at last down came my Lord Justice Page to hold the assizes; and so the fellow was had up, and Frank was had up for a witness. To be sure, I shall never forget the face of the judge, when he began to ask him what he had to say against the prisoner. He made poor Frank tremble and shake in his shoes. `Well you, fellow,' says my lord, `what have you to say? Don't stand humming and hawing, but speak out.' But, however, he soon turned altogether as civil to Frank, and began to thunder at the fellow; and when he asked him if he had anything to say for himself, the fellow said, he had found the horse. `Ay!' answered the judge, `thou art a lucky fellow: I have travelled the circuit these forty years, and never found a horse in my life: but I'll tell thee what, friend, thou wast more lucky than thou didst know of; for thou didst not only find a horse, but a halter too, I promise thee.' To be sure, I shall never forget the word. Upon which everybody fell a laughing, as how could they help it? Nay, and twenty other jests he made, which I can't remember now. There was something about his skill in horse-flesh which made all the folks laugh. To be certain, the judge must have been a very brave man, as well as a man of much learning. It is indeed charming sport to hear trials upon life and death. One thing I own I thought a little hard, that the prisoner's counsel was not suffered to speak for him, though he desired only to be heard one very short word, but my lord would not hearken to him, though he suffered a counsellor to talk against him for above half-an-hour. I thought it hard, I own, that there should be so many of them; my lord, and the court, and the jury, and the counsellors, and the witnesses, all upon one poor man, and he too in chains. Well, the fellow was hanged, as to be sure it could be no otherwise, and poor Frank could never be easy about it. He never was in the dark alone, but he fancied he saw the fellow's spirit.”--“Well, and is this thy story?” cries Jones. “No, no,” answered Partridge. “O Lord have mercy upon me! I am just now coming to the matter; for one night, coming from the alehouse, in a long, narrow, dark lane, there he ran directly up against him; and the spirit was all in white, and fell upon Frank; and Frank, who was a sturdy lad, fell upon the spirit again, and there they had a tussel together, and poor Frank was dreadfully beat: indeed he made a shift at last to crawl home; but what with the beating, and what with the fright, he lay ill above a fortnight; and all this is most certainly true, and the whole parish will bear witness to it.”\n\nThe stranger smiled at this story, and Jones burst into a loud fit of laughter; upon which Partridge cried, “Ay, you may laugh, sir; and so did some others, particularly a squire, who is thought to be no better than an atheist; who, forsooth, because there was a calf with a white face found dead in the same lane the next morning, would fain have it that the battle was between Frank and that, as if a calf would set upon a man. Besides, Frank told me he knew it to be a spirit, and could swear to him in any court in Christendom; and he had not drank above a quart or two or such a matter of liquor, at the time. Lud have mercy upon us, and keep us all from dipping our hands in blood, I say!”\n\n“Well, sir,” said Jones to the stranger, “Mr Partridge hath finished his story, and I hope will give you no future interruption, if you will be so kind to proceed.” He then resumed his narration; but as he hath taken breath for a while, we think proper to give it to our reader, and shall therefore put an end to this chapter.\n\nIn which the Man of the Hill continues his history.\n“I had now regained my liberty,” said the stranger; “but I had lost my reputation; for there is a wide difference between the case of a man who is barely acquitted of a crime in a court of justice, and of him who is acquitted in his own heart, and in the opinion of the people. I was conscious of my guilt, and ashamed to look any one in the face; so resolved to leave Oxford the next morning, before the daylight discovered me to the eyes of any beholders.\n\n“When I had got clear of the city, it first entered into my head to return home to my father, and endeavour to obtain his forgiveness; but as I had no reason to doubt his knowledge of all which had past, and as I was well assured of his great aversion to all acts of dishonesty, I could entertain no hopes of being received by him, especially since I was too certain of all the good offices in the power of my mother; nay, had my father's pardon been as sure, as I conceived his resentment to be, I yet question whether I could have had the assurance to behold him, or whether I could, upon any terms, have submitted to live and converse with those who, I was convinced, knew me to have been guilty of so base an action.\n\n“I hastened therefore back to London, the best retirement of either grief or shame, unless for persons of a very public character; for here you have the advantage of solitude without its disadvantage, since you may be alone and in company at the same time; and while you walk or sit unobserved, noise, hurry, and a constant succession of objects, entertain the mind, and prevent the spirits from preying on themselves, or rather on grief or shame, which are the most unwholesome diet in the world; and on which (though there are many who never taste either but in public) there are some who can feed very plentifully and very fatally when alone.\n\n“But as there is scarce any human good without its concomitant evil, so there are people who find an inconvenience in this unobserving temper of mankind; I mean persons who have no money; for as you are not put out of countenance, so neither are you cloathed or fed by those who do not know you. And a man may be as easily starved in Leadenhall-market as in the deserts of Arabia.\n\n“It was at present my fortune to be destitute of that great evil, as it is apprehended to be by several writers, who I suppose were overburthened with it, namely, money.”--“With submission, sir,” said Partridge, “I do not remember any writers who have called it malorum; but irritamenta malorum. Effodiuntur opes, irritamenta malorum”--“Well, sir,” continued the stranger, “whether it be an evil, or only the cause of evil, I was entirely void of it, and at the same time of friends, and, as I thought, of acquaintance; when one evening, as I was passing through the Inner Temple, very hungry, and very miserable, I heard a voice on a sudden hailing me with great familiarity by my Christian name; and upon turning about, I presently recollected the person who so saluted me to have been my fellow-collegiate; one who had left the university above a year, and long before any of my misfortunes had befallen me. This gentleman, whose name was Watson, shook me heartily by the hand; and expressing great joy at meeting me, proposed our immediately drinking a bottle together. I first declined the proposal, and pretended business, but as he was very earnest and pressing, hunger at last overcame my pride, and I fairly confessed to him I had no money in my pocket; yet not without framing a lie for an excuse, and imputing it to my having changed my breeches that morning. Mr Watson answered, `I thought, Jack, you and I had been too old acquaintance for you to mention such a matter.' He then took me by the arm, and was pulling me along; but I gave him very little trouble, for my own inclinations pulled me much stronger than he could do.\n\n“We then went into the Friars, which you know is the scene of all mirth and jollity. Here, when we arrived at the tavern, Mr Watson applied himself to the drawer only, without taking the least notice of the cook; for he had no suspicion but that I had dined long since. However, as the case was really otherwise, I forged another falsehood, and told my companion I had been at the further end of the city on business of consequence, and had snapt up a mutton-chop in haste; so that I was again hungry, and wished he would add a beef-steak to his bottle.”--“Some people,” cries Partridge, “ought to have good memories; or did you find just money enough in your breeches to pay for the mutton-chop?”--“Your observation is right,” answered the stranger, “and I believe such blunders are inseparable from all dealing in untruth.--But to proceed--I began now to feel myself extremely happy. The meat and wine soon revived my spirits to a high pitch, and I enjoyed much pleasure in the conversation of my old acquaintance, the rather as I thought him entirely ignorant of what had happened at the university since his leaving it.\n\n“But he did not suffer me to remain long in this agreeable delusion; for taking a bumper in one hand, and holding me by the other, `Here, my boy,' cries he, `here's wishing you joy of your being so honourably acquitted of that affair laid to your charge.' I was thunderstruck with confusion at those words, which Watson observing, proceeded thus: `Nay, never be ashamed, man; thou hast been acquitted, and no one now dares call thee guilty; but, prithee, do tell me, who am thy friend--I hope thou didst really rob him? for rat me if it was not a meritorious action to strip such a sneaking, pitiful rascal; and instead of the two hundred guineas, I wish you had taken as many thousand. Come, come, my boy, don't be shy of confessing to me: you are not now brought before one of the pimps. D--n me if I don't honour you for it; for, as I hope for salvation, I would have made no manner of scruple of doing the same thing.'\n\n“This declaration a little relieved my abashment; and as wine had now somewhat opened my heart, I very freely acknowledged the robbery, but acquainted him that he had been misinformed as to the sum taken, which was little more than a fifth part of what he had mentioned.\n\n“`I am sorry for it with all my heart,' quoth he, `and I wish thee better success another time. Though, if you will take my advice, you shall have no occasion to run any such risque. Here,' said he, taking some dice out of his pocket, `here's the stuff. Here are the implements; here are the little doctors which cure the distempers of the purse. Follow but my counsel, and I will show you a way to empty the pocket of a queer cull without any danger of the nubbing cheat.'”\n\n“Nubbing cheat!” cries Partridge: “pray, sir, what is that?”\n\n“Why that, sir,” says the stranger, “is a cant phrase for the gallows; for as gamesters differ little from highwaymen in their morals, so do they very much resemble them in their language.\n\n“We had now each drank our bottle, when Mr Watson said, the board was sitting, and that he must attend, earnestly pressing me at the same time to go with him and try my fortune. I answered he knew that was at present out of my power, as I had informed him of the emptiness of my pocket. To say the truth, I doubted not from his many strong expressions of friendship, but that he would offer to lend me a small sum for that purpose, but he answered, `Never mind that, man; e'en boldly run a levant' [Partridge was going to inquire the meaning of that word, but Jones stopped his mouth]: `but be circumspect as to the man. I will tip you the proper person, which may be necessary, as you do not know the town, nor can distinguish a rum cull from a queer one.”\n\n“The bill was now brought, when Watson paid his share, and was departing. I reminded him, not without blushing, of my having no money. He answered, `That signifies nothing; score it behind the door, or make a bold brush and take no notice.--Or--stay,' says he; `I will go down-stairs first, and then do you take up my money, and score the whole reckoning at the bar, and I will wait for you at the corner.' I expressed some dislike at this, and hinted my expectations that he would have deposited the whole; but he swore he had not another sixpence in his pocket.\n\n“He then went down, and I was prevailed on to take up the money and follow him, which I did close enough to hear him tell the drawer the reckoning was upon the table. The drawer past by me up-stairs; but I made such haste into the street, that I heard nothing of his disappointment, nor did I mention a syllable at the bar, according to my instructions.\n\n“We now went directly to the gaming-table, where Mr Watson, to my surprize, pulled out a large sum of money and placed it before him, as did many others; all of them, no doubt, considering their own heaps as so many decoy birds, which were to intice and draw over the heaps of their neighbours.\n\n“Here it would be tedious to relate all the freaks which Fortune, or rather the dice, played in this her temple. Mountains of gold were in a few moments reduced to nothing at one part of the table, and rose as suddenly in another. The rich grew in a moment poor, and the poor as suddenly became rich; so that it seemed a philosopher could nowhere have so well instructed his pupils in the contempt of riches, at least he could nowhere have better inculcated the incertainty of their duration.\n\n“For my own part, after having considerably improved my small estate, I at last entirely demolished it. Mr Watson too, after much variety of luck, rose from the table in some heat, and declared he had lost a cool hundred, and would play no longer. Then coming up to me, he asked me to return with him to the tavern; but I positively refused, saying, I would not bring myself a second time into such a dilemma, and especially as he had lost all his money and was now in my own condition. `Pooh!' says he, `I have just borrowed a couple of guineas of a friend, and one of them is at your service.' He immediately put one of them into my hand, and I no longer resisted his inclination.\n\n“I was at first a little shocked at returning to the same house whence we had departed in so unhandsome a manner; but when the drawer, with very civil address, told us, `he believed we had forgot to pay our reckoning,' I became perfectly easy, and very readily gave him a guinea, bid him pay himself, and acquiesced in the unjust charge which had been laid on my memory.\n\n“Mr Watson now bespoke the most extravagant supper he could well think of; and though he had contented himself with simple claret before, nothing now but the most precious Burgundy would serve his purpose.\n\n“Our company was soon encreased by the addition of several gentlemen from the gaming-table; most of whom, as I afterwards found, came not to the tavern to drink, but in the way of business; for the true gamesters pretended to be ill, and refused their glass, while they plied heartily two young fellows, who were to be afterwards pillaged, as indeed they were without mercy. Of this plunder I had the good fortune to be a sharer, though I was not yet let into the secret.\n\n“There was one remarkable accident attended this tavern play; for the money by degrees totally disappeared; so that though at the beginning the table was half covered with gold, yet before the play ended, which it did not till the next day, being Sunday, at noon, there was scarce a single guinea to be seen on the table; and this was the stranger as every person present, except myself, declared he had lost; and what was become of the money, unless the devil himself carried it away, is difficult to determine.”\n\n“Most certainly he did,” says Partridge, “for evil spirits can carry away anything without being seen, though there were never so many folk in the room; and I should not have been surprized if he had carried away all the company of a set of wicked wretches, who were at play in sermon time. And I could tell you a true story, if I would, where the devil took a man out of bed from another man's wife, and carried him away through the keyhole of the door. I've seen the very house where it was done, and nobody hath lived in it these thirty years.”\n\nThough Jones was a little offended by the impertinence of Partridge, he could not however avoid smiling at his simplicity. The stranger did the same, and then proceeded with his story, as will be seen in the next chapter.\n\nIn which the foregoing story is farther continued.\n\n“My fellow-collegiate had now entered me in a new scene of life. I soon became acquainted with the whole fraternity of sharpers, and was let into their secrets; I mean, into the knowledge of those gross cheats which are proper to impose upon the raw and unexperienced; for there are some tricks of a finer kind, which are known only to a few of the gang, who are at the head of their profession; a degree of honour beyond my expectation; for drink, to which I was immoderately addicted, and the natural warmth of my passions, prevented me from arriving at any great success in an art which requires as much coolness as the most austere school of philosophy.\n\n“Mr Watson, with whom I now lived in the closest amity, had unluckily the former failing to a very great excess; so that instead of making a fortune by his profession, as some others did, he was alternately rich and poor, and was often obliged to surrender to his cooler friends, over a bottle which they never tasted, that plunder that he had taken from culls at the public table.\n\n“However, we both made a shift to pick up an uncomfortable livelihood; and for two years I continued of the calling; during which time I tasted all the varieties of fortune, sometimes flourishing in affluence, and at others being obliged to struggle with almost incredible difficulties. To-day wallowing in luxury, and to-morrow reduced to the coarsest and most homely fare. My fine clothes being often on my back in the evening, and at the pawn-shop the next morning.\n\n“One night, as I was returning pennyless from the gaming-table, I observed a very great disturbance, and a large mob gathered together in the street. As I was in no danger from pickpockets, I ventured into the croud, where upon enquiry I found that a man had been robbed and very ill used by some ruffians. The wounded man appeared very bloody, and seemed scarce able to support himself on his legs. As I had not therefore been deprived of my humanity by my present life and conversation, though they had left me very little of either honesty or shame, I immediately offered my assistance to the unhappy person, who thankfully accepted it, and, putting himself under my conduct, begged me to convey him to some tavern, where he might send for a surgeon, being, as he said, faint with loss of blood. He seemed indeed highly pleased at finding one who appeared in the dress of a gentleman; for as to all the rest of the company present, their outside was such that he could not wisely place any confidence in them.\n\n“I took the poor man by the arm, and led him to the tavern where we kept our rendezvous, as it happened to be the nearest at hand. A surgeon happening luckily to be in the house, immediately attended, and applied himself to dressing his wounds, which I had the pleasure to hear were not likely to be mortal.\n\n“The surgeon having very expeditiously and dextrously finished his business, began to enquire in what part of the town the wounded man lodged; who answered, `That he was come to town that very morning; that his horse was at an inn in Piccadilly, and that he had no other lodging, and very little or no acquaintance in town.'\n\n“This surgeon, whose name I have forgot, though I remember it began with an R, had the first character in his profession, and was serjeant-surgeon to the king. He had moreover many good qualities, and was a very generous good-natured man, and ready to do any service to his fellow-creatures. He offered his patient the use of his chariot to carry him to his inn, and at the same time whispered in his ear, `That if he wanted any money, he would furnish him.'\n\n“The poor man was not now capable of returning thanks for this generous offer; for having had his eyes for some time stedfastly on me, he threw himself back in his chair, crying, `Oh, my son! my son!' and then fainted away.\n\n“Many of the people present imagined this accident had happened through his loss of blood; but I, who at the same time began to recollect the features of my father, was now confirmed in my suspicion, and satisfied that it was he himself who appeared before me. I presently ran to him, raised him in my arms, and kissed his cold lips with the utmost eagerness. Here I must draw a curtain over a scene which I cannot describe; for though I did not lose my being, as my father for a while did, my senses were however so overpowered with affright and surprize, that I am a stranger to what passed during some minutes, and indeed till my father had again recovered from his swoon, and I found myself in his arms, both tenderly embracing each other, while the tears trickled a-pace down the cheeks of each of us.\n\n“Most of those present seemed affected by this scene, which we, who might be considered as the actors in it, were desirous of removing from the eyes of all spectators as fast as we could; my father therefore accepted the kind offer of the surgeon's chariot, and I attended him in it to his inn.\n\n“When we were alone together, he gently upbraided me with having neglected to write to him during so long a time, but entirely omitted the mention of that crime which had occasioned it. He then informed me of my mother's death, and insisted on my returning home with him, saying, `That he had long suffered the greatest anxiety on my account; that he knew not whether he had most feared my death or wished it, since he had so many more dreadful apprehensions for me. At last, he said, a neighbouring gentleman, who had just recovered a son from the same place, informed him where I was; and that to reclaim me from this course of life was the sole cause of his journey to London.' He thanked Heaven he had succeeded so far as to find me out by means of an accident which had like to have proved fatal to him; and had the pleasure to think he partly owed his preservation to my humanity, with which he profest himself to be more delighted than he should have been with my filial piety, if I had known that the object of all my care was my own father.\n\n“Vice had not so depraved my heart as to excite in it an insensibility of so much paternal affection, though so unworthily bestowed. I presently promised to obey his commands in my return home with him, as soon as he was able to travel, which indeed he was in a very few days, by the assistance of that excellent surgeon who had undertaken his cure.\n\n“The day preceding my father's journey (before which time I scarce ever left him), I went to take my leave of some of my most intimate acquaintance, particularly of Mr Watson, who dissuaded me from burying myself, as he called it, out of a simple compliance with the fond desires of a foolish old fellow. Such sollicitations, however, had no effect, and I once more saw my own home. My father now greatly sollicited me to think of marriage; but my inclinations were utterly averse to any such thoughts. I had tasted of love already, and perhaps you know the extravagant excesses of that most tender and most violent passion.”--Here the old gentleman paused, and looked earnestly at Jones; whose countenance, within a minute's space, displayed the extremities of both red and white. Upon which the old man, without making any observations, renewed his narrative.\n\n“Being now provided with all the necessaries of life, I betook myself once again to study, and that with a more inordinate application than I had ever done formerly. The books which now employed my time solely were those, as well antient as modern, which treat of true philosophy, a word which is by many thought to be the subject only of farce and ridicule. I now read over the works of Aristotle and Plato, with the rest of those inestimable treasures which antient Greece had bequeathed to the world.\n\n“These authors, though they instructed me in no science by which men may promise to themselves to acquire the least riches or worldly power, taught me, however, the art of despising the highest acquisitions of both. They elevate the mind, and steel and harden it against the capricious invasions of fortune. They not only instruct in the knowledge of Wisdom, but confirm men in her habits, and demonstrate plainly, that this must be our guide, if we propose ever to arrive at the greatest worldly happiness, or to defend ourselves, with any tolerable security, against the misery which everywhere surrounds and invests us.\n\n“To this I added another study, compared to which, all the philosophy taught by the wisest heathens is little better than a dream, and is indeed as full of vanity as the silliest jester ever pleased to represent it. This is that Divine wisdom which is alone to be found in the Holy Scriptures; for they impart to us the knowledge and assurance of things much more worthy our attention than all which this world can offer to our acceptance; of things which Heaven itself hath condescended to reveal to us, and to the smallest knowledge of which the highest human wit unassisted could never ascend. I began now to think all the time I had spent with the best heathen writers was little more than labour lost: for, however pleasant and delightful their lessons may be, or however adequate to the right regulation of our conduct with respect to this world only; yet, when compared with the glory revealed in Scripture, their highest documents will appear as trifling, and of as little consequence, as the rules by which children regulate their childish little games and pastime. True it is, that philosophy makes us wiser, but Christianity makes us better men. Philosophy elevates and steels the mind, Christianity softens and sweetens it. The former makes us the objects of human admiration, the latter of Divine love. That insures us a temporal, but this an eternal happiness.--But I am afraid I tire you with my rhapsody.”\n\n“Not at all,” cries Partridge; “Lud forbid we should be tired with good things!”\n\n“I had spent,” continued the stranger, “about four years in the most delightful manner to myself, totally given up to contemplation, and entirely unembarrassed with the affairs of the world, when I lost the best of fathers, and one whom I so entirely loved, that my grief at his loss exceeds all description. I now abandoned my books, and gave myself up for a whole month to the effects of melancholy and despair. Time, however, the best physician of the mind, at length brought me relief.”--“Ay, ay; Tempus edax rerum” said Partridge.--“I then,” continued the stranger, “betook myself again to my former studies, which I may say perfected my cure; for philosophy and religion may be called the exercises of the mind, and when this is disordered, they are as wholesome as exercise can be to a distempered body. They do indeed produce similar effects with exercise; for they strengthen and confirm the mind, till man becomes, in the noble strain of Horace--\n\nFortis, et in seipso totus teres atque rotundus, Externi ne quid valeat per laeve morari; In quem manca ruit semper Fortuna.“[*]\n\n[*] Firm in himself, who on himself relies, Polish'd and round, who runs his proper course And breaks misfortunes with superior force.--MR FRANCIS.\n\nHere Jones smiled at some conceit which intruded itself into his imagination; but the stranger, I believe, perceived it not, and proceeded thus:--\n\n“My circumstances were now greatly altered by the death of that best of men; for my brother, who was now become master of the house, differed so widely from me in his inclinations, and our pursuits in life had been so very various, that we were the worst of company to each other: but what made our living together still more disagreeable, was the little harmony which could subsist between the few who resorted to me, and the numerous train of sportsmen who often attended my brother from the field to the table; for such fellows, besides the noise and nonsense with which they persecute the ears of sober men, endeavour always to attack them with affront and contempt. This was so much the case, that neither I myself, nor my friends, could ever sit down to a meal with them without being treated with derision, because we were unacquainted with the phrases of sportsmen. For men of true learning, and almost universal knowledge, always compassionate the ignorance of others; but fellows who excel in some little, low, contemptible art, are always certain to despise those who are unacquainted with that art.\n\n“In short, we soon separated, and I went, by the advice of a physician, to drink the Bath waters; for my violent affliction, added to a sedentary life, had thrown me into a kind of paralytic disorder, for which those waters are accounted an almost certain cure. The second day after my arrival, as I was walking by the river, the sun shone so intensely hot (though it was early in the year), that I retired to the shelter of some willows, and sat down by the river side. Here I had not been seated long before I heard a person on the other side of the willows sighing and bemoaning himself bitterly. On a sudden, having uttered a most impious oath, he cried, `I am resolved to bear it no longer,' and directly threw himself into the water. I immediately started, and ran towards the place, calling at the same time as loudly as I could for assistance. An angler happened luckily to be a-fishing a little below me, though some very high sedge had hid him from my sight. He immediately came up, and both of us together, not without some hazard of our lives, drew the body to the shore. At first we perceived no sign of life remaining; but having held the body up by the heels (for we soon had assistance enough), it discharged a vast quantity of water at the mouth, and at length began to discover some symptoms of breathing, and a little afterwards to move both its hands and its legs.\n\n“An apothecary, who happened to be present among others, advised that the body, which seemed now to have pretty well emptied itself of water, and which began to have many convulsive motions, should be directly taken up, and carried into a warm bed. This was accordingly performed, the apothecary and myself attending.\n\n“As we were going towards an inn, for we knew not the man's lodgings, luckily a woman met us, who, after some violent screaming, told us that the gentleman lodged at her house.\n\n“When I had seen the man safely deposited there, I left him to the care of the apothecary; who, I suppose, used all the right methods with him, for the next morning I heard he had perfectly recovered his senses.\n\n“I then went to visit him, intending to search out, as well as I could, the cause of his having attempted so desperate an act, and to prevent, as far as I was able, his pursuing such wicked intentions for the future. I was no sooner admitted into his chamber, than we both instantly knew each other; for who should this person be but my good friend Mr Watson! Here I will not trouble you with what past at our first interview; for I would avoid prolixity as much as possible.”--“Pray let us hear all,” cries Partridge; “I want mightily to know what brought him to Bath.”\n\n“You shall hear everything material,” answered the stranger; and then proceeded to relate what we shall proceed to write, after we have given a short breathing time to both ourselves and the reader.\n\nIn which the Man of the Hill concludes his history.\n\n“Mr Watson,” continued the stranger, “very freely acquainted me, that the unhappy situation of his circumstances, occasioned by a tide of ill luck, had in a manner forced him to a resolution of destroying himself.\n\n“I now began to argue very seriously with him, in opposition to this heathenish, or indeed diabolical, principle of the lawfulness of self-murder; and said everything which occurred to me on the subject; but, to my great concern, it seemed to have very little effect on him. He seemed not at all to repent of what he had done, and gave me reason to fear he would soon make a second attempt of the like horrible kind.\n\n“When I had finished my discourse, instead of endeavouring to answer my arguments, he looked me stedfastly in the face, and with a smile said, `You are strangely altered, my good friend, since I remember you. I question whether any of our bishops could make a better argument against suicide than you have entertained me with; but unless you can find somebody who will lend me a cool hundred, I must either hang, or drown, or starve; and, in my opinion, the last death is the most terrible of the three.'\n\n“I answered him very gravely that I was indeed altered since I had seen him last. That I had found leisure to look into my follies and to repent of them. I then advised him to pursue the same steps; and at last concluded with an assurance that I myself would lend him a hundred pound, if it would be of any service to his affairs, and he would not put it into the power of a die to deprive him of it.\n\n“Mr Watson, who seemed almost composed in slumber by the former part of my discourse, was roused by the latter. He seized my hand eagerly, gave me a thousand thanks, and declared I was a friend indeed; adding that he hoped I had a better opinion of him than to imagine he had profited so little by experience, as to put any confidence in those damned dice which had so often deceived him. `No, no,' cries he; `let me but once handsomely be set up again, and if ever Fortune makes a broken merchant of me afterwards, I will forgive her.'\n\n“I very well understood the language of setting up, and broken merchant. I therefore said to him, with a very grave face, Mr Watson, you must endeavour to find out some business or employment, by which you may procure yourself a livelihood; and I promise you, could I see any probability of being repaid hereafter, I would advance a much larger sum than what you have mentioned, to equip you in any fair and honourable calling; but as to gaming, besides the baseness and wickedness of making it a profession, you are really, to my own knowledge, unfit for it, and it will end in your certain ruin.\n\n“`Why now, that's strange,' answered he; `neither you, nor any of my friends, would ever allow me to know anything of the matter, and yet I believe I am as good a hand at every game as any of you all; and I heartily wish I was to play with you only for your whole fortune: I should desire no better sport, and I would let you name your game into the bargain: but come, my dear boy, have you the hundred in your pocket?”\n\n“I answered I had only a bill for £50, which I delivered him, and promised to bring him the rest next morning; and after giving him a little more advice, took my leave.\n\n“I was indeed better than my word; for I returned to him that very afternoon. When I entered the room, I found him sitting up in his bed at cards with a notorious gamester. This sight, you will imagine, shocked me not a little; to which I may add the mortification of seeing my bill delivered by him to his antagonist, and thirty guineas only given in exchange for it.\n\n“The other gamester presently quitted the room, and then Watson declared he was ashamed to see me; `but,' says he, `I find luck runs so damnably against me, that I will resolve to leave off play for ever. I have thought of the kind proposal you made me ever since, and I promise you there shall be no fault in me, if I do not put it in execution.'\n\n“Though I had no great faith in his promises, I produced him the remainder of the hundred in consequence of my own; for which he gave me a note, which was all I ever expected to see in return for my money.\n\n“We were prevented from any further discourse at present by the arrival of the apothecary; who, with much joy in his countenance, and without even asking his patient how he did, proclaimed there was great news arrived in a letter to himself, which he said would shortly be public, `That the Duke of Monmouth was landed in the west with a vast army of Dutch; and that another vast fleet hovered over the coast of Norfolk, and was to make a descent there, in order to favour the duke's enterprize with a diversion on that side.'\n\n“This apothecary was one of the greatest politicians of his time. He was more delighted with the most paultry packet, than with the best patient, and the highest joy he was capable of, he received from having a piece of news in his possession an hour or two sooner than any other person in the town. His advices, however, were seldom authentic; for he would swallow almost anything as a truth--a humour which many made use of to impose upon him.\n\n“Thus it happened with what he at present communicated; for it was known within a short time afterwards that the duke was really landed, but that his army consisted only of a few attendants; and as to the diversion in Norfolk, it was entirely false.\n\n“The apothecary staid no longer in the room than while he acquainted us with his news; and then, without saying a syllable to his patient on any other subject, departed to spread his advices all over the town.\n\n“Events of this nature in the public are generally apt to eclipse all private concerns. Our discourse therefore now became entirely political.[*] For my own part, I had been for some time very seriously affected with the danger to which the Protestant religion was so visibly exposed under a Popish prince, and thought the apprehension of it alone sufficient to justify that insurrection; for no real security can ever be found against the persecuting spirit of Popery, when armed with power, except the depriving it of that power, as woeful experience presently showed. You know how King James behaved after getting the better of this attempt; how little he valued either his royal word, or coronation oath, or the liberties and rights of his people. But all had not the sense to foresee this at first; and therefore the Duke of Monmouth was weakly supported; yet all could feel when the evil came upon them; and therefore all united, at last, to drive out that king, against whose exclusion a great party among us had so warmly contended during the reign of his brother, and for whom they now fought with such zeal and affection.”\n\n“What you say,” interrupted Jones, “is very true; and it has often struck me, as the most wonderful thing I ever read of in history, that so soon after this convincing experience which brought our whole nation to join so unanimously in expelling King James, for the preservation of our religion and liberties, there should be a party among us mad enough to desire the placing his family again on the throne.” “You are not in earnest!” answered the old man; “there can be no such party. As bad an opinion as I have of mankind, I cannot believe them infatuated to such a degree. There may be some hot-headed Papists led by their priests to engage in this desperate cause, and think it a holy war; but that Protestants, that are members of the Church of England, should be such apostates, such felos de se, I cannot believe it; no, no, young man, unacquainted as I am with what has past in the world for these last thirty years, I cannot be so imposed upon as to credit so foolish a tale; but I see you have a mind to sport with my ignorance.”--“Can it be possible,” replied Jones, “that you have lived so much out of the world as not to know that during that time there have been two rebellions in favour of the son of King James, one of which is now actually raging in the very heart of the kingdom.” At these words the old gentleman started up, and in a most solemn tone of voice, conjured Jones by his Maker to tell him if what he said was really true; which the other as solemnly affirming, he walked several turns about the room in a profound silence, then cried, then laughed, and at last fell down on his knees, and blessed God, in a loud thanksgiving prayer, for having delivered him from all society with human nature, which could be capable of such monstrous extravagances. After which, being reminded by Jones that he had broke off his story, he resumed it again in this manner:--\n\n“As mankind, in the days I was speaking of, was not yet arrived at that pitch of madness which I find they are capable of now, and which, to be sure, I have only escaped by living alone, and at a distance from the contagion, there was a considerable rising in favour of Monmouth; and my principles strongly inclining me to take the same part, I determined to join him; and Mr Watson, from different motives concurring in the same resolution (for the spirit of a gamester will carry a man as far upon such an occasion as the spirit of patriotism), we soon provided ourselves with all necessaries, and went to the duke at Bridgewater.\n\n“The unfortunate event of this enterprize, you are, I conclude, as well acquainted with as myself. I escaped, together with Mr Watson, from the battle at Sedgemore, in which action I received a slight wound. We rode near forty miles together on the Exeter road, and then abandoning our horses, scrambled as well as we could through the fields and bye-roads, till we arrived at a little wild hut on a common, where a poor old woman took all the care of us she could, and dressed my wound with salve, which quickly healed it.”\n\n“Pray, sir, where was the wound?” says Partridge. The stranger satisfied him it was in his arm, and then continued his narrative. “Here, sir,” said he, “Mr Watson left me the next morning, in order, as he pretended, to get us some provision from the town of Collumpton; but--can I relate it, or can you believe it?--this Mr Watson, this friend, this base, barbarous, treacherous villain, betrayed me to a party of horse belonging to King James, and at his return delivered me into their hands.\n\n“The soldiers, being six in number, had now seized me, and were conducting me to Taunton gaol; but neither my present situation, nor the apprehensions of what might happen to me, were half so irksome to my mind as the company of my false friend, who, having surrendered himself, was likewise considered as a prisoner, though he was better treated, as being to make his peace at my expense. He at first endeavoured to excuse his treachery; but when he received nothing but scorn and upbraiding from me, he soon changed his note, abused me as the most atrocious and malicious rebel, and laid all his own guilt to my charge, who, as he declared, had solicited, and even threatened him, to make him take up arms against his gracious as well as lawful sovereign.\n\n“This false evidence (for in reality he had been much the forwarder of the two) stung me to the quick, and raised an indignation scarce conceivable by those who have not felt it. However, fortune at length took pity on me; for as we were got a little beyond Wellington, in a narrow lane, my guards received a false alarm, that near fifty of the enemy were at hand; upon which they shifted for themselves, and left me and my betrayer to do the same. That villain immediately ran from me, and I am glad he did, or I should have certainly endeavoured, though I had no arms, to have executed vengeance on his baseness.\n\n“I was now once more at liberty; and immediately withdrawing from the highway into the fields, I travelled on, scarce knowing which way I went, and making it my chief care to avoid all public roads and all towns--nay, even the most homely houses; for I imagined every human creature whom I saw desirous of betraying me.\n\n“At last, after rambling several days about the country, during which the fields afforded me the same bed and the same food which nature bestows on our savage brothers of the creation, I at length arrived at this place, where the solitude and wildness of the country invited me to fix my abode. The first person with whom I took up my habitation was the mother of this old woman, with whom I remained concealed till the news of the glorious revolution put an end to all my apprehensions of danger, and gave me an opportunity of once more visiting my own home, and of enquiring a little into my affairs, which I soon settled as agreeably to my brother as to myself; having resigned everything to him, for which he paid me the sum of a thousand pounds, and settled on me an annuity for life.\n\n“His behaviour in this last instance, as in all others, was selfish and ungenerous. I could not look on him as my friend, nor indeed did he desire that I should; so I presently took my leave of him, as well as of my other acquaintance; and from that day to this, my history is little better than a blank.”\n\n“And is it possible, sir,” said Jones, “that you can have resided here from that day to this?”--“O no, sir,” answered the gentleman; “I have been a great traveller, and there are few parts of Europe with which I am not acquainted.” “I have not, sir,” cried Jones, “the assurance to ask it of you now; indeed it would be cruel, after so much breath as you have already spent: but you will give me leave to wish for some further opportunity of hearing the excellent observations which a man of your sense and knowledge of the world must have made in so long a course of travels.”--“Indeed, young gentleman,” answered the stranger, “I will endeavour to satisfy your curiosity on this head likewise, as far as I am able.” Jones attempted fresh apologies, but was prevented; and while he and Partridge sat with greedy and impatient ears, the stranger proceeded as in the next chapter.\n\n[*] The rest of this paragraph and the two following paragraphs in the first edition were as follows:\n\n“For my own part, I had been for some time very seriously affected with the danger to which the Protestant religion was so visibly exposed, that nothing but the immediate interposition of Providence seemed capable of preserving it; for King James had indeed declared war against the Protestant cause. He had brought known papists into the army and attempted to bring them into the Church and into the University. Popish priests swarmed through the nation, appeared publicly in their habits, and boasted that they should shortly walk in procession through the streets. Our own clergy were forbid to preach against popery, and bishops were ordered to supend those who did; and to do the business at once an illegal ecclesiastical commission was erected, little inferior to an inquisition, of which, probably, it was intended to be the ringleader. Thus, as our duty to the king can never be called more than our second duty, he had discharged us from this by making it incompatible with our preserving the first, which is surely to heaven. Besides this, he had dissolved his subjects from their allegiance by breaking his Coronation Oath, to which their allegiance is annexed; for he had imprisoned bishops because they would not give up their religion, and turned out judges because they would not absolutely surrender the law into his hands; nay, he seized this himself, and when he claimed a dispensing power, he declared himself, in fact, as absolute as any tyrant ever was or can be. I have recapitulated these matters in full lest some of them should have been omitted in history; and I think nothing less than such provocations as I have here mentioned, nothing less than certain and imminent danger to their religion and liberties, can justify or even mitigate the dreadful sin of rebellion in any people.”\n\n“I promise you, sir,” says Jones, “all these facts, and more, I have read in history, but I will tell you a fact which is not yet recorded and of which I suppose you are ignorant. There is actually now a rebellion on foot in this kingdom in favour of the son of that very King James, a professed papist, more bigoted, if possible, than his father, and this carried on by Protestants against a king who hath never in one single instance made the least invasion on our liberties.”\n\n“Prodigious indeed!” answered the stranger. “You tell me what would be incredible of a nation which did not deserve the character that Virgil gives of a woman, varium et mutabile semper. Surely this is to be unworthy of the care which Providence seems to have taken of us in the preservation of our religion against the powerful designs and constant machinations of Popery, a preservation so strange and unaccountable that I almost think we may appeal to it as to a miracle for the proof of its holiness. Prodigious indeed! A Protestant rebellion in favour of a popish prince! The folly of mankind is as wonderful as their knavery--But to conclude my story: I resolved to take arms in defence of my country, of my religion, and my liberty, and Mr. Watson joined in the same resolution. We soon provided ourselves with an necessaries and joined the Duke at Bridgewater.”\n\n“The unfortunate event of this enterprise you are perhaps better acquainted with than myself. I escaped together with Mr. Watson from the battle at Sedgemore,...\n\n# Chapter xv.\n\nA brief history of Europe; and a curious discourse between Mr Jones and the Man of the Hill.\n\n“In Italy the landlords are very silent. In France they are more talkative, but yet civil. In Germany and Holland they are generally very impertinent. And as for their honesty, I believe it is pretty equal in all those countries. The laquais à louange are sure to lose no opportunity of cheating you; and as for the postilions, I think they are pretty much alike all the world over. These, sir, are the observations on men which I made in my travels; for these were the only men I ever conversed with. My design, when I went abroad, was to divert myself by seeing the wondrous variety of prospects, beasts, birds, fishes, insects, and vegetables, with which God has been pleased to enrich the several parts of this globe; a variety which, as it must give great pleasure to a contemplative beholder, so doth it admirably display the power, and wisdom, and goodness of the Creator. Indeed, to say the truth, there is but one work in his whole creation that doth him any dishonour, and with that I have long since avoided holding any conversation.”\n\n“You will pardon me,” cries Jones; “but I have always imagined that there is in this very work you mention as great variety as in all the rest; for, besides the difference of inclination, customs and climates have, I am told, introduced the utmost diversity into human nature.”\n\n“Very little indeed,” answered the other: “those who travel in order to acquaint themselves with the different manners of men might spare themselves much pains by going to a carnival at Venice; for there they will see at once all which they can discover in the several courts of Europe. The same hypocrisy, the same fraud; in short, the same follies and vices dressed in different habits. In Spain, these are equipped with much gravity; and in Italy, with vast splendor. In France, a knave is dressed like a fop; and in the northern countries, like a sloven. But human nature is everywhere the same, everywhere the object of detestation and scorn.\n\n“As for my own part, I past through all these nations as you perhaps may have done through a croud at a shew-jostling to get by them, holding my nose with one hand, and defending my pockets with the other, without speaking a word to any of them, while I was pressing on to see what I wanted to see; which, however entertaining it might be in itself, scarce made me amends for the trouble the company gave me.”\n\n“Did not you find some of the nations among which you travelled less troublesome to you than others?” said Jones. “O yes,” replied the old man: “the Turks were much more tolerable to me than the Christians; for they are men of profound taciturnity, and never disturb a stranger with questions. Now and then indeed they bestow a short curse upon him, or spit in his face as he walks the streets, but then they have done with him; and a man may live an age in their country without hearing a dozen words from them. But of all the people I ever saw, heaven defend me from the French! With their damned prate and civilities, and doing the honour of their nation to strangers (as they are pleased to call it), but indeed setting forth their own vanity; they are so troublesome, that I had infinitely rather pass my life with the Hottentots than set my foot in Paris again. They are a nasty people, but their nastiness is mostly without; whereas, in France, and some other nations that I won't name, it is all within, and makes them stink much more to my reason than that of Hottentots does to my nose.\n\n“Thus, sir, I have ended the history of my life; for as to all that series of years during which I have lived retired here, it affords no variety to entertain you, and may be almost considered as one day.[*] The retirement has been so compleat, that I could hardly have enjoyed a more absolute solitude in the deserts of the Thebais than here in the midst of this populous kingdom. As I have no estate, I am plagued with no tenants or stewards: my annuity is paid me pretty regularly, as indeed it ought to be; for it is much less than what I might have expected in return for what I gave up. Visits I admit none; and the old woman who keeps my house knows that her place entirely depends upon her saving me all the trouble of buying the things that I want, keeping off all sollicitation or business from me, and holding her tongue whenever I am within hearing. As my walks are all by night, I am pretty secure in this wild unfrequented place from meeting any company. Some few persons I have met by chance, and sent them home heartily frighted, as from the oddness of my dress and figure they took me for a ghost or a hobgoblin. But what has happened to-night shows that even here I cannot be safe from the villany of men; for without your assistance I had not only been robbed, but very probably murdered.”\n[*] the rest of this paragraph is omitted in the third edition\n\nJones thanked the stranger for the trouble he had taken in relating his story, and then expressed some wonder how he could possibly endure a life of such solitude; “in which,” says he, “you may well complain of the want of variety. Indeed I am astonished how you have filled up, or rather killed, so much of your time.”\n\n“I am not at all surprized,” answered the other, “that to one whose affections and thoughts are fixed on the world my hours should appear to have wanted employment in this place: but there is one single act, for which the whole life of man is infinitely too short: what time can suffice for the contemplation and worship of that glorious, immortal, and eternal Being, among the works of whose stupendous creation not only this globe, but even those numberless luminaries which we may here behold spangling all the sky, though they should many of them be suns lighting different systems of worlds, may possibly appear but as a few atoms opposed to the whole earth which we inhabit? Can a man who by divine meditations is admitted as it were into the conversation of this ineffable, incomprehensible Majesty, think days, or years, or ages, too long for the continuance of so ravishing an honour? Shall the trifling amusements, the palling pleasures, the silly business of the world, roll away our hours too swiftly from us; and shall the pace of time seem sluggish to a mind exercised in studies so high, so important, and so glorious? As no time is sufficient, so no place is improper, for this great concern. On what object can we cast our eyes which may not inspire us with ideas of his power, of his wisdom, and of his goodness? It is not necessary that the rising sun should dart his fiery glories over the eastern horizon; nor that the boisterous winds should rush from their caverns, and shake the lofty forest; nor that the opening clouds should pour their deluges on the plains: it is not necessary, I say, that any of these should proclaim his majesty: there is not an insect, not a vegetable, of so low an order in the creation as not to be honoured with bearing marks of the attributes of its great Creator; marks not only of his power, but of his wisdom and goodness. Man alone, the king of this globe, the last and greatest work of the Supreme Being, below the sun; man alone hath basely dishonoured his own nature; and by dishonesty, cruelty, ingratitude, and treachery, hath called his Maker's goodness in question, by puzzling us to account how a benevolent being should form so foolish and so vile an animal. Yet this is the being from whose conversation you think, I suppose, that I have been unfortunately restrained, and without whose blessed society, life, in your opinion, must be tedious and insipid.”\n\n“In the former part of what you said,” replied Jones, “I most heartily and readily concur; but I believe, as well as hope, that the abhorrence which you express for mankind in the conclusion, is much too general. Indeed, you here fall into an error, which in my little experience I have observed to be a very common one, by taking the character of mankind from the worst and basest among them; whereas, indeed, as an excellent writer observes, nothing should be esteemed as characteristical of a species, but what is to be found among the best and most perfect individuals of that species. This error, I believe, is generally committed by those who from want of proper caution in the choice of their friends and acquaintance, have suffered injuries from bad and worthless men; two or three instances of which are very unjustly charged on all human nature.”\n\n“I think I had experience enough of it,” answered the other: “my first mistress and my first friend betrayed me in the basest manner, and in matters which threatened to be of the worst of consequences--even to bring me to a shameful death.”\n\n“But you will pardon me,” cries Jones, “if I desire you to reflect who that mistress and who that friend were. What better, my good sir, could be expected in love derived from the stews, or in friendship first produced and nourished at the gaming-table? To take the characters of women from the former instance, or of men from the latter, would be as unjust as to assert that air is a nauseous and unwholesome element, because we find it so in a jakes. I have lived but a short time in the world, and yet have known men worthy of the highest friendship, and women of the highest love.”\n\n“Alas! young man,” answered the stranger, “you have lived, you confess, but a very short time in the world: I was somewhat older than you when I was of the same opinion.”\n\n“You might have remained so still,” replies Jones, “if you had not been unfortunate, I will venture to say incautious, in the placing your affections. If there was, indeed, much more wickedness in the world than there is, it would not prove such general assertions against human nature, since much of this arrives by mere accident, and many a man who commits evil is not totally bad and corrupt in his heart. In truth, none seem to have any title to assert human nature to be necessarily and universally evil, but those whose own minds afford them one instance of this natural depravity; which is not, I am convinced, your case.”\n\n“And such,” said the stranger, “will be always the most backward to assert any such thing. Knaves will no more endeavour to persuade us of the baseness of mankind, than a highwayman will inform you that there are thieves on the road. This would, indeed, be a method to put you on your guard, and to defeat their own purposes. For which reason, though knaves, as I remember, are very apt to abuse particular persons, yet they never cast any reflection on human nature in general.” The old gentleman spoke this so warmly, that as Jones despaired of making a convert, and was unwilling to offend, he returned no answer.\n\nThe day now began to send forth its first streams of light, when Jones made an apology to the stranger for having staid so long, and perhaps detained him from his rest. The stranger answered, “He never wanted rest less than at present; for that day and night were indifferent seasons to him; and that he commonly made use of the former for the time of his repose and of the latter for his walks and lucubrations. However,” said he, “it is now a most lovely morning, and if you can bear any longer to be without your own rest or food, I will gladly entertain you with the sight of some very fine prospects which I believe you have not yet seen.”\n\nJones very readily embraced this offer, and they immediately set forward together from the cottage. As for Partridge, he had fallen into a profound repose just as the stranger had finished his story; for his curiosity was satisfied, and the subsequent discourse was not forcible enough in its operation to conjure down the charms of sleep. Jones therefore left him to enjoy his nap; and as the reader may perhaps be at this season glad of the same favour, we will here put an end to the eighth book of our history.\n\nOf those who lawfully may, and of those who may not, write such histories as this.\n\nAmong other good uses for which I have thought proper to institute these several introductory chapters, I have considered them as a kind of mark or stamp, which may hereafter enable a very indifferent reader to distinguish what is true and genuine in this historic kind of writing, from what is false and counterfeit. Indeed, it seems likely that some such mark may shortly become necessary, since the favourable reception which two or three authors have lately procured for their works of this nature from the public, will probably serve as an encouragement to many others to undertake the like. Thus a swarm of foolish novels and monstrous romances will be produced, either to the great impoverishing of booksellers, or to the great loss of time and depravation of morals in the reader; nay, often to the spreading of scandal and calumny, and to the prejudice of the characters of many worthy and honest people.\n\nI question not but the ingenious author of the Spectator was principally induced to prefix Greek and Latin mottos to every paper, from the same consideration of guarding against the pursuit of those scribblers, who having no talents of a writer but what is taught by the writing-master, are yet nowise afraid nor ashamed to assume the same titles with the greatest genius, than their good brother in the fable was of braying in the lion's skin.\n\nBy the device therefore of his motto, it became impracticable for any man to presume to imitate the Spectators, without understanding at least one sentence in the learned languages. In the same manner I have now secured myself from the imitation of those who are utterly incapable of any degree of reflection, and whose learning is not equal to an essay.\n\nI would not be here understood to insinuate, that the greatest merit of such historical productions can ever lie in these introductory chapters; but, in fact, those parts which contain mere narrative only, afford much more encouragement to the pen of an imitator, than those which are composed of observation and reflection. Here I mean such imitators as Rowe was of Shakespear, or as Horace hints some of the Romans were of Cato, by bare feet and sour faces.\n\nTo invent good stories, and to tell them well, are possibly very rare talents, and yet I have observed few persons who have scrupled to aim at both: and if we examine the romances and novels with which the world abounds, I think we may fairly conclude, that most of the authors would not have attempted to show their teeth (if the expression may be allowed me) in any other way of writing; nor could indeed have strung together a dozen sentences on any other subject whatever.\n\nScribimus indocti doctique passim,[*]\n\n[*] --Each desperate blockhead dares to write: Verse is the trade of every living wight.--FRANCIS.\n\nmay be more truly said of the historian and biographer, than of any other species of writing; for all the arts and sciences (even criticism itself) require some little degree of learning and knowledge. Poetry, indeed, may perhaps be thought an exception; but then it demands numbers, or something like numbers: whereas, to the composition of novels and romances, nothing is necessary but paper, pens, and ink, with the manual capacity of using them. This, I conceive, their productions show to be the opinion of the authors themselves: and this must be the opinion of their readers, if indeed there be any such.\n\nHence we are to derive that universal contempt which the world, who always denominate the whole from the majority, have cast on all historical writers who do not draw their materials from records. And it is the apprehension of this contempt that hath made us so cautiously avoid the term romance, a name with which we might otherwise have been well enough contented. Though, as we have good authority for all our characters, no less indeed than the vast authentic doomsday-book of nature, as is elsewhere hinted, our labours have sufficient title to the name of history. Certainly they deserve some distinction from those works, which one of the wittiest of men regarded only as proceeding from a pruritus, or indeed rather from a looseness of the brain.\n\nBut besides the dishonour which is thus cast on one of the most useful as well as entertaining of all kinds of writing, there is just reason to apprehend, that by encouraging such authors we shall propagate much dishonour of another kind; I mean to the characters of many good and valuable members of society; for the dullest writers, no more than the dullest companions, are always inoffensive. They have both enough of language to be indecent and abusive. And surely if the opinion just above cited be true, we cannot wonder that works so nastily derived should be nasty themselves, or have a tendency to make others so.\n\nTo prevent therefore, for the future, such intemperate abuses of leisure, of letters, and of the liberty of the press, especially as the world seems at present to be more than usually threatened with them, I shall here venture to mention some qualifications, every one of which are in a pretty high degree necessary to this order of historians.\n\nThe first is, genius, without a full vein of which no study, says Horace, can avail us. By genius I would understand that power or rather those powers of the mind, which are capable of penetrating into all things within our reach and knowledge, and of distinguishing their essential differences. These are no other than invention and judgment; and they are both called by the collective name of genius, as they are of those gifts of nature which we bring with us into the world. Concerning each of which many seem to have fallen into very great errors; for by invention, I believe, is generally understood a creative faculty, which would indeed prove most romance writers to have the highest pretensions to it; whereas by invention is really meant no more (and so the word signifies) than discovery, or finding out; or to explain it at large, a quick and sagacious penetration into the true essence of all the objects of our contemplation. This, I think, can rarely exist without the concomitancy of judgment; for how we can be said to have discovered the true essence of two things, without discerning their difference, seems to me hard to conceive. Now this last is the undisputed province of judgment, and yet some few men of wit have agreed with all the dull fellows in the world in representing these two to have been seldom or never the property of one and the same person.\n\nBut though they should be so, they are not sufficient for our purpose, without a good share of learning; for which I could again cite the authority of Horace, and of many others, if any was necessary to prove that tools are of no service to a workman, when they are not sharpened by art, or when he wants rules to direct him in his work, or hath no matter to work upon. All these uses are supplied by learning; for nature can only furnish us with capacity; or, as I have chose to illustrate it, with the tools of our profession; learning must fit them for use, must direct them in it, and, lastly, must contribute part at least of the materials. A competent knowledge of history and of the belles-lettres is here absolutely necessary; and without this share of knowledge at least, to affect the character of an historian, is as vain as to endeavour at building a house without timber or mortar, or brick or stone. Homer and Milton, who, though they added the ornament of numbers to their works, were both historians of our order, were masters of all the learning of their times.\n\nAgain, there is another sort of knowledge, beyond the power of learning to bestow, and this is to be had by conversation. So necessary is this to the understanding the characters of men, that none are more ignorant of them than those learned pedants whose lives have been entirely consumed in colleges, and among books; for however exquisitely human nature may have been described by writers, the true practical system can be learnt only in the world. Indeed the like happens in every other kind of knowledge. Neither physic nor law are to be practically known from books. Nay, the farmer, the planter, the gardener, must perfect by experience what he hath acquired the rudiments of by reading. How accurately soever the ingenious Mr Miller may have described the plant, he himself would advise his disciple to see it in the garden. As we must perceive, that after the nicest strokes of a Shakespear or a Jonson, of a Wycherly or an Otway, some touches of nature will escape the reader, which the judicious action of a Garrick, of a Cibber, or a Clive,[*] can convey to him; so, on the real stage, the character shows himself in a stronger and bolder light than he can be described. And if this be the case in those fine and nervous descriptions which great authors themselves have taken from life, how much more strongly will it hold when the writer himself takes his lines not from nature, but from books? Such characters are only the faint copy of a copy, and can have neither the justness nor spirit of an original.\n\n[*] There is a peculiar propriety in mentioning this great actor, and these two most justly celebrated actresses, in this place, as they have all formed themselves on the study of nature only, and not on the imitation of their predecessors. Hence they have been able to excel all who have gone before them; a degree of merit which the servile herd of imitators can never possibly arrive at.\n\nNow this conversation in our historian must be universal, that is, with all ranks and degrees of men; for the knowledge of what is called high life will not instruct him in low; nor, e converso, will his being acquainted with the inferior part of mankind teach him the manners of the superior. And though it may be thought that the knowledge of either may sufficiently enable him to describe at least that in which he hath been conversant, yet he will even here fall greatly short of perfection; for the follies of either rank do in reality illustrate each other. For instance, the affectation of high life appears more glaring and ridiculous from the simplicity of the low; and again, the rudeness and barbarity of this latter, strikes with much stronger ideas of absurdity, when contrasted with, and opposed to, the politeness which controuls the former. Besides, to say the truth, the manners of our historian will be improved by both these conversations; for in the one he will easily find examples of plainness, honesty, and sincerity; in the other of refinement, elegance, and a liberality of spirit; which last quality I myself have scarce ever seen in men of low birth and education.\n\nNor will all the qualities I have hitherto given my historian avail him, unless he have what is generally meant by a good heart, and be capable of feeling. The author who will make me weep, says Horace, must first weep himself. In reality, no man can paint a distress well which he doth not feel while he is painting it; nor do I doubt, but that the most pathetic and affecting scenes have been writ with tears. In the same manner it is with the ridiculous. I am convinced I never make my reader laugh heartily but where I have laughed before him; unless it should happen at any time, that instead of laughing with me he should be inclined to laugh at me. Perhaps this may have been the case at some passages in this chapter, from which apprehension I will here put an end to it.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nContaining a very surprizing adventure indeed, which Mr Jones met with in his walk with the Man of the Hill.\n\nAurora now first opened her casement, Anglice the day began to break, when Jones walked forth in company with the stranger, and mounted Mazard Hill; of which they had no sooner gained the summit than one of the most noble prospects in the world presented itself to their view, and which we would likewise present to the reader, but for two reasons: first, we despair of making those who have seen this prospect admire our description; secondly, we very much doubt whether those who have not seen it would understand it.\n\nJones stood for some minutes fixed in one posture, and directing his eyes towards the south; upon which the old gentleman asked, What he was looking at with so much attention? “Alas! sir,” answered he with a sigh, “I was endeavouring to trace out my own journey hither. Good heavens! what a distance is Gloucester from us! What a vast track of land must be between me and my own home!”--“Ay, ay, young gentleman,” cries the other, “and by your sighing, from what you love better than your own home, or I am mistaken. I perceive now the object of your contemplation is not within your sight, and yet I fancy you have a pleasure in looking that way.” Jones answered with a smile, “I find, old friend, you have not yet forgot the sensations of your youth. I own my thoughts were employed as you have guessed.”\n\nThey now walked to that part of the hill which looks to the north-west, and which hangs over a vast and extensive wood. Here they were no sooner arrived than they heard at a distance the most violent screams of a woman, proceeding from the wood below them. Jones listened a moment, and then, without saying a word to his companion (for indeed the occasion seemed sufficiently pressing), ran, or rather slid, down the hill, and, without the least apprehension or concern for his own safety, made directly to the thicket, whence the sound had issued.\n\nHe had not entered far into the wood before he beheld a most shocking sight indeed, a woman stript half naked, under the hands of a ruffian, who had put his garter round her neck, and was endeavouring to draw her up to a tree. Jones asked no questions at this interval, but fell instantly upon the villain, and made such good use of his trusty oaken stick that he laid him sprawling on the ground before he could defend himself, indeed almost before he knew he was attacked; nor did he cease the prosecution of his blows till the woman herself begged him to forbear, saying, she believed he had sufficiently done his business.\n\nThe poor wretch then fell upon her knees to Jones, and gave him a thousand thanks for her deliverance. He presently lifted her up, and told her he was highly pleased with the extraordinary accident which had sent him thither for her relief, where it was so improbable she should find any; adding, that Heaven seemed to have designed him as the happy instrument of her protection. “Nay,” answered she, “I could almost conceive you to be some good angel; and, to say the truth, you look more like an angel than a man in my eye.” Indeed he was a charming figure; and if a very fine person, and a most comely set of features, adorned with youth, health, strength, freshness, spirit, and good-nature, can make a man resemble an angel, he certainly had that resemblance.\n\nThe redeemed captive had not altogether so much of the human-angelic species: she seemed to be at least of the middle age, nor had her face much appearance of beauty; but her cloaths being torn from all the upper part of her body, her breasts, which were well formed and extremely white, attracted the eyes of her deliverer, and for a few moments they stood silent, and gazing at each other; till the ruffian on the ground beginning to move, Jones took the garter which had been intended for another purpose, and bound both his hands behind him. And now, on contemplating his face, he discovered, greatly to his surprize, and perhaps not a little to his satisfaction, this very person to be no other than ensign Northerton. Nor had the ensign forgotten his former antagonist, whom he knew the moment he came to himself. His surprize was equal to that of Jones; but I conceive his pleasure was rather less on this occasion.\n\nJones helped Northerton upon his legs, and then looking him stedfastly in the face, “I fancy, sir,” said he, “you did not expect to meet me any more in this world, and I confess I had as little expectation to find you here. However, fortune, I see, hath brought us once more together, and hath given me satisfaction for the injury I have received, even without my own knowledge.”\n\n“It is very much like a man of honour, indeed,” answered Northerton, “to take satisfaction by knocking a man down behind his back. Neither am I capable of giving you satisfaction here, as I have no sword; but if you dare behave like a gentleman, let us go where I can furnish myself with one, and I will do by you as a man of honour ought.”\n\n“Doth it become such a villain as you are,” cries Jones, “to contaminate the name of honour by assuming it? But I shall waste no time in discourse with you. Justice requires satisfaction of you now, and shall have it.” Then turning to the woman, he asked her, if she was near her home; or if not, whether she was acquainted with any house in the neighbourhood, where she might procure herself some decent cloaths, in order to proceed to a justice of the peace.\n\nShe answered she was an entire stranger in that part of the world. Jones then recollecting himself, said, he had a friend near who would direct them; indeed, he wondered at his not following; but, in fact, the good Man of the Hill, when our heroe departed, sat himself down on the brow, where, though he had a gun in his hand, he with great patience and unconcern had attended the issue.\n\nJones then stepping without the wood, perceived the old man sitting as we have just described him; he presently exerted his utmost agility, and with surprizing expedition ascended the hill.\n\nThe old man advised him to carry the woman to Upton, which, he said, was the nearest town, and there he would be sure of furnishing her with all manner of conveniencies. Jones having received his direction to the place, took his leave of the Man of the Hill, and, desiring him to direct Partridge the same way, returned hastily to the wood.\n\nOur heroe, at his departure to make this enquiry of his friend, had considered, that as the ruffian's hands were tied behind him, he was incapable of executing any wicked purposes on the poor woman. Besides, he knew he should not be beyond the reach of her voice, and could return soon enough to prevent any mischief. He had moreover declared to the villain, that if he attempted the least insult, he would be himself immediately the executioner of vengeance on him. But Jones unluckily forgot, that though the hands of Northerton were tied, his legs were at liberty; nor did he lay the least injunction on the prisoner that he should not make what use of these he pleased. Northerton therefore having given no parole of that kind, thought he might without any breach of honour depart; not being obliged, as he imagined, by any rules, to wait for a formal discharge. He therefore took up his legs, which were at liberty, and walked off through the wood, which favoured his retreat; nor did the woman, whose eyes were perhaps rather turned toward her deliverer, once think of his escape, or give herself any concern or trouble to prevent it.\n\nJones therefore, at his return, found the woman alone. He would have spent some time in searching for Northerton, but she would not permit him; earnestly entreating that he would accompany her to the town whither they had been directed. “As to the fellow's escape,” said she, “it gives me no uneasiness; for philosophy and Christianity both preach up forgiveness of injuries. But for you, sir, I am concerned at the trouble I give you; nay, indeed, my nakedness may well make me ashamed to look you in the face; and if it was not for the sake of your protection, I should wish to go alone.”\n\nJones offered her his coat; but, I know not for what reason, she absolutely refused the most earnest solicitations to accept it. He then begged her to forget both the causes of her confusion. “With regard to the former,” says he, “I have done no more than my duty in protecting you; and as for the latter, I will entirely remove it, by walking before you all the way; for I would not have my eyes offend you, and I could not answer for my power of resisting the attractive charms of so much beauty.”\n\nThus our heroe and the redeemed lady walked in the same manner as Orpheus and Eurydice marched heretofore; but though I cannot believe that Jones was designedly tempted by his fair one to look behind him, yet as she frequently wanted his assistance to help her over stiles, and had besides many trips and other accidents, he was often obliged to turn about. However, he had better fortune than what attended poor Orpheus, for he brought his companion, or rather follower, safe into the famous town of Upton.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nThe arrival of Mr Jones with his lady at the inn; with a very full description of the battle of Upton.\n\nThough the reader, we doubt not, is very eager to know who this lady was, and how she fell into the hands of Mr Northerton, we must beg him to suspend his curiosity for a short time, as we are obliged, for some very good reasons which hereafter perhaps he may guess, to delay his satisfaction a little longer.\n\nMr Jones and his fair companion no sooner entered the town, than they went directly to that inn which in their eyes presented the fairest appearance to the street. Here Jones, having ordered a servant to show a room above stairs, was ascending, when the dishevelled fair, hastily following, was laid hold on by the master of the house, who cried, “Heyday, where is that beggar wench going? Stay below stairs, I desire you.” But Jones at that instant thundered from above, “Let the lady come up,” in so authoritative a voice, that the good man instantly withdrew his hands, and the lady made the best of her way to the chamber.\n\nHere Jones wished her joy of her safe arrival, and then departed, in order, as he promised, to send the landlady up with some cloaths. The poor woman thanked him heartily for all his kindness, and said, she hoped she should see him again soon, to thank him a thousand times more. During this short conversation, she covered her white bosom as well as she could possibly with her arms; for Jones could not avoid stealing a sly peep or two, though he took all imaginable care to avoid giving any offence.\n\nOur travellers had happened to take up their residence at a house of exceeding good repute, whither Irish ladies of strict virtue, and many northern lasses of the same predicament, were accustomed to resort in their way to Bath. The landlady therefore would by no means have admitted any conversation of a disreputable kind to pass under her roof. Indeed, so foul and contagious are all such proceedings, that they contaminate the very innocent scenes where they are committed, and give the name of a bad house, or of a house of ill repute, to all those where they are suffered to be carried on.\n\nNot that I would intimate that such strict chastity as was preserved in the temple of Vesta can possibly be maintained at a public inn. My good landlady did not hope for such a blessing, nor would any of the ladies I have spoken of, or indeed any others of the most rigid note, have expected or insisted on any such thing. But to exclude all vulgar concubinage, and to drive all whores in rags from within the walls, is within the power of every one. This my landlady very strictly adhered to, and this her virtuous guests, who did not travel in rags, would very reasonably have expected of her.\n\nNow it required no very blameable degree of suspicion to imagine that Mr Jones and his ragged companion had certain purposes in their intention, which, though tolerated in some Christian countries, connived at in others, and practised in all, are however as expressly forbidden as murder, or any other horrid vice, by that religion which is universally believed in those countries. The landlady, therefore, had no sooner received an intimation of the entrance of the above-said persons than she began to meditate the most expeditious means for their expulsion. In order to this, she had provided herself with a long and deadly instrument, with which, in times of peace, the chambermaid was wont to demolish the labours of the industrious spider. In vulgar phrase, she had taken up the broomstick, and was just about to sally from the kitchen, when Jones accosted her with a demand of a gown and other vestments, to cover the half-naked woman upstairs.\n\nNothing can be more provoking to the human temper, nor more dangerous to that cardinal virtue, patience, than solicitations of extraordinary offices of kindness on behalf of those very persons with whom we are highly incensed. For this reason Shakespear hath artfully introduced his Desdemona soliciting favours for Cassio of her husband, as the means of inflaming, not only his jealousy, but his rage, to the highest pitch of madness; and we find the unfortunate Moor less able to command his passion on this occasion, than even when he beheld his valued present to his wife in the hands of his supposed rival. In fact, we regard these efforts as insults on our understanding, and to such the pride of man is very difficultly brought to submit.\n\nMy landlady, though a very good-tempered woman, had, I suppose, some of this pride in her composition, for Jones had scarce ended his request, when she fell upon him with a certain weapon, which, though it be neither long, nor sharp, nor hard, nor indeed threatens from its appearance with either death or wound, hath been however held in great dread and abhorrence by many wise men--nay, by many brave ones; insomuch, that some who have dared to look into the mouth of a loaded cannon, have not dared to look into a mouth where this weapon was brandished; and rather than run the hazard of its execution, have contented themselves with making a most pitiful and sneaking figure in the eyes of all their acquaintance.\n\nTo confess the truth, I am afraid Mr Jones was one of these; for though he was attacked and violently belaboured with the aforesaid weapon, he could not be provoked to make any resistance; but in a most cowardly manner applied, with many entreaties, to his antagonist to desist from pursuing her blows; in plain English, he only begged her with the utmost earnestness to hear him; but before he could obtain his request, my landlord himself entered into the fray, and embraced that side of the cause which seemed to stand very little in need of assistance.\n\nThere are a sort of heroes who are supposed to be determined in their chusing or avoiding a conflict by the character and behaviour of the person whom they are to engage. These are said to know their men, and Jones, I believe, knew his woman; for though he had been so submissive to her, he was no sooner attacked by her husband, than he demonstrated an immediate spirit of resentment, and enjoined him silence under a very severe penalty; no less than that, I think, of being converted into fuel for his own fire.\n\nThe husband, with great indignation, but with a mixture of pity, answered, “You must pray first to be made able. I believe I am a better man than yourself; ay, every way, that I am;” and presently proceeded to discharge half-a-dozen whores at the lady above stairs, the last of which had scarce issued from his lips, when a swinging blow from the cudgel that Jones carried in his hand assaulted him over the shoulders.\n\nIt is a question whether the landlord or the landlady was the most expeditious in returning this blow. My landlord, whose hands were empty, fell to with his fist, and the good wife, uplifting her broom and aiming at the head of Jones, had probably put an immediate end to the fray, and to Jones likewise, had not the descent of this broom been prevented--not by the miraculous intervention of any heathen deity, but by a very natural though fortunate accident, viz., by the arrival of Partridge; who entered the house at that instant (for fear had caused him to run every step from the hill), and who, seeing the danger which threatened his master or companion (which you chuse to call him), prevented so sad a catastrophe, by catching hold of the landlady's arm, as it was brandished aloft in the air.\n\nThe landlady soon perceived the impediment which prevented her blow; and being unable to rescue her arm from the hands of Partridge, she let fall the broom; and then leaving Jones to the discipline of her husband, she fell with the utmost fury on that poor fellow, who had already given some intimation of himself, by crying, “Zounds! do you intend to kill my friend?”\n\nPartridge, though not much addicted to battle, would not however stand still when his friend was attacked; nor was he much displeased with that part of the combat which fell to his share; he therefore returned my landlady's blows as soon as he received them: and now the fight was obstinately maintained on all parts, and it seemed doubtful to which side Fortune would incline, when the naked lady, who had listened at the top of the stairs to the dialogue which preceded the engagement, descended suddenly from above, and without weighing the unfair inequality of two to one, fell upon the poor woman who was boxing with Partridge; nor did that great champion desist, but rather redoubled his fury, when he found fresh succours were arrived to his assistance.\n\nVictory must now have fallen to the side of the travellers (for the bravest troops must yield to numbers) had not Susan the chambermaid come luckily to support her mistress. This Susan was as two-handed a wench (according to the phrase) as any in the country, and would, I believe, have beat the famed Thalestris herself, or any of her subject Amazons; for her form was robust and man-like, and every way made for such encounters. As her hands and arms were formed to give blows with great mischief to an enemy, so was her face as well contrived to receive blows without any great injury to herself, her nose being already flat to her face; her lips were so large, that no swelling could be perceived in them, and moreover they were so hard, that a fist could hardly make any impression on them. Lastly, her cheek-bones stood out, as if nature had intended them for two bastions to defend her eyes in those encounters for which she seemed so well calculated, and to which she was most wonderfully well inclined.\n\nThis fair creature entering the field of battle, immediately filed to that wing where her mistress maintained so unequal a fight with one of either sex. Here she presently challenged Partridge to single combat. He accepted the challenge, and a most desperate fight began between them.\n\nNow the dogs of war being let loose, began to lick their bloody lips; now Victory, with golden wings, hung hovering in the air; now Fortune, taking her scales from her shelf, began to weigh the fates of Tom Jones, his female companion, and Partridge, against the landlord, his wife, and maid; all which hung in exact balance before her; when a good-natured accident put suddenly an end to the bloody fray, with which half of the combatants had already sufficiently feasted. This accident was the arrival of a coach and four; upon which my landlord and landlady immediately desisted from fighting, and at their entreaty obtained the same favour of their antagonists: but Susan was not so kind to Partridge; for that Amazonian fair having overthrown and bestrid her enemy, was now cuffing him lustily with both her hands, without any regard to his request of a cessation of arms, or to those loud exclamations of murder which he roared forth.\n\nNo sooner, however, had Jones quitted the landlord, than he flew to the rescue of his defeated companion, from whom he with much difficulty drew off the enraged chambermaid: but Partridge was not immediately sensible of his deliverance, for he still lay flat on the floor, guarding his face with his hands; nor did he cease roaring till Jones had forced him to look up, and to perceive that the battle was at an end.\n\nThe landlord, who had no visible hurt, and the landlady, hiding her well-scratched face with her handkerchief, ran both hastily to the door to attend the coach, from which a young lady and her maid now alighted. These the landlady presently ushered into that room where Mr Jones had at first deposited his fair prize, as it was the best apartment in the house. Hither they were obliged to pass through the field of battle, which they did with the utmost haste, covering their faces with their handkerchiefs, as desirous to avoid the notice of any one. Indeed their caution was quite unnecessary; for the poor unfortunate Helen, the fatal cause of all the bloodshed, was entirely taken up in endeavouring to conceal her own face, and Jones was no less occupied in rescuing Partridge from the fury of Susan; which being happily effected, the poor fellow immediately departed to the pump to wash his face, and to stop that bloody torrent which Susan had plentifully set a-flowing from his nostrils.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nIn which the arrival of a man of war puts a final end to hostilities, and causes the conclusion of a firm and lasting peace between all parties.\n\nA serjeant and a file of musqueteers, with a deserter in their custody, arrived about this time. The serjeant presently enquired for the principal magistrate of the town, and was informed by my landlord, that he himself was vested in that office. He then demanded his billets, together with a mug of beer, and complaining it was cold, spread himself before the kitchen fire.\n\nMr Jones was at this time comforting the poor distressed lady, who sat down at a table in the kitchen, and leaning her head upon her arm, was bemoaning her misfortunes; but lest my fair readers should be in pain concerning a particular circumstance, I think proper here to acquaint them, that before she had quitted the room above stairs, she had so well covered herself with a pillowbeer which she there found, that her regard to decency was not in the least violated by the presence of so many men as were now in the room.\n\nOne of the soldiers now went up to the serjeant, and whispered something in his ear; upon which he stedfastly fixed his eyes on the lady, and having looked at her for near a minute, he came up to her, saying, “I ask pardon, madam; but I am certain I am not deceived; you can be no other person than Captain Waters's lady?”\n\nThe poor woman, who in her present distress had very little regarded the face of any person present, no sooner looked at the serjeant than she presently recollected him, and calling him by his name, answered, “That she was indeed the unhappy person he imagined her to be;” but added, “I wonder any one should know me in this disguise.” To which the serjeant replied, “He was very much surprized to see her ladyship in such a dress, and was afraid some accident had happened to her.”--“An accident hath happened to me, indeed,” says she, “and I am highly obliged to this gentleman” (pointing to Jones) “that it was not a fatal one, or that I am now living to mention it.”--“Whatever the gentleman hath done,” cries the serjeant, “I am sure the captain will make him amends for it; and if I can be of any service, your ladyship may command me, and I shall think myself very happy to have it in my power to serve your ladyship; and so indeed may any one, for I know the captain will well reward them for it.”\n\nThe landlady, who heard from the stairs all that past between the serjeant and Mrs Waters, came hastily down, and running directly up to her, began to ask pardon for the offences she had committed, begging that all might be imputed to ignorance of her quality: for, “Lud! madam,” says she, “how should I have imagined that a lady of your fashion would appear in such a dress? I am sure, madam, if I had once suspected that your ladyship was your ladyship, I would sooner have burnt my tongue out, than have said what I have said; and I hope your ladyship will accept of a gown, till you can get your own cloaths.”\n\n“Prithee, woman,” says Mrs Waters, “cease your impertinence: how can you imagine I should concern myself about anything which comes from the lips of such low creatures as yourself? But I am surprized at your assurance in thinking, after what is past, that I will condescend to put on any of your dirty things. I would have you know, creature, I have a spirit above that.”\n\nHere Jones interfered, and begged Mrs Waters to forgive the landlady, and to accept her gown: “for I must confess,” cries he, “our appearance was a little suspicious when first we came in; and I am well assured all this good woman did was, as she professed, out of regard to the reputation of her house.”\n\n“Yes, upon my truly was it,” says she: “the gentleman speaks very much like a gentleman, and I see very plainly is so; and to be certain the house is well known to be a house of as good reputation as any on the road, and though I say it, is frequented by gentry of the best quality, both Irish and English. I defy anybody to say black is my eye, for that matter. And, as I was saying, if I had known your ladyship to be your ladyship, I would as soon have burnt my fingers as have affronted your ladyship; but truly where gentry come and spend their money, I am not willing that they should be scandalized by a set of poor shabby vermin, that, wherever they go, leave more lice than money behind them; such folks never raise my compassion, for to be certain it is foolish to have any for them; and if our justices did as they ought, they would be all whipt out of the kingdom, for to be certain it is what is most fitting for them. But as for your ladyship, I am heartily sorry your ladyship hath had a misfortune, and if your ladyship will do me the honour to wear my cloaths till you can get some of your ladyship's own, to be certain the best I have is at your ladyship's service.”\n\nWhether cold, shame, or the persuasions of Mr Jones prevailed most on Mrs Waters, I will not determine, but she suffered herself to be pacified by this speech of my landlady, and retired with that good woman, in order to apparel herself in a decent manner.\n\nMy landlord was likewise beginning his oration to Jones, but was presently interrupted by that generous youth, who shook him heartily by the hand, and assured him of entire forgiveness, saying, “If you are satisfied, my worthy friend, I promise you I am;” and indeed, in one sense, the landlord had the better reason to be satisfied; for he had received a bellyfull of drubbing, whereas Jones had scarce felt a single blow.\n\nPartridge, who had been all this time washing his bloody nose at the pump, returned into the kitchen at the instant when his master and the landlord were shaking hands with each other. As he was of a peaceable disposition, he was pleased with those symptoms of reconciliation; and though his face bore some marks of Susan's fist, and many more of her nails, he rather chose to be contented with his fortune in the last battle than to endeavour at bettering it in another.\n\nThe heroic Susan was likewise well contented with her victory, though it had cost her a black eye, which Partridge had given her at the first onset. Between these two, therefore, a league was struck, and those hands which had been the instruments of war became now the mediators of peace.\n\nMatters were thus restored to a perfect calm; at which the serjeant, though it may seem so contrary to the principles of his profession, testified his approbation. “Why now, that's friendly,” said he; “d--n me, I hate to see two people bear ill-will to one another after they have had a tussel. The only way when friends quarrel is to see it out fairly in a friendly manner, as a man may call it, either with a fist, or sword, or pistol, according as they like, and then let it be all over; for my own part, d--n me if ever I love my friend better than when I am fighting with him! To bear malice is more like a Frenchman than an Englishman.”\n\nHe then proposed a libation as a necessary part of the ceremony at all treaties of this kind. Perhaps the reader may here conclude that he was well versed in antient history; but this, though highly probable, as he cited no authority to support the custom, I will not affirm with any confidence. Most likely indeed it is, that he founded his opinion on very good authority, since he confirmed it with many violent oaths.\n\nJones no sooner heard the proposal than, immediately agreeing with the learned serjeant, he ordered a bowl, or rather a large mug, filled with the liquor used on these occasions, to be brought in, and then began the ceremony himself. He placed his right hand in that of the landlord, and, seizing the bowl with his left, uttered the usual words, and then made his libation. After which, the same was observed by all present. Indeed, there is very little need of being particular in describing the whole form, as it differed so little from those libations of which so much is recorded in antient authors and their modern transcribers. The principal difference lay in two instances; for, first, the present company poured the liquor only down their throats; and, secondly, the serjeant, who officiated as priest, drank the last; but he preserved, I believe, the antient form, in swallowing much the largest draught of the whole company, and in being the only person present who contributed nothing towards the libation besides his good offices in assisting at the performance.\n\nThe good people now ranged themselves round the kitchen fire, where good humour seemed to maintain an absolute dominion; and Partridge not only forgot his shameful defeat, but converted hunger into thirst, and soon became extremely facetious. We must however quit this agreeable assembly for a while, and attend Mr Jones to Mrs Waters's apartment, where the dinner which he had bespoke was now on the table. Indeed, it took no long time in preparing, having been all drest three days before, and required nothing more from the cook than to warm it over again.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nAn apology for all heroes who have good stomachs, with a description of a battle of the amorous kind.\nHeroes, notwithstanding the high ideas which, by the means of flatterers, they may entertain of themselves, or the world may conceive of them, have certainly more of mortal than divine about them. However elevated their minds may be, their bodies at least (which is much the major part of most) are liable to the worst infirmities, and subject to the vilest offices of human nature. Among these latter, the act of eating, which hath by several wise men been considered as extremely mean and derogatory from the philosophic dignity, must be in some measure performed by the greatest prince, heroe, or philosopher upon earth; nay, sometimes Nature hath been so frolicsome as to exact of these dignified characters a much more exorbitant share of this office than she hath obliged those of the lowest order to perform.\n\nTo say the truth, as no known inhabitant of this globe is really more than man, so none need be ashamed of submitting to what the necessities of man demand; but when those great personages I have just mentioned condescend to aim at confining such low offices to themselves--as when, by hoarding or destroying, they seem desirous to prevent any others from eating--then they surely become very low and despicable.\n\nNow, after this short preface, we think it no disparagement to our heroe to mention the immoderate ardour with which he laid about him at this season. Indeed, it may be doubted whether Ulysses, who by the way seems to have had the best stomach of all the heroes in that eating poem of the Odyssey, ever made a better meal. Three pounds at least of that flesh which formerly had contributed to the composition of an ox was now honoured with becoming part of the individual Mr Jones.\n\nThis particular we thought ourselves obliged to mention, as it may account for our heroe's temporary neglect of his fair companion, who eat but very little, and was indeed employed in considerations of a very different nature, which passed unobserved by Jones, till he had entirely satisfied that appetite which a fast of twenty-four hours had procured him; but his dinner was no sooner ended than his attention to other matters revived; with these matters therefore we shall now proceed to acquaint the reader.\n\nMr Jones, of whose personal accomplishments we have hitherto said very little, was, in reality, one of the handsomest young fellows in the world. His face, besides being the picture of health, had in it the most apparent marks of sweetness and good-nature. These qualities were indeed so characteristical in his countenance, that, while the spirit and sensibility in his eyes, though they must have been perceived by an accurate observer, might have escaped the notice of the less discerning, so strongly was this good-nature painted in his look, that it was remarked by almost every one who saw him.\n\nIt was, perhaps, as much owing to this as to a very fine complexion that his face had a delicacy in it almost inexpressible, and which might have given him an air rather too effeminate, had it not been joined to a most masculine person and mien: which latter had as much in them of the Hercules as the former had of the Adonis. He was besides active, genteel, gay, and good-humoured; and had a flow of animal spirits which enlivened every conversation where he was present.\n\nWhen the reader hath duly reflected on these many charms which all centered in our heroe, and considers at the same time the fresh obligations which Mrs Waters had to him, it will be a mark more of prudery than candour to entertain a bad opinion of her because she conceived a very good opinion of him.\n\nBut, whatever censures may be passed upon her, it is my business to relate matters of fact with veracity. Mrs Waters had, in truth, not only a good opinion of our heroe, but a very great affection for him. To speak out boldly at once, she was in love, according to the present universally-received sense of that phrase, by which love is applied indiscriminately to the desirable objects of all our passions, appetites, and senses, and is understood to be that preference which we give to one kind of food rather than to another.\n\nBut though the love to these several objects may possibly be one and the same in all cases, its operations however must be allowed to be different; for, how much soever we may be in love with an excellent surloin of beef, or bottle of Burgundy; with a damask rose, or Cremona fiddle; yet do we never smile, nor ogle, nor dress, nor flatter, nor endeavour by any other arts or tricks to gain the affection of the said beef, &c. Sigh indeed we sometimes may; but it is generally in the absence, not in the presence, of the beloved object. For otherwise we might possibly complain of their ingratitude and deafness, with the same reason as Pasiphae doth of her bull, whom she endeavoured to engage by all the coquetry practised with good success in the drawing-room on the much more sensible as well as tender hearts of the fine gentlemen there.\n\nThe contrary happens in that love which operates between persons of the same species, but of different sexes. Here we are no sooner in love than it becomes our principal care to engage the affection of the object beloved. For what other purpose indeed are our youth instructed in all the arts of rendering themselves agreeable? If it was not with a view to this love, I question whether any of those trades which deal in setting off and adorning the human person would procure a livelihood. Nay, those great polishers of our manners, who are by some thought to teach what principally distinguishes us from the brute creation, even dancing-masters themselves, might possibly find no place in society. In short, all the graces which young ladies and young gentlemen too learn from others, and the many improvements which, by the help of a looking-glass, they add of their own, are in reality those very spicula et faces amoris so often mentioned by Ovid; or, as they are sometimes called in our own language, the whole artillery of love.\n\nNow Mrs Waters and our heroe had no sooner sat down together than the former began to play this artillery upon the latter. But here, as we are about to attempt a description hitherto unassayed either in prose or verse, we think proper to invoke the assistance of certain aërial beings, who will, we doubt not, come kindly to our aid on this occasion.\n\n“Say then, ye Graces! you that inhabit the heavenly mansions of Seraphina's countenance; for you are truly divine, are always in her presence, and well know all the arts of charming; say, what were the weapons now used to captivate the heart of Mr Jones.”\n\n“First, from two lovely blue eyes, whose bright orbs flashed lightning at their discharge, flew forth two pointed ogles; but, happily for our heroe, hit only a vast piece of beef which he was then conveying into his plate, and harmless spent their force. The fair warrior perceived their miscarriage, and immediately from her fair bosom drew forth a deadly sigh. A sigh which none could have heard unmoved, and which was sufficient at once to have swept off a dozen beaus; so soft, so sweet, so tender, that the insinuating air must have found its subtle way to the heart of our heroe, had it not luckily been driven from his ears by the coarse bubbling of some bottled ale, which at that time he was pouring forth. Many other weapons did she assay; but the god of eating (if there be any such deity, for I do not confidently assert it) preserved his votary; or perhaps it may not be dignus vindice nodus, and the present security of Jones may be accounted for by natural means; for as love frequently preserves from the attacks of hunger, so may hunger possibly, in some cases, defend us against love.\n\n“The fair one, enraged at her frequent disappointments, determined on a short cessation of arms. Which interval she employed in making ready every engine of amorous warfare for the renewing of the attack when dinner should be over.\n\n“No sooner then was the cloth removed than she again began her operations. First, having planted her right eye sidewise against Mr Jones, she shot from its corner a most penetrating glance; which, though great part of its force was spent before it reached our heroe, did not vent itself absolutely without effect. This the fair one perceiving, hastily withdrew her eyes, and levelled them downwards, as if she was concerned for what she had done; though by this means she designed only to draw him from his guard, and indeed to open his eyes, through which she intended to surprize his heart. And now, gently lifting up those two bright orbs which had already begun to make an impression on poor Jones, she discharged a volley of small charms at once from her whole countenance in a smile. Not a smile of mirth, nor of joy; but a smile of affection, which most ladies have always ready at their command, and which serves them to show at once their good-humour, their pretty dimples, and their white teeth.\n\n“This smile our heroe received full in his eyes, and was immediately staggered with its force. He then began to see the designs of the enemy, and indeed to feel their success. A parley now was set on foot between the parties; during which the artful fair so slily and imperceptibly carried on her attack, that she had almost subdued the heart of our heroe before she again repaired to acts of hostility. To confess the truth, I am afraid Mr Jones maintained a kind of Dutch defence, and treacherously delivered up the garrison, without duly weighing his allegiance to the fair Sophia. In short, no sooner had the amorous parley ended and the lady had unmasked the royal battery, by carelessly letting her handkerchief drop from her neck, than the heart of Mr Jones was entirely taken, and the fair conqueror enjoyed the usual fruits of her victory.”\n\nHere the Graces think proper to end their description, and here we think proper to end the chapter.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nA friendly conversation in the kitchen, which had a very common, though not very friendly, conclusion.\n\nWhile our lovers were entertaining themselves in the manner which is partly described in the foregoing chapter, they were likewise furnishing out an entertainment for their good friends in the kitchen. And this in a double sense, by affording them matter for their conversation, and, at the same time, drink to enliven their spirits.\n\nThere were now assembled round the kitchen fire, besides my landlord and landlady, who occasionally went backward and forward, Mr Partridge, the serjeant, and the coachman who drove the young lady and her maid.\n\nPartridge having acquainted the company with what he had learnt from the Man of the Hill concerning the situation in which Mrs Waters had been found by Jones, the serjeant proceeded to that part of her history which was known to him. He said she was the wife of Mr Waters, who was a captain in their regiment, and had often been with him at quarters. “Some folks,” says he, “used indeed to doubt whether they were lawfully married in a church or no. But, for my part, that's no business of mine: I must own, if I was put to my corporal oath, I believe she is little better than one of us; and I fancy the captain may go to heaven when the sun shines upon a rainy day. But if he does, that is neither here nor there; for he won't want company. And the lady, to give the devil his due, is a very good sort of lady, and loves the cloth, and is always desirous to do strict justice to it; for she hath begged off many a poor soldier, and, by her good-will, would never have any of them punished. But yet, to be sure, Ensign Northerton and she were very well acquainted together at our last quarters; that is the very right and truth of the matter. But the captain he knows nothing about it; and as long as there is enough for him too, what does it signify? He loves her not a bit the worse, and I am certain would run any man through the body that was to abuse her; therefore I won't abuse her, for my part. I only repeat what other folks say; and, to be certain, what everybody says, there must be some truth in.”--“Ay, ay, a great deal of truth, I warrant you,” cries Partridge; “Veritas odium parit”--“All a parcel of scandalous stuff,” answered the mistress of the house. “I am sure, now she is drest, she looks like a very good sort of lady, and she behaves herself like one; for she gave me a guinea for the use of my cloaths.”--“A very good lady indeed!” cries the landlord; “and if you had not been a little too hasty, you would not have quarrelled with her as you did at first.”--“You need mention that with my truly!” answered she: “if it had not been for your nonsense, nothing had happened. You must be meddling with what did not belong to you, and throw in your fool's discourse.”--“Well, well,” answered he; “what's past cannot be mended, so there's an end of the matter.”--“Yes,” cries she, “for this once; but will it be mended ever the more hereafter? This is not the first time I have suffered for your numscull's pate. I wish you would always hold your tongue in the house, and meddle only in matters without doors, which concern you. Don't you remember what happened about seven years ago?”--“Nay, my dear,” returned he, “don't rip up old stories. Come, come, all's well, and I am sorry for what I have done.” The landlady was going to reply, but was prevented by the peace-making serjeant, sorely to the displeasure of Partridge, who was a great lover of what is called fun, and a great promoter of those harmless quarrels which tend rather to the production of comical than tragical incidents.\n\nThe serjeant asked Partridge whither he and his master were travelling? “None of your magisters,” answered Partridge; “I am no man's servant, I assure you; for, though I have had misfortunes in the world, I write gentleman after my name; and, as poor and simple as I may appear now, I have taught grammar-school in my time; sed hei mihi! non sum quod fui.”--“No offence, I hope, sir,” said the serjeant; “where, then, if I may venture to be so bold, may you and your friend be travelling?”--“You have now denominated us right,” says Partridge. “Amici sumus. And I promise you my friend is one of the greatest gentlemen in the kingdom” (at which words both landlord and landlady pricked up their ears). “He is the heir of Squire Allworthy.”--“What, the squire who doth so much good all over the country?” cries my landlady. “Even he,” answered Partridge.--“Then I warrant,” says she, “he'll have a swinging great estate hereafter.”--“Most certainly,” answered Partridge.--“Well,” replied the landlady, “I thought the first moment I saw him he looked like a good sort of gentleman; but my husband here, to be sure, is wiser than anybody.”--“I own, my dear,” cries he, “it was a mistake.”--“A mistake, indeed!” answered she; “but when did you ever know me to make such mistakes?”--“But how comes it, sir,” cries the landlord, “that such a great gentleman walks about the country afoot?”--“I don't know,” returned Partridge; “great gentlemen have humours sometimes. He hath now a dozen horses and servants at Gloucester; and nothing would serve him, but last night, it being very hot weather, he must cool himself with a walk to yon high hill, whither I likewise walked with him to bear him company; but if ever you catch me there again: for I was never so frightened in all my life. We met with the strangest man there.”--“I'll be hanged,” cries the landlord, “if it was not the Man of the Hill, as they call him; if indeed he be a man; but I know several people who believe it is the devil that lives there.”--“Nay, nay, like enough,” says Partridge; “and now you put me in the head of it, I verily and sincerely believe it was the devil, though I could not perceive his cloven foot: but perhaps he might have the power given him to hide that, since evil spirits can appear in what shapes they please.”--“And pray, sir,” says the serjeant, “no offence, I hope; but pray what sort of a gentleman is the devil? For I have heard some of our officers say there is no such person; and that it is only a trick of the parsons, to prevent their being broke; for, if it was publickly known that there was no devil, the parsons would be of no more use than we are in time of peace.”--“Those officers,” says Partridge, “are very great scholars, I suppose.”--“Not much of schollards neither,” answered the serjeant; “they have not half your learning, sir, I believe; and, to be sure, I thought there must be a devil, notwithstanding what they said, though one of them was a captain; for methought, thinks I to myself, if there be no devil, how can wicked people be sent to him? and I have read all that upon a book.”--“Some of your officers,” quoth the landlord, “will find there is a devil, to their shame, I believe. I don't question but he'll pay off some old scores upon my account. Here was one quartered upon me half a year, who had the conscience to take up one of my best beds, though he hardly spent a shilling a day in the house, and suffered his men to roast cabbages at the kitchen fire, because I would not give them a dinner on a Sunday. Every good Christian must desire there should be a devil for the punishment of such wretches.”--“Harkee, landlord,” said the serjeant, “don't abuse the cloth, for I won't take it.”--“D--n the cloth!” answered the landlord, “I have suffered enough by them.”--“Bear witness, gentlemen,” says the serjeant, “he curses the king, and that's high treason.”--“I curse the king! you villain,” said the landlord. “Yes, you did,” cries the serjeant; “you cursed the cloth, and that's cursing the king. It's all one and the same; for every man who curses the cloth would curse the king if he durst; so for matter o' that, it's all one and the same thing.”--“Excuse me there, Mr Serjeant,” quoth Partridge, “that's a non sequitur.”--“None of your outlandish linguo,” answered the serjeant, leaping from his seat; “I will not sit still and hear the cloth abused.”--“You mistake me, friend,” cries Partridge. “I did not mean to abuse the cloth; I only said your conclusion was a non sequitur.[*]”--“You are another,” cries the serjeant,” an you come to that. No more a sequitur than yourself. You are a pack of rascals, and I'll prove it; for I will fight the best man of you all for twenty pound.” This challenge effectually silenced Partridge, whose stomach for drubbing did not so soon return after the hearty meal which he had lately been treated with; but the coachman, whose bones were less sore, and whose appetite for fighting was somewhat sharper, did not so easily brook the affront, of which he conceived some part at least fell to his share. He started therefore from his seat, and, advancing to the serjeant, swore he looked on himself to be as good a man as any in the army, and offered to box for a guinea. The military man accepted the combat, but refused the wager; upon which both immediately stript and engaged, till the driver of horses was so well mauled by the leader of men, that he was obliged to exhaust his small remainder of breath in begging for quarter.\n\n[*] This word, which the serjeant unhappily mistook for an affront, is a term in logic, and means that the conclusion does not follow from the premises.\n\nThe young lady was now desirous to depart, and had given orders for her coach to be prepared; but all in vain, for the coachman was disabled from performing his office for that evening. An antient heathen would perhaps have imputed this disability to the god of drink, no less than to the god of war; for, in reality, both the combatants had sacrificed as well to the former deity as to the latter. To speak plainly, they were both dead drunk, nor was Partridge in a much better situation. As for my landlord, drinking was his trade; and the liquor had no more effect on him than it had on any other vessel in his house.\n\nThe mistress of the inn, being summoned to attend Mr Jones and his companion at their tea, gave a full relation of the latter part of the foregoing scene; and at the same time expressed great concern for the young lady, “who,” she said, “was under the utmost uneasiness at being prevented from pursuing her journey. She is a sweet pretty creature,” added she, “and I am certain I have seen her face before. I fancy she is in love, and running away from her friends. Who knows but some young gentleman or other may be expecting her, with a heart as heavy as her own?”\n\nJones fetched a heavy sigh at those words; of which, though Mrs Waters observed it, she took no notice while the landlady continued in the room; but, after the departure of that good woman, she could not forbear giving our heroe certain hints on her suspecting some very dangerous rival in his affections. The aukward behaviour of Mr Jones on this occasion convinced her of the truth, without his giving her a direct answer to any of her questions; but she was not nice enough in her amours to be greatly concerned at the discovery. The beauty of Jones highly charmed her eye; but as she could not see his heart, she gave herself no concern about it. She could feast heartily at the table of love, without reflecting that some other already had been, or hereafter might be, feasted with the same repast. A sentiment which, if it deals but little in refinement, deals, however, much in substance; and is less capricious, and perhaps less ill-natured and selfish, than the desires of those females who can be contented enough to abstain from the possession of their lovers, provided they are sufficiently satisfied that no one else possesses them.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nContaining a fuller account of Mrs Waters, and by what means she came into that distressful situation from which she was rescued by Jones.\n\nThough Nature hath by no means mixed up an equal share either of curiosity or vanity in every human composition, there is perhaps no individual to whom she hath not allotted such a proportion of both as requires much arts, and pains too, to subdue and keep under;--a conquest, however, absolutely necessary to every one who would in any degree deserve the characters of wisdom or good breeding.\n\nAs Jones, therefore, might very justly be called a well-bred man, he had stifled all that curiosity which the extraordinary manner in which he had found Mrs Waters must be supposed to have occasioned. He had, indeed, at first thrown out some few hints to the lady; but, when he perceived her industriously avoiding any explanation, he was contented to remain in ignorance, the rather as he was not without suspicion that there were some circumstances which must have raised her blushes, had she related the whole truth.\n\nNow since it is possible that some of our readers may not so easily acquiesce under the same ignorance, and as we are very desirous to satisfy them all, we have taken uncommon pains to inform ourselves of the real fact, with the relation of which we shall conclude this book.\n\nThis lady, then, had lived some years with one Captain Waters, who was a captain in the same regiment to which Mr Northerton belonged. She past for that gentleman's wife, and went by his name; and yet, as the serjeant said, there were some doubts concerning the reality of their marriage, which we shall not at present take upon us to resolve.\n\nMrs Waters, I am sorry to say it, had for some time contracted an intimacy with the above-mentioned ensign, which did no great credit to her reputation. That she had a remarkable fondness for that young fellow is most certain; but whether she indulged this to any very criminal lengths is not so extremely clear, unless we will suppose that women never grant every favour to a man but one, without granting him that one also.\n\nThe division of the regiment to which Captain Waters belonged had two days preceded the march of that company to which Mr Northerton was the ensign; so that the former had reached Worcester the very day after the unfortunate re-encounter between Jones and Northerton which we have before recorded.\n\nNow, it had been agreed between Mrs Waters and the captain that she would accompany him in his march as far as Worcester, where they were to take their leave of each other, and she was thence to return to Bath, where she was to stay till the end of the winter's campaign against the rebels.\n\nWith this agreement Mr Northerton was made acquainted. To say the truth, the lady had made him an assignation at this very place, and promised to stay at Worcester till his division came thither; with what view, and for what purpose, must be left to the reader's divination; for, though we are obliged to relate facts, we are not obliged to do a violence to our nature by any comments to the disadvantage of the loveliest part of the creation.\n\nNortherton no sooner obtained a release from his captivity, as we have seen, than he hasted away to overtake Mrs Waters; which, as he was a very active nimble fellow, he did at the last-mentioned city, some few hours after Captain Waters had left her. At his first arrival he made no scruple of acquainting her with the unfortunate accident; which he made appear very unfortunate indeed, for he totally extracted every particle of what could be called fault, at least in a court of honour, though he left some circumstances which might be questionable in a court of law.\n\nWomen, to their glory be it spoken, are more generally capable of that violent and apparently disinterested passion of love, which seeks only the good of its object, than men. Mrs Waters, therefore, was no sooner apprized of the danger to which her lover was exposed, than she lost every consideration besides that of his safety; and this being a matter equally agreeable to the gentleman, it became the immediate subject of debate between them.\n\nAfter much consultation on this matter, it was at length agreed that the ensign should go across the country to Hereford, whence he might find some conveyance to one of the sea-ports in Wales, and thence might make his escape abroad. In all which expedition Mrs Waters declared she would bear him company; and for which she was able to furnish him with money, a very material article to Mr Northerton, she having then in her pocket three bank-notes to the amount of £90, besides some cash, and a diamond ring of pretty considerable value on her finger. All which she, with the utmost confidence, revealed to this wicked man, little suspecting she should by these means inspire him with a design of robbing her. Now, as they must, by taking horses from Worcester, have furnished any pursuers with the means of hereafter discovering their route, the ensign proposed, and the lady presently agreed, to make their first stage on foot; for which purpose the hardness of the frost was very seasonable.\n\nThe main part of the lady's baggage was already at Bath, and she had nothing with her at present besides a very small quantity of linen, which the gallant undertook to carry in his own pockets. All things, therefore, being settled in the evening, they arose early the next morning, and at five o'clock departed from Worcester, it being then above two hours before day, but the moon, which was then at the full, gave them all the light she was capable of affording.\n\nMrs Waters was not of that delicate race of women who are obliged to the invention of vehicles for the capacity of removing themselves from one place to another, and with whom consequently a coach is reckoned among the necessaries of life. Her limbs were indeed full of strength and agility, and, as her mind was no less animated with spirit, she was perfectly able to keep pace with her nimble lover.\n\nHaving travelled on for some miles in a high road, which Northerton said he was informed led to Hereford, they came at the break of day to the side of a large wood, where he suddenly stopped, and, affecting to meditate a moment with himself, expressed some apprehensions from travelling any longer in so public a way. Upon which he easily persuaded his fair companion to strike with him into a path which seemed to lead directly through the wood, and which at length brought them both to the bottom of Mazard Hill.\n\nWhether the execrable scheme which he now attempted to execute was the effect of previous deliberation, or whether it now first came into his head, I cannot determine. But being arrived in this lonely place, where it was very improbable he should meet with any interruption, he suddenly slipped his garter from his leg, and, laying violent hands on the poor woman, endeavoured to perpetrate that dreadful and detestable fact which we have before commemorated, and which the providential appearance of Jones did so fortunately prevent.\n\nHappy was it for Mrs Waters that she was not of the weakest order of females; for no sooner did she perceive, by his tying a knot in his garter, and by his declarations, what his hellish intentions were, than she stood stoutly to her defence, and so strongly struggled with her enemy, screaming all the while for assistance, that she delayed the execution of the villain's purpose several minutes, by which means Mr Jones came to her relief at that very instant when her strength failed and she was totally overpowered, and delivered her from the ruffian's hands, with no other loss than that of her cloaths, which were torn from her back, and of the diamond ring, which during the contention either dropped from her finger, or was wrenched from it by Northerton.\n\nThus, reader, we have given thee the fruits of a very painful enquiry which for thy satisfaction we have made into this matter. And here we have opened to thee a scene of folly as well as villany, which we could scarce have believed a human creature capable of being guilty of, had we not remembered that this fellow was at that time firmly persuaded that he had already committed a murder, and had forfeited his life to the law. As he concluded therefore that his only safety lay in flight, he thought the possessing himself of this poor woman's money and ring would make him amends for the additional burthen he was to lay on his conscience.\n\nAnd here, reader, we must strictly caution thee that thou dost not take any occasion, from the misbehaviour of such a wretch as this, to reflect on so worthy and honourable a body of men as are the officers of our army in general. Thou wilt be pleased to consider that this fellow, as we have already informed thee, had neither the birth nor education of a gentleman, nor was a proper person to be enrolled among the number of such. If, therefore, his baseness can justly reflect on any besides himself, it must be only on those who gave him his commission.\n\n# BOOK X.\n\nIN WHICH THE HISTORY GOES FORWARD ABOUT TWELVE HOURS. Chapter i.\nContaining instructions very necessary to be perused by modern critics.\n\nReader, it is impossible we should know what sort of person thou wilt be; for, perhaps, thou may'st be as learned in human nature as Shakespear himself was, and, perhaps, thou may'st be no wiser than some of his editors. Now, lest this latter should be the case, we think proper, before we go any farther together, to give thee a few wholesome admonitions; that thou may'st not as grossly misunderstand and misrepresent us, as some of the said editors have misunderstood and misrepresented their author.\nFirst, then, we warn thee not too hastily to condemn any of the incidents in this our history as impertinent and foreign to our main design, because thou dost not immediately conceive in what manner such incident may conduce to that design. This work may, indeed, be considered as a great creation of our own; and for a little reptile of a critic to presume to find fault with any of its parts, without knowing the manner in which the whole is connected, and before he comes to the final catastrophe, is a most presumptuous absurdity. The allusion and metaphor we have here made use of, we must acknowledge to be infinitely too great for our occasion; but there is, indeed, no other, which is at all adequate to express the difference between an author of the first rate and a critic of the lowest.\n\nAnother caution we would give thee, my good reptile, is, that thou dost not find out too near a resemblance between certain characters here introduced; as, for instance, between the landlady who appears in the seventh book and her in the ninth. Thou art to know, friend, that there are certain characteristics in which most individuals of every profession and occupation agree. To be able to preserve these characteristics, and at the same time to diversify their operations, is one talent of a good writer. Again, to mark the nice distinction between two persons actuated by the same vice or folly is another; and, as this last talent is found in very few writers, so is the true discernment of it found in as few readers; though, I believe, the observation of this forms a very principal pleasure in those who are capable of the discovery; every person, for instance, can distinguish between Sir Epicure Mammon and Sir Fopling Flutter; but to note the difference between Sir Fopling Flutter and Sir Courtly Nice requires a more exquisite judgment: for want of which, vulgar spectators of plays very often do great injustice in the theatre; where I have sometimes known a poet in danger of being convicted as a thief, upon much worse evidence than the resemblance of hands hath been held to be in the law. In reality, I apprehend every amorous widow on the stage would run the hazard of being condemned as a servile imitation of Dido, but that happily very few of our play-house critics understand enough of Latin to read Virgil.\n\nIn the next place, we must admonish thee, my worthy friend (for, perhaps, thy heart may be better than thy head), not to condemn a character as a bad one, because it is not perfectly a good one. If thou dost delight in these models of perfection, there are books enow written to gratify thy taste; but, as we have not, in the course of our conversation, ever happened to meet with any such person, we have not chosen to introduce any such here. To say the truth, I a little question whether mere man ever arrived at this consummate degree of excellence, as well as whether there hath ever existed a monster bad enough to verify that\n\n----nulla virtute redemptum A vitiis----[*]\n\n[*] Whose vices are not allayed with a single virtue\n\nin Juvenal; nor do I, indeed, conceive the good purposes served by inserting characters of such angelic perfection, or such diabolical depravity, in any work of invention; since, from contemplating either, the mind of man is more likely to be overwhelmed with sorrow and shame than to draw any good uses from such patterns; for in the former instance he may be both concerned and ashamed to see a pattern of excellence in his nature, which he may reasonably despair of ever arriving at; and in contemplating the latter he may be no less affected with those uneasy sensations, at seeing the nature of which he is a partaker degraded into so odious and detestable a creature.\n\nIn fact, if there be enough of goodness in a character to engage the admiration and affection of a well-disposed mind, though there should appear some of those little blemishes quas humana parum cavit natura, they will raise our compassion rather than our abhorrence. Indeed, nothing can be of more moral use than the imperfections which are seen in examples of this kind; since such form a kind of surprize, more apt to affect and dwell upon our minds than the faults of very vicious and wicked persons. The foibles and vices of men, in whom there is great mixture of good, become more glaring objects from the virtues which contrast them and shew their deformity; and when we find such vices attended with their evil consequence to our favourite characters, we are not only taught to shun them for our own sake, but to hate them for the mischiefs they have already brought on those we love.\n\nAnd now, my friend, having given you these few admonitions, we will, if you please, once more set forward with our history.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nContaining the arrival of an Irish gentleman, with very extraordinary adventures which ensued at the inn.\n\nNow the little trembling hare, which the dread of all her numerous enemies, and chiefly of that cunning, cruel, carnivorous animal, man, had confined all the day to her lurking-place, sports wantonly o'er the lawns; now on some hollow tree the owl, shrill chorister of the night, hoots forth notes which might charm the ears of some modern connoisseurs in music; now, in the imagination of the half-drunk clown, as he staggers through the churchyard, or rather charnelyard, to his home, fear paints the bloody hobgoblin; now thieves and ruffians are awake, and honest watchmen fast asleep; in plain English, it was now midnight; and the company at the inn, as well those who have been already mentioned in this history, as some others who arrived in the evening, were all in bed. Only Susan Chambermaid was now stirring, she being obliged to wash the kitchen before she retired to the arms of the fond expecting hostler.\n\nIn this posture were affairs at the inn when a gentleman arrived there post. He immediately alighted from his horse, and, coming up to Susan, enquired of her, in a very abrupt and confused manner, being almost out of breath with eagerness, Whether there was any lady in the house? The hour of night, and the behaviour of the man, who stared very wildly all the time, a little surprized Susan, so that she hesitated before she made any answer; upon which the gentleman, with redoubled eagerness, begged her to give him a true information, saying, He had lost his wife, and was come in pursuit of her. “Upon my shoul,” cries he, “I have been near catching her already in two or three places, if I had not found her gone just as I came up with her. If she be in the house, do carry me up in the dark and show her to me; and if she be gone away before me, do tell me which way I shall go after her to meet her, and, upon my shoul, I will make you the richest poor woman in the nation.” He then pulled out a handful of guineas, a sight which would have bribed persons of much greater consequence than this poor wench to much worse purposes.\n\nSusan, from the account she had received of Mrs Waters, made not the least doubt but that she was the very identical stray whom the right owner pursued. As she concluded, therefore, with great appearance of reason, that she never could get money in an honester way than by restoring a wife to her husband, she made no scruple of assuring the gentleman that the lady he wanted was then in the house; and was presently afterwards prevailed upon (by very liberal promises, and some earnest paid into her hands) to conduct him to the bedchamber of Mrs Waters.\n\nIt hath been a custom long established in the polite world, and that upon very solid and substantial reasons, that a husband shall never enter his wife's apartment without first knocking at the door. The many excellent uses of this custom need scarce be hinted to a reader who hath any knowledge of the world; for by this means the lady hath time to adjust herself, or to remove any disagreeable object out of the way; for there are some situations in which nice and delicate women would not be discovered by their husbands.\n\nTo say the truth, there are several ceremonies instituted among the polished part of mankind, which, though they may, to coarser judgments, appear as matters of mere form, are found to have much of substance in them, by the more discerning; and lucky would it have been had the custom above mentioned been observed by our gentleman in the present instance. Knock, indeed, he did at the door, but not with one of those gentle raps which is usual on such occasions. On the contrary, when he found the door locked, he flew at it with such violence, that the lock immediately gave way, the door burst open, and he fell headlong into the room.\n\nHe had no sooner recovered his legs than forth from the bed, upon his legs likewise, appeared--with shame and sorrow are we obliged to proceed--our heroe himself, who, with a menacing voice, demanded of the gentleman who he was, and what he meant by daring to burst open his chamber in that outrageous manner.\n\nThe gentleman at first thought he had committed a mistake, and was going to ask pardon and retreat, when, on a sudden, as the moon shone very bright, he cast his eyes on stays, gowns, petticoats, caps, ribbons, stockings, garters, shoes, clogs, &c., all which lay in a disordered manner on the floor. All these, operating on the natural jealousy of his temper, so enraged him, that he lost all power of speech; and, without returning any answer to Jones, he endeavoured to approach the bed.\n\nJones immediately interposing, a fierce contention arose, which soon proceeded to blows on both sides. And now Mrs Waters (for we must confess she was in the same bed), being, I suppose, awakened from her sleep, and seeing two men fighting in her bedchamber, began to scream in the most violent manner, crying out murder! robbery! and more frequently rape! which last, some, perhaps, may wonder she should mention, who do not consider that these words of exclamation are used by ladies in a fright, as fa, la, la, ra, da, &c., are in music, only as the vehicles of sound, and without any fixed ideas.\n\nNext to the lady's chamber was deposited the body of an Irish gentleman who arrived too late at the inn to have been mentioned before. This gentleman was one of those whom the Irish call a calabalaro, or cavalier. He was a younger brother of a good family, and, having no fortune at home, was obliged to look abroad in order to get one; for which purpose he was proceeding to the Bath, to try his luck with cards and the women.\n\nThis young fellow lay in bed reading one of Mrs Behn's novels; for he had been instructed by a friend that he would find no more effectual method of recommending himself to the ladies than the improving his understanding, and filling his mind with good literature. He no sooner, therefore, heard the violent uproar in the next room, than he leapt from his bolster, and, taking his sword in one hand, and the candle which burnt by him in the other, he went directly to Mrs Waters's chamber.\n\nIf the sight of another man in his shirt at first added some shock to the decency of the lady, it made her presently amends by considerably abating her fears; for no sooner had the calabalaro entered the room than he cried out, “Mr Fitzpatrick, what the devil is the maning of this?” Upon which the other immediately answered, “O, Mr Maclachlan! I am rejoiced you are here.--This villain hath debauched my wife, and is got into bed with her.”--“What wife?” cries Maclachlan; “do not I know Mrs Fitzpatrick very well, and don't I see that the lady, whom the gentleman who stands here in his shirt is lying in bed with, is none of her?”\n\nFitzpatrick, now perceiving, as well by the glimpse he had of the lady, as by her voice, which might have been distinguished at a greater distance than he now stood from her, that he had made a very unfortunate mistake, began to ask many pardons of the lady; and then, turning to Jones, he said, “I would have you take notice I do not ask your pardon, for you have bate me; for which I am resolved to have your blood in the morning.”\n\nJones treated this menace with much contempt; and Mr Maclachlan answered, “Indeed, Mr Fitzpatrick, you may be ashamed of your own self, to disturb people at this time of night; if all the people in the inn were not asleep, you would have awakened them as you have me. The gentleman has served you very rightly. Upon my conscience, though I have no wife, if you had treated her so, I would have cut your throat.”\n\nJones was so confounded with his fears for his lady's reputation, that he knew neither what to say or do; but the invention of women is, as hath been observed, much readier than that of men. She recollected that there was a communication between her chamber and that of Mr Jones; relying, therefore, on his honour and her own assurance, she answered, “I know not what you mean, villains! I am wife to none of you. Help! Rape! Murder! Rape!”--And now, the landlady coming into the room, Mrs Waters fell upon her with the utmost virulence, saying, “She thought herself in a sober inn, and not in a bawdy-house; but that a set of villains had broke into her room, with an intent upon her honour, if not upon her life; and both, she said, were equally dear to her.”\n\nThe landlady now began to roar as loudly as the poor woman in bed had done before. She cried, “She was undone, and that the reputation of her house, which was never blown upon before, was utterly destroyed.” Then, turning to the men, she cried, “What, in the devil's name, is the reason of all this disturbance in the lady's room?” Fitzpatrick, hanging down his head, repeated, “That he had committed a mistake, for which he heartily asked pardon,” and then retired with his countryman. Jones, who was too ingenious to have missed the hint given him by his fair one, boldly asserted, “That he had run to her assistance upon hearing the door broke open, with what design he could not conceive, unless of robbing the lady; which, if they intended, he said, he had the good fortune to prevent.” “I never had a robbery committed in my house since I have kept it,” cries the landlady; “I would have you to know, sir, I harbour no highwaymen here; I scorn the word, thof I say it. None but honest, good gentlefolks, are welcome to my house; and, I thank good luck, I have always had enow of such customers; indeed as many as I could entertain. Here hath been my lord--,” and then she repeated over a catalogue of names and titles, many of which we might, perhaps, be guilty of a breach of privilege by inserting.\n\nJones, after much patience, at length interrupted her, by making an apology to Mrs Waters, for having appeared before her in his shirt, assuring her “That nothing but a concern for her safety could have prevailed on him to do it.” The reader may inform himself of her answer, and, indeed, of her whole behaviour to the end of the scene, by considering the situation which she affected, it being that of a modest lady, who was awakened out of her sleep by three strange men in her chamber. This was the part which she undertook to perform; and, indeed, she executed it so well, that none of our theatrical actresses could exceed her, in any of their performances, either on or off the stage.\n\nAnd hence, I think, we may very fairly draw an argument, to prove how extremely natural virtue is to the fair sex; for, though there is not, perhaps, one in ten thousand who is capable of making a good actress, and even among these we rarely see two who are equally able to personate the same character, yet this of virtue they can all admirably well put on; and as well those individuals who have it not, as those who possess it, can all act it to the utmost degree of perfection.\n\nWhen the men were all departed, Mrs Waters, recovering from her fear, recovered likewise from her anger, and spoke in much gentler accents to the landlady, who did not so readily quit her concern for the reputation of the house, in favour of which she began again to number the many great persons who had slept under her roof; but the lady stopt her short, and having absolutely acquitted her of having had any share in the past disturbance, begged to be left to her repose, which, she said, she hoped to enjoy unmolested during the remainder of the night. Upon which the landlady, after much civility and many courtsies, took her leave.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nA dialogue between the landlady and Susan the chamber-maid, proper to be read by all inn-keepers and their servants; with the arrival, and affable behaviour of a beautiful young lady; which may teach persons of condition how they may acquire the love of the whole world.\n\nThe landlady, remembering that Susan had been the only person out of bed when the door was burst open, resorted presently to her, to enquire into the first occasion of the disturbance, as well as who the strange gentleman was, and when and how he arrived.\n\nSusan related the whole story which the reader knows already, varying the truth only in some circumstances, as she saw convenient, and totally concealing the money which she had received. But whereas her mistress had, in the preface to her enquiry, spoken much in compassion for the fright which the lady had been in concerning any intended depredations on her virtue, Susan could not help endeavouring to quiet the concern which her mistress seemed to be under on that account, by swearing heartily she saw Jones leap out from her bed.\n\nThe landlady fell into a violent rage at these words. “A likely story, truly,” cried she, “that a woman should cry out, and endeavour to expose herself, if that was the case! I desire to know what better proof any lady can give of her virtue than her crying out, which, I believe, twenty people can witness for her she did? I beg, madam, you would spread no such scandal of any of my guests; for it will not only reflect on them, but upon the house; and I am sure no vagabonds, nor wicked beggarly people, come here.”\n\n“Well,” says Susan, “then I must not believe my own eyes.” “No, indeed, must you not always,” answered her mistress; “I would not have believed my own eyes against such good gentlefolks. I have not had a better supper ordered this half-year than they ordered last night; and so easy and good-humoured were they, that they found no fault with my Worcestershire perry, which I sold them for champagne; and to be sure it is as well tasted and as wholesome as the best champagne in the kingdom, otherwise I would scorn to give it 'em; and they drank me two bottles. No, no, I will never believe any harm of such sober good sort of people.”\n\nSusan being thus silenced, her mistress proceeded to other matters. “And so you tell me,” continued she, “that the strange gentleman came post, and there is a footman without with the horses; why, then, he is certainly some of your great gentlefolks too. Why did not you ask him whether he'd have any supper? I think he is in the other gentleman's room; go up and ask whether he called. Perhaps he'll order something when he finds anybody stirring in the house to dress it. Now don't commit any of your usual blunders, by telling him the fire's out, and the fowls alive. And if he should order mutton, don't blab out that we have none. The butcher, I know, killed a sheep just before I went to bed, and he never refuses to cut it up warm when I desire it. Go, remember there's all sorts of mutton and fowls; go, open the door with, Gentlemen, d'ye call? and if they say nothing, ask what his honour will be pleased to have for supper? Don't forget his honour. Go; if you don't mind all these matters better, you'll never come to anything.”\n\nSusan departed, and soon returned with an account that the two gentlemen were got both into the same bed. “Two gentlemen,” says the landlady, “in the same bed! that's impossible; they are two arrant scrubs, I warrant them; and I believe young Squire Allworthy guessed right, that the fellow intended to rob her ladyship; for, if he had broke open the lady's door with any of the wicked designs of a gentleman, he would never have sneaked away to another room to save the expense of a supper and a bed to himself. They are certainly thieves, and their searching after a wife is nothing but a pretence.”\n\nIn these censures my landlady did Mr Fitzpatrick great injustice; for he was really born a gentleman, though not worth a groat; and though, perhaps, he had some few blemishes in his heart as well as in his head, yet being a sneaking or a niggardly fellow was not one of them. In reality, he was so generous a man, that, whereas he had received a very handsome fortune with his wife, he had now spent every penny of it, except some little pittance which was settled upon her; and, in order to possess himself of this, he had used her with such cruelty, that, together with his jealousy, which was of the bitterest kind, it had forced the poor woman to run away from him.\n\nThis gentleman then being well tired with his long journey from Chester in one day, with which, and some good dry blows he had received in the scuffle, his bones were so sore, that, added to the soreness of his mind, it had quite deprived him of any appetite for eating. And being now so violently disappointed in the woman whom, at the maid's instance, he had mistaken for his wife, it never once entered into his head that she might nevertheless be in the house, though he had erred in the first person he had attacked. He therefore yielded to the dissuasions of his friend from searching any farther after her that night, and accepted the kind offer of part of his bed.\n\nThe footman and post-boy were in a different disposition. They were more ready to order than the landlady was to provide; however, after being pretty well satisfied by them of the real truth of the case, and that Mr Fitzpatrick was no thief, she was at length prevailed on to set some cold meat before them, which they were devouring with great greediness, when Partridge came into the kitchen. He had been first awaked by the hurry which we have before seen; and while he was endeavouring to compose himself again on his pillow, a screech-owl had given him such a serenade at his window, that he leapt in a most horrible affright from his bed, and, huddling on his cloaths with great expedition, ran down to the protection of the company, whom he heard talking below in the kitchen.\n\nHis arrival detained my landlady from returning to her rest; for she was just about to leave the other two guests to the care of Susan; but the friend of young Squire Allworthy was not to be so neglected, especially as he called for a pint of wine to be mulled. She immediately obeyed, by putting the same quantity of perry to the fire; for this readily answered to the name of every kind of wine.\n\nThe Irish footman was retired to bed, and the post-boy was going to follow; but Partridge invited him to stay and partake of his wine, which the lad very thankfully accepted. The schoolmaster was indeed afraid to return to bed by himself; and as he did not know how soon he might lose the company of my landlady, he was resolved to secure that of the boy, in whose presence he apprehended no danger from the devil or any of his adherents.\n\nAnd now arrived another post-boy at the gate; upon which Susan, being ordered out, returned, introducing two young women in riding habits, one of which was so very richly laced, that Partridge and the post-boy instantly started from their chairs, and my landlady fell to her courtsies, and her ladyships, with great eagerness.\n\nThe lady in the rich habit said, with a smile of great condescension, “If you will give me leave, madam, I will warm myself a few minutes at your kitchen fire, for it is really very cold; but I must insist on disturbing no one from his seat.” This was spoken on account of Partridge, who had retreated to the other end of the room, struck with the utmost awe and astonishment at the splendor of the lady's dress. Indeed, she had a much better title to respect than this; for she was one of the most beautiful creatures in the world.\n\nThe lady earnestly desired Partridge to return to his seat; but could not prevail. She then pulled off her gloves, and displayed to the fire two hands, which had every property of snow in them, except that of melting. Her companion, who was indeed her maid, likewise pulled off her gloves, and discovered what bore an exact resemblance, in cold and colour, to a piece of frozen beef.\n\n“I wish, madam,” quoth the latter, “your ladyship would not think of going any farther to-night. I am terribly afraid your ladyship will not be able to bear the fatigue.”\n\n“Why sure,” cries the landlady, “her ladyship's honour can never intend it. O, bless me! farther to-night, indeed! let me beseech your ladyship not to think on't----But, to be sure, your ladyship can't. What will your honour be pleased to have for supper? I have mutton of all kinds, and some nice chicken.”\n\n“I think, madam,” said the lady, “it would be rather breakfast than supper; but I can't eat anything; and, if I stay, shall only lie down for an hour or two. However, if you please, madam, you may get me a little sack whey, made very small and thin.”\n\n“Yes, madam,” cries the mistress of the house, “I have some excellent white wine.”--“You have no sack, then?” says the lady. “Yes, an't please your honour, I have; I may challenge the country for that--but let me beg your ladyship to eat something.”\n\n“Upon my word, I can't eat a morsel,” answered the lady; “and I shall be much obliged to you if you will please to get my apartment ready as soon as possible; for I am resolved to be on horseback again in three hours.”\n\n“Why, Susan,” cries the landlady, “is there a fire lit yet in the Wild-goose? I am sorry, madam, all my best rooms are full. Several people of the first quality are now in bed. Here's a great young squire, and many other great gentlefolks of quality.” Susan answered, “That the Irish gentlemen were got into the Wild-goose.”\n\n“Was ever anything like it?” says the mistress; “why the devil would you not keep some of the best rooms for the quality, when you know scarce a day passes without some calling here?----If they be gentlemen, I am certain, when they know it is for her ladyship, they will get up again.”\n\n“Not upon my account,” says the lady; “I will have no person disturbed for me. If you have a room that is commonly decent, it will serve me very well, though it be never so plain. I beg, madam, you will not give yourself so much trouble on my account.” “O, madam!” cries the other, “I have several very good rooms for that matter, but none good enough for your honour's ladyship. However, as you are so condescending to take up with the best I have, do, Susan, get a fire in the Rose this minute. Will your ladyship be pleased to go up now, or stay till the fire is lighted?” “I think I have sufficiently warmed myself,” answered the lady; “so, if you please, I will go now; I am afraid I have kept people, and particularly that gentleman (meaning Partridge), too long in the cold already. Indeed, I cannot bear to think of keeping any person from the fire this dreadful weather.”--She then departed with her maid, the landlady marching with two lighted candles before her.\n\nWhen that good woman returned, the conversation in the kitchen was all upon the charms of the young lady. There is indeed in perfect beauty a power which none almost can withstand; for my landlady, though she was not pleased at the negative given to the supper, declared she had never seen so lovely a creature. Partridge ran out into the most extravagant encomiums on her face, though he could not refrain from paying some compliments to the gold lace on her habit; the post-boy sung forth the praises of her goodness, which were likewise echoed by the other post-boy, who was now come in. “She's a true good lady, I warrant her,” says he; “for she hath mercy upon dumb creatures; for she asked me every now and tan upon the journey, if I did not think she should hurt the horses by riding too fast? and when she came in she charged me to give them as much corn as ever they would eat.”\n\nSuch charms are there in affability, and so sure is it to attract the praises of all kinds of people. It may indeed be compared to the celebrated Mrs Hussey.[*] It is equally sure to set off every female perfection to the highest advantage, and to palliate and conceal every defect. A short reflection, which we could not forbear making in this place, where my reader hath seen the loveliness of an affable deportment; and truth will now oblige us to contrast it, by showing the reverse.\n\n[*] A celebrated mantua-maker in the Strand, famous for setting off the shapes of women.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nContaining infallible nostrums for procuring universal disesteem and hatred.\n\nThe lady had no sooner laid herself on her pillow than the waiting-woman returned to the kitchen to regale with some of those dainties which her mistress had refused.\n\nThe company, at her entrance, shewed her the same respect which they had before paid to her mistress, by rising; but she forgot to imitate her, by desiring them to sit down again. Indeed, it was scarce possible they should have done so, for she placed her chair in such a posture as to occupy almost the whole fire. She then ordered a chicken to be broiled that instant, declaring, if it was not ready in a quarter of an hour, she would not stay for it. Now, though the said chicken was then at roost in the stable, and required the several ceremonies of catching, killing, and picking, before it was brought to the gridiron, my landlady would nevertheless have undertaken to do all within the time; but the guest, being unfortunately admitted behind the scenes, must have been witness to the fourberie; the poor woman was therefore obliged to confess that she had none in the house; “but, madam,” said she, “I can get any kind of mutton in an instant from the butcher's.”\n\n“Do you think, then,” answered the waiting-gentlewoman, “that I have the stomach of a horse, to eat mutton at this time of night? Sure you people that keep inns imagine your betters are like yourselves. Indeed, I expected to get nothing at this wretched place. I wonder my lady would stop at it. I suppose none but tradesmen and grasiers ever call here.” The landlady fired at this indignity offered to her house; however, she suppressed her temper, and contented herself with saying, “Very good quality frequented it, she thanked heaven!” “Don't tell me,” cries the other, “of quality! I believe I know more of people of quality than such as you.--But, prithee, without troubling me with any of your impertinence, do tell me what I can have for supper; for, though I cannot eat horse-flesh, I am really hungry.” “Why, truly, madam,” answered the landlady, “you could not take me again at such a disadvantage; for I must confess I have nothing in the house, unless a cold piece of beef, which indeed a gentleman's footman and the post-boy have almost cleared to the bone.” “Woman,” said Mrs Abigail (so for shortness we will call her), “I entreat you not to make me sick. If I had fasted a month, I could not eat what had been touched by the fingers of such fellows. Is there nothing neat or decent to be had in this horrid place?” “What think you of some eggs and bacon, madam?” said the landlady. “Are your eggs new laid? are you certain they were laid to-day? and let me have the bacon cut very nice and thin; for I can't endure anything that's gross.--Prithee try if you can do a little tolerably for once, and don't think you have a farmer's wife, or some of those creatures, in the house.”--The landlady began then to handle her knife; but the other stopt her, saying, “Good woman, I must insist upon your first washing your hands; for I am extremely nice, and have been always used from my cradle to have everything in the most elegant manner.”\n\nThe landlady, who governed herself with much difficulty, began now the necessary preparations; for as to Susan, she was utterly rejected, and with such disdain, that the poor wench was as hard put to it to restrain her hands from violence as her mistress had been to hold her tongue. This indeed Susan did not entirely; for, though she literally kept it within her teeth, yet there it muttered many “marry-come-ups, as good flesh and blood as yourself;” with other such indignant phrases.\n\nWhile the supper was preparing, Mrs Abigail began to lament she had not ordered a fire in the parlour; but, she said, that was now too late. “However,” said she, “I have novelty to recommend a kitchen; for I do not believe I ever eat in one before.” Then, turning to the post-boys, she asked them, “Why they were not in the stable with their horses? If I must eat my hard fare here, madam,” cries she to the landlady, “I beg the kitchen may be kept clear, that I may not be surrounded with all the blackguards in town: as for you, sir,” says she to Partridge, “you look somewhat like a gentleman, and may sit still if you please; I don't desire to disturb anybody but mob.”\n\n“Yes, yes, madam,” cries Partridge, “I am a gentleman, I do assure you, and I am not so easily to be disturbed. Non semper vox casualis est verbo nominativus.” This Latin she took to be some affront, and answered, “You may be a gentleman, sir; but you don't show yourself as one to talk Latin to a woman.” Partridge made a gentle reply, and concluded with more Latin; upon which she tossed up her nose, and contented herself by abusing him with the name of a great scholar.\n\nThe supper being now on the table, Mrs Abigail eat very heartily for so delicate a person; and, while a second course of the same was by her order preparing, she said, “And so, madam, you tell me your house is frequented by people of great quality?”\n\nThe landlady answered in the affirmative, saying, “There were a great many very good quality and gentlefolks in it now. There's young Squire Allworthy, as that gentleman there knows.”\n\n“And pray who is this young gentleman of quality, this young Squire Allworthy?” said Abigail.\n\n“Who should he be,” answered Partridge, “but the son and heir of the great Squire Allworthy, of Somersetshire!”\n\n“Upon my word,” said she, “you tell me strange news; for I know Mr Allworthy of Somersetshire very well, and I know he hath no son alive.”\n\nThe landlady pricked up her ears at this, and Partridge looked a little confounded. However, after a short hesitation, he answered, “Indeed, madam, it is true, everybody doth not know him to be Squire Allworthy's son; for he was never married to his mother; but his son he certainly is, and will be his heir too, as certainly as his name is Jones.” At that word, Abigail let drop the bacon which she was conveying to her mouth, and cried out, “You surprize me, sir! Is it possible Mr Jones should be now in the house?” “Quare non?” answered Partridge, “it is possible, and it is certain.”\n\nAbigail now made haste to finish the remainder of her meal, and then repaired back to her mistress, when the conversation passed which may be read in the next chapter.\n\nShowing who the amiable lady, and her unamiable maid, were.\n\nAs in the month of June, the damask rose, which chance hath planted among the lilies, with their candid hue mixes his vermilion; or as some playsome heifer in the pleasant month of May diffuses her odoriferous breath over the flowery meadows; or as, in the blooming month of April, the gentle, constant dove, perched on some fair bough, sits meditating on her mate; so, looking a hundred charms and breathing as many sweets, her thoughts being fixed on her Tommy, with a heart as good and innocent as her face was beautiful, Sophia (for it was she herself) lay reclining her lovely head on her hand, when her maid entered the room, and, running directly to the bed, cried, “Madam--madam--who doth your ladyship think is in the house?” Sophia, starting up, cried, “I hope my father hath not overtaken us.” “No, madam, it is one worth a hundred fathers; Mr Jones himself is here at this very instant.” “Mr Jones!” says Sophia, “it is impossible! I cannot be so fortunate.” Her maid averred the fact, and was presently detached by her mistress to order him to be called; for she said she was resolved to see him immediately.\n\nMrs Honour had no sooner left the kitchen in the manner we have before seen than the landlady fell severely upon her. The poor woman had indeed been loading her heart with foul language for some time, and now it scoured out of her mouth, as filth doth from a mud-cart, when the board which confines it is removed. Partridge likewise shovelled in his share of calumny, and (what may surprize the reader) not only bespattered the maid, but attempted to sully the lily-white character of Sophia herself. “Never a barrel the better herring,” cries he, “Noscitur a socio, is a true saying. It must be confessed, indeed, that the lady in the fine garments is the civiller of the two; but I warrant neither of them are a bit better than they should be. A couple of Bath trulls, I'll answer for them; your quality don't ride about at this time o' night without servants.” “Sbodlikins, and that's true,” cries the landlady, “you have certainly hit upon the very matter; for quality don't come into a house without bespeaking a supper, whether they eat or no.”\n\nWhile they were thus discoursing, Mrs Honour returned and discharged her commission, by bidding the landlady immediately wake Mr Jones, and tell him a lady wanted to speak with him. The landlady referred her to Partridge, saying, “he was the squire's friend: but, for her part, she never called men-folks, especially gentlemen,” and then walked sullenly out of the kitchen. Honour applied herself to Partridge; but he refused, “for my friend,” cries he, “went to bed very late, and he would be very angry to be disturbed so soon.” Mrs Honour insisted still to have him called, saying, “she was sure, instead of being angry, that he would be to the highest degree delighted when he knew the occasion.” “Another time, perhaps, he might,” cries Partridge; “but non omnia possumus omnes. One woman is enough at once for a reasonable man.” “What do you mean by one woman, fellow?” cries Honour. “None of your fellow,” answered Partridge. He then proceeded to inform her plainly that Jones was in bed with a wench, and made use of an expression too indelicate to be here inserted; which so enraged Mrs Honour, that she called him jackanapes, and returned in a violent hurry to her mistress, whom she acquainted with the success of her errand, and with the account she had received; which, if possible, she exaggerated, being as angry with Jones as if he had pronounced all the words that came from the mouth of Partridge. She discharged a torrent of abuse on the master, and advised her mistress to quit all thoughts of a man who had never shown himself deserving of her. She then ripped up the story of Molly Seagrim, and gave the most malicious turn to his formerly quitting Sophia herself; which, I must confess, the present incident not a little countenanced.\n\nThe spirits of Sophia were too much dissipated by concern to enable her to stop the torrent of her maid. At last, however, she interrupted her, saying, “I never can believe this; some villain hath belied him. You say you had it from his friend; but surely it is not the office of a friend to betray such secrets.” “I suppose,” cries Honour, “the fellow is his pimp; for I never saw so ill-looked a villain. Besides, such profligate rakes as Mr Jones are never ashamed of these matters.”\n\nTo say the truth, this behaviour of Partridge was a little inexcusable; but he had not slept off the effect of the dose which he swallowed the evening before; which had, in the morning, received the addition of above a pint of wine, or indeed rather of malt spirits; for the perry was by no means pure. Now, that part of his head which Nature designed for the reservoir of drink being very shallow, a small quantity of liquor overflowed it, and opened the sluices of his heart; so that all the secrets there deposited run out. These sluices were indeed, naturally, very ill-secured. To give the best-natured turn we can to his disposition, he was a very honest man; for, as he was the most inquisitive of mortals, and eternally prying into the secrets of others, so he very faithfully paid them by communicating, in return, everything within his knowledge.\n\nWhile Sophia, tormented with anxiety, knew not what to believe, nor what resolution to take, Susan arrived with the sack-whey. Mrs Honour immediately advised her mistress, in a whisper, to pump this wench, who probably could inform her of the truth. Sophia approved it, and began as follows: “Come hither, child; now answer me truly what I am going to ask you, and I promise you I will very well reward you. Is there a young gentleman in this house, a handsome young gentleman, that----.” Here Sophia blushed and was confounded. “A young gentleman,” cries Honour, “that came hither in company with that saucy rascal who is now in the kitchen?” Susan answered, “There was.”--“Do you know anything of any lady?” continues Sophia, “any lady? I don't ask you whether she is handsome or no; perhaps she is not; that's nothing to the purpose; but do you know of any lady?” “La, madam,” cries Honour, “you will make a very bad examiner. Hark'ee, child,” says she, “is not that very young gentleman now in bed with some nasty trull or other?” Here Susan smiled, and was silent. “Answer the question, child,” says Sophia, “and here's a guinea for you.”--“A guinea! madam,” cries Susan; “la, what's a guinea? If my mistress should know it I shall certainly lose my place that very instant.” “Here's another for you,” says Sophia, “and I promise you faithfully your mistress shall never know it.” Susan, after a very short hesitation, took the money, and told the whole story, concluding with saying, “If you have any great curiosity, madam, I can steal softly into his room, and see whether he be in his own bed or no.” She accordingly did this by Sophia's desire, and returned with an answer in the negative.\n\nSophia now trembled and turned pale. Mrs Honour begged her to be comforted, and not to think any more of so worthless a fellow. “Why there,” says Susan, “I hope, madam, your ladyship won't be offended; but pray, madam, is not your ladyship's name Madam Sophia Western?” “How is it possible you should know me?” answered Sophia. “Why that man, that the gentlewoman spoke of, who is in the kitchen, told about you last night. But I hope your ladyship is not angry with me.” “Indeed, child,” said she, “I am not; pray tell me all, and I promise you I'll reward you.” “Why, madam,” continued Susan, “that man told us all in the kitchen that Madam Sophia Western--indeed I don't know how to bring it out.”--Here she stopt, till, having received encouragement from Sophia, and being vehemently pressed by Mrs Honour, she proceeded thus:--“He told us, madam, though to be sure it is all a lie, that your ladyship was dying for love of the young squire, and that he was going to the wars to get rid of you. I thought to myself then he was a false-hearted wretch; but, now, to see such a fine, rich, beautiful lady as you be, forsaken for such an ordinary woman; for to be sure so she is, and another man's wife into the bargain. It is such a strange unnatural thing, in a manner.”\n\nSophia gave her a third guinea, and, telling her she would certainly be her friend if she mentioned nothing of what had passed, nor informed any one who she was, dismissed the girl, with orders to the post-boy to get the horses ready immediately.\n\nBeing now left alone with her maid, she told her trusty waiting-woman, “That she never was more easy than at present. I am now convinced,” said she, “he is not only a villain, but a low despicable wretch. I can forgive all rather than his exposing my name in so barbarous a manner. That renders him the object of my contempt. Yes, Honour, I am now easy; I am indeed; I am very easy;” and then she burst into a violent flood of tears.\n\nAfter a short interval spent by Sophia, chiefly in crying, and assuring her maid that she was perfectly easy, Susan arrived with an account that the horses were ready, when a very extraordinary thought suggested itself to our young heroine, by which Mr Jones would be acquainted with her having been at the inn, in a way which, if any sparks of affection for her remained in him, would be at least some punishment for his faults.\n\nThe reader will be pleased to remember a little muff, which hath had the honour of being more than once remembered already in this history. This muff, ever since the departure of Mr Jones, had been the constant companion of Sophia by day, and her bedfellow by night; and this muff she had at this very instant upon her arm; whence she took it off with great indignation, and, having writ her name with her pencil upon a piece of paper which she pinned to it, she bribed the maid to convey it into the empty bed of Mr Jones, in which, if he did not find it, she charged her to take some method of conveying it before his eyes in the morning.\n\nThen, having paid for what Mrs Honour had eaten, in which bill was included an account for what she herself might have eaten, she mounted her horse, and, once more assuring her companion that she was perfectly easy, continued her journey.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nContaining, among other things, the ingenuity of Partridge, the madness of Jones, and the folly of Fitzpatrick.\n\nIt was now past five in the morning, and other company began to rise and come to the kitchen, among whom were the serjeant and the coachman, who, being thoroughly reconciled, made a libation, or, in the English phrase, drank a hearty cup together.\n\nIn this drinking nothing more remarkable happened than the behaviour of Partridge, who, when the serjeant drank a health to King George, repeated only the word King; nor could he be brought to utter more; for though he was going to fight against his own cause, yet he could not be prevailed upon to drink against it.\n\nMr Jones, being now returned to his own bed (but from whence he returned we must beg to be excused from relating), summoned Partridge from this agreeable company, who, after a ceremonious preface, having obtained leave to offer his advice, delivered himself as follows:--\n\n“It is, sir, an old saying, and a true one, that a wise man may sometimes learn counsel from a fool; I wish, therefore, I might be so bold as to offer you my advice, which is to return home again, and leave these horrida bella, these bloody wars, to fellows who are contented to swallow gunpowder, because they have nothing else to eat. Now, everybody knows your honour wants for nothing at home; when that's the case, why should any man travel abroad?”\n\n“Partridge,” cries Jones, “thou art certainly a coward; I wish, therefore, thou wouldst return home thyself, and trouble me no more.”\n\n“I ask your honour's pardon,” cries Partridge; “I spoke on your account more than my own; for as to me, Heaven knows my circumstances are bad enough, and I am so far from being afraid, that I value a pistol, or a blunderbuss, or any such thing, no more than a pop-gun. Every man must die once, and what signifies the manner how? besides, perhaps I may come off with the loss only of an arm or a leg. I assure you, sir, I was never less afraid in my life; and so, if your honour is resolved to go on, I am resolved to follow you. But, in that case, I wish I might give my opinion. To be sure, it is a scandalous way of travelling, for a great gentleman like you to walk afoot. Now here are two or three good horses in the stable, which the landlord will certainly make no scruple of trusting you with; but, if he should, I can easily contrive to take them; and, let the worst come to the worst, the king would certainly pardon you, as you are going to fight in his cause.”\n\nNow, as the honesty of Partridge was equal to his understanding, and both dealt only in small matters, he would never have attempted a roguery of this kind, had he not imagined it altogether safe; for he was one of those who have more consideration of the gallows than of the fitness of things; but, in reality, he thought he might have committed this felony without any danger; for, besides that he doubted not but the name of Mr Allworthy would sufficiently quiet the landlord, he conceived they should be altogether safe, whatever turn affairs might take; as Jones, he imagined, would have friends enough on one side, and as his friends would as well secure him on the other.\n\nWhen Mr Jones found that Partridge was in earnest in this proposal, he very severely rebuked him, and that in such bitter terms, that the other attempted to laugh it off, and presently turned the discourse to other matters; saying, he believed they were then in a bawdy house, and that he had with much ado prevented two wenches from disturbing his honour in the middle of the night. “Heyday!” says he, “I believe they got into your chamber whether I would or no; for here lies the muff of one of them on the ground.” Indeed, as Jones returned to his bed in the dark, he had never perceived the muff on the quilt, and, in leaping into his bed, he had tumbled it on the floor. This Partridge now took up, and was going to put into his pocket, when Jones desired to see it. The muff was so very remarkable, that our heroe might possibly have recollected it without the information annexed. But his memory was not put to that hard office; for at the same instant he saw and read the words Sophia Western upon the paper which was pinned to it. His looks now grew frantic in a moment, and he eagerly cried out, “Oh Heavens! how came this muff here?” “I know no more than your honour,” cried Partridge; “but I saw it upon the arm of one of the women who would have disturbed you, if I would have suffered them.” “Where are they?” cries Jones, jumping out of bed, and laying hold of his cloaths. “Many miles off, I believe, by this time,” said Partridge. And now Jones, upon further enquiry, was sufficiently assured that the bearer of this muff was no other than the lovely Sophia herself.\n\nThe behaviour of Jones on this occasion, his thoughts, his looks, his words, his actions, were such as beggar all description. After many bitter execrations on Partridge, and not fewer on himself, he ordered the poor fellow, who was frightened out of his wits, to run down and hire him horses at any rate; and a very few minutes afterwards, having shuffled on his clothes, he hastened down-stairs to execute the orders himself, which he had just before given.\n\nBut before we proceed to what passed on his arrival in the kitchen, it will be necessary to recur to what had there happened since Partridge had first left it on his master's summons.\n\nThe serjeant was just marched off with his party, when the two Irish gentlemen arose, and came downstairs; both complaining that they had been so often waked by the noises in the inn, that they had never once been able to close their eyes all night.\n\nThe coach which had brought the young lady and her maid, and which, perhaps, the reader may have hitherto concluded was her own, was, indeed, a returned coach belonging to Mr King, of Bath, one of the worthiest and honestest men that ever dealt in horse-flesh, and whose coaches we heartily recommend to all our readers who travel that road. By which means they may, perhaps, have the pleasure of riding in the very coach, and being driven by the very coachman, that is recorded in this history.\n\nThe coachman, having but two passengers, and hearing Mr Maclachlan was going to Bath, offered to carry him thither at a very moderate price. He was induced to this by the report of the hostler, who said that the horse which Mr Maclachlan had hired from Worcester would be much more pleased with returning to his friends there than to prosecute a long journey; for that the said horse was rather a two-legged than a four-legged animal.\n\nMr Maclachlan immediately closed with the proposal of the coachman, and, at the same time, persuaded his friend Fitzpatrick to accept of the fourth place in the coach. This conveyance the soreness of his bones made more agreeable to him than a horse; and, being well assured of meeting with his wife at Bath, he thought a little delay would be of no consequence.\n\nMaclachlan, who was much the sharper man of the two, no sooner heard that this lady came from Chester, with the other circumstances which he learned from the hostler, than it came into his head that she might possibly be his friend's wife; and presently acquainted him with this suspicion, which had never once occurred to Fitzpatrick himself. To say the truth, he was one of those compositions which nature makes up in too great a hurry, and forgets to put any brains into their head.\n\nNow it happens to this sort of men, as to bad hounds, who never hit off a fault themselves; but no sooner doth a dog of sagacity open his mouth than they immediately do the same, and, without the guidance of any scent, run directly forwards as fast as they are able. In the same manner, the very moment Mr Maclachlan had mentioned his apprehension, Mr Fitzpatrick instantly concurred, and flew directly up-stairs, to surprize his wife, before he knew where she was; and unluckily (as Fortune loves to play tricks with those gentlemen who put themselves entirely under her conduct) ran his head against several doors and posts to no purpose. Much kinder was she to me, when she suggested that simile of the hounds, just before inserted; since the poor wife may, on these occasions, be so justly compared to a hunted hare. Like that little wretched animal, she pricks up her ears to listen after the voice of her pursuer; like her, flies away trembling when she hears it; and, like her, is generally overtaken and destroyed in the end.\n\nThis was not however the case at present; for after a long fruitless search, Mr Fitzpatrick returned to the kitchen, where, as if this had been a real chace, entered a gentleman hallowing as hunters do when the hounds are at a fault. He was just alighted from his horse, and had many attendants at his heels.\n\nHere, reader, it may be necessary to acquaint thee with some matters, which, if thou dost know already, thou art wiser than I take thee to be. And this information thou shalt receive in the next chapter.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nIn which are concluded the adventures that happened at the inn at Upton.\nIn the first place, then, this gentleman just arrived was no other person than Squire Western himself, who was come hither in pursuit of his daughter; and, had he fortunately been two hours earlier, he had not only found her, but his niece into the bargain; for such was the wife of Mr Fitzpatrick, who had run away with her five years before, out of the custody of that sage lady, Madam Western.\n\nNow this lady had departed from the inn much about the same time with Sophia; for, having been waked by the voice of her husband, she had sent up for the landlady, and being by her apprized of the matter, had bribed the good woman, at an extravagant price, to furnish her with horses for her escape. Such prevalence had money in this family; and though the mistress would have turned away her maid for a corrupt hussy, if she had known as much as the reader, yet she was no more proof against corruption herself than poor Susan had been.\n\nMr Western and his nephew were not known to one another; nor indeed would the former have taken any notice of the latter if he had known him; for, this being a stolen match, and consequently an unnatural one in the opinion of the good squire, he had, from the time of her committing it, abandoned the poor young creature, who was then no more than eighteen, as a monster, and had never since suffered her to be named in his presence.\n\nThe kitchen was now a scene of universal confusion, Western enquiring after his daughter, and Fitzpatrick as eagerly after his wife, when Jones entered the room, unfortunately having Sophia's muff in his hand.\n\nAs soon as Western saw Jones, he set up the same holla as is used by sportsmen when their game is in view. He then immediately run up and laid hold of Jones, crying, “We have got the dog fox, I warrant the bitch is not far off.” The jargon which followed for some minutes, where many spoke different things at the same time, as it would be very difficult to describe, so would it be no less unpleasant to read.\n\nJones having, at length, shaken Mr Western off, and some of the company having interfered between them, our heroe protested his innocence as to knowing anything of the lady; when Parson Supple stepped up, and said, “It is folly to deny it; for why, the marks of guilt are in thy hands. I will myself asseverate and bind it by an oath, that the muff thou bearest in thy hand belongeth unto Madam Sophia; for I have frequently observed her, of later days, to bear it about her.” “My daughter's muff!” cries the squire in a rage. “Hath he got my daughter's muff? bear witness the goods are found upon him. I'll have him before a justice of peace this instant. Where is my daughter, villain?” “Sir,” said Jones, “I beg you would be pacified. The muff, I acknowledge, is the young lady's; but, upon my honour, I have never seen her.” At these words Western lost all patience, and grew inarticulate with rage.\n\nSome of the servants had acquainted Fitzpatrick who Mr Western was. The good Irishman, therefore, thinking he had now an opportunity to do an act of service to his uncle, and by that means might possibly obtain his favour, stept up to Jones, and cried out, “Upon my conscience, sir, you may be ashamed of denying your having seen the gentleman's daughter before my face, when you know I found you there upon the bed together.” Then, turning to Western, he offered to conduct him immediately to the room where his daughter was; which offer being accepted, he, the squire, the parson, and some others, ascended directly to Mrs Waters's chamber, which they entered with no less violence than Mr Fitzpatrick had done before.\n\nThe poor lady started from her sleep with as much amazement as terror, and beheld at her bedside a figure which might very well be supposed to have escaped out of Bedlam. Such wildness and confusion were in the looks of Mr Western; who no sooner saw the lady than he started back, shewing sufficiently by his manner, before he spoke, that this was not the person sought after.\n\nSo much more tenderly do women value their reputation than their persons, that, though the latter seemed now in more danger than before, yet, as the former was secure, the lady screamed not with such violence as she had done on the other occasion. However, she no sooner found herself alone than she abandoned all thoughts of further repose; and, as she had sufficient reason to be dissatisfied with her present lodging, she dressed herself with all possible expedition.\n\nMr Western now proceeded to search the whole house, but to as little purpose as he had disturbed poor Mrs Waters. He then returned disconsolate into the kitchen, where he found Jones in the custody of his servants.\n\nThis violent uproar had raised all the people in the house, though it was yet scarcely daylight. Among these was a grave gentleman, who had the honour to be in the commission of the peace for the county of Worcester. Of which Mr Western was no sooner informed than he offered to lay his complaint before him. The justice declined executing his office, as he said he had no clerk present, nor no book about justice business; and that he could not carry all the law in his head about stealing away daughters, and such sort of things.\n\nHere Mr Fitzpatrick offered to lend him his assistance, informing the company that he had been himself bred to the law. (And indeed he had served three years as clerk to an attorney in the north of Ireland, when, chusing a genteeler walk in life, he quitted his master, came over to England, and set up that business which requires no apprenticeship, namely, that of a gentleman, in which he had succeeded, as hath been already partly mentioned.)\n\nMr Fitzpatrick declared that the law concerning daughters was out of the present case; that stealing a muff was undoubtedly felony, and the goods being found upon the person, were sufficient evidence of the fact.\n\nThe magistrate, upon the encouragement of so learned a coadjutor, and upon the violent intercession of the squire, was at length prevailed upon to seat himself in the chair of justice, where being placed, upon viewing the muff which Jones still held in his hand, and upon the parson's swearing it to be the property of Mr Western, he desired Mr Fitzpatrick to draw up a commitment, which he said he would sign.\n\nJones now desired to be heard, which was at last, with difficulty, granted him. He then produced the evidence of Mr Partridge, as to the finding it; but, what was still more, Susan deposed that Sophia herself had delivered the muff to her, and had ordered her to convey it into the chamber where Mr Jones had found it.\n\nWhether a natural love of justice, or the extraordinary comeliness of Jones, had wrought on Susan to make the discovery, I will not determine; but such were the effects of her evidence, that the magistrate, throwing himself back in his chair, declared that the matter was now altogether as clear on the side of the prisoner as it had before been against him: with which the parson concurred, saying, the Lord forbid he should be instrumental in committing an innocent person to durance. The justice then arose, acquitted the prisoner, and broke up the court.\n\nMr Western now gave every one present a hearty curse, and, immediately ordering his horses, departed in pursuit of his daughter, without taking the least notice of his nephew Fitzpatrick, or returning any answer to his claim of kindred, notwithstanding all the obligations he had just received from that gentleman. In the violence, moreover, of his hurry, and of his passion, he luckily forgot to demand the muff of Jones: I say luckily; for he would have died on the spot rather than have parted with it.\n\nJones likewise, with his friend Partridge, set forward the moment he had paid his reckoning, in quest of his lovely Sophia, whom he now resolved never more to abandon the pursuit of. Nor could he bring himself even to take leave of Mrs Waters; of whom he detested the very thoughts, as she had been, though not designedly, the occasion of his missing the happiest interview with Sophia, to whom he now vowed eternal constancy.\n\nAs for Mrs Waters, she took the opportunity of the coach which was going to Bath; for which place she set out in company with the two Irish gentlemen, the landlady kindly lending her her cloaths; in return for which she was contented only to receive about double their value, as a recompence for the loan. Upon the road she was perfectly reconciled to Mr Fitzpatrick, who was a very handsome fellow, and indeed did all she could to console him in the absence of his wife.\n\nThus ended the many odd adventures which Mr Jones encountered at his inn at Upton, where they talk, to this day, of the beauty and lovely behaviour of the charming Sophia, by the name of the Somersetshire angel.\n\nIn which the history goes backward.\n\nBefore we proceed any farther in our history, it may be proper to look a little back, in order to account for the extraordinary appearance of Sophia and her father at the inn at Upton.\n\nThe reader may be pleased to remember that, in the ninth chapter of the seventh book of our history, we left Sophia, after a long debate between love and duty, deciding the cause, as it usually, I believe, happens, in favour of the former.\n\nThis debate had arisen, as we have there shown, from a visit which her father had just before made her, in order to force her consent to a marriage with Blifil; and which he had understood to be fully implied in her acknowledgment “that she neither must nor could refuse any absolute command of his.”\n\nNow from this visit the squire retired to his evening potation, overjoyed at the success he had gained with his daughter; and, as he was of a social disposition, and willing to have partakers in his happiness, the beer was ordered to flow very liberally into the kitchen; so that before eleven in the evening there was not a single person sober in the house except only Mrs Western herself and the charming Sophia.\n\nEarly in the morning a messenger was despatched to summon Mr Blifil; for, though the squire imagined that young gentleman had been much less acquainted than he really was with the former aversion of his daughter, as he had not, however, yet received her consent, he longed impatiently to communicate it to him, not doubting but that the intended bride herself would confirm it with her lips. As to the wedding, it had the evening before been fixed, by the male parties, to be celebrated on the next morning save one.\n\nBreakfast was now set forth in the parlour, where Mr Blifil attended, and where the squire and his sister likewise were assembled; and now Sophia was ordered to be called.\n\nO, Shakespear! had I thy pen! O, Hogarth! had I thy pencil! then would I draw the picture of the poor serving-man, who, with pale countenance, staring eyes, chattering teeth, faultering tongue, and trembling limbs,\n\n(E'en such a man, so faint, so spiritless, So dull, so dead in look, so woe-begone, Drew Priam's curtains in the dead of night, And would have told him, half his Troy was burn'd)\n\nentered the room, and declared--That Madam Sophia was not to be found.\n\n“Not to be found!” cries the squire, starting from his chair; “Zounds and d--nation! Blood and fury! Where, when, how, what--Not to be found! Where?”\n\n“La! brother,” said Mrs Western, with true political coldness, “you are always throwing yourself into such violent passions for nothing. My niece, I suppose, is only walked out into the garden. I protest you are grown so unreasonable, that it is impossible to live in the house with you.”\n\n“Nay, nay,” answered the squire, returning as suddenly to himself, as he had gone from himself; “if that be all the matter, it signifies not much; but, upon my soul, my mind misgave me when the fellow said she was not to be found.” He then gave orders for the bell to be rung in the garden, and sat himself contentedly down.\n\nNo two things could be more the reverse of each other than were the brother and sister in most instances; particularly in this, That as the brother never foresaw anything at a distance, but was most sagacious in immediately seeing everything the moment it had happened; so the sister eternally foresaw at a distance, but was not so quick-sighted to objects before her eyes. Of both these the reader may have observed examples: and, indeed, both their several talents were excessive; for, as the sister often foresaw what never came to pass, so the brother often saw much more than was actually the truth.\n\nThis was not however the case at present. The same report was brought from the garden as before had been brought from the chamber, that Madam Sophia was not to be found.\n\nThe squire himself now sallied forth, and began to roar forth the name of Sophia as loudly, and in as hoarse a voice, as whilome did Hercules that of Hylas; and, as the poet tells us that the whole shore echoed back the name of that beautiful youth, so did the house, the garden, and all the neighbouring fields resound nothing but the name of Sophia, in the hoarse voices of the men, and in the shrill pipes of the women; while echo seemed so pleased to repeat the beloved sound, that, if there is really such a person, I believe Ovid hath belied her sex.\n\nNothing reigned for a long time but confusion; till at last the squire, having sufficiently spent his breath, returned to the parlour, where he found Mrs Western and Mr Blifil, and threw himself, with the utmost dejection in his countenance, into a great chair.\n\nHere Mrs Western began to apply the following consolation:\n\n“Brother, I am sorry for what hath happened; and that my niece should have behaved herself in a manner so unbecoming her family; but it is all your own doings, and you have nobody to thank but yourself. You know she hath been educated always in a manner directly contrary to my advice, and now you see the consequence. Have I not a thousand times argued with you about giving my niece her own will? But you know I never could prevail upon you; and when I had taken so much pains to eradicate her headstrong opinions, and to rectify your errors in policy, you know she was taken out of my hands; so that I have nothing to answer for. Had I been trusted entirely with the care of her education, no such accident as this had ever befallen you; so that you must comfort yourself by thinking it was all your own doing; and, indeed, what else could be expected from such indulgence?”\n\n“Zounds! sister,” answered he, “you are enough to make one mad. Have I indulged her? Have I given her her will?----It was no longer ago than last night that I threatened, if she disobeyed me, to confine her to her chamber upon bread and water as long as she lived.----You would provoke the patience of Job.”\n\n“Did ever mortal hear the like?” replied she. “Brother, if I had not the patience of fifty Jobs, you would make me forget all decency and decorum. Why would you interfere? Did I not beg you, did I not intreat you, to leave the whole conduct to me? You have defeated all the operations of the campaign by one false step. Would any man in his senses have provoked a daughter by such threats as these? How often have I told you that English women are not to be treated like Ciracessian[*] slaves. We have the protection of the world; we are to be won by gentle means only, and not to be hectored, and bullied, and beat into compliance. I thank Heaven no Salique law governs here. Brother, you have a roughness in your manner which no woman but myself would bear. I do not wonder my niece was frightened and terrified into taking this measure; and, to speak honestly, I think my niece will be justified to the world for what she hath done. I repeat it to you again, brother, you must comfort yourself by rememb'ring that it is all your own fault. How often have I advised--” Here Western rose hastily from his chair, and, venting two or three horrid imprecations, ran out of the room.\n\n[*] Possibly Circassian.\nWhen he was departed, his sister expressed more bitterness (if possible) against him than she had done while he was present; for the truth of which she appealed to Mr Blifil, who, with great complacence, acquiesced entirely in all she said; but excused all the faults of Mr Western, “as they must be considered,” he said, “to have proceeded from the too inordinate fondness of a father, which must be allowed the name of an amiable weakness.” “So much the more inexcuseable,” answered the lady; “for whom doth he ruin by his fondness but his own child?” To which Blifil immediately agreed.\n\nMrs Western then began to express great confusion on the account of Mr Blifil, and of the usage which he had received from a family to which he intended so much honour. On this subject she treated the folly of her niece with great severity; but concluded with throwing the whole on her brother, who, she said, was inexcuseable to have proceeded so far without better assurances of his daughter's consent: “But he was (says she) always of a violent, headstrong temper; and I can scarce forgive myself for all the advice I have thrown away upon him.”\n\nAfter much of this kind of conversation, which, perhaps, would not greatly entertain the reader, was it here particularly related, Mr Blifil took his leave and returned home, not highly pleased with his disappointment: which, however, the philosophy which he had acquired from Square, and the religion infused into him by Thwackum, together with somewhat else, taught him to bear rather better than more passionate lovers bear these kinds of evils.\n\nThe escape of Sophia.\n\nIt is now time to look after Sophia; whom the reader, if he loves her half so well as I do, will rejoice to find escaped from the clutches of her passionate father, and from those of her dispassionate lover.\n\nTwelve times did the iron register of time beat on the sonorous bell-metal, summoning the ghosts to rise and walk their nightly round.----In plainer language, it was twelve o'clock, and all the family, as we have said, lay buried in drink and sleep, except only Mrs Western, who was deeply engaged in reading a political pamphlet, and except our heroine, who now softly stole down-stairs, and, having unbarred and unlocked one of the house-doors, sallied forth, and hastened to the place of appointment.\n\nNotwithstanding the many pretty arts which ladies sometimes practise, to display their fears on every little occasion (almost as many as the other sex uses to conceal theirs), certainly there is a degree of courage which not only becomes a woman, but is often necessary to enable her to discharge her duty. It is, indeed, the idea of fierceness, and not of bravery, which destroys the female character; for who can read the story of the justly celebrated Arria without conceiving as high an opinion of her gentleness and tenderness as of her fortitude? At the same time, perhaps, many a woman who shrieks at a mouse, or a rat, may be capable of poisoning a husband; or, what is worse, of driving him to poison himself.\n\nSophia, with all the gentleness which a woman can have, had all the spirit which she ought to have. When, therefore, she came to the place of appointment, and, instead of meeting her maid, as was agreed, saw a man ride directly up to her, she neither screamed out nor fainted away: not that her pulse then beat with its usual regularity; for she was, at first, under some surprize and apprehension: but these were relieved almost as soon as raised, when the man, pulling off his hat, asked her, in a very submissive manner, “If her ladyship did not expect to meet another lady?” and then proceeded to inform her that he was sent to conduct her to that lady.\n\nSophia could have no possible suspicion of any falsehood in this account: she therefore mounted resolutely behind the fellow, who conveyed her safe to a town about five miles distant, where she had the satisfaction of finding the good Mrs Honour: for, as the soul of the waiting-woman was wrapt up in those very habiliments which used to enwrap her body, she could by no means bring herself to trust them out of her sight. Upon these, therefore, she kept guard in person, while she detached the aforesaid fellow after her mistress, having given him all proper instructions.\n\nThey now debated what course to take, in order to avoid the pursuit of Mr Western, who they knew would send after them in a few hours. The London road had such charms for Honour, that she was desirous of going on directly; alleging that, as Sophia could not be missed till eight or nine the next morning, her pursuers would not be able to overtake her, even though they knew which way she had gone. But Sophia had too much at stake to venture anything to chance; nor did she dare trust too much to her tender limbs, in a contest which was to be decided only by swiftness. She resolved, therefore, to travel across the country, for at least twenty or thirty miles, and then to take the direct road to London. So, having hired horses to go twenty miles one way, when she intended to go twenty miles the other, she set forward with the same guide behind whom she had ridden from her father's house; the guide having now taken up behind him, in the room of Sophia, a much heavier, as well as much less lovely burden; being, indeed, a huge portmanteau, well stuffed with those outside ornaments, by means of which the fair Honour hoped to gain many conquests, and, finally, to make her fortune in London city.\n\nWhen they had gone about two hundred paces from the inn on the London road, Sophia rode up to the guide, and, with a voice much fuller of honey than was ever that of Plato, though his mouth is supposed to have been a bee-hive, begged him to take the first turning which led towards Bristol.\n\nReader, I am not superstitious, nor any great believer of modern miracles. I do not, therefore, deliver the following as a certain truth; for, indeed, I can scarce credit it myself: but the fidelity of an historian obliges me to relate what hath been confidently asserted. The horse, then, on which the guide rode, is reported to have been so charmed by Sophia's voice, that he made a full stop, and expressed an unwillingness to proceed any farther.\n\nPerhaps, however, the fact may be true, and less miraculous than it hath been represented; since the natural cause seems adequate to the effect: for, as the guide at that moment desisted from a constant application of his armed right heel (for, like Hudibras, he wore but one spur), it is more than possible that this omission alone might occasion the beast to stop, especially as this was very frequent with him at other times.\n\nBut if the voice of Sophia had really an effect on the horse, it had very little on the rider. He answered somewhat surlily, “That measter had ordered him to go a different way, and that he should lose his place if he went any other than that he was ordered.”\n\nSophia, finding all her persuasions had no effect, began now to add irresistible charms to her voice; charms which, according to the proverb, makes the old mare trot, instead of standing still; charms! to which modern ages have attributed all that irresistible force which the antients imputed to perfect oratory. In a word, she promised she would reward him to his utmost expectation.\n\nThe lad was not totally deaf to these promises; but he disliked their being indefinite; for, though perhaps he had never heard that word, yet that, in fact, was his objection. He said, “Gentlevolks did not consider the case of poor volks; that he had like to have been turned away the other day, for riding about the country with a gentleman from Squire Allworthy's, who did not reward him as he should have done.”\n\n“With whom?” says Sophia eagerly. “With a gentleman from Squire Allworthy's,” repeated the lad; “the squire's son, I think they call 'un.”--“Whither? which way did he go?” says Sophia.--“Why, a little o' one side o' Bristol, about twenty miles off,” answered the lad.--“Guide me,” says Sophia, “to the same place, and I'll give thee a guinea, or two, if one is not sufficient.”--“To be certain,” said the boy, “it is honestly worth two, when your ladyship considers what a risk I run; but, however, if your ladyship will promise me the two guineas, I'll e'en venture: to be certain it is a sinful thing to ride about my measter's horses; but one comfort is, I can only be turned away, and two guineas will partly make me amends.”\n\nThe bargain being thus struck, the lad turned aside into the Bristol road, and Sophia set forward in pursuit of Jones, highly contrary to the remonstrances of Mrs Honour, who had much more desire to see London than to see Mr Jones: for indeed she was not his friend with her mistress, as he had been guilty of some neglect in certain pecuniary civilities, which are by custom due to the waiting-gentlewoman in all love affairs, and more especially in those of a clandestine kind. This we impute rather to the carelessness of his temper than to any want of generosity; but perhaps she derived it from the latter motive. Certain it is that she hated him very bitterly on that account, and resolved to take every opportunity of injuring him with her mistress. It was therefore highly unlucky for her, that she had gone to the very same town and inn whence Jones had started, and still more unlucky was she in having stumbled on the same guide, and on this accidental discovery which Sophia had made.\n\nOur travellers arrived at Hambrook[*] at the break of day, where Honour was against her will charged to enquire the route which Mr Jones had taken. Of this, indeed, the guide himself could have informed them; but Sophia, I know not for what reason, never asked him the question.\n\n[*] This was the village where Jones met the Quaker.\n\nWhen Mrs Honour had made her report from the landlord, Sophia, with much difficulty, procured some indifferent horses, which brought her to the inn where Jones had been confined rather by the misfortune of meeting with a surgeon than by having met with a broken head.\n\nHere Honour, being again charged with a commission of enquiry, had no sooner applied herself to the landlady, and had described the person of Mr Jones, than that sagacious woman began, in the vulgar phrase, to smell a rat. When Sophia therefore entered the room, instead of answering the maid, the landlady, addressing herself to the mistress, began the following speech: “Good lack-a-day! why there now, who would have thought it? I protest the loveliest couple that ever eye beheld. I-fackins, madam, it is no wonder the squire run on so about your ladyship. He told me indeed you was the finest lady in the world, and to be sure so you be. Mercy on him, poor heart! I bepitied him, so I did, when he used to hug his pillow, and call it his dear Madam Sophia. I did all I could to dissuade him from going to the wars: I told him there were men enow that were good for nothing else but to be killed, that had not the love of such fine ladies.” “Sure,” says Sophia, “the good woman is distracted.” “No, no,” cries the landlady, “I am not distracted. What, doth your ladyship think I don't know then? I assure you he told me all.” “What saucy fellow,” cries Honour, “told you anything of my lady?” “No saucy fellow,” answered the landlady, “but the young gentleman you enquired after, and a very pretty young gentleman he is, and he loves Madam Sophia Western to the bottom of his soul.” “He love my lady! I'd have you to know, woman, she is meat for his master.”--“Nay, Honour,” said Sophia, interrupting her, “don't be angry with the good woman; she intends no harm.” “No, marry, don't I,” answered the landlady, emboldened by the soft accents of Sophia; and then launched into a long narrative too tedious to be here set down, in which some passages dropt that gave a little offence to Sophia, and much more to her waiting-woman, who hence took occasion to abuse poor Jones to her mistress the moment they were alone together, saying, “that he must be a very pitiful fellow, and could have no love for a lady, whose name he would thus prostitute in an ale-house.”\n\nSophia did not see his behaviour in so very disadvantageous a light, and was perhaps more pleased with the violent raptures of his love (which the landlady exaggerated as much as she had done every other circumstance) than she was offended with the rest; and indeed she imputed the whole to the extravagance, or rather ebullience, of his passion, and to the openness of his heart.\n\nThis incident, however, being afterwards revived in her mind, and placed in the most odious colours by Honour, served to heighten and give credit to those unlucky occurrences at Upton, and assisted the waiting-woman in her endeavours to make her mistress depart from that inn without seeing Jones.\n\nThe landlady finding Sophia intended to stay no longer than till her horses were ready, and that without either eating or drinking, soon withdrew; when Honour began to take her mistress to task (for indeed she used great freedom), and after a long harangue, in which she reminded her of her intention to go to London, and gave frequent hints of the impropriety of pursuing a young fellow, she at last concluded with this serious exhortation: “For heaven's sake, madam, consider what you are about, and whither you are going.”\n\nThis advice to a lady who had already rode near forty miles, and in no very agreeable season, may seem foolish enough. It may be supposed she had well considered and resolved this already; nay, Mrs Honour, by the hints she threw out, seemed to think so; and this I doubt not is the opinion of many readers, who have, I make no doubt, been long since well convinced of the purpose of our heroine, and have heartily condemned her for it as a wanton baggage.\n\nBut in reality this was not the case. Sophia had been lately so distracted between hope and fear, her duty and love to her father, her hatred to Blifil, her compassion, and (why should we not confess the truth?) her love for Jones; which last the behaviour of her father, of her aunt, of every one else, and more particularly of Jones himself, had blown into a flame, that her mind was in that confused state which may be truly said to make us ignorant of what we do, or whither we go, or rather, indeed, indifferent as to the consequence of either.\n\nThe prudent and sage advice of her maid produced, however, some cool reflection; and she at length determined to go to Gloucester, and thence to proceed directly to London.\n\nBut, unluckily, a few miles before she entered that town, she met the hack-attorney, who, as is before mentioned, had dined there with Mr Jones. This fellow, being well known to Mrs Honour, stopt and spoke to her; of which Sophia at that time took little notice, more than to enquire who he was.\n\nBut, having had a more particular account from Honour of this man afterwards at Gloucester, and hearing of the great expedition he usually made in travelling, for which (as hath been before observed) he was particularly famous; recollecting, likewise, that she had overheard Mrs Honour inform him that they were going to Gloucester, she began to fear lest her father might, by this fellow's means, be able to trace her to that city; wherefore, if she should there strike into the London road, she apprehended he would certainly be able to overtake her. She therefore altered her resolution; and, having hired horses to go a week's journey a way which she did not intend to travel, she again set forward after a light refreshment, contrary to the desire and earnest entreaties of her maid, and to the no less vehement remonstrances of Mrs Whitefield, who, from good breeding, or perhaps from good nature (for the poor young lady appeared much fatigued), pressed her very heartily to stay that evening at Gloucester.\n\nHaving refreshed herself only with some tea, and with lying about two hours on the bed, while her horses were getting ready, she resolutely left Mrs Whitefield's about eleven at night, and, striking directly into the Worcester road, within less than four hours arrived at that very inn where we last saw her.\n\nHaving thus traced our heroine very particularly back from her departure, till her arrival at Upton, we shall in a very few words bring her father to the same place; who, having received the first scent from the post-boy, who conducted his daughter to Hambrook, very easily traced her afterwards to Gloucester; whence he pursued her to Upton, as he had learned Mr Jones had taken that route (for Partridge, to use the squire's expression, left everywhere a strong scent behind him), and he doubted not in the least but Sophia travelled, or, as he phrased it, ran, the same way. He used indeed a very coarse expression, which need not be here inserted; as fox-hunters, who alone will understand it, will easily suggest it to themselves.\n\nA crust for the critics.\n\nIn our last initial chapter we may be supposed to have treated that formidable set of men who are called critics with more freedom than becomes us; since they exact, and indeed generally receive, great condescension from authors. We shall in this, therefore, give the reasons of our conduct to this august body; and here we shall, perhaps, place them in a light in which they have not hitherto been seen.\n\nThis word critic is of Greek derivation, and signifies judgment. Hence I presume some persons who have not understood the original, and have seen the English translation of the primitive, have concluded that it meant judgment in the legal sense, in which it is frequently used as equivalent to condemnation.\n\nI am the rather inclined to be of that opinion, as the greatest number of critics hath of late years been found amongst the lawyers. Many of these gentlemen, from despair, perhaps, of ever rising to the bench in Westminster-hall, have placed themselves on the benches at the playhouse, where they have exerted their judicial capacity, and have given judgment, i.e., condemned without mercy.\n\nThe gentlemen would, perhaps, be well enough pleased, if we were to leave them thus compared to one of the most important and honourable offices in the commonwealth, and, if we intended to apply to their favour, we would do so; but, as we design to deal very sincerely and plainly too with them, we must remind them of another officer of justice of a much lower rank; to whom, as they not only pronounce, but execute, their own judgment, they bear likewise some remote resemblance.\n\nBut in reality there is another light, in which these modern critics may, with great justice and propriety, be seen; and this is that of a common slanderer. If a person who prys into the characters of others, with no other design but to discover their faults, and to publish them to the world, deserves the title of a slanderer of the reputations of men, why should not a critic, who reads with the same malevolent view, be as properly stiled the slanderer of the reputation of books?\n\nVice hath not, I believe, a more abject slave; society produces not a more odious vermin; nor can the devil receive a guest more worthy of him, nor possibly more welcome to him, than a slanderer. The world, I am afraid, regards not this monster with half the abhorrence which he deserves; and I am more afraid to assign the reason of this criminal lenity shown towards him; yet it is certain that the thief looks innocent in the comparison; nay, the murderer himself can seldom stand in competition with his guilt: for slander is a more cruel weapon than a sword, as the wounds which the former gives are always incurable. One method, indeed, there is of killing, and that the basest and most execrable of all, which bears an exact analogy to the vice here disclaimed against, and that is poison: a means of revenge so base, and yet so horrible, that it was once wisely distinguished by our laws from all other murders, in the peculiar severity of the punishment.\n\nBesides the dreadful mischiefs done by slander, and the baseness of the means by which they are effected, there are other circumstances that highly aggravate its atrocious quality; for it often proceeds from no provocation, and seldom promises itself any reward, unless some black and infernal mind may propose a reward in the thoughts of having procured the ruin and misery of another.\n\nShakespear hath nobly touched this vice, when he says--\n\n“Who steals my purse steals trash; 't is something, nothing; 'Twas mine, 'tis his, and hath been slave to thousands: But he that filches from me my good name Robs me of that WHICH NOT ENRICHES HIM, BUT MAKES ME POOR INDEED.”\n\nWith all this my good reader will doubtless agree; but much of it will probably seem too severe, when applied to the slanderer of books. But let it here be considered that both proceed from the same wicked disposition of mind, and are alike void of the excuse of temptation. Nor shall we conclude the injury done this way to be very slight, when we consider a book as the author's offspring, and indeed as the child of his brain.\n\nThe reader who hath suffered his muse to continue hitherto in a virgin state can have but a very inadequate idea of this kind of paternal fondness. To such we may parody the tender exclamation of Macduff, “Alas! Thou hast written no book.” But the author whose muse hath brought forth will feel the pathetic strain, perhaps will accompany me with tears (especially if his darling be already no more), while I mention the uneasiness with which the big muse bears about her burden, the painful labour with which she produces it, and, lastly, the care, the fondness, with which the tender father nourishes his favourite, till it be brought to maturity, and produced into the world.\n\nNor is there any paternal fondness which seems less to savour of absolute instinct, and which may so well be reconciled to worldly wisdom, as this. These children may most truly be called the riches of their father; and many of them have with true filial piety fed their parent in his old age: so that not only the affection, but the interest, of the author may be highly injured by these slanderers, whose poisonous breath brings his book to an untimely end.\n\nLastly, the slander of a book is, in truth, the slander of the author: for, as no one can call another bastard, without calling the mother a whore, so neither can any one give the names of sad stuff, horrid nonsense, &c., to a book, without calling the author a blockhead; which, though in a moral sense it is a preferable appellation to that of villain, is perhaps rather more injurious to his worldly interest.\n\nNow, however ludicrous all this may appear to some, others, I doubt not, will feel and acknowledge the truth of it; nay, may, perhaps, think I have not treated the subject with decent solemnity; but surely a man may speak truth with a smiling countenance. In reality, to depreciate a book maliciously, or even wantonly, is at least a very ill-natured office; and a morose snarling critic may, I believe, be suspected to be a bad man.\n\nI will therefore endeavour, in the remaining part of this chapter, to explain the marks of this character, and to show what criticism I here intend to obviate: for I can never be understood, unless by the very persons here meant, to insinuate that there are no proper judges of writing, or to endeavour to exclude from the commonwealth of literature any of those noble critics to whose labours the learned world are so greatly indebted. Such were Aristotle, Horace, and Longinus, among the antients, Dacier and Bossu among the French, and some perhaps among us; who have certainly been duly authorised to execute at least a judicial authority in foro literario.\n\nBut without ascertaining all the proper qualifications of a critic, which I have touched on elsewhere, I think I may very boldly object to the censures of any one past upon works which he hath not himself read. Such censurers as these, whether they speak from their own guess or suspicion, or from the report and opinion of others, may properly be said to slander the reputation of the book they condemn.\n\nSuch may likewise be suspected of deserving this character, who, without assigning any particular faults, condemn the whole in general defamatory terms; such as vile, dull, d--d stuff, &c., and particularly by the use of the monosyllable low; a word which becomes the mouth of no critic who is not RIGHT HONOURABLE.\n\nAgain, though there may be some faults justly assigned in the work, yet, if those are not in the most essential parts, or if they are compensated by greater beauties, it will savour rather of the malice of a slanderer than of the judgment of a true critic to pass a severe sentence upon the whole, merely on account of some vicious part. This is directly contrary to the sentiments of Horace:\n\nVerum ubi plura nitent in carmine, non ego paucis Offendor maculis, quas aut incuria fudit, Aut humana parum cavit natura----\n\nBut where the beauties, more in number, shine, I am not angry, when a casual line (That with some trivial faults unequal flows) A careless hand or human frailty shows.--MR FRANCIS.\n\nFor, as Martial says, Aliter non fit, Avite, liber. No book can be otherwise composed. All beauty of character, as well as of countenance, and indeed of everything human, is to be tried in this manner. Cruel indeed would it be if such a work as this history, which hath employed some thousands of hours in the composing, should be liable to be condemned, because some particular chapter, or perhaps chapters, may be obnoxious to very just and sensible objections. And yet nothing is more common than the most rigorous sentence upon books supported by such objections, which, if they were rightly taken (and that they are not always), do by no means go to the merit of the whole. In the theatre especially, a single expression which doth not coincide with the taste of the audience, or with any individual critic of that audience, is sure to be hissed; and one scene which should be disapproved would hazard the whole piece. To write within such severe rules as these is as impossible as to live up to some splenetic opinions: and if we judge according to the sentiments of some critics, and of some Christians, no author will be saved in this world, and no man in the next.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nThe adventures which Sophia met with after her leaving Upton.\n\nOur history, just before it was obliged to turn about and travel backwards, had mentioned the departure of Sophia and her maid from the inn; we shall now therefore pursue the steps of that lovely creature, and leave her unworthy lover a little longer to bemoan his ill-luck, or rather his ill-conduct.\n\nSophia having directed her guide to travel through bye-roads, across the country, they now passed the Severn, and had scarce got a mile from the inn, when the young lady, looking behind her, saw several horses coming after on full speed. This greatly alarmed her fears, and she called to the guide to put on as fast as possible.\n\nHe immediately obeyed her, and away they rode a full gallop. But the faster they went, the faster were they followed; and as the horses behind were somewhat swifter than those before, so the former were at length overtaken. A happy circumstance for poor Sophia; whose fears, joined to her fatigue, had almost overpowered her spirits; but she was now instantly relieved by a female voice, that greeted her in the softest manner, and with the utmost civility. This greeting Sophia, as soon as she could recover her breath, with like civility, and with the highest satisfaction to herself, returned.\n\nThe travellers who joined Sophia, and who had given her such terror, consisted, like her own company, of two females and a guide. The two parties proceeded three full miles together before any one offered again to open their mouths; when our heroine, having pretty well got the better of her fear (but yet being somewhat surprized that the other still continued to attend her, as she pursued no great road, and had already passed through several turnings), accosted the strange lady in a most obliging tone, and said, “She was very happy to find they were both travelling the same way.” The other, who, like a ghost, only wanted to be spoke to, readily answered, “That the happiness was entirely hers; that she was a perfect stranger in that country, and was so overjoyed at meeting a companion of her own sex, that she had perhaps been guilty of an impertinence, which required great apology, in keeping pace with her.” More civilities passed between these two ladies; for Mrs Honour had now given place to the fine habit of the stranger, and had fallen into the rear. But, though Sophia had great curiosity to know why the other lady continued to travel on through the same bye-roads with herself, nay, though this gave her some uneasiness, yet fear, or modesty, or some other consideration, restrained her from asking the question.\n\nThe strange lady now laboured under a difficulty which appears almost below the dignity of history to mention. Her bonnet had been blown from her head not less than five times within the last mile; nor could she come at any ribbon or handkerchief to tie it under her chin. When Sophia was informed of this, she immediately supplied her with a handkerchief for this purpose; which while she was pulling from her pocket, she perhaps too much neglected the management of her horse, for the beast, now unluckily making a false step, fell upon his fore-legs, and threw his fair rider from his back.\n\nThough Sophia came head foremost to the ground, she happily received not the least damage: and the same circumstances which had perhaps contributed to her fall now preserved her from confusion; for the lane which they were then passing was narrow, and very much overgrown with trees, so that the moon could here afford very little light, and was moreover, at present, so obscured in a cloud, that it was almost perfectly dark. By these means the young lady's modesty, which was extremely delicate, escaped as free from injury as her limbs, and she was once more reinstated in her saddle, having received no other harm than a little fright by her fall.\n\nDaylight at length appeared in its full lustre; and now the two ladies, who were riding over a common side by side, looking stedfastly at each other, at the same moment both their eyes became fixed; both their horses stopt, and, both speaking together, with equal joy pronounced, the one the name of Sophia, the other that of Harriet.\n\nThis unexpected encounter surprized the ladies much more than I believe it will the sagacious reader, who must have imagined that the strange lady could be no other than Mrs Fitzpatrick, the cousin of Miss Western, whom we before mentioned to have sallied from the inn a few minutes after her.\n\nSo great was the surprize and joy which these two cousins conceived at this meeting (for they had formerly been most intimate acquaintance and friends, and had long lived together with their aunt Western), that it is impossible to recount half the congratulations which passed between them, before either asked a very natural question of the other, namely, whither she was going?\n\nThis at last, however, came first from Mrs Fitzpatrick; but, easy and natural as the question may seem, Sophia found it difficult to give it a very ready and certain answer. She begged her cousin therefore to suspend all curiosity till they arrived at some inn, “which I suppose,” says she, “can hardly be far distant; and, believe me, Harriet, I suspend as much curiosity on my side; for, indeed, I believe our astonishment is pretty equal.”\n\nThe conversation which passed between these ladies on the road was, I apprehend, little worth relating; and less certainly was that between the two waiting-women; for they likewise began to pay their compliments to each other. As for the guides, they were debarred from the pleasure of discourse, the one being placed in the van, and the other obliged to bring up the rear.\n\nIn this posture they travelled many hours, till they came into a wide and well-beaten road, which, as they turned to the right, soon brought them to a very fair promising inn, where they all alighted: but so fatigued was Sophia, that as she had sat her horse during the last five or six miles with great difficulty, so was she now incapable of dismounting from him without assistance. This the landlord, who had hold of her horse, presently perceiving, offered to lift her in his arms from her saddle; and she too readily accepted the tender of his service. Indeed fortune seems to have resolved to put Sophia to the blush that day, and the second malicious attempt succeeded better than the first; for my landlord had no sooner received the young lady in his arms, than his feet, which the gout had lately very severely handled, gave way, and down he tumbled; but, at the same time, with no less dexterity than gallantry, contrived to throw himself under his charming burden, so that he alone received any bruise from the fall; for the great injury which happened to Sophia was a violent shock given to her modesty by an immoderate grin, which, at her rising from the ground, she observed in the countenances of most of the bye-standers. This made her suspect what had really happened, and what we shall not here relate for the indulgence of those readers who are capable of laughing at the offence given to a young lady's delicacy. Accidents of this kind we have never regarded in a comical light; nor will we scruple to say that he must have a very inadequate idea of the modesty of a beautiful young woman, who would wish to sacrifice it to so paltry a satisfaction as can arise from laughter.\n\nThis fright and shock, joined to the violent fatigue which both her mind and body had undergone, almost overcame the excellent constitution of Sophia, and she had scarce strength sufficient to totter into the inn, leaning on the arm of her maid. Here she was no sooner seated than she called for a glass of water; but Mrs Honour, very judiciously, in my opinion, changed it into a glass of wine.\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick, hearing from Mrs Honour that Sophia had not been in bed during the two last nights, and observing her to look very pale and wan with her fatigue, earnestly entreated her to refresh herself with some sleep. She was yet a stranger to her history, or her apprehensions; but, had she known both, she would have given the same advice; for rest was visibly necessary for her; and their long journey through bye-roads so entirely removed all danger of pursuit, that she was herself perfectly easy on that account.\n\nSophia was easily prevailed on to follow the counsel of her friend, which was heartily seconded by her maid. Mrs Fitzpatrick likewise offered to bear her cousin company, which Sophia, with much complacence, accepted.\n\nThe mistress was no sooner in bed than the maid prepared to follow her example. She began to make many apologies to her sister Abigail for leaving her alone in so horrid a place as an inn; but the other stopt her short, being as well inclined to a nap as herself, and desired the honour of being her bedfellow. Sophia's maid agreed to give her a share of her bed, but put in her claim to all the honour. So, after many courtsies and compliments, to bed together went the waiting-women, as their mistresses had done before them.\n\nIt was usual with my landlord (as indeed it is with the whole fraternity) to enquire particularly of all coachmen, footmen, postboys, and others, into the names of all his guests; what their estate was, and where it lay. It cannot therefore be wondered at that the many particular circumstances which attended our travellers, and especially their retiring all to sleep at so extraordinary and unusual an hour as ten in the morning, should excite his curiosity. As soon, therefore, as the guides entered the kitchen, he began to examine who the ladies were, and whence they came; but the guides, though they faithfully related all they knew, gave him very little satisfaction. On the contrary, they rather enflamed his curiosity than extinguished it.\n\nThis landlord had the character, among all his neighbours, of being a very sagacious fellow. He was thought to see farther and deeper into things than any man in the parish, the parson himself not excepted. Perhaps his look had contributed not a little to procure him this reputation; for there was in this something wonderfully wise and significant, especially when he had a pipe in his mouth; which, indeed, he seldom was without. His behaviour, likewise, greatly assisted in promoting the opinion of his wisdom. In his deportment he was solemn, if not sullen; and when he spoke, which was seldom, he always delivered himself in a slow voice; and, though his sentences were short, they were still interrupted with many hums and ha's, ay ays, and other expletives: so that, though he accompanied his words with certain explanatory gestures, such as shaking or nodding the head, or pointing with his fore-finger, he generally left his hearers to understand more than he expressed; nay, he commonly gave them a hint that he knew much more than he thought proper to disclose. This last circumstance alone may, indeed, very well account for his character of wisdom; since men are strangely inclined to worship what they do not understand. A grand secret, upon which several imposers on mankind have totally relied for the success of their frauds.\n\nThis polite person, now taking his wife aside, asked her “what she thought of the ladies lately arrived?” “Think of them?” said the wife, “why, what should I think of them?” “I know,” answered he, “what I think. The guides tell strange stories. One pretends to be come from Gloucester, and the other from Upton; and neither of them, for what I can find, can tell whither they are going. But what people ever travel across the country from Upton hither, especially to London? And one of the maid-servants, before she alighted from her horse, asked if this was not the London road? Now I have put all these circumstances together, and whom do you think I have found them out to be?” “Nay,” answered she, “you know I never pretend to guess at your discoveries.”----“It is a good girl,” replied he, chucking her under the chin; “I must own you have always submitted to my knowledge of these matters. Why, then, depend upon it; mind what I say--depend upon it, they are certainly some of the rebel ladies, who, they say, travel with the young Chevalier; and have taken a roundabout way to escape the duke's army.”\n\n“Husband,” quoth the wife, “you have certainly hit it; for one of them is dressed as fine as any princess; and, to be sure, she looks for all the world like one.----But yet, when I consider one thing”----“When you consider,” cries the landlord contemptuously----“Come, pray let's hear what you consider.”----“Why, it is,” answered the wife, “that she is too humble to be any very great lady: for, while our Betty was warming the bed, she called her nothing but child, and my dear, and sweetheart; and, when Betty offered to pull off her shoes and stockings, she would not suffer her, saying, she would not give her the trouble.”\n\n“Pugh!” answered the husband, “that is nothing. Dost think, because you have seen some great ladies rude and uncivil to persons below them, that none of them know how to behave themselves when they come before their inferiors? I think I know people of fashion when I see them--I think I do. Did not she call for a glass of water when she came in? Another sort of women would have called for a dram; you know they would. If she be not a woman of very great quality, sell me for a fool; and, I believe, those who buy me will have a bad bargain. Now, would a woman of her quality travel without a footman, unless upon some such extraordinary occasion?” “Nay, to be sure, husband,” cries she, “you know these matters better than I, or most folk.” “I think I do know something,” said he. “To be sure,” answered the wife, “the poor little heart looked so piteous, when she sat down in the chair, I protest I could not help having a compassion for her almost as much as if she had been a poor body. But what's to be done, husband? If an she be a rebel, I suppose you intend to betray her up to the court. Well, she's a sweet-tempered, good-humoured lady, be she what she will, and I shall hardly refrain from crying when I hear she is hanged or beheaded.” “Pooh!” answered the husband.----“But, as to what's to be done, it is not so easy a matter to determine. I hope, before she goes away, we shall have the news of a battle; for, if the Chevalier should get the better, she may gain us interest at court, and make our fortunes without betraying her.” “Why, that's true,” replied the wife; “and I heartily hope she will have it in her power. Certainly she's a sweet good lady; it would go horribly against me to have her come to any harm.” “Pooh!” cries the landlord, “women are always so tender-hearted. Why, you would not harbour rebels, would you?” “No, certainly,” answered the wife; “and as for betraying her, come what will on't, nobody can blame us. It is what anybody would do in our case.”\n\nWhile our politic landlord, who had not, we see, undeservedly the reputation of great wisdom among his neighbours, was engaged in debating this matter with himself (for he paid little attention to the opinion of his wife), news arrived that the rebels had given the duke the slip, and had got a day's march towards London; and soon after arrived a famous Jacobite squire, who, with great joy in his countenance, shook the landlord by the hand, saying, “All's our own, boy, ten thousand honest Frenchmen are landed in Suffolk. Old England for ever! ten thousand French, my brave lad! I am going to tap away directly.”\n\nThis news determined the opinion of the wise man, and he resolved to make his court to the young lady when she arose; for he had now (he said) discovered that she was no other than Madam Jenny Cameron herself.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nA very short chapter, in which however is a sun, a moon, a star, and an angel.\n\nThe sun (for he keeps very good hours at this time of the year) had been some time retired to rest when Sophia arose greatly refreshed by her sleep; which, short as it was, nothing but her extreme fatigue could have occasioned; for, though she had told her maid, and perhaps herself too, that she was perfectly easy when she left Upton, yet it is certain her mind was a little affected with that malady which is attended with all the restless symptoms of a fever, and is perhaps the very distemper which physicians mean (if they mean anything) by the fever on the spirits.\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick likewise left her bed at the same time; and, having summoned her maid, immediately dressed herself. She was really a very pretty woman, and, had she been in any other company but that of Sophia, might have been thought beautiful; but when Mrs Honour of her own accord attended (for her mistress would not suffer her to be waked), and had equipped our heroine, the charms of Mrs Fitzpatrick, who had performed the office of the morning-star, and had preceded greater glories, shared the fate of that star, and were totally eclipsed the moment those glories shone forth.\n\nPerhaps Sophia never looked more beautiful than she did at this instant. We ought not, therefore, to condemn the maid of the inn for her hyperbole, who, when she descended, after having lighted the fire, declared, and ratified it with an oath, that if ever there was an angel upon earth, she was now above-stairs.\n\nSophia had acquainted her cousin with her design to go to London; and Mrs Fitzpatrick had agreed to accompany her; for the arrival of her husband at Upton had put an end to her design of going to Bath, or to her aunt Western. They had therefore no sooner finished their tea than Sophia proposed to set out, the moon then shining extremely bright, and as for the frost she defied it; nor had she any of those apprehensions which many young ladies would have felt at travelling by night; for she had, as we have before observed, some little degree of natural courage; and this, her present sensations, which bordered somewhat on despair, greatly encreased. Besides, as she had already travelled twice with safety by the light of the moon, she was the better emboldened to trust to it a third time.\n\nThe disposition of Mrs Fitzpatrick was more timorous; for, though the greater terrors had conquered the less, and the presence of her husband had driven her away at so unseasonable an hour from Upton, yet, being now arrived at a place where she thought herself safe from his pursuit, these lesser terrors of I know not what operated so strongly, that she earnestly entreated her cousin to stay till the next morning, and not expose herself to the dangers of travelling by night.\n\nSophia, who was yielding to an excess, when she could neither laugh nor reason her cousin out of these apprehensions, at last gave way to them. Perhaps, indeed, had she known of her father's arrival at Upton, it might have been more difficult to have persuaded her; for as to Jones, she had, I am afraid, no great horror at the thoughts of being overtaken by him; nay, to confess the truth, I believe she rather wished than feared it; though I might honestly enough have concealed this wish from the reader, as it was one of those secret spontaneous emotions of the soul to which the reason is often a stranger.\n\nWhen our young ladies had determined to remain all that evening in their inn they were attended by the landlady, who desired to know what their ladyships would be pleased to eat. Such charms were there in the voice, in the manner, and in the affable deportment of Sophia, that she ravished the landlady to the highest degree; and that good woman, concluding that she had attended Jenny Cameron, became in a moment a stanch Jacobite, and wished heartily well to the young Pretender's cause, from the great sweetness and affability with which she had been treated by his supposed mistress.\n\nThe two cousins began now to impart to each other their reciprocal curiosity to know what extraordinary accidents on both sides occasioned this so strange and unexpected meeting. At last Mrs Fitzpatrick, having obtained of Sophia a promise of communicating likewise in her turn, began to relate what the reader, if he is desirous to know her history, may read in the ensuing chapter.\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick, after a silence of a few moments, fetching a deep sigh, thus began:\n\n“It is natural to the unhappy to feel a secret concern in recollecting those periods of their lives which have been most delightful to them. The remembrance of past pleasures affects us with a kind of tender grief, like what we suffer for departed friends; and the ideas of both may be said to haunt our imaginations.\n\n“For this reason, I never reflect without sorrow on those days (the happiest far of my life) which we spent together when both were under the care of my aunt Western. Alas! why are Miss Graveairs and Miss Giddy no more? You remember, I am sure, when we knew each other by no other names. Indeed, you gave the latter appellation with too much cause. I have since experienced how much I deserved it. You, my Sophia, was always my superior in everything, and I heartily hope you will be so in your fortune. I shall never forget the wise and matronly advice you once gave me, when I lamented being disappointed of a ball, though you could not be then fourteen years old.----O my Sophy, how blest must have been my situation, when I could think such a disappointment a misfortune; and when indeed it was the greatest I had ever known!”\n\n“And yet, my dear Harriet,” answered Sophia, “it was then a serious matter with you. Comfort yourself therefore with thinking, that whatever you now lament may hereafter appear as trifling and contemptible as a ball would at this time.”\n\n“Alas, my Sophia,” replied the other lady, “you yourself will think otherwise of my present situation; for greatly must that tender heart be altered if my misfortunes do not draw many a sigh, nay, many a tear, from you. The knowledge of this should perhaps deter me from relating what I am convinced will so much affect you.” Here Mrs Fitzpatrick stopt, till, at the repeated entreaties of Sophia, she thus proceeded:\n\n“Though you must have heard much of my marriage; yet, as matters may probably have been misrepresented, I will set out from the very commencement of my unfortunate acquaintance with my present husband; which was at Bath, soon after you left my aunt, and returned home to your father.\n\n“Among the gay young fellows who were at this season at Bath, Mr Fitzpatrick was one. He was handsome, dégagé, extremely gallant, and in his dress exceeded most others. In short, my dear, if you was unluckily to see him now, I could describe him no better than by telling you he was the very reverse of everything which he is: for he hath rusticated himself so long, that he is become an absolute wild Irishman. But to proceed in my story: the qualifications which he then possessed so well recommended him, that, though the people of quality at that time lived separate from the rest of the company, and excluded them from all their parties, Mr Fitzpatrick found means to gain admittance. It was perhaps no easy matter to avoid him; for he required very little or no invitation; and as, being handsome and genteel, he found it no very difficult matter to ingratiate himself with the ladies, so, he having frequently drawn his sword, the men did not care publickly to affront him. Had it not been for some such reason, I believe he would have been soon expelled by his own sex; for surely he had no strict title to be preferred to the English gentry; nor did they seem inclined to show him any extraordinary favour. They all abused him behind his back, which might probably proceed from envy; for by the women he was well received, and very particularly distinguished by them.\n\n“My aunt, though no person of quality herself, as she had always lived about the court, was enrolled in that party; for, by whatever means you get into the polite circle, when you are once there, it is sufficient merit for you that you are there. This observation, young as you was, you could scarce avoid making from my aunt, who was free, or reserved, with all people, just as they had more or less of this merit.\n\n“And this merit, I believe, it was, which principally recommended Mr Fitzpatrick to her favour. In which he so well succeeded, that he was always one of her private parties. Nor was he backward in returning such distinction; for he soon grew so very particular in his behaviour to her, that the scandal club first began to take notice of it, and the better-disposed persons made a match between them. For my own part, I confess, I made no doubt but that his designs were strictly honourable, as the phrase is; that is, to rob a lady of her fortune by way of marriage. My aunt was, I conceived, neither young enough nor handsome enough to attract much wicked inclination; but she had matrimonial charms in great abundance.\n\n“I was the more confirmed in this opinion from the extraordinary respect which he showed to myself from the first moment of our acquaintance. This I understood as an attempt to lessen, if possible, that disinclination which my interest might be supposed to give me towards the match; and I know not but in some measure it had that effect; for, as I was well contented with my own fortune, and of all people the least a slave to interested views, so I could not be violently the enemy of a man with whose behaviour to me I was greatly pleased; and the more so, as I was the only object of such respect; for he behaved at the same time to many women of quality without any respect at all.\n\n“Agreeable as this was to me, he soon changed it into another kind of behaviour, which was perhaps more so. He now put on much softness and tenderness, and languished and sighed abundantly. At times, indeed, whether from art or nature I will not determine, he gave his usual loose to gaiety and mirth; but this was always in general company, and with other women; for even in a country-dance, when he was not my partner, he became grave, and put on the softest look imaginable the moment he approached me. Indeed he was in all things so very particular towards me, that I must have been blind not to have discovered it. And, and, and----” “And you was more pleased still, my dear Harriet,” cries Sophia; “you need not be ashamed,” added she, sighing; “for sure there are irresistible charms in tenderness, which too many men are able to affect.” “True,” answered her cousin; “men, who in all other instances want common sense, are very Machiavels in the art of loving. I wish I did not know an instance.--Well, scandal now began to be as busy with me as it had before been with my aunt; and some good ladies did not scruple to affirm that Mr Fitzpatrick had an intrigue with us both.\n\n“But, what may seem astonishing, my aunt never saw, nor in the least seemed to suspect, that which was visible enough, I believe, from both our behaviours. One would indeed think that love quite puts out the eyes of an old woman. In fact, they so greedily swallow the addresses which are made to them, that, like an outrageous glutton, they are not at leisure to observe what passes amongst others at the same table. This I have observed in more cases than my own; and this was so strongly verified by my aunt, that, though she often found us together at her return from the pump, the least canting word of his, pretending impatience at her absence, effectually smothered all suspicion. One artifice succeeded with her to admiration. This was his treating me like a little child, and never calling me by any other name in her presence but that of pretty miss. This indeed did him some disservice with your humble servant; but I soon saw through it, especially as in her absence he behaved to me, as I have said, in a different manner. However, if I was not greatly disobliged by a conduct of which I had discovered the design, I smarted very severely for it; for my aunt really conceived me to be what her lover (as she thought him) called me, and treated me in all respects as a perfect infant. To say the truth, I wonder she had not insisted on my again wearing leading-strings.\n\n“At last, my lover (for so he was) thought proper, in a most solemn manner, to disclose a secret which I had known long before. He now placed all the love which he had pretended to my aunt to my account. He lamented, in very pathetic terms, the encouragement she had given him, and made a high merit of the tedious hours in which he had undergone her conversation.--What shall I tell you, my dear Sophia?--Then I will confess the truth. I was pleased with my man. I was pleased with my conquest. To rival my aunt delighted me; to rival so many other women charmed me. In short, I am afraid I did not behave as I should do, even upon the very first declaration--I wish I did not almost give him positive encouragement before we parted.\n\n“The Bath now talked loudly--I might almost say, roared against me. Several young women affected to shun my acquaintance, not so much, perhaps, from any real suspicion, as from a desire of banishing me from a company in which I too much engrossed their favourite man. And here I cannot omit expressing my gratitude to the kindness intended me by Mr Nash, who took me one day aside, and gave me advice, which if I had followed, I had been a happy woman. `Child,' says he, `I am sorry to see the familiarity which subsists between you and a fellow who is altogether unworthy of you, and I am afraid will prove your ruin. As for your old stinking aunt, if it was to be no injury to you and my pretty Sophy Western (I assure you I repeat his words), I should be heartily glad that the fellow was in possession of all that belongs to her. I never advise old women: for, if they take it into their heads to go to the devil, it is no more possible than worth while to keep them from him. Innocence and youth and beauty are worthy a better fate, and I would save them from his clutches. Let me advise you therefore, dear child, never suffer this fellow to be particular with you again.' Many more things he said to me, which I have now forgotten, and indeed I attended very little to them at the time; for inclination contradicted all he said; and, besides, I could not be persuaded that women of quality would condescend to familiarity with such a person as he described.\n\n“But I am afraid, my dear, I shall tire you with a detail of so many minute circumstances. To be concise, therefore, imagine me married; imagine me with my husband, at the feet of my aunt; and then imagine the maddest woman in Bedlam, in a raving fit, and your imagination will suggest to you no more than what really happened.\n\n“The very next day my aunt left the place, partly to avoid seeing Mr Fitzpatrick or myself, and as much perhaps to avoid seeing any one else; for, though I am told she hath since denied everything stoutly, I believe she was then a little confounded at her disappointment. Since that time, I have written to her many letters, but never could obtain an answer, which I must own sits somewhat the heavier, as she herself was, though undesignedly, the occasion of all my sufferings: for, had it not been under the colour of paying his addresses to her, Mr Fitzpatrick would never have found sufficient opportunities to have engaged my heart, which, in other circumstances, I still flatter myself would not have been an easy conquest to such a person. Indeed, I believe I should not have erred so grossly in my choice if I had relied on my own judgment; but I trusted totally to the opinion of others, and very foolishly took the merit of a man for granted whom I saw so universally well received by the women. What is the reason, my dear, that we, who have understandings equal to the wisest and greatest of the other sex, so often make choice of the silliest fellows for companions and favourites? It raises my indignation to the highest pitch to reflect on the numbers of women of sense who have been undone by fools.” Here she paused a moment; but, Sophia making no answer, she proceeded as in the next chapter.\n\nIn which the history of Mrs Fitzpatrick is continued.\n\n“We remained at Bath no longer than a fortnight after our wedding; for as to any reconciliation with my aunt, there were no hopes; and of my fortune not one farthing could be touched till I was of age, of which I now wanted more than two years. My husband therefore was resolved to set out for Ireland; against which I remonstrated very earnestly, and insisted on a promise which he had made me before our marriage that I should never take this journey against my consent; and indeed I never intended to consent to it; nor will anybody, I believe, blame me for that resolution; but this, however, I never mentioned to my husband, and petitioned only for the reprieve of a month; but he had fixed the day, and to that day he obstinately adhered.\n\n“The evening before our departure, as we were disputing this point with great eagerness on both sides, he started suddenly from his chair, and left me abruptly, saying he was going to the rooms. He was hardly out of the house when I saw a paper lying on the floor, which, I suppose, he had carelessly pulled from his pocket, together with his handkerchief. This paper I took up, and, finding it to be a letter, I made no scruple to open and read it; and indeed I read it so often that I can repeat it to you almost word for word. This then was the letter:\n\n'To Mr Brian Fitzpatrick.\n\n'YOURS received, and am surprized you should use me in this manner, as have never seen any of your cash, unless for one linsey-woolsey coat, and your bill now is upwards of £150. Consider, sir, how often you have fobbed me off with your being shortly to be married to this lady and t'other lady; but I can neither live on hopes or promises, nor will my woollen-draper take any such in payment. You tell me you are secure of having either the aunt or the niece, and that you might have married the aunt before this, whose jointure you say is immense, but that you prefer the niece on account of her ready money. Pray, sir, take a fool's advice for once, and marry the first you can get. You will pardon my offering my advice, as you know I sincerely wish you well. Shall draw on you per next post, in favour of Messieurs John Drugget and company, at fourteen days, which doubt not your honouring, and am,\n\nSir, your humble servant, 'SAM. COSGRAVE.'\n\n“This was the letter, word for word. Guess, my dear girl--guess how this letter affected me. You prefer the niece on account of her ready money! If every one of these words had been a dagger, I could with pleasure have stabbed them into his heart; but I will not recount my frantic behaviour on the occasion. I had pretty well spent my tears before his return home; but sufficient remains of them appeared in my swollen eyes. He threw himself sullenly into his chair, and for a long time we were both silent. At length, in a haughty tone, he said, `I hope, madam, your servants have packed up all your things; for the coach will be ready by six in the morning.' My patience was totally subdued by this provocation, and I answered, `No, sir, there is a letter still remains unpacked;' and then throwing it on the table I fell to upbraiding him with the most bitter language I could invent.\n\n“Whether guilt, or shame, or prudence, restrained him I cannot say; but, though he is the most passionate of men, he exerted no rage on this occasion. He endeavoured, on the contrary, to pacify me by the most gentle means. He swore the phrase in the letter to which I principally objected was not his, nor had he ever written any such. He owned, indeed, the having mentioned his marriage, and that preference which he had given to myself, but denied with many oaths the having mentioned any such matter at all on account of the straits he was in for money, arising, he said, from his having too long neglected his estate in Ireland. And this, he said, which he could not bear to discover to me, was the only reason of his having so strenuously insisted on our journey. He then used several very endearing expressions, and concluded by a very fond caress, and many violent protestations of love.\n\n“There was one circumstance which, though he did not appeal to it, had much weight with me in his favour, and that was the word jointure in the taylor's letter, whereas my aunt never had been married, and this Mr Fitzpatrick well knew.----As I imagined, therefore, that the fellow must have inserted this of his own head, or from hearsay, I persuaded myself he might have ventured likewise on that odious line on no better authority. What reasoning was this, my dear? was I not an advocate rather than a judge?--But why do I mention such a circumstance as this, or appeal to it for the justification of my forgiveness?--In short, had he been guilty of twenty times as much, half the tenderness and fondness which he used would have prevailed on me to have forgiven him. I now made no farther objections to our setting out, which we did the next morning, and in a little more than a week arrived at the seat of Mr Fitzpatrick.\n\n“Your curiosity will excuse me from relating any occurrences which past during our journey; for it would indeed be highly disagreeable to travel it over again, and no less so to you to travel it over with me.\n\n“This seat, then, is an ancient mansion-house: if I was in one of those merry humours in which you have so often seen me, I could describe it to you ridiculously enough. It looked as if it had been formerly inhabited by a gentleman. Here was room enough, and not the less room on account of the furniture; for indeed there was very little in it. An old woman, who seemed coeval with the building, and greatly resembled her whom Chamont mentions in the Orphan, received us at the gate, and in a howl scarce human, and to me unintelligible, welcomed her master home. In short, the whole scene was so gloomy and melancholy, that it threw my spirits into the lowest dejection; which my husband discerning, instead of relieving, encreased by two or three malicious observations. `There are good houses, madam,' says he, `as you find, in other places besides England; but perhaps you had rather be in a dirty lodgings at Bath.'\n\n“Happy, my dear, is the woman who, in any state of life, hath a cheerful good-natured companion to support and comfort her! But why do I reflect on happy situations only to aggravate my own misery? my companion, far from clearing up the gloom of solitude, soon convinced me that I must have been wretched with him in any place, and in any condition. In a word, he was a surly fellow, a character perhaps you have never seen; for, indeed, no woman ever sees it exemplified but in a father, a brother, or a husband; and, though you have a father, he is not of that character. This surly fellow had formerly appeared to me the very reverse, and so he did still to every other person. Good heaven! how is it possible for a man to maintain a constant lie in his appearance abroad and in company, and to content himself with shewing disagreeable truth only at home? Here, my dear, they make themselves amends for the uneasy restraint which they put on their tempers in the world; for I have observed, the more merry and gay and good-humoured my husband hath at any time been in company, the more sullen and morose he was sure to become at our next private meeting. How shall I describe his barbarity? To my fondness he was cold and insensible. My little comical ways, which you, my Sophy, and which others, have called so agreeable, he treated with contempt. In my most serious moments he sung and whistled; and whenever I was thoroughly dejected and miserable he was angry, and abused me: for, though he was never pleased with my good-humour, nor ascribed it to my satisfaction in him, yet my low spirits always offended him, and those he imputed to my repentance of having (as he said) married an Irishman.\n\n“You will easily conceive, my dear Graveairs (I ask your pardon, I really forgot myself), that, when a woman makes an imprudent match in the sense of the world, that is, when she is not an arrant prostitute to pecuniary interest, she must necessarily have some inclination and affection for her man. You will as easily believe that this affection may possibly be lessened; nay, I do assure you, contempt will wholly eradicate it. This contempt I now began to entertain for my husband, whom I now discovered to be--I must use the expression--an arrant blockhead. Perhaps you will wonder I did not make this discovery long before; but women will suggest a thousand excuses to themselves for the folly of those they like: besides, give me leave to tell you, it requires a most penetrating eye to discern a fool through the disguises of gaiety and good breeding.\n\n“It will be easily imagined that, when I once despised my husband, as I confess to you I soon did, I must consequently dislike his company; and indeed I had the happiness of being very little troubled with it; for our house was now most elegantly furnished, our cellars well stocked, and dogs and horses provided in great abundance. As my gentleman therefore entertained his neighbours with great hospitality, so his neighbours resorted to him with great alacrity; and sports and drinking consumed so much of his time, that a small part of his conversation, that is to say, of his ill-humours, fell to my share.\n\n“Happy would it have been for me if I could as easily have avoided all other disagreeable company; but, alas! I was confined to some which constantly tormented me; and the more, as I saw no prospect of being relieved from them. These companions were my own racking thoughts, which plagued and in a manner haunted me night and day. In this situation I past through a scene, the horrors of which can neither be painted nor imagined. Think, my dear, figure, if you can, to yourself, what I must have undergone. I became a mother by the man I scorned, hated, and detested. I went through all the agonies and miseries of a lying-in (ten times more painful in such a circumstance than the worst labour can be when one endures it for a man one loves) in a desert, or rather, indeed, a scene of riot and revel, without a friend, without a companion, or without any of those agreeable circumstances which often alleviate, and perhaps sometimes more than compensate, the sufferings of our sex at that season.”\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nIn which the mistake of the landlord throws Sophia into a dreadful consternation.\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick was proceeding in her narrative when she was interrupted by the entrance of dinner, greatly to the concern of Sophia; for the misfortunes of her friend had raised her anxiety, and left her no appetite but what Mrs Fitzpatrick was to satisfy by her relation.\n\nThe landlord now attended with a plate under his arm, and with the same respect in his countenance and address which he would have put on had the ladies arrived in a coach and six.\n\nThe married lady seemed less affected with her own misfortunes than was her cousin; for the former eat very heartily, whereas the latter could hardly swallow a morsel. Sophia likewise showed more concern and sorrow in her countenance than appeared in the other lady; who, having observed these symptoms in her friend, begged her to be comforted, saying, “Perhaps all may yet end better than either you or I expect.”\n\nOur landlord thought he had now an opportunity to open his mouth, and was resolved not to omit it. “I am sorry, madam,” cries he, “that your ladyship can't eat; for to be sure you must be hungry after so long fasting. I hope your ladyship is not uneasy at anything, for, as madam there says, all may end better than anybody expects. A gentleman who was here just now brought excellent news; and perhaps some folks who have given other folks the slip may get to London before they are overtaken; and if they do, I make no doubt but they will find people who will be very ready to receive them.”\n\nAll persons under the apprehension of danger convert whatever they see and hear into the objects of that apprehension. Sophia therefore immediately concluded, from the foregoing speech, that she was known, and pursued by her father. She was now struck with the utmost consternation, and for a few minutes deprived of the power of speech; which she no sooner recovered than she desired the landlord to send his servants out of the room, and then, addressing herself to him, said, “I perceive, sir, you know who we are; but I beseech you--nay, I am convinced, if you have any compassion or goodness, you will not betray us.”\n\n“I betray your ladyship!” quoth the landlord; “no (and then he swore several very hearty oaths); I would sooner be cut into ten thousand pieces. I hate all treachery. I! I never betrayed any one in my life yet, and I am sure I shall not begin with so sweet a lady as your ladyship. All the world would very much blame me if I should, since it will be in your ladyship's power so shortly to reward me. My wife can witness for me, I knew your ladyship the moment you came into the house: I said it was your honour, before I lifted you from your horse, and I shall carry the bruises I got in your ladyship's service to the grave; but what signified that, as long as I saved your ladyship? To be sure some people this morning would have thought of getting a reward; but no such thought ever entered into my head. I would sooner starve than take any reward for betraying your ladyship.”\n\n“I promise you, sir,” says Sophia, “if it be ever in my power to reward you, you shall not lose by your generosity.”\n\n“Alack-a-day, madam!” answered the landlord; “in your ladyship's power! Heaven put it as much into your will! I am only afraid your honour will forget such a poor man as an innkeeper; but, if your ladyship should not, I hope you will remember what reward I refused--refused! that is, I would have refused, and to be sure it may be called refusing, for I might have had it certainly; and to be sure you might have been in some houses;--but, for my part, would not methinks for the world have your ladyship wrong me so much as to imagine I ever thought of betraying you, even before I heard the good news.”\n\n“What news, pray?” says Sophia, something eagerly.\n\n“Hath not your ladyship heard it, then?” cries the landlord; “nay, like enough, for I heard it only a few minutes ago; and if I had never heard it, may the devil fly away with me this instant if I would have betrayed your honour! no, if I would, may I--” Here he subjoined several dreadful imprecations, which Sophia at last interrupted, and begged to know what he meant by the news.--He was going to answer, when Mrs Honour came running into the room, all pale and breathless, and cried out, “Madam, we are all undone, all ruined, they are come, they are come!” These words almost froze up the blood of Sophia; but Mrs Fitzpatrick asked Honour who were come?--“Who?” answered she, “why, the French; several hundred thousands of them are landed, and we shall be all murdered and ravished.”\n\nAs a miser, who hath, in some well-built city, a cottage, value twenty shillings, when at a distance he is alarmed with the news of a fire, turns pale and trembles at his loss; but when he finds the beautiful palaces only are burnt, and his own cottage remains safe, he comes instantly to himself, and smiles at his good fortunes: or as (for we dislike something in the former simile) the tender mother, when terrified with the apprehension that her darling boy is drowned, is struck senseless and almost dead with consternation; but when she is told that little master is safe, and the Victory only, with twelve hundred brave men, gone to the bottom, life and sense again return, maternal fondness enjoys the sudden relief from all its fears, and the general benevolence which at another time would have deeply felt the dreadful catastrophe, lies fast asleep in her mind;--so Sophia, than whom none was more capable of tenderly feeling the general calamity of her country, found such immediate satisfaction from the relief of those terrors she had of being overtaken by her father, that the arrival of the French scarce made any impression on her. She gently chid her maid for the fright into which she had thrown her, and said “she was glad it was no worse; for that she had feared somebody else was come.”\n\n“Ay, ay,” quoth the landlord, smiling, “her ladyship knows better things; she knows the French are our very best friends, and come over hither only for our good. They are the people who are to make Old England flourish again. I warrant her honour thought the duke was coming; and that was enough to put her into a fright. I was going to tell your ladyship the news.--His honour's majesty, Heaven bless him, hath given the duke the slip, and is marching as fast as he can to London, and ten thousand French are landed to join him on the road.”\n\nSophia was not greatly pleased with this news, nor with the gentleman who related it; but, as she still imagined he knew her (for she could not possibly have any suspicion of the real truth), she durst not show any dislike. And now the landlord, having removed the cloth from the table, withdrew; but at his departure frequently repeated his hopes of being remembered hereafter.\n\nThe mind of Sophia was not at all easy under the supposition of being known at this house; for she still applied to herself many things which the landlord had addressed to Jenny Cameron; she therefore ordered her maid to pump out of him by what means he had become acquainted with her person, and who had offered him the reward for betraying her; she likewise ordered the horses to be in readiness by four in the morning, at which hour Mrs Fitzpatrick promised to bear her company; and then, composing herself as well as she could, she desired that lady to continue her story.\n\nIn which Mrs Fitzpatrick concludes her history.\n\nWhile Mrs Honour, in pursuance of the commands of her mistress, ordered a bowl of punch, and invited my landlord and landlady to partake of it, Mrs Fitzpatrick thus went on with her relation.\n\n“Most of the officers who were quartered at a town in our neighbourhood were of my husband's acquaintance. Among these there was a lieutenant, a very pretty sort of man, and who was married to a woman, so agreeable both in her temper and conversation, that from our first knowing each other, which was soon after my lying-in, we were almost inseparable companions; for I had the good fortune to make myself equally agreeable to her.\n\n“The lieutenant, who was neither a sot nor a sportsman, was frequently of our parties; indeed he was very little with my husband, and no more than good breeding constrained him to be, as he lived almost constantly at our house. My husband often expressed much dissatisfaction at the lieutenant's preferring my company to his; he was very angry with me on that account, and gave me many a hearty curse for drawing away his companions; saying, `I ought to be d--n'd for having spoiled one of the prettiest fellows in the world, by making a milksop of him.'\n\n“You will be mistaken, my dear Sophia, if you imagine that the anger of my husband arose from my depriving him of a companion; for the lieutenant was not a person with whose society a fool could be pleased; and, if I should admit the possibility of this, so little right had my husband to place the loss of his companion to me, that I am convinced it was my conversation alone which induced him ever to come to the house. No, child, it was envy, the worst and most rancorous kind of envy, the envy of superiority of understanding. The wretch could not bear to see my conversation preferred to his, by a man of whom he could not entertain the least jealousy. O my dear Sophy, you are a woman of sense; if you marry a man, as is most probable you will, of less capacity than yourself, make frequent trials of his temper before marriage, and see whether he can bear to submit to such a superiority.--Promise me, Sophy, you will take this advice; for you will hereafter find its importance.” “It is very likely I shall never marry at all,” answered Sophia; “I think, at least, I shall never marry a man in whose understanding I see any defects before marriage; and I promise you I would rather give up my own than see any such afterwards.” “Give up your understanding!” replied Mrs Fitzpatrick; “oh, fie, child! I will not believe so meanly of you. Everything else I might myself be brought to give up; but never this. Nature would not have allotted this superiority to the wife in so many instances, if she had intended we should all of us have surrendered it to the husband. This, indeed, men of sense never expect of us; of which the lieutenant I have just mentioned was one notable example; for though he had a very good understanding, he always acknowledged (as was really true) that his wife had a better. And this, perhaps, was one reason of the hatred my tyrant bore her.\n\n“Before he would be so governed by a wife, he said, especially such an ugly b-- (for, indeed, she was not a regular beauty, but very agreeable and extremely genteel), he would see all the women upon earth at the devil, which was a very usual phrase with him. He said, he wondered what I could see in her to be so charmed with her company: since this woman, says he, hath come among us, there is an end of your beloved reading, which you pretended to like so much, that you could not afford time to return the visits of the ladies in this country; and I must confess I had been guilty of a little rudeness this way; for the ladies there are at least no better than the mere country ladies here; and I think I need make no other excuse to you for declining any intimacy with them.\n\n“This correspondence, however, continued a whole year, even all the while the lieutenant was quartered in that town; for which I was contented to pay the tax of being constantly abused in the manner above mentioned by my husband; I mean when he was at home; for he was frequently absent a month at a time at Dublin, and once made a journey of two months to London: in all which journeys I thought it a very singular happiness that he never once desired my company; nay, by his frequent censures on men who could not travel, as he phrased it, without a wife tied up to their tail, he sufficiently intimated that, had I been never so desirous of accompanying him, my wishes would have been in vain; but, Heaven knows, such wishes were very far from my thoughts.\n\n“At length my friend was removed from me, and I was again left to my solitude, to the tormenting conversation with my own reflections, and to apply to books for my only comfort. I now read almost all day long. How many books do you think I read in three months?” “I can't guess, indeed, cousin,” answered Sophia. “Perhaps half a score.” “Half a score! half a thousand, child!” answered the other. “I read a good deal in Daniel's English History of France; a great deal in Plutarch's Lives, the Atalantis, Pope's Homer, Dryden's Plays, Chillingworth, the Countess D'Aulnois, and Locke's Human Understanding.\n\n“During this interval I wrote three very supplicating, and, I thought, moving letters to my aunt; but, as I received no answer to any of them, my disdain would not suffer me to continue my application.” Here she stopt, and, looking earnestly at Sophia, said, “Methinks, my dear, I read something in your eyes which reproaches me of a neglect in another place, where I should have met with a kinder return.” “Indeed, dear Harriet,” answered Sophia, “your story is an apology for any neglect; but, indeed, I feel that I have been guilty of a remissness, without so good an excuse.--Yet pray proceed; for I long, though I tremble, to hear the end.”\n\nThus, then, Mrs Fitzpatrick resumed her narrative:--“My husband now took a second journey to England, where he continued upwards of three months; during the greater part of this time I led a life which nothing but having led a worse could make me think tolerable; for perfect solitude can never be reconciled to a social mind, like mine, but when it relieves you from the company of those you hate. What added to my wretchedness was the loss of my little infant: not that I pretend to have had for it that extravagant tenderness of which I believe I might have been capable under other circumstances; but I resolved, in every instance, to discharge the duty of the tenderest mother; and this care prevented me from feeling the weight of that heaviest of all things, when it can be at all said to lie heavy on our hands.\n\n“I had spent full ten weeks almost entirely by myself, having seen nobody all that time, except my servants and a very few visitors, when a young lady, a relation to my husband, came from a distant part of Ireland to visit me. She had staid once before a week at my house, and then I gave her a pressing invitation to return; for she was a very agreeable woman, and had improved good natural parts by a proper education. Indeed, she was to me a welcome guest.\n\n“A few days after her arrival, perceiving me in very low spirits, without enquiring the cause, which, indeed, she very well knew, the young lady fell to compassionating my case. She said, `Though politeness had prevented me from complaining to my husband's relations of his behaviour, yet they all were very sensible of it, and felt great concern upon that account; but none more than herself.' And after some more general discourse on this head, which I own I could not forbear countenancing, at last, after much previous precaution and enjoined concealment, she communicated to me, as a profound secret--that my husband kept a mistress.\n\n“You will certainly imagine I heard this news with the utmost insensibility--Upon my word, if you do, your imagination will mislead you. Contempt had not so kept down my anger to my husband, but that hatred rose again on this occasion. What can be the reason of this? Are we so abominably selfish, that we can be concerned at others having possession even of what we despise? Or are we not rather abominably vain, and is not this the greatest injury done to our vanity? What think you, Sophia?”\n\n“I don't know, indeed,” answered Sophia; “I have never troubled myself with any of these deep contemplations; but I think the lady did very ill in communicating to you such a secret.”\n\n“And yet, my dear, this conduct is natural,” replied Mrs Fitzpatrick; “and, when you have seen and read as much as myself, you will acknowledge it to be so.”\n\n“I am sorry to hear it is natural,” returned Sophia; “for I want neither reading nor experience to convince me that it is very dishonourable and very ill-natured: nay, it is surely as ill-bred to tell a husband or wife of the faults of each other as to tell them of their own.”\n\n“Well,” continued Mrs Fitzpatrick, “my husband at last returned; and, if I am thoroughly acquainted with my own thoughts, I hated him now more than ever; but I despised him rather less: for certainly nothing so much weakens our contempt, as an injury done to our pride or our vanity.\n\n“He now assumed a carriage to me so very different from what he had lately worn, and so nearly resembling his behaviour the first week of our marriage, that, had I now had any spark of love remaining, he might, possibly, have rekindled my fondness for him. But, though hatred may succeed to contempt, and may perhaps get the better of it, love, I believe, cannot. The truth is, the passion of love is too restless to remain contented without the gratification which it receives from its object; and one can no more be inclined to love without loving than we can have eyes without seeing. When a husband, therefore, ceases to be the object of this passion, it is most probable some other man--I say, my dear, if your husband grows indifferent to you--if you once come to despise him--I say--that is--if you have the passion of love in you--Lud! I have bewildered myself so--but one is apt, in these abstracted considerations, to lose the concatenation of ideas, as Mr Locke says:--in short, the truth is--in short, I scarce know what it is; but, as I was saying, my husband returned, and his behaviour, at first, greatly surprized me; but he soon acquainted me with the motive, and taught me to account for it. In a word, then, he had spent and lost all the ready money of my fortune; and, as he could mortgage his own estate no deeper, he was now desirous to supply himself with cash for his extravagance, by selling a little estate of mine, which he could not do without my assistance; and to obtain this favour was the whole and sole motive of all the fondness which he now put on.\n\n“With this I peremptorily refused to comply. I told him, and I told him truly, that, had I been possessed of the Indies at our first marriage, he might have commanded it all; for it had been a constant maxim with me, that where a woman disposes of her heart, she should always deposit her fortune; but, as he had been so kind, long ago, to restore the former into my possession, I was resolved likewise to retain what little remained of the latter.\n\n“I will not describe to you the passion into which these words, and the resolute air in which they were spoken, threw him: nor will I trouble you with the whole scene which succeeded between us. Out came, you may be well assured, the story of the mistress; and out it did come, with all the embellishments which anger and disdain could bestow upon it.\n\n“Mr Fitzpatrick seemed a little thunderstruck with this, and more confused than I had seen him, though his ideas are always confused enough, heaven knows. He did not, however, endeavour to exculpate himself; but took a method which almost equally confounded me. What was this but recrimination? He affected to be jealous:--he may, for aught I know, be inclined enough to jealousy in his natural temper; nay, he must have had it from nature, or the devil must have put it into his head; for I defy all the world to cast a just aspersion on my character: nay, the most scandalous tongues have never dared censure my reputation. My fame, I thank heaven, hath been always as spotless as my life; and let falsehood itself accuse that if it dare. No, my dear Graveairs, however provoked, however ill-treated, however injured in my love, I have firmly resolved never to give the least room for censure on this account.--And yet, my dear, there are some people so malicious, some tongues so venomous, that no innocence can escape them. The most undesigned word, the most accidental look, the least familiarity, the most innocent freedom, will be misconstrued, and magnified into I know not what, by some people. But I despise, my dear Graveairs, I despise all such slander. No such malice, I assure you, ever gave me an uneasy moment. No, no, I promise you I am above all that.--But where was I? O let me see, I told you my husband was jealous--And of whom, I pray?--Why, of whom but the lieutenant I mentioned to you before! He was obliged to resort above a year and more back to find any object for this unaccountable passion, if, indeed, he really felt any such, and was not an arrant counterfeit in order to abuse me.\n\n“But I have tired you already with too many particulars. I will now bring my story to a very speedy conclusion. In short, then, after many scenes very unworthy to be repeated, in which my cousin engaged so heartily on my side, that Mr Fitzpatrick at last turned her out of doors; when he found I was neither to be soothed nor bullied into compliance, he took a very violent method indeed. Perhaps you will conclude he beat me; but this, though he hath approached very near to it, he never actually did. He confined me to my room, without suffering me to have either pen, ink, paper, or book: and a servant every day made my bed, and brought me my food.\n\n“When I had remained a week under this imprisonment, he made me a visit, and, with the voice of a schoolmaster, or, what is often much the same, of a tyrant, asked me, `If I would yet comply?' I answered, very stoutly, `That I would die first.' `Then so you shall, and be d--nd!' cries he; `for you shall never go alive out of this room.'\n\n“Here I remained a fortnight longer; and, to say the truth, my constancy was almost subdued, and I began to think of submission; when, one day, in the absence of my husband, who was gone abroad for some short time, by the greatest good fortune in the world, an accident happened.--I--at a time when I began to give way to the utmost despair----everything would be excusable at such a time--at that very time I received----But it would take up an hour to tell you all particulars.--In one word, then (for I will not tire you with circumstances), gold, the common key to all padlocks, opened my door, and set me at liberty.\n\n“I now made haste to Dublin, where I immediately procured a passage to England; and was proceeding to Bath, in order to throw myself into the protection of my aunt, or of your father, or of any relation who would afford it me. My husband overtook me last night at the inn where I lay, and which you left a few minutes before me; but I had the good luck to escape him, and to follow you.\n\n“And thus, my dear, ends my history: a tragical one, I am sure, it is to myself; but, perhaps, I ought rather to apologize to you for its dullness.”\n\nSophia heaved a deep sigh, and answered, “Indeed, Harriet, I pity you from my soul!----But what could you expect? Why, why, would you marry an Irishman?”\n\n“Upon my word,” replied her cousin, “your censure is unjust. There are, among the Irish, men of as much worth and honour as any among the English: nay, to speak the truth, generosity of spirit is rather more common among them. I have known some examples there, too, of good husbands; and I believe these are not very plenty in England. Ask me, rather, what I could expect when I married a fool; and I will tell you a solemn truth; I did not know him to be so.”--“Can no man,” said Sophia, in a very low and altered voice, “do you think, make a bad husband, who is not a fool?” “That,” answered the other, “is too general a negative; but none, I believe, is so likely as a fool to prove so. Among my acquaintance, the silliest fellows are the worst husbands; and I will venture to assert, as a fact, that a man of sense rarely behaves very ill to a wife who deserves very well.”\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nA dreadful alarm in the inn, with the arrival of an unexpected friend of Mrs Fitzpatrick.\nSophia now, at the desire of her cousin, related--not what follows, but what hath gone before in this history: for which reason the reader will, I suppose, excuse me for not repeating it over again.\n\nOne remark, however, I cannot forbear making on her narrative, namely, that she made no more mention of Jones, from the beginning to the end, than if there had been no such person alive. This I will neither endeavour to account for nor to excuse. Indeed, if this may be called a kind of dishonesty, it seems the more inexcusable, from the apparent openness and explicit sincerity of the other lady.--But so it was.\n\nJust as Sophia arrived at the conclusion of her story, there arrived in the room where the two ladies were sitting a noise, not unlike, in loudness, to that of a pack of hounds just let out from their kennel; nor, in shrillness, to cats, when caterwauling; or to screech owls; or, indeed, more like (for what animal can resemble a human voice?) to those sounds which, in the pleasant mansions of that gate which seems to derive its name from a duplicity of tongues, issue from the mouths, and sometimes from the nostrils, of those fair river nymphs, ycleped of old the Naïades; in the vulgar tongue translated oyster-wenches; for when, instead of the antient libations of milk and honey and oil, the rich distillation from the juniper-berry, or, perhaps, from malt, hath, by the early devotion of their votaries, been poured forth in great abundance, should any daring tongue with unhallowed license prophane, i.e., depreciate, the delicate fat Milton oyster, the plaice sound and firm, the flounder as much alive as when in the water, the shrimp as big as a prawn, the fine cod alive but a few hours ago, or any other of the various treasures which those water-deities who fish the sea and rivers have committed to the care of the nymphs, the angry Naïades lift up their immortal voices, and the prophane wretch is struck deaf for his impiety.\n\nSuch was the noise which now burst from one of the rooms below; and soon the thunder, which long had rattled at a distance, began to approach nearer and nearer, till, having ascended by degrees upstairs, it at last entered the apartment where the ladies were. In short, to drop all metaphor and figure, Mrs Honour, having scolded violently below-stairs, and continued the same all the way up, came in to her mistress in a most outrageous passion, crying out, “What doth your ladyship think? Would you imagine that this impudent villain, the master of this house, hath had the impudence to tell me, nay, to stand it out to my face, that your ladyship is that nasty, stinking wh--re (Jenny Cameron they call her), that runs about the country with the Pretender? Nay, the lying, saucy villain had the assurance to tell me that your ladyship had owned yourself to be so; but I have clawed the rascal; I have left the marks of my nails in his impudent face. My lady! says I, you saucy scoundrel; my lady is meat for no pretenders. She is a young lady of as good fashion, and family, and fortune, as any in Somersetshire. Did you never hear of the great Squire Western, sirrah? She is his only daughter; she is----, and heiress to all his great estate. My lady to be called a nasty Scotch wh--re by such a varlet!--To be sure I wish I had knocked his brains out with the punch-bowl.”\n\nThe principal uneasiness with which Sophia was affected on this occasion Honour had herself caused, by having in her passion discovered who she was. However, as this mistake of the landlord sufficiently accounted for those passages which Sophia had before mistaken, she acquired some ease on that account; nor could she, upon the whole, forbear smiling. This enraged Honour, and she cried, “Indeed, madam, I did not think your ladyship would have made a laughing matter of it. To be called whore by such an impudent low rascal. Your ladyship may be angry with me, for aught I know, for taking your part, since proffered service, they say, stinks; but to be sure I could never bear to hear a lady of mine called whore.--Nor will I bear it. I am sure your ladyship is as virtuous a lady as ever sat foot on English ground, and I will claw any villain's eyes out who dares for to offer to presume for to say the least word to the contrary. Nobody ever could say the least ill of the character of any lady that ever I waited upon.”\n\nHinc illae lachrymae; in plain truth, Honour had as much love for her mistress as most servants have, that is to say--But besides this, her pride obliged her to support the character of the lady she waited on; for she thought her own was in a very close manner connected with it. In proportion as the character of her mistress was raised, hers likewise, as she conceived, was raised with it; and, on the contrary, she thought the one could not be lowered without the other.\n\nOn this subject, reader, I must stop a moment, to tell thee a story. “The famous Nell Gwynn, stepping one day, from a house where she had made a short visit, into her coach, saw a great mob assembled, and her footman all bloody and dirty; the fellow, being asked by his mistress the reason of his being in that condition, answered, `I have been fighting, madam, with an impudent rascal who called your ladyship a wh--re.' `You blockhead,' replied Mrs Gwynn, `at this rate you must fight every day of your life; why, you fool, all the world knows it.' `Do they?' cries the fellow, in a muttering voice, after he had shut the coach-door, `they shan't call me a whore's footman for all that.'”\n\nThus the passion of Mrs Honour appears natural enough, even if it were to be no otherwise accounted for; but, in reality, there was another cause of her anger; for which we must beg leave to remind our reader of a circumstance mentioned in the above simile. There are indeed certain liquors, which, being applied to our passions, or to fire, produce effects the very reverse of those produced by water, as they serve to kindle and inflame, rather than to extinguish. Among these, the generous liquor called punch is one. It was not, therefore, without reason, that the learned Dr Cheney used to call drinking punch pouring liquid fire down your throat.\n\nNow, Mrs Honour had unluckily poured so much of this liquid fire down her throat, that the smoke of it began to ascend into her pericranium and blinded the eyes of Reason, which is there supposed to keep her residence, while the fire itself from the stomach easily reached the heart, and there inflamed the noble passion of pride. So that, upon the whole, we shall cease to wonder at the violent rage of the waiting-woman; though at first sight we must confess the cause seems inadequate to the effect.\n\nSophia and her cousin both did all in their power to extinguish these flames which had roared so loudly all over the house. They at length prevailed; or, to carry the metaphor one step farther, the fire, having consumed all the fuel which the language affords, to wit, every reproachful term in it, at last went out of its own accord.\n\nBut, though tranquillity was restored above-stairs, it was not so below; where my landlady, highly resenting the injury done to the beauty of her husband by the flesh-spades of Mrs Honour, called aloud for revenge and justice. As to the poor man, who had principally suffered in the engagement, he was perfectly quiet. Perhaps the blood which he lost might have cooled his anger: for the enemy had not only applied her nails to his cheeks, but likewise her fist to his nostrils, which lamented the blow with tears of blood in great abundance. To this we may add reflections on his mistake; but indeed nothing so effectually silenced his resentment as the manner in which he now discovered his error; for as to the behaviour of Mrs Honour, it had the more confirmed him in his opinion; but he was now assured by a person of great figure, and who was attended by a great equipage, that one of the ladies was a woman of fashion, and his intimate acquaintance.\n\nBy the orders of this person, the landlord now ascended, and acquainted our fair travellers that a great gentleman below desired to do them the honour of waiting on them. Sophia turned pale and trembled at this message, though the reader will conclude it was too civil, notwithstanding the landlord's blunder, to have come from her father; but fear hath the common fault of a justice of peace, and is apt to conclude hastily from every slight circumstance, without examining the evidence on both sides.\n\nTo ease the reader's curiosity, therefore, rather than his apprehensions, we proceed to inform him that an Irish peer had arrived very late that evening at the inn, in his way to London. This nobleman, having sallied from his supper at the hurricane before commemorated, had seen the attendant of Mrs Fitzpatrick, and upon a short enquiry, was informed that her lady, with whom he was very particularly acquainted, was above. This information he had no sooner received than he addressed himself to the landlord, pacified him, and sent him upstairs with compliments rather civiller than those which were delivered.\n\nIt may perhaps be wondered at that the waiting-woman herself was not the messenger employed on this occasion; but we are sorry to say she was not at present qualified for that, or indeed for any other office. The rum (for so the landlord chose to call the distillation from malt) had basely taken the advantage of the fatigue which the poor woman had undergone, and had made terrible depredations on her noble faculties, at a time when they were very unable to resist the attack.\n\nWe shall not describe this tragical scene too fully; but we thought ourselves obliged, by that historic integrity which we profess, shortly to hint a matter which we would otherwise have been glad to have spared. Many historians, indeed, for want of this integrity, or of diligence, to say no worse, often leave the reader to find out these little circumstances in the dark, and sometimes to his great confusion and perplexity.\n\nSophia was very soon eased of her causeless fright by the entry of the noble peer, who was not only an intimate acquaintance of Mrs Fitzpatrick, but in reality a very particular friend of that lady. To say truth, it was by his assistance that she had been enabled to escape from her husband; for this nobleman had the same gallant disposition with those renowned knights of whom we read in heroic story, and had delivered many an imprisoned nymph from durance. He was indeed as bitter an enemy to the savage authority too often exercised by husbands and fathers, over the young and lovely of the other sex, as ever knight-errant was to the barbarous power of enchanters; nay, to say truth, I have often suspected that those very enchanters with which romance everywhere abounds were in reality no other than the husbands of those days; and matrimony itself was, perhaps, the enchanted castle in which the nymphs were said to be confined.\n\nThis nobleman had an estate in the neighbourhood of Fitzpatrick, and had been for some time acquainted with the lady. No sooner, therefore, did he hear of her confinement, than he earnestly applied himself to procure her liberty; which he presently effected, not by storming the castle, according to the example of antient heroes, but by corrupting the governor, in conformity with the modern art of war, in which craft is held to be preferable to valour, and gold is found to be more irresistible than either lead or steel.\n\nThis circumstance, however, as the lady did not think it material enough to relate to her friend, we would not at that time impart it to the reader. We rather chose to leave him a while under a supposition that she had found, or coined, or by some very extraordinary, perhaps supernatural means, had possessed herself of the money with which she had bribed her keeper, than to interrupt her narrative by giving a hint of what seemed to her of too little importance to be mentioned.\n\nThe peer, after a short conversation, could not forbear expressing some surprize at meeting the lady in that place; nor could he refrain from telling her he imagined she had been gone to Bath. Mrs Fitzpatrick very freely answered, “That she had been prevented in her purpose by the arrival of a person she need not mention. In short,” says she, “I was overtaken by my husband (for I need not affect to conceal what the world knows too well already). I had the good fortune to escape in a most surprizing manner, and am now going to London with this young lady, who is a near relation of mine, and who hath escaped from as great a tyrant as my own.”\n\nHis lordship, concluding that this tyrant was likewise a husband, made a speech full of compliments to both the ladies, and as full of invectives against his own sex; nor indeed did he avoid some oblique glances at the matrimonial institution itself, and at the unjust powers given by it to man over the more sensible and more meritorious part of the species. He ended his oration with an offer of his protection, and of his coach and six, which was instantly accepted by Mrs Fitzpatrick, and at last, upon her persuasions, by Sophia.\n\nMatters being thus adjusted, his lordship took his leave, and the ladies retired to rest, where Mrs Fitzpatrick entertained her cousin with many high encomiums on the character of the noble peer, and enlarged very particularly on his great fondness for his wife; saying, she believed he was almost the only person of high rank who was entirely constant to the marriage bed. “Indeed,” added she, “my dear Sophy, that is a very rare virtue amongst men of condition. Never expect it when you marry; for, believe me, if you do, you will certainly be deceived.”\n\nA gentle sigh stole from Sophia at these words, which perhaps contributed to form a dream of no very pleasant kind; but, as she never revealed this dream to any one, so the reader cannot expect to see it related here.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\nThe morning introduced in some pretty writing. A stagecoach. The civility of chambermaids. The heroic temper of Sophia. Her generosity. The return to it. The departure of the company, and their arrival at London; with some remarks for the use of travellers.\n\nThose members of society who are born to furnish the blessings of life now began to light their candles, in order to pursue their daily labours for the use of those who are born to enjoy these blessings. The sturdy hind now attends the levee of his fellow-labourer the ox; the cunning artificer, the diligent mechanic, spring from their hard mattress; and now the bonny housemaid begins to repair the disordered drum-room, while the riotous authors of that disorder, in broken interrupted slumbers, tumble and toss, as if the hardness of down disquieted their repose.\n\nIn simple phrase, the clock had no sooner struck seven than the ladies were ready for their journey; and, at their desire, his lordship and his equipage were prepared to attend them.\n\nAnd now a matter of some difficulty arose; and this was how his lordship himself should be conveyed; for though in stage-coaches, where passengers are properly considered as so much luggage, the ingenious coachman stows half a dozen with perfect ease into the place of four; for well he contrives that the fat hostess, or well-fed alderman, may take up no more room than the slim miss, or taper master; it being the nature of guts, when well squeezed, to give way, and to lie in a narrow compass; yet in these vehicles, which are called, for distinction's sake, gentlemen's coaches, though they are often larger than the others, this method of packing is never attempted.\n\nHis lordship would have put a short end to the difficulty, by very gallantly desiring to mount his horse; but Mrs Fitzpatrick would by no means consent to it. It was therefore concluded that the Abigails should, by turns, relieve each other on one of his lordship's horses, which was presently equipped with a side-saddle for that purpose.\n\nEverything being settled at the inn, the ladies discharged their former guides, and Sophia made a present to the landlord, partly to repair the bruise which he had received under herself, and partly on account of what he had suffered under the hands of her enraged waiting-woman. And now Sophia first discovered a loss which gave her some uneasiness; and this was of the hundred-pound bank-bill which her father had given her at their last meeting; and which, within a very inconsiderable trifle, was all the treasure she was at present worth. She searched everywhere, and shook and tumbled all her things to no purpose, the bill was not to be found: and she was at last fully persuaded that she had lost it from her pocket when she had the misfortune of tumbling from her horse in the dark lane, as before recorded: a fact that seemed the more probable, as she now recollected some discomposure in her pockets which had happened at that time, and the great difficulty with which she had drawn forth her handkerchief the very instant before her fall, in order to relieve the distress of Mrs Fitzpatrick.\n\nMisfortunes of this kind, whatever inconveniencies they may be attended with, are incapable of subduing a mind in which there is any strength, without the assistance of avarice. Sophia, therefore, though nothing could be worse timed than this accident at such a season, immediately got the better of her concern, and, with her wonted serenity and cheerfulness of countenance, returned to her company. His lordship conducted the ladies into the vehicle, as he did likewise Mrs Honour, who, after many civilities, and more dear madams, at last yielded to the well-bred importunities of her sister Abigail, and submitted to be complimented with the first ride in the coach; in which indeed she would afterwards have been contented to have pursued her whole journey, had not her mistress, after several fruitless intimations, at length forced her to take her turn on horseback.\n\nThe coach, now having received its company, began to move forwards, attended by many servants, and led by two captains, who had before rode with his lordship, and who would have been dismissed from the vehicle upon a much less worthy occasion than was this of accommodating two ladies. In this they acted only as gentlemen; but they were ready at any time to have performed the office of a footman, or indeed would have condescended lower, for the honour of his lordship's company, and for the convenience of his table.\n\nMy landlord was so pleased with the present he had received from Sophia, that he rather rejoiced in than regretted his bruise or his scratches. The reader will perhaps be curious to know the quantum of this present; but we cannot satisfy his curiosity. Whatever it was, it satisfied the landlord for his bodily hurt; but he lamented he had not known before how little the lady valued her money; “For to be sure,” says he, “one might have charged every article double, and she would have made no cavil at the reckoning.”\n\nHis wife, however, was far from drawing this conclusion; whether she really felt any injury done to her husband more than he did himself, I will not say: certain it is, she was much less satisfied with the generosity of Sophia. “Indeed,” cries she, “my dear, the lady knows better how to dispose of her money than you imagine. She might very well think we should not put up such a business without some satisfaction, and the law would have cost her an infinite deal more than this poor little matter, which I wonder you would take.” “You are always so bloodily wise,” quoth the husband: “it would have cost her more, would it? dost fancy I don't know that as well as thee? but would any of that more, or so much, have come into our pockets? Indeed, if son Tom the lawyer had been alive, I could have been glad to have put such a pretty business into his hands. He would have got a good picking out of it; but I have no relation now who is a lawyer, and why should I go to law for the benefit of strangers?” “Nay, to be sure,” answered she, “you must know best.” “I believe I do,” replied he. “I fancy, when money is to be got, I can smell it out as well as another. Everybody, let me tell you, would not have talked people out of this. Mind that, I say; everybody would not have cajoled this out of her, mind that.” The wife then joined in the applause of her husband's sagacity; and thus ended the short dialogue between them on this occasion.\n\nWe will therefore take our leave of these good people, and attend his lordship and his fair companions, who made such good expedition that they performed a journey of ninety miles in two days, and on the second evening arrived in London, without having encountered any one adventure on the road worthy the dignity of this history to relate. Our pen, therefore, shall imitate the expedition which it describes, and our history shall keep pace with the travellers who are its subject. Good writers will, indeed, do well to imitate the ingenious traveller in this instance, who always proportions his stay at any place to the beauties, elegancies, and curiosities which it affords. At Eshur, at Stowe, at Wilton, at Eastbury, and at Prior's Park, days are too short for the ravished imagination; while we admire the wondrous power of art in improving nature. In some of these, art chiefly engages our admiration; in others, nature and art contend for our applause; but, in the last, the former seems to triumph. Here Nature appears in her richest attire, and Art, dressed with the modestest simplicity, attends her benignant mistress. Here Nature indeed pours forth the choicest treasures which she hath lavished on this world; and here human nature presents you with an object which can be exceeded only in the other.\n\nThe same taste, the same imagination, which luxuriously riots in these elegant scenes, can be amused with objects of far inferior note. The woods, the rivers, the lawns of Devon and of Dorset, attract the eye of the ingenious traveller, and retard his pace, which delay he afterwards compensates by swiftly scouring over the gloomy heath of Bagshot, or that pleasant plain which extends itself westward from Stockbridge, where no other object than one single tree only in sixteen miles presents itself to the view, unless the clouds, in compassion to our tired spirits, kindly open their variegated mansions to our prospect.\n\nNot so travels the money-meditating tradesman, the sagacious justice, the dignified doctor, the warm-clad grazier, with all the numerous offspring of wealth and dulness. On they jog, with equal pace, through the verdant meadows or over the barren heath, their horses measuring four miles and a half per hour with the utmost exactness; the eyes of the beast and of his master being alike directed forwards, and employed in contemplating the same objects in the same manner. With equal rapture the good rider surveys the proudest boasts of the architect, and those fair buildings with which some unknown name hath adorned the rich cloathing town; where heaps of bricks are piled up as a kind of monument to show that heaps of money have been piled there before.\n\nAnd now, reader, as we are in haste to attend our heroine, we will leave to thy sagacity to apply all this to the Boeotian writers, and to those authors who are their opposites. This thou wilt be abundantly able to perform without our aid. Bestir thyself therefore on this occasion; for, though we will always lend thee proper assistance in difficult places, as we do not, like some others, expect thee to use the arts of divination to discover our meaning, yet we shall not indulge thy laziness where nothing but thy own attention is required; for thou art highly mistaken if thou dost imagine that we intended, when we began this great work, to leave thy sagacity nothing to do; or that, without sometimes exercising this talent, thou wilt be able to travel through our pages with any pleasure or profit to thyself.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nContaining a hint or two concerning virtue, and a few more concerning suspicion.\nOur company, being arrived at London, were set down at his lordship's house, where, while they refreshed themselves after the fatigue of their journey, servants were despatched to provide a lodging for the two ladies; for, as her ladyship was not then in town, Mrs Fitzpatrick would by no means consent to accept a bed in the mansion of the peer.\n\nSome readers will, perhaps, condemn this extraordinary delicacy, as I may call it, of virtue, as too nice and scrupulous; but we must make allowances for her situation, which must be owned to have been very ticklish; and, when we consider the malice of censorious tongues, we must allow, if it was a fault, the fault was an excess on the right side, and which every woman who is in the self-same situation will do well to imitate. The most formal appearance of virtue, when it is only an appearance, may, perhaps, in very abstracted considerations, seem to be rather less commendable than virtue itself without this formality; but it will, however, be always more commended; and this, I believe, will be granted by all, that it is necessary, unless in some very particular cases, for every woman to support either the one or the other.\n\nA lodging being prepared, Sophia accompanied her cousin for that evening; but resolved early in the morning to enquire after the lady into whose protection, as we have formerly mentioned, she had determined to throw herself when she quitted her father's house. And this she was the more eager in doing from some observations she had made during her journey in the coach.\n\nNow, as we would by no means fix the odious character of suspicion on Sophia, we are almost afraid to open to our reader the conceits which filled her mind concerning Mrs Fitzpatrick; of whom she certainly entertained at present some doubts; which, as they are very apt to enter into the bosoms of the worst of people, we think proper not to mention more plainly till we have first suggested a word or two to our reader touching suspicion in general.\n\nOf this there have always appeared to me to be two degrees. The first of these I chuse to derive from the heart, as the extreme velocity of its discernment seems to denote some previous inward impulse, and the rather as this superlative degree often forms its own objects; sees what is not, and always more than really exists. This is that quick-sighted penetration whose hawk's eyes no symptom of evil can escape; which observes not only upon the actions, but upon the words and looks, of men; and, as it proceeds from the heart of the observer, so it dives into the heart of the observed, and there espies evil, as it were, in the first embryo; nay, sometimes before it can be said to be conceived. An admirable faculty, if it were infallible; but, as this degree of perfection is not even claimed by more than one mortal being; so from the fallibility of such acute discernment have arisen many sad mischiefs and most grievous heart-aches to innocence and virtue. I cannot help, therefore, regarding this vast quick-sightedness into evil as a vicious excess, and as a very pernicious evil in itself. And I am the more inclined to this opinion, as I am afraid it always proceeds from a bad heart, for the reasons I have above mentioned, and for one more, namely, because I never knew it the property of a good one. Now, from this degree of suspicion I entirely and absolutely acquit Sophia.\n\nA second degree of this quality seems to arise from the head. This is, indeed, no other than the faculty of seeing what is before your eyes, and of drawing conclusions from what you see. The former of these is unavoidable by those who have any eyes, and the latter is perhaps no less certain and necessary a consequence of our having any brains. This is altogether as bitter an enemy to guilt as the former is to innocence: nor can I see it in an unamiable light, even though, through human fallibility, it should be sometimes mistaken. For instance, if a husband should accidentally surprize his wife in the lap or in the embraces of some of those pretty young gentlemen who profess the art of cuckold-making, I should not highly, I think, blame him for concluding something more than what he saw, from the familiarities which he really had seen, and which we are at least favourable enough to when we call them innocent freedoms. The reader will easily suggest great plenty of instances to himself; I shall add but one more, which, however unchristian it may be thought by some, I cannot help esteeming to be strictly justifiable; and this is a suspicion that a man is capable of doing what he hath done already, and that it is possible for one who hath been a villain once to act the same part again. And, to confess the truth, of this degree of suspicion I believe Sophia was guilty. From this degree of suspicion she had, in fact, conceived an opinion that her cousin was really not better than she should be.\n\nThe case, it seems, was this: Mrs Fitzpatrick wisely considered that the virtue of a young lady is, in the world, in the same situation with a poor hare, which is certain, whenever it ventures abroad, to meet its enemies; for it can hardly meet any other. No sooner therefore was she determined to take the first opportunity of quitting the protection of her husband, than she resolved to cast herself under the protection of some other man; and whom could she so properly choose to be her guardian as a person of quality, of fortune, of honour; and who, besides a gallant disposition which inclines men to knight-errantry, that is, to be the champions of ladies in distress, had often declared a violent attachment to herself, and had already given her all the instances of it in his power?\n\nBut, as the law hath foolishly omitted this office of vice-husband, or guardian to an eloped lady, and as malice is apt to denominate him by a more disagreeable appellation, it was concluded that his lordship should perform all such kind offices to the lady in secret, and without publickly assuming the character of her protector. Nay, to prevent any other person from seeing him in this light, it was agreed that the lady should proceed directly to Bath, and that his lordship should first go to London, and thence should go down to that place by the advice of his physicians.\n\nNow all this Sophia very plainly understood, not from the lips or behaviour of Mrs Fitzpatrick, but from the peer, who was infinitely less expert at retaining a secret than was the good lady; and perhaps the exact secrecy which Mrs Fitzpatrick had observed on this head in her narrative served not a little to heighten those suspicions which were now risen in the mind of her cousin.\n\nSophia very easily found out the lady she sought; for indeed there was not a chairman in town to whom her house was not perfectly well known; and, as she received, in return of her first message, a most pressing invitation, she immediately accepted it. Mrs Fitzpatrick, indeed, did not desire her cousin to stay with her with more earnestness than civility required. Whether she had discerned and resented the suspicion above-mentioned, or from what other motive it arose, I cannot say; but certain it is, she was full as desirous of parting with Sophia as Sophia herself could be of going.\n\nThe young lady, when she came to take leave of her cousin, could not avoid giving her a short hint of advice. She begged her, for heaven's sake, to take care of herself, and to consider in how dangerous a situation she stood; adding, she hoped some method would be found of reconciling her to her husband. “You must remember, my dear,” says she, “the maxim which my aunt Western hath so often repeated to us both; That whenever the matrimonial alliance is broke, and war declared between husband and wife, she can hardly make a disadvantageous peace for herself on any conditions. These are my aunt's very words, and she hath had a great deal of experience in the world.” Mrs Fitzpatrick answered, with a contemptuous smile, “Never fear me, child, take care of yourself; for you are younger than I. I will come and visit you in a few days; but, dear Sophy, let me give you one piece of advice: leave the character of Graveairs in the country, for, believe me, it will sit very awkwardly upon you in this town.”\n\nThus the two cousins parted, and Sophia repaired directly to Lady Bellaston, where she found a most hearty, as well as a most polite, welcome. The lady had taken a great fancy to her when she had seen her formerly with her aunt Western. She was indeed extremely glad to see her, and was no sooner acquainted with the reasons which induced her to leave the squire and to fly to London than she highly applauded her sense and resolution; and after expressing the highest satisfaction in the opinion which Sophia had declared she entertained of her ladyship, by chusing her house for an asylum, she promised her all the protection which it was in her power to give.\n\nAs we have now brought Sophia into safe hands, the reader will, I apprehend, be contented to deposit her there a while, and to look a little after other personages, and particularly poor Jones, whom we have left long enough to do penance for his past offences, which, as is the nature of vice, brought sufficient punishment upon him themselves.\n\n# BOOK XII.\n\nCONTAINING THE SAME INDIVIDUAL TIME WITH THE FORMER. Chapter i.\n\nShowing what is to be deemed plagiarism in a modern author, and what is to be considered as lawful prize.\n\nThe learned reader must have observed that in the course of this mighty work, I have often translated passages out of the best antient authors, without quoting the original, or without taking the least notice of the book from whence they were borrowed.\n\nThis conduct in writing is placed in a very proper light by the ingenious Abbé Bannier, in his preface to his Mythology, a work of great erudition and of equal judgment. “It will be easy,” says he, “for the reader to observe that I have frequently had greater regard to him than to my own reputation: for an author certainly pays him a considerable compliment, when, for his sake, he suppresses learned quotations that come in his way, and which would have cost him but the bare trouble of transcribing.”\n\nTo fill up a work with these scraps may, indeed, be considered as a downright cheat on the learned world, who are by such means imposed upon to buy a second time, in fragments and by retail, what they have already in gross, if not in their memories, upon their shelves; and it is still more cruel upon the illiterate, who are drawn in to pay for what is of no manner of use to them. A writer who intermixes great quantity of Greek and Latin with his works, deals by the ladies and fine gentlemen in the same paultry manner with which they are treated by the auctioneers, who often endeavour so to confound and mix up their lots, that, in order to purchase the commodity you want, you are obliged at the same time to purchase that which will do you no service.\n\nAnd yet, as there is no conduct so fair and disinterested but that it may be misunderstood by ignorance, and misrepresented by malice, I have been sometimes tempted to preserve my own reputation at the expense of my reader, and to transcribe the original, or at least to quote chapter and verse, whenever I have made use either of the thought or expression of another. I am, indeed, in some doubt that I have often suffered by the contrary method; and that, by suppressing the original author's name, I have been rather suspected of plagiarism than reputed to act from the amiable motive assigned by that justly celebrated Frenchman.\n\nNow, to obviate all such imputations for the future, I do here confess and justify the fact. The antients may be considered as a rich common, where every person who hath the smallest tenement in Parnassus hath a free right to fatten his muse. Or, to place it in a clearer light, we moderns are to the antients what the poor are to the rich. By the poor here I mean that large and venerable body which, in English, we call the mob. Now, whoever hath had the honour to be admitted to any degree of intimacy with this mob, must well know that it is one of their established maxims to plunder and pillage their rich neighbours without any reluctance; and that this is held to be neither sin nor shame among them. And so constantly do they abide and act by this maxim, that, in every parish almost in the kingdom, there is a kind of confederacy ever carrying on against a certain person of opulence called the squire, whose property is considered as free-booty by all his poor neighbours; who, as they conclude that there is no manner of guilt in such depredations, look upon it as a point of honour and moral obligation to conceal, and to preserve each other from punishment on all such occasions.\n\nIn like manner are the antients, such as Homer, Virgil, Horace, Cicero, and the rest, to be esteemed among us writers, as so many wealthy squires, from whom we, the poor of Parnassus, claim an immemorial custom of taking whatever we can come at. This liberty I demand, and this I am as ready to allow again to my poor neighbours in their turn. All I profess, and all I require of my brethren, is to maintain the same strict honesty among ourselves which the mob show to one another. To steal from one another is indeed highly criminal and indecent; for this may be strictly stiled defrauding the poor (sometimes perhaps those who are poorer than ourselves), or, to set it under the most opprobrious colours, robbing the spittal.\n\nSince, therefore, upon the strictest examination, my own conscience cannot lay any such pitiful theft to my charge, I am contented to plead guilty to the former accusation; nor shall I ever scruple to take to myself any passage which I shall find in an antient author to my purpose, without setting down the name of the author from whence it was taken. Nay, I absolutely claim a property in all such sentiments the moment they are transcribed into my writings, and I expect all readers henceforwards to regard them as purely and entirely my own. This claim, however, I desire to be allowed me only on condition that I preserve strict honesty towards my poor brethren, from whom, if ever I borrow any of that little of which they are possessed, I shall never fail to put their mark upon it, that it may be at all times ready to be restored to the right owner.\n\nThe omission of this was highly blameable in one Mr Moore, who, having formerly borrowed some lines of Pope and company, took the liberty to transcribe six of them into his play of the Rival Modes. Mr Pope, however, very luckily found them in the said play, and, laying violent hands on his own property, transferred it back again into his own works; and, for a further punishment, imprisoned the said Moore in the loathsome dungeon of the Dunciad, where his unhappy memory now remains, and eternally will remain, as a proper punishment for such his unjust dealings in the poetical trade.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nIn which, though the squire doth not find his daughter, something is found which puts an end to his pursuit.\n\nThe history now returns to the inn at Upton, whence we shall first trace the footsteps of Squire Western; for, as he will soon arrive at an end of his journey, we shall have then full leisure to attend our heroe.\n\nThe reader may be pleased to remember that the said squire departed from the inn in great fury, and in that fury he pursued his daughter. The hostler having informed him that she had crossed the Severn, he likewise past that river with his equipage, and rode full speed, vowing the utmost vengeance against poor Sophia, if he should but overtake her.\n\nHe had not gone far before he arrived at a crossway. Here he called a short council of war, in which, after hearing different opinions, he at last gave the direction of his pursuit to fortune, and struck directly into the Worcester road.\n\nIn this road he proceeded about two miles, when he began to bemoan himself most bitterly, frequently crying out, “What pity is it! Sure never was so unlucky a dog as myself!” And then burst forth a volley of oaths and execrations.\n\nThe parson attempted to administer comfort to him on this occasion. “Sorrow not, sir,” says he, “like those without hope. Howbeit we have not yet been able to overtake young madam, we may account it some good fortune that we have hitherto traced her course aright. Peradventure she will soon be fatigated with her journey, and will tarry in some inn, in order to renovate her corporeal functions; and in that case, in all moral certainty, you will very briefly be compos voti.”\n\n“Pogh! d--n the slut!” answered the squire, “I am lamenting the loss of so fine a morning for hunting. It is confounded hard to lose one of the best scenting days, in all appearance, which hath been this season, and especially after so long a frost.”\n\nWhether Fortune, who now and then shows some compassion in her wantonest tricks, might not take pity of the squire; and, as she had determined not to let him overtake his daughter, might not resolve to make him amends some other way, I will not assert; but he had hardly uttered the words just before commemorated, and two or three oaths at their heels, when a pack of hounds began to open their melodious throats at a small distance from them, which the squire's horse and his rider both perceiving, both immediately pricked up their ears, and the squire, crying, “She's gone, she's gone! Damn me if she is not gone!” instantly clapped spurs to the beast, who little needed it, having indeed the same inclination with his master; and now the whole company, crossing into a corn-field, rode directly towards the hounds, with much hallowing and whooping, while the poor parson, blessing himself, brought up the rear.\n\nThus fable reports that the fair Grimalkin, whom Venus, at the desire of a passionate lover, converted from a cat into a fine woman, no sooner perceived a mouse than, mindful of her former sport, and still retaining her pristine nature, she leaped from the bed of her husband to pursue the little animal.\n\nWhat are we to understand by this? Not that the bride was displeased with the embraces of her amorous bridegroom; for, though some have remarked that cats are subject to ingratitude, yet women and cats too will be pleased and purr on certain occasions. The truth is, as the sagacious Sir Roger L'Estrange observes, in his deep reflections, that, “if we shut Nature out at the door, she will come in at the window; and that puss, though a madam, will be a mouser still.” In the same manner we are not to arraign the squire of any want of love for his daughter; for in reality he had a great deal; we are only to consider that he was a squire and a sportsman, and then we may apply the fable to him, and the judicious reflections likewise.\n\nThe hounds ran very hard, as it is called, and the squire pursued over hedge and ditch, with all his usual vociferation and alacrity, and with all his usual pleasure; nor did the thoughts of Sophia ever once intrude themselves to allay the satisfaction he enjoyed in the chace, which, he said, was one of the finest he ever saw, and which he swore was very well worth going fifty miles for. As the squire forgot his daughter, the servants, we may easily believe, forgot their mistress; and the parson, after having expressed much astonishment, in Latin, to himself, at length likewise abandoned all farther thoughts of the young lady, and, jogging on at a distance behind, began to meditate a portion of doctrine for the ensuing Sunday.\n\nThe squire who owned the hounds was highly pleased with the arrival of his brother squire and sportsman; for all men approve merit in their own way, and no man was more expert in the field than Mr Western, nor did any other better know how to encourage the dogs with his voice, and to animate the hunt with his holla.\n\nSportsmen, in the warmth of a chace, are too much engaged to attend to any manner of ceremony, nay, even to the offices of humanity: for, if any of them meet with an accident by tumbling into a ditch, or into a river, the rest pass on regardless, and generally leave him to his fate: during this time, therefore, the two squires, though often close to each other, interchanged not a single word. The master of the hunt, however, often saw and approved the great judgment of the stranger in drawing the dogs when they were at a fault, and hence conceived a very high opinion of his understanding, as the number of his attendants inspired no small reverence to his quality. As soon, therefore, as the sport was ended by the death of the little animal which had occasioned it, the two squires met, and in all squire-like greeting saluted each other.\n\nThe conversation was entertaining enough, and what we may perhaps relate in an appendix, or on some other occasion; but as it nowise concerns this history, we cannot prevail on ourselves to give it a place here. It concluded with a second chace, and that with an invitation to dinner. This being accepted, was followed by a hearty bout of drinking, which ended in as hearty a nap on the part of Squire Western.\n\nOur squire was by no means a match either for his host, or for parson Supple, at his cups that evening; for which the violent fatigue of mind as well as body that he had undergone, may very well account, without the least derogation from his honour. He was indeed, according to the vulgar phrase, whistle drunk; for before he had swallowed the third bottle, he became so entirely overpowered that though he was not carried off to bed till long after, the parson considered him as absent, and having acquainted the other squire with all relating to Sophia, he obtained his promise of seconding those arguments which he intended to urge the next morning for Mr Western's return.\n\nNo sooner, therefore, had the good squire shaken off his evening, and began to call for his morning draught, and to summon his horses in order to renew his pursuit, than Mr Supple began his dissuasives, which the host so strongly seconded, that they at length prevailed, and Mr Western agreed to return home; being principally moved by one argument, viz., that he knew not which way to go, and might probably be riding farther from his daughter instead of towards her. He then took leave of his brother sportsman, and expressing great joy that the frost was broken (which might perhaps be no small motive to his hastening home), set forwards, or rather backwards, for Somersetshire; but not before he had first despatched part of his retinue in quest of his daughter, after whom he likewise sent a volley of the most bitter execrations which he could invent.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nThe departure of Jones from Upton, with what passed between him and Partridge on the road.\n\nAt length we are once more come to our heroe; and, to say truth, we have been obliged to part with him so long, that, considering the condition in which we left him, I apprehend many of our readers have concluded we intended to abandon him for ever; he being at present in that situation in which prudent people usually desist from enquiring any farther after their friends, lest they should be shocked by hearing such friends had hanged themselves.\n\nBut, in reality, if we have not all the virtues, I will boldly say, neither have we all the vices of a prudent character; and though it is not easy to conceive circumstances much more miserable than those of poor Jones at present, we shall return to him, and attend upon him with the same diligence as if he was wantoning in the brightest beams of fortune.\n\nMr Jones, then, and his companion Partridge, left the inn a few minutes after the departure of Squire Western, and pursued the same road on foot, for the hostler told them that no horses were by any means to be at that time procured at Upton. On they marched with heavy hearts; for though their disquiet proceeded from very different reasons, yet displeased they were both; and if Jones sighed bitterly, Partridge grunted altogether as sadly at every step.\n\nWhen they came to the cross-roads where the squire had stopt to take counsel, Jones stopt likewise, and turning to Partridge, asked his opinion which track they should pursue. “Ah, sir,” answered Partridge, “I wish your honour would follow my advice.” “Why should I not?” replied Jones; “for it is now indifferent to me whither I go, or what becomes of me.” “My advice, then,” said Partridge, “is, that you immediately face about and return home; for who that hath such a home to return to as your honour, would travel thus about the country like a vagabond? I ask pardon, sed vox ea sola reperta est.”\n\n“Alas!” cries Jones, “I have no home to return to;--but if my friend, my father, would receive me, could I bear the country from which Sophia is flown? Cruel Sophia! Cruel! No; let me blame myself!--No; let me blame thee. D--nation seize thee--fool--blockhead! thou hast undone me, and I will tear thy soul from thy body.”--At which words he laid violent hands on the collar of poor Partridge, and shook him more heartily than an ague-fit, or his own fears had ever done before.\n\nPartridge fell trembling on his knees, and begged for mercy, vowing he had meant no harm--when Jones, after staring wildly on him for a moment, quitted his hold, and discharged a rage on himself, that, had it fallen on the other, would certainly have put an end to his being, which indeed the very apprehension of it had almost effected.\n\nWe would bestow some pains here in minutely describing all the mad pranks which Jones played on this occasion, could we be well assured that the reader would take the same pains in perusing them; but as we are apprehensive that, after all the labour which we should employ in painting this scene, the said reader would be very apt to skip it entirely over, we have saved ourselves that trouble. To say the truth, we have, from this reason alone, often done great violence to the luxuriance of our genius, and have left many excellent descriptions out of our work, which would otherwise have been in it. And this suspicion, to be honest, arises, as is generally the case, from our own wicked heart; for we have, ourselves, been very often most horridly given to jumping, as we have run through the pages of voluminous historians.\n\nSuffice it then simply to say, that Jones, after having played the part of a madman for many minutes, came, by degrees, to himself; which no sooner happened, than, turning to Partridge, he very earnestly begged his pardon for the attack he had made on him in the violence of his passion; but concluded, by desiring him never to mention his return again; for he was resolved never to see that country any more.\n\nPartridge easily forgave, and faithfully promised to obey the injunction now laid upon him. And then Jones very briskly cried out, “Since it is absolutely impossible for me to pursue any farther the steps of my angel--I will pursue those of glory. Come on, my brave lad, now for the army:--it is a glorious cause, and I would willingly sacrifice my life in it, even though it was worth my preserving.” And so saying, he immediately struck into the different road from that which the squire had taken, and, by mere chance, pursued the very same through which Sophia had before passed.\n\nOur travellers now marched a full mile, without speaking a syllable to each other, though Jones, indeed, muttered many things to himself. As to Partridge, he was profoundly silent; for he was not, perhaps, perfectly recovered from his former fright; besides, he had apprehensions of provoking his friend to a second fit of wrath, especially as he now began to entertain a conceit, which may not, perhaps, create any great wonder in the reader. In short, he began now to suspect that Jones was absolutely out of his senses.\n\nAt length, Jones, being weary of soliloquy, addressed himself to his companion, and blamed him for his taciturnity; for which the poor man very honestly accounted, from his fear of giving offence. And now this fear being pretty well removed, by the most absolute promises of indemnity, Partridge again took the bridle from his tongue; which, perhaps, rejoiced no less at regaining its liberty, than a young colt, when the bridle is slipt from his neck, and he is turned loose into the pastures.\n\nAs Partridge was inhibited from that topic which would have first suggested itself, he fell upon that which was next uppermost in his mind, namely, the Man of the Hill. “Certainly, sir,” says he, “that could never be a man, who dresses himself and lives after such a strange manner, and so unlike other folks. Besides, his diet, as the old woman told me, is chiefly upon herbs, which is a fitter food for a horse than a Christian: nay, landlord at Upton says that the neighbours thereabouts have very fearful notions about him. It runs strangely in my head that it must have been some spirit, who, perhaps, might be sent to forewarn us: and who knows but all that matter which he told us, of his going to fight, and of his being taken prisoner, and of the great danger he was in of being hanged, might be intended as a warning to us, considering what we are going about? besides, I dreamt of nothing all last night but of fighting; and methought the blood ran out of my nose, as liquor out of a tap. Indeed, sir, infandum, regina, jubes renovare dolorem.”\n\n“Thy story, Partridge,” answered Jones, “is almost as ill applied as thy Latin. Nothing can be more likely to happen than death to men who go into battle. Perhaps we shall both fall in it--and what then?” “What then?” replied Partridge; “why then there is an end of us, is there not? when I am gone, all is over with me. What matters the cause to me, or who gets the victory, if I am killed? I shall never enjoy any advantage from it. What are all the ringing of bells, and bonfires, to one that is six foot under ground? there will be an end of poor Partridge.” “And an end of poor Partridge,” cries Jones, “there must be, one time or other. If you love Latin, I will repeat you some fine lines out of Horace, which would inspire courage into a coward.\n\n`Dulce et decorum est pro patria mori Mors et fugacem persequitur virum Nec parcit imbellis juventae Poplitibus, timidoque tergo.'”\n\n“I wish you would construe them,” cries Partridge; “for Horace is a hard author, and I cannot understand as you repeat them.”\n\n“I will repeat you a bad imitation, or rather paraphrase, of my own,” said Jones; “for I am but an indifferent poet:\n\n`Who would not die in his dear country's cause? Since, if base fear his dastard step withdraws, From death he cannot fly:--One common grave Receives, at last, the coward and the brave.'”\n\n“That's very certain,” cries Partridge. “Ay, sure, Mors omnibus communis: but there is a great difference between dying in one's bed a great many years hence, like a good Christian, with all our friends crying about us, and being shot to-day or to-morrow, like a mad dog; or, perhaps, hacked in twenty pieces with the sword, and that too before we have repented of all our sins. O Lord, have mercy upon us! to be sure the soldiers are a wicked kind of people. I never loved to have anything to do with them. I could hardly bring myself ever to look upon them as Christians. There is nothing but cursing and swearing among them. I wish your honour would repent: I heartily wish you would repent before it is too late; and not think of going among them.--Evil communication corrupts good manners. That is my principal reason. For as for that matter, I am no more afraid than another man, not I; as to matter of that. I know all human flesh must die; but yet a man may live many years, for all that. Why, I am a middle-aged man now, and yet I may live a great number of years. I have read of several who have lived to be above a hundred, and some a great deal above a hundred. Not that I hope, I mean that I promise myself, to live to any such age as that, neither.--But if it be only to eighty or ninety. Heaven be praised, that is a great ways off yet; and I am not afraid of dying then, no more than another man; but, surely, to tempt death before a man's time is come seems to me downright wickedness and presumption. Besides, if it was to do any good indeed; but, let the cause be what it will, what mighty matter of good can two people do? and, for my part, I understand nothing of it. I never fired off a gun above ten times in my life; and then it was not charged with bullets. And for the sword, I never learned to fence, and know nothing of the matter. And then there are those cannons, which certainly it must be thought the highest presumption to go in the way of; and nobody but a madman--I ask pardon; upon my soul I meant no harm; I beg I may not throw your honour into another passion.”\n\n“Be under no apprehension, Partridge,” cries Jones; “I am now so well convinced of thy cowardice, that thou couldst not provoke me on any account.” “Your honour,” answered he, “may call me coward, or anything else you please. If loving to sleep in a whole skin makes a man a coward, non immunes ab illis malis sumus. I never read in my grammar that a man can't be a good man without fighting. Vir bonus est quis? Qui consulta patrum, qui leges juraque servat. Not a word of fighting; and I am sure the scripture is so much against it, that a man shall never persuade me he is a good Christian while he sheds Christian blood.”\n\nThe adventure of a beggar-man.\n\nJust as Partridge had uttered that good and pious doctrine, with which the last chapter concluded, they arrived at another cross-way, when a lame fellow in rags asked them for alms; upon which Partridge gave him a severe rebuke, saying, “Every parish ought to keep their own poor.” Jones then fell a-laughing, and asked Partridge, “if he was not ashamed, with so much charity in his mouth, to have no charity in his heart. Your religion,” says he, “serves you only for an excuse for your faults, but is no incentive to your virtue. Can any man who is really a Christian abstain from relieving one of his brethren in such a miserable condition?” And at the same time, putting his hand in his pocket, he gave the poor object a shilling.\n\n“Master,” cries the fellow, after thanking him, “I have a curious thing here in my pocket, which I found about two miles off, if your worship will please to buy it. I should not venture to pull it out to every one; but, as you are so good a gentleman, and so kind to the poor, you won't suspect a man of being a thief only because he is poor.” He then pulled out a little gilt pocket-book, and delivered it into the hands of Jones.\n\nJones presently opened it, and (guess, reader, what he felt) saw in the first page the words Sophia Western, written by her own fair hand. He no sooner read the name than he prest it close to his lips; nor could he avoid falling into some very frantic raptures, notwithstanding his company; but, perhaps, these very raptures made him forget he was not alone.\n\nWhile Jones was kissing and mumbling the book, as if he had an excellent brown buttered crust in his mouth or as if he had really been a book-worm, or an author who had nothing to eat but his own works, a piece of paper fell from its leaves to the ground, which Partridge took up, and delivered to Jones, who presently perceived it to be a bank-bill. It was, indeed, the very bill which Western had given his daughter the night before her departure; and a Jew would have jumped to purchase it at five shillings less than £100.\n\nThe eyes of Partridge sparkled at this news, which Jones now proclaimed aloud; and so did (though with somewhat a different aspect) those of the poor fellow who had found the book; and who (I hope from a principle of honesty) had never opened it: but we should not deal honestly by the reader if we omitted to inform him of a circumstance which may be here a little material, viz. that the fellow could not read.\n\nJones, who had felt nothing but pure joy and transport from the finding the book, was affected with a mixture of concern at this new discovery; for his imagination instantly suggested to him that the owner of the bill might possibly want it before he should be able to convey it to her. He then acquainted the finder that he knew the lady to whom the book belonged, and would endeavour to find her out as soon as possible, and return it her.\n\nThe pocket-book was a late present from Mrs Western to her niece; it had cost five-and-twenty shillings, having been bought of a celebrated toyman; but the real value of the silver which it contained in its clasp was about eighteen-pence; and that price the said toyman, as it was altogether as good as when it first issued from his shop, would now have given for it. A prudent person would, however, have taken proper advantage of the ignorance of this fellow, and would not have offered more than a shilling, or perhaps sixpence, for it; nay, some perhaps would have given nothing, and left the fellow to his action of trover, which some learned serjeants may doubt whether he could, under these circumstances, have maintained.\n\nJones, on the contrary, whose character was on the outside of generosity, and may perhaps not very unjustly have been suspected of extravagance, without any hesitation gave a guinea in exchange for the book. The poor man, who had not for a long time before been possessed of so much treasure, gave Mr Jones a thousand thanks, and discovered little less of transport in his muscles than Jones had before shown when he had first read the name of Sophia Western.\n\nThe fellow very readily agreed to attend our travellers to the place where he had found the pocket-book. Together, therefore, they proceeded directly thither; but not so fast as Mr Jones desired; for his guide unfortunately happened to be lame, and could not possibly travel faster than a mile an hour. As this place, therefore, was at above three miles' distance, though the fellow had said otherwise, the reader need not be acquainted how long they were in walking it.\n\nJones opened the book a hundred times during their walk, kissed it as often, talked much to himself, and very little to his companions. At all which the guide exprest some signs of astonishment to Partridge; who more than once shook his head, and cryed, Poor gentleman! orandum est ut sit mens sana in corpore sano.\n\nAt length they arrived at the very spot where Sophia unhappily dropt the pocket-book, and where the fellow had as happily found it. Here Jones offered to take leave of his guide, and to improve his pace; but the fellow, in whom that violent surprize and joy which the first receipt of the guinea had occasioned was now considerably abated, and who had now had sufficient time to recollect himself, put on a discontented look, and, scratching his head, said, “He hoped his worship would give him something more. Your worship,” said he, “will, I hope, take it into your consideration that if I had not been honest I might have kept the whole.” And, indeed, this the reader must confess to have been true. “If the paper there,” said he, “be worth £100, I am sure the finding it deserves more than a guinea. Besides, suppose your worship should never see the lady, nor give it her--and, though your worship looks and talks very much like a gentleman, yet I have only your worship's bare word; and, certainly, if the right owner ben't to be found, it all belongs to the first finder. I hope your worship will consider of all these matters: I am but a poor man, and therefore don't desire to have all; but it is but reasonable I should have my share. Your worship looks like a good man, and, I hope, will consider my honesty; for I might have kept every farthing, and nobody ever the wiser.” “I promise thee, upon my honour,” cries Jones, “that I know the right owner, and will restore it her.” “Nay, your worship,” answered the fellow, “may do as you please as to that; if you will but give me my share, that is, one-half of the money, your honour may keep the rest yourself if you please;” and concluded with swearing, by a very vehement oath, “that he would never mention a syllable of it to any man living.”\n\n“Lookee, friend,” cries Jones, “the right owner shall certainly have again all that she lost; and as for any farther gratuity, I really cannot give it you at present; but let me know your name, and where you live, and it is more than possible you may hereafter have further reason to rejoice at this morning's adventure.”\n\n“I don't know what you mean by venture,” cries the fellow; “it seems I must venture whether you will return the lady her money or no; but I hope your worship will consider--” “Come, come,” said Partridge, “tell his honour your name, and where you may be found; I warrant you will never repent having put the money into his hands.” The fellow, seeing no hopes of recovering the possession of the pocket-book, at last complied in giving in his name and place of abode, which Jones writ upon a piece of paper with the pencil of Sophia; and then, placing the paper in the same page where she had writ her name, he cried out, “There, friend, you are the happiest man alive; I have joined your name to that of an angel.” “I don't know anything about angels,” answered the fellow; “but I wish you would give me a little more money, or else return me the pocket-book.” Partridge now waxed wrath: he called the poor cripple by several vile and opprobrious names, and was absolutely proceeding to beat him, but Jones would not suffer any such thing: and now, telling the fellow he would certainly find some opportunity of serving him, Mr Jones departed as fast as his heels would carry him; and Partridge, into whom the thoughts of the hundred pound had infused new spirits, followed his leader; while the man, who was obliged to stay behind, fell to cursing them both, as well as his parents; “for had they,” says he, “sent me to charity-school to learn to write and read and cast accounts, I should have known the value of these matters as well as other people.”\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nContaining more adventures which Mr Jones and his companion met on the road.\n\nOur travellers now walked so fast, that they had very little time or breath for conversation; Jones meditating all the way on Sophia, and Partridge on the bank-bill, which, though it gave him some pleasure, caused him at the same time to repine at fortune, which, in all his walks, had never given him such an opportunity of showing his honesty. They had proceeded above three miles, when Partridge, being unable any longer to keep up with Jones, called to him, and begged him a little to slacken his pace: with this he was the more ready to comply, as he had for some time lost the footsteps of the horses, which the thaw had enabled him to trace for several miles, and he was now upon a wide common, where were several roads.\n\nHe here therefore stopt to consider which of these roads he should pursue; when on a sudden they heard the noise of a drum, that seemed at no great distance. This sound presently alarmed the fears of Partridge, and he cried out, “Lord have mercy upon us all; they are certainly a coming!” “Who is coming?” cries Jones; for fear had long since given place to softer ideas in his mind; and since his adventure with the lame man, he had been totally intent on pursuing Sophia, without entertaining one thought of an enemy. “Who?” cries Partridge, “why, the rebels: but why should I call them rebels? they may be very honest gentlemen, for anything I know to the contrary. The devil take him that affronts them, I say; I am sure, if they have nothing to say to me, I will have nothing to say to them, but in a civil way. For Heaven's sake, sir, don't affront them if they should come, and perhaps they may do us no harm; but would it not be the wiser way to creep into some of yonder bushes, till they are gone by? What can two unarmed men do perhaps against fifty thousand? Certainly nobody but a madman; I hope your honour is not offended; but certainly no man who hath mens sana in corpore sano----” Here Jones interrupted this torrent of eloquence, which fear had inspired, saying, “That by the drum he perceived they were near some town.” He then made directly towards the place whence the noise proceeded, bidding Partridge “take courage, for that he would lead him into no danger;” and adding, “it was impossible the rebels should be so near.”\n\nPartridge was a little comforted with this last assurance; and though he would more gladly have gone the contrary way, he followed his leader, his heart beating time, but not after the manner of heroes, to the music of the drum, which ceased not till they had traversed the common, and were come into a narrow lane.\n\nAnd now Partridge, who kept even pace with Jones, discovered something painted flying in the air, a very few yards before him, which fancying to be the colours of the enemy, he fell a bellowing, “Oh Lord, sir, here they are; there is the crown and coffin. Oh Lord! I never saw anything so terrible; and we are within gun-shot of them already.”\n\nJones no sooner looked up, than he plainly perceived what it was which Partridge had thus mistaken. “Partridge,” says he, “I fancy you will be able to engage this whole army yourself; for by the colours I guess what the drum was which we heard before, and which beats up for recruits to a puppet-show.”\n\n“A puppet-show!” answered Partridge, with most eager transport. “And is it really no more than that? I love a puppet-show of all the pastimes upon earth. Do, good sir, let us tarry and see it. Besides, I am quite famished to death; for it is now almost dark, and I have not eat a morsel since three o'clock in the morning.”\n\nThey now arrived at an inn, or indeed an ale-house, where Jones was prevailed upon to stop, the rather as he had no longer any assurance of being in the road he desired. They walked both directly into the kitchen, where Jones began to enquire if no ladies had passed that way in the morning, and Partridge as eagerly examined into the state of their provisions; and indeed his enquiry met with the better success; for Jones could not hear news of Sophia; but Partridge, to his great satisfaction, found good reason to expect very shortly the agreeable sight of an excellent smoaking dish of eggs and bacon.\n\nIn strong and healthy constitutions love hath a very different effect from what it causes in the puny part of the species. In the latter it generally destroys all that appetite which tends towards the conservation of the individual; but in the former, though it often induces forgetfulness, and a neglect of food, as well as of everything else; yet place a good piece of well-powdered buttock before a hungry lover, and he seldom fails very handsomely to play his part. Thus it happened in the present case; for though Jones perhaps wanted a prompter, and might have travelled much farther, had he been alone, with an empty stomach; yet no sooner did he sit down to the bacon and eggs, than he fell to as heartily and voraciously as Partridge himself.\n\nBefore our travellers had finished their dinner, night came on, and as the moon was now past the full, it was extremely dark. Partridge therefore prevailed on Jones to stay and see the puppet-show, which was just going to begin, and to which they were very eagerly invited by the master of the said show, who declared that his figures were the finest which the world had ever produced, and that they had given great satisfaction to all the quality in every town in England.\n\nThe puppet-show was performed with great regularity and decency. It was called the fine and serious part of the Provoked Husband; and it was indeed a very grave and solemn entertainment, without any low wit or humour, or jests; or, to do it no more than justice, without anything which could provoke a laugh. The audience were all highly pleased. A grave matron told the master she would bring her two daughters the next night, as he did not show any stuff; and an attorney's clerk and an exciseman both declared, that the characters of Lord and Lady Townley were well preserved, and highly in nature. Partridge likewise concurred with this opinion.\n\nThe master was so highly elated with these encomiums, that he could not refrain from adding some more of his own. He said, “The present age was not improved in anything so much as in their puppet-shows; which, by throwing out Punch and his wife Joan, and such idle trumpery, were at last brought to be a rational entertainment. I remember,” said he, “when I first took to the business, there was a great deal of low stuff that did very well to make folks laugh; but was never calculated to improve the morals of young people, which certainly ought to be principally aimed at in every puppet-show: for why may not good and instructive lessons be conveyed this way, as well as any other? My figures are as big as the life, and they represent the life in every particular; and I question not but people rise from my little drama as much improved as they do from the great.” “I would by no means degrade the ingenuity of your profession,” answered Jones, “but I should have been glad to have seen my old acquaintance master Punch, for all that; and so far from improving, I think, by leaving out him and his merry wife Joan, you have spoiled your puppet-show.”\n\nThe dancer of wires conceived an immediate and high contempt for Jones, from these words. And with much disdain in his countenance, he replied, “Very probably, sir, that may be your opinion; but I have the satisfaction to know the best judges differ from you, and it is impossible to please every taste. I confess, indeed, some of the quality at Bath, two or three years ago, wanted mightily to bring Punch again upon the stage. I believe I lost some money for not agreeing to it; but let others do as they will; a little matter shall never bribe me to degrade my own profession, nor will I ever willingly consent to the spoiling the decency and regularity of my stage, by introducing any such low stuff upon it.”\n\n“Right, friend,” cries the clerk, “you are very right. Always avoid what is low. There are several of my acquaintance in London, who are resolved to drive everything which is low from the stage.” “Nothing can be more proper,” cries the exciseman, pulling his pipe from his mouth. “I remember,” added he, “(for I then lived with my lord) I was in the footman's gallery, the night when this play of the Provoked Husband was acted first. There was a great deal of low stuff in it about a country gentleman come up to town to stand for parliament-man; and there they brought a parcel of his servants upon the stage, his coachman I remember particularly; but the gentlemen in our gallery could not bear anything so low, and they damned it. I observe, friend, you have left all that matter out, and you are to be commended for it.”\n\n“Nay, gentlemen,” cries Jones, “I can never maintain my opinion against so many; indeed, if the generality of his audience dislike him, the learned gentleman who conducts the show might have done very right in dismissing Punch from his service.”\n\nThe master of the show then began a second harangue, and said much of the great force of example, and how much the inferior part of mankind would be deterred from vice, by observing how odious it was in their superiors; when he was unluckily interrupted by an incident, which, though perhaps we might have omitted it at another time, we cannot help relating at present, but not in this chapter.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nFrom which it may be inferred that the best things are liable to be misunderstood and misinterpreted.\n\nA violent uproar now arose in the entry, where my landlady was well cuffing her maid both with her fist and tongue. She had indeed missed the wench from her employment, and, after a little search, had found her on the puppet-show stage in company with the Merry Andrew, and in a situation not very proper to be described.\n\nThough Grace (for that was her name) had forfeited all title to modesty; yet had she not impudence enough to deny a fact in which she was actually surprized; she, therefore, took another turn, and attempted to mitigate the offence. “Why do you beat me in this manner, mistress?” cries the wench. “If you don't like my doings, you may turn me away. If I am a w--e” (for the other had liberally bestowed that appellation on her), “my betters are so as well as I. What was the fine lady in the puppet-show just now? I suppose she did not lie all night out from her husband for nothing.”\n\nThe landlady now burst into the kitchen, and fell foul on both her husband and the poor puppet-mover. “Here, husband,” says she, “you see the consequence of harbouring these people in your house. If one doth draw a little drink the more for them, one is hardly made amends for the litter they make; and then to have one's house made a bawdy-house of by such lousy vermin. In short, I desire you would be gone to-morrow morning; for I will tolerate no more such doings. It is only the way to teach our servants idleness and nonsense; for to be sure nothing better can be learned by such idle shows as these. I remember when puppet-shows were made of good scripture stories, as Jephthah's Rash Vow, and such good things, and when wicked people were carried away by the devil. There was some sense in those matters; but as the parson told us last Sunday, nobody believes in the devil now-a-days; and here you bring about a parcel of puppets drest up like lords and ladies, only to turn the heads of poor country wenches; and when their heads are once turned topsy-turvy, no wonder everything else is so.”\n\nVirgil, I think, tells us, that when the mob are assembled in a riotous and tumultuous manner, and all sorts of missile weapons fly about, if a man of gravity and authority appears amongst them, the tumult is presently appeased, and the mob, which when collected into one body, may be well compared to an ass, erect their long ears at the grave man's discourse.\n\nOn the contrary, when a set of grave men and philosophers are disputing; when wisdom herself may in a manner be considered as present, and administering arguments to the disputants; should a tumult arise among the mob, or should one scold, who is herself equal in noise to a mighty mob, appear among the said philosophers; their disputes cease in a moment, wisdom no longer performs her ministerial office, and the attention of every one is immediately attracted by the scold alone.\n\nThus the uproar aforesaid, and the arrival of the landlady, silenced the master of the puppet-show, and put a speedy and final end to that grave and solemn harangue, of which we have given the reader a sufficient taste already. Nothing indeed could have happened so very inopportune as this accident; the most wanton malice of fortune could not have contrived such another stratagem to confound the poor fellow, while he was so triumphantly descanting on the good morals inculcated by his exhibitions. His mouth was now as effectually stopt, as that of quack must be, if, in the midst of a declamation on the great virtues of his pills and powders, the corpse of one of his martyrs should be brought forth, and deposited before the stage, as a testimony of his skill.\n\nInstead, therefore, of answering my landlady, the puppet-show man ran out to punish his Merry Andrew; and now the moon beginning to put forth her silver light, as the poets call it (though she looked at that time more like a piece of copper), Jones called for his reckoning, and ordered Partridge, whom my landlady had just awaked from a profound nap, to prepare for his journey; but Partridge, having lately carried two points, as my reader hath seen before, was emboldened to attempt a third, which was to prevail with Jones to take up a lodging that evening in the house where he then was. He introduced this with an affected surprize at the intention which Mr Jones declared of removing; and, after urging many excellent arguments against it, he at last insisted strongly that it could be to no manner of purpose whatever; for that, unless Jones knew which way the lady was gone, every step he took might very possibly lead him the farther from her; “for you find, sir,” said he, “by all the people in the house, that she is not gone this way. How much better, therefore, would it be to stay till the morning, when we may expect to meet with somebody to enquire of?”\n\nThis last argument had indeed some effect on Jones, and while he was weighing it the landlord threw all the rhetoric of which he was master into the same scale. “Sure, sir,” said he, “your servant gives you most excellent advice; for who would travel by night at this time of the year?” He then began in the usual stile to trumpet forth the excellent accommodation which his house afforded; and my landlady likewise opened on the occasion----But, not to detain the reader with what is common to every host and hostess, it is sufficient to tell him Jones was at last prevailed on to stay and refresh himself with a few hours' rest, which indeed he very much wanted; for he had hardly shut his eyes since he had left the inn where the accident of the broken head had happened.\n\nAs soon as Jones had taken a resolution to proceed no farther that night, he presently retired to rest, with his two bedfellows, the pocket-book and the muff; but Partridge, who at several times had refreshed himself with several naps, was more inclined to eating than to sleeping, and more to drinking than to either.\n\nAnd now the storm which Grace had raised being at an end, and my landlady being again reconciled to the puppet-man, who on his side forgave the indecent reflections which the good woman in her passion had cast on his performances, a face of perfect peace and tranquillity reigned in the kitchen; where sat assembled round the fire the landlord and landlady of the house, the master of the puppet-show, the attorney's clerk, the exciseman, and the ingenious Mr Partridge; in which company past the agreeable conversation which will be found in the next chapter.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nContaining a remark or two of our own and many more of the good company assembled in the kitchen.\n\nThough the pride of Partridge did not submit to acknowledge himself a servant, yet he condescended in most particulars to imitate the manners of that rank. One instance of this was, his greatly magnifying the fortune of his companion, as he called Jones: such is a general custom with all servants among strangers, as none of them would willingly be thought the attendant on a beggar: for, the higher the situation of the master is, the higher consequently is that of the man in his own opinion; the truth of which observation appears from the behaviour of all the footmen of the nobility.\n\nBut, though title and fortune communicate a splendor all around them, and the footmen of men of quality and of estate think themselves entitled to a part of that respect which is paid to the quality and estate of their masters, it is clearly otherwise with regard to virtue and understanding. These advantages are strictly personal, and swallow themselves all the respect which is paid to them. To say the truth, this is so very little, that they cannot well afford to let any others partake with them. As these therefore reflect no honour on the domestic, so neither is he at all dishonoured by the most deplorable want of both in his master. Indeed it is otherwise in the want of what is called virtue in a mistress, the consequence of which we have before seen: for in this dishonour there is a kind of contagion, which, like that of poverty, communicates itself to all who approach it.\n\nNow for these reasons we are not to wonder that servants (I mean among the men only) should have so great regard for the reputation of the wealth of their masters, and little or none at all for their character in other points, and that, though they would be ashamed to be the footman of a beggar, they are not so to attend upon a rogue or a blockhead; and do consequently make no scruple to spread the fame of the iniquities and follies of their said masters as far as possible, and this often with great humour and merriment. In reality, a footman is often a wit as well as a beau, at the expence of the gentleman whose livery he wears.\n\nAfter Partridge, therefore, had enlarged greatly on the vast fortune to which Mr Jones was heir, he very freely communicated an apprehension, which he had begun to conceive the day before, and for which, as we hinted at that very time, the behaviour of Jones seemed to have furnished a sufficient foundation. In short, he was now pretty well confirmed in an opinion that his master was out of his wits, with which opinion he very bluntly acquainted the good company round the fire.\n\nWith this sentiment the puppet-show man immediately coincided. “I own,” said he, “the gentleman surprized me very much, when he talked so absurdly about puppet-shows. It is indeed hardly to be conceived that any man in his senses should be so much mistaken; what you say now accounts very well for all his monstrous notions. Poor gentleman! I am heartily concerned for him; indeed he hath a strange wildness about his eyes, which I took notice of before, though I did not mention it.”\n\nThe landlord agreed with this last assertion, and likewise claimed the sagacity of having observed it. “And certainly,” added he, “it must be so; for no one but a madman would have thought of leaving so good a house to ramble about the country at that time of night.”\n\nThe exciseman, pulling his pipe from his mouth, said, “He thought the gentleman looked and talked a little wildly;” and then turning to Partridge, “if he be a madman,” says he, “he should not be suffered to travel thus about the country; for possibly he may do some mischief. It is a pity he was not secured and sent home to his relations.”\n\nNow some conceits of this kind were likewise lurking in the mind of Partridge; for, as he was now persuaded that Jones had run away from Mr Allworthy, he promised himself the highest rewards if he could by any means convey him back. But fear of Jones, of whose fierceness and strength he had seen, and indeed felt, some instances, had however represented any such scheme as impossible to be executed, and had discouraged him from applying himself to form any regular plan for the purpose. But no sooner did he hear the sentiments of the exciseman than he embraced that opportunity of declaring his own, and expressed a hearty wish that such a matter could be brought about.\n\n“Could be brought about!” says the exciseman: “why, there is nothing easier.”\n\n“Ah! sir,” answered Partridge, “you don't know what a devil of a fellow he is. He can take me up with one hand, and throw me out at window; and he would, too, if he did but imagine--”\n\n“Pogh!” says the exciseman, “I believe I am as good a man as he. Besides, here are five of us.”\n\n“I don't know what five,” cries the landlady, “my husband shall have nothing to do in it. Nor shall any violent hands be laid upon anybody in my house. The young gentleman is as pretty a young gentleman as ever I saw in my life, and I believe he is no more mad than any of us. What do you tell of his having a wild look with his eyes? they are the prettiest eyes I ever saw, and he hath the prettiest look with them; and a very modest civil young man he is. I am sure I have bepitied him heartily ever since the gentleman there in the corner told us he was crost in love. Certainly that is enough to make any man, especially such a sweet young gentleman as he is, to look a little otherwise than he did before. Lady, indeed! what the devil would the lady have better than such a handsome man with a great estate? I suppose she is one of your quality folks, one of your Townly ladies that we saw last night in the puppet-show, who don't know what they would be at.”\n\nThe attorney's clerk likewise declared he would have no concern in the business without the advice of counsel. “Suppose,” says he, “an action of false imprisonment should be brought against us, what defence could we make? Who knows what may be sufficient evidence of madness to a jury? But I only speak upon my own account; for it don't look well for a lawyer to be concerned in these matters, unless it be as a lawyer. Juries are always less favourable to us than to other people. I don't therefore dissuade you, Mr Thomson (to the exciseman), nor the gentleman, nor anybody else.”\n\nThe exciseman shook his head at this speech, and the puppet-show man said, “Madness was sometimes a difficult matter for a jury to decide: for I remember,” says he, “I was once present at a tryal of madness, where twenty witnesses swore that the person was as mad as a March hare; and twenty others, that he was as much in his senses as any man in England.--And indeed it was the opinion of most people, that it was only a trick of his relations to rob the poor man of his right.”\n\n“Very likely!” cries the landlady. “I myself knew a poor gentleman who was kept in a mad-house all his life by his family, and they enjoyed his estate, but it did them no good; for though the law gave it them, it was the right of another.”\n\n“Pogh!” cries the clerk, with great contempt, “who hath any right but what the law gives them? If the law gave me the best estate in the country, I should never trouble myself much who had the right.”\n\n“If it be so,” says Partridge, “Felix quem faciunt aliena pericula cautum.”\n\nMy landlord, who had been called out by the arrival of a horseman at the gate, now returned into the kitchen, and with an affrighted countenance cried out, “What do you think, gentlemen? The rebels have given the duke the slip, and are got almost to London. It is certainly true, for a man on horseback just now told me so.”\n\n“I am glad of it with all my heart,” cries Partridge; “then there will be no fighting in these parts.”\n\n“I am glad,” cries the clerk, “for a better reason; for I would always have right take place.”\n\n“Ay, but,” answered the landlord, “I have heard some people say this man hath no right.”\n\n“I will prove the contrary in a moment,” cries the clerk: “if my father dies seized of a right; do you mind me, seized of a right, I say; doth not that right descend to his son; and doth not one right descend as well as another?”\n\n“But how can he have any right to make us papishes?” says the landlord.\n\n“Never fear that,” cries Partridge. “As to the matter of right, the gentleman there hath proved it as clear as the sun; and as to the matter of religion, it is quite out of the case. The papists themselves don't expect any such thing. A popish priest, whom I know very well, and who is a very honest man, told me upon his word and honour they had no such design.”\n\n“And another priest, of my acquaintance,” said the landlady, “hath told me the same thing; but my husband is always so afraid of papishes. I know a great many papishes that are very honest sort of people, and spend their money very freely; and it is always a maxim with me, that one man's money is as good as another's.”\n\n“Very true, mistress,” said the puppet-show man, “I don't care what religion comes; provided the Presbyterians are not uppermost; for they are enemies to puppet-shows.”\n\n“And so you would sacrifice your religion to your interest,” cries the exciseman; “and are desirous to see popery brought in, are you?”\n\n“Not I, truly,” answered the other; “I hate popery as much as any man; but yet it is a comfort to one, that one should be able to live under it, which I could not do among Presbyterians. To be sure, every man values his livelihood first; that must be granted; and I warrant, if you would confess the truth, you are more afraid of losing your place than anything else; but never fear, friend, there will be an excise under another government as well as under this.”\n\n“Why, certainly,” replied the exciseman, “I should be a very ill man if I did not honour the king, whose bread I eat. That is no more than natural, as a man may say: for what signifies it to me that there would be an excise-office under another government, since my friends would be out, and I could expect no better than to follow them? No, no, friend, I shall never be bubbled out of my religion in hopes only of keeping my place under another government; for I should certainly be no better, and very probably might be worse.”\n\n“Why, that is what I say,” cries the landlord, “whenever folks say who knows what may happen! Odsooks! should not I be a blockhead to lend my money to I know not who, because mayhap he may return it again? I am sure it is safe in my own bureau, and there I will keep it.”\n\nThe attorney's clerk had taken a great fancy to the sagacity of Partridge. Whether this proceeded from the great discernment which the former had into men, as well as things, or whether it arose from the sympathy between their minds; for they were both truly Jacobites in principle; they now shook hands heartily, and drank bumpers of strong beer to healths which we think proper to bury in oblivion.\n\nThese healths were afterwards pledged by all present, and even by my landlord himself, though reluctantly; but he could not withstand the menaces of the clerk, who swore he would never set his foot within his house again, if he refused. The bumpers which were swallowed on this occasion soon put an end to the conversation. Here, therefore, we will put an end to the chapter.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nIn which fortune seems to have been in a better humour with Jones than we have hitherto seen her.\n\nAs there is no wholesomer, so perhaps there are few stronger, sleeping potions than fatigue. Of this Jones might be said to have taken a very large dose, and it operated very forcibly upon him. He had already slept nine hours, and might perhaps have slept longer, had he not been awakened by a most violent noise at his chamber-door, where the sound of many heavy blows was accompanied with many exclamations of murder. Jones presently leapt from his bed, where he found the master of the puppet-show belabouring the back and ribs of his poor Merry-Andrew, without either mercy or moderation.\n\nJones instantly interposed on behalf of the suffering party, and pinned the insulting conqueror up to the wall: for the puppet-show man was no more able to contend with Jones than the poor party-coloured jester had been to contend with this puppet-man.\n\nBut though the Merry-Andrew was a little fellow, and not very strong, he had nevertheless some choler about him. He therefore no sooner found himself delivered from the enemy, than he began to attack him with the only weapon at which he was his equal. From this he first discharged a volley of general abusive words, and thence proceeded to some particular accusations--“D--n your bl--d, you rascal,” says he, “I have not only supported you (for to me you owe all the money you get), but I have saved you from the gallows. Did you not want to rob the lady of her fine riding-habit, no longer ago than yesterday, in the back-lane here? Can you deny that you wished to have her alone in a wood to strip her--to strip one of the prettiest ladies that ever was seen in the world? and here you have fallen upon me, and have almost murdered me, for doing no harm to a girl as willing as myself, only because she likes me better than you.”\n\nJones no sooner heard this than he quitted the master, laying on him at the same time the most violent injunctions of forbearance from any further insult on the Merry-Andrew; and then taking the poor wretch with him into his own apartment, he soon learned tidings of his Sophia, whom the fellow, as he was attending his master with his drum the day before, had seen pass by. He easily prevailed with the lad to show him the exact place, and then having summoned Partridge, he departed with the utmost expedition.\n\nIt was almost eight of the clock before all matters could be got ready for his departure: for Partridge was not in any haste, nor could the reckoning be presently adjusted; and when both these were settled and over, Jones would not quit the place before he had perfectly reconciled all differences between the master and the man.\n\nWhen this was happily accomplished, he set forwards, and was by the trusty Merry-Andrew conducted to the spot by which Sophia had past; and then having handsomely rewarded his conductor, he again pushed on with the utmost eagerness, being highly delighted with the extraordinary manner in which he received his intelligence. Of this Partridge was no sooner acquainted, than he, with great earnestness, began to prophesy, and assured Jones that he would certainly have good success in the end: for, he said, “two such accidents could never have happened to direct him after his mistress, if Providence had not designed to bring them together at last.” And this was the first time that Jones lent any attention to the superstitious doctrines of his companion.\n\nThey had not gone above two miles when a violent storm of rain overtook them; and, as they happened to be at the same time in sight of an ale-house, Partridge, with much earnest entreaty, prevailed with Jones to enter, and weather the storm. Hunger is an enemy (if indeed it may be called one) which partakes more of the English than of the French disposition; for, though you subdue this never so often, it will always rally again in time; and so it did with Partridge, who was no sooner arrived within the kitchen, than he began to ask the same questions which he had asked the night before. The consequence of this was an excellent cold chine being produced upon the table, upon which not only Partridge, but Jones himself, made a very hearty breakfast, though the latter began to grow again uneasy, as the people of the house could give him no fresh information concerning Sophia.\n\nTheir meal being over, Jones was again preparing to sally, notwithstanding the violence of the storm still continued; but Partridge begged heartily for another mug; and at last casting his eyes on a lad at the fire, who had entered into the kitchen, and who at that instant was looking as earnestly at him, he turned suddenly to Jones, and cried, “Master, give me your hand, a single mug shan't serve the turn this bout. Why, here's more news of Madam Sophia come to town. The boy there standing by the fire is the very lad that rode before her. I can swear to my own plaister on his face.”--“Heavens bless you, sir,” cries the boy, “it is your own plaister sure enough; I shall have always reason to remember your goodness; for it hath almost cured me.”\n\nAt these words Jones started from his chair, and, bidding the boy follow him immediately, departed from the kitchen into a private apartment; for, so delicate was he with regard to Sophia, that he never willingly mentioned her name in the presence of many people; and, though he had, as it were, from the overflowings of his heart, given Sophia as a toast among the officers, where he thought it was impossible she should be known; yet, even there, the reader may remember how difficultly he was prevailed upon to mention her surname.\n\nHard therefore was it, and perhaps, in the opinion of many sagacious readers, very absurd and monstrous, that he should principally owe his present misfortune to the supposed want of that delicacy with which he so abounded; for, in reality, Sophia was much more offended at the freedoms which she thought (and not without good reason) he had taken with her name and character, than at any freedoms, in which, under his present circumstances, he had indulged himself with the person of another woman; and to say truth, I believe Honour could never have prevailed on her to leave Upton without her seeing Jones, had it not been for those two strong instances of a levity in his behaviour, so void of respect, and indeed so highly inconsistent with any degree of love and tenderness in great and delicate minds.\n\nBut so matters fell out, and so I must relate them; and if any reader is shocked at their appearing unnatural, I cannot help it. I must remind such persons that I am not writing a system, but a history, and I am not obliged to reconcile every matter to the received notions concerning truth and nature. But if this was never so easy to do, perhaps it might be more prudent in me to avoid it. For instance, as the fact at present before us now stands, without any comment of mine upon it, though it may at first sight offend some readers, yet, upon more mature consideration, it must please all; for wise and good men may consider what happened to Jones at Upton as a just punishment for his wickedness with regard to women, of which it was indeed the immediate consequence; and silly and bad persons may comfort themselves in their vices by flattering their own hearts that the characters of men are rather owing to accident than to virtue. Now, perhaps the reflections which we should be here inclined to draw would alike contradict both these conclusions, and would show that these incidents contribute only to confirm the great, useful, and uncommon doctrine, which it is the purpose of this whole work to inculcate, and which we must not fill up our pages by frequently repeating, as an ordinary parson fills his sermon by repeating his text at the end of every paragraph.\n\nWe are contented that it must appear, however unhappily Sophia had erred in her opinion of Jones, she had sufficient reason for her opinion; since, I believe, every other young lady would, in her situation, have erred in the same manner. Nay, had she followed her lover at this very time, and had entered this very alehouse the moment he was departed from it, she would have found the landlord as well acquainted with her name and person as the wench at Upton had appeared to be. For while Jones was examining his boy in whispers in an inner room, Partridge, who had no such delicacy in his disposition, was in the kitchen very openly catechising the other guide who had attended Mrs Fitzpatrick; by which means the landlord, whose ears were open on all such occasions, became perfectly well acquainted with the tumble of Sophia from her horse, &c., with the mistake concerning Jenny Cameron, with the many consequences of the punch, and, in short, with almost everything which had happened at the inn whence we despatched our ladies in a coach-and-six when we last took our leaves of them.\n\nContaining little more than a few odd observations.\n\nJones had been absent a full half-hour, when he returned into the kitchen in a hurry, desiring the landlord to let him know that instant what was to pay. And now the concern which Partridge felt at being obliged to quit the warm chimney-corner, and a cup of excellent liquor, was somewhat compensated by hearing that he was to proceed no farther on foot, for Jones, by golden arguments, had prevailed with the boy to attend him back to the inn whither he had before conducted Sophia; but to this however the lad consented, upon condition that the other guide would wait for him at the alehouse; because, as the landlord at Upton was an intimate acquaintance of the landlord at Gloucester, it might some time or other come to the ears of the latter that his horses had been let to more than one person; and so the boy might be brought to account for money which he wisely intended to put in his own pocket.\n\nWe were obliged to mention this circumstance, trifling as it may seem, since it retarded Mr Jones a considerable time in his setting out; for the honesty of this latter boy was somewhat high--that is, somewhat high-priced, and would indeed have cost Jones very dear, had not Partridge, who, as we have said, was a very cunning fellow, artfully thrown in half-a-crown to be spent at that very alehouse, while the boy was waiting for his companion. This half-crown the landlord no sooner got scent of, than he opened after it with such vehement and persuasive outcry, that the boy was soon overcome, and consented to take half-a-crown more for his stay. Here we cannot help observing, that as there is so much of policy in the lowest life, great men often overvalue themselves on those refinements in imposture, in which they are frequently excelled by some of the lowest of the human species.\n\nThe horses being now produced, Jones directly leapt into the side-saddle, on which his dear Sophia had rid. The lad, indeed, very civilly offered him the use of his; but he chose the side-saddle, probably because it was softer. Partridge, however, though full as effeminate as Jones, could not bear the thoughts of degrading his manhood; he therefore accepted the boy's offer: and now, Jones being mounted on the side-saddle of his Sophia, the boy on that of Mrs Honour, and Partridge bestriding the third horse, they set forwards on their journey, and within four hours arrived at the inn where the reader hath already spent so much time. Partridge was in very high spirits during the whole way, and often mentioned to Jones the many good omens of his future success which had lately befriended him; and which the reader, without being the least superstitious, must allow to have been particularly fortunate. Partridge was moreover better pleased with the present pursuit of his companion than he had been with his pursuit of glory; and from these very omens, which assured the pedagogue of success, he likewise first acquired a clear idea of the amour between Jones and Sophia; to which he had before given very little attention, as he had originally taken a wrong scent concerning the reasons of Jones's departure; and as to what happened at Upton, he was too much frightened just before and after his leaving that place to draw any other conclusions from thence than that poor Jones was a downright madman: a conceit which was not at all disagreeable to the opinion he before had of his extraordinary wildness, of which, he thought, his behaviour on their quitting Gloucester so well justified all the accounts he had formerly received. He was now, however, pretty well satisfied with his present expedition, and henceforth began to conceive much worthier sentiments of his friend's understanding.\n\nThe clock had just struck three when they arrived, and Jones immediately bespoke post-horses; but unluckily there was not a horse to be procured in the whole place; which the reader will not wonder at when he considers the hurry in which the whole nation, and especially this part of it, was at this time engaged, when expresses were passing and repassing every hour of the day and night.\n\nJones endeavoured all he could to prevail with his former guide to escorte him to Coventry; but he was inexorable. While he was arguing with the boy in the inn-yard, a person came up to him, and saluting him by his name, enquired how all the good family did in Somersetshire; and now Jones casting his eyes upon this person, presently discovered him to be Mr Dowling, the lawyer, with whom he had dined at Gloucester, and with much courtesy returned the salutation.\n\nDowling very earnestly pressed Mr Jones to go no further that night; and backed his solicitations with many unanswerable arguments, such as, that it was almost dark, that the roads were very dirty, and that he would be able to travel much better by day-light, with many others equally good, some of which Jones had probably suggested to himself before; but as they were then ineffectual, so they were still: and he continued resolute in his design, even though he should be obliged to set out on foot.\n\nWhen the good attorney found he could not prevail on Jones to stay, he as strenuously applied himself to persuade the guide to accompany him. He urged many motives to induce him to undertake this short journey, and at last concluded with saying, “Do you think the gentleman won't very well reward you for your trouble?”\n\nTwo to one are odds at every other thing as well as at foot-ball. But the advantage which this united force hath in persuasion or entreaty must have been visible to a curious observer; for he must have often seen, that when a father, a master, a wife, or any other person in authority, have stoutly adhered to a denial against all the reasons which a single man could produce, they have afterwards yielded to the repetition of the same sentiments by a second or third person, who hath undertaken the cause, without attempting to advance anything new in its behalf. And hence, perhaps, proceeds the phrase of seconding an argument or a motion, and the great consequence this is of in all assemblies of public debate. Hence, likewise, probably it is, that in our courts of law we often hear a learned gentleman (generally a serjeant) repeating for an hour together what another learned gentleman, who spoke just before him, had been saying.\n\nInstead of accounting for this, we shall proceed in our usual manner to exemplify it in the conduct of the lad above mentioned, who submitted to the persuasions of Mr Dowling, and promised once more to admit Jones into his side-saddle; but insisted on first giving the poor creatures a good bait, saying, they had travelled a great way, and been rid very hard. Indeed this caution of the boy was needless; for Jones, notwithstanding his hurry and impatience, would have ordered this of himself; for he by no means agreed with the opinion of those who consider animals as mere machines, and when they bury their spurs in the belly of their horse, imagine the spur and the horse to have an equal capacity of feeling pain.\n\nWhile the beasts were eating their corn, or rather were supposed to eat it (for, as the boy was taking care of himself in the kitchen, the ostler took great care that his corn should not be consumed in the stable), Mr Jones, at the earnest desire of Mr Dowling, accompanied that gentleman into his room, where they sat down together over a bottle of wine.\n\nIn which Mr Jones and Mr Dowling drink a bottle together.\n\nMr Dowling, pouring out a glass of wine, named the health of the good Squire Allworthy; adding, “If you please, sir, we will likewise remember his nephew and heir, the young squire: Come, sir, here's Mr Blifil to you, a very pretty young gentleman; and who, I dare swear, will hereafter make a very considerable figure in his country. I have a borough for him myself in my eye.”\n\n“Sir,” answered Jones, “I am convinced you don't intend to affront me, so I shall not resent it; but I promise you, you have joined two persons very improperly together; for one is the glory of the human species, and the other is a rascal who dishonours the name of man.”\n\nDowling stared at this. He said, “He thought both the gentlemen had a very unexceptionable character. As for Squire Allworthy himself,” says he, “I never had the happiness to see him; but all the world talks of his goodness. And, indeed, as to the young gentleman, I never saw him but once, when I carried him the news of the loss of his mother; and then I was so hurried, and drove, and tore with the multiplicity of business, that I had hardly time to converse with him; but he looked so like a very honest gentleman, and behaved himself so prettily, that I protest I never was more delighted with any gentleman since I was born.”\n\n“I don't wonder,” answered Jones, “that he should impose upon you in so short an acquaintance; for he hath the cunning of the devil himself, and you may live with him many years, without discovering him. I was bred up with him from my infancy, and we were hardly ever asunder; but it is very lately only that I have discovered half the villany which is in him. I own I never greatly liked him. I thought he wanted that generosity of spirit, which is the sure foundation of all that is great and noble in human nature. I saw a selfishness in him long ago which I despised; but it is lately, very lately, that I have found him capable of the basest and blackest designs; for, indeed, I have at last found out, that he hath taken an advantage of the openness of my own temper, and hath concerted the deepest project, by a long train of wicked artifice, to work my ruin, which at last he hath effected.”\n\n“Ay! ay!” cries Dowling; “I protest, then, it is a pity such a person should inherit the great estate of your uncle Allworthy.”\n\n“Alas, sir,” cries Jones, “you do me an honour to which I have no title. It is true, indeed, his goodness once allowed me the liberty of calling him by a much nearer name; but as this was only a voluntary act of goodness, I can complain of no injustice when he thinks proper to deprive me of this honour; since the loss cannot be more unmerited than the gift originally was. I assure you, sir, I am no relation of Mr Allworthy; and if the world, who are incapable of setting a true value on his virtue, should think, in his behaviour to me, he hath dealt hardly by a relation, they do an injustice to the best of men: for I--but I ask your pardon, I shall trouble you with no particulars relating to myself; only as you seemed to think me a relation of Mr Allworthy, I thought proper to set you right in a matter that might draw some censures upon him, which I promise you I would rather lose my life than give occasion to.”\n\n“I protest, sir,” cried Dowling, “you talk very much like a man of honour; but instead of giving me any trouble, I protest it would give me great pleasure to know how you came to be thought a relation of Mr Allworthy's, if you are not. Your horses won't be ready this half-hour, and as you have sufficient opportunity, I wish you would tell me how all that happened; for I protest it seems very surprizing that you should pass for a relation of a gentleman, without being so.”\n\nJones, who in the compliance of his disposition (though not in his prudence) a little resembled his lovely Sophia, was easily prevailed on to satisfy Mr Dowling's curiosity, by relating the history of his birth and education, which he did, like Othello.\n\n------Even from his boyish years, To th' very moment he was bad to tell:\n\nthe which to hear, Dowling, like Desdemona, did seriously incline;\n\nHe swore 'twas strange, 'twas passing strange; 'Twas pitiful, 'twas wonderous pitiful.\n\nMr Dowling was indeed very greatly affected with this relation; for he had not divested himself of humanity by being an attorney. Indeed, nothing is more unjust than to carry our prejudices against a profession into private life, and to borrow our idea of a man from our opinion of his calling. Habit, it is true, lessens the horror of those actions which the profession makes necessary, and consequently habitual; but in all other instances, Nature works in men of all professions alike; nay, perhaps, even more strongly with those who give her, as it were, a holiday, when they are following their ordinary business. A butcher, I make no doubt, would feel compunction at the slaughter of a fine horse; and though a surgeon can feel no pain in cutting off a limb, I have known him compassionate a man in a fit of the gout. The common hangman, who hath stretched the necks of hundreds, is known to have trembled at his first operation on a head: and the very professors of human blood-shedding, who, in their trade of war, butcher thousands, not only of their fellow-professors, but often of women and children, without remorse; even these, I say, in times of peace, when drums and trumpets are laid aside, often lay aside all their ferocity, and become very gentle members of civil society. In the same manner an attorney may feel all the miseries and distresses of his fellow-creatures, provided he happens not to be concerned against them.\n\nJones, as the reader knows, was yet unacquainted with the very black colours in which he had been represented to Mr Allworthy; and as to other matters, he did not shew them in the most disadvantageous light; for though he was unwilling to cast any blame on his former friend and patron; yet he was not very desirous of heaping too much upon himself. Dowling therefore observed, and not without reason, that very ill offices must have been done him by somebody: “For certainly,” cries he, “the squire would never have disinherited you only for a few faults, which any young gentleman might have committed. Indeed, I cannot properly say disinherited: for to be sure by law you cannot claim as heir. That's certain; that nobody need go to counsel for. Yet when a gentleman had in a manner adopted you thus as his own son, you might reasonably have expected some very considerable part, if not the whole; nay, if you had expected the whole, I should not have blamed you: for certainly all men are for getting as much as they can, and they are not to be blamed on that account.”\n\n“Indeed you wrong me,” said Jones; “I should have been contented with very little: I never had any view upon Mr Allworthy's fortune; nay, I believe I may truly say, I never once considered what he could or might give me. This I solemnly declare, if he had done a prejudice to his nephew in my favour, I would have undone it again. I had rather enjoy my own mind than the fortune of another man. What is the poor pride arising from a magnificent house, a numerous equipage, a splendid table, and from all the other advantages or appearances of fortune, compared to the warm, solid content, the swelling satisfaction, the thrilling transports, and the exulting triumphs, which a good mind enjoys, in the contemplation of a generous, virtuous, noble, benevolent action? I envy not Blifil in the prospect of his wealth; nor shall I envy him in the possession of it. I would not think myself a rascal half an hour, to exchange situations. I believe, indeed, Mr Blifil suspected me of the views you mention; and I suppose these suspicions, as they arose from the baseness of his own heart, so they occasioned his baseness to me. But, I thank Heaven, I know, I feel--I feel my innocence, my friend; and I would not part with that feeling for the world. For as long as I know I have never done, nor even designed, an injury to any being whatever,\n\n_Pone me pigris ubi nulla campis Arbor aestiva recreatur aura, Quod latus mundi nebulae, malusque Jupiter urget.\n\nPone sub curru nimium propinqui Solis in terra dominibus negata; Dulce ridentem Lalagen amabo, Dulce loquentem._[*]\n\n[*] Place me where never summer breeze Unbinds the glebe, or warms the trees: Where ever-lowering clouds appear, And angry Jove deforms th' inclement year.\n\nPlace me beneath the burning ray, Where rolls the rapid car of day; Love and the nymph shall charm my toils, The nymph who sweetly speaks, and sweetly smiles. MR FRANCIS.\n\nHe then filled a bumper of wine, and drunk it off to the health of his dear Lalage; and, filling Dowling's glass likewise up to the brim, insisted on his pledging him. “Why, then, here's Miss Lalage's health with all my heart,” cries Dowling. “I have heard her toasted often, I protest, though I never saw her; but they say she's extremely handsome.”\n\nThough the Latin was not the only part of this speech which Dowling did not perfectly understand; yet there was somewhat in it that made a very strong impression upon him. And though he endeavoured by winking, nodding, sneering, and grinning, to hide the impression from Jones (for we are as often ashamed of thinking right as of thinking wrong), it is certain he secretly approved as much of his sentiments as he understood, and really felt a very strong impulse of compassion for him. But we may possibly take some other opportunity of commenting upon this, especially if we should happen to meet Mr Dowling any more in the course of our history. At present we are obliged to take our leave of that gentleman a little abruptly, in imitation of Mr Jones; who was no sooner informed, by Partridge, that his horses were ready, than he deposited his reckoning, wished his companion a good night, mounted, and set forward towards Coventry, though the night was dark, and it just then began to rain very hard.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\nThe disasters which befel Jones on his departure for Coventry; with the sage remarks of Partridge.\nNo road can be plainer than that from the place where they now were to Coventry; and though neither Jones, nor Partridge, nor the guide, had ever travelled it before, it would have been almost impossible to have missed their way, had it not been for the two reasons mentioned in the conclusion of the last chapter.\n\nThese two circumstances, however, happening both unfortunately to intervene, our travellers deviated into a much less frequented track; and after riding full six miles, instead of arriving at the stately spires of Coventry, they found themselves still in a very dirty lane, where they saw no symptoms of approaching the suburbs of a large city.\n\nJones now declared that they must certainly have lost their way; but this the guide insisted upon was impossible; a word which, in common conversation, is often used to signify not only improbable, but often what is really very likely, and, sometimes, what hath certainly happened; an hyperbolical violence like that which is so frequently offered to the words infinite and eternal; by the former of which it is usual to express a distance of half a yard, and by the latter, a duration of five minutes. And thus it is as usual to assert the impossibility of losing what is already actually lost. This was, in fact, the case at present; for, notwithstanding all the confident assertions of the lad to the contrary, it is certain they were no more in the right road to Coventry, than the fraudulent, griping, cruel, canting miser is in the right road to heaven.\n\nIt is not, perhaps, easy for a reader, who hath never been in those circumstances, to imagine the horror with which darkness, rain, and wind, fill persons who have lost their way in the night; and who, consequently, have not the pleasant prospect of warm fires, dry cloaths, and other refreshments, to support their minds in struggling with the inclemencies of the weather. A very imperfect idea of this horror will, however, serve sufficiently to account for the conceits which now filled the head of Partridge, and which we shall presently be obliged to open.\n\nJones grew more and more positive that they were out of their road; and the boy himself at last acknowledged he believed they were not in the right road to Coventry; though he affirmed, at the same time, it was impossible they should have mist the way. But Partridge was of a different opinion. He said, “When they first set out he imagined some mischief or other would happen.--Did not you observe, sir,” said he to Jones, “that old woman who stood at the door just as you was taking horse? I wish you had given her a small matter, with all my heart; for she said then you might repent it; and at that very instant it began to rain, and the wind hath continued rising ever since. Whatever some people may think, I am very certain it is in the power of witches to raise the wind whenever they please. I have seen it happen very often in my time: and if ever I saw a witch in all my life, that old woman was certainly one. I thought so to myself at that very time; and if I had had any halfpence in my pocket, I would have given her some; for to be sure it is always good to be charitable to those sort of people, for fear what may happen; and many a person hath lost his cattle by saving a halfpenny.”\n\nJones, though he was horridly vexed at the delay which this mistake was likely to occasion in his journey, could not help smiling at the superstition of his friend, whom an accident now greatly confirmed in his opinion. This was a tumble from his horse; by which, however, he received no other injury than what the dirt conferred on his cloaths.\n\nPartridge had no sooner recovered his legs, than he appealed to his fall, as conclusive evidence of all he had asserted; but Jones finding he was unhurt, answered with a smile: “This witch of yours, Partridge, is a most ungrateful jade, and doth not, I find, distinguish her friends from others in her resentment. If the old lady had been angry with me for neglecting her, I don't see why she should tumble you from your horse, after all the respect you have expressed for her.”\n\n“It is ill jesting,” cries Partridge, “with people who have power to do these things; for they are often very malicious. I remember a farrier, who provoked one of them, by asking her when the time she had bargained with the devil for would be out; and within three months from that very day one of his best cows was drowned. Nor was she satisfied with that; for a little time afterwards he lost a barrel of best-drink: for the old witch pulled out the spigot, and let it run all over the cellar, the very first evening he had tapped it to make merry with some of his neighbours. In short, nothing ever thrived with him afterwards; for she worried the poor man so, that he took to drinking; and in a year or two his stock was seized, and he and his family are now come to the parish.”\n\nThe guide, and perhaps his horse too, were both so attentive to this discourse, that, either through want of care, or by the malice of the witch, they were now both sprawling in the dirt.\n\nPartridge entirely imputed this fall, as he had done his own, to the same cause. He told Mr Jones, “It would certainly be his turn next; and earnestly entreated him to return back, and find out the old woman, and pacify her. We shall very soon,” added he, “reach the inn; for though we have seemed to go forward, I am very certain we are in the identical place in which we were an hour ago; and I dare swear, if it was daylight, we might now see the inn we set out from.”\n\nInstead of returning any answer to this sage advice, Jones was entirely attentive to what had happened to the boy, who received no other hurt than what had before befallen Partridge, and which his cloaths very easily bore, as they had been for many years inured to the like. He soon regained his side-saddle, and by the hearty curses and blows which he bestowed on his horse, quickly satisfied Mr Jones that no harm was done.\n\n# Chapter xii.\n\nRelates that Mr Jones continued his journey, contrary to the advice of Partridge, with what happened on that occasion.\n\nThey now discovered a light at some distance, to the great pleasure of Jones, and to the no small terror of Partridge, who firmly believed himself to be bewitched, and that this light was a Jack-with-a-lantern, or somewhat more mischievous.\n\nBut how were these fears increased, when, as they approached nearer to this light (or lights as they now appeared), they heard a confused sound of human voices; of singing, laughing, and hallowing, together with a strange noise that seemed to proceed from some instruments; but could hardly be allowed the name of music! indeed, to favour a little the opinion of Partridge, it might very well be called music bewitched.\n\nIt is impossible to conceive a much greater degree of horror than what now seized on Partridge; the contagion of which had reached the post-boy, who had been very attentive to many things that the other had uttered. He now, therefore, joined in petitioning Jones to return; saying he firmly believed what Partridge had just before said, that though the horses seemed to go on, they had not moved a step forwards during at least the last half-hour.\n\nJones could not help smiling in the midst of his vexation, at the fears of these poor fellows. “Either we advance,” says he, “towards the lights, or the lights have advanced towards us; for we are now at a very little distance from them; but how can either of you be afraid of a set of people who appear only to be merry-making?”\n\n“Merry-making, sir!” cries Partridge; “who could be merry-making at this time of night, and in such a place, and such weather? They can be nothing but ghosts or witches, or some evil spirits or other, that's certain.”\n\n“Let them be what they will,” cries Jones, “I am resolved to go up to them, and enquire the way to Coventry. All witches, Partridge, are not such ill-natured hags as that we had the misfortune to meet with last.”\n\n“O Lord, sir,” cries Partridge, “there is no knowing what humour they will be in; to be sure it is always best to be civil to them; but what if we should meet with something worse than witches, with evil spirits themselves?----Pray, sir, be advised; pray, sir, do. If you had read so many terrible accounts as I have of these matters, you would not be so fool-hardy.----The Lord knows whither we have got already, or whither we are going; for sure such darkness was never seen upon earth, and I question whether it can be darker in the other world.”\n\nJones put forwards as fast as he could, notwithstanding all these hints and cautions, and poor Partridge was obliged to follow; for though he hardly dared to advance, he dared still less to stay behind by himself.\n\nAt length they arrived at the place whence the lights and different noises had issued. This Jones perceived to be no other than a barn, where a great number of men and women were assembled, and diverting themselves with much apparent jollity.\n\nJones no sooner appeared before the great doors of the barn, which were open, than a masculine and very rough voice from within demanded, who was there?--To which Jones gently answered, a friend; and immediately asked the road to Coventry.\n\n“If you are a friend,” cries another of the men in the barn, “you had better alight till the storm is over” (for indeed it was now more violent than ever;) “you are very welcome to put up your horse; for there is sufficient room for him at the end of the barn.”\n\n“You are very obliging,” returned Jones; “and I will accept your offer for a few minutes, whilst the rain continues; and here are two more who will be glad of the same favour.” This was accorded with more good-will than it was accepted: for Partridge would rather have submitted to the utmost inclemency of the weather than have trusted to the clemency of those whom he took for hobgoblins; and the poor post-boy was now infected with the same apprehensions; but they were both obliged to follow the example of Jones; the one because he durst not leave his horse, and the other because he feared nothing so much as being left by himself.\n\nHad this history been writ in the days of superstition, I should have had too much compassion for the reader to have left him so long in suspense, whether Beelzebub or Satan was about actually to appear in person, with all his hellish retinue; but as these doctrines are at present very unfortunate, and have but few, if any believers, I have not been much aware of conveying any such terrors. To say truth, the whole furniture of the infernal regions hath long been appropriated by the managers of playhouses, who seem lately to have laid them by as rubbish, capable only of affecting the upper gallery; a place in which few of our readers ever sit.\n\nHowever, though we do not suspect raising any great terror on this occasion, we have reason to fear some other apprehensions may here arise in our reader, into which we would not willingly betray him; I mean that we are going to take a voyage into fairy-land, and introduce a set of beings into our history, which scarce any one was ever childish enough to believe, though many have been foolish enough to spend their time in writing and reading their adventures.\n\nTo prevent, therefore, any such suspicions, so prejudicial to the credit of an historian, who professes to draw his materials from nature only, we shall now proceed to acquaint the reader who these people were, whose sudden appearance had struck such terrors into Partridge, had more than half frightened the post-boy, and had a little surprized even Mr Jones himself.\n\nThe people then assembled in this barn were no other than a company of Egyptians, or, as they are vulgarly called, gypsies, and they were now celebrating the wedding of one of their society.\n\nIt is impossible to conceive a happier set of people than appeared here to be met together. The utmost mirth, indeed, shewed itself in every countenance; nor was their ball totally void of all order and decorum. Perhaps it had more than a country assembly is sometimes conducted with: for these people are subject to a formal government and laws of their own, and all pay obedience to one great magistrate, whom they call their king.\n\nGreater plenty, likewise, was nowhere to be seen than what flourished in this barn. Here was indeed no nicety nor elegance, nor did the keen appetite of the guests require any. Here was good store of bacon, fowls, and mutton, to which every one present provided better sauce himself than the best and dearest French cook can prepare.\n\nAeneas is not described under more consternation in the temple of Juno,\n\nDum stupet obtutuque haeret defixus in uno,\n\nthan was our heroe at what he saw in this barn. While he was looking everywhere round him with astonishment, a venerable person approached him with many friendly salutations, rather of too hearty a kind to be called courtly. This was no other than the king of the gypsies himself. He was very little distinguished in dress from his subjects, nor had he any regalia of majesty to support his dignity; and yet there seemed (as Mr Jones said) to be somewhat in his air which denoted authority, and inspired the beholders with an idea of awe and respect; though all this was perhaps imaginary in Jones; and the truth may be, that such ideas are incident to power, and almost inseparable from it.\n\nThere was somewhat in the open countenance and courteous behaviour of Jones which, being accompanied with much comeliness of person, greatly recommended him at first sight to every beholder. These were, perhaps, a little heightened in the present instance, by that profound respect which he paid to the king of the gypsies, the moment he was acquainted with his dignity, and which was the sweeter to his gypseian majesty, as he was not used to receive such homage from any but his own subjects.\n\nThe king ordered a table to be spread with the choicest of their provisions for his accommodation; and, having placed himself at his right hand, his majesty began to discourse with our heroe in the following manner:--\n\n“Me doubt not, sir, but you have often seen some of my people, who are what you call de parties detache: for dey go about everywhere; but me fancy you imagine not we be so considrable body as we be; and may be you will be surprize more when you hear de gypsy be as orderly and well govern people as any upon face of de earth.\n\n“Me have honour, as me say, to be deir king, and no monarch can do boast of more dutiful subject, ne no more affectionate. How far me deserve deir good-will, me no say; but dis me can say, dat me never design anyting but to do dem good. Me sall no do boast of dat neider: for what can me do oderwise dan consider of de good of dose poor people who go about all day to give me always de best of what dey get. Dey love and honour me darefore, because me do love and take care of dem; dat is all, me know no oder reason.\n\n“About a tousand or two tousand year ago, me cannot tell to a year or two, as can neider write nor read, dere was a great what you call--a volution among de gypsy; for dere was de lord gypsy in dose days; and dese lord did quarrel vid one anoder about de place; but de king of de gypsy did demolish dem all, and made all his subject equal vid each oder; and since dat time dey have agree very well; for dey no tink of being king, and may be it be better for dem as dey be; for me assure you it be ver troublesome ting to be king, and always to do justice; me have often wish to be de private gypsy when me have been forced to punish my dear friend and relation; for dough we never put to death, our punishments be ver severe. Dey make de gypsy ashamed of demselves, and dat be ver terrible punishment; me ave scarce ever known de gypsy so punish do harm any more.”\n\nThe king then proceeded to express some wonder that there was no such punishment as shame in other governments. Upon which Jones assured him to the contrary; for that there were many crimes for which shame was inflicted by the English laws, and that it was indeed one consequence of all punishment. “Dat be ver strange,” said the king; “for me know and hears good deal of your people, dough me no live among dem; and me have often hear dat sham is de consequence and de cause too of many of your rewards. Are your rewards and punishments den de same ting?”\n\nWhile his majesty was thus discoursing with Jones, a sudden uproar arose in the barn, and as it seems upon this occasion:--the courtesy of these people had by degrees removed all the apprehensions of Partridge, and he was prevailed upon not only to stuff himself with their food, but to taste some of their liquors, which by degrees entirely expelled all fear from his composition, and in its stead introduced much more agreeable sensations.\n\nA young female gypsy, more remarkable for her wit than her beauty, had decoyed the honest fellow aside, pretending to tell his fortune. Now, when they were alone together in a remote part of the barn, whether it proceeded from the strong liquor, which is never so apt to inflame inordinate desire as after moderate fatigue; or whether the fair gypsy herself threw aside the delicacy and decency of her sex, and tempted the youth Partridge with express solicitations; but they were discovered in a very improper manner by the husband of the gypsy, who, from jealousy it seems, had kept a watchful eye over his wife, and had dogged her to the place, where he found her in the arms of her gallant.\n\nTo the great confusion of Jones, Partridge was now hurried before the king; who heard the accusation, and likewise the culprit's defence, which was indeed very trifling; for the poor fellow was confounded by the plain evidence which appeared against him, and had very little to say for himself. His majesty, then turning towards Jones, said, “Sir, you have hear what dey say; what punishment do you tink your man deserve?”\n\nJones answered, “He was sorry for what had happened, and that Partridge should make the husband all the amends in his power: he said, he had very little money about him at that time;” and, putting his hand into his pocket, offered the fellow a guinea. To which he immediately answered, “He hoped his honour would not think of giving him less than five.”\n\nThis sum, after some altercation, was reduced to two; and Jones, having stipulated for the full forgiveness of both Partridge and the wife, was going to pay the money; when his majesty, restraining his hand, turned to the witness and asked him, “At what time he had discovered the criminals?” To which he answered, “That he had been desired by the husband to watch the motions of his wife from her first speaking to the stranger, and that he had never lost sight of her afterwards till the crime had been committed.” The king then asked, “if the husband was with him all that time in his lurking-place?” To which he answered in the affirmative. His Egyptian majesty then addressed himself to the husband as follows: “Me be sorry to see any gypsy dat have no more honour dan to sell de honour of his wife for money. If you had de love for your wife, you would have prevented dis matter, and not endeavour to make her de whore dat you might discover her. Me do order dat you have no money given you, for you deserve punishment, not reward; me do order derefore, dat you be de infamous gypsy, and do wear pair of horns upon your forehead for one month, and dat your wife be called de whore, and pointed at all dat time; for you be de infamous gypsy, but she be no less de infamous whore.”\n\nThe gypsies immediately proceeded to execute the sentence, and left Jones and Partridge alone with his majesty.\n\nJones greatly applauded the justice of the sentence: upon which the king, turning to him, said, “Me believe you be surprize: for me suppose you have ver bad opinion of my people; me suppose you tink us all de tieves.”\n\n“I must confess, sir,” said Jones, “I have not heard so favourable an account of them as they seem to deserve.”\n\n“Me vil tell you,” said the king, “how the difference is between you and us. My people rob your people, and your people rob one anoder.”\n\nJones afterwards proceeded very gravely to sing forth the happiness of those subjects who live under such a magistrate.\n\nIndeed their happiness appears to have been so compleat, that we are aware lest some advocate for arbitrary power should hereafter quote the case of those people, as an instance of the great advantages which attend that government above all others.\n\nAnd here we will make a concession, which would not perhaps have been expected from us, that no limited form of government is capable of rising to the same degree of perfection, or of producing the same benefits to society, with this. Mankind have never been so happy, as when the greatest part of the then known world was under the dominion of a single master; and this state of their felicity continued during the reigns of five successive princes.[*] This was the true aera of the golden age, and the only golden age which ever had any existence, unless in the warm imaginations of the poets, from the expulsion from Eden down to this day.\n\n[*] Nerva, Trajan, Adrian, and the two Antonini.\n\nIn reality, I know but of one solid objection to absolute monarchy. The only defect in which excellent constitution seems to be, the difficulty of finding any man adequate to the office of an absolute monarch: for this indispensably requires three qualities very difficult, as it appears from history, to be found in princely natures: first, a sufficient quantity of moderation in the prince, to be contented with all the power which is possible for him to have. 2ndly, Enough of wisdom to know his own happiness. And, 3rdly, Goodness sufficient to support the happiness of others, when not only compatible with, but instrumental to his own.\n\nNow if an absolute monarch, with all these great and rare qualifications, should be allowed capable of conferring the greatest good on society; it must be surely granted, on the contrary, that absolute power, vested in the hands of one who is deficient in them all, is likely to be attended with no less a degree of evil.\n\nIn short, our own religion furnishes us with adequate ideas of the blessing, as well as curse, which may attend absolute power. The pictures of heaven and of hell will place a very lively image of both before our eyes; for though the prince of the latter can have no power but what he originally derives from the omnipotent Sovereign in the former, yet it plainly appears from Scripture that absolute power in his infernal dominions is granted to their diabolical ruler. This is indeed the only absolute power which can by Scripture be derived from heaven. If, therefore, the several tyrannies upon earth can prove any title to a Divine authority, it must be derived from this original grant to the prince of darkness; and these subordinate deputations must consequently come immediately from him whose stamp they so expressly bear.\n\nTo conclude, as the examples of all ages shew us that mankind in general desire power only to do harm, and, when they obtain it, use it for no other purpose; it is not consonant with even the least degree of prudence to hazard an alteration, where our hopes are poorly kept in countenance by only two or three exceptions out of a thousand instances to alarm our fears. In this case it will be much wiser to submit to a few inconveniencies arising from the dispassionate deafness of laws, than to remedy them by applying to the passionate open ears of a tyrant.\n\nNor can the example of the gypsies, though possibly they may have long been happy under this form of government, be here urged; since we must remember the very material respect in which they differ from all other people, and to which perhaps this their happiness is entirely owing, namely, that they have no false honours among them, and that they look on shame as the most grievous punishment in the world.\n\nA dialogue between Jones and Partridge.\nThe honest lovers of liberty will, we doubt not, pardon that long digression into which we were led at the close of the last chapter, to prevent our history from being applied to the use of the most pernicious doctrine which priestcraft had ever the wickedness or the impudence to preach.\n\nWe will now proceed with Mr Jones, who, when the storm was over, took leave of his Egyptian majesty, after many thanks for his courteous behaviour and kind entertainment, and set out for Coventry; to which place (for it was still dark) a gypsy was ordered to conduct him.\n\nJones having, by reason of his deviation, travelled eleven miles instead of six, and most of those through very execrable roads, where no expedition could have been made in quest of a midwife, did not arrive at Coventry till near twelve. Nor could he possibly get again into the saddle till past two; for post-horses were now not easy to get; nor were the hostler or post-boy in half so great a hurry as himself, but chose rather to imitate the tranquil disposition of Partridge; who, being denied the nourishment of sleep, took all opportunities to supply its place with every other kind of nourishment, and was never better pleased than when he arrived at an inn, nor ever more dissatisfied than when he was again forced to leave it.\n\nJones now travelled post; we will follow him, therefore, according to our custom, and to the rules of Longinus, in the same manner. From Coventry he arrived at Daventry, from Daventry at Stratford, and from Stratford at Dunstable, whither he came the next day a little after noon, and within a few hours after Sophia had left it; and though he was obliged to stay here longer than he wished, while a smith, with great deliberation, shoed the post-horse he was to ride, he doubted not but to overtake his Sophia before she should set out from St Albans; at which place he concluded, and very reasonably, that his lordship would stop and dine.\n\nAnd had he been right in this conjecture, he most probably would have overtaken his angel at the aforesaid place; but unluckily my lord had appointed a dinner to be prepared for him at his own house in London, and, in order to enable him to reach that place in proper time, he had ordered a relay of horses to meet him at St Albans. When Jones therefore arrived there, he was informed that the coach-and-six had set out two hours before.\n\nIf fresh post-horses had been now ready, as they were not, it seemed so apparently impossible to overtake the coach before it reached London, that Partridge thought he had now a proper opportunity to remind his friend of a matter which he seemed entirely to have forgotten; what this was the reader will guess, when we inform him that Jones had eat nothing more than one poached egg since he had left the alehouse where he had first met the guide returning from Sophia; for with the gypsies he had feasted only his understanding.\n\nThe landlord so entirely agreed with the opinion of Mr Partridge, that he no sooner heard the latter desire his friend to stay and dine, than he very readily put in his word, and retracting his promise before given of furnishing the horses immediately, he assured Mr Jones he would lose no time in bespeaking a dinner, which, he said, could be got ready sooner than it was possible to get the horses up from grass, and to prepare them for their journey by a feed of corn.\n\nJones was at length prevailed on, chiefly by the latter argument of the landlord; and now a joint of mutton was put down to the fire. While this was preparing, Partridge, being admitted into the same apartment with his friend or master, began to harangue in the following manner.\n\n“Certainly, sir, if ever man deserved a young lady, you deserve young Madam Western; for what a vast quantity of love must a man have, to be able to live upon it without any other food, as you do? I am positive I have eat thirty times as much within these last twenty-four hours as your honour, and yet I am almost famished; for nothing makes a man so hungry as travelling, especially in this cold raw weather. And yet I can't tell how it is, but your honour is seemingly in perfect good health, and you never looked better nor fresher in your life. It must be certainly love that you live upon.”\n\n“And a very rich diet too, Partridge,” answered Jones. “But did not fortune send me an excellent dainty yesterday? Dost thou imagine I cannot live more than twenty-four hours on this dear pocket-book?”\n\n“Undoubtedly,” cries Partridge, “there is enough in that pocket-book to purchase many a good meal. Fortune sent it to your honour very opportunely for present use, as your honour's money must be almost out by this time.”\n\n“What do you mean?” answered Jones; “I hope you don't imagine that I should be dishonest enough, even if it belonged to any other person, besides Miss Western----”\n\n“Dishonest!” replied Partridge, “heaven forbid I should wrong your honour so much! but where's the dishonesty in borrowing a little for present spending, since you will be so well able to pay the lady hereafter? No, indeed, I would have your honour pay it again, as soon as it is convenient, by all means; but where can be the harm in making use of it now you want it? Indeed, if it belonged to a poor body, it would be another thing; but so great a lady, to be sure, can never want it, especially now as she is along with a lord, who, it can't be doubted, will let her have whatever she hath need of. Besides, if she should want a little, she can't want the whole, therefore I would give her a little; but I would be hanged before I mentioned the having found it at first, and before I got some money of my own; for London, I have heard, is the very worst of places to be in without money. Indeed, if I had not known to whom it belonged, I might have thought it was the devil's money, and have been afraid to use it; but as you know otherwise, and came honestly by it, it would be an affront to fortune to part with it all again, at the very time when you want it most; you can hardly expect she should ever do you such another good turn; for fortuna nunquam perpetuo est bona. You will do as you please, notwithstanding all I say; but for my part, I would be hanged before I mentioned a word of the matter.”\n\n“By what I can see, Partridge,” cries Jones, “hanging is a matter non longe alienum a Scaevolae studiis.” “You should say alienus,” says Partridge,--“I remember the passage; it is an example under communis, alienus, immunis, variis casibus serviunt.” “If you do remember it,” cries Jones, “I find you don't understand it; but I tell thee, friend, in plain English, that he who finds another's property, and wilfully detains it from the known owner, deserves, in foro conscientiae, to be hanged, no less than if he had stolen it. And as for this very identical bill, which is the property of my angel, and was once in her dear possession, I will not deliver it into any hands but her own, upon any consideration whatever, no, though I was as hungry as thou art, and had no other means to satisfy my craving appetite; this I hope to do before I sleep; but if it should happen otherwise, I charge thee, if thou would'st not incur my displeasure for ever, not to shock me any more by the bare mention of such detestable baseness.”\n\n“I should not have mentioned it now,” cries Partridge, “if it had appeared so to me; for I'm sure I scorn any wickedness as much as another; but perhaps you know better; and yet I might have imagined that I should not have lived so many years, and have taught school so long, without being able to distinguish between fas et nefas; but it seems we are all to live and learn. I remember my old schoolmaster, who was a prodigious great scholar, used often to say, Polly matete cry town is my daskalon. The English of which, he told us, was, That a child may sometimes teach his grandmother to suck eggs. I have lived to a fine purpose, truly, if I am to be taught my grammar at this time of day. Perhaps, young gentleman, you may change your opinion, if you live to my years: for I remember I thought myself as wise when I was a stripling of one or two and twenty as I am now. I am sure I always taught alienus, and my master read it so before me.”\n\nThere were not many instances in which Partridge could provoke Jones, nor were there many in which Partridge himself could have been hurried out of his respect. Unluckily, however, they had both hit on one of these. We have already seen Partridge could not bear to have his learning attacked, nor could Jones bear some passage or other in the foregoing speech. And now, looking upon his companion with a contemptuous and disdainful air (a thing not usual with him), he cried, “Partridge, I see thou art a conceited old fool, and I wish thou art not likewise an old rogue. Indeed, if I was as well convinced of the latter as I am of the former, thou should'st travel no farther in my company.”\n\nThe sage pedagogue was contented with the vent which he had already given to his indignation; and, as the vulgar phrase is, immediately drew in his horns. He said, he was sorry he had uttered anything which might give offence, for that he had never intended it; but Nemo omnibus horis sapit.\n\nAs Jones had the vices of a warm disposition, he was entirely free from those of a cold one; and if his friends must have confest his temper to have been a little too easily ruffled, his enemies must at the same time have confest, that it as soon subsided; nor did it at all resemble the sea, whose swelling is more violent and dangerous after a storm is over than while the storm itself subsists. He instantly accepted the submission of Partridge, shook him by the hand, and with the most benign aspect imaginable, said twenty kind things, and at the same time very severely condemned himself, though not half so severely as he will most probably be condemned by many of our good readers.\n\nPartridge was now highly comforted, as his fears of having offended were at once abolished, and his pride completely satisfied by Jones having owned himself in the wrong, which submission he instantly applied to what had principally nettled him, and repeated in a muttering voice, “To be sure, sir, your knowledge may be superior to mine in some things; but as to the grammar, I think I may challenge any man living. I think, at least, I have that at my finger's end.”\n\nIf anything could add to the satisfaction which the poor man now enjoyed, he received this addition by the arrival of an excellent shoulder of mutton, that at this instant came smoaking to the table. On which, having both plentifully feasted, they again mounted their horses, and set forward for London.\n\nWhat happened to Mr Jones in his journey from St Albans.\n\nThey were got about two miles beyond Barnet, and it was now the dusk of the evening, when a genteel-looking man, but upon a very shabby horse, rode up to Jones, and asked him whether he was going to London; to which Jones answered in the affirmative. The gentleman replied, “I should be obliged to you, sir, if you will accept of my company; for it is very late, and I am a stranger to the road.” Jones readily complied with the request; and on they travelled together, holding that sort of discourse which is usual on such occasions.\n\nOf this, indeed, robbery was the principal topic: upon which subject the stranger expressed great apprehensions; but Jones declared he had very little to lose, and consequently as little to fear. Here Partridge could not forbear putting in his word. “Your honour,” said he, “may think it a little, but I am sure, if I had a hundred-pound bank-note in my pocket, as you have, I should be very sorry to lose it; but, for my part, I never was less afraid in my life; for we are four of us, and if we all stand by one another, the best man in England can't rob us. Suppose he should have a pistol, he can kill but one of us, and a man can die but once.--That's my comfort, a man can die but once.”\n\nBesides the reliance on superior numbers, a kind of valour which hath raised a certain nation among the moderns to a high pitch of glory, there was another reason for the extraordinary courage which Partridge now discovered; for he had at present as much of that quality as was in the power of liquor to bestow.\n\nOur company were now arrived within a mile of Highgate, when the stranger turned short upon Jones, and pulling out a pistol, demanded that little bank-note which Partridge had mentioned.\n\nJones was at first somewhat shocked at this unexpected demand; however, he presently recollected himself, and told the highwayman, all the money he had in his pocket was entirely at his service; and so saying, he pulled out upwards of three guineas, and offered to deliver it; but the other answered with an oath, That would not do. Jones answered coolly, he was very sorry for it, and returned the money into his pocket.\n\nThe highwayman then threatened, if he did not deliver the bank-note that moment, he must shoot him; holding his pistol at the same time very near to his breast. Jones instantly caught hold of the fellow's hand, which trembled so that he could scarce hold the pistol in it, and turned the muzzle from him. A struggle then ensued, in which the former wrested the pistol from the hand of his antagonist, and both came from their horses on the ground together, the highwayman upon his back, and the victorious Jones upon him.\n\nThe poor fellow now began to implore mercy of the conqueror: for, to say the truth, he was in strength by no means a match for Jones. “Indeed, sir,” says he, “I could have had no intention to shoot you; for you will find the pistol was not loaded. This is the first robbery I ever attempted, and I have been driven by distress to this.”\n\nAt this instant, at about a hundred and fifty yards' distance, lay another person on the ground, roaring for mercy in a much louder voice than the highwayman. This was no other than Partridge himself, who, endeavouring to make his escape from the engagement, had been thrown from his horse, and lay flat on his face, not daring to look up, and expecting every minute to be shot.\n\nIn this posture he lay, till the guide, who was no otherwise concerned than for his horses, having secured the stumbling beast, came up to him, and told him his master had got the better of the highwayman.\n\nPartridge leapt up at this news, and ran back to the place where Jones stood with his sword drawn in his hand to guard the poor fellow; which Partridge no sooner saw than he cried out, “Kill the villain, sir, run him through the body, kill him this instant!”\n\nLuckily, however, for the poor wretch, he had fallen into more merciful hands; for Jones having examined the pistol, and found it to be really unloaded, began to believe all the man had told him, before Partridge came up: namely, that he was a novice in the trade, and that he had been driven to it by the distress he mentioned, the greatest indeed imaginable, that of five hungry children, and a wife lying in of the sixth, in the utmost want and misery. The truth of all which the highwayman most vehemently asserted, and offered to convince Mr Jones of it, if he would take the trouble to go to his house, which was not above two miles off; saying, “That he desired no favour, but upon condition of proving all he had all alledged.”\n\nJones at first pretended that he would take the fellow at his word, and go with him, declaring that his fate should depend entirely on the truth of his story. Upon this the poor fellow immediately expressed so much alacrity, that Jones was perfectly satisfied with his veracity, and began now to entertain sentiments of compassion for him. He returned the fellow his empty pistol, advised him to think of honester means of relieving his distress, and gave him a couple of guineas for the immediate support of his wife and his family; adding, “he wished he had more for his sake, for the hundred pound that had been mentioned was not his own.”\n\nOur readers will probably be divided in their opinions concerning this action; some may applaud it perhaps as an act of extraordinary humanity, while those of a more saturnine temper will consider it as a want of regard to that justice which every man owes his country. Partridge certainly saw it in that light; for he testified much dissatisfaction on the occasion, quoted an old proverb, and said, he should not wonder if the rogue attacked them again before they reached London.\n\nThe highwayman was full of expressions of thankfulness and gratitude. He actually dropt tears, or pretended so to do. He vowed he would immediately return home, and would never afterwards commit such a transgression: whether he kept his word or no, perhaps may appear hereafter.\n\nOur travellers having remounted their horses, arrived in town without encountering any new mishap. On the road much pleasant discourse passed between Jones and Partridge, on the subject of their last adventure: in which Jones exprest a great compassion for those highwaymen who are, by unavoidable distress, driven, as it were, to such illegal courses, as generally bring them to a shameful death: “I mean,” said he, “those only whose highest guilt extends no farther than to robbery, and who are never guilty of cruelty nor insult to any person, which is a circumstance that, I must say, to the honour of our country, distinguishes the robbers of England from those of all other nations; for murder is, amongst those, almost inseparably incident to robbery.”\n\n“No doubt,” answered Partridge, “it is better to take away one's money than one's life; and yet it is very hard upon honest men, that they can't travel about their business without being in danger of these villains. And to be sure it would be better that all rogues were hanged out of the way, than that one honest man should suffer. For my own part, indeed, I should not care to have the blood of any of them on my own hands; but it is very proper for the law to hang them all. What right hath any man to take sixpence from me, unless I give it him? Is there any honesty in such a man?”\n\n“No, surely,” cries Jones, “no more than there is in him who takes the horses out of another man's stable, or who applies to his own use the money which he finds, when he knows the right owner.”\n\nThese hints stopt the mouth of Partridge; nor did he open it again till Jones, having thrown some sarcastical jokes on his cowardice, he offered to excuse himself on the inequality of fire-arms, saying, “A thousand naked men are nothing to one pistol; for though it is true it will kill but one at a single discharge, yet who can tell but that one may be himself?”\n\nAn Invocation.\n\nCome, bright love of fame, inspire my glowing breast: not thee I will call, who, over swelling tides of blood and tears, dost bear the heroe on to glory, while sighs of millions waft his spreading sails; but thee, fair, gentle maid, whom Mnesis, happy nymph, first on the banks of Hebrus did produce. Thee, whom Maeonia educated, whom Mantua charmed, and who, on that fair hill which overlooks the proud metropolis of Britain, sat'st, with thy Milton, sweetly tuning the heroic lyre; fill my ravished fancy with the hopes of charming ages yet to come. Foretel me that some tender maid, whose grandmother is yet unborn, hereafter, when, under the fictitious name of Sophia, she reads the real worth which once existed in my Charlotte, shall from her sympathetic breast send forth the heaving sigh. Do thou teach me not only to foresee, but to enjoy, nay, even to feed on future praise. Comfort me by a solemn assurance, that when the little parlour in which I sit at this instant shall be reduced to a worse furnished box, I shall be read with honour by those who never knew nor saw me, and whom I shall neither know nor see.\n\nAnd thou, much plumper dame, whom no airy forms nor phantoms of imagination cloathe; whom the well-seasoned beef, and pudding richly stained with plums, delight: thee I call: of whom in a treckschuyte, in some Dutch canal, the fat ufrow gelt, impregnated by a jolly merchant of Amsterdam, was delivered: in Grub-street school didst thou suck in the elements of thy erudition. Here hast thou, in thy maturer age, taught poetry to tickle not the fancy, but the pride of the patron. Comedy from thee learns a grave and solemn air; while tragedy storms aloud, and rends th' affrighted theatres with its thunders. To soothe thy wearied limbs in slumber, Alderman History tells his tedious tale; and, again, to awaken thee, Monsieur Romance performs his surprizing tricks of dexterity. Nor less thy well-fed bookseller obeys thy influence. By thy advice the heavy, unread, folio lump, which long had dozed on the dusty shelf, piecemealed into numbers, runs nimbly through the nation. Instructed by thee, some books, like quacks, impose on the world by promising wonders; while others turn beaus, and trust all their merits to a gilded outside. Come, thou jolly substance, with thy shining face, keep back thy inspiration, but hold forth thy tempting rewards; thy shining, chinking heap; thy quickly convertible bank-bill, big with unseen riches; thy often-varying stock; the warm, the comfortable house; and, lastly, a fair portion of that bounteous mother, whose flowing breasts yield redundant sustenance for all her numerous offspring, did not some too greedily and wantonly drive their brethren from the teat. Come thou, and if I am too tasteless of thy valuable treasures, warm my heart with the transporting thought of conveying them to others. Tell me, that through thy bounty, the pratling babes, whose innocent play hath often been interrupted by my labours, may one time be amply rewarded for them.\n\nAnd now, this ill-yoked pair, this lean shadow and this fat substance, have prompted me to write, whose assistance shall I invoke to direct my pen?\n\nFirst, Genius; thou gift of Heaven; without whose aid in vain we struggle against the stream of nature. Thou who dost sow the generous seeds which art nourishes, and brings to perfection. Do thou kindly take me by the hand, and lead me through all the mazes, the winding labyrinths of nature. Initiate me into all those mysteries which profane eyes never beheld. Teach me, which to thee is no difficult task, to know mankind better than they know themselves. Remove that mist which dims the intellects of mortals, and causes them to adore men for their art, or to detest them for their cunning, in deceiving others, when they are, in reality, the objects only of ridicule, for deceiving themselves. Strip off the thin disguise of wisdom from self-conceit, of plenty from avarice, and of glory from ambition. Come, thou that hast inspired thy Aristophanes, thy Lucian, thy Cervantes, thy Rabelais, thy Molière, thy Shakespear, thy Swift, thy Marivaux, fill my pages with humour; till mankind learn the good-nature to laugh only at the follies of others, and the humility to grieve at their own.\n\nAnd thou, almost the constant attendant on true genius, Humanity, bring all thy tender sensations. If thou hast already disposed of them all between thy Allen and thy Lyttleton, steal them a little while from their bosoms. Not without these the tender scene is painted. From these alone proceed the noble, disinterested friendship, the melting love, the generous sentiment, the ardent gratitude, the soft compassion, the candid opinion; and all those strong energies of a good mind, which fill the moistened eyes with tears, the glowing cheeks with blood, and swell the heart with tides of grief, joy, and benevolence.\n\nAnd thou, O Learning! (for without thy assistance nothing pure, nothing correct, can genius produce) do thou guide my pen. Thee in thy favourite fields, where the limpid, gently-rolling Thames washes thy Etonian banks, in early youth I have worshipped. To thee, at thy birchen altar, with true Spartan devotion, I have sacrificed my blood. Come then, and from thy vast, luxuriant stores, in long antiquity piled up, pour forth the rich profusion. Open thy Maeonian and thy Mantuan coffers, with whatever else includes thy philosophic, thy poetic, and thy historical treasures, whether with Greek or Roman characters thou hast chosen to inscribe the ponderous chests: give me a while that key to all thy treasures, which to thy Warburton thou hast entrusted.\n\nLastly, come Experience, long conversant with the wise, the good, the learned, and the polite. Nor with them only, but with every kind of character, from the minister at his levee, to the bailiff in his spunging-house; from the dutchess at her drum, to the landlady behind her bar. From thee only can the manners of mankind be known; to which the recluse pedant, however great his parts or extensive his learning may be, hath ever been a stranger.\n\nCome all these, and more, if possible; for arduous is the task I have undertaken; and, without all your assistance, will, I find, be too heavy for me to support. But if you all smile on my labours I hope still to bring them to a happy conclusion.\n\nWhat befel Mr Jones on his arrival in London.\n\nThe learned Dr Misaubin used to say, that the proper direction to him was To Dr Misaubin, in the World; intimating that there were few people in it to whom his great reputation was not known. And, perhaps, upon a very nice examination into the matter, we shall find that this circumstance bears no inconsiderable part among the many blessings of grandeur.\n\nThe great happiness of being known to posterity, with the hopes of which we so delighted ourselves in the preceding chapter, is the portion of few. To have the several elements which compose our names, as Sydenham expresses it, repeated a thousand years hence, is a gift beyond the power of title and wealth; and is scarce to be purchased, unless by the sword and the pen. But to avoid the scandalous imputation, while we yet live, of being one whom nobody knows (a scandal, by the bye, as old as the days of Homer[*]) will always be the envied portion of those, who have a legal title either to honour or estate.\n\n[*] See the 2d Odyssey, ver. 175.\n\nFrom that figure, therefore, which the Irish peer, who brought Sophia to town, hath already made in this history, the reader will conclude, doubtless, it must have been an easy matter to have discovered his house in London without knowing the particular street or square which he inhabited, since he must have been one whom everybody knows. To say the truth, so it would have been to any of those tradesmen who are accustomed to attend the regions of the great; for the doors of the great are generally no less easy to find than it is difficult to get entrance into them. But Jones, as well as Partridge, was an entire stranger in London; and as he happened to arrive first in a quarter of the town, the inhabitants of which have very little intercourse with the householders of Hanover or Grosvenor-square (for he entered through Gray's-inn-lane), so he rambled about some time before he could even find his way to those happy mansions where fortune segregates from the vulgar those magnanimous heroes, the descendants of antient Britons, Saxons, or Danes, whose ancestors, being born in better days, by sundry kinds of merit, have entailed riches and honour on their posterity.\n\nJones, being at length arrived at those terrestrial Elysian fields, would now soon have discovered his lordship's mansion; but the peer unluckily quitted his former house when he went for Ireland; and as he was just entered into a new one, the fame of his equipage had not yet sufficiently blazed in the neighbourhood; so that, after a successless enquiry till the clock had struck eleven, Jones at last yielded to the advice of Partridge, and retreated to the Bull and Gate in Holborn, that being the inn where he had first alighted, and where he retired to enjoy that kind of repose which usually attends persons in his circumstances.\n\nEarly in the morning he again set forth in pursuit of Sophia; and many a weary step he took to no better purpose than before. At last, whether it was that Fortune relented, or whether it was no longer in her power to disappoint him, he came into the very street which was honoured by his lordship's residence; and, being directed to the house, he gave one gentle rap at the door.\n\nThe porter, who, from the modesty of the knock, had conceived no high idea of the person approaching, conceived but little better from the appearance of Mr Jones, who was drest in a suit of fustian, and had by his side the weapon formerly purchased of the serjeant; of which, though the blade might be composed of well-tempered steel, the handle was composed only of brass, and that none of the brightest. When Jones, therefore, enquired after the young lady who had come to town with his lordship, this fellow answered surlily, “That there were no ladies there.” Jones then desired to see the master of the house; but was informed that his lordship would see nobody that morning. And upon growing more pressing the porter said, “he had positive orders to let no person in; but if you think proper,” said he, “to leave your name, I will acquaint his lordship; and if you call another time you shall know when he will see you.”\n\nJones now declared, “that he had very particular business with the young lady, and could not depart without seeing her.” Upon which the porter, with no very agreeable voice or aspect, affirmed, “that there was no young lady in that house, and consequently none could he see;” adding, “sure you are the strangest man I ever met with, for you will not take an answer.”\n\nI have often thought that, by the particular description of Cerberus, the porter of hell, in the 6th Aeneid, Virgil might possibly intend to satirize the porters of the great men in his time; the picture, at least, resembles those who have the honour to attend at the doors of our great men. The porter in his lodge answers exactly to Cerberus in his den, and, like him, must be appeased by a sop before access can be gained to his master. Perhaps Jones might have seen him in that light, and have recollected the passage where the Sibyl, in order to procure an entrance for Aeneas, presents the keeper of the Stygian avenue with such a sop. Jones, in like manner, now began to offer a bribe to the human Cerberus, which a footman, overhearing, instantly advanced, and declared, “if Mr Jones would give him the sum proposed, he would conduct him to the lady.” Jones instantly agreed, and was forthwith conducted to the lodging of Mrs Fitzpatrick by the very fellow who had attended the ladies thither the day before.\n\nNothing more aggravates ill success than the near approach to good. The gamester, who loses his party at piquet by a single point, laments his bad luck ten times as much as he who never came within a prospect of the game. So in a lottery, the proprietors of the next numbers to that which wins the great prize are apt to account themselves much more unfortunate than their fellow-sufferers. In short, these kind of hairbreadth missings of happiness look like the insults of Fortune, who may be considered as thus playing tricks with us, and wantonly diverting herself at our expense.\n\nJones, who more than once already had experienced this frolicsome disposition of the heathen goddess, was now again doomed to be tantalized in the like manner; for he arrived at the door of Mrs Fitzpatrick about ten minutes after the departure of Sophia. He now addressed himself to the waiting-woman belonging to Mrs Fitzpatrick; who told him the disagreeable news that the lady was gone, but could not tell him whither; and the same answer he afterwards received from Mrs Fitzpatrick herself. For as that lady made no doubt but that Mr Jones was a person detached from her uncle Western, in pursuit of his daughter, so she was too generous to betray her.\n\nThough Jones had never seen Mrs Fitzpatrick, yet he had heard that a cousin of Sophia was married to a gentleman of that name. This, however, in the present tumult of his mind, never once recurred to his memory; but when the footman, who had conducted him from his lordship's, acquainted him with the great intimacy between the ladies, and with their calling each other cousin, he then recollected the story of the marriage which he had formerly heard; and as he was presently convinced that this was the same woman, he became more surprized at the answer which he had received, and very earnestly desired leave to wait on the lady herself; but she as positively refused him that honour.\n\nJones, who, though he had never seen a court, was better bred than most who frequent it, was incapable of any rude or abrupt behaviour to a lady. When he had received, therefore, a peremptory denial, he retired for the present, saying to the waiting-woman, “That if this was an improper hour to wait on her lady, he would return in the afternoon; and that he then hoped to have the honour of seeing her.” The civility with which he uttered this, added to the great comeliness of his person, made an impression on the waiting-woman, and she could not help answering; “Perhaps, sir, you may;” and, indeed, she afterwards said everything to her mistress, which she thought most likely to prevail on her to admit a visit from the handsome young gentleman; for so she called him.\n\nJones very shrewdly suspected that Sophia herself was now with her cousin, and was denied to him; which he imputed to her resentment of what had happened at Upton. Having, therefore, dispatched Partridge to procure him lodgings, he remained all day in the street, watching the door where he thought his angel lay concealed; but no person did he see issue forth, except a servant of the house, and in the evening he returned to pay his visit to Mrs Fitzpatrick, which that good lady at last condescended to admit.\n\nThere is a certain air of natural gentility, which it is neither in the power of dress to give, nor to conceal. Mr Jones, as hath been before hinted, was possessed of this in a very eminent degree. He met, therefore, with a reception from the lady somewhat different from what his apparel seemed to demand; and after he had paid her his proper respects, was desired to sit down.\n\nThe reader will not, I believe, be desirous of knowing all the particulars of this conversation, which ended very little to the satisfaction of poor Jones. For though Mrs Fitzpatrick soon discovered the lover (as all women have the eyes of hawks in those matters), yet she still thought it was such a lover, as a generous friend of the lady should not betray her to. In short, she suspected this was the very Mr Blifil, from whom Sophia had flown; and all the answers which she artfully drew from Jones, concerning Mr Allworthy's family, confirmed her in this opinion. She therefore strictly denied any knowledge concerning the place whither Sophia was gone; nor could Jones obtain more than a permission to wait on her again the next evening.\n\nWhen Jones was departed Mrs Fitzpatrick communicated her suspicion concerning Mr Blifil to her maid; who answered, “Sure, madam, he is too pretty a man, in my opinion, for any woman in the world to run away from. I had rather fancy it is Mr Jones.”--“Mr Jones!” said the lady, “what Jones?” For Sophia had not given the least hint of any such person in all their conversation; but Mrs Honour had been much more communicative, and had acquainted her sister Abigail with the whole history of Jones, which this now again related to her mistress.\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick no sooner received this information, than she immediately agreed with the opinion of her maid; and, what is very unaccountable, saw charms in the gallant, happy lover, which she had overlooked in the slighted squire. “Betty,” says she, “you are certainly in the right: he is a very pretty fellow, and I don't wonder that my cousin's maid should tell you so many women are fond of him. I am sorry now I did not inform him where my cousin was; and yet, if he be so terrible a rake as you tell me, it is a pity she should ever see him any more; for what but her ruin can happen from marrying a rake and a beggar against her father's consent? I protest, if he be such a man as the wench described him to you, it is but an office of charity to keep her from him; and I am sure it would be unpardonable in me to do otherwise, who have tasted so bitterly of the misfortunes attending such marriages.”\n\nHere she was interrupted by the arrival of a visitor, which was no other than his lordship; and as nothing passed at this visit either new or extraordinary, or any ways material to this history, we shall here put an end to this chapter.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nA project of Mrs Fitzpatrick, and her visit to Lady Bellaston.\nWhen Mrs Fitzpatrick retired to rest, her thoughts were entirely taken up by her cousin Sophia and Mr Jones. She was, indeed, a little offended with the former, for the disingenuity which she now discovered. In which meditation she had not long exercised her imagination before the following conceit suggested itself; that could she possibly become the means of preserving Sophia from this man, and of restoring her to her father, she should, in all human probability, by so great a service to the family, reconcile to herself both her uncle and her aunt Western.\n\nAs this was one of her most favourite wishes, so the hope of success seemed so reasonable, that nothing remained but to consider of proper methods to accomplish her scheme. To attempt to reason the case with Sophia did not appear to her one of those methods: for as Betty had reported from Mrs Honour, that Sophia had a violent inclination to Jones, she conceived that to dissuade her from the match was an endeavour of the same kind, as it would be very heartily and earnestly to entreat a moth not to fly into a candle.\n\nIf the reader will please to remember that the acquaintance which Sophia had with Lady Bellaston was contracted at the house of Mrs Western, and must have grown at the very time when Mrs Fitzpatrick lived with this latter lady, he will want no information, that Mrs Fitzpatrick must have been acquainted with her likewise. They were, besides, both equally her distant relations.\n\nAfter much consideration, therefore, she resolved to go early in the morning to that lady, and endeavour to see her, unknown to Sophia, and to acquaint her with the whole affair. For she did not in the least doubt, but that the prudent lady, who had often ridiculed romantic love, and indiscreet marriages, in her conversation, would very readily concur in her sentiments concerning this match, and would lend her utmost assistance to prevent it.\n\nThis resolution she accordingly executed; and the next morning before the sun, she huddled on her cloaths, and at a very unfashionable, unseasonable, unvisitable hour, went to Lady Bellaston, to whom she got access, without the least knowledge or suspicion of Sophia, who, though not asleep, lay at that time awake in her bed, with Honour snoring by her side.\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick made many apologies for an early, abrupt visit, at an hour when, she said, “she should not have thought of disturbing her ladyship, but upon business of the utmost consequence.” She then opened the whole affair, told all she had heard from Betty; and did not forget the visit which Jones had paid to herself the preceding evening.\n\nLady Bellaston answered with a smile, “Then you have seen this terrible man, madam; pray, is he so very fine a figure as he is represented? for Etoff entertained me last night almost two hours with him. The wench I believe is in love with him by reputation.” Here the reader will be apt to wonder; but the truth is, that Mrs Etoff, who had the honour to pin and unpin the Lady Bellaston, had received compleat information concerning the said Mr Jones, and had faithfully conveyed the same to her lady last night (or rather that morning) while she was undressing; on which accounts she had been detained in her office above the space of an hour and a half.\n\nThe lady indeed, though generally well enough pleased with the narratives of Mrs Etoff at those seasons, gave an extraordinary attention to her account of Jones; for Honour had described him as a very handsome fellow, and Mrs Etoff, in her hurry, added so much to the beauty of his person to her report, that Lady Bellaston began to conceive him to be a kind of miracle in nature.\n\nThe curiosity which her woman had inspired was now greatly increased by Mrs Fitzpatrick, who spoke as much in favour of the person of Jones as she had before spoken in dispraise of his birth, character, and fortune.\n\nWhen Lady Bellaston had heard the whole, she answered gravely, “Indeed, madam, this is a matter of great consequence. Nothing can certainly be more commendable than the part you act; and I shall be very glad to have my share in the preservation of a young lady of so much merit, and for whom I have so much esteem.”\n\n“Doth not your ladyship think,” says Mrs Fitzpatrick eagerly, “that it would be the best way to write immediately to my uncle, and acquaint him where my cousin is?”\n\nThe lady pondered a little upon this, and thus answered--“Why, no, madam, I think not. Di Western hath described her brother to me to be such a brute, that I cannot consent to put any woman under his power who hath escaped from it. I have heard he behaved like a monster to his own wife, for he is one of those wretches who think they have a right to tyrannise over us, and from such I shall ever esteem it the cause of my sex to rescue any woman who is so unfortunate to be under their power.--The business, dear cousin, will be only to keep Miss Western from seeing this young fellow, till the good company, which she will have an opportunity of meeting here, give her a properer turn.”\n\n“If he should find her out, madam,” answered the other, “your ladyship may be assured he will leave nothing unattempted to come at her.”\n\n“But, madam,” replied the lady, “it is impossible he should come here--though indeed it is possible he may get some intelligence where she is, and then may lurk about the house--I wish therefore I knew his person.\n\n“Is there no way, madam, by which I could have a sight of him? for, otherwise, you know, cousin, she may contrive to see him here without my knowledge.” Mrs Fitzpatrick answered, “That he had threatened her with another visit that afternoon, and that, if her ladyship pleased to do her the honour of calling upon her then, she would hardly fail of seeing him between six and seven; and if he came earlier she would, by some means or other, detain him till her ladyship's arrival.”--Lady Bellaston replied, “She would come the moment she could get from dinner, which she supposed would be by seven at farthest; for that it was absolutely necessary she should be acquainted with his person. Upon my word, madam,” says she, “it was very good to take this care of Miss Western; but common humanity, as well as regard to our family, requires it of us both; for it would be a dreadful match indeed.”\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick failed not to make a proper return to the compliment which Lady Bellaston had bestowed on her cousin, and, after some little immaterial conversation, withdrew; and, getting as fast as she could into her chair, unseen by Sophia or Honour, returned home.\n\nWhich consists of visiting.\n\nMr Jones had walked within sight of a certain door during the whole day, which, though one of the shortest, appeared to him to be one of the longest in the whole year. At length, the clock having struck five, he returned to Mrs Fitzpatrick, who, though it was a full hour earlier than the decent time of visiting, received him very civilly; but still persisted in her ignorance concerning Sophia.\n\nJones, in asking for his angel, had dropped the word cousin, upon which Mrs Fitzpatrick said, “Then, sir, you know we are related: and, as we are, you will permit me the right of enquiring into the particulars of your business with my cousin.” Here Jones hesitated a good while, and at last answered, “He had a considerable sum of money of hers in his hands, which he desired to deliver to her.” He then produced the pocket-book, and acquainted Mrs Fitzpatrick with the contents, and with the method in which they came into his hands. He had scarce finished his story, when a most violent noise shook the whole house. To attempt to describe this noise to those who have heard it would be in vain; and to aim at giving any idea of it to those who have never heard the like, would be still more vain: for it may be truly said--\n--Non acuta Sic geminant Corybantes aera.\n\nThe priests of Cybele do not so rattle their sounding brass.\n\nIn short, a footman knocked, or rather thundered, at the door. Jones was a little surprized at the sound, having never heard it before; but Mrs Fitzpatrick very calmly said, that, as some company were coming, she could not make him any answer now; but if he pleased to stay till they were gone, she intimated she had something to say to him.\n\nThe door of the room now flew open, and, after pushing in her hoop sideways before her, entered Lady Bellaston, who having first made a very low courtesy to Mrs Fitzpatrick, and as low a one to Mr Jones, was ushered to the upper end of the room.\n\nWe mention these minute matters for the sake of some country ladies of our acquaintance, who think it contrary to the rules of modesty to bend their knees to a man.\n\nThe company were hardly well settled, before the arrival of the peer lately mentioned, caused a fresh disturbance, and a repetition of ceremonials.\n\nThese being over, the conversation began to be (as the phrase is) extremely brilliant. However, as nothing past in it which can be thought material to this history, or, indeed, very material in itself, I shall omit the relation; the rather, as I have known some very fine polite conversation grow extremely dull, when transcribed into books, or repeated on the stage. Indeed, this mental repast is a dainty, of which those who are excluded from polite assemblies must be contented to remain as ignorant as they must of the several dainties of French cookery, which are served only at the tables of the great. To say the truth, as neither of these are adapted to every taste, they might both be often thrown away on the vulgar.\n\nPoor Jones was rather a spectator of this elegant scene, than an actor in it; for though, in the short interval before the peer's arrival, Lady Bellaston first, and afterwards Mrs Fitzpatrick, had addressed some of their discourse to him; yet no sooner was the noble lord entered, than he engrossed the whole attention of the two ladies to himself; and as he took no more notice of Jones than if no such person had been present, unless by now and then staring at him, the ladies followed his example.\n\nThe company had now staid so long, that Mrs Fitzpatrick plainly perceived they all designed to stay out each other. She therefore resolved to rid herself of Jones, he being the visitant to whom she thought the least ceremony was due. Taking therefore an opportunity of a cessation of chat, she addressed herself gravely to him, and said, “Sir, I shall not possibly be able to give you an answer to-night as to that business; but if you please to leave word where I may send to you to-morrow---”\n\nJones had natural, but not artificial good-breeding. Instead therefore of communicating the secret of his lodgings to a servant, he acquainted the lady herself with it particularly, and soon after very ceremoniously withdrew.\n\nHe was no sooner gone than the great personages, who had taken no notice of him present, began to take much notice of him in his absence; but if the reader hath already excused us from relating the more brilliant part of this conversation, he will surely be very ready to excuse the repetition of what may be called vulgar abuse; though, perhaps, it may be material to our history to mention an observation of Lady Bellaston, who took her leave in a few minutes after him, and then said to Mrs Fitzpatrick, at her departure, “I am satisfied on the account of my cousin; she can be in no danger from this fellow.”\n\nOur history shall follow the example of Lady Bellaston, and take leave of the present company, which was now reduced to two persons; between whom, as nothing passed, which in the least concerns us or our reader, we shall not suffer ourselves to be diverted by it from matters which must seem of more consequence to all those who are at all interested in the affairs of our heroe.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nAn adventure which happened to Mr Jones at his lodgings, with some account of a young gentleman who lodged there, and of the mistress of the house, and her two daughters.\n\nThe next morning, as early as it was decent, Jones attended at Mrs Fitzpatrick's door, where he was answered that the lady was not at home; an answer which surprized him the more, as he had walked backwards and forwards in the street from break of day; and if she had gone out, he must have seen her. This answer, however, he was obliged to receive, and not only now, but to five several visits which he made her that day.\n\nTo be plain with the reader, the noble peer had from some reason or other, perhaps from a regard for the lady's honour, insisted that she should not see Mr Jones, whom he looked on as a scrub, any more; and the lady had complied in making that promise to which we now see her so strictly adhere.\n\nBut as our gentle reader may possibly have a better opinion of the young gentleman than her ladyship, and may even have some concern, should it be apprehended that, during this unhappy separation from Sophia, he took up his residence either at an inn, or in the street; we shall now give an account of his lodging, which was indeed in a very reputable house, and in a very good part of the town.\n\nMr Jones, then, had often heard Mr Allworthy mention the gentlewoman at whose house he used to lodge when he was in town. This person, who, as Jones likewise knew, lived in Bond-street, was the widow of a clergyman, and was left by him, at his decease, in possession of two daughters, and of a compleat set of manuscript sermons.\n\nOf these two daughters, Nancy, the elder, was now arrived at the age of seventeen, and Betty, the younger, at that of ten.\n\nHither Jones had despatched Partridge, and in this house he was provided with a room for himself in the second floor, and with one for Partridge in the fourth.\n\nThe first floor was inhabited by one of those young gentlemen, who, in the last age, were called men of wit and pleasure about town, and properly enough; for as men are usually denominated from their business or profession, so pleasure may be said to have been the only business or profession of those gentlemen to whom fortune had made all useful occupations unnecessary. Playhouses, coffeehouses, and taverns were the scenes of their rendezvous. Wit and humour were the entertainment of their looser hours, and love was the business of their more serious moments. Wine and the muses conspired to kindle the brightest flames in their breasts; nor did they only admire, but some were able to celebrate the beauty they admired, and all to judge of the merit of such compositions.\n\nSuch, therefore, were properly called the men of wit and pleasure; but I question whether the same appellation may, with the same propriety, be given to those young gentlemen of our times, who have the same ambition to be distinguished for parts. Wit certainly they have nothing to do with. To give them their due, they soar a step higher than their predecessors, and may be called men of wisdom and vertù (take heed you do not read virtue). Thus at an age when the gentlemen above mentioned employ their time in toasting the charms of a woman, or in making sonnets in her praise; in giving their opinion of a play at the theatre, or of a poem at Will's or Button's; these gentlemen are considering the methods to bribe a corporation, or meditating speeches for the House of Commons, or rather for the magazines. But the science of gaming is that which above all others employs their thoughts. These are the studies of their graver hours, while for their amusements they have the vast circle of connoisseurship, painting, music, statuary, and natural philosophy, or rather unnatural, which deals in the wonderful, and knows nothing of Nature, except her monsters and imperfections.\n\nWhen Jones had spent the whole day in vain enquiries after Mrs Fitzpatrick, he returned at last disconsolate to his apartment. Here, while he was venting his grief in private, he heard a violent uproar below-stairs; and soon after a female voice begged him for heaven's sake to come and prevent murder. Jones, who was never backward on any occasion to help the distressed, immediately ran down-stairs; when stepping into the dining-room, whence all the noise issued, he beheld the young gentleman of wisdom and vertù just before mentioned, pinned close to the wall by his footman, and a young woman standing by, wringing her hands, and crying out, “He will be murdered! he will be murdered!” and, indeed, the poor gentleman seemed in some danger of being choaked, when Jones flew hastily to his assistance, and rescued him, just as he was breathing his last, from the unmerciful clutches of the enemy.\n\nThough the fellow had received several kicks and cuffs from the little gentleman, who had more spirit than strength, he had made it a kind of scruple of conscience to strike his master, and would have contented himself with only choaking him; but towards Jones he bore no such respect; he no sooner therefore found himself a little roughly handled by his new antagonist, than he gave him one of those punches in the guts which, though the spectators at Broughton's amphitheatre have such exquisite delight in seeing them, convey but very little pleasure in the feeling.\n\nThe lusty youth had no sooner received this blow, than he meditated a most grateful return; and now ensued a combat between Jones and the footman, which was very fierce, but short; for this fellow was no more able to contend with Jones than his master had before been to contend with him.\n\nAnd now, Fortune, according to her usual custom, reversed the face of affairs. The former victor lay breathless on the ground, and the vanquished gentleman had recovered breath enough to thank Mr Jones for his seasonable assistance; he received likewise the hearty thanks of the young woman present, who was indeed no other than Miss Nancy, the eldest daughter of the house.\n\nThe footman, having now recovered his legs, shook his head at Jones, and, with a sagacious look, cried--“O d--n me, I'll have nothing more to do with you; you have been upon the stage, or I'm d--nably mistaken.” And indeed we may forgive this his suspicion; for such was the agility and strength of our heroe, that he was, perhaps, a match for one of the first-rate boxers, and could, with great ease, have beaten all the muffled[*] graduates of Mr Broughton's school.\n\n[*] Lest posterity should be puzzled by this epithet, I think proper to explain it by an advertisement which was published Feb. 1, 1747.\nN.B.--Mr Broughton proposes, with proper assistance, to open an academy at his house in the Haymarket, for the instruction of those who are willing to be initiated in the mystery of boxing: where the whole theory and practice of that truly British art, with all the various stops, blows, cross-buttocks, &c., incident to combatants, will be fully taught and explained; and that persons of quality and distinction may not be deterred from entering into A course of those lectures, they will be given with the utmost tenderness and regard to the delicacy of the frame and constitution of the pupil, for which reason muffles are provided, that will effectually secure them from the inconveniency of black eyes, broken jaws, and bloody noses.\n\nThe master, foaming with wrath, ordered his man immediately to strip, to which the latter very readily agreed, on condition of receiving his wages. This condition was presently complied with, and the fellow was discharged.\n\nAnd now the young gentleman, whose name was Nightingale, very strenuously insisted that his deliverer should take part of a bottle of wine with him; to which Jones, after much entreaty, consented, though more out of complacence than inclination; for the uneasiness of his mind fitted him very little for conversation at this time. Miss Nancy likewise, who was the only female then in the house, her mamma and sister being both gone to the play, condescended to favour them with her company.\n\nWhen the bottle and glasses were on the table the gentleman began to relate the occasion of the preceding disturbance.\n\n“I hope, sir,” said he to Jones, “you will not from this accident conclude, that I make a custom of striking my servants, for I assure you this is the first time I have been guilty of it in my remembrance, and I have passed by many provoking faults in this very fellow, before he could provoke me to it; but when you hear what hath happened this evening, you will, I believe, think me excusable. I happened to come home several hours before my usual time, when I found four gentlemen of the cloth at whist by my fire;--and my Hoyle, sir--my best Hoyle, which cost me a guinea, lying open on the table, with a quantity of porter spilt on one of the most material leaves of the whole book. This, you will allow, was provoking; but I said nothing till the rest of the honest company were gone, and then gave the fellow a gentle rebuke, who, instead of expressing any concern, made me a pert answer, `That servants must have their diversions as well as other people; that he was sorry for the accident which had happened to the book, but that several of his acquaintance had bought the same for a shilling, and that I might stop as much in his wages, if I pleased.' I now gave him a severer reprimand than before, when the rascal had the insolence to---In short, he imputed my early coming home to----In short, he cast a reflection----He mentioned the name of a young lady, in a manner--in such a manner that incensed me beyond all patience, and, in my passion, I struck him.”\n\nJones answered, “That he believed no person living would blame him; for my part,” said he, “I confess I should, on the last-mentioned provocation, have done the same thing.”\n\nOur company had not sat long before they were joined by the mother and daughter, at their return from the play. And now they all spent a very chearful evening together; for all but Jones were heartily merry, and even he put on as much constrained mirth as possible. Indeed, half his natural flow of animal spirits, joined to the sweetness of his temper, was sufficient to make a most amiable companion; and notwithstanding the heaviness of his heart, so agreeable did he make himself on the present occasion, that, at their breaking up, the young gentleman earnestly desired his further acquaintance. Miss Nancy was well pleased with him; and the widow, quite charmed with her new lodger, invited him, with the other, next morning to breakfast.\n\nJones on his part was no less satisfied. As for Miss Nancy, though a very little creature, she was extremely pretty, and the widow had all the charms which can adorn a woman near fifty. As she was one of the most innocent creatures in the world, so she was one of the most chearful. She never thought, nor spoke, nor wished any ill, and had constantly that desire of pleasing, which may be called the happiest of all desires in this, that it scarce ever fails of attaining its ends, when not disgraced by affectation. In short, though her power was very small, she was in her heart one of the warmest friends. She had been a most affectionate wife, and was a most fond and tender mother. As our history doth not, like a newspaper, give great characters to people who never were heard of before, nor will ever be heard of again, the reader may hence conclude, that this excellent woman will hereafter appear to be of some importance in our history.\n\nNor was Jones a little pleased with the young gentleman himself, whose wine he had been drinking. He thought he discerned in him much good sense, though a little too much tainted with town-foppery; but what recommended him most to Jones were some sentiments of great generosity and humanity, which occasionally dropt from him; and particularly many expressions of the highest disinterestedness in the affair of love. On which subject the young gentleman delivered himself in a language which might have very well become an Arcadian shepherd of old, and which appeared very extraordinary when proceeding from the lips of a modern fine gentleman; but he was only one by imitation, and meant by nature for a much better character.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nWhat arrived while the company were at breakfast, with some hints concerning the government of daughters.\nOur company brought together in the morning the same good inclinations towards each other, with which they had separated the evening before; but poor Jones was extremely disconsolate; for he had just received information from Partridge, that Mrs Fitzpatrick had left her lodging, and that he could not learn whither she was gone. This news highly afflicted him, and his countenance, as well as his behaviour, in defiance of all his endeavours to the contrary, betrayed manifest indications of a disordered mind.\n\nThe discourse turned at present, as before, on love; and Mr Nightingale again expressed many of those warm, generous, and disinterested sentiments upon this subject, which wise and sober men call romantic, but which wise and sober women generally regard in a better light. Mrs Miller (for so the mistress of the house was called) greatly approved these sentiments; but when the young gentleman appealed to Miss Nancy, she answered only, “That she believed the gentleman who had spoke the least was capable of feeling most.”\n\nThis compliment was so apparently directed to Jones, that we should have been sorry had he passed it by unregarded. He made her indeed a very polite answer, and concluded with an oblique hint, that her own silence subjected her to a suspicion of the same kind: for indeed she had scarce opened her lips either now or the last evening.\n\n“I am glad, Nanny,” says Mrs Miller, “the gentleman hath made the observation; I protest I am almost of his opinion. What can be the matter with you, child? I never saw such an alteration. What is become of all your gaiety? Would you think, sir, I used to call her my little prattler? She hath not spoke twenty words this week.”\n\nHere their conversation was interrupted by the entrance of a maid-servant, who brought a bundle in her hand, which, she said, “was delivered by a porter for Mr Jones.” She added, “That the man immediately went away, saying, it required no answer.”\n\nJones expressed some surprize on this occasion, and declared it must be some mistake; but the maid persisting that she was certain of the name, all the women were desirous of having the bundle immediately opened; which operation was at length performed by little Betsy, with the consent of Mr Jones: and the contents were found to be a domino, a mask, and a masquerade ticket.\n\nJones was now more positive than ever in asserting, that these things must have been delivered by mistake; and Mrs Miller herself expressed some doubt, and said, “She knew not what to think.” But when Mr Nightingale was asked, he delivered a very different opinion. “All I can conclude from it, sir,” said he, “is, that you are a very happy man; for I make no doubt but these were sent you by some lady whom you will have the happiness of meeting at the masquerade.”\n\nJones had not a sufficient degree of vanity to entertain any such flattering imagination; nor did Mrs Miller herself give much assent to what Mr Nightingale had said, till Miss Nancy having lifted up the domino, a card dropt from the sleeve, in which was written as follows:--\n\nThe queen of the fairies sends you this; Use her favours not amiss.\n\nMrs Miller and Miss Nancy now both agreed with Mr Nightingale; nay, Jones himself was almost persuaded to be of the same opinion. And as no other lady but Mrs Fitzpatrick, he thought, knew his lodging, he began to flatter himself with some hopes, that it came from her, and that he might possibly see his Sophia. These hopes had surely very little foundation; but as the conduct of Mrs Fitzpatrick, in not seeing him according to her promise, and in quitting her lodgings, had been very odd and unaccountable, he conceived some faint hopes, that she (of whom he had formerly heard a very whimsical character) might possibly intend to do him that service in a strange manner, which she declined doing by more ordinary methods. To say the truth, as nothing certain could be concluded from so odd and uncommon an incident, he had the greater latitude to draw what imaginary conclusions from it he pleased. As his temper therefore was naturally sanguine, he indulged it on this occasion, and his imagination worked up a thousand conceits, to favour and support his expectations of meeting his dear Sophia in the evening.\n\nReader, if thou hast any good wishes towards me, I will fully repay them by wishing thee to be possessed of this sanguine disposition of mind; since, after having read much and considered long on that subject of happiness which hath employed so many great pens, I am almost inclined to fix it in the possession of this temper; which puts us, in a manner, out of the reach of Fortune, and makes us happy without her assistance. Indeed, the sensations of pleasure it gives are much more constant as well as much keener, than those which that blind lady bestows; nature having wisely contrived, that some satiety and languor should be annexed to all our real enjoyments, lest we should be so taken up by them, as to be stopt from further pursuits. I make no manner of doubt but that, in this light, we may see the imaginary future chancellor just called to the bar, the archbishop in crape, and the prime minister at the tail of an opposition, more truly happy than those who are invested with all the power and profit of those respective offices.\n\nMr Jones having now determined to go to the masquerade that evening, Mr Nightingale offered to conduct him thither. The young gentleman, at the same time, offered tickets to Miss Nancy and her mother; but the good woman would not accept them. She said, “she did not conceive the harm which some people imagined in a masquerade; but that such extravagant diversions were proper only for persons of quality and fortune, and not for young women who were to get their living, and could, at best, hope to be married to a good tradesman.”----“A tradesman!” cries Nightingale, “you shan't undervalue my Nancy. There is not a nobleman upon earth above her merit.” “O fie! Mr Nightingale,” answered Mrs Miller, “you must not fill the girl's head with such fancies: but if it was her good luck” (says the mother with a simper) “to find a gentleman of your generous way of thinking, I hope she would make a better return to his generosity than to give her mind up to extravagant pleasures. Indeed, where young ladies bring great fortunes themselves, they have some right to insist on spending what is their own; and on that account I have heard the gentlemen say, a man has sometimes a better bargain with a poor wife, than with a rich one.----But let my daughters marry whom they will, I shall endeavour to make them blessings to their husbands:----I beg, therefore, I may hear of no more masquerades. Nancy is, I am certain, too good a girl to desire to go; for she must remember when you carried her thither last year, it almost turned her head; and she did not return to herself, or to her needle, in a month afterwards.”\n\nThough a gentle sigh, which stole from the bosom of Nancy, seemed to argue some secret disapprobation of these sentiments, she did not dare openly to oppose them. For as this good woman had all the tenderness, so she had preserved all the authority of a parent; and as her indulgence to the desires of her children was restrained only by her fears for their safety and future welfare, so she never suffered those commands which proceeded from such fears to be either disobeyed or disputed. And this the young gentleman, who had lodged two years in the house, knew so well, that he presently acquiesced in the refusal.\n\nMr Nightingale, who grew every minute fonder of Jones, was very desirous of his company that day to dinner at the tavern, where he offered to introduce him to some of his acquaintance; but Jones begged to be excused, “as his cloaths,” he said, “were not yet come to town.”\n\nTo confess the truth, Mr Jones was now in a situation, which sometimes happens to be the case of young gentlemen of much better figure than himself. In short, he had not one penny in his pocket; a situation in much greater credit among the antient philosophers than among the modern wise men who live in Lombard-street, or those who frequent White's chocolate-house. And, perhaps, the great honours which those philosophers have ascribed to an empty pocket may be one of the reasons of that high contempt in which they are held in the aforesaid street and chocolate-house.\n\nNow if the antient opinion, that men might live very comfortably on virtue only, be, as the modern wise men just above-mentioned pretend to have discovered, a notorious error; no less false is, I apprehend, that position of some writers of romance, that a man can live altogether on love; for however delicious repasts this may afford to some of our senses or appetites, it is most certain it can afford none to others. Those, therefore, who have placed too great a confidence in such writers, have experienced their error when it was too late; and have found that love was no more capable of allaying hunger, than a rose is capable of delighting the ear, or a violin of gratifying the smell.\n\nNotwithstanding, therefore, all the delicacies which love had set before him, namely, the hopes of seeing Sophia at the masquerade; on which, however ill-founded his imagination might be, he had voluptuously feasted during the whole day, the evening no sooner came than Mr Jones began to languish for some food of a grosser kind. Partridge discovered this by intuition, and took the occasion to give some oblique hints concerning the bank-bill; and, when these were rejected with disdain, he collected courage enough once more to mention a return to Mr Allworthy.\n\n“Partridge,” cries Jones, “you cannot see my fortune in a more desperate light than I see it myself; and I begin heartily to repent that I suffered you to leave a place where you was settled, and to follow me. However, I insist now on your returning home; and for the expense and trouble which you have so kindly put yourself to on my account, all the cloaths I left behind in your care I desire you would take as your own. I am sorry I can make you no other acknowledgment.”\n\nHe spoke these words with so pathetic an accent, that Partridge, among whose vices ill-nature or hardness of heart were not numbered, burst into tears; and after swearing he would not quit him in his distress, he began with the most earnest entreaties to urge his return home. “For heaven's sake, sir,” says he, “do but consider; what can your honour do?--how is it possible you can live in this town without money? Do what you will, sir, or go wherever you please, I am resolved not to desert you. But pray, sir, consider--do pray, sir, for your own sake, take it into your consideration; and I'm sure,” says he, “that your own good sense will bid you return home.”\n\n“How often shall I tell thee,” answered Jones, “that I have no home to return to? Had I any hopes that Mr Allworthy's doors would be open to receive me, I want no distress to urge me--nay, there is no other cause upon earth, which could detain me a moment from flying to his presence; but, alas! that I am for ever banished from. His last words were--O, Partridge, they still ring in my ears--his last words were, when he gave me a sum of money--what it was I know not, but considerable I'm sure it was--his last words were--`I am resolved from this day forward, on no account to converse with you any more.'”\n\nHere passion stopt the mouth of Jones, as surprize for a moment did that of Partridge; but he soon recovered the use of speech, and after a short preface, in which he declared he had no inquisitiveness in his temper, enquired what Jones meant by a considerable sum--he knew not how much--and what was become of the money.\n\nIn both these points he now received full satisfaction; on which he was proceeding to comment, when he was interrupted by a message from Mr Nightingale, who desired his master's company in his apartment.\n\nWhen the two gentlemen were both attired for the masquerade, and Mr Nightingale had given orders for chairs to be sent for, a circumstance of distress occurred to Jones, which will appear very ridiculous to many of my readers. This was how to procure a shilling; but if such readers will reflect a little on what they have themselves felt from the want of a thousand pounds, or, perhaps, of ten or twenty, to execute a favourite scheme, they will have a perfect idea of what Mr Jones felt on this occasion. For this sum, therefore, he applied to Partridge, which was the first he had permitted him to advance, and was the last he intended that poor fellow should advance in his service. To say the truth, Partridge had lately made no offer of this kind. Whether it was that he desired to see the bank-bill broke in upon, or that distress should prevail on Jones to return home, or from what other motive it proceeded, I will not determine.\n\nContaining the whole humours of a masquerade.\n\nOur cavaliers now arrived at that temple, where Heydegger, the great Arbiter Deliciarum, the great high-priest of pleasure, presides; and, like other heathen priests, imposes on his votaries by the pretended presence of the deity, when in reality no such deity is there.\n\nMr Nightingale, having taken a turn or two with his companion, soon left him, and walked off with a female, saying, “Now you are here, sir, you must beat about for your own game.”\n\nJones began to entertain strong hopes that his Sophia was present; and these hopes gave him more spirits than the lights, the music, and the company; though these are pretty strong antidotes against the spleen. He now accosted every woman he saw, whose stature, shape, or air, bore any resemblance to his angel. To all of whom he endeavoured to say something smart, in order to engage an answer, by which he might discover that voice which he thought it impossible he should mistake. Some of these answered by a question, in a squeaking voice, Do you know me? Much the greater number said, I don't know you, sir, and nothing more. Some called him an impertinent fellow; some made him no answer at all; some said, Indeed I don't know your voice, and I shall have nothing to say to you; and many gave him as kind answers as he could wish, but not in the voice he desired to hear.\n\nWhilst he was talking with one of these last (who was in the habit of a shepherdess) a lady in a domino came up to him, and slapping him on the shoulder, whispered him, at the same time, in the ear, “If you talk any longer with that trollop, I will acquaint Miss Western.”\n\nJones no sooner heard that name, than, immediately quitting his former companion, he applied to the domino, begging and entreating her to show him the lady she had mentioned, if she was then in the room.\n\nThe mask walked hastily to the upper end of the innermost apartment before she spoke; and then, instead of answering him, sat down, and declared she was tired. Jones sat down by her, and still persisted in his entreaties; at last the lady coldly answered, “I imagined Mr Jones had been a more discerning lover, than to suffer any disguise to conceal his mistress from him.” “Is she here, then, madam?” replied Jones, with some vehemence. Upon which the lady cried--“Hush, sir, you will be observed. I promise you, upon my honour, Miss Western is not here.”\n\nJones, now taking the mask by the hand, fell to entreating her in the most earnest manner, to acquaint him where he might find Sophia; and when he could obtain no direct answer, he began to upbraid her gently for having disappointed him the day before; and concluded, saying, “Indeed, my good fairy queen, I know your majesty very well, notwithstanding the affected disguise of your voice. Indeed, Mrs Fitzpatrick, it is a little cruel to divert yourself at the expense of my torments.”\n\nThe mask answered, “Though you have so ingeniously discovered me, I must still speak in the same voice, lest I should be known by others. And do you think, good sir, that I have no greater regard for my cousin, than to assist in carrying on an affair between you two, which must end in her ruin, as well as your own? Besides, I promise you, my cousin is not mad enough to consent to her own destruction, if you are so much her enemy as to tempt her to it.”\n\n“Alas, madam!” said Jones, “you little know my heart, when you call me an enemy of Sophia.”\n\n“And yet to ruin any one,” cries the other, “you will allow, is the act of an enemy; and when by the same act you must knowingly and certainly bring ruin on yourself, is it not folly or madness, as well as guilt? Now, sir, my cousin hath very little more than her father will please to give her; very little for one of her fashion--you know him, and you know your own situation.”\n\nJones vowed he had no such design on Sophia, “That he would rather suffer the most violent of deaths than sacrifice her interest to his desires.” He said, “he knew how unworthy he was of her, every way, that he had long ago resolved to quit all such aspiring thoughts, but that some strange accidents had made him desirous to see her once more, when he promised he would take leave of her for ever. No, madam,” concluded he, “my love is not of that base kind which seeks its own satisfaction at the expense of what is most dear to its object. I would sacrifice everything to the possession of my Sophia, but Sophia herself.”\n\nThough the reader may have already conceived no very sublime idea of the virtue of the lady in the mask; and though possibly she may hereafter appear not to deserve one of the first characters of her sex; yet, it is certain, these generous sentiments made a strong impression upon her, and greatly added to the affection she had before conceived for our young heroe.\n\nThe lady now, after silence of a few moments, said, “She did not see his pretensions to Sophia so much in the light of presumption, as of imprudence. Young fellows,” says she, “can never have too aspiring thoughts. I love ambition in a young man, and I would have you cultivate it as much as possible. Perhaps you may succeed with those who are infinitely superior in fortune; nay, I am convinced there are women----but don't you think me a strange creature, Mr Jones, to be thus giving advice to a man with whom I am so little acquainted, and one with whose behaviour to me I have so little reason to be pleased?”\n\nHere Jones began to apologize, and to hope he had not offended in anything he had said of her cousin.--To which the mask answered, “And are you so little versed in the sex, to imagine you can well affront a lady more than by entertaining her with your passion for another woman? If the fairy queen had conceived no better opinion of your gallantry, she would scarce have appointed you to meet her at the masquerade.”\n\nJones had never less inclination to an amour than at present; but gallantry to the ladies was among his principles of honour; and he held it as much incumbent on him to accept a challenge to love, as if it had been a challenge to fight. Nay, his very love to Sophia made it necessary for him to keep well with the lady, as he made no doubt but she was capable of bringing him into the presence of the other.\n\nHe began therefore to make a very warm answer to her last speech, when a mask, in the character of an old woman, joined them. This mask was one of those ladies who go to a masquerade only to vent ill-nature, by telling people rude truths, and by endeavouring, as the phrase is, to spoil as much sport as they are able. This good lady, therefore, having observed Jones, and his friend, whom she well knew, in close consultation together in a corner of the room, concluded she could nowhere satisfy her spleen better than by interrupting them. She attacked them, therefore, and soon drove them from their retirement; nor was she contented with this, but pursued them to every place which they shifted to avoid her; till Mr Nightingale, seeing the distress of his friend, at last relieved him, and engaged the old woman in another pursuit.\n\nWhile Jones and his mask were walking together about the room, to rid themselves of the teazer, he observed his lady speak to several masks, with the same freedom of acquaintance as if they had been barefaced. He could not help expressing his surprize at this; saying, “Sure, madam, you must have infinite discernment, to know people in all disguises.” To which the lady answered, “You cannot conceive anything more insipid and childish than a masquerade to the people of fashion, who in general know one another as well here as when they meet in an assembly or a drawing-room; nor will any woman of condition converse with a person with whom she is not acquainted. In short, the generality of persons whom you see here may more properly be said to kill time in this place than in any other; and generally retire from hence more tired than from the longest sermon. To say the truth, I begin to be in that situation myself; and if I have any faculty at guessing, you are not much better pleased. I protest it would be almost charity in me to go home for your sake.” “I know but one charity equal to it,” cries Jones, “and that is to suffer me to wait on you home.” “Sure,” answered the lady, “you have a strange opinion of me, to imagine, that upon such an acquaintance, I would let you into my doors at this time of night. I fancy you impute the friendship I have shown my cousin to some other motive. Confess honestly; don't you consider this contrived interview as little better than a downright assignation? Are you used, Mr Jones, to make these sudden conquests?” “I am not used, madam,” said Jones, “to submit to such sudden conquests; but as you have taken my heart by surprize, the rest of my body hath a right to follow; so you must pardon me if I resolve to attend you wherever you go.” He accompanied these words with some proper actions; upon which the lady, after a gentle rebuke, and saying their familiarity would be observed, told him, “She was going to sup with an acquaintance, whither she hoped he would not follow her; for if you should,” said she, “I shall be thought an unaccountable creature, though my friend indeed is not censorious: yet I hope you won't follow me; I protest I shall not know what to say if you do.”\n\nThe lady presently after quitted the masquerade, and Jones, notwithstanding the severe prohibition he had received, presumed to attend her. He was now reduced to the same dilemma we have mentioned before, namely, the want of a shilling, and could not relieve it by borrowing as before. He therefore walked boldly on after the chair in which his lady rode, pursued by a grand huzza, from all the chairmen present, who wisely take the best care they can to discountenance all walking afoot by their betters. Luckily, however, the gentry who attend at the Opera-house were too busy to quit their stations, and as the lateness of the hour prevented him from meeting many of their brethren in the street, he proceeded without molestation, in a dress, which, at another season, would have certainly raised a mob at his heels.\n\nThe lady was set down in a street not far from Hanover-square, where the door being presently opened, she was carried in, and the gentleman, without any ceremony, walked in after her.\n\nJones and his companion were now together in a very well-furnished and well-warmed room; when the female, still speaking in her masquerade voice, said she was surprized at her friend, who must absolutely have forgot her appointment; at which, after venting much resentment, she suddenly exprest some apprehension from Jones, and asked him what the world would think of their having been alone together in a house at that time of night? But instead of a direct answer to so important a question, Jones began to be very importunate with the lady to unmask; and at length having prevailed, there appeared not Mrs Fitzpatrick, but the Lady Bellaston herself.\n\nIt would be tedious to give the particular conversation, which consisted of very common and ordinary occurrences, and which lasted from two till six o'clock in the morning. It is sufficient to mention all of it that is anywise material to this history. And this was a promise that the lady would endeavour to find out Sophia, and in a few days bring him to an interview with her, on condition that he would then take his leave of her. When this was thoroughly settled, and a second meeting in the evening appointed at the same place, they separated; the lady returned to her house, and Jones to his lodgings.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nContaining a scene of distress, which will appear very extraordinary to most of our readers.\nJones having refreshed himself with a few hours' sleep, summoned Partridge to his presence; and delivering him a bank-note of fifty pounds, ordered him to go and change it. Partridge received this with sparkling eyes, though, when he came to reflect farther, it raised in him some suspicions not very advantageous to the honour of his master: to these the dreadful idea he had of the masquerade, the disguise in which his master had gone out and returned, and his having been abroad all night, contributed. In plain language, the only way he could possibly find to account for the possession of this note, was by robbery: and, to confess the truth, the reader, unless he should suspect it was owing to the generosity of Lady Bellaston, can hardly imagine any other.\n\nTo clear, therefore, the honour of Mr Jones, and to do justice to the liberality of the lady, he had really received this present from her, who, though she did not give much into the hackney charities of the age, such as building hospitals, &c., was not, however, entirely void of that Christian virtue; and conceived (very rightly I think) that a young fellow of merit, without a shilling in the world, was no improper object of this virtue.\n\nMr Jones and Mr Nightingale had been invited to dine this day with Mrs Miller. At the appointed hour, therefore, the two young gentlemen, with the two girls, attended in the parlour, where they waited from three till almost five before the good woman appeared. She had been out of town to visit a relation, of whom, at her return, she gave the following account.\n\n“I hope, gentlemen, you will pardon my making you wait; I am sure if you knew the occasion--I have been to see a cousin of mine, about six miles off, who now lies in.--It should be a warning to all persons (says she, looking at her daughters) how they marry indiscreetly. There is no happiness in this world without a competency. O Nancy! how shall I describe the wretched condition in which I found your poor cousin? she hath scarce lain in a week, and there was she, this dreadful weather, in a cold room, without any curtains to her bed, and not a bushel of coals in her house to supply her with fire; her second son, that sweet little fellow, lies ill of a quinzy in the same bed with his mother; for there is no other bed in the house. Poor little Tommy! I believe, Nancy, you will never see your favourite any more; for he is really very ill. The rest of the children are in pretty good health: but Molly, I am afraid, will do herself an injury: she is but thirteen years old, Mr Nightingale, and yet, in my life, I never saw a better nurse: she tends both her mother and her brother; and, what is wonderful in a creature so young, she shows all the chearfulness in the world to her mother; and yet I saw her--I saw the poor child, Mr Nightingale, turn about, and privately wipe the tears from her eyes.” Here Mrs Miller was prevented, by her own tears, from going on, and there was not, I believe, a person present who did not accompany her in them; at length she a little recovered herself, and proceeded thus: “In all this distress the mother supports her spirits in a surprizing manner. The danger of her son sits heaviest upon her, and yet she endeavours as much as possible to conceal even this concern, on her husband's account. Her grief, however, sometimes gets the better of all her endeavours; for she was always extravagantly fond of this boy, and a most sensible, sweet-tempered creature it is. I protest I was never more affected in my life than when I heard the little wretch, who is hardly yet seven years old, while his mother was wetting him with her tears, beg her to be comforted. `Indeed, mamma,' cried the child, `I shan't die; God Almighty, I'm sure, won't take Tommy away; let heaven be ever so fine a place, I had rather stay here and starve with you and my papa than go to it.' Pardon me, gentlemen, I can't help it” (says she, wiping her eyes), “such sensibility and affection in a child.--And yet, perhaps, he is least the object of pity; for a day or two will, most probably, place him beyond the reach of all human evils. The father is, indeed, most worthy of compassion. Poor man, his countenance is the very picture of horror, and he looks like one rather dead than alive. Oh heavens! what a scene did I behold at my first coming into the room! The good creature was lying behind the bolster, supporting at once both his child and his wife. He had nothing on but a thin waistcoat; for his coat was spread over the bed, to supply the want of blankets.--When he rose up at my entrance, I scarce knew him. As comely a man, Mr Jones, within this fortnight, as you ever beheld; Mr Nightingale hath seen him. His eyes sunk, his face pale, with a long beard. His body shivering with cold, and worn with hunger too; for my cousin says she can hardly prevail upon him to eat.--He told me himself in a whisper--he told me--I can't repeat it--he said he could not bear to eat the bread his children wanted. And yet, can you believe it, gentlemen? in all this misery his wife has as good caudle as if she lay in the midst of the greatest affluence; I tasted it, and I scarce ever tasted better.--The means of procuring her this, he said, he believed was sent him by an angel from heaven. I know not what he meant; for I had not spirits enough to ask a single question.\n\n“This was a love-match, as they call it, on both sides; that is, a match between two beggars. I must, indeed, say, I never saw a fonder couple; but what is their fondness good for, but to torment each other?” “Indeed, mamma,” cries Nancy, “I have always looked on my cousin Anderson” (for that was her name) “as one of the happiest of women.” “I am sure,” says Mrs Miller, “the case at present is much otherwise; for any one might have discerned that the tender consideration of each other's sufferings makes the most intolerable part of their calamity, both to the husband and wife. Compared to which, hunger and cold, as they affect their own persons only, are scarce evils. Nay, the very children, the youngest, which is not two years old, excepted, feel in the same manner; for they are a most loving family, and, if they had but a bare competency, would be the happiest people in the world.” “I never saw the least sign of misery at her house,” replied Nancy; “I am sure my heart bleeds for what you now tell me.”--“O child,” answered the mother, “she hath always endeavoured to make the best of everything. They have always been in great distress; but, indeed, this absolute ruin hath been brought upon them by others. The poor man was bail for the villain his brother; and about a week ago, the very day before her lying-in, their goods were all carried away, and sold by an execution. He sent a letter to me of it by one of the bailiffs, which the villain never delivered.--What must he think of my suffering a week to pass before he heard of me?”\n\nIt was not with dry eyes that Jones heard this narrative; when it was ended he took Mrs Miller apart with him into another room, and, delivering her his purse, in which was the sum of £50, desired her to send as much of it as she thought proper to these poor people. The look which Mrs Miller gave Jones, on this occasion, is not easy to be described. She burst into a kind of agony of transport, and cryed out--“Good heavens! is there such a man in the world?”--But recollecting herself, she said, “Indeed I know one such; but can there be another?” “I hope, madam,” cries Jones, “there are many who have common humanity; for to relieve such distresses in our fellow-creatures, can hardly be called more.” Mrs Miller then took ten guineas, which were the utmost he could prevail with her to accept, and said, “She would find some means of conveying them early the next morning;” adding, “that she had herself done some little matter for the poor people, and had not left them in quite so much misery as she found them.”\n\nThey then returned to the parlour, where Nightingale expressed much concern at the dreadful situation of these wretches, whom indeed he knew; for he had seen them more than once at Mrs Miller's. He inveighed against the folly of making oneself liable for the debts of others; vented many bitter execrations against the brother; and concluded with wishing something could be done for the unfortunate family. “Suppose, madam,” said he, “you should recommend them to Mr Allworthy? Or what think you of a collection? I will give them a guinea with all my heart.”\n\nMrs Miller made no answer; and Nancy, to whom her mother had whispered the generosity of Jones, turned pale upon the occasion; though, if either of them was angry with Nightingale, it was surely without reason. For the liberality of Jones, if he had known it, was not an example which he had any obligation to follow; and there are thousands who would not have contributed a single halfpenny, as indeed he did not in effect, for he made no tender of anything; and therefore, as the others thought proper to make no demand, he kept his money in his pocket.\n\nI have, in truth, observed, and shall never have a better opportunity than at present to communicate my observation, that the world are in general divided into two opinions concerning charity, which are the very reverse of each other. One party seems to hold, that all acts of this kind are to be esteemed as voluntary gifts, and, however little you give (if indeed no more than your good wishes), you acquire a great degree of merit in so doing. Others, on the contrary, appear to be as firmly persuaded, that beneficence is a positive duty, and that whenever the rich fall greatly short of their ability in relieving the distresses of the poor, their pitiful largesses are so far from being meritorious, that they have only performed their duty by halves, and are in some sense more contemptible than those who have entirely neglected it.\n\nTo reconcile these different opinions is not in my power. I shall only add, that the givers are generally of the former sentiment, and the receivers are almost universally inclined to the latter.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\nWhich treats of matters of a very different kind from those in the preceding chapter.\n\nIn the evening Jones met his lady again, and a long conversation again ensued between them: but as it consisted only of the same ordinary occurrences as before, we shall avoid mentioning particulars, which we despair of rendering agreeable to the reader; unless he is one whose devotion to the fair sex, like that of the papists to their saints, wants to be raised by the help of pictures. But I am so far from desiring to exhibit such pictures to the public, that I would wish to draw a curtain over those that have been lately set forth in certain French novels; very bungling copies of which have been presented us here under the name of translations.\n\nJones grew still more and more impatient to see Sophia; and finding, after repeated interviews with Lady Bellaston, no likelihood of obtaining this by her means (for, on the contrary, the lady began to treat even the mention of the name of Sophia with resentment), he resolved to try some other method. He made no doubt but that Lady Bellaston knew where his angel was, so he thought it most likely that some of her servants should be acquainted with the same secret. Partridge therefore was employed to get acquainted with those servants, in order to fish this secret out of them.\n\nFew situations can be imagined more uneasy than that to which his poor master was at present reduced; for besides the difficulties he met with in discovering Sophia, besides the fears he had of having disobliged her, and the assurances he had received from Lady Bellaston of the resolution which Sophia had taken against him, and of her having purposely concealed herself from him, which he had sufficient reason to believe might be true; he had still a difficulty to combat which it was not in the power of his mistress to remove, however kind her inclination might have been. This was the exposing of her to be disinherited of all her father's estate, the almost inevitable consequence of their coming together without a consent, which he had no hopes of ever obtaining.\n\nAdd to all these the many obligations which Lady Bellaston, whose violent fondness we can no longer conceal, had heaped upon him; so that by her means he was now become one of the best-dressed men about town; and was not only relieved from those ridiculous distresses we have before mentioned, but was actually raised to a state of affluence beyond what he had ever known.\n\nNow, though there are many gentlemen who very well reconcile it to their consciences to possess themselves of the whole fortune of a woman, without making her any kind of return; yet to a mind, the proprietor of which doth not deserved to be hanged, nothing is, I believe, more irksome than to support love with gratitude only; especially where inclination pulls the heart a contrary way. Such was the unhappy case of Jones; for though the virtuous love he bore to Sophia, and which left very little affection for any other woman, had been entirely out of the question, he could never have been able to have made any adequate return to the generous passion of this lady, who had indeed been once an object of desire, but was now entered at least into the autumn of life, though she wore all the gaiety of youth, both in her dress and manner; nay, she contrived still to maintain the roses in her cheeks; but these, like flowers forced out of season by art, had none of that lively blooming freshness with which Nature, at the proper time, bedecks her own productions. She had, besides, a certain imperfection, which renders some flowers, though very beautiful to the eye, very improper to be placed in a wilderness of sweets, and what above all others is most disagreeable to the breath of love.\n\nThough Jones saw all these discouragements on the one side, he felt his obligations full as strongly on the other; nor did he less plainly discern the ardent passion whence those obligations proceeded, the extreme violence of which if he failed to equal, he well knew the lady would think him ungrateful; and, what is worse, he would have thought himself so. He knew the tacit consideration upon which all her favours were conferred; and as his necessity obliged him to accept them, so his honour, he concluded, forced him to pay the price. This therefore he resolved to do, whatever misery it cost him, and to devote himself to her, from that great principle of justice, by which the laws of some countries oblige a debtor, who is no otherwise capable of discharging his debt, to become the slave of his creditor.\n\nWhile he was meditating on these matters, he received the following note from the lady:--\n\n“A very foolish, but a very perverse accident hath happened since our last meeting, which makes it improper I should see you any more at the usual place. I will, if possible, contrive some other place by to-morrow. In the meantime, adieu.”\n\nThis disappointment, perhaps, the reader may conclude was not very great; but if it was, he was quickly relieved; for in less than an hour afterwards another note was brought him from the same hand, which contained as follows:--\n\n“I have altered my mind since I wrote; a change which, if you are no stranger to the tenderest of all passions, you will not wonder at. I am now resolved to see you this evening at my own house, whatever may be the consequence. Come to me exactly at seven; I dine abroad, but will be at home by that time. A day, I find, to those that sincerely love, seems longer than I imagined.\n\n“If you should accidentally be a few moments before me, bid them show you into the drawing-room.”\n\nTo confess the truth, Jones was less pleased with this last epistle than he had been with the former, as he was prevented by it from complying with the earnest entreaties of Mr Nightingale, with whom he had now contracted much intimacy and friendship. These entreaties were to go with that young gentleman and his company to a new play, which was to be acted that evening, and which a very large party had agreed to damn, from some dislike they had taken to the author, who was a friend to one of Mr Nightingale's acquaintance. And this sort of fun, our heroe, we are ashamed to confess, would willingly have preferred to the above kind appointment; but his honour got the better of his inclination.\n\nBefore we attend him to this intended interview with the lady, we think proper to account for both the preceding notes, as the reader may possibly be not a little surprized at the imprudence of Lady Bellaston, in bringing her lover to the very house where her rival was lodged.\n\nFirst, then, the mistress of the house where these lovers had hitherto met, and who had been for some years a pensioner to that lady, was now become a methodist, and had that very morning waited upon her ladyship, and after rebuking her very severely for her past life, had positively declared that she would, on no account, be instrumental in carrying on any of her affairs for the future.\n\nThe hurry of spirits into which this accident threw the lady made her despair of possibly finding any other convenience to meet Jones that evening; but as she began a little to recover from her uneasiness at the disappointment, she set her thoughts to work, when luckily it came into her head to propose to Sophia to go to the play, which was immediately consented to, and a proper lady provided for her companion. Mrs Honour was likewise despatched with Mrs Etoff on the same errand of pleasure; and thus her own house was left free for the safe reception of Mr Jones, with whom she promised herself two or three hours of uninterrupted conversation after her return from the place where she dined, which was at a friend's house in a pretty distant part of the town, near her old place of assignation, where she had engaged herself before she was well apprized of the revolution that had happened in the mind and morals of her late confidante.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nA chapter which, though short, may draw tears from some eyes.\nMr Jones was just dressed to wait on Lady Bellaston, when Mrs Miller rapped at his door; and, being admitted, very earnestly desired his company below-stairs, to drink tea in the parlour.\n\nUpon his entrance into the room, she presently introduced a person to him, saying, “This, sir, is my cousin, who hath been so greatly beholden to your goodness, for which he begs to return you his sincerest thanks.”\n\nThe man had scarce entered upon that speech which Mrs Miller had so kindly prefaced, when both Jones and he, looking stedfastly at each other, showed at once the utmost tokens of surprize. The voice of the latter began instantly to faulter; and, instead of finishing his speech, he sunk down into a chair, crying, “It is so, I am convinced it is so!”\n\n“Bless me! what's the meaning of this?” cries Mrs Miller; “you are not ill, I hope, cousin? Some water, a dram this instant.”\n\n“Be not frighted, madam,” cries Jones, “I have almost as much need of a dram as your cousin. We are equally surprized at this unexpected meeting. Your cousin is an acquaintance of mine, Mrs Miller.”\n\n“An acquaintance!” cries the man.--“Oh, heaven!”\n\n“Ay, an acquaintance,” repeated Jones, “and an honoured acquaintance too. When I do not love and honour the man who dares venture everything to preserve his wife and children from instant destruction, may I have a friend capable of disowning me in adversity!”\n\n“Oh, you are an excellent young man,” cries Mrs Miller:--“Yes, indeed, poor creature! he hath ventured everything.--If he had not had one of the best of constitutions, it must have killed him.”\n\n“Cousin,” cries the man, who had now pretty well recovered himself, “this is the angel from heaven whom I meant. This is he to whom, before I saw you, I owed the preservation of my Peggy. He it was to whose generosity every comfort, every support which I have procured for her, was owing. He is, indeed, the worthiest, bravest, noblest; of all human beings. O cousin, I have obligations to this gentleman of such a nature!”\n\n“Mention nothing of obligations,” cries Jones eagerly; “not a word, I insist upon it, not a word” (meaning, I suppose, that he would not have him betray the affair of the robbery to any person). “If, by the trifle you have received from me, I have preserved a whole family, sure pleasure was never bought so cheap.”\n\n“Oh, sir!” cries the man, “I wish you could this instant see my house. If any person had ever a right to the pleasure you mention, I am convinced it is yourself. My cousin tells me she acquainted you with the distress in which she found us. That, sir, is all greatly removed, and chiefly by your goodness.----My children have now a bed to lie on----and they have----they have----eternal blessings reward you for it!----they have bread to eat. My little boy is recovered; my wife is out of danger, and I am happy. All, all owing to you, sir, and to my cousin here, one of the best of women. Indeed, sir, I must see you at my house.--Indeed my wife must see you, and thank you.--My children too must express their gratitude.----Indeed, sir, they are not without a sense of their obligation; but what is my feeling when I reflect to whom I owe that they are now capable of expressing their gratitude.----Oh, sir, the little hearts which you have warmed had now been cold as ice without your assistance.”\n\nHere Jones attempted to prevent the poor man from proceeding; but indeed the overflowing of his own heart would of itself have stopped his words. And now Mrs Miller likewise began to pour forth thanksgivings, as well in her own name, as in that of her cousin, and concluded with saying, “She doubted not but such goodness would meet a glorious reward.”\n\nJones answered, “He had been sufficiently rewarded already. Your cousin's account, madam,” said he, “hath given me a sensation more pleasing than I have ever known. He must be a wretch who is unmoved at hearing such a story; how transporting then must be the thought of having happily acted a part in this scene! If there are men who cannot feel the delight of giving happiness to others, I sincerely pity them, as they are incapable of tasting what is, in my opinion, a greater honour, a higher interest, and a sweeter pleasure than the ambitious, the avaricious, or the voluptuous man can ever obtain.”\n\nThe hour of appointment being now come, Jones was forced to take a hasty leave, but not before he had heartily shaken his friend by the hand, and desired to see him again as soon as possible; promising that he would himself take the first opportunity of visiting him at his own house. He then stept into his chair, and proceeded to Lady Bellaston's, greatly exulting in the happiness which he had procured to this poor family; nor could he forbear reflecting, without horror, on the dreadful consequences which must have attended them, had he listened rather to the voice of strict justice than to that of mercy, when he was attacked on the high road.\n\nMrs Miller sung forth the praises of Jones during the whole evening, in which Mr Anderson, while he stayed, so passionately accompanied her, that he was often on the very point of mentioning the circumstance of the robbery. However, he luckily recollected himself, and avoided an indiscretion which would have been so much the greater, as he knew Mrs Miller to be extremely strict and nice in her principles. He was likewise well apprized of the loquacity of this lady; and yet such was his gratitude, that it had almost got the better both of discretion and shame, and made him publish that which would have defamed his own character, rather than omit any circumstances which might do the fullest honour to his benefactor.\n\nIn which the reader will be surprized.\n\nMr Jones was rather earlier than the time appointed, and earlier than the lady; whose arrival was hindered, not only by the distance of the place where she dined, but by some other cross accidents very vexatious to one in her situation of mind. He was accordingly shown into the drawing-room, where he had not been many minutes before the door opened, and in came----no other than Sophia herself, who had left the play before the end of the first act; for this, as we have already said, being, a new play, at which two large parties met, the one to damn, and the other to applaud, a violent uproar, and an engagement between the two parties, had so terrified our heroine, that she was glad to put herself under the protection of a young gentleman who safely conveyed her to her chair.\n\nAs Lady Bellaston had acquainted her that she should not be at home till late, Sophia, expecting to find no one in the room, came hastily in, and went directly to a glass which almost fronted her, without once looking towards the upper end of the room, where the statue of Jones now stood motionless.---In this glass it was, after contemplating her own lovely face, that she first discovered the said statue; when, instantly turning about, she perceived the reality of the vision: upon which she gave a violent scream, and scarce preserved herself from fainting, till Jones was able to move to her, and support her in his arms.\n\nTo paint the looks or thoughts of either of these lovers, is beyond my power. As their sensations, from their mutual silence, may be judged to have been too big for their own utterance, it cannot be supposed that I should be able to express them: and the misfortune is, that few of my readers have been enough in love to feel by their own hearts what past at this time in theirs.\n\nAfter a short pause, Jones, with faultering accents, said--“I see, madam, you are surprized.”--“Surprized!” answered she; “Oh heavens! Indeed, I am surprized. I almost doubt whether you are the person you seem.”--“Indeed,” cries he, “my Sophia, pardon me, madam, for this once calling you so, I am that very wretched Jones, whom fortune, after so many disappointments, hath, at last, kindly conducted to you. Oh! my Sophia, did you know the thousand torments I have suffered in this long, fruitless pursuit.”--“Pursuit of whom?” said Sophia, a little recollecting herself, and assuming a reserved air.--“Can you be so cruel to ask that question?” cries Jones; “Need I say, of you?” “Of me!” answered Sophia: “Hath Mr Jones, then, any such important business with me?”--“To some, madam,” cries Jones, “this might seem an important business” (giving her the pocket-book). “I hope, madam, you will find it of the same value as when it was lost.” Sophia took the pocket-book, and was going to speak, when he interrupted her thus:--“Let us not, I beseech you, lose one of these precious moments which fortune hath so kindly sent us. O, my Sophia! I have business of a much superior kind. Thus, on my knees, let me ask your pardon.”--“My pardon!” cries she; “Sure, sir, after what is past, you cannot expect, after what I have heard.”--“I scarce know what I say,” answered Jones. “By heavens! I scarce wish you should pardon me. O my Sophia! henceforth never cast away a thought on such a wretch as I am. If any remembrance of me should ever intrude to give a moment's uneasiness to that tender bosom, think of my unworthiness; and let the remembrance of what passed at Upton blot me for ever from your mind.”\n\nSophia stood trembling all this while. Her face was whiter than snow, and her heart was throbbing through her stays. But, at the mention of Upton, a blush arose in her cheeks, and her eyes, which before she had scarce lifted up, were turned upon Jones with a glance of disdain. He understood this silent reproach, and replied to it thus: “O my Sophia! my only love! you cannot hate or despise me more for what happened there than I do myself; but yet do me the justice to think that my heart was never unfaithful to you. That had no share in the folly I was guilty of; it was even then unalterably yours. Though I despaired of possessing you, nay, almost of ever seeing you more, I doated still on your charming idea, and could seriously love no other woman. But if my heart had not been engaged, she, into whose company I accidently fell at that cursed place, was not an object of serious love. Believe me, my angel, I never have seen her from that day to this; and never intend or desire to see her again.” Sophia, in her heart, was very glad to hear this; but forcing into her face an air of more coldness than she had yet assumed, “Why,” said she, “Mr Jones, do you take the trouble to make a defence where you are not accused? If I thought it worth while to accuse you, I have a charge of unpardonable nature indeed.”--“What is it, for heaven's sake?” answered Jones, trembling and pale, expecting to hear of his amour with Lady Bellaston. “Oh,” said she, “how is it possible! can everything noble and everything base be lodged together in the same bosom?” Lady Bellaston, and the ignominious circumstance of having been kept, rose again in his mind, and stopt his mouth from any reply. “Could I have expected,” proceeded Sophia, “such treatment from you? Nay, from any gentleman, from any man of honour? To have my name traduced in public; in inns, among the meanest vulgar! to have any little favours that my unguarded heart may have too lightly betrayed me to grant, boasted of there! nay, even to hear that you had been forced to fly from my love!”\n\nNothing could equal Jones's surprize at these words of Sophia; but yet, not being guilty, he was much less embarrassed how to defend himself than if she had touched that tender string at which his conscience had been alarmed. By some examination he presently found, that her supposing him guilty of so shocking an outrage against his love, and her reputation, was entirely owing to Partridge's talk at the inns before landlords and servants; for Sophia confessed to him it was from them that she received her intelligence. He had no very great difficulty to make her believe that he was entirely innocent of an offence so foreign to his character; but she had a great deal to hinder him from going instantly home, and putting Partridge to death, which he more than once swore he would do. This point being cleared up, they soon found themselves so well pleased with each other, that Jones quite forgot he had begun the conversation with conjuring her to give up all thoughts of him; and she was in a temper to have given ear to a petition of a very different nature; for before they were aware they had both gone so far, that he let fall some words that sounded like a proposal of marriage. To which she replied, “That, did not her duty to her father forbid her to follow her own inclinations, ruin with him would be more welcome to her than the most affluent fortune with another man.” At the mention of the word ruin, he started, let drop her hand, which he had held for some time, and striking his breast with his own, cried out, “Oh, Sophia! can I then ruin thee? No; by heavens, no! I never will act so base a part. Dearest Sophia, whatever it costs me, I will renounce you; I will give you up; I will tear all such hopes from my heart as are inconsistent with your real good. My love I will ever retain, but it shall be in silence; it shall be at a distance from you; it shall be in some foreign land; from whence no voice, no sigh of my despair, shall ever reach and disturb your ears. And when I am dead”--He would have gone on, but was stopt by a flood of tears which Sophia let fall in his bosom, upon which she leaned, without being able to speak one word. He kissed them off, which, for some moments, she allowed him to do without any resistance; but then recollecting herself, gently withdrew out of his arms; and, to turn the discourse from a subject too tender, and which she found she could not support, bethought herself to ask him a question she never had time to put to him before, “How he came into that room?” He began to stammer, and would, in all probability, have raised her suspicions by the answer he was going to give, when, at once, the door opened, and in came Lady Bellaston.\n\nHaving advanced a few steps, and seeing Jones and Sophia together, she suddenly stopt; when, after a pause of a few moments, recollecting herself with admirable presence of mind, she said--though with sufficient indications of surprize both in voice and countenance--“I thought, Miss Western, you had been at the play?”\n\nThough Sophia had no opportunity of learning of Jones by what means he had discovered her, yet, as she had not the least suspicion of the real truth, or that Jones and Lady Bellaston were acquainted, so she was very little confounded; and the less, as the lady had, in all their conversations on the subject, entirely taken her side against her father. With very little hesitation, therefore, she went through the whole story of what had happened at the play-house, and the cause of her hasty return.\n\nThe length of this narrative gave Lady Bellaston an opportunity of rallying her spirits, and of considering in what manner to act. And as the behaviour of Sophia gave her hopes that Jones had not betrayed her, she put on an air of good humour, and said, “I should not have broke in so abruptly upon you, Miss Western, if I had known you had company.”\n\nLady Bellaston fixed her eyes on Sophia whilst she spoke these words. To which that poor young lady, having her face overspread with blushes and confusion, answered, in a stammering voice, “I am sure, madam, I shall always think the honour of your ladyship's company----” “I hope, at least,” cries Lady Bellaston, “I interrupt no business.”--“No, madam,” answered Sophia, “our business was at an end. Your ladyship may be pleased to remember I have often mentioned the loss of my pocket-book, which this gentleman, having very luckily found, was so kind to return it to me with the bill in it.”\n\nJones, ever since the arrival of Lady Bellaston, had been ready to sink with fear. He sat kicking his heels, playing with his fingers, and looking more like a fool, if it be possible, than a young booby squire, when he is first introduced into a polite assembly. He began, however, now to recover himself; and taking a hint from the behaviour of Lady Bellaston, who he saw did not intend to claim any acquaintance with him, he resolved as entirely to affect the stranger on his part. He said, “Ever since he had the pocket-book in his possession, he had used great diligence in enquiring out the lady whose name was writ in it; but never till that day could be so fortunate to discover her.”\n\nSophia had indeed mentioned the loss of her pocket-book to Lady Bellaston; but as Jones, for some reason or other, had never once hinted to her that it was in his possession, she believed not one syllable of what Sophia now said, and wonderfully admired the extreme quickness of the young lady in inventing such an excuse. The reason of Sophia's leaving the playhouse met with no better credit; and though she could not account for the meeting between these two lovers, she was firmly persuaded it was not accidental.\n\nWith an affected smile, therefore, she said, “Indeed, Miss Western, you have had very good luck in recovering your money. Not only as it fell into the hands of a gentleman of honour, but as he happened to discover to whom it belonged. I think you would not consent to have it advertised.--It was great good fortune, sir, that you found out to whom the note belonged.”\n\n“Oh, madam,” cries Jones, “it was enclosed in a pocket-book, in which the young lady's name was written.”\n\n“That was very fortunate, indeed,” cries the lady:--“And it was no less so, that you heard Miss Western was at my house; for she is very little known.”\n\nJones had at length perfectly recovered his spirits; and as he conceived he had now an opportunity of satisfying Sophia as to the question she had asked him just before Lady Bellaston came in, he proceeded thus: “Why, madam,” answered he, “it was by the luckiest chance imaginable I made this discovery. I was mentioning what I had found, and the name of the owner, the other night to a lady at the masquerade, who told me she believed she knew where I might see Miss Western; and if I would come to her house the next morning she would inform me, I went according to her appointment, but she was not at home; nor could I ever meet with her till this morning, when she directed me to your ladyship's house. I came accordingly, and did myself the honour to ask for your ladyship; and upon my saying that I had very particular business, a servant showed me into this room; where I had not been long before the young lady returned from the play.”\n\nUpon his mentioning the masquerade, he looked very slily at Lady Bellaston, without any fear of being remarked by Sophia; for she was visibly too much confounded to make any observations. This hint a little alarmed the lady, and she was silent; when Jones, who saw the agitation of Sophia's mind, resolved to take the only method of relieving her, which was by retiring; but, before he did this, he said, “I believe, madam, it is customary to give some reward on these occasions;--I must insist on a very high one for my honesty;--it is, madam, no less than the honour of being permitted to pay another visit here.”\n\n“Sir,” replied the lady, “I make no doubt that you are a gentleman, and my doors are never shut to people of fashion.”\n\nJones then, after proper ceremonials, departed, highly to his own satisfaction, and no less to that of Sophia; who was terribly alarmed lest Lady Bellaston should discover what she knew already but too well.\n\nUpon the stairs Jones met his old acquaintance, Mrs Honour, who, notwithstanding all she had said against him, was now so well bred to behave with great civility. This meeting proved indeed a lucky circumstance, as he communicated to her the house where he lodged, with which Sophia was unacquainted.\n\nIn which the thirteenth book is concluded.\nThe elegant Lord Shaftesbury somewhere objects to telling too much truth: by which it may be fairly inferred, that, in some cases, to lie is not only excusable but commendable.\n\nAnd surely there are no persons who may so properly challenge a right to this commendable deviation from truth, as young women in the affair of love; for which they may plead precept, education, and above all, the sanction, nay, I may say the necessity of custom, by which they are restrained, not from submitting to the honest impulses of nature (for that would be a foolish prohibition), but from owning them.\n\nWe are not, therefore, ashamed to say, that our heroine now pursued the dictates of the above-mentioned right honourable philosopher. As she was perfectly satisfied then, that Lady Bellaston was ignorant of the person of Jones, so she determined to keep her in that ignorance, though at the expense of a little fibbing.\n\nJones had not been long gone, before Lady Bellaston cryed, “Upon my word, a good pretty young fellow; I wonder who he is; for I don't remember ever to have seen his face before.”\n\n“Nor I neither, madam,” cries Sophia. “I must say he behaved very handsomely in relation to my note.”\n\n“Yes; and he is a very handsome fellow,” said the lady: “don't you think so?”\n\n“I did not take much notice of him,” answered Sophia, “but I thought he seemed rather awkward, and ungenteel than otherwise.”\n\n“You are extremely right,” cries Lady Bellaston: “you may see, by his manner, that he hath not kept good company. Nay, notwithstanding his returning your note, and refusing the reward, I almost question whether he is a gentleman.----I have always observed there is a something in persons well born, which others can never acquire.----I think I will give orders not to be at home to him.”\n\n“Nay, sure, madam,” answered Sophia, “one can't suspect after what he hath done;--besides, if your ladyship observed him, there was an elegance in his discourse, a delicacy, a prettiness of expression that, that----”\n\n“I confess,” said Lady Bellaston, “the fellow hath words----And indeed, Sophia, you must forgive me, indeed you must.”\n\n“I forgive your ladyship!” said Sophia.\n\n“Yes, indeed you must,” answered she, laughing; “for I had a horrible suspicion when I first came into the room----I vow you must forgive it; but I suspected it was Mr Jones himself.”\n\n“Did your ladyship, indeed?” cries Sophia, blushing, and affecting a laugh.\n\n“Yes, I vow I did,” answered she. “I can't imagine what put it into my head: for, give the fellow his due, he was genteely drest; which, I think, dear Sophy, is not commonly the case with your friend.”\n\n“This raillery,” cries Sophia, “is a little cruel, Lady Bellaston, after my promise to your ladyship.”\n\n“Not at all, child,” said the lady;----“It would have been cruel before; but after you have promised me never to marry without your father's consent, in which you know is implied your giving up Jones, sure you can bear a little raillery on a passion which was pardonable enough in a young girl in the country, and of which you tell me you have so entirely got the better. What must I think, my dear Sophy, if you cannot bear a little ridicule even on his dress? I shall begin to fear you are very far gone indeed; and almost question whether you have dealt ingenuously with me.”\n\n“Indeed, madam,” cries Sophia, “your ladyship mistakes me, if you imagine I had any concern on his account.”\n\n“On his account!” answered the lady: “You must have mistaken me; I went no farther than his dress;----for I would not injure your taste by any other comparison--I don't imagine, my dear Sophy, if your Mr Jones had been such a fellow as this--”\n\n“I thought,” says Sophia, “your ladyship had allowed him to be handsome”----\n\n“Whom, pray?” cried the lady hastily.\n\n“Mr Jones,” answered Sophia;--and immediately recollecting herself, “Mr Jones!--no, no; I ask your pardon;--I mean the gentleman who was just now here.”\n\n“O Sophy! Sophy!” cries the lady; “this Mr Jones, I am afraid, still runs in your head.”\n\n“Then, upon my honour, madam,” said Sophia, “Mr Jones is as entirely indifferent to me, as the gentleman who just now left us.”\n\n“Upon my honour,” said Lady Bellaston, “I believe it. Forgive me, therefore, a little innocent raillery; but I promise you I will never mention his name any more.”\n\nAnd now the two ladies separated, infinitely more to the delight of Sophia than of Lady Bellaston, who would willingly have tormented her rival a little longer, had not business of more importance called her away. As for Sophia, her mind was not perfectly easy under this first practice of deceit; upon which, when she retired to her chamber, she reflected with the highest uneasiness and conscious shame. Nor could the peculiar hardship of her situation, and the necessity of the case, at all reconcile her mind to her conduct; for the frame of her mind was too delicate to bear the thought of having been guilty of a falsehood, however qualified by circumstances. Nor did this thought once suffer her to close her eyes during the whole succeeding night.\n\nAn essay to prove that an author will write the better for having some knowledge of the subject on which he writes.\n\nAs several gentlemen in these times, by the wonderful force of genius only, without the least assistance of learning, perhaps, without being well able to read, have made a considerable figure in the republic of letters; the modern critics, I am told, have lately begun to assert, that all kind of learning is entirely useless to a writer; and, indeed, no other than a kind of fetters on the natural sprightliness and activity of the imagination, which is thus weighed down, and prevented from soaring to those high flights which otherwise it would be able to reach.\n\nThis doctrine, I am afraid, is at present carried much too far: for why should writing differ so much from all other arts? The nimbleness of a dancing-master is not at all prejudiced by being taught to move; nor doth any mechanic, I believe, exercise his tools the worse by having learnt to use them. For my own part, I cannot conceive that Homer or Virgil would have writ with more fire, if instead of being masters of all the learning of their times, they had been as ignorant as most of the authors of the present age. Nor do I believe that all the imagination, fire, and judgment of Pitt, could have produced those orations that have made the senate of England, in these our times, a rival in eloquence to Greece and Rome, if he had not been so well read in the writings of Demosthenes and Cicero, as to have transferred their whole spirit into his speeches, and, with their spirit, their knowledge too.\n\nI would not here be understood to insist on the same fund of learning in any of my brethren, as Cicero persuades us is necessary to the composition of an orator. On the contrary, very little reading is, I conceive, necessary to the poet, less to the critic, and the least of all to the politician. For the first, perhaps, Byshe's Art of Poetry, and a few of our modern poets, may suffice; for the second, a moderate heap of plays; and, for the last, an indifferent collection of political journals.\n\nTo say the truth, I require no more than that a man should have some little knowledge of the subject on which he treats, according to the old maxim of law, Quam quisque nôrit artem in eâ se exerceat. With this alone a writer may sometimes do tolerably well; and, indeed, without this, all the other learning in the world will stand him in little stead.\n\nFor instance, let us suppose that Homer and Virgil, Aristotle and Cicero, Thucydides and Livy, could have met all together, and have clubbed their several talents to have composed a treatise on the art of dancing: I believe it will be readily agreed they could not have equalled the excellent treatise which Mr Essex hath given us on that subject, entitled, The Rudiments of Genteel Education. And, indeed, should the excellent Mr Broughton be prevailed on to set fist to paper, and to complete the above-said rudiments, by delivering down the true principles of athletics, I question whether the world will have any cause to lament, that none of the great writers, either antient or modern, have ever treated about that noble and useful art.\n\nTo avoid a multiplicity of examples in so plain a case, and to come at once to my point, I am apt to conceive, that one reason why many English writers have totally failed in describing the manners of upper life, may possibly be, that in reality they know nothing of it.\n\nThis is a knowledge unhappily not in the power of many authors to arrive at. Books will give us a very imperfect idea of it; nor will the stage a much better: the fine gentleman formed upon reading the former will almost always turn out a pedant, and he who forms himself upon the latter, a coxcomb.\n\nNor are the characters drawn from these models better supported. Vanbrugh and Congreve copied nature; but they who copy them draw as unlike the present age as Hogarth would do if he was to paint a rout or a drum in the dresses of Titian and of Vandyke. In short, imitation here will not do the business. The picture must be after Nature herself. A true knowledge of the world is gained only by conversation, and the manners of every rank must be seen in order to be known.\n\nNow it happens that this higher order of mortals is not to be seen, like all the rest of the human species, for nothing, in the streets, shops, and coffee-houses; nor are they shown, like the upper rank of animals, for so much a-piece. In short, this is a sight to which no persons are admitted without one or other of these qualifications, viz., either birth or fortune, or, what is equivalent to both, the honourable profession of a gamester. And, very unluckily for the world, persons so qualified very seldom care to take upon themselves the bad trade of writing; which is generally entered upon by the lower and poorer sort, as it is a trade which many think requires no kind of stock to set up with.\n\nHence those strange monsters in lace and embroidery, in silks and brocades, with vast wigs and hoops; which, under the name of lords and ladies, strut the stage, to the great delight of attorneys and their clerks in the pit, and of the citizens and their apprentices in the galleries; and which are no more to be found in real life than the centaur, the chimera, or any other creature of mere fiction. But to let my reader into a secret, this knowledge of upper life, though very necessary for preventing mistakes, is no very great resource to a writer whose province is comedy, or that kind of novels which, like this I am writing, is of the comic class.\n\nWhat Mr Pope says of women is very applicable to most in this station, who are, indeed, so entirely made up of form and affectation, that they have no character at all, at least none which appears. I will venture to say the highest life is much the dullest, and affords very little humour or entertainment. The various callings in lower spheres produce the great variety of humorous characters; whereas here, except among the few who are engaged in the pursuit of ambition, and the fewer still who have a relish for pleasure, all is vanity and servile imitation. Dressing and cards, eating and drinking, bowing and courtesying, make up the business of their lives.\n\nSome there are, however, of this rank upon whom passion exercises its tyranny, and hurries them far beyond the bounds which decorum prescribes; of these the ladies are as much distinguished by their noble intrepidity, and a certain superior contempt of reputation, from the frail ones of meaner degree, as a virtuous woman of quality is by the elegance and delicacy of her sentiments from the honest wife of a yeoman and shopkeeper. Lady Bellaston was of this intrepid character; but let not my country readers conclude from her, that this is the general conduct of women of fashion, or that we mean to represent them as such. They might as well suppose that every clergyman was represented by Thwackum, or every soldier by ensign Northerton.\n\nThere is not, indeed, a greater error than that which universally prevails among the vulgar, who, borrowing their opinion from some ignorant satirists, have affixed the character of lewdness to these times. On the contrary, I am convinced there never was less of love intrigue carried on among persons of condition than now. Our present women have been taught by their mothers to fix their thoughts only on ambition and vanity, and to despise the pleasures of love as unworthy their regard; and being afterwards, by the care of such mothers, married without having husbands, they seem pretty well confirmed in the justness of those sentiments; whence they content themselves, for the dull remainder of life, with the pursuit of more innocent, but I am afraid more childish amusements, the bare mention of which would ill suit with the dignity of this history. In my humble opinion, the true characteristic of the present beau monde is rather folly than vice, and the only epithet which it deserves is that of frivolous.\n\nContaining letters and other matters which attend amours.\n\nJones had not been long at home before he received the following letter:--\n\n“I was never more surprized than when I found you was gone. When you left the room I little imagined you intended to have left the house without seeing me again. Your behaviour is all of a piece, and convinces me how much I ought to despise a heart which can doat upon an idiot; though I know not whether I should not admire her cunning more than her simplicity: wonderful both! For though she understood not a word of what passed between us, yet she had the skill, the assurance, the----what shall I call it? to deny to my face that she knows you, or ever saw you before.----Was this a scheme laid between you, and have you been base enough to betray me?----O how I despise her, you, and all the world, but chiefly myself! for----I dare not write what I should afterwards run mad to read; but remember, I can detest as violently as I have loved.”\n\nJones had but little time given him to reflect on this letter, before a second was brought him from the same hand; and this, likewise, we shall set down in the precise words.\n\n“When you consider the hurry of spirits in which I must have writ, you cannot be surprized at any expressions in my former note.--Yet, perhaps, on reflection, they were rather too warm. At least I would, if possible, think all owing to the odious playhouse, and to the impertinence of a fool, which detained me beyond my appointment.----How easy is it to think well of those we love!----Perhaps you desire I should think so. I have resolved to see you to-night; so come to me immediately.\n\n“P.S.--I have ordered to be at home to none but yourself.\n\n“P.S.--Mr Jones will imagine I shall assist him in his defence; for I believe he cannot desire to impose on me more than I desire to impose on myself.\n\n“P.S.--Come immediately.”\n\nTo the men of intrigue I refer the determination, whether the angry or the tender letter gave the greatest uneasiness to Jones. Certain it is, he had no violent inclination to pay any more visits that evening, unless to one single person. However, he thought his honour engaged, and had not this been motive sufficient, he would not have ventured to blow the temper of Lady Bellaston into that flame of which he had reason to think it susceptible, and of which he feared the consequence might be a discovery to Sophia, which he dreaded. After some discontented walks therefore about the room, he was preparing to depart, when the lady kindly prevented him, not by another letter, but by her own presence. She entered the room very disordered in her dress, and very discomposed in her looks, and threw herself into a chair, where, having recovered her breath, she said--“You see, sir, when women have gone one length too far, they will stop at none. If any person would have sworn this to me a week ago, I would not have believed it of myself.” “I hope, madam,” said Jones, “my charming Lady Bellaston will be as difficult to believe anything against one who is so sensible of the many obligations she hath conferred upon him.” “Indeed!” says she, “sensible of obligations! Did I expect to hear such cold language from Mr Jones?” “Pardon me, my dear angel,” said he, “if, after the letters I have received, the terrors of your anger, though I know not how I have deserved it.”--“And have I then,” says she, with a smile, “so angry a countenance?--Have I really brought a chiding face with me?”--“If there be honour in man,” said he, “I have done nothing to merit your anger.--You remember the appointment you sent me; I went in pursuance.”--“I beseech you,” cried she, “do not run through the odious recital.--Answer me but one question, and I shall be easy. Have you not betrayed my honour to her?”--Jones fell upon his knees, and began to utter the most violent protestations, when Partridge came dancing and capering into the room, like one drunk with joy, crying out, “She's found! she's found!--Here, sir, here, she's here--Mrs Honour is upon the stairs.” “Stop her a moment,” cries Jones--“Here, madam, step behind the bed, I have no other room nor closet, nor place on earth to hide you in; sure never was so damned an accident.”--“D--n'd indeed!” said the lady, as she went to her place of concealment; and presently afterwards in came Mrs Honour. “Hey-day!” says she, “Mr Jones, what's the matter?--That impudent rascal your servant would scarce let me come upstairs. I hope he hath not the same reason to keep me from you as he had at Upton.--I suppose you hardly expected to see me; but you have certainly bewitched my lady. Poor dear young lady! To be sure, I loves her as tenderly as if she was my own sister. Lord have mercy upon you, if you don't make her a good husband! and to be sure, if you do not, nothing can be bad enough for you.” Jones begged her only to whisper, for that there was a lady dying in the next room. “A lady!” cries she; “ay, I suppose one of your ladies.--O Mr Jones, there are too many of them in the world; I believe we are got into the house of one, for my Lady Bellaston I darst to say is no better than she should be.”--“Hush! hush!” cries Jones, “every word is overheard in the next room.” “I don't care a farthing,” cries Honour, “I speaks no scandal of any one; but to be sure the servants make no scruple of saying as how her ladyship meets men at another place--where the house goes under the name of a poor gentlewoman; but her ladyship pays the rent, and many's the good thing besides, they say, she hath of her.”--Here Jones, after expressing the utmost uneasiness, offered to stop her mouth:--“Hey-day! why sure, Mr Jones, you will let me speak; I speaks no scandal, for I only says what I heard from others--and thinks I to myself, much good may it do the gentlewoman with her riches, if she comes by it in such a wicked manner. To be sure it is better to be poor and honest.” “The servants are villains,” cries Jones, “and abuse their lady unjustly.”--“Ay, to be sure, servants are always villains, and so my lady says, and won't hear a word of it.”--“No, I am convinced,” says Jones, “my Sophia is above listening to such base scandal.” “Nay, I believe it is no scandal, neither,” cries Honour, “for why should she meet men at another house?--It can never be for any good: for if she had a lawful design of being courted, as to be sure any lady may lawfully give her company to men upon that account: why, where can be the sense?”--“I protest,” cries Jones, “I can't hear all this of a lady of such honour, and a relation of Sophia; besides, you will distract the poor lady in the next room.--Let me entreat you to walk with me down stairs.”--“Nay, sir, if you won't let me speak, I have done.--Here, sir, is a letter from my young lady--what would some men give to have this? But, Mr Jones, I think you are not over and above generous, and yet I have heard some servants say----but I am sure you will do me the justice to own I never saw the colour of your money.” Here Jones hastily took the letter, and presently after slipped five pieces into her hand. He then returned a thousand thanks to his dear Sophia in a whisper, and begged her to leave him to read her letter: she presently departed, not without expressing much grateful sense of his generosity.\n\nLady Bellaston now came from behind the curtain. How shall I describe her rage? Her tongue was at first incapable of utterance; but streams of fire darted from her eyes, and well indeed they might, for her heart was all in a flame. And now as soon as her voice found way, instead of expressing any indignation against Honour or her own servants, she began to attack poor Jones. “You see,” said she, “what I have sacrificed to you; my reputation, my honour--gone for ever! And what return have I found? Neglected, slighted for a country girl, for an idiot.”--“What neglect, madam, or what slight,” cries Jones, “have I been guilty of?”--“Mr Jones,” said she, “it is in vain to dissemble; if you will make me easy, you must entirely give her up; and as a proof of your intention, show me the letter.”--“What letter, madam?” said Jones. “Nay, surely,” said she, “you cannot have the confidence to deny your having received a letter by the hands of that trollop.”--“And can your ladyship,” cries he, “ask of me what I must part with my honour before I grant? Have I acted in such a manner by your ladyship? Could I be guilty of betraying this poor innocent girl to you, what security could you have that I should not act the same part by yourself? A moment's reflection will, I am sure, convince you that a man with whom the secrets of a lady are not safe must be the most contemptible of wretches.”--“Very well,” said she--“I need not insist on your becoming this contemptible wretch in your own opinion; for the inside of the letter could inform me of nothing more than I know already. I see the footing you are upon.”--Here ensued a long conversation, which the reader, who is not too curious, will thank me for not inserting at length. It shall suffice, therefore, to inform him, that Lady Bellaston grew more and more pacified, and at length believed, or affected to believe, his protestations, that his meeting with Sophia that evening was merely accidental, and every other matter which the reader already knows, and which, as Jones set before her in the strongest light, it is plain that she had in reality no reason to be angry with him.\n\nShe was not, however, in her heart perfectly satisfied with his refusal to show her the letter; so deaf are we to the clearest reason, when it argues against our prevailing passions. She was, indeed, well convinced that Sophia possessed the first place in Jones's affections; and yet, haughty and amorous as this lady was, she submitted at last to bear the second place; or, to express it more properly in a legal phrase, was contented with the possession of that of which another woman had the reversion.\n\nIt was at length agreed that Jones should for the future visit at the house: for that Sophia, her maid, and all the servants, would place these visits to the account of Sophia; and that she herself would be considered as the person imposed upon.\n\nThis scheme was contrived by the lady, and highly relished by Jones, who was indeed glad to have a prospect of seeing his Sophia at any rate; and the lady herself was not a little pleased with the imposition on Sophia, which Jones, she thought, could not possibly discover to her for his own sake.\n\nThe next day was appointed for the first visit, and then, after proper ceremonials, the Lady Bellaston returned home.\n\nContaining various matters.\n\nJones was no sooner alone than he eagerly broke open his letter, and read as follows:--\n\n“Sir, it is impossible to express what I have suffered since you left this house; and as I have reason to think you intend coming here again, I have sent Honour, though so late at night, as she tells me she knows your lodgings, to prevent you. I charge you, by all the regard you have for me, not to think of visiting here; for it will certainly be discovered; nay, I almost doubt, from some things which have dropt from her ladyship, that she is not already without some suspicion. Something favourable perhaps may happen; we must wait with patience; but I once more entreat you, if you have any concern for my ease, do not think of returning hither.”\n\nThis letter administered the same kind of consolation to poor Jones, which Job formerly received from his friends. Besides disappointing all the hopes which he promised to himself from seeing Sophia, he was reduced to an unhappy dilemma, with regard to Lady Bellaston; for there are some certain engagements, which, as he well knew, do very difficultly admit of any excuse for the failure; and to go, after the strict prohibition from Sophia, he was not to be forced by any human power. At length, after much deliberation, which during that night supplied the place of sleep, he determined to feign himself sick: for this suggested itself as the only means of failing the appointed visit, without incensing Lady Bellaston, which he had more than one reason of desiring to avoid.\n\nThe first thing, however, which he did in the morning, was, to write an answer to Sophia, which he inclosed in one to Honour. He then despatched another to Lady Bellaston, containing the above-mentioned excuse; and to this he soon received the following answer:--\n\n“I am vexed that I cannot see you here this afternoon, but more concerned for the occasion; take great care of yourself, and have the best advice, and I hope there will be no danger.--I am so tormented all this morning with fools, that I have scarce a moment's time to write to you. Adieu.\n\n“P.S.--I will endeavour to call on you this evening, at nine.--Be sure to be alone.”\n\nMr Jones now received a visit from Mrs Miller, who, after some formal introduction, began the following speech:--“I am very sorry, sir, to wait upon you on such an occasion; but I hope you will consider the ill consequence which it must be to the reputation of my poor girls, if my house should once be talked of as a house of ill-fame. I hope you won't think me, therefore, guilty of impertinence, if I beg you not to bring any more ladies in at that time of night. The clock had struck two before one of them went away.”--“I do assure you, madam,” said Jones, “the lady who was here last night, and who staid the latest (for the other only brought me a letter), is a woman of very great fashion, and my near relation.”--“I don't know what fashion she is of,” answered Mrs Miller; “but I am sure no woman of virtue, unless a very near relation indeed, would visit a young gentleman at ten at night, and stay four hours in his room with him alone; besides, sir, the behaviour of her chairmen shows what she was; for they did nothing but make jests all the evening in the entry, and asked Mr Partridge, in the hearing of my own maid, if madam intended to stay with his master all night; with a great deal of stuff not proper to be repeated. I have really a great respect for you, Mr Jones, upon your own account; nay, I have a very high obligation to you for your generosity to my cousin. Indeed, I did not know how very good you had been till lately. Little did I imagine to what dreadful courses the poor man's distress had driven him. Little did I think, when you gave me the ten guineas, that you had given them to a highwayman! O heavens! what goodness have you shown! How have you preserved this family!--The character which Mr Allworthy hath formerly given me of you was, I find, strictly true.--And indeed, if I had no obligation to you, my obligations to him are such, that, on his account, I should shew you the utmost respect in my power.--Nay, believe me, dear Mr Jones, if my daughters' and my own reputation were out of the case, I should, for your own sake, be sorry that so pretty a young gentleman should converse with these women; but if you are resolved to do it, I must beg you to take another lodging; for I do not myself like to have such things carried on under my roof; but more especially upon the account of my girls, who have little, heaven knows, besides their characters, to recommend them.” Jones started and changed colour at the name of Allworthy. “Indeed, Mrs Miller,” answered he, a little warmly, “I do not take this at all kind. I will never bring any slander on your house; but I must insist on seeing what company I please in my own room; and if that gives you any offence, I shall, as soon as I am able, look for another lodging.”--“I am sorry we must part then, sir,” said she; “but I am convinced Mr Allworthy himself would never come within my doors, if he had the least suspicion of my keeping an ill house.”--“Very well, madam,” said Jones.--“I hope, sir,” said she, “you are not angry; for I would not for the world offend any of Mr Allworthy's family. I have not slept a wink all night about this matter.”--“I am sorry I have disturbed your rest, madam,” said Jones, “but I beg you will send Partridge up to me immediately;” which she promised to do, and then with a very low courtesy retired.\n\nAs soon as Partridge arrived, Jones fell upon him in the most outrageous manner. “How often,” said he, “am I to suffer for your folly, or rather for my own in keeping you? is that tongue of yours resolved upon my destruction?” “What have I done, sir?” answered affrighted Partridge. “Who was it gave you authority to mention the story of the robbery, or that the man you saw here was the person?” “I, sir?” cries Partridge. “Now don't be guilty of a falsehood in denying it,” said Jones. “If I did mention such a matter,” answers Partridge, “I am sure I thought no harm; for I should not have opened my lips, if it had not been to his own friends and relations, who, I imagined, would have let it go no farther.” “But I have a much heavier charge against you,” cries Jones, “than this. How durst you, after all the precautions I gave you, mention the name of Mr Allworthy in this house?” Partridge denied that he ever had, with many oaths. “How else,” said Jones, “should Mrs Miller be acquainted that there was any connexion between him and me? And it is but this moment she told me she respected me on his account.” “O Lord, sir,” said Partridge, “I desire only to be heard out; and to be sure, never was anything so unfortunate: hear me but out, and you will own how wrongfully you have accused me. When Mrs Honour came downstairs last night she met me in the entry, and asked me when my master had heard from Mr Allworthy; and to be sure Mrs Miller heard the very words; and the moment Madam Honour was gone, she called me into the parlour to her. `Mr Partridge,' says she, `what Mr Allworthy is it that the gentlewoman mentioned? is it the great Mr Allworthy of Somersetshire?' `Upon my word, madam,' says I, `I know nothing of the matter.' `Sure,' says she, `your master is not the Mr Jones I have heard Mr Allworthy talk of?' `Upon my word, madam,' says I, `I know nothing of the matter.' `Then,' says she, turning to her daughter Nancy, says she, `as sure as tenpence this is the very young gentleman, and he agrees exactly with the squire's description.' The Lord above knows who it was told her: for I am the arrantest villain that ever walked upon two legs if ever it came out of my mouth. I promise you, sir, I can keep a secret when I am desired. Nay, sir, so far was I from telling her anything about Mr Allworthy, that I told her the very direct contrary; for, though I did not contradict it at that moment, yet, as second thoughts, they say, are best, so when I came to consider that somebody must have informed her, thinks I to myself, I will put an end to the story; and so I went back again into the parlour some time afterwards, and says I, upon my word, says I, whoever, says I, told you that this gentleman was Mr Jones; that is, says I, that this Mr Jones was that Mr Jones, told you a confounded lie: and I beg, says I, you will never mention any such matter, says I; for my master, says I, will think I must have told you so; and I defy anybody in the house ever to say I mentioned any such word. To be certain, sir, it is a wonderful thing, and I have been thinking with myself ever since, how it was she came to know it; not but I saw an old woman here t'other day a begging at the door, who looked as like her we saw in Warwickshire, that caused all that mischief to us. To be sure it is never good to pass by an old woman without giving her something, especially if she looks at you; for all the world shall never persuade me but that they have a great power to do mischief, and to be sure I shall never see an old woman again, but I shall think to myself, Infandum, regina, jubes renovare dolorem.”\n\nThe simplicity of Partridge set Jones a laughing, and put a final end to his anger, which had indeed seldom any long duration in his mind; and, instead of commenting on his defence, he told him he intended presently to leave those lodgings, and ordered him to go and endeavour to get him others.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nWhich we hope will be very attentively perused by young people of both sexes.\nPartridge had no sooner left Mr Jones than Mr Nightingale, with whom he had now contracted a great intimacy, came to him, and, after a short salutation, said, “So, Tom, I hear you had company very late last night. Upon my soul you are a happy fellow, who have not been in town above a fortnight, and can keep chairs waiting at your door till two in the morning.” He then ran on with much commonplace raillery of the same kind, till Jones at last interrupted him, saying, “I suppose you have received all this information from Mrs Miller, who hath been up here a little while ago to give me warning. The good woman is afraid, it seems, of the reputation of her daughters.” “Oh! she is wonderfully nice,” says Nightingale, “upon that account; if you remember, she would not let Nancy go with us to the masquerade.” “Nay, upon my honour, I think she's in the right of it,” says Jones: “however, I have taken her at her word, and have sent Partridge to look for another lodging.” “If you will,” says Nightingale, “we may, I believe, be again together; for, to tell you a secret, which I desire you won't mention in the family, I intend to quit the house to-day.” “What, hath Mrs Miller given you warning too, my friend?” cries Jones. “No,” answered the other; “but the rooms are not convenient enough. Besides, I am grown weary of this part of the town. I want to be nearer the places of diversion; so I am going to Pall-mall.” “And do you intend to make a secret of your going away?” said Jones. “I promise you,” answered Nightingale, “I don't intend to bilk my lodgings; but I have a private reason for not taking a formal leave.” “Not so private,” answered Jones; “I promise you, I have seen it ever since the second day of my coming to the house. Here will be some wet eyes on your departure. Poor Nancy, I pity her, faith! Indeed, Jack, you have played the fool with that girl. You have given her a longing, which I am afraid nothing will ever cure her of.” Nightingale answered, “What the devil would you have me do? would you have me marry her to cure her?” “No,” answered Jones, “I would not have had you make love to her, as you have often done in my presence. I have been astonished at the blindness of her mother in never seeing it.” “Pugh, see it!” cries Nightingale. “What, the devil should she see?” “Why, see,” said Jones, “that you have made her daughter distractedly in love with you. The poor girl cannot conceal it a moment; her eyes are never off from you, and she always colours every time you come into the room. Indeed, I pity her heartily; for she seems to be one of the best-natured and honestest of human creatures.” “And so,” answered Nightingale, “according to your doctrine, one must not amuse oneself by any common gallantries with women, for fear they should fall in love with us.” “Indeed, Jack,” said Jones, “you wilfully misunderstand me; I do not fancy women are so apt to fall in love; but you have gone far beyond common gallantries.” “What, do you suppose,” says Nightingale, “that we have been a-bed together?” “No, upon my honour,” answered Jones, very seriously, “I do not suppose so ill of you; nay, I will go farther, I do not imagine you have laid a regular premeditated scheme for the destruction of the quiet of a poor little creature, or have even foreseen the consequence: for I am sure thou art a very good-natured fellow; and such a one can never be guilty of a cruelty of that kind; but at the same time you have pleased your own vanity, without considering that this poor girl was made a sacrifice to it; and while you have had no design but of amusing an idle hour, you have actually given her reason to flatter herself that you had the most serious designs in her favour. Prithee, Jack, answer me honestly; to what have tended all those elegant and luscious descriptions of happiness arising from violent and mutual fondness? all those warm professions of tenderness, and generous disinterested love? Did you imagine she would not apply them? or, speak ingenuously, did not you intend she should?” “Upon my soul, Tom,” cries Nightingale, “I did not think this was in thee. Thou wilt make an admirable parson. So I suppose you would not go to bed to Nancy now, if she would let you?” “No,” cries Jones, “may I be d--n'd if I would.” “Tom, Tom,” answered Nightingale, “last night; remember last night----\n\nWhen every eye was closed, and the pale moon, And silent stars, shone conscious of the theft.”\n\n“Lookee, Mr Nightingale,” said Jones, “I am no canting hypocrite, nor do I pretend to the gift of chastity, more than my neighbours. I have been guilty with women, I own it; but am not conscious that I have ever injured any.--Nor would I, to procure pleasure to myself, be knowingly the cause of misery to any human being.”\n\n“Well, well,” said Nightingale, “I believe you, and I am convinced you acquit me of any such thing.”\n\n“I do, from my heart,” answered Jones, “of having debauched the girl, but not from having gained her affections.”\n\n“If I have,” said Nightingale, “I am sorry for it; but time and absence will soon wear off such impressions. It is a receipt I must take myself; for, to confess the truth to you--I never liked any girl half so much in my whole life; but I must let you into the whole secret, Tom. My father hath provided a match for me with a woman I never saw; and she is now coming to town, in order for me to make my addresses to her.”\n\nAt these words Jones burst into a loud fit of laughter; when Nightingale cried--“Nay, prithee, don't turn me into ridicule. The devil take me if I am not half mad about this matter! my poor Nancy! Oh! Jones, Jones, I wish I had a fortune in my own possession.”\n\n“I heartily wish you had,” cries Jones; “for, if this be the case, I sincerely pity you both; but surely you don't intend to go away without taking your leave of her?”\n\n“I would not,” answered Nightingale, “undergo the pain of taking leave, for ten thousand pounds; besides, I am convinced, instead of answering any good purpose, it would only serve to inflame my poor Nancy the more. I beg, therefore, you would not mention a word of it to-day, and in the evening, or to-morrow morning, I intend to depart.”\n\nJones promised he would not; and said, upon reflection, he thought, as he had determined and was obliged to leave her, he took the most prudent method. He then told Nightingale he should be very glad to lodge in the same house with him; and it was accordingly agreed between them, that Nightingale should procure him either the ground floor, or the two pair of stairs; for the young gentleman himself was to occupy that which was between them.\n\nThis Nightingale, of whom we shall be presently obliged to say a little more, was in the ordinary transactions of life a man of strict honour, and, what is more rare among young gentlemen of the town, one of strict honesty too; yet in affairs of love he was somewhat loose in his morals; not that he was even here as void of principle as gentlemen sometimes are, and oftener affect to be; but it is certain he had been guilty of some indefensible treachery to women, and had, in a certain mystery, called making love, practised many deceits, which, if he had used in trade, he would have been counted the greatest villain upon earth.\n\nBut as the world, I know not well for what reason, agree to see this treachery in a better light, he was so far from being ashamed of his iniquities of this kind, that he gloried in them, and would often boast of his skill in gaining of women, and his triumphs over their hearts, for which he had before this time received some rebukes from Jones, who always exprest great bitterness against any misbehaviour to the fair part of the species, who, if considered, he said, as they ought to be, in the light of the dearest friends, were to be cultivated, honoured, and caressed with the utmost love and tenderness; but, if regarded as enemies, were a conquest of which a man ought rather to be ashamed than to value himself upon it.\n\nA short account of the history of Mrs Miller.\n\nJones this day eat a pretty good dinner for a sick man, that is to say, the larger half of a shoulder of mutton. In the afternoon he received an invitation from Mrs Miller to drink tea; for that good woman, having learnt, either by means of Partridge, or by some other means natural or supernatural, that he had a connexion with Mr Allworthy, could not endure the thoughts of parting with him in an angry manner.\n\nJones accepted the invitation; and no sooner was the tea-kettle removed, and the girls sent out of the room, than the widow, without much preface, began as follows: “Well, there are very surprizing things happen in this world; but certainly it is a wonderful business that I should have a relation of Mr Allworthy in my house, and never know anything of the matter. Alas! sir, you little imagine what a friend that best of gentlemen hath been to me and mine. Yes, sir, I am not ashamed to own it; it is owing to his goodness that I did not long since perish for want, and leave my poor little wretches, two destitute, helpless, friendless orphans, to the care, or rather to the cruelty, of the world.\n\n“You must know, sir, though I am now reduced to get my living by letting lodgings, I was born and bred a gentlewoman. My father was an officer of the army, and died in a considerable rank: but he lived up to his pay; and, as that expired with him, his family, at his death, became beggars. We were three sisters. One of us had the good luck to die soon after of the small-pox; a lady was so kind as to take the second out of charity, as she said, to wait upon her. The mother of this lady had been a servant to my grand-mother; and, having inherited a vast fortune from her father, which he had got by pawnbroking, was married to a gentleman of great estate and fashion. She used my sister so barbarously, often upbraiding her with her birth and poverty, calling her in derision a gentlewoman, that I believe she at length broke the heart of the poor girl. In short, she likewise died within a twelvemonth after my father. Fortune thought proper to provide better for me, and within a month from his decease I was married to a clergyman, who had been my lover a long time before, and who had been very ill used by my father on that account: for though my poor father could not give any of us a shilling, yet he bred us up as delicately, considered us, and would have had us consider ourselves, as highly as if we had been the richest heiresses. But my dear husband forgot all this usage, and the moment we were become fatherless he immediately renewed his addresses to me so warmly, that I, who always liked, and now more than ever esteemed him, soon complied. Five years did I live in a state of perfect happiness with that best of men, till at last--Oh! cruel! cruel fortune, that ever separated us, that deprived me of the kindest of husbands and my poor girls of the tenderest parent.--O my poor girls! you never knew the blessing which ye lost.--I am ashamed, Mr Jones, of this womanish weakness; but I shall never mention him without tears.” “I ought rather, madam,” said Jones, “to be ashamed that I do not accompany you.” “Well, sir,” continued she, “I was now left a second time in a much worse condition than before; besides the terrible affliction I was to encounter, I had now two children to provide for; and was, if possible, more pennyless than ever; when that great, that good, that glorious man, Mr Allworthy, who had some little acquaintance with my husband, accidentally heard of my distress, and immediately writ this letter to me. Here, sir, here it is; I put it into my pocket to shew it you. This is the letter, sir; I must and will read it to you.\n\n“'Madam,\n\n“'I heartily condole with you on your late grievous loss, which your own good sense, and the excellent lessons you must have learnt from the worthiest of men, will better enable you to bear than any advice which I am capable of giving. Nor have I any doubt that you, whom I have heard to be the tenderest of mothers, will suffer any immoderate indulgence of grief to prevent you from discharging your duty to those poor infants, who now alone stand in need of your tenderness.\n\n“`However, as you must be supposed at present to be incapable of much worldly consideration, you will pardon my having ordered a person to wait on you, and to pay you twenty guineas, which I beg you will accept till I have the pleasure of seeing you, and believe me to be, madam, &c.'\n\n“This letter, sir, I received within a fortnight after the irreparable loss I have mentioned; and within a fortnight afterwards, Mr Allworthy--the blessed Mr Allworthy, came to pay me a visit, when he placed me in the house where you now see me, gave me a large sum of money to furnish it, and settled an annuity of £50 a-year upon me, which I have constantly received ever since. Judge, then, Mr Jones, in what regard I must hold a benefactor, to whom I owe the preservation of my life, and of those dear children, for whose sake alone my life is valuable. Do not, therefore, think me impertinent, Mr Jones (since I must esteem one for whom I know Mr Allworthy hath so much value), if I beg you not to converse with these wicked women. You are a young gentleman, and do not know half their artful wiles. Do not be angry with me, sir, for what I said upon account of my house; you must be sensible it would be the ruin of my poor dear girls. Besides, sir, you cannot but be acquainted that Mr Allworthy himself would never forgive my conniving at such matters, and particularly with you.”\n\n“Upon my word, madam,” said Jones, “you need make no farther apology; nor do I in the least take anything ill you have said; but give me leave, as no one can have more value than myself for Mr Allworthy, to deliver you from one mistake, which, perhaps, would not be altogether for his honour; I do assure you, I am no relation of his.”\n\n“Alas! sir,” answered she, “I know you are not, I know very well who you are; for Mr Allworthy hath told me all; but I do assure you, had you been twenty times his son, he could not have expressed more regard for you than he hath often expressed in my presence. You need not be ashamed, sir, of what you are; I promise you no good person will esteem you the less on that account. No, Mr Jones, the words `dishonourable birth' are nonsense, as my dear, dear husband used to say, unless the word `dishonourable' be applied to the parents; for the children can derive no real dishonour from an act of which they are intirely innocent.”\n\nHere Jones heaved a deep sigh, and then said, “Since I perceive, madam, you really do know me, and Mr Allworthy hath thought proper to mention my name to you; and since you have been so explicit with me as to your own affairs, I will acquaint you with some more circumstances concerning myself.” And these Mrs Miller having expressed great desire and curiosity to hear, he began and related to her his whole history, without once mentioning the name of Sophia.\n\nThere is a kind of sympathy in honest minds, by means of which they give an easy credit to each other. Mrs Miller believed all which Jones told her to be true, and exprest much pity and concern for him. She was beginning to comment on the story, but Jones interrupted her; for, as the hour of assignation now drew nigh, he began to stipulate for a second interview with the lady that evening, which he promised should be the last at her house; swearing, at the same time, that she was one of great distinction, and that nothing but what was intirely innocent was to pass between them; and I do firmly believe he intended to keep his word.\n\nMrs Miller was at length prevailed on, and Jones departed to his chamber, where he sat alone till twelve o'clock, but no Lady Bellaston appeared.\n\nAs we have said that this lady had a great affection for Jones, and as it must have appeared that she really had so, the reader may perhaps wonder at the first failure of her appointment, as she apprehended him to be confined by sickness, a season when friendship seems most to require such visits. This behaviour, therefore, in the lady, may, by some, be condemned as unnatural; but that is not our fault; for our business is only to record truth.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\nContaining a scene which we doubt not will affect all our readers.\n\nMr Jones closed not his eyes during all the former part of the night; not owing to any uneasiness which he conceived at being disappointed by Lady Bellaston; nor was Sophia herself, though most of his waking hours were justly to be charged to her account, the present cause of dispelling his slumbers. In fact, poor Jones was one of the best-natured fellows alive, and had all that weakness which is called compassion, and which distinguishes this imperfect character from that noble firmness of mind, which rolls a man, as it were, within himself, and like a polished bowl, enables him to run through the world without being once stopped by the calamities which happen to others. He could not help, therefore, compassionating the situation of poor Nancy, whose love for Mr Nightingale seemed to him so apparent, that he was astonished at the blindness of her mother, who had more than once, the preceding evening, remarked to him the great change in the temper of her daughter, “who from being,” she said, “one of the liveliest, merriest girls in the world, was, on a sudden, become all gloom and melancholy.”\n\nSleep, however, at length got the better of all resistance; and now, as if he had already been a deity, as the antients imagined, and an offended one too, he seemed to enjoy his dear-bought conquest.--To speak simply, and without any metaphor, Mr Jones slept till eleven the next morning, and would, perhaps, have continued in the same quiet situation much longer, had not a violent uproar awakened him.\n\nPartridge was now summoned, who, being asked what was the matter, answered, “That there was a dreadful hurricane below-stairs; that Miss Nancy was in fits; and that the other sister, and the mother, were both crying and lamenting over her.” Jones expressed much concern at this news; which Partridge endeavoured to relieve, by saying, with a smile, “he fancied the young lady was in no danger of death; for that Susan” (which was the name of the maid) “had given him to understand, it was nothing more than a common affair. In short,” said he, “Miss Nancy hath had a mind to be as wise as her mother; that's all; she was a little hungry, it seems, and so sat down to dinner before grace was said; and so there is a child coming for the Foundling Hospital.”----“Prithee, leave thy stupid jesting,” cries Jones. “Is the misery of these poor wretches a subject of mirth? Go immediately to Mrs Miller, and tell her I beg leave--Stay, you will make some blunder; I will go myself; for she desired me to breakfast with her.” He then rose and dressed himself as fast as he could; and while he was dressing, Partridge, notwithstanding many severe rebukes, could not avoid throwing forth certain pieces of brutality, commonly called jests, on this occasion. Jones was no sooner dressed than he walked downstairs, and knocking at the door, was presently admitted by the maid, into the outward parlour, which was as empty of company as it was of any apparatus for eating. Mrs Miller was in the inner room with her daughter, whence the maid presently brought a message to Mr Jones, “That her mistress hoped he would excuse the disappointment, but an accident had happened, which made it impossible for her to have the pleasure of his company at breakfast that day; and begged his pardon for not sending him up notice sooner.” Jones desired, “She would give herself no trouble about anything so trifling as his disappointment; that he was heartily sorry for the occasion; and that if he could be of any service to her, she might command him.”\n\nHe had scarce spoke these words, when Mrs Miller, who heard them all, suddenly threw open the door, and coming out to him, in a flood of tears, said, “O Mr Jones! you are certainly one of the best young men alive. I give you a thousand thanks for your kind offer of your service; but, alas! sir, it is out of your power to preserve my poor girl.--O my child! my child! she is undone, she is ruined for ever!” “I hope, madam,” said Jones, “no villain”----“O Mr Jones!” said she, “that villain who yesterday left my lodgings, hath betrayed my poor girl; hath destroyed her.--I know you are a man of honour. You have a good--a noble heart, Mr Jones. The actions to which I have been myself a witness, could proceed from no other. I will tell you all: nay, indeed, it is impossible, after what hath happened, to keep it a secret. That Nightingale, that barbarous villain, hath undone my daughter. She is--she is--oh! Mr Jones, my girl is with child by him; and in that condition he hath deserted her. Here! here, sir, is his cruel letter: read it, Mr Jones, and tell me if such another monster lives.”\n\nThe letter was as follows:\n\n“As I found it impossible to mention to you what, I am afraid, will be no less shocking to you, than it is to me, I have taken this method to inform you, that my father insists upon my immediately paying my addresses to a young lady of fortune, whom he hath provided for my--I need not write the detested word. Your own good understanding will make you sensible, how entirely I am obliged to an obedience, by which I shall be for ever excluded from your dear arms. The fondness of your mother may encourage you to trust her with the unhappy consequence of our love, which may be easily kept a secret from the world, and for which I will take care to provide, as I will for you. I wish you may feel less on this account than I have suffered; but summon all your fortitude to your assistance, and forgive and forget the man, whom nothing but the prospect of certain ruin could have forced to write this letter. I bid you forget me, I mean only as a lover; but the best of friends you shall ever find in your faithful, though unhappy,\n\nWhen Jones had read this letter, they both stood silent during a minute, looking at each other; at last he began thus: “I cannot express, madam, how much I am shocked at what I have read; yet let me beg you, in one particular, to take the writer's advice. Consider the reputation of your daughter.”----“It is gone, it is lost, Mr Jones,” cryed she, “as well as her innocence. She received the letter in a room full of company, and immediately swooning away upon opening it, the contents were known to every one present. But the loss of her reputation, bad as it is, is not the worst; I shall lose my child; she hath attempted twice to destroy herself already; and though she hath been hitherto prevented, vows she will not outlive it; nor could I myself outlive any accident of that nature.--What then will become of my little Betsy, a helpless infant orphan? and the poor little wretch will, I believe, break her heart at the miseries with which she sees her sister and myself distracted, while she is ignorant of the cause. O 'tis the most sensible, and best-natured little thing! The barbarous, cruel----hath destroyed us all. O my poor children! Is this the reward of all my cares? Is this the fruit of all my prospects? Have I so chearfully undergone all the labours and duties of a mother? Have I been so tender of their infancy, so careful of their education? Have I been toiling so many years, denying myself even the conveniences of life, to provide some little sustenance for them, to lose one or both in such a manner?” “Indeed, madam,” said Jones, with tears in his eyes, “I pity you from my soul.”--“O! Mr Jones,” answered she, “even you, though I know the goodness of your heart, can have no idea of what I feel. The best, the kindest, the most dutiful of children! O my poor Nancy, the darling of my soul! the delight of my eyes! the pride of my heart! too much, indeed, my pride; for to those foolish, ambitious hopes, arising from her beauty, I owe her ruin. Alas! I saw with pleasure the liking which this young man had for her. I thought it an honourable affection; and flattered my foolish vanity with the thoughts of seeing her married to one so much her superior. And a thousand times in my presence, nay, often in yours, he hath endeavoured to soothe and encourage these hopes by the most generous expressions of disinterested love, which he hath always directed to my poor girl, and which I, as well as she, believed to be real. Could I have believed that these were only snares laid to betray the innocence of my child, and for the ruin of us all?”--At these words little Betsy came running into the room, crying, “Dear mamma, for heaven's sake come to my sister; for she is in another fit, and my cousin can't hold her.” Mrs Miller immediately obeyed the summons; but first ordered Betsy to stay with Mr Jones, and begged him to entertain her a few minutes, saying, in the most pathetic voice, “Good heaven! let me preserve one of my children at least.”\n\nJones, in compliance with this request, did all he could to comfort the little girl, though he was, in reality, himself very highly affected with Mrs Miller's story. He told her “Her sister would be soon very well again; that by taking on in that manner she would not only make her sister worse, but make her mother ill too.” “Indeed, sir,” says she, “I would not do anything to hurt them for the world. I would burst my heart rather than they should see me cry.--But my poor sister can't see me cry.--I am afraid she will never be able to see me cry any more. Indeed, I can't part with her; indeed, I can't.--And then poor mamma too, what will become of her?--She says she will die too, and leave me: but I am resolved I won't be left behind.” “And are you not afraid to die, my little Betsy?” said Jones. “Yes,” answered she, “I was always afraid to die; because I must have left my mamma, and my sister; but I am not afraid of going anywhere with those I love.”\n\nJones was so pleased with this answer, that he eagerly kissed the child; and soon after Mrs Miller returned, saying, “She thanked heaven Nancy was now come to herself. And now, Betsy,” says she, “you may go in, for your sister is better, and longs to see you.” She then turned to Jones, and began to renew her apologies for having disappointed him of his breakfast.\n\n“I hope, madam,” said Jones, “I shall have a more exquisite repast than any you could have provided for me. This, I assure you, will be the case, if I can do any service to this little family of love. But whatever success may attend my endeavours, I am resolved to attempt it. I am very much deceived in Mr Nightingale, if, notwithstanding what hath happened, he hath not much goodness of heart at the bottom, as well as a very violent affection for your daughter. If this be the case, I think the picture which I shall lay before him will affect him. Endeavour, madam, to comfort yourself, and Miss Nancy, as well as you can. I will go instantly in quest of Mr Nightingale; and I hope to bring you good news.”\n\nMrs Miller fell upon her knees and invoked all the blessings of heaven upon Mr Jones; to which she afterwards added the most passionate expressions of gratitude. He then departed to find Mr Nightingale, and the good woman returned to comfort her daughter, who was somewhat cheared at what her mother told her; and both joined in resounding the praises of Mr Jones.\n\nThe interview between Mr Jones and Mr Nightingale.\nThe good or evil we confer on others very often, I believe, recoils on ourselves. For as men of a benign disposition enjoy their own acts of beneficence equally with those to whom they are done, so there are scarce any natures so entirely diabolical, as to be capable of doing injuries, without paying themselves some pangs for the ruin which they bring on their fellow-creatures.\n\nMr Nightingale, at least, was not such a person. On the contrary, Jones found him in his new lodgings, sitting melancholy by the fire, and silently lamenting the unhappy situation in which he had placed poor Nancy. He no sooner saw his friend appear than he arose hastily to meet him; and after much congratulation said, “Nothing could be more opportune than this kind visit; for I was never more in the spleen in my life.”\n\n“I am sorry,” answered Jones, “that I bring news very unlikely to relieve you: nay, what I am convinced must, of all other, shock you the most. However, it is necessary you should know it. Without further preface, then, I come to you, Mr Nightingale, from a worthy family, which you have involved in misery and ruin.” Mr Nightingale changed colour at these words; but Jones, without regarding it, proceeded, in the liveliest manner, to paint the tragical story with which the reader was acquainted in the last chapter.\n\nNightingale never once interrupted the narration, though he discovered violent emotions at many parts of it. But when it was concluded, after fetching a deep sigh, he said, “What you tell me, my friend, affects me in the tenderest manner. Sure there never was so cursed an accident as the poor girl's betraying my letter. Her reputation might otherwise have been safe, and the affair might have remained a profound secret; and then the girl might have gone off never the worse; for many such things happen in this town: and if the husband should suspect a little, when it is too late, it will be his wiser conduct to conceal his suspicion both from his wife and the world.”\n\n“Indeed, my friend,” answered Jones, “this could not have been the case with your poor Nancy. You have so entirely gained her affections, that it is the loss of you, and not of her reputation, which afflicts her, and will end in the destruction of her and her family.” “Nay, for that matter, I promise you,” cries Nightingale, “she hath my affections so absolutely, that my wife, whoever she is to be, will have very little share in them.” “And is it possible then,” said Jones, “you can think of deserting her?” “Why, what can I do?” answered the other. “Ask Miss Nancy,” replied Jones warmly. “In the condition to which you have reduced her, I sincerely think she ought to determine what reparation you shall make her. Her interest alone, and not yours, ought to be your sole consideration. But if you ask me what you shall do, what can you do less,” cries Jones, “than fulfil the expectations of her family, and her own? Nay, I sincerely tell you, they were mine too, ever since I first saw you together. You will pardon me if I presume on the friendship you have favoured me with, moved as I am with compassion for those poor creatures. But your own heart will best suggest to you, whether you have never intended, by your conduct, to persuade the mother, as well as the daughter, into an opinion, that you designed honourably: and if so, though there may have been no direct promise of marriage in the case, I will leave to your own good understanding, how far you are bound to proceed.”\n\n“Nay, I must not only confess what you have hinted,” said Nightingale; “but I am afraid even that very promise you mention I have given.” “And can you, after owning that,” said Jones, “hesitate a moment?” “Consider, my friend,” answered the other; “I know you are a man of honour, and would advise no one to act contrary to its rules; if there were no other objection, can I, after this publication of her disgrace, think of such an alliance with honour?” “Undoubtedly,” replied Jones, “and the very best and truest honour, which is goodness, requires it of you. As you mention a scruple of this kind, you will give me leave to examine it. Can you with honour be guilty of having under false pretences deceived a young woman and her family, and of having by these means treacherously robbed her of her innocence? Can you, with honour, be the knowing, the wilful occasion, nay, the artful contriver of the ruin of a human being? Can you, with honour, destroy the fame, the peace, nay, probably, both the life and soul too, of this creature? Can honour bear the thought, that this creature is a tender, helpless, defenceless, young woman? A young woman, who loves, who doats on you, who dies for you; who hath placed the utmost confidence in your promises; and to that confidence hath sacrificed everything which is dear to her? Can honour support such contemplations as these a moment?”\n\n“Common sense, indeed,” said Nightingale, “warrants all you say; but yet you well know the opinion of the world is so contrary to it, that, was I to marry a whore, though my own, I should be ashamed of ever showing my face again.”\n\n“Fie upon it, Mr Nightingale!” said Jones, “do not call her by so ungenerous a name: when you promised to marry her she became your wife; and she hath sinned more against prudence than virtue. And what is this world which you would be ashamed to face but the vile, the foolish, and the profligate? Forgive me if I say such a shame must proceed from false modesty, which always attends false honour as its shadow.--But I am well assured there is not a man of real sense and goodness in the world who would not honour and applaud the action. But, admit no other would, would not your own heart, my friend, applaud it? And do not the warm, rapturous sensations, which we feel from the consciousness of an honest, noble, generous, benevolent action, convey more delight to the mind than the undeserved praise of millions? Set the alternative fairly before your eyes. On the one side, see this poor, unhappy, tender, believing girl, in the arms of her wretched mother, breathing her last. Hear her breaking heart in agonies, sighing out your name; and lamenting, rather than accusing, the cruelty which weighs her down to destruction. Paint to your imagination the circumstances of her fond despairing parent, driven to madness, or, perhaps, to death, by the loss of her lovely daughter. View the poor, helpless, orphan infant; and when your mind hath dwelt a moment only on such ideas, consider yourself as the cause of all the ruin of this poor, little, worthy, defenceless family. On the other side, consider yourself as relieving them from their temporary sufferings. Think with what joy, with what transports that lovely creature will fly to your arms. See her blood returning to her pale cheeks, her fire to her languid eyes, and raptures to her tortured breast. Consider the exultations of her mother, the happiness of all. Think of this little family made by one act of yours completely happy. Think of this alternative, and sure I am mistaken in my friend if it requires any long deliberation whether he will sink these wretches down for ever, or, by one generous, noble resolution, raise them all from the brink of misery and despair to the highest pitch of human happiness. Add to this but one consideration more; the consideration that it is your duty so to do--That the misery from which you will relieve these poor people is the misery which you yourself have wilfully brought upon them.”\n\n“O, my dear friend!” cries Nightingale, “I wanted not your eloquence to rouse me. I pity poor Nancy from my soul, and would willingly give anything in my power that no familiarities had ever passed between us. Nay, believe me, I had many struggles with my passion before I could prevail with myself to write that cruel letter, which hath caused all the misery in that unhappy family. If I had no inclinations to consult but my own, I would marry her to-morrow morning: I would, by heaven! but you will easily imagine how impossible it would be to prevail on my father to consent to such a match; besides, he hath provided another for me; and to-morrow, by his express command, I am to wait on the lady.”\n\n“I have not the honour to know your father,” said Jones; “but, suppose he could be persuaded, would you yourself consent to the only means of preserving these poor people?” “As eagerly as I would pursue my happiness,” answered Nightingale: “for I never shall find it in any other woman.--O, my dear friend! could you imagine what I have felt within these twelve hours for my poor girl, I am convinced she would not engross all your pity. Passion leads me only to her; and, if I had any foolish scruples of honour, you have fully satisfied them: could my father be induced to comply with my desires, nothing would be wanting to compleat my own happiness or that of my Nancy.”\n\n“Then I am resolved to undertake it,” said Jones. “You must not be angry with me, in whatever light it may be necessary to set this affair, which, you may depend on it, could not otherwise be long hid from him: for things of this nature make a quick progress when once they get abroad, as this unhappily hath already. Besides, should any fatal accident follow, as upon my soul I am afraid will, unless immediately prevented, the public would ring of your name in a manner which, if your father hath common humanity, must offend him. If you will therefore tell me where I may find the old gentleman, I will not lose a moment in the business; which, while I pursue, you cannot do a more generous action than by paying a visit to the poor girl. You will find I have not exaggerated in the account I have given of the wretchedness of the family.”\n\nNightingale immediately consented to the proposal; and now, having acquainted Jones with his father's lodging, and the coffee-house where he would most probably find him, he hesitated a moment, and then said, “My dear Tom, you are going to undertake an impossibility. If you knew my father you would never think of obtaining his consent.----Stay, there is one way--suppose you told him I was already married, it might be easier to reconcile him to the fact after it was done; and, upon my honour, I am so affected with what you have said, and I love my Nancy so passionately, I almost wish it was done, whatever might be the consequence.”\n\nJones greatly approved the hint, and promised to pursue it. They then separated, Nightingale to visit his Nancy, and Jones in quest of the old gentleman.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\nWhat passed between Jones and old Mr Nightingale; with the arrival of a person not yet mentioned in this history.\n\nNotwithstanding the sentiment of the Roman satirist, which denies the divinity of fortune, and the opinion of Seneca to the same purpose; Cicero, who was, I believe, a wiser man than either of them, expressly holds the contrary; and certain it is, there are some incidents in life so very strange and unaccountable, that it seems to require more than human skill and foresight in producing them.\n\nOf this kind was what now happened to Jones, who found Mr Nightingale the elder in so critical a minute, that Fortune, if she was really worthy all the worship she received at Rome, could not have contrived such another. In short, the old gentleman, and the father of the young lady whom he intended for his son, had been hard at it for many hours; and the latter was just now gone, and had left the former delighted with the thoughts that he had succeeded in a long contention, which had been between the two fathers of the future bride and bridegroom; in which both endeavoured to overreach the other, and, as it not rarely happens in such cases, both had retreated fully satisfied of having obtained the victory.\n\nThis gentleman, whom Mr Jones now visited, was what they call a man of the world; that is to say, a man who directs his conduct in this world as one who, being fully persuaded there is no other, is resolved to make the most of this. In his early years he had been bred to trade; but, having acquired a very good fortune, he had lately declined his business; or, to speak more properly, had changed it from dealing in goods, to dealing only in money, of which he had always a plentiful fund at command, and of which he knew very well how to make a very plentiful advantage, sometimes of the necessities of private men, and sometimes of those of the public. He had indeed conversed so entirely with money, that it may be almost doubted whether he imagined there was any other thing really existing in the world; this at least may be certainly averred, that he firmly believed nothing else to have any real value.\n\nThe reader will, I fancy, allow that Fortune could not have culled out a more improper person for Mr Jones to attack with any probability of success; nor could the whimsical lady have directed this attack at a more unseasonable time.\n\nAs money then was always uppermost in this gentleman's thoughts, so the moment he saw a stranger within his doors it immediately occurred to his imagination, that such stranger was either come to bring him money, or to fetch it from him. And according as one or other of these thoughts prevailed, he conceived a favourable or unfavourable idea of the person who approached him.\n\nUnluckily for Jones, the latter of these was the ascendant at present; for as a young gentleman had visited him the day before, with a bill from his son for a play debt, he apprehended, at the first sight of Jones, that he was come on such another errand. Jones therefore had no sooner told him that he was come on his son's account than the old gentleman, being confirmed in his suspicion, burst forth into an exclamation, “That he would lose his labour.” “Is it then possible, sir,” answered Jones, “that you can guess my business?” “If I do guess it,” replied the other, “I repeat again to you, you will lose your labour. What, I suppose you are one of those sparks who lead my son into all those scenes of riot and debauchery, which will be his destruction? but I shall pay no more of his bills, I promise you. I expect he will quit all such company for the future. If I had imagined otherwise, I should not have provided a wife for him; for I would be instrumental in the ruin of nobody.” “How, sir,” said Jones, “and was this lady of your providing?” “Pray, sir,” answered the old gentleman, “how comes it to be any concern of yours?”--“Nay, dear sir,” replied Jones, “be not offended that I interest myself in what regards your son's happiness, for whom I have so great an honour and value. It was upon that very account I came to wait upon you. I can't express the satisfaction you have given me by what you say; for I do assure you your son is a person for whom I have the highest honour.--Nay, sir, it is not easy to express the esteem I have for you; who could be so generous, so good, so kind, so indulgent to provide such a match for your son; a woman, who, I dare swear, will make him one of the happiest men upon earth.”\n\nThere is scarce anything which so happily introduces men to our good liking, as having conceived some alarm at their first appearance; when once those apprehensions begin to vanish we soon forget the fears which they occasioned, and look on ourselves as indebted for our present ease to those very persons who at first raised our fears.\n\nThus it happened to Nightingale, who no sooner found that Jones had no demand on him, as he suspected, than he began to be pleased with his presence. “Pray, good sir,” said he, “be pleased to sit down. I do not remember to have ever had the pleasure of seeing you before; but if you are a friend of my son, and have anything to say concerning this young lady, I shall be glad to hear you. As to her making him happy, it will be his own fault if she doth not. I have discharged my duty, in taking care of the main article. She will bring him a fortune capable of making any reasonable, prudent, sober man, happy.” “Undoubtedly,” cries Jones, “for she is in herself a fortune; so beautiful, so genteel, so sweet-tempered, and so well-educated; she is indeed a most accomplished young lady; sings admirably well, and hath a most delicate hand at the harpsichord.” “I did not know any of these matters,” answered the old gentleman, “for I never saw the lady: but I do not like her the worse for what you tell me; and I am the better pleased with her father for not laying any stress on these qualifications in our bargain. I shall always think it a proof of his understanding. A silly fellow would have brought in these articles as an addition to her fortune; but, to give him his due, he never mentioned any such matter; though to be sure they are no disparagements to a woman.” “I do assure you, sir,” cries Jones, “she hath them all in the most eminent degree: for my part, I own I was afraid you might have been a little backward, a little less inclined to the match; for your son told me you had never seen the lady; therefore I came, sir, in that case, to entreat you, to conjure you, as you value the happiness of your son, not to be averse to his match with a woman who hath not only all the good qualities I have mentioned, but many more.”--“If that was your business, sir,” said the old gentleman, “we are both obliged to you; and you may be perfectly easy; for I give you my word I was very well satisfied with her fortune.” “Sir,” answered Jones, “I honour you every moment more and more. To be so easily satisfied, so very moderate on that account, is a proof of the soundness of your understanding, as well as the nobleness of your mind.”----“Not so very moderate, young gentleman, not so very moderate,” answered the father.--“Still more and more noble,” replied Jones; “and give me leave to add, sensible: for sure it is little less than madness to consider money as the sole foundation of happiness. Such a woman as this with her little, her nothing of a fortune”--“I find,” cries the old gentleman, “you have a pretty just opinion of money, my friend, or else you are better acquainted with the person of the lady than with her circumstances. Why, pray, what fortune do you imagine this lady to have?” “What fortune?” cries Jones, “why, too contemptible a one to be named for your son.”--“Well, well, well,” said the other, “perhaps he might have done better.”--“That I deny,” said Jones, “for she is one of the best of women.”--“Ay, ay, but in point of fortune I mean,” answered the other. “And yet, as to that now, how much do you imagine your friend is to have?”--“How much?” cries Jones, “how much? Why, at the utmost, perhaps £200.” “Do you mean to banter me, young gentleman?” said the father, a little angry. “No, upon my soul,” answered Jones, “I am in earnest: nay, I believe I have gone to the utmost farthing. If I do the lady an injury, I ask her pardon.” “Indeed you do,” cries the father; “I am certain she hath fifty times that sum, and she shall produce fifty to that before I consent that she shall marry my son.” “Nay,” said Jones, “it is too late to talk of consent now; if she had not fifty farthings your son is married.”--“My son married!” answered the old gentleman, with surprize. “Nay,” said Jones, “I thought you was unacquainted with it.” “My son married to Miss Harris!” answered he again. “To Miss Harris!” said Jones; “no, sir; to Miss Nancy Miller, the daughter of Mrs Miller, at whose house he lodged; a young lady, who, though her mother is reduced to let lodgings--“--“Are you bantering, or are you in earnest?” cries the father, with a most solemn voice. “Indeed, sir,” answered Jones, “I scorn the character of a banterer. I came to you in most serious earnest, imagining, as I find true, that your son had never dared acquaint you with a match so much inferior to him in point of fortune, though the reputation of the lady will suffer it no longer to remain a secret.”\n\nWhile the father stood like one struck suddenly dumb at this news, a gentleman came into the room, and saluted him by the name of brother.\n\nBut though these two were in consanguinity so nearly related, they were in their dispositions almost the opposites to each other. The brother who now arrived had likewise been bred to trade, in which he no sooner saw himself worth £6000 than he purchased a small estate with the greatest part of it, and retired into the country; where he married the daughter of an unbeneficed clergyman; a young lady, who, though she had neither beauty nor fortune, had recommended herself to his choice entirely by her good humour, of which she possessed a very large share.\n\nWith this woman he had, during twenty-five years, lived a life more resembling the model which certain poets ascribe to the golden age, than any of those patterns which are furnished by the present times. By her he had four children, but none of them arrived at maturity, except only one daughter, whom, in vulgar language, he and his wife had spoiled; that is, had educated with the utmost tenderness and fondness, which she returned to such a degree, that she had actually refused a very extraordinary match with a gentleman a little turned of forty, because she could not bring herself to part with her parents.\n\nThe young lady whom Mr Nightingale had intended for his son was a near neighbour of his brother, and an acquaintance of his niece; and in reality it was upon the account of his projected match that he was now come to town; not, indeed, to forward, but to dissuade his brother from a purpose which he conceived would inevitably ruin his nephew; for he foresaw no other event from a union with Miss Harris, notwithstanding the largeness of her fortune, as neither her person nor mind seemed to him to promise any kind of matrimonial felicity: for she was very tall, very thin, very ugly, very affected, very silly, and very ill-natured.\n\nHis brother, therefore, no sooner mentioned the marriage of his nephew with Miss Miller, than he exprest the utmost satisfaction; and when the father had very bitterly reviled his son, and pronounced sentence of beggary upon him, the uncle began in the following manner:\n\n“If you was a little cooler, brother, I would ask you whether you love your son for his sake or for your own. You would answer, I suppose, and so I suppose you think, for his sake; and doubtless it is his happiness which you intended in the marriage you proposed for him.\n\n“Now, brother, to prescribe rules of happiness to others hath always appeared to me very absurd, and to insist on doing this, very tyrannical. It is a vulgar error, I know; but it is, nevertheless, an error. And if this be absurd in other things, it is mostly so in the affair of marriage, the happiness of which depends entirely on the affection which subsists between the parties.\n\n“I have therefore always thought it unreasonable in parents to desire to chuse for their children on this occasion; since to force affection is an impossible attempt; nay, so much doth love abhor force, that I know not whether, through an unfortunate but uncurable perverseness in our natures, it may not be even impatient of persuasion.\n\n“It is, however, true that, though a parent will not, I think, wisely prescribe, he ought to be consulted on this occasion; and, in strictness, perhaps, should at least have a negative voice. My nephew, therefore, I own, in marrying, without asking your advice, hath been guilty of a fault. But, honestly speaking, brother, have you not a little promoted this fault? Have not your frequent declarations on this subject given him a moral certainty of your refusal, where there was any deficiency in point of fortune? Nay, doth not your present anger arise solely from that deficiency? And if he hath failed in his duty here, did you not as much exceed that authority when you absolutely bargained with him for a woman, without his knowledge, whom you yourself never saw, and whom, if you had seen and known as well as I, it must have been madness in you to have ever thought of bringing her into your family?\n\n“Still I own my nephew in a fault; but surely it is not an unpardonable fault. He hath acted indeed without your consent, in a matter in which he ought to have asked it, but it is in a matter in which his interest is principally concerned; you yourself must and will acknowledge that you consulted his interest only, and if he unfortunately differed from you, and hath been mistaken in his notion of happiness, will you, brother, if you love your son, carry him still wider from the point? Will you increase the ill consequences of his simple choice? Will you endeavour to make an event certain misery to him, which may accidentally prove so? In a word, brother, because he hath put it out of your power to make his circumstances as affluent as you would, will you distress them as much as you can?”\n\nBy the force of the true Catholic faith St Anthony won upon the fishes. Orpheus and Amphion went a little farther, and by the charms of music enchanted things merely inanimate. Wonderful, both! but neither history nor fable have ever yet ventured to record an instance of any one, who, by force of argument and reason, hath triumphed over habitual avarice.\n\nMr Nightingale, the father, instead of attempting to answer his brother, contented himself with only observing, that they had always differed in their sentiments concerning the education of their children. “I wish,” said he, “brother, you would have confined your care to your own daughter, and never have troubled yourself with my son, who hath, I believe, as little profited by your precepts, as by your example.” For young Nightingale was his uncle's godson, and had lived more with him than with his father. So that the uncle had often declared he loved his nephew almost equally with his own child.\n\nJones fell into raptures with this good gentleman; and when, after much persuasion, they found the father grew still more and more irritated, instead of appeased, Jones conducted the uncle to his nephew at the house of Mrs Miller.\n\nContaining strange matters.\n\nAt his return to his lodgings, Jones found the situation of affairs greatly altered from what they had been in at his departure. The mother, the two daughters, and young Mr Nightingale, were now sat down to supper together, when the uncle was, at his own desire, introduced without any ceremony into the company, to all of whom he was well known; for he had several times visited his nephew at that house.\n\nThe old gentleman immediately walked up to Miss Nancy, saluted and wished her joy, as he did afterwards the mother and the other sister; and lastly, he paid the proper compliments to his nephew, with the same good humour and courtesy, as if his nephew had married his equal or superior in fortune, with all the previous requisites first performed.\n\nMiss Nancy and her supposed husband both turned pale, and looked rather foolish than otherwise upon the occasion; but Mrs Miller took the first opportunity of withdrawing; and, having sent for Jones into the dining-room, she threw herself at his feet, and in a most passionate flood of tears, called him her good angel, the preserver of her poor little family, with many other respectful and endearing appellations, and made him every acknowledgment which the highest benefit can extract from the most grateful heart.\n\nAfter the first gust of her passion was a little over, which she declared, if she had not vented, would have burst her, she proceeded to inform Mr Jones that all matters were settled between Mr Nightingale and her daughter, and that they were to be married the next morning; at which Mr Jones having expressed much pleasure, the poor woman fell again into a fit of joy and thanksgiving, which he at length with difficulty silenced, and prevailed on her to return with him back to the company, whom they found in the same good humour in which they had left them.\n\nThis little society now past two or three very agreeable hours together, in which the uncle, who was a very great lover of his bottle, had so well plyed his nephew, that this latter, though not drunk, began to be somewhat flustered; and now Mr Nightingale, taking the old gentleman with him upstairs into the apartment he had lately occupied, unbosomed himself as follows:--\n\n“As you have been always the best and kindest of uncles to me, and as you have shown such unparalleled goodness in forgiving this match, which to be sure may be thought a little improvident, I should never forgive myself if I attempted to deceive you in anything.” He then confessed the truth, and opened the whole affair.\n\n“How, Jack?” said the old gentleman, “and are you really then not married to this young woman?” “No, upon my honour,” answered Nightingale, “I have told you the simple truth.” “My dear boy,” cries the uncle, kissing him, “I am heartily glad to hear it. I never was better pleased in my life. If you had been married I should have assisted you as much as was in my power to have made the best of a bad matter; but there is a great difference between considering a thing which is already done and irrecoverable, and that which is yet to do. Let your reason have fair play, Jack, and you will see this match in so foolish and preposterous a light, that there will be no need of any dissuasive arguments.” “How, sir?” replies young Nightingale, “is there this difference between having already done an act, and being in honour engaged to do it?” “Pugh!” said the uncle, “honour is a creature of the world's making, and the world hath the power of a creator over it, and may govern and direct it as they please. Now you well know how trivial these breaches of contract are thought; even the grossest make but the wonder and conversation of a day. Is there a man who afterwards will be more backward in giving you his sister, or daughter? or is there any sister or daughter who would be more backward to receive you? Honour is not concerned in these engagements.” “Pardon me, dear sir,” cries Nightingale, “I can never think so; and not only honour, but conscience and humanity, are concerned. I am well satisfied, that, was I now to disappoint the young creature, her death would be the consequence, and I should look upon myself as her murderer; nay, as her murderer by the cruellest of all methods, by breaking her heart.” “Break her heart, indeed! no, no, Jack,” cries the uncle, “the hearts of women are not so soon broke; they are tough, boy, they are tough.” “But, sir,” answered Nightingale, “my own affections are engaged, and I never could be happy with any other woman. How often have I heard you say, that children should be always suffered to chuse for themselves, and that you would let my cousin Harriet do so?” “Why, ay,” replied the old gentleman, “so I would have them; but then I would have them chuse wisely.--Indeed, Jack, you must and shall leave the girl.”----“Indeed, uncle,” cries the other, “I must and will have her.” “You will, young gentleman;” said the uncle; “I did not expect such a word from you. I should not wonder if you had used such language to your father, who hath always treated you like a dog, and kept you at the distance which a tyrant preserves over his subjects; but I, who have lived with you upon an equal footing, might surely expect better usage: but I know how to account for it all: it is all owing to your preposterous education, in which I have had too little share. There is my daughter, now, whom I have brought up as my friend, never doth anything without my advice, nor ever refuses to take it when I give it her.” “You have never yet given her advice in an affair of this kind,” said Nightingale; “for I am greatly mistaken in my cousin, if she would be very ready to obey even your most positive commands in abandoning her inclinations.” “Don't abuse my girl,” answered the old gentleman with some emotion; “don't abuse my Harriet. I have brought her up to have no inclinations contrary to my own. By suffering her to do whatever she pleases, I have enured her to a habit of being pleased to do whatever I like.” “Pardon, me, sir,” said Nightingale, “I have not the least design to reflect on my cousin, for whom I have the greatest esteem; and indeed I am convinced you will never put her to so severe a tryal, or lay such hard commands on her as you would do on me.--But, dear sir, let us return to the company; for they will begin to be uneasy at our long absence. I must beg one favour of my dear uncle, which is that he would not say anything to shock the poor girl or her mother.” “Oh! you need not fear me,” answered he, “I understand myself too well to affront women; so I will readily grant you that favour; and in return I must expect another of you.” “There are but few of your commands, sir,” said Nightingale, “which I shall not very chearfully obey.” “Nay, sir, I ask nothing,” said the uncle, “but the honour of your company home to my lodging, that I may reason the case a little more fully with you; for I would, if possible, have the satisfaction of preserving my family, notwithstanding the headstrong folly of my brother, who, in his own opinion, is the wisest man in the world.”\n\nNightingale, who well knew his uncle to be as headstrong as his father, submitted to attend him home, and then they both returned back into the room, where the old gentleman promised to carry himself with the same decorum which he had before maintained.\n\nA short chapter, which concludes the book.\n\nThe long absence of the uncle and nephew had occasioned some disquiet in the minds of all whom they had left behind them; and the more, as, during the preceding dialogue, the uncle had more than once elevated his voice, so as to be heard downstairs; which, though they could not distinguish what he said, had caused some evil foreboding in Nancy and her mother, and, indeed, even in Jones himself.\n\nWhen the good company, therefore, again assembled, there was a visible alteration in all their faces; and the good-humour which, at their last meeting, universally shone forth in every countenance, was now changed into a much less agreeable aspect. It was a change, indeed, common enough to the weather in this climate, from sunshine to clouds, from June to December.\n\nThis alteration was not, however, greatly remarked by any present; for as they were all now endeavouring to conceal their own thoughts, and to act a part, they became all too busily engaged in the scene to be spectators of it. Thus neither the uncle nor nephew saw any symptoms of suspicion in the mother or daughter; nor did the mother or daughter remark the overacted complacence of the old man, nor the counterfeit satisfaction which grinned in the features of the young one.\n\nSomething like this, I believe, frequently happens, where the whole attention of two friends being engaged in the part which each is to act, in order to impose on the other, neither sees nor suspects the arts practised against himself; and thus the thrust of both (to borrow no improper metaphor on the occasion) alike takes place.\n\nFrom the same reason it is no unusual thing for both parties to be overreached in a bargain, though the one must be always the greater loser; as was he who sold a blind horse, and received a bad note in payment.\n\nOur company in about half an hour broke up, and the uncle carried off his nephew; but not before the latter had assured Miss Nancy, in a whisper, that he would attend her early in the morning, and fulfil all his engagements.\n\nJones, who was the least concerned in this scene, saw the most. He did indeed suspect the very fact; for, besides observing the great alteration in the behaviour of the uncle, the distance he assumed, and his overstrained civility to Miss Nancy; the carrying off a bridegroom from his bride at that time of night was so extraordinary a proceeding that it could be accounted for only by imagining that young Nightingale had revealed the whole truth, which the apparent openness of his temper, and his being flustered with liquor, made too probable.\n\nWhile he was reasoning with himself, whether he should acquaint these poor people with his suspicion, the maid of the house informed him that a gentlewoman desired to speak with him.----He went immediately out, and, taking the candle from the maid, ushered his visitant upstairs, who, in the person of Mrs Honour, acquainted him with such dreadful news concerning his Sophia, that he immediately lost all consideration for every other person; and his whole stock of compassion was entirely swallowed up in reflections on his own misery, and on that of his unfortunate angel.\n\nWhat this dreadful matter was, the reader will be informed, after we have first related the many preceding steps which produced it, and those will be the subject of the following book.\n\nToo short to need a preface.\n\nThere are a set of religious, or rather moral writers, who teach that virtue is the certain road to happiness, and vice to misery, in this world. A very wholesome and comfortable doctrine, and to which we have but one objection, namely, that it is not true.\n\nIndeed, if by virtue these writers mean the exercise of those cardinal virtues, which like good housewives stay at home, and mind only the business of their own family, I shall very readily concede the point; for so surely do all these contribute and lead to happiness, that I could almost wish, in violation of all the antient and modern sages, to call them rather by the name of wisdom, than by that of virtue; for, with regard to this life, no system, I conceive, was ever wiser than that of the antient Epicureans, who held this wisdom to constitute the chief good; nor foolisher than that of their opposites, those modern epicures, who place all felicity in the abundant gratification of every sensual appetite.\n\nBut if by virtue is meant (as I almost think it ought) a certain relative quality, which is always busying itself without-doors, and seems as much interested in pursuing the good of others as its own; I cannot so easily agree that this is the surest way to human happiness; because I am afraid we must then include poverty and contempt, with all the mischiefs which backbiting, envy, and ingratitude, can bring on mankind, in our idea of happiness; nay, sometimes perhaps we shall be obliged to wait upon the said happiness to a jail; since many by the above virtue have brought themselves thither.\n\nI have not now leisure to enter upon so large a field of speculation, as here seems opening upon me; my design was to wipe off a doctrine that lay in my way; since, while Mr Jones was acting the most virtuous part imaginable in labouring to preserve his fellow-creatures from destruction, the devil, or some other evil spirit, one perhaps cloathed in human flesh, was hard at work to make him completely miserable in the ruin of his Sophia.\n\nThis therefore would seem an exception to the above rule, if indeed it was a rule; but as we have in our voyage through life seen so many other exceptions to it, we chuse to dispute the doctrine on which it is founded, which we don't apprehend to be Christian, which we are convinced is not true, and which is indeed destructive of one of the noblest arguments that reason alone can furnish for the belief of immortality.\n\nBut as the reader's curiosity (if he hath any) must be now awake, and hungry, we shall provide to feed it as fast as we can.\n\nIn which is opened a very black design against Sophia.\n\nI remember a wise old gentleman who used to say, “When children are doing nothing, they are doing mischief.” I will not enlarge this quaint saying to the most beautiful part of the creation in general; but so far I may be allowed, that when the effects of female jealousy do not appear openly in their proper colours of rage and fury, we may suspect that mischievous passion to be at work privately, and attempting to undermine, what it doth not attack above-ground.\n\nThis was exemplified in the conduct of Lady Bellaston, who, under all the smiles which she wore in her countenance, concealed much indignation against Sophia; and as she plainly saw that this young lady stood between her and the full indulgence of her desires, she resolved to get rid of her by some means or other; nor was it long before a very favourable opportunity of accomplishing this presented itself to her.\n\nThe reader may be pleased to remember, that when Sophia was thrown into that consternation at the playhouse, by the wit and humour of a set of young gentlemen who call themselves the town, we informed him, that she had put herself under the protection of a young nobleman, who had very safely conducted her to her chair.\n\nThis nobleman, who frequently visited Lady Bellaston, had more than once seen Sophia there, since her arrival in town, and had conceived a very great liking to her; which liking, as beauty never looks more amiable than in distress, Sophia had in this fright so encreased, that he might now, without any great impropriety, be said to be actually in love with her.\n\nIt may easily be believed, that he would not suffer so handsome an occasion of improving his acquaintance with the beloved object as now offered itself to elapse, when even good breeding alone might have prompted him to pay her a visit.\n\nThe next morning therefore, after this accident, he waited on Sophia, with the usual compliments, and hopes that she had received no harm from her last night's adventure.\n\nAs love, like fire, when once thoroughly kindled, is soon blown into a flame, Sophia in a very short time compleated her conquest. Time now flew away unperceived, and the noble lord had been two hours in company with the lady, before it entered into his head that he had made too long a visit. Though this circumstance alone would have alarmed Sophia, who was somewhat more a mistress of computation at present; she had indeed much more pregnant evidence from the eyes of her lover of what past within his bosom; nay, though he did not make any open declaration of his passion, yet many of his expressions were rather too warm, and too tender, to have been imputed to complacence, even in the age when such complacence was in fashion; the very reverse of which is well known to be the reigning mode at present.\n\nLady Bellaston had been apprized of his lordship's visit at his first arrival; and the length of it very well satisfied her, that things went as she wished, and as indeed she had suspected the second time she saw this young couple together. This business, she rightly I think concluded, that she should by no means forward by mixing in the company while they were together; she therefore ordered her servants, that when my lord was going, they should tell him she desired to speak with him; and employed the intermediate time in meditating how best to accomplish a scheme, which she made no doubt but his lordship would very readily embrace the execution of.\n\nLord Fellamar (for that was the title of this young nobleman) was no sooner introduced to her ladyship, than she attacked him in the following strain: “Bless me, my lord, are you here yet? I thought my servants had made a mistake, and let you go away; and I wanted to see you about an affair of some importance.”----“Indeed, Lady Bellaston,” said he, “I don't wonder you are astonished at the length of my visit; for I have staid above two hours, and I did not think I had staid above half-a-one.”----“What am I to conclude from thence, my lord?” said she. “The company must be very agreeable which can make time slide away so very deceitfully.”----“Upon my honour,” said he, “the most agreeable I ever saw. Pray tell me, Lady Bellaston, who is this blazing star which you have produced among us all of a sudden?”----“What blazing star, my lord?” said she, affecting a surprize. “I mean,” said he, “the lady I saw here the other day, whom I had last night in my arms at the playhouse, and to whom I have been making that unreasonable visit.”----“O, my cousin Western!” said she; “why, that blazing star, my lord, is the daughter of a country booby squire, and hath been in town about a fortnight, for the first time.”----“Upon my soul,” said he, “I should swear she had been bred up in a court; for besides her beauty, I never saw anything so genteel, so sensible, so polite.”----“O brave!” cries the lady, “my cousin hath you, I find.”----“Upon my honour,” answered he, “I wish she had; for I am in love with her to distraction.”----“Nay, my lord,” said she, “it is not wishing yourself very ill neither, for she is a very great fortune: I assure you she is an only child, and her father's estate is a good £3000 a-year.” “Then I can assure you, madam,” answered the lord, “I think her the best match in England.” “Indeed, my lord,” replied she, “if you like her, I heartily wish you had her.” “If you think so kindly of me, madam,” said he, “as she is a relation of yours, will you do me the honour to propose it to her father?” “And are you really then in earnest?” cries the lady, with an affected gravity. “I hope, madam,” answered he, “you have a better opinion of me, than to imagine I would jest with your ladyship in an affair of this kind.” “Indeed, then,” said the lady, “I will most readily propose your lordship to her father; and I can, I believe, assure you of his joyful acceptance of the proposal; but there is a bar, which I am almost ashamed to mention; and yet it is one you will never be able to conquer. You have a rival, my lord, and a rival who, though I blush to name him, neither you, nor all the world, will ever be able to conquer.” “Upon my word, Lady Bellaston,” cries he, “you have struck a damp to my heart, which hath almost deprived me of being.” “Fie, my lord,” said she, “I should rather hope I had struck fire into you. A lover, and talk of damps in your heart! I rather imagined you would have asked your rival's name, that you might have immediately entered the lists with him.” “I promise you, madam,” answered he, “there are very few things I would not undertake for your charming cousin; but pray, who is this happy man?”--“Why, he is,” said she, “what I am sorry to say most happy men with us are, one of the lowest fellows in the world. He is a beggar, a bastard, a foundling, a fellow in meaner circumstances than one of your lordship's footmen.” “And is it possible,” cried he, “that a young creature with such perfections should think of bestowing herself so unworthily?” “Alas! my lord,” answered she, “consider the country--the bane of all young women is the country. There they learn a set of romantic notions of love, and I know not what folly, which this town and good company can scarce eradicate in a whole winter.” “Indeed, madam,” replied my lord, “your cousin is of too immense a value to be thrown away; such ruin as this must be prevented.” “Alas!” cries she, “my lord, how can it be prevented? The family have already done all in their power; but the girl is, I think, intoxicated, and nothing less than ruin will content her. And to deal more openly with you, I expect every day to hear she is run away with him.” “What you tell me, Lady Bellaston,” answered his lordship, “affects me most tenderly, and only raises my compassion, instead of lessening my adoration of your cousin. Some means must be found to preserve so inestimable a jewel. Hath your ladyship endeavoured to reason with her?” Here the lady affected a laugh, and cried, “My dear lord, sure you know us better than to talk of reasoning a young woman out of her inclinations? These inestimable jewels are as deaf as the jewels they wear: time, my lord, time is the only medicine to cure their folly; but this is a medicine which I am certain she will not take; nay, I live in hourly horrors on her account. In short, nothing but violent methods will do.” “What is to be done?” cries my lord; “what methods are to be taken?--Is there any method upon earth?--Oh! Lady Bellaston! there is nothing which I would not undertake for such a reward.”----“I really know not,” answered the lady, after a pause; and then pausing again, she cried out--“Upon my soul, I am at my wit's end on this girl's account.--If she can be preserved, something must be done immediately; and, as I say, nothing but violent methods will do.----If your lordship hath really this attachment to my cousin (and to do her justice, except in this silly inclination, of which she will soon see her folly, she is every way deserving), I think there may be one way, indeed, it is a very disagreeable one, and what I am almost afraid to think of.--It requires a great spirit, I promise you.” “I am not conscious, madam,” said he, “of any defect there; nor am I, I hope, suspected of any such. It must be an egregious defect indeed, which could make me backward on this occasion.” “Nay, my lord,” answered she, “I am so far from doubting you, I am much more inclined to doubt my own courage; for I must run a monstrous risque. In short, I must place such a confidence in your honour as a wise woman will scarce ever place in a man on any consideration.” In this point likewise my lord very well satisfied her; for his reputation was extremely clear, and common fame did him no more than justice, in speaking well of him. “Well, then,” said she, “my lord,--I--I vow, I can't bear the apprehension of it.--No, it must not be.----At least every other method shall be tried. Can you get rid of your engagements, and dine here to-day? Your lordship will have an opportunity of seeing a little more of Miss Western.--I promise you we have no time to lose. Here will be nobody but Lady Betty, and Miss Eagle, and Colonel Hampsted, and Tom Edwards; they will all go soon--and I shall be at home to nobody. Then your lordship may be a little more explicit. Nay, I will contrive some method to convince you of her attachment to this fellow.” My lord made proper compliments, accepted the invitation, and then they parted to dress, it being now past three in the morning, or to reckon by the old style, in the afternoon.\n\nA further explanation of the foregoing design.\nThough the reader may have long since concluded Lady Bellaston to be a member (and no inconsiderable one) of the great world; she was in reality a very considerable member of the little world; by which appellation was distinguished a very worthy and honourable society which not long since flourished in this kingdom.\n\nAmong other good principles upon which this society was founded, there was one very remarkable; for, as it was a rule of an honourable club of heroes, who assembled at the close of the late war, that all the members should every day fight once at least; so 'twas in this, that every member should, within the twenty-four hours, tell at least one merry fib, which was to be propagated by all the brethren and sisterhood.\n\nMany idle stories were told about this society, which from a certain quality may be, perhaps not unjustly, supposed to have come from the society themselves. As, that the devil was the president; and that he sat in person in an elbow-chair at the upper end of the table; but, upon very strict enquiry, I find there is not the least truth in any of those tales, and that the assembly consisted in reality of a set of very good sort of people, and the fibs which they propagated were of a harmless kind, and tended only to produce mirth and good humour.\n\nEdwards was likewise a member of this comical society. To him therefore Lady Bellaston applied as a proper instrument for her purpose, and furnished him with a fib, which he was to vent whenever the lady gave him her cue; and this was not to be till the evening, when all the company but Lord Fellamar and himself were gone, and while they were engaged in a rubber at whist.\n\nTo this time then, which was between seven and eight in the evening, we will convey our reader; when Lady Bellaston, Lord Fellamar, Miss Western, and Tom, being engaged at whist, and in the last game of their rubbers, Tom received his cue from Lady Bellaston, which was, “I protest, Tom, you are grown intolerable lately; you used to tell us all the news of the town, and now you know no more of the world than if you lived out of it.”\n\nMr Edwards then began as follows: “The fault is not mine, madam: it lies in the dulness of the age, that doth nothing worth talking of.----O la! though now I think on't there hath a terrible accident befallen poor Colonel Wilcox.----Poor Ned.----You know him, my lord, everybody knows him; faith! I am very much concerned for him.”\n\n“What is it, pray?” says Lady Bellaston.\n\n“Why, he hath killed a man this morning in a duel, that's all.”\n\nHis lordship, who was not in the secret, asked gravely, whom he had killed? To which Edwards answered, “A young fellow we none of us know; a Somersetshire lad just came to town, one Jones his name is; a near relation of one Mr Allworthy, of whom your lordship I believe hath heard. I saw the lad lie dead in a coffee-house.--Upon my soul, he is one of the finest corpses I ever saw in my life!”\n\nSophia, who had just began to deal as Tom had mentioned that a man was killed, stopt her hand, and listened with attention (for all stories of that kind affected her), but no sooner had he arrived at the latter part of the story than she began to deal again; and having dealt three cards to one, and seven to another, and ten to a third, at last dropt the rest from her hand, and fell back in her chair.\n\nThe company behaved as usually on these occasions. The usual disturbance ensued, the usual assistance was summoned, and Sophia at last, as it is usual, returned again to life, and was soon after, at her earnest desire, led to her own apartment; where, at my lord's request, Lady Bellaston acquainted her with the truth, attempted to carry it off as a jest of her own, and comforted her with repeated assurances, that neither his lordship nor Tom, though she had taught him the story, were in the true secret of the affair.\n\nThere was no farther evidence necessary to convince Lord Fellamar how justly the case had been represented to him by Lady Bellaston; and now, at her return into the room, a scheme was laid between these two noble persons, which, though it appeared in no very heinous light to his lordship (as he faithfully promised, and faithfully resolved too, to make the lady all the subsequent amends in his power by marriage), yet many of our readers, we doubt not, will see with just detestation.\n\nThe next evening at seven was appointed for the fatal purpose, when Lady Bellaston undertook that Sophia should be alone, and his lordship should be introduced to her. The whole family were to be regulated for the purpose, most of the servants despatched out of the house; and for Mrs Honour, who, to prevent suspicion, was to be left with her mistress till his lordship's arrival, Lady Bellaston herself was to engage her in an apartment as distant as possible from the scene of the intended mischief, and out of the hearing of Sophia.\n\nMatters being thus agreed on, his lordship took his leave, and her ladyship retired to rest, highly pleased with a project, of which she had no reason to doubt the success, and which promised so effectually to remove Sophia from being any further obstruction to her amour with Jones, by a means of which she should never appear to be guilty, even if the fact appeared to the world; but this she made no doubt of preventing by huddling up a marriage, to which she thought the ravished Sophia would easily be brought to consent, and at which all the rest of her family would rejoice.\n\nBut affairs were not in so quiet a situation in the bosom of the other conspirator; his mind was tost in all the distracting anxiety so nobly described by Shakespear--\n\n“Between the acting of a dreadful thing, And the first motion, all the interim is Like a phantasma, or a hideous dream; The genius and the mortal instruments Are then in council; and the state of man, Like to a little kingdom, suffers then The nature of an insurrection.”----\n\nThough the violence of his passion had made him eagerly embrace the first hint of this design, especially as it came from a relation of the lady, yet when that friend to reflection, a pillow, had placed the action itself in all its natural black colours before his eyes, with all the consequences which must, and those which might probably attend it, his resolution began to abate, or rather indeed to go over to the other side; and after a long conflict, which lasted a whole night, between honour and appetite, the former at length prevailed, and he determined to wait on Lady Bellaston, and to relinquish the design.\n\nLady Bellaston was in bed, though very late in the morning, and Sophia sitting by her bed-side, when the servant acquainted her that Lord Fellamar was below in the parlour; upon which her ladyship desired him to stay, and that she would see him presently; but the servant was no sooner departed than poor Sophia began to intreat her cousin not to encourage the visits of that odious lord (so she called him, though a little unjustly) upon her account. “I see his design,” said she; “for he made downright love to me yesterday morning; but as I am resolved never to admit it, I beg your ladyship not to leave us alone together any more, and to order the servants that, if he enquires for me, I may be always denied to him.”\n\n“La! child,” says Lady Bellaston, “you country girls have nothing but sweethearts in your head; you fancy every man who is civil to you is making love. He is one of the most gallant young fellows about town, and I am convinced means no more than a little gallantry. Make love to you indeed! I wish with all my heart he would, and you must be an arrant mad woman to refuse him.”\n\n“But as I shall certainly be that mad woman,” cries Sophia, “I hope his visits shall not be intruded upon me.”\n\n“O child!” said Lady Bellaston, “you need not be so fearful; if you resolve to run away with that Jones, I know no person who can hinder you.”\n\n“Upon my honour, madam,” cries Sophia, “your ladyship injures me. I will never run away with any man; nor will I ever marry contrary to my father's inclinations.”\n\n“Well, Miss Western,” said the lady, “if you are not in a humour to see company this morning, you may retire to your own apartment; for I am not frightened at his lordship, and must send for him up into my dressing-room.”\n\nSophia thanked her ladyship, and withdrew; and presently afterwards Fellamar was admitted upstairs.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\nBy which it will appear how dangerous an advocate a lady is when she applies her eloquence to an ill purpose.\n\nWhen Lady Bellaston heard the young lord's scruples, she treated them with the same disdain with which one of those sages of the law, called Newgate solicitors, treats the qualms of conscience in a young witness. “My dear lord,” said she, “you certainly want a cordial. I must send to Lady Edgely for one of her best drams. Fie upon it! have more resolution. Are you frightened by the word rape? Or are you apprehensive----? Well! if the story of Helen was modern, I should think it unnatural. I mean the behaviour of Paris, not the fondness of the lady; for all women love a man of spirit. There is another story of the Sabine ladies--and that too, I thank heaven, is very antient. Your lordship, perhaps, will admire my reading; but I think Mr Hook tells us, they made tolerable good wives afterwards. I fancy few of my married acquaintance were ravished by their husbands.” “Nay, dear Lady Bellaston,” cried he, “don't ridicule me in this manner.” “Why, my good lord,” answered she, “do you think any woman in England would not laugh at you in her heart, whatever prudery she might wear in her countenance?----You force me to use a strange kind of language, and to betray my sex most abominably; but I am contented with knowing my intentions are good, and that I am endeavouring to serve my cousin; for I think you will make her a husband notwithstanding this; or, upon my soul, I would not even persuade her to fling herself away upon an empty title. She should not upbraid me hereafter with having lost a man of spirit; for that his enemies allow this poor young fellow to be.”\n\nLet those who have had the satisfaction of hearing reflections of this kind from a wife or a mistress, declare whether they are at all sweetened by coming from a female tongue. Certain it is, they sunk deeper into his lordship than anything which Demosthenes or Cicero could have said on the occasion.\n\nLady Bellaston, perceiving she had fired the young lord's pride, began now, like a true orator, to rouse other passions to its assistance. “My lord,” says she, in a graver voice, “you will be pleased to remember, you mentioned this matter to me first; for I would not appear to you in the light of one who is endeavouring to put off my cousin upon you. Fourscore thousand pounds do not stand in need of an advocate to recommend them.” “Nor doth Miss Western,” said he, “require any recommendation from her fortune; for, in my opinion, no woman ever had half her charms.” “Yes, yes, my lord,” replied the lady, looking in the glass, “there have been women with more than half her charms, I assure you; not that I need lessen her on that account: she is a most delicious girl, that's certain; and within these few hours she will be in the arms of one, who surely doth not deserve her, though I will give him his due, I believe he is truly a man of spirit.”\n\n“I hope so, madam,” said my lord; “though I must own he doth not deserve her; for, unless heaven or your ladyship disappoint me, she shall within that time be in mine.”\n\n“Well spoken, my lord,” answered the lady; “I promise you no disappointment shall happen from my side; and within this week I am convinced I shall call your lordship my cousin in public.”\n\nThe remainder of this scene consisted entirely of raptures, excuses, and compliments, very pleasant to have heard from the parties; but rather dull when related at second hand. Here, therefore, we shall put an end to this dialogue, and hasten to the fatal hour when everything was prepared for the destruction of poor Sophia.\n\nBut this being the most tragical matter in our whole history, we shall treat it in a chapter by itself.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nContaining some matters which may affect, and others which may surprize, the reader.\n\nThe clock had now struck seven, and poor Sophia, alone and melancholy, sat reading a tragedy. It was the Fatal Marriage; and she was now come to that part where the poor distrest Isabella disposes of her wedding-ring.\n\nHere the book dropt from her hand, and a shower of tears ran down into her bosom. In this situation she had continued a minute, when the door opened, and in came Lord Fellamar. Sophia started from her chair at his entrance; and his lordship advancing forwards, and making a low bow, said, “I am afraid, Miss Western, I break in upon you abruptly.” “Indeed, my lord,” says she, “I must own myself a little surprized at this unexpected visit.” “If this visit be unexpected, madam,” answered Lord Fellamar, “my eyes must have been very faithless interpreters of my heart, when last I had the honour of seeing you; for surely you could not otherwise have hoped to detain my heart in your possession, without receiving a visit from its owner.” Sophia, confused as she was, answered this bombast (and very properly I think) with a look of inconceivable disdain. My lord then made another and a longer speech of the same sort. Upon which Sophia, trembling, said, “Am I really to conceive your lordship to be out of your senses? Sure, my lord, there is no other excuse for such behaviour.” “I am, indeed, madam, in the situation you suppose,” cries his lordship; “and sure you will pardon the effects of a frenzy which you yourself have occasioned; for love hath so totally deprived me of reason, that I am scarce accountable for any of my actions.” “Upon my word, my lord,” said Sophia, “I neither understand your words nor your behaviour.” “Suffer me then, madam,” cries he, “at your feet to explain both, by laying open my soul to you, and declaring that I doat on you to the highest degree of distraction. O most adorable, most divine creature! what language can express the sentiments of my heart?” “I do assure you, my lord,” said Sophia, “I shall not stay to hear any more of this.” “Do not,” cries he, “think of leaving me thus cruelly; could you know half the torments which I feel, that tender bosom must pity what those eyes have caused.” Then fetching a deep sigh, and laying hold of her hand, he ran on for some minutes in a strain which would be little more pleasing to the reader than it was to the lady; and at last concluded with a declaration, “That if he was master of the world, he would lay it at her feet.” Sophia then, forcibly pulling away her hand from his, answered with much spirit, “I promise you, sir, your world and its master I should spurn from me with equal contempt.” She then offered to go; and Lord Fellamar, again laying hold of her hand, said, “Pardon me, my beloved angel, freedoms which nothing but despair could have tempted me to take.----Believe me, could I have had any hope that my title and fortune, neither of them inconsiderable, unless when compared with your worth, would have been accepted, I had, in the humblest manner, presented them to your acceptance.----But I cannot lose you.--By heaven, I will sooner part with my soul!--You are, you must, you shall be only mine.” “My lord,” says she, “I intreat you to desist from a vain pursuit; for, upon my honour, I will never hear you on this subject. Let go my hand, my lord; for I am resolved to go from you this moment; nor will I ever see you more.” “Then, madam,” cries his lordship, “I must make the best use of this moment; for I cannot live, nor will I live without you.”----“What do you mean, my lord?” said Sophia; “I will raise the family.” “I have no fear, madam,” answered he, “but of losing you, and that I am resolved to prevent, the only way which despair points to me.”--He then caught her in his arms: upon which she screamed so loud, that she must have alarmed some one to her assistance, had not Lady Bellaston taken care to remove all ears.\n\nBut a more lucky circumstance happened for poor Sophia; another noise now broke forth, which almost drowned her cries; for now the whole house rang with, “Where is she? D--n me, I'll unkennel her this instant. Show me her chamber, I say. Where is my daughter? I know she's in the house, and I'll see her if she's above-ground. Show me where she is.”--At which last words the door flew open, and in came Squire Western, with his parson and a set of myrmidons at his heels.\n\nHow miserable must have been the condition of poor Sophia, when the enraged voice of her father was welcome to her ears! Welcome indeed it was, and luckily did he come; for it was the only accident upon earth which could have preserved the peace of her mind from being for ever destroyed.\n\nSophia, notwithstanding her fright, presently knew her father's voice; and his lordship, notwithstanding his passion, knew the voice of reason, which peremptorily assured him, it was not now a time for the perpetration of his villany. Hearing, therefore, the voice approach, and hearing likewise whose it was (for as the squire more than once roared forth the word daughter, so Sophia, in the midst of her struggling, cried out upon her father), he thought proper to relinquish his prey, having only disordered her handkerchief, and with his rude lips committed violence on her lovely neck.\n\nIf the reader's imagination doth not assist me, I shall never be able to describe the situation of these two persons when Western came into the room. Sophia tottered into a chair, where she sat disordered, pale, breathless, bursting with indignation at Lord Fellamar; affrighted, and yet more rejoiced, at the arrival of her father.\n\nHis lordship sat down near her, with the bag of his wig hanging over one of his shoulders, the rest of his dress being somewhat disordered, and rather a greater proportion of linen than is usual appearing at his bosom. As to the rest, he was amazed, affrighted, vexed, and ashamed.\n\nAs to Squire Western, he happened at this time to be overtaken by an enemy, which very frequently pursues, and seldom fails to overtake, most of the country gentlemen in this kingdom. He was, literally speaking, drunk; which circumstance, together with his natural impetuosity, could produce no other effect than his running immediately up to his daughter, upon whom he fell foul with his tongue in the most inveterate manner; nay, he had probably committed violence with his hands, had not the parson interposed, saying, “For heaven's sake, sir, animadvert that you are in the house of a great lady. Let me beg you to mitigate your wrath; it should minister a fulness of satisfaction that you have found your daughter; for as to revenge, it belongeth not unto us. I discern great contrition in the countenance of the young lady. I stand assured, if you will forgive her, she will repent her of all past offences, and return unto her duty.”\n\nThe strength of the parson's arms had at first been of more service than the strength of his rhetoric. However, his last words wrought some effect, and the squire answered, “I'll forgee her if she wull ha un. If wot ha un, Sophy, I'll forgee thee all. Why dost unt speak? Shat ha un! d--n me, shat ha un! Why dost unt answer? Was ever such a stubborn tuoad?”\n\n“Let me intreat you, sir, to be a little more moderate,” said the parson; “you frighten the young lady so, that you deprive her of all power of utterance.”\n\n“Power of mine a--,” answered the squire. “You take her part then, you do? A pretty parson, truly, to side with an undutiful child! Yes, yes, I will gee you a living with a pox. I'll gee un to the devil sooner.”\n\n“I humbly crave your pardon,” said the parson; “I assure your worship I meant no such matter.”\n\nMy Lady Bellaston now entered the room, and came up to the squire, who no sooner saw her, than, resolving to follow the instructions of his sister, he made her a very civil bow, in the rural manner, and paid her some of his best compliments. He then immediately proceeded to his complaints, and said, “There, my lady cousin; there stands the most undutiful child in the world; she hankers after a beggarly rascal, and won't marry one of the greatest matches in all England, that we have provided for her.”\n\n“Indeed, cousin Western,” answered the lady, “I am persuaded you wrong my cousin. I am sure she hath a better understanding. I am convinced she will not refuse what she must be sensible is so much to her advantage.”\n\nThis was a wilful mistake in Lady Bellaston, for she well knew whom Mr Western meant; though perhaps she thought he would easily be reconciled to his lordship's proposals.\n\n“Do you hear there,” quoth the squire, “what her ladyship says? All your family are for the match. Come, Sophy, be a good girl, and be dutiful, and make your father happy.”\n\n“If my death will make you happy, sir,” answered Sophia, “you will shortly be so.”\n\n“It's a lye, Sophy; it's a d--n'd lye, and you know it,” said the squire.\n\n“Indeed, Miss Western,” said Lady Bellaston, “you injure your father; he hath nothing in view but your interest in this match; and I and all your friends must acknowledge the highest honour done to your family in the proposal.”\n\n“Ay, all of us,” quoth the squire; “nay, it was no proposal of mine. She knows it was her aunt proposed it to me first.--Come, Sophy, once more let me beg you to be a good girl, and gee me your consent before your cousin.”\n\n“Let me give him your hand, cousin,” said the lady. “It is the fashion now-a-days to dispense with time and long courtships.”\n\n“Pugh!” said the squire, “what signifies time; won't they have time enough to court afterwards? People may court very well after they have been a-bed together.”\n\nAs Lord Fellamar was very well assured that he was meant by Lady Bellaston, so, never having heard nor suspected a word of Blifil, he made no doubt of his being meant by the father. Coming up, therefore, to the squire, he said, “Though I have not the honour, sir, of being personally known to you, yet, as I find I have the happiness to have my proposals accepted, let me intercede, sir, in behalf of the young lady, that she may not be more solicited at this time.”\n\n“You intercede, sir!” said the squire; “why, who the devil are you?”\n\n“Sir, I am Lord Fellamar,” answered he, “and am the happy man whom I hope you have done the honour of accepting for a son-in-law.”\n\n“You are a son of a b----,” replied the squire, “for all your laced coat. You my son-in-law, and be d--n'd to you!”\n\n“I shall take more from you, sir, than from any man,” answered the lord; “but I must inform you that I am not used to hear such language without resentment.”\n\n“Resent my a--,” quoth the squire. “Don't think I am afraid of such a fellow as thee art! because hast got a spit there dangling at thy side. Lay by your spit, and I'll give thee enough of meddling with what doth not belong to thee. I'll teach you to father-in-law me. I'll lick thy jacket.”\n\n“It's very well, sir,” said my lord, “I shall make no disturbance before the ladies. I am very well satisfied. Your humble servant, sir; Lady Bellaston, your most obedient.”\n\nHis lordship was no sooner gone, than Lady Bellaston, coming up to Mr Western, said, “Bless me, sir, what have you done? You know not whom you have affronted; he is a nobleman of the first rank and fortune, and yesterday made proposals to your daughter; and such as I am sure you must accept with the highest pleasure.”\n\n“Answer for yourself, lady cousin,” said the squire, “I will have nothing to do with any of your lords. My daughter shall have an honest country gentleman; I have pitched upon one for her--and she shall ha' un.--I am sorry for the trouble she hath given your ladyship with all my heart.” Lady Bellaston made a civil speech upon the word trouble; to which the squire answered--“Why, that's kind--and I would do as much for your ladyship. To be sure relations should do for one another. So I wish your ladyship a good night.--Come, madam, you must go along with me by fair means, or I'll have you carried down to the coach.”\n\nSophia said she would attend him without force; but begged to go in a chair, for she said she should not be able to ride any other way.\n\n“Prithee,” cries the squire, “wout unt persuade me canst not ride in a coach, wouldst? That's a pretty thing surely! No, no, I'll never let thee out of my sight any more till art married, that I promise thee.” Sophia told him, she saw he was resolved to break her heart. “O break thy heart and be d--n'd,” quoth he, “if a good husband will break it. I don't value a brass varden, not a halfpenny, of any undutiful b-- upon earth.” He then took violent hold of her hand; upon which the parson once more interfered, begging him to use gentle methods. At that the squire thundered out a curse, and bid the parson hold his tongue, saying, “At'nt in pulpit now? when art a got up there I never mind what dost say; but I won't be priest-ridden, nor taught how to behave myself by thee. I wish your ladyship a good-night. Come along, Sophy; be a good girl, and all shall be well. Shat ha' un, d--n me, shat ha' un!”\n\nMrs Honour appeared below-stairs, and with a low curtesy to the squire offered to attend her mistress; but he pushed her away, saying, “Hold, madam, hold, you come no more near my house.” “And will you take my maid away from me?” said Sophia. “Yes, indeed, madam, will I,” cries the squire: “you need not fear being without a servant; I will get you another maid, and a better maid than this, who, I'd lay five pounds to a crown, is no more a maid than my grannum. No, no, Sophy, she shall contrive no more escapes, I promise you.” He then packed up his daughter and the parson into the hackney coach, after which he mounted himself, and ordered it to drive to his lodgings. In the way thither he suffered Sophia to be quiet, and entertained himself with reading a lecture to the parson on good manners, and a proper behaviour to his betters.\n\nIt is possible he might not so easily have carried off his daughter from Lady Bellaston, had that good lady desired to have detained her; but, in reality, she was not a little pleased with the confinement into which Sophia was going; and as her project with Lord Fellamar had failed of success, she was well contented that other violent methods were now going to be used in favour of another man.\n\nBy what means the squire came to discover his daughter.\n\nThough the reader, in many histories, is obliged to digest much more unaccountable appearances than this of Mr Western, without any satisfaction at all; yet, as we dearly love to oblige him whenever it is in our power, we shall now proceed to shew by what method the squire discovered where his daughter was.\n\nIn the third chapter, then, of the preceding book, we gave a hint (for it is not our custom to unfold at any time more than is necessary for the occasion) that Mrs Fitzpatrick, who was very desirous of reconciling her uncle and aunt Western, thought she had a probable opportunity, by the service of preserving Sophia from committing the same crime which had drawn on herself the anger of her family. After much deliberation, therefore, she resolved to inform her aunt Western where her cousin was, and accordingly she writ the following letter, which we shall give the reader at length, for more reasons than one.\n\n“The occasion of my writing this will perhaps make a letter of mine agreeable to my dear aunt, for the sake of one of her nieces, though I have little reason to hope it will be so on the account of another.\n\n“Without more apology, as I was coming to throw my unhappy self at your feet, I met, by the strangest accident in the world, my cousin Sophy, whose history you are better acquainted with than myself, though, alas! I know infinitely too much; enough indeed to satisfy me, that unless she is immediately prevented, she is in danger of running into the same fatal mischief, which, by foolishly and ignorantly refusing your most wise and prudent advice, I have unfortunately brought on myself.\n\n“In short, I have seen the man, nay, I was most part of yesterday in his company, and a charming young fellow I promise you he is. By what accident he came acquainted with me is too tedious to tell you now; but I have this morning changed my lodgings to avoid him, lest he should by my means discover my cousin; for he doth not yet know where she is, and it is adviseable he should not, till my uncle hath secured her.----No time therefore is to be lost; and I need only inform you, that she is now with Lady Bellaston, whom I have seen, and who hath, I find, a design of concealing her from her family. You know, madam, she is a strange woman; but nothing could misbecome me more than to presume to give any hint to one of your great understanding and great knowledge of the world, besides barely informing you of the matter of fact.\n\n“I hope, madam, the care which I have shewn on this occasion for the good of my family will recommend me again to the favour of a lady who hath always exerted so much zeal for the honour and true interest of us all; and that it may be a means of restoring me to your friendship, which hath made so great a part of my former, and is so necessary to my future happiness.\n\n“I am, with the utmost respect, honoured madam, your most dutiful obliged niece, and most obedient humble servant, HARRIET FITZPATRICK.”\n\nMrs Western was now at her brother's house, where she had resided ever since the flight of Sophia, in order to administer comfort to the poor squire in his affliction. Of this comfort, which she doled out to him in daily portions, we have formerly given a specimen.\n\nShe was now standing with her back to the fire, and, with a pinch of snuff in her hand, was dealing forth this daily allowance of comfort to the squire, while he smoaked his afternoon pipe, when she received the above letter; which she had no sooner read than she delivered it to him, saying, “There, sir, there is an account of your lost sheep. Fortune hath again restored her to you, and if you will be governed by my advice, it is possible you may yet preserve her.”\n\nThe squire had no sooner read the letter than he leaped from his chair, threw his pipe into the fire, and gave a loud huzza for joy. He then summoned his servants, called for his boots, and ordered the Chevalier and several other horses to be saddled, and that parson Supple should be immediately sent for. Having done this, he turned to his sister, caught her in his arms, and gave her a close embrace, saying, “Zounds! you don't seem pleased; one would imagine you was sorry I have found the girl.”\n\n“Brother,” answered she, “the deepest politicians, who see to the bottom, discover often a very different aspect of affairs, from what swims on the surface. It is true, indeed, things do look rather less desperate than they did formerly in Holland, when Lewis the Fourteenth was at the gates of Amsterdam; but there is a delicacy required in this matter, which you will pardon me, brother, if I suspect you want. There is a decorum to be used with a woman of figure, such as Lady Bellaston, brother, which requires a knowledge of the world, superior, I am afraid, to yours.”\n\n“Sister,” cries the squire, “I know you have no opinion of my parts; but I'll shew you on this occasion who is a fool. Knowledge, quotha! I have not been in the country so long without having some knowledge of warrants and the law of the land. I know I may take my own wherever I can find it. Shew me my own daughter, and if I don't know how to come at her, I'll suffer you to call me a fool as long as I live. There be justices of peace in London, as well as in other places.”\n\n“I protest,” cries she, “you make me tremble for the event of this matter, which, if you will proceed by my advice, you may bring to so good an issue. Do you really imagine, brother, that the house of a woman of figure is to be attacked by warrants and brutal justices of the peace? I will inform you how to proceed. As soon as you arrive in town, and have got yourself into a decent dress (for indeed, brother, you have none at present fit to appear in), you must send your compliments to Lady Bellaston, and desire leave to wait on her. When you are admitted to her presence, as you certainly will be, and have told her your story, and have made proper use of my name (for I think you just know one another only by sight, though you are relations), I am confident she will withdraw her protection from my niece, who hath certainly imposed upon her. This is the only method.--Justices of peace, indeed! do you imagine any such event can arrive to a woman of figure in a civilised nation?”\n\n“D--n their figures,” cries the squire; “a pretty civilised nation, truly, where women are above the law. And what must I stand sending a parcel of compliments to a confounded whore, that keeps away a daughter from her own natural father? I tell you, sister, I am not so ignorant as you think me----I know you would have women above the law, but it is all a lye; I heard his lordship say at size, that no one is above the law. But this of yours is Hanover law, I suppose.”\n\n“Mr Western,” said she, “I think you daily improve in ignorance.----I protest you are grown an arrant bear.”\n\n“No more a bear than yourself, sister Western,” said the squire.--“Pox! you may talk of your civility an you will, I am sure you never shew any to me. I am no bear, no, nor no dog neither, though I know somebody, that is something that begins with a b; but pox! I will show you I have got more good manners than some folks.”\n\n“Mr Western,” answered the lady, “you may say what you please, je vous mesprise de tout mon coeur. I shall not therefore be angry.----Besides, as my cousin, with that odious Irish name, justly says, I have that regard for the honour and true interest of my family, and that concern for my niece, who is a part of it, that I have resolved to go to town myself upon this occasion; for indeed, indeed, brother, you are not a fit minister to be employed at a polite court.--Greenland--Greenland should always be the scene of the tramontane negociation.”\n\n“I thank Heaven,” cries the squire, “I don't understand you now. You are got to your Hanoverian linguo. However, I'll shew you I scorn to be behind-hand in civility with you; and as you are not angry for what I have said, so I am not angry for what you have said. Indeed I have always thought it a folly for relations to quarrel; and if they do now and then give a hasty word, why, people should give and take; for my part, I never bear malice; and I take it very kind of you to go up to London; for I never was there but twice in my life, and then I did not stay above a fortnight at a time, and to be sure I can't be expected to know much of the streets and the folks in that time. I never denied that you know'd all these matters better than I. For me to dispute that would be all as one as for you to dispute the management of a pack of dogs, or the finding a hare sitting, with me.”--“Which I promise you,” says she, “I never will.”--“Well, and I promise you,” returned he, “that I never will dispute the t'other.”\n\nHere then a league was struck (to borrow a phrase from the lady) between the contending parties; and now the parson arriving, and the horses being ready, the squire departed, having promised his sister to follow her advice, and she prepared to follow him the next day.\n\nBut having communicated these matters to the parson on the road, they both agreed that the prescribed formalities might very well be dispensed with; and the squire, having changed his mind, proceeded in the manner we have already seen.\n\nIn which various misfortunes befel poor Jones.\n\nAffairs were in the aforesaid situation when Mrs Honour arrived at Mrs Miller's, and called Jones out from the company, as we have before seen, with whom, when she found herself alone, she began as follows:--\n\n“O, my dear sir! how shall I get spirits to tell you; you are undone, sir, and my poor lady's undone, and I am undone.” “Hath anything happened to Sophia?” cries Jones, staring like a madman. “All that is bad,” cries Honour: “Oh, I shall never get such another lady! Oh that I should ever live to see this day!” At these words Jones turned pale as ashes, trembled, and stammered; but Honour went on--“O! Mr Jones, I have lost my lady for ever.” “How? what! for Heaven's sake, tell me. O, my dear Sophia!” “You may well call her so,” said Honour; “she was the dearest lady to me. I shall never have such another place.”----“D--n your place!” cries Jones; “where is--what--what is become of my Sophia?” “Ay, to be sure,” cries she, “servants may be d--n'd. It signifies nothing what becomes of them, though they are turned away, and ruined ever so much. To be sure they are not flesh and blood like other people. No, to be sure, it signifies nothing what becomes of them.” “If you have any pity, any compassion,” cries Jones, “I beg you will instantly tell me what hath happened to Sophia?” “To be sure, I have more pity for you than you have for me,” answered Honour; “I don't d--n you because you have lost the sweetest lady in the world. To be sure you are worthy to be pitied, and I am worthy to be pitied too: for, to be sure, if ever there was a good mistress----” “What hath happened?” cries Jones, in almost a raving fit. “What?--What?” said Honour: “Why, the worst that could have happened both for you and for me.--Her father is come to town, and hath carried her away from us both.” Here Jones fell on his knees in thanksgiving that it was no worse. “No worse!” repeated Honour; “what could be worse for either of us? He carried her off, swearing she should marry Mr Blifil; that's for your comfort; and, for poor me, I am turned out of doors.” “Indeed, Mrs Honour,” answered Jones, “you frightened me out of my wits. I imagined some most dreadful sudden accident had happened to Sophia; something, compared to which, even seeing her married to Blifil would be a trifle; but while there is life there are hopes, my dear Honour. Women in this land of liberty, cannot be married by actual brutal force.” “To be sure, sir,” said she, “that's true. There may be some hopes for you; but alack-a-day! what hopes are there for poor me? And to be sure, sir, you must be sensible I suffer all this upon your account. All the quarrel the squire hath to me is for taking your part, as I have done, against Mr Blifil.” “Indeed, Mrs Honour,” answered he, “I am sensible of my obligations to you, and will leave nothing in my power undone to make you amends.” “Alas! sir,” said she, “what can make a servant amends for the loss of one place but the getting another altogether as good?” “Do not despair, Mrs Honour,” said Jones, “I hope to reinstate you again in the same.” “Alack-a-day, sir,” said she, “how can I flatter myself with such hopes when I know it is a thing impossible? for the squire is so set against me: and yet, if you should ever have my lady, as to be sure I now hopes heartily you will; for you are a generous, good-natured gentleman; and I am sure you loves her, and to be sure she loves you as dearly as her own soul; it is a matter in vain to deny it; because as why, everybody, that is in the least acquainted with my lady, must see it; for, poor dear lady, she can't dissemble: and if two people who loves one another a'n't happy, why who should be so? Happiness don't always depend upon what people has; besides, my lady has enough for both. To be sure, therefore, as one may say, it would be all the pity in the world to keep two such loviers asunder; nay, I am convinced, for my part, you will meet together at last; for, if it is to be, there is no preventing it. If a marriage is made in heaven, all the justices of peace upon earth can't break it off. To be sure I wishes that parson Supple had but a little more spirit, to tell the squire of his wickedness in endeavouring to force his daughter contrary to her liking; but then his whole dependance is on the squire; and so the poor gentleman, though he is a very religious good sort of man, and talks of the badness of such doings behind the squire's back, yet he dares not say his soul is his own to his face. To be sure I never saw him make so bold as just now; I was afeard the squire would have struck him. I would not have your honour be melancholy, sir, nor despair; things may go better, as long as you are sure of my lady, and that I am certain you may be; for she never will be brought to consent to marry any other man. Indeed I am terribly afeared the squire will do her a mischief in his passion, for he is a prodigious passionate gentleman; and I am afeared too the poor lady will be brought to break her heart, for she is as tender-hearted as a chicken. It is pity, methinks, she had not a little of my courage. If I was in love with a young man, and my father offered to lock me up, I'd tear his eyes out but I'd come at him; but then there's a great fortune in the case, which it is in her father's power either to give her or not; that, to be sure, may make some difference.”\n\nWhether Jones gave strict attention to all the foregoing harangue, or whether it was for want of any vacancy in the discourse, I cannot determine; but he never once attempted to answer, nor did she once stop till Partridge came running into the room, and informed him that the great lady was upon the stairs.\n\nNothing could equal the dilemma to which Jones was now reduced. Honour knew nothing of any acquaintance that subsisted between him and Lady Bellaston, and she was almost the last person in the world to whom he would have communicated it. In this hurry and distress, he took (as is common enough) the worst course, and, instead of exposing her to the lady, which would have been of little consequence, he chose to expose the lady to her; he therefore resolved to hide Honour, whom he had but just time to convey behind the bed, and to draw the curtains.\n\nThe hurry in which Jones had been all day engaged on account of his poor landlady and her family, the terrors occasioned by Mrs Honour, and the confusion into which he was thrown by the sudden arrival of Lady Bellaston, had altogether driven former thoughts out of his head; so that it never once occurred to his memory to act the part of a sick man; which, indeed, neither the gaiety of his dress, nor the freshness of his countenance, would have at all supported.\n\nHe received her ladyship therefore rather agreeably to her desires than to her expectations, with all the good humour he could muster in his countenance, and without any real or affected appearance of the least disorder.\n\nLady Bellaston no sooner entered the room, than she squatted herself down on the bed: “So, my dear Jones,” said she, “you find nothing can detain me long from you. Perhaps I ought to be angry with you, that I have neither seen nor heard from you all day; for I perceive your distemper would have suffered you to come abroad: nay, I suppose you have not sat in your chamber all day drest up like a fine lady to see company after a lying-in; but, however, don't think I intend to scold you; for I never will give you an excuse for the cold behaviour of a husband, by putting on the ill-humour of a wife.”\n\n“Nay, Lady Bellaston,” said Jones, “I am sure your ladyship will not upbraid me with neglect of duty, when I only waited for orders. Who, my dear creature, hath reason to complain? Who missed an appointment last night, and left an unhappy man to expect, and wish, and sigh, and languish?”\n\n“Do not mention it, my dear Mr Jones,” cried she. “If you knew the occasion, you would pity me. In short, it is impossible to conceive what women of condition are obliged to suffer from the impertinence of fools, in order to keep up the farce of the world. I am glad, however, all your languishing and wishing have done you no harm; for you never looked better in your life. Upon my faith! Jones, you might at this instant sit for the picture of Adonis.”\n\nThere are certain words of provocation which men of honour hold can properly be answered only by a blow. Among lovers possibly there may be some expressions which can be answered only by a kiss. Now the compliment which Lady Bellaston now made Jones seems to be of this kind, especially as it was attended with a look, in which the lady conveyed more soft ideas than it was possible to express with her tongue.\n\nJones was certainly at this instant in one of the most disagreeable and distressed situations imaginable; for, to carry on the comparison we made use of before, though the provocation was given by the lady, Jones could not receive satisfaction, nor so much as offer to ask it, in the presence of a third person; seconds in this kind of duels not being according to the law of arms. As this objection did not occur to Lady Bellaston, who was ignorant of any other woman being there but herself, she waited some time in great astonishment for an answer from Jones, who, conscious of the ridiculous figure he made, stood at a distance, and, not daring to give the proper answer, gave none at all. Nothing can be imagined more comic, nor yet more tragical, than this scene would have been if it had lasted much longer. The lady had already changed colour two or three times; had got up from the bed and sat down again, while Jones was wishing the ground to sink under him, or the house to fall on his head, when an odd accident freed him from an embarrassment out of which neither the eloquence of a Cicero, nor the politics of a Machiavel, could have delivered him, without utter disgrace.\n\nThis was no other than the arrival of young Nightingale, dead drunk; or rather in that state of drunkenness which deprives men of the use of their reason without depriving them of the use of their limbs.\n\nMrs Miller and her daughters were in bed, and Partridge was smoaking his pipe by the kitchen fire; so that he arrived at Mr Jones's chamber-door without any interruption. This he burst open, and was entering without any ceremony, when Jones started from his seat and ran to oppose him, which he did so effectually, that Nightingale never came far enough within the door to see who was sitting on the bed.\n\nNightingale had in reality mistaken Jones's apartment for that in which himself had lodged; he therefore strongly insisted on coming in, often swearing that he would not be kept from his own bed. Jones, however, prevailed over him, and delivered him into the hands of Partridge, whom the noise on the stairs soon summoned to his master's assistance.\n\nAnd now Jones was unwillingly obliged to return to his own apartment, where at the very instant of his entrance he heard Lady Bellaston venting an exclamation, though not a very loud one; and at the same time saw her flinging herself into a chair in a vast agitation, which in a lady of a tender constitution would have been an hysteric fit.\n\nIn reality the lady, frightened with the struggle between the two men, of which she did not know what would be the issue, as she heard Nightingale swear many oaths he would come to his own bed, attempted to retire to her known place of hiding, which to her great confusion she found already occupied by another.\n\n“Is this usage to be borne, Mr Jones?” cries the lady.--“Basest of men?----What wretch is this to whom you have exposed me?” “Wretch!” cries Honour, bursting in a violent rage from her place of concealment--“Marry come up!----Wretch forsooth?----as poor a wretch as I am, I am honest; this is more than some folks who are richer can say.”\n\nJones, instead of applying himself directly to take off the edge of Mrs Honour's resentment, as a more experienced gallant would have done, fell to cursing his stars, and lamenting himself as the most unfortunate man in the world; and presently after, addressing himself to Lady Bellaston, he fell to some very absurd protestations of innocence. By this time the lady, having recovered the use of her reason, which she had as ready as any woman in the world, especially on such occasions, calmly replied: “Sir, you need make no apologies, I see now who the person is; I did not at first know Mrs Honour: but now I do, I can suspect nothing wrong between her and you; and I am sure she is a woman of too good sense to put any wrong constructions upon my visit to you; I have been always her friend, and it may be in my power to be much more hereafter.”\n\nMrs Honour was altogether as placable as she was passionate. Hearing, therefore, Lady Bellaston assume the soft tone, she likewise softened hers.----“I'm sure, madam,” says she, “I have been always ready to acknowledge your ladyship's friendships to me; sure I never had so good a friend as your ladyship----and to be sure, now I see it is your ladyship that I spoke to, I could almost bite my tongue off for very mad.--I constructions upon your ladyship--to be sure it doth not become a servant as I am to think about such a great lady--I mean I was a servant: for indeed I am nobody's servant now, the more miserable wretch is me.--I have lost the best mistress----” Here Honour thought fit to produce a shower of tears.--“Don't cry, child,” says the good lady; “ways perhaps may be found to make you amends. Come to me to-morrow morning.” She then took up her fan which lay on the ground, and without even looking at Jones walked very majestically out of the room; there being a kind of dignity in the impudence of women of quality, which their inferiors vainly aspire to attain to in circumstances of this nature.\n\nJones followed her downstairs, often offering her his hand, which she absolutely refused him, and got into her chair without taking any notice of him as he stood bowing before her.\n\nAt his return upstairs, a long dialogue past between him and Mrs Honour, while she was adjusting herself after the discomposure she had undergone. The subject of this was his infidelity to her young lady; on which she enlarged with great bitterness; but Jones at last found means to reconcile her, and not only so, but to obtain a promise of most inviolable secrecy, and that she would the next morning endeavour to find out Sophia, and bring him a further account of the proceedings of the squire.\n\nThus ended this unfortunate adventure to the satisfaction only of Mrs Honour; for a secret (as some of my readers will perhaps acknowledge from experience) is often a very valuable possession: and that not only to those who faithfully keep it, but sometimes to such as whisper it about till it come to the ears of every one except the ignorant person who pays for the supposed concealing of what is publickly known.\n\nShort and sweet.\n\nNotwithstanding all the obligations she had received from Jones, Mrs Miller could not forbear in the morning some gentle remonstrances for the hurricane which had happened the preceding night in his chamber. These were, however, so gentle and so friendly, professing, and indeed truly, to aim at nothing more than the real good of Mr Jones himself, that he, far from being offended, thankfully received the admonition of the good woman, expressed much concern for what had past, excused it as well as he could, and promised never more to bring the same disturbances into the house.\n\nBut though Mrs Miller did not refrain from a short expostulation in private at their first meeting, yet the occasion of his being summoned downstairs that morning was of a much more agreeable kind, being indeed to perform the office of a father to Miss Nancy, and to give her in wedlock to Mr Nightingale, who was now ready drest, and full as sober as many of my readers will think a man ought to be who receives a wife in so imprudent a manner.\n\nAnd here perhaps it may be proper to account for the escape which this young gentleman had made from his uncle, and for his appearance in the condition in which we have seen him the night before.\n\nNow when the uncle had arrived at his lodgings with his nephew, partly to indulge his own inclinations (for he dearly loved his bottle), and partly to disqualify his nephew from the immediate execution of his purpose, he ordered wine to be set on the table; with which he so briskly plyed the young gentleman, that this latter, who, though not much used to drinking, did not detest it so as to be guilty of disobedience or want of complacence by refusing, was soon completely finished.\n\nJust as the uncle had obtained this victory, and was preparing a bed for his nephew, a messenger arrived with a piece of news, which so entirely disconcerted and shocked him, that he in a moment lost all consideration for his nephew, and his whole mind became entirely taken up with his own concerns.\n\nThis sudden and afflicting news was no less than that his daughter had taken the opportunity of almost the first moment of his absence, and had gone off with a neighbouring young clergyman; against whom, though her father could have had but one objection, namely, that he was worth nothing, yet she had never thought proper to communicate her amour even to that father; and so artfully had she managed, that it had never been once suspected by any, till now that it was consummated.\n\nOld Mr Nightingale no sooner received this account, than in the utmost confusion he ordered a post-chaise to be instantly got ready, and, having recommended his nephew to the care of a servant, he directly left the house, scarce knowing what he did, nor whither he went.\n\nThe uncle thus departed, when the servant came to attend the nephew to bed, had waked him for that purpose, and had at last made him sensible that his uncle was gone, he, instead of accepting the kind offices tendered him, insisted on a chair being called; with this the servant, who had received no strict orders to the contrary, readily complied; and, thus being conducted back to the house of Mrs Miller, he had staggered up to Mr Jones's chamber, as hath been before recounted.\n\nThis bar of the uncle being now removed (though young Nightingale knew not as yet in what manner), and all parties being quickly ready, the mother, Mr Jones, Mr Nightingale, and his love, stept into a hackney-coach, which conveyed them to Doctors' Commons; where Miss Nancy was, in vulgar language, soon made an honest woman, and the poor mother became, in the purest sense of the word, one of the happiest of all human beings.\n\nAnd now Mr Jones, having seen his good offices to that poor woman and her family brought to a happy conclusion, began to apply himself to his own concerns; but here, lest many of my readers should censure his folly for thus troubling himself with the affairs of others, and lest some few should think he acted more disinterestedly than indeed he did, we think proper to assure our reader, that he was so far from being unconcerned in this matter, that he had indeed a very considerable interest in bringing it to that final consummation.\n\nTo explain this seeming paradox at once, he was one who could truly say with him in Terence, Homo sum: humani nihil a me alienum puto. He was never an indifferent spectator of the misery or happiness of any one; and he felt either the one or the other in great proportion as he himself contributed to either. He could not, therefore, be the instrument of raising a whole family from the lowest state of wretchedness to the highest pitch of joy without conveying great felicity to himself; more perhaps than worldly men often purchase to themselves by undergoing the most severe labour, and often by wading through the deepest iniquity.\n\nThose readers who are of the same complexion with him will perhaps think this short chapter contains abundance of matter; while others may probably wish, short as it is, that it had been totally spared as impertinent to the main design, which I suppose they conclude is to bring Mr Jones to the gallows, or, if possible, to a more deplorable catastrophe.\n\nContaining love-letters of several sorts.\n\nMr Jones, at his return home, found the following letters lying on his table, which he luckily opened in the order they were sent. LETTER I.\n\n“Surely I am under some strange infatuation; I cannot keep my resolutions a moment, however strongly made or justly founded. Last night I resolved never to see you more; this morning I am willing to hear if you can, as you say, clear up this affair. And yet I know that to be impossible. I have said everything to myself which you can invent.----Perhaps not. Perhaps your invention is stronger. Come to me, therefore, the moment you receive this. If you can forge an excuse I almost promise you to believe it. Betrayed too----I will think no more.----Come to me directly.----This is the third letter I have writ, the two former are burnt----I am almost inclined to burn this too----I wish I may preserve my senses.----Come to me presently.”\n\n# LETTER II.\n\n“If you ever expect to be forgiven, or even suffered within my doors, come to me this instant.” LETTER III.\n\n“I now find you was not at home when my notes came to your lodgings. The moment you receive this let me see you;--I shall not stir out; nor shall anybody be let in but yourself. Sure nothing can detain you long.”\n\nJones had just read over these three billets when Mr Nightingale came into the room. “Well, Tom,” said he, “any news from Lady Bellaston, after last night's adventure?” (for it was now no secret to any one in that house who the lady was). “The Lady Bellaston?” answered Jones very gravely.----“Nay, dear Tom,” cries Nightingale, “don't be so reserved to your friends. Though I was too drunk to see her last night, I saw her at the masquerade. Do you think I am ignorant who the queen of the fairies is?” “And did you really then know the lady at the masquerade?” said Jones. “Yes, upon my soul, did I,” said Nightingale, “and have given you twenty hints of it since, though you seemed always so tender on that point, that I would not speak plainly. I fancy, my friend, by your extreme nicety in this matter, you are not so well acquainted with the character of the lady as with her person. Don't be angry, Tom, but upon my honour, you are not the first young fellow she hath debauched. Her reputation is in no danger, believe me.”\n\nThough Jones had no reason to imagine the lady to have been of the vestal kind when his amour began; yet, as he was thoroughly ignorant of the town, and had very little acquaintance in it, he had no knowledge of that character which is vulgarly called a demirep; that is to say, a woman who intrigues with every man she likes, under the name and appearance of virtue; and who, though some over-nice ladies will not be seen with her, is visited (as they term it) by the whole town, in short, whom everybody knows to be what nobody calls her.\n\nWhen he found, therefore, that Nightingale was perfectly acquainted with his intrigue, and began to suspect that so scrupulous a delicacy as he had hitherto observed was not quite necessary on the occasion, he gave a latitude to his friend's tongue, and desired him to speak plainly what he knew, or had ever heard of the lady.\n\nNightingale, who, in many other instances, was rather too effeminate in his disposition, had a pretty strong inclination to tittle-tattle. He had no sooner, therefore, received a full liberty of speaking from Jones, than he entered upon a long narrative concerning the lady; which, as it contained many particulars highly to her dishonour, we have too great a tenderness for all women of condition to repeat. We would cautiously avoid giving an opportunity to the future commentators on our works, of making any malicious application and of forcing us to be, against our will, the author of scandal, which never entered into our head.\n\nJones, having very attentively heard all that Nightingale had to say, fetched a deep sigh; which the other, observing, cried, “Heyday! why, thou art not in love, I hope! Had I imagined my stories would have affected you, I promise you should never have heard them.” “O my dear friend!” cries Jones, “I am so entangled with this woman, that I know not how to extricate myself. In love, indeed! no, my friend, but I am under obligations to her, and very great ones. Since you know so much, I will be very explicit with you. It is owing, perhaps, solely to her, that I have not, before this, wanted a bit of bread. How can I possibly desert such a woman? and yet I must desert her, or be guilty of the blackest treachery to one who deserves infinitely better of me than she can; a woman, my Nightingale, for whom I have a passion which few can have an idea of. I am half distracted with doubts how to act.” “And is this other, pray, an honourable mistress?” cries Nightingale. “Honourable!” answered Jones; “no breath ever yet durst sully her reputation. The sweetest air is not purer, the limpid stream not clearer, than her honour. She is all over, both in mind and body, consummate perfection. She is the most beautiful creature in the universe: and yet she is mistress of such noble elevated qualities, that, though she is never from my thoughts, I scarce ever think of her beauty but when I see it.”--“And can you, my good friend,” cries Nightingale, “with such an engagement as this upon your hands, hesitate a moment about quitting such a--” “Hold,” said Jones, “no more abuse of her: I detest the thought of ingratitude.” “Pooh!” answered the other, “you are not the first upon whom she hath conferred obligations of this kind. She is remarkably liberal where she likes; though, let me tell you, her favours are so prudently bestowed, that they should rather raise a man's vanity than his gratitude.” In short, Nightingale proceeded so far on this head, and told his friend so many stories of the lady, which he swore to the truth of, that he entirely removed all esteem for her from the breast of Jones; and his gratitude was lessened in proportion. Indeed, he began to look on all the favours he had received rather as wages than benefits, which depreciated not only her, but himself too in his own conceit, and put him quite out of humour with both. From this disgust, his mind, by a natural transition, turned towards Sophia; her virtue, her purity, her love to him, her sufferings on his account, filled all his thoughts, and made his commerce with Lady Bellaston appear still more odious. The result of all was, that, though his turning himself out of her service, in which light he now saw his affair with her, would be the loss of his bread; yet he determined to quit her, if he could but find a handsome pretence: which being communicated to his friend, Nightingale considered a little, and then said, “I have it, my boy! I have found out a sure method; propose marriage to her, and I would venture hanging upon the success.” “Marriage?” cries Jones. “Ay, propose marriage,” answered Nightingale, “and she will declare off in a moment. I knew a young fellow whom she kept formerly, who made the offer to her in earnest, and was presently turned off for his pains.”\n\nJones declared he could not venture the experiment. “Perhaps,” said he, “she may be less shocked at this proposal from one man than from another. And if she should take me at my word, where am I then? caught, in my own trap, and undone for ever.” “No;” answered Nightingale, “not if I can give you an expedient by which you may at any time get out of the trap.”----“What expedient can that be?” replied Jones. “This,” answered Nightingale. “The young fellow I mentioned, who is one of the most intimate acquaintances I have in the world, is so angry with her for some ill offices she hath since done him, that I am sure he would, without any difficulty, give you a sight of her letters; upon which you may decently break with her; and declare off before the knot is tyed, if she should really be willing to tie it, which I am convinced she will not.”\n\nAfter some hesitation, Jones, upon the strength of this assurance, consented; but, as he swore he wanted the confidence to propose the matter to her face, he wrote the following letter, which Nightingale dictated:--\n\n“I am extremely concerned, that, by an unfortunate engagement abroad, I should have missed receiving the honour of your ladyship's commands the moment they came; and the delay which I must now suffer of vindicating myself to your ladyship greatly adds to this misfortune. O, Lady Bellaston! what a terror have I been in for fear your reputation should be exposed by these perverse accidents! There is one only way to secure it. I need not name what that is. Only permit me to say, that as your honour is as dear to me as my own, so my sole ambition is to have the glory of laying my liberty at your feet; and believe me when I assure you, I can never be made completely happy without you generously bestow on me a legal right of calling you mine for ever.--I am,\n\nmadam, with most profound respect, your ladyship's most obliged, obedient, humble servant, THOMAS JONES.”\n\nTo this she presently returned the following answer:\n\n“When I read over your serious epistle, I could, from its coldness and formality, have sworn that you already had the legal right you mention; nay, that we had for many years composed that monstrous animal a husband and wife. Do you really then imagine me a fool? or do you fancy yourself capable of so entirely persuading me out of my senses, that I should deliver my whole fortune into your power, in order to enable you to support your pleasures at my expense? Are these the proofs of love which I expected? Is this the return for--? but I scorn to upbraid you, and am in great admiration of your profound respect.\n\n“P.S. I am prevented from revising:----Perhaps I have said more than I meant.----Come to me at eight this evening.”\n\nJones, by the advice of his privy-council, replied:\n\n“It is impossible to express how much I am shocked at the suspicion you entertain of me. Can Lady Bellaston have conferred favours on a man whom she could believe capable of so base a design? or can she treat the most solemn tie of love with contempt? Can you imagine, madam, that if the violence of my passion, in an unguarded moment, overcame the tenderness which I have for your honour, I would think of indulging myself in the continuance of an intercourse which could not possibly escape long the notice of the world; and which, when discovered, must prove so fatal to your reputation? If such be your opinion of me, I must pray for a sudden opportunity of returning those pecuniary obligations, which I have been so unfortunate to receive at your hands; and for those of a more tender kind, I shall ever remain, &c.” And so concluded in the very words with which he had concluded the former letter.\n\nThe lady answered as follows:\n\n“I see you are a villain! and I despise you from my soul. If you come here I shall not be at home.”\n\nThough Jones was well satisfied with his deliverance from a thraldom which those who have ever experienced it will, I apprehend, allow to be none of the lightest, he was not, however, perfectly easy in his mind. There was in this scheme too much of fallacy to satisfy one who utterly detested every species of falshood or dishonesty: nor would he, indeed, have submitted to put it in practice, had he not been involved in a distressful situation, where he was obliged to be guilty of some dishonour, either to the one lady or the other; and surely the reader will allow, that every good principle, as well as love, pleaded strongly in favour of Sophia.\n\nNightingale highly exulted in the success of his stratagem, upon which he received many thanks and much applause from his friend. He answered, “Dear Tom, we have conferred very different obligations on each other. To me you owe the regaining your liberty; to you I owe the loss of mine. But if you are as happy in the one instance as I am in the other, I promise you we are the two happiest fellows in England.”\n\nThe two gentlemen were now summoned down to dinner, where Mrs Miller, who performed herself the office of cook, had exerted her best talents to celebrate the wedding of her daughter. This joyful circumstance she ascribed principally to the friendly behaviour of Jones, her whole soul was fired with gratitude towards him, and all her looks, words, and actions, were so busied in expressing it, that her daughter, and even her new son-in-law, were very little objects of her consideration.\n\nDinner was just ended when Mrs Miller received a letter; but as we have had letters enow in this chapter, we shall communicate its contents in our next.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\nConsisting partly of facts, and partly of observations upon them.\nThe letter then which arrived at the end of the preceding chapter was from Mr Allworthy, and the purport of it was, his intention to come immediately to town, with his nephew Blifil, and a desire to be accommodated with his usual lodgings, which were the first floor for himself, and the second for his nephew.\n\nThe chearfulness which had before displayed itself in the countenance of the poor woman was a little clouded on this occasion. This news did indeed a good deal disconcert her. To requite so disinterested a match with her daughter, by presently turning her new son-in-law out of doors, appeared to her very unjustifiable on the one hand; and on the other, she could scarce bear the thoughts of making any excuse to Mr Allworthy, after all the obligations received from him, for depriving him of lodgings which were indeed strictly his due; for that gentleman, in conferring all his numberless benefits on others, acted by a rule diametrically opposite to what is practised by most generous people. He contrived, on all occasions, to hide his beneficence, not only from the world, but even from the object of it. He constantly used the words Lend and Pay, instead of Give; and by every other method he could invent, always lessened with his tongue the favours he conferred, while he was heaping them with both his hands. When he settled the annuity of £50 a year therefore on Mrs Miller, he told her, “it was in consideration of always having her first-floor when he was in town (which he scarce ever intended to be), but that she might let it at any other time, for that he would always send her a month's warning.” He was now, however, hurried to town so suddenly, that he had no opportunity of giving such notice; and this hurry probably prevented him, when he wrote for his lodgings, adding, if they were then empty; for he would most certainly have been well satisfied to have relinquished them, on a less sufficient excuse than what Mrs Miller could now have made.\n\nBut there are a sort of persons, who, as Prior excellently well remarks, direct their conduct by something\n\nBeyond the fix'd and settled rules Of vice and virtue in the schools, Beyond the letter of the law.\n\nTo these it is so far from being sufficient that their defence would acquit them at the Old Bailey, that they are not even contented, though conscience, the severest of all judges, should discharge them. Nothing short of the fair and honourable will satisfy the delicacy of their minds; and if any of their actions fall short of this mark, they mope and pine, are as uneasy and restless as a murderer, who is afraid of a ghost, or of the hangman.\n\nMrs Miller was one of these. She could not conceal her uneasiness at this letter; with the contents of which she had no sooner acquainted the company, and given some hints of her distress, than Jones, her good angel, presently relieved her anxiety. “As for myself, madam,” said he, “my lodging is at your service at a moment's warning; and Mr Nightingale, I am sure, as he cannot yet prepare a house fit to receive his lady, will consent to return to his new lodging, whither Mrs Nightingale will certainly consent to go.” With which proposal both husband and wife instantly agreed.\n\nThe reader will easily believe, that the cheeks of Mrs Miller began again to glow with additional gratitude to Jones; but, perhaps, it may be more difficult to persuade him, that Mr Jones having in his last speech called her daughter Mrs Nightingale (it being the first time that agreeable sound had ever reached her ears), gave the fond mother more satisfaction, and warmed her heart more towards Jones, than his having dissipated her present anxiety.\n\nThe next day was then appointed for the removal of the new-married couple, and of Mr Jones, who was likewise to be provided for in the same house with his friend. And now the serenity of the company was again restored, and they past the day in the utmost chearfulness, all except Jones, who, though he outwardly accompanied the rest in their mirth, felt many a bitter pang on the account of his Sophia, which were not a little heightened by the news of Mr Blifil's coming to town (for he clearly saw the intention of his journey); and what greatly aggravated his concern was, that Mrs Honour, who had promised to inquire after Sophia, and to make her report to him early the next evening, had disappointed him.\n\nIn the situation that he and his mistress were in at this time, there were scarce any grounds for him to hope that he should hear any good news; yet he was as impatient to see Mrs Honour as if he had expected she would bring him a letter with an assignation in it from Sophia, and bore the disappointment as ill. Whether this impatience arose from that natural weakness of the human mind, which makes it desirous to know the worst, and renders uncertainty the most intolerable of pains; or whether he still flattered himself with some secret hopes, we will not determine. But that it might be the last, whoever has loved cannot but know. For of all the powers exercised by this passion over our minds, one of the most wonderful is that of supporting hope in the midst of despair. Difficulties, improbabilities, nay, impossibilities, are quite overlooked by it; so that to any man extremely in love, may be applied what Addison says of Caesar,\n\n“The Alps, and Pyrenaeans, sink before him!”\n\nYet it is equally true, that the same passion will sometimes make mountains of molehills, and produce despair in the midst of hope; but these cold fits last not long in good constitutions. Which temper Jones was now in, we leave the reader to guess, having no exact information about it; but this is certain, that he had spent two hours in expectation, when, being unable any longer to conceal his uneasiness, he retired to his room; where his anxiety had almost made him frantick, when the following letter was brought him from Mrs Honour, with which we shall present the reader verbatim et literatim.\n\n“I shud sartenly haf kaled on you a cordin too mi prommiss haddunt itt bin that hur lashipp prevent mee; for to bee sur, Sir, you nose very well that evere persun must luk furst at ome, and sartenly such anuther offar mite not have ever hapned, so as I shud ave bin justly to blam, had I not excepted of it when her lashipp was so veri kind as to offar to mak mee hur one uman without mi ever askin any such thing, to be sur shee is won of thee best ladis in thee wurld, and pepil who sase to the kontrari must bee veri wiket pepil in thare harts. To bee sur if ever I ave sad any thing of that kine it as bin thru ignorens, and I am hartili sorri for it. I nose your onur to be a genteelman of more onur and onesty, if I ever said ani such thing, to repete it to hurt a pore servant that as alwais add thee gratest respect in thee wurld for ure onur. To be sur won shud kepe wons tung within wons teeth, for no boddi nose what may hapen; and to bee sur if ani boddi ad tolde mee yesterday, that I shud haf bin in so gud a plase to day, I shud not haf beleeved it; for to be sur I never was a dremd of any such thing, nor shud I ever have soft after ani other bodi's plase; but as her lashipp wass so kine of her one a cord too give it mee without askin, to be sur Mrs Etoff herself, nor no other boddi can blam mee for exceptin such a thing when it fals in mi waye. I beg ure Onur not to menshion ani thing of what I haf sad, for I wish ure Onur all thee gud luk in the wurld; and I don't cuestion butt thatt u will haf Madam Sofia in the end; butt ass to miself ure onur nose I kant bee of ani farder sarvis to u in that matar, nou bein under thee cumand off anuther parson, and nott mi one mistress, I begg ure Onur to say nothing of what past, and belive me to be, sir, ure Onur's umble servant to cumand till deth,\n\nVarious were the conjectures which Jones entertained on this step of Lady Bellaston; who, in reality, had little farther design than to secure within her own house the repository of a secret, which she chose should make no farther progress than it had made already; but mostly, she desired to keep it from the ears of Sophia; for though that young lady was almost the only one who would never have repeated it again, her ladyship could not persuade herself of this; since, as she now hated poor Sophia with most implacable hatred, she conceived a reciprocal hatred to herself to be lodged in the tender breast of our heroine, where no such passion had ever yet found an entrance.\n\nWhile Jones was terrifying himself with the apprehension of a thousand dreadful machinations, and deep political designs, which he imagined to be at the bottom of the promotion of Honour, Fortune, who hitherto seems to have been an utter enemy to his match with Sophia, tried a new method to put a final end to it, by throwing a temptation in his way, which in his present desperate situation it seemed unlikely he should be able to resist.\n\nContaining curious, but not unprecedented matter.\n\nThere was a lady, one Mrs Hunt, who had often seen Jones at the house where he lodged, being intimately acquainted with the women there, and indeed a very great friend to Mrs Miller. Her age was about thirty, for she owned six-and-twenty; her face and person very good, only inclining a little too much to be fat. She had been married young by her relations to an old Turkey merchant, who, having got a great fortune, had left off trade. With him she lived without reproach, but not without pain, in a state of great self-denial, for about twelve years; and her virtue was rewarded by his dying and leaving her very rich. The first year of her widowhood was just at an end, and she had past it in a good deal of retirement, seeing only a few particular friends, and dividing her time between her devotions and novels, of which she was always extremely fond. Very good health, a very warm constitution, and a good deal of religion, made it absolutely necessary for her to marry again; and she resolved to please herself in her second husband, as she had done her friends in the first. From her the following billet was brought to Jones:--\n\n“From the first day I saw you, I doubt my eyes have told you too plainly that you were not indifferent to me; but neither my tongue nor my hand should have ever avowed it, had not the ladies of the family where you are lodged given me such a character of you, and told me such proofs of your virtue and goodness, as convince me you are not only the most agreeable, but the most worthy of men. I have also the satisfaction to hear from them, that neither my person, understanding, or character, are disagreeable to you. I have a fortune sufficient to make us both happy, but which cannot make me so without you. In thus disposing of myself, I know I shall incur the censure of the world; but if I did not love you more than I fear the world, I should not be worthy of you. One only difficulty stops me: I am informed you are engaged in a commerce of gallantry with a woman of fashion. If you think it worth while to sacrifice that to the possession of me, I am yours; if not, forget my weakness, and let this remain an eternal secret between you and\n\nAt the reading of this, Jones was put into a violent flutter. His fortune was then at a very low ebb, the source being stopt from which hitherto he had been supplied. Of all he had received from Lady Bellaston, not above five guineas remained; and that very morning he had been dunned by a tradesman for twice that sum. His honourable mistress was in the hands of her father, and he had scarce any hopes ever to get her out of them again. To be subsisted at her expense, from that little fortune she had independent of her father, went much against the delicacy both of his pride and his love. This lady's fortune would have been exceeding convenient to him, and he could have no objection to her in any respect. On the contrary, he liked her as well as he did any woman except Sophia. But to abandon Sophia, and marry another, that was impossible; he could not think of it upon any account, Yet why should he not, since it was plain she could not be his? Would it not be kinder to her, than to continue her longer engaged in a hopeless passion for him? Ought he not to do so in friendship to her? This notion prevailed some moments, and he had almost determined to be false to her from a high point of honour: but that refinement was not able to stand very long against the voice of nature, which cried in his heart that such friendship was treason to love. At last he called for pen, ink, and paper, and writ as follows to Mrs Hunt:--\n\n“It would be but a poor return to the favour you have done me to sacrifice any gallantry to the possession of you, and I would certainly do it, though I were not disengaged, as at present I am, from any affair of that kind. But I should not be the honest man you think me, if I did not tell you that my affections are engaged to another, who is a woman of virtue, and one that I never can leave, though it is probable I shall never possess her. God forbid that, in return of your kindness to me, I should do you such an injury as to give you my hand when I cannot give my heart. No; I had much rather starve than be guilty of that. Even though my mistress were married to another, I would not marry you unless my heart had entirely effaced all impressions of her. Be assured that your secret was not more safe in your own breast, than in that of your most obliged, and grateful humble servant,\n\nWhen our heroe had finished and sent this letter, he went to his scrutore, took out Miss Western's muff, kissed it several times, and then strutted some turns about his room, with more satisfaction of mind than ever any Irishman felt in carrying off a fortune of fifty thousand pounds.\n\nA discovery made by Partridge.\n\nWhile Jones was exulting in the consciousness of his integrity, Partridge came capering into the room, as was his custom when he brought, or fancied he brought, any good tidings. He had been despatched that morning by his master, with orders to endeavour, by the servants of Lady Bellaston, or by any other means, to discover whither Sophia had been conveyed; and he now returned, and with a joyful countenance told our heroe that he had found the lost bird. “I have seen, sir,” says he, “Black George, the gamekeeper, who is one of the servants whom the squire hath brought with him to town. I knew him presently, though I have not seen him these several years; but you know, sir, he is a very remarkable man, or, to use a purer phrase, he hath a most remarkable beard, the largest and blackest I ever saw. It was some time, however, before Black George could recollect me.” “Well, but what is your good news?” cries Jones; “what do you know of my Sophia?” “You shall know presently, sir,” answered Partridge, “I am coming to it as fast as I can. You are so impatient, sir, you would come at the infinitive mood before you can get to the imperative. As I was saying, sir, it was some time before he recollected my face.”--“Confound your face!” cries Jones, “what of my Sophia?” “Nay, sir,” answered Partridge, “I know nothing more of Madam Sophia than what I am going to tell you; and I should have told you all before this if you had not interrupted me; but if you look so angry at me you will frighten all of it out of my head, or, to use a purer phrase, out of my memory. I never saw you look so angry since the day we left Upton, which I shall remember if I was to live a thousand years.”--“Well, pray go on your own way,” said Jones: “you are resolved to make me mad I find.” “Not for the world,” answered Partridge, “I have suffered enough for that already; which, as I said, I shall bear in my remembrance the longest day I have to live.” “Well, but Black George?” cries Jones. “Well, sir, as I was saying, it was a long time before he could recollect me; for, indeed, I am very much altered since I saw him. Non sum qualis eram. I have had troubles in the world, and nothing alters a man so much as grief. I have heard it will change the colour of a man's hair in a night. However, at last, know me he did, that's sure enough; for we are both of an age, and were at the same charity school. George was a great dunce, but no matter for that; all men do not thrive in the world according to their learning. I am sure I have reason to say so; but it will be all one a thousand years hence. Well, sir, where was I?--O--well, we no sooner knew each other, than, after many hearty shakes by the hand, we agreed to go to an alehouse and take a pot, and by good luck the beer was some of the best I have met with since I have been in town. Now, sir, I am coming to the point; for no sooner did I name you, and told him that you and I came to town together, and had lived together ever since, than he called for another pot, and swore he would drink to your health; and indeed he drank your health so heartily that I was overjoyed to see there was so much gratitude left in the world; and after we had emptied that pot I said I would buy my pot too, and so we drank another to your health; and then I made haste home to tell you the news.”\n\n“What news?” cries Jones, “you have not mentioned a word of my Sophia!” “Bless me! I had like to have forgot that. Indeed, we mentioned a great deal about young Madam Western, and George told me all; that Mr Blifil is coming to town in order to be married to her. He had best make haste then, says I, or somebody will have her before he comes; and, indeed, says I, Mr Seagrim, it is a thousand pities somebody should not have her; for he certainly loves her above all the women in the world. I would have both you and she know, that it is not for her fortune he follows her; for I can assure you, as to matter of that, there is another lady, one of much greater quality and fortune than she can pretend to, who is so fond of somebody that she comes after him day and night.”\n\nHere Jones fell into a passion with Partridge, for having, as he said, betrayed him; but the poor fellow answered, he had mentioned no name: “Besides, sir,” said he, “I can assure you George is sincerely your friend, and wished Mr Blifil at the devil more than once; nay, he said he would do anything in his power upon earth to serve you; and so I am convinced he will. Betray you, indeed! why, I question whether you have a better friend than George upon earth, except myself, or one that would go farther to serve you.”\n\n“Well,” says Jones, a little pacified, “you say this fellow, who, I believe, indeed, is enough inclined to be my friend, lives in the same house with Sophia?”\n\n“In the same house!” answered Partridge; “why, sir, he is one of the servants of the family, and very well drest I promise you he is; if it was not for his black beard you would hardly know him.”\n\n“One service then at least he may do me,” says Jones: “sure he can certainly convey a letter to my Sophia.”\n\n“You have hit the nail ad unguem” cries Partridge; “how came I not to think of it? I will engage he shall do it upon the very first mentioning.”\n\n“Well, then,” said Jones, “do you leave me at present, and I will write a letter, which you shall deliver to him to-morrow morning; for I suppose you know where to find him.”\n\n“O yes, sir,” answered Partridge, “I shall certainly find him again; there is no fear of that. The liquor is too good for him to stay away long. I make no doubt but he will be there every day he stays in town.”\n\n“So you don't know the street then where my Sophia is lodged?” cries Jones.\n\n“Indeed, sir, I do,” says Partridge.\n\n“What is the name of the street?” cries Jones.\n\n“The name, sir? why, here, sir, just by,” answered Partridge, “not above a street or two off. I don't, indeed, know the very name; for, as he never told me, if I had asked, you know, it might have put some suspicion into his head. No, no, sir, let me alone for that. I am too cunning for that, I promise you.”\n\n“Thou art most wonderfully cunning, indeed,” replied Jones; “however, I will write to my charmer, since I believe you will be cunning enough to find him to-morrow at the alehouse.”\n\nAnd now, having dismissed the sagacious Partridge, Mr Jones sat himself down to write, in which employment we shall leave him for a time. And here we put an end to the fifteenth book.\n\nOf prologues.\n\nI have heard of a dramatic writer who used to say, he would rather write a play than a prologue; in like manner, I think, I can with less pains write one of the books of this history than the prefatory chapter to each of them.\n\nTo say the truth, I believe many a hearty curse hath been devoted on the head of that author who first instituted the method of prefixing to his play that portion of matter which is called the prologue; and which at first was part of the piece itself, but of latter years hath had usually so little connexion with the drama before which it stands, that the prologue to one play might as well serve for any other. Those indeed of more modern date, seem all to be written on the same three topics, viz., an abuse of the taste of the town, a condemnation of all contemporary authors, and an eulogium on the performance just about to be represented. The sentiments in all these are very little varied, nor is it possible they should; and indeed I have often wondered at the great invention of authors, who have been capable of finding such various phrases to express the same thing.\n\nIn like manner I apprehend, some future historian (if any one shall do me the honour of imitating my manner) will, after much scratching his pate, bestow some good wishes on my memory, for having first established these several initial chapters; most of which, like modern prologues, may as properly be prefixed to any other book in this history as to that which they introduce, or indeed to any other history as to this.\n\nBut however authors may suffer by either of these inventions, the reader will find sufficient emolument in the one as the spectator hath long found in the other.\n\nFirst, it is well known that the prologue serves the critic for an opportunity to try his faculty of hissing, and to tune his cat-call to the best advantage; by which means, I have known those musical instruments so well prepared, that they have been able to play in full concert at the first rising of the curtain.\n\nThe same advantages may be drawn from these chapters, in which the critic will be always sure of meeting with something that may serve as a whetstone to his noble spirit; so that he may fall with a more hungry appetite for censure on the history itself. And here his sagacity must make it needless to observe how artfully these chapters are calculated for that excellent purpose; for in these we have always taken care to intersperse somewhat of the sour or acid kind, in order to sharpen and stimulate the said spirit of criticism.\n\nAgain, the indolent reader, as well as spectator, finds great advantage from both these; for, as they are not obliged either to see the one or read the others, and both the play and the book are thus protracted, by the former they have a quarter of an hour longer allowed them to sit at dinner, and by the latter they have the advantage of beginning to read at the fourth or fifth page instead of the first, a matter by no means of trivial consequence to persons who read books with no other view than to say they have read them, a more general motive to reading than is commonly imagined; and from which not only law books, and good books, but the pages of Homer and Virgil, of Swift and Cervantes, have been often turned over.\n\nMany other are the emoluments which arise from both these, but they are for the most part so obvious, that we shall not at present stay to enumerate them; especially since it occurs to us that the principal merit of both the prologue and the preface is that they be short.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\nA whimsical adventure which befel the squire, with the distressed situation of Sophia.\n\nWe must now convey the reader to Mr Western's lodgings, which were in Piccadilly, where he was placed by the recommendation of the landlord at the Hercules Pillars at Hyde Park Corner; for at the inn, which was the first he saw on his arrival in town, he placed his horses, and in those lodgings, which were the first he heard of, he deposited himself.\n\nHere, when Sophia alighted from the hackney-coach, which brought her from the house of Lady Bellaston, she desired to retire to the apartment provided for her; to which her father very readily agreed, and whither he attended her himself. A short dialogue, neither very material nor pleasant to relate minutely, then passed between them, in which he pressed her vehemently to give her consent to the marriage with Blifil, who, as he acquainted her, was to be in town in a few days; but, instead of complying, she gave a more peremptory and resolute refusal than she had ever done before. This so incensed her father, that after many bitter vows, that he would force her to have him whether she would or no, he departed from her with many hard words and curses, locked the door, and put the key into his pocket.\n\nWhile Sophia was left with no other company than what attend the closest state prisoner, namely, fire and candle, the squire sat down to regale himself over a bottle of wine, with his parson and the landlord of the Hercules Pillars, who, as the squire said, would make an excellent third man, and could inform them of the news of the town, and how affairs went; for to be sure, says he, he knows a great deal, since the horses of many of the quality stand at his house.\n\nIn this agreeable society Mr Western past that evening and great part of the succeeding day, during which period nothing happened of sufficient consequence to find a place in this history. All this time Sophia past by herself; for her father swore she should never come out of her chamber alive, unless she first consented to marry Blifil; nor did he ever suffer the door to be unlocked, unless to convey her food, on which occasions he always attended himself.\n\nThe second morning after his arrival, while he and the parson were at breakfast together on a toast and tankard, he was informed that a gentleman was below to wait on him.\n\n“A gentleman!” quoth the squire, “who the devil can he be? Do, doctor, go down and see who 'tis. Mr Blifil can hardly be come to town yet.--Go down, do, and know what his business is.”\n\nThe doctor returned with an account that it was a very well-drest man, and by the ribbon in his hat he took him for an officer of the army; that he said he had some particular business, which he could deliver to none but Mr Western himself.\n\n“An officer!” cries the squire; “what can any such fellow have to do with me? If he wants an order for baggage-waggons, I am no justice of peace here, nor can I grant a warrant.--Let un come up then, if he must speak to me.”\n\nA very genteel man now entered the room; who, having made his compliments to the squire, and desired the favour of being alone with him, delivered himself as follows:--\n\n“Sir, I come to wait upon you by the command of my Lord Fellamar; but with a very different message from what I suppose you expect, after what past the other night.”\n\n“My lord who?” cries the squire; “I never heard the name o'un.”\n\n“His lordship,” said the gentleman, “is willing to impute everything to the effect of liquor, and the most trifling acknowledgment of that kind will set everything right; for as he hath the most violent attachment to your daughter, you, sir, are the last person upon earth from whom he would resent an affront; and happy is it for you both that he hath given such public demonstrations of his courage as to be able to put up an affair of this kind without danger of any imputation on his honour. All he desires, therefore, is, that you will before me make some acknowledgment; the slightest in the world will be sufficient; and he intends this afternoon to pay his respects to you, in order to obtain your leave of visiting the young lady on the footing of a lover.”\n\n“I don't understand much of what you say, sir,” said the squire; “but I suppose, by what you talk about my daughter, that this is the lord which my cousin, Lady Bellaston, mentioned to me, and said something about his courting my daughter. If so be that how that be the case--you may give my service to his lordship, and tell un the girl is disposed of already.”\n\n“Perhaps, sir,” said the gentleman, “you are not sufficiently apprized of the greatness of this offer. I believe such a person, title, and fortune would be nowhere refused.”\n\n“Lookee, sir,” answered the squire; “to be very plain, my daughter is bespoke already; but if she was not, I would not marry her to a lord upon any account; I hate all lords; they are a parcel of courtiers and Hanoverians, and I will have nothing to do with them.”\n\n“Well, sir,” said the gentleman, “if that is your resolution, the message I am to deliver to you is that my lord desires the favour of your company this morning in Hyde Park.”\n\n“You may tell my lord,” answered the squire, “that I am busy and cannot come. I have enough to look after at home, and can't stir abroad on any account.”\n\n“I am sure, sir,” quoth the other, “you are too much a gentleman to send such a message; you will not, I am convinced, have it said of you, that, after having affronted a noble peer, you refuse him satisfaction. His lordship would have been willing, from his great regard to the young lady, to have made up matters in another way; but unless he is to look on you as a father, his honour will not suffer his putting up such an indignity as you must be sensible you offered him.”\n\n“I offered him!” cries the squire; “it is a d--n'd lie! I never offered him anything.”\n\nUpon these words the gentleman returned a very short verbal rebuke, and this he accompanied at the same time with some manual remonstrances, which no sooner reached the ears of Mr Western, than that worthy squire began to caper very briskly about the room, bellowing at the same time with all his might, as if desirous to summon a greater number of spectators to behold his agility.\n\nThe parson, who had left great part of the tankard unfinished, was not retired far; he immediately attended therefore on the squire's vociferation, crying, “Bless me! sir, what's the matter?”--“Matter!” quoth the squire, “here's a highwayman, I believe, who wants to rob and murder me--for he hath fallen upon me with that stick there in his hand, when I wish I may be d--n'd if I gid un the least provocation.”\n\n“How, sir,” said the captain, “did you not tell me I lyed?”\n\n“No, as I hope to be saved,” answered the squire, “--I believe I might say, 'Twas a lie that I had offered any affront to my lord--but I never said the word, `you lie.'--I understand myself better, and you might have understood yourself better than to fall upon a naked man. If I had a stick in my hand, you would not have dared strike me. I'd have knocked thy lantern jaws about thy ears. Come down into yard this minute, and I'll take a bout with thee at single stick for a broken head, that I will; or I will go into naked room and box thee for a belly-full. At unt half a man, at unt, I'm sure.”\n\nThe captain, with some indignation, replied, “I see, sir, you are below my notice, and I shall inform his lordship you are below his. I am sorry I have dirtied my fingers with you.” At which words he withdrew, the parson interposing to prevent the squire from stopping him, in which he easily prevailed, as the other, though he made some efforts for the purpose, did not seem very violently bent on success. However, when the captain was departed, the squire sent many curses and some menaces after him; but as these did not set out from his lips till the officer was at the bottom of the stairs, and grew louder and louder as he was more and more remote, they did not reach his ears, or at least did not retard his departure.\n\nPoor Sophia, however, who, in her prison, heard all her father's outcries from first to last, began now first to thunder with her foot, and afterwards to scream as loudly as the old gentleman himself had done before, though in a much sweeter voice. These screams soon silenced the squire, and turned all his consideration towards his daughter, whom he loved so tenderly, that the least apprehension of any harm happening to her, threw him presently into agonies; for, except in that single instance in which the whole future happiness of her life was concerned, she was sovereign mistress of his inclinations.\n\nHaving ended his rage against the captain, with swearing he would take the law of him, the squire now mounted upstairs to Sophia, whom, as soon as he had unlocked and opened the door, he found all pale and breathless. The moment, however, that she saw her father, she collected all her spirits, and, catching him hold by the hand, she cryed passionately, “O my dear sir, I am almost frightened to death! I hope to heaven no harm hath happened to you.” “No, no,” cries the squire, “no great harm. The rascal hath not hurt me much, but rat me if I don't ha the la o' un.” “Pray, dear sir,” says she, “tell me what's the matter; who is it that hath insulted you?” “I don't know the name o' un,” answered Western; “some officer fellow, I suppose, that we are to pay for beating us; but I'll make him pay this bout, if the rascal hath got anything, which I suppose he hath not. For thof he was drest out so vine, I question whether he had got a voot of land in the world.” “But, dear sir,” cries she, “what was the occasion of your quarrel?” “What should it be, Sophy,” answered the squire, “but about you, Sophy? All my misfortunes are about you; you will be the death of your poor father at last. Here's a varlet of a lord, the Lord knows who, forsooth! who hath a taan a liking to you, and because I would not gi un my consent, he sent me a kallenge. Come, do be a good girl, Sophy, and put an end to all your father's troubles; come, do consent to ha un; he will be in town within this day or two; do but promise me to marry un as soon as he comes, and you will make me the happiest man in the world, and I will make you the happiest woman; you shall have the finest cloaths in London, and the finest jewels, and a coach and six at your command. I promised Allworthy already to give up half my estate--od rabbet it! I should hardly stick at giving up the whole.” “Will my papa be so kind,” says she, “as to hear me speak?”--“Why wout ask, Sophy?” cries he, “when dost know I had rather hear thy voice than the musick of the best pack of dogs in England.--Hear thee, my dear little girl! I hope I shall hear thee as long as I live; for if I was ever to lose that pleasure, I would not gee a brass varden to live a moment longer. Indeed, Sophy, you do not know how I love you, indeed you don't, or you never could have run away and left your poor father, who hath no other joy, no other comfort upon earth, but his little Sophy.” At these words the tears stood in his eyes; and Sophia (with the tears streaming from hers) answered, “Indeed, my dear papa, I know you have loved me tenderly, and heaven is my witness how sincerely I have returned your affection; nor could anything but an apprehension of being forced into the arms of this man have driven me to run from a father whom I love so passionately, that I would, with pleasure, sacrifice my life to his happiness; nay, I have endeavoured to reason myself into doing more, and had almost worked up a resolution to endure the most miserable of all lives, to comply with your inclination. It was that resolution alone to which I could not force my mind; nor can I ever.” Here the squire began to look wild, and the foam appeared at his lips, which Sophia, observing, begged to be heard out, and then proceeded: “If my father's life, his health, or any real happiness of his was at stake, here stands your resolved daughter; may heaven blast me if there is a misery I would not suffer to preserve you!--No, that most detested, most loathsome of all lots would I embrace. I would give my hand to Blifil for your sake.”--“I tell thee, it will preserve me,” answers the father; “it will give me health, happiness, life, everything.--Upon my soul I shall die if dost refuse me; I shall break my heart, I shall, upon my soul.”--“Is it possible,” says she, “you can have such a desire to make me miserable?”--“I tell thee noa,” answered he loudly, “d--n me if there is a thing upon earth I would not do to see thee happy.”--“And will not my dear papa allow me to have the least knowledge of what will make me so? If it be true that happiness consists in opinion, what must be my condition, when I shall think myself the most miserable of all the wretches upon earth?” “Better think yourself so,” said he, “than know it by being married to a poor bastardly vagabond.” “If it will content you, sir,” said Sophia, “I will give you the most solemn promise never to marry him, nor any other, while my papa lives, without his consent. Let me dedicate my whole life to your service; let me be again your poor Sophy, and my whole business and pleasure be, as it hath been, to please and divert you.” “Lookee, Sophy,” answered the squire, “I am not to be choused in this manner. Your aunt Western would then have reason to think me the fool she doth. No, no, Sophy, I'd have you to know I have a got more wisdom, and know more of the world, than to take the word of a woman in a matter where a man is concerned.” “How, sir, have I deserved this want of confidence?” said she; “have I ever broke a single promise to you? or have I ever been found guilty of a falsehood from my cradle?” “Lookee, Sophy,” cries he; “that's neither here nor there. I am determined upon this match, and have him you shall, d--n me if shat unt. D--n me if shat unt, though dost hang thyself the next morning.” At repeating which words he clinched his fist, knit his brows, bit his lips, and thundered so loud, that the poor afflicted, terrified Sophia sunk trembling into her chair, and, had not a flood of tears come immediately to her relief, perhaps worse had followed.\n\nWestern beheld the deplorable condition of his daughter with no more contrition or remorse than the turnkey of Newgate feels at viewing the agonies of a tender wife, when taking her last farewel of her condemned husband; or rather he looked down on her with the same emotions which arise in an honest fair tradesman, who sees his debtor dragged to prison for £10, which, though a just debt, the wretch is wickedly unable to pay. Or, to hit the case still more nearly, he felt the same compunction with a bawd, when some poor innocent, whom she hath ensnared into her hands, falls into fits at the first proposal of what is called seeing company. Indeed this resemblance would be exact, was it not that the bawd hath an interest in what she doth, and the father, though perhaps he may blindly think otherwise, can, in reality, have none in urging his daughter to almost an equal prostitution.\n\nIn this condition he left his poor Sophia, and, departing with a very vulgar observation on the effect of tears, he locked the room, and returned to the parson, who said everything he durst in behalf of the young lady, which, though perhaps it was not quite so much as his duty required, yet was it sufficient to throw the squire into a violent rage, and into many indecent reflections on the whole body of the clergy, which we have too great an honour for that sacred function to commit to paper.\n\nWhat happened to Sophia during her confinement.\n\nThe landlady of the house where the squire lodged had begun very early to entertain a strange opinion of her guests. However, as she was informed that the squire was a man of vast fortune, and as she had taken care to exact a very extraordinary price for her rooms, she did not think proper to give any offence; for, though she was not without some concern for the confinement of poor Sophia, of whose great sweetness of temper and affability the maid of the house had made so favourable a report, which was confirmed by all the squire's servants, yet she had much more concern for her own interest than to provoke one, whom, as she said, she perceived to be a very hastish kind of a gentleman.\n\nThough Sophia eat but little, yet she was regularly served with her meals; indeed, I believe, if she had liked any one rarity, that the squire, however angry, would have spared neither pains nor cost to have procured it for her; since, however strange it may appear to some of my readers, he really doated on his daughter, and to give her any kind of pleasure was the highest satisfaction of his life.\n\nThe dinner-hour being arrived, Black George carried her up a pullet, the squire himself (for he had sworn not to part with the key) attending the door. As George deposited the dish, some compliments passed between him and Sophia (for he had not seen her since she left the country, and she treated every servant with more respect than some persons shew to those who are in a very slight degree their inferiors). Sophia would have had him take the pullet back, saying, she could not eat; but George begged her to try, and particularly recommended to her the eggs, of which he said it was full.\n\nAll this time the squire was waiting at the door; but George was a great favourite with his master, as his employment was in concerns of the highest nature, namely, about the game, and was accustomed to take many liberties. He had officiously carried up the dinner, being, as he said, very desirous to see his young lady; he made therefore no scruple of keeping his master standing above ten minutes, while civilities were passing between him and Sophia, for which he received only a good-humoured rebuke at the door when he returned.\n\nThe eggs of pullets, partridges, pheasants, &c., were, as George well knew, the most favourite dainties of Sophia. It was therefore no wonder that he, who was a very good-natured fellow, should take care to supply her with this kind of delicacy, at a time when all the servants in the house were afraid she would be starved; for she had scarce swallowed a single morsel in the last forty hours.\n\nThough vexation hath not the same effect on all persons as it usually hath on a widow, whose appetite it often renders sharper than it can be rendered by the air on Bansted Downs, or Salisbury Plain; yet the sublimest grief, notwithstanding what some people may say to the contrary, will eat at last. And Sophia, herself, after some little consideration, began to dissect the fowl, which she found to be as full of eggs as George had reported it.\n\nBut, if she was pleased with these, it contained something which would have delighted the Royal Society much more; for if a fowl with three legs be so invaluable a curiosity, when perhaps time hath produced a thousand such, at what price shall we esteem a bird which so totally contradicts all the laws of animal oeconomy, as to contain a letter in its belly? Ovid tells us of a flower into which Hyacinthus was metamorphosed, that bears letters on its leaves, which Virgil recommended as a miracle to the Royal Society of his day; but no age nor nation hath ever recorded a bird with a letter in its maw.\n\nBut though a miracle of this kind might have engaged all the Académies des Sciences in Europe, and perhaps in a fruitless enquiry; yet the reader, by barely recollecting the last dialogue which passed between Messieurs Jones and Partridge, will be very easily satisfied from whence this letter came, and how it found its passage into the fowl.\n\nSophia, notwithstanding her long fast, and notwithstanding her favourite dish was there before her, no sooner saw the letter than she immediately snatched it up, tore it open, and read as follows:--\n\n“Was I not sensible to whom I have the honour of writing, I should endeavour, however difficult, to paint the horrors of my mind at the account brought me by Mrs Honour; but as tenderness alone can have any true idea of the pangs which tenderness is capable of feeling, so can this most amiable quality, which my Sophia possesses in the most eminent degree, sufficiently inform her what her Jones must have suffered on this melancholy occasion. Is there a circumstance in the world which can heighten my agonies, when I hear of any misfortune which hath befallen you? Surely there is one only, and with that I am accursed. It is, my Sophia, the dreadful consideration that I am myself the wretched cause. Perhaps I here do myself too much honour, but none will envy me an honour which costs me so extremely dear. Pardon me this presumption, and pardon me a greater still, if I ask you, whether my advice, my assistance, my presence, my absence, my death, or my tortures can bring you any relief? Can the most perfect admiration, the most watchful observance, the most ardent love, the most melting tenderness, the most resigned submission to your will, make you amends for what you are to sacrifice to my happiness? If they can, fly, my lovely angel, to those arms which are ever open to receive and protect you; and to which, whether you bring yourself alone, or the riches of the world with you, is, in my opinion, an alternative not worth regarding. If, on the contrary, wisdom shall predominate, and, on the most mature reflection, inform you, that the sacrifice is too great; and if there be no way left to reconcile your father, and restore the peace of your dear mind, but by abandoning me, I conjure you drive me for ever from your thoughts, exert your resolution, and let no compassion for my sufferings bear the least weight in that tender bosom. Believe me, madam, I so sincerely love you better than myself, that my great and principal end is your happiness. My first wish (why would not fortune indulge me in it?) was, and pardon me if I say, still is, to see you every moment the happiest of women; my second wish is, to hear you are so; but no misery on earth can equal mine, while I think you owe an uneasy moment to him who is,\n\nMadam, in every sense, and to every purpose, your devoted, THOMAS JONES.”\n\nWhat Sophia said, or did, or thought, upon this letter, how often she read it, or whether more than once, shall all be left to our reader's imagination. The answer to it he may perhaps see hereafter, but not at present: for this reason, among others, that she did not now write any, and that for several good causes, one of which was this, she had no paper, pen, nor ink.\n\nIn the evening, while Sophia was meditating on the letter she had received, or on something else, a violent noise from below disturbed her meditations. This noise was no other than a round bout at altercation between two persons. One of the combatants, by his voice, she immediately distinguished to be her father; but she did not so soon discover the shriller pipes to belong to the organ of her aunt Western, who was just arrived in town, where having, by means of one of her servants, who stopt at the Hercules Pillars, learned where her brother lodged, she drove directly to his lodgings.\n\nWe shall therefore take our leave at present of Sophia, and, with our usual good-breeding, attend her ladyship.\n\nIn which Sophia is delivered from her confinement.\n\nThe squire and the parson (for the landlord was now otherwise engaged) were smoaking their pipes together, when the arrival of the lady was first signified. The squire no sooner heard her name, than he immediately ran down to usher her upstairs; for he was a great observer of such ceremonials, especially to his sister, of whom he stood more in awe than of any other human creature, though he never would own this, nor did he perhaps know it himself.\n\nMrs Western, on her arrival in the dining-room, having flung herself into a chair, began thus to harangue: “Well, surely, no one ever had such an intolerable journey. I think the roads, since so many turnpike acts, are grown worse than ever. La, brother, how could you get into this odious place? no person of condition, I dare swear, ever set foot here before.” “I don't know,” cries the squire, “I think they do well enough; it was landlord recommended them. I thought, as he knew most of the quality, he could best shew me where to get among um.” “Well, and where's my niece?” says the lady; “have you been to wait upon Lady Bellaston yet?” “Ay, ay,” cries the squire, “your niece is safe enough; she is upstairs in chamber.” “How!” answered the lady, “is my niece in this house, and does she not know of my being here?” “No, nobody can well get to her,” says the squire, “for she is under lock and key. I have her safe; I vetched her from my lady cousin the first night I came to town, and I have taken care o' her ever since; she is as secure as a fox in a bag, I promise you.” “Good heaven!” returned Mrs Western, “what do I hear? I thought what a fine piece of work would be the consequence of my consent to your coming to town yourself; nay, it was indeed your own headstrong will, nor can I charge myself with having ever consented to it. Did not you promise me, brother, that you would take none of these headstrong measures? Was it not by these headstrong measures that you forced my niece to run away from you in the country? Have you a mind to oblige her to take such another step?” “Z--ds and the devil!” cries the squire, dashing his pipe on the ground; “did ever mortal hear the like? when I expected you would have commended me for all I have done, to be fallen upon in this manner!” “How, brother!” said the lady, “have I ever given you the least reason to imagine I should commend you for locking up your daughter? Have I not often told you that women in a free country are not to be treated with such arbitrary power? We are as free as the men, and I heartily wish I could not say we deserve that freedom better. If you expect I should stay a moment longer in this wretched house, or that I should ever own you again as my relation, or that I should ever trouble myself again with the affairs of your family, I insist upon it that my niece be set at liberty this instant.” This she spoke with so commanding an air, standing with her back to the fire, with one hand behind her, and a pinch of snuff in the other, that I question whether Thalestris, at the head of her Amazons, ever made a more tremendous figure. It is no wonder, therefore, that the poor squire was not proof against the awe which she inspired. “There,” he cried, throwing down the key, “there it is, do whatever you please. I intended only to have kept her up till Blifil came to town, which can't be long; and now if any harm happens in the mean time, remember who is to be blamed for it.”\n\n“I will answer it with my life,” cried Mrs Western, “but I shall not intermeddle at all, unless upon one condition, and that is, that you will commit the whole entirely to my care, without taking any one measure yourself, unless I shall eventually appoint you to act. If you ratify these preliminaries, brother, I yet will endeavour to preserve the honour of your family; if not, I shall continue in a neutral state.”\n\n“I pray you, good sir,” said the parson, “permit yourself this once to be admonished by her ladyship: peradventure, by communing with young Madam Sophia, she will effect more than you have been able to perpetrate by more rigorous measures.”\n\n“What, dost thee open upon me?” cries the squire: “if thee dost begin to babble, I shall whip thee in presently.”\n\n“Fie, brother,” answered the lady, “is this language to a clergyman? Mr Supple is a man of sense, and gives you the best advice; and the whole world, I believe, will concur in his opinion; but I must tell you I expect an immediate answer to my categorical proposals. Either cede your daughter to my disposal, or take her wholly to your own surprizing discretion, and then I here, before Mr Supple, evacuate the garrison, and renounce you and your family for ever.”\n\n“I pray you let me be a mediator,” cries the parson, “let me supplicate you.”\n\n“Why, there lies the key on the table,” cries the squire. “She may take un up, if she pleases: who hinders her?”\n\n“No, brother,” answered the lady, “I insist on the formality of its being delivered me, with a full ratification of all the concessions stipulated.”\n\n“Why then I will deliver it to you.--There 'tis,” cries the squire. “I am sure, sister, you can't accuse me of ever denying to trust my daughter to you. She hath a-lived wi' you a whole year and muore to a time, without my ever zeeing her.”\n\n“And it would have been happy for her,” answered the lady, “if she had always lived with me. Nothing of this kind would have happened under my eye.”\n\n“Ay, certainly,” cries he, “I only am to blame.”\n\n“Why, you are to blame, brother,” answered she. “I have been often obliged to tell you so, and shall always be obliged to tell you so. However, I hope you will now amend, and gather so much experience from past errors, as not to defeat my wisest machinations by your blunders. Indeed, brother, you are not qualified for these negociations. All your whole scheme of politics is wrong. I once more, therefore, insist, that you do not intermeddle. Remember only what is past.”----\n\n“Z--ds and bl--d, sister,” cries the squire, “what would you have me say? You are enough to provoke the devil.”\n\n“There, now,” said she, “just according to the old custom. I see, brother, there is no talking to you. I will appeal to Mr Supple, who is a man of sense, if I said anything which could put any human creature into a passion; but you are so wrongheaded every way.”\n\n“Let me beg you, madam,” said the parson, “not to irritate his worship.”\n\n“Irritate him?” said the lady; “sure, you are as great a fool as himself. Well, brother, since you have promised not to interfere, I will once more undertake the management of my niece. Lord have mercy upon all affairs which are under the directions of men! The head of one woman is worth a thousand of yours.” And now having summoned a servant to show her to Sophia, she departed, bearing the key with her.\n\nShe was no sooner gone, than the squire (having first shut the door) ejaculated twenty bitches, and as many hearty curses against her, not sparing himself for having ever thought of her estate; but added, “Now one hath been a slave so long, it would be pity to lose it at last, for want of holding out a little longer. The bitch can't live for ever, and I know I am down for it upon the will.”\n\nThe parson greatly commended this resolution: and now the squire having ordered in another bottle, which was his usual method when anything either pleased or vexed him, did, by drinking plentifully of this medicinal julap, so totally wash away his choler, that his temper was become perfectly placid and serene, when Mrs Western returned with Sophia into the room. The young lady had on her hat and capuchin, and the aunt acquainted Mr Western, “that she intended to take her niece with her to her own lodgings; for, indeed, brother,” says she, “these rooms are not fit to receive a Christian soul in.”\n\n“Very well, madam,” quoth Western, “whatever you please. The girl can never be in better hands than yours; and the parson here can do me the justice to say, that I have said fifty times behind your back, that you was one of the most sensible women in the world.”\n\n“To this,” cries the parson, “I am ready to bear testimony.”\n\n“Nay, brother,” says Mrs Western, “I have always, I'm sure, given you as favourable a character. You must own you have a little too much hastiness in your temper; but when you will allow yourself time to reflect I never knew a man more reasonable.”\n\n“Why then, sister, if you think so,” said the squire, “here's your good health with all my heart. I am a little passionate sometimes, but I scorn to bear any malice. Sophy, do you be a good girl, and do everything your aunt orders you.”\n\n“I have not the least doubt of her,” answered Mrs Western. “She hath had already an example before her eyes in the behaviour of that wretch her cousin Harriet, who ruined herself by neglecting my advice. O brother, what think you? You was hardly gone out of hearing, when you set out for London, when who should arrive but that impudent fellow with the odious Irish name--that Fitzpatrick. He broke in abruptly upon me without notice, or I would not have seen him. He ran on a long, unintelligible story about his wife, to which he forced me to give him a hearing; but I made him very little answer, and delivered him the letter from his wife, which I bid him answer himself. I suppose the wretch will endeavour to find us out, but I beg you will not see her, for I am determined I will not.”\n\n“I zee her!” answered the squire; “you need not fear me. I'll ge no encouragement to such undutiful wenches. It is well for the fellow, her husband, I was not at huome. Od rabbit it, he should have taken a dance thru the horse-pond, I promise un. You zee, Sophy, what undutifulness brings volks to. You have an example in your own family.”\n\n“Brother,” cries the aunt, “you need not shock my niece by such odious repetitions. Why will you not leave everything entirely to me?” “Well, well, I wull, I wull,” said the squire.\n\nAnd now Mrs Western, luckily for Sophia, put an end to the conversation by ordering chairs to be called. I say luckily, for had it continued much longer, fresh matter of dissension would, most probably, have arisen between the brother and sister; between whom education and sex made the only difference; for both were equally violent and equally positive: they had both a vast affection for Sophia, and both a sovereign contempt for each other.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\nIn which Jones receives a letter from Sophia, and goes to a play with Mrs Miller and Partridge.\n\nThe arrival of Black George in town, and the good offices which that grateful fellow had promised to do for his old benefactor, greatly comforted Jones in the midst of all the anxiety and uneasiness which he had suffered on the account of Sophia; from whom, by the means of the said George, he received the following answer to his letter, which Sophia, to whom the use of pen, ink, and paper was restored with her liberty, wrote the very evening when she departed from her confinement:\n\n“Sir,\n\n“As I do not doubt your sincerity in what you write, you will be pleased to hear that some of my afflictions are at an end, by the arrival of my aunt Western, with whom I am at present, and with whom I enjoy all the liberty I can desire. One promise my aunt hath insisted on my making, which is, that I will not see or converse with any person without her knowledge and consent. This promise I have most solemnly given, and shall most inviolably keep: and though she hath not expressly forbidden me writing, yet that must be an omission from forgetfulness; or this, perhaps, is included in the word conversing. However, as I cannot but consider this as a breach of her generous confidence in my honour, you cannot expect that I shall, after this, continue to write myself or to receive letters, without her knowledge. A promise is with me a very sacred thing, and to be extended to everything understood from it, as well as to what is expressed by it; and this consideration may, perhaps, on reflection, afford you some comfort. But why should I mention a comfort to you of this kind; for though there is one thing in which I can never comply with the best of fathers, yet am I firmly resolved never to act in defiance of him, or to take any step of consequence without his consent. A firm persuasion of this must teach you to divert your thoughts from what fortune hath (perhaps) made impossible. This your own interest persuades you. This may reconcile, I hope, Mr Allworthy to you; and if it will, you have my injunctions to pursue it. Accidents have laid some obligations on me, and your good intentions probably more. Fortune may, perhaps, be some time kinder to us both than at present. Believe this, that I shall always think of you as I think you deserve, and am,\n\nSir, your obliged humble servant, Sophia Western.\n\n“I charge you write to me no more--at present at least; and accept this, which is now of no service to me, which I know you must want, and think you owe the trifle only to that fortune by which you found it.”[*]\n\n[*] Meaning, perhaps, the bank-bill for £100.\n\nA child who hath just learnt his letters would have spelt this letter out in less time than Jones took in reading it. The sensations it occasioned were a mixture of joy and grief; somewhat like what divide the mind of a good man when he peruses the will of his deceased friend, in which a large legacy, which his distresses make the more welcome, is bequeathed to him. Upon the whole, however, he was more pleased than displeased; and, indeed, the reader may probably wonder that he was displeased at all; but the reader is not quite so much in love as was poor Jones; and love is a disease which, though it may, in some instances, resemble a consumption (which it sometimes causes), in others proceeds in direct opposition to it, and particularly in this, that it never flatters itself, or sees any one symptom in a favourable light.\n\nOne thing gave him complete satisfaction, which was, that his mistress had regained her liberty, and was now with a lady where she might at least assure herself of a decent treatment. Another comfortable circumstance was the reference which she made to her promise of never marrying any other man; for however disinterested he might imagine his passion, and notwithstanding all the generous overtures made in his letter, I very much question whether he could have heard a more afflicting piece of news than that Sophia was married to another, though the match had been never so great, and never so likely to end in making her completely happy. That refined degree of Platonic affection which is absolutely detached from the flesh, and is, indeed, entirely and purely spiritual, is a gift confined to the female part of the creation; many of whom I have heard declare (and, doubtless, with great truth), that they would, with the utmost readiness, resign a lover to a rival, when such resignation was proved to be necessary for the temporal interest of such lover. Hence, therefore, I conclude that this affection is in nature, though I cannot pretend to say I have ever seen an instance of it.\n\nMr Jones having spent three hours in reading and kissing the aforesaid letter, and being, at last, in a state of good spirits, from the last-mentioned considerations, he agreed to carry an appointment, which he had before made, into execution. This was, to attend Mrs Miller, and her younger daughter, into the gallery at the play-house, and to admit Mr Partridge as one of the company. For as Jones had really that taste for humour which many affect, he expected to enjoy much entertainment in the criticisms of Partridge, from whom he expected the simple dictates of nature, unimproved, indeed, but likewise unadulterated, by art.\n\nIn the first row then of the first gallery did Mr Jones, Mrs Miller, her youngest daughter, and Partridge, take their places. Partridge immediately declared it was the finest place he had ever been in. When the first music was played, he said, “It was a wonder how so many fiddlers could play at one time, without putting one another out.” While the fellow was lighting the upper candles, he cried out to Mrs Miller, “Look, look, madam, the very picture of the man in the end of the common-prayer book before the gunpowder-treason service.” Nor could he help observing, with a sigh, when all the candles were lighted, “That here were candles enough burnt in one night, to keep an honest poor family for a whole twelvemonth.”\n\nAs soon as the play, which was Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, began, Partridge was all attention, nor did he break silence till the entrance of the ghost; upon which he asked Jones, “What man that was in the strange dress; something,” said he, “like what I have seen in a picture. Sure it is not armour, is it?” Jones answered, “That is the ghost.” To which Partridge replied with a smile, “Persuade me to that, sir, if you can. Though I can't say I ever actually saw a ghost in my life, yet I am certain I should know one, if I saw him, better than that comes to. No, no, sir, ghosts don't appear in such dresses as that, neither.” In this mistake, which caused much laughter in the neighbourhood of Partridge, he was suffered to continue, till the scene between the ghost and Hamlet, when Partridge gave that credit to Mr Garrick, which he had denied to Jones, and fell into so violent a trembling, that his knees knocked against each other. Jones asked him what was the matter, and whether he was afraid of the warrior upon the stage? “O la! sir,” said he, “I perceive now it is what you told me. I am not afraid of anything; for I know it is but a play. And if it was really a ghost, it could do one no harm at such a distance, and in so much company; and yet if I was frightened, I am not the only person.” “Why, who,” cries Jones, “dost thou take to be such a coward here besides thyself?” “Nay, you may call me coward if you will; but if that little man there upon the stage is not frightened, I never saw any man frightened in my life. Ay, ay: go along with you: Ay, to be sure! Who's fool then? Will you? Lud have mercy upon such fool-hardiness!--Whatever happens, it is good enough for you.----Follow you? I'd follow the devil as soon. Nay, perhaps it is the devil----for they say he can put on what likeness he pleases.--Oh! here he is again.----No farther! No, you have gone far enough already; farther than I'd have gone for all the king's dominions.” Jones offered to speak, but Partridge cried “Hush, hush! dear sir, don't you hear him?” And during the whole speech of the ghost, he sat with his eyes fixed partly on the ghost and partly on Hamlet, and with his mouth open; the same passions which succeeded each other in Hamlet, succeeding likewise in him.\n\nWhen the scene was over Jones said, “Why, Partridge, you exceed my expectations. You enjoy the play more than I conceived possible.” “Nay, sir,” answered Partridge, “if you are not afraid of the devil, I can't help it; but to be sure, it is natural to be surprized at such things, though I know there is nothing in them: not that it was the ghost that surprized me, neither; for I should have known that to have been only a man in a strange dress; but when I saw the little man so frightened himself, it was that which took hold of me.” “And dost thou imagine, then, Partridge,” cries Jones, “that he was really frightened?” “Nay, sir,” said Partridge, “did not you yourself observe afterwards, when he found it was his own father's spirit, and how he was murdered in the garden, how his fear forsook him by degrees, and he was struck dumb with sorrow, as it were, just as I should have been, had it been my own case?--But hush! O la! what noise is that? There he is again.----Well, to be certain, though I know there is nothing at all in it, I am glad I am not down yonder, where those men are.” Then turning his eyes again upon Hamlet, “Ay, you may draw your sword; what signifies a sword against the power of the devil?”\n\nDuring the second act, Partridge made very few remarks. He greatly admired the fineness of the dresses; nor could he help observing upon the king's countenance. “Well,” said he, “how people may be deceived by faces! Nulla fides fronti is, I find, a true saying. Who would think, by looking in the king's face, that he had ever committed a murder?” He then enquired after the ghost; but Jones, who intended he should be surprized, gave him no other satisfaction, than, “that he might possibly see him again soon, and in a flash of fire.”\n\nPartridge sat in a fearful expectation of this; and now, when the ghost made his next appearance, Partridge cried out, “There, sir, now; what say you now? is he frightened now or no? As much frightened as you think me, and, to be sure, nobody can help some fears. I would not be in so bad a condition as what's his name, squire Hamlet, is there, for all the world. Bless me! what's become of the spirit? As I am a living soul, I thought I saw him sink into the earth.” “Indeed, you saw right,” answered Jones. “Well, well,” cries Partridge, “I know it is only a play: and besides, if there was anything in all this, Madam Miller would not laugh so; for as to you, sir, you would not be afraid, I believe, if the devil was here in person.--There, there--Ay, no wonder you are in such a passion, shake the vile wicked wretch to pieces. If she was my own mother, I would serve her so. To be sure all duty to a mother is forfeited by such wicked doings.----Ay, go about your business, I hate the sight of you.”\n\nOur critic was now pretty silent till the play, which Hamlet introduces before the king. This he did not at first understand, till Jones explained it to him; but he no sooner entered into the spirit of it, than he began to bless himself that he had never committed murder. Then turning to Mrs Miller, he asked her, “If she did not imagine the king looked as if he was touched; though he is,” said he, “a good actor, and doth all he can to hide it. Well, I would not have so much to answer for, as that wicked man there hath, to sit upon a much higher chair than he sits upon. No wonder he ran away; for your sake I'll never trust an innocent face again.”\n\nThe grave-digging scene next engaged the attention of Partridge, who expressed much surprize at the number of skulls thrown upon the stage. To which Jones answered, “That it was one of the most famous burial-places about town.” “No wonder then,” cries Partridge, “that the place is haunted. But I never saw in my life a worse grave-digger. I had a sexton, when I was clerk, that should have dug three graves while he is digging one. The fellow handles a spade as if it was the first time he had ever had one in his hand. Ay, ay, you may sing. You had rather sing than work, I believe.”--Upon Hamlet's taking up the skull, he cried out, “Well! it is strange to see how fearless some men are: I never could bring myself to touch anything belonging to a dead man, on any account.--He seemed frightened enough too at the ghost, I thought. Nemo omnibus horis sapit.”\n\nLittle more worth remembering occurred during the play, at the end of which Jones asked him, “Which of the players he had liked best?” To this he answered, with some appearance of indignation at the question, “The king, without doubt.” “Indeed, Mr Partridge,” says Mrs Miller, “you are not of the same opinion with the town; for they are all agreed, that Hamlet is acted by the best player who ever was on the stage.” “He the best player!” cries Partridge, with a contemptuous sneer, “why, I could act as well as he myself. I am sure, if I had seen a ghost, I should have looked in the very same manner, and done just as he did. And then, to be sure, in that scene, as you called it, between him and his mother, where you told me he acted so fine, why, Lord help me, any man, that is, any good man, that had such a mother, would have done exactly the same. I know you are only joking with me; but indeed, madam, though I was never at a play in London, yet I have seen acting before in the country; and the king for my money; he speaks all his words distinctly, half as loud again as the other.--Anybody may see he is an actor.”\n\nWhile Mrs Miller was thus engaged in conversation with Partridge, a lady came up to Mr Jones, whom he immediately knew to be Mrs Fitzpatrick. She said, she had seen him from the other part of the gallery, and had taken that opportunity of speaking to him, as she had something to say, which might be of great service to himself. She then acquainted him with her lodgings, and made him an appointment the next day in the morning; which, upon recollection, she presently changed to the afternoon; at which time Jones promised to attend her.\n\nThus ended the adventure at the playhouse; where Partridge had afforded great mirth, not only to Jones and Mrs Miller, but to all who sat within hearing, who were more attentive to what he said, than to anything that passed on the stage.\n\nHe durst not go to bed all that night, for fear of the ghost; and for many nights after sweated two or three hours before he went to sleep, with the same apprehensions, and waked several times in great horrors, crying out, “Lord have mercy upon us! there it is.”\n\nIn which the history is obliged to look back.\nIt is almost impossible for the best parent to observe an exact impartiality to his children, even though no superior merit should bias his affection; but sure a parent can hardly be blamed, when that superiority determines his preference.\n\nAs I regard all the personages of this history in the light of my children; so I must confess the same inclination of partiality to Sophia; and for that I hope the reader will allow me the same excuse, from the superiority of her character.\n\nThis extraordinary tenderness which I have for my heroine never suffers me to quit her any long time without the utmost reluctance. I could now, therefore, return impatiently to enquire what hath happened to this lovely creature since her departure from her father's, but that I am obliged first to pay a short visit to Mr Blifil.\n\nMr Western, in the first confusion into which his mind was cast upon the sudden news he received of his daughter, and in the first hurry to go after her, had not once thought of sending any account of the discovery to Blifil. He had not gone far, however, before he recollected himself, and accordingly stopt at the very first inn he came to, and dispatched away a messenger to acquaint Blifil with his having found Sophia, and with his firm resolution to marry her to him immediately, if he would come up after him to town.\n\nAs the love which Blifil had for Sophia was of that violent kind, which nothing but the loss of her fortune, or some such accident, could lessen, his inclination to the match was not at all altered by her having run away, though he was obliged to lay this to his own account. He very readily, therefore, embraced this offer. Indeed, he now proposed the gratification of a very strong passion besides avarice, by marrying this young lady, and this was hatred; for he concluded that matrimony afforded an equal opportunity of satisfying either hatred or love; and this opinion is very probably verified by much experience. To say the truth, if we are to judge by the ordinary behaviour of married persons to each other, we shall perhaps be apt to conclude that the generality seek the indulgence of the former passion only, in their union of everything but of hearts.\n\nThere was one difficulty, however, in his way, and this arose from Mr Allworthy. That good man, when he found by the departure of Sophia (for neither that, nor the cause of it, could be concealed from him), the great aversion which she had for his nephew, began to be seriously concerned that he had been deceived into carrying matters so far. He by no means concurred with the opinion of those parents, who think it as immaterial to consult the inclinations of their children in the affair of marriage, as to solicit the good pleasure of their servants when they intend to take a journey; and who are by law, or decency at least, withheld often from using absolute force. On the contrary, as he esteemed the institution to be of the most sacred kind, he thought every preparatory caution necessary to preserve it holy and inviolate; and very wisely concluded, that the surest way to effect this was by laying the foundation in previous affection.\n\nBlifil indeed soon cured his uncle of all anger on the score of deceit, by many vows and protestations that he had been deceived himself, with which the many declarations of Western very well tallied; but now to persuade Allworthy to consent to the renewing his addresses was a matter of such apparent difficulty, that the very appearance was sufficient to have deterred a less enterprizing genius; but this young gentleman so well knew his own talents, that nothing within the province of cunning seemed to him hard to be achieved.\n\nHere then he represented the violence of his own affection, and the hopes of subduing aversion in the lady by perseverance. He begged that, in an affair on which depended all his future repose, he might at least be at liberty to try all fair means for success. Heaven forbid, he said, that he should ever think of prevailing by any other than the most gentle methods! “Besides, sir,” said he, “if they fail, you may then (which will be surely time enough) deny your consent.” He urged the great and eager desire which Mr Western had for the match; and lastly, he made great use of the name of Jones, to whom he imputed all that had happened; and from whom, he said, to preserve so valuable a young lady was even an act of charity.\n\nAll these arguments were well seconded by Thwackum, who dwelt a little stronger on the authority of parents than Mr Blifil himself had done. He ascribed the measures which Mr Blifil was desirous to take to Christian motives; “and though,” says he, “the good young gentleman hath mentioned charity last, I am almost convinced it is his first and principal consideration.”\n\nSquare, possibly, had he been present, would have sung to the same tune, though in a different key, and would have discovered much moral fitness in the proceeding: but he was now gone to Bath for the recovery of his health.\n\nAllworthy, though not without reluctance, at last yielded to the desires of his nephew. He said he would accompany him to London, where he might be at liberty to use every honest endeavour to gain the lady: “But I declare,” said he, “I will never give my consent to any absolute force being put on her inclinations, nor shall you ever have her, unless she can be brought freely to compliance.”\n\nThus did the affection of Allworthy for his nephew betray the superior understanding to be triumphed over by the inferior; and thus is the prudence of the best of heads often defeated by the tenderness of the best of hearts.\n\nBlifil, having obtained this unhoped-for acquiescence in his uncle, rested not till he carried his purpose into execution. And as no immediate business required Mr Allworthy's presence in the country, and little preparation is necessary to men for a journey, they set out the very next day, and arrived in town that evening, when Mr Jones, as we have seen, was diverting himself with Partridge at the play.\n\nThe morning after his arrival Mr Blifil waited on Mr Western, by whom he was most kindly and graciously received, and from whom he had every possible assurance (perhaps more than was possible) that he should very shortly be as happy as Sophia could make him; nor would the squire suffer the young gentleman to return to his uncle till he had, almost against his will, carried him to his sister.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\nIn which Mr Western pays a visit to his sister, in company with Mr Blifil.\nMrs Western was reading a lecture on prudence, and matrimonial politics, to her niece, when her brother and Blifil broke in with less ceremony than the laws of visiting require. Sophia no sooner saw Blifil than she turned pale, and almost lost the use of all her faculties; but her aunt, on the contrary, waxed red, and, having all her faculties at command, began to exert her tongue on the squire.\n\n“Brother,” said she, “I am astonished at your behaviour; will you never learn any regard to decorum? Will you still look upon every apartment as your own, or as belonging to one of your country tenants? Do you think yourself at liberty to invade the privacies of women of condition, without the least decency or notice?”----“Why, what a pox is the matter now?” quoth the squire; “one would think I had caught you at--“--“None of your brutality, sir, I beseech you,” answered she.----“You have surprized my poor niece so, that she can hardly, I see, support herself.----Go, my dear, retire, and endeavour to recruit your spirits; for I see you have occasion.” At which words Sophia, who never received a more welcome command, hastily withdrew.\n\n“To be sure, sister,” cries the squire, “you are mad, when I have brought Mr Blifil here to court her, to force her away.”\n\n“Sure, brother,” says she, “you are worse than mad, when you know in what situation affairs are, to----I am sure I ask Mr Blifil's pardon, but he knows very well to whom to impute so disagreeable a reception. For my own part, I am sure I shall always be very glad to see Mr Blifil; but his own good sense would not have suffered him to proceed so abruptly, had you not compelled him to it.”\n\nBlifil bowed and stammered, and looked like a fool; but Western, without giving him time to form a speech for the purpose, answered, “Well, well, I am to blame, if you will, I always am, certainly; but come, let the girl be fetched back again, or let Mr Blifil go to her.----He's come up on purpose, and there is no time to be lost.”\n\n“Brother,” cries Mrs Western, “Mr Blifil, I am confident, understands himself better than to think of seeing my niece any more this morning, after what hath happened. Women are of a nice contexture; and our spirits, when disordered, are not to be recomposed in a moment. Had you suffered Mr Blifil to have sent his compliments to my niece, and to have desired the favour of waiting on her in the afternoon, I should possibly have prevailed on her to have seen him; but now I despair of bringing about any such matter.”\n\n“I am very sorry, madam,” cried Blifil, “that Mr Western's extraordinary kindness to me, which I can never enough acknowledge, should have occasioned--” “Indeed, sir,” said she, interrupting him, “you need make no apologies, we all know my brother so well.”\n\n“I don't care what anybody knows of me,” answered the squire;----“but when must he come to see her? for, consider, I tell you, he is come up on purpose, and so is Allworthy.”--“Brother,” said she, “whatever message Mr Blifil thinks proper to send to my niece shall be delivered to her; and I suppose she will want no instructions to make a proper answer. I am convinced she will not refuse to see Mr Blifil at a proper time.”--“The devil she won't!” answered the squire.--“Odsbud!--Don't we know--I say nothing, but some volk are wiser than all the world.----If I might have had my will, she had not run away before: and now I expect to hear every moment she is guone again. For as great a fool as some volk think me, I know very well she hates----” “No matter, brother,” replied Mrs Western, “I will not hear my niece abused. It is a reflection on my family. She is an honour to it; and she will be an honour to it, I promise you. I will pawn my whole reputation in the world on her conduct.----I shall be glad to see you, brother, in the afternoon; for I have somewhat of importance to mention to you.--At present, Mr Blifil, as well as you, must excuse me; for I am in haste to dress.” “Well, but,” said the squire, “do appoint a time.” “Indeed,” said she, “I can appoint no time. I tell you I will see you in the afternoon.”--“What the devil would you have me do?” cries the squire, turning to Blifil; “I can no more turn her, than a beagle can turn an old hare. Perhaps she will be in a better humour in the afternoon.”--“I am condemned, I see, sir, to misfortune,” answered Blifil; “but I shall always own my obligations to you.” He then took a ceremonious leave of Mrs Western, who was altogether as ceremonious on her part; and then they departed, the squire muttering to himself with an oath, that Blifil should see his daughter in the afternoon.\n\nIf Mr Western was little pleased with this interview, Blifil was less. As to the former, he imputed the whole behaviour of his sister to her humour only, and to her dissatisfaction at the omission of ceremony in the visit; but Blifil saw a little deeper into things. He suspected somewhat of more consequence, from two or three words which dropt from the lady; and, to say the truth, he suspected right, as will appear when I have unfolded the several matters which will be contained in the following chapter.\n\nSchemes of Lady Bellaston for the ruin of Jones.\n\nLove had taken too deep a root in the mind of Lord Fellamar to be plucked up by the rude hands of Mr Western. In the heat of resentment he had, indeed, given a commission to Captain Egglane, which the captain had far exceeded in the execution; nor had it been executed at all, had his lordship been able to find the captain after he had seen Lady Bellaston, which was in the afternoon of the day after he had received the affront; but so industrious was the captain in the discharge of his duty, that, having after long enquiry found out the squire's lodgings very late in the evening, he sat up all night at a tavern, that he might not miss the squire in the morning, and by that means missed the revocation which my lord had sent to his lodgings.\n\nIn the afternoon then next after the intended rape of Sophia, his lordship, as we have said, made a visit to Lady Bellaston, who laid open so much of the character of the squire, that his lordship plainly saw the absurdity he had been guilty of in taking any offence at his words, especially as he had those honourable designs on his daughter. He then unbosomed the violence of his passion to Lady Bellaston, who readily undertook the cause, and encouraged him with certain assurance of a most favourable reception from all the elders of the family, and from the father himself when he should be sober, and should be made acquainted with the nature of the offer made to his daughter. The only danger, she said, lay in the fellow she had formerly mentioned, who, though a beggar and a vagabond, had, by some means or other, she knew not what, procured himself tolerable cloaths, and past for a gentleman. “Now,” says she, “as I have, for the sake of my cousin, made it my business to enquire after this fellow, I have luckily found out his lodgings;” with which she then acquainted his lordship. “I am thinking, my lord,” added she “(for this fellow is too mean for your personal resentment), whether it would not be possible for your lordship to contrive some method of having him pressed and sent on board a ship. Neither law nor conscience forbid this project: for the fellow, I promise you, however well drest, is but a vagabond, and as proper as any fellow in the streets to be pressed into the service; and as for the conscientious part, surely the preservation of a young lady from such ruin is a most meritorious act; nay, with regard to the fellow himself, unless he could succeed (which Heaven forbid) with my cousin, it may probably be the means of preserving him from the gallows, and perhaps may make his fortune in an honest way.”\n\nLord Fellamar very heartily thanked her ladyship for the part which she was pleased to take in the affair, upon the success of which his whole future happiness entirely depended. He said, he saw at present no objection to the pressing scheme, and would consider of putting it in execution. He then most earnestly recommended to her ladyship to do him the honour of immediately mentioning his proposals to the family; to whom he said he offered a carte blanche, and would settle his fortune in almost any manner they should require. And after uttering many ecstasies and raptures concerning Sophia, he took his leave and departed, but not before he had received the strongest charge to beware of Jones, and to lose no time in securing his person, where he should no longer be in a capacity of making any attempts to the ruin of the young lady.\n\nThe moment Mrs Western was arrived at her lodgings, a card was despatched with her compliments to Lady Bellaston; who no sooner received it than, with the impatience of a lover, she flew to her cousin, rejoiced at this fair opportunity, which beyond her hopes offered itself, for she was much better pleased with the prospect of making the proposals to a woman of sense, and who knew the world, than to a gentleman whom she honoured with the appellation of Hottentot; though, indeed, from him she apprehended no danger of a refusal.\n\nThe two ladies being met, after very short previous ceremonials, fell to business, which was indeed almost as soon concluded as begun; for Mrs Western no sooner heard the name of Lord Fellamar than her cheeks glowed with pleasure; but when she was acquainted with the eagerness of his passion, the earnestness of his proposals, and the generosity of his offer, she declared her full satisfaction in the most explicit terms.\n\nIn the progress of their conversation their discourse turned to Jones, and both cousins very pathetically lamented the unfortunate attachment which both agreed Sophia had to that young fellow; and Mrs Western entirely attributed it to the folly of her brother's management. She concluded, however, at last, with declaring her confidence in the good understanding of her niece, who, though she would not give up her affection in favour of Blifil, will, I doubt not, says she, soon be prevailed upon to sacrifice a simple inclination to the addresses of a fine gentleman, who brings her both a title and a large estate: “For, indeed,” added she, “I must do Sophy the justice to confess this Blifil is but a hideous kind of fellow, as you know, Bellaston, all country gentlemen are, and hath nothing but his fortune to recommend him.”\n\n“Nay,” said Lady Bellaston, “I don't then so much wonder at my cousin; for I promise you this Jones is a very agreeable fellow, and hath one virtue, which the men say is a great recommendation to us. What do you think, Mrs Western--I shall certainly make you laugh; nay, I can hardly tell you myself for laughing--will you believe that the fellow hath had the assurance to make love to me? But if you should be inclined to disbelieve it, here is evidence enough, his own handwriting, I assure you.” She then delivered her cousin the letter with the proposals of marriage, which, if the reader hath a desire to see, he will find already on record in the XVth book of this history.\n\n“Upon my word I am astonished,” said Mrs Western; “this is, indeed, a masterpiece of assurance. With your leave I may possibly make some use of this letter.” “You have my full liberty,” cries Lady Bellaston, “to apply it to what purpose you please. However, I would not have it shewn to any but Miss Western, nor to her unless you find occasion.” “Well, and how did you use the fellow?” returned Mrs Western. “Not as a husband,” said the lady; “I am not married, I promise you, my dear. You know, Bell, I have tried the comforts once already; and once, I think, is enough for any reasonable woman.”\n\nThis letter Lady Bellaston thought would certainly turn the balance against Jones in the mind of Sophia, and she was emboldened to give it up, partly by her hopes of having him instantly dispatched out of the way, and partly by having secured the evidence of Honour, who, upon sounding her, she saw sufficient reason to imagine was prepared to testify whatever she pleased.\n\nBut perhaps the reader may wonder why Lady Bellaston, who in her heart hated Sophia, should be so desirous of promoting a match which was so much to the interest of the young lady. Now, I would desire such readers to look carefully into human nature, page almost the last, and there he will find, in scarce legible characters, that women, notwithstanding the preposterous behaviour of mothers, aunts, &c., in matrimonial matters, do in reality think it so great a misfortune to have their inclinations in love thwarted, that they imagine they ought never to carry enmity higher than upon these disappointments; again, he will find it written much about the same place, that a woman who hath once been pleased with the possession of a man, will go above halfway to the devil, to prevent any other woman from enjoying the same.\n\nIf he will not be contented with these reasons, I freely confess I see no other motive to the actions of that lady, unless we will conceive she was bribed by Lord Fellamar, which for my own part I see no cause to suspect.\n\nNow this was the affair which Mrs Western was preparing to introduce to Sophia, by some prefatory discourse on the folly of love, and on the wisdom of legal prostitution for hire, when her brother and Blifil broke abruptly in upon her; and hence arose all that coldness in her behaviour to Blifil, which, though the squire, as was usual with him, imputed to a wrong cause, infused into Blifil himself (he being a much more cunning man) a suspicion of the real truth.\n\nIn which Jones pays a visit to Mrs Fitzpatrick.\n\nThe reader may now, perhaps, be pleased to return with us to Mr Jones, who, at the appointed hour, attended on Mrs Fitzpatrick; but before we relate the conversation which now past it may be proper, according to our method, to return a little back, and to account for so great an alteration of behaviour in this lady, that from changing her lodging principally to avoid Mr Jones, she had now industriously, as hath been seen, sought this interview.\n\nAnd here we shall need only to resort to what happened the preceding day, when, hearing from Lady Bellaston that Mr Western was arrived in town, she went to pay her duty to him, at his lodgings at Piccadilly, where she was received with many scurvy compellations too coarse to be repeated, and was even threatened to be kicked out of doors. From hence, an old servant of her aunt Western, with whom she was well acquainted, conducted her to the lodgings of that lady, who treated her not more kindly, but more politely; or, to say the truth, with rudeness in another way. In short, she returned from both, plainly convinced, not only that her scheme of reconciliation had proved abortive, but that she must for ever give over all thoughts of bringing it about by any means whatever. From this moment desire of revenge only filled her mind; and in this temper meeting Jones at the play, an opportunity seemed to her to occur of effecting this purpose.\n\nThe reader must remember that he was acquainted by Mrs Fitzpatrick, in the account she gave of her own story, with the fondness Mrs Western had formerly shewn for Mr Fitzpatrick at Bath, from the disappointment of which Mrs Fitzpatrick derived the great bitterness her aunt had expressed toward her. She had, therefore, no doubt but that the good lady would as easily listen to the addresses of Mr Jones as she had before done to the other; for the superiority of charms was clearly on the side of Mr Jones; and the advance which her aunt had since made in age, she concluded (how justly I will not say), was an argument rather in favour of her project than against it.\n\nTherefore, when Jones attended, after a previous declaration of her desire of serving him, arising, as she said, from a firm assurance how much she should by so doing oblige Sophia; and after some excuses for her former disappointment, and after acquainting Mr Jones in whose custody his mistress was, of which she thought him ignorant; she very explicitly mentioned her scheme to him, and advised him to make sham addresses to the older lady, in order to procure an easy access to the younger, informing him at the same time of the success which Mr Fitzpatrick had formerly owed to the very same stratagem.\n\nMr Jones expressed great gratitude to the lady for the kind intentions towards him which she had expressed, and indeed testified, by this proposal; but, besides intimating some diffidence of success from the lady's knowledge of his love to her niece, which had not been her case in regard to Mr Fitzpatrick, he said, he was afraid Miss Western would never agree to an imposition of this kind, as well from her utter detestation of all fallacy as from her avowed duty to her aunt.\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick was a little nettled at this; and indeed, if it may not be called a lapse of the tongue, it was a small deviation from politeness in Jones, and into which he scarce would have fallen, had not the delight he felt in praising Sophia hurried him out of all reflection; for this commendation of one cousin was more than a tacit rebuke on the other.\n\n“Indeed, sir,” answered the lady, with some warmth, “I cannot think there is anything easier than to cheat an old woman with a profession of love, when her complexion is amorous; and, though she is my aunt, I must say there never was a more liquorish one than her ladyship. Can't you pretend that the despair of possessing her niece, from her being promised to Blifil, has made you turn your thoughts towards her? As to my cousin Sophia, I can't imagine her to be such a simpleton as to have the least scruple on such an account, or to conceive any harm in punishing one of these haggs for the many mischiefs they bring upon families by their tragi-comic passions; for which I think it is a pity they are not punishable by law. I had no such scruple myself; and yet I hope my cousin Sophia will not think it an affront when I say she cannot detest every real species of falsehood more than her cousin Fitzpatrick. To my aunt, indeed, I pretend no duty, nor doth she deserve any. However, sir, I have given you my advice; and if you decline pursuing it, I shall have the less opinion of your understanding--that's all.”\n\nJones now clearly saw the error he had committed, and exerted his utmost power to rectify it; but he only faultered and stuttered into nonsense and contradiction. To say the truth, it is often safer to abide by the consequences of the first blunder than to endeavour to rectify it; for by such endeavours we generally plunge deeper instead of extricating ourselves; and few persons will on such occasions have the good-nature which Mrs Fitzpatrick displayed to Jones, by saying, with a smile, “You need attempt no more excuses; for I can easily forgive a real lover, whatever is the effect of fondness for his mistress.”\n\nShe then renewed her proposal, and very fervently recommended it, omitting no argument which her invention could suggest on the subject; for she was so violently incensed against her aunt, that scarce anything was capable of affording her equal pleasure with exposing her; and, like a true woman, she would see no difficulties in the execution of a favourite scheme.\n\nJones, however, persisted in declining the undertaking, which had not, indeed, the least probability of success. He easily perceived the motives which induced Mrs Fitzpatrick to be so eager in pressing her advice. He said he would not deny the tender and passionate regard he had for Sophia; but was so conscious of the inequality of their situations, that he could never flatter himself so far as to hope that so divine a young lady would condescend to think on so unworthy a man; nay, he protested, he could scarce bring himself to wish she should. He concluded with a profession of generous sentiments, which we have not at present leisure to insert.\n\nThere are some fine women (for I dare not here speak in too general terms) with whom self is so predominant, that they never detach it from any subject; and, as vanity is with them a ruling principle, they are apt to lay hold of whatever praise they meet with; and, though the property of others, convey it to their own use. In the company of these ladies it is impossible to say anything handsome of another woman which they will not apply to themselves; nay, they often improve the praise they seize; as, for instance, if her beauty, her wit, her gentility, her good humour deserve so much commendation, what do I deserve, who possess those qualities in so much more eminent a degree?\n\nTo these ladies a man often recommends himself while he is commending another woman; and, while he is expressing ardour and generous sentiments for his mistress, they are considering what a charming lover this man would make to them, who can feel all this tenderness for an inferior degree of merit. Of this, strange as it may seem, I have seen many instances besides Mrs Fitzpatrick, to whom all this really happened, and who now began to feel a somewhat for Mr Jones, the symptoms of which she much sooner understood than poor Sophia had formerly done.\n\nTo say the truth, perfect beauty in both sexes is a more irresistible object than it is generally thought; for, notwithstanding some of us are contented with more homely lots, and learn by rote (as children to repeat what gives them no idea) to despise outside, and to value more solid charms; yet I have always observed, at the approach of consummate beauty, that these more solid charms only shine with that kind of lustre which the stars have after the rising of the sun.\n\nWhen Jones had finished his exclamations, many of which would have become the mouth of Oroöndates himself, Mrs Fitzpatrick heaved a deep sigh, and, taking her eyes off from Jones, on whom they had been some time fixed, and dropping them on the ground, she cried, “Indeed, Mr Jones, I pity you; but it is the curse of such tenderness to be thrown away on those who are insensible of it. I know my cousin better than you, Mr Jones, and I must say, any woman who makes no return to such a passion, and such a person, is unworthy of both.”\n\n“Sure, madam,” said Jones, “you can't mean----” “Mean!” cries Mrs Fitzpatrick, “I know not what I mean; there is something, I think, in true tenderness bewitching; few women ever meet with it in men, and fewer still know how to value it when they do. I never heard such truly noble sentiments, and I can't tell how it is, but you force one to believe you. Sure she must be the most contemptible of women who can overlook such merit.”\n\nThe manner and look with which all this was spoke infused a suspicion into Jones which we don't care to convey in direct words to the reader. Instead of making any answer, he said, “I am afraid, madam, I have made too tiresome a visit;” and offered to take his leave.\n\n“Not at all, sir,” answered Mrs Fitzpatrick.--“Indeed I pity you, Mr Jones; indeed I do: but if you are going, consider of the scheme I have mentioned--I am convinced you will approve it--and let me see you again as soon as you can.--To-morrow morning if you will, or at least some time to-morrow. I shall be at home all day.”\n\nJones, then, after many expressions of thanks, very respectfully retired; nor could Mrs Fitzpatrick forbear making him a present of a look at parting, by which if he had understood nothing, he must have had no understanding in the language of the eyes. In reality, it confirmed his resolution of returning to her no more; for, faulty as he hath hitherto appeared in this history, his whole thoughts were now so confined to his Sophia, that I believe no woman upon earth could have now drawn him into an act of inconstancy.\n\nFortune, however, who was not his friend, resolved, as he intended to give her no second opportunity, to make the best of this; and accordingly produced the tragical incident which we are now in sorrowful notes to record.\n\nThe consequence of the preceding visit.\n\nMr Fitzpatrick having received the letter before mentioned from Mrs Western, and being by that means acquainted with the place to which his wife was retired, returned directly to Bath, and thence the day after set forward to London.\n\nThe reader hath been already often informed of the jealous temper of this gentleman. He may likewise be pleased to remember the suspicion which he had conceived of Jones at Upton, upon his finding him in the room with Mrs Waters; and, though sufficient reasons had afterwards appeared entirely to clear up that suspicion, yet now the reading so handsome a character of Mr Jones from his wife, caused him to reflect that she likewise was in the inn at the same time, and jumbled together such a confusion of circumstances in a head which was naturally none of the clearest, that the whole produced that green-eyed monster mentioned by Shakespear in his tragedy of Othello.\n\nAnd now, as he was enquiring in the street after his wife, and had just received directions to the door, unfortunately Mr Jones was issuing from it.\n\nFitzpatrick did not yet recollect the face of Jones; however, seeing a young well-dressed fellow coming from his wife, he made directly up to him, and asked him what he had been doing in that house? “for I am sure,” said he, “you must have been in it, as I saw you come out of it.”\n\nJones answered very modestly, “That he had been visiting a lady there.” To which Fitzpatrick replied, “What business have you with the lady?” Upon which Jones, who now perfectly remembered the voice, features, and indeed coat, of the gentleman, cried out----“Ha, my good friend! give me your hand; I hope there is no ill blood remaining between us, upon a small mistake which happened so long ago.”\n\n“Upon my soul, sir,” said Fitzpatrick, “I don't know your name nor your face.” “Indeed, sir,” said Jones, “neither have I the pleasure of knowing your name, but your face I very well remember to have seen before at Upton, where a foolish quarrel happened between us, which, if it is not made up yet, we will now make up over a bottle.”\n\n“At Upton!” cried the other;----“Ha! upon my soul, I believe your name is Jones?” “Indeed,” answered he, “it is.”--“O! upon my soul,” cries Fitzpatrick, “you are the very man I wanted to meet.--Upon my soul I will drink a bottle with you presently; but first I will give you a great knock over the pate. There is for you, you rascal. Upon my soul, if you do not give me satisfaction for that blow, I will give you another.” And then, drawing his sword, put himself in a posture of defence, which was the only science he understood.\n\nJones was a little staggered by the blow, which came somewhat unexpectedly; but presently recovering himself he also drew, and though he understood nothing of fencing, prest on so boldly upon Fitzpatrick, that he beat down his guard, and sheathed one half of his sword in the body of the said gentleman, who had no sooner received it than he stept backwards, dropped the point of his sword, and leaning upon it, cried, “I have satisfaction enough: I am a dead man.”\n\n“I hope not,” cries Jones, “but whatever be the consequence, you must be sensible you have drawn it upon yourself.” At this instant a number of fellows rushed in and seized Jones, who told them he should make no resistance, and begged some of them at least would take care of the wounded gentleman.\n\n“Ay,” cries one of the fellows, “the wounded gentleman will be taken care enough of; for I suppose he hath not many hours to live. As for you, sir, you have a month at least good yet.” “D--n me, Jack,” said another, “he hath prevented his voyage; he's bound to another port now;” and many other such jests was our poor Jones made the subject of by these fellows, who were indeed the gang employed by Lord Fellamar, and had dogged him into the house of Mrs Fitzpatrick, waiting for him at the corner of the street when this unfortunate accident happened.\n\nThe officer who commanded this gang very wisely concluded that his business was now to deliver his prisoner into the hands of the civil magistrate. He ordered him, therefore, to be carried to a public-house, where, having sent for a constable, he delivered him to his custody.\n\nThe constable, seeing Mr Jones very well drest, and hearing that the accident had happened in a duel, treated his prisoner with great civility, and at his request dispatched a messenger to enquire after the wounded gentleman, who was now at a tavern under the surgeon's hands. The report brought back was, that the wound was certainly mortal, and there were no hopes of life. Upon which the constable informed Jones that he must go before a justice. He answered, “Wherever you please; I am indifferent as to what happens to me; for though I am convinced I am not guilty of murder in the eye of the law, yet the weight of blood I find intolerable upon my mind.”\n\nJones was now conducted before the justice, where the surgeon who dressed Mr Fitzpatrick appeared, and deposed that he believed the wound to be mortal; upon which the prisoner was committed to the Gatehouse. It was very late at night, so that Jones would not send for Partridge till the next morning; and, as he never shut his eyes till seven, so it was near twelve before the poor fellow, who was greatly frightened at not hearing from his master so long, received a message which almost deprived him of his being when he heard it.\n\nHe went to the Gatehouse with trembling knees and a beating heart, and was no sooner arrived in the presence of Jones than he lamented the misfortune that had befallen him with many tears, looking all the while frequently about him in great terror; for as the news now arrived that Mr Fitzpatrick was dead, the poor fellow apprehended every minute that his ghost would enter the room. At last he delivered him a letter, which he had like to have forgot, and which came from Sophia by the hands of Black George.\n\nJones presently dispatched every one out of the room, and, having eagerly broke open the letter, read as follows:--\n\n“You owe the hearing from me again to an accident which I own surprizes me. My aunt hath just now shown me a letter from you to Lady Bellaston, which contains a proposal of marriage. I am convinced it is your own hand; and what more surprizes me is, that it is dated at the very time when you would have me imagine you was under such concern on my account.--I leave you to comment on this fact. All I desire is, that your name may never more be mentioned to\n\nOf the present situation of Mr Jones's mind, and of the pangs with which he was now tormented, we cannot give the reader a better idea than by saying, his misery was such that even Thwackum would almost have pitied him. But, bad as it is, we shall at present leave him in it, as his good genius (if he really had any) seems to have done. And here we put an end to the sixteenth book of our history.\n\nContaining a portion of introductory writing.\n\nWhen a comic writer hath made his principal characters as happy as he can, or when a tragic writer hath brought them to the highest pitch of human misery, they both conclude their business to be done, and that their work is come to a period.\n\nHad we been of the tragic complexion, the reader must now allow we were very nearly arrived at this period, since it would be difficult for the devil, or any of his representatives on earth, to have contrived much greater torments for poor Jones than those in which we left him in the last chapter; and as for Sophia, a good-natured woman would hardly wish more uneasiness to a rival than what she must at present be supposed to feel. What then remains to complete the tragedy but a murder or two and a few moral sentences!\n\nBut to bring our favourites out of their present anguish and distress, and to land them at last on the shore of happiness, seems a much harder task; a task indeed so hard that we do not undertake to execute it. In regard to Sophia, it is more than probable that we shall somewhere or other provide a good husband for her in the end--either Blifil, or my lord, or somebody else; but as to poor Jones, such are the calamities in which he is at present involved, owing to his imprudence, by which if a man doth not become felon to the world, he is at least a felo de se; so destitute is he now of friends, and so persecuted by enemies, that we almost despair of bringing him to any good; and if our reader delights in seeing executions, I think he ought not to lose any time in taking a first row at Tyburn.\n\nThis I faithfully promise, that, notwithstanding any affection which we may be supposed to have for this rogue, whom we have unfortunately made our heroe, we will lend him none of that supernatural assistance with which we are entrusted, upon condition that we use it only on very important occasions. If he doth not therefore find some natural means of fairly extricating himself from all his distresses, we will do no violence to the truth and dignity of history for his sake; for we had rather relate that he was hanged at Tyburn (which may very probably be the case) than forfeit our integrity, or shock the faith of our reader.\n\nIn this the antients had a great advantage over the moderns. Their mythology, which was at that time more firmly believed by the vulgar than any religion is at present, gave them always an opportunity of delivering a favourite heroe. Their deities were always ready at the writer's elbow, to execute any of his purposes; and the more extraordinary the invention was, the greater was the surprize and delight of the credulous reader. Those writers could with greater ease have conveyed a heroe from one country to another, nay from one world to another, and have brought him back again, than a poor circumscribed modern can deliver him from a jail.\n\nThe Arabians and Persians had an equal advantage in writing their tales from the genii and fairies, which they believe in as an article of their faith, upon the authority of the Koran itself. But we have none of these helps. To natural means alone we are confined; let us try therefore what, by these means, may be done for poor Jones; though to confess the truth, something whispers me in the ear that he doth not yet know the worst of his fortune; and that a more shocking piece of news than any he hath yet heard remains for him in the unopened leaves of fate.\n\nMr Allworthy and Mrs Miller were just sat down to breakfast, when Blifil, who had gone out very early that morning, returned to make one of the company.\n\nHe had not been long seated before he began as follows: “Good Lord! my dear uncle, what do you think hath happened? I vow I am afraid of telling it you, for fear of shocking you with the remembrance of ever having shewn any kindness to such a villain.” “What is the matter, child?” said the uncle. “I fear I have shewn kindness in my life to the unworthy more than once. But charity doth not adopt the vices of its objects.” “O, sir!” returned Blifil, “it is not without the secret direction of Providence that you mention the word adoption. Your adopted son, sir, that Jones, that wretch whom you nourished in your bosom, hath proved one of the greatest villains upon earth.” “By all that's sacred 'tis false,” cries Mrs Miller. “Mr Jones is no villain. He is one of the worthiest creatures breathing; and if any other person had called him villain, I would have thrown all this boiling water in his face.” Mr Allworthy looked very much amazed at this behaviour. But she did not give him leave to speak, before, turning to him, she cried, “I hope you will not be angry with me; I would not offend you, sir, for the world; but, indeed, I could not bear to hear him called so.” “I must own, madam,” said Allworthy, very gravely, “I am a little surprized to hear you so warmly defend a fellow you do not know.” “O! I do know him, Mr Allworthy,” said she, “indeed I do; I should be the most ungrateful of all wretches if I denied it. O! he hath preserved me and my little family; we have all reason to bless him while we live.--And I pray Heaven to bless him, and turn the hearts of his malicious enemies. I know, I find, I see, he hath such.” “You surprize me, madam, still more,” said Allworthy; “sure you must mean some other. It is impossible you should have any such obligations to the man my nephew mentions.” “Too surely,” answered she, “I have obligations to him of the greatest and tenderest kind. He hath been the preserver of me and mine. Believe me, sir, he hath been abused, grossly abused to you; I know he hath, or you, whom I know to be all goodness and honour, would not, after the many kind and tender things I have heard you say of this poor helpless child, have so disdainfully called him fellow.--Indeed, my best of friends, he deserves a kinder appellation from you, had you heard the good, the kind, the grateful things which I have heard him utter of you. He never mentions your name but with a sort of adoration. In this very room I have seen him on his knees, imploring all the blessings of heaven upon your head. I do not love that child there better than he loves you.”\n\n“I see, sir, now,” said Blifil, with one of those grinning sneers with which the devil marks his best beloved, “Mrs Miller really doth know him. I suppose you will find she is not the only one of your acquaintance to whom he hath exposed you. As for my character, I perceive, by some hints she hath thrown out, he hath been very free with it, but I forgive him.” “And the Lord forgive you, sir!” said Mrs Miller; “we have all sins enough to stand in need of his forgiveness.”\n\n“Upon my word, Mrs Miller,” said Allworthy, “I do not take this behaviour of yours to my nephew kindly; and I do assure you, as any reflections which you cast upon him must come only from that wickedest of men, they would only serve, if that were possible, to heighten my resentment against him: for I must tell you, Mrs Miller, the young man who now stands before you hath ever been the warmest advocate for the ungrateful wretch whose cause you espouse. This, I think, when you hear it from my own mouth, will make you wonder at so much baseness and ingratitude.”\n\n“You are deceived, sir,” answered Mrs Miller; “if they were the last words which were to issue from my lips, I would say you were deceived; and I once more repeat it, the Lord forgive those who have deceived you! I do not pretend to say the young man is without faults; but they are all the faults of wildness and of youth; faults which he may, nay, which I am certain he will, relinquish, and, if he should not, they are vastly overbalanced by one of the most humane, tender, honest hearts that ever man was blest with.”\n\n“Indeed, Mrs Miller,” said Allworthy, “had this been related of you, I should not have believed it.” “Indeed, sir,” answered she, “you will believe everything I have said, I am sure you will: and when you have heard the story which I shall tell you (for I will tell you all), you will be so far from being offended, that you will own (I know your justice so well), that I must have been the most despicable and most ungrateful of wretches if I had acted any other part than I have.”\n\n“Well, madam,” said Allworthy, “I shall be very glad to hear any good excuse for a behaviour which, I must confess, I think wants an excuse. And now, madam, will you be pleased to let my nephew proceed in his story without interruption. He would not have introduced a matter of slight consequence with such a preface. Perhaps even this story will cure you of your mistake.”\n\nMrs Miller gave tokens of submission, and then Mr Blifil began thus: “I am sure, sir, if you don't think proper to resent the ill-usage of Mrs Miller, I shall easily forgive what affects me only. I think your goodness hath not deserved this indignity at her hands.” “Well, child,” said Allworthy, “but what is this new instance? What hath he done of late?” “What,” cries Blifil, “notwithstanding all Mrs Miller hath said, I am very sorry to relate, and what you should never have heard from me, had it not been a matter impossible to conceal from the whole world. In short he hath killed a man; I will not say murdered--for perhaps it may not be so construed in law, and I hope the best for his sake.”\n\nAllworthy looked shocked, and blessed himself; and then, turning to Mrs Miller, he cried, “Well, madam, what say you now?”\n\n“Why, I say, sir,” answered she, “that I never was more concerned at anything in my life; but, if the fact be true, I am convinced the man, whoever he is, was in fault. Heaven knows there are many villains in this town who make it their business to provoke young gentlemen. Nothing but the greatest provocation could have tempted him; for of all the gentlemen I ever had in my house, I never saw one so gentle or so sweet-tempered. He was beloved by every one in the house, and every one who came near it.”\n\nWhile she was thus running on, a violent knocking at the door interrupted their conversation, and prevented her from proceeding further, or from receiving any answer; for, as she concluded this was a visitor to Mr Allworthy, she hastily retired, taking with her her little girl, whose eyes were all over blubbered at the melancholy news she heard of Jones, who used to call her his little wife, and not only gave her many playthings, but spent whole hours in playing with her himself.\n\nSome readers may, perhaps, be pleased with these minute circumstances, in relating of which we follow the example of Plutarch, one of the best of our brother historians; and others, to whom they may appear trivial, will, we hope, at least pardon them, as we are never prolix on such occasions.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nThe arrival of Mr Western, with some matters concerning the paternal authority.\nMrs Miller had not long left the room when Mr Western entered; but not before a small wrangling bout had passed between him and his chairmen; for the fellows, who had taken up their burden at the Hercules Pillars, had conceived no hopes of having any future good customer in the squire; and they were moreover farther encouraged by his generosity (for he had given them of his own accord sixpence more than their fare); they therefore very boldly demanded another shilling, which so provoked the squire, that he not only bestowed many hearty curses on them at the door, but retained his anger after he came into the room; swearing that all the Londoners were like the court, and thought of nothing but plundering country gentlemen. “D--n me,” says he, “if I won't walk in the rain rather than get into one of their hand-barrows again. They have jolted me more in a mile than Brown Bess would in a long fox-chase.”\n\nWhen his wrath on this occasion was a little appeased, he resumed the same passionate tone on another. “There,” says he, “there is fine business forwards now. The hounds have changed at last; and when we imagined we had a fox to deal with, od-rat it, it turns out to be a badger at last!”\n\n“Pray, my good neighbour,” said Allworthy, “drop your metaphors, and speak a little plainer.” “Why, then,” says the squire, “to tell you plainly, we have been all this time afraid of a son of a whore of a bastard of somebody's, I don't know whose, not I. And now here's a confounded son of a whore of a lord, who may be a bastard too for what I know or care, for he shall never have a daughter of mine by my consent. They have beggared the nation, but they shall never beggar me. My land shall never be sent over to Hanover.”\n\n“You surprize me much, my good friend,” said Allworthy. “Why, zounds! I am surprized myself,” answered the squire. “I went to zee sister Western last night, according to her own appointment, and there I was had into a whole room full of women. There was my lady cousin Bellaston, and my Lady Betty, and my Lady Catherine, and my lady I don't know who; d--n me, if ever you catch me among such a kennel of hoop-petticoat b--s! D--n me, I'd rather be run by my own dogs, as one Acton was, that the story-book says was turned into a hare, and his own dogs killed un and eat un. Od-rabbit it, no mortal was ever run in such a manner; if I dodged one way, one had me; if I offered to clap back, another snapped me. `O! certainly one of the greatest matches in England,' says one cousin (here he attempted to mimic them); `A very advantageous offer indeed,' cries another cousin (for you must know they be all my cousins, thof I never zeed half o' um before). `Surely,' says that fat a--se b--, my Lady Bellaston, `cousin, you must be out of your wits to think of refusing such an offer.'”\n\n“Now I begin to understand,” says Allworthy; “some person hath made proposals to Miss Western, which the ladies of the family approve, but is not to your liking.”\n\n“My liking!” said Western, “how the devil should it? I tell you it is a lord, and those are always volks whom you know I always resolved to have nothing to do with. Did unt I refuse a matter of vorty years' purchase now for a bit of land, which one o' um had a mind to put into a park, only because I would have no dealings with lords, and dost think I would marry my daughter zu? Besides, ben't I engaged to you, and did I ever go off any bargain when I had promised?”\n\n“As to that point, neighbour,” said Allworthy, “I entirely release you from any engagement. No contract can be binding between parties who have not a full power to make it at the time, nor ever afterwards acquire the power of fulfilling it.”\n\n“Slud! then,” answered Western, “I tell you I have power, and I will fulfil it. Come along with me directly to Doctors' Commons, I will get a licence; and I will go to sister and take away the wench by force, and she shall ha un, or I will lock her up, and keep her upon bread and water as long as she lives.”\n\n“Mr Western,” said Allworthy, “shall I beg you will hear my full sentiments on this matter?”--“Hear thee; ay, to be sure I will,” answered he. “Why, then, sir,” cries Allworthy, “I can truly say, without a compliment either to you or the young lady, that when this match was proposed, I embraced it very readily and heartily, from my regard to you both. An alliance between two families so nearly neighbours, and between whom there had always existed so mutual an intercourse and good harmony, I thought a most desirable event; and with regard to the young lady, not only the concurrent opinion of all who knew her, but my own observation assured me that she would be an inestimable treasure to a good husband. I shall say nothing of her personal qualifications, which certainly are admirable; her good nature, her charitable disposition, her modesty, are too well known to need any panegyric: but she hath one quality which existed in a high degree in that best of women, who is now one of the first of angels, which, as it is not of a glaring kind, more commonly escapes observation; so little indeed is it remarked, that I want a word to express it. I must use negatives on this occasion. I never heard anything of pertness, or what is called repartee, out of her mouth; no pretence to wit, much less to that kind of wisdom which is the result only of great learning and experience, the affectation of which, in a young woman, is as absurd as any of the affectations of an ape. No dictatorial sentiments, no judicial opinions, no profound criticisms. Whenever I have seen her in the company of men, she hath been all attention, with the modesty of a learner, not the forwardness of a teacher. You'll pardon me for it, but I once, to try her only, desired her opinion on a point which was controverted between Mr Thwackum and Mr Square. To which she answered, with much sweetness, `You will pardon me, good Mr Allworthy; I am sure you cannot in earnest think me capable of deciding any point in which two such gentlemen disagree.' Thwackum and Square, who both alike thought themselves sure of a favourable decision, seconded my request. She answered with the same good humour, `I must absolutely be excused: for I will affront neither so much as to give my judgment on his side.' Indeed, she always shewed the highest deference to the understandings of men; a quality absolutely essential to the making a good wife. I shall only add, that as she is most apparently void of all affectation, this deference must be certainly real.”\n\nHere Blifil sighed bitterly; upon which Western, whose eyes were full of tears at the praise of Sophia, blubbered out, “Don't be chicken-hearted, for shat ha her, d--n me, shat ha her, if she was twenty times as good.”\n\n“Remember your promise, sir,” cried Allworthy, “I was not to be interrupted.” “Well, shat unt,” answered the squire; “I won't speak another word.”\n\n“Now, my good friend,” continued Allworthy, “I have dwelt so long on the merit of this young lady, partly as I really am in love with her character, and partly that fortune (for the match in that light is really advantageous on my nephew's side) might not be imagined to be my principal view in having so eagerly embraced the proposal. Indeed, I heartily wished to receive so great a jewel into my family; but though I may wish for many good things, I would not, therefore, steal them, or be guilty of any violence or injustice to possess myself of them. Now to force a woman into a marriage contrary to her consent or approbation, is an act of such injustice and oppression, that I wish the laws of our country could restrain it; but a good conscience is never lawless in the worst regulated state, and will provide those laws for itself, which the neglect of legislators hath forgotten to supply. This is surely a case of that kind; for, is it not cruel, nay, impious, to force a woman into that state against her will; for her behaviour in which she is to be accountable to the highest and most dreadful court of judicature, and to answer at the peril of her soul? To discharge the matrimonial duties in an adequate manner is no easy task; and shall we lay this burthen upon a woman, while we at the same time deprive her of all that assistance which may enable her to undergo it? Shall we tear her very heart from her, while we enjoin her duties to which a whole heart is scarce equal? I must speak very plainly here. I think parents who act in this manner are accessories to all the guilt which their children afterwards incur, and of course must, before a just judge, expect to partake of their punishment; but if they could avoid this, good heaven! is there a soul who can bear the thought of having contributed to the damnation of his child?\n\n“For these reasons, my best neighbour, as I see the inclinations of this young lady are most unhappily averse to my nephew, I must decline any further thoughts of the honour you intended him, though I assure you I shall always retain the most grateful sense of it.”\n\n“Well, sir,” said Western (the froth bursting forth from his lips the moment they were uncorked), “you cannot say but I have heard you out, and now I expect you'll hear me; and if I don't answer every word on't, why then I'll consent to gee the matter up. First then, I desire you to answer me one question--Did not I beget her? did not I beget her? answer me that. They say, indeed, it is a wise father that knows his own child; but I am sure I have the best title to her, for I bred her up. But I believe you will allow me to be her father, and if I be, am I not to govern my own child? I ask you that, am I not to govern my own child? and if I am to govern her in other matters, surely I am to govern her in this, which concerns her most. And what am I desiring all this while? Am I desiring her to do anything for me? to give me anything?--Zu much on t'other side, that I am only desiring her to take away half my estate now, and t'other half when I die. Well, and what is it all vor? Why, is unt it to make her happy? It's enough to make one mad to hear volks talk; if I was going to marry myself, then she would ha reason to cry and to blubber; but, on the contrary, han't I offered to bind down my land in such a manner, that I could not marry if I would, seeing as narro' woman upon earth would ha me. What the devil in hell can I do more? I contribute to her damnation!--Zounds! I'd zee all the world d--n'd bevore her little vinger should be hurt. Indeed, Mr Allworthy, you must excuse me, but I am surprized to hear you talk in zuch a manner, and I must say, take it how you will, that I thought you had more sense.”\n\nAllworthy resented this reflection only with a smile; nor could he, if he would have endeavoured it, have conveyed into that smile any mixture of malice or contempt. His smiles at folly were indeed such as we may suppose the angels bestow on the absurdities of mankind.\n\nBlifil now desired to be permitted to speak a few words. “As to using any violence on the young lady, I am sure I shall never consent to it. My conscience will not permit me to use violence on any one, much less on a lady for whom, however cruel she is to me, I shall always preserve the purest and sincerest affection; but yet I have read that women are seldom proof against perseverance. Why may I not hope then by such perseverance at last to gain those inclinations, in which for the future I shall, perhaps, have no rival; for as for this lord, Mr Western is so kind to prefer me to him; and sure, sir, you will not deny but that a parent hath at least a negative voice in these matters; nay, I have heard this very young lady herself say so more than once, and declare that she thought children inexcusable who married in direct opposition to the will of their parents. Besides, though the other ladies of the family seem to favour the pretensions of my lord, I do not find the lady herself is inclined to give him any countenance; alas! I am too well assured she is not; I am too sensible that wickedest of men remains uppermost in her heart.”\n\n“Ay, ay, so he does,” cries Western.\n\n“But surely,” says Blifil, “when she hears of this murder which he hath committed, if the law should spare his life----”\n\n“What's that?” cries Western. “Murder! hath he committed a murder, and is there any hopes of seeing him hanged?--Tol de rol, tol lol de rol.” Here he fell a singing and capering about the room.\n\n“Child,” says Allworthy, “this unhappy passion of yours distresses me beyond measure. I heartily pity you, and would do every fair thing to promote your success.”\n\n“I desire no more,” cries Blifil; “I am convinced my dear uncle hath a better opinion of me than to think that I myself would accept of more.”\n\n“Lookee,” says Allworthy, “you have my leave to write, to visit, if she will permit it--but I insist on no thoughts of violence. I will have no confinement, nothing of that kind attempted.”\n\n“Well, well,” cries the squire, “nothing of that kind shall be attempted; we will try a little longer what fair means will effect; and if this fellow be but hanged out of the way--Tol lol de rol! I never heard better news in my life--I warrant everything goes to my mind.--Do, prithee, dear Allworthy, come and dine with me at the Hercules Pillars: I have bespoke a shoulder of mutton roasted, and a spare-rib of pork, and a fowl and egg-sauce. There will be nobody but ourselves, unless we have a mind to have the landlord; for I have sent Parson Supple down to Basingstoke after my tobacco-box, which I left at an inn there, and I would not lose it for the world; for it is an old acquaintance of above twenty years' standing. I can tell you landlord is a vast comical bitch, you will like un hugely.”\n\nMr Allworthy at last agreed to this invitation, and soon after the squire went off, singing and capering at the hopes of seeing the speedy tragical end of poor Jones.\n\nWhen he was gone, Mr Allworthy resumed the aforesaid subject with much gravity. He told his nephew, “He wished with all his heart he would endeavour to conquer a passion, in which I cannot,” says he, “flatter you with any hopes of succeeding. It is certainly a vulgar error, that aversion in a woman may be conquered by perseverance. Indifference may, perhaps, sometimes yield to it; but the usual triumphs gained by perseverance in a lover are over caprice, prudence, affectation, and often an exorbitant degree of levity, which excites women not over-warm in their constitutions to indulge their vanity by prolonging the time of courtship, even when they are well enough pleased with the object, and resolve (if they ever resolve at all) to make him a very pitiful amends in the end. But a fixed dislike, as I am afraid this is, will rather gather strength than be conquered by time. Besides, my dear, I have another apprehension which you must excuse. I am afraid this passion which you have for this fine young creature hath her beautiful person too much for its object, and is unworthy of the name of that love which is the only foundation of matrimonial felicity. To admire, to like, and to long for the possession of a beautiful woman, without any regard to her sentiments towards us, is, I am afraid, too natural; but love, I believe, is the child of love only; at least, I am pretty confident that to love the creature who we are assured hates us is not in human nature. Examine your heart, therefore, thoroughly, my good boy, and if, upon examination, you have but the least suspicion of this kind, I am sure your own virtue and religion will impel you to drive so vicious a passion from your heart, and your good sense will soon enable you to do it without pain.”\n\nThe reader may pretty well guess Blifil's answer; but, if he should be at a loss, we are not at present at leisure to satisfy him, as our history now hastens on to matters of higher importance, and we can no longer bear to be absent from Sophia.\n\nAn extraordinary scene between Sophia and her aunt.\n\nThe lowing heifer and the bleating ewe, in herds and flocks, may ramble safe and unregarded through the pastures. These are, indeed, hereafter doomed to be the prey of man; yet many years are they suffered to enjoy their liberty undisturbed. But if a plump doe be discovered to have escaped from the forest, and to repose herself in some field or grove, the whole parish is presently alarmed, every man is ready to set his dogs after her; and, if she is preserved from the rest by the good squire, it is only that he may secure her for his own eating.\n\nI have often considered a very fine young woman of fortune and fashion, when first found strayed from the pale of her nursery, to be in pretty much the same situation with this doe. The town is immediately in an uproar; she is hunted from park to play, from court to assembly, from assembly to her own chamber, and rarely escapes a single season from the jaws of some devourer or other; for, if her friends protect her from some, it is only to deliver her over to one of their own chusing, often more disagreeable to her than any of the rest; while whole herds or flocks of other women securely, and scarce regarded, traverse the park, the play, the opera, and the assembly; and though, for the most part at least, they are at last devoured, yet for a long time do they wanton in liberty, without disturbance or controul.\n\nOf all these paragons none ever tasted more of this persecution than poor Sophia. Her ill stars were not contented with all that she had suffered on account of Blifil, they now raised her another pursuer, who seemed likely to torment her no less than the other had done. For though her aunt was less violent, she was no less assiduous in teizing her, than her father had been before.\n\nThe servants were no sooner departed after dinner than Mrs Western, who had opened the matter to Sophia, informed her, “That she expected his lordship that very afternoon, and intended to take the first opportunity of leaving her alone with him.” “If you do, madam,” answered Sophia, with some spirit, “I shall take the first opportunity of leaving him by himself.” “How! madam!” cries the aunt; “is this the return you make me for my kindness in relieving you from your confinement at your father's?” “You know, madam,” said Sophia, “the cause of that confinement was a refusal to comply with my father in accepting a man I detested; and will my dear aunt, who hath relieved me from that distress, involve me in another equally bad?” “And do you think then, madam,” answered Mrs Western, “that there is no difference between my Lord Fellamar and Mr Blifil?” “Very little, in my opinion,” cries Sophia; “and, if I must be condemned to one, I would certainly have the merit of sacrificing myself to my father's pleasure.” “Then my pleasure, I find,” said the aunt, “hath very little weight with you; but that consideration shall not move me. I act from nobler motives. The view of aggrandizing my family, of ennobling yourself, is what I proceed upon. Have you no sense of ambition? Are there no charms in the thoughts of having a coronet on your coach?” “None, upon my honour,” said Sophia. “A pincushion upon my coach would please me just as well.” “Never mention honour,” cries the aunt. “It becomes not the mouth of such a wretch. I am sorry, niece, you force me to use these words, but I cannot bear your groveling temper; you have none of the blood of the Westerns in you. But, however mean and base your own ideas are, you shall bring no imputation on mine. I will never suffer the world to say of me that I encouraged you in refusing one of the best matches in England; a match which, besides its advantage in fortune, would do honour to almost any family, and hath, indeed, in title, the advantage of ours.” “Surely,” says Sophia, “I am born deficient, and have not the senses with which other people are blessed; there must be certainly some sense which can relish the delights of sound and show, which I have not; for surely mankind would not labour so much, nor sacrifice so much for the obtaining, nor would they be so elate and proud with possessing, what appeared to them, as it doth to me, the most insignificant of all trifles.”\n\n“No, no, miss,” cries the aunt; “you are born with as many senses as other people; but I assure you you are not born with a sufficient understanding to make a fool of me, or to expose my conduct to the world; so I declare this to you, upon my word, and you know, I believe, how fixed my resolutions are, unless you agree to see his lordship this afternoon, I will, with my own hands, deliver you to-morrow morning to my brother, and will never henceforth interfere with you, nor see your face again.” Sophia stood a few moments silent after this speech, which was uttered in a most angry and peremptory tone; and then, bursting into tears, she cryed, “Do with me, madam, whatever you please; I am the most miserable undone wretch upon earth; if my dear aunt forsakes me where shall I look for a protector?” “My dear niece,” cries she, “you will have a very good protector in his lordship; a protector whom nothing but a hankering after that vile fellow Jones can make you decline.” “Indeed, madam,” said Sophia, “you wrong me. How can you imagine, after what you have shewn me, if I had ever any such thoughts, that I should not banish them for ever? If it will satisfy you, I will receive the sacrament upon it never to see his face again.” “But, child, dear child,” said the aunt, “be reasonable; can you invent a single objection?” “I have already, I think, told you a sufficient objection,” answered Sophia. “What?” cries the aunt; “I remember none.” “Sure, madam,” said Sophia, “I told you he had used me in the rudest and vilest manner.” “Indeed, child,” answered she, “I never heard you, or did not understand you:--but what do you mean by this rude, vile manner?” “Indeed, madam,” said Sophia, “I am almost ashamed to tell you. He caught me in his arms, pulled me down upon the settee, and thrust his hand into my bosom, and kissed it with such violence that I have the mark upon my left breast at this moment.” “Indeed!” said Mrs Western. “Yes, indeed, madam,” answered Sophia; “my father luckily came in at that instant, or Heaven knows what rudeness he intended to have proceeded to.” “I am astonished and confounded,” cries the aunt. “No woman of the name of Western hath been ever treated so since we were a family. I would have torn the eyes of a prince out, if he had attempted such freedoms with me. It is impossible! sure, Sophia, you must invent this to raise my indignation against him.” “I hope, madam,” said Sophia, “you have too good an opinion of me to imagine me capable of telling an untruth. Upon my soul it is true.” “I should have stabbed him to the heart, had I been present,” returned the aunt. “Yet surely he could have no dishonourable design; it is impossible! he durst not: besides, his proposals shew he hath not; for they are not only honourable, but generous. I don't know; the age allows too great freedoms. A distant salute is all I would have allowed before the ceremony. I have had lovers formerly, not so long ago neither; several lovers, though I never would consent to marriage, and I never encouraged the least freedom. It is a foolish custom, and what I never would agree to. No man kissed more of me than my cheek. It is as much as one can bring oneself to give lips up to a husband; and, indeed, could I ever have been persuaded to marry, I believe I should not have soon been brought to endure so much.” “You will pardon me, dear madam,” said Sophia, “if I make one observation: you own you have had many lovers, and the world knows it, even if you should deny it. You refused them all, and, I am convinced, one coronet at least among them.” “You say true, dear Sophy,” answered she; “I had once the offer of a title.” “Why, then,” said Sophia, “will you not suffer me to refuse this once?” “It is true, child,” said she, “I have refused the offer of a title; but it was not so good an offer; that is, not so very, very good an offer.”--“Yes, madam,” said Sophia; “but you have had very great proposals from men of vast fortunes. It was not the first, nor the second, nor the third advantageous match that offered itself.” “I own it was not,” said she. “Well, madam,” continued Sophia, “and why may not I expect to have a second, perhaps, better than this? You are now but a young woman, and I am convinced would not promise to yield to the first lover of fortune, nay, or of title too. I am a very young woman, and sure I need not despair.” “Well, my dear, dear Sophy,” cries the aunt, “what would you have me say?” “Why, I only beg that I may not be left alone, at least this evening; grant me that, and I will submit, if you think, after what is past, I ought to see him in your company.” “Well, I will grant it,” cries the aunt. “Sophy, you know I love you, and can deny you nothing. You know the easiness of my nature; I have not always been so easy. I have been formerly thought cruel; by the men, I mean. I was called the cruel Parthenissa. I have broke many a window that has had verses to the cruel Parthenissa in it. Sophy, I was never so handsome as you, and yet I had something of you formerly. I am a little altered. Kingdoms and states, as Tully Cicero says in his epistles, undergo alterations, and so must the human form.” Thus run she on for near half an hour upon herself, and her conquests, and her cruelty, till the arrival of my lord, who, after a most tedious visit, during which Mrs Western never once offered to leave the room, retired, not much more satisfied with the aunt than with the niece; for Sophia had brought her aunt into so excellent a temper, that she consented to almost everything her niece said; and agreed that a little distant behaviour might not be improper to so forward a lover.\n\nThus Sophia, by a little well-directed flattery, for which surely none will blame her, obtained a little ease for herself, and, at least, put off the evil day. And now we have seen our heroine in a better situation than she hath been for a long time before, we will look a little after Mr Jones, whom we left in the most deplorable situation that can be well imagined.\n\nMrs Miller and Mr Nightingale visit Jones in the prison.\n\nWhen Mr Allworthy and his nephew went to meet Mr Western, Mrs Miller set forwards to her son-in-law's lodgings, in order to acquaint him with the accident which had befallen his friend Jones; but he had known it long before from Partridge (for Jones, when he left Mrs Miller, had been furnished with a room in the same house with Mr Nightingale). The good woman found her daughter under great affliction on account of Mr Jones, whom having comforted as well as she could, she set forwards to the Gatehouse, where she heard he was, and where Mr Nightingale was arrived before her.\n\nThe firmness and constancy of a true friend is a circumstance so extremely delightful to persons in any kind of distress, that the distress itself, if it be only temporary, and admits of relief, is more than compensated by bringing this comfort with it. Nor are instances of this kind so rare as some superficial and inaccurate observers have reported. To say the truth, want of compassion is not to be numbered among our general faults. The black ingredient which fouls our disposition is envy. Hence our eye is seldom, I am afraid, turned upward to those who are manifestly greater, better, wiser, or happier than ourselves, without some degree of malignity; while we commonly look downwards on the mean and miserable with sufficient benevolence and pity. In fact, I have remarked, that most of the defects which have discovered themselves in the friendships within my observation have arisen from envy only: a hellish vice; and yet one from which I have known very few absolutely exempt. But enough of a subject which, if pursued, would lead me too far.\n\nWhether it was that Fortune was apprehensive lest Jones should sink under the weight of his adversity, and that she might thus lose any future opportunity of tormenting him, or whether she really abated somewhat of her severity towards him, she seemed a little to relax her persecution, by sending him the company of two such faithful friends, and what is perhaps more rare, a faithful servant. For Partridge, though he had many imperfections, wanted not fidelity; and though fear would not suffer him to be hanged for his master, yet the world, I believe, could not have bribed him to desert his cause.\n\nWhile Jones was expressing great satisfaction in the presence of his friends, Partridge brought an account that Mr Fitzpatrick was still alive, though the surgeon declared that he had very little hopes. Upon which, Jones fetching a deep sigh, Nightingale said to him, “My dear Tom, why should you afflict yourself so upon an accident, which, whatever be the consequence, can be attended with no danger to you, and in which your conscience cannot accuse you of having been the least to blame? If the fellow should die, what have you done more than taken away the life of a ruffian in your own defence? So will the coroner's inquest certainly find it; and then you will be easily admitted to bail; and, though you must undergo the form of a trial, yet it is a trial which many men would stand for you for a shilling.” “Come, come, Mr Jones,” says Mrs Miller, “chear yourself up. I knew you could not be the aggressor, and so I told Mr Allworthy, and so he shall acknowledge too, before I have done with him.”\n\nJones gravely answered, “That whatever might be his fate, he should always lament the having shed the blood of one of his fellow-creatures, as one of the highest misfortunes which could have befallen him. But I have another misfortune of the tenderest kind----O! Mrs Miller, I have lost what I held most dear upon earth.” “That must be a mistress,” said Mrs Miller; “but come, come; I know more than you imagine” (for indeed Partridge had blabbed all); “and I have heard more than you know. Matters go better, I promise you, than you think; and I would not give Blifil sixpence for all the chance which he hath of the lady.”\n\n“Indeed, my dear friend, indeed,” answered Jones, “you are an entire stranger to the cause of my grief. If you was acquainted with the story, you would allow my case admitted of no comfort. I apprehend no danger from Blifil. I have undone myself.” “Don't despair,” replied Mrs Miller; “you know not what a woman can do; and if anything be in my power, I promise you I will do it to serve you. It is my duty. My son, my dear Mr Nightingale, who is so kind to tell me he hath obligations to you on the same account, knows it is my duty. Shall I go to the lady myself? I will say anything to her you would have me say.”\n\n“Thou best of women,” cries Jones, taking her by the hand, “talk not of obligations to me;--but as you have been so kind to mention it, there is a favour which, perhaps, may be in your power. I see you are acquainted with the lady (how you came by your information I know not), who sits, indeed, very near my heart. If you could contrive to deliver this (giving her a paper from his pocket), I shall for ever acknowledge your goodness.”\n\n“Give it me,” said Mrs Miller. “If I see it not in her own possession before I sleep, may my next sleep be my last! Comfort yourself, my good young man! be wise enough to take warning from past follies, and I warrant all shall be well, and I shall yet see you happy with the most charming young lady in the world; for I so hear from every one she is.”\n\n“Believe me, madam,” said he, “I do not speak the common cant of one in my unhappy situation. Before this dreadful accident happened, I had resolved to quit a life of which I was become sensible of the wickedness as well as folly. I do assure you, notwithstanding the disturbances I have unfortunately occasioned in your house, for which I heartily ask your pardon, I am not an abandoned profligate. Though I have been hurried into vices, I do not approve a vicious character, nor will I ever, from this moment, deserve it.”\n\nMrs Miller expressed great satisfaction in these declarations, in the sincerity of which she averred she had an entire faith; and now the remainder of the conversation past in the joint attempts of that good woman and Mr Nightingale to cheer the dejected spirits of Mr Jones, in which they so far succeeded as to leave him much better comforted and satisfied than they found him; to which happy alteration nothing so much contributed as the kind undertaking of Mrs Miller to deliver his letter to Sophia, which he despaired of finding any means to accomplish; for when Black George produced the last from Sophia, he informed Partridge that she had strictly charged him, on pain of having it communicated to her father, not to bring her any answer. He was, moreover, not a little pleased to find he had so warm an advocate to Mr Allworthy himself in this good woman, who was, in reality, one of the worthiest creatures in the world.\n\nAfter about an hour's visit from the lady (for Nightingale had been with him much longer), they both took their leave, promising to return to him soon; during which Mrs Miller said she hoped to bring him some good news from his mistress, and Mr Nightingale promised to enquire into the state of Mr Fitzpatrick's wound, and likewise to find out some of the persons who were present at the rencounter.\n\nThe former of these went directly in quest of Sophia, whither we likewise shall now attend her.\n\nIn which Mrs Miller pays a visit to Sophia.\n\nAccess to the young lady was by no means difficult; for, as she lived now on a perfect friendly footing with her aunt, she was at full liberty to receive what visitants she pleased.\n\nSophia was dressing when she was acquainted that there was a gentlewoman below to wait on her. As she was neither afraid, nor ashamed, to see any of her own sex, Mrs Miller was immediately admitted.\n\nCurtsies and the usual ceremonials between women who are strangers to each other, being past, Sophia said, “I have not the pleasure to know you, madam.” “No, madam,” answered Mrs Miller, “and I must beg pardon for intruding upon you. But when you know what has induced me to give you this trouble, I hope----” “Pray, what is your business, madam?” said Sophia, with a little emotion. “Madam, we are not alone,” replied Mrs Miller, in a low voice. “Go out, Betty,” said Sophia.\n\nWhen Betty was departed, Mrs Miller said, “I was desired, madam, by a very unhappy young gentleman, to deliver you this letter.” Sophia changed colour when she saw the direction, well knowing the hand, and after some hesitation, said--“I could not conceive, madam, from your appearance, that your business had been of such a nature.--Whomever you brought this letter from, I shall not open it. I should be sorry to entertain an unjust suspicion of any one; but you are an utter stranger to me.”\n\n“If you will have patience, madam,” answered Mrs Miller, “I will acquaint you who I am, and how I came by that letter.” “I have no curiosity, madam, to know anything,” cries Sophia; “but I must insist on your delivering that letter back to the person who gave it you.”\n\nMrs Miller then fell upon her knees, and in the most passionate terms implored her compassion; to which Sophia answered: “Sure, madam, it is surprizing you should be so very strongly interested in the behalf of this person. I would not think, madam”--“No, madam,” says Mrs Miller, “you shall not think anything but the truth. I will tell you all, and you will not wonder that I am interested. He is the best-natured creature that ever was born.”--She then began and related the story of Mr Anderson.--After this she cried, “This, madam, this is his goodness; but I have much more tender obligations to him. He hath preserved my child.”--Here, after shedding some tears, she related everything concerning that fact, suppressing only those circumstances which would have most reflected on her daughter, and concluded with saying, “Now, madam, you shall judge whether I can ever do enough for so kind, so good, so generous a young man; and sure he is the best and worthiest of all human beings.”\n\nThe alterations in the countenance of Sophia had hitherto been chiefly to her disadvantage, and had inclined her complexion to too great paleness; but she now waxed redder, if possible, than vermilion, and cried, “I know not what to say; certainly what arises from gratitude cannot be blamed--But what service can my reading this letter do your friend, since I am resolved never----” Mrs Miller fell again to her entreaties, and begged to be forgiven, but she could not, she said, carry it back. “Well, madam,” says Sophia, “I cannot help it, if you will force it upon me.--Certainly you may leave it whether I will or no.” What Sophia meant, or whether she meant anything, I will not presume to determine; but Mrs Miller actually understood this as a hint, and presently laying the letter down on the table, took her leave, having first begged permission to wait again on Sophia; which request had neither assent nor denial.\n\nThe letter lay upon the table no longer than till Mrs Miller was out of sight; for then Sophia opened and read it.\n\nThis letter did very little service to his cause; for it consisted of little more than confessions of his own unworthiness, and bitter lamentations of despair, together with the most solemn protestations of his unalterable fidelity to Sophia, of which, he said, he hoped to convince her, if he had ever more the honour of being admitted to her presence; and that he could account for the letter to Lady Bellaston in such a manner, that, though it would not entitle him to her forgiveness, he hoped at least to obtain it from her mercy. And concluded with vowing that nothing was ever less in his thoughts than to marry Lady Bellaston.\n\nThough Sophia read the letter twice over with great attention, his meaning still remained a riddle to her; nor could her invention suggest to her any means to excuse Jones. She certainly remained very angry with him, though indeed Lady Bellaston took up so much of her resentment, that her gentle mind had but little left to bestow on any other person.\n\nThat lady was most unluckily to dine this very day with her aunt Western, and in the afternoon they were all three, by appointment, to go together to the opera, and thence to Lady Thomas Hatchet's drum. Sophia would have gladly been excused from all, but would not disoblige her aunt; and as to the arts of counterfeiting illness, she was so entirely a stranger to them, that it never once entered into her head. When she was drest, therefore, down she went, resolved to encounter all the horrors of the day, and a most disagreeable one it proved; for Lady Bellaston took every opportunity very civilly and slily to insult her; to all which her dejection of spirits disabled her from making any return; and, indeed, to confess the truth, she was at the very best but an indifferent mistress of repartee.\n\nAnother misfortune which befel poor Sophia was the company of Lord Fellamar, whom she met at the opera, and who attended her to the drum. And though both places were too publick to admit of any particularities, and she was farther relieved by the musick at the one place, and by the cards at the other, she could not, however, enjoy herself in his company; for there is something of delicacy in women, which will not suffer them to be even easy in the presence of a man whom they know to have pretensions to them which they are disinclined to favour.\n\nHaving in this chapter twice mentioned a drum, a word which our posterity, it is hoped, will not understand in the sense it is here applied, we shall, notwithstanding our present haste, stop a moment to describe the entertainment here meant, and the rather as we can in a moment describe it.\n\nA drum, then, is an assembly of well-dressed persons of both sexes, most of whom play at cards, and the rest do nothing at all; while the mistress of the house performs the part of the landlady at an inn, and like the landlady of an inn prides herself in the number of her guests, though she doth not always, like her, get anything by it.\n\nNo wonder then, as so much spirits must be required to support any vivacity in these scenes of dulness, that we hear persons of fashion eternally complaining of the want of them; a complaint confined entirely to upper life. How insupportable must we imagine this round of impertinence to have been to Sophia at this time; how difficult must she have found it to force the appearance of gaiety into her looks, when her mind dictated nothing but the tenderest sorrow, and when every thought was charged with tormenting ideas!\n\nNight, however, at last restored her to her pillow, where we will leave her to soothe her melancholy at least, though incapable we fear of rest, and shall pursue our history, which, something whispers us, is now arrived at the eve of some great event.\n\nA pathetic scene between Mr Allworthy and Mrs Miller.\n\nMrs Miller had a long discourse with Mr Allworthy, at his return from dinner, in which she acquainted him with Jones's having unfortunately lost all which he was pleased to bestow on him at their separation; and with the distresses to which that loss had subjected him; of all which she had received a full account from the faithful retailer Partridge. She then explained the obligations she had to Jones; not that she was entirely explicit with regard to her daughter; for though she had the utmost confidence in Mr Allworthy, and though there could be no hopes of keeping an affair secret which was unhappily known to more than half a dozen, yet she could not prevail with herself to mention those circumstances which reflected most on the chastity of poor Nancy, but smothered that part of her evidence as cautiously as if she had been before a judge, and the girl was now on her trial for the murder of a bastard.\n\nAllworthy said, there were few characters so absolutely vicious as not to have the least mixture of good in them. “However,” says he, “I cannot deny but that you have some obligations to the fellow, bad as he is, and I shall therefore excuse what hath past already, but must insist you never mention his name to me more; for, I promise you, it was upon the fullest and plainest evidence that I resolved to take the measures I have taken.” “Well, sir,” says she, “I make not the least doubt but time will shew all matters in their true and natural colours, and that you will be convinced this poor young man deserves better of you than some other folks that shall be nameless.”\n\n“Madam,” cries Allworthy, a little ruffled, “I will not hear any reflections on my nephew; and if ever you say a word more of that kind, I will depart from your house that instant. He is the worthiest and best of men; and I once more repeat it to you, he hath carried his friendship to this man to a blameable length, by too long concealing facts of the blackest die. The ingratitude of the wretch to this good young man is what I most resent; for, madam, I have the greatest reason to imagine he had laid a plot to supplant my nephew in my favour, and to have disinherited him.”\n\n“I am sure, sir,” answered Mrs Miller, a little frightened (for, though Mr Allworthy had the utmost sweetness and benevolence in his smiles, he had great terror in his frowns), “I shall never speak against any gentleman you are pleased to think well of. I am sure, sir, such behaviour would very little become me, especially when the gentleman is your nearest relation; but, sir, you must not be angry with me, you must not indeed, for my good wishes to this poor wretch. Sure I may call him so now, though once you would have been angry with me if I had spoke of him with the least disrespect. How often have I heard you call him your son? How often have you prattled to me of him with all the fondness of a parent? Nay, sir, I cannot forget the many tender expressions, the many good things you have told me of his beauty, and his parts, and his virtues; of his good-nature and generosity. I am sure, sir, I cannot forget them, for I find them all true. I have experienced them in my own cause. They have preserved my family. You must pardon my tears, sir, indeed you must. When I consider the cruel reverse of fortune which this poor youth, to whom I am so much obliged, hath suffered; when I consider the loss of your favour, which I know he valued more than his life, I must, I must lament him. If you had a dagger in your hand, ready to plunge into my heart, I must lament the misery of one whom you have loved, and I shall ever love.”\n\nAllworthy was pretty much moved with this speech, but it seemed not to be with anger; for, after a short silence, taking Mrs Miller by the hand, he said very affectionately to her, “Come, madam, let us consider a little about your daughter. I cannot blame you for rejoicing in a match which promises to be advantageous to her, but you know this advantage, in a great measure, depends on the father's reconciliation. I know Mr Nightingale very well, and have formerly had concerns with him; I will make him a visit, and endeavour to serve you in this matter. I believe he is a worldly man; but as this is an only son, and the thing is now irretrievable, perhaps he may in time be brought to reason. I promise you I will do all I can for you.”\n\nMany were the acknowledgments which the poor woman made to Allworthy for this kind and generous offer, nor could she refrain from taking this occasion again to express her gratitude towards Jones, “to whom,” said she, “I owe the opportunity of giving you, sir, this present trouble.” Allworthy gently stopped her; but he was too good a man to be really offended with the effects of so noble a principle as now actuated Mrs Miller; and indeed, had not this new affair inflamed his former anger against Jones, it is possible he might have been a little softened towards him, by the report of an action which malice itself could not have derived from an evil motive.\n\nMr Allworthy and Mrs Miller had been above an hour together, when their conversation was put an end to by the arrival of Blifil and another person, which other person was no less than Mr Dowling, the attorney, who was now become a great favourite with Mr Blifil, and whom Mr Allworthy, at the desire of his nephew, had made his steward; and had likewise recommended him to Mr Western, from whom the attorney received a promise of being promoted to the same office upon the first vacancy; and, in the meantime, was employed in transacting some affairs which the squire then had in London in relation to a mortgage.\n\nThis was the principal affair which then brought Mr Dowling to town; therefore he took the same opportunity to charge himself with some money for Mr Allworthy, and to make a report to him of some other business; in all which, as it was of much too dull a nature to find any place in this history, we will leave the uncle, nephew, and their lawyer concerned, and resort to other matters.\n\nContaining various matters.\n\nBefore we return to Mr Jones, we will take one more view of Sophia.\n\nThough that young lady had brought her aunt into great good humour by those soothing methods which we have before related, she had not brought her in the least to abate of her zeal for the match with Lord Fellamar. This zeal was now inflamed by Lady Bellaston, who had told her the preceding evening, that she was well satisfied from the conduct of Sophia, and from her carriage to his lordship, that all delays would be dangerous, and that the only way to succeed was to press the match forward with such rapidity that the young lady should have no time to reflect, and be obliged to consent while she scarce knew what she did; in which manner, she said, one-half of the marriages among people of condition were brought about. A fact very probably true, and to which, I suppose, is owing the mutual tenderness which afterwards exists among so many happy couples.\n\nA hint of the same kind was given by the same lady to Lord Fellamar; and both these so readily embraced the advice that the very next day was, at his lordship's request, appointed by Mrs Western for a private interview between the young parties. This was communicated to Sophia by her aunt, and insisted upon in such high terms, that, after having urged everything she possibly could invent against it without the least effect, she at last agreed to give the highest instance of complacence which any young lady can give, and consented to see his lordship.\n\nAs conversations of this kind afford no great entertainment, we shall be excused from reciting the whole that past at this interview; in which, after his lordship had made many declarations of the most pure and ardent passion to the silent blushing Sophia, she at last collected all the spirits she could raise, and with a trembling low voice said, “My lord, you must be yourself conscious whether your former behaviour to me hath been consistent with the professions you now make.” “Is there,” answered he, “no way by which I can atone for madness? what I did I am afraid must have too plainly convinced you, that the violence of love had deprived me of my senses.” “Indeed, my lord,” said she, “it is in your power to give me a proof of an affection which I much rather wish to encourage, and to which I should think myself more beholden.” “Name it, madam,” said my lord, very warmly. “My lord,” says she, looking down upon her fan, “I know you must be sensible how uneasy this pretended passion of yours hath made me.” “Can you be so cruel to call it pretended?” says he. “Yes, my lord,” answered Sophia, “all professions of love to those whom we persecute are most insulting pretences. This pursuit of yours is to me a most cruel persecution: nay, it is taking a most ungenerous advantage of my unhappy situation.” “Most lovely, most adorable charmer, do not accuse me,” cries he, “of taking an ungenerous advantage, while I have no thoughts but what are directed to your honour and interest, and while I have no view, no hope, no ambition, but to throw myself, honour, fortune, everything at your feet.” “My lord,” says she, “it is that fortune and those honours which gave you the advantage of which I complain. These are the charms which have seduced my relations, but to me they are things indifferent. If your lordship will merit my gratitude, there is but one way.” “Pardon me, divine creature,” said he, “there can be none. All I can do for you is so much your due, and will give me so much pleasure, that there is no room for your gratitude.” “Indeed, my lord,” answered she, “you may obtain my gratitude, my good opinion, every kind thought and wish which it is in my power to bestow; nay, you may obtain them with ease, for sure to a generous mind it must be easy to grant my request. Let me beseech you, then, to cease a pursuit in which you can never have any success. For your own sake as well as mine I entreat this favour; for sure you are too noble to have any pleasure in tormenting an unhappy creature. What can your lordship propose but uneasiness to yourself, by a perseverance, which, upon my honour, upon my soul, cannot, shall not prevail with me, whatever distresses you may drive me to.” Here my lord fetched a deep sigh, and then said--“Is it then, madam, that I am so unhappy to be the object of your dislike and scorn; or will you pardon me if I suspect there is some other?” Here he hesitated, and Sophia answered with some spirit, “My lord, I shall not be accountable to you for the reasons of my conduct. I am obliged to your lordship for the generous offer you have made; I own it is beyond either my deserts or expectations; yet I hope, my lord, you will not insist on my reasons, when I declare I cannot accept it.” Lord Fellamar returned much to this, which we do not perfectly understand, and perhaps it could not all be strictly reconciled either to sense or grammar; but he concluded his ranting speech with saying, “That if she had pre-engaged herself to any gentleman, however unhappy it would make him, he should think himself bound in honour to desist.” Perhaps my lord laid too much emphasis on the word gentleman; for we cannot else well account for the indignation with which he inspired Sophia, who, in her answer, seemed greatly to resent some affront he had given her.\n\nWhile she was speaking, with her voice more raised than usual, Mrs Western came into the room, the fire glaring in her cheeks, and the flames bursting from her eyes. “I am ashamed,” says she, “my lord, of the reception which you have met with. I assure your lordship we are all sensible of the honour done us; and I must tell you, Miss Western, the family expect a different behaviour from you.” Here my lord interfered on behalf of the young lady, but to no purpose; the aunt proceeded till Sophia pulled out her handkerchief, threw herself into a chair, and burst into a violent fit of tears.\n\nThe remainder of the conversation between Mrs Western and his lordship, till the latter withdrew, consisted of bitter lamentations on his side, and on hers of the strongest assurances that her niece should and would consent to all he wished. “Indeed, my lord,” says she, “the girl hath had a foolish education, neither adapted to her fortune nor her family. Her father, I am sorry to say it, is to blame for everything. The girl hath silly country notions of bashfulness. Nothing else, my lord, upon my honour; I am convinced she hath a good understanding at the bottom, and will be brought to reason.”\n\nThis last speech was made in the absence of Sophia; for she had some time before left the room, with more appearance of passion than she had ever shown on any occasion; and now his lordship, after many expressions of thanks to Mrs Western, many ardent professions of passion which nothing could conquer, and many assurances of perseverance, which Mrs Western highly encouraged, took his leave for this time.\n\nBefore we relate what now passed between Mrs Western and Sophia, it may be proper to mention an unfortunate accident which had happened, and which had occasioned the return of Mrs Western with so much fury, as we have seen.\n\nThe reader then must know that the maid who at present attended on Sophia was recommended by Lady Bellaston, with whom she had lived for some time in the capacity of a comb-brush: she was a very sensible girl, and had received the strictest instructions to watch her young lady very carefully. These instructions, we are sorry to say, were communicated to her by Mrs Honour, into whose favour Lady Bellaston had now so ingratiated herself, that the violent affection which the good waiting-woman had formerly borne to Sophia was entirely obliterated by that great attachment which she had to her new mistress.\n\nNow, when Mrs Miller was departed, Betty (for that was the name of the girl), returning to her young lady, found her very attentively engaged in reading a long letter, and the visible emotions which she betrayed on that occasion might have well accounted for some suspicions which the girl entertained; but indeed they had yet a stronger foundation, for she had overheard the whole scene which passed between Sophia and Mrs Miller.\n\nMrs Western was acquainted with all this matter by Betty, who, after receiving many commendations and some rewards for her fidelity, was ordered, that, if the woman who brought the letter came again, she should introduce her to Mrs Western herself.\n\nUnluckily, Mrs Miller returned at the very time when Sophia was engaged with his lordship. Betty, according to order, sent her directly to the aunt; who, being mistress of so many circumstances relating to what had past the day before, easily imposed upon the poor woman to believe that Sophia had communicated the whole affair; and so pumped everything out of her which she knew relating to the letter and relating to Jones.\n\nThis poor creature might, indeed, be called simplicity itself. She was one of that order of mortals who are apt to believe everything which is said to them; to whom nature hath neither indulged the offensive nor defensive weapons of deceit, and who are consequently liable to be imposed upon by any one who will only be at the expense of a little falshood for that purpose. Mrs Western, having drained Mrs Miller of all she knew, which, indeed, was but little, but which was sufficient to make the aunt suspect a great deal, dismissed her with assurances that Sophia would not see her, that she would send no answer to the letter, nor ever receive another; nor did she suffer her to depart without a handsome lecture on the merits of an office to which she could afford no better name than that of procuress.--This discovery had greatly discomposed her temper, when, coming into the apartment next to that in which the lovers were, she overheard Sophia very warmly protesting against his lordship's addresses. At which the rage already kindled burst forth, and she rushed in upon her niece in a most furious manner, as we have already described, together with what past at that time till his lordship's departure.\n\nNo sooner was Lord Fellamar gone than Mrs Western returned to Sophia, whom she upbraided in the most bitter terms for the ill use she had made of the confidence reposed in her; and for her treachery in conversing with a man with whom she had offered but the day before to bind herself in the most solemn oath never more to have any conversation. Sophia protested she had maintained no such conversation. “How, how! Miss Western,” said the aunt; “will you deny your receiving a letter from him yesterday?” “A letter, madam!” answered Sophia, somewhat surprized. “It is not very well bred, miss,” replies the aunt, “to repeat my words. I say a letter, and I insist upon your showing it me immediately.” “I scorn a lie, madam,” said Sophia; “I did receive a letter, but it was without my desire, and, indeed, I may say, against my consent.” “Indeed, indeed, miss,” cries the aunt, “you ought to be ashamed of owning you had received it at all; but where is the letter? for I will see it.”\n\nTo this peremptory demand, Sophia paused some time before she returned an answer; and at last only excused herself by declaring she had not the letter in her pocket, which was, indeed, true; upon which her aunt, losing all manner of patience, asked her niece this short question, whether she would resolve to marry Lord Fellamar, or no? to which she received the strongest negative. Mrs Western then replied with an oath, or something very like one, that she would early the next morning deliver her back into her father's hand.\n\nSophia then began to reason with her aunt in the following manner:--“Why, madam, must I of necessity be forced to marry at all? Consider how cruel you would have thought it in your own case, and how much kinder your parents were in leaving you to your liberty. What have I done to forfeit this liberty? I will never marry contrary to my father's consent, nor without asking yours----And when I ask the consent of either improperly, it will be then time enough to force some other marriage upon me.” “Can I bear to hear this,” cries Mrs Western, “from a girl who hath now a letter from a murderer in her pocket?” “I have no such letter, I promise you,” answered Sophia; “and, if he be a murderer, he will soon be in no condition to give you any further disturbance.” “How, Miss Western!” said the aunt, “have you the assurance to speak of him in this manner; to own your affection for such a villain to my face?” “Sure, madam,” said Sophia, “you put a very strange construction on my words.” “Indeed, Miss Western,” cries the lady, “I shall not bear this usage; you have learnt of your father this manner of treating me; he hath taught you to give me the lie. He hath totally ruined you by this false system of education; and, please heaven, he shall have the comfort of its fruits; for once more I declare to you, that to-morrow morning I will carry you back. I will withdraw all my forces from the field, and remain henceforth, like the wise king of Prussia, in a state of perfect neutrality. You are both too wise to be regulated by my measures; so prepare yourself, for to-morrow morning you shall evacuate this house.”\n\nSophia remonstrated all she could; but her aunt was deaf to all she said. In this resolution therefore we must at present leave her, as there seems to be no hopes of bringing her to change it.\n\nWhat happened to Mr Jones in the prison.\n\nMr Jones passed about twenty-four melancholy hours by himself, unless when relieved by the company of Partridge, before Mr Nightingale returned; not that this worthy young man had deserted or forgot his friend; for, indeed, he had been much the greatest part of the time employed in his service.\n\nHe had heard, upon enquiry, that the only persons who had seen the beginning of the unfortunate rencounter were a crew belonging to a man-of-war which then lay at Deptford. To Deptford therefore he went in search of this crew, where he was informed that the men he sought after were all gone ashore. He then traced them from place to place, till at last he found two of them drinking together, with a third person, at a hedge-tavern near Aldersgate.\n\nNightingale desired to speak with Jones by himself (for Partridge was in the room when he came in). As soon as they were alone, Nightingale, taking Jones by the hand, cried, “Come, my brave friend, be not too much dejected at what I am going to tell you----I am sorry I am the messenger of bad news; but I think it my duty to tell you.” “I guess already what that bad news is,” cries Jones. “The poor gentleman then is dead.”--“I hope not,” answered Nightingale. “He was alive this morning; though I will not flatter you; I fear, from the accounts I could get, that his wound is mortal. But if the affair be exactly as you told it, your own remorse would be all you would have reason to apprehend, let what would happen; but forgive me, my dear Tom, if I entreat you to make the worst of your story to your friends. If you disguise anything to us, you will only be an enemy to yourself.”\n\n“What reason, my dear Jack, have I ever given you,” said Jones, “to stab me with so cruel a suspicion?” “Have patience,” cries Nightingale, “and I will tell you all. After the most diligent enquiry I could make, I at last met with two of the fellows who were present at this unhappy accident, and I am sorry to say, they do not relate the story so much in your favour as you yourself have told it.” “Why, what do they say?” cries Jones. “Indeed what I am sorry to repeat, as I am afraid of the consequence of it to you. They say that they were at too great a distance to overhear any words that passed between you: but they both agree that the first blow was given by you.” “Then, upon my soul,” answered Jones, “they injure me. He not only struck me first, but struck me without the least provocation. What should induce those villains to accuse me falsely?” “Nay, that I cannot guess,” said Nightingale, “and if you yourself, and I, who am so heartily your friend, cannot conceive a reason why they should belie you, what reason will an indifferent court of justice be able to assign why they should not believe them? I repeated the question to them several times, and so did another gentleman who was present, who, I believe, is a seafaring man, and who really acted a very friendly part by you; for he begged them often to consider that there was the life of a man in the case; and asked them over and over, if they were certain; to which they both answered, that they were, and would abide by their evidence upon oath. For heaven's sake, my dear friend, recollect yourself; for, if this should appear to be the fact, it will be your business to think in time of making the best of your interest. I would not shock you; but you know, I believe, the severity of the law, whatever verbal provocations may have been given you.” “Alas! my friend,” cries Jones, “what interest hath such a wretch as I? Besides, do you think I would even wish to live with the reputation of a murderer? If I had any friends (as, alas! I have none), could I have the confidence to solicit them to speak in the behalf of a man condemned for the blackest crime in human nature? Believe me, I have no such hope; but I have some reliance on a throne still greatly superior; which will, I am certain, afford me all the protection I merit.”\n\nHe then concluded with many solemn and vehement protestations of the truth of what he had at first asserted.\n\nThe faith of Nightingale was now again staggered, and began to incline to credit his friend, when Mrs Miller appeared, and made a sorrowful report of the success of her embassy; which when Jones had heard, he cried out most heroically, “Well, my friend, I am now indifferent as to what shall happen, at least with regard to my life; and if it be the will of Heaven that I shall make an atonement with that for the blood I have spilt, I hope the Divine Goodness will one day suffer my honour to be cleared, and that the words of a dying man, at least, will be believed, so far as to justify his character.”\n\nA very mournful scene now past between the prisoner and his friends, at which, as few readers would have been pleased to be present, so few, I believe, will desire to hear it particularly related. We will, therefore, pass on to the entrance of the turnkey, who acquainted Jones that there was a lady without who desired to speak with him when he was at leisure.\n\nJones declared his surprize at this message. He said, “He knew no lady in the world whom he could possibly expect to see there.” However, as he saw no reason to decline seeing any person, Mrs Miller and Mr Nightingale presently took their leave, and he gave orders to have the lady admitted.\n\nIf Jones was surprized at the news of a visit from a lady, how greatly was he astonished when he discovered this lady to be no other than Mrs Waters! In this astonishment then we shall leave him awhile, in order to cure the surprize of the reader, who will likewise, probably, not a little wonder at the arrival of this lady.\n\nWho this Mrs Waters was, the reader pretty well knows; what she was, he must be perfectly satisfied. He will therefore be pleased to remember that this lady departed from Upton in the same coach with Mr Fitzpatrick and the other Irish gentleman, and in their company travelled to Bath.\n\nNow there was a certain office in the gift of Mr Fitzpatrick at that time vacant, namely that of a wife: for the lady who had lately filled that office had resigned, or at least deserted her duty. Mr Fitzpatrick therefore, having thoroughly examined Mrs Waters on the road, found her extremely fit for the place, which, on their arrival at Bath, he presently conferred upon her, and she without any scruple accepted. As husband and wife this gentleman and lady continued together all the time they stayed at Bath, and as husband and wife they arrived together in town.\n\nWhether Mr Fitzpatrick was so wise a man as not to part with one good thing till he had secured another, which he had at present only a prospect of regaining; or whether Mrs Waters had so well discharged her office, that he intended still to retain her as principal, and to make his wife (as is often the case) only her deputy, I will not say; but certain it is, he never mentioned his wife to her, never communicated to her the letter given him by Mrs Western, nor ever once hinted his purpose of repossessing his wife; much less did he ever mention the name of Jones. For, though he intended to fight with him wherever he met him, he did not imitate those prudent persons who think a wife, a mother, a sister, or sometimes a whole family, the safest seconds on these occasions. The first account therefore which she had of all this was delivered to her from his lips, after he was brought home from the tavern where his wound had been drest.\n\nAs Mr Fitzpatrick, however, had not the clearest way of telling a story at any time, and was now, perhaps, a little more confused than usual, it was some time before she discovered that the gentleman who had given him this wound was the very same person from whom her heart had received a wound, which, though not of a mortal kind, was yet so deep that it had left a considerable scar behind it. But no sooner was she acquainted that Mr Jones himself was the man who had been committed to the Gatehouse for this supposed murder, than she took the first opportunity of committing Mr Fitzpatrick to the care of his nurse, and hastened away to visit the conqueror.\n\nShe now entered the room with an air of gaiety, which received an immediate check from the melancholy aspect of poor Jones, who started and blessed himself when he saw her. Upon which she said, “Nay, I do not wonder at your surprize; I believe you did not expect to see me; for few gentlemen are troubled here with visits from any lady, unless a wife. You see the power you have over me, Mr Jones. Indeed, I little thought, when we parted at Upton, that our next meeting would have been in such a place.” “Indeed, madam,” says Jones, “I must look upon this visit as kind; few will follow the miserable, especially to such dismal habitations.” “I protest, Mr Jones,” says she, “I can hardly persuade myself you are the same agreeable fellow I saw at Upton. Why, your face is more miserable than any dungeon in the universe. What can be the matter with you?” “I thought, madam,” said Jones, “as you knew of my being here, you knew the unhappy reason.” “Pugh!” says she, “you have pinked a man in a duel, that's all.” Jones exprest some indignation at this levity, and spoke with the utmost contrition for what had happened. To which she answered, “Well, then, sir, if you take it so much to heart, I will relieve you; the gentleman is not dead, and, I am pretty confident, is in no danger of dying. The surgeon, indeed, who first dressed him was a young fellow, and seemed desirous of representing his case to be as bad as possible, that he might have the more honour from curing him: but the king's surgeon hath seen him since, and says, unless from a fever, of which there are at present no symptoms, he apprehends not the least danger of life.” Jones shewed great satisfaction in his countenance at this report; upon which she affirmed the truth of it, adding, “By the most extraordinary accident in the world I lodge at the same house; and have seen the gentleman, and I promise you he doth you justice, and says, whatever be the consequence, that he was entirely the aggressor, and that you was not in the least to blame.”\n\nJones expressed the utmost satisfaction at the account which Mrs Waters brought him. He then informed her of many things which she well knew before, as who Mr Fitzpatrick was, the occasion of his resentment, &c. He likewise told her several facts of which she was ignorant, as the adventure of the muff, and other particulars, concealing only the name of Sophia. He then lamented the follies and vices of which he had been guilty; every one of which, he said, had been attended with such ill consequences, that he should be unpardonable if he did not take warning, and quit those vicious courses for the future. He lastly concluded with assuring her of his resolution to sin no more, lest a worse thing should happen to him.\n\nMrs Waters with great pleasantry ridiculed all this, as the effects of low spirits and confinement. She repeated some witticisms about the devil when he was sick, and told him, “She doubted not but shortly to see him at liberty, and as lively a fellow as ever; and then,” says she, “I don't question but your conscience will be safely delivered of all these qualms that it is now so sick in breeding.”\n\nMany more things of this kind she uttered, some of which it would do her no great honour, in the opinion of some readers, to remember; nor are we quite certain but that the answers made by Jones would be treated with ridicule by others. We shall therefore suppress the rest of this conversation, and only observe that it ended at last with perfect innocence, and much more to the satisfaction of Jones than of the lady; for the former was greatly transported with the news she had brought him; but the latter was not altogether so pleased with the penitential behaviour of a man whom she had, at her first interview, conceived a very different opinion of from what she now entertained of him.\n\nThus the melancholy occasioned by the report of Mr Nightingale was pretty well effaced; but the dejection into which Mrs Miller had thrown him still continued. The account she gave so well tallied with the words of Sophia herself in her letter, that he made not the least doubt but that she had disclosed his letter to her aunt, and had taken a fixed resolution to abandon him. The torments this thought gave him were to be equalled only by a piece of news which fortune had yet in store for him, and which we shall communicate in the second chapter of the ensuing book.\n\nA farewel to the reader.\n\nWe are now, reader, arrived at the last stage of our long journey. As we have, therefore, travelled together through so many pages, let us behave to one another like fellow-travellers in a stage coach, who have passed several days in the company of each other; and who, notwithstanding any bickerings or little animosities which may have occurred on the road, generally make all up at last, and mount, for the last time, into their vehicle with chearfulness and good humour; since after this one stage, it may possibly happen to us, as it commonly happens to them, never to meet more.\n\nAs I have here taken up this simile, give me leave to carry it a little farther. I intend, then, in this last book, to imitate the good company I have mentioned in their last journey. Now, it is well known that all jokes and raillery are at this time laid aside; whatever characters any of the passengers have for the jest-sake personated on the road are now thrown off, and the conversation is usually plain and serious.\n\nIn the same manner, if I have now and then, in the course of this work, indulged any pleasantry for thy entertainment, I shall here lay it down. The variety of matter, indeed, which I shall be obliged to cram into this book, will afford no room for any of those ludicrous observations which I have elsewhere made, and which may sometimes, perhaps, have prevented thee from taking a nap when it was beginning to steal upon thee. In this last book thou wilt find nothing (or at most very little) of that nature. All will be plain narrative only; and, indeed, when thou hast perused the many great events which this book will produce, thou wilt think the number of pages contained in it scarce sufficient to tell the story.\n\nAnd now, my friend, I take this opportunity (as I shall have no other) of heartily wishing thee well. If I have been an entertaining companion to thee, I promise thee it is what I have desired. If in anything I have offended, it was really without any intention. Some things, perhaps, here said, may have hit thee or thy friends; but I do most solemnly declare they were not pointed at thee or them. I question not but thou hast been told, among other stories of me, that thou wast to travel with a very scurrilous fellow; but whoever told thee so did me an injury. No man detests and despises scurrility more than myself; nor hath any man more reason; for none hath ever been treated with more; and what is a very severe fate, I have had some of the abusive writings of those very men fathered upon me, who, in other of their works, have abused me themselves with the utmost virulence.\n\nAll these works, however, I am well convinced, will be dead long before this page shall offer itself to thy perusal; for however short the period may be of my own performances, they will most probably outlive their own infirm author, and the weakly productions of his abusive contemporaries.\n\nContaining a very tragical incident.\n\nWhile Jones was employed in those unpleasant meditations, with which we left him tormenting himself, Partridge came stumbling into the room with his face paler than ashes, his eyes fixed in his head, his hair standing an end, and every limb trembling. In short, he looked as he would have done had he seen a spectre, or had he, indeed, been a spectre himself.\n\nJones, who was little subject to fear, could not avoid being somewhat shocked at this sudden appearance. He did, indeed, himself change colour, and his voice a little faultered while he asked him, What was the matter?\n\n“I hope, sir,” said Partridge, “you will not be angry with me. Indeed I did not listen, but I was obliged to stay in the outward room. I am sure I wish I had been a hundred miles off, rather than have heard what I have heard.” “Why, what is the matter?” said Jones. “The matter, sir? O good Heaven!” answered Partridge, “was that woman who is just gone out the woman who was with you at Upton?” “She was, Partridge,” cried Jones. “And did you really, sir, go to bed with that woman?” said he, trembling.--“I am afraid what past between us is no secret,” said Jones.--“Nay, but pray, sir, for Heaven's sake, sir, answer me,” cries Partridge. “You know I did,” cries Jones. “Why then, the Lord have mercy upon your soul, and forgive you,” cries Partridge; “but as sure as I stand here alive, you have been a-bed with your own mother.”\n\nUpon these words Jones became in a moment a greater picture of horror than Partridge himself. He was, indeed, for some time struck dumb with amazement, and both stood staring wildly at each other. At last his words found way, and in an interrupted voice he said, “How! how! what's this you tell me?” “Nay, sir,” cries Partridge, “I have not breath enough left to tell you now, but what I have said is most certainly true.--That woman who now went out is your own mother. How unlucky was it for you, sir, that I did not happen to see her at that time, to have prevented it! Sure the devil himself must have contrived to bring about this wickedness.”\n\n“Sure,” cries Jones, “Fortune will never have done with me till she hath driven me to distraction. But why do I blame Fortune? I am myself the cause of all my misery. All the dreadful mischiefs which have befallen me are the consequences only of my own folly and vice. What thou hast told me, Partridge, hath almost deprived me of my senses! And was Mrs Waters, then--but why do I ask? for thou must certainly know her--If thou hast any affection for me, nay, if thou hast any pity, let me beseech thee to fetch this miserable woman back again to me. O good Heavens! incest----with a mother! To what am I reserved!” He then fell into the most violent and frantic agonies of grief and despair, in which Partridge declared he would not leave him; but at last, having vented the first torrent of passion, he came a little to himself; and then, having acquainted Partridge that he would find this wretched woman in the same house where the wounded gentleman was lodged, he despatched him in quest of her.\n\nIf the reader will please to refresh his memory, by turning to the scene at Upton, in the ninth book, he will be apt to admire the many strange accidents which unfortunately prevented any interview between Partridge and Mrs Waters, when she spent a whole day there with Mr Jones. Instances of this kind we may frequently observe in life, where the greatest events are produced by a nice train of little circumstances; and more than one example of this may be discovered by the accurate eye, in this our history.\n\nAfter a fruitless search of two or three hours, Partridge returned back to his master, without having seen Mrs Waters. Jones, who was in a state of desperation at his delay, was almost raving mad when he brought him his account. He was not long, however, in this condition before he received the following letter:\n\n“Since I left you I have seen a gentleman, from whom I have learned something concerning you which greatly surprizes and affects me; but as I have not at present leisure to communicate a matter of such high importance, you must suspend your curiosity till our next meeting, which shall be the first moment I am able to see you. O, Mr Jones, little did I think, when I past that happy day at Upton, the reflection upon which is like to embitter all my future life, who it was to whom I owed such perfect happiness. Believe me to be ever sincerely your unfortunate\n\n“P.S. I would have you comfort yourself as much as possible, for Mr Fitzpatrick is in no manner of danger; so that whatever other grievous crimes you may have to repent of, the guilt of blood is not among the number.”\n\nJones having read the letter, let it drop (for he was unable to hold it, and indeed had scarce the use of any one of his faculties). Partridge took it up, and having received consent by silence, read it likewise; nor had it upon him a less sensible effect. The pencil, and not the pen, should describe the horrors which appeared in both their countenances. While they both remained speechless the turnkey entered the room, and, without taking any notice of what sufficiently discovered itself in the faces of them both, acquainted Jones that a man without desired to speak with him. This person was presently introduced, and was no other than Black George.\n\nAs sights of horror were not so usual to George as they were to the turnkey, he instantly saw the great disorder which appeared in the face of Jones. This he imputed to the accident that had happened, which was reported in the very worst light in Mr Western's family; he concluded, therefore, that the gentleman was dead, and that Mr Jones was in a fair way of coming to a shameful end. A thought which gave him much uneasiness; for George was of a compassionate disposition, and notwithstanding a small breach of friendship which he had been over-tempted to commit, was, in the main, not insensible of the obligations he had formerly received from Mr Jones.\n\nThe poor fellow, therefore, scarce refrained from a tear at the present sight. He told Jones he was heartily sorry for his misfortunes, and begged him to consider if he could be of any manner of service. “Perhaps, sir,” said he, “you may want a little matter of money upon this occasion; if you do, sir, what little I have is heartily at your service.”\n\nJones shook him very heartily by the hand, and gave him many thanks for the kind offer he had made; but answered, “He had not the least want of that kind.” Upon which George began to press his services more eagerly than before. Jones again thanked him, with assurances that he wanted nothing which was in the power of any man living to give. “Come, come, my good master,” answered George, “do not take the matter so much to heart. Things may end better than you imagine; to be sure you an't the first gentleman who hath killed a man, and yet come off.” “You are wide of the matter, George,” said Partridge, “the gentleman is not dead, nor like to die. Don't disturb my master, at present, for he is troubled about a matter in which it is not in your power to do him any good.” “You don't know what I may be able to do, Mr Partridge,” answered George; “if his concern is about my young lady, I have some news to tell my master.” “What do you say, Mr George?” cried Jones. “Hath anything lately happened in which my Sophia is concerned? My Sophia! how dares such a wretch as I mention her so profanely.” “I hope she will be yours yet,” answered George. “Why yes, sir, I have something to tell you about her. Madam Western hath just brought Madam Sophia home, and there hath been a terrible to do. I could not possibly learn the very right of it; but my master he hath been in a vast big passion, and so was Madam Western, and I heard her say, as she went out of doors into her chair, that she would never set her foot in master's house again. I don't know what's the matter, not I, but everything was very quiet when I came out; but Robin, who waited at supper, said he had never seen the squire for a long while in such good humour with young madam; that he kissed her several times, and swore she should be her own mistress, and he never would think of confining her any more. I thought this news would please you, and so I slipped out, though it was so late, to inform you of it.” Mr Jones assured George that it did greatly please him; for though he should never more presume to lift his eyes toward that incomparable creature, nothing could so much relieve his misery as the satisfaction he should always have in hearing of her welfare.\n\nThe rest of the conversation which passed at the visit is not important enough to be here related. The reader will, therefore, forgive us this abrupt breaking off, and be pleased to hear how this great good-will of the squire towards his daughter was brought about.\n\nMrs Western, on her first arrival at her brother's lodging, began to set forth the great honours and advantages which would accrue to the family by the match with Lord Fellamar, which her niece had absolutely refused; in which refusal, when the squire took the part of his daughter, she fell immediately into the most violent passion, and so irritated and provoked the squire, that neither his patience nor his prudence could bear it any longer; upon which there ensued between them both so warm a bout at altercation, that perhaps the regions of Billingsgate never equalled it. In the heat of this scolding Mrs Western departed, and had consequently no leisure to acquaint her brother with the letter which Sophia received, which might have possibly produced ill effects; but, to say truth, I believe it never once occurred to her memory at this time.\n\nWhen Mrs Western was gone, Sophia, who had been hitherto silent, as well indeed from necessity as inclination, began to return the compliment which her father had made her, in taking her part against her aunt, by taking his likewise against the lady. This was the first time of her so doing, and it was in the highest degree acceptable to the squire. Again, he remembered that Mr Allworthy had insisted on an entire relinquishment of all violent means; and, indeed, as he made no doubt but that Jones would be hanged, he did not in the least question succeeding with his daughter by fair means; he now, therefore, once more gave a loose to his natural fondness for her, which had such an effect on the dutiful, grateful, tender, and affectionate heart of Sophia, that had her honour, given to Jones, and something else, perhaps, in which he was concerned, been removed, I much doubt whether she would not have sacrificed herself to a man she did not like, to have obliged her father. She promised him she would make it the whole business of her life to oblige him, and would never marry any man against his consent; which brought the old man so near to his highest happiness, that he was resolved to take the other step, and went to bed completely drunk.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\nAllworthy visits old Nightingale; with a strange discovery that he made on that occasion.\n\nThe morning after these things had happened, Mr Allworthy went, according to his promise, to visit old Nightingale, with whom his authority was so great, that, after having sat with him three hours, he at last prevailed with him to consent to see his son.\n\nHere an accident happened of a very extraordinary kind; one indeed of those strange chances whence very good and grave men have concluded that Providence often interposes in the discovery of the most secret villany, in order to caution men from quitting the paths of honesty, however warily they tread in those of vice.\n\nMr Allworthy, at his entrance into Mr Nightingale's, saw Black George; he took no notice of him, nor did Black George imagine he had perceived him.\n\nHowever, when their conversation on the principal point was over, Allworthy asked Nightingale, Whether he knew one George Seagrim, and upon what business he came to his house? “Yes,” answered Nightingale, “I know him very well, and a most extraordinary fellow he is, who, in these days, hath been able to hoard up £500 from renting a very small estate of £30 a year.” “And is this the story which he hath told you?” cries Allworthy. “Nay, it is true, I promise you,” said Nightingale, “for I have the money now in my own hands, in five bank-bills, which I am to lay out either in a mortgage, or in some purchase in the north of England.” The bank-bills were no sooner produced at Allworthy's desire than he blessed himself at the strangeness of the discovery. He presently told Nightingale that these bank-bills were formerly his, and then acquainted him with the whole affair. As there are no men who complain more of the frauds of business than highwaymen, gamesters, and other thieves of that kind, so there are none who so bitterly exclaim against the frauds of gamesters, &c., as usurers, brokers, and other thieves of this kind; whether it be that the one way of cheating is a discountenance or reflection upon the other, or that money, which is the common mistress of all cheats, makes them regard each other in the light of rivals; but Nightingale no sooner heard the story than he exclaimed against the fellow in terms much severer than the justice and honesty of Allworthy had bestowed on him.\n\nAllworthy desired Nightingale to retain both the money and the secret till he should hear farther from him; and, if he should in the meantime see the fellow, that he would not take the least notice to him of the discovery which he had made. He then returned to his lodgings, where he found Mrs Miller in a very dejected condition, on account of the information she had received from her son-in-law. Mr Allworthy, with great chearfulness, told her that he had much good news to communicate; and, with little further preface, acquainted her that he had brought Mr Nightingale to consent to see his son, and did not in the least doubt to effect a perfect reconciliation between them; though he found the father more sowered by another accident of the same kind which had happened in his family. He then mentioned the running away of the uncle's daughter, which he had been told by the old gentleman, and which Mrs Miller and her son-in-law did not yet know.\n\nThe reader may suppose Mrs Miller received this account with great thankfulness, and no less pleasure; but so uncommon was her friendship to Jones, that I am not certain whether the uneasiness she suffered for his sake did not overbalance her satisfaction at hearing a piece of news tending so much to the happiness of her own family; nor whether even this very news, as it reminded her of the obligations she had to Jones, did not hurt as well as please her; when her grateful heart said to her, “While my own family is happy, how miserable is the poor creature to whose generosity we owe the beginning of all this happiness!”\n\nAllworthy, having left her a little while to chew the cud (if I may use that expression) on these first tidings, told her he had still something more to impart, which he believed would give her pleasure. “I think,” said he, “I have discovered a pretty considerable treasure belonging to the young gentleman, your friend; but perhaps, indeed, his present situation may be such that it will be of no service to him.” The latter part of the speech gave Mrs Miller to understand who was meant, and she answered with a sigh, “I hope not, sir.” “I hope so too,” cries Allworthy, “with all my heart; but my nephew told me this morning he had heard a very bad account of the affair.”----“Good Heaven! sir,” said she--“Well, I must not speak, and yet it is certainly very hard to be obliged to hold one's tongue when one hears.”--“Madam,” said Allworthy, “you may say whatever you please, you know me too well to think I have a prejudice against any one; and as for that young man, I assure you I should be heartily pleased to find he could acquit himself of everything, and particularly of this sad affair. You can testify the affection I have formerly borne him. The world, I know, censured me for loving him so much. I did not withdraw that affection from him without thinking I had the justest cause. Believe me, Mrs Miller, I should be glad to find I have been mistaken.” Mrs Miller was going eagerly to reply, when a servant acquainted her that a gentleman without desired to speak with her immediately. Allworthy then enquired for his nephew, and was told that he had been for some time in his room with the gentleman who used to come to him, and whom Mr Allworthy guessing rightly to be Mr Dowling, he desired presently to speak with him.\n\nWhen Dowling attended, Allworthy put the case of the bank-notes to him, without mentioning any name, and asked in what manner such a person might be punished. To which Dowling answered, “He thought he might be indicted on the Black Act; but said, as it was a matter of some nicety, it would be proper to go to counsel. He said he was to attend counsel presently upon an affair of Mr Western's, and if Mr Allworthy pleased he would lay the case before them.” This was agreed to; and then Mrs Miller, opening the door, cried, “I ask pardon, I did not know you had company;” but Allworthy desired her to come in, saying he had finished his business. Upon which Mr Dowling withdrew, and Mrs Miller introduced Mr Nightingale the younger, to return thanks for the great kindness done him by Allworthy: but she had scarce patience to let the young gentleman finish his speech before she interrupted him, saying, “O sir! Mr Nightingale brings great news about poor Mr Jones: he hath been to see the wounded gentleman, who is out of all danger of death, and, what is more, declares he fell upon poor Mr Jones himself, and beat him. I am sure, sir, you would not have Mr Jones be a coward. If I was a man myself, I am sure, if any man was to strike me, I should draw my sword. Do pray, my dear, tell Mr Allworthy, tell him all yourself.” Nightingale then confirmed what Mrs Miller had said; and concluded with many handsome things of Jones, who was, he said, one of the best-natured fellows in the world, and not in the least inclined to be quarrelsome. Here Nightingale was going to cease, when Mrs Miller again begged him to relate all the many dutiful expressions he had heard him make use of towards Mr Allworthy. “To say the utmost good of Mr Allworthy,” cries Nightingale, “is doing no more than strict justice, and can have no merit in it: but indeed, I must say, no man can be more sensible of the obligations he hath to so good a man than is poor Jones. Indeed, sir, I am convinced the weight of your displeasure is the heaviest burthen he lies under. He hath often lamented it to me, and hath as often protested in the most solemn manner he hath never been intentionally guilty of any offence towards you; nay, he hath sworn he would rather die a thousand deaths than he would have his conscience upbraid him with one disrespectful, ungrateful, or undutiful thought towards you. But I ask pardon, sir, I am afraid I presume to intermeddle too far in so tender a point.” “You have spoke no more than what a Christian ought,” cries Mrs Miller. “Indeed, Mr Nightingale,” answered Allworthy, “I applaud your generous friendship, and I wish he may merit it of you. I confess I am glad to hear the report you bring from this unfortunate gentleman; and, if that matter should turn out to be as you represent it (and, indeed, I doubt nothing of what you say), I may, perhaps, in time, be brought to think better than lately I have of this young man; for this good gentlewoman here, nay, all who know me, can witness that I loved him as dearly as if he had been my own son. Indeed, I have considered him as a child sent by fortune to my care. I still remember the innocent, the helpless situation in which I found him. I feel the tender pressure of his little hands at this moment. He was my darling, indeed he was.” At which words he ceased, and the tears stood in his eyes.\n\nAs the answer which Mrs Miller made may lead us into fresh matters, we will here stop to account for the visible alteration in Mr Allworthy's mind, and the abatement of his anger to Jones. Revolutions of this kind, it is true, do frequently occur in histories and dramatic writers, for no other reason than because the history or play draws to a conclusion, and are justified by authority of authors; yet, though we insist upon as much authority as any author whatever, we shall use this power very sparingly, and never but when we are driven to it by necessity, which we do not at present foresee will happen in this work.\n\nThis alteration then in the mind of Mr Allworthy was occasioned by a letter he had just received from Mr Square, and which we shall give the reader in the beginning of the next chapter.\n\nContaining two letters in very different stiles.\n\n“MY WORTHY FRIEND,--I informed you in my last that I was forbidden the use of the waters, as they were found by experience rather to increase than lessen the symptoms of my distemper. I must now acquaint you with a piece of news, which, I believe, will afflict my friends more than it hath afflicted me. Dr Harrington and Dr Brewster have informed me that there is no hopes of my recovery.\n\n“I have somewhere read, that the great use of philosophy is to learn to die. I will not therefore so far disgrace mine as to shew any surprize at receiving a lesson which I must be thought to have so long studied. Yet, to say the truth, one page of the Gospel teaches this lesson better than all the volumes of antient or modern philosophers. The assurance it gives us of another life is a much stronger support to a good mind than all the consolations that are drawn from the necessity of nature, the emptiness or satiety of our enjoyments here, or any other topic of those declamations which are sometimes capable of arming our minds with a stubborn patience in bearing the thoughts of death, but never of raising them to a real contempt of it, and much less of making us think it is a real good. I would not here be understood to throw the horrid censure of atheism, or even the absolute denial of immortality, on all who are called philosophers. Many of that sect, as well antient as modern, have, from the light of reason, discovered some hopes of a future state; but in reality, that light was so faint and glimmering, and the hopes were so incertain and precarious, that it may be justly doubted on which side their belief turned. Plato himself concludes his Phaedon with declaring that his best arguments amount only to raise a probability; and Cicero himself seems rather to profess an inclination to believe, than any actual belief in the doctrines of immortality. As to myself, to be very sincere with you, I never was much in earnest in this faith till I was in earnest a Christian.\n\n“You will perhaps wonder at the latter expression; but I assure you it hath not been till very lately that I could, with truth, call myself so. The pride of philosophy had intoxicated my reason, and the sublimest of all wisdom appeared to me, as it did to the Greeks of old, to be foolishness. God hath, however, been so gracious to shew me my error in time, and to bring me into the way of truth, before I sunk into utter darkness forever.\n\n“I find myself beginning to grow weak, I shall therefore hasten to the main purpose of this letter.\n\n“When I reflect on the actions of my past life, I know of nothing which sits heavier upon my conscience than the injustice I have been guilty of to that poor wretch your adopted son. I have, indeed, not only connived at the villany of others, but been myself active in injustice towards him. Believe me, my dear friend, when I tell you, on the word of a dying man, he hath been basely injured. As to the principal fact, upon the misrepresentation of which you discarded him, I solemnly assure you he is innocent. When you lay upon your supposed deathbed, he was the only person in the house who testified any real concern; and what happened afterwards arose from the wildness of his joy on your recovery; and, I am sorry to say it, from the baseness of another person (but it is my desire to justify the innocent, and to accuse none). Believe me, my friend, this young man hath the noblest generosity of heart, the most perfect capacity for friendship, the highest integrity, and indeed every virtue which can ennoble a man. He hath some faults, but among them is not to be numbered the least want of duty or gratitude towards you. On the contrary, I am satisfied, when you dismissed him from your house, his heart bled for you more than for himself.\n\n“Worldly motives were the wicked and base reasons of my concealing this from you so long; to reveal it now I can have no inducement but the desire of serving the cause of truth, of doing right to the innocent, and of making all the amends in my power for a past offence. I hope this declaration, therefore, will have the effect desired, and will restore this deserving young man to your favour; the hearing of which, while I am yet alive, will afford the utmost consolation to,\n\nSir, Your most obliged, obedient humble servant, THOMAS SQUARE.”\n\nThe reader will, after this, scarce wonder at the revolution so visibly appearing in Mr Allworthy, notwithstanding he received from Thwackum, by the same post, another letter of a very different kind, which we shall here add, as it may possibly be the last time we shall have occasion to mention the name of that gentleman.\n\n“I am not at all surprized at hearing from your worthy nephew a fresh instance of the villany of Mr Square the atheist's young pupil. I shall not wonder at any murders he may commit; and I heartily pray that your own blood may not seal up his final commitment to the place of wailing and gnashing of teeth.\n\n“Though you cannot want sufficient calls to repentance for the many unwarrantable weaknesses exemplified in your behaviour to this wretch, so much to the prejudice of your own lawful family, and of your character; I say, though these may sufficiently be supposed to prick and goad your conscience at this season, I should yet be wanting to my duty, if I spared to give you some admonition in order to bring you to a due sense of your errors. I therefore pray you seriously to consider the judgment which is likely to overtake this wicked villain; and let it serve at least as a warning to you, that you may not for the future despise the advice of one who is so indefatigable in his prayers for your welfare.\n\n“Had not my hand been withheld from due correction, I had scourged much of this diabolical spirit out of a boy, of whom from his infancy I discovered the devil had taken such entire possession. But reflections of this kind now come too late.\n\n“I am sorry you have given away the living of Westerton so hastily. I should have applied on that occasion earlier, had I thought you would not have acquainted me previous to the disposition.----Your objection to pluralities is being righteous over-much. If there were any crime in the practice, so many godly men would not agree to it. If the vicar of Aldergrove should die (as we hear he is in a declining way), I hope you will think of me, since I am certain you must be convinced of my most sincere attachment to your highest welfare--a welfare to which all worldly considerations are as trifling as the small tithes mentioned in Scripture are, when compared to the weighty matters of the law.\n\nI am, sir, Your faithful humble servant, ROGER THWACKUM.”\n\nThis was the first time Thwackum ever wrote in this authoritative stile to Allworthy, and of this he had afterwards sufficient reason to repent, as in the case of those who mistake the highest degree of goodness for the lowest degree of weakness. Allworthy had indeed never liked this man. He knew him to be proud and ill-natured; he also knew that his divinity itself was tinctured with his temper, and such as in many respects he himself did by no means approve; but he was at the same time an excellent scholar, and most indefatigable in teaching the two lads. Add to this, the strict severity of his life and manners, an unimpeached honesty, and a most devout attachment to religion. So that, upon the whole, though Allworthy did not esteem nor love the man, yet he could never bring himself to part with a tutor to the boys, who was, both by learning and industry, extremely well qualified for his office; and he hoped, that as they were bred up in his own house, and under his own eye, he should be able to correct whatever was wrong in Thwackum's instructions.\n\nIn which the history is continued.\nMr Allworthy, in his last speech, had recollected some tender ideas concerning Jones, which had brought tears into the good man's eyes. This Mrs Miller observing, said, “Yes, yes, sir, your goodness to this poor young man is known, notwithstanding all your care to conceal it; but there is not a single syllable of truth in what those villains said. Mr Nightingale hath now discovered the whole matter. It seems these fellows were employed by a lord, who is a rival of poor Mr Jones, to have pressed him on board a ship.--I assure them I don't know who they will press next. Mr Nightingale here hath seen the officer himself, who is a very pretty gentleman, and hath told him all, and is very sorry for what he undertook, which he would never have done, had he known Mr Jones to have been a gentleman; but he was told that he was a common strolling vagabond.”\n\nAllworthy stared at all this, and declared he was a stranger to every word she said. “Yes, sir,” answered she, “I believe you are.----It is a very different story, I believe, from what those fellows told this lawyer.”\n\n“What lawyer, madam? what is it you mean?” said Allworthy. “Nay, nay,” said she, “this is so like you to deny your own goodness: but Mr Nightingale here saw him.” “Saw whom, madam?” answered he. “Why, your lawyer, sir,” said she, “that you so kindly sent to enquire into the affair.” “I am still in the dark, upon my honour,” said Allworthy. “Why then do you tell him, my dear sir,” cries she. “Indeed, sir,” said Nightingale, “I did see that very lawyer who went from you when I came into the room, at an alehouse in Aldersgate, in company with two of the fellows who were employed by Lord Fellamar to press Mr Jones, and who were by that means present at the unhappy rencounter between him and Mr Fitzpatrick.” “I own, sir,” said Mrs Miller, “when I saw this gentleman come into the room to you, I told Mr Nightingale that I apprehended you had sent him thither to inquire into the affair.” Allworthy shewed marks of astonishment in his countenance at this news, and was indeed for two or three minutes struck dumb by it. At last, addressing himself to Mr Nightingale, he said, “I must confess myself, sir, more surprized at what you tell me than I have ever been before at anything in my whole life. Are you certain this was the gentleman?” “I am most certain,” answered Nightingale. “At Aldersgate?” cries Allworthy. “And was you in company with this lawyer and the two fellows?”--“I was, sir,” said the other, “very near half an hour.” “Well, sir,” said Allworthy, “and in what manner did the lawyer behave? did you hear all that past between him and the fellows?” “No, sir,” answered Nightingale, “they had been together before I came.--In my presence the lawyer said little; but, after I had several times examined the fellows, who persisted in a story directly contrary to what I had heard from Mr Jones, and which I find by Mr Fitzpatrick was a rank falshood, the lawyer then desired the fellows to say nothing but what was the truth, and seemed to speak so much in favour of Mr Jones, that, when I saw the same person with you, I concluded your goodness had prompted you to send him thither.”--“And did you not send him thither?” says Mrs Miller.--“Indeed I did not,” answered Allworthy; “nor did I know he had gone on such an errand till this moment.”--“I see it all!” said Mrs Miller, “upon my soul, I see it all! No wonder they have been closeted so close lately. Son Nightingale, let me beg you run for these fellows immediately----find them out if they are above-ground. I will go myself”--“Dear madam,” said Allworthy, “be patient, and do me the favour to send a servant upstairs to call Mr Dowling hither, if he be in the house, or, if not, Mr Blifil.” Mrs Miller went out muttering something to herself, and presently returned with an answer, “That Mr Dowling was gone; but that the t'other,” as she called him, “was coming.”\n\nAllworthy was of a cooler disposition than the good woman, whose spirits were all up in arms in the cause of her friend. He was not however without some suspicions which were near akin to hers. When Blifil came into the room, he asked him with a very serious countenance, and with a less friendly look than he had ever before given him, “Whether he knew anything of Mr Dowling's having seen any of the persons who were present at the duel between Jones and another gentleman?”\n\nThere is nothing so dangerous as a question which comes by surprize on a man whose business it is to conceal truth, or to defend falshood. For which reason those worthy personages, whose noble office it is to save the lives of their fellow-creatures at the Old Bailey, take the utmost care, by frequent previous examination, to divine every question which may be asked their clients on the day of tryal, that they may be supplyed with proper and ready answers, which the most fertile invention cannot supply in an instant. Besides, the sudden and violent impulse on the blood, occasioned by these surprizes, causes frequently such an alteration in the countenance, that the man is obliged to give evidence against himself. And such indeed were the alterations which the countenance of Blifil underwent from this sudden question, that we can scarce blame the eagerness of Mrs Miller, who immediately cryed out, “Guilty, upon my honour! guilty, upon my soul!”\n\nMr Allworthy sharply rebuked her for this impetuosity; and then turning to Blifil, who seemed sinking into the earth, he said, “Why do you hesitate, sir, at giving me an answer? You certainly must have employed him; for he would not, of his own accord, I believe, have undertaken such an errand, and especially without acquainting me.”\n\nBlifil then answered, “I own, sir, I have been guilty of an offence, yet may I hope your pardon?”--“My pardon,” said Allworthy, very angrily.--“Nay, sir,” answered Blifil, “I knew you would be offended; yet surely my dear uncle will forgive the effects of the most amiable of human weaknesses. Compassion for those who do not deserve it, I own is a crime; and yet it is a crime from which you yourself are not entirely free. I know I have been guilty of it in more than one instance to this very person; and I will own I did send Mr Dowling, not on a vain and fruitless enquiry, but to discover the witnesses, and to endeavour to soften their evidence. This, sir, is the truth; which, though I intended to conceal from you, I will not deny.”\n\n“I confess,” said Nightingale, “this is the light in which it appeared to me from the gentleman's behaviour.”\n\n“Now, madam,” said Allworthy, “I believe you will once in your life own you have entertained a wrong suspicion, and are not so angry with my nephew as you was.”\n\nMrs Miller was silent; for, though she could not so hastily be pleased with Blifil, whom she looked upon to have been the ruin of Jones, yet in this particular instance he had imposed upon her as well as upon the rest; so entirely had the devil stood his friend. And, indeed, I look upon the vulgar observation, “That the devil often deserts his friends, and leaves them in the lurch,” to be a great abuse on that gentleman's character. Perhaps he may sometimes desert those who are only his cup acquaintance; or who, at most, are but half his; but he generally stands by those who are thoroughly his servants, and helps them off in all extremities, till their bargain expires.\n\nAs a conquered rebellion strengthens a government, or as health is more perfectly established by recovery from some diseases; so anger, when removed, often gives new life to affection. This was the case of Mr Allworthy; for Blifil having wiped off the greater suspicion, the lesser, which had been raised by Square's letter, sunk of course, and was forgotten; and Thwackum, with whom he was greatly offended, bore alone all the reflections which Square had cast on the enemies of Jones.\n\nAs for that young man, the resentment of Mr Allworthy began more and more to abate towards him. He told Blifil, “He did not only forgive the extraordinary efforts of his good-nature, but would give him the pleasure of following his example.” Then, turning to Mrs Miller with a smile which would have become an angel, he cryed, “What say you, madam? shall we take a hackney-coach, and all of us together pay a visit to your friend? I promise you it is not the first visit I have made in a prison.”\n\nEvery reader, I believe, will be able to answer for the worthy woman; but they must have a great deal of good-nature, and be well acquainted with friendship, who can feel what she felt on this occasion. Few, I hope, are capable of feeling what now passed in the mind of Blifil; but those who are will acknowledge that it was impossible for him to raise any objection to this visit. Fortune, however, or the gentleman lately mentioned above, stood his friend, and prevented his undergoing so great a shock; for at the very instant when the coach was sent for, Partridge arrived, and, having called Mrs Miller from the company, acquainted her with the dreadful accident lately come to light; and hearing Mr Allworthy's intention, begged her to find some means of stopping him: “For,” says he, “the matter must at all hazards be kept a secret from him; and if he should now go, he will find Mr Jones and his mother, who arrived just as I left him, lamenting over one another the horrid crime they have ignorantly committed.”\n\nThe poor woman, who was almost deprived of her senses at his dreadful news, was never less capable of invention than at present. However, as women are much readier at this than men, she bethought herself of an excuse, and, returning to Allworthy, said, “I am sure, sir, you will be surprized at hearing any objection from me to the kind proposal you just now made; and yet I am afraid of the consequence of it, if carried immediately into execution. You must imagine, sir, that all the calamities which have lately befallen this poor young fellow must have thrown him into the lowest dejection of spirits; and now, sir, should we all on a sudden fling him into such a violent fit of joy, as I know your presence will occasion, it may, I am afraid, produce some fatal mischief, especially as his servant, who is without, tells me he is very far from being well.”\n\n“Is his servant without?” cries Allworthy; “pray call him hither. I will ask him some questions concerning his master.”\n\nPartridge was at first afraid to appear before Mr Allworthy; but was at length persuaded, after Mrs Miller, who had often heard his whole story from his own mouth, had promised to introduce him.\n\nAllworthy recollected Partridge the moment he came into the room, though many years had passed since he had seen him. Mrs Miller, therefore, might have spared here a formal oration, in which, indeed, she was something prolix; for the reader, I believe, may have observed already that the good woman, among other things, had a tongue always ready for the service of her friends.\n\n“And are you,” said Allworthy to Partridge, “the servant of Mr Jones?” “I can't say, sir,” answered he, “that I am regularly a servant, but I live with him, an't please your honour, at present. Non sum qualis eram, as your honour very well knows.”\n\nMr Allworthy then asked him many questions concerning Jones, as to his health, and other matters; to all which Partridge answered, without having the least regard to what was, but considered only what he would have things appear; for a strict adherence to truth was not among the articles of this honest fellow's morality or his religion.\n\nDuring this dialogue Mr Nightingale took his leave, and presently after Mrs Miller left the room, when Allworthy likewise despatched Blifil; for he imagined that Partridge when alone with him would be more explicit than before company. They were no sooner left in private together than Allworthy began, as in the following chapter.\n\nIn which the history is farther continued\n“Sure, friend,” said the good man, “you are the strangest of all human beings. Not only to have suffered as you have formerly for obstinately persisting in a falshood, but to persist in it thus to the last, and to pass thus upon the world for a servant of your own son! What interest can you have in all this? What can be your motive?”\n\n“I see, sir,” said Partridge, falling down upon his knees, “that your honour is prepossessed against me, and resolved not to believe anything I say, and, therefore, what signifies my protestations? but yet there is one above who knows that I am not the father of this young man.”\n\n“How!” said Allworthy, “will you yet deny what you was formerly convicted of upon such unanswerable, such manifest evidence? Nay, what a confirmation is your being now found with this very man, of all which twenty years ago appeared against you! I thought you had left the country! nay, I thought you had been long since dead.--In what manner did you know anything of this young man? Where did you meet with him, unless you had kept some correspondence together? Do not deny this; for I promise you it will greatly raise your son in my opinion, to find that he hath such a sense of filial duty as privately to support his father for so many years.”\n\n“If your honour will have patience to hear me,” said Partridge, “I will tell you all.”--Being bid go on, he proceeded thus: “When your honour conceived that displeasure against me, it ended in my ruin soon after; for I lost my little school; and the minister, thinking I suppose it would be agreeable to your honour, turned me out from the office of clerk; so that I had nothing to trust to but the barber's shop, which, in a country place like that, is a poor livelihood; and when my wife died (for till that time I received a pension of £12 a year from an unknown hand, which indeed I believe was your honour's own, for nobody that ever I heard of doth these things besides)--but, as I was saying, when she died, this pension forsook me; so that now, as I owed two or three small debts, which began to be troublesome to me, particularly one[*] which an attorney brought up by law-charges from 15s. to near £30, and as I found all my usual means of living had forsook me, I packed up my little all as well as I could, and went off.\n\n[*] This is a fact which I knew happen to a poor clergyman in Dorsetshire, by the villany of an attorney who, not contented with the exorbitant costs to which the poor man was put by a single action, brought afterwards another action on the judgment, as it was called. A method frequently used to oppress the poor, and bring money into the pockets of attorneys, to the great scandal of the law, of the nation, of Christianity, and even of human nature itself.\n\n“The first place I came to was Salisbury, where I got into the service of a gentleman belonging to the law, and one of the best gentlemen that ever I knew, for he was not only good to me, but I know a thousand good and charitable acts which he did while I staid with him; and I have known him often refuse business because it was paultry and oppressive.” “You need not be so particular,” said Allworthy; “I know this gentleman, and a very worthy man he is, and an honour to his profession.”--“Well, sir,” continued Partridge, “from hence I removed to Lymington, where I was above three years in the service of another lawyer, who was likewise a very good sort of a man, and to be sure one of the merriest gentlemen in England. Well, sir, at the end of the three years I set up a little school, and was likely to do well again, had it not been for a most unlucky accident. Here I kept a pig; and one day, as ill fortune would have it, this pig broke out, and did a trespass, I think they call it, in a garden belonging to one of my neighbours, who was a proud, revengeful man, and employed a lawyer, one--one--I can't think of his name; but he sent for a writ against me, and had me to size. When I came there, Lord have mercy upon me--to hear what the counsellors said! There was one that told my lord a parcel of the confoundedest lies about me; he said that I used to drive my hogs into other folk's gardens, and a great deal more; and at last he said, he hoped I had at last brought my hogs to a fair market. To be sure, one would have thought that, instead of being owner only of one poor little pig, I had been the greatest hog-merchant in England. Well--” “Pray,” said Allworthy, “do not be so particular, I have heard nothing of your son yet.” “O it was a great many years,” answered Partridge, “before I saw my son, as you are pleased to call him.----I went over to Ireland after this, and taught school at Cork (for that one suit ruined me again, and I lay seven years in Winchester jail).”--“Well,” said Allworthy, “pass that over till your return to England.”--“Then, sir,” said he, “it was about half a year ago that I landed at Bristol, where I staid some time, and not finding it do there, and hearing of a place between that and Gloucester where the barber was just dead, I went thither, and there I had been about two months when Mr Jones came thither.” He then gave Allworthy a very particular account of their first meeting, and of everything, as well as he could remember, which had happened from that day to this; frequently interlarding his story with panegyrics on Jones, and not forgetting to insinuate the great love and respect which he had for Allworthy. He concluded with saying, “Now, sir, I have told your honour the whole truth.” And then repeated a most solemn protestation, “That he was no more the father of Jones than of the Pope of Rome;” and imprecated the most bitter curses on his head, if he did not speak truth.\n\n“What am I to think of this matter?” cries Allworthy. “For what purpose should you so strongly deny a fact which I think it would be rather your interest to own?” “Nay, sir,” answered Partridge (for he could hold no longer), “if your honour will not believe me, you are like soon to have satisfaction enough. I wish you had mistaken the mother of this young man, as well as you have his father.”--And now being asked what he meant, with all the symptoms of horror, both in his voice and countenance, he told Allworthy the whole story, which he had a little before expressed such desire to Mrs Miller to conceal from him.\n\nAllworthy was almost as much shocked at this discovery as Partridge himself had been while he related it. “Good heavens!” says he, “in what miserable distresses do vice and imprudence involve men! How much beyond our designs are the effects of wickedness sometimes carried!” He had scarce uttered these words, when Mrs Waters came hastily and abruptly into the room. Partridge no sooner saw her than he cried, “Here, sir, here is the very woman herself. This is the unfortunate mother of Mr Jones. I am sure she will acquit me before your honour. Pray, madam----”\n\nMrs Waters, without paying any regard to what Partridge said, and almost without taking any notice of him, advanced to Mr Allworthy. “I believe, sir, it is so long since I had the honour of seeing you, that you do not recollect me.” “Indeed,” answered Allworthy, “you are so very much altered, on many accounts, that had not this man already acquainted me who you are, I should not have immediately called you to my remembrance. Have you, madam, any particular business which brings you to me?” Allworthy spoke this with great reserve; for the reader may easily believe he was not well pleased with the conduct of this lady; neither with what he had formerly heard, nor with what Partridge had now delivered.\n\nMrs Waters answered--“Indeed, sir, I have very particular business with you; and it is such as I can impart only to yourself. I must desire, therefore, the favour of a word with you alone: for I assure you what I have to tell you is of the utmost importance.”\n\nPartridge was then ordered to withdraw, but before he went, he begged the lady to satisfy Mr Allworthy that he was perfectly innocent. To which she answered, “You need be under no apprehension, sir; I shall satisfy Mr Allworthy very perfectly of that matter.”\n\nThen Partridge withdrew, and that past between Mr Allworthy and Mrs Waters which is written in the next chapter.\n\nContinuation of the history.\n\nMrs Waters remaining a few moments silent, Mr Allworthy could not refrain from saying, “I am sorry, madam, to perceive, by what I have since heard, that you have made so very ill a use----” “Mr Allworthy,” says she, interrupting him, “I know I have faults, but ingratitude to you is not one of them. I never can nor shall forget your goodness, which I own I have very little deserved; but be pleased to wave all upbraiding me at present, as I have so important an affair to communicate to you concerning this young man, to whom you have given my maiden name of Jones.”\n\n“Have I then,” said Allworthy, “ignorantly punished an innocent man, in the person of him who hath just left us? Was he not the father of the child?” “Indeed he was not,” said Mrs Waters. “You may be pleased to remember, sir, I formerly told you, you should one day know; and I acknowledge myself to have been guilty of a cruel neglect, in not having discovered it to you before. Indeed, I little knew how necessary it was.” “Well, madam,” said Allworthy, “be pleased to proceed.” “You must remember, sir,” said she, “a young fellow, whose name was Summer.” “Very well,” cries Allworthy, “he was the son of a clergyman of great learning and virtue, for whom I had the highest friendship.” “So it appeared, sir,” answered she; “for I believe you bred the young man up, and maintained him at the university; where, I think, he had finished his studies, when he came to reside at your house; a finer man, I must say, the sun never shone upon; for, besides the handsomest person I ever saw, he was so genteel, and had so much wit and good breeding.” “Poor gentleman,” said Allworthy, “he was indeed untimely snatched away; and little did I think he had any sins of this kind to answer for; for I plainly perceive you are going to tell me he was the father of your child.”\n\n“Indeed, sir,” answered she, “he was not.” “How!” said Allworthy, “to what then tends all this preface?” “To a story,” said she, “which I am concerned falls to my lot to unfold to you. O, sir! prepare to hear something which will surprize you, will grieve you.” “Speak,” said Allworthy, “I am conscious of no crime, and cannot be afraid to hear.” “Sir,” said she, “that Mr Summer, the son of your friend, educated at your expense, who, after living a year in the house as if he had been your own son, died there of the small-pox, was tenderly lamented by you, and buried as if he had been your own; that Summer, sir, was the father of this child.” “How!” said Allworthy; “you contradict yourself.” “That I do not,” answered she; “he was indeed the father of this child, but not by me.” “Take care, madam,” said Allworthy, “do not, to shun the imputation of any crime, be guilty of falshood. Remember there is One from whom you can conceal nothing, and before whose tribunal falshood will only aggravate your guilt.” “Indeed, sir,” says she, “I am not his mother; nor would I now think myself so for the world.” “I know your reason,” said Allworthy, “and shall rejoice as much as you to find it otherwise; yet you must remember, you yourself confest it before me.” “So far what I confest,” said she, “was true, that these hands conveyed the infant to your bed; conveyed it thither at the command of its mother; at her commands I afterwards owned it, and thought myself, by her generosity, nobly rewarded, both for my secrecy and my shame.” “Who could this woman be?” said Allworthy. “Indeed, I tremble to name her,” answered Mrs Waters. “By all this preparation I am to guess that she was a relation of mine,” cried he. “Indeed she was a near one.” At which words Allworthy started, and she continued--“You had a sister, sir.” “A sister!” repeated he, looking aghast.--“As there is truth in heaven,” cries she, “your sister was the mother of that child you found between your sheets.” “Can it be possible?” cries he, “Good heavens!” “Have patience, sir,” said Mrs Waters, “and I will unfold to you the whole story. Just after your departure for London, Miss Bridget came one day to the house of my mother. She was pleased to say she had heard an extraordinary character of me, for my learning and superior understanding to all the young women there, so she was pleased to say. She then bid me come to her to the great house; where, when I attended, she employed me to read to her. She expressed great satisfaction in my reading, shewed great kindness to me, and made me many presents. At last she began to catechise me on the subject of secrecy, to which I gave her such satisfactory answers, that, at last, having locked the door of her room, she took me into her closet, and then locking that door likewise, she said she should convince me of the vast reliance she had on my integrity, by communicating a secret in which her honour, and consequently her life, was concerned. She then stopt, and after a silence of a few minutes, during which she often wiped her eyes, she enquired of me if I thought my mother might safely be confided in. I answered, I would stake my life on her fidelity. She then imparted to me the great secret which laboured in her breast, and which, I believe, was delivered with more pains than she afterwards suffered in child-birth. It was then contrived that my mother and myself only should attend at the time, and that Mrs Wilkins should be sent out of the way, as she accordingly was, to the very furthest part of Dorsetshire, to enquire the character of a servant; for the lady had turned away her own maid near three months before; during all which time I officiated about her person upon trial, as she said, though, as she afterwards declared, I was not sufficiently handy for the place. This, and many other such things which she used to say of me, were all thrown out to prevent any suspicion which Wilkins might hereafter have, when I was to own the child; for she thought it could never be believed she would venture to hurt a young woman with whom she had intrusted such a secret. You may be assured, sir, I was well paid for all these affronts, which, together with being informed with the occasion of them, very well contented me. Indeed, the lady had a greater suspicion of Mrs Wilkins than of any other person; not that she had the least aversion to the gentlewoman, but she thought her incapable of keeping a secret, especially from you, sir; for I have often heard Miss Bridget say, that, if Mrs Wilkins had committed a murder, she believed she would acquaint you with it. At last the expected day came, and Mrs Wilkins, who had been kept a week in readiness, and put off from time to time, upon some pretence or other, that she might not return too soon, was dispatched. Then the child was born, in the presence only of myself and my mother, and was by my mother conveyed to her own house, where it was privately kept by her till the evening of your return, when I, by the command of Miss Bridget, conveyed it into the bed where you found it. And all suspicions were afterwards laid asleep by the artful conduct of your sister, in pretending ill-will to the boy, and that any regard she shewed him was out of meer complacence to you.”\n\nMrs Waters then made many protestations of the truth of this story, and concluded by saying, “Thus, sir, you have at last discovered your nephew; for so I am sure you will hereafter think him, and I question not but he will be both an honour and a comfort to you under that appellation.”\n\n“I need not, madam,” said Allworthy, “express my astonishment at what you have told me; and yet surely you would not, and could not, have put together so many circumstances to evidence an untruth. I confess I recollect some passages relating to that Summer, which formerly gave me a conceit that my sister had some liking to him. I mentioned it to her; for I had such a regard to the young man, as well on his own account as on his father's, that I should willingly have consented to a match between them; but she exprest the highest disdain of my unkind suspicion, as she called it; so that I never spoke more on the subject. Good heavens! Well! the Lord disposeth all things.--Yet sure it was a most unjustifiable conduct in my sister to carry this secret with her out of the world.” “I promise you, sir,” said Mrs Waters, “she always profest a contrary intention, and frequently told me she intended one day to communicate it to you. She said, indeed, she was highly rejoiced that her plot had succeeded so well, and that you had of your own accord taken such a fancy to the child, that it was yet unnecessary to make any express declaration. Oh! sir, had that lady lived to have seen this poor young man turned like a vagabond from your house: nay, sir, could she have lived to hear that you had yourself employed a lawyer to prosecute him for a murder of which he was not guilty----Forgive me, Mr Allworthy, I must say it was unkind.--Indeed, you have been abused, he never deserved it of you.” “Indeed, madam,” said Allworthy, “I have been abused by the person, whoever he was, that told you so.” “Nay, sir,” said she, “I would not be mistaken, I did not presume to say you were guilty of any wrong. The gentleman who came to me proposed no such matter; he only said, taking me for Mr Fitzpatrick's wife, that, if Mr Jones had murdered my husband, I should be assisted with any money I wanted to carry on the prosecution, by a very worthy gentleman, who, he said, was well apprized what a villain I had to deal with. It was by this man I found out who Mr Jones was; and this man, whose name is Dowling, Mr Jones tells me is your steward. I discovered his name by a very odd accident; for he himself refused to tell it me; but Partridge, who met him at my lodgings the second time he came, knew him formerly at Salisbury.”\n\n“And did this Mr Dowling,” says Allworthy, with great astonishment in his countenance, “tell you that I would assist in the prosecution?”--“No, sir,” answered she, “I will not charge him wrongfully. He said I should be assisted, but he mentioned no name. Yet you must pardon me, sir, if from circumstances I thought it could be no other.”--“Indeed, madam,” says Allworthy, “from circumstances I am too well convinced it was another. Good Heaven! by what wonderful means is the blackest and deepest villany sometimes discovered!--Shall I beg you, madam, to stay till the person you have mentioned comes, for I expect him every minute? nay, he may be, perhaps, already in the house.”\n\nAllworthy then stept to the door, in order to call a servant, when in came, not Mr Dowling, but the gentleman who will be seen in the next chapter.\n\nFurther continuation.\nThe gentleman who now arrived was no other than Mr Western. He no sooner saw Allworthy, than, without considering in the least the presence of Mrs Waters, he began to vociferate in the following manner: “Fine doings at my house! A rare kettle of fish I have discovered at last! who the devil would be plagued with a daughter?” “What's the matter, neighbour?” said Allworthy. “Matter enough,” answered Western: “when I thought she was just a coming to; nay, when she had in a manner promised me to do as I would ha her, and when I was a hoped to have had nothing more to do than to have sent for the lawyer, and finished all; what do you think I have found out? that the little b-- hath bin playing tricks with me all the while, and carrying on a correspondence with that bastard of yours. Sister Western, whom I have quarrelled with upon her account, sent me word o't, and I ordered her pockets to be searched when she was asleep, and here I have got un signed with the son of a whore's own name. I have not had patience to read half o't, for 'tis longer than one of parson Supple's sermons; but I find plainly it is all about love; and indeed what should it be else? I have packed her up in chamber again, and to-morrow morning down she goes into the country, unless she consents to be married directly, and there she shall live in a garret upon bread and water all her days; and the sooner such a b-- breaks her heart the better, though, d--n her, that I believe is too tough. She will live long enough to plague me.” “Mr Western,” answered Allworthy, “you know I have always protested against force, and you yourself consented that none should be used.” “Ay,” cries he, “that was only upon condition that she would consent without. What the devil and doctor Faustus! shan't I do what I will with my own daughter, especially when I desire nothing but her own good?” “Well, neighbour,” answered Allworthy, “if you will give me leave, I will undertake once to argue with the young lady.” “Will you?” said Western; “why that is kind now, and neighbourly, and mayhap you will do more than I have been able to do with her; for I promise you she hath a very good opinion of you.” “Well, sir,” said Allworthy, “if you will go home, and release the young lady from her captivity, I will wait upon her within this half-hour.” “But suppose,” said Western, “she should run away with un in the meantime? For lawyer Dowling tells me there is no hopes of hanging the fellow at last; for that the man is alive, and like to do well, and that he thinks Jones will be out of prison again presently.” “How!” said Allworthy; “what, did you employ him then to enquire or to do anything in that matter?” “Not I,” answered Western, “he mentioned it to me just now of his own accord.” “Just now!” cries Allworthy, “why, where did you see him then? I want much to see Mr Dowling.” “Why, you may see un an you will presently at my lodgings; for there is to be a meeting of lawyers there this morning about a mortgage. 'Icod! I shall lose two or dree thousand pounds, I believe, by that honest gentleman, Mr Nightingale.” “Well, sir,” said Allworthy, “I will be with you within the half-hour.” “And do for once,” cries the squire, “take a fool's advice; never think of dealing with her by gentle methods, take my word for it those will never do. I have tried 'um long enough. She must be frightened into it, there is no other way. Tell her I'm her father; and of the horrid sin of disobedience, and of the dreadful punishment of it in t'other world, and then tell her about being locked up all her life in a garret in this, and being kept only on bread and water.” “I will do all I can,” said Allworthy; “for I promise you there is nothing I wish for more than an alliance with this amiable creature.” “Nay, the girl is well enough for matter o' that,” cries the squire; “a man may go farther and meet with worse meat; that I may declare o'her, thof she be my own daughter. And if she will but be obedient to me, there is narrow a father within a hundred miles o' the place, that loves a daughter better than I do; but I see you are busy with the lady here, so I will go huome and expect you; and so your humble servant.”\n\nAs soon as Mr Western was gone Mrs Waters said, “I see, sir, the squire hath not the least remembrance of my face. I believe, Mr Allworthy, you would not have known me neither. I am very considerably altered since that day when you so kindly gave me that advice, which I had been happy had I followed.” “Indeed, madam,” cries Allworthy, “it gave me great concern when I first heard the contrary.” “Indeed, sir,” says she, “I was ruined by a very deep scheme of villany, which if you knew, though I pretend not to think it would justify me in your opinion, it would at least mitigate my offence, and induce you to pity me: you are not now at leisure to hear my whole story; but this I assure you, I was betrayed by the most solemn promises of marriage; nay, in the eye of heaven I was married to him; for, after much reading on the subject, I am convinced that particular ceremonies are only requisite to give a legal sanction to marriage, and have only a worldly use in giving a woman the privileges of a wife; but that she who lives constant to one man, after a solemn private affiance, whatever the world may call her, hath little to charge on her own conscience.” “I am sorry, madam,” said Allworthy, “you made so ill a use of your learning. Indeed, it would have been well that you had been possessed of much more, or had remained in a state of ignorance. And yet, madam, I am afraid you have more than this sin to answer for.” “During his life,” answered she, “which was above a dozen years, I most solemnly assure you I had not. And consider, sir, on my behalf, what is in the power of a woman stript of her reputation and left destitute; whether the good-natured world will suffer such a stray sheep to return to the road of virtue, even if she was never so desirous. I protest, then, I would have chose it had it been in my power; but necessity drove me into the arms of Captain Waters, with whom, though still unmarried, I lived as a wife for many years, and went by his name. I parted with this gentleman at Worcester, on his march against the rebels, and it was then I accidentally met with Mr Jones, who rescued me from the hands of a villain. Indeed, he is the worthiest of men. No young gentleman of his age is, I believe, freer from vice, and few have the twentieth part of his virtues; nay, whatever vices he hath had, I am firmly persuaded he hath now taken a resolution to abandon them.” “I hope he hath,” cries Allworthy, “and I hope he will preserve that resolution. I must say, I have still the same hopes with regard to yourself. The world, I do agree, are apt to be too unmerciful on these occasions; yet time and perseverance will get the better of this their disinclination, as I may call it, to pity; for though they are not, like heaven, ready to receive a penitent sinner; yet a continued repentance will at length obtain mercy even with the world. This you may be assured of, Mrs Waters, that whenever I find you are sincere in such good intentions, you shall want no assistance in my power to make them effectual.”\n\nMrs Waters fell now upon her knees before him, and, in a flood of tears, made him many most passionate acknowledgments of his goodness, which, as she truly said, savoured more of the divine than human nature.\n\nAllworthy raised her up, and spoke in the most tender manner, making use of every expression which his invention could suggest to comfort her, when he was interrupted by the arrival of Mr Dowling, who, upon his first entrance, seeing Mrs Waters, started, and appeared in some confusion; from which he soon recovered himself as well as he could, and then said he was in the utmost haste to attend counsel at Mr Western's lodgings; but, however, thought it his duty to call and acquaint him with the opinion of counsel upon the case which he had before told him, which was that the conversion of the moneys in that case could not be questioned in a criminal cause, but that an action of trover might be brought, and if it appeared to the jury to be the moneys of plaintiff, that plaintiff would recover a verdict for the value.\n\nAllworthy, without making any answer to this, bolted the door, and then, advancing with a stern look to Dowling, he said, “Whatever be your haste, sir, I must first receive an answer to some questions. Do you know this lady?”--“That lady, sir!” answered Dowling, with great hesitation. Allworthy then, with the most solemn voice, said, “Look you, Mr Dowling, as you value my favour, or your continuance a moment longer in my service, do not hesitate nor prevaricate; but answer faithfully and truly to every question I ask.----Do you know this lady?”--“Yes, sir,” said Dowling, “I have seen the lady.” “Where, sir?” “At her own lodgings.”--“Upon what business did you go thither, sir; and who sent you?” “I went, sir, to enquire, sir, about Mr Jones.” “And who sent you to enquire about him?” “Who, sir? why, sir, Mr Blifil sent me.” “And what did you say to the lady concerning that matter?” “Nay, sir, it is impossible to recollect every word.” “Will you please, madam, to assist the gentleman's memory?” “He told me, sir,” said Mrs Waters, “that if Mr Jones had murdered my husband, I should be assisted by any money I wanted to carry on the prosecution, by a very worthy gentleman, who was well apprized what a villain I had to deal with. These, I can safely swear, were the very words he spoke.”--“Were these the words, sir?” said Allworthy. “I cannot charge my memory exactly,” cries Dowling, “but I believe I did speak to that purpose.”--“And did Mr Blifil order you to say so?” “I am sure, sir, I should not have gone on my own accord, nor have willingly exceeded my authority in matters of this kind. If I said so, I must have so understood Mr Blifil's instructions.” “Look you, Mr Dowling,” said Allworthy; “I promise you before this lady, that whatever you have done in this affair by Mr Blifil's order I will forgive, provided you now tell me strictly the truth; for I believe what you say, that you would not have acted of your own accord and without authority in this matter.----Mr Blifil then likewise sent you to examine the two fellows at Aldersgate?”--“He did, sir.” “Well, and what instructions did he then give you? Recollect as well as you can, and tell me, as near as possible, the very words he used.”--“Why, sir, Mr Blifil sent me to find out the persons who were eye-witnesses of this fight. He said, he feared they might be tampered with by Mr Jones, or some of his friends. He said, blood required blood; and that not only all who concealed a murderer, but those who omitted anything in their power to bring him to justice, were sharers in his guilt. He said, he found you was very desirous of having the villain brought to justice, though it was not proper you should appear in it.” “He did so?” says Allworthy.--“Yes, sir,” cries Dowling; “I should not, I am sure, have proceeded such lengths for the sake of any other person living but your worship.”--“What lengths, sir?” said Allworthy.--“Nay, sir,” cries Dowling, “I would not have your worship think I would, on any account, be guilty of subornation of perjury; but there are two ways of delivering evidence. I told them, therefore, that if any offers should be made them on the other side, they should refuse them, and that they might be assured they should lose nothing by being honest men, and telling the truth. I said, we were told that Mr Jones had assaulted the gentleman first, and that, if that was the truth, they should declare it; and I did give them some hints that they should be no losers.”--“I think you went lengths indeed,” cries Allworthy.--“Nay, sir,” answered Dowling, “I am sure I did not desire them to tell an untruth;----nor should I have said what I did, unless it had been to oblige you.”--“You would not have thought, I believe,” says Allworthy, “to have obliged me, had you known that this Mr Jones was my own nephew.”--“I am sure, sir,” answered he, “it did not become me to take any notice of what I thought you desired to conceal.”--“How!” cries Allworthy, “and did you know it then?”--“Nay, sir,” answered Dowling, “if your worship bids me speak the truth, I am sure I shall do it.--Indeed, sir, I did know it; for they were almost the last words which Madam Blifil ever spoke, which she mentioned to me as I stood alone by her bedside, when she delivered me the letter I brought your worship from her.”--“What letter?” cries Allworthy.--“The letter, sir,” answered Dowling, “which I brought from Salisbury, and which I delivered into the hands of Mr Blifil.”--“O heavens!” cries Allworthy: “Well, and what were the words? What did my sister say to you?”--“She took me by the hand,” answered he, “and, as she delivered me the letter, said, `I scarce know what I have written. Tell my brother, Mr Jones is his nephew--He is my son.--Bless him,' says she, and then fell backward, as if dying away. I presently called in the people, and she never spoke more to me, and died within a few minutes afterwards.”--Allworthy stood a minute silent, lifting up his eyes; and then, turning to Dowling, said, “How came you, sir, not to deliver me this message?” “Your worship,” answered he, “must remember that you was at that time ill in bed; and, being in a violent hurry, as indeed I always am, I delivered the letter and message to Mr Blifil, who told me he would carry them both to you, which he hath since told me he did, and that your worship, partly out of friendship to Mr Jones, and partly out of regard to your sister, would never have it mentioned, and did intend to conceal it from the world; and therefore, sir, if you had not mentioned it to me first, I am certain I should never have thought it belonged to me to say anything of the matter, either to your worship or any other person.”\n\nWe have remarked somewhere already, that it is possible for a man to convey a lie in the words of truth; this was the case at present; for Blifil had, in fact, told Dowling what he now related, but had not imposed upon him, nor indeed had imagined he was able so to do. In reality, the promises which Blifil had made to Dowling were the motives which had induced him to secrecy; and, as he now very plainly saw Blifil would not be able to keep them, he thought proper now to make this confession, which the promises of forgiveness, joined to the threats, the voice, the looks of Allworthy, and the discoveries he had made before, extorted from him, who was besides taken unawares, and had no time to consider of evasions.\n\nAllworthy appeared well satisfied with this relation, and, having enjoined on Dowling strict silence as to what had past, conducted that gentleman himself to the door, lest he should see Blifil, who was returned to his chamber, where he exulted in the thoughts of his last deceit on his uncle, and little suspected what had since passed below-stairs.\n\nAs Allworthy was returning to his room he met Mrs Miller in the entry, who, with a face all pale and full of terror, said to him, “O! sir, I find this wicked woman hath been with you, and you know all; yet do not on this account abandon the poor young man. Consider, sir, he was ignorant it was his own mother; and the discovery itself will most probably break his heart, without your unkindness.”\n\n“Madam,” says Allworthy, “I am under such an astonishment at what I have heard, that I am really unable to satisfy you; but come with me into my room. Indeed, Mrs Miller, I have made surprizing discoveries, and you shall soon know them.”\n\nThe poor woman followed him trembling; and now Allworthy, going up to Mrs Waters, took her by the hand, and then, turning to Mrs Miller, said, “What reward shall I bestow upon this gentlewoman, for the services she hath done me?--O! Mrs Miller, you have a thousand times heard me call the young man to whom you are so faithful a friend, my son. Little did I then think he was indeed related to me at all.--Your friend, madam, is my nephew; he is the brother of that wicked viper which I have so long nourished in my bosom.--She will herself tell you the whole story, and how the youth came to pass for her son. Indeed, Mrs Miller, I am convinced that he hath been wronged, and that I have been abused; abused by one whom you too justly suspected of being a villain. He is, in truth, the worst of villains.”\n\nThe joy which Mrs Miller now felt bereft her of the power of speech, and might perhaps have deprived her of her senses, if not of life, had not a friendly shower of tears come seasonably to her relief. At length, recovering so far from her transport as to be able to speak, she cried, “And is my dear Mr Jones then your nephew, sir, and not the son of this lady? And are your eyes opened to him at last? And shall I live to see him as happy as he deserves?” “He certainly is my nephew,” says Allworthy, “and I hope all the rest.”--“And is this the dear good woman, the person,” cries she, “to whom all this discovery is owing?”--“She is indeed,” says Allworthy.--“Why, then,” cried Mrs Miller, upon her knees, “may Heaven shower down its choicest blessings upon her head, and for this one good action forgive her all her sins, be they never so many!”\n\nMrs Waters then informed them that she believed Jones would very shortly be released; for that the surgeon was gone, in company with a nobleman, to the justice who committed him, in order to certify that Mr Fitzpatrick was out of all manner of danger, and to procure his prisoner his liberty.\n\nAllworthy said he should be glad to find his nephew there at his return home; but that he was then obliged to go on some business of consequence. He then called to a servant to fetch him a chair, and presently left the two ladies together.\n\nMr Blifil, hearing the chair ordered, came downstairs to attend upon his uncle; for he never was deficient in such acts of duty. He asked his uncle if he was going out, which is a civil way of asking a man whither he is going: to which the other making no answer, he again desired to know when he would be pleased to return?--Allworthy made no answer to this neither, till he was just going into his chair, and then, turning about, he said--“Harkee, sir, do you find out, before my return, the letter which your mother sent me on her death-bed.” Allworthy then departed, and left Blifil in a situation to be envied only by a man who is just going to be hanged.\n\nA further continuation.\nAllworthy took an opportunity, whilst he was in the chair, of reading the letter from Jones to Sophia, which Western delivered him; and there were some expressions in it concerning himself which drew tears from his eyes. At length he arrived at Mr Western's, and was introduced to Sophia.\n\nWhen the first ceremonies were past, and the gentleman and lady had taken their chairs, a silence of some minutes ensued; during which the latter, who had been prepared for the visit by her father, sat playing with her fan, and had every mark of confusion both in her countenance and behaviour. At length Allworthy, who was himself a little disconcerted, began thus: “I am afraid, Miss Western, my family hath been the occasion of giving you some uneasiness; to which, I fear, I have innocently become more instrumental than I intended. Be assured, madam, had I at first known how disagreeable the proposals had been, I should not have suffered you to have been so long persecuted. I hope, therefore, you will not think the design of this visit is to trouble you with any further solicitations of that kind, but entirely to relieve you from them.”\n\n“Sir,” said Sophia, with a little modest hesitation, “this behaviour is most kind and generous, and such as I could expect only from Mr Allworthy; but as you have been so kind to mention this matter, you will pardon me for saying it hath, indeed, given me great uneasiness, and hath been the occasion of my suffering much cruel treatment from a father who was, till that unhappy affair, the tenderest and fondest of all parents. I am convinced, sir, you are too good and generous to resent my refusal of your nephew. Our inclinations are not in our own power; and whatever may be his merit, I cannot force them in his favour.” “I assure you, most amiable young lady,” said Allworthy, “I am capable of no such resentment, had the person been my own son, and had I entertained the highest esteem for him. For you say truly, madam, we cannot force our inclinations, much less can they be directed by another.” “Oh! sir,” answered Sophia, “every word you speak proves you deserve that good, that great, that benevolent character the whole world allows you. I assure you, sir, nothing less than the certain prospect of future misery could have made me resist the commands of my father.” “I sincerely believe you, madam,” replied Allworthy, “and I heartily congratulate you on your prudent foresight, since by so justifiable a resistance you have avoided misery indeed!” “You speak now, Mr Allworthy,” cries she, “with a delicacy which few men are capable of feeling! but surely, in my opinion, to lead our lives with one to whom we are indifferent must be a state of wretchedness.----Perhaps that wretchedness would be even increased by a sense of the merits of an object to whom we cannot give our affections. If I had married Mr Blifil--” “Pardon my interrupting you, madam,” answered Allworthy, “but I cannot bear the supposition.--Believe me, Miss Western, I rejoice from my heart, I rejoice in your escape.--I have discovered the wretch for whom you have suffered all this cruel violence from your father to be a villain.” “How, sir!” cries Sophia--“you must believe this surprizes me.”--“It hath surprized me, madam,” answered Allworthy, “and so it will the world.----But I have acquainted you with the real truth.” “Nothing but truth,” says Sophia, “can, I am convinced, come from the lips of Mr Allworthy.----Yet, sir, such sudden, such unexpected news.----Discovered, you say----may villany be ever so!”--“You will soon enough hear the story,” cries Allworthy;--“at present let us not mention so detested a name.--I have another matter of a very serious nature to propose.--O! Miss Western, I know your vast worth, nor can I so easily part with the ambition of being allied to it.--I have a near relation, madam, a young man whose character is, I am convinced, the very opposite to that of this wretch, and whose fortune I will make equal to what his was to have been. Could I, madam, hope you would admit a visit from him?” Sophia, after a minute's silence, answered, “I will deal with the utmost sincerity with Mr Allworthy. His character, and the obligation I have just received from him, demand it. I have determined at present to listen to no such proposals from any person. My only desire is to be restored to the affection of my father, and to be again the mistress of his family. This, sir, I hope to owe to your good offices. Let me beseech you, let me conjure you, by all the goodness which I, and all who know you, have experienced, do not, the very moment when you have released me from one persecution, do not engage me in another as miserable and as fruitless.” “Indeed, Miss Western,” replied Allworthy, “I am capable of no such conduct; and if this be your resolution, he must submit to the disappointment, whatever torments he may suffer under it.” “I must smile now, Mr Allworthy,” answered Sophia, “when you mention the torments of a man whom I do not know, and who can consequently have so little acquaintance with me.” “Pardon me, dear young lady,” cries Allworthy, “I begin now to be afraid he hath had too much acquaintance for the repose of his future days; since, if ever man was capable of a sincere, violent, and noble passion, such, I am convinced, is my unhappy nephew's for Miss Western.” “A nephew of your's, Mr Allworthy!” answered Sophia. “It is surely strange. I never heard of him before.” “Indeed, madam,” cries Allworthy, “it is only the circumstance of his being my nephew to which you are a stranger, and which, till this day, was a secret to me.--Mr Jones, who has long loved you, he! he is my nephew!” “Mr Jones your nephew, sir!” cries Sophia, “can it be possible?”--“He is, indeed, madam,” answered Allworthy; “he is my own sister's son--as such I shall always own him; nor am I ashamed of owning him. I am much more ashamed of my past behaviour to him; but I was as ignorant of his merit as of his birth. Indeed, Miss Western, I have used him cruelly----Indeed I have.”--Here the good man wiped his eyes, and after a short pause proceeded--“I never shall be able to reward him for his sufferings without your assistance.----Believe me, most amiable young lady, I must have a great esteem of that offering which I make to your worth. I know he hath been guilty of faults; but there is great goodness of heart at the bottom. Believe me, madam, there is.” Here he stopped, seeming to expect an answer, which he presently received from Sophia, after she had a little recovered herself from the hurry of spirits into which so strange and sudden information had thrown her: “I sincerely wish you joy, sir, of a discovery in which you seem to have such satisfaction. I doubt not but you will have all the comfort you can promise yourself from it. The young gentleman hath certainly a thousand good qualities, which makes it impossible he should not behave well to such an uncle.”--“I hope, madam,” said Allworthy, “he hath those good qualities which must make him a good husband.--He must, I am sure, be of all men the most abandoned, if a lady of your merit should condescend--” “You must pardon me, Mr Allworthy,” answered Sophia; “I cannot listen to a proposal of this kind. Mr Jones, I am convinced, hath much merit; but I shall never receive Mr Jones as one who is to be my husband--Upon my honour I never will.”--“Pardon me, madam,” cries Allworthy, “if I am a little surprized, after what I have heard from Mr Western--I hope the unhappy young man hath done nothing to forfeit your good opinion, if he had ever the honour to enjoy it.--Perhaps, he may have been misrepresented to you, as he was to me. The same villany may have injured him everywhere.--He is no murderer, I assure you; as he hath been called.”--“Mr Allworthy,” answered Sophia, “I have told you my resolution. I wonder not at what my father hath told you; but, whatever his apprehensions or fears have been, if I know my heart, I have given no occasion for them; since it hath always been a fixed principle with me, never to have married without his consent. This is, I think, the duty of a child to a parent; and this, I hope, nothing could ever have prevailed with me to swerve from. I do not indeed conceive that the authority of any parent can oblige us to marry in direct opposition to our inclinations. To avoid a force of this kind, which I had reason to suspect, I left my father's house, and sought protection elsewhere. This is the truth of my story; and if the world, or my father, carry my intentions any farther, my own conscience will acquit me.” “I hear you, Miss Western,” cries Allworthy, “with admiration. I admire the justness of your sentiments; but surely there is more in this. I am cautious of offending you, young lady; but am I to look on all which I have hitherto heard or seen as a dream only? And have you suffered so much cruelty from your father on the account of a man to whom you have been always absolutely indifferent?” “I beg, Mr Allworthy,” answered Sophia, “you will not insist on my reasons;--yes, I have suffered indeed; I will not, Mr Allworthy, conceal----I will be very sincere with you--I own I had a great opinion of Mr Jones--I believe--I know I have suffered for my opinion--I have been treated cruelly by my aunt, as well as by my father; but that is now past--I beg I may not be farther pressed; for, whatever hath been, my resolution is now fixed. Your nephew, sir, hath many virtues--he hath great virtues, Mr Allworthy. I question not but he will do you honour in the world, and make you happy.”--“I wish I could make him so, madam,” replied Allworthy; “but that I am convinced is only in your power. It is that conviction which hath made me so earnest a solicitor in his favour.” “You are deceived indeed, sir; you are deceived,” said Sophia. “I hope not by him. It is sufficient to have deceived me. Mr Allworthy, I must insist on being pressed no farther on this subject. I should be sorry--nay, I will not injure him in your favour. I wish Mr Jones very well. I sincerely wish him well; and I repeat it again to you, whatever demerit he may have to me, I am certain he hath many good qualities. I do not disown my former thoughts; but nothing can ever recal them. At present there is not a man upon earth whom I would more resolutely reject than Mr Jones; nor would the addresses of Mr Blifil himself be less agreeable to me.”\n\nWestern had been long impatient for the event of this conference, and was just now arrived at the door to listen; when, having heard the last sentiments of his daughter's heart, he lost all temper, and, bursting open the door in a rage, cried out--“It is a lie! It is a d--n'd lie! It is all owing to that d--n'd rascal Jones; and if she could get at un, she'd ha un any hour of the day.” Here Allworthy interposed, and addressing himself to the squire with some anger in his look, he said, “Mr Western, you have not kept your word with me. You promised to abstain from all violence.”--“Why, so I did,” cries Western, “as long as it was possible; but to hear a wench telling such confounded lies----Zounds! doth she think, if she can make vools of other volk, she can make one of me?--No, no, I know her better than thee dost.” “I am sorry to tell you, sir,” answered Allworthy, “it doth not appear, by your behaviour to this young lady, that you know her at all. I ask pardon for what I say: but I think our intimacy, your own desires, and the occasion justify me. She is your daughter, Mr Western, and I think she doth honour to your name. If I was capable of envy, I should sooner envy you on this account than any other man whatever.”--“Odrabbit it!” cries the squire, “I wish she was thine, with all my heart--wouldst soon be glad to be rid of the trouble o' her.” “Indeed, my good friend,” answered Allworthy, “you yourself are the cause of all the trouble you complain of. Place that confidence in the young lady which she so well deserves, and I am certain you will be the happiest father on earth.”--“I confidence in her?” cries the squire. “'Sblood! what confidence can I place in her, when she won't do as I would ha' her? Let her gi' but her consent to marry as I would ha' her, and I'll place as much confidence in her as wouldst ha' me.”--“You have no right, neighbour,” answered Allworthy, “to insist on any such consent. A negative voice your daughter allows you, and God and nature have thought proper to allow you no more.”--“A negative voice!” cries the squire, “Ay! ay! I'll show you what a negative voice I ha.--Go along, go into your chamber, go, you stubborn----.” “Indeed, Mr Western,” said Allworthy, “indeed you use her cruelly--I cannot bear to see this--you shall, you must behave to her in a kinder manner. She deserves the best of treatment.” “Yes, yes,” said the squire, “I know what she deserves: now she's gone, I'll shew you what she deserves. See here, sir, here is a letter from my cousin, my Lady Bellaston, in which she is so kind to gi' me to understand that the fellow is got out of prison again; and here she advises me to take all the care I can o' the wench. Odzookers! neighbour Allworthy, you don't know what it is to govern a daughter.”\n\nThe squire ended his speech with some compliments to his own sagacity; and then Allworthy, after a formal preface, acquainted him with the whole discovery which he had made concerning Jones, with his anger to Blifil, and with every particular which hath been disclosed to the reader in the preceding chapters.\n\nMen over-violent in their dispositions are, for the most part, as changeable in them. No sooner then was Western informed of Mr Allworthy's intention to make Jones his heir, than he joined heartily with the uncle in every commendation of the nephew, and became as eager for her marriage with Jones as he had before been to couple her to Blifil.\n\nHere Mr Allworthy was again forced to interpose, and to relate what had passed between him and Sophia, at which he testified great surprize.\n\nThe squire was silent a moment, and looked wild with astonishment at this account.--At last he cried out, “Why, what can be the meaning of this, neighbour Allworthy? Vond o'un she was, that I'll be sworn to.----Odzookers! I have hit o't. As sure as a gun I have hit o' the very right o't. It's all along o' zister. The girl hath got a hankering after this son of a whore of a lord. I vound 'em together at my cousin my Lady Bellaston's. He hath turned the head o' her, that's certain--but d--n me if he shall ha her--I'll ha no lords nor courtiers in my vamily.”\n\nAllworthy now made a long speech, in which he repeated his resolution to avoid all violent measures, and very earnestly recommended gentle methods to Mr Western, as those by which he might be assured of succeeding best with his daughter. He then took his leave, and returned back to Mrs Miller, but was forced to comply with the earnest entreaties of the squire, in promising to bring Mr Jones to visit him that afternoon, that he might, as he said, “make all matters up with the young gentleman.” At Mr Allworthy's departure, Western promised to follow his advice in his behaviour to Sophia, saying, “I don't know how 'tis, but d--n me, Allworthy, if you don't make me always do just as you please; and yet I have as good an estate as you, and am in the commission of the peace as well as yourself.”\n\nWherein the history begins to draw towards a conclusion.\n\nWhen Allworthy returned to his lodgings, he heard Mr Jones was just arrived before him. He hurried therefore instantly into an empty chamber, whither he ordered Mr Jones to be brought to him alone.\n\nIt is impossible to conceive a more tender or moving scene than the meeting between the uncle and nephew (for Mrs Waters, as the reader may well suppose, had at her last visit discovered to him the secret of his birth). The first agonies of joy which were felt on both sides are indeed beyond my power to describe: I shall not therefore attempt it. After Allworthy had raised Jones from his feet, where he had prostrated himself, and received him into his arms, “O my child!” he cried, “how have I been to blame! how have I injured you! What amends can I ever make you for those unkind, those unjust suspicions which I have entertained, and for all the sufferings they have occasioned to you?” “Am I not now made amends?” cries Jones. “Would not my sufferings, if they had been ten times greater, have been now richly repaid? O my dear uncle, this goodness, this tenderness overpowers, unmans, destroys me. I cannot bear the transports which flow so fast upon me. To be again restored to your presence, to your favour; to be once more thus kindly received by my great, my noble, my generous benefactor.”--“Indeed, child,” cries Allworthy, “I have used you cruelly.”----He then explained to him all the treachery of Blifil, and again repeated expressions of the utmost concern, for having been induced by that treachery to use him so ill. “O, talk not so!” answered Jones; “indeed, sir, you have used me nobly. The wisest man might be deceived as you were; and, under such a deception, the best must have acted just as you did. Your goodness displayed itself in the midst of your anger, just as it then seemed. I owe everything to that goodness, of which I have been most unworthy. Do not put me on self-accusation, by carrying your generous sentiments too far. Alas! sir, I have not been punished more than I have deserved; and it shall be the whole business of my future life to deserve that happiness you now bestow on me; for, believe me, my dear uncle, my punishment hath not been thrown away upon me: though I have been a great, I am not a hardened sinner; I thank Heaven, I have had time to reflect on my past life, where, though I cannot charge myself with any gross villany, yet I can discern follies and vices more than enough to repent and to be ashamed of; follies which have been attended with dreadful consequences to myself, and have brought me to the brink of destruction.” “I am rejoiced, my dear child,” answered Allworthy, “to hear you talk thus sensibly; for as I am convinced hypocrisy (good Heaven! how have I been imposed on by it in others!) was never among your faults, so I can readily believe all you say. You now see, Tom, to what dangers imprudence alone may subject virtue (for virtue, I am now convinced, you love in a great degree). Prudence is indeed the duty which we owe to ourselves; and if we will be so much our own enemies as to neglect it, we are not to wonder if the world is deficient in discharging their duty to us; for when a man lays the foundation of his own ruin, others will, I am afraid, be too apt to build upon it. You say, however, you have seen your errors, and will reform them. I firmly believe you, my dear child; and therefore, from this moment, you shall never be reminded of them by me. Remember them only yourself so far as for the future to teach you the better to avoid them; but still remember, for your comfort, that there is this great difference between those faults which candor may construe into imprudence, and those which can be deduced from villany only. The former, perhaps, are even more apt to subject a man to ruin; but if he reform, his character will, at length, be totally retrieved; the world, though not immediately, will in time be reconciled to him; and he may reflect, not without some mixture of pleasure, on the dangers he hath escaped; but villany, my boy, when once discovered is irretrievable; the stains which this leaves behind, no time will wash away. The censures of mankind will pursue the wretch, their scorn will abash him in publick; and if shame drives him into retirement, he will go to it with all those terrors with which a weary child, who is afraid of hobgoblins, retreats from company to go to bed alone. Here his murdered conscience will haunt him.--Repose, like a false friend, will fly from him. Wherever he turns his eyes, horror presents itself; if he looks backward, unavailable repentance treads on his heels; if forward, incurable despair stares him in the face, till, like a condemned prisoner confined in a dungeon, he detests his present condition, and yet dreads the consequence of that hour which is to relieve him from it. Comfort yourself, I say, my child, that this is not your case; and rejoice with thankfulness to him who hath suffered you to see your errors, before they have brought on you that destruction to which a persistance in even those errors must have led you. You have deserted them; and the prospect now before you is such, that happiness seems in your own power.” At these words Jones fetched a deep sigh; upon which, when Allworthy remonstrated, he said, “Sir, I will conceal nothing from you: I fear there is one consequence of my vices I shall never be able to retrieve. O, my dear uncle! I have lost a treasure.” “You need say no more,” answered Allworthy; “I will be explicit with you; I know what you lament; I have seen the young lady, and have discoursed with her concerning you. This I must insist on, as an earnest of your sincerity in all you have said, and of the stedfastness of your resolution, that you obey me in one instance. To abide intirely by the determination of the young lady, whether it shall be in your favour or no. She hath already suffered enough from solicitations which I hate to think of; she shall owe no further constraint to my family: I know her father will be as ready to torment her now on your account as he hath formerly been on another's; but I am determined she shall suffer no more confinement, no more violence, no more uneasy hours.” “O, my dear uncle!” answered Jones, “lay, I beseech you, some command on me, in which I shall have some merit in obedience. Believe me, sir, the only instance in which I could disobey you would be to give an uneasy moment to my Sophia. No, sir, if I am so miserable to have incurred her displeasure beyond all hope of forgiveness, that alone, with the dreadful reflection of causing her misery, will be sufficient to overpower me. To call Sophia mine is the greatest, and now the only additional blessing which heaven can bestow; but it is a blessing which I must owe to her alone.” “I will not flatter you, child,” cries Allworthy; “I fear your case is desperate: I never saw stronger marks of an unalterable resolution in any person than appeared in her vehement declarations against receiving your addresses; for which, perhaps, you can account better than myself.” “Oh, sir! I can account too well,” answered Jones; “I have sinned against her beyond all hope of pardon; and guilty as I am, my guilt unfortunately appears to her in ten times blacker than the real colours. O, my dear uncle! I find my follies are irretrievable; and all your goodness cannot save me from perdition.”\n\nA servant now acquainted them that Mr Western was below-stairs; for his eagerness to see Jones could not wait till the afternoon. Upon which Jones, whose eyes were full of tears, begged his uncle to entertain Western a few minutes, till he a little recovered himself; to which the good man consented, and, having ordered Mr Western to be shewn into a parlour, went down to him.\n\nMrs Miller no sooner heard that Jones was alone (for she had not yet seen him since his release from prison) than she came eagerly into the room, and, advancing towards Jones, wished him heartily joy of his new-found uncle and his happy reconciliation; adding, “I wish I could give you joy on another account, my dear child; but anything so inexorable I never saw.”\n\nJones, with some appearance of surprize, asked her what she meant. “Why then,” says she, “I have been with your young lady, and have explained all matters to her, as they were told to me by my son Nightingale. She can have no longer any doubt about the letter; of that I am certain; for I told her my son Nightingale was ready to take his oath, if she pleased, that it was all his own invention, and the letter of his inditing. I told her the very reason of sending the letter ought to recommend you to her the more, as it was all upon her account, and a plain proof that you was resolved to quit all your profligacy for the future; that you had never been guilty of a single instance of infidelity to her since your seeing her in town: I am afraid I went too far there; but Heaven forgive me! I hope your future behaviour will be my justification. I am sure I have said all I can; but all to no purpose. She remains inflexible. She says, she had forgiven many faults on account of youth; but expressed such detestation of the character of a libertine, that she absolutely silenced me. I often attempted to excuse you; but the justness of her accusation flew in my face. Upon my honour, she is a lovely woman, and one of the sweetest and most sensible creatures I ever saw. I could have almost kissed her for one expression she made use of. It was a sentiment worthy of Seneca, or of a bishop. `I once fancied madam.' and she, `I had discovered great goodness of heart in Mr Jones; and for that I own I had a sincere esteem; but an entire profligacy of manners will corrupt the best heart in the world; and all which a good-natured libertine can expect is, that we should mix some grains of pity with our contempt and abhorrence.' She is an angelic creature, that is the truth on't.” “O, Mrs Miller!” answered Jones, “can I bear to think that I have lost such an angel?” “Lost! no,” cries Mrs Miller; “I hope you have not lost her yet. Resolve to leave such vicious courses, and you may yet have hopes, nay, if she would remain inexorable, there is another young lady, a sweet pretty young lady, and a swinging fortune, who is absolutely dying for love of you. I heard of it this very morning, and I told it to Miss Western; nay, I went a little beyond the truth again; for I told her you had refused her; but indeed I knew you would refuse her. And here I must give you a little comfort; when I mentioned the young lady's name, who is no other than the pretty widow Hunt, I thought she turned pale; but when I said you had refused her, I will be sworn her face was all over scarlet in an instant; and these were her very words: `I will not deny but that I believe he has some affection for me.'”\n\nHere the conversation was interrupted by the arrival of Western, who could no longer be kept out of the room even by the authority of Allworthy himself; though this, as we have often seen, had a wonderful power over him.\n\nWestern immediately went up to Jones, crying out, “My old friend Tom, I am glad to see thee with all my heart! all past must be forgotten; I could not intend any affront to thee, because, as Allworthy here knows, nay, dost know it thyself, I took thee for another person; and where a body means no harm, what signifies a hasty word or two? One Christian must forget and forgive another.” “I hope, sir,” said Jones, “I shall never forget the many obligations I have had to you; but as for any offence towards me, I declare I am an utter stranger.” “A't,” says Western, “then give me thy fist; a't as hearty an honest cock as any in the kingdom. Come along with me; I'll carry thee to thy mistress this moment.” Here Allworthy interposed; and the squire being unable to prevail either with the uncle or nephew, was, after some litigation, obliged to consent to delay introducing Jones to Sophia till the afternoon; at which time Allworthy, as well in compassion to Jones as in compliance with the eager desires of Western, was prevailed upon to promise to attend at the tea-table.\n\nThe conversation which now ensued was pleasant enough; and with which, had it happened earlier in our history, we would have entertained our reader; but as we have now leisure only to attend to what is very material, it shall suffice to say that matters being entirely adjusted as to the afternoon visit Mr Western again returned home.\n\nThe history draws nearer to a conclusion.\nWhen Mr Western was departed, Jones began to inform Mr Allworthy and Mrs Miller that his liberty had been procured by two noble lords, who, together with two surgeons and a friend of Mr Nightingale's, had attended the magistrate by whom he had been committed, and by whom, on the surgeons' oaths, that the wounded person was out of all manner of danger from his wound, he was discharged.\n\nOne only of these lords, he said, he had ever seen before, and that no more than once; but the other had greatly surprized him by asking his pardon for an offence he had been guilty of towards him, occasioned, he said, entirely by his ignorance who he was.\n\nNow the reality of the case, with which Jones was not acquainted till afterwards, was this:--The lieutenant whom Lord Fellamar had employed, according to the advice of Lady Bellaston, to press Jones as a vagabond into the sea-service, when he came to report to his lordship the event which we have before seen, spoke very favourably of the behaviour of Mr Jones on all accounts, and strongly assured that lord that he must have mistaken the person, for that Jones was certainly a gentleman; insomuch that his lordship, who was strictly a man of honour, and would by no means have been guilty of an action which the world in general would have condemned, began to be much concerned for the advice which he had taken.\n\nWithin a day or two after this, Lord Fellamar happened to dine with the Irish peer, who, in a conversation upon the duel, acquainted his company with the character of Fitzpatrick; to which, indeed, he did not do strict justice, especially in what related to his lady. He said she was the most innocent, the most injured woman alive, and that from compassion alone he had undertaken her cause. He then declared an intention of going the next morning to Fitzpatrick's lodgings, in order to prevail with him, if possible, to consent to a separation from his wife, who, the peer said, was in apprehensions for her life, if she should ever return to be under the power of her husband. Lord Fellamar agreed to go with him, that he might satisfy himself more concerning Jones and the circumstances of the duel; for he was by no means easy concerning the part he had acted. The moment his lordship gave a hint of his readiness to assist in the delivery of the lady, it was eagerly embraced by the other nobleman, who depended much on the authority of Lord Fellamar, as he thought it would greatly contribute to awe Fitzpatrick into a compliance; and perhaps he was in the right; for the poor Irishman no sooner saw these noble peers had undertaken the cause of his wife than he submitted, and articles of separation were soon drawn up and signed between the parties.\n\nFitzpatrick, who had been so well satisfied by Mrs Waters concerning the innocence of his wife with Jones at Upton, or perhaps, from some other reasons, was now become so indifferent to that matter, that he spoke highly in favour of Jones to Lord Fellamar, took all the blame upon himself, and said the other had behaved very much like a gentleman and a man of honour; and upon that lord's further enquiry concerning Mr Jones, Fitzpatrick told him he was nephew to a gentleman of very great fashion and fortune, which was the account he had just received from Mrs Waters after her interview with Dowling.\n\nLord Fellamar now thought it behoved him to do everything in his power to make satisfaction to a gentleman whom he had so grossly injured, and without any consideration of rivalship (for he had now given over all thoughts of Sophia), determined to procure Mr Jones's liberty, being satisfied, as well from Fitzpatrick as his surgeon, that the wound was not mortal. He therefore prevailed with the Irish peer to accompany him to the place where Jones was confined, to whom he behaved as we have already related.\n\nWhen Allworthy returned to his lodgings, he immediately carried Jones into his room, and then acquainted him with the whole matter, as well what he had heard from Mrs Waters as what he had discovered from Mr Dowling.\n\nJones expressed great astonishment and no less concern at this account, but without making any comment or observation upon it. And now a message was brought from Mr Blifil, desiring to know if his uncle was at leisure that he might wait upon him. Allworthy started and turned pale, and then in a more passionate tone than I believe he had ever used before, bid the servant tell Blifil he knew him not. “Consider, dear sir,” cries Jones, in a trembling voice. “I have considered,” answered Allworthy, “and you yourself shall carry my message to the villain. No one can carry him the sentence of his own ruin so properly as the man whose ruin he hath so villanously contrived.” “Pardon me, dear sir,” said Jones; “a moment's reflection will, I am sure, convince you of the contrary. What might perhaps be but justice from another tongue, would from mine be insult; and to whom?--my own brother and your nephew. Nor did he use me so barbarously--indeed, that would have been more inexcusable than anything he hath done. Fortune may tempt men of no very bad dispositions to injustice; but insults proceed only from black and rancorous minds, and have no temptations to excuse them. Let me beseech you, sir, to do nothing by him in the present height of your anger. Consider, my dear uncle, I was not myself condemned unheard.” Allworthy stood silent a moment, and then, embracing Jones, he said, with tears gushing from his eyes, “O my child! to what goodness have I been so long blind!”\n\nMrs Miller entering the room at that moment, after a gentle rap which was not perceived, and seeing Jones in the arms of his uncle, the poor woman in an agony of joy fell upon her knees, and burst forth into the most ecstatic thanksgivings to heaven for what had happened; then, running to Jones, she embraced him eagerly, crying, “My dearest friend, I wish you joy a thousand and a thousand times of this blest day.” And next Mr Allworthy himself received the same congratulations. To which he answered, “Indeed, indeed, Mrs Miller, I am beyond expression happy.” Some few more raptures having passed on all sides, Mrs Miller desired them both to walk down to dinner in the parlour, where she said there were a very happy set of people assembled--being indeed no other than Mr Nightingale and his bride, and his cousin Harriet with her bridegroom.\n\nAllworthy excused himself from dining with the company, saying he had ordered some little thing for him and his nephew in his own apartment, for that they had much private business to discourse of; but would not resist promising the good woman that both he and Jones would make part of her society at supper.\n\nMrs Miller then asked what was to be done with Blifil? “for indeed,” says she, “I cannot be easy while such a villain is in my house.”--Allworthy answered, “He was as uneasy as herself on the same account.” “Oh!” cries she, “if that be the case, leave the matter to me, I'll soon show him the outside out of my doors, I warrant you. Here are two or three lusty fellows below-stairs.” “There will be no need of any violence,” cries Allworthy; “if you will carry him a message from me, he will, I am convinced, depart of his own accord.” “Will I?” said Mrs Miller; “I never did anything in my life with a better will.” Here Jones interfered, and said, “He had considered the matter better, and would, if Mr Allworthy pleased, be himself the messenger. I know,” says he, “already enough of your pleasure, sir, and I beg leave to acquaint him with it by my own words. Let me beseech you, sir,” added he, “to reflect on the dreadful consequences of driving him to violent and sudden despair. How unfit, alas! is this poor man to die in his present situation.” This suggestion had not the least effect on Mrs Miller. She left the room, crying, “You are too good, Mr Jones, infinitely too good to live in this world.” But it made a deeper impression on Allworthy. “My good child,” said he, “I am equally astonished at the goodness of your heart, and the quickness of your understanding. Heaven indeed forbid that this wretch should be deprived of any means or time for repentance! That would be a shocking consideration indeed. Go to him, therefore, and use your own discretion; yet do not flatter him with any hopes of my forgiveness; for I shall never forgive villany farther than my religion obliges me, and that extends not either to our bounty or our conversation.”\n\nJones went up to Blifil's room, whom he found in a situation which moved his pity, though it would have raised a less amiable passion in many beholders. He cast himself on his bed, where he lay abandoning himself to despair, and drowned in tears; not in such tears as flow from contrition, and wash away guilt from minds which have been seduced or surprized into it unawares, against the bent of their natural dispositions, as will sometimes happen from human frailty, even to the good; no, these tears were such as the frighted thief sheds in his cart, and are indeed the effects of that concern which the most savage natures are seldom deficient in feeling for themselves.\n\nIt would be unpleasant and tedious to paint this scene in full length. Let it suffice to say, that the behaviour of Jones was kind to excess. He omitted nothing which his invention could supply, to raise and comfort the drooping spirits of Blifil, before he communicated to him the resolution of his uncle that he must quit the house that evening. He offered to furnish him with any money he wanted, assured him of his hearty forgiveness of all he had done against him, that he would endeavour to live with him hereafter as a brother, and would leave nothing unattempted to effectuate a reconciliation with his uncle.\n\nBlifil was at first sullen and silent, balancing in his mind whether he should yet deny all; but, finding at last the evidence too strong against him, he betook himself at last to confession. He then asked pardon of his brother in the most vehement manner, prostrated himself on the ground, and kissed his feet; in short he was now as remarkably mean as he had been before remarkably wicked.\n\nJones could not so far check his disdain, but that it a little discovered itself in his countenance at this extreme servility. He raised his brother the moment he could from the ground, and advised him to bear his afflictions more like a man; repeating, at the same time, his promises, that he would do all in his power to lessen them; for which Blifil, making many professions of his unworthiness, poured forth a profusion of thanks; and then, he having declared he would immediately depart to another lodging, Jones returned to his uncle.\n\nAmong other matters, Allworthy now acquainted Jones with the discovery which he had made concerning the £500 bank-notes. “I have,” said he, “already consulted a lawyer, who tells me, to my great astonishment, that there is no punishment for a fraud of this kind. Indeed, when I consider the black ingratitude of this fellow toward you, I think a highwayman, compared to him, is an innocent person.”\n\n“Good Heaven!” says Jones, “is it possible?--I am shocked beyond measure at this news. I thought there was not an honester fellow in the world.----The temptation of such a sum was too great for him to withstand; for smaller matters have come safe to me through his hand. Indeed, my dear uncle, you must suffer me to call it weakness rather than ingratitude; for I am convinced the poor fellow loves me, and hath done me some kindnesses, which I can never forget; nay, I believe he hath repented of this very act; for it is not above a day or two ago, when my affairs seemed in the most desperate situation, that he visited me in my confinement, and offered me any money I wanted. Consider, sir, what a temptation to a man who hath tasted such bitter distress, it must be, to have a sum in his possession which must put him and his family beyond any future possibility of suffering the like.”\n\n“Child,” cries Allworthy, “you carry this forgiving temper too far. Such mistaken mercy is not only weakness, but borders on injustice, and is very pernicious to society, as it encourages vice. The dishonesty of this fellow I might, perhaps, have pardoned, but never his ingratitude. And give me leave to say, when we suffer any temptation to atone for dishonesty itself, we are as candid and merciful as we ought to be; and so far I confess I have gone; for I have often pitied the fate of a highwayman, when I have been on the grand jury; and have more than once applied to the judge on the behalf of such as have had any mitigating circumstances in their case; but when dishonesty is attended with any blacker crime, such as cruelty, murder, ingratitude, or the like, compassion and forgiveness then become faults. I am convinced the fellow is a villain, and he shall be punished; at least as far as I can punish him.”\n\nThis was spoken with so stern a voice, that Jones did not think proper to make any reply; besides, the hour appointed by Mr Western now drew so near, that he had barely time left to dress himself. Here therefore ended the present dialogue, and Jones retired to another room, where Partridge attended, according to order, with his cloaths.\n\nPartridge had scarce seen his master since the happy discovery. The poor fellow was unable either to contain or express his transports. He behaved like one frantic, and made almost as many mistakes while he was dressing Jones as I have seen made by Harlequin in dressing himself on the stage.\n\nHis memory, however, was not in the least deficient. He recollected now many omens and presages of this happy event, some of which he had remarked at the time, but many more he now remembered; nor did he omit the dreams he had dreamt the evening before his meeting with Jones; and concluded with saying, “I always told your honour something boded in my mind that you would one time or other have it in your power to make my fortune.” Jones assured him that this boding should as certainly be verified with regard to him as all the other omens had been to himself; which did not a little add to all the raptures which the poor fellow had already conceived on account of his master.\n\nApproaching still nearer to the end.\n\nJones, being now completely dressed, attended his uncle to Mr Western's. He was, indeed, one of the finest figures ever beheld, and his person alone would have charmed the greater part of womankind; but we hope it hath already appeared in this history that Nature, when she formed him, did not totally rely, as she sometimes doth, on this merit only, to recommend her work.\n\nSophia, who, angry as she was, was likewise set forth to the best advantage, for which I leave my female readers to account, appeared so extremely beautiful, that even Allworthy, when he saw her, could not forbear whispering Western, that he believed she was the finest creature in the world. To which Western answered, in a whisper, overheard by all present, “So much the better for Tom;--for d--n me if he shan't ha the tousling her.” Sophia was all over scarlet at these words, while Tom's countenance was altogether as pale, and he was almost ready to sink from his chair.\n\nThe tea-table was scarce removed before Western lugged Allworthy out of the room, telling him he had business of consequence to impart, and must speak to him that instant in private, before he forgot it.\n\nThe lovers were now alone, and it will, I question not, appear strange to many readers, that those who had so much to say to one another when danger and difficulty attended their conversation, and who seemed so eager to rush into each other's arms when so many bars lay in their way, now that with safety they were at liberty to say or do whatever they pleased, should both remain for some time silent and motionless; insomuch that a stranger of moderate sagacity might have well concluded they were mutually indifferent; but so it was, however strange it may seem; both sat with their eyes cast downwards on the ground, and for some minutes continued in perfect silence.\n\nMr Jones during this interval attempted once or twice to speak, but was absolutely incapable, muttering only, or rather sighing out, some broken words; when Sophia at length, partly out of pity to him, and partly to turn the discourse from the subject which she knew well enough he was endeavouring to open, said--\n\n“Sure, sir, you are the most fortunate man in the world in this discovery.” “And can you really, madam, think me so fortunate,” said Jones, sighing, “while I have incurred your displeasure?”--“Nay, sir,” says she, “as to that you best know whether you have deserved it.” “Indeed, madam,” answered he, “you yourself are as well apprized of all my demerits. Mrs Miller hath acquainted you with the whole truth. O! my Sophia, am I never to hope for forgiveness?”--“I think, Mr Jones,” said she, “I may almost depend on your own justice, and leave it to yourself to pass sentence on your own conduct.”--“Alas! madam,” answered he, “it is mercy, and not justice, which I implore at your hands. Justice I know must condemn me.--Yet not for the letter I sent to Lady Bellaston. Of that I most solemnly declare you have had a true account.” He then insisted much on the security given him by Nightingale of a fair pretence for breaking off, if, contrary to their expectations, her ladyship should have accepted his offer; but confest that he had been guilty of a great indiscretion to put such a letter as that into her power, “which,” said he, “I have dearly paid for, in the effect it has upon you.” “I do not, I cannot,” says she, “believe otherwise of that letter than you would have me. My conduct, I think, shews you clearly I do not believe there is much in that. And yet, Mr Jones, have I not enough to resent? After what past at Upton, so soon to engage in a new amour with another woman, while I fancied, and you pretended, your heart was bleeding for me? Indeed, you have acted strangely. Can I believe the passion you have profest to me to be sincere? Or, if I can, what happiness can I assure myself of with a man capable of so much inconstancy?” “O! my Sophia,” cries he, “do not doubt the sincerity of the purest passion that ever inflamed a human breast. Think, most adorable creature, of my unhappy situation, of my despair. Could I, my Sophia, have flattered myself with the most distant hopes of being ever permitted to throw myself at your feet in the manner I do now, it would not have been in the power of any other woman to have inspired a thought which the severest chastity could have condemned. Inconstancy to you! O Sophia! if you can have goodness enough to pardon what is past, do not let any cruel future apprehensions shut your mercy against me. No repentance was ever more sincere. O! let it reconcile me to my heaven in this dear bosom.” “Sincere repentance, Mr Jones,” answered she, “will obtain the pardon of a sinner, but it is from one who is a perfect judge of that sincerity. A human mind may be imposed on; nor is there any infallible method to prevent it. You must expect, however, that if I can be prevailed on by your repentance to pardon you, I will at least insist on the strongest proof of its sincerity.” “Name any proof in my power,” answered Jones eagerly. “Time,” replied she; “time alone, Mr Jones, can convince me that you are a true penitent, and have resolved to abandon these vicious courses, which I should detest you for, if I imagined you capable of persevering in them.” “Do not imagine it,” cries Jones. “On my knees I intreat, I implore your confidence, a confidence which it shall be the business of my life to deserve.” “Let it then,” said she, “be the business of some part of your life to shew me you deserve it. I think I have been explicit enough in assuring you, that, when I see you merit my confidence, you will obtain it. After what is past, sir, can you expect I should take you upon your word?”\n\nHe replied, “Don't believe me upon my word; I have a better security, a pledge for my constancy, which it is impossible to see and to doubt.” “What is that?” said Sophia, a little surprized. “I will show you, my charming angel,” cried Jones, seizing her hand and carrying her to the glass. “There, behold it there in that lovely figure, in that face, that shape, those eyes, that mind which shines through these eyes; can the man who shall be in possession of these be inconstant? Impossible! my Sophia; they would fix a Dorimant, a Lord Rochester. You could not doubt it, if you could see yourself with any eyes but your own.” Sophia blushed and half smiled; but, forcing again her brow into a frown--“If I am to judge,” said she, “of the future by the past, my image will no more remain in your heart when I am out of your sight, than it will in this glass when I am out of the room.” “By heaven, by all that is sacred!” said Jones, “it never was out of my heart. The delicacy of your sex cannot conceive the grossness of ours, nor how little one sort of amour has to do with the heart.” “I will never marry a man,” replied Sophia, very gravely, “who shall not learn refinement enough to be as incapable as I am myself of making such a distinction.” “I will learn it,” said Jones. “I have learnt it already. The first moment of hope that my Sophia might be my wife taught it me at once; and all the rest of her sex from that moment became as little the objects of desire to my sense as of passion to my heart.” “Well,” says Sophia, “the proof of this must be from time. Your situation, Mr Jones, is now altered, and I assure you I have great satisfaction in the alteration. You will now want no opportunity of being near me, and convincing me that your mind is altered too.” “O! my angel,” cries Jones, “how shall I thank thy goodness! And are you so good to own that you have a satisfaction in my prosperity?----Believe me, believe me, madam, it is you alone have given a relish to that prosperity, since I owe to it the dear hope----O! my Sophia, let it not be a distant one.--I will be all obedience to your commands. I will not dare to press anything further than you permit me. Yet let me intreat you to appoint a short trial. O! tell me when I may expect you will be convinced of what is most solemnly true.” “When I have gone voluntarily thus far, Mr Jones,” said she, “I expect not to be pressed. Nay, I will not.”--“O! don't look unkindly thus, my Sophia,” cries he. “I do not, I dare not press you.--Yet permit me at least once more to beg you would fix the period. O! consider the impatience of love.”--“A twelvemonth, perhaps,” said she. “O! my Sophia,” cries he, “you have named an eternity.”--“Perhaps it may be something sooner,” says she; “I will not be teazed. If your passion for me be what I would have it, I think you may now be easy.”--“Easy! Sophia, call not such an exulting happiness as mine by so cold a name.----O! transporting thought! am I not assured that the blessed day will come, when I shall call you mine; when fears shall be no more; when I shall have that dear, that vast, that exquisite, ecstatic delight of making my Sophia happy?”--“Indeed, sir,” said she, “that day is in your own power.”--“O! my dear, my divine angel,” cried he, “these words have made me mad with joy.----But I must, I will thank those dear lips which have so sweetly pronounced my bliss.” He then caught her in his arms, and kissed her with an ardour he had never ventured before.\n\nAt this instant Western, who had stood some time listening, burst into the room, and, with his hunting voice and phrase, cried out, “To her, boy, to her, go to her.----That's it, little honeys, O that's it! Well! what, is it all over? Hath she appointed the day, boy? What, shall it be to-morrow or next day? It shan't be put off a minute longer than next day, I am resolved.” “Let me beseech you, sir,” says Jones, “don't let me be the occasion”----“Beseech mine a----,” cries Western. “I thought thou hadst been a lad of higher mettle than to give way to a parcel of maidenish tricks.----I tell thee 'tis all flimflam. Zoodikers! she'd have the wedding to-night with all her heart. Would'st not, Sophy? Come, confess, and be an honest girl for once. What, art dumb? Why dost not speak?” “Why should I confess, sir,” says Sophia, “since it seems you are so well acquainted with my thoughts?”----“That's a good girl,” cries he, “and dost consent then?” “No, indeed, sir,” says Sophia, “I have given no such consent.”---“And wunt not ha un then to-morrow, nor next day?” says Western.--“Indeed, sir,” says she, “I have no such intention.” “But I can tell thee,” replied he, “why hast nut; only because thou dost love to be disobedient, and to plague and vex thy father.” “Pray, sir,” said Jones, interfering----“I tell thee thou art a puppy,” cries he. “When I vorbid her, then it was all nothing but sighing and whining, and languishing and writing; now I am vor thee, she is against thee. All the spirit of contrary, that's all. She is above being guided and governed by her father, that is the whole truth on't. It is only to disoblige and contradict me.” “What would my papa have me do?” cries Sophia. “What would I ha thee do?” says he, “why, gi' un thy hand this moment.”--“Well, sir,” says Sophia, “I will obey you.--There is my hand, Mr Jones.” “Well, and will you consent to ha un to-morrow morning?” says Western.--“I will be obedient to you, sir,” cries she.--“Why then to-morrow morning be the day,” cries he. “Why then to-morrow morning shall be the day, papa, since you will have it so,” says Sophia. Jones then fell upon his knees, and kissed her hand in an agony of joy, while Western began to caper and dance about the room, presently crying out--“Where the devil is Allworthy? He is without now, a talking with that d--d lawyer Dowling, when he should be minding other matters.” He then sallied out in quest of him, and very opportunely left the lovers to enjoy a few tender minutes alone.\n\nBut he soon returned with Allworthy, saying, “If you won't believe me, you may ask her yourself. Hast nut gin thy consent, Sophy, to be married to-morrow?” “Such are your commands, sir,” cries Sophia, “and I dare not be guilty of disobedience.” “I hope, madam,” cries Allworthy, “my nephew will merit so much goodness, and will be always as sensible as myself of the great honour you have done my family. An alliance with so charming and so excellent a young lady would indeed be an honour to the greatest in England.” “Yes,” cries Western, “but if I had suffered her to stand shill I shall I, dilly dally, you might not have had that honour yet a while; I was forced to use a little fatherly authority to bring her to.” “I hope not, sir,” cries Allworthy, “I hope there is not the least constraint.” “Why, there,” cries Western, “you may bid her unsay all again if you will. Dost repent heartily of thy promise, dost not, Sophia?” “Indeed, papa,” cries she, “I do not repent, nor do I believe I ever shall, of any promise in favour of Mr Jones.” “Then, nephew,” cries Allworthy, “I felicitate you most heartily; for I think you are the happiest of men. And, madam, you will give me leave to congratulate you on this joyful occasion: indeed, I am convinced you have bestowed yourself on one who will be sensible of your great merit, and who will at least use his best endeavours to deserve it.” “His best endeavours!” cries Western, “that he will, I warrant un.----Harkee, Allworthy, I'll bet thee five pounds to a crown we have a boy to-morrow nine months; but prithee tell me what wut ha! Wut ha Burgundy, Champaigne, or what? for, please Jupiter, we'll make a night on't.” “Indeed, sir,” said Allworthy, “you must excuse me; both my nephew and I were engaged before I suspected this near approach of his happiness.”--“Engaged!” quoth the squire, “never tell me.--I won't part with thee to-night upon any occasion. Shalt sup here, please the lord Harry.” “You must pardon me, my dear neighbour!” answered Allworthy; “I have given a solemn promise, and that you know I never break.” “Why, prithee, who art engaged to?” cries the squire.----Allworthy then informed him, as likewise of the company.----“Odzookers!” answered the squire, “I will go with thee, and so shall Sophy! for I won't part with thee to-night; and it would be barbarous to part Tom and the girl.” This offer was presently embraced by Allworthy, and Sophia consented, having first obtained a private promise from her father that he would not mention a syllable concerning her marriage.\n\nIn which the history is concluded.\nYoung Nightingale had been that afternoon, by appointment, to wait on his father, who received him much more kindly than he expected. There likewise he met his uncle, who was returned to town in quest of his new-married daughter.\n\nThis marriage was the luckiest incident which could have happened to the young gentleman; for these brothers lived in a constant state of contention about the government of their children, both heartily despising the method which each other took. Each of them therefore now endeavoured, as much as he could, to palliate the offence which his own child had committed, and to aggravate the match of the other. This desire of triumphing over his brother, added to the many arguments which Allworthy had used, so strongly operated on the old gentleman that he met his son with a smiling countenance, and actually agreed to sup with him that evening at Mrs Miller's.\n\nAs for the other, who really loved his daughter with the most immoderate affection, there was little difficulty in inclining him to a reconciliation. He was no sooner informed by his nephew where his daughter and her husband were, than he declared he would instantly go to her. And when he arrived there he scarce suffered her to fall upon her knees before he took her up, and embraced her with a tenderness which affected all who saw him; and in less than a quarter of an hour was as well reconciled to both her and her husband as if he had himself joined their hands.\n\nIn this situation were affairs when Mr Allworthy and his company arrived to complete the happiness of Mrs Miller, who no sooner saw Sophia than she guessed everything that had happened; and so great was her friendship to Jones, that it added not a few transports to those she felt on the happiness of her own daughter.\n\nThere have not, I believe, been many instances of a number of people met together, where every one was so perfectly happy as in this company. Amongst whom the father of young Nightingale enjoyed the least perfect content; for, notwithstanding his affection for his son, notwithstanding the authority and the arguments of Allworthy, together with the other motive mentioned before, he could not so entirely be satisfied with his son's choice; and, perhaps, the presence of Sophia herself tended a little to aggravate and heighten his concern, as a thought now and then suggested itself that his son might have had that lady, or some other such. Not that any of the charms which adorned either the person or mind of Sophia created the uneasiness; it was the contents of her father's coffers which set his heart a longing. These were the charms which he could not bear to think his son had sacrificed to the daughter of Mrs Miller.\n\nThe brides were both very pretty women; but so totally were they eclipsed by the beauty of Sophia, that, had they not been two of the best-tempered girls in the world, it would have raised some envy in their breasts; for neither of their husbands could long keep his eyes from Sophia, who sat at the table like a queen receiving homage, or, rather, like a superior being receiving adoration from all around her. But it was an adoration which they gave, not which she exacted; for she was as much distinguished by her modesty and affability as by all her other perfections.\n\nThe evening was spent in much true mirth. All were happy, but those the most who had been most unhappy before. Their former sufferings and fears gave such a relish to their felicity as even love and fortune, in their fullest flow, could not have given without the advantage of such a comparison. Yet, as great joy, especially after a sudden change and revolution of circumstances, is apt to be silent, and dwells rather in the heart than on the tongue, Jones and Sophia appeared the least merry of the whole company; which Western observed with great impatience, often crying out to them, “Why dost not talk, boy? Why dost look so grave? Hast lost thy tongue, girl? Drink another glass of wine; sha't drink another glass.” And, the more to enliven her, he would sometimes sing a merry song, which bore some relation to matrimony and the loss of a maidenhead. Nay, he would have proceeded so far on that topic as to have driven her out of the room, if Mr Allworthy had not checkt him, sometimes by looks, and once or twice by a “Fie! Mr Western!” He began, indeed, once to debate the matter, and assert his right to talk to his own daughter as he thought fit; but, as nobody seconded him, he was soon reduced to order.\n\nNotwithstanding this little restraint, he was so pleased with the chearfulness and good-humour of the company, that he insisted on their meeting the next day at his lodgings. They all did so; and the lovely Sophia, who was now in private become a bride too, officiated as the mistress of the ceremonies, or, in the polite phrase, did the honours of the table. She had that morning given her hand to Jones, in the chapel at Doctors'-Commons, where Mr Allworthy, Mr Western, and Mrs Miller, were the only persons present.\n\nSophia had earnestly desired her father that no others of the company, who were that day to dine with him, should be acquainted with her marriage. The same secrecy was enjoined to Mrs Miller, and Jones undertook for Allworthy. This somewhat reconciled the delicacy of Sophia to the public entertainment which, in compliance with her father's will, she was obliged to go to, greatly against her own inclinations. In confidence of this secrecy she went through the day pretty well, till the squire, who was now advanced into the second bottle, could contain his joy no longer, but, filling out a bumper, drank a health to the bride. The health was immediately pledged by all present, to the great confusion of our poor blushing Sophia, and the great concern of Jones upon her account. To say truth, there was not a person present made wiser by this discovery; for Mrs Miller had whispered it to her daughter, her daughter to her husband, her husband to his sister, and she to all the rest.\n\nSophia now took the first opportunity of withdrawing with the ladies, and the squire sat in to his cups, in which he was, by degrees, deserted by all the company except the uncle of young Nightingale, who loved his bottle as well as Western himself. These two, therefore, sat stoutly to it during the whole evening, and long after that happy hour which had surrendered the charming Sophia to the eager arms of her enraptured Jones.\n\nThus, reader, we have at length brought our history to a conclusion, in which, to our great pleasure, though contrary, perhaps, to thy expectation, Mr Jones appears to be the happiest of all humankind; for what happiness this world affords equal to the possession of such a woman as Sophia, I sincerely own I have never yet discovered.\n\nAs to the other persons who have made any considerable figure in this history, as some may desire to know a little more concerning them, we will proceed, in as few words as possible, to satisfy their curiosity.\n\nAllworthy hath never yet been prevailed upon to see Blifil, but he hath yielded to the importunity of Jones, backed by Sophia, to settle £200 a-year upon him; to which Jones hath privately added a third. Upon this income he lives in one of the northern counties, about 200 miles distant from London, and lays up £200 a-year out of it, in order to purchase a seat in the next parliament from a neighbouring borough, which he has bargained for with an attourney there. He is also lately turned Methodist, in hopes of marrying a very rich widow of that sect, whose estate lies in that part of the kingdom.\n\nSquare died soon after he writ the before-mentioned letter; and as to Thwackum, he continues at his vicarage. He hath made many fruitless attempts to regain the confidence of Allworthy, or to ingratiate himself with Jones, both of whom he flatters to their faces, and abuses behind their backs. But in his stead, Mr Allworthy hath lately taken Mr Abraham Adams into his house, of whom Sophia is grown immoderately fond, and declares he shall have the tuition of her children.\n\nMrs Fitzpatrick is separated from her husband, and retains the little remains of her fortune. She lives in reputation at the polite end of the town, and is so good an economist, that she spends three times the income of her fortune, without running into debt. She maintains a perfect intimacy with the lady of the Irish peer; and in acts of friendship to her repays all obligations she owes her husband.\n\nMrs Western was soon reconciled to her niece Sophia, and hath spent two months together with her in the country. Lady Bellaston made the latter a formal visit at her return to town, where she behaved to Jones as a perfect stranger, and, with great civility, wished him joy on his marriage.\n\nMr Nightingale hath purchased an estate for his son in the neighbourhood of Jones, where the young gentleman, his lady, Mrs Miller, and her little daughter reside, and the most agreeable intercourse subsists between the two families.\n\nAs to those of lower account, Mrs Waters returned into the country, had a pension of £60 a-year settled upon her by Mr Allworthy, and is married to Parson Supple, on whom, at the instance of Sophia, Western hath bestowed a considerable living.\n\nBlack George, hearing the discovery that had been made, ran away, and was never since heard of; and Jones bestowed the money on his family, but not in equal proportions, for Molly had much the greatest share.\n\nAs for Partridge, Jones hath settled £50 a-year on him; and he hath again set up a school, in which he meets with much better encouragement than formerly, and there is now a treaty of marriage on foot between him and Miss Molly Seagrim, which, through the mediation of Sophia, is likely to take effect.\n\nWe now return to take leave of Mr Jones and Sophia, who, within two days after their marriage, attended Mr Western and Mr Allworthy into the country. Western hath resigned his family seat, and the greater part of his estate, to his son-in-law, and hath retired to a lesser house of his in another part of the country, which is better for hunting. Indeed, he is often as a visitant with Mr Jones, who, as well as his daughter, hath an infinite delight in doing everything in their power to please him. And this desire of theirs is attended with such success, that the old gentleman declares he was never happy in his life till now. He hath here a parlour and ante-chamber to himself, where he gets drunk with whom he pleases: and his daughter is still as ready as formerly to play to him whenever he desires it; for Jones hath assured her that, as, next to pleasing her, one of his highest satisfactions is to contribute to the happiness of the old man; so, the great duty which she expresses and performs to her father, renders her almost equally dear to him with the love which she bestows on himself.\n\nSophia hath already produced him two fine children, a boy and a girl, of whom the old gentleman is so fond, that he spends much of his time in the nursery, where he declares the tattling of his little grand-daughter, who is above a year and a half old, is sweeter music than the finest cry of dogs in England.\n\nAllworthy was likewise greatly liberal to Jones on the marriage, and hath omitted no instance of shewing his affection to him and his lady, who love him as a father. Whatever in the nature of Jones had a tendency to vice, has been corrected by continual conversation with this good man, and by his union with the lovely and virtuous Sophia. He hath also, by reflection on his past follies, acquired a discretion and prudence very uncommon in one of his lively parts.\n\nTo conclude, as there are not to be found a worthier man and woman, than this fond couple, so neither can any be imagined more happy. They preserve the purest and tenderest affection for each other, an affection daily encreased and confirmed by mutual endearments and mutual esteem. Nor is their conduct towards their relations and friends less amiable than towards one another. And such is their condescension, their indulgence, and their beneficence to those below them, that there is not a neighbour, a tenant, or a servant, who doth not most gratefully bless the day when Mr Jones was married to his Sophia.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-6593"]="이 헌사 앞머리에 귀하의 이름을 올려도 좋겠느냐고 청할 때마다 한결같이 거절하셨음에도, 저는 이 작품에 대한 귀하의 보호를 바랄 권리만큼은 여전히 굽히지 않겠습니다.\n\n경이시여, 이 이야기가 시작될 수 있었던 것 자체가 귀하 덕분입니다. 제가 이런 작품을 처음 떠올린 것도 귀하의 권유 때문이었습니다. 그 후로 어찌나 여러 해가 흘렀는지, 어쩌면 귀하께서는 이 일을 잊으셨을지도 모르겠습니다. 그러나 귀하의 바람은 제게 곧 명령과도 같은 것이어서, 그 자취는 제 기억에서 결코 지워지지 않을 것입니다.\n\n또한, 경이시여, 귀하의 도움이 없었다면 이 이야기는 결코 완성되지 못했을 것입니다. 이 단언에 놀라지는 마십시오. 귀하를 두고 연애소설 작가라는 의심을 사게 할 뜻은 없습니다. 제가 말하려는 것은 다만, 이 작품을 쓰는 데 들인 그 오랜 세월의 상당 부분 동안 제가 살아갈 수 있었던 것이 어느 정도 귀하 덕분이라는 사실입니다. 이것 또한 귀하께 일깨워 드릴 필요가 있을지 모르겠습니다. 귀하께서는 어떤 행위들에 대해서는 유난히 잘 잊으시는 경향이 있으시니 말입니다. 다만 그런 일들에 대해서만큼은, 제가 늘 귀하보다 더 또렷한 기억을 간직하기를 바랍니다.\n\n끝으로, 이 이야기가 지금과 같은 모습으로 나올 수 있었던 것도 귀하 덕분입니다. 어떤 분들이 기꺼이 말씀해 주셨듯, 만일 이 작품 안에 다른 어떤 작품에서도 찾아보기 힘들 만큼 진정으로 자애로운 마음의 더 강렬한 그림이 담겨 있다면, 귀하를, 그리고 귀하와 가까운 어떤 분을 아는 이라면 그 자애로움이 어디서 본떠 온 것인지 누가 의심하겠습니까? 세상이 그것을 제 자신에게서 가져왔다고 여겨 주는 칭찬을 제게 베풀지는 않으리라 믿습니다. 저는 개의치 않습니다. 다만 세상이 인정하게 될 한 가지는, 제가 그것을 본떠 온 두 분, 다시 말해 이 세상에서 가장 훌륭하고 가치 있는 사람 가운데 두 분이 강렬하고 열렬하게 저의 벗이라는 사실입니다. 저는 이것으로 만족할 수도 있겠지만, 그래도 저의 허영심이 그 수에 한 분을 더 보태려 합니다. 그분은 지위에서뿐 아니라 모든 공적·사적 덕에서도 가장 위대하고 고귀한 분 가운데 한 분이십니다. 그러나 베드퍼드 공작의 왕후 같은 후의에 대한 제 감사가 가슴에서 터져 나오는 이 자리에서, 제가 처음 그 은인의 눈에 띄도록 천거해 주신 분이 바로 귀하였음을 일깨워 드리는 것을 부디 용서해 주십시오.\n\n그렇다면 제가 간청해 온 이 영예를 허락하지 못하시겠다는 귀하의 반대 이유는 무엇입니까? 글쎄요, 귀하께서 이 책을 어찌나 따뜻하게 칭찬하셨던지, 헌사 앞에 놓인 자신의 이름을 읽기가 부끄러우시다는 것입니다. 참으로, 경이시여, 책 그 자체가 귀하로 하여금 그 칭찬을 부끄러워하게 만들지 못한다면, 제가 여기에 무엇을 쓴들 그렇게 만들 수 없을 것이며, 또 그래서도 안 될 것입니다. 귀하께서 제 책을 칭찬하셨다 하여, 제가 귀하의 보호와 후원을 받을 권리를 포기할 수는 없습니다. 저는 귀하께 그토록 많은 신세를 졌음을 인정하지만, 이 칭찬만큼은 그 목록에 넣지 않겠습니다. 거기에는 우정이 끼어든 자리가 거의 없다고 확신하기 때문입니다. 우정이란 귀하의 판단을 기울게 하지도, 귀하의 곧음을 비틀지도 못합니다. 적이라도 다만 그럴 자격만 갖춘다면 언제든 귀하의 칭찬을 얻을 수 있고, 벗의 허물이 바랄 수 있는 최대치란 귀하의 침묵, 혹 너무 가혹하게 비난받는 경우라면 어쩌면 귀하의 너그러운 두둔 정도입니다.\n\n요컨대, 경이시여, 저는 귀하께서 공개적인 찬사를 꺼리시는 것이야말로 제 청을 들어주지 않으시는 진짜 이유라고 짐작합니다. 저는 귀하께서 저의 다른 두 벗과 마찬가지로, 자신의 덕에 대한 한 마디 언급조차 듣기 싫어하심을 보아 왔습니다. 어느 위대한 시인이 귀하 가운데 한 분을 두고 (실은 세 분 모두에게 마땅히 그리 말할 수 있었을 텐데) 노래했듯이, 귀하께서는\n\n남몰래 선을 행하고, 그것이 명성이 됨을 부끄러워하시는 분입니다.\n\n이런 성품의 사람들이 다른 이들이 비난을 피하려 애쓰는 만큼이나 갈채를 멀리하려 조심한다면, 귀하의 인품이 제 손에 들어가는 데 대한 두려움이 얼마나 당연하겠습니까. 제가 귀하께 진 신세만큼이나 큰 해를 그에게서 입은 작가에게 공격당한다면, 사람이 어찌 떨지 않을 까닭이 있겠습니까!\n\n그리고 이 비난에 대한 두려움은, 사람이 스스로 그 빌미를 제공했음을 의식하는 정도에 비례하여 커지지 않겠습니까? 이를테면 어떤 이의 평생이 풍자의 끊임없는 소재였다면, 격분한 풍자가가 그를 다루기 시작할 때 그가 떨 만도 합니다. 자, 경이시여, 이것을 찬사에 대한 귀하의 겸손한 거부감에 적용해 본다면, 저를 향한 귀하의 두려움이 얼마나 이치에 맞아 보이겠습니까!\n\n하지만 분명 귀하께서는, 제가 언제나 제 자신의 만족보다 귀하의 뜻을 받드는 쪽을 택하리라는 이 한 가지 믿음만으로도 저의 야심을 채워 주실 수 있었을 것입니다. 그 강력한 본보기를 저는 이 헌사에서 보여 드리려 합니다. 여기서 저는 다른 모든 헌사 작가의 본을 따르기로 마음먹었으니, 저의 후원자가 정말로 쓰일 자격이 있는 것이 무엇인지가 아니라, 그가 읽어서 가장 흡족할 만한 것이 무엇인지를 헤아릴 것입니다.\n\n그러니 더 이상의 서론 없이, 저는 여기 제 인생의 여러 해에 걸친 노고를 귀하께 바칩니다. 이 노고에 어떤 가치가 있는지는 이미 귀하께서 알고 계십니다. 만일 귀하의 호의 어린 판단으로부터 제가 그 노고에 대해 어떤 자긍심을 품게 되었다 해도, 그것을 허영으로 돌릴 수는 없습니다. 귀하의 의견이 다른 어떤 사람의 작품을 두둔하는 쪽으로 내려졌더라도, 저는 그에 똑같이 고스란히 동의했을 것이기 때문입니다. 적어도 부정의 방식으로는 이렇게 말해도 좋을 듯합니다. 만일 제가 이 작품에 큰 흠이 있음을 느꼈다면, 귀하야말로 그 보호를 감히 청하려 할 마지막 사람이었으리라고요.\n\n실로 저의 후원자의 이름만 보아도, 독자는 이 작품에 첫발을 들이는 바로 그 순간 확신하게 되리라 바랍니다. 그 전체 흐름 어디에서도 종교와 덕의 대의에 해로운 것, 가장 엄격한 예법의 규칙에 어긋나는 것, 읽는 동안 가장 순결한 눈조차 불쾌하게 할 만한 것은 찾지 못하리라고 말입니다. 오히려 저는 선함과 순결함을 권하는 것이 이 이야기에서 제가 기울인 진실한 노력이었음을 분명히 밝힙니다. 귀하께서는 제가 이 정직한 목적을 이루었다고 기꺼이 여겨 주셨습니다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이러한 종류의 책에서야말로 그 목적이 가장 이루어지기 쉽습니다. 본보기란 일종의 그림과 같아서, 그 안에서 덕은 말하자면 눈에 보이는 대상이 되며, 플라톤이 덕의 벌거벗은 매력 속에 있다고 단언한 그 사랑스러움의 관념으로 우리를 사로잡기 때문입니다.\n\n인류의 찬탄을 끌어낼 만한 덕의 아름다움을 보여 주는 것에 더하여, 저는 사람들에게 그들의 진정한 이익이 덕을 좇으라 이끈다는 것을 납득시킴으로써, 덕을 위한 인간 행동의 더 강력한 동기를 끌어내려 했습니다. 이를 위해 저는, 죄로 얻는 어떤 소득도 순결함과 덕에 늘 따라붙는 동반자인 그 단단한 내면의 평안을 잃은 것을 보상할 수 없으며, 죄가 그 자리에 우리 가슴속으로 들여놓는 공포와 불안의 해악과는 조금도 견줄 수 없음을 보여 주었습니다. 또한 그런 소득은 그 자체로 대개 무가치할 뿐 아니라, 그것을 얻는 수단 또한 비천하고 수치스러우며, 잘해야 불확실하고 언제나 위험으로 가득함을 보여 주었습니다. 끝으로 저는, 덕과 순결함은 경솔함이 아니고서는 거의 해를 입지 않으며, 기만과 악행이 그들을 노리고 쳐 놓은 덫으로 그들을 빠뜨리는 것도 흔히 바로 이 경솔함뿐이라는 점을 강하게 새기려 애썼습니다. 제가 이 교훈에 더욱 부지런히 공을 들인 까닭은, 그것을 가르치는 일이 다른 무엇보다 성공이 따르기 쉬운 일이기 때문입니다. 제 믿음으로는, 나쁜 사람을 선하게 만드는 것보다 선한 사람을 지혜롭게 만드는 편이 훨씬 쉬우니까요.\n이러한 목적을 위해 저는 다음의 이야기에 제가 다룰 수 있는 온갖 재치와 해학을 쏟아부었습니다. 그 안에서 저는 인류가 아끼는 어리석음과 악덕을 웃음으로 떨쳐 내게 하려 애썼습니다. 이 갸륵한 시도에서 제가 얼마나 성공했는지는 공정한 독자의 판단에 맡기겠으며, 다만 두 가지만 청하고자 합니다. 첫째, 이 작품에서 완벽함을 기대하지 말아 주십시오. 둘째, 어떤 대목들이, 다른 대목들에서는 부디 드러나기를 바라는 그 작은 미덕에조차 미치지 못하더라도 너그러이 보아 주십시오.\n\n경이시여, 더는 붙잡아 두지 않겠습니다. 실로 저는 헌사를 쓴다 공언해 놓고 어느새 서문으로 빠져들고 말았습니다. 하지만 달리 어쩌겠습니까? 저는 감히 귀하를 칭송하지 못하니, 귀하가 제 생각 속에 계실 때 그것을 피할 유일한 방법이란, 아예 입을 다물거나 아니면 제 생각을 다른 주제로 돌리는 것뿐임을 압니다.\n\n그러니 이 서한에서 제가 한 말을, 귀하의 동의 없이 했을 뿐 아니라 명백히 그 뜻을 거슬러 한 것이니, 부디 용서해 주십시오. 그리고 적어도 이렇게 공개적인 방식으로, 제가 더없는 존경과 감사를 담아 다음과 같은 사람임을 밝히는 것만은 허락해 주십시오.\n경이시여,\n\n귀하께 더없이 큰 신세를 진,\n순종하는 미천한 종,\n\n헨리 필딩 올림.\n\n업둥이 톰 존스의 이야기.\n\n# BOOK I.\n\n작가란 자기 자신을, 사사로이 혹은 자선 삼아 한 끼를 베푸는 신사가 아니라, 누구든 돈만 내면 환영받는 공중식당을 꾸려 가는 주인으로 여겨야 한다. 앞의 경우라면, 잘 알려진 대로 음식을 베푸는 이가 제 마음에 드는 요리를 마음껏 내놓으며, 설령 그 음식이 형편없고 손님들의 입맛에 도무지 맞지 않더라도 손님들은 흠을 잡아서는 안 된다. 아니, 오히려 예법이라는 것이 그들로 하여금 앞에 차려진 것이 무엇이든 겉으로는 인정하고 칭찬하도록 강요한다. 그런데 식당 주인에게는 정반대의 일이 벌어진다. 먹는 값을 치르는 사람들은, 그 입맛이 제아무리 까다롭고 변덕스럽다 한들, 제 입을 만족시키라고 고집할 것이며, 모든 것이 제 입맛에 맞지 않으면 거리낌 없이 식사를 흠잡고, 욕하고, 저주할 권리를 내세울 것이다.\n\n그러므로 이런 실망으로 손님의 기분을 상하게 하는 일을 막기 위해, 정직하고 선의를 품은 주인이라면 으레 차림표를 마련해 두는 법이다. 누구든 가게에 들어서는 순간 그것을 죽 훑어보고, 자신이 기대할 수 있는 음식을 미리 알아챈 다음, 머물러 차려진 음식을 즐기거나, 아니면 제 입맛에 더 잘 맞는 다른 식당으로 떠나면 되는 것이다.\n\n우리는 재치든 지혜든, 그것을 빌려줄 능력이 있는 사람에게서라면 누구에게든 빌리기를 부끄러워하지 않으므로, 이 정직한 음식 장수들에게서 한 가지 착상을 기꺼이 받아들이기로 했다. 그리하여 우리의 향연 전체에 대한 총괄 차림표를 앞머리에 내걸 뿐 아니라, 이 책과 뒤이은 권들에서 차려 낼 코스마다 독자에게 따로따로 차림표를 마련해 드리고자 한다.\n\n그렇다면 우리가 여기 마련한 음식은 다름 아닌 인간 본성이다. 비록 내가 단 한 가지 품목만을 이름 지어 내놓았다 해서, 입맛이 더없이 호사스러운 분별 있는 독자라 한들 놀라거나, 트집을 잡거나, 기분이 상하지는 않으리라 믿는다. 거북은—먹는 일에 두루 통달한 브리스틀의 시참사회원이 풍부한 경험으로 아는 바와 같이—그 맛깔진 등딱지 살과 배딱지 살 말고도 여러 가지 다른 먹을거리를 품고 있다. 또한 학식 있는 독자라면 모르지 않을 것이다. 인간 본성이란, 여기서는 하나의 두루뭉술한 이름 아래 모아 두었으나, 그 안에 어찌나 엄청난 다양함이 들어 있는지, 요리사가 세상의 온갖 동물성·식물성 먹을거리를 죄다 거쳐 가는 것이, 작가가 이토록 광대한 주제를 다 길어 올리는 것보다 차라리 빠르다는 것을.\n\n어쩌면 더 까다로운 분들에게서, 이 요리는 너무 흔하고 천하다는 이의가 나올지도 모르겠다. 좌판마다 넘쳐 나는 그 숱한 로맨스며 소설, 희곡, 시들이 다루는 것이 이것 말고 또 무엇이란 말인가? 미식가가 어떤 진귀한 음식을 두고, 가장 누추한 골목에서도 같은 이름의 무언가를 찾아볼 수 있다는 이유만으로 그것을 천하다 업신여겨도 좋다면, 수많은 절묘한 요리들이 퇴짜를 맞을 것이다. 사실, 작가들에게서 진짜 본성을 만나기란, 가게에서 바욘 햄이나 볼로냐 소시지를 찾기만큼이나 어려운 일이다.\n\n그러나 같은 비유를 이어 가자면, 모든 것은 작가의 요리 솜씨에 달려 있다. 포프 씨가 우리에게 일러 주듯이—\n\n\"참된 재치란 본성을 한껏 돋보이게 차려 낸 것, 누구나 늘 생각했으되 이토록 잘 표현된 적은 없던 것.\"\n\n공작의 식탁에서 그 살의 어느 부위가 먹히는 영예를 누리는 바로 그 짐승이, 다른 부위에서는 격이 떨어져, 그 사지의 일부가 마치 효수당하듯 읍내에서 가장 천한 좌판에 내걸릴 수도 있다. 그렇다면 귀족의 음식과 짐꾼의 음식 사이에 무슨 차이가 있겠는가. 둘 다 같은 소나 송아지로 저녁을 든다면, 그 차이란 간을 맞추고, 손질하고, 곁들임을 더하고, 차려 내는 솜씨에 있을 뿐이다. 그리하여 한쪽은 가장 시들한 식욕마저 자극해 돋우는데, 다른 한쪽은 가장 날카롭고 예리한 식욕마저 돌려세우고 김빠지게 만든다.\n\n이와 마찬가지로, 정신의 향연이 지닌 빼어남도 주제 자체보다는 그것을 잘 차려 내는 작가의 솜씨에 달려 있다. 그러니 독자께서는, 다음의 작품에서 우리가 이 시대—어쩌면 헬리오가발루스의 시대까지 통틀어—가 배출한 최고 요리사의 가장 드높은 원칙 하나를 충실히 따랐음을 알고 얼마나 흐뭇해하실 것인가. 이 위대한 인물은, 우아한 미식을 사랑하는 이라면 누구나 잘 알듯이, 처음에는 굶주린 손님들 앞에 담백한 음식부터 내놓고, 그 뒤로 손님들의 위장이 차츰 줄어들리라 짐작되는 만큼, 소스와 향신료의 정수에 이르기까지 단계를 밟아 올라간다. 우리도 이와 같이, 처음에는 우리 독자의 날카로운 식욕에 시골에서 볼 수 있는 그 한결 꾸밈없고 소박한 모습으로 인간 본성을 내놓고, 그다음에는 궁정과 도시가 제공하는 온갖 거창한 프랑스풍·이탈리아풍 양념인 허식과 악덕을 곁들여 그것을 잘게 다지고 졸여 낼 것이다. 이런 방법으로, 방금 말한 그 위대한 인물이 어떤 이들에게 끝없이 먹게 만들었다 전해지듯, 우리 독자께서도 영영 읽어 나가고 싶어지시리라 믿어 의심치 않는다.\n\n이만큼 미리 일러 두었으니, 우리의 차림표가 마음에 드는 분들을 더는 식사에서 붙잡아 두지 않겠다. 이제 곧장 우리 이야기의 첫 번째 코스를 그분들의 즐거움을 위해 차려 내는 일로 나아가겠다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n올워디 영주에 대한 짧은 소개, 그리고 그의 누이 브리짓 올워디 양에 대한 좀 더 자세한 이야기.\n\n흔히 서머싯셔라고 불리는, 이 왕국 서부 지역의 어느 한구석에 얼마 전까지 한 신사가 살고 있었다. 어쩌면 지금도 살아 있을지 모른다. 그의 이름은 올워디였으며, 자연과 운명 양쪽 모두의 총애를 받은 사람이라 불러도 좋을 만했다. 이 둘은 누가 그를 더 많이 축복하고 더 풍요롭게 해줄지 서로 겨루기라도 한 듯했기 때문이다. 어떤 이들은 이 다툼에서 자연이 승리했다고 볼지도 모른다. 자연은 그에게 여러 가지 선물을 베푼 반면, 운명은 단 하나의 선물만을 손에 쥐고 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 운명은 그 하나를 쏟아붓는 데 어찌나 아낌이 없었던지, 어쩌면 어떤 이들은 이 단 하나의 선물이 그가 자연으로부터 누린 온갖 축복을 다 합친 것보다 더 값지다고 여길지도 모른다. 자연으로부터 그는 호감 가는 풍채와 튼튼한 체질, 견실한 분별력, 그리고 자애로운 마음을 물려받았다. 운명으로부터는 이 고장에서 손꼽히게 큰 영지 가운데 하나를 상속받도록 점지받았다.\n\n이 신사는 젊은 시절에 매우 훌륭하고 아름다운 여인과 혼인했고, 그녀를 끔찍이도 아꼈다. 그녀와의 사이에서 세 아이를 두었으나, 모두 어린 나이에 세상을 떠났다. 게다가 그는 이 이야기가 막을 올리기로 한 때로부터 약 오 년 전에, 사랑하던 아내마저 땅에 묻는 불행을 겪었다. 이 상실은 아무리 컸어도, 그는 분별 있고 한결같은 사람답게 견뎌냈다. 다만 이 대목에서만큼은 그가 종종 좀 별난 소리를 하곤 했음을 인정해야겠다. 그는 이따금 자신이 아직도 혼인한 몸이라 여긴다고 말하면서, 아내는 단지 자기보다 조금 먼저 떠났을 뿐이며, 그 길은 자신도 머지않아 틀림없이 그녀를 따라 떠날 여정이라고 생각한다고 했다. 그리고 다시는 헤어지지 않을 어느 곳에서 아내와 다시 만나리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않는다고도 했다. 이런 생각을 두고 이웃 가운데 일부는 그의 분별력을, 다른 일부는 그의 신앙심을, 또 다른 일부는 그의 진심을 도마 위에 올렸다.\n\n그는 이제 대부분의 시간을 시골에 은거하며 지냈는데, 누이 한 사람과 함께였고, 그녀에게 매우 다정한 애정을 품고 있었다. 이 부인은 당시 서른을 조금 넘긴 나이였으니, 심술궂은 이들의 눈에는 노처녀라는 호칭을 갖다 붙여도 별 무리가 없을 시기였다. 그녀는 아름다움보다는 좋은 품성으로 칭찬받는 부류의 여인이었으며, 같은 여자들 사이에서 흔히 \"참 좋은 사람\"이라고 불리는 그런 여인이었다. \"부인, 알아두면 좋을 만큼 참 좋은 사람이지요\" 하는 식으로 말이다. 사실 그녀는 미모가 없음을 아쉬워하기는커녕, 그것을 미덕이라 부를 수 있다면 그 미덕을 언급할 때마다 늘 경멸을 담았다. 그리고 자신이 아무개 양만큼 곱지 않은 것을 자주 하느님께 감사하곤 했는데, 아무개 양은 어쩌면 그 미모 탓에, 미모만 없었다면 피했을 잘못의 길로 빠져들었을지도 모른다는 것이었다. 브리짓 올워디 양은—이 부인의 이름이 바로 그러했다—여인의 외모가 지닌 매력이란 남에게나 자신에게나 한낱 덫에 지나지 않는다고 아주 옳게 여겼다. 그러면서도 그녀의 처신은 어찌나 신중했던지, 마치 온 여성을 노려 놓인 모든 덫을 자기가 다 경계해야 하는 사람처럼 빈틈없이 조심했다. 사실 나는, 독자에게는 영문 모를 일로 보일지 모르나, 이 신중함이라는 경비병이 마치 훈련된 민병대처럼 위험이 가장 적은 곳에 가장 먼저 달려가 보초를 선다는 것을 눈여겨봐 왔다. 그것은 남자들이 한결같이 갈망하고 한숨짓고 애태우며 온갖 그물을 다 펼쳐대는 그런 절세의 미인들은 비겁하고 야비하게 저버리기 일쑤다. 그러면서도 다른 성이 좀 더 멀찍이서 두려운 경의를 품을 뿐, (아마도 성공할 가망이 없다는 절망 때문에) 감히 공략할 엄두조차 내지 못하는, 그 한 단계 높은 부류의 여인들 발치에는 늘 따라붙어 시중을 든다.\n\n독자여, 우리가 더 멀리 함께 나아가기 전에, 나는 이 이야기 전체에 걸쳐 적당하다고 여겨질 때마다 곁길로 새겠다는 뜻을 그대에게 알려두는 것이 옳다고 생각한다. 그 적당함을 판단하는 데는 그 어떤 가련한 비평가보다도 나 자신이 더 나은 판관이다. 그러니 여기서 나는 그 모든 비평가에게 부디 제 일이나 신경 쓰고, 자기와는 아무 상관도 없는 일이나 작품에 간섭하지 말라고 당부해야겠다. 그들이 무슨 권위로 판관 자리에 앉게 되었는지 그 근거를 내놓기 전까지는, 나는 그들의 재판 관할권을 인정하여 변론에 나서지 않을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n올워디 씨가 집으로 돌아왔을 때 그에게 일어난 기묘한 사건. 데버러 윌킨스 부인의 점잖은 처신, 그리고 사생아에 관한 몇 가지 적절한 논평.\n\n앞 장에서 나는 독자에게 올워디 씨가 막대한 재산을 물려받았고, 선한 마음씨를 지녔으며, 가족이 없다는 것을 이야기했다. 그러니 많은 이들이 틀림없이 이런 결론을 내릴 것이다. 그는 정직한 사람답게 살았고, 누구에게도 한 푼 빚지지 않았으며, 제 것이 아닌 것은 결코 취하지 않았고, 좋은 집을 건사하며 이웃을 식탁으로 맞아 진심으로 환대했고, 가난한 이들에게—말하자면 일하기보다 구걸하기를 택하는 자들에게—식탁에서 남은 음식을 베풀어 자선을 행했으며, 마침내 어마어마한 부자로 죽어 병원을 하나 세웠으리라고 말이다.\n\n그가 이 일들 가운데 많은 것을 실제로 행한 것은 사실이다. 그러나 그것 말고 한 일이 아무것도 없었다면, 나는 그를 그 병원 문 위에 놓인 어느 매끈한 사암 비석에다 제 공덕이나 새기도록 내버려 두었을 것이다. 이 이야기의 주제가 되어야 할 것은 그보다 훨씬 비범한 종류의 일들이다. 그렇지 않다면 나는 이토록 방대한 작품을 쓰느라 시간을 형편없이 허비하는 셈이 될 것이며, 명민한 벗이여, 그대 또한 어떤 익살스러운 작가들이 우스갯소리로 《영국사》라 부르기를 즐겨 온 몇몇 책장을 넘기는 편이 똑같은 이득과 즐거움을 줄 것이다.\n\n올워디 씨는 매우 특별한 용무로 런던에 꼬박 석 달을 머물러 집을 비웠는데, 그 용무가 무엇이었는지 나는 알지 못한다. 다만 여러 해 동안 한 번에 한 달 이상 집을 비운 적이 없던 그를 그토록 오래 붙들어 둔 것을 보면, 그 중요성을 짐작할 수 있으리라. 그는 저녁 매우 늦은 시각에 집에 도착했고, 누이와 짧게 저녁을 든 뒤 몹시 지친 몸으로 침실로 물러났다. 거기서 그는 무릎을 꿇고 몇 분을 보낸 뒤—어떤 일이 있어도 거르지 않는 습관이었다—막 잠자리에 들려던 참이었다. 그런데 이불을 들치자, 그는 크게 놀랍게도 거친 아마포에 싸인 채 시트 사이에서 달고 깊은 잠에 빠져 있는 한 갓난아이를 보게 되었다. 그는 이 광경에 넋을 잃고 한동안 서 있었다. 그러나 그의 마음에서는 늘 선한 천성이 우위를 차지하고 있던 터라, 그는 곧 눈앞의 가엾은 어린 것에게 연민의 정을 느끼기 시작했다. 그는 종을 울려 나이 든 하녀에게 당장 일어나 자기에게 오라고 일렀다. 그러는 동안 그는 갓난아이와 잠이 언제나 드러내 보이는 그 생생한 빛깔로 나타난 순결의 아름다움을 들여다보는 데 너무도 골몰한 나머지, 나이 든 하녀가 들어왔을 때 자신이 속옷 바람이라는 사실을 미처 떠올리지 못할 만큼 생각에 깊이 빠져 있었다. 사실 그 하녀는 주인에게 옷을 갖춰 입을 시간을 넉넉히 주었다. 주인에 대한 존경과 예법을 지키려는 마음에서, 그녀는 하인이 그토록 다급하게 불러댔음에도, 또 그녀가 아는 한 주인이 어쩌면 졸중이나 다른 발작으로 숨을 거두고 있을지도 모르는 와중에도, 거울 앞에서 머리를 매만지느라 여러 분을 보냈으니 말이다.\n\n제 몸가짐에 그토록 엄격하게 예법을 지키는 사람이 남에게서 그 예법이 조금이라도 어긋나는 것을 보고 충격을 받는다 한들 놀랄 일은 아니다. 그리하여 그녀는 문을 열고 주인이 속옷 바람으로 손에 촛불을 든 채 침대 곁에 서 있는 것을 보자마자, 끔찍한 공포에 사로잡혀 뒷걸음질을 쳤고, 만일 그가 그제야 자신이 옷을 벗고 있었음을 떠올리고서, 등에 옷가지를 걸쳐 데버러 윌킨스 부인의 순결한 눈에 더는 충격을 줄 수 없는 상태가 될 때까지 문밖에서 기다려 달라고 청해 그녀의 공포를 가라앉히지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 그대로 기절해 버렸을지도 모른다. 그녀는 비록 쉰두 살의 나이였으나, 외투를 걸치지 않은 남자를 한 번도 본 적이 없노라고 맹세하던 사람이었다. 비웃기 좋아하는 자들과 신성을 모독하는 익살꾼들은 어쩌면 그녀의 첫 번째 공포를 두고 웃을지도 모른다. 그러나 좀 더 진지한 내 독자라면, 한밤중이라는 시각과, 잠자리에서 불려 나온 정황과, 주인이 처해 있던 상태를 헤아릴 때, 그녀의 처신을 크게 정당하다 여기며 갈채를 보낼 것이다. 다만 데버러 부인이 다다른 그 나이의 처녀들에게 으레 따른다고 여겨지는 신중함이, 그 감탄을 조금 누그러뜨리지 않는다면 말이다.\n\n데버러 부인이 방으로 돌아와 주인에게서 그 어린 갓난아이를 발견한 일을 전해 듣자, 그녀의 경악은 오히려 주인의 그것보다 더했다. 그녀는 말투에서나 표정에서나 큰 공포를 담아 이렇게 외치지 않을 수 없었다. \"나리! 이를 어쩌면 좋답니까?\" 올워디 씨는 그날 저녁은 그녀가 아이를 돌보아야 하며, 아침이 되면 유모를 구하도록 지시하겠노라고 대답했다. \"예, 나리.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 부디 나리께서 영장을 내리시어 이 아이의 어미인 그 화냥년을 잡아들이시기를 바랍니다. 분명 이 근방 사람일 테니까요. 저는 그 계집이 브라이드웰 감화원에 끌려가 짐수레 꽁무니에 묶인 채 채찍질당하는 꼴을 봤으면 좋겠습니다. 정말이지, 이런 사악한 잡것들은 아무리 호되게 벌해도 지나치지 않지요. 감히 그걸 나리께 뒤집어씌우는 뻔뻔함을 보면, 이번이 처음이 아닐 거라고 장담합니다.\" \"나에게 뒤집어씌우다니, 데버러!\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"그 여자에게 그런 속셈이 있다고는 생각되지 않네. 내 짐작으로는 그저 제 아이를 거둘 방도로 이런 방법을 택한 것일 뿐이고, 솔직히 그보다 더 나쁜 짓을 하지 않은 것이 다행스럽군.\" \"이보다 더 나쁜 것이 무엇인지 저는 모르겠습니다.\" 데버러가 외쳤다. \"이런 사악한 갈보들이 제 죄를 정직한 사람의 문간에 떠넘기는 것보다 말입니다. 나리께서야 당신의 결백을 아시지만, 세상 사람들은 험구가 심하지요. 정직한 사람이 자기가 낳지도 않은 아이의 아비로 몰리는 일은 흔하디흔합니다. 게다가 나리께서 그 아이를 거두시면, 사람들이 그 말을 더 곧이듣기 십상이지요. 그리고 어차피 교구가 떠맡아 기를 수밖에 없는 것을 어찌하여 나리께서 거두려 하십니까? 제 생각으로는, 정녕 정직한 사람의 아이라면 또 모르겠으나—제 생각으로는, 저는 이 잘못 태어난 가엾은 것들을 손대는 것조차 거슬립니다. 저는 이런 것들을 같은 사람으로 여기지도 않으니까요. 아유! 어찌나 고약한 냄새가 나는지! 기독교인의 냄새 같지가 않아요. 제가 감히 한 말씀 올려도 된다면, 저는 이 아이를 바구니에 담아 교구위원의 문간에 내다 놓겠습니다. 오늘 밤은 좋은 편이고, 다만 비가 조금 오고 바람이 좀 불 뿐이지요. 잘 싸서 따뜻한 바구니에 담아 두면, 아침에 발견될 때까지 살아 있을 확률이 두 배는 됩니다. 설령 그러지 못한다 해도, 우리는 마땅한 보살핌을 베풀어 우리의 도리를 다한 셈이지요. 그리고 이런 것들은 자라나서 제 어미를 흉내 내느니, 차라리 순결한 상태에서 죽는 편이 어쩌면 더 나을 겁니다. 그것들에게서 그보다 나은 것을 기대할 수는 없으니까요.\"\n\n이 말 가운데에는 올워디 씨가 만일 귀 기울여 들었더라면 아마 기분이 상했을 대목이 더러 있었다. 그러나 그는 어느새 손가락 하나를 갓난아이의 손에 쥐여 주고 있었는데, 그 아이가 부드럽게 손가락을 쥐며 마치 그의 도움을 간청하는 듯한 그 몸짓은, 데버러 부인의 웅변이 설령 지금보다 열 배는 더 대단했다 한들 그것을 능히 눌러 이겼을 것이 분명했다. 그는 이제 데버러 부인에게, 아이를 그녀 자신의 침대로 데려가고 하녀 하나를 깨워 아이가 깰 때를 대비해 미음과 다른 것들을 마련해 두라고 단호히 명했다. 또한 아침 일찍 아이에게 알맞은 옷을 구하도록 하고, 자신이 일어나는 대로 곧장 아이를 데려오라고 일렀다.\n\n윌킨스 부인의 분별이 그러했고, 또 더없이 좋은 자리를 누리게 해 주는 주인을 향한 그녀의 존경이 그러했기에, 그녀의 거리낌은 그의 단호한 명령 앞에 물러서고 말았다. 그녀는 아이의 출생이 떳떳지 못하다는 것에 겉으로는 아무런 혐오도 내비치지 않은 채 아이를 두 팔에 안아 들었고, 사랑스러운 어린 갓난아이라고 말하며 아이를 안고 제 방으로 걸어갔다.\n\n여기서 올워디는, 선함을 갈망하는 마음이 흡족하게 채워졌을 때 누리기 쉬운 그 흐뭇한 잠 속으로 빠져들었다. 이런 잠은 어쩌면 그 어떤 푸짐한 식사에서 비롯된 잠보다도 달콤할 터이니, 만일 그런 식욕을 돋우려면 어떤 곳의 공기를 마시라고 독자에게 권할지를 내가 알았더라면, 나는 그 잠을 독자에게 보여 주려고 더 많은 공을 들였을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n묘사 하나로 독자의 목이 위태로워지나, 무사히 빠져나오고, 브리짓 올워디 양이 보여 준 크나큰 너그러움에 관하여.\n\n고딕 양식의 건축물이 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 고귀한 것이 있다면, 그것은 바로 올워디 씨의 저택이었다. 거기에는 보는 이를 경외감으로 사로잡는 웅장한 기운이 서려 있어, 가장 빼어난 그리스 건축의 아름다움에 견줄 만했다. 밖으로는 위엄이 넘쳤고, 안으로는 그만큼 편안하고 쓰임새가 좋았다.\n\n저택은 어느 언덕의 남동쪽 비탈에 서 있었는데, 꼭대기보다는 기슭에 더 가까운 자리였다. 그래서 거의 반 마일에 걸쳐 완만하게 솟아오른 오래된 떡갈나무 숲이 저택 위로 우거져 북동풍을 막아 주었고, 그러면서도 아래로 펼쳐진 골짜기의 더없이 매혹적인 경관을 즐길 만큼은 높은 자리였다.\n\n그 숲 한가운데에는 저택 쪽으로 비스듬히 기운 고운 잔디밭이 있었고, 그 꼭대기 가까이에는 전나무로 뒤덮인 바위에서 물이 콸콸 솟아 나오는 풍성한 샘이 있었다. 샘은 약 삼십 피트에 이르는 끊임없는 폭포를 이루었는데, 가지런히 다듬은 계단을 따라 흘러내리는 것이 아니라, 깨지고 이끼 낀 돌들 위로 자연스럽게 떨어지며 굴러내려 바위 밑동에 다다랐다. 그러고는 자갈 깔린 물길을 따라 흘러가며 크고 작은 여러 폭포를 이루어 굽이굽이 돌아, 마침내 언덕 기슭의 호수로 떨어졌다. 그 호수는 저택 남쪽으로 사 분의 일 마일쯤 아래에 있었고, 정면의 모든 방에서 내다보였다. 너도밤나무와 느릅나무 무리가 곳곳을 수놓고 양 떼가 풀을 뜯는 아름다운 평원의 한가운데를 채운 이 호수에서 강 하나가 흘러나왔다. 그 강은 몇 마일에 걸쳐 놀랍도록 다채로운 목초지와 숲 사이를 굽이쳐 흐르다가 마침내 바다로 흘러들었으니, 바다의 너른 한 줄기와 그 너머의 섬이 이 경관의 끝을 닫아 주었다.\n\n이 골짜기의 오른쪽으로는 좀 더 작은 또 하나의 골짜기가 열렸는데, 여러 마을이 점점이 그곳을 꾸미고 있었고, 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮인 채 정면 일부만이 아직 온전히 남은 어느 무너진 옛 수도원의 탑 하나가 그 끝을 맺고 있었다.\n\n왼쪽 풍경은 무척 빼어난 정원의 모습을 보여 주었다. 높낮이가 고르지 않은 땅으로 이루어져 있었고, 언덕과 잔디밭, 숲과 물이 어우러져 감탄스러운 취향으로 배치된, 그러나 인공보다는 자연의 손길에 더 많이 기댄 온갖 변화로 보기 좋게 다채로웠다. 그 너머로는 들판이 차츰 솟아올라 험준한 산줄기를 이루었는데, 그 봉우리들은 구름 위로 솟아 있었다.\n\n때는 바야흐로 오월 중순이었고, 아침은 유난히 맑고 고요했다. 올워디 씨가 테라스로 걸어 나오자, 동틀 녘의 빛은 우리가 앞서 묘사한 그 사랑스러운 경관을 시시각각 그의 눈앞에 펼쳐 보였다. 이윽고 빛줄기들이 그의 앞에서 푸른 창공으로 솟아오르며 그 위풍에 앞서가는 선구자처럼 길을 트자, 위엄을 한껏 내뿜으며 태양이 떠올랐다. 그런데 이 낮은 세상의 피조물 가운데 그 태양보다 더 영광스러울 수 있는 대상이 단 하나 있었으니, 바로 올워디 씨 자신이 그것을 보여 주었다. 자애로움으로 가득 찬 한 인간이, 제 피조물들에게 가장 큰 선을 베풂으로써 어떻게 하면 창조주의 마음에 가장 흡족한 사람이 될 수 있을까를 깊이 헤아리고 있었던 것이다.\n\n독자여, 조심하시라. 나는 경솔하게도 그대를 올워디 씨의 저택만큼이나 높은 언덕 꼭대기로 이끌고 말았으니, 그대의 목이 부러지지 않게끔 어떻게 내려보내야 할지 나로서도 도무지 알 수가 없다. 하지만 그래도 함께 미끄러져 내려가 보기로 하자. 브리짓 양이 종을 울리고 있고, 올워디 씨가 아침 식사 자리로 불려 가고 있으니 말이다. 나도 그 자리에 가 봐야 하는데, 괜찮으시다면 그대가 함께해 주신다면 더없이 기쁘겠다.\n\n올워디 씨와 브리짓 양 사이에 으레 오가는 인사말이 끝나고 차가 따라지자, 그는 윌킨스 부인을 불러들이고는 누이에게 선물을 하나 준비해 두었다고 말했다. 누이는 그 말에 고맙다고 했는데, 짐작건대 가운이나 몸을 꾸밀 무슨 장신구쯤으로 여긴 모양이었다. 사실 그는 누이에게 그런 선물을 꽤 자주 했고, 누이도 그를 흡족하게 해 주려는 마음에서 자신을 단장하는 데 적잖은 시간을 들였다. 굳이 그를 흡족하게 해 주려는 마음이라 말하는 까닭은, 누이가 늘 옷차림과 그것에 골몰하는 부인네들을 더없이 경멸한다고 입버릇처럼 말해 왔기 때문이다.\n\n그런데 만일 그것이 누이의 기대였다면, 윌킨스 부인이 주인에게서 받은 지시대로 그 자그마한 아기를 내놓았을 때 누이가 얼마나 실망했겠는가? 앞서 말한 대로, 큰 놀라움은 흔히 침묵을 부르는 법이다. 브리짓 양도 마찬가지여서, 오라비가 입을 열어 자초지종을 다 들려줄 때까지 잠자코 있었다. 그 이야기는 독자가 이미 알고 있으니, 여기서 되풀이하지는 않겠다.\n\n브리짓 양은 부인네들이 즐겨 정숙함이라 부르는 것에 늘 대단한 존중을 드러냈고, 스스로도 그토록 엄격한 품성을 지켜 왔던 터라, 특히 윌킨스로서는 이번 일에 누이가 모진 말을 잔뜩 쏟아내며 그 아이를 무슨 해로운 짐승이라도 되는 양 당장 집 밖으로 내보내자고 할 줄로 예상했다. 그런데 정반대로, 누이는 오히려 너그러운 쪽으로 마음을 기울여, 이 가엾고 무력한 작은 생명에게 얼마간 측은한 마음을 내비치고, 오라비가 베푼 그 자비로운 처사를 칭찬하기까지 했다.\n\n어쩌면 독자는 누이의 이런 태도를 올워디 씨에 대한 순종에서 비롯된 것으로 여길지도 모르겠다. 이 선량한 양반이 자신의 이야기를 마무리하면서, 그 아이를 거두어 친자식처럼 길러 내겠다는 결심을 털어놓았다는 사실을 우리가 일러둔다면 말이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 누이는 늘 오라비의 뜻을 기꺼이 받들 채비가 되어 있었고, 그의 생각에 거스르는 일이란 좀처럼, 아니 거의 없다시피 했다. 다만 이따금 몇 마디 한마디씩 덧붙이기는 했는데, 이를테면 사내들이란 고집이 세어 제 멋대로 해야 직성이 풀린다느니, 자기에게도 남에게 기대지 않을 만한 재산이 있었더라면 좋았을 것을, 하는 식이었다. 그러나 이런 말들은 늘 낮은 목소리로 흘러나왔고, 기껏해야 흔히 중얼거림이라 부르는 정도에 그쳤다.\n\n그렇지만 누이는 아기에게 아끼던 그 모진 말들을, 이름도 모르는 가엾은 어미에게는 더없이 아낌없이 퍼부었다. 뻔뻔한 계집이니, 음탕한 년이니, 대담한 화냥년이니, 못된 잡년이니, 비천한 갈보니 하며, 정숙함의 혀가 제 성을 욕보인 자들을 후려칠 때 결코 빼놓는 법이 없는 온갖 호칭을 죄다 갖다 붙였다.\n\n이제 어미를 찾아내려면 어떻게 해야 할지 의논이 시작되었다. 먼저 집안 여자 하인들의 행실을 살펴보았는데, 이들은 모두 윌킨스 부인의 명백한 보증으로 혐의를 벗었다. 하인들은 부인이 손수 골라 모은 이들이었고, 어쩌면 그렇게 못생긴 허수아비 같은 무리를 또 한 패 찾아내기란 쉽지 않았을 것이다.\n\n다음 단계는 교구 주민들 사이를 캐 보는 일이었는데, 이는 윌킨스 부인에게 맡겨졌다. 부인은 온갖 정성을 다해 알아본 뒤 오후에 그 결과를 보고하기로 했다.\n\n이렇게 일이 정해지자 올워디 씨는 늘 하던 대로 서재로 물러갔고, 아이는 누이에게 맡겨 두었다. 누이는 그의 부탁에 따라 아이를 돌보는 일을 떠맡았던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n주인이 자리를 뜨자, 데버라 부인은 브리짓 아가씨의 신호를 기다리며 말없이 서 있었다. 주인 앞에서 오갔던 말에 대해서는, 신중한 가정부는 결코 그것을 믿지 않았으니, 오라비가 없을 때의 아가씨 속마음이 그가 있는 자리에서 내뱉은 말과 크게 어긋나는 경우를 여러 번 겪어 보았기 때문이다. 그러나 브리짓 아가씨는 그녀를 그 미심쩍은 처지에 오래 두지는 않았다. 데버라 부인의 무릎 위에서 잠든 아이를 한동안 진지하게 들여다보던 마음씨 고운 아가씨는, 끝내 아이에게 다정한 입맞춤을 하지 않고는 견딜 수 없었고, 동시에 그 아름다움과 천진함이 더없이 마음에 든다고 선언했다. 이를 본 데버라 부인은 곧장 아이를 끌어안고 입을 맞추기 시작했는데, 그 황홀함이란 마흔다섯 먹은 점잖은 부인이 젊고 혈기왕성한 신랑을 향해 이따금 품는 그것에 못지않았으니, 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"어머, 이 귀여운 것!—사랑스럽고 어여쁜 것! 정말이지, 이렇게 잘생긴 사내아이는 난생처음 본다니까요!\"\n\n이 감탄은 아가씨가 끼어들 때까지 이어졌다. 이제 아가씨는 오라비가 맡긴 임무를 수행하기 시작하여, 아이에게 필요한 온갖 것들을 마련하라 일렀고, 집 안의 아주 좋은 방 하나를 아이의 육아실로 정해 주었다. 그 분부가 어찌나 후하던지, 만일 제 친자식이었다 한들 그 이상 베풀 수는 없었을 것이다. 다만 덕망 높은 독자께서, 천출 아이에게—법으로 보면 그런 아이에게 베푸는 모든 자선은 불경한 짓으로 단죄되거니와—지나친 관심을 보였다 하여 그녀를 나무라실까 하여, 우리는 그녀가 이 모든 일을 이런 말로 마무리했음을 일러두는 것이 마땅하다고 본다. \"오라비가 이 어린 것을 거두겠다는 변덕을 부린 이상, 도련님을 더없이 살뜰히 다뤄야겠지요. 나로서야 이게 악덕을 부추기는 짓이 아닌가 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없지만, 사람이란 고집이 어떤 것인지 너무도 잘 아는 터라, 그 우스꽝스러운 변덕에 대놓고 맞설 마음은 없어요.\"\n\n앞서 넌지시 비친 대로, 그녀는 오라비의 뜻을 따르는 행위 하나하나에 으레 이런 식의 토를 곁들이곤 했다. 그리고 자기가 굴복하는 그 뜻이 어리석고 사리에 맞지 않음을 동시에 알고 있노라 선언하는 것만큼, 이 순종의 공덕을 한층 더 돋보이게 하는 것도 분명 없을 것이다. 말없는 복종이란 의지에 아무런 강제도 없음을 뜻하며, 따라서 별다른 수고 없이 손쉽게 지킬 수 있다. 그러나 아내가, 자식이, 친척이, 혹은 벗이 우리가 바라는 바를 투덜대며 마지못해, 싫고 못마땅하다는 기색을 내비치며 행한다면, 그들이 겪는 그 뚜렷한 괴로움이야말로 우리가 진 은혜를 크게 더해 줄 수밖에 없다.\n\n이것은 스스로 떠올릴 수 있는 독자가 극히 드물 만한 깊은 통찰 가운데 하나이므로, 나는 독자에게 내 도움의 손길을 빌려주는 것이 마땅하다 여겼다. 다만 이런 호의는 이 작품을 읽어 나가는 동안 좀처럼 기대하기 어려운 것이다. 실로 나는 이런 경우가 아니고서는 좀처럼, 아니 거의 결코 그를 그렇게 떠받들지 않을 것이다. 우리 작가들에게 주어진 영감이 아니고서는 누구도 도무지 그런 발견에 이를 수 없는, 바로 이런 경우가 아니라면 말이다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n데버라 부인이 비유와 함께 이 교구로 등장하다. 제니 존스에 대한 짤막한 소개와, 배움을 좇는 젊은 여인들에게 따라붙기 마련인 어려움과 낙담들.\n\n주인의 뜻에 따라 아이를 처리한 데버라 부인은, 이제 그 아이의 어미를 숨기고 있으리라 여겨지는 집들을 찾아 나설 채비를 했다.\n\n무시무시한 새인 솔개가 하늘 높이 솟아올라 머리 위를 맴도는 모습을 깃털 달린 무리가 바라볼 때와 다를 바 없었다. 사랑에 빠진 비둘기며 죄 없는 작은 새들은 저마다 사방으로 경보를 퍼뜨리고, 벌벌 떨며 숨을 곳으로 날아든다. 솔개는 제 위엄을 의식한 채 의기양양하게 허공을 치며, 마음먹은 악행을 곰곰이 궁리한다.\n\n그리하여 데버라 부인이 다가온다는 소문이 거리에 퍼지자, 모든 주민이 벌벌 떨며 제 집으로 달려 들어갔고, 안주인마다 그 방문이 제 차례로 떨어질까 두려워했다. 그녀는 위풍당당한 걸음으로 의기양양하게 벌판을 가로질러 나아간다. 제 자신의 탁월함에 대한 자만으로 가득 찬 그 우뚝 솟은 머리를 높이 쳐들고서, 마음먹은 발각을 이뤄 낼 책략을 짠다.\n\n눈치 빠른 독자라면 이 비유를 보고, 이 가엾은 사람들이 윌킨스 부인이 지금 자신들을 향해 다가오는 그 속셈을 무슨 낌새라도 챘으리라고 상상하지는 않을 것이다. 다만 이 비유의 빼어난 아름다움이라는 것이, 훗날 어느 주석가가 이 작품을 손에 들 때까지 어쩌면 백 년쯤 잠들어 있을지도 모르는 노릇이니, 나는 이 대목에서 독자에게 약간의 도움을 빌려주는 것이 마땅하다고 본다.\n\n그러므로 내가 일러두고자 하는 바는 이렇다. 작은 새들을 잡아먹는 것이 솔개의 본성이듯, 작은 사람들을 모욕하고 짓누르며 군림하는 것이 윌킨스 부인 같은 부류의 본성이라는 것이다. 이것이야말로 그들이 윗사람들에게 바친 극도의 굽실거림과 비굴함을 스스로에게 보상하는 수단이다. 노예와 아첨꾼이 윗사람 모두에게 바치는 것과 똑같은 세금을 제 아랫사람 모두에게서 거둬들이는 것보다 더 이치에 맞는 일은 없으니 말이다.\n\n데버라 부인은 브리짓 마님에게 유난히 굽실거려야 할 일이 생겨 그 바람에 타고난 성미가 좀 시큼해졌다 싶을 때면, 이 사람들 사이로 나가 거니는 것이 버릇이었다. 묵은 짜증을 죄다 쏟아 내고, 말하자면 깨끗이 비워 냄으로써 제 기분을 다스리기 위함이었다. 그러니 그녀는 결코 반가운 손님일 수 없었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀는 그들 모두에게 두루 두려움과 미움을 받았다.\n\n이곳에 도착한 그녀는 곧장 어느 나이 든 안주인의 집으로 갔다. 이 안주인은 다행히도 나이뿐 아니라 인물의 곱상함에서도 자신을 닮은 데가 있어, 데버라 부인은 평소 다른 누구보다 이 여자에게 호의를 베풀어 왔다. 그녀는 이 여자에게 무슨 일이 있었는지, 그리고 그날 아침 무슨 목적으로 여기까지 왔는지를 털어놓았다. 두 사람은 이내 그 집들 가운데 어디든 살고 있는 젊은 처녀들 하나하나의 평판을 샅샅이 따지기 시작했고, 마침내 제니 존스라는 처녀 하나에게 가장 강한 의심을 두었다. 두 사람 모두 그녀야말로 이 일을 저질렀을 법한 가장 유력한 인물이라는 데 뜻을 같이했다.\n\n이 제니 존스는 얼굴이든 몸매든 그리 곱상한 처녀는 아니었다. 그러나 자연은 그 미모의 부족을, 판단력이 완전히 무르익을 나이에 이른 부인들이 흔히 더 높이 치는 그 무엇으로 어느 정도 채워 주었다. 다름 아니라 자연이 그녀에게 보기 드물 만큼 넉넉한 분별력을 내려 준 것이다. 이 천부의 재능을 제니는 학식으로 한층 갈고닦았다. 그녀는 여러 해 동안 어느 학교 선생 밑에서 하녀로 살았는데, 그 선생은 처녀에게서 재주의 뛰어난 민첩함과 배움에 대한 남다른 열망을 알아보고는 — 틈만 나면 늘 학생들의 책을 읽고 있는 모습이 눈에 띄었던 것이다 — 그녀를 가르칠 만큼 마음씨가 좋았던 것이다. 아니, 독자가 부르고 싶은 대로라면 어리석었던 것이다. 그 덕에 그녀는 라틴어에 제법 능숙해졌고, 어쩌면 그 시대의 웬만한 양갓집 젊은이들 못지않은 학자였을 것이다. 그러나 이 강점도 유별난 종류의 다른 강점들이 대개 그렇듯, 자잘한 불편을 몇 가지 데리고 다녔다. 이토록 뛰어난 소양을 갖춘 젊은 여인이, 운명은 자신과 같은 처지로 만들었으나 교육은 자신보다 훨씬 못한 처지로 만든 이들과 어울리는 데 흥이 나지 않는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니듯, 제니의 이 우월함이, 그리고 그 우월함에 으레 따라붙는 처신이 다른 이들 사이에 그녀를 향한 자잘한 시샘과 악의를 빚어내는 것 또한 더 놀랄 일이 못 된다. 어쩌면 이런 감정은 그녀가 하녀살이에서 돌아온 이래로 줄곧 이웃들의 가슴속에서 남몰래 타오르고 있었으리라.\n\n그러나 그들의 시샘은 겉으로 드러나지 않다가, 마침내 가엾은 제니가 모두를 놀라게 하고 이 일대 모든 젊은 여인들을 분하게 하면서, 어느 일요일에 새 비단 가운에 레이스 달린 모자며 거기에 걸맞은 갖은 부속물까지 갖추고 사람들 앞에서 광채를 발하자 비로소 터져 나왔다.\n\n전에는 알 속에 잠들어 있던 그 불길이 이제 와락 타올랐다. 제니는 학식으로 제 자긍심을 한껏 키워 놓았건만, 이웃 가운데 누구 하나 그녀가 요구하는 듯한 그 명예로 그 자긍심을 먹여 줄 만큼 친절하지 않았다. 그리하여 이제 그녀는 그 호사스러운 차림으로 존경과 흠모는커녕 미움과 욕설만 얻었다. 온 교구가 그녀더러 그런 것들을 정직하게 얻었을 리 없다고 떠들어 댔고, 부모들은 제 딸도 그런 것을 가지기를 바라기는커녕, 제 자식이 그런 것을 가지지 않았음을 다행으로 여겼다.\n\n아마도 그래서, 앞서 말한 그 마음씨 좋은 여자가 이 가엾은 처녀의 이름을 윌킨스 부인에게 처음 입에 올렸을 것이다. 그러나 윌킨스 부인의 의심을 굳히게 한 또 다른 사정이 있었다. 제니가 근래에 올워디 씨 댁을 자주 드나들었던 것이다. 그녀는 브리짓 아가씨가 심한 병을 앓을 때 간병인 노릇을 하며 여러 밤을 그 아가씨 곁에서 지새웠고, 그뿐 아니라 올워디 씨가 돌아오기 바로 전날에도 그곳에 있는 모습을 다름 아닌 윌킨스 부인 자신이 보았던 것이다. 다만 그 눈치 빠른 인물도 처음에는 그 일로 그녀를 의심할 생각을 품지 않았다. 그녀 자신이 말했듯, \"나는 늘 제니를 아주 얌전한 처녀로 여겨 왔거든요(사실 그녀에 대해 아는 거라곤 거의 없었지만요). 그보다는 잘난 척 거들먹대는 그 음탕한 계집애들 가운데 하나가 아닐까 의심했죠. 제깟 것들이 어디 예쁘다고 그리 으스대니 말이에요.\" 하고 말이다.\n\n이제 제니는 데버라 부인 앞에 직접 출두하라는 호출을 받았고, 곧바로 그리했다. 그러자 데버라 부인은 재판관의 근엄함에다 그 이상의 매서움까지 얹어, \"이 뻔뻔한 갈보 같으니!\"라는 말로 일장 연설을 시작했는데, 그 연설에서 그녀는 죄수를 고발한다기보다 차라리 선고를 내리는 쪽으로 나아갔다.\n\n데버라 부인은 앞서 보인 까닭들로 제니의 죄를 굳게 확신하고 있었지만, 올워디 씨라면 그녀에게 유죄를 선고하기 위해 좀 더 확실한 증거를 요구했을지도 모른다. 그러나 제니는 고발당한 그 일 전부를 순순히 자백함으로써, 자신을 단죄하려는 이들의 그러한 수고를 덜어 주었다.\n\n이 자백은 보아하니 차라리 뉘우치는 말투로 전해졌건만, 데버라 부인의 마음을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 못했다. 그녀는 이제 전보다 더 모욕적인 말투로 두 번째 선고를 그녀에게 내렸다. 어느덧 매우 많아진 구경꾼들에게도 그 자백은 더 나은 효과를 거두지 못했다. 그들 중 많은 이가 \"마님 비단 가운이 어떻게 끝장날지 다 알아봤다니까.\" 하고 소리쳤고, 더러는 그녀의 학식을 두고 비아냥거렸다. 거기 있던 여자치고 가엾은 제니를 향한 혐오를 어떻게든 드러내지 않는 이가 없었다. 제니는 그 모든 것을 아주 참을성 있게 견뎌 냈지만, 한 여자의 악의만은 예외였다. 그 여자는 그녀의 인물을 들먹이며 콧대를 치켜세우고는 \"이런 시시한 것한테 비단 가운을 내주는 걸 보면, 그 사내는 어지간히 비위가 좋은 모양이지!\" 하고 말했다. 이에 제니는 신중한 사람이라면 놀랄 만한 신랄함으로 받아쳤다. 자신의 정조를 향한 온갖 모욕을 그토록 태연하게 견뎌 내던 그 모습을 지켜본 사람이라면 말이다. 하지만 그녀의 인내심이 어쩌면 바닥났던 것이리라. 인내란 쓰면 쓸수록 쉬이 지치는 미덕이니 말이다.\n\n데버라 부인은 그 탐문에서 기대 이상의 성과를 거두고 의기양양하게 돌아와, 정해진 시각에 올워디 씨에게 충실히 보고를 올렸다. 그는 그 이야기에 몹시 놀랐다. 그 처녀의 남다른 재주와 향상에 대해 익히 들어 알고 있었던 데다, 그녀를 이웃의 어느 부목사에게 자그마한 성직록과 함께 시집보낼 작정이었던 까닭이다. 그러니 이 일을 두고 그가 느낀 근심은 적어도 데버라 부인에게서 엿보인 흡족함만큼은 되었고, 많은 독자에게는 그쪽이 훨씬 더 이치에 맞아 보일 것이다.\n\n브리짓 아가씨는 성호를 그으며 말했다. \"나로서는, 이제부터 어떤 여자에 대해서도 두 번 다시 좋게 보지 않을 거예요.\" 제니는 이 일이 있기 전까지 그녀의 총애 또한 듬뿍 받는 행운을 누렸던 것이다.\n\n신중한 가정부는 다시 한번 그 가엾은 죄인을 올워디 씨 앞으로 데려오라는 명을 받고 떠났다. 다만 더러는 바라고 모두는 으레 그러리라 짐작한 대로 교화소로 보내기 위함이 아니라, 유익한 훈계와 꾸지람을 받게 하기 위함이었다. 그런 종류의 교훈적인 글을 즐기는 이라면, 다음 장에서 그것을 찬찬히 읽어 볼 수 있을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n너무도 무거운 내용을 담고 있어, 독자는 이 장을 통틀어 한 번도 웃을 수 없을 것이다. 혹 작가를 비웃는다면 또 모를까.\n\n제니가 모습을 나타내자, 올워디 씨는 그녀를 자기 서재로 데려가 다음과 같이 말했다. \"얘야, 너도 알겠지만, 나는 치안판사로서 네가 저지른 일에 대해 너를 아주 엄하게 벌할 권한을 쥐고 있다. 그리고 너는 어쩌면 내가 그 권한을 실제로 행사하지 않을까 더 두려워할지도 모르겠구나. 어떤 의미에서는 네가 네 죄를 내 문 앞에 갖다 놓은 셈이니 말이다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩌면 바로 그 점이, 내가 너를 좀 더 너그럽게 대하기로 마음먹은 한 가지 까닭일지도 모른다. 사사로운 원한이 치안판사를 좌우해서는 결코 안 되는 법이니, 나는 네가 그 아이를 내 집에 두고 간 일을 네 죄를 무겁게 하는 사유로 여기기는커녕, 오히려 네게 유리하게 헤아릴 생각이다. 즉 그것이 네 아이에 대한 자연스러운 정에서 비롯된 것이라 짐작하겠다는 말이다. 너 자신이나 그 못된 아비가 마련해 줄 수 있는 것보다 이렇게 하는 편이 아이를 더 잘 보살펴 줄 수 있으리라는 어떤 희망을 품었을 테니 말이다. 만일 네가 그 가엾은 것을, 정조를 버린 것 못지않게 인간다움마저 저버린 듯 보이는 어떤 비정한 어미들처럼 내다 버렸더라면, 나는 진실로 너에게 크게 노여워했을 것이다. 그러니 내가 너에게 타이르려는 것은 네 죄의 다른 한 부분, 다시 말해 네 정조를 더럽힌 일이다. 방탕한 자들은 그것을 아무리 가볍게 여길지 몰라도, 그 자체로 몹시 흉악하며 그 결과 또한 몹시 무서운 죄다.\n\n\"이 죄의 흉악한 본질은 모든 그리스도인에게 충분히 분명할 것이다. 그것은 우리 종교의 율법을, 그리고 그 종교를 세우신 분의 명백한 계명을 거역하여 저지른 일이기 때문이다.\n\n\"그리고 여기서 그 결과가 무섭다고 충분히 논할 수 있다. 신의 계명을 어겨 신의 노여움을 사는 것보다 더 무서운 일이 어디 있겠느냐. 더구나 가장 준엄한 응징이 콕 집어 선포된 그런 일에서라면 말이다.\n\n\"하지만 이런 것들은, 안타깝게도 너무 가볍게 여겨지긴 해도 워낙 명백한 일이라, 사람들이 다시 일깨워질 필요는 있을지언정 새로 알아야 할 것은 없다. 그러니 이 일에 대한 네 분별을 일깨우기 위한 한마디면 충분하겠다. 나는 네게 회개의 마음을 불어넣으려는 것이지, 절망으로 내몰려는 것이 아니니까.\n\n\"이 죄에는 또 다른 결과들도 있다. 앞서 말한 것만큼 무섭거나 공포로 가득하지는 않으나, 그래도 곰곰이 헤아려 보면 적어도 너희 여인들이라면 누구라도 이 죄를 저지르지 못하게 막아 줄 만한 것들이다.\n\n\"이 죄로 인해 너는 오명을 뒤집어쓰고, 옛날의 나병 환자들처럼 사회 밖으로 내쫓긴다. 적어도 사악하고 타락한 자들을 빼고는 그 누구와도 어울리지 못하게 되는 것이다. 다른 이들은 결코 너와 함께하려 하지 않을 테니 말이다.\n\n\"재산이 있다 한들, 너는 그것을 누릴 자격을 잃는다. 재산이 없다면, 새로 얻을 길조차 막힌다. 아니, 입에 풀칠할 양식을 구하는 것마저 거의 불가능해진다. 평판 있는 사람이라면 누구도 너를 제 집에 들이지 않을 테니까. 그리하여 너는 흔히 그 궁핍 자체에 떠밀려 수치와 비참의 처지에 빠지고, 그것은 결국 몸과 영혼 둘 다의 파멸로 어쩔 수 없이 끝나고 만다.\n\n\"어떤 쾌락이 이런 해악들을 보상할 수 있겠느냐? 어떤 유혹이 이토록 어리석은 거래를 받아들이도록 너를 구슬릴 만큼 강한 궤변과 기만을 갖출 수 있겠느냐? 또 어떤 육체의 욕망이 너의 이성을 그토록 압도하거나 그토록 깊이 잠재울 수 있어, 이런 벌을 늘 달고 다니는 죄로부터 네가 놀라 두려워하며 달아나는 것을 막을 수 있겠느냐?\n\n\"가장 천한 짐승과 자신을 같은 자리에 끌어내리고, 그 안의 위대하고 고귀한 모든 것, 그 천상의 부분 전부를, 창조물 가운데 가장 비천한 무리와 똑같이 지닌 한낱 욕망에 제물로 바칠 수 있는 여인이라면, 그 여인은 얼마나 비루하고 천박하며, 마음의 그 품위와 단정한 자긍심—그것이 없다면 우리는 인간이라 불릴 자격조차 없는—이 얼마나 텅 비어 있겠느냐! 정녕 어떤 여인도 사랑의 정열을 핑계로 삼지는 못하리라. 그것은 곧 자신이 남자의 한낱 도구이자 거품에 지나지 않음을 자인하는 꼴이니까. 사랑이란, 우리가 아무리 야만스럽게 그 뜻을 더럽히고 비틀어 놓는다 해도, 칭송받을 만한 것인 동시에 이성적인 정열이며, 서로 주고받는 사이가 아니고서는 결코 격렬할 수 없는 것이다. 성경이 원수를 사랑하라 이른다 해도, 그것은 우리가 벗에게 자연스레 품는 그 뜨거운 사랑을 뜻하는 것이 아니며, 하물며 그들을 위해 우리 목숨을, 또 우리에게 그보다 더 귀해야 할 우리의 순결을 제물로 바치라는 뜻은 더더욱 아니다. 그러니 분별 있는 여인이라면, 내가 너에게 들려준 그 모든 비참을 스스로 짊어지도록 꾀어내고, 그토록 큰 그녀의 대가를 치르게 하여 자신은 짧고 하찮고 경멸스러운 쾌락을 사들이려는 남자를, 원수가 아니고서 무엇으로 여길 수 있겠느냐! 관습의 법도에 따르면, 그 모든 무서운 결과를 동반한 수치는 오롯이 여인에게만 떨어지기 때문이다. 언제나 그 대상의 이로움을 구하는 사랑이, 여인이 이토록 크게 손해 볼 거래로 그녀를 배신해 빠뜨리려 들 수 있겠느냐? 그러니 그런 타락한 자가 그녀에게 진정한 애정을 품은 척 뻔뻔하게 굴거든, 여인은 그를 한낱 원수로뿐만 아니라 모든 원수 가운데 가장 못된 원수, 곧 거짓되고 음흉하며 배신을 일삼는, 벗인 척하는 자로 여겨야 하지 않겠느냐? 그자는 그녀의 몸뿐 아니라 그녀의 분별까지 동시에 망쳐 놓으려는 자이니 말이다.\"\n\n여기서 제니가 깊은 괴로움을 내비치자, 올워디는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 다시 이어 갔다. \"얘야, 내가 네게 이렇게 말한 것은, 이미 지나가 돌이킬 수 없는 일로 너를 모욕하려는 게 아니라, 앞날을 위해 너를 일깨우고 굳세게 하려는 것이다. 또 네가 저지른 그 무서운 잘못에도 불구하고 너의 분별을 어느 정도 믿는 마음이 없었다면, 그리고 너의 솔직하고 진실한 고백에 바탕을 둔, 네가 진심으로 뉘우치리라는 어떤 희망이 없었다면, 나는 이런 수고를 하지도 않았을 것이다. 만일 이런 믿음이 나를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 나는 네가 이 수치의 무대를 벗어나도록 손써 줄 것이다. 그곳에서 너는 아무에게도 알려지지 않은 채, 앞서 말했듯 이 세상에서 네 죄에 매겨진 벌을 피할 수 있을 것이다. 그리고 회개를 통해, 저세상에서 그 죄에 선포된 훨씬 더 무거운 형벌도 면하기를 바란다. 남은 평생 착하게 살아가거라. 그러면 궁핍이 너를 옆길로 빠지게 할 빌미가 되지 않을 것이다. 그리고 내 말을 믿어라. 이 세상에서조차, 순결하고 덕스러운 삶에는 방탕하고 사악한 삶보다 더 큰 즐거움이 있느니라.\n\n\"네 아이에 대해서는, 어떤 근심도 너를 괴롭히지 못하게 하여라. 내가 네가 바랄 수 있는 것보다 더 나은 방식으로 그 아이를 보살펴 주마. 이제 남은 일이라곤, 너를 유혹한 그 못된 자가 누구인지 내게 알려 주는 것뿐이다. 그자를 향한 나의 노여움은 네가 이 일로 겪은 것보다 훨씬 클 테니 말이다.\"\n\n이제 제니는 땅에 떨군 눈을 들어 올리고, 조신한 표정과 단정한 목소리로 이렇게 입을 열었다.\n\n\"나리를 알면서도 그 선하심을 사랑하지 않는다면, 그것은 누구에게든 분별이나 선함이 송두리째 결여되어 있다는 증거일 것입니다. 제 경우라면 그것은 더없는 배은망덕이 되겠지요. 이번 일에서 나리께서 기꺼이 베풀어 주신 그 크나큰 선하심을 가장 절실한 마음으로 느끼지 못한다면 말입니다. 지나간 일에 대한 제 뉘우침에 관해서는, 나리께서 제가 다시 얼굴 붉히지 않도록 되풀이를 아껴 주시리라 압니다. 앞으로의 제 행실이, 지금 제가 입으로 늘어놓는 어떤 다짐보다도 제 마음을 훨씬 잘 드러내 줄 것입니다. 나리, 감히 한 말씀 올리자면, 저는 나리께서 말씀 끝에 베풀어 주신 그 너그러운 제안보다도 나리의 충고를 훨씬 고맙게 받아들이고 있습니다. 나리께서 친히 말씀하셨듯, 그것은 제 분별을 인정해 주시는 표시이기 때문입니다.\" 여기서 그녀의 눈물이 빠르게 흘러내려, 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이렇게 이어 갔다. \"참으로, 나리, 나리의 인자하심이 저를 압도합니다. 하지만 저는 이 좋은 평가를 받을 자격을 갖추도록 애쓰겠습니다. 나리께서 그토록 다정하게 인정해 주시는 그 분별이 제게 있다면, 그런 충고가 제게 헛되이 던져질 리 없으니까요. 제 가엾고 의지할 데 없는 아이에게 베풀고자 하시는 인자하심에 대해, 나리, 진심으로 감사드립니다. 그 아이는 죄가 없으며, 살아남아 나리께서 베풀어 주실 모든 은혜에 감사할 줄 아는 사람이 되기를 바랍니다. 하지만 이제, 나리, 무릎 꿇고 간청드리니, 제 아이의 아비가 누구인지 밝히라고 계속 다그치지는 말아 주십시오. 언젠가는 반드시 나리께서 아시게 되리라 굳게 약속드립니다. 그러나 저는 지금 그 이름을 감추기로, 가장 엄숙한 명예의 약속과 맹세에, 또한 가장 경건한 서원과 다짐에 묶여 있습니다. 그리고 저는 나리를 너무도 잘 알기에, 나리께서 제 명예든 제 신앙이든 제물로 바치기를 바라시리라고는 생각하지 않습니다.\"\n\n그 거룩한 말이 조금만 언급되어도 마음이 흔들리고 마는 올워디 씨는 대답하기 전에 잠시 머뭇거리더니, 이윽고 그녀에게 말했다. 악당에게 그런 약속을 한 것은 잘못한 일이지만, 이미 그리했으니 그것을 깨라고 우길 수는 없다고. 그가 물은 것은 부질없는 호기심에서가 아니라 그 사내를 벌하기 위함이며, 적어도 자격 없는 자에게 모르고 은혜를 베푸는 일이 없도록 하기 위함이라고 말했다.\n\n이 점들에 관하여, 제니는 가장 엄숙한 다짐으로 그를 안심시켰다. 그 남자는 그가 닿을 수 있는 범위에서 완전히 벗어나 있으며, 그의 권한에 매여 있지도 않고, 그의 선의의 대상이 될 가망도 전혀 없다고 말이다.\n\n이 솔직한 태도 덕분에 제니는 이 훌륭한 사람에게 큰 신뢰를 얻었으므로, 그는 그녀가 한 말을 어렵잖게 믿어 주었다. 그녀가 거짓말로 자신을 변명하기를 떳떳하게 거부하고, 또 다른 이를 팔아 자기 명예나 정직을 저버리기보다는 차라리 지금 처지에서 그의 노여움을 더 사는 위험을 무릅썼으니, 그녀가 그 자신에게 거짓을 저지르지는 않으리라는 우려가 그에게는 거의 없었던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 그는, 그녀가 뒤집어쓴 그 오명이 닿지 못할 곳으로 그녀를 머잖아 옮겨 주겠노라 다짐하며 그녀를 돌려보냈다. 끝으로 그는 회개를 권하는 몇 마디를 덧붙이며 이렇게 마무리했다. \"얘야, 생각해 보아라. 너에게는 아직 화해해야 할 또 다른 분이 계시니, 그분의 은총은 나의 것보다 너에게 훨씬 더 중요하니라.\"\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n브리짓과 데버러 두 부인 사이의 대화. 앞 장보다 재미는 더하나 교훈은 덜한 내용을 담음.\n\n앞서 본 대로 올워디 씨가 제니 존스를 데리고 서재로 물러간 사이, 브리짓 부인은 마음씨 좋은 가정부와 함께 바로 그 서재에 잇닿은 자리로 가 진을 쳤다. 거기서 두 사람은 열쇠 구멍이 전해 주는 소리를 따라, 올워디 씨가 베푼 그 교훈적인 훈계며 제니의 대답이며, 실로 앞 장에서 오간 온갖 자질구레한 이야기까지 귀로 빨아들였다.\n\n오라비의 서재 문에 난 이 구멍은 브리짓 부인에게 너무도 익숙한 것이어서, 옛날 티스베가 그 유명한 담벼락 구멍을 드나든 만큼이나 자주 부인이 이용해 온 터였다. 이 구멍은 여러모로 쓸모가 많았다. 이런 수단을 통해 브리짓 부인은 오라비가 굳이 거듭 일러 주는 수고를 끼치지 않고도 그의 생각을 자주 알아챌 수 있었으니까. 물론 이 통로에는 더러 불편이 따랐고, 부인은 셰익스피어에 나오는 티스베처럼 \"오, 못된, 못된 담벼락아!\"라고 외치고 싶을 때도 있었다. 올워디 씨가 치안판사였던 까닭에, 사생아 따위에 관한 심문 자리에서는 처녀의 정숙한 귀를 몹시 거스를 만한 일들이 더러 벌어지곤 했기 때문이다. 더구나 그 처녀가 마흔 줄에 가까워지는 경우라면 더욱 그러한데, 바로 브리짓 부인이 그러했다. 그렇지만 이런 때 부인에게는 남자들의 눈을 피해 자신의 홍조를 감출 수 있다는 이점이 있었다. 그리고 \"나타나지 않는 것과 존재하지 않는 것은 같은 이치로 다룬다\"는 법언이 있으니, 우리말로 옮기면 \"여자가 얼굴 붉히는 것을 들키지 않으면, 그 여자는 아예 붉히지 않은 것이다\"라는 뜻이다.\n\n올워디 씨와 그 처녀 사이의 장면이 이어지는 내내 두 마음씨 좋은 여인은 입을 꼭 다물고 있었다. 그러나 그 장면이 끝나고 그 신사가 더는 들을 수 없는 거리로 사라지자마자, 데버러 부인은 주인의 너그러움에 대해, 특히 아이 아비를 감추도록 내버려 둔 처사에 대해 불평을 터뜨리지 않을 수 없었다. 부인은 해가 지기 전에 그 처녀에게서 기어이 아비의 이름을 캐내고야 말겠다고 으름장을 놓았다.\n\n이 말에 브리짓 부인은 얼굴을 풀며 미소를 지었다(그로서는 무척 드문 일이었다). 그렇다고 독자께서 이것을 호메로스가 베누스를 두고 웃음을 사랑하는 여신이라 부를 때 떠올리게 하려던 그런 음탕한 미소 가운데 하나라고 여기시지는 않기를 바란다. 또한 세라피나 부인이 무대 곁 특별석에서 쏘아 보내는, 베누스가 그것에 견줄 수만 있다면 제 불멸성마저 내놓을 법한 그런 미소 가운데 하나도 아니었다. 아니, 이것은 차라리 위엄 어린 티시포네의 보조개 팬 뺨에서나, 아니면 그 자매인 처녀들 가운데 하나에게서 나왔으리라 짐작할 만한 미소였다.\n\n그러니까 그런 미소를 띠고서, 그리고 화창한 십일월에 부는 보레아스의 저녁 산들바람처럼 달콤한 목소리로, 브리짓 부인은 데버러 부인의 호기심을 부드럽게 나무랐다. 아무래도 데버러 부인은 그 악덕에 너무 깊이 물든 듯했고, 브리짓 부인은 그것을 몹시 신랄하게 성토하면서 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"내게 온갖 허물이 있다 해도, 적들조차 내가 남의 일을 캐고 다닌다고는 탓하지 못할 테니, 그 점만은 하느님께 감사한다오.\"\n\n이어서 부인은 제니가 보여 준 명예로움과 기개를 칭찬하기 시작했다. 자기 고백의 진실함과 연인에 대한 충직함에 어떤 갸륵함이 있다는 오라비의 말에 동의하지 않을 수 없다고 했다. 부인은 늘 제니를 아주 착한 처녀로 여겨 왔으며, 분명 어떤 못된 놈에게 꾐을 당했을 것이고, 그놈이 제니보다 한없이 더 큰 잘못을 저질렀으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다고 했다. 또 십중팔구 그놈이 결혼하겠다는 약속이나 그밖의 무슨 음흉한 수작으로 제니를 넘어가게 했을 거라고 했다.\n\n브리짓 부인의 이런 태도에 데버러 부인은 크게 놀랐다. 이 예의 바른 여인은 주인에게든 그 누이에게든 먼저 그들의 속내를 떠보지 않고는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않았고, 자기 생각은 늘 그들의 생각과 한목소리였기 때문이다. 그런데 이번만큼은 마음 놓고 거침없이 떠들어도 되겠다고 여겼던 것이다. 그렇다고 눈치 빠른 독자께서 데버러 부인이 그렇게 한 것을 두고 미리 헤아리는 분별이 모자랐다고 탓하지는 않으실 테고, 오히려 자기가 엉뚱한 방향으로 키를 잡았음을 깨달았을 때 얼마나 놀라운 민첩함으로 뱃머리를 돌리는지를 감탄하실 것이다.\n\n\"아니죠, 마님.\" 이 유능한 여인, 참으로 위대한 책략가가 말했다. \"솔직히 말씀드려, 저도 마님처럼 그 처녀의 기개를 칭찬하지 않을 수 없네요. 그리고 마님 말씀대로, 그 애가 어떤 못된 사내에게 속았다면, 그 가엾은 것이 딱하기 짝이 없지요. 그리고 정말이지, 마님 말씀대로, 그 처녀는 늘 착하고 정직하고 수수한 아이로 보였지, 이 동네 어떤 음탕한 계집들처럼 제 얼굴을 뽐내거나 하지는 않았으니까요.\"\n\n\"네 말이 맞다, 데버러.\" 브리짓 아가씨가 말했다. \"그 애가 우리 교구에 너무도 많은 그런 허영 떠는 잡것들 가운데 하나였다면, 나는 그 애를 너그럽게 대한 오라비를 나무랐을 게다. 며칠 전 교회에서 목덜미를 드러낸 농부 딸 둘을 보았는데, 정말이지 그 꼴에 충격을 받았다. 계집들이 사내를 낚으려고 미끼를 내거는 짓을 한다면, 그들이 어떤 험한 꼴을 당하든 알 바 아니지. 나는 그런 것들이 끔찍하게 싫다. 차라리 그 얼굴이 천연두 자국으로 얽었더라면 그들에게 훨씬 나았을 게다. 하지만 솔직히 말해, 가엾은 제니에게서는 그런 음탕한 행실을 단 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 분명 어떤 교활한 악당이 그 애를 배신했거나, 어쩌면 강제로 욕보였을 게야. 나는 그 가엾은 것이 진심으로 가엾다.\"\n\n데버러 부인은 이 모든 생각에 맞장구를 쳤고, 두 사람의 대화는 아름다움을 싸잡아 신랄하게 헐뜯는 것으로, 그리고 기만하는 사내들의 못된 술수에 속아 넘어가는 모든 정직하고 수수한 처녀들을 향한 갖은 가엾어하는 마음으로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n독자를 놀라게 할 일들을 담은 장.\n\n제니는 올워디 씨에게서 받은 대접에 흡족해하며 집으로 돌아왔고, 그가 자신에게 베푼 너그러움을 부지런히 사방에 알리고 다녔다. 어쩌면 그것은 한편으로는 자기 자존심을 채우려는 제물이었고, 다른 한편으로는 이웃들의 마음을 누그러뜨려 그들의 떠들썩한 비난을 잠재우려는 더 영리한 속셈이었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 만일 그녀가 정말 이 후자의 속셈을 품었다면 그것은 제법 이치에 맞아 보일지 몰라도, 막상 일은 그녀의 기대대로 풀리지 않았다. 그녀가 치안판사 앞에 불려 나갔을 때, 사람들은 누구나 그녀의 운명이 교화소행이리라 짐작했다. 몇몇 젊은 여자들은 \"그년한테 딱 알맞은 꼴이지\" 하고 소리치며, 비단 가운을 걸친 채 삼을 두드리는 그녀의 모습을 상상하며 즐거워했다. 그래도 그녀의 처지를 가엾게 여기기 시작한 사람들도 많았다. 하지만 올워디 씨가 어떻게 처신했는지가 알려지자, 분위기는 그녀에게 등을 돌렸다. 한 사람은 이렇게 말했다. \"정말이지, 마님께서는 운도 참 좋으셔.\" 두 번째 사람은 외쳤다. \"총애받는다는 게 어떤 건지 보라고!\" 세 번째 사람은 말했다. \"그럼, 이게 다 그년이 글 배운 덕이지.\" 사람들은 저마다 이 일을 두고 짓궂은 말을 한마디씩 보태며, 치안판사의 편파성을 비꼬았다.\n\n올워디 씨의 권세와 자비로움을 떠올리는 독자에게는, 이 사람들의 행태가 분별없고 배은망덕해 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 그의 권세로 말하자면, 그는 결코 그것을 휘두른 적이 없었다. 또 그의 자비로움으로 말하자면, 그는 그것을 어찌나 많이 베풀었던지 그 바람에 이웃들 모두의 비위를 거슬러 놓고 말았다. 위대한 사람들이라면 잘 아는 한 가지 비밀이 있으니, 은혜를 베푼다고 해서 늘 벗을 얻는 것은 아니며 오히려 숱한 적을 만드는 일만큼은 틀림없다는 것이다.\n\n그러나 제니는 올워디 씨의 보살핌과 호의 덕분에 머잖아 비난의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 옮겨졌다. 그리하여 악의가 더는 그녀에게 분풀이를 할 수 없게 되자, 그것은 또 다른 분풀이 대상을 찾기 시작했다. 그 대상은 다름 아닌 올워디 씨 본인이었다. 그가 바로 그 업둥이 아이의 아버지라는 수군거림이 곧 퍼져 나갔던 것이다.\n\n이 추측은 그의 처신을 사람들의 통념과 어찌나 그럴듯하게 맞아떨어지게 했는지, 너나없이 그 말에 동조했다. 그의 관대함을 향하던 비난은 곧 방향을 틀어, 가엾은 처녀를 향한 그의 잔인함을 성토하는 쪽으로 바뀌었다. 점잖고 선량한 부인네들은 아이를 낳아 놓고 모른 척하는 사내들을 두고 목청을 높였다. 게다가 제니가 떠난 뒤로는, 그녀가 입에 담기조차 너무 시커먼 음모에 휘말려 어디론가 감쪽같이 빼돌려졌다고 넌지시 흘리는 자들도 있었고, 이 모든 일을 두고 법으로 조사를 벌여야 마땅하며 어떤 사람들은 그 처녀를 내놓도록 강제되어야 한다고 자꾸만 운을 떼는 자들도 있었다.\n\n이런 비방들은 올워디 씨가 복으로 지닌 성품보다 더 미덥지 못하고 의심받기 쉬운 사람에게라면 아마 나쁜 결과를 빚어냈을지도, 적어도 어떤 골칫거리쯤은 일으켰을지도 모른다. 그러나 그의 경우에는 그런 영향이 전혀 없었다. 그가 그것들을 진심으로 우습게 여겼기에, 비방들은 그저 동네의 선량한 수다쟁이들에게 죄 없는 심심풀이 거리나 되어 주었을 뿐이다.\n\n그런데 우리로서는 독자가 어떤 기질의 사람인지 도무지 짐작할 길이 없고, 또 그가 제니에 관한 이야기를 다시 듣기까지는 한참이 걸릴 터이므로, 여기서 미리 일러두는 편이 좋겠다. 올워디 씨는 그 어떤 죄스러운 의도와도 전혀 무관했으며, 앞으로도 그러하리라는 점을 말이다. 그가 저지른 잘못이라곤 정치적인 실책 하나뿐이었으니, 정의에 자비를 섞었다는 것, 그리고 군중*의 그 선량한 기질을 만족시켜 주기를 거부했다는 것이다. 군중은 가엾은 제니를 동정의 대상으로 삼아 그 연민을 마음껏 풀고 싶어 했고, 정작 그녀를 가엾이 여기기 위해서는 그녀가 브라이드웰에서 수치스러운 교화를 받아 파멸과 오욕으로 내던져지는 꼴을 보고 싶어 했던 것이다.\n\n* 군중: 이 단어가 우리 글에 나올 때면, 그것은 지위 고하를 막론하고 덕도 분별도 없는 사람들을 가리키며, 흔히 가장 높은 신분의 사람들 가운데 상당수를 두고 하는 말이다.\n\n이런 그들의 바람을 따랐더라면 그녀가 새사람이 될 모든 희망은 사라졌을 것이고, 훗날 그녀 자신의 마음이 덕의 길을 택하도록 이끈다 해도 그 문조차 닫혀 버렸을 것이다. 그러나 올워디 씨는 그러기는커녕, 그 처녀가 그 길로 되돌아오도록 단 하나 가능한 방법으로 북돋아 주는 쪽을 택했다. 안타깝지만 너무도 참된 사실은, 숱한 여인들이 첫 번째 실수를 만회하지 못한 탓에 타락하여 악의 마지막 나락까지 떨어지고 만다는 것이다. 그리고 안타깝게도 이런 일은, 그들이 옛 지인들 사이에 머무는 한 언제까지나 되풀이될 것이다. 그러므로 올워디 씨가 제니를, 평판을 잃은 쓰라린 결과를 맛본 뒤에라도 좋은 평판이라는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있는 곳으로 옮겨 준 것은 참으로 현명한 처사였다.\n\n그러니 그곳이 어디든, 우리는 그녀가 그곳으로 무사히 가기를 빌어 주며, 지금으로서는 그녀와, 그녀의 아이인 그 어린 업둥이에게 작별을 고하기로 하자. 우리에게는 독자에게 전할, 훨씬 더 중대한 일들이 있으니 말이다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n올워디 씨의 손님 대접, 그리고 그에게 환대를 받은 두 형제, 곧 한 사람은 의사이고 다른 한 사람은 대위인 두 인물에 대한 짧은 소묘.\n\n올워디 씨의 집도, 그의 마음도 그 누구에게든 닫혀 있지 않았으나, 특히 덕망 있는 사람들에게는 더욱 활짝 열려 있었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이 나라 안에서 그럴 자격을 갖춘 것만으로 식사 한 끼를 확실히 얻을 수 있는 집은 이곳뿐이었다.\n\n무엇보다도 천재성과 학식을 갖춘 사람들이 그의 호의 가운데 가장 으뜸가는 자리를 차지했다. 그리고 이런 부류를 가려내는 데에 그는 안목이 상당했다. 비록 그는 정식 학문 교육의 혜택을 누리지 못했으나, 타고난 재능이 워낙 풍부한 데다, 뒤늦게나마 글공부에 힘차게 매진하고 또 이 방면에서 뛰어난 인물들과 자주 대화를 나눈 덕분에 큰 이득을 보아, 그 자신이 대부분의 문학 갈래에서 꽤나 유능한 감식가가 되어 있었다.\n\n이런 종류의 재능이 거의 유행을 타지 못하고 또 그에 대한 대접도 변변치 못한 시대에, 그것을 지닌 사람들이 큰 호의로 맞아 줄 것이 분명한 곳으로 앞다투어 몰려드는 것은 조금도 이상한 일이 아니었다. 실로 그곳에서 그들은, 마치 자기 권리로 누릴 자격이 있기라도 한 것처럼, 넉넉한 재산이 주는 거의 같은 혜택을 누릴 수 있었기 때문이다. 올워디 씨는, 재치와 학식을 갖춘 사람들에게 고기와 술과 잠자리를 더없이 후하게 베풀면서도 그 대가로 오락과 가르침과 아첨과 굴종 말고는 아무것도 기대하지 않는 척하는, 그런 부류의 너그러운 사람이 아니었다. 한마디로, 그런 사람들이 주인의 옷을 입지도 않고 삯을 받지도 않으면서 하인의 명부에 이름을 올리게 만드는 그런 사람이 아니었던 것이다.\n\n오히려 이 집에서는 누구나 자기 시간의 완전한 주인이었다. 그래서 그는 법과 덕과 종교라는 제약 안에서만큼은 마음껏 자기 욕구를 채울 수 있었고, 또 건강이 요구하거나 절제를, 심지어 금욕을 마음먹게 되면, 언제든 내키는 대로 어떤 식사 자리든 빠지거나 도중에 물러날 수 있었다. 그러지 말라는 권유조차 받지 않으면서 말이다. 실로 윗사람의 그런 권유에는 늘 명령의 냄새가 짙게 배어 있는 법이다. 그러나 이곳에서는 모두가 그런 무례함에서 자유로웠다. 다른 어떤 곳에서라면 비슷한 재산 덕에 그 동석 자체가 호의로 여겨질 사람들뿐 아니라, 가난한 형편 탓에 이런 자선의 거처가 편리하기에, 바로 그 필요 때문에 도리어 대갓집 식탁에서 덜 환영받기 마련인 사람들까지도 그러했다.\n\n이런 부류 가운데 블리필 박사라는 신사가 있었다. 그는 자기가 싫어하는 직업을 억지로 시키려 든 아버지의 고집 때문에, 큰 재능이 주는 이점을 잃어버리는 불운을 겪은 사람이었다. 그 고집에 복종하느라 박사는 젊은 시절 의학을 공부해야만 했다. 아니, 공부했다고 말해야 한다고 하는 편이 옳겠다. 실은 이런 종류의 책이야말로 그가 거의 유일하게 친숙하지 못한 분야였기 때문이다. 그에게 불행하게도 박사는 정작 밥벌이로 삼아야 할 그 학문만 빼고는 거의 모든 다른 학문에 통달해 있었다. 그 결과 박사는 마흔이 되도록 먹을 빵 한 조각 없는 처지가 되고 말았다.\n\n이런 사람이라면 올워디 씨의 식탁에서 환영받을 것이 분명했다. 올워디 씨에게는, 불운이 그 불운한 사람 자신의 어리석음이나 악행이 아니라 남들의 어리석음이나 악행에서 비롯된 것이기만 하다면, 언제나 그것이 곧 추천장이었기 때문이다. 이런 소극적인 미덕 말고도, 박사에게는 한 가지 적극적인 추천 거리가 있었다. 바로 신앙심이 깊어 보인다는 점이었다. 그의 신앙이 진짜인지, 아니면 겉모습뿐인지는 내가 감히 말하지 않겠다. 참된 것과 거짓된 것을 가려낼 만한 시금석을 나는 가지고 있지 않으니 말이다.\n\n그의 인품 가운데 이런 면이 올워디 씨를 흡족하게 했다면, 브리짓 양은 아예 기쁘게 했다. 그녀는 그를 끌어들여 종교 논쟁을 여러 차례 벌였는데, 그럴 때마다 그녀는 박사의 해박함에 늘 큰 만족을 드러냈고, 또 그가 자신의 해박함에 자주 보내 주는 찬사에도 그에 못지않게 만족스러워했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀는 영국 신학 서적을 많이 읽어, 이웃의 보좌 사제들 가운데 한둘이 아니라 여럿을 쩔쩔매게 한 바 있었다. 실로 그녀의 대화는 어찌나 정결하고, 그 표정은 어찌나 현숙하며, 몸가짐 전체가 어찌나 진중하고 엄숙했던지, 같은 이름을 지닌 성녀와, 아니 로마 성인력에 오른 그 어떤 여인과도 똑같이 성녀라는 이름을 받을 만해 보였다.\n\n온갖 종류의 공감이 사랑을 낳기 쉽듯이, 서로 다른 성별의 사람들 사이에서 종교적 공감만큼 이쪽으로 곧장 기우는 것이 없다는 사실을 경험은 우리에게 일러 준다. 박사는 자신이 브리짓 양에게 무척 마음에 드는 사람이라는 것을 알아채고는, 이제 한 십 년쯤 전에 자기에게 일어난 불운한 사건 하나를 한탄하기 시작했다. 다름 아니라 다른 여자와 결혼한 일이었는데, 그 여자는 아직 살아 있었을 뿐 아니라, 더 고약하게도 올워디 씨가 그 사실을 알고 있다는 점이었다. 이것은, 그렇지 않았다면 이 젊은 아가씨와 더불어 얻을 가능성이 충분해 보이던 그 행복을 가로막는 치명적인 장벽이었다. 떳떳치 못한 방종으로 말하자면, 그는 분명 그런 것은 결코 생각조차 하지 않았으니까. 이것은 아마도 그의 신앙심 덕분이었거나, 아니면 그의 열정이 정결한 까닭이었을 것이다. 그 열정은 오직 결혼만이 손에 넣게 해 주거나 권리를 부여할 수 있는 것, 떳떳치 못한 관계로는 결코 얻을 수 없는 것에 단단히 고정되어 있었다.\n\n이런 문제들을 곰곰이 곱씹은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 그의 기억 속에 자기에게 그런 불행한 결격 사유가 전혀 없는 동생이 하나 있다는 사실이 떠올랐다. 이 동생이라면 틀림없이 성공하리라고 그는 의심치 않았다. 그가 보기에 아가씨에게 결혼할 마음이 있어 보였기 때문이다. 그리고 독자도 아마 그 동생의 자질을 듣고 나면, 그가 동생의 성공을 굳게 믿은 것을 나무라지 않으리라.\n\n이 신사는 나이가 서른다섯쯤이었다. 중간 키에, 이른바 체격이 다부진 사람이었다. 이마에는 흉터가 하나 있었는데, 그것은 그의 잘생긴 외모를 해친다기보다는 오히려 그의 용맹함을 드러내 주었다(그는 반액 봉급을 받는 퇴역 장교였으니까). 치아가 좋았고, 마음만 내키면 미소에 어딘가 상냥한 데가 있었다. 비록 타고난 얼굴 생김은 물론 분위기와 목소리에도 거친 구석이 많았으나, 그는 언제든 그것을 거두어들이고 더없이 부드럽고 유쾌한 모습으로 나타날 줄 알았다. 그는 천박하지도 않았고, 재치가 아주 없는 것도 아니었으며, 젊은 시절에는 발랄함이 넘쳤다. 비록 근래에는 더 진지한 성품을 두르고 있었으나, 마음만 먹으면 언제든 그 발랄함을 되살릴 수 있었다.\n\n그도 박사와 마찬가지로 대학 교육을 받았다. 그의 아버지가 앞서 말한 그 똑같은 아버지의 권위로 그를 성직자로 정해 두었기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 서품을 받기 전에 노신사가 세상을 떠나자, 그는 교회 대신 군대를 택했고, 주교의 성직 임명보다 국왕의 임관을 더 좋아했다.\n\n그는 용기병 중위 자리를 사들였고, 나중에는 대위까지 올랐다. 그러나 자기 연대장과 다툰 끝에, 그 사람의 입김으로 자리를 팔아야만 했다. 그때부터 그는 완전히 시골에 파묻혀 지내며 성경 공부에 매달렸고, 감리교에 마음이 기울어 있다는 의심을 적잖이 받았다.\n\n그러니 이런 사람이라면, 그토록 성녀 같은 기질을 지녔고 마음이 어느 특정한 상대가 아니라 그저 결혼이라는 상태 자체에 쏠려 있을 뿐인 아가씨에게서 성공을 거두는 것도 그리 가망 없는 일은 아니어 보였다. 하지만 분명 동생과 그리 깊은 우애가 있는 것도 아닌 박사가, 어째서 동생을 위해 올워디의 환대에 이토록 못된 보답을 할 생각을 하게 되었는지는 그리 쉽게 설명할 수 있는 문제가 아니다.\n\n어떤 본성은 악을 즐기는 것일까, 다른 본성이 덕을 즐긴다고 여겨지듯이? 아니면 우리 자신이 직접 저지를 수 없을 때 도둑질의 공범이 되는 데에 어떤 쾌감이 있는 것일까? 아니면 마지막으로(경험이 그럴듯하다고 일러 주는 바이지만), 우리는 자기 가문에 손톱만큼의 애정이나 존경도 품고 있지 않으면서도, 그 가문을 번성시키는 데에서 만족을 느끼는 것일까?\n\n이런 동기들 가운데 어느 것이 박사에게 작용했는지는 단정하지 않겠다. 어쨌든 사실은 이러했다. 그는 동생을 불러들였고, 어렵지 않게 방도를 찾아내어, 그저 자기를 잠깐 찾아온 손님인 것처럼 동생을 올워디의 집에 소개했다.\n\n대위가 그 집에 든 지 일주일도 채 되지 않아, 박사는 자기 안목을 자축할 만한 이유를 얻었다. 실로 대위는 옛날의 오비디우스 못지않게 사랑의 기술에 통달한 대가였다. 게다가 그는 형으로부터 적절한 귀띔까지 받았고, 그것을 더없이 유리하게 살려 내는 데에 빈틈이 없었다.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\n이 사랑에 빠지는 일에 관하여 많은 규칙과 몇 가지 사례를 담고 있다. 또한 아름다움에 대한 묘사와, 결혼으로 이끄는 그 밖의 좀 더 타산적인 유인들도 함께 다룬다.\n\n현명한 남자였는지 여자였는지는 잊어버렸으나, 어떤 이가 이렇게 말한 적이 있다. 모든 사람은 평생에 한 번은 사랑에 빠질 운명을 타고난다고. 내 기억으로는 그 일에 정해진 특정한 시기는 없다. 그러나 브리짓 양이 이른 그 나이야말로 이 목적을 위해 정해 둘 수 있는 어느 시기 못지않게 적절해 보인다. 실제로 그 일은 훨씬 더 이른 나이에 일어나는 경우가 많다. 그러나 그렇지 않을 때라면, 내가 보아 온 바로는 대개 이맘때를 거의 거르는 법이 없다. 더구나 우리는 이 시기의 사랑이 인생의 더 젊은 시절에 이따금 모습을 드러내는 사랑보다 훨씬 진지하고 한결같은 성질을 지닌다는 점을 짚어 둘 만하다. 소녀들의 사랑은 종잡을 수 없고, 변덕스러우며, 어찌나 어리석은지 그 아가씨가 도대체 무엇을 원하는지 우리가 늘 알아챌 수가 없다. 아니, 그녀 자신조차 늘 그것을 알고 있는지 의심스러울 지경이다.\n\n그런데 마흔쯤 된 여인에게서는 우리가 이를 알아보는 데 조금도 어려움이 없다. 그처럼 점잖고 진중하며 경험 많은 부인들은 자기 마음이 무엇을 뜻하는지 잘 알고 있으니, 웬만큼만 눈치가 있는 남자라면 누구든 그것을 더없이 확실하게 알아채기란 늘 아주 쉬운 일이다.\n\n브리짓 양은 이 모든 관찰의 본보기였다. 그녀는 대위와 자리를 함께한 지 얼마 되지 않아 이 격정에 사로잡히고 말았다. 그렇다고 자기 병을 알지도 못하는 가냘프고 어리석은 소녀처럼 집 안을 시름시름 풀 죽어 돌아다니지도 않았다. 그녀는 그 기분 좋은 감각을 느꼈고, 알았으며, 즐겼다. 그리고 그것이 죄 없는 것일 뿐 아니라 칭찬받을 만한 것임을 확신했기에, 두려워하지도 부끄러워하지도 않았다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 이 나이의 여인들이 남자를 향해 품는 이치에 맞는 격정과, 소녀가 소년에게 품는 부질없고 유치한 호감 사이에는 모든 면에서 큰 차이가 있다. 후자는 흔히 겉모습에만, 그것도 별 가치도 없고 오래가지도 못하는 것들에만 매달린다. 이를테면 앵두 같은 뺨, 작고 백합처럼 흰 손, 자두처럼 까만 눈, 흘러내리는 머리카락, 솜털 보송한 턱, 날렵한 몸매 같은 것들이다. 아니, 때로는 이보다 더 하잘것없고, 그나마 본인의 것도 아닌 매력에 빠지기도 한다. 그런 것은 사람 겉을 꾸미는 장식들로, 남자들이 그 덕을 입는 것은 자연이 아니라 재단사, 레이스 장수, 가발 장인, 모자 장수, 그리고 여성용 장신구 상인이다. 소녀들이 그런 격정을 자기 자신에게든 남에게든 인정하기를 부끄러워하는 것은 당연한 일이며, 실제로 대개 그렇게 부끄러워한다.\n\n브리짓 양의 사랑은 그런 종류가 아니었다. 대위는 옷차림에서 그런 멋쟁이 제조업자들에게 신세 진 것이 하나도 없었고, 그의 몸도 자연에게 그리 많은 신세를 진 편이 아니었다. 그의 옷차림과 몸이란, 무도회나 응접실에 나타났더라면 그곳에 모인 멋쟁이 부인들 모두에게 멸시와 비웃음을 샀을 만한 것이었다. 그의 옷은 과연 단정하기는 했으나, 수수하고 거칠며, 멋이라곤 없고 유행에도 뒤떨어진 것이었다. 그의 몸으로 말하자면, 우리는 이미 앞에서 또렷이 묘사한 바 있다. 그의 뺨 살갗은 앵두 빛깔과는 거리가 멀어도 한참 멀어서, 뺨의 본래 색이 무엇인지조차 분간할 수 없을 지경이었다. 눈가까지 뻗쳐 올라온 시커먼 수염이 온통 뒤덮고 있었기 때문이다. 그의 체격과 팔다리는 과연 정확히 균형이 잡혀 있었으나, 어찌나 큼지막한지 다른 무엇보다도 쟁기질하는 농부의 힘을 떠올리게 했다. 그의 어깨는 가늠하기 어려울 만큼 떡 벌어졌고, 종아리는 흔한 가마꾼의 것보다도 굵었다. 한마디로 그의 온몸에는, 우람한 힘과는 정반대되는 그 우아함과 아름다움이 전혀 없었다. 그 우아함과 아름다움이란 우리네 멋쟁이 신사들 대부분을 그토록 보기 좋게 돋보이게 하는 것으로, 한편으로는 그 조상들의 고귀한 핏줄, 다시 말해 진한 소스와 좋은 포도주로 빚어진 피 덕분이요, 다른 한편으로는 어려서부터 받은 도회적 교육 덕분이다.\n\n브리짓 양은 더없이 섬세한 안목을 지닌 여인이었지만, 대위의 대화가 지닌 매력이 어찌나 대단했던지 그녀는 그의 외모의 결점을 깡그리 못 본 척했다. 그녀는 훨씬 잘생긴 사내와 함께할 때보다 대위와 함께할 때 더 기분 좋은 시간을 누리리라 생각했고, 어쩌면 그것은 무척 현명한 판단이었다. 그래서 그녀는 눈을 즐겁게 하는 일은 제쳐 두고, 그 대신 훨씬 더 단단한 만족을 자기 것으로 삼고자 했다.\n\n대위는 이런 일을 알아채는 데 눈이 무척 밝았던지라, 브리짓 양의 격정을 알아채기가 무섭게 그것에 성실히 화답했다. 이 부인 역시 그녀의 연인 못지않게 미모로 이름난 사람은 아니었다. 그녀의 모습을 그려 보고 싶지만, 그 일은 이미 더 솜씨 좋은 거장, 다름 아닌 호가스 씨께서 해 놓으셨다. 그녀는 여러 해 전 그 앞에 모델로 앉았고, 근래에 그 신사가 〈겨울 아침〉이라는 판화로 세상에 내놓았으니, 그녀야말로 그 그림의 더없이 어울리는 상징이라 할 만했다. 그림 속에서 그녀는 코번트 가든 교회로 걸어가는(과연 그림 속에서 그녀는 걷고 있다) 모습으로, 뒤에는 비쩍 곯은 사동 하나가 그녀의 기도서를 들고 따라가고 있는 것을 볼 수 있다.\n\n대위 또한 매우 현명하게도, 덧없는 외모의 매력보다 이 부인에게서 기대되는 더 단단한 즐거움을 택했다. 그는 이성의 아름다움을 지극히 하찮고 겉만 번지르르한 자질로 여기는 그런 현명한 사내들 가운데 하나였다. 좀 더 정확히 말하자면, 그런 편의는 하나도 없는 미인보다는 못생긴 여인이라도 인생의 온갖 편의를 다 갖춘 쪽을 차라리 택하는 부류였다. 게다가 그는 식욕이 무척 왕성하고 입맛은 그리 까다롭지 않은 사람이었던지라, 아름다움이라는 소스가 없어도 결혼이라는 잔치에서 제 몫을 톡톡히 해내리라 여겼다.\n\n독자에게 솔직히 털어놓자면, 대위는 도착한 이래로, 적어도 그의 형이 그에게 이 혼사를 제안한 그 순간부터, 브리짓 양에게서 무슨 호의의 기미를 알아채기 한참 전부터, 이미 단단히 반해 있었다. 다시 말해 올워디 씨의 저택과 정원, 그의 토지와 가옥, 그리고 상속 재산에 반해 있었던 것이다. 대위는 이 모든 것을 어찌나 열렬히 사랑했던지, 거래 조건에 엔도르의 마녀까지 끼워 받아야 하는 처지였더라도 십중팔구 그것들과 혼인 계약을 맺었을 것이다.\n\n그리하여 올워디 씨가 의사에게 다시는 두 번째 아내를 맞을 뜻이 없노라 밝힌 바 있고, 누이가 그의 가장 가까운 혈육이었으며, 또 의사가 그의 속내가 누이에게서 난 어떤 아이든 자신의 상속자로 삼는 데 있음을 슬쩍 캐낸 터였으니—사실 이는 그가 끼어들지 않아도 법이 알아서 해 줄 일이었다—의사와 그의 동생은, 행복의 가장 본질적인 수단을 그토록 넉넉하게 갖추게 될 한 인간 존재를 세상에 태어나게 하는 것이야말로 자비로운 행위라고 여겼다. 그리하여 두 형제의 온 생각은 오로지 이 사랑스러운 부인의 마음을 어떻게 사로잡느냐에 쏠려 있었다.\n\n그러나 운명은 다정한 어버이여서, 곧잘 자기가 아끼는 자식을 위해 그들이 받을 자격이 있거나 바라는 것보다 더 많은 일을 해 주는 법인데, 그 운명이 대위를 위해 어찌나 부지런을 떨었던지, 그가 자기 목적을 이루려 계략을 짜고 있는 동안 부인 쪽에서도 그와 똑같은 욕망을 품게 되어, 자기 편에서 어떻게 하면 대위에게 너무 들이대는 기색 없이 적절한 격려를 보낼까 궁리하고 있었다. 그녀는 온갖 예법의 규칙을 엄격히 지키는 사람이었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 일에서 그녀는 손쉽게 성공했다. 대위가 늘 빈틈없이 살피고 있었던 까닭에, 눈짓 하나, 몸짓 하나, 말 한마디도 그를 비껴가지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n브리짓 양의 다정한 태도에서 대위가 얻는 만족은, 올워디 씨에 대한 그의 염려 탓에 적잖이 깎여 나갔다. 올워디 씨가 사심 없음을 입버릇처럼 내세우기는 했어도, 막상 행동에 나설 때가 되면 세상 사람들이 다 그러듯 누이에게 이익 면에서 그토록 불리한 혼사에는 동의하지 않으리라고 대위는 짐작했던 것이다. 그가 이런 생각을 어느 신탁에서 받아 왔는지는 독자의 판단에 맡기겠다. 그러나 어찌하여 그런 생각을 품게 되었든, 그것은 그를 묘하게도 곤혹스럽게 만들었다. 어떻게 처신해야 부인에게는 자기 애정을 전하는 동시에 그녀의 오라비에게는 그것을 감출 수 있을지 말이다. 그는 마침내 이렇게 마음먹었다. 사사로이 둘만의 기회가 닿을 때마다 구애를 펼치되, 올워디 씨 앞에서는 되도록 말을 아끼고 한껏 경계를 늦추지 않기로. 그리고 이런 처신을 그 형은 크게 흡족해했다.\n\n그는 곧 자기 연인에게 분명한 말로 구애를 펼칠 방도를 찾아냈고, 그녀에게서 격식에 맞는 대답을 받았다. 다시 말해 수천 년 전에 처음 행해진 이래로 어머니에게서 딸로 면면히 전해 내려온 바로 그 대답이었다. 이것을 라틴어로 옮긴다면, 나는 두 마디, 즉 놀로 에피스코파리(Nolo Episcopari)로 번역하겠다. 이 또한 또 다른 경우에 아득한 옛날부터 쓰여 온 문구다.\n\n대위는 어디서 그런 식견을 얻었든 간에 이 부인의 마음을 완벽하게 헤아렸고, 머지않아 전보다 더한 열기와 간절함으로 다시 청을 넣었다가, 또 한 번 마땅한 격식에 따라 거절당했다. 그러나 그의 욕망이 점점 더 간절해진 만큼, 부인 또한 그에 걸맞은 절도로 거절의 강도를 점점 누그러뜨렸다.\n\n이 구애의 장면 하나하나를 다 거치게 하여 독자를 지치게 하지는 않겠다. 어느 위대한 작가의 견해로는 그것이 그것을 펼치는 당사자에게는 인생에서 가장 즐거운 장면이라지만, 구경하는 이에게는 아마 그 무엇 못지않게 따분하고 진력나는 장면일 테니 말이다. 어쨌든 대위는 격식에 맞게 진격했고, 성채는 격식에 맞게 방어되었으며, 마침내 마땅한 격식에 따라 항복하고 말았다.\n\n거의 한 달에 이르는 이 기간 내내 대위는 형이 보는 앞에서는 부인에게 한껏 거리를 두는 태도를 유지했다. 사사로운 자리에서 그녀와 잘 풀려 갈수록, 사람들 앞에서는 그만큼 더 말을 아꼈다. 부인으로 말하자면, 연인을 손에 넣자마자 사람들 앞에서는 더없이 무관심한 태도로 그를 대했다. 그리하여 올워디 씨가 그 사이에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 털끝만큼이라도 의심을 품으려면, 악마의 통찰력(혹은 어쩌면 악마의 더 고약한 자질 몇 가지)이라도 지녀야 했을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter xii.\n\n싸움이든 결혼이든, 아니면 그 비슷한 어떤 일에 관한 것이든, 모든 흥정에서 양쪽 당사자가 진정으로 진지하기만 하다면 일을 매듭짓는 데에 미리 갖춰야 할 격식 따위는 거의 필요가 없다. 지금이 바로 그러한 경우였으니, 한 달도 채 지나기 전에 대위와 그의 여인은 부부가 되었다.\n\n이제 가장 큰 걱정거리는 이 일을 올워디 씨에게 어떻게 알리느냐 하는 것이었다. 그리고 그 일은 의사가 맡았다.\n\n그리하여 어느 날, 올워디가 자기 정원을 거닐고 있을 때, 의사가 그에게 다가와 더없이 근엄한 표정으로, 얼굴에 띄울 수 있는 온갖 근심을 끌어모아 이렇게 말했다. \"선생님, 저는 더할 나위 없이 중대한 일을 알려 드리려고 왔습니다. 그러나 생각만 해도 거의 정신이 나갈 지경인 이 일을 어떻게 입에 올려야 할지요!\" 그러고는 남자와 여자 모두를 향해 더없이 신랄한 비난을 쏟아붓기 시작했다. 남자는 제 이익에만 매달릴 뿐 다른 애착이라곤 없다고 몰아붙였고, 여자는 사악한 성향에 어찌나 빠져 있는지 도무지 다른 성(性)의 사람과 안심하고 맺어 줄 수가 없다고 했다. \"선생님,\" 그가 말했다, \"그토록 신중하고, 그토록 분별 있고, 그토록 학식 있는 부인이 그처럼 경솔한 정념에 자신을 내맡기리라고 제가 어찌 의심이나 했겠습니까! 아니면 제 동생이—아니, 어찌 제가 그를 그렇게 부르겠습니까? 그는 더 이상 제 동생이 아닙니다——\"\n\n\"아니, 그는 분명 자네 동생일세,\" 올워디가 말했다, \"그리고 내 동생이기도 하지.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 선생님!\" 의사가 말했다, \"이 충격적인 일을 알고 계신 겁니까?\"\n\n\"이보게, 블리필 씨,\" 선량한 신사가 대답했다, \"일어나는 모든 일을 가장 좋은 쪽으로 받아들이는 것이 내 평생 한결같은 신조였네. 내 누이는 나보다 여러 해 어리긴 하지만, 적어도 분별이 설 나이는 충분히 되었지. 만일 그가 아이를 속였다면 나도 더 용서하기 어려웠을 걸세. 그러나 서른이 넘은 여자라면 무엇이 자신을 가장 행복하게 해 줄지는 분명히 안다고 보아야 하지 않겠나. 그녀는 한 신사와 결혼했네. 비록 재산에서는 그녀와 어울리는 짝이 아닐지 모르나, 그 부족함을 메워 줄 만한 어떤 미덕이 그녀의 눈에 그에게 있다면, 그녀가 자신의 행복을 스스로 고른 것에 내가 반대할 이유를 나는 도무지 찾지 못하겠네. 나도 그녀와 마찬가지로, 행복이 오직 엄청난 재산에만 있다고 여기지는 않으니까. 거의 어떤 제안이든 따르겠다고 내가 누차 밝혀 온 터라, 이번 일에 대해서는 어쩌면 내게 상의가 있으리라 기대했을지도 모르지. 그러나 이런 일이란 매우 미묘한 성질의 것이고, 부끄러움이라는 망설임은 어쩌면 이겨 내기 어려운 법일세. 자네 동생으로 말하자면, 나는 그에게 정말이지 조금의 노여움도 없네. 그는 내게 빚진 것이 없으며, 또 내 동의를 구해야 할 어떤 필요도 없었다고 생각하네. 그 여인은, 내가 말했듯, 제 권리를 가진 사람(sui juris)이요, 자기 행실에 대해 오직 자기 자신에게만 책임지면 되는 적절한 나이이니 말일세.\"\n\n의사는 올워디 씨가 너무 관대하다고 나무라며, 자기 동생을 향한 비난을 거듭하고는, 다시는 그를 만나지도, 친척으로 인정하지도 않겠다고 선언했다. 그러고는 올워디의 선량함을 향한 찬사로 말문을 열어, 그의 우정에 대한 더없이 높은 칭송을 늘어놓더니, 끝으로 자기 동생이 그 우정 속에서 자신이 차지하고 있던 자리를 위태롭게 만든 일을 결코 용서하지 않겠다고 맺었다.\n\n이에 올워디가 이렇게 답했다. \"내가 자네 동생에게 어떤 불쾌함을 품었다 한들, 죄 없는 사람에게까지 그 원망을 옮기는 일은 결코 없었을 걸세. 하지만 분명히 말하건대, 나는 그런 불쾌함이 전혀 없네. 자네 동생은 내가 보기에 분별 있고 명예로운 사람일세. 나는 내 누이의 안목을 못마땅히 여기지 않으며, 그녀 또한 그의 마음을 똑같이 끄는 대상임을 의심하지 않겠네. 나는 늘 사랑이야말로 결혼 생활에서 행복의 유일한 토대라고 생각해 왔네. 사랑만이 이 결합의 접착제가 되어야 할 그 높고 다정한 우정을 길러 낼 수 있으니까. 그리고 내 생각에는, 다른 동기에서 맺어지는 모든 결혼은 큰 죄악일세. 그것은 더없이 거룩한 의식을 모독하는 일이며, 대개는 불화와 비참으로 끝나고 마네. 이 가장 신성한 제도를 정욕이나 탐욕에 바치는 사악한 제물로 바꾸는 것을 모독이라 부르지 않을 수 있겠나? 그리고 단지 아름다운 외모나 막대한 재산을 헤아려 사람을 결혼으로 끌어들이는 짝짓기를 두고, 그보다 나은 말을 무엇이라 할 수 있겠나?\n\n\"아름다움이 눈에 흐뭇한 대상이며, 심지어 어느 정도 찬탄을 받을 만하다는 것을 부정하는 일은 거짓되고 어리석은 짓일세. '아름답다'는 말은 성경에서도 자주 쓰이며, 언제나 명예로운 뜻으로 언급되지. 나 또한 세상이 미인이라 여긴 여인과 결혼하는 행운을 누렸고, 그 점 때문에 그녀를 더 좋아했다고 진실로 말할 수 있네. 그러나 이것을 결혼의 유일한 고려 사항으로 삼아, 그것을 위해 온갖 결함을 못 본 척할 만큼 격렬하게 갈망하거나, 단지 외모의 우아함이 모자란다는 이유만으로—본디 훨씬 더 높은 완전함을 지닌 자질인 신앙과 덕과 분별을 물리치고 멸시할 만큼 그것을 절대적으로 요구한다면, 이는 분명 지혜로운 사람에게도, 선량한 그리스도인에게도 어울리지 않는 일일세. 그리고 그런 사람들이 자기 결혼으로 육욕을 채우는 것 이상의 무엇을 뜻한다고 결론짓는 것은, 어쩌면 지나치게 너그러운 일일 걸세. 그것을 채우라고 결혼이 정해진 것은 아니라고 우리는 배웠으니 말일세.\n\n\"다음으로 재산에 관해서네. 세속의 분별은 어쩌면 이 점에 대해 어느 정도의 고려를 요구하지. 나도 그것을 완전히, 송두리째 비난하지는 않겠네. 세상이 이루어진 모양새가 그러하니, 결혼 생활의 요구와 후손을 돌보는 일은 우리가 형편이라 부르는 것에 어느 정도의 관심을 기울이게 만들지. 그러나 이 대비책은 실제로 필요한 정도를 훨씬 넘어, 어리석음과 허영 때문에 크게 부풀려지네. 그것들은 자연이 요구하는 것보다 훨씬 많은 욕구를 만들어 내니까. 아내를 위한 마차며 자식들을 위한 큰 재산이 관습에 따라 필수품의 목록에 올라 있고, 이것들을 마련하느라 진실로 단단하고 달콤하며, 덕스럽고 신앙심 깊은 모든 것이 소홀히 여겨지고 외면당하지.\n\n\"그리고 이것은 여러 단계로 나뉘는데, 그 마지막이자 가장 심한 단계는 광기와 거의 구별되지 않는 듯하네.—내가 말하는 것은, 막대한 재산을 가진 사람들이 자신에게 못마땅하고 또 못마땅할 수밖에 없는 자들에게—어리석은 자들과 악당들에게—이미 자기 즐거움의 요구를 넘어설 만큼 큰 재산을 더 불리려고 스스로를 묶어 버리는 경우일세. 분명 그런 사람들은, 미치광이로 여겨지기를 바라지 않는다면, 둘 중 하나는 인정해야 할 걸세. 그들이 가장 다정한 우정의 달콤함을 맛볼 줄 모르는 무능한 자들이라는 것, 아니면 자신이 누릴 수 있는 가장 큰 행복을, 어리석음에서 그 힘과 토대를 똑같이 빌려 온 저 헛되고 불확실하며 무의미한 세속 통념의 법칙에 제물로 바치고 있다는 것을 말이야.\"\n\n여기서 올워디는 자신의 설교를 마쳤고, 블리필은 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 그것을 들었다. 비록 이따금 근육이 슬며시 흐트러지려는 것을 막느라 적잖이 애를 먹기는 했지만 말이다. 그는 이제 자기가 들은 그 모든 대목을 젊은 성직자의 열성으로 칭송했다. 마치 주교가 설교단에 오른 바로 그날, 그분과 함께 식사하는 영광을 누린 젊은 성직자처럼.\n\n# Chapter xiii.\n\n제1권을 마무리하는 장. 부디 부자연스러워 보이기를 바라는, 배은망덕의 한 사례를 담아.\n\n지금까지 한 이야기로 미루어, 독자는 그 화해가(과연 그것을 화해라 부를 수 있다면) 형식에 불과한 것이었음을 짐작할 것이다. 그러니 우리는 그 대목을 건너뛰고, 분명 알맹이가 있는 일이라 여겨질 만한 쪽으로 서둘러 가보자.\n\n의사는 자기 형에게 올워디 씨와 자신 사이에 오간 일을 알려주고, 빙긋 웃으며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"장담하건대 내가 형 빚을 톡톡히 갚았소. 아니, 나는 그 선량한 분께 형을 용서하지 말아달라고 아예 못박아 부탁했단 말이오. 그분이 이미 형에게 호의를 표명하신 뒤였으니, 그런 성품의 분에게 그런 청쯤은 마음 놓고 던져볼 수 있었지. 게다가 나는 형을 위해서나 나 자신을 위해서나, 조금이라도 의심받을 여지를 막아두고 싶었던 거요.\"\n\n블리필 대위는 그때만큼은 이 말에 조금도 내색하지 않았다. 그러나 나중에 그는 이것을 아주 요긴하게 써먹었다.\n\n악마가 얼마 전 지상을 다녀가며 제 제자들에게 남긴 격언 중 하나는, 일단 올라서고 나면 발밑의 발판을 걷어차버리라는 것이다. 쉬운 우리말로 풀자면, 친구의 호의 덕에 출세를 이루었거든 가능한 한 빨리 그를 내쳐버리라는 충고다.\n\n대위가 이 격언대로 행동했는지는 단정해 말하지 않겠다. 다만 그의 행동이 이 악마적인 원리에서 비롯되었다고 봐도 무리가 없다는 것만큼은 자신 있게 말할 수 있다. 실로 그 행동에 다른 어떤 동기를 갖다 붙이기란 어려운 노릇이다. 그는 브리짓 양을 손에 넣고 올워디와도 화해하자마자, 곧바로 동생에게 냉랭한 기색을 드러내기 시작했고, 그 냉기는 날이 갈수록 심해지다가 마침내 무례함으로까지 번져 누구의 눈에도 훤히 보일 지경이 되었다.\n\n의사는 이 처사에 대해 사사로이 그에게 따져 물었으나, 다음과 같은 노골적인 선언 말고는 어떤 만족스러운 대답도 얻지 못했다. \"내 형님 집에서 마음에 안 드는 게 있거든, 언제든 떠나도 좋다는 걸 알아두게.\" 대위의 이 기이하고 잔인하며 거의 설명조차 되지 않는 배은망덕은 가엾은 의사의 가슴을 완전히 무너뜨렸다. 배은망덕이란, 우리가 그자를 위해 죄까지 저질러준 바로 그런 이들에게서 나올 때 인간의 가슴을 가장 사무치게 후벼파는 법이니까. 위대하고 선한 행동을 떠올리는 일은, 그 덕을 입은 자들이 그것을 어떻게 받아들이고 어떻게 갚든 간에 늘 우리에게 얼마간의 위안을 준다. 그러나 친구의 배은망덕이라는 이토록 쓰라린 재앙 아래에서, 동시에 우리의 상처 입은 양심마저 우리 얼굴에 달려들어 그토록 하잘것없는 자를 위해 양심에 얼룩을 묻혔다고 꾸짖는다면, 우리는 대체 어디서 위로를 받을 수 있겠는가!\n\n올워디 씨도 직접 대위에게 그 동생을 두둔하는 말을 건네며, 의사가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀느냐고 물었다. 그러자 그 매정한 악당은 비열하게도 이렇게 대답했다. 동생이 자기를 위한답시고 자기에게 해를 끼치려 했던 그 일만큼은 결코 용서할 수 없다고. 그것은 자기가 동생에게서 캐낸 일이며, 도저히 용서받아서는 안 될 잔인한 짓이었다는 것이다.\n\n올워디는 이 선언을 두고 아주 준엄한 말로 꾸짖으며, 그것은 인간된 자에게 어울리지 않는 태도라고 했다. 실로 그는 용서할 줄 모르는 성정에 대해 어찌나 큰 분노를 드러냈던지, 대위는 마침내 그의 논리에 설득당한 척하며 겉으로는 화해했노라고 공언했다.\n\n신부로 말하자면, 그녀는 이제 한창 신혼이라 새 남편에게 어찌나 열렬히 빠져 있던지, 남편이 잘못했다고는 결코 보이지 않았다. 그리고 남편이 누군가에게 품은 불쾌감은, 그녀 역시 그 사람을 싫어할 충분한 이유가 되었다.\n\n앞서 말했듯 대위는 올워디 씨의 권유로 겉으로는 동생과 화해했다. 그러나 마음속에는 여전히 똑같은 원한이 도사리고 있었고, 그는 그 원한을 동생에게 은밀히 내비칠 기회를 어찌나 자주 찾아냈던지, 가엾은 의사에게 그 집은 마침내 견딜 수 없는 곳이 되고 말았다. 그래서 그는 그토록 많은 것을 베풀어준 형에게서 이런 잔인하고 배은망덕한 모욕을 더 견디느니, 차라리 세상에 나가 부딪칠 온갖 불편을 감수하는 편을 택했다.\n\n그는 한때 올워디에게 그 전말을 모조리 알려줄까도 생각했다. 그러나 그렇게 고백하려면 죄의 큰 몫을 제 몫으로 떠안아야 하니, 도저히 그 일에 마음을 굽힐 수가 없었다. 게다가 형을 더 나쁜 사람으로 그려낼수록, 올워디 앞에서 자기 잘못도 그만큼 더 커 보일 테고, 또 그만큼 올워디의 분노도 더 커지리라고 짐작할 만한 이유가 있었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 떠난다는 핑계로 무슨 볼일이 있는 척 둘러대고는, 곧 다시 돌아오겠노라 약속했다. 그러고는 어찌나 흡족한 표정을 그럴듯하게 꾸며 형에게 작별을 고했던지, 대위 또한 자기 역할을 똑같이 완벽하게 해낸 터라, 올워디는 그 화해가 진심이라고 철석같이 믿게 되었다.\n\n의사는 곧장 런던으로 갔고, 그곳에서 얼마 안 가 상심병으로 세상을 떠났다. 이 병은 흔히 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 사람을 죽음으로 몰아가니, 한 가지 점에서만 다른 모든 병과 구별되지 않았다면 사망자 명부에 당당히 한 자리를 차지할 자격이 있을 것이다. 그 한 가지란, 어떤 의사도 이 병만은 고치지 못한다는 점이다.\n\n자, 이 두 형제의 지난 삶을 아무리 부지런히 캐어 보아도, 나는 위에서 말한 그 저주받고 지옥 같은 처세의 격언 말고도 대위의 행동을 설명할 또 다른 이유를 하나 더 찾아냈다. 앞서 그에 대해 말한 것 외에도, 대위는 자존심이 세고 사나운 사람이어서, 자기와는 기질이 다르고 그 두 가지 성품이 한참 모자랐던 동생을 늘 더없이 우월한 태도로 대해왔다. 그러나 학식으로 말하자면 의사가 훨씬 더 깊었고, 많은 이들이 그가 더 나은 분별을 지녔다고 평했다. 대위는 이 사실을 알고 있었고, 그것을 견딜 수 없어 했다. 시기심이란 그 자체로도 아주 악독한 감정이지만, 같은 대상을 향한 경멸이 거기에 뒤섞이면 그 쓴맛은 한층 더 짙어지는 법이다. 그리고 나는 적이 염려스럽다. 이 두 가지에 신세까지 더해질 때면, 그 셋이 함께 빚어내는 것은 감사가 아니라 분노이리라는 점이.\n\n# BOOK II.\n\n서로 다른 처지의 사람들에게서 펼쳐지는 결혼 생활의 여러 단계의 행복, 그리고 블리필 대위와 브리짓 올워디 양의 혼인 이후 첫 두 해 동안에 벌어진 갖가지 일들을 담은 권.\n\n이 이야기가 어떤 종류의 이야기인지, 무엇과 닮았고 무엇과는 닮지 않았는지를 보여 주는 장.\n\n우리는 이 작품을 한 사람의 일생도 아니고, 요즘 더 유행하는 일생에 대한 변명도 아닌, 마땅히 하나의 역사라고 이름 붙였다. 그렇지만 우리는 이 안에서, 어느 한 시대의 규칙적인 흐름을 지킨다는 명목으로, 인간이라는 무대 위에서 가장 위대한 장면들이 펼쳐진 저 주목할 만한 시대에 들이는 만큼의 종이를, 아무 일도 일어나지 않은 달과 해의 세세한 기록으로 채워야 한다고 여기는 그 고통스럽고 장황한 역사가를 본뜨기보다는, 차라리 여러 나라의 흥망을 밝히겠노라 공언하는 작가들의 방식을 따르려 한다.\n\n이런 종류의 역사책은 사실상, 그 안에 소식이 있든 없든 늘 똑같은 분량의 글자로 채워지는 신문과 무척 닮았다. 또한 이런 책은 텅 빈 채로든 가득 찬 채로든 한결같이 같은 노선을 달리는 역마차에 빗댈 수도 있다. 실로 그 작가는, 자신이 그 비서 노릇을 하는 시간과 똑같은 보폭을 유지해야 한다고 여기는 듯하다. 그래서 마치 제 주인을 본받듯, 세상이 잠든 것만 같았던 수도사들의 그 둔중한 세월을 지날 때나, 저 빼어난 라틴 시인이 그토록 고결하게 노래한 밝고 분주한 시대를 지날 때나 한결같이 더디게 흘러간다.\n사방에서 카르타고인들이 싸우러 몰려오니,\n\n온 세상이 전쟁의 떨리는 소란에 뒤흔들리고\n높은 하늘의 바람 아래 무섭게 진동하였도다.\n\n바다에서나 땅에서나 모든 인간이\n어느 편의 통치 아래 떨어질지 알 수 없었느니라.\n\n이 구절을 우리는 크리치 씨의 번역보다 더 어울리는 번역으로 독자들에게 전해 줄 수 있었으면 한다.\n무서운 카르타고가 무기로 로마를 위협하고,\n\n온 세상이 사나운 경보에 뒤흔들리던 때,\n아직 어느 편이 쓰러질지, 어느 나라가 일어나\n\n온 세상의 영광스러운 주인이 될지 정해지지 않았던 그때.\n자, 이제 이어지는 글에서 우리가 뜻하는 바는 그와는 정반대의 방식을 따르는 것이다. 어떤 특별한 장면이 나타날 때면(자주 그러하리라 믿는다), 우리는 그것을 독자에게 한껏 펼쳐 보이기 위해 수고도 종이도 아끼지 않을 것이다. 그러나 독자가 눈여겨볼 만한 일을 아무것도 빚어내지 못한 채 여러 해가 그냥 흘러간다면, 우리는 이야기에 빈틈이 생기는 것을 두려워하지 않을 것이다. 오히려 우리는 중요한 일들로 서둘러 나아가며, 그런 세월은 아예 거들떠보지도 않은 채 남겨 둘 것이다.\n\n실로 이런 시간은 거대한 시간의 복권에서 꽝으로 여겨야 마땅하다. 그러므로 그 복권의 기록자인 우리는, 길드홀에서 추첨되는 복권을 다루며 자기들이 팔아 치운 그 숱한 꽝으로는 결코 대중을 성가시게 하지 않는 저 약삭빠른 사람들을 본받으려 한다. 그러나 큰 상이 당첨되기라도 하면, 신문들은 곧장 그 소식으로 가득 차고, 세상은 어김없이 그것이 어느 가게에서 팔렸는지를 알게 된다. 실로 보통은 두세 군데의 서로 다른 가게가 저마다 그것을 팔았다는 영예를 주장하는데, 짐작건대 이로써 복권을 산 사람들은 어떤 중개인들이 운명의 비밀, 아니 운명의 내각 회의에까지 통해 있다고 믿게 되는 것이다.\n그러니 이 작품을 읽어 나가다가, 어떤 장은 무척 짧고 어떤 장은 그만큼 길며, 어떤 장은 단 하루의 시간만 담고 있는가 하면 어떤 장은 여러 해를 아우르고 있다 하더라도, 한마디로 내 이야기가 때로는 멈춰 선 듯하고 때로는 날아가는 듯하더라도, 독자는 놀라서는 안 된다. 이 모든 것에 대해 나는 그 어떤 비평의 법정에도 책임질 까닭이 없다고 여긴다. 나는 실로 글쓰기의 새로운 영토를 일군 창건자이니, 그 안에서 마음껏 법을 만들 자유가 있기 때문이다. 그리고 나의 백성으로 여기는 내 독자들은, 이 법들을 믿고 또 따라야 할 의무가 있다. 다만 그들이 기꺼이, 그리고 흔쾌히 그 법을 따를 수 있도록, 나는 이로써 그들에게 약속하노니, 그러한 모든 제도에서 나는 무엇보다 그들의 편안함과 이익을 먼저 헤아릴 것이다. 나는 결코 왕권신수설을 내세우는 폭군처럼 그들을 내 노예나 내 소유물로 여기지 않기 때문이다. 실로 나는 오직 그들 자신의 이로움을 위해 그들 위에 세워졌으며, 그들을 위해 지어졌을 뿐 그들이 나를 위해 지어진 것이 아니다. 또한 나는 그들의 이익을 내 글쓰기의 가장 큰 준칙으로 삼는 한, 그들이 한마음으로 나의 위엄을 떠받치고, 내가 마땅히 누리거나 바라는 모든 영예를 내게 돌려주리라는 것을 의심치 않는다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n서출 자식에게 지나친 호의를 베푸는 것에 대한 종교적 경계, 그리고 데버러 윌킨스 부인이 이룬 위대한 발견.\n\n블리필 대위와 브리짓 올워디 양의 혼례를 치른 지 여덟 달 만에, 빼어난 미모와 덕성과 재산을 두루 갖춘 이 처녀, 곧 브리짓 양은 어떤 놀란 일을 겪은 탓에 잘생긴 사내아이를 낳았다. 그 아이는 겉보기에는 흠 하나 없이 멀쩡했으나, 산파는 그 아이가 달을 한 달 채우기 전에 태어났음을 알아차렸다.\n\n사랑하는 누이가 상속자를 낳은 것은 올워디 씨에게 더없이 큰 기쁨이었으나, 그렇다고 그 작은 업둥이를 향한 그의 애정이 식은 것은 아니었다. 그는 그 아이의 대부였고, 자기 이름인 토머스를 그 아이에게 지어 주었으며, 지금껏 하루에 적어도 한 번은 거의 빠짐없이 그 아이의 방을 찾아가 돌보아 왔다.\n\n그는 누이에게, 원한다면 갓 태어난 이 아기를 어린 토미와 함께 길러도 좋겠다고 말했고, 누이는 약간 마지못한 기색이긴 했으나 이에 동의했다. 누이는 실로 오라비를 무척이나 흡족하게 여겼으며, 그런 까닭에 그동안 그 업둥이를 대할 때도, 엄격한 덕을 지닌 부인들이 이런 아이들—아무리 죄가 없다 한들 방종이 남긴 살아 있는 기념물이라 부를 만한 아이들—에게 좀처럼 보이기 어려운 친절함을 늘 베풀어 왔던 것이다.\n\n대위는 올워디 씨의 허물이라 단죄하는 그 일을 그리 쉽게 견딜 수가 없었다. 그는 죄의 열매를 거두어 기르는 것은 곧 죄를 두둔하는 것이라는 뜻을 자주 넌지시 비쳤다. 그는 여러 성경 구절을 인용했는데(그는 성경에 밝았다), 이를테면 \"그분은 아비의 죄를 그 자식에게 갚으시며, 아비가 신 포도를 먹었으니 그 자식의 이가 시리다\" 같은 구절들이었다. 이로부터 그는 어버이의 죄를 서출에게 벌하는 것이 정당하다고 주장했다. 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"법이 그런 천한 출생의 아이들을 죽이는 것을 명시적으로 허락하지는 않으나, 그래도 그들을 누구의 자식도 아닌 자로 본다. 교회 또한 그들을 누구의 자식도 아닌 자로 여긴다. 그리고 잘해야 그들은 나라에서 가장 낮고 천한 일에나 길러져야 마땅하다.\"\n\n이 모든 말, 그리고 대위가 이 문제를 두고 들이댄 그 밖의 숱한 말에 대해 올워디 씨는 이렇게 답했다. \"어버이가 아무리 죄가 있다 한들 그 자식들은 분명 죄가 없습니다. 그리고 그대가 인용한 구절들로 말하자면, 앞엣것은 유대인들이 우상을 섬긴 죄, 곧 하늘에 계신 자신들의 왕을 저버리고 미워한 죄에 대한 특정한 단죄였습니다. 뒤엣것은 비유로 말한 것이어서, 죄에 대한 어떤 명시적인 심판이라기보다는 죄가 빚는 확실하고도 필연적인 결과를 나타내려는 뜻이었습니다. 그러나 전능하신 분께서 죄 있는 자의 죄를 죄 없는 자에게 갚아 보복하시는 분으로 그리는 것은, 신성을 모독하는 짓까지는 아니더라도 온당치 못한 일입니다. 그것은 그분이 자연적 정의의 제일 원리에 어긋나게, 또 그분 자신이 우리 마음속에 심어 두신 옳고 그름에 대한 본래의 관념에 어긋나게 행하시는 것으로 그리는 셈이기 때문입니다. 우리는 바로 그 관념으로써, 계시되지 않은 모든 일을 판단할 뿐 아니라 계시 그 자체의 참됨까지도 판단하는 것입니다.\" 그는 또 이 문제에서 대위와 같은 원칙을 지닌 이가 많다는 것을 안다고 했다. 그러나 자신은 그와 정반대로 굳게 확신하며, 만일 적법한 자식이 운 좋게 같은 자리에서 발견되었더라도 했을 것과 똑같은 방식으로 이 가엾은 아기를 보살피겠노라고 했다.\n\n대위가 틈만 나면 이러한 논리, 그리고 이와 비슷한 논리를 내세워 그 작은 업둥이를 올워디 씨에게서 떼어 놓으려 애쓰는 동안—그는 올워디 씨가 그 아이에게 쏟는 정에 슬슬 시샘이 일기 시작했던 것이다—데버러 부인은 한 가지 발견을 했고, 그 결과는 적어도 대위의 온갖 논변보다 가엾은 토미에게 더 치명적인 것으로 드러날 조짐을 보였다.\n\n이 선량한 부인의 채울 길 없는 호기심이 그 일에 그녀를 떠밀었는지, 아니면 블리필 부인의 환심을 더 굳히려고 그리한 것인지—블리필 부인은 업둥이를 향한 겉치레 행동과는 달리, 사사로이는 그 아기를 자주 욕했고, 그 아이에게 정을 쏟는 오라비마저 욕하곤 했다—나는 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 이제, 스스로 생각하기에, 그 업둥이의 아비를 완전히 찾아낸 셈이었다.\n\n자, 이는 자못 중대한 발견이었으니, 그 일을 근원에서부터 더듬어 보는 것이 필요하겠다. 그러므로 우리는 그 발견을 낳은 앞선 사정들을 아주 세세하게 펼쳐 보일 것이다. 그러기 위해 우리는 지금 독자가 전혀 알지 못하는 어느 작은 집안의 온갖 비밀을 낱낱이 드러내야만 할 텐데, 그 집안의 살림살이가 어찌나 진귀하고 별난지, 결혼한 많은 이들의 더없는 곧이곧대로 믿는 마음마저 충격에 빠뜨리지 않을까 염려스럽다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n아리스토텔레스의 가르침과는 정반대되는 규칙 위에 세워진 한 가정 통치를 묘사함.\n\n독자께서는 제니 존스가 어느 학교 선생과 몇 해를 함께 지냈으며, 그 선생이 그녀의 간곡한 청에 따라 라틴어를 가르쳐 주었다는 이야기를 앞서 들으셨음을 기억해 주시기 바란다. 그녀의 재능을 공정히 말하자면, 그녀는 그 라틴어 실력을 어찌나 키웠던지 스승보다 더 나은 학자가 되어 있었다.\n\n사실, 이 가엾은 사내는 학식이 반드시 필요하다고 여겨지는 직업을 택하긴 했으나, 학식이란 그가 내세울 수 있는 장점 중 가장 사소한 것이었다. 그는 세상에서 가장 마음씨 좋은 사람 가운데 하나였고, 동시에 익살과 해학에 어찌나 능했던지 그 고장의 재담꾼으로 이름이 나 있었다. 그래서 이웃의 신사들이 모두 그와 함께 어울리기를 바랐는데, 거절이란 그의 재주가 아니었던 까닭에, 그는 자기 학교에서 보냈더라면 더 많은 벌이가 되었을 시간을 그들의 집에서 흘려보냈다.\n\n이만한 자질을 갖추고 또 이만한 성품을 지닌 신사라면, 이튼이나 웨스트민스터 같은 명문 학당에 위협이 될 염려는 조금도 없었으리라 짐작할 만하다. 솔직히 말하자면, 그의 학생들은 두 부류로 나뉘어 있었다. 윗반에는 이웃 지주의 아들인 한 젊은 신사가 있었는데, 그는 열일곱 나이에 이제 막 구문론에 들어선 참이었다. 그리고 아랫반에는 같은 신사의 둘째 아들이 있었는데, 이 아이는 마을 소년 일곱 명과 함께 읽기와 쓰기를 배우고 있었다.\n\n여기서 나오는 보수만으로는 이 학교 선생이 삶의 호사를 누리기 어려웠을 것이다. 다만 그가 이 직책에 교회 서기와 이발사 노릇을 보탰고, 또 올워디 씨가 이 모든 것에 더해 해마다 십 파운드의 연금을 주었기에 사정이 달랐다. 가엾은 사내는 그 돈을 매년 성탄절마다 받았고, 그 덕분에 그 거룩한 명절 동안 마음을 흐뭇하게 다독일 수 있었다.\n\n이 훈장에게는 여러 보물 가운데 아내가 하나 있었다. 그는 그녀의 재산, 곧 그녀가 그곳에서 모은 이십 파운드를 보고 올워디 씨의 주방에서 그녀를 데려와 혼인했던 것이다.\n\n이 여인은 생김새가 그리 곱지는 않았다. 그녀가 내 벗 호가스의 그림 모델로 앉았는지 아닌지는 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 〈매춘부의 행로〉 세 번째 그림에서 여주인의 차를 따르고 있는 젊은 여자와 똑 닮아 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 옛적 크산티페가 세운 그 고귀한 종파의 공공연한 추종자였다. 그 덕분에 그녀는 학교에서 남편보다 더 무서운 존재가 되었는데, 사실대로 고백하자면 남편은 그곳에서든 다른 어디에서든 그녀가 곁에 있는 한 결코 주인 노릇을 한 적이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴이 천성적으로 다정한 기질을 그리 드러내 보이지는 않았지만, 그 다정함마저도 부부의 행복을 흔히 망쳐 놓는 어떤 사정 탓에 다소 시어졌는지도 모른다. 아이들은 응당 사랑의 증표라 불리는데, 그녀의 남편은 결혼한 지 아홉 해가 되도록 그녀에게 그런 증표를 하나도 주지 못했던 것이다. 이 결함에 대해 그는 나이로도 건강으로도 변명할 거리가 없었으니, 아직 서른도 되지 않은 데다 사람들이 말하는 이른바 혈기 왕성하고 활달한 젊은이였기 때문이다.\n\n여기서 또 다른 화근이 생겨났는데, 이는 가엾은 훈장에게 적잖은 고통을 안겨 주었다. 그녀가 그를 어찌나 한결같이 질투하던지, 그는 교구 안의 어떤 여자에게도 감히 말을 건네지 못할 지경이었다. 어떤 여자에게든 조금이라도 예의를 차리거나, 심지어 편지 한 통이라도 주고받는 일이 있으면, 그것은 영락없이 아내를 그 여자에게, 그리고 자기 자신에게 달려들게 만들었기 때문이다.\n\n제 집 안에서 부부 사이의 해를 막고자, 그녀는 하녀를 하나만 두면서도 늘 신경 써서, 그 얼굴이 곧 정조의 일종의 담보로 통하는 그런 부류의 여자들 중에서 골랐다. 그리고 앞서 독자께서 들으신 대로, 제니 존스도 바로 그런 부류에 속하는 여자였다.\n\n이 젊은 여자의 얼굴은 앞서 말한 종류의 담보로는 꽤 든든하다 할 만했고, 또 그녀의 행실은 늘 더없이 정숙했으니—이는 여자에게 있어 분별의 확실한 결과다—그래서 그녀는 파트리지 씨(이것이 그 학교 선생의 이름이다)의 집에서 네 해 넘게 지내는 동안 안주인에게 추호의 의심도 사지 않았다. 아니, 오히려 그녀는 남다른 친절을 받았고, 안주인은 파트리지 씨가 앞서 이야기한 그 가르침을 그녀에게 베푸는 것까지 허락했던 것이다.\n\n그러나 질투란 통풍과도 같다. 그런 병이 핏속에 들어 있으면 언제 터져 나올지 결코 안심할 수 없으며, 더구나 그것은 흔히 가장 사소한 계기에, 가장 의심하지 않을 때 터져 나온다.\n\n파트리지 부인에게도 바로 이런 일이 일어났다. 그녀는 네 해 동안 남편이 이 젊은 여자를 가르치는 일을 묵묵히 받아들였고, 그녀가 공부를 좇느라 제 일을 게을리하는 것도 여러 번 눈감아 주었다. 그런데 어느 날 그 곁을 지나가던 차에, 그 처녀가 글을 읽고 있고 선생이 그녀 위로 몸을 기울이고 있는데, 무슨 까닭에서인지 처녀가 별안간 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다. 안주인의 머릿속에 의심이 처음으로 깃든 것은 바로 이때였다. 그러나 그 의심은 그때 곧장 모습을 드러내지는 않고, 마치 숨어 있는 적이 더 큰 힘의 증원을 기다렸다가 그제야 공공연히 정체를 드러내고 적대 행위에 나서듯, 그녀의 마음속에 도사리고 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 의심을 단단히 굳혀 줄 그 증원군이 머지않아 당도했다. 얼마 지나지 않은 어느 날, 부부가 함께 식사를 하던 중에 선생이 하녀에게 \"다 미히 알리퀴드 포툼\"이라고 말했던 것이다. 그러자 가엾은 처녀가 빙긋 웃었는데, 어쩌면 그 라틴어가 형편없어서였을 것이다. 그리고 안주인이 자기에게 눈길을 던지자, 처녀는 얼굴을 붉혔으니, 아마도 주인을 비웃었다는 자각 때문이었으리라. 이에 파트리지 부인은 그 즉시 격분하여, 자기가 먹고 있던 나무 접시를 가엾은 제니의 머리를 향해 내던지며 소리쳤다. \"이 뻔뻔한 화냥년아, 내 눈앞에서 내 남편이랑 수작을 부려?\" 그러고는 같은 순간에 칼을 손에 들고 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰는데, 이는 십중팔구 비극적인 복수를 실행에 옮길 작정이었을 것이다. 다만 처녀가 안주인보다 문에 더 가까이 있는 이점을 살려 달아남으로써 그 분노를 피한 덕분이었다. 가엾은 남편으로 말하자면, 놀라움에 몸이 굳었는지, 아니면 (이쪽이 훨씬 더 그럴듯한데) 두려움에 어떤 맞섬도 감히 시도하지 못했는지, 그저 의자에 앉은 채 눈만 휘둥그레 뜨고 벌벌 떨고 있었다. 그는 한 번도 움직이려 하거나 입을 떼려 하지 않았으니, 마침내 아내가 제니를 쫓다가 돌아와 그가 제 한 몸을 지키기 위해 방어 조치를 취하지 않을 수 없게 만들 때까지 그러했다. 그리하여 그 역시 하녀의 본을 따라 물러나야만 했다.\n이 선량한 여인은 오셀로와 마찬가지로,\n\n질투로 한평생을 살아가며\n달이 바뀔 때마다 한결같이\n\n새로운 의심을 좇는—\n그런 기질의 사람은 아니었다. 오셀로가 그러했듯 그녀에게도,\n\n—한번 의심한다는 것은\n\n곧 단번에 마음을 정하는 것이었으니—\n\n그래서 그녀는 제니에게 당장 제 짐을 죄다 꾸려서 떠나라고 명했다. 그날 밤 그 처녀가 결단코 자기 집 담장 안에서 잠들게 두지 않으리라 마음먹은 것이다.\n\n파트리지 씨는 경험으로 너무 많은 것을 배운 터라 이런 일에 끼어들지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 늘 쓰던 처방인 인내에 의지했으니, 그가 라틴어의 대가는 아니었지만 다음 말에 담긴 충고만큼은 기억하고 또 잘 헤아리고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n—레베 피트 쿼드 베네 페르투르 오누스\n\n영어로 하면 이렇다.\n\n짐이란 잘 짊어질 때 가장 가벼워진다—\n\n그는 이 말을 늘 입에 달고 살았으며, 사실대로 말하면 그 말의 참뜻을 몸소 겪을 일이 자주 있었다.\n\n제니는 자기 결백을 항변하려 했으나, 폭풍이 너무 거세어 그녀의 말은 들리지조차 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 짐을 꾸리는 일에 매달렸는데, 거기에는 갈색 종이 한 줌이면 충분했다. 그러고 나서 쥐꼬리만 한 품삯을 받아 들고 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n학교 선생과 그의 배우자는 그날 저녁을 자못 언짢게 보냈다. 그러나 다음 날 아침이 되기 전에 무슨 일인가가 일어나 파트리지 부인의 분노를 조금 누그러뜨렸고, 마침내 그녀는 남편이 변명할 기회를 허락했다. 그녀가 그 말을 한결 선뜻 믿어 준 것은, 남편이 제니를 다시 불러들이자고 청하기는커녕 오히려 그녀가 쫓겨난 것이 흡족하다고 공언했기 때문이다. 그의 말로는, 제니는 하녀로서 별 쓸모가 없어졌으니 온종일 책만 읽고, 게다가 몹시 건방지고 고집스러워졌다는 것이었다. 실은 근래 들어 그녀와 그 스승은 학문을 두고 자주 다투었는데, 앞서 말했듯이 그 다툼에서 그녀는 그를 크게 앞지른 처지가 되어 있었다. 그러나 그는 이 점을 결코 인정하려 들지 않았고, 그녀가 옳은 편을 고집하는 것을 두고 고집불통이라 부르며, 적잖은 앙심을 품고 그녀를 미워하기 시작했다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n앞 장에서 말한 여러 이유들, 그리고 대부분의 남편들에게는 잘 알려져 있되 프리메이슨의 비밀처럼 그 명예로운 결사의 회원이 아닌 이에게는 결코 누설되어서는 안 될 다른 몇 가지 부부 사이의 양보들 덕분에, 파트리지 부인은 자신이 까닭 없이 남편을 단죄했음을 어지간히 깨닫게 되었고, 잘못된 의심을 보상하고자 다정한 행동으로 그에게 갚으려 애썼다. 사실 그녀의 격정은 어느 쪽으로 기울든 똑같이 사나웠다. 더없이 화를 낼 수 있는 만큼이나, 또한 더없이 살갑게 굴 수도 있었으니 말이다.\n\n그러나 이 두 격정이 보통은 번갈아 찾아들어, 이 학자 양반이 어느 정도는 그 둘 모두의 대상이 되지 않고 지나가는 날이 거의 하루도 없었음에도, 특별한 경우, 곧 분노의 격정이 몹시 거세게 들끓고 난 뒤에는 그 가라앉는 기간이 대개 더 길었다. 이번이 바로 그러했다. 이 질투의 발작이 끝난 뒤, 그녀는 남편이 일찍이 겪어 본 그 어느 때보다도 오래도록 다정한 상태에 머물렀다. 그래서 크산티페의 모든 추종자들이 날마다 행하지 않을 수 없는 그 자잘한 운동들만 아니었던들, 파트리지 씨는 여러 달에 걸친 더없는 평온을 누렸을 것이다.\n\n바다에서의 완벽한 잔잔함을, 노련한 뱃사람은 언제나 폭풍의 전조로 의심하게 마련이다. 또 나는 평소 미신을 떠받드는 부류가 아니면서도, 크고 별난 평화나 고요가 그 반대의 것을 동반하리라 짐작하는 사람들을 몇 알고 있다. 바로 그 까닭에, 옛사람들은 그런 경우에 네메시스 여신에게 제물을 바치곤 했다. 그들은 이 여신이 인간의 행복을 시샘하는 눈으로 바라보며, 그것을 뒤엎는 데 유난한 기쁨을 느낀다고 여겼다.\n\n우리는 그런 이교의 여신을 믿는다거나 어떤 미신을 부추기는 것과는 거리가 멀기에, 부디 존 프 씨, 아니면 그런 부류의 다른 어떤 철학자가 조금이나마 분발하여, 그토록 자주 목격되어 온, 또 우리가 이제부터 한 사례를 들어 보이려 하는, 좋은 운에서 나쁜 운으로의 이 갑작스러운 전환의 진짜 원인을 밝혀내 주기를 바랄 따름이다. 사실을 이야기하는 것이 우리의 소관이요, 원인은 우리보다 훨씬 뛰어난 천재들에게 맡겨 두려 하니 말이다.\n\n인류는 언제나 남들의 행실을 알고 그것을 두고 떠드는 데서 큰 즐거움을 누려 왔다. 그리하여 어느 시대 어느 나라에나, 호기심 많은 이들이 모여 서로의 궁금증을 채울 수 있는, 공공의 만남을 위해 따로 마련된 특정한 장소들이 있어 왔다. 그중에서도 이발소가 단연 으뜸의 자리를 차지해 온 것은 마땅한 일이다. 그리스인들 사이에서 '이발소 소식'은 속담처럼 쓰이는 표현이었고, 호라티우스도 그의 한 서간에서 로마의 이발사들을 같은 맥락으로 명예롭게 언급한 바 있다.\n\n잉글랜드의 이발소들이 그 그리스나 로마의 선배들에 결코 뒤지지 않음은 익히 알려진 사실이다. 거기서 여러분은 커피하우스에서 다뤄지는 것에 거의 뒤지지 않는 솜씨로 외국 정세가 논의되는 광경을 볼 수 있고, 집안 안팎의 사건들은 후자보다 전자에서 훨씬 폭넓고 거리낌 없이 다뤄진다. 그러나 이는 어디까지나 남자들에게만 해당하는 이야기다. 그런데 이 나라의 여인들, 특히 하층 신분의 여인들은 다른 나라 여인들보다 훨씬 자주 어울려 모이는 만큼, 만약 그녀들의 호기심을 마음껏 채울 장소가 따로 마련되어 있지 않다면 우리의 사회 제도는 크게 모자란 것이 되리라. 이 점에서 그녀들은 인류의 나머지 절반에 조금도 뒤지지 않으니 말이다.\n\n그러므로 그러한 만남의 장소를 누린다는 점에서, 영국의 미인들은 그 어느 외국 자매들보다도 스스로를 더 행복하다 여겨야 마땅하다. 나로서는 역사책에서 읽은 적도, 여행 중에 본 적도, 그와 비슷한 무엇을 기억하지 못하니 말이다.\n\n그렇다면 그 장소란 다름 아닌 잡화상, 곧 온갖 소식이 모여드는 익히 알려진 자리요, 잉글랜드의 모든 교구에서 흔히 '수다 떨기'라 불리는 바로 그곳이다.\n\n어느 날 파트리지 부인이 이 여인들의 모임에 와 있는데, 이웃 가운데 한 사람이 요즘 제니 존스의 소식을 들은 것이 없느냐고 물었다. 부인은 없다고 답했다. 그러자 상대는 미소를 띠며, 그렇게 제니를 내쫓아 준 덕에 교구가 부인께 크게 신세를 졌노라고 말했다.\n\n독자도 잘 알다시피 질투가 이미 오래전에 나은 데다, 하녀와 달리 다툴 거리가 없던 파트리지 부인은 거리낌 없이 대답했다. 그 일로 교구가 자기한테 무슨 신세를 졌다는 건지 모르겠노라고, 자기가 보기엔 제니가 자기만 한 사람을 뒤에 남겨 두고 가지도 못했을 거라고 말이다.\n\n\"아무렴, 그렇고말고요.\" 수다쟁이가 말했다. \"그러지 않았기를 바라죠. 하긴 우리 동네에도 헤픈 계집이야 차고 넘치지만요. 그럼 그 소식도 못 들으셨나 보군요? 그 애가 사생아 둘을 낳고 산후조리 중이라는 거 말이에요. 하지만 여기서 난 게 아니니, 우리 남편이랑 다른 빈민감독관 말로는 우리가 걔들을 떠맡을 의무는 없다더라고요.\"\n\n\"사생아 둘이라니!\" 파트리지 부인이 황급히 받았다. \"놀랍군요! 우리가 그 애들을 떠맡아야 하는지 어떤지는 모르겠지만, 분명 여기서 잉태된 게 틀림없어요. 그 계집이 떠난 지 아홉 달도 채 안 됐으니까요.\"\n\n마음의 작용만큼 빠르고 돌연한 것은 없다. 더구나 희망이나 두려움, 또는 그 두 가지를 한낱 졸개로 부리는 질투가 마음을 부려 댈 때면 더욱 그러하다. 제니가 함께 사는 동안 제집 밖을 거의 나간 적이 없었다는 사실이 그녀에게 단번에 떠올랐다. 의자에 기대 몸을 숙이던 일, 화들짝 일어서던 일, 그 라틴어, 그 미소, 그 밖의 숱한 일들이 한꺼번에 그녀에게 밀려들었다. 제니가 떠날 때 남편이 내비친 만족스러움이 이제는 한낱 꾸민 것으로만 보였다가, 같은 순간에 다시 진심으로 보였다가, 그러나 그것이 물림과 그 밖의 백 가지 못된 까닭에서 비롯된 것이라며 도리어 질투를 굳혀 주었다. 한마디로, 그녀는 남편의 죄를 확신했고, 곧장 혼란에 빠진 채 모임을 떠났다.\n\n아름다운 그리말킨은 고양잇과 중 가장 막내이면서도 그 집안 손위 가지들에 못지않은 사나움을 지녔고, 힘은 뒤질지언정 그 흉포함만큼은 고귀한 호랑이 그 자체에 견줄 만한지라, 오래도록 장난삼아 괴롭혀 온 어린 생쥐가 잠시 그 발톱에서 빠져나가면 안달하고 호통치고 으르렁대고 욕설을 퍼붓다가도, 생쥐가 숨었던 궤짝이나 상자가 다시 치워지면 번개처럼 먹잇감에게 달려들어 독기 어린 분노로 그 작은 짐승을 물어뜯고 할퀴고 우물거리고 갈가리 찢는다.\n\n그에 못지않은 사나움으로 파트리지 부인은 가엾은 학자 양반에게 달려들었다. 혀와 이와 손이 한꺼번에 그에게 쏟아졌다. 가발은 순식간에 머리에서 뜯겨 나갔고, 셔츠는 등에서 찢겨 나갔으며, 얼굴에서는 다섯 줄기 피가 흘러내려, 자연이 불행하게도 이 적에게 쥐여 준 발톱의 수를 일러 주었다.\n\n파트리지 씨는 한동안 방어만 했다. 사실 그는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리려 애쓸 뿐이었다. 그러나 적수가 분노를 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않는 것을 보고는, 적어도 무장을 풀어 놓거나, 아니 차라리 그 두 팔을 붙들어 두려고는 해 봐야겠다 싶었다. 그러는 사이 몸싸움 끝에 그녀의 모자가 벗겨졌고, 어깨에 닿기에는 너무 짧은 머리카락이 머리 위로 곤두섰다. 또한 밑단의 구멍 하나로만 끈을 꿴 그녀의 코르셋이 터져 벌어지면서, 머리카락보다 훨씬 풍성한 가슴이 허리 아래까지 늘어졌다. 얼굴에는 남편의 피가 묻어 있었고, 이는 분노로 뿌득뿌득 갈렸으며, 대장간 화덕에서 튀는 듯한 불꽃이 두 눈에서 쏟아졌다. 그러니 이 모든 것을 합쳐 놓고 보면, 이 아마존 여전사는 파트리지 씨보다 훨씬 대담한 남자에게조차 공포의 대상이 되고도 남았을 것이다.\n\n마침내 그는 운 좋게도 그녀의 두 팔을 붙잡아, 손끝에 달린 그 무기들을 무용지물로 만들었다. 그러자 그녀는 그것을 알아채기가 무섭게, 여인 특유의 여린 천성이 분노를 누르고 이내 눈물로 녹아내리더니, 곧 그 끝에 발작으로 이어졌다.\n\n이 분노의 한바탕 소동 내내 그 까닭조차 알지 못한 채 파트리지 씨가 그나마 지켜 온 그 얼마 안 되는 분별마저, 이제는 그를 완전히 저버렸다. 그는 곧장 거리로 뛰쳐나가, 아내가 죽음의 고통 속에 있다고 고래고래 외치며, 이웃들에게 한시바삐 달려와 그녀를 도와 달라고 애원했다. 몇몇 마음씨 좋은 아낙들이 그 부름에 응해 그의 집으로 들어와, 이런 경우에 흔히 쓰는 처방을 베푸니, 마침내 파트리지 부인은 남편의 크나큰 기쁨 속에 정신을 차렸다.\n\n기력을 조금 추스르고 강심제로 어느 정도 마음을 가라앉히자, 그녀는 남편에게서 받은 갖가지 해코지를 좌중에게 일러바치기 시작했다. 그녀의 말인즉, 남편은 잠자리에서 자기를 욕보이는 것으로 모자라, 그 일을 두고 따져 묻자 상상할 수 있는 가장 잔인한 방식으로 자기를 다루었으며, 모자와 머리카락을 머리에서 쥐어뜯고 몸에서 코르셋을 잡아 뜯었을 뿐 아니라, 그러는 와중에 여러 차례 매질까지 해 그 자국을 무덤에까지 지고 가게 만들었다는 것이었다.\n\n얼굴에 아내의 분노가 남긴 자국을 그보다 훨씬 또렷이 달고 있던 이 가엾은 남자는, 이 고발 앞에 말없이 아연실색하여 서 있었다. 독자도 그를 위해 증언해 주리라 믿거니와, 그 고발은 진실을 한참 넘어선 것이었다. 실로 그는 그녀를 단 한 차례도 때린 적이 없었으니 말이다. 그런데 이 침묵이 온 법정에 의해 혐의를 인정하는 것으로 풀이되니, 그녀들은 일제히 한목소리로 그를 나무라고 욕하기 시작하여, 여자에게 손찌검하는 자는 비겁한 놈밖에 없다고 거듭거듭 되뇌었다.\n\n파트리지 씨는 이 모든 것을 꾹 참고 견뎠다. 그러나 아내가 자기 얼굴의 피를 그의 야만성의 증거랍시고 들이대자, 그는 그것이 제 피임을 주장하지 않을 수 없었다. 실제로도 그것은 그의 피였으니, 살해당한 자의 피가 흔히 그러하다고들 배워 온 것처럼 이 피가 자기에게 복수하려 들고일어나는 것은 참으로 가당찮다고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n이에 아낙들은 다른 대꾸 없이, 그 피가 얼굴이 아니라 그의 심장에서 나왔더라면 좋았을 것을 안타깝다고만 했다. 그러면서 하나같이, 만약 자기 남편이 자기들에게 손을 쳐든다면 그놈 몸에서 심장의 피를 뽑아내고 말겠다고 큰소리쳤다.\n\n지난 일에 대한 숱한 훈계와, 앞으로의 처신에 대해 파트리지 씨에게 건네는 숱한 좋은 충고가 오간 끝에, 마침내 좌중은 흩어지고 부부만 남아 둘만의 담판을 벌이게 되었다. 그 자리에서 파트리지 씨는 곧 자신이 겪은 그 모든 고초의 원인을 알게 되었다.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n독자의 판단과 성찰을 일깨우는 많은 내용을 담은 장.\n\n비밀이 단 한 사람에게만 새어 나가는 일은 드물다는 말은 참된 관찰이라고 나는 믿는다. 그러나 이런 종류의 사실이 교구 하나 전체에 알려지고도 그 이상으로 퍼지지 않는다면, 그것이야말로 기적에 버금가는 일이 분명할 것이다.\n\n실제로 며칠 지나지 않아, 흔한 표현을 빌리자면, 리틀 배딩턴의 학교 선생 이야기로 온 동네가 떠들썩해졌다. 그가 아내를 더없이 잔인하게 때렸다는 것이었다. 아니, 어떤 곳에서는 그가 아내를 죽였다는 소문이 돌았고, 다른 곳에서는 팔을 부러뜨렸다고, 또 다른 곳에서는 다리를 부러뜨렸다고들 했다. 한마디로, 인간이라는 피조물에게 가해질 수 있는 상해 가운데 파트리지 부인이 남편에게서 당했다고 어딘가에서 단언되지 않은 것은 거의 없을 지경이었다.\n\n이 다툼의 원인 또한 가지각색으로 전해졌다. 어떤 이들은 파트리지 부인이 남편이 하녀와 한 침대에 있는 것을 붙잡았다고 했고, 또 사뭇 다른 종류의 다른 이유들도 여럿 나돌았다. 아니, 어떤 이들은 죄를 아내에게로 돌리고 질투를 남편에게로 돌리기까지 했다.\n\n윌킨스 부인은 이 다툼에 대해 오래전에 들어 알고 있었다. 그러나 진짜 원인과는 다른 사연이 그녀의 귀에 들어왔기에, 그녀는 그것을 덮어 두는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼다. 어쩌면 그 비난이 한결같이 파트리지 씨에게 쏟아지고 있었기 때문에 더욱 그러했는지도 모른다. 게다가 그의 아내는 올워디 씨의 하녀로 있던 시절 무언가로 윌킨스 부인의 비위를 거슬렀던 적이 있었는데, 윌킨스 부인은 그리 너그럽게 용서하는 성품이 아니었다.\n\n그러나 멀리 있는 대상까지 내다보는 눈을 지녔고, 몇 해 앞의 미래까지도 능히 헤아릴 줄 알았던 윌킨스 부인은, 블리필 대위가 머지않아 자신의 주인이 될 가능성이 다분함을 알아차렸다. 그리고 대위가 그 어린 업둥이에게 별다른 호의를 품고 있지 않음을 분명히 간파했기에, 올워디 씨가 이 아이에게 품은 듯한 애정—대위에게는 눈에 띄게 거북함을 주었고, 그래서 그가 올워디 씨 앞에서조차 온전히 감추지 못했던 그 애정—을 덜어 줄 만한 무언가를 캐낼 수 있다면, 그것이 대위에게 흡족한 봉사가 되리라고 생각했다. 그의 아내는 사람들 앞에서 자기 역할을 훨씬 능란하게 해내며, 남편에게 자신의 본보기를 자주 권했다. 즉 자기 오라비의 어리석음을 못 본 척 눈감아 주라는 것이었는데, 그녀는 자기야말로 그 어리석음을 누구 못지않게 잘 알아채고 누구 못지않게 못마땅해한다고 말하곤 했다.\n\n그리하여 윌킨스 부인은 우연히 위 이야기의 참된 냄새를 맡게 되자—일이 벌어지고 한참이 지난 뒤이긴 했으나—온갖 자세한 사정을 빠짐없이 확인하는 일을 게을리하지 않았다. 그러고는 대위에게, 마침내 그 어린 사생아의 진짜 아비를 알아냈노라고, 주인께서 그 아이를 그토록 마음에 두시는 탓에 시골에서 명성을 잃으시는 것이 안타깝다고 아뢰었다.\n\n대위는 그녀가 말끝에 덧붙인 결론을 꾸짖었다. 주인의 행동을 함부로 판단하는 주제넘은 짓이라는 것이었다. 설령 그의 명예나 분별이 대위로 하여금 윌킨스 부인과 한패가 되도록 허락했다 하더라도, 그의 자존심은 결코 그것을 받아들이지 않았을 것이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 벗의 하인들과 손잡고 그 주인에게 맞서는 것만큼 분별없는 처사도 없다. 그렇게 하다가는 나중에 바로 그 하인들의 종이 되고 말며, 그들에게 언제든 배신당할 위험에 놓이기 때문이다. 어쩌면 바로 이런 고려 때문에 블리필 대위는 윌킨스 부인에게 더 속을 터놓지 못했고, 그녀가 올워디에게 퍼부은 험담을 부추기지도 않았을 것이다.\n\n그러나 그는 이 발견에 대해 윌킨스 부인에게 만족하는 기색을 전혀 내비치지 않았으면서도, 속으로는 그것을 적잖이 즐겼고, 그것을 할 수 있는 한 최대로 활용하기로 작정했다.\n\n그는 올워디 씨가 다른 누군가에게서 이 일을 듣게 되기를 바라며, 이 문제를 오랫동안 제 가슴속에만 묻어 두었다. 그러나 윌킨스 부인은, 대위의 처신에 앙심을 품었기 때문인지, 아니면 그의 교활함이 자기 머리 위에 있어 그 발설이 그의 비위를 거스를까 두려웠기 때문인지, 그 후로는 이 일에 대해 결코 입을 열지 않았다.\n\n돌이켜 생각해 보니, 여자들은 모든 소식을 우리 남자보다는 자기와 같은 성에게 전하려는 성향이 더 강한 법인데도, 그 가정부가 이 소식을 블리필 부인에게 한 번도 알리지 않았다는 것이 다소 이상하게 여겨졌다. 내가 보기에 이 난점을 풀 유일한 길은, 그것을 이제 부인과 가정부 사이에 자라난 그 거리감 탓으로 돌리는 것이다. 이 거리감이 윌킨스가 업둥이에게 지나치게 큰 존중을 보인 데서 비롯된 블리필 부인의 질투에서 생겨난 것인지는 모르겠으나, 윌킨스는 대위의 환심을 사기 위해 그 어린 갓난것을 망치려 애쓰는 한편으로, 올워디의 그 아이에 대한 애정이 날로 커져 가자 그 앞에서는 날마다 점점 더 그 아이를 칭찬했던 것이다. 다른 때에는 블리필 부인에게 정반대를 표하려 그토록 조심했음에도, 이 일은 어쩌면 그 까다로운 부인의 비위를 거슬렀고, 부인은 이제 분명 윌킨스 부인을 미워하게 되었다. 그리고 부인은 그녀를 그 자리에서 완전히 내쫓지는 않았거나, 어쩌면 내쫓을 수 없었으나, 어쨌든 그녀의 삶을 몹시 고달프게 만드는 방법은 찾아냈다. 윌킨스 부인은 마침내 이에 분개한 나머지, 블리필 부인에게 보란 듯이 어린 토미에게 온갖 존중과 애정을 아주 공공연히 드러냈다.\n\n그리하여 대위는 그 이야기가 사그라들 위험에 처한 것을 알고, 마침내 기회를 잡아 스스로 그것을 폭로하기에 이르렀다.\n\n어느 날 그는 올워디 씨와 자선에 관한 담론에 빠져 있었는데, 거기서 대위는 대단한 학식을 동원하여, 성경에서 자선이라는 말이 어디에서도 선행이나 너그러움을 뜻하지 않는다는 것을 올워디 씨에게 입증해 보였다.\n\n\"기독교는,\" 그가 말했다. \"수많은 이교 철학자들이 우리에게 이미 오래전에 가르쳐 준 교훈을 강요하려고 세워진 것이 아니라, 그보다 훨씬 더 고귀한 목적을 위해 세워진 것입니다. 그 교훈이란 것은, 어쩌면 도덕적 미덕이라 불릴 수는 있겠으나, 오직 은총으로만 이르고, 표현하고, 느낄 수 있는 그 숭고하고 기독교다운 성품, 천사의 완전함에 순결함으로 다가가는 그 드높은 사고의 고양과는 거의 닮은 데가 없습니다. 그 말을 너그러움, 곧 우리 형제들에 대해 선의의 견해를 품고 그들의 행동에 호의적인 판단을 내리는 것으로 이해한 이들이야말로 성경의 뜻에 더 가까이 다가간 것입니다. 이것은 그 본성에서 보잘것없는 적선의 분배보다 훨씬 더 높고 훨씬 더 폭넓은 미덕입니다. 적선이란, 우리가 아무리 우리 가족을 해치거나 심지어 파산시키더라도, 결코 많은 이에게 미칠 수 없는 것이지만, 다른 의미, 곧 더 참된 의미의 자선은 온 인류에게 미칠 수 있는 것이니까요.\"\n\n그가 말했다. \"그 제자들이 어떤 사람들이었는지를 생각하면, 너그러움이나 적선의 교리가 그들에게 설파되었으리라 여기는 것은 터무니없는 일입니다. 또한 이 교리가 그것을 실천할 수 없는 사람들에게 그 거룩하신 창시자에 의해 설파되었다고 상상하기 어려운 만큼, 그것을 실천할 수 있으면서도 하지 않는 사람들이 그렇게 이해했으리라고는 더더욱 생각해서는 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그러나,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"이런 자선 행위에는 유감스럽게도 공덕이 거의 없을지언정, 한 가지 고려 사항만 없다면 선한 마음에는 거기서 큰 기쁨이 있으리라는 것을 저도 인정하지 않을 수 없습니다. 제 말은, 우리가 속아 넘어가, 우리의 가장 귀한 호의를 자격 없는 자에게 베푸는 일이 흔하다는 것입니다. 저 변변찮은 파트리지에게 베푸신 후의가 바로 그런 경우였음을 어르신께서도 인정하셔야 할 것입니다. 그런 사례가 두세 번만 있어도, 선한 사람이 너그러움 속에서 으레 찾을 그 내면의 만족이 크게 줄어들 수밖에 없습니다. 아니, 그것은 그가 베푸는 일조차 두려워하게 만들 수도 있습니다. 악덕을 떠받치고 사악한 자를 부추기는 죄를 짓게 될까 봐서요. 그것은 그 빛깔이 아주 시커먼 죄이며, 우리가 실제로 그런 부추김을 의도하지는 않았다는 변명만으로는 결코 충분치 못합니다. 우리가 선행의 대상을 고르는 데 더없는 신중함을 기하지 않았다면 말이지요. 의심할 바 없이 이 고려 사항은 수많은 가치 있고 경건한 사람의 베풂을 크게 위축시켜 왔을 것입니다.\"\n\n올워디 씨가 대답했다. \"나는 그리스어를 두고 대위와 다툴 수가 없으니, 자선으로 번역되는 그 말의 참뜻에 대해서는 아무 말도 할 수 없네. 다만 나는 그것이 늘 행동에 있다고 풀이된다고 생각해 왔고, 적선이야말로 적어도 그 미덕의 한 갈래를 이룬다고 여겨 왔네.\"\n\n\"공덕의 측면에 관해서는,\" 그가 말했다. \"기꺼이 대위의 의견에 동의하네. 한낱 의무를 다하는 데 무슨 공덕이 있겠는가? 자선이라는 말이 어떤 해석을 가지든, 그것이 의무임은 신약 전체의 흐름으로 보아 충분히 드러난다고 보네. 또한 나는 그것이 기독교의 율법으로나 자연의 법칙 그 자체로나 명해진, 없어서는 안 될 의무라고 생각하는데, 그러면서도 그것은 어찌나 즐거운 일인지, 만약 어떤 의무가 그 자체로 보상이 된다거나 우리가 그것을 행하는 동안 우리에게 보답한다고 말할 수 있다면, 그것이 바로 이 의무일 것이네.\"\n\n\"솔직히 털어놓자면,\" 그가 말했다. \"한 가지 정도의 너그러움(나는 그것을 자선이라 부르고 싶네만)은 얼마간 공덕의 빛을 띠는 듯하네. 곧 선의와 기독교적 사랑의 원리에서, 우리 자신에게 정말로 필요한 것을 남에게 베푸는 경우, 곧 남의 고통을 덜어 주기 위해 우리 자신의 궁핍으로도 도저히 내어 줄 형편이 못 되는 것을 내어 줌으로써 그 고통의 일부를 기꺼이 나누어 지는 경우 말이네. 이것은, 내 생각에, 공덕이 있는 일이지. 그러나 우리의 남아도는 것으로만 형제들을 돕는 것, 우리 자신을 축내기보다는 우리 금고를 축내어 자선을 베푸는 것(이 말을 꼭 써야겠군), 집에 비범한 그림 한 점을 걸어 두거나 그 밖의 헛되고 우스꽝스러운 허영을 채우기보다는 차라리 여러 가정을 비참에서 구해 내는 것—이것은 그저 인간다운 피조물이 되는 것에 지나지 않는 듯하네. 아니, 나는 더 나아가 감히 말하겠네. 그것은 어느 정도 미식가가 되는 것이라고. 가장 대단한 미식가라도 입 하나가 아니라 여러 입과 함께 먹는 것보다 더 바랄 게 무엇이겠는가? 여러 사람의 빵이 자기 베풂 덕분임을 아는 자라면 누구에게나 이렇게 말할 수 있다고 나는 생각하네.\"\n\n\"앞으로 자격 없는 대상으로 드러날지도 모를 자에게 후의를 베풀까 봐 염려하는 것에 대해서는, 많은 이가 그렇게 드러났다는 이유로 그러한 것일 텐데, 그것이 선한 사람을 너그러움에서 물러서게 할 수는 결코 없을 것이네. 나는 배은망덕의 사례가 적든 많든, 그것이 사람이 자기 동료 피조물의 고통 앞에서 마음을 굳게 닫는 일을 정당화할 수 있다고는 생각하지 않으며, 진실로 선의 어린 마음에 그런 영향을 미칠 수 있으리라고도 믿지 않네. 만인이 타락했다는 확신만이 선한 사람의 자선을 닫아걸 수 있을 것이며, 그 확신은, 내 생각에, 그를 무신론이나 광신으로 이끌 수밖에 없을 것이네. 그러나 몇몇 사악한 개인들을 두고 그런 만인의 타락을 논하는 것은 분명 온당치 못한 일이며, 자기 마음을 들여다보아 그 일반 법칙에 대해 단 하나라도 확실한 예외를 찾아낸 사람이라면, 결코 그런 짓을 하지 않으리라고 나는 믿네.\" 그러고서 그는 이렇게 물으며 말을 맺었다. \"그런데 자네가 변변찮은 자라 부른 그 파트리지란 누구인가?\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드리는 건,\" 대위가 말했다. \"이발사 파트리지, 그 학교 선생, 뭐라고 부르시던가요? 파트리지 말입니다, 어르신께서 침대에서 발견하신 그 어린아이의 아비 말이지요.\"\n\n올워디 씨는 이 이야기에 크게 놀라움을 드러냈고, 대위는 그가 그것을 모르고 있었다는 데에 그만큼이나 크게 놀랐다. 자기는 그 일을 한 달도 더 전부터 알고 있었노라고 했기 때문이다. 그러고는 한참을 어렵사리 떠올린 끝에, 그것을 윌킨스 부인에게서 들었노라고 했다.\n\n이에 윌킨스가 즉시 불려 왔다. 그녀가 대위의 말을 확인해 주자, 올워디 씨는 대위의 조언에 따라 그녀를 리틀 배딩턴으로 보내 그 사실의 진위를 직접 알아 오게 했다. 대위가 형사적인 문제에서 성급하게 처리하는 것을 몹시 꺼리는 기색을 보이며, 후자가 죄를 지었음을 확실히 하기 전에는 올워디 씨가 그 아이나 그 아비에게 불리한 어떤 결정도 결코 내리지 않으셨으면 한다고 말했기 때문이다. 사실 그는 파트리지의 이웃 가운데 하나에게서 이 일을 사사로이 확인해 두었으면서도, 너무도 너그러운 나머지 그런 증거를 올워디 씨에게 내놓지 않았던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n교사 파트리지가 부정을 저질렀다는 죄목으로 받는 재판. 그의 아내가 내놓은 증언. 우리 법의 지혜에 대한 짧은 단상. 그리고 가장 잘 이해하는 이들이 가장 좋아할 다른 묵직한 문제들.\n\n이토록 널리 알려져 그 많은 입길에 오르내린 이야기가, 어째서 정작 올워디 씨 본인에게는 한 번도 전해지지 않았는지 의아할 법하다. 그 고장에서 이 일을 들어 본 적 없는 사람은 아마 그가 유일했을 테니 말이다.\n\n독자에게 이 사정을 어느 정도 풀어 드리고자, 나는 이렇게 일러두는 편이 옳다고 본다. 앞 장에서 다룬바, 자선이라는 낱말의 뜻을 둘러싼 그 논리에 맞설 마음이 이 나라에서 우리의 선량한 분만큼 적은 이도 없었다고 말이다. 실로 그는 어느 쪽 의미로 보든 이 미덕을 똑같이 누릴 자격이 있었다. 남의 궁핍을 누구보다 잘 헤아렸고 남의 곤경을 누구보다 기꺼이 덜어 주었듯, 남의 평판에 누구보다 너그러웠으며 그들에게 불리한 일이라면 무엇이든 누구보다 더디게 믿었기 때문이다.\n\n그러니 험담은 결코 그의 식탁에 발붙이지 못했다. 사람을 그 벗으로 알 수 있다는 말이 예부터 있어 왔듯, 나 또한 감히 이렇게 말해 두겠다. 위대한 인물의 식탁에서 오가는 대화에 귀를 기울이면, 그 사람의 신앙과 정치 성향, 취향, 나아가 됨됨이 전체를 어렵잖이 가늠할 수 있다고. 어디서든 제 생각을 거리낌 없이 내뱉는 별난 자들이 몇 있긴 하지만, 사람들 대다수는 윗사람의 취향과 기호에 제 대화를 맞출 만큼의 아첨기는 충분히 지니고 있으니 말이다.\n\n하지만 윌킨스 부인에게로 돌아가 보자. 그녀는 십오 마일이나 떨어진 곳에서도 임무를 어찌나 빠르게 처리했던지, 교사가 죄를 지었다는 사실을 확실히 뒷받침할 증거를 가지고 돌아왔다. 그리하여 올워디 씨는 그 죄인을 불러들여 직접 입으로 묻기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 파트리지 씨는 이 고발에 맞서 (할 수 있다면) 변론하라는 소환을 받았다.\n* 직접 입으로 묻기: 원문은 라틴어 viva voce, 곧 구두로 직접 심문한다는 뜻.\n\n정해진 시각에 파라다이스홀의 올워디 씨 앞으로, 앞서 말한 파트리지가 아내 앤과 함께, 그리고 그를 고발한 윌킨스 부인이 나란히 나왔다.\n\n이제 올워디 씨가 재판관의 자리에 앉자, 파트리지 씨가 그 앞에 끌려 나왔다. 윌킨스 부인의 입에서 자기 죄목을 듣고 난 그는 결백을 주장하며, 자신은 무죄라고 격렬하게 거듭 항변했다.\n\n다음으로 파트리지 부인이 심문을 받았다. 그녀는 남편에게 불리한 진실을 말할 수밖에 없게 된 처지를 겸손하게 변명한 뒤, 독자가 이미 알고 있는 정황을 빠짐없이 늘어놓았고, 마침내 남편이 제 죄를 자백했다는 말로 끝맺었다.\n\n그녀가 그를 용서했는지 어떤지는 내가 감히 단정하지 못하겠다. 다만 이 사건에서 그녀가 마지못해 나선 증인이었다는 것만큼은 분명하다. 또 다른 몇 가지 이유로 미루어 보건대, 윌킨스 부인이 큰 수완을 발휘해 그녀의 집에서 모든 이야기를 낚아내지 않았더라면, 그리고 남편에 대한 처벌이 그 가족에게는 결코 해가 미치지 않게 하겠다고 올워디 씨의 이름을 빌려 약속해 주지 않았더라면, 그녀는 결코 그렇게 증언하도록 끌려 나오지 않았을 것이다.\n\n파트리지는 앞서 말한 자백을 한 적이 있음은 인정하면서도 여전히 자신의 결백을 주장했다. 다만 그는 그 자백을 이렇게 해명하려 했다. 아내가 끊임없이 들볶는 통에 어쩔 수 없이 자백하게 되었노라고. 아내는 그가 죄를 지은 것이 틀림없으니, 그가 인정할 때까지 절대 괴롭히기를 멈추지 않겠다고 다짐했고, 일단 인정만 하면 다시는 그 일을 입에 올리지 않겠노라 굳게 약속했다는 것이다. 그래서 그는 결백한데도 거짓으로 죄를 인정하게 되었으며, 같은 까닭이라면 살인이라도 자백했을 것이라고 했다.\n\n파트리지 부인은 이 비난을 참고 견딜 수가 없었다. 이 자리에서 그녀가 기댈 수단이라곤 눈물뿐이었으므로, 그녀는 눈물을 펑펑 불러내 도움을 청한 다음 올워디 씨를 향해 이렇게 말했다(아니, 차라리 울부짖었다). \"나리, 어디 저 비열한 인간한테 저처럼 당한 가엾은 계집이 또 있겠습니까. 저 인간이 저를 속인 게 어디 이번 한 번뿐이겠어요. 아닙니다요, 나리, 저 인간은 좋은 시절 내내 몇 번이고 제 잠자리를 더럽혔답니다. 술주정에 일까지 내팽개치는 거야 그러려니 참아 줄 수도 있었어요, 신성한 계명 하나만 깨뜨리지 않았다면요. 게다가 그게 바깥에서 있었던 일이라면 그렇게까지 마음 쓰지도 않았을 텐데, 제 집 종년이랑, 제 집에서, 제 지붕 아래서, 제 정숙한 잠자리를 더럽히다니요. 정말이지 저 인간이 그 짐승 같고 구린내 나는 갈보들이랑 그 짓을 했단 말이에요. 그래, 이 악당아, 네가 내 잠자리를 더럽혀 놓고선, 이제 와서 내가 너를 윽박질러 사실을 인정하게 했다고 뒤집어씌워? 나리, 제가 저 인간을 윽박지를 만하게 생겼습니까요? 저 인간이 저한테 얼마나 모질게 굴었는지 제 몸뚱이에 흔적이 차고 넘칩니다. 이 악당아, 네가 사내였다면 그런 식으로 계집을 괴롭히는 짓은 부끄러워서라도 못 했을 거다. 하지만 넌 반쪽짜리 사내도 못 돼, 너도 알잖아. 나한테 반쪽짜리 남편 노릇도 못 했고. 갈보들 꽁무니나 쫓아다녀야겠지, 그래야겠지, 내가 분명히 아는데—저 인간이 저를 이렇게 부추기니, 나리, 저는 두 사람이 한 침대에 같이 누워 있는 걸 제 눈으로 봤다고 이 몸에 걸고 맹세할 준비가 돼 있습니다. 뭐야, 잊어버린 모양인데, 내가 그저 점잖게 간통을 따졌다는 이유로 네가 나를 두들겨 패서 발작을 일으키게 하고 이마에서 피가 흐르게 한 일은 어쩌고! 그건 이웃 사람 누구한테 물어봐도 다 증명할 수 있다고. 네가 내 가슴을 거의 찢어 놓은 거야, 네가, 네가.\"\n\n여기서 올워디 씨가 말을 끊고는, 정의는 반드시 세워질 테니 부디 진정하라고 그녀를 달랬다. 그러고는 파트리지에게로 몸을 돌렸는데, 그는 넋이 반쯤 나가 서 있었다. 정신의 반은 놀라움에, 나머지 반은 두려움에 휩쓸려 가 버린 꼴이었다. 올워디 씨는 세상에 이토록 사악한 자가 있는 것을 보니 안타깝다고 말했다. 그리고 이리저리 말을 둘러대며 거짓을 늘어놓는 것은 그 죄를 한층 무겁게 만드는 일이며, 그에 대해 할 수 있는 유일한 속죄란 자백과 회개뿐이라고 단언했다. 그러므로 그는 파트리지에게, 우선 곧장 사실을 자백하는 것부터 시작하고, 제 아내까지 나서서 이토록 명백히 증명한 일을 끝까지 부인하지 말라고 권고했다.\n\n여기서, 독자여, 잠시만 인내해 주시기를 청한다. 그동안 나는 우리 법의 위대한 지혜와 분별력에 마땅한 찬사를 보내려 한다. 우리 법은 남편을 위해서든 그에게 불리하게든 아내의 증언을 받아들이기를 거부하니 말이다. 어느 학식 높은 저자의 말을 빌리자면(내 생각에 이 사람의 글이 법률서가 아닌 데서 인용된 적은 여태 한 번도 없을 것이다), 그렇지 않으면 부부 사이에 끝없는 불화의 씨앗을 뿌리는 셈이 된다고 한다. 실로 그것은 숱한 위증을 낳고, 숱한 채찍질과 벌금형, 투옥과 유배, 그리고 교수형을 불러오는 빌미가 될 것이다.\n\n파트리지는 한동안 말없이 서 있다가, 말하라는 명을 받자 자신은 이미 진실을 말했노라고 했다. 그러고는 자신의 결백을 두고 하늘에 호소했고, 마지막으로는 그 처녀 본인에게 호소하며, 부디 나리께서 당장 그녀를 불러 주십사 청했다. 그녀가 이미 그 고장을 떠난 줄을 그는 모르고 있었거나, 적어도 모르는 척하고 있었다.\n\n올워디 씨는 타고난 정의감에 차분한 성품까지 더해진 덕에, 피고가 제 변론을 위해 내세울 수 있는 증인이라면 누구든 끝까지 참을성 있게 들어 주는 더없이 인내심 깊은 재판관이었다. 그리하여 그는 제니가 도착할 때까지 이 사건의 최종 판결을 미루기로 했고, 곧장 그녀를 데려올 심부름꾼을 보냈다. 그런 다음 파트리지와 그 아내 사이에 화목하기를 권한 뒤(다만 그는 주로 엉뚱한 쪽을 향해 그 말을 했다), 사흘째 되는 날 다시 출두하라고 두 사람에게 명했다. 제니를 자기 집에서 꼬박 하루 거리나 떨어진 곳으로 보냈던 까닭이다.\n\n정해진 시각에 당사자들이 모두 모였는데, 심부름꾼이 돌아와 전한 말은 제니를 찾을 수 없다는 것이었다. 그녀가 며칠 전에 어느 모병 장교와 함께 살던 집을 떠나 버렸다는 것이다.\n\n그러자 올워디 씨는, 보아하니 그런 헤픈 계집의 증언이라면 애초에 믿을 만한 가치도 없었을 것이라고 잘라 말했다. 다만 그녀가 그 자리에 있어 진실을 밝혔더라면, 그토록 많은 정황과 더불어 그 자신의 자백, 그리고 남편이 현장에서 붙잡힌 것을 보았다는 아내의 진술이 충분히 증명한 바를 그녀 역시 확인해 주었을 것이 틀림없다고 생각하지 않을 수 없다고 했다. 그래서 그는 파트리지에게 다시 한번 자백하라고 권했으나, 그가 여전히 결백을 주장하자, 올워디 씨는 그의 죄를 확신한다고, 그리고 그는 자신에게서 어떤 격려도 받기에는 너무 나쁜 인간이라고 선언했다. 그리하여 그에게서 연금을 거두어들이고는, 저세상을 생각하여 회개하고 이 세상에서는 부지런히 일해 제 한 몸과 아내를 건사하라고 권했다.\n\n가엾은 파트리지보다 더 불행한 사람은 아마 그리 많지 않았을 것이다. 그는 아내의 증언 탓에 수입의 가장 큰 몫을 잃었으면서도, 다른 일들과 더불어 바로 그 연금까지 빼앗기게 만든 장본인이라는 이유로 날마다 아내에게 들볶였다. 하지만 그것이 그의 팔자였고, 그는 그것을 받아들이는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n앞 문단에서 그를 가엾은 파트리지라 불렀지만, 독자는 그 수식어를 그의 결백을 선언하는 말로 받아들이기보다 내 천성에 깃든 연민에서 나온 것으로 여겨 주기를 바란다. 그가 결백했는지 아닌지는 어쩌면 이후에 드러날 것이다. 다만 역사의 뮤즈가 내게 어떤 비밀을 맡겨 두었다 해도, 그녀가 허락을 내리기 전까지는 그것을 누설하는 죄를 결코 짓지 않겠다.\n\n그러니 여기서 독자는 잠시 호기심을 미뤄 두어야 한다. 분명한 것은, 이 사건의 진실이 무엇이었든 간에 올워디 앞에서 그를 유죄로 단정할 증거가 차고 넘쳤다는 사실이다. 실로 사생아 부양 명령을 내리는 치안판사석이라면 이보다 훨씬 적은 증거로도 만족했을 것이다. 그런데도, 이 일을 두고 성찬에 걸고서라도 맹세하려 들었을 파트리지 부인의 단호함에도 불구하고, 그 교사가 완전히 결백했을 가능성은 여전히 있다. 제니가 리틀 배딩턴을 떠난 시점과 그녀가 아이를 낳은 시점을 견주어 보면 그녀가 그곳에서 이 아이를 잉태했다는 것이 분명해 보이긴 했으나, 그렇다고 해서 파트리지가 반드시 그 아비여야만 한다는 결론이 필연적으로 따라 나오는 것은 결코 아니었다. 다른 자잘한 정황은 접어 두더라도, 같은 집에 열여덟 살 가까운 사내아이가 하나 있었는데, 그와 제니 사이에는 합당한 의심을 품을 만한 친밀함이 충분히 있었던 것이다. 그런데도 질투란 워낙 눈먼 것이어서, 격분한 아내의 머릿속에는 이런 정황이 단 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다.\n\n파트리지가 올워디 씨의 충고에 따라 회개했는지 어떤지는 그리 분명치 않다. 분명한 것은, 그의 아내가 남편에게 불리하게 한 증언을 진심으로 후회했다는 사실이다. 특히 데버라 부인이 자기를 속였으며, 자기를 위해 올워디 씨에게 어떤 청도 넣어 주지 않으려 한다는 것을 알았을 때 더욱 그러했다. 그래도 블리필 부인에게는 다소 나은 결과를 거두었는데, 독자도 익히 짐작했겠지만 그녀는 훨씬 너그러운 성품의 여인이어서, 연금을 되돌려 주도록 제 오라비에게 청을 넣어 주는 일을 아주 친절히 떠맡았다. 여기에 선한 마음도 얼마쯤 몫을 했겠지만, 더 강하고 자연스러운 동기가 무엇이었는지는 다음 장에서 드러날 것이다.\n\n그럼에도 이런 청탁은 끝내 받아들여지지 않았다. 올워디 씨는 자비란 오로지 죄인을 벌하는 데 있다고 본 요즘 어떤 저술가들과 생각을 달리하긴 했으나, 그렇다고 아무런 까닭도 없이 큰 죄인을 함부로 용서하는 것이 그 훌륭한 덕성에 걸맞은 일이라고 여기는 것과는 한참 거리가 멀었다. 사실 관계에 의심스러운 점이 있거나 정상을 참작할 만한 사정이 있다면 그는 결코 그것을 무시하지 않았다. 그러나 죄인 본인의 탄원이나 다른 이들의 중재는 그의 마음을 조금도 움직이지 못했다. 한마디로 그는 죄인 본인이나 그 벗들이 처벌을 원치 않는다는 이유만으로 누군가를 용서한 적이 없었다.\n\n그리하여 파트리지와 그 아내는 둘 다 제 운명에 굴복하는 수밖에 없었으니, 그 운명이란 실로 가혹하기 그지없는 것이었다. 수입이 줄어든 만큼 부지런함을 곱절로 늘리기는커녕, 그는 어느새 절망에 제 몸을 내맡겨 버렸다. 게다가 그는 천성이 게을렀던지라 이제 그 악덕이 더욱 심해졌고, 그 탓에 가지고 있던 자그마한 학교마저 잃고 말았다. 그래서 어느 마음씨 고운 기독교인의 자선이 끼어들어 겨우 끼니나 이을 만큼을 마련해 주지 않았더라면, 그의 아내도 그 자신도 입에 풀칠할 빵 한 조각조차 없었을 것이다.\n\n이 도움이 누군지 모를 손길을 통해 그들에게 전해졌으므로, 그들은 올워디 씨 본인이 자기들의 은밀한 은인이리라 짐작했고, 독자 역시 그러하리라고 나는 의심치 않는다. 그분은 드러내 놓고 악을 두둔하지는 않으려 했으나, 악한 자들이 겪는 곤경이 그 죗값에 견주어 지나치게 혹독하고 어울리지 않을 만큼 커지면 남몰래 그것을 덜어 줄 수는 있는 분이었다. 이제 운명의 여신마저 그들의 비참함을 바로 그런 시선으로 바라보았던 모양이다. 여신은 마침내 이 가련한 부부를 가엾이 여겨, 파트리지의 비참한 처지를 적잖이 덜어 주었으니, 곧 아내의 비참함에 종지부를 찍어 준 것이다. 아내는 얼마 지나지 않아 천연두에 걸려 세상을 떠났다.\n\n올워디 씨가 파트리지에게 내린 처분은 처음에는 모두의 찬동을 얻었다. 그러나 사람들이 그 결과를 직접 보게 되자마자, 이웃들은 마음이 누그러져 그의 처지를 가엾게 여기기 시작했고, 이내 전에는 정의라 부르던 것을 이제 와 가혹하고 모진 처사라고 탓하기 시작했다. 이제 그들은 냉혹하게 벌을 내리는 짓을 두고 소리 높여 비난하면서, 자비와 용서를 찬양하는 노래를 불러 댔다.\n\n이 아우성은 파트리지 부인의 죽음으로 한층 거세졌다. 그 죽음이 가난이나 곤경과는 아무 상관도 없는 앞서 말한 그 돌림병 탓이었는데도, 많은 이들이 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 그것을 올워디 씨의 모진 처사, 아니 그들이 이제 부르는 말로 하자면 잔인함의 탓으로 돌렸다.\n\n이제 아내도, 학교도, 연금도 다 잃은 데다, 그 정체 모를 사람마저 앞서 말한 자선을 끊어 버리자, 파트리지는 사는 무대를 바꾸기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 굶어 죽을 위기에 놓인 그 고장을, 이웃 모두의 한결같은 동정을 받으며 떠나갔다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n분별 있는 부부가 증오에서 길어 올릴 수 있는 그 행복을 짧게 그려 보임. 더불어 벗의 결점을 못 본 척 넘기는 사람들을 위한 짤막한 변호도 곁들임.\n\n대위는 가엾은 파트리지를 완전히 무너뜨리는 데에는 성공했지만, 정작 그가 바라던 수확, 곧 그 업둥이를 올워디 씨의 집에서 내쫓는 일만큼은 거두지 못했다.\n\n오히려 그 신사는 날이 갈수록 어린 토미를 더욱 어여삐 여겼으니, 마치 아버지에게 베푼 가혹함을 아들에게 베푸는 유별난 애정과 다정함으로 상쇄하려는 듯했다.\n\n이것은 대위의 심사를 적잖이 뒤틀어 놓았고, 올워디 씨가 날마다 보이는 다른 모든 너그러움 또한 마찬가지였다. 그는 그러한 모든 베풂을 제 몫이 될 재산이 줄어드는 것으로 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n이 점에서 그는 아내와 뜻이 맞지 않았다고 우리는 이미 말한 바 있는데, 사실 다른 어떤 일에서도 마찬가지였다. 지성에 둔 애정이 아름다움에 토대를 둔 애정보다 더 오래간다고 많은 현명한 이들이 여기지만, 이번 경우만큼은 그렇지 못했다. 아니, 이 부부의 지성이야말로 다툼의 으뜸가는 빌미였고, 때때로 둘 사이에서 일어나던 숱한 언쟁의 큰 원인이었으니, 그 언쟁은 마침내 아내 쪽에서는 남편을 향한 더없는 경멸로, 남편 쪽에서는 아내를 향한 철저한 혐오로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n두 사람 모두 신학 연구에 주로 재능을 쏟아 왔던 터라, 이것은 처음 알게 된 무렵부터 둘 사이에서 가장 흔한 대화 주제였다. 대위는 예의 바른 사람답게 결혼 전에는 늘 제 의견을 아내의 의견에 양보했는데, 그것도 잘난 척하는 멍청이가 흔히 하는 그 서툴고 어색한 방식—토론에서 윗사람에게 점잖게 굽히면서도 속으로는 여전히 제가 옳다고 여기는 줄을 남이 알아주기를 바라는 방식—은 아니었다. 오히려 대위는 세상에서 가장 자존심 센 사내 가운데 하나였음에도, 상대에게 승리를 어찌나 깔끔하게 내주었던지, 그의 진심을 조금도 의심치 않던 아내는 언제나 제 지성에 대한 감탄과 그의 지성에 대한 사랑을 품은 채 논쟁에서 물러나곤 했다.\n\n그러나 대위가 속으로 철저히 경멸하던 사람에게 보인 이 너그러운 굴종은, 가령 승진의 기대 때문에 호들리 같은 이나 그 학문에서 큰 명성을 떨치는 다른 누군가에게 똑같은 복종을 보여야 했을 때만큼 그에게 거북한 일은 아니었다 해도, 그래도 어떤 동기 없이 견디기에는 너무 큰 대가를 치르는 일이었다. 그러니 결혼이 그러한 모든 동기를 없애 버리자, 그는 이 굽힘에 싫증을 느끼기 시작했고, 아내의 의견을 그 거만하고 무례한 태도로 대하기에 이르렀다. 그것은 제 자신이 얼마간 경멸받아 마땅한 자들만이 베풀 수 있고, 조금도 경멸받을 일 없는 자들만이 견뎌 낼 수 있는 그런 태도였다.\n\n다정함의 첫 격류가 잦아들고, 발작과 발작 사이의 그 잔잔하고 긴 틈에서 이성이 아내의 눈을 뜨게 하기 시작하여, 마침내 자신의 모든 논변에 그저 흥, 칫 따위로만 답하는 대위의 달라진 행실을 그녀가 보게 되었을 때, 그녀는 그 모욕을 고분고분 받아들이며 견딜 사람이 결코 아니었다. 실로 그 일은 처음에는 그녀를 어찌나 격하게 자극했던지, 만일 더 해롭지 않은 쪽으로 방향을 틀지 않았더라면 어떤 비극적 사건을 낳았을지도 모른다. 그 방향이란, 남편의 지성을 향한 더없는 경멸로 그녀의 가슴을 가득 채우는 것이었으니, 이것이 남편을 향한 증오를 어느 정도 누그러뜨려 주었다. 비록 그 증오 또한 제법 적당한 몫만큼은 그녀 안에 있었지만 말이다.\n\n대위가 아내에게 품은 증오는 좀 더 순수한 종류였다. 그녀의 지식이나 지성에 어떤 결함이 있다 해서 그가 그녀를 경멸한 것은 아니었으니, 그것은 그녀의 키가 육 척이 못 된다 해서 경멸하지 않는 것과 다를 바 없었다. 여성을 보는 그의 견해는 아리스토텔레스 자신의 까탈스러움마저 능가했다. 그는 여자를 집안일에 쓰이는 짐승쯤으로 여겼는데, 고양이보다는 조금 더 쳐줄 만하다 했으니, 그 맡은 일이 좀 더 중요하기 때문이었다. 그러나 그 둘 사이의 차이란 그의 평가로는 어찌나 작던지, 올워디 씨의 토지와 가옥을 노리고 맺은 그 결혼에서, 둘 중 어느 쪽을 덤으로 떠안았든 거의 매한가지였을 정도였다. 그러면서도 그의 자존심은 어찌나 여리던지, 이제 아내가 그를 향해 드러내기 시작한 경멸을 고스란히 느꼈다. 그리고 이것은, 전에 그녀의 사랑에 물리도록 시달린 경험과 더해져, 그의 안에 어쩌면 더 넘어설 수 없을 만큼의 역겨움과 혐오를 만들어 냈다.\n\n결혼 생활에서 즐거움이 배제되는 처지는 단 하나뿐이니, 그것은 곧 무관심의 상태다. 그러나 내 독자들 가운데 많은 분은 사랑하는 대상에게 기쁨을 전하는 일에 얼마나 절묘한 즐거움이 있는지 아시리라 믿는 한편, 안타깝게도 몇몇 분은 미워하는 자를 괴롭히는 데서 오는 만족을 겪어 보셨을지도 모른다. 내가 짐작건대, 바로 이 후자의 즐거움에 다다르려고, 우리는 남녀 양쪽이 결혼 생활에서 누릴 수도 있었을 그 편안함을 곧잘 내던지는 것을 본다. 비록 상대가 아무리 마음에 들지 않았다 하더라도 말이다. 그리하여 아내는 흔히 사랑이며 질투의 발작을 꾸며 내고, 아니 제 즐거움마저 마다하면서까지 남편의 즐거움을 어지럽히고 막으려 든다. 그리고 남편은 또 그 보답으로, 제 자신에게 자주 제약을 가하며, 마음에 들지 않는 사람들과 더불어 집에 머무르니, 이는 아내를 그녀가 똑같이 질색하는 일에 붙잡아 두기 위함이다. 또한 여기서, 끊임없는 불안과 소란으로 점철된 삶을 함께한 남편의 유골 위에 과부가 때때로 그토록 펑펑 쏟아 내는 그 눈물도 흘러나올 수밖에 없으니, 이제는 그를 더 괴롭힐 길이 영영 사라졌기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 어떤 부부가 이 즐거움을 누린 적이 있다면, 지금 바로 대위와 그 부인이 그것을 겪고 있었다. 어느 한쪽에게는, 다른 한쪽이 앞서 그 반대를 주장했다는 사실만으로도 어떤 의견에든 고집을 부릴 충분한 이유가 되었다. 한쪽이 무슨 놀이를 제안하면 다른 쪽은 어김없이 반대했다. 두 사람은 같은 사람을 사랑하거나 미워하는 일도, 칭찬하거나 헐뜯는 일도 결코 없었다. 그리고 바로 이런 까닭에, 대위가 그 어린 업둥이를 못마땅한 눈으로 보았으므로, 이제 아내는 거의 제 자식과 다름없을 만큼 그 아이를 어루만지기 시작했다.\n\n독자는 부부 사이의 이러한 행실이 올워디 씨의 평온에 그다지 보탬이 되지 않았으리라 짐작하기 쉬울 것이다. 그가 이 혼인으로 세 사람 모두에게 마련해 주려 했던 그 잔잔한 행복에 거의 이바지하지 못했으니 말이다. 그러나 사실인즉, 그가 부푼 기대에서 조금 실망했을지언정, 그 일의 전말을 죄다 알고 있었던 것과는 거리가 멀었다. 대위는 몇 가지 뻔한 이유로 그 앞에서 무던히도 몸을 사렸고, 아내 또한 오라비의 노여움이 두려워 같은 처신을 따를 수밖에 없었기 때문이다. 실로, 웬만큼이라도 분별을 갖춘 부부와 더불어 제삼자가 아주 가깝게 지내며, 아니 같은 집에서 오래 산다 해도, 그들이 서로에게 품은 그 시큰둥한 감정을 짐작조차 못 하는 일은 얼마든지 가능하다. 하루가 통째로 증오에는, 사랑에 그렇듯이, 때로 너무 짧다 한들, 아무도 보는 이 없이 둘이 자연스레 함께 보내는 그 숱한 시간은, 웬만큼 절제할 줄 아는 사람들에게 둘 중 어느 감정이든 누릴 넉넉한 기회를 마련해 주기에, 그들이 사랑한다면 몇 시간쯤 시시덕거리지 않고도 함께 있을 수 있고, 미워한다면 서로의 얼굴에 침을 뱉지 않고도 함께 있을 수 있는 것이다.\n\n그렇긴 해도, 올워디 씨가 자신을 조금쯤 불편하게 할 만큼은 보았을 수도 있다. 우리는 현명한 사람이 어린아이 같거나 여린 기질의 사람들처럼 소리쳐 울며 신세 한탄을 하지 않는다 해서, 그가 상처받지 않았다고 늘 단정해서는 안 되니까. 그러나 실로 그는 아무런 불편함 없이도 대위의 어떤 허물을 보았을 수 있다. 참된 지혜와 선함을 갖춘 사람들은 사람이든 사물이든 있는 그대로 받아들이는 데 만족하며, 그 결함을 두고 불평하거나 그것을 고치려 들지 않기 때문이다. 그들은 벗이나 친척, 또는 아는 이의 허물을 보고도 그 당사자에게든 다른 누구에게든 결코 그것을 입에 올리지 않으며, 그러면서도 흔히 애정이 줄어드는 일도 없다. 실로 빼어난 분별력이 이 못 본 척 넘기는 성향으로 다스려지지 않는 한, 우리는 우리가 속여 넘길 만큼의 어리석음을 지닌 사람과나 우정을 맺어야 할 것이다. 내 벗들이 부디 용서해 주기를 바라며 나는 분명히 말하건대, 나는 그들 가운데 허물 없는 이를 하나도 알지 못한다. 또한 내 허물을 보지 못할 벗이 내게 있다고 여겨야 한다면 나는 서글플 것이다. 이런 종류의 용서를 우리는 번갈아 주고받는다. 그것은 우정의 한 행함이요, 어쩌면 그중에서도 가장 즐거운 축에 든다. 그리고 이 용서를 우리는 고치라는 바람 없이 베풀어야 한다. 우리가 사랑하는 이들의 타고난 약점을 고쳐 보려는 시도보다 더 확실한 어리석음의 표시는 아마 없을 것이다. 인간 본성의 가장 빼어난 됨됨이도, 가장 빼어난 도자기와 마찬가지로 흠이 하나쯤 있을 수 있고, 안타깝게도 이것은 어느 쪽이든 똑같이 고칠 수 없는 법이다. 그럼에도 그 본은 여전히 더없이 값진 채로 남을 수 있지만 말이다.\n\n요컨대 그렇다면, 올워디 씨는 분명 대위에게서 몇 가지 결함을 보았다. 그러나 대위는 워낙 교활한 사람이라 그 앞에서 끝없이 몸을 사렸으므로, 그 결함들은 올워디 씨에게 한낱 훌륭한 인품에 박힌 티끌쯤으로만 비쳤고, 그의 선함은 그것을 못 본 척 넘기게 했으며, 그의 지혜는 그것을 대위 본인에게 들춰내지 못하게 막았다. 만일 그가 전말을 죄다 알아챘더라면 그의 감정은 사뭇 달랐을 것이다. 그리고 만일 부부가 서로에 대한 이런 식의 행실을 오래도록 이어 갔더라면, 어쩌면 머지않아 그리되었을 터다. 그러나 그런 친절을 운명은 효과적으로 막아 버렸으니, 대위로 하여금 그를 다시금 아내에게 사랑스러운 존재로 만들고, 그를 향한 그녀의 모든 다정함과 애정을 되살려 놓을 어떤 일을 하도록 떠밀었던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n아내의 잃어버린 애정을 되찾는 처방, 가장 절망적인 경우에도 한 번도 실패한 적이 없다고 알려진 비법.\n\n대위는 아내와의 대화 속에서 보낸 그 불쾌한 순간들을 — 그는 그 시간을 가능한 한 짧게 줄이려 애썼다 — 혼자 있을 때 즐기는 흐뭇한 사색으로 넉넉히 보상받았다.\n\n이 사색은 오로지 올워디 씨의 재산에만 쏠려 있었다. 우선 그는 그 전체의 정확한 가치를 가능한 한 정밀하게 셈하느라 적잖은 생각을 쏟았는데, 이 계산은 종종 그 자신에게 유리한 쪽으로 고칠 구실을 발견하곤 했다. 그리고 둘째로, 또 무엇보다도, 그는 저택과 정원을 어떻게 바꿀지 마음속으로 그려 보며, 그 영지의 가치를 높이는 것은 물론 그 장소의 웅장함을 더할 여러 계획을 세우는 일로 스스로를 즐겁게 했다. 이를 위해 그는 건축과 조경 공부에 매달려, 이 두 주제를 다룬 책을 여러 권 통독했다. 사실 이 학문들은 그의 시간을 통째로 차지했고, 그의 유일한 소일거리가 되었다. 그는 마침내 더없이 훌륭한 설계도를 완성했다. 그러나 우리로서는 그것을 독자에게 보여 드릴 능력이 없으니 참으로 안타까울 뿐이다. 내 생각으로는 지금 이 시대의 사치조차 거기에는 좀처럼 견주지 못할 것이기 때문이다. 그 설계도는 실로, 이런 종류의 위대하고 고귀한 구상을 추천할 만하게 만드는 두 가지 핵심 요소를 더할 나위 없이 갖추고 있었다. 곧 그것을 실행하려면 엄청난 비용이 들었고, 어느 정도라도 완성에 이르게 하려면 까마득한 세월이 필요했다. 이 가운데 앞의 것은, 대위가 올워디 씨가 지녔다고 짐작하며 또 자신이 틀림없이 물려받으리라 여긴 그 막대한 재산이 아주 효과적으로 채워 줄 듯했다. 그리고 뒤의 것은, 그 자신의 튼튼한 체질과, 이른바 중년이라 불리는 정도에 지나지 않는 그의 나이가, 그것을 다 이루기 전에 죽지 않을까 하는 모든 염려를 거두어 주었다.\n\n이 계획을 당장이라도 실행에 옮길 수 있도록 하는 데 필요한 것은 오직 올워디 씨의 죽음 하나뿐이었다. 그는 이를 계산하느라 자신의 대수학을 적잖이 동원했고, 그 밖에도 수명과 상속권의 가치 따위를 다룬 책이라면 시중에 나와 있는 것을 모조리 사들였다. 이 모든 것을 통해 그는 스스로 이렇게 만족스럽게 결론지었다. 그 일이 일어날 가능성이 날마다 있으니, 몇 해 안에 그것이 일어날 확률 또한 절반을 넘는다고 말이다.\n\n그러나 어느 날 대위가 이런 종류의 깊은 명상에 잠겨 있던 바로 그때, 그에게 더없이 불운하고도 더없이 때를 잘못 만난 사고가 닥쳤다. 실로 운명이 아무리 짓궂은 악의를 짜낸들, 이토록 잔인하고, 이토록 엉뚱하며, 그의 모든 계획을 이토록 송두리째 무너뜨릴 일을 꾸며 내지는 못했을 것이다. 요컨대, 독자를 오래 애태우지 않고 말하자면, 올워디 씨의 죽음으로 자신에게 굴러들 행복을 떠올리며 그의 가슴이 한껏 우쭐대던 바로 그 순간에, 정작 그 자신이 — 뇌졸중으로 죽고 말았다.\n\n이 불행은 대위가 홀로 저녁 산책을 하고 있을 때 닥쳤다. 그래서 곁에는 그를 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없었다. 설령 어떤 도움이 그를 살릴 수 있었다 한들 말이다. 그리하여 그는 이제 자신의 모든 미래 계획에 꼭 맞는 만큼의 흙뙈기를 자기 몸으로 재고 말았으며, 땅바닥에 죽은 채 누워, 호라티우스의 다음 관찰이 참됨을 보여 주는 거대한 본보기가 되었다(살아 있는 본보기는 아니었지만).\n너는 장례를 코앞에 두고도\n\n대리석을 잘라 오게 하고, 무덤은\n까맣게 잊은 채 집을 짓는구나.\n\n이 뜻을 나는 영국 독자에게 이렇게 옮겨 드리겠다. \"너는 곡괭이와 삽 한 자루면 족할 때에 가장 고귀한 건축 자재를 마련하고, 가로 육 척에 세로 이 척짜리 집은 잊은 채 가로 백 척에 세로 오백 척짜리 집을 짓는구나.\"\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\n앞서 말한 처방의 틀림없음을 증명하는 장. 과부의 비탄과 함께, 의사들 따위 죽음에 어울리는 여러 장식들, 그리고 참된 문체로 쓴 비문을 곁들임.\n\n올워디 씨와 그의 누이, 그리고 또 한 부인이 여느 때처럼 저녁 식탁이 차려지는 방에 모여 있었다. 평소보다 한참을 더 기다린 끝에, 올워디 씨가 먼저 대위가 늦는 것이 슬슬 마음에 걸린다고 입을 열었다. 대위는 늘 식사 시간만큼은 누구보다 정확한 사람이었기 때문이다. 그는 문밖에 종을 울리라고, 특히 대위가 즐겨 거닐던 산책로 쪽을 향해 울리라고 일렀다.\n\n이 모든 부름이 아무 소용이 없자(대위는 짓궂은 우연으로 그날 저녁 새로운 산책로로 발길을 돌렸던 것이다), 블리필 부인은 정말 무서워졌다고 말했다. 그러자 그녀의 가장 가까운 친구 가운데 한 사람이자 그녀의 속마음을 잘 아는 또 한 부인이 온갖 말로 그녀를 달래려 애썼다. 물론 불안하지 않을 수야 없겠지만 그래도 가장 좋은 쪽으로 생각하시라고, 어쩌면 저녁 공기가 너무 좋아서 대위가 평소보다 멀리까지 걸었거나, 이웃집에 붙잡혀 있는 것인지도 모른다고 말이다. 블리필 부인은 아니라고, 분명 무슨 사고를 당한 것이라고 대답했다. 그이는 자기가 얼마나 마음 졸일지 잘 알기에, 한마디 전갈도 없이 밖에 머무를 사람이 결코 아니라는 것이었다. 더 댈 말이 없어진 그 부인은 이런 때 으레 쓰는 간청에 매달려, 부디 그렇게 스스로를 무섭게 몰아가지 마시라고, 그러다 정작 본인 건강을 크게 해칠 수 있다고 빌었다. 그러고는 포도주를 한 잔 가득 따라 권했고, 마침내 그녀로 하여금 그것을 마시게 했다.\n\n이제 올워디 씨가 거실로 돌아왔다. 그 자신이 직접 대위를 찾아 나섰던 터였다. 그의 얼굴빛은 그가 얼마나 큰 충격에 빠져 있는지를 충분히 보여 주었고, 실로 그 충격은 그에게서 말까지 거의 앗아 가 버렸다. 그러나 슬픔이 사람마다 다르게 작용하듯, 그의 목소리를 짓눌렀던 바로 그 두려움이 블리필 부인의 목소리는 오히려 높여 놓았다. 그녀는 이제 더없이 쓰라린 말로 스스로를 한탄하기 시작했고, 그 비탄에는 눈물의 홍수가 뒤따랐다. 그녀의 동무인 부인은 그것을 나무랄 수는 없다면서도, 동시에 너무 그러지 말라고 만류했다. 그러면서 인간의 삶이 날마다 겪는 숱한 좌절에 관한 철학적인 견해로 친구의 슬픔을 누그러뜨리려 했다. 그런 좌절들을 헤아려 두는 것이야말로, 아무리 갑작스럽고 끔찍한 일이 닥치더라도 우리 마음을 단단히 지켜 줄 충분한 근거가 된다는 것이었다. 그녀는 오라버니의 본을 보고 인내를 배워야 한다고 말했다. 오라버니가 부인만큼 깊이 마음 쓸 처지는 아니라 하더라도 분명 몹시 괴로워하고 있으며, 다만 하느님의 뜻에 순종하는 마음이 그의 슬픔을 마땅한 한도 안에 붙들어 두고 있을 뿐이라고 했다.\n\n\"제 오라버니 얘기는 꺼내지 마세요.\" 블리필 부인이 말했다. \"지금 가엾이 여겨야 할 사람은 오직 저뿐이에요. 이런 일에 아내가 느끼는 것에 비하면, 우정의 두려움 따위가 다 무어란 말인가요? 오, 그이는 죽었어요! 누군가 그이를 죽인 거예요. 저는 다시는 그이를 볼 수 없을 거예요!\" 여기서 눈물이 한바탕 쏟아져, 올워디 씨에게는 말을 억누른 것이 가져온 것과 똑같은 결과를 그녀에게 안겨, 그녀는 입을 다물고 말았다.\n\n바로 그때 하인 하나가 숨이 턱에 차서 뛰어 들어와 외쳤다. 대위를 찾았다는 것이었다. 그가 더 말을 잇기도 전에, 둘이서 시신을 마주 들고 옮기는 하인 둘이 그 뒤를 따라 들어왔다.\n\n여기서 호기심 많은 독자는 슬픔이 작용하는 또 다른 차이를 눈여겨볼 수 있을 것이다. 누이를 그토록 큰 소리로 울게 만든 바로 그 원인이 올워디 씨는 앞서 입을 다물게 했듯이, 이번에는 그 신사에게서 눈물을 자아낸 그 광경이 부인의 눈물은 뚝 멈추게 했다. 그녀는 먼저 날카로운 비명을 한 번 지르더니, 곧이어 발작을 일으키며 쓰러졌다.\n\n방은 이내 하인들로 가득 찼다. 그 가운데 일부는 방문한 부인과 함께 아내를 돌보는 데 매달렸고, 나머지는 올워디 씨와 함께 대위를 따뜻한 침상으로 옮기는 일을 거들었다. 거기서 그를 되살리려고 온갖 방법이 시도되었다.\n\n이 두 몸이 똑같은 성과를 거두었다고 독자에게 알릴 수 있다면 기쁘겠다. 부인을 돌본 이들은 큰 성공을 거두어, 발작이 점잖게 적당한 시간 동안 이어진 뒤에 그녀가 다시 깨어났고, 그들은 크게 만족했다. 그러나 대위에 관해서는 피를 빼고, 문지르고, 약을 떨구는 등 온갖 시도가 다 헛수고였다. 죽음이라는 그 가차 없는 재판관이 이미 그에게 선고를 내려 형의 유예를 거절했으니, 한 순간에 동시에 도착해 똑같이 진료비를 받은 두 의사가 그의 변호인이었음에도 그러했다.\n\n이 두 의사를, 행여 악의적인 추측을 살까 하여, 우리는 Y 박사와 Z 박사라는 이름으로 구별하겠다. 그들이 그의 맥을 짚었는데, Y 박사는 오른팔을, Z 박사는 왼팔을 짚었다. 둘 다 그가 완전히 숨졌다는 데에는 의견을 같이했으나, 그 병이나 사인에 관해서는 견해가 갈렸다. Y 박사는 그가 뇌졸중으로 죽었다고 했고, Z 박사는 간질로 죽었다고 했다.\n\n여기서 두 학식 있는 사람 사이에 논쟁이 벌어졌고, 저마다 자기 견해의 근거를 펼쳐 놓았다. 그 근거들이 어찌나 힘이 팽팽했던지, 양쪽 모두 자기 생각을 굳히는 데에만 보탬이 되었을 뿐, 상대에게는 조금의 자국도 남기지 못했다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 거의 모든 의사에게는 저마다 아끼는 병이 하나씩 있어서, 인간의 몸을 이긴 모든 승리를 그 병의 탓으로 돌린다. 통풍, 류머티즘, 결석, 요사, 폐병에는 모두 의료계에 저마다의 후원자가 있는데, 그중에서도 신경열, 곧 정신에 깃드는 열만큼 후원자가 많은 병은 없다. 바로 여기서, 환자의 사인을 두고 그 학식 높은 대가들 사이에서 이따금 벌어지는 그 의견 불일치를 설명할 수 있다. 우리가 위에서 말한 사실을 몰랐던 세상 사람들은 그런 불일치에 크게 놀라곤 했던 것이다.\n\n독자는 어쩌면, 환자를 되살리려 애쓰기는커녕 이 학식 있는 신사들이 곧장 사인을 두고 논쟁에 빠져든 것이 의아할지도 모른다. 그러나 사실 그런 온갖 처치는 그들이 도착하기 전에 이미 다 행해져 있었다. 대위는 따뜻한 침상에 뉘여졌고, 혈관을 칼로 째어 피를 뺐으며, 이마를 문질렀고, 온갖 독한 물약을 그의 입술과 콧구멍에 발랐던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 의사들은 자기들이 지시할 만한 모든 것이 이미 앞질러 행해진 것을 알고는, 진료비를 받기 위해 머무는 것이 으레 점잖게 여겨지는 그 얼마간의 시간을 어찌 메워야 할지 난감해졌다. 그래서 무엇이든 이야깃거리를 찾을 수밖에 없었으니, 앞서 말한 그 주제보다 더 자연스레 떠오를 것이 무엇이었겠는가?\n\n우리의 의사들이 막 작별을 고하려던 참에, 대위를 단념하고 하느님의 뜻에 따른 올워디 씨가 누이의 안부를 묻기 시작하더니, 떠나기 전에 그녀를 한 번 살펴봐 달라고 청했다.\n\n이 부인은 이제 발작에서 깨어나, 흔히 쓰는 말마따나 그녀의 처지에서 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 회복되어 있었다. 그리하여 의사들은, 새 환자인 만큼 앞서의 모든 격식을 다 갖춘 뒤, 청에 따라 그녀를 돌보러 가서, 앞서 시신에게 했던 그대로 그녀의 양손을 각각 하나씩 잡았다.\n\n부인의 경우는 남편의 경우와 정반대 끝에 놓여 있었다. 남편은 의술의 어떤 도움도 받을 수 없는 지경이었던 반면, 그녀는 사실 아무런 도움도 필요치 않았던 것이다.\n\n세상에 떠도는 속된 견해만큼 부당한 것도 없으니, 그 견해는 의사들을 마치 죽음의 친구인 양 왜곡한다. 오히려 나는 이렇게 믿는다. 의술로 회복한 사람의 수를 그 의술의 희생자가 된 사람의 수와 견주어 본다면, 앞쪽이 오히려 뒤쪽을 웃돌 것이라고. 아니, 어떤 의사들은 이 점에서 어찌나 조심스러운지, 환자를 죽일 가능성을 피하려고 아예 모든 치료법을 삼가고, 득도 해도 끼치지 못할 것 외에는 아무것도 처방하지 않는다. 나는 이런 의사들 가운데 몇이 자못 진지하게 다음과 같은 금언을 내놓는 것을 들은 적이 있다. \"자연이 제 할 일을 하도록 내버려 두어야 하며, 의사는 그저 곁에 서서, 자연이 일을 잘 해낼 때 그 등을 두드리며 북돋아 주기만 하면 된다.\"\n\n이렇듯 우리 의사들은 죽음을 그리 반기지 않았기에 진료비 한 번만 받고 시신을 놓아주었다. 그러나 살아 있는 환자에게는 그만큼 싫증을 내지 않아서, 그녀의 경우에 대해서는 곧바로 의견을 모으고 자못 부지런히 처방에 매달렸다.\n\n부인이 처음에 자기 의사들로 하여금 그녀가 아프다고 믿게 만들었던 것이, 이제 그 보답으로 의사들이 그녀로 하여금 스스로 아프다고 믿게 만든 것인지 아닌지는 내가 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 꼬박 한 달 동안 병의 온갖 장식을 두른 채 지냈다. 그동안 그녀는 의사들의 방문을 받고, 간병인의 시중을 받았으며, 안부를 묻는 지인들의 전갈을 끊임없이 받았다.\n\n마침내 병과 지나친 슬픔에 어울리는 점잖은 기간이 다 지나자, 의사들은 물러났고, 부인은 손님을 맞기 시작했다. 다만 예전과 달라진 점이 있다면, 그녀가 제 몸과 얼굴에 둘러 입은 그 슬픔의 빛깔뿐이었다.\n\n대위는 이제 땅에 묻혔고, 어쩌면 이미 망각을 향해 한참을 나아갔을지도 모른다. 만일 올워디 씨의 우정이 다음과 같은 비문으로 그의 기억을 지켜 두려 애쓰지 않았더라면 말이다. 이 비문은 정직함만큼이나 큰 재능을 지녔으며 대위를 더없이 잘 알았던 한 사람이 지은 것이었다.\n\n# CAPTAIN JOHN BLIFIL.\n\n여기, 기쁜 부활을 기다리며, 한 사람의 육신이 잠들다.\n존 블리필 대위.\n\n# LONDON HAD THE HONOUR OF HIS BIRTH, OXFORD OF HIS EDUCATION.\n\n런던은 그가 태어난 영광을 누렸고, 옥스퍼드는 그가 배운 영광을 누렸다.\n그의 재능은 그의 직업과 그의 조국에 영예였으며, 그의 삶은 그의 종교와 인간 본성에 영예였다. 그는 효성스러운 아들이요, 다정한 남편이요, 자애로운 아버지요, 더없이 친절한 형제요, 진실한 벗이요, 독실한 그리스도교인이요, 선한 사람이었다.\n\n위로받을 길 없는 그의 과부가 이 돌을 세우니, 그의 덕과 그녀의 사랑을 기리는 비석이라.\n\n# BOOK III.\n\n토미 존스가 열네 살에 이른 때부터 열아홉 살에 이르기까지, 올워디 씨 집안에서 일어난 가장 기억할 만한 일들을 담음. 이 권에서 독자는 아이들의 교육에 관한 몇 가지 단서를 주워 모을 수 있을 것이다.\n\n거의 또는 전혀 아무것도 담지 않은 장.\n\n독자께서는 이 이야기 제2권의 첫머리에서, 우리가 이런 종류의 연대기에 기록할 만한 일이 아무것도 일어나지 않은 여러 큰 시간의 토막들을 건너뛰겠노라 넌지시 알려 드렸음을 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다.\n\n그렇게 하는 것은 우리 자신의 품위와 편안함을 헤아린 것일 뿐 아니라, 독자의 이로움과 이익을 헤아린 일이기도 하다. 이렇게 함으로써 우리는 독자가 즐거움도 소득도 없는 글을 읽느라 시간을 허비하는 것을 막아 드릴 뿐 아니라, 그런 모든 대목마다 독자가 지닌 그 놀라운 통찰력을 발휘할 기회를 드리는 셈이기 때문이다. 다시 말해 이 비어 있는 시간의 공간들을 독자 자신의 추측으로 채워 보게 하려는 것인데, 우리는 바로 이 목적을 위해 앞선 여러 쪽에서 독자가 그럴 자격을 갖추도록 미리 정성을 들여 두었다.\n\n예컨대, 올워디 씨가 처음에 벗을 잃고서 슬픔의 감정에 휩싸였으리라는 것을 모를 독자가 어디 있겠는가? 그런 일을 당했을 때, 가슴이 부싯돌로 빚어지지 않았거나 머리가 그만큼 단단한 재료로 이루어지지 않은 사람이라면 누구나 그러한 감정에 잠기는 법이다. 또한 철학과 종교가 시간이 흐르면서 이 슬픔을 누그러뜨리고 마침내 꺼뜨려 버렸다는 것을 모를 독자가 어디 있겠는가? 앞의 것은 슬픔의 어리석음과 헛됨을 가르치고, 뒤의 것은 그것을 옳지 못한 것으로 바로잡는 한편, 앞날의 희망과 확신을 일으켜 그것을 달래 준다. 그 덕에 굳건하고 신실한 마음을 가진 이는, 마치 긴 여행을 떠날 채비를 하는 사람을 대하듯, 임종을 맞은 벗과도 그에 못지않은 담담함으로 작별할 수 있으며, 실로 그를 다시 만나리라는 희망 또한 그에 못지않게 품을 수 있는 것이다.\n\n또한 분별 있는 독자라면 브리짓 블리필 부인에 대해서도 그리 크게 헤맬 까닭이 없을 것이다. 그녀가 슬픔이 몸 바깥으로 드러나야 하는 그 모든 기간 내내, 관습과 예법의 온갖 규칙을 가장 엄격하게 지키며 처신했음을, 그리하여 자기 얼굴빛의 변화를 옷차림의 갖가지 변화에 맞추어 갔음을 독자는 믿어도 좋다. 그녀의 옷이 상복에서 검은색으로, 검은색에서 회색으로, 회색에서 흰색으로 바뀌어 감에 따라, 그녀의 얼굴빛 또한 침울함에서 비통함으로, 비통함에서 서글픔으로, 서글픔에서 진지함으로 바뀌어 갔으니, 마침내 그녀가 예전의 평온함으로 돌아가도 좋다고 허락된 날이 올 때까지 그러하였다.\n\n우리가 이 두 사람을 언급한 것은, 다만 가장 낮은 등급의 독자에게 부과될 법한 과제의 본보기로서일 뿐이다. 비평에서 더 높은 학위를 받은 독자들에게는, 이보다 훨씬 높고 까다로운 판단력과 통찰력의 연습을 마땅히 기대할 만하다. 우리가 건너뛰는 편이 좋겠다고 여긴 그 모든 세월 동안, 우리의 가치 있는 인물의 집안에서 일어난 일들에 관하여, 그런 독자들이 주목할 만한 발견을 많이 해내리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다. 그 기간 안에 이 이야기에 자리를 차지할 만한 일이라곤 아무것도 일어나지 않았으되, 그래도 이 시대의 일간·주간 사료 기록자들이 보도하는 사건들 못지않게 중요한 사건들이 여럿 일어나기는 했으니 말이다. 수많은 사람이 그런 글을 읽느라 자기 시간의 상당 부분을 써 버리지만, 유감스럽게도 그들에게 돌아가는 소득은 거의 없는 듯하다. 자, 여기서 우리가 권하는 추측 속에서라면, 마음의 가장 뛰어난 능력 가운데 몇 가지를 크게 이롭게 부려 볼 수 있을 것이다. 어떤 상황에서든 사람들의 성격으로부터 그들의 행동을 미리 내다보는 능력은, 그들의 행동으로부터 그 성격을 판단하는 능력보다 더 쓸모 있는 재간이기 때문이다. 인정하건대 앞의 것이 더 큰 통찰력을 요구한다. 하지만 참된 명민함만 있다면 뒤의 것에 못지않은 확실함으로 이루어 낼 수 있다.\n\n우리 독자의 대부분이 이 자질을 대단히 두드러지게 갖추고 있음을 잘 알기에, 우리는 그것을 발휘하시라고 열두 해의 공간을 남겨 드렸다. 그리고 이제 우리의 주인공을 열네 살쯤 된 모습으로 내보내려 하는데, 많은 분이 그와 알고 지내게 되기를 오래도록 애타게 기다려 오셨으리라는 것을 우리는 조금도 의심하지 않는다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n이 위대한 이야기의 주인공이 매우 불길한 징조와 더불어 등장한다. 어떤 이들은 눈여겨볼 가치조차 없다고 여길 만큼 너무나 저속한 작은 일화 하나. 어느 시골 지주에 관한 한두 마디와, 사냥터지기와 학교 선생에 관한 좀 더 많은 이야기.\n\n이 이야기를 쓰려고 처음 자리에 앉았을 때 우리는 그 누구에게도 아첨하지 않고, 오직 진실의 인도를 따라 펜을 움직이기로 마음먹었다. 그러니 우리는 바라는 것보다 훨씬 불리한 방식으로 주인공을 무대에 올릴 수밖에 없으며, 그가 처음 등장하는 바로 그 순간조차 정직하게 밝혀야만 한다. 올워디 씨 댁의 모든 사람이 한결같이 품은 의견은, 이 아이가 틀림없이 교수대에 매달리려고 태어났다는 것이었다고.\n\n실로 유감스럽게도 이 추측에는 너무도 많은 근거가 있었음을 말해 두어야겠다. 이 소년은 아주 어린 시절부터 여러 악덕에 기우는 성향을 드러냈으며, 특히 우리가 방금 그에게 예언처럼 선고되었다고 말한 그 운명으로 곧장 이끄는, 다른 무엇 못지않게 직접적인 성향 하나를 보였던 것이다. 그는 이미 세 건의 도둑질로 유죄가 입증된 바 있었으니, 곧 과수원을 턴 것, 어느 농부의 마당에서 오리를 한 마리 훔친 것, 그리고 블리필 도련님의 주머니에서 공을 슬쩍한 것이었다.\n\n게다가 이 어린아이의 악덕은, 그의 동무인 블리필 도련님의 미덕과 견주어졌을 때 한층 불리한 빛 속에 도드라져 보였다. 블리필은 어린 존스와는 너무도 다른 부류의 소년이어서, 집안뿐 아니라 온 이웃이 그의 칭찬을 울려 퍼뜨렸다. 그는 참으로 남다른 기질의 소년이었다. 제 나이를 넘어설 만큼 차분하고 신중하며 경건했으니, 이런 자질들은 그를 아는 모든 이의 사랑을 얻게 했다. 반면 톰 존스는 누구에게나 미움을 받았으며, 많은 이들이 올워디 씨가 어찌하여 저런 아이를 자기 조카와 함께 교육받도록 내버려 두는지 의아해했다. 조카의 품행이 그 본보기 때문에 더럽혀질까 염려했던 것이다.\n\n이즈음에 일어난 한 사건은, 아무리 긴 논설로도 못할 만큼 이 두 소년의 성격을 분별 있는 독자 앞에 더 공정하게 펼쳐 보여 줄 것이다.\n\n톰 존스는, 비록 그 행실이 나쁘다 해도 어쨌든 이 이야기의 주인공 노릇을 해야 할 인물인데, 집안의 모든 하인 가운데 단 한 명의 벗밖에 없었다. 윌킨스 부인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 오래전에 그를 단념하고 제 여주인과 완전히 화해한 터였기 때문이다. 그 벗이란 바로 사냥터지기였으니, 그는 헐겁고 헤픈 기질의 사내로서, '내 것(meum)'과 '네 것(tuum)'의 구별에 관해 그 젊은 도련님 자신보다 별로 더 엄격한 관념을 품고 있지 않다고 여겨지는 자였다. 그리하여 이 우정은 하인들 사이에서 많은 빈정거림의 빌미가 되었는데, 그 대부분은 이미 속담이었거나, 적어도 이제는 속담이 되었다. 그리고 사실 그 모든 재담의 묘미는 짧은 라틴어 속담 하나에 다 담을 수 있으니, 곧 \"노스키투르 아 소키오(Noscitur a socio)\"다. 내 생각에 이것은 영어로 \"그가 어울리는 무리를 보면 그를 알 수 있다\"로 옮겨진다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 우리가 방금 세 가지 예를 든 존스의 그 끔찍한 못된 짓 가운데 얼마간은, 어쩌면 이 사내에게서 받은 부추김에서 비롯된 것일지도 모른다. 그는 두세 차례에 걸쳐, 법에서 이른바 사후 종범(從犯)이라 부르는 역할을 했던 것이다. 오리 한 마리는 통째로, 사과는 그 태반이 사냥터지기와 그 식구들의 몫으로 돌아갔으니 말이다. 그런데도 존스 혼자만 발각되었기에, 가엾은 소년은 온갖 매질뿐 아니라 온갖 비난까지 도맡았다. 그리고 그 둘은 다음과 같은 사건에서 또다시 그의 차지가 되었다.\n\n올워디 씨의 영지에 잇닿아, 이른바 사냥감 보호자라 불리는 신사들 가운데 한 사람의 영지가 있었다. 이런 부류의 사람들은 산토끼나 자고새의 죽음을 어찌나 가혹하게 앙갚음하는지, 인도의 바니안교도와 똑같은 미신을 떠받든다고 여겨질 만하다. 우리가 듣기로 그들 중 많은 이들은 어떤 동물들의 보존과 보호에 평생을 바친다는 것이다. 다만 우리 잉글랜드의 바니안교도들은 그 짐승들을 다른 적들로부터 지켜 주면서도, 정작 저희가 말 등에 가득 실을 만큼을 더없이 무자비하게 도살해 버린다는 점이 다를 뿐이다. 그러니 그들은 그런 이교도적 미신의 혐의에서만큼은 깨끗이 벗어난 셈이다.\n\n실로 나는 이런 부류의 사람들에 대해 어떤 이들이 품은 것보다 훨씬 나은 견해를 지니고 있다. 그들이야말로 자연의 질서와, 그들이 마련된 그 선한 목적에 다른 많은 이들보다 더 충실하게 부응한다고 보기 때문이다. 자, 호라티우스가 우리에게 일러 주기를, 인간들 가운데는 한 부류가 있어\n프루게스 콘수메레 나티(Fruges consumere nati),\n\n곧 \"땅의 열매를 소비하려고 태어난 자들\"이 있다 하니, 나는 또 다른 부류가 있음을 조금도 의심하지 않는다.\n페라스 콘수메레 나티(Feras consumere nati),\n\n곧 \"들판의 짐승을 소비하려고 태어난 자들\", 흔히 이르는 대로 사냥감을 소비하려 태어난 자들 말이다. 그리고 저 시골 지주들이야말로 그들이 창조된 이 목적을 충실히 이룬다는 데에는 아무도 토를 달지 않으리라 믿는다.\n\n어린 존스는 어느 날 사냥터지기와 함께 사냥을 나갔다. 그러다 자고새 떼를 그 영지의 경계 가까이에서 푸드덕 날려 보내게 되었는데, 그곳은 운명이 자연의 현명한 목적을 이루고자 사냥감 소비자 한 사람을 심어 둔 땅이었다. 새들은 그쪽으로 날아들었고, 두 사냥꾼은 올워디 씨 영토를 이삼백 보쯤 넘어선 어느 가시금작화 덤불 속에서 그 새들이 내려앉은 자리를 (이른바) 점찍어 두었다.\n\n올워디 씨는 그 사내에게, 자리를 잃는 벌을 무릅쓰더라도 결코 이웃의 어느 땅에도 함부로 들어가지 말라고 엄히 명해 두었다. 이 일에 덜 엄격한 자들의 땅이든, 이 영지의 주인 같은 자의 땅이든 가리지 말라는 것이었다. 실로 다른 이들에 관해서라면 이 명령이 늘 그렇게 꼼꼼히 지켜지지는 않았다. 그러나 자고새들이 피신처로 삼은 그 신사의 기질이 워낙 잘 알려져 있었기에, 사냥터지기는 그때껏 그의 영역을 침범하려 시도한 적이 한 번도 없었다. 이번에도 마찬가지였을 것이다. 날아가는 사냥감을 좇으려고 안달이 난 어린 사냥꾼이 그를 한사코 설득하지만 않았더라면 말이다. 그러나 존스가 어찌나 졸라 대던지, 저 역시 사냥에 충분히 열을 올리던 사내는 그 설득에 넘어가 영지에 들어갔고, 자고새 한 마리를 쏘아 잡았다.\n\n그 신사 본인이 마침 그때 그들에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에서 말을 타고 있었다. 그는 총소리를 듣자 곧장 그 자리로 향했고, 가엾은 톰을 찾아냈다. 사냥터지기는 가시금작화 덤불의 가장 빽빽한 속으로 뛰어들어 다행히 몸을 숨긴 터였기 때문이다.\n\n그 신사는 소년의 몸을 뒤져 자고새를 찾아내고는, 올워디 씨에게 알리겠노라 맹세하며 단단히 앙갚음하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 그는 제 말을 어김없이 지켰다. 곧장 말을 달려 올워디 씨 댁으로 가서, 마치 제 집이 부서져 열리고 가장 값진 가구들이 도둑맞기라도 한 것처럼, 더없이 거센 어조와 더없이 신랄한 말로 제 영지를 침범당한 일을 하소연한 것이다. 그는 덧붙이기를, 일행 가운데 또 다른 누군가가 있었으나 미처 찾아내지는 못했다고 했다. 거의 같은 순간에 총이 두 발 발사되었기 때문이라는 것이었다. 그러고는 이렇게 말했다. \"우리는 이 자고새 한 마리밖에 못 찾았소만, 그놈들이 무슨 못된 짓을 저질렀는지는 하느님만 아실 거요.\"\n\n집에 돌아오자 톰은 곧바로 올워디 씨 앞으로 불려 나왔다. 그는 그 일을 시인했고, 정말로 사실인 것 말고는 다른 어떤 변명도 늘어놓지 않았다. 곧 그 새 떼가 본디 올워디 씨 자신의 영지에서 날아오른 것이라는 사실이었다.\n\n이어 톰은 누구와 함께 있었느냐는 신문을 받았다. 올워디 씨는 기어이 알아내고야 말겠다고 선언하며, 그 지주와 그의 두 하인이 진술한 대로 총이 두 발 발사되었다는 정황을 이 죄인에게 일러 주었다. 그러나 톰은 자기 혼자였다고 한사코 우겨 댔다. 다만 사실대로 말하자면, 처음에 그는 잠깐 머뭇거렸으니, 만일 그 지주와 하인들이 한 말에 더 보탤 확증이 필요했더라면 이 머뭇거림이 올워디 씨의 믿음을 굳혀 주었을 것이다.\n\n이제 의심을 받던 사냥터지기가 불려 와 같은 질문을 받았다. 그러나 그는 모든 것을 제가 뒤집어쓰겠다던 톰의 약속을 믿고서, 그 젊은 도련님과 함께 있었다는 것은 물론, 실은 그날 오후 내내 그를 본 적조차 없다고 아주 단호하게 부인했다.\n\n그러자 올워디 씨는 평소보다 더한 노여움을 얼굴에 띤 채 톰 쪽으로 돌아서서, 누구와 함께 있었는지 실토하라고 타이르며 기어이 알아내고야 말겠다는 말을 거듭했다. 그러나 소년은 여전히 제 결심을 굽히지 않았고, 올워디 씨는 단단히 진노하여 그를 물러가게 했다. 그러면서 이튿날 아침까지 곰곰이 생각해 둘 시간을 주겠노라고, 그때는 다른 사람이 다른 방식으로 너를 신문하게 될 것이라고 일렀다.\n\n가엾은 존스는 몹시도 울적한 밤을 보냈다. 더욱이 늘 함께하던 동무마저 곁에 없었으니 그 울적함은 한층 더했다. 블리필 도련님은 어머니와 함께 어디론가 방문을 떠나 있었던 것이다. 이번 경우에 그가 받게 될 벌에 대한 두려움은 그에게 가장 작은 근심거리였다. 그의 가장 큰 걱정은, 혹여 제 굳건함이 무너져 사냥터지기를 배신하게 되지나 않을까 하는 것이었으니, 이제 그 결과가 그 사내의 파멸일 수밖에 없음을 알았기 때문이다.\n\n사냥터지기 또한 그보다 그리 나은 시간을 보내지는 못했다. 그는 소년과 똑같은 불안을 품고 있었으니, 제 가죽보다도 소년의 명예를 한결 더 애틋하게 여겼던 것이다.\n\n아침이 되어 톰이 트와컴 목사 앞에 나아갔을 때 — 그는 올워디 씨가 두 소년의 가르침을 맡긴 사람이었다 — 그 신사는 전날 저녁에 받았던 것과 똑같은 질문들을 그에게 던졌고, 톰은 똑같은 대답으로 응했다. 그 결과는 어찌나 혹독한 매질이었던지, 어쩌면 어떤 나라들에서 죄인에게서 자백을 쥐어짜 내는 그 고문에 거의 못지않았을 정도였다.\n\n톰은 굳은 결심으로 제 벌을 견뎌 냈다. 매를 한 대씩 칠 때마다 선생이 그에게 이래도 실토하지 않겠느냐고 물었지만, 그는 제 벗을 배신하거나 제가 한 약속을 어기느니 차라리 살가죽이 벗겨지는 편을 달게 받아들였다.\n\n이제 사냥터지기는 불안에서 놓여났고, 올워디 씨 자신도 톰의 고초를 마음에 걸려 하기 시작했다. 트와컴 씨가 그 소년에게서 제가 듣고 싶은 말을 끌어내지 못한 데 잔뜩 격분한 나머지 그 가혹함을 이 선량한 분의 본뜻을 훨씬 넘어서까지 몰고 갔다는 점은 차치하더라도, 올워디 씨는 이제 그 지주가 잘못 안 것이 아닐까 의심하기 시작했던 것이다. 그 지주의 지나친 열기와 노여움이 그럴 법하게 보이도록 만들었기 때문이다. 그리고 하인들이 제 주인의 말을 뒷받침하느라 한 진술에 관해서는, 그는 그것에 별로 무게를 두지 않았다. 자, 잔혹함과 부당함이란 올워디 씨로서는 단 한순간도 그 의식을 도저히 견뎌 낼 수 없는 두 관념이었으므로, 그는 톰을 불러들였다. 그러고는 다정하고 정겨운 훈계를 잔뜩 늘어놓은 끝에 이렇게 말했다. \"얘야, 내 의심이 너를 억울하게 했다는 것을 이제 확신하게 되었구나. 이 일로 네가 그토록 모질게 벌을 받았으니 참으로 안타깝다.\" 그러고는 마침내 그에게 보상으로 작은 망아지 한 마리를 주면서, 지나간 일에 대한 자신의 안타까움을 거듭 되뇌었다.\n\n이제 톰의 죄책감은 어떤 가혹함도 못 미칠 만큼 그의 얼굴을 후려쳤다. 그는 트와컴의 채찍질보다 올워디의 너그러움을 더 견디기 어려웠다. 두 눈에서 눈물이 터져 나왔고, 그는 무릎을 꿇으며 울부짖었다. \"오, 어르신, 어르신은 제게 너무 잘해 주십니다. 정말로 그러십니다. 정말이지 저는 그럴 자격이 없습니다.\" 그리고 바로 그 순간, 벅차오르는 가슴에 못 이겨 그는 비밀을 거의 털어놓을 뻔했다. 그러나 사냥터지기의 선한 수호신이 그에게, 그 가엾은 사내에게 어떤 결과가 닥칠지를 일깨워 주었고, 이 생각이 그의 입술을 봉해 버렸다.\n\n트와컴은 올워디가 그 소년에게 어떤 동정이나 친절도 베풀지 못하도록 설득하느라 갖은 애를 썼다. \"그 아이는 거짓을 끝까지 우겨 댔습니다\" 하고 말하면서, 두 번째 매질이라면 아마도 이 일의 진상을 밝혀낼 수 있을 것이라는 암시를 흘리기도 했다.\n\n그러나 올워디 씨는 그 시도에 동의하기를 단호히 거부했다. 그는 말하기를, 설령 그 아이가 죄가 있다 한들 진실을 숨긴 것만으로도 이미 충분히 고초를 겪었으며, 그렇게 한 데에는 잘못 짚은 명예의 관념 말고는 다른 어떤 동기도 있을 수 없었을 것이라 했다.\n\n\"명예라니요!\" 트와컴이 다소 발끈하며 외쳤다. \"그저 고집이요 외고집일 뿐입니다! 명예가 누구에게 거짓말을 하라고 가르칠 수 있단 말입니까, 또 종교와 동떨어진 채로 무슨 명예가 존재할 수 있단 말입니까?\"\n\n이 대화는 막 저녁 식사가 끝났을 무렵 식탁에서 오간 것이었다. 그 자리에는 올워디 씨와 트와컴 씨, 그리고 이제 이 논쟁에 끼어든 세 번째 신사가 함께 있었다. 우리는 더 나아가기에 앞서 그를 독자에게 간략히 소개해 두려 한다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n철학자 스퀘어 씨와 신학자 스왜컴 씨의 성격, 그리고 ――에 관한 논쟁.\n\n그 무렵 올워디 씨의 저택에 한동안 머물고 있던 이 신사의 이름은 스퀘어 씨였다. 타고난 재능은 일류라 할 만한 것은 아니었으나, 그는 학문적 교육으로 그것을 크게 갈고닦은 사람이었다. 그는 고대 저술가들을 깊이 읽었으며, 플라톤과 아리스토텔레스의 모든 저작에 통달했다고 자처했다. 그는 이 위대한 두 본보기를 바탕으로 주로 자신을 빚어 갔으니, 어떤 때는 한 사람의 의견에 따르고 또 어떤 때는 다른 사람의 의견에 따랐다. 도덕에서는 자처하는 플라톤주의자였고, 종교에서는 아리스토텔레스주의 쪽으로 기울었다.\n\n그러나 앞서 말했듯 그가 자신의 도덕을 플라톤의 본보기 위에 세웠음에도, 그 위대한 인물을 입법자라기보다는 차라리 철학자나 사변가의 자질로 여기는 점에서는 아리스토텔레스의 의견에 완전히 동의했다. 이러한 생각을 그는 꽤나 멀리까지 밀고 나갔으니, 실로 모든 덕을 이론의 문제일 뿐이라고 여기는 데까지 이르렀다. 사실 그가 이런 말을 누구에게 단언했다는 이야기는, 내가 들은 바로는 없다. 그렇지만 그의 행실을 조금만 눈여겨보면, 나는 그것이 그의 진짜 생각이었다고 여기지 않을 수 없다. 그래야만 그의 성격에서 달리 보면 모순으로 보일 법한 몇 가지가 말끔히 풀리기 때문이다.\n\n이 신사와 스왜컴 씨는 만나기만 하면 거의 빠짐없이 논쟁을 벌였다. 두 사람의 신조가 정말이지 정반대로 어긋나 있었기 때문이다. 스퀘어는 인간 본성이야말로 모든 덕의 완성이며, 악덕이란 몸의 기형이 그렇듯 우리 본성에서 벗어난 일탈이라고 주장했다. 반대로 스왜컴은, 인간의 정신이란 타락 이후로 은총에 의해 정화되고 구원받기 전까지는 죄악의 시궁창에 지나지 않는다고 고집했다. 두 사람이 의견을 같이하는 점은 단 하나뿐이었으니, 도덕을 두고 논할 때 결코 선함이라는 말을 입에 올리지 않는다는 것이었다. 전자가 즐겨 쓰는 표현은 덕의 자연스러운 아름다움이었고, 후자가 즐겨 쓰는 표현은 은총의 신성한 권능이었다. 전자는 모든 행위를 변치 않는 옳음의 규칙과 사물의 영원한 합당함으로 가늠했고, 후자는 모든 문제를 권위로 판가름했다. 다만 그렇게 할 때면 그는 늘 성경과 그 주석가들을 끌어다 썼으니, 마치 법률가가 자신의 《라이틀턴 주해 코크》를 인용할 때 주석이 본문과 똑같은 권위를 지니는 것과 같았다.\n\n이 짧은 소개를 마쳤으니, 독자는 그 목사가 자신의 말을 의기양양한 물음으로 끝맺었으며, 거기에 어떤 대답도 돌아오지 않으리라 짐작했다는 사실을 떠올려 주기 바란다. 그 물음이란 곧, 종교와 무관하게 명예라는 것이 존재할 수 있느냐는 것이었다.\n\n이에 스퀘어가 대답했다. 어떤 말이든 그 뜻이 먼저 정해지기 전에는 철학적으로 논할 수 없는데, 그가 입에 올린 두 단어만큼 막연하고 불확실한 의미를 지닌 말도 좀처럼 없다고 했다. 명예에 관한 견해가 종교에 관한 견해 못지않게 제각각이기 때문이라는 것이었다. \"하지만,\" 그가 말했다, \"만약 명예라는 말로 그대가 덕의 참된 자연스러운 아름다움을 뜻하는 것이라면, 나는 그것이 그 어떤 종교와도 무관하게 존재할 수 있다고 주장하겠소. 아니,\" 그가 덧붙였다, \"그대 자신도 그것이 단 하나를 뺀 모든 종교와 무관하게 존재할 수 있음을 인정할 것이오. 마호메트교도도, 유대인도, 세상에 있는 온갖 다른 교파를 지지하는 사람들도 다 그러할 것이오.\"\n\n스왜컴이 받아쳤다. 이는 참된 교회의 모든 적들이 으레 부리는 악의로 논쟁하는 짓이라고 말이다. 그는, 세상의 모든 무신론자와 이단자들이 할 수만 있다면 명예라는 것을 자기네의 터무니없는 오류와 저주받을 기만 속에 가두어 두려 들리라는 점을 의심치 않는다고 했다. \"하지만 명예가,\" 그가 말했다, \"그것에 관한 어리석은 견해가 많다고 해서 여러 갈래가 되는 것은 아니오. 종교 또한 세상에 갖가지 교파와 이단이 있다고 해서 여러 갈래가 되는 것은 아니오. 내가 종교를 입에 올릴 때 내가 뜻하는 것은 기독교요. 그저 기독교만이 아니라 프로테스탄트 종교이며, 그저 프로테스탄트 종교만이 아니라 잉글랜드 교회요. 그리고 내가 명예를 입에 올릴 때 내가 뜻하는 것은, 이 종교와 어긋나지 않을 뿐 아니라 이 종교에 의지하는, 또 다른 어떤 종교와도 양립하지 않고 의지하지도 않는 그런 신성한 은총의 양식이오. 그러니 내가 여기서 뜻하는 명예, 또 내가 뜻할 법한 명예라곤 그것뿐이라 여겼던 그 명예가 거짓을 떠받친다, 하물며 거짓을 명령한다고 말하는 것은, 차마 떠올리기조차 끔찍한 터무니없는 소리를 내뱉는 것이오.\"\n\n\"나는 일부러,\" 스퀘어가 말했다, \"내가 한 말에서 분명히 끌어낼 수 있다고 여긴 결론을 끄집어내지 않았소. 그런데 그대가 그것을 알아챘다면, 분명 그대는 거기에 답하려 들지 못했을 거요. 어쨌든 종교라는 항목은 접어 두고 말하자면, 그대가 한 말로 미루어 우리가 명예에 대해 서로 다른 관념을 지녔다는 것이 명백한 듯하오. 그렇지 않다면 어찌 우리가 그 풀이의 똑같은 말에서 의견이 갈리겠소? 나는 참된 명예와 참된 덕이 거의 같은 뜻의 말이며, 둘 다 변치 않는 옳음의 규칙과 사물의 영원한 합당함 위에 세워져 있다고 주장했소. 거짓은 그것과 절대로 어긋나고 반대되는 것이니, 참된 명예가 거짓을 떠받칠 수 없다는 것은 분명하오. 그러므로 이 점에서는 우리가 의견이 같다고 생각하오. 다만 이 명예가 종교 위에 세워져 있다고 말할 수는 있다 한들, 명예란 종교보다 앞서는 것이니, 만약 종교라는 말로 어떤 실정법을 뜻하는 것이라면――\"\n\n\"내가 동의할 줄 아시오,\" 스왜컴이 잔뜩 열을 올리며 대답했다, \"명예가 종교보다 앞선다고 주장하는 사람에게 말이오! 올워디 씨, 제가 동의했습니까――?\"\n\n그가 말을 이어 가려는데 올워디 씨가 끼어들어, 두 사람 다 자기 말뜻을 잘못 알아들었다고 아주 차갑게 일렀다. 자기는 참된 명예에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다는 것이었다. 그러나 두 논쟁자가 똑같이 달아오르고 있던 터라, 이제 또 다른 일이 벌어져 이 대화에 당장 종지부를 찍지 않았더라면, 올워디 씨도 그들을 쉽사리 가라앉히지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n4장.\n작가를 위한 부득이한 변명, 그리고 어쩌면 그것대로 변명이 필요할 어린아이다운 사건을 담은 장.\n\n더 나아가기에 앞서, 몇몇 독자의 열정이 그들을 빠뜨릴지도 모를 오해를 미리 바로잡아 두고자 한다. 나는 어느 누구에게도, 특히 덕이나 종교의 대의에 뜨겁게 헌신하는 이들에게는 결코 기꺼이 거슬리고 싶지 않기 때문이다.\n\n그러므로 바라건대, 어떤 사람도 내 뜻을 더없이 거칠게 곡해하거나 비틀어, 내가 인간 본성의 가장 위대한 완성—실로 인간의 마음을 정화하고 고귀하게 만들며, 인간을 짐승의 무리 위로 끌어올리는 그 유일한 것—을 조롱하려 든다고 그릇되이 전하지 말기를 바란다. 독자여, 나는 감히 이렇게 말하겠다(그리고 그대 자신이 더 훌륭한 사람일수록 그만큼 더 내 말을 믿으려 하리라). 나는 차라리 이 두 인물의 생각을 영원한 망각 속에 묻어 버릴지언정, 이 영광스러운 두 대의 가운데 어느 하나에도 해를 끼치고 싶지는 않았다.\n\n오히려 나는 그 대의에 봉사할 뜻으로, 그것을 거짓되이 자처하며 위선적으로 옹호하는 두 사람의 삶과 행적을 기록하는 일을 떠맡은 것이다. 배신하는 벗이야말로 가장 위험한 적이다. 그리고 나는 대담하게 말하겠다. 종교와 덕은 가장 재치 있는 난봉꾼이나 불신자가 던질 수 있는 그 어떤 것보다도, 위선자들로부터 더 큰 실질적 불명예를 입어 왔다고. 아니, 한 걸음 더 나아가, 이 둘은 순수한 상태에서는 마땅히 시민 사회의 끈이라 불리며 실로 가장 큰 축복이지만, 기만과 가식과 허세로 오염되고 부패할 때에는 시민 사회의 가장 나쁜 저주가 되어, 인간으로 하여금 제 동족에게 가장 잔혹한 해악을 저지르게 만들었다.\n\n실로 나는 이 조롱이 대체로 받아들여지리라는 것을 의심하지 않는다. 다만 내가 가장 염려하는 바는, 이 인물들의 입에서 참되고 올바른 생각이 적잖이 흘러나왔기에, 그 전체가 한데 뭉뚱그려져 내가 그 모두를 똑같이 조롱한다고 여겨지지나 않을까 하는 점이다. 그러니 독자는 부디 이렇게 헤아려 주기 바란다. 이 두 사람 중 어느 쪽도 바보는 아니었으니, 그릇된 원칙만을 품고 터무니없는 말만 늘어놓았다고 볼 수는 없다. 그렇다면 만일 내가 그들에게서 나쁜 것만을 골라냈다면, 그들의 인물 됨됨이에 얼마나 큰 부당함을 저질렀겠는가! 또 그들의 논변이 얼마나 끔찍하게 초라하고 불구처럼 보였겠는가!\n\n요컨대 여기서 드러나는 것은 종교나 덕이 아니라, 그것의 결핍이다. 스왝컴이 제 나름의 체계를 꾸리면서 덕을 지나치게 등한시하지 않았더라면, 또 스퀘어가 종교를 그리하지 않았더라면, 그리고 두 사람이 마음의 모든 천성적인 선함을 송두리째 내버리지 않았더라면, 그들은 이 이야기 속에서 결코 조롱의 대상으로 그려지지 않았을 것이다. 이제 우리는 그 이야기를 이어 가 보자.\n\n앞 장에서 말한 논쟁을 끝맺게 한 그 일이란, 다름 아닌 블리필 도련님과 톰 존스 사이의 다툼이었다. 그 결과로 전자는 코피를 흘리게 되었다. 블리필 도련님은 비록 나이는 더 어렸지만 몸집은 톰을 웃돌았는데도, 권투라는 고귀한 기술에서는 톰이 그보다 훨씬 뛰어났기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 톰은 그 소년과의 모든 충돌을 조심스레 피해 왔다. 토미 존스는 온갖 장난기에도 불구하고 남에게 해를 끼치지 않는 아이였으며 진심으로 블리필을 좋아했던 데다, 스왝컴 선생이 늘 블리필 편에 서는 후원자였으니 그것만으로도 그를 단념시키기에 충분했던 것이다.\n\n하지만 어느 작가가 잘 말했듯, 어느 누구도 매 순간 현명할 수는 없다. 그러니 한 소년이 그렇지 못하다 한들 이상할 것이 없다. 놀이를 하던 중 두 소년 사이에 다툼이 일어나자, 블리필 도련님이 톰을 거지 같은 사생아라고 불렀다. 그러자 다소 격한 기질을 지닌 톰은 곧바로, 앞서 우리가 떠올린 그 현상을 전자의 얼굴에 만들어 냈다.\n\n이제 블리필 도련님은 코에서 피를 흘리고 눈에서는 그 뒤를 쫓듯 눈물이 줄달음치는 모습으로, 자신의 외삼촌과 무시무시한 스왝컴 앞에 나타났다. 이 법정에서 톰을 상대로 폭행과 구타, 상해의 죄목이 즉각 제기되었다. 톰은 변명으로 다만 도발이 있었다는 점만을 내세웠는데, 그것이야말로 블리필 도련님이 쏙 빼놓은 사정이었다.\n\n실로 이 사정이 그의 기억에서 빠져나갔을 수도 있다. 답변에서 그는 자신이 그런 호칭을 쓴 적이 결코 없다고 단호히 우기며 이렇게 덧붙였으니 말이다. \"그런 못된 말이 제 입에서 나오다니, 하늘이 막아 주시기를!\"\n\n톰은, 비록 모든 법도에 어긋나는 일이었지만, 그 말이 있었다고 거듭 주장했다. 그러자 블리필 도련님이 말했다. \"당연하지. 거짓말 하나를 하는 사람은 또 다른 거짓말도 거리낌 없이 하는 법이니까. 만약 내가 너처럼 그런 못된 거짓말을 선생님께 했다면, 나는 부끄러워서 얼굴도 못 들 거야.\"\n\n\"무슨 거짓말 말이냐, 얘야?\" 스왝컴이 꽤나 다급하게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"저, 쟤가 자고새를 잡을 때 사냥에 아무도 같이 가지 않았다고 선생님께 말했잖아요. 그런데 쟤는 알아요.\" (여기서 그는 왈칵 눈물을 쏟았다.) \"네, 쟤는 알아요. 저한테 직접 털어놓았으니까요. 사냥터지기 검둥이 조지가 거기 있었다는 걸요. 아니, 쟤가 그랬어요—맞아, 너 그랬잖아—할 수 있으면 부인해 봐, 선생님이 너를 갈가리 찢어 놓는다 해도 진실을 털어놓지 않았을 거라고 말이야.\"\n\n이 말에 스왝컴의 눈에서 불꽃이 번뜩였고, 그는 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"오! 호! 이게 바로 네가 떠받드는 그 잘못된 명예라는 거지! 이놈이 바로 다시는 매를 맞아선 안 된다던 그 녀석이야!\" 그러나 올워디 씨는 좀 더 부드러운 낯빛으로 소년을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"이게 사실이냐, 얘야? 어쩌다 그렇게 고집스레 거짓을 우기게 되었느냐?\"\n\n톰이 말했다. 자기도 누구 못지않게 거짓말을 경멸하지만, 자신의 명예가 그리하도록 자신을 묶었다고 여겼다는 것이었다. 그 가엾은 사람을 숨겨 주겠다고 약속했기 때문이라 했다. 그리고 사냥터지기가 신사 양반의 영지로는 들어가지 말라고 자신에게 사정했는데도 끝내 자신의 설득에 따라 함께 가 준 처지였으니, 그 약속을 더더욱 지켜야 한다고 생각했노라고 했다. 그는 \"이것이 이 일의 진실 전부이며, 자기는 그것을 두고 맹세라도 하겠다\"고 말했다. 그러고는 올워디 씨에게 \"그 가엾은 사람의 가족을 불쌍히 여겨 달라\"고 몹시 간절히 빌며 끝맺었다. 더욱이 죄는 오직 자기 혼자 지었을 뿐이고, 그 사람은 한 일조차 몹시 힘겹게 설득당해 한 것이라 했다. \"정말입니다, 어르신.\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 한 말은 거짓말이라고 하기도 어렵습니다. 그 가엾은 사람은 이 일 전체에 아무 죄도 없으니까요. 새를 쫓아간 건 저 혼자였어야 했고, 아니, 처음엔 저 혼자 갔고, 그는 그저 더 큰 사달이 나지 않게 하려고 저를 따라온 것뿐입니다. 부디, 어르신, 저를 벌해 주십시오. 제 작은 말을 다시 빼앗아 가셔도 좋습니다. 하지만 제발, 어르신, 가엾은 조지만은 용서해 주십시오.\"\n\n올워디 씨는 잠시 머뭇거리다가 두 소년을 물러가게 하며, 앞으로는 좀 더 사이좋고 평화롭게 지내라고 타일렀다.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n두 소년에 관한 신학자와 철학자의 견해, 그리고 그 견해의 몇 가지 근거와 그 밖의 일들.\n\n어린 블리필이 이 비밀을 털어놓은 덕분에—더없이 굳은 신뢰 속에 자기에게 전해진 비밀이었지만—제 동무는 흠씬 매 맞는 일을 면했을 가능성이 크다. 코피를 터뜨린 잘못만으로도 스웨컴이 매질에 나설 충분한 빌미가 되었겠지만, 이제 그 잘못은 다른 사안을 헤아리는 가운데 완전히 묻혀 버렸기 때문이다. 그리고 이 사안에 관해 올워디 씨는, 그 아이가 벌이 아니라 오히려 상을 받아야 마땅하다고 생각한다는 뜻을 사사로이 밝혔다. 그리하여 스웨컴의 손은 전반적인 사면으로 묶이고 말았다.\n\n머릿속이 자작나무 회초리 생각으로 가득했던 스웨컴은, 이런 나약한—그리고 그가 감히 그렇게 부르겠노라 말했듯이—사악한 관대함을 큰 소리로 성토했다. 그런 죄에 대한 벌을 면제해 주는 것은 그 죄를 부추기는 짓이라고 그는 말했다. 그는 아이들을 매로 다스리는 일에 대해 장황하게 늘어놓았고, 솔로몬을 비롯한 여러 사람의 글귀를 숱하게 인용했는데, 그것들은 다른 책에 워낙 많이 실려 있으니 여기서는 굳이 옮기지 않겠다. 그러고 나서 그는 거짓말이라는 악덕으로 화제를 옮겼는데, 이 대목에서도 앞서의 주제 못지않게 박식했다.\n\n스퀘어는, 톰의 행동을 자기가 품은 완전한 덕의 관념과 어떻게든 조화시켜 보려 애썼으나 끝내 그럴 수 없었다고 말했다. 그는 그 행위에 언뜻 보기에 굳건한 용기 같은 무언가가 있음을 인정했다. 그러나 용기는 덕이요 거짓은 악덕이니, 둘은 결코 어울리거나 하나로 합쳐질 수 없다는 것이었다. 그는 덧붙이기를, 이 일이 어느 정도는 덕과 악덕을 뒤섞어 놓는 셈이므로, 그 점을 고려해 더 큰 매질을 가하는 편이 낫지 않을지 스웨컴 씨가 한번 헤아려 볼 만하다고 했다.\n\n이 두 학식 있는 사람이 존스를 나무라는 데 한뜻이었던 만큼, 블리필 도련님을 칭찬하는 데에서도 그에 못지않게 일치를 보였다. 진실을 환히 드러내는 일이야말로 신앙심 있는 모든 사람의 의무라고 목사는 단언했고, 철학자는 이것이 올바름의 규칙, 그리고 사물의 영원하고 변치 않는 합당함에 더없이 부합한다고 천명했다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 말도 올워디 씨에게는 거의 무게가 없었다. 그는 끝내 존스를 벌하라는 명령서에 서명하도록 설득당하지 않았다. 그의 가슴속에는, 그 소년이 지켜 낸 그 꺾이지 않는 의리와 훨씬 잘 들어맞는 무언가가 있었으니, 그것은 스웨컴의 신앙이나 스퀘어의 덕과 들어맞던 것보다 한결 잘 어울렸다. 그리하여 그는 앞의 신사에게, 지난 일을 두고 톰에게 손찌검을 하지 말라고 엄히 명했다. 그 훈장은 그 명령에 따를 수밖에 없었으나, 큰 마지못함이 없지 않았고, 저 아이는 분명 버릇이 잘못 들고 말 거라고 자주 투덜거렸다.\n\n사냥터지기를 향해서는 이 선한 분도 한층 매섭게 굴었다. 그는 곧장 그 가엾은 사내를 불러들여, 모진 말로 한참 나무란 끝에 품삯을 치러 주고는 일자리에서 내보냈다. 올워디 씨가 옳게 보았듯이, 자기 자신을 변명하려고 거짓을 저지르는 것과 남을 변명하려고 거짓을 저지르는 것 사이에는 큰 차이가 있기 때문이었다. 그는 또한 이 사내에게 가한 굽힐 줄 모르는 엄벌의 주된 동기로, 그가 비열하게도 톰 존스로 하여금 자기 대신 그토록 무거운 벌을 받게 내버려 두었으며, 마땅히 스스로 진실을 밝혀 그것을 막았어야 했다는 점을 들었다.\n\n이 이야기가 널리 알려지자, 그 일에서 두 소년이 보인 행동을 두고 많은 사람이 스퀘어나 스웨컴과는 다른 판단을 내렸다. 블리필 도련님은 대체로 비실비실 알랑대는 놈이니, 옹졸하고 좀스러운 자식이니 하는, 그와 비슷한 말로 불렸다. 반면 톰은 용감한 아이, 호탕한 녀석, 정직한 사내라는 칭호로 떠받들어졌다. 실로 블랙 조지를 대한 그의 행동은 하인들 모두의 호감을 크게 샀다. 그 사내는 전에는 누구에게나 미움받던 자였으나, 쫓겨나자마자 그만큼 누구에게나 동정을 받게 되었다. 그리고 톰 존스의 우정과 의협심은 그들 모두에게서 더없이 높은 갈채로 기려졌으며, 그들은 블리필의 어머니의 심기를 거스를 위험을 무릅쓰지 않는 선에서, 감히 할 수 있는 한 드러내 놓고 블리필 도련님을 욕했다. 그러나 이 모든 일에도 불구하고, 가엾은 톰은 살갗에 매를 맞아야 했다. 스웨컴이 앞서의 일을 두고는 팔을 휘두르는 일을 금지당했지만, 속담에도 있듯, 매를 들 핑계는 찾기 쉬운 법이니, 회초리 또한 찾기 쉬웠다. 사실 회초리를 찾지 못하는 것만이 스웨컴으로 하여금 가엾은 존스를 오래도록 매질하지 못하게 막을 유일한 길이었으리라.\n\n만일 이 훈장을 부추긴 것이 오로지 매질의 즐거움뿐이었다면, 블리필 도련님 역시 제 몫을 받았을 가능성이 크다. 그러나 올워디 씨가 두 아이를 차별하지 말라고 거듭 명했음에도, 스웨컴은 한 소년에게 그토록 매섭게, 아니 야만스럽기까지 했던 것만큼이나, 다른 소년에게는 다정하고 부드러웠다. 사실대로 말하자면, 블리필은 스승의 애정을 크게 사로잡았다. 한편으로는 늘 그 인품에 깊은 존경을 보인 까닭이었으나, 그보다 훨씬 더 큰 이유는 스승의 가르침을 받아들이는 그 단정한 공경심에 있었다. 그는 스승의 어구들을 외워 두었다가 자주 되뇌었고, 스승의 종교적 신조를 하나같이 옹호하기를 그토록 어린 나이에서는 놀라울 만큼 열성적으로 했으니, 그 점이 그 갸륵한 스승에게 그를 더없이 사랑스럽게 만들었다.\n\n반면 톰 존스는 겉으로 드러내는 존경의 표시에 모자랐을 뿐 아니라—스승이 다가올 때 모자를 벗거나 절을 하는 것을 잊기 일쑤였다—스승의 가르침이나 본보기 또한 도무지 마음에 두지 않았다. 그는 실로 생각 없고 들뜬 소년이라, 몸가짐에 진중함이 거의 없었고, 표정에는 그보다 더 없었다. 게다가 그 진지한 행실을 두고 제 동무를 아주 뻔뻔스럽고 무례하게 비웃곤 했다.\n\n스퀘어 씨도 앞의 소년을 더 아끼는 까닭은 같았다. 톰 존스는 이 신사가 이따금 허비하듯 그에게 들려주는 학식 있는 설교에도, 스웨컴의 설교에 보이던 것 이상의 관심을 두지 않았기 때문이다. 그는 한번은 감히 올바름의 규칙을 두고 농을 던졌고, 또 한번은 자기 아버지(올워디 씨가 그렇게 불리는 것을 허락했다) 같은 사람을 만들어 낼 수 있는 규칙이란 세상에 없을 거라고 말하기도 했다.\n\n이와 달리 블리필 도련님은 열여섯 나이에 이미, 이 상반된 두 사람의 마음을 동시에 사로잡을 만한 수완을 갖추고 있었다. 한 사람에게는 온통 신앙으로, 다른 한 사람에게는 온통 덕으로 대했다. 그리고 두 사람이 함께 있을 때는 깊이 입을 다물었는데, 둘 다 그것을 자기에게 유리하게, 또한 제 편으로 해석했다.\n\n블리필은 이 두 신사를 면전에서 추켜세우는 것으로 만족하지 않았다. 그는 등 뒤에서도 올워디에게 그들을 칭찬할 기회를 자주 만들었다. 외삼촌과 단둘이 있을 때, 외삼촌이 어떤 종교적이거나 덕스러운 생각을 칭찬하면(그런 말이 그에게서 끊임없이 나왔다), 그는 그것을 스웨컴이나 스퀘어에게서 받은 좋은 가르침 덕으로 돌리는 일을 거의 빠뜨리지 않았다. 외삼촌이 그런 모든 칭찬을 그 말이 가리키는 당사자들에게 그대로 옮긴다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 그것이 신학자에게나 철학자에게나 얼마나 큰 인상을 남기는지를 경험으로 알아냈다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이렇게 한 다리 건너 전해지는 아첨만큼 뿌리치기 어려운 아첨은 없으니 말이다.\n\n게다가 이 젊은 신사는, 자기 스승들을 향한 그 모든 찬사가 올워디 씨 자신에게 얼마나 지극히 흐뭇한 일인지를 곧 알아차렸다. 그것이 그가 세워 둔 그 독특한 교육 계획에 대한 칭찬을 그토록 우렁차게 울려 주었기 때문이다. 이 갸륵한 분은 우리네 공립학교의 불완전한 제도와, 그곳에서 소년들이 익히기 쉬운 숱한 악덕을 눈여겨본 끝에, 자기 조카와, 어느 모로는 양자처럼 거둔 다른 소년을 모두 자기 집에서 가르치기로 결심했었다. 그곳이라면 어느 공립학교나 대학에서든 피할 수 없이 노출될 그 타락의 위험에서 두 아이의 품행이 모두 벗어나리라 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 이 소년들을 사사로운 가정교사의 가르침에 맡기기로 정했을 때, 스웨컴 씨가 그 자리에 천거되었다. 올워디 씨가 그 식견을 매우 높이 사고 그 성실함을 크게 신뢰하던 어느 각별한 벗이 추천한 것이었다. 이 스웨컴은 어느 대학의 특별연구원으로, 거의 내내 그곳에 머물렀으며, 학식과 신앙과 단정한 몸가짐으로 큰 명성을 누리고 있었다. 올워디 씨의 벗이 그를 추천하게 된 것도 분명 이런 자질들 때문이었으리라. 다만 실은 이 벗이 스웨컴 집안에 어느 정도 신세를 진 바가 있었으니, 그 집안은 이 신사가 의회에서 대표하던 어느 자치구에서 가장 유력한 인사들이었다.\n\n스웨컴은 처음 도착했을 때 올워디에게 더없이 흡족했고, 실로 그에게 주어졌던 평판에 완벽하게 들어맞았다. 그러나 더 오래 사귀고 더 가까이 이야기를 나누면서, 이 갸륵한 분은 그 가정교사에게서 없었으면 싶은 결점들을 보게 되었다. 다만 그 결점들이 그의 장점들에 크게 가려지는 듯했으므로, 올워디 씨로서는 그를 내보낼 마음이 들지 않았다. 사실 그런 처사가 정당화되지도 않았을 것이다. 만일 독자가, 이 이야기 속에서 스웨컴이 독자에게 비치는 것과 똑같은 모습으로 올워디 씨에게도 비쳤으리라 여긴다면, 그것은 크나큰 오해다. 또 만일 독자가, 자신이 그 신학자와 아무리 가깝게 사귀었던들 우리가 영감을 통해 능히 드러내고 밝혀낼 수 있는 그런 것들까지 알게 되었으리라 상상한다면, 그것도 똑같이 잘못된 생각이다. 이런 식의 억측에서 올워디 씨의 지혜나 통찰을 나무라는 독자들을 두고, 나는 서슴없이 이렇게 말하겠다. 그들은 우리가 그들에게 전해 준 그 지식을 아주 나쁘고 배은망덕하게 쓰고 있는 것이라고.\n\n스웨컴의 교리에 드러난 이런 결함들은, 우리의 선한 분이 못지않게 보고 또 나무랐던 스퀘어의 교리 속 상반된 결함들을 크게 덜어 주는 구실을 했다. 그는 실로, 이 두 신사의 서로 다른 지나침이 그들의 서로 다른 불완전함을 바로잡아 주리라 생각했고, 또한 두 사람 모두에게서, 특히 자신의 도움이 더해진다면, 두 소년이 참된 신앙과 덕의 가르침을 넉넉히 얻어 가리라 여겼다. 만일 그 결과가 그의 기대와 어긋나게 나타났다면, 그것은 어쩌면 그 계획 자체에 어떤 흠이 있었던 탓일 것이다. 그 흠이 무엇인지는, 독자가 찾아낼 수 있다면 찾아내도 좋다. 우리는 이 이야기에 어떤 흠 없는 인물을 들여놓는 척하지 않으니, 여기에서는 일찍이 인간 본성에서 보인 적 없는 것이 하나도 발견되지 않기를 바라는 바이다.\n\n그러니 본론으로 돌아가자. 앞서 적은 두 소년의 서로 다른 행동이, 독자가 이미 그 한 사례를 본 그 서로 다른 결과를 낳았다 한들, 독자는 그리 놀라지 않으리라 생각한다. 그리고 이것 말고도, 철학자와 훈장의 행동에는 또 다른 까닭이 있었다. 그러나 이는 매우 중요한 사안이니, 다음 장에서 밝히기로 하겠다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n앞에서 다룬 그 두 학식 있는 인물이 이 이야기의 무대에 최근 제법 비중 있게 등장했다는 사실은 이미 알려진 바와 같다. 그런데 그들은 올워디 씨의 집에 처음 도착한 순간부터, 한 사람은 그의 덕을, 다른 한 사람은 그의 신앙심을 어찌나 깊이 사모하게 되었던지, 그와 더없이 긴밀한 결속을 맺으리라 마음속으로 궁리해 왔다.\n\n이를 위해 그들은 어느 아름다운 미망인에게 눈길을 두었다. 한동안 그녀를 입에 올리지 않았으나, 독자께서는 부디 잊지 않으셨으리라 믿는다. 두 사람이 한결같이 흠모한 대상은 다름 아닌 블리필 부인이었다.\n\n올워디 씨의 집에서 우리가 기록해 온 네 사람 가운데 셋이, 미모로 크게 이름을 떨친 적도 없거니와 이제는 나이의 골짜기로 조금 접어든 한 부인에게 마음을 두었다는 것은 어쩌면 눈여겨볼 만한 일로 보일 수도 있다. 하지만 실상 가슴 깊이 사귄 벗이나 가까운 지인이란, 친구의 집에 있는 특정한 여인들에게 일종의 자연스러운 끌림을 품기 마련이다. 곧 부유하다면 그 친구의 할머니, 어머니, 누이, 딸, 고모, 조카딸, 사촌에게 끌리고, 미모가 빼어나다면 그 친구의 아내, 누이, 딸, 조카딸, 사촌, 정부, 혹은 하녀에게 끌리는 법이다.\n\n그렇지만 우리는 독자께서, 스왁컴과 스퀘어 같은 인물이 어떤 엄격한 도덕가들에게 다소 비난을 사 온 이런 종류의 일을, 철저히 따져 보지도 않고 그것이 (셰익스피어의 말마따나) \"양심에 걸리는 것\"인지 아닌지 헤아려 보지도 않은 채 덥석 벌이리라고는 상상하지 않으시기를 바란다. 스왁컴은 이웃의 누이를 탐하는 것은 어디에서도 금지되어 있지 않다는 점을 곱씹으며 이 일에 용기를 얻었다. 그는 모든 법을 해석할 때 \"명시된 것은 침묵하는 것을 멈추게 한다(Expressum facit cessare tacitum)\"라는 원칙이 있음을 알고 있었다. 그 뜻인즉, \"입법자가 자신의 뜻 전부를 분명히 적어 놓았을 때, 우리는 그가 우리 멋대로 바라는 바를 의미하게 만들 수 없다\"는 것이다. 이웃의 재물을 탐하지 말라 금하는 신의 율법에 몇몇 여인들이 거명되어 있으되 누이는 빠져 있으므로, 그는 그것이 적법하다고 결론지었다. 그리고 스퀘어로 말하자면, 그는 흔히 말하는 쾌활한 사내, 곧 미망인에게 어울리는 부류의 사람이었던 터라, 자신의 선택을 사물의 영원한 합당함과 어렵잖게 화해시켰다.\n\n그런데 이 두 신사는 미망인에게 자신을 좋게 보이게 할 기회라면 무엇이든 부지런히 붙잡았고, 그 확실한 방법 하나가 그녀의 아들을 다른 소년보다 늘 앞세워 주는 것이라 여겼다. 또한 올워디 씨가 그 다른 소년에게 보이는 친절과 애정이 그녀에게 몹시 거슬릴 것이라 짐작했으므로, 그 아이를 깎아내리고 욕보일 기회를 빠짐없이 붙잡는 것이야말로 그녀를 크게 흡족케 하리라 믿어 의심치 않았다. 그 아이를 미워하는 그녀라면, 그에게 해를 끼치는 자라면 누구든 사랑할 수밖에 없을 테니 말이다. 이 점에서는 스왁컴이 유리했다. 스퀘어가 가엾은 소년의 평판에 생채기를 낼 수 있을 뿐이라면, 그는 아이의 살가죽을 벗길 수 있었으니 말이다. 실로 그는 아이에게 매질 한 번을 할 때마다 그것을 자신의 연인에게 바치는 찬사로 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 더없이 그럴듯하게 이 오래된 매질의 구절을 읊을 수 있었다. \"내가 너를 벌하는 것은 미움 때문이 아니라 사랑 때문이다(Castigo te non quod odio habeam, sed quod amem).\" 실로 그는 이 말을 입에 달고 살았으니, 옛말을 빌리자면 이보다 더 적절히 쓰인 적이 없을 만큼 손끝에 익히고 있었다.\n\n주로 이런 까닭에, 위에서 본 대로 두 신사는 두 소년에 관한 의견에서 한목소리를 냈다. 사실 이것은 그들이 어떤 점에서든 의견을 같이한 거의 유일한 경우였다. 원칙이 서로 다른 데다, 두 사람은 이미 오래전부터 서로의 속셈을 강하게 의심해 왔고, 적잖이 뿌리 깊은 증오로 서로를 미워하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 서로에 대한 적개심은 번갈아 거둔 성공으로 한층 부풀어 올랐다. 블리필 부인은 그들이 무엇을 노리는지를, 그들이 짐작하기 훨씬 전부터, 아니 그녀가 알아채기를 그들이 바라기도 훨씬 전부터 이미 꿰뚫고 있었기 때문이다. 그들은 그녀의 비위를 거슬러 올워디 씨에게 일러바칠까 두려워 매사 무척 조심스럽게 움직였다. 하지만 그런 두려움을 품을 까닭은 없었다. 그녀는 오직 자신만이 그 열매를 거두기로 작정한 그 연정을 자못 흡족해하고 있었던 것이다. 그녀가 스스로를 위해 작정한 열매란 다름 아닌 아첨과 구애였으니, 이를 위해 그녀는 두 사람을 번갈아, 그것도 오랫동안 똑같이 어르고 달랬다. 실은 그녀는 목사의 원칙 쪽으로 다소 마음이 기울어 있었으나, 스퀘어의 외양이 그녀의 눈에는 더 흡족했다. 그는 풍채 좋은 사내였던 반면, 그 훈장의 얼굴은 〈창부 일대기〉에서 브라이드웰 감화원의 여인들을 매질하는 모습으로 그려진 그 신사와 거의 빼닮았기 때문이다.\n\n블리필 부인이 결혼의 단맛에 물려 버린 것인지, 그 쓴맛에 신물이 난 것인지, 아니면 또 다른 무슨 까닭에서인지 나는 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 두 번째 청혼이라면 어느 것에도 끝내 귀를 기울이려 하지 않았다. 그렇지만 그녀는 마침내 스퀘어와 어찌나 친밀하게 어울렸던지, 짓궂은 입들이 그녀를 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 수군대기 시작했다. 하지만 그것은 부인을 위해서나, 올바름의 규칙과 사물의 합당함에 몹시 어긋나는 것이기에 우리는 믿지 않을 것이며, 따라서 그런 말로 우리의 지면을 더럽히지 않겠다. 다만 그 훈장이 여정의 끝에는 한 걸음도 다가서지 못한 채 매질만 계속했다는 것은 분명하다.\n\n실로 그는 큰 실수를 저질렀고, 그것을 스퀘어는 그 자신보다 훨씬 일찍 알아챘다. 블리필 부인은 (어쩌면 독자께서 진작 짐작하셨을지 모르나) 남편의 처신을 그다지 마뜩잖아했다. 아니, 솔직히 말하자면 그녀는 그를 철저히 미워했고, 끝내 그의 죽음에 이르러서야 그가 그녀의 애정과 조금이나마 화해할 수 있었다. 그러니 그녀가 그에게서 얻은 자식에게 그리 격렬한 애정을 두지 않았다 한들 크게 놀랄 일은 아니다. 사실 그녀에게 그런 정은 어찌나 적었던지, 아이가 갓난아기였을 적에 그녀는 아들을 거의 들여다보지도, 아랑곳하지도 않았다. 그런 까닭에 그녀는 다소 마지못해하다가도, 올워디 씨가 그 업둥이에게 쏟아붓는 온갖 은혜를 잠자코 받아들였다. 선량한 그는 그 아이를 제 친아들이라 불렀고 매사에 블리필 도련님과 완전히 똑같이 대했다. 블리필 부인의 이런 묵인을 이웃들과 집안 사람들은 오라비의 기분을 맞춰 주려는 양보의 표시로 여겼고, 스왁컴과 스퀘어를 비롯한 다른 모든 이들도 그녀가 속으로는 그 업둥이를 미워한다고 짐작했다. 아니, 그녀가 그에게 예의를 차릴수록 사람들은 그녀가 그를 더더욱 혐오하며, 그의 파멸을 위해 더욱 확실한 계략을 꾸미고 있으리라 믿었다. 그를 미워하는 것이 그녀에게 이익이라 여겼던 터라, 그렇지 않다고 그들을 납득시키기란 그녀로서는 무척 어려운 일이었다.\n\n스왁컴은 자신의 생각을 한층 굳혔으니, 이 매질을 적대시하던 올워디 씨가 집을 비웠을 때 그녀가 한두 번이 아니게 슬그머니 톰 존스에게 매질을 하도록 그를 부추겼기 때문이다. 반면 그녀는 어린 블리필에 관해서는 그런 명령을 내린 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그리고 이는 스퀘어마저 속아 넘어가게 했다. 실상 그녀는 분명 제 친아들을 미워하기는 했으나 — 아무리 괴이쩍게 보일지라도, 그녀가 그런 유일무이한 본보기는 아니라고 나는 확신한다 — 겉으로 온갖 순응을 보이면서도 속으로는 올워디 씨가 업둥이에게 베푸는 온갖 호의를 충분히 못마땅해하는 듯했다. 그녀는 오라비 등 뒤에서 이를 두고 자주 불평했고, 스왁컴과 스퀘어 앞에서 그를 몹시 날카롭게 나무랐다. 아니, 두 사람 사이에 자그마한 다툼, 흔히 말하는 토라짐이 생기면 올워디 본인의 면전에다 대고 그 말을 쏘아붙이기도 했다.\n\n그렇지만 톰이 자라나, 남자를 여자에게 크게 매력적으로 보이게 하는 그 호방한 기질의 낌새를 드러내자, 어릴 적 그에게 보였던 이 거부감은 차츰 누그러졌다. 그리하여 마침내 그녀는 제 친아들에게 품은 것보다 그에게 품은 애정이 훨씬 더 강하다는 것을 어찌나 뚜렷이 드러냈던지, 더는 그 마음을 오해할 수 없을 지경이 되었다. 그녀는 그를 자주 보고 싶어 안달했고, 그와 함께 있는 데서 어찌나 큰 만족과 기쁨을 드러냈던지, 그가 열여덟이 되기도 전에 그는 스퀘어와 스왁컴 두 사람 모두의 연적이 되어 있었다. 게다가 더 고약한 것은, 온 고장이 예전에 그녀가 스퀘어에게 보인 끌림을 두고 떠들어대던 만큼이나 이제는 톰을 향한 그녀의 끌림을 큰 소리로 입에 올리기 시작했다는 점이다. 이런 까닭에 그 철학자는 우리의 가엾은 주인공에게 도무지 풀 길 없는 증오를 품게 되었다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n지은이가 손수 무대에 모습을 드러내는 장.\n\n올워디 씨는 본디 무슨 일이든 불리한 쪽으로 성급하게 보는 사람이 아니었고, 세상의 입소문—이웃 모두의 귀에는 쩌렁쩌렁 울려도 정작 형제나 남편에게는 좀체 닿지 않는 그 소문—과도 거리가 먼 사람이었다. 그럼에도 블리필 부인이 톰에게 보이는 이 애정, 그리고 친아들보다 톰을 더 위하는 그 너무도 빤한 편애는, 그 소년에게는 더없이 큰 손해였다.\n\n올워디 씨의 마음속에는 깊은 연민이 자리잡고 있어서, 오직 정의라는 강철만이 그것을 눌러 이길 수 있을 뿐이었다. 어떤 점에서든 불행한 처지에 놓인 것만으로도—그 불행을 상쇄할 만한 잘못이 없는 한—이 선한 사람의 동정심을 그쪽으로 기울게 하고, 그의 우정과 자선을 끌어내기에 충분했다.\n\n그리하여 블리필 도련님이 다름 아닌 친어머니에게 철저히 미움을 받고 있음을(실제로 그러했다) 똑똑히 알아차리자, 올워디 씨는 오직 그 이유 하나만으로 그를 연민의 눈으로 바라보기 시작했다. 그리고 선하고 자애로운 마음속에서 연민이 어떤 결과를 낳는지는, 여기서 내가 독자 대부분에게 굳이 설명할 필요가 없으리라.\n\n이때부터 그는 소년에게서 보이는 온갖 미덕의 기미는 확대경으로 들여다보듯 크게 보았고, 그의 결점은 거꾸로 뒤집은 유리로 보아서 거의 눈에 띄지 않을 만큼 작게 보았다. 어쩌면 이런 일은 연민이라는 정다운 성정 덕에 칭찬받을 만한 것일지도 모른다. 그러나 그다음 단계만큼은 오직 인간 본성의 나약함으로만 변명할 수 있을 뿐이다. 블리필 부인이 톰에게 주는 그 편애를 알아차리자마자, 그 가엾은 소년은(아무 죄가 없는데도) 부인의 마음속에서 떠오르는 만큼 올워디 씨의 마음속에서는 가라앉기 시작했으니 말이다. 사실 이것만으로는 결코 존스를 그의 가슴에서 뿌리째 뽑아내지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 이는 소년에게 큰 해를 끼쳤고, 훗날 이 이야기 안에 담길 그 엄청난 사건들을 빚어낼 어떤 인상들을 받아들이도록 올워디 씨의 마음을 미리 길들여 놓았다. 그리고 솔직히 말하건대, 그 불행한 소년은 제 방종과 거침, 그리고 조심성 없음으로 그 일에 너무 많이 한몫을 거들고 말았다.\n\n이러한 일들의 몇몇 사례를 적어 나가면서, 우리가 제대로만 이해된다면, 앞으로 우리의 독자가 될 마음씨 고운 젊은이들에게 무척 쓸모 있는 교훈을 건넬 수 있을 것이다. 그들은 여기서 이런 것을 깨닫게 될 테니 말이다. 따뜻한 마음과 솔직한 성품은 비록 속으로는 큰 위안을 주고 제 안에 정직한 자긍심을 심어 줄지언정, 아아, 세상살이에서는 결코 제 몫을 해내지 못한다는 것을. 분별과 신중함은 아무리 훌륭한 사람에게도 반드시 필요하다. 그것은 실로 덕을 지키는 호위병과도 같아서, 그것이 없으면 덕은 결코 안전할 수 없다. 그대의 의도가, 아니 그대의 행동이 본래 선한 것만으로는 충분치 않다. 그것이 선하게 보이도록 마음을 써야 한다. 그대의 속이 아무리 아름다워도, 겉모습 또한 곱게 간수해야 한다. 이를 끊임없이 살피지 않으면, 악의와 시기가 어떻게든 그 겉을 새카맣게 칠해 놓을 것이고, 그리되면 올워디 같은 이의 명민함과 선량함으로도 그 너머를 꿰뚫어 속에 든 아름다움을 알아보지 못하게 된다. 나의 젊은 독자들이여, 이것을 그대들의 변치 않는 좌우명으로 삼으라. 어떤 사람도 분별의 규칙을 무시해도 좋을 만큼 선할 수는 없으며, 덕 자신조차도 예의와 품위라는 바깥의 장식으로 단장하지 않고서는 결코 아름다워 보이지 않는다는 것을. 그리고 이 가르침을, 나의 갸륵한 제자들이여, 마땅한 주의를 기울여 읽는다면, 뒤이은 페이지들에서 여러 사례로 충분히 뒷받침되고 있음을 발견하게 되리라 믿는다.\n\n코러스 삼아 이렇게 잠깐 무대에 나선 것을 용서해 주기 바란다. 사실 이것은 나 자신을 위해서다. 순결과 선함이 흔히 부딪혀 깨지는 그 바위들을 들춰 보이는 동안, 정작 내가 갸륵한 독자들에게 파멸로 이르는 길임을 보여 주려는 바로 그 수단을 도리어 권하는 것으로 오해받지 않으려는 것이다. 그리고 이 말은, 내 배우들 가운데 누구에게도 입을 떼게 할 수 없었던 터라, 나 스스로 직접 밝힐 수밖에 없었다.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n어린아이다운 한 사건. 그러나 그 안에서 톰 존스의 선량한 마음씨를 엿볼 수 있다.\n\n독자께서는 올워디 씨가 톰 존스에게 작은 말 한 마리를 주었던 일을 기억하실 것이다. 죄 없이 벌을 받았다고 여긴 그 일에 대한 일종의 위자료 같은 것이었다.\n\n톰은 이 말을 반년 남짓 데리고 있다가, 이웃 마을 장터로 끌고 가 팔아 버렸다.\n\n돌아온 톰에게 트와컴은 말을 판 돈을 어떻게 했느냐고 캐물었다. 톰은 말하지 않겠노라고 거리낌 없이 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"오호!\" 트와컴이 말했다. \"말을 안 하겠다고! 그렇다면 네 엉덩이에서 그걸 뽑아내 주마.\" 의심스러운 일이 생길 때마다 그가 진실을 캐내는 곳은 늘 그곳이었다.\n\n이제 톰은 하인의 등에 업혔고, 형 집행을 위한 모든 준비가 갖추어졌다. 그때 올워디 씨가 방으로 들어와 죄인에게 집행 유예를 내리고는, 그를 데리고 다른 방으로 들어갔다. 톰과 단둘이 남게 되자 그는 조금 전 트와컴이 물었던 것과 똑같은 질문을 던졌다.\n\n톰은 대답했다. 도리로 보아 어르신께는 아무것도 숨길 수 없으나, 그 폭군 같은 악당에게만큼은 몽둥이가 아니고서는 다른 어떤 대답도 결코 하지 않겠다고. 그 몽둥이로 머지않아 그가 저지른 온갖 잔혹한 짓을 죄다 갚아 주기를 바란다고.\n\n올워디 씨는 자기 스승을 두고 그처럼 무례하고 막된 말을 내뱉은 소년을 몹시 엄하게 꾸짖었으나, 복수하겠다는 마음을 드러낸 것에 대해서는 한층 더 호되게 나무랐다. 다시 그런 말이 입에서 나오는 것을 듣는다면 자신의 호의를 완전히 거두겠노라고 으름장을 놓았다. 자신은 결코 패륜아를 거두거나 벗으로 삼지 않겠다는 것이었다. 이런저런 말로 그는 톰에게서 약간의 뉘우침을 끌어냈으나, 그 소년의 마음은 그리 진실하지 못했다. 사실 톰은 그 훈장에게서 받은 따끔따끔한 은혜를 어떻게든 되갚아 줄 궁리를 하고 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 올워디 씨는 트와컴에 대한 원한을 후회한다는 말을 톰의 입에서 끌어냈고, 그런 다음 그 선량한 사람은 몇 마디 유익한 훈계를 덧붙인 뒤 이야기를 계속하라고 허락했다. 톰은 다음과 같이 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지, 어르신, 저는 이 세상 누구보다 어르신을 사랑하고 존경합니다. 제가 어르신께 얼마나 큰 신세를 졌는지 잘 알고 있으며, 만일 제 마음이 은혜를 모를 수 있는 그런 마음이라고 생각된다면 저는 제 자신을 혐오하고 말 것입니다. 어르신께서 주신 그 작은 말이 입을 열 수 있다면, 제가 그 선물을 얼마나 아꼈는지 분명히 어르신께 말해 줄 것입니다. 저는 그 말을 타는 것보다 먹이를 주는 일이 더 즐거웠으니까요. 정말로, 어르신, 그 말과 헤어지는 일은 제 가슴을 후벼 팠습니다. 그리고 제가 한 그 까닭이 아니었다면, 이 세상 어떤 이유로도 저는 그 말을 팔지 않았을 것입니다. 어르신, 만일 제 처지셨다면 어르신께서도 똑같이 하셨으리라 저는 확신합니다. 어르신만큼 남의 불행을 그토록 절절히 느끼시는 분은 일찍이 없었으니까요. 그런데, 어르신, 만일 그 불행의 원인이 바로 자기 자신이라고 생각하신다면 어떤 심정이시겠습니까? 정말로, 어르신, 그들의 비참함 같은 것은 어디에도 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"그들이라니, 누구의 비참함 말이냐?\" 올워디 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 말이냐?\"\n\n\"오, 어르신!\" 톰이 대답했다. \"어르신의 그 가엾은 사냥터지기 말입니다. 어르신께서 그를 내치신 뒤로, 그 많은 식구를 거느린 그가 추위와 굶주림의 온갖 고통 속에 죽어 가고 있었습니다. 저는 그 불쌍한 사람들이 헐벗고 굶주리는 꼴을 차마 볼 수가 없었습니다. 더구나 그 모든 고통의 원인이 바로 저였다는 것을 알면서요. 저는 견딜 수가 없었습니다, 어르신. 맹세코, 견딜 수가 없었습니다.\" (이 대목에서 톰의 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내렸고, 그는 이어서 말했다.) \"그들이 영영 무너지는 것을 막으려고, 저는 그토록 소중히 여기던 어르신의 선물과 헤어졌던 것입니다. 그들을 위해 말을 팔았고, 그 돈은 한 푼도 빠짐없이 그들에게 갔습니다.\"\n\n올워디 씨는 한동안 말없이 서 있었고, 입을 열기에 앞서 두 눈에서 눈물이 솟구쳤다. 그는 마침내 부드러운 꾸지람과 함께 톰을 돌려보내며, 앞으로는 곤경에 처한 이들을 직접 비상한 방법으로 구하려 들기보다 자신에게 의논하라고 일러 주었다.\n\n이 일은 그 뒤 트와컴과 스퀘어 사이에서 한참 논쟁거리가 되었다. 트와컴은 이것이 올워디 씨의 뜻을 정면으로 거스른 짓이라고 주장했다. 올워디 씨는 그 녀석의 불복종을 벌하려 했다는 것이었다. 그는 말하기를, 어떤 경우에는 세상이 자선이라 부르는 것이 자기 눈에는 전능하신 분의 뜻을 거스르는 것으로 보인다고 했다. 그분께서는 어떤 특정한 사람들을 멸망하도록 점지해 두셨다는 것이다. 그러니 이것 역시 마찬가지로 올워디 씨의 뜻을 거스르는 행동이라고 했고, 늘 그렇듯 자작나무 회초리를 진심으로 권하는 것으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n스퀘어는 그 반대편에서 강하게 논변했는데, 어쩌면 트와컴에게 맞서기 위해서였거나, 아니면 존스가 한 일을 무척 흡족해하는 듯 보이던 올워디 씨의 비위를 맞추기 위해서였을 것이다. 이 자리에서 그가 펼친 주장에 대해서는, 내 독자 대부분이 가엾은 존스를 위해 훨씬 더 훌륭한 변호인이 되어 주리라 확신하기에, 굳이 늘어놓는 것은 주제넘은 일이 될 것이다. 사실 그릇됨의 잣대로는 도무지 끌어낼 수 없었을 행동을 올바름의 잣대와 화해시키는 일은 그리 어렵지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\n9장.\n트와컴과 스퀘어의 논평과 더불어, 한층 더 흉악한 종류의 사건을 담은 이야기.\n\n나보다 지혜로 훨씬 더 명성이 높은 어떤 분이 일찍이 말씀하신 바 있다. 불행이란 좀처럼 혼자 오지 않는 법이라고. 이 말의 한 본보기는, 제 못된 짓거리 가운데 하나가 발각되는 불행을 겪은 사람들에게서 찾아볼 수 있으리라 믿는다. 그런 경우에는 한 번 발각이 시작되면 모든 것이 다 드러날 때까지 좀처럼 멈추지 않으니 말이다. 가엾은 톰에게도 꼭 그런 일이 일어났다. 말을 판 일로 막 용서를 받자마자, 얼마 전에 올워디 씨가 준 좋은 성경책 한 권을 팔아버린 사실이 드러난 것이다. 그 책을 판 돈 역시 같은 방식으로 써버린 터였다. 이 성경책은 블리필 도련님이 사들였는데, 이미 똑같은 자기 책을 한 권 가지고 있었음에도 그리한 것은 한편으로는 그 책에 대한 존중에서, 한편으로는 톰을 향한 우정에서 비롯된 일이었다. 성경책이 반값에 집안 밖으로 팔려 나가는 것을 못마땅하게 여겼기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 그 반값을 손수 치렀다. 그는 매우 신중한 소년이었고 돈을 어찌나 알뜰히 챙겼던지, 올워디 씨에게서 받은 돈은 거의 한 푼도 빠짐없이 모아두고 있었다.\n\n세상에는 자기 책이 아니면 어떤 책도 읽지 못하는 사람들이 있다고들 한다. 그런데 블리필 도련님은 그와 정반대였다. 이 성경책이 처음 제 손에 들어온 그때부터, 그는 다른 책은 결코 쓰지 않았다. 아니, 오히려 전에 자기 책을 읽던 것보다 훨씬 더 자주 이 책을 들여다보는 모습이 눈에 띄었다. 그런데 그가 어려운 구절을 풀어 달라고 트와컴에게 자주 청하다 보니, 그 책 여러 군데에 적혀 있던 톰의 이름을 그 양반이 그만 발견하고 만 것이다. 이 일로 추궁이 시작되었고, 블리필 도련님은 어쩔 수 없이 모든 사정을 털어놓아야 했다.\n\n트와컴은 자신이 신성모독이라 부르는 이런 종류의 죄가 벌을 받지 않고 넘어가서는 안 된다고 단단히 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그는 곧장 매질에 들어갔다. 그것으로도 모자라, 다음번 만남에서 올워디 씨에게 자기 눈에 비친 이 끔찍한 죄에 대해 일러바쳤다. 더없이 신랄한 말로 톰을 헐뜯으며, 그를 성전에서 쫓겨난 장사꾼들에 빗대기까지 했다.\n\n스퀘어는 이 일을 사뭇 다른 시각으로 보았다. 그는 책 한 권을 파는 데 다른 책을 파는 것보다 더 큰 죄가 있다고는 도무지 볼 수 없다고 했다. 성경책을 파는 일은 신의 법으로 보나 인간의 법으로 보나 엄연히 합법이며, 따라서 거기에 부적절한 구석이라곤 없다는 것이었다. 그는 트와컴에게, 이번 일로 그가 보인 그 대단한 노심초사를 보니 어느 무척 독실한 여인의 이야기가 떠오른다고 말했다. 그 여인은 순전히 종교를 향한 마음에서, 아는 어느 부인에게서 틸럿슨의 설교집을 훔쳤다는 것이었다.\n\n이 이야기에 목사의 얼굴로 엄청난 양의 피가 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그 얼굴이 본디 창백한 축도 아니었는데 말이다. 그는 잔뜩 열을 올리며 노여움에 차 받아치려던 참이었으나, 이 논쟁 자리에 함께 있던 블리필 부인이 끼어드는 바람에 그러지 못했다. 그 부인은 자신이 전적으로 스퀘어 씨 편이라고 밝혔다. 실로 그녀는 그의 의견을 떠받치며 매우 박식하게 논변을 펼쳤다. 그리고 끝으로, 만일 톰에게 잘못이 있다면 자기 아들도 똑같이 죄가 있다고 인정할 수밖에 없다고 말했다. 사는 자와 파는 자 사이에 아무런 차이도 볼 수 없으며, 둘 다 똑같이 성전에서 쫓겨나야 마땅하다는 것이었다.\n\n블리필 부인이 자기 의견을 밝히자 논쟁은 끝이 났다. 스퀘어는 굳이 말이 더 필요했더라도 승리감에 겨워 말문이 막힐 지경이었다. 그리고 앞서 말한 까닭들로 감히 그 부인의 비위를 거스를 엄두를 내지 못한 트와컴은 분노로 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 올워디 씨로 말하자면, 아이가 이미 벌을 받았으니 이 일에 대해 자기 견해를 내놓지는 않겠다고 했다. 그가 이 소년에게 화가 났는지 안 났는지는, 독자 여러분의 짐작에 맡길 수밖에 없겠다.\n\n이 일이 있고 얼마 지나지 않아, 웨스턴 지주(자고새가 잡혔던 영지의 주인 양반)가 사냥터지기를 상대로 비슷한 종류의 침해 행위를 걸어 소송을 제기했다. 이는 그 사내에게 더없이 불운한 일이었다. 그 자체로 그를 파멸로 몰아넣을 위협이었을 뿐 아니라, 실제로 올워디 씨가 그를 다시 거두어 주는 것을 가로막았기 때문이다. 어느 날 저녁 그 양반이 블리필 도련님과 어린 존스를 데리고 산책을 나갔을 때, 존스가 슬쩍 그를 블랙 조지의 거처로 이끌었다. 그곳에서는 그 가엾은 사내의 식구들, 곧 그의 아내와 아이들이 추위와 굶주림과 헐벗음이 인간을 짓누를 수 있는 온갖 비참함 속에 놓여 있었다. 존스에게서 받은 돈으로 말하자면, 이전의 빚이 거의 다 삼켜버린 터였다.\n\n이러한 광경이 올워디 씨의 마음을 움직이지 못할 리 없었다. 그는 곧바로 어미에게 1기니 금화 두 닢을 건네며, 그것으로 아이들에게 옷을 입히라고 일렀다. 가엾은 여인은 이 자비로움에 그만 눈물을 터뜨렸고, 그에게 고마움을 전하면서도 톰을 향한 감사의 마음을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀의 말로는, 톰이 오래도록 자기와 식구들을 굶주림에서 지켜 주었다는 것이었다. \"저희는,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그분의 자비가 베풀어 주신 것 말고는 한 입 먹을 것도 없었고, 이 가엾은 아이들도 몸에 걸칠 천 쪼가리 하나 없었답니다.\" 실로 톰은 말과 성경책 말고도, 잠옷이며 다른 물건들까지 이 곤궁한 식구들을 위해 내어주었던 것이다.\n\n집으로 돌아오는 길에, 톰은 이 사람들의 비참함과 블랙 조지 자신의 뉘우침을 드러내 보이려 온갖 말솜씨를 동원했다. 그리고 이 일에 어찌나 잘 성공했던지, 올워디 씨는 그 사내가 지난 일에 대해 충분히 고생했다고 여겨진다고 말했다. 그를 용서하고, 그와 그 식구들을 돌볼 어떤 방도를 궁리해 보겠다는 것이었다.\n\n존스는 이 소식에 어찌나 기뻤던지, 집에 돌아왔을 때는 이미 날이 어두웠는데도 쏟아지는 빗속을 1마일이나 되짚어 달려가 그 가엾은 여인에게 기쁜 소식을 알리지 않고는 못 배겼다. 그러나 성급하게 소식을 퍼뜨리는 다른 이들과 마찬가지로, 그는 제 입으로 한 말을 도로 뒤집어야 하는 곤란만 자초하고 말았다. 블랙 조지의 불운이 바로 그 친구가 자리를 비운 틈을 타, 모든 것을 다시 뒤엎어 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n블리필 도련님과 존스가 서로 다른 빛으로 비치는 이야기.\n\n블리필 도련님은 자비라는 사랑스러운 덕에서는 동무에게 한참 못 미쳤다. 그러나 그보다 훨씬 높은 부류의 덕, 곧 정의에서는 그를 크게 앞질렀다. 이 점에서 그는 스웨컴과 스퀘어의 가르침과 본보기를 그대로 따랐다. 두 사람 모두 자비라는 말을 입에 자주 올리기는 했으나, 실제로는 스퀘어가 그것을 옳음의 규칙과 어긋나는 것으로 여겼음이 분명했고, 스웨컴은 정의를 행하고 자비는 하늘에 맡기자는 쪽이었다. 다만 두 신사는 이 숭고한 덕의 대상이 누구인가를 두고는 다소 의견이 갈렸으니, 이 셈법대로라면 스웨컴은 아마 인류의 절반을, 스퀘어는 나머지 절반을 멸했을 것이다.\n\n그러니 블리필 도련님은 존스 앞에서는 입을 다물고 있었지만, 그 일을 곰곰이 더 따져 본 뒤로는 자기 외삼촌이 받을 자격 없는 자에게 은혜를 베푸는 꼴을 도저히 두고 볼 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 우리가 앞서 독자들에게 슬쩍 비쳐 두었던 그 사실을 곧장 외삼촌에게 알리기로 마음먹었다. 그 진상은 이러했다.\n\n그 사냥터지기는 올워디 씨의 일자리에서 쫓겨난 지 한 해쯤 지난 뒤, 그리고 톰이 말을 팔기 전, 제 입이든 식구들의 입이든 채울 빵이 없어 궁하던 차에, 웨스턴 씨 소유의 어느 밭을 지나다가 보금자리에 웅크려 앉은 산토끼 한 마리를 발견했다. 그는 이 토끼를 비열하고 무자비하게 머리를 후려쳐 잡았으니, 나라의 법에 어긋났을 뿐 아니라 사냥꾼의 법도에도 못지않게 어긋나는 짓이었다.\n\n그 토끼를 사 갔던 행상은 불행히도 몇 달 뒤에 사냥감을 잔뜩 지닌 채 붙잡혔고, 어느 밀렵꾼에게 불리한 증언을 하는 조건으로 향사와 화해할 수밖에 없게 되었다. 그리하여 그가 지목한 사람이 바로 블랙 조지였으니, 이미 웨스턴 씨의 눈 밖에 난 자인 데다 동네에서 평판도 좋지 못한 인물이었기 때문이다. 게다가 그는 행상이 내놓을 수 있는 가장 마땅한 제물이기도 했다. 그 뒤로 그에게 사냥감을 대 준 적이 없었던 까닭이다. 이런 식으로 증인은 더 좋은 단골들을 감싸 줄 기회를 얻었다. 향사는 단 한 번의 위법으로도 충분히 파멸시킬 수 있는 블랙 조지를 벌하는 힘에 흡족해하여 더는 캐묻지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n이 사실이 올워디 씨 앞에 있는 그대로 진술되었다면, 아마 사냥터지기에게 이렇다 할 화는 미치지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 죄인을 향해 정의를 사랑하는 마음에서 타오르는 열성만큼 눈먼 열성도 없다. 블리필 도련님은 그 일이 일어난 시점이 얼마나 오래전이었는지를 잊고 있었다. 게다가 사실을 전하는 방식에서도 어긋났으니, 'S'라는 글자 하나를 성급히 덧붙임으로써 이야기를 상당히 바꿔 놓고 말았다. 조지가 산토끼들에게 올가미를 놓았다고 말한 것이다. 이런 왜곡들은 아마 바로잡힐 수도 있었으련만, 불운하게도 블리필 도련님이 이 일을 털어놓기에 앞서 올워디 씨에게 비밀을 지키겠다는 약속부터 받아 둔 것이 문제였다. 그 탓에 가엾은 사냥터지기는 스스로를 변호할 기회조차 갖지 못한 채 단죄당하고 말았다. 토끼를 잡은 사실과 소송이 제기된 사실은 분명히 참이었으니, 올워디 씨는 나머지에 대해서도 의심을 품지 않았던 것이다.\n\n그러니 이 가난한 사람들의 기쁨은 오래가지 못했다. 올워디 씨가 이튿날 아침, 까닭은 밝히지 않은 채 자신의 노여움에 새로운 이유가 생겼다고 선언하며, 톰에게 다시는 조지의 이름을 입에 올리지 말라고 엄히 금했기 때문이다. 다만 그 식구들에 대해서는 굶어 죽지 않도록 애써 보겠노라 했으나, 정작 그 사내만큼은 법에 맡겨 두겠으며, 그 무엇으로도 그가 법을 어기는 것을 막을 수 없으리라 했다.\n\n톰은 무엇이 올워디 씨를 그토록 격분케 했는지 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었다. 블리필 도련님에 대해서는 털끝만큼의 의심도 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 우정은 어떤 실망으로도 지칠 줄 모르는 것이었기에, 그는 이제 가엾은 사냥터지기를 파멸에서 구할 또 다른 방법을 시도해 보기로 했다.\n\n존스는 근래에 웨스턴 씨와 무척 가까워져 있었다. 그는 다섯 칸짜리 울타리 문을 훌쩍 뛰어넘는다든가 하는 여러 사냥 솜씨로 그 신사의 마음에 어찌나 들었던지, 향사는 톰이 충분한 격려만 받는다면 틀림없이 대단한 인물이 되리라고 단언하기에 이르렀다. 그는 자기에게 그런 재능을 지닌 아들이 있었으면 좋겠다고 자주 바랐고, 어느 날은 술자리에서 자못 엄숙하게, 톰이라면 자기 돈 천 파운드를 걸고 이 고장의 어떤 사냥꾼과 견주어도 한 무리의 사냥개를 거뜬히 몰 것이라고 장담했다.\n\n이런 재주들 덕분에 그는 향사에게 어찌나 환심을 샀던지, 그의 식탁에서 더없이 반가운 손님이자 그의 사냥에서 가장 아끼는 길동무가 되었다. 향사가 가장 귀히 여기는 것, 다시 말해 그의 총과 개와 말은 이제 마치 제 것이기라도 한 듯 존스의 뜻대로 부릴 수 있었다. 그리하여 그는 이 호의를 벗 블랙 조지를 위해 써먹기로 마음먹었으니, 전에 올워디 씨를 모시던 바로 그 자리에 조지를 웨스턴 씨 집안으로 들여보내고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n독자는, 이 사내가 이미 웨스턴 씨의 눈 밖에 나 있었다는 점을 헤아리고, 더 나아가 그 신사의 노여움을 산 그 묵직한 사연까지 떠올린다면, 어쩌면 이 일을 어리석고 무모한 짓이라 나무랄지도 모른다. 그러나 그 까닭으로 젊은 존스를 통째로 단죄하기보다는, 이토록 험난한 마당에서 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 연줄로 제 입지를 다지려 한 그를 크게 칭찬하게 될 것이다.\n\n그리하여 이 목적을 위해 톰은 웨스턴 씨의 딸에게 도움을 청했다. 열일곱 살쯤 된 젊은 아가씨로, 그녀의 아버지는 방금 말한 그 사냥에 꼭 필요한 도구들 다음으로 세상 그 무엇보다 그녀를 사랑하고 아꼈다. 자, 그녀가 향사에게 어느 정도 영향을 미쳤듯이, 톰도 그녀에게 작으나마 어느 정도 영향을 미치고 있었다. 그러나 이 사람이야말로 이 작품의 여주인공으로 점지된 아가씨이며, 우리 자신이 깊이 사랑하는 이요, 헤어지기 전에 우리 독자들 가운데 많은 이도 아마 사랑하게 될 그 아가씨이니, 그녀가 한 권의 끝자락에서 모습을 드러내는 것은 결코 온당치 않다.\n\n일 년이라는 시간을 담은 권.\n\n다섯 쪽 분량의 종이를 담은 장.\n진실이야말로 우리의 글을, 괴물들로 가득 찬 저 한가한 연애담들과 갈라놓는다. 그런 이야기들은 자연이 빚은 것이 아니라 열에 들뜬 머릿속에서 나온 산물이며, 그래서 어느 저명한 비평가가 오직 빵 굽는 사람에게나 쓰라고 권한 것들이다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 우리는 어느 이름난 시인이 양조업자의 잇속을 채우기에 꼭 알맞다고 여긴 듯한 그런 종류의 역사와도 닮기를 피하고자 한다. 그런 역사는 읽을 때 늘 질 좋은 맥주 한 잔이 곁들여져야 한다는 것이니—\n\n역사가 그 동무인 맥주와 더불어, 자신의 그 엄숙한 이야기의 서글픈 줄거리를 달래는 동안\n\n이것이 근대 역사가들의 음료요, 아니 어쩌면 그들의 시신(詩神)이기도 하니—맥주에 영감을 돌린 버틀러의 견해를 믿어도 좋다면—마땅히 그 독자들의 음료이기도 해야 한다. 무릇 모든 책은 쓰인 것과 같은 기운으로, 같은 방식으로 읽혀야 하기 때문이다. 그래서 《허를로트럼보》의 그 유명한 작가는 어느 학식 깊은 주교에게 이렇게 말했다. 주교 각하께서 제 작품의 뛰어남을 음미하지 못하시는 까닭은, 그것을 손에 바이올린을 들고 읽지 않으셨기 때문이라고 말이다. 작가 자신은 그 곡을 지을 때 늘 그 악기를 손에 쥐고 있었다는 것이다.\n\n그러므로 우리의 작품이 이런 역사가들의 노고와 견주어질 위험에 조금도 빠지지 않도록, 우리는 글 전체에 걸쳐 갖가지 비유와 묘사, 그 밖의 시적인 꾸밈들을 군데군데 끼워 넣을 기회를 빠짐없이 잡아 왔다. 이것들은 사실 앞서 말한 그 맥주의 자리를 대신하고, 긴 작품에서는 독자에게나 작가에게나 슬며시 찾아들기 마련인 그 졸음이 독자에게 스멀스멀 기어들기 시작할 때마다 그 마음을 새롭게 깨우려는 것이다. 이런 종류의 끼어듦이 없다면, 평범한 사실을 다룬 아무리 훌륭한 서술이라도 모든 독자를 짓눌러 버리고 말 것이다. 호메로스가 오직 유피테르 자신에게만 돌린 그 영원한 깨어 있음이 아니고서는, 여러 권에 이르는 신문을 견뎌 낼 길이 없으니 말이다.\n\n우리가 작품의 이 장식적인 부분들을 끼워 넣을 여러 자리를 어떤 판단으로 골랐는지는, 독자의 헤아림에 맡겨 두기로 하자. 분명 지금 이 자리야말로 더없이 알맞은 곳임은 인정받으리라. 지금 우리는 한 비중 있는 인물을 무대에 올리려 하는 참이니, 그 인물은 실로 이 영웅적이고 역사적이며 산문적인 시의 여주인공에 다름 아니다. 그러므로 우리는 자연의 얼굴에서 길어 올릴 수 있는 온갖 즐거운 이미지로 독자의 마음을 가득 채워, 그녀를 맞이할 채비를 갖추게 하는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다. 그리고 이 방법에 대해서는 여러 선례를 들어 변호하겠다. 첫째로, 이것은 우리의 비극 시인들이 익히 알고 즐겨 쓰는 기술로서, 그들은 자신들의 주요 인물을 맞아들이도록 관객을 채비시키는 일을 좀처럼 빠뜨리지 않는다.\n\n그리하여 영웅은 언제나 북과 나팔의 우렁찬 울림과 함께 등장하는데, 이는 관객의 가슴에 무인의 기개를 불러일으키고, 허풍스럽고 과장된 대사에 그들의 귀를 길들이기 위함이다. 로크 씨의 그 눈먼 사람이 그런 대사를 나팔 소리에 빗대었다 한들 그리 크게 틀린 말은 아니었으리라. 또한 연인들이 나설 때면, 흔히 부드러운 음악이 그들을 무대로 이끌어 들인다. 이는 그 애틋한 사랑의 부드러움으로 관객을 어루만지거나, 아니면 뒤이어 펼쳐질 장면이 십중팔구 그들을 잠재울 그 잔잔한 졸음으로 그들을 다독여 채비시키기 위함이다.\n\n그리고 비단 시인들뿐 아니라, 그 시인들의 주인 격인 극장 운영자들 또한 이 비밀을 알고 있는 듯하다. 앞서 말한 영웅의 다가옴을 알리는 큰북 따위 말고도, 영웅은 대개 무대장치 옮기는 일꾼 대여섯 명으로 이루어진 큰 무리의 안내를 받으며 무대에 오르기 때문이다. 그리고 이들이 영웅의 등장에 얼마나 필수적이라 여겨졌는지는, 다음의 극장 일화에서 능히 짐작할 수 있다—\n\n피로스 왕이 극장에 잇닿은 어느 맥줏집에서 저녁을 들고 있는데, 무대에 오르라는 부름을 받았다. 영웅은 양고기 다리를 놓아두기도 싫었고, 그렇다고 관객을 기다리게 해서 동료 운영자인 윌크스 씨의 분노를 사기도 싫었던지라, 자신의 그 길잡이들을 매수해 자리를 비우게 해 두었다. 그래서 윌크스 씨가 \"피로스 왕 앞에서 걸어 나갈 목수들은 어디 있느냐?\" 하고 우레같이 호통치는 동안, 그 군주는 아주 태연하게 양고기를 먹었고, 관객들은 제아무리 애가 탔어도 그가 없는 사이 음악으로나마 스스로를 달랠 수밖에 없었다.\n\n솔직히 말해, 나는 대체로 코가 밝은 정치가가 이런 관행의 유용함을 어느 정도 냄새 맡아 내지 않았을까 자못 의심스럽다. 저 위엄 있는 행정관, 우리의 시장 각하께서 일 년 내내 따라다니는 그 존경을 적잖이 거둬들이는 것도, 그의 화려한 행차에 앞서는 여러 가두 행렬 덕분임을 나는 확신한다. 아니, 솔직히 고백하건대, 겉치레에 좀처럼 사로잡히지 않는 나 자신조차도 한껏 앞세운 위풍의 인상에 적잖이 굴복하고 만 적이 있다. 어떤 사람이 그저 그의 앞에서 걸어가는 것이 본분인 다른 이들의 뒤를 따라 행렬 속에서 거드름을 피우며 걷는 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 그를 예사로운 자리에서 보았을 때보다 그의 위엄을 더 높이 여기곤 했다. 그러나 내 뜻에 꼭 들어맞는 한 가지 사례가 있다. 대관식 때 그 화려한 행렬에 앞서 나아가, 위대한 인물들이 행진을 시작하기 전에 무대에 꽃을 흩뿌리는 바구니 든 여인을 내보내는 관습이 그것이다. 옛사람들이라면 분명 이 일을 위해 꽃의 여신 플로라를 불러냈을 것이며, 그들의 사제나 정치가가 백성에게 그 여신이 실제로 거기 임하셨다고 설득하기란 그리 어렵지 않았으리라. 비록 한낱 평범한 인간이 그 여신을 흉내 내어 그 역할을 대신했을 뿐이라 해도 말이다. 그러나 우리에게는 독자를 그렇게 속이려는 의도가 없으니, 이교의 신학에 거부감을 느끼는 이들은 원한다면 우리의 여신을 앞서 말한 그 바구니 든 여인으로 바꾸어 두어도 좋다. 요컨대 우리의 의도는, 우리가 부릴 수 있는 최대한의 장엄함과 격조 높은 문체, 그리고 독자의 경외심을 자아낼 만한 그 밖의 온갖 정황을 갖추어 우리의 여주인공을 소개하는 것이다.—실로 우리는 어떤 이유들로 인해, 가슴이라는 것을 지닌 우리의 남성 독자들에게 더 이상 읽지 말라고 권하고 싶을 지경이다. 다만 우리의 여주인공의 그림이 제아무리 사랑스럽게 보일지라도, 그것이 실은 자연을 본뜬 모사인 만큼, 우리의 붓이 그려 낼 수 있는 그 어떤 정념이든 채워 주고 여성의 완전함에 대한 그 어떤 관념에든 부응할 만한 우리 고운 동포 여인들이 많이 있으리라 우리가 굳게 확신하지 않았던들 그리했을 것이다.\n\n자, 이제 더 이상의 머리말 없이, 우리는 다음 장으로 나아가기로 한다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n거친 숨결은 모두 잠재워라. 바람을 다스리는 이교의 신이여, 시끄러운 북풍 보레아스의 우악스러운 팔다리를, 그리고 매섭게 쏘아붙이는 동풍 에우루스의 뾰족한 콧날을 쇠사슬로 묶어 가두소서. 그대 다정한 서풍 제피루스여, 향기로운 잠자리에서 일어나 서쪽 하늘로 올라, 저 감미로운 산들바람을 이끌어 오라. 그 산들바람의 매력이 사랑스러운 꽃의 여신 플로라를 진주 같은 이슬로 향기 어린 그녀의 방에서 불러내니, 6월 초하루 그녀의 생일에, 활짝 핀 이 처녀가 헐렁한 옷차림으로 푸른 풀밭 위를 사뿐히 거닐면, 송이마다 꽃들이 일어나 그녀에게 경의를 표하고, 마침내 들판 전체가 칠보처럼 빛나며, 빛깔과 향기가 서로 그녀를 누가 더 사로잡을지 다투는 것이다.\n\n그러니 그녀가 지금 이토록 매혹적으로 나타나리라! 그리고 그대들, 헨델조차 능가하지 못할 가장 감미로운 가락을 지닌 자연의 깃털 입은 합창대여, 가락 고운 목청을 가다듬어 그녀의 등장을 노래하라. 그대들의 음악은 사랑에서 비롯되어, 다시 사랑으로 돌아간다. 그러니 모든 젊은 양치기의 가슴에 그 부드러운 정념을 일깨워라. 보라! 자연이 그녀를 꾸밀 수 있는 온갖 매력으로 단장하고, 아름다움과 젊음, 발랄함, 순수함, 정숙함, 그리고 다정함으로 치장한 채, 장밋빛 입술에서 감미로움을 내뿜고 반짝이는 두 눈에서 광채를 쏘아 보내며, 사랑스러운 소피아가 오고 있다!\n\n독자여, 그대는 어쩌면 메디치의 비너스 조각상을 본 적이 있을지 모른다. 어쩌면 햄프턴 코트의 미녀 화랑도 보았을지 모른다. 그대는 그 화려한 무리 가운데 빛나던 처칠가의 여인들을 하나하나 기억할 것이고, 키트캣 클럽에서 건배의 주인공이 된 미녀들도 모두 떠올릴 것이다. 혹 그들의 전성기가 그대의 시대보다 앞섰다면, 적어도 그들의 딸들, 다시 말해 그에 못지않게 눈부신 이 시대의 미녀들은 보았을 것이다. 그 이름들을 여기에 다 적는다면 책 한 권이 통째로 채워지지 않을까 싶다.\n\n자, 그대가 이들을 모두 보았다면, 일찍이 로체스터 경이 온갖 것을 다 보았다는 어떤 사내에게 던진 그 거친 대답 같은 것은 두려워하지 말라. 아니다. 만일 그대가 이들을 다 보고도 아름다움이 무엇인지 모른다면, 그대에겐 눈이 없는 것이다. 그 힘을 느끼지 못한 채 보았다면, 그대에겐 가슴이 없는 것이다.\n\n하지만 벗이여, 그대가 이들을 모두 보았다 해도 소피아의 모습을 정확히 그려 내지는 못할 수도 있다. 그녀는 그 누구와도 똑같이 닮지는 않았기 때문이다. 그녀는 래닐러 부인의 초상화와 가장 비슷했고, 내가 듣기로는 그 유명한 마자랭 공작부인과 더 닮았다고 한다. 그러나 무엇보다도, 그 모습이 결코 내 가슴에서 떠나지 않는 어떤 이를 가장 닮았으니, 벗이여, 그대가 그이를 기억한다면 그대는 소피아에 대한 마땅한 그림을 이미 지닌 셈이다.\n\n그러나 그런 행운이 그대에게 없었을 경우를 대비하여, 우리는 우리의 가장 뛰어난 솜씨로 이 본보기 같은 여인을 그려 내려 애써 보겠다. 비록 우리의 최고 역량으로도 이 일에는 턱없이 모자란다는 것을 잘 알고 있지만 말이다.\n\n자, 웨스턴 씨의 외동딸인 소피아는 중간 키였으나, 오히려 큰 편에 가까웠다. 그녀의 몸매는 정확할 뿐 아니라 더없이 섬세했고, 균형 잡힌 두 팔은 사지의 더할 나위 없는 조화를 약속하고 있었다. 검은 머리카락은 어찌나 풍성했던지, 요즘 유행을 따르려 자르기 전에는 허리께까지 닿았다. 지금은 목덜미에서 어찌나 우아하게 곱슬져 있던지, 그것이 본래 그녀의 머리라고는 믿기 어려운 이가 많을 정도였다. 시기심이 그녀의 얼굴에서 다른 부분만큼 찬사받을 자격이 덜한 데를 찾으려 든다면, 어쩌면 이마가 조금 더 높았어도 흠이 되지 않았으리라 여길지 모른다. 그녀의 두 눈썹은 짙고 고르며, 기교로는 흉내 낼 수 없을 만큼 활처럼 휘어 있었다. 검은 눈동자에는 광채가 어려 있어, 그 모든 부드러움으로도 그 빛을 끌 수 없었다. 코는 더없이 반듯했고, 상아 같은 두 줄의 이가 들어찬 입은 존 서클링 경의 시구에 그려진 묘사와 정확히 들어맞았다.\n그녀의 입술은 붉었고, 그 가운데 하나는\n\n턱에 가까운 아랫입술에 견주어 얇았으니,\n어느 벌이 갓 쏘아 놓은 듯하였다.\n\n그녀의 두 뺨은 갸름한 달걀형이었고, 오른쪽 뺨에는 보조개가 있어 아주 옅은 미소에도 드러났다. 턱 역시 분명 그녀 얼굴의 아름다움을 이루는 데 제 몫을 했으나, 크다고도 작다고도 말하기 어려웠다. 다만 어쩌면 큰 편에 가까웠다고 할 수 있겠다. 그녀의 살빛은 장미보다는 백합 쪽에 가까웠다. 그러나 운동이나 수줍음으로 타고난 혈색이 짙어질 때면, 그 어떤 진홍빛도 그에 견줄 수 없었다. 그럴 때면 우리는 정녕 저 이름난 던 박사처럼 외칠 만하다.\n―그녀의 맑고도 웅변하는 피가\n\n두 뺨에서 말을 하니, 그 결이 어찌나 또렷하던지\n그녀의 몸이 생각을 한다고 말할 수 있을 정도였다.\n\n그녀의 목은 길고 곱게 빠졌다. 여기서, 그녀의 섬세함을 거스를까 두렵지만 않다면, 나는 저 유명한 메디치의 비너스가 지닌 최고의 아름다움마저 능가했다고 떳떳이 말할 수 있으리라. 거기엔 어떤 백합도, 상아도, 설화석고도 견줄 수 없는 흰빛이 있었다. 더없이 고운 무명천조차 시기심에서 자기보다 훨씬 흰 그 가슴을 가리려 든다고 여길 만했다. 그것은 실로,\n\n니토르 스플렌덴스 파리오 마르모레 푸리우스.\n\n파로스 대리석의 가장 순결한 빛마저 넘어서는 광택.\n\n이것이 소피아의 겉모습이었다. 그리고 이 아름다운 틀은 그에 어울리지 않는 자가 깃들어 그 품격을 떨어뜨리는 일도 없었다. 그녀의 마음은 모든 면에서 그 몸에 견줄 만했다. 아니, 몸은 오히려 마음에서 얼마간 매력을 빌려 왔으니, 그녀가 미소 지을 때면 그 온화한 성품의 감미로움이 얼굴 위로 영광을 퍼뜨려, 아무리 반듯한 이목구비로도 줄 수 없는 빛을 더했기 때문이다. 그러나 우리가 이 매혹적인 젊은 여인을 통해 독자를 이끌어 들이려는 그 완전한 친밀함 속에서 절로 드러나지 않을 마음의 미덕이란 없으니, 여기서 그것들을 일일이 언급할 필요는 없다. 아니, 그렇게 하는 것은 독자의 이해력에 대한 일종의 은근한 모욕이며, 또한 독자가 그녀의 성격을 스스로 판단하며 누릴 그 즐거움을 빼앗는 일이 될 수도 있다.\n\n다만 이것만은 말해 두는 편이 좋겠다. 그녀가 천성에서 물려받은 어떤 정신적 자질이 무엇이든, 그것은 기예에 의해 어느 정도 다듬어지고 가꾸어졌다는 점이다. 그녀는 한 이모의 보살핌 아래 자랐는데, 이 부인은 분별이 깊은 데다 세상 물정에 두루 밝았으니, 젊은 시절 궁정 언저리에서 지내다가 몇 해 전 시골로 물러난 분이었다. 이모의 대화와 가르침 덕분에 소피아는 더없이 훌륭한 교양을 갖추게 되었다. 다만 몸가짐에서는, 오직 습관과 이른바 사교계 안에서의 생활을 통해서만 얻을 수 있는 그 여유로움이 어쩌면 조금 부족했을지 모른다. 그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 그런 여유로움은 흔히 너무 비싼 값을 치르고 얻는 것이다. 또 그것은 이루 말로 다 할 수 없는 매력을 지녀서, 어쩌면 프랑스인들이 다른 여러 자질과 더불어 \"그것이 무엇인지 우리도 모르겠다\"고 단언할 때 표현하려는 것이 바로 이것인지도 모른다. 그렇지만 그것이 없더라도 순수함이 충분히 그 자리를 메워 주며, 또 분별 있는 양식과 타고난 기품이 있는 이라면 그것이 결코 필요치 않다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n3장.\n이야기가 잠시 거슬러 올라가, 몇 해 전에 일어난 사소한 사건 하나를 되짚는다. 비록 사소하기 짝이 없었으나, 훗날 적잖은 결과를 낳은 일이다.\n\n사랑스러운 소피아가 이 이야기에 등장하는 지금, 그녀는 열여덟 살이 되어 있었다. 앞서 말했듯, 그녀의 아버지는 이 세상 그 누구보다 그녀를 아꼈다. 그래서 톰 존스는 자기 벗인 사냥터지기를 위해 그녀의 마음을 얻으려고 그녀를 찾아갔다.\n\n하지만 이 일로 넘어가기 전에, 앞서 있었던 몇 가지 일을 짧게 되짚어 두는 편이 좋겠다.\n\n올워디 씨와 웨스턴 씨는 기질이 서로 달라 그리 가까운 왕래까지는 없었으나, 그래도 점잖다 할 만한 사이로는 지내고 있었다. 그 덕분에 두 집안의 아이들은 어릴 적부터 서로 알고 지냈고, 모두 또래였던 터라 함께 자주 어울려 놀던 사이였다.\n\n쾌활한 톰의 기질은, 진중하고 무던한 블리필 도련님의 성품보다 소피아와 더 잘 맞았다. 그리고 그녀가 두 사람 가운데 톰을 더 좋아한다는 사실은 자주 너무도 또렷하게 드러나서, 블리필 도련님보다 성정이 격한 소년이었다면 그에 대해 얼마간 언짢음을 내비쳤을 법도 했다.\n\n그러나 그는 그런 불쾌함을 겉으로 드러내지 않았으니, 우리가 그의 마음 가장 깊은 구석까지 들여다보려 드는 것은 못된 짓이 될 것이다. 마치 어떤 입방아 찧기 좋아하는 자들이 벗의 가장 은밀한 일까지 캐고 다니며, 오직 그 가난과 옹졸함을 세상에 까발리려고 옷장과 찬장까지 들춰 보는 것처럼 말이다.\n\n하지만 남에게 노여움을 살 빌미를 주었다고 의심하는 사람일수록 상대가 화가 났으리라 지레짐작하기 마련이다. 그래서 소피아도 블리필 도련님의 어떤 행동을 그의 노여움 탓으로 돌렸으나, 트와컴과 스퀘어의 더 뛰어난 통찰력은 그 행동이 훨씬 더 갸륵한 원리에서 비롯되었다고 보았다.\n\n톰 존스는 아주 어렸을 적에 둥지에서 꺼내 직접 길러 노래까지 가르친 작은 새 한 마리를 소피아에게 선물한 적이 있었다.\n\n그때 열세 살쯤이던 소피아는 이 새를 어찌나 끔찍이 아꼈던지, 모이를 주고 보살피는 것이 그녀의 가장 큰 일이요, 함께 노는 것이 가장 큰 즐거움이었다. 그렇게 지내다 보니 어린 토미는 — 그 새의 이름이 그러했다 — 아주 길이 잘 들어서, 안주인의 손에서 모이를 받아먹고, 손가락에 내려앉으며, 그녀의 품에 안겨 만족스레 누워 있곤 했다. 마치 제 행복을 거의 알아차리기라도 하는 듯했다. 다만 그녀는 늘 새의 다리에 가느다란 끈을 매어 두었고, 날아가 버릴 자유만큼은 결코 허락하지 않았다.\n\n어느 날, 올워디 씨가 온 식구를 데리고 웨스턴 씨 댁에서 만찬을 들 때였다. 블리필 도련님은 어린 소피아와 정원에 있다가, 그녀가 작은 새를 끔찍이 아끼는 것을 보고는 잠깐만 새를 자기 손에 맡겨 달라고 청했다. 소피아는 곧바로 도련님의 청을 들어주었고, 미리 몇 마디 당부를 한 뒤 새를 건넸다. 그런데 그는 새를 손에 쥐자마자 다리에 매인 끈을 풀어 공중으로 휙 날려 보냈다.\n\n그 어리석은 짐승은 제가 자유로워진 것을 알아채자마자, 소피아에게서 받은 온갖 은혜를 다 잊고 곧장 그녀에게서 날아가 조금 떨어진 나뭇가지에 내려앉았다.\n\n새가 날아가 버린 것을 본 소피아가 어찌나 크게 비명을 질렀던지, 멀지 않은 곳에 있던 톰 존스가 곧장 그녀를 도우러 달려왔다.\n\n그는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 듣자마자 블리필을 두고 비열하고 심술궂은 악당이라며 욕을 퍼부었고, 곧바로 웃옷을 벗어 던지고는 새가 달아난 나무로 기어오르기 시작했다.\n\n톰이 제 이름을 딴 그 작은 새를 거의 다 붙잡았을 무렵, 새가 앉아 있던 가지가 — 그것이 운하 위로 뻗어 있었는데 — 그만 부러지는 바람에, 가엾은 소년은 머리끝까지 물속으로 풍덩 빠지고 말았다.\n\n이제 소피아의 걱정은 대상을 바꾸었다. 소년의 목숨이 위태롭다고 여긴 그녀는 아까보다 열 배는 더 크게 비명을 질렀다. 그리고 정말로, 이번에는 블리필 도련님 자신도 있는 힘껏 목청을 보태어 함께 소리쳤다.\n\n정원 옆방에 앉아 있던 사람들이 곧장 놀라 모두 뛰쳐나왔다. 그러나 그들이 운하에 막 다다랐을 때, 톰은 — 다행히 그 부분의 물이 꽤 얕았던 덕분에 — 무사히 뭍으로 올라온 참이었다.\n\n트와컴이 물을 뚝뚝 흘리며 그 앞에서 덜덜 떨고 선 가엾은 톰에게 사납게 달려들려 하자, 올워디 씨가 좀 참으라고 이른 뒤 블리필 도련님 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"얘야, 이 소동이 다 어찌 된 영문인지 말해 보아라.\"\n\n블리필 도련님이 대답했다. \"정말이지 외삼촌, 제가 한 일이 너무도 후회됩니다. 불행히도 제가 이 모든 일의 빌미가 되고 말았어요. 제 손에 소피아 양의 새가 있었는데, 그 가엾은 것이 자유를 그리워하며 시들어 가는 것 같아서, 솔직히 그것이 바라는 바를 주지 않을 수가 없었습니다. 저는 무언가를 가두는 데에는 늘 몹시 잔인한 구석이 있다고 여겨 왔거든요. 그것은 만물이 자유에 대한 권리를 지니는 자연의 법칙에 어긋나는 일처럼 보였습니다. 아니, 그것은 그리스도인답지 않은 일이기까지 합니다. 우리가 남에게 대접받고자 하는 대로 남을 대접하는 일이 아니니까요. 하지만 소피아 양이 이토록 마음 아파할 줄 알았더라면, 결단코 그러지 않았을 겁니다. 아니, 새 자신에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지 알았더라면요. 존스 도련님이 새를 잡으러 저 나무에 올라갔다가 물에 빠지자, 새는 다시 한 번 날아올랐고, 곧바로 흉측한 매 한 마리가 그것을 채 가 버렸으니까요.\"\n\n이제야 어린 토미의 운명을 처음 듣게 된 가엾은 소피아는 — 존스를 걱정하느라 그 일이 벌어졌을 때는 미처 알아채지 못했던 것이다 — 눈물을 비 오듯 쏟았다. 올워디 씨는 훨씬 더 고운 새를 구해 주겠다고 약속하며 그 눈물을 달래려 했으나, 그녀는 다시는 다른 새를 갖지 않겠다고 잘라 말했다. 그녀의 아버지는 한낱 어리석은 새 따위로 그리 운다고 딸을 나무라면서도, 어린 블리필을 향해 만일 네가 내 아들이었다면 엉덩이 가죽이 벗겨지도록 흠씬 두들겨 맞았을 거라는 말만큼은 끝내 참지 못했다.\n\n이윽고 소피아는 자기 방으로 돌아갔고, 두 도련님은 집으로 보내졌으며, 나머지 사람들은 다시 술병 앞으로 돌아왔다. 거기서 그 새를 두고 대화가 오갔는데, 그것이 어찌나 흥미로웠던지 우리는 따로 한 장을 할애할 만하다고 여기는 바이다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n스퀘어는 파이프에 불을 붙이기가 무섭게 올워디 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 이렇게 말문을 열었다. \"선생, 댁의 조카를 두고 축하의 말씀을 드리지 않을 수가 없군요. 또래의 아이들 대부분이 감각으로 느낄 수 있는 것들 말고는 아무 관념도 없을 나이에, 그 아이는 벌써 옳고 그름을 분별하는 능력에 이르렀으니 말입니다. 무엇이든 가두어 두는 것은 제가 보기에 자연의 법칙에 어긋나는 일입니다. 그 법칙에 따르면 모든 것은 자유를 누릴 권리를 지니고 있지요. 이것이 그 아이가 한 말입니다. 그 말이 제게 남긴 인상은 결코 지워지지 않을 것입니다. 어느 누가 옳음의 법칙과 사물의 영원한 합당함에 대해 이보다 더 높은 관념을 품을 수 있겠습니까? 저는 이런 새벽빛을 보고서, 이 젊은이의 한낮이 형 브루투스든 동생 브루투스든 그 어느 쪽에도 못지않으리라 자신하지 않을 수 없습니다.\"\n\n여기서 스웨컴이 황급히 끼어들어, 포도주를 조금 흘리고 나머지는 단숨에 들이켜고는 대답했다. \"그 아이가 쓴 또 다른 표현으로 미루어, 저는 그 아이가 훨씬 더 훌륭한 사람들을 닮기를 바랍니다. 자연의 법칙이란 아무 뜻도 없는 말장난에 지나지 않습니다. 저는 그런 법칙도, 거기서 끌어낼 수 있는 어떤 권리도 알지 못합니다. 남이 내게 해주기를 바라는 대로 남에게 행하라는 것이야말로 참으로 기독교의 동기이며, 그 아이도 그것을 훌륭히 말로 옮겼지요. 제 가르침이 이토록 좋은 열매를 맺은 것을 확인하니 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"허영이 합당한 것이라면\" 하고 스퀘어가 말했다. \"저도 같은 일을 두고 어느 정도 허영을 부려도 좋을 듯합니다. 그 아이가 옳고 그름에 관한 관념을 대체 어디서 배웠는지는 꽤 분명해 보이니까요. 자연의 법칙이 없다면, 옳음도 그름도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 하고 목사가 말했다. \"그러면 댁은 계시를 내쳐 버리는 겁니까? 내가 지금 이신론자나 무신론자와 이야기하고 있는 것입니까?\"\n\n\"한 잔씩들 드시오.\" 하고 웨스턴이 말했다. \"당신네 자연의 법칙 따위 염병할! 옳음이니 그름이니 하는 게 두 분 다 무슨 소린지 나는 도무지 모르겠소. 내 딸의 새를 빼앗아 간 건 내 생각에는 잘못된 일이오. 이웃인 올워디 양반이야 마음대로 하시면 그만이지만, 사내아이들에게 그런 짓을 부추기는 건 그놈들을 교수대로 키우는 것이나 다름없소.\"\n\n올워디는 이렇게 대답했다. 조카가 한 일에 대해서는 자기도 유감스럽게 여기지만, 그 아이가 비열한 동기에서가 아니라 오히려 너그러운 동기에서 한 일이니 벌하는 데에는 동의할 수 없다고. 그는 말했다. \"만일 그 아이가 새를 훔쳤다면 저보다 더 앞장서서 엄한 매질에 찬성할 사람은 없었을 겁니다. 하지만 그것이 그 아이의 의도가 아니었음은 분명합니다.\" 실로 그에게는, 그 아이가 스스로 고백한 것 말고는 다른 어떤 속셈도 있을 수 없다는 것이 자명해 보였다. (소피아가 의심했던 그 악의에 찬 목적은 올워디의 머릿속에 단 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었던 것이다.) 그는 마침내 그 행동을 다시 한번 경솔했다고 나무라며 말을 맺었는데, 그런 일은 아이에게서나 너그러이 보아 넘길 수 있는 일이라고 했다.\n\n스퀘어는 자기 의견을 워낙 거리낌 없이 내놓았던 터라, 이제 와서 잠자코 있다가는 자신의 판단이 비난받는 것을 감수해야 할 처지였다. 그래서 그는 다소 열을 올리며 말했다. \"올워디 선생은 재산이라는 그 천박한 고려에 너무 무게를 두십니다. 위대하고 장엄한 행위를 두고 판단을 내릴 때에는 모든 사사로운 고려를 한쪽으로 치워 두어야 마땅합니다. 그런 옹졸한 규칙에 매달린 탓에 동생 브루투스는 배은망덕하다 단죄받았고, 형 브루투스는 친족 살해범으로 몰렸으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그런 죄로 두 사람이 교수형을 당했더라도\" 하고 스웨컴이 외쳤다. \"그건 제 죗값을 받은 것에 지나지 않았을 겁니다. 한 쌍의 이교도 악당들이지요! 요즘 세상에 브루투스 같은 자들이 없는 것을 하늘에 감사할 따름입니다! 스퀘어 선생, 제발 제 제자들의 머릿속을 그런 반기독교적인 것들로 채우는 일은 그만두시면 좋겠습니다. 그 결과란, 그 아이들이 제 손아래 있는 한, 그것을 다시 호되게 매질로 뽑아내는 일이 될 수밖에 없으니까요. 댁의 제자 톰만 해도 벌써 거의 망쳐졌습니다. 일전에 그 아이가 블리필 도련님과 다투면서, 행위가 따르지 않는 믿음에는 아무 공덕이 없다고 하는 것을 제가 엿들었습니다. 그게 댁의 신조 가운데 하나인 줄 알고 있으며, 그 아이도 댁에게서 배운 것이리라 짐작합니다.\"\n\n\"그 아이를 망쳤다고 저를 탓하지 마십시오.\" 하고 스퀘어가 말했다. \"덕스럽고 점잖으며, 사물의 본성에 합당하고 옳은 것이라면 무엇이든 비웃도록 그 아이를 가르친 게 누굽니까? 그 아이는 댁이 가르친 학생이니, 저는 그 아이와 인연을 끊겠습니다. 아니지요, 아닙니다. 블리필 도련님이야말로 제 아이입니다. 어린 나이임에도 그 아이가 지닌 도덕적 올바름의 관념만큼은, 댁이 결코 뽑아낼 수 없으리라 장담합니다.\"\n\n스웨컴은 이 말에 경멸 어린 비웃음을 띠며 대꾸했다. \"암, 그렇고말고요. 그 아이라면 댁에게 맡겨 봐도 괜찮습니다. 댁의 그 온갖 철학적 헛소리로도 해칠 수 없을 만큼 단단히 다져진 아이니까요. 아니지요, 아닙니다. 저는 그 아이에게 그런 원칙들을 단단히 새겨 넣어 두는 데 정성을 들였단 말입니다—\"\n\n\"저도 그 아이에게 원칙들을 새겨 넣었습니다.\" 하고 스퀘어가 외쳤다. \"자유를 베푼다는 너그러운 생각을 사람의 마음속에 불어넣을 수 있는 것이, 덕이라는 숭고한 관념 말고 또 무엇이 있겠습니까? 다시 한번 말씀드리지만, 자랑하는 것이 합당한 일이라면 저는 그 관념을 불어넣은 영예를 제 것이라 주장할 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 자랑이 금지된 것이 아니라면\" 하고 스웨컴이 말했다. \"저는 그 아이가 스스로 자기 동기로 내세운 바로 그 의무를 가르쳐 준 일을 뽐낼 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"그러니 두 분 덕에\" 하고 향사가 말했다. \"그 도련님이 내 딸의 새를 훔치는 법을 배운 셈이로군. 아무래도 내 자고새 사육장을 단속해야겠소. 어떤 덕스럽고 신앙심 깊은 양반이 와서 내 자고새들을 죄다 풀어놓을 판이니 말이오.\" 그러고는 그 자리에 있던 법률가 한 사람의 등을 철썩 치며 외쳤다. \"이 일에 대해 어찌 생각하시오, 변호사 양반? 이건 법에 어긋나는 일이 아니오?\"\n\n법률가는 자못 엄숙하게 다음과 같이 견해를 밝혔다.\n\n\"만일 자고새의 경우라면, 소송이 성립하리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없습니다. 이는 본래 야생의 것이긴 하나, 길들여졌으니 소유권이 귀속되기 때문이지요. 그러나 노래하는 새의 경우라면, 비록 길들여졌다 하더라도 천한 본성을 지닌 것이므로 누구의 소유물도 아닌 것으로 보아야 합니다. 따라서 이 경우 원고는 패소할 수밖에 없다고 사료되며, 저로서는 그러한 소송을 제기하지 않으시기를 권하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋소,\" 하고 향사가 말했다. \"그것이 누구의 것도 아니라면, 한 잔씩들 들면서 나라 형편이나 우리 모두가 알아들을 만한 그런 이야기나 좀 나눕시다. 나는 이 말은 한 마디도 못 알아듣겠으니 말이오. 내가 모르는 학식이고 분별일 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 댁들이 나를 거기에 끌어들이지는 못할 거요. 염병할! 두 분 다 칭찬받아 마땅한 그 가엾은 아이에 대해서는 한 마디도 꺼내지 않으셨소. 내 딸의 비위를 맞추려고 제 목이 부러질 위험을 무릅쓴 건 너그러운 기상에서 나온 행동이오. 그 정도는 나도 알아볼 학식은 되오. 빌어먹을, 자, 톰의 건강을 위하여! 나는 살아 있는 마지막 날까지 그 일로 그 아이를 아낄 거요.\"\n\n이렇게 해서 그 논쟁은 중단되었다. 하지만 올워디가 곧바로 마차를 부르며 두 논객을 데리고 가지 않았더라면 아마 머지않아 다시 이어졌을 것이다.\n\n새를 둘러싼 이 사건과 그로 말미암아 벌어진 대화는 이렇게 끝을 맺었다. 비록 이 일이 우리 이야기가 지금 이른 그 단계나 시점보다 몇 해 전에 일어난 일이긴 하나, 우리는 이를 독자 여러분께 들려드리지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n모든 취향에 두루 맞는 이야기를 담다.\n\n“Parva leves capiunt animos—사소한 것들이 가벼운 마음을 사로잡는다”라는 말은, 사랑이라는 정념을 다루는 어느 위대한 거장의 생각이었다. 그리고 분명한 사실은, 이날부터 소피아가 톰 존스에게 작은 호의를 품기 시작했고, 그의 동무에게는 결코 작지 않은 반감을 갖게 되었다는 것이다.\n\n여러 가지 일들이 때때로 그녀 가슴속에서 이 두 감정을 모두 키워 갔다. 굳이 우리가 일일이 들려주지 않더라도, 앞서 이 두 소년의 서로 다른 기질에 대해, 그리고 그중 하나가 다른 하나보다 얼마나 더 그녀 자신의 성향에 들어맞았는지에 대해 넌지시 일러둔 바를 떠올리면, 독자는 능히 짐작할 수 있을 것이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 소피아는 아주 어린 나이에 이미 알아챘다. 톰은 비록 게으르고 생각 없고 수다스러운 개구쟁이였지만 자기 자신 말고는 누구의 적도 아니었고, 블리필 도련님은 비록 신중하고 분별 있고 진중한 젊은 신사였지만 동시에 오직 단 한 사람의 이익에만 강하게 매여 있었다는 것을. 그 한 사람이 누구인지는, 우리가 거들지 않아도 독자가 능히 짐작해 낼 수 있으리라.\n\n이 두 부류의 사람은, 저마다 응당 받아 마땅해 보이는, 그리고 인류가 제 잇속을 따져서라도 베풀 법한 그 서로 다른 대우를 세상에서 늘 받는 것은 아니다. 하지만 거기에는 어쩌면 어떤 영리한 까닭이 있는지도 모른다. 사람들은 진정으로 자애로운 성품의 사람을 하나 발견하면, 보물을 찾았다고 여기는 것도 무리가 아니어서, 다른 온갖 좋은 것들과 마찬가지로 그것을 자기들끼리만 간직하고 싶어 할 법하다. 그래서 그들은, 그런 사람의 미덕을 나팔 불어 떠벌리는 일이 흔한 말로 하자면 \"고기 구워졌다고 외쳐\" 자기들만 쓰려고 마음먹은 것에 동참할 이들을 불러 모으는 격이라고 여길지도 모른다. 만일 이 설명이 독자를 흡족하게 하지 못한다면, 나로서는 인간 본성에 참으로 큰 영광을 안기고 사회에 더없이 큰 이로움을 낳는 그런 성품에, 내가 흔히 보아 온 대로 어찌 그리 작은 존경밖에 돌아가지 않는지를 달리 설명할 길을 알지 못한다. 그러나 소피아는 달랐다. 그녀는 그 두 단어의 뜻을 알게 되자마자 거의 곧바로 톰 존스를 우러르고 블리필 도련님을 업신여겼다.\n\n소피아는 삼 년 남짓 고모와 함께 떨어져 지냈는데, 그동안 이 두 젊은 신사를 거의 본 적이 없었다. 다만 한 번, 고모와 함께 올워디 씨 댁에서 식사를 한 적이 있었다. 그것은 앞서 말한 자고 사건이 있고 며칠 뒤의 일이었다. 소피아는 식탁에서 그 이야기를 처음부터 끝까지 들었으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 집으로 돌아오는 길에도 고모는 그녀에게서 별다른 말을 끌어내지 못했다. 그런데 그녀의 시중을 들던 하녀가 옷을 벗기다가 무심코 \"아씨, 오늘 어린 블리필 도련님을 뵈었겠네요?\" 하고 말하자, 그녀는 몹시 격하게 대답했다. \"나는 블리필 도련님이라는 이름을, 비열하고 음흉한 모든 것을 미워하듯 미워해. 그리고 올워디 씨가 어찌 그 늙고 잔인한 선생을 그냥 두어, 그저 착한 마음에서 한 일 때문에 가엾은 아이를 그토록 모질게 매질하게 했는지 정말 모르겠어.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 하녀에게 그 이야기를 들려주고는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"그 아이가 고결한 기상을 지닌 소년이라고 생각하지 않니?\"\n\n이제 이 젊은 아가씨는 아버지 곁으로 돌아왔다. 아버지는 그녀에게 집안의 살림을 맡기고, 식탁 윗자리에 앉혔는데, 그 자리에는 (사냥을 무척 좋아한 덕에 지주의 큰 총애를 받게 된) 톰이 자주 와서 식사를 했다. 솔직하고 너그러운 성품의 젊은이는 본디 여인을 정중히 대하는 쪽으로 기울기 마련인데, 만일 분별까지 갖추었다면—실제로 톰이 그러했다—그 마음씨는 모든 여인을 두루 살뜰히 배려하는 상냥한 태도로 드러난다. 이 점이 톰을, 한편으로는 그저 시골 지주들의 거칠고 무뚝뚝한 됨됨이로부터, 다른 한편으로는 블리필 도련님의 엄숙하고 다소 시무룩한 태도로부터 크게 돋보이게 했다. 그리하여 그는 이제 스무 살에 이르러, 이웃의 모든 여인들 사이에서 멋진 청년이라는 이름을 얻기 시작했다.\n\n톰은 소피아를 대할 때 특별한 기색을 내비치지 않았다. 다만 다른 누구에게보다 더 높은 존경을 보였다는 점만은 예외라면 예외였을 것이다. 이런 차이는 그녀의 아름다움과 재산과 분별, 그리고 사랑스러운 몸가짐이 마땅히 요구하는 것처럼 보였다. 그러나 그녀의 몸을 노린 어떤 속셈도 그에게는 없었으니, 이 점에서 우리는 당장은 독자가 그를 어리석다고 나무라도록 내버려 두겠다. 다만 어쩌면 나중에 그 까닭을 그럭저럭 잘 설명할 수 있을 것이다.\n\n소피아는 더없이 순결하고 정숙하면서도, 그 기질에 두드러진 발랄함이 있었다. 그리고 그 발랄함은 톰과 함께 있을 때면 어찌나 크게 부풀어 올랐던지, 만일 톰이 그토록 어리고 생각이 없지만 않았다면 분명 그것을 알아챘으리라. 또 만일 웨스턴 씨의 머릿속이 늘 사냥터나 마구간이나 개집에 가 있지만 않았다면, 어쩌면 그의 마음에 어떤 질투를 일으켰을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 선량한 양반은 그런 의심을 품기는커녕, 어떤 연인이라도 바랄 법한 온갖 기회를 톰에게 제 딸과 더불어 마련해 주었다. 그리고 톰은 그저 타고난 정중함과 착한 마음씨가 시키는 대로만 따랐는데도, 이 기회를 천진하게 잘 살려 냈으니, 어쩌면 그 아가씨에게 깊은 속셈을 품었더라면 오히려 그만큼 잘 해내지 못했을지도 모른다.\n\n그러나 실로 이 일이 다른 사람들의 눈을 벗어났다는 것은 그리 놀랄 일이 아니다. 가엾은 소피아 자신조차도 결코 그것을 알아채지 못했으며, 그녀의 마음은 위태로운 줄 의심해 보기도 전에 이미 돌이킬 수 없이 빼앗기고 말았으니 말이다.\n\n일이 이런 형편에 놓여 있던 어느 날 오후, 톰은 소피아가 혼자 있는 것을 보고는, 짧게 양해를 구한 뒤 자못 진지한 얼굴로 그녀에게 청할 부탁이 하나 있는데 그녀의 너그러움으로 들어주기를 바란다고 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n그 젊은이의 태도도, 또 이 용건을 꺼내는 방식도, 그가 그녀에게 사랑을 고백하려 한다고 의심할 만한 정당한 빌미를 줄 만한 것은 아니었다. 그런데도 자연이 그녀의 귀에 무언가를 속삭였던 것인지, 아니면 어떤 까닭에서 비롯되었는지는 내가 단정하지 않겠으나, 분명한 것은 그런 종류의 어떤 생각이 그녀에게 슬며시 끼어들었으리라는 점이다. 그녀의 뺨에서 핏기가 가시고, 사지가 떨렸으며, 만일 톰이 대답을 기다리며 말을 멈추기라도 했다면 혀까지 더듬거렸으리라. 그러나 그는 곧 자신의 청을 마저 알림으로써 그녀를 그 당혹에서 풀어 주었다. 그 청이란, 웨스턴 씨가 사냥터지기를 상대로 소송을 계속 밀어붙인다면 그 결과 본인은 물론 대식구가 통째로 파멸할 수밖에 없으니, 그를 위해 아버지께 힘써 달라는 것이었다.\n\n소피아는 이내 혼란에서 벗어나, 더없이 달콤한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그토록 엄숙하게 청하신 그 대단한 부탁이 고작 이거였나요? 기꺼이 들어드리겠어요. 저는 정말로 그 가엾은 사람이 딱해서, 바로 어제도 그 아내에게 변변찮은 것이나마 보냈답니다.\" 이 변변찮은 것이란, 그녀의 옷 한 벌과 약간의 아마포, 그리고 돈 십 실링이었는데, 톰은 이 일을 전해 듣고 있었고, 실은 바로 그것이 이 청을 그의 머릿속에 떠올려 준 것이었다.\n\n이제 성공에 한껏 용기를 얻은 우리의 젊은이는, 일을 한 걸음 더 밀고 나가기로 마음먹고, 감히 그를 아버지의 일꾼으로 천거해 달라는 부탁까지 내놓았다. 그는 그 사람이야말로 이 고장에서 가장 정직한 사람 가운데 하나이며, 마침 그때 운 좋게 비어 있던 사냥터지기 자리에 더없이 잘 어울린다고 힘주어 말했다.\n\n소피아가 대답했다. \"좋아요, 이 일도 맡아 보겠어요. 다만 앞서 부탁만큼 성공을 장담할 수는 없네요. 앞 부탁이라면 분명 아버지께 허락을 얻기 전에는 물러나지 않으리라 약속드릴 수 있지만요. 그래도 그 가엾은 사람을 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 해 보겠어요. 저는 진심으로 그와 그 식구들을 크게 가엾이 여기고 있으니까요. 그런데 존스 씨, 이제는 제가 당신께 한 가지 부탁을 드려야겠네요.\"\n\n\"부탁이라니요, 아가씨!\" 톰이 외쳤다. \"아가씨께 분부를 받게 되리라는 기대만으로 제게 얼마나 큰 기쁨을 주셨는지 아신다면, 그 말씀을 꺼내신 것만으로도 제게 더없이 큰 은혜를 베푸신 셈임을 아실 겁니다. 이 사랑스러운 손에 두고 맹세컨대, 아가씨를 기쁘게 해 드리기 위해서라면 저는 제 목숨도 바치겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고는 그녀의 손을 덥석 잡아 열렬히 입을 맞추었으니, 그의 입술이 그녀에게 닿은 것은 이때가 처음이었다. 조금 전에 뺨을 떠났던 피가 이제는 얼굴과 목 전체로 어찌나 거세게 몰려들어 충분히 보상을 했던지, 온통 새빨갛게 물들고 말았다. 그녀는 이때 처음으로, 전에는 낯설기만 했던 어떤 감각을 느꼈는데, 그것은 그녀가 한가로이 곱씹어 볼 여유가 생기자 어떤 비밀들을 일러 주기 시작했다. 그 비밀이 무엇인지, 독자가 아직 짐작하지 못했다면 머지않아 알게 될 것이다.\n\n소피아는 말을 할 수 있게 되자마자(이는 곧바로는 아니었다), 자기가 그에게 청하려는 부탁이란 다름 아니라, 사냥할 때 아버지를 그토록 많은 위험 속으로 끌고 다니지 말아 달라는 것이라고 그에게 알렸다. 들은 바에 따르면, 두 사람이 함께 나갈 때마다 그녀는 끔찍하게 겁이 나서, 언젠가는 아버지가 팔다리가 부러진 채 실려 오는 모습을 보게 되지나 않을까 마음을 졸인다는 것이었다. 그러니 부디 자기를 봐서라도 좀 더 조심해 달라고, 또 웨스턴 씨가 으레 그를 뒤따른다는 것을 그가 잘 아는 만큼, 앞으로는 그렇게 무모하게 말을 몰지도, 그런 위험한 도약도 하지 말아 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n톰은 그녀의 분부를 충실히 따르겠노라 약속했다. 그리고 자신의 청을 너그러이 들어준 데 감사를 표한 뒤 작별을 고하고, 자신의 성공에 더없이 들뜬 채 떠났다.\n\n가엾은 소피아 역시 마음이 들떴으나, 그 결은 사뭇 달랐다. 그러나 그녀의 감정이 어떠했는지는, 설령 내가 옛 시인이 그토록 바라던 만큼 많은 입을 가졌다 한들—아마 그토록 푸짐하게 차려진 그 온갖 진미를 먹기 위해서였겠지—내가 그려 내는 것보다, 독자의 마음이(그나 그녀에게 마음이라는 것이 있다면) 더 잘 그려 낼 것이다.\n\n웨스턴 씨에게는 매일 오후, 술이 거나하게 오르자마자 딸이 하프시코드를 타는 것을 듣는 버릇이 있었다. 그는 음악을 무척 사랑하는 사람이어서, 어쩌면 도시에 살았더라면 감식가로 통했을지도 모른다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그는 헨델 씨의 더없이 빼어난 곡들마저 늘 못마땅해했으니 말이다. 그는 가볍고 경쾌한 음악이 아니면 결코 즐기지 않았으며, 실로 그가 가장 좋아하는 곡들은 〈늙은 시몬 왕〉, 〈성 조지는 잉글랜드 편이라네〉, 〈까딱거리는 조앤〉, 그리고 그 밖의 몇 곡이었다.\n\n그의 딸은 음악에 완벽히 통달했고, 마음대로 할 수만 있다면 결코 헨델의 곡 말고는 타려 하지 않았겠지만, 아버지의 즐거움에 어찌나 헌신적이었던지 그를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 그 모든 곡을 익혔다. 그래도 그녀는 이따금 아버지를 자기 취향 쪽으로 이끌어 보려 애썼고, 아버지가 그 발라드들을 다시 한 번 더 청하면 \"아니에요, 아버지\" 하고 대답하며, 부디 다른 것을 타게 해 달라고 종종 졸랐다.\n\n그러나 이날 저녁, 그 양반이 술병에서 물러나자 그녀는 조르지도 않았는데 그가 좋아하는 곡들을 죄다 세 번씩 거푸 타 주었다. 이에 어찌나 흡족했던지 선량한 지주는 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 딸에게 입을 맞추고는, 솜씨가 부쩍 늘었다고 맹세하듯 칭찬했다. 그녀는 이 기회를 틈타 톰에게 한 약속을 실행에 옮겼는데, 그 일이 어찌나 잘 풀렸던지 지주는, 〈늙은 시몬 왕〉을 한 번만 더 타 주면 이튿날 아침에 사냥터지기에게 임명장을 내주겠노라고 선언했다. 〈시몬 왕〉은 거듭거듭 연주되었고, 마침내 그 음악의 매력에 달래진 웨스턴 씨는 스르르 잠이 들었다. 아침이 되자 소피아는 그 약속을 일깨우는 것을 잊지 않았다. 그러자 곧바로 그의 변호사가 불려 와, 그 소송의 모든 후속 절차를 중단하고 임명장을 작성하라는 명을 받았다.\n\n이 일에서 거둔 톰의 성공은 머지않아 온 고장에 울려 퍼지기 시작했고, 그에 대한 평은 가지각색이었다. 어떤 이들은 착한 마음에서 나온 행동이라며 크게 칭찬했고, 또 어떤 이들은 비웃으며 \"게으른 녀석이 게으른 녀석을 좋아하는 거야 놀랄 것도 없지\" 하고 말했다. 어린 블리필은 그 일에 몹시 격분했다. 그는 존스가 블랙 조지를 좋아하는 꼭 그만큼 오랫동안 그를 미워해 왔는데, 자신이 그에게서 어떤 모욕을 받아서가 아니라 종교와 덕을 향한 자신의 크나큰 사랑 때문이었다. 블랙 조지는 행실이 헤픈 부류라는 평판이 있었기 때문이다. 그래서 블리필은 이 일을 두고 올워디 씨의 얼굴에 정면으로 침을 뱉는 짓이라고 몰아세우며, 그런 못된 자에게 선을 베풀 다른 동기란 도무지 찾을 수 없다고 자못 근심스러운 듯 단언했다.\n\n스왜컴과 스퀘어 역시 같은 가락을 읊어 댔다. 그들은 이제(특히 후자가) 그 과부를 두고 어린 존스를 몹시 질투하게 되었다. 그도 그럴 것이 그는 이제 스무 살에 가까워졌고, 정말이지 훤칠한 젊은이가 되어 있었으며, 그 부인 또한 그를 부추기는 태도로 보아 날이 갈수록 그를 점점 더 그렇게 여기는 듯했기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 올워디는 그들의 악의에 마음이 흔들리지 않았다. 그는 존스가 한 일에 매우 만족한다고 밝혔다. 그 우정의 한결같음과 곧음이 크게 칭찬받아 마땅하며, 그런 미덕의 본보기를 더 자주 볼 수 있으면 좋겠다고 그는 말했다.\n\n그러나 운명의 여신은, 어쩌면 그런 이들이 자기에게 더 열렬히 구애를 바치지 않는다는 이유에서인지, 내 벗 톰 같은 멋쟁이들을 좀처럼 그리 달갑게 여기지 않는데, 이제 그의 모든 행동에 사뭇 다른 방향으로 비틀어 놓았고, 그것들을 올워디 씨에게, 그 양반의 선량함이 그때껏 보아 온 것보다 훨씬 덜 흐뭇한 빛으로 비춰 보이기 시작했다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n아름다운 소피아의 그 모든 매력 앞에서도 무덤덤했던 존스 씨를 위한 변명. 여기서 우리는, 우리 시대 희극 대부분에 등장하는 주인공을 흡족해하는 재치와 풍류의 신사들이 보기에, 그의 인물됨을 상당히 깎아내리게 될지도 모르겠다.\n\n두 부류의 사람들이, 소피아를 대하는 그의 처신을 두고 이미 내 주인공에게 얼마간 경멸을 품었을까 봐 나는 염려스럽다. 앞쪽 부류는 웨스턴 씨의 재산을 차지할 기회를 흘려보낸 그의 분별없음을 탓할 것이고, 뒤쪽 부류는 그토록 어여쁜 처녀, 그가 팔만 벌리면 곧장 그 품으로 날아들 것 같던 처녀에게 그가 보인 머뭇거림을 못지않게 깔볼 것이다.\n\n자, 비록 내가 이 두 가지 비난 가운데 어느 쪽으로부터도 그를 온전히 벗겨 줄 수는 없겠지만(분별없음에는 변명의 여지가 없고, 뒤쪽 비난에 맞서 내가 내놓을 만한 것도 좀처럼 흡족하지 않으리라 짐작되기에), 그래도 증거란 때로 형량을 덜어 달라는 뜻으로 제출될 수도 있는 법이니, 나는 있는 그대로의 사실만 펼쳐 보이고 나머지 전부는 독자의 판단에 맡기겠다.\n\n존스 씨에게는 무언가가 깃들어 있었는데, 작가들이 그 이름에 대해 온전히 합의를 보지는 못한 듯해도, 그것이 어떤 사람들의 가슴속에 분명히 자리 잡고 있다는 점만은 확실하다. 그것의 쓰임새는 옳고 그름을 가려내는 데 있다기보다는, 앞의 것으로 사람을 부추기고 떠밀며, 뒤의 것으로부터는 사람을 붙잡아 멈춰 세우는 데 있다.\n\n이 무언가는 사실 극장의 그 유명한 궤짝 두드리는 사내에 견줄 만하다. 그것을 지닌 사람이 옳은 일을 할 때면, 넋을 잃고 박수를 보내는 다정한 관객조차 그토록 열렬하거나 그토록 소리 높여 갈채를 보내지는 못하기 때문이다. 반대로 그가 그릇된 일을 할 때면, 어떤 비평가도 그처럼 거세게 야유하고 그를 무대에서 끌어내리려 들지는 못한다.\n\n* 궤짝 두드리는 사내: 당시 극장에서 마음에 드는 장면마다 궤짝을 막대기로 요란하게 두드려 갈채를 보낸 것으로 유명한 관객을 가리킨다.\n내가 말하려는 이 원리에 대해 더 높은 관념을, 또한 지금 이 시대에 더 친숙한 관념을 떠올리게 하자면, 그것은 마음속 옥좌에 앉아 있는 것으로 여길 수 있겠다. 마치 이 왕국의 대법관이 자기 법정에 앉아 있듯이 말이다. 그곳에서 그것은 좌장이 되어 다스리고, 이끌고, 판결하며, 공로와 정의에 따라 무죄를 선고하고 유죄를 선고한다. 그 무엇도 빠져나가지 못하는 지식과, 그 무엇도 속일 수 없는 통찰과, 그 무엇도 더럽힐 수 없는 청렴함을 지닌 채로.\n\n이 능동적인 원리야말로 어쩌면 우리와 우리의 이웃인 짐승들 사이에 놓인 가장 본질적인 울타리라 할 수 있을 것이다. 사람의 형상을 하고도 그런 다스림 아래 있지 않은 자들이 있다면, 나는 차라리 그들을 우리에게서 우리 이웃 쪽으로 넘어간 탈영병으로 여기겠다. 그곳에서 그들은 탈영병의 운명을 맞아, 결코 첫째 줄에는 끼지 못할 것이다.\n\n우리의 주인공은, 이 원리를 스왝검에게서 물려받았는지 스퀘어에게서 물려받았는지 나는 단정하지 않겠지만, 그 인도 아래 무척이나 강하게 놓여 있었다. 그가 늘 옳게만 행동한 것은 아니었으나, 그릇되게 행동할 때면 결코 그것을 느끼고 괴로워하지 않은 적이 없었기 때문이다. 바로 이것이 그에게 가르쳐 주었다. 환대가 베푼 친절과 작은 우정에, 그 친절을 받았던 그 집을 터는 것으로 갚는 짓이야말로 도둑 중에서도 가장 비열하고 가장 천한 도둑이 되는 일임을. 그는 저질러진 해악의 크기가 크다고 해서 이 죄의 비열함이 덜어진다고는 여기지 않았다. 오히려 남의 은접시를 훔치는 것이 죽음과 오명을 받아 마땅하다면, 한 사람에게서 그의 재산 전부를, 게다가 그의 딸까지 통째로 앗아 가는 짓에 걸맞은 벌을 정하기란 그로서는 어려운 일로 보였다.\n\n그러므로 이 원리는 그가 그런 수단으로 제 재산을 일굴 생각조차 못 하게 막아 주었다(앞서 말했듯 이것은 능동적인 원리여서, 아는 것이나 믿는 것만으로는 결코 만족하지 않으니까). 만일 그가 소피아에게 깊이 빠져 있었다면 어쩌면 다르게 생각했을지도 모른다. 하지만 한마디 하게 해 달라. 사랑이라는 동기에서 남의 딸을 데리고 달아나는 것과, 도둑질이라는 동기에서 똑같은 짓을 하는 것 사이에는 큰 차이가 있다.\n\n자, 이 젊은 신사가 소피아의 매력에 무감각했던 것은 아니다. 그녀의 아름다움을 무척 좋아했고 그녀의 다른 모든 자질도 높이 샀지만, 그래도 그녀는 그의 마음에 깊은 자국을 남기지는 못했다. 이 점은 그를 우둔하다는, 적어도 안목이 없다는 비난에 노출시키니, 우리는 이제 그 까닭을 밝히는 데로 나아가겠다.\n\n사실인즉, 그의 마음은 다른 여인이 차지하고 있었다. 이 대목에서 독자가 우리가 이 문제에 대해 그토록 오래 입을 다물고 있었던 것에 놀라리라는 점, 그리고 이 여인이 누구인지 도무지 가늠하지 못하리라는 점을 나는 의심하지 않는다. 지금까지 우리는 소피아의 연적이 될 만한 누군가에 대해 단 한 마디 귀띔조차 흘리지 않았으니 말이다. 블리필 부인으로 말하자면, 톰을 향한 그녀의 애정에 얼마간 의심을 품고 있다는 사실을 언급하지 않을 수 없었으나, 지금껏 그가 그녀에게 어떤 애정이라도 품었다고 상상할 만한 여지는 조금도 주지 않았다. 그리고 참으로, 이런 말을 하기는 안타깝지만, 남녀를 막론하고 젊은이들은, 나이가 더 든 사람들이 때로 그토록 다정하게 베풀어 주는 그 마음에 대해 감사할 줄 모르기가 너무도 쉽다.\n\n독자가 더 이상 마음을 졸이지 않도록 하기 위해, 우리가 조지 시그림(흔히 사냥터지기 검은 조지라 불리는)의 가족을 자주 언급해 왔음을 떠올려 주기 바란다. 그 가족은 지금 아내와 다섯 아이로 이루어져 있었다.\n\n이 아이들 가운데 둘째는 딸이었는데, 이름은 몰리였고, 그 고장 전체에서 가장 어여쁜 처녀 가운데 하나로 꼽혔다.\n\n콩그리브가 잘 말했듯, 진정한 아름다움에는 속된 영혼이 도무지 우러러볼 수 없는 무언가가 있다. 마찬가지로 어떤 흙먼지나 누더기도, 속된 틀에서 빚어지지 않은 영혼들에게서 이 무언가를 가릴 수는 없다.\n\n그러나 이 처녀의 아름다움은 그녀가 열여섯에 가까워질 무렵까지는 톰에게 아무런 자국도 남기지 못하다가, 마침내 그녀보다 세 살 가까이 위였던 톰이 비로소 그녀에게 애정 어린 눈길을 보내기 시작했다. 그리고 그는 차마 그녀의 몸을 차지하려 시도하기로 마음먹기 훨씬 전부터 이 애정을 그 처녀에게 붙박아 두고 있었다. 그의 타고난 기질은 그를 이쪽으로 무던히도 떠밀었으나, 그의 원리 또한 그에 못지않게 강하게 그를 붙들었기 때문이다. 처지가 아무리 미천한 처녀라도 그녀를 유혹해 타락시키는 일은 그에게 매우 가증스러운 죄로 비쳤고, 그가 그 아버지에게 품은 호의와 그 가족에게 느끼는 연민이 이런 진중한 성찰을 모두 무척 강하게 떠받쳐 주었다. 그리하여 그는 한때 자기 욕망을 이겨 내기로 결심했고, 실제로 꼬박 석 달 동안이나 시그림의 집에 가지도, 그 딸을 보지도 않으며 자제했다.\n\n자, 앞서 말했듯 몰리는 대체로 매우 어여쁜 처녀로 여겨졌고 실제로도 그러했지만, 그녀의 아름다움은 가장 사랑스러운 종류는 아니었다. 사실 거기에는 여성스러운 구석이 거의 없어서, 적어도 여자에게 어울리는 만큼은 남자에게도 어울렸을 법했다. 솔직히 말하자면 젊음과 혈색 좋은 건강이 그 아름다움을 이루는 데 상당히 큰 몫을 했기 때문이다.\n\n그녀의 마음도 그 몸뚱이보다 더 여성스럽지는 않았다. 몸이 키 크고 다부졌듯, 마음도 대담하고 거침이 없었다. 그녀에게는 정숙함이라곤 어찌나 적었던지, 존스가 그녀 자신보다도 그녀의 정조를 더 아꼈을 정도였다. 그리고 십중팔구 그녀가 톰을 좋아하는 만큼 톰도 그녀를 좋아했으니, 그녀는 그의 머뭇거림을 알아채자 그에 비례하여 스스로 더 적극적으로 나섰다. 그리고 그가 그 집을 아예 발길을 끊은 것을 보자, 그녀는 그의 앞길에 자신을 내던질 방도를 찾아내어, 만일 그녀의 그 노력이 실패로 돌아갔다면 그 젊은이는 영웅 기질이 너무 넘치거나 너무 모자랐다고 할 수밖에 없을 만큼 그렇게 처신했다. 한마디로, 그녀는 이내 존스의 그 모든 정숙한 결심을 무너뜨리고 승리를 거두었다. 끝에 가서는 그녀가 자못 점잖게 마지못한 척 굴긴 했지만, 나는 차라리 그 승리를 그녀에게 돌리겠다. 사실 성공한 것은 그녀의 계략이었으니까.\n\n이 일의 처리에서, 다시 말하건대, 몰리는 제 역할을 어찌나 잘 해냈던지, 존스는 그 정복을 온전히 자기 공으로 돌렸고, 이 젊은 처녀를 제 격정의 거센 공세에 굴복한 사람으로 여겼다. 그는 또한 그녀의 굴복을 그를 향한 그녀의 걷잡을 수 없는 사랑의 힘 탓으로 돌렸다. 그리고 우리가 그의 빼어난 풍채를 한두 번이 아니게 언급해 왔으니, 독자는 이것이 매우 자연스럽고 그럴듯한 짐작이었음을 인정할 것이다. 실로 그는 세상에서 가장 잘생긴 젊은이 가운데 하나였으니까.\n\n블리필 도련님처럼 그 애정이 오로지 단 한 사람에게만 쏠려, 매사에 오직 그 사람의 이익과 안락만을 헤아리고, 다른 모든 이의 좋고 나쁨은 그 사람의 즐거움이나 이익에 보탬이 되는 한도에서만 따질 뿐 그 너머로는 그저 무관심하게 여기는 그런 마음들이 있는가 하면, 자기애에서조차 얼마간의 미덕을 빌려 오는 다른 기질의 마음도 있다. 이런 마음은 누군가에게서 어떤 종류의 만족을 받든, 그 만족을 베푼 상대를 사랑하지 않고서는, 또 그 상대의 안녕을 어느 정도 제 자신의 평안에 꼭 필요한 것으로 삼지 않고서는 결코 그 만족을 받아들이지 못한다.\n\n우리의 주인공은 바로 이 뒤쪽 부류였다. 그는 이 가엾은 처녀를, 그 행복과 불행을 자기 손에 매달려 있게 만든 사람으로 여겼다. 그녀의 아름다움은 여전히 욕망의 대상이었으나, 더 빼어난 아름다움이나 더 싱싱한 대상이었다면 더더욱 그러했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 욕망이 충족됨으로써 이 아름다움에 생겨난 작은 시들함은, 그녀가 그에게 눈에 보이게 품고 있는 애정에 대한 헤아림과, 그가 그녀를 몰아넣은 처지에 대한 헤아림으로 넉넉히 메워지고도 남았다. 앞의 것은 감사를, 뒤의 것은 연민을 자아냈고, 이 둘이 그녀의 몸을 향한 그의 욕망과 더불어 그의 안에 하나의 격정을 일으켰으니, 그 말을 크게 비틀지 않고도 사랑이라 부를 만한 것이었다. 비록 처음에는 어쩌면 그리 분별 있게 자리 잡은 사랑은 아니었을지라도 말이다.\n\n바로 이것이, 그가 소피아의 매력 앞에서 보인 그 무덤덤함의, 그리고 그를 향한 구애를 부추기는 것으로 충분히 그럴듯하게 풀이될 만했던 그녀의 그 처신 앞에서 보인 무덤덤함의 진짜 까닭이었다. 그는 자기 몰리를, 그토록 가난하고 의지가지없는 그녀를 저버린다는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었고, 마찬가지로 소피아 같은 사람을 배신한다는 관념도 품을 수 없었다. 그리고 만일 그가 그 젊은 아가씨를 향한 어떤 격정에 조금이라도 부채질을 했다면, 그는 그 두 죄 가운데 하나를 어김없이 저지른 셈이 되었을 것이다. 내 생각에는 그 어느 쪽이든, 이 이야기에 그가 처음 등장할 때 내가 그의 확정된 운명으로 흔히 예언되었다고 말했던 바로 그 신세를, 그에게 매우 정당하게 안겨 주었을 것이다.\n\n이 책에서 가장 짧은 장.\n\n몰리의 몸매가 달라진 것을 가장 먼저 알아챈 사람은 그녀의 어머니였다. 이웃들의 눈을 피하려고 그녀는 어리석게도 소피아가 보내 준 그 자루 같은 드레스를 몰리에게 입혔다. 사실 소피아라는 젊은 아가씨는 그 가엾은 여인이 자기 딸 가운데 누구에게라도 그 옷을 그런 모양으로 입히게 할 만큼 분별이 없으리라고는 거의 짐작조차 하지 못했다.\n\n몰리는 난생처음으로 자신의 아름다움을 한껏 돋보이게 할 기회를 얻은 것에 들떴다. 누더기를 걸치고 있을 때조차도 거울에 비친 자신을 흐뭇하게 바라볼 수 있었고, 또 그 누더기 차림으로 존스의 마음을, 어쩌면 다른 몇 사람의 마음까지도 사로잡은 그녀였지만, 그래도 고운 옷을 더하면 자신의 매력이 한결 빛나고 정복의 범위도 넓어지리라 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 몰리는 이 자루 같은 드레스에, 톰이 준 새 레이스 모자며 몇 가지 다른 장신구를 더해 차려입고는, 바로 다음 일요일에 부채를 손에 들고 교회로 향했다. 지체 높은 이들이 야심과 허영을 저희만의 것으로 여긴다면 큰 착각이다. 이 고귀한 성정들은 응접실이나 골방에서만큼이나 시골 교회와 그 묘지에서도 두드러지게 무성히 피어난다. 실로 교회 제의실에서도, 추기경 회의에 견주어 부끄럽지 않을 만한 음모들이 꾸며진 적이 있다. 여기에도 집권 세력이 있고, 여기에도 반대파가 있다. 여기에도 궁정에서나 볼 법한 책략과 술수, 당파와 파벌이 고스란히 있다.\n\n여기 여인들이라고 해서 지체와 재산에서 자기보다 나은 귀부인들에 비해 그 지극한 여성의 기예에 덜 능숙한 것도 아니다. 여기에도 새침데기와 요염한 여자가 있다. 여기에도 단장과 추파, 거짓과 시기, 악의와 험담이 있으니, 한마디로 가장 화려한 모임이나 가장 세련된 사교계에 흔한 것이라면 무엇이든 다 있다. 그러니 높은 자리에 있는 이들이여, 더는 아랫사람들의 무지를 업신여기지 말 것이며, 또 천한 이들이여, 더는 윗사람들의 악덕을 욕하지 말 일이다.\n\n몰리는 한동안 자리에 앉아 있었으나 이웃들은 그녀를 알아보지 못했다. 그러다 이윽고 회중 전체에 \"저 여자가 누구지?\" 하는 수군거림이 번졌다. 그러나 그녀의 정체가 드러나자, 여인들 사이에서 어찌나 비웃고 킥킥대고 낄낄대고 깔깔대는 소리가 이어졌던지, 올워디 씨가 직접 위엄을 내세워 그 자리에 그나마 체면이라도 지키게 해야 할 지경이었다.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n호메로스풍으로 뮤즈가 노래하는 한바탕 전투, 고전에 밝은 독자가 아니고서는 그 맛을 알 수 없는 이야기.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 이 교구에 영지를 두고 있었다. 그의 저택이 자기 집 교회보다 이 교회에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 있었던 까닭에, 그는 이곳으로 예배를 보러 오는 일이 잦았다. 그리고 마침 이날, 그와 사랑스러운 소피아가 함께 자리하고 있었다.\n\n소피아는 그 처녀의 아름다움에 무척 마음이 끌렸고, 동시에 그토록 차려입은 그 단순함을 가엾이 여겼다. 그것이 또래들 사이에 불러일으킨 시샘을 보았기 때문이다. 그녀는 집에 돌아오자마자 사냥터지기를 불러, 딸을 데려오라 일렀다. 집안에서 그 아이를 거두어 주겠노라고, 지금 떠나려 하는 자기 하녀가 나가고 나면 어쩌면 그 처녀를 자기 곁에 둘지도 모르겠다고 말하면서.\n\n가엾은 시그림은 이 말에 벼락을 맞은 듯 얼어붙었다. 그는 딸의 몸매에 생긴 허물을 모르는 바가 아니었던 것이다. 그는 더듬거리는 목소리로 대답했다. \"몰리가 한 번도 일을 해 본 적이 없으니 아씨를 시중들기엔 너무 서투를까 두렵습니다.\" \"그런 건 상관없네.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"곧 나아질 게야. 나는 그 아이가 마음에 드니, 한번 써 보기로 작정했네.\"\n\n블랙 조지는 이제 아내에게로 발길을 돌렸다. 이 곤경에서 자기를 빼내 줄 아내의 슬기로운 조언에 기대를 걸었던 것이다. 그러나 막상 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 집안이 어수선해진 것을 보았다. 그 화려한 드레스가 어찌나 큰 시샘을 불러일으켰던지, 올워디 씨와 다른 양반들이 교회를 떠나자, 그때까지 억눌려 있던 노여움이 한바탕 소동으로 터져 나왔던 것이다. 그 노여움은 처음에는 욕설과 웃음과 야유와 손짓으로 분출되더니, 마침내는 어떤 던지는 무기들에까지 손을 뻗쳤다. 그것들은 무른 성질을 지닌 탓에 목숨이나 사지를 잃을 위협까지는 아니었으나, 잘 차려입은 처녀에게는 충분히 끔찍한 것이었다. 몰리는 이런 대접을 고분고분 견디기엔 너무도 기가 셌다. 그리하여 그녀는 ─ 그러나 잠깐, 우리 자신의 재주가 미덥지 못하니, 여기서는 우리보다 한 수 위인 어떤 힘을 불러 도움을 청하기로 하자.\n\n오, 뮤즈들이여, 그대들이 누구든, 전투를 노래하기 좋아하는 이들이여, 특히 일찍이 후디브라스와 트룰라가 싸우던 그 들판의 살육을 읊었던 그대여, 만일 그대가 벗 버틀러와 함께 굶주려 죽지 않았다면, 이 위대한 자리에서 나를 도우라. 모든 것이 모든 이의 능력 안에 있는 것은 아니니.\n\n부유한 농부의 마당에 모인 한 무리의 소 떼가, 젖을 짜이는 동안 멀리서 자기 송아지가 그 빼앗김을 슬퍼하며 우는 소리를 듣고는 으르렁대고 울부짖듯이, 그렇게 서머싯셔의 군중도 함성을 내질렀다. 그 안에 모인 사람의 수만큼이나, 아니 그들 속에 든 감정의 수만큼이나 많은 비명과 고함과 갖가지 소리가 뒤섞인 함성이었다. 어떤 이들은 노여움에 사로잡혔고, 어떤 이들은 두려움에 떨었으며, 또 어떤 이들은 그저 재미를 좇는 마음뿐 머릿속에 다른 것이라곤 없었다. 그러나 무엇보다도 사탄의 누이이자 그의 한결같은 동무인 시샘이 군중 속으로 뛰어들어, 여자들의 광기를 부채질했다. 그들은 몰리에게 다가서기가 무섭게 진흙과 쓰레기를 퍼부었다.\n\n몰리는 보기 좋게 물러나 보려 했으나 헛수고였고, 마침내 돌아서서 맞섰다. 그녀는 적의 선두에서 나아오던 누더기 베스를 붙잡아, 단 한 방에 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 적의 온 군대는 (그 수가 백에 가까웠음에도) 자기네 장수의 운명을 보고는 여러 걸음 물러나, 새로 판 무덤 뒤로 후퇴했다. 교회 묘지가 바로 전쟁터였던 것인데, 그날 저녁에 그곳에서 장례가 치러질 참이었다. 몰리는 승세를 몰아, 무덤가에 놓여 있던 해골 하나를 집어 들어 어찌나 사납게 내던졌던지, 그것이 한 재단사의 머리에 맞으면서 두 해골이 부딪치며 똑같이 텅 빈 소리를 울려 냈다. 재단사는 그 자리에서 땅바닥에 제 키를 쟀고, 두 해골은 나란히 누워 둘 중 어느 것이 더 값진지 가늠하기 어려웠다. 그러고 나서 몰리는 넓적다리뼈 하나를 손에 쥐고 달아나는 대열 한복판으로 뛰어들어, 양옆으로 후하게 매질을 베풀며 숱한 용맹한 영웅과 여걸의 몸뚱이를 거꾸러뜨렸다.\n\n오, 뮤즈여, 이 운명의 날에 쓰러진 자들의 이름을 읊어 다오. 먼저 제미 트위들이 뒤통수에 그 무시무시한 뼈를 맞았다. 그를 길러 낸 것은 굽이굽이 다정하게 흐르는 스타우어 강의 정겨운 강둑이었으니, 그가 노래의 재주를 처음 배운 곳이 바로 거기였다. 그는 그 재주를 들고 밤샘 잔치와 장터를 이리저리 떠돌며, 시골 처녀와 총각들이 풀밭 위에서 흥겨운 춤을 엮어 갈 때 그들을 즐겁게 해 주었다. 그러는 동안 그 자신은 제 가락에 맞추어 바이올린을 켜고 폴짝거렸다. 이제 그의 바이올린이 무슨 소용이랴! 그는 제 몸뚱이로 푸른 바닥을 쿵 두드린다. 다음으로 늙은 돼지 거세업자 에키폴이 우리의 여전사 같은 여걸에게 이마를 한 대 맞고 곧장 땅에 고꾸라졌다. 그는 살집이 출렁이는 뚱뚱한 사내라, 집채만 한 소리를 내며 쓰러졌다. 동시에 그의 담배통이 주머니에서 떨어졌는데, 몰리는 그것을 정당한 전리품으로 챙겼다. 그다음에는 방앗간의 케이트가 운 나쁘게도 묘비에 걸려 나뒹굴었으니, 그 묘비가 대님 풀린 그녀의 양말을 붙잡는 바람에 자연의 질서가 뒤집혀, 발꿈치가 머리보다 위로 솟구쳤다. 베티 피핀은 젊은 연인 로저와 함께 둘 다 땅에 쓰러졌는데, 아, 짓궂은 운명이여! 그녀는 땅에 인사하고 그는 하늘에 인사했다. 대장장이의 아들 톰 프레클이 그녀의 노여움에 쓰러진 다음 제물이었다. 그는 솜씨 좋은 장인이라 훌륭한 나막신을 만들었으니, 더구나 그를 때려눕힌 바로 그 나막신이 제 손으로 만든 것이었다. 그 시각에 교회에서 시편을 부르고 있었더라면 머리가 깨지는 일은 면했으련만. 농부의 딸 크로 양, 그 자신이 농부인 존 기디시, 낸 슬라우치, 에스더 코들링, 윌 스프레이, 톰 베닛, 붉은 사자 간판을 내건 아버지를 둔 포터가의 세 자매, 하녀 베티, 마부 잭, 그 밖에도 이름이 덜 알려진 숱한 이들이 무덤들 사이에 나뒹굴었다.\n\n물론 몰리의 억센 팔이 이들 모두에게 닿은 것은 아니었다. 그들 가운데 많은 이가 달아나다가 서로 걸려 넘어졌으니 말이다.\n\n그러나 이제 운명의 여신은, 자기가 본분에 어긋나게 행동했다 싶고, 너무 오래 한쪽 편으로만 기울었다 ─ 더구나 그것이 옳은 편이었으니 ─ 싶어, 황급히 방향을 틀었다. 바야흐로 구디 브라운이 나선 것이다. 지키엘 브라운이 두 팔로 어루만지던 여인, 아니 그뿐 아니라 교구의 절반이 어루만지던 여인이었으니, 그만큼 그녀는 비너스의 들판에서 이름이 높았고, 마르스의 들판에서도 못지않았다. 그 두 들판에서 얻은 전리품을 그녀의 남편은 늘 제 머리와 얼굴에 달고 다녔다. 일찍이 사람의 머리가 그 뿔로 아내의 음탕한 영광을 드러낸 적이 있다면, 지키엘의 머리가 바로 그러했고, 잔뜩 할퀴어진 그의 얼굴 또한 그녀의 또 다른 종류의 재능을 (아니, 차라리 발톱이라 해야 할까) 적잖이 일러 주었다.\n\n이 여전사는 제 편의 수치스러운 패주를 더는 두고 볼 수 없었다. 그녀는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 달아나는 모든 이를 향해 큰 소리로 이렇게 외쳤다. \"이 서머싯셔 사내들아, 아니 차라리 서머싯셔 아낙들아, 너희는 이렇게 계집 하나한테서 달아나는 것이 부끄럽지도 않으냐? 허나 아무도 저 계집을 막지 않겠다면, 나와 여기 이 조앤 톱이 승리의 영예를 차지하겠다.\" 이렇게 말하고 나서 그녀는 몰리 시그림에게 달려들어, 손쉽게 그 손에서 넓적다리뼈를 비틀어 빼앗고, 동시에 그녀의 머리에서 모자를 낚아챘다. 그러고는 왼손으로 몰리의 머리채를 움켜쥐고, 오른손으로 어찌나 사납게 얼굴을 후려치는지, 이내 코에서 피가 줄줄 흘러내리기 시작했다. 그러는 동안 몰리도 가만있지 않았다. 그녀는 곧 구디 브라운의 머리에서 두건을 벗겨 내고, 한 손으로 머리채를 거머쥔 채 다른 손으로 적의 콧구멍에서도 또 한 줄기 핏줄기가 터져 나오게 했다.\n\n두 싸움꾼이 각자 상대의 머리에서 머리카락을 넉넉히 전리품으로 뜯어낸 다음, 그다음 분노는 옷가지를 향했다. 이 공격에서 그들은 어찌나 거센 힘을 쏟았던지, 불과 몇 분도 채 안 되어 둘 다 허리까지 알몸이 되었다.\n\n여자들에게 다행스러운 점은, 주먹다짐 전쟁의 요새가 그들에게는 남자들과 같지 않다는 것이다. 비록 싸움터에 나설 때면 그들이 제 성별에서 조금 벗어난 듯 보일지라도, 내가 보아 온 바로는, 서로의 가슴팍을 공격할 만큼 그렇게까지 잊는 법은 결코 없다. 그곳이라면 몇 대만 맞아도 대개는 치명적일 텐데 말이다. 어떤 이들은 이를 두고, 여자들이 남자들보다 피를 더 즐기는 기질을 타고난 탓이라 말하기도 한다. 그래서 피가 가장 쉽게 흐르는 부위인 코를 노린다는 것이다. 그러나 이는 억지스러운 데다 심술궂은 추측으로 보인다.\n\n구디 브라운은 이 점에서 몰리보다 크게 유리했다. 전자에게는 실로 젖가슴이라 할 것이 없었으니, 그 가슴팍은 (그렇게 부를 수 있다면 말이다) 빛깔로 보나 다른 여러 성질로 보나 오래된 양피지 한 조각을 꼭 빼닮아서, 누구든 거기를 한참을 두드려 대도 그녀에게 별 손상을 입히지 못했을 것이다.\n\n몰리는 지금 처한 딱한 형편은 그렇다 치더라도, 그 부위가 사뭇 다르게 빚어져 있었으니, 어쩌면 브라운의 시샘을 자극해 치명적인 일격을 부르고 말았을지도 모른다. 다행히 바로 이 순간 톰 존스가 운 좋게 당도하여 이 피비린내 나는 장면에 즉각 종지부를 찍지 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n이 우연한 등장은 운 좋게도 스퀘어 씨 덕분이었다. 그와 블리필 도련님과 존스는 예배가 끝난 뒤 바람을 쐬러 말에 올라 사 분의 일 마일쯤 달려갔는데, 그때 스퀘어가 마음을 바꾸어 (괜한 변덕이 아니라 한 가지 이유가 있었으니, 그것은 우리에게 틈이 나는 대로 곧 펼쳐 보이겠다) 두 젊은이에게 처음 작정했던 길과는 다른 길로 함께 가자고 청했던 것이다. 이 청이 받아들여진 탓에 그들은 어쩔 수 없이 다시 교회 묘지로 되돌아오게 되었다.\n\n앞장서 말을 달리던 블리필 도련님은 그렇게 모여든 군중과, 우리가 떠나올 때의 그 자세로 있는 두 여인을 보고는, 무슨 일인가 물어보려 말을 세웠다. 한 시골뜨기가 머리를 긁적이며 그에게 대답했다. \"지가 알게 뭡니까요, 나리. 황송하오나, 여기서 한바탕 싸움이 벌어진 모양인데요, 구디 브라운하고 몰 시그림 사이에 말입죠.\"\n\n\"누구, 누구라고?\" 톰이 외쳤다. 그러나 대답을 기다리지도 않고, 그는 지금 온통 흐트러진 와중에도 제 몰리의 얼굴 생김새를 알아보고는, 황급히 말에서 내려 말을 풀어놓고 담장을 뛰어넘어 그녀에게 달려갔다. 이제야 비로소 그녀는 울음을 터뜨리며, 자기가 얼마나 야만스러운 대접을 받았는지 그에게 일러 주었다. 이에 그는 구디 브라운이 여자라는 사실을 잊고서, 아니 어쩌면 노여움에 사로잡혀 그것을 알아채지 못하고서 ─ 사실 그녀에게는 페티코트 말고는 여자다운 데라곤 없었는데, 그마저도 그는 눈에 들어오지 않았을 것이다 ─ 그녀에게 말채찍으로 한두 대를 후려쳤다. 그러고는 몰이 죄다 지목한 군중에게 달려들어, 사방으로 어찌나 거침없이 매질을 퍼부었던지, 내가 또다시 뮤즈를 불러내지 않고서는 (마음씨 고운 독자는 뮤즈가 방금 그토록 호되게 땀을 뺀 터라 이를 좀 가혹하다 여길지도 모르겠지만) 그날의 채찍질을 도무지 다 읊을 수 없을 지경이었다.\n\n호메로스의 어느 영웅 못지않게, 또 돈키호테나 이 세상의 어느 편력 기사 못지않게 적의 온 진영을 말끔히 쓸어 버린 뒤, 그는 몰리에게로 돌아왔다. 그녀가 처한 형편은, 여기서 묘사한다면 나와 독자 모두에게 고통을 안길 만한 것이었다. 톰은 미친 사람처럼 길길이 날뛰며, 제 가슴을 치고, 머리를 쥐어뜯고, 땅을 구르며, 이 일에 가담한 모든 자에게 끝까지 복수하겠노라 맹세했다. 그러고는 제 외투를 벗어 그녀에게 입혀 단추를 채우고, 제 모자를 그녀의 머리에 씌우고, 손수건으로 그녀의 얼굴에서 피를 되도록 깨끗이 닦아 낸 다음, 하인에게 외쳐 되도록 빨리 말을 달려 옆안장이나 뒷안장을 구해 오라 일렀다. 그녀를 무사히 집으로 데려가기 위해서였다.\n\n블리필 도련님은 하인을 보내는 데 반대했다. 그들에게 하인이 하나밖에 없었던 것이다. 그러나 스퀘어가 존스의 명령을 거들어 주는 바람에, 그는 따를 수밖에 없었다.\n\n하인은 아주 짧은 시간 만에 뒷안장을 가지고 돌아왔고, 몰리는 제 누더기를 되도록 잘 추슬러 그의 뒤에 앉혀졌다. 그렇게 그녀는 집으로 실려 갔으며, 스퀘어와 블리필과 존스가 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n이때 존스는 제 외투를 돌려받고, 그녀에게 슬그머니 입맞춤을 한 뒤, 저녁에 다시 오겠노라 속삭이고는 제 몰리와 작별하고 일행을 뒤쫓아 말을 달려갔다.\n\n9장.\n그다지 평화롭다고는 할 수 없는 빛깔의 일을 담은 장.\n\n몰리가 늘 입던 누더기로 다시 갈아입기가 무섭게 언니들이 사납게 그녀에게 달려들었다. 특히 맏언니가 그러했는데, 꼴좋다고 쏘아붙였다. \"어디서 감히 어머니한테 웨스턴 댁 아씨가 주신 가운을 입을 생각을 한 거야! 우리 중 누가 그걸 입어야 한다면 말이야,\" 맏언니가 말했다. \"내가 제일 자격이 있다고 생각해. 그런데 넌 그게 네 미모한테나 어울린다고 여기겠지. 우리 중에 네가 제일 곱다고 생각하는 모양이지.\"—\"찬장 위에 있는 거울 조각이나 갖다줘,\" 다른 언니가 소리쳤다. \"나 같으면 미모 운운하기 전에 얼굴에 묻은 피부터 씻겠다.\"—\"넌 목사님 말씀이나 새겨들었어야지,\" 맏언니가 외쳤다. \"사내들 꽁무니나 좇지 말고.\"—\"정말이지, 얘야, 그랬어야 했어,\" 어머니가 흐느끼며 말했다. \"이 애가 우리 모두에게 망신을 안겼어. 우리 집안에서 갈보가 된 건 얘가 처음이라고.\"\n\n\"그걸로 저를 나무라실 건 없잖아요, 어머니,\" 몰리가 외쳤다. \"어머니도 결혼한 지 일주일도 안 돼서 저기 언니를 낳으셨으면서.\"\n\n\"그래, 이 못된 것아,\" 격분한 어머니가 대꾸했다. \"그랬다, 그래서 그게 무슨 대단한 일이라도 되더냐? 난 그때 떳떳한 여자가 됐어. 너도 떳떳한 여자가 된다면야 화도 안 내지. 그런데 넌 하필 신사 나리랑 놀아나야겠더냐, 이 더러운 계집년아. 넌 사생아를 배게 될 거다, 이것아, 그렇고말고. 그런 소리는 누구도 나한테는 못 한다, 어디 해 봐라.\"\n\n바로 이런 판국에 블랙 조지가 앞서 말한 용건을 가지고 집에 돌아와 가족을 마주했다. 아내와 세 딸이 한꺼번에 떠들어 대고, 그중 대부분이 울고 있었던 탓에, 그가 자기 말을 들려줄 틈을 얻기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 그러나 그런 틈이 생기자마자, 그는 소피아가 자기에게 한 말을 식구들에게 전했다.\n\n그러자 시그림 아주머니가 다시금 딸을 욕하기 시작했다. \"거 봐라,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네가 우릴 아주 고약한 곤경에 빠뜨렸어. 그 불룩한 배를 보면 아씨가 뭐라고 하시겠냐? 아이고, 이 꼴을 보자고 내가 여태 살았더란 말이냐!\"\n\n몰리가 기죽지 않고 대꾸했다. \"그래서 아버지가 저한테 구해 주셨다는 그 대단한 자리란 게 뭔데요?\" (소피아가 쓴 '몸종으로 곁에 두겠다'는 말의 뜻을 그가 제대로 알아듣지 못했던 까닭이다.) \"요리사 밑이겠죠, 뭐. 하지만 난 누구를 위해서도 설거지 따위 안 할 거예요. 우리 나리께서 더 잘 챙겨 주실 테니까. 오늘 오후에 나한테 뭘 주셨는지 보세요. 나리는 내가 평생 돈 걱정 안 하게 해 주겠다고 약속하셨어요. 그리고 어머니도 입만 다물고 가만히 분수를 지키면, 돈 걱정 안 하실 거예요.\" 그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 기니 금화 몇 닢을 꺼내 어머니에게 한 닢을 건넸다.\n\n착한 아주머니는 손바닥 안에 금화의 감촉을 느끼기가 무섭게 (그 만병통치약의 효험이란 이런 것이다) 성미가 누그러지기 시작했다. \"아니, 영감,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신처럼 멍청한 사람 말고 누가, 받아들이기 전에 그게 무슨 자리인지부터 물어보지 않았겠어요? 어쩌면 몰리 말마따나 부엌일일지도 모르지. 솔직히 난 내 딸이 부엌데기 노릇 하는 건 싫어요. 내 비록 가난하지만 양가집 여자라고요. 목사였던 우리 아버지가 빈털터리만도 못한 채로 돌아가시는 바람에 내게 한 푼 지참금도 못 물려주셨고, 그래서 나도 가난한 남자랑 결혼해 스스로를 낮출 수밖에 없었지만, 그래도 알아 두세요, 난 그런 모든 것보다 높은 기개를 가진 사람이에요. 원, 별꼴이야! 웨스턴 아씨야말로 제 집안이나 돌아보고, 제 할아버지가 누구였는지나 기억하는 게 좋을걸. 내가 알기로 우리 집안 사람들 중엔, 어떤 이들 할아버지가 두 발로 걸어 다닐 때 마차를 타고 다닌 사람들도 있었다고요. 그 낡은 가운 한 벌 보내 주고선 무슨 대단한 일이라도 한 줄 아나 본데, 우리 집안 사람들 같으면 그런 누더기는 길에 떨어져 있어도 줍지 않았을 거예요. 그저 가난한 사람들은 늘 짓밟히기 마련이지.—교구 사람들도 몰리 일로 그렇게 호들갑 떨 것 없었어. 얘야, 너도 그 사람들한테 말해 줬어야지. 네 할머니는 그보다 더 좋은 옷을 가게에서 새것으로 사 입었다고 말이야.\"\n\n\"아 그래, 그건 그렇다 치고,\" 조지가 외쳤다. \"내가 아씨한테 무슨 대답을 해야 하느냐고.\"\n\n\"무슨 대답이고 뭐고 난 모르겠소,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 늘 이렇게 식구들을 이런저런 곤경에 몰아넣는다니까. 당신이 자고새를 쏴서 우리 모든 불행의 발단을 만든 거 기억나요? 내가 웨스턴 나리 영지엔 절대 들어가지 말라고 충고하지 않았어요? 그 일이 어떻게 될지 내가 몇 해 전부터 누누이 말하지 않았느냐고요? 그런데도 당신은 기어이 그 고집대로만 했지. 그래, 그랬어, 이 못된 사람아.\"\n\n블랙 조지는 대체로 평화로운 부류의 사내여서, 성을 잘 내지도 경솔하지도 않았다. 그러나 그에게도 옛사람들이 '발끈하는 기질'이라 부르던 무언가가 깃들어 있었으니, 만일 그의 아내에게 지혜가 넉넉했더라면 그것을 두려워했을 것이다. 그는 오래도록 겪어 보아 알고 있었다. 폭풍이 거세게 일 때 논리란 그저 바람일 뿐이어서, 그것을 가라앉히기는커녕 오히려 부추긴다는 것을. 그래서 그는 작은 회초리 하나쯤은 늘 지니고 다녔는데, 여러 차례 써 본 결과 그것은 놀라운 효력을 지닌 처방이었으며, '못된 사람'이라는 말이 그 처방을 쓰라는 신호 노릇을 했다.\n\n그러므로 이 증상이 나타나기가 무섭게, 그는 즉시 앞서 말한 처방에 손을 댔다. 매우 효험 좋은 모든 약이 다 그러하듯 처음에는 병세를 한층 돋우고 들끓게 하는 듯 보였으나, 이내 완전한 평온을 가져와 환자를 더없는 안락과 고요로 되돌려 놓았다.\n\n하지만 이것은 일종의 말에게나 쓰는 독한 약이어서, 받아 삭이려면 아주 튼튼한 체질이 필요하고, 따라서 오직 천한 사람들에게나 맞는 처방이다. 다만 한 가지 경우만은 예외인데, 곧 태생의 우월함이 불쑥 튀어나오는 경우다. 그럴 때라면 어떤 남편이 그 처방을 쓴들 그리 잘못이라 여기지 않을 법도 하다. 단, 그 행위 자체가 워낙 비천한 것이어서, 굳이 입에 올릴 것도 없는 어떤 의술적 처치들처럼, 그 일에 쓰인 손을 너무도 깎아내리고 더럽히는 까닭에, 점잖은 신사라면 그토록 낮고 가증스러운 짓은 떠올리는 것조차 견디지 못할 것이다.\n\n온 가족은 곧 완전한 평정 상태로 돌아갔다. 이 약의 효능이란 전기의 그것과 같아서, 흔히 한 사람을 통해 그 도구에 닿지도 않은 여러 사람에게로 전해지기 때문이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 둘 다 마찰로 작용하는 것이니, 그 사이에 어떤 유사한 점이 있지 않을까 의심해 볼 만하다. 프리크 씨가 자기 책의 다음 판을 내기 전에 한번 잘 살펴보면 좋을 일이다.\n\n이제 회의가 소집되었고, 여러 논의 끝에, 몰리가 여전히 하녀로는 가지 않겠다고 버티는 통에, 마침내 시그림 아주머니가 직접 웨스턴 아가씨를 찾아뵙고 맏딸을 위해 그 자리를 얻어 보기로 결정되었다. 맏딸은 기꺼이 받아들이겠노라 밝혔다. 그러나 이 작은 가족에게 적이었던 듯한 운명의 여신은, 그 후 그녀의 출세에 제동을 걸고 말았다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n이튿날 아침 톰 존스는 웨스턴 씨와 함께 사냥을 나갔고, 돌아오는 길에 그 신사로부터 만찬에 초대받았다.\n\n사랑스러운 소피아는 그날따라 여느 때보다 한층 밝고 생기 넘치는 모습으로 빛났다. 그녀의 포화는 분명 우리의 주인공을 겨누고 있었다. 비록 그녀 자신조차 아직 제 속내를 잘 알지 못했으리라 믿지만, 그를 매혹할 의도가 조금이라도 있었다면, 이날 그녀는 보기 좋게 성공한 셈이었다.\n\n올워디 씨 교구의 부목사인 서플 씨도 그 자리에 함께한 사람 중 하나였다. 그는 천성이 너그럽고 훌륭한 사람이었으나, 무엇보다 식탁에서 유난히 말이 없는 것으로 이름났다. 물론 그렇다고 그 자리에서 입이 닫혀 있는 것은 결코 아니었다. 한마디로 그는 세상에서 손꼽히게 식욕이 좋은 사람이었다. 그러나 식탁보가 걷히기가 무섭게 그는 늘 자신의 침묵을 충분히 보상하고도 남았다. 워낙 마음이 따뜻한 친구였고, 그의 이야기는 종종 즐거웠으되 결코 거슬리는 법이 없었다.\n\n그가 처음 도착한 것은 구운 쇠고기가 들어오기 바로 직전이었는데, 그는 가져온 소식이 있노라고 넌지시 말을 꺼내며, 방금 올워디 씨 댁에서 오는 길이라고 이야기를 시작하려던 참이었다. 그러나 구운 쇠고기를 보는 순간 그는 입이 딱 막혀, 겨우 식전 기도를 올리고는 저 남작께 경의를 표해야겠다고 한마디 했을 뿐이었다. 그가 남작이라 부른 것은 다름 아닌 등심 고기였다.\n\n만찬이 끝나자 소피아가 그의 소식을 다시 일깨워 주었고, 그는 다음과 같이 입을 열었다. \"마님, 어제 저녁 기도 때 교회에서 젊은 여인 하나를 보셨을 줄 압니다. 마님께서나 입으실 법한, 별스러운 옷을 차려입고 있었지요. 마님께서 그런 옷을 입으신 걸 본 적이 있는 것 같습니다. 그렇지만 시골에서는 그런 차림이란,\n\n라라 아비스 인 테르리스, 니그로퀘 시밀리마 키그노.\n\n라는 거지요. 부인, 풀어 말하자면 '땅 위의 진귀한 새요, 검은 백조와 매우 흡사하다'는 뜻입니다. 유베날리스의 시구지요. 그러나 다시 제가 하던 이야기로 돌아가자면, 그런 옷차림은 시골에서 보기 드문 광경이라는 말이었습니다. 게다가 어쩌면 그 옷을 입은 사람으로 인해 더욱 진귀하게 여겨졌는지도 모릅니다. 사람들이 말하기를 그 여인은 나리의 사냥터지기인 검은 조지의 딸이라더군요. 제 생각으로는, 그가 겪은 고초가 그에게 더 분별을 가르쳐 주어, 제 딸년들을 그토록 화려한 옷으로 치장하지 않게 했어야 마땅했을 텐데 말입니다. 그 여인이 회중 가운데 어찌나 큰 소란을 일으켰던지, 만약 올워디 나리께서 잠재우지 않으셨다면 예배가 중단될 뻔했습니다. 저 자신도 첫 번째 성경 봉독 중간에 한 번은 멈추려 했을 정도였으니까요. 그렇긴 하나, 어쨌든 기도가 끝나고 제가 집으로 떠난 뒤에, 이 일이 교회 묘지에서 한바탕 싸움을 일으켰고, 그 와중에 다른 험한 일들 가운데 떠돌이 바이올린 악사 하나가 머리가 몹시 깨지는 일까지 있었습니다. 오늘 아침 그 악사가 올워디 나리께 영장을 청하러 왔고, 그 여인도 나리 앞으로 끌려왔지요. 나리께서는 일을 원만히 무마하려 하셨습니다. 그런데, 보십시오! 갑자기 그 여인이 (마님, 송구합니다) 말하자면 사생아를 막 낳을 임박에 이른 것으로 드러나는 게 아니겠습니까. 나리께서 그 아비가 누구냐고 물으셨지요. 그러나 그 여인은 한사코 아무 대답도 하지 않으려 했습니다. 그래서 제가 떠날 무렵 나리께서는 그 여인을 브라이드웰 감화원으로 보내는 수감 영장을 발부하려던 참이었습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 계집애가 사생아 가진 게 자네 소식의 전부란 말인가, 박사 양반?\" 웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"난 무슨 공적인 일이라도, 나라에 관한 무슨 일이라도 되는 줄 알았네.\"\n\n\"그게 너무 흔한 일이긴 합니다, 정말로요.\" 목사가 대답했다. \"하지만 이 이야기 전체가 한번 두루 곱씹어 볼 만하다고 여겼지요. 나랏일로 말하자면 나리께서 가장 잘 아시지 않습니까. 제 소관은 제 교구 너머로는 미치지 못합니다.\"\n\n\"하긴, 그렇지.\" 지주가 말했다. \"자네 말마따나 그 일이라면 나도 좀 안다고 할 수 있지. 자, 그건 그렇고, 토미, 한 잔 돌리게. 술병이 자네 앞에 있구먼.\"\n\n톰은 따로 볼일이 있다며 양해를 구하고는, 식탁에서 일어나 자기를 붙들려고 몸을 일으키는 지주의 손아귀를 빠져나가, 거의 예의도 차리지 않고 떠나 버렸다.\n\n지주는 그가 떠날 때 한바탕 욕을 퍼붓고는, 목사를 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"감 잡았네, 감 잡았어. 톰이 분명 이 사생아의 아비야. 이런, 목사 양반, 그놈이 그 계집의 아비를 내게 천거하던 거 기억나지 않나. 빌어먹을, 그 음흉한 놈 같으니. 그래, 그래, 두 푼짜리만큼이나 틀림없이 톰이 그 사생아의 아비야.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 참으로 안타까운 일이겠습니다.\" 목사가 말했다.\n\n\"안타깝긴 왜?\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"그게 무슨 대단한 일이라고? 뭐, 자네는 사생아 한 번 안 가져 봤다고 시치미라도 떼려는가? 빌어먹을! 그럼 자네 운이 더 좋은 게지. 장담컨대 자네도 그런 짓을 좋은 시절에 여러 번, 그것도 자주 해 봤을 테니까.\"\n\n\"나리께서 농을 즐기시는군요.\" 목사가 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 단지 그 행실의 죄스러움만을 나무라는 게 아닙니다. 물론 그것이야 분명 크게 경계해야 마땅한 일이지요. 다만 저는 그의 그 옳지 못한 행실이 올워디 씨와의 사이에서 그에게 해가 되지 않을까 염려하는 겁니다. 그리고 정말이지 말씀드리건대, 그가 좀 거칠다는 평판이야 있지만, 저는 그 젊은이에게서 어떤 해악도 본 적이 없습니다. 게다가 나리께서 지금 말씀하시는 것 말고는 그런 이야기를 들은 적도 없다 하겠습니다. 다만 그가 교회에서 응답할 때 조금 더 또박또박했으면 하는 바람이야 있지요. 그러나 두루 보아 그는,\n\n인게누이 불투스 푸에르 인게누이퀘 푸도리스.\n\n한 듯합니다. 아가씨, 이건 고전의 한 구절입니다. 영어로 옮기면 '천진한 얼굴빛에 천진한 부끄러움을 지닌 소년'이라는 뜻이지요. 이것이 라틴인들과 그리스인들 사이에서 크게 칭송받던 미덕이었으니까요. 제가 말씀드리건대 그 젊은 신사는 (출생이야 어떻든 저는 그렇게 불러도 좋다고 생각합니다) 제게는 무척 겸손하고 예의 바른 젊은이로 보입니다. 그러니 그가 올워디 나리의 평가에 스스로 해를 입히는 일이 생긴다면 안타까울 따름입니다.\"\n\n\"체!\" 지주가 말했다. \"올워디한테 해를 입어? 아니, 올워디 자신도 계집이라면 좋아하는 사람이야. 톰이 누구 아들인지 온 고을이 다 모른단 말인가? 그딴 얘기는 다른 사람한테나 하게. 난 대학 시절의 올워디를 기억하거든.\"\n\n\"저는,\" 목사가 말했다. \"그분이 대학에 다니신 적이 없는 줄 알았습니다.\"\n\n\"아니야, 아니, 다녔다네.\" 지주가 말했다. \"우리 둘이서 계집도 숱하게 데리고 놀았지. 오 마일 안에서 둘째가라면 서러울 만큼 지독한 오입쟁이였어. 아니지, 아니야. 그 일이 올워디한테는 아무 해도 안 될 걸세, 장담하지. 다른 누구한테도 마찬가지고. 저기 소피한테 물어보게. 너는 사생아 하나 가졌다고 젊은 녀석을 더 나쁘게 보지는 않겠지, 안 그러냐, 얘야? 아니, 아니지, 여자들은 그래서 그놈을 더 좋아할 게다.\"\n\n이것은 가엾은 소피아에게 잔인한 질문이었다. 그녀는 목사의 이야기에 톰의 얼굴빛이 변하는 것을 보았고, 그것이 그의 황급하고 돌연한 떠남과 더불어, 아버지의 의심이 근거 없는 것이 아니라고 여길 충분한 이유가 되었다. 이제 그녀의 마음은, 그토록 오랫동안 조금씩 조금씩 드러내 오던 그 커다란 비밀을 단번에 그녀에게 펼쳐 보였고, 그녀는 자신이 이 일에 깊이 마음 쓰고 있음을 깨달았다. 그런 처지에서 아버지의 무례한 질문이 별안간 그녀에게 들이닥치자, 의심 많은 마음이라면 능히 놀랐을 만한 몇 가지 기색이 드러났다. 그러나 지주를 위해 공정히 말하자면, 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니었다. 그래서 그녀가 의자에서 일어나, 아버지가 한마디 넌지시 비치기만 해도 늘 물러나기에 충분하다고 말하자, 그는 그녀를 방에서 나가게 두었다. 그러고는 무척 근엄한 얼굴로 한마디 했다. \"딸자식이란 지나치게 앞서는 것보다 지나치게 얌전한 편을 보는 게 낫지.\" 이 말에 목사는 크게 갈채를 보냈다.\n\n이제 지주와 목사 사이에는, 신문과 정치 소책자에서 짜낸 더없이 훌륭한 정치 담론이 이어졌다. 그 담론 속에서 그들은 나라의 안녕을 위해 포도주 네 병을 헌주로 바쳤다. 그러고는 지주가 곤히 잠들자, 목사는 파이프에 불을 붙이고 말에 올라 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n지주가 반 시간의 낮잠을 끝내고 나서 딸을 불러 하프시코드 앞에 앉히려 했으나, 그녀는 심한 두통을 이유로 그날 저녁만은 양해를 구했다. 이 면제는 곧바로 허락되었다. 사실 그녀가 그에게 두 번 청해야 할 일은 좀처럼 없었으니, 그는 그녀를 어찌나 뜨거운 애정으로 사랑했던지, 그녀를 흡족하게 해 주는 일이 곧 자기 자신에게 더없는 흡족함을 안겨 주는 일이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 그가 자주 부르던 대로 정말이지 그의 작은 사랑이었고, 또한 그렇게 불릴 자격이 충분했다. 그녀는 그의 모든 애정에 더없이 넘치는 방식으로 보답했기 때문이다. 그녀는 모든 일에서 그에게 더없이 흠 없는 효성을 지켜 왔으며, 이 효성을 그녀의 사랑은 쉬운 일로 만들었을 뿐 아니라 무척 즐거운 일로 만들어 주었다. 그래서 한번은 그녀의 벗 하나가, 그 젊은 아가씨의 표현을 빌리자면 그런 까다로운 순종에 그토록 큰 가치를 둔다며 그녀를 비웃었을 때, 소피아는 이렇게 대답했다. \"제가 이 일로 스스로를 대단하게 여긴다고 생각하신다면, 저를 잘못 아신 거예요. 저는 그저 제 도리를 다하고 있을 뿐인 데다, 동시에 저 자신을 기쁘게 하고 있는 거니까요. 정말이지 저는 아버지의 행복에 보탬이 되는 것만큼 큰 기쁨을 알지 못해요. 그리고 제가 스스로를 대단히 여긴다면, 그건 그렇게 할 수 있는 힘을 가졌다는 점에서지, 그것을 실행한다는 점에서가 아니에요.\"\n\n그러나 이것은 가엾은 소피아가 이날 저녁만은 맛볼 수 없는 만족이었다. 그래서 그녀는 하프시코드 앞에서 시중드는 일을 면해 달라고 청했을 뿐 아니라, 저녁 식사에도 빠지게 해 달라고 부탁했다. 이 청에도 지주는 동의했으나, 다소 마지못한 기색이 없지는 않았다. 그가 말이나 개나 술병에 빠져 있을 때를 빼고는, 그녀를 자기 눈앞에서 벗어나게 두는 일이 좀처럼 없었기 때문이다. 그럼에도 그는 딸의 바람에 굽혔다. 다만 그 가엾은 사람은 동시에, (이렇게 표현해도 좋다면) 자기 자신과 단둘이 있는 것을 피하기 위해, 이웃 농부를 불러다 함께 앉아 있어야 했다.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\n훨씬 깊이 인간의 본성 속으로 잠수해 들어가야만 했던 몇 가지 관찰과 더불어, 몰리 시그림이 가까스로 위기를 모면한 일.\n\n톰 존스는 그날 아침 사냥에서 웨스턴 씨의 말 가운데 한 필을 타고 다녔다. 그래서 자기 말은 그 지주의 마구간에 없었던 터라, 집까지는 걸어서 돌아가는 수밖에 없었다. 그는 어찌나 부지런히 걸었던지, 반시간 만에 삼 마일 남짓을 내달렸다.\n\n올워디 씨의 바깥 대문에 막 다다랐을 때, 그는 몰리를 붙잡아 끌고 가는 치안관 일행과 마주쳤다. 그들이 그녀를 데려가던 곳은, 신분이 낮은 부류의 사람들이 한 가지 좋은 교훈을 배울 수 있는 집, 곧 윗사람에 대한 존경과 공경을 깨우치는 곳이었다. 그곳은 운명이 정해 둔 커다란 차이를, 즉 자신의 잘못 때문에 교정을 받아야 하는 사람들과 그렇지 않은 사람들 사이의 차이를 그들에게 똑똑히 보여 줄 것이기 때문이다. 만일 그 교훈마저 배우지 못한다면, 그들은 교정의 집에서 다른 어떤 좋은 교훈도 좀처럼 배우지 못하고, 자기 품행을 나아지게 하는 일도 거의 없으리라 나는 생각한다.\n\n법률가라면 어쩌면 올워디 씨가 이 일에서 자기 권한을 다소 넘어섰다고 여길지도 모른다. 그리고 솔직히 말하자면, 그의 앞에 정식 고발 절차가 없었던 만큼, 그의 처사가 엄밀하게 적법한 것이었는지는 나로서도 의문이다. 그러나 그의 의도가 참으로 올곧았으니, 양심의 법정에서는 마땅히 용서받아야 할 것이다. 이만한 변명조차 내세울 수 없는 치안판사들이 날마다 그토록 많은 자의적 행위를 저지르고 있으니 말이다.\n\n톰은 치안관에게서 그들이 어디로 가는 길인지 듣자마자(사실 그는 혼자서도 거의 짐작하고 있었다), 곧장 몰리를 두 팔로 끌어안고는 모두가 보는 앞에서 그녀를 다정하게 껴안으며, 누구든 그녀에게 손대려 하는 자가 있으면 첫째로 그를 죽여 버리겠다고 맹세했다. 그는 그녀에게 눈물을 닦고 마음을 놓으라고 일렀다. 그녀가 어디로 가든 자기가 함께 가겠노라고. 그러고는 모자를 벗어 든 채 부들부들 떨며 서 있는 치안관을 돌아보며, 아주 부드러운 목소리로, 잠시만이라도 자기와 함께 아버지에게(이제 그는 올워디를 그렇게 불렀다) 돌아가 달라고 청했다. 그는 자신이 그녀를 위해 해야 할 말을 다 아뢰고 나면, 그 처녀가 분명히 풀려나리라고 장담했다.\n\n톰이 요구했다면 죄인을 순순히 내주었을 게 틀림없는 치안관은, 이 청에 아주 선뜻 응했다. 그리하여 그들은 모두 올워디 씨의 홀로 되돌아갔다. 거기서 톰은 자기가 돌아올 때까지 그들에게 기다려 달라고 한 뒤, 직접 그 선량한 사람을 찾아 나섰다. 그를 찾아내자마자 톰은 그의 발치에 몸을 던졌고, 끝까지 들어 달라고 간청한 뒤, 몰리가 지금 배고 있는 아이의 아버지가 바로 자기라고 고백했다. 그는 그 가엾은 처녀를 불쌍히 여겨 달라고, 그리고 이 일에 무슨 죄가 있다면 그 죄는 주로 자기 쪽에 있음을 헤아려 달라고 애원했다.\n\n\"이 일에 무슨 죄가 있다면이라니!\" 올워디가 발끈하여 대답했다. \"그래, 너는 하나님과 인간의 법을 어기는 것이, 가엾은 처녀를 타락시키고 파멸로 몰아넣는 것이 죄인지 아닌지를 의심할 만큼 그토록 방탕하고 막돼먹은 난봉꾼이란 말이냐? 그래, 정말이지 그 죄는 주로 너에게 있다. 게다가 어찌나 무거운지, 너는 그것이 너를 짓눌러 으스러뜨릴 것을 각오해야 마땅하다.\"\n\n\"제 운명이 어찌 되든,\" 톰이 말했다. \"이 가엾은 처녀를 위한 제 간청만은 들어주십시오. 제가 그녀를 타락시킨 것은 인정합니다! 하지만 그녀가 파멸하느냐 마느냐는 어르신께 달려 있습니다. 제발 부탁드립니다, 어르신, 영장을 거두어 주십시오. 그녀를 보내면 틀림없이 파멸하고 말 그런 곳으로는 보내지 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n올워디는 그에게 당장 하인을 부르라고 일렀다. 톰은 그럴 필요가 없다고 대답했다. 다행히 대문에서 그들과 마주쳤고, 그의 선량함을 믿고서 그들을 모두 홀로 데려와 두었으니, 이제 그들이 거기서 그분의 최종 결정을 기다리고 있다는 것이었다. 그는 무릎을 꿇은 채, 부디 그 결정이 처녀에게 유리한 쪽이기를, 그녀가 부모에게 돌아가도록 허락받기를, 그리고 그녀에게 어쩔 수 없이 떨어질 수치와 멸시보다 더 큰 수치와 멸시에 내몰리지 않기를 빌었다. \"압니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그것조차 지나친 청인 줄 압니다. 제가 그 사악한 빌미를 만들었음을 압니다. 가능하다면 보상하려 애쓰겠습니다. 그리고 언젠가 어르신께서 너그러이 저를 용서해 주신다면, 저는 그 용서를 받을 만한 사람이 되기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n올워디는 한동안 머뭇거리다가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"좋다, 구금 영장을 거두마. 치안관을 내게 보내거라.\" 곧 그가 불려 왔고, 임무가 해제되었으며, 그 처녀도 함께 풀려났다.\n\n올워디 씨가 이 일을 두고 톰에게 아주 호된 훈계를 빼놓지 않았으리라는 점은 짐작이 갈 것이다. 다만 그것을 여기 굳이 옮길 필요는 없다. 우리가 첫째 권에서 그가 제니 존스에게 한 말을 충실히 옮겨 적어 두었으니, 그 대부분은 여자에게나 남자에게나 똑같이 적용될 수 있기 때문이다. 굳은살 박인 죄인이 아니었던 그 젊은이에게 이 꾸지람은 자못 크게 와닿아, 그는 자기 방으로 물러가 그날 저녁을 홀로 깊은 우울에 잠긴 채 보냈다.\n\n올워디는 존스의 이 잘못에 적잖이 마음이 상했다. 웨스턴 씨가 뭐라고 단언했든 간에, 이 훌륭한 사람이 여자와 어떤 방탕한 쾌락에 빠져 본 적이 한 번도 없으며, 남들의 음란한 악덕을 몹시 나무라곤 했다는 것은 분명한 사실이기 때문이다. 실로 웨스턴 씨가 단언한 말에는 손톱만큼의 진실도 없었으리라 짐작할 만한 근거가 충분하다. 더구나 그는 그 부정한 짓들의 무대를 대학으로 설정했는데, 올워디 씨는 대학 근처에도 가 본 적이 없었으니 말이다. 사실 그 선량한 지주는, 흔히 허풍이라 불리는 그런 농담을 조금 지나치게 즐기는 버릇이 있었다. 그런데 그 농담은 훨씬 더 짧은 한 마디로도 적절히 표현될 수 있는 것이다. 어쩌면 우리는 그 짧은 한 음절의 자리를 다른 말들로 너무 자주 메우고 있는지도 모른다. 세상에서 흔히 재치와 해학으로 통하는 것 가운데 상당수는, 언어의 가장 엄정한 순수성으로 따지자면 마땅히 그 짧은 명칭을 받아야 할 것인데, 나는 여기서 점잖은 관습의 법도를 좇아 그 말을 삼간다.\n\n그러나 올워디 씨가 이 악덕이나 다른 어떤 악덕을 아무리 혐오한다 해도, 그 혐오에 눈이 멀어 죄지은 자에게 깃든 미덕을 못 알아볼 정도는 아니었다. 실로 그는, 같은 사람 안에 악덕이 조금도 섞여 있지 않을 때만큼이나 또렷이 그 미덕을 분간해 냈다. 그러므로 그는 존스의 음란함에는 노여워하면서도, 스스로를 고발해 보인 그 명예롭고 정직한 태도에는 그에 못지않게 흐뭇해했다. 그는 이제 이 젊은이에 대해, 우리 독자가 마음속에 품었기를 바라는 그런 의견을 마음속에 그리기 시작했다. 그리고 그의 결점을 그의 장점과 저울질해 보니, 오히려 장점 쪽으로 저울이 기우는 듯했다.\n\n그러므로 블리필 씨에게서 곧장 이 이야기를 전해 들은 스왜컴이 가엾은 톰에게 온갖 앙심을 다 쏟아부은 것도 아무 소용이 없었다. 올워디는 그들의 험담을 끈기 있게 다 들어주고 나서 냉랭하게 대답했다. \"톰 같은 기질의 젊은이들이 대체로 이런 악덕에 너무 쉽게 빠지는 법이오. 하지만 나는 그 젊은이가 이번 일로 내가 한 말에 진심으로 마음이 움직였다고 믿으며, 다시는 잘못을 저지르지 않으리라 바라오.\" 그리하여 매질의 시절은 이미 끝난 터라, 이 가정교사는 자기 입 말고는 쓸개즙을 풀 다른 출구가 없었으니, 그것은 무력한 복수가 흔히 기대는 가련한 수단이었다.\n\n그런데 스퀘어는 덜 거칠었던 대신 훨씬 더 교활한 사람이었다. 그가 어쩌면 스왜컴보다도 더 존스를 미워했던 만큼, 그는 올워디 씨의 마음속에서 톰에게 더 큰 해를 끼칠 궁리를 했다.\n\n독자는 둘째 권에서 이야기된 자고새와 말과 성경에 얽힌 자질구레한 몇 가지 사건을 기억할 것이다. 그 모든 일을 통해 존스는, 올워디 씨가 그에게 품으려 하던 애정을 해치기보다는 오히려 키워 놓았다. 우정과 너그러움과 큰 도량의 관념을 조금이라도 지닌 사람, 다시 말해 마음속에 선함의 자취를 조금이라도 지닌 사람이라면 누구에게나 그와 똑같은 일이 일어났으리라고 나는 믿는다.\n\n스퀘어 자신도, 그 여러 선행의 사례가 올워디의 그 빼어난 마음에 남긴 참된 인상을 모르지 않았다. 이 철학자는 비록 그것을 추구하는 데 늘 한결같지는 못했을지라도, 미덕이 무엇인지는 아주 잘 알고 있었던 것이다. 하지만 스왜컴으로 말하자면, 무슨 까닭에서인지는 단정하지 않겠으나, 그런 생각이라곤 그의 머릿속에 한 번도 들어선 적이 없었다. 그는 존스를 나쁜 쪽으로만 보았고, 올워디도 그를 같은 눈으로 보고 있으면서, 다만 자존심과 고집 때문에 한때 아꼈던 소년을 차마 내치지 못하는 것이라고 상상했다. 그렇게 하는 것은 곧 그 소년에 대한 자신의 예전 판단이 틀렸음을 슬며시 인정하는 셈이 되기 때문이라는 것이었다.\n\n그리하여 스퀘어는 이 기회를 붙잡아, 앞서 말한 그 모든 사건들을 아주 나쁜 쪽으로 비틀어 풀이함으로써 존스의 가장 여린 부분을 찔러 상처를 입혔다. \"어르신,\" 그가 말했다. \"유감스럽게도 저 역시 어르신과 마찬가지로 속았음을 고백하지 않을 수 없습니다. 솔직히 말씀드려, 저는 그것을 우정이라는 동기에서 비롯된 것으로 여겨 흐뭇해하지 않을 수 없었습니다. 비록 그것이 지나친 데까지 치달았고, 모든 지나침이란 결함이요 악덕이긴 하지만 말입니다. 다만 이 점에서는 젊은 나이를 참작했지요. 진실을 희생한 그 행위가, 우리 둘 다 우정을 위한 희생이라 짐작했던 그 행위가, 실은 타락하고 방탕한 욕정에 진실을 팔아넘긴 짓이었으리라고는 꿈에도 의심치 못했습니다. 이제 어르신께서는 이 젊은이가 사냥터지기 가족에게 베푼 그 겉보기 너그러움이 다 어디서 비롯되었는지를 똑똑히 보실 것입니다. 그는 딸을 타락시키기 위해 아비를 거두었고, 그 가족 가운데 하나를 수치와 파멸로 몰아넣으려고 그 집안을 굶주림에서 건져 낸 것입니다. 이것이 우정이라니! 이것이 너그러움이라니! 리처드 스틸 경이 말한 대로, '진미에 비싼 값을 치르는 식충이들이야말로 너그럽다고 불릴 자격이 충분하다'는 격이지요. 요컨대 저는 이 일을 계기로, 다시는 인간 본성의 나약함에 굴복하지 않으리라, 또 어김없는 올바름의 규칙에 정확히 들어맞지 않는 것은 그 무엇도 미덕이라 여기지 않으리라 결심했습니다.\"\n\n올워디의 선량함은 그런 생각들이 스스로 떠오르는 것을 막아 주었다. 그러나 다른 사람이 그의 눈앞에 그것을 펼쳐 놓고 보니, 그 생각들은 단번에 또 성급히 물리쳐 버리기에는 너무나 그럴듯했다. 실로 스퀘어가 한 말은 그의 마음속에 아주 깊이 파고들었고, 그것이 거기 일으킨 불편함은 상대방의 눈에도 또렷이 보일 정도였다. 그 선량한 사람은 이것을 인정하려 들지 않고 아주 시큰둥하게 대답하며, 애써 화제를 다른 데로 돌려 버리긴 했지만 말이다. 어쩌면 가엾은 톰으로서는, 그가 용서받기 전에 그런 말이 나오지 않았던 것이 다행이었는지도 모른다. 그 말은 분명 올워디의 마음속에 존스에 대한 첫 번째 나쁜 인상을 새겨 놓았으니 말이다.\n\n# Chapter xii.\n\n앞 장보다 훨씬 분명한 이야기를 담고 있으나, 그 앞 장의 사연과 같은 샘에서 흘러나온 것들에 관하여.\n\n독자께서는 저와 함께 소피아에게로 돌아가 주시면 기쁘겠다. 우리가 마지막으로 그녀를 본 뒤, 그녀는 그리 편안하지 못한 밤을 보냈다. 잠은 그녀에게 좀처럼 벗이 되어 주지 않았고, 꿈은 더더욱 그러했다. 아침에 시녀 어너 부인이 평소의 시각에 그녀를 시중들러 왔을 때, 그녀는 이미 일어나 옷까지 차려입은 채였다.\n\n시골에서 이삼 마일 떨어져 사는 사람들은 바로 옆집 이웃으로 여겨지며, 한 집에서 일어난 일은 믿기 어려울 만큼 빠른 속도로 다른 집까지 날아간다. 그러니 어너 부인은 몰리의 수치에 얽힌 이야기를 죄다 들어 알고 있었다. 본디 입이 가벼운 성품인 그녀는 안주인의 방에 들어서기가 무섭게 다음과 같이 이야기를 풀어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"어머, 아씨, 글쎄 아씨께선 어떻게 생각하세요? 일요일에 교회에서 아씨께서 보시고 그렇게 곱다고 여기셨던 그 처녀 말이에요. 하기야 가까이서 보셨더라면 그리 곱다고는 못 하셨겠지만요. 아무튼 그 애가 배가 불러서 치안판사 앞에 끌려갔다지 뭐예요. 제 눈엔 영 뻔뻔스러운 계집으로 보이더라니까요. 그러더니 그 애가 그 아이를 젊은 존스 도련님한테 떠넘겼답니다. 온 교구가 다 말하기를, 올워디 나리께서 젊은 존스 도련님한테 어찌나 화가 나셨는지 얼굴도 안 보려 하신대요. 그야 그 가엾은 젊은 분이 딱하긴 하지요. 그래도 그런 천한 것과 어울려 제 몸을 그리 떨어뜨렸으니 동정받을 만한 처지도 못 되긴 하지요. 그래도 워낙 잘생긴 신사분이라, 문밖으로 쫓겨나신다면 제가 다 안타까울 거예요. 장담컨대 그 계집도 도련님 못지않게 마음이 동했을 거예요. 늘 사내한테 들이대는 부류였으니까요. 계집들이 그렇게 먼저 달려드는데야 젊은 사내들을 그리 탓할 것도 없지요. 그야 본성대로 행동한 것뿐일 테니까요. 사실 그런 더럽고 질질 끌리는 치마짜리들과 얽히는 건 도련님 체면을 깎는 일이지요. 그런 것들이야 무슨 일을 당해도 싸요. 그래도 정작 가장 잘못한 건 그 천한 계집들이라니까요. 진심으로 그것들을 수레 꽁무니에 매달아 채찍질이라도 해 줬으면 좋겠어요. 잘생긴 젊은 신사분을 망쳐 놓다니 그게 어디 될 말이에요. 존스 도련님이 여태껏 본 가장 잘생긴 젊은 사내 가운데 한 분이란 건 누구도 부정 못 할……\"\n\n이렇게 줄줄 늘어놓고 있을 때, 소피아가 여태껏 그녀에게 써 본 적 없는 한결 짜증 섞인 목소리로 외쳤다. \"제발, 어쩌자고 그런 시시한 이야기로 나를 성가시게 하는 거냐? 존스 씨가 무얼 하든 그게 나와 무슨 상관이라고? 내 보기엔 너희는 다 한통속이야. 게다가 너는 정작 네 일이 아니어서 분한 모양이로구나.\"\n\n\"제가요, 아씨!\" 어너 부인이 대꾸했다. \"아씨께서 저를 그리 보신다니 서운하옵니다. 정말이지 누구도 저를 두고 그런 말은 못 할 거예요. 세상 젊은 사내들이 죄다 악마한테 가 버려도 저는 상관없어요. 제가 그분더러 잘생겼다고 한 게 잘못인가요? 그건 저뿐 아니라 다들 하는 말이에요. 정말이지 젊은 사내더러 잘생겼다고 하는 게 무슨 흉이 될 줄은 미처 몰랐어요. 하지만 이제는 결코 그분을 그리 여기지 않겠어요. 잘생겼다는 건 행실이 잘생긴 걸 두고 하는 말이니까요. 거지 계집 따위가!\"\n\n\"그 무엄한 말의 봇물을 멈추거라.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"그리고 아버님께서 아침 식사 자리에 나를 찾으시는지 가서 보거라.\"\n\n그러자 어너 부인은 혼잣말을 잔뜩 중얼거리며 방을 박차고 나갔는데, 그중 또렷이 알아들을 수 있었던 것이라곤 \"흥, 정말 너무하시네, 참\" 하는 말뿐이었다.\n\n어너 부인이 정말로 그 안주인이 넌지시 비친 의심을 받을 만했는지는, 그 답을 밝혀 독자의 호기심을 채워 드릴 수 없는 일이다. 그래도 소피아의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 오갔는지를 드러냄으로써 그 보상은 해 드리겠다.\n\n독자께서는, 존스 씨를 향한 은밀한 애정이 어느새 이 젊은 아가씨의 가슴속으로 슬며시 스며들었다는 것을 떠올려 주시면 기쁘겠다. 그리고 그 애정은 그녀 자신이 미처 알아차리기도 전에 이미 제법 높은 데까지 자라 있었다는 것도. 처음 그 징후를 느끼기 시작했을 때, 그 감정이 어찌나 달콤하고 흐뭇했던지 그녀는 그것을 억누르거나 물리칠 만한 결심을 차마 내지 못했다. 그렇게 그녀는 그 끝이 어찌 될지 단 한 번도 헤아려 보지 않은 채 그 연정을 고이 품어 왔던 것이다.\n\n몰리에 얽힌 이 사건이 처음으로 그녀의 눈을 뜨게 했다. 그제야 그녀는 비로소 자신이 저지른 마음의 약함을 깨달았다. 그것이 그녀의 마음에 이루 말할 수 없는 동요를 일으키기는 했으나, 한편으로는 여느 역겨운 약과 같은 효력을 발휘하여 한동안 그 병을 몰아내 주었다. 그 약효는 실로 놀랍도록 빨라서, 시녀가 자리를 비운 그 짧은 사이에 모든 징후를 어찌나 말끔히 걷어 갔던지, 어너 부인이 아버지의 부름을 전하러 돌아왔을 무렵에는 그녀가 완전히 마음이 편안해져, 존스 씨에 대해 철저한 무관심에 이르러 있었다.\n\n마음의 병은 거의 모든 면에서 몸의 병을 그대로 흉내 낸다. 그런 까닭에, 우리가 그토록 깊이 존경하는 그 박식한 의학계 여러분께서, 본디 그분들의 것인 여러 낱말과 표현에 우리가 어쩔 수 없이 함부로 손을 댄 것을 부디 용서해 주시기를 바란다. 그런 말들이 없었다면 우리의 묘사는 흔히 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 것이 되고 말았을 테니 말이다.\n\n그런데 마음의 병이 이른바 몸의 병과 가장 정확히 닮은 점이 있다면, 그것은 양쪽 다 재발하기 쉽다는 그 성질이다. 야망과 탐욕이라는 격렬한 병에서 이 점은 분명히 드러난다. 나는 궁정에서 거듭된 좌절(그것이야말로 야망의 유일한 약이다)로 야망이 다 나았던 사람이, 순회재판소에서 대배심 배심장 자리를 두고 다투며 그 병이 다시 도지는 것을 본 적이 있다. 또 탐욕을 어찌나 잘 다스렸던지 6펜스짜리 동전을 숱하게 베풀던 어떤 사람이, 끝내 임종의 자리에서, 자기 외동자식과 혼인한 어느 장의사와 자기 장례에 관하여 교활하고도 잇속 밝은 흥정을 맺으며 스스로를 위로했다는 이야기도 들은 바 있다.\n\n사랑이라는 일에 있어서도 ─ 스토아 철학에 엄격히 따라 우리는 이것을 여기서 하나의 병으로 다루겠다 ─ 이 재발하기 쉬운 성질은 조금도 덜 뚜렷하지 않다. 가엾은 소피아에게도 바로 그런 일이 일어났다. 그녀가 젊은 존스를 다시 본 바로 그 순간, 이전의 모든 징후가 되살아났고, 그때부터 차가운 발작과 뜨거운 발작이 번갈아 그녀의 가슴을 사로잡았다.\n\n이제 이 젊은 아가씨의 처지는 이전 그 어느 때와도 사뭇 달라져 있었다. 한때 그토록 더없이 달콤하던 그 연정이 이제는 그녀의 가슴속 전갈이 되었다. 그래서 그녀는 온 힘을 다해 그것에 맞섰고, 그것을 억누르고 몰아내기 위해 자신의 이성(나이에 비해 놀랍도록 강했다)이 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 논리를 끌어모았다. 이 일에서 그녀는 어느 정도 성공을 거두어, 시간과 떨어져 지냄으로써 완전히 나으리라는 희망을 품기 시작했다. 그리하여 그녀는 되도록 톰 존스를 피하기로 마음먹었고, 이를 위해 고모를 찾아뵙겠다는 계획을 세우기 시작했는데, 아버지의 승낙을 얻는 일이야 의심할 바 없다고 여겼다.\n\n그러나 머릿속에 다른 속셈을 품고 있던 운명의 여신은, 한 가지 사건을 끼워 넣음으로써 그런 일을 당장에 가로막아 버렸다. 그 사건은 다음 장에서 이야기하겠다.\n\n# Chapter xiii.\n\n끔찍한 사고가 소피아에게 닥치다. 존스의 용감한 행동, 그리고 그 행동이 이 젊은 아가씨에게 불러온 한층 더 끔찍한 결과. 더불어 여성을 옹호하는 짧은 여담.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 날이 갈수록 소피아를 더욱 아끼게 되었고, 급기야 그토록 사랑하던 사냥개들조차 그의 애정 속에서 그녀에게 거의 자리를 내줄 지경이었다. 그러나 그는 차마 개들을 포기할 수 없었던 터라, 아주 교묘한 꾀를 내어, 딸에게 함께 사냥을 나가 말을 타라고 고집함으로써 개들과 딸의 곁을 한꺼번에 누리려 했다.\n\n아버지의 말이라면 곧 법이었던 소피아는, 자신의 성정에는 너무 거칠고 사내답기만 한 그 놀이에 조금의 즐거움도 느끼지 못했지만, 순순히 그 뜻을 따랐다. 그러나 그녀가 노신사를 따라 사냥에 나선 데에는 순종 말고도 또 다른 까닭이 있었다. 자신이 곁에 있으면 어느 정도나마 아버지의 무모함을 억누를 수 있고, 그토록 자주 목숨을 위태로움 한복판에 내던지는 일을 막을 수 있으리라 바랐던 것이다.\n\n가장 큰 걸림돌은, 예전 같았으면 도리어 그녀를 끌어당겼을 바로 그것, 곧 그녀가 피하기로 마음먹은 젊은 존스와 자주 마주치게 되는 일이었다. 그러나 이제 사냥철이 끝나갈 무렵이었기에, 그녀는 고모와 함께 잠시 떠나 있는 동안 자신의 불행한 연정을 이성으로 온전히 떨쳐 내기를 바랐다. 그리고 다음 철에 들판에서 그를 다시 만나더라도 조금의 위험도 없으리라 의심치 않았다.\n\n사냥에 나선 둘째 날, 그녀가 사냥을 마치고 돌아오다가 웨스턴 씨의 집에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 이르렀을 때였다. 더 나은 기수가 다루어야 마땅했을 만큼 혈기 넘치는 그녀의 말이 갑자기 껑충거리며 날뛰기 시작했고, 그녀는 당장이라도 떨어질 듯한 절박한 위험에 빠졌다. 조금 뒤처져 있던 톰 존스가 이를 보고는 곧장 말을 몰아 그녀를 도우러 달려왔다. 다가오자마자 그는 자신의 말에서 뛰어내려 그녀의 말 고삐를 붙잡았다. 그 사나운 짐승은 이내 뒷다리로 곧추서며 그 사랑스러운 짐을 등에서 내던졌고, 존스가 그녀를 두 팔로 받아 안았다.\n\n그녀는 놀란 가슴에 어찌나 충격을 받았던지, 혹 다친 데는 없는지 몹시 알고 싶어 하던 존스에게 곧바로 대답조차 하지 못했다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 정신을 가다듬고, 무사하다고 그를 안심시키며, 자신을 돌봐 준 데 대해 고마움을 전했다. 존스가 대답했다. \"제가 아가씨를 구했다면, 그것으로 저는 충분히 보답받은 것입니다. 약속드리건대, 이번에 제가 겪은 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 불행을 무릅쓰고서라도 아가씨를 조금의 해도 입지 않게 지켰을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 불행 말씀이세요?\" 소피아가 다급히 되물었다. \"설마 어디 다치신 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"염려 마십시오, 아가씨.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"아가씨가 처했던 위험을 생각하면, 이렇게 무사하시니 하늘에 감사할 따름입니다. 제가 팔이 부러졌다 한들, 아가씨 일로 두려워하던 것에 견주면 그건 하찮은 일에 지나지 않습니다.\"\n\n그러자 소피아가 비명을 질렀다. \"팔이 부러지셨다고요! 어찌 그런 일이.\"\n\n\"아무래도 그런 듯합니다, 아가씨.\" 존스가 말했다. \"하지만 우선 제가 아가씨를 보살피게 해 주십시오. 아직 성한 오른손이 있으니, 다음 들판까지는 아가씨를 도와 드릴 수 있습니다. 거기서 아버님 댁까지는 잠깐만 걸으면 되니까요.\"\n\n소피아는 그가 한쪽 손으로 자신을 이끄는 동안 다른 한 팔, 곧 왼팔이 옆구리에 축 늘어져 흔들리는 것을 보고는 더 이상 그 말을 의심하지 않았다. 그녀는 조금 전 자신을 향한 두려움으로 질렸던 것보다 훨씬 더 새파랗게 질렸다. 온몸이 떨려 와, 존스가 그녀를 부축하기도 버거울 지경이었다. 마음 또한 그에 못지않게 요동쳤던 터라, 그녀는 존스에게 너무도 다정함이 가득한 눈빛을 보내고야 말았다. 그 눈빛은, 가장 강력한 제3의 감정의 도움 없이는 감사와 연민이 한데 합쳐진다 해도 가장 부드러운 여인의 가슴속에 불러일으킬 수 있는 것보다 더 강한 무언가가 그녀의 마음속에 일고 있음을 거의 드러내고 있었다.\n\n이 사고가 일어났을 때 얼마간 앞서 나가 있던 웨스턴 씨가 이제 돌아왔고, 다른 기수들도 마찬가지였다. 소피아는 곧바로 그들에게 존스에게 일어난 일을 알리고, 그를 잘 돌봐 달라고 청했다. 그러자 기수 없이 돌아온 딸의 말을 보고 몹시 놀랐다가, 이제 딸이 무사한 것을 알고 한껏 기뻐하던 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"이만하길 다행이로구나. 톰이 팔이 부러졌다면, 목수를 불러 다시 붙여 주면 될 일이지.\"\n\n향사는 말에서 내려, 딸과 존스와 함께 걸어서 집으로 향했다. 길에서 그들과 마주친 공정한 구경꾼이 있었다면, 그 세 사람의 표정을 살펴보고는 동정을 받아야 할 사람은 오직 소피아뿐이라고 결론지었을 것이다. 존스로 말하자면, 그는 겨우 뼈 하나가 부러진 대가로 어쩌면 이 젊은 아가씨의 목숨을 구했다는 사실에 의기양양했고, 웨스턴 씨는 존스에게 닥친 사고를 무심히 넘긴 것은 아니었으나, 딸이 운 좋게 화를 면한 것에 훨씬 더 높은 정도로 기뻐하고 있었으니 말이다.\n\n너그러운 소피아의 천성은 존스의 이 행동을 대단한 용기로 받아들였고, 그것은 그녀의 가슴에 깊은 자국을 남겼다. 이것만큼 남자를 여자에게 널리 추천해 주는 자질은 달리 없는 것이 분명하니 말이다. 흔한 의견을 믿자면, 이는 여성의 타고난 소심함에서 비롯하는 것인데, 오즈번 씨의 말로는 그 소심함이 \"어찌나 큰지, 여자는 하느님이 지으신 모든 피조물 가운데 가장 겁 많은 존재\"라는 것이다. 그러나 이는 진실보다는 그 무뚝뚝함으로 더 두드러지는 견해다. 아리스토텔레스는 그의 《정치학》에서, 내 생각으로는 여성에게 좀 더 공정하게 말하고 있다. \"남자의 정숙함과 굳셈은 여자에게서의 그 덕목들과 다르다. 여자에게 어울리는 굳셈은 남자에게서는 비겁함이 될 것이며, 남자에게 어울리는 정숙함은 여자에게서는 건방짐이 될 것이다.\" 또한 여자들이 용감한 자에게 보이는 편애를, 이 지나친 두려움에서 끌어내는 사람들의 견해에도 더 많은 진실이 담겨 있는 것 같지는 않다. 베일 씨는 (내 기억으로는 헬레네에 관한 항목에서) 이를, 한층 더 그럴듯하게, 여자들의 격렬한 영예욕에서 비롯한 것으로 본다. 그 진실성에 대해서는, 누구보다도 인간 본성을 가장 멀리까지 꿰뚫어 본 그 시인의 권위가 우리 곁에 있다. 그는 자신의 《오디세이아》의 여주인공, 부부간 사랑과 정절의 위대한 본보기로 하여금, 남편을 향한 자신의 애정의 유일한 원천으로 남편의 영예를 꼽게 하고 있으니 말이다.[*]\n\n[*] 영어권 독자는 이 대목을 그 시에서 찾지 못할 것이다. 이 정서는 번역본에서 통째로 빠져 있기 때문이다.\n\n어찌 되었든, 이 사고가 소피아에게 매우 강하게 작용한 것만은 분명하다. 그리고 사실, 이 문제를 한참 캐어 본 끝에 나는 이렇게 믿게 되었다. 바로 이때, 어여쁜 소피아 또한 존스의 마음에 그에 못지않은 자국을 남겼으리라고. 사실대로 말하자면, 그는 얼마 전부터 그녀의 매력이 지닌 거역할 수 없는 힘을 느끼고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter xiv.\n\n외과의가 도착하다. 그의 처치, 그리고 소피아와 하녀 사이의 긴 대화.\n\n웨스턴 씨의 저택 현관에 다다랐을 때, 어렵사리 비틀거리며 걸어온 소피아는 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 그러나 사슴뿔 정기와 물의 도움으로 기절은 면했고, 존스에게 부르러 보냈던 외과의가 나타났을 무렵에는 꽤 정신을 차린 상태였다. 딸의 이런 증세를 낙마 탓으로 돌린 웨스턴 씨는, 예방 삼아 당장 피를 빼는 게 좋겠다고 권했다. 이 의견에 외과의도 거들었는데, 그는 사혈을 해야 하는 이유를 어찌나 많이 늘어놓고, 사혈을 하지 않아 잘못된 사례들을 어찌나 많이 인용했던지, 향사는 몹시 끈질겨졌으며, 실로 딸이 반드시 피를 빼야 한다고 단호하게 고집했다.\n\n소피아는 자신의 뜻과는 전혀 어긋났지만 곧 아버지의 명령에 따랐다. 내 생각에 그녀는 이 충격에서 비롯될 위험을 향사나 외과의보다 더 작게 여겼던 것 같다. 그녀는 이윽고 아름다운 팔을 내밀었고, 시술자는 작업 채비를 시작했다.\n\n하인들이 분주히 도구를 마련하는 동안, 외과의는 소피아가 보인 머뭇거림을 두려움 탓으로 돌리고는, 조금도 위험할 일이 없다고 거듭 안심시키며 그녀를 달래기 시작했다. 그의 말로는, 사혈에서 사고가 생기는 일이란 외과술을 흉내 내는 자들의 끔찍한 무지에서나 비롯될 뿐인데, 지금 이 자리에서는 그런 걱정을 할 필요가 없다는 것을 그는 꽤 노골적으로 내비쳤다. 소피아는 자신은 조금도 두렵지 않다고 밝히며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"혹 동맥을 잘못 여신다 해도, 약속하는데 용서해 드리겠어요.\" \"용서한다고?\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"제기랄, 나는 안 그래. 이자가 너에게 조금이라도 해를 끼친다면, 빌어먹을, 이자의 심장 피를 다 뽑아내고 말겠다.\" 외과의는 이런 조건 아래 사혈하기로 동의하고는 시술에 들어갔는데, 약속한 대로 능숙하게, 또 그만큼 신속하게 해냈다. 그는 그녀에게서 피를 조금만 뽑으며, 한꺼번에 너무 많이 빼는 것보다 거듭거듭 빼는 편이 훨씬 안전하다고 말했다.\n\n팔을 동여매고 나자 소피아는 자리를 떴다. 존스에게 시술하는 자리에 함께 있고 싶지 않았기 때문이다(어쩌면 엄밀히 따져 그게 예법에 맞지도 않았을 것이다). 사실 그녀가 사혈을 꺼린 한 가지 이유는(입 밖에 내지는 않았지만) 그것이 부러진 뼈를 맞추는 일을 늦추리라는 점이었다. 소피아와 관련된 일에서라면 웨스턴은 오직 딸 생각뿐이었고, 존스 본인으로 말하자면 그는 \"묘비 위에 앉은 인내처럼 슬픔을 향해 미소 짓고\" 있었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 소피아의 사랑스러운 팔에서 피가 솟구치는 것을 보았을 때 그는 자기 몸에 닥친 일은 거의 떠올리지도 못했다.\n\n이제 외과의는 환자에게 셔츠만 남기고 옷을 벗기라 명하고는, 팔을 완전히 드러낸 다음 그것을 잡아당기고 살피기 시작했는데, 그 통증이 어찌나 심했던지 존스는 몇 번이나 얼굴을 일그러뜨렸다. 이를 지켜보던 외과의는 몹시 의아해하며 외쳤다. \"왜 그러십니까, 선생? 제가 선생을 아프게 했을 리가 없는데요.\" 그러고는 부러진 팔을 들어 올린 채, 길고도 무척이나 학식 있는 해부학 강의를 시작했다. 단순 골절과 복합 골절을 더없이 정밀하게 따지고, 존스가 팔을 부러뜨릴 수 있었던 여러 경우를 논하면서, 그 가운데 몇 가지는 지금보다 나았을 것이고 몇 가지는 더 나빴을 것이라는 적절한 주석까지 곁들였다.\n\n마침내 그 공들인 일장연설을 끝냈을 때—듣는 이들의 주의와 감탄은 크게 끌어올렸으나, 그가 한 말이라곤 단 한 음절도 정작 알아듣지 못했으니 별로 깨우친 바는 없었다—그는 본업에 들어갔고, 시작할 때보다는 마무리하는 데 훨씬 더 빨랐다.\n\n이윽고 존스는 침대로 옮겨지라는 명을 받았는데, 웨스턴 씨가 자기 집에서 그것을 받아들이라고 강권한 것이었다. 그리고 그에게는 물죽이라는 처분이 내려졌다.\n\n뼈를 맞추는 동안 현관에 모여 있던 점잖은 사람들 가운데 어너 부인도 있었다. 일이 끝나자마자 그녀는 안주인에게 불려 갔고, 안주인이 그 젊은 신사가 어떠냐고 묻자 곧장 그의 행동이 보인 의연함—그녀의 표현이었다—을 두고 과장된 찬사를 쏟아냈다. 그녀의 말로는 \"그토록 잘생긴 사람에게 그런 의연함이 어찌나 매력적인지\" 모른다는 것이었다. 그러더니 그의 외모가 지닌 아름다움에 대해 한층 더 뜨거운 칭송을 터뜨리며, 이런저런 특징을 일일이 열거하다가 끝내 그 살결의 흰빛으로 마무리했다.\n\n이 이야기는 소피아의 낯빛에 어떤 변화를 일으켰는데, 만일 그 영민한 시중꾼이 말하는 내내 한 번이라도 안주인의 얼굴을 마주 보았더라면 그 변화를 놓치지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 맞은편 더없이 편리한 자리에 놓인 거울이, 그녀가 무엇보다 가장 흐뭇해하는 그 이목구비—다름 아닌 자기 자신의 얼굴—를 들여다볼 기회를 주었던 터라, 그녀는 말을 하는 동안 단 한 번도 그 사랑스러운 대상에서 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n어너 부인은 자신의 혀를 놀리는 주제와 눈앞의 대상에 어찌나 완전히 빠져 있었던지, 안주인이 당혹감을 다스릴 시간을 주고 말았다. 마음을 추스른 소피아는 하녀를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다. \"너 분명 이 젊은이를 사랑하고 있구나.\" \"제가 사랑한다고요, 아씨!\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"맹세코, 아씨, 정말 아니에요, 아씨, 제 영혼을 걸고요, 아씨, 절대 아닙니다.\" \"왜, 설령 그렇다 해도,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"부끄러워할 이유는 없어 보이는데. 정말 잘생긴 청년이니까.\" \"맞아요, 아씨,\" 다른 쪽이 대답했다. \"정말 그래요, 제 평생 본 사람 중에 가장 잘생겼지요. 네, 정말 그렇고요, 아씨 말씀처럼 그를 사랑한다 해도 부끄러워할 까닭이 없지요, 비록 저보다 윗분이긴 해도요. 하긴, 양반네라고 해 봤자 우리 같은 하인들과 똑같이 피와 살로 된 사람일 뿐인걸요. 게다가 존스 씨로 말하면, 올워디 향사께서 그를 신사로 만들어 주셨다지만, 태생으로 따지면 저만도 못했어요. 저야 가난한 몸이지만 그래도 정직한 사람의 자식이고, 제 아버지와 어머니는 혼인한 사이였으니까요. 그건 아무리 콧대 높이 쳐든 사람이라도 다 할 수 있는 말은 아니잖아요. 흥, 어디 한번 잘난 척해 보시지! 정말이지, 이 더러운 친척아! 그 살결이 그토록 희다지만, 하긴 여태 본 중에 가장 흰 살결이긴 하지만, 저도 그 사람만큼이나 떳떳한 기독교인이고, 누구도 저를 천하게 태어났다고는 못 해요. 제 할아버지는 성직자셨고요, 당신네 집안 누군가가 몰리 시그림이 버린 더러운 찌꺼기를 주워 든다는 걸 아셨다면 무척이나 노여워하셨을 거예요.\"\n\n[*] 이 이야기에서 우리가 성직자 출신으로 기록한 신분 낮은 인물로는 이번이 두 번째다. 바라건대 훗날, 하급 성직자의 가족에게 어떤 생계 대책이 마련되는 시대가 오면, 이런 사례가 지금 여겨질 수 있는 것보다 한층 더 기이하게 보이기를 바란다.\n\n어쩌면 소피아는 하녀의 혀를 멈출 만한 기력이 모자라 그녀가 이런 식으로 떠들게 내버려 두었는지도 모른다. 독자도 짐작하겠지만, 그 혀를 멈추는 일은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었으니까. 분명 그녀의 말 가운데에는 아씨에게 도무지 달갑지 않은 대목들이 있었다. 그러나 이제 소피아는 그 봇물을 막아섰다. 흐르는 데 끝이 없어 보였기 때문이다. \"참 놀랍구나,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어찌 그리 뻔뻔하게 우리 아버지의 친구 한 분을 두고 이렇게 함부로 떠들 수 있는지. 그리고 그 계집애로 말하자면, 다시는 내 앞에서 그 이름을 입에 올리지 말거라. 또 그 젊은 신사의 출생에 관해서라면, 그 점 말고는 그를 깎아내릴 거리가 없는 자들은 그 일에 대해 차라리 입을 다무는 편이 낫지. 너도 앞으로는 그렇게 하기를 바란다.\"\n\n\"아씨를 노엽게 해 드려 죄송합니다,\" 어너 부인이 대답했다. \"저도 몰리 시그림이라면 아씨 못지않게 미워한다고요. 또 존스 도련님을 욕하다니요, 집안의 하인들을 다 증인으로 부를 수 있어요. 사생아 이야기가 나올 때마다 저는 늘 그분 편을 들었거든요. 제가 하인들한테 그랬어요, 너희 중에 누가 할 수만 있다면 신사로 키워지려고 사생아가 되지 않겠느냐고요? 그리고 제가 그랬죠, 그분은 정말 훌륭한 신사고, 세상에서 가장 흰 손을 가진 분 중 하나라고요. 정말 그러니까요. 또 제가 그랬어요, 세상에서 가장 성품 곱고, 가장 마음씨 좋은 분 중 하나라고요. 또 제가 그랬죠, 온 동네 하인들과 이웃들이 모두 그분을 좋아한다고요. 그리고 정말이지, 아씨께 한 가지 말씀드릴 게 있긴 한데, 노여워하실까 봐 두렵네요.\" \"무슨 말을 할 수 있다는 거냐, 어너?\" 소피아가 물었다. \"아니에요, 아씨, 정말이지 그분은 아무 뜻 없이 그러신 거예요, 그러니 아씨께서 노여워하지 않으셨으면 해서요.\" \"어서 말해 보거라,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"지금 당장 알아야겠다.\" \"그게요, 아씨,\" 어너 부인이 대답했다. \"지난주 어느 날 제가 일을 하고 있는데 그분이 방에 들어오셨어요. 그때 의자 위에 아씨의 토시가 놓여 있었는데, 정말이지 그분이 거기에 두 손을 넣으셨지 뭐예요. 바로 어제 아씨께서 제게 주신 그 토시 말이에요. 어머! 제가 그랬죠, 존스 씨, 우리 아씨 토시가 늘어나서 못 쓰게 돼요. 그런데도 그분은 손을 그 안에 그대로 두시더니, 거기에 입을 맞추셨어요. 정말이지 제 평생 그런 입맞춤은 거의 본 적이 없을 정도였어요.\" \"그게 내 것인 줄 몰랐겠지,\" 소피아가 받았다. \"아씨, 끝까지 들어 보세요. 그분은 그 토시에 몇 번이고 입을 맞추시며, 세상에서 가장 예쁜 토시라고 하셨어요. 어머나! 제가 그랬죠, 그건 백 번도 더 보신 거잖아요. 그래요, 어너 부인, 그분이 외치셨어요. 하지만 그대의 아씨가 계신 자리에서, 그분 자신 말고 누가 무슨 아름다운 것을 볼 수 있겠소? 아니, 그게 다가 아니에요. 그래도 아씨께서 노여워하지 않으셨으면 해요, 정말 아무 뜻 없이 하신 말이니까요. 어느 날 아씨께서 주인어른께 하프시코드를 연주해 드리고 계실 때, 존스 씨는 옆방에 앉아 계셨는데, 제 눈엔 그분이 우울해 보였어요. 어머! 제가 그랬죠, 존스 씨, 무슨 일이세요? 무슨 생각을 그리 하세요, 라고요. 아니, 이 아가씨야, 그분이 꿈에서 깨어나듯 벌떡 일어나며 말씀하셨어요, 저 천사 같은 그대의 아씨가 연주하고 계신데 내가 무슨 다른 생각을 할 수 있겠소? 그러고는 제 손을 꼭 쥐며, 아! 어너 부인, 그분이 말씀하셨어요, 저 사람을 차지하는 남자는 얼마나 행복할까! 그러고는 한숨을 쉬셨어요. 정말이지 그분의 숨결은 꽃다발처럼 향긋했답니다. 하지만 정말 아무 나쁜 뜻은 없으셨어요. 그러니 아씨께서 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않으셨으면 해요. 그분이 이 일을 절대 말하지 말라고 제게 한 닢을 주시고, 책에 대고 맹세까지 시키셨거든요. 하지만 사실 그건 성경은 아니었던 것 같아요.\"\n\n주홍보다 더 고운 붉은빛이 발견되기 전까지는, 이 순간 소피아의 낯빛에 대해 나는 아무 말도 하지 않으련다. \"어—너,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나, 만일 네가 이 일을 다시는 내게도, 다른 누구에게도 입에 올리지 않는다면, 나도 너를 일러바치지 않으마. 그러니까, 화내지 않겠다는 말이다. 다만 네 혀가 두렵구나. 얘야, 어찌 혀에 그런 자유를 주려 하느냐?\" \"아니에요, 아씨,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"정말이지, 아씨를 노엽게 하느니 차라리 제 혀를 잘라 버리겠어요. 정말로 아씨께서 원치 않으실 말은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않겠습니다.\" \"그래, 나는 네가 이 일을 더는 입에 올리지 않았으면 한다,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그게 아버지 귀에 들어가면 존스 씨에게 노여워하실 테니까. 비록 네 말처럼 그분이 아무 뜻 없이 그러셨다고 정말 믿기는 하지만 말이다. 만일 내가 다른 뜻이 있었다고 여겼다면, 나 자신이 무척 화가 났을 거다—\" \"아니에요, 아씨,\" 어너가 말했다. \"맹세코 저는 그분이 아무 뜻 없으셨다고 믿어요. 그분은 마치 제정신이 아닌 사람처럼 말씀하셨거든요. 아니, 그분 스스로도 그 말을 하고 나서 자기가 정신이 나갔던 것 같다고 하셨어요. 네, 도련님, 제가 그랬죠, 저도 그런 것 같다고요. 그래, 어너, 그분이 말씀하셨어요. 아, 아씨, 용서하세요. 아씨를 노엽게 했다니 제 혀를 뽑아내고 싶을 지경이에요.\" \"계속해 보거라,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"전에 내게 하지 않은 이야기라면 무엇이든 해도 좋다.\" \"그래, 어너, 그분이 말씀하셨어요(이건 얼마 뒤, 그분이 제게 한 닢을 주실 때 일이에요). 나는 그분을 나의 여신으로 여기는 것 말고 다른 어떤 기쁨으로도 떠올릴 만큼 그런 멋쟁이도, 그런 악당도 아니오. 여신으로서 나는 숨이 붙어 있는 한 언제나 그분을 경배하고 흠모할 것이오. 이게 다였어요, 아씨, 제 기억이 닿는 한 맹세할 수 있어요. 저도 처음엔 그분에게 화가 났다가, 아무 나쁜 뜻이 없다는 걸 알고 나서야 풀렸답니다.\" \"정말이지, 어너,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"너는 나를 진심으로 아끼는 것 같구나. 며칠 전 네게 해고를 통고했을 때는 내가 화가 나 있었지. 하지만 네가 내 곁에 남고 싶다면, 그리하려무나.\" \"물론이죠, 아씨,\" 어너 부인이 대답했다. \"저는 결코 아씨 곁을 떠나고 싶지 않아요. 정말이지 아씨께서 해고를 말씀하셨을 때 저는 눈이 빠지도록 울었답니다. 아씨 곁을 떠나고 싶어 한다면 그건 정말 배은망덕한 일이지요. 왜냐하면요, 다시는 이렇게 좋은 자리를 얻지 못할 테니까요. 정말이지 저는 아씨와 함께 살다 함께 죽겠어요. 가엾은 존스 씨가 말씀하셨듯이, 행복한 자는—\"\n\n여기서 저녁 식사를 알리는 종이 울리며 대화가 끊겼다. 그 대화는 소피아에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤던지, 어쩌면 그녀는 그 순간 자신이 짐작했던 것보다 아침의 사혈에 더 큰 신세를 진 셈이었다. 지금 그녀의 마음 상태로 말하자면, 나는 호라티우스의 한 규칙을 좇아, 성공할 가망이 없으니 그것을 묘사하려는 시도조차 하지 않으련다. 내 독자 대부분은 손쉽게 스스로 그것을 떠올릴 것이다. 그러지 못하는 몇몇은, 아무리 잘 그려 낸다 한들 그 그림을 이해하지 못하거나, 적어도 그것이 자연스럽다는 것을 인정하려 들지 않을 테니까.\n\n# BOOK V.\n\n혹여 이 방대한 작품 가운데, 읽는 독자에게 가장 적은 즐거움을 주는 부분이, 정작 글쓴이가 짓느라 가장 큰 수고를 들인 부분일지도 모르겠다. 그 가운데 아마도, 우리가 각 권에 담긴 이야기 앞머리에 붙여 둔 저 서두의 논설들이 꼽힐 것이다. 우리 스스로 그 선두에 서기로 한 이런 종류의 글쓰기에, 이 논설들이 본질적으로 꼭 필요하다고 우리는 단정해 두었다.\n\n이런 단정에 대해, 우리는 굳이 그 이유를 대야 한다고 생각하지 않는다. 산문이자 희극이자 서사시인 이 글쓰기 전반에서 반드시 지켜야 할 규칙으로 우리가 그것을 못 박아 두었다는 것만으로도 차고 넘치게 충분하기 때문이다. 시간과 장소의 그 엄정한 일치—오늘날 극시에 그토록 본질적이라고 자리 잡은 그 규칙—의 근거를 누가 따져 물은 적이 있던가? 어느 비평가가, 어째서 한 편의 극이 하루뿐 아니라 이틀을 담아서는 안 되느냐는 질문을 받아 본 적이 있던가? 또는 어째서 관객이 (마치 유권자들처럼 아무 비용 없이 이동만 한다면) 오 마일이 아니라 오십 마일을 실려 가서는 안 되느냐고? 어느 주석가가, 어떤 옛 비평가가 극에 그어 놓은 한계—그가 다섯 막을 더도 덜도 아니게 담아야 한다고 우긴 그 한계—를 제대로 설명해 낸 적이 있던가? 또는 살아 있는 누군가가, 우리 극장의 현대 심판관들이 저 \"저급하다\"는 말로 무엇을 뜻하는지 풀어 보려 한 적이 있던가? 그들은 그 말 덕분에 무대에서 모든 해학을 몰아내는 데 멋지게 성공했고, 극장을 응접실만큼이나 따분하게 만들어 버렸다! 이 모든 경우에 세상은 우리 법조계의 한 격언을 받아들인 듯하다. 곧 \"각자 제 기술에 정통한 이의 말은 믿어야 한다\"는 것이다. 어떤 사람이 아무런 근거도 없이 어느 기술이나 학문에 독단적인 규칙을 세울 만큼 뻔뻔할 수 있으리라고는, 어쩌면 상상하기 어렵기 때문이리라. 그러니 그런 경우에 우리는, 비록 안타깝게도 우리가 거기까지 내다보지는 못할지언정, 그 밑바닥에는 건전하고 훌륭한 이유가 깔려 있으리라고 결론짓기 십상이다.\n\n그러나 실은, 세상은 비평가들에게 지나친 찬사를 바쳐 왔으며, 그들을 실제보다 훨씬 더 깊이 있는 사람들로 여겨 왔다. 이런 너그러운 호의에 힘입어 비평가들은 대담하게도 군림하는 권력을 떠맡았고, 끝내는 주인이 되기에 이르렀다. 그래서 이제 그들은, 본래 자신들의 선배들로부터 법을 받았던 바로 그 작가들에게 도리어 법을 내릴 만큼 뻔뻔해졌다.\n\n비평가란, 제대로 따져 보면 한낱 서기에 지나지 않는다. 그의 직분이란, 천재의 막대한 힘 덕분에 자신이 다스리는 여러 학문에서 입법자의 자리에 올라선 저 위대한 심판관들이 세운 규칙과 법을 베껴 적는 일이다. 옛 비평가들이 바라던 것은 오로지 이 직분뿐이었다. 그들은 어떤 판단을 내릴 때마다, 그것을 빌려 온 심판관의 권위로 떠받치지 않고서는 감히 한 마디도 앞세우지 못했다.\n\n그러나 세월이 흐르고 무지의 시대가 닥치자, 서기는 제 주인의 권력을 침범하고 그 위엄을 떠맡기 시작했다. 글쓰기의 법칙은 더 이상 작가의 실제 작업이 아니라 비평가의 명령 위에 세워졌다. 서기가 입법자가 되었고, 처음에는 그저 그것을 베껴 적는 것이 일이었던 자들이 이제는 더없이 단호하게 법을 내렸다.\n\n여기서 명백한, 어쩌면 피할 수 없는 오류가 생겨났다. 이 비평가들이 얕은 그릇의 사람들이었던 탓에, 그들은 한낱 형식을 실체로 너무도 쉽게 착각해 버린 것이다. 그들은 법조문의 생명 없는 글자에만 매달리고 그 정신을 내치는 심판관처럼 굴었다. 위대한 작가에게서는 어쩌면 우연이었을 사소한 정황들을, 이 비평가들은 그의 으뜸가는 장점이라 여겼고, 그의 모든 후예가 지켜야 할 본질로 물려주었다. 이러한 월권에 대해, 사기를 떠받치는 두 기둥인 세월과 무지가 권위를 실어 주었다. 그리하여 진실에도 자연에도 손톱만큼의 근거가 없는, 좋은 글쓰기를 위한 수많은 규칙이 자리 잡았다. 그것들은 흔히 천재를 옭아매고 억누르는 것 말고는 아무 쓸모가 없으니, 마치 춤에 관한 그 숱한 훌륭한 논문들이, 사람은 누구나 쇠사슬을 차고 춤춰야 한다는 것을 본질적인 규칙으로 못 박아 두어 무용 선생을 옭아맸을 법한 것과 똑같다.\n\n그러니 오로지 \"그분이 그리 말씀하셨다\"는 권위에만 기대어 후대를 위한 규칙을 세웠다는 비난—사실대로 말하자면 우리는 그런 권위에 깊은 경의를 품고 있지 않다—을 모조리 피하기 위해, 우리는 앞서 다투어 주장한 그 특권을 여기서 내려놓겠다. 그리고 이 작품의 흐름 곳곳에 이 여러 군데 곁가지 논설을 끼워 넣게 된 까닭을 독자 앞에 펼쳐 보이겠다.\n\n여기서 우리는 어쩔 수 없이 새로운 지식의 광맥을 열어야 할 처지에 놓인다. 설령 누군가 그것을 발견했더라도, 우리 기억으로는 옛 작가든 오늘날의 작가든 그것을 캐낸 이는 없었다. 이 광맥이란 다름 아닌 대조의 광맥이다. 그것은 창조된 만물을 두루 꿰뚫고 흐르며, 자연의 것이든 인공의 것이든 우리 안에 온갖 아름다움의 관념을 빚어내는 데 큰 몫을 하리라. 무엇이든 그 아름다움과 빼어남을 드러내 주는 것은 바로 그 반대편 아니던가? 그리하여 낮의 아름다움과 여름의 아름다움은 밤과 겨울의 끔찍함에 의해 돋보인다. 또 내 믿기로는, 만일 사람이 앞의 두 가지만 본다는 것이 가능했다면, 그는 그 아름다움에 대해 아주 불완전한 관념밖에 갖지 못했을 것이다.\n\n그러나 지나치게 진지한 태도는 피하기로 하자. 세상에서 가장 아름다운 여인이라도, 다른 부류의 여인을 한 번도 본 적 없는 남자의 눈에는 그 매력의 덕을 모조리 잃고 마리라는 것을, 누가 의심할 수 있겠는가? 여인들 자신도 이를 잘 아는 듯하다. 그래서 그들은 하나같이 자신을 돋보이게 할 들러리를 마련하느라 분주하다. 아니, 그들은 스스로 제 자신의 들러리가 되기도 한다. 나는 (특히 배스에서) 그들이 아침에는 될 수 있는 한 못나 보이려고 애쓰는 것을 보았는데, 이는 저녁에 보여 줄 작정인 그 아름다움을 한층 돋보이게 하기 위함이었다.\n\n대다수 예술가는 이 비결을 실천하고 있다. 다만 그 이론까지 깊이 연구한 이는 어쩌면 많지 않을 것이다. 보석 세공인은 가장 빼어난 광채의 보석에 받침이 필요함을 안다. 화가는 인물들을 대조시킴으로써 흔히 큰 갈채를 얻는다.\n\n우리 가운데 한 위대한 천재가 이 문제를 남김없이 밝혀 줄 것이다. 사실 나는 그를 흔한 예술가의 어떤 일반 항목 아래 묶을 수가 없다. 그는 다음과 같은 이들 사이에 놓일 자격이 있는 까닭이다.\n\n발명한 기술로써 삶을 가꾸어 낸 이들. * 라틴어 원문: \"Inventas qui vitam excoluere per artes.\"\n\n내가 여기서 말하는 이는, 영국 팬터마임이라 불리는 그 더없이 절묘한 오락을 발명한 사람이다.\n\n이 오락은 두 부분으로 이루어졌는데, 발명자는 그것들을 진지극과 희극이라는 이름으로 구분했다. 진지극은 이교의 신들과 영웅들을 얼마간 등장시켰으니, 그들은 관객이 여태껏 마주한 가운데 가장 고약하고 가장 따분한 패거리였음이 분명하다. 그리고 (이는 아는 이가 거의 없던 비밀인데) 사실 일부러 그렇게 만든 것이었으니, 오락의 희극 부분을 대조하고 어릿광대의 익살을 한층 돋보이게 드러내기 위함이었다.\n\n이는 어쩌면 그런 인물들을 그리 정중하게 다룬 처사는 아니었다. 그러나 그 궁리는 그럼에도 제법 기발했고, 효과도 있었다. 진지극과 희극이라는 말 대신에 따분한 쪽과 가장 따분한 쪽이라는 말을 넣어 보면 이 점이 이제 분명히 드러난다. 희극은 분명 여태껏 무대에 오른 그 무엇보다 따분했고, 진지극을 이루는 저 최상급의 따분함으로만 겨우 돋보일 수 있었기 때문이다. 실로 이 신들과 영웅들이 어찌나 견디기 힘들 만큼 진지했던지, 어릿광대는 (이 이름을 가진 영국 신사는 프랑스 가문과는 전혀 친척이 아니다. 그는 훨씬 더 진지한 기질의 소유자이니까) 무대에 나올 때마다 늘 환영을 받았다. 그가 관객을 더 고약한 패거리로부터 구해 주었기 때문이다.\n\n분별 있는 작가들은 늘 이 대조의 기술을 큰 성공과 함께 실천해 왔다. 나는 호라티우스가 호메로스에게서 이 기술을 트집 잡았다는 것에 놀랐다. 그러나 실은 그가 바로 다음 줄에서 스스로 모순에 빠진다.\n\n\"Indignor quandoque bonus dormitat Homerus; Verum opere in longo fas est obrepere somnum.\"\n\n위대한 호메로스가 어쩌다 잠드는 일이 있으면 나는 슬프다. 허나 긴 작품에서는 졸음이 슬며시 기어드는 것도 마땅하다.\n\n여기서 우리는, 어쩌면 더러 그리 여겼을 법하지만, 작가가 글을 쓰는 동안 정말로 잠들어 버린다는 뜻으로 이해해서는 안 된다. 독자가 그렇게 졸음에 사로잡히기 쉬운 것은 사실이다. 그러나 작품이 올드믹슨의 어느 것만큼이나 길다 한들, 작가 자신은 너무도 흥겹게 빠져 있어 조금도 졸음에 빠질 까닭이 없다. 그는, 포프 씨가 일렀듯이,\n\n스스로는 잠 못 이루며 제 독자에게 잠을 안긴다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 이 졸음을 부르는 부분들이란, 나머지를 대조하고 돋보이게 하려고 솜씨껏 짜 넣은 진지함의 장면들에 다름 아니다. 그리고 이것이야말로, 자신이 따분할 때면 거기에 반드시 의도가 있는 줄 알아도 좋다고 대중에게 일러둔 어느 근래의 익살스러운 작가가 남긴 말의 참뜻이다.\n\n그러니 이 빛 아래에서, 아니 차라리 이 어둠 속에서, 독자가 이 서두의 논설들을 헤아려 주기를 나는 바란다. 그리고 이렇게 미리 일러두었으니, 만일 그가 이 이야기의 다른 부분에서 진지함을 넉넉히 찾을 수 있겠다고 여긴다면, 우리가 애써 따분하기를 자처하는 이 부분은 건너뛰고 다음 권들을 둘째 장부터 시작해도 좋다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n존스 씨가 요양하는 동안 여러 다정한 문병을 받게 되는 이야기. 더불어 맨눈으로는 좀처럼 보이지 않는 사랑이라는 감정의 미묘한 결들도 함께 그려진다.\n\n톰 존스는 요양하는 동안 많은 문병객을 맞았으나, 그중 더러는 그에게 그리 달갑지 않은 손님이었을 것이다. 올워디 씨는 거의 매일 그를 보러 왔다. 다만 그는 톰의 고통을 가엾이 여기고, 그 고통을 불러온 용감한 행동을 크게 칭찬하면서도, 지금이야말로 톰을 자신의 경솔했던 처신에 대해 차분히 돌아보게 만들 좋은 기회라고 여겼다. 그리고 그런 목적에 쓰일 유익한 충고는, 마음이 아픔과 병으로 누그러지고 위험으로 놀라 있으며, 우리를 쾌락의 추구로 끌어들이는 그 사나운 열정들로부터 그 주의가 흐트러지지 않은 지금보다 더 알맞은 때에 베풀어질 수 없으리라 생각했다.\n\n그리하여 이 선량한 사람은 그 청년과 단둘이 있을 때마다, 특히 청년이 더없이 편안해 보일 때를 골라, 지난날의 그릇된 행실을 일깨워 주었다. 그러나 그것은 더없이 부드럽고 다정한 방식이었으며, 오직 앞으로의 처신을 위해 그가 일러 주는 경계의 말을 꺼내기 위한 것일 뿐이었다. 그는 톰에게 이렇게 다짐했다. \"오직 그 처신에 너 자신의 행복이 달려 있고, 앞으로 네가 나의 양아버지로서 베풀 호의를 받을지 여부도 거기에 달려 있다. 다만 네가 이후에 나의 좋은 평가를 스스로 잃어버리지만 않는다면 말이다. 지나간 일에 대해서라면,\" 그는 말했다, \"모두 용서하고 잊어 줄 것이다.\" 그러면서 그는 톰에게, 이 사고를 잘 활용하여 결국에는 그것이 너 자신을 위한 시련이었음이 드러나도록 하라고 권했다.\n\n스왁검도 못지않게 부지런히 문병을 왔는데, 그 역시 병상이야말로 설교를 늘어놓기에 안성맞춤인 무대라고 여겼다. 다만 그의 어조는 올워디 씨보다 훨씬 가혹했다. 그는 자기 제자에게 이렇게 말했다. \"너는 부러진 그 팔다리를 네 죄에 대한 하늘의 심판으로 여겨야 마땅하다. 매일 무릎 꿇고, 목이 아니라 겨우 팔만 부러진 것에 감사를 쏟아 올려야 옳다. 그 목이라는 것은,\" 그는 말했다, \"필시 훗날의 어떤 기회를 위해 남겨져 있을 터이며, 어쩌면 그리 먼 날의 일도 아닐 것이다. 나로서는,\" 그가 말했다, \"진작에 어떤 심판이 너를 덮치지 않은 것이 늘 의아했다. 그러나 이로써 알 수 있는 것이, 신의 벌이란 더디기는 해도 언제나 어김없다는 사실이다.\" 그리하여 그는 또한 이렇게 충고했다. \"아직 남아 있는 더 큰 재앙들도 그만큼 확실하게 내다보아라. 그것들은 이번 일과 마찬가지로, 네가 버림받은 죄인의 처지에 있는 한 어김없이 너를 덮칠 것이다. 그것들은,\" 그가 말했다, \"오직 철저하고 진실한 회개로만 피할 수 있는데, 그런 회개란 어릴 때부터 그토록 타락한 자, 게다가 그 마음이 송두리째 썩어 버렸을까 두려운 자에게서는 기대하거나 바랄 수 없는 것이다. 그러나 너에게 이 회개를 권하는 것이 내 의무다. 그 모든 권면이 헛되고 보람 없으리라는 것을 너무도 잘 알고는 있지만 말이다. 그래도 나는 내 영혼만큼은 자유롭게 했다(liberavi animam meam). 내 양심에는 어떤 태만의 죄도 물을 수 없다. 비록 그와 동시에, 네가 이 세상에서는 확실한 비참을 향해, 다음 세상에서는 그만큼 확실한 저주를 향해 걸어가는 것을 더없는 근심으로 지켜보고 있지만 말이다.\"\n\n스퀘어는 전혀 다른 가락으로 이야기했다. 그는 말했다. \"뼈가 부러진 것 따위의 사고는 현명한 사람이 마음 쓸 만한 것이 못 된다. 이런 불운을 마음에 받아들이기에는, 그것이 인류 가운데 가장 지혜로운 자에게도 닥칠 수 있는 일이며, 의심할 바 없이 전체의 선을 위한 것임을 헤아리는 것만으로 충분하고도 남는다.\" 그는 말했다. \"도덕적 부적합함이 전혀 없는 것들을 두고 악이라 부르는 것은 그저 말의 오용일 뿐이다. 이런 사고의 가장 나쁜 결과인 고통이라는 것도, 세상에서 가장 하찮은 것에 지나지 않는다.\" 이와 비슷한 문구들이 더 이어졌는데, 그것들은 툴리의 《투스쿨룸 담론》 제2권과 저 위대한 섀프츠베리 경에게서 따온 것이었다. 그런데 어느 날 그는 이런 말들을 어찌나 열을 올려 내뱉던지, 불행히도 자기 혀를 깨물고 말았다. 그것도 어찌나 심하게 깨물었던지, 말이 끊겼을 뿐 아니라 그의 마음에 큰 동요가 일어 욕설을 한두 마디 중얼거리기까지 했다. 그러나 무엇보다도 고약했던 것은, 그 자리에 있으면서 그런 학설을 죄다 이교도적이고 무신론적인 것으로 여기던 스왁검에게, 이 사고가 그의 등에 심판 한 방을 먹일 기회를 주었다는 점이다. 그런데 그것이 어찌나 짓궂은 비웃음을 곁들여 행해졌던지, 혀를 깨물어 가뜩이나 흐트러져 있던 이 철학자의 평정심을 (이렇게 말해도 된다면) 완전히 무너뜨리고 말았다. 그는 입술로 분노를 풀 수 없게 된 터라, 어쩌면 자신을 위해 더 난폭한 복수의 방법을 찾아냈을지도 모른다. 마침 그때 운 좋게도 방 안에 있던 외과의가, 자기 잇속과는 반대로 끼어들어 평화를 지켜 주지 않았다면 말이다.\n\n블리필 씨는 친구 존스를 찾아오는 일이 드물었고, 결코 혼자 오는 법이 없었다. 그래도 이 갸륵한 청년은 그에게 큰 관심을 표하고 그 불행에 깊은 염려를 나타냈으나, 어떤 친밀함이라도 조심스레 피했으니, 자신이 자주 넌지시 비치곤 했듯이 그것이 제 인품의 단정함을 더럽힐까 염려해서였다. 그런 까닭에 그는 솔로몬이 나쁜 교제를 두고 한 잠언을 입에 달고 다녔다. 그렇다고 그가 스왁검만큼 신랄했던 것은 아니다. 그는 늘 톰이 개과천선하리라는 약간의 희망을 내비쳤으니, \"이번 일에 숙부께서 보여 주신 그 견줄 데 없는 자애로움이라면,\" 그는 말했다, \"아주 못 쓰게 버려진 사람이 아닌 다음에야 반드시 그런 변화를 일으킬 것\"이라고 했다. 그러나 이렇게 매듭지었다. \"만약 존스 씨가 이후에라도 또 잘못을 저지른다면, 나는 그를 두둔하는 말을 단 한마디도 할 수 없을 것입니다.\"\n\n웨스턴 영주로 말하자면, 그는 들판에 나가 있거나 술병을 끼고 있을 때가 아니면 좀처럼 병실을 떠나지 않았다. 아니, 그는 가끔 맥주를 마시러 일부러 이 방으로 물러나오기까지 했고, 존스에게도 억지로 맥주를 마시게 하려는 것을 말리느라 적잖이 애를 먹어야 했다. 어떤 돌팔이도 제 묘약을 이만큼 만병통치약이라 여기지는 못했을 만큼, 그는 이 맥주를 떠받들었으니, 약방의 온갖 약을 다 합친 것보다 더 큰 효험이 이 안에 들어 있다고 했다. 그래도 그는 거듭된 간청에 못 이겨 이 약을 들이대는 것만큼은 삼가기로 했다. 그러나 사냥하는 아침마다 창문 밑에서 나팔로 환자에게 노래를 불러 주는 것만은 도무지 말릴 수가 없었다. 또 그는 존스를 찾아올 때면 환자가 그 시각에 깨어 있는지 잠들어 있는지는 아랑곳없이, 어느 자리에 들어설 때나 내지르던 그 우렁찬 고함을 한 번도 거두는 법이 없었다.\n\n이런 떠들썩한 행동은 악의가 없었던 만큼 다행히 아무런 해도 끼치지 않았고, 존스가 일어나 앉을 수 있게 되자마자 영주가 데려온 소피아의 동행으로 넉넉히 보상받았다. 게다가 존스가 그녀를 따라 하프시코드 곁으로 갈 수 있게 되기까지는 정말이지 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그곳에서 그녀는 친절하게도 몸을 낮추어, 몇 시간이고 더없이 감미로운 음악으로 그를 사로잡아 주곤 했다. 다만 영주가 〈올드 사이먼 경〉이나 자기가 좋아하는 다른 곡들을 연주하라고 우겨 그녀를 가로막는 것이 마땅하다고 여길 때만은 예외였다.\n\n소피아는 자기 행동에 더없이 섬세한 경계를 두려 애썼지만, 그래도 이따금 어떤 기색이 새어 나오는 것을 막을 수는 없었다. 사랑이란 이런 점에서 다시 한번 병에 견줄 만한데, 한쪽에서 빠져나갈 길이 막히면 반드시 다른 쪽에서 터져 나오기 때문이다. 그러므로 그녀의 입술이 감춘 것을, 그녀의 눈과 붉어진 뺨과 자신도 모르게 나오는 자잘한 몸짓들이 들통내고 말았다.\n\n어느 날, 소피아가 하프시코드를 연주하고 존스가 그 곁에서 듣고 있는데, 영주가 방으로 들어오며 소리쳤다. \"이봐, 톰, 내가 아래층에서 자네를 위해 저 둔한 목사 스왁검과 한바탕 싸우고 왔네. 그자가 내 면전에서 올워디에게, 그 부러진 뼈가 자네에 대한 심판이라고 떠들어 대지 뭔가. 빌어먹을, 내가 말했지, 그게 어떻게 그렇소? 그 친구가 젊은 여자를 지키려다 그리된 거 아니오? 심판은 무슨 놈의 심판! 망할, 이보다 더 나쁜 짓만 안 한다면, 그 친구는 이 고장 어느 목사보다도 먼저 천국에 갈 거요. 부끄러워할 게 아니라 오히려 자랑할 만한 일이라고.\" \"정말이지, 어르신,\" 존스가 말했다, \"저로서는 자랑할 일도 부끄러워할 일도 아닙니다. 다만 그 일로 웨스턴 양을 지킬 수 있었다면, 저는 그것을 언제까지나 제 인생에서 가장 행복한 사고였다고 여길 것입니다.\" \"그런데 그걸로,\" 영주가 말했다, \"올워디를 자네에게 등 돌리게 하려 들다니! 빌어먹을, 그 목사가 사제복만 안 걸치고 있었어도 내가 한 대 갈겨 줬을 거야. 나는 자넬 끔찍이도 아끼니까 말이지, 이 사람아. 자네를 위해서라면 내 힘닿는 일은 무엇이든 안 할 게 없다네. 내일 아침에 내 마구간의 말 가운데 아무거나 마음대로 골라 가게. 슈발리에랑 미스 슬라우치만 빼고 말이야.\" 존스는 고마워하면서도 그 제안을 받기를 사양했다. \"아니,\" 영주가 덧붙였다, \"소피가 타던 그 적갈색 암말을 갖게. 그놈을 사는 데 오십 기니가 들었고, 이번 봄에 여섯 살이 된다네.\" \"그 말이 천 기니를 줬다 한들,\" 존스가 격하게 외쳤다, \"저는 그놈을 개들에게나 던져 줬을 겁니다.\" \"쯧, 쯧!\" 웨스턴이 대꾸했다, \"이런! 그놈이 자네 팔을 부러뜨려서 그러나? 잊고 용서해야지. 나는 자네가 말 못 하는 짐승에게 앙심을 품을 만큼 옹졸한 사람은 아니라고 여겼는데.\" 여기서 소피아가 끼어들어, 아버지께 한 곡 연주해 드리게 해 달라고 청하며 이야기를 끝냈다. 그것은 그가 결코 거절하는 법이 없는 부탁이었다.\n\n앞서 오간 이야기들이 이어지는 동안 소피아의 얼굴빛은 한 번 이상 바뀌었다. 그리고 존스가 그 암말에 대해 드러낸 격한 분노를, 그녀는 아마도 자기 아버지가 짐작한 것과는 다른 동기에서 비롯된 것으로 풀이했을 것이다. 이때 그녀의 마음은 눈에 띄게 떨리고 있었고, 연주가 어찌나 견딜 수 없을 만큼 형편없던지, 웨스턴이 곧 잠들어 버리지 않았더라면 그도 분명 그것을 알아챘을 것이다. 그러나 충분히 깨어 있던 존스는, 눈이 없지 않은 것과 마찬가지로 귀도 없지 않았던지라 몇 가지를 알아차렸다. 그리고 그것을, 독자가 앞서 일어났던 일들로 기억할 만한 모든 정황과 함께 엮어 전체를 곰곰이 되짚어 보니, 소피아의 여린 가슴속에 무언가 심상치 않은 것이 있다는 꽤 강한 확신이 들었다. 많은 젊은 신사들은 그가 이 사실을 진작에 단단히 확인해 두지 못한 것을 몹시 의아해하리라는 데 나는 의심이 없다. 사실대로 고백하자면, 그는 자기 자신을 좀 지나치게 미더워하지 못했고, 젊은 아가씨가 보내는 호의의 신호를 알아채는 데도 그리 민첩하지 못했다. 이것은 요즘 그토록 널리 유행하는 이른 도회지 교육으로만 고칠 수 있는 결함이었다.\n\n이런 생각들이 존스의 마음을 온통 사로잡자, 그것은 그의 마음에 동요를 불러일으켰다. 그의 것보다 덜 순수하고 덜 굳건한 체질이었다면, 그런 시기에 그 동요는 자칫 매우 위험한 결과로 이어졌을지도 모른다. 그는 소피아의 큰 가치를 진정으로 느끼고 있었다. 그녀의 자태를 더없이 좋아했고, 그 못지않게 그녀의 재주에 감탄했으며, 그 선량함을 애틋하게 사랑했다. 사실 그는 그녀를 차지하겠다는 생각을 단 한 번도 품어 본 적이 없었고, 자기 마음에 스스로 조금이라도 고삐를 늦춰 준 적도 없었기에, 정작 그 자신이 알고 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 강한 열정을 그녀에게 품고 있었다. 이제 그의 가슴은 그 온전한 비밀을 끄집어냈고, 그와 동시에 그 사랑스러운 대상이 자신의 애정에 화답하고 있음을 그에게 확신시켜 주었다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n마음이 없는 이들은 모두 이 장이 별것 아닌 일로 호들갑을 떤다고 여길 것이다.\n\n독자는 어쩌면 존스의 가슴속에 지금 막 피어오른 그 느낌들이 너무도 달콤하고 황홀해서, 우리가 앞서 말한 그 위험한 결과들보다는 차라리 마음에 잔잔한 평온을 가져다주리라 짐작할지도 모른다. 그러나 사실 이런 종류의 느낌은 아무리 황홀하다 해도 처음 깨닫는 순간에는 몹시 소란스러운 성질을 지니며, 사람을 잠재우는 진정제 같은 구석은 거의 없다. 더구나 지금의 경우 그 느낌들은 어떤 사정들로 인해 쓰디쓰게 물들어 있었고, 그 사정들이 더 달콤한 재료와 뒤섞이면서 결국 쓴맛과 단맛이 함께 도는 음료라 부를 만한 것을 빚어냈다. 그런데 입에 이보다 더 거슬리는 것이 없듯, 비유적인 의미로도 이보다 마음에 더 해로운 것은 없다.\n\n먼저, 그는 소피아에게서 본 것들을 근거로 스스로를 기쁘게 부풀릴 만한 충분한 토대를 갖고 있었지만, 그래도 자신이 연민을, 혹은 잘해야 호감을, 더 따뜻한 마음으로 잘못 읽고 있는 것은 아닐까 하는 의심에서 아직 자유롭지 못했다. 그는 소피아가 자기에게 그런 애정을 품고 있어서, 자기 마음이 자라나고 보살핌을 받으면 마침내 요구하게 될 그 수확을 약속해 주리라고 낙관할 만큼 확신에 차 있지 못했다. 게다가 설령 딸 쪽에서는 행복을 가로막는 장애가 없으리라 바랄 수 있다 해도, 아버지 쪽에서는 확실한 장벽을 만나리라고 그는 생각했다. 그 아버지는 오락을 즐길 때는 시골 지주였으나, 재산과 관련된 일에서는 완벽하게 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이었고, 외동딸에게 더없이 격렬한 애정을 품고 있었으며, 술잔을 기울일 때면 딸이 이 고장에서 가장 부유한 남자 가운데 하나와 결혼하는 것을 보는 즐거움을 종종 내비치곤 했다. 존스는 웨스턴이 자기에게 보여 온 호의를 빌미로, 그가 딸을 출세시키려는 이 계획을 언젠가 접게 되리라 기대할 만큼 허영심 많고 분별없는 멋쟁이는 아니었다. 그는 이런 일에서 가장 훌륭한 부모조차 재산이야말로 유일하지는 않더라도 주된 고려 대상이 됨을 잘 알고 있었다. 우정은 우리로 하여금 남의 이익을 따뜻하게 편들게 하지만, 남의 욕망을 채워 주는 일에는 몹시 차갑기 때문이다. 사실 그 욕망에서 비롯될 행복을 느끼려면, 우리 자신이 그 욕망을 직접 지니고 있어야 한다. 그러니 그는 아버지의 동의를 얻을 가망이 없는 만큼, 동의 없이 일을 이루어 보려 애쓸까도 생각했지만, 그런 식으로 웨스턴 씨의 일생일대의 큰 목표를 좌절시킨다는 것은 그의 환대를 몹시 못되게 이용하는 짓이요, 그의 손에서 받은(아무리 거칠게 받았다 해도) 숱한 자잘한 호의에 대한 더없이 배은망덕한 보답이었다. 그런 결과를 그가 두려움과 경멸로 바라보았다면, 올워디 씨와 관련된 일에는 얼마나 더 큰 충격을 받았겠는가. 그는 올워디 씨에게 자식 이상의 의무를 지고 있었으니, 그분께 자식 이상의 효심을 품고 있었던 것이다! 그는 그 선한 분의 천성이 어떤 비열함이나 배신에도 너무나 질색하는지라, 그런 종류의 시도가 조금이라도 있으면 그 죄지은 자의 모습이 그분의 눈에 영영 가증스러워지고, 그 이름이 그분의 귀에 혐오스러운 소리가 되리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 이렇게 넘을 수 없는 난관들의 모습만으로도, 그의 소망이 아무리 뜨거웠다 한들 그를 절망에 빠뜨리기에 충분했다. 그러나 이런 난관들조차 또 다른 여인에 대한 연민으로 다스려지고 있었다. 사랑스러운 몰리의 모습이 이제 그의 앞을 비집고 들어왔다. 그는 그녀의 품에서 영원한 변치 않음을 맹세했고, 그녀도 그가 자기를 버리면 결코 살아남지 않겠노라 그만큼이나 자주 다짐했었다. 그는 이제 그녀가 가장 끔찍한 죽음의 온갖 자세로 누워 있는 모습을 보았다. 아니, 그녀가 빠지게 될 매춘의 온갖 비참함까지 떠올렸으니, 그 비참함의 원인은 두 곱절로 그 자신이었다. 먼저 그녀를 유혹했고, 그다음에 그녀를 버린 까닭이다. 그는 그녀의 이웃들이, 심지어 그녀 자신의 자매들까지도 그녀에게 품은 미움을, 그리고 그들 모두가 얼마나 기꺼이 그녀를 갈가리 찢어 놓으려 들지를 잘 알고 있었다. 실로 그는 그녀를 수치보다는 시샘에 더 내몰았으니, 아니 차라리 시샘을 통해 수치에 내몬 셈이었다. 많은 여자들이 그녀를 창녀라 욕하면서도 그녀의 연인과 그녀의 고운 옷을 부러워했고, 자기들도 같은 대가를 치르고 그것들을 손에 넣을 수 있었다면 기뻐했을 테니 말이다. 그러므로 자기가 그녀를 버리면 그 가엾은 처녀의 파멸이 피할 수 없이 뒤따르리라는 것을 그는 내다보았고, 이 생각이 그의 영혼을 찔렀다. 가난과 곤궁이 누군가에게 그런 불행을 더 무겁게 얹을 권리를 준다고는 그에게 결코 여겨지지 않았다. 그녀의 처지가 미천하다 해서 그녀의 비참함이 그의 눈에 하찮게 보이지도 않았고, 그 비참함을 그녀에게 안긴 자기 죄가 정당화되거나 조금이라도 가벼워지는 것 같지도 않았다. 그러나 내가 어찌 정당화를 입에 올리겠는가? 그의 마음 자체가, 그가 생각하기에 자기를 사랑했고 그 사랑을 위해 순결을 바친 한 인간을 파멸시키는 일을 차마 견디지 못했다. 그의 선한 마음이 그녀를 변호했다. 돈에 매수된 차가운 변호인으로서가 아니라, 그 결말에 직접 얽혀 있어, 자기 주인이 남에게 안긴 모든 고통을 스스로도 깊이 나누어 가질 수밖에 없는 자로서였다.\n\n이 강력한 변호인이 가엾은 몰리를 비참함의 온갖 정경 속에 그려 보임으로써 존스의 연민을 충분히 끌어올리자, 그것은 교묘하게 또 다른 정념의 도움을 불러들여, 그 처녀를 젊음과 건강과 아름다움의 온갖 사랑스러운 빛깔로 그려 냈다. 그녀를 크나큰 욕망의 대상으로, 그리고 적어도 선한 마음에는, 동시에 연민의 대상이기도 하다는 점에서 한층 더한 욕망의 대상으로 비추어 보인 것이다.\n\n이런 생각들 가운데서 가엾은 존스는 잠 못 이루는 긴 밤을 보냈고, 아침이 되자 그 모든 것의 결론은 몰리 곁에 머무르고 소피아는 더 이상 생각하지 않겠다는 것이었다.\n\n그는 이 갸륵한 결심을 이튿날 저녁이 될 때까지 온종일 이어 가며, 몰리에 대한 생각을 소중히 품고 소피아를 마음에서 몰아냈다. 그러나 그 운명의 저녁, 아주 사소한 사건 하나가 그의 모든 정념을 다시금 일렁이게 했고, 그의 마음에 어찌나 완전한 변화를 일으켰던지, 우리는 그것을 새로운 장에서 전하는 편이 마땅하다고 여긴다.\n\n짧은 장. 그 안에 자그마한 사건 하나가 담겨 있다.\n\n감금된 처지의 이 젊은 신사에게 문안 인사를 건넨 여러 방문객 가운데 어너 부인도 있었다. 독자께서는, 앞서 그녀의 입에서 흘러나온 몇몇 말들을 떠올리며, 어쩌면 그녀 자신이 존스 씨에게 아주 각별한 애정을 품고 있었으리라 짐작할지도 모른다. 그러나 실상은 전혀 그렇지 않았다. 톰은 잘생긴 젊은이였고, 어너 부인은 그런 부류의 남자들에게 약간의 호감을 두고 있었다. 다만 그 호감은 누구에게나 똑같이 향하는 것이어서, 따로 가리는 법이 없었다. 어느 귀족의 하인에게 품었던 사랑이 어긋난 뒤로—그 사내는 결혼을 약속해 놓고 비열하게 그녀를 버렸다—그녀는 깨어진 마음의 잔해를 어찌나 단단히 한데 그러모아 두었던지, 그 후로는 어떤 남자도 그 조각 하나조차 차지하지 못했다. 그녀는 모든 잘생긴 남자를, 차분하고 정숙한 마음이 모든 선한 것을 대하듯 한결같은 호의와 너그러움으로 바라보았다. 실로 그녀는 인간을 사랑하는 사람이라 불릴 만했으니, 소크라테스가 인류를 사랑한 것과 같았다. 다만 소크라테스가 정신의 자질을 두고 한 사람을 다른 사람보다 더 좋아했다면, 그녀는 육체의 자질을 두고 그리했을 뿐이다. 그러나 그 선호를 결코 지나치게 끌고 가는 법이 없어, 철학자다운 그녀 기질의 고요함을 흩뜨리는 일은 없었다.\n\n존스 씨가 앞 장에서 우리가 본 그 내면의 갈등을 겪은 다음 날, 어너 부인이 그의 방으로 들어왔다. 그가 혼자 있는 것을 보고는 이렇게 말문을 열었다. \"어머, 나리, 제가 어디 다녀왔는지 아세요? 장담컨대 오십 년을 맞혀 보셔도 못 맞히실 거예요. 그렇지만 맞히신다 해도, 그건 정말 말씀드리면 안 되는 거랍니다.\" \"허, 내게 말해선 안 되는 것이라면,\" 존스가 받았다. \"더더욱 궁금해서 캐묻고 싶어지는걸. 그리고 자네가 나를 그렇게 매정하게 거절하지는 않으리라는 걸 나는 알지.\" \"글쎄요,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"사실 거절할 이유도 없긴 하죠. 나리께서 두 번 다시 입 밖에 안 내실 게 분명하니까요. 게다가 제가 어디 다녀왔는지 아신들, 거기서 무얼 했는지 모르시면 별 의미도 없잖아요. 아니, 제 입장에선 굳이 비밀로 할 까닭도 없죠. 그분이야말로 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 아가씨시니까요.\" 이 말에 존스는 그 비밀을 알려 달라고 간곡히 청하기 시작했고, 절대 발설하지 않겠노라 굳게 약속했다. 그러자 그녀가 이렇게 이어 갔다. \"그러니까 말이죠, 나리, 우리 아가씨께서 저더러 몰리 시그림의 안부를 알아보고, 그 계집애가 뭐 필요한 게 없는지 살펴보고 오라 하셨어요. 솔직히 저야 가고 싶지 않았죠, 정말로요. 하지만 하인은 시키는 대로 해야 하는 법이니까요. 나리, 어쩌자고 스스로를 그렇게 값싸게 굴리셨어요? 아무튼 아가씨께서 가서 그 애한테 속옷이며 이것저것 갖다주라 하셨어요. 우리 아가씨는 너무 마음이 고우셔서 탈이에요. 그런 헤픈 계집들은 교화소로 보내는 편이 차라리 그 애들한테도 나을 텐데요. 제가 아가씨께 그랬어요, 마님, 마님께서는 게으름을 부추기고 계신 거예요, 하고요.\" \"그래서 우리 소피아가 그렇게 마음씨가 고우셨다고?\" 존스가 말했다. \"우리 소피아라뇨! 어머나, 별말씀을 다 하시네.\" 어너가 대꾸했다. \"그래도 나리께서 전부 다 아신다면 말이죠. 정말이지, 제가 만약 존스 나리라면 몰리 시그림 같은 하찮은 것보다는 좀 더 높은 데를 바라볼 거예요.\" \"다 안다면이라니, 그게 무슨 말인가?\" 존스가 되물었다. \"제 말은 제가 뜻하는 그대로지요,\" 어너가 말했다. \"언젠가 나리께서 우리 아가씨 토시 속에 손을 넣으셨던 일, 기억 안 나세요? 아가씨가 절대 못 들으실 게 확실하다면, 정말이지 다 털어놓고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같은데요.\" 그러자 존스가 엄숙하게 여러 번 다짐을 했다. 어너가 말을 이었다. \"그러니까 말이죠, 아가씨께서 그 토시를 제게 주셨어요. 그런데 나중에 나리께서 하신 일을 듣고 나서는—\" \"그럼 자네가 내가 한 일을 아가씨께 말씀드렸단 말인가?\" 존스가 끼어들었다. \"설령 그랬다 해도요, 나리,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"저한테 화내실 필요는 없어요. 자기들이 그런 줄 알았다면 우리 아가씨께 일러바칠 기회를 얻으려고 목이라도 내놓았을 사내가 한둘이 아닐 테니까요. 정말이지, 이 나라에서 제일가는 귀족이라도 우쭐할 만한 일이거든요. 그렇지만, 맹세코, 저는 나리께 말씀 안 드릴 작정이 굴뚝같아요.\" 존스가 사정사정하자, 그녀는 곧 마음이 풀려 이렇게 말을 이어 갔다. \"그러니까 나리, 아가씨께서 그 토시를 제게 주셨다고 했잖아요. 그런데 제가 그 얘기를 말씀드린 지 하루 이틀쯤 지나서, 아가씨가 새 토시에 트집을 잡으시는 거예요. 그게 정말이지 여태껏 본 것 중에 제일 예쁜 토시인데도요. 어너야, 하시더니, 이 토시는 영 보기 싫구나. 내겐 너무 커서 못 끼겠다. 다른 걸 구할 때까지 옛것을 도로 내게 다오, 그러면 그 대신 이걸 네가 가지렴, 하시는 거예요. 우리 아가씨는 정말 마음이 고우셔서, 줬다 뺐는 짓 같은 건 경멸하시거든요, 그건 제가 장담해요. 그래서 제가 그걸 도로 갖다 드렸죠. 그런데 아가씨는 그 후로 거의 줄곧 그 토시를 팔에 끼고 계셨던 것 같아요. 그리고 분명히 아무도 안 볼 때 거기다 입맞춤도 숱하게 하셨을 거예요.\"\n\n여기서 대화는 웨스턴 씨가 몸소 들이닥치는 바람에 끊기고 말았다. 그는 존스를 하프시코드 앞으로 불러내러 온 것이었다. 가엾은 젊은이는 새파랗게 질려 부들부들 떨며 그리로 갔다. 웨스턴도 그 모습을 보았으나, 어너 부인이 있는 것을 보고는 엉뚱한 데로 까닭을 돌렸다. 그러고는 농담 반 진담 반으로 존스에게 걸쭉한 욕설을 한바탕 퍼붓더니, 다른 데 가서나 쏘다니지 제 사냥터에서 사냥감을 슬쩍 가로채지는 말라고 일렀다.\n\n이날 저녁 소피아는 여느 때보다도 더 아름다워 보였다. 그리고 존스 씨의 눈에는, 그녀가 마침 그 오른팔에 바로 그 토시를 끼고 있다는 사실이 그 매력에 적지 않게 보태졌으리라 믿어도 좋을 것이다.\n\n소피아가 아버지가 좋아하는 곡 가운데 하나를 연주하고 있을 때, 그는 그녀의 의자에 기대어 서 있었다. 그런데 그 토시가 그녀의 손가락 위로 흘러내려 연주를 그르치게 했다. 이에 향사는 어찌나 짜증이 났던지 그녀에게서 토시를 낚아채더니, 걸쭉한 욕설과 함께 불 속으로 던져 버렸다. 소피아는 즉시 벌떡 일어나, 더없이 다급한 손길로 불길 속에서 그것을 건져 냈다.\n\n이 사건이 우리 독자들 가운데 많은 분께는 아마 대수롭지 않게 보일 것이다. 그러나 그토록 하찮은 일이 가엾은 존스에게는 어찌나 격렬한 영향을 미쳤던지, 우리는 이를 전하는 것이 우리의 의무라 여겼다. 사실, 분별없는 기록자들이 너무도 자주 빠뜨리는 자그마한 정황들 가운데서 더없이 중대한 사건들이 솟아나는 법이다. 세상이란 실로 거대한 기계와 같다고 할 수 있다. 그 안에서 큰 톱니바퀴들은 본디 아주 미세하여, 어지간히 날카로운 눈이 아니고서는 거의 알아챌 수 없는 작은 톱니바퀴들에 의해 비로소 움직이기 시작하는 것이다.\n\n이리하여 견줄 데 없는 소피아의 그 모든 매력도, 그녀 눈동자의 그 눈부신 광채와 애틋한 부드러움도, 그 목소리와 자태의 조화도, 그 모든 재치와 쾌활함과 드넓은 마음과 다정한 성품도, 이 토시라는 자그마한 사건만큼 가엾은 존스의 마음을 그토록 완전히 정복하고 사로잡지는 못했다. 시인이 트로이를 두고 감미롭게 노래한 그대로다—\n속임수에 사로잡히고 눈물에 무릎 꿇었으니,\n\n튀데우스의 아들도, 라리사의 아킬레우스도,\n십 년 세월도, 천 척의 배도 이루지 못한 것을.\n\n디오메데스도, 테티스의 더 위대한 아들도,\n천 척의 배도, 십 년의 포위도 못 해낸 일을,\n\n거짓 눈물과 아첨하는 말이 그 도성을 빼앗았네.\n이제 존스의 성채는 기습으로 함락되었다. 우리의 주인공이 얼마 전 그토록 군인다운 지혜로 제 마음의 길목마다 파수꾼으로 세워 두었던 명예와 신중함의 그 모든 헤아림은 제자리를 버리고 달아났고, 사랑의 신이 의기양양하게 진군해 들어왔다.\n\n매우 긴 장으로, 매우 중대한 사건을 담고 있다.\n\n그러나 이 승리의 신이 존스의 가슴에서 공공연한 적들을 손쉽게 몰아내기는 했어도, 정작 자기 손으로 그곳에 세워 두었던 수비대를 밀어내는 일은 한결 어렵게 느껴졌다. 비유는 모두 접어 두고 말하자면, 가엾은 몰리가 앞으로 어찌 될 것인가 하는 걱정이 이 마음씨 고운 청년의 머릿속을 크게 어지럽히고 괴롭혔던 것이다. 소피아의 빼어난 미덕은 이 가련한 처녀의 온갖 아름다움을 완전히 가렸다기보다 아예 꺼뜨려 버렸다. 그러나 사랑이 떠난 자리를 차지한 것은 경멸이 아니라 연민이었다. 그는 그 처녀가 자신의 온 사랑과, 앞날의 행복에 대한 모든 기대를 오로지 자기 한 사람에게만 걸어 두었음을 확신하고 있었다. 그가 그녀에게 더할 나위 없이 넘치도록 애정을 쏟아부어, 그렇게 만들 충분한 빌미를 스스로 제공했다는 사실을 그는 알고 있었다. 게다가 그 애정을 언제까지나 지켜 나가겠노라고 온갖 방법을 다해 그녀에게 믿게 만들었던 것이다. 그녀 또한 그의 약속을 굳게 믿는다고 다짐했고, 그가 그 약속을 지키느냐 깨느냐에 따라 자신이 여인들 가운데 가장 행복한 사람이 될지, 아니면 가장 비참한 사람이 될지가 달려 있다고 더없이 엄숙한 맹세로 선언했었다. 한 인간에게 이처럼 극심한 불행을 안겨 준 장본인이 된다는 생각은, 그가 단 한순간도 곱씹어 견딜 수 없는 것이었다. 그는 이 가엾은 처녀가 자신의 보잘것없는 힘이 미치는 모든 것을 자기에게 바쳤으며, 제 스스로를 희생하면서까지 자기 쾌락의 대상이 되어 주었고, 바로 지금 이 순간에도 자기를 그리며 한숨짓고 애태우고 있으리라 여겼다. 그렇다면, 하고 그는 혼잣말을 했다, 그녀가 그토록 간절히 바라던 나의 회복이, 그녀가 그토록 애타게 기다리던 나의 출현이, 그녀가 스스로 부풀려 온 그 기쁨을 안겨 주기는커녕 도리어 그녀를 단숨에 비참과 절망 속으로 떨어뜨린단 말인가? 내가 그런 악당이 될 수 있단 말인가? 이렇게 가엾은 몰리의 수호신이 승리를 거두는 듯하던 바로 그 순간, 이제 더는 의심할 여지가 없어 보이는 소피아를 향한 그의 사랑이 그의 마음속으로 밀려들어, 앞을 가로막는 온갖 장애물을 모조리 쓸어 가 버렸다.\n\n마침내 그의 머릿속에 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. 어쩌면 다른 방식으로 몰리에게 보상해 줄 수 있을지도 모른다는 것, 즉 그녀에게 한 뭉치의 돈을 쥐여 주는 것이었다. 그러나 세상을 그와 저울에 올려 견준다 해도 그를 잃은 것을 결코 메워 줄 수 없으리라고, 그녀가 자주 그리고 격렬하게 다짐했던 말을 떠올리자, 그녀가 그것을 받아들이리라는 데에는 거의 절망스러운 마음이 들었다. 그럼에도 그녀의 극심한 가난, 그리고 무엇보다도 그녀의 유별난 허영심이(이에 대해서는 독자에게 이미 어느 정도 귀띔한 바 있다) 그에게 약간의 희망을 안겨 주었다. 비록 그녀가 그토록 애정을 떠벌렸다 해도, 자신의 기대를 뛰어넘는 재물, 게다가 그녀를 또래 누구보다도 위에 올려놓아 그 허영심을 채워 줄 재물이라면, 머지않아 그것으로 만족할 마음이 생길지도 모른다는 희망이었다. 그리하여 그는 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 이런 제안을 꺼내 보기로 결심했다.\n\n그래서 어느 날, 그의 팔이 어지간히 회복되어 띠로 둘러 멘 채로도 수월히 걸을 수 있게 되자, 향사가 들에서 자신의 운동에 한창 빠져 있는 틈을 타 그는 슬며시 빠져나가 어여쁜 자기 여인을 찾아갔다. 차를 마시고 있던 그녀의 어머니와 자매들은 그에게 우선 몰리가 집에 없다고 알렸다. 그러나 곧이어 맏언니가 짓궂은 미소를 지으며, 그녀가 위층에서 잠자리에 누워 있다고 일러 주었다. 톰은 자기 연인이 그런 자세로 있다는 데 아무런 이의가 없었으므로, 곧장 그녀의 침실로 이어지는 사다리를 올라갔다. 그러나 꼭대기에 다다랐을 때, 그는 크게 놀랍게도 문이 단단히 잠겨 있는 것을 발견했다. 한참 동안이나 안에서는 아무런 대답도 들려오지 않았으니, 몰리가, 그녀가 나중에 직접 그에게 일러 준 바로는, 깊이 잠들어 있었기 때문이었다.\n\n슬픔과 기쁨의 극단은 매우 비슷한 결과를 낳는다고들 한다. 이 둘 가운데 어느 하나가 느닷없이 우리에게 밀어닥치면, 그것은 온통 마음을 뒤흔들고 어지럽혀 우리가 가진 모든 능력을 쓸 수 없게 만들어 버리기 일쑤다. 그러니 존스 씨의 뜻밖의 모습이 몰리의 마음에 그토록 강하게 작용해, 그녀를 그런 혼란으로 압도해 버린 것도 이상한 일은 아니다. 그래서 몇 분 동안 그녀는, 이 자리에서 그녀가 휩싸였으리라고 독자가 짐작할 그 크나큰 환희를 미처 표현하지 못했다. 존스로 말하자면, 그는 사랑하는 대상의 출현에 완전히 사로잡혀, 이를테면 홀린 듯했던 까닭에, 한동안 소피아를, 따라서 자신이 이곳을 찾은 본래의 목적까지도 까맣게 잊고 있었다.\n\n그러나 이내 그것이 그의 기억 속에 되살아났다. 만남의 첫 격정이 가라앉고 나자, 그는 조금씩 화제를 끌어내어, 자신에게 그녀를 다시는 만나지 말라고 엄히 금하셨던 올워디 씨가 자기가 여전히 이런 왕래를 이어 가고 있음을 알아채기라도 한다면, 그들의 정사에 어떤 끔찍한 결과가 뒤따를 수밖에 없는지를 이야기하기 시작했다. 그런 발각은, 자기의 적들이 미루어 보건대 피할 수 없으리라 여겨질 만한 일이며, 그렇게 되면 결국 자신의 파멸로, 따라서 그녀의 파멸로 끝날 수밖에 없다고 그는 말했다. 그러니 모진 운명이 두 사람을 갈라놓아야 한다고 정해 놓은 이상, 그녀에게 그것을 의연히 견뎌 달라고 그는 당부했다. 그리고 자기 평생에 걸쳐, 만약 언젠가 그럴 힘이 자기에게 생긴다면, 그녀의 더없는 기대를, 아니 그녀의 바람마저도 뛰어넘는 방식으로 그녀를 보살펴 줌으로써 자기 애정의 진실함을 보여 줄 기회를 결코 놓치지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 끝으로 그는, 그녀가 머지않아 자신과 결혼해 줄 어떤 남자를, 그것도 그녀가 그와 떳떳지 못한 삶을 이어 가는 것보다 훨씬 더 행복하게 해 줄 남자를 만날 수 있을 거라고 마무리 지었다.\n\n몰리는 잠시 말없이 가만히 있더니, 이윽고 눈물을 펑펑 쏟으며 다음과 같은 말로 그를 나무라기 시작했다. \"이게 저를 향한 당신의 사랑인가요? 이제 저를 망쳐 놓고서, 이런 식으로 저를 버리는 게요! 남자란 다 거짓되고 위증하기는 매한가지라서, 우리에게서 못된 욕심을 채우자마자 우리한테 싫증을 낸다고 제가 말할 때마다, 당신은 얼마나 자주 저를 결코 버리지 않겠다고 맹세했던가요! 그래 놓고 결국 그런 거짓 맹세나 일삼는 사람일 수가 있나요? 이제 당신이 제 마음을 다 가져갔는데, 정말 그래 놓고서, 당신 없는 세상의 온갖 재물이 제게 무슨 소용이 있겠어요? 어째서 제게 다른 남자 이야기를 꺼내는 거예요? 저는 살아 있는 한 다른 어떤 남자도 사랑할 수 없어요. 다른 남자들은 다 제겐 아무것도 아니에요. 이 고장에서 제일가는 향사가 내일 저한테 청혼하러 온다 해도, 저는 그에게 곁을 내주지 않을 거예요. 아니, 저는 당신 때문에라도 그 종자 전체를 늘 미워하고 경멸할 거라고요.\"\n\n이렇게 말을 이어 가던 그녀의 혀가, 미처 그 절반도 달리기 전에 한 사건으로 인해 뚝 멈춰 버렸다. 몰리가 누워 있던 방은, 아니 다락방이라고 해야겠지만, 계단 하나를 올라간 곳, 다시 말해 집의 맨 꼭대기에 자리하고 있어, 그리스 문자의 커다란 델타를 닮은 비스듬한 형상이었다. 영국 독자라면, 한가운데 말고는 어디서도 똑바로 설 수 없었다는 말을 들으면 그 모양을 더 잘 떠올릴 수 있으리라. 그런데 이 방에는 벽장이라는 편의 시설이 없었으므로, 몰리는 그 결함을 메우려고 집의 서까래에 낡은 깔개 하나를 못으로 박아 두었다. 그것이 작은 구멍 하나를 가려 주고 있었는데, 그 안에는 그녀의 가장 좋은 옷가지들, 이를테면 앞서 언급한 적이 있는 그 자루 같은 드레스의 남은 부분이며, 두건 몇 개, 그리고 그녀가 최근에 마련해 둔 다른 물건들이 먼지를 타지 않게 걸려 보관되어 있었다.\n\n이 가려진 자리는 침대 발치 바로 맞은편에 있었고, 사실 그 깔개가 침대에 어찌나 가까이 드리워져 있었던지, 어떤 의미에서는 커튼이 없는 것을 대신해 주는 노릇까지 했다. 그런데 몰리가 분노의 격정 속에서 발로 이 깔개를 밀쳤는지, 아니면 존스가 그것에 손을 댔는지, 그것도 아니면 핀이나 못이 저 혼자 빠진 것인지 나로서는 확실치 않다. 다만 몰리가 위에 적어 둔 그 마지막 말을 내뱉는 순간, 고약한 깔개가 그 못질에서 풀려나 그 뒤에 감춰져 있던 모든 것을 드러내고 말았다. 거기에는 다른 여인네의 살림 도구들 틈에 끼여 ― 부끄러움을 무릅쓰고 적거니와, 또한 슬픔에 잠겨 읽힐 일이거니와 ― 철학자 스퀘어가, 도무지 떠올릴 수 없을 만큼 우스꽝스러운 자세로(그 자리는 그가 똑바로 서는 것을 도저히 허락하지 않았으므로) 모습을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n실로 그가 서 있던 자세는, 목과 발꿈치가 한데 묶인 병사의 꼴과 그리 다르지 않았다. 아니, 그보다는 런던의 큰길거리에서 우리가 흔히 보는 사내들, 즉 그렇게 서서 형벌을 받고 있는 것이 아니라 마땅히 그런 형벌을 받아야 할 자들의 모양새에 더 가까웠다. 그는 몰리의 것인 잠자리 두건을 머리에 쓰고 있었고, 깔개가 떨어지는 순간 그의 커다란 두 눈은 곧장 존스를 향해 부릅떠졌다. 그리하여 이제 막 드러난 그 꼴에 철학이라는 관념까지 더해지고 보니, 어떤 구경꾼이라도 터져 나오는 웃음을 참기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었을 것이다.\n\n이 대목에서 독자의 놀라움도 존스의 놀라움 못지않으리라는 데 나는 의심이 없다. 이 현명하고 근엄한 사내가 그런 곳에 나타난 데서 솟아날 수밖에 없는 의혹들이, 그가 여태껏 모든 이의 눈에 의심할 바 없이 지켜 왔을 그 성품과 너무도 어긋나 보일 테니 말이다.\n\n그러나 사실대로 고백하자면, 이 어긋남이란 실제라기보다는 차라리 상상 속의 것이다. 철학자들 또한 다른 인간 피조물들과 마찬가지로 살과 피로 이루어져 있다. 그들의 이론이 아무리 고상하고 정련되어 있다 한들, 약간의 실천적 나약함이란 다른 평범한 인간들에게 그러하듯 그들에게도 흔히 따라붙는 법이다. 앞서 넌지시 비친 바와 같이, 그 차이라는 것은 실로 이론에만 있을 뿐 실천에 있는 것이 아니다. 그런 위대한 존재들은 훨씬 더 훌륭하고 더 지혜롭게 생각하기는 해도, 행동은 언제나 다른 사람들과 똑같이 하기 때문이다. 그들은 온갖 식욕과 정념을 굴복시키고 고통도 쾌락도 모두 경멸하는 법을 아주 잘 알고 있으며, 이 앎은 그들에게 더없이 즐거운 사색거리를 안겨 주고 또 손쉽게 익혀진다. 그러나 그것을 실천하기란 성가시고 골치 아픈 일이다. 그러므로 이를 알도록 가르쳐 주는 바로 그 지혜가, 그것을 굳이 실행에 옮기는 일은 피하라고도 그들에게 가르치는 것이다.\n\n스퀘어 씨는 마침 그 일요일에 교회에 와 있었다. 독자가 기억해 주시면 좋겠지만, 자루 같은 드레스를 입은 몰리의 등장이 그 온갖 소동을 불러일으켰던 바로 그날이다. 그는 여기서 처음으로 그녀를 눈여겨보았고, 그 미모에 어찌나 흡족했던지 그날 저녁 젊은 신사들을 구슬려 본래 예정했던 승마 길을 바꾸게 했다. 그래야 몰리의 거처를 지나치면서 그녀를 한 번 더 볼 기회를 얻을 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 까닭을 그가 그 당시 누구에게도 말하지 않았으므로, 우리 또한 그때 독자에게 굳이 알리는 것이 옳다고 여기지 않았던 것이다.\n\n스퀘어 씨가 보기에 사물의 부적합함을 이루는 여러 요소 가운데, 위험과 어려움이 그 둘이었다. 그러므로 이 젊은 처녀를 타락시키는 데 있을지 모를 어려움, 그리고 발각될 경우 자기 평판에 미칠 위험이 어찌나 강한 만류의 힘이 되었던지, 아마 처음에는 미모를 바라보는 데서 얻어지는 그 즐거운 상념들로 만족하려 했던 듯하다. 더없이 근엄한 사내들도 진지한 명상을 한바탕 배불리 하고 난 뒤에는, 그런 상념들을 후식 삼아 종종 스스로에게 허락하곤 한다. 바로 그 목적으로, 어떤 책이며 그림 따위가 그들 서재의 가장 은밀한 구석까지 흘러들고, 자연철학의 어떤 음탕한 대목이 흔히 그들 대화의 으뜸가는 주제가 되는 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 철학자가 하루 이틀 뒤에 정조의 요새가 이미 함락되었다는 소식을 듣자, 그는 자신의 욕망을 더 넓게 풀어놓기 시작했다. 그의 식욕은, 누가 이미 맛보았다 하여 별미를 입에 대지 못하는 그런 까다로운 부류가 아니었다. 한마디로, 그는 그녀가 정조를 잃었다는 바로 그 이유로 그녀가 더욱 마음에 들었다. 만약 그녀가 그 정조를 지니고 있었다면, 그것은 그의 쾌락을 가로막는 장애가 되었을 테니 말이다. 그는 그녀를 쫓아다녔고, 끝내 손에 넣었다.\n\n만약 몰리가 자기보다 어린 연인보다 스퀘어를 더 좋아했다고 독자가 여긴다면, 그것은 오해다. 오히려 그녀가 단 한 사람만을 골라야 하는 처지였다면, 그 둘 가운데 의심할 바 없이 톰 존스가 승자가 되었을 것이다. 또한 스퀘어가 성공을 거둔 까닭이 오로지 하나보다 둘이 낫다는 셈속(물론 이것도 나름의 무게를 지니기는 했다) 때문만도 아니었다. 존스가 몸져누워 있는 동안 자리를 비웠던 것이 불운한 사정이었고, 그 틈에 철학자가 골라 보낸 몇 가지 선물이 처녀의 마음을 어찌나 누그러뜨리고 무방비로 만들어 놓았던지, 절호의 기회가 거역할 수 없는 것이 되어 버렸다. 그리하여 스퀘어는 몰리의 가슴속에 남아 있던 정조의 가련한 잔재마저 짓밟고 승리를 거두었다.\n\n이 정복이 있은 지 어느덧 두 주쯤 지났을 무렵, 존스가 앞서 말한 대로 자기 연인을 찾아갔던 것이니, 바로 그녀와 스퀘어가 한 침대에 누워 있던 때였다. 우리가 본 대로 어머니가 그녀를 없다고 둘러댄 진짜 이유가 바로 이것이었다. 그 늙은 여인은 딸의 부정에서 생기는 이익을 나누어 가졌던 까닭에, 제 힘이 닿는 데까지 딸을 부추기고 또 감싸 주었던 것이다. 그러나 맏언니가 몰리에게 품은 시샘과 미움이 어찌나 컸던지, 자기도 그 노획물의 한몫을 차지하고 있었음에도, 동생을 망쳐 그 장사를 망쳐 놓을 수만 있다면 기꺼이 그 몫을 내던질 작정이었다. 그래서 그녀는 혹시 존스가 스퀘어의 품에 안긴 동생을 덮칠 수도 있으리라는 속셈으로, 동생이 위층에서 잠자리에 누워 있다고 그에게 일러 주었던 것이다. 그러나 문이 잠겨 있던 덕에 몰리는 이를 막아 낼 방도를 찾았고, 그 틈을 이용해 자기 연인을 그 깔개 혹은 담요 뒤로 숨겨 두었던 것이다. 그가 이제 거기서 불행히도 발각된 바로 그 자리 말이다.\n\n스퀘어가 모습을 드러내기가 무섭게 몰리는 침대에 벌렁 뒤로 자빠지며 자기는 끝장났다고 부르짖고는, 절망에 온몸을 내맡겨 버렸다. 이 가엾은 처녀는 아직 자기 장사에서 풋내기에 지나지 않았던지라, 어떤 위기에서도 도시의 귀부인을 척척 빠져나가게 해 주는 그 완벽한 뻔뻔함의 경지에는 미처 이르지 못했던 것이다. 그 뻔뻔함이란, 변명을 일러 주든가, 아니면 남편 앞에서 끝까지 시치미를 떼고 버티도록 부추겨 주는 것이다. 그 남편이란 자는 평온을 사랑하는 마음에서, 혹은 제 평판이 두려워서 ― 그리고 더러는 어쩌면 연극 속의 콘스탄트 씨처럼 칼을 차고 있는 정부가 무서워서 ― 기꺼이 두 눈을 질끈 감고, 제 뿔을 호주머니에 넣고 다니는 데 만족하는 법이다. 그러나 이와는 달리 몰리는 이 증거 앞에서 입이 막혀 버렸고, 여태껏 그토록 많은 눈물과, 더없이 순수한 사랑과 한결같음에 대한 그토록 엄숙하고 격렬한 항변으로 지켜 오던 명분을 깨끗이 포기하고 말았다.\n\n휘장 뒤의 그 신사로 말하자면, 그 또한 못지않게 큰 낭패에 빠져 있었다. 그는 한동안 꼼짝도 못 하고 서서, 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 또 눈길을 어디로 돌려야 할지조차 똑같이 막막해하는 듯했다. 존스는 셋 가운데 아마 가장 놀랐을 터인데도, 가장 먼저 입을 열었다. 그리고 몰리의 나무람이 자아냈던 그 거북한 심사에서 곧바로 벗어나, 그는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 이어 스퀘어 씨에게 인사를 건네고는, 그의 손을 잡아 그 갇힌 자리에서 풀어 주려고 다가갔다.\n\n스퀘어는 이제 방 한가운데, 즉 똑바로 설 수 있는 그 부분에 이르러서는, 자못 근엄한 낯빛으로 존스를 바라보며 이렇게 말했다. \"이보게, 자네가 이 대단한 발견을 즐기고 있다는 걸 알겠네. 그리고 단언컨대, 나를 폭로할 생각에 크게 흐뭇해하고 있겠지. 하지만 이 일을 공정하게 따져 본다면, 탓할 사람은 오로지 자네 자신뿐임을 알게 될 걸세. 나는 순결을 더럽힌 죄를 짓지 않았네. 옳고 그름의 잣대로 일을 판단하는 그런 부류의 사람들이 나를 단죄할 만한 짓은 하나도 하지 않았어. 적합함이란 사물의 본성에 의해 다스려지는 것이지, 관습이나 형식, 또는 시정의 법률에 의해 다스려지는 게 아닐세. 자연스럽지 않은 것이 아니라면, 실로 그 어떤 것도 부적합하지 않으니까.\" \"참 그럴듯한 논리로군, 이 친구야.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"한데 어째서 내가 자넬 폭로하고 싶어 하리라 여기는 건가? 정말이지, 난 평생 자네가 지금처럼 마음에 든 적이 없었네. 그러니 자네 스스로 이 일을 드러낼 작정이 아니라면, 이 일은 내게는 깊은 비밀로 남아 있을 걸세.\" \"아닐세, 존스 군.\" 스퀘어가 받아 말했다. \"내가 평판을 하찮게 여긴다고는 생각지 말게. 좋은 평판이란 '칼론'의 한 종류이니, 그것을 소홀히 하는 것은 결코 적합한 일이 아니지. 게다가 제 평판을 죽이는 것은 일종의 자살이며, 가증스럽고 혐오스러운 악덕일세. 그러니 만약 자네가 나의 어떤 약점을(나도 그런 게 있을 수 있지, 완전무결한 사람은 없으니 말일세) 기꺼이 감춰 줄 마음이 있다면, 나도 내 입으로 나를 들춰내지 않겠다고 약속하겠네. 행하기에는 적합하나 자랑하기에는 적합지 않은 일들이 있는 법이니까. 세상의 비뚤어진 판단으로 말미암아, 사실은 죄가 없을 뿐 아니라 칭찬받아 마땅한 일이 흔히 비난의 대상이 되곤 하니 말일세.\" \"옳거니!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"자연스러운 식욕을 채우는 것보다 더 죄 없는 일이 어디 있겠나? 또 우리 종족을 퍼뜨리는 것보다 더 칭찬받을 만한 일이 어디 있겠어?\" \"진지하게 말하자면,\" 스퀘어가 대답했다, \"내게는 그것이 늘 그렇게 보였다고 고백하네.\" \"한데도,\" 존스가 말했다, \"이 처녀와의 내 일이 처음 드러났을 때는 자네 생각이 달랐잖나.\" \"글쎄, 인정하지 않을 수 없군.\" 스퀘어가 말했다. \"그 일이 저 스왜컴 목사 녀석을 통해 내게 잘못 전해진 탓에, 나는 순결을 더럽힌 짓을 단죄했을지도 모르네. 그게, 이보게, 바로 그것 때문이었어 ― 그리고 또 그것 ―. 알다시피, 존스 군, 적합함을 헤아릴 때에는 아주 사소한 정황, 이보게, 아주 사소한 정황이 큰 차이를 빚어내는 법이거든.\" \"좋아.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그건 그렇다 치고, 내가 약속한 대로, 자네가 이 사건에 대해 다시 무슨 말을 듣게 된다면 그건 오로지 자네 자신의 탓일 걸세. 이 처녀에게 다정하게 대해 주게. 그러면 나는 이 일에 관해 누구에게도 결코 입을 열지 않겠네. 그리고 몰리, 너는 네 벗에게 충실해 다오. 그러면 나는 네가 내게 보인 부정을 용서할 뿐 아니라, 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 도움을 다 베풀어 주마.\" 이렇게 말하고서 그는 서둘러 작별을 고하고는, 사다리를 미끄러져 내려가 부리나케 물러갔다.\n\n스퀘어는 이 사건이 더 나쁜 결말로 치닫지는 않을 듯하자 무척 기뻐했다. 몰리로 말하자면, 혼란에서 벗어나자 처음에는 자기가 존스를 잃은 빌미가 되었다고 스퀘어를 나무라기 시작했다. 그러나 그 신사는 곧 그녀의 노여움을 누그러뜨릴 방도를 찾아냈으니, 한편으로는 어루만짐으로, 또 한편으로는 제 지갑에서 꺼낸 작은 묘약으로였다. 마음의 나쁜 기운을 씻어 내고 그것을 다시 좋은 기분으로 되돌려 놓는 데 놀랍도록 효험이 입증된 묘약 말이다.\n\n그러자 그녀는 새 연인을 향해 엄청난 애정을 한껏 쏟아부었다. 자기가 존스에게 했던 말이며 존스라는 사람 자체를 죄다 비웃음거리로 돌려 버렸고, 비록 한때 그가 자기 몸을 차지한 적은 있을지언정, 자기 마음의 주인이 되었던 사람은 오직 스퀘어뿐이라고 맹세했다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n앞 장과 견주어 보면, 독자는 자신이 전에 '사랑'이라는 말을 잘못 적용해 저질렀을지도 모를 어떤 오용을 바로잡게 될지도 모른다.\n\n이제 존스가 알아챈 몰리의 부정(不貞)은, 그가 그 자리에서 드러낸 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 분노를 정당화할 만한 것이었으리라. 그가 바로 그 순간 그녀를 곧장 내쳤다 한들, 그를 탓할 사람은 거의 없었으리라 나는 믿는다.\n\n그러나 분명한 것은, 그가 그녀를 연민의 눈으로 바라보았다는 사실이다. 그녀를 향한 그의 사랑은 그녀의 변심에 크게 괴로워할 만한 종류의 것이 아니었지만, 그래도 그는 애초에 자신이 그녀의 순결을 더럽혔다는 데 생각이 미치자 적잖이 충격을 받았다. 이제 그녀가 그토록 빠져들 것만 같은 온갖 악덕을, 그는 모두 이 타락의 탓으로 돌렸던 것이다.\n\n이 생각은 그에게 적잖은 괴로움을 안겨 주었으나, 얼마 후 언니인 베티가 친절하게도 한 가지 귀띔으로 그를 말끔히 낫게 해 주었다. 몰리를 처음 유혹한 자는 그 자신이 아니라 윌 반스라는 사내였으며, 지금껏 그가 그토록 확신하며 제 자식이라 단정해 온 그 어린아이도 적어도 반스를 아비로 주장할 동등한 권리쯤은 충분히 가졌을 법하다는 것이었다.\n\n존스는 그 단서를 처음 받아 들자마자 열심히 그 자취를 좇았다. 그리고 머지않아 그 아가씨가 진실을 말했음을 충분히 확신하게 되었는데, 그 사내의 자백뿐 아니라 마침내 몰리 자신의 입을 통해서도 그러했다.\n\n이 윌 반스는 시골의 바람둥이로, 이런 종류의 전리품이라면 왕국의 어느 소위나 변호사 서기 못지않게 많이 거둬들인 자였다. 실제로 그는 여러 여인을 완전한 타락의 구렁으로 몰아넣었고, 몇몇의 가슴을 찢어 놓았으며, 한 가엾은 처녀의 비참한 죽음을 불러일으키는 '영예'까지 누렸다. 그 처녀는 스스로 물에 빠져 죽었거나, 그보다 더 그럴듯하게는 그가 빠뜨려 죽인 것이었다.\n\n이 사내는 다른 여러 정복담 가운데서도 베티 시그림의 마음을 손에 넣어 의기양양했었다. 그는 몰리가 그런 놀이의 알맞은 상대가 되기 한참 전부터 그녀에게 구애했으나, 그 뒤에 그녀를 버리고 동생에게 들러붙었으며, 거기서는 거의 단번에 성공을 거두었다. 사실 윌이야말로 몰리의 애정을 독차지하고 있었고, 존스와 스퀘어는 그녀의 잇속과 허영에 거의 똑같이 바쳐진 제물에 지나지 않았다.\n\n우리가 앞서 베티의 마음속에서 들끓는 것을 본 그 누그러질 줄 모르는 증오는 바로 여기서 자라난 것이었다. 다만 우리는 그 까닭을 더 일찍 밝힐 필요까지는 없다고 여겼는데, 질투 하나만으로도 우리가 언급한 모든 결과를 빚어내기에 충분했기 때문이다.\n\n존스는 몰리에 관한 한 이 비밀을 손에 쥐고 나서 마음이 완전히 편안해졌다. 그러나 소피아에 관해서라면 결코 평온한 상태가 아니었으니, 아니, 실로 그는 더없이 격렬한 동요에 휩싸여 있었다. 비유를 써도 좋다면, 이제 그의 마음은 완전히 비워졌고 소피아가 그곳을 송두리째 차지해 버렸다. 그는 끝 모를 열정으로 그녀를 사랑했고, 그녀가 자신에게 품은 다정한 감정을 또렷이 알아보았다. 그러나 이 확신도 그녀 아버지의 동의를 얻으리라는 절망을 덜어 주지는 못했고, 어떤 비열하거나 배신적인 방법으로 그녀를 좇는 데 따르는 공포 또한 가시게 해 주지 못했다.\n\n그리하여 그가 웨스턴 씨에게 끼치게 될 해악과, 올워디 씨에게 미칠 근심은 온종일 그를 괴롭히고 밤이면 베갯머리에서 그를 따라다니며 시달리게 하는 사정들이었다. 그의 삶은 명예와 욕망 사이의 끊임없는 다툼이었고, 둘은 그의 마음속에서 번갈아 서로를 이겼다. 소피아가 곁에 없을 때면 그는 종종 그녀 아버지의 집을 떠나 다시는 그녀를 보지 않으리라 결심했고, 그녀가 곁에 있을 때면 그 모든 결심을 잊은 채 목숨을 걸고, 또 그에게 목숨보다 훨씬 더 소중한 것마저 잃을 각오로 그녀를 좇으리라 마음먹기 일쑤였다.\n\n이 갈등은 곧 매우 강하고 또렷한 결과를 빚어내기 시작했다. 그는 평소의 활기와 쾌활한 기질을 모두 잃었고, 혼자 있을 때 우울할 뿐 아니라 사람들 틈에서도 풀이 죽고 넋을 놓았다. 아니, 웨스턴 씨의 비위를 맞추려 억지 흥겨움을 꾸며 낼 때조차 그 부자연스러움이 너무도 빤히 드러나, 그렇게 과시하면서 감추려 애쓰던 바로 그것을 도리어 가장 확실하게 증명해 보이는 듯했다.\n\n어쩌면 이런 의문이 들지도 모른다. 그가 자신의 열정을 감추려 쓴 기교와, 정직한 자연이 그것을 드러내려 동원한 수단 가운데 무엇이 그를 더 많이 배신했을까? 기교가 그로 하여금 어느 때보다도 소피아에게 더 조심스럽게 굴게 하고, 그녀에게 어떤 말도 건네지 못하게 막았으며, 아니, 그녀의 눈길과 마주치는 것조차 더없이 신중하게 피하도록 했지만, 그러는 동안 자연도 못지않게 분주히 그를 거슬러 일을 꾸몄다. 그리하여 그 아가씨가 다가올 때면 그는 창백해졌고, 그것이 갑작스러우면 흠칫 놀랐다. 그의 눈이 우연히 그녀의 눈과 마주치면 피가 두 뺨으로 솟구쳐 얼굴이 온통 새빨개졌다. 흔한 예의상 그녀에게 말을 건네야 할 때면, 이를테면 식탁에서 그녀의 건강을 비는 잔을 들 때면 그의 혀는 어김없이 떨렸다. 그녀에게 손이 닿으면 그의 손이, 아니 온몸이 떨렸다. 그리고 어떤 이야기가 아무리 멀게나마 사랑의 관념을 불러일으키는 쪽으로 흐르면, 저도 모르게 새어 나오는 한숨이 그의 가슴에서 빠져나오지 않는 일이 드물었다. 그리고 이런 일들의 대부분을 자연은 날마다 그의 앞길에 던져 놓으려 놀랍도록 부지런히 애를 썼다.\n\n이 모든 징후가 지주의 눈은 피해 갔으나, 소피아의 눈은 그러지 못했다. 그녀는 곧 존스의 이런 심란함을 알아챘고, 그 까닭을 찾아내는 데 조금도 어려움이 없었다. 실로 그녀는 자기 가슴속에서 바로 그것을 알아보았던 것이다. 그리고 이 알아봄이야말로, 내 생각으로는, 연인들에게서 그토록 자주 언급되어 온 그 공감이며, 그녀가 아버지보다 훨씬 빨리 눈치챈 까닭을 충분히 설명해 줄 것이다.\n\n그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 어떤 사람들이 나머지 인류보다 월등히 뛰어난 통찰력을 지닌 것을 우리가 보게 될 때, 그것을 설명하는 더 단순하고 명백한 방법이 하나 있으니, 이는 연인의 경우뿐 아니라 다른 모든 경우에도 들어맞는다. 어찌하여 악당은 자신보다 훨씬 더 분별 있는 정직한 사람조차 곧잘 속아 넘어가는 그 악행의 징후와 작용을, 대개 그토록 재빠르게 알아보는 것일까? 악당들 사이에 무슨 보편적 공감 같은 것이 있을 리 없고, 프리메이슨처럼 서로 통하는 공통의 신호가 있는 것도 아니다. 실은 다만 그들의 머릿속에 같은 것이 들어 있고, 그들의 생각이 같은 방향으로 향해 있기 때문일 따름이다. 그러니 소피아는 보았고 웨스턴은 보지 못했던 존스의 빤한 사랑의 징후도, 그 아비의 머릿속에는 사랑의 관념이라곤 들어선 적이 없었던 반면 그 딸은 지금 다른 것은 아무것도 생각하지 않았다는 점을 헤아려 보면, 조금도 이상할 게 없다.\n\n가엾은 존스를 괴롭히는 격렬한 열정을 소피아가 충분히 납득하고, 또 그 대상이 다름 아닌 자기 자신임을 못지않게 확신하게 되자, 그녀는 지금 그의 행동의 참된 까닭을 알아내는 데 조금도 어려움이 없었다. 이로 인해 그는 그녀에게 더없이 사랑스러운 존재가 되었고, 어떤 연인이 연인의 마음속에 불러일으키기를 바랄 수 있는 가장 좋은 두 가지 감정을 그녀의 마음에 일으켰으니, 바로 존경과 연민이었다. 자기 때문에 비참해진 한 남자를 가엾이 여겼다 하여, 그녀의 성(性) 가운데 아무리 모질게 엄격한 이라도 그녀를 용서하지 않을 수 없을 것이다. 또한 더없이 명예로운 동기에서, 자신의 가슴속에서 타오르는 불꽃—저 유명한 스파르타 소년의 도둑질처럼 바로 그의 오장육부를 파먹고 좀먹어 들어가던 그 불꽃—을 눈에 띄게 억누르려 애쓰는 남자를 존경했다 하여 그녀를 탓할 수도 없으리라. 이리하여 그의 머뭇거림, 그녀를 피하는 것, 그의 냉담함, 그의 침묵이야말로 가장 적극적이고 가장 부지런하며 가장 따뜻하고 가장 웅변적인 변호인이 되어, 예민하고 다정한 그녀의 마음에 어찌나 격렬히 작용했던지, 그녀는 곧 정숙하고 고결한 여인의 마음에 어울리는 그 모든 부드러운 정을 그에게 느끼게 되었다. 요컨대, 존경과 감사와 연민이 그런 마음으로 하여금 호감 가는 남자를 향해 불러일으킬 수 있는 모든 것, 실로 더없이 섬세한 정결함이 허락할 수 있는 모든 것이었다. 한마디로, 그녀는 정신을 잃을 만큼 그를 사랑하고 있었다.\n\n어느 날 이 젊은 두 사람은 정원에서 우연히 마주쳤다. 두 산책로의 끝자락이었는데, 그 두 길은 모두 저 운하에 닿아 있었으니, 바로 존스가 전에 소피아가 잃어버린 작은 새를 되찾으려 익사할 위험까지 무릅썼던 그 운하였다.\n\n이곳은 근래 소피아가 자주 찾는 곳이 되어 있었다. 그녀는 여기서, 그 자체로는 아무리 사소할지언정 어쩌면 지금 그녀의 마음속에서 이토록 무르익은 그 애정의 첫 씨앗을 뿌렸을지도 모를 한 사건을, 괴로움과 즐거움이 뒤섞인 심정으로 곱씹곤 했다.\n\n그리하여 이곳에서 이 젊은 두 사람이 마주쳤다. 둘은 서로 다가오는 것을 알아채기도 전에 거의 코앞에 다다라 있었다. 곁에서 지켜보는 이라면 둘의 얼굴에서 당혹의 빛을 넉넉히 알아챘을 테지만, 정작 두 사람은 자기 감정에 너무 휩싸여 무엇을 살펴볼 겨를이 없었다. 존스는 처음의 놀람에서 조금 정신을 차리자마자 흔한 인사말 몇 마디로 그 아가씨에게 말을 건넸고, 그녀도 같은 식으로 답했다. 그리고 둘의 대화는 늘 그렇듯 아침의 황홀한 아름다움에서 시작되었다. 거기서 그들은 이 장소의 아름다움으로 옮겨 갔는데, 존스는 거기에 더없이 드높은 찬사를 늘어놓았다. 그가 전에 운하로 곤두박질쳤던 그 나무에 이르자, 소피아는 그 사고를 떠올려 주지 않을 수 없어 이렇게 말했다. \"존스 씨, 저 물을 보시면 조금은 몸서리가 쳐지지 않을까 싶네요.\"—\"맹세컨대, 아가씨,\" 존스가 답했다. \"그 모험에서 제게 가장 큰 일은 언제까지나, 작은 새를 잃고 아가씨께서 느끼신 그 안타까움일 겁니다. 가엾은 토미! 저기 저 가지가 그 녀석이 앉아 있던 곳이지요. 그 작은 가엾은 것이 어쩌자고 제가 영광스럽게 마련해 준 그 행복한 처지에서 날아가 버리는 어리석음을 저질렀을까요? 그 운명은 녀석의 배은망덕에 대한 마땅한 벌이었습니다.\"—\"정말이지, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신의 그 용맹도 똑같이 가혹한 운명을 아슬아슬하게 면했지요. 그 일을 떠올리면 분명 마음이 흔들리실 텐데요.\"—\"실로, 아가씨,\" 그가 답했다. \"제가 그 일을 슬픔으로 돌이켜 볼 까닭이 있다면, 그것은 어쩌면 그 물이 조금만 더 깊지 않았다는 점일 겁니다. 그랬다면 운명이 제게 마련해 둔 듯한 그 숱한 쓰라린 마음앓이를 면했을 테니까요.\"—\"어머, 존스 씨!\" 소피아가 받아쳤다. \"지금 진심이실 리 없어요. 이렇게 삶을 짐짓 가벼이 여기시는 건 그저 제게 베푸시는 지나친 호의일 뿐이에요. 저를 위해 두 번이나 목숨을 거셨다는 그 빚의 무게를 덜어 보려 하시는 거지요. 세 번째는 조심하세요.\" 그녀는 이 마지막 말을, 형언할 수 없는 미소와 부드러움을 담아 했다. 존스는 한숨으로 답했다. \"조심하기엔 이미 너무 늦은 것 같아 두렵습니다.\" 그러고는 그녀를 다정하고도 한결같이 바라보며 외쳤다. \"오, 웨스턴 아가씨! 제게 살라고 하실 수 있습니까? 제게 그토록 모진 것을 바라실 수 있습니까?\" 소피아는 땅을 내려다보며 다소 머뭇거리는 목소리로 답했다. \"정말이지, 존스 씨, 저는 당신께 모진 것을 바라지 않아요.\"—\"오, 저는 그 천국 같은 마음씨를 너무도 잘 압니다,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"다른 어떤 매력보다도 뛰어난 그 거룩한 선함을요.\"—\"아니에요, 이제,\" 그녀가 답했다. \"저는 당신을 못 알아듣겠어요. 더는 머물 수가 없네요.\"—\"저는… 저는 알아들으시지 않기를 바랐습니다!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"아니, 알아들으실 수가 없지요. 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모르겠습니다. 여기서 이토록 뜻밖에 아가씨를 뵙고는 그만 마음을 놓아 버렸습니다. 제발, 제가 아가씨를 거스를 말을 했다면 용서해 주십시오. 그럴 뜻은 없었습니다. 실로 저는 차라리 죽는 편이 나았을 겁니다. 아니, 그런 생각만으로도 저는 죽을 것 같습니다.\"—\"놀랍군요,\" 그녀가 답했다. \"어떻게 당신이 저를 거슬렀다고 생각하실 수 있나요?\"—\"두려움이란, 아가씨,\" 그가 말했다. \"쉬이 광기로 치닫는 법이지요. 그리고 아가씨를 거스를까 하는 두려움만 한 두려움은 없습니다. 그러니 제가 어찌 말을 할 수 있겠습니까? 아니, 노여운 눈으로 저를 보지 말아 주십시오. 한 번의 찡그림이 저를 무너뜨릴 겁니다. 저는 아무 뜻도 없습니다. 제 눈을 탓하시든지, 아니면 저 아름다움을 탓하십시오. 제가 무슨 말을 하는 걸까요? 너무 많은 말을 했다면 용서해 주십시오. 제 마음이 넘쳐흘렀습니다. 저는 제 사랑과 끝까지 싸워 왔고, 제 오장육부를 파먹는 이 열병을 감추려 애써 왔습니다. 바라건대 그 열병이 머지않아, 제가 다시는 아가씨를 거스를 수 없게 만들어 줄 것입니다.\"\n\n이제 존스는 학질 발작에 흔들리기라도 하듯 부들부들 떨기 시작했다. 그와 그리 다르지 않은 처지에 놓인 소피아는 이렇게 답했다. \"존스 씨, 저는 당신을 못 알아들은 척하지 않겠어요. 실은 너무도 잘 알아들었으니까요. 하지만 제발, 제게 조금이라도 정이 있으시다면, 제가 무사히 집으로 들어가게 해 주세요. 거기까지 몸을 가눌 수 있으면 좋으련만.\"\n\n자기 몸조차 가누기 힘들던 존스는 그녀에게 팔을 내밀었고, 그녀는 황송하게도 그것을 받아들였다. 다만 지금은 이런 종류의 말을 한마디도 더 꺼내지 말아 달라고 청했다. 그는 그러겠노라 약속하며, 다만 자기 의지의 허락도 없이 사랑이 억지로 끌어낸 그 말을 용서해 달라고만 거듭 졸랐다. 그녀는 앞으로의 처신으로 그 용서를 얻는 법을 그가 알고 있을 거라고 말했다. 그리하여 이 젊은 한 쌍은 비틀거리고 떨면서 걸어갔는데, 연인은 자기 손에 그녀의 손이 맞물려 있었는데도 단 한 번도 감히 그 손을 꼭 쥐어 보지 못했다.\n\n소피아는 곧장 자기 방으로 물러갔고, 그곳으로 어너 부인과 사슴뿔 정기(精氣)가 그녀를 돕도록 불려 왔다. 가엾은 존스로 말하자면, 병든 그의 마음에 유일한 위안이 된 것은 달갑지 않은 한 가지 소식이었다. 이 소식은 독자가 근래 접해 온 것과는 사뭇 다른 성격의 장면을 여는 것이므로, 다음 장에서 그에게 전하기로 하겠다.\n\n올워디 씨가 병상에 누운 이야기.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 어느새 존스를 무척 좋아하게 되어, 그의 팔이 다 나은 지 오래였는데도 그를 떠나보내려 하지 않았다. 존스 또한 사냥을 좋아해서였는지 아니면 다른 어떤 이유에서였는지, 그의 집에 계속 머물러 달라는 청에 쉽게 넘어갔다. 그래서 가끔은 보름씩 내리 그 집에 머무르며 올워디 씨 댁에 한 번도 들르지 않았고, 더구나 그곳 소식조차 듣지 못한 채 지내곤 했다.\n\n올워디 씨는 며칠 전부터 감기로 몸이 편치 않았는데, 거기에 가벼운 열까지 따라붙었다. 그러나 그는 이것을 대수롭지 않게 여겼다. 자신을 침대에 눕게 만들거나 여러 기능을 평소처럼 발휘하지 못하게 가로막지 않는 종류의 병이라면 모두 그렇게 무심히 넘기는 것이 그의 버릇이었던 까닭이다. 우리는 이런 처신을 결코 옳다 여기거나 본받으라 권할 마음은 없다. 의술을 다루는 신사 분들이 권하는 말, 곧 병이 한쪽 문으로 들어오는 그 순간 의사를 다른 쪽 문으로 들여보내야 한다는 충고야말로 분명 지당한 것이기 때문이다. 저 오래된 격언, \"베니엔티 오쿠리테 모르보(Venienti occurrite morbo)\"가 달리 무엇을 뜻하겠는가? \"병이 처음 다가올 때 맞서 싸우라\"는 말이다. 그리하면 의사와 병은 공정하고 대등한 싸움에서 맞붙게 된다. 그러나 병에게 시간을 내어주면, 우리는 흔히 그것이 프랑스 군대처럼 스스로를 굳건히 다지고 참호를 파게 내버려두는 셈이 된다. 그래서 그 박식한 신사 분이 적에게 다가가기가 무척 어려워지고, 때로는 아예 불가능해진다. 아니, 때로는 병이 시간을 벌어 프랑스식 군사 책략을 끌어들이고, 자연마저 제 편으로 매수해 버린다. 그러면 의술의 온갖 힘이 도착해도 이미 너무 늦은 것이다. 이런 관찰과 잘 들어맞는 것이, 내가 기억하기로, 저 위대한 미소뱅 박사의 한탄이다. 그는 자신의 솜씨를 너무 늦게 찾는 일을 무척 애처롭게 탄식하며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"맙소사, 내 환자들은 나를 장의사로 아는 모양이야. 의사가 저들을 죽여 놓기 전에는 결코 나를 부르지 않으니 말이지.\"\n\n올워디 씨의 병은 이렇게 방치된 탓에 어찌나 깊어졌던지, 열이 더 심해져 마침내 도움을 청해야 했을 때, 의사는 도착하자마자 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 좀 더 일찍 불렀어야 했다고 아쉬워했고, 환자가 매우 위태로운 지경에 이른 것 같다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 올워디 씨는 이 세상의 일을 모두 정리해 두었고, 인간의 본성이 다른 세상을 향해 준비할 수 있는 한 가장 잘 준비되어 있던 터라, 이 소식을 더없이 차분하고 태연하게 받아들였다. 실로 그는 잠자리에 누울 때마다 저 비극 시 속의 카토처럼 이렇게 말할 수 있었다.\n\n죄나 두려움일랑 사람의 안식을 어지럽히게 두라. 카토는 그 둘을 다 모른다. 잠들든 죽든, 그 선택에 마음이 흔들리지 않으니.\n\n사실 그는 카토보다, 아니 고금의 어떤 자긍심 높은 영웅들보다 열 배는 더 떳떳하고 확신에 차서 이렇게 말할 수 있었다. 그는 두려움이 없을 뿐 아니라, 추수가 끝날 무렵 후한 주인의 손에서 삯을 받으라 불려가는 충실한 일꾼에 견줄 만한 사람이었기 때문이다.\n\n이 선량한 분은 곧바로 온 가족을 자기 곁으로 불러 모으라 명했다. 그때 집을 떠나 있던 사람은 한동안 런던에 가 있던 블리필 부인과, 독자가 방금 웨스턴 씨 댁에서 작별한 존스 씨뿐이었는데, 존스는 소피아가 막 그의 곁을 떠난 바로 그 순간에 이 부름을 받았다.\n\n올워디 씨가 위독하다는 소식은(하인이 그에게 주인이 죽어 간다고 전했으므로) 사랑에 관한 온갖 생각을 그의 머릿속에서 몰아내 버렸다. 그는 자신을 데리러 온 마차에 곧장 뛰어올라 마부에게 상상할 수 있는 한 가장 빠르게 몰아 달라고 명했다. 그리고 가는 길에 소피아의 모습은, 내 생각으로는, 단 한 번도 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다.\n\n이제 온 가족, 곧 블리필 씨, 존스 씨, 스웨컴 씨, 스퀘어 씨, 그리고 하인 몇몇이(올워디 씨가 그렇게 명했던 까닭이다) 모두 그의 침대 둘레에 모이자, 이 선량한 분은 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 막 말을 꺼내려 했다. 그때 블리필이 흐느끼기 시작하더니 아주 크고 비통한 탄식을 쏟아내기 시작했다. 그러자 올워디 씨는 그의 손을 잡아 흔들며 말했다. \"사랑하는 조카야, 인간에게 일어나는 일 가운데 가장 흔한 일을 두고 그리 슬퍼하지 마라. 벗에게 불운이 닥칠 때 우리가 슬퍼하는 것은 마땅하다. 그런 것은 흔히 피할 수도 있었던 사고요, 한 사람의 처지를 다른 이들보다 유난히 더 불행하게 만드는 것처럼 보이는 일이니까. 그러나 죽음은 분명 피할 수 없는 것이며, 모든 사람의 운명이 오직 그 한 가지에서만은 똑같아지는, 그 공통된 몫이다. 또한 이 일이 우리에게 언제 닥치느냐는 그리 중요하지 않다. 가장 지혜로운 사람이 인생을 한 뼘에 견주었다면, 우리야 그것을 하루에 견주어도 좋으리라. 저녁에 그 자리를 떠나는 것이 내 운명이다. 그러나 더 일찍 불려 간 이들은 고작 몇 시간을 잃었을 뿐이니, 그 몇 시간이란 잘해야 애석해할 만한 가치도 별로 없고, 훨씬 더 흔하게는 노고와 피로, 고통과 슬픔의 시간일 따름이다. 내 기억으로는 로마의 한 시인이 우리가 삶을 떠나는 것을 잔치 자리에서 물러나는 것에 견주었더구나. 이것은 사람들이 즐거운 자리를 조금이라도 더 끌어 보려, 벗들과 함께하는 시간을 잠시라도 더 누려 보려 애쓰는 것을 볼 때마다 내게 자주 떠오르곤 하던 생각이었다. 아아! 그런 즐거움이란 아무리 길게 끈다 한들 얼마나 짧은가! 가장 먼저 자리를 뜨는 사람과 가장 늦게까지 남는 사람 사이의 차이가 얼마나 하찮은가! 이것이 삶을 가장 좋게 보는 길이며, 벗들과 헤어지기 싫어하는 이 마음이야말로 우리가 죽음에 대한 두려움을 길어 올릴 수 있는 가장 사랑스러운 동기다. 그러나 우리가 이런 종류의 즐거움으로 바랄 수 있는 가장 긴 누림조차 그 지속이 너무나 보잘것없어서, 지혜로운 사람에게는 참으로 하찮게 여겨질 따름이다. 나도 인정하건대, 이렇게 생각하는 이는 드물다. 실로 사람들은 죽음의 아가리에 들어서기 전에는 죽음을 생각하지 않으니까. 죽음이 다가올 때 그 대상이 아무리 거대하고 무섭게 보일지라도, 사람들은 멀찍이 떨어진 거리에서는 그것을 보지 못한다. 아니, 죽을 위험에 처했다고 느꼈을 때 제아무리 놀라고 겁에 질렸던 사람이라도, 그 두려움에서 벗어나기가 무섭게 죽음에 대한 공포마저 마음에서 깨끗이 지워 버린다. 그러나 아아! 죽음에서 벗어난 자는 사면받은 것이 아니다. 그저 집행이 미뤄졌을 뿐이며, 그것도 잠깐 동안 미뤄졌을 따름이다.\n\n\"그러니 사랑하는 아이야, 이 일로 더는 슬퍼하지 마라. 시시각각 일어날 수 있는 일, 우리를 둘러싼 모든 원소가, 아니 거의 모든 물질의 알갱이 하나하나가 능히 일으킬 수 있는 일, 그리고 끝내는 더없이 피할 길 없이 우리 모두에게 반드시 닥치고야 말 일이라면, 그것은 우리의 놀람도 우리의 탄식도 불러일으켜서는 안 되는 것이다.\n\n\"내 의사가 내게 알려 주기를(이는 그의 친절로 고맙게 여긴다), 내가 머지않아 너희 모두를 떠날 위험에 처했다 하니, 나는 이 작별의 자리에서 너희에게 몇 마디 말을 남기기로 마음먹었다. 내가 보기에 무척 빠르게 깊어 가는 이 병이 그럴 힘마저 앗아가 버리기 전에 말이다.\n\n\"하지만 내 기력을 너무 많이 소진하겠구나. 나는 내 유언에 대해 말하려 했다. 비록 오래전에 다 정해 둔 것이지만, 너희 가운데 누구와든 관련된 대목만은 짚어 두는 것이 옳다고 본다. 그래야 내가 거기에 너희를 위해 마련해 둔 몫에 너희 모두가 흡족해하는 것을 보는 위안을 누릴 수 있을 테니.\n\n\"조카 블리필, 나는 너를 내 전 재산의 상속자로 삼는다. 다만 연 500파운드는 예외로, 이는 네 어머니가 세상을 떠난 뒤에 네게 돌아갈 것이며, 또 연 500파운드의 다른 재산 한 몫과 6000파운드의 돈도 예외인데, 이것들은 내가 다음과 같이 나누어 주었다.\n\n\"연 500파운드의 그 재산은 그대, 존스 군에게 주었네. 그리고 나는 당장 쓸 현금이 없을 때 따르는 불편을 잘 알기에, 거기에 현금 1000파운드를 보탰네. 이 일로 내가 그대의 기대를 넘었는지 모자랐는지는 나도 모르겠네. 어쩌면 그대는 내가 너무 적게 주었다 여길지도 모르고, 세상은 또 내가 너무 많이 주었다며 나를 나무라려 들겠지. 그러나 뒤의 비난이라면 나는 경멸하네. 앞의 것에 대해 말하자면, 그대가 내가 살면서 자주 들어 온 그 흔한 잘못된 생각을 품지만 않는다면 말일세. 곧 자발적인 은혜의 베풂으로 감사를 일으키기는커녕, 도리어 요구를 키우기 쉽다는 생각, 그 요구란 무엇보다 끝이 없고 무엇보다 채우기 어렵다는 그 생각 말이네. 자선이 전혀 없는 것을 변명하는 핑계로 자주 들먹여지던 그 말 말일세. 이런 것을 입에 올린 것조차 용서하게. 나는 그대에게 그런 일은 조금도 의심하지 않겠네.\"\n\n존스는 자기 은인의 발치에 몸을 던지고 그의 손을 간절히 부여잡으며, 이번에도 그렇고 다른 모든 때에도 그렇고, 자신에게 베풀어 주신 그 자애가 자신의 공로는 물론 자신의 바람조차 한없이 넘어선 것이어서 어떤 말로도 그 고마움을 다 표현할 수 없다고 단언했다. \"그리고 분명히 말씀드리지만, 어르신,\" 그가 말했다. \"지금 베풀어 주신 이 너그러움은 제게, 오직 이 슬픈 자리에 대한 근심 말고는 어떤 다른 근심도 남겨 두지 않았습니다. 오, 나의 벗이여, 나의 아버지여!\" 여기서 말이 그의 목에 메었고, 그는 눈에서 솟아나는 눈물을 감추려 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n그러자 올워디는 그의 손을 부드럽게 꼭 쥐고는 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"내 확신하네, 아이야, 그대의 천성에는 선함과 너그러움과 명예가 많이 깃들어 있어. 여기에 신중함과 신앙심까지 보탠다면 그대는 반드시 행복할 것이네. 인정하건대 앞의 세 가지 자질은 그대를 행복을 누릴 자격이 있는 사람으로 만들어 주지만, 그 행복을 실제로 손에 쥐게 해 주는 것은 오직 뒤의 두 가지뿐이라네.\n\n\"그대, 스웨컴 씨에게는 1000파운드를 주었네. 이는 그대의 바람은 물론 그대의 필요까지도 크게 넘어서는 액수라고 나는 확신하네. 그래도 내 우정의 정표로 받아 주게. 그리고 그대에게 넘쳐 남는 것이 무엇이든, 그대가 그토록 엄격히 지켜 온 그 경건함이 그것을 어떻게 처분할지 일러 줄 것이네.\n\n\"스퀘어 씨, 그대에게도 같은 액수를 남겼네. 이것이 그대로 하여금 지금까지보다 더 나은 성취를 거두며 그대의 직업을 이어 가게 해 주기를 바라네. 나는 곤궁이 동정보다 경멸을 불러일으키기 더 쉽다는 것을, 특히 사업하는 사람들 사이에서 그러하다는 것을 자주 안타깝게 지켜보았네. 그들에게 가난이란 능력이 모자란다는 표시로 통하니 말일세. 그러나 내가 그대에게 남길 수 있었던 이 얼마 안 되는 것이, 그대가 전에 씨름하던 그 어려움에서 그대를 건져 줄 것이네. 그러고 나면 그대 같은 철학자 기질의 사람이 필요로 하는 것을 채워 줄 만한 넉넉한 번영을 그대가 분명 누리게 되리라 나는 의심치 않네.\n\n\"기운이 점점 빠져 가는구나. 그러니 나머지를 어떻게 처분했는지는 내 유언장을 보아 다오. 내 하인들도 거기서 나를 기억할 만한 정표 몇 가지를 찾게 될 것이며, 또 몇 가지 자선이 있으니, 내 유언 집행인들이 그것을 충실히 이행해 주리라 믿는다. 너희 모두에게 축복이 있기를. 나는 너희보다 조금 앞서 길을 떠나는 것이란다.\"\n\n이때 한 하인이 황급히 방으로 들어와, 솔즈베리에서 온 변호사가 특별한 전갈을 가지고 왔으며 그것을 반드시 올워디 씨께 직접 전해야 한다고 한다고 알렸다. 그가 몹시 다급해 보였으며, 처리할 일이 어찌나 많은지 자기 몸을 넷으로 쪼갠다 해도 다 못 할 지경이라고 큰소리를 치더라는 것이었다.\n\n\"가 보아라, 아이야,\" 올워디가 블리필에게 말했다. \"그 사람이 무엇을 원하는지 보고 오너라. 나는 지금 어떤 일도 처리할 수 없거니와, 그가 나와 볼일이 있다 한들 그 일은 지금 나보다 네가 더 깊이 관련된 일일 테니. 게다가 나는 정말이지 지금은 누구도 만날 수 없고, 더 오래 무언가에 마음을 쏟을 수도 없구나.\" 그러고는 그들 모두에게 작별 인사를 건네며, 어쩌면 다시 그들을 볼 수 있을지도 모르겠으나 지금은 좀 마음을 가라앉히고 싶다고, 이야기에 너무 많은 기력을 쏟은 것 같다고 말했다.\n\n자리에 있던 몇몇은 작별하며 눈물을 흘렸다. 좀처럼 마음이 녹는 일이 없던 철학자 스퀘어조차 눈가를 훔쳤다. 윌킨스 부인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 아라비아의 나무들이 약재로 쓰이는 진을 뚝뚝 흘리듯 진주 같은 눈물방울을 줄줄 떨구었다. 이는 그 부인이 마땅한 자리에서라면 결코 빠뜨리는 법이 없는 의식이었던 까닭이다.\n\n이런 일이 있은 뒤 올워디 씨는 다시 베개에 몸을 누이고, 마음을 가라앉혀 쉬어 보려 애썼다.\n\n여덟째 장.\n유쾌하다기보다는 자연스러운 일을 담은 장.\n\n주인을 향한 슬픔 말고도, 가정부의 두 산봉우리 같은 광대뼈 위로 그토록 넘치게 솟아오른 그 짭짤한 물줄기에는 또 다른 원천이 있었다. 그녀는 방에서 물러나오자마자 다음과 같이 흥겨운 가락으로 혼자 중얼거리기 시작했다. “정말이지, 주인 나리께서 나하고 다른 하인들을 좀 구별해 주실 만도 했을 텐데. 내게 상복 값이나 남겨 주신 모양이지. 하지만 어머나! 그게 다라면, 그건 나리를 위해 악마더러 입으라지, 난 안 입을 테야. 나리께서 알아 두셨으면 좋겠네, 나는 거지가 아니라는 걸. 나리를 모시면서 오백 파운드나 모았는데, 결국 이런 대접을 받다니. 하인더러 정직하게 살라고 부추기는 데 참 좋은 본보기로다. 하긴, 내가 가끔 이것저것 좀 챙겼다 쳐도, 다른 이들은 그 열 배는 챙겼지. 그런데 이제 우리를 죄다 한 덩어리로 뭉뚱그려 놓았으니. 정 그렇다면, 그 유산은 그걸 준 사람과 함께 악마한테나 가 버려라. 아니, 그래도 포기는 안 할 테야. 그러면 어떤 작자들이 좋아할 테니까. 아니, 구할 수 있는 가장 화려한 가운을 사서, 그걸 입고 그 노랑이의 무덤 위에서 춤을 출 테야. 온 고장 사람들이 그 사생아를 그런 식으로 키운다고 부끄러운 줄 알라며 손가락질할 때마다 그렇게도 자주 나리 편을 들어 준 보답이 이거라니. 하지만 이제 나리는 모든 걸 갚아야 할 곳으로 가시는 길이지. 임종 자리에서 죄를 뉘우치는 편이 훨씬 어울렸을 텐데, 도리어 그 죄를 자랑하며 제 집안 식구도 아닌 천출의 자식에게 재산을 떼어 주다니. 침대에서 주웠다고? 참, 그럴듯한 이야기로군! 흥, 감추는 자들이 어디서 찾을지 아는 법이지. 주님, 저분을 용서하소서! 진실이 밝혀진다면, 책임져야 할 사생아가 더 많이 있을 게 분명해. 한 가지 위안이라면, 나리께서 이제 가시는 곳에서는 그것들이 죄다 밝혀지리란 거지. ‘하인들에게도 나를 기억할 만한 표시를 남겨 두겠노라.’ 바로 그 말씀이었어. 천 년을 산다 해도 나는 그 말을 잊지 못할 거야. 흥, 흥, 나를 하인들 틈에 욱여넣으신 걸 똑똑히 기억해 드리지. 스퀘어의 이름을 부르신 것처럼 내 이름도 부를 만하셨을 텐데. 하지만 그자는 신사이시란다, 처음 여기 왔을 적엔 등에 걸칠 옷 한 벌 없었으면서. 그런 신사 나리들과는 어디 한번 어울려 보시지! 그자가 여기서 그 여러 해를 살았어도, 이 집 하인 중에 그자 돈 색깔이라도 본 사람이 있을 성싶지 않아. 그런 신사 나리는 악마더러나 모시라지.” 그녀는 이런 식의 말을 훨씬 더 많이 혼자 중얼거렸지만, 이쯤이면 독자에게 그 맛보기로 충분할 것이다.\n\n스왜컴도 스퀘어도 제 유산에 그다지 만족하지 못하기는 마찬가지였다. 두 사람 다 그 원망을 그토록 큰 소리로 토해 내지는 않았으나, 얼굴에 떠오른 불만으로 보나 다음에 이어지는 대화로 보나, 우리는 그들의 마음속에 대단한 흡족함이 깃들지는 않았음을 짐작하게 된다.\n\n병실을 나선 지 한 시간쯤 지나, 스퀘어는 현관 홀에서 스왜컴과 마주치자 이렇게 말을 건넸다. “자, 선생, 우리가 그분 곁을 떠난 뒤로 댁의 벗에 관한 소식을 좀 들으셨소?” “올워디 씨를 두고 하는 말씀이라면,” 스왜컴이 대꾸했다. “차라리 그분을 댁의 벗이라 부르는 편이 맞을 듯하오. 내가 보기엔 그분이 그 칭호를 받을 자격이 있는 듯하니 말이오.” “그 칭호라면 댁 쪽에도 똑같이 합당하오.” 스퀘어가 받아쳤다. “그분의 그 후한 베풂이라는 것이, 그 정도가 어떻든 우리 둘에게 똑같았으니 말이오.” “먼저 꺼낼 생각은 없었소만,” 스왜컴이 외쳤다. “댁이 시작했으니 알려 드려야겠소, 나는 생각이 다르오. 자발적인 호의와 보상 사이에는 큰 차이가 있소. 내가 그 집안에서 해 온 직무와, 두 소년의 교육에 기울인 정성은, 어떤 이들이라면 더 큰 보답을 기대했을 만한 봉사요. 그렇다고 내가 불만스러워한다고 여기지는 마시오. 성 바울께서 내게 작은 것에 만족하라 가르치셨으니. 설령 그 몫이 더 적었다 한들, 나는 내 본분을 알았을 거요. 그러나 성경이 내게 만족하라 명하신다 하여, 내 공적에 눈을 감으라 명하시는 것은 아니며, 부당한 비교로 손해를 입었을 때 그것을 알아차리는 일까지 막으시는 것도 아니오.” “댁이 나를 자극하니 하는 말인데,” 스퀘어가 응수했다. “그 손해를 입은 것은 바로 나요. 올워디 씨가 내 우정을 그토록 가벼이 여겨, 품삯을 받는 자와 나를 저울에 나란히 올리리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했소. 어쩌다 그리 되었는지 나는 알고 있소. 그것은 댁이 그토록 오랫동안 그분께 불어넣으려 애써 온 그 옹졸한 원칙들에서 비롯된 것이오. 위대하고 고귀한 모든 것을 업신여기는 그 원칙 말이오. 우정의 아름다움과 사랑스러움은 흐린 눈으로 보기엔 너무도 강렬해서, 댁이 그토록 자주 비웃으려 들던 그 어김없는 옳음의 척도가 아니고서는 어떤 매개로도 알아볼 수가 없소. 댁은 그것을 비웃은 끝에, 댁의 벗의 분별력마저 비틀어 놓고 만 거요.” “나는 바라오,” 스왜컴이 격분하여 외쳤다. “그분의 영혼을 위해 바라건대, 댁의 그 저주받을 교리가 그분의 믿음마저 비틀어 놓지 않았기를 말이오. 나는 그분의 지금 행실을, 그리스도인답지 않은 그 행실을 바로 거기에 돌리는 바요. 무신론자가 아니고서야 그 누가, 먼저 제 셈을 다 치르지도 않고서 이 세상을 떠날 생각을 하겠소? 죄를 고백하지도 않고, 집안에 그것을 베풀 권한을 정당히 받은 사람이 하나 있는 줄 알면서도 그 사죄를 받지 않고서 말이오? 그분은 너무 늦었을 때에야 이 필수품들의 부재를 느끼게 될 거요. 통곡과 이 가는 소리가 있는 그곳에 다다랐을 때에 말이오. 바로 그때 그분은, 댁과 이 시대의 다른 모든 이신론자들이 떠받드는 그 이교의 여신, 그 덕이라는 것이 자기에게 얼마나 큰 도움이 되는지 깨닫게 될 거요. 그때 그분은 사제를 부를 테지만 찾을 수 없을 것이고, 그것 없이는 어떤 죄인도 안전할 수 없는 그 사죄의 부재를 한탄하게 될 거요.” “그것이 그토록 긴요하다면,” 스퀘어가 말했다. “어찌하여 댁이 자청해서 그분께 베풀지 않으시오?” “그것은,” 스왜컴이 외쳤다. “그것을 청할 만한 충분한 은총을 지닌 자에게가 아니고서는 아무 효력이 없소. 한데 내가 어쩌자고 이교도이자 불신자에게 이런 말을 하고 있는가? 그분께 이 가르침을 일러 준 것은 바로 댁이오. 그 대가로 댁은 이 세상에서 두둑한 보답을 받았으니, 댁의 제자가 머지않아 저세상에서도 그러하리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않소.” “보답이라는 말로 무엇을 뜻하는지 모르겠소.” 스퀘어가 말했다. “하지만 그분이 우리 우정의 기념이랍시고 내게 남겨 주기로 한 그 보잘것없는 표시를 빗대는 거라면, 나는 그것을 경멸하오. 내 처지가 불운하지만 않았던들, 그 무엇도 나로 하여금 그것을 받아들이게 하지 못했을 거요.”\n\n이제 의사가 도착하여, 위층 우리 모두의 안부가 어떠한지 두 논객에게 묻기 시작했다. “비참한 지경이오.” 스왜컴이 대답했다. “내가 짐작한 그대로로군요.” 의사가 외쳤다. “한데 부디 알려 주시오, 내가 자리를 비운 사이에 어떤 증세가 나타났는지요?” “좋은 것은 없는 듯하오.” 스왜컴이 대꾸했다. “우리가 떠날 적에 오간 일을 보면, 거의 희망이 없는 듯하오.” 육신의 의사는 아마도 영혼의 치유자를 잘못 알아들은 모양이었다. 한데 두 사람이 그 뜻을 풀어 밝히기도 전에, 블리필 씨가 더없이 침울한 얼굴로 다가와, 슬픈 소식을 가져왔노라 알렸다. 그의 어머니가 솔즈베리에서 세상을 떠났다는 것이었다. 집으로 돌아오는 길에 머리와 위장에 통풍이 덮쳐, 몇 시간 만에 그분을 데려가 버렸다고 했다. “저런, 이를 어쩌나!” 의사가 말했다. “이런 일이야 누구도 장담할 수 없는 노릇이지만, 그래도 내가 곁에 있어 불려 갈 수 있었더라면 좋았을 것을. 통풍은 다스리기 까다로운 병이지요. 그래도 나는 그 병에 유달리 성과가 좋았소.” 스왜컴과 스퀘어는 둘 다 블리필 씨에게 어머니를 여읜 일에 조의를 표했는데, 한 사람은 그에게 사내답게 견디라 충고했고, 다른 한 사람은 그리스도인답게 견디라 충고했다. 젊은 신사는, 우리 모두가 죽을 운명임을 자기도 잘 알고 있으며, 이 상실에 되도록 잘 순응하도록 애써 보겠노라 말했다. 다만 자기 운명의 유별난 가혹함에 대해서는 조금 한탄하지 않을 수 없노라고 했다. 그토록 큰 재앙의 소식이 불시에 자기에게 닥쳐왔으며, 그것도 운명의 악의로부터 감당할 수 있는 가장 모진 일격을 시시각각 기다리던 바로 그 시점에 그리되었다는 것이었다. 그는 말하기를, 지금 이 일이야말로 자기가 스왜컴 씨와 스퀘어 씨에게서 배운 그 훌륭한 기초 가르침들을 시험대에 올리게 될 것이며, 만일 자기가 이런 불행들을 견뎌 낼 수 있다면 그것은 전적으로 그 가르침 덕분일 것이라고 했다.\n\n이제 올워디 씨에게 누이의 죽음을 알려야 할지 말지를 두고 의논이 벌어졌다. 의사는 이를 격렬히 반대했는데, 내 생각으로는 의학계 전체가 그와 뜻을 같이했을 것이다. 그러나 블리필 씨는, 어떤 비밀이든 그것이 줄지도 모를 불안을 염려하여 결코 자기에게 숨기지 말라는 분부를 숙부로부터 그토록 단호하고 거듭 받아 왔으므로, 그 결과가 어떠하든 감히 거역할 생각은 못 하겠노라 말했다. 그는 말하기를, 자기로서는 숙부의 신앙심 깊고 철학적인 기질을 헤아릴 때, 의사의 그 염려에 동의할 수 없다고 했다. 그리하여 그는 그것을 숙부에게 전하기로 결심했다. 만일 숙부가 회복하신다면(그는 그리되시기를 진심으로 기도했다) 이런 종류의 비밀을 자기에게 숨기려 한 일을 결코 용서치 않으시리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다.\n\n의사는 이 결심에 따를 수밖에 없었고, 다른 두 학식 있는 신사는 그 결심을 매우 높이 칭찬했다. 그리하여 블리필 씨와 의사는 함께 병실 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼고, 거기서 의사가 먼저 들어가 침상에 다가가 환자의 맥을 짚었다. 맥을 짚자마자 그는 환자가 훨씬 나아졌노라 선언했다. 마지막 처방이 기적처럼 효험을 보여 열이 한풀 꺾였으니, 앞서 그가 희망이 있다고 본 만큼이나 이제는 위험이 거의 없어 보인다고 말했다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 올워디 씨의 상태는 의사의 그 대단한 신중함이 묘사했던 만큼 나빴던 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그러나 지혜로운 장수가 적의 병력이 아무리 보잘것없을지라도 결코 그 적을 얕보지 않듯이, 지혜로운 의사 또한 병세가 아무리 대수롭지 않을지라도 결코 그 병을 얕보지 않는 법이다. 앞의 장수가 적이 아무리 약하더라도 똑같이 엄격한 군기를 유지하고, 똑같은 경계병을 세우며, 똑같은 정찰병을 부리듯이, 뒤의 의사는 병세가 아무리 하찮을지라도 똑같이 무거운 표정을 짓고, 똑같이 의미심장한 태도로 고개를 가로젓는다. 그리고 두 사람은, 그 밖의 여러 그럴듯한 이유들 가운데서도 자기네 처신에 대해 이 단단한 이유 하나를 댈 수 있다. 곧, 이런 방식으로 하면 승리를 거둘 경우 더 큰 영광이 자기에게 돌아오고, 어쩌다 불운한 사고로 패하게 되더라도 망신은 덜하다는 것이다.\n\n올워디 씨는 눈을 떠 회복의 희망을 두고 하늘에 감사를 올리기가 무섭게, 블리필 씨가 몹시 풀죽은 낯빛으로 가까이 다가와, 눈물을 닦으려는 것인지 아니면 오비디우스가 다른 자리에서 어디선가 표현한 대로 하려는 것인지, 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다.\n\nSi nullus erit, tamen excute nullum,\n\n설령 눈물이 없을지라도, 그 없는 눈물을 닦아 내라,\n\n그러고는 독자가 바로 앞서 알게 된 그 일을 숙부에게 전했다.\n\n올워디는 그 소식을 근심과 인내와 체념으로 받아들였다. 그는 부드러운 눈물 한 방울을 떨구고는, 곧 낯빛을 가다듬고, 마침내 외쳤다. “모든 일에 주님의 뜻이 이루어지이다.”\n\n이제 그는 전령을 찾았으나, 블리필은 그를 한순간도 붙들어 둘 수가 없었노라 말했다. 그가 어찌나 몹시 서두르던지 무슨 중대한 볼일을 손에 쥐고 있는 듯 보였다는 것이었다. 그는 정신없이 쫓기고 내몰려 죽을 지경이라 투덜대며, 자기 몸을 네 조각으로 나눌 수만 있다면 그 하나하나를 어디다 써야 할지 안다고 여러 번 되뇌었다고 했다.\n\n그러자 올워디는 블리필에게 장례를 맡아 달라 부탁했다. 그는 누이를 자기 집안 예배당에 안치하고 싶다고 말했고, 세부적인 일들은 블리필의 재량에 맡기되, 다만 이 일에 부려 쓰고 싶은 사람만을 따로 일러두었다.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\n이 장은 여러 가지 가운데서도, “술취함은 거울이 사람의 모습을 비추듯 그 마음을 드러낸다”는 아이스키네스의 말에 대한 주석으로 읽힐 수 있을 것이다.\n\n독자는 지난 장에서 존스 씨에 관한 이야기가 전혀 없었던 것을 의아해할지도 모른다. 사실 그의 행동은 거기 언급된 사람들의 그것과 너무도 달라서, 우리는 그의 이름을 그들의 이름과 뒤섞고 싶지 않았다.\n\n그 선량한 사람이 연설을 마쳤을 때, 그 방을 가장 마지막으로 떠난 이는 존스였다. 그는 거기서 자기 방으로 물러나 마음속 근심을 풀어내려 했다. 그러나 마음의 들끓음이 그를 오래 그곳에 머물게 두지 않았다. 그래서 그는 살그머니 올워디의 침실 문 앞으로 미끄러져 가서, 안에서 어떤 기척도 들리지 않는 가운데 한참을 귀 기울였는데, 다만 사나운 코 고는 소리만은 들렸고, 끝내 그의 두려움은 그것을 신음 소리로 잘못 알아들었다. 이것이 그를 어찌나 놀라게 했던지, 그는 방으로 들어가지 않을 수 없었다. 거기서 그는 선량한 사람이 침대에 누워 달콤하고 평온한 잠에 빠져 있는 것을, 그리고 그를 돌보는 간병인이 침대 발치에서 앞서 말한 그 우렁찬 방식으로 코를 골고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 곧바로 이 깊은 저음을 잠재울 유일한 방법을 택했다. 그 소리가 올워디 씨의 잠을 깨우지 않을까 두려웠기 때문이다. 그러고는 간병인 곁에 앉아 꼼짝도 하지 않고 있었는데, 마침내 블리필과 의사가 함께 들어와 환자를 깨웠다. 의사가 맥을 짚기 위해서였고, 다른 한 사람은 환자에게 그 소식을 전하기 위해서였는데, 만일 존스가 그것을 미리 알았더라면, 그 소식은 이런 때에 올워디 씨의 귀에 닿는 데 큰 어려움을 겪었을 것이다.\n\n블리필이 삼촌에게 그 이야기를 하는 것을 처음 들었을 때, 존스는 상대의 분별없음에 끓어오르는 분노를 좀처럼 참기 어려웠다. 더구나 의사가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 그 일을 환자에게 언급하는 것을 꺼린다고 말한 터라 더욱 그러했다. 그러나 그의 격정이 이성을 모두 앗아갈 만큼은 아니어서, 블리필을 향한 어떤 격렬한 표현이든 환자에게 미칠 결과가 그의 눈앞에 가려지지는 않았다. 이 염려가 당장은 그의 분노를 가라앉혔고, 그 뒤로 그 소식이 사실 아무런 해를 끼치지 않았음을 알게 되자 그는 무척 흡족해져서, 자신의 분노가 블리필에게 한마디도 내비치지 않은 채 제 가슴속에서 사그라지도록 내버려두었다.\n\n그 의사는 그날 올워디 씨 댁에서 식사를 했다. 식사 후 환자를 살펴본 뒤 그는 다시 좌중으로 돌아와, 이제 자신의 환자가 모든 위험에서 벗어났음을 확신을 가지고 말할 수 있게 되어 만족스럽다고 전했다. 그는 환자의 열을 완전한 소강 상태로 끌어내렸으며, 키니네 껍질을 처방해 두면 그 재발을 막을 수 있으리라 의심치 않는다고 했다.\n\n이 소식은 존스를 어찌나 기쁘게 했던지, 그를 도를 넘는 황홀경에 빠뜨려, 그가 정말로 기쁨에 취했다고 말해도 좋을 정도였다. 이러한 도취는 술의 효과를 크게 부추기는 법이다. 게다가 이번 자리에서 그는 술병을 아주 거리낌 없이 다루었으니 (의사의 건강을 위해, 또 다른 여러 축배를 위해 가득 채운 잔을 여러 차례 비웠으므로), 그는 머지않아 말 그대로 취하고 말았다.\n\n존스는 천성적으로 격렬한 혈기를 지니고 있었다. 이것이 술기운에 둥둥 떠올라 더욱 부풀어 오르니, 더없이 터무니없는 결과를 빚어냈다. 그는 의사에게 입을 맞추고, 더없이 열렬한 애정 표현으로 그를 끌어안으며, 올워디 씨 본인 다음으로 살아 있는 모든 사람 가운데 그를 가장 사랑한다고 맹세했다. “선생,” 그가 덧붙였다. “선생은 공금을 들여서라도 동상을 세워 마땅한 분이오. 한 사람을 살려냈으니 말이오. 그분은 그를 아는 모든 선한 이들의 사랑을 받는 분일 뿐 아니라, 사회의 축복이요, 조국의 영광이요, 인류의 명예이기도 하오. 내가 그분을 내 영혼보다 더 사랑하지 않는다면 빌어먹을 놈이라 불러도 좋소.”\n\n“그건 자네에게 더욱 수치스러운 일이로군.” 스왜컴이 소리쳤다. “하기야 자네가 그분을 사랑할 만한 이유는 있다고 생각하네. 그분이 자네를 위해 아주 잘 마련해 두셨으니 말이야. 그리고 어쩌면 어떤 사람들에게는, 그분이 그 선물을 거두실 정당한 이유를 보실 때까지 살아 계시지 않는 편이 더 나았을지도 모르지.”\n\n존스는 이제 형언할 수 없는 경멸의 눈으로 스왜컴을 바라보며 대답했다. “그래, 자네의 비천한 영혼은 그따위 따짐이 내게 조금이라도 무게가 나가리라 상상하는가? 천만에. 차라리 땅이 열려 제 흙을 집어삼키게 하라 (내게 수백만 에이커가 있다 해도 나는 그렇게 말하겠네). 내 소중하고 영광스러운 벗을 집어삼키느니 말일세.”\n\n그토록 소중한 분을 향한 그리움에 어찌 부끄러움이나 절도가 있으랴?\n\n[*] “그토록 소중한 벗을 향한 우리의 갈망에 어떤 절제나 한도가 한계를 지을 수 있으랴?” 여기 쓰인 desiderium이라는 말은 쉽게 옮기기 어렵다. 그것은 우리의 벗을 다시 누리고자 하는 갈망과, 그 갈망에 따르는 슬픔을 함께 담고 있다.\n\n이제 의사가 끼어들어, 존스와 스왜컴 사이에 타오르던 분노의 결과를 막았다. 그 후 존스는 흥겨움에 몸을 맡겨 사랑 노래 두세 곡을 부르고, 고삐 풀린 기쁨이 으레 불러일으키는 온갖 광란의 무질서에 빠져들었다. 그러나 다툴 기색은 조금도 없었으니, 가능하다면 술 깬 때보다 열 배는 더 기분이 좋았다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 술에 취하면 심술궂고 시비를 거는 사람이 술이 깨면 매우 훌륭한 사람이라는 흔한 통념만큼 잘못된 것도 없다. 술은 실상 사람의 본성을 뒤집거나, 전에 없던 격정을 그에게서 새로 만들어내는 것이 아니기 때문이다. 술은 이성의 파수병을 거두어가며, 따라서 많은 이들이 멀쩡할 때는 능숙하게 감추던 그 징후들을 우리로 하여금 드러내게 만든다. 술은 우리의 격정을(대개는 우리 마음속에서 가장 윗자리를 차지한 격정을) 부추기고 불타오르게 하니, 화 잘 내는 기질, 정 많은 기질, 너그러운 기질, 쾌활한 기질, 인색한 기질, 그 밖의 온갖 사람의 성향들이 술잔 속에서 한껏 부풀려지고 까발려지는 것이다.\n\n그렇지만 어떤 나라도 영국만큼 술취한 다툼을, 특히 하층민들 사이에서 많이 빚어내지 않는다 해서 (사실 그들에게 함께 마시는 것과 함께 싸우는 것은 거의 같은 뜻의 말이다), 거기서 영국인이 세상에서 가장 심성 고약한 사람들이라는 결론을 끌어내고 싶지는 않다. 어쩌면 그 바탕에는 오직 명예에 대한 사랑이 깔려 있을지도 모른다. 그러니 공정한 결론은, 우리 동포가 그 어떤 다른 평민들보다도 그 사랑을, 그리고 더 많은 용맹을 지니고 있다는 것이리라. 더욱이 그러한 자리에서 너그럽지 못하거나 부당하거나 심술궂은 짓이 행해지는 일은 드물다는 점이 이를 뒷받침한다. 아니, 싸움이 벌어지는 그 와중에도 서로에게 호의를 내비치는 것이 그들에게는 흔한 일이다. 또한 그들의 취한 흥이 대개 한바탕 싸움으로 끝나듯, 그들의 싸움도 대개는 우정으로 끝나는 법이다.\n\n그러나 우리의 이야기로 돌아가자. 존스가 모욕을 줄 의도를 전혀 보이지 않았는데도, 블리필 씨는 자기 기질의 차분하고 신중한 절제와 너무도 어긋나는 그 행동에 몹시 기분이 상했다. 그는 그것을 한층 더 참기 어려워했는데, 그에게는 그것이 이런 때에 매우 무례하게 비쳤기 때문이다. 그가 말하기를, “지금 이 집은 사랑하는 어머니 때문에 상중에 있는 집이거늘, 만일 하늘이 기뻐하시어 올워디 씨의 회복에 어떤 희망을 주셨다면, 우리가 마음의 환희를 표하는 데에는 술취함과 소란보다 감사 기도가 더 어울리는 법이오. 술취함과 소란은 하늘의 진노를 돌리기보다 오히려 키우는 방법에 더 가깝소.” 존스보다 더 많은 술을 들이켰으나 머리에는 아무 해도 없던 스왜컴이 블리필의 경건한 일장 연설을 거들었다. 그러나 스퀘어는, 독자가 아마 짐작할 만한 이유로, 완전히 입을 다물고 있었다.\n\n술이 존스를 완전히 압도한 것은 아니어서, 블리필 씨의 상실이 언급되는 순간 그는 그것을 떠올렸다. 그래서, 자신의 잘못을 인정하고 책망하는 데 그보다 더 선뜻한 사람도 없었기에, 그는 블리필 씨에게 악수를 청하며 용서를 빌었다. “올워디 씨의 회복에 대한 지나친 기쁨이 내 마음에서 다른 모든 생각을 몰아내고 말았소.” 그가 말했다.\n\n블리필은 경멸하듯 그의 손을 뿌리치고, 분노에 차서 대답했다. “비극적인 광경이 눈먼 자에게 아무런 인상을 남기지 않는다 해도 그리 놀랄 일은 아니지. 하지만 나로 말하자면, 내 부모가 누구인지 아는 불행을 지녔으니, 그분들을 잃은 슬픔에 마음이 흔들리는 것이 당연하오.”\n\n존스는 비록 쾌활한 기질이긴 했어도 그 천성에 다소 노여움을 잘 타는 면이 섞여 있던 터라, 황급히 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 블리필의 멱살을 움켜쥐며 소리쳤다. “이 악당 놈, 빌어먹을, 내 출생의 불행으로 나를 모욕하려는 거냐?” 그는 이 말에 어찌나 거친 행동을 곁들였던지, 그것이 이내 블리필 씨의 평화로운 기질마저 압도하고 말았다. 곧바로 한바탕 몸싸움이 벌어졌고, 만일 스왜컴과 의사가 끼어들어 말리지 않았다면 사달이 났을 것이다. 스퀘어로 말하자면, 그의 철학이 그를 모든 감정 위에 올려놓은지라, 그는 모든 난투극에서 그러하듯 매우 침착하게 파이프 담배를 피우고 있었다. 다만 입에 물린 파이프가 부러질 위험이 있다고 느껴질 때만은 예외였다.\n\n이제 서로에게 당장의 복수를 실행하지 못하게 된 두 싸움꾼은 좌절된 분노가 으레 기대는 수단으로 돌아서서, 위협과 도전으로 화를 풀어냈다. 이런 종류의 다툼에서는, 몸으로 맞붙었을 때 존스 쪽으로 기우는 듯하던 운수가 이제는 그의 적에게 못지않게 우호적이었다.\n\n그렇지만 결국 중립을 지킨 이들의 중재로 휴전이 합의되었고, 좌중은 다시 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 거기서 존스는 용서를 구하도록, 블리필은 그것을 받아들이도록 설득되어, 평화가 회복되었고 모든 것이 본래의 상태로 돌아간 듯 보였다.\n\n그러나 다툼은 겉보기에 완전히 화해되었으되, 그 다툼으로 끊겼던 흥겨움은 결코 되살아나지 않았다. 모든 즐거움이 이제 끝나버렸고, 뒤이은 대화는 다만 사실에 관한 진지한 이야기와, 그에 못지않게 진지한 논평으로만 채워졌다. 이런 종류의 대화에는 품위와 교훈은 많을지언정 재미는 거의 없는 법이다. 그러므로 우리는 그 마지막 것만을 독자에게 전하려 하니, 좌중의 나머지 사람들이 하나둘 자리를 뜨고 마침내 스퀘어와 의사만 남을 때까지 오간 말은 모두 건너뛰겠다. 그때쯤 두 젊은이 사이에 벌어진 일에 대한 몇 마디 논평으로 대화가 조금 달아올랐는데, 의사는 두 사람 다 악당에 지나지 않는다고 단언했고, 그 호칭에 철학자도 자못 현명하게 고개를 끄덕이며 동의했다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n오비디우스를 비롯해 더 근엄한 여러 저술가들의 관찰이 참되다는 것을 보여 주는 장. 그들은 술이 흔히 음란함의 앞잡이가 된다는 사실을, 반박할 여지 없이 증명해 보였다.\n\n존스는 우리가 앞서 그가 함께 있는 모습을 보았던 무리에서 빠져나와 들판으로 나갔다. 올워디 씨를 모시러 가기 전에 탁 트인 바깥 공기 속을 거닐며 머리를 식힐 작정이었다. 거기서 그는, 벗이자 은인의 위태로운 병환으로 한동안 끊겨 있던, 사랑하는 소피아에 대한 사색을 다시 이어 갔다. 그런데 그때 한 사건이 일어났다. 우리는 슬픔에 잠겨 이를 전하며, 읽는 이들 또한 분명 슬픔에 잠겨 읽게 될 것이다. 그러나 우리가 그토록 어길 수 없이 떠받든다고 공언한 역사적 진실이, 이 일을 후세에 전하도록 우리를 떠밀고 있다.\n\n때는 6월 말의 어느 흐뭇한 저녁이었고, 우리의 주인공은 더없이 감미로운 숲속을 거닐고 있었다. 그곳에서는 부드러운 산들바람이 나뭇잎을 어루만지고, 졸졸 흐르는 시냇물의 달콤한 떨림과 밤꾀꼬리의 가락 고운 노랫소리가 한데 어우러져 더없이 황홀한 화음을 이루고 있었다. 사랑에 그토록 달콤하게 어울리는 이 정경 속에서 그는 사랑하는 소피아를 떠올렸다. 그의 들뜬 공상이 그녀의 온갖 아름다움 위를 거침없이 떠돌고, 그의 생생한 상상이 그 매혹적인 처녀를 갖가지 황홀한 모습으로 그려 내자, 그의 뜨거운 가슴은 애틋함으로 녹아내렸다. 마침내 그는 잔잔히 속삭이는 시냇가에 몸을 던지듯 누워, 이렇게 마음을 토해 냈다.\n\n\"오, 소피아여, 하늘이 그대를 내 품에 안겨 준다면 내 처지가 얼마나 축복받은 것이랴! 우리 사이에 거리를 둔 저 운명에 저주가 있을지어다. 그대만 내 것이 된다면, 누더기 한 벌이 그대 재산의 전부라 한들, 이 세상에 내가 부러워할 사람이 어디 있겠는가! 인도의 온갖 보석으로 치장한 가장 빛나는 키르카시아의 미인인들 내 눈엔 얼마나 하찮아 보이랴! 한데 내가 어찌 다른 여인을 입에 올리는가? 이 눈이 다른 누군가를 애틋한 눈길로 바라볼 수 있다 여겨진다면, 이 손으로 그 눈을 머리에서 뽑아 버리리라. 아니, 나의 소피아여, 모진 운명이 우리를 영영 갈라놓더라도, 내 영혼은 오직 그대만을 흠모하리라. 그대의 모습에 가장 순결한 절개를 길이 지키리라. 내 비록 그대의 매혹적인 몸을 결코 차지하지 못할지라도, 그대만은 여전히 나의 생각과 사랑과 영혼을 차지하리라. 오! 나의 애틋한 가슴은 그 부드러운 품에 어찌나 휘감겨 있는지, 가장 빛나는 미인들도 내겐 아무 매력이 없고, 그들의 품에 안긴 은수자라도 나보다 더 차갑지는 않으리라. 소피아, 오직 소피아만이 내 것이 되리라. 그 이름 속에 깃든 황홀함이여! 나는 그 이름을 나무란 나무마다 새기리라.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 벌떡 일어나, 바라보았다. 그러나 그것은 그의 소피아가 아니었다. 아니, 술탄의 후궁을 위해 화려하고 우아하게 차려입은 키르카시아 처녀도 아니었다. 천만에. 겉옷도 걸치지 않은 채, 다소 거칠고 그다지 깨끗하지도 않은 속옷 차림에, 게다가 하루 일의 산물인 향긋하달 수 없는 냄새까지 풍기며, 손에 쇠스랑을 든 몰리 시그림이 다가오고 있었다. 우리의 주인공은 앞서 말한 대로 나무껍질에 이름을 새기려고 펼쳐 둔 주머니칼을 손에 쥐고 있었는데, 그 처녀가 가까이 다가오며 미소 띤 얼굴로 소리쳤다. \"도련님, 설마 절 죽이실 작정은 아니시겠죠!\" \"내가 왜 너를 죽일 거라 생각하느냐?\" 존스가 대답했다. \"원,\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"지난번에 절 만나셨을 때 그토록 모질게 대하셨으니, 절 죽여 주시는 거라면 어쩌면 제가 바라기엔 너무 큰 친절이겠죠.\"\n\n여기서 한바탕 옥신각신이 이어졌는데, 굳이 그것을 전할 의무가 있다고 여기지 않으므로 나는 생략하겠다. 그것이 꼬박 십오 분이나 이어졌고, 그 끝에 두 사람이 숲에서 가장 우거진 곳으로 물러갔다는 것만으로 충분하다.\n\n내 독자 가운데 더러는 이 사건이 부자연스럽다고 여기고픈 마음이 들지도 모르겠다. 그러나 사실은 사실이다. 그리고 어쩌면 이렇게 짚어 주면 충분히 설명이 될 것이다. 존스는 아마 여자가 하나라도 있는 편이 아예 없는 것보다 낫다고 여겼을 테고, 몰리 또한 사내가 하나보다는 둘인 편이 낫다고 짐작했을 것이라고. 앞서 존스의 이번 행동에 부여한 동기 외에도, 독자께서는 그에게 유리하게 다음을 떠올려 주시면 기쁘겠다. 그는 이때 그 놀라운 이성의 힘을 온전히 다스리는 처지가 아니었다는 것을. 근엄하고 지혜로운 사람들이 그 힘 덕분에 다스릴 길 없는 정념을 잘도 억누르고, 이런 금지된 즐길 거리들을 마다하게 되는, 바로 그 힘 말이다. 이제 술이 존스의 그 힘을 완전히 꺾어 버렸다. 그는 실로, 이성이 끼어든다 한들, 그저 충고만 하려 들더라도, 옛날 클레오스트라투스가 어느 어리석은 자에게 돌려준 그 대답을 받기 십상인 처지였다. 그 어리석은 자가 그에게 술 취한 게 부끄럽지 않으냐고 묻자, 클레오스트라투스는 이렇게 말했다. \"그대는 술 취한 사람에게 훈계하는 게 부끄럽지 않은가?\" 사실대로 말하자면, 법정에서야 만취가 변명이 되어서는 안 되겠으나, 양심의 법정에서는 크게 변명이 된다. 그래서 아리스토텔레스는 피타쿠스의 법, 곧 술 취한 자가 저지른 죄에 갑절의 벌을 내리던 그 법을 칭찬하면서도, 그 법에는 정의보다 정략이 더 많이 담겼음을 인정한다. 자, 만취 때문에 용서받을 만한 잘못이 있다면, 그것은 분명 지금 존스 씨가 저지른 것과 같은 종류의 잘못이다. 이 대목을 두고 나는 방대한 학식을 한껏 쏟아부을 수도 있겠으나, 그것이 내 독자를 즐겁게 하거나 그가 이미 아는 것 이상을 가르쳐 주리라 여겨지지 않는다. 그러니 독자를 위해 나는 내 학식을 혼자 간직하고, 다시 이야기로 돌아가겠다.\n\n운명의 여신은 좀처럼 일을 어중간하게 하는 법이 없다는 말이 있다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀가 누군가를 흡족하게 하거나 언짢게 하기로 마음먹으면 그 변덕에는 끝이 없다. 우리의 주인공이 자신의 디도와 함께 물러나기가 무섭게,\n\n같은 동굴로 블리필 대장과 거룩한 점쟁이가\n이르렀으니—\n\n진지하게 산책을 하던 목사와 젊은 도련님이 숲으로 통하는 층계 울짱에 다다랐고, 그 가운데 도련님이 연인들이 막 시야에서 사라지려는 바로 그 순간 그들의 모습을 흘끗 보았던 것이다.\n\n블리필은 백 야드 넘게 떨어져 있었음에도 존스를 아주 잘 알아보았고, 그 동행이 어느 성별인지도, 비록 누구인지까지는 아니어도, 그만큼 확신했다. 그는 흠칫 놀라 성호를 긋고는, 자못 엄숙하게 한마디를 내뱉었다.\n\n스웨컴은 이 느닷없는 동요에 다소 놀라며 그 까닭을 물었다. 블리필은 이렇게 대답했다. \"사내 하나와 계집 하나가 함께 덤불 속으로 물러가는 걸 분명히 보았는데, 무슨 못된 속셈이 있는 게 틀림없습니다.\" 다만 존스의 이름은 감추는 편이 낫다고 여겼는데, 그가 왜 그랬는지는 명민한 독자의 판단에 맡겨 두는 수밖에 없다. 우리가 틀릴 가능성이 조금이라도 있을 때에는 결코 사람들의 행동에 동기를 함부로 갖다 붙이지 않기로 했으니 말이다.\n\n목사는 자신이 몸가짐에서 엄격히 정결할 뿐 아니라 다른 모든 이의 그 반대 악덕에도 큰 적이었던 터라, 이 소식에 발끈했다. 그는 블리필 씨에게 당장 그곳으로 안내하라 청했고, 다가가는 내내 한탄을 섞어 복수의 숨결을 토해 냈다. 또한 올워디 씨를 향해 비스듬한 비난을 던지는 것도 마다하지 않았다. 이 고장의 사악함은 주로 그가 사생아에게 그토록 친절을 베풀고, 헤픈 계집들에게 아주 혹독한 벌을 매기는 그 정당하고 유익한 법의 엄정함을 누그러뜨려, 악덕을 부추긴 탓이라고 넌지시 빗대었다.\n\n우리의 사냥꾼들이 사냥감을 쫓아 지나가야 할 길은 가시덤불이 어찌나 무성하던지 걸음을 크게 가로막았고, 게다가 어찌나 부스럭 소리를 냈던지, 존스는 그들이 그를 덮치기도 전에 충분히 그 도착을 미리 알아챘다. 아니, 실로 스웨컴은 자신의 분노를 감추는 데 어찌나 서툴렀던지, 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 어찌나 복수를 중얼거렸던지, 이것만으로도 존스는 (사냥꾼들의 말투를 빌리자면) 자신이 앉아 있는 채로 발각되었음을 충분히 알아챘을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\n파이프 씨의 시 한 구절에 나오는 비유가 강철이나 차디찬 쇠붙이의 도움 없이 벌어질 수 있는 가장 피비린내 나는 전투를 끌어들이는 장.\n\n발정기에는—이 거친 말은 햄프셔의 우거진[*] 숲에서 야성을 지닌 연인들 사이에 오가는 그 점잖은 희롱을 두고 속된 무리가 쓰는 표현이다—만일 높다란 뿔을 곤두세운 수사슴이 그 사랑의 유희를 궁리하는 동안, 강아지 두 마리나 그 밖에 적의를 품은 어떤 짐승이 야성의 비너스 신전 가까이 어슬렁거려, 아름다운 암사슴이 자연이 모든 암컷에게 입혀 두었거나 적어도 어떻게 두를지 일러 둔 그 무엇—두려움이든 장난기든, 까다로움이든 변덕이든—에 닿아 그 자리에서 움찔 물러난다면, 그것은 수컷들의 무례함으로 인해 사메의 신비가 신성치 못한 눈에 엿보이지나 않을까 함이다. 이 의식을 거행할 때, 여사제는 베르길리우스에 나오는 그녀와 더불어 (그 시인은 아마 그때 그런 거행에 한창 몰두해 있었으리라) 이렇게 외치기 때문이다.\n* 사메의 신비: 고대 밀교의 비의(秘儀)를 가리킨다.\n\n—멀리, 오 멀리 물러가라, 부정한 자들이여,\n무녀가 외치니, 온 숲에서 떠나라.\n\n—부정한 영혼들아 여기서 멀리 떠나라,\n무녀가 외쳤으니, 그 숲에서 물러나라.—드라이든.\n\n[*] 이것은 뜻이 두 갈래인 표현으로, 나무로 잘 뒤덮인 숲을 뜻할 수도 있고, 나무가 말끔히 베어져 헐벗은 숲을 뜻할 수도 있다.\n\n내 말인즉, 모든 살아 있는 것에 공통된 이 신성한 의식이 수사슴과 그 애인 사이에서 한창 무르익는 동안, 어떤 적대적인 짐승이 너무 가까이 다가오는 모험을 감행한다면, 겁먹은 암사슴이 처음 신호를 보내기가 무섭게 수사슴은 사납고 무시무시하게 덤불 어귀로 뛰쳐나간다. 거기서 그는 제 사랑을 지키는 보초로 서서, 발로 땅을 구르고, 뿔을 허공 높이 휘두르며, 다가오는 적에게 당당히 싸움을 걸어 도발하는 것이다.\n\n이처럼, 아니 그보다 더 무섭게, 적이 다가오는 것을 알아챈 우리의 주인공은 펄쩍 뛰쳐나왔다. 그는 떨고 있는 암사슴을 감추고, 될 수 있다면 그녀가 물러날 길을 지켜 주려고 앞으로 여러 걸음 나아갔다. 그러자 스왜컴은 먼저 그 불타는 두 눈에서 시퍼런 번갯불을 쏘아 보내더니, 천둥처럼 호통치기 시작했다. \"이런 망측한! 망측한 일이로다! 존스 군. 자네가 정말 그 장본인일 수 있단 말인가?\"—\"보시다시피,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"제가 여기 있을 수도 있는 일이지요.\"—\"그리고 자네와 함께 있는 저 고약한 계집은 누구냐?\" 스왜컴이 말했다.—\"제게 무슨 고약한 계집이 있다 한들,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그게 누구인지 선생님께 알려 드리지 않을 수도 있지요.\"—\"당장 말하라고 명한다,\" 스왜컴이 말했다. \"그리고 젊은이, 자네 나이가 비록 가르침의 목적을 다소 줄여 놓았다 해서, 스승의 권위마저 송두리째 앗아 갔다고는 생각지 말게. 스승과 제자의 관계는 지워지지 않는 것이야. 실로 다른 모든 관계가 그러하듯이 말일세. 그 모든 관계는 하늘에서 그 근원을 받아 왔으니까. 그러니 자네는 내가 처음 자네에게 기초를 가르치던 때와 똑같이, 지금도 나에게 복종할 의무가 있다고 여겨야 할 게야.\"—\"그러시겠지요,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"하지만 저를 납득시킬 그 자작나무 회초리의 논거를 똑같이 갖추지 않으신 이상, 그런 일은 없을 겁니다.\"—\"그렇다면 분명히 말해 두겠네,\" 스왜컴이 말했다. \"나는 그 고약한 년의 정체를 기어이 밝혀내고야 말겠다.\"—\"그럼 저도 분명히 말씀드리지요,\" 존스가 받아쳤다. \"저는 기어이 그러지 못하게 하겠습니다.\" 그러자 스왜컴이 앞으로 나아가려 했고, 존스는 그의 팔을 붙잡았다. 블리필이 이를 떼어 놓으려 애쓰며, \"내 옛 스승이 모욕당하는 꼴은 두고 볼 수 없다\"고 외쳤다.\n\n이제 둘을 한꺼번에 상대하게 된 존스는, 가능한 한 빨리 적 가운데 하나를 처치하는 것이 필요하겠다고 판단했다. 그래서 그는 먼저 가장 약한 쪽을 공략하기로 하고, 목사를 놓아 준 다음 젊은 지주의 가슴을 향해 한 방을 날렸다. 그 일격이 운 좋게 제대로 들어가, 블리필은 땅바닥에 제 키만큼 길게 뻗고 말았다.\n\n스왜컴은 정체를 밝히는 데 어찌나 골몰해 있었던지, 자유로워졌다 싶은 순간 그새 제 친구에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지는 별로 생각지도 않고 곧장 양치식물 덤불 속으로 발을 들였다. 그러나 그가 덤불 안으로 몇 걸음 채 나아가기도 전에, 블리필을 쓰러뜨린 존스가 목사를 따라잡아 그의 외투 자락을 잡고 뒤로 끌어당겼다.\n\n이 목사는 젊은 시절에 한가락 하던 투사로, 학교에서나 대학에서나 주먹으로 적잖은 명예를 얻은 사람이었다. 물론 이제는 오랜 세월 그 고귀한 기술의 연마를 게을리해 왔다. 그래도 그의 용기는 그의 믿음만큼이나 굳셌고, 그의 몸뚱이도 그 둘 못지않게 단단했다. 게다가 독자도 이미 짐작했을 테지만, 그는 성미가 다소 발끈하는 편이었다. 그러니 그가 뒤를 돌아보아 친구가 땅바닥에 뻗어 있는 것을 보고, 동시에 자기가 예전 둘 사이의 모든 다툼에서 늘 당하기만 하던 자에게 이토록 거칠게 다루어지고 있음을 깨달았을 때 (이 사정이 사태 전체를 한층 더 부아 나게 했다), 마침내 그의 인내심도 무너지고 말았다. 그는 공격 자세를 취하더니, 온 힘을 그러모아 예전에 뒤에서 존스를 덮쳤던 그 기세 그대로 이번에는 정면에서 존스에게 달려들었다.\n\n우리의 주인공은 더없이 의연한 담대함으로 적의 공격을 받아 냈고, 그 일격에 그의 가슴이 쿵 하고 울렸다. 그는 즉시 못지않은 사나움으로 되갚으며 역시 목사의 가슴을 노렸지만, 목사가 능숙하게 존스의 주먹을 아래로 쳐 내는 바람에 주먹은 그의 배에만 닿고 말았다. 그 배에는 그때 쇠고기 두 파운드와 푸딩 두 파운드가 들어앉아 있던 터라, 당연히 빈 소리 따위는 울려 나올 리 없었다. 양쪽에서 우람한 주먹질이 숱하게 오갔으니, 그것은 읽거나 묘사하기보다 직접 보는 편이 훨씬 흥겹고 또한 수월한 광경이었다. 마침내 존스가 무릎을 스왜컴의 가슴팍에 처박는 격렬한 한판이 벌어져 목사를 어찌나 약하게 만들었던지, 이제 막 기운을 되찾은 블리필이 다시 싸움에 끼어들어 존스와 맞붙어 줌으로써 목사에게 귀를 털고 숨을 고를 잠깐의 틈을 주지 않았더라면, 승부는 더 이상 의심할 여지가 없었을 것이다.\n\n이제 두 사람이 한꺼번에 우리의 주인공을 공격해 왔는데, 스왜컴과의 싸움으로 어찌나 지쳐 있었던지 존스의 주먹은 처음 내리꽂힐 때의 그 힘을 더는 간직하지 못했다. 비록 이 훈장은 인간이라는 악기로 독주를 켜는 쪽을 더 즐겨, 근래에는 오직 그것에만 익숙해져 있었으나, 그래도 이중주에서 자기 몫을 썩 잘 해낼 만큼의 옛 솜씨는 여태 지니고 있었던 것이다.\n\n요즘 풍습대로 승부는 머릿수로 결판이 날 듯하던 차에, 별안간 네 번째 주먹 한 쌍이 싸움판에 나타나 곧장 목사에게 인사를 차렸다. 그 주먹의 임자는 그와 동시에 이렇게 고함쳤다. \"둘이서 하나한테 달려들다니, 이 빌어먹을 놈들, 부끄럽지도 않으냐?\"\n\n이제 그 싸움은—구별을 위해 난전(亂戰)이라 부르는 종류였는데—몇 분 동안 더없이 격렬하게 타올랐다. 그러다 블리필이 두 번째로 존스에게 나가떨어지자, 스왜컴은 자기 새 적수에게 항복을 청하기에 이르렀다. 그 적수가 다름 아닌 웨스턴 씨 본인임이 그제야 밝혀졌으니, 격렬한 난투 와중에 어느 싸움꾼도 그를 알아보지 못했던 것이다.\n\n사실인즉, 이 정직한 지주는 그날 오후 몇몇 일행과 산책을 하다가 마침 그 피비린내 나는 싸움이 벌어진 들판을 지나게 되었는데, 세 사람이 엉겨 붙어 있는 것을 보고는 그중 둘은 한편일 것이라고 단정 짓고는, 일행에게서 부리나케 달려와 분별보다는 객기로 더 약한 쪽 편을 들었던 것이다. 이 너그러운 행동 덕분에 그는 십중팔구 존스가 스왜컴의 분노와, 블리필이 옛 스승에게 품은 그 경건한 우정의 제물이 되는 것을 막아 준 셈이었다. 그런 수적 열세의 불리함은 차치하고라도, 존스는 부러졌던 팔의 예전 힘을 아직 충분히 되찾지 못한 터였기 때문이다. 어쨌든 이 원군이 가세하자 싸움은 이내 끝이 났고, 존스는 자기 동맹과 더불어 승리를 거두었다.\n\n# Chapter xii.\n\n써그럼과 블리필을 비롯해 그런 무리 스무 명의 몸속에 흐르는 피를 다 합친 것보다도 더 가슴을 뭉클하게 하는 광경이 펼쳐지는 장.\n\n이제 웨스턴 씨 일행의 나머지 사람들이 막 도착했는데, 마침 싸움이 끝나는 바로 그 순간이었다. 이들은 앞서 웨스턴 씨의 식탁에서 본 적 있는 그 정직한 목사와, 소피아의 고모인 웨스턴 부인, 그리고 마지막으로 사랑스러운 소피아 본인이었다.\n\n이때 피로 물든 그 전장의 모습은 다음과 같았다. 한쪽에는 패배한 블리필이 온통 창백한 얼굴로, 거의 숨도 쉬지 못한 채 땅바닥에 쓰러져 있었다. 그 곁에는 승리자 존스가 거의 온몸을 피로 뒤덮은 채 서 있었는데, 그 피의 일부는 당연히 그 자신의 것이었고, 일부는 얼마 전까지 써그럼 목사의 것이었다. 세 번째 자리에는 그 써그럼이 마치 포루스 왕처럼 시무룩하게 정복자에게 굴복한 채 서 있었다. 이 그림의 마지막 인물은 위대한 웨스턴으로, 패배한 적을 더없이 영광스럽게 너그러이 봐주고 있었다.\n\n생기라곤 거의 보이지 않던 블리필이 처음에는 모두의 관심을 한 몸에 받는 주된 대상이었고, 특히 웨스턴 부인이 그러했다. 부인은 주머니에서 각성제 한 병을 꺼내 직접 그의 콧구멍에 갖다 대려던 참이었다. 그런데 갑자기 일행 전체의 주의가 가엾은 블리필에게서 멀어지고 말았으니, 만일 그의 혼이 그럴 작정이었다면 이 틈을 타 아무런 격식도 없이 저세상으로 슬쩍 빠져나갈 기회를 얻었을지도 모른다.\n\n이제 더 애처롭고 더 사랑스러운 대상이 그들 앞에 꼼짝없이 누워 있었기 때문이다. 다름 아닌 매혹적인 소피아 본인이었다. 그녀는 피를 본 탓인지, 아버지에 대한 두려움 탓인지, 아니면 또 다른 어떤 까닭인지, 누구도 손을 쓸 새도 없이 그만 정신을 잃고 쓰러져 버린 것이었다.\n\n웨스턴 부인이 먼저 그녀를 보고 비명을 질렀다. 곧바로 두세 사람의 목소리가 외쳤다. \"웨스턴 양이 죽었어요.\" 각성제, 물, 온갖 처방이 거의 동시에 한꺼번에 불려 나왔다.\n\n독자는 우리가 이 숲을 묘사하면서 졸졸 흐르는 시냇물을 언급했던 것을 기억할 것이다. 그 시냇물은 흔해 빠진 연애소설 속에서 그저 졸졸거리는 것 말고는 아무 목적 없이 흘러가는 그런 잔잔한 물줄기로 거기 있었던 것이 아니다. 아니다! 운명은 이 작은 시냇물에게, 아르카디아의 들판을 적시는 어떤 시내가 누렸던 것보다도 더 높은 영예를 안겨 주기로 정해 두었던 것이다.\n\n존스는 블리필의 관자놀이를 문지르고 있었다. 한 대를 너무 세게 친 게 아닌가 슬슬 겁이 나기 시작했던 것이다. 그때 \"웨스턴 양\"과 \"죽었다\"는 말이 한꺼번에 그의 귀로 쏟아져 들어왔다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 블리필을 그의 운명에 내맡긴 채 소피아에게로 날아갔다. 다른 사람들이 모두 앞으로 뒤로 서로 부딪쳐 가며 메마른 길에서 물을 찾고 있는 동안, 그는 소피아를 두 팔로 안아 올린 뒤, 앞서 말한 그 시냇물을 향해 들판을 가로질러 달려갔다. 거기서 물속에 몸을 던져 가며, 그녀의 얼굴과 머리와 목에 물을 흠뻑 끼얹는 데 성공했다.\n\n소피아에게는 다행스러운 일이었다. 다른 벗들이 그녀를 돕지 못하게 만든 바로 그 혼란이, 그들이 존스를 방해하지 못하게도 막아 주었으니 말이다. 사람들이 그가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알아채기도 전에 그는 이미 그녀를 절반쯤 데려가 있었고, 그들이 물가에 닿기도 전에 그는 정말로 그녀를 되살려 놓았다. 그녀는 두 팔을 뻗고 눈을 떠 \"오! 맙소사!\" 하고 외쳤는데, 마침 그녀의 아버지와 고모와 목사가 다가온 바로 그 순간이었다.\n\n그때까지 이 사랑스러운 짐을 두 팔에 안고 있던 존스는 이제 손을 놓았다. 하지만 그와 동시에 그녀에게 다정한 손길로 한 번 어루만져 주었으니, 만일 그녀가 그때 정신을 온전히 차린 상태였다면 결코 그 손길을 알아채지 못했을 리 없었다. 그런데 그녀가 이 무례함에 아무런 불쾌함도 드러내지 않았으니, 우리는 그녀가 그 순간 아직 기절에서 충분히 깨어나지 못했던 것이라 짐작한다.\n\n이 비극적인 장면은 이제 별안간 기쁨의 장면으로 바뀌었다. 여기서 우리의 주인공이야말로 단연 주연 인물이었다. 그는 아마도 소피아를 구한 데서, 그녀 자신이 구원받은 데서 느낀 것보다 더 황홀한 기쁨을 맛보았을 것이고, 그녀에게 쏟아진 축하의 말 또한 존스에게 바쳐진 것에는 미치지 못했으니 말이다. 특히 웨스턴 씨 본인이 그러했는데, 그는 딸을 한두 번 끌어안고 나더니 이내 존스를 부둥켜안고 입을 맞추기 시작했다. 그는 존스를 소피아의 구원자라 부르며, 딸과 자기 영지를 빼고는 무엇이든 그에게 주지 못할 것이 없노라고 선언했다. 하지만 다시 곰곰 생각해 보더니, 나중에 가서는 자기 폭스하운드와 슈발리에와 슬라우치 양(그가 자기 애마를 그렇게 불렀다)만은 예외로 두었다.\n\n소피아에 대한 모든 걱정이 이제 사라지자, 존스가 지주의 관심거리가 되었다. \"이리 와라, 얘야.\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"웃옷을 벗고 얼굴을 좀 씻어라. 정말이지 꼴이 말이 아니구나. 어서, 어서, 몸을 씻고 나하고 같이 집으로 가자. 다른 웃옷을 한 벌 마련해 주마.\"\n\n존스는 곧바로 따랐다. 웃옷을 벗어 던지고 물가로 내려가 얼굴과 가슴을 둘 다 씻었으니, 가슴 역시 얼굴만큼이나 다 드러나 있고 피투성이였기 때문이다. 그러나 물로 피는 씻어 낼 수 있었어도, 써그럼이 그의 얼굴과 가슴에 둘 다 새겨 놓은 시퍼런 멍자국까지 지울 수는 없었다. 그 자국을 알아본 소피아는 한숨과 함께, 이루 말할 수 없는 애틋함이 가득 담긴 눈길을 보냈다.\n\n존스는 이 눈길을 두 눈으로 고스란히 받아들였는데, 그것은 그가 앞서 입은 그 모든 타박상보다도 한없이 더 강한 영향을 그에게 미쳤다. 다만 그 영향은 사뭇 다른 종류의 것이었으니, 어찌나 부드럽고 향기로웠던지, 만일 그가 앞서 맞은 모든 타격이 칼에 찔린 상처였다 해도 몇 분 동안은 그 쓰라림을 느끼지 못하게 했을 정도였다.\n\n이제 일행은 뒤로 발길을 돌렸고, 곧 써그럼이 블리필 씨를 다시 일으켜 세워 놓은 곳에 이르렀다. 여기서 우리는 한 가지 경건한 소망을 억누를 수가 없다. 모든 싸움이 오직, 무엇이 우리에게 알맞은지를 아는 자연이 우리에게 마련해 준 그 무기로만 결판났으면, 그리고 차가운 쇠붙이는 오직 땅의 창자를 파헤치는 데에만 쓰였으면 하는 소망이다. 그렇게만 된다면 군주들의 소일거리인 전쟁도 거의 해롭지 않은 것이 될 테고, 거대한 군대들 사이의 전투도 몇몇 지체 높은 부인들의 특별한 청에 따라 벌어질 수 있으리라. 그러면 부인들은 왕들과 더불어 그 싸움을 실제로 구경하는 관객이 될 수도 있을 것이다. 또한 들판은 이 순간 인간의 시체로 흥건히 뒤덮였다가, 다음 순간에는 죽은 자들이, 아니 그 압도적인 대다수가, 베이즈 씨의 군대처럼 벌떡 일어나, 미리 합의한 대로 북소리든 바이올린 소리든 그 소리에 맞추어 행진해 나갈 수도 있으리라.\n\n나는 가능하다면 이 문제를 우스갯거리로 다루지 않으려 한다. 농담이라면 발끈하는 줄 내가 잘 아는 근엄한 분들과 정치가들이 그것을 보고 \"쳇\" 하며 콧방귀를 뀔까 봐 말이다. 하지만 사실, 전투라는 것이 갈가리 찢기고 살해당한 인간의 몸뚱이를 더 높이 쌓아 올리는 쪽으로 결판나는 것 못지않게, 깨진 머리와 피투성이 코와 시퍼런 눈두덩이를 더 많이 만드는 쪽으로 결판날 수도 있지 않겠는가? 도시도 같은 방식으로 다투어 차지할 수 있지 않겠는가? 하기야 이것은 프랑스의 이익에는 너무 손해가 큰 계획이라 여겨질 법도 하다. 그렇게 되면 그들이 다른 나라들에 대해 가진 우위, 즉 뛰어난 공병들의 우월함이라는 이점을 잃게 될 테니 말이다. 그러나 그 국민의 용맹함과 너그러움을 헤아려 보건대, 나는 그들이 적과 대등한 처지에 서는 것을, 즉 흔한 표현대로 적의 호적수가 되기를 결코 마다하지 않으리라 확신한다.\n\n하지만 그러한 개혁이란 바라는 것이지 기대할 것은 못 된다. 그러니 나는 이 짤막한 암시 하나로 만족하고, 다시 내 이야기로 돌아가겠다.\n\n이제 웨스턴은 이 싸움이 애당초 어떻게 일어난 것인지 캐묻기 시작했다. 그런데 거기에 블리필도 존스도 아무런 대답을 하지 않았고, 다만 써그럼이 뚱하게 말했다. \"그 까닭이라면 멀지 않은 데 있을 겁니다. 덤불을 잘 뒤져 보면 그 계집을 찾을 수 있을 테지요.\" \"그 계집을 찾는다?\" 웨스턴이 받아쳤다. \"뭐라고! 자네들이 계집 하나를 두고 싸웠단 말이야?\" \"저기 조끼 차림인 신사 양반한테 물어보시지요.\" 써그럼이 말했다. \"그자가 제일 잘 알 테니.\" \"허, 그렇다면,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"틀림없이 계집 때문이로군. 아, 톰, 톰, 너 이 색 밝히는 놈 같으니. 하지만 자, 여러분, 모두 친구가 되어 나하고 같이 집으로 가서, 술 한 병 놓고 화해를 매듭짓읍시다.\" \"송구하오나, 선생,\" 써그럼이 말했다. \"저 같은 신분의 사람이 이렇게 모욕적인 대접을 받고, 그것도 한낱 풋내기에게 두들겨 맞다니, 결코 그리 가볍게 넘길 일이 아닙니다. 그저 음란한 계집을 적발해 처벌받게 하려고 제 본분을 다하려 했을 뿐인데 말입니다. 하지만 사실, 가장 큰 잘못은 올워디 씨와 선생 본인께 있습니다. 두 분이 마땅히 하셔야 할 대로 법을 집행하신다면, 이내 이 고장에서 그런 해충 떼를 쓸어버릴 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그런 거라면 차라리 이 고장에서 여우를 쓸어버리는 게 낫겠소.\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"내 생각엔 우리가 전쟁으로 날마다 잃어 가는 그 머릿수를 다시 채우는 일을 오히려 장려해야 할 것 같은데. 한데 그 계집은 어디 있나? 자, 톰, 어디 보여 다오.\" 그러더니 그는 마치 산토끼를 몰아내듯 똑같은 말투에 똑같은 몸짓으로 이리저리 덤불을 두들기기 시작하더니, 마침내 외쳤다. \"어이! 토끼가 멀지 않은 데 있군. 여기 그 잠자리 자국이 있어, 정말이지. 이쯤이면 '도망쳤다!'고 외쳐도 되겠는걸.\" 과연 그렇게 외칠 만했다. 그 가엾은 처녀가 싸움이 시작되던 무렵에, 산토끼가 줄행랑칠 때 흔히 놀리는 만큼이나 부지런히 발을 놀려 슬그머니 빠져나간 바로 그 자리를 이제 막 찾아낸 참이었으니 말이다.\n\n이제 소피아가 아버지에게 집으로 돌아가자고 청했다. 몸이 몹시 어지럽고, 또 혼절이 도질까 걱정이 된다는 것이었다. 지주는 즉시 딸의 청에 따랐다(그는 더없이 자식을 끔찍이 아끼는 아비였으니까). 그는 일행 전부에게 자기 집에 가서 함께 저녁을 먹자고 간곡히 권했다. 그러나 블리필과 써그럼은 한사코 거절했다. 블리필은 지금 그 자리에서 다 말할 수 없는 여러 이유로 이 영광을 사양할 수밖에 없다고 했고, 써그럼은 (아마 옳은 말이었겠지만) 자기 같은 직분을 가진 사람이 지금 이런 꼴로 어디든 모습을 드러내는 것은 온당치 못하다고 잘라 말했다.\n\n존스는 자기 소피아와 함께 있는 기쁨을 차마 마다할 수가 없었다. 그리하여 그는 웨스턴 지주와 그 부인들과 함께 발걸음을 옮겼고, 목사가 그 뒤를 따라왔다. 사실 이 목사는 자기 형제뻘인 써그럼 곁에 남겠다고 나섰더랬다. 같은 성직자에 대한 도리상 차마 떠날 수 없다는 것이었다. 그러나 써그럼은 그 호의를 받아들이지 않고, 그다지 정중하달 것도 없이 그를 웨스턴 씨 뒤로 떠밀어 버렸다.\n\n이렇게 이 피비린내 나는 싸움은 끝이 났다. 그리고 이렇게 이 이야기의 다섯 번째 권도 끝을 맺으리라.\n\n약 3주간의 이야기를 담음.\n사랑에 관하여.\n\n우리는 앞선 권에서 사랑이라는 정념을 꽤 많이 다룰 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 이어질 권에서는 이 주제를 한층 더 폭넓게 다룰 수밖에 없을 것이다. 그러니 이 자리에서, 어떤 철학자들이 수많은 놀라운 발견들 가운데 하나로, 인간의 가슴속에는 그런 정념 따위가 존재하지 않는다는 것을 알아냈노라 자처하는 그 현대의 학설을 한번 살펴보는 것도 그리 부적절하지는 않을 것이다.\n이 철학자들이, 고(故) 스위프트 박사가 명예롭게 언급한 바 있는 그 놀라운 무리—오로지 타고난 천재성의 힘만으로, 어떤 종류의 학식이나 심지어 독서의 도움조차 조금도 받지 않고서, 신이란 존재하지 않는다는 그 심오하고도 값진 비밀을 발견해 냈다는 무리—와 같은 자들인지, 아니면 몇 해 전 인간 본성 속에는 덕이나 선이라 할 만한 것이 실제로는 전혀 존재하지 않으며 우리의 가장 훌륭한 행위조차 자만심에서 비롯된 것이라고 주장하여 세상을 적잖이 놀라게 한 그 자들과 차라리 같은 부류인지, 나는 여기서 함부로 단정하지 않으련다. 사실대로 말하자면, 나는 이 여러 진리의 발견자들이라는 자들이, 다른 이들이 황금을 캐는 자라 부르는 바로 그 똑같은 인간들이 아닐까 의심하는 쪽으로 기운다. 진리를 캐는 일과 황금을 캐는 일에 쓰이는 방법이란 실은 하나요 같은 것이니, 곧 더러운 곳을 뒤지고, 헤집고, 살피는 일이다. 더구나 앞의 경우에는 모든 더러운 곳 가운데서도 가장 더러운 곳, 곧 나쁜 마음을 파헤치는 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 점에서, 그리고 어쩌면 그 성과에서도 진리를 캐는 자와 황금을 캐는 자를 나란히 견주는 것이 제법 그럴듯하다 하더라도, 겸손함에서만큼은 분명 둘 사이에 견줄 거리가 없다. 황금을 캐는 자가 자기 탐색이 신통치 못했다 하여 세상에 황금 따위는 존재하지 않는다고 뻔뻔하거나 어리석게 주장했다는 이야기를, 일찍이 누가 들어 본 적이 있던가? 그런데 진리를 캐는 자는 제 마음이라는 그 뒷간을 박박 긁어 보고도 거기서 신성의 빛줄기 하나, 덕스럽거나 선하거나 사랑스럽거나 사랑하는 것 하나 찾아내지 못하자, 아주 공정하고 정직하며 논리적으로, 그런 것들은 온 피조물 가운데 어디에도 존재하지 않는다고 결론짓는 것이다.\n\n하지만 가능하다면 이 철학자들—그렇게 불리고자 한다면—과의 온갖 다툼을 피하고, 우리 사이의 문제를 평화롭게 매듭짓고자 하는 우리 자신의 뜻을 보이기 위해, 여기서 우리는 그들에게 몇 가지 양보를 해 보려 한다. 어쩌면 이로써 이 논쟁이 끝날지도 모를 일이다.\n\n첫째, 우리는 많은 마음이, 그리고 어쩌면 그 철학자들의 마음이, 그러한 정념의 흔적이라곤 조금도 없이 완전히 깨끗하다는 것을 인정하겠다.\n\n둘째, 흔히 사랑이라 불리는 것, 곧 일정한 분량의 보드랍고 하얀 인육으로 게걸스러운 식욕을 채우려는 욕망은, 내가 여기서 옹호하려는 그 정념과는 결코 같지 않다는 것을 인정하겠다. 그런 것은 실은 더 정확히 말해 허기다. 어떤 대식가도 제 식욕에다 사랑이라는 말을 붙여 이러저러한 요리를 사랑한다고 말하기를 부끄러워하지 않듯이, 이런 부류의 연인도 똑같이 적절하게, 자신이 이러저러한 여인을 향해 허기진다고 말할 수 있을 것이다.\n\n셋째, 나는, 아마도 가장 흔쾌히 받아들여질 양보가 되리라 믿는바, 내가 옹호하는 이 사랑이 비록 훨씬 더 섬세한 방식으로 스스로를 만족시킬지언정, 그럼에도 우리의 모든 식욕 가운데 가장 천박한 것에 못지않게 제 나름의 만족을 구한다는 것을 인정하겠다.\n\n그리고 끝으로, 이 사랑이 다른 성(性)의 누군가를 향해 작용할 때에는, 그 만족을 온전히 이루기 위해 앞서 말한 그 허기의 도움을 불러들이기 십상이라는 것, 그리고 이 사랑은 그 허기를 누그러뜨리기는커녕 오히려 그 모든 기쁨을, 오로지 식욕에서만 비롯된 감정 말고는 다른 어떤 감정도 느껴 본 적 없는 자들로서는 좀처럼 상상조차 할 수 없는 정도로까지 드높여 준다는 것을 인정하겠다.\n\n이 모든 양보의 대가로, 나는 철학자들에게 다음의 것들을 인정해 달라고 청한다. 어떤(내 믿기로는 많은) 인간의 가슴속에는, 남들의 행복에 보탬이 됨으로써 흡족해지는 너그럽고 자애로운 성향이 자리하고 있다는 것. 우정에서, 부모와 자식 간의 정에서, 그리고 실로 널리 인류를 사랑하는 마음에서와 마찬가지로, 바로 이 흡족함 그 자체 속에 크고도 정묘한 기쁨이 깃들어 있다는 것. 만약 우리가 그런 성향을 사랑이라 부르지 않겠다면, 그것을 가리킬 다른 이름이 우리에게는 없다는 것. 그리고 그런 순수한 사랑에서 솟아나는 기쁨이 비록 연정 어린 욕망의 도움으로 한층 드높아지고 달콤해질 수 있다 해도, 앞의 것은 홀로도 능히 존립할 수 있으며, 뒤의 것이 끼어든다 하여 무너지는 것도 아니라는 것. 끝으로, 젊음과 아름다움이 욕망의 마땅한 동기이듯, 존경과 감사는 사랑의 마땅한 동기라는 것. 그러므로 그 대상에 늙음이나 병이 닥치면 그런 욕망은 자연히 사그라질 수 있을지언정, 이런 것들은 사랑에는 아무런 영향도 미치지 못하며, 감사와 존경을 그 바탕으로 삼는 그 감각이나 정념을 선한 마음에서 결코 흔들거나 몰아내지 못한다는 것이다.\n\n우리가 그 뚜렷한 사례들을 자주 보게 되는 정념을 두고 그 존재를 부정한다는 것은 참으로 기이하고 터무니없는 일로 보인다. 그리고 그것은 실로 앞서 말한 그 자기 본위의 잣대에서만 비롯될 수 있다. 그러나 이것이 얼마나 부당한가! 제 가슴속에서 탐욕이나 야심의 흔적을 찾지 못한다 하여, 인간 본성에는 그런 정념이 존재하지 않는다고 결론짓는 사람이 어디 있는가? 어찌하여 우리는, 남들의 악을 헤아릴 때와 마찬가지로 남들의 선을 헤아릴 때에도 그 같은 규칙을 겸손하게 따르려 하지 않는가? 또 어찌하여 우리는, 어떤 경우에든 셰익스피어의 표현처럼 \"온 세상을 제 한 몸으로 빗대려\" 드는가?\n\n아무래도 여기에는 우리를 지배하는 허영심이 너무 깊이 끼어 있는 듯하다. 이는 우리가 우리 자신의 마음에 바치는 그 아첨의 한 본보기이며, 그것도 거의 누구에게나 보편적인 본보기다. 아첨꾼이라는 인물을 제아무리 경멸하는 사람이라 할지라도, 가장 비루한 방식으로 자기 자신에게는 기꺼이 아첨하려 들지 않는 이가 거의 없으니 말이다.\n\n그러므로 위의 관찰들이 참임을 가려 줄 일에 대해, 나는 자기 마음이 내가 펼친 주장을 증언해 줄 수 있는 이들에게 호소하는 바이다.\n\n착한 독자여, 그대의 마음을 한번 살펴보고, 그대가 나와 더불어 이런 일들을 믿는지 결단해 보라. 만약 믿는다면, 이제 이어지는 책장들에서 그 실례를 보러 나아가도 좋다. 만약 믿지 않는다면, 분명히 말해 두건대 그대는 이미 자신이 이해한 것보다 더 많이 읽은 것이니, 맛볼 수도 깨달을 수도 없는 글을 읽느라 더는 시간을 허비하기보다, 차라리 그대의 일이나 (그것이 무엇이든) 그대의 즐거움을 좇는 편이 더 지혜로울 것이다. 그대에게 사랑의 효력을 논한다는 것은, 날 때부터 눈먼 사람에게 색깔을 늘어놓는 것만큼이나 터무니없는 일임에 틀림없다. 그도 그럴 것이, 사랑에 대한 그대의 관념이란, 어떤 눈먼 사람이 진홍빛에 대해 한때 품었다고 전해지는 그 관념만큼이나 터무니없을지도 모르기 때문이다. 그 사람에게는 진홍빛이 나팔 소리와 무척 비슷하게 여겨졌다 한다. 그러니 그대 생각에 사랑이란, 어쩌면 수프 한 접시나 등심 구운 쇠고기 한 덩이와 아주 흡사한 것일지도 모를 일이다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n웨스턴 부인의 인물됨. 그녀의 대단한 학식과 세상물정에 대한 식견, 그리고 그러한 강점에서 비롯된 깊은 통찰력의 한 사례.\n\n독자께서는 웨스턴 씨와 그 누이, 그리고 그 딸이 젊은 존스, 목사와 함께 웨스턴 씨의 집으로 향하는 것을 이미 보았다. 그곳에서 일행의 대부분은 무척이나 흥겹고 떠들썩하게 저녁을 보냈다. 진지한 표정을 짓고 있는 사람은 사실 소피아뿐이었다. 존스로 말하자면, 이제 사랑이 그의 가슴을 온전히 사로잡고 있었음에도, 올워디 씨가 회복되었다는 흐뭇한 생각과 사랑하는 여인이 곁에 있다는 사실, 게다가 그녀가 이따금 그에게 던지지 않고는 못 배기던 다정한 눈길까지 더해져, 우리의 주인공은 한껏 기분이 들떠 나머지 세 사람의 즐거움에 함께 어울렸다. 어쩌면 그 세 사람은 세상에서 둘도 없이 쾌활한 이들이었으리라.\n\n소피아는 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서도 똑같이 굳은 얼굴을 하고 있었고, 그래서 평소보다 일찍 자리를 떠 아버지와 고모를 단둘이 남겨 두었다. 지주 양반은 딸의 기분에 일어난 이 변화를 전혀 알아차리지 못했다. 사실을 말하자면, 그는 다소간 정치가 흉내를 내는 사람이었고 선거 때 시골 진영의 후보로 두 번이나 나선 적도 있었지만, 관찰력이 그리 뛰어난 인물은 아니었다. 그의 누이는 결이 다른 부인이었다. 그녀는 궁정 근처에서 지내며 세상을 두루 보아 왔다. 그래서 이른바 그 세상이 으레 전해 주는 온갖 지식을 익혔고, 예법과 관습, 의례와 유행에 통달한 완벽한 안주인이었다. 그녀의 박식함은 거기서 그치지 않았다. 그녀는 공부로 상당히 마음을 갈고닦았다. 현대의 희곡과 오페라, 오라토리오, 시, 연애소설을 죄다 읽었을 뿐 아니라—이 모든 분야에서 그녀는 평론가였다—라팽의 《영국사》, 에처드의 《로마사》, 그리고 수많은 프랑스어 《역사에 이바지하기 위한 회고록》까지 두루 섭렵했다. 여기에 더해 지난 이십 년 동안 출간된 정치 소책자와 잡지의 대부분도 읽어 두었다. 이로써 그녀는 정치에 제법 능숙한 솜씨를 얻었고, 유럽 정세를 무척 유식하게 논할 수 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 연애의 이치에도 훤히 통달해, 누구와 누가 그렇고 그런 사이인지를 누구보다 잘 알고 있었다. 이런 지식은 그만큼 더 쉽게 얻은 것이었으니, 그것을 좇는 데 자기 자신의 연애사가 끼어들어 방해한 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이다. 그녀에게는 그런 마음이 아예 없었거나, 아니면 그런 청을 받아 본 적이 없었던 것이다. 후자가 사실 매우 그럴듯하다. 키가 거의 육 척에 이르는 그 사내 같은 체구에다 몸가짐과 학식까지 더해지니, 치마를 두르고 있음에도 다른 성(性)이 그녀를 여자로 여기지 못하게 막았을 법하기 때문이다. 그러나 이 문제를 학문적으로 헤아려 온 터라, 그녀는 비록 한 번도 직접 써먹어 본 적은 없을지언정, 우아한 부인들이 호감을 부추기거나 좋아하는 마음을 감추려 할 때 쓰는 온갖 기교를, 그리고 오늘날 상류 사교계에서 행해지는 미소와 추파, 곁눈질 따위의 그 길고 긴 부속물 일체를 완벽히 꿰고 있었다. 한마디로, 어떤 종류의 가식이나 위장도 그녀의 눈을 피하지 못했다. 다만 정직한 본성이 빚어내는 꾸밈없는 단순한 움직임에 대해서라면, 그런 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없었던 만큼, 거의 아는 바가 없었다.\n\n이 놀라운 통찰력 덕분에 웨스턴 부인은 이제, 적어도 자기 생각으로는, 소피아의 마음속에서 무언가를 발견해 냈다. 이에 대한 첫 실마리는 그 싸움터에서 보였던 조카딸의 거동에서 잡아낸 것이었고, 그때 품은 의심은 그날 저녁과 이튿날 아침에 그녀가 해 둔 몇 가지 관찰로 한층 굳어졌다. 그러나 잘못 짚은 것으로 드러나지 않으려 무척이나 조심했던 까닭에, 그녀는 그 비밀을 꼬박 두 주 동안 가슴속에 묻어 둔 채, 다만 능청스러운 웃음과 윙크, 고갯짓, 그리고 이따금 알쏭달쏭한 말 한마디를 흘려 에둘러 암시만 던졌다. 그것은 소피아를 충분히 불안하게 만들었으되, 그녀의 오라비에게는 아무런 영향도 미치지 못했다.\n\n그러나 마침내 자신의 관찰이 옳다는 것을 철저히 확신하게 된 그녀는, 어느 날 아침 오라비와 단둘이 있을 때를 노려, 그가 한창 휘파람을 불고 있는 것을 다음과 같이 끊고 들어갔다.\n\n\"오라버니, 요즘 제 조카에게서 뭔가 아주 심상찮은 점을 못 보셨어요?\" \"아니, 못 봤는데.\" 웨스턴이 대답했다. \"그 애한테 무슨 일이라도 있나?\" \"있는 것 같아요.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"그것도 아주 중대한 일이요.\" \"허, 그 애는 아무 데도 아프다고 한 적이 없는데.\" 웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"게다가 천연두도 이미 앓았고.\" \"오라버니,\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"처녀들이 천연두 말고도 걸리는 병이 있고, 때로는 어쩌면 훨씬 더 고약한 병도 있답니다.\" 여기서 웨스턴은 무척 다급하게 그녀의 말을 끊고, 딸에게 무슨 탈이라도 있다면 당장 알려 달라고 간청했다. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, \"그 애를 제 목숨보다 더 사랑한다는 걸 너도 알지 않느냐, 그 애를 위해서라면 세상 끝까지라도 사람을 보내 가장 용한 의사를 데려오겠다.\" 했다. \"아니, 아니에요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"그 병은 그렇게 무서운 게 아니랍니다. 하지만 오라버니, 제가 세상물정을 안다는 건 오라버니도 인정하시잖아요. 그래서 약속하건대, 제 조카가 지독하게 사랑에 빠진 게 아니라면, 제 평생 이렇게까지 속아 본 적은 없을 거예요.\" \"뭐라고! 사랑에 빠져?\" 웨스턴이 발끈하며 소리쳤다. \"사랑에 빠졌다고, 그것도 나한테 알리지도 않고! 그 애를 상속에서 빼 버리겠다. 실오라기 하나 걸치지 못하게 한 푼도 없이 문밖으로 내쫓아 버리겠어. 내가 그 애를 그토록 아끼고 위해 준 게 고작 이 꼴을 보자고 한 짓이었나, 내 허락도 구하지 않고 사랑에 빠지다니?\" \"하지만 오라버니,\" 웨스턴 부인이 대답했다. \"그 애가 고른 상대를 오라버니가 마음에 들어 할지 어떨지 알기도 전에, 제 목숨보다 더 사랑한다는 그 딸을 문밖으로 내쫓진 않으시겠죠. 만약 그 애가 오라버니 자신이 바라던 바로 그 사람을 점찍어 두었다면, 그래도 화를 내시겠어요?\" \"아니, 아니지.\" 웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"그건 얘기가 다르지. 내가 점찍어 둔 사내와 결혼만 한다면야, 그 애가 누굴 사랑하든 그건 마음대로 하라지. 그런 것까지 내가 머리 싸맬 일은 아니고.\" \"그건 분별 있는 사람다운 말씀이네요.\" 누이가 대답했다. \"하지만 그 애가 고른 바로 그 사람이, 오라버니가 그 애를 위해 고를 바로 그 사람일 거라고 저는 믿어요. 만약 그렇지 않다면, 제가 세상물정을 안다는 걸 깡그리 부인하겠어요. 그리고 오라버니, 제게 그쯤 되는 식견은 있다는 걸 인정하실 테죠.\" \"허, 들어 보게, 누이.\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"나도 자네가 여느 여자만큼은 식견이 있다고 믿네. 게다가 그런 건 확실히 여자들 일이고. 자네도 알다시피 나는 자네가 정치 얘기 하는 건 듣기 싫어하네. 정치는 우리 남자들 몫이고, 치마 두른 사람은 끼어들 게 아니지. 자, 그래서 그 사내가 누군가?\" \"원, 세상에!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"굳이 원하신다면 직접 알아내실 수 있을 텐데요. 그토록 대단한 정치가이신 오라버니라면 그리 크게 헤매실 일도 아니죠. 군주들의 내실을 꿰뚫어 보고, 유럽의 온갖 정치 기계 속에서 거대한 국가의 바퀴를 돌리는 은밀한 태엽을 찾아내는 그 판단력이라면, 처녀의 거칠고 어수룩한 마음속에 무슨 일이 벌어지는지쯤이야 거의 힘들이지 않고 알아내실 게 틀림없잖아요.\" \"누이,\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"그 궁정식 횡설수설일랑 내게 늘어놓지 말라고 몇 번이나 일렀잖나. 다시 말하지만, 나는 그 외국말 같은 건 알아듣지 못해. 하지만 잡지나 《런던 이브닝 포스트》쯤은 읽을 줄 안다네. 하긴 가끔 글자가 절반쯤 빠져 있어서 도무지 알아먹기 힘든 구절이 끼어 있긴 하지. 그래도 그게 무슨 뜻인지는 아주 잘 알아. 뇌물과 부패 때문에 우리 나라 일이 마땅히 돌아가야 할 만큼 잘 돌아가지 않는다는 뜻이지.\" \"그 시골뜨기 무지를 진심으로 가엾게 여겨요.\" 부인이 소리쳤다. \"그래?\" 웨스턴이 대답했다. \"나는 자네의 그 도시물 든 학식이 가엾네. 나는 어떤 사람이 되든 좋지만, 궁정 아첨꾼이나 장로교도, 거기다 어떤 자들이 그렇듯 하노버 지지자만큼은 되기 싫네.\" \"저를 두고 하시는 말씀이라면,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"아시다시피 저는 여자예요, 오라버니. 제가 어떤 사람인지는 아무 상관이 없죠. 게다가―\" \"자네가 여자인 줄은 잘 알지.\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"여자인 게 자네한테는 다행인 줄 알게. 사내였다면, 진작 한 대 후려쳐 줬을 테니까.\" \"옳거니, 바로 그거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오라버니가 잘난 척하는 그 우월함이라는 게 죄다 그 한 대에 들어 있는 거죠. 우리보다 강한 건 오라버니들의 머리가 아니라 몸뚱이일 뿐이라니까. 정말이지, 오라버니가 우리를 두들겨 팰 수 있다는 게 다행인 줄 아세요. 안 그랬다면, 우리 지력이 워낙 뛰어나서, 용감하고 지혜롭고 재치 있고 세련된 자들이 이미 그렇게 되어 있듯, 오라버니들 모두를 우리 노예로 만들어 버렸을 테니까요.\" \"자네 속마음을 알게 되어 다행이군.\" 지주가 대답했다. \"하지만 이 얘기는 다음에 더 하기로 하고. 지금은 내 딸 일로 자네가 말하는 그 사내가 누군지나 어서 말해 보게.\" \"잠깐만요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신네 성(性)에 대한 이 지독한 경멸을 좀 삭이고요. 안 그러면 저도 오라버니한테 화를 내야 할 판이니까. 자―어찌어찌 꿀꺽 삼켰네요. 자, 그럼 훌륭하신 정치가 양반, 블리필 씨를 어떻게 생각하세요? 그 애가 그분이 땅바닥에 숨이 끊긴 듯 누워 있는 걸 보고 까무러치지 않았나요? 그분이 깨어나신 뒤에도, 우리가 그분이 서 있던 그 들판으로 다가가는 순간 그 애가 다시 새파랗게 질리지 않던가요? 그렇다면 그날 밤 저녁 식사 자리에서, 또 이튿날 아침에, 아니 사실 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 애가 보인 우울이 대체 무엇 때문이겠어요?\" \"맙소사!\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"자네가 그렇게 짚어 주니, 이제야 죄다 떠오르는군. 분명 그런 거야, 나는 진심으로 기쁘기 그지없네. 우리 소피가 착한 애라서 날 화나게 할 사랑에는 빠지지 않을 줄 알았지. 내 평생 이보다 더 기뻤던 적이 없네. 우리 두 영지만큼 딱 들어맞게 붙어 있는 게 또 어디 있겠나. 사실 나도 얼마 전부터 이 일을 머릿속에 두고 있었네. 두 영지가 어떤 의미에서는 이미 혼인으로 한데 묶여 있는 셈이니, 그걸 갈라놓는다면 천 번 만 번 애석한 일이지. 하긴 이 나라에 더 큰 영지들이 있는 건 사실이지만, 이 고을 안에는 없으니까. 게다가 나는 차라리 조금 손해를 보더라도, 내 딸을 낯선 이방인들 틈에 시집보내고 싶지는 않네. 더구나 그런 큰 영지는 대개 귀족 나리들 손에 들어 있는데, 나는 그 양반들 이름만 들어도 진저리가 나거든. 자, 그런데 누이, 내가 어찌하면 좋을지 좀 일러 주겠나. 다시 말하지만, 이런 일은 자네들 여자가 우리보다 더 잘 아니까.\" \"오, 황송하옵니다, 나리.\" 부인이 대답했다. \"우리한테 무슨 일에든 능력이 있다고 인정해 주시니 그저 송구할 따름이죠. 그럼 그토록 능란하신 정치가 양반께서 기꺼이 제 조언을 구하시니, 제 생각에는 오라버니가 직접 올워디 씨에게 혼담을 꺼내시는 게 좋겠어요. 어느 쪽 부모가 먼저 청혼을 내놓는다 해도 예법에 어긋날 건 없으니까요. 포프 씨의 《오디세이아》에서도 알키노오스 왕이 자기 딸을 율리시스에게 내주겠다고 청하잖아요. 그토록 능란한 분께 굳이 일러 둘 필요야 없겠지만, 따님이 사랑에 빠졌다는 말씀만은 하지 마세요. 그건 정말이지 모든 법도에 어긋나는 일이니까요.\" \"좋아.\" 지주가 말했다. \"내가 청해 보지. 하지만 그가 날 퇴짜 놓으면, 분명 한 대 후려쳐 줄 테야.\" \"걱정 마세요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 소리쳤다. \"이 혼사는 너무나 유리해서 거절당할 리가 없어요.\" \"그건 모르는 일이지.\" 지주가 대답했다. \"올워디는 별난 작자라서, 돈 따위는 먹히질 않거든.\" \"오라버니,\" 부인이 말했다. \"그 정치 식견이라는 게 정말 놀랍네요. 정말로 입에 발린 말에 그렇게 넘어가실 작정이세요? 올워디 씨가 남들보다 돈을 더 멸시한다고 떠들어 댄다고 해서 정말 더 멸시하는 줄 아세요? 그런 잘 믿는 버릇은, 하늘이 정치가가 되라고 빚으신 그 지혜로운 성(性)보다는, 우리처럼 연약한 여자들에게 더 어울리는 거랍니다. 정말이지 오라버니, 오라버니가 프랑스와 협상하는 전권대사로 나선다면 볼만하겠어요. 그쪽 사람들은 자기네가 순전히 방어 원칙에서 도시들을 빼앗는 거라고 금세 오라버니를 설득해 버릴 테니까요.\" \"누이,\" 지주가 잔뜩 비웃으며 대답했다. \"함락된 도시들에 대해서는 자네 궁정 친구들더러 책임지라고 하게. 자네는 여자니까 나는 아무 탓도 않겠네. 그자들도 여자한테 비밀을 맡길 만큼 어리석진 않을 만큼은 영리할 테니까.\" 그러고는 어찌나 빈정대며 웃어 댔던지, 웨스턴 부인은 더 참을 수가 없었다. 그녀는 줄곧 가장 예민한 데를 찔려 속이 부글거리던 터였고(사실 그녀는 이런 문제에 매우 깊이 통달해 있었고, 또 그만큼 격렬하기도 했다), 그리하여 분통을 터뜨리며 오라비를 두고 촌놈에다 멍청이라고 못 박더니, 이런 집에는 더 이상 머물지 않겠다고 선언했다.\n\n지주는 비록 마키아벨리는 읽어 본 적이 없을지 몰라도, 많은 점에서 완벽한 정치가였다. 그는 익스체인지 골목의 그 정치적 소요학파에서 그토록 잘 가르치는 온갖 현명한 신조를 굳게 신봉했다. 그는 돈의 정당한 가치와 유일한 쓰임새, 곧 차곡차곡 쌓아 두는 것을 알고 있었다. 또한 그는 상속권과 기대 재산 따위의 정확한 가치에도 능통해 있어서, 누이의 재산이 얼마나 되는지, 그리고 그것을 자신이나 자손이 물려받을 가망이 얼마나 되는지를 곧잘 따져 보곤 했다. 이것을 하찮은 분풀이 따위에 내던지기에는 그는 한없이 영리했다. 그래서 일을 너무 멀리 끌고 갔다 싶자, 그는 둘 사이를 화해시킬 궁리를 하기 시작했다. 그것은 그리 어려운 일이 아니었으니, 부인은 오라비를 무척 아꼈고, 조카딸은 그보다 더 아꼈기 때문이다. 또한 그녀가 스스로 무척 자부하던 정치 식견을 모욕당하는 데는 지나치게 발끈하긴 했어도, 본바탕은 더없이 착하고 다정한 성품의 여자였던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 그는 우선 말들에게 우격다짐을 가했다. 말들이 마구간에서 빠져나갈 통로라고는 창문밖에 열려 있지 않았던 것이다. 그러고는 누이에게로 향해, 자신이 했던 말을 죄다 주워 담고 그녀를 분노케 했던 주장과 정반대되는 말들을 늘어놓으며 그녀를 누그러뜨리고 달랬다. 마지막으로 그는 소피아의 말솜씨를 거들미로 불러들였다. 소피아는 더없이 우아하고 사람을 끄는 화술뿐 아니라, 고모가 큰 호의와 편애로 자기 말에 귀 기울여 준다는 이점까지 누리고 있었다.\n\n이 모든 일의 결말은 웨스턴 부인의 다정한 미소였다. 그녀가 말했다. \"오라버니, 오라버니는 영락없이 완벽한 크로아트 용병이네요. 하지만 그런 자들도 황후 폐하의 군대에서는 나름의 쓸모가 있듯, 오라버니에게도 좋은 구석은 있는 셈이죠. 그러니 한 번 더 오라버니와 평화 조약에 서명하겠어요. 다만 오라버니 쪽에서 그걸 어기지 않도록 잘 지켜보겠어요. 적어도, 오라버니가 그토록 뛰어난 정치가이시니, 프랑스 사람들처럼 자기 이해관계가 깨뜨리라고 부추기기 전까지는 동맹을 지키시리라 기대해도 되겠죠.\"\n\n비평가들에게 보내는 두 가지 도전장을 담은 장.\n\n앞 장에서 보았듯 누이와 일을 매듭지은 향사는 그 제안을 올워디 씨에게 전하고 싶어 안달이 어찌나 심했던지, 웨스턴 부인은 그가 병중인 그분을 이 일로 찾아가는 것을 막느라 그야말로 진땀을 빼야 했다.\n\n올워디 씨는 자신이 병으로 쓰러질 무렵, 웨스턴 씨와 식사를 함께하기로 약속이 잡혀 있었다. 그래서 그는 약물의 손아귀에서 풀려나기가 무섭게, (가장 큰일에서든 가장 사소한 일에서든 늘 그러했듯이) 그 약속을 지킬 생각을 했다.\n\n앞 장의 대화가 오간 때와 이 공식 만찬의 날 사이의 그 짬에, 소피아는 고모가 흘린 어떤 모호한 암시들로 미루어, 그 빈틈없는 부인이 자신이 존스를 향해 품은 연정을 의심하고 있다는 약간의 불안을 모으게 되었다. 그녀는 이제 이 기회를 빌려 그런 의심을 모조리 지워 버리기로, 그리고 그 목적을 위해 자신의 행동에 완전한 굴레를 씌우기로 마음먹었다.\n\n먼저 그녀는 두근대는 우울한 마음을 더없이 발랄한 표정과 더없이 명랑한 태도 뒤에 숨기려 애썼다. 둘째로, 그녀는 자신의 모든 이야기를 블리필 씨에게 건넸고, 가엾은 존스에게는 하루 종일 눈길 한 번 주지 않았다.\n\n향사는 딸의 이런 처신이 어찌나 흐뭇했던지 식사를 거의 입에 대지도 못하고, 거의 온 시간을 누이에게 윙크와 끄덕임으로 흡족함의 신호를 보낼 틈을 엿보는 데 썼다. 그런데 누이는 처음에는 자신이 본 것이 오라비만큼 그렇게 마냥 흡족하지만은 않았다.\n\n요컨대 소피아가 자신의 배역을 어찌나 과하게 연기했던지, 고모는 처음에는 흠칫하며 조카에게 어떤 꾸밈이 있는 것은 아닌가 의심하기 시작했다. 그러나 그녀 자신이 술수에 능한 여인이었던 터라, 곧 이것을 소피아의 극도의 술수 탓으로 돌렸다. 그녀는 조카가 사랑에 빠졌다는 것을 두고 자신이 던졌던 숱한 암시를 떠올리고는, 이 처녀가 과장된 예의로 자신을 놀려 그 짐작을 물리치려고 이 방법을 택한 것이라고 상상했다. 이 짐작은 그 모든 처신에 곁들여진 지나친 명랑함으로 인해 한층 더 굳어졌다. 우리는 여기서 한 가지 짚고 넘어가지 않을 수 없다. 만일 소피아가 그로스브너 광장의 공기 속에서 십 년을 살았더라면 이 추측은 한결 근거가 탄탄했으리라는 점이다. 그곳의 젊은 처녀들은 런던에서 백 마일이나 떨어진 숲과 작은 숲에서는 무척이나 진지한 일인 그 연정을 놀리고 가지고 노는 놀라운 재주를 정말로 익히기 때문이다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 남의 속임수를 알아채는 데 있어서는, 이렇게 표현해도 좋다면, 우리 자신의 술수가 그들의 것과 같은 가락에 맞춰 감겨 있는 것이 무척 중요하다. 매우 술수에 능한 사람들도 때로는 남들이 실제보다 더 현명하다고, 다시 말해 더 큰 악당이라고 지레짐작하다가 일을 그르치기 때문이다. 이 통찰이 제법 깊은 만큼, 나는 이를 다음의 짧은 이야기로 풀어 보이겠다. 시골 사람 셋이 윌트셔 출신 도둑 하나를 쫓아 브렌트퍼드를 지나고 있었다. 그중 가장 단순한 자가 어느 간판 아래 \"윌트셔 하우스\"라고 쓰인 것을 보고는, 들어가 보자고 동료들에게 권했다. 거기라면 십중팔구 자기네 고향 사람을 찾을 수 있으리라는 것이었다. 좀 더 영리한 둘째는 이 어수룩함을 비웃었다. 그러나 한층 더 영리한 셋째가 대답했다. \"그래도 들어가 보세. 그자는 우리가 설마 제 고향 사람들 틈에 끼어 있으리라고는 의심하지 않을 거라 여길지도 모르니.\" 그래서 그들은 들어가 그 집을 뒤졌고, 그 통에 도둑을 따라잡을 기회를 놓쳤다. 그 도둑은 그때 그들 바로 앞쪽 멀지 않은 곳에 있었는데, 그들 모두가 알면서도 단 한 번도 떠올리지 못했던 사실이지만, 글을 읽을 줄 모르는 자였던 것이다.\n\n독자는 이토록 값진 비밀이 전해지는 이 여담을 너그러이 보아 줄 것이다. 어떤 노름꾼이든, 상대의 패를 거꾸로 무너뜨리려면 그 수를 정확히 아는 것이 얼마나 긴요한지 동의할 테니 말이다. 더구나 이는 흔히 보듯 더 영리한 자가 어째서 더 어수룩한 자에게 속아 넘어가는지, 또 어째서 그토록 많은 단순하고 순진한 사람들이 그렇게나 흔히 오해받고 잘못 비쳐지는지에 대한 까닭도 제공해 줄 것이다. 그러나 무엇보다 중요한 것은, 이것이 소피아가 자신의 빈틈없는 고모에게 씌운 그 속임수를 설명해 준다는 점이다.\n\n식사가 끝나고 일행이 정원으로 물러나자, 누이가 들려준 말이 틀림없다고 철석같이 믿은 웨스턴 씨는 올워디 씨를 한쪽으로 데려가, 소피아와 젊은 블리필 씨의 혼사를 아주 무뚝뚝하게 제안했다.\n\n올워디 씨는 세속적 이익에 대한 예기치 못한 갑작스러운 소식에 가슴이 뛰는 그런 부류의 사람이 아니었다. 그의 마음은 실로 한 인간으로서, 또 한 그리스도인으로서 어울리는 그 철학으로 다스려져 있었다. 그는 모든 쾌락과 고통, 모든 기쁨과 슬픔 위에 완전히 군림하는 척하지는 않았다. 그러나 동시에 우연히 부는 바람마다, 운명의 미소나 찡그림마다 흔들리고 헝클어질 사람도 아니었다. 그리하여 그는 웨스턴 씨의 제안을 겉으로 드러나는 동요 하나 없이, 표정의 변화 하나 없이 받아들였다. 그는 이 혼인이 자신이 진심으로 바라던 바라고 말했고, 이어 그 처녀의 덕에 대한 매우 정당한 찬사를 늘어놓았다. 또 재산의 측면에서 이 제안이 유리함을 인정했고, 자신의 조카를 좋게 보아 준 데 대해 웨스턴 씨에게 감사를 표한 뒤, 만일 젊은이들이 서로 마음에 들어 한다면 자신도 이 일을 매듭짓기를 몹시 바란다고 결론지었다.\n\n웨스턴은 올워디 씨의 대답이 자신이 기대한 만큼 따뜻하지 않아 다소 실망했다. 그는 젊은이들이 서로 마음에 들어 할지 어떨지에 대한 그 의문을 크게 깔보며 이렇게 말했다. \"자식에게 알맞은 혼처를 가리는 데는 부모가 가장 훌륭한 판관이지요. 나로 말하자면 딸에게 더없이 고분고분한 복종을 요구할 작정입니다. 그리고 어떤 젊은 녀석이든 그런 잠자리 동무를 마다할 수 있다면야, 나야 그저 그의 미천한 종이외다, 부디 아무 탈도 없기를 바랄 뿐이오.\"\n\n올워디는 소피아에 대한 숱한 찬사로 이 노여움을 누그러뜨리려 애쓰며, 블리필 씨가 이 제안을 기꺼이 받아들이리라는 데 한 점 의심도 없다고 단언했다. 그러나 모두 헛수고였다. 그는 향사에게서 이런 대답밖에 얻어 내지 못했다. \"더는 말하지 않겠소. 그저 아무 탈도 없기를 삼가 바랄 뿐이오. 그게 다요.\" 그는 이 말을 헤어지기 전에 적어도 백 번은 되풀이했다.\n\n올워디는 이 이웃을 너무도 잘 아는지라 이런 처신에 기분이 상하지는 않았다. 또 그는 혼인 문제에서 어떤 부모들이 자식에게 휘두르는 그 가혹함을 몹시 못마땅히 여겨, 조카의 마음을 결코 억지로 꺾지 않으리라 다짐해 둔 터였음에도, 이 혼사의 전망에 무척 흐뭇해했다. 온 고장이 소피아의 칭송으로 떠들썩했고, 그 자신도 그녀의 마음과 인물 양쪽에 깃든 보기 드문 자질을 크게 흠모해 왔던 까닭이다.\n\n여기에 우리는 그녀의 막대한 재산에 대한 고려도 보탤 수 있으리라 생각한다. 그는 그 재산에 도취되기에는 너무 진중했으나, 또한 그것을 가벼이 여기기에는 너무 분별 있는 사람이었다.\n\n그리고 여기서, 세상의 짖어 대는 모든 비평가에게 맞서, 나는 참된 지혜에 관한 여담을 기어이 끌어들이지 않을 수 없다. 올워디 씨는 실로 선함의 본보기였던 만큼이나 이 참된 지혜의 본보기이기도 했으니 말이다.\n\n참된 지혜란, 호가스 씨가 그린 가난한 시인이 부에 맞서 무어라 써 갈겼든, 또 잘 먹고 사는 어떤 부유한 성직자가 쾌락에 맞서 무어라 설교했든, 이 둘 중 어느 것을 경멸하는 데 있지 않다. 사람은 넉넉한 재산을 누리면서도 거리의 어느 거지 못지않은 지혜를 지닐 수 있고, 또 어여쁜 아내나 마음 든든한 벗을 누리면서도, 자신의 모든 사교적 능력을 묻어 버리고 등을 호되게 매질하며 배를 굶기는 그 어떤 시큰둥한 가톨릭 은둔자 못지않게 여전히 지혜로울 수 있다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 가장 지혜로운 사람이야말로 세상의 온갖 복을 가장 높은 경지로 누릴 가능성이 크다. 지혜가 일러 주는 그 절제야말로 쓸모 있는 부에 이르는 가장 확실한 길이듯, 오직 그 절제만이 우리로 하여금 숱한 쾌락을 맛볼 자격을 갖추게 하기 때문이다. 지혜로운 사람은 온갖 욕구와 온갖 열정을 두루 채우지만, 어리석은 자는 단 하나를 물리도록 채우고 물려 버리느라 나머지 전부를 희생한다.\n\n매우 지혜로운 사람들이 소문난 탐욕가였던 적이 있다고 반박할지도 모른다. 나는 답한다. 그 점에서는 지혜롭지 않았던 것이라고. 또 가장 지혜로운 사람들이 젊은 시절 도를 넘게 쾌락을 탐했다고도 할지 모른다. 나는 답한다. 그때 그들은 지혜롭지 않았던 것이라고.\n\n요컨대 지혜란, 한 번도 그 학교에 다녀 본 적 없는 자들이 배우기 너무 어려운 것인 양 떠들어 댔지만, 다만 가장 비천한 삶에서조차 누구나 알고 따르는 단순한 격언 하나를, 그 삶이 데려가는 데서 조금 더 멀리까지 늘이는 법을 가르칠 뿐이다. 그 격언이란 바로, 너무 비싼 값을 치르고 사지 말라는 것이다.\n\n이제 누구든 이 격언을 품고 세상이라는 거대한 장터로 나아가, 명예에, 부에, 쾌락에, 그리고 그 장터가 내놓는 그 밖의 온갖 물건에 끊임없이 이를 적용하는 사람이라면, 나는 감히 단언하건대 지혜로운 사람이며, 세속적인 의미에서도 마땅히 그렇게 인정받아야 한다. 그는 가장 좋은 흥정을 해내는 셈이기 때문이다. 실로 그는 모든 것을 그저 약간의 수고라는 값만으로 사들이며, 내가 앞서 말한 온갖 좋은 것들을 집으로 가져가면서도, 남들이 그것들에 흔히 치르는 값인 자신의 건강과 순결과 명성을 고스란히 제 것으로 온전히 지켜 내기 때문이다.\n\n이 절제에서 그는 또한 자신의 인격을 완성해 주는 다른 두 가지 가르침도 배운다. 첫째, 가장 좋은 흥정을 해냈을 때 결코 도취되지 않는 것, 그리고 장터가 비었거나 그 물건들이 제 주머니로 사기에 너무 비쌀 때 결코 풀이 죽지 않는 것이다.\n\n그러나 나는 내가 무엇을 주제로 글을 쓰고 있는지 잊지 말아야 하고, 마음씨 좋은 비평가의 인내심을 너무 멀리까지 침범해서도 안 되겠다. 그러니 여기서 나는 이 장을 끝맺는다.\n\n여러 가지 흥미로운 사정이 담긴 장.\n\n올워디 씨는 집에 돌아오자마자 블리필 씨를 따로 불러, 몇 마디 운을 뗀 뒤 웨스턴 씨가 내놓은 제안을 그에게 전했다. 그러면서 동시에 이 혼사가 자신에게 얼마나 흡족한 일인지도 일러 주었다.\n\n소피아의 매력은 블리필에게 손톱만큼의 인상도 남기지 못했다. 그의 마음이 이미 다른 데 가 있어서가 아니었다. 아름다움에 전혀 둔감하다거나 여자를 싫어해서도 아니었다. 다만 그의 욕망이 본래부터 어찌나 절제되어 있던지, 그는 철학으로든 학문으로든 아니면 다른 어떤 방법으로든 그것을 손쉽게 억누를 수 있었다. 그리고 이 책 제1장에서 우리가 다룬 바 있는 그 정념으로 말하자면, 그의 됨됨이 전체를 통틀어 그 기색이라곤 티끌만큼도 없었다.\n\n하지만 우리가 거기서 다루었던, 소피아의 덕과 아름다움이 그토록 빼어난 대상을 이루었던 그 뒤섞인 정념으로부터는 완전히 자유로웠을지언정, 그는 또 다른 정념들만큼은 똑같이 넉넉히 갖추고 있었다. 그 정념들은 이 젊은 아가씨의 재산에서 더없이 충만한 만족을 약속받았다. 바로 탐욕과 야심이었으니, 이 둘은 그의 마음의 지배권을 사이좋게 나눠 가지고 있었다. 그는 이 재산을 손에 넣는 일을 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번이나 무척 탐나는 것으로 여겨 왔고, 그에 관해 막연한 속셈을 품기도 했다. 그러나 자기 자신의 젊음과 그 아가씨의 어린 나이, 그리고 무엇보다도 웨스턴 씨가 재혼하여 자식을 더 둘지도 모른다는 생각이 그를 너무 성급하거나 조급한 추구에서 붙들어 두었다.\n\n이 마지막이자 가장 중대한 걸림돌이 이제 상당 부분 사라졌으니, 제안이 다름 아닌 웨스턴 씨 본인에게서 나왔기 때문이다. 그리하여 블리필은 아주 잠깐 망설인 뒤 올워디 씨에게 대답하기를, 혼인이란 자기가 아직 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없는 주제이지만, 숙부의 다정하고도 아버지 같은 보살핌을 너무도 깊이 느끼고 있으니 모든 일에서 그분의 뜻에 따르겠노라고 했다.\n\n올워디는 본디 혈기 있는 사람이었고, 지금의 진중함도 천성에 깃든 무덤덤함에서가 아니라 참된 지혜와 철학에서 비롯된 것이었다. 젊은 시절에는 가슴속에 불길을 많이 품었던 사람이었고, 사랑으로 아름다운 여인과 결혼하기도 했으니 말이다. 그래서 그는 조카의 이런 미적지근한 대답이 그리 흡족하지 않았다. 그는 소피아에 대한 칭찬을 늘어놓지 않을 수 없었고, 젊은이의 마음이 그런 매력의 힘에도 끄떡없다는 것이 좀 의아하다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 다만 그 마음이 이미 다른 정으로 단단히 지켜지고 있는 게 아니라면 말이다.\n\n블리필은 자기에게 그런 방패 따위는 없다고 숙부를 안심시켰다. 그러고는 사랑과 결혼에 관하여 어찌나 지혜롭고 경건하게 늘어놓았던지, 자기 숙부보다 신앙심이 훨씬 옅은 부모라 해도 그 입을 다물게 할 정도였다. 결국 이 선량한 분은 흡족한 마음으로 이렇게 믿게 되었다. 조카는 소피아에게 아무런 거부감도 없을뿐더러, 오히려 그녀를 깍듯이 존경하는데, 이런 존경이야말로 진중하고 덕 있는 마음에서는 우정과 사랑의 든든한 주춧돌이라고 말이다. 또한 그는 머지않아 이 구혼자가 그 연인에게도 똑같이 마음에 드는 사람이 되리라 의심치 않았기에, 이토록 합당하고 바람직한 결합으로 모든 이에게 큰 행복이 찾아오리라 내다보았다. 그리하여 블리필 씨의 동의를 얻어 이튿날 아침 웨스턴 씨에게 편지를 썼다. 조카가 그 제안을 무척 고맙고 기쁘게 받아들였으며, 아가씨가 그의 방문을 기꺼이 받아들이고자 하실 때면 언제든 찾아뵐 준비가 되어 있다는 내용이었다.\n\n웨스턴은 이 편지에 무척 기뻐하며 곧바로 답장을 보냈다. 그 답장에서 그는 딸에게는 한마디 말도 꺼내지 않은 채, 바로 그날 오후를 구애의 막을 여는 시각으로 정해 버렸다.\n\n이 심부름꾼을 떠나보내자마자 그는 누이를 찾아 나섰는데, 마침 누이는 서플 목사에게 《가제트》를 읽어 주며 풀이해 주고 있었다. 그는 입을 열기까지 그 풀이를 거의 십오 분이나 들어 주어야 했으니, 본래 다혈질인 그에게는 여간 고역이 아니었다. 그래도 마침내 그는 누이에게 더없이 중대한 용건이 있노라 알릴 기회를 잡았고, 그러자 누이가 대답했다. \"오라버니, 저는 온전히 오라버니께 마음을 쏟고 있어요. 북쪽 정세가 어찌나 좋게 돌아가는지, 제 기분이 이보다 더 좋았던 적이 없답니다.\"\n\n그러자 목사가 물러갔고, 웨스턴은 누이에게 그간의 일을 모조리 알린 뒤 그 일을 소피아에게 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 누이는 기꺼이, 그리고 흔쾌히 그 일을 맡았다. 어쩌면 그 오라버니는 누이를 그토록 들뜨게 한 그 흐뭇한 북쪽 정세 덕을 조금 본 셈이었으니, 덕분에 그는 자기 처사에 대한 잔소리를 한마디도 듣지 않을 수 있었다. 그의 처사란 분명 다소 지나치게 성급하고 거칠었으니 말이다.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n소피아는 자기 방에서 책을 읽고 있었는데, 그때 고모가 들어왔다. 웨스턴 부인을 보는 순간 소피아가 어찌나 황급히 책을 덮던지, 마음씨 고운 부인은 그토록 보여 주기를 꺼리는 듯한 그 책이 무엇이냐고 묻지 않을 수 없었다. \"정말이지, 고모님,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"이 책은 제가 읽었다는 걸 부끄러워하지도, 두려워하지도 않을 책이에요. 한 젊은 숙녀가 쓴 작품인데, 제 생각엔 그분의 빼어난 식견은 여성에게 영예가 되고, 그분의 어진 마음은 인간 본성에 영예가 됩니다.\" 그러자 웨스턴 부인이 책을 집어 들었다가 곧바로 내던지며 말했다. \"그래, 작가는 아주 좋은 집안 출신이지. 하지만 우리가 아는 사람들 사이에는 별로 끼지 못하는 사람이야. 나는 읽어 본 적이 없어. 안목 있는 사람들 말로는 별 내용이 없다더구나.\" \"고모님, 저는 감히 안목 있는 분들에 맞서 제 의견을 내세울 수는 없어요.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"하지만 제게는 그 안에 인간 본성이 가득해 보였고, 여러 대목에서 어찌나 진실한 다정함과 섬세함이 깃들어 있던지, 그 때문에 여러 번 눈물을 흘렸어요.\" \"아니, 그래 너는 우는 걸 좋아하느냐?\" 고모가 말했다. \"저는 마음을 울리는 느낌을 사랑해요,\" 조카가 대답했다. \"그걸 위해서라면 언제든 눈물 한 방울쯤은 기꺼이 치르겠어요.\" \"그래, 그건 그렇고, 어디 보자,\" 고모가 말했다. \"내가 들어왔을 때 읽고 있던 게 뭐였니. 거기엔 무언가 아주 다정한 게 있었을 것 같구나. 그리고 아주 애틋한 것도 말이야. 얼굴을 붉히는구나, 소피아야. 아! 얘야, 너는 위선을 좀 가르쳐 줄 만한 책을, 네 속마음을 조금 더 잘 감추는 법을 일러 줄 책을 읽어야겠다.\" \"고모님, 저는 드러내기 부끄러워해야 할 생각 따위는 없는 것 같아요.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"부끄러워해? 아니지,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"나는 네게 부끄러워해야 할 생각이 있다고는 보지 않아. 그런데도, 얘야, 방금 내가 '애틋하다'는 말을 꺼냈을 때 너는 얼굴을 붉혔지. 소피야, 명심하렴. 내가 훤히 알지 못하는 생각은 네게 하나도 없단다. 우리가 어떤 일을 실행에 옮기기 한참 전부터 프랑스 사람들이 우리 움직임을 다 알고 있는 것만큼이나 환히 알고 있어. 얘야, 네가 아버지는 속일 수 있었다고 해서 나까지 속일 수 있을 거라 생각했니? 어제 네가 블리필 씨에게 그 우정을 그토록 과장해 보인 까닭을 내가 모를 줄 알았어? 나는 세상을 너무 많이 봐서 그렇게 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니란다. 아니, 아니, 또 얼굴 붉히지 마라. 그건 부끄러워할 필요 없는 감정이라고 내가 말하잖니. 그건 나 자신도 인정하는 감정이고, 이미 네 아버지도 그걸 인정하도록 끌어들였단다. 정말이지, 나는 오로지 네 마음만을 헤아리고 있어. 가능하기만 하다면 나는 언제나 그것을 채워 주고 싶거든. 비록 더 높은 가망을 희생하는 한이 있더라도 말이야. 자, 네 영혼을 더없이 기쁘게 할 소식이 있단다. 나를 네 속내를 털어놓을 사람으로 삼으렴. 그러면 네가 바라는 그 끝까지 행복해지도록 내가 책임지마.\" \"어머, 고모님,\" 소피아가 평생 어느 때보다도 어리석어 보이는 표정으로 말했다. \"뭐라 말해야 할지 모르겠어요. 어째서, 고모님, 무얼 의심하시는 거예요?\" \"아니, 거짓은 안 된다.\" 웨스턴 부인이 받아쳤다. \"생각해 보렴, 너는 같은 여자에게, 고모에게 말하고 있어. 그리고 나는 네가 벗에게 말하고 있다는 걸 확신하길 바란다. 생각해 보렴, 너는 내가 이미 알고 있는 것을, 어제 네가 둘러쓴 그 온갖 위장 중에서도 가장 교묘한 위장을 꿰뚫어 내가 똑똑히 본 것을 내게 털어놓고 있을 뿐이야. 세상을 완벽하게 알지 못하는 사람이라면 누구라도 속아 넘어갔을 그런 위장 말이다. 끝으로, 그것이 내가 크게 인정하는 감정이라는 걸 생각하렴.\" \"어머, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"이렇게 갑작스럽게, 느닷없이 들이대시니. 분명, 고모님, 저도 눈이 멀지는 않았어요. 그리고 정말로, 인간의 온갖 완벽함이 한데 모인 것을 보는 게 잘못이라면 모르겠지만, 아버지와 고모님이, 정말 제 눈으로 보시는 것과 같이 보실 수 있을까요?\" \"내 말하잖니,\" 고모가 대답했다. \"우리는 전적으로 인정한다고. 그리고 바로 오늘 오후로 네 아버지가 네가 네 연인을 맞이할 시간을 정해 두셨어.\" \"아버지가, 오늘 오후에요!\" 소피아가 외쳤다. 그 얼굴에서 핏기가 확 가셨다. \"그래, 얘야,\" 고모가 말했다. \"오늘 오후란다. 너도 내 오라버니의 급한 성미를 알잖니. 네가 들판에서 기절했던 그날 저녁, 내가 네게서 처음 발견한 그 감정을 오라버니에게 알려 드렸지. 나는 네가 기절할 때 그걸 봤어. 네가 정신을 차리자마자 곧바로 봤지. 그날 저녁 만찬 자리에서도 봤고, 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서도 봤단다(얘야, 나는 세상을 봐 온 사람이라니까). 그래, 내가 오라버니에게 알리자마자 곧장 올워디 씨에게 그 얘기를 꺼내려 하셨지. 어제 그걸 제안했고, 올워디 씨가 승낙했어(물론 기쁘게 그러셨을 게 분명하지). 그러니 오늘 오후, 내 말하잖니, 너는 네 가장 멋진 자태를 다 갖추어 입어야 한단다.\"\n\n\"오늘 오후에요!\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"고모님, 저를 정신이 나가도록 겁주시네요.\" \"오, 얘야,\" 고모가 말했다. \"너는 곧 다시 제정신으로 돌아올 거란다. 그는 매력적인 젊은이거든, 그게 사실이니까.\" \"아니, 인정해요,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그만한 완벽함을 갖춘 사람은 저도 알지 못해요. 그렇게 용감하면서도 그렇게 부드럽고, 그렇게 재치 있으면서도 그렇게 남의 마음 상하게 하지 않고, 그렇게 인정 많고, 그렇게 예의 바르고, 그렇게 품위 있고, 그렇게 잘생겼으니! 이런 자질들에 견주면 그가 천하게 태어났다는 게 무슨 대수겠어요?\" \"천하게 태어나? 그게 무슨 말이냐?\" 고모가 말했다. \"블리필 씨가 천하게 태어나다니!\" 소피아는 그 이름에 순간 새파랗게 질려 가냘프게 그 이름을 되뇌었다. 그러자 고모가 외쳤다. \"블리필 씨 말이다. 그래, 블리필 씨, 우리가 그 말고 누구 얘기를 하고 있었겠니?\" \"맙소사,\" 소피아가 금방이라도 쓰러질 듯이 대답했다. \"저는 존스 씨 얘긴 줄 알았어요. 그만한 자격을 갖춘 다른 사람은 정말 알지 못하는걸요.\" \"맙소사,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"이번엔 네가 나를 겁주는구나. 네 애정의 대상이 블리필 씨가 아니라 존스 씨란 말이냐?\" \"블리필 씨라고요!\" 소피아가 되뇌었다. \"정말 진심이실 리는 없겠죠. 만약 진심이시라면, 저는 살아 있는 여자 중에 가장 비참한 여자예요.\" 웨스턴 부인은 이제 몇 순간 말없이 서 있었고, 그동안 두 눈에서는 불같은 분노의 불꽃이 번쩍였다. 마침내 목소리의 온 힘을 끌어모아 다음과 같이 또렷한 소리로 우레같이 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그래, 네가 사생아 따위와 짝을 맺어 네 가문을 욕보일 생각을 할 수 있단 말이냐? 웨스턴 가의 핏줄이 그런 오염을 받아들일 수 있어? 그런 끔찍한 마음을 억누를 만한 분별이 네게 없다 하더라도, 나는 우리 가문의 자긍심만큼은 네가 그토록 천한 애정에 손톱만큼의 부추김도 주지 못하게 막아 주리라 여겼다. 더더욱 네가 그것을 내 면전에 대고 인정할 만한 뻔뻔함을 가지리라고는 상상조차 못 했어.\"\n\"고모님,\" 소피아가 떨면서 대답했다. \"제가 말씀드린 건 고모님이 제게서 억지로 끌어내신 거예요. 저는 전에 누구에게도 존스 씨의 이름을 좋게 입에 올린 기억이 없어요. 그리고 그분이 고모님의 인정을 받고 있다고 여기지 않았더라면 지금도 그러지 않았을 거예요. 그 가엾고 불행한 젊은이에 대한 제 마음이 어떠했든, 저는 그것을 무덤까지 가지고 갈 작정이었어요. 이제 보니 제가 안식을 찾아야 할 곳은 오직 그 무덤뿐이군요.\" 여기서 그녀는 눈물에 잠긴 채 의자에 무너지듯 주저앉았고, 말로 다 할 수 없는 슬픔이 자아내는 그 가슴 저미는 침묵 속에서, 거의 가장 모진 마음이라도 움직였을 광경을 자아냈다.\n\n그러나 이 애틋한 슬픔도 고모에게는 아무런 연민을 불러일으키지 못했다. 오히려 그녀는 이제 더없이 격렬한 분노에 빠져들었다. \"나라면 차라리,\" 그녀가 더없이 사나운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"네가 그런 혼인으로 너 자신과 가문을 욕보이는 꼴을 보느니, 너를 무덤까지 따라가는 편을 택하겠다. 오 하늘이시여! 내 조카가 그런 작자에게 연정을 품었노라 선언하는 걸 들으려고 내가 이렇게 살아 있었다니, 내 어찌 짐작이나 했겠느냐? 네가 처음이야. 그래, 웨스턴 아가씨, 네가 그 성을 가진 사람 중에 그토록 비천한 생각을 품은 첫 번째 사람이다. 여인들의 분별로 그토록 이름난 가문에서.\" 여기서 그녀는 족히 십오 분을 내리 떠들어 댔고, 마침내 분노가 아니라 숨이 다 빠져 버린 끝에, 당장 가서 오라버니에게 알리겠다는 위협으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n그러자 소피아는 고모의 발치에 몸을 던지고는 그 손을 붙잡고, 자기에게서 끌어낸 그것을 부디 숨겨 달라고 눈물로 빌었다. 그녀는 아버지 성미의 격렬함을 들어 호소하며, 자기 마음이 어떻든 결코 아버지를 화나게 할 어떤 일도 하도록 이끌지 못할 거라고 다짐했다.\n웨스턴 부인은 잠시 서서 그녀를 바라보다가, 마음을 추스른 뒤 이렇게 말했다. \"오직 한 가지 조건이라면 이 비밀을 오라버니에게 알리지 않겠다. 그 조건이란, 소피아가 바로 오늘 오후에 블리필 씨를 연인으로 맞이하고, 그를 장차 자기 남편이 될 사람으로 여기겠노라 약속하는 것이다.\"\n\n가엾은 소피아는 고모의 손아귀에 너무 깊이 잡혀 있어 어떤 것도 딱 잘라 거절할 수가 없었다. 그녀는 블리필 씨를 만나고 가능한 한 그에게 예의 바르게 대하겠노라 약속할 수밖에 없었다. 다만 혼사를 서두르지는 말아 달라고 고모에게 빌었다. 그녀가 말했다. \"블리필 씨는 제게 결코 마음에 드는 사람이 아니에요. 그리고 아버지가 저를 세상에서 가장 비참한 여자로 만들지 않도록 설득되시길 바라요.\"\n웨스턴 부인은 그녀에게 단언했다. \"이 혼사는 완전히 합의된 거고, 어떤 것도 그것을 막을 수 없고 막아서도 안 된다. 나도 인정하마,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 이걸 대수롭지 않은 일로 봤어. 아니, 어쩌면 전에는 약간 꺼림칙한 데가 있었지만, 이게 네 마음에도 무척 흡족하리라 여기게 되면서 실은 그게 다 가셨단다. 하지만 이제는 이걸 세상에서 가장 바람직한 일로 여긴다. 그리고 내가 막을 수 있는 한, 이 일에는 한 순간도 허비되지 않을 거야.\"\n\n소피아가 대답했다. \"적어도 미루는 것만은, 고모님, 고모님의 인자함과 아버지의 인자함 양쪽에서 기대할 수 있겠지요. 분명 제게 시간을 주셔서, 지금 이 사람에게 품고 있는 이토록 강한 거부감을 이겨 내도록 애써 볼 수 있게 해 주실 거예요.\"\n\n고모가 대답했다. \"나는 세상을 너무 많이 알아서 그렇게 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니다. 다른 남자가 네 애정을 차지하고 있는 걸 아는 이상, 나는 웨스턴 씨를 설득해 혼사를 최대한 서두르게 하겠다. 정말이지,\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"적군이 코앞에 와서 포위를 풀어 줄 위험이 있는데 공성을 질질 끄는 건 어리석은 전략이지. 안 돼, 안 돼, 소피야,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네가 명예롭게는 결코 채울 수 없는 격렬한 정념을 품고 있다고 확신하니, 나는 네 명예를 가문의 근심거리에서 벗어나게 하려고 할 수 있는 건 다 하겠다. 네가 결혼하고 나면 그런 일들은 오로지 네 남편이 헤아릴 몫이 될 테니 말이야. 얘야, 나는 네가 언제나 너답게 처신할 만큼 분별을 갖추길 바란다. 하지만 설령 그러지 못하더라도, 결혼은 숱한 여인을 파멸에서 구해 왔단다.\"\n\n소피아는 고모의 뜻을 잘 알아들었지만, 거기에 답하는 것이 적절치 않다고 여겼다. 그렇지만 그녀는 블리필 씨를 만나, 할 수 있는 한 예의 바르게 그를 대하기로 마음을 굳혔다. 오직 그 조건 아래서만, 웨스턴 부인의 무슨 계략이라기보다 자신의 불운이 불행히도 그녀에게서 끌어낸 그 호감을 비밀로 지켜 주겠다는 약속을 고모에게서 얻어 냈기 때문이다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n소피아와 아너 부인 사이의 대화를 담은 장으로, 앞 장면이 마음씨 고운 독자의 마음에 불러일으켰을 애틋한 감정을 조금이나마 누그러뜨려 줄지도 모른다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 앞 장에서 본 대로 조카에게서 약속을 받아낸 뒤 물러갔고, 곧이어 아너 부인이 들어왔다. 그녀는 옆방에서 일을 하고 있었는데, 앞선 대화에서 터져 나온 고함 소리에 이끌려 열쇠 구멍으로 불려갔고, 그 대화가 끝날 때까지 거기에 붙어 있었던 것이다. 방으로 들어선 그녀는 소피아가 눈에서 눈물을 뚝뚝 흘리며 꼼짝 않고 서 있는 모습을 보았다. 그러자 그녀는 곧장 자기 눈에도 알맞은 양의 눈물을 불러내고는 입을 열었다. \"어머나 세상에, 아씨, 무슨 일이세요?\"—\"아무것도 아니야.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"아무것도 아니라니요! 아이고 아씨!\" 아너가 대답했다. \"아씨께서 이 지경이 되시고, 아씨와 웨스턴 마님 사이에 그런 한바탕 말이 오간 마당에, 그런 말씀으로 저를 속이시면 안 되지요.\"—\"성가시게 굴지 마.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"아무 일도 없다니까. 아, 하느님! 나는 왜 태어났을까?\"—\"아니에요, 아씨.\" 아너 부인이 말했다. \"아씨께서 아무 까닭도 없이 그렇게 슬퍼하신다고는 저를 결코 설득하지 못하실 거예요. 물론 저야 한낱 하인에 불과하지만, 그래도 저는 늘 아씨께 충성을 다해 왔고, 또 제 목숨을 바쳐서라도 아씨를 모시고 싶은 사람이에요.\"—\"내 사랑하는 아너야.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"네가 나에게 도움이 될 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없단다. 나는 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장이 났어.\"—\"맙소사, 그런 말씀 마세요!\" 시중드는 여인이 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 아씨께 아무 도움이 못 된다 하더라도, 부디 말씀해 주세요, 아씨—사연을 아는 것만으로도 저에게는 조금이나마 위로가 될 테니—제발, 아씨, 무슨 일인지 말씀해 주세요.\"—\"아버지가.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"내가 경멸하고 또 증오하는 남자에게 나를 시집보내려 하셔.\"—\"아이고, 아씨.\" 다른 여인이 대답했다. \"그 못된 남자가 대체 누구예요? 분명 아주 나쁜 사람일 테지요. 안 그러면 아씨께서 그를 경멸하실 리가 없으니까요.\"—\"그 이름은 내 혀에 독이나 마찬가지야.\" 소피아가 받았다. \"너도 머지않아 알게 될 테지.\" 사실 솔직히 말하자면 그녀는 이미 그 이름을 알고 있었고, 그래서 그 점에 대해서는 그리 캐묻지 않았다. 그러고는 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"한낱 하인 주제에 아씨께 무슨 충고를 드리겠다는 건 아니에요. 그런 거야 아씨께서 저보다 훨씬 잘 아시니까요. 하지만, 정말이지! 영국의 어떤 아버지도 제 동의 없이 저를 시집보낼 수는 없을 거예요. 또 분명 나리께서는 워낙 인자하신 분이라, 아씨가 그 젊은이를 경멸하고 증오한다는 걸 아시기만 한다면, 분명 아씨더러 그 사람과 결혼하라고 바라지 않으실 거예요. 아씨께서 제게 그 말씀을 나리께 전하라고 허락만 해 주신다면요. 물론 아씨 입으로 직접 말씀하시는 게 더 마땅하겠지만, 아씨께서 그 더러운 이름으로 혀를 더럽히고 싶어 하지 않으시니—\"—\"네가 잘못 알고 있어, 아너야.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"아버지는 그 이야기를 나에게 꺼내기로 마음먹기도 전에 이미 결심을 굳히셨어.\"—\"그렇다면 더더욱 부끄러운 일이지요.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"잠자리를 함께해야 하는 건 나리가 아니라 아씨인데 말이에요. 어떤 남자가 아무리 번듯한 사람이라 한들, 여자라고 다들 그를 똑같이 잘생겼다 여기는 건 아니잖아요. 분명 우리 나리께서 제 머리로 혼자 이런 식으로 일을 처리하실 분은 아니에요. 어떤 사람들은 제 일에만 신경을 쓰면 좋으련만. 제 처지였다면 그렇게 당하는 걸 좋아하지 않을 텐데 말이지요. 저야 비록 처녀지만, 모든 남자가 다 똑같이 마음에 드는 건 아니라는 걸 쉽게 헤아릴 수 있거든요. 아씨께서 가장 잘생겼다 여기는 남자로 마음을 채울 수 없다면, 그 큰 재산이 다 무슨 소용이겠어요? 글쎄요, 저야 아무 말 않겠어요. 하지만 어떤 사람들이 더 좋은 집안에서 태어나지 못한 게 안타까운 일이긴 하지요. 아니, 사실 그 점이라면 저 같으면 개의치 않겠어요. 다만 그러면 돈이 그리 많지 않다는 게 문제인데, 그게 또 뭐 어때서요? 아씨께는 두 사람 몫의 재산이 넉넉히 있으니, 그 재산을 어디다 그보다 더 잘 쓰시겠어요? 분명 누구나 인정할 거예요. 그분이야말로 세상에서 가장 잘생기고, 가장 매력 있고, 가장 멋지고, 가장 키 크고, 가장 번듯한 남자라고요.\"—\"네가 나에게 무슨 뜻으로 그렇게 떠들어 대는 거니?\" 소피아가 자못 엄숙한 얼굴로 외쳤다. \"내가 언제 너한테 이런 무례를 부려도 좋다고 허락이라도 했더냐?\"—\"아니에요, 아씨, 용서하세요. 나쁜 뜻은 없었어요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 정말이지, 오늘 아침에 그 가엾은 분을 본 뒤로 줄곧 그분이 제 머릿속을 떠나지 않았어요. 분명 아씨께서도 방금 그분을 보셨더라면 가엾게 여기실 수밖에 없으셨을 거예요. 가엾은 분! 그분께 무슨 불행한 일이라도 생기지 않았으면 좋겠어요. 오늘 아침 내내 팔짱을 끼고 이리저리 거닐면서 어찌나 우울한 얼굴을 하고 계셨던지요. 정말이지, 그분을 보고 있자니 저도 거의 울 뻔했다니까요.\"—\"누구를 봤다는 거니?\" 소피아가 말했다. \"가엾은 존스 씨요.\" 아너가 대답했다. \"그분을 봤다고! 아니, 어디서 봤는데?\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"운하 옆에서요, 아씨.\" 아너가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 내내 거기를 거닐다가, 마침내 거기에 몸을 누이셨어요. 아마 지금도 거기 누워 계실 거예요. 정말이지, 제가 처녀로서의 부끄러움만 없었다면, 가서 말이라도 걸었을 거예요. 아씨, 제가 가서 그분이 아직도 거기 계신지, 그냥 궁금해서 한번 보고 와도 될까요?\"—\"흥!\" 소피아가 말했다. \"거기라니! 안 돼, 안 돼. 그 사람이 거기서 뭘 한다고? 분명 지금쯤은 벌써 갔을 거야. 게다가, 왜—뭐 하러—네가 왜 가서 본다는 거니? 게다가 너한테 다른 시킬 일이 있어. 가서 내 모자와 장갑을 가져오너라. 저녁 식사 전에 고모님과 함께 숲을 거닐어야겠다.\" 아너는 시킨 대로 곧장 했고, 소피아는 모자를 썼다. 그러다 거울을 들여다보니 모자를 묶은 리본이 자기에게 어울리지 않는 것 같아, 하녀를 다시 보내 다른 색깔의 리본을 가져오게 했다. 그러고는 아너 부인에게, 그 일이 무척 급하니 무슨 일이 있어도 손을 놓아서는 안 되며 바로 그날 안에 끝내야 한다고 거듭 당부한 뒤, 숲으로 간다는 둥 무언가를 더 중얼거리고는, 그와는 반대 방향으로 빠져나가, 가냘프게 떨리는 다리가 데려다줄 수 있는 한 빠른 걸음으로 곧장 운하를 향해 걸어갔다.\n\n존스는 아너 부인이 말한 대로 거기에 있었다. 사실 그는 그날 아침 그곳에서 소피아를 생각하며 우울한 명상에 잠겨 두 시간을 보냈고, 그녀가 한쪽 문으로 정원에 들어선 바로 그 순간 다른 쪽 문으로 정원을 빠져나가 버린 것이다. 그리하여 리본을 바꾸느라 흘려보낸 그 불운한 몇 분이, 이번에는 두 연인이 만나는 것을 막아 버리고 말았다. 참으로 불행한 우연이니, 나의 아리따운 여성 독자들이라면 여기서 더없이 유익한 교훈 하나를 어김없이 길어 올릴 것이다. 그리고 여기서 나는 모든 남성 비평가들에게, 오직 숙녀들을 위해서만 이야기했고 또 오직 숙녀들만이 마음대로 논평할 수 있는 이 정황에 함부로 끼어드는 것을 엄히 금하는 바이다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n의례적인 구애를 작게 축소하여 마땅히 그려져야 할 모습대로 그린 그림 한 폭, 그리고 한층 부드러운 종류의 한 장면을 전신상으로 그린 그림.\n\n불행은 홀로 오지 않는다고 어떤 이가(어쩌면 여러 사람이) 절묘하게 말한 바 있다. 이 지혜로운 격언은 이제 소피아에게서 그대로 확인되었으니, 그녀는 사랑하는 남자를 만나리라는 기대가 어긋났을 뿐 아니라, 미워하는 남자의 방문을 맞기 위해 차려입어야 하는 곤욕까지 치르게 되었던 것이다.\n\n그날 오후, 웨스턴 씨는 처음으로 딸에게 자신의 뜻을 알렸다. 그러면서 이미 누이에게서 그 이야기를 들었으리라는 것을 잘 안다고 말했다. 이 말에 소피아는 몹시 어두운 얼굴을 했고, 진주알 같은 눈물 몇 방울이 슬며시 눈에 고이는 것을 막을 수가 없었다. \"자, 자,\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"처녀티 내는 짓은 그만두려무나. 나는 다 안다. 단언컨대 누이가 내게 전부 다 말해 주었으니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 정말,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"고모께서 벌써 저를 일러바치셨단 말씀인가요?\"—\"그래, 그렇다.\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"일러바쳤다고! 그래. 아니, 너는 어제 저녁 식사 자리에서 네 스스로 다 드러내지 않았느냐. 내가 보기엔 네 마음을 아주 또렷이 보여 주었어. 하지만 너희 젊은 처녀들이란 자기가 무엇을 바라는지조차 모른단 말이지. 그래, 사랑하는 남자에게 너를 시집보내려 하니까 운다는 게냐! 내 기억에 네 어미도 꼭 이런 식으로 훌쩍이고 칭얼댔지. 하지만 우리가 혼인하고 스물네 시간도 안 되어 다 끝나 버렸다. 블리필 군은 활기찬 청년이니 네 까탈스러움쯤은 금세 끝장내 줄 게야. 자, 기운 내라, 기운 내. 나는 그가 곧 올 줄 알고 있다.\"\n\n소피아는 이제 고모가 자신에게 명예롭게 처신했음을 확신하게 되었다. 그래서 그 불쾌한 오후를 되도록 굳센 마음으로 견뎌 내되, 아버지에게 추호의 의심도 사지 않으리라 마음먹었다.\n\n블리필 씨가 곧 도착했고, 얼마 지나지 않아 웨스턴 씨가 물러나며 젊은 두 사람만 함께 남겨 두었다.\n\n여기서 거의 십오 분에 가까운 긴 침묵이 이어졌다. 대화를 시작해야 할 신사가 부끄럼이라는, 어울리지 않는 그 수줍음을 온통 짊어지고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 여러 번 입을 떼려 했고, 그때마다 말이 막 터져 나오려는 바로 그 순간 다시 삼켜 버렸다. 마침내 억지스럽고 과장된 찬사가 봇물 터지듯 쏟아져 나왔고, 그녀 쪽에서는 내리뜬 눈길과 어정쩡한 목례, 그리고 예의 바른 단음절로 그것에 응했다. 블리필은 여자를 다루는 법에 미숙한 데다 제 자신에 대한 자만이 가득했던 터라, 이 태도를 자신의 구애에 대한 정숙한 동의로 받아들였다. 그래서 더는 견딜 수 없는 그 자리를 줄이려고 소피아가 일어나 방을 나가자, 그것마저도 한낱 수줍음 탓으로 돌리며, 곧 그녀와 충분히 함께할 수 있으리라 스스로를 위안했다.\n\n실제로 그는 자신의 성공 전망에 더없이 만족하고 있었다. 낭만적인 연인들이 요구하는, 연인의 마음을 온전히 절대적으로 차지한다는 것 따위는 애초에 그의 머릿속에 떠오른 적도 없었다. 그가 바라는 것은 오로지 그녀의 재산과 그녀의 몸뿐이었으며, 그 절대적 소유권을 곧 얻으리라는 데 그는 조금의 의심도 없었다. 웨스턴 씨의 마음이 이 혼사에 그토록 간절히 기울어 있었기 때문이요, 또 소피아가 늘 아버지의 뜻에 기꺼이 바치는 그 엄정한 복종, 그리고 필요하다면 아버지가 강요해 낼 더욱 큰 복종을 그가 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 따라서 이 권위에 더하여, 자기 자신의 인품과 말솜씨에 있다고 믿는 매력까지 곁들여진다면, 그 마음이 전적으로 자유롭다고 의심치 않는 젊은 숙녀에게 성공하지 못할 리 없다고 그는 생각했다.\n\n존스에 대해서는 그가 분명 일말의 질투조차 품지 않았으니, 나는 그가 그러지 않았다는 것이 종종 놀랍게 여겨졌다. 어쩌면 그는 존스가 온 고장에 떨치고 있는 평판—잉글랜드에서 가장 거친 사내 가운데 하나라는—이(그것이 얼마나 정당한지는 독자의 판단에 맡긴다) 더없이 모범적으로 정숙한 숙녀에게 그를 혐오스러운 존재로 만들리라 상상했는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 그들이 다 함께 자리할 때 보였던 소피아와 존스 자신의 태도가 그의 의심을 잠재웠는지도 모른다. 마지막으로, 그리고 사실 무엇보다도, 그는 이 일에 또 다른 자아 같은 것은 없다고 굳게 믿었다. 그는 존스를 속속들이 안다고 여겼으며, 사실 그의 분별력을 크게 경멸하고 있었다. 자기 자신의 이익에 더 집착하지 않는다는 이유에서였다. 그는 존스가 소피아를 사랑한다는 우려는 전혀 하지 않았고, 어떤 잇속을 노린 동기로 말하자면, 그토록 어리석은 녀석에게는 그런 것이 거의 영향을 미치지 못하리라 상상했다. 게다가 블리필은 몰리 시그림과의 정사가 아직 계속되고 있으며, 실은 그것이 결혼으로 끝나리라고까지 믿고 있었다. 존스는 어릴 적부터 진심으로 그를 좋아했고, 올워디 씨가 병들었을 때 보인 그의 행실이 그의 마음을 완전히 멀어지게 하기 전까지는 그에게 어떤 비밀도 숨기지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 바로 이 일을 계기로 벌어진, 아직 화해되지 못한 그 다툼 탓에, 블리필 씨는 존스가 예전에 몰리에게 품었던 애정에 일어난 변화를 전혀 알지 못했던 것이다.\n\n그러므로 이러한 이유들에서, 블리필 씨는 소피아와의 일에 어떤 걸림돌도 보지 못했다. 그는 그녀의 태도가 연인의 첫 방문을 받은 여느 젊은 숙녀들과 다를 바 없다고 결론지었으며, 그것은 과연 그의 기대에 온전히 들어맞았다.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 연인이 자신의 연모하는 이에게서 나오는 길목을 지켜 잡으려고 신경 썼다. 그는 그가 성공에 한껏 들떠 있고, 딸에게 흠뻑 빠져 있으며, 그녀의 환대에 너무도 만족해하는 것을 보고는, 이 노신사는 자기 집 큰 방을 이리저리 깡충거리며 춤추기 시작했고, 그 밖에도 여러 우스꽝스러운 동작으로 주체할 수 없는 기쁨을 드러냈다. 그는 자신의 어떤 감정도 조금도 다스리지 못하는 사람이었고, 어느 때든 그의 마음을 사로잡은 감정은 그를 가장 거친 극단으로 몰아갔던 것이다.\n\n블리필이 떠나자—그것도 웨스턴이 그에게 진심 어린 입맞춤과 포옹을 숱하게 퍼붓고 나서야 떠났는데—이 선량한 지주는 곧장 딸을 찾아 나섰다. 그리고 딸을 발견하자마자 더없이 과장된 환희를 쏟아 내며, 마음에 드는 옷이든 보석이든 무엇이든 고르라 일렀고, 자신에게 재산이란 오로지 딸을 행복하게 해 주기 위한 것일 뿐 다른 쓸모는 없다고 선언했다. 그러고는 더없이 흘러넘치는 다정함으로 그녀를 거듭거듭 어루만지며, 가장 사랑스러운 이름들로 부르고, 그녀야말로 이 세상에서 자신의 유일한 기쁨이라고 단언했다.\n\n소피아는 아버지가 이런 애정의 발작에 사로잡힌 것을 보았으나, 그 까닭을 정확히 알지는 못했다(이런 다정함의 발작이 그에게 드문 일은 아니었지만, 이번 것은 여느 때보다 다소 격렬했다). 그녀는 적어도 블리필 씨에 관한 한 지금보다 더 좋은 기회는 결코 없으리라 생각했고, 또 머지않아 자신이 모든 것을 낱낱이 털어놓아야 할 처지에 놓이리라는 것도 너무도 잘 내다보고 있었다. 그래서 지주의 그 모든 다정한 말들에 감사를 드린 뒤, 형언할 수 없이 부드러운 표정으로 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그럼 정말 우리 아버지께서 당신의 모든 기쁨을 이 소피의 행복에 두실 만큼 그렇게 자애로우실 수가 있을까요?\" 웨스턴이 굳은 맹세와 입맞춤으로 그것을 확인해 주자, 그녀는 그의 손을 붙잡고 무릎을 꿇으며, 애정과 효성을 담은 뜨겁고 간절한 다짐을 여러 번 늘어놓은 끝에 그에게 애원했다. \"제가 몹시 싫어하는 남자와 억지로 혼인하게 하시어 저를 이 세상에서 가장 비참한 존재로 만들지 말아 주세요. 사랑하는 아버지, 이것을 제가 간청드립니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저 자신을 위해서이기도 하지만 아버지를 위해서이기도 합니다. 아버지의 행복이 제 행복에 달려 있다고 그토록 다정히 말씀해 주셨으니까요.\"—\"뭐라고! 무엇이!\" 웨스턴이 사납게 눈을 부릅뜨며 말했다. \"오! 아버지,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"가엾은 소피의 행복만이 아니라, 그 애의 목숨, 그 존재 자체가 아버지께서 이 청을 들어주시는 데 달려 있습니다. 저는 블리필 씨와는 살 수 없습니다. 이 혼인을 제게 강요하시는 것은 저를 죽이시는 일이 될 것입니다.\"—\"네가 블리필 씨와 살 수 없다고?\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"네, 맹세코 저는 그럴 수 없습니다.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"그럼 죽어라, 빌어먹을,\" 그가 외치며 그녀를 밀쳐 냈다. \"오! 아버지,\" 소피아가 그의 외투 자락을 붙들며 외쳤다. \"부디 저를 가엾이 여겨 주세요, 간청드립니다. 그렇게 보시며 그토록 잔인한 말씀을—이 끔찍한 처지에 빠진 소피를 보시면서도 마음이 흔들리지 않으실 수 있나요? 더없이 훌륭하신 아버지께서 제 가슴을 찢으실 수 있나요? 가장 고통스럽고 잔인하며 더디게 끌리는 죽음으로 저를 죽이시려는 건가요?\"—\"쳇! 쳇!\" 지주가 외쳤다. \"다 헛소리에 쓸데없는 소리다. 죄다 처녀티 내는 수작이지. 죽긴 무얼 죽어! 혼인이 너를 죽이기라도 한단 말이냐?\"—\"오! 아버지,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"그런 혼인은 죽음보다 더 나쁩니다. 그는 제게 무덤덤한 정도조차 아니에요. 저는 그를 미워하고 혐오합니다.\"—\"네가 그를 아무리 혐오한다 해도,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"너는 그를 갖게 될 게다.\" 그는 여기에 차마 옮기기조차 끔찍한 맹세를 덧붙였고, 격렬한 단언을 숱하게 늘어놓은 끝에 이런 말로 끝맺었다. \"나는 이 혼사를 굳게 작정했다. 네가 거기에 동의하지 않는다면 단 한 푼도, 단 한 닢도 네게 주지 않겠다. 그래, 네가 거리에서 굶주려 숨이 끊어지는 꼴을 본다 해도, 빵 한 조각으로도 너를 구해 주지 않을 테다. 이것이 내 확고한 결심이니, 그 일을 곰곰이 생각해 보라고 너를 두고 가마.\" 그러고는 어찌나 사납게 그녀에게서 떨쳐 나왔는지, 그녀의 얼굴이 마룻바닥에 부딪혔고, 그는 가엾은 소피아를 바닥에 엎드린 채 남겨 둔 채 곧장 방을 박차고 나갔다.\n\n웨스턴이 큰 방으로 들어섰을 때, 그곳에서 그는 존스를 마주쳤다. 존스는 자기 벗이 사납고 창백하며 거의 숨이 넘어갈 듯한 모습인 것을 보고는, 이 모든 침울한 기색의 까닭을 묻지 않을 수 없었다. 그러자 지주는 즉시 그에게 일의 전말을 들려주었으며, 소피아를 향한 모진 저주의 말과, 딸을 둔 불운을 안은 모든 아비들의 비참함에 대한 더없이 애처로운 한탄으로 이야기를 끝맺었다.\n\n블리필에게 유리하게 내려진 그 모든 결정이 아직 비밀로 남아 있던 존스는, 처음 이 이야기를 듣고 거의 죽을 듯이 충격을 받았다. 그러나 정신을 조금 추스르고 나자, 훗날 그 자신이 말했듯 순전한 절망감이 그를 부추겨, 인간의 낯짝에 일찍이 주어진 적 없는 뻔뻔함이 필요해 보이는 일 하나를 웨스턴 씨에게 꺼내게 했다. 그는 소피아에게 가서, 그녀가 아버지의 뜻에 따르도록 설득해 보겠다며 허락을 청한 것이다.\n\n만일 지주가 둔하기로 그토록 이름났던 것과는 반대로 눈치가 빨랐더라면, 지금쯤 격정이 그의 눈을 가렸을 법도 했다. 그는 그 일을 맡아 주겠다는 존스의 제안에 고마워하며 말했다. \"가게, 가, 부디 자네가 할 수 있는 걸 한번 해 보게.\" 그러고는 그녀가 이 혼사에 동의하지 않으면 그녀를 집 밖으로 내쫓겠다며 차마 입에 담기 어려운 저주의 맹세를 숱하게 퍼부었다.\n\n존스와 소피아의 만남.\n\n존스는 곧장 소피아를 찾아 떠났고, 아버지가 그녀를 두고 간 그 자리에서 막 일어선 그녀를 발견했다. 두 눈에서는 눈물이 흘러내리고, 입술에서는 피가 배어 나오고 있었다. 그는 곧바로 그녀에게 달려가, 다정함과 두려움이 한꺼번에 가득 담긴 목소리로 외쳤다. \"오, 나의 소피아, 이 끔찍한 광경은 대체 무엇이오?\" 그녀는 입을 열기 전 잠시 그를 부드럽게 바라보더니 말했다. \"존스 씨, 제발 부탁이에요, 어떻게 여기까지 오신 거예요?—제발 부탁드려요, 지금 당장 저를 떠나 주세요.\"—\"그런,\" 그가 말했다, \"그토록 모진 명령을 제게 내리지 마시오—내 가슴은 저 입술보다 더 빨리 피를 흘리고 있소. 오, 소피아, 그 소중한 피 한 방울을 지킬 수 있다면 내 핏줄을 다 비워 낸들 어찌 어렵겠소.\"—\"이미 당신께 너무 많은 은혜를 입었어요,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"분명 당신은 그것을 은혜로 베푸셨겠지요.\" 여기서 그녀는 거의 일 분 가까이 그를 다정하게 바라보더니, 이내 격한 슬픔에 북받쳐 외쳤다. \"오, 존스 씨, 어째서 제 목숨을 구하셨나요? 제가 죽었더라면 우리 둘 모두에게 더 행복했을 텐데요.\"—\"우리 둘 모두에게 더 행복하다니!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"고문대나 형차인들 소피아의—그 무서운 말을 차마 견딜 수가 없소. 내가 그녀 말고 무엇을 위해 산단 말이오?\"\n이 말을 할 때 그의 목소리와 표정에는 말로 다 할 수 없는 다정함이 가득했다. 그러면서 그는 그녀의 손을 부드럽게 잡았고, 그녀는 그것을 빼지 않았다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀는 자신이 무엇을 하고 무엇을 겪고 있는지조차 거의 알지 못했다. 이제 이 연인들 사이에 잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그의 두 눈은 간절하게 소피아에게 못 박혀 있었으며, 그녀의 눈은 땅을 향해 내리깔렸다. 마침내 그녀는 다시 그에게 떠나 달라고 청할 만큼의 기운을 되찾았다. 두 사람이 함께 있다가 들키면 자신의 파멸이 분명한 결과로 닥칠 것이기 때문이었다. 그녀는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"오, 존스 씨, 당신은 모르세요, 이 잔인한 오후에 무슨 일이 있었는지 정말 모르세요.\"—\"나는 다 알고 있소, 나의 소피아,\" 그가 대답했다, \"당신의 모진 아버님이 내게 모두 말씀하셨고, 그분이 직접 나를 당신에게 보내셨소.\"—\"아버지가 당신을 제게 보내셨다고요!\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"분명 꿈을 꾸고 계신 거예요.\"—\"하늘에 비노니,\" 그가 외쳤다, \"이것이 한낱 꿈이라면 얼마나 좋겠소! 오, 소피아, 당신의 아버님이 나를 당신에게 보내신 것은, 내 가증스러운 연적을 대신해 변호하라고, 그를 위해 당신을 설득하라고 하신 것이오. 나는 당신에게 다가갈 수만 있다면 어떤 수단이든 가리지 않았소. 오, 내게 말해 주오, 소피아! 피 흘리는 내 가슴을 달래 주오. 분명 나처럼 사랑한, 나처럼 애틋이 흠모한 이는 일찍이 없었을 것이오. 이 소중한, 이 부드러운, 이 가녀린 손을 매정하게 거두지 마오—어쩌면 한순간이 당신을 내게서 영원히 앗아 갈지도 모르오—이토록 잔인한 사정이 아니고서는, 당신이 내게 불어넣은 그 존경과 경외를 무엇으로도 이겨 낼 수 없었을 것이오.\" 그녀는 잠시 말없이 서서 당혹감에 휩싸여 있었다. 그러더니 부드럽게 눈을 들어 그를 향해 외쳤다. \"존스 씨는 제가 무슨 말을 하기를 바라시나요?\"—\"오, 다만 약속만이라도 해 주오,\" 그가 외쳤다, \"결코 당신 자신을 블리필에게 내주지 않겠다고.\"—\"그 혐오스러운 이름은,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"입에 올리지도 마세요. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 제가 거절할 수 있는 한 결코 그에게 저를 내주지 않겠어요.\"—\"그렇다면 이제,\" 그가 외쳤다, \"이토록 더없이 다정하신 김에 한 걸음만 더 나아가, 제가 희망을 품어도 된다고 덧붙여 주오.\"—\"아아!\" 그녀가 말했다, \"존스 씨, 저를 어디로 몰아가시려는 거예요? 제가 무슨 희망을 드릴 수 있겠어요? 아버지의 뜻을 당신도 알고 계시잖아요.\"—\"하지만 나는 알고 있소,\" 그가 대답했다, \"당신이 그 뜻에 따르도록 강요할 수는 없다는 것을.\"—\"제가 거역하면,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그 끔찍한 결과가 어떻겠어요? 제 자신의 파멸은 가장 작은 걱정이에요. 제가 아버지를 불행하게 만드는 원인이 된다는 생각만은 견딜 수가 없어요.\"—\"그분 스스로가 원인이오,\" 존스가 외쳤다, \"자연이 그분께 주지 않은 권력을 당신에게 휘두르려 하시니 말이오. 만일 내가 당신을 잃어야 한다면 내가 겪어야 할 그 비참함을 생각해 보오, 그리고 동정이 어느 쪽으로 저울을 기울일지 헤아려 보오.\"—\"그걸 생각해 보라고요!\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"제가 당신의 바람을 따른다면 당신께 끼치게 될 그 파멸을 제가 느끼지 못한다고 여기시는 거예요? 바로 그 생각이, 당신께 영원히 제게서 달아나 당신 자신의 파멸을 피하라고 말할 결심을 제게 주는 거예요.\"—\"나는 어떤 파멸도 두렵지 않소,\" 그가 외쳤다, \"소피아를 잃는 것 말고는. 가장 쓰라린 고통에서 나를 구하고 싶다면, 그 잔인한 선고를 거두어 주오. 정말이지, 나는 결코 당신과 헤어질 수 없소, 정말로 그럴 수 없소.\"\n\n이제 두 연인은 모두 말없이 떨며 서 있었다. 소피아는 존스에게서 손을 빼낼 수가 없었고, 그 역시 거의 그 손을 잡고 있을 수 없을 지경이었다. 그때, 내 독자들 가운데 더러는 이미 충분히 길었다고 여길 법한 이 장면이, 사뭇 다른 성질의 또 다른 장면에 의해 끊기고 말았다. 그 이야기는 다른 장으로 미루어 두기로 하겠다.\n\n9장.\n앞 장보다 훨씬 더 격렬한 종류의 이야기.\n\n지금 우리 연인들에게 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지로 넘어가기 전에, 그 애틋한 만남이 이어지는 동안 거실에서 어떤 일이 오갔는지를 먼저 들려주는 편이 좋겠다.\n\n존스가 앞서 이야기한 대로 웨스턴 씨를 떠난 직후, 그의 누이가 그에게 왔고, 곧 블리필을 둘러싸고 오라비와 소피아 사이에 오간 모든 일을 전해 듣게 되었다.\n\n이 선량한 부인은 조카딸의 이런 처신을, 자신이 존스 씨를 향한 그 애정을 비밀에 부쳐 두기로 약속했던 조건을 완전히 깨뜨린 것으로 받아들였다. 그래서 그녀는 자신이 아는 모든 것을 지주에게 털어놓을 완전한 자유가 있다고 여겼고, 곧장 아무런 격식이나 서두도 없이 더없이 분명한 말로 그렇게 했다.\n\n존스와 자기 딸의 결혼이라는 발상은, 그 젊은이를 향한 애정이 가장 뜨겁던 순간에도, 의심에서든, 그 밖의 어떤 경우에든, 지주의 머릿속에 단 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그는 재산과 처지가 엇비슷한 것이야말로 결혼에서 남녀가 서로 다른 성이라는 점이나 그 밖의 어떤 본질적 요소만큼이나 물리적으로 빠질 수 없는 성분이라고 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 자기 딸이 가난한 사내와 사랑에 빠지는 일을, 종이 다른 어떤 짐승과 사랑에 빠지는 일만큼이나 도무지 있을 수 없는 일로 보았다.\n\n그러니 그는 누이의 이야기에 벼락이라도 맞은 사람처럼 굳어 버렸다. 처음에는 어떤 대답도 할 수 없었으니, 놀라움의 격렬함에 숨이 거의 막혀 버린 탓이었다. 그러나 숨은 이내 돌아왔고, 다른 경우들에서도 으레 그렇듯, 한차례 멎었다가 갑절의 힘과 분노로 되살아났다.\n\n그가 그 갑작스러운 경악의 충격에서 깨어나 말할 힘을 되찾은 뒤 가장 먼저 한 일은, 욕설과 저주를 한바탕 무더기로 쏟아내는 것이었다. 그러고는 연인들이 있으리라 짐작되는 방으로 황급히 나아가며, 걸음을 옮길 때마다 복수의 뜻을 중얼거리듯, 아니 사실은 으르렁대듯 토해 냈다.\n\n두 마리 비둘기가, 혹은 두 마리 산비둘기가, 아니면 스트레폰과 필리스가 (이쪽이 더 들어맞겠다) 사랑이라는 그 수줍은 소년의 달콤한 속삭임을 즐기려고 어느 한적하고 아늑한 숲으로 물러나 있을 때— 그 소년은 사람들 앞에서는 입을 떼지 못하고, 한 번에 두 사람 넘게는 결코 좋은 동무가 되어 주지 못하는 법이다—그렇게 온갖 것이 고요하기만 한데, 별안간 갈라진 구름을 뚫고 쉰 목소리의 천둥이 터져 나와 우르릉 하늘을 따라 굴러간다면, 놀란 처녀는 이끼 낀 둑이나 푸른 잔디밭에서 화들짝 일어나고, 조금 전 사랑이 그 뺨에 입혀 두었던 붉은 군복 같은 빛 대신 죽음의 창백한 제복이 들어서며, 두려움이 그녀의 온몸을 떨게 하고, 연인은 떨며 휘청대는 그녀의 사지를 간신히 떠받친다.\n\n또는 그 고장의 놀라운 익살을 알지 못하는 두 신사가 솔즈베리의 어느 여관이나 선술집에서 함께 술병을 비우고 있을 때, 그 부추기는 패거리 가운데 누구는 바보 역을 곧잘 해내듯이 미치광이 역을 곧잘 해내는 그 거대한 다우디가 회랑을 따라 제 쇠사슬을 절그럭거리며 투덜대는 가락을 무시무시하게 흥얼거린다면, 겁에 질린 두 손님은 그 끔찍한 소리에 얼이 빠져 멍하니 서 있다가, 다가오는 위험을 피할 은신처를 찾고, 만일 단단히 빗장 지른 창문이 빠져나갈 틈이라도 내어 준다면 닥쳐오는 그 협박조의 광기에서 벗어나려 제 목숨조차 내걸 것이다.\n\n가엾은 소피아도 그렇게 떨었고, 아버지의 고함 소리에 그렇게 얼굴이 새하얗게 질렸다. 아버지는 듣기에 더없이 무시무시한 목소리로 욕하고, 저주하고, 존스를 끝장내겠노라 맹세하며 다가오고 있었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그 젊은이 자신도 어떤 신중한 셈속에서라면 이 순간만큼은 다른 거처를 더 바랐으리라고 나는 믿는다. 다만 소피아를 향한 공포가, 그녀에게 닥치는 모든 일을 사랑 때문에 함께 나누어 짊어지게 하는 것 말고 달리 제 자신과 관계된 무엇을 단 한순간이라도 헤아릴 겨를을 그에게 허락하지 않았을 뿐이다.\n\n그리하여 지주는 문을 박차고 들어서다가, 존스를 향한 모든 분노를 그 자리에서 멎게 하는 한 광경을 마주했다. 그것은 연인의 품 안에서 정신을 잃은 소피아의 핏기 없는 모습이었다. 이 비극적인 장면을 보자마자 웨스턴 씨의 분노는 온데간데없이 사라졌다. 그는 있는 힘껏 사람을 부르며 울부짖었고, 먼저 딸에게 달려갔다가, 다시 문 쪽으로 돌아가 물을 외쳤다가, 또다시 소피아에게로 되돌아왔다. 그녀가 그때 누구의 품에 안겨 있는지는 전혀 헤아리지 못했고, 어쩌면 존스라는 사람이 이 세상에 있다는 사실조차 단 한 번도 떠올리지 못했다. 실로 그때 그의 머릿속을 채운 단 하나의 생각은 딸의 지금 처지뿐이었다고 나는 믿는다.\n\n이내 웨스턴 부인과 많은 하인들이 물과 강심제, 그리고 그런 경우에 필요한 온갖 것들을 들고 소피아를 도우러 달려왔다. 그것들이 어찌나 효험을 발휘했던지, 소피아는 채 몇 분도 지나지 않아 깨어나기 시작했고, 살아 있는 모든 기색이 되돌아왔다. 그러자 그녀는 곧 자기 하녀와 웨스턴 부인의 부축을 받아 그 자리를 떠났다. 그 선량한 부인은 자기 격정의, 아니 그녀가 즐겨 부르듯 광기의 무시무시한 결과에 대해 오라비에게 몇 마디 유익한 충고를 남겨 두고서야 자리를 떴다.\n\n지주는 이 좋은 충고를 알아듣지 못했을지도 모른다. 그것이 알쏭달쏭한 암시와 어깨짓과 탄식 섞인 가락으로 전해진 까닭이다. 적어도 설령 알아들었다 해도, 거기서 얻은 것은 거의 없었다. 딸을 향한 당장의 두려움에서 풀려나기가 무섭게 그는 곧장 이전의 광란으로 되돌아갔으니, 만일 매우 기운이 센 사내인 서플 목사가 그 자리에 있어 순전한 힘으로 지주의 적대 행위를 막지 않았다면 그 즉시 존스와 한판 싸움이 벌어졌을 것이다.\n\n소피아가 떠나가자마자, 존스는 더없이 애원하는 태도로 웨스턴 씨에게 다가갔다. 목사가 그를 양팔로 붙들고 있었는데, 존스는 부디 노여움을 가라앉혀 달라고 빌었다. 그가 그토록 격분해 있는 한 그에게 어떤 사죄도 드릴 수 없을 것이기 때문이었다.\n\n\"내 너헌테 만족을 받아낼 테다.\" 지주가 대꾸했다. \"그러니 옷을 벗어라. 넌 사내 반푼어치도 안 되는 놈이니, 네 평생 맞아 본 것 중에 제일 흠씬 두들겨 패 주마.\" 그러고는 그 젊은이에게, 시골 신사들이 견해를 달리하여 서로 맞설 때 주고받는 그런 말들을 잔뜩 퍼부었다. 게다가 영국 하급 향신 계층이 경마장이며 닭싸움판이며 그 밖의 공공장소에서 벌이는 온갖 다툼에 으레 끼어드는 그 신체 부위에 입을 맞추라는 요구까지 빈번히 덧붙였다. 이 부위에 빗댄 말은 농지거리 삼아서도 자주 나오는 법이다. 그런데 여기서 나는 그 익살이 흔히 잘못 이해된다고 믿는다. 실상 그 묘미는, 방금 상대의 엉덩이를 걷어차겠노라 위협해 놓고는 도리어 제 엉덩이에 입을 맞추라 청하는 데에 있다. 나는 아주 면밀히 관찰한 바, 누구도 제게 속한 것을 걷어차 달라 청하는 법이 없고, 또 남의 그 부위에 입 맞추겠노라 나서는 일도 없으니 말이다.\n\n또 한 가지 놀라운 일이 있다. 시골 신사들과 말을 섞어 본 사람이라면 누구나 수천 번은 들어 보았을 이런 종류의 다정한 초대 가운데, 그 청이 실제로 받아들여진 사례를 단 한 번이라도 본 사람은 없으리라 믿는다는 것이다. 이는 그들이 예의를 갖추지 못했음을 보여 주는 큰 증거다. 도회지에서야 더없이 세련된 신사들이 윗사람에게 매일같이 이 의식을 베푸는 일이, 그것도 그런 호의를 한 번 청 받은 적도 없이 베푸는 일이 흔하디흔하니 말이다.\n\n이 모든 익살에 대해 존스는 아주 차분하게 대답했다. \"어르신, 이런 대우가 어쩌면 어르신께서 제게 베푸신 다른 모든 은혜를 다 지워 버릴지도 모릅니다. 하지만 결코 지우실 수 없는 은혜가 하나 있습니다. 또한 저는 어르신의 모욕에 자극받아 소피아의 아버지께 손을 들어 올리는 일은 하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n이 말에 지주는 전보다 더욱 길길이 날뛰었고, 그래서 목사는 존스에게 물러가 달라 청했다. \"보다시피 그대가 여기 머무는 것에 저분이 노여움을 더 키우고 있소. 그러니 부디 더는 지체하지 마시오. 지금은 분노가 너무 거세어 그대가 저분과 말을 나눌 형편이 못 되오. 그러니 방문을 이만 마무리하고, 그대를 위해 호소할 일들은 다른 기회로 미루는 편이 좋겠소.\"\n\n존스는 고마운 마음으로 이 충고를 받아들이고 곧 떠났다. 그제야 지주는 두 팔의 자유를 되찾았고, 마음도 어지간히 가라앉아 자신에게 가해졌던 그 제지에 대해 어느 정도 흡족함을 내비쳤다. 그러지 않았다면 틀림없이 그 녀석의 골통을 박살내고 말았으리라 하면서, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그런 놈팡이 때문에 교수형이라도 당했더라면 그야말로 분통이 터질 노릇이었지.\"\n\n이제 목사는 화해를 이끌어 낸 자기 노력의 성과에 으쓱해하기 시작했고, 분노를 경계하는 일장 훈계를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 다만 그 훈계는 성마른 어떤 이들에게는 오히려 그 격정을 가라앉히기보다 부추기는 쪽으로 작용했을지도 모른다. 그는 이 훈계를 고대 사람들에게서, 특히 세네카에게서 끌어온 값진 인용으로 풍성하게 꾸몄다. 세네카는 실로 이 격정을 어찌나 능란하게 다루었던지, 어지간히 성난 사람이 아니고서는 누구라도 그를 읽으며 큰 즐거움과 유익을 얻지 않을 수 없을 정도다. 목사는 이 일장 연설을 알렉산드로스와 클리투스의 그 유명한 이야기로 마무리했다. 그러나 그 이야기는 내 비망록의 \"취중(醉中)\"이라는 항목 아래 적혀 있으니, 여기에는 옮기지 않겠다.\n\n지주는 이 이야기에 아무런 관심도 두지 않았고, 어쩌면 목사가 한 어떤 말에도 마찬가지였다. 그가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 맥주 한 잔을 가져오라 외치며 끊어 버렸으니 말이다. 그러면서 그는 (마음의 이 열병에 관한 어떤 관찰만큼이나 어쩌면 참된 관찰을) 이렇게 한마디 했다. 화가 나면 사람은 목이 마르는 법이라고.\n\n지주는 큼직한 한 모금을 들이켜기가 무섭게 다시 존스 이야기로 되돌아갔고, 이튿날 아침 일찌감치 올워디 씨에게 알리러 가겠노라 결심을 밝혔다. 그의 벗은 순전히 선량한 마음에서 그를 만류하려 했으나, 그 만류는 욕설과 저주를 한바탕 무더기로 끌어낸 것 말고는 아무 효과도 없었다. 그 욕설과 저주는 서플의 경건한 귀를 크게 거슬렸으나, 자유민으로 태어난 영국인으로서 지주가 누리노라 주장하는 특권에 그는 감히 항의하지 못했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 목사는 지주의 식탁에서 입의 즐거움을 누리는 대가로 이따금 이런 폭언에 귀를 내맡기는 일을 감수했다. 그는 자신이 이 못된 습관을 부추긴 것은 아니며, 설령 자신이 그 집 대문 안에 발 한 번 들이지 않았더라도 지주가 욕설을 한 마디라도 덜 했을 리 없다고 생각하며 스스로를 위안했다. 다만 남의 집에서 신사를 꾸짖는 무례를 저지르지는 않았으되, 그는 설교단에서 그를 에둘러 갚아 주었다. 그것이 실로 지주 본인을 바로잡는 좋은 효험을 내지는 못했으나, 그래도 그의 양심에 어느 정도는 작용하여, 그는 다른 사람들에게는 이 법을 아주 엄하게 집행했다. 그리하여 그 교구에서 아무 벌도 받지 않고 욕설을 할 수 있는 사람은 오직 그 치안판사 한 명뿐이었다.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 조카가 소피아를 찾아간 일이 성공적이었다는 보고에 흡족해하며 함께 아침 식사를 마치고 막 자리에서 물러난 참이었다(올워디 씨가 이 혼사를 그토록 바란 것은 처녀의 재산보다도 그녀의 인품 때문이었다). 그때 웨스턴 씨가 느닷없이 두 사람 사이로 뛰어들더니, 예의고 뭐고 없이 다짜고짜 이렇게 입을 열었다.\n\n\"자, 댁이 참으로 훌륭한 일을 해 놓으셨소그래! 그 사생아 녀석을 잘도 키워 놓으셨더군. 그렇다고 댁이 거기에 손을 댔다는 건 아니오, 그러니까 말하자면 일부러 그랬다는 건 아니라는 말이지. 하지만 우리 집은 지금 아주 난장판이 되어 버렸소이다.\" \"대체 무슨 일입니까, 웨스턴 씨?\" 올워디가 물었다. \"오, 양심에 비춰 봐도 일은 차고도 넘치오. 내 딸년이 댁의 사생아 놈한테 홀딱 빠져 버렸단 말이오, 그게 다요. 하지만 내 그년한테는 반 푼도 안 줄 거요, 놋쇠 동전 한 닢의 스무 분의 일도 안 줄 거란 말이오. 사생아를 신사처럼 길러서 남의 집을 들락거리게 하면 무슨 꼴이 날지 내 진작에 알아봤지. 그놈 손에 못 닿은 게 천만다행이지. 안 그랬으면 흠씬 두들겨 패서 그 발정 난 울음소리를 못 내게 만들어 놨을 거요. 그 화냥년의 자식한테 제 주인의 고깃점에 함부로 손대면 어찌 되는지 단단히 가르쳐 줬을 텐데. 그놈은 내 고기 한 점도, 그걸 살 동전 한 닢도 절대 못 가질 거요. 그년이 기어이 그놈을 갖겠다면, 입은 속치마 한 장이 그년 몫의 전부일 거란 말이오. 차라리 내 재산을 감채기금에 넘겨서 하노버로 보내 우리 나라를 썩히는 데 쓰게 하는 편이 낫겠소.\" \"진심으로 유감입니다.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"유감이고 나발이고,\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"내가 하나뿐인 자식을, 내 가엾은 소피를, 내 가슴의 기쁨이자 늘그막의 온갖 희망이요 위안이던 그 아이를 잃고 나면, 댁의 유감이 내게 무슨 큰 도움이 되겠소. 하지만 내 그년을 문밖으로 내쫓기로 작심했소. 빌어먹게 하고, 굶기고, 길바닥에서 썩게 할 거요. 단 반 푼도, 반 푼도 그년은 절대 못 받을 거란 말이오. 그 개자식은 늘 토끼가 웅크린 자리를 잘도 찾아냈지, 썩어 문드러질 놈 같으니. 그놈이 노리던 게 어떤 토끼였는지 내 미처 몰랐던 거요. 하지만 이번 토끼는 그놈 평생에 잡은 것 중 가장 고약한 놈이 될 거요. 그년은 짐승 시체나 다름없는 신세가 될 테고, 그놈이 가질 수 있는 건 그 껍데기뿐일 거란 말이오. 그러니 그렇게 전해도 좋소.\" \"도무지 영문을 모르겠습니다.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"바로 어제, 내 조카와 그 처녀 사이에 오간 일을 생각하면 더더욱 그렇습니다.\" \"그렇소, 선생,\" 웨스턴이 대답했다. \"댁의 조카와 그년 사이에 그 일이 오간 다음에야 모든 게 들통났단 말이오. 저기 블리필 씨가 떠나기가 무섭게 그 화냥년의 자식이 집 주변을 어슬렁거렸지. 사냥꾼이랍시고 그놈을 아끼던 시절에, 그놈이 내내 내 딸년을 노리고 밀렵을 하고 있었을 줄 누가 알았겠소.\" \"사실은,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그에게 그 아이와 함께할 기회를 그토록 많이 주지 않으셨으면 했습니다. 그리고 내가 늘 그가 댁의 집에 그토록 오래 머무는 걸 탐탁지 않아 했다는 건 댁도 인정해 주시리라 믿습니다. 다만 이런 종류의 의심은 전혀 품지 못했음을 고백합니다.\" \"원, 제기랄,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"누가 그런 걸 생각이나 했겠소? 그놈이 그년하고 무슨 상관이 있었겠냔 말이오? 그놈은 그년한테 구애하러 온 게 아니라, 나하고 사냥하러 온 거였소.\" \"하지만 그럴 수가 있습니까,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그토록 자주 둘이 함께 있는 걸 보시면서 그들 사이의 사랑의 기색을 단 한 번도 알아채지 못하셨다니 말입니다.\" \"맹세코 내 평생 단 한 번도 없었소,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"그놈이 그년한테 입 맞추는 걸 내 평생 한 번도 본 적이 없단 말이오. 게다가 구애는커녕, 그놈은 그년이 같이 있을 때면 다른 때보다 오히려 더 말이 없었소. 그리고 그년으로 말하자면, 집에 오는 어떤 젊은 놈한테보다도 그놈한테 늘 더 쌀쌀맞게 굴었단 말이오. 그런 일에 관해서라면 나도 남들 못지않게 잘 속아 넘어가지 않는 사람이오. 내가 그렇다고 여기진 마시오, 이웃 양반.\" 올워디는 이 말에 웃음을 참기 어려웠으나 억지로 자신을 다잡았다. 그는 인간이라는 존재를 너무도 잘 알았고, 또 지금 같은 처지의 지주를 언짢게 하기에는 너무나 교양 있고 천성이 선량한 사람이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 웨스턴에게 이번 일에 자기가 어찌해 주기를 바라느냐고 물었다. 이에 상대는 이렇게 답했다. \"그 망나니 놈을 자기 집에 얼씬도 못 하게 막아 주었으면 좋겠소. 그리고 자기는 가서 그 계집애를 가둬 놓겠소. 이를 악물고 버티더라도 기어이 블리필 씨와 혼인시킬 작정이니까 말이오.\" 그러고는 블리필의 손을 흔들며, 다른 사위는 절대 두지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 곧이어 그는 작별을 고하며, 집안이 워낙 엉망이 되어 서둘러 돌아가 딸년이 빠져나가지 못하도록 단속해야 한다고 했다. 그리고 존스로 말하자면, 만일 자기 집에서 그놈을 붙잡으면 거세마 경주에나 나가도록 손을 봐 주겠노라 맹세했다.\n\n올워디와 블리필만 다시 남게 되자, 두 사람 사이에는 긴 침묵이 이어졌다. 그동안 내내 젊은 신사는 한숨으로 그 틈을 메웠는데, 그 한숨은 일부는 실망에서, 그러나 더 많게는 증오에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그에게는 소피아를 잃은 것보다 존스가 성공을 거둔 것이 훨씬 더 가슴 쓰린 일이었기 때문이다.\n\n마침내 그의 숙부가 어찌하기로 마음먹었느냐고 묻자, 그는 다음과 같은 말로 대답했다. \"아아! 숙부님, 이성과 정념이 서로 다른 길을 가리킬 때, 사랑에 빠진 자가 어느 쪽으로 걸음을 옮길지가 과연 물어볼 일이겠습니까? 그런 갈림길에서 그가 언제나 후자를 따르리라는 것은 너무도 분명한 듯하여 두렵습니다. 이성은 제게 다른 사람에게 마음을 둔 여인에 대한 생각일랑 전부 버리라고 명합니다. 그러나 제 정념은 그녀가 언젠가 마음을 돌려 제게로 기울지도 모른다는 희망을 품으라 합니다. 다만 여기서 한 가지 반론이 제기될 수 있다고 봅니다. 만일 그 반론에 충분히 답할 수 없다면, 저는 더 이상의 추구를 완전히 단념하게 될 것입니다. 곧, 이미 다른 사람이 차지한 듯한 마음에서 그를 밀어내려 애쓰는 일의 부당함을 말하는 것입니다. 하지만 웨스턴 씨의 단호한 결심으로 보건대, 이 경우에는 제가 그렇게 함으로써 오히려 모든 당사자의 행복을 증진하게 됩니다. 그리하여 더없이 깊은 비참함에서 구원받게 될 그 아버지의 행복뿐 아니라, 이 혼사로 반드시 파멸하고 말 다른 두 사람의 행복까지도 말입니다. 그 처녀는 분명 모든 의미에서 신세를 망치게 될 것입니다. 자기 재산의 대부분을 잃을 뿐 아니라, 거지에게 시집가는 셈이 되고, 게다가 그녀의 아버지가 차마 떼어 놓지 못할 그 얼마 안 되는 재산마저도, 제가 알기로 그가 아직도 만나고 다니는 그 계집에게 탕진되고 말 테니까요. 아니, 그것은 사소한 일입니다. 저는 그가 세상에서 가장 못된 사람 중 하나임을 알고 있으니까요. 만일 제가 지금껏 숨기려 애써 온 일을 사랑하는 숙부님께서 아셨더라면, 진작에 그토록 방탕한 망나니를 내치셨을 것입니다.\" \"뭐라!\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그가 내가 이미 아는 것보다 더 못된 짓을 저질렀단 말이냐? 부디 말해 다오.\" \"아닙니다,\" 블리필이 대답했다. \"이제 다 지난 일이고, 어쩌면 그가 뉘우쳤을지도 모릅니다.\" \"네 도리를 걸고 명하노니,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"네가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 내게 말하거라.\" \"아시다시피, 숙부님,\" 블리필이 말했다. \"저는 한 번도 숙부님의 뜻을 거스른 적이 없습니다. 하지만 이 일을 입에 올린 것이 후회스럽습니다. 이제 와서는 마치 복수처럼 보일 수도 있으니까요. 그러나 하늘에 감사하게도, 제 마음에는 그런 동기가 단 한 번도 들어온 적이 없습니다. 그러니 숙부님께서 굳이 제게 이 일을 밝히라 하신다면, 저는 그를 위해 숙부님께 용서를 비는 탄원자가 되어야겠습니다.\" \"나는 어떤 조건도 받아들이지 않겠다,\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"나는 그에게 충분히, 어쩌면 네가 내게 고마워해야 할 것보다도 더 많은 너그러움을 베풀어 왔다고 생각한다.\" \"정말로, 그가 받을 자격이 있는 것보다 더 많이 베푸셨을까 두렵습니다,\" 블리필이 외쳤다. \"숙부님께서 가장 위태로우셨던 바로 그날, 저와 온 집안이 눈물에 잠겨 있을 때, 그는 온 집 안을 떠들썩한 소동과 방탕으로 가득 채웠으니까요. 술을 마시고, 노래를 부르고, 고래고래 소리를 질렀습니다. 그래서 제가 그 행동이 얼마나 무례한지 점잖게 일러 주었더니, 그는 불같이 화를 내며 온갖 욕설을 퍼붓고, 저를 망나니라 부르며 저를 때렸습니다.\" \"뭐라!\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그가 감히 너를 때렸단 말이냐?\" \"분명히,\" 블리필이 외쳤다. \"그 일은 제가 오래전에 용서했습니다. 그가 가장 훌륭한 은인께 저지른 그 배은망덕도 그렇게 쉽게 잊을 수 있다면 좋으련만. 하지만 그것마저도 숙부님께서 부디 용서해 주시기를 바랍니다. 그는 분명 악마에 씌어 있었을 테니까요. 바로 그날 저녁, 스왁검 선생님과 제가 들판으로 바람을 쐬러 나가, 그제야 처음으로 나타나기 시작한 좋은 차도에 기뻐하고 있을 때, 불행히도 우리는 그가 차마 입에 올릴 수 없는 모양새로 어떤 계집과 엉켜 있는 것을 보고 말았습니다. 스왁검 선생님은 신중함보다 대담함이 앞서 그를 꾸짖으러 다가가셨는데, (말씀드리기 송구하오나) 그가 그 훌륭하신 분께 달려들어 어찌나 모질게 두들겨 팼던지, 그 멍이 아직도 다 나았을지 모르겠습니다. 또한 제가 제 스승을 지켜 드리려 애쓰는 동안 저 역시 그의 악의가 미친 영향에서 무사하지 못했습니다. 하지만 그것은 제가 오래전에 용서했고, 아니, 스왁검 선생님께도 그를 용서하시고 그에게 치명적일까 두려운 그 비밀을 숙부님께 알리지 않으시도록 제가 권했습니다. 그런데 이제, 제가 경솔하게 이 일의 단서를 흘리고 말았고, 숙부님의 명에 못 이겨 모든 것을 밝히게 되었으니, 부디 그를 위해 제가 숙부님께 간청하도록 허락해 주십시오.\" \"오, 얘야!\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그런 악행을 한순간이라도 숨긴 네 착한 마음을 두고, 내가 너를 나무라야 할지 칭찬해야 할지 모르겠구나. 그런데 스왁검 선생은 어디 계시냐? 네 말에 무슨 확증이 필요해서가 아니라, 이 일의 모든 증거를 살펴, 그런 괴물을 본보기로 삼으려는 내 결심을 세상에 정당하게 밝히려는 것이다.\"\n\n곧 스왁검이 불려 왔고, 이내 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 상대가 진술한 모든 정황을 뒷받침했다. 아니, 그는 자신의 가슴에 새겨진 증거까지 내보였는데, 거기에는 존스 씨의 필적이 시퍼렇게 멍든 채로 또렷이 남아 있었다. 그는 올워디 씨에게, 블리필 씨가 더없이 간곡하게 만류하지만 않았더라면 진작에 이 일을 알려 드렸을 것이라고 밝히며 말을 맺었다. \"그 사람은,\" 그가 말했다. \"참으로 훌륭한 젊은이입니다. 다만 원수를 그렇게까지 용서하는 것은 일을 너무 지나치게 끌고 가는 것이지요.\"\n\n사실 블리필은 그때 이 일이 발각되지 않도록 목사를 구슬리는 데 적잖이 공을 들였는데, 거기에는 여러 이유가 있었다. 그는 사람의 마음이 병을 앓고 나면 평소의 엄격함이 누그러지고 풀어지기 쉽다는 것을 알고 있었다. 게다가 그는, 만일 사건이 일어난 지 얼마 안 되어, 진상을 밝혀냈을지도 모를 의사가 집에 머무는 동안 이 이야기를 했다가는, 자신이 의도한 악의적인 방향으로 그것을 비틀어 놓을 수 없으리라 짐작했다. 또한 그는 존스의 경솔함이 새로운 불만거리를 보태 줄 때까지 이 일을 묵혀 두기로 마음먹었다. 여러 사실이 한꺼번에 그에게 떨어져 그 무게가 합쳐질 때, 그를 짓뭉개기에 가장 좋으리라 여겼기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 지금 운명이 친절하게 마련해 준 것과 같은 그런 기회를 노리고 있었던 것이다. 끝으로, 스왁검을 구슬려 한동안 이 일을 숨기게 함으로써, 그는 자신이 올워디 씨에게 심어 두려고 그토록 애써 온 존스에 대한 우정의 인상을 더욱 굳히게 되리라는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\n짧은 장이지만, 마음씨 고운 독자의 가슴을 울리기에 충분한 이야기를 담고 있다.\n\n올워디 씨는 화가 난 상태에서는 누구도, 심지어 하인 한 사람조차도 결코 벌하지 않는 것을 원칙으로 삼고 있었다. 그래서 그는 존스에 대한 선고를 오후로 미루기로 마음먹었다.\n\n가엾은 젊은이는 여느 때처럼 만찬 자리에 함께했다. 그러나 그의 가슴은 너무도 무거워 무엇 하나 먹을 수가 없었다. 게다가 그의 슬픔은 올워디 씨의 차가운 눈길에 적잖이 더해졌다. 그 눈길을 보고 그는 웨스턴이 자신과 소피아 사이의 모든 일을 알아챘다고 결론지었다. 그러나 블리필 씨가 늘어놓은 이야기에 대해서만큼은 조금도 짐작하지 못했다. 그 대부분에 대해 그는 전적으로 결백했고, 나머지 부분에 대해서도, 그 자신이 이미 용서하고 잊어버렸기에 상대편 역시 기억하고 있으리라고는 의심조차 하지 않았던 것이다. 만찬이 끝나고 하인들이 물러가자, 올워디 씨가 일장 훈계를 시작했다. 그는 긴 연설을 통해 존스가 저지른 수많은 악행을, 특히 바로 이날 드러난 일들을 낱낱이 펼쳐 보였다. 그러고는 이렇게 말하며 끝을 맺었다. \"이 혐의에서 스스로를 깨끗이 벗기지 못한다면, 나는 너를 영원히 내 눈앞에서 쫓아낼 작정이다.\"\n\n가엾은 존스가 자신을 변호하는 데에는 여러 불리한 사정이 따랐다. 아니, 사실 그는 자신에게 씌워진 죄목조차 제대로 알지 못했다. 올워디 씨가 자신이 앓아누웠을 때의 만취 등을 늘어놓으면서도, 겸손한 마음에 자기 자신과 직접 관련된 부분—실은 바로 그것이 죄의 핵심을 이루고 있었는데—은 모조리 덮어 두었기 때문이다. 그러니 존스로서는 혐의를 부인할 수가 없었다. 게다가 그의 가슴은 이미 거의 무너져 있었고, 기력은 한껏 가라앉아 자신을 위해 아무 말도 할 수가 없었다. 그저 모든 것을 인정하고는, 절망에 빠진 죄인처럼 자비에 몸을 던질 뿐이었다. 그러면서 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"제가 수많은 어리석음과 부주의를 저질렀음은 인정할 수밖에 없지만, 저로서는 이 세상에서 가장 큰 벌이 될 일을 받을 만한 짓은 결코 하지 않았다고 믿습니다.\"\n\n올워디는 이렇게 답했다. \"나는 너의 어림을 가엾이 여겨, 또 네가 고쳐지기를 바라며 이미 너무 자주 너를 용서해 왔다. 이제 보니 너는 구제할 길 없는 망나니이며, 누구든 너를 떠받치고 부추기는 것 자체가 죄가 될 자다. 아니,\" 올워디 씨가 그에게 말했다. \"그 아가씨를 몰래 빼돌리려 한 너의 뻔뻔한 시도는, 너를 벌함으로써 내 자신의 명예를 지키지 않을 수 없게 만든다. 내가 너에게 베푼 관심을 두고 이미 세상은 나를 비난해 왔다. 그런 마당에 이토록 비열하고 야만스러운 행위를—네가 내 혐오를 분명히 알고 있었을 그런 행위를—내가 눈감아 준다고 여긴다면, 적어도 어느 정도는 정당한 명분이 있다고 세상이 생각할 만하지 않겠느냐. 만일 네가 나의 우정뿐 아니라 나의 평안과 명예를 조금이라도 헤아렸다면, 너는 결코 그런 일을 꾀할 생각조차 하지 않았을 것이다. 부끄러운 줄 알아라, 젊은이! 참으로 너의 죄에 걸맞은 벌이라고는 거의 없으며, 내가 지금 너에게 베풀려는 것조차 과연 정당한 일인지 나 스스로도 의심스럽다. 그러나 나는 너를 내 친자식처럼 길러 왔으니, 네 몸뚱이 하나 벌거숭이로 세상에 내던지지는 않겠다. 그러니 이 종이를 펼쳐 보면, 부지런히 애쓴다면 너로 하여금 떳떳한 생계를 꾸려 갈 수 있게 해 줄 무언가를 발견하게 될 것이다. 하지만 그것을 더 나쁜 데에 쓴다면, 나는 더 이상 너를 도울 의무가 있다고 여기지 않을 것이다. 오늘 이날부터, 어떤 이유로도 다시는 너와 말을 섞지 않기로 결심했기 때문이다. 그리고 이 말만은 하지 않을 수 없구나. 너의 모든 행실 가운데, 너에게 그토록 다정하고 명예롭게 대해 준 저 착한 젊은이(블리필을 가리킨 것이다)를 네가 모질게 대한 일만큼 내가 분개하는 일은 없다.\"\n\n이 마지막 말은 삼키기에 너무도 쓴 약이었다. 이제 존스의 두 눈에서 눈물이 홍수처럼 쏟아졌고, 말하고 움직이는 모든 능력이 그를 저버린 듯했다. 그가 떠나라는 올워디의 단호한 명령에 따를 수 있기까지는 얼마간의 시간이 걸렸다. 그는 마침내 그렇게 했는데, 먼저 올워디의 두 손에 입을 맞추었다. 꾸며 내기도 어렵고, 또 그만큼 묘사하기도 어려운 격정을 담아서.\n\n그때 존스가 올워디 씨에게 어떤 모습으로 비쳤는지를 헤아려 본다면, 그 선고의 엄격함을 탓하는 독자가 있다면 그는 어지간히 모자란 사람일 것이다. 그런데도 인근의 온 동네는, 그런 모자람 때문이든 아니면 더 못된 속셈 때문이든, 이 정의롭고 준엄한 처사를 더없이 잔인한 짓이라며 비난했다. 아니, 전에는 그 선량한 분이 사생아(세간의 통설로는 그 자신의 자식이라던)에게 베푼 친절과 다정함을 두고 흉보던 바로 그 사람들이, 이제는 그가 제 자식을 집 밖으로 내쫓는다며 한층 더 목청을 높였다. 특히 여인네들은 한목소리로 존스의 편을 들었고, 이 일을 두고 이 장에 다 적을 수 없을 만큼 많은 이야기를 지어냈다.\n\n한 가지 빠뜨려서는 안 될 것이 있다. 이 일을 두고 비난을 퍼붓는 가운데, 올워디가 존스에게 건넨 그 종이에 담긴 액수—그것은 무려 오백 파운드나 되었다—를 입에 올리는 사람은 아무도 없었다는 점이다. 다들 입을 모아, 그가 무자비한 아버지의 집에서 한 푼도 없이 쫓겨났다고 했고, 더러는 벌거숭이로 내쫓겼다고까지 말했다.\n\n연애편지 따위가 담긴 장.\n\n존스는 즉시 그 집을 떠나라는 명을 받았다. 옷가지며 그 밖의 모든 것은, 그가 어디로든 일러두기만 하면 그리로 보내 주겠다는 말도 함께 들었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 길을 나섰고, 1마일 남짓을 걸었다. 어디로 가는지 마음에 두지도 않았고, 사실상 어디로 가는지조차 거의 알지 못한 채였다. 마침내 작은 시냇물이 앞을 가로막자, 그는 그 곁에 몸을 던지듯 주저앉았다. 그리고 약간의 분함을 담아 이렇게 중얼거리지 않을 수 없었다. \"설마 아버지께서 이렇게 쉴 자리 하나야 마다하시지는 않으시겠지!\"\n\n여기서 그는 곧 더없이 격렬한 고뇌에 빠져들어, 머리카락을 쥐어뜯고, 광기와 분노와 절망의 발작에 흔히 뒤따르는 온갖 몸짓을 거듭했다.\n\n이런 식으로 격정의 첫 동요를 쏟아낸 뒤에야, 그는 조금씩 제정신을 되찾기 시작했다. 이제 그의 슬픔은 다른 방향으로 흘러, 한결 부드러운 방식으로 풀려나갔다. 마침내 그는 자신의 격정과 이치를 따져 볼 만큼 차분해졌고, 이 한심한 처지에서 어떤 조치를 취하는 것이 마땅한지를 헤아릴 수 있게 되었다.\n\n이제 가장 큰 고민은, 소피아를 두고 어떻게 처신할 것인가였다. 그녀를 떠난다는 생각만으로도 가슴이 거의 둘로 찢기는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀를 파멸과 거지 신세로 떨어뜨리게 된다는 생각은, 가능하다면, 그를 한층 더 고통스럽게 짓눌렀다. 설령 그녀를 차지하고 싶은 격렬한 욕망이 잠시나마 그로 하여금 이 다른 길에 귀 기울이게 만들 수 있었다 해도, 그토록 큰 대가를 치르면서까지 그녀가 그의 바람을 들어줄 결심을 하리라고는 결코 확신할 수 없었다. 올워디 씨의 노여움, 그리고 그분의 평온함에 끼칠 수밖에 없는 상처가 이 후자의 길을 강하게 가로막았다. 끝으로, 설령 그가 이 모든 고려를 다 희생한다 해도 그 일이 성공할 가망이 거의 없다는 명백한 사실이 그를 거들었다. 이리하여 마침내 절망이, 그리고 은인을 향한 감사와 연인을 향한 진실한 사랑이 뒤를 받쳐 준 명예가, 타오르는 욕망을 이겨 냈다. 그는 소피아를 파멸로 몰아가며 좇느니 차라리 그녀를 단념하기로 결심했다.\n\n이런 격정을 이겨 낸 이 승리를 처음 곱씹을 때 그의 가슴을 채운 그 뜨거운 온기를, 그것을 느껴 본 적 없는 사람이 떠올리기란 어려운 일이다. 자긍심이 어찌나 기분 좋게 그를 다독여 주던지, 그의 마음은 어쩌면 완전한 행복을 누렸는지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은 한순간뿐이었다. 곧 소피아가 그의 상상 속으로 되돌아와, 그 승리의 기쁨을 못지않게 쓰라린 아픔으로 가라앉혀 버렸다. 마치 마음씨 고운 장군이, 그 피를 대가로 자신의 월계관을 사들인 피투성이 시신 더미를 둘러볼 때 느낄 법한 그 아픔처럼. 우리의 정복자 앞에는 수천 가지 애틋한 생각들이 살해당한 채 누워 있었으니 말이다.\n\n그러나 거인 같은 시인 리가 이름 붙였듯 이 '거인 같은 명예'의 길을 걷기로 결심한 그는, 소피아에게 작별의 편지를 쓰기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 멀지 않은 어느 집으로 가서, 알맞은 필기도구를 갖춘 뒤 다음과 같이 써 내려갔다.\n\"아가씨,\n\n제가 이 글을 쓰고 있는 처지를 헤아려 주신다면, 이 편지에 담긴 어떤 모순이나 터무니없음도 당신의 고운 마음씨가 너그러이 용서해 주시리라 믿습니다. 여기 적힌 모든 것은 가득 차오른 가슴에서 흘러나온 것이라, 어떤 말로도 그 가슴이 일러 주는 바를 다 담아낼 수 없기 때문입니다.\n\n아가씨, 저는 당신의 명을 따르기로, 당신의 그 사랑스러운 모습으로부터 영원히 달아나기로 결심했습니다. 그 명은 참으로 가혹합니다. 그러나 그것은 운명에서 비롯된 가혹함일 뿐, 제 소피아에게서 나온 것이 아닙니다. 운명은, 저 같은 가련한 인간이 이 세상에 있었다는 사실조차 잊는 것을, 당신을 지키기 위한 필연으로 만들어 버렸습니다.\n\n믿어 주십시오. 제 모든 고통이 어떻게든 당신의 귀에 닿으리라 생각했다면, 저는 그것을 당신께 한 마디도 비치지 않았을 것입니다. 저는 당신 마음의 선함과 다정함을 압니다. 그래서 당신이 늘 불행한 이들을 보며 느끼는 그 아픔을 조금도 드리고 싶지 않습니다. 부디 제 모진 운명에 관해 들으시는 어떤 것도, 단 한순간의 근심거리조차 되지 않게 해 주십시오. 당신을 잃은 뒤로는, 제게 그 어떤 것도 하찮은 일에 지나지 않으니까요.\n\n오, 소피아! 당신을 떠나는 일은 견디기 어렵습니다. 당신께 저를 잊어 달라고 청하는 일은 더더욱 견디기 어렵습니다. 그러나 가장 진실한 사랑이 저로 하여금 그 두 가지를 다 하지 않을 수 없게 합니다. 저에 대한 어떤 기억이라도 당신께 불안을 안길 수 있으리라 여기는 저를 용서해 주십시오. 그러나 제가 이토록 영광스럽게 비참한 존재라면, 부디 당신의 위안을 위해 저를 모든 방식으로 희생물로 삼아 주십시오. 제가 한 번도 당신을 사랑하지 않았다고 여기십시오. 아니면, 제가 당신을 받을 자격이 얼마나 없는지를 있는 그대로 헤아리십시오. 그리고 아무리 혹독하게 벌해도 모자랄 이 주제넘음을 두고 저를 경멸하는 법을 배우십시오. 더는 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 수호천사들이 영원토록 당신을 지켜 주기를!\"\n\n이제 그는 봉랍을 찾으려 주머니를 뒤졌으나, 봉랍은커녕 그 안에 아무것도 없었다. 사실 그는 정신없이 날뛰던 와중에 모든 것을 내던져 버렸는데, 그중에는 올워디 씨에게서 받았으나 한 번도 열어 보지 않은 지갑도 있었다. 그 지갑 생각이 이제야 비로소 그의 머리에 떠올랐다.\n\n그 집에서 지금 쓸 봉함지 한 장을 얻은 그는, 그것으로 편지를 봉한 뒤, 거기서 잃어버린 물건들을 찾으러 서둘러 시냇가 쪽으로 되돌아갔다. 가는 길에 그는 옛 친구 블랙 조지를 만났는데, 조지는 그의 불운을 진심으로 위로해 주었다. 그 소식은 이미 그의 귀에, 아니 사실 온 동네 사람들의 귀에 닿아 있었기 때문이다.\n\n존스는 사냥터지기에게 자신이 물건을 잃어버렸다는 사정을 알렸고, 조지도 선뜻 그와 함께 시냇가로 돌아갔다. 그들은 존스가 다녀온 곳뿐 아니라 다녀오지 않은 곳까지, 풀밭의 풀포기 하나하나를 다 뒤졌다. 그러나 모두 헛수고였으니, 아무것도 찾지 못했기 때문이다. 사실 그 물건들이 그때 그 풀밭에 있기는 했으나, 그들은 그것들이 들어 있는 단 한 군데를 뒤지지 않았다. 다름 아닌 바로 그 조지의 주머니 속이었다. 그는 조금 전에 이미 그것들을 발견했고, 다행스럽게도 그 값어치를 알아챈 터라, 제 몫으로 쓰려고 아주 조심스레 챙겨 둔 것이었다.\n\n사냥터지기는 마치 그것들을 정말로 찾아 주기를 바라는 사람처럼 잃어버린 물건을 뒤지는 데 온 부지런을 떨고 나서, 존스 씨에게 혹시 다른 곳에 들른 적은 없는지 잘 떠올려 보라고 청했다. \"분명히,\" 그가 말했다. \"이렇게 얼마 전에 여기서 잃어버리셨다면, 물건들은 아직 여기 그대로 있어야 할 겁니다요. 이런 데는 누가 지나다닐 만한 곳이 도무지 아니니까요.\" 사실 그 자신이 그 들판을 지나게 된 것도 큰 우연 덕분이었다. 다음 날 아침 배스의 어느 닭장수에게 댈 산토끼를 잡으려고 올가미를 놓으러 온 길이었던 것이다.\n\n이제 존스는 잃어버린 것을 되찾을 모든 희망을, 아니 그에 관한 거의 모든 생각을 접었다. 그러고는 블랙 조지를 돌아보며, 세상에서 가장 큰 부탁 하나를 들어주겠느냐고 간곡히 물었다.\n\n조지는 다소 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"나리, 제 힘이 닿는 일이라면 무엇이든 분부만 하시면 되는 거 아시잖습니까요. 저야 진심으로 나리께 무슨 도움이라도 되었으면 좋겠습니다요.\" 사실 그 물음은 그를 흠칫하게 했다. 그는 웨스턴 씨 밑에서 사냥감을 팔아 제법 두둑한 돈을 모아 둔 터라, 존스가 자기에게 얼마간 돈을 꾸려는 것이 아닐까 겁이 났던 것이다. 그러나 소피아에게 편지 한 통을 전해 달라는 부탁임을 알자 그는 곧 그 불안에서 풀려났고, 큰 기쁨으로 그러겠노라 약속했다. 사실 나는 그가 존스 씨에게 기꺼이 베풀지 않았을 부탁이란 거의 없으리라 믿는다. 그는 자기 깜냥껏 그에게 감사하는 마음을 품고 있었고, 이 우주의 그 무엇보다 돈을 더 사랑하는 사람들이 대개 그러한 만큼은 정직했으니 말이다.\n\n이 편지를 소피아에게 전할 알맞은 통로로는 어너 부인이 적임이라는 데 둘 다 뜻이 모였다. 그런 다음 그들은 헤어졌다. 사냥터지기는 웨스턴 씨 댁으로 돌아갔고, 존스는 심부름꾼이 돌아오기를 기다리려고 반 마일 떨어진 어느 선술집으로 걸어갔다.\n\n조지는 주인집에 막 돌아오자마자 어너 부인을 만났다. 그는 먼저 몇 가지 미리 던지는 물음으로 그녀의 속을 떠본 뒤, 그녀의 아가씨에게 전할 편지를 건넸고, 동시에 그녀에게서 존스 씨에게 전할 또 다른 편지 한 통을 받았다. 어너는 그 편지를 온종일 가슴속에 품고 다녔으나 전할 길을 도무지 찾지 못해 단념하려던 참이었다고 그에게 말했다.\n\n사냥터지기는 서둘러, 그리고 기쁜 마음으로 존스에게 돌아왔다. 그에게서 소피아의 편지를 받아 든 존스는 곧장 자리를 물러나, 안달하며 봉투를 뜯어 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\"존스 씨,\n\n당신을 본 뒤로 제가 무엇을 느꼈는지는 말로 다 표현할 길이 없습니다. 저 때문에 아버지로부터 그토록 모진 모욕을 감내하신 일은, 제가 평생 두고 갚아야 할 빚으로 남겨 두었습니다. 당신은 아버지의 성미를 아시니, 부디 저를 위해서라도 아버지를 피해 주시기를 청합니다. 당신께 보내 드릴 무슨 위안거리라도 있으면 좋으련만. 다만 이것만은 믿어 주십시오. 더없이 극단적인 강압이 아니고서는, 당신이 보고 슬퍼하실 자리에 제 손과 마음을 결코 내어주지 않으리라는 것을요.\"\n\n존스는 이 편지를 백 번도 더 거듭 읽었고, 그만큼 백 번도 더 거기에 입을 맞췄다. 이제 그의 격정은 온갖 애틋한 욕망을 다시 그의 마음속으로 불러들였다. 그는 앞서 우리가 본 그런 식으로 소피아에게 편지를 쓴 것을 후회했다. 그러나 그보다 더 후회스러운 것은, 심부름꾼이 자리를 비운 그 사이를 틈타 올워디 씨에게 편지를 써 보낸 일이었다. 그 편지에서 그는 자신의 사랑에 대한 모든 생각을 단념하겠노라고 진심으로 약속하고 스스로를 묶어 두었던 것이다. 그러나 차분한 성찰이 되돌아오자, 그는 소피아의 짧은 편지로 자신의 처지가 나아지지도 달라지지도 않았음을 또렷이 깨달았다. 다만 그녀의 한결같음 덕분에, 훗날 어떤 좋은 일이라도 생기리라는 작은 희망의 빛 한 줄기를 얻었을 뿐이었다. 그리하여 그는 결심을 다시 다잡았고, 블랙 조지에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 5마일쯤 떨어진 어느 읍내를 향해 길을 떠났다. 그는 올워디 씨에게, 처분을 거두실 뜻이 없으시다면 자신의 물건을 그곳으로 부쳐 달라고 청해 둔 터였다.\n\n# Chapter xiii.\n\n소피아가 이번 일을 두고 보인 처신에 관하여. 같은 처지에서 똑같이 행동할 줄 아는 여인이라면 그 누구도 이를 탓하지 못할 것이다. 그리고 양심의 법정에서 다루어진 한 까다로운 쟁점에 관한 논의.\n\n소피아는 지난 스물네 시간을 그리 달갑지 않은 모습으로 보냈다. 그 시간의 상당 부분 동안 그녀는 고모로부터 분별에 관한 설교를 들어야 했는데, 고모는 상류 사교계의 본을 따르라 권했다. 그 점잖은 부인의 말씀에 따르면, 그 세계에서는 사랑이란 지금 완전히 비웃음거리가 되어 버렸고, 여인들은 결혼을 마치 남자들이 공직을 대하듯 여겨, 오로지 재산을 불리고 세상에서 자기 자리를 끌어올리는 수단으로만 본다는 것이었다. 이 본문을 풀이하며 웨스턴 부인은 몇 시간에 걸쳐 자신의 웅변을 펼쳐 보였다.\n\n이런 약삭빠른 설교들은 소피아의 취향이나 마음에 도무지 맞지 않았지만, 그래도 밤 내내 그녀를 사로잡은 자신의 생각보다는 덜 괴로웠다. 그날 밤 그녀는 단 한 번도 눈을 붙이지 못했다.\n\n그러나 잠도 못 자고 침대에서 쉬지도 못했으나, 달리 거기서 빠져나갈 일도 없었던 터라, 올워디 댁에서 돌아온 아버지는 침대에 누운 그녀를 발견했다. 아버지가 돌아온 것은 아침 열 시가 넘어서였다. 그는 곧장 그녀의 방으로 올라가 문을 열었고, 그녀가 아직 일어나지 않은 것을 보고는 외쳤다. \"오! 그래 넌 무사하구나. 그럼 이대로 무사하게 지켜 주마.\" 그러고는 문을 잠그고 그 열쇠를 어너에게 건넸다. 먼저 그녀에게 가장 엄한 분부를 내리며, 충실히 지키면 큰 상을 주겠다 약속하는 한편, 만일 그 소임을 저버리면 더없이 끔찍한 벌을 내리겠노라 으름장을 놓았다.\n\n어너가 받은 명령은 이러했다. 영주 본인의 허락 없이는 아씨를 방에서 나오지 못하게 할 것, 그리고 영주와 고모 외에는 그 누구도 들이지 말 것. 다만 어너 자신은 소피아가 원하는 무엇이든 곁에서 시중들되, 오직 펜과 잉크와 종이만은 예외로 하여 그 사용을 금했다.\n\n영주는 딸에게 옷을 차려입고 만찬에 자신을 모시라 일렀고, 그녀는 그대로 따랐다. 그리고 늘 그렇듯 만찬 자리에 앉아 있다가, 다시 자신의 감옥으로 끌려갔다.\n\n저녁이 되자 간수 어너가 사냥터지기에게서 받은 편지를 그녀에게 가져왔다. 소피아는 그 편지를 두세 번 아주 꼼꼼히 읽고는 침대에 몸을 던지며 눈물을 왈칵 쏟았다. 어너 부인은 아씨의 이런 모습에 크게 놀라움을 드러냈고, 이 격정의 까닭을 못내 간절히 알고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다. 소피아는 한동안 아무 대답도 하지 않다가, 갑자기 벌떡 일어나 하녀의 손을 붙잡고 외쳤다. \"오 어너! 난 끝장났어.\" \"원, 천만에요.\" 어너가 외쳤다. \"그 편지를 아씨께 가져다드리기 전에 차라리 태워 버렸더라면 좋았을 것을. 정말이지 그 편지가 아씨를 위로해 드릴 줄 알았어요. 아니었다면 손도 대기 전에 차라리 마귀에게나 줘 버렸을 거예요.\" \"어너.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"넌 착한 아이야. 더 이상 너에게 내 약함을 감추려 해도 소용없구나. 나는 나를 버린 남자에게 내 마음을 던져 버렸어.\" \"그럼 존스 도련님이,\" 하녀가 대답했다. \"그렇게 신의 없는 분이란 말씀이세요?\" \"그이는 저 편지로 나에게 영영 작별을 고했어.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"아니, 나더러 자기를 잊으라고까지 했어. 나를 사랑했다면 그런 걸 바랄 수 있었겠니? 그런 생각을 견딜 수 있었겠어? 그런 말을 쓸 수 있었겠어?\" \"없고말고요, 아씨.\" 어너가 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 잉글랜드에서 제일 잘난 남자가 저더러 자기를 잊으라 한다면, 저는 그 말 그대로 받아들이겠어요. 원, 어림없는 소리! 정말이지 아씨께서는 그분을 한 번이라도 마음에 두신 것만으로도 너무 과한 영예를 베푸신 거예요. 이 고장 젊은 남자들 가운데 누구든 골라잡으실 수 있는 아씨가 말이에요. 그리고 정말이지, 외람되이 제 변변찮은 의견을 말씀드려도 된다면, 젊은 블리필 도련님이 계시잖아요. 정직한 부모에게서 나신 데다 이 근방에서 제일가는 영주가 되실 분이고, 게다가 제 변변찮은 눈에는 절반은 더 잘생기고 절반은 더 점잖으신 분이에요. 더구나 품행이 점잖은 젊은 신사라서, 이웃 누구도 그분에게 흠 하나 잡지 못할 거예요. 지저분한 들병이를 쫓아다니지도 않고, 그분 문 앞에 갖다 놓을 사생아 하나 없으니까요. 잊으라니, 참! 저로 말하자면, 하느님 덕분에 두 번씩이나 잊으라는 소리를 들을 만큼 막바지 기도나 올리는 신세는 아니랍니다. 머리 달린 사내 가운데 제일가는 자가 저에게 그런 무례한 말을 감히 입에 올린다면, 이 나라에 다른 젊은이가 또 있는 한 다시는 그자와 어울려 주지 않을 거예요. 그러니 아까 말씀드린 대로, 정말이지 젊은 블리필 도련님이 계시잖아요.\" \"그 끔찍한 이름은 입에 올리지도 마.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"아니, 아씨.\" 어너가 말했다. \"아씨께서 그분이 싫으시다면, 조금만 부추겨 주셔도 아씨께 구애할 잘나고 흥겨운 젊은이들이야 얼마든지 더 있는걸요. 이 고장이든 옆 고장이든, 아씨께서 마음 있는 듯한 눈빛만 보내셔도 곧장 청혼하러 달려오지 않을 젊은 신사는 하나도 없을 거예요.\" \"넌 나를 어떤 못된 계집으로 여기기에,\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"그런 소리로 내 귀를 모욕하는 거니! 나는 사내란 사내는 다 진저리가 나.\" \"아니, 정말이지요, 아씨.\" 어너가 대답했다. \"아씨께서야 사내라면 물릴 만큼 겪으셨지요. 그런 가난하고 거지 같고 사생아 같은 자에게 함부로 대접받으셨으니.\" \"그 망측한 혀를 거두지 못할까.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"감히 내 앞에서 그이의 이름을 함부로 입에 담다니? 그이가 나를 함부로 대했다고? 아니야, 그 잔인한 말을 쓸 때 그이의 가엾고 피 흘리는 가슴이, 그것을 읽은 내 가슴보다 더 아팠을 거야. 오! 그이는 영웅 같은 덕과 천사 같은 선함 그 자체야. 나는 내 격정의 나약함이 부끄러워. 마땅히 우러러야 할 것을 탓했으니. 오, 어너! 그이가 헤아리는 것은 오직 나의 행복뿐이야. 나의 이익을 위해 그이는 자기 자신과 나를 함께 희생한 거야. 나를 망칠지 모른다는 두려움이 그이를 절망으로 내몬 거라고.\" \"참 다행이에요.\" 어너가 말했다. \"아씨께서 그것까지 헤아리신다니요. 정말이지, 집에서 쫓겨나 세상에 한 푼어치도 못 되는 사람에게 마음을 주는 건 신세를 망치는 일밖에 안 되니까요.\" \"쫓겨났다고!\" 소피아가 다급히 외쳤다. \"뭐라고! 그게 무슨 말이니?\" \"원, 정말이지요, 아씨. 우리 주인 나리께서 존스 도련님이 아씨께 사랑을 고백하려 했다는 걸 올워디 나리께 말씀드리자마자, 올워디 나리께서 그분을 발가벗겨 알몸으로 문밖에 내쫓으셨답니다!\" \"아!\" 소피아가 말했다. \"내가 바로 그이를 파멸시킨 저주받고 가련한 원인이로구나! 발가벗겨 문밖에 쫓겨나다니! 자, 어너, 내가 가진 돈을 다 가져가. 내 손가락에서 반지도 빼 가. 자, 내 시계도. 그이에게 다 가져다줘. 어서 가서 그이를 찾아.\" \"제발, 아씨.\" 어너 부인이 대답했다. \"한번 생각해 보세요. 주인 나리께서 이 물건들 가운데 하나라도 없어진 걸 아시면, 그 책임이 저에게 돌아온답니다. 그러니 부디 시계와 보석만은 내놓지 마시기를 빌게요. 게다가 돈이라면, 제 양심에 거리낌 없이 말씀드려도 충분하고도 남아요. 그리고 그 돈이야 주인 나리께서 결코 아실 수 없는 일이고요.\" \"그럼 여기,\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"내가 가진 마지막 한 푼까지 가져가서 당장 그이를 찾아내 건네줘. 가, 가, 한순간도 지체하지 마.\"\n\n어너 부인은 분부대로 길을 나섰고, 아래층에서 블랙 조지를 발견하고는 그에게 지갑을 건넸다. 그 안에는 열여섯 기니가 들어 있었는데, 사실 그것이 소피아가 가진 전 재산이었다. 아버지가 그녀에게 무척 후하긴 했으나, 그녀는 부자가 되기에는 너무도 베푸는 마음이 컸던 것이다.\n\n지갑을 받아 든 블랙 조지는 술집을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그런데 가는 길에 문득 이 돈도 슬쩍 가로채 버릴까 하는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 그의 양심이 곧장 이 부추김에 펄쩍 뛰며, 은인에게 배은망덕하다고 그를 나무라기 시작했다. 이에 그의 탐욕이 응수하기를, 양심이라면 진작에, 다시 말해 가엾은 존스에게서 오백 파운드를 가로챘을 때 이 문제를 따졌어야 했다고 했다. 그보다 훨씬 더 중한 일에는 잠자코 눈감아 놓고, 이런 하찮은 일에 새삼 거리끼는 척하는 것이야말로 어처구니없는 일이며, 노골적인 위선이나 다름없다는 것이었다. 이에 맞서 양심은 마치 노련한 변호사처럼, 이번처럼 물건이 정식으로 전달된 경우의 명백한 신의 위반과, 앞서처럼 그저 주운 것을 숨기기만 한 경우 사이를 구분 지으려 했다. 탐욕은 이를 곧장 비웃음으로 받아치며, 차이도 없는 구분이라 일컫고는, 일단 어느 한 경우에 명예니 덕이니 하는 핑계를 모조리 내던진 마당에, 두 번째 경우에 그것들을 다시 끌어다 쓸 전례란 없다고 딱 잘라 우겼다. 요컨대 가엾은 양심은 그 논쟁에서 영락없이 패하고 말았을 텐데, 마침 두려움이 끼어들어 양심을 도왔다. 두려움은 두 행위의 진짜 차이가 명예의 정도가 아니라 안전의 정도에 있다고 아주 힘주어 주장했다. 오백 파운드를 숨기는 것은 위험이 거의 없는 일이었던 반면, 열여섯 기니를 가로채는 것은 발각될 위험이 더없이 크다는 것이었다.\n\n이렇게 두려움이 우정 어린 도움을 베푼 덕분에, 양심은 블랙 조지의 마음속에서 완벽한 승리를 거두었다. 그리고 그의 정직함에 몇 마디 치하를 건넨 뒤, 그에게 그 돈을 존스에게 건네도록 만들었다.\n\n# Chapter xiv.\n\n웨스턴 영주와 그의 누이 사이에 오간 짧은 대화를 담은 짧은 장.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 그날 하루 종일 바깥일로 나가 있었다. 영주는 그녀가 집에 돌아왔을 때 그녀를 맞이했다. 그녀가 소피아의 안부를 묻자, 그는 그녀를 단단히 가둬 두었다고 일러 주었다. \"방 안에 자물쇠로 걸어 잠가 놨고,\" 그가 외쳤다, \"열쇠는 어너가 가지고 있소.\" 그가 누이에게 이 소식을 전할 때 그의 얼굴은 엄청난 지혜와 명민함으로 가득 차 있었으니, 아마도 그는 자기가 한 일에 대해 그녀에게서 큰 갈채를 기대했을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 더없이 경멸하는 표정으로 이렇게 외쳤을 때 그가 얼마나 실망했겠는가. \"정말이지, 오라버니, 오라버니는 모든 남자 중에서도 가장 무르고 어리석은 사람이에요. 어째서 조카딸을 다루는 일을 저한테 맡기지 않으시는 거예요? 어째서 끼어드시는 거냐고요? 제가 그 애 마음에 심어 주려고 그토록 숨이 차도록 애써 온 모든 것을, 오라버니가 지금 죄다 망쳐 놓으셨어요. 제가 그 애 마음을 신중함의 격언으로 채우려 애쓰는 동안, 오라버니는 그 애가 그것들을 내팽개치도록 자극하고 계셨던 거예요. 오라버니, 하늘에 감사하게도, 영국 여자들은 노예가 아니에요. 우리는 스페인이나 이탈리아 아내들처럼 가둬 둘 수 있는 존재가 아니라고요. 우리에게도 오라버니들 못지않게 자유에 대한 떳떳한 권리가 있어요. 우리는 오직 이성과 설득으로 납득시켜야 할 존재이지, 힘으로 다스릴 대상이 아니에요. 오라버니, 저는 세상을 두루 보아 왔고, 어떤 논리를 써야 하는지 알아요. 그러니 오라버니의 어리석음이 저를 가로막지만 않았더라면, 제가 예전에 그 애에게 가르쳤던 그 신중함과 분별의 규칙에 따라 처신하도록 그 애를 설득해 냈을 거예요.\" \"그렇겠지,\" 영주가 말했다, \"어차피 잘못은 늘 내 쪽이니까.\" \"오라버니,\" 부인이 대답했다, \"오라버니가 잘못된 게 아니에요, 오라버니의 식견을 벗어난 일에 참견할 때만 빼고요. 제가 세상을 누구보다 많이 보아 왔다는 건 인정하셔야 해요. 그 애가 제 보살핌에서 벗어나지만 않았더라면 얼마나 좋았을까요. 그 애가 사랑이니 뭐니 하는 그 허무맹랑하고 낭만적인 관념을 배운 건 다 오라버니랑 집에서 지냈기 때문이에요.\" \"설마,\" 영주가 외쳤다, \"내가 그 애한테 그런 걸 가르쳤다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지.\" \"오라버니의 그 무지는,\" 그녀가 받아쳤다, \"위대한 밀턴이 말했듯이, 제 인내심을 거의 무너뜨리는군요.\"[*] \"밀턴이고 뭐고!\" 영주가 대답했다. \"그놈이 뻔뻔하게 내 면전에 대고 그런 소릴 했다면, 제아무리 대단한 인물이라 해도 한 대 갈겨 줬을 거야. 인내심이라고! 그 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 누이, 인내심이 더 필요한 건 오히려 나야. 누이한테 다 큰 학생 녀석 취급이나 당하고 있으니 말이지. 궁정 같은 데나 드나들지 않으면 아무도 식견이란 게 없다고 생각하는 거야? 빌어먹을! 머리 깎은 청교도 놈들이랑 하노버 쥐새끼들 몇 마리 빼고는 우리가 죄다 바보라면, 세상 참 꼴 좋게 돌아가는 거지. 빌어먹을! 우리가 그놈들을 바보로 만들고, 사람마다 제 몫을 누리는 시절이 오기를 바라네. 그게 다야, 누이. 사람마다 제 몫을 누리는 거 말이지. 하노버 쥐새끼들이 우리 곡식을 죄다 갉아먹고 순무밖에 먹을 게 안 남게 되기 전에, 그날을 보고 싶구먼, 누이.\" \"이건 분명히 말하지만, 오라버니,\" 그녀가 외쳤다, \"이젠 제 식견을 한참 넘어선 얘기를 하고 계시네요. 순무니 하노버 쥐새끼니 하는 그 횡설수설은 저로선 도무지 알아들을 수가 없어요.\" \"암,\" 그가 외쳤다, \"그런 얘기 듣고 싶지 않은 거겠지. 하지만 그래도 시골 토박이들의 뜻이 언젠가는 이루어질 날이 올 거야.\" \"제 바람은,\" 부인이 대답했다, \"오라버니가 따님 일을 조금이라도 생각해 주셨으면 하는 거예요. 정말이지, 그 애는 나라보다 더 큰 위험에 처해 있으니까요.\" \"방금 전엔,\" 그가 말했다, \"내가 그 애 생각한다고 나무라더니, 그 애를 누이한테 맡기라며.\" \"오라버니가 더는 끼어들지 않겠다고 약속만 하신다면,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"제가 조카딸을 아끼는 마음으로 그 일을 맡겠어요.\" \"그럼 그러구려,\" 영주가 말했다, \"여자 다루는 데는 여자가 제일 적격이라는 데에 나도 늘 동의했으니 말이오.\"\n\n[*] 독자께서 이 구절을 밀턴에게서 찾으려 든다면, 아마 자신의 인내심부터 무너뜨리게 될지도 모른다.\n\n그러고 나서 웨스턴 부인은 여자들과 나라를 다스리는 일에 관해 경멸 어린 기색으로 뭔가를 중얼거리며 자리를 떴다. 그녀는 곧장 소피아의 방으로 향했다. 소피아는 하루 동안의 감금 끝에 다시 갇힌 신세에서 풀려난 참이었다.\n\n세상은 흔히 극장에 비유되어 왔다. 시인들뿐 아니라 점잖은 글쟁이들도 인간의 삶을 하나의 거대한 연극으로 여겨 왔으니, 거의 모든 면에서 테스피스가 처음 고안했다고 전해지는, 그리고 그 후로 교양 있는 모든 나라에서 그토록 큰 호평과 즐거움을 받아 온 저 무대 위 재현과 닮았다는 것이다.\n\n이 생각은 어찌나 멀리까지 나아갔는지, 또 어찌나 널리 퍼졌는지, 본래 극장에만 어울리던 몇몇 낱말, 처음에는 비유적으로 세상에 갖다 붙였던 그 말들이 이제는 양쪽 모두에 가리지 않고 글자 그대로 쓰이게 되었다. 그리하여 무대니 장면이니 하는 말은, 우리가 삶 전반을 이야기할 때나 연극 공연만을 두고 말할 때나 똑같이 익숙해졌다. 그리고 막 뒤에서 벌어지는 일을 입에 올리면, 우리 머릿속에는 드루어리 레인보다 세인트 제임스 궁이 떠오르기 십상이다.\n\n이 모든 것을 설명하기란 제법 쉬워 보일지도 모른다. 연극 무대란 실제로 존재하는 것의 재현, 아리스토텔레스의 말을 빌리자면 모방에 지나지 않는다는 점을 떠올리면 되니까. 그러니 어쩌면 우리는, 글이나 행동으로 삶을 어찌나 잘 흉내 냈던지 그 그림이 원본과 뒤섞이거나 원본으로 오인될 지경에 이른 이들에게, 마땅히 아주 높은 찬사를 보낼 만도 하다.\n\n하지만 사실 우리는 이런 사람들에게 찬사를 보내기를 그리 좋아하지 않는다. 오히려 아이들이 제 놀잇감을 다루듯 그들을 대하니, 그들의 뛰어남에 감탄하기보다는 야유하고 두들기는 데서 훨씬 큰 즐거움을 얻는다. 우리가 세상과 무대 사이의 이 유사함을 보게 된 데에는 그 밖에도 여러 까닭이 있다.\n\n어떤 이들은 인류의 대다수를 배우로 여겨 왔다. 그들이 자기 것도 아니고, 실은 그것에 대한 정당한 자격이라곤 배우가 자신이 연기하는 왕이나 황제로 진지하게 여겨질 자격만큼밖에 없는 인물을 흉내 내고 있다는 것이다. 그러니 위선자도 배우라 할 만하다. 실제로 그리스인들은 그 둘을 똑같은 하나의 이름으로 불렀다.\n\n삶의 덧없음 또한 이 비유가 나오게 된 빌미였다. 그리하여 불멸의 셰익스피어는 이렇게 노래했다—\n\n—삶이란 가련한 배우라, 무대 위에서 한때를 으스대고 안달하다가, 이내 더는 들리지 않게 되는구나.\n\n이 닳고 닳은 인용에 대한 보상으로, 나는 독자에게 아주 고귀한 시 한 편을 들려주려 한다. 아마 읽어 본 이가 거의 없으리라 믿는다. 그것은 아홉 해쯤 전에 출간되었다가 오래전 망각 속에 묻힌 《신성》이라는 시에서 따온 것이다. 좋은 사람이 늘 나쁜 사람보다 오래 살아남지 않듯, 좋은 책도 늘 살아남는 것은 아님을 보여 주는 증거이다.\n\n당신*에게서 모든 인간의 행위가 그 샘을 길어 올리나니, 제국의 흥기도, 임금의 몰락도 그러하도다! 펼쳐진 저 거대한 시간의 극장을 보라, 잇따라 나타난 영웅들이 무대 위를 밟고 지나가는 것을! 빛나는 형상들이 위풍당당하게 뒤를 이으니, 어떤 장수는 개선하고, 어떤 군주는 피를 흘리는가! 당신의 섭리가 맡긴 배역을 그들은 연기하나니, 그들의 자만도, 그들의 정념도 당신의 목적을 향해 기울도다. 한동안 그들은 대낮의 얼굴 앞에서 반짝이다가, 당신의 고갯짓 한 번에 그 허깨비들은 스러져 가도다. 그 분주하던 무대의 자취란 하나도 남지 않고, 다만 기억만이 말하는도다—그런 것들이 있었노라고!\n\n* 《신성》.\n\n그러나 이 모든 비유에서도, 그리고 삶을 극장에 빗댄 다른 어떤 비유에서도, 그 닮은 점은 언제나 무대에서만 끌어 왔을 뿐이다. 내 기억으로는, 이 거대한 연극의 관객을 헤아려 본 이는 아무도 없었다.\n\n하지만 자연이 흔히 만석의 객석을 앞에 두고 제 최고의 공연을 펼쳐 보이듯, 그 구경꾼들의 행동 또한 배우들의 행동 못지않게 앞서 말한 비유에 들어맞는다. 이 거대한 시간의 극장에는 벗과 비평가가 나란히 앉아 있다. 여기에는 박수와 환호가 있고, 야유와 신음이 있다. 한마디로, 왕립극장에서 보고 들을 수 있던 온갖 것이 다 있다.\n\n이것을 한 가지 예로 따져 보자. 이를테면, 자연이 앞 권 열두째 장에서 흔쾌히 펼쳐 보인 그 장면, 곧 검은 조지가 자기 벗이자 은인에게서 오백 파운드를 들고 달아나는 장면을 두고 그 거대한 관객이 보인 행동을 살펴보자.\n\n세상의 맨 위층 관람석에 앉은 이들은, 내가 확신하건대 그 사건을 언제나처럼 떠들썩하게 받아들였을 것이다. 아마도 그 자리에서 입에 담을 수 있는 온갖 상스러운 욕설이 터져 나왔을 것이다.\n\n그다음 등급의 구경꾼들에게로 내려가 보면, 똑같은 정도의 혐오를 발견하되 소란과 상스러움은 덜했을 것이다. 그렇더라도 여기서는 마음씨 고운 부인네들이 검은 조지를 악마에게나 가 버리라고 빌어 댔고, 그들 가운데 많은 이가 갈라진 발굽의 신사가 당장에라도 제 것을 거두러 오려니 하고 시시각각 기다렸을 것이다.\n\n일층 평토석은 늘 그렇듯 의견이 갈렸을 것이 분명하다. 영웅적인 덕과 완벽한 인물을 좋아하는 이들은, 이런 악행의 본보기를 내놓으면서 본보기 삼아 아주 호되게 벌하지도 않는다고 트집을 잡았다. 작가의 벗 가운데 몇몇은 이렇게 외쳤다. \"보십시오, 여러분, 이 사람이 악당이긴 하나, 그래도 이것이 인간의 본성이올시다.\" 그리고 당대의 젊은 비평가라는 자들, 곧 점원이며 견습공 따위는 그것을 천박하다 일컫고는 일제히 신음을 토했다.\n\n특별석으로 말하자면, 그들은 늘 그렇듯 점잖게 굴었다. 대부분은 딴 데 정신이 팔려 있었다. 그나마 장면에 조금이라도 눈길을 준 몇 안 되는 이들 가운데 더러는 그가 나쁜 부류의 사람이라 단언했고, 다른 이들은 가장 식견 있는 사람들의 의견을 들어 보기 전까지는 제 견해를 내놓기를 마다했다.\n\n자, 이 자연이라는 거대한 극장의 막 뒤로 들여보내진 우리는(이런 특권을 누리지 못하는 자라면 사전이나 철자 교본 말고는 아무것도 쓰지 말아야 마땅하다), 그 행위를 나무라되 그 사람 자체를 절대적으로 혐오하지는 않을 수 있다. 어쩌면 자연은 그를 자신의 모든 연극에서 악역만 맡도록 점지하지는 않았을지 모르니 말이다. 바로 이 점에서 삶은 무대와 더없이 똑같이 닮았다. 악당과 영웅을 같은 한 사람이 연기하는 일이 잦으며, 오늘 당신의 감탄을 자아낸 자가 내일이면 당신의 경멸을 끌어내기 십상이기 때문이다. 내가 비극에서만큼은 세상이 낳은 가장 위대한 천재로 꼽는 개릭이 이따금 어릿광대 노릇으로 몸을 낮추듯, 호라티우스에 따르면 위대한 스키피오와 현명한 라일리우스도 오래전에 그러했다. 아니, 키케로는 그들이 \"믿기 어려울 만큼 아이 같았다\"고 전한다. 하기야 이들은 내 벗 개릭처럼 그저 장난삼아 어릿광대 노릇을 한 것이 사실이다. 하지만 적잖은 저명한 인물들은 제 삶의 무수한 대목에서 진지하게, 그것도 어처구니없을 만큼 어릿광대짓을 했으니, 그들의 지혜가 우세한지 어리석음이 우세한지, 또 그들이 인류의 갈채와 비난, 감탄과 경멸, 사랑과 미움 가운데 어느 쪽을 받을 자격이 더 있는지가 자못 의심스러울 지경이다.\n\n실로, 이 거대한 극장의 막 뒤에서 얼마간 시간을 보낸 사람들, 그곳에서 걸치는 갖가지 변장뿐 아니라 이 극장의 지배인이자 연출가인 정념들의 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수 없는 행태에까지 훤히 정통한 이들은(특허권자인 이성으로 말하자면, 그는 지독히 게으른 자라 좀처럼 제 힘을 쓰지 않기로 소문이 났으니), 호라티우스의 그 유명한 닐 아드미라리, 우리말로 옮기자면 무엇에도 놀라 입을 벌리지 않는다는 말을 십중팔구 깨우쳤을 법하다.\n\n단 한 번의 못된 행위가 삶에서 악당을 만들지 못하는 것은, 무대에서 단 한 번의 못된 배역이 그러하지 못하는 것과 같다. 정념은 극장의 지배인처럼 사람의 판단을 묻지도 않고, 때로는 재능을 헤아리지도 않은 채 곧잘 어떤 배역으로 사람을 떠민다. 그러니 배우와 마찬가지로 사람도 자신이 연기하는 것을 스스로 못마땅해할 수 있다. 아니, 어떤 사람들에게 악덕이 어색하게 들러붙은 꼴이란, 마치 이아고 역이 윌리엄 밀스 씨의 정직한 얼굴에 들러붙은 것만큼이나 흔히 볼 수 있는 광경이다.\n\n그러니 통틀어 말하건대, 마음이 너그럽고 참된 식견을 갖춘 사람은 결코 섣불리 단죄하지 않는다. 그는 죄지은 당사자를 향해 분노를 터뜨리지 않고도 결함을, 심지어 악덕까지도 나무랄 수 있다. 한마디로, 삶에서나 무대에서나 온갖 아우성과 소란을 일으키는 것은 바로 그 똑같은 어리석음, 그 똑같은 유치함, 그 똑같은 무례함, 그 똑같은 심술이다. 가장 못된 자들이 으레 악당이니 불한당이니 하는 말을 입에 가장 많이 달고 다니는 법이니, 모든 가련한 자 가운데 가장 천한 이들이 평토석에서 천박하다고 외쳐 대기에 가장 능한 것과 같다.\n\n존스가 자기 자신과 나눈 대화에 관하여.\n\n존스는 이른 아침 올워디 씨 댁에서 자신의 소지품을 돌려받았는데, 그가 보낸 편지에 대한 답장이 함께 딸려 있었다.\n“귀하,\n\n“숙부님의 명을 받아 알려드립니다. 숙부님께서 귀하에게 취하신 그 조치들은 더없이 깊은 숙고를 거쳐, 그리고 귀하의 부덕함이 가장 명백히 드러난 뒤에야 비로소 내리신 것이니만큼, 귀하가 그 결심에 조금이라도 변화를 일으키는 일은 영원히 불가능할 것입니다. 숙부님께서는 귀하가 어느 젊은 숙녀에 대한 모든 미련을 단념했다고 말한 그 주제넘음에 크게 놀라움을 표하셨습니다. 그 숙녀의 태생과 재산이 귀하보다 한없이 위에 있으니, 귀하가 애초에 그런 미련을 품는 일 자체가 가당치 않았기 때문입니다. 끝으로, 숙부님께서 귀하에게 요구하시는 그분 뜻에 대한 단 하나의 순종은, 귀하가 즉시 이 고장을 떠나는 것임을 전하라 명하셨습니다. 한 사람의 그리스도인으로서 귀하에게 충고 한마디를 드리지 않고는 이 글을 맺을 수 없으니, 부디 귀하의 삶을 고치는 일을 진지하게 생각해보시기 바랍니다. 귀하가 그리할 수 있도록 은총의 도움을 받기를, 늘 다음과 같은 사람이 기도하겠습니다.\n“귀하의 미천한 종,\n\n“W. 블리필 올림.”\n\n이 편지는 우리 주인공의 마음속에 서로 다투는 여러 격정을 불러일으켰다. 그러나 결국 부드러운 감정이 분노하고 노여워하는 감정을 이겨냈고, 때맞춰 한바탕 쏟아진 눈물이 그를 도와주었으며, 어쩌면 그 불행이 그의 정신을 무너뜨리거나 그의 가슴을 터뜨리는 일을 막아주었는지도 모른다.\n\n그러나 그는 곧 이 위안에 빠져 있는 것이 부끄러워졌다. 그래서 벌떡 일어나며 이렇게 외쳤다. “좋아, 그렇다면 올워디 씨가 요구하시는 그 단 하나의 순종을 보여드리겠다. 지금 이 순간 떠나겠다. 하지만 어디로? 그래, 운명이 이끄는 대로 가자. 이 비참한 자가 어찌 되든 그것이 무슨 대수냐고 여길 사람이 달리 아무도 없으니, 나 자신에게도 똑같이 아무래도 좋은 일이 되게 하자. 다른 누구도 마음 쓰지 않는 것을 나 혼자 마음 쓸 까닭이 있을까. 아, 그런데 마음 써줄 사람이 또 하나 있다고 여길 만한 근거가 내게 없는 것은 아니지 않은가. 온 세상을 다 합친 것보다 값진 사람이! 나의 소피아만큼은 내가 어찌 되든 무심하지 않으리라고, 나는 그렇게 상상할 수 있다, 아니 상상해야만 한다. 그렇다면 이 유일한 벗을, 그것도 그런 벗을 떠나도 되는가. 그녀 곁에 머물러서는 안 되는가. 어디서, 어떻게 그녀 곁에 머문단 말인가. 설령 그녀가 나만큼 간절히 바란다 해도, 그녀를 아버지의 노여움에 내맡기지 않고서 그녀를 다시 볼 희망이 내게 있기나 한가. 게다가 그래봤자 무슨 소용인가. 그런 사람더러 제 파멸에 스스로 동의하라고 졸라댈 생각을 할 수 있겠는가. 그런 대가를 치르면서까지 내 격정 하나를 채워야 하겠는가. 그런 속셈을 품고 도둑처럼 이 고장을 어슬렁거려야 하겠는가. 아니다, 그런 생각은 경멸스럽다, 혐오스럽다. 잘 있어요, 소피아. 잘 있어요, 더없이 사랑스럽고 더없이 사랑하는 사람이여.” 여기서 격정이 그의 입을 막아버렸고, 그 격정은 그의 두 눈으로 출구를 찾았다.\n\n이제 이 고장을 떠나기로 결심한 그는, 어디로 가야 할지를 스스로 따져보기 시작했다. 밀턴의 표현을 빌리자면, 세상이 온통 그의 앞에 펼쳐져 있었다. 그리고 존스에게는, 아담이 그러했듯, 위로나 도움을 청하러 갈 만한 사람이 하나도 없었다. 그가 아는 사람이라고는 모두 올워디 씨가 아는 사람들이었고, 그 신사가 그에게서 호의를 거두어들인 마당에 그들에게서 어떤 두둔을 기대할 까닭도 없었다. 실로 위대하고 선한 인품을 지닌 사람들은 자기에게 딸린 이들을 내칠 때 매우 신중해야 마땅하다. 그 결과란 불행한 당사자가 다른 모든 사람에게서도 내쳐지는 일이기 때문이다.\n\n어떤 삶의 길을 좇을지, 또 어떤 일에 자신을 바칠지가 두 번째 고민거리였다. 그리고 여기서는 그 앞날이 온통 음울한 공허였다. 어떤 직업이든, 어떤 장사든 오랜 시간이 필요했고, 더 나쁜 것은 돈이 필요하다는 사실이었다. 세상의 이치란 이렇게 짜여 있어서, “무에서는 아무것도 나오지 않는다”는 격언은 자연의 이치에서만큼이나 세상살이의 이치에서도 참되며, 돈이 몹시 궁한 사람은 바로 그 이유로 돈을 얻을 모든 수단에서 완전히 배제되고 마는 것이다.\n\n마침내, 비참한 자들에게 너그러운 벗인 저 대양이 그를 받아들이려 너른 품을 활짝 벌렸다. 그리고 그는 즉시 그 다정한 초대를 받아들이기로 마음먹었다. 좀 덜 비유적으로 말하자면, 그는 바다로 나가기로 결심한 것이다.\n\n실로 이 생각은 떠오르기가 무섭게 그의 마음을 사로잡았다. 그는 곧장 말을 빌려, 그것을 실행에 옮기고자 브리스틀을 향해 길을 나섰다.\n\n그러나 이 여정에 그를 따라나서기 전에, 우리는 잠시 웨스턴 씨 댁으로 돌아가, 사랑스러운 소피아에게 그 뒤로 어떤 일이 더 일어났는지 살펴보기로 하자.\n\n여러 대화를 담은 장.\n\n존스 씨가 떠난 그날 아침, 웨스턴 부인은 소피아를 자기 방으로 불러들였다. 부인은 우선 소피아의 아버지로부터 그녀를 풀어 줄 자유를 얻어 냈다고 알린 다음, 결혼이라는 주제로 긴 강연을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 부인은 결혼을 시인들이 그려 온 것처럼 사랑에서 솟아나는 낭만적인 행복의 구상으로 다루지도 않았고, 신학자들이 가르치는바 신성한 권위로 제정된 것이라 여길 만한 어떤 목적도 입에 올리지 않았다. 오히려 부인은 결혼을, 분별 있는 여자들이 다른 어디서보다 더 큰 이자를 받기 위해 자신의 재산을 가장 유리하게 맡겨 두는 일종의 기금쯤으로 여겼다.\n\n웨스턴 부인이 말을 마치자 소피아가 대답했다. \"고모님처럼 뛰어난 지식과 경험을 갖추신 분과 논쟁할 만한 능력이 제게는 도무지 없습니다. 더구나 이 결혼이라는 문제처럼 제가 거의 생각해 본 적 없는 주제라면 더욱 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"나하고 논쟁이라니, 얘야!\" 부인이 받아쳤다. \"나도 그런 걸 바라는 건 정말 아니란다. 내가 네 또래의 아이와 논쟁이나 벌일 거라면, 그동안 세상을 헛산 셈이겠지. 내가 이 수고를 들이는 건 너를 가르치기 위해서야. 소크라테스나 알키비아데스 같은 옛 철학자들은 제자들과 논쟁하는 법이 없었어. 얘야, 너는 나를 소크라테스라고 여기면 된다. 네 의견을 묻는 게 아니라, 다만 내 의견을 일러 줄 뿐이니까.\" 이 마지막 말로 미루어 독자는 어쩌면, 이 부인이 알키비아데스의 철학에 대해 아는 만큼이나 소크라테스의 철학에 대해서도 읽은 바가 없으리라 짐작할지도 모른다. 그리고 솔직히 우리로서도 이 점에 관한 독자의 궁금증을 풀어 줄 길이 없다.\n\n\"고모님.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"저는 고모님의 어떤 의견에도 감히 맞서려 한 적이 없습니다. 그리고 이 주제로 말하자면, 말씀드렸듯이 아직 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없고, 어쩌면 앞으로도 영영 그러지 않을지 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 소피야.\" 고모가 대꾸했다. \"내 앞에서 그렇게 시치미를 떼는 건 무척 어리석은 짓이다. 프랑스인들이 오로지 제 나라를 지키기 위해 외국 도시들을 점령한다고 나를 설득할 수 없듯이, 네가 결혼을 진지하게 생각해 본 적이 한 번도 없다고 나를 믿게 만들 수도 없어. 네가 어떤 사람과 인연을 맺고 싶어 하는지를 내가 이미 다 알고 있는데, 어찌 너는 그런 결합을 궁리한 적 없다고 시치미를 뗄 수가 있느냐? 그 인연이라는 것이 얼마나 부자연스럽고 네 이익에 어긋나는지, 마치 네덜란드인들에게 프랑스와의 단독 동맹이 그들의 이익에 어긋나는 것과 똑같은데 말이다! 하지만 설령 네가 지금껏 이 문제를 생각해 본 적이 없다 해도, 분명히 말해 두마, 이제는 그럴 때가 무르익었다. 네 아버지가 블리필 씨와의 혼담을 당장 매듭짓기로 결심하셨으니까. 사실 나도 이 일에서 일종의 보증인 노릇을 하고 있고, 네가 응하리라고 약속까지 해 두었다.\"\n\n\"고모님.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"이 일만큼은, 고모님과 아버지 두 분 모두의 뜻을 거역할 수밖에 없는 유일한 경우입니다. 이 혼사는 거절하는 데 제가 거의 고민할 필요조차 없는 일이니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 소크라테스 본인만큼이나 대단한 철학자가 아니었다면,\" 웨스턴 부인이 응수했다. \"너는 내 인내심을 다 바닥내고도 남았을 게다. 그 젊은 신사에게 무슨 트집을 잡을 수 있단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"제 생각으로는 아주 확실한 트집이 있습니다.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"저는 그분이 싫습니다.\"\n\n\"너는 끝내 말을 제대로 쓰는 법을 못 배우겠느냐?\" 고모가 대꾸했다. \"정말이지, 얘야, 너는 베일리 사전이라도 들춰 봐야겠다. 네게 아무 해도 끼친 적 없는 남자를 미워한다는 건 있을 수 없는 일이야. 그러니 네가 미움이라고 하는 건 그저 싫음일 뿐이고, 그건 그와 결혼하지 않을 이유로는 충분치 않다. 나는 서로를 완전히 싫어하면서도 아주 편안하고 점잖은 삶을 누리는 부부를 여럿 보아 왔다. 내 말을 믿으렴, 얘야, 이런 일들은 내가 너보다 더 잘 안다. 너도 내가 세상을 좀 보아 왔다는 건 인정하겠지. 그 세상에서 내가 아는 여자들 가운데, 제 남편을 좋아한다고 여겨지기보다 싫어한다고 여겨지길 바라지 않는 이는 하나도 없다. 그 반대로 행동하는 건 한물간 낭만적 허튼소리라서, 상상만 해도 끔찍할 지경이야.\"\n\n\"고모님.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"저는 결코 싫어하는 남자와 결혼하지 않을 겁니다. 제가 아버지께 그분의 뜻에 어긋나는 결혼에는 절대 동의하지 않겠다고 약속드린다면, 아버지께서도 제 뜻에 어긋나게 저를 그런 처지로 몰아넣지는 않으시리라 바랄 수 있다고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"뜻이라고!\" 고모가 다소 발끈하여 외쳤다. \"뜻이라니! 네 그 당돌함에 기가 막히는구나. 네 나이의 처녀가, 그것도 미혼인 처녀가, 뜻 운운하다니! 하지만 네 뜻이 무엇이든, 내 동생은 이미 결심했다. 아니, 네가 뜻을 들먹이니, 나는 그더러 혼담을 서두르라고 권하겠다. 뜻이라니!\"\n\n그러자 소피아는 무릎을 꿇고 엎드렸고, 빛나는 두 눈에서 눈물이 방울방울 흘러내리기 시작했다. 그녀는 고모에게 \"자기를 가엾이 여겨 주시고, 스스로 비참해지기를 꺼리는 마음을 그토록 모질게 책망하지 말아 달라\"고 애원했다. 그러면서 \"이 일에 걸린 것은 오직 자기 하나뿐이며, 위태로운 것은 다른 누구도 아닌 자기 자신의 행복뿐\"이라고 거듭 호소했다.\n\n집행관이 영장으로 제대로 권한을 부여받아 어느 가엾은 채무자의 몸을 붙들고 나면, 그자의 눈물을 죄다 무심히 바라보는 것처럼. 그 비참한 포로가 동정을 자아내려 애써 본들 헛일이요, 동무를 빼앗긴 가련한 아내며, 재잘대는 어린 사내아이며, 겁에 질린 계집아이를 차마 떼어 놓지 못할 이유로 들먹여 본들 헛일이다. 그 고귀한 포박꾼은 온갖 비탄의 정경에 눈멀고 귀먹은 채, 인간다운 마음을 자극하는 모든 것을 한참 넘어서서, 그 가엾은 먹잇감을 옥졸의 손에 넘겨주기로 굳게 마음먹는다.\n\n수완 좋은 그 고모도 소피아의 눈물에 못지않게 눈멀어 있었고, 그녀의 어떤 애원에도 못지않게 귀먹어 있었으며, 떨고 있는 처녀를 옥졸 블리필의 품에 넘겨주려는 결심 또한 못지않게 단호했다. 부인은 격하게 대답했다. \"이 일에 걸린 게 너 하나뿐이라니, 천만의 말씀이다. 네 일은 가장 사소한, 아니 분명 가장 덜 중요한 것이야. 이 혼사에 걸린 것은 네 가문의 명예다. 너는 그저 도구일 뿐이야. 얘야, 너는 왕국과 왕국 사이의 혼인, 이를테면 프랑스의 공주가 에스파냐로 시집갈 때, 그 혼사에서 공주 자신만이 고려되리라 생각하느냐? 아니지! 그건 두 사람 사이의 혼인이라기보다 두 왕국 사이의 혼인이야. 우리 같은 대갓집에서도 마찬가지다. 가문과 가문 사이의 결합이야말로 으뜸가는 문제다. 너는 네 한 몸보다 네 가문의 명예를 더 귀히 여겨야 한다. 그리고 공주의 본보기로도 너에게 이런 고귀한 생각을 불어넣을 수 없다면, 적어도 모든 공주가 겪는 것보다 조금도 더 나쁘게 대접받는 게 아니니 불평할 수는 없겠지.\"\n\n\"고모님.\" 소피아가 목소리를 조금 높여 외쳤다. \"저는 제 가문을 욕되게 할 짓은 결코 하지 않겠습니다. 하지만 블리필 씨로 말하자면, 그 결과가 어떻든 간에 저는 그분을 거절하기로 마음먹었고, 어떤 힘으로도 그분을 위해 제 뜻을 꺾을 수는 없습니다.\"\n\n앞선 대화의 거의 대부분을 들을 수 있는 곳에 있던 웨스턴은 이제 인내심이 바닥나고 말았다. 그리하여 그는 사납게 격분한 채 방으로 들어서며 외쳤다. \"그럼 빌어먹을, 네가 그놈을 안 받겠다면 가만두지 않겠다, 빌어먹을, 가만두지 않아, 그게 다야, 그게 다라고. 빌어먹을, 안 받겠다면 가만두지 않겠다.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 소피아에게 쓰려고 충분한 분량의 노여움을 그러모아 두었지만, 이제 그것을 죄다 향사에게 쏟아부었다. \"동생아.\" 부인이 말했다. \"전적으로 내 교섭에 맡겨 둔 일에 끼어들다니, 어이가 없구나. 가문을 생각하는 마음에 내가 중재자 노릇을 자청한 것은, 네가 딸아이 교육에서 저지른 그 처신의 실책을 바로잡기 위해서였다. 동생아, 그 애의 여린 마음에 내가 전에 뿌려 둔 씨앗을 죄다 뽑아 버린 게 바로 너, 바로 너의 그 어처구니없는 행실이란 말이다. 그 애에게 불복종을 가르친 것이 다름 아닌 너 자신이야.\" \"제기랄!\" 향사가 입에 거품을 물고 외쳤다. \"누님은 악마의 인내심마저 거덜 낼 사람이오! 내가 언제 내 딸에게 불복종을 가르쳤단 말이오? 여기 그 애가 서 있으니, 정직하게 말해 보아라, 얘야, 내가 언제 너더러 나를 거역하라 시킨 적이 있느냐? 내가 네 비위를 맞추고 너를 흡족하게 해 주고 너를 내게 순종하게 만들려고 안 해 본 일이 있더냐? 누님이 그 애를 떠맡아 머릿속에 한 보따리 궁정 사상이나 가득 채워 그 애를 망쳐 놓기 전, 어린 계집애였을 적엔 나한테 아주 잘만 순종했단 말이오. 아니, 아니, 누님이 그 애더러 공주처럼 처신해야 한다고 말하는 걸 내가 엿듣지 않았소? 누님이 그 계집애를 휘그당원으로 만들어 놓은 거요. 그러니 제 아비든 다른 누구든 그 애에게서 어떻게 순종을 바랄 수 있겠소?\" \"동생아.\" 웨스턴 부인이 더없이 경멸하는 기색으로 대답했다. \"너의 그 온갖 정치 따위에 내가 품은 경멸은 이루 말로 다 할 수가 없구나. 하지만 그 애에게 내가 불복종의 원리를 조금이라도 가르친 적이 있는지, 이 아가씨 본인에게도 똑같이 물어보마. 오히려, 조카야, 내가 너에게 인간이라는 존재가 사회 속에서 맺는 여러 관계에 대한 참된 관념을 불어넣으려 애쓰지 않았더냐? 자식이 부모에게 도리를 다하는 것을 자연의 법칙이 명한다는 것을, 내가 한없는 공을 들여 보여 주지 않았더냐? 그 주제에 대해 플라톤이 뭐라고 했는지 일러 주지 않았더냐? 네가 처음 내 손에 맡겨졌을 때 그 주제에 대해 어찌나 형편없이 무지했던지, 나는 정말이지 네가 딸과 아버지 사이의 관계조차 모르는 줄 알았다.\" \"거짓말이오.\" 웨스턴이 대답했다. \"그 애가 그렇게 바보는 아니어서, 열한 살이 되도록 제가 제 아비의 혈육이라는 것도 모르고 살았을 리는 없소.\" \"오! 고트족도 무색할 무지로구나.\" 부인이 대답했다. \"그리고 동생아, 너의 그 버르장머리로 말할 것 같으면, 회초리를 맞아 마땅하다고 일러둬야겠다.\" \"그럼 누님이 할 수 있겠다 싶거든 나한테 한번 휘둘러 보시구려.\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"아니, 거기 누님 조카가 기꺼이 거들어 줄 것 같소만.\" \"동생아.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"내 너를 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 경멸하지만, 그래도 너의 그 무례를 더는 견디지 못하겠다. 그러니 당장 내 마차를 채비해 다오. 나는 바로 오늘 아침에 이 집을 떠나기로 마음먹었으니까.\" \"그것참 잘됐구려.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이왕 그렇게 됐으니, 나도 누님의 무례를 더는 못 견디겠소. 제기랄! 누님이 시도 때도 없이 나더러 경멸한다고 떠들어 대는 걸 들으면, 그것만으로도 내 딸이 제 아비의 분별을 깔보게 만들기에 충분하단 말이오.\" \"그럴 리가, 그럴 리가.\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"이런 촌뜨기를 깔볼 사람은 아무도 없다.\" \"촌돼지라니,\" 향사가 대답했다. \"나는 돼지가 아니오. 아니, 당나귀도 아니고, 쥐새끼도 아니오, 누님. 그것만은 기억해 두시오. 나는 쥐새끼가 아니란 말이오. 나는 진짜 잉글랜드인이지, 나라를 거덜 낸 하노버 종자가 아니오.\" \"너야말로 그 잘난 식자들 가운데 하나지.\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"그 터무니없는 신조로 나라를 망쳐 놓은 자들 말이다. 안으로는 우리 정부의 손발을 묶고, 밖으로는 우리 벗들의 기를 꺾고 적들의 기를 살려 주면서.\" \"허! 또 정치 타령으로 돌아왔구려?\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"그딴 것들로 말하자면, 나는 방귀만큼이나 우습게 여기오.\" 그러고는 이 마지막 말에다, 무엇보다도 그 말에 가장 어울리는 바로 그 행동을 곁들여 멋을 더했다. 그리고 웨스턴 부인의 심기를 가장 거스른 것이 그 말이었는지, 아니면 자신의 정치에 대해 드러낸 그 경멸이었는지는 내가 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 부인은 더없이 격렬한 분노에 휩싸여, 여기 옮기기에 마땅찮은 말들을 내뱉고는, 곧장 집을 박차고 뛰쳐나갔다. 동생도 조카도 그녀를 붙잡거나 뒤따라가는 것이 옳다고 여기지 않았다. 한 사람은 근심에, 다른 한 사람은 분노에 어찌나 사로잡혀 있던지, 거의 옴짝달싹 못 할 지경이었으니까.\n\n그래도 향사는 누이의 뒤를 향해, 사냥개들 앞에서 산토끼가 처음 튀어나와 달아날 때 따라붙는 바로 그 함성을 내질렀다. 그는 실로 이런 종류의 고함에 통달한 명수였고, 인생의 거의 모든 경우에 알맞은 함성을 하나씩 갖추고 있었다.\n\n웨스턴 부인처럼 세상을 알고 철학과 정치에 몰두해 온 여자들이라면, 자리에 없는 적수를 깎아내리는 대가로 웨스턴 씨의 분별을 추켜세우는 교묘한 찬사 몇 마디를 슬쩍 던져, 지금 그의 마음 상태를 곧장 이용해 먹었을 것이다. 하지만 가엾은 소피아는 온통 순박함 그 자체였다. 이 말로써 우리가 독자에게 그녀가 어리석었다고 넌지시 일러 두려는 것은 아니다. 흔히 어리석음이 순박함과 같은 뜻으로 통하긴 하지만 말이다. 사실 그녀는 더없이 사리 분별이 밝은 처녀였고, 그 명석함은 일류였으니까. 다만 그녀에게는, 여자들이 살아가며 그토록 많은 좋은 목적에 써먹는 그 쓸모 있는 술수가 도무지 없었다. 그리고 그 술수란 머리보다는 가슴에서 비롯되는 것이라, 흔히 여자들 가운데 가장 어리석은 이들의 몫인 경우가 많다.\n\n실생활에서 그려 낸 시골 귀부인의 초상.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 사냥꾼 특유의 함성을 마저 내지르고 잠시 숨을 고른 뒤, 자못 처량한 어조로 남자라는 존재의 가련한 처지를 한탄하기 시작했다. 그의 말인즉, 남자란 \"늘 어떤 빌어먹을 망할 계집의 변덕에 이리저리 휘둘리며 산다\"는 것이었다. \"내 생각엔 한 사내가 감당하기에 자네 어머니 하나만으로도 충분히 호되게 시달렸지. 그런데 겨우 그 여자를 한번 따돌려 놓았더니, 이번엔 또 다른 계집이 냄새를 맡고 내 뒤를 쫓아온단 말이야. 하지만 내 윗도리를 걸고 맹세하건대, 누가 이런 식으로 날 몰아세우게 두지는 않겠어.\"\n\n소피아는 이 불운한 블리필 사건이 있기 전까지, 어머니를 변호하는 일을 빼고는 아버지와 단 한 번도 다툰 적이 없었다. 소피아는 열한 살 되던 해에 어머니를 잃었지만, 그토록 애틋하게 사랑했던 어머니였다. 결혼 생활 내내 그 가엾은 여인은 향사에게 충실한 상급 하녀나 다름없었고, 향사는 세상에서 흔히 말하는 좋은 남편 노릇을 해 줌으로써 그 처신에 보답했다. 그는 아내에게 욕설을 퍼붓는 일이 좀처럼 없었고(아마 일주일에 한 번을 넘지 않았으리라) 손찌검은 결코 하지 않았다. 아내는 질투할 만한 일이라곤 조금도 없었고, 자기 시간을 온전히 주무를 수 있었다. 남편이 그녀를 방해하는 일이 전혀 없었기 때문이다. 그는 오전 내내 들판에서 사냥에 빠져 있었고, 저녁 내내 술친구들과 어울렸다. 사실 아내는 식사 때 말고는 남편을 거의 본 적이 없었는데, 식탁에서 그녀가 누리는 즐거움이란 미리 손질을 거든 음식을 직접 썰어 나누는 것 정도였다. 그녀는 이런 식사 자리에서 다른 하인들보다 오 분쯤 늦게 물러났는데, 그저 \"바다 건너 계신 임금님\"을 위한 축배를 들기 위해 남아 있었을 뿐이다. 듣자 하니 그것이 웨스턴 씨의 분부였다. 그에게는 여자란 첫 요리와 함께 들어와 첫 잔을 마신 뒤 나가야 한다는 신조가 있었기 때문이다. 이 분부를 따르는 일은 아마 그리 어렵지 않았을 것이다. 그 자리의 대화란(그것을 대화라 부를 수 있다면) 귀부인을 즐겁게 할 만한 것이 좀처럼 못 되었으니 말이다. 그것은 주로 고함치기, 노래 부르기, 사냥 무용담 늘어놓기, 음담패설, 그리고 여자와 정부(政府)에 대한 욕설로 이루어져 있었다.\n\n그렇지만 웨스턴 씨가 아내를 보는 때라곤 이런 자리뿐이었다. 그가 아내의 침상에 들 무렵이면 으레 너무 취해서 앞이 보이지 않았고, 사냥철이면 동도 트기 전에 자리를 떴기 때문이다. 이리하여 그녀는 자기 시간을 온전히 누렸고, 게다가 보통은 마음대로 부릴 수 있는 사두마차까지 거느렸다. 다만 안타깝게도 동네가 험하고 길이 나빠서 이것이 별 쓸모가 없었다. 제 목숨을 조금이라도 아끼는 사람이라면 그 길을 지나려 하지 않았을 테고, 제 시간을 조금이라도 아끼는 사람이라면 그런 동네를 찾아오려 하지 않았을 테니 말이다. 자, 독자에게 솔직히 털어놓자면, 그녀는 이토록 넘치는 너그러움에 기대되는 만큼의 보답을 다 하지는 못했다. 그녀는 다정한 아버지의 뜻에 떠밀려 마음에도 없이 결혼했는데, 따지고 보면 이 혼사는 오히려 그녀 쪽에 득이 되는 것이었다. 향사의 재산은 한 해에 삼천 파운드가 넘었던 반면, 그녀의 지참금은 고작 팔천 파운드에 불과했기 때문이다. 그래서인지 그녀는 약간 우울한 기질을 품게 된 듯했다. 그녀는 좋은 아내라기보다는 차라리 좋은 하녀에 가까웠고, 향사가 떠들썩한 환희로 맞아 줄 때조차 그것에 고마운 미소 한 번으로 화답할 만한 마음의 여유가 늘 있었던 것도 아니다. 게다가 그녀는 이따금 자신과 상관없는 일에 끼어들곤 했는데, 이를테면 남편의 지나친 음주 같은 것이었다. 그녀는 남편이 드물게 내어 주는 몇 안 되는 기회를 틈타, 더없이 부드러운 말로 그것을 나무라곤 했다. 그리고 평생 단 한 번, 그녀는 두 달만 런던에 데려가 달라고 진심으로 간청한 적이 있었는데, 남편은 단호히 거절했다. 아니, 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그는 그런 청을 했다는 이유만으로 줄곧 아내에게 화를 냈다. 런던의 남편이란 죄다 오쟁이 진 자들이라고 굳게 믿었기 때문이다.\n\n이 마지막 이유에다 다른 여러 그럴듯한 이유까지 더해져, 웨스턴은 마침내 진심으로 아내를 미워하게 되었다. 그는 아내가 죽기 전에도 이 증오를 결코 숨기지 않았거니와, 죽은 뒤에도 결코 잊지 않았다. 냄새가 잘 풍기지 않는 날이라든가 사냥개들 사이에 병이 돈다든가 그 밖의 비슷한 불운처럼, 조금이라도 그의 비위를 거스르는 일이 생기면 그는 어김없이 죽은 아내를 향한 독설로 울분을 풀어냈다. \"내 마누라가 지금 살아 있었다면 이걸 보고 좋아 죽었을 거다\"라고 말이다.\n\n이런 독설을 그는 특히 소피아 앞에서 내뱉고 싶어 안달했다. 그는 다른 누구보다 소피아를 사랑했던 만큼, 딸이 자기보다 어미를 더 사랑했다는 사실에 정말로 질투를 느꼈기 때문이다. 그리고 이런 자리에서 소피아는 그 질투를 부추기지 않는 법이 거의 없었다. 그는 어미를 욕하는 말로 딸의 귀를 더럽히는 것만으로는 성에 차지 않아, 그 모든 욕설에 딸이 노골적으로 동조하도록 억지로 강요하려 들었다. 하지만 그 어떤 약속이나 협박으로도 딸을 그 뜻에 따르게 할 수는 없었다.\n\n그래서 내 독자 가운데 어떤 이들은 어쩌면 의아해할지도 모르겠다. 향사가 어째서 소피아를 그 어미만큼 미워하지 않았는가 하고 말이다. 그러나 이것만은 일러두어야겠다. 증오란 질투라는 매개를 거친다 해도 사랑의 산물은 아니라는 것을. 질투에 사로잡힌 사람이 그 질투의 대상을 죽이는 일은 얼마든지 있을 수 있으나, 그 대상을 미워하는 일은 그렇지 않다. 이 견해는 제법 씹어 삼키기 어려운 한 덩어리인 데다 어딘가 역설의 분위기를 풍기니, 독자가 이 장이 끝날 때까지 그것을 곱씹어 보도록 맡겨 두기로 하자.\n\n소피아가 고모에게 보인 너그러운 태도.\n\n소피아는 아버지가 앞서 늘어놓은 말이 이어지는 동안 줄곧 입을 다물었고, 한숨 말고는 단 한 번도 다른 대꾸를 하지 않았다. 그러나 아버지는 눈빛이라는 언어를, 그가 부르는 말로 하자면 그 \"눈짓\"이라는 것을 전혀 알아듣지 못하는 사람이었기에, 자기 생각에 대한 좀 더 분명한 동의를 얻지 못하고는 만족하지 못했다. 그래서 그는 이제 딸에게 그것을 요구하며, 평소 하던 식으로 일러두었다. \"너는 늘 그 빌어먹을 네 어미 편을 들었던 것처럼, 이제 나를 거슬러 누구 편이든 들 채비가 되어 있겠지.\" 소피아가 여전히 잠자코 있자, 그는 버럭 소리쳤다. \"뭐냐, 벙어리라도 됐냐? 왜 말을 안 해? 네 어미가 나한테 빌어먹을 화냥년이 아니었더냐? 그것부터 대답해 봐라. 뭐냐, 너도 이 아비를 깔보고, 말 한마디 섞기에도 모자란 인간이라 여기는 게로구나?\"\n\n\"제발요, 아버지.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"제 침묵을 그토록 모질게 받아들이지 말아 주세요. 저는 아버지께 조금이라도 무례를 저지르느니 차라리 죽는 편이 낫다고 정말로 생각해요. 하지만 제가 어찌 감히 입을 열 수 있겠어요? 한마디 한마디가 사랑하는 아버지의 마음을 상하게 하거나, 아니면 가장 훌륭한 어머니의 추억에 대해 가장 새카만 배은망덕과 불효를 스스로 인정하는 꼴이 될 텐데요. 제게 어머니는 언제나 그런 분이셨다고 저는 확신하니까요.\"\n\n\"그리고 네 고모도 가장 훌륭한 누이라는 말이겠지!\" 지주가 받아쳤다. \"그래, 고맙게도 그 사람이 화냥년이라는 것 정도는 인정해 주겠느냐? 그 정도는 내가 떳떳이 우겨도 되겠지, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 아버지.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"저는 고모께 큰 신세를 졌어요. 제게는 두 번째 어머니나 다름없는 분이셨지요.\"\n\n\"나한테는 두 번째 아내이기도 했지.\" 웨스턴이 응수했다. \"그러니 너는 그 사람 편도 들겠다는 거로구나! 그 여자가 세상에서 가장 못된 누이 노릇을 했다는 것조차 인정 못 하겠다 이 말이지?\"\n\n\"맹세코, 아버지.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"그렇게 말한다면 저는 제 마음을 사악하게 속이는 거예요. 고모와 아버지의 생각하시는 방식이 무척 다르다는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 저는 고모가 아버지를 향해 더없이 깊은 애정을 표하시는 걸 천 번도 더 들었어요. 그리고 저는 확신해요. 고모는 세상에서 가장 못된 누이이기는커녕, 오라버니를 그보다 더 사랑하는 누이는 거의 없다고요.\"\n\n\"그 말을 알아듣기 쉽게 풀면,\" 지주가 대답했다. \"결국 내가 틀렸다는 소리로구나. 그래, 분명히 그렇지. 암, 틀림없이 여자가 옳고, 남자는 언제나 그른 법이렷다.\"\n\n\"용서하세요, 아버지.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"저는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"뭘 말하지 않았다는 거냐?\" 아버지가 대꾸했다. \"네가 뻔뻔스럽게도 그 사람이 옳다고 했으니, 그러면 당연히 내가 그르다는 결론이 따라 나오는 게 아니냐? 하기야, 그런 장로교도에 하노버 패거리인 화냥년을 내 집에 들인 내가 그른 건지도 모르지. 내가 알 게 뭐냐, 그 여자가 나를 음모죄로 고발해서 내 영지를 정부에 넘겨 버릴지도 모르는 일이야.\"\n\n\"아버지를 해치거나 영지를 가로채기는커녕요, 아버지.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"고모가 어제 돌아가셨다면, 저는 고모가 자기 재산을 몽땅 아버지께 남기셨으리라고 확신해요.\"\n\n소피아가 그것을 의도했는지 아닌지는 내가 함부로 단언하지 않겠다. 그러나 이 마지막 말이 아버지의 귀에 아주 깊숙이 파고들어, 앞서 그녀가 한 어떤 말보다 훨씬 더 뚜렷한 효과를 냈다는 것만은 분명하다. 그는 머리에 총알을 맞은 사람과 거의 똑같은 동작으로 그 소리를 받아들였다. 그는 흠칫 놀라 비틀거리며 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다. 그러고는 일 분 넘게 입을 다물고 있다가, 이윽고 다음과 같이 더듬거리며 입을 뗐다. \"어제라고! 그 여자가 어제 나한테 영지를 남겼을 거라고! 그랬을 거란 말이지? 하필이면 일 년 중에 왜 어제냐? 내 짐작에는 그 여자가 내일 죽으면, 영지를 딴 사람한테 남길 테고, 어쩌면 집안 밖으로 흘려보낼지도 모르겠구나.\" \"고모는요, 아버지.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"성정이 무척 격하셔서, 그 격정에 휩싸이면 무슨 일을 하실지 저로서는 장담할 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"장담할 수가 없다고!\" 아버지가 받아쳤다. \"그래, 도대체 그 여자를 그런 격정에 빠뜨린 빌미를 댄 게 누구냐? 아니, 실제로 그 여자를 그 지경으로 몰아넣은 게 누구냔 말이다? 내가 방에 들어오기도 전에 너와 그 여자가 한창 맞붙어 있지 않았더냐? 게다가 우리 싸움이 죄다 너 때문에 벌어진 게 아니더냐? 내가 누이와 다툰 게 이 여러 해 동안 죄다 너 때문이었는데, 이제 와서 너는 그 모든 허물을 나한테 뒤집어씌우려 하는구나. 마치 그 여자가 영지를 집안 밖으로 빼돌리게 만든 빌미가 나라는 듯이 말이야. 하기야 더 나은 걸 바랄 수도 없었지. 이게 내가 너한테 쏟은 그 온갖 애정에 대해 네가 돌려주는 보답이로구나.\"\n\n\"그러면 제발 부탁드려요.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"무릎 꿇고 비옵나니, 만일 제가 이 불화의 불행한 빌미가 되었다면, 부디 고모와 화해를 시도하시고, 고모가 이렇게 격한 노여움에 사로잡힌 채 아버지 집을 떠나시게 두지 말아 주세요. 고모는 본디 마음씨가 무척 너그러운 분이라, 정중한 말 몇 마디면 풀어지실 거예요. 부디 청을 들어주세요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"그래서 내가 가서 네 잘못을 대신 빌어야 한다 이 말이냐?\" 웨스턴이 대답했다. \"네가 토끼를 놓쳤는데, 그걸 다시 찾겠다고 내가 사방으로 그물을 끌고 다녀야 한단 말이지? 하기야, 내가 확신만 든다면야……\" 여기서 그는 말을 멈췄고, 소피아가 더욱 간곡히 매달리자 마침내 그를 설득해 냈다. 그리하여 그는 딸을 향해 신랄하고 비꼬는 말을 두세 마디 내뱉은 뒤, 누이의 마차가 채비되기 전에 그녀를 붙잡으려고 할 수 있는 한 빠르게 자리를 떴다.\n\n소피아는 그러고 나서 슬픔에 잠긴 자기 방으로 돌아갔고, 거기서 (이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 애틋한 비탄의 온갖 호사를 마음껏 누렸다. 그녀는 존스에게서 받은 편지를 한 번 넘게 거듭 읽었다. 토시 역시 이 자리에 동원되었다. 그녀는 그 둘을, 그리고 자기 자신까지도 눈물로 흠뻑 적셨다. 이런 처지에서 다정한 어너 부인은 비탄에 빠진 안주인을 위로하려 온갖 재주를 다 부렸다. 그녀는 여러 젊은 신사들의 이름을 줄줄이 읊었고, 그들의 재능과 외모를 한껏 추켜세운 뒤, 소피아에게 그중 누구든 마음대로 고를 수 있다고 장담했다. 이런 방법들은 분명 비슷한 종류의 병에 어느 정도 성공적으로 쓰였을 것이 틀림없다. 그렇지 않고서야 어너 부인처럼 노련한 시술자가 감히 이를 처방하려 들지는 않았을 테니까. 아니, 내가 듣기로는 하녀들의 학계에서 이 방법들을 여성용 약전에 실린 그 어떤 약 못지않은 만병통치약으로 여긴다고 한다. 다만 소피아의 병이 겉으로 드러난 증상은 그런 사례들과 일치하면서도 속으로는 달랐던 탓인지 어떤지, 나는 단언하지 않겠다. 그러나 사실은, 이 착한 시녀는 득보다 해를 더 끼쳤고, 끝내는 안주인의 화를 어찌나 돋우었던지 (이는 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었다) 안주인이 성난 목소리로 그녀를 제 앞에서 물러가게 하고 말았다.\n\n다양한 사건을 담은 장.\n\n지주는 누이가 막 마차에 오르려는 찰나에 그녀를 따라잡았고, 반은 힘으로, 반은 간청으로 설득하여 말들을 다시 마구간으로 돌려보내라고 명하게 만들었다. 이 시도는 큰 어려움 없이 성공했다. 우리가 이미 넌지시 일러두었듯, 그녀는 더없이 누그러지기 쉬운 성품이었고, 비록 그의 재능을, 아니 그보다는 세상사에 대한 얕은 식견을 업신여기기는 했어도 오라비를 몹시 아꼈기 때문이다.\n\n가엾은 소피아는 이 화해의 물꼬를 처음 튼 장본인이었으나, 이제 도리어 그 화해의 제물이 되고 말았다. 두 사람은 그녀의 행실을 한목소리로 나무랐고, 합심하여 그녀에게 선전포고를 했으며, 곧장 의논에 들어가 어떻게 하면 이 일을 가장 힘차게 밀어붙일 수 있을지를 짰다. 이를 위해 웨스턴 부인은 올워디와의 협약을 즉각 매듭지을 뿐 아니라, 그 즉시 실행에 옮기자고 제안했다. 그녀의 말은 이러했다. \"조카아이를 다룰 길은 격렬한 방법 말고는 없어요. 소피아에게는 그것을 버텨 낼 만한 결단력이 없다고 나는 확신해요. 격렬하다는 건,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"차라리 다급하다는 뜻이에요. 감금이나 노골적인 완력 같은 건 시도해서도 안 되고, 시도할 수도 없으니까요. 우리 계획은 기습을 위한 것이어야지, 정면 돌격을 위한 것이어선 안 돼요.\"\n\n이런 일들이 결정되었을 무렵, 블리필 씨가 자기 정인을 찾아 방문하러 왔다. 지주는 그가 도착했다는 말을 듣자마자 누이의 조언에 따라 한쪽으로 비켜서서, 딸에게 구혼자를 제대로 맞이하라는 명을 내렸다. 그는 딸이 거절할 경우를 두고 더없이 모진 악담과 천벌의 선고를 퍼부으며 그 명을 내렸다.\n\n지주의 격정은 앞을 가로막는 모든 것을 휩쓸어 버렸고, 소피아는 그녀의 고모가 매우 현명하게 내다보았던 대로 그를 당해 낼 수 없었다. 그리하여 그녀는 블리필을 만나는 데 동의했지만, 그 승낙을 입 밖에 낼 기운도 힘도 거의 남아 있지 않았다. 사실, 그토록 애틋하게 사랑하는 아버지에게 단호한 거절을 들이대는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었다. 만일 이 사정만 빠져 있었더라면, 그녀가 실제로 지닌 결단력보다 훨씬 적은 것으로도 아마 충분했을 것이다. 그러나 대체로 사랑에서 비롯된 행동을 전적으로 두려움 탓으로 돌리는 일은 그리 드물지 않다.\n\n그리하여 아버지의 단호한 명에 따라, 소피아는 이제 블리필 씨의 방문을 허락했다. 이런 장면들은, 우리가 이미 짚었듯이, 장황하게 그려 놓으면 독자에게 거의 즐거움을 주지 못한다. 그러므로 여기서 우리는 호라티우스의 한 가지 법칙을 엄격히 따르려 한다. 그 법칙은 작가들에게, 빛나게 그려 낼 가망이 없는 모든 소재는 건너뛰라고 일러 준다. 우리가 생각건대 이는 시인에게뿐 아니라 역사가에게도 더없이 쓸모 있는 법칙이며, 이를 따른다면 적어도 이런 좋은 효과는 있을 것이다. 많은 거대한 악(큰 책을 두고들 그렇게 부르니 말이다)이 이로써 작은 악으로 줄어들리라는 것이다.\n\n이 면담에서 블리필이 구사한 대단한 기술이 만일 같은 처지의 다른 남자였다면 소피아로 하여금 그를 속내를 털어놓을 상대로 삼고 마음속 비밀을 모두 드러내게 했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀는 이 젊은 신사에 대해 이미 너무도 나쁜 인상을 품고 있던 터라, 그에게 어떤 신뢰도 두지 않기로 마음먹은 뒤였다. 순박함이란 일단 경계 태세를 갖추고 나면 흔히 교활함과도 맞설 만하기 때문이다. 그래서 그를 대하는 그녀의 태도는 온통 억지스러운 것이었고, 실로 장차 남편이 될 사람의 두 번째 공식 방문을 받을 때 처녀들에게 으레 권해지는 그런 식이었다.\n\n그러나 블리필이 지주에게 자신이 받은 응대에 더없이 만족한다고 떠벌렸음에도, 누이와 함께 그 모든 것을 엿들었던 그 신사는 그다지 흡족하지 않았다. 그는 그 현명한 부인의 조언에 따라 일을 되도록 빨리 밀어붙이기로 작정하고, 사위가 될 사람을 향해 사냥꾼들이 쓰는 말투로 큰 소리로 \"워어!\" 외친 뒤 이렇게 외쳤다. \"쫓아라, 이 사람아, 쫓아가! 달려들어, 달려들라고. 그렇지, 옳거니. 잡았다, 잡았어, 잡았다고. 절대 수줍어하지 말고, '할까 말까' 하며 머뭇대지도 말게. 올워디와 나는 오늘 오후에 우리끼리 모든 일을 매듭지을 수 있으니, 혼례는 내일 치르세.\"\n\n블리필은 더할 나위 없는 만족을 얼굴 가득 띄우며 대답했다. \"어르신, 이 세상에서 제가 그토록 간절히 바라는 것은 어르신 가문과 맺어지는 일뿐이고, 그에 견줄 만한 건 오직 더없이 사랑스럽고 가치 있는 소피아 양과 한 몸이 되는 일뿐입니다. 그러니 저의 두 가지 가장 큰 소원을 제 손안에 거머쥐는 그 순간을 얼마나 애타게 기다리고 있을지 쉬이 짐작하실 겁니다. 제가 이 일을 두고 어르신을 졸라 대지 않은 까닭이 있다면, 그것은 오직 이 더없이 복된 일을 예법과 격식의 온갖 규칙이 허락하는 것보다 더 빨리 서둘러 몰아붙이다가 소피아 양의 마음을 상하게 할까 두려웠던 탓이라 여겨 주십시오. 하지만 어르신의 힘으로 그녀가 어떤 격식 따위는 면제하도록 이끌어질 수만 있다면—\"\n\n\"격식이라니, 빌어먹을!\" 지주가 대꾸했다. \"쳇, 다 부질없는 헛소리야! 내 자네에게 이르노니, 그애는 내일 자네를 맞을 걸세. 자네도 내 나이쯤 되면 세상을 더 잘 알게 될 게야. 이 사람아, 여자란 어떻게든 피할 수만 있으면 결코 승낙 같은 건 하지 않는 법이야. 그게 세상 이치라네. 만일 내가 그애 어미의 승낙을 기다렸더라면, 나는 오늘까지도 홀아비 신세였을 게야. 어서 그애한테 가게, 가, 가라니까, 그렇지, 이 유쾌한 친구야. 내 자네에게 이르노니, 내일 아침에 그애를 맞게 될 걸세.\"\n\n블리필은 지주의 거센 언변에 못 이기는 척 자신을 내맡겼다. 그리하여 웨스턴이 바로 그날 오후에 올워디와 합의를 매듭짓기로 정해지자, 이 구혼자는 집으로 떠났다. 다만 떠나기에 앞서, 이렇게 서두르다가 소피아 양에게 어떤 폭력도 가해지지 않기를 간곡히 빌었다. 그것은 마치 교황파의 종교재판관이, 교회가 이미 판결을 내린 이단자를 세속 권력에 넘기면서 그에게 어떤 폭력도 가하지 말라고 비는 것과 똑같은 모양새였다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 블리필은 소피아에게 이미 판결을 내린 셈이었다. 그가 웨스턴에게 자신이 받은 응대에 만족한다고 아무리 떠벌렸어도, 그가 흡족했던 단 한 가지는 자기 정인이 자신을 미워하고 경멸한다는 사실을 확신했다는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 이것은 그의 안에서도 똑같은 미움과 경멸을 자아냈다. 그렇다면 어째서 그는 더 이상의 구애를 곧장 끝내지 않았느냐고 물을지도 모르겠다. 나는 답하노니, 바로 그 이유 때문이며, 그에 못지않게 그럴듯한 여러 다른 이유 때문이기도 하다. 이제 그것을 독자에게 펼쳐 보이려 한다.\n\n블리필 씨는 존스 같은 기질을 타고나지도 않았고, 눈에 띄는 여자마다 집어삼킬 준비가 되어 있는 위인도 아니었다. 그러나 모든 동물의 공통된 속성이라 일컬어지는 그 식욕만큼은 그에게도 결코 모자라지 않았다. 게다가 그는 사람들이 저마다의 식욕을 채울 대상이나 먹잇감을 고르도록 이끌어 주는 그 분별의 취향 또한 지니고 있었다. 그리고 이 취향이 그로 하여금 소피아를 더없이 입맛 도는 한 점 진미로 여기게 했으니, 실로 그는 식도락가의 영혼을 사로잡는 멧새 한 마리를 바라보는 것과 똑같은 욕망으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그런데 소피아의 마음을 괴롭히던 고통은 도리어 그녀의 아름다움을 깎아내리기는커녕 더 돋우어 놓았다. 눈물은 그녀의 눈에 광채를 더했고, 한숨에 그녀의 가슴은 더 높이 부풀어 올랐다. 실로 아름다움이 고뇌에 잠긴 모습을 한 번도 본 적 없는 사람은, 그 아름다움이 가장 눈부신 광채를 발하는 모습을 본 적이 없는 셈이다. 그리하여 블리필은 이 인간 멧새를 지난번 보았을 때보다 한층 더한 욕망으로 바라보았다. 그가 그녀에게서 발견한 자신에 대한 혐오도 그 욕망을 조금도 줄이지 못했다. 오히려 그것은 정욕에 승리감마저 보태었으니, 그녀의 매력을 노략질하는 데서 그가 기대한 쾌락을 한층 더 부추기는 구실을 했을 뿐이다. 아니, 그는 그녀의 몸을 완전히 손에 넣음으로써 얻고자 한 또 다른 속셈까지 품고 있었으나, 그것은 입에 올리기조차 너무도 역겨운 것이다. 그리고 그가 스스로에게 약속한 그 만족들 가운데에는 복수심 또한 한몫을 차지하고 있었다. 가엾은 존스의 연적이 되어 그녀의 애정에서 그를 밀어내는 일은 그의 추격에 또 하나의 박차를 더했고, 그의 향락에 또 하나의 황홀을 약속해 주었다.\n\n엄격한 이들에게는 지나치게 악의의 냄새를 풍기는 듯 보일 이 모든 속셈 말고도, 그에게는 또 한 가지 전망이 있었는데, 이것만큼은 어떤 독자도 그리 크게 혐오하지는 않을 것이다. 다름 아닌 웨스턴 씨의 재산이었다. 그 재산은 모두 그의 딸과 그 자손에게 물려질 참이었으니, 그 자애로운 아비의 애정이 어찌나 분에 넘쳤던지, 제 자식이 자기가 고른 남편과 함께 불행해지는 데 승낙만 해 준다면, 그 남편을 어떤 값에 사들이든 그는 개의치 않았던 것이다.\n\n이런 이유들로 블리필 씨는 이 혼사를 어찌나 바랐던지, 소피아에게는 사랑하는 척하여 그녀를 속이고, 그녀의 아버지와 자신의 숙부에게는 그녀에게 사랑받는 척하여 그들을 속이려 들었다. 이렇게 하면서 그는 스왜컴의 신앙심을 끌어다 썼는데, 그 신앙심인즉 내세운 목적이 종교적인 것이라면(혼인이야 분명 그러하니), 그 수단이 아무리 사악하든 상관없다는 것이었다. 그 밖의 경우에는 스퀘어의 철학을 갖다 댔는데, 그 철학인즉 수단이 정당하고 도덕적 올바름에 어긋나지 않기만 하면 목적은 아무래도 좋다는 것이었다. 사실대로 말하면, 인생에서 일어나는 일 가운데 이 두 위대한 스승 중 하나의 가르침에서 이득을 끌어낼 수 없는 경우란 거의 없었다.\n\n웨스턴 씨에게는 실로 속임수를 부릴 필요가 거의 없었다. 그는 제 딸의 마음 따위를 블리필 자신이 여기는 것만큼이나 하찮게 여겼기 때문이다. 그러나 올워디 씨의 생각은 전혀 다른 종류의 것이었으니, 그를 속이는 일만큼은 반드시 필요했다. 다만 이 일에서 블리필은 웨스턴의 도움을 어찌나 잘 받았던지 어려움 없이 성공을 거두었다. 올워디 씨는 소피아의 아버지로부터 그녀가 블리필에게 마땅한 애정을 품고 있으며, 존스에 관해 자신이 의심했던 모든 것이 전적으로 거짓이라는 다짐을 이미 받은 터였으므로, 블리필이 할 일이라곤 그저 이 단언들을 굳혀 주는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 그는 그것을 얼마나 애매한 말로 해냈던지, 제 양심을 위한 빠져나갈 구실을 마련해 두는 한편, 거짓말을 했다는 죄책감 없이 숙부에게 거짓을 전하는 만족까지 누렸다. 올워디가 소피아의 마음에 관해 그를 캐물으며 \"나는 결코 어린 처녀를 제 뜻에 어긋나는 혼인으로 몰아넣는 일에 가담하지 않겠다\"고 말하자, 그는 이렇게 대답했다. \"어린 처녀들의 진짜 속마음이란 도무지 헤아리기가 어렵습니다. 다만 그녀가 저를 대하는 태도는 제가 바라는 만큼이나 적극적이었고, 그녀의 아버지 말을 믿어도 좋다면, 그녀는 어떤 연인이라도 바랄 법한 온갖 애정을 제게 품고 있다 합니다. 존스로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다, \"그가 어르신께 보인 행실로 보면 그 이름값을 충분히 하고도 남지만, 저는 차마 그를 악당이라 부르고 싶지 않습니다. 그의 허영심이, 어쩌면 무슨 사악한 속셈이 그로 하여금 거짓을 떠벌리게 했을지도 모릅니다. 만일 웨스턴 양이 그를 향해 품은 사랑에 조금이라도 진실이 있었더라면, 그토록 막대한 그녀의 재산을 두고 그가 그녀를 저버리는 일은 결코 없었을 테니까요. 어르신께서도 익히 들어 아시듯 그는 정녕 그녀를 저버렸지 않습니까. 끝으로 어르신, 제가 어르신께 약속드리건대, 만일 이 처녀가 제가 바라는 만큼의 온 애정을 제게 품고 있다고 확신하지 못한다면, 저 자신은 어떤 대가를 치른다 해도, 아니 온 세상을 다 준다 해도 결코 이 처녀와 혼인하는 데 동의하지 않을 것입니다.\"\n\n거짓을 오로지 마음으로만 전하면서, 애매한 표현과 사기로써 혀로는 거짓말의 죄를 짓지 않는 이 절묘한 수법은 적잖은 이름난 사기꾼들의 양심을 잠재워 왔다. 그러나 이런 자들이 속이려 드는 대상이 다름 아닌 전지(全知)하신 분임을 헤아려 보면, 그것은 아마도 더없이 얄팍한 위안밖에 주지 못하는 것일 터다. 그리고 거짓을 전하는 것과 거짓말을 하는 것 사이의 이 교묘하고 정교한 구분이란, 그것을 짜내는 데 드는 수고만큼의 값어치도 좀체 못 되는 것이다.\n\n올워디는 웨스턴 씨와 블리필 씨가 일러 준 말에 제법 만족했고, 협약은 이제 이틀 만에 매듭지어졌다. 그리하여 사제의 직무에 앞서 남은 것이라곤 법률가들의 직무뿐이었는데, 이것이 어찌나 많은 시간을 잡아먹을 듯 위협했던지, 웨스턴은 차라리 온갖 종류의 서약으로 스스로를 옭아맬지언정 이 젊은 한 쌍의 행복을 미루지는 않겠다고 나섰다. 실로 그가 어찌나 열성적이고 다급했던지, 무심한 사람이라면 그가 실제보다 이 혼사에서 더 주된 당사자라고 여겼을 법했다. 그러나 이런 열성은 그가 무슨 일에서나 으레 드러내던 것이었다. 그는 자신이 손댄 모든 계획을, 마치 오직 그 계획의 성공만으로 제 인생의 온 행복이 이루어지기라도 하는 양 그렇게 밀어붙였다.\n\n아버지와 사위 될 사람의 합세한 성화에 올워디 씨는 아마 넘어가고 말았을 것이다. 그는 남에게 행복을 베푸는 일이 조금이라도 미뤄지는 것을 좀체 견디지 못하는 사람이었으니 말이다. 그러나 소피아 자신이 그것을 막아섰고, 이 모든 협약에 마침내 종지부를 찍을 방책을 강구했으니, 이로써 인류를 합법적인 방식으로 번식시키는 데서 그 세금을 거두어들이는 것이 마땅하다고 여겨 온 그 현명한 두 기관, 곧 교회와 법으로부터 그 세금을 가로채 버린 셈이었다. 그 이야기는 다음 장에서 다루기로 하자.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n7장.\n소피아의 기이한 결심, 그리고 아너 부인의 더더욱 기이한 책략.\n\n아너 부인은 무엇보다 제 잇속에 충실한 사람이었지만, 소피아를 향한 작은 애정마저 없는 것은 아니었다. 사실을 말하자면, 그 젊은 아가씨를 알게 된 사람치고 그녀를 사랑하지 않기란 무척 어려운 일이었다. 그래서 그녀는 자기 안주인에게 매우 중대하다고 여겨지는 소식 하나를 듣자마자, 이틀 전 소피아 앞에서 불쾌하게 물러나게 된 일로 품었던 노여움 따위는 까맣게 잊고, 그 소식을 알리려 허둥지둥 달려갔다.\n\n그녀의 말은 방으로 들어서는 모양새만큼이나 다짜고짜 시작되었다. \"어머나, 아가씨!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아가씨께서는 어찌 생각하실까요? 정말이지 저는 넋이 나갈 만큼 놀랐답니다. 그래도 아가씨께 말씀드리는 것이 제 도리라고 여겼어요. 어쩌면 아가씨를 화나게 할지도 모르지만요. 우리 같은 하인들은 아가씨들을 무엇이 화나게 할지 늘 알지 못하니까요. 정말이지, 무슨 일이든 죄다 하인 탓으로 돌려지는 법이잖아요. 아가씨들이 기분이 언짢으시면, 우리야 꾸지람을 들어야 하는 게 당연하고요. 또 아가씨께서 기분이 상하신대도 저는 조금도 이상하지 않을 거예요. 아니, 분명 놀라실 거예요. 그래요, 충격까지 받으실 거고요.\"—\"착한 아너, 더 이상 서론 늘어놓지 말고 어서 말해 보거라.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"장담하건대 나를 놀라게 할 일은 거의 없고, 충격을 줄 일은 더더욱 적단다.\"—\"아가씨,\" 아너가 대답했다. \"정말이지, 바로 오늘 오후에 나리께서 서플 목사님과 혼인 허가증을 받는 일을 두고 이야기하시는 걸 제가 엿들었답니다. 그리고 분명히, 나리께서 아가씨를 내일 아침에 혼인시키겠다고 말씀하시는 걸 들었어요.\" 소피아는 이 말에 얼굴이 창백해지며 다급하게 되물었다. \"내일 아침이라고!\"—\"네, 아가씨.\" 믿음직한 시녀가 대답했다. \"맹세코, 나리께서 그리 말씀하시는 걸 들었어요.\"—\"아너,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"너는 나를 놀라게도 하고 충격에 빠뜨리기도 해서, 이제 숨도 정신도 거의 남지 않았구나. 이 끔찍한 처지에서 어찌해야 한단 말이냐?\"—\"제가 아가씨께 조언을 드릴 수 있다면 좋으련만.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"조언을 해 다오.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"부디, 착한 아너, 나에게 조언을 해 다오. 만일 네 일이라면 무엇을 시도하겠는지 생각해 보거라.\"—\"정말이지, 아가씨.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"아가씨와 제가 처지를 바꿀 수 있다면 좋겠어요. 그러니까, 아가씨를 해코지하자는 뜻이 아니라요. 아가씨가 하인이 되시기를 바랄 만큼 못된 마음은 절대 아니에요. 다만 만일 그게 제 일이라면, 저는 조금도 어려울 게 없으리라는 뜻이지요. 제 변변찮은 소견으로는, 젊은 블리필 도련님은 매력 있고, 다정하고, 잘생긴 분이니까요.\"—\"그런 소리는 입에 담지도 마라.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"그런 소리라고요!\" 아너가 되받았다. \"원, 저런. 그래요, 한 사람의 진수성찬이 다른 사람에겐 독이라더니, 여자들에게도 똑같이 들어맞는 말인가 봐요.\"—\"아너,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"저 경멸스러운 인간의 아내가 되느니, 차라리 내 가슴에 단검을 꽂겠다.\"—\"어머나! 아가씨!\" 상대가 대답했다. \"정말이지 이제는 아가씨께서 저를 넋 나가게 하시네요. 부디 그런 못된 생각이 머릿속에 들어오지 못하게 하시기를 간청드려요. 아이고! 정말이지 제 온몸이 떨려요. 아가씨, 생각해 보세요, 기독교식 매장을 거부당하고, 시신이 큰길에 묻혀, 옥스 크로스에서 농부 하프페니가 당한 것처럼 말뚝이 몸을 꿰뚫고 박힌다는 게 어떤 일인지. 그리고 정말이지, 그 후로 그의 유령이 거기를 떠돈다지요. 여러 사람이 그를 봤다니까요. 정말이지, 누구의 머릿속에 그런 못된 생각을 집어넣는 건 악마밖에 없을 거예요. 분명히, 자기 자신을 해치는 것보다는 온 세상을 해치는 것이 덜 사악한 일이니까요. 한두 분 목사님께 들은 게 아니라고요. 만일 아가씨께서 그토록 격렬한 거부감을 품으시고, 그 젊은 도련님을 너무도 끔찍이 싫어하셔서 그와 한 침대에 든다는 생각조차 견딜 수 없으시다면요. 정말이지 천성에 그런 반감이 있을 수도 있고, 어떤 사람의 살갗을 만지느니 두꺼비를 만지는 편이 낫다고 여기는 이도 있으니까요.\"—\n\n소피아는 너무도 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있어, 하녀의 앞선 그 훌륭한 일장 연설에 별 관심을 기울이지 못했다. 그래서 그녀는 거기에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 말을 끊으며 이렇게 말했다. \"아너, 나는 결심을 굳혔다. 바로 오늘 밤 아버지의 집을 떠나기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 네가 늘 입버릇처럼 말하던 그 우정을 나에게 품고 있다면, 나와 동행해 줄 것이다.\"—\"그럼요, 아가씨, 세상 끝까지라도 따르겠어요.\" 아너가 대답했다. \"하지만 부디, 어떤 무모한 행동을 벌이시기 전에 그 결과를 헤아려 보시기를 청해요. 아가씨께서 대체 어디로 가실 수 있겠어요?\"—\"런던에,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"지체 높은 부인 한 분이 계신데, 나의 친척이란다. 시골에서 여러 달을 내 고모와 함께 보내셨지. 그동안 내내 나에게 무척 다정하게 대해 주셨고, 내 곁에 있는 것을 어찌나 즐거워하셨던지, 나를 런던으로 데려가게 해 달라고 고모에게 간곡히 청하실 정도였단다. 워낙 이름난 분이시니, 나는 쉽게 찾아낼 수 있을 테고, 그분께 아주 따뜻하고 친절하게 맞아들여지리라는 데 조금도 의심이 없다.\"—\"아가씨, 그 일에 너무 자신만만하지는 마세요.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"제가 처음 모셨던 마님은 사람들을 자기 집으로 무척 간곡히 초대하시곤 했지만, 나중에 정작 그들이 온다는 말을 들으면 슬그머니 자리를 피하시곤 했답니다. 게다가, 그 부인이 아가씨를 뵙는 거야 무척 반가워하시겠지요. 정말이지 누구라도 아가씨를 뵈면 반가워할 테니까요. 그렇더라도 아가씨께서 나리에게서 도망쳐 나오셨다는 말을 들으시면—\" \"네가 잘못 알고 있다, 아너.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그분은 아버지의 권위라는 것을 나보다 훨씬 가볍게 여기시거든. 그분은 나에게 런던으로 함께 가자고 거세게 졸라 대셨고, 내가 아버지의 허락 없이는 안 가겠다고 했더니, 나를 비웃으며 어리석은 시골 처녀라 부르시고는, 이토록 효성스러운 딸이니 분명 더없이 다정한 아내가 되겠다고 하셨단다. 그러니 그분이 나를 받아 주시고, 또 보호해 주시리라는 것을 의심하지 않는다. 아버지가 내가 당신 손아귀를 벗어난 것을 깨닫고 어느 정도 이치에 닿게 마음을 돌릴 때까지 말이다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그런데 아가씨,\" 아너가 대답했다. \"어떻게 도망칠 생각이세요? 말이나 탈것은 어디서 구하시려고요? 아가씨 말로 말씀드리자면, 하인들이 다들 나리와 아가씨 사이가 어떤지 어느 정도 알고 있으니, 로빈은 나리의 명백한 분부 없이는 말을 마구간에서 내보내느니 차라리 목매달리는 편을 택할 거예요.\" \"나는,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"문이 열려 있을 때 그냥 걸어 나가서 도망칠 작정이다. 하늘에 감사하게도 내 다리는 나를 충분히 데려갈 수 있단다. 여러 긴 저녁 동안 나를 지탱해 주었으니 말이다.\"—\"네, 정말이지요.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"저는 세상 끝까지 아가씨를 따르겠어요. 하지만 아가씨께서는 차라리 혼자 가시는 편이 나을지도 몰라요. 도둑이나 다른 악당들이 아가씨와 마주치면, 저는 아가씨를 지켜 드릴 수 없을 테니까요. 아니, 저야말로 아가씨만큼이나 끔찍하게 겁에 질릴 거예요. 분명히 그자들은 우리 둘 다 욕보일 테니까요. 게다가 아가씨, 요즘 밤이 얼마나 추운지 생각해 보세요. 우리는 얼어 죽고 말 거예요.\"—\"빠르고 활기차게 걸으면,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"추위는 막아 줄 게다. 그리고 네가 악당으로부터 나를 지켜 줄 수 없다면, 아너, 내가 너를 지켜 주마. 권총을 가지고 갈 테니까. 홀에 늘 장전된 채로 두 자루가 있단다.\"—\"아가씨, 점점 더 무섭게 하시네요.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"설마 아가씨께서 그걸 발사하실 작정은 아니시지요! 아가씨께서 그런 일을 하시느니, 저는 차라리 어떤 위험이든 무릅쓰겠어요.\"—\"왜 그러느냐?\" 소피아가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아너, 네 정조를 노리고 덤벼드는 자가 있다면 너는 권총을 쏘지 않겠느냐?\"—\"정말이지요, 아가씨,\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"정조란 소중한 것이지요. 특히 우리 같은 가난한 하인들에게는요. 말하자면 그게 우리 밥줄이니까요. 그래도 저는 총칼을 죽도록 싫어해요. 그것 때문에 사고가 어찌나 많이 일어나는지요.\"—\"그래, 그래.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"내 생각엔 우리가 무기를 지니지 않아도 네 정조쯤은 아주 헐값에 지켜 줄 수 있을 듯하구나. 처음 닿는 마을에서 곧장 말을 구할 작정이고, 거기까지 가는 동안에 우리가 공격당하는 일은 거의 없을 테니까. 보거라, 아너, 나는 가기로 결심했다. 그리고 네가 나를 따라 준다면, 내 힘이 닿는 끝까지 너에게 보답하리라 약속하마.\"\n\n이 마지막 말은 앞선 그 어떤 논리보다 아너에게 더 강한 영향을 미쳤다. 그리고 자기 안주인이 그토록 단단히 결심한 것을 보자, 그녀는 더 이상의 만류를 그만두었다. 두 사람은 곧 계획을 실행할 방법과 수단을 두고 의논에 들어갔다. 여기서 매우 까다로운 난관 하나가 불거졌으니, 바로 짐을 옮기는 문제였다. 이는 안주인 쪽보다 하녀 쪽에게 훨씬 더 넘기 어려운 일이었다. 한 아가씨가 연인에게 달려가거나 그에게서 도망치기로 일단 마음을 정하고 나면, 모든 장애물은 하찮은 것으로 여겨지는 법이니까. 그러나 아너에게는 그런 동기가 없었다. 기대할 황홀함도, 피해야 할 두려움도 없었던 것이다. 게다가 자기 재산의 상당 부분을 이루는 옷가지의 실제 값어치는 차치하고라도, 그녀는 여러 벌의 드레스와 다른 물건들에 변덕스러운 애착을 품고 있었다. 그것이 자기에게 잘 어울려서든, 아니면 어떤 특별한 사람에게서 받은 것이어서든, 최근에 산 것이어서든, 오래도록 지녀 온 것이어서든, 그밖에 그만큼이나 그럴듯한 갖가지 이유로 말이다. 그래서 그녀는 그 가엾은 물건들을 뒤에 남겨, 웨스턴의 자비에 내맡긴다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없었다. 그가 분노에 차서 그것들을 순교의 고통에 처하게 하리라는 것을 그녀는 의심치 않았으니까.\n\n재간 있는 아너 부인은 안주인의 결심을 단념시키려 온갖 언변을 다 동원했지만, 그녀가 단호하게 마음을 굳힌 것을 보고는, 마침내 자기 옷가지를 옮길 다음과 같은 방책을 떠올렸다. 바로 그날 저녁에 자기 자신이 집에서 쫓겨나는 것이었다. 소피아는 이 방법을 크게 찬성했으나, 어찌 그 일을 꾸밀 수 있을지 미심쩍어했다. \"오, 아가씨,\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"그 일은 저에게 맡기셔도 돼요. 우리 같은 하인들은 주인 나리나 마님께 이런 혜택을 얻어 내는 법을 아주 잘 안답니다. 물론 때로는, 그분들이 우리에게 곧장 치를 수 없을 만큼 밀린 품삯이 있을 때면, 우리의 온갖 무례를 다 참아 넘기시고, 우리가 아무리 으름장을 놓아도 좀처럼 알아듣지 못하시지만요. 그러나 나리께서는 그런 분이 아니시지요. 아가씨께서 오늘 밤에 떠나기로 결심하셨으니, 제가 오늘 오후에 해고당하는 걸 틀림없이 해 보이겠어요.\" 그리하여 그녀가 소피아를 위해 속옷 몇 벌과 잠옷 한 벌을 자기 물건과 함께 꾸리기로 정해졌다. 그리고 자기의 다른 모든 옷가지에 대해서는, 젊은 아가씨는 자기 목숨을 구하려 남의 짐을 바다에 던져 버리는 뱃사람만큼이나 거리낌 없이 그것들을 단념했다.\n\n그다지 드물지 않은 종류의 말다툼 장면들을 담은 장.\n\n아너 부인은 젊은 아가씨와 헤어지기가 무섭게, 무언가가(나는 케베도의 그 노파처럼 거짓 고발로 악마를 모함하고 싶지는 않으며, 어쩌면 이번 일에는 악마가 손을 대지 않았을지도 모르니) 떠올랐는데, 다시 말해 무언가가 그녀에게 이런 생각을 불어넣었다. 소피아와 그녀의 온갖 비밀을 웨스턴 씨에게 팔아넘긴다면, 자신의 팔자를 고칠 수 있으리라는 생각이었다. 여러 가지 고려가 이 폭로를 부추겼다. 지주에게 그토록 크고 반가운 봉사를 해 준 대가로 두둑한 보상을 받을 거라는 화사한 전망이 그녀의 탐욕을 자극했고, 또 한편으로는 자신이 맡은 이 모험의 위험, 그 성공이 불확실하다는 점, 밤, 추위, 강도, 겁탈자—이 모두가 그녀의 두려움을 일깨웠다. 이 모든 것이 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 그녀는 곧장 지주에게 가서 사정을 죄다 털어놓기로 거의 결심할 뻔했다. 그러나 그녀는 한쪽 말만 듣고 한쪽 편을 들기에는 너무나 올곧은 재판관이었다. 그리고 여기서, 먼저 런던으로 가는 여정이 소피아 편을 매우 강하게 뒷받침했다. 그녀는 황홀경에 든 성인이 천국에서나 상상할 법한 그것에 조금 못 미칠 뿐인 매력으로 가득하리라고 마음속에 그리던 그곳을 간절히 보고 싶어 했다. 다음으로, 그녀는 소피아가 자기 주인보다 훨씬 더 너그럽다는 것을 알았기에, 충실함을 지키는 편이 배신으로 얻을 수 있는 것보다 더 큰 보상을 약속해 주었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 반대편에서 자신의 두려움을 키웠던 항목들을 하나하나 따져 보았고, 문제를 공정하게 추려 보니 거기에는 별것 없다는 것을 알게 되었다. 이제 양쪽 저울이 거의 평형에 이르렀고, 여주인을 향한 사랑이 그녀의 충직함 쪽 저울판에 얹히자 그쪽이 다소 무겁게 기울었다. 그때 한 가지 사정이 그녀의 상상을 때렸으니, 만일 그 무게가 고스란히 반대쪽 저울판에 실렸더라면 위험한 결과를 낳았을 만한 것이었다. 그것은 소피아가 약속을 지킬 수 있게 되기까지 흘러야 할 세월의 길이였다. 비록 소피아는 아버지가 죽으면 어머니의 재산을 물려받을 권리가 있고, 성년이 되면 어느 삼촌이 남긴 3천 파운드의 돈도 받을 권리가 있었지만, 그날들은 아직 멀었고, 또 많은 일이 끼어들어 이 젊은 아가씨가 베풀려는 너그러움을 가로막을 수도 있었다. 반면에 웨스턴 씨에게서 기대할 수 있는 보상은 당장의 것이었다. 그러나 그녀가 이 생각을 좇고 있을 때, 소피아의 선한 수호신이, 혹은 아너 부인의 충직함을 다스리는 그 무엇이, 어쩌면 그저 우연이 그녀의 길에 한 사건을 보내 주었으니, 그것이 단번에 그녀의 충실함을 지켜 주었을 뿐 아니라 의도된 일까지 수월하게 만들어 주었다.\n\n웨스턴 부인의 하녀는 여러 가지 이유로 아너 부인보다 자신이 훨씬 위라고 주장했다. 첫째, 그녀의 출생이 더 높았다. 어머니 쪽 증조할머니가 어느 아일랜드 귀족과 그리 멀지 않은 사촌뻘이었기 때문이다. 둘째, 그녀의 급료가 더 많았다. 그리고 마지막으로, 그녀는 런던에 가 본 적이 있었고, 그 덕분에 세상을 더 많이 본 셈이었다. 그래서 그녀는 늘 아너 부인을 대할 때 그런 거리를 두었고, 모든 계급의 여인이 아랫계급의 여인과 대화할 때 지키고 또 요구하는 그 차별의 표시를 늘 강요했다. 그런데 아너는 이 교리에 언제나 동의하지는 않았고, 상대가 요구하는 존경을 곧잘 깨뜨리곤 했으므로, 웨스턴 부인의 하녀는 그녀와 함께 있는 것을 전혀 달가워하지 않았다. 실로 그녀는 자기 여주인의 저택으로 돌아가기를 간절히 바랐는데, 그곳에서는 다른 하인들 위에 마음껏 군림할 수 있었기 때문이다. 그래서 그녀는 아침에, 웨스턴 부인이 막 떠나려던 바로 그 순간에 마음을 바꾸었을 때 크게 실망했고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 흔히 말하는 부루퉁한 기분에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n이런 기분으로, 그것도 결코 곱지 않은 기분으로, 그녀는 아너가 위에서 이야기한 식으로 혼자 곰곰이 따지고 있던 방으로 들어왔다. 아너는 그녀를 보자마자 다음과 같이 상냥한 말투로 인사를 건넸다. \"어머, 마님, 댁의 동행을 더 오래 누리는 즐거움을 갖게 됐네요. 제 주인 어른과 댁의 아씨 사이의 다툼 때문에 그 즐거움을 빼앗기지나 않을까 걱정했는데 말이에요.\" \"마님이라뇨, 저는,\" 상대가 대답했다, \"'우리'니 '우리네'니 하는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠군요. 분명히 말해 두는데, 저는 이 집의 어떤 하인도 저와 어울릴 만한 동행이라고 보지 않아요. 저는 일주일 내내 그들보다 윗사람들과 어울릴 자격이 있다고 생각하거든요. 아너 부인, 당신을 두고 하는 말은 아니에요. 당신은 교양 있는 젊은 여자니까요. 세상을 조금만 더 겪으면, 세인트 제임스 공원을 당신과 함께 거니는 것도 부끄럽지 않을 거예요.\" \"어머나 세상에!\" 아너가 외쳤다, \"마님께서 거드름을 피우시네요, 정말. '아너 부인'이라니, 원! 마님, 제 성으로 불러 주셔도 될 텐데요. 우리 아씨가 저를 아너라고 부르시긴 하지만, 저도 다른 사람들처럼 성이 있다고요. 저와 함께 걷기가 부끄럽다니, 참! 어머, 저도 당신만큼은 된답니다.\" \"내 예의에 그렇게 갚는다면,\" 상대가 말했다, \"아너 부인, 분명히 말해 두지만, 당신은 나만큼 못해요. 시골에서야 별의별 잡것들과 다 어울려야 하지만, 도시에서 저는 지체 높은 부인네들만 찾아뵙거든요. 정말이지, 아너 부인, 당신과 저 사이에는 어느 정도 차이가 있다고 생각해요.\" \"저도 그렇게 생각해요,\" 아너가 대꾸했다. \"우리 나이에 어느 정도 차이가 있고, 또—제 생각엔 우리 인물에도요.\" 마지막 말을 내뱉으면서 그녀는 더없이 약을 올리는 경멸의 태도로 웨스턴 부인의 하녀 곁을 으스대며 지나갔다. 코를 치켜들고, 고개를 까딱 젖히고, 자기 치마 버팀살로 경쟁자의 버팀살을 거칠게 쓸어 넘기면서. 상대 여인은 더없이 악의에 찬 비웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"이런 것이! 너는 내 화를 받을 가치도 없어. 그런 뻔뻔하고 건방진 잡년에게 험한 말을 주고받는 것조차 내 격에 안 맞지. 하지만 이년아, 분명히 말해 두는데, 네 행실은 네 출생의 천함과 더불어 네 교육의 천함까지 그대로 드러내는구나. 둘 다 너를 어느 시골 계집의 천한 하녀 노릇이나 하기에 딱 알맞게 만들어 놓았어.\" \"우리 아씨를 욕하지 마요,\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"그건 못 참아요. 우리 아씨는 댁의 아씨보다 젊은 만큼 훨씬 나으시고, 만 배는 더 아름다우시다고요.\"\n\n여기서 불운이, 아니 오히려 행운이 웨스턴 부인을 보내, 자기 하녀가 눈물 흘리는 모습을 보게 했다. 그 눈물은 부인이 다가오자 펑펑 쏟아지기 시작했다. 여주인이 그 까닭을 묻자, 하녀는 곧바로 자신의 눈물이 저기 저 것이—아너를 가리키는 말이었다—거칠게 굴어서 나온 것이라고 일러바쳤다. \"그리고 마님,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다, \"저한테 한 말은 다 무시할 수 있었어요. 그런데 저 여자가 뻔뻔하게도 마님을 모욕하면서 마님을 못생겼다고 했지 뭐예요—네, 마님, 제 면전에서 마님을 못생긴 늙은 고양이라고 불렀어요. 마님이 못생겼다는 말을 듣고는 도저히 견딜 수가 없었어요.\" \"그 무엄한 말을 왜 자꾸 되풀이하느냐?\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. 그러고 나서 아너 부인 쪽으로 돌아서서, \"어떻게 감히 내 이름을 함부로 입에 올렸느냐?\"고 물었다. \"함부로라뇨, 마님!\" 아너가 대답했다, \"저는 마님 이름을 입에 올린 적이 전혀 없어요. 그저 누군가가 우리 아씨만큼 아름답지 못하다고 했을 뿐이고, 그건 분명히 마님도 저만큼 잘 아실 텐데요.\" \"이년아,\" 부인이 받아쳤다, \"너 같은 건방진 잡년에게, 내가 네 입에 오르내릴 만한 사람이 아니라는 걸 톡톡히 알게 해 주마. 내 동생이 지금 당장 너를 내쫓지 않는다면, 나는 다시는 이 집에서 잠자지 않을 테다. 그를 찾아내서, 너를 이 순간 당장 쫓아내게 하겠다.\" \"쫓겨난다고요!\" 아너가 외쳤다, \"그렇게 된다 해도, 세상에 일자리가 한 군데뿐인 줄 아세요. 다행히도, 좋은 하인은 일자리가 모자랄 일이 없어요. 그리고 마님을 아름답다고 여기지 않는 사람을 죄다 내보내신다면, 곧 부릴 하인이 모자랄 거예요. 분명히 말씀드리는데요.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 대답으로 말을 했다, 아니 오히려 천둥을 쳤다. 그러나 거의 알아들을 수 없는 소리였으므로, 우리는 그 똑같은 말이 무엇이었는지 확실히 알 수 없다. 따라서 우리는 잘해 봐야 그녀의 명예에 별로 보탬이 되지 않을 그 말을 옮겨 적는 일은 생략하겠다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 동생을 찾아 나섰는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 분노로 가득했던지, 인간이라기보다 복수의 여신 가운데 하나를 닮아 있었다.\n\n두 하녀가 다시 단둘이 남게 되자, 두 번째 말다툼이 시작되었고, 그것은 이내 더 격렬한 종류의 싸움으로 번졌다. 이 싸움에서 승리는 신분이 낮은 쪽 여인에게 돌아갔으나, 피와 머리카락, 그리고 고운 아마포와 모슬린의 손실이 없지는 않았다.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\n9장.\n치안판사 노릇을 맡은 웨스턴 씨의 현명한 처신. 치안판사들에게 서기에게 꼭 필요한 자질에 관해 던지는 한마디 조언. 그리고 아비의 광기와 딸의 효심에 관한 유별난 사례들.\n\n논리학자들은 때때로 하나의 논증으로 너무 많은 것을 증명하려 들고, 정치가들은 곧잘 책략을 부리다가 제 꾀에 넘어간다. 어너 부인에게도 하마터면 그런 일이 벌어질 뻔했다. 그녀는 남은 옷가지를 되찾기는커녕, 도리어 지금 등에 걸치고 있는 옷마저 빼앗길 뻔했다. 어너가 누이를 욕보였다는 말을 듣자마자, 지주는 스무 번도 넘게 욕설을 퍼부으며 그녀를 브라이드웰로 보내 버리겠다고 맹세했기 때문이다.\n\n웨스턴 양은 본래 마음씨가 무척 너그러운 여인으로, 평소에는 잘 용서하는 성품이었다. 얼마 전에는 자신의 마차를 도랑에 처박은 역마차 마부의 잘못도 눈감아 주었다. 아니, 심지어 그녀에게서 돈뿐 아니라 귀고리까지 털어 간 노상강도를 고발하지 않음으로써 법을 어기기까지 했다. 그 강도는 그녀에게 욕을 퍼부으며 이렇게 말했던 것이다. \"당신처럼 예쁜 ××년은 보석 따위로 꾸밀 필요가 없으니, 빌어먹을, 그런 줄이나 아쇼.\" 그러나 지금은—사람의 성미란 이토록 종잡을 수 없고, 우리는 때에 따라 이렇게도 제 자신과 달라지는 법이라—그녀는 어떤 감형의 말도 들으려 하지 않았다. 어너가 아무리 뉘우치는 척해 보아도, 소피아가 제 하녀를 위해 아무리 간청해 보아도, 그녀가 동생더러 저 계집에게 정의를(아니, 사실은 정의보다 한 음절 더한 무엇을) 집행하라고 간절히 졸라 대는 것을 멈추게 할 수는 없었다.\n\n그러나 다행히도 그 서기에게는 치안판사의 서기라면 결코 없어서는 안 될 자질이 하나 있었으니, 다름 아니라 이 나라 법에 대한 얼마간의 식견이었다. 그리하여 그는 판사의 귀에 대고 속삭이기를, 평화를 깨뜨리려는 시도가 전혀 없었으니 저 처녀를 브라이드웰에 가두는 것은 권한을 넘어서는 일이라고 했다. \"송구하오나 나리,\" 그가 말했다. \"그저 버르장머리가 없다는 이유만으로는 누구도 합법적으로 브라이드웰에 가둘 수 없습니다.\"\n\n매우 중대한 일, 특히 사냥감에 관한 사건에서는 판사가 늘 서기의 이런 충고에 귀를 기울이는 것은 아니었다. 사실 그 항목의 법을 집행할 때면, 많은 치안판사들이 자신에게 폭넓은 재량권이 있다고 여겨, 사냥감을 잡아 없애는 도구를 수색하고 압수한다는 명목 아래 제멋대로 불법 침입을, 때로는 중죄까지도 저지르곤 했다.\n\n하지만 이번 죄는 그리 무거운 성질의 것이 아니었고, 사회에 그리 위험한 것도 아니었다. 그러므로 이번만큼은 판사도 서기의 조언에 어느 정도 귀를 기울였다. 실은 이미 왕좌재판소에 그를 고발하는 진정이 두 건이나 들어와 있었던 터라, 세 번째를 시험해 볼 호기심 따위는 없었던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 지주는 더없이 현명하고 의미심장한 표정을 짓고서, 흠흠 하하 하며 몇 번 운을 뗀 뒤, 누이에게 말하기를, 더 무르익게 곰곰이 따져 본 결과 자기 생각으로는 이러하다는 것이었다. \"법에서 평화를 깨뜨렸다고 부르는 일, 그러니까 문을 부수어 연다거나, 울타리를 부순다거나, 머리통을 깬다거나, 뭐 그런 종류의 깨뜨림이 없었으니, 이 일은 중죄에 해당하는 것도 아니고, 불법 침입도 아니고, 손해를 끼친 것도 아니라네. 그러니 법에 이걸 벌할 처벌이 없단 말일세.\"\n\n웨스턴 양은 \"자기가 법을 훨씬 더 잘 안다\"고 말했다. \"주인을 모욕했다는 이유로 하인이 아주 호되게 벌받는 걸 본 적이 있다\"고 하면서, 런던의 어느 치안판사를 콕 집어 말하기를, \"그분은 주인이나 안주인이 원하기만 하면 언제든 하인을 브라이드웰에 가둬 버린다\"는 것이었다.\n\n\"그럴 법도 하지,\" 지주가 외쳤다. \"런던에선 그럴지 모르나, 시골에선 법이 다르거든.\" 이어서 남매 사이에 법을 둘러싼 매우 학식 깊은 논쟁이 벌어졌는데, 우리 독자들 가운데 그것을 알아들을 만한 이가 많으리라 여겨졌다면 여기 옮겨 적었을 것이다. 어쨌든 이 논쟁은 마침내 양쪽 모두의 합의로 서기에게 판가름을 맡겼고, 서기는 치안판사 쪽에 유리하게 결론을 내렸다. 그리하여 웨스턴 양은 결국 어너를 내쫓는 것으로 만족할 수밖에 없었으니, 이에는 소피아 자신도 아주 선뜻, 기꺼이 동의했다.\n\n이렇게 운명의 여신은 늘 하던 대로 두세 차례 장난을 치며 즐긴 끝에, 마침내 모든 일을 우리 여주인공에게 유리하게 매듭지어 주었다. 사실 소피아는 난생처음 부려 본 속임수치고는 놀랍도록 훌륭하게 성공한 셈이었다. 그리고 솔직히 말하자면, 나는 이런 결론을 자주 내리곤 했다. 만약 정직한 사람들이 죄를 무릅쓸 마음을 먹거나 그만한 수고를 들일 가치가 있다고 여기기만 한다면, 그들은 교활한 자들보다 훨씬 더 능히 이길 것이라고.\n\n어너는 제 역할을 더없이 완벽하게 해냈다. 그녀의 마음속에 더없이 끔찍한 상상을 불러일으켰던 그 말, 브라이드웰의 위험에서 벗어났음을 알아차리자마자, 그녀는 조금 전 공포에 눌려 잠시 수그러들었던 그 거드름을 도로 되찾았다. 그러고는 자기 자리를 내놓으면서, 훨씬 더 높은 자리를 내려놓을 때에나 어울릴 만큼 만족한 척, 아니 경멸하는 척까지 부려 가며 굴었다. 그러니 독자께서 괜찮으시다면, 우리는 차라리 그녀가 \"사임했다\"고 말하기로 하겠다. 이 말은 사실 늘 쫓겨났다거나 내쫓겼다는 말과 같은 뜻으로 통해 왔으니 말이다.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 그녀더러 짐을 어서 싸라고 명했다. 누이가 그런 뻔뻔한 계집과 한 지붕 아래서 하룻밤도 더 자지 않겠다고 선언했기 때문이다. 그리하여 어너는 일에 매달렸고, 어찌나 부지런히 했던지 저녁 일찍 모든 채비가 끝났다. 품삯을 받고 나자 그녀는 봇짐이며 짐가지를 챙겨 떠나갔는데, 이는 모든 이의 큰 만족이었으나 그 누구보다 소피아의 만족이 가장 컸다. 소피아는 하녀에게 집에서 그리 멀지 않은 어느 장소에서 정확히 으스스하고 귀신 나올 법한 자정에 만나기로 일러 두고는, 자신의 출발 채비를 시작했다.\n\n그러나 그에 앞서 그녀는 두 차례 괴로운 면담을 견뎌야 했으니, 하나는 고모와의 면담이요 다른 하나는 아버지와의 면담이었다. 이 자리에서 웨스턴 양은 전보다 더 단호한 어조로 그녀에게 말하기 시작했다. 그러나 아버지는 어찌나 사납고 무지막지하게 그녀를 다루었던지, 그녀를 겁에 질리게 하여 그 뜻을 따르는 척하게 만들고 말았다. 이에 마음씨 좋은 지주는 어찌나 흡족해했던지, 찌푸린 얼굴을 미소로 바꾸고 으름장을 약속으로 바꾸었다. 그는 자기 온 영혼이 딸의 영혼에 휘감겨 있노라 맹세했고, 딸의 승낙이(그는 \"아버님, 아시다시피 저는 아버님의 절대적인 명령이라면 무엇이든 거역해서도 안 되고 거역할 수도 없습니다\"라는 말을 그렇게 해석했던 것이다) 자신을 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람으로 만들었노라 했다. 그러고는 그녀가 원하는 패물이라면 무엇이든 사라며 큼직한 은행권을 건네주고, 더없이 다정하게 입 맞추고 끌어안았다. 그러는 동안 기쁨의 눈물이 그 두 눈에서 흘러내렸으니, 바로 그 눈이 몇 분 전만 해도 자신의 온 애정이 쏠린 그 사랑스러운 대상을 향해 불길과 분노를 내쏘았던 것이다.\n\n부모에게서 이런 행동이 나타나는 사례는 워낙 흔한지라, 독자께서도 웨스턴 씨의 이 모든 처신에 그리 크게 놀라지는 않으시리라 믿는다. 만약 놀라신다면, 솔직히 나로서는 그것을 설명할 길이 없음을 고백하겠다. 그가 딸을 더없이 살뜰하게 사랑한 것만큼은 다툼의 여지가 없다고 생각하기 때문이다. 실로 다른 많은 이들도 그러했으니, 그들은 바로 이 똑같은 행동으로 제 자식을 더없이 비참하게 만들어 버렸다. 이런 행동은 부모들에게 거의 보편적이긴 하나, 나에게는 늘 인간이라는 저 기이하고 놀라운 피조물의 머릿속에 떠오른 온갖 부조리 가운데서도 가장 설명하기 어려운 것으로 보여 왔다.\n\n웨스턴 씨가 보인 처신의 뒷부분은 소피아의 여린 마음에 어찌나 강하게 와닿았던지, 그녀에게 한 가지 생각을 불러일으켰다. 그것은 약삭빠른 고모의 온갖 궤변으로도, 아버지의 온갖 으름장으로도 단 한 번도 그녀의 머릿속에 떠올려 놓지 못했던 생각이었다. 그녀는 아버지를 어찌나 경건하게 우러르고 어찌나 열렬하게 사랑했던지, 그분의 즐거움에—때로는 어쩌면 더 큰 만족에—자주 한몫 보태는 데서 우러나는 것보다 더 기쁜 감정을 거의 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 아버지는 딸이 칭찬받는 소리를 들을 때면 그 기쁨을 도무지 억누르지 못했고, 그 흐뭇한 소리를 거의 매일같이 듣는 행복을 누렸기 때문이다. 그러니 이 혼사에 승낙함으로써 아버지에게 안겨 드릴 더없이 큰 행복을 떠올리자, 그것이 그녀의 마음에 깊은 자국을 남겼다. 또한 그처럼 순종하는 행위에 깃든 더없는 경건함이 무척이나 강하게 작용했으니, 그녀는 신앙심이 아주 깊었기 때문이다. 끝으로, 자신이 효성과 도리를 위한 제물, 아니 순교자나 다름없는 처지가 되어 얼마나 큰 고통을 겪어야 할지를 곱씹어 보았을 때, 그녀는 어떤 작은 열정 속에서 흐뭇한 간지러움을 느꼈다. 그 열정은 신앙이나 덕과 직접적인 친연이라고는 없으면서도, 곧잘 친절을 베풀어 그 둘의 목적을 이루는 데 큰 도움을 주곤 하는 것이다.\n\n소피아는 그토록 영웅적인 행동을 떠올리는 데 흠뻑 취해, 너무 이른 자화자찬으로 스스로를 추어올리기 시작했다. 그런데 그때, 그녀의 토시 속에 숨어 있던 큐피드가 별안간 기어 나오더니, 인형극의 펀치넬로처럼 앞에 놓인 것을 죄다 걷어차 버렸다. 사실(우리는 독자를 속이거나, 우리 여주인공의 행동을 초자연적인 충동 탓으로 돌려 그녀의 됨됨이를 변호할 마음 따위는 추호도 없으니), 사랑하는 존스에 대한 생각과, 그가 아주 각별히 얽혀 있는 어떤 희망이(아무리 아득한 것일지라도) 곧장, 효성과 경건함과 자존심이 힘을 합쳐 애써 이뤄 놓으려던 그 모든 것을 산산이 무너뜨려 버렸다.\n\n그러나 소피아의 이야기를 더 이어 가기에 앞서, 이제 우리는 존스 씨에게로 눈길을 돌려야겠다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n독자께서는 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다. 우리는 이 권의 첫머리에서 존스 씨를 브리스틀로 가는 길 위에 두고 떠났다. 그는 바다에서 운을 찾아보겠다고, 아니 사실은 뭍에서의 자기 운명으로부터 달아나겠다고 마음을 굳혔던 것이다.\n\n그런데 (그리 드문 일은 아니지만) 마침 그를 안내하기로 한 길잡이가 공교롭게도 그 길을 잘 알지 못했다. 그래서 옳은 길을 놓치고도 부끄러워 차마 물어보지 못한 채 앞으로 갔다 뒤로 갔다 헤매다 보니 어느새 밤이 되어 사방이 어두워지기 시작했다. 존스는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 짐작하고는 길잡이에게 제 걱정을 털어놓았다. 그러나 길잡이는 우리가 바른 길에 있다고 우겼고, 자기가 브리스틀로 가는 길을 모른다면 그것이야말로 참 이상한 일이라고 덧붙였다. 사실은, 평생 그 길을 한 번도 지나본 적이 없었으니, 그가 그 길을 안다면 그게 훨씬 더 이상한 일이었겠지만 말이다.\n\n존스는 길잡이를 그렇게까지 곧이곧대로 믿지는 않았기에, 어느 마을에 다다르자 처음 마주친 사내에게 여기가 브리스틀로 가는 길이 맞느냐고 물었다. \"어디서 오셨수?\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"그건 됐고,\" 존스가 조금 성급하게 말했다. \"이 길이 브리스틀로 가는 길인지 알고 싶을 뿐이오.\" \"브리스틀로 가는 길!\" 사내가 머리를 긁적이며 외쳤다. \"아니, 나리, 이 길로는 오늘 밤 브리스틀에 가기 어려우실 거구먼유.\" \"그럼 친구여,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"어느 쪽이 길인지 좀 일러주게.\" \"그게 말이유, 나리,\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"하느님만 아실 어딘가로 길을 잘못 드신 게 분명해유. 저쪽 길은 글로스터로 가는 길이니께유.\" \"그래, 그럼 어느 쪽이 브리스틀로 가는가?\" 존스가 말했다. \"아니, 나리는 지금 브리스틀에서 멀어지고 계시잖유.\" 사내가 대답했다. \"그럼,\" 존스가 말했다. \"우리가 다시 되돌아가야 한다는 말인가?\" \"아믄유, 그래야쥬.\" 사내가 말했다. \"좋아, 그럼 언덕 꼭대기로 되돌아가면 어느 길로 가야 하나?\" \"그야 곧장 난 길로 가셔야쥬.\" \"한데 내 기억엔 길이 둘이던데, 하나는 오른쪽, 하나는 왼쪽으로.\" \"그야 오른쪽 길로 가셔서 곧장 앞으로 가셔유. 다만 첨엔 오른쪽으로 돌고, 그담엔 또 왼쪽으로, 그담엔 오른쪽으로 돌면, 그러면 향사 댁이 나와유. 거기서 또 곧장 앞으로 가다가 왼쪽으로 도시면 돼유.\"\n\n이제 또 다른 사내가 다가오더니 신사분들이 어느 쪽으로 가시느냐고 물었다. 존스에게서 그 말을 듣자 그는 먼저 머리를 긁적이고는, 손에 들고 있던 막대기에 몸을 기대고 이렇게 일러주기 시작했다. \"오른쪽 길로 한 일 마일, 아니 일 마일 반쯤, 뭐 그쯤 가셔야 해유. 그담에 왼쪽으로 바짝 꺾으면, 진 베언스 나리 댁을 빙 돌아 나오게 돼유.\" \"한데 존 베언스 씨가 누구인가?\" 존스가 말했다. \"이런 세상에!\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"아니, 진 베언스 나리를 모르신단 말이유? 그럼 대체 어디서 오신 거유?\"\n\n이 두 사내는 존스의 인내심을 거의 바닥내고 말았는데, 그때 행색이 단정하고 사람 좋아 보이는 한 남자가(실은 퀘이커교도였다) 그에게 이렇게 말을 걸었다. \"벗이여, 보아하니 길을 잃으신 모양이오. 내 충고를 들으시려거든, 오늘 밤은 그 길을 찾으려 들지 마시오. 거의 어두워졌고, 길은 찾기가 어렵소. 게다가 요즈음 여기서 브리스틀 사이에 노상강도가 여럿 일어났소이다. 바로 곁에 아주 믿을 만한 좋은 집이 있으니, 거기서 아침까지 그대와 그대의 말이 편히 묵을 수 있을 것이오.\" 존스는 조금 설득당한 끝에 아침까지 이곳에 머물기로 했고, 그 벗의 안내를 받아 여관으로 갔다.\n\n여관 주인은 무척 점잖은 사람이었는데, 존스에게 이렇게 말했다. \"변변치 못한 잠자리를 부디 너그러이 봐주시길 바랍니다요. 안사람이 집을 비웠는데, 거의 모든 걸 잠가놓고 열쇠를 죄다 가져가버렸지 뭡니까요.\" 사실인즉, 그의 아끼는 딸이 막 혼인을 해서 그날 아침 남편을 따라 집으로 떠났던 것이다. 그리고 딸과 그 어미가 함께 이 가엾은 남자에게서 돈은 물론이요 세간까지 거의 다 벗겨가다시피 한 터였다. 그에게는 자식이 여럿 있었지만, 어미가 마음에 두는 것은 오직 그가 가장 사랑하는 이 딸 하나뿐이었으니, 이 한 자식의 비위를 맞추기 위해서라면 나머지 자식들은 물론이고 남편까지 덤으로 기꺼이 희생시켰을 사람이었다.\n\n존스는 누구와도 어울릴 처지가 못 되어 차라리 혼자 있고 싶었지만, 그래도 이 정직한 퀘이커교도의 끈질긴 청을 뿌리칠 수가 없었다. 퀘이커교도는 존스의 얼굴과 거동 모두에 깃든 우울을 알아차렸기에 더욱 그와 함께 앉아 있고 싶어했고, 가엾게도 자신의 이야기가 그 우울을 얼마쯤 덜어주지 않을까 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그들이 한동안 함께 앉아 있었는데, 그 모양새가 어찌나 조용했던지 나의 이 정직한 벗은 자기네 침묵 집회 가운데 하나에 와 있다고 여겼을 법했다. 그러던 차에 퀘이커교도는 어떤 영(靈)인가에, 아마도 호기심의 영에 이끌려 입을 열었다. \"벗이여, 보아하니 그대에게 무슨 슬픈 재난이 닥친 모양이오. 허나 부디 마음을 다잡으시오. 어쩌면 벗을 잃으신 게로군요. 그렇다면, 우리 모두 언젠가 죽을 몸임을 생각하셔야 하오. 그대의 슬픔이 그대의 벗에게 아무 도움도 되지 못함을 알면서, 어찌하여 슬퍼하시오? 우리는 다 고난을 짊어지고 태어났소. 나 또한 그대 못지않은 슬픔을 안고 있으며, 아마 그대보다 더 큰 슬픔일 게요. 비록 나는 한 해에 백 파운드씩 나오는 깨끗한 재산이 있어 모자람이 없고, 주님께 감사하게도 거리낌 없는 양심을 지녔으며, 몸도 튼튼하고 건강하여 내게 빚을 갚으라 할 사람도, 내가 해를 끼쳤다고 탓할 사람도 없소이다. 그런데도 벗이여, 그대가 나만큼이나 비참하다고 생각하니 마음이 아프오.\"\n\n여기서 퀘이커교도는 깊은 한숨으로 말을 맺었고, 존스는 곧바로 대답했다. \"선생, 무엇이 그 까닭이든 선생의 불행이 참으로 안타깝습니다.\" \"아아! 벗이여,\" 퀘이커교도가 답했다. \"그 까닭은 외동딸 하나라오. 이 세상에서 내 가장 큰 기쁨이던 그 아이가, 이 한 주 안에 내게서 달아나 내 뜻을 거스르고 혼인을 해버렸소. 나는 그 아이에게 마땅한 짝을 정해두었으니, 점잖고 살림도 넉넉한 사람이었소. 그런데 그 아이는, 어이없게도, 제 멋대로 고르겠다며 한 푼어치도 안 되는 젊은 녀석과 함께 떠나버렸소. 만일 그 아이가, 짐작건대 그대의 벗처럼, 차라리 죽었더라면 나는 행복했을 거요.\" \"그것 참 이상한 말씀이군요, 선생.\" 존스가 말했다. \"아니, 거지가 되느니 차라리 죽는 편이 그 아이에게 낫지 않겠소?\" 퀘이커교도가 대답했다. \"내 말했듯이, 그 녀석은 한 푼어치도 안 되는 자요. 그러니 내가 그 아이에게 한 닢이라도 줄 거라곤 기대할 수 없겠지요. 안 되오, 사랑을 좇아 혼인을 했으니, 할 수 있거든 사랑으로 먹고살라지요. 사랑을 장에 들고 나가서, 누가 그걸 은화로, 아니 반 푼짜리 동전으로라도 바꿔주는지 어디 보라지요.\" \"선생의 일은 선생이 가장 잘 아시겠지요.\" 존스가 말했다. \"이건 분명,\" 퀘이커교도가 말을 이었다. \"나를 속이려고 오래전부터 꾸민 흉계였을 게요. 그 둘은 어릴 적부터 서로 알고 지냈으니까요. 나는 늘 그 아이에게 사랑이란 걸 멀리하라 타일렀고, 그건 다 어리석고 사악한 짓이라고 수천 번도 더 일러주었소. 아니, 그 교활한 계집애는 내 말을 새겨듣는 척, 육신의 온갖 음탕함을 경멸하는 척하더니, 결국엔 이 층 창문으로 뛰어내려 달아나버렸소. 실은 내가 그 아이를 조금 의심하기 시작해서, 바로 다음 날 아침에 내 마음에 드는 짝과 혼인시킬 작정으로 단단히 가둬두었던 거요. 그런데 몇 시간도 못 되어 그 아이는 나를 저버리고, 제가 고른 그 정인에게로 달아나버렸소. 그 녀석도 꾸물대지 않아서, 그 둘은 한 시간 안에 혼인하고 잠자리까지 다 치러버렸소. 허나 그건 그 둘이 평생 한 일 중에 가장 못된 한 시간의 짓거리가 될 게요. 나로 말하자면, 그 둘이 함께 굶든, 빌어먹든, 도둑질을 하든 상관없소. 나는 결코 그 둘 중 누구에게도 한 푼도 주지 않을 테니.\" 여기서 존스가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"정말이지 양해를 구해야겠소. 나를 좀 혼자 있게 해주시오.\" \"이런, 이런, 벗이여,\" 퀘이커교도가 말했다. \"근심에 그리 휩쓸리지 마시오. 보다시피 이 세상엔 그대 말고도 비참한 사람들이 있다오.\" \"이 세상엔 미친 자들과, 바보들과, 악당들이 있다는 건 알겠소.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"허나 내 한마디 충고하겠소. 따님과 사위를 집으로 불러들이시오. 그리고 사랑한다 말하는 사람에게 비참함을 안기는 유일한 장본인이 선생 자신이 되지 마시오.\" \"그 아이와 그 남편을 집으로 불러들이라니!\" 퀘이커교도가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"그럴 바엔 차라리 이 세상에서 가장 미워하는 두 원수를 부르고 말겠소!\" \"그렇다면 선생이나 집으로 가시오, 아니면 가고 싶은 데로 가시든가.\" 존스가 말했다. \"나는 더 이상 그런 사람과 함께 앉아 있지 않겠소.\" \"아니, 벗이여,\" 퀘이커교도가 대답했다. \"내 함께 있어달라고 누군가에게 떼쓰는 건 질색이오.\" 그러고는 주머니에서 돈을 꺼내려 했으나, 존스가 다소 거칠게 그를 방 밖으로 떠밀어냈다.\n\n퀘이커교도가 늘어놓은 이야기의 주제가 존스의 마음을 어찌나 깊이 흔들어놓았던지, 그는 상대가 말하는 내내 사뭇 사납게 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었다. 퀘이커교도는 이를 알아차렸고, 거기에 더해 그의 나머지 거동까지 겹쳐 보고는, 이 정직한 챙 넓은 모자 양반은 제 길동무가 정말로 제정신이 아니라는 생각에 사로잡히고 말았다. 그래서 그 모욕에 분개하기는커녕, 퀘이커교도는 그 불행한 처지를 가엾이 여겨 마음이 움직였다. 그리하여 자신의 견해를 여관 주인에게 일러주며, 손님을 각별히 잘 보살피고 더없이 정중하게 대접하라고 당부했다.\n\n\"천만에요,\" 여관 주인이 말했다. \"그자한테 그런 정중함 따윈 베풀지 않을 겁니다요. 보아하니 저기 레이스 달린 조끼를 걸쳤어도, 저자는 나보다 나을 게 없는 신사기는커녕, 한 삼십 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 대단한 향사 댁에서 자라난 가난한 교구 사생아일 뿐이고, 지금은 (틀림없이 좋은 일로는 아닐 테고) 집에서 쫓겨난 신세랍니다요. 되도록 빨리 저자를 내 집에서 내보내야겠어요. 숙박비를 떼인다 해도, 첫 손해가 늘 가장 나은 손해인 법이니까요. 일 년도 채 안 됐어요, 내가 은수저 하나를 잃어버린 게.\"\n\n\"교구 사생아라니 무슨 소릴 하는 게요, 로빈?\" 퀘이커교도가 대답했다. \"분명 사람을 잘못 본 게요.\"\n\n\"천만에요,\" 로빈이 대답했다. \"저자를 아주 잘 아는 길잡이가 내게 일러준 겁니다요.\" 사실 그 길잡이는 부엌 화롯가에 자리를 잡자마자, 존스에 관해 자기가 알거나 들어본 모든 것을 거기 모인 사람들에게 죄다 늘어놓았던 것이다.\n\n이 사내에게서 존스의 출생과 보잘것없는 처지를 확인하기가 무섭게, 퀘이커교도의 가엾어하던 마음은 죄다 사라져버렸다. 그리고 이 정직하고 소박한 남자는, 공작이 그런 사람에게서 모욕을 당했을 때 느꼈을 법한 노여움 못지않은 분노에 불타올라 집으로 돌아가버렸다.\n\n여관 주인 또한 자기 손님에게 똑같은 멸시를 품게 되었다. 그래서 존스가 잠자리에 들려고 종을 울리자, 여기엔 내줄 잠자리가 없다는 대답이 돌아왔다. 손님의 비천한 처지를 멸시하는 데다, 로빈은 그자의 속셈에 대해서도 격한 의심을 품고 있었다. 그자가 집을 털 만한 좋은 기회를 노리고 있다고 짐작한 것이다. 사실 그는 이런 걱정을 충분히 덜 수도 있었을 것이다. 아내와 딸이 신중하게도 부동산에 붙박이로 딸린 것 말고는 이미 모조리 치워버렸으니 말이다. 하지만 그는 천성이 의심이 많았고, 은수저를 잃은 뒤로는 유독 더 그러했다. 한마디로, 도둑맞을지 모른다는 두려움이, 사실은 잃을 것이 아무것도 없다는 마음 편한 생각을 송두리째 집어삼켜버린 것이다.\n\n존스는 잠자리를 얻을 수 없다는 말을 듣고서, 골풀로 엮은 커다란 의자에 아주 흡족하게 몸을 맡겼다. 그러자 요즘 들어 훨씬 더 좋은 방에서도 그를 피하던 잠이, 이 초라한 골방으로 너그러이 그를 찾아와주었다.\n\n여관 주인으로 말하자면, 그는 두려움 탓에 잠자리에 들지 못했다. 그래서 부엌 화롯가로 돌아갔는데, 거기서는 존스가 앉아 있는 거실로, 아니 거실이라기보다는 굴이라 할 만한 방으로 통하는 단 하나뿐인 문을 지켜볼 수 있었다. 그 방의 창문으로 말하자면, 고양이보다 큰 짐승이라면 도저히 그리로 빠져나갈 수 없을 만큼 작았다.\n\n군인 무리에 얽힌 모험.\n\n여관 주인은 곧장 응접실 문 맞은편에 자리를 잡고는, 밤새도록 그곳에서 보초를 서기로 마음먹었다. 길잡이와 또 다른 사내 하나가 그와 함께 오래도록 그 임무에 붙어 있었으나, 두 사람은 주인의 의심을 알지도 못했고 제 나름의 의심을 품은 것도 아니었다. 사실 그들이 자리를 지킨 진짜 이유는 끝내 그 보초 서기를 끝장내고 말았으니, 그 이유란 다름 아닌 맥주의 힘과 그 맛이 좋았다는 데 있었다. 어마어마한 양을 들이켠 두 사람은 처음에는 몹시 시끄럽고 떠들썩해지더니, 이윽고 둘 다 곯아떨어지고 말았다.\n\n그러나 술의 힘으로도 로빈의 두려움만은 잠재울 수 없었다. 그는 여전히 의자에 앉은 채 깨어 있었고, 두 눈을 존스 씨의 방으로 통하는 문에 똑바로 못 박고 있었다. 그러던 차에 바깥문을 거세게 두드리는 소리가 그를 자리에서 불러냈고, 문을 열지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 그가 문을 열기가 무섭게, 그의 부엌은 곧장 붉은 군복을 입은 신사들로 가득 찼다. 그들은 마치 그의 작은 성을 기습으로 함락시키려는 듯, 한꺼번에 우르르 그에게 들이닥쳤다.\n\n이제 여관 주인은 자기 자리를 떠나, 그 숱한 손님들에게 맥주를 내올 수밖에 없었다. 손님들은 무척이나 다급하게 맥주를 외쳐 댔다. 그가 두 번째인지 세 번째인지 지하 저장고에서 돌아왔을 때, 그는 군인들 한가운데 불 앞에 서 있는 존스 씨를 보았다. 이렇게 좋은 일행이 잔뜩 들이닥쳤으니, 어지간한 잠은 다 달아나기 마련이라는 것은 쉬이 믿을 만한 일이다. 마지막 나팔 소리에야 비로소 깨어날 그런 잠이 아니라면 말이다.\n\n이제 일행은 갈증을 어지간히 푼 터라, 남은 일이라곤 셈을 치르는 것뿐이었다. 그런데 이것은 신분이 낮은 축의 신사들 사이에서 흔히 적잖은 말썽과 불만을 낳는 일이다. 그들은 분배의 정의에 정확히 맞추어 액수를 매기는 데 큰 어려움을 겪기 일쑤니, 그 정의란 사람마다 자기가 마신 양에 따라 값을 치러야 한다고 일러 주는 법이다. 이번에도 그 어려움이 닥쳤다. 게다가 어떤 신사들은 어찌나 급했던지 첫 잔을 들이켠 뒤 곧장 행군해 가 버려서, 그 셈에 한 푼 보태는 일조차 까맣게 잊은 터라 어려움은 한층 더 컸다.\n\n이제 격렬한 다툼이 일어났는데, 그 자리에서 오간 모든 말은 가히 선서를 곁들여 진술된 것이라 해도 좋았다. 그도 그럴 것이, 욕설이 적어도 다른 모든 말의 수만큼은 되었기 때문이다. 이 입씨름에서 일행은 모두가 한꺼번에 떠들어 댔고, 저마다 제 몫으로 떨어지는 액수를 깎아 보려고 온통 안간힘을 쓰는 듯했다. 그러니 미루어 짐작할 수 있는 가장 그럴듯한 결말이란, 셈의 상당 부분이 여관 주인의 몫으로 떨어져 그가 갚게 되거나, 아니면 (사실상 같은 말이지만) 끝내 받지 못한 채 남게 되리라는 것이었다.\n\n이러는 내내 존스 씨는 하사와 대화를 나누고 있었다. 그 하사관은 지금의 다툼과는 전혀 무관했으니, 아득한 옛 관습에 따라 어떤 셈에서도 면제받는 특권을 누렸기 때문이다.\n\n다툼이 어찌나 뜨거워졌던지, 이제 막 무력으로 판가름이 날 지경에 이르렀다. 그때 존스가 앞으로 나서서, 자기가 셈을 전부 치르겠노라 선언하자 그들의 떠들썩한 소동이 단번에 잠잠해졌다. 그 셈이란 실은 통틀어 3실링 4펜스밖에 되지 않았다.\n\n이 선언으로 존스는 일행 전부의 고마움과 갈채를 한 몸에 받았다. 명예로운 분, 고귀한 분, 훌륭한 신사라는 말들이 방 안에 울려 퍼졌다. 아니, 여관 주인 자신마저 그를 더 좋게 보기 시작했고, 길잡이가 들려준 이야기를 거의 믿지 않게 될 지경이었다.\n\n하사는 존스 씨에게, 자기들이 반란군을 토벌하러 행군하는 길이며, 영광스러운 컴벌랜드 공작의 지휘를 받게 되리라 기대한다고 일러 주었다. 이로써 독자는 (지금까지 굳이 알릴 필요가 없다고 여겼던 한 가지 사정을) 알아챌 수 있을 것이다. 즉 이때야말로 지난날의 그 반란이 한창 절정에 이르렀던 바로 그 무렵이었던 것이다. 실로 그 도적 떼는 이제 잉글랜드 안으로 진군해 들어와, 사람들 짐작으로는 국왕의 군대와 맞서 싸우고 수도까지 밀고 나아가려는 참이었다.\n\n존스의 됨됨이에는 영웅다운 기질이 얼마간 섞여 있었고, 그는 자유와 신교라는 영광스러운 대의를 진심으로 빌어 주는 사람이었다. 그러니 훨씬 더 무모하고 거친 일을 벌인다 해도 정당하다 할 만한 이런 상황에서, 그가 이번 원정에 의용병으로 나서 볼 생각을 떠올린 것도 이상한 일은 아니다.\n\n우리의 지휘관은 그 좋은 마음가짐을 알게 된 첫 순간부터, 그것을 북돋우고 부추기느라 할 수 있는 말은 다 했다. 그는 이제 그 갸륵한 결심을 큰 소리로 알렸고, 일행 모두가 이를 크게 기뻐하며 받아들여 한목소리로 외쳤다. \"조지 국왕 폐하와 나리께 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\" 그러고는 숱한 욕설을 곁들여 덧붙였다. \"우리는 마지막 피 한 방울이 다할 때까지 두 분 곁을 지키겠습니다!\"\n\n밤새도록 술집에서 진탕 마셔 댄 그 신사는, 한 상병이 그의 손에 쥐여 준 몇 마디 설득에 넘어가 같은 원정에 나서기로 했다. 그리고 이제 존스 씨의 여행 가방이 짐수레에 실리고, 부대가 막 앞으로 움직이려던 참이었다. 그때 길잡이가 존스에게 다가와 말했다. \"나리, 말들이 밤새도록 바깥에 매여 있었고, 우리가 길을 한참이나 벗어나 멀리 왔다는 점을 헤아려 주셨으면 합니다.\" 존스는 이 뻔뻔한 요구에 어이가 없어, 군인들에게 자기 사정의 정당함을 일러 주었다. 그러자 그들은 신사를 등쳐 먹으려 든 길잡이를 한목소리로 나무랐다. 누구는 그를 목과 발을 한데 묶어 두어야 한다고 했고, 또 누구는 태형의 늘어선 매를 맞아야 마땅하다고 했다. 하사는 그에게 지팡이를 흔들어 보이며, 그가 제 휘하에 있었으면 좋겠다고, 본때를 보여 주겠노라 한껏 욕설을 퍼부었다.\n\n그러나 존스는 벌을 주지 않는 것으로 만족하고는, 새로 사귄 동료들과 함께 떠나며, 길잡이에게는 욕하고 헐뜯는 가련한 앙갚음이나 하도록 내버려 두었다. 그 헐뜯는 일에는 여관 주인도 끼어들어 말했다. \"그렇고말고, 저자는 아주 별난 작자지, 내 장담하리다. 군인이 되겠다는 어지간한 신사 나리께서 말이오! 정말이지 화려하게 수놓은 조끼나 차려입겠지. 옛 속담에 이런 말이 있는데, 다 옳은 말이라오. 번쩍인다고 다 금은 아니다. 저자가 우리 집에서 깨끗이 떠나 주니 속이 다 시원하구먼.\"\n\n그날 내내 하사와 젊은 신병은 나란히 행군했다. 능청스러운 위인이던 하사는 신병에게 자기 출정 시절의 재미난 이야기를 잔뜩 들려주었다. 하지만 사실 그는 출정 따위는 한 번도 해 본 적이 없었다. 군에 들어온 지도 얼마 되지 않았는데, 제 잔꾀로 상관들의 비위를 어찌나 잘 맞추었던지 스스로 미늘창을 든 하사 자리에까지 올라간 것이다. 실은 주로 신병을 끌어모으는 솜씨 덕이었으니, 그 일에 있어서는 더없이 능란했다.\n\n행군하는 동안 군인들 사이에는 흥겨움과 떠들썩함이 넘쳐흘렀다. 그 와중에 지난 숙영지에서 있었던 숱한 일들이 떠올랐고, 저마다 거리낌 없이 제 상관들을 두고 마음껏 농을 쳐 댔다. 그중에는 다소 거친 것도 있어, 거의 험담에 가까울 지경이었다. 이 광경은 우리 주인공의 머릿속에, 책에서 읽은 그리스인과 로마인의 풍습을 떠올리게 했다. 어떤 축제와 엄숙한 행사 때면 노예들에게도, 주인을 향해 거침없이 말할 자유를 허락하던 그 풍습 말이다.\n\n두 개의 보병 중대로 이루어진 우리의 작은 군대는 이제 그날 저녁 머물기로 한 곳에 다다랐다. 하사는 곧 지휘관인 자기 중위에게, 그날 행군 중에 사내 둘을 거두어들였노라 일러 주었다. 그중 하나는 (술꾼을 두고 한 말이었다) 여태 본 적 없이 잘생긴 사내라고 했으니, 키가 6피트에 가깝고 몸의 균형이 잘 잡혔으며 팔다리가 다부지다는 것이었다. 그리고 다른 하나는 (존스를 두고 한 말이었다) 후열에 세우기에 그런대로 괜찮을 것이라 했다.\n\n이제 새 병사들이 장교 앞에 불려 나왔다. 먼저 불려 나온 6피트 사내를 살펴본 장교는, 다음으로 존스를 훑어보러 왔다. 그를 처음 본 순간, 중위는 적잖은 놀라움을 감추지 못했다. 그가 옷차림이 무척 단정하고 타고나기를 기품 있었던 데다, 그의 표정에는 좀처럼 천한 무리에서는 볼 수 없는, 그렇다고 그들의 윗사람들 이목구비에 반드시 따라붙는 것도 아닌, 도드라진 위엄의 기운이 서려 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"선생,\" 중위가 말했다. \"내 하사가 일러 주기로, 선생께서 지금 내 휘하에 있는 중대에 입대하고 싶어 한다고 하더이다. 그렇다면, 선생, 우리 중대에서 무기를 들어 큰 명예를 더해 주겠다 약속하는 신사를 우리는 무척 기쁘게 맞이하겠소.\"\n\n존스는 이렇게 답했다. \"저는 입대에 관해서는 한마디도 한 적이 없습니다. 다만 여러분이 싸우러 나가는 그 영광스러운 대의에 더없이 열렬히 마음이 끌려, 의용병으로 섬기기를 간절히 바랄 뿐입니다.\" 그러고는 중위에게 몇 마디 칭송의 말을 곁들이며, 그의 지휘 아래 있게 되어 무척 흡족하다는 뜻을 밝히는 것으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n중위는 그의 정중함에 화답하며 그 결심을 치하했고, 그와 악수를 나눈 뒤, 자기와 다른 장교들과 함께 식사하자고 청했다.\n\n전 장에서 언급했던, 이 부대를 지휘하던 그 중위는 이제 나이가 예순에 가까웠다. 그는 아주 젊어서 군에 들어가 타니에르 전투에서 소위로 복무했는데, 거기서 두 군데 부상을 입었고 어찌나 두드러지게 공을 세웠던지, 그 전투가 끝나자마자 말버러 공작의 손으로 중위로 진급했다.\n\n그는 그 이후로, 그러니까 거의 사십 년 동안이나 줄곧 이 직위에 머물러 있었다. 그동안 그는 자기보다 위로 올라가는 사람을 수도 없이 보아 왔으며, 이제는 자신이 처음 군에 들어왔을 무렵 아버지가 아직 젖먹이였던 애송이들의 지휘를 받는 굴욕까지 겪고 있었다.\n\n그가 직업에서 이렇게 출세하지 못한 것이 오로지 권력자들 사이에 벗 하나 두지 못한 탓만은 아니었다. 그는 불운하게도 자기 연대장의 미움을 사고 말았는데, 그 연대장은 여러 해 동안 이 연대의 지휘를 맡고 있었다. 게다가 이자가 그에게 품은 그 누그러질 줄 모르는 악의는 장교로서의 어떤 태만이나 부족함에서, 아니 사실 그 자신의 어떤 허물에서 비롯된 것도 아니었다. 그것은 오로지 그의 아내가 보인 분별없음 때문이었다. 그녀는 무척 아름다운 여인이었고, 남편을 유난히 사랑하면서도, 연대장이 요구하는 어떤 호의들을 대가로 치르면서까지 남편의 진급을 사들이려 하지는 않았던 것이다.\n\n가엾은 중위가 이 일에서 유독 더 불행했던 것은, 그가 연대장의 적의가 빚어낸 결과를 몸소 겪으면서도 연대장이 정말로 자기에게 그런 마음을 품고 있다는 것을 알지도, 의심하지도 못했다는 점이다. 자신이 빌미를 준 기억이 전혀 없는 악의를 그가 어찌 의심할 수 있었겠는가. 그리고 그의 아내는, 명예에 까다로운 남편의 성정이 어떤 일을 빚어낼지 두려워한 나머지, 자신의 정조를 지킨 것에 만족할 뿐 그 승리의 개가는 누리려 하지 않았다.\n\n이 불운한 장교는(그를 이렇게 불러도 좋으리라 생각한다) 직업적인 공로 말고도 좋은 자질을 많이 갖추고 있었다. 그는 신앙심 깊고 정직하며 마음씨 고운 사람이었고, 지휘하는 일에서도 어찌나 처신이 훌륭했던지 자기 중대의 병사들뿐 아니라 연대 전체로부터 높이 존경받고 사랑받았다.\n\n그와 함께 행군하던 다른 장교들로는, 프랑스를 떠난 지 너무 오래되어 제 나라 말은 잊어버렸으되 영국에 머문 지는 우리말을 익히기엔 짧았던 어느 프랑스인 중위가 있었다. 그래서 그는 사실상 어떤 언어도 제대로 구사하지 못했고, 가장 평범한 상황에서조차 겨우 뜻을 통하게 할 정도였다. 또한 둘 다 아주 젊은 소위가 있었는데, 하나는 변호사 밑에서 자랐고, 다른 하나는 어느 귀족의 집사 아내가 낳은 아들이었다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나자마자 존스는 행군 중에 병사들 사이에서 벌어졌던 흥겨운 일을 일행에게 이야기해 주었다. \"그렇지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"그렇게들 떠들썩하게 소리를 질러대도, 막상 적과 맞닥뜨리면 저들은 트로이 사람들보다는 그리스 사람들처럼 처신할 거라고 장담하겠소.\" \"그리스 사람과 트로이 사람이라!\" 소위 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"그게 대체 누구요? 나는 유럽의 군대란 군대는 다 들어봤지만, 그런 자들 얘긴 한 번도 못 들어봤소.\"\n\n\"노더턴 군, 자네가 가진 것 이상으로 무식한 척은 하지 말게.\" 그 훌륭한 중위가 말했다. \"포프의 호메로스는 읽은 적이 없을지 몰라도, 그리스 사람과 트로이 사람 이야기쯤은 들어봤을 테지. 이제 이 신사분이 말을 꺼내니 생각나는데, 포프는 트로이 사람들의 행군을 거위 떼 꽥꽥대는 소리에 견주고, 그리스 사람들의 침묵을 크게 칭송했지. 그리고 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 이 젊은이의 관찰에는 큰 일리가 있네.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 나도 그자들 아주 잘 기억하지.\" 프랑스인 중위가 말했다. \"나도 학교에서 다시에 부인 책으로 그거 읽었어, 그리스 사람들, 트로이 사람들, 그들이 여자 하나 때문에 싸웠지. 그래, 그래, 나도 그거 다 읽었어.\"\n\n\"호모인지 뭔지, 진심으로 빌어먹으라지.\" 노더턴이 말했다. \"나는 아직도 그놈 자국이 내 엉덩이에 남아 있단 말이오. 우리 연대에 토머스란 자가 있는데, 늘 호모를 호주머니에 넣고 다닌다오. 빌어먹을, 내 손에 그게 들어오기만 해 봐, 태워버리지 않으면 사람이 아니지. 그리고 코르데리우스라는 또 다른 후레자식 놈도 있는데, 그놈 때문에 내가 매를 어찌나 많이 맞았는지.\"\n\n\"그러면 노더턴 군, 자네도 학교를 다녔다는 말인가?\" 중위가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 빌어먹게도 다녔지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"나를 거기 보낸 우리 아버지를 악마가 잡아갔으면! 그 영감탱이가 나를 목사로 만들려고 했단 말이오. 하지만 빌어먹을, 나는 속으로 생각했지, 영감, 내가 거기서 한 방 먹여 주마, 당신의 그 헛소리는 손톱만큼도 내 머릿속에 안 들어오게 해 주지. 우리 연대에 제미 올리버란 자가 있는데, 그자도 하마터면 뚜쟁이가 될 뻔했지 뭐요. 그랬다면 천만 번 아까운 일이었을 거요. 빌어먹을, 그자야말로 온 세상에서 가장 멋진 친구 가운데 하나니까. 하지만 그자는 나보다 더 멀리까지 그 영감탱이를 골탕 먹였소, 지미는 글을 쓸 줄도 읽을 줄도 모르니 말이오.\"\n\n\"자네 친구에게 아주 좋은 인물평을 해 주는군.\" 중위가 말했다. \"그리고 아주 마땅한 평이겠지. 하지만 노더턴 군, 부탁이니 그 어리석으면서도 고약한 욕설 버릇은 좀 버리게. 거기에 무슨 재치나 품위가 있다고 여긴다면, 내 분명히 말해두는데 그건 자네가 속고 있는 걸세. 그리고 내 충고를 받아들여, 성직자들을 헐뜯는 짓도 그만두었으면 하네. 어떤 무리에게든 망신스러운 이름을 붙이고 비난을 퍼붓는 것은 늘 부당한 일이지만, 그토록 거룩한 직분을 두고 그러는 것은 더욱이나 그러하네. 그 직분에 종사하는 무리를 욕보이는 것은 곧 그 직분 자체를 욕보이는 일이니까. 그리고 신교의 신앙을 지키려 싸우러 가는 사람들이 그런 짓을 하는 것이 얼마나 앞뒤가 맞지 않는지는 자네 판단에 맡기겠네.\"\n\n다른 소위의 이름은 애덜리였는데, 그는 그때까지 발뒤꿈치를 까딱거리고 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 이야기에는 귀를 기울이지 않는 듯이 앉아 있었다. 이제 그가 대답했다. \"오, 무슈, 전쟁터에서는 종교 이야기는 하지 않는 법이오.\" \"잘 말했네, 잭.\" 노더턴이 외쳤다. \"종교가 유일한 문제라면, 목사 놈들더러 제 싸움은 제가 하라지, 나는 빠지겠소.\"\n\n\"여러분 생각이 어떤지는 모르겠소만,\" 존스가 말했다. \"내 생각에는 사람이 제 신앙을 위한 것보다 더 고귀한 대의에 몸을 던질 수는 없을 듯하오. 그리고 내가 역사를 얼마간 읽어 본 바로는, 신앙의 열의에 고무된 병사들만큼 용감하게 싸운 병사는 없었소. 나로 말하자면, 비록 이 나라의 어느 누구 못지않게 내 임금과 나라를 사랑한다고 믿지만, 그래도 신교의 이익이야말로 내가 이 대의에 지원병으로 나선 데 적잖은 동기가 되었소.\"\n\n이제 노더턴은 애덜리에게 눈짓을 보내며 슬며시 속삭였다. \"저 잘난 척하는 녀석을 골려 주자고, 애덜리, 골려 줘.\" 그러고는 존스에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"선생, 우리 연대를 지원병으로 택해 주시니 참 기쁘오. 우리 목사가 어쩌다 술을 한 잔 과하게 들이켤 때면, 보아하니 선생이 그 자리를 메워 줄 수 있겠구려. 짐작건대 선생은 대학을 다녔겠지요. 어느 학료였는지 여쭈어도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"선생,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"나는 대학을 다니기는커녕, 오히려 선생보다 한 수 위라오. 나는 학교라는 데를 다녀 본 적조차 없으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"내 짐작은,\" 소위가 외쳤다. \"그저 선생의 대단한 학식에 근거한 것이었소만.\" \"오! 선생,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"학교를 다녔으면서도 아무것도 모를 수 있는 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 학교를 다니지 않고도 무언가를 알 수 있는 법이라오.\"\n\n\"잘 말했네, 젊은 지원병.\" 중위가 외쳤다. \"정말이지 노더턴 군, 자네는 저 사람을 그냥 두는 편이 낫겠어. 자네에겐 너무 벅찬 상대일 테니까.\"\n\n노더턴은 존스의 비꼬는 말이 영 입맛에 맞지 않았다. 하지만 그 도발이 주먹질을 정당화하기에도, 그렇다고 머릿속에 떠오른 유일한 응수인 '악당'이나 '비열한 놈' 같은 말을 내뱉기에도 모자란다고 여겼다. 그래서 그는 당장은 입을 다물었으나, 첫 기회가 오는 대로 욕설로 그 농담을 되갚아 주리라 마음먹었다.\n\n이제 흔히 말하는 건배사를 할 차례가 존스에게로 돌아왔고, 그는 사랑하는 소피아를 입에 올리지 않을 수 없었다. 자기가 가리키는 사람이 누구인지 그 자리의 어느 누구도 절대 알아맞히지 못하리라 여겼기에, 그는 더욱 거리낌 없이 그렇게 했다.\n\n그러나 건배 진행을 맡은 중위는 소피아라는 이름만으로는 성에 차지 않았다. 그는 성까지 대야 한다고 했고, 그러자 존스는 잠시 머뭇거리다가 곧 소피아 웨스턴 아가씨라고 이름을 댔다. 노더턴 소위는 누군가가 그녀의 신원을 보증해 주지 않는 한, 자기 건배 상대와 같은 잔으로 그녀의 건강을 위해 마시지는 않겠다고 선언했다. \"내가 소피 웨스턴이란 여자를 하나 알았는데,\" 그가 말했다. \"배스에서 젊은 사내들 절반과 동침한 여자였소. 어쩌면 같은 여자일지도 모르지.\" 존스는 자못 엄숙하게 그렇지 않다고 그에게 단언하며, 자기가 말한 그 아가씨는 대단한 지체와 재산을 갖춘 분이라고 못 박았다. \"그래, 그래,\" 소위가 말했다. \"그 여자도 바로 그렇소. 빌어먹을, 같은 여자가 틀림없소. 우리 연대의 톰 프렌치가 브리지스가의 어느 술집에서든 그 여자를 우리 자리로 데려온다는 데 부르고뉴 포도주 여섯 병을 걸겠소.\" 그러고는 그녀의 외모를 정확하게 묘사하기 시작하더니(그는 그녀가 이모와 함께 있는 것을 본 적이 있었다), \"그 여자 아버지가 서머싯셔에 큰 영지를 가지고 있다\"는 말로 끝맺었다.\n\n연인의 애틋한 마음은 제 연인의 이름을 두고 조금이라도 농을 거는 것을 도무지 견디지 못한다. 그렇지만 존스는 천성에 연인의 기질도 영웅의 기질도 충분히 갖추고 있었으면서도, 어쩌면 마땅히 그래야 했을 만큼 성급하게 이 비방에 발끈하지는 않았다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그는 이런 종류의 재담을 별로 접해 본 적이 없어 얼른 알아듣지 못했고, 한참 동안은 노더턴이 자기 연인을 다른 누군가와 정말로 착각한 줄로만 알았다. 그러나 이제 험악한 낯빛으로 소위를 돌아보며 그가 말했다. \"선생, 부탁이니 그 재치는 다른 주제를 골라 부려 주시오. 분명히 말해두는데, 이 아가씨의 인격을 두고 농을 거는 것은 결코 참아주지 않겠소.\" \"농이라!\" 상대가 외쳤다. \"빌어먹을, 내 평생 이보다 더 진지했던 적이 없소. 우리 연대의 톰 프렌치가 배스에서 그 여자도 그 이모도 둘 다 데리고 있었단 말이오.\" \"그렇다면 나도 진지하게 말해야겠군.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"당신은 이 세상에서 가장 뻔뻔한 악당 가운데 하나요.\"\n\n이 말을 내뱉기가 무섭게 소위는 욕설을 한바탕 퍼부으며 술병을 가득 채운 채로 존스의 머리를 향해 던졌고, 그것이 오른쪽 관자놀이 조금 위를 맞히는 바람에 존스는 즉각 바닥에 쓰러지고 말았다.\n\n승자는 제 앞에 적이 꼼짝 않고 누워 있고 상처에서 피가 제법 많이 흐르기 시작하는 것을 보고는, 이제 더 얻을 명예도 없는 이 싸움터를 뜨려고 생각하기 시작했다. 그러나 중위가 문 앞을 가로막고 나서며 그의 퇴로를 끊어버렸다.\n\n노더턴은 풀어달라고 중위를 몹시 졸라댔다. 자기가 머물러 있으면 좋지 않은 결과가 따를 거라며, 자기가 이보다 덜 어쩔 수 있었겠느냐고 따져 물었다. \"이런 빌어먹을!\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그저 그 친구한테 농담을 했을 뿐이오. 웨스턴 아가씨에 관한 나쁜 소문 따위 내 평생 들어본 적도 없소.\" \"들어본 적이 없다고?\" 중위가 말했다. \"그렇다면 자네는 그런 농을 던진 죄로나, 그런 흉기를 쓴 죄로나 교수형에 처해 마땅하군. 자네는 내 포로일세. 자네를 가둘 적절한 호위병이 올 때까지 여기서 한 발짝도 못 움직일 줄 알게.\"\n\n우리 중위는 이 소위에게 그토록 위세가 있었으니, 우리의 가엾은 영웅을 바닥에 눕혀버린 그 끓어오르던 용기조차도, 설령 그때 그의 옆구리에 칼이 한 자루 달려 있었다 한들, 이 소위로 하여금 중위를 향해 칼을 빼들도록 부추기기엔 어림없었을 것이다. 그러나 방 안에 걸려 있던 칼은 죄다 다툼이 시작되자마자 그 프랑스인 장교가 챙겨버린 뒤였다. 그리하여 노더턴은 이 사건이 어떻게 결말지어질지 잠자코 기다릴 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그 프랑스인 신사와 애덜리는 지휘관의 분부에 따라 존스의 몸을 일으켜 세웠으나, 그에게서 살아 있는 기색이 거의(아니 조금도) 보이지 않자 다시 그를 떨어뜨렸다. 애덜리는 그가 자기 조끼에 피를 묻혔다고 욕을 퍼부었고, 프랑스인은 이렇게 선언했다. \"빌어먹을, 나는 이 죽은 영국 사람 안 만지겠어. 나는 영국 법, 법인지 율인지, 뭐라고 부르든, 맨 나중에 만진 사람을 목매단다는 얘기 들었어.\"\n\n그 선량한 중위는 문에 매달리는 동시에 종에도 매달렸다. 술 시중꾼이 곧바로 달려오자 그는 그를 보내 머스킷 총병 한 분대와 외과의를 불러오게 했다. 이 명령에 더해 그 시중꾼이 자기가 직접 본 것을 떠벌리는 통에, 병사들이 나타났을 뿐 아니라 어느새 그 집 주인과 그 아내며 하인들, 그리고 사실상 그때 마침 여관 안에 있던 사람 모두가 우르르 몰려들었다.\n\n이어진 장면의 자초지종을 낱낱이 묘사하고 거기 오간 대화를 통째로 옮기는 일은, 내게 펜이 마흔 자루쯤 있어서 사람들이 지금처럼 동시에 떠들어대듯 그 모두로 한꺼번에 쓸 수 있지 않은 한, 내 능력 밖의 일이다. 그러니 독자는 가장 눈여겨볼 만한 사건들만으로 만족해야 하리라. 어쩌면 나머지는 너그러이 넘겨주어도 좋을 것이다.\n\n가장 먼저 한 일은 노더턴의 몸을 단속하는 것이었다. 그는 상등병을 우두머리로 한 여섯 사람의 손에 넘겨졌고, 그들에게 이끌려 그가 몹시 떠나고 싶어 하던 곳을 떠나게 되었으나, 공교롭게도 그것은 그가 몹시 가기 싫어하던 곳으로의 길이었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 야심의 욕망이란 어찌나 변덕스러운지, 이 젊은이는 앞서 말한 그 명예를 손에 넣은 바로 그 순간, 그 소문이 결코 제 귀에 닿지 못할 세상 어느 구석으로든 물러나 있었으면 좋겠다고 흔쾌히 여겼을 것이다.\n\n훌륭하고 선량한 사람인 중위가 부상자의 목숨을 살리는 일보다 가해자를 단속하는 일에 주된 신경을 쏟았다는 것이 우리를 놀라게 하며, 어쩌면 독자도 그러할 것이다. 우리가 이 점을 짚는 것은 그토록 별난 처신을 굳이 변명해 보겠다는 뜻에서가 아니라, 훗날 어느 비평가가 그것을 발견했다고 으스댈까 봐서다. 우리는 그 양반들에게 알려주고 싶다. 우리도 그들 못지않게 인물 속의 별난 구석을 볼 줄 안다는 것을. 다만 우리가 할 일은 사실을 있는 그대로 전하는 것이며, 그 일을 마쳤거든, 우리 작품의 모든 대목이 베껴진 그 자연이라는 원전을 들여다보는 것은 박식하고 슬기로운 독자의 몫이다. 비록 우리가 그 전거로서 정확한 쪽수까지 늘 인용하지는 않을지라도 말이다.\n\n이제 도착한 사람들은 기질이 사뭇 달랐다. 그들은 소위라는 인물에 대한 호기심은 훗날 그가 좀 더 그럴듯한 자세를 취한 모습을 보게 될 때까지 미루어 두었다. 지금 그들의 온 관심과 주의는 바닥에 쓰러진 그 피투성이 대상에게로 쏠려 있었다. 그를 일으켜 의자에 똑바로 앉히자, 그는 곧 살아 있다는, 움직인다는 몇 가지 기미를 보이기 시작했다. 사람들이 이를 알아채자마자(처음에는 다들 존스가 죽었다고 단정하던 터였다), 그들은 일제히 그에게 처방을 내리기 시작했다(의술에 몸담은 이가 하나도 없던 자리라, 거기 있던 사람마다 그 일을 제 소임으로 떠맡았던 것이다).\n\n피를 뽑아야 한다는 것이 방 안 모두의 한목소리였다. 그러나 공교롭게도 그 일을 해낼 사람이 곁에 없었다. 그러자 너나없이 외쳤다. \"이발사를 불러와!\" 하지만 한 발짝도 움직이는 이가 없었다. 여러 가지 강심제도 마찬가지로 아무런 효험 없이 처방되었고, 마침내 여관 주인이 독한 맥주 한 잔에 토스트를 곁들여 가져오게 하면서, 그것이야말로 영국에서 가장 좋은 강심제라고 말했다.\n\n이 자리에서 주로 거든 사람, 아니 사실 무언가 쓸모 있는 일을 했거나 할 성싶은 유일한 사람은 안주인이었다. 그녀는 제 머리카락을 얼마간 잘라 상처에 대어 피를 멎게 했고, 손으로 젊은이의 관자놀이를 문지르기 시작했다. 그리고 맥주를 처방하는 남편을 몹시 업신여기며, 하녀 하나를 자기 골방으로 보내 브랜디 한 병을 가져오게 하더니, 그것이 오자마자 막 정신을 차린 존스를 구슬려 아주 크고 넉넉한 한 모금을 들이켜게 했다.\n\n곧이어 외과의가 도착했는데, 그는 상처를 살펴보고 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 여태 행해진 일을 죄다 나무라더니, 환자를 당장 잠자리에 들게 하라고 일렀다. 우리는 그를 거기 그 자리에 얼마간 두어 편히 쉬게 하는 것이 마땅하리라 여기며, 그리하여 이쯤에서 이 장을 끝맺으려 한다.\n\n# Chapter xiii.\n\n여관 안주인의 빼어난 말솜씨와, 어느 외과의의 대단한 학식과, 갸륵한 중위의 결의론에 관한 단단한 솜씨를 담은 장.\n\n부상당한 사내가 침대로 옮겨지고, 이 사고로 들썩이던 집안이 다시 진정되기 시작하자, 안주인은 지휘관에게 이렇게 말을 건넸다. \"나리, 죄송한 말씀이오나,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 젊은이가 나리들께 마땅히 갖춰야 할 처신을 못한 것 같습니다. 만약 죽임을 당했다 한들, 제 생각엔 자업자득이지요. 정말이지, 신사 양반들이 자기보다 못한 작자들을 자기 무리에 끼워 줄 적엔 거리를 두어야 마땅한데, 제 첫 남편 말마따나 그걸 할 줄 아는 자가 드물지요. 저로 말씀드리면, 어떤 작자도 신사분들 자리에 자기를 끼워 넣게 그냥 두진 않았을 겁니다. 그런데 저 사람도 장교인 줄로만 알았지 뭡니까, 하사가 그저 신병일 뿐이라고 일러 줄 때까진요.\"\n\n\"안주인,\" 중위가 대답했다. \"당신은 일의 전말을 완전히 잘못 알고 있소. 저 젊은이는 더없이 훌륭하게 처신했고, 내가 보기엔 그를 모욕한 소위보다 훨씬 나은 신사요. 만약 저 젊은이가 죽는다면, 그를 친 자가 가장 후회하게 될 거요. 연대로서는 군의 수치인 골칫덩어리 하나를 떨어내는 셈이 될 테니까. 그리고 그자가 법의 손아귀에서 빠져나간다면, 부인, 그땐 나를 탓하시오, 그뿐이오.\"\n\n\"아이고! 아이고! 저런 변이 있나!\" 안주인이 말했다. \"누가 그런 생각을 했겠어요? 그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고, 나리께서 정의가 이뤄지게 하실 줄 알았습니다. 정말이지 누구에게나 그래야 마땅하지요. 신사 양반들이 가난한 사람을 죽여 놓고 책임도 안 진대서야 쓰겠습니까. 가난한 사람도 윗사람들과 똑같이 구원받을 영혼이 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"정말이오, 부인,\" 중위가 말했다. \"당신은 저 지원병을 잘못 보고 있소. 단언컨대 그는 저 장교보다 훨씬 신사다운 사람이오.\"\n\n\"아이고!\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"그것 보세요, 자, 그러니까요. 글쎄, 제 첫 남편이 지혜로운 사람이었지요. 늘 그러더군요, 겉만 보고 속을 다 알 순 없다고요. 하긴 그것도 그럴 만했지요. 저는 그가 온통 피투성이가 되고 나서야 봤으니까요. 누가 그런 생각을 했겠어요? 아마, 사랑에 가로막힌 어느 젊은 신사 양반인지도 모르지요. 저런, 만약 그가 죽기라도 한다면 그 부모에게 얼마나 큰 걱정거리겠어요! 어휴, 정말이지 그런 짓을 하다니 그 사악한 작자에게 악마가 씌었던 게 분명해요. 정말이지, 나리 말씀처럼 그는 군의 수치예요. 제가 여태 본 군의 신사 양반들은 대부분 영 다른 사람들이라서, 그 누구 못지않게 기독교인의 피 흘리는 걸 경멸할 것처럼 보이는걸요. 제 말은, 그러니까, 제 첫 남편 말마따나 점잖은 방식으로 말씀드리는 거예요. 물론 전쟁에 나가면 피를 보는 일이야 있어야겠지요. 하지만 그건 탓할 일이 아니지요. 거기서 우리 적을 많이 죽일수록 좋은 거고요. 저는 진심으로, 그들이 적이란 적은 어미가 낳은 아들놈 하나 남김없이 다 죽여 주었으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"아이고, 저런, 부인!\" 중위가 웃으며 말했다. \"하나 남김없이라니, 그건 좀 너무 살벌한 바람이오.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 나리,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"저는 조금도 살벌하지 않아요, 다만 우리 적들한테만 그렇지요. 그게 무슨 나쁜 일이겠어요. 정말이지 우리 적이 죽기를 바라는 건 당연한 일이에요. 그래야 전쟁이 끝나고 세금도 내려갈 테니까요. 우리처럼 세금을 내야 한다는 건 끔찍한 일이거든요. 글쎄, 지금 창문세만 해도 사십 실링이 넘는다니까요, 그것도 막을 수 있는 데는 다 막아 놨는데 말이에요. 정말이지 집을 거의 눈멀게 만들어 놨다고요. 제가 세무관한테 그랬어요, 제가 그랬죠, 제 생각엔 당신네가 우리를 좀 봐줘야 할 것 같다고요. 우리야말로 정부에 아주 좋은 친구가 아니냐고요. 정말로 그렇잖아요, 우리가 그들한테 엄청난 돈을 내는걸요. 그런데도 저는 가끔 혼자 생각해요, 정부는 우리한테 빚진 게 한 푼도 안 내는 사람들보다 더 많다고는 여기지 않을 거라고요. 그렇죠, 그렇죠, 그게 세상 이치인걸요.\"\n\n그녀가 이런 식으로 이야기를 이어 가고 있을 때 외과의가 방으로 들어왔다. 중위는 곧바로 환자가 어떠냐고 물었다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 대답할 뿐이었다. \"내가 불려 오지 않았다면 지금쯤 됐을 것보다는 나은 것 같소. 그리고 이만한 것도, 좀 더 일찍 불려 올 수 있었더라면 어쩌면 다행이었을 거요.\" \"선생,\" 중위가 말했다. \"두개골이 골절되지는 않았기를 바라오.\" \"흠,\" 외과의가 외쳤다. \"골절이 늘 가장 위험한 증상은 아니라오. 타박상이나 열상이 골절보다 더 나쁜 현상을 동반하고 더 치명적인 결과를 부르는 경우가 흔하지요. 이 일을 전혀 모르는 사람들은 두개골만 부러지지 않으면 다 괜찮다고 단정하지만, 나라면 내가 봐 온 어떤 타박상들보다는 차라리 두개골이 산산조각 나는 편을 보겠소.\" \"여기엔 그런 증상이 없기를 바라오.\" 중위가 말했다. \"증상이란,\" 외과의가 대답했다. \"늘 규칙적이지도 한결같지도 않소. 나는 아침엔 아주 불길하던 증상이 정오엔 좋은 쪽으로 바뀌었다가 밤엔 다시 불길해지는 경우를 본 적이 있소. 상처에 관해서라면 참으로 옳고 진실하게 일컫는 말이 있지요. '네모 레펜테 푸이트 투르피시무스'라고. 한번은 기억건대, 정강이뼈에 심한 타박상을 입은 환자에게 불려 간 적이 있소. 그로 인해 바깥쪽 피부가 찢겨서 출혈이 심했고, 안쪽 막들이 어찌나 찢겨 나갔던지 그 상처, 곧 불누스라는 그 틈으로 오스, 곧 뼈가 아주 또렷이 드러나 보였소. 그와 동시에 몇 가지 열성 증상이 끼어들었는데(맥박이 넘쳐서 많은 사혈이 필요함을 가리켰소), 나는 당장 괴저가 올까 걱정했소. 그걸 막으려고 나는 즉시 왼팔 정맥에 큰 구멍을 내어 거기서 피 스무 온스를 뽑았소. 나는 그 피가, 늑막염을 앓는 사람의 것처럼 몹시 끈적하고 들러붙거나, 실은 응고된 상태일 거라 예상했소. 그런데 놀랍게도 그 피는 장밋빛으로 생기 있게 나타났고, 그 농도가 더없이 건강한 사람의 피와 거의 다르지 않았소. 그래서 나는 그 부위에 찜질을 했고, 그것이 의도한 바에 톡톡히 부응했지요. 서너 차례 처치를 한 뒤로 그 상처가 짙은 고름, 곧 농을 흘려보내기 시작했고, 그 덕분에 유착이—한데 혹시 내 말을 제대로 못 알아듣고 계시는 건 아니오?\" \"아니, 정말이지,\" 중위가 대답했다. \"한 마디도 알아듣고 있다고는 말 못 하겠소.\" \"그럼, 선생,\" 외과의가 말했다. \"당신 인내심을 더는 시험하지 않겠소. 요컨대, 여섯 주 만에 내 환자는 타박상을 입기 전 그대로 두 다리로 걸어 다닐 수 있게 되었소.\" \"선생,\" 중위가 말했다. \"부디 친절을 베풀어 이 한 가지만 알려 주시오. 이 젊은 신사가 불행히도 입은 이 상처가 치명상이 될 것 같소?\" \"선생,\" 외과의가 대답했다. \"첫 처치에서 상처가 치명적일지 아닐지를 말한다는 건 참으로 어리석고 경솔한 억측이오. 우리는 모두 죽을 몸이고, 치료 과정에서는 우리 직업의 가장 위대한 자라도 결코 예견 못 할 증상이 흔히 생기니까.\" \"하지만 그가 위험하다고 보시오?\" 상대가 물었다. \"위험하냐고! 그야 물론이지요,\" 의사가 외쳤다. \"우리 가운데 누가, 더없이 완벽하게 건강한 상태인들 위험하지 않다고 할 수 있겠소? 그러니 이렇게 나쁜 상처를 입은 사람을 두고 위험에서 벗어났다고 할 수 있겠소? 지금 내가 할 수 있는 말은 다만, 내가 그때 불려 온 게 다행이었고, 어쩌면 좀 더 일찍 불려 왔더라면 더 나았으리란 것뿐이오. 내일 아침 일찍 다시 그를 보러 오겠소. 그동안엔 그를 더없이 조용히 두고, 물죽을 넉넉히 마시게 하시오.\" \"백포도주 유장은 안 되나요?\" 안주인이 말했다. \"그래요, 그래요, 백포도주 유장도,\" 의사가 외쳤다. \"원한다면, 아주 묽게 한다면 괜찮소.\" \"닭고기 국물도 조금?\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그래요, 그래요, 닭고기 국물은,\" 의사가 말했다. \"아주 좋소.\" \"젤리도 좀 만들어 주면 안 될까요?\" 안주인이 말했다. \"그럼요, 그럼요,\" 의사가 대답했다. \"젤리는 상처에 아주 좋소, 유착을 돕거든.\" 그리고 정말이지, 그녀가 수프나 진한 소스를 들먹이지 않은 것이 다행이었다. 의사는 이 집의 단골을 잃느니 차라리 거기에도 응했을 테니까.\n\n의사가 자리를 뜨기가 무섭게 안주인은 중위에게 그의 명성을 나팔 불기 시작했다. 중위는 짧은 만남으로는 이 착한 여인이며 동네 사람들 모두가 품은(어쩌면 아주 옳게도) 만큼이나 그의 의술에 호의적인 의견을 갖지는 못했다. 비록 그 의사가 다소 멋부리는 허풍쟁이였던 건 사실일까 봐 걱정되지만, 그럼에도 그는 꽤 솜씨 있는 외과의였을 수도 있으니 말이다.\n\n중위는 외과의의 그 학식 있는 이야기에서 존스 씨가 큰 위험에 처해 있음을 헤아리고, 노더턴 씨를 아주 엄중한 감시 아래 두라고 명령했다. 그러고는 아침에 그를 치안판사에게 데려가고, 글로스터로 가는 병력의 인솔은 프랑스인 중위에게 맡길 작정이었다. 이 프랑스인은 어떤 언어도 읽지도, 쓰지도, 말하지도 못했지만 그래도 훌륭한 장교였다.\n\n저녁이 되자 우리의 지휘관은 존스 씨에게 전갈을 보냈다. 방문이 폐가 되지 않는다면 한번 들르겠노라고. 이 정중함을 존스는 무척 다정하고 고맙게 받아들였고, 그리하여 중위는 그의 방으로 올라갔다. 거기서 그는 부상당한 사내가 예상보다 훨씬 나아진 것을 보았다. 아니, 존스는 친구에게, 외과의로부터 그러지 말라는 분명한 지시를 받지 않았더라면 진작에 일어났을 거라고 장담했다. 자기가 보기엔 여느 때처럼 멀쩡한 것 같았고, 머리 한쪽이 몹시 쓰라린 것 말고는 상처로 인한 다른 불편은 느끼지 못한다는 것이었다.\n\n\"내가 무척 기쁘겠소,\" 중위가 말했다. \"당신이 스스로 여기는 만큼 정말 멀쩡하다면 말이오. 그러면 곧장 명예를 회복할 수 있을 테니까. 한 대 얻어맞은 경우처럼 일을 무마할 수 없을 적엔, 그자를 빨리 불러내 결판 짓는 게 좋소. 다만 당신이 실제보다 자기를 더 멀쩡하다 여기는 게 아닐까 걱정이오. 그러면 그자가 당신보다 너무 유리해질 테니까.\"\n\n\"그래도 한번 해 보겠소,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"괜찮으시다면, 부디 친절을 베풀어 칼을 한 자루 빌려 주시오. 여기엔 제 칼이 없으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"내 칼은 기꺼이 자네를 위해 쓰이겠네, 사랑스러운 친구,\" 중위가 그에게 입을 맞추며 외쳤다. \"자네는 용감한 젊은이고, 나는 그 기개가 마음에 드네. 다만 자네 기력이 걱정이야. 그만한 일격에 그만큼 피를 흘렸으니 자네는 분명 무척 약해졌을 거야. 침대에 누워서는 기력이 모자란 줄 못 느낄지 몰라도, 칼을 한두 번 찌르고 나면 십중팔구 느끼게 될 걸세. 오늘 밤 그자를 불러내는 데는 동의할 수 없네. 다만 우리가 며칠 행군해 나아가기 전에 자네가 따라잡을 수 있기를 바라네. 그리고 내 명예를 걸고 약속하지, 자네는 반드시 명예 회복의 기회를 얻을 걸세. 아니면 자네를 해친 그자가 우리 연대에 남아 있지 못하게 하겠네.\"\n\n\"바라기는,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이 일을 오늘 밤 결판 지을 수 있었으면 좋겠소. 이제 당신이 그 얘기를 꺼냈으니, 나는 도저히 쉬지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"오, 그런 생각일랑 마시게,\" 상대가 대꾸했다. \"며칠 늦어진다고 달라질 건 없네. 명예의 상처는 자네 몸의 상처와는 다르거든. 치료가 늦어진다고 덧나는 법이 없지. 지금 명예를 회복하든 일주일 뒤에 회복하든 자네에겐 매한가지일 걸세.\"\n\n\"하지만 만약,\" 존스가 말했다. \"내가 더 나빠져서, 지금 이 상처의 후유증으로 죽기라도 한다면 어쩌오?\"\n\n\"그럼 자네 명예는,\" 중위가 대답했다. \"아무런 보상도 필요로 하지 않을 걸세. 내가 직접 자네의 인격을 위해 정의를 세우고, 자네가 회복했더라면 마땅히 처신했으리란 그 뜻을 세상에 증언해 주겠네.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"나는 이 지체가 마음에 걸리오. 군인인 당신께 이 얘기를 꺼내기가 거의 두렵소만, 비록 내가 무척 방종한 젊은이로 살아오긴 했어도, 가장 진지한 순간엔, 그리고 마음 밑바닥에선, 나는 정말로 기독교인이오.\"\n\n\"나 또한 그렇다네, 정말이야,\" 장교가 말했다. \"그것도 어찌나 열심인지, 저녁 식사 때 자네가 자기 종교의 편을 드는 걸 보고 흐뭇했다네. 그런데 젊은 신사 양반, 자네가 누구 앞에서든 자기 신앙을 밝히기를 두려워하는 기색을 보이니 지금은 조금 언짢구먼.\"\n\n\"하지만 얼마나 끔찍한 일이겠소,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"정말로 기독교인인 사람이, 그것을 명백히 금하신 그분의 명령을 거스르며 가슴속에 원한을 품는다는 것이? 병상에서 어떻게 내가 이 일을 견딜 수 있겠소? 아니, 가슴속에 이런 죄목을 나 자신을 거스르는 채로 품고서 어떻게 내 셈을 치를 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그런 명령이 있다는 건 나도 믿네,\" 중위가 외쳤다. \"하지만 명예를 아는 사람은 그걸 지킬 수가 없어. 그리고 군에 몸담으려면 자네는 명예를 아는 사람이어야만 하네. 언젠가 펀치 한 사발을 놓고 우리 군목에게 이 문제를 던진 적이 있는데, 그도 거기에 어려움이 많다는 건 인정하더군. 다만 이 한 가지 경우만큼은 군인에게 좀 너그러운 여지가 주어질 수 있기를 바란다고 하더라고. 그리고 정말이지 우리야 그러기를 바라는 게 마땅하지. 명예 없이 살아가는 걸 누가 견디겠나? 아니지, 아니야, 사랑스러운 친구, 살아 있는 동안 좋은 기독교인이 되게. 하지만 명예를 아는 사람도 되게. 결코 모욕을 그냥 넘기지 말게. 세상의 그 어떤 책도, 세상의 그 어떤 목사도 나를 그렇게 설득하진 못할 거야. 나는 내 종교를 무척 사랑하지만, 내 명예는 더 사랑하거든. 그 성경 구절의 표현에든, 번역에든, 아니면 그걸 이해하는 데든, 어디엔가 무슨 착오가 있는 게 분명해. 하지만 그게 어떻든 사람은 위험을 무릅써야 하네, 명예는 지켜야 하니까. 그러니 오늘 밤은 마음을 가라앉히게. 자네가 스스로 명예를 회복할 기회를 갖게 되리라 약속하네.\" 여기서 그는 존스에게 다정하게 입을 맞추고, 손을 잡아 흔들고는 작별했다.\n\n그러나 중위의 그 논리는 자신에게는 무척 만족스러웠을지언정, 그의 친구에게는 온전히 그렇지가 못했다. 그리하여 존스는 이 문제를 머릿속에서 한참 곱씹은 끝에, 마침내 한 가지 결심에 이르렀다. 그 결심이 무엇인지는 독자가 다음 장에서 보게 될 것이다.\n\n# Chapter xiv.\n\n참으로 무시무시한 장이다. 어지간한 독자라면 저녁 시간에, 그것도 혼자 있을 때에는 함부로 들어서지 않는 편이 좋을 것이다.\n\n존스는 닭, 아니 정확히 말하면 수탉으로 끓인 죽을 큰 그릇으로 아주 맛있게 들이켰다. 사실 그 죽의 재료가 된 수탉을 통째로, 거기에 베이컨 한 근을 곁들여서라도 먹어 치웠을 만한 식욕이었다. 이제 몸 상태에도 기력에도 모자람이 없음을 느낀 그는, 자리를 털고 일어나 적을 찾아 나서기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그러나 그는 먼저 중사를 불러오게 했다. 이 군인 양반들 가운데 처음으로 안면을 튼 사람이 바로 그였기 때문이다. 그런데 공교롭게도 그 갸륵한 장교는 말 그대로 술을 잔뜩 퍼마신 뒤 한참 전부터 베개에 머리를 누이고 있었는데, 어찌나 크게 코를 골던지 그 콧구멍에서 새어 나오는 소리를 덮을 만한 소음을 그의 귀에 들이밀기란 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었다.\n\n그래도 존스가 그를 보겠다고 고집을 부리자, 목청 좋은 술집 종업원이 마침내 그의 잠을 흔들어 깨우고 전갈을 전할 방도를 찾아냈다. 그 전갈을 알아듣자마자 중사는 침상에서 일어났고, 옷을 이미 걸친 채였던 터라 곧장 달려왔다. 존스는 자신의 계획을 중사에게 알리는 것이 적절치 않다고 여겼다. 사실 알려도 큰 탈은 없었을 것이다. 그 미늘창 든 사내 자신이 명예를 아는 사람이었고, 이미 한 사람을 베어 죽인 적도 있었으니 말이다. 그러니 그는 이런 비밀쯤은, 아니 발설에 현상금이 걸리지 않은 어떤 비밀이든 충실히 지켜 주었을 것이다. 그러나 존스는 그토록 짧은 사귐만으로 그의 그런 됨됨이를 알 길이 없었으니, 그의 조심성은 어쩌면 사려 깊고 칭찬할 만한 것이었으리라.\n\n그리하여 그는 먼저 이렇게 말문을 열었다. 이제 군에 들어온 마당에, 군인에게 어쩌면 가장 필요한 도구라 할 것, 곧 칼 한 자루가 없는 처지가 부끄럽다고. 그러면서 만일 한 자루 구해 줄 수 있다면 더없이 고맙겠노라 덧붙였다. \"그 값으로는,\" 그가 말했다, \"합당한 만큼 얼마든 치르겠소. 자루를 은으로 두른 것까지는 바라지 않소. 그저 좋은 칼날, 군인의 허리에 어울릴 만한 것이면 되오.\"\n\n무슨 일이 있었는지 빤히 알고 있었고, 존스가 매우 위태로운 상태라는 말도 들었던 중사는, 이런 한밤중에, 이런 처지의 사람에게서 이런 전갈을 받자, 곧 그가 정신이 오락가락한다고 단정해 버렸다. 한데 그는 늘 재치를(이 말을 흔히 쓰는 뜻으로 쓰자면) 갖추고 있던 자라, 이 환자의 헛소리를 제 잇속에 이용할 궁리를 떠올렸다. \"나리,\" 그가 말했다, \"제가 딱 맞춰 드릴 수 있을 것 같습니다요. 마침 더없이 훌륭한 물건을 하나 가지고 있지요. 은으로 두른 자루는 아닙니다만, 나리 말씀대로 그건 군인에게 어울리지도 않고요. 그래도 손잡이가 제법 점잖고, 칼날로 말하자면 유럽에서 손꼽히는 물건입니다. 이게 바로, 이게 바로 그러니까, 당장 가져다 드리겠습니다, 직접 보시고 손에 쥐어 보십쇼. 나리께서 이리 정정하신 걸 뵈니 진심으로 기쁩니다요.\"\n\n그는 곧장 칼을 들고 돌아와 존스에게 건넸다. 존스는 그것을 받아 칼집에서 뽑아 보고는, 아주 쓸 만하다고 중사에게 말한 뒤 값을 불러 보라 했다.\n\n이제 중사는 제 물건을 칭찬하는 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그는 말했다(아니, 아주 거창하게 맹세까지 했다). \"이 칼날은 데팅겐 전투에서 지위가 아주 높은 프랑스 장교한테서 빼앗은 겁니다. 제가 직접,\" 그가 말했다, \"그놈 대가리를 후려친 뒤에 옆구리에서 뽑아낸 거지요. 자루는 황금으로 된 거였는데, 그건 우리 멋쟁이 양반들 중 하나한테 팔아넘겼습니다. 나리, 황송합니다만 그런 양반들 중에는 칼날보다 칼자루를 더 치는 자들이 더러 있거든요.\"\n\n여기서 존스가 그의 말을 자르고 값이나 부르라고 청했다. 존스가 완전히 제정신이 아닌 데다 죽음이 코앞에 닥쳤다고 여긴 중사는, 너무 적게 불러 제 식구들에게 손해를 끼치지나 않을까 겁이 났다. 그래도 잠시 머뭇거린 끝에, 스무 기니를 부르는 것으로 만족하고는, 친형제에게라도 이보다 싸게는 못 판다고 맹세했다.\n\n\"스무 기니라!\" 존스가 더없이 놀라 소리쳤다. \"내가 미쳤거나, 평생 칼이라곤 구경도 못 해 본 줄 아는 게로군. 스무 기니라니, 참! 네가 나를 속여 먹으려 들 줄은 몰랐다. 자, 칼 도로 가져가라. 아니, 가만 생각해 보니 내가 직접 가지고 있다가 아침에 네 상관에게 보여 주겠다. 네가 이걸 얼마에 부르더라고 함께 일러 주면서 말이지.\"\n\n앞서 말했듯 중사는 늘 재치를(앞서 말한 그 뜻으로) 곁에 지니고 있던 자라, 이제 존스가 자기가 짐작했던 그런 상태가 아님을 똑똑히 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 존스 못지않게 놀란 척을 꾸며 보이며 말했다. \"나리, 제가 정말이지 그렇게 터무니없이 부른 건 아닙니다. 게다가 헤아려 주십쇼, 이게 제가 가진 단 하나뿐인 칼이라, 저 자신이 칼도 없이 다니면서 상관의 노여움을 살 위험까지 무릅써야 하는 겁니다. 정말이지 이걸 다 합쳐 놓고 보면, 스무 실링이 그렇게 터무니없는 값은 아닐 듯싶습니다요.\"\n\n\"스무 실링이라!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"이놈, 방금은 스무 기니라 부르지 않았더냐.\"—\"아니!\" 중사가 외쳤다. \"나리께서 잘못 들으셨거나, 아니면 제가 잘못 말했나 봅니다. 사실 제가 아직 잠이 덜 깨서요. 스무 기니라니, 참! 나리께서 그리 발끈하신 것도 무리가 아니지요. 제가 스무 기니라 했다니. 아니, 아닙니다, 스무 실링이라 한 겁니다, 정말입니다요. 나리께서 이것저것 다 헤아려 보시면, 그게 그렇게 과한 값이라곤 여기지 않으실 줄 압니다. 하기야 이만큼 그럴듯해 보이는 무기를 더 싸게 살 수야 있겠지요. 허나—\"\n\n여기서 존스가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"너와 입씨름할 생각이 조금도 없으니, 네가 부른 값보다 한 실링 더 얹어 주마.\" 그러고는 그에게 1기니를 건네며 가서 자라 이르고, 행군에 행운을 빌어 주었다. 그러면서 부대가 우스터에 닿기 전에 따라잡았으면 좋겠다고 덧붙였다.\n\n중사는 아주 공손하게 작별을 고했다. 제 장사에 흡족했을 뿐 아니라, 환자가 헛소리를 한다고 넘겨짚었다가 빠질 뻔한 그 헛디딤에서 솜씨 좋게 벗어난 것에도 적잖이 흐뭇해하면서 말이다.\n\n중사가 떠나가자마자 존스는 침상에서 일어나 옷을 빠짐없이 갖춰 입었다. 윗옷까지 걸쳤는데, 그것이 흰색이었던 탓에 흘러내린 핏줄기가 아주 또렷이 드러나 보였다. 그러고는 새로 산 칼을 손에 거머쥐고 막 나서려는데, 자기가 벌이려는 일이 어떤 일인지 하는 생각이 문득 그를 사로잡았다. 그는 몇 분 안에 어쩌면 한 인간의 목숨을 앗을 수도, 자신의 목숨을 잃을 수도 있음을 곰곰 곱씹기 시작했다. \"좋다,\" 그가 말했다, \"한데 나는 무엇을 위해 목숨을 거는가? 그야 내 명예를 위해서다. 그러면 그 인간이란 누구인가? 까닭도 없이 나를 해치고 모욕한 악당이다. 하지만 복수는 하늘이 금하지 않으셨던가? 그렇다, 그러나 세상은 그것을 명한다. 그럼 나는 하늘의 분명한 명을 거스르면서까지 세상에 따라야 하는가? 신의 노여움을 사느니, 차라리—흥—겁쟁이니, 비겁한 놈이니 하는 소리를 들으란 말인가? 더는 생각지 않겠다. 나는 결심했고, 그와 싸워야만 한다.\"\n\n이제 시계가 자정을 알렸고, 노더턴을 지키느라 서 있는 보초를 빼고는 집 안의 모두가 잠자리에 들어 있었다. 존스가 가만히 문을 열고 적을 쫓아 나섰을 때 말이다. 그가 갇힌 곳에 대해서는 술집 종업원에게 완벽한 설명을 들어 둔 터였다. 이때 그가 드러낸 것보다 더 끔찍한 형상을 떠올리기란 쉽지 않을 것이다. 앞서 말했듯 그는 핏줄기가 줄줄이 묻은 옅은 색 윗옷을 걸치고 있었다. 바로 그 피를, 게다가 외과의가 더 뽑아낸 스무 온스의 피까지 잃은 그의 얼굴은 핏기가 가셔 창백했다. 머리 둘레에는 붕대가 잔뜩 감겨, 흡사 터번 같았다. 오른손에는 칼을, 왼손에는 촛불을 들고 있었다. 그러니 피투성이 뱅코의 유령조차 그에 견줄 자격이 없었다. 사실, 이보다 더 무시무시한 망령이 묘지에 불려 나온 적도, 서머싯셔의 어느 겨울 저녁 크리스마스 모닥불가에 둘러앉은 선량한 사람들의 상상 속에 떠오른 적도 없었으리라 나는 믿는다.\n\n보초가 우리 주인공이 다가오는 것을 처음 본 순간, 그의 머리카락이 슬그머니 척탄병 모자를 들어 올리기 시작했다. 그와 동시에 두 무릎이 서로 부딪치며 싸움을 벌였다. 이내 그의 온몸이 학질 발작보다 더한 떨림에 사로잡혔다. 그러더니 그는 총을 발사하고는 얼굴을 땅에 박은 채 풀썩 쓰러졌다.\n\n그가 총을 쏜 것이 두려움 탓이었는지 용기 탓이었는지, 아니면 그 공포의 대상을 정말로 겨냥했던 것인지는 나도 알 수 없다. 다만 겨냥했다 하더라도, 그는 다행히 상대를 빗맞히는 행운을 누렸다.\n\n그 사내가 쓰러지는 것을 본 존스는 그가 겁에 질린 까닭을 짐작하고는 웃음을 참지 못했는데, 정작 자신이 방금 벗어난 위험에 대해서는 조금도 생각하지 않았다. 그러고는 쓰러진 그 자세 그대로 엎어져 있는 사내를 지나쳐, 들은 대로 노더턴이 갇혀 있다는 방으로 들어섰다. 그곳, 호젓한 그 자리에서 그가 발견한 것은—탁자 위에 놓인 빈 한 쿼트들이 술잔이었다. 거기 맥주가 좀 엎질러져 있어, 방금까지 누가 머물렀던 듯 보였다. 그러나 지금은 텅 비어 있었다.\n\n그래서 존스는 이 방이 다른 방으로 이어질지도 모른다고 생각했다. 그러나 사방을 샅샅이 뒤져 보아도, 자신이 들어온 문, 곧 보초가 서 있던 그 문 말고는 다른 문을 찾을 수 없었다. 그는 이어 노더턴의 이름을 몇 번이고 불러 보았다. 그러나 아무도 대답하지 않았다. 그리고 이것은 보초의 공포를 더욱 굳히는 것 말고는 아무 소용이 없었으니, 보초는 이제 그 지원병이 상처로 죽었으며 그 유령이 자기를 죽인 자를 찾아온 것이라고 확신해 버렸다. 그는 이제 공포의 온갖 고통 속에 누워 있었다. 나는 진심으로 바라거니와, 앞으로 혼이 빠지도록 겁에 질린 사람을 연기할 배우들 가운데 누군가가 그를 보았더라면 좋았을 것이다. 그랬다면 그들이, 객석의 흥과 갈채를 노린 우스꽝스러운 재주와 몸짓이나 부려 대는 대신, 자연 그대로를 본뜨는 법을 배웠을 테니 말이다.\n\n새가 이미 날아가 버렸음을, 적어도 그를 찾기란 글렀음을 깨닫고, 또 총소리가 온 집안을 깨우리라는 것을 옳게 알아챈 우리 주인공은, 이제 촛불을 끄고 가만히 제 방으로, 제 침상으로 살그머니 되돌아갔다. 만일 같은 계단에 다른 사람이 하나라도 있었더라면 들키지 않고 거기까지 가지는 못했을 것이나, 마침 그 계단에는 통풍으로 침상에 누워 지내는 신사 한 사람뿐이었다. 그가 미처 제 방 문에 닿기도 전에, 보초가 서 있던 그 홀은 셔츠 바람인 사람, 옷도 제대로 못 걸친 사람으로 이미 절반쯤 들어찼고, 다들 무슨 일이냐고 서로에게 다급히 캐묻고 있었으니 말이다.\n\n이제 그 병사는 우리가 방금 그를 두고 온 바로 그 자리, 그 자세 그대로 누워 있는 채로 발견되었다. 여럿이 곧장 달려들어 그를 일으키려 했고, 더러는 그가 죽었다고 단정했다. 그러나 그들은 이내 자기들이 잘못 봤음을 알았으니, 그는 자기 몸에 손을 대는 자들과 몸부림치며 다툴 뿐 아니라, 황소처럼 고래고래 울부짖기 시작했기 때문이다. 사실 그는 수많은 혼령이나 악마들이 자기를 붙잡고 있다고 여기고 있었다. 망령의 공포에 사로잡힌 그의 상상이, 보거나 만지는 모든 것을 한낱 귀신이며 유령으로 바꿔 놓았던 것이다.\n\n마침내 그는 머릿수에 눌려 두 다리로 일어섰다. 촛불이 들어오고 동료 두세 명이 곁에 있는 것을 보자, 그는 조금 정신을 차렸다. 그러나 사람들이 무슨 일이냐고 묻자, 그는 이렇게 대답했다. \"나는 죽은 목숨이야, 그뿐이지, 나는 죽은 목숨이라고, 도저히 못 살아나, 내가 그를 봤단 말이야.\" \"잭, 뭘 봤다는 거야?\" 병사 중 하나가 물었다. \"어제 죽은 그 젊은 지원병을 봤다고.\" 그러고는 만일 자신이 그 지원병을 본 게 아니라면 가장 무거운 저주가 제게 떨어져도 좋다고 퍼부었다. 온몸이 피투성이인 채로 입과 콧구멍에서 불을 토해 내며 자기 곁을 지나 노더턴 소위가 있던 방으로 들어가더니, 소위의 멱을 거머쥐고는 우레 같은 소리와 함께 그를 데리고 날아가 버렸다는 것이었다.\n\n이 이야기는 청중에게서 너그러운 환대를 받았다. 그 자리의 여인들은 모두 그것을 굳게 믿고는, 부디 살해당하는 일에서 자기들을 지켜 달라고 하늘에 기도했다. 남자들 중에도 이 이야기를 믿는 이가 많았다. 그러나 더러는 그것을 비웃음과 조롱거리로 돌렸고, 그 자리에 있던 한 중사는 아주 냉랭하게 대꾸했다. \"젊은 친구, 보초를 서다 잠이 들어 꿈이나 꾼 일로 자네는 두고두고 시달리게 될 걸세.\"\n\n병사가 대꾸했다. \"벌을 주시려거든 얼마든지 주십쇼. 하지만 저는 지금처럼 또렷이 깨어 있었습니다. 소위를 데려갔듯이 악마가 저를 데려가도 좋으니, 제가 말씀드린 대로 그 죽은 사람을, 두 자루 커다란 횃불처럼 크고 시뻘건 눈을 한 그 사람을 정녕 보지 않았다면 말입니다.\"\n\n이제 병력의 지휘관과 이 집의 안주인이 둘 다 당도했다. 전자는 그때 깨어 있다가 보초가 총 쏘는 소리를 듣고는, 무슨 변고가 있으리라 크게 걱정한 것은 아니었으나 즉시 일어나는 것이 제 도리라 여겼던 것이다. 반면 후자의 걱정은 훨씬 더 컸으니, 제 숟가락이며 술잔들이 자기로부터 그런 명령을 받은 바도 없이 행군길에 오르지나 않았을까 해서였다.\n\n우리의 가엾은 보초에게는, 앞서 보았다고 여긴 그 망령보다 이 장교의 모습이 그리 더 반가울 것도 없었지만, 그는 또 한 번 그 끔찍한 이야기를, 피와 불을 잔뜩 보태어 들려주었다. 그러나 그는 방금 말한 두 사람 중 누구의 믿음도 얻지 못하는 불운을 겪었다. 장교는 매우 신앙심 깊은 사람이었으되 이런 종류의 공포에서는 자유로웠고, 게다가 우리가 본 그 상태로 존스를 떠나온 지 얼마 되지도 않은 터라 그가 죽었으리라곤 조금도 의심하지 않았기 때문이다. 안주인으로 말하자면, 그리 신앙심이 깊지는 않았으나 혼령에 관한 가르침을 딱히 꺼리지도 않았다. 다만 이 이야기에는 그녀가 거짓임을 빤히 아는 한 대목이 있었으니, 그것은 곧 독자에게 알려 주겠다.\n\n그러나 노더턴이 우레에 실려 갔든 불에 실려 갔든, 아니면 그 밖의 어떤 식으로 사라졌든 간에, 이제 그의 몸이 더는 감금 상태에 있지 않다는 것만큼은 분명했다. 이 일을 두고 중위는 앞서 그 중사가 내렸다고 방금 말한 것과 그리 다르지 않은 결론에 이르렀고, 즉시 보초를 잡아 가두라 명했다. 그리하여 운명의 기묘한 뒤집힘으로(군대 생활에서 그리 드문 일은 아니지만), 지키던 자가 지킴을 당하는 신세가 되고 말았다.\n\n앞서 벌어진 모험의 결말.\n\n잠들었다는 의심 말고도, 중위는 가엾은 보초병을 향해 또 하나의, 그보다 더 고약한 의심을 품고 있었다. 바로 배신이라는 의심이었다. 그는 유령 이야기 따위는 단 한 마디도 믿지 않았기에, 그 모든 것이 자기를 속이려고 꾸며낸 지어낸 이야기일 뿐이며, 실은 이 사내가 노더턴에게 매수되어 그를 도망치게 놓아준 것이라고 짐작했다. 더욱이 그가 이렇게 의심한 까닭은, 연대에서 누구 못지않게 용감하고 대담하다는 평을 듣던 자, 여러 차례 전투를 겪고 여러 군데 부상을 입었으며, 한마디로 늘 훌륭하고 용맹한 병사답게 처신해 온 자에게 그런 공포란 더욱 부자연스러워 보였기 때문이다.\n\n그러니 독자가 이런 사람에 대해 조금이라도 나쁜 인상을 품지 않도록, 우리는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 그의 인품을 이 죄의 혐의에서 구해 내려 한다.\n\n그런데 노더턴 씨는, 앞서 말했듯이 이번 일로 얻은 영광에 더없이 흡족해하고 있었다. 그는 어쩌면 명성에는 시기가 따라붙기 마련이라는 것을 보았거나, 들었거나, 짐작했는지도 모른다. 그렇다고 여기서 내가, 그가 이교도처럼 복수의 여신 네메시스를 믿거나 떠받드는 쪽으로 기울었다는 뜻을 넌지시 비치려는 것은 아니다. 사실 나는 그가 그 여신의 이름조차 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없으리라 확신한다. 게다가 그는 활동적인 기질이었던지라, 치안판사가 그에게 숙소를 마련해 줄지도 모를 글로스터 성의 그 비좁은 감방을 몹시 꺼렸다. 또한 그는, 내가 차마 이름을 입에 올리지 않으려는 어떤 나무로 된 구조물에 대해서도 불안한 상념을 떨치지 못하고 있었다. 이 구조물은 다른 거의 모든 공공 건축물보다 사회에 더 유익한 것이거나 적어도 그렇게 만들 수 있는 것이니, 내 생각에 사람들은 그것을 부끄러워하기보다는 오히려 기려야 마땅하건만, 나는 세상 사람들의 통념에 따라 그 이름을 삼가려 한다. 한마디로, 그의 행동에 대해 더는 이유를 들먹이지 않더라도, 노더턴 씨는 그날 저녁에 떠나고 싶어 했고, 그에게 남은 일이라곤 그 방법을 궁리하는 것뿐이었는데, 그것이 자못 어려운 문제로 보였다.\n\n그런데 이 젊은 신사는, 도덕은 다소 비뚤어졌을지언정 몸만큼은 더할 나위 없이 곧았으니, 매우 튼튼하고 잘 빠진 체격이었다. 게다가 그의 얼굴은 대다수 여인들에게 잘생겼다는 평을 받았는데, 널찍하고 혈색이 좋은 데다 치아도 그런대로 가지런했기 때문이다. 이런 매력은 이런 부류의 미남을 적잖이 즐기던 우리 안주인에게 인상을 남기지 않을 수 없었다. 실로 그녀는 이 젊은이를 진심으로 가엾게 여겼고, 외과의로부터 지원병 쪽 형편이 좋지 않을 듯하다는 말을 듣자, 앞으로 소위에게도 사정이 결코 순탄하지 않으리라 짐작했다. 그리하여 그를 찾아가도 좋다는 허락을 받아 낸 뒤, 몹시 우울한 기색에 잠긴 그를 보고서, 지원병의 목숨이 거의 가망 없다는 말로 그 우울을 한층 부추긴 다음, 그녀는 슬며시 몇 마디 운을 띄우기 시작했다. 상대가 그것을 선뜻, 또 열렬히 받아들이니, 두 사람은 이내 제대로 뜻이 통했고, 마침내 이렇게 합의를 보았다. 소위가 어떤 신호에 맞추어 굴뚝을 타고 올라가면, 그 굴뚝이 곧장 부엌 굴뚝과 통하니 거기로 다시 내려올 수 있고, 그녀가 사람들이 다니지 않도록 길을 비워 그 틈을 마련해 준다는 것이었다.\n\n하지만 기질이 다른 독자가 이 일을 빌미로, 모든 연민을 어리석음이요 사회에 해로운 것이라고 너무 성급히 단죄하지 않도록, 우리는 이 행동에 어쩌면 조금쯤 한몫했을 또 다른 사정을 밝혀 두는 편이 옳다고 본다. 마침 이때 소위는 오십 파운드의 돈을 지니고 있었는데, 이 돈은 실은 중대 전체의 것이었다. 대위가 자기 중위와 다툰 터라 중대의 급료 지급을 소위에게 맡겼던 것이다. 그런데 그는 이 돈을 우리 안주인의 손에 맡겨 두는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼으니, 아마도 앞으로 출두하여 자신에게 걸린 혐의에 답하겠다는 보석금이나 담보 삼아 그리한 것이리라. 그러나 조건이 무엇이었든 간에, 확실한 것은 그녀는 돈을 손에 넣었고 소위는 자유를 얻었다는 사실이다.\n\n독자는 어쩌면 이 마음씨 고운 여인의 동정심 많은 성품으로 미루어, 죄가 없는 줄 뻔히 아는 일로 가엾은 보초병이 잡혀가는 것을 보았으니 곧장 그를 위해 나서 주었으리라 기대할지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀가 앞서 말한 일에서 이미 연민을 모조리 써 버린 탓인지, 아니면 이 사내의 이목구비가 소위와 그리 다르지 않았는데도 그 연민을 자아내지 못한 탓인지는 내가 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 지금 잡힌 이 죄수를 두둔하기는커녕, 오히려 그가 죄인이라고 장교에게 다그쳐 말하면서, 눈과 두 손을 치켜든 채 이 세상을 다 준대도 살인자를 도망치게 하는 일에는 끼어들지 않았을 것이라고 단언했다.\n\n이제 모든 것이 다시 한번 잠잠해졌고, 사람들 대부분은 도로 잠자리로 돌아갔다. 그러나 안주인은 타고난 부산한 기질 탓인지, 아니면 자기 은그릇이 걱정된 탓인지 도무지 잠이 오지 않아, 한 시간 남짓 뒤면 행군해야 할 장교들을 구슬려 그 시간을 자기와 함께 펀치 한 사발을 들며 보내게 했다.\n\n존스는 그동안 내내 깨어 누운 채 그 소동과 부산함의 상당 부분을 들었던 터라, 이제 그 자초지종이 궁금해졌다. 그래서 종을 당겼는데, 적어도 스무 번은 울려 댔건만 아무 소용이 없었다. 안주인이 일행과 어찌나 흥겹게 떠들썩한지, 그곳에서는 그녀 자신의 입놀림 말고는 어떤 종소리도 들리지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 부엌에 함께 앉아 있던 사환과 하녀로 말하자면(사환은 혼자 깨어 앉아 있기가 무서웠고 하녀는 혼자 잠자리에 들기가 무서웠던 까닭이다), 종이 울리는 소리를 들을수록 더욱 겁에 질려, 마치 제자리에 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝도 하지 못했다.\n\n마침내 잡담이 운 좋게 끊긴 틈을 타, 그 소리가 우리 마음씨 좋은 안주인의 귀에 닿았고, 그녀는 곧장 두 하인을 불러댔으니, 둘 다 즉시 그 부름에 따랐다. \"조,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"저 신사분 종소리 안 들려? 왜 안 올라가는 게냐?\"—\"방 시중드는 건,\" 사환이 대답했다. \"제 일이 아닙니다요. 그건 하녀 베티 일이지요.\"—\"그렇게 따질 거면,\" 하녀가 받아쳤다. \"신사분 시중드는 것도 제 일이 아니에요. 가끔 해 드린 적이야 있지만, 네가 그렇게 토를 다는 마당이니 다시 하면 악마가 날 잡아가도 좋아요.\" 종이 여전히 사납게 울려 대자, 안주인은 발끈하여, 사환이 당장 올라가지 않으면 바로 그날 아침에 내쫓아 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. \"그러시면, 마님,\" 사환이 말했다. \"저도 어쩔 수 없지요. 다른 하인 일은 안 합니다요.\" 그러자 그녀는 하녀에게로 돌아서서 부드러운 말로 구슬려 보려 했으나, 모두 헛수고였다. 베티는 조만큼이나 요지부동이었다. 둘 다 그건 제 일이 아니라며 우겨 댔고, 절대 하지 않겠다고 버텼다.\n\n그러자 중위가 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"자, 내가 이 다툼을 끝내 주지.\" 그러고는 두 하인을 돌아보며, 끝까지 고집을 꺾지 않은 그 결의를 칭찬하더니, 다만 한쪽이 가는 데 동의하면 다른 쪽도 갈 것이 분명하다고 덧붙였다. 이 제안에 둘은 그 자리에서 곧바로 동의했고, 그리하여 아주 다정하게 바싹 붙은 채 함께 올라갔다. 둘이 가고 나자, 중위는 어째서 둘 다 혼자 가기를 그토록 꺼렸는지 까닭을 일러 주어 안주인의 노여움을 가라앉혔다.\n\n둘은 곧 돌아와, 그 아픈 신사가 죽기는커녕 멀쩡한 사람처럼 기운차게 말하더라고, 그리고 대위께 안부를 전하며 행군하기 전에 한번 만나 뵐 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘겠다고 하더라고 안주인에게 알렸다.\n\n마음씨 좋은 중위는 곧바로 그 청에 응하여, 침상 곁에 앉아 아래층에서 벌어진 광경을 일러 주고는, 그 보초병을 본보기로 삼겠다는 자신의 뜻으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n이에 존스는 그에게 모든 진실을 들려주고, 그 가엾은 병사를 벌하지 말아 달라고 간곡히 청했다. \"저는 확신합니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그자가 소위의 도주에 아무 죄가 없는 것은, 거짓말을 지어내거나 당신을 속이려 든 일이 전혀 없는 것과 마찬가지입니다.\"\n\n중위는 잠깐 머뭇거리다가 이렇게 답했다. \"흠, 자네가 그 친구의 혐의 한쪽을 벗겨 주었으니, 다른 한쪽도 입증하기란 불가능하겠군. 보초가 그자 혼자만은 아니었으니 말이야. 하지만 그 비겁한 죄만큼은 그 녀석을 벌하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같아. 그래도 그런 체포의 공포가 어떤 효과를 낼지 누가 알겠나? 게다가 솔직히 말하면, 그자는 적을 상대로는 늘 잘 처신해 왔거든. 자, 이런 친구들에게서 신앙의 기미라도 보이는 건 좋은 일이지. 그러니 약속하지, 우리가 행군할 때 그자를 풀어 주겠네. 그런데 들어 보게, 집합 북이 울리는군. 사랑하는 친구여, 입맞춤 한 번 더 해 주게. 마음을 어지럽히거나 서두르지 말고, 인내라는 기독교의 가르침을 기억하게. 그러면 머잖아 자네 손으로 정의를 세우고, 자네에게 상처를 입힌 그자에게 떳떳한 복수를 할 수 있게 되리라 내 장담하네.\" 그러고 나서 중위는 떠나갔고, 존스는 마음을 가라앉혀 쉬어 보려 애썼다.\n\n약 이틀간의 이야기.\n경이로움에 관한, 놀랍도록 긴 장. 우리의 모든 서론 격 장들 가운데 단연 가장 길다.\n\n이제 우리는 지금까지 등장한 그 어떤 것보다도 더 기이하고 놀라운 일들을 들려주지 않을 수 없는 책으로 들어서려 하므로, 이 머리말 격 서론 장에서 이른바 경이로움이라 불리는 글쓰기의 한 갈래에 대해 몇 마디 해 두는 것도 나쁘지 않을 듯하다. 우리는 우리 자신을 위해서나 남들을 위해서나 여기에 어떤 분명한 한계를 그어 보려 애쓸 것이며, 실로 이보다 더 필요한 일도 없다. 이 대목에서는 서로 다른 기질의 비평가들이 매우 다른 양극단으로 치닫기 쉽기 때문이다. 어떤 이들은 다시에 씨처럼, 불가능한 바로 그것이 그럼에도 개연성을 지닐 수 있다고 선뜻 인정하는가 하면, 다른 이들은 역사적 믿음도 시적 믿음도 너무나 박약하여, 자기 눈으로 직접 보지 못한 것과 비슷한 일이라면 가능하지도 개연성도 없다고 여긴다.\n\n[*] 여기서, 그리고 우리 작품의 거의 모든 다른 대목에서 이 말은 세상의 모든 독자를 뜻한다. [**] 다시에 씨가 아일랜드 사람이 아니었던 것은 다행이다.\n\n그러니 먼저, 나는 모든 작가에게 가능성의 한계 안에 머물 것을 요구하는 것이 매우 합당하다고 생각한다. 그리고 인간이 행할 수 없는 일이라면, 인간이 그것을 행했다고 믿기란 거의 불가능하다는 점을 늘 기억해야 한다. 어쩌면 이러한 확신이 옛 이교 신들에 관한 숱한 이야기를 낳았는지도 모른다(그 대부분이 시적 기원을 지니기 때문이다). 시인은 제멋대로 분방한 상상을 마음껏 펼치고 싶은 나머지, 그 위력의 정도를 독자들이 판가름할 수 없는, 아니 오히려 독자들이 무한하다고 상상하는 그런 힘에 기댔다. 그리하여 독자들은 그 힘에 관해 어떤 기적이 이야기되더라도 충격받을 일이 없었던 것이다. 이는 호메로스의 기적들을 옹호하는 데 강력하게 내세워져 왔으며, 어쩌면 변호가 될 만하다. 다만 포프 씨가 주장하듯, 율리시스가 매우 아둔한 민족이던 파이아키아인들에게 어리석은 거짓말 한 보따리를 늘어놓았기 때문이 아니라, 시인 자신이 이교도들을 위해 글을 썼고 그들에게는 시적 우화가 신앙의 조항이었기 때문이다. 내 자신으로 말하자면, 내 성정이 워낙 측은지심이 많은지라, 폴리페모스가 우유 식단에 그쳐 제 눈을 보전했더라면 좋았겠다 싶다고 고백하지 않을 수 없다. 또한 키르케가 율리시스의 동료들을 돼지로 둔갑시켰을 때 율리시스가 느낀 것 못지않게 나도 마음이 쓰였는데, 키르케는 내 생각에 나중에 사람의 살에 너무 큰 애착을 보였으니 그것을 베이컨으로 만들 수 있는 사람이라고는 도저히 여겨지지 않는다. 마찬가지로 나는 진심으로, 호메로스가 초자연적 행위자를 되도록 드물게 등장시키라는 호라티우스의 규칙을 알았더라면 좋았겠다 싶다. 그랬다면 우리는 그의 신들이 하찮은 심부름이나 다니고, 흔히 존경받을 자격을 죄다 잃을 뿐 아니라 조롱과 비웃음의 대상이 될 만큼 처신하는 꼴을 보지 않았을 것이다. 이런 행태는 경건하고 사려 깊은 이교도의 믿음조차 충격에 빠뜨렸을 것이며, 결코 변호될 수 없는 것이었다. 다만 내가 이따금 거의 기울곤 했던 어떤 가정에 동의한다면 모를까 말이다. 즉 이 더없이 영광스러운 시인이—실제로 그러했지만—제 시대와 나라의 미신적 신앙을 풍자하려는 의도를 품었으리라는 가정이다.\n\n하지만 나는 기독교 작가에게는 아무 쓸모도 없는 교리에 너무 오래 머물렀다. 그는 자기 신조의 일부를 이루는 저 천상의 무리를 작품에 끌어들일 수 없거니와, 그렇다고 오래전에 불멸의 자리에서 끌어내려진 신들을 이교 신학에서 뒤지는 것 또한 끔찍한 유치함이기 때문이다. 섀프츠베리 경은 현대인이 뮤즈를 불러내는 것만큼 싸늘한 것도 없다고 지적했는데, 거기에 그것만큼 터무니없는 것도 없다고 덧붙일 수도 있었으리라. 현대인이라면 어떤 이들이 호메로스가 그랬으리라 여겼듯 발라드를 불러내거나, 《휴디브라스》의 작가처럼 에일 한 잔을 불러내는 편이 훨씬 더 우아할 것이다. 어쩌면 이 에일 한 잔이 히포크레네나 헬리콘의 온갖 샘물을 다 합친 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 시와 산문에 영감을 불어넣었을지도 모른다.\n\n우리 현대인에게 어떤 식으로든 허용될 수 있는 유일한 초자연적 행위자는 유령뿐이다. 하지만 이마저도 작가에게는 극도로 아껴 쓰라고 권하고 싶다. 이것들은 실로 의약에서의 비소나 그 밖의 위험한 약물처럼 더없이 조심스럽게 써야 하는 법이다. 또한 독자의 폭소가 작품이나 작가에게 큰 흠이나 망신이 될 만한 그런 작품, 그런 작가의 경우에는 아예 유령을 끌어들이지 말라고 권하고 싶다.\n\n요정이며 선녀며 그 밖의 그런 익살극에 대해서는, 나는 일부러 언급을 빼겠다. 인간 본성의 한계로는 너무 좁아 다 담을 수 없을 만큼 광대한 그릇을 지닌 저 놀라운 상상력을 어떤 한계 안에 가두는 것이 나로서는 영 내키지 않기 때문이다. 그들의 작품은 하나의 새로운 창조로 여겨져야 하며, 따라서 그들은 제 것을 가지고 제 마음대로 할 정당한 권리를 지닌다.\n\n그러므로 인간이야말로(정말로 매우 특별한 경우가 아니고서는) 우리 역사가나 우리 시인의 붓 앞에 놓이는 가장 높은 소재이다. 그리고 그의 행동을 들려줄 때는, 우리가 묘사하는 행위자의 능력 범위를 넘어서지 않도록 크게 주의해야 한다.\n\n또한 가능성만으로 우리가 정당화되는 것도 아니다. 우리는 마찬가지로 개연성의 규칙 안에도 머물러야 한다. 이는 내 생각에 아리스토텔레스의 견해이거나, 설령 아니더라도 어떤 현인의 견해이다. 그 권위는 세월이 지나 오래될수록 그만큼 묵직해질 것이다. \"믿기 어려운 것을 들려주는 시인에게, 들려준 그것이 실제로 사실이라는 점은 변명이 되지 못한다.\" 이는 어쩌면 시에 관해서는 옳다고 인정할 만하지만, 역사가에게까지 넓히기란 실현 불가능하다고 여겨질 수 있다. 역사가는 자기가 발견한 그대로 일들을 기록해야 하기 때문이다. 비록 그것들이 삼키려면 적잖은 역사적 믿음이 필요할 만큼 비범한 성질의 것이라 해도 말이다. 헤로도토스가 묘사한 크세르크세스의 실패한 군사 원정이나, 아리아노스가 들려준 알렉산드로스의 성공한 원정이 그러하다. 후대로 와서는 헨리 5세가 거둔 아쟁쿠르의 승리, 혹은 스웨덴의 카를 12세가 거둔 나르바의 승리가 그러하다. 이 모든 사례는 곱씹어 볼수록 한층 더 놀랍게만 보인다.\n\n그러나 그러한 사실들은 이야기의 실타래 속에 나타나는 한, 아니 실로 이야기의 본질적인 부분을 이루는 한, 역사가는 그것들을 실제로 일어난 그대로 기록하는 것이 정당할 뿐 아니라, 만일 그것들을 빠뜨리거나 고친다면 도리어 용서받지 못할 것이다. 하지만 그만큼 중대하지도 않고 그만큼 꼭 필요하지도 않은 다른 사실들이 있는데, 이런 것들은 아무리 잘 입증되었다 한들 독자의 회의(懷疑)에 영합하여 망각 속에 묻어 버려도 무방하다. 조지 빌리어스의 유령이라는 저 유명한 이야기가 그러하니, 그것은 《반란의 역사》처럼 그토록 엄숙한 저작에 끌어들여지기보다는, 드렐랭쿠르 박사에게 선사되어 그의 《죽음에 관한 논고》 머리에 실린 빌 부인의 유령과 짝을 이루게 했더라면 훨씬 더 어울렸을 것이다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 역사가가 실제로 일어난 일에만 자신을 한정하고, 아무리 잘 입증되었다 해도 거짓임이 분명한 정황이라면 무엇이든 단호히 물리친다면, 그는 이따금 경이로움에 빠질지언정 결코 믿을 수 없는 것에 빠지지는 않을 것이다. 그는 독자의 놀라움과 경탄을 자주 불러일으키겠지만, 호라티우스가 말한 저 못 믿겠다는 식의 혐오는 결코 일으키지 않을 것이다. 그러므로 우리가 이 규칙, 곧 개연성을 저버리는 일을 어기는 것은 대체로 허구에 빠질 때이며, 역사가는 제 본분을 버리고 연애소설 작가로 나서기 전까지는 좀처럼, 아니 결코 이 개연성을 떠나지 않는다. 다만 이 점에서, 공적 사건을 들려주는 역사가들은 사사로운 삶의 장면에 자신을 한정하는 우리보다 유리하다. 전자의 신빙성은 널리 알려진 사실에 의해 오래도록 떠받쳐지며, 공적 기록과 여러 저자의 일치하는 증언이 훗날의 시대에까지 그 진실성을 증언해 준다. 그리하여 트라야누스와 안토니누스, 네로와 칼리굴라가 모두 후세의 믿음을 얻었으니, 그토록 선한 사람들과 그토록 악한 사람들이 한때 인류의 주인이었음을 아무도 의심하지 않는다.\n\n그러나 사사로운 인물을 다루고, 가장 외진 구석을 뒤지며, 세상의 구멍과 모퉁이에서 미덕과 악덕의 본보기를 끄집어내는 우리는 한층 위태로운 처지에 놓인다. 우리에게는 널리 알려진 명성도, 일치하는 증언도, 우리가 전하는 바를 떠받치고 입증해 줄 기록도 없으니, 우리는 가능성의 한계뿐 아니라 개연성의 한계 안에도 머물러야 마땅하다. 특히 크게 선하고 사랑스러운 것을 그려 낼 때는 더욱 그러하다. 악행과 어리석음은 아무리 터무니없어도 한결 쉽게 수긍을 얻는다. 심술궂은 본성이 믿음에 큰 버팀목과 힘을 보태 주기 때문이다.\n\n그리하여 우리는 어쩌면 별 위험 없이 피셔의 이야기를 들려줄 수 있을 것이다. 그는 오랫동안 더비 씨의 관대함에 빌붙어 빵을 얻어먹어 왔고, 어느 날 아침 그의 손에서 적잖은 후한 선물을 받았으면서도, 친구의 서랍장에 남은 것을 제 것으로 삼으려고 템플에 있는 한 공용 사무실에 몸을 숨겼다. 그곳을 통하면 더비 씨의 방으로 통하는 통로가 있었던 것이다. 그는 여기서 여러 시간 동안 더비 씨가 그날 저녁 친구들에게 베푼 연회—피셔도 초대받았던 그 연회—에서 즐거워하는 소리를 엿들었다. 그 내내 그의 목적을 가로막을 어떤 다정한 생각도, 어떤 고마운 회상도 떠오르지 않았다. 그러나 가엾은 신사가 손님들을 그 사무실을 통해 배웅하고 나자, 피셔는 숨어 있던 곳에서 불쑥 나와, 친구의 뒤를 따라 살금살금 그의 방으로 걸어 들어가서는, 그의 머리에 권총 탄환을 발사했다. 이런 일은 피셔의 뼈가 그의 심장만큼이나 썩어 없어졌을 무렵에야 믿어질 만한 것이다. 아니, 어쩌면 이런 것도 믿어질 것이다. 그 악당이 이틀 뒤에 몇몇 젊은 숙녀들과 함께 《햄릿》 연극을 보러 갔고, 그 숙녀들 중 하나가—자기가 그 장본인 곁에 얼마나 가까이 있는지는 꿈에도 모른 채—\"맙소사! 더비 씨를 죽인 그 사람이 지금 여기 있다면!\" 하고 외치는 것을 낯빛 하나 변치 않고 들었다는 것이다. 이로써 그는 네로보다도 더 무뎌지고 굳어 버린 양심을 드러냈으니, 네로에 관해서는 수에토니우스가 이렇게 전한다. \"어머니가 죽은 뒤 자기 죄에 대한 의식이 곧바로 견딜 수 없게 되었고, 그대로 계속되었다. 군대와 원로원과 백성의 온갖 축하도 그의 양심의 공포를 가라앉히지 못했다.\"\n\n그러나 이제 반대로, 만일 내가 독자에게 이런 사람을 알고 있다고 말한다면 어떨까. 그의 통찰력 있는 천재성은, 아무런 출발선도 그어져 있지 않은 곳에서 막대한 재산을 일구도록 그를 이끌었으며, 그는 그 일을 자신의 정직함을 더없이 완벽하게 지켜 가며 해냈고, 어느 한 개인에게도 티끌만 한 부당함이나 해를 끼치지 않았을 뿐 아니라 오히려 상업에 더없는 이익을 안기고 공공 세입을 막대하게 늘렸다. 또한 그는 이 재산에서 나오는 수입의 한 부분을, 더없이 높은 품위가 더없이 순수한 소박함과 어우러진 작품들을 통해 대다수 사람들보다 뛰어난 안목을 드러내는 데 썼고, 또 다른 한 부분은, 오로지 그 가치나 궁핍만이 추천장이 되는 대상들에게 자선을 베풂으로써 모든 사람보다 뛰어난 선함을 펼쳐 보이는 데 썼다. 그는 곤경에 빠진 가치를 찾아내는 데 더없이 부지런했고, 그것을 구제하는 데 더없이 열심이었으며, 그러고 나서는 자기가 한 일을 감추는 데 (어쩌면 지나치다 싶을 만큼) 조심스러웠다. 그의 집, 그의 가구, 그의 정원, 그의 식탁, 그의 사사로운 환대와 공적인 선행, 이 모든 것이 그것들이 흘러나온 마음을 드러냈으며, 번지르르한 겉치레나 바깥으로의 과시 없이도 하나같이 본질에서 풍요롭고 고귀했다. 그는 인생의 모든 관계를 더없이 합당한 미덕으로 채웠다. 그는 창조주를 향해 더없이 경건하게 신앙심이 깊었고, 군주를 향해 더없이 열렬하게 충성스러웠으며, 아내에게는 더없이 다정한 남편이요, 친절한 친족이며, 후한 후원자요, 따뜻하고 굳건한 벗이며, 식견 있고 유쾌한 동무이고, 하인들에게는 너그럽고, 이웃에게는 후하며, 가난한 이에게는 자비롭고, 온 인류에게 자애로웠다. 여기에 더해 내가 현명하고, 용감하고, 우아하다는, 아니 실로 우리말의 다른 온갖 사랑스러운 수식어를 다 붙인다 한들, 나는 분명 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없으리라.\n\n누가 믿겠는가? 맹세코 아무도! 아무도 없으리니, 혹 둘이거나, 아니면 아무도 없으리.\n\n그럼에도 나는 여기 묘사한 그 모든 것을 갖춘 사람을 한 명 알고 있다. 하지만 단 하나의 사례(실은 그와 같은 다른 사례를 나는 정말로 알지 못한다)는, 그 사람도 그 비슷한 무엇도 들어 본 적 없는 수천 명을 향해 글을 쓰는 우리를 정당화하기에는 충분하지 않다. 그러한 진귀한 새는 묘비명 작가에게나, 혹은 그를 이행시 한 짝에 슬쩍 끼워 넣거나 무심하고 소홀한 척하며 운율 속에 살그머니 밀어 넣어 독자에게 아무런 거슬림도 주지 않을 만한 어떤 시인에게나 넘겨 버려야 마땅하다.\n\n마지막으로, 행동이란 인간의 능력 범위 안에 있고 인간 행위자가 능히 할 법한 것이어야 할 뿐 아니라, 바로 그 행위자나 인물 자신이 행했음 직한 것이어야 한다. 한 사람에게는 그저 놀랍고 경이로울 뿐인 일이, 다른 사람에 관해 이야기될 때는 개연성이 없거나 실로 불가능한 일이 될 수도 있기 때문이다.\n\n이 마지막 요건이 바로 극 비평가들이 인물의 일관성이라 부르는 것인데, 이는 매우 비범한 정도의 판단력과 인간 본성에 대한 더없이 정확한 지식을 요구한다.\n\n어느 더없이 빼어난 작가가 감탄스럽게 지적한 바, 빠른 물살이 제 흐름을 거슬러 배를 실어 갈 수 없듯, 열정도 사람을 제 자신과 정면으로 어긋나게 행동하도록 몰아갈 수 없다. 나는 감히 말하건대, 사람이 제 본성의 명령과 정면으로 모순되게 행동한다는 것은, 불가능하지는 않더라도, 능히 떠올릴 수 있는 그 무엇 못지않게 개연성 없고 기적 같은 일이다. 만일 안토니누스의 이야기에서 가장 훌륭한 부분이 네로에게 돌려진다거나, 네로의 삶에서 가장 고약한 사건들이 안토니누스에게 씌워진다면, 그 어느 쪽인들 믿음에 이보다 더 충격적인 것이 있겠는가? 반면 이 둘이 각기 제 마땅한 행위자에 관해 이야기될 때, 그것이야말로 참된 경이로움을 이룬다.\n\n우리 현대 희극 작가들은 여기서 넌지시 일러둔 오류에 거의 너 나 할 것 없이 빠져들었다. 그들의 주인공은 처음 네 막 동안 대개 소문난 악당이고, 그들의 여주인공은 막돼먹은 계집이다. 그러나 다섯째 막에 이르면 전자는 매우 훌륭한 신사가 되고, 후자는 미덕과 분별을 갖춘 여인이 된다. 게다가 작가는 이 괴이한 변화와 모순을 짜 맞추거나 설명하려고 조금이라도 수고를 들일 만큼 친절한 경우가 드물다. 실로 거기에는 연극이 결말로 치닫고 있다는 것 말고는 달리 댈 만한 이유가 없으니, 마치 악당이 연극의 마지막 막에서 뉘우치는 것이 제 삶의 마지막 순간에 뉘우치는 것 못지않게 자연스럽기라도 한 듯하다. 우리는 그런 일이 타이번에서 흔히 벌어짐을 보거니와, 그곳이야말로 실로 어떤 희극의 장면을 매우 어울리게 닫을 만한 자리이다. 이런 희극의 주인공들은 사람을 교수대로 끌고 갈 뿐 아니라 막상 거기 섰을 때 영웅적인 모습을 꾸며 내게도 해 주는 바로 그 재능으로 가장 흔히 이름을 떨치기 때문이다.\n\n이 몇 가지 제약 안에서라면, 내 생각에 어떤 작가든 제 마음껏 경이로움을 다루도록 허락받을 수 있다. 아니, 이렇게 신빙성의 규칙 안에 머문다면, 독자를 놀라게 하면 할수록 그만큼 더 독자의 주의를 사로잡고, 그만큼 더 독자를 매혹할 것이다. 더없이 높은 반열의 한 천재가 《바토스》 제5장에서 지적했듯, \"모든 시의 위대한 기술은 진실을 허구와 섞어, 믿을 만한 것을 놀라운 것과 결합하는 데 있다.\"\n\n무릇 훌륭한 작가라면 누구나 개연성의 한계 안에 자신을 가두겠지만, 그렇다고 그의 인물이나 사건이 진부하거나 흔하거나 천박해야 할 까닭은 결코 없다. 거리마다, 집집마다 벌어지는 일이라든가 신문의 국내 단신에서 마주칠 법한 일 같은 것 말이다. 또한 그가 독자 대다수의 식견 안에 한 번도 들어온 적 없을 법한 숱한 인물과 사물을 보여 주는 일을 금해서도 안 된다. 작가가 앞서 말한 규칙들을 엄격히 지킨다면, 그는 제 몫을 다한 것이며, 그렇다면 독자로부터 어느 정도의 믿음을 받을 자격이 있다. 그를 못 믿겠다고 한다면, 독자야말로 실로 비평적 불신의 죄를 저지르는 셈이다.\n\n이런 믿음이 얼마간 모자란 탓에, 나는 어느 지체 높은 젊은 숙녀의 인물이 무대에서 부자연스럽다는 이유로, 사무원과 도제들이 잔뜩 모인 자리에서 만장일치의 목소리로 비난받았던 일을 떠올린다. 그 인물은 앞서 일류 가문의 여러 숙녀에게서 찬성표를 받은 터였고, 그중 식견으로 매우 이름난 한 분은 그것이 자기가 아는 젊은이의 절반쯤을 그려 낸 초상이라고 단언했는데도 말이다.\n\n주막 안주인이 존스를 찾아오다.\n\n존스는 친구인 중위와 작별한 뒤 눈을 붙여 보려 애썼지만 모두 헛수고였다. 마음이 너무 또렷하고 말짱해서 도무지 잠으로 달랠 수가 없었다. 그렇게 날이 훤히 밝을 때까지 소피아 생각으로 스스로를 달래었다기보다는 차라리 괴롭히고 나서, 그는 차를 좀 가져다 달라고 청했다. 바로 그 기회에 안주인이 친히 그를 찾아오는 영광을 베풀었다.\n\n사실 안주인이 그를 본 것은, 아니 적어도 그에게 조금이라도 관심을 둔 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그러나 중위가 그를 두고 틀림없이 어느 귀한 집안의 젊은 신사라고 장담해 두었던 터라, 그녀는 이제 자기가 할 수 있는 모든 예우를 그에게 베풀기로 작정했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이곳은 광고 문구를 빌려 표현하자면 신사들이 제 돈값만큼 정중한 대접을 받는 그런 집들 가운데 하나였으니까.\n\n그녀는 그에게 차를 우려내기 시작하자마자 이야기도 함께 풀어놓기 시작했다. \"어머나! 나리,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이렇게 잘생긴 젊은 신사분이 저런 군인 나부랭이들이랑 어울려 다니느라 스스로를 그리 낮추시다니, 참으로 안타까운 일이지 뭐예요. 그자들이야 제 입으로 신사라 떠들고 다니겠지만, 우리 첫 남편이 늘 하던 말마따나, 그자들 봉급을 대는 게 바로 우리라는 걸 잊지 말아야지요. 게다가 우리 같은 술장수들은 그자들한테 돈을 치를 뿐 아니라 먹여 재우기까지 해야 하니, 정말이지 죽을 노릇이에요. 어젯밤만 해도 장교들 말고도 스무 명이나 받았다니까요. 아니, 그런 걸로 치자면 차라리 장교보다 사병들이 나아요. 그 멋쟁이 양반들은 뭘 갖다 줘도 성에 차는 법이 없거든요. 그 계산서들을 보셨다면, 어머나! 나리, 정말 가관이라니까요. 장담컨대 점잖은 시골 지주 식구들을 치를 때가 훨씬 수월하답니다. 그런 분들은 말 값 말고도 하룻밤에 사십, 오십 실링은 너끈히 내고 가시거든요. 그런데도 장담하건대, 저 장교 나부랭이들은 하나같이 제 깐엔 연 오백 파운드짜리 지주 어른 못지않다고 여긴다니까요. 그자들 졸병들이 졸졸 따라다니면서 '나리, 나리' 하고 외쳐대는 꼴을 보면 정말 속이 다 후련해요. 거참, 나리 좋아하시네, 머리 하나에 한 실링짜리 정식이나 처먹는 주제에 말이죠. 게다가 그자들끼리 욕지거리를 어찌나 해대는지, 정말이지 무서워서 정신이 다 나갈 지경이에요. 저런 못된 인간들한테 좋은 일이 생길 리가 있나 싶다니까요. 그런데 그 가운데 하나가 나리한테 그토록 야만스럽게 굴었으니. 나머지 작자들이 그놈을 어떻게든 빼돌릴 거라고 진작에 짐작했지요. 그자들은 죄다 한통속이거든요. 나리께서 목숨이 위태로우셨더라도—다행히 그렇지 않으셔서 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라요—저런 못된 인간들한테야 다 매한가지였을 거예요. 살인자를 그냥 놓아주었겠지요. 하느님, 저들에게 자비를 베푸소서. 저라면 온 세상을 다 준대도 그런 죄를 짊어지진 않겠어요. 하지만 나리께서는 다행히도 신의 가호로 회복하실 듯하니, 그놈한테는 아직 법이 남아 있답니다. 스몰 변호사를 쓰시면, 장담컨대 그 작자를 이 나라에서 도망치게 만들어 줄 거예요. 하긴 어쩌면 그놈은 벌써 줄행랑을 쳤을지도 모르지요. 그런 부류는 오늘 여기 있다가도 내일이면 사라지는 법이니까요. 하지만 앞으로는 부디 더 분별을 차리셔서 친지분들 곁으로 돌아가시길 바라요. 장담컨대 나리를 잃고 다들 애가 타고 계실 거예요. 무슨 일이 있었는지 그분들이 아시기라도 하면—어머나, 세상에! 그분들이 아시는 건 정말이지 천만의 말씀이에요. 자, 자, 우리도 사정이야 다 알지요. 하지만 한 사람이 마다하면 또 다른 사람이 있는 법, 이렇게 잘생긴 신사분이야 여인이 모자랄 일이 결코 없으시지요. 정말이지 제가 나리라면, 어떤 여자 때문에 군인이 되느니 차라리 세상에서 제일 고운 여자가 목매달리는 꼴을 보고 말겠어요. 아니, 그렇게 얼굴 붉히지 마세요\"(실제로 그는 격하게 얼굴을 붉혔던 것이다). \"어머, 나리는 제가 그 소피아 아씨 일을 아무것도 모르는 줄 아셨나 본데, 장담컨대 다 알고 있다니까요.\" \"뭐라고요,\" 존스가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"당신이 내 소피아를 안다고요?\" \"알다마다요! 아믄요,\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"그 아씨가 이 집에 묵으신 게 한두 번이 아닌걸요.\" \"이모님하고 함께였겠지요,\" 존스가 말했다. \"거 봐요, 바로 그거예요,\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"그래요, 그래요, 그 노부인도 제가 아주 잘 알지요. 그리고 소피아 아씨는 참으로 사랑스러운 젊은 분이세요, 그건 정말이에요.\" \"사랑스러운 분이고말고요,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"오, 하늘이여!\"\n\n천사들도 그녀를 닮으라고 곱게 그려졌도다. 그녀 안에는 우리가 천국이라 믿는 모든 것이 깃들어 있으니, 눈부신 광채와 순결함과 진실함, 영원한 기쁨과 끝없는 사랑이로다.\n\n\"당신이 내 소피아를 알고 있었으리라곤 정말 상상도 못 했어요!\" \"저는요,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"나리가 그 절반만큼이라도 그 아씨를 아셨으면 좋겠네요. 그 아씨 침상 곁에 앉아 보셨다면 무엇인들 내놓지 않으셨겠어요? 그 목덜미가 얼마나 고운지! 그 어여쁜 팔다리가 바로 지금 나리께서 누워 계신 그 침대에 길게 뉘어졌었다니까요.\" \"여기요!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"소피아가 여기 누운 적이 있다고요?\" \"그럼요, 그럼요, 바로 여기요. 거기, 바로 그 침대에요,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"지금 이 순간 나리께서 그 아씨를 거기 모셨으면 얼마나 좋을까요. 그리고 그 아씨도 어쩌면 그러길 바라실지 누가 알겠어요. 제가 아는 바로는 그렇지 않다 할 까닭이 없거든요. 그 아씨가 제게 나리 이름을 말씀하셨으니 말이에요.\" \"아!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그녀가 정말로 가엾은 이 존스를 입에 올렸단 말이오? 지금 나를 추켜세우는 거지요. 도저히 그렇게까지는 믿을 수가 없소.\" \"그럼요, 그러니까요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"제가 구원받기를 바라는 만큼 진실인데요, 제가 진실보다 한 마디라도 더 보탰다면 악마가 저를 잡아가도 좋아요. 그 아씨가 존스 나리 얘기를 하시는 걸 제가 들었답니다. 하지만 점잖고 정숙하게 말씀하신 거예요, 그건 인정해요. 그래도 입 밖에 낸 것보다 훨씬 많은 생각을 품고 계신 걸 저는 알아챌 수 있었지요.\" \"오, 친절한 분이여!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그녀가 나를 어떻게 생각하든 나는 결코 그럴 자격이 없는 사람이오. 오, 그녀는 온통 부드러움이요, 다정함이요, 선함 그 자체인데! 어찌하여 나 같은 망나니가 태어나, 그 보드라운 가슴을 단 한 순간이라도 불편하게 한단 말이오? 어찌하여 나는 저주받은 몸인가? 그녀에게 작은 행복 하나라도 가져다줄 수만 있다면, 어떤 악마가 인류를 위해 고안한 온갖 재앙과 고통이라도 기꺼이 견딜 이 몸인데. 아니, 그녀가 행복하다는 것만 알 수 있다면 고문조차 내게는 고통이 아닐 텐데.\" \"거 봐요, 보세요,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"제가 그 아씨한테 나리는 한결같은 연인이라고 했다니까요.\" \"한데 부인, 부디 말해 주시오, 당신이 나에 관해 무엇을 알게 된 게 언제, 어디서요? 나는 여기 와 본 적이 한 번도 없고, 당신을 본 기억도 없으니 말이오.\" \"그러실 만도 하지요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"제가 지주 어른 댁에서 나리를 무릎에 안고 있을 때 나리는 코딱지만 한 어린애였으니까요.\" \"뭐라고요, 지주 댁이라니요?\" 존스가 말했다. \"아니, 그럼 그 위대하고 훌륭하신 올워디 어른을 아신단 말이오?\" \"그럼요, 아믄요, 알다마다요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 고장에서 그분을 모르는 사람이 어디 있겠어요?\" \"그분의 선하심에 대한 명성이야,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"분명 여기보다 훨씬 멀리까지 뻗어 나갔을 거요. 하지만 오직 하늘만이 그분을 알 수 있소—하늘이 제 자신에게서 본떠 와 그 본보기로 삼아 땅으로 내려보낸 그 자애로움을 알 수 있는 건 오직 하늘뿐이오. 인류는 그런 신성한 선함을 누릴 자격이 없는 만큼이나 그것을 알지도 못하오. 하지만 그 누구도 나만큼 그럴 자격이 없는 사람은 없을 거요. 그분 손에 그토록 높이 들어 올려진 나, 당신도 잘 알다시피 가엾은 사생아로 거두어져 그분의 양자가 되고 친아들처럼 길러진 이 몸이, 감히 내 어리석음으로 그분의 노여움을 사고 그 진노를 내게 끌어들였으니. 그렇소, 나는 그 모든 걸 받아 마땅하오. 나는 그분이 내게 부당한 짓을 하셨다고는 결코 배은망덕하게 생각하지 않을 거니까. 아니오, 나는 지금 이 꼴처럼 문밖으로 내쫓겨 마땅한 사람이오. 그러니 이제, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 군인이 되기로 한 걸 탓하지는 않으시겠지요. 더구나 내 주머니에 이만한 재산이 들었으니 말이오.\" 그 말과 함께 그는 지갑을 흔들어 보였는데, 그 안에는 아주 적은 돈밖에 들어 있지 않았고, 안주인 눈에는 그보다도 더 적어 보였다.\n\n마음씨 좋은 안주인은 이 이야기를 듣고 (속된 표현을 빌리자면) 한 방에 폭삭 주저앉고 말았다. 그녀는 쌀쌀맞게 대답했다. \"뭐, 자기 형편에 뭐가 가장 알맞은지는 본인이 제일 잘 아는 법이지요. 그런데 어머,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"누가 부르는 소리가 들리는 것 같네요. 가요! 가요! 우리 집 사람들은 죄다 귀신이 씌었나, 아무도 귀가 없다니까. 저는 아래층으로 내려가 봐야겠어요. 아침을 더 드시려거든 하녀를 올려보낼게요. 가요!\" 그 말과 함께 그녀는 작별 인사도 없이 방을 휙 나가 버렸다. 신분이 낮은 부류의 사람들은 대접받는 일에 무척 까다로운 법이라, 지체 높은 분들에게는 기꺼이 공짜로 예우를 바치면서도 제 또래의 사람들에게는 그 수고에 대한 값을 단단히 받아 챙기기 전에는 결코 예우를 베푸는 법이 없으니 말이다.\n\n외과의가 두 번째로 등장하는 장.\n\n더 나아가기 전에, 독자가 여관 안주인이 실제 아는 것보다 더 많이 안다고 착각하거나, 혹은 그녀가 그렇게나 많이 안다는 데 놀라는 일이 없도록, 다음 사실을 일러두는 편이 좋겠다. 중위가 그녀에게 소피아라는 이름이 그 싸움의 발단이었음을 알려 주었던 것이다. 그리고 나머지 그녀가 아는 바에 대해서는, 눈치 빠른 독자라면 그녀가 앞 장면에서 그것을 어떻게 알아냈는지 알아차렸을 것이다. 실로 그녀의 미덕에는 큰 호기심이 뒤섞여 있었다. 그녀는 누구든 자기 집을 떠나려 할 때, 그 이름과 가문과 재산에 대해 될 수 있는 대로 캐묻지 않고는 결코 순순히 보내지 않았다.\n\n그녀가 나가자마자 존스는 그녀의 행동을 따져 보기는커녕, 자신이 누워 있는 이 침대가 바로 사랑하는 소피아를 품었었다고 들은 그 침대임을 떠올렸다. 이 생각은 천 가지 애틋하고 다정한 상념을 불러일으켰는데, 우리가 여기에 더 오래 머물고 싶기는 하나, 이런 부류의 연인들이 우리 독자 가운데 지극히 작은 일부일 뿐임을 헤아리지 않을 수 없다. 외과의가 상처를 치료하러 왔을 때 존스는 이런 상태에 있었다. 의사는 진찰을 해 보고 그의 맥이 흐트러져 있음을 알아채고, 또 그가 잠을 자지 못했다는 말을 듣고는, 그가 큰 위험에 처해 있다고 단언했다. 그는 열이 오르려 한다고 보았고, 사혈로 그것을 막으려 했으나, 존스는 응하지 않으며 더는 피를 흘리지 않겠다고 잘라 말했다. \"그리고 선생님,\" 그가 말했다. \"친절을 베푸셔서 제 머리만 싸매 주신다면, 하루 이틀이면 나으리라는 데에 의심이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"제가,\" 외과의가 대답했다. \"한두 달 안에 나으시리라 장담할 수 있다면 좋겠군요. 나아요, 그것 참! 아니, 아니지요. 이런 타박상은 그리 빨리 낫는 게 아닙니다. 그런데 선생, 나는 이 나이가 되도록 환자에게서 내 시술법을 배우는 처지는 아니올시다. 그러니 치료에 앞서 반드시 반동 요법(피를 다른 곳으로 돌리는 처치)을 행하겠소.\"\n\n존스가 한사코 거절을 고집하자, 의사는 마침내 물러섰다. 다만 그러면서도 그 나쁜 결과에 대해서는 책임지지 않겠노라고, 자신이 그와 반대되는 조언을 했음을 환자가 정당하게 인정해 주기를 바란다고 일러두었다. 환자는 그리하겠노라 약속했다.\n\n의사는 부엌으로 물러나 안주인에게 다가가, 열이 있는데도 사혈을 받지 않으려는 환자의 고분고분하지 못한 태도를 쓴소리로 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"그렇다면 먹는 열인가 보군요.\" 안주인이 말했다. \"오늘 아침 식사로 버터 바른 두툼한 토스트를 두 장이나 먹어 치웠으니까요.\"\n\n\"그럴 법하지요.\" 의사가 말했다. \"열이 났는데도 먹는 사람을 본 적이 있소. 그건 아주 쉽게 설명이 됩니다. 열을 일으키는 물질에서 생긴 산기(酸氣)가 횡격막의 신경을 자극하여, 그로 인해 타고난 식욕과 좀처럼 구별되지 않는 갈망이 생기는 것이지요. 그러나 그 음식은 엉기지도 않고 유미(乳糜, 소화된 영양액)로 흡수되지도 않으니, 결국 혈관의 입구를 갉아 들어가 열병의 증세를 더 악화시킬 것이오. 정말이지 저 신사는 매우 위태로운 지경에 있다고 봅니다. 사혈을 받지 않으면 죽지 않을까 걱정스럽소.\"\n\n\"사람은 누구나 언젠가 한 번은 죽게 마련이지요.\" 마음씨 좋은 여인이 대답했다. \"제 알 바 아니랍니다. 선생님, 설마 사혈하시는 동안 제가 저이를 붙들고 있길 바라시는 건 아니겠지요. 그런데 말이에요, 귀에 한마디 넣어 드릴게요. 너무 깊이 손을 대시기 전에, 누가 선생님께 값을 치를 사람인지부터 챙기시라고 권하고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"값을 치를 사람이라!\" 의사가 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했다. \"아니, 내 손 안에 신사 한 분이 있지 않소, 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"저도 선생님처럼 그리 여겼지요.\" 안주인이 말했다. \"하지만, 제 첫 남편이 늘 하던 말마따나, 모든 것이 겉보기 그대로는 아니랍니다. 정말이지 저이는 영락없는 거렁뱅이예요. 그래도 제가 이 일을 입에 올렸다는 티는 내지 마세요. 다만 장사하는 사람들끼리는 이런 일을 서로 알려 주는 게 으레 도리라고 생각해서요.\"\n\n\"그래서 내가 이런 작자한테,\" 의사가 발끈하여 외쳤다. \"가르침을 받았단 말이오? 값도 치르지 않을 자에게 내 의술이 모욕당하는 꼴을 들어야 한단 말이오? 때맞춰 이 사실을 알게 되어 다행이군. 이제 저이가 사혈을 받나 안 받나 두고 봅시다.\" 그러고는 곧장 위층으로 올라가 방문을 거칠게 열어젖혀, 막 깊은 단잠에 빠져 있던 가엾은 존스를 깨우고 말았다. 게다가 더 고약하게도, 소피아에 관한 달콤한 꿈에서 그를 끌어낸 것이었다.\n\n\"사혈을 받겠소, 안 받겠소?\" 의사가 노발대발하며 외쳤다. \"이미 제 결심을 말씀드렸습니다.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"제 대답을 받아들여 주셨더라면 진심으로 좋았을 텐데요. 제 평생 가장 단꿈 같은 잠에서 저를 깨우셨으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"옳지, 옳지.\" 의사가 외쳤다. \"많은 이들이 그렇게 잠에 빠져 목숨을 흘려보냈소. 잠이라고 늘 좋은 게 아니지, 음식과 마찬가지로 말이오. 하지만 기억하시오, 마지막으로 다시 묻겠소. 사혈을 받겠소?\" \"마지막으로 답하겠습니다.\" 존스가 말했다. \"받지 않겠습니다.\" \"그렇다면 나는 당신한테서 손을 떼겠소.\" 의사가 외쳤다. \"그리고 이미 내가 들인 수고에 대한 값을 치러 주시오. 왕진 두 차례에 한 번에 5실링씩, 치료 두 차례에 또 5실링씩, 그리고 사혈에 반 크라운이오.\" \"설마,\" 존스가 말했다. \"저를 이런 상태로 내버려 두실 작정은 아니겠지요.\" \"아니, 그럴 작정이올시다.\" 상대가 말했다. \"그렇다면,\" 존스가 말했다. \"당신은 저를 악당처럼 다룬 셈이니, 한 푼도 치르지 않겠습니다.\" \"아주 좋소.\" 의사가 외쳤다. \"맨 처음 손해가 제일 나은 손해라더니. 대체 우리 안주인은 무슨 심사로 이런 부랑배들한테 나를 불러댔단 말인가!\" 이 말을 내뱉으며 그는 방을 박차고 나갔고, 환자는 몸을 돌려 누워 이내 잠을 되찾았다. 그러나 그의 꿈은 불행히도 사라지고 없었다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n바그다드의 이발사도, 돈키호테에 나오는 그 이발사도 따라올 수 없는, 역사에 기록된 가장 유쾌한 이발사 한 사람을 소개하는 이야기.\n\n시계가 막 다섯 시를 알렸을 때 존스는 일곱 시간의 단잠에서 깨어났다. 어찌나 개운하고 건강과 기운이 완전히 돌아왔던지, 그는 자리에서 일어나 옷을 차려입기로 마음먹었다. 그 채비로 그는 여행 가방을 열어 깨끗한 속옷 한 벌과 옷 한 벌을 꺼냈다. 그러나 먼저 겉옷을 걸치고 부엌으로 내려갔는데, 뱃속에서 일어나기 시작한 어떤 소란을 가라앉혀 줄 무언가를 주문하기 위해서였다.\n\n여주인을 마주친 그는 무척 정중하게 인사를 건네며 \"저녁으로 무엇을 먹을 수 있겠느냐\"고 물었다. \"저녁이라뇨!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저녁을 생각하시기엔 참 어중간한 시간이네요. 집에 차려 놓은 음식이라곤 하나도 없고, 불도 거의 다 꺼졌는걸요.\" \"그래도,\" 그가 말했다. \"뭐든 먹을 게 있어야겠소. 무엇이든 거의 상관없으니 말이오. 솔직히 말하자면, 평생 이렇게 배고팠던 적이 없거든.\" \"그러시다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"차게 식힌 소 엉덩잇살 한 점과 당근이 있는데, 손님께 맞으실 것 같네요.\" \"더할 나위 없소.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"다만 그것을 기름에 좀 구워 주신다면 고맙겠소.\" 여주인은 그러마 하고, 미소 지으며 \"이렇게 건강을 잘 회복하신 걸 보니 기쁘다\"고 말했다. 우리 주인공의 다정한 성품은 거의 거역할 수 없는 것이었기 때문이다. 게다가 그녀는 본바탕이 결코 심술궂은 여인은 아니었다. 다만 돈을 어찌나 사랑했던지, 가난의 기미가 비치는 것이라면 무엇이든 미워했을 따름이다.\n\n이제 존스는 저녁이 준비되는 동안 옷을 차려입으려고 돌아갔고, 자신이 시킨 대로 이발사의 시중을 받았다.\n\n이 이발사는 꼬마 벤저민이라는 이름으로 통했는데, 별나고 익살스럽기 그지없는 사내였다. 그 성미 탓에 그는 종종 자잘한 곤욕을 치르곤 했으니, 이를테면 따귀를 맞거나, 엉덩이를 걷어차이거나, 뼈가 부러지거나 하는 식이었다. 누구나 농담을 알아듣는 것은 아니며, 알아듣는 이들조차 정작 자신이 농담의 표적이 되는 것은 못마땅해하는 법이니까. 그러나 이 버릇은 그에게 고칠 수 없는 것이었다. 그 때문에 여러 번 호되게 당하고도, 일단 농담거리가 떠오르면 그는 상대가 누구든, 때가 언제든, 장소가 어디든 조금도 가리지 않고 기어이 그것을 입 밖에 내고야 말았다.\n\n그의 성격에는 이 밖에도 별난 구석이 무척 많았는데, 그것들은 따로 언급하지 않겠다. 독자가 이 비범한 인물과 좀 더 친해지다 보면 스스로 아주 쉽게 알아차릴 테니 말이다.\n\n쉽게 짐작이 갈 만한 이유로 어서 옷을 입고 싶어 안달이 난 존스는, 이 면도꾼이 비누 거품을 푸는 데 너무 굼뜨다고 여겨 좀 서둘러 달라고 청했다. 그러자 상대는 무슨 일이 있어도 결코 표정을 흩뜨리는 법이 없었으므로, 자못 진지하게 대답했다. \"'천천히 서둘러라'(Festina lente)는, 제가 면도칼을 잡기 훨씬 전부터 배워 둔 격언입지요.\" \"이보게, 자네는 학식이 있는 사람이로군.\" 존스가 응수했다. \"변변찮습니다요.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"'우리 모두가 모든 것을 할 수 있는 것은 아니지요'(non omnia possumus omnes).\" \"또 나오는군!\" 존스가 말했다. \"자네는 시구 받아 잇기에 능한 모양이야.\" \"용서하십시오, 나리.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"'저는 그만한 영예를 누릴 자격이 못 됩니다'(non tanto me dignor honore).\" 그러고는 일을 계속하면서 그가 말했다. \"나리, 제가 비누 거품을 다루기 시작한 이래로, 면도를 하는 까닭이라곤 두 가지밖에는 도무지 찾아낼 수가 없더군요. 하나는 수염을 기르려는 것이고, 다른 하나는 수염을 없애려는 것입지요. 제 짐작으로는, 나리께서 앞쪽 까닭으로 면도를 하신 지가 그리 오래되지 않은 듯합니다. 정말이지 그 결과가 아주 좋으십니다. 나리의 수염을 두고 이렇게 말할 만하니까요. '깎는 자에게 꽤나 묵직하다'(tondenti gravior)고요.\" \"내 짐작으로는,\" 존스가 말했다. \"자네는 아주 익살맞은 친구로군.\" \"크게 잘못 보셨습니다, 나리.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"저는 철학 공부에 너무 빠진 사람입니다. '여기서 그 눈물이 나오는 것이지요'(hinc illae lacrymae), 나리. 그것이 제 불운입니다. 지나친 학식이 저를 망쳤지요.\" \"이런,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이보게, 자네가 그 직업에 흔히 따르는 것보다 훨씬 많은 학식을 지녔다는 건 인정하겠네. 하지만 그것이 어떻게 자네를 망쳤다는 건지는 모르겠군.\" \"아아! 나리.\" 면도꾼이 대답했다. \"제 아버지는 그 때문에 저를 상속에서 내치셨답니다. 아버지는 무용 선생이셨는데, 제가 춤을 추기도 전에 글을 읽을 줄 알았다는 이유로 저를 미워하셔서, 한 푼도 남김없이 다른 자식들에게 다 물려주셨지요. 관자놀이 쪽도 손봐 드릴깝쇼ㅡ아이고! 죄송합니다, 아무래도 '원고에 빈틈이 있는'(hiatus in manuscriptis) 모양입니다. 나리께서 전쟁터로 가신다는 말을 들었습니다만, 잘못 안 것 같군요.\" \"어째서 그렇게 단정하나?\" 존스가 물었다. \"분명히, 나리.\" 이발사가 대답했다. \"나리께선 깨진 머리를 그곳까지 들고 가실 만큼 어리석은 분은 아니실 테니까요. 그건 '뉴캐슬에 석탄을 나르는' 격이 될 테니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"자네는 아주 별난 친구일세. 자네의 익살이 마음에 쏙 드는군. 저녁을 먹고 난 뒤에 내게 와서 나와 한잔 나눠 준다면 무척 기쁘겠네. 자네와 좀 더 가까워지고 싶구먼.\"\n\n\"오, 그러신다면 나리!\" 이발사가 말했다. \"받아 주시기만 한다면, 그보다 스무 배는 더 큰 호의를 베풀어 드릴 수 있습니다요.\" \"그게 무엇인가, 친구?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그게요, 원하신다면 제가 나리와 한 병을 비워 드리겠습니다. 저는 좋은 마음씨를 진정으로 사랑하거든요. 나리께서 저를 익살맞은 친구라고 알아보셨으니, 만일 나리께서 이 세상에서 가장 마음씨 고운 신사 가운데 한 분이 아니시라면, 제겐 관상 보는 재주가 영 없는 셈이겠지요.\" 이제 말끔히 차려입은 존스는 아래층으로 내려왔는데, 아마 저 아름다운 아도니스라도 그보다 더 사랑스러운 모습은 아니었을 것이다. 그런데도 우리 여주인에게는 그가 아무런 매력도 없었다. 그 마음씨 좋은 여인이 생김새에서 비너스를 조금도 닮지 않았듯이, 취향에서도 마찬가지였기 때문이다. 객실 하녀 내니가 제 안주인의 눈으로 그를 보았더라면 차라리 다행이었을 것을. 그 가엾은 처녀는 단 오 분 만에 존스에게 어찌나 격렬하게 빠져 버렸던지, 그 연정이 훗날 그녀에게 숱한 한숨을 치르게 했으니 말이다. 이 내니는 무척 예뻤고, 또 그만큼 도도했다. 술 따르는 사내며 동네의 젊은 농부 한둘을 마다한 적도 있었으니까. 하지만 우리 주인공의 빛나는 눈동자는 한순간에 그녀의 얼음을 죄다 녹여 버렸다.\n\n존스가 부엌으로 돌아왔을 때, 식탁보는 아직 깔려 있지 않았다. 사실 그럴 필요도 없었으니, 그의 저녁은 여전히 '있던 그대로'(in statu quo)였고, 그것을 익혀 줄 불 또한 마찬가지였다. 이런 낭패라면 어지간한 철학자의 기질도 발끈하게 만들 만했지만, 존스에게는 그런 효과가 없었다. 그는 다만 여주인에게 부드럽게 한마디 나무라며 말했다. \"데우기가 그렇게 어렵다면, 고기를 차게 그냥 먹겠소.\" 그러나 이번에는 그 마음씨 좋은 여인이, 측은한 마음에서였는지, 부끄러움에서였는지, 아니면 다른 어떤 까닭에서였는지 나로서는 알 수 없으나, 먼저 자신이 내린 적도 없는 명령을 어겼다며 하인들을 한바탕 호되게 꾸짖고는, 술 따르는 사내에게 '해의 방'에 냅킨을 깔라고 이른 뒤, 진심으로 일에 매달려 곧 그것을 해냈다.\n\n존스가 이제 안내받아 들어간 이 '해의 방'은 참으로 '빛이 들지 않는데도 숲이라 불리는'(lucus a non lucendo) 식의 이름이었다. 해라곤 거의 들여다본 적이 없는 방이었기 때문이다. 사실 그 집에서 가장 형편없는 방이었는데, 존스로서는 그게 차라리 다행이었다. 어쨌든 그는 지금 너무 배가 고팠던 터라 흠을 잡을 겨를이 없었다. 그러나 일단 허기를 채우고 나자, 그는 술 따르는 사내에게 포도주 한 병을 더 나은 방으로 가져오라 이르며, 자신을 토굴 같은 데로 안내한 것에 약간의 불만을 내비쳤다.\n\n술 따르는 사내가 그 분부를 따른 뒤, 얼마쯤 지나 이발사가 그의 시중을 들러 왔다. 사실 이발사는 존스를 이렇게 오래 기다리게 두지 않았을 테지만, 부엌에서 여주인의 이야기에 귀를 기울이느라 그리된 것이었다. 여주인은 자기 둘레에 사람들을 한 무리 그러모아 놓고 가엾은 존스의 내력을 들려주고 있었는데, 그 일부는 존스 본인의 입에서 캐낸 것이었고, 나머지는 그녀 자신이 기발하게 지어낸 것이었다. 그녀의 말인즉, \"그는 가엾은 교구 출신 소년인데, 올워디 영주님 댁에 들어가 거기서 도제로 길러지다가, 이제 못된 짓 때문에 쫓겨난 거라우. 특히 젊은 안주인께 연정을 품은 죄가 크고, 아마도 그 집을 턴 죄도 있을 거유. 안 그러면 저 사람이 가진 그 푼돈이 대체 어디서 나왔겠수. 그러니,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저게 바로 당신들이 말하는 그 신사 양반이라우!\" \"올워디 영주님 댁 하인이라고요!\" 이발사가 말했다. \"그자 이름이 뭐랍니까?\" \"글쎄, 제 입으로는 존스라고 합디다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어쩌면 가짜 이름으로 행세하는 걸지도 모르지요. 게다가 그러더군요, 영주님이 자길 친아들처럼 거두어 주셨는데, 이제 그분과 틀어졌다고 말이우.\" \"이름이 존스라면, 당신한테 사실대로 말한 거요.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"나도 그 고장에 사는 친척이 있는데, 사람들 중에는 그가 영주님의 아들이라고 말하는 이들도 있으니까요.\" \"그럼 어째서 아버지 이름을 쓰지 않는답니까?\" \"그건 나도 모르겠소.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"많은 사람들의 아들이 제 아버지 이름을 쓰지 않고 살지요.\" \"하긴,\" 여주인이 말했다. \"그가 비록 사생아라 해도 신사의 아들이라고 생각했더라면, 내가 그를 사뭇 다르게 대접했을 텐데. 그런 사생아들 중에 큰 인물이 되는 이가 한둘이 아니거든. 그리고 내 가엾은 첫 남편이 늘 하던 말마따나, 신사인 손님은 절대 함부로 대하면 안 되는 법이니까.\"\n\n존스 씨와 이발사가 나눈 대화.\n\n이 대화는 일부는 존스가 자신의 토굴 같은 방에서 저녁을 먹는 동안에, 또 일부는 그가 응접실에서 이발사를 기다리는 동안에 오갔다. 그리고 그 대화가 끝나자마자, 앞서 말했듯이 벤저민 씨가 그를 시중들러 왔고, 존스는 매우 친절하게 그에게 앉으라고 청했다. 그러고 나서 존스는 포도주 한 잔을 따라, \"도시시메 톤소룸(이발사 중에 가장 박식하신 분)\"이라는 호칭으로 그의 건강을 위해 잔을 비웠다. \"아고 티비 그라티아스, 도미네(감사드립니다, 나리).\" 이발사가 말했다. 그러고는 존스를 아주 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 마치 전에 본 적 있는 얼굴을 떠올리기라도 한 듯, 자못 진지하고도 놀란 기색으로 이렇게 말했다. \"나리, 외람되오나 혹 성함이 존스가 아니신지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\" 이에 상대는 \"그렇소\"라고 답했다. \"프로 데움 아트퀘 호미눔 피뎀(신과 인간의 신의를 두고)!\" 이발사가 말했다. \"참으로 묘하게도 일이 이렇게 풀리는군요! 존스 나리, 저는 나리의 더없이 순종하는 종입니다. 저를 못 알아보시는 듯한데, 하기야 당연한 일이지요. 단 한 번 저를 보셨을 뿐이고, 그때 나리께서는 무척 어리셨으니까요. 그래, 나리, 그 어지신 올워디 나리께서는 어떻게 지내십니까? 일레 옵티무스 옴니움 파트로누스(모든 후원자 가운데 가장 훌륭하신 그분)께서는 어떻게 지내시는지요?\" 존스가 말했다. \"보아하니 그대가 정말 나를 아는 모양이오. 하나 나는 그대를 떠올리는 그런 기쁨은 누리지 못하는구려.\" \"그건 이상할 게 없습니다.\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"다만 제가 나리를 더 빨리 알아보지 못한 게 놀라울 따름이지요. 조금도 변하지 않으셨으니까요. 그런데 나리, 결례가 안 된다면, 이 길로 어디를 가시는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\" \"잔이나 채우시오, 이발사 양반.\" 존스가 말했다. \"그리고 더는 묻지 마시오.\" \"아닙니다, 나리.\" 벤저민이 답했다. \"저는 성가시게 굴 생각이 없습니다. 또 부디 저를 무례하게 캐묻기나 하는 사람으로 여기지는 말아 주십시오. 그건 누구도 제게 씌울 수 없는 흠이니까요. 하지만 용서를 빕니다. 나리 같은 풍모의 신사분이 하인도 없이 여행을 하실 때면, 흔히 말하듯 인 카수 인코그니토(신분을 숨긴 채) 다니시는 것으로 짐작해야 마땅하니, 어쩌면 제가 나리의 성함을 입에 올리지 말았어야 했는지도 모르겠습니다.\" \"고백하건대,\" 존스가 말했다. \"내가 이 지방에서 이렇게까지 잘 알려져 있을 줄은 몰랐소. 그러나 특별한 사정이 있으니, 내가 이곳을 떠날 때까지 다른 누구에게도 내 이름을 말하지 않는다면 고맙겠소.\" \"파우카 베르바(말은 적게).\" 이발사가 답했다. \"그리고 저 말고는 이곳의 누구도 나리를 알지 못했으면 좋겠습니다. 입이 가벼운 사람들이 더러 있으니까요. 하지만 저는 비밀을 지킬 수 있다고 약속드립니다. 제 원수라도 그 미덕만큼은 제게 인정해 줄 것입니다.\" \"하나 그것이 그대 직업의 특징은 아니지 않소, 이발사 양반.\" 존스가 답했다. \"아아! 나리.\" 벤저민이 대꾸했다. \"논 시 말레 눙크 에트 올림 시크 에리트(지금 형편이 나쁘다고 늘 그러하지는 않으리라). 분명히 말씀드리지만, 저는 이발사로 태어나지도 길러지지도 않았습니다. 저는 대부분의 세월을 신사분들 사이에서 보냈고, 제 입으로 말하긴 뭣하지만, 점잖음이 무엇인지도 어느 정도 압니다. 만일 나리께서 다른 어떤 사람들에게 그러셨던 만큼 저를 신뢰할 만하다고 여기셨더라면, 제가 비밀을 더 잘 지킬 수 있는 사람임을 보여 드렸을 것입니다. 저라면 공개된 부엌에서 나리의 성함을 욕보이지는 않았을 테니까요. 실로, 나리, 어떤 사람들은 나리를 곱게 대하지 않았습니다. 나리와 올워디 나리 사이의 다툼에 관해 나리께서 말씀하신 것을 온 동네에 떠벌렸을 뿐 아니라, 거기에 제 멋대로 거짓말까지 보탰으니까요. 제가 거짓인 줄 아는 것들 말입니다.\" \"참으로 놀라운 말이오.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"맹세코, 나리.\" 벤저민이 답했다. \"저는 진실을 말씀드리는 것이며, 그게 제 안주인이었다는 사실은 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요. 그 이야기를 듣고 마음이 흔들린 건 분명하고, 부디 그게 다 거짓이기를 바랍니다. 저는 나리를 무척 존경하니까요, 정말로 그렇습니다. 그리고 나리께서 블랙 조지에게 베푸신 그 인정 어린 처사 이래로 줄곧 그래 왔습니다. 그 일은 온 동네에 회자되었고, 저도 그에 관한 편지를 한 통 넘게 받았습니다. 실로 그 일로 나리는 모두에게 사랑받게 되셨지요. 그러니 용서해 주십시오. 제가 이것저것 많이 여쭌 것은 제가 들은 일에 대한 진심 어린 걱정 때문이었습니다. 저에게는 무례하게 캐묻는 호기심 따위는 없습니다. 다만 저는 인정 많은 마음을 사랑하고, 그리하여 아모리스 아분단티아 에르가 테(나리를 향한 넘치는 애정)에 이르게 된 것이지요.\"\n\n우정의 고백이란 비참한 처지에 놓인 이에게는 쉽사리 믿음을 얻기 마련이다. 그러니 비참할 뿐 아니라 천성이 더없이 솔직하기까지 했던 존스가 벤저민의 모든 고백을 선뜻 믿고 그를 가슴으로 받아들인 것도 이상할 게 없다. 라틴어 토막들은, 비록 깊은 학식이 배어 있지는 않았으나 그중 더러는 벤저민이 제법 적절하게 가져다 쓴 터라, 평범한 이발사 이상의 무언가를 일러 주는 듯했다. 실로 그의 행동거지 전체가 그러했다. 그리하여 존스는 그의 출신과 교육에 관해 그가 한 말이 사실이라고 믿었다. 그리고 마침내, 여러 번 간청을 받은 끝에 그는 말했다. \"벗이여, 그대가 내 사정을 그토록 많이 들었고, 또 진실을 알고 싶어 그토록 안달하는 듯하니, 끝까지 참고 들어 줄 수 있다면 내가 모든 것을 일러 주겠소.\" \"참으라고요!\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"이야기가 아무리 길어도 그러고말고요. 그리고 제게 베풀어 주시는 이 영광에 깊이 감사드립니다.\"\n\n이제 존스는 이야기를 시작했고, 자초지종을 모두 들려주었다. 다만 한두 가지 정황만은 빼놓았으니, 곧 스왜컴과 싸웠던 그날에 있었던 일은 모조리 빠뜨린 것이다. 그리고 바다로 나가려 했던 결심에서, 북부의 반란이 그 작정을 바꾸어 놓아 그가 지금 있는 이곳까지 오게 된 데까지 이야기를 마쳤다.\n\n줄곧 온 신경을 기울이고 있던 꼬마 벤저민은 단 한 번도 그 이야기를 가로막지 않았다. 그러나 이야기가 끝나자, 분명 나리의 원수들이 무언가를 더 지어내어 올워디 씨에게 일러바쳤음이 틀림없다는 말을 하지 않을 수 없었다. 그렇지 않고서야 그토록 어진 분이 그토록 다정하게 아끼던 사람을 그런 식으로 내쫓았을 리 없다는 것이었다. 이에 존스는 \"그를 파멸시키려고 그런 악랄한 술책이 쓰였음을 의심치 않는다\"고 답했다.\n\n실로 이발사와 똑같은 말을 하지 않을 사람은 거의 없었을 것이다. 그는 존스에게서, 그가 단죄받은 근거가 된 정황을 단 하나도 듣지 못했으니 말이다. 그의 행동은 이제 올워디에게 왜곡되어 전해졌던 그 해로운 빛깔로 놓여 있지 않았고, 또 존스는 그동안 올워디에게 거듭 제기되었던 그 숱한 거짓 고발들을 입에 올릴 수도 없었으니, 그 자신이 그 가운데 어느 것도 알지 못했기 때문이다. 게다가 앞서 보았듯이, 그는 지금의 이야기에서 중요한 사실들을 여럿 빠뜨리기도 했다. 요컨대 실로 이제 모든 일이 존스에게 너무도 유리한 빛깔로 비쳤으므로, 악의 그 자체라도 그에게 어떤 허물을 씌우기란 결코 쉽지 않았을 것이다.\n\n존스가 진실을 숨기거나 꾸미려 했다는 말은 아니다. 오히려 그는 자신이 벌받아 마땅한 행동을 했다는 비난을 받기보다, 자신을 벌한 일로 올워디 씨가 어떤 비난이라도 받는 것을 더 견디지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 실제로는 그렇게 되었고, 또 늘 그렇게 되기 마련이다. 사람이 아무리 정직하다 해도, 제 행실에 관한 이야기는 본인의 의도와는 상관없이 너무도 유리하게 흘러가서, 그의 악덕은 입술을 거치며 정화되어 나오고, 잘 거른 탁한 물처럼 그 모든 더러움을 뒤에 남겨 두고 만다. 사실 자체는 드러난다 해도, 사람이 제 이야기를 할 때와 원수가 그 이야기를 할 때, 그 동기와 정황과 결과가 어찌나 다른지, 우리는 그것이 하나의 똑같은 사실임을 좀처럼 알아보지 못하기 때문이다.\n\n이발사는 이 이야기를 게걸스러운 귀로 들이켰지만, 그래도 아직 만족하지 못했다. 그 뒤에는 그의 호기심이, 차분한 척하면서도 가장 간절히 갈망하는 한 가지 정황이 남아 있었다. 존스는 자신의 연애 사실과, 자신이 블리필의 연적이라는 사실은 언급했으나, 그 처녀의 이름만은 조심스레 숨겨 두었던 것이다. 그리하여 이발사는 얼마간 머뭇거리고 헛기침을 여러 번 한 끝에, 마침내 이 모든 화근의 주된 원인으로 보이는 그 숙녀의 이름을 알려 달라고 청했다. 존스는 잠시 멈칫하더니 이렇게 말했다. \"내가 그대에게 이미 그토록 많은 것을 털어놓았고, 또 이 일로 그녀의 이름이 벌써 너무 널리 알려졌을까 두려우니, 그대에게 숨기지 않겠소. 그녀의 이름은 소피아 웨스턴이오.\"\n\n\"프로 데움 아트퀘 호미눔 피뎀(신과 인간의 신의를 두고)! 웨스턴 나리께 여인이 된 딸이 있다니!\" \"그렇소, 그것도 어떤 여인이냐 하면,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"세상에 견줄 이가 없는 여인이오. 그토록 아름다운 것을 본 눈은 없었지. 하나 그건 그녀의 가장 작은 미덕에 지나지 않소. 그 분별력이라니! 그 선함이라니! 오, 영원히 칭송해도 그녀의 미덕은 절반도 다 못 말할 거요!\" \"웨스턴 씨에게 다 큰 딸이 있다니!\" 이발사가 외쳤다. \"저는 그 아버지가 소년이던 시절을 기억하는데요. 허, 템푸스 에닥스 레룸(세월은 만물을 삼키는 것)이로군요.\"\n\n이제 포도주가 다 떨어지자 이발사는 자기가 한 병 내겠다고 자못 열을 올리며 권했다. 그러나 존스는 한사코 거절하며 말했다. \"이미 마실 것보다 더 마셨소. 이제 내 방으로 물러가고 싶은데, 거기서 책이라도 한 권 구할 수 있으면 좋겠소.\" \"책이라고요!\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"어떤 책을 원하십니까? 라틴어 책입니까, 영어 책입니까? 저에게는 두 언어로 된 진귀한 책이 더러 있습니다. 이를테면 에라스무스의 《대화집》, 오비디우스의 《비가》, 《파르나소스로 오르는 계단》 같은 것들이지요. 영어로도 가장 훌륭한 책을 여러 권 갖고 있습니다. 더러 좀 찢어지긴 했지만요. 스토의 《연대기》 상당 부분, 포프의 《호메로스》 제6권, 《스펙테이터》 제3권, 에처드의 《로마사》 제2권, 《크래프츠맨》, 《로빈슨 크루소》, 토마스 아 켐피스, 그리고 톰 브라운의 《작품집》 두 권이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"마지막 그것은,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"내가 한 번도 본 적 없는 책이오. 그러니 괜찮다면 그 가운데 한 권을 빌려 주시오.\" 이발사는 더없이 즐거우실 거라고 장담하며, 그 작가야말로 이 나라가 배출한 가장 위대한 재사 가운데 하나라고 여긴다고 했다. 그러고는 바로 가까이 있던 자기 집으로 건너가더니 곧 돌아왔다. 그 후, 이발사가 존스에게서 비밀을 지키라는 엄중한 다짐을 받고, 그것을 어김없이 지키겠노라 맹세한 다음, 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 이발사는 집으로 돌아갔고, 존스는 자기 방으로 물러갔다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n6장.\n여기서 벤저민 씨의 또 다른 재주가 드러나며, 이 별난 인물이 대체 누구인지도 밝혀진다.\n\n아침이 되자 존스는 외과의가 사라진 것이 조금 마음에 걸렸다. 상처를 치료하지 않고 두면 어떤 불편이, 어쩌면 위험까지도 따를지 모른다는 염려가 들었기 때문이다. 그는 술집 종업원에게 이 근방에서 만날 수 있는 다른 외과의가 누가 있느냐고 물었다. 종업원은 멀지 않은 곳에 한 사람이 있긴 하다고 일러 주었지만, 그가 다른 사람이 먼저 불려 온 뒤에 끼어드는 일을 자주 마다하더라고 했다. \"그렇지만 손님,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 말을 들으실 거라면, 손님 일을 어젯밤에 함께 계셨던 그 이발사보다 더 잘 처리할 사람은 이 나라 어디에도 없습니다. 저희는 그분을 이 일대에서 칼 다루는 데 가장 솜씨 좋은 사람 가운데 하나로 보고 있지요. 이곳에 온 지 석 달밖에 안 됐는데도, 벌써 큰 병을 여럿 고쳤거든요.\"\n\n종업원은 곧장 리틀 벤저민을 데리러 보내졌고, 벤저민은 자신이 어떤 자격으로 불려 가는지를 알고는 그에 맞게 채비를 갖춘 뒤 찾아왔다. 그런데 그 모습이 옆구리에 대야를 끼고 있을 때와는 어찌나 다른 분위기와 표정이었던지, 같은 사람이라고 알아보기 힘들 지경이었다.\n\n\"이런, 이발사 양반,\" 존스가 말했다. \"보아하니 한 가지 직업만 가진 게 아니로군. 어째서 어젯밤엔 이걸 알려 주지 않았소?\" \"외과란,\" 벤저민이 자못 근엄하게 대답했다. \"직업이 아니라 하나의 전문 학문입니다. 제가 어젯밤에 이 의술을 익혔다는 사실을 알려 드리지 않은 까닭은, 그때 손님께서 다른 신사분의 손에 맡겨져 계신 줄 알았기 때문입니다. 저는 한 번도 동업자의 일에 끼어드는 걸 좋아한 적이 없거든요. 의술은 모두에게 공통된 것이니까요. 자, 이제 손님, 괜찮으시다면 머리를 살펴보겠습니다. 두개골 속까지 들여다본 뒤에 손님 상태에 대한 제 소견을 말씀드리지요.\"\n\n존스는 이 새 전문가를 그리 믿지는 않았으나, 그래도 붕대를 풀고 상처를 들여다보도록 내버려 두었다. 벤저민은 그렇게 하자마자 신음 소리를 내며 머리를 세차게 가로젓기 시작했다. 그러자 존스는 짜증 섞인 투로, 어릿광대 짓 그만하고 자기가 어떤 상태인지나 말해 보라고 했다. \"외과의로서 답해 드릴까요, 아니면 친구로서 답해 드릴까요?\" 벤저민이 물었다. \"친구로서, 그리고 진지하게.\" 존스가 말했다. \"그렇다면, 정말이지,\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"치료를 몇 번만 하고 나면 손님이 나아 버리는 걸 막는 데도 오히려 대단한 기술이 필요할 겁니다. 그러니 제 연고를 좀 발라 드리게만 허락해 주시면, 그 결과는 제가 책임지겠습니다.\" 존스가 승낙하자, 그에 따라 고약이 발라졌다.\n\n\"자, 손님,\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"이제 괜찮으시다면, 저는 본래의 제 모습으로 돌아오겠습니다. 다만 이런 시술을 하는 동안에는 사람이 얼굴에 어느 정도 위엄을 갖추지 않을 수 없습니다. 안 그러면 세상 사람들이 그런 사람의 손에 몸을 맡기려 하질 않거든요. 손님은 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다, 근엄한 인물에게 근엄한 표정이 얼마나 중요한지를요. 이발사는 손님을 웃게 만들어도 되지만, 외과의라면 차라리 손님을 울려야 마땅하지요.\"\n\n\"이발사 양반, 아니 외과의 양반, 아니 이발외과의 양반이라고 해야 하나.\" 존스가 말했다. \"아아, 손님!\" 벤저민이 말을 가로채며 대답했다. \"여왕이여, 차마 입에 올릴 수 없는 슬픔을 되살리라 명하시는군요. 손님께서는 제게 그 잔인한 분리를 떠올리게 하셨습니다. 하나로 묶여 있던 두 조합이 갈라져, 양쪽 단체 모두에게 그토록 큰 손해를 입힌 그 일 말이지요. 모든 분리란 본래 그런 법입니다. 옛 격언에 이르기를, 힘은 합쳐질 때 더 강해진다 하지 않습니까. 물론 어느 한쪽 조합이든 이 말을 풀이할 줄 아는 이가 없지는 않겠지요. 두 가지를 제 한 몸에 합쳐 지닌 저로서는, 그것이 얼마나 큰 타격이었겠습니까!\" \"뭐, 당신이 어떤 이름으로 불리고 싶든 간에,\" 존스가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 분명 내가 여태 만나 본 사람 가운데 가장 별나고 가장 우스꽝스러운 친구요. 그러니 당신 사연에는 무언가 아주 놀라운 게 틀림없이 있을 거요. 그리고 그걸 들을 권리가 내게 있다는 건 당신도 인정해야 할 거요.\" \"인정합니다.\" 벤저민이 대답했다. \"그리고 손님께서 넉넉히 짬이 나실 때 기꺼이 들려 드리지요. 장담컨대 시간이 꽤 걸릴 이야기거든요.\" 존스는 지금보다 더 한가한 때는 없을 거라고 말했다. \"그럼 좋습니다,\" 벤저민이 말했다. \"분부에 따르지요. 다만 먼저 문을 걸어 잠그겠습니다. 누구도 우리를 방해하지 못하도록요.\" 그는 그렇게 하고 나서, 엄숙한 태도로 존스에게 다가오며 말했다. \"먼저 이 말씀부터 드려야겠습니다, 손님. 손님이야말로 제가 여태 가진 가장 큰 원수였답니다.\" 존스는 이 느닷없는 선언에 적잖이 놀랐다. \"내가 당신 원수라고!\" 그가 몹시 놀라며, 표정에 다소 매서운 기색까지 띤 채 말했다. \"아니, 노여워 마십시오.\" 벤저민이 말했다. \"맹세코 저는 노여워하지 않으니까요. 손님은 제게 어떤 해를 끼치려는 뜻이 전혀 없었습니다. 그때 손님은 갓난아기였으니까요. 다만 제 이름을 대는 순간, 이 모든 수수께끼가 풀리리라 믿습니다. 손님, 혹시 패트리지라는 사람 이야기를 들어 보신 적 없습니까? 손님의 아버지라는 평판을 얻는 영예를 누렸으나, 바로 그 영예 때문에 신세를 망치는 불운을 겪은 사람 말입니다.\" \"그 패트리지 이야기라면 정말 들어 보았소.\" 존스가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 늘 내가 그의 아들이라고 믿어 왔소.\" \"그렇습니다, 손님.\" 벤저민이 대답했다. \"제가 바로 그 패트리지입니다. 하지만 저는 이 자리에서 손님을 모든 자식 된 도리에서 풀어 드리겠습니다. 단언컨대, 손님은 결코 제 아들이 아니니까요.\" \"어떻게!\" 존스가 받았다. \"그럼 잘못된 의심 하나가, 내가 너무도 잘 아는 그 모든 불행한 결과를 당신에게 몽땅 끌어왔다는 게 가능한 일이오?\" \"가능합니다.\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"실제로 그러했으니까요. 하지만 사람이 제 고통의 죄 없는 원인조차 미워하는 건 충분히 자연스러운 일이라 해도, 저는 기질이 좀 다릅니다. 아까 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 손님이 블랙 조지에게 보여 준 처신을 전해 들은 그 순간부터 손님을 사랑해 왔지요. 그리고 이 뜻밖의 만남을 통해 확신하게 되었습니다. 손님은 그 일로 제가 겪은 모든 고통을 제게 갚아 주려고 태어난 분이라고요. 게다가 손님을 만나기 전날 밤에는 발판에 걸려 넘어졌는데도 다치지 않는 꿈을 꾸었답니다. 좋은 일이 제게 다가오고 있다는 분명한 표시였지요. 그리고 어젯밤에는 또 우윳빛처럼 흰 암말을 타고 손님 뒤에 앉아 가는 꿈을 꾸었습니다. 이건 아주 훌륭한 꿈이라, 큰 행운을 점치는 징조랍니다. 손님이 잔인하게 거절만 하지 않으신다면, 저는 그 행운을 꼭 좇기로 마음먹었습니다.\"\n\n\"나는 정말 기쁘겠소, 패트리지 씨.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"비록 지금은 그럴 가망이 도무지 보이지 않지만, 나 때문에 당신이 겪은 고통을 갚아 줄 힘이 내게 있다면 말이오. 어쨌든 약속하건대, 내 힘으로 들어줄 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 거절하지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"분명히 손님의 힘으로 들어주실 수 있는 일입니다.\" 벤저민이 받았다. \"저는 이번 여정에 손님을 모시고 따라가도 좋다는 허락 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않으니까요. 아니, 저는 여기에 어찌나 마음을 단단히 쏟았던지, 손님이 저를 거절하신다면 한숨에 이발사 하나와 외과의 하나를 함께 죽이시는 셈이 될 겁니다.\"\n\n존스는 웃으며, 자기가 세상에 그토록 큰 손실을 끼치는 빌미가 된다면 무척 안타까울 거라고 대답했다. 그러고 나서 그는 벤저민(이제부터 우리는 그를 패트리지라 부르기로 하자)이 그 뜻을 단념하도록 신중한 이유를 여럿 늘어놓았으나, 모두 헛수고였다. 패트리지는 우윳빛 흰 암말 꿈에 단단히 기대고 있었다. \"게다가 손님,\" 그가 말했다. \"장담컨대 저는 그 대의에 대해 누구 못지않은 좋은 뜻을 품고 있습니다. 그러니 손님이 동행으로 받아 주시든 안 받아 주시든, 저는 기어이 가고 말 겁니다.\"\n\n존스도 패트리지가 자기를 좋아하는 만큼이나 패트리지가 마음에 들었고, 또 그에게 뒤에 남으라고 했던 것도 자기 좋자고가 아니라 상대의 이익을 헤아려서였던 터라, 친구가 그토록 굳게 결심한 것을 보고는 마침내 승낙하고 말았다. 하지만 곧 정신을 차리고 그는 말했다. \"패트리지 씨, 어쩌면 당신은 내가 당신을 거둘 수 있으리라 여기는 모양인데, 실은 정말 그럴 형편이 못 되오.\" 그러고는 지갑을 꺼내 아홉 기니를 세어 보이며, 이것이 자기 전 재산이라고 밝혔다.\n\n패트리지가 대답했다. \"제가 기대는 건 오직 앞으로 베풀어 주실 호의뿐입니다. 저는 손님이 머지않아 손안에 넉넉한 재물을 쥐게 되시리라 철석같이 믿고 있으니까요. 지금으로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다. \"둘 중에서는 오히려 제가 더 가진 사람이지 싶습니다. 하지만 제가 가진 것은 모두 손님의 것이요, 손님 뜻대로 쓰실 것입니다. 부디 전부 받아 주십시오. 저는 그저 손님의 하인 자격으로 모시고 따라가기만을 청합니다. 토이케르가 이끌고 토이케르가 굽어보는 한, 결코 절망할 것이 없습니다.\" 그러나 돈에 관한 이 너그러운 제안만큼은 존스가 결코 받아들이려 하지 않았다.\n\n이튿날 아침에 길을 떠나기로 정해졌는데, 그때 짐 문제로 한 가지 난관이 생겼다. 존스 씨의 여행 가방이 너무 커서 말 없이는 운반할 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"제가 감히 한 말씀 드려도 된다면,\" 패트리지가 말했다. \"셔츠 몇 벌만 빼고, 이 여행 가방은 안에 든 것까지 통째로 두고 가시는 게 좋겠습니다. 그 셔츠 몇 벌이야 제가 손님을 위해 거뜬히 들고 갈 수 있고, 나머지 옷가지는 제 집에 단단히 잠가 두면 아주 안전하게 보관될 테니까요.\"\n\n이 방안은 제안되기가 무섭게 합의되었다. 그러고 나서 이발사는 떠나기로 한 여정을 위해 만반의 준비를 갖추러 자리를 떴다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n파트리지의 처신에 대해 지금까지 나온 어떤 것보다 더 그럴듯한 이유들을 담고, 존스의 약점에 대한 변명과, 우리 안주인에 관한 몇 가지 일화를 더 보탠다.\n\n파트리지가 세상에서 손꼽히게 미신을 신봉하는 사람이긴 했지만, 만일 그가 바랄 수 있는 전망이라는 게 고작 전쟁터에서 얻은 노획물을 나눠 갖는 정도였다면, 그저 다리 셋 달린 의자와 흰 암말의 징조만 보고 존스의 원정에 따라나서고 싶어 하지는 않았을 것이다. 사실 파트리지가 존스에게서 들은 이야기를 곰곰이 되씹어 보니, 자기가 들은 그 어떤 이유를 들이대더라도 올워디 씨가 자기 아들을(그는 존스를 누구보다 굳게 그렇게 믿고 있었다) 집 밖으로 내쫓았다는 것이 도무지 납득되지 않았다. 그래서 그는 그 모든 이야기가 꾸며 낸 것이고, 자기 통신원들에게서 그 누구보다 거친 사람이라는 평판을 자주 들어 온 존스가 실은 아버지에게서 도망쳐 나온 것이라고 결론지었다. 그러자 그의 머릿속에 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. 만일 자기가 이 젊은 신사를 설득해 아버지에게 돌아가게 할 수만 있다면, 그렇게 함으로써 올워디에게 큰 도움을 베푸는 셈이 되어 그동안의 노여움을 모조리 씻어 낼 수 있으리라고. 아니, 실은 그 노여움조차 거짓으로 꾸민 것이고, 올워디가 자기 명예를 지키려고 그를 희생양으로 삼은 것이라고 그는 생각했다. 그리고 이 의심에는 그 나름대로 충분한 근거가 있었다. 그 훌륭한 분이 업둥이 아이에게 보인 다정한 태도, 자기는 결백함을 알기에 다른 사람이 자기를 죄인으로 여길 수 있다는 걸 상상조차 못 했던 파트리지 자신에게 보인 그 모진 엄격함, 그리고 마지막으로, 그에게 주던 연금이 공개적으로 끊긴 한참 뒤에도 은밀히 받아 온 그 보조금이 그것이었다. 그는 그 보조금을 일종의 위자료, 아니 오히려 부당함에 대한 속죄의 표시쯤으로 여겼다. 내 생각에, 사람이란 자기가 받은 호의를 다른 어떤 동기로든 돌릴 수 있을 때에는 그것을 순수한 자선으로 돌리는 경우가 매우 드무니 말이다. 그러므로 만일 어떤 수단으로든 이 젊은 신사를 설득해 집으로 돌려보낼 수만 있다면, 자기가 다시 올워디의 총애를 받아 그 수고에 대해 후한 보답을 얻으리라는 것, 아니 그뿐 아니라 자기 고향 땅으로 다시 돌아가게 되리라는 것을 그는 의심치 않았다. 그 귀향이라면, 가엾은 파트리지가 율리시스 못지않게 간절히 바라던 것이었다.\n\n* 율리시스: 호메로스의 서사시 속 인물로, 오랜 방랑 끝에 고향으로 돌아가기를 애타게 갈망한 영웅.\n한편 존스로 말하자면, 그는 상대가 한 말이 모두 진실이라고 흡족하게 믿었고, 파트리지에게 자기에 대한 애정과 대의를 향한 열의 말고는 다른 속셈이 없다고 여겼다. 이는 남의 진실성을 믿는 데서 경계심과 의심이 부족했던 탓인데, 이 점에서 그는 크게 나무람을 받아 마땅했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 사람이 이 빼어난 자질을 갖추게 되는 길은 두 가지뿐이다. 하나는 오랜 경험에서 오는 것이고, 다른 하나는 타고나는 것이다. 후자는 흔히 천재성, 또는 위대한 천부의 재능이라 불리는 것을 가리키지 싶다. 그리고 이 둘 가운데 후자가 비할 데 없이 낫다. 인생에서 훨씬 일찍 그것을 손에 넣을 수 있을 뿐 아니라, 훨씬 더 어김없고 확실하기 때문이다. 아무리 많은 사람에게 속아 넘어간 사람이라도 여전히 더 정직한 이를 만나기를 바랄 수 있지만, 그것이 불가능하다는 어떤 긴요한 깨우침을 마음속에서 받는 사람은, 만일 단 한 번이라도 속을 처지에 빠진다면 정말이지 이해력이 너무도 모자란 사람일 테니 말이다. 존스는 이 자질을 타고나지 못했고, 경험으로 얻기에는 너무 젊었다. 이런 식으로 얻게 되는, 의심할 줄 아는 지혜에는 우리가 인생의 아주 늦은 시기에 이르러서야 겨우 다다르기 때문이다. 어쩌면 이것이, 어떤 노인들이 자기보다 조금이라도 어린 사람들의 이해력을 죄다 업신여기기 쉬운 까닭일 것이다.\n\n존스는 그날 하루의 대부분을 새로 사귄 사람과 함께 보냈다. 그 사람은 다름 아닌 이 집의 주인, 아니 더 정확히 말하면 안주인의 남편이었다. 그는 오랜 통풍 발작을 앓다가 얼마 전에야 겨우 아래층으로 내려왔는데, 이 병 때문에 그는 한 해의 절반은 대개 방 안에 갇혀 지냈고, 나머지 절반 동안은 집 안을 어슬렁거리며 담배를 피우고 벗들과 술병을 비우면서, 그 어떤 일에도 도통 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 그는 이른바 신사로 길러졌다. 다시 말해, 아무 일도 하지 않도록 길러졌다는 뜻이다. 그는 부지런한 농사꾼이던 삼촌에게서 물려받은 얼마 안 되는 재산을 사냥과 경마와 닭싸움에 다 써 버렸고, 우리 안주인이 어떤 목적을 위해 그와 결혼했으나, 그는 이미 오래전부터 그 목적에 부응하기를 그만둔 터라 안주인은 그를 진심으로 미워했다. 그러나 그가 워낙 무뚝뚝한 사내였던 까닭에, 안주인은 그를 입에 달고 칭송하던 첫 남편과 불리하게 견주어 가며 자주 면박을 주는 것으로 만족했다. 또 대부분의 수입을 안주인이 쥐고 있었던 만큼, 그녀는 집안의 살림과 관리를 스스로 떠맡는 것으로 흡족해했고, 오랫동안 보람 없는 다툼 끝에 결국 남편이 제 한 몸의 주인 노릇이나 하도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n저녁이 되어 존스가 자기 방으로 물러가자, 이 살가운 부부 사이에 그를 두고 자그마한 말다툼이 벌어졌다. \"뭐예요,\" 아내가 말했다. \"보아하니 그 신사 양반이랑 한잔 걸치셨구먼?\" \"그래,\" 남편이 대꾸했다. \"한 병 같이 비웠지. 아주 신사다운 사람이고, 말에 대해서도 제법 안목이 있더군. 하기야 아직 젊고 세상 구경을 많이 못 해서 그런지, 경마장에는 거의 가 본 적이 없는 모양이야.\" \"오호! 그럼 당신 같은 부류 사람이로구먼?\" 안주인이 받아쳤다. \"경마꾼이라면 분명 신사일 테지. 그런 양반들은 악마나 데려가라지! 정말이지, 그런 작자들 따위 평생 안 봤더라면 좋았을걸. 내가 경마꾼들을 사랑할 만한 까닭이 참 많기도 하다!\" \"암, 많고말고,\" 남편이 말했다. \"내가 바로 그 경마꾼이었으니까 말이야.\" \"그래요,\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"당신 참 순수한 경마꾼이지. 첫 남편이 늘 하던 말마따나, 내가 당신한테서 얻은 좋은 거라곤 몽땅 눈에 넣어도 끄떡없을 만큼 쥐뿔도 없을 거예요.\" \"당신 첫 남편 같은 건 빌어먹을!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신보다 나은 사람을 두고 빌어먹으라 하지 마세요,\" 아내가 받아쳤다. \"그이가 살아 있었다면 당신은 감히 그런 말 못 했을 테니.\" \"그러니까 당신 생각엔,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 당신만큼도 배짱이 없다 이거지. 당신이야말로 내 앞에서 그 사람을 노상 빌어먹으라 욕했잖아.\" \"내가 그랬다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여러 번 두고두고 뉘우쳤어요. 그이가 마음 너그러워서 홧김에 내뱉은 말 한마디쯤 용서해 줬다 한들, 당신 같은 사람이 그걸 가지고 나를 놀려 댈 자격은 없어요. 그이는 내게 진정한 남편이었어요, 정말로. 그리고 내가 설령 발끈해서 험한 말 한마디를 했더라도, 그이를 두고 망나니라고는 한 번도 안 불렀어요. 그이를 망나니라고 불렀다면 그건 거짓말이었을 테니까.\" 그녀는 훨씬 더 많은 말을 늘어놓았으나, 남편이 듣는 데서는 아니었다. 남편은 담뱃대에 불을 붙이고는 비척비척 부리나케 자리를 떠 버렸기 때문이다. 그러므로 우리는 그녀의 말을 더 옮겨 적지 않겠다. 그것이 이 이야기에 도무지 끼워 넣을 수 없을 만큼 점잖지 못한 주제로 점점 더 가까이 다가갔기 때문이다.\n\n이른 아침, 파트리지가 등에 배낭을 멘 채 여행 채비를 단단히 갖추고 존스의 침대 머리맡에 나타났다. 이 배낭은 그가 손수 만든 것이었다. 그는 다른 여러 생업 말고도 솜씨가 제법인 재단사였기 때문이다. 그는 이미 셔츠 넉 장으로 된 자기 옷가지 전부를 그 안에 챙겨 넣었고, 거기에 이제 존스 씨의 셔츠 여덟 장을 더 보탰다. 그러고는 여행 가방을 꾸려 그것을 들고 자기 집을 향해 떠나려 했으나, 가는 길에 안주인에게 가로막혔다. 안주인은 셈을 치르기 전에는 어떤 물건도 옮겨 나가게 둘 수 없다고 버텼다.\n\n앞서 말했듯, 안주인은 이 영역의 절대적인 지배자였다. 그러니 그녀의 규칙을 따를 수밖에 없었다. 그리하여 계산서가 곧 작성되었는데, 그 액수는 존스가 받은 대접에 견주어 예상보다 훨씬 큰 금액에 이르렀다. 그러나 여기서 우리는 술집 주인들이 자기네 장사의 비법으로 떠받드는 몇 가지 격언을 밝히지 않을 수 없다. 첫째, 만일 집 안에 무언가 좋은 것이 있거든(실은 그런 일이 아주 드물게 일어나지만), 그것을 큰 마차 행렬을 거느리고 여행하는 손님들에게만 내놓을 것. 둘째, 가장 형편없는 음식을 가장 좋은 음식인 양 똑같은 값에 매길 것. 마지막으로, 어느 손님이 조금밖에 시키지 않거든, 그가 먹은 모든 것에 두 배의 값을 물려서, 머릿수로 따진 총액이 엇비슷하도록 맞출 것.\n\n계산서가 작성되고 셈이 치러지자, 존스는 배낭을 멘 파트리지와 함께 길을 나섰다. 안주인은 그에게 좋은 여행 되라는 인사 한마디조차 베풀려 하지 않았다. 보아하니 이 여관은 상류층 사람들이 즐겨 찾는 곳이었던 모양인데, 어찌 된 영문인지는 모르겠으나, 상류층 사람들 덕에 생계를 잇는 자들은 하나같이 마치 자기네가 정말로 그 계급에 속하기라도 하는 양, 나머지 사람들에게는 그만큼 거만하게 굴기 마련이다.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n존스가 글로스터에 도착해 벨 여관으로 들어간다. 그 여관의 됨됨이와, 그곳에서 만난 어느 삼류 변호사의 됨됨이.\n\n존스 씨와 파트리지, 다시 말해 꼬마 벤저민(이 ‘꼬마’라는 별명은 어쩌면 비꼬는 뜻으로 붙은 듯하다. 그는 실은 키가 6피트에 가까웠으니 말이다)은 앞서 묘사한 방식으로 마지막 거처를 떠난 뒤, 이렇다 할 이야깃거리가 될 만한 사건 하나 만나지 않고 글로스터까지 걸어왔다.\n\n이곳에 다다른 두 사람은 묵을 곳으로 벨이라는 간판을 단 여관을 골랐다. 참으로 훌륭한 여관이라, 나는 이 유서 깊은 도시를 찾을 모든 독자에게 진심으로 이곳을 권하는 바이다. 주인은 저 위대한 설교가 화이트필드의 형제이지만, 감리교의 해로운 교리나 그 밖의 어떤 이단 교파의 물도 전혀 들지 않은 사람이다. 그는 실로 매우 정직하고 소박한 사람이며, 내 생각으로는 교회에서든 나라에서든 어떤 소동도 일으킬 만한 위인이 아니다. 그의 아내는 내가 보기에 한때 미모를 꽤나 자랑할 만했고, 지금도 여전히 무척 고운 여인이다. 그녀의 자태와 몸가짐이라면 가장 세련된 모임에서도 빛나는 인물이 될 만했을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 이 점과 그 밖의 여러 미덕을 분명 의식하고 있을 텐데도, 자신에게 주어진 그 삶의 자리에 더없이 만족하며 순순히 따르는 듯 보인다. 이 순응은 전적으로 그녀의 신중하고 슬기로운 성품 덕분이다. 지금의 그녀는 남편만큼이나 감리교적 관념에서 자유롭기 때문이다. ‘지금’이라 말하는 까닭은, 그녀가 솔직히 털어놓기를, 처음에는 오라비의 가르침이 자기에게 얼마간 인상을 남겨서, 성령의 특별한 감동을 받아 보려고 긴 두건을 사 입느라 돈까지 들였노라 하기 때문이다. 그러나 삼 주에 걸친 시험 끝에, 단돈 한 푼어치도 될 만한 감동이 없음을 깨닫고는, 아주 현명하게도 그 두건을 치워 두고 그 교파를 버렸다고 한다. 요컨대 그녀는 무척 다정하고 마음씨 좋은 여인이며, 손님을 받드는 데 어찌나 부지런한지, 그녀의 여관에서 더없이 흡족하지 못한 손님이 있다면 그건 성미가 몹시 까다로운 사람일 수밖에 없다.\n\n존스와 그의 일행이 들어설 때, 마침 화이트필드 부인은 마당에 있었다. 그녀의 밝은 눈썰미는 우리 주인공의 분위기에서 무언가 범상한 사람들과는 다른 점을 곧 알아챘다. 그리하여 그녀는 하인들에게 즉시 그를 방으로 안내하라 이르고는, 이내 그를 자기와 함께 식사하자고 청했다. 존스는 그 청을 무척 고맙게 받아들였다. 실로 그토록 오래 굶고 그토록 오래 걸은 뒤라면, 화이트필드 부인보다 훨씬 덜 유쾌한 벗이라도, 또 그녀가 차린 것보다 훨씬 못한 음식이라도 반가웠을 테니 말이다.\n\n존스 씨와 이 여관의 어진 안주인 외에도, 식탁에는 솔즈베리에서 온 한 변호사가 앉아 있었다. 실은 블리필 부인의 부고를 올워디 씨에게 전했던 바로 그 사람으로, 이름은 앞서 언급하지 않았던 듯한데, 다울링이라 했다. 또 한 사람이 함께 있었으니, 스스로를 법률가라 일컫는 자로, 서머싯셔의 린린치 부근 어딘가에 사는 인물이었다. 이 작자는 제 입으로 법률가라 했지만, 실은 무슨 분별이나 지식이라곤 한 톨도 없는 더없이 천한 삼류 변호사였다. 법의 들러리라 부를 만한 부류, 곧 그 직업의 잉여 인력 같은 자들로, 변호사들의 삯말 노릇을 하며, 반 크라운만 받으면 우편배달 소년보다도 더 먼 거리를 마다 않고 달리는 그런 위인이었다.\n\n식사가 이어지는 동안, 서머싯셔의 그 법률가는 존스의 얼굴을 기억해 냈다. 올워디 씨 댁에서 본 적이 있었던 것이다. 그는 그 신사의 부엌을 곧잘 드나들었기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 올워디 씨와 가까운 벗이나 지인에게나 어울릴 법한 허물없는 태도로, 그 댁의 안부를 묻는 기회를 잡았다. 실로 그는 자신이 그런 사이인 양 보이려고 온갖 수를 다 썼으나, 그 집안에서 집사보다 높은 사람과는 한마디 나눠 본 영광조차 없는 자였다. 존스는 그 삼류 변호사를 전에 본 기억이 도무지 없었고, 또 그자의 겉모습과 행실로 미루어 제 분수에 넘치는 허물없음을 윗사람에게 함부로 부린다고 결론지었으면서도, 그의 모든 물음에 무척 정중하게 답해 주었다.\n\n이런 부류의 작자들과 나누는 대화란, 조금이라도 분별 있는 사람에게는 무엇보다도 역겨운 법이라, 식탁보가 걷히기가 무섭게 존스 씨는 자리를 떴다. 그리하여 가엾은 화이트필드 부인을 다소 매정하게 남겨 두고 떠난 셈인데, 나는 티머시 해리스 씨를 비롯해 안목 있는 여러 여관 주인들이, 손님과 어울려 앉아 있어야 하는 것이야말로 자기네 생업에 따라붙은 가장 가혹한 형벌이라고 한탄하는 소리를 곧잘 들어 왔다.\n\n존스가 방을 나서기가 무섭게, 삼류 변호사는 속삭이는 목소리로 화이트필드 부인에게 물었다. “저 멀끔한 멋쟁이가 누군지 아시오?” 그녀는 “저 신사분은 전에 본 적이 없다”고 답했다. “신사라니, 원!” 삼류 변호사가 받았다. “참으로 그럴듯한 신사로군! 아니, 저자는 말 도둑질로 교수형 당한 놈의 사생아요. 올워디 나리 댁 문 앞에 버려진 것을, 하인 하나가 어찌나 빗물이 가득 찬 상자 속에서 찾아냈던지, 다른 운명을 위해 남겨진 게 아니었다면 영락없이 빠져 죽었을 거요.” “아, 아, 굳이 말 안 해도 됩니다. 정말이지, 그 운명이 뭔지야 우리도 잘 아니까요.” 다울링이 더없이 익살스러운 히죽임을 지으며 외쳤다. “그래서,” 다른 자가 말을 이었다. “나리께서 그놈을 거두라 명하셨소. 다들 알다시피 나리는 마음 약한 양반이라, 괜한 곤경에 휘말릴까 두려웠던 게지. 그렇게 해서 그 사생아 놈은 어엿한 신사처럼 길러지고, 먹여지고, 옷도 떡 벌어지게 입혀졌다오. 그러더니 거기서 하녀 하나를 임신시키고는, 그게 나리의 아이라고 맹세하라고 꼬드겼지. 그다음엔 색시질을 나무랐다는 이유 하나로 색이라는 목사 양반의 팔을 부러뜨렸고, 그 뒤엔 블리필 씨 등 뒤에서 권총을 들이댔다오. 또 한번은 올워디 나리가 병이 나자, 북을 구해다가 잠 못 들게 하려고 온 집안을 돌며 둥둥 쳐 댔지. 그 밖에도 스무 가지 장난을 쳤으니, 그 모든 일 때문에 나흘이나 닷새 전쯤, 마침 내가 고향을 떠나기 직전에, 나리께서 그놈을 발가벗겨 문밖으로 내쫓아 버리셨소.”\n\n“그것도 지극히 마땅한 일이고말고요, 정말이지.” 다울링이 외쳤다. “내 아들이라도 그 절반만 저질렀어도 문밖으로 내쫓았을 거요. 한데 그 멀끔한 신사분 이름이 대체 뭐랍니까?”\n\n“그놈 이름 말이오?” 삼류 변호사가 답했다. “아, 토머스 존스라고 한다오.”\n\n“존스라고요!” 다울링이 다소 다급하게 받았다. “뭐, 올워디 씨 댁에 살던 그 존스 씨 말이오? 우리와 함께 식사한 그 신사가 그 사람이었소?” “바로 그 사람이오.” 다른 자가 말했다. “그 신사 얘기야 자주 들었지요.” 다울링이 외쳤다. “하지만 그에 대한 나쁜 평판은 한 번도 들은 적이 없는데.” “정말이지,” 화이트필드 부인이 말했다. “이분이 하신 말씀 가운데 절반만 사실이라 해도, 존스 씨는 내 평생 본 가운데 가장 속을 알 수 없는 얼굴을 지닌 사람이네요. 그 인상은 분명 그와는 전혀 다른 무언가를 약속하는 듯하니 말이에요. 또 내가 잠깐 본 바로는, 그는 누구라도 함께 이야기 나누고 싶을 만큼 점잖고 예의 바른 사람이라고 말할 수밖에 없네요.”\n\n삼류 변호사는, 평소 증언하기 전에 늘 하던 것처럼 선서를 하지 않았다는 사실이 문득 떠오르자, 이제 자신이 한 말에 어찌나 많은 맹세와 저주를 갖다 붙여 못을 박았던지, 안주인이 그 소리에 질겁하여, 그를 믿는다고 다짐해 줌으로써 그의 맹세를 멈추게 했다. 그러자 그가 말했다. “부인, 내가 사실이라 알지 못하는 일을 두고 어느 누구에 대해서든 이런 얘기를 떠벌릴 만큼 비열한 사람이라 여기진 않으시겠지요. 내게 해코지 한 번 한 적 없는 사람의 명예를 깎아내려서 내가 얻을 이익이 뭐가 있겠소? 약속하건대 내가 한 말은 한 음절도 빠짐없이 사실이며, 온 고을이 다 아는 일이오.”\n\n화이트필드 부인으로서는 그 삼류 변호사가 존스를 헐뜯을 만한 동기나 유혹을 가졌으리라 의심할 까닭이 없었으니, 독자도 그가 그토록 자신만만하게 숱한 맹세까지 곁들여 단언한 말을 그녀가 믿었다 하여 탓할 수는 없으리라. 그리하여 그녀는 자신의 관상 솜씨를 접어 두고, 이때부터 그 손님에 대해 어찌나 나쁜 견해를 품었던지, 그가 자기 여관에서 나가 버리기를 진심으로 바라게 되었다.\n\n이 반감은 이제 화이트필드 씨가 부엌에서 전해 온 이야기로 한층 더 커졌다. 거기서 파트리지가 사람들에게 일러바치기를, “비록 자기가 배낭을 메고, 톰 존스(그는 그를 이렇게 불렀다)가 응접실에서 융숭히 대접받는 동안 하인들 틈에 끼어 있는 것으로 만족하고는 있지만, 자기는 그의 하인이 아니라 그저 벗이자 길동무일 뿐이며, 존스 씨 못지않게 어엿한 신사”라 했다는 것이다.\n\n다울링은 이 내내 말없이 앉아, 손가락을 깨물고, 얼굴을 찡그리고, 히죽거리며, 놀랍도록 능청스러운 낯빛을 지었다. 마침내 그가 입을 열어, 그 신사는 그와는 전혀 다른 부류의 사람처럼 보이더라고 단언했다. 그러고는 부랴부랴 계산서를 청하고, 그날 저녁 안에 헤리퍼드에 닿아야 한다며, 일이 너무 바빠 죽겠다고 한탄하고는, 스무 군데에 한꺼번에 있을 수 있도록 제 몸을 스무 조각으로 나눌 수 있었으면 좋겠다고 했다.\n\n이제 삼류 변호사도 마찬가지로 떠나갔고, 그러자 존스는 화이트필드 부인에게 함께 차나 한잔하자고 청하는 호의를 베풀어 달라 했다. 그러나 그녀는 거절했는데, 그 태도가 식사 자리에서 그를 맞이하던 것과 어찌나 다르던지 그는 다소 놀랐다. 이제 그는 그녀의 행동이 완전히 달라졌음을 곧 알아챘다. 앞서 우리가 기렸던 그 천성적인 상냥함 대신, 그녀는 얼굴에 억지스러운 엄격함을 띠고 있었는데, 그것이 존스 씨에게는 어찌나 거북하던지, 그는 아무리 늦더라도 그날 저녁 안에 이 여관을 떠나기로 마음먹었다.\n\n사실 그는 이 갑작스러운 변화를 다소 부당하게 해석했다. 여자의 변덕스러움과 변하기 쉬운 마음에 대한 가혹하고 부당한 억측은 차치하고서라도, 그는 이 무례함이 자기에게 말이 없는 탓이라 의심하기 시작했다. 말이라는 짐승은 침대보를 더럽히지 않기에, 여관에서는 그 타는 사람보다 잠자리 값을 더 잘 치른다고 여겨지고, 그래서 더 반가운 손님으로 대접받는 법이니 말이다. 그러나 화이트필드 부인은, 공정하게 말하자면, 훨씬 더 너그러운 사고방식을 지닌 여인이었다. 그녀는 더없이 예의 바른 사람이라, 비록 걸어서 온 신사라 해도 무척 정중하게 대할 줄 알았다. 실은 그녀가 우리 주인공을 형편없는 악당으로 보았기에 그렇게 대했던 것이며, 만일 존스 자신도 독자만큼 사정을 알았더라면 그조차 그녀를 탓할 수 없었으리라. 아니, 오히려 그는 그녀의 처신을 옳다 여기고, 자기에게 보인 그 무례함 때문에 그녀를 더욱 높이 샀을 것이다. 이것이야말로 사람의 명예를 부당하게 앗아 가는 일에 따라붙는 가장 통탄할 사정이다. 자신이 나쁜 평판을 지녔음을 의식하는 사람은, 자기를 소홀히 하고 업신여기는 이들에게 정당하게 화를 낼 수 없으며, 도리어 자기와 어울리려 드는 이들을 경멸해 마땅하다. 다만 더없이 깊은 친분이 있어 그 벗의 평판이 거짓되고 해롭게 더럽혀졌음을 그들에게 확신시킨 경우라면 모를 일이다.\n\n그러나 이는 존스의 경우는 아니었다. 그는 진실을 전혀 모르는 처지였으니, 자기가 받은 대접에 화를 낸 것도 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 그는 셈을 치르고 떠났다. 파트리지 씨의 뜻에는 몹시 거슬리는 일이었는데, 그는 이에 대해 한참을 항변했으나 아무 소용이 없자, 마침내 마지못해 제 배낭을 둘러메고 벗을 따라나섰다.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\n사랑과 추위와 굶주림을 비롯한 여러 문제에 관해 존스와 파트리지가 나눈 몇 차례의 대화를 담았으며, 아울러 파트리지가 자신의 벗에게 치명적인 비밀을 폭로하기 직전까지 갔다가 운 좋게 가까스로 빠져나온 이야기를 담았다.\n\n이제 높은 산들로부터 그림자가 점점 길게 내려오기 시작했고, 깃털 달린 무리들은 저마다 보금자리로 돌아갔다. 바야흐로 가장 높은 신분의 인간들은 만찬 자리에 앉고, 가장 낮은 신분의 인간들은 저녁 끼니 앞에 앉을 무렵이었다. 한마디로, 존스 씨가 글로스터에 작별을 고하던 바로 그때 시계가 다섯 시를 쳤다. 한겨울인 지금이라면 밤의 더러운 손가락이 그 검은 장막을 온 세상 위로 드리웠을 시각이었으나, 달이 그것을 막았다. 달은 마치 자기처럼 밤을 낮으로 바꾸며 사는 어느 유쾌한 인간들의 얼굴마냥 넓고 붉은 얼굴을 하고서, 하루 종일 잠들어 빈둥거리던 잠자리에서 이제 일어나, 밤새 깨어 있으려고 몸을 일으키기 시작했다. 존스는 멀리 가기도 전에 그 아름다운 별에게 경의를 표했고, 동행을 돌아보며 이토록 감미로운 저녁을 본 적이 있느냐고 물었다. 파트리지가 그 물음에 선뜻 대답을 내놓지 못하자, 그는 달의 아름다움에 대해 한마디 덧붙이고는, 천상의 빛나는 것들을 묘사하는 데 있어 다른 어떤 시인보다 단연 뛰어났던 밀턴의 시구 몇 구절을 읊었다. 그러고는 파트리지에게 《스펙테이터》에 실린 두 연인의 이야기를 들려주었다. 두 사람은 서로 멀리 떨어져 있을 때, 정해진 어느 시각에 함께 달을 바라보기로 약속함으로써 스스로를 달래기로 했다는 것이다. 같은 시각에 둘 다 같은 대상을 바라보고 있다는 생각으로 서로 위안을 삼은 셈이었다. \"그 연인들은,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"인간의 모든 정념 가운데 가장 숭고한 그 정념의 온갖 애틋함을 진정으로 느낄 수 있는 영혼을 지녔던 게 분명해.\"—\"아마 그렇겠지요.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"하지만 저는 그들이 추위를 느끼지 못하는 몸을 가졌다면 더 부럽겠습니다. 저는 지금 거의 얼어 죽을 지경이라, 다음 여관에 닿기도 전에 코끝이 한 조각 떨어져 나가지 않을까 몹시 두렵거든요. 아니, 사실, 제가 평생 발을 들여본 곳 가운데 가장 훌륭한 여관 하나를 밤에 이렇게 박차고 나온 우리의 어리석음에 대해서는 어떤 천벌이 내려도 마땅합니다. 정말이지 저는 그렇게 좋은 것들을 평생 본 적이 없습니다. 이 나라에서 제일가는 영주라 해도 제 집에서 그곳보다 더 잘 지내지는 못할 겁니다. 그런데 그런 곳을 버려두고, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채, 들길을 헤매며 시골을 떠돌다니, 저야 입을 다물겠습니다만, 어떤 이들은 우리가 제정신이었다고 너그러이 봐줄 만큼 인정이 넉넉하지는 않을 겁니다.\"—\"저런, 파트리지 씨!\" 존스가 말했다. \"기운을 좀 내요. 당신은 적과 맞서러 가는 길임을 생각해요. 그런 당신이 고작 약간의 추위와 맞서기를 두려워한단 말이오? 정말이지 어느 길로 가야 할지 일러줄 안내인이라도 하나 있었으면 좋겠소.\"—\"제가 외람되이,\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"제 의견을 좀 아뢰어도 될까요? 때로는 어리석은 자도 적절한 말을 하는 법이지요.\"—\"그래, 그 길들 중에서,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"어느 쪽을 권하겠소?\"—\"실은 어느 쪽도 권하지 않습니다.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"우리가 확실히 찾을 수 있는 유일한 길은 우리가 왔던 길입니다. 부지런히 한 시간만 걸으면 글로스터로 되돌아갈 수 있지요. 하지만 앞으로 나아가면, 우리가 어딘가에 닿기는 할지 하느님만 아실 일입니다. 제 앞으로는 적어도 오십 마일은 펼쳐져 있는데, 그 길 어디에도 집 한 채 보이지 않으니까요.\"—\"과연 매우 멋진 경치가 보이는군.\" 존스가 말했다. \"더없이 환한 달빛 덕분에 한층 더 아름다워 보이고 말이오. 그래도 나는 왼쪽 길을 택하겠소. 그쪽이 우스터에서 멀지 않다고 들은 저 언덕들로 곧장 이어지는 듯하니 말이오. 자, 나를 떠나고 싶거든 그래도 좋소, 돌아가도 되오. 하지만 나로 말하면, 앞으로 나아가기로 굳게 마음먹었소.\"\n\n\"나리, 제가 그런 마음을 품었으리라 의심하시다니 인정머리 없으십니다.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"제가 드린 의견은 제 자신을 위한 것만큼이나 나리를 위한 것이었습니다. 하지만 나리께서 나아가기로 결심하셨으니, 저도 따르기로 그만큼 굳게 결심했습니다. 먼저 가십시오, 제가 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n이제 두 사람은 서로 한마디도 나누지 않은 채 몇 마일을 걸었는데, 그렇게 말이 끊긴 사이 존스는 자주 한숨을 내쉬었고, 벤저민 역시 못지않게 쓰라리게 끙끙거렸으나 그 까닭은 사뭇 달랐다. 마침내 존스가 우뚝 멈춰 서더니 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"누가 알겠나, 파트리지. 이 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 사람이 지금 바로 내가 이 순간 바라보는 저 달에 눈을 두고 있을지도 모르지 않나?\" \"그럴 법도 합니다, 나리.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"그리고 제 눈이 잘 구운 소 등심 한 덩이에 가 있다면, 달이고 그 뿔이고 죄다 악마가 가져가 버려도 좋겠습니다.\" \"산 너머 촌사람이라 한들 그런 대꾸를 한 자가 있었겠나?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"이보게 파트리지, 자네는 평생 사랑이란 걸 느껴본 적이 있기는 한가, 아니면 세월이 자네 기억에서 그 흔적을 죄다 닳아 없애버린 건가?\" \"아아, 한심한 노릇이지요!\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"차라리 사랑이 뭔지 영영 몰랐더라면 제게 좋았을 겁니다. 여왕이시여, 말로 못할 슬픔을 다시 들추라 명하시는군요. 저야말로 그 정념의 온갖 애틋함과 숭고함과 쓰디씀을 죄다 맛본 사람이라고 확신합니다.\" \"그렇다면 자네 연인이 매정했단 말인가?\" 존스가 말했다. \"정말이지 무척이나 매정했지요, 나리.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"저와 결혼해서 세상에서 가장 지긋지긋한 아내 노릇을 했으니까요. 그래도, 하느님 감사하게도, 그 사람은 갔습니다. 그리고 만약 그 사람이 달 속에 있다고 믿었다면, 언젠가 읽은 어느 책이 달을 떠난 영혼들이 머무는 곳이라 가르치더이다만, 저는 그 사람을 볼까 두려워 결코 달을 쳐다보지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 나리, 나리를 위해서라면 달이 거울이 되어, 소피아 웨스턴 아가씨가 지금 그 앞에 놓여 있으면 좋겠습니다.\" \"이보게 파트리지,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"거 참 기막힌 생각이로군! 사랑하는 자의 마음이 아니고서는 결코 떠오를 수 없는 생각이야. 오 파트리지! 그 얼굴을 다시 한 번 볼 수만 있다면. 하지만, 아아! 그 황금빛 꿈들은 모두 영영 사라져버렸고, 앞으로의 비참함에서 내가 피할 유일한 길이란 지난날의 모든 행복의 대상을 잊는 것뿐이라네.\" \"정말로 웨스턴 아가씨를 다시 볼 수 없으리라 절망하시는 겁니까?\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"제 충고를 따르신다면, 나리께서 그분을 다시 보실 뿐 아니라 품에 안으시도록 제가 책임지겠습니다.\" \"하! 그런 종류의 생각일랑 일깨우지 말게나.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"나는 이미 그런 온갖 바람을 누르려고 충분히 애써왔다네.\" \"원, 참,\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"연인을 품에 안기를 바라지 않으신다면, 나리야말로 참으로 별난 연인이십니다.\" \"알았네, 알았어.\" 존스가 말했다. \"이 이야기는 그만두세. 그런데 자네 충고가 무엇인지나 말해보게.\" \"그렇다면 군대식으로 말씀드리지요.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"우리도 병사이니, '뒤로 돌아.' 왔던 길로 돌아갑시다. 늦더라도 오늘 밤 안에 글로스터에 닿을 수 있을 겁니다. 반면에 계속 나아가면, 제가 보기엔 집도 절도 못 만난 채 영영 떠돌게 될 듯합니다.\" \"내 결심은 나아가는 것이라고 이미 말했네.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"다만 자네는 돌아갔으면 하네. 여기까지 함께해준 것은 고마우이. 부디 내 작은 감사의 표시로 이 일 기니를 받아주게. 아니, 자네를 더 멀리 끌고 가는 건 내가 잔인한 짓이 될 거야. 솔직히 털어놓자면, 나의 가장 큰 목적이자 바람은 임금과 나라를 위해 싸우다 영광스럽게 죽는 것이거든.\" \"돈에 관해서라면,\" 파트리지가 대꾸했다. \"나리, 부디 도로 넣어두십시오. 이번엔 한 푼도 받지 않겠습니다. 지금으로선 둘 중에 제가 더 가진 사람일 테니까요. 그리고 나리의 결심이 나아가는 것이듯, 제 결심은 나리께서 가시면 따르는 것입니다. 아니, 이제는 나리를 보살피기 위해 제가 곁에 있는 것이 반드시 필요해 보입니다. 나리의 작정이 그토록 무모하니 말입니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만 제 속셈은 훨씬 더 신중하지요. 나리께서 할 수만 있다면 싸우다 쓰러지기로 작정하셨듯, 저는 할 수만 있다면 조금도 다치지 않기로 그만큼 굳게 작정했으니까요. 그리고 사실, 위험은 거의 없으리라 생각하니 마음이 놓입니다. 며칠 전 어느 가톨릭 신부가 제게 말하기를, 이 일은 곧 끝날 거고, 자기 생각엔 싸움도 없이 끝나리라더군요.\" \"가톨릭 신부라!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"내가 듣기로 그런 자들은 제 종교를 위해 말할 때 늘 믿을 만한 건 아니라던데.\" \"예, 하지만 그 점으로 말하면,\" 상대가 대답했다. \"그 신부는 자기 종교를 위해 말하기는커녕, 가톨릭 신도들은 이번 변화로 무얼 얻으리라 기대하지도 않는다고 확언했습니다. 찰스 왕자가 잉글랜드의 어느 누구 못지않게 훌륭한 신교도이고, 그분과 가톨릭 일파가 자코바이트가 된 것은 오로지 정당한 권리를 존중해서라는 겁니다.\"—\"내가 그자에게 무슨 권리가 있다고 믿는 만큼만 그가 신교도라고 믿겠네.\" 존스가 말했다. \"우리가 이기리라는 것은 의심치 않지만, 싸움 없이는 아니야. 그러니 나는 자네 친구인 그 가톨릭 신부만큼 낙관하지는 않네.\" \"아니, 정말입니다, 나리.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"제가 읽어본 예언이란 예언은 죄다 이 싸움에서 엄청난 피가 흐를 거라고 말하더군요. 지금 살아 있는 엄지가 셋 달린 방앗간 주인이 무릎까지 피에 잠긴 채 세 임금의 말고삐를 잡게 된다지요. 주여, 우리 모두를 불쌍히 여기시고, 더 좋은 시절을 보내주소서!\" \"어떻게 그런 헛소리며 말도 안 되는 것들로 머릿속을 채웠나!\" 존스가 대답했다. \"이것도 그 가톨릭 신부한테서 나온 거겠지. 괴물이며 흉조 따위는 괴물 같고 터무니없는 교설을 떠받치기에 딱 맞는 근거니까. 조지 왕의 대의는 자유와 참된 종교의 대의일세. 다시 말해, 이 사람아, 그건 상식의 대의야. 브리아레우스 본인이 엄지 백 개를 달고 되살아나 방앗간 주인 노릇을 한다 해도, 우리가 이길 거라고 내가 장담하지.\" 파트리지는 여기에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 실은 존스의 이 선언에 그는 극도의 혼란에 빠지고 말았다. 독자에게 한 가지 비밀을 알려드리자면—여태 적절히 밝힐 기회가 없었던 비밀인데—파트리지는 사실 자코바이트였고, 존스도 같은 일파이며 지금 반군에 합류하러 가는 길이라고 결론지었던 것이다. 그런 생각에는 근거가 없지도 않았다. 허디브라스가 언급한 그 키 크고 옆구리 긴 여인—눈도 많고, 혀도 많고, 입도 많고, 귀도 많은 베르길리우스의 그 괴물이, 존스와 그 장교 사이에 벌어진 다툼 이야기를, 진실에 대한 여느 때만큼의 존중을 담아 퍼뜨렸던 것이다. 그 여인은 실로 소피아라는 이름을 왕위 참칭자의 이름으로 바꿔치기했고, 존스가 얻어맞은 것은 그자의 건강을 위해 축배를 든 탓이라고 전했다. 이 이야기를 파트리지는 들었고, 더없이 굳게 믿었다. 그러니 그가 거기서 존스에 대해 앞서 말한 그런 생각을 품게 된 것도 이상할 게 없으며, 자기 착각을 깨닫기 전까지 하마터면 그 생각을 존스에게 들킬 뻔했던 것이다. 이에 대해서도 독자는 덜 놀라게 될 것이다. 존스가 자신의 결심을 파트리지 씨에게 처음 알릴 때 썼던 그 모호한 표현을 떠올려본다면 말이다. 사실, 그 말이 덜 애매했더라도 파트리지는 자기 식대로 해석했을 법하다. 그는 온 나라가 마음속으로는 같은 성향이라고 굳게 믿고 있었으니까. 존스가 병사들과 함께 여행했다는 사실도 그의 믿음을 흔들지 못했다. 그는 군대에 대해서도 나머지 백성에 대해 가진 것과 똑같은 생각을 품고 있었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 그가 제임스나 찰스에게 아무리 호의를 품고 있었다 한들, 그는 그 둘 중 누구보다 어린 벤저민에게 훨씬 더 애착이 있었다. 그런 까닭에 그는 동행자의 신조를 알아채자마자, 자기 출세를 의탁한 그 사람 앞에서 제 신조를 감추고 겉으로는 내려놓는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다. 그도 그럴 것이 그는 존스의 처지가 올워디 씨와의 관계에서 실제만큼 절망적이라고는 결코 믿지 않았던 것이다. 그 고장을 떠난 뒤로도 몇몇 이웃과 꾸준히 편지를 주고받아 온 터라, 그는 올워디 씨가 이 젊은이에게 품은 깊은 애정에 관해 많은 이야기를, 실로 사실보다 더 부풀려진 이야기를 들어왔다. 파트리지가 전해 들은 바로는 그 젊은이가 그 신사의 상속자가 될 사람이었고, 또 앞서 말했듯 파트리지는 그가 자기 아들임을 추호도 의심하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n그러므로 그는 두 사람 사이에 어떤 다툼이 있었든, 존스 씨가 돌아가면 반드시 화해가 이루어지리라 생각했다. 그리고 그 일에서 큰 이득을 기대했으니, 이번 기회에 그 젊은 신사의 환심을 살 수 있다면, 그리고 어떻게든 그가 돌아가게 하는 데 한몫할 수 있다면, 앞서 말한 대로 그것이 올워디 씨의 호의 속에서 자신을 그만큼 높여주리라 의심치 않았던 것이다.\n\n우리는 이미 그가 매우 마음씨 고운 사람임을 살펴보았고, 그 자신도 존스의 인품과 사람됨에 대한 격렬한 애착을 털어놓은 바 있다. 하지만 어쩌면 내가 방금 언급한 그 속셈들 역시, 그로 하여금 이 원정에 나서도록—적어도 주인과 자신이, 신중한 어떤 아버지와 아들들처럼, 더없이 다정하게 함께 길을 가면서도 서로 반대 당파를 끌어안고 있음을 알아챈 뒤에도 그것을 계속하도록 다그치는 데—작으나마 한몫했을 것이다. 내가 이런 추측에 이르게 된 것은, 사랑이며 우정이며 존경이며 그 비슷한 것들이 인간의 마음속에서 매우 강력하게 작용하기는 하나, 지혜로운 자들이 남을 제 뜻대로 부리려 할 때 좀처럼 빠뜨리지 않는 재료가 바로 이익이라는 점을 눈여겨보았기 때문이다. 이것이야말로 실로 더없이 탁월한 약이어서, 워드의 환약처럼, 혀든 손이든 그 밖의 어느 부위든 당신이 작용시키고 싶은 바로 그 신체 부위로 단번에 날아가, 거기서 원하는 효과를 즉각 내는 데 좀처럼 실패하는 법이 없다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n우리의 여행자들이 매우 기이한 모험을 만나는 이야기.\n\n앞 장에서 존스와 그의 벗이 막 대화를 끝낼 무렵, 두 사람은 몹시 가파른 언덕 기슭에 다다랐다. 여기서 존스는 우뚝 걸음을 멈추고는 눈을 위로 향한 채 한동안 말없이 서 있었다. 마침내 그는 동행을 불러 이렇게 말했다. \"파트리지, 이 언덕 꼭대기에 올라가 보고 싶군. 거기서는 분명 더없이 아름다운 경치가 펼쳐질 거야. 특히 이런 달빛 아래서는 말이지. 달이 모든 사물 위에 드리우는 그 엄숙한 어스름은 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 아름다워. 더구나 우울한 상념을 가꾸고 싶어 하는 상상력에는 더더욱 그렇지.\" \"그럴 수도 있겠지요.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"하지만 언덕 꼭대기가 우울한 생각을 자아내기에 가장 알맞은 곳이라면, 그 아래쪽은 즐거운 생각을 자아내기에 가장 알맞은 곳일 테고, 둘 중에는 단연 이쪽이 훨씬 낫지 않겠습니까. 솔직히 말씀드리면 그 산꼭대기 이야기를 꺼내는 것만으로도 제 피가 다 식어 버렸습니다. 제 눈에는 세상에서 가장 높은 산 중 하나처럼 보이는걸요. 아니, 아닙니다. 무언가를 찾으시려거든, 차라리 이 추위를 피할 땅 밑 자리나 찾아보시지요.\" \"그렇게 하게.\" 존스가 말했다. \"다만 이곳에서 부르면 들릴 만한 곳에 있게나. 내가 돌아오는 길에 자네를 부를 테니.\" \"이런, 나리, 정신이 나가신 건 아니시지요.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"정말로 그렇다네.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"이 언덕을 오르는 게 미친 짓이라면 말일세. 하지만 자네가 벌써부터 그토록 추위를 호소하니, 아래에 남아 있게나. 한 시간 안에 반드시 자네에게 돌아오겠네.\" \"용서하십시오, 나리.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"저는 나리가 어디를 가시든 따라가기로 마음먹었습니다.\" 사실 그는 이제 뒤에 남는 게 두려웠다. 그는 모든 면에서 충분히 겁쟁이였지만, 그가 가장 두려워하는 것은 유령이었고, 지금 이 밤 시각과 황량한 장소는 그 두려움과 더없이 잘 어울렸기 때문이다.\n\n바로 그 순간, 파트리지는 나무들 사이로 가물거리는 불빛 하나를 발견했는데, 그것은 그들에게 아주 가까이 있는 듯했다. 그는 곧장 환희에 차서 외쳤다. \"오, 나리! 마침내 하늘이 제 기도를 들으시고 우리를 어느 집으로 이끄셨습니다. 어쩌면 여관일지도 모르지요. 부디 간청드립니다, 나리, 저든 나리 자신이든 조금이라도 가엾이 여기는 마음이 있으시거든, 하늘의 은혜를 저버리지 마시고 저 불빛 쪽으로 곧장 가십시다. 그곳이 술집이든 아니든, 거기 사는 이들이 그리스도인이라면 우리처럼 비참한 처지에 놓인 사람들에게 잠시 집 한 귀퉁이쯤 내어 주기를 거절하지는 않을 겁니다.\" 존스는 마침내 파트리지의 간곡한 애원에 굴복했고, 두 사람은 함께 불빛이 새어 나오는 곳을 향해 곧장 나아갔다.\n\n그들은 곧 이 집, 아니 오두막이라 해도 좋을, 어느 쪽으로 불러도 크게 어긋나지 않을 그곳의 문 앞에 다다랐다. 여기서 존스는 여러 번 문을 두드렸으나 안에서는 아무런 대답도 없었다. 그러자 머릿속이 유령이며 악마며 마녀 같은 것들로 가득 차 있던 파트리지가 떨기 시작하며 외쳤다. \"주여, 저희에게 자비를 베푸소서! 분명 사람들이 다 죽은 게 틀림없습니다. 이제는 불빛도 보이지 않는걸요. 그런데도 저는 바로 조금 전에 촛불이 타는 걸 분명히 보았단 말입니다. 아이고! 저는 이런 일에 대해 들어 본 적이 있습니다.\" \"무엇에 대해 들었다는 건가?\" 존스가 말했다. \"사람들이 깊이 잠들었거나, 아니면 여기가 외진 곳이니 아마도 문을 여는 게 무서운 거겠지.\" 그러고는 꽤 큰 소리로 외치기 시작했고, 마침내 한 늙은 여인이 위층 여닫이창을 열고 누구냐고, 무엇을 원하느냐고 물었다. 존스가 대답하기를, 자기들은 길을 잃은 여행자들인데, 창문에 불빛이 보이기에 몸을 녹일 불이라도 찾을까 하는 마음에 이리로 이끌려 왔노라고 했다. \"당신들이 누구든,\" 여인이 외쳤다. \"여기 볼일은 없을 텐데. 이런 밤 시각에 누구에게도 문을 열어 줄 수는 없소.\" 사람의 목소리를 듣고 겁에서 어느 정도 벗어난 파트리지는, 잠깐만이라도 불 곁에 들여 달라며 더없이 간곡하게 애원하기 시작했다. 추위로 거의 죽을 지경이라는 것이었는데, 사실 그 추위에는 두려움도 서리만큼이나 한몫하고 있었다. 그는 자기에게 말을 건 신사가 이 고장에서 손꼽히는 대지주 중 한 분이라고 그녀에게 장담했고, 단 하나만 빼고 온갖 논리를 다 동원했다. 그 하나는 나중에 존스가 효과적으로 보탰으니, 바로 반 크라운을 주겠다는 약속이었다. 그런 사람에게는 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 뇌물이었다. 더구나 달빛이 그녀에게 또렷이 드러내 준 존스의 점잖은 차림새와 그의 상냥한 태도가, 처음에 그녀가 품었던 도둑에 대한 경계심을 완전히 가라앉힌 터였다. 그리하여 그녀는 마침내 두 사람을 들이기로 했고, 파트리지는 자기를 맞이할 준비가 된 따뜻한 불을 보고 더없이 기뻐했다.\n\n그러나 이 가엾은 사내는 몸을 녹이자마자, 늘 머릿속을 가장 크게 차지하고 있던 생각들이 다시 그의 머리를 조금씩 어지럽히기 시작했다. 그의 신조 가운데 마법보다 더 굳게 믿는 항목은 없었으니, 지금 그의 앞에 서 있는 늙은 여인보다 그런 생각을 불러일으키기에 더 알맞은 형상은 독자도 떠올리기 어려울 것이다. 그녀는 오트웨이가 그의 작품 《고아》에서 그린 그 그림에 정확히 들어맞았다. 실로 이 여인이 제임스 1세 치세에 살았더라면, 그 생김새 하나만으로도 거의 아무런 증거 없이 교수형에 처해졌을 것이다.\n\n여러 정황 또한 파트리지의 생각을 굳히는 데 한몫했다. 그가 그때 짐작하기로 그녀는 이토록 외진 곳에서 홀로 살고 있었고, 게다가 겉모습은 그녀에게 어울리지 않게 너무 좋아 보이는 집인데도 그 안은 더없이 정갈하고 우아하게 꾸며져 있었다. 사실대로 말하자면 존스 자신도 눈앞의 광경에 적잖이 놀랐다. 방이 유난히 단정한 데다, 애호가의 관심을 끌 만한 수많은 진기한 물건과 골동품으로 장식되어 있었기 때문이다.\n\n존스가 이 물건들에 감탄하고, 파트리지가 자기가 마녀의 집에 있다고 굳게 믿으며 떨고 앉아 있는 동안, 늙은 여인이 말했다. \"신사 양반들, 부디 되도록 서둘러 주셨으면 합니다. 곧 주인 어른이 오실 텐데, 두 배 돈을 준다 해도 이분이 여러분을 여기서 보시게 하고 싶지는 않거든요.\" \"그러면 주인이 있단 말이오?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"이거 정말 미안하오, 착한 부인. 하지만 댁에 저 좋은 물건들이 다 있는 걸 보고 놀랐던 거요.\" \"아이고, 나리.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 물건들의 스무 분의 일이라도 제 것이라면 저는 제가 부자라고 여길 겁니다. 하지만 부디, 나리, 너무 오래 머무르지 마세요. 매 순간 그분이 오시기를 기다리는 처지니까요.\" \"아니, 설마 흔한 자선을 베푼 일로 부인에게 화를 내지는 않을 텐데.\" 존스가 말했다. \"아이고 맙소사, 나리!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그분은 이상한 분이라, 다른 사람들과는 전혀 다릅니다. 누구와도 어울리지 않고, 좀처럼 밖에 나가는 일도 없으며, 나가더라도 밤에만 나가시지요. 남에게 보이는 걸 싫어하시니까요. 그리고 온 고장 사람들도 그분과 마주치는 걸 그만큼 두려워합니다. 그분의 차림새는 익숙하지 않은 이들을 겁먹게 하기에 충분하거든요. 사람들은 그분을 '언덕의 사나이'라고 부른답니다(밤이면 거기를 거니시니까요). 제 생각에 고장 사람들은 악마 그 자체보다 그분을 덜 두려워하지는 않을 거예요. 여러분을 여기서 보시면 무섭게 화를 내실 겁니다.\" \"제발, 나리,\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"저 어른의 심기를 거스르지 맙시다. 저는 걸을 채비가 되어 있고, 평생 이보다 더 따뜻했던 적이 없습니다. 제발, 나리, 갑시다. 저기 굴뚝 위에 권총이 있습니다. 장전이 되어 있는지 아닌지 누가 알겠으며, 그분이 그걸로 무슨 짓을 할지 누가 알겠습니까?\" \"아무것도 두려워 말게, 파트리지.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"내가 자네를 위험으로부터 지켜 주겠네.\" \"아니, 그 일이라면, 그분이 누구를 해코지하는 일은 결코 없답니다.\" 여인이 말했다. \"하지만 분명 자기 안전을 위해 무기를 좀 갖추어 둘 필요가 있긴 하지요. 이 집이 한두 번 습격당한 게 아니거든요. 도둑이 집 주변을 어슬렁거린다고 우리가 여긴 게 불과 며칠 밤 전 일입니다. 저로 말하자면, 그분이 그런 시각에 홀로 거닐면서도 어떤 악당에게든 살해당하지 않은 게 종종 신기할 따름이에요. 하지만 아까 말씀드렸듯이, 사람들이 그분을 무서워하는 데다, 짐작건대 그분에게 가져갈 만한 물건이 없다고들 여기는 거겠지요.\" \"이 진기한 물건들로 미루어 보건대,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"댁의 주인은 여행을 많이 다닌 분인가 보군.\" \"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그분은 아주 대단한 여행가셨지요. 온갖 일에 그분보다 더 많이 아는 신사는 드물 겁니다. 제 짐작으로는 사랑에 어긋난 일이 있었거나 뭐 그런 게 아닌가 싶은데, 무엇인지는 저도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 저는 이분과 삼십 년 넘게 함께 살아왔는데, 그 긴 세월 동안 그분이 살아 있는 사람 여섯 명과도 채 말을 나누지 않았답니다.\" 그러고서 그녀는 다시 그들의 출발을 재촉했고, 파트리지가 거기에 가세했다. 그러나 존스는 일부러 시간을 끌었으니, 이 비범한 사람을 보고 싶은 호기심이 크게 일었기 때문이다. 그리하여 늙은 여인은 대답마다 끝에 어서 가 달라는 말을 붙였고, 파트리지는 그의 소매를 잡아당기는 데까지 이르렀지만, 그는 여전히 새로운 질문을 짜내며 머물렀다. 마침내 늙은 여인이 겁에 질린 얼굴로 주인의 신호 소리가 들린다고 말했고, 바로 그 순간 문밖에서 한 사람 이상의 목소리가 들려왔다. \"이 피도 안 마른 놈아, 당장 돈을 내놔. 돈을 내놓으라고, 이 악당아, 안 그러면 네 골통을 날려 버릴 테다.\"\n\n\"오, 맙소사!\" 늙은 여인이 외쳤다. \"분명 어떤 악당들이 주인 어른을 덮친 거예요. 아이고! 어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아?\" \"어떻게!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"어떻게! 이 권총들은 장전되어 있소?\" \"오, 착하신 나리, 정말 그 안엔 아무것도 없어요. 오, 제발 저희를 죽이지 마세요, 나리들!\"(사실 그녀는 이제 안에 있는 자들에 대해서도 밖에 있는 자들에 대해 품었던 것과 똑같은 생각을 하고 있었던 것이다.) 존스는 그녀에게 대꾸도 하지 않고, 방에 걸려 있던 낡은 폭검 하나를 낚아채서는 곧장 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 거기서 그는 늙은 신사가 두 악당과 몸싸움을 벌이며 자비를 구걸하고 있는 것을 보았다. 존스는 아무것도 묻지 않고 폭검을 들고 어찌나 날쌔게 일에 나섰던지, 그자들은 곧장 붙잡았던 손을 놓았다. 그리고 우리의 주인공을 감히 공격하려 들지도 못한 채 줄행랑을 쳐 달아났다. 존스는 그들을 뒤쫓으려 하지 않았으니, 늙은 신사를 구해 낸 것으로 만족했기 때문이다. 실로 그는 그자들을 꽤 단단히 손봐 주었다고 결론지었는데, 둘 다 달아나면서 자기들은 죽은 목숨이라고 모진 욕설과 함께 외쳐 댔기 때문이다.\n\n존스는 곧장 달려가 몸싸움 중에 쓰러졌던 늙은 신사를 일으켜 세우면서, 혹시 그가 악당들에게 무슨 해라도 입지 않았을까 깊은 염려를 드러냈다. 노인은 한순간 존스를 빤히 쳐다보더니 외쳤다. \"아니오, 선생, 아니오, 다친 데는 거의 없소이다, 고맙소. 주여, 제게 자비를 베푸소서!\" \"보아하니, 선생,\" 존스가 말했다. \"당신을 구해 드리는 행운을 누린 이들에 대해서조차 경계를 풀지 못하시는군요. 당신이 품으실 어떤 의심도 나무랄 수는 없습니다만, 사실 그러실 실제 까닭은 전혀 없습니다. 여기 있는 이들은 당신의 벗들뿐이니까요. 이 추운 밤에 길을 잃은 우리가 외람되게도 당신의 불 곁에서 몸을 녹이고 막 떠나려던 참에, 당신이 도움을 청하는 소리를 들었습니다. 분명히 말씀드리건대, 그 외침은 오직 하늘의 섭리가 당신에게 보낸 것 같습니다.\" \"섭리라, 정말이지,\" 늙은 신사가 외쳤다. \"그게 사실이라면 말이오.\" \"사실입니다, 분명히 말씀드리지요.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"여기 당신의 검을 받으십시오, 선생. 당신을 지키는 데 썼으니, 이제 다시 당신 손에 돌려드립니다.\" 노인은 적들의 피로 얼룩진 검을 받아 들고는 한동안 존스를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 한숨을 내쉬며 외쳤다. \"용서하시오, 젊은 양반. 나도 늘 의심 많은 성미였던 건 아니며, 배은망덕을 가까이하는 사람도 아니라오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 감사하십시오,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"당신의 구원을 빚진 그 섭리에 말입니다. 제 몫으로 말하자면, 저는 인간으로서의 흔한 도리를 다했을 뿐이며, 당신과 같은 처지의 어떤 동료에게라도 했을 일을 했을 따름입니다.\" \"당신을 좀 더 보게 해 주시오,\" 늙은 신사가 외쳤다. \"그러면 당신은 사람이란 말이오? 음, 그럴지도 모르겠구려. 자, 부디 내 작은 오두막으로 들어오시오. 당신은 정말로 나의 구원자였소이다.\"\n\n늙은 여인은 주인에 대한 두려움과 주인을 위한 두려움 사이에서 정신이 없었고, 파트리지는 그게 가능하다면 그보다 더 큰 공포에 빠져 있었다. 그러나 늙은 여인은 주인이 존스에게 다정하게 말하는 것을 듣고 무슨 일이 있었는지 알아차리자 다시 제정신을 차렸다. 하지만 파트리지는 그 신사를 보자마자, 그 차림새의 기이함이 이 가엾은 사내에게 앞서 느꼈던 것보다 더 큰 공포를 불어넣었다. 들었던 그 기이한 묘사로 인한 것이든, 문 앞에서 벌어진 그 소동으로 인한 것이든 말이다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 그것은 파트리지보다 훨씬 더 굳건한 마음이라도 동요시켰을 법한 행색이었다. 이 사람은 키가 더없이 컸고, 눈처럼 새하얀 긴 수염을 기르고 있었다. 그의 몸은 나귀 가죽으로 만든, 외투 비슷한 형태로 다듬은 것을 걸치고 있었다. 또한 그는 다리에 장화를 신고 머리에 모자를 썼는데, 둘 다 어떤 다른 짐승의 가죽으로 지은 것이었다.\n\n늙은 신사가 집 안으로 들어서자마자, 늙은 여인은 그가 악당들에게서 무사히 벗어난 것을 축하하기 시작했다. \"그래,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"정말로 벗어났지, 나를 구해 준 분 덕분에.\" \"오, 그분에게 축복이 있기를!\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"장담컨대 좋은 신사 양반이에요. 그분을 들였다고 나리께서 제게 화를 내시지 않을까 걱정했답니다. 분명 저도 그러지 않았을 거예요. 달빛에 그분이 신사인 데다 추위에 거의 얼어 죽을 지경인 걸 보지 못했더라면요. 그리고 분명 그분을 이리로 보내고 제가 그러도록 이끈 건 어떤 착한 천사였을 거예요.\"\n\n\"미안하오, 선생,\" 늙은 신사가 존스에게 말했다. \"이 집에는 당신이 먹거나 마실 만한 게 아무것도 없소이다. 브랜디 한 잔이라도 받아 주신다면 모를까. 더없이 훌륭한 걸로 좀 드릴 수 있는데, 삼십 년이나 곁에 두어 온 것이라오.\" 존스는 매우 정중하고 적절한 말로 이 제안을 사양했다. 그러자 노인은 그에게 길을 잃었을 때 어디로 가는 길이었느냐고 물으며 말했다. \"당신 같은 사람이 이런 밤 시각에 걸어서 여행하는 걸 보니 솔직히 놀랐다고 인정하지 않을 수 없구려. 짐작건대, 선생, 당신은 이 근방의 신사겠지요. 말도 없이 먼 길을 다니는 데 익숙한 사람으로는 보이지 않으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"겉모습이란,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"흔히 사람을 속이는 법이지요. 사람은 때로 실제와 다른 모습으로 보이니까요. 분명히 말씀드리지만 저는 이 고장 사람이 아닙니다. 그리고 제가 어디로 가고 있는지는, 실은 저 자신도 거의 알지 못합니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 누구든, 어디로 가고 있든,\" 노인이 대답했다. \"나는 당신에게 결코 갚을 수 없는 신세를 졌소이다.\"\n\n\"다시 한 번 말씀드리지만,\" 존스가 답했다. \"당신은 제게 아무 신세도 지지 않으셨습니다. 제가 가치를 두지 않는 것을 당신을 위해 걸었다고 해서 무슨 공이 있을 수 있겠습니까. 그리고 제 눈에 목숨만큼 하찮은 것은 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"안타깝구려, 젊은 양반,\" 낯선 이가 대답했다. \"그 나이에 그토록 불행할 까닭이 있다니 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다, 선생.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"저는 인류 가운데 가장 불행한 사람입니다.\" \"혹시 벗이나 연인이 있었던 게요?\" 상대가 물었다. \"어떻게,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"저를 미칠 지경으로 몰아넣기에 충분한 두 마디를 입에 올리실 수 있단 말입니까?\" \"그 둘 중 어느 하나라도 어떤 사람이든 미칠 지경으로 몰아넣기에 충분하지요.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"더는 묻지 않겠소, 선생. 아마 내 호기심이 이미 너무 멀리 나아간 듯하구려.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 선생,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"저는 바로 이 순간 더없이 강하게 느끼고 있는 그 마음을 나무랄 수 없습니다. 이 집에 처음 들어선 뒤로 제가 보고 들은 모든 것이 제 안에 가장 큰 호기심을 불러일으켰다고 분명히 말씀드리면, 당신은 저를 용서해 주실 겁니다. 무언가 매우 비범한 일이 당신을 이런 삶의 방식으로 이끌었을 게 틀림없고, 저는 당신 자신의 이야기에도 불행이 없지 않을 거라 짐작할 까닭이 있습니다.\"\n\n여기서 늙은 신사는 다시 한숨을 내쉬며 몇 분간 말이 없었다. 마침내 그는 존스를 진지하게 바라보며 말했다. \"선한 인상은 추천장과 같다고 어디선가 읽은 적이 있소. 만일 그렇다면, 당신만큼 강력하게 추천받을 만한 사람은 없을 게요. 만약 내가 또 다른 까닭으로 당신에게 어떤 끌림을 느끼지 못한다면, 나는 이 땅에서 가장 배은망덕한 괴물일 테지요. 그리고 내가 말 외의 다른 방법으로는 당신에게 내 감사를 보일 길이 없다는 게 정말이지 마음에 걸리는구려.\"\n\n존스는 잠시 망설이다가 대답하기를, 말로도 자기를 더없이 흡족하게 해 주실 수 있다고 했다. \"저는 호기심을 고백했습니다, 선생.\" 그가 말했다. \"만일 당신이 황송하게도 그것을 풀어 주신다면 제가 얼마나 감사하겠는지, 굳이 말씀드릴 필요가 있겠습니까? 그러니 어떤 사정이 당신을 막지 않는다면, 부디 청하건대, 무슨 동기로 이렇게 인류 사회에서 물러나 보아하니 당신이 타고나지 않은 게 분명한 삶의 방식에 몸을 맡기게 되셨는지 제게 알려 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그동안 있었던 일을 생각하면, 당신에게 무엇이든 거절할 자유가 내게 있다고는 여기기 어렵구려.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"그러니 불행한 한 사람의 이야기를 듣고 싶다면, 당신에게 들려드리리다. 실로 당신의 판단이 옳소. 사회에서 달아나는 자들의 운명에는 흔히 무언가 비범한 게 있다고 생각하다니 말이오. 역설처럼, 아니 모순처럼 보일지 몰라도, 분명한 사실은 위대한 박애야말로 무엇보다 우리를 인류로부터 멀어지고 그들을 혐오하게 만든다는 것이오. 그것은 그들의 사사롭고 이기적인 악덕 때문이라기보다는, 관계에서 비롯되는 종류의 악덕, 이를테면 시기, 악의, 배신, 잔인함을 비롯한 온갖 다른 종류의 악의 때문이오. 이것들이야말로 참된 박애가 혐오하는 악덕이며, 박애는 그것들을 보고 더불어 이야기하느니 차라리 사회 그 자체를 피해 버린다오. 하지만 당신에게 빈말을 하려는 게 아니라, 내 보기에 당신은 내가 피하거나 혐오할 사람들 가운데 하나가 아닌 듯하오. 아니, 말하건대, 당신에게서 흘러나온 그 얼마 안 되는 말 속에 우리 둘의 운명에 어떤 닮은 데가 있어 보이는구려. 다만 당신의 운명은 더 성공적으로 끝맺기를 바라오.\"\n\n여기서 우리의 주인공과 그의 주인 사이에 몇 마디 인사치레가 오갔고, 그런 뒤 주인이 막 자기 이야기를 시작하려는데 파트리지가 그를 가로막았다. 그의 두려움은 이제 어지간히 가셨으나, 그 공포의 여운이 얼마간 남아 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 그는 신사에게 아까 언급했던 그 훌륭한 브랜디를 일깨워 주었다. 브랜디는 곧 나왔고, 파트리지는 큰 잔으로 가득 들이켰다.\n\n그러고서 신사는 더 이상의 서두 없이, 다음 장에서 읽게 될 대로 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n산속 사람이 자신의 이야기를 들려주기 시작하다.\n\n\"나는 1657년, 서머싯셔의 마크라는 마을에서 태어났습니다. 아버지는 사람들이 흔히 신사 농부라 부르는 부류였지요. 연 300파운드쯤 되는 자그마한 토지를 직접 소유하고, 거기에 거의 같은 값어치의 다른 토지를 빌려 농사를 지었습니다. 신중하고 부지런했으며, 농사 솜씨가 어찌나 좋았던지, 지독한 암여우 같은 아내가 집안의 평온을 들쑤시지만 않았다면 아주 편안하고 안락한 삶을 누릴 수 있었을 겁니다. 그러나 이런 사정이 아버지를 비참하게 만들었을지언정 가난하게 만들지는 않았습니다. 아버지는 어머니를 거의 집 안에만 붙들어 두었고, 어머니가 바깥에서 부리고 싶어 하던 사치에 재산을 축내느니 차라리 집 안에서 끝없는 잔소리를 견디는 쪽을 택했으니까요.\n\n\"이 크산티페에게서—\" (소크라테스의 아내가 그렇게 불렸지요, 하고 파트리지가 끼어들었다) \"—이 크산티페에게서 아버지는 두 아들을 두었는데, 내가 동생이었습니다. 아버지는 우리 둘 다 좋은 교육을 시키려 했지요. 그러나 형은, 자기에게는 불행하게도 어머니의 총애를 한 몸에 받던 터라, 공부를 아예 내팽개치고 말았습니다. 어찌나 그랬던지, 대여섯 해를 학교에 다니고도 거의 늘지를 않으니, 결국 선생이 더 붙들어 두어 봐야 소용없다고 아버지에게 말하기에 이르렀습니다. 마침내 아버지는 어머니의 뜻에 따라 형을 그 폭군의 손에서—어머니는 선생을 그렇게 불렀지요—집으로 데려왔습니다. 사실 그 선생은 형의 게으름이 마땅히 받아야 할 것보다 훨씬 덜 매를 들었는데도, 정작 도련님 입맛에는 너무 가혹했던 모양이라, 형은 늘 가혹한 대우를 받는다며 어머니에게 일러바쳤고, 어머니는 또 한결같이 그 말을 들어주곤 했습니다.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 맞아.\" 하고 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"나도 그런 어머니들을 본 적이 있지요. 나 자신도 그런 이들에게 부당하게 욕을 먹은 적이 있고요. 그런 부모는 제 자식만큼이나 매를 맞아 마땅합니다.\"\n\n존스가 끼어든다고 선생을 나무라자, 낯선 이는 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"이제 형은 열다섯 나이에 모든 배움에, 아니 개와 총 말고는 다른 모든 것에 작별을 고했습니다. 그중 총 솜씨는 어찌나 능숙해졌던지, 어쩌면 믿기 어려우실지 모르겠습니다만, 멈춰 있는 표적을 아주 정확히 맞히는 것은 물론이요, 공중을 날아가는 까마귀를 실제로 쏘아 떨어뜨릴 정도였습니다. 또 앉아 있는 토끼를 찾아내는 데도 능했고, 머지않아 이 고장에서 손꼽히는 사냥꾼으로 이름을 날렸습니다. 형도 어머니도 그 명성을, 마치 형이 가장 빼어난 학자로 여겨지기라도 한 양 한껏 즐겼지요.\n\n\"형의 처지를 보면서 처음에는 학교에 계속 매여 있는 내 신세가 더 고달프다고 여겼습니다. 그러나 곧 생각이 바뀌었지요. 나는 배움에서 제법 빠르게 나아갔기에 공부가 수월해졌고, 그 익힘이 어찌나 즐거웠던지 도리어 방학이 가장 괴로운 때가 되었으니까요. 나를 한 번도 사랑한 적 없던 어머니는, 이제 아버지의 애정을 내가 더 많이 차지한다고 여기고, 학식 있는 몇몇 신사들, 특히 교구 목사가 형보다 나를 더 눈여겨본다는 사실을 알게 되자—적어도 그렇게 생각하게 되자—내 얼굴 보는 것조차 미워하게 되었고, 집을 어찌나 견디기 싫은 곳으로 만들었던지, 학생들이 흔히 '검은 월요일'이라 부르는 그날이 내게는 한 해 통틀어 가장 하얗고 반가운 날이 되었습니다.\n\n\"마침내 톤턴의 학교 과정을 모두 마치자, 나는 거기서 옥스퍼드의 엑서터 칼리지로 옮겨가 네 해를 머물렀습니다. 그 끝에 한 사건이 나를 학업에서 완전히 떼어 놓았지요. 그러니 그 후 내 인생에 일어난 모든 일의 시작을, 나는 진정 그때로 잡을 수 있습니다.\n\n\"나와 같은 칼리지에 조지 그레셤 경이라는 젊은이가 있었습니다. 상당한 재산을 물려받을 자격이 있었지만, 아버지의 유언에 따라 스물다섯이 되기 전에는 그 재산을 온전히 손에 넣을 수 없는 사람이었지요. 그렇지만 후견인들의 후한 씀씀이 덕에, 그는 아버지가 베푼 그 넘치는 신중함을 아쉬워할 까닭이 거의 없었습니다. 후견인들은 그가 대학에 머무는 동안 연 500파운드를 내주었고, 그는 그 돈으로 말과 정부를 거느리며, 제 재산을 완전히 제 마음대로 주물렀더라도 더 나빴을 리 없을 만큼 사악하고 방탕한 삶을 살았으니까요. 후견인들에게서 받는 연 500파운드 말고도, 그는 천 파운드를 더 쓸 방도를 찾아냈습니다. 스물한 살이 넘은 나이라, 마음껏 외상을 얻는 데에도 아무 어려움이 없었지요.\n\n\"이 젊은이는 그럭저럭 봐줄 만한 여러 악덕 가운데, 그야말로 악마 같은 하나를 지니고 있었습니다. 자기보다 형편이 못한 젊은이들을, 감당할 수 없는 씀씀이로 끌어들여 망치고 파멸시키는 데서 큰 기쁨을 느꼈던 것이지요. 그리고 어떤 젊은이가 더 착하고, 더 훌륭하고, 더 절제할수록, 그자는 그를 파멸시키는 데서 더 큰 쾌감과 승리감을 맛보았습니다. 그야말로 악마에 관해 기록된 그 모습 그대로, 누구를 삼킬까 찾아 헤매며 돌아다닌 셈이지요.\n\n\"이 신사와 알고 지내며 가까워진 것이 내 불행이었습니다. 학업에 부지런하다는 내 평판이 도리어 그의 못된 의도에 더없이 탐나는 먹잇감이 되게 했고, 내 천성 또한 그가 뜻을 이루기에 어렵잖게 만들어 주었지요. 나는 책에 열심히 매달렸고 거기서 큰 즐거움을 얻기도 했지만, 그보다 훨씬 더 큰 즐거움을 누릴 수 있는 다른 쾌락들도 있었으니까요. 나는 혈기왕성하고, 동물적 기운이 거세게 솟구쳤으며, 야심이 조금 있었고, 무엇보다 욕정이 지독했습니다.\n\n\"조지 경과 가까워진 지 얼마 되지 않아, 나는 그의 모든 쾌락에 함께하는 처지가 되었습니다. 그리고 일단 그 무대에 발을 들이고 나니, 내 성정도 내 기개도 결코 조연 노릇은 견디지 못했습니다. 어떤 방탕한 짓에서도 나는 그 무리 가운데 누구에게도 뒤지지 않았지요. 아니, 머지않아 온갖 난동과 소란에서 어찌나 두드러지게 이름을 떨쳤던지, 비행자 명단에는 늘 내 이름이 맨 앞에 올랐습니다. 그래서 조지 경의 불운한 제자라며 동정받기는커녕, 이제 나는 그 장래가 촉망되던 젊은 신사를 잘못 이끌어 타락시킨 장본인으로 고발당하는 처지가 되었습니다. 정작 모든 못된 짓의 우두머리이자 부추긴 자는 그였건만, 그렇게 여겨지는 일은 한 번도 없었지요. 나는 결국 부총장의 견책을 받았고, 퇴학을 아슬아슬하게 면했습니다.\n\n\"이런 삶, 지금 내가 늘어놓고 있는 이런 삶이 내 학업의 더 나은 진척과 양립할 수 없다는 것을, 선생도 쉬이 믿으시겠지요. 그리고 내가 헤픈 쾌락에 점점 더 빠져들수록, 학업에 쏟는 정성도 점점 더 게을러질 수밖에 없었다는 것도요. 정말 그런 결과가 따랐습니다. 그러나 그뿐이 아니었지요. 이제 내 씀씀이는 예전 수입은 물론, 머지않은 문학사 학위 준비에 돈이 필요하다는 핑계로 가엾고 너그러운 아버지에게서 뜯어낸 그 보탬마저도 훌쩍 넘어섰습니다. 그런데 이 요구가 마침내 어찌나 잦고 터무니없어졌던지, 아버지는 차츰 여러 군데서 들려오는 내 요즘 행실에 관한 이야기에 귀를 열기 시작했습니다. 어머니는 그 이야기를 아주 충실하고 요란하게 되울리는 일을 빠뜨리지 않았지요. 거기에 이렇게 덧붙이면서요. '암, 이게 바로 그 잘난 신사, 가문에 그토록 영광을 안기고 가문을 일으켜 세울 그 학자님이시지. 이 배움이란 게 다 어디로 갈지 내 진작 알아봤다니까. 결국 우리 모두를 망쳐 놓을 게야. 보아하니, 큰애가 동생 교육 마무리해 준답시고 필요한 것조차 못 받고 지냈는데, 그 대가로 우리한테 그런 이자를 갚아 준다더니, 그 이자란 게 어떻게 될지 내 진작 알아봤지.' 이런 식의 말이 훨씬 더 많았지만, 이 정도면 충분히 짐작하셨으리라 믿습니다.\n\n\"그리하여 아버지는 이제 내 요구에 돈 대신 훈계를 돌려보내기 시작했고, 그 때문에 내 형편이 어쩌면 조금 더 일찍 위기로 치달았는지도 모릅니다. 하지만 설령 아버지가 자기 수입을 몽땅 내게 부쳐 주었더라도, 조지 그레셤 경의 씀씀이를 따라가는 사람을 지탱하기에는 아주 짧은 동안밖에 못 버텼으리라는 걸 짐작하시겠지요.\n\n\"빚더미에 깊이 휘말려 도무지 빠져나올 가망이 보이지 않게 되기 전에 눈을 떴더라면, 지금 처한 이 돈에 쪼들리는 곤경과 이런 식으로는 더 못 버틴다는 막막함이 어쩌면 나를 단번에 제정신으로, 또 학업으로 되돌려 놓았을 법도 합니다. 사실 이것이야말로 조지 경의 대단한 수법이었고, 그것으로 그는 숱한 이들을 파멸시킨 뒤, 그의 표현을 빌리자면 자기 같은 재력가와 겨루었다며 그들을 바보요 멋쟁이 허풍선이라고 비웃었지요. 이 일을 꾸미려고 그는 이따금 자기 돈을 조금 내주어, 그 불운한 젊은이의 신용을 남들 앞에서 떠받쳐 주곤 했습니다. 그러다 바로 그 신용 때문에 젊은이는 돌이킬 수 없이 파멸하고 마는 것이지요.\n\n\"이런 식으로 내 마음이 내 형편만큼이나 절망에 빠지자, 거기서 벗어나 보려고 궁리하지 않은 악행이 거의 없을 지경이었습니다. 자살마저도 진지한 숙고의 대상이 되었고, 더 부끄러운, 그러나 어쩌면 덜 죄스러운 생각이 그것을 머릿속에서 몰아내지 않았다면 나는 분명 그렇게 작정했을 겁니다.\" 여기서 그는 잠시 머뭇거리더니 이윽고 외쳤다. \"단언컨대, 이토록 여러 해가 흘렀어도 이 짓의 수치만은 씻기지 않아서, 이야기하는 동안 얼굴이 붉어질 겁니다.\" 존스는 이야기하기에 괴로운 대목은 그냥 건너뛰라고 청했지만, 파트리지는 안달하며 외쳤다. \"오, 제발, 그건 꼭 들려주십시오. 나머지 다 합친 것보다 이게 더 듣고 싶습니다. 구원받기를 바라는 마음을 걸고, 절대 한마디도 발설하지 않겠습니다.\" 존스가 그를 나무라려는데, 낯선 이가 이렇게 이야기를 이어 가며 막았다. \"나에게는 한 방을 쓰는 동무가 있었는데, 아주 신중하고 검소한 젊은이였습니다. 그리 넉넉한 용돈을 받지도 않았건만, 알뜰함으로 마흔 기니가 넘는 돈을 모아, 책상 서랍에 넣어 둔 걸 내가 알고 있었지요. 그래서 나는 그가 잠든 틈을 타 바지 주머니에서 열쇠를 슬쩍 빼내, 그렇게 그의 모든 재물의 주인이 되었습니다. 그러고는 열쇠를 다시 그의 주머니에 넣어 두고 잠든 척했지요. 사실 나는 한순간도 눈을 붙이지 못했으면서도, 그가 일어나 기도하러 갈 때까지—오랫동안 손을 놓고 지내던 그 기도 말입니다—침대에 누워 있었습니다.\n\n\"겁 많은 도둑은 지나친 조심 탓에, 도리어 더 대담한 부류라면 피해 갈 발각을 자초하는 일이 많습니다. 내게도 바로 그런 일이 벌어졌지요. 만약 그의 책상을 대담하게 부수고 열었더라면, 어쩌면 의심조차 사지 않았을지 모릅니다. 그러나 자기 물건을 턴 자가 열쇠를 손에 넣었던 것이 분명했으니, 돈이 없어진 걸 처음 알아챘을 때 그는 한방 동무가 틀림없는 도둑이라고 의심치 않았던 것이지요. 그런데 그는 겁이 많은 성미인 데다, 힘으로는 나보다 한참 못했고, 내 생각엔 용기에서도 그러했기에, 더 나쁜 몸싸움이라도 벌어질까 두려워 감히 죄를 들이대며 나와 맞서지는 못했습니다. 그래서 그는 곧장 부총장에게 달려가, 도둑맞은 사실과 그 정황을 두고 맹세하고는, 이제 온 대학에 걸쳐 그토록 평판이 나빠진 사람을 잡을 영장을 아주 쉽게 받아냈습니다.\n\n\"다행스럽게도, 나는 그다음 날 저녁 칼리지 밖에서 묵었습니다. 그날 한 젊은 아가씨를 마차에 태우고 위트니까지 모셔 갔다가 거기서 밤을 지냈으니까요. 그리고 이튿날 아침 옥스퍼드로 돌아오는 길에 친구 하나를 만났는데, 그가 나에 관한 소식을 충분히 알려준 덕에 나는 말 머리를 다른 쪽으로 돌리게 되었습니다.\"\n\n\"여쭙는데, 그가 영장에 관한 이야기는 좀 했습니까?\" 하고 파트리지가 물었다. 그러나 존스는 신사에게 그런 주제넘은 질문일랑 개의치 말고 계속하라고 청했고, 그는 이렇게 이어 갔다.\n\n\"이제 옥스퍼드로 돌아갈 생각은 죄다 접고 나니, 다음으로 떠오른 것이 런던으로 가는 길이었습니다. 나는 이 뜻을 내 여자 동행에게 알렸는데, 그녀는 처음엔 반대했지만, 내가 가진 재물을 꺼내 보이자 곧바로 동의하더군요. 그래서 우리는 들판을 가로질러 사이런세스터로 이어지는 큰길로 나섰고, 어찌나 서둘렀던지 그다음다음 날 저녁을 런던에서 맞았습니다.\n\n\"내가 지금 와 있던 곳이 어떤 데였는지, 또 함께 어울리던 무리가 어떤 자들이었는지를 헤아려 보시면, 그토록 사악하게 손에 넣은 그 돈이 머지않아 바닥나고 말았으리라는 걸 짐작하시겠지요.\n\n\"이제 나는 전보다 훨씬 더 깊은 곤경에 빠졌습니다. 살아가는 데 꼭 필요한 것들마저 내가 갖지 못한 것들 축에 들기 시작했지요. 그리고 내 처지를 한층 더 비참하게 만든 것은, 이제 도가 지나치도록 빠져 버린 내 연인이 나와 똑같은 곤경을 함께 겪고 있다는 사실이었습니다. 사랑하는 여인이 곤경에 처한 것을 보면서도 그녀를 구해 줄 수 없고, 게다가 그녀를 그 처지로 몰아넣은 것이 바로 자신이라는 걸 곱씹어야 하는 것—이것은 아마, 겪어 보지 못한 사람에게는 그 끔찍함을 어떤 상상으로도 그려 보일 수 없는 저주일 겁니다.\" \"진심으로 그 말을 믿습니다.\" 하고 존스가 외쳤다. \"그리고 마음 깊은 곳에서부터 당신이 가엾습니다.\" 그러고는 방 안을 두세 차례 어지러이 서성이다가, 마침내 용서를 구하며 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지고는 외쳤다. \"하늘에 감사할 일이지요, 나는 그것만은 면했으니!\"\n\n\"이 사정이,\" 하고 신사가 이어 갔다. \"지금 내 처지의 끔찍함을 어찌나 모질게 더해 놓았던지, 도무지 견딜 수 없는 지경이 되었습니다. 굶주림이든 목마름이든, 채워지지 않은 내 천성의 욕구가 날뛰는 것을 견디는 편이, 그토록 터무니없이 떠받들던 여인의 그 변덕스럽기 짝이 없는 욕망 하나를 채워 주지 못하고 두는 것보다 차라리 덜 괴로웠으니까요. 그녀가 내 지인 절반의 정부였다는 걸 알면서도, 나는 그녀와 결혼하겠다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었습니다. 그러나 이 착한 여인은, 세상이 내게 너무도 불리하다 여길 그런 행동에 선뜻 동의하려 하지 않았습니다. 그리고 어쩌면 자기 때문에 내가 날마다 시달리는 그 불안을 가엾이 여겼던지, 내 고통을 끝내 주기로 마음먹었지요. 정말이지 그녀는 곧 나를 이 성가시고 뒤엉킨 처지에서 풀어 줄 방도를 찾아냈습니다. 내가 그녀에게 어떤 즐거움을 마련해 줄까 온갖 궁리로 정신이 없을 때, 그녀가 아주 친절하게도—나를 옥스퍼드의 옛 정부 가운데 하나에게 팔아넘긴 것이지요. 그자의 정성과 부지런함 덕에 나는 곧장 붙잡혀 감옥에 갇혔습니다.\n\n\"여기서 나는 비로소 지난 삶의 잘못들을, 내가 저지른 과오들을, 스스로 불러들인 불행들을, 그리고 더없이 훌륭한 아버지 가운데 한 분에게 안겼을 그 슬픔을 진지하게 곱씹기 시작했습니다. 여기에 내 정부의 배신까지 보태고 나니, 내 마음의 끔찍함이 어찌나 컸던지, 삶이 더는 바랄 만한 것이 아니라 도리어 혐오의 대상이 되었습니다. 그래서 만약 죽음이 수치를 동반하지 않은 채 내 앞에 골라잡으라 내밀어졌다면, 나는 그것을 가장 소중한 벗으로 기꺼이 끌어안았을 겁니다.\n\n\"순회재판의 때가 곧 다가왔고, 나는 인신보호영장에 따라 옥스퍼드로 옮겨졌습니다. 거기서 나는 영락없이 유죄 판결과 단죄를 받으리라 여겼지요. 그런데 놀랍게도 나를 고발하러 나서는 자가 아무도 없었고, 나는 회기가 끝날 무렵 기소가 없다는 이유로 풀려났습니다. 요컨대, 내 한방 동무가 옥스퍼드를 떠났던 것이지요. 게으름 탓이었는지 다른 무슨 까닭이었는지는 모르겠으나, 그는 그 일에 더는 끼어들기를 그만두었던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아마,\" 하고 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"그자는 당신 피를 제 손에 묻히고 싶지 않았던 게지요. 그게 옳았고요. 만약 내 증언으로 누가 교수형에 처해진다면, 나는 그 귀신이 보일까 무서워서 그 뒤로 다시는 혼자서는 못 누워 잘 겁니다.\"\n\n\"파트리지,\" 하고 존스가 말했다. \"이제 곧 네가 더 용감한 자인지 더 지혜로운 자인지 헷갈리겠구나.\" \"정 그러시면 비웃으셔도 좋습니다, 도련님.\" 하고 파트리지가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 제가 들려드릴 수 있는, 아주 짧고 또 틀림없이 사실인 이야기를 들으시면, 어쩌면 생각이 바뀌실지도 모르지요. 제가 태어난 교구에서는—\" 여기서 존스가 그를 입 다물게 하려 했으나, 낯선 이가 그에게 이야기를 마저 하도록 허락해 주자고 거들며, 그동안 자신은 나머지 이야기를 가다듬어 두겠노라 약속했다.\n\n그리하여 파트리지는 이렇게 이어 갔다. \"제가 태어난 교구에는 브라이들이라는 이름의 농부가 살았는데, 그에게 프랜시스라는 아들이 있었습니다. 장래가 촉망되는 착한 젊은이였지요. 저는 그와 같이 문법학교에 다녔는데, 거기서 그가 오비디우스의 《서한집》까지 나아간 게 기억나고, 때로는 사전 한번 안 들여다보고도 세 줄을 잇달아 해석해 내기도 했지요. 게다가 아주 착한 녀석이라, 일요일에 교회를 거른 적이 없고, 온 교구를 통틀어 손꼽히는 시편 찬송가 잘하는 이로 쳤습니다. 정말이지 이따금 술을 한 잔 과하게 들이켜는 게, 그가 가진 단 하나뿐인 흠이었지요.\" \"그래, 알았으니 귀신 이야기로 넘어가게.\" 하고 존스가 외쳤다. \"걱정 마십시오, 도련님. 곧 그자에게 이를 테니까요.\" 하고 파트리지가 대답했다. \"그러니까 말입니다, 농부 브라이들이 암말 한 마리를 잃었는데, 제 기억으론 밤색 말이었지요. 한데 마침 이 젊은 프랜시스가 얼마 안 가 힌든의 장에 갔는데, 제 생각엔 그게—날짜는 기억이 안 납니다만, 아무튼 거기서 무슨 일이 있었는고 하니, 글쎄 제 아버지 암말을 탄 사내를 떡하니 마주친 게 아니겠습니까. 프랭크는 대뜸 '도둑 잡아라!' 하고 외쳤고, 장 한복판이었던지라 그 사내는 도무지 달아날 수가 없었지요, 아시다시피요. 그래서 사람들이 그를 붙잡아 치안판사 앞으로 데려갔습니다. 노일의 윌러비 판사였던 게 기억나는데, 아주 훌륭하고 좋은 신사였지요. 그가 그 사내를 감옥에 가두고, 프랭크에게는—그걸 뭐라더라, 출두서약이라고 하던가요—그런 걸 받았습니다. '레(re)'와 '코그노스코(cognosco)'를 합친 어려운 낱말인데, 다른 여러 합성어가 그렇듯 그 뜻이 단순한 본디말 쓰임과는 다르지요. 자, 마침내 페이지 판사 나리께서 순회재판을 열러 내려오셨고, 그래서 그 사내가 끌려 나오고 프랭크는 증인으로 불려 나왔습니다. 확실히 저는 판사가 그에게 죄수에 대해 할 말이 무엇이냐고 묻기 시작했을 때 그 얼굴을 영영 못 잊을 겁니다. 가엾은 프랭크는 신발 속에서 다리가 다 후들거리고 떨렸지요. '자, 이보게,' 하고 나리가 말합니다. '할 말이 무엇인가? 우물쭈물 더듬거리지 말고 똑똑히 말하게.' 하지만 그러고는 곧 프랭크에게는 더없이 정중하게 굴면서, 그 사내를 향해 천둥같이 호통치기 시작했습니다. 그리고 사내에게 변명할 말이 있느냐고 묻자, 사내는 말을 주웠노라고 했지요. '허!' 하고 판사가 답했습니다. '운도 좋은 놈이로구나. 나는 이 순회재판 길을 사십 년이나 다녔어도 평생 말 한 마리 주워 본 적이 없거늘. 한데 친구, 내 일러두지. 자네는 자네가 안 것보다 더 운이 좋았어. 말만 주운 게 아니라 올가미도 같이 주웠으니 말일세, 내 장담하지.' 정말이지 저는 그 말을 영영 못 잊을 겁니다. 그 말에 다들 웃음보를 터뜨렸지요. 어떻게 안 웃을 수가 있겠습니까? 아니, 그 밖에도 우스갯소리를 스무 가지는 더 했는데 지금은 다 기억나지 않습니다. 그가 말고기 보는 안목에 관해 뭐라 한 게 있어서 사람들이 다 웃었지요. 확실히 그 판사는 학식이 깊은 분일 뿐 아니라, 아주 담대한 분이었음에 틀림없습니다. 정말이지 목숨이 걸린 재판을 듣는 건 더없이 흥미진진한 구경거리지요. 한 가지, 제가 좀 가혹하다 여긴 게 있는데, 죄수의 변호인이 그를 위해 변론하는 게 허락되지 않았다는 겁니다. 그저 아주 짧은 한마디만 들어 달라 청했는데도, 나리께서는 들으려 하지 않으셨지요. 정작 그를 반박하는 변호인이 반 시간 넘게 떠드는 건 그냥 두셨으면서요. 솔직히 좀 가혹하다 여겼습니다, 그런 자들이 그렇게나 많다는 게요. 나리에, 법정에, 배심원에, 변호인들에, 증인들까지, 죄다 가엾은 한 사람한테 달려드는데, 게다가 그 사람은 쇠사슬까지 차고 있었으니까요. 자, 그 사내는 교수형을 당했지요. 어차피 달리될 수가 없었으니까요. 그리고 가엾은 프랭크는 그 일로 도무지 마음이 편할 수가 없었습니다. 어두운 데 혼자 있기만 하면 그 사내의 혼령이 보이는 것 같았으니까요.\" \"그래, 이게 자네 이야기인가?\" 하고 존스가 외쳤다. \"아니요, 아니요.\" 하고 파트리지가 답했다. \"오, 주여, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서! 이제 막 본론으로 들어가려는 참입니다. 어느 날 밤, 술집에서 돌아오는데, 길고 좁고 어두운 골목길에서 그가 그 사내와 정통으로 맞부딪쳤지 뭡니까. 그 혼령은 온통 흰옷을 두르고 프랭크에게 덤벼들었고, 다부진 녀석이었던 프랭크도 그 혼령에게 맞서 덤볐지요. 그래서 둘이 거기서 한바탕 드잡이를 벌였는데, 가엾은 프랭크는 끔찍하게 두들겨 맞았습니다. 정말이지 그는 겨우겨우 기다시피 해서 집에 당도했지요. 한데 두들겨 맞은 데다 놀라기까지 한 탓에, 보름 넘게 앓아누웠습니다. 이게 다 틀림없는 사실이고, 온 교구가 증인이 되어 줄 겁니다.\"\n\n낯선 이는 이 이야기에 빙그레 웃었고, 존스는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러자 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"암요, 웃으셔도 좋습니다, 도련님. 다른 몇몇도 그랬으니까요. 특히 어떤 향사 하나는, 무신론자나 다름없다고들 하는 자인데, 글쎄 그 이튿날 아침 같은 골목에서 흰 얼굴을 한 송아지 한 마리가 죽어 있는 게 발견됐다는 이유로, 그 싸움이 프랭크와 그 송아지 사이에 벌어진 거라고 우겨 댔지 뭡니까. 마치 송아지가 사람한테 덤비기라도 한다는 듯이요. 게다가 프랭크는 제게, 그게 혼령인 줄 똑똑히 알았고 기독교 세계 어느 법정에서든 그걸 두고 맹세할 수 있다고 했습니다. 게다가 그때 그가 들이켠 술이라곤 한두 쿼트 남짓, 그 정도밖에 안 됐고요. 주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기시고, 우리 모두가 피에 손 담그는 일이 없게 지켜 주소서, 저는 그렇게 빕니다!\"\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 하고 존스가 낯선 이에게 말했다. \"파트리지가 이야기를 끝냈으니, 부디 친절히 이어 가 주신다면 앞으로는 더 끼어들지 않으리라 믿습니다.\" 그러자 그는 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러나 그가 잠시 숨을 고른 만큼, 우리도 독자에게 그 틈을 드리는 것이 마땅하다고 여겨, 이로써 이 장을 마치고자 한다.\n\n「이제 나는 자유를 되찾았소.」 낯선 사내가 말했다. 「하지만 평판은 잃어버렸지요. 법정에서 겨우 무죄 판결을 받은 사람과, 제 양심 속에서 그리고 사람들의 평가 속에서 무죄가 된 사람 사이에는 커다란 차이가 있으니 말이오. 나는 내 죄를 스스로 의식하고 있었고, 누구의 얼굴도 똑바로 볼 면목이 없었소. 그래서 이튿날 아침, 날이 밝아 누구의 눈에라도 띄기 전에 옥스퍼드를 떠나기로 마음먹었소.\n\n「도시를 벗어나자 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각은, 집으로 돌아가 아버지께 용서를 구해 보자는 것이었소. 그러나 그동안 일어난 모든 일을 아버지가 모르실 리 없다고 여길 만했고, 아버지가 온갖 부정한 행위를 몹시 혐오하신다는 것 또한 잘 알고 있던 터라, 그분께 받아들여지리라는 희망은 도무지 품을 수 없었소. 더욱이 어머니의 힘이 미치는 온갖 술책을 너무도 확신하고 있었으니 말이오. 아니, 설령 아버지의 용서가 내가 짐작한 그분의 노여움만큼이나 확실했다 하더라도, 과연 내가 그분의 얼굴을 마주할 만한 뻔뻔함을 지닐 수 있었을지, 혹은 어떤 조건으로든, 내가 그토록 비열한 짓을 저질렀음을 알고 있다고 내가 확신하는 사람들과 함께 살며 이야기를 나누는 데 굴복할 수 있었을지 의문이오.\n\n「그래서 나는 슬픔이든 수치든 가장 잘 숨어들 수 있는 곳, 런던으로 서둘러 돌아갔소. 다만 지극히 공적인 인물이 아닌 한에서 말이오. 이곳에서는 고독의 이점을 누리되 그 단점은 피할 수 있으니, 혼자이면서 동시에 무리 속에 있을 수 있기 때문이오. 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 걷거나 앉아 있는 동안에도, 소음과 분주함, 그리고 끊임없이 이어지는 볼거리가 마음을 즐겁게 해 주어, 정신이 스스로를 갉아먹지 않게, 아니 차라리 슬픔이나 수치를 갉아먹지 않게 막아 주오. 그것들은 세상에서 가장 해로운 양식이라, (사람들 앞에서가 아니고는 결코 그것을 맛보지 않는 이들도 많지만) 혼자 있을 때면 아주 배불리, 아주 치명적으로 그것을 먹어 치우는 이들도 있는 법이오.\n\n「하지만 어떤 인간의 좋음이든 거기에 따라붙는 나쁨이 없는 경우란 거의 없으니, 인간이 지닌 이 무관심한 기질에서 불편을 느끼는 사람들도 있소. 다름 아니라 돈이 없는 사람들 말이오. 남의 눈에 띄어 무안해질 일은 없지만, 그렇다고 당신을 모르는 사람들이 당신에게 옷을 입혀 주거나 먹여 주지도 않기 때문이오. 그러니 사람은 아라비아 사막에서나 리든홀 시장에서나 똑같이 쉽게 굶어 죽을 수 있는 법이오.\n\n「당시 나는 그 크나큰 악, 다시 말해 돈이라는 것을 갖지 못하는 처지였소. 여러 작가들이 그것을 악이라 여겼는데, 짐작건대 그들은 그것에 너무 짓눌려 있었던 게지요.」—「외람되오나, 선생,」 파트리지가 말했다. 「저는 그것을 말로룸이라 부른 작가는 기억하지 못하옵고, 다만 이리타멘타 말로룸이라 했을 뿐이지요. 에포디운투르 오페스, 이리타멘타 말로룸.」*—「자, 선생,」 낯선 사내가 말을 이었다. 「그것이 악이든, 아니면 한낱 악의 원인일 뿐이든, 나는 그것이 완전히 비어 있었고, 동시에 벗도 없었으며, 내 생각으로는 아는 사람조차 없었소. 그러던 어느 날 저녁, 몹시 허기지고 몹시 비참한 채로 이너 템플을 지나고 있을 때, 별안간 어떤 목소리가 내 세례명을 아주 친근하게 부르며 나를 불러 세우는 것이었소. 돌아보니, 그렇게 인사를 건넨 사람이 내 대학 동기였음을 곧바로 알아보았소. 대학을 떠난 지 일 년이 넘은 사람으로, 내게 어떤 불행이 닥치기 훨씬 전에 떠난 친구였소. 왓슨이라는 이름의 이 신사는 내 손을 진심으로 굳게 잡으며, 나를 만난 큰 기쁨을 표하더니, 당장 함께 술 한 병을 마시자고 청했소. 나는 처음엔 그 제안을 사양하며 볼일이 있는 척했으나, 그가 워낙 간곡하고 끈질기게 권하는 통에, 결국 허기가 내 자존심을 이겼소. 그래서 나는 주머니에 한 푼도 없다고 솔직히 고백했는데, 다만 변명 삼아 거짓말 하나를 지어내어, 그날 아침 바지를 갈아입은 탓이라 둘러댔소. 왓슨 군이 대답하길, '이보게 잭, 자네와 나는 그런 일을 입에 올리기엔 너무 오랜 친구 사이라고 여겼는데.' 그러더니 내 팔을 붙들고 끌고 가는 것이었소. 하지만 나는 그를 별로 힘들게 하지 않았으니, 내 마음이 그가 끄는 것보다 훨씬 더 세게 나를 끌어당기고 있었기 때문이오.\n\n「그러고 나서 우리는 프라이어스로 들어갔는데, 아시다시피 그곳은 온갖 흥과 환락의 무대지요. 술집에 다다랐을 때 왓슨 군은 요리사에게는 눈길 한 번 주지 않고 술 따르는 사람에게만 말을 걸었소. 내가 한참 전에 저녁을 먹었으리라 의심치 않았던 까닭이오. 그러나 실상은 그렇지 않았으므로, 나는 또 하나의 거짓을 꾸며 내어, 동료에게 도시 저편 끝에서 중대한 볼일을 보다가 양고기 한 조각을 급히 먹었을 뿐이라, 다시 허기가 진다며 술에 비프스테이크를 곁들여 주면 좋겠다고 말했소.」—「어떤 사람들은,」 파트리지가 외쳤다. 「기억력이 좋아야 하는 법이지요. 아니면 그 양고기 값을 치를 만큼의 돈이 바지에 딱 들어 있기라도 했더이까?」—「자네 지적이 옳네.」 낯선 사내가 대답했다. 「거짓말을 다루다 보면 그런 실수가 따라붙기 마련이라고 믿네.—하지만 이야기를 계속하자면—나는 이제 더없이 행복해지기 시작했소. 고기와 술이 곧 내 기운을 한껏 되살려 주었고, 옛 친구와 나누는 대화에서 큰 즐거움을 누렸소. 더욱이 그가 떠난 뒤 대학에서 일어난 일을 그가 전혀 모르고 있다고 여겼던 터라 더욱 그러했소.\n\n「하지만 그는 내가 이 흐뭇한 착각 속에 오래 머물도록 내버려 두지 않았소. 한 손에는 술잔을 가득 채워 들고, 다른 손으로는 나를 붙든 채 외치길, '자, 이보게,' 하더니, '자네에게 씌워졌던 그 일에서 그토록 명예롭게 무죄를 받은 것을 축하하며 한잔하세.' 그 말에 나는 어쩔 줄 모르고 벼락 맞은 듯 굳어 버렸소. 그걸 알아챈 왓슨이 이렇게 이어 말했소. '아니, 부끄러워할 것 없네, 이 사람아. 자네는 무죄를 받았고, 이제 누구도 감히 자네를 죄인이라 부르지 못해. 그러니 부디 자네 친구인 내게만은 말해 주게—정말로 그자를 털어먹은 게지? 그런 비굴하고 한심한 악당의 옷을 벗긴 건 칭찬받을 만한 일이거든. 이백 기니가 아니라 그만큼의 천 기니라도 챙겼더라면 좋았을 것을. 자, 자, 이 사람아, 내게 고백하는 걸 수줍어 말게. 자넨 지금 그 뚜쟁이 같은 자들 앞에 끌려 나온 게 아니잖나. 망할, 나는 그 일로 자네를 오히려 우러러본다네. 구원을 바라는 마음으로 맹세컨대, 나라도 똑같이 했을 때 조금도 거리낌이 없었을 테니.'\n\n「이 말이 나의 무안함을 조금 덜어 주었소. 그리고 이제 술기운에 마음이 다소 풀린 터라, 나는 그 강도질을 아주 거리낌 없이 인정했소. 다만 그에게 알려 주기를, 빼앗은 액수에 관해서는 잘못 전해 들었으며, 실은 그가 말한 것의 오분의 일이 조금 넘는 정도였다고 했소.\n\n「'그것 참 진심으로 안타깝군,' 그가 말하길, '다음번엔 더 잘되길 바라네. 하기야 내 충고를 받아들인다면, 그런 위험을 무릅쓸 일은 없을 걸세. 자,' 하더니 그는 주머니에서 주사위 몇 개를 꺼내며, '여기 물건이 있네. 여기 도구가 있어. 여기, 지갑의 병을 고쳐 주는 작은 의사 양반들이 있다네. 내 조언만 따르면, 교수대에 걸릴 위험 하나 없이 어수룩한 봉의 주머니를 비우는 길을 보여 주지.'」\n\n「교수대라!」 파트리지가 외쳤다. 「선생, 그게 대체 무엇이옵니까?」\n\n「그게 말이오, 선생,」 낯선 사내가 말했다. 「교수대를 가리키는 은어요. 노름꾼들은 도덕에서 노상강도와 별반 다를 게 없으니, 말투에서도 그들과 무척 닮았지요.\n\n「우리는 이제 각자 한 병씩 비웠는데, 그때 왓슨 군이 노름판이 열렸으니 가 봐야 한다며, 동시에 나더러 함께 가서 운을 시험해 보자고 간곡히 졸랐소. 나는 주머니가 비었다고 이미 일러 준 터라, 지금은 그럴 형편이 못 됨을 그도 알지 않느냐고 대답했소. 사실대로 말하자면, 그가 그토록 우정을 강하게 표하던 터라, 그런 목적으로 약간의 돈을 빌려 주겠노라 나설 줄 믿어 의심치 않았소. 그러나 그는 이렇게 대답하더이다. '그런 건 신경 쓰지 말게, 이 사람아. 그냥 대담하게 레반트를 한 판 뛰어 보게.' [파트리지가 그 말의 뜻을 물으려 했으나, 존스가 그의 입을 막았다.] '다만 상대만은 신중히 고르게. 적당한 사람을 내가 슬쩍 점찍어 줄 테니. 자넨 이 도시를 모르고, 멀쩡한 봉과 어수룩한 봉을 구별하지도 못하니, 그게 필요할 걸세.'\n\n「이제 계산서가 나오자, 왓슨은 제 몫을 치르고 떠나려 했소. 나는 얼굴을 붉히며, 내게 돈이 없음을 그에게 상기시켰소. 그가 대답하길, '그건 아무것도 아닐세. 문 뒤에다 외상으로 달아 놓든가, 아니면 대담하게 슬쩍 나가 버리고 모른 척하게.—아니—잠깐만,' 하더니, '내가 먼저 아래층으로 내려갈 테니, 자네는 내 돈을 집어 들고, 계산은 전부 카운터에 외상으로 달아 두게. 나는 모퉁이에서 기다리고 있겠네.' 나는 이게 다소 못마땅하다는 기색을 내비치며, 그가 전부 내놓을 줄 알았다고 넌지시 말했소. 그러나 그는 주머니에 육 펜스 한 닢도 더는 없다고 맹세하더이다.\n\n「그러더니 그는 아래층으로 내려갔고, 나는 떠밀리듯 그 돈을 집어 들고 그를 뒤따랐소. 어찌나 바싹 따라붙었던지, 그가 술 따르는 사람에게 계산은 탁자 위에 있다고 말하는 것까지 들렸소. 그 사람은 내 곁을 지나 위층으로 올라갔지만, 나는 어찌나 서둘러 거리로 나섰던지 그가 실망하는 소리는 듣지도 못했고, 일러 준 대로 카운터에서는 한마디도 꺼내지 않았소.\n\n「우리는 이제 곧장 노름판으로 갔는데, 그곳에서 왓슨 군은 놀랍게도 큰돈을 꺼내 제 앞에 늘어놓았고, 다른 많은 이들도 그리했소. 그들 모두 분명, 제 돈 무더기를 일종의 미끼 새로 여겨, 그것으로 이웃의 돈 무더기를 꾀어내 끌어오려는 속셈이었던 게지요.\n\n「이곳에서 운명, 아니 차라리 주사위가 제 신전에서 부린 온갖 변덕을 죄다 늘어놓자면 지루할 따름이오. 산더미 같던 금화가 탁자 한쪽에서는 순식간에 흔적도 없이 사라졌다가, 다른 한쪽에서는 또 그만큼 갑작스레 솟아올랐소. 부자가 한순간에 가난뱅이가 되고, 가난뱅이가 또 그만큼 갑작스레 부자가 되니, 어느 철학자라도 부의 덧없음을 그곳에서보다 더 잘 제자들에게 가르칠 곳은 없을 듯했소. 적어도 부가 지속되는 것의 불확실함을 그곳에서보다 더 잘 새겨 줄 곳은 없었을 게요.\n\n「나로 말하자면, 내 작은 밑천을 상당히 불린 끝에, 결국 그것을 통째로 거덜내고 말았소. 왓슨 군 역시 운이 갖가지로 오락가락하더니, 다소 열이 오른 채 탁자에서 일어나, 백 파운드를 고스란히 잃었으니 더는 안 하겠다고 선언했소. 그러더니 내게 다가와, 함께 술집으로 돌아가자고 청했소. 그러나 나는 단호히 거절하며, 또다시 그런 곤경에 빠지지 않겠다고, 더구나 그도 돈을 죄다 잃어 이제 나와 같은 처지가 아니냐고 말했소. '쳇!' 그가 말하길, '내 방금 친구한테서 두어 기니를 빌렸는데, 그중 하나는 자네 쓰라고 주지.' 그러고는 즉시 그중 하나를 내 손에 쥐여 주었고, 나는 더는 그의 뜻을 거스르지 않았소.\n\n「처음엔 그토록 떳떳치 못하게 빠져나왔던 바로 그 집으로 돌아가는 게 다소 꺼림칙했소. 그러나 술 따르는 사람이 아주 공손한 말씨로 우리에게 '계산을 깜빡 잊고 가신 듯합니다' 하고 말하자, 나는 완전히 마음이 놓여, 선뜻 그에게 일 기니를 건네며 알아서 받으라 일렀고, 내 기억력에 씌워진 부당한 혐의를 잠자코 받아들였소.\n\n「이제 왓슨 군은 그가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 사치스러운 저녁을 주문했소. 그리고 아까는 소박한 클라레로 만족하던 그가, 이제는 더없이 값진 부르고뉴가 아니고선 성에 차지 않는 것이었소.\n\n「우리 자리에는 곧 노름판에서 온 여러 신사들이 더해져 일행이 불어났소. 나중에 알게 된 일이지만, 그들 대부분은 술을 마시러 술집에 온 게 아니라 장사 삼아 온 것이었소. 진짜 노름꾼들은 몸이 아픈 척하며 술잔을 마다하면서, 나중에 털어먹을 두 젊은이에게는 부지런히 술을 권했고, 과연 그 둘은 인정사정없이 털리고 말았소. 이 노략질에서 나도 운 좋게 한몫을 챙겼으나, 아직 그 속내까지는 알지 못했소.\n\n「이 술집 노름에는 한 가지 묘한 일이 따라붙었소. 돈이 점차 깡그리 사라져 버린 것이오. 처음엔 탁자가 금화로 절반쯤 덮여 있었건만, 이튿날, 곧 일요일 정오까지 이어진 그 노름이 끝날 무렵에는 탁자 위에서 기니 한 닢조차 거의 볼 수 없었소. 게다가 더 이상한 것은, 나를 뺀 그 자리의 모든 사람이 저마다 잃었노라 단언했다는 점이오. 악마 자신이 그 돈을 가져가지 않은 한, 도대체 그것이 어디로 갔는지는 가늠하기 어려운 일이오.」\n\n「틀림없이 악마가 가져갔지요.」 파트리지가 말했다. 「악령은 방 안에 사람이 아무리 많아도 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않고 무엇이든 가져갈 수 있으니까요. 설교 시간에 노름을 하던 그 못된 무리를 통째로 데려갔대도 저는 놀라지 않았을 겁니다. 그리고 마음만 먹으면 실화 하나를 들려드릴 수도 있는데요, 악마가 어떤 남자를 남의 아내 곁 잠자리에서 끌어내어 문 열쇠 구멍으로 데려가 버린 이야기랍니다. 그 일이 벌어진 바로 그 집을 제 눈으로 봤는데, 지난 삼십 년 동안 아무도 그 집에 살지 않았지요.」\n존스는 파트리지의 주제넘은 말에 다소 언짢았으나, 그 순박함에 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었다. 낯선 사내도 마찬가지였고, 그러고 나서 다음 장에서 보게 될 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n* 라틴어: \"재물이 캐어지나니, 그것이 악을 부추기는 자극제로다.\" (오비디우스의 시구로, 돈을 악 그 자체가 아니라 악을 자극하는 것이라 한 표현.)\n\n앞의 이야기가 계속 이어진다.\n\n“이제 그 대학 동무가 나를 전혀 새로운 인생의 무대로 끌어들였습니다. 나는 곧 사기꾼 무리 전체와 안면을 트게 되었고, 그들의 비밀까지 알게 되었습니다. 다시 말해, 풋내기와 경험 없는 자들을 속이기에 알맞은, 그 조잡한 속임수들의 지식 속으로 들어선 것이지요. 더 정교한 부류의 술수들도 있는데, 그것은 이 직업의 정점에 선 몇몇 두목 패거리만이 아는 것이었습니다. 그건 내가 기대했던 것 이상의 영예였지요. 술이라면 내가 도를 넘게 빠져 있었고, 또 타고난 격정의 뜨거움 탓에, 가장 엄격한 철학 학파만큼이나 냉정함을 요하는 그 기술에서 나는 큰 성공에 이르지 못했으니까요.\n\n“이제 더없이 가까운 우정을 나누며 함께 지내던 왓슨 씨는 불행하게도 앞서 말한 그 결함, 즉 술에 지나치게 빠져 있었습니다. 그래서 어떤 자들처럼 그 직업으로 재산을 모으기는커녕, 부유했다가 가난했다가를 번갈아 오갔고, 공개 도박판에서 호구들에게서 뜯어낸 그 노획물을, 자신들은 입도 대지 않는 술병을 앞에 두고 더 냉정한 동료들에게 자주 내놓아야 했지요.\n\n“그래도 우리 둘은 그럭저럭 불편한 생계를 꾸려 나갔습니다. 그렇게 나는 이 년 동안 그 노릇을 이어 갔지요. 그동안 나는 온갖 종류의 운명을 다 맛보았습니다. 어느 때는 풍족함 속에서 번성하다가, 어느 때는 거의 믿기 힘든 곤경과 씨름해야 했지요. 오늘은 사치 속에 뒹굴다가, 내일은 가장 거칠고 초라한 끼니로 내몰리는 식이었습니다. 저녁이면 등에 걸쳐져 있던 멋진 옷이, 이튿날 아침이면 전당포에 가 있는 일이 잦았지요.\n\n“어느 날 밤, 도박판에서 한 푼 없이 빈손으로 돌아오던 길에, 나는 큰 소동이 일어나고 거리에 많은 군중이 몰려 있는 것을 보았습니다. 나로서는 소매치기에게 잃을 것이 없었으므로, 나는 인파 속으로 과감히 끼어들었습니다. 그러고 물어보니, 어떤 사람이 강도질을 당하고 불한당들에게 심하게 당했다는 것이었습니다. 그 다친 사람은 피투성이로 보였고, 제 다리로 몸을 지탱하기조차 힘들어 보였습니다. 지금의 내 삶과 어울림이 정직함도 수치심도 거의 남겨 놓지 않았지만, 그래도 인간애만큼은 앗아 가지 않았던 터라, 나는 즉시 그 불행한 사람에게 도움을 내밀었습니다. 그는 고맙게 그것을 받아들이며 내 인도에 몸을 맡겼고, 어느 선술집으로든 자신을 데려가 외과의를 부를 수 있게 해 달라고 청했습니다. 피를 너무 흘려 정신이 아득하다는 것이었지요. 그는 신사 차림으로 보이는 사람을 만난 것을 참으로 기뻐하는 듯했습니다. 그 자리에 있던 나머지 사람들로 말하자면, 겉모습이 그러해서 그가 도무지 분별 있게 믿음을 둘 수 없었으니까요.\n\n“나는 그 가엾은 사람의 팔을 부축하여, 마침 가장 가까이에 있던, 우리가 모이던 그 선술집으로 데려갔습니다. 운 좋게도 외과의 한 사람이 그 집에 있어 곧바로 와서, 그의 상처를 손보는 일에 매달렸습니다. 그리고 나는 그 상처가 목숨을 앗아 갈 것 같지는 않다는 말을 들어 기뻤지요.\n\n“외과의가 아주 신속하고 능숙하게 일을 마치고 나서, 다친 사람에게 도시의 어느 동네에 묵고 있느냐고 묻기 시작했습니다. 그는 ‘바로 그날 아침에 도시에 막 도착했고, 말은 피카딜리의 어느 여관에 매어 두었으며, 달리 묵을 곳도 없고, 도시에 아는 사람도 거의 또는 전혀 없다’고 대답했습니다.\n\n“이 외과의는—이름은 잊었지만 R로 시작했던 것은 기억합니다—그 직업에서 으뜸가는 명성을 지녔고, 국왕의 시의(侍醫)였습니다. 게다가 그는 좋은 자질을 많이 갖춘, 매우 너그럽고 마음씨 고운 사람이어서, 동료 인간에게 무슨 도움이든 기꺼이 베푸는 사람이었지요. 그는 자신의 환자에게 여관까지 데려다주도록 자기 마차를 쓰라고 권하면서, 동시에 그의 귀에 대고 ‘혹시 돈이 필요하면 마련해 주겠다’고 속삭였습니다.\n\n“그 가엾은 사람은 이제 이 너그러운 제안에 감사를 표할 처지가 못 되었습니다. 한동안 나에게 눈길을 고정한 채 바라보고 있더니, 의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히며 ‘오, 내 아들! 내 아들!’ 하고 외치고는, 그대로 기절해 버렸으니까요.\n\n“그 자리에 있던 사람들 가운데 많은 이가 이 일이 피를 흘린 탓에 생긴 것이라 여겼습니다. 그러나 그와 동시에 아버지의 모습을 떠올리기 시작한 나는, 이제 내 의심이 확실해졌고, 내 앞에 나타난 이가 바로 아버지 그분이라는 것을 알게 되었습니다. 나는 곧장 그에게 달려가 두 팔로 안아 일으키고, 더없이 간절하게 그 차가운 입술에 입을 맞추었습니다. 여기서 나는 차마 묘사할 수 없는 한 장면 위로 장막을 드리워야겠습니다. 아버지가 한동안 그러셨듯 정신을 잃지는 않았으나, 내 정신도 두려움과 놀라움에 너무나 짓눌려, 그 몇 분 동안 무슨 일이 있었는지 나로서는 알 길이 없으니까요. 정말이지 아버지가 다시 혼절에서 깨어나, 우리 둘이 서로의 품에 안겨 다정히 끌어안고, 두 사람의 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내리는 그 순간에 이르러서야 나는 정신을 차렸습니다.\n\n“그 자리에 있던 사람들 대부분이 이 장면에 감동한 듯했습니다. 그 안의 배우라 할 만한 우리는, 가능한 한 빨리 모든 구경꾼의 눈에서 벗어나고 싶었지요. 그래서 아버지는 외과의의 친절한 마차 제안을 받아들였고, 나는 그 안에 함께 타 아버지를 여관까지 모셨습니다.\n\n“우리 둘만 남게 되자, 아버지는 그토록 오랜 세월 동안 편지 한 통 쓰지 않고 소홀히 했다며 나를 부드럽게 나무라셨지만, 그 까닭이 된 그 죄에 대해서는 한마디도 꺼내지 않으셨습니다. 그러고는 어머니의 별세를 알려 주시며, 함께 집으로 돌아가자고 고집하셨지요. ‘오랫동안 나 때문에 더없는 근심에 시달렸으며, 나에 대해 그토록 더 끔찍한 걱정들을 품고 있었던 터라, 내가 죽기를 가장 두려워했는지 아니면 차라리 바랐는지 자신도 모르겠다’고 하셨습니다. 마침내, 같은 곳에서 막 아들 하나를 되찾은 이웃 신사가 내가 어디 있는지 알려 주었고, 이 방탕한 삶에서 나를 되돌려 오는 것만이 런던으로 온 유일한 이유라고 하셨습니다. 아버지는 하마터면 목숨을 잃을 뻔했던 사고를 통해서나마 나를 찾아내게 된 것을 하늘에 감사하셨고, 자신이 목숨을 부지한 데에 부분적으로 내 인간애의 덕을 입었다고 생각하는 것을 기뻐하셨습니다. 그러면서, 만일 내가 그토록 보살핀 대상이 바로 내 아버지인 줄 알았더라면 효심에서 그리한 것이겠지만, 그것을 모른 채 베푼 인간애가 오히려 더 기쁘다고 하셨지요.\n\n“악덕이 내 마음을 그토록 더럽혀, 비록 받을 자격 없이 베풀어진 것일지언정 그토록 큰 부정(父情)에 무감각하도록 만들지는 못했습니다. 나는 곧, 여행이 가능해지는 대로 함께 집으로 돌아가라는 아버지의 명을 따르겠다고 약속했습니다. 실제로 아버지는, 그의 치료를 맡았던 그 훌륭한 외과의의 도움으로 며칠 만에 여행할 수 있게 되었지요.\n\n“아버지가 길을 떠나기 전날(그때까지는 나는 아버지 곁을 거의 떠나지 않았습니다), 나는 가장 가까운 몇몇 지인에게, 특히 왓슨 씨에게 작별을 고하러 갔습니다. 그는, 그가 부르던 표현대로, 어리석은 늙은이의 어리석은 소망에 그저 순순히 따르느라 나 자신을 파묻어 버리지 말라며 만류했습니다. 그러나 그런 권유는 아무 소용이 없었고, 나는 다시 한번 내 집을 보게 되었지요. 아버지는 이제 내게 결혼을 생각해 보라고 간곡히 권하셨습니다. 하지만 내 마음은 그런 생각이라면 도무지 내키지 않았습니다. 나는 이미 사랑을 맛본 적이 있었고, 어쩌면 당신도 그 더없이 부드럽고 더없이 격렬한 정념의 무절제한 극단을 아실 테지요.”—여기서 노신사는 말을 멈추고 존스를 진지하게 바라보았다. 존스의 얼굴은 한순간에 새빨개졌다가 새하얘지는 양극단을 드러냈다. 그러나 노인은 아무 말도 덧붙이지 않고 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n“이제 생활에 필요한 온갖 것을 갖추게 된 나는, 다시 한번 학문에 몰두했습니다. 그것도 예전 그 어느 때보다 더 지나치리만치 열심이었지요. 이제 오로지 내 시간을 채운 책들은, 고대의 것이든 근대의 것이든, 참된 철학을 다룬 것들이었습니다. 많은 이가 그저 익살극과 조롱의 소재로나 여기는 그 ‘철학’ 말이지요. 나는 이제 아리스토텔레스와 플라톤의 저작들을, 그리고 고대 그리스가 세상에 물려준 그 헤아릴 수 없는 보배들의 나머지를 두루 읽었습니다.\n\n“이 저자들은, 비록 사람이 조금이라도 부나 세속의 권력을 얻으리라 기대할 만한 어떤 학문도 가르쳐 주지는 않았지만, 그 둘의 가장 높은 성취조차 경멸하는 기술만큼은 가르쳐 주었습니다. 그것들은 마음을 드높이고, 운명의 변덕스러운 침입에 맞서 마음을 단련하고 굳세게 합니다. 그것들은 지혜의 지식을 가르쳐 줄 뿐 아니라, 사람을 그 지혜의 습관 안에 단단히 세워 주며, 만일 우리가 가장 큰 세속의 행복에 이르고자 하거나, 어디서나 우리를 에워싸고 둘러싼 그 불행에 맞서 그런대로 안전하게 자신을 지키고자 한다면, 바로 이 지혜야말로 우리의 인도자가 되어야 함을 또렷이 일러 줍니다.\n\n“여기에 나는 또 하나의 공부를 더했는데, 이에 비하면 가장 지혜로운 이교도들이 가르친 온갖 철학도 한낱 꿈만큼이나 못한 것이며, 실로 더없이 어리석은 익살꾼이 멋대로 그려 보인 것만큼이나 허망함으로 가득 찬 것입니다. 그것은 바로 성경 안에서만 찾을 수 있는 신성한 지혜입니다. 성경은 이 세상이 우리에게 받아들이라 내놓을 수 있는 그 모든 것보다 훨씬 더 주목할 가치가 있는 것들의 지식과 확신을 우리에게 전해 주니까요. 그것은 하늘 그 자신이 우리에게 굽어살피사 드러내 주신 것들이며, 그 가장 작은 지식조차 도움 없는 가장 높은 인간의 지혜로는 결코 다다를 수 없는 것들입니다. 나는 이제, 최고의 이교도 저자들과 보낸 그 모든 시간이 헛수고나 다름없었다고 여기기 시작했습니다. 그들의 가르침이 아무리 즐겁고 유쾌하다 한들, 또 오직 이 세상에 관한 우리 행동을 바르게 다스리는 데 아무리 알맞다 한들, 성경에 계시된 영광에 견주면, 그들의 가장 드높은 가르침조차 아이들이 어린애 같은 놀이와 소일을 정하는 규칙만큼이나 하찮고 보잘것없어 보일 테니까요. 참으로, 철학은 우리를 더 지혜롭게 하지만, 기독교는 우리를 더 나은 사람으로 만듭니다. 철학은 마음을 드높이고 단련하지만, 기독교는 마음을 부드럽게 하고 다정하게 합니다. 전자는 우리를 인간의 찬탄의 대상으로 만들지만, 후자는 신의 사랑의 대상으로 만들지요. 전자는 우리에게 한때의 행복을 보장하지만, 후자는 영원한 행복을 보장합니다.—하지만 내 이 장광설로 당신을 지치게 하는 건 아닌지 모르겠군요.”\n\n“전혀 아닙니다,” 파트리지가 외쳤다. “좋은 이야기에 우리가 지치다니, 그런 일이 있어서야 되겠습니까!”\n\n“나는,” 낯선 이가 말을 이었다, “약 사 년을, 나로서는 더없이 즐거운 방식으로 보냈습니다. 온전히 사색에 몸을 맡기고, 세상사에 조금도 시달리지 않은 채로 말이지요. 그러던 차에 나는 더없이 훌륭한 아버지를, 그토록 온 마음으로 사랑했던 그분을 잃었습니다. 그 상실의 슬픔은 도무지 무어라 말로 다 할 수가 없습니다. 나는 이제 책을 내던지고, 꼬박 한 달을 우울과 절망의 손아귀에 나를 내맡겼지요. 그러나 마음의 가장 좋은 의사인 시간이 마침내 내게 위안을 가져다주었습니다.”—“그렇지요, 그렇고말고요. ‘템푸스 에닥스 레룸(시간은 만물을 삼키는 것).’” 파트리지가 말했다.—“그래서 나는,” 낯선 이가 말을 이었다, “다시 예전의 공부로 돌아갔고, 그것이 나를 온전히 낫게 했다고 할 수 있습니다. 철학과 종교는 마음의 운동이라 부를 수 있는데, 마음이 흐트러졌을 때 그것들은 병든 몸에 운동이 그러하듯 이롭기 때문이지요. 그것들은 실로 운동과 비슷한 효과를 냅니다. 마음을 튼튼히 하고 굳건히 다져, 마침내 사람이 호라티우스의 그 고귀한 가락처럼 되게 하니까요—\n\n포르티스, 에트 인 세입소 토투스 테레스 아트퀘 로툰두스,\n엑스테르니 네 퀴드 발레아트 페르 라이베 모라리;\n\n인 퀘엠 만카 루이트 셈페르 포르투나.”[*]\n[*] 스스로 안에 굳세게 서서, 자신에게 기대고,\n\n반들반들 둥글게 다듬어져, 제 길을 달리며\n남보다 큰 힘으로 불운을 부수는 사람.—프랜시스 씨 역.\n\n여기서 존스는 제 상상 속으로 비집고 든 어떤 익살스러운 생각에 빙긋 웃었다. 그러나 낯선 이는 그것을 눈치채지 못한 듯, 이렇게 이야기를 이어 갔다.—\n“그 더없이 훌륭한 분의 죽음으로 내 형편은 이제 크게 달라졌습니다. 이제 그 집의 주인이 된 형은 성향이 나와 너무나 달랐고, 우리의 인생 행로가 워낙 판이했던 탓에, 우리는 서로에게 더없이 못된 길동무였으니까요. 그런데 함께 사는 것을 한층 더 거북하게 만든 것은, 나를 찾아오는 몇 안 되는 이들과, 들판에서 식탁까지 형을 자주 따라다니는 그 수많은 사냥꾼 무리 사이에 도무지 화합이라곤 있을 수 없다는 점이었습니다. 그런 자들은 진중한 이들의 귀를 시끄러움과 헛소리로 괴롭히는 것도 모자라, 늘 모욕과 경멸로 그들을 공격하려 들었으니까요. 그 정도가 어찌나 심했던지, 나든 내 벗들이든 그들과 한 끼라도 함께 앉으면 으레 조롱을 당하기 일쑤였습니다. 우리가 사냥꾼들의 은어를 모른다는 이유에서였지요. 참된 학식과 거의 두루 통하는 지식을 갖춘 사람은 늘 남의 무지를 가엾이 여기는 법이지만, 어떤 자질구레하고 천하며 하찮은 기술에 뛰어난 자들은 영락없이 그 기술을 모르는 이들을 업신여기게 마련이니까요.\n\n“요컨대, 우리는 곧 갈라섰고, 나는 한 의사의 권유에 따라 바스의 광천수를 마시러 갔습니다. 격렬한 비탄에 앉아만 지내는 생활까지 더해져, 나는 일종의 마비성 질환에 걸렸는데, 그 광천수가 거의 ‘확실한’ 치료제로 통했기 때문이지요. 도착한 이튿날, 강가를 거닐고 있는데 (아직 한 해의 이른 철이었음에도) 햇볕이 어찌나 뜨겁게 내리쬐던지, 나는 버드나무 그늘로 물러나 강가에 앉았습니다. 거기 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 버드나무 저편에서 누군가가 한숨을 쉬며 비통하게 신세를 한탄하는 소리가 들렸습니다. 그러더니 갑자기 더없이 불경한 욕설을 내뱉고는, ‘더는 못 견디겠다’고 외치며 곧장 물속으로 몸을 던지는 것이었습니다. 나는 즉시 벌떡 일어나 그 자리로 달려가면서, 동시에 있는 힘껏 큰 소리로 도움을 청했습니다. 운 좋게도 내 조금 아래쪽에서 한 낚시꾼이 낚시를 하고 있었는데, 무성히 높은 사초가 그를 내 시야에서 가리고 있었지요. 그가 곧장 올라와, 우리 둘이 함께, 적잖이 목숨을 건 끝에 그 몸을 강가로 끌어냈습니다. 처음에는 생명의 기색이 조금도 남아 있지 않은 듯했지만, 발꿈치를 잡아 몸을 거꾸로 들어 올리니(곧 충분히 도울 손길이 모였으니까요), 입에서 엄청난 양의 물이 쏟아져 나왔고, 마침내 숨을 쉬는 기미가 보이기 시작하더니, 조금 뒤에는 두 손과 두 다리를 움직이기 시작했습니다.\n\n“마침 다른 이들 틈에 있던 한 약제사가, 이제 물을 거의 다 토해 낸 듯하고 경련하듯 몸을 자주 움직이기 시작한 그 사람을 곧장 들어 따뜻한 침상으로 옮기는 것이 좋겠다고 일러 주었습니다. 그래서 약제사와 내가 그를 돌보며 그렇게 했지요.\n\n“우리는 그 사람의 숙소를 몰랐던 터라 어느 여관 쪽으로 향하고 있었는데, 운 좋게도 한 여인이 우리를 만났습니다. 그녀는 한바탕 요란하게 비명을 지른 뒤, 그 신사가 자기 집에 묵고 있다고 알려 주었지요.\n\n“나는 그 사람이 거기 무사히 눕는 것을 보고 나서, 약제사의 손에 그를 맡기고 자리를 떴습니다. 약제사가 그에게 온갖 알맞은 처치를 했으리라 짐작합니다. 이튿날 아침 그가 정신을 완전히 되찾았다는 소식을 들었으니까요.\n\n“그래서 나는 그를 찾아갔습니다. 그토록 절망적인 짓을 저지르려 한 까닭을 가능한 한 캐어내고, 또 앞으로 그런 사악한 의도를 좇지 못하도록 내 힘닿는 데까지 막아 보려는 마음에서였지요. 그의 방으로 들어서기가 무섭게, 우리 둘은 단번에 서로를 알아보았습니다. 그 사람이 다름 아닌 나의 좋은 벗 왓슨 씨였으니까요! 여기서 우리가 처음 마주했을 때 오간 이야기로 당신을 번거롭게 하지는 않겠습니다. 되도록 장황함은 피하고 싶으니까요.”—“부디 다 들려주십시오,” 파트리지가 외쳤다. “그가 무슨 일로 바스에 왔는지 너무도 알고 싶습니다.”\n\n“중요한 것은 모두 들려드리지요,” 낯선 이가 대답했다. 그러고는 그가 이야기하려던 바를 풀어놓기 시작했는데, 그것은 우리가 우리 자신과 독자에게 잠시 숨 돌릴 틈을 준 뒤에 이어서 쓰기로 하겠다.\n\n「언덕 위의 사내」가 자신의 이야기를 마무리하는 장.\n\n\"왓슨 씨는,\" 낯선 사내가 말을 이었다. \"거듭된 불운의 물결로 형편이 더없이 비참해진 나머지, 어떻게 보면 스스로 목숨을 끊겠다는 결심에까지 떠밀리고 말았노라고 아주 거리낌 없이 털어놓더이다.\n\n\"나는 이 이교도적인, 아니 실로 악마적인 자살 정당화의 원리에 맞서 그와 매우 진지하게 따져 묻기 시작했고, 그 문제에 관해 떠오르는 것은 무엇이든 다 말했소이다. 그러나 매우 안타깝게도, 그것은 그에게 거의 아무런 영향도 미치지 못하는 듯했소. 그는 자기가 저지른 일을 조금도 뉘우치는 기색이 없었고, 머지않아 같은 끔찍한 짓을 또다시 시도하리라는 두려움을 내게 안겨 주었소.\n\n\"내 이야기를 끝맺었을 때, 그는 내 논리에 대답하려 애쓰는 대신, 나를 뚫어지게 바라보더니 미소를 지으며 이렇게 말하더이다. '여보게, 좋은 친구, 내가 기억하던 자네와는 참 묘하게 달라졌군. 자살을 반대하는 논리라면, 우리 주교들 가운데 누구라도 자네가 내게 들려준 것보다 더 나은 걸 펼칠 수 있을지 의문일세. 하지만 내게 선뜻 백 파운드를 빌려줄 누군가를 찾아내지 못하는 한, 나는 목을 매든, 물에 빠지든, 굶어 죽든 해야 할 처지일세. 그리고 내 생각엔 그 세 가지 중에서 마지막 죽음이 가장 끔찍하지.'\n\n\"나는 매우 엄숙하게 대답했소. 지난번에 본 이후로 나는 정말 달라졌노라고. 내 어리석음을 들여다보고 그것을 뉘우칠 여유를 얻었노라고. 그러고는 그에게도 똑같은 길을 걸으라고 권했고, 마침내 그의 일에 조금이나마 보탬이 된다면, 그리고 그가 그것을 주사위 한 번에 빼앗기는 처지로 내몰지만 않는다면, 내가 직접 백 파운드를 빌려주겠노라는 다짐으로 말을 맺었소.\n\n\"내 이야기의 앞부분에서는 거의 졸음에 잠긴 듯 보이던 왓슨 씨가, 뒷부분에서는 정신이 번쩍 들었던 모양이오. 그는 내 손을 와락 붙잡고는 천 번이나 고맙다 하며 내가 진정한 친구라고 외쳤소. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, 자기가 그토록 자주 자기를 속여 먹은 그 빌어먹을 주사위 따위에 다시 믿음을 둘 만큼 경험에서 배운 게 없는 인간이라고 여길 정도로, 내가 자기를 그리 못나게 보지는 않으리라 믿는다고 했소. '아니, 아니지.' 그가 외쳤소. '한 번만 그럴듯하게 다시 일어설 밑천만 마련된다면, 그 뒤에 운명이 또다시 나를 망한 장사꾼으로 만든다 해도 나는 그 운명을 용서하겠네.'\n\n\"나는 '밑천을 세운다'느니 '망한 장사꾼'이라느니 하는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 잘 알고 있었소. 그래서 나는 아주 엄숙한 얼굴로 그에게 말했소. 왓슨 씨, 당신은 어떻게든 생계를 꾸릴 만한 사업이나 일자리를 찾아내도록 애써야 하오. 그리고 약속하건대, 훗날 되돌려 받을 가망이 조금이라도 보인다면, 나는 당신이 정직하고 떳떳한 어떤 일에든 발을 들이도록 당신이 말한 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 액수라도 대 주겠소. 허나 노름으로 말하자면, 그것을 직업으로 삼는 일의 비천함과 사악함은 차치하더라도, 내가 직접 아는 바로는 당신은 정말로 노름에 맞지 않는 사람이고, 그것은 당신의 확실한 파멸로 끝날 것이오.\n\n\"'아니, 그것 참 이상하군.' 그가 대답했소. '자네도, 내 친구들 가운데 누구도, 내가 그 일에 대해 뭔가 안다는 걸 도무지 인정해 주질 않으려 하지. 하지만 내 생각엔 어떤 노름에서든 나는 자네들 누구 못지않게 솜씨가 좋은 사람일세. 자네의 전 재산을 걸고 나하고만 한판 붙어 보면 좋으련만. 그보다 더 좋은 놀이는 바라지도 않을 테고, 게다가 종목은 자네더러 정하라 하겠네. 한데 여보게, 친구, 그 백 파운드는 주머니에 있는가?'\n\n\"나는 50파운드짜리 어음 한 장밖에 없다고 대답하고는 그것을 그에게 건넸으며, 나머지는 다음 날 아침에 가져다주겠노라 약속했소. 그러고는 충고를 몇 마디 더 해 준 뒤에 작별을 고했소.\n\n\"실은 나는 약속보다 더 잘 지킨 셈이오. 바로 그날 오후에 그에게 다시 돌아갔으니 말이오. 방에 들어서니, 그는 침대에 일어나 앉아 악명 높은 노름꾼 하나와 카드놀이를 하고 있었소. 짐작하겠지만 이 광경에 나는 적잖이 충격을 받았소. 게다가 내 어음이 그의 손에서 상대편에게 건네지고, 그 대가로 고작 30기니만 받는 꼴을 본 굴욕까지 더해졌소.\n\n\"상대 노름꾼은 곧 방을 나갔고, 그러자 왓슨은 나를 볼 면목이 없다고 했소. '하지만,' 그가 말하더이다. '운이 어찌나 지독하게 나를 거스르는지, 이제 노름이라면 영영 손을 떼기로 결심하겠네. 자네가 내게 해 준 친절한 제안을 그 뒤로 줄곧 생각해 왔는데, 약속하건대 그것을 실행에 옮기지 못한다면 그건 내 탓이 아닐 걸세.'\n\n\"그의 약속에 큰 믿음은 없었지만, 나는 내 약속에 따라 백 파운드의 나머지를 그에게 내주었소. 그는 그 대가로 차용증 한 장을 써 주었는데, 그것이 내 돈의 대가로 내가 보게 되리라 기대한 전부였소.\n\n\"우리는 약제사가 들이닥치는 바람에 더 이상의 이야기를 나누지 못했소. 그는 얼굴에 가득한 기쁨을 띠고, 환자에게 안부조차 묻지 않은 채, 자기 앞으로 온 편지에 굉장한 소식이 도착했다고 떠벌렸소. 곧 세상에 알려질 일이라면서 말이오. '몬머스 공작이 네덜란드의 대군을 이끌고 서부에 상륙했으며, 또 다른 대규모 함대가 노퍽 해안 위를 맴돌다가 그곳에 상륙하여, 그쪽에서의 양동작전으로 공작의 거사를 거들 것이다.'\n\n\"이 약제사는 당대 최고의 정치통 가운데 하나였소. 그는 더없이 보잘것없는 우편물 하나에도 최고의 환자보다 더 기뻐했고, 그가 누릴 수 있는 가장 큰 기쁨이란 어떤 소식을 마을의 다른 누구보다 한두 시간 일찍 손에 넣는 데서 오는 것이었소. 하지만 그의 정보는 미덥기 어려운 것이어서, 그는 거의 무엇이든 진실이라고 덥석 삼켰소. 많은 이들이 그 버릇을 이용해 그를 속여 먹었소.\n\n\"이번에 그가 전한 소식도 그러했소. 머지않아 알려진 바에 따르면, 공작이 실제로 상륙한 것은 사실이었으나 그의 군대라고는 고작 몇 안 되는 수행원들뿐이었고, 노퍽에서의 양동작전에 관해서는 완전히 거짓이었소.\n\n\"약제사는 우리에게 소식을 전하는 동안만 방에 머물렀다가, 환자에게 다른 어떤 화제에 관해서도 한마디 건네지 않은 채, 자기 정보를 온 마을에 퍼뜨리러 떠났소.\n\n\"이런 종류의 공적인 사건들은 대체로 온갖 사사로운 관심사를 무색하게 만들기 마련이오. 그래서 우리의 이야기도 이제 완전히 정치적인 것이 되었소.[*] 나로 말하자면, 가톨릭교도 군주 아래에서 신교가 그토록 뚜렷이 위험에 노출되어 있는 것에 한동안 매우 진지하게 마음을 쓰고 있었고, 그 우려 하나만으로도 그 봉기를 정당화하기에 충분하다고 여겼소. 권력으로 무장한 가톨릭의 박해 정신에 맞설 진정한 안전이란, 곧 드러난 비참한 경험이 보여 주었듯, 그 권력을 빼앗는 것 말고는 결코 찾을 수 없으니 말이오. 제임스 왕이 이 시도를 제압한 뒤에 어떻게 처신했는지, 자기 왕의 말이나 대관식 서약이나 백성의 자유와 권리를 얼마나 하찮게 여겼는지는 그대도 알 것이오. 그러나 모두가 처음부터 이를 내다볼 분별을 지녔던 것은 아니오. 그래서 몬머스 공작은 변변치 못한 지지를 받았소. 허나 화가 닥치자 모두가 느낄 수 있었고, 그리하여 마침내 모두가 한데 뭉쳐 그 왕을 몰아냈소. 그의 형이 다스리던 시절에 우리 가운데 큰 무리가 그토록 열렬히 그의 왕위 계승 배제에 맞서 싸웠던 바로 그 왕, 그들이 이제 그토록 열의와 애정으로 옹호하며 싸웠던 바로 그 왕을 말이오.\"\n\n\"하시는 말씀이,\" 존스가 끼어들었다. \"참으로 옳습니다. 그리고 제가 역사책에서 읽은 것 가운데 가장 놀라운 일이라고 늘 여겨 온 것이 있는데, 우리 종교와 자유를 지키기 위해 온 나라가 그토록 한마음으로 제임스 왕을 몰아내는 데 함께 나서게 한 그 명백한 경험 직후에, 우리 가운데 그의 가문을 다시 왕좌에 앉히고 싶어 할 만큼 미친 무리가 있을 수 있다는 사실입니다.\" \"진심으로 하는 말은 아니겠지!\" 노인이 대답했다. \"그런 무리가 있을 리 없네. 내가 인류를 아무리 나쁘게 본다 해도, 그들이 그 정도까지 정신이 홀렸으리라고는 믿을 수 없네. 사제들에게 이끌려 이 무모한 대의에 뛰어들어 그것을 성전으로 여기는 머리 뜨거운 가톨릭교도 몇몇이야 있을 수 있겠지. 허나 신교도들이, 그것도 영국 국교회의 신자들이 그런 배교자, 그런 자살자가 되리라고는 도무지 믿을 수 없네. 아니, 아니야, 젊은이, 내가 지난 삼십 년 동안 세상에서 일어난 일에 어둡기는 하다만, 그토록 어리석은 이야기를 곧이듣도록 속아 넘어갈 수는 없네. 보아하니 그대는 내 무지를 가지고 장난을 칠 작정인 게로군.\"—\"그토록 세상을 벗어나 사신 나머지,\" 존스가 대꾸했다. \"그동안 제임스 왕의 아들을 옹립하려는 반란이 두 차례나 있었고, 그 가운데 하나가 지금 실제로 이 왕국의 한복판에서 맹렬히 타오르고 있다는 것을 모르신다는 게 가당한 일입니까?\" 이 말에 노신사는 벌떡 일어나, 더없이 엄숙한 목소리로 그가 한 말이 정말 사실이냐고 창조주의 이름으로 존스에게 다짐을 받으려 했다. 상대방이 똑같이 엄숙하게 그것을 확인해 주자, 그는 깊은 침묵 속에 방 안을 몇 바퀴 거닐더니, 울다가 웃다가 하다가 마침내 무릎을 꿇고는, 그토록 괴이한 광기를 부릴 수 있는 인간 본성과의 온갖 교제로부터 자신을 건져 주신 것에 대해 큰 소리로 감사 기도를 올리며 하느님을 찬미했다. 그 뒤에 존스로부터 이야기가 끊겼다는 것을 일깨움받고는, 그는 다시 이런 식으로 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"내가 말하던 그 시절에는 인류가, 지금은 그들이 능히 부릴 수 있다고 내가 깨닫게 된 그런 광기의 절정에는 아직 이르지 못했었소. 물론 나야 홀로, 그 전염에서 멀찍이 떨어져 살았던 덕에 그것을 면했을 뿐이오만. 어쨌든 그때 몬머스를 옹호하는 상당한 봉기가 일어났고, 내 신념도 같은 편에 서도록 나를 강하게 기울게 한 터라, 나는 그에게 합류하기로 결심했소. 그리고 왓슨 씨도 다른 동기에서 같은 결심에 뜻을 같이하니(노름꾼의 기질이란 그런 경우에 애국심의 기질만큼이나 멀리까지 사람을 이끄는 법이오), 우리는 곧 필요한 것들을 모두 갖추고 브리지워터에 있는 공작에게로 갔소.\n\n\"이 거사의 불행한 결말은, 짐작건대 그대도 나만큼 잘 알고 있을 것이오. 나는 왓슨 씨와 함께 세지무어 전투에서 빠져나왔는데, 그 싸움에서 나는 가벼운 상처를 입었소. 우리는 함께 엑서터로 가는 길을 40마일 가까이 말을 달렸고, 그런 다음 말을 버리고 들판과 샛길을 헤치며 갈 수 있는 데까지 기어가다시피 하여, 마침내 어느 공유지에 있는 자그마한 외딴 오두막에 다다랐소. 거기서 가난한 노파 하나가 우리를 정성껏 보살펴 주었고, 내 상처에 연고를 발라 주니 금세 아물었소.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 어르신, 상처는 어디였습니까?\" 파트리지가 물었다. 낯선 사내는 팔이었다고 일러 그를 만족시켜 준 다음, 이야기를 이어 갔다. \"바로 여기서,\" 그가 말했다. \"왓슨 씨는 이튿날 아침에 나를 떠났소. 그는 우리가 먹을 식량을 콜럼튼 마을에서 좀 구해 오겠다는 핑계를 댔소. 그러나—내가 이 이야기를 할 수 있을까, 또 그대가 믿을 수 있을까?—이 왓슨 씨가, 이 친구가, 이 비열하고 야만스럽고 배신을 일삼는 악당이, 나를 제임스 왕에게 속한 기병 한 무리에게 팔아넘기고는, 돌아와서 나를 그들의 손에 넘겨주었소.\n\n\"여섯 명으로 이루어진 그 병사들은 이제 나를 붙잡아 톤턴 감옥으로 끌고 갔소. 허나 나의 지금 처지도, 내게 닥칠지 모를 일에 대한 두려움도, 그 거짓된 친구와 한자리에 있는 것의 절반만큼도 내 마음을 괴롭히지는 못했소. 그는 스스로 항복했기에 마찬가지로 포로로 간주되었으나, 나를 희생시켜 제 안위를 도모할 자였기에 더 나은 대접을 받았소. 그는 처음에는 자기 배신을 변명하려 들었지만, 내게서 멸시와 책망 말고는 아무것도 돌아오지 않자, 금세 어조를 바꾸어 나를 더없이 흉악하고 악의에 찬 반역자라고 욕하며, 자기 죄를 모조리 내게 뒤집어씌웠소. 그의 말로는, 자비롭고도 합법적인 군주에게 맞서 무기를 들도록 내가 그를 꾀고 심지어 협박까지 했다는 것이었소.\n\n\"이 거짓 증언은(실은 둘 중에 훨씬 더 앞장섰던 것은 바로 그였소만) 나를 뼛속까지 찔렀고, 그것을 겪어 보지 못한 이들은 좀처럼 헤아릴 수 없을 분노를 불러일으켰소. 그러나 마침내 운명이 내게 연민을 베풀었소. 우리가 웰링턴을 조금 지나 좁은 샛길에 이르렀을 때, 내 호위병들에게 적군 쉰 명 가까이가 코앞에 있다는 거짓 경보가 들어왔소. 그러자 그들은 제 한 몸 챙기기에 바빠, 나와 나를 배신한 자를 내버려 두고 각자 알아서 하게 했소. 그 악당은 즉시 내게서 달아났는데, 차라리 잘된 일이오. 그러지 않았다면 나는 무기 하나 없는 처지였음에도 분명 그의 비열함에 복수하려 들었을 테니 말이오.\n\n\"이제 나는 다시 한 번 자유의 몸이 되었소. 그래서 곧장 큰길에서 벗어나 들판으로 들어가, 어느 쪽으로 가는지도 거의 모른 채 계속 걸었고, 모든 큰길과 모든 마을을—아니, 심지어 가장 허름한 집까지도—피하는 것을 가장 큰 일로 삼았소. 내 눈에 띄는 사람이라면 누구나 나를 팔아넘기려 한다고 여겼기 때문이오.\n\n\"마침내 며칠을 시골 일대를 떠돌며—그동안 들판은 조물주가 미물인 우리 형제들에게 베푸는 것과 똑같은 잠자리와 똑같은 먹을거리를 내게 주었소—나는 이윽고 이곳에 다다랐소. 이 고장의 한적함과 거친 정취가 나더러 거처를 정하라 손짓했소. 내가 처음 거처를 함께한 사람은 이 노파의 어머니였는데, 나는 영광스러운 혁명의 소식이 위험에 대한 내 모든 두려움을 끝내 주고, 다시 한 번 내 고향 집을 찾아가 내 일을 조금 알아볼 기회를 줄 때까지 그녀와 함께 숨어 지냈소. 그 일은 곧 형에게나 내게나 흡족하게 매듭지어졌소. 나는 모든 것을 형에게 넘겼고, 그 대가로 형은 내게 천 파운드의 돈을 치르고 평생 받을 연금을 정해 주었소.\n\n\"이 마지막 경우에도, 다른 모든 경우와 마찬가지로 그의 처신은 이기적이고 인색했소. 나는 그를 내 벗으로 여길 수 없었고, 사실 그 또한 내가 그러기를 바라지 않았소. 그래서 나는 곧 그에게도, 또 다른 지인들에게도 작별을 고했소. 그날 이후 오늘에 이르기까지 내 이야기는 백지나 다름없소.\"\n\n\"그러면 어르신,\" 존스가 말했다. \"그날부터 지금까지 이곳에서 줄곧 지내신 게 가능한 일입니까?\"—\"오, 아니라네.\" 신사가 대답했다. \"나는 대단한 여행가였고, 내가 모르는 유럽 땅이라곤 거의 없네.\" \"지금 어르신께 그것을 청할 만큼,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"뻔뻔하지는 못하겠습니다. 정말이지 이미 그토록 많은 기운을 쓰셨는데 그것을 청한다면 너무 가혹한 일이겠지요. 다만 어르신처럼 분별 있고 세상 물정에 밝으신 분이 그토록 오랜 여정 동안 하셨을 훌륭한 견문을 들을 기회를 다음에 또 갖기를 바라는 것만은 허락해 주십시오.\"—\"정말이지, 젊은 신사 양반,\" 낯선 사내가 대답했다. \"그 점에 관해서도 내 능력이 닿는 한 그대의 호기심을 채워 주도록 애써 보겠네.\" 존스는 새삼 사양의 말을 꺼내려 했으나 가로막혔다. 그가 파트리지와 함께 탐욕스럽고 안달 난 귀를 기울이고 앉아 있는 동안, 낯선 사내는 다음 장에서와 같이 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n[*] 초판에서는 이 문단의 나머지 부분과 뒤이은 두 문단이 다음과 같았다.\n\n\"나로 말하자면, 신교가 그토록 뚜렷이 위험에 노출되어 있어 오직 섭리의 즉각적인 개입 말고는 그것을 지켜 낼 길이 없어 보이는 것에 한동안 매우 진지하게 마음을 쓰고 있었소. 제임스 왕은 실로 신교의 대의에 전쟁을 선포한 셈이었으니 말이오. 그는 알려진 가톨릭교도들을 군대에 들여놓았고, 그들을 교회와 대학에까지 들여놓으려 했소. 가톨릭 사제들이 온 나라에 들끓었고, 자기네 사제복을 입고 공공연히 나돌았으며, 머지않아 거리를 행렬 지어 행진하게 되리라고 떠벌렸소. 우리 성직자들은 가톨릭을 반박하는 설교가 금지되었고, 주교들은 그런 설교를 하는 자들을 정직시키라는 명령을 받았소. 그리고 이 일을 단번에 해치우려고, 종교재판소에 거의 못지않은 불법적인 교회위원회가 세워졌는데, 아마도 그것이 그 우두머리 노릇을 하도록 의도된 것이었소. 이리하여 왕에 대한 우리의 의무란 결코 우리의 두 번째 의무 이상으로 불릴 수 없는 것인데, 그는 그것을 우리의 첫 번째 의무, 곧 하늘을 향한 의무를 지키는 일과 양립할 수 없게 만듦으로써 우리를 그 의무에서 풀어 준 셈이었소. 이뿐 아니라 그는 자신의 대관식 서약을 깨뜨림으로써 백성들을 그 충성의 의무에서 풀어 주었으니, 백성들의 충성이란 바로 그 서약에 매여 있는 것이었소. 그는 주교들이 자기네 종교를 포기하려 하지 않는다는 이유로 그들을 가두었고, 법을 자기 손에 고스란히 넘겨주려 하지 않는다는 이유로 판사들을 내쫓았소. 아니, 그는 법마저 스스로 거머쥐었고, 법을 면제할 권한을 주장했을 때, 그는 실로 일찍이 있었거나 있을 수 있는 그 어떤 폭군 못지않게 자신이 절대적임을 선언한 셈이었소. 이런 일들을 내가 낱낱이 되짚어 본 것은 그 가운데 일부가 역사에 빠졌을까 염려해서요. 그리고 내 생각에, 여기서 내가 말한 것과 같은 도발, 곧 자기네 종교와 자유에 닥친 분명하고도 임박한 위험에 못 미치는 그 어떤 것으로도, 그 어떤 백성에게든 반란이라는 무서운 죄를 정당화하거나 심지어 덜어 줄 수조차 없을 것이오.\"\n\n\"장담하건대, 어르신,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이 모든 사실은, 아니 그 이상의 것까지도 저는 역사책에서 읽었습니다. 허나 아직 기록되지 않은, 그래서 어르신께서도 모르시리라 짐작되는 한 가지 사실을 말씀드리겠습니다. 지금 이 왕국에는 바로 그 제임스 왕의 아들을 옹립하려는 반란이 실제로 일고 있는데, 그는 공언된 가톨릭교도로서 가능하다면 제 아비보다 더 광신적인 자입니다. 그리고 이 반란을 신교도들이, 우리의 자유에 단 한 차례도 털끝만 한 침해조차 가한 적 없는 왕에게 맞서 벌이고 있다는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"실로 어처구니없는 일이로군!\" 낯선 사내가 대답했다. \"베르길리우스가 여인을 두고 한 평, 곧 '언제나 변덕스럽고 변하기 쉽다'는 그 평에 합당하지 않은 나라라면, 그대가 하는 말은 믿을 수 없는 일이었을 게요. 분명 이는, 섭리가 가톨릭의 강력한 책략과 끊임없는 음모에 맞서 우리 종교를 지켜 주려 기울인 듯한 그 보살핌을 받을 자격이 없는 짓이오. 그토록 기이하고 헤아릴 수 없는 그 지켜 줌이란, 그 거룩함의 증거로서 거의 기적에 호소해도 좋으리라고 내가 여길 정도인데 말이오. 실로 어처구니없는 일이로군! 가톨릭 군주를 옹립하려는 신교도의 반란이라니! 인류의 어리석음은 그들의 간악함만큼이나 놀랍구려.—허나 내 이야기를 마무리하자면, 나는 내 나라와 내 종교와 내 자유를 지키기 위해 무기를 들기로 결심했고, 왓슨 씨도 같은 결심에 뜻을 같이했소. 우리는 곧 필요한 것들을 모두 갖추고 브리지워터에 있는 공작에게 합류했소.\"\n\n\"이 거사의 불행한 결말은 어쩌면 그대가 나보다 더 잘 알고 있을 것이오. 나는 왓슨 씨와 함께 세지무어 전투에서 빠져나왔는데,……\"\n\n# Chapter xv.\n\n유럽에 관한 짤막한 역사, 그리고 존스 씨와 산속 노인 사이에 오간 흥미로운 대화.\n\n\"이탈리아에서는 여관 주인들이 무척 말이 없습니다. 프랑스에서는 좀 더 수다스럽긴 해도 그래도 예의는 차립니다. 독일과 네덜란드에서는 대개 아주 무례하지요. 그리고 정직함으로 말하자면, 이 모든 나라가 거의 엇비슷하다고 봅니다. 삯을 받고 알랑거리는 하인 놈들은 당신을 속일 기회를 절대 놓치지 않을 게 분명하고, 마부들로 말하자면 세상 어디를 가나 다 거기서 거기인 것 같더군요. 선생, 이것이 제가 여행하면서 사람들에 대해 얻은 관찰입니다. 제가 말을 섞어 본 인간이라곤 그들뿐이었으니까요. 제가 외국으로 떠났을 때의 의도는, 하느님께서 기꺼이 이 지구의 여러 곳을 풍요롭게 채워 주신 그 경이로운 갖가지 풍경과 짐승, 새, 물고기, 곤충, 식물을 보면서 스스로를 즐겁게 하려는 것이었습니다. 그 다양함은 사색하는 구경꾼에게 큰 기쁨을 줄 뿐 아니라, 창조주의 권능과 지혜와 선하심을 더없이 훌륭하게 드러내 보여 주지요. 사실 진실을 말하자면, 그분의 온 창조물 가운데 그분께 욕을 돌리는 작품은 단 하나뿐인데, 저는 오래전부터 그것과는 어떤 대화도 나누기를 피해 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"하지만 저는 늘 생각해 왔습니다. 어르신께서 말씀하시는 바로 그 작품 안에도 다른 모든 것 못지않은 큰 다양함이 있다고요. 성향의 차이뿐 아니라, 풍습과 기후 또한 인간 본성에 더없이 큰 다채로움을 끌어들였다고 들었으니까요.\"\n\n\"실은 거의 없습니다.\" 다른 이가 대답했다. \"사람들의 서로 다른 풍속을 알고자 여행하는 자들은 베네치아의 사육제에 한 번 가 보는 것만으로도 많은 수고를 덜 수 있을 겁니다. 거기서 그들은 유럽 여러 궁정을 돌며 발견할 수 있는 것을 한꺼번에 다 보게 될 테니까요. 똑같은 위선, 똑같은 사기, 한마디로 똑같은 어리석음과 악덕이 그저 다른 옷을 걸치고 있을 뿐입니다. 스페인에서는 그것들이 무척 진중한 차림을 하고, 이탈리아에서는 굉장한 화려함으로 치장하지요. 프랑스에서는 악당이 멋쟁이처럼 차려입고, 북쪽 나라들에서는 칠칠치 못한 게으름뱅이처럼 입습니다. 그러나 인간 본성은 어디서나 똑같고, 어디서나 혐오와 경멸의 대상입니다.\n\n\"저로 말하자면, 저는 이 모든 나라를 거쳐 가기를, 마치 당신이 구경거리를 보러 가는 군중 속을 헤쳐 나가듯 했습니다. 그들을 지나치려 떠밀며, 한 손으로는 코를 막고 다른 손으로는 주머니를 지키면서, 누구에게도 한마디 건네지 않은 채 제가 보고 싶은 것을 향해 밀고 나아갔지요. 그런데 그 보고 싶던 것이 제아무리 그 자체로 즐거웠다 한들, 그 무리가 제게 끼친 성가심을 보상해 주기에는 턱없이 부족했습니다.\"\n\n\"여행하신 나라들 가운데, 다른 곳보다 덜 성가신 곳도 있지 않으셨습니까?\" 존스가 말했다. \"오, 있고말고요.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"튀르크 사람들이 기독교도들보다 제게는 훨씬 견딜 만했습니다. 그들은 깊은 과묵함을 지닌 자들이라, 낯선 이를 질문으로 괴롭히는 법이 없거든요. 물론 이따금 짤막한 욕을 던지거나, 거리를 걷는 그의 얼굴에 침을 뱉기도 하지만, 그러고 나면 그걸로 끝입니다. 그 나라에서는 한평생을 살아도 그들에게서 열두 마디조차 듣지 못할 수 있습니다. 그러나 제가 본 모든 민족 가운데, 부디 하늘이 저를 프랑스인들로부터 지켜 주시기를! 그 빌어먹을 수다와 인사치레, 그리고 이방인에게 제 나라의 명예를 베푼다면서 (그들 좋을 대로 그렇게 부릅니다만) 실은 제 허영을 늘어놓는 그 짓이라니. 어찌나 성가신지, 저는 파리에 다시 발을 들이느니 차라리 호텐토트인들과 평생을 보내는 편이 한없이 낫겠습니다. 호텐토트인들은 더러운 족속이지만, 그들의 더러움은 대개 겉에 있습니다. 반면 프랑스나, 굳이 이름을 대지 않을 몇몇 다른 나라에서는 그 더러움이 죄다 속에 있어서, 호텐토트인의 냄새가 제 코를 찌르는 것보다 그자들이 제 이성을 훨씬 더 고약하게 찌릅니다.\n\n\"이리하여, 선생, 저는 제 인생의 이야기를 마쳤습니다. 제가 이곳에 은거하며 살아온 그 모든 세월로 말하자면, 당신을 즐겁게 할 만한 변화라곤 없으니, 거의 하루로 쳐도 될 정도지요. 이 은거는 어찌나 완벽했던지, 테바이스의 사막 한가운데에서라 한들 이 인구 많은 왕국 한복판에서 누리는 것보다 더 완전한 고독을 누리지는 못했을 겁니다. 저는 재산이 없으니, 소작인이나 집사 따위로 시달릴 일이 없습니다. 제 연금은 꽤 꼬박꼬박 지급되는데, 사실 그래야 마땅하지요. 제가 내놓은 것에 대한 대가로 기대할 수 있었던 것보다 훨씬 적은 액수니까요. 방문은 일절 받지 않습니다. 제 집을 돌보는 노파는, 자기 자리가 전적으로 제게 달려 있음을 압니다. 즉 제가 필요한 물건을 사는 온갖 수고를 대신 덜어 주고, 모든 청탁이나 용무를 제게서 막아 주고, 제 귀에 들릴 만한 자리에서는 입을 다무는 일에 달려 있다는 것을요. 제 산책은 모두 밤에 이루어지므로, 이 인적 드문 황량한 곳에서 누군가와 마주칠 걱정은 거의 없습니다. 몇몇 사람을 우연히 만나기는 했는데, 제 옷차림과 모습이 하도 괴이해서 저를 유령이나 도깨비로 여기는 바람에 혼비백산하여 집으로 돌려보냈지요. 그러나 오늘 밤 일어난 일은, 이곳에서조차 제가 인간의 악행으로부터 안전할 수 없음을 보여 줍니다. 당신의 도움이 없었다면 저는 강도를 당했을 뿐 아니라, 십중팔구 살해당했을 테니까요.\"\n\n존스는 이방인이 자기 이야기를 들려주느라 애써 준 것에 감사한 뒤, 그가 어떻게 그토록 고독한 삶을 견뎌 낼 수 있었는지 다소 놀라움을 표했다. \"그런 삶에서는,\" 그가 말했다. \"변화가 없다고 하소연하실 만도 합니다. 정말이지 저는 어르신께서 그 많은 시간을 어떻게 채우셨는지, 아니 차라리 어떻게 죽이셨는지 놀라울 따름입니다.\"\n\n\"전혀 놀랄 일이 아닙니다.\" 다른 이가 대답했다. \"애정과 생각이 이 세상에 붙박여 있는 사람에게야, 이런 곳에서 제 시간이 할 일을 잃은 듯 보이는 게 당연하지요. 하지만 단 한 가지 일이 있으니, 인간의 한평생을 다 바쳐도 한없이 모자란 일입니다. 저 영광스럽고 불멸하며 영원하신 존재를 묵상하고 경배하는 데에 무슨 시간인들 넉넉하겠습니까? 그분의 그 어마어마한 창조물 가운데서는, 이 지구는 물론이거니와 우리가 여기서 온 하늘에 반짝이는 것으로 바라보는 저 헤아릴 수 없는 빛들조차도—그 가운데 상당수가 서로 다른 세계의 체계를 비추는 태양일 수 있음에도—우리가 사는 이 온 지구에 견주면 한낱 몇 개의 티끌처럼 보일 따름일 텐데 말입니다. 거룩한 묵상으로 말미암아 이 말로 다할 수 없고 헤아릴 수 없는 위엄과의 대화에 이를테면 받아들여진 사람이, 그토록 황홀한 영예가 이어지는 데에 며칠이든, 몇 년이든, 몇 세기든 너무 길다고 여기겠습니까? 하찮은 오락, 물리도록 시들한 쾌락, 세상의 어리석은 용무 따위가 우리에게서 시간을 너무 빨리 굴려 가 버리는데, 그토록 높고, 그토록 중대하며, 그토록 영광스러운 연구에 힘쓰는 정신에게 시간의 걸음이 굼떠 보이겠습니까? 어떤 시간도 넉넉하지 않듯, 이 위대한 관심사에 어울리지 않는 장소도 없습니다. 우리가 눈길을 던지는 그 어떤 대상이 그분의 권능과 지혜와 선하심에 대한 생각을 우리에게 불어넣지 않겠습니까? 떠오르는 해가 동녘 지평선 위로 그 불타는 영광을 쏘아 올릴 필요도 없고, 사나운 바람이 동굴에서 뛰쳐나와 우뚝한 숲을 뒤흔들 필요도 없으며, 갈라진 구름이 들판에 홍수를 쏟아부을 필요도 없습니다. 다시 말하건대, 이 가운데 어느 것도 그분의 위엄을 선포할 필요가 없습니다. 창조물 가운데 아무리 낮은 등급의 곤충이라도, 식물이라도, 그 위대한 창조주의 속성을 새긴 표지를 영광스럽게 지니지 않은 것은 없습니다. 그분의 권능뿐 아니라 지혜와 선하심의 표지를 말입니다. 오직 인간만이, 이 지구의 왕이요, 태양 아래에서 지존하신 존재의 마지막이자 가장 위대한 작품인 인간만이, 비열하게도 제 본성에 욕을 돌렸습니다. 그리고 부정직과 잔인함, 배은망덕과 배반으로써 제 창조주의 선하심을 의문에 부쳤으니, 자애로운 존재가 어찌하여 이토록 어리석고 비천한 짐승을 빚으셨는지를 헤아리느라 우리를 곤혹스럽게 만든 것입니다. 그런데도 이것이 바로, 제가 안타깝게도 그와의 대화로부터 차단되어 있고, 또 그 복된 무리와 함께하지 못하니 당신 생각으로는 제 삶이 따분하고 무미건조할 수밖에 없다고 여기시는, 바로 그 존재로군요.\"\n\n\"방금 말씀하신 앞부분에는,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"제가 진심으로 또 기꺼이 동의합니다. 그러나 결론에서 인류를 향해 드러내신 그 혐오는, 너무 지나치게 일반화된 것이라고 저는 믿고 또 바랍니다. 정말이지, 어르신께서는 여기서 한 가지 오류에 빠지셨는데, 제 짧은 경험으로 보아도 매우 흔한 오류입니다. 인류 가운데 가장 나쁘고 가장 비열한 자들에게서 인류의 성격을 끌어내신 것이지요. 어느 훌륭한 작가가 말했듯, 사실 어떤 종(種)의 특징으로 삼아야 할 것은, 그 종 가운데 가장 뛰어나고 가장 완전한 개체들에게서 찾아볼 수 있는 것뿐인데 말입니다. 이 오류는, 제 믿음으로는, 벗이나 지인을 고를 때 마땅한 신중함이 부족하여 나쁘고 보잘것없는 자들에게서 해를 입은 이들이 흔히 저지르는 것입니다. 그런 두세 가지 사례를 두고 인간 본성 전체에 부당하게 죄를 씌우는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"저는 그것을 충분히 겪어 봤다고 생각합니다.\" 다른 이가 대답했다. \"제 첫 연인과 제 첫 벗이 더없이 비열한 방식으로 저를 배신했고, 그것도 최악의 결과로 이어질 뻔한 일들에서—심지어 저를 수치스러운 죽음으로 몰아갈 뻔한 일들에서 그리했습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 용서하십시오.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그 연인이 누구였고 그 벗이 누구였는지 한번 돌이켜 보시기를 청하고 싶습니다. 어르신, 매음굴에서 비롯된 사랑이나, 도박판에서 처음 생겨나 자라난 우정에서 그보다 더 나은 무엇을 기대할 수 있었겠습니까? 앞의 사례에서 여자의 성격을 끌어내거나 뒤의 사례에서 남자의 성격을 끌어내는 것은, 변소에서 그렇다는 이유로 공기가 역겹고 해로운 원소라고 단언하는 것만큼이나 부당한 일입니다. 저는 이 세상에서 짧은 시간밖에 살지 않았지만, 그래도 더없이 높은 우정을 받을 만한 남자들과, 더없이 높은 사랑을 받을 만한 여자들을 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아아! 젊은이,\" 이방인이 대답했다. \"당신 스스로 고백했듯이, 당신은 이 세상에서 아주 짧은 시간밖에 살지 않았습니다. 저도 당신과 같은 생각을 품었을 때는 당신보다 다소 나이가 위였지요.\"\n\n\"어르신께서 애정을 두는 일에서 불운하지만 않으셨다면—감히 말하건대 부주의하지만 않으셨다면—지금도 여전히 그 생각을 간직하고 계셨을 겁니다.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"설령 세상에 지금보다 훨씬 많은 악이 있다 한들, 그것이 인간 본성에 대한 그런 일반적 단언을 입증해 주지는 못할 것입니다. 그 악의 상당 부분은 그저 우연으로 일어나는 것이고, 악을 저지르는 많은 사람도 그 마음속까지 온통 나쁘고 타락한 것은 아니니까요. 사실, 인간 본성이 필연적이고 보편적으로 악하다고 단언할 자격을 가진 자는, 오직 제 마음속에서 그 타고난 사악함의 한 사례를 찾아내는 자들뿐인데, 그것이 어르신의 경우가 아님을 저는 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"그런 자들이야말로,\" 이방인이 말했다. \"언제나 그런 말을 입에 올리기를 가장 꺼리는 법이지요. 악당들이 인류의 비열함을 우리에게 납득시키려 애쓰지 않는 것은, 노상강도가 길에 도둑이 있다고 당신에게 일러 주지 않는 것과 같습니다. 그렇게 한다면 당신을 경계하게 만들어 제 목적을 그르치는 셈이 될 테니까요. 그런 까닭에, 제가 기억하기로 악당들은 특정한 개인을 헐뜯기는 무척 좋아해도, 인간 본성 일반에 대해서는 결코 어떤 비난도 던지지 않습니다.\" 노신사가 어찌나 열을 올려 이 말을 하던지, 존스는 그를 설득해 생각을 바꾸게 하기를 단념했고 또 그의 비위를 거스르고 싶지도 않아서 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n이제 날이 밝아 첫 빛줄기를 내보내기 시작하자, 존스는 너무 오래 머무른 것에 대해, 또 어쩌면 그가 쉬는 것을 방해했을지도 모르는 일에 대해 이방인에게 사과했다. 이방인이 대답하기를, \"지금처럼 휴식이 필요 없을 때도 없습니다. 제게는 낮과 밤이 아무런 차이도 없는 시간이며, 보통 낮은 휴식의 시간으로, 밤은 산책과 밤늦은 사색의 시간으로 쓰니까요. 그건 그렇고,\" 그가 말했다. \"지금 무척 사랑스러운 아침이 밝았으니, 당신이 휴식이나 음식 없이 조금 더 버틸 수 있다면, 아직 못 보셨을 듯한 아주 멋진 풍경 몇 가지를 보여 드리며 기꺼이 대접하겠습니다.\"\n\n존스는 이 제안을 흔쾌히 받아들였고, 둘은 곧장 함께 오두막에서 길을 나섰다. 파트리지로 말하자면, 이방인이 이야기를 막 끝냈을 때 깊은 잠에 빠져 있었다. 그의 호기심은 충족되었고, 뒤이은 담론은 그 잠의 마력을 물리칠 만큼 강한 힘을 부리지 못했던 것이다. 그래서 존스는 그가 낮잠을 즐기도록 내버려 두었다. 그리고 독자께서도 이쯤에서 어쩌면 같은 호의를 반기실지 모르니, 우리는 이 이야기의 여덟 번째 권을 여기서 마치기로 하자.\n\n열두 시간을 담은 이야기.\n이런 이야기를 쓸 자격이 있는 자와 없는 자에 대하여.\n\n내가 이 여러 머리말 장을 따로 두는 것이 마땅하다고 여긴 데에는 여러 가지 좋은 쓰임새가 있는데, 그중 하나로 나는 이것들을 일종의 표지나 낙인쯤으로 생각해 왔다. 앞으로 어지간히 무딘 독자라도 이런 역사류 글에서 무엇이 참되고 진짜인지, 무엇이 거짓이고 가짜인지를 가려낼 수 있게 해 주는 표지 말이다. 실로 이런 표지가 머지않아 필요해질 듯도 하다. 요즘 두세 작가가 이런 종류의 작품으로 세상의 호의적인 환대를 얻은 일이, 아마도 다른 많은 이들에게 같은 일에 손대 보라고 부추기는 계기가 될 테니 말이다. 그리하여 어리석은 소설과 괴상한 연애담이 떼 지어 쏟아져 나올 것이다. 그것은 서적상을 크게 가난하게 만들거나, 아니면 독자의 시간을 크게 허비하게 하고 그 도덕을 타락시킬 것이다. 아니, 흔히는 추문과 비방을 퍼뜨려, 점잖고 정직한 많은 사람들의 명예를 해치는 데까지 이를 것이다.\n\n나는 그 재기 넘치는 《스펙테이터》의 저자가 글마다 그리스어와 라틴어 제사(題詞)를 앞머리에 붙인 것도 주로 같은 고려에서 비롯되었으리라 의심치 않는다. 즉, 작가로서의 재능이라고는 글씨 선생에게 배운 것밖에 없으면서도, 가장 위대한 천재와 똑같은 이름을 감히 내거는 데 두려움도 부끄러움도 없는 그런 끄적이는 무리들의 추격을 막으려는 것이었다. 마치 우화 속 그 어리석은 형제가 사자 가죽을 뒤집어쓰고 당나귀 울음을 우는 데 거리낌이 없었던 것처럼 말이다.\n\n그리하여 그는 제사라는 장치 덕분에, 적어도 그 학식 있는 언어로 된 한 문장은 이해하지 못하고서는 누구도 감히 《스펙테이터》를 흉내 내려 들 수 없게 만들었다. 같은 방식으로 나도 이제, 조금의 성찰조차 도무지 해낼 줄 모르고 그 학식이 한 편의 에세이에도 못 미치는 자들의 모방으로부터 나 자신을 지켜 둔 셈이다.\n\n여기서 내가, 이런 역사류 작품의 가장 큰 가치가 이 머리말 장들에 있다고 넌지시 말하는 것으로 오해되지는 않기를 바란다. 사실 단순한 서사만 담은 부분이야말로, 관찰과 성찰로 이루어진 부분보다 모방자의 붓에 훨씬 더 큰 부추김을 준다. 여기서 내가 말하는 모방자란, 로(Rowe)가 셰익스피어를 흉내 낸 것 같은 부류, 혹은 호라티우스가 넌지시 일렀듯 어떤 로마인들이 맨발과 신 얼굴만으로 카토를 흉내 낸 것 같은 부류를 뜻한다.\n\n좋은 이야기를 지어내는 것과 그것을 잘 들려주는 것은 어쩌면 아주 드문 재능일 텐데, 그런데도 나는 이 두 가지 모두를 겨냥하는 데 거리낌을 느끼는 사람을 거의 보지 못했다. 그리고 세상에 넘쳐 나는 연애담과 소설들을 살펴본다면, 우리는 이렇게 단언해도 무방하리라 본다. 그 작가 대부분은 다른 어떤 종류의 글쓰기로는 (이런 표현을 허락해 준다면) 이빨을 드러내 보일 엄두조차 내지 못했으리라고. 아니, 실은 다른 어떤 주제로도 열두 문장을 이어 엮어 낼 수조차 없었으리라고.\n\n배운 자나 못 배운 자나 가리지 않고 우리는 쓴다,[*]\n\n[*] 그 어떤 못난 멍청이도 감히 글을 쓰려 든다.\n운문은 살아 있는 모든 자의 생업이로다. — 프랜시스.\n\n이 말은 다른 어떤 종류의 글쓰기보다도 역사가와 전기 작가에게 더 진실하게 들어맞는다. 모든 기예와 학문은 (비평조차도) 약간의 배움과 지식을 요하기 때문이다. 시(詩)는 어쩌면 예외라 여겨질지도 모르겠다. 하지만 시는 운율, 혹은 운율 비슷한 무언가를 요구한다. 반면 소설과 연애담을 짓는 데는 종이와 펜, 잉크, 그리고 그것들을 손으로 부릴 능력 외에는 아무것도 필요치 않다. 이것이 작가들 스스로의 견해임을 그들의 작품이 보여 준다고 나는 생각한다. 그리고 그 독자라는 것이 정말 존재한다면, 이것이 그들 독자의 견해이기도 할 것이다.\n\n여기서 우리는, 기록에서 그 재료를 길어 내지 않는 모든 역사 저술가들에게 세상이 던지는 그 보편적인 경멸이 어디서 비롯되는지를 짐작할 수 있다. 세상이란 늘 다수를 가지고 전체를 일컫는 법이니까. 그리고 우리가 그토록 조심스레 '연애담'이라는 말을 피해 온 것도 바로 이 경멸이 두려워서다. 안 그랬다면 우리는 그 이름에 그런대로 만족했을지도 모른다. 하지만 다른 곳에서 넌지시 일렀듯, 우리는 우리의 모든 인물에 대해 든든한 전거(典據)를 갖고 있으니, 그것은 다름 아닌 자연이라는 저 방대하고도 진실한 심판의 장부이다. 그러니 우리의 노고는 '역사'라는 이름을 얻을 충분한 자격이 있다. 분명 우리의 노고는, 가장 재치 있는 어떤 사람이 그저 가려움증, 아니 차라리 두뇌의 헐거움에서 나온 것으로만 여겼던 그런 작품들과는 얼마쯤 구별될 자격이 있다.\n\n그러나 이렇게 하여, 모든 글쓰기 가운데 가장 유익하면서도 가장 재미있는 종류의 하나에 불명예가 씌워지는 것 외에도, 또 다른 종류의 불명예를 우리가 퍼뜨리게 되리라는 걱정에는 그만한 까닭이 있다. 내가 말하는 것은 사회의 점잖고 값진 많은 구성원의 명예를 두고 하는 말이다. 가장 둔한 글쟁이도 가장 둔한 동석자와 다를 바 없이 늘 무해하지만은 않기 때문이다. 둘 다 무례하고 험한 말을 늘어놓을 만큼의 언어는 충분히 갖추고 있다. 그리고 앞서 인용한 그 견해가 참이라면, 그토록 추잡한 데서 비롯된 작품이 그 자체로 추잡하거나 남들을 추잡하게 만드는 경향이 있다 한들 놀랄 일이 못 된다.\n\n그러므로 앞으로 이런 무절제한 여가의 남용, 글의 남용, 출판 자유의 남용을 막기 위해 — 특히 지금 세상이 평소보다 더 그런 위협에 시달리는 듯하니 — 나는 여기서 감히 몇 가지 자질을 들어 보려 한다. 그 하나하나가 이 부류의 역사가에게 상당히 높은 정도로 꼭 필요한 것들이다.\n\n첫째는 천부의 재능이다. 호라티우스의 말마따나, 이것이 풍부히 흐르지 않고서는 어떤 공부도 우리에게 소용이 없다. 천부의 재능이라 함은, 우리의 손과 지식이 닿는 범위 안의 모든 것을 꿰뚫어 보고 그것들의 본질적인 차이를 분별해 낼 수 있는 정신의 힘, 아니 차라리 그 여러 힘을 뜻한다. 이는 다름 아닌 발상과 판단이다. 그리고 이 둘은, 우리가 세상에 태어날 때 지니고 나오는 자연의 선물이라는 점에서, 천부의 재능이라는 한 이름으로 통틀어 불린다. 그런데 그 각각에 대해 많은 사람이 매우 큰 오류에 빠진 듯하다. 발상이라 하면 흔히 창조하는 능력으로 이해되는데, 만일 그렇다면 대부분의 연애담 작가야말로 그 능력을 가장 높이 주장할 자격이 있는 셈이 될 것이다. 그러나 발상이 정말로 뜻하는 바는 (이 말이 본디 그러하듯) 발견, 즉 찾아내는 것에 지나지 않는다. 좀 더 풀어 말하자면, 우리가 사색하는 모든 대상의 참된 본질을 빠르고 슬기롭게 꿰뚫어 보는 것이다. 이것은 판단이 함께하지 않고서는 좀처럼 존재할 수 없다고 나는 생각한다. 두 사물의 차이를 가려내지 않고서 어떻게 그 둘의 참된 본질을 발견했다 말할 수 있겠는가, 내게는 그것이 도무지 그려지지 않는다. 그런데 바로 이 후자야말로 누구도 다투지 않는 판단의 영역이다. 그런데도 재치 있는 몇몇 사람은 세상의 모든 둔한 자들과 똑같이, 이 둘이 한 사람 안에 같이 깃든 적이 거의, 아니 전혀 없다고 말하는 데 뜻을 같이했다.\n\n그러나 설령 이 둘을 다 갖추었다 해도, 상당한 배움이 더해지지 않고서는 우리의 목적에 충분치 않다. 이 점에 대해서도 호라티우스를 비롯해 많은 이들의 권위를 다시 끌어다 댈 수 있겠지만, 그럴 필요까지 있을까. 연장이란 기예로 날을 세우지 않으면, 또는 일을 이끌어 줄 규칙이 없거나 손댈 재료가 없으면, 일꾼에게 아무 쓸모가 없다는 것을 증명하는 데 말이다. 이 모든 쓰임은 배움이 채워 준다. 자연은 우리에게 그저 역량만을, 혹은 내가 즐겨 비유했듯이 우리 직업의 연장만을 마련해 줄 뿐이다. 배움은 그것들을 쓸 수 있게 다듬어 주고, 쓰는 길을 일러 주며, 끝으로 적어도 재료의 일부는 보태 주어야 한다. 여기서는 역사와 순수문학에 대한 충분한 지식이 절대로 필요하다. 적어도 이만한 지식 없이 역사가의 자격을 흉내 내려는 것은, 목재도 회반죽도 벽돌도 돌도 없이 집을 지으려 애쓰는 것만큼이나 헛된 일이다. 호메로스와 밀턴은, 비록 자기네 작품에 운율이라는 장식을 더하긴 했어도 둘 다 우리와 같은 부류의 역사가였는데, 그들은 자기 시대의 모든 학식의 대가였다.\n\n또한 배움의 힘으로는 베풀 수 없는, 또 다른 종류의 지식이 있다. 이것은 사람들과 어울려 대화하는 가운데 얻어진다. 이것은 사람들의 성격을 이해하는 데 어찌나 긴요한지, 평생을 대학과 책 사이에서만 보낸 그 학식 있는 책상물림들이야말로 사람의 성격에 가장 어두운 자들이다. 작가들이 인간 본성을 아무리 절묘하게 묘사해 놓았다 해도, 그 참된 실천적 체계는 오직 세상 속에서만 배울 수 있는 까닭이다. 실로 다른 모든 종류의 지식에서도 마찬가지다. 의술도 법률도 책만으로는 실제로 익힐 수 없다. 아니, 농부도, 농장주도, 정원사도, 읽어서 그 기초를 익힌 것을 경험으로 완성해야 한다. 그 솜씨 좋은 밀러 씨가 식물을 아무리 정확히 묘사했다 한들, 그조차도 제 제자에게 정원에 나가 직접 보라고 권할 것이다. 셰익스피어나 존슨, 위철리나 오트웨이의 더없이 섬세한 필치 뒤에도 자연의 어떤 손길은 독자를 비껴가기 마련이며, 그것을 독자에게 전해 주는 것은 개릭이나 시버, 혹은 클라이브[*]의 빈틈없는 연기임을 우리가 알아야 하듯이, 실제 무대 위에서 인물은 글로 그려질 수 있는 것보다 더 강렬하고 더 또렷한 빛으로 제 모습을 드러낸다. 위대한 작가들 스스로가 삶에서 길어 낸 그 정교하고 힘찬 묘사에서도 사정이 이러할진대, 작가가 그 글줄을 자연이 아니라 책에서 끌어왔을 때는 이 말이 얼마나 더 강하게 들어맞겠는가? 그런 인물들은 베낀 것을 다시 베낀 흐릿한 모사에 지나지 않으니, 원본의 정확함도 그 생기도 가질 수 없다.\n\n[*] 이 위대한 배우와, 더없이 마땅한 명성을 누리는 이 두 여배우를 이 자리에서 언급하는 데에는 각별한 이유가 있다. 이들은 모두 선배들의 모방이 아니라 오직 자연을 연구하는 데서 스스로를 빚어냈기 때문이다. 그리하여 이들은 앞서간 모든 이를 능가할 수 있었으니, 그것은 노예처럼 따라 하는 모방자의 무리는 결코 다다를 수 없는 경지의 가치다.\n\n그런데 우리 역사가의 이 대화는 두루 걸쳐야 한다. 다시 말해 온갖 신분과 계층의 사람들과 두루 나누는 것이라야 한다. 이른바 상류층의 삶을 안다고 해서 하류층을 알게 되는 것은 아니며, 거꾸로 인류의 낮은 부류와 친숙하다 해서 높은 이들의 풍속을 배우게 되는 것도 아니기 때문이다. 어느 한쪽을 아는 것만으로도 적어도 자기가 익숙한 그 부류만큼은 충분히 그려낼 수 있으리라 여길지도 모르겠으나, 그렇더라도 여기서마저 완벽에는 크게 못 미칠 것이다. 두 신분의 어리석음은 실은 서로를 비춰 주기 때문이다. 이를테면 상류층의 허세는 하류층의 소박함과 견줄 때 한층 더 두드러지고 우스꽝스러워 보인다. 또 거꾸로, 후자의 거칠고 야만스러움은 전자를 다스리는 그 세련됨과 대비되어 맞세워질 때 훨씬 더 강렬한 부조리의 인상으로 다가온다. 게다가 솔직히 말하면, 우리 역사가의 몸가짐도 이 두 가지 사귐을 통해 나아질 것이다. 한쪽에서는 꾸밈없음과 정직함과 진실함의 본보기를 손쉽게 발견할 것이요, 다른 쪽에서는 세련됨과 우아함, 그리고 너그러운 기품을 발견할 것이기 때문이다. 그런데 이 마지막 자질만큼은, 낮은 태생과 교육을 받은 사람들에게서 나 스스로 본 적이 거의 없다.\n\n그러나 내가 지금껏 우리 역사가에게 부여한 모든 자질도, 그가 흔히 말하는 따뜻한 마음을 지니고 또 느낄 줄 알지 못한다면 그에게 아무 소용이 없을 것이다. 호라티우스의 말마따나, 나를 울리려는 작가는 먼저 제가 울어야 한다. 실로 어떤 사람도 자기가 그려 내는 동안 스스로 느끼지 못하는 고통을 잘 그려 낼 수는 없다. 또한 가장 애틋하고 가슴을 울리는 장면들이 눈물로 쓰였다는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다. 우스운 것에 대해서도 마찬가지다. 나는 확신한다. 내가 독자보다 먼저 웃어 본 대목이 아니고서는 결코 독자를 진심으로 웃기지 못했음을. 다만 어쩌다 가끔, 독자가 나와 함께 웃는 대신 나를 비웃고 싶어지는 일이 생길 수는 있겠다. 어쩌면 이 장의 어떤 대목들에서 바로 그런 일이 벌어졌을지도 모르겠다. 그런 걱정이 들기에, 나는 여기서 이 장을 끝맺으려 한다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n참으로 매우 놀라운 모험을 담은 장. 존스가 산속 노인과 함께 걷던 길에서 마주친 일이라.\n\n이제 막 오로라가 자기 창문을 처음 열어젖혔을 때, 영어로 옮기자면 날이 막 밝아 오기 시작했을 때, 존스는 낯선 노인과 함께 길을 나서 매자드 언덕에 올랐다. 두 사람이 그 꼭대기에 막 다다르자마자 세상에서 가장 장엄한 풍경 가운데 하나가 그들의 눈앞에 펼쳐졌으니, 우리 또한 그것을 독자에게 펼쳐 보이고 싶지만 두 가지 이유로 그러지 못한다. 첫째, 이 풍경을 직접 본 이들이 우리의 묘사에 감탄하도록 만들 자신이 도무지 없기 때문이고, 둘째, 그것을 보지 못한 이들이 우리의 묘사를 이해할 수 있을지 매우 의심스럽기 때문이다.\n\n존스는 몇 분 동안 한 자세로 꼼짝 않고 서서 두 눈을 남쪽으로 향하고 있었다. 그러자 노신사가 무엇을 그토록 골똘히 바라보고 있느냐고 물었다. \"아아! 어르신,\" 그가 한숨을 내쉬며 대답했다. \"저는 여기까지 온 제 여정을 더듬어 보려 애쓰고 있었습니다. 맙소사! 글로스터가 우리에게서 얼마나 먼지요! 저와 제 고향 사이에 얼마나 광대한 땅이 가로놓여 있을까요!\" \"그래, 그래, 젊은이,\" 노인이 외쳤다. \"게다가 자네의 그 한숨으로 미루어 보건대, 자네 고향보다 더 사랑하는 무언가에서부터 떨어진 거리겠지. 내가 잘못 짚은 게 아니라면 말일세. 이제 보니 자네가 골똘히 생각하는 그 대상은 자네 눈에 들어오지 않는 곳에 있군. 그런데도 자네는 그쪽을 바라보는 데서 즐거움을 느끼는 모양이야.\" 존스가 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"보아하니 어르신께서는 아직 젊은 날의 그 감정을 잊지 않으셨군요. 제 생각이 어르신께서 짐작하신 그대로였음을 인정합니다.\"\n\n이제 두 사람은 북서쪽을 바라보는 언덕 쪽으로 걸어갔는데, 그곳은 광대하게 펼쳐진 숲 위로 비스듬히 내려다보이는 자리였다. 그곳에 막 다다르자마자 그들은 저 아래 숲에서 들려오는, 한 여인의 더없이 격렬한 비명을 멀리서 들었다. 존스는 잠시 귀를 기울이더니, 동행에게 한마디 말도 없이(실로 사정이 충분히 다급해 보였으므로) 언덕을 달려, 아니 차라리 미끄러져 내려갔고, 제 안전에 대한 일말의 두려움이나 걱정도 없이 그 소리가 새어 나온 덤불을 향해 곧장 나아갔다.\n\n숲속으로 멀리 들어가기도 전에 그는 참으로 끔찍하기 그지없는 광경을 목격했다. 한 여인이 반쯤 벌거벗긴 채 어느 흉한의 손아귀에 잡혀 있었는데, 그자는 자기 대님을 그녀의 목에 둘러 그녀를 나무에 매달아 끌어올리려 하고 있었다. 존스는 이 순간 아무것도 묻지 않고 즉시 그 악당에게 달려들어, 믿음직한 떡갈나무 막대기를 어찌나 잘 휘둘렀던지 상대가 미처 방어하기도 전에, 실로 자신이 공격당하는 줄 알아채기도 전에 그를 땅바닥에 나뒹굴게 만들었다. 그리고 여인이 직접 이제 그만두라고, 할 일은 충분히 다 한 것 같다고 애원할 때까지 그는 매질을 멈추지 않았다.\n\n그러자 그 가련한 여인은 존스 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 자신을 구해 준 데 대해 천 번이나 감사를 올렸다. 그는 곧바로 그녀를 일으켜 세우며, 그녀가 도움을 받으리라곤 도무지 기대할 수 없던 이런 곳으로 자신을 보내어 그녀를 구하게 한 그 기묘한 우연이 무척이나 기쁘다고 말했다. 그러고는 하늘이 자신을 그녀를 지켜 줄 행복한 도구로 점지하신 듯하다고 덧붙였다. \"아니에요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"저는 당신을 어떤 선한 천사라고 여기고 싶을 정도예요. 사실대로 말하자면, 제 눈에 당신은 사람이라기보다 천사처럼 보이는걸요.\" 실로 그는 매혹적인 모습이었다. 매우 훌륭한 풍채와 더없이 준수한 이목구비에 젊음과 건강, 힘과 생기, 활력과 선한 천성까지 어우러져 사람을 천사처럼 보이게 할 수 있다면, 그는 분명 그러한 닮음을 지니고 있었다.\n\n구원받은 포로는 그처럼 인간이면서 천사 같은 부류에 그리 가깝지는 않았다. 그녀는 적어도 중년에 접어든 듯했고, 얼굴에도 미모라 할 만한 것이 그리 많지 않았다. 그러나 옷이 상체에서 죄다 찢겨 나간 탓에, 모양 좋고 더없이 흰 그녀의 가슴이 구해 준 이의 눈길을 끌었으니, 두 사람은 잠시 말없이 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다. 그러다 땅바닥에 쓰러져 있던 흉한이 움직이기 시작하자, 존스는 본래 다른 용도로 쓰일 뻔했던 그 대님을 집어 그자의 두 손을 등 뒤로 묶었다. 그러고 나서 그자의 얼굴을 들여다보던 그는, 크게 놀라며, 어쩌면 적잖이 흡족해하며, 바로 이 인물이 다름 아닌 노더턴 소위임을 알아차렸다. 그 소위 또한 옛 적수를 잊지 않았으니, 정신이 들자마자 곧바로 그를 알아보았다. 그의 놀라움은 존스의 놀라움에 못지않았으나, 이 경우 그가 느낀 기쁨은 다소 덜했으리라 짐작된다.\n\n존스는 노더턴이 다리로 설 수 있게 부축해 일으킨 다음, 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 응시하며 말했다. \"이보시오, 당신은 이 세상에서 나를 다시 만나리라곤 생각지 못했겠지요. 나 또한 당신을 여기서 마주치리라곤 거의 기대하지 못했음을 고백하오. 하지만 보다시피 운명이 우리를 다시 한번 맺어 주었고, 내가 스스로 알지도 못하는 새에 내가 입은 상처에 대한 보상을 내게 안겨 주었구려.\"\n\n\"등 뒤에서 사람을 때려눕혀 보상을 챙긴다니, 참으로 명예로운 사내다운 짓이오.\" 노더턴이 대꾸했다. \"게다가 나는 여기서 당신에게 만족을 줄 처지도 못 되오. 칼이 없으니 말이오. 하지만 당신이 신사답게 굴 배짱이 있다면, 내가 칼을 구할 수 있는 곳으로 갑시다. 그러면 명예로운 사내라면 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 내 당신을 상대해 주겠소.\"\n\n\"당신 같은 악당이,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"명예라는 이름을 함부로 입에 올려 그것을 더럽혀도 된단 말이오? 그러나 당신과 입씨름하느라 시간을 허비하지는 않겠소. 정의가 지금 당신에게 응분의 대가를 요구하니, 반드시 받아 낼 것이오.\" 그러고는 여인 쪽으로 돌아서서, 집이 가까운지, 그렇지 않다면 이 근방에 점잖은 옷가지를 마련해 치안판사에게 갈 채비를 할 만한 집을 알고 있는지 물었다.\n\n그녀는 자신이 이 지방에서는 전혀 낯선 사람이라고 대답했다. 그러자 존스는 문득 생각이 나서, 가까운 곳에 길을 일러 줄 벗이 한 사람 있다고 말했다. 실로 그는 그 노인이 따라오지 않은 것을 의아해했다. 그러나 사실인즉, 우리의 주인공이 떠나갈 때 그 선량한 산속 노인은 언덕 비탈에 주저앉아, 손에 총을 쥐고 있으면서도 큰 인내심과 무심함으로 그 일의 결말을 지켜보고 있었던 것이다.\n\n이윽고 존스가 숲 밖으로 걸어 나오자, 방금 우리가 묘사한 그대로 앉아 있는 노인이 눈에 들어왔다. 그는 곧 온 힘을 다해 날쌔게 움직여 놀라운 속도로 언덕을 올랐다.\n\n노인은 그 여인을 업턴으로 데려가라고 일러 주었는데, 그곳이 가장 가까운 마을이니 거기서라면 그녀에게 온갖 편의를 마련해 줄 수 있으리라는 것이었다. 존스는 그곳으로 가는 길을 일러 받고 산속 노인에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 파트리지에게도 같은 길로 오라고 일러 달라 부탁하고는 서둘러 숲으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n우리의 주인공은 벗에게 이 일을 물으러 떠나면서, 흉한의 두 손이 등 뒤로 묶여 있으니 그 가련한 여인에게 어떤 못된 짓도 저지를 수 없으리라 헤아렸다. 게다가 그는 자신이 그녀의 목소리가 닿는 범위를 벗어나지 않을 것이며, 어떤 해코지든 막을 만큼 충분히 빨리 돌아올 수 있으리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 더욱이 그는 그 악당에게, 만일 조금이라도 무례한 짓을 시도한다면 자신이 직접 그자에게 복수의 형 집행자가 되겠노라 선언해 두었다. 그러나 존스는 불운하게도, 노더턴의 두 손은 묶였어도 두 다리는 자유롭다는 사실을 잊고 말았다. 또한 그 죄수에게 그 다리를 제멋대로 쓰지 말라는 명령은 조금도 내려 두지 않았다. 그리하여 노더턴은 그런 약속을 한 적이 없으니 명예를 조금도 저버림 없이 떠나도 된다고 여겼다. 자기 생각에 어떤 규칙으로도 정식 방면을 기다릴 의무가 없었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 자유로운 두 다리를 놀려 숲을 가로질러 걸어 나갔고, 숲은 그의 도주를 도와주었다. 그 여인 또한, 어쩌면 두 눈이 자신을 구해 준 이 쪽으로 더 쏠려 있었던 탓인지, 그자의 도주를 한 번도 떠올리지 않았으며 그것을 막으려 애쓰거나 신경 쓰지도 않았다.\n\n그리하여 존스는 돌아왔을 때 여인 혼자만 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 노더턴을 찾는 데 얼마간 시간을 쓰려 했으나, 그녀가 이를 허락하지 않았다. 그녀는 두 사람이 일러 받은 그 마을까지 자신과 동행해 달라고 간절히 청했다. \"그자가 달아난 일이라면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 조금도 마음이 편치 않을 것이 없어요. 철학도 기독교도 모두 받은 상처를 용서하라 가르치니까요. 다만 당신께는, 제가 끼치는 수고가 마음에 걸립니다. 아니, 실로 이 헐벗은 꼴 탓에 당신 얼굴을 마주 보기조차 부끄럽군요. 당신의 보호가 필요한 것만 아니라면 저는 혼자 가고 싶을 지경이에요.\"\n\n존스는 그녀에게 자기 외투를 내밀었으나, 무슨 까닭에서인지 그녀는 그것을 받아 달라는 더없이 간곡한 청을 한사코 거절했다. 그러자 그는 그녀의 그 두 가지 거북함을 모두 잊으라고 권했다. \"전자에 관해서라면,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 당신을 보호하는 제 본분을 다한 것뿐이고, 후자에 관해서라면 제가 길 내내 당신 앞에서 걸어 그것을 완전히 없애 드리지요. 제 눈이 당신을 불쾌하게 만들기를 원치 않거니와, 이토록 빼어난 아름다움이 지닌 매혹의 마력에 제가 능히 맞설 자신도 없으니 말입니다.\"\n\n그리하여 우리의 주인공과 구원받은 여인은 옛날 오르페우스와 에우리디케가 걸어갔던 것과 똑같은 모양새로 걸음을 옮겼다. 그러나 나는 존스가 그 어여쁜 동행에게 일부러 꾐을 받아 뒤를 돌아보았다고는 믿을 수 없지만, 그녀가 계단 울타리를 넘을 때마다 그의 도움을 자주 필요로 했고, 게다가 여러 차례 발을 헛디디는 등 갖가지 일을 겪었던 까닭에, 그는 부득이 자주 뒤를 돌아보아야 했다. 그래도 그는 가련한 오르페우스에게 닥쳤던 것보다는 나은 운을 누렸으니, 자기 동행을, 아니 차라리 자기 뒤를 따르던 이를 그 유명한 업턴 마을까지 무사히 데려갔던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n존스 씨가 자신의 동행한 부인과 함께 여관에 도착하다. 그리고 업튼 전투에 관한 매우 상세한 묘사.\n\n독자께서 이 부인이 누구이며 어떻게 노더턴 씨의 손아귀에 떨어지게 되었는지 몹시 알고 싶어 하시리라는 것을 우리는 의심치 않는다. 하지만 잠시만 그 호기심을 눌러 두시기를 청해야겠다. 우리로서는, 나중에 어쩌면 짐작하시게 될 매우 그럴듯한 몇 가지 이유 때문에, 그 만족을 조금 더 미룰 수밖에 없기 때문이다.\n\n존스 씨와 그의 아리따운 동행은 마을에 들어서자마자 거리를 향해 가장 그럴듯한 모습으로 서 있던 여관으로 곧장 향했다. 여기서 존스는 하인에게 위층 방을 안내하라 이른 뒤 계단을 오르고 있었는데, 머리가 헝클어진 미인이 황급히 그 뒤를 따르자 여관 주인이 그녀를 붙잡으며 소리쳤다. \"이런, 저 거지 계집은 어딜 가는 거야? 아래층에 좀 있으라고.\" 그러나 바로 그 순간 존스가 위에서 우레와 같이 호령했다. \"그 부인을 올라오게 하시오.\" 그 목소리가 어찌나 위엄이 서려 있던지, 착한 주인은 곧바로 손을 놓았고 부인은 서둘러 방으로 향했다.\n\n여기서 존스는 그녀가 무사히 도착한 것을 기뻐하며 축하해 준 뒤, 약속한 대로 여관 안주인을 시켜 옷가지를 올려 보내려고 자리를 떴다. 가엾은 여인은 그의 모든 친절에 진심으로 고마움을 표하며, 곧 다시 그를 만나 천 번이라도 더 감사 인사를 드릴 수 있기를 바란다고 말했다. 이 짧은 대화를 나누는 동안 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 두 팔로 자신의 흰 가슴을 가렸다. 존스가 아무리 조금이라도 무례를 범하지 않으려 온갖 신경을 썼다 해도, 슬쩍 한두 번 곁눈질하는 것까지는 어쩔 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n우리의 나그네들은 우연히도 평판이 매우 좋은 집에 묵게 되었는데, 이곳은 정조가 엄격한 아일랜드 부인들과, 같은 처지의 여러 북부 처녀들이 바스로 가는 길에 즐겨 들르던 곳이었다. 그러니 안주인은 자기 지붕 아래에서 평판을 떨어뜨릴 만한 어떤 거래도 결코 용납하려 하지 않았다. 실로 그런 행위들이란 어찌나 더럽고 전염성이 강한지, 그것이 저질러진 무고한 장소마저 오염시키고, 그런 일이 행해지도록 내버려 둔 모든 집에 '나쁜 집' 혹은 '평판 나쁜 집'이라는 이름을 붙이고 만다.\n\n물론 베스타 신전에서 지켜졌던 만큼의 엄정한 순결이 공공 여관에서 유지될 수 있다고 넌지시 말하려는 것은 아니다. 내 착한 안주인도 그런 복은 바라지 않았고, 앞서 말한 부인들 가운데 누구도, 아니 그 누구보다 가장 엄격한 축에 드는 이들조차 그런 것을 기대하거나 고집하지는 않았을 것이다. 그러나 모든 천박한 동거를 막고, 누더기를 걸친 창녀들을 담장 안에서 죄다 몰아내는 일은 누구든 할 수 있는 일이다. 내 안주인은 이 원칙을 아주 엄격히 지켰고, 누더기 차림으로 여행하지 않는 그녀의 정숙한 손님들도 그녀에게 그쯤은 마땅히 기대했을 것이다.\n\n자, 존스 씨와 그의 누더기 동행이 어떤 속셈을 품고 있다고 상상하는 데에는 그다지 비난받을 만큼의 의심도 필요치 않았다. 그런 일이란 어떤 기독교 국가에서는 용인되고, 다른 곳에서는 눈감아 주며, 모든 곳에서 실제로 행해지지만, 그럼에도 그 나라들에서 두루 믿어지는 종교에 의해서는 살인이나 그 밖의 끔찍한 악덕만큼이나 명백히 금지된 일이었다. 그러므로 안주인은 위에서 말한 인물들이 들어왔다는 기별을 받자마자, 그들을 쫓아낼 가장 신속한 방법을 궁리하기 시작했다. 이를 위해 그녀는 길고도 무시무시한 도구 하나를 준비했는데, 그것은 평화로운 시절에는 객실 하녀가 부지런한 거미가 애써 지은 집을 허무는 데 쓰던 물건이었다. 쉽게 말하자면, 그녀는 빗자루를 집어 들고 막 부엌에서 출격하려던 참이었는데, 바로 그때 존스가 위층의 반쯤 헐벗은 여인을 가릴 가운과 그 밖의 옷가지를 달라고 청해 왔다.\n\n우리가 몹시 분노하고 있는 바로 그 사람들을 위해 특별한 친절을 베풀어 달라는 부탁만큼 인간의 성미를 돋우는 것도, 인내라는 으뜸가는 미덕에 위태로운 것도 없다. 이런 까닭에 셰익스피어는 교묘하게도 그의 데스데모나로 하여금 남편에게 캐시오를 위해 호의를 베풀어 달라 청하게 함으로써, 남편의 질투뿐 아니라 분노까지 광기의 절정에 이르도록 부추기는 수단으로 삼았다. 그리하여 우리는 그 불운한 무어인이, 아내에게 준 소중한 선물을 연적으로 의심하는 자의 손에서 보았을 때보다도, 오히려 이 대목에서 자신의 격정을 다스리지 못함을 보게 된다. 사실 우리는 이런 노력을 우리의 분별에 대한 모욕으로 여기며, 인간의 자존심이란 거기에 좀처럼 굴복하려 들지 않는 법이다.\n\n내 안주인은 비록 성품이 매우 너그러운 여인이긴 했으나, 짐작건대 그녀에게도 이런 자존심이 얼마간 섞여 있었던 모양이다. 존스가 부탁을 채 마치기도 전에 그녀는 어떤 무기를 들고 그에게 달려들었던 것이다. 그 무기란 길지도, 날카롭지도, 단단하지도 않으며, 겉모습만 보아서는 죽음이나 상처를 위협하지도 않건만, 그럼에도 수많은 현명한 사람들이, 아니, 수많은 용감한 사람들조차 크게 두려워하고 몹시 꺼려 온 물건이었다. 그리하여 장전된 대포의 아가리를 감히 들여다본 자들 가운데서도, 이 무기가 휘둘러지는 입속만큼은 차마 들여다보지 못하고, 그것이 가져올 결과의 위험을 무릅쓰느니 차라리 자기 지인들 모두의 눈앞에서 더없이 가련하고 비굴한 꼴을 보이는 편을 택한 이들이 있을 정도였다.\n\n사실대로 고백하자면, 존스 씨도 그런 부류 가운데 하나가 아니었을까 싶다. 앞서 말한 그 무기에 공격을 받고 사정없이 두들겨 맞으면서도, 그는 도무지 저항할 마음이 동하지 않았기 때문이다. 도리어 그는 더없이 비겁한 태도로 거듭 애원하며, 자기 적수에게 매질을 그만두라 청할 뿐이었다. 쉬운 말로 하자면, 그는 그저 자기 말을 들어 달라고 더없이 간절하게 빌기만 했던 것이다. 그러나 그가 그 청을 이루기도 전에, 내 여관 주인이 몸소 싸움판에 끼어들어 도움이 거의 필요 없어 보이는 쪽 편을 들고 나섰다.\n\n세상에는 자기가 맞붙어야 할 상대의 성품과 행실을 보고 싸움을 택할지 피할지를 정한다고 하는 영웅들이 있다. 이들은 상대를 잘 알아본다고들 하는데, 존스도 자기 상대 여인을 잘 알아보았던 모양이다. 그녀에게는 그토록 고분고분했으면서도, 그녀의 남편에게 공격당하자마자 그는 곧바로 분개하는 기색을 드러내며, 매우 가혹한 벌을 내걸고 그에게 입을 다물라 명령했던 것이다. 그 벌이란 다름 아니라, 내 생각에는 그 자신의 불 땔감으로 만들어 버리겠다는 정도였다.\n\n그 남편은 크나큰 분노에, 그러나 얼마간의 동정심을 섞어 대답했다. \"그럴 힘부터 갖게 해 달라고 먼저 기도나 하시지. 내가 댁보다 더 나은 사내라고. 그래, 어느 모로 보나 말이야.\" 그러고는 곧장 위층의 부인을 향해 '창녀'라는 욕설을 대여섯 번이나 퍼붓기 시작했는데, 그 마지막 말이 채 입에서 나오기도 전에 존스가 손에 쥐고 있던 몽둥이가 휘둘러져 그의 어깨를 후려쳤다.\n\n이 일격을 되갚는 데 여관 주인과 안주인 가운데 누가 더 빨랐는지는 가늠하기 어렵다. 손이 비어 있던 내 여관 주인은 주먹으로 덤벼들었고, 착한 안주인은 빗자루를 치켜들어 존스의 머리를 겨냥했으니, 만일 이 빗자루의 낙하가 막히지 않았더라면 아마 싸움도, 더불어 존스도 그 자리에서 끝장이 났을 것이다. 그런데 그것을 막은 것은 어느 이교의 신이 기적처럼 끼어든 덕분이 아니라, 지극히 자연스러우면서도 다행스러운 우연, 곧 파트리지의 도착 덕분이었다. 그는 바로 그 순간 집 안으로 들어섰는데(공포에 사로잡혀 언덕에서부터 한 걸음도 쉬지 않고 내달려 온 터였다), 자기 주인 혹은 동행(어느 쪽으로 부르든 좋을 대로)을 위협하는 위험을 보고는, 허공 높이 휘둘러진 안주인의 팔을 붙잡아 그토록 슬픈 참사를 막아 냈다.\n\n안주인은 자기 일격을 가로막은 장애물을 곧 알아챘다. 그러나 파트리지의 손아귀에서 팔을 빼낼 수 없자 빗자루를 떨어뜨리고는, 존스는 남편의 손에 맡겨 둔 채 그 가엾은 친구에게 무서운 기세로 달려들었다. 파트리지는 이미 \"젠장! 내 친구를 죽일 셈이오?\" 하고 외치며 자기 존재를 알린 터였다.\n\n파트리지는 싸움을 그리 즐기는 위인은 아니었으나, 그래도 친구가 공격당하는 마당에 가만히 서 있으려 하지는 않았다. 게다가 자기 몫으로 떨어진 그 싸움 상대에게는 그리 불만도 없었던지라, 그는 안주인의 매를 받자마자 곧바로 되갚아 주었다. 그리하여 이제 싸움은 모든 진영에서 완강하게 이어졌고, 행운의 여신이 어느 편으로 기울지 의심스러운 가운데, 싸움이 시작되기에 앞서 오고 간 대화를 계단 꼭대기에서 엿듣고 있던 그 헐벗은 부인이 위에서 별안간 내려왔다. 그녀는 둘이 하나를 상대하는 불공평한 형세 따위는 따져 보지도 않고, 파트리지와 주먹다짐을 벌이고 있던 가엾은 여인에게 덤벼들었다. 그러자 그 위대한 용사는 손을 멈추기는커녕, 새로운 원군이 자기를 도우러 도착한 것을 보고 도리어 그 기세를 곱절로 키웠다.\n\n이제 승리는 마땅히 나그네들 편으로 떨어졌을 것이다(아무리 용맹한 군대라도 수에는 굴복하는 법이니). 그런데 그때 객실 하녀 수전이 마침맞게 와서 제 주인을 거들었다. 이 수전은 (그 표현 그대로) 이 고장 누구 못지않게 양손잡이로 억센 계집이었으니, 내 짐작으로는 그 이름난 탈레스트리스 자신이나 그 휘하의 어떤 아마존 여전사라도 너끈히 두들겨 팼을 법했다. 그녀의 몸은 사내처럼 건장하여 이런 싸움판에 안성맞춤으로 만들어진 듯했기 때문이다. 그녀의 손과 팔이 적에게 큰 타격을 입히도록 만들어진 것처럼, 그녀의 얼굴 또한 자기는 별로 다치지 않으면서 매를 받아 내기에 알맞게 생겨먹었다. 코는 이미 얼굴에 납작하게 붙어 있었고, 입술은 어찌나 큼지막한지 부어올라도 표가 나지 않았으며, 게다가 어찌나 단단한지 주먹으로 쳐도 좀처럼 자국조차 남길 수 없었다. 끝으로 그녀의 광대뼈는 불쑥 튀어나와 있었으니, 마치 자연이 그토록 잘 어울리고 또 그토록 놀라우리만치 좋아하는 이 싸움판에서 그녀의 두 눈을 지킬 보루 두 개로 삼으려 한 것만 같았다.\n\n이 아리따운 피조물은 전장에 들어서자마자 곧장 한쪽 날개로 진격했으니, 바로 그녀의 주인이 남녀 양쪽을 상대로 그토록 불리한 싸움을 벌이던 자리였다. 여기서 그녀는 대뜸 파트리지에게 일대일 결투를 청했다. 그는 그 도전을 받아들였고, 둘 사이에 더없이 필사적인 싸움이 시작되었다.\n\n이제 전쟁의 사냥개들이 풀려나 그 피투성이 입술을 핥기 시작했다. 이제 승리의 여신이 황금빛 날개를 펼친 채 허공에서 맴돌았다. 이제 운명의 여신이 선반에서 저울을 꺼내 톰 존스와 그의 여인 동행과 파트리지의 운명을, 여관 주인과 그 아내와 하녀의 운명에 맞대어 달기 시작했다. 그 모든 것이 그녀 앞에서 정확히 균형을 이루고 매달려 있었는데, 그때 마음씨 좋은 우연 하나가 그 피비린내 나는 싸움에 별안간 종지부를 찍었다. 싸움꾼들 가운데 절반은 이미 충분히 잔치를 벌인 뒤였다. 그 우연이란 다름 아닌 사두마차의 도착이었다. 그러자 내 여관 주인과 안주인은 즉시 싸움을 멈추었고, 그들의 간청에 적수들도 똑같이 응해 주었다. 그러나 수전은 파트리지에게 그토록 너그럽지 못했다. 그 아마존 같은 미인은 적을 쓰러뜨려 깔고 앉은 채, 무기를 거두자는 그의 청도, 그가 목청껏 질러 대는 \"사람 죽인다\"는 외침도 아랑곳없이 두 손으로 그를 신나게 후려치고 있었다.\n\n그러나 존스는 여관 주인에게서 풀려나기가 무섭게 패배한 동행을 구하러 날아갔고, 갖은 애를 쓴 끝에 격분한 객실 하녀를 그에게서 떼어 냈다. 그런데 파트리지는 자기가 구출되었다는 사실을 곧바로 알아차리지 못했다. 그는 여전히 바닥에 납작 엎드린 채 두 손으로 얼굴을 감싸고 있었던 것이다. 존스가 억지로 그를 고개 들게 하여 싸움이 끝났음을 깨닫게 하기 전까지, 그는 고함치기를 멈추지 않았다.\n\n겉으로 보아 다친 데가 없는 여관 주인과, 잔뜩 할퀸 얼굴을 손수건으로 가린 안주인은 둘 다 황급히 문간으로 달려가 마차를 맞이했다. 마차에서는 이제 젊은 부인 하나와 그 하녀가 내렸다. 안주인은 곧 이들을 존스 씨가 처음에 그의 아리따운 전리품을 들여놓았던 바로 그 방으로 안내했는데, 그곳이 집 안에서 가장 좋은 방이었기 때문이다. 그리로 가려면 이들은 전쟁터를 가로질러야 했고, 누구의 눈에도 띄고 싶지 않은 듯 손수건으로 얼굴을 가린 채 더없이 서둘러 그곳을 지났다. 하지만 그들의 그런 조심은 전혀 부질없는 것이었다. 가엾고 불운한 헬레네, 그 모든 유혈극의 치명적 빌미가 된 여인은 제 얼굴을 가리느라 온통 정신이 팔려 있었고, 존스 또한 수전의 분노에서 파트리지를 구해 내느라 그에 못지않게 바빴기 때문이다. 그 일이 다행히 마무리되자, 가엾은 친구는 곧장 펌프로 가서 얼굴을 씻고, 수전이 자기 콧구멍에서 콸콸 쏟아지게 만든 그 핏줄기를 막으려 했다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n군함 한 척이 도착하여 적대 행위에 마침내 종지부를 찍고, 모든 당사자 사이에 굳고 오래갈 평화의 결말을 이끌어내는 장.\n\n이 무렵 하사 한 명과 머스킷 총병 한 무리가, 자기들이 잡아 호송하던 탈영병을 데리고 도착했다. 하사는 곧바로 이 고을의 으뜸가는 행정관이 누구냐고 물었고, 주인장으로부터 바로 자기 자신이 그 직책을 맡고 있다는 말을 들었다. 그러자 하사는 숙박표를 내놓으라고 요구하면서 맥주 한 잔을 청했고, 날이 춥다고 투덜대며 부엌 화롯불 앞에 몸을 쭉 펴고 자리를 잡았다.\n\n이때 존스는 그 가엾고 곤경에 처한 부인을 위로하고 있었는데, 부인은 부엌의 탁자에 앉아 팔에 머리를 기댄 채 자신의 불운을 한탄하고 있었다. 그런데 나의 어여쁜 여성 독자들이 어떤 한 가지 사정을 두고 마음을 졸이실까 싶어, 여기서 미리 알려드리는 것이 옳겠다. 부인은 위층 방을 떠나기 전에, 그곳에서 찾아낸 베갯잇 하나로 몸을 잘 가렸기에, 지금 그 방에 모여 있는 그 많은 남자들 앞에서도 그녀의 정숙함은 조금도 손상되지 않았다는 사실을 말이다.\n\n이때 병사들 가운데 하나가 하사에게 다가가 귀에 대고 무언가를 속삭였다. 그러자 하사는 부인에게 시선을 똑바로 박은 채 일 분 가까이 그녀를 바라보더니, 그녀에게 다가가 말했다. \"용서하십시오, 마님. 그러나 저는 제가 잘못 본 게 아니라고 확신합니다. 마님은 다름 아닌 워터스 대위님의 부인이 아니십니까?\"\n\n지금의 곤경 속에서 그 자리에 있는 누구의 얼굴에도 거의 눈길을 주지 않던 가엾은 여인은, 하사를 바라보자마자 곧 그를 알아보고는 그의 이름을 부르며 대답했다. \"그래요, 나는 정말로 당신이 짐작하는 그 불행한 사람이에요.\" 그러고는 덧붙였다. \"이런 차림을 한 나를 알아보는 사람이 있다니 놀랍군요.\" 이에 하사가 대꾸했다. \"마님께서 이런 옷차림을 하고 계신 것을 보고 무척 놀랐으며, 마님께 무슨 변고라도 생긴 게 아닐까 걱정했습니다.\" \"변고가 생긴 건 사실이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 그것이 치명적인 일로 끝나지 않은 것, 또 내가 지금 살아서 이 일을 이야기할 수 있는 것은 모두 이 신사분 덕분이니, 나는 그분께 큰 신세를 졌어요.\" 하고 존스를 가리켰다. \"이 신사분이 무슨 일을 하셨든,\" 하사가 외쳤다. \"대위님께서 분명 그에 합당한 보답을 해주실 것입니다. 그리고 제가 무슨 도움이라도 될 수 있다면, 마님께서는 무엇이든 분부만 하십시오. 마님을 위해 힘을 쓸 수 있다면 저는 더없이 기쁠 것입니다. 사실 누구에게나 그럴 것입니다. 대위님께서 그 사람에게 후하게 보답해 주실 것을 제가 잘 알고 있으니까요.\"\n\n계단에서 하사와 워터스 부인 사이에 오간 말을 모두 엿들은 안주인은 부랴부랴 내려와 곧장 그녀에게로 달려가더니, 자신이 저지른 무례를 용서해 달라고 빌기 시작했다. 모든 것을 마님의 신분을 몰랐던 탓으로 돌려 달라고 애원하면서 말이다. \"어머나, 마님!\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"마님 같은 지체 높으신 분이 이런 차림으로 나타나실 줄 제가 어찌 상상이나 했겠어요? 정말이지, 마님, 만약 제가 마님께서 마님이신 줄 조금이라도 짐작했더라면, 제가 한 말을 입에 담느니 차라리 제 혀를 뽑아 태워 버렸을 거예요. 마님께서 마님 옷을 구하실 때까지 부디 제 가운이라도 받아주시면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"여보세요, 아주머니.\" 하고 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"그 주제넘은 짓 좀 그만둬요. 당신 같은 천한 사람 입에서 나오는 말 따위에 내가 마음을 쓸 거라고 생각하다니? 그보다 더 놀라운 건, 그런 일이 있고도 내가 당신의 그 더러운 물건을 몸에 걸치는 꼴을 보일 거라고 뻔뻔스레 여기는 당신의 배짱이에요. 똑똑히 알아두어요, 이 사람아. 나는 그런 것보다 높은 기개를 지닌 사람이니까.\"\n\n여기서 존스가 끼어들어, 워터스 부인에게 안주인을 용서하고 그 가운을 받아 달라고 청했다. \"솔직히 말씀드리자면,\" 하고 그가 외쳤다. \"우리가 처음 들어왔을 때 우리 행색이 조금 수상쩍어 보이긴 했습니다. 그리고 이 선량한 아주머니가 한 일은, 본인이 말하듯, 모두 이 집의 평판을 지키려는 마음에서 비롯된 것이라고 저는 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"네, 정말로 그렇답니다.\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"이 신사분은 말씀하시는 게 영락없는 신사분이세요. 척 보아도 그러신 게 분명하고요. 그리고 확실히 이 집으로 말씀드리자면, 이 길에 있는 어느 집 못지않게 평판 좋은 집으로 널리 알려져 있고, 제 입으로 말하긴 뭣하지만, 아일랜드분이든 영국분이든 가장 지체 높은 양반들이 드나드는 곳이지요. 그 점에 대해선 누구라도 제게 트집을 잡아 보라죠. 그리고 아까도 말씀드렸듯이, 만약 제가 마님께서 마님이신 줄 알았더라면, 마님께 무례를 범하느니 차라리 제 손가락을 태워 버렸을 거예요. 하지만 정말이지, 양반들이 와서 돈을 쓰는 자리에서, 가는 곳마다 돈보다 이를 더 많이 남기고 가는 가난뱅이 비렁뱅이 떼거리한테 양반들이 욕을 보는 건 제가 두고 볼 수 없거든요. 그런 작자들은 도무지 제 동정심을 끌어내지 못해요. 확실히 그런 자들에게 동정을 베푸는 건 어리석은 짓이니까요. 그리고 우리 치안판사님들이 마땅히 하셔야 할 일을 하신다면, 그런 작자들은 죄다 매를 맞고 이 나라에서 쫓겨났을 거예요. 확실히 그게 그들에게 가장 어울리는 일이거든요. 하지만 마님으로 말씀드리자면, 마님께서 변고를 당하신 것이 진심으로 안타깝습니다. 그러니 마님께서 마님 옷을 구하실 때까지 제 옷을 입어 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 제가 가진 가장 좋은 것이 마님 분부대로 마련되어 있답니다.\"\n\n추위 탓이었는지, 부끄러움 탓이었는지, 아니면 존스의 설득이 가장 크게 작용했는지 나는 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 워터스 부인은 안주인의 이 말에 마음이 누그러지도록 자신을 내맡겼고, 그 선량한 여인과 함께 점잖은 차림을 갖추려고 물러갔다.\n\n주인장 역시 존스에게 일장 연설을 시작하려 했으나, 이내 그 너그러운 젊은이에게 가로막혔다. 존스는 그의 손을 다정하게 꼭 잡으며 전적으로 용서한다고 단언하고는 말했다. \"그대가 흡족하다면, 나의 갸륵한 친구여, 나도 흡족하다고 약속하겠소.\" 그런데 실은, 어떤 의미에서는 주인장 쪽이 흡족해할 더 그럴듯한 이유가 있었다. 그는 흠씬 두들겨 맞아 배가 가득 찰 지경이었던 반면, 존스는 단 한 대도 거의 맞지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n이 내내 펌프 앞에서 피투성이가 된 코를 씻고 있던 파트리지는, 자기 주인과 주인장이 서로 손을 맞잡고 있는 바로 그 순간에 부엌으로 돌아왔다. 그는 평화를 사랑하는 성미였던 터라, 이 화해의 조짐들을 보고 기뻐했다. 그리고 비록 그의 얼굴에는 수전의 주먹 자국이 더러 남아 있고 손톱 자국은 그보다 훨씬 많았지만, 그는 또 한바탕 싸워서 형편을 나아지게 해보려 애쓰기보다 지난 전투에서의 자기 운명에 만족하기로 마음먹었다.\n\n영웅적인 수전 역시 자신의 승리에 흡족해했다. 비록 그 승리의 대가로 첫 접전 때 파트리지에게 얻어맞은 시퍼런 멍 자국이 눈가에 남기는 했지만 말이다. 그리하여 이 두 사람 사이에 동맹이 맺어졌고, 전쟁의 도구였던 그 손들은 이제 평화의 중재자가 되었다.\n\n이렇게 하여 사태는 완전한 고요로 돌아갔는데, 하사는, 비록 그것이 자기 직업의 원칙에 몹시 어긋나 보일지 몰라도, 이를 두고 찬성의 뜻을 내비쳤다. \"자, 이제 이래야 화목하지.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"젠장, 한바탕 붙고 난 다음에도 두 사람이 서로 앙심을 품고 있는 꼴은 정말 보기 싫어. 친구끼리 다툴 땐, 말하자면 친구다운 방식으로, 주먹이든 칼이든 권총이든 좋을 대로 골라서 정정당당하게 끝장을 보고는, 그걸로 깨끗이 끝내는 게 유일한 길이지. 나로 말하자면, 젠장, 친구하고 싸울 때만큼 그 친구가 더 좋은 적이 없다니까! 앙심을 품는 건 영국 사람보다 프랑스 사람한테나 어울리는 짓이야.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는, 이런 종류의 모든 조약에서 의식의 필수적인 부분이라며 헌주를 제안했다. 어쩌면 독자는 여기서 그가 고대 역사에 정통한 사람이라고 결론지을지도 모르겠다. 그러나 그것은, 비록 충분히 그럴 법한 일이긴 해도, 그가 그 관습을 뒷받침할 어떤 전거도 들지 않았으므로, 나는 자신 있게 단언하지는 않겠다. 사실 그가 매우 든든한 전거 위에 자신의 견해를 세웠을 가능성이 가장 높기는 하다. 그가 그것을 무수한 거친 맹세로 확인해 주었으니 말이다.\n\n존스는 그 제안을 듣자마자 즉시 그 박식한 하사에게 동의하고는, 이런 자리에 쓰이는 술을 가득 채운 사발, 아니 차라리 커다란 잔을 가져오라 일렀고, 이어서 몸소 그 의식을 시작했다. 그는 오른손을 주인장의 손에 얹고, 왼손으로는 그 사발을 움켜쥐고서, 으레 하는 말을 읊은 다음, 헌주를 올렸다. 그러고 나서 그 자리에 있는 모든 사람이 똑같이 따라 했다. 사실 그 의식 전체의 형식을 시시콜콜 묘사할 필요는 거의 없다. 고대 작가들과 그들을 본뜬 근대의 필사자들이 그토록 많이 기록해 둔 그 헌주들과 별로 다를 게 없었으니 말이다. 주된 차이는 두 가지였다. 첫째, 지금의 이 일행은 술을 오직 자기들 목구멍으로만 들이부었다는 점이고, 둘째, 사제 노릇을 한 하사가 맨 마지막에 마셨다는 점이다. 그러나 내 생각에 그는, 일행 가운데 단연 가장 큰 잔을 들이켰다는 점에서, 그리고 의식을 거드는 자신의 수고 말고는 헌주에 아무것도 보태지 않은 유일한 사람이었다는 점에서, 고대의 형식을 그대로 지킨 셈이었다.\n\n이제 이 선량한 사람들은 부엌 화롯불 둘레에 빙 둘러앉았고, 그곳에서는 유쾌한 기분이 완전한 지배권을 쥔 듯 보였다. 파트리지는 자신의 부끄러운 패배를 잊었을 뿐 아니라, 허기를 갈증으로 바꾸어 곧 더없이 익살스러운 사람이 되었다. 그러나 우리는 이 기분 좋은 모임을 잠시 떠나, 존스를 따라 워터스 부인의 방으로 가야겠다. 그가 미리 주문해 둔 식사가 지금 그곳 탁자 위에 차려져 있으니 말이다. 사실 그것을 준비하는 데는 그리 오랜 시간이 걸리지 않았다. 사흘 전에 이미 모두 조리해 둔 것이라, 요리사가 할 일이라곤 그저 다시 데우는 것뿐이었다.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n배가 든든한 모든 영웅을 위한 변호, 그리고 사랑의 전투에 관한 묘사라 해 두자. 아첨꾼들 덕분에 영웅들이 스스로를 어떻게 높이 여기든, 혹은 세상이 그들을 어떻게 떠받들든, 그들에게는 분명 신적인 면보다 인간적인 면이 더 많다. 그들의 정신이 아무리 드높다 한들, 적어도 그들의 몸뚱이는 (대부분의 사람에게 이 몸뚱이가 훨씬 더 큰 부분을 차지한다) 가장 고약한 병에 걸리기 쉽고, 인간 본성의 가장 천한 일들에 매여 있다. 그 천한 일들 가운데 먹는 행위는, 여러 현인이 지극히 비천하며 철학자의 품위를 깎아내리는 것이라 여겨 왔음에도, 지상에서 가장 위대한 군주든 영웅이든 철학자든 어느 정도는 반드시 치러야 하는 일이다. 아니, 때로 자연은 어찌나 짓궂은지, 이 고귀한 인물들에게 가장 미천한 부류의 사람들에게 시킨 것보다 훨씬 더 과한 몫의 이 일을 요구하기도 한다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 이 지구에 알려진 어느 거주자도 실은 인간 이상은 아니므로, 인간의 필요가 요구하는 바에 굴복하는 것을 부끄러워할 사람은 아무도 없다. 그러나 내가 방금 언급한 그 위대한 인물들이 이런 천한 일을 자기들끼리만 독차지하려고 몸을 낮출 때—이를테면 쌓아 두거나 망쳐 버림으로써 다른 누구도 먹지 못하게 막으려는 듯이 굴 때—그들은 분명 아주 비천하고 경멸스러운 존재가 되고 만다.\n\n자, 이 짧은 머리말을 늘어놓았으니, 이제 우리 영웅이 이 무렵 얼마나 절제 없는 열의로 먹어 댔는지를 말한다 해도 그의 명예를 깎는 일은 아니리라 믿는다. 실로, 그 먹는 시(詩) 《오디세이아》에 등장하는 모든 영웅 가운데 가장 좋은 위장을 지녔던 듯한 오디세우스조차 이보다 더 잘 먹은 적이 있었을지 의심스러울 정도다. 한때 소 한 마리를 이루는 데 보탬이 되었던 그 살코기가 적어도 세 파운드는, 이제 존스 씨라는 개인의 일부가 되는 영예를 누렸다.\n\n이 세세한 사정을 굳이 언급해야겠다고 여긴 것은, 그래야만 우리 영웅이 잠시 그 어여쁜 동반자를 소홀히 한 까닭이 설명되기 때문이다. 그녀는 아주 조금밖에 먹지 않았고, 실은 전혀 다른 종류의 생각에 잠겨 있었는데, 존스는 스물네 시간의 단식이 그에게 안겨 준 그 식욕을 완전히 채울 때까지 그것을 알아차리지 못했다. 그러나 식사가 끝나기가 무섭게 다른 일들에 대한 그의 주의가 되살아났다. 그러니 이제 그 일들을 독자에게 알려 드리기로 하자.\n\n존스 씨의 외모에 관해서는 지금껏 거의 말하지 않았으나, 그는 사실 세상에서 가장 잘생긴 젊은이 가운데 하나였다. 그의 얼굴은 건강 그 자체의 그림일 뿐 아니라, 다정함과 선량함의 가장 또렷한 자취를 담고 있었다. 이런 자질들이 어찌나 그의 얼굴에 뚜렷이 새겨져 있던지, 그의 눈에 깃든 생기와 감수성은 세심한 관찰자라면 알아챘겠으나 안목이 덜한 이의 눈은 놓쳤을 법한 데 반해, 그 선량함만큼은 그의 표정에 너무도 강하게 그려져 있어 그를 본 거의 모든 사람이 알아보았다.\n\n어쩌면 바로 이 점, 그리고 더없이 고운 안색 덕분에 그의 얼굴에는 거의 말로 표현하기 힘든 섬세함이 깃들어 있었는데, 이 섬세함은 자칫 그에게 다소 여성스러운 분위기를 줄 뻔했으나, 더없이 남성적인 체격과 풍채가 함께 어우러진 덕에 그러지 않았다. 그 풍채에는 헤라클레스의 면모가 그의 얼굴에 깃든 아도니스의 면모만큼이나 담겨 있었다. 게다가 그는 활달하고, 품위 있고, 명랑하며, 성품이 좋았다. 또한 넘쳐흐르는 생기가 있어 그가 함께한 자리는 어떤 대화든 생동감이 돌았다.\n\n독자가 우리 영웅에게 한데 모인 이 숱한 매력을 충분히 헤아리고, 동시에 워터스 부인이 그에게 새로이 진 신세까지 고려한다면, 그녀가 그에 대해 아주 좋은 생각을 품었다 하여 그녀를 나쁘게 보는 것은 솔직함이라기보다 새침함의 표시일 것이다.\n\n그러나 그녀에게 어떤 비난이 쏟아지든, 사실을 진실하게 전하는 것이 내 일이다. 워터스 부인은 사실 우리 영웅을 좋게 여겼을 뿐 아니라, 그에게 아주 깊은 애정을 품고 있었다. 거리낌 없이 단번에 말하자면, 그녀는 사랑에 빠졌다. 오늘날 누구나 받아들이는 그 말의 뜻대로의 사랑인데, 이 뜻에서 사랑이란 우리의 온갖 욕정과 식욕과 감각이 탐내는 대상에 가리지 않고 적용되는 것이며, 우리가 어떤 종류의 음식을 다른 음식보다 더 선호하는 그 마음과 같은 것으로 이해된다.\n\n그러나 이 여러 대상을 향한 사랑이 어느 경우든 하나이며 똑같은 것일 수 있다 해도, 그 작용만큼은 다르다고 인정해야 한다. 우리가 빼어난 등심 한 점이나 부르고뉴 포도주 한 병에, 혹은 진홍빛 장미나 크레모나산(産) 바이올린에 아무리 깊이 빠져 있다 한들, 그 쇠고기 따위의 애정을 얻으려고 미소 짓거나, 추파를 던지거나, 차려입거나, 아첨하거나, 그 밖의 어떤 재주나 수법을 부리는 일은 결코 없다. 한숨이야 이따금 내쉴지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은 대개 사랑하는 대상이 곁에 있을 때가 아니라 없을 때다. 그러지 않고서야 우리는 어쩌면 그것들의 무정함과 귀먹음을 탓하게 될지도 모른다. 마치 파시파에가 자기 황소를 두고 그랬듯이 말이다. 그녀는 응접실에서 훨씬 더 분별 있고 다감한 멋쟁이 신사들의 마음을 사로잡는 데 곧잘 성공했던 온갖 교태를 부려 그 황소를 꾀어 보려 애썼던 것이다.\n\n같은 종(種)이되 성(性)이 다른 사람들 사이에서 작용하는 사랑에서는 그 반대의 일이 벌어진다. 여기서는 우리가 사랑에 빠지기가 무섭게, 사랑하는 대상의 애정을 얻는 일이 무엇보다 중요한 관심사가 된다. 우리 젊은이들이 스스로를 호감 가게 만드는 온갖 기술을 배우는 것이, 실은 또 다른 무슨 목적에서겠는가? 만일 이 사랑을 염두에 두지 않았다면, 인간의 몸을 돋보이게 하고 꾸며 주는 일을 다루는 그 어떤 직업이 과연 생계를 꾸릴 수 있을지 의문이다. 아니, 우리의 예법을 다듬는 그 위대한 연마자들, 어떤 이들은 그들이야말로 우리를 짐승과 가장 뚜렷이 구별 짓는 바를 가르친다고 여기는데, 심지어 무용 선생들조차 사회에서 설 자리를 찾지 못할지도 모른다. 요컨대, 젊은 숙녀들과 젊은 신사들이 남에게서 배우는 온갖 우아함, 그리고 거울의 도움을 받아 스스로 보태는 그 숱한 손질은, 실은 오비디우스가 그토록 자주 언급한 바로 그 사랑의 화살과 횃불(spicula et faces amoris)이며, 우리말로는 이따금 사랑의 온갖 화포(火砲)라 불리는 것이다.\n\n자, 워터스 부인과 우리 영웅이 마주 앉기가 무섭게, 전자는 후자를 향해 이 화포를 쏘아 대기 시작했다. 그런데 여기서 우리는 산문으로든 운문으로든 여태 시도된 바 없는 묘사에 도전하려는 참이니, 어떤 공중의 존재들에게 도움을 청하는 것이 마땅하다고 본다. 그들은 이 자리에서 우리를 친절히 거들어 주러 오리라 믿어 의심치 않는다.\n\n\"그러니 말해 다오, 그대 우아의 여신들이여! 세라피나의 얼굴이라는 천상의 저택에 깃들어 사는 그대들이여. 그대들은 참으로 신성하며, 늘 그녀 곁에 있고, 매혹의 온갖 기술을 훤히 아는 이들이니. 말해 다오, 이제 존스 씨의 마음을 사로잡으려 동원된 무기들이 무엇이었는지를.\"\n\n\"먼저, 사랑스러운 푸른 두 눈에서—그 빛나는 눈동자는 발사할 때 번개를 번뜩였으니—날카로운 추파 두 발이 쏘아져 나왔다. 그러나 우리 영웅에게는 다행히도, 그것은 그가 마침 접시에 옮겨 담고 있던 큼직한 쇠고기 한 점만 맞히고는 그 힘을 헛되이 소진하고 말았다. 그 어여쁜 전사는 빗나간 것을 알아채고, 즉시 고운 가슴속에서 치명적인 한숨 하나를 꺼내 들었다. 누구라도 흔들리지 않고는 들을 수 없는 한숨, 단번에 멋쟁이 열두 명쯤은 쓸어버리기에 족한 한숨이었다. 어찌나 부드럽고, 어찌나 달콤하고, 어찌나 애틋했던지, 그 파고드는 숨결은 우리 영웅의 마음으로 그 은밀한 길을 기어이 찾아들었으리라. 다행히도 마침 그가 따르고 있던 병맥주의 거친 보글거림이 그것을 그의 귓전에서 몰아내지 않았던들 말이다. 그녀는 그 밖에도 숱한 무기를 시험해 보았다. 그러나 먹기의 신이 (그런 신이 정말 있다면 말인데, 나는 확언하지는 않는다) 자신의 신도를 지켜 주었다. 혹은 어쩌면 이것이 신의 개입이 필요할 만한 매듭(dignus vindice nodus)이 아니어서, 존스의 이번 무사함이 자연스러운 까닭으로 설명될 수도 있겠다. 사랑이 흔히 굶주림의 공격으로부터 우리를 지켜 주듯이, 굶주림 또한 어떤 경우에는 사랑으로부터 우리를 막아 줄지도 모르니 말이다.\n\n\"그 어여쁜 여인은 거듭된 실패에 격분하여, 잠시 무기를 거두기로 결심했다. 그 짬을 그녀는 만찬이 끝나면 다시 공격을 펼칠 사랑의 온갖 병기를 채비하는 데 썼다.\n\n\"그리하여 식탁보가 치워지기 무섭게 그녀는 다시 작전을 개시했다. 먼저, 오른쪽 눈을 비스듬히 존스 씨에게 겨눈 다음, 그 눈꼬리에서 더없이 꿰뚫는 눈길 한 줄기를 쏘았다. 비록 그 힘의 대부분이 우리 영웅에게 닿기도 전에 소진되긴 했으나, 아무런 효과도 없이 헛되이 흩어지지는 않았다. 이를 알아챈 그 어여쁜 여인은 황급히 눈길을 거두어 아래로 떨구었는데, 마치 자기가 저지른 일을 뉘우치는 듯한 모양이었다. 하지만 이 방법으로 그녀가 노린 것은 다만 그의 경계를 풀고, 나아가 그의 눈을 뜨게 하여 그 눈을 통해 그의 마음을 기습하려는 것이었다. 그러고는 이미 가엾은 존스에게 자국을 남기기 시작한 그 빛나는 두 눈동자를 가만히 들어 올리며, 그녀는 얼굴 전체에서 한꺼번에 자잘한 매력의 일제 사격을 미소에 실어 퍼부었다. 즐거움의 미소도, 기쁨의 미소도 아니었다. 그것은 애정의 미소였으니, 대부분의 숙녀가 언제든 마음대로 부릴 수 있는 것이며, 그녀들의 좋은 기분과 어여쁜 보조개와 새하얀 이를 한꺼번에 내보이는 데 쓰이는 것이다.\n\n\"이 미소를 우리 영웅은 두 눈에 정통으로 맞고는, 곧장 그 힘에 휘청거렸다. 이제 그는 적의 의도를 알아채기 시작했고, 실로 그 성공을 몸으로 느끼기 시작했다. 두 진영 사이에 협상이 시작되었는데, 그동안 그 교묘한 미녀는 어찌나 은밀하고 알아챌 수 없게 공격을 이어 갔던지, 다시 적대 행위에 나서기도 전에 우리 영웅의 마음을 거의 굴복시켜 놓았다. 사실대로 고백하자면, 존스 씨는 일종의 네덜란드식 방어를 취하여, 어여쁜 소피아를 향한 충성을 제대로 가늠하지도 않은 채 배신하듯 요새를 내주고 만 것이 아닌가 싶다. 요컨대, 사랑의 협상이 끝나고 그 부인이 목에 두른 손수건을 무심한 듯 떨어뜨려 왕의 포대를 드러내자마자, 존스 씨의 마음은 완전히 함락되었고, 그 어여쁜 정복자는 승리의 통상적인 열매를 누렸다.\"\n\n여기서 우아의 여신들은 묘사를 끝내는 것이 마땅하다고 보았고, 우리 또한 여기서 이 장을 끝내는 것이 마땅하다고 본다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n부엌에서 오간 정겨운 대화. 흔히 볼 수 있는, 그러나 그리 정겹지는 않은 결말로 끝났다.\n\n우리의 연인들이 앞 장에서 일부 그려진 방식으로 둘만의 시간을 즐기는 동안, 그들은 부엌에 있는 좋은 친구들을 위해서도 즐길 거리를 마련해 주고 있었다. 그것도 두 가지 의미에서였다. 그들에게 대화의 소재를 제공함과 동시에, 흥을 돋울 술까지 베푼 셈이었다.\n\n이제 부엌 난롯가에는, 이따금 들락날락하는 주인 부부 말고도 파트리지 씨와 상사, 그리고 젊은 아가씨와 그 하녀를 태우고 온 마부가 둘러앉아 있었다.\n\n파트리지가 산속 노인에게서 들은 이야기, 곧 존스가 워터스 부인을 발견한 정황을 일행에게 들려주자, 상사는 자신이 아는 부분의 사연을 이어 갔다. 그는 그녀가 워터스 씨의 아내이며, 워터스 씨는 자기네 연대의 대위로서 주둔지에서 자주 함께 지냈다고 했다. \"어떤 사람들은 말이오,\" 그가 말했다, \"두 사람이 교회에서 정식으로 혼인을 올렸는지 어떤지 미심쩍어들 하더이다. 하지만 내 입장에서야 그거야 내 알 바 아니지. 솔직히 군인의 맹세를 걸고 말하라면, 내 보기엔 저 여자도 우리네와 별반 다를 게 없는 사람이오. 그리고 내 짐작엔 그 대위 양반은 비 오는 날 해 뜰 때나 천국에 가게 될 게요. 하지만 설령 간다 한들 그건 이러나저러나 마찬가지지. 동행이 없을 리 없으니 말이오. 그리고 그 부인으로 말하자면, 악마에게도 마땅한 몫을 주듯 공평하게 말하건대, 참으로 좋은 부류의 부인이고, 군복을 사랑하며, 늘 군인들에게 엄정히 의리를 다하려 애쓰는 사람이오. 가엾은 병사들 여럿을 위해 사정해서 벌을 면하게 해 주었고, 자기 뜻대로라면 그 누구도 벌받게 하지 않았을 게요. 하지만 그래도 분명한 건, 노더턴 소위와 그 부인이 우리 지난 주둔지에서 아주 가까운 사이였다는 거요. 그게 바로 옳고도 참된 사정이지. 그런데 대위는 그 일을 아무것도 모르오. 그리고 자기 몫이 충분히 있는 한, 그게 무슨 대수겠소? 그래도 부인을 조금도 덜 사랑하는 게 아니고, 누구든 그 부인을 욕보이는 자가 있으면 그 몸뚱이를 칼로 꿰뚫어 버릴 게 분명하오. 그러니 나로서는 그 부인을 욕하지 않겠소. 나야 그저 다른 사람들이 하는 말을 옮길 뿐이오. 그리고 분명한 건, 모두가 하는 말에는 어느 정도 진실이 있게 마련이라는 거요.\"—\"암, 암, 분명 진실이 아주 많고말고요.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"베리타스 오디움 파리트(진실은 미움을 낳는다).\"—\"죄다 망측한 헛소리들뿐이오.\" 안주인이 대꾸했다. \"내 분명히 말하건대, 지금 옷을 갖춰 입은 모습을 보면 아주 좋은 부류의 부인 같고, 처신도 그러한 부인답습디다. 내 옷을 빌려 입은 값으로 1기니를 주었으니 말이오.\"—\"참으로 좋은 부인이고말고!\" 주인이 외쳤다. \"당신이 조금만 덜 성급했더라면, 처음에 그렇게 싸우지도 않았을 텐데.\"—\"참, 별소릴 다 하시네!\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"당신의 그 헛소리만 아니었으면 아무 일도 없었을 거요. 당신은 늘 당신 일도 아닌 데 끼어들어서 그 바보 같은 말참견을 해 대니 탈이지.\"—\"알았어, 알았어.\" 그가 대답했다. \"지난 일은 돌이킬 수 없으니, 그걸로 그만하자고.\"—\"그래요,\" 그녀가 외쳤다, \"이번 한 번이야 그렇다 칩시다. 하지만 앞으로는 좀 나아지기나 할까? 당신의 그 멍청한 대가리 때문에 내가 고생한 게 이번이 처음도 아니오. 제발 집 안에서는 늘 입을 다물고, 당신과 상관있는 바깥일에만 끼어들었으면 좋겠소. 한 칠 년 전에 무슨 일이 있었는지 기억 안 나요?\"—\"이봐, 여보,\" 그가 받아쳤다, \"옛날 얘기를 들추지 좀 마. 자, 자, 다 잘됐고, 내가 한 일은 미안하게 됐으니까.\" 안주인이 막 대꾸하려는데, 중재에 나선 상사가 가로막았다. 파트리지로서는 몹시 못마땅한 일이었으니, 그는 이른바 재미라는 것을 무척 좋아하는 사람이었고, 비극보다는 희극적인 사건을 빚어내기 십상인 그런 무해한 다툼을 부추기는 데 일가견이 있는 인물이었다.\n\n상사가 파트리지에게 그와 그의 주인은 어디로 여행하는 길이냐고 물었다. \"주인이라니 당치 않소,\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"장담컨대 나는 누구의 하인도 아니오. 비록 세상살이에서 불운을 겪긴 했어도, 내 이름 뒤에는 '신사'라고 쓰는 사람이오. 그리고 지금이야 이렇게 가난하고 보잘것없어 보여도, 한때는 문법학교에서 가르치던 몸이오. 세드 헤이 미히! 논 숨 쿠오드 푸이(허나 아아, 슬프도다! 나는 이제 예전의 내가 아니로다).\"—\"기분 상하게 할 뜻은 없소만,\" 상사가 말했다. \"그럼, 외람되지만 한번 여쭤보겠소. 당신과 당신 친구분은 어디로 가시는 길이오?\"—\"이제야 우리를 제대로 칭하시는구려.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"아미키 수무스(우리는 친구라오). 장담컨대 내 친구로 말하자면 이 왕국에서 손꼽히는 대단한 신사 가운데 한 분이오\"(이 말에 주인과 안주인 둘 다 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다). \"올워디 향사 어른의 상속자이시라오.\"—\"뭐라고요, 온 고장에서 그토록 선행을 많이 베푸는 그 향사 어른 말이오?\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"바로 그분이오.\" 파트리지가 대답했다.—\"그렇다면 분명,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"장차 어마어마하게 큰 재산을 물려받으시겠구려.\"—\"틀림없소.\" 파트리지가 대답했다.—\"그것 보오,\" 안주인이 받았다, \"내 처음 그분을 본 순간부터 좋은 부류의 신사 같다고 했잖소. 그런데 여기 우리 양반은, 참, 누구보다 잘난 줄 알지.\"—\"인정하리다, 여보,\" 그가 외쳤다, \"그건 내 실수였소.\"—\"실수고말고요!\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"한데 내가 그런 실수를 하는 걸 당신이 언제 본 적이나 있소?\"—\"한데 어찌 된 영문이오, 선생,\" 주인이 외쳤다, \"그런 대단한 신사분이 걸어서 시골을 돌아다니신단 말이오?\"—\"낸들 알겠소,\" 파트리지가 받았다. \"대단한 신사들은 때때로 별난 기분이 들곤 하니까. 지금 글로스터에 말도 열두 마리쯤 있고 하인들도 거느리고 있다오. 그런데 어젯밤, 날씨가 어찌나 더웠던지, 한사코 저 높은 언덕으로 산책을 가서 몸을 식히겠다지 않겠소. 나도 동무 삼아 함께 걸어 올라갔지요. 하지만 다시 한 번 거기로 나를 끌고 갈 수 있는지 어디 두고 보시오. 내 평생 그렇게 겁먹은 적이 없었으니. 거기서 어찌나 괴상한 사람을 만났던지.\"—\"내 목을 걸겠소,\" 주인이 외쳤다, \"그게 사람들이 산속 노인이라 부르는 그 양반이 아니라면 말이오. 정말로 그게 사람이라면야. 한데 나는 거기 사는 게 악마라고 믿는 사람들을 여럿 알고 있소.\"—\"아니, 아니, 충분히 그럴 법하오.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"그러고 보니 당신이 일깨워 주니 하는 말인데, 나는 그게 악마였다고 진실로 진심으로 믿소. 비록 갈라진 발굽은 보지 못했지만 말이오. 어쩌면 그걸 숨길 능력을 받았는지도 모르지. 악령이란 제멋대로 어떤 모습으로든 나타날 수 있으니 말이오.\"—\"한데 외람되지만, 선생,\" 상사가 말했다. \"기분 상하게 할 뜻은 없소만, 대체 악마라는 게 어떤 부류의 신사요? 우리 장교들 중 몇몇이 그런 자는 없다고 말하는 걸 들은 적이 있어서 말이오. 그건 그저 목사들이 자기들 밥줄 끊기지 않으려고 꾸며낸 수작일 뿐이라고 합디다. 악마가 없다는 게 세상에 공공연히 알려지면, 목사들이란 평화로운 시절의 우리 군인만큼이나 쓸모없어질 테니 말이오.\"—\"그 장교들로 말하자면,\" 파트리지가 말했다, \"필시 아주 대단한 학자들이겠구려.\"—\"학자라고 할 것도 못 되오,\" 상사가 대답했다. \"내 보기엔 그자들 학식이 선생 절반에도 못 미칠 거요. 그리고 분명한 건, 나는 그자들 말에도 불구하고 악마가 있을 수밖에 없다고 생각했다는 거요. 비록 그중 하나가 대위였지만 말이오. 나 혼자 속으로 생각하길, 악마가 없다면 사악한 자들을 대체 어디로 보낸단 말인가 싶었거든. 그리고 나는 그 모든 걸 책에서 읽었소.\"—\"당신네 장교들 가운데 몇몇은,\" 주인이 말했다, \"수치스럽게도 악마가 있다는 걸 알게 될 거요, 내 장담하리다. 그자가 내 몫으로 묵은 빚 몇 가지를 갚아 줄 게 틀림없소. 여기 반년이나 묵었던 자가 하나 있었는데, 글쎄 하루에 1실링도 안 쓰면서 뻔뻔스럽게 내 제일 좋은 침대를 차지하지 않았겠소. 게다가 내가 일요일에 저녁 한 끼 안 차려 준다고, 부하들을 시켜 부엌 불에 양배추나 구워 먹게 했다오. 선량한 기독교인이라면 누구나 그런 망나니들을 벌하기 위해서라도 악마가 있기를 바랄 게요.\"—\"이보시오, 주인장,\" 상사가 말했다, \"군복을 욕하지 마시오. 나는 그건 못 참으니까.\"—\"빌어먹을 군복 같으니!\" 주인이 대답했다. \"나는 그자들 때문에 충분히 당했단 말이오.\"—\"증인이 되어 주시오, 여러분,\" 상사가 말했다, \"이자가 임금님을 저주했소. 그건 대역죄요.\"—\"내가 임금님을 저주했다고! 이 못된 놈아.\" 주인이 말했다. \"그렇소, 당신이 그랬소,\" 상사가 외쳤다. \"당신은 군복을 저주했고, 그건 곧 임금님을 저주한 거요. 그게 그거니까. 군복을 저주하는 자는 누구든 감히 그럴 수만 있다면 임금님도 저주할 테니, 따져 보면 다 똑같은 일이오.\"—\"그건 좀 봐드릴 수 없소이다, 상사 양반,\" 파트리지가 말했다, \"그건 논 세퀴투르(말이 안 되는 결론)요.\"—\"그 알아먹지도 못할 외국 말 좀 작작하시오.\" 상사가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 대답했다. \"나는 가만히 앉아서 군복이 욕먹는 꼴을 두고 볼 수 없소.\"—\"오해하셨소, 친구,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"나는 군복을 욕할 뜻이 없었소. 그저 당신의 결론이 논 세퀴투르라고 했을 뿐이오.\"—\"당신도 마찬가지요.\" 상사가 외쳤다. \"정 그렇게 나온다면 말이오. 당신이야말로 세퀴투르고 뭐고 없는 작자지. 당신들은 죄다 악당 무리야. 내 그걸 증명해 보이겠소. 20파운드 걸고 당신들 중 제일 센 놈하고 한판 붙어 주지.\" 이 도전에 파트리지는 그만 입을 다물고 말았으니, 얼마 전 푸짐하게 대접받은 밥이 아직 꺼지지 않은 터라 두들겨 맞을 식욕이 그리 빨리 돌아오지 않았던 것이다. 하지만 마부는 뼈마디가 덜 쑤셨고 싸움에 대한 식욕은 다소 날카로웠던지라, 적어도 자기 몫 일부는 돌아온다고 여긴 이 모욕을 그리 쉽게 참아 넘기지 못했다. 그리하여 그는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 상사에게 다가가서는, 자기도 군대의 그 누구 못지않은 사내라고 큰소리치며 1기니를 걸고 주먹다짐을 하자고 청했다. 군인은 대결은 받아들였으나 내기는 거절했다. 그러자 둘은 즉시 웃통을 벗어 던지고 맞붙었는데, 말몰이꾼이 사람들의 지휘자에게 어찌나 흠씬 두들겨 맞았던지, 결국 얼마 남지 않은 숨을 쥐어짜 가며 살려 달라 비는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n* 논 세퀴투르: 상사가 불행히도 모욕으로 오해한 이 말은 논리학 용어로, 결론이 전제로부터 도출되지 않는다는 뜻이다.\n\n젊은 아가씨는 이제 떠나고 싶어 마차를 준비하라고 일러두었다. 그러나 다 헛일이었으니, 마부가 그날 저녁 제 소임을 다할 수 없게 되어 버린 까닭이었다. 옛 이교도였다면 아마 이 무능을 전쟁의 신 못지않게 술의 신 탓으로 돌렸으리라. 사실 두 결투자 모두 전자의 신에게도 후자의 신에게도 똑같이 제물을 바친 셈이었으니 말이다. 솔직히 말하자면 둘 다 곤드레만드레 취해 있었고, 파트리지의 형편도 그리 나을 게 없었다. 주인으로 말하자면 술 마시는 게 그의 본업이었으니, 술이 그에게 미치는 효력이란 집 안의 다른 어떤 그릇에 미치는 것과 다를 바 없었다.\n\n여관 안주인은 존스 씨와 그 일행이 차를 마시는 자리에 시중을 들러 불려 가서, 앞서 벌어진 장면의 뒷부분을 낱낱이 일러바쳤다. 그러면서 동시에 젊은 아가씨에 대해 큰 걱정을 내비쳤다. \"그분이,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"여정을 이어 가지 못하게 된 일로 여간 마음이 편치 않으신 게 아니라오. 참으로 곱고 사랑스러운 분이에요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그리고 분명히 어디선가 그 얼굴을 본 적이 있는 것 같아요. 내 짐작엔 사랑에 빠져서 식구들한테서 달아나는 길인가 싶어요. 또 누가 알겠어요, 어디선가 어느 젊은 신사분이 그분 못지않게 무거운 마음을 안고 그분을 기다리고 있을지?\"\n\n존스는 그 말에 무거운 한숨을 내쉬었다. 워터스 부인은 그것을 알아챘지만, 안주인이 방에 머무는 동안에는 모르는 체했다. 그러나 그 선량한 여인이 떠난 뒤, 부인은 그의 애정 속에 어떤 몹시 위태로운 연적이 있는 게 아닌가 의심하며 우리 주인공에게 넌지시 운을 떼지 않을 수 없었다. 이때 보인 존스 씨의 어색한 태도는, 그가 부인의 어떤 물음에도 직접 답을 주지 않았는데도 그 진실을 확인시켜 주었다. 하지만 부인은 자기 연애에 그리 까다로운 사람이 아니어서, 그 발견에 크게 마음 쓰지는 않았다. 존스의 미모는 그녀의 눈을 한껏 사로잡았다. 그러나 그의 마음속까지 들여다볼 수는 없는 노릇이었으므로, 그녀는 그 점에 대해 조금도 개의치 않았다. 그녀는 사랑의 식탁에서 마음껏 포식할 수 있었으니, 이미 다른 누군가가 같은 음식을 즐겼거나 앞으로 즐기게 될지도 모른다는 생각 따위는 떠올리지도 않았다. 이런 마음가짐은, 비록 세련됨과는 거리가 멀지언정 알맹이만큼은 풍부한 것이며, 또 변덕도 덜하고, 어쩌면 심술도 이기심도 덜한 것이리라. 자기 연인을 다른 누구도 차지하지 않았다는 확신만 충분히 선다면 정작 자신이 그 연인을 차지하지 못해도 그럭저럭 만족할 수 있는 그런 여인들의 욕망에 비한다면 말이다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n7장.\n워터스 부인에 대한 더 자세한 설명, 그리고 그녀가 존스에게 구출된 그 비참한 처지에 어떻게 빠지게 되었는가에 관하여.\n\n자연이 모든 인간에게 호기심과 허영심을 똑같은 몫으로 섞어 넣은 것은 결코 아니지만, 그 둘을 다스리고 억누르는 데 적잖은 기교와 수고가 필요할 만큼의 분량을 받지 않은 사람은 아마 하나도 없을 것이다. 그런데 이 정복이야말로, 지혜롭다거나 교양 있다는 평을 조금이라도 들을 만한 사람이라면 누구에게나 반드시 필요한 것이다.\n\n따라서 존스는 교양 있는 사람이라 불릴 자격이 충분했으므로, 워터스 부인을 그토록 기이한 방식으로 만난 일이 분명 불러일으켰을 그 모든 호기심을 억눌렀다. 사실 그는 처음에는 부인에게 몇 마디 넌지시 물어보기도 했다. 그러나 부인이 어떤 설명도 애써 피하는 것을 알아채자, 그는 모르는 채로 머무는 데 만족했다. 더구나 그녀가 모든 진실을 다 털어놓았다면 얼굴을 붉혔을 만한 사정이 얼마쯤 있으리라는 의심도 없지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n이제 우리 독자들 가운데 더러는 그처럼 모르는 채로 쉽게 수긍하지 않을지도 모르고, 또 우리는 그들 모두를 만족시키고 싶은 마음이 간절하므로, 우리는 실제 사실을 알아내려고 남다른 수고를 들였다. 그 이야기로 이 권을 마무리하려 한다.\n\n그러니까 이 부인은 몇 해 동안 워터스 대위라는 사람과 함께 살았는데, 그는 노더턴 씨가 속한 바로 그 연대의 대위였다. 그녀는 그 신사의 아내로 통했고 그의 성을 따랐다. 그러나 하사관이 말했듯이, 두 사람이 정말로 혼인한 것인지에는 약간의 의문이 있었는데, 그 문제는 지금 우리가 나서서 풀려 하지 않겠다.\n\n말하기 안타깝지만, 워터스 부인은 한동안 앞서 말한 그 소위와 친밀한 관계를 맺어 왔고, 이는 그녀의 평판에 그리 좋지 못한 일이었다. 그녀가 그 젊은 친구를 유난히 좋아했다는 것은 거의 틀림없다. 그러나 그녀가 그 애정을 정말로 죄가 될 만한 데까지 마음껏 풀어놓았는지는 그리 분명하지 않다. 여자란 한 가지 호의만 빼고 나머지 모든 호의를 남자에게 베풀면서 그 한 가지마저 함께 베풀지 않는 법이 없다고 우리가 가정하지 않는 한 말이다.\n\n워터스 대위가 속한 연대의 부대는 노더턴 씨가 소위로 있던 중대보다 이틀 앞서 행군을 떠났다. 그래서 대위 쪽은 우리가 앞서 기록한 존스와 노더턴의 그 불운한 재회 바로 다음 날에 우스터에 닿았다.\n\n그런데 워터스 부인과 대위 사이에는, 그녀가 우스터까지 그의 행군에 동행하고 거기서 서로 작별한 뒤, 그녀는 다시 바스로 돌아가 반란군에 맞선 겨울 원정이 끝날 때까지 머무르기로 약속이 되어 있었다.\n\n이 약속을 노더턴 씨도 알고 있었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 부인은 바로 이 장소에서 그와 밀회를 약속하고 그의 부대가 도착할 때까지 우스터에 머물겠노라 약속했던 것이다. 무슨 속셈으로, 무슨 목적으로 그랬는지는 독자의 짐작에 맡길 수밖에 없다. 우리는 사실을 전할 의무는 있으나, 피조물 가운데 가장 사랑스러운 부류에게 불리한 어떤 논평을 달아 우리 본성을 거스르는 짓까지 할 의무는 없으니 말이다.\n\n노더턴은 우리가 본 대로 감금에서 풀려나자마자 워터스 부인을 따라잡으려 서둘러 달려갔다. 그는 무척 날쌔고 민첩한 친구였으므로, 워터스 대위가 그녀와 헤어진 지 몇 시간 지나지 않아 앞서 말한 그 도시에서 그녀를 따라잡았다. 도착하자마자 그는 거리낌 없이 그 불운한 사고를 그녀에게 알렸는데, 그것을 정말로 몹시 불운한 일로 보이게 꾸몄다. 그는 적어도 명예의 법정에서라면 잘못이라 할 만한 부분은 한 톨도 남김없이 빼버렸으나, 법률의 법정에서라면 시비를 따질 만한 사정 몇 가지는 그대로 남겨두었다.\n\n여자들은 — 그들의 영예를 위해 하는 말이지만 — 오직 사랑하는 대상의 행복만을 바라는 그 격렬하고 겉보기에 사심 없는 사랑의 열정을, 남자들보다 대체로 더 잘 품을 줄 안다. 그래서 워터스 부인은 자기 연인이 처한 위험을 알게 되자마자, 그의 안전 말고는 다른 모든 생각을 잃어버렸다. 그리고 이것은 그 신사에게도 똑같이 반가운 일이었으므로, 곧 두 사람 사이의 논의 거리가 되었다.\n\n이 문제를 한참 의논한 끝에, 마침내 소위가 시골길을 가로질러 헤리퍼드로 가고, 거기서 웨일스의 어느 항구로 가는 교통편을 구해 해외로 달아나기로 합의가 되었다. 이 모든 여정에 워터스 부인은 그와 동행하겠노라 선언했고, 또 그에게 돈도 마련해 줄 수 있었다. 노더턴 씨에게는 매우 긴요한 항목이었다. 그녀는 그때 주머니에 90파운드어치의 은행권 석 장과 그 밖의 현금을 지니고 있었고, 손가락에는 제법 값나가는 다이아몬드 반지를 끼고 있었다. 그녀는 이 모든 것을 더없는 신뢰로 이 악한에게 털어놓았으니, 이로써 자신이 그에게 강도질의 흉계를 불어넣게 되리라고는 거의 의심하지 못했다. 그런데 우스터에서 말을 빌려 가면, 뒤쫓는 자들에게 훗날 자기들의 경로를 알아낼 단서를 주게 될 것이므로, 소위는 첫 구간을 걸어서 가자고 제안했고 부인도 곧 동의했다. 이를 위해서는 단단히 얼어붙은 서리가 무척 알맞았다.\n\n부인의 짐 대부분은 이미 바스에 가 있었고, 지금 그녀가 지닌 것이라곤 아주 적은 양의 속옷가지뿐이었는데, 그 멋쟁이가 자기 주머니에 넣어 나르겠노라 떠맡았다. 그리하여 저녁에 모든 것을 정해 두고, 두 사람은 이튿날 아침 일찍 일어나 다섯 시에 우스터를 떠났다. 그때는 동트기까지 두 시간이 넘게 남았으나, 마침 보름이던 달이 베풀어 줄 수 있는 빛을 다 내어 주었다.\n\n워터스 부인은 한 곳에서 다른 곳으로 몸을 옮기는 능력을 탈것의 발명에 기대야만 하고, 따라서 마차를 생필품 가운데 하나로 꼽는 그런 가냘픈 부류의 여자가 아니었다. 그녀의 팔다리는 사실 힘과 날렵함으로 가득했고, 마음 또한 그에 못지않게 기운차게 살아 있었으므로, 그녀는 자기의 날쌘 연인과 보조를 맞추는 데 조금도 모자람이 없었다.\n\n노더턴이 헤리퍼드로 통한다고 들었다는 큰길을 따라 몇 마일을 가고 나니, 두 사람은 동틀 무렵 어느 큰 숲가에 이르렀다. 거기서 그가 갑자기 걸음을 멈추더니, 혼자 잠시 골똘히 생각하는 척하면서, 이렇게 사람들 눈에 띄는 길로 더 가는 것이 마음에 걸린다고 말했다. 그러면서 그는 숲을 곧장 가로지르는 듯한 오솔길로 함께 들어서자고 어여쁜 길동무를 어렵잖게 설득했고, 그 길은 마침내 두 사람을 매저드 언덕 기슭으로 데려갔다.\n\n그가 이제 실행에 옮기려 한 그 끔찍한 흉계가 미리 작정해 둔 결과였는지, 아니면 그제야 비로소 머릿속에 떠오른 것인지는 나도 단정할 수 없다. 다만 어떤 방해도 받을 것 같지 않은 이 외딴곳에 이르자, 그는 갑자기 다리에서 양말 대님을 풀어 그 가엾은 여인에게 거칠게 손을 댔고, 우리가 앞서 적은 바 있는, 또 존스의 섭리 같은 등장이 그토록 다행히 막아 준, 그 무섭고 가증스러운 짓을 저지르려 했다.\n\n워터스 부인으로서는 자기가 가장 연약한 축의 여자가 아니라는 것이 다행이었다. 그가 대님에 매듭을 짓는 모습과 그의 말을 통해 그 지옥 같은 속셈을 알아채자마자, 그녀는 꿋꿋이 맞서 방어했다. 줄곧 도와달라 비명을 지르며 적과 어찌나 격렬하게 몸싸움을 벌였던지, 그녀는 그 악당의 목적이 실행되는 것을 몇 분이나 늦추었다. 그 덕분에 존스 씨는 그녀의 힘이 다하여 완전히 제압당하는 바로 그 순간에 그녀를 구하러 와, 그 무뢰한의 손아귀에서 그녀를 건져냈다. 잃은 것이라곤 등에서 찢겨 나간 옷과, 다투는 사이 손가락에서 빠졌거나 노더턴에게 비틀려 빼앗긴 다이아몬드 반지뿐이었다.\n\n이리하여, 독자여, 우리는 그대의 만족을 위해 이 일을 무척 고생스럽게 캐물어 얻은 결실을 그대에게 내어놓았다. 그리고 여기서 우리는 흉악함 못지않게 어리석음의 한 장면을 그대 앞에 펼쳐 보였는데, 인간이라는 존재가 이런 짓을 저지를 수 있으리라고는 우리도 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠을 것이다. 다만 이 친구가 그때 이미 살인을 저질러 자기 목숨을 법에 내놓게 되었다고 굳게 믿고 있었음을 우리가 기억하지 않았다면 말이다. 그러니 자기의 유일한 살길은 도주뿐이라 결론짓고 보니, 그는 이 가엾은 여인의 돈과 반지를 차지하는 것이, 양심에 더 얹어야 할 그 짐을 어느 정도 보상해 주리라 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그리고 여기서, 독자여, 우리는 그대에게 엄히 당부해야겠다. 이런 비열한 자의 못된 짓을 빌미로, 우리 군대의 장교들이라는 대체로 훌륭하고 명예로운 집단을 함부로 비난하지 말라고 말이다. 부디 생각해 주기 바란다. 이 친구는, 우리가 이미 알려 준 대로, 신사다운 혈통도 교육도 갖추지 못했고, 그런 사람들 축에 끼일 만한 인물도 아니었다. 그러므로 그의 비천함이 그 자신 말고 다른 누구에게라도 정당히 누를 끼칠 수 있다면, 그것은 오로지 그에게 임관장을 내준 자들에게뿐일 것이다.\n\n# BOOK X.\n\n독자여, 그대가 어떤 부류의 사람일지 우리로서는 알 길이 없다. 어쩌면 그대는 셰익스피어 그 자신만큼이나 인간 본성에 통달한 사람일 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그의 편집자 몇몇보다 조금도 더 현명하지 않은 사람일 수도 있다. 자, 후자의 경우일까 염려스러우니, 더 멀리 함께 나아가기 전에 그대에게 몇 마디 유익한 충고를 건네는 것이 마땅하다고 본다. 앞서 말한 편집자 몇몇이 자기네 작가를 오해하고 잘못 전한 것처럼, 그대가 우리를 그토록 심하게 오해하고 잘못 전하는 일이 없도록 말이다.\n\n그러면 우선, 이 우리 이야기 속의 어떤 사건이든 그것이 우리의 큰 구상과 무관하고 동떨어진 것이라며 너무 성급히 단죄하지 말라고 그대에게 경고하겠다. 그저 그 사건이 어떤 식으로 그 구상에 이바지하는지 당장 떠오르지 않는다고 해서 말이다. 사실 이 작품은 우리 자신이 빚어낸 하나의 거대한 창조물로 볼 수 있다. 그런데 한낱 벌레만 한 비평가가, 전체가 어떻게 연결되어 있는지도 모른 채, 그리고 마지막 파국에 이르기도 전에, 감히 그 일부에 흠을 잡으려 든다면 이는 더없이 주제넘은 어리석음이다. 여기서 우리가 끌어 쓴 비유와 은유가 우리 처지에는 한없이 과분하다는 것을 인정해야겠다. 하지만 실로, 일류 작가와 최하급 비평가 사이의 차이를 표현하기에 이만큼이라도 들어맞는 다른 비유가 달리 없다.\n\n나의 갸륵한 벌레여, 또 하나 그대에게 건네고 싶은 주의는, 여기 등장하는 어떤 인물들 사이에서 너무 가까운 닮은 점을 찾아내지 말라는 것이다. 이를테면 일곱째 권에 나오는 여관 안주인과 아홉째 권의 안주인 사이에서 말이다. 벗이여, 그대는 알아야 한다. 어느 직업과 생업에서든 대다수 사람이 공유하는 어떤 특성들이 있다는 것을. 이 특성들을 잘 지켜내면서 동시에 그들의 행동을 다채롭게 변주하는 것이 훌륭한 작가의 한 재능이다. 또한, 같은 악덕이나 같은 어리석음에 휘둘리는 두 사람 사이의 미묘한 차이를 짚어내는 것은 또 다른 재능이다. 그리고 이 마지막 재능을 갖춘 작가가 극히 드문 만큼, 그것을 제대로 알아보는 독자 또한 그만큼 드물다. 다만 나는 이를 알아챌 줄 아는 이들에게는 그 관찰이야말로 가장 으뜸가는 즐거움 가운데 하나가 되리라 믿는다. 예컨대 누구든 에피큐어 매먼 경과 포플링 플러터 경은 구별해낼 수 있다. 그러나 포플링 플러터 경과 코틀리 나이스 경 사이의 차이를 알아채려면 한층 더 섬세한 분별력이 필요하다. 그 분별력이 모자란 탓에, 연극을 보는 저속한 관객들은 극장에서 종종 큰 부당함을 저지른다. 나는 더러, 법에서 필적이 닮았다는 것을 증거로 삼는 것보다 훨씬 못한 증거를 가지고 어느 시인이 도둑으로 몰릴 위기에 처하는 것을 본 적이 있다. 실로 내가 우려하건대, 무대 위의 사랑에 빠진 과부란 과부는 모조리 디도를 비굴하게 베껴낸 것이라며 단죄당할 위험에 놓이리라. 다행히도 우리 극장 비평가 가운데 베르길리우스를 읽을 만큼 라틴어를 아는 이가 극히 드물다는 점이 그들을 구해줄 뿐이다.\n\n다음으로, 나의 귀한 벗이여(어쩌면 그대의 가슴이 그대의 머리보다 나을지도 모르니), 우리는 그대에게 일러두어야겠다. 어떤 인물이 완벽하게 선하지 않다고 해서 그를 나쁜 인물이라 단죄하지는 말라고. 만일 그대가 그런 완벽함의 본보기를 좋아한다면, 그대의 취향을 만족시켜줄 책은 얼마든지 쓰여 있다. 그러나 우리는 사람들과 어울려 살아오는 동안 그런 사람을 단 한 번도 만난 적이 없으니, 여기에 그런 인물을 들여놓지 않기로 했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 나는 한낱 인간이 이토록 완전무결한 경지의 탁월함에 이른 적이 과연 있었는지 다소 의심스럽다. 마찬가지로, 유베날리스의 시구\n\n\"----nulla virtute redemptum\n\nA vitiis----\"[*]\n\n를 입증할 만큼 충분히 사악한 괴물이 과연 존재한 적이 있었는지도 의심스럽다. 또한 실로 나는, 그런 천사 같은 완벽함이나 그런 악마 같은 타락함을 지닌 인물을 어떤 상상의 작품 속에 집어넣어서 이루어지는 유익이 무엇인지 도무지 알 수 없다. 둘 중 어느 쪽을 들여다보든, 사람의 마음은 그런 본보기에서 무언가 좋은 쓸모를 끌어내기보다 슬픔과 부끄러움에 짓눌릴 가능성이 더 크기 때문이다. 전자의 경우, 자기 본성 안에 결코 다다를 수 없으리라 마땅히 단념할 만한 탁월함의 본보기를 보면서 마음이 어지럽고 부끄러워질 수 있다. 그리고 후자를 들여다볼 때에도, 자기도 한몫 나누어 가진 그 본성이 그토록 가증스럽고 혐오스러운 존재로 추락한 것을 보면서 못지않게 괴로운 감정에 사로잡힐 수 있다.\n[*] 그 악덕이 단 하나의 미덕으로도 누그러지지 않은 자.\n\n사실, 한 인물 안에 선의가 충분히 들어 있어 올바른 마음을 지닌 이의 찬탄과 애정을 끌어낼 정도라면, \"인간의 본성이 미처 다 막아내지 못한\"(quas humana parum cavit natura) 그 자잘한 흠 몇 가지가 드러난다 해도, 그것은 우리의 혐오가 아니라 오히려 우리의 연민을 불러일으킬 것이다. 실로, 이런 본보기에서 보이는 결점들만큼 도덕적으로 쓸모 있는 것도 없다. 그런 결점은 일종의 놀라움을 자아내어, 몹시 사악하고 못된 사람들의 허물보다 우리 마음에 더 깊이 와닿고 더 오래 머물기 때문이다. 선이 크게 뒤섞인 사람들의 약점과 악덕은, 그것과 대비되어 그 일그러짐을 드러내 보이는 미덕들 때문에 한층 더 도드라진 대상이 된다. 그리고 우리가 아끼는 인물들에게 그런 악덕이 좋지 못한 결과를 데리고 따라붙는 것을 볼 때, 우리는 우리 자신을 위해 그것을 멀리하라는 가르침을 얻을 뿐 아니라, 그 악덕이 우리가 사랑하는 이들에게 이미 끼친 해악 때문에 그것을 미워하라는 가르침까지 얻게 된다.\n\n자 이제, 나의 벗이여, 그대에게 이 몇 마디 충고를 건넸으니, 괜찮다면 우리 다시 한번 이야기를 가지고 앞으로 나아가 보자.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n아일랜드 신사 한 사람의 도착과, 그 뒤를 이어 여관에서 벌어진 매우 기이한 사건들에 관하여.\n\n이제 저 작고 떨리는 산토끼는, 수많은 적들, 그중에서도 교활하고 잔인하며 고기를 탐하는 짐승인 인간에 대한 두려움 때문에 온종일 숨어 지내던 은신처를 벗어나, 풀밭 위를 마음껏 뛰논다. 이제 어느 텅 빈 나무 위에서는 밤의 째지는 합창단원인 올빼미가, 요즘 음악 애호가 몇몇의 귀를 호릴 만한 가락으로 부엉부엉 운다. 이제 절반쯤 취한 시골뜨기가 비틀거리며 교회 묘지를, 아니 차라리 납골당 마당을 가로질러 집으로 돌아가는 동안, 그의 상상 속에서는 두려움이 피투성이 도깨비를 그려 낸다. 이제 도둑과 불한당들은 깨어나고, 정직한 야경꾼들은 곤히 잠들어 있다. 쉬운 말로 하자면, 때는 이제 한밤중이었다. 여관에 머무는 사람들도, 이 이야기에 이미 언급된 이들이든, 저녁나절에 도착한 다른 몇몇이든, 모두 잠자리에 든 뒤였다. 오직 하녀 수전만이 아직 움직이고 있었으니, 그녀는 자신을 애타게 기다리는 마부의 품에 안기러 가기 전에 부엌을 닦아야 했던 것이다.\n\n여관의 사정이 이러할 때, 한 신사가 역마를 타고 그곳에 당도했다. 그는 곧장 말에서 내려 수전에게 다가오더니, 열의에 차 숨이 거의 넘어갈 듯한 채로, 몹시 다급하고 어수선한 투로, 이 집에 부인이 한 사람 묵고 있느냐고 물었다. 밤늦은 시각과, 그 시간 내내 사납게 눈을 두리번거리는 그 사내의 행동에 수전은 조금 놀란 터라, 무어라 대답하기 전에 잠시 머뭇거렸다. 그러자 신사는 곱절로 다급하게, 부디 제대로 알려 달라고 사정하며 말했다. 자기는 아내를 잃어버려 그녀를 쫓아왔노라고. \"맹세코,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"두세 군데서 이미 거의 붙잡을 뻔했는데, 따라잡았다 싶으면 번번이 막 떠난 뒤였소. 만약 그 여자가 이 집에 있다면, 부디 어둠 속으로 나를 데려가 그 여자를 보여 주시오. 또 그 여자가 나보다 앞서 떠났다면, 어느 길로 쫓아가야 그 여자를 만날 수 있을지 일러 주시오. 그러면 맹세코, 내 그대를 이 나라에서 가장 부유한 가난뱅이 여인으로 만들어 주겠소.\" 그러고는 기니 금화를 한 줌 꺼내 보였는데, 그 광경이라면 이 가련한 처녀보다 훨씬 지체 높은 사람들조차 훨씬 더 못된 일을 하도록 매수했을 만했다.\n\n수전은 워터스 부인에 관해 들은 이야기로 미루어, 그 신사가 정당한 주인으로서 쫓아온 바로 그 길 잃은 사람이 틀림없다고 한 치의 의심도 하지 않았다. 그리하여, 남편에게 아내를 되돌려 주는 것보다 더 정직하게 돈을 벌 길은 없으리라고 자못 그럴듯한 이치로 결론을 내린 그녀는, 신사가 찾는 부인이 지금 이 집에 있노라고 망설임 없이 장담했다. 그리고 곧이어 (아주 후한 약속과, 손에 쥐여 준 약간의 선금에 힘입어) 그를 워터스 부인의 침실로 안내하기로 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n상류 사회에서는 아주 단단하고 실질적인 이유에 근거하여 오래전부터 굳어진 관습이 하나 있으니, 남편은 결코 먼저 문을 두드리지 않고서는 아내의 방에 들어가서는 안 된다는 것이다. 이 관습의 숱한 훌륭한 쓰임새는, 세상 물정을 조금이라도 아는 독자에게라면 굳이 귀띔할 필요조차 없으리라. 이 방법 덕분에 부인은 매무새를 다듬거나, 거치적거리는 물건을 미리 치워 둘 시간을 얻기 때문이다. 까다롭고 섬세한 여인이라면 남편에게 들키고 싶지 않은 상황도 더러 있는 법이니까.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 세련된 부류의 사람들 사이에 정해진 여러 격식들이 있는데, 거친 판단에는 그저 형식에 불과한 것으로 보일지 몰라도, 더 분별 있는 이들은 그 안에 실질이 적잖이 담겨 있음을 안다. 그러니 만약 우리의 신사가 이번 경우에 앞서 말한 그 관습을 지켰더라면 얼마나 다행이었으랴. 과연 그도 문을 두드리기는 했다. 그러나 그런 자리에서 흔히 쓰이는 점잖은 노크 따위는 아니었다. 오히려 문이 잠긴 것을 알자, 그는 어찌나 거칠게 달려들었던지, 자물쇠가 단번에 떨어져 나가고 문이 벌컥 열리며, 그는 머리부터 방 안으로 고꾸라졌다.\n\n그가 미처 두 다리로 몸을 일으키기도 전에, 침대에서 마찬가지로 두 다리로 벌떡 일어선 인물이 나타났으니―부끄럽고 슬픈 마음으로 이 이야기를 이어 갈 수밖에 없거니와―바로 우리의 주인공 그 자신이었다. 그는 위협하는 목소리로 그 신사에게 누구냐고, 또 무슨 까닭으로 감히 그토록 무례하게 자기 방문을 부수고 들어왔느냐고 따져 물었다.\n\n신사는 처음엔 자기가 무언가 착각한 줄로 알고, 용서를 청하며 물러나려 했다. 그런데 별안간, 달빛이 아주 밝게 비치는 통에, 코르셋이며 가운, 페티코트, 모자, 리본, 스타킹, 양말대님, 신발, 나막신 따위가 죄다 바닥에 어지러이 널려 있는 것이 눈에 들어왔다. 이 모든 것이 그의 천성적인 질투심을 자극하여, 그를 어찌나 격분케 했던지 그는 말할 힘마저 잃고 말았다. 그래서 존스에게 아무 대답도 하지 않은 채, 그는 침대 쪽으로 다가가려 했다.\n\n존스가 즉시 가로막자 격렬한 다툼이 일어났고, 이내 양쪽 모두 주먹다짐으로 번졌다. 이때 워터스 부인이 (실토하건대 그녀가 같은 침대에 있었으니) 짐작건대 잠에서 깨어, 자기 침실에서 두 사내가 싸우는 것을 보고는 더없이 격렬하게 비명을 지르기 시작했다. 살인이야! 강도야! 그리고 더 자주 외친 것은 겁탈이야! 라는 소리였다. 어쩌면 누군가는 그녀가 마지막 말을 입에 올린 것을 의아해할지 모르나, 이는 그 외침의 말들이라는 것이, 겁에 질린 여인들에게는 음악에서의 파, 라, 라, 라, 다 따위와 마찬가지로, 단지 소리를 실어 나르는 도구일 뿐 어떤 정해진 뜻도 담고 있지 않음을 헤아리지 못한 탓이다.\n\n부인의 방 바로 옆에는 한 아일랜드 신사가 묵고 있었는데, 그는 여관에 너무 늦게 도착한 까닭에 앞서 언급되지 못한 사람이었다. 이 신사는 아일랜드 사람들이 칼라발라로, 곧 기사라 부르는 부류에 속했다. 그는 괜찮은 집안의 차남으로, 집안에 물려받을 재산이 없는 터라 한밑천 마련하려고 밖으로 눈을 돌릴 수밖에 없었다. 그래서 그 목적으로 카드와 여자들로 운수를 시험해 보고자 배스로 가는 길이었다.\n\n이 젊은이는 침대에 누워 벤 부인의 소설 한 권을 읽고 있었다. 한 친구로부터, 자기 식견을 갈고닦아 마음을 훌륭한 문학으로 채우는 것보다 숙녀들에게 자신을 더 효과적으로 추천하는 방법은 없다고 일러 받은 터였기 때문이다. 그러니 옆방에서 그 요란한 소동이 들려오자마자, 그는 베개에서 벌떡 뛰어 일어나, 한 손에는 칼을, 다른 손에는 곁에서 타고 있던 촛불을 들고 곧장 워터스 부인의 방으로 향했다.\n* 벤 부인: 영국 최초의 여성 직업 작가로 꼽히는 애프라 벤(Aphra Behn)을 가리킨다.\n\n셔츠 바람의 또 다른 사내가 등장한 광경이 처음엔 부인의 정숙함에 얼마간 충격을 더했다면, 그것은 곧 그녀의 두려움을 상당히 가라앉혀 주는 것으로 보상을 해 주었다. 칼라발라로가 방에 들어서자마자 이렇게 외쳤기 때문이다. \"피츠패트릭 씨, 도대체 이게 무슨 영문이오?\" 그러자 상대는 즉시 대답했다. \"오, 매클라클런 씨! 당신이 여기 있다니 반갑소.―이 악당이 내 아내를 농락하고는 그 여자와 한 침대에 들어가 있단 말이오.\"―\"무슨 아내요?\" 매클라클런이 외쳤다. \"내가 피츠패트릭 부인을 잘 모를 리가 있소? 그리고 여기 셔츠 바람으로 서 있는 이 신사가 한 침대에 누워 있는 저 부인이, 그분이 전혀 아니라는 게 안 보이오?\"\n\n피츠패트릭은 이제, 부인의 모습을 얼핏 본 것으로도, 또 지금 자기가 서 있는 거리보다 훨씬 멀리서도 알아챌 수 있었을 그녀의 목소리로도, 자기가 아주 불운한 착각을 저질렀음을 깨닫고, 부인에게 거듭 용서를 빌기 시작했다. 그러고는 존스 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"당신한테만은 용서를 빌지 않겠다는 걸 알아 두시오. 당신이 날 때렸으니, 아침에 기어이 당신 피를 보고야 말겠소.\"\n\n존스는 이 협박을 한껏 경멸하며 받아넘겼고, 매클라클런 씨가 대꾸했다. \"정말이지, 피츠패트릭 씨, 이런 한밤중에 사람들을 깨워 놓다니, 부끄러운 줄 아시오. 여관 사람들이 죄다 잠들어 있지 않았더라면, 당신이 나를 깨운 것처럼 그들도 다 깨워 놨을 거요. 이 신사가 당신을 아주 마땅히 다뤘소. 양심을 걸고 말하건대, 나는 아내가 없는 몸이지만, 당신이 내 아내를 그런 식으로 대했다면 나도 당신 목을 베어 버렸을 거요.\"\n\n존스는 자기 부인의 평판이 걱정되어 어찌나 당황했던지, 무어라 말해야 할지도 어찌해야 할지도 알 수 없었다. 그러나 앞서도 살펴보았듯이, 여자들의 임기응변은 남자들의 그것보다 훨씬 더 재빠른 법이다. 그녀는 자기 방과 존스 씨의 방 사이에 통로가 있음을 떠올렸다. 그래서 그의 신의와 자기 자신의 뻔뻔함에 기대어, 이렇게 대답했다. \"무슨 소린지 모르겠어요, 악당들 같으니! 나는 당신들 누구의 아내도 아니에요. 사람 살려! 겁탈이야! 살인이야! 겁탈이야!\"―그리고 이제 여관 안주인이 방으로 들어오자, 워터스 부인은 더없이 사납게 그녀에게 달려들며 말했다. \"나는 이 집을 점잖은 여관인 줄 알았지 매춘굴인 줄은 몰랐어요. 그런데 한 무리의 악당들이 내 방에 쳐들어와 내 정조를, 어쩌면 내 목숨까지 노렸단 말이에요. 그런데 나한테는 그 둘이 똑같이 소중하다고요.\"\n\n이제 여관 안주인이 조금 전 침대 속 가련한 여인이 그랬던 것만큼이나 큰 소리로 울부짖기 시작했다. 그녀가 외쳤다. \"나는 이제 끝장이에요. 여태껏 한 번도 더럽혀진 적 없던 우리 집 평판이 완전히 망가져 버렸어요.\" 그러고는 사내들 쪽으로 돌아서서 외쳤다. \"도대체 무슨 까닭으로 부인 방에서 이 난리법석을 떠는 거예요?\" 피츠패트릭은 고개를 푹 숙인 채, \"자기가 착각을 했으니 진심으로 용서를 빈다\"고 거듭 말하고는, 동향 사람과 함께 물러갔다. 존스는 워낙 영리한지라 미인이 던져 준 암시를 놓칠 리 없었으니, 대담하게 주장하기를, \"문이 부서지는 소리를 듣고 그녀를 도우러 달려온 것이며, 부인을 터는 것 말고는 저들이 무슨 속셈이었는지 짐작도 가지 않거니와, 만약 그게 저들의 작정이었다면, 다행히도 자기가 그걸 막아 낸 셈\"이라고 했다. \"내가 이 집을 꾸려 온 이래로 우리 집에서 강도질이 벌어진 적은 한 번도 없어요.\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"분명히 말해 두는데요, 선생님, 나는 여기서 노상강도 따위는 절대 들이지 않아요. 내 입으로 말하긴 뭣하지만, 그런 말은 입에 담기도 싫어요. 우리 집엔 정직하고 점잖은 양반들만 환영이지요. 그리고 운 좋게도 그런 손님이라면 늘 차고 넘치게 받아 왔답니다. 정말이지 내가 다 모실 수 없을 만큼 많았어요. 여기엔 무슨 무슨 경께서도 묵으셨고―,\" 그러고는 이름과 작위를 줄줄이 늘어놓았는데, 그중 상당수는 여기 옮겨 적었다가는 자칫 그분들의 특권을 침해하는 죄를 저지를지도 모르겠다.\n\n존스는 한참을 참다가, 마침내 워터스 부인에게 셔츠 바람으로 그녀 앞에 나타난 것을 사과하며 그녀의 말을 가로막았으니, \"그녀의 안전을 염려하는 마음이 아니었다면 결코 그런 짓을 하지 않았을 것\"이라고 다짐을 두었다. 그녀의 대답이 어떠했는지, 아니 실은 이 장면이 끝날 때까지 그녀가 보인 처신 전부가 어떠했는지는, 그녀가 꾸며 낸 처지, 곧 한밤중 낯선 사내 셋에게 잠을 깬 정숙한 부인의 처지를 헤아려 보면 독자 스스로 짐작할 수 있으리라. 이것이 그녀가 떠맡아 연기한 배역이었다. 그리고 정말이지 그녀는 그 역을 어찌나 잘 해냈던지, 우리네 극장의 어떤 여배우도 무대 위에서든 무대 밖에서든 그 어떤 연기로도 그녀를 능가하지 못했으리라.\n\n그리고 여기서, 내 생각에 우리는 정숙함이라는 덕이 여성에게 얼마나 지극히 자연스러운 것인지를 증명할 논거 하나를 꽤 정당하게 끌어낼 수 있을 듯하다. 만 명에 한 명도 훌륭한 여배우가 될 만한 사람은 없을 테고, 그런 이들 가운데서도 같은 배역을 똑같이 잘 연기할 수 있는 둘을 보기란 드문 일이지만, 그럼에도 이 정숙함이라는 덕만큼은 여자들 모두가 감탄스러울 만큼 능숙하게 걸쳐 보일 줄 알기 때문이다. 그것을 지니지 못한 이들도, 그것을 지닌 이들 못지않게, 하나같이 더할 나위 없이 완벽하게 그것을 연기해 낼 수 있는 것이다.\n\n사내들이 모두 떠나자, 워터스 부인은 두려움에서 벗어남과 동시에 노여움에서도 풀려나, 한결 부드러운 어조로 안주인에게 말을 건넸다. 안주인은 그래도 집안 평판에 대한 걱정을 그리 쉽게 놓지 못하고, 그 평판을 두둔하느라 자기 지붕 아래 묵어 갔던 숱한 고귀한 분들을 다시 헤아리기 시작했다. 그러나 부인이 그녀의 말을 짧게 끊고는, 그녀가 지난 소동에 아무런 관련도 없음을 분명히 인정해 준 뒤, 부디 자기를 쉬게 내버려 두기를, 그리고 남은 밤 동안 방해받지 않고 그 쉼을 누리기를 바란다고 청했다. 그러자 안주인은 정중한 인사와 거듭된 무릎 굽힌 절을 한 뒤에 물러갔다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n여관 안주인과 객실 하녀 수전이 나누는 대화. 모든 여관 주인과 그 하인들이 읽어 둘 만한 내용이다. 더불어 아름다운 젊은 숙녀가 도착하여 보이는 상냥한 처신이 그려지는데, 이는 지체 높은 사람들에게 어떻게 하면 온 세상의 사랑을 얻을 수 있는지를 가르쳐 줄 것이다.\n\n여관 안주인은 문이 부서져 열렸을 때 잠자리에서 일어나 있던 사람이 수전뿐이었음을 떠올리고, 곧장 그녀에게 가서 이 소동이 처음 어떻게 시작되었는지, 그리고 그 낯선 신사는 누구이며 언제 어떻게 도착했는지를 물었다.\n\n수전은 독자가 이미 아는 이야기를 모조리 들려주었는데, 다만 자기 편할 대로 몇몇 정황만 사실과 달리 둘러댔고, 자기가 받은 돈에 대해서는 완전히 입을 다물었다. 그런데 안주인이 질문을 꺼내기에 앞서, 그 숙녀가 자신의 정조를 노린 어떤 침범이 있지 않을까 겁에 질렸던 일을 두고 한참 측은해하며 말했던 터라, 수전은 안주인이 그 일로 품은 듯한 걱정을 가라앉히려는 마음에서, 존스가 그녀의 침대에서 뛰쳐나오는 것을 똑똑히 보았노라고 한껏 힘주어 맹세하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n이 말에 안주인은 불같이 화를 냈다. \"그거 참 그럴듯한 이야기로구나.\" 그녀가 소리쳤다. \"정말 그랬다면 여자가 비명을 지르며 제 일을 다 까발리려 들었겠느냐! 어떤 숙녀가 비명을 지르는 것 말고 제 정조를 더 잘 증명할 무슨 방도가 있는지 나는 알고 싶구나. 게다가 그 비명은 스무 사람이라도 증언해 줄 수 있을 게다. 부탁이니, 얘야, 내 손님 어느 누구에 대해서도 그런 추문은 퍼뜨리지 말거라. 그건 손님들 체면뿐 아니라 우리 집 체면에까지 먹칠하는 일이고, 분명히 말하지만 여기엔 떠돌이도, 못된 거지 같은 인간도 오지 않는다.\"\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 수전이 말했다. \"그럼 제 두 눈으로 본 것도 못 믿어야 한다는 말씀이네요.\" \"그래, 정말이지 늘 제 눈을 믿을 게 못 된단다.\" 안주인이 대답했다. \"나라도 저런 점잖은 양반들을 두고는 내 눈조차 믿지 않았을 게다. 반년 동안 어젯밤만큼 좋은 저녁상을 주문받은 적이 없었어. 또 어찌나 너그럽고 기분 좋은 분들이던지, 내가 샴페인이라며 우스터셔산 배술을 팔았는데도 흠 하나 잡지 않으셨다. 하기야 그건 이 나라 최고급 샴페인 못지않게 맛도 좋고 몸에도 이로우니까, 아니라면 내가 부끄러워서 내드리지도 않았겠지. 그분들이 그걸 두 병이나 비우셨어. 아니, 아니야, 나는 저렇게 점잖고 착실한 분들을 두고 무슨 나쁜 일이 있었다고는 절대 믿지 않을 게다.\"\n\n이렇게 수전의 입을 막은 뒤, 안주인은 다른 일로 화제를 옮겼다. \"그러니까 네 말은,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그 낯선 신사가 역마차를 타고 왔고, 밖에는 말을 끄는 하인이 하나 있다는 거지. 그렇다면 그분도 분명 너희가 말하는 그 지체 높은 양반들 중 하나일 게다. 왜 저녁을 드시겠느냐고 묻지 않았느냐? 그분이 그 신사분 방에 함께 있는 모양인데, 올라가서 부르셨는지 여쭤 보거라. 집 안에 누가 깨어 음식을 차릴 수 있다는 걸 알면 뭐라도 시키실지 모르니. 자, 평소처럼 실수는 하지 말거라. 불이 다 꺼졌다느니, 닭이 아직 살아 있다느니 하는 소리 말이다. 혹시 양고기를 시키시거든, 우리한텐 없다고 함부로 떠벌리지도 말고. 푸줏간 주인이 내가 자러 가기 직전에 양 한 마리를 잡은 걸 내가 아는데, 내가 청하면 따뜻할 때 곧장 잘라 주는 걸 한 번도 마다한 적이 없으니까. 가거라, 잊지 말고, 양고기든 닭고기든 다 있다고 하거라. 가서 문을 열며 이렇게 말해. '신사 여러분, 부르셨습니까?' 그래도 아무 말씀 없으시거든, 나리께서 저녁으로 무얼 드시면 좋겠느냐고 여쭙거라. '나리'라는 말 잊지 말고. 어서 가. 이런 일들을 더 잘 챙기지 못하면 너는 평생 변변한 사람이 되지 못할 게다.\"\n\n수전은 떠났다가 이내 돌아와, 두 신사가 둘 다 같은 침대에 들어가 있더라고 전했다. \"신사 둘이,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"한 침대에 든다고! 그럴 리가. 분명히 둘 다 형편없는 건달들일 게다. 게다가 젊은 올워디 도련님이 옳게 짐작하신 모양이다. 그자가 마님을 털려고 했다는 그 말 말이야. 만약 그자가 신사가 품을 법한 못된 속셈으로 그 숙녀의 방문을 부수고 들어간 거라면, 제 저녁값과 침대값을 아끼겠다고 슬그머니 다른 방으로 기어들어 갈 리가 없지 않겠느냐. 그자들은 틀림없이 도둑이고, 아내를 찾는다는 건 그저 핑계일 뿐이야.\"\n\n이런 비난으로 우리 안주인은 피츠패트릭 씨를 크게 부당하게 대한 셈이었다. 그는 사실 한 푼도 없을지언정 분명 신사로 태어난 사람이었고, 머리에든 마음에든 더러 흠이 몇 가지 있었을지는 몰라도, 비굴하다거나 인색하다는 것만은 그 흠 가운데 들지 않았다. 실은 그는 어찌나 씀씀이가 큰 사람이었던지, 아내와 함께 꽤 두둑한 재산을 받았음에도 이제는 아내 앞으로 묶어 둔 약간의 보잘것없는 몫만 빼고 한 푼도 남김없이 다 써 버린 처지였다. 그런데 그 몫마저 차지하려고 그는 아내를 어찌나 모질게 대했던지, 더없이 지독한 그의 질투까지 겹쳐, 그 가엾은 여인은 끝내 그에게서 달아날 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그러니 이 신사는 하루 만에 체스터에서부터 달려온 긴 여정으로 몹시 지쳐 있었고, 거기에다 몸싸움에서 단단히 얻어맞은 매까지 더해져 뼈마디가 어찌나 쑤시던지, 마음의 쓰라림과 합쳐져 음식 생각이 싹 가셔 버린 터였다. 또한 하녀의 부추김에 자기 아내인 줄로 착각했던 그 여자에게 이토록 격하게 낙담한 나머지, 처음 덮친 사람을 잘못 골랐을 뿐 그녀가 그래도 이 집 안에 있을지 모른다는 생각은 단 한 번도 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 그날 밤 더 이상 그녀를 찾지 말라는 친구의 만류에 따르고, 침대 한쪽을 나누어 주겠다는 친절한 제안을 받아들였다.\n\n하인과 마차 소년은 사정이 달랐다. 그들은 안주인이 내어 줄 마음보다 훨씬 더 거리낌 없이 주문을 했다. 그래도 그들에게서 일이 정말 어찌 된 것인지, 그리고 피츠패트릭 씨가 도둑이 아니라는 것을 충분히 납득한 뒤, 안주인은 마침내 못 이기는 척 그들 앞에 찬 고기를 좀 차려 놓았는데, 그들이 그것을 게걸스럽게 먹어치우고 있을 때 파트리지가 부엌으로 들어왔다. 그는 앞서 우리가 본 그 소란에 처음 잠을 깼다가, 다시 베개에 머리를 누이고 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓰던 차에, 부엉이 한 마리가 창가에서 어찌나 으스스한 세레나데를 불러 대던지 끔찍한 공포에 사로잡혀 침대에서 벌떡 뛰어내렸고, 부랴부랴 옷을 걸치고는 부엌에서 두런두런 이야기 소리가 들려오는 무리의 보호를 찾아 아래층으로 달려 내려온 것이었다.\n\n그가 온 탓에 우리 안주인은 다시 잠자리로 돌아가지 못했다. 그녀는 마침 다른 두 손님을 수전에게 맡기고 자리를 뜨려던 참이었기 때문이다. 그러나 젊은 올워디 도련님의 친구를 그렇게 소홀히 대할 수는 없었고, 더구나 그가 데운 포도주 한 파인트를 청하는 마당에는 더더욱 그러했다. 그녀는 곧장 응해, 같은 양의 배술을 불에 올렸다. 이 술은 어떤 종류의 포도주 이름에든 척척 둔갑해 주었으니 말이다.\n\n아일랜드인 하인은 잠자리로 물러갔고, 마차 소년도 따라가려던 참이었다. 그러나 파트리지가 남아서 자기 술을 함께 들자고 권하니, 소년은 감지덕지 받아들였다. 사실 그 교사는 혼자 잠자리로 돌아가기가 무서웠던 것이다. 그리고 우리 안주인의 동행을 얼마나 빨리 잃게 될지 알 수 없었기에, 그는 소년만이라도 곁에 붙들어 두기로 작정했다. 소년이 곁에 있는 한 악마든 그 졸개든 두려울 게 없으리라 여겼던 까닭이다.\n\n바로 그때 또 다른 마차 소년이 대문 앞에 다다랐다. 수전이 나가 보라는 명을 받고 나갔다가 돌아오면서, 승마복을 차려입은 두 젊은 여인을 안내해 들였는데, 그중 한 사람은 옷에 어찌나 화려하게 레이스가 둘려 있던지, 파트리지와 마차 소년이 즉시 의자에서 벌떡 일어났고, 우리 안주인은 더없이 부산스레 절을 하며 \"마님, 마님\" 하고 굽실거렸다.\n\n화려한 옷차림의 숙녀가 더없이 너그러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"허락해 주신다면, 부인, 부엌 불가에서 잠깐만 몸을 좀 녹였으면 합니다. 정말 무척 춥군요. 다만 누구도 자리에서 일어나게 하는 일만은 없으면 좋겠어요.\" 이 말은 파트리지를 두고 한 것이었다. 그는 숙녀의 눈부신 옷차림에 더없는 경외와 놀라움에 사로잡혀 방 저편 끝으로 물러나 있었다. 사실 그녀는 이 정도의 존경을 받을 자격을 훨씬 웃돌았으니, 세상에서 가장 아름다운 사람 가운데 하나였기 때문이다.\n\n숙녀는 파트리지에게 자리로 돌아가라고 간곡히 청했으나, 끝내 그를 움직이지는 못했다. 그러자 그녀는 장갑을 벗어, 불 앞에 두 손을 내밀어 보였다. 그 손은 녹는다는 한 가지만 빼면 눈(雪)의 온갖 성질을 다 지니고 있었다. 사실 그녀의 하녀였던 동행 또한 장갑을 벗었는데, 차갑기로나 빛깔로나 영락없이 얼어붙은 쇠고기 한 덩이를 빼닮은 것을 드러내 보였다.\n\n\"마님,\" 하녀가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 더 멀리 가실 생각일랑 마님께서 거두셨으면 합니다. 마님께서 그 피로를 견디지 못하실까 봐 정말 너무 걱정스럽습니다.\"\n\n\"아무렴요.\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"고귀하신 마님께서 설마 그러실 리가요. 어머나, 세상에! 오늘 밤에 더 가시다니요, 정말! 부디 그런 생각일랑 마시기를 마님께 간청드립니다. 하지만 정말이지, 마님께서야 그러실 수 없으시지요. 저녁으로 무얼 드시면 좋겠습니까, 마님? 온갖 양고기가 다 있고, 맛 좋은 닭도 좀 있습니다.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 부인,\" 숙녀가 말했다. \"저녁이라기보다는 아침이 되겠군요. 하지만 저는 아무것도 먹을 수 없어요. 또 머문다 해도 한두 시간 누워 있을 뿐일 거예요. 그래도 괜찮으시다면, 부인, 백포도주를 탄 유장(乳漿)을 아주 묽고 연하게 좀 만들어 주시겠어요.\"\n* 색 위: 백포도주를 데워 우유를 넣어 응고시킨 음료.\n\n\"네, 마님,\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"아주 훌륭한 백포도주가 좀 있습지요.\" \"그럼 색 와인은 없는 건가요?\" 숙녀가 말했다. \"있다마다요, 고귀하신 마님, 있고말고요. 그것만은 이 고장 어디와도 견주어 자신 있습니다. 하지만 부디 마님께 청하오니 무어라도 좀 드시지요.\"\n\n\"정말이지 저는 한 입도 먹을 수가 없어요.\" 숙녀가 대답했다. \"그러니 제 방을 되도록 빨리 채비해 주시면 정말 고맙겠어요. 저는 세 시간 안에 다시 말 위에 오르기로 마음먹었으니까요.\"\n\n\"이런, 수전아,\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"기러기 방에는 벌써 불을 지폈느냐? 죄송합니다, 마님, 제일 좋은 방들이 죄다 찼습니다. 최상류층 손님 여러 분이 지금 잠자리에 들어 계셔서요. 여기 대단한 젊은 도련님 한 분에, 그 밖에도 지체 높은 양반들이 많이 계십니다.\" 수전이 대답했다. \"그 아일랜드 신사분들이 기러기 방에 드셨는데요.\"\n\n\"세상에 이런 일이 다 있나?\" 안주인이 말했다. \"아니, 거의 하루도 빠짐없이 누군가 여기 들르는 걸 뻔히 알면서, 어째서 제일 좋은 방 몇 개는 지체 높은 분들을 위해 비워 두지 않았느냐? 그분들이 신사라면, 이게 마님을 위한 일이라는 걸 알면 분명 다시 일어나 주실 게다.\"\n\n\"저 때문에는 안 됩니다.\" 숙녀가 말했다. \"저를 위해 누구도 방해받게 하고 싶지 않아요. 보통 깨끗한 방이라면, 아무리 수수해도 저한테는 더없이 충분해요. 부디, 부인, 저 때문에 그렇게 애쓰지 마세요.\" \"어머, 마님!\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"그런 거라면야 제법 괜찮은 방이 여럿 있긴 합니다만, 고귀하신 마님께 어울릴 만큼 좋은 방은 하나도 없어서요. 하지만 마님께서 이렇게 너그러이 제가 가진 제일 좋은 방으로 만족해 주시겠다니, 수전아, 어서 장미 방에 지금 당장 불을 지피거라. 마님, 지금 올라가시겠습니까, 아니면 불이 지펴질 때까지 기다리시겠습니까?\" \"몸은 충분히 녹인 것 같군요.\" 숙녀가 대답했다. \"그러니 괜찮으시다면 지금 올라가겠어요. 벌써 사람들을, 특히 저 신사분을(파트리지를 가리키며) 이 추위 속에 너무 오래 붙잡아 둔 것 같아 걱정스럽네요. 정말이지 이 끔찍한 날씨에 누구라도 불가에서 떼어 놓는다는 건 생각만 해도 견딜 수가 없어요.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 하녀와 함께 떠났는데, 안주인이 불 켜진 촛대 두 개를 들고 앞장서 걸었다.\n\n그 착한 여인이 돌아왔을 때, 부엌 안의 대화는 온통 젊은 숙녀의 매력에 관한 것이었다. 정녕 완벽한 아름다움에는 거의 아무도 거스를 수 없는 힘이 있는 법이다. 우리 안주인만 해도, 저녁 식사를 마다당한 것은 못마땅했으면서도, 그토록 사랑스러운 사람은 본 적이 없노라고 단언했으니까. 파트리지는 그 얼굴을 두고 더없이 호들갑스러운 찬사를 쏟아 냈는데, 그러면서도 그 옷에 둘린 금빛 레이스에 한두 마디 칭찬을 보태는 것만은 참지 못했다. 마차 소년은 그녀의 착한 마음씨를 소리 높여 칭송했고, 그 칭송은 이제 막 들어온 또 한 사람의 마차 소년에 의해서도 똑같이 되울려졌다. \"그분은 정말 마음씨 고운 마님이세요, 틀림없어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"말 못 하는 짐승한테까지 자비를 베푸시거든요. 오시는 내내 이따금 저한테, 너무 빨리 달려서 말들이 상하지 않겠느냐고 물으셨고, 들어오실 때는 말들이 먹고 싶은 만큼 실컷 여물을 주라고 신신당부하셨으니까요.\"\n\n상냥함에는 이런 매력이 있어, 온갖 부류의 사람들로부터 칭송을 끌어내는 것이 어김없다. 실로 그것은 저 유명한 허시 부인에 견줄 만하다. 상냥함은 여인의 온갖 장점을 더없이 돋보이게 하고, 온갖 결점을 가려 덮어 주는 데에 그만큼 어김이 없다. 이 자리에서 우리는 이 짧은 생각을 적어 두지 않을 수 없었으니, 독자께서는 여기서 상냥한 처신의 사랑스러움을 보셨다. 그러니 이제 진실은, 그 정반대의 모습을 보여 줌으로써 그것과 대비를 이루도록 우리에게 요구할 것이다.\n\n* 허시 부인: 스트랜드 가의 이름난 여성복 재단사로, 여인의 몸매를 돋보이게 하는 솜씨로 유명했다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n누구에게나 미움받고 멸시당하는 비법, 그 틀림없는 묘약에 관하여.\n\n부인이 베개에 머리를 채 누이기도 전에 시중드는 하녀는 부엌으로 돌아왔다. 제 마님이 마다한 그 맛난 음식들을 실컷 즐기려는 참이었다.\n\n그녀가 들어서자 좌중은 앞서 마님께 보였던 것과 똑같은 예를 갖추어 일제히 자리에서 일어났다. 그러나 그녀는 마님을 본받아 다들 다시 앉으라 권하는 일은 잊고 말았다. 사실 다시 앉기란 거의 불가능했으니, 그녀가 의자를 불 거의 전부를 차지하도록 떡하니 놓아 버렸기 때문이다. 이어 그녀는 당장 닭 한 마리를 구워 내라 명하면서, 십오 분 안에 준비되지 않으면 기다리지 않겠다고 못 박았다. 그런데 정작 그 닭은 그때 마구간에서 홰에 올라 자고 있었고, 석쇠에 오르기까지 잡고 죽이고 털을 뽑는 여러 절차를 거쳐야 했다. 그래도 우리 안주인은 그 시간 안에 다 해 보이겠노라 떠맡았을 것이다. 그러나 손님이 운 나쁘게도 무대 뒤편을 보게 된 처지였으니, 그 속임수를 고스란히 목격할 수밖에 없었으리라. 그리하여 가엾은 안주인은 집에 닭이 없음을 실토할 수밖에 없었다. \"하지만 마님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"정육점에서 양고기라면 어떤 부위든 당장 가져올 수 있습니다요.\"\n\n\"그래, 자네는,\" 시중드는 귀부인 행세를 하는 하녀가 대꾸했다. \"내가 이 야밤에 양고기나 뜯게 말 같은 위장을 가졌다고 보는 건가? 정말이지 자네들처럼 여관 치는 사람들은 윗사람도 죄다 자기네 같은 줄 아는가 봐. 하긴 이런 누추한 데서 뭘 얻어먹으리라 기대하지도 않았지. 우리 마님이 어쩌자고 여기 멈추셨는지 모르겠어. 여기야 상인이나 가축 장수나 드나드는 데지 누가 또 오겠어.\" 안주인은 제 집을 두고 던진 이 모욕에 발끈했다. 그러나 화를 억누르고 이렇게 말하는 것으로 만족했다. \"고맙게도, 아주 점잖은 분들이 자주 찾으신답니다요!\" \"점잖은 분들이라니, 그딴 소리 말게!\" 상대가 소리쳤다. \"점잖은 분들로 말하자면 내가 자네 따위보다야 훨씬 더 잘 안다고. 그러니 제발 그 건방진 말로 날 성가시게 하지 말고, 저녁으로 뭘 먹을 수 있는지나 말해 봐. 나는 말고기는 못 먹지만, 정말이지 배가 고프단 말이야.\" \"이런, 정말이지 마님,\" 안주인이 대답했다. \"이번만큼은 또 이렇게 곤란하게 붙드시면 안 됩지요. 솔직히 집에 아무것도 없습니다요. 차게 식은 쇠고기 한 덩이가 있긴 하나, 실은 어느 신사분 하인하고 마부 녀석이 거진 뼈까지 발라 먹어 치웠고요.\" \"여보게,\" 애비게일 부인이(짧게 부르려고 우리는 그렇게 부르기로 한다) 말했다. \"제발 날 속 메스껍게 만들지 좀 말게. 한 달을 굶었다 해도 그런 작자들 손가락이 닿은 건 못 먹어. 이 끔찍한 데서 깔끔하고 점잖은 건 하나도 못 구하나?\" \"달걀에 베이컨은 어떠십니까요, 마님?\" 안주인이 말했다. \"달걀은 갓 낳은 거야? 오늘 낳은 게 틀림없는가? 그리고 베이컨은 아주 곱고 얇게 썰어 줘. 나는 기름지고 투박한 건 도무지 못 견디니까. 제발 한 번쯤은 그럭저럭 먹을 만하게 해 보고, 집에 농부 마누라나 그런 부류가 있는 줄 알지 좀 마.\" 안주인이 칼을 집어 들려 하자 상대가 그녀를 멈춰 세우며 말했다. \"이봐, 우선 손부터 씻어 줘야겠어. 나는 워낙 까다로운 사람이라, 요람에 있을 적부터 늘 가장 고상한 방식으로만 모든 걸 받아 왔으니까.\"\n\n안주인은 가까스로 자신을 다스리면서 이제 필요한 채비를 시작했다. 수전으로 말하자면 아예 내쳐졌는데, 그 멸시가 어찌나 심했던지 가엾은 계집아이는 손이 거칠게 나가는 것을 참느라, 제 마님이 혀를 붙들고 있느라 애쓴 것 못지않게 안간힘을 써야 했다. 사실 수전은 그것을 온전히 참아내지는 못했다. 말 그대로 이빨 사이에 가두기는 했으나, 그 안에서 \"어이구 잘났네, 너나 나나 똑같은 살덩이 핏덩이지\" 따위 분에 받친 말들을 여러 번 중얼거렸으니 말이다.\n\n저녁이 준비되는 동안 애비게일 부인은 거실에 불을 피워 두라 시키지 않은 것을 한탄하기 시작했다. 그러나 이제는 너무 늦었다고 했다. \"그래도,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"부엌에서 먹는 것도 새롭다고 봐줄 만하지. 전에 한 번도 부엌에서 먹어 본 적이 없는 것 같으니 말이야.\" 그러고는 마부들을 돌아보며 물었다. \"자네들은 왜 말들하고 마구간에 있지 않고?\" \"여기서 이 험한 끼니를 먹어야 한다면요, 마님,\" 그녀가 안주인에게 외쳤다. \"부디 부엌을 좀 비워 줬으면 좋겠어. 동네 잡것들에게 둘러싸이지 않게 말이야. 그쪽 양반으로 말하자면,\" 그녀가 파트리지에게 말했다. \"신사 비슷해 보이니 원하시면 그대로 앉아 계셔도 괜찮습니다. 내가 성가시게 하려는 건 천한 무리들뿐이니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 그렇고말고요, 마님,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"장담컨대 저는 신사올시다. 그리 쉽게 성가셔하는 사람이 아니지요. Non semper vox casualis est verbo nominativus.\" 이 라틴어를 그녀는 무슨 모욕으로 여기고 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"신사이실지는 모르겠으나, 여자한테 라틴어나 늘어놓는 걸 보니 신사답게 보이지는 않는군요.\" 파트리지가 부드럽게 응수하면서 끝에 또 라틴어를 보탰다. 그러자 그녀는 코를 치켜올리고는, 대단한 학자 나리라고 비아냥거리는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n이제 저녁이 식탁에 올랐고, 애비게일 부인은 그토록 까다로운 사람치고는 아주 맛있게 먹어 치웠다. 그리고 자기 명에 따라 같은 음식을 한 차례 더 준비하는 동안 그녀가 말했다. \"그래, 마님, 그쪽 집에 아주 점잖은 분들이 자주 드나든다고 했지?\"\n\n안주인은 그렇다고 답하며 말했다. \"지금도 아주 점잖은 분들하고 양반네가 여럿 묵고 계십지요. 저 젊은 올워디 도련님도 계신데, 저기 저 양반이 잘 아실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 도대체 그 점잖은 젊은 양반, 그 젊은 올워디 도련님이라는 분은 누구죠?\" 애비게일이 말했다.\n\n\"누구긴 누굽니까,\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"서머싯셔의 그 훌륭하신 올워디 나리의 아들이자 상속자지요!\"\n\n\"어머나, 정말이지,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이상한 소릴 다 하시네요. 나는 서머싯셔의 올워디 나리를 아주 잘 아는데, 살아 있는 아들이 없다는 것도 압니다.\"\n\n이 말에 안주인은 귀를 쫑긋 세웠고, 파트리지는 다소 당황한 기색이었다. 그러나 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤 그가 대답했다. \"사실 그렇습니다, 마님. 누구나 그가 올워디 나리의 아들인 줄 아는 건 아니지요. 나리가 그 어머니와 결혼한 적이 없으니 말입니다. 하지만 그가 나리의 아들인 건 분명하고, 그 이름이 존스인 것이 분명한 만큼이나 나리의 상속자가 될 것도 분명합니다.\" 그 말에 애비게일은 입으로 가져가던 베이컨을 떨어뜨리며 외쳤다. \"놀라운 말이네요, 양반! 존스 씨가 지금 이 집에 있다는 게 정말이에요?\" \"Quare non?\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"있을 수 있고말고요, 게다가 분명합니다.\"\n\n애비게일은 이제 서둘러 남은 식사를 마치고는 제 마님에게로 돌아갔다. 그때 오간 대화는 다음 장에서 읽을 수 있다.\n\n사랑스러운 여인과 사랑스럽지 못한 그 하녀가 누구였는지를 밝히는 장.\n\n6월이 되면, 우연이 백합 사이에 심어 놓은 다마스크 장미가 그 순결한 빛깔 속에 진홍빛을 섞어 넣듯, 또는 즐거운 5월에 장난기 어린 어린 암송아지가 꽃 핀 풀밭 위로 향기로운 숨결을 흩뿌리듯, 또는 꽃 피는 4월에 부드럽고 한결같은 비둘기가 어느 아름다운 가지에 앉아 제 짝을 생각하며 명상에 잠기듯, 백 가지 매력을 머금고 그만큼 많은 향기를 내뿜으며, 제 토미에게 온통 마음을 두고서, 얼굴이 아름다운 만큼이나 착하고 순결한 마음을 지닌 소피아가(바로 그녀 자신이었다) 사랑스러운 머리를 손에 기대고 누워 있을 때, 하녀가 방에 들어와 곧장 침대로 달려오더니 외쳤다. \"아씨, 아씨, 지금 이 집에 누가 와 있는지 아세요?\" 소피아가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"설마 아버지께서 우리를 따라잡으신 건 아니겠지.\" \"아니에요, 아씨, 아버지 백 분보다도 더 귀한 분이에요. 바로 지금 이 순간, 존스 도련님이 여기 와 계세요.\" \"존스 씨라고!\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그럴 리가 없어. 내게 그런 행운이 있을 리 없잖아.\" 하녀는 사실이라고 단언했고, 곧 그녀의 주인은 하녀를 보내 그를 불러오게 했다. 당장 그를 만나기로 마음먹었다고 했기 때문이다.\n\n아너 부인이 앞서 우리가 본 그 모양새로 부엌을 나서자마자, 안주인은 그녀에게 호되게 퍼부었다. 사실 이 가엾은 여인은 한동안 가슴에 험한 말들을 잔뜩 쌓아 두고 있었던 터라, 이제 그것들이, 진흙 수레를 막아 두던 판자가 치워지면 오물이 쏟아져 나오듯 그녀의 입에서 쏟아져 나왔다. 파트리지 역시 자기 몫의 비방을 한 삽 거들었는데, (독자를 놀라게 할 만하게도) 하녀만 더럽힌 것이 아니라 소피아 자신의 백합처럼 흰 인품마저 더럽히려 들었다. \"그 통에 그 청어지, 더 나을 게 어디 있나.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"같이 어울리는 자를 보면 그자를 안다(Noscitur a socio)는 말이 참말이야. 하긴, 고운 옷 입은 여자 쪽이 둘 중에선 더 점잖다고는 인정해야겠지. 하지만 장담컨대 둘 다 제 처지에 걸맞은 것보다 나을 것이 하나도 없어. 바스에서 굴러온 갈보 한 쌍이라고 내가 책임지지. 점잖은 분들은 이런 야밤에 하인도 없이 말 타고 돌아다니지 않거든.\" \"에라, 그것 참 맞는 말이네.\" 안주인이 외쳤다. \"당신 정말 정곡을 찔렀구먼. 점잖은 분들이라면 먹든 안 먹든 저녁부터 시켜 놓지 않고는 집에 들어서는 법이 없지.\"\n* Noscitur a socio: \"어울리는 자를 보면 그 사람을 안다\"는 라틴어 격언.\n\n이렇게들 이야기를 나누고 있는데, 아너 부인이 돌아와 자신의 임무를 전했다. 안주인에게 당장 존스 씨를 깨워, 한 숙녀가 그와 이야기하고 싶어 한다고 전하라는 것이었다. 안주인은 그녀를 파트리지에게 떠넘기며 말했다. \"저 사람이 도련님 친구잖수. 나로 말하자면 사내들, 특히 신사 양반들을 부르러 가는 일은 해 본 적이 없으니.\" 그러고는 부루퉁하게 부엌을 걸어 나갔다. 아너는 파트리지에게 매달렸지만, 그는 거절했다. \"내 친구는,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"아주 늦게 잠자리에 들었는데, 이렇게 일찍 깨우면 몹시 화를 낼 거요.\" 아너 부인은 그래도 그를 불러야 한다고 우기며 말했다. \"장담컨대, 도련님은 화를 내기는커녕, 무슨 일인지 알면 더없이 기뻐하실 거예요.\" \"다른 때라면 모를까,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"하지만 우리가 모든 걸 다 할 수는 없는 법(non omnia possumus omnes)이지. 분별 있는 사내한테는 한 번에 여자 하나면 족하거든.\" \"여자 하나라니, 무슨 소리예요, 이 사람아?\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"'이 사람아'라니, 함부로 그러지 마시오.\" 파트리지가 대꾸했다. 그러고는 존스가 어떤 계집과 한 침대에 누워 있다고 대놓고 일러 주며, 여기 옮기기엔 너무 상스러운 표현까지 써 댔다. 이에 아너 부인은 어찌나 격분했는지 그를 건방진 원숭이라고 불러 대고는, 잔뜩 성이 나 부랴부랴 주인에게 돌아갔다. 그러고는 심부름의 결과와 들은 이야기를 전했는데, 가능한 한 부풀려 전했으니, 마치 파트리지의 입에서 나온 그 모든 말을 존스가 직접 내뱉기라도 한 양 그에게 화가 나 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 그 주인에게 욕설을 폭포처럼 쏟아부었고, 단 한 번도 그녀에게 걸맞은 모습을 보인 적 없는 사내에 대한 생각일랑 모두 접으시라고 주인에게 권했다. 그러고는 몰리 시그림의 이야기를 들춰내고, 그가 예전에 소피아 자신을 떠났던 일에도 더없이 악의적인 해석을 갖다 붙였다. 솔직히 고백하건대, 지금의 이 사건이 그런 해석을 적잖이 거들어 준 셈이었다.\n* non omnia possumus omnes: \"우리 모두가 모든 것을 다 할 수는 없다\"는 라틴어 구절.\n\n소피아는 근심으로 마음이 너무나 흩어진 나머지 하녀의 폭포 같은 말을 막을 수가 없었다. 그러나 마침내 그녀는 하녀의 말을 끊으며 말했다. \"도무지 믿을 수가 없어. 어떤 악당이 그를 모함한 거야. 네 말로는 그의 친구한테서 들었다지만, 그런 비밀을 발설하는 게 친구가 할 일은 아니잖니.\" \"제 생각엔,\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"그 사람이 도련님의 뚜쟁이일 거예요. 그렇게 흉악하게 생긴 악당은 처음 봤다니까요. 게다가 존스 씨 같은 방탕한 난봉꾼들은 그런 일을 부끄러워하는 법이 없거든요.\"\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 파트리지의 이 행동은 다소 변명의 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그는 전날 저녁에 들이켠 술기운을 아직 다 자고 떨쳐 내지 못한 상태였고, 거기에 아침이 되어 와인을, 아니 실은 맥아주를 한 파인트 넘게 더 보탠 터였다. 그 배 술이라는 것도 결코 순수한 것이 아니었으니 말이다. 그런데 자연이 술을 담아 두라고 마련해 준 그의 머릿속 저수지가 워낙 얕은지라, 조금만 술이 들어가도 넘쳐흘러 그의 마음의 수문을 열어 버렸고, 그리하여 거기 담겨 있던 온갖 비밀이 죄다 흘러나오고 말았다. 사실 이 수문이라는 것은 본디 단속이 아주 허술하게 되어 있었다. 그의 기질을 최대한 좋게 해석해 보자면, 그는 매우 정직한 사람이었다. 그는 인간 중에서도 가장 캐묻기 좋아하여 늘 남의 비밀을 들쑤시고 다녔는데, 그 대가로 자기가 아는 것은 무엇이든 죄다 알려 줌으로써 아주 충실하게 빚을 갚았기 때문이다.\n\n소피아가 불안에 시달리며 무엇을 믿어야 할지, 어떤 결단을 내려야 할지 갈피를 못 잡고 있을 때, 수전이 색 휘저음술을 가지고 도착했다. 아너 부인은 즉시 주인에게 귀엣말로, 이 계집을 떠보면 아마 진실을 알아낼 수 있을 거라고 권했다. 소피아는 그것이 좋겠다 싶어 다음과 같이 말문을 열었다. \"이리 와 보렴, 얘야. 지금 내가 묻는 말에 사실대로 답해 주면, 약속하건대 후하게 보답하마. 이 집에 젊은 신사 한 분, 잘생긴 젊은 신사 한 분이 계시는데, 혹시…….\" 여기서 소피아는 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 잇지 못했다. \"젊은 신사분 말이다,\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"지금 부엌에 있는 저 건방진 악당이랑 같이 이 집에 들어온 그 젊은 신사 분 말이야.\" 수전이 답했다. \"예, 계셨어요.\" \"그 어떤 부인에 대해서는 아는 게 없니?\" 소피아가 계속 물었다. \"어떤 부인 말이다. 그 여자가 예쁜지 아닌지를 묻는 게 아니야. 어쩌면 안 예쁠 수도 있겠지. 그건 중요한 게 아니야. 하지만 혹시 어떤 부인에 대해 아는 게 있느냐 말이다.\" \"아이고, 아씨,\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"아씨는 심문을 영 못하시겠네요. 얘야, 들어 봐라,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"바로 그 젊은 신사가 지금 어떤 더러운 갈보랑 한 침대에 누워 있지 않니?\" 여기서 수전은 빙긋 웃기만 할 뿐 입을 다물었다. \"물음에 답하렴, 얘야,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"자, 너한테 줄 1기니다.\" \"1기니라고요, 아씨!\" 수전이 외쳤다. \"아이고, 1기니가 뭐 대수라고요. 주인마님이 아시기라도 하면 저는 그 자리에서 당장 일자리를 잃고 말걸요.\" \"여기 하나 더 주마,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그리고 약속하건대, 네 주인마님은 절대 모를 거야.\" 수전은 아주 잠깐 망설인 뒤 돈을 받아들고는 자초지종을 모두 이야기했고, 끝에 가서 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"정 궁금하시다면, 아씨, 제가 살그머니 그분 방에 들어가서 자기 침대에 있는지 없는지 보고 올게요.\" 그래서 그녀는 소피아의 청대로 그렇게 했고, 없다는 대답을 가지고 돌아왔다.\n\n이제 소피아는 부들부들 떨며 얼굴이 창백해졌다. 아너 부인은 부디 마음을 추스르시고, 그런 못난 사내일랑 더는 생각지 마시라고 간청했다. \"저, 아씨,\" 수전이 말했다. \"아씨께서 노여워하지 않으셨으면 좋겠는데, 외람되지만 아씨, 혹시 아씨 성함이 소피아 웨스턴 아씨 아니신가요?\" \"네가 어떻게 나를 안단 말이냐?\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"저, 부엌에 있는 그 사람, 저 여자분이 말한 그 사람이요, 어젯밤에 아씨에 대해 떠들어 댔거든요. 그렇지만 아씨, 제게 화는 안 내셨으면 해요.\" \"정말이지, 얘야,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"화 안 났단다. 부디 다 말해 주렴. 약속하건대 보답하마.\" \"그러니까요, 아씨,\" 수전이 계속했다. \"그 사람이 부엌에서 우리한테 죄다 떠들기를, 소피아 웨스턴 아씨께서…… 아이고, 정말 어떻게 입에 올려야 할지 모르겠네요.\" 여기서 그녀는 말을 멈췄다가, 소피아에게서 용기를 얻고 아너 부인에게서 거세게 재촉을 받자, 이렇게 이어 갔다. \"그 사람이 그러더라고요, 아씨. 물론 죄다 거짓말이겠지만요. 아씨께서 그 젊은 도련님을 사랑하다 못해 죽을 지경이 되셨고, 그래서 도련님은 아씨를 떨쳐 버리려고 전쟁터에 나가는 거라고요. 그때 저는 속으로, 참 마음씨 고약한 못난이로구나 했지요. 그런데 이제 아씨처럼 이렇게 곱고 부유하고 아름다운 분이 저런 평범한 여자 때문에 버림받았다니요. 그게 정말 그렇거든요, 게다가 남의 아내인 여자고요. 어쩐지 참 이상하고 자연스럽지 못한 일이지 뭐예요.\"\n\n소피아는 그녀에게 세 번째 기니를 주고는, 지금 있었던 일을 아무에게도 말하지 않고 자신이 누구인지도 알리지 않는다면 반드시 그녀의 편이 되어 주겠노라고 말한 뒤, 그 아이를 내보냈다. 그러면서 마부 소년에게 당장 말을 준비시키라고 일러 두었다.\n\n이제 하녀와 단둘이 남게 되자, 그녀는 믿음직한 시녀에게 말했다. \"지금보다 더 마음이 편했던 적이 없구나. 이제 똑똑히 알겠어.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 사람은 악당일 뿐만 아니라, 비열하고 천박한 못난이야. 다른 건 다 용서할 수 있어도, 내 이름을 그토록 야만스럽게 입에 올린 것만은 못 참겠어. 그 때문에 그는 내 경멸의 대상이 되었어. 그래, 아너, 이제 마음이 편해. 정말이지 편안하다니까. 아주 편해.\" 그러더니 그녀는 격렬한 눈물을 왈칵 쏟아 냈다.\n\n소피아가 주로 울면서, 그리고 자기는 더없이 마음이 편하다고 하녀에게 거듭 다짐하면서 잠깐의 시간을 보낸 뒤, 수전이 말이 준비되었다는 소식을 가지고 왔다. 그때 우리의 젊은 여주인공에게 아주 기발한 생각 하나가 떠올랐는데, 그 방법대로라면 존스 씨는 그녀가 그 여관에 다녀갔다는 사실을 알게 될 터였고, 만일 그에게 그녀를 향한 애정의 불씨가 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 그것이 적어도 그의 잘못에 대한 얼마간의 벌은 되어 줄 것이었다.\n\n독자께서는 이 이야기에서 이미 한 번 이상 언급되는 영광을 누렸던 작은 토시 하나를 기억해 주시면 좋겠다. 이 토시는 존스 씨가 떠난 이후로 낮에는 소피아의 변함없는 동무였고 밤에는 잠자리의 동무였으며, 바로 지금 이 순간에도 그녀의 팔에 끼워져 있었다. 그녀는 잔뜩 분이 나서 그것을 팔에서 빼더니, 연필로 종이 한 장에 자기 이름을 적어 그 토시에 핀으로 꽂은 뒤, 하녀를 매수해 그것을 존스 씨의 빈 침대에 갖다 두게 했다. 그리고 만일 그가 거기서 그것을 찾지 못한다면, 아침에 어떻게든 그의 눈에 띄게 할 방도를 마련하라고 단단히 일러 두었다.\n\n그런 다음, 아너 부인이 먹은 것에 대한 셈을 치렀는데, 그 계산서에는 그녀 자신이 먹었을 법한 몫에 대한 값까지 포함되어 있었다. 그러고는 말에 올라, 자기는 더없이 마음이 편하다고 동행에게 다시 한번 다짐하면서 여정을 이어 갔다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n여러 가지 가운데서도, 파트리지의 잔꾀와 존스의 광기, 그리고 피츠패트릭의 어리석음을 담은 이야기.\n\n때는 어느덧 새벽 다섯 시가 넘어 있었고, 다른 손님들도 하나둘 일어나 부엌으로 모여들기 시작했다. 그 가운데에는 그 하사관과 마부도 있었는데, 두 사람은 이제 완전히 화해한 터라 함께 헌주를, 영국식으로 말하자면 한 잔 거나하게 들이켰다.\n\n이 술자리에서 파트리지의 행동만큼 눈에 띄는 일은 없었다. 하사관이 조지 왕의 만수무강을 위해 잔을 들었을 때, 파트리지는 오직 \"왕\"이라는 한마디만 따라 했을 뿐, 그 이상은 도무지 입 밖에 내려 하지 않았다. 비록 자기 신념과는 반대편에 서서 싸우러 가는 길이었지만, 그렇다고 제 신념을 거스르며 잔을 들이켤 수는 없었던 것이다.\n\n이제 자기 침대로 돌아온 존스 씨는—다만 그가 어디서 돌아왔는지에 대해서는 굳이 밝히지 않기를 양해해 주기 바란다—이 흥겨운 자리에서 파트리지를 불러올렸다. 파트리지는 격식을 차린 서두를 한참 늘어놓은 끝에 조언을 올려도 좋다는 허락을 받고는 이렇게 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"나리, 옛말에, 그리고 참으로 옳은 말에, 현명한 사람도 때로는 어리석은 자에게서 충고를 얻는다는 말이 있습죠. 그러니 외람되지만 제 조언을 한 말씀 올릴까 합니다. 다름이 아니라, 다시 집으로 돌아가시고, 이 '호리다 벨라(horrida bella)', 이 피비린내 나는 전쟁일랑은 달리 먹을 게 없어서 화약이라도 삼키는 걸로 만족하는 자들에게 맡기시라는 겁니다. 자, 나리께서는 집에 계시면 아쉬울 게 하나도 없다는 걸 누구나 다 압니다. 그런 형편인데, 사람이 어찌하여 객지로 떠돌아야 한답니까?\"\n* 호리다 벨라: 라틴어로 \"끔찍한 전쟁들\"이라는 뜻.\n\n\"파트리지,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"너는 확실히 겁쟁이로구나. 그러니 너야말로 집으로 돌아가서 나를 더는 성가시게 하지 말았으면 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"나리, 송구하옵니다.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"저는 제 일보다 나리 일을 더 생각해서 드린 말씀입니다. 제 형편으로 말하자면, 하늘도 아시다시피 워낙 딱한 처지라, 무서워하기는커녕 권총이든 나팔총이든 그런 것들을 장난감 딱총만큼도 대수롭게 여기지 않습니다. 사람은 누구나 한 번은 죽는 법, 그 방식이 무슨 대수겠습니까? 게다가 운이 좋으면 팔 하나나 다리 하나쯤 잃는 것으로 끝날지도 모르고요. 정말이지 나리, 제 평생 이토록 겁이 안 났던 적이 없습니다. 그러니 나리께서 기어이 가시겠다면, 저도 기어이 따라가겠습니다. 다만 그렇다면 제 의견을 하나 올렸으면 합니다. 나리처럼 훌륭하신 분이 걸어서 길을 가시는 건 정말이지 망신스러운 여행 방식입니다. 그런데 마침 마구간에 쓸 만한 말이 두세 필 있는데, 주인장이 나리께 빌려주는 데 분명 거리낌이 없을 겁니다. 혹시라도 거리낀다면, 제가 쉽게 손을 써서 끌어낼 수도 있고요. 게다가 최악의 경우라 해도, 나리께서는 왕의 대의를 위해 싸우러 가시는 길이니 왕께서 틀림없이 사면해 주실 겁니다.\"\n\n그런데 파트리지의 정직함은 그의 분별력과 엇비슷한 수준이었고, 둘 다 사소한 일에서나 발휘되는 것이었던지라, 그는 이런 도둑질을 완전히 안전하다고 여기지 않았다면 결코 시도하지 않았을 것이다. 그는 사물의 옳고 그름보다 교수대를 더 마음에 두는 부류의 사람이었으니 말이다. 하지만 실은 그는 이 중죄를 아무런 위험 없이 저지를 수 있으리라 믿었다. 올워디 씨의 이름을 대면 주인장을 충분히 잠재울 수 있으리라는 확신은 물론이거니와, 일이 어느 쪽으로 흘러가든 자기들은 안전하리라 여겼던 것이다. 그의 생각으로는, 한쪽으로는 존스에게 든든한 벗들이 있을 테고, 또 다른 쪽으로는 자기 벗들이 자신을 지켜 줄 테니 말이다.\n\n존스 씨는 파트리지가 이 제안을 진심으로 하고 있다는 것을 알아채자 그를 호되게 꾸짖었는데, 그 말투가 어찌나 신랄했던지, 파트리지는 웃어넘기려 하면서 이내 화제를 다른 데로 돌렸다. 그는 이곳이 아무래도 매음굴인 모양이라며, 한밤중에 두 계집이 나리를 방해하지 못하도록 자기가 진땀을 빼며 막았노라 했다. \"이런 참!\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 막든 말든 기어이 나리 방에 들어갔던 모양입니다. 보십쇼, 여기 그중 한 계집의 토시가 바닥에 떨어져 있잖습니까.\" 사실 존스는 어둠 속에서 침대로 돌아오느라 이불 위에 놓인 토시를 미처 보지 못했고, 침대로 뛰어들면서 그것을 바닥으로 떨어뜨린 것이었다. 파트리지가 이제 그 토시를 집어 들어 제 주머니에 넣으려는데, 존스가 그것을 좀 보자고 했다. 그 토시는 워낙 눈에 띄는 물건이어서, 우리 주인공은 거기 달린 정보가 없었더라도 어쩌면 기억해 냈을지 모른다. 하지만 그의 기억력이 그런 힘든 일을 감당할 필요는 없었다. 바로 그 순간 그는 토시에 핀으로 꽂힌 종이 위에서 \"소피아 웨스턴\"이라는 글자를 보고 읽었기 때문이다. 그의 표정은 순식간에 광란에 휩싸였고, 그는 다급하게 외쳤다. \"오, 맙소사! 이 토시가 어떻게 여기 있단 말이냐?\" \"저도 나리만큼밖에 모릅니다.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"다만 제가 막지 않았더라면 나리를 방해했을 그 여인 중 하나의 팔에 끼워져 있던 걸 봤을 뿐입니다.\" \"그들은 어디 있느냐?\" 존스가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 옷을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"지금쯤이면 몇 마일은 떨어진 곳에 있을 겁니다.\" 파트리지가 말했다. 그리고 이제 존스는 더 캐물은 끝에, 이 토시의 주인이 다름 아닌 사랑스러운 소피아 본인이라는 것을 충분히 확신하게 되었다.\n\n이 일을 두고 존스가 보인 행동, 그의 생각, 그의 표정, 그의 말, 그의 몸짓은 무어라 형용할 길이 없는 것이었다. 파트리지를 향해 쓰디쓴 저주를 숱하게 퍼붓고, 제 자신에게도 그에 못지않게 욕설을 쏟아 낸 끝에, 그는 혼비백산한 가엾은 사내에게 어서 아래층으로 내려가 값이 얼마가 들든 말을 빌려 오라고 명령했다. 그러고는 불과 몇 분 후, 부랴부랴 옷을 걸치고 방금 자신이 내린 그 명령을 직접 실행하려고 황급히 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n그러나 그가 부엌에 도착했을 때 벌어진 일로 넘어가기 전에, 파트리지가 주인의 부름을 받고 그곳을 처음 떠난 이후 그 사이에 일어난 일들을 먼저 되짚어 볼 필요가 있겠다.\n\n하사관이 막 자기 무리를 거느리고 떠나자, 두 아일랜드 신사가 일어나 아래층으로 내려왔다. 둘 다 여관 안의 소란에 어찌나 자주 깼던지 밤새 한 번도 눈을 붙이지 못했다며 투덜거렸다.\n\n그 젊은 숙녀와 하녀를 태우고 온 마차는—어쩌면 독자는 지금까지 그것을 그녀 소유의 마차라고 여겼을지도 모르지만—실은 바스의 킹 씨에게 속한 회송용 삯마차였다. 킹 씨로 말하자면 말 거래에 종사한 사람 중에서도 가장 훌륭하고 정직한 이로 손꼽히는 분이었으니, 그의 마차를 그 길로 여행하는 모든 독자에게 진심으로 추천하는 바이다. 그렇게 하면 독자도 어쩌면 이 이야기에 기록된 바로 그 마차를 타고, 바로 그 마부의 모는 솜씨를 누리는 즐거움을 얻게 될지 모른다.\n\n마부는 승객이 둘밖에 없는 데다 매클라클런 씨가 바스로 갈 참이라는 말을 듣고는, 아주 적당한 값에 그를 그곳까지 태워다 주겠노라 제안했다. 그가 이런 제안을 한 데에는 마구간지기의 말이 한몫했다. 마구간지기 말이, 매클라클런 씨가 우스터에서 빌린 말은 긴 여정을 이어 가기보다는 거기 제 동무들에게 돌아가는 편을 훨씬 반길 거라는 것이었다. 그 말이라는 게 네 발 달린 짐승이라기보다는 두 발 달린 짐승에 가깝다나.\n\n매클라클런 씨는 즉시 마부의 제안에 응했고, 동시에 자기 벗 피츠패트릭을 설득해 마차의 남은 한 자리를 받아들이게 했다. 온몸의 뼈마디가 쑤셨던 터라 피츠패트릭에게는 이 이동 수단이 말보다 한결 반가웠다. 게다가 바스에서 아내를 틀림없이 만나리라 확신하고 있었으므로, 조금쯤 늦어진들 대수롭지 않으리라 생각했다.\n\n둘 중 훨씬 영리한 매클라클런은 이 숙녀가 체스터에서 왔다는 말과 더불어 마구간지기에게서 들은 다른 정황들을 듣자마자, 그녀가 어쩌면 제 벗의 아내일지도 모른다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐다. 그리고 이내 이 의심을 피츠패트릭에게 알렸는데, 정작 피츠패트릭 자신은 단 한 번도 그런 생각을 해 본 적이 없었다. 사실대로 말하자면 그는, 자연이 너무 서두른 나머지 머릿속에 뇌라곤 채워 넣는 걸 깜빡한 부류의 인간 가운데 하나였다.\n\n그런데 이런 부류의 사람들은 못난 사냥개와 같아서, 제 힘으로는 결코 냄새를 짚어 내지 못한다. 다만 영민한 개 한 마리가 입을 열어 짖기 시작하면, 그제야 똑같이 따라 짖고, 무슨 냄새의 안내도 없이 그저 곧장 앞으로 있는 힘껏 내달릴 뿐이다. 꼭 그처럼, 매클라클런 씨가 자기 의심을 입에 올리기 무섭게 피츠패트릭 씨도 즉각 맞장구를 쳤고, 아내가 어디 있는지도 모르는 채로 곧장 위층으로 날아 올라가 그녀를 덮치려 했다. 그러고는 운수 사납게도—운명의 여신은 자기에게 모든 걸 내맡긴 신사들을 골탕 먹이기 좋아하는 법이라—여러 문짝과 기둥에 머리를 들이받았지만 다 부질없는 짓이었다. 운명의 여신은 차라리 내게 훨씬 다정했으니, 방금 끼워 넣은 저 사냥개의 비유를 떠올려 주었던 것이다. 이런 경우의 가엾은 아내야말로 쫓기는 산토끼에 견주기에 더없이 어울리니 말이다. 그 작고 가련한 짐승처럼, 아내는 쫓는 자의 목소리에 귀를 쫑긋 세우고, 그 소리가 들리면 그 짐승처럼 부들부들 떨며 달아나며, 결국에는 그 짐승처럼 대개 따라잡혀 끝장나고 만다.\n\n그러나 지금 이 경우는 그렇지 않았다. 한참을 헛되이 뒤진 끝에 피츠패트릭 씨는 부엌으로 돌아왔는데, 마치 이것이 진짜 사냥이라도 되는 양, 사냥개들이 냄새를 놓쳤을 때 사냥꾼들이 하듯 소리를 지르며 한 신사가 들어섰다. 그는 막 말에서 내린 참이었고, 그 뒤꽁무니에는 여러 수행원이 따라붙어 있었다.\n\n여기서, 독자여, 몇 가지 사정을 그대에게 알려 두는 편이 필요할 듯하다. 만일 그대가 그것을 이미 알고 있다면, 그대는 내가 헤아리는 것보다 더 지혜로운 사람일 터이다. 그리고 이 정보는 다음 장에서 받게 될 것이다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n업튼의 여관에서 막 도착한 이 신사는 다름 아닌 웨스턴 영주 본인이었다. 그는 딸을 뒤쫓아 이곳까지 온 것이었다. 만일 운 좋게도 두 시간만 더 일찍 왔더라면 딸뿐 아니라 조카딸까지 덤으로 찾았을 것이다. 그 조카딸이란 바로 피츠패트릭 씨의 아내였으니, 그녀는 다섯 해 전 저 지혜로운 부인 웨스턴 여사의 보호에서 벗어나 그와 함께 달아났던 것이다.\n\n그런데 이 부인은 소피아와 거의 같은 시각에 여관을 떠났다. 남편의 목소리에 잠이 깬 그녀는 사람을 보내 안주인을 불러올렸고, 사정을 전해 듣자 그 선량한 여인에게 터무니없는 값을 치러 매수하여 달아날 말을 마련하게 했던 것이다. 돈이란 이 집안에서 그토록 위력이 컸다. 안주인이 만일 독자만큼 사정을 다 알았더라면 자기 하녀를 타락한 계집이라 내쫓았겠지만, 정작 그녀 자신도 가엾은 수전 못지않게 매수 앞에서 무력했던 것이다.\n\n웨스턴 씨와 그 조카사위는 서로 아는 사이가 아니었다. 설령 알았더라도 웨스턴 씨는 조카사위에게 눈길 한 번 주지 않았을 것이다. 이 결혼은 몰래 이루어진 것이었고, 따라서 선량한 영주의 생각으로는 자연을 거스른 것이었기에, 그는 딸이 그 일을 저지른 그때부터 — 당시 그 가엾은 어린것은 겨우 열여덟에 지나지 않았다 — 그녀를 괴물로 여겨 내버렸고, 그 후로는 자기 앞에서 그녀의 이름이 입에 오르는 것조차 결코 용납하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n주방은 이제 온통 아수라장이었다. 웨스턴은 딸을 찾아 묻고, 피츠패트릭은 그 못지않게 다급히 아내를 찾아 물었다. 그때 존스가 방으로 들어섰으니, 불운하게도 그의 손에는 소피아의 토시가 들려 있었다.\n\n웨스턴은 존스를 보자마자, 사냥꾼들이 사냥감을 눈앞에 두었을 때 내지르는 바로 그 고함을 질렀다. 그러고는 즉시 달려들어 존스를 붙잡으며 외쳤다. \"수여우를 잡았다, 장담컨대 암여우도 멀리 있지 않을 게다.\" 그 뒤로 몇 분 동안 이어진 알아들을 수 없는 소동에서는 여러 사람이 동시에 제각기 다른 말을 떠들어댔으니, 그것을 묘사하기란 무척 어려운 일이거니와 읽는 것 또한 그에 못지않게 불쾌한 일일 것이다.\n\n마침내 존스가 웨스턴 씨를 떨쳐내고 좌중의 몇 사람이 둘 사이를 가로막자, 우리의 주인공은 그 부인에 관해서는 아무것도 모른다며 자신의 결백을 항변했다. 그때 서플 목사가 나서서 말했다. \"그것을 부인하는 것은 어리석은 짓이오. 어찌하여 그러냐 하면, 죄의 증거가 그대의 손에 있기 때문이오. 그대가 손에 든 그 토시가 소피아 아씨의 것임을, 내 친히 단언하며 맹세로써 보증하리다. 근래 들어 그 아씨가 그것을 지니고 다니는 것을 내 자주 보았으니 말이오.\" \"내 딸의 토시라고!\" 영주가 노발대발하며 소리쳤다. \"저자가 내 딸의 토시를 가졌다고? 증인이 되어 주시오, 장물이 저자에게서 나왔소. 당장 저자를 치안판사 앞에 끌고 가겠소. 내 딸 어디 있느냐, 이 악당아?\" \"어르신,\" 존스가 말했다. \"부디 진정하시기를 청합니다. 이 토시가 그 아씨의 것임은 인정하나, 제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리건대, 저는 그분을 결코 뵌 적이 없습니다.\" 이 말에 웨스턴은 인내심을 완전히 잃고, 분에 차서 말도 제대로 잇지 못했다.\n\n하인 몇이 피츠패트릭에게 웨스턴 씨가 누구인지 일러주었다. 그래서 이 선량한 아일랜드 사람은 이제 자기 처삼촌에게 도움을 베풀 기회가 생겼으니, 그렇게 하면 어쩌면 그의 환심을 살 수 있으리라 생각하여 존스에게 다가가 외쳤다. \"양심을 걸고 말하건대, 선생, 내 면전에서 그 신사분의 따님을 본 적이 없다고 부인하다니 부끄럽지도 않소. 내가 침대 위에서 두 사람이 함께 있는 것을 똑똑히 보았는데 말이오.\" 그러고는 웨스턴을 향해 돌아서서, 따님이 있는 방으로 당장 안내하겠노라 청했다. 그 청이 받아들여지자, 그와 영주, 목사, 그리고 몇몇 사람들이 곧장 워터스 부인의 방으로 올라갔는데, 그들은 앞서 피츠패트릭 씨가 그랬던 것 못지않게 거칠게 방으로 들이닥쳤다.\n\n가엾은 부인은 공포 못지않은 경악에 휩싸여 잠에서 화들짝 깨어났고, 침대맡에 베들럼에서 막 뛰쳐나왔다 해도 곧이들을 만한 형상이 서 있는 것을 보았다. 웨스턴 씨의 얼굴에는 그토록 사납고 어지러운 기색이 서려 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그는 그 부인을 보자마자 흠칫 물러섰으니, 입을 열기도 전에 그 거동만으로도 이 여인이 찾던 사람이 아님을 충분히 드러냈다.\n\n여자들은 제 몸보다 제 평판을 그토록 더 애틋이 여기는 법이어서, 이제 몸이 전보다 더 위험에 처한 듯했음에도, 평판만큼은 안전했던지라 부인은 앞서 다른 경우에 그랬던 것처럼 격하게 비명을 지르지는 않았다. 그러나 혼자 남게 되자마자 그녀는 더 잘 생각은 모두 접어버렸고, 지금의 거처에 만족하지 못할 충분한 이유가 있었던 만큼, 할 수 있는 한 서둘러 옷을 차려입었다.\n* 베들럼: 런던의 정신병원으로, 흔히 광인을 가리키는 말로 쓰였다.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 이제 집안 전체를 뒤지기 시작했으나, 가엾은 워터스 부인을 놀라게 한 것만큼이나 아무 소득도 없었다. 그는 낙담하여 주방으로 돌아왔고, 거기서 자기 하인들에게 붙들려 있는 존스를 발견했다.\n\n이 격렬한 소란은 아직 동도 채 트지 않았는데도 집 안의 모든 사람을 깨워 일으켰다. 그 가운데에는 우스터 주의 치안판사직을 맡는 영예를 누리던 점잖은 신사 한 명도 있었다. 웨스턴 씨는 그 사실을 알자마자 그 앞에 자신의 고소를 제기하겠노라 했다. 판사는 그 직무를 집행하기를 사양하며, 지금 서기도 곁에 없고 사법 업무에 관한 책도 한 권 가진 게 없으며, 딸을 빼돌리는 일이라든가 그런 따위의 법을 머릿속에 죄다 담아둘 수는 없는 노릇이라고 말했다.\n\n이때 피츠패트릭 씨가 자기 도움을 빌려주겠노라 나서며, 자신이 본디 법조계에서 자랐노라고 좌중에 일러주었다. (실제로 그는 북아일랜드의 한 변호사 밑에서 삼 년간 서기로 일했었는데, 그 후 좀 더 점잖은 인생행로를 택하여 주인을 떠나 잉글랜드로 건너왔고, 도제 수업 따위가 전혀 필요 없는 직업, 다시 말해 신사라는 직업을 차렸으니, 앞서 일부 언급한 바와 같이 그 일에 성공을 거두었던 것이다.)\n\n피츠패트릭 씨는 딸에 관한 법은 지금의 사건과는 무관하다고 단언했다. 토시를 훔친 것은 의심할 여지 없는 중죄이며, 그 물건이 당사자에게서 발견되었으니 그 사실을 입증하는 충분한 증거라는 것이었다.\n\n판사는 이토록 박식한 조력자의 격려에 더하여 영주의 격렬한 간청에 힘입어, 마침내 사법의 의자에 앉도록 설득당했다. 그렇게 자리를 잡고서, 존스가 여전히 손에 들고 있던 토시를 살펴보고, 그것이 웨스턴 씨의 소유물이라는 목사의 맹세를 듣고 나서, 피츠패트릭 씨에게 구금 영장을 작성하라 청하며 자신이 서명하겠노라 말했다.\n\n이제 존스가 자기 말을 들어달라 청하니, 마침내 어렵사리 허락을 받았다. 그는 토시를 발견한 경위에 관해 파트리지 씨의 증언을 제시했다. 그러나 그보다 더한 것은, 수전이 진술하기를 소피아가 직접 그 토시를 자기에게 건네주며 존스 씨가 그것을 발견한 바로 그 방으로 옮겨다 놓으라 시켰다는 것이었다.\n\n타고난 정의감 때문이었는지, 아니면 존스의 유난한 준수함이 수전을 움직여 그 사실을 털어놓게 한 것인지는 내 판단하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀의 증언이 빚어낸 결과인즉, 판사는 의자에 등을 기대고 앉으며, 이 사건은 이제 앞서 피고에게 불리했던 것만큼이나 깡그리 피고에게 유리해졌노라고 선언했다. 목사도 이에 동의하여, 무고한 사람을 구금하는 데 자신이 도구가 되는 일이 없도록 주께서 막아주시기를 바란다고 말했다. 그러자 판사는 자리에서 일어나 피고를 무죄 방면하고 재판을 파했다.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 이제 그 자리에 있는 모든 이에게 진심 어린 저주를 퍼붓고는, 즉시 말을 대령시켜 딸을 뒤쫓아 떠났다. 조카사위 피츠패트릭에게는 눈길 한 번 주지 않았고, 그 신사에게서 방금 받은 온갖 신세에도 불구하고 친척이라 주장하는 그의 말에 아무런 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 더구나 그토록 다급하고 격분한 와중에, 그는 다행히도 존스에게 토시를 내놓으라 요구하는 것을 잊어버렸다. 다행이라고 말하는 까닭은, 존스라면 그것을 내놓느니 차라리 그 자리에서 죽었을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n존스 역시 셈을 치르자마자 벗 파트리지와 함께, 사랑스러운 소피아를 찾아 길을 나섰다. 그는 이제 결코 다시는 그 뒤쫓기를 포기하지 않으리라 결심했다. 그는 워터스 부인에게 작별 인사를 하는 것조차 견딜 수 없었으니, 그녀를 떠올리는 것만으로도 진저리가 났던 것이다. 비록 의도한 바는 아니었으나 그녀가 소피아와의 더없이 행복했을 만남을 놓치게 한 빌미였기 때문인데, 그는 이제 그 소피아에게 영원한 변함없음을 맹세했다.\n\n워터스 부인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 마침 배스로 가는 마차를 잡아탈 기회를 얻었다. 그래서 두 아일랜드 신사와 일행이 되어 그곳을 향해 떠났는데, 안주인은 친절하게도 그녀에게 옷을 빌려주었다. 그 대가로 안주인은 옷값의 두 배쯤 되는 돈만 받는 것으로 만족했으니, 빌려준 데 대한 보상인 셈이었다. 길 위에서 그녀는 피츠패트릭 씨와 완전히 화해했는데, 그는 매우 잘생긴 사내였던지라, 그녀는 그가 아내가 곁에 없는 것을 달래주기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다 했다.\n\n이렇게 하여 존스 씨가 업튼의 여관에서 겪은 그 숱한 기이한 모험들이 끝을 맺었다. 그곳에서는 오늘날까지도 매혹적인 소피아의 아름다움과 사랑스러운 거동을, 서머싯셔의 천사라는 이름으로 입에 올린다.\n\n이야기가 거꾸로 거슬러 올라가는 장.\n\n우리의 이야기를 더 앞으로 끌고 가기 전에, 소피아와 그녀의 아버지가 업튼의 여관에 그토록 뜻밖에 나타난 까닭을 밝히기 위해, 잠시 뒤를 돌아보는 것이 좋겠다.\n\n독자께서는 이 이야기 일곱째 권 아홉째 장에서, 사랑과 의무 사이의 긴 다툼 끝에 소피아가 — 내 생각에 흔히 그렇듯 — 전자의 손을 들어 그 문제를 매듭짓고 떠났던 일을 기억해 주시면 좋겠다.\n\n이 다툼은, 거기서 보여드린 대로, 바로 직전에 그녀의 아버지가 그녀를 찾아온 데서 비롯되었다. 그는 블리필과의 혼인에 그녀의 동의를 억지로 받아 내려고 왔고, 그 동의는 \"아버지의 어떤 절대적인 명령도 거절해서는 안 되며 거절할 수도 없다\"는 그녀의 말 속에 온전히 담겨 있다고 그가 받아들인 것이었다.\n\n자, 이 면담을 마치고 향사는 딸에게서 거둔 성공에 더없이 기뻐하며 저녁 술자리로 물러났다. 그는 사람들과 어울리기를 좋아하는 성품인지라 자기 행복을 함께 나눌 동무를 두고 싶어 했고, 그리하여 맥주가 부엌으로 아주 넉넉히 흘러가도록 명했다. 그 결과 저녁 열한 시가 되기도 전에 집 안에서 멀쩡히 깨어 있는 사람이라곤 웨스턴 부인 본인과 사랑스러운 소피아뿐이었다.\n\n이른 아침, 블리필 씨를 불러오라고 심부름꾼이 보내졌다. 비록 향사는 그 젊은 신사가 자기 딸이 전부터 품어 온 거부감을 실제보다 훨씬 모르고 있으리라 짐작했지만, 아직 딸의 동의를 받아 두지는 못한 터라, 한시바삐 그 소식을 그에게 전하고 싶어 안달이었다. 신붓감으로 점찍힌 당사자가 제 입으로 그것을 확인해 주리라는 것을 그는 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 혼례로 말하자면, 그 전날 저녁에 남자 측 사람들끼리 다음다음 날 아침에 치르기로 이미 정해 둔 상태였다.\n\n이제 응접실에 아침 식사가 차려졌고, 거기에는 블리필 씨가 와 있었으며, 향사와 그의 누이도 함께 모여 있었다. 그러고 나서 소피아를 불러오라는 분부가 떨어졌다.\n\n오, 셰익스피어여! 그대의 펜이 내게 있다면! 오, 호가스여! 그대의 화필이 내게 있다면! 그렇다면 나는 저 가엾은 하인의 모습을 그려 보이리라. 핏기 없는 얼굴에, 휘둥그레진 눈, 딱딱 부딪치는 이, 더듬거리는 혀, 그리고 떨리는 사지로,\n\n(바로 그런 사내, 그토록 기진하고, 그토록 넋이 빠진,\n그토록 멍하고, 죽은 듯한 낯빛으로, 그토록 비탄에 잠긴 채,\n\n깊은 밤중에 프리아모스의 침장을 걷어 젖히고,\n그에게 트로이의 절반이 불탔노라 고하려 했더라)\n\n하인은 방으로 들어와 이렇게 알렸다. 소피아 아씨가 보이지 않는다고.\n\"보이지 않는다고!\" 향사가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 소리쳤다. \"이런 빌어먹을, 저주받을! 피 튀기고 분통 터지는 노릇이로다! 어디서, 언제, 어떻게, 무슨—보이지 않다니! 어디서?\"\n\n\"어머나! 오라버니,\" 웨스턴 부인이 정치가다운 냉정함으로 말했다. \"오라버니는 늘 아무것도 아닌 일에 그렇게 펄펄 끓어오르시는군요. 제 조카는 그저 정원에 산책을 나갔을 뿐일 거예요. 분명히 말하지만, 오라버니가 어찌나 사리 분별을 잃으셨는지 도무지 한집에서 같이 살 수가 없을 지경이에요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니,\" 향사가 정신을 놓았던 것만큼이나 갑작스레 제정신으로 돌아오며 대꾸했다. \"그게 다라면야 별일 아니지. 하지만 정말이지, 그놈이 애가 안 보인다고 했을 때 내 마음에 불길한 예감이 스쳤단 말이다.\" 그러고는 정원에서 종을 울리라고 명한 뒤, 흡족한 듯 자리에 앉았다.\n\n이 오누이만큼 거의 모든 면에서 서로 정반대인 두 사람도 없을 것이다. 특히 이 점에서 그러했다. 오라버니는 멀찍이 앞일을 내다보는 법이 결코 없으면서도 일이 벌어진 바로 그 순간에 모든 것을 알아채는 데는 더없이 날카로웠고, 반면에 누이는 영원히 멀리 있는 일을 내다보면서도 눈앞에 놓인 것에는 그리 빠른 눈을 갖지 못했다. 이 둘에 관한 본보기를 독자께서는 이미 보아 왔을 것이다. 게다가 두 사람의 그 재주는 저마다 도를 지나쳤으니, 누이는 끝내 일어나지도 않을 일을 곧잘 내다보았고, 오라버니는 실제 사실보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을 곧잘 보아 버리곤 했다.\n\n그러나 이번만큼은 그렇지 않았다. 정원에서도 앞서 방에서 전해진 것과 똑같은 소식이 들려왔으니, 소피아 아씨가 보이지 않는다는 것이었다.\n\n이제 향사가 몸소 나서서, 옛날에 헤라클레스가 힐라스의 이름을 부르짖던 것만큼이나 우렁차고 쉰 목소리로 소피아의 이름을 외쳐 대기 시작했다. 시인이 우리에게 일러 주듯, 온 해안이 그 아름다운 젊은이의 이름을 메아리쳐 돌려보냈던 것처럼, 집과 정원과 이웃한 들판 전체가 오로지 소피아의 이름만을 되울렸다. 사내들의 쉰 목소리로, 또 여인들의 째지는 가락으로 말이다. 그동안 메아리는 그 사랑스러운 소리를 되풀이하는 것이 어찌나 즐거운지, 만일 정말로 그런 존재가 있다면, 내 생각에 오비디우스는 그녀의 성별을 잘못 일러바친 것이리라.\n\n오랫동안 어수선함 말고는 아무것도 지배하지 못했다. 마침내 향사가 숨을 다 써 버린 뒤 응접실로 돌아왔고, 거기서 그는 웨스턴 부인과 블리필 씨를 발견하고는, 얼굴 가득 더없는 낙심을 띤 채 커다란 안락의자에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n여기서 웨스턴 부인은 다음과 같은 위로의 말을 건네기 시작했다.\n\n\"오라버니, 벌어진 일은 저도 안타까워요. 제 조카가 제 집안에 그토록 어울리지 않는 짓을 저질렀다는 것도 말이에요. 하지만 이건 모두 오라버니가 자초한 일이고, 탓할 사람은 오라버니 자신밖에 없어요. 그 애가 늘 제 충고와는 정반대로 길러져 왔다는 걸 오라버니도 아시잖아요. 그러니 이제 그 결과를 보시는 거예요. 조카에게 제멋대로 굴게 내버려 두는 일을 두고 제가 천 번도 더 오라버니와 입씨름하지 않았던가요? 하지만 한 번도 오라버니를 꺾을 수 없었던 걸 아시잖아요. 그리고 제가 그토록 애를 써서 그 애의 고집스러운 생각을 뿌리 뽑고 오라버니의 처신상 잘못을 바로잡아 놓았건만, 그 애가 제 손에서 떨어져 나갔다는 것도 아시잖아요. 그러니 제가 책임질 것은 아무것도 없어요. 만약 그 애의 교육이 온전히 제게 맡겨졌더라면, 이런 사고는 결코 오라버니에게 닥치지 않았을 거예요. 그러니 이 모든 게 오라버니 자신이 한 일이라 여기며 스스로를 다독이셔야 해요. 사실 그렇게 오냐오냐했으니 이 말고 무엇을 바랄 수 있었겠어요?\"\n\n\"제기랄! 누이야,\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"너는 사람 미치게 하고도 남겠구나. 내가 그 애를 오냐오냐했다고? 내가 제멋대로 하게 두었다고?——바로 어젯밤에도 나는 그 애가 내 말을 거역하면 평생토록 빵과 물만 먹이며 제 방에 가둬 두겠다고 으름장을 놓았단 말이다.——너는 욥의 인내심마저 돋워 화나게 할 사람이로구나.\"\n\n\"세상에 이런 소리를 듣는 사람이 또 있을까요?\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"오라버니, 제가 욥 쉰 명만큼의 인내심을 갖고 있지 않았더라면, 오라버니 때문에 온갖 체면과 예법을 다 잊어버렸을 거예요. 어째서 끼어드셨어요? 모든 일을 제게 맡겨 달라고 제가 간청하지 않았던가요, 애원하지 않았던가요? 오라버니는 단 한 번의 헛디딤으로 이 전투의 모든 작전을 망쳐 버렸어요. 제정신인 사람이라면 그런 으름장으로 딸을 자극했겠어요? 영국 여인들을 키르카시아의 노예처럼 다뤄서는 안 된다고 제가 몇 번이나 말했나요? 우리에게는 세상의 보호가 있어요. 우리는 오직 다정한 방법으로만 마음을 얻을 수 있을 뿐, 윽박지르고 못살게 굴고 두들겨서 복종시킬 수 있는 존재가 아니에요. 다행히도 여기엔 살리카 법이 다스리지 않는답니다. 오라버니, 오라버니에게는 저 말고 어떤 여자도 견디지 못할 거친 태도가 있어요. 제 조카가 겁에 질리고 무서워서 이런 짓을 저질렀다 해도 저는 조금도 놀랍지 않아요. 솔직히 말하자면, 제 조카가 한 일은 세상에서 정당하다 인정받으리라 생각해요. 다시 한번 말하지만, 오라버니, 이게 모두 오라버니 자신의 잘못임을 기억하며 스스로를 위로하셔야 해요. 제가 얼마나 자주 충고했던가요——\" 여기서 웨스턴이 황급히 의자에서 일어나, 두세 마디 끔찍한 욕설을 내뱉고는 방 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n키르카시아: 원문은 'Ciracessian'으로, 아마도 캅카스 지방의 키르카시아(Circassian)를 가리키는 듯하다.\n\n그가 떠나자, 그의 누이는 그가 있을 때보다도 (그게 가능하다면) 더 매서운 독설을 그에게 퍼부었다. 그 말이 옳다는 것에 대해 그녀는 블리필 씨에게 동의를 구했고, 그는 더없이 흡족한 낯으로 그녀가 한 말 전부에 온전히 맞장구를 쳐 주었다. 다만 웨스턴 씨의 모든 잘못만큼은 너그러이 보아 주었으니, 그것은 \"아버지의 지나치게 도를 넘은 애정에서 비롯된 것으로 보아야 마땅하며, 그런 애정에는 사랑스러운 약점이라는 이름을 붙여 줄 만하다\"는 것이 그의 변이었다. \"그러니 더더욱 용서받을 수 없지요.\" 부인이 대꾸했다. \"그 애정으로 그분이 망치는 사람이 자기 자식 말고 또 누가 있겠어요?\" 이 말에 블리필은 즉시 동의했다.\n\n그러고 나서 웨스턴 부인은 블리필 씨를 생각하니, 또 그가 그토록 큰 영예를 베풀어 주려던 집안으로부터 받은 대접을 생각하니 몹시 면목이 없다는 뜻을 표하기 시작했다. 이 대목에서 그녀는 조카의 어리석음을 아주 매섭게 다루었으나, 결국에는 모든 것을 제 오라버니에게 돌리며 끝맺었다. 그녀의 말로는, 딸의 동의에 대해 더 확실한 다짐도 받아 두지 않은 채 그렇게까지 일을 밀어붙인 것은 오라버니로서 용서받을 수 없는 일이라는 것이었다. \"하지만 그분은 (그녀가 말하길) 늘 난폭하고 고집스러운 성미였어요. 그분에게 헛되이 쏟아부은 그 모든 충고를 생각하면 저 자신조차 좀처럼 용서가 안 돼요.\"\n\n이런 식의 대화가 한참 더 오갔지만, 그것을 여기 일일이 옮겨 적는다 한들 독자께 큰 즐거움이 되지는 못할 터이니, 그 끝에 블리필 씨는 작별을 고하고 집으로 돌아갔다. 자신의 좌절에 그리 흡족한 기분은 아니었다. 그러나 스퀘어에게서 익힌 철학과 스왁컴이 그에게 불어넣은 신앙심, 게다가 그 밖의 또 다른 무언가가 더해진 덕분에, 그는 더 열정적인 연인들이 이런 종류의 불행을 견디는 것보다는 한결 더 잘 견뎌 낼 수 있었다.\n\n소피아의 탈출.\n\n이제 소피아를 살펴볼 때다. 독자가 그녀를 내가 아끼는 것의 절반만큼이라도 아낀다면, 격정에 사로잡힌 아버지의 손아귀에서, 그리고 냉담하기 그지없는 구혼자의 손아귀에서 그녀가 벗어났음을 알고 기뻐할 것이다.\n\n시간을 새기는 쇠바늘이 낭랑한 종 쇠를 열두 번 두드려, 유령들에게 일어나 밤마다의 순회를 돌라고 불러냈다. 더 쉽게 말하자면, 자정이었다. 그리고 우리가 앞서 말했듯 온 식구가 술과 잠에 파묻혀 누워 있었다. 다만 정치 소책자를 읽는 데 깊이 빠진 웨스턴 부인만이 예외였고, 또 한 사람, 우리의 여주인공만이 예외였다. 그녀는 이제 살그머니 계단을 내려와, 집의 문 하나를 빗장 풀고 자물쇠를 연 뒤 밖으로 나와, 약속한 장소로 서둘러 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n숙녀들이 더러 부리는 그 갖가지 어여쁜 재주, 곧 사소한 일마다 자신의 두려움을 드러내 보이려는 재주(남성들이 자신의 두려움을 감추려고 쓰는 재주만큼이나 그 수가 많다)에도 불구하고, 분명 어떤 정도의 용기는 여자에게 어울릴 뿐 아니라 흔히 그녀가 본분을 다하는 데 꼭 필요하기도 하다. 사실 여성의 품격을 망가뜨리는 것은 사나움의 관념이지 용감함의 관념이 아니다. 마땅히 칭송받는 아리아의 이야기를 읽으면서, 그녀의 굳센 기개 못지않게 그녀의 온유함과 다정함에도 높은 평가를 품지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠는가? 그런가 하면, 생쥐나 들쥐를 보고 비명을 지르는 여자라도 남편을 독살할 수 있고, 더 나쁘게는 남편을 몰아세워 스스로 독을 마시게 만들 수도 있는 법이다.\n\n소피아는 여자가 지닐 수 있는 온갖 온유함을 다 갖추었으면서도, 마땅히 지녀야 할 기개 또한 다 갖추고 있었다. 그래서 그녀가 약속한 장소에 이르렀을 때, 합의했던 대로 하녀를 만나는 대신 한 남자가 곧장 자기 쪽으로 말을 몰아 다가오는 것을 보았으나, 그녀는 비명을 지르지도 기절하지도 않았다. 그렇다고 그때 그녀의 맥박이 평소처럼 고르게 뛰었던 것은 아니다. 처음에는 다소 놀라움과 불안에 휩싸였으니 말이다. 하지만 그 감정들은 솟아오르자마자 거의 곧바로 가라앉았다. 그 남자가 모자를 벗으며 무척 공손한 태도로 \"마님께서 다른 숙녀분을 만나기로 하지 않으셨는지요?\" 하고 물었고, 이어서 자신은 그 숙녀에게 마님을 모셔 가도록 보내진 사람이라고 알려 주었기 때문이다.\n\n소피아로서는 이 말에 어떤 거짓이 있으리라고는 도무지 의심할 수 없었다. 그리하여 그녀는 단호히 그 사내 뒤에 올라탔고, 사내는 그녀를 다섯 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 읍내까지 무사히 데려다주었다. 그곳에서 그녀는 마음씨 고운 어너 부인을 만나는 기쁨을 누렸다. 사실 이 시중 드는 여인의 영혼은 늘 자기 몸을 감싸던 바로 그 옷가지들에 온통 휘감겨 있었던 터라, 그 옷들을 도무지 자기 눈 밖으로 내보내 맡길 수가 없었던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 그 옷들을 몸소 지키고 앉아 있는 한편, 앞서 말한 그 사내에게 온갖 적절한 지시를 일러 주어 자기 여주인에게로 떠나보냈던 터였다.\n\n이제 두 사람은 웨스턴 씨의 추격을 피하려면 어느 길을 잡아야 할지 의논했다. 몇 시간 안에 그가 사람을 풀어 자기들을 뒤쫓으리란 것은 익히 알고 있었다. 런던으로 가는 길은 어너에게 어찌나 매력적이었던지, 그녀는 곧장 그쪽으로 가자고 졸랐다. 소피아가 없어진 것을 이튿날 아침 여덟아홉 시까지는 아무도 알아채지 못할 테니, 설령 추격자들이 그녀가 어느 길로 갔는지 안다 해도 따라잡지 못하리라는 것이었다. 그러나 소피아는 걸린 것이 너무 많아 무엇 하나 운에 맡길 수 없었다. 또 오직 빠르기로만 판가름 날 겨루기에서 자신의 가녀린 다리를 너무 믿고 나설 엄두도 나지 않았다. 그리하여 그녀는 적어도 이삼십 마일은 들판을 가로질러 간 다음, 거기서부터 런던으로 곧장 가는 길을 잡기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 실은 반대편으로 이십 마일을 갈 작정이면서도, 한쪽으로 이십 마일을 갈 말을 빌렸고, 아버지 집에서 타고 왔던 바로 그 안내인을 다시 뒤에 두고 길을 나섰다. 다만 안내인은 이제 소피아 대신, 훨씬 더 무겁고 또 훨씬 덜 사랑스러운 짐을 뒤에 싣고 있었으니, 그것은 다름 아닌 커다란 여행 가방이었다. 그 안에는 어여쁜 어너가 그것으로 숱한 승리를 거두고 끝내는 런던 시내에서 한밑천 잡으려 마음먹은 그 온갖 겉치레 장식들이 가득 채워져 있었다.\n\n런던으로 가는 길로 여관에서 이백 걸음쯤 갔을 때, 소피아는 안내인 곁으로 말을 몰아붙이고는, 입이 벌집이었다고들 하는 플라톤의 목소리조차 그만 못했을 만큼 꿀이 흠뻑 밴 목소리로, 브리스틀로 이어지는 첫 갈림길로 들어서 달라고 청했다.\n\n독자여, 나는 미신을 믿는 사람도 아니고, 요즘의 기적이라는 것을 그리 대단히 믿는 사람도 아니다. 그러니 다음 이야기를 확실한 사실인 양 전하는 것은 아니다. 사실 나 자신도 도무지 믿기 어려운 노릇이니 말이다. 하지만 역사를 적는 자로서의 충실함이, 사람들이 자신 있게 단언해 온 바를 전하지 않을 수 없게 만든다. 그러니까, 안내인이 타고 있던 그 말이 소피아의 목소리에 어찌나 홀렸던지 딱 멈춰 서서는 더 나아가기를 꺼리는 기색을 보였다는 것이다.\n\n하지만 어쩌면 그 일은 사실이되, 전해지는 것만큼 기적적인 일은 아닐지도 모른다. 자연스러운 원인만으로도 그 결과를 충분히 설명할 수 있는 듯하니 말이다. 안내인이 바로 그 순간 무장한 오른쪽 뒤꿈치를 끊임없이 박차던 것을 멈췄으니(휴디브라스처럼 그도 박차를 한쪽만 차고 있었다), 어쩌면 이 한 가지 소홀함만으로도 그 짐승이 멈춰 섰으리라는 것은 충분히 있을 법한 일이다. 더구나 이 말로 말하자면 다른 때에도 곧잘 그러기를 잘했던 터다.\n\n그러나 소피아의 목소리가 정말로 말에게 효험이 있었다 한들, 말 탄 사내에게는 거의 통하지 않았다. 그는 다소 퉁명스레 대답했다. \"나리께서 다른 길로 가라 분부하셨는데, 분부받은 길 말고 딴 데로 갔다간 일자리를 잃을 거구먼유.\"\n\n소피아는 자신의 온갖 설득이 아무 효험이 없음을 알자, 이제 자기 목소리에 거역할 수 없는 마력을 더하기 시작했다. 속담대로 가만히 서 있는 늙은 암말도 종종거리게 만든다는 그 마력, 옛사람들이 완벽한 웅변에 돌렸던 그 거역할 수 없는 힘을 요즘 세상이 죄다 거기에 돌리는 그 마력 말이다. 한마디로, 그녀는 그가 바랄 수 있는 최대치로 그에게 사례하겠노라 약속했다.\n\n그 사내아이는 이 약속들에 완전히 귀를 닫고 있지는 않았다. 다만 그 약속이 두루뭉술하다는 점이 마뜩잖았다. 비록 그 '두루뭉술하다'는 말을 들어 본 적은 없었을지 몰라도, 실은 바로 그것이 그가 못마땅해하는 까닭이었다. 그가 말했다. \"양반님네들은 가난뱅이 사정을 통 헤아려 주질 않는다니께유. 요전 날에두 올워디 나리 댁 어떤 양반을 모시구 시골길을 돌아댕기다가 하마터면 쫓겨날 뻔했구먼유. 그 양반이 제대로 사례두 안 해 주구서리.\"\n\n\"누구하고?\" 소피아가 다급히 물었다. \"올워디 나리 댁 어떤 양반하구유.\" 사내아이가 되풀이했다. \"나리 아드님이라구들 부르는 것 같던디유.\" \"어디로? 어느 길로 갔지?\" 소피아가 물었다. \"그게유, 브리스틀에서 한쪽으로 조금 빠진 데루, 한 이십 마일쯤 떨어진 데였구먼유.\" 사내아이가 대답했다. \"나를 그 자리로 데려다 다오.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그러면 너에게 일 기니를 주마. 그것으로 모자라면 이 기니라도.\" \"암요, 마님께서 제가 무릅쓰는 위험을 헤아려 주시면야 정직하게 따져 이 기니 값은 되구말구유.\" 사내아이가 말했다. \"허나 그래두, 마님께서 이 기니를 약속해 주신다면야 한번 무릅써 보겠습니다유. 나리 말을 끌구 돌아댕기는 건 분명 죄될 짓입지요. 그래두 한 가지 위안이라면, 기껏해야 쫓겨나기밖에 더 하겠습니까. 그러면 이 기니가 어느 정도는 그 손해를 메워 주겠지유.\"\n\n이렇게 흥정이 맺어지자, 사내아이는 브리스틀 길로 방향을 틀었고, 소피아는 존스를 뒤쫓아 길을 나섰다. 이는 어너 부인의 항변과는 한참 어긋나는 일이었으니, 그녀는 존스 씨를 보고 싶은 마음보다 런던을 보고 싶은 마음이 훨씬 더 컸던 것이다. 사실 그녀는 여주인 앞에서 존스의 편이 아니었다. 모든 연애 사건에서, 더구나 은밀한 종류의 연애 사건에서 관례상 시중 드는 여인에게 으레 바쳐야 할 어떤 금전상의 예의를 그가 소홀히 한 죄가 있었기 때문이다. 우리는 이것을 그의 인색함 탓이라기보다 무심한 성미 탓으로 돌리고 싶지만, 어쩌면 그녀는 그것을 인색함 탓으로 여겼을지도 모른다. 분명한 것은, 그녀가 그 일로 그를 몹시 모질게 미워했으며, 기회 있을 때마다 여주인 앞에서 그를 깎아내리기로 작정했다는 사실이다. 그러니 그녀로서는, 하필 존스가 출발했던 바로 그 읍내, 그 여관으로 들게 된 것이 무척 운 나쁜 일이었고, 게다가 바로 그 안내인과 마주치고, 또 소피아가 우연히 알아낸 이 사실을 맞닥뜨리게 된 것은 더더욱 운 나쁜 일이었다.\n\n우리의 여행자들은 날이 밝을 무렵 햄브룩에 도착했고, 거기서 어너는 마지못해 존스 씨가 어느 길로 갔는지 알아보라는 임무를 맡았다. 사실 이런 것쯤은 안내인 자신도 일러 줄 수 있었을 테지만, 소피아는 무슨 까닭에서인지 그에게 그것을 묻는 일이 없었다.\n\n* 햄브룩: 존스가 퀘이커교도를 만났던 마을이다.\n\n어너 부인이 주인장에게서 알아낸 것을 보고하자, 소피아는 꽤 애를 먹은 끝에 시원찮은 말 몇 마리를 구했고, 그 말들이 그녀를 어느 여관까지 데려다주었다. 존스가 머리가 깨져서라기보다 외과의를 만나는 불운 탓에 발이 묶여 있던 바로 그 여관이었다.\n\n여기서 어너는 다시 탐문의 임무를 맡았는데, 안주인에게 다가가 존스 씨의 인상착의를 미처 다 늘어놓기도 전에, 그 눈치 빠른 여인은 흔한 말로 수상한 낌새를 챘다. 그리하여 소피아가 방으로 들어서자, 안주인은 하녀에게 대답하는 대신 여주인 쪽을 향해 다음과 같은 말을 늘어놓기 시작했다. \"아이고 세상에! 이런, 글쎄, 누가 이런 일을 짐작이나 했겠어요? 단언컨대 이 눈으로 본 중에 가장 어여쁜 한 쌍이로구먼요. 정말이지, 마님, 그 도련님이 마님 얘기로 그렇게나 안달복달하셨던 게 하나도 이상할 게 없네요. 사실 그분이 저더러 마님이 세상에서 제일 고운 숙녀라고 하셨거든요. 한데 정말로 그러시네요. 가엾어라, 그 도련님, 그 딱한 마음을! 그분이 베개를 끌어안고서 그걸 사랑하는 소피아 아씨라 부르던 걸 보고 제가 어찌나 가여웠는지 몰라요. 전쟁터에 나가지 마시라고 제가 할 수 있는 건 다 했답니다. 그런 고운 숙녀의 사랑도 받지 못하는, 죽으러 가는 것 말고는 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 사내들이 천지에 널렸다고 말씀드렸지요.\" \"이 마음씨 좋은 분이 제정신이 아니구나.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 안주인이 소리쳤다. \"전 제정신이 아닌 게 아니랍니다. 아니, 마님은 제가 아무것도 모른다고 여기시나요? 장담컨대 그분이 저한테 죄다 얘기하셨어요.\" \"어떤 건방진 작자가\" 어너가 소리쳤다. \"우리 아씨에 대해 뭐라도 지껄였단 말이오?\" \"건방진 작자가 아니라\" 안주인이 대답했다. \"댁이 찾던 그 젊은 도련님이지요. 아주 잘생긴 젊은 분이세요. 게다가 그분은 소피아 웨스턴 아씨를 영혼 밑바닥까지 사랑하신답니다.\" \"그분이 우리 아씨를 사랑한다고! 이봐요, 잘 들어 두시오. 우리 아씨는 그분의 주인 나리께나 어울리는 분이라우.\" \"이런, 어너.\" 소피아가 그녀의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"이 마음씨 좋은 분에게 화내지 마라. 나쁜 뜻이 있는 게 아니잖니.\" \"아무렴요, 정말 그런 뜻이 아니에요.\" 소피아의 부드러운 말투에 용기를 얻은 안주인이 대답했다. 그러고는 여기 다 옮기기엔 너무 지루한 긴 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 그 가운데 몇 마디가 소피아의 기분을 조금 상하게 했고, 그녀의 시중 드는 여인의 기분은 훨씬 더 상하게 했다. 그래서 어너는 두 사람만 남게 되자마자 이를 빌미로 여주인 앞에서 가엾은 존스를 헐뜯었다. \"그분은 틀림없이 아주 한심한 작자임에 분명해요. 술집에서 아씨의 이름을 그렇게 함부로 입에 올려 댈 정도라면, 숙녀에게 무슨 사랑이 있을 리 없잖아요.\"\n\n소피아는 그의 행실을 그렇게까지 나쁘게만 보지는 않았으며, 오히려 그 격렬한 사랑의 황홀경(이것도 안주인은 다른 모든 정황을 부풀린 것 못지않게 부풀려 놓았다)에 기분이 상하기보다 더 흐뭇해했는지도 모른다. 사실 그녀는 그 모든 것을 그의 격정이 지나치게 넘쳐난 탓, 아니 차라리 끓어 넘친 탓, 그리고 그의 솔직한 마음 탓으로 돌렸다.\n\n그러나 이 일은 나중에 그녀의 마음속에서 되살아나, 어너에 의해 더없이 추한 빛깔로 칠해진 채, 업튼에서 벌어진 그 불운한 사건들을 한층 키우고 그럴듯하게 만들어 주었으며, 여주인이 존스를 보지 않고 그 여관을 떠나게 하려는 시중 드는 여인의 노력에 힘을 보태 주었다.\n\n소피아가 말이 준비될 때까지만, 그것도 먹지도 마시지도 않고 머물 작정임을 알게 된 안주인은 곧 물러갔다. 그러자 어너는 여주인을 나무라기 시작했고(사실 그녀는 무척 거리낌 없이 굴었다), 한참 일장 연설을 늘어놓으며 런던으로 가려던 애초의 뜻을 일깨우고, 젊은 사내를 뒤쫓는 일이 얼마나 온당치 못한지 거듭 넌지시 비춘 끝에, 마침내 이런 진지한 권고로 말을 맺었다. \"제발 부탁이에요, 아씨, 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 계신지, 또 어디로 가고 계신지 좀 생각해 보세요.\"\n\n이미 사십 마일 가까이를, 그것도 그리 좋지도 않은 계절에 말을 달려온 숙녀에게 이런 충고는 어지간히 어리석게 들릴 법도 하다. 그녀가 이미 이것을 잘 헤아리고 마음을 굳혔으리라 짐작할 수도 있다. 아니, 어너 부인조차 자기가 흘린 말들로 미루어 그렇게 여기는 듯했다. 그리고 이것이 많은 독자의 생각이리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다. 그들은 진작에 우리 여주인공의 속셈을 훤히 알아채고는, 그 일로 그녀를 바람기 가득한 계집이라고 진심으로 나무라 왔을 테니 말이다.\n\n그러나 실은 그렇지가 않았다. 소피아는 요즈음 희망과 두려움 사이에서, 아버지에 대한 본분과 애정 사이에서, 블리필에 대한 미움과, 그 연민과, 그리고 (어찌 사실을 고백하지 않을 수 있으랴?) 존스에 대한 사랑 사이에서 너무도 마음이 어지러웠다. 그 마지막 사랑은 아버지의 행실, 고모의 행실, 그 밖의 모든 사람의 행실, 그리고 무엇보다 존스 자신의 행실로 인해 활활 불길로 타올랐던 터라, 그녀의 마음은 가히 우리가 무엇을 하는지, 어디로 가는지조차 모르게 만든다고, 아니 차라리 그 어느 쪽의 결말에도 무심하게 만든다고 할 만한 그 어지러운 상태에 빠져 있었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 하녀의 신중하고 사려 깊은 충고는 얼마간 냉정한 성찰을 불러일으켰고, 그녀는 마침내 글로스터로 갔다가 거기서 곧장 런던으로 향하기로 마음을 정했다.\n\n그런데 운수 사납게도, 그 도시로 들어서기 몇 마일 전에 그녀는 그 삯일꾼 변호사와 마주쳤다. 앞서 말했듯이 글로스터에서 존스 씨와 함께 식사를 했던 바로 그 사내였다. 이 작자는 어너 부인과 잘 아는 사이여서, 멈춰 서서 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 소피아는 그때만 해도 그가 누구인지 물어보는 것 말고는 별로 마음에 두지 않았다.\n\n하지만 나중에 글로스터에서 어너에게서 이 사내에 대한 더 자세한 이야기를 듣고, 또 (앞서 말한 대로) 그가 길을 다니는 데 유난히 빠르기로 이름났다는 말을 듣고, 게다가 어너 부인이 그에게 자기들이 글로스터로 간다고 일러 주는 것을 자기가 엿들었던 일까지 떠올리자, 그녀는 아버지가 이 작자를 통해 자기 자취를 그 도시까지 추적해 낼지도 모른다고 두려워하기 시작했다. 그러니 만일 거기서 런던 길로 들어선다면, 아버지가 틀림없이 자기를 따라잡을 수 있으리라 우려했다. 그래서 그녀는 마음을 바꾸었다. 그리하여 갈 작정도 없는 길로 일주일 거리를 갈 말을 빌린 다음, 가벼운 요기를 마치고 다시 길을 나섰다. 이는 하녀의 바람과 간절한 애원을 거스르는 일이었고, 또한 화이트필드 부인의 못지않게 격렬한 만류를 거스르는 일이기도 했다. 화이트필드 부인은 좋은 교양에서, 어쩌면 좋은 천성에서(이 가엾은 젊은 아씨가 무척 지쳐 보였기에) 그날 저녁만은 글로스터에 머물러 가라고 진심으로 권했던 것이다.\n\n차 몇 잔으로만 기운을 차리고, 말이 준비되는 동안 두어 시간 침대에 누워 쉰 뒤, 그녀는 밤 열한 시쯤 단호히 화이트필드 부인의 여관을 나섰다. 그러고는 곧장 우스터 길로 들어서서, 네 시간이 채 못 되어 우리가 마지막으로 그녀를 보았던 바로 그 여관에 다다랐다.\n\n이렇게 우리의 여주인공을 떠나던 순간부터 업튼에 도착하기까지 아주 세세하게 되짚어 보았으니, 이제 몇 마디로 그녀의 아버지를 같은 곳으로 데려가 보자. 그는 딸을 햄브룩까지 데려다준 마부 소년에게서 첫 단서를 얻은 뒤, 아주 손쉽게 그녀의 자취를 글로스터까지 좇았다. 거기서 다시 그는 그녀를 업튼까지 뒤쫓았으니, 존스 씨가 그 길을 잡았다는 것을 알아냈기 때문이다(파트리지로 말하자면, 그 시골 양반의 표현을 빌리건대, 가는 곳마다 짙은 냄새를 뒤에 남기고 다녔던 것이다). 그리고 그는 소피아도, 자기 표현대로 하자면, 같은 길로 달려갔으리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 사실 그는 무척 상스러운 표현을 썼지만, 여기 굳이 옮길 필요는 없겠다. 그 말을 알아들을 사람이라곤 여우 사냥꾼들뿐일 테니, 그들이 알아서 쉬이 떠올릴 것이다.\n\n사흘 남짓을 담다.\n\n지난번 첫머리 장에서 우리는 비평가라 불리는 저 만만찮은 무리를, 우리에게 어울리는 것보다 더 거리낌 없이 다룬 듯하다. 그들은 작가들로부터 큰 굽힘을 요구하고, 실제로도 대개는 그것을 받아 내곤 하니 말이다. 그러므로 이번 장에서는 이 위엄 있는 무리에게 우리가 그리 행동한 까닭을 밝히겠다. 그리고 여기서 우리는, 어쩌면 그들을 지금까지 한 번도 비추어진 적 없는 새로운 빛 아래 세우게 될지도 모른다.\n\n이 비평가(critic)라는 말은 그리스어에서 온 것으로, 판단을 뜻한다. 그래서 나는 짐작하건대, 원어를 제대로 이해하지 못한 채 그 본디 낱말의 영어 번역만 본 어떤 이들은, 그것이 법적 의미에서의 판단을 가리킨다고 결론지었을 것이다. 그 법적 의미에서는 이 말이 흔히 유죄 선고와 같은 뜻으로 쓰인다.\n\n나는 차라리 그 견해 쪽으로 기우는데, 최근 몇 해 사이 가장 많은 비평가가 법률가들 사이에서 나왔기 때문이다. 이 신사들 가운데 다수는, 어쩌면 웨스트민스터 홀의 법관석에는 결코 오르지 못하리라는 절망 때문에, 극장의 좌석에 스스로 자리를 잡았다. 거기서 그들은 자신의 사법적 능력을 발휘하여 판결을 내려 왔으니, 다시 말해 인정사정없이 유죄를 선고해 온 것이다.\n\n이 신사들은, 우리가 그들을 이렇게 이 나라에서 가장 중요하고 명예로운 직책 가운데 하나에 견준 채로 내버려 둔다면 아마 꽤나 흡족해할 것이다. 또 우리가 그들의 호의를 얻으려 했다면 정말 그렇게 했을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 그들을 아주 진솔하게, 게다가 가식 없이 대하려 하므로, 훨씬 낮은 지위의 또 다른 사법 집행관을 그들에게 일깨워 주어야겠다. 그들은 자기네 판결을 선고할 뿐 아니라 스스로 집행하기까지 하므로, 그 집행관과도 어느 정도 먼 닮음이 있는 것이다.\n\n그러나 실은, 이 현대의 비평가들을 아주 정당하고 합당하게 비추어 볼 또 다른 빛이 있다. 바로 흔한 중상꾼의 모습이다. 남의 인격을 들여다보되, 오직 그 허물을 들춰내 세상에 퍼뜨릴 속셈만 품은 사람이 사람들의 명예를 헐뜯는 중상꾼이라는 이름을 받아 마땅하다면, 똑같이 악의에 찬 눈길로 책을 읽는 비평가를 어찌하여 책의 명예를 헐뜯는 중상꾼이라 부르지 못하겠는가?\n\n내가 믿기로, 악덕에는 이보다 더 비천한 종이 없고, 사회에는 이보다 더 가증스러운 해충이 없으며, 악마조차 중상꾼보다 더 자기에게 어울리는 손님을, 아니 더없이 반가운 손님을 맞이할 수는 없을 것이다. 세상은, 안타깝게도 이 괴물을 그가 받아 마땅한 혐오의 절반만큼도 여기지 않는다. 그리고 그에게 베풀어지는 이 죄스러운 관대함의 까닭을 짚어 보기란 나로서는 더욱 두려운 일이다. 그러나 분명한 것은, 이에 견주면 도둑조차 무고해 보인다는 사실이다. 아니, 살인자라 해도 그의 죄와는 좀처럼 견줄 수 없다. 중상이란 칼보다 더 잔인한 무기여서, 그것이 입히는 상처는 언제나 낫지 않기 때문이다. 사람을 죽이는 한 가지 방법, 그것도 온갖 방법 중 가장 비열하고 가장 저주스러운 방법이 있는데, 그것은 여기서 규탄하는 악덕과 정확히 닮은꼴이다. 바로 독이다. 그것은 너무도 비열하면서도 너무도 끔찍한 복수의 수단이라, 한때 우리 법이 그 처벌의 유난한 가혹함으로써 다른 모든 살인과 슬기롭게 구별해 두었을 정도다.\n\n중상이 저지르는 무시무시한 해악과 그것이 이루어지는 수단의 비열함 말고도, 그 흉악한 성질을 한층 더 무겁게 만드는 다른 정황들이 있다. 중상은 흔히 아무런 도발 없이도 일어나며, 어떤 검고 지옥 같은 마음이 남의 파멸과 비참을 빚어냈다는 생각 속에서 보상을 찾는 경우가 아니라면, 좀처럼 스스로에게 어떤 보상도 약속하지 않기 때문이다.\n\n셰익스피어는 이 악덕을 고결하게 짚어 냈으니, 그가 이렇게 말할 때다.\n\n\"내 지갑을 훔치는 자는 쓰레기를 훔치는 것, 그것은 무언가이면서 아무것도 아니라네. 그것은 내 것이었다가 그의 것이 되고, 수천 사람의 종 노릇을 해 온 것. 그러나 내게서 나의 좋은 이름을 슬쩍 가져가는 자는 그를 부유하게 하지도 못하면서 나만을 정녕 가난하게 하는 것을 앗아 가네.\"\n\n이 모든 말에 너그러운 독자는 분명 동의하리라. 그러나 이 가운데 많은 부분은, 책을 헐뜯는 중상꾼에게 적용하면 아마 너무 가혹해 보일 것이다. 그러나 여기서 헤아려 보라. 둘 다 똑같이 사악한 마음의 성향에서 나오며, 똑같이 유혹이라는 변명조차 없다는 것을. 또한 책을 작가의 자식으로, 실로 그의 머리에서 난 아이로 여긴다면, 이런 식으로 가해지는 해악이 결코 가볍지 않다고 결론지을 것이다.\n\n이제껏 자기 시신(詩神)을 처녀의 상태로 그대로 둔 독자라면, 이런 종류의 부성애를 도무지 제대로 그려 보기 어려울 것이다. 그런 이에게는 맥더프의 애틋한 탄식을 살짝 바꾸어 이렇게 말해 줄 수 있겠다. \"아아! 그대는 책 한 권 써 본 적이 없구나.\" 그러나 자기 시신이 무언가를 낳아 본 작가라면 이 애절한 가락을 느낄 것이며, 어쩌면 눈물로 나와 함께해 줄지도 모른다(더구나 그 사랑스러운 아이가 이미 세상에 없다면 더욱 그러하리라). 내가, 만삭의 시신이 그 짐을 안고 다닐 때의 거북함을, 그것을 낳을 때의 고통스러운 진통을, 그리고 마지막으로 다정한 아비가 그 아이를 다 자라 세상에 내보낼 때까지 길러 내는 그 보살핌과 애정을 이야기하는 동안 말이다.\n\n또한 이만큼 순전한 본능의 냄새가 덜 나면서, 동시에 세속의 지혜와도 이토록 잘 들어맞는 부성애도 달리 없을 것이다. 이 자식들이야말로 참으로 아비의 재산이라 불릴 만하며, 그들 가운데 많은 이가 진정한 효심으로 늙은 어버이를 봉양해 왔다. 그러니 이런 중상꾼들의 독기 어린 입김이 그의 책을 때 이른 죽음으로 몰아갈 때, 작가의 애정뿐 아니라 그의 이익까지도 크게 다칠 수 있는 것이다.\n\n마지막으로, 책에 대한 중상은 사실상 작가에 대한 중상이다. 누구든 남을 사생아라 부르려면 그 어미를 창녀라 부르지 않을 수 없듯이, 한심한 물건이니 끔찍한 헛소리니 하는 이름을 책에 붙이려면 그 작가를 멍청이라 부르지 않을 수 없기 때문이다. 멍청이라는 호칭은 도덕적 의미에서는 악당이라는 호칭보다 차라리 나을지 몰라도, 그의 세속적 이익에는 어쩌면 더 해로울 것이다.\n\n자, 이 모든 것이 어떤 이들에게는 우습게 보일지 몰라도, 다른 이들은 그 진실을 느끼고 인정하리라는 것을 나는 의심하지 않는다. 아니, 어쩌면 내가 이 주제를 마땅한 무게로 다루지 않았다고 여길지도 모르겠다. 그러나 사람은 분명 웃는 낯으로도 진실을 말할 수 있는 법이다. 사실, 악의로, 아니 그저 장난삼아서라도 책을 깎아내리는 것은 적어도 매우 심술궂은 짓이다. 그러니 까칠하게 으르렁대는 비평가는, 내 믿기로, 나쁜 사람이 아닐까 의심받아도 좋을 것이다.\n\n그러므로 나는 이 장의 남은 부분에서 이런 부류의 특징들을 밝히고, 내가 여기서 막아 내려는 비평이 어떤 것인지를 보여 주려 한다. 글을 제대로 판단할 자가 아무도 없다고 넌지시 말하거나, 그 노고에 학계가 그토록 큰 빚을 진 저 고결한 비평가들을 문학의 공화국에서 몰아내려 한다는 식으로는, 여기서 지목된 바로 그 사람들이 아니고서는 누구도 나를 오해하지 못할 것이다. 옛사람 가운데는 아리스토텔레스, 호라티우스, 롱기누스가, 프랑스인 가운데는 다시에와 보쉬가 그러했고, 어쩌면 우리들 가운데도 몇 사람 있을 것이다. 이들은 적어도 문학의 법정에서 사법적 권한을 행사하도록 정당하게 인정받은 이들이다.\n\n그러나 비평가의 마땅한 자질을 모두 가려내지 않더라도—그 점은 다른 곳에서 다룬 바 있다—나는 자기가 직접 읽지도 않은 작품에 대해 누군가 내린 혹평에 대해서만큼은 아주 대담하게 이의를 제기할 수 있다고 본다. 이런 혹평가들은, 제 짐작이나 의심에서 떠들든 남의 소문과 의견에서 떠들든, 자기가 단죄하는 책의 명예를 헐뜯는 자라고 마땅히 일컬을 수 있다.\n\n어떤 구체적인 허물도 짚어 내지 않으면서, 한심하다느니 따분하다느니 빌어먹을 물건이라느니 하는 두루뭉술한 비방의 말로 전체를 통째로 단죄하는 자, 특히 천하다라는 한마디를 즐겨 쓰는 자 또한 이런 부류로 의심받아 마땅하다. 이 천하다라는 말은, 진정으로 고귀한 신분이 아닌 한 어떤 비평가의 입에도 어울리지 않는 말이다.\n\n다시 말하건대, 작품에 정당하게 짚을 만한 허물이 더러 있다 해도, 그것이 가장 본질적인 부분에 있는 것이 아니거나, 더 큰 아름다움으로 충분히 메워지는 것이라면, 그저 몇몇 흠 있는 대목 때문에 전체에 가혹한 판결을 내리는 것은 참된 비평가의 판단이라기보다 중상꾼의 악의에 가까울 것이다. 이는 호라티우스의 생각과 정면으로 어긋난다.\n\n베룸 우비 플루라 니텐트 인 카르미네, 논 에고 파우키스 오펜도르 마쿨리스, 콰스 아우트 인쿠리아 푸디트, 아우트 후마나 파룸 카비트 나투라——\n\n허나 아름다움이 더 많이 빛나는 곳에서는, 어쩌다 한 줄이(사소한 흠으로 고르지 못하게 흐르는 그 한 줄이) 부주의한 손길이나 인간의 연약함을 드러낸들, 나는 노여워하지 않는다.—프랜시스 씨 옮김.\n\n마르티알리스가 말했듯, 알리테르 논 피트, 아비테, 리베르. 책이란 달리 지어질 수 없는 법이다. 인격의 아름다움이든, 얼굴의 아름다움이든, 실로 인간의 모든 것의 아름다움이란 이런 식으로 가늠해야 한다. 이 이야기와 같은 작품이, 짓는 데 수천 시간이 들어간 작품이, 어느 특정한 한 장이, 어쩌면 여러 장이 아주 정당하고 합당한 반론의 표적이 될 수 있다는 이유로 단죄당할 처지에 놓인다면, 그것은 참으로 잔인한 일일 것이다. 그런데도 그런 반론에 기대어 책에 가장 가혹한 판결을 내리는 일만큼 흔한 것이 없다. 그 반론들이란, 설령 옳게 짚은 것이라 해도(늘 그런 것도 아니지만) 결코 작품 전체의 가치에까지 미치는 것이 아닌데도 말이다. 특히 극장에서는, 관객의 취향에, 또는 그 관객 가운데 어느 한 비평가의 취향에 들어맞지 않는 표현 한마디면 어김없이 야유를 받기 마련이고, 못마땅한 장면 하나가 작품 전체를 위태롭게 한다. 이토록 가혹한 규칙에 맞추어 글을 쓴다는 것은, 어떤 심통 사나운 견해에 맞추어 살아가는 것만큼이나 불가능한 일이다. 그리고 만일 우리가 어떤 비평가들의, 또 어떤 기독교인들의 생각대로 판단한다면, 이 세상에서 구원받을 작가도 없고, 저세상에서 구원받을 사람도 없을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n소피아가 어튼을 떠난 뒤에 겪은 모험들.\n\n우리 이야기는 방향을 돌려 거꾸로 거슬러 올라가야만 하기 직전에, 소피아와 그녀의 하녀가 여관을 떠났다는 사실을 언급해 두었다. 그러니 이제 우리는 그 사랑스러운 여인의 발자취를 뒤좇기로 하고, 그녀의 변변찮은 연인은 잠시 더 자신의 불운을, 아니 차라리 자신의 못난 행실을 한탄하도록 내버려 두자.\n\n소피아가 안내인에게 시골을 가로질러 샛길로만 가라고 일러 두었던 터라, 그들은 이제 세번강을 건넜고, 여관에서 채 일 마일도 못 갔을 무렵이었다. 그때 젊은 아가씨가 뒤를 돌아보니 여러 마리의 말이 전속력으로 뒤쫓아 오고 있었다. 이에 그녀의 두려움이 크게 솟구쳐, 그녀는 안내인에게 할 수 있는 한 빨리 달리라고 외쳤다.\n\n그는 즉시 그녀의 말을 따랐고, 두 사람은 전속력으로 말을 달렸다. 그러나 그들이 빨리 갈수록, 뒤쫓는 자들도 그만큼 빨리 따라왔다. 그리고 뒤의 말들이 앞의 말들보다 다소 날쌨던 까닭에, 마침내 앞선 이들이 따라잡히고 말았다. 가엾은 소피아에게는 다행한 일이었다. 두려움에 피로까지 겹쳐 그녀의 기력은 거의 바닥난 참이었으나, 이제 더없이 부드러운 태도로, 더할 나위 없이 정중하게 그녀에게 인사를 건네는 어느 여인의 목소리에 그녀는 단번에 마음이 놓였다. 소피아는 숨을 가다듬자마자 똑같이 정중하게, 그리고 스스로도 무척 흡족해하며 그 인사에 답했다.\n\n소피아 일행에 합류하여 그녀에게 그토록 큰 공포를 안겨 준 길손들은, 그녀 자신의 일행과 마찬가지로 두 여인과 한 안내인으로 이루어져 있었다. 두 무리는 누구도 다시 입을 열려 하지 않은 채 꼬박 삼 마일을 함께 나아갔다. 그러다 우리의 여주인공은 두려움을 어지간히 가라앉히고서도(다만 자신이 큰길로 가지 않고 이미 여러 갈림길을 지나왔는데도 상대방이 여전히 곁을 따라오는 것에 다소 의아해하며), 더없이 상냥한 어조로 낯선 부인에게 말을 건넸다. \"두 사람이 같은 길을 가게 되어 무척 기쁘다\"고 말이다. 마치 유령처럼 그저 말을 걸어 주기만을 기다리던 상대편은 선뜻 대답했다. \"기쁨이라면 온전히 자기 것이며, 자기는 이 고장에 아주 낯선 사람인데 같은 여자 동행을 만나게 되어 어찌나 기쁜지, 어쩌면 큰 사과를 받아야 마땅할 만큼 무례하게 그녀와 보조를 맞추어 따라온 것일지도 모르겠다\"고. 이 두 부인 사이에는 더 많은 인사치레가 오갔다. 그도 그럴 것이, 아너 부인은 이제 낯선 여인의 고운 차림새에 자리를 내주고 뒤로 물러나 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 소피아는 저 부인이 어째서 자기와 똑같은 샛길로 줄곧 따라오는지 무척이나 궁금했고, 아니 그 일이 다소 마음에 걸리기까지 했으나, 두려움 때문인지 수줍음 때문인지 아니면 또 다른 무슨 헤아림 때문인지, 차마 그 질문을 입에 올리지는 못했다.\n\n이제 낯선 부인은 차마 역사의 품위에 어울리지 않을 만큼 사소해 보이는 곤란을 겪고 있었다. 지난 일 마일 사이에 그녀의 모자가 머리에서 적어도 다섯 번이나 바람에 날아갔는데, 그것을 턱 밑에 묶을 리본이나 손수건이라곤 도무지 손에 닿지 않았던 것이다. 이 사정을 알게 된 소피아는 곧바로 그 용도로 쓰라며 손수건 하나를 건네주었다. 그런데 손수건을 주머니에서 꺼내느라 아마도 말 다루는 데 너무 소홀했던 모양이다. 마침 불운하게도 짐승이 발을 헛디뎌 앞다리로 고꾸라지면서, 등에 탄 고운 주인을 떨어뜨리고 말았다.\n\n소피아는 머리부터 땅에 떨어졌으나 다행히 조금도 다치지 않았다. 그리고 어쩌면 낙마의 원인이 되었을 바로 그 사정이 이번에는 그녀를 망신에서 구해 주었다. 그들이 지나던 오솔길은 좁은 데다 나무가 빽빽이 우거져, 달빛이 이곳까지는 거의 들지 못했고, 게다가 마침 달이 구름에 가려 거의 완전한 어둠에 잠겨 있었기 때문이다. 이런 까닭에 더없이 섬세했던 이 젊은 아가씨의 정숙함은 그녀의 사지만큼이나 멀쩡히 상처를 면했고, 그녀는 낙마로 인한 약간의 놀라움 외에는 아무런 해도 입지 않은 채 다시 안장에 올랐다.\n\n마침내 날이 환히 밝아 왔다. 이제 두 부인은 들판을 나란히 말을 몰아가다가 서로를 가만히 바라보았는데, 바로 그 순간 두 사람의 눈이 동시에 한 곳에 멎었다. 두 사람의 말도 멈췄고, 두 사람은 함께 입을 열어 똑같은 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다. 한쪽은 소피아의 이름을, 다른 한쪽은 해리엇의 이름을.\n\n이 뜻밖의 만남은 두 부인을 무척이나 놀라게 했으나, 영민한 독자께서는 이만큼 놀라지 않으셨으리라 믿는다. 그 낯선 부인이란 다름 아닌 피츠패트릭 부인, 곧 웨스턴 양의 사촌일 수밖에 없음을 짐작하셨을 테니 말이다. 우리는 앞서 그녀가 소피아보다 몇 분 늦게 여관을 박차고 나섰다고 일러 둔 바 있다.\n\n이 두 사촌이 이 만남에서 느낀 놀라움과 기쁨이 어찌나 컸던지(그도 그럴 것이 두 사람은 예전에 더없이 가까운 친지이자 벗이었고, 오랫동안 웨스턴 고모 댁에서 함께 살았던 사이였다), 두 사람 사이에 오간 축하의 말을 절반도 다 옮기기란 불가능할 지경이었다. 그러고 나서야 비로소 한 사람이 다른 사람에게 지극히 자연스러운 질문을 던졌으니, 곧 어디로 가는 길이냐는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 이 질문은 마침내 피츠패트릭 부인의 입에서 먼저 나왔다. 다만 손쉽고 자연스러워 보이는 질문이었음에도, 소피아는 거기에 선뜻 분명한 답을 내놓기가 어려웠다. 그리하여 그녀는 사촌에게, 어느 여관에 닿을 때까지는 모든 궁금증을 잠시 미뤄 달라고 청했다. \"여관이라면 여기서 멀지 않으리라 싶어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 해리엇, 내 말을 믿어요, 나도 내 쪽 궁금증을 그만큼 미뤄 두고 있으니까요. 사실 우리 둘의 놀라움이 어슷비슷하리라 싶거든요.\"\n\n이 두 부인이 길에서 나눈 대화는, 내 짐작으로는 굳이 옮길 만한 값어치가 별로 없다. 두 시중드는 여인들 사이에 오간 이야기는 더더욱 그러하다. 그들 역시 서로에게 인사치레를 차리기 시작했던 것이다. 안내인들로 말하자면, 한 사람은 맨 앞에 서고 다른 한 사람은 맨 뒤에 따라가야 했던 탓에, 이야기를 나누는 즐거움일랑 누리지 못했다.\n\n이런 형국으로 그들은 여러 시간을 달려, 마침내 넓고 잘 다져진 길로 들어섰다. 그 길에서 오른쪽으로 꺾어 들자 곧 보기에도 그럴듯한 여관에 닿았고, 일행은 모두 말에서 내렸다. 그런데 소피아는 어찌나 지쳤던지, 지난 대여섯 마일을 무척 힘겹게 말 위에 버티고 앉아 왔던 터라, 이제는 부축 없이는 도저히 말에서 내릴 수가 없었다. 그녀의 말고삐를 잡고 있던 여관 주인이 이를 곧 알아채고는 자기 팔로 그녀를 안장에서 받아 내리겠노라 청했고, 그녀도 그 친절한 제안을 선뜻 받아들였다. 실로 운명은 그날 소피아의 얼굴을 붉히게 하기로 작정한 듯했다. 두 번째의 짓궂은 시도는 첫 번째보다 더 잘 들어맞았다. 여관 주인이 젊은 아가씨를 두 팔로 받아 안기가 무섭게, 근래 통풍으로 몹시 시달리던 그의 두 발이 풀썩 꺾이며 그가 나동그라지고 만 것이다. 그러나 동시에 그는 늠름함 못지않은 날쌘 솜씨로 자기 몸을 그 어여쁜 짐 밑으로 던져 넣었으니, 떨어지면서 멍이 든 것은 오직 그뿐이었다. 소피아에게 닥친 큰 해라면, 땅에서 일어설 때 곁에 둘러선 사람들 대부분의 얼굴에서 그녀가 본 가당찮은 히죽거림이 그녀의 정숙함에 안긴 격렬한 충격이었다. 이로 인해 그녀는 실제로 무슨 일이 일어났는지를 의심하게 되었는데, 그것이 무엇인지는 젊은 아가씨의 섬세함이 입은 모욕을 두고 웃을 줄 아는 독자들의 흥을 돋우자고 여기 굳이 옮기지는 않겠다. 우리는 이런 종류의 사고를 결코 우스운 일로 본 적이 없다. 그리고 한 아름다운 젊은 여인의 정숙함을, 고작 웃음에서나 얻을 수 있는 그토록 하찮은 만족과 맞바꾸려는 자가 있다면, 그는 그 정숙함이 무엇인지를 지극히 모자라게 헤아리는 사람임에 틀림없다고 우리는 거리낌 없이 말하겠다.\n\n이 놀라움과 충격에, 그녀의 마음과 몸이 함께 겪은 격심한 피로까지 겹치니, 소피아의 빼어난 기력마저 거의 무너질 지경이었다. 그녀는 하녀의 팔에 기댄 채 비틀거리며 가까스로 여관 안으로 들어갈 힘밖에 남아 있지 않았다. 자리에 앉기가 무섭게 그녀는 물 한 잔을 청했으나, 내가 보기엔 무척 현명하게도, 아너 부인이 그것을 포도주 한 잔으로 바꿔 놓았다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인은 소피아가 지난 이틀 밤을 잠자리에 들지 못했다는 말을 아너 부인에게서 듣고, 또 그녀가 피로로 몹시 창백하고 핼쑥해진 것을 보고는, 한숨 자며 기운을 차리라고 간곡히 권했다. 부인은 아직 소피아의 사연도, 그녀의 근심도 알지 못했다. 그러나 설령 둘 다 알았더라도 똑같이 권했을 것이다. 소피아에게 휴식이 절실히 필요함은 눈에 뻔히 보였고, 또 그들이 샛길로 그 먼 길을 돌아온 덕분에 추격의 위험이 깡그리 사라져, 그 점에서는 부인 자신도 마음이 더없이 편했기 때문이다.\n\n소피아는 어렵잖게 친구의 권유를 따르기로 했고, 그 권유에는 하녀도 진심으로 거들었다. 피츠패트릭 부인 역시 사촌의 곁을 지켜 주겠노라 청했으니, 소피아는 무척 흐뭇하게 그 제안을 받아들였다.\n\n여주인이 잠자리에 들기가 무섭게 하녀도 그 본을 따를 채비를 했다. 그녀는 여관처럼 끔찍한 곳에 자기 자매뻘 되는 시녀를 홀로 두고 가는 것에 대해 거듭 사과를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그러나 상대방은 자기도 한잠 자고 싶기는 매한가지인지라 그 말을 가로막고는, 함께 자리를 같이하는 영광을 달라고 청했다. 소피아의 하녀는 자기 잠자리를 나눠 주는 데 동의하면서도, 그 영광은 온전히 제 몫이라 우겼다. 그리하여 숱한 절과 인사치레가 오간 뒤, 시중드는 여인들도 앞서 그들의 여주인들이 그랬듯 함께 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n우리 여관 주인은(실은 그 업종 사람들이 다 그러하듯) 모든 마부와 하인, 파발꾼 따위에게 손님들의 이름이며, 그들의 재산은 어느 정도이고 어디에 있는지를 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 버릇이 있었다. 그러니 우리 길손들에게 따라붙은 그 숱한 별스러운 사정들, 특히 아침 열 시라는 유난스럽고 별난 시각에 다들 잠자리에 들어 버린 일이 그의 호기심을 부추긴 것도 이상할 게 없었다. 그리하여 안내인들이 부엌에 들어서자마자 그는 저 부인들이 누구이며 어디서 왔는지 따져 묻기 시작했다. 그러나 안내인들은 자기들이 아는 바를 충실히 일러 주었음에도, 그의 호기심을 거의 채워 주지 못했다. 오히려 그의 호기심을 잠재우기는커녕 더욱 부채질했을 뿐이다.\n\n이 여관 주인은 이웃들 사이에서 무척 영민한 사람이라는 평을 듣고 있었다. 사람들은 그가 교구의 어느 누구보다, 심지어 목사보다도 더 멀리, 더 깊이 사물을 꿰뚫어 본다고 여겼다. 어쩌면 그의 인상이 이런 평판을 얻는 데 적잖이 한몫했을 것이다. 그 인상에는 어딘가 놀랍도록 현명하고 의미심장한 데가 있었는데, 특히 입에 담뱃대를 물고 있을 때 그러했고, 사실 그는 담뱃대를 물지 않은 적이 거의 없었다. 그의 처신 또한 그가 지혜롭다는 평판을 키우는 데 크게 거들었다. 거동에 있어 그는 시무룩하다고까지는 못해도 자못 근엄했고, 좀처럼 말을 하지 않았으되 입을 열 때면 늘 느릿한 목소리로 말했다. 게다가 그 문장이 짧으면서도, \"흠\"이며 \"하\" 하는 소리, \"어이\" 하는 추임새, 그 밖의 군소리로 거듭 끊기곤 했다. 그래서 비록 그가 머리를 흔들거나 끄덕이는 일, 검지로 가리키는 일 따위의 설명조 몸짓을 말에 곁들이기는 했어도, 그는 대개 자기가 입에 담은 것보다 더 많은 것을 듣는 이들이 알아채도록 내버려 두었다. 아니, 그는 으레 자기가 적당하다고 여겨 털어놓는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을 알고 있다는 낌새를 풍기곤 했다. 실로 이 마지막 한 가지만으로도 그가 지혜롭다는 평판을 얻은 까닭이 넉넉히 설명될 법하다. 사람이란 자기가 이해하지 못하는 것을 떠받들고 싶어 하는 야릇한 성향이 있으니 말이다. 이것이야말로 인류를 속여 온 여러 사기꾼들이 그 사기의 성공을 온전히 기대어 온 어떤 거대한 비결이다.\n\n이 점잖으신 양반은 이제 아내를 한쪽으로 데려가더니, \"근래 도착한 부인들을 어찌 보느냐\"고 물었다. \"그분들을 어찌 보느냐고요?\" 아내가 말했다. \"원, 제가 그분들을 어찌 봐야 한단 말이에요?\" \"나는 알지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"내가 어찌 보는지는 말이야. 안내인들이 묘한 소리를 늘어놓더군. 하나는 글로스터에서 왔다 하고, 다른 하나는 어튼에서 왔다는데, 내가 알아낼 수 있는 한, 둘 다 그분들이 어디로 가는지는 말을 못 해. 한데 어튼에서 여기까지, 게다가 런던으로 가겠다고 시골을 가로질러 다니는 사람이 대체 어디 있느냔 말이야. 그리고 하녀 하나는 말에서 내리기 전에, 여기가 런던 가는 길이 아니냐고 묻더란 말이지. 자, 내가 이 모든 사정을 한데 모아 봤는데, 그분들이 누구일 거라 알아냈는지 짐작이나 가나?\" \"원, 참.\" 아내가 대답했다. \"제가 당신이 알아내신 걸 함부로 넘겨짚는 법이 없는 줄 잘 아시잖아요.\" \"착한 사람이지.\" 그가 아내의 턱을 살짝 치켜올리며 대꾸했다. \"이런 일에 관한 한 당신이 늘 내 식견에 따라 줬다는 건 나도 인정하지. 그러니 단단히 믿어 두라고. 내 말 잘 들어 — 틀림없어, 그분들은 분명 그 반란군 부인들 가운데 일부야. 그 젊은 기사 나리와 함께 다닌다는 사람들 말이야. 공작님 군대를 피하느라 빙 둘러 가는 길을 잡은 거라고.\"\n\n\"여보.\" 아내가 말했다. \"당신 분명 제대로 짚으셨어요. 그분들 중 하나는 어느 공주님 못지않게 곱게 차려입었던 데다, 정말이지 영락없는 공주님 같았으니까요. 하지만 그래도 한 가지를 곰곰 생각해 보면요—\" \"당신이 곰곰 생각을 한다.\" 여관 주인이 깔보듯 외쳤다. \"어디 한번, 당신이 곰곰 생각한 걸 들어나 봅시다.\" \"아니, 그게요.\" 아내가 대답했다. \"그분이 그리 대단한 귀부인이라기엔 너무 겸손하시단 말이에요. 우리 베티가 잠자리를 데우는 동안에도, 그분은 베티더러 그저 '얘야' '아가' '예쁜이' 라고만 부르셨고, 베티가 신발이며 양말을 벗겨 드리겠다고 했을 때도 한사코 못 하게 하시면서, 수고를 끼칠 수는 없다고 하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"흥!\" 남편이 대꾸했다. \"그까짓 게 무슨 대수라고. 당신이 어떤 귀부인들이 아랫사람들에게 무례하고 막되게 구는 꼴을 좀 봤다고 해서, 귀부인 가운데 아랫사람 앞에서 처신할 줄 아는 이가 하나도 없을 줄 아나? 나는 멋쟁이 양반들을 보면 알아본다고 — 암, 알아보고말고. 그분이 들어오자마자 물 한 잔을 청하지 않았나? 딴 부류의 여자들 같았으면 독한 술 한 잔을 청했을 거야 — 당신도 알잖아, 그랬을 거라고. 만약 저분이 더없이 지체 높은 귀부인이 아니라면, 나를 바보로 팔아넘기라지. 한데 나를 사는 사람은 손해 보는 흥정을 한 셈일 거야. 자, 저만한 지체의 여자가 이런 별난 사정이 아니고서야 하인 하나 없이 길을 다니겠어?\" \"원, 그야 분명 그렇겠죠, 여보.\" 아내가 외쳤다. \"당신은 이런 일을 나보다, 또 웬만한 사람들보다 더 잘 아시니까요.\" \"내가 뭐 좀 안다 싶긴 하지.\" 그가 말했다. \"정말이지.\" 아내가 대답했다. \"그 가엾고 조그만 분이 의자에 앉을 때 어찌나 애처로워 보이던지, 맹세코 그분이 가난한 사람이기라도 한 양 측은한 마음을 누를 수가 없더라고요. 한데 어쩌실 거예요, 여보? 만약 그분이 반란군이라면, 당신은 그분을 관아에 넘겨 일러바칠 작정이시겠죠. 글쎄, 누구든 간에 그분은 천성이 곱고 마음씨 좋은 분이라, 그분이 교수형을 당했다거나 목이 잘렸다는 소리를 들으면 나는 울음을 참기 어려울 거예요.\" \"쳇!\" 남편이 대답했다. \"한데 어찌해야 하느냐 하는 문제는 그리 쉽게 정할 일이 아니야. 그분이 떠나기 전에 전투 소식이 들려오면 좋으련만. 그 기사 나리 쪽이 이기기라도 하면, 저분이 우리한테 궁정의 연줄을 마련해 주어, 굳이 일러바치지 않고도 우리 살림을 일으켜 줄지도 모르거든.\" \"원, 그건 옳은 말씀이에요.\" 아내가 대꾸했다. \"그리고 저분이 그럴 힘을 갖게 되시길 진심으로 바라요. 정말이지 그분은 곱고 착한 부인이라, 그분에게 무슨 해라도 닥치는 건 나로선 끔찍이도 싫거든요.\" \"쳇!\" 여관 주인이 외쳤다. \"여편네들이란 늘 마음이 그리 무르다니까. 원, 당신 설마 반란군을 숨겨 줄 작정은 아니겠지?\" \"아뇨, 그럴 리가요.\" 아내가 대답했다. \"그리고 일러바치는 일이라면, 어찌 되든 누구도 우릴 탓할 수 없을 거예요. 우리 처지라면 누구라도 할 만한 일이니까요.\"\n\n이렇듯 우리의 약삭빠른 여관 주인이—보다시피 이웃들 사이에서 큰 지혜로 이름을 떨친 것이 괜한 일은 아니었으니—이 문제를 홀로 곰곰이 따지는 동안(그는 아내의 의견에는 별 귀를 기울이지 않았으므로), 반란군이 공작을 따돌리고 런던 쪽으로 하루 거리를 앞질러 진군했다는 소식이 당도했다. 그리고 곧이어 이름난 자코바이트 지주 한 사람이 들이닥쳤는데, 그는 얼굴에 큰 기쁨을 띤 채 여관 주인의 손을 부여잡으며 말했다. \"다 우리 차지가 됐네, 이 사람아. 정직한 프랑스 사람 만 명이 서퍽에 상륙했다네. 옛 잉글랜드 만세! 프랑스 사람 만 명일세, 이 용감한 친구야! 나는 곧장 술통을 따러 가는 길일세.\"\n\n이 소식이 그 현명한 양반의 판단을 굳혀 놓았으니, 그는 젊은 아가씨가 일어나거든 그녀의 환심을 사기로 마음먹었다. 이제 그는(스스로 말하기를) 그녀가 다름 아닌 제니 캐머런 마님 바로 그 사람임을 알아냈다고 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n아주 짧은 장이지만, 그럼에도 그 안에는 해와 달과 별, 그리고 천사가 들어 있다.\n\n해는(한 해의 이맘때면 무척 규칙적인 시간을 지키는 법이다) 이미 한참 전에 잠자리에 들었을 무렵, 소피아는 잠으로 한결 개운해진 몸을 일으켰다. 그토록 짧은 잠이었으나, 그렇게라도 잠들 수 있었던 것은 오로지 극심한 피로 덕분이었다. 그녀는 어퍼튼을 떠날 때 자신이 더없이 편안하다고 하녀에게, 어쩌면 스스로에게도 말했지만, 사실 그녀의 마음은 그 병에 조금쯤 시달리고 있었던 것이다. 열병에 따라붙는 온갖 안절부절못하는 증세를 동반하는 그 병, 어쩌면 의사들이(그들이 무언가를 뜻하는 것이라면) 정신의 열병이라 부르는 바로 그 병 말이다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인도 같은 시각에 잠자리에서 일어났고, 하녀를 불러 곧장 옷을 차려입었다. 그녀는 정말이지 무척 어여쁜 여인이어서, 소피아만 곁에 없었더라면 아름답다는 소리를 들었을 만했다. 그러나 어너 부인이 제 발로 시중을 들러 와서는(소피아가 그녀를 깨우지 못하게 한 까닭이었다) 우리의 여주인공을 단장해 놓자, 새벽별 노릇을 하며 더 큰 광채에 앞서 떠올랐던 피츠패트릭 부인의 매력은 그 별의 운명을 그대로 따랐다. 더 큰 광채가 빛을 발하는 순간 완전히 가려져 버린 것이다.\n\n아마 소피아가 이 순간보다 더 아름다워 보인 적은 없었으리라. 그러니 여관의 하녀가 늘어놓은 과장을 두고 우리가 나무랄 일은 아니다. 그녀는 불을 지피고 아래층으로 내려와서는, 만일 이 땅에 천사라는 것이 있다면 바로 지금 위층에 있노라고, 맹세까지 곁들여 단언했던 것이다.\n\n소피아는 사촌에게 런던으로 갈 작정임을 알린 터였고, 피츠패트릭 부인은 함께 가기로 했다. 남편이 어퍼튼에 나타나는 바람에 배스로, 또는 웨스턴 고모에게로 가려던 그녀의 계획이 무산되었기 때문이다. 그리하여 두 사람은 차를 다 마시기가 무섭게, 소피아가 곧 길을 나서자고 제안했다. 마침 달이 무척 밝게 빛나고 있었고, 서리 따위는 두렵지 않았다. 밤길을 가는 일에 대해 다른 젊은 여인들이라면 느꼈을 그런 불안도 그녀에게는 없었다. 앞서 일러두었듯이 그녀에게는 타고난 용기가 얼마간 있었던 데다, 지금 그녀의 심정이 거의 절망에 가까웠던 터라 그 용기가 한층 북돋워졌기 때문이다. 게다가 그녀는 이미 두 차례나 달빛에 의지해 무사히 길을 다녀 본 적이 있었으므로, 세 번째로 그것에 몸을 맡기는 데 더욱 대담해질 수 있었다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인의 기질은 좀 더 겁이 많은 편이었다. 비록 더 큰 두려움이 작은 두려움을 눌렀고, 남편이 나타난 탓에 그토록 때 아닌 시각에 어퍼튼을 떠나기는 했지만, 이제 남편의 추격에서 안전하다 여겨지는 곳에 다다르자, 정체를 알 수 없는 그 작은 두려움들이 어찌나 거세게 일었던지 그녀는 사촌에게 이튿날 아침까지 머물러, 밤길의 위험에 몸을 내맡기지 말자고 간곡히 청했다.\n\n소피아는 어지간하면 양보하는 성품이라, 웃음으로도 이치로도 사촌을 그 불안에서 끌어내지 못하자 결국 그 뜻에 따르고 말았다. 사실, 아버지가 어퍼튼에 와 있다는 사실을 알았더라면 그녀를 설득하기가 더 어려웠을지도 모른다. 존스에 대해서라면, 송구하게도 그녀는 그에게 따라잡힐 생각에 그리 큰 두려움이 없었으니 말이다. 아니, 사실대로 고백하자면, 그녀는 그것을 두려워하기보다 오히려 바라고 있었던 듯하다. 비록 이 바람을 독자에게 솔직히 감출 수도 있었으련만 그러지 않은 까닭은, 그것이 이성으로는 좀처럼 헤아릴 수 없는, 영혼의 은밀하고 절로 솟아나는 감정 가운데 하나였기 때문이다.\n\n우리의 젊은 두 여인이 그날 저녁 내내 여관에 머물기로 정하자, 안주인이 시중을 들러 와서 두 아씨께서 무엇을 드시고 싶으신지 알고자 했다. 소피아의 목소리며, 몸가짐이며, 상냥한 태도에는 어찌나 매력이 넘쳤던지, 안주인은 더없이 흠뻑 매료되고 말았다. 그 마음씨 고운 여인은 자신이 시중든 이가 제니 캐머런이라고 단정 짓고는, 추정컨대 그의 정부라는 이 여인에게서 받은 더없는 다정함과 상냥함에 감격하여, 순식간에 골수 자코바이트가 되어 젊은 왕위 요구자의 대의가 잘되기를 진심으로 빌었다.\n\n이제 두 사촌은 서로에게, 양쪽 모두 어떤 기이한 사연으로 이토록 야릇하고 뜻밖의 만남에 이르게 되었는지 알고 싶어하는 호기심을 털어놓기 시작했다. 마침내 피츠패트릭 부인은 소피아에게서 자기 차례에도 마찬가지로 이야기를 들려주겠다는 약속을 받아 낸 뒤, 독자가 그녀의 내력을 알고자 한다면 이어지는 장에서 읽을 수 있는 이야기를 들려주기 시작했다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인은 잠시 침묵하다가 깊은 한숨을 한 번 내쉬고는 이렇게 입을 열었다.\n\n“불행한 사람일수록, 살아오면서 가장 즐거웠던 시절을 떠올릴 때 남모를 아픔을 느끼는 법인가 봐. 지나간 기쁨을 되새기는 일은 우리에게 일종의 애틋한 슬픔을 안기지. 마치 떠나보낸 벗을 그리워할 때 느끼는 그런 슬픔 말이야. 그리고 그 두 가지의 기억이 우리 마음을 떠나지 않고 맴돈다고 해도 좋을 거야.\n\n“그래서 나는, 우리 둘이 웨스턴 고모의 보살핌을 받으며 함께 보냈던 그 시절(내 인생에서 단연 가장 행복했던 날들)을 슬픔 없이는 결코 떠올리지 못해. 아아! 어째서 새침데기 아가씨와 덤벙이 아가씨는 이제 더 이상 없는 걸까? 우리가 서로를 다른 이름이 아니라 바로 그 이름으로 불렀던 때를 너도 분명 기억하고 있겠지. 사실 너는 그 뒤쪽 별명을 붙일 만한 이유가 너무도 충분했어. 내가 그 별명을 얼마나 받을 만했는지는 그 후로 몸소 겪어 알게 되었으니까. 소피아, 너는 무엇에서든 늘 나보다 나았어. 그리고 운명에서도 네가 나보다 나으리라고 나는 진심으로 바라. 내가 무도회에 못 가게 되었다고 한탄했을 때, 그때 너는 분명 열네 살도 채 안 되었을 텐데, 네가 내게 들려준 그 현명하고도 어른스러운 충고를 나는 결코 잊지 못할 거야.----오 나의 소피, 그런 실망쯤을 불행이라 여길 수 있던 그때 내 처지는 얼마나 복된 것이었을까. 그리고 정말이지 그게 그때까지 내가 겪은 가장 큰 불행이었으니!”\n\n“그래도 말이야, 사랑하는 해리엇,” 소피아가 대답했다. “그때 너한테는 그게 정말 심각한 문제였잖아. 그러니 이렇게 생각하며 마음을 달래봐. 네가 지금 한탄하는 그 무엇도, 훗날에는 지금 무도회를 떠올리는 만큼이나 사소하고 하찮게 여겨질지 모른다고 말이야.”\n\n“아아, 나의 소피아,” 다른 부인이 대답했다. “지금 내 처지를 두고서는 너도 그렇게 생각하지 못할 거야. 내 불행이 너에게서 숱한 한숨을, 아니, 숱한 눈물을 자아내지 않는다면, 그 다정한 마음이 어지간히도 변한 게 분명할 테니까. 이런 걸 알면서도, 내가 너를 그토록 아프게 할 거라고 확신하는 이야기를 풀어놓으려 한다는 게, 어쩌면 나를 망설이게 만들어야 마땅하겠지.” 여기서 피츠패트릭 부인은 말을 멈추었다가, 소피아가 거듭 간청하자 다시 이렇게 이어갔다.\n\n“너도 내 결혼에 대해 이런저런 이야기를 많이 들었을 테지만, 어쩌면 사실이 잘못 전해졌을 수도 있으니, 나는 지금 남편과 처음 알게 된 그 불운한 시작부터 짚어 나갈게. 그건 바스에서의 일이었어. 네가 고모 곁을 떠나 아버지 댁으로 돌아가고 얼마 지나지 않아서였지.\n\n“그 무렵 바스에 모여든 멋쟁이 젊은이들 가운데 피츠패트릭 씨도 있었어. 그는 잘생기고, 거동이 거리낌 없고, 더없이 정중했으며, 옷차림에서는 대부분의 다른 사람들을 능가했지. 한마디로, 얘, 만일 네가 운 나쁘게 지금의 그를 본다면, 나는 그가 지금 모습의 정반대였다고 말하는 것밖에는 달리 묘사할 길이 없을 거야. 그는 너무 오래 시골에 처박혀 지내는 바람에 완전히 거친 아일랜드 촌사람이 되고 말았거든. 그건 그렇고 내 이야기를 계속하자면, 그때 그가 지닌 자질이 어찌나 그를 돋보이게 했던지, 당시 지체 높은 사람들은 나머지 무리와 따로 어울리며 자기네 모임에서 그들을 죄다 배제했는데도, 피츠패트릭 씨만은 끼어들 방도를 찾아냈어. 어쩌면 그를 피하기가 쉽지 않았던 건지도 몰라. 그는 초대 같은 건 거의, 아니 전혀 필요로 하지 않았으니까. 게다가 잘생기고 품위 있는 덕에 부인들의 환심을 사는 일이 그리 어렵지 않았고, 또 칼을 자주 빼 든 터라 남자들도 대놓고 그를 모욕하기는 꺼렸어. 그런 이유라도 없었다면, 나는 그가 동성들 손에 금방 쫓겨났으리라 믿어. 그가 영국 신사들보다 우대받을 정당한 권리가 있던 것도 분명 아니었고, 그들 역시 그에게 별다른 호의를 베풀 마음은 없어 보였으니까. 그들은 모두 그가 없는 자리에서 그를 헐뜯었는데, 아마 시샘에서 나온 일이었을 거야. 여자들에게는 그가 잘 받아들여졌고, 또 유난히 떠받들어졌으니까.\n\n“우리 고모는 본인이 지체 높은 사람은 아니었지만, 줄곧 궁정 주변에서 지내온 터라 그 무리에 이름을 올리고 있었어. 어떤 수단으로든 일단 상류 사회에 발을 들여놓고 나면, 거기 있다는 사실 자체가 충분한 자격이 되는 법이거든. 너도 어렸지만 우리 고모를 보며 이 점만은 알아챌 수밖에 없었을 거야. 고모는 사람들이 이 자격을 더 갖췄느냐 덜 갖췄느냐에 딱 맞춰서, 누구에게는 스스럼없이 대하고 누구에게는 데면데면하게 굴었으니까.\n\n“그리고 내 생각엔, 피츠패트릭 씨를 고모의 총애로 이끈 것도 주로 바로 이 자격이었던 것 같아. 거기서 그가 어찌나 성공했던지, 그는 늘 고모의 사적인 모임에 빠지지 않는 일원이 되었지. 그도 그런 우대를 되갚는 데 게으르지 않아서, 곧 고모를 대하는 태도가 어찌나 각별해졌던지, 험담패들이 먼저 그걸 입에 올리기 시작했고, 좀 더 호의적인 사람들은 둘을 짝지어 주려 했어. 나로 말하자면, 솔직히 그의 속셈이 흔히 하는 말로 ‘점잖은’ 것이라는 데에는 조금도 의심이 없었어. 다시 말해 결혼을 빌미로 한 부인의 재산을 가로채려는 속셈 말이야. 내가 보기에 고모는 젊지도 곱지도 않아서 그리 못된 욕심을 끌 만한 분은 아니었지만, 혼인이 주는 매력만큼은 차고 넘치게 지니고 있었거든.\n\n“그가 우리가 처음 알게 된 순간부터 내게 보여 준 유별난 존중 때문에 나는 이 생각을 더욱 굳히게 되었어. 나는 그걸, 그 혼사에 대해 내 이해관계상 품을 법한 거부감을 어떻게든 누그러뜨리려는 시도라고 받아들였지. 그리고 어느 정도는 정말 그런 효과가 있었는지도 몰라. 나는 내 재산에 흡족했고, 누구보다도 이해타산에 매이지 않는 사람이었으니, 나를 대하는 태도가 무척이나 마음에 드는 남자를 거세게 적대할 수는 없었어. 게다가 그런 존중을 받는 대상이 오직 나뿐이었기에 더욱 그랬지. 그는 같은 시간에 지체 높은 다른 여인들에게는 조금의 존중도 없이 굴었으니까.\n\n“이게 내게는 흐뭇한 일이었는데, 그는 곧 그것을 또 다른 태도로 바꾸었어. 어쩌면 그게 더 흐뭇했는지도 몰라. 이제 그는 한껏 부드럽고 다정한 태도를 짓고는, 넘치도록 애를 태우며 한숨을 내쉬었지. 물론 이따금은, 그게 꾸민 것인지 타고난 것인지 나는 단정하지 않겠지만, 평소처럼 흥겨움과 명랑함을 마음껏 풀어놓기도 했어. 하지만 그건 늘 여럿이 모인 자리에서, 그것도 다른 여자들을 상대로였지. 시골풍 춤을 출 때조차도, 내 짝이 아닐 때면 그는 진지해졌고, 내게 다가오는 순간엔 상상할 수 있는 가장 부드러운 표정을 지었어. 정말이지 그는 매사에 나를 향해 어찌나 각별했던지, 그걸 알아채지 못했다면 내가 눈먼 사람이었을 거야. 그리고, 그리고, 그리고----” “그리고 너는 그게 더더욱 흐뭇했던 거지, 사랑하는 해리엇.” 소피아가 외쳤다. “부끄러워할 것 없어.” 그녀는 한숨을 내쉬며 덧붙였다. “다정함에는 거역할 수 없는 매력이 있는데, 너무 많은 남자들이 그걸 꾸며낼 줄 알거든.” “맞아.” 사촌이 대답했다. “다른 모든 면에서는 상식조차 없는 남자들이, 사랑을 부리는 기술에서는 어찌나 능구렁이들인지. 나도 그런 본보기를 몰랐다면 좋았을 텐데.--아무튼, 이제 험담은 전에 고모를 두고 그랬던 것만큼이나 나를 두고도 분주해졌어. 몇몇 점잖은 부인네들은 피츠패트릭 씨가 우리 둘 모두와 정을 통한다고 서슴없이 단언하기까지 했지.\n\n“그런데 놀라울지 모르겠지만, 우리 고모만은 우리 둘의 행동에서 충분히 드러났을 그걸 전혀 보지 못했고, 조금도 의심하는 기색이 없었어. 정말이지 사랑이란 늙은 여인의 눈을 완전히 멀게 만드는 모양이야. 사실 그들은 자기에게 바쳐지는 구애를 어찌나 게걸스럽게 삼키는지, 마치 사나운 대식가처럼, 같은 식탁에서 다른 이들 사이에 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 살필 겨를이 없거든. 나는 내 경우 말고도 여러 경우에서 이걸 보아 왔는데, 우리 고모에게서 그게 어찌나 확실히 들어맞던지, 고모가 약수터에서 돌아왔을 때 우리가 함께 있는 걸 종종 보고도, 고모의 부재를 못 견디겠다는 척하는 그의 능청맞은 말 한마디면 모든 의심이 깔끔히 잠재워졌어. 한 가지 술책이 고모에게는 기막히게 잘 통했지. 그건 나를 어린아이처럼 다루며, 고모 앞에서는 ‘예쁜 아가씨’ 말고는 결코 다른 이름으로 부르지 않는 거였어. 사실 이건 너의 변변찮은 친구인 나에게는 오히려 손해를 끼쳤지만, 나는 곧 그 속을 꿰뚫어 보았어. 특히 고모가 없을 때면 그가, 앞서 말했듯이 다른 식으로 나를 대했으니까. 하지만 그 속셈을 간파한 행동에 내가 크게 언짢지는 않았더라도, 나는 그것 때문에 아주 호되게 시달려야 했어. 고모가 정말로 나를, 자기 연인(그렇게 여겼던 사람)이 부르는 그대로의 사람으로 여기고는, 모든 면에서 나를 완전히 갓난아이처럼 대했으니까. 솔직히 말해, 고모가 나더러 다시 걸음마 끈을 차고 다니라고 우기지 않은 게 신기할 지경이야.\n\n“마침내 내 연인은(그는 정말 그랬으니까), 내가 진작부터 알고 있던 비밀을 더없이 엄숙한 태도로 털어놓는 게 좋겠다고 여겼어. 그는 이제 고모에게 가장한 척했던 그 사랑을 모조리 내 앞으로 돌렸지. 고모가 자기에게 베푼 호의를 무척이나 애처로운 말투로 한탄했고, 고모와의 대화를 견뎌낸 그 지루한 시간들을 대단한 공로인 양 내세웠어.--내가 너에게 무슨 말을 해야 할까, 사랑하는 소피아?--그래, 솔직히 고백할게. 나는 그 남자가 마음에 들었어. 내가 거둔 정복이 흐뭇했어. 고모의 연적이 된다는 게 즐거웠고, 그토록 많은 다른 여자들의 연적이 된다는 게 황홀했어. 한마디로, 나는 처음 그가 고백하던 바로 그 순간조차도 마땅히 해야 할 처신을 하지 못했던 것 같아.--우리가 헤어지기 전에 그에게 거의 분명한 호응을 내비치지 않았기를 바랄 뿐이야.\n\n“이제 바스는 나를 두고 떠들썩하게--거의 으르렁댔다고 해도 좋을 만큼--입방아를 찧기 시작했어. 여러 젊은 여자들이 내 곁을 일부러 멀리하는 척했는데, 그건 아마 무슨 진짜 의심에서라기보다는, 자기네가 아끼는 남자를 내가 너무 독차지하고 있던 그 무리에서 나를 쫓아내고 싶은 마음에서였을 거야. 그리고 여기서 내가 빼놓을 수 없는 게, 내게 베풀어 주려 한 내쉬 씨의 호의에 대한 고마움이야. 그분은 어느 날 나를 한쪽으로 데려가더니 충고를 해 주었는데, 내가 그걸 따랐더라면 나는 행복한 여자가 되었을 거야. ‘얘야,’ 그분이 말했어. ‘너와, 너에게 도무지 어울리지 않는 데다 결국 네 파멸이 되고 말 것 같은 그 작자 사이에 오가는 허물없음을 보자니 안타깝구나. 네 고약한 늙은 고모로 말하자면, 너와 내 예쁜 소피 웨스턴(분명히 말하지만 나는 그분의 말을 그대로 옮기는 거야)에게 해가 되지만 않는다면, 나는 그 작자가 고모에게 딸린 모든 걸 차지하는 걸 진심으로 기뻐했을 거다. 나는 결코 늙은 여자들에게는 충고하지 않아. 그들이 일단 악마에게 가기로 작정하면, 그를 떼어놓는 건 가능하지도 않거니와 그럴 만한 값어치도 없으니까. 하지만 순결과 젊음과 아름다움은 더 나은 운명을 누릴 자격이 있으니, 나는 그것들을 그 작자의 마수에서 구해 내고 싶구나. 그러니 사랑하는 얘야, 내 충고를 들어라. 다시는 그 작자가 너에게 각별히 굴도록 결코 내버려 두지 말거라.’ 그분은 그 밖에도 여러 말을 해 주셨는데, 이제는 다 잊어버렸어. 사실 그때도 나는 그 말들에 거의 귀를 기울이지 않았거든. 내 마음이 그분의 모든 말에 어깃장을 놓았으니까. 게다가 나는 지체 높은 여인들이 그분이 묘사한 그런 인물과 허물없이 지낼 만큼 자신을 낮추리라고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었어.\n\n“하지만 얘, 이렇게 사소한 사정들을 시시콜콜 늘어놓다가는 너를 지치게 만들 것 같아 걱정이야. 그러니 간추려서, 내가 결혼한 모습을 떠올려 봐. 남편과 함께 고모 발치에 엎드려 있는 내 모습을 떠올려 봐. 그리고 베들럼의 가장 미쳐 날뛰는 여인이 광란의 발작을 일으키는 모습을 떠올려 봐. 그러면 네 상상은 실제로 일어난 일 그대로만을 그려 줄 거야.\n\n“바로 그 이튿날 고모는 그곳을 떠났어. 피츠패트릭 씨나 나를 보지 않으려는 마음도 있었겠지만, 어쩌면 다른 그 누구도 마주치지 않으려는 마음이 그만큼 컸을 거야. 듣자 하니 고모는 그 뒤로 모든 걸 한사코 부인해 왔다지만, 그때만은 자신의 어긋난 기대에 적잖이 당황했던 거라고 나는 믿어. 그 후로 나는 고모에게 숱한 편지를 썼지만, 끝내 답장 한 통 받지 못했어. 솔직히 이게 좀 더 무겁게 마음을 짓누르는 건, 본의는 아니었다 해도 내 모든 고통의 빌미가 바로 고모 자신이었기 때문이야. 고모에게 구애한다는 명목이 아니었다면, 피츠패트릭 씨는 내 마음을 사로잡을 만한 기회를 결코 충분히 얻지 못했을 테니까. 다른 상황이었다면, 내 마음이 그런 사람에게 그리 쉬운 정복은 아니었으리라고 나는 여전히 자위하고 있어. 정말이지, 내가 내 판단에만 기댔더라면 그렇게 형편없이 잘못 고르지는 않았을 거야. 그런데 나는 온통 남들의 의견만 믿고서, 여자들에게 그토록 두루 잘 받아들여지는 걸 본 어느 남자의 됨됨이를 어리석게도 당연하게 여겨 버렸지. 얘, 도대체 어찌 된 일일까? 우리는 저 다른 성의 가장 지혜롭고 위대한 이들에 못지않은 분별을 지녔으면서도, 어째서 그토록 자주 가장 어리석은 작자들을 반려이자 총아로 골라 버리는 걸까? 어리석은 자들 때문에 신세를 망친 분별 있는 여자들이 그토록 많다는 걸 떠올리면, 나는 분노가 머리끝까지 치밀어 올라.” 여기서 그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추었지만, 소피아가 아무 대답이 없자 다음 장에서처럼 이야기를 이어갔다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인의 이야기가 이어지는 장.\n\n\"우리는 결혼식을 올린 뒤로 보름 남짓밖에 바스에 머물지 않았어요. 숙모와 화해할 가망은 조금도 없었고, 제 재산은 성년이 되기 전까지 한 푼도 손댈 수 없었는데, 그때까지는 아직 두 해도 더 남아 있었으니까요. 그래서 남편은 아일랜드로 떠나기로 마음을 굳혔어요. 저는 한사코 반대하며, 결혼 전에 그가 제게 한 약속, 곧 제 동의 없이는 결코 이 여행을 시키지 않겠다던 그 약속을 들어 따졌지요. 사실 저는 결코 동의할 마음이 없었어요. 누구도 그 결심을 두고 저를 탓하지는 않으리라 믿어요. 그렇지만 이 속내만큼은 남편에게 한 번도 말하지 않았고, 다만 한 달만 늦춰 달라고 청했을 뿐이에요. 하지만 그는 날짜를 못 박아 두었고, 그날을 고집스레 밀어붙였습니다.\n\n\"떠나기 전날 저녁, 우리가 양쪽 다 격렬하게 이 문제를 다투고 있을 때, 그는 갑자기 의자에서 벌떡 일어나더니 무도장에 다녀오겠다는 말만 남기고 저를 매정하게 두고 나가 버렸어요. 그가 집을 채 나서기도 전에, 저는 바닥에 종이 한 장이 떨어져 있는 것을 보았어요. 손수건과 함께 무심코 호주머니에서 빼낸 모양이었지요. 그 종이를 집어 들어 보니 편지였고, 저는 거리낌 없이 펼쳐 읽었어요. 어찌나 여러 번 읽었던지 거의 한 자 한 자 그대로 당신에게 옮길 수 있을 정도예요. 그 편지는 이러했어요.\n\n'브라이언 피츠패트릭 귀하.\n\n'선생님,\n'보내신 편지 받았습니다만, 이런 식으로 저를 대하시니 놀라울 따름입니다. 저는 무명 모직 외투 한 벌 값 외에는 선생님의 현금이라곤 구경도 못 했는데, 지금 외상이 150파운드를 넘었습니다. 생각해 보십시오, 선생님. 곧 이 부인이니 저 부인이니 하는 분과 결혼한다는 말로 저를 몇 번이나 따돌리셨는지요. 그러나 저는 희망이나 약속만으로는 살 수 없고, 제 모직 도매상도 그런 걸 대금으로 받아 주지 않습니다. 선생님은 그 숙모든 조카딸이든 둘 중 하나는 손에 넣은 거나 다름없다 하셨고, 또 진작에 숙모와 결혼할 수도 있었는데—그 과부 재산이 어마어마하다 하셨지요—다만 현금이 곧바로 들어오는 조카딸 쪽을 더 좋아하신다 하셨지요. 부디 선생님, 이번 한 번만은 어리석은 자의 충고를 들으시어, 먼저 잡히는 쪽과 어서 결혼하십시오. 제가 충고를 드리는 것을 용서하십시오. 제가 선생님을 진심으로 위하는 줄 아시잖습니까. 다음 우편으로 존 드러깃 상회 앞으로 14일짜리 어음을 발행할 터이니, 부디 인수해 주시리라 믿어 의심치 않으며, 이만 줄입니다.\n\n선생님의 비천한 종, 샘. 코스그레이브 올림.'\n\n\"이것이 한 자도 빠짐없는 그 편지였어요. 짐작해 보세요, 사랑하는 그대—이 편지가 저를 어떻게 뒤흔들어 놓았을지 짐작해 보세요. '현금이 곧바로 들어오는 조카딸 쪽을 더 좋아하신다'! 만약 이 낱말 하나하나가 단검이었다면, 저는 기꺼이 그것들을 그의 심장에 꽂아 넣었을 거예요. 하지만 그 순간 제가 얼마나 미친 듯이 굴었는지는 늘어놓지 않겠어요. 그가 집에 돌아오기 전에 저는 눈물을 거의 다 쏟아 버렸지만, 부어오른 눈에는 그 흔적이 충분히 남아 있었지요. 그는 시무룩하게 의자에 몸을 던졌고, 한참 동안 우리 둘 다 말이 없었어요. 마침내 그가 거만한 어조로 말했어요. '부인, 하인들이 짐을 다 꾸렸기를 바라오. 마차가 아침 여섯 시에는 준비될 테니.' 이 도발에 제 인내심은 완전히 무너졌고, 저는 이렇게 대답했어요. '아니에요, 선생님. 아직 꾸리지 못한 편지 한 장이 남아 있어요.' 그러고는 그것을 탁자 위에 내던지며, 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 신랄한 말로 그를 몰아붙이기 시작했어요.\n\n\"죄책감이었는지, 부끄러움이었는지, 아니면 신중함이었는지는 모르겠지만, 그는 누구보다 격정적인 사람인데도 그때만큼은 분노를 터뜨리지 않았어요. 오히려 더없이 부드러운 말로 저를 달래려 애썼지요. 그는 제가 가장 문제 삼은 그 구절은 자기가 쓴 것이 아니며, 그런 글을 쓴 적이 결코 없다고 맹세했어요. 결혼 이야기를 꺼낸 것, 그리고 저를 더 좋아한다고 한 것은 인정했지만, 돈에 쪼들린다는 이유로 그런 일을 입에 올린 적은 절대 없다고 거듭 맹세하며 부인했어요. 그 곤궁은 아일랜드의 영지를 너무 오래 돌보지 않은 데서 비롯된 것이라더군요. 그리고 차마 제게 털어놓을 수 없었던 바로 이 사정이, 그가 이 여행을 그토록 완강하게 고집한 유일한 이유였다고 했어요. 그러고 나서 그는 무척 다정한 말들을 여러 마디 건네더니, 더없이 애틋한 손길과 사랑의 격렬한 맹세로 끝을 맺었어요.\n\n\"그가 들어 따지지는 않았지만, 한 가지 정황이 그에게 유리하게 제 마음을 크게 움직였어요. 바로 재단사의 편지에 나온 '과부 재산'이라는 말이었지요. 제 숙모는 결혼한 적이 한 번도 없었고, 이 사실을 피츠패트릭 씨도 잘 알고 있었거든요. 그러니 저는 그 작자가 제멋대로, 혹은 풍문으로 들은 것을 적어 넣은 게 분명하다고 생각했고, 그렇다면 그 끔찍한 한 줄도 별다른 근거 없이 마음대로 써 갈긴 것일지 모른다고 스스로를 설득했어요. 이게 무슨 추론이었을까요, 그대? 저는 재판관이 아니라 변호인이 아니었을까요? 그런데 제가 어째서 이런 정황을 끌어와 제 용서를 변명하려 드는 걸까요? 한마디로, 설령 그가 그보다 스무 배쯤 더 큰 죄를 지었다 해도, 그가 보인 그 다정함과 애틋함의 절반만으로도 저는 그를 용서하고 말았을 거예요. 저는 더 이상 출발에 토를 달지 않았고, 우리는 이튿날 아침 길을 떠나 일주일 남짓 만에 피츠패트릭 씨의 영지에 닿았어요.\n\n\"여행 중에 있었던 일들은, 당신의 호기심을 핑계로 그냥 넘어가도 너그러이 봐 주세요. 그 길을 다시 더듬어 가는 일은 제게 몹시 불쾌할 테고, 저와 함께 그 길을 되짚는 일이 당신에게도 못지않게 괴로울 테니까요.\n\n\"그 영지는 오래된 저택이에요. 제가 당신이 그토록 자주 보았던 그 유쾌한 기분에 잠겨 있었다면, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 재미있게 그곳을 그려 보일 수도 있었겠지요. 그곳은 한때 신사가 살았던 곳처럼 보였어요. 방은 넉넉했고, 가구 때문에 좁아질 일도 없었어요. 정말이지 가구라곤 거의 없었으니까요. 그 건물만큼이나 늙어 보이는, 《고아》에서 셔몬트가 언급한 그 노파를 쏙 빼닮은 한 노파가 문간에서 우리를 맞으며, 사람의 소리라고 하기 어렵고 제게는 알아들을 수도 없는 울부짖음으로 제 주인의 귀가를 환영했어요. 한마디로 그 광경 전체가 어찌나 음울하고 우울하던지, 제 기분은 더없이 깊은 낙담 속으로 가라앉았어요. 남편은 그것을 알아채고도 달래기는커녕, 두세 마디 심술궂은 말로 더 부추겼어요. '부인,' 그가 말했어요. '보다시피 잉글랜드 말고 다른 곳에도 훌륭한 집은 있는 법이오. 하지만 부인은 차라리 바스의 지저분한 셋방에 있는 편이 낫겠지.'\\n\\n* 《고아》: 토머스 오트웨이의 비극 〈The Orphan〉을 가리킨다.\n\n\"어떤 처지에 있든, 받쳐 주고 위로해 줄 명랑하고 너그러운 동반자를 둔 여인은 행복한 사람이에요, 그대! 하지만 제가 어째서 제 비참함을 더 키우려고 행복한 처지를 떠올리는 걸까요? 제 동반자는 고독의 음울함을 걷어 주기는커녕, 어디서든 어떤 형편에서든 그와 함께라면 비참할 수밖에 없다는 것을 곧 제게 깨우쳐 주었어요. 한마디로 그는 무뚝뚝하고 심술궂은 사람이었어요. 당신은 어쩌면 그런 부류를 한 번도 본 적이 없을 거예요. 사실 여자는 아버지든, 오빠든, 남편이든, 그런 사람을 통해서만 그 본보기를 보게 되니까요. 게다가 당신에게는 아버지가 계시지만, 그분은 그런 부류가 아니지요. 이 무뚝뚝한 사람은 한때 제게는 정반대로 보였고, 다른 모든 이에게는 지금도 여전히 그렇게 보이지요. 맙소사! 어떻게 한 사람이 밖에서, 사람들 사이에서는 한결같이 거짓 모습을 꾸미면서, 불쾌한 진실은 오로지 집 안에서만 드러내는 것으로 만족할 수 있을까요? 바로 여기서, 그대, 그들은 세상에서 성미를 억누르느라 받은 불편한 속박을 스스로 보상받는 거예요. 제가 보니, 남편이 사람들 사이에서 더 유쾌하고 밝고 너그럽게 굴수록, 다음번 둘만의 자리에서는 어김없이 더 시무룩하고 까칠해지더군요. 그의 야만스러움을 어떻게 다 그려 낼까요? 그는 제 애정에 차갑고 무감각했어요. 당신, 제 소피아가, 또 다른 이들이 그토록 사랑스럽다 일컬어 주던 제 소소하고 익살스러운 모습들을, 그는 경멸로 대했어요. 제가 더없이 진지한 순간에는 노래를 흥얼대고 휘파람을 불었으며, 제가 완전히 의기소침하고 비참해 있을 때면 화를 내며 저를 모욕했어요. 제 명랑함을 결코 기뻐하지 않았고 그것을 그에게 만족하는 표시로 여기지도 않았으면서, 제 가라앉은 기분만큼은 늘 그의 비위를 거슬렀고, 그것을 (그의 말마따나) 아일랜드 사람과 결혼한 일을 후회하는 탓으로 돌렸으니까요.\n\n\"쉽게 짐작하겠지요, 사랑하는 그레이베어스—아, 미안해요, 정말 깜빡했네요—세상의 기준으로 경솔한 결혼을 한 여자라 해도, 다시 말해 그 여자가 돈벌이만 좇는 노골적인 매춘부가 아닌 이상, 그 남자에게 얼마간의 끌림과 애정은 반드시 품기 마련이라는 것을요. 그리고 그 애정이 줄어들 수도 있다는 것 또한 쉽게 믿어지겠지요. 아니, 분명히 말해 두는데, 경멸은 그것을 송두리째 뿌리 뽑아 버려요. 제가 이제 남편에게 품기 시작한 것이 바로 그 경멸이었어요. 저는 그가—이 표현을 쓸 수밖에 없네요—완전한 멍청이라는 것을 비로소 알아차렸으니까요. 어쩌면 당신은 제가 진작에 이를 알아채지 못한 것을 의아해할지도 몰라요. 하지만 여자란 자기가 좋아하는 사람의 어리석음에 대해서는 천 가지 변명을 스스로 지어내는 법이에요. 게다가 한마디 하자면, 쾌활함과 좋은 교양이라는 가면 너머로 바보를 알아보려면 더없이 날카로운 눈이 필요하답니다.\n\n\"제가 일단 남편을 경멸하게 되자—고백하건대 곧 그렇게 되었지요—결국 그와 함께 있는 것을 싫어하게 되었으리라는 것도 쉽게 짐작할 거예요. 그런데 실은 그와 함께 있어야 하는 일이 거의 없어 다행이었어요. 우리 집은 이제 더없이 우아하게 꾸며졌고, 지하 저장고에는 술이 그득했으며, 개와 말도 넘치도록 갖추어졌으니까요. 그래서 제 신랑은 이웃들을 후하게 대접했고, 이웃들도 기꺼이 그를 찾아왔어요. 사냥과 술자리가 그의 시간을 어찌나 많이 잡아먹던지, 그의 대화—다시 말해 그의 고약한 성미—는 그 작은 일부만이 제 몫으로 떨어졌지요.\n\n\"다른 불쾌한 동무들도 그처럼 쉽게 피할 수 있었다면 제게 얼마나 좋았을까요. 하지만, 아아! 저는 끊임없이 저를 괴롭히는 어떤 것들에 갇혀 있었어요. 게다가 거기서 벗어날 가망이 보이지 않았기에 더욱 그러했지요. 그 동무들이란 바로 저를 들볶는 제 자신의 생각들이었어요. 그것들은 밤낮으로 저를 괴롭히며, 말하자면 유령처럼 저를 따라다녔어요. 그런 처지에서 저는 한 장면을 통과했는데, 그 끔찍함은 그림으로도 그려 낼 수 없고 상상으로도 떠올릴 수 없을 거예요. 생각해 보세요, 그대—할 수 있다면 제가 무엇을 견뎌야 했을지 머릿속에 그려 보세요. 저는 제가 멸시하고 미워하고 혐오하는 남자의 아이를 낳는 어미가 되었어요. 저는 산고의 온갖 고통과 비참함을 다 겪었지요. (그런 처지에서의 산고는, 사랑하는 남자를 위해 겪는 가장 모진 진통보다도 열 배는 더 괴로운 거예요.) 그것도 황량한 사막에서, 아니 오히려 흥청대고 떠들썩한 난장판 한가운데서, 벗 하나 없이, 동무 하나 없이, 또 그 시기에 우리 여자들의 고통을 흔히 덜어 주고 때로는 그 이상으로 메워 주기도 하는 그 어떤 다정한 정경 하나 없이 말이에요.\"\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n6장.\n여관 주인의 오해로 소피아가 끔찍한 공포에 빠지는 이야기.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인이 한창 이야기를 이어 가던 참에, 저녁 식사가 들어오면서 말이 끊겼다. 소피아로서는 무척 안타까운 일이었다. 벗의 불행이 그녀의 마음을 졸이게 한 터라, 피츠패트릭 부인의 이야기로나 채워질 식욕 말고는 어떤 입맛도 남아 있지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n이윽고 여관 주인이 접시 하나를 옆구리에 낀 채 나타났다. 그 얼굴과 말투에는, 마치 두 숙녀가 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차를 타고 도착하기라도 한 듯한 정중함이 그대로 깃들어 있었다.\n\n결혼한 쪽의 부인은 사촌보다 자신의 불행에 덜 흔들리는 듯 보였다. 앞쪽은 아주 맛있게 먹었으나, 뒤쪽은 한 입조차 좀처럼 삼키지 못했다. 소피아 역시 다른 부인보다 더 짙은 근심과 슬픔을 얼굴에 드러내고 있었다. 벗에게서 이런 기색을 알아챈 피츠패트릭 부인이 그녀에게 마음을 다잡으라며 말했다. \"어쩌면 모든 일이 너나 나의 예상보다 더 잘 끝날지도 몰라.\"\n\n이제 여관 주인은 입을 열 기회가 왔다고 여겼고, 그 기회를 놓치지 않기로 마음먹었다. \"마님,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"마님께서 드시질 못하니 안타깝습니다. 이렇게 오래 굶으셨으니 분명 시장하실 텐데요. 부디 무슨 일로 마음 졸이지 않으시길 바랍니다. 저기 저 마님 말씀대로, 모든 일이 누구의 예상보다 더 잘 풀릴 수도 있으니까요. 방금 여기 다녀가신 어떤 신사분이 아주 반가운 소식을 전해 주셨습죠. 어쩌면 남을 따돌리고 빠져나간 어떤 분들은 따라잡히기 전에 런던에 닿을 수도 있겠고요. 그렇게만 된다면, 그분들을 기꺼이 맞아 줄 사람들을 분명히 만나게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n위험을 두려워하는 사람은 누구나 보고 듣는 모든 것을 그 두려움의 대상으로 바꿔 버린다. 그래서 소피아는 앞선 말로 미루어, 자신이 들통났으며 아버지가 뒤쫓고 있다고 곧바로 단정해 버렸다. 그녀는 극심한 공포에 사로잡혀 잠시 말할 힘조차 잃었다. 겨우 정신을 차리자마자, 그녀는 주인에게 하인들을 방에서 내보내 달라고 청한 뒤, 그를 향해 말했다. \"주인장, 보아하니 우리가 누구인지 아시는군요. 하지만 간청합니다—아니, 저는 확신합니다. 주인장께 조금이라도 동정심이나 선한 마음이 있다면, 우리를 배신하지 않으시리라는 걸요.\"\n\n\"제가 마님을 배신한다고요!\" 주인이 말했다. \"아니지요(그러고는 아주 진심 어린 맹세를 몇 마디 늘어놓았다). 차라리 제 몸이 만 조각으로 잘리는 편이 낫습니다. 저는 배신이라면 질색입니다. 제가요! 저는 평생 누구도 배신해 본 적이 없는데, 하물며 마님처럼 어여쁜 분으로 그 시작을 끊을 리가 있겠습니까. 그랬다간 온 세상이 저를 크게 나무랄 겁니다. 머지않아 마님께서 저에게 보답해 주실 수 있는 처지이실 테니까요. 제 아내가 증인입니다. 마님께서 이 집에 들어서신 그 순간 저는 마님을 알아보았습니다. 말에서 내려 드리기도 전에 이분이 바로 그 귀하신 분이라고 말했습죠. 그리고 마님을 모시다 얻은 이 멍은 무덤까지 안고 갈 겁니다. 하지만 마님을 구해 드릴 수만 있었다면 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까? 분명 오늘 아침 어떤 사람들은 상금 받을 궁리를 했겠지만, 제 머릿속엔 그런 생각이 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없습니다. 마님을 배신하고 상금을 받느니 차라리 굶어 죽고 말겠습니다.\"\n\n\"약속하지요, 주인장,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"언젠가 제게 보답할 힘이 생긴다면, 당신의 그 너그러움이 헛되지 않게 하겠어요.\"\n\n\"아이고, 마님!\" 여관 주인이 대답했다. \"마님 손안에 있다니요! 부디 하늘이 그 일을 마님의 뜻에 그만큼 담아 주시기를! 다만 저는 마님께서 여관 주인 따위 가난한 사람을 잊으실까 봐 걱정될 뿐입니다. 하오나 마님께서 잊지 않으신다면, 제가 어떤 상금을 마다했는지—마다했다! 그러니까, 마다했을 거라는 말씀이고, 분명 마다했다고 부를 만하지요. 마음만 먹었으면 틀림없이 받을 수 있었으니까요. 그리고 마님께선 다른 어떤 집에 묵으셨을 수도 있고요—허나 저로 말하자면, 그 반가운 소식을 듣기도 전부터, 제가 마님을 배신할 생각을 단 한 번이라도 품었으리라 여기시며 저를 그토록 오해하시는 일만은 세상 무엇과도 바꾸고 싶지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 소식 말인가요?\" 소피아가 다소 다급하게 물었다.\n\n\"그럼 마님께선 아직 못 들으셨군요?\" 여관 주인이 외쳤다. \"하긴 그럴 만도 하지요. 저도 불과 몇 분 전에야 들었으니까요. 게다가 제가 그 소식을 영영 못 들었다 한들, 제가 마님을 배신했다면 당장 이 순간 악마가 저를 채 가도 좋습니다! 아니, 제가 그랬다면, 저는—\" 여기서 그는 끔찍한 저주를 몇 마디 덧붙였고, 마침내 소피아가 그를 가로막으며 그 소식이 도대체 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었다. 그가 막 대답하려는데, 어너 부인이 새파랗게 질려 숨을 헐떡이며 방으로 뛰어 들어와 소리쳤다. \"마님, 우리 모두 끝장났어요, 다 망했어요, 그들이 왔어요, 그들이 왔다고요!\" 이 말에 소피아의 피는 거의 얼어붙을 지경이었다. 그러나 피츠패트릭 부인은 어너에게 누가 왔느냐고 물었다. \"누구라뇨?\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"아니, 프랑스군이요, 수십만이 상륙했답니다. 우리는 다 살해당하고 욕을 당할 거예요.\"\n\n잘 지어진 어느 도시에 스무 실링짜리 오두막 한 채를 가진 구두쇠가 멀리서 불이 났다는 소식에 놀라, 자기 손해에 새파래져 부들부들 떨다가도, 막상 화려한 궁전들만 타고 자기 오두막은 멀쩡히 남아 있음을 알게 되면 단숨에 제정신을 되찾고 제 행운에 미소 짓는 것처럼. 아니면(앞의 비유가 어딘가 마음에 들지 않으니), 사랑하는 아들이 물에 빠졌다는 두려움에 휩싸여 정신을 잃고 공포로 거의 죽을 지경이던 자애로운 어머니가, 어린 도련님은 무사하고 다만 빅토리호가 용맹한 천이백 명과 함께 바닷속으로 가라앉았을 뿐이라는 말을 듣자 다시 목숨과 정신이 돌아오고, 모성의 애정은 온갖 두려움에서 풀려난 갑작스러운 안도를 만끽하며, 다른 때 같았으면 그 끔찍한 참사에 깊이 마음 아파했을 그 보편적 인정은 그녀의 마음속에 깊이 잠들어 버리는 것처럼. 그렇게 소피아도—제 나라의 보편적 재앙을 그 누구보다 가슴 아프게 느낄 수 있는 사람이었건만—아버지에게 따라잡힐지 모른다는 그 공포에서 풀려난 안도가 어찌나 즉각적인 만족을 주었던지, 프랑스군이 상륙했다는 소식은 그녀에게 거의 아무런 인상도 남기지 못했다. 그녀는 자신을 그토록 놀라게 한 하녀를 부드럽게 나무라며, \"더 나쁜 일이 아니라 다행이구나. 나는 다른 누가 온 줄 알고 겁이 났단다\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"옳지요, 옳지요,\" 여관 주인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"마님께선 사정을 잘 아시는군요. 프랑스인이야말로 우리의 가장 좋은 벗이고, 오직 우리 잘되라고 이리로 건너오는 것이지요. 늙은 잉글랜드를 다시 번성하게 해 줄 사람들이 바로 그들입니다. 분명 마님께서는 공작이 오는 줄 아셨겠지요. 그것만으로도 겁이 나실 만하지요. 제가 막 마님께 그 소식을 전하려던 참이었습니다. 부디 하늘이 보살피시기를, 폐하께서 공작을 따돌리시고 한사코 빠른 걸음으로 런던을 향해 진군 중이시랍니다. 게다가 도중에 합류하려고 프랑스군 만 명이 상륙했고요.\"\n\n소피아는 이 소식도, 그것을 전한 사내도 그다지 반갑지 않았다. 그러나 여전히 그가 자신을 알아보았다고 여겼기에(진짜 진실이야 그녀로서는 도무지 짐작할 수조차 없었으므로), 감히 싫은 기색을 내비치지 못했다. 이윽고 여관 주인은 식탁보를 걷어 내고 물러갔는데, 떠나면서도 훗날 자신을 기억해 달라는 바람을 거듭 되풀이했다.\n\n이 집에서 자신이 들통났다는 짐작에 소피아의 마음은 조금도 편치 않았다. 여관 주인이 제니 캐머런에게 건넨 여러 말을 그녀는 여전히 자신에게 끌어다 붙이고 있었기 때문이다. 그래서 그녀는 하녀에게 일러, 그가 무슨 수로 자기 신분을 알게 되었는지, 또 누가 자신을 배신하는 대가로 상금을 내걸었는지 그에게서 캐내라고 시켰다. 또한 새벽 네 시까지 말을 준비해 두라고 일렀는데, 그 시각에 피츠패트릭 부인이 함께 길을 나서겠다고 약속한 터였다. 그런 다음 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 마음을 가라앉히고, 그 부인에게 이야기를 계속해 달라고 청했다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인이 자신의 이야기를 마무리하는 장.\n\n오너 부인이 안주인의 분부에 따라 펀치 한 사발을 시키고, 집주인 내외를 불러 함께 들자고 권하는 동안, 피츠패트릭 부인은 이렇게 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"우리 동네 인근 마을에 주둔한 장교들은 대부분 제 남편과 아는 사이였어요. 그 가운데 중위 한 사람이 있었는데, 아주 멋진 사람이었고, 성품으로 보나 대화로 보나 더없이 상냥한 여인과 결혼한 처지였지요. 제가 산후조리를 마친 직후 처음 서로 알게 된 그때부터, 우리는 거의 떨어질 수 없는 동무가 되었답니다. 저 또한 그 여인에게 그만큼 마음에 드는 사람이 되는 행운을 누렸으니까요.\n\n\"그 중위는 술꾼도 사냥꾼도 아니었던 터라, 우리 모임에 자주 끼었어요. 사실 제 남편하고는 어울리는 일이 거의 없었고, 그저 예의가 강요하는 만큼만 함께했을 뿐이었지요. 그는 거의 늘 우리 집에서 지내다시피 했으니까요. 제 남편은 중위가 자기보다 제 곁을 더 좋아한다며 자주 큰 불만을 드러냈어요. 그 일로 제게 몹시 화를 냈고, 제가 자기 동무를 빼앗아 간다며 욕설을 마구 퍼부었답니다. 이러더군요. '세상에서 가장 멋진 친구 중 하나를 물러 터진 겁쟁이로 만들어 망쳐 놓았으니, 나는 저주를 받아 마땅하지.'\n\n\"사랑하는 소피아, 제 남편의 분노가 제가 동무를 빼앗아 간 데서 비롯되었다고 여긴다면 그건 오해예요. 그 중위는 어리석은 자가 어울려 즐거울 만한 사람이 아니었거든요. 설령 그럴 가능성을 인정한다 해도, 제 남편이 동무를 잃은 책임을 제게 돌릴 권리는 거의 없었어요. 애초에 그 사람을 집에 오게 한 것은 오직 저와의 대화뿐이었다고 저는 확신하니까요. 아니에요, 얘야, 그건 시기였어요. 가장 고약하고 가장 악독한 종류의 시기, 곧 더 뛰어난 지성에 대한 시기 말이에요. 그 한심한 작자는, 조금도 질투할 구석이 없는 남자가 자기보다 저와의 대화를 더 좋아하는 꼴을 견딜 수 없었던 거예요. 오, 사랑하는 소피, 너는 분별 있는 여자야. 만일 네가 결혼을 한다면—아마 그러겠지만—너보다 못한 그릇의 남자와 맺어질 가능성이 크니, 결혼 전에 그 사람의 성품을 자주 시험해 보고, 그런 우월함에 굽힐 줄 아는 사람인지 잘 살펴보렴. 약속해 다오, 소피. 이 충고를 받아들이겠다고. 나중에 가서야 그 중요함을 깨닫게 될 테니까.\" \"저는 아마 영영 결혼하지 않을 것 같아요.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"적어도, 결혼 전에 그 지성에 어떤 흠이 보이는 남자와는 결코 결혼하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 약속드리는데, 저는 결혼한 뒤에 그런 흠을 보느니 차라리 제 지성을 포기하는 편을 택하겠어요.\" \"네 지성을 포기한다고!\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 받아쳤다. \"오, 저런, 얘야! 나는 너를 그토록 하찮게 여기진 않을 거야. 다른 건 다 내놓으라면 나도 못 이겨 내놓을지 모르지만, 이것만은 절대 안 돼. 만일 자연이 우리 모두에게 그 우월함을 남편에게 넘겨주기를 바랐다면, 이토록 많은 경우에 아내에게 그것을 나누어 주지 않았을 거야. 사실, 분별 있는 남자라면 우리에게 그런 걸 결코 기대하지 않아. 방금 말한 그 중위가 바로 그 두드러진 본보기였지. 그는 무척 좋은 지성을 지녔으면서도, 아내가 자기보다 더 낫다는 걸 늘 인정했거든—실제로도 그게 사실이었고. 그리고 어쩌면 이것이, 그 폭군 같은 내 남편이 그 부인을 미워한 한 가지 이유였을 거야.\n\n\"그는 아내에게, 그것도 그렇게 못생긴 계집(사실 그 부인이 반듯한 미인은 아니었지만 무척 상냥하고 더없이 우아했답니다)에게 휘둘릴 바에야 차라리 세상의 모든 여자가 악마에게 끌려가는 꼴을 보겠다더군요—그건 그가 입버릇처럼 쓰던 말이었어요. 그 여자한테 대체 뭘 봤기에 그렇게 함께 있는 걸 즐거워하는지 모르겠다며, 이렇게 말했죠. '이 여자가 우리 사이에 끼어든 뒤로 당신이 그토록 좋아한다던 그 잘난 독서도 끝장이 났군. 어찌나 좋아했으면 이 고장 부인들 답방할 틈조차 못 낸다고 했었나.' 솔직히 고백하자면, 이 점에선 제가 조금 무례했던 게 사실이에요. 거기 부인들이래야 적어도 여기 시골 부인들보다 나을 게 없었거든요. 그러니 그들과 가까이 지내기를 마다한 까닭에 대해서는 너한테 달리 변명할 필요도 없을 것 같구나.\n\n\"하지만 이 교제는 그 중위가 그 마을에 주둔해 있던 내내, 꼬박 한 해 동안 이어졌어요. 그 대가로 저는 앞서 말한 식으로 남편에게 줄곧 욕먹는 세금을 기꺼이 치렀지요. 물론 남편이 집에 있을 때 말이에요. 그는 자주 한 달씩 더블린에 나가 있었고, 한 번은 두 달이나 런던으로 여행을 떠나기도 했거든요. 그 모든 여행에서 그가 단 한 번도 제 동행을 원치 않았다는 게 저에겐 참으로 유별난 행운으로 여겨졌답니다. 아니, 그는 그가 말하던 식으로 '아내를 꽁무니에 매달지 않고는 여행도 못 하는' 남자들을 자주 헐뜯음으로써, 설령 제가 아무리 따라가고 싶어 한들 그 바람이 헛수고였으리란 걸 충분히 내비쳤어요. 하지만 하늘은 아시지요, 그런 바람은 제 생각에서 한참이나 멀리 있었답니다.\n\n\"마침내 제 벗이 제 곁에서 떠나갔고, 저는 다시 외로움 속에, 제 자신의 생각과 나누는 그 괴로운 대화 속에 홀로 남겨졌어요. 그러고는 오직 책에 기대어 유일한 위안을 찾았지요. 이제 저는 거의 온종일 책을 읽었어요. 제가 석 달 동안 책을 몇 권이나 읽었을 것 같니?\" \"정말 짐작이 안 가요, 언니.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"어쩌면 열 권쯤?\" \"열 권이라니! 오백 권이야, 얘야!\" 상대가 답했다. \"대니얼의 『프랑스 영문사』를 꽤 읽었고, 플루타르코스의 『영웅전』, 『아탈란티스』, 포프가 옮긴 호메로스, 드라이든의 희곡들, 칠링워스, 돌누아 백작부인의 글, 그리고 로크의 『인간지성론』도 잔뜩 읽었지.\n\n\"이 기간 동안 저는 숙모님께 무척 간절하고, 제 딴엔 마음을 움직일 만한 편지를 세 통이나 썼어요. 하지만 어느 편지에도 답을 받지 못하니, 자존심이 더는 매달리도록 두지 않더군요.\" 여기서 그녀는 말을 멈추고, 소피아를 골똘히 바라보며 말했다. \"얘야, 아무래도 네 눈빛 속에서 무언가를 읽은 것 같구나. 더 다정한 답을 받았어야 마땅한 다른 곳에다 내가 소홀했다고 나무라는 빛 말이야.\" \"정말이지, 사랑하는 해리엇,\" 소피아가 답했다. \"언니의 이야기는 그 어떤 소홀함도 변명해 주는걸요. 하지만 사실, 저야말로 그만한 핑계도 없이 게을렀던 죄가 있다고 느껴요. 그래도 부디 계속해 주세요. 떨리긴 해도, 그 끝을 어서 듣고 싶거든요.\"\n\n그러자 피츠패트릭 부인은 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다. \"남편은 이제 두 번째로 잉글랜드 여행을 떠나, 거기서 석 달 남짓 머물렀어요. 그 대부분의 시간 동안 저는, 그보다 더한 삶을 겪어 봤기에 비로소 견딜 만하다 여겨지는 그런 삶을 살았답니다. 완전한 고독이란 저처럼 사람을 좋아하는 마음과는 결코 화해할 수 없는 거예요. 다만 미워하는 자들과의 동석에서 벗어나게 해 줄 때만은 예외지요. 제 비참함을 더한 것은 어린 갓난아이를 잃은 일이었어요. 다른 상황이었다면 제가 품을 수도 있었을 그 지나친 애틋함을 그 아이에게 쏟았노라 우길 생각은 없어요. 하지만 저는 매 순간 가장 다정한 어머니의 도리를 다하기로 마음먹었답니다. 그리고 이 보살핌 덕분에, 만일 그것이 우리 손에 무겁게 얹혀 있다고 말할 수 있을 때라면, 세상에서 가장 무거운 그 짐의 무게를 느끼지 않을 수 있었어요.\n\n\"저는 꼬박 열 주를 거의 온전히 혼자 보냈어요. 그동안 하인들과 극히 드문 방문객 말고는 아무도 본 적이 없었지요. 그러던 차에, 제 남편의 친척인 젊은 부인이 아일랜드의 먼 곳에서 저를 보러 찾아왔답니다. 그녀는 전에도 한 번 우리 집에 일주일을 머문 적이 있었고, 그때 제가 다시 와 달라고 간곡히 청해 두었어요. 무척 상냥한 여인인 데다, 타고난 좋은 자질을 알맞은 교육으로 잘 가꾸어 둔 사람이었거든요. 정말이지 저에겐 반가운 손님이었지요.\n\n\"그녀가 온 지 며칠 만에, 제가 무척 풀 죽어 있는 걸 알아챈 그 젊은 부인은—사실 그 까닭을 그녀도 잘 알고 있었기에—이유를 묻지도 않고 제 처지를 가엾이 여기기 시작했어요. 그녀가 말하더군요. '예의 때문에 제가 남편의 친척들에게 그의 행실을 하소연하지는 않았지만, 그들 모두 그 일을 잘 알고 있었고 그 때문에 무척 마음 아파했어요. 그중에서도 자기가 가장 그러했고요.' 그러고는 이 화제로 좀 더 일반적인 이야기를 주고받은 끝에—사실 저도 맞장구를 치지 않을 수 없었지요—그녀는 마침내 갖은 사전 경계를 거듭하고 비밀로 해 달라 단단히 다짐을 받은 뒤, 더없이 깊은 비밀이라며 제게 알려 주었답니다. 제 남편이 정부를 두고 있다는 사실을요.\n\n\"너는 분명 내가 이 소식을 더없이 무덤덤하게 들었으리라 상상하겠지. 하지만 맹세컨대, 만일 그렇게 여긴다면 그 상상이 너를 잘못 이끄는 거야. 남편에 대한 경멸이 내 분노를 짓눌러 두긴 했어도, 이 일을 계기로 미움이 다시 솟구쳤거든. 대체 그 까닭이 무엇일까? 우리는 그토록 끔찍이도 이기적이어서, 심지어 우리가 업신여기는 것조차 남이 차지하면 마음이 쓰이는 걸까? 아니면 우리는 차라리 끔찍이도 허영심이 많아서, 이것이 우리 허영심에 가해지는 가장 큰 상처인 걸까? 너는 어떻게 생각하니, 소피아?\"\n\n\"정말 모르겠어요.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"저는 그런 깊은 생각으로 머리를 썩여 본 적이 없거든요. 다만 그 부인이 언니한테 그런 비밀을 알려 준 건 아주 잘못한 일이라고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"그렇지만, 얘야, 그런 행동은 자연스러운 거란다.\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 받았다. \"너도 나만큼 보고 읽어 본다면, 그게 그렇다는 걸 인정하게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"그게 자연스러운 일이라니 듣기가 안타깝네요.\" 소피아가 응수했다. \"그게 무척 떳떳지 못하고 무척 심술궂은 짓이라는 건, 책을 읽거나 겪어 보지 않아도 알 수 있으니까요. 아니, 남편이나 아내에게 서로의 허물을 일러바치는 건 분명, 그들 자신의 허물을 일러 주는 것만큼이나 무례한 짓이에요.\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 말을 이었다. \"남편이 마침내 돌아왔어. 내 마음을 내가 제대로 안다면, 나는 이제 그를 그 어느 때보다 미워했어. 하지만 업신여기는 마음은 오히려 좀 덜해졌지. 우리의 경멸을 가장 약하게 만드는 건, 분명 우리의 자존심이나 허영심에 가해진 상처거든.\n\n\"그는 이제 최근까지 보이던 것과는 사뭇 다른, 결혼 첫 주의 행동과 거의 흡사한 태도를 내게 취하더구나. 그래서 만일 내게 사랑의 불씨가 한 점이라도 남아 있었다면, 어쩌면 그가 나의 애정을 다시 지펴 놓을 수도 있었을 거야. 하지만 미움은 경멸의 뒤를 이을 수 있고 어쩌면 그것을 이겨 낼 수도 있어도, 사랑은 그러지 못한다고 나는 믿어. 사실인즉, 사랑이라는 정념은 그 대상에게서 받는 만족 없이는 가만히 흡족해할 수 없을 만큼 너무도 들떠 있는 거야. 그래서 눈이 있으면서 보지 않을 수 없듯, 사랑하지 않으면서 사랑하려는 마음을 품을 수도 없는 거지. 그러니 남편이 더 이상 이 정념의 대상이 되지 못하면, 십중팔구 다른 어떤 남자가—그러니까, 얘야, 만일 네 남편이 너한테 무덤덤해진다면—네가 일단 그를 업신여기게 된다면—내 말은—그러니까—네 안에 사랑이라는 정념이 있다면—아이고! 내가 그만 갈피를 잃고 말았네—하지만 사람이 이런 추상적인 생각에 빠지다 보면, 로크 선생 말마따나 관념의 연쇄를 놓치기 쉬운 법이지. 요컨대, 사실인즉—요컨대, 그게 뭔지 나도 잘 모르겠다만—아무튼 말했다시피, 남편이 돌아왔고, 그 행동이 처음엔 나를 무척 놀라게 했어. 하지만 그는 곧 그 속셈을 내게 알려 주었고, 그 까닭을 헤아리게 해 주었지. 한마디로, 그는 내 재산 가운데 당장 쓸 수 있던 돈을 죄다 써서 날려 버렸던 거야. 자기 영지는 더 깊이 저당 잡힐 수도 없으니, 이제 자신의 사치를 위한 현금을 마련하려고 내 작은 영지 하나를 팔고 싶어 했지. 그건 내 도움 없이는 할 수 없는 일이었고. 그가 지금 꾸며 보이던 그 온갖 다정함은, 오로지 이 호의를 얻으려는 것이 전부이자 유일한 속셈이었던 거야.\n\n\"이 일에 대해 나는 단호히 응하지 않겠다고 거절했어. 그에게 말했지, 그것도 진심으로 말이야. 우리가 처음 결혼했을 때 내가 동인도제도를 다 가졌더라도, 그가 그 전부를 마음대로 부릴 수 있었을 거라고. 왜냐하면 여자가 자기 마음을 내어 줄 때는 언제나 재산도 함께 맡겨야 한다는 것이 나의 한결같은 신조였으니까. 하지만 그가 오래전 친절하게도 전자, 곧 내 마음을 도로 내 차지로 돌려주었으니, 나도 후자 가운데 얼마 남지 않은 것만은 똑같이 간직하기로 마음먹었다고 말이야.\n\n\"이 말과, 그것을 내뱉을 때의 그 단호한 태도가 그를 어떤 광분 속에 몰아넣었는지는 네게 묘사하지 않으마. 그 뒤로 우리 사이에 벌어진 그 모든 장면으로 너를 성가시게 하지도 않을게. 정부 이야기가 튀어나왔다는 건 너도 능히 짐작하겠지. 그리고 그 이야기는 분노와 경멸이 그 위에 덧입힐 수 있는 온갖 치장을 다 걸치고 튀어나왔단다.\n\n\"피츠패트릭 씨는 이 말에 다소 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했고, 내가 일찍이 본 그 어느 때보다 당황한 기색이었어. 뭐, 그의 머릿속이야 하늘이 아시다시피 늘 충분히 뒤죽박죽이지만 말이야. 그래도 그는 자신을 변명하려 들지는 않았어. 대신, 거의 똑같이 나를 어리둥절하게 만드는 수법을 썼지. 그게 다름 아닌 맞고발이었어. 그가 질투하는 척을 하더구나. 어쩌면 그는 타고난 성품 자체가 질투에 기우는 쪽일지도 몰라. 아니, 그는 그걸 천성으로 타고났거나, 아니면 악마가 그의 머릿속에 집어넣었거나 했을 거야. 내 품행에 정당한 오점을 던질 수 있는 자가 있다면 온 세상을 향해 어디 한번 해 보라지. 아니, 아무리 험한 혀라도 감히 내 평판을 헐뜯은 적이 없었어. 하늘에 감사하게도, 내 명성은 늘 내 삶만큼이나 티 한 점 없었고, 거짓이 제 입으로 그걸 고발할 배짱이 있거든 어디 해 보라지. 아니야, 사랑하는 점잔 양, 아무리 화가 치밀고, 아무리 학대를 당하고, 아무리 사랑에 상처를 입었어도, 나는 이 일에 관해서만은 비난의 빌미를 조금도 주지 않겠다고 굳게 마음먹었단다. 그렇지만, 얘야, 세상엔 어찌나 악의에 찬 사람들, 어찌나 독을 품은 혀들이 있는지, 어떤 결백도 그들을 피할 수 없는 법이야. 가장 무심코 던진 말, 가장 우연한 눈길, 아주 조그만 허물없음, 더없이 순진한 스스럼없음조차도, 어떤 사람들에 의해 곡해되고 부풀려져 무엇이 되는지 알 수도 없게 된단다. 하지만 나는 업신여겨, 사랑하는 점잔 양, 그런 비방 따위는 죄다 업신여긴다고. 분명히 말하지만, 그런 악의가 나를 단 한순간이라도 불편하게 한 적은 없어. 아냐, 아냐, 단언컨대 나는 그런 것쯤은 다 초월한 사람이거든. 그런데 내가 어디까지 말했더라? 아, 그래, 남편이 질투하더라고 말했지. 그래서, 대체 누구를 두고? 글쎄, 앞서 네게 말한 그 중위 말고 누구겠니! 그 영문 모를 정념의 대상이라는 걸 찾으려고 그는 일 년도 더 거슬러 올라가야 했단다. 정말로 그런 감정을 느끼기나 했다면, 그리고 나를 학대하려고 꾸며 댄 새빨간 거짓이 아니었다면 말이야.\n\n\"하지만 내가 벌써 너무 자질구레한 이야기로 너를 지치게 했구나. 이제 내 이야기를 아주 빨리 매듭지을게. 요컨대, 다시 옮길 가치도 없는 여러 장면이 지난 뒤에—그 와중에 내 사촌이 어찌나 진심으로 내 편을 들었던지, 피츠패트릭 씨가 끝내 그녀를 문밖으로 내쫓아 버렸지—내가 달래도 윽박질러도 응하지 않으리란 걸 알아챈 그는, 정말이지 아주 난폭한 방법을 택했어. 너는 아마 그가 나를 때렸으리라 결론지을 테지. 하지만 그 짓은, 거의 그 직전까지 가긴 했어도, 실제로 한 적은 결코 없어. 그는 나를 방에 가두고, 펜도 잉크도 종이도 책도 일절 갖지 못하게 했지. 그리고 하인이 날마다 내 침대를 정돈하고 음식을 가져다주었단다.\n\n\"이렇게 갇혀 일주일을 보냈을 때, 그가 나를 찾아왔어. 그러고는 학교 선생 같은—아니, 흔히 그것과 별반 다르지 않은, 폭군 같은 목소리로 묻더구나. '이제는 따를 텐가?' 나는 아주 당차게 대답했지. '차라리 죽고 말겠어요.' 그러자 그가 소리치더라. '그렇다면 죽어, 빌어먹을! 너는 살아서는 이 방을 절대 못 나갈 테니까.'\n\n\"나는 여기서 다시 두 주를 더 머물렀어. 그리고 솔직히 말하면, 내 꿋꿋함도 거의 꺾여서, 굴복을 생각하기 시작하던 참이었지. 그런데 어느 날, 잠시 외출하느라 남편이 집을 비운 사이, 세상에서 가장 큰 행운으로 한 가지 일이 일어났단다. 나는—내가 더없는 절망에 빠져들기 시작하던 그때에—그런 때라면 무슨 일이든 용서받을 만하지—바로 그때에 받았어—하지만 그 자초지종을 다 이야기하려면 한 시간은 걸릴 거야. 그러니 한마디로 말하면(자질구레한 사정으로 너를 지치게 하지 않으마), 모든 자물쇠의 공통된 열쇠인 황금이 내 방문을 열어 주었고, 나를 자유롭게 풀어 주었단다.\n\n\"나는 곧장 더블린으로 서둘러 갔고, 거기서 즉시 잉글랜드로 가는 뱃길을 마련했어. 그러고는 숙모님이나 네 아버지, 아니면 보호해 줄 만한 어떤 친척에게든 몸을 의탁하려고 바스로 향하던 중이었지. 어젯밤 내가 묵던 여관에서 남편이 나를 따라잡았는데, 그 여관은 네가 나보다 몇 분 앞서 떠난 바로 그곳이었어. 하지만 나는 다행히 그를 따돌리고 너를 뒤따라올 수 있었단다.\n\n\"그리고, 얘야, 이렇게 내 이야기는 끝이 나는구나. 나 자신에게는 분명 비극 같은 이야기지만, 어쩌면 그 따분함에 대해 오히려 네게 사과를 해야 할지도 모르겠다.\"\n\n소피아는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 대답했다. \"정말이지, 해리엇, 저는 영혼 깊은 곳에서부터 언니가 가여워요! 그렇지만 무얼 기대하셨겠어요? 어쩌자고, 어쩌자고 아일랜드 남자와 결혼하셨어요?\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 사촌이 받았다. \"네 비난은 부당하구나. 아일랜드 사람들 중에도 잉글랜드 사람 못지않게 가치 있고 명예로운 남자들이 있어. 아니, 솔직히 말하면, 너그러운 기상은 오히려 그들에게서 더 흔하지. 거기서도 좋은 남편의 본보기를 몇몇 보았는데, 그런 사람은 잉글랜드에선 그리 흔치 않을 것 같구나. 차라리 내게 물으렴, 어리석은 자와 결혼해 놓고 무얼 기대했느냐고. 그럼 엄숙한 진실을 말해 주마. 나는 그가 그런 자인 줄 몰랐단다.\" \"그럼 언니 생각엔,\" 소피아가 무척 낮고 달라진 목소리로 말했다. \"어리석지 않은 남자라면 나쁜 남편이 될 수 없다는 건가요?\" \"그건,\" 상대가 답했다. \"너무 싸잡은 부정이지. 하지만 어리석은 자만큼 나쁜 남편이 되기 쉬운 사람은 없을 거라고 나는 믿어. 내가 아는 사람들 가운데서도 가장 멍청한 작자들이 가장 못된 남편들이거든. 그리고 나는 하나의 사실로서 감히 단언하겠어. 분별 있는 남자라면, 충분히 잘해 줄 자격이 있는 아내에게 아주 못되게 구는 일이 좀처럼 없다고 말이야.\"\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n소피아는 이제 사촌의 청에 따라 자신의 이야기를 들려주었다. 다만 그것은 앞으로 일어날 일이 아니라, 이 이야기에서 이미 지나간 일이었다. 그러니 독자께서는 내가 그것을 다시 되풀이하지 않더라도 너그러이 양해해 주시리라 믿는다.\n\n그러나 그녀의 이야기에 대해 한 가지만큼은 짚지 않고 넘어갈 수가 없다. 그것은 곧, 그녀가 처음부터 끝까지 존스에 대해서는, 마치 이 세상에 그런 사람이 살아 있지도 않은 듯, 단 한 마디도 입에 올리지 않았다는 사실이다. 이 점은 내가 설명하려 들지도, 변명하려 들지도 않겠다. 사실 이것을 일종의 부정직함이라 부를 수 있다면, 상대편 부인이 보여 준 그 분명한 솔직함과 거리낌 없는 진실함에 견주어 볼 때 더더욱 용서받기 어려워 보인다. 그러나 어쨌든 사정은 그러했다.\n\n소피아가 이야기를 막 끝맺을 무렵, 두 부인이 앉아 있던 방으로 어떤 소리가 들이닥쳤다. 그 소리는 우렁차기로 치자면 막 개집에서 풀려난 사냥개 떼의 짖음과 다르지 않았고, 째지기로 치자면 발정 난 고양이들의 울음이나 부엉이의 비명과도 같았다. 아니, 차라리 (어떤 짐승인들 사람의 목소리를 닮을 수 있겠는가?) 두 갈래 혀에서 그 이름을 따온 듯한 그 관문의 유쾌한 저택에서, 옛적에 나이아데스라 불렸고 속된 말로는 굴 파는 아낙으로 옮겨지는 그 어여쁜 강의 요정들이 입으로, 때로는 콧구멍으로 내뿜는 소리에 더 가까웠다. 옛 제례에서 바치던 우유와 꿀과 기름 대신, 노간주 열매에서 뽑아낸 진한 술, 혹은 보리에서 빚어낸 술이 그 숭배자들의 이른 아침 헌신으로 흥건히 부어진 마당에, 만일 어떤 겁 없는 혀가 불경한 방종으로 신성을 모독한다면, 즉 그 보드랍고 살진 밀턴 굴이며, 단단하고 야무진 가자미며, 물속에 있을 때만큼이나 펄떡이는 넙치며, 보통 새우만 한 작은 새우며, 불과 몇 시간 전까지 살아 있던 싱싱한 대구며, 또 바다와 강을 누비는 그 물의 신들이 요정들의 손에 맡겨 둔 온갖 보물들을 헐뜯기라도 한다면, 노한 나이아데스는 불멸의 목청을 드높이고, 그 불경한 가엾은 자는 제 불경의 죄로 귀가 먹먹해지고 마는 것이다.\n\n지금 아래층 방 하나에서 터져 나온 소리가 바로 그러했다. 그리고 곧, 멀리서 한참 동안 우르릉대던 천둥이 점점 가까이 다가오기 시작하더니, 계단을 차츰차츰 올라와 마침내 두 부인이 있던 방으로 들어섰다. 요컨대, 온갖 비유와 수사를 다 걷어 내고 말하자면, 아너 부인이 아래층에서 사납게 악다구니를 퍼붓고는 올라오는 내내 그 기세를 이어 가다가, 가장 노발대발한 모습으로 제 아씨에게 들이닥쳐 이렇게 외쳐 댄 것이다. \"아씨, 이걸 어찌 생각하셔요? 글쎄, 이 뻔뻔한 악당, 이 집 주인 놈이 저더러 뭐라고 했는지 짐작이나 가셔요? 제 면전에 대고 끝까지 우기더라니까요, 아씨가 그 더럽고 구린내 나는 갈, 갈보(사람들이 제니 캐머런이라 부른다죠)라고요, 그 왕위 참칭자랑 온 나라를 싸돌아다닌다는 그 계집 말이에요! 아니, 그 거짓말쟁이에 건방진 악당 놈이 글쎄, 아씨가 스스로 그렇다고 인정했다고까지 뻔뻔하게 지껄이지 뭐예요. 하지만 제가 그 악당 놈을 할퀴어 줬어요. 그 뻔뻔한 낯짝에 제 손톱 자국을 남겨 놓고 왔다고요. 제가 그랬죠, 우리 아씨라고! 이 건방진 망나니야, 우리 아씨는 어떤 참칭자 따위가 넘볼 분이 아니야. 우리 아씨로 말할 것 같으면 서머싯셔의 그 누구 못지않게 가문 좋고 집안 좋고 재산 좋은 젊은 아가씨란 말이야. 이놈아, 그 대단하신 웨스턴 지주님 얘기를 한 번도 못 들어 봤어? 우리 아씨가 바로 그분의 외동따님이시고, 그 어마어마한 재산을 다 물려받으실 상속녀시란 말이야! 우리 아씨가 그런 천한 종놈한테 더러운 스코틀랜드 갈보 소리를 듣다니!—정말이지 펀치 사발로 그놈 골통을 깨 버리지 못한 게 한이에요.\"\n\n이번 일로 소피아가 느낀 가장 큰 불안은, 다름 아닌 아너 자신이 흥분한 나머지 그녀의 정체를 까발려 버린 데서 비롯된 것이었다. 그러나 여관 주인의 이 착각 덕분에, 소피아가 앞서 잘못 짐작했던 여러 대목이 이제 충분히 설명되었으므로, 그녀는 그 점에서는 어느 정도 마음이 놓였고, 전체적으로 보아 웃음을 참을 수도 없었다. 이 모습에 아너는 더욱 발끈하여 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 아씨, 저는 아씨가 이 일을 웃어넘기실 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요. 그런 뻔뻔하고 천한 악당 놈한테 갈보 소리를 듣다니요. 아씨야 어쩌면 제가 아씨 편을 든 게 못마땅하실지도 모르죠, 자청한 봉사는 구린내가 난다는 말도 있으니까요. 하지만 정말이지 저는 우리 아씨가 갈보라고 불리는 걸 도저히 못 들어 주겠어요.—참지도 않을 거고요. 우리 아씨는 영국 땅을 밟은 그 어떤 분 못지않게 정숙한 분이시고, 감히 그와 반대되는 말을 한 마디라도 입에 올리려 드는 악당이 있다면 누구든 그 눈깔을 후벼 파 버리겠어요. 제가 모셨던 그 어떤 아씨에 대해서도 누구 하나 흠 잡을 말을 한 적이 없었다니까요.\"\n\n여기서 그 눈물이 비롯된 것이다. 솔직히 말하자면, 아너는 여느 하인들이 그러하듯 제 아씨를 사랑했다. 다시 말해—. 그러나 이뿐만 아니라, 그녀의 자존심이 자기가 모시는 아씨의 명예를 떠받들지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 자기 명예가 아씨의 명예와 아주 긴밀하게 얽혀 있다고 여겼기 때문이다. 아씨의 명예가 높아지는 만큼 제 명예도 그에 따라 높아진다고 생각했고, 반대로 한쪽이 낮아지면 다른 쪽도 낮아질 수밖에 없다고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n이 대목에서, 독자여, 나는 잠시 멈춰 그대에게 한 가지 이야기를 들려주어야겠다. \"그 유명한 넬 귄이 어느 날 잠시 들렀던 집에서 나와 제 마차에 오르려다가, 큰 무리가 몰려 있고 제 마부가 온통 피투성이에 흙투성이가 된 것을 보았다. 아씨가 어쩌다 그 꼴이 되었느냐고 묻자, 사내는 이렇게 답했다. '아씨, 어떤 뻔뻔한 악당 놈이 아씨를 갈보라고 불러서 제가 그놈하고 싸웠습니다요.' '이 멍청아,' 귄 부인이 받아쳤다. '그런 식이면 너는 평생 날마다 싸워야 할 게다. 이 바보야, 세상이 다 아는 일을.' '그래요?' 사내가 마차 문을 닫고 나서 중얼대는 목소리로 외쳤다. '그렇다 해도 그놈들이 날더러 갈보의 마부라고 부르게 둘 순 없죠.'\"\n\n이렇게 보면, 달리 설명할 길이 없다 하더라도 아너 부인의 격분은 충분히 자연스러워 보인다. 하지만 사실 그녀의 분노에는 또 다른 원인이 있었으니, 이를 위해 우리는 위의 비유에서 언급한 한 가지 정황을 독자께 다시 일깨워 드리고자 한다. 실로 어떤 종류의 술은 우리의 격정에, 혹은 불에 닿으면 물이 일으키는 것과는 정반대의 효과를 낸다. 끄기보다는 오히려 불붙이고 타오르게 하는 것이다. 그중 하나가 바로 펀치라는 그 후한 술이다. 그러니 학식 높은 체인 박사가 펀치를 마시는 것을 두고 액체로 된 불을 목구멍에 들이붓는 일이라 부르곤 했던 것도 까닭 없는 말은 아니었다.\n\n그런데 아너 부인은 불운하게도 이 액체로 된 불을 어찌나 많이 목구멍에 들이부었던지, 그 연기가 머리뼈 속으로 피어오르기 시작하여, 그곳에 거처를 둔다고 여겨지는 이성의 눈을 멀게 했고, 한편 위장에서 솟은 불 그 자체는 어렵잖게 심장에 이르러 거기서 자존심이라는 고결한 격정을 활활 타오르게 했다. 그러니 전체적으로 보아, 우리는 그 시중드는 여인의 격렬한 분노에 더는 놀라지 않게 될 것이다. 비록 언뜻 보기에는 그 원인이 결과에 견주어 너무 사소해 보인다고 인정할 수밖에 없지만 말이다.\n\n소피아와 그녀의 사촌은 온 집 안을 그토록 요란하게 울려 댄 이 불길을 끄려고 둘 다 안간힘을 다했다. 마침내 그들은 뜻을 이루었다. 아니, 비유를 한 걸음 더 밀고 나가자면, 그 불은 언어가 제공할 수 있는 모든 땔감, 곧 거기 담긴 온갖 욕설을 다 태워 버린 끝에, 마침내 제풀에 사그라들고 말았다.\n\n그러나 위층에는 평온이 돌아왔어도, 아래층은 그렇지 못했다. 그곳에서는 우리의 여관 안주인이 제 남편의 잘생긴 얼굴을 망쳐 놓은 아너 부인의 그 손톱 호미질에 잔뜩 분개하여, 복수와 정의를 큰 소리로 부르짖고 있었다. 정작 이 난투에서 가장 크게 당한 그 가엾은 사내는 더없이 잠잠했다. 어쩌면 그가 흘린 피가 그의 분노를 식혀 주었는지도 모른다. 적수가 그 손톱을 그의 뺨에 박았을 뿐 아니라 주먹을 그의 콧구멍에도 먹였고, 콧구멍은 흥건한 핏물의 눈물로 그 일격을 애도했기 때문이다. 여기에 더해 제 착각에 대한 반성도 한몫했을 것이다. 그러나 사실 그의 분노를 가장 확실히 잠재운 것은, 그가 이제 제 잘못을 깨닫게 된 그 방식이었다. 아너 부인의 행태로 말하자면 도리어 그의 생각을 굳혀 놓기만 했으니 말이다. 그런데 이제 그는 대단한 풍채에다 화려한 마차 행렬을 거느린 한 인물로부터, 그 두 부인 가운데 한 분이 지체 높은 여인이며 자신의 절친한 지인이라는 사실을 확언받게 된 것이다.\n\n이 인물의 명에 따라, 여관 주인이 이제 위층으로 올라와 우리의 어여쁜 나그네들에게 아래층의 한 대단한 신사분께서 두 분을 찾아뵙는 영광을 청하신다고 알렸다. 소피아는 이 전갈에 얼굴이 새파래지며 부들부들 떨었다. 비록 독자께서는 여관 주인의 실수가 있긴 했어도 이 전갈이 그녀의 아버지에게서 온 것이라기엔 너무 정중하다고 단정하시겠지만, 두려움이란 치안판사가 흔히 저지르는 잘못을 그대로 닮아서, 양편의 증거를 가려 보지도 않고 아주 사소한 정황 하나만으로도 성급히 결론을 내려 버리기 일쑤인 것이다.\n\n그러니 독자의 불안보다는 호기심을 풀어 드리고자, 우리는 그날 저녁 아주 늦게 한 아일랜드 귀족이 런던으로 가던 길에 그 여관에 당도했음을 알리고자 한다. 이 귀족은 앞서 이야기한 그 회오리바람 같은 소동이 일었을 때 저녁 식사 자리에서 뛰쳐나왔다가 피츠패트릭 부인의 하인을 보았고, 잠깐 물어본 끝에 자신과 아주 각별히 아는 사이인 그 부인이 위층에 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 이 소식을 듣자마자 그는 여관 주인에게 다가가 그를 달랜 뒤, 앞서 전해진 것보다는 한결 정중한 인사를 들려 그를 위층으로 올려 보냈다.\n\n이 경우에 어째서 시중드는 여인 본인이 그 전갈을 맡지 않았는지 의아해할 분도 계실 것이다. 그러나 유감스럽게도, 그녀는 지금 그 일은 물론이거니와 다른 어떤 일도 감당할 처지가 못 되었음을 말씀드려야겠다. 럼주(여관 주인은 보리에서 빚은 그 술을 굳이 그렇게 불렀다)가 비열하게도 그 가엾은 여인이 겪은 피로를 틈타, 그녀의 고결한 정신 기능이 그 공격에 도무지 맞설 수 없는 바로 그때, 거기에 끔찍한 노략질을 자행해 놓은 것이다.\n\n우리는 이 비극적인 장면을 너무 낱낱이 그려 내지는 않겠다. 다만 우리가 표방하는 그 역사가로서의 진실성에 따라, 본디 같으면 기꺼이 덮어 두고 싶었을 이 사안을 간략하게나마 귀띔해 두지 않을 수 없다고 여겼다. 실로 많은 역사가들이 이 진실성, 혹은 좋게 말해 부지런함이 모자란 탓에, 독자로 하여금 이런 자잘한 정황들을 어둠 속에서 더듬어 찾게 만들고, 때로는 큰 혼란과 당혹 속에 빠뜨리곤 한다.\n\n소피아는 그 고귀한 귀족이 들어서자 까닭 없던 두려움에서 이내 벗어났다. 그는 피츠패트릭 부인의 절친한 지인일 뿐 아니라, 실은 그 부인의 아주 각별한 벗이었던 것이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀가 남편에게서 달아날 수 있었던 것도 그의 도움 덕분이었다. 이 귀족은 우리가 영웅 이야기에서 읽는 그 이름난 기사들과 똑같은 의협의 기질을 지녀, 갇혀 있던 수많은 요정을 결박에서 풀어 주었던 것이다. 실로 그는, 편력 기사가 마법사들의 야만적인 힘을 적대했던 것 못지않게, 남편과 아버지들이 이성異性의 젊고 사랑스러운 이들에게 너무도 자주 휘두르는 그 야만적 권위에 신랄한 적이었다. 아니, 사실대로 말하자면, 나는 로맨스 곳곳에 그득한 바로 그 마법사들이 실은 그 시절의 남편들에 다름 아니었으리라고, 그리고 결혼 그 자체가 어쩌면 요정들이 갇혀 있다고들 하던 그 마법의 성이었으리라고 종종 의심해 왔다.\n\n이 귀족은 피츠패트릭의 이웃에 영지를 두고 있어, 한동안 그 부인과 알고 지내던 사이였다. 그러니 그녀가 갇혀 있다는 소식을 듣자마자 그는 열심히 나서서 그녀의 자유를 도모했고, 이를 곧 이루어 냈다. 다만 옛 영웅들의 본보기를 따라 성을 들이쳐 빼앗은 것이 아니라, 근대의 전술에 걸맞게 그 성주를 매수함으로써였다. 근대의 전술에서는 용맹보다 책략이 낫다고 치고, 황금이 납이나 강철 어느 쪽보다도 더 거역할 수 없는 것으로 통하는 법이다.\n\n그러나 이 정황은 그 부인이 제 벗에게 들려줄 만큼 중요하다고 여기지 않았던 까닭에, 우리도 그때는 독자께 밝히지 않았다. 우리는 차라리 독자께서, 그녀가 자기를 가둔 자를 매수한 그 돈을 어디서 주웠거나, 직접 찍어 냈거나, 아니면 뭔가 아주 기이한, 어쩌면 초자연적인 수단으로 손에 넣었으리라 짐작하시도록 한동안 내버려 두는 편을 택했다. 그녀가 보기에는 언급할 가치도 없을 만큼 사소해 보였던 일을 귀띔하느라 그녀의 이야기를 끊기보다는 말이다.\n\n그 귀족은 잠깐의 대화 끝에, 이런 곳에서 그 부인과 마주친 데 대한 다소의 놀라움을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다. 또한 그녀가 배스로 떠난 줄로만 알았노라고 말하지 않을 수도 없었다. 피츠패트릭 부인은 아주 거리낌 없이 대답했다. \"굳이 입에 올릴 것도 없는 한 사람이 들이닥치는 바람에 제 뜻이 좌절되고 말았답니다. 요컨대,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"남편에게 따라잡히고 만 거지요(세상이 이미 너무 잘 아는 일을 굳이 감추는 척할 것도 없으니까요). 다행히 저는 아주 놀라운 방식으로 빠져나왔고, 지금은 이 젊은 아가씨와 함께 런던으로 가는 길이랍니다. 이 아가씨는 저의 가까운 친척으로, 제 남편 못지않은 폭군에게서 달아난 처지지요.\"\n\n그 귀족 나리는 이 폭군 또한 남편이려니 단정하고, 두 부인을 향해 온통 찬사로 가득한 연설을 늘어놓는 한편, 자기 성별을 향해서는 그만큼 온통 비난을 퍼부었다. 그뿐 아니라 결혼 제도 그 자체와, 그것이 남자에게 부여하여 더 분별 있고 더 가치 있는 쪽 인간들 위에 휘두르게 하는 그 부당한 권력을 향해서도 비스듬한 눈길을 던지기를 마다하지 않았다. 그는 자신의 보호와 더불어 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 자신의 마차를 내어 주겠다는 제안으로 그 일장 연설을 맺었고, 이는 피츠패트릭 부인이 그 자리에서 받아들였으며, 마침내 그녀의 설득으로 소피아도 받아들이게 되었다.\n\n이렇게 일이 정해지자 그 귀족 나리는 물러갔고, 두 부인은 잠자리에 들었다. 그곳에서 피츠패트릭 부인은 그 고귀한 귀족의 인품에 대한 온갖 드높은 찬사로 사촌을 즐겁게 해 주었고, 특히 그가 제 아내를 끔찍이 아낀다는 점을 유난히 길게 늘어놓으며, 지체 높은 사람 가운데 결혼의 잠자리에 온전히 한결같은 이는 아마 그가 거의 유일할 것이라고 말했다. \"정말이지,\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"내 사랑하는 소피야, 그건 지체 있는 남자들 사이에서는 참으로 드문 미덕이란다. 결혼할 때 그런 걸 절대 기대하지 마라. 정말이지, 그랬다간 너는 틀림없이 속고 말 테니까.\"\n\n이 말에 소피아의 입에서 가만한 한숨이 새어 나왔고, 어쩌면 이것이 그리 유쾌하지 못한 어떤 꿈을 빚어내는 데 한몫했는지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀가 이 꿈을 누구에게도 털어놓지 않았으니, 독자께서도 그것이 여기 적혀 있기를 기대하실 수는 없겠다.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\n아침이 멋들어진 글솜씨로 소개된다. 역마차. 하녀들의 친절. 소피아의 영웅적 기질. 그녀의 너그러움. 그에 대한 보답. 일행의 출발과 런던 도착. 더불어 여행자들에게 도움이 될 몇 가지 소견.\n\n삶의 축복을 마련하도록 태어난 사회 구성원들이, 이제 그 축복을 누리도록 태어난 이들을 위해 하루의 노동을 시작하려 촛불을 밝히기 시작했다. 억센 일꾼은 이제 동료 일꾼인 황소의 기침起寢을 살피러 가고, 영리한 장인과 부지런한 직공은 딱딱한 매트리스에서 몸을 일으킨다. 그리고 어여쁜 하녀는 어질러진 응접실을 정돈하기 시작하는데, 그 난장판을 만든 시끌벅적한 장본인들은 토막토막 끊기는 선잠 속에서 마치 부드러운 깃털이 너무 단단해 잠을 방해하기라도 하듯 이리저리 뒤척이고 있다.\n\n쉽게 말하자면, 시계가 일곱 시를 치자마자 부인들은 여행 채비를 마쳤다. 그리고 그들의 청에 따라 그분과 그분의 마차 행렬도 그들을 모실 준비를 갖추었다.\n\n그런데 이제 다소 까다로운 문제가 하나 생겼으니, 다름 아니라 그분 자신을 어떻게 모실 것인가 하는 것이었다. 역마차에서야 승객을 그저 짐짝쯤으로 치기에, 재간 좋은 마부는 네 사람 자리에 여섯 사람쯤은 거뜬히 욱여넣는다. 뚱뚱한 여관 안주인이나 잘 먹어 살찐 시참사회원이 호리호리한 아가씨나 가냘픈 도련님보다 자리를 더 차지하지 않도록 용케 꾸려 넣는 것이다. 창자란 잘 눌러 두면 물러나서 좁은 데에도 들어앉는 성질이 있으니 말이다. 그러나 구분을 위해 신사용 마차라 부르는 이런 탈것에서는, 비록 다른 마차보다 흔히 더 큰데도, 이런 식으로 짐 싸듯 끼워 넣는 법이 결코 없다.\n\n그분은 매우 정중하게 말을 타겠다고 청하여 이 곤란을 단숨에 해결하려 했으나, 피츠패트릭 부인이 결코 동의하지 않았다. 그리하여 시녀들이 번갈아 가며 그분의 말 가운데 한 마리를 타기로 결론이 났고, 그 말에는 곧 그 용도로 여성용 안장이 채워졌다.\n\n여관에서 모든 것이 정리되자, 부인들은 앞서 길을 안내하던 자들을 돌려보냈고, 소피아는 여관 주인에게 선물을 건넸다. 한편으로는 자기 밑에 깔려 그가 입은 타박상을 위로하기 위함이요, 다른 한편으로는 격분한 자기 시녀의 손에 그가 당한 봉변을 보상하기 위함이었다. 그런데 이때 소피아는 처음으로 한 가지 분실을 알아차리고 적잖이 불안해졌다. 다름 아니라 아버지가 마지막 만남에서 건네준 백 파운드짜리 은행권이었으니, 그것은 아주 하찮은 푼돈을 빼고는 지금 그녀가 지닌 재산의 전부였다. 그녀는 사방을 뒤지고 가진 물건을 죄다 흔들고 헤집어 보았으나 헛수고였다. 은행권은 도무지 보이지 않았다. 그리하여 그녀는 마침내, 앞서 적은 대로 어두운 골목길에서 말에서 떨어지는 불운을 겪었을 때 주머니에서 빠뜨린 것이라고 확신하게 되었다. 그렇게 여길 만한 까닭은, 그때 주머니가 흐트러졌던 것이며, 떨어지기 바로 직전 피츠패트릭 부인의 곤경을 덜어 주려고 손수건을 꺼내는 데 무척 애를 먹었던 일이 새삼 떠올랐기 때문이다.\n\n이런 종류의 불운은 그것이 어떤 불편을 동반하든, 탐욕의 거듦이 없는 한, 조금이라도 강단이 있는 마음을 꺾지는 못한다. 그러므로 소피아는, 하필 이런 때에 닥친 이 사고만큼 시기가 나쁜 일도 없었으련만, 곧바로 근심을 이겨 내고는 늘 그렇듯 평온하고 밝은 얼굴로 일행에게 돌아왔다. 그분은 부인들을 마차로 안내했고, 어너 부인도 마찬가지로 안내했다. 어너 부인은 한참 동안 사양하고 \"어머, 마님\" 소리를 거듭하다가, 마침내 동료 시녀의 예의 바른 권유에 못 이겨 마차에 먼저 타는 영광을 받아들였다. 사실 그녀로서는 그 후로도 내내 마차를 타고 가는 것으로 만족했을 터이나, 그녀의 여주인이 몇 번이나 헛되이 눈치를 준 끝에 결국 자기 차례에는 말을 타도록 떠밀고 말았다.\n\n이제 일행을 다 태운 마차가 앞으로 나아가기 시작했다. 여러 하인들이 따랐고, 앞서 그분과 함께 말을 타고 온 두 대위가 길잡이를 섰는데, 이들은 두 부인을 모시는 이런 가치 있는 일이 아니라 훨씬 시시한 일을 위해서라도 기꺼이 마차에서 내릴 사람들이었다. 이 일에서 그들은 다만 신사답게 행동했을 뿐이나, 그분의 동행이라는 영예와 그분의 식탁이 주는 편익을 위해서라면 언제든 하인 노릇도 마다하지 않을 자들이었고, 실은 그보다 더 낮은 일도 기꺼이 떠맡았을 위인들이었다.\n\n여관 주인은 소피아에게서 받은 선물에 어찌나 흡족했던지, 타박상이나 긁힌 상처를 후회하기는커녕 오히려 기뻐할 지경이었다. 독자는 어쩌면 이 선물의 액수가 얼마인지 궁금할지도 모르겠다. 그러나 우리는 그 호기심을 채워 줄 수 없다. 액수가 얼마였든, 그것은 주인의 몸이 입은 상처를 보상하기에 충분했다. 다만 그는 그 부인이 돈을 그토록 대수롭지 않게 여긴다는 사실을 진작 몰랐던 것을 한탄했다. \"정말이지,\" 그가 말했다. \"물건값마다 곱절을 매겼더라도, 셈을 두고 한마디 트집도 잡지 않았을 텐데.\"\n\n그러나 그의 아내는 이런 결론과는 거리가 멀었다. 그녀가 정말로 자기 남편이 입은 손해를 남편 자신보다 더 절절히 느꼈는지 어떤지는 말하지 않겠다. 다만 분명한 것은, 그녀가 소피아의 너그러움에 훨씬 덜 흡족해했다는 사실이다. \"정말이지,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"여보, 그 부인은 당신 생각보다 돈 쓸 줄을 잘 아는 사람이에요. 우리가 무슨 보상도 없이 이런 일을 그냥 넘기지 않으리라는 걸 빤히 알았겠죠. 게다가 법으로 갔으면 이 가엾은 푼돈보다 훨씬 더 큰돈이 들었을 텐데, 당신이 그걸 받았다는 게 난 신기하네요.\" \"당신은 늘 그렇게 지긋지긋하게 약아빠졌지.\" 남편이 말했다. \"그쪽이 돈을 더 썼을 거라고? 내가 그걸 당신만큼 모를 줄 알아? 한데 그 더 든다는 돈이, 아니 그만한 돈이라도, 우리 주머니로 들어왔겠냐고? 정말이지, 변호사였던 우리 톰이 살아 있었으면, 이런 짭짤한 건을 그 애 손에 넘겼으면 나도 기뻤겠지. 그 애라면 거기서 한몫 잘 챙겼을 테니까. 하지만 지금 나한테는 변호사 친척이라곤 없는데, 생판 남들 좋으라고 내가 왜 법정에 나가겠어?\" \"그래요, 하긴,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"당신이 제일 잘 알겠죠.\" \"내 생각엔 그래.\" 그가 받았다. \"돈이 생길 일이라면, 나도 남 못지않게 냄새는 잘 맡는다고. 누구나 다 이렇게 사람한테서 말로 떼어 낼 수 있는 건 아니야. 그 점을 새겨 둬. 그래, 누구나 다 그 여자한테서 이걸 살살 구슬려 받아 낼 수 있는 건 아니라고, 그 점을 새겨 두란 말이야.\" 그러자 아내도 남편의 영민함을 칭송하는 데 합세했다. 그렇게 해서 이 일을 두고 두 사람 사이에 오간 짧은 대화는 끝이 났다.\n\n그러니 우리도 이 선량한 부부에게 작별을 고하고, 그분과 그 아리따운 동행들을 따라가 보자. 그들은 어찌나 부지런히 길을 재촉했던지, 이틀 만에 구십 마일을 달려 둘째 날 저녁에 런던에 도착했다. 길에서 이 이야기의 품격에 어울려 들려줄 만한 모험이라고는 단 하나도 겪지 않은 채 말이다. 그러므로 우리의 붓도 이 묘사하는 신속함을 본받을 것이며, 우리의 이야기도 그 주인공인 여행자들과 발걸음을 맞추어 나아갈 것이다. 좋은 작가라면 실로 이 대목에서, 어느 곳에 머무는 시간을 늘 그 고장이 베푸는 아름다움과 우아함과 진기함에 견주어 정하는 재간 있는 여행자를 본받음이 마땅하다. 에셔에서, 스토에서, 윌턴에서, 이스트버리에서, 그리고 프라이어스 파크에서는, 황홀해진 상상력에 하루해가 너무 짧다. 자연을 가다듬는 기예의 경이로운 힘에 우리가 감탄하는 동안에는 말이다. 그중 어떤 곳에서는 주로 기예가 우리의 감탄을 자아내고, 어떤 곳에서는 자연과 기예가 우리의 갈채를 다툰다. 그러나 마지막 곳에서는 자연이 승리하는 듯 보인다. 여기서는 자연이 가장 화려한 옷을 차려입고 나타나며, 기예는 더없이 수수한 단순함으로 단장하고서 그 자애로운 여주인을 섬긴다. 여기서야말로 자연은 이 세상에 아낌없이 베푼 보화 가운데 가장 빼어난 것들을 쏟아 놓는다. 그리고 여기서 인간 본성은, 오직 저세상에서만 능가할 수 있을 대상을 그대 앞에 내보인다.\n\n이 우아한 풍경 속에서 호사스레 노니는 바로 그 안목, 그 상상력은, 훨씬 격이 낮은 대상으로도 즐거움을 누릴 수 있다. 데번과 도싯의 숲과 강과 풀밭은 재간 있는 여행자의 눈길을 사로잡아 그 걸음을 늦춘다. 그렇게 지체한 시간은, 뒤이어 음울한 백숏 황야나, 스톡브리지에서 서쪽으로 뻗어 나가는 그 흐뭇한 평원을 잽싸게 가로질러 달림으로써 메운다. 그 평원에서는 십육 마일을 가도록 외딴 나무 한 그루 말고는 눈에 띄는 것이 없으니, 지친 우리 마음을 가엾이 여긴 구름이 그 알록달록한 누각을 우리 시야에 다정히 열어 보여 주지 않는 한 그러하다.\n\n돈만 궁리하는 장사꾼, 영민한 치안판사, 위엄 부리는 의사, 따뜻이 차려입은 목축업자, 그리고 부유함과 둔함이 낳은 그 수많은 자손들은 이렇게 여행하지 않는다. 그들은 푸르른 풀밭을 지나든 메마른 황야를 넘든 한결같은 걸음으로 터덜터덜 나아가는데, 그 말들은 시속 사 마일 반을 더없이 정확하게 재며 걷는다. 짐승의 눈도 그 주인의 눈도 똑같이 앞만 바라보며, 똑같은 대상을 똑같은 방식으로 응시하는 데 쓰일 뿐이다. 그 어진 기수는 건축가의 가장 자랑스러운 걸작들도, 또 어떤 이름 모를 자가 부유한 직물 도시를 꾸며 놓은 그 번듯한 건물들도 똑같은 황홀함으로 바라본다. 그 도시에서는 벽돌 무더기가 쌓여 올라가 있으니, 일찍이 그곳에 돈 무더기가 쌓여 올랐음을 보여 주는 일종의 기념비인 셈이다.\n\n자, 독자여, 이제 우리는 우리의 여주인공을 따라가기에 바쁘니, 이 모든 것을 보이오티아풍의 작가들과 그 정반대편에 선 작가들에게 적용해 보는 일은 그대의 영민함에 맡기겠다. 이 정도는 우리의 도움 없이도 그대가 넉넉히 해낼 수 있으리라. 그러니 이 기회에 그대 스스로 분발하라. 까다로운 대목에서는 우리가 늘 알맞은 도움을 빌려주겠지만 — 더러 다른 이들처럼 그대더러 점치는 재주를 부려 우리의 뜻을 알아내라 요구하지는 않으니 — 그대 자신의 주의력만 있으면 될 곳에서까지 그대의 게으름을 받아 주지는 않을 것이다. 우리가 이 위대한 작업을 시작할 때 그대의 영민함이 할 일을 하나도 남겨 두지 않으려 했다고 여긴다면, 혹은 이따금 그 재능을 부려 보지 않고서도 우리의 책장을 헤쳐 가며 스스로 어떤 즐거움이나 유익을 얻을 수 있으리라 여긴다면, 그것은 그대의 큰 착각이기 때문이다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n런던에 다다른 우리 일행은 백작 댁 앞에서 내렸다. 그곳에서 그들이 여행의 피로를 푸는 동안, 두 부인을 위한 거처를 마련하라는 명을 받은 하인들이 길을 나섰다. 그때 백작 부인이 시내에 없었던 까닭에, 피츠패트릭 부인은 그 귀족의 저택에서 잠자리를 받는 일만큼은 한사코 받아들이려 하지 않았다.\n\n어떤 독자들은 아마도 이 유별난, 굳이 그렇게 부르자면, 덕의 섬세함을 너무 까탈스럽고 깐깐하다며 나무랄지도 모른다. 그러나 우리는 그녀의 처지를 헤아려 주어야 한다. 그 처지는 누가 보아도 매우 아슬아슬한 것이었음을 인정해야 하니 말이다. 게다가 트집 잡기 좋아하는 입들의 악의를 생각하면, 설령 이것이 잘못이었다 하더라도 그 잘못은 옳은 쪽으로 지나친 것이었음을 인정해야 하며, 똑같은 처지에 놓인 여인이라면 누구나 이를 본받는 편이 좋을 것이다. 덕의 가장 격식 차린 겉모습이란, 그것이 한낱 겉모습에 지나지 않을 때는, 아마도 매우 추상적인 견지에서 본다면 이런 격식 없는 덕 그 자체보다 도리어 조금 덜 칭찬할 만한 것으로 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 그럼에도 그것은 언제나 더 많은 칭찬을 받을 것이다. 그리고 다음 한 가지는 모두가 인정하리라 믿는다. 아주 특별한 몇몇 경우를 빼고는, 모든 여인이 둘 중 어느 하나만큼은 지켜 나가야 한다는 것을 말이다.\n\n거처가 마련되자, 소피아는 그날 저녁만큼은 사촌과 함께 머물렀다. 그러나 이튿날 아침 일찍, 앞서 말한 대로 아버지의 집을 떠날 때 그 보호 아래로 몸을 던지기로 결심했던 그 부인의 행방을 수소문하기로 마음먹었다. 마차로 오는 동안 그녀가 눈여겨본 몇 가지 일들 때문에, 그녀는 이 일을 더욱 서두르고 싶어졌다.\n\n자, 우리는 소피아에게 의심이라는 그 가증스러운 성격을 결코 덧씌우고 싶지 않은 까닭에, 피츠패트릭 부인을 두고 그녀의 마음을 가득 채운 그 상상들을 독자에게 펼쳐 보이기가 거의 두렵다. 소피아가 지금 그녀에 대해 어떤 의혹을 품고 있었음은 분명하다. 그런데 그런 의혹은 가장 못된 사람들의 가슴속으로도 곧잘 비집고 들어오는 법이니, 우리는 의심이라는 것 자체에 대해 독자에게 먼저 한두 마디 운을 떼기 전에는 그것을 더 또렷이 말하지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 본다.\n\n이 의심에는 늘 두 가지 정도가 있는 것으로 내게는 보였다. 그 첫 번째 의심은 마음에서 비롯된 것으로 보고 싶다. 그 분별이 지나치게 빠른 것을 보면 어떤 미리 자리한 내면의 충동을 드러내는 듯하고, 더구나 이 극단적인 정도의 의심은 흔히 제 대상마저 스스로 만들어 내어, 있지도 않은 것을 보고, 언제나 실제보다 더 많은 것을 보기 때문이다. 이것이 바로 그 재빠른 통찰력이다. 그 매의 눈앞에서는 어떤 악의 징후도 빠져나가지 못하며, 사람들의 행동뿐 아니라 말과 표정까지 살핀다. 그리고 그것이 관찰하는 자의 마음에서 나오는 만큼, 관찰당하는 자의 마음속으로도 파고들어, 그 안에서 악을 이를테면 첫 배아 상태로, 아니, 때로는 잉태되었다고 말할 수도 없는 단계에서 찾아낸다. 만일 이것이 결코 틀리는 법이 없다면 참으로 놀라운 재능일 것이다. 그러나 이 정도의 완벽함은 단 한 분의 존재 말고는 그 누구도 자기 것이라 내세우지 못한다. 그러기에 이토록 날카로운 분별의 그릇됨에서, 죄 없고 덕 있는 이들에게 수많은 슬픈 재앙과 더없이 쓰라린 가슴앓이가 생겨났다. 그러므로 나는 악을 향한 이 엄청난 재빠른 시야를 어쩔 수 없이 악덕에 가까운 지나침으로, 또한 그 자체로 매우 해로운 악으로 여기게 된다. 그리고 나는 이 의견 쪽으로 더욱 기울게 되는데, 앞서 말한 이유들 때문에, 그리고 한 가지 더, 곧 나는 그것이 선한 마음의 속성인 적을 한 번도 본 적이 없는 까닭에, 그것이 늘 나쁜 마음에서 나오는 것이 아닐까 두렵기 때문이다. 자, 이 정도의 의심에서 나는 소피아를 온전히, 그리고 절대적으로 결백하다 인정한다.\n\n이 자질의 두 번째 등급은 머리에서 비롯되는 듯하다. 이것은 실로, 눈앞에 있는 것을 보고, 본 것으로부터 결론을 끌어내는 능력에 다름 아니다. 앞의 것은 눈이라는 것을 가진 사람이라면 누구도 피할 수 없고, 뒤의 것은 어쩌면 우리가 뇌라는 것을 가진 데서 비롯되는, 그에 못지않게 확실하고 필연적인 결과일 것이다. 이것은 앞의 자질이 순결함에게 더없이 모진 적이듯, 죄악에게도 꼭 그만큼 모진 적이다. 게다가 나로서는, 인간의 오류 가능성 탓에 그것이 때로 잘못 짚는 일이 있다 하더라도, 그것을 곱지 않은 눈으로 볼 수가 없다. 이를테면, 어떤 남편이 우연히 자기 아내가 오쟁이 지우는 기술을 업으로 삼는 저 잘생긴 젊은 신사들 가운데 누군가의 무릎 위에 있거나 그 품에 안겨 있는 것을 들켰을 때, 그가 실제로 본 그 친밀한 정경으로부터—그리고 우리가 적어도 너그럽게 보아 줄 때는 순진한 장난이라 부르는 그런 정경으로부터—본 것 이상의 무언가를 결론짓는다 하여, 나는 그를 그리 심하게 탓하지는 않으리라 생각한다. 독자라면 스스로 무수한 사례를 쉽게 떠올릴 것이다. 나는 다만 하나만 더 보태겠는데, 이는 어떤 이들에게는 비기독교적으로 여겨질지언정, 나로서는 엄격히 따져 정당하다고 여기지 않을 수 없는 것이다. 그것은 바로, 사람이란 이미 저지른 일을 또 저지를 수 있으며, 한 번 악당이었던 자는 같은 짓을 다시 할 수도 있다는 의심이다. 그리고 진실을 고백하자면, 소피아는 바로 이 등급의 의심에 빠져 있었던 듯하다. 실은 이 등급의 의심에서, 그녀는 자기 사촌이 정말로 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 나은 사람은 아니라는 생각을 품게 되었던 것이다.\n\n사정은 이러했던 것 같다. 피츠패트릭 부인은 현명하게도, 젊은 숙녀의 정조란 이 세상에서 가련한 토끼와 똑같은 처지에 놓여 있다고 여겼다. 토끼는 밖으로 나설 때마다 어김없이 제 적을 만나기 마련이니, 사실 적 말고는 좀처럼 다른 무엇을 만나기 어렵다. 그리하여 그녀는 남편의 보호에서 벗어날 첫 기회를 잡기로 마음먹자마자, 곧바로 다른 어떤 남자의 보호 아래로 몸을 던지기로 작정했다. 그렇다면 신분 있고, 재산 있고, 명예로운 인물, 게다가 남자를 기사도적 모험으로—다시 말해 곤경에 빠진 숙녀의 투사로—이끄는 그 늠름한 기질을 지녔을 뿐 아니라, 그녀 자신에게 격렬한 애정을 거듭 고백했고 이미 제 능력이 닿는 한 온갖 증표를 보여 준 인물보다, 누구를 더 적절한 보호자로 고를 수 있었겠는가?\n\n그러나 법이란 어리석게도 이 부부 대리인, 곧 가출한 숙녀의 보호자라는 직책을 빠뜨려 놓았고, 또 악의는 그를 한층 더 거슬리는 호칭으로 부르기 십상이었으므로, 그의 영주께서는 이 모든 친절한 직무를 숙녀에게 은밀히, 그녀의 보호자라는 역할을 공공연히 떠맡는 일 없이 수행하기로 결론지어졌다. 아니, 다른 누군가가 그를 그런 시선으로 보는 일을 막기 위해, 숙녀는 곧장 바스로 향하고, 영주께서는 먼저 런던으로 갔다가 거기서 주치의들의 권고에 따라 그곳으로 내려가기로 합의되었다.\n\n그런데 이 모든 것을 소피아는 아주 똑똑히 알아차렸으니, 피츠패트릭 부인의 입이나 거동에서가 아니라, 그 귀족에게서였다. 그는 그 착한 숙녀보다 비밀을 간직하는 데 한없이 서툴렀던 것이다. 그리고 어쩌면 피츠패트릭 부인이 자기 이야기 중에 이 대목에서만은 딱 잘라 비밀을 지켰던 바로 그 점이, 이제 사촌의 마음속에 솟아오른 그 의심을 적잖이 부추기는 데 한몫했을지도 모른다.\n\n소피아는 찾던 숙녀를 아주 쉽게 찾아냈다. 실로 이 도시에 그녀의 집을 훤히 모르는 가마꾼은 하나도 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 첫 전갈에 대한 답으로 더없이 간곡한 초대를 받자, 그녀는 곧바로 그것을 받아들였다. 사실 피츠패트릭 부인이 사촌에게 함께 머물러 달라고 청한 마음에는 예의가 요구하는 것 이상의 간절함은 없었다. 그녀가 앞서 말한 그 의심을 알아채고 언짢아한 것인지, 아니면 다른 어떤 동기에서 비롯된 것인지는 내가 말할 수 없다. 다만 분명한 것은, 소피아가 떠나기를 바라는 그만큼이나 부인도 소피아와 헤어지기를 바라고 있었다는 사실이다.\n\n이 젊은 숙녀는 사촌에게 작별을 고하러 왔을 때, 짤막한 충고 한마디를 건네지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 부인에게 제발 몸을 잘 챙기고, 자신이 얼마나 위태로운 처지에 서 있는지를 생각해 보라고 간청했다. 그러고는 남편과 화해할 어떤 방도가 찾아지기를 바란다고 덧붙였다. \"잊지 말아요, 언니.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"웨스턴 고모가 우리 둘에게 그토록 자주 되풀이하시던 그 격언 말이에요. 혼인의 결속이 깨지고 부부 사이에 전쟁이 선포되고 나면, 아내는 어떤 조건으로도 자신에게 불리하지 않은 화평을 맺기가 좀처럼 어렵다는 말이요. 이건 고모가 하신 바로 그 말씀이고, 고모는 세상 경험이 무척 많은 분이잖아요.\" 피츠패트릭 부인은 경멸 어린 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"내 걱정은 말고, 얘야, 너나 잘 챙기렴. 넌 나보다 어리잖니. 며칠 안에 너를 보러 갈게. 하지만 사랑하는 소피, 한 가지만 충고해 줄게. 그 새침데기 노릇은 시골에 두고 오렴. 정말이지, 이 도시에서는 그게 너한테 무척 어색하게 들러붙을 테니까.\"\n\n이렇게 두 사촌은 헤어졌고, 소피아는 곧장 벨러스턴 부인에게로 갔다. 거기서 그녀는 더없이 정중하면서도 더없이 진심 어린 환대를 받았다. 부인은 예전에 웨스턴 고모와 함께 있는 그녀를 보았을 때 그녀를 무척 마음에 들어 했던 터였다. 부인은 실로 그녀를 보고 몹시 반가워했고, 그녀가 지주를 떠나 런던으로 달아나게 된 사정을 알게 되자마자 그녀의 분별과 결단을 한껏 칭찬했다. 그리고 소피아가 제 집을 피난처로 택함으로써 부인에 대해 품고 있다고 밝힌 그 생각에 더없는 만족을 표한 뒤, 부인은 자기 힘으로 베풀 수 있는 모든 보호를 그녀에게 약속했다.\n\n이제 우리가 소피아를 안전한 손에 맡겨 두었으니, 독자께서는 잠시 그녀를 그곳에 내려놓고, 다른 인물들을, 특히 가련한 존스를 좀 살펴보는 데 흔쾌히 동의해 주리라 짐작한다. 우리는 그가 지난 잘못을 속죄하도록 충분히 오래 내버려 두었는데, 그 잘못이란 악덕의 본성이 으레 그러하듯, 제 스스로 그에게 넉넉한 벌을 안겨 준 것이었다.\n\n# BOOK XII.\n\n앞 권과 동일한 시간을 다룸.\n\n현대 작가에게서 무엇을 표절로 보아야 하고, 무엇을 정당한 노획물로 여겨야 하는지 보여 줌.\n\n박식한 독자라면 이 방대한 작업을 진행하는 동안, 내가 가장 뛰어난 고대 작가들의 구절을 자주 옮겨 오면서도 원문을 인용하지 않거나, 그 글을 빌려 온 책에 대해 일말의 언급조차 하지 않았음을 눈치챘을 것이다.\n\n글쓰기에서의 이러한 처신은, 박식한 바니에 신부가 그의 《신화》 서문에서—이 책은 대단한 학식과 그에 못지않은 판단력이 어우러진 노작이다—아주 적절하게 밝혀 두었다. 그는 이렇게 말한다. \"독자는 내가 종종 내 자신의 평판보다 그를 더 배려해 왔음을 어렵지 않게 알아차릴 것이다. 작가가, 독자를 위하여, 마침 손에 닿아 그저 베껴 적기만 하면 되었을 박식한 인용들을 억눌러 둔다면, 그것은 분명 독자에게 적잖은 경의를 표하는 일이기 때문이다.\"\n\n이런 자투리들로 한 권의 책을 채우는 일은, 실로 박식한 세상을 노골적으로 속이는 짓이라 볼 수 있다. 그들이 이미 통째로—기억에는 없을지언정 적어도 서가에는—갖고 있는 것을, 그런 수법에 넘어가 조각조각, 그것도 소매로 두 번째로 사들이게 되니 말이다. 게다가 배우지 못한 이들에게는 한층 더 잔인한 일이니, 그들은 자기들에게 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 것을 사도록 꾀임을 당하기 때문이다. 자기 작품에 그리스어와 라틴어를 잔뜩 뒤섞어 넣는 작가는, 경매인이 부인들과 멋쟁이 신사들을 다루는 것과 똑같이 치사한 방식으로 그들을 대하는 셈이다. 경매인은 흔히 물건 묶음을 일부러 헷갈리게 뒤섞어 놓아, 정작 원하는 물건을 사려면 아무 쓸모 없는 것까지 함께 사지 않을 수 없게 만들지 않던가.\n\n하지만 아무리 공정하고 사심 없는 처신이라 한들 무지에 오해받고 악의에 곡해되지 않는 법이 없으니, 나는 가끔 독자를 희생시켜 내 평판을 지켜 보고 싶은 유혹에 빠지기도 했다. 다른 사람의 생각이나 표현을 쓸 때마다 원문을 베껴 두거나, 적어도 몇 장 몇 절인지 그 출처를 밝혀 두고 싶었던 것이다. 실로 나는 그 반대의 방식 때문에 종종 손해를 보아 온 것이 아닌가 의심하기도 한다. 원작자의 이름을 숨김으로써, 그 정당하게 이름 높은 프랑스인이 일러 준 갸륵한 동기에서 그리하는 것으로 인정받기보다는 오히려 표절을 의심받아 온 것이 아닌가 하고 말이다.\n\n이제 앞으로 그런 모든 혐의를 미리 막아 두기 위하여, 나는 여기에서 그 사실을 고백하고 또 정당화하고자 한다. 고대인이란 풍요로운 공유지라 볼 수 있으니, 파르나소스에 손바닥만 한 땅뙈기라도 가진 사람이면 누구나 그곳에서 제 뮤즈를 마음껏 살찌울 자유로운 권리가 있다. 더 또렷하게 말해 보자면, 우리 현대인은 고대인에 대하여 가난한 자가 부자에 대한 관계와 같다. 여기서 가난한 자라 함은, 영어로 우리가 군중이라 부르는 저 거대하고 유서 깊은 무리를 두고 하는 말이다. 그런데 이 군중과 얼마간이라도 가까이 지내는 영예를 누려 본 사람이라면, 부유한 이웃을 일말의 망설임도 없이 약탈하고 노략질하는 것이 그들의 확립된 신조 가운데 하나임을 잘 알 것이다. 그리고 그것이 그들 사이에서는 죄도 수치도 아닌 것으로 여겨진다는 사실 또한 알 것이다. 그들이 이 신조를 어찌나 한결같이 지키고 따르는지, 이 왕국의 거의 모든 교구에는 향사라 불리는 어떤 부유한 인물을 상대로 늘 이어지는 일종의 결탁이 있을 정도다. 그의 재산은 가난한 이웃 모두에게 자유로운 노획물로 여겨지며, 그들은 그런 약탈에 아무런 죄도 없다고 결론짓는 까닭에, 그런 일이 있을 때마다 서로를 숨겨 주고 처벌에서 지켜 주는 것을 명예의 한 점이자 도덕적 의무로 여긴다.\n\n이와 마찬가지로, 호메로스, 베르길리우스, 호라티우스, 키케로, 그 밖의 고대인들은 우리 작가들 사이에서 수많은 부유한 향사들로 받들어 마땅하다. 우리, 곧 파르나소스의 가난한 자들은 그들에게서 손에 닿는 것이면 무엇이든 가져가는 것을 까마득한 옛날부터의 관습으로 주장하는 것이다. 나는 이 자유를 요구하며, 또한 가난한 이웃들에게도 그들 차례가 되면 똑같이 기꺼이 허락하려 한다. 내가 공언하는 전부, 그리고 내가 동료들에게 요구하는 전부는, 군중이 서로에게 지키는 것과 똑같이 엄격한 정직함을 우리끼리 지키자는 것이다. 서로의 것을 훔치는 일은 실로 지극히 죄스럽고 점잖지 못한 짓이니, 이것이야말로 엄밀히 말해 가난한 자를(어쩌면 때로는 우리 자신보다 더 가난한 자를) 등쳐 먹는 짓이라 부를 만하며, 가장 치욕스러운 빛깔로 칠하자면 자선병원을 터는 짓이라 하겠다.\n\n그러므로 아무리 엄정하게 따져 보아도 내 양심이 그런 비루한 도둑질을 내게 씌울 수 없는 이상, 나는 앞서의 혐의에 대해서는 기꺼이 유죄를 인정한다. 그리고 어느 고대 작가에게서 내 목적에 맞는 구절을 발견하면, 그것을 가져온 작가의 이름을 적어 두지 않고 내 것으로 삼는 데 결코 거리낌이 없을 것이다. 아니, 나는 그런 모든 사상이 내 글 속에 옮겨 적히는 순간 그것에 대한 소유권을 절대적으로 주장하며, 앞으로 모든 독자가 그것을 순전히 또 온전히 내 것으로 여겨 주기를 바란다. 다만 이 주장이 내게 허락되기를 청하는 데에는 한 가지 조건이 있으니, 내가 가난한 동료들에게 엄격한 정직함을 지킨다는 것이다. 그들이 가진 그 보잘것없는 것 가운데 무엇이라도 빌려 올 때면, 나는 반드시 그 위에 그들의 표를 찍어 두어, 언제든 정당한 주인에게 돌려줄 수 있도록 할 것이다.\n\n이것을 빠뜨린 일로 무어라는 자는 크게 비난받아 마땅했다. 그는 전에 포프와 그 일행에게서 몇 줄을 빌려 와 놓고, 그중 여섯 줄을 제 희곡 《경쟁하는 유행》에 멋대로 옮겨 적었다. 그러나 포프 씨는 천만다행으로 그 줄들을 그 희곡 안에서 찾아내고는, 제 재산에 거센 손길을 뻗어 다시금 제 작품 속으로 되가져왔다. 그리고 한층 더한 벌로, 그 무어를 《던시어드》라는 역겨운 지하 감옥에 가두어 버렸으니, 그의 가련한 기억은 이제 그곳에 남아 있고, 또 영원히 남아 있을 것이다. 시문학의 거래에서 저지른 그런 부당한 처사에 대한 마땅한 벌로서 말이다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n이 장에서, 지주는 딸을 찾지는 못하지만, 그의 추격에 종지부를 찍는 무언가가 발견된다.\n\n이제 이야기는 업튼의 여관으로 되돌아간다. 거기서 우리는 먼저 웨스턴 지주의 발자취를 따라가 보려 한다. 그는 곧 여정의 끝에 다다를 터이니, 그 뒤에는 우리의 주인공에게 충분히 여유롭게 마음을 쏟을 수 있을 것이다.\n\n앞서 그 지주가 잔뜩 성이 나서 여관을 떠났고, 그 분노에 휩싸인 채 딸을 뒤쫓았다는 것을 독자는 부디 기억해 주기 바란다. 마구간지기가 딸이 세번강을 건넜다고 일러 주자, 그 역시 일행을 거느리고 강을 건넜고, 가엾은 소피아를 따라잡기만 한다면 더없이 매서운 복수를 퍼붓겠노라 맹세하며 전속력으로 말을 몰았다.\n\n얼마 가지 못해 그는 갈림길에 이르렀다. 여기서 그는 짧은 작전 회의를 열었는데, 여러 의견을 다 들은 끝에 마침내 추격의 방향을 운명에 맡기기로 하고, 곧장 우스터로 가는 길로 들어섰다.\n\n이 길을 한 이 마일쯤 나아갔을 때, 그는 자기 신세를 더없이 쓰라리게 한탄하기 시작했다. \"이런 가엾을 데가! 정녕 나만큼 운수 사나운 개 같은 놈은 없었을 게다!\" 하고 연방 외쳐 대더니, 이내 욕설과 저주를 한바탕 쏟아 냈다.\n\n목사는 이 자리에서 그를 위로해 보려 했다. \"슬퍼하지 마십시오, 나리.\" 그가 말했다. \"희망 없는 자들처럼 슬퍼하지는 마십시오. 비록 우리가 아직 젊은 아씨를 따라잡지는 못하였으나, 여태껏 그 자취를 바르게 좇아온 것만으로도 어느 정도 다행한 일로 여길 수 있습니다. 어쩌면 아씨께서 곧 여정에 지쳐, 몸의 기력을 되살리려 어느 여관에 머무를지도 모릅니다. 그리되면, 도덕적으로 거의 확실하게도, 나리께서는 머지않아 소원을 이루실 것입니다.\"\n* 콤포스 보티(compos voti): 라틴어로 \"소원을 이룬 자\"라는 뜻.\n\n\"풋! 빌어먹을 계집!\" 지주가 대꾸했다. \"내가 한탄하는 건 이 좋은 아침을 사냥도 못 하고 날려 버린 일이오. 이번 철 들어 보아하니 가장 좋은 냄새 맡기 날 중 하루를, 그것도 그 긴 서리 끝에 이렇게 놓치다니, 분통 터질 노릇이지 뭐요.\"\n\n이따금 가장 짓궂은 장난 속에서도 얼마간 자비를 베푸는 운명의 여신이, 혹 이 지주를 가엾이 여겼는지, 그리하여 그가 딸을 따라잡지는 못하게 작정해 놓고도 다른 방식으로 그에게 보상해 주기로 마음먹은 것은 아닌지, 나는 단언하지 않겠다. 그러나 그가 방금 적은 그 말을 채 내뱉기가 무섭게, 그 뒤로 두세 마디 욕설을 줄줄이 붙이자마자, 그들로부터 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 한 무리의 사냥개들이 그 가락진 목청을 트기 시작했다. 지주의 말과 그 위에 탄 사람이 둘 다 이를 알아채고는, 둘 다 즉시 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다. 지주는 \"갔다, 갔어! 안 갔으면 내 목을 내놓겠다!\" 하고 외치며, 곧장 짐승에게 박차를 가했다. 짐승은 박차가 거의 필요치도 않았으니, 실로 제 주인과 똑같은 마음이었던 것이다. 이제 일행 전부가 옥수수밭으로 가로질러 들어, 떠들썩하게 고함치고 함성을 지르며 곧장 사냥개들을 향해 말을 몰았고, 그 와중에 가엾은 목사는 성호를 그으며 맨 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n이렇게 우화는 전한다. 어느 열렬한 연인의 청에 따라 비너스가 고양이에서 어여쁜 여인으로 바꾸어 준 아리따운 그리말킨은, 생쥐 한 마리를 보자마자 옛 놀이를 떠올리고 본래의 천성을 그대로 간직한 터라, 남편의 잠자리에서 펄쩍 뛰쳐나와 그 작은 짐승을 뒤쫓았다고.\n\n이것을 우리는 어떻게 이해해야 할까? 신부가 사랑에 겨운 신랑의 포옹을 싫어했다는 뜻은 아니다. 더러 고양이가 배은망덕하기 쉽다고들 하지만, 여인도 고양이도 어떤 때에는 흡족해하며 가르랑거리는 법이니까. 진실은, 명민한 로저 레스트레인지 경이 그 깊은 사색 속에서 말한 그대로다. \"자연을 문으로 내쫓으면, 그것은 창으로 들어온다. 그리고 고양이란, 비록 귀부인이 되었어도 여전히 쥐잡이일 것이다.\" 같은 식으로, 우리는 지주가 딸을 향한 사랑이 모자랐다고 책망해서는 안 된다. 사실 그에게는 사랑이 넘치도록 많았으니까. 다만 그가 지주이자 사냥꾼이었음을 헤아리면, 우리는 그 우화를, 그리고 그 사려 깊은 성찰까지도 그에게 적용해 볼 수 있을 것이다.\n\n사냥개들은, 그들 말마따나 무척 세차게 내달렸고, 지주는 산울타리와 도랑을 넘으며 여느 때와 다름없는 고함과 민첩함으로, 또 여느 때와 다름없는 즐거움으로 그 뒤를 쫓았다. 소피아에 대한 생각은 그가 사냥에서 누리는 만족을 가시게 하려고 단 한 번도 끼어들지 않았다. 그는 이것이 여태 본 사냥 중에서도 손꼽히게 멋진 것이라 했고, 오십 마일을 달려와도 아깝지 않을 만큼 값진 것이라 맹세했다. 지주가 딸을 잊었으니, 하인들이 안주인을 잊었으리라는 것도 우리는 쉽사리 믿을 수 있다. 목사 또한, 혼자서 라틴어로 한참 경탄을 늘어놓은 끝에 마침내 젊은 아씨에 대한 더 이상의 생각을 죄다 내려놓고, 저만치 뒤에서 느릿느릿 따라가며 다가오는 일요일을 위한 설교 한 토막을 궁리하기 시작했다.\n\n그 사냥개들의 주인인 지주는, 같은 지주이자 사냥꾼인 동무가 끼어든 것을 무척 기뻐했다. 사람이란 누구나 제 분야에서 솜씨를 알아보는 법인데, 들판에서 웨스턴 씨보다 노련한 사람은 없었고, 목소리로 개들을 북돋고 고함으로 사냥을 달구는 일에서도 그를 능가할 자가 없었던 것이다.\n\n사냥꾼들은 추격의 열기에 휩싸이면 어떤 예법에도, 아니 사람으로서 마땅히 챙겨야 할 도리에조차 마음 쓸 겨를이 없을 만큼 빠져 든다. 누군가가 도랑이나 강에 곤두박질치는 사고를 당해도, 나머지는 아랑곳없이 지나쳐 가며 대개는 그를 제 운명에 맡겨 둔다. 그러므로 그동안 두 지주는, 서로 가까이 붙어 있을 때가 많았어도 단 한 마디도 주고받지 않았다. 그래도 사냥의 주인은, 개들이 냄새를 놓쳤을 때 그 낯선 이가 개들을 끌어 모으는 빼어난 판단을 여러 번 보고 감탄하여, 그의 식견을 매우 높이 사게 되었다. 게다가 그를 따르는 수행원의 수는 그의 지체에 대한 적잖은 경의를 불러일으켰다. 그리하여 추격을 일으킨 그 작은 짐승이 죽음으로써 사냥이 끝나자마자, 두 지주는 마주하여 지주다운 격식을 한껏 갖추며 서로 인사를 나누었다.\n\n두 사람의 대화는 제법 재미있었으니, 어쩌면 부록에서나 다른 기회에 옮겨 볼 수도 있겠다. 다만 이 이야기와는 아무 상관이 없으므로, 여기에 그것을 끼워 넣을 마음은 도무지 들지 않는다. 대화는 두 번째 사냥으로 마무리되었고, 그것은 다시 저녁 식사 초대로 이어졌다. 이 초대를 받아들이자 거나한 술자리가 뒤따랐는데, 그 술자리는 웨스턴 지주 쪽에서 똑같이 거나한 낮잠으로 끝이 났다.\n\n우리의 지주는 그날 저녁 술잔치에서 주인에게도, 서플 목사에게도 도무지 상대가 되지 못했다. 다만 이는, 그가 겪은 몸과 마음의 극심한 피로로 충분히 설명되는 일이니, 그의 명예에는 조금도 누가 되지 않는다. 그는 실로, 흔한 말로 하자면 휘파람도 못 불 만큼 곯아떨어졌다. 세 병째를 채 비우기도 전에 완전히 인사불성이 된 것이다. 그가 잠자리로 옮겨진 것은 한참 뒤였지만, 목사는 그를 이미 자리에 없는 사람으로 여기고, 다른 지주에게 소피아에 관한 일을 죄다 일러 준 뒤, 이튿날 아침 웨스턴 씨를 집으로 돌려보내려 펼칠 논리에 힘을 보태 주겠다는 약속을 받아 냈다.\n\n그리하여 마음씨 좋은 지주가 저녁 술기운을 떨쳐 내고, 아침 해장술을 청하며 추격을 다시 시작하려 말을 대령하라 이르기가 무섭게, 서플 씨가 만류하기 시작했다. 주인 또한 어찌나 거들었던지, 마침내 그 설득이 먹혀들어 웨스턴 씨는 집으로 돌아가기로 했다. 그를 가장 크게 움직인 논거는 한 가지였으니, 곧 그가 어디로 가야 할지 알 수 없는 터라, 딸 쪽으로 가기는커녕 오히려 딸에게서 더 멀어지는 쪽으로 말을 몰고 있는지도 모른다는 것이었다. 이윽고 그는 동료 사냥꾼에게 작별을 고하고, 서리가 풀린 것을 크게 기뻐하며(어쩌면 이것이야말로 그가 서둘러 귀가하게 만든 적잖은 동기였을 것이다) 서머싯셔를 향해 앞으로, 아니 차라리 뒤로 출발했다. 다만 그에 앞서, 먼저 수행원 일부를 딸을 찾아 떠나보냈고, 그 뒤를 좇아 자기가 지어낼 수 있는 가장 쓰디쓴 저주를 한바탕 함께 날려 보냈다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n3장.\n존스가 업턴을 떠나는 일과, 길 위에서 그와 파트리지 사이에 오간 이야기.\n\n마침내 우리는 다시 한번 우리의 주인공에게로 돌아왔다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그와 너무 오래 떨어져 있을 수밖에 없었던 터라, 그를 두고 떠나올 때의 처지를 생각하면, 독자들 가운데 많은 이가 우리가 그를 영영 내버려 둘 작정이었다고 결론지었으리라 짐작된다. 지금 그가 처한 상황이란, 분별 있는 사람들이라면 더 이상 친구의 안부를 캐묻기를 그만두는 그런 상황이기 때문이다. 행여 그런 친구가 목을 매었다는 소식이라도 듣고 충격받을까 두려워서 말이다.\n\n그러나 실은, 우리에게 분별 있는 사람의 미덕이 다 갖춰져 있지는 않을지언정, 그 악덕 또한 다 갖춰져 있지는 않다고 나는 당당히 말하겠다. 지금 가엾은 존스의 처지보다 훨씬 더 비참한 상황을 떠올리기란 쉽지 않지만, 그래도 우리는 그에게로 돌아가 그를 살필 것이다. 마치 그가 가장 눈부신 행운의 햇살 속에서 마음껏 노닐고 있기라도 한 것처럼, 똑같은 정성을 다해서 말이다.\n\n그리하여 존스 군과 그의 동행 파트리지는, 웨스턴 영주가 떠난 지 몇 분 지나지 않아 여관을 나서서 같은 길을 걸어 나아갔다. 마구간지기가 그들에게, 지금 업턴에서는 무슨 수를 써도 말을 구할 수 없다고 일러 준 까닭이었다. 두 사람은 무거운 마음을 안고 걸음을 옮겼다. 비록 그 심란함이 서로 전혀 다른 이유에서 비롯된 것이긴 했어도, 어쨌든 둘 다 언짢기는 매한가지였다. 존스가 쓰디쓴 한숨을 내쉬면, 파트리지는 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 그에 못지않게 구슬프게 끙끙거렸다.\n\n영주가 어느 길로 갈지 의논하느라 멈춰 섰던 그 갈림길에 이르자, 존스도 걸음을 멈추고 파트리지를 돌아보며 어느 길로 가야 할지 의견을 물었다. \"아, 나리,\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"나리께서 제 충고를 따라 주셨으면 합니다.\" \"내가 따르지 못할 까닭이 무엇이냐?\" 존스가 대꾸했다. \"이제 내가 어디로 가든, 내 신세가 어찌 되든 아무 상관도 없으니 말이다.\" \"그러시다면 제 충고는,\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"당장 발길을 돌려 집으로 돌아가시라는 것입니다. 나리처럼 돌아갈 만한 집이 있는 분이라면, 어찌 이렇게 떠돌이처럼 온 나라를 헤매고 다니시겠습니까? 용서하십시오, 다만 그 말밖에는 달리 떠오르질 않아서요.\"\n\n\"아아!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"나에게는 돌아갈 집이 없다. 설령 내 친구이자 아버지 같은 그분이 나를 받아 주신다 한들, 소피아가 떠나 버린 그 고장을 내가 어찌 견딜 수 있겠느냐? 모진 소피아! 모질구나! 아니, 나 자신을 탓하자! 아니, 너를 탓하자. 빌어먹을 놈, 멍청이, 돌대가리 같으니! 네가 나를 망쳐 놓았다. 내 너의 몸뚱이에서 영혼을 뽑아내고 말겠다.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 가엾은 파트리지의 멱살을 거칠게 움켜쥐고는, 학질의 발작이나 그 자신의 공포가 일찍이 그를 떨게 한 것보다도 더 세차게 흔들어 댔다.\n\n파트리지는 부들부들 떨며 무릎을 꿇고 자비를 구하면서, 결코 해를 끼칠 뜻은 없었노라 맹세했다. 그러자 존스는 잠시 그를 사납게 노려보더니, 움켜쥔 손을 놓고는 자기 자신에게 분노를 쏟아부었다. 만일 그 분노가 상대에게 떨어졌더라면 그의 목숨을 끝장내고도 남았을 정도였으니, 실은 그러리라는 두려움만으로도 파트리지는 거의 숨이 넘어갈 뻔했다.\n\n여기서 우리는 이때 존스가 부린 그 온갖 미친 짓을 일일이 세세하게 묘사하느라 적잖은 공을 들일 수도 있겠다. 독자가 그것을 읽는 데도 똑같은 공을 들여 주리라 확신만 할 수 있다면 말이다. 하지만 우리가 이 장면을 그려 내느라 온갖 노고를 다 기울인들, 그 독자는 그 대목을 통째로 건너뛰어 버리기 십상이리라는 걱정이 들기에, 우리는 아예 그 수고를 덜기로 했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 바로 이 한 가지 이유 때문에 우리는 우리 재능의 무성함을 종종 크게 억눌러 왔고, 그러지 않았더라면 작품 속에 들어갔을 훌륭한 묘사를 여럿 빼 버리곤 했다. 그리고 솔직히 말해, 이런 의심은 흔히 그렇듯 우리 자신의 못된 마음에서 비롯된 것이다. 우리 또한 방대한 역사가들의 책장을 넘기며, 끔찍하게도 건너뛰기에 빠져들곤 했던 적이 한두 번이 아니기 때문이다.\n\n그러니 그저 이렇게만 말해 두자. 존스는 여러 분 동안 미치광이 노릇을 한 끝에, 차츰 제정신을 되찾았다. 그러자마자 그는 파트리지를 돌아보며, 격정에 사로잡혀 그에게 손찌검한 일을 진심으로 용서해 달라고 빌었다. 그러나 끝에 가서는, 다시는 집으로 돌아가자는 말을 꺼내지 말라고 당부했다. 그 고장은 두 번 다시 보지 않기로 마음을 굳혔기 때문이었다.\n\n파트리지는 선뜻 용서하고는, 이제 자기에게 내려진 그 분부를 충실히 따르겠노라 굳게 약속했다. 그러자 존스가 아주 활기차게 외쳤다. \"내 천사의 발자취를 더는 좇을 길이 도무지 없으니, 이제 나는 영광의 발자취를 좇으리라. 자, 가자, 용감한 친구야, 이제 군대로 가는 거다. 그것은 영광스러운 대의이니, 비록 내 목숨이 지킬 만한 값어치가 있다 해도 나는 기꺼이 그 대의에 그것을 바치겠다.\" 이렇게 말하고 그는 곧장 영주가 택한 길과는 다른 길로 접어들었는데, 순전히 우연으로, 앞서 소피아가 지나간 바로 그 길을 따라가게 되었다.\n\n이제 우리의 나그네들은 서로 한마디도 주고받지 않은 채 꼬박 일 마일을 걸었다. 물론 존스는 혼자서 이런저런 말을 중얼거리기는 했다. 파트리지로 말하자면, 그는 깊은 침묵에 잠겨 있었다. 어쩌면 앞서 받은 놀라움에서 아직 완전히 헤어나지 못한 탓이었으리라. 게다가 그는 친구를 두 번째 분노의 발작으로 몰아넣을까 봐 겁이 났는데, 특히 이제 한 가지 생각을 품기 시작한 터라 더욱 그랬다. 이 생각은 독자에게 그리 큰 놀라움을 자아내지는 않을 것이다. 요컨대 그는 이제 존스가 완전히 정신이 나가 버린 것이 아닌가 의심하기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n마침내 존스는 혼잣말에 싫증이 나서 동행에게 말을 건네며, 그의 과묵함을 나무랐다. 그러자 가엾은 사내는 아주 솔직하게, 노여움을 살까 두려워서 그랬노라고 해명했다. 이제 결코 책망하지 않겠다는 더없이 확고한 약속으로 그 두려움이 어지간히 가시자, 파트리지는 다시 자기 혀에서 고삐를 풀었다. 그 혀는, 목에서 고삐가 벗겨져 풀밭으로 풀려난 어린 망아지 못지않게, 자유를 되찾은 것을 기뻐했으리라.\n\n파트리지는 가장 먼저 떠올랐을 그 화제를 입에 올리지 못하게 된 터라, 마음속에서 그다음으로 떠오른 화제로 옮겨 갔으니, 바로 산속의 사내 이야기였다. \"정말이지요, 나리,\" 그가 말했다. \"그렇게 괴상한 차림으로, 그렇게 이상하게, 여느 사람들과는 영 딴판으로 살아가는 자가 사람일 리가 없습니다. 게다가 그 노파가 제게 말하기를, 그자가 먹는 것은 주로 풀이라던데, 그건 기독교인보다 말에게나 어울릴 먹이지요. 아니, 업턴의 여관 주인 말로는, 그 근방 이웃들이 그자에 대해 아주 무시무시한 생각을 품고 있답니다. 제 머릿속에는 이상하게도, 그게 어떤 혼령이었음에 틀림없다는 생각이 자꾸만 맴돕니다. 어쩌면 우리에게 미리 경고하러 보내진 혼령인지도 모르지요. 그자가 우리에게 들려준 그 모든 이야기, 싸우러 갔다는 것이며 포로로 잡혔다는 것이며 교수형에 처해질 큰 위험에 놓였다는 것이, 우리가 지금 하려는 일을 생각하면 우리에게 주는 경고로 의도된 것인지 누가 알겠습니까? 게다가 지난밤 내내 저는 싸우는 꿈만 꾸었습니다. 그것도 제 코에서 술통 꼭지에서 술이 쏟아지듯 피가 흘러나오는 꿈이었지요. 정말이지요, 나리, 여왕이시여, 차마 입에 담지 못할 슬픔을 되살리라 명하시는군요.\"\n\n\"네 이야기는, 파트리지,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"네 라틴어만큼이나 영 어울리지 않게 갖다 붙인 거다. 전장에 나가는 사람에게 죽음보다 더 일어남 직한 일이 어디 있겠느냐. 어쩌면 우리 둘 다 거기서 쓰러질지도 모르지. 그래서 어떻단 말이냐?\" \"그래서 어떻단 말이냐고요?\" 파트리지가 받아쳤다. \"그야 그러면 우리는 그걸로 끝이지요, 안 그렇습니까? 제가 가 버리면 저로서는 모든 게 끝장입니다. 제가 죽고 나면 그 대의가 저에게 무슨 상관이며, 누가 승리를 거두든 저에게 무슨 상관이겠습니까? 저는 거기서 아무런 득도 누리지 못할 텐데요. 종을 울리고 모닥불을 피운들, 땅속 여섯 자 아래 묻힌 사람에게 그게 다 무슨 소용입니까? 가엾은 파트리지는 그걸로 끝장이지요.\" \"가엾은 파트리지도 끝장이 나야겠지.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"언젠가 한 번은. 네가 라틴어를 그토록 좋아한다면, 호라티우스의 멋진 시구를 몇 줄 읊어 주마. 겁쟁이에게도 용기를 불어넣어 줄 시구다.\n조국을 위해 죽는 것은 감미롭고 명예로운 일이라.\n\n죽음은 달아나는 자도 끝까지 쫓아가나니,\n전쟁을 두려워하는 젊은이의 오금도,\n\n겁먹고 돌아선 등짝도 봐주지 않는다.\"\n\"그걸 좀 풀어 주셨으면 합니다.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"호라티우스는 어려운 작가라, 나리께서 그렇게 읊으시기만 해서는 도무지 알아들을 수가 없거든요.\"\n\n\"내 솜씨로 서투르게 흉내 낸 것, 아니 풀어 옮긴 것을 읊어 주마.\" 존스가 말했다. \"나야 변변찮은 시인에 불과하니 말이다.\n\n사랑하는 조국의 대의를 위해 죽기를 마다할 자 누구랴? 비천한 두려움에 못난 발걸음을 물린다 한들, 죽음으로부터 달아나지는 못하리니. 마침내 하나의 무덤이 겁쟁이와 용사를 똑같이 거두어들이리라.\"\n\n\"그거야 아주 분명한 사실이지요.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"암요, 그럼요, 죽음은 누구에게나 공평하지요. 하지만 지금부터 한참 세월이 흐른 뒤에 침대에 누워, 착한 기독교인답게, 온 벗들이 곁에서 울어 주는 가운데 죽는 것과, 오늘내일 미친개처럼 총에 맞아 죽거나, 어쩌면 칼에 스무 토막으로 난도질당해 죽는 것, 그것도 우리가 우리의 온갖 죄를 뉘우치기도 전에 그리되는 것 사이에는 엄청난 차이가 있습니다. 오, 주여, 저희를 불쌍히 여기소서! 정말이지 군인이란 사악한 부류의 인간들입니다. 저는 그자들과 무슨 상관이라도 얽히는 걸 도무지 좋아한 적이 없습니다. 그자들을 기독교인이라 여기는 것조차 저로서는 좀처럼 어려운 일이었지요. 그자들 사이에는 욕설과 악담밖에 없으니까요. 나리께서 부디 뉘우치셨으면 합니다. 너무 늦기 전에 부디 뉘우치시고, 그자들 틈에 끼어들 생각일랑 마시기를 진심으로 바랍니다. 나쁜 사귐은 좋은 품행을 망치는 법이니까요. 그게 제 가장 큰 이유입니다. 그 점에 대해 말하자면, 저도 다른 사람만큼은 겁이 없습니다, 정말이지요. 그 점에 관해서는요. 인간의 육신이란 다 죽기 마련임을 저도 압니다. 하지만 그래도 사람이 여러 해를 살 수도 있는 것 아닙니까. 아니, 저도 이제 중년에 접어든 사람이지만, 그래도 앞으로 숱한 세월을 더 살 수 있을지도 모릅니다. 저는 백 살을 넘기도록 산 사람들 이야기를 여럿 읽었고, 더러는 백 살을 훨씬 넘긴 이들도 있었지요. 그렇다고 제가 그런 나이까지 살기를 바란다거나, 저 자신에게 그러리라 장담한다는 건 아닙니다만. 하지만 여든이나 아흔까지만이라도 말이지요. 하늘이여 찬미받으소서, 그건 아직 까마득히 먼 일입니다. 그때 가서 죽는 거라면 저도 다른 사람 못지않게 두렵지 않습니다. 하지만 분명, 자기 때가 오기도 전에 죽음을 자초하는 것은 제가 보기에 그야말로 사악하고 주제넘은 짓입니다. 게다가 그게 정말로 무슨 보탬이라도 된다면 또 모르지요. 하지만 대의가 무엇이든 간에, 사람 둘이 무슨 대단한 보탬이 될 수 있겠습니까? 그리고 저로 말하자면, 그런 일은 아무것도 모릅니다. 저는 평생 총을 열 번 넘게 쏴 본 적도 없고, 그나마도 총알을 잰 것은 아니었지요. 칼로 말하자면, 검술이라곤 배워 본 적도 없으니 그 방면은 전혀 모릅니다. 그리고 또 저 대포라는 게 있는데, 그 앞으로 뛰어드는 건 분명 더없이 주제넘은 짓이라 여길 만하지요. 미치광이가 아니고서야 누가 그런 짓을 하겠습니까. 용서하십시오, 맹세코 해를 끼칠 뜻은 없었습니다. 부디 제가 나리를 또다시 분노에 빠뜨리지 않기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"걱정 마라, 파트리지.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"나는 이제 네 비겁함을 어찌나 확실히 알게 되었는지, 네가 무슨 짓을 해도 나를 화나게 할 수는 없을 것이다.\" \"나리,\" 그가 대답했다. \"저를 겁쟁이라 부르시든, 그 밖에 무엇이라 부르시든 마음대로 하십시오. 성한 살가죽으로 잠자기를 좋아하는 것이 사람을 겁쟁이로 만든다면, 우리는 누구도 그 결점에서 자유롭지 못하지요. 저는 문법책 어디에서도 싸우지 않고서는 좋은 사람이 될 수 없다는 구절을 읽어 본 적이 없습니다. 누가 좋은 사람인가? 원로원의 결의와 법과 정의를 지키는 사람이라. 싸움에 대해서는 한마디도 없지 않습니까. 그리고 성경 또한 그것을 어찌나 반대하는지, 어떤 사람이 기독교인의 피를 흘리면서도 자기가 착한 기독교인이라고 저를 설득하려 든들, 저는 결코 넘어가지 않을 것입니다.\"\n\n거지를 만난 일.\n\n파트리지가 앞 장을 맺은 그 선하고 경건한 가르침을 막 늘어놓았을 때, 그들은 또 다른 갈림길에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 누더기를 걸친 한 절름발이가 다가와 적선을 청했다. 그러자 파트리지가 그를 호되게 나무라며 말했다. \"교구마다 제 가난한 사람은 제가 거두어야 마땅하지.\" 그 말에 존스는 웃음을 터뜨리며 파트리지에게 물었다. \"입으로는 그토록 자선을 떠들면서, 정작 마음에는 자선이 없는 것이 부끄럽지 않은가. 자네의 신앙은,\" 그가 말했다. \"자네 허물의 핑곗거리로나 쓰일 뿐, 자네의 덕을 북돋우는 데는 아무 쓸모가 없군. 정말로 그리스도인이라 하는 자라면, 이토록 비참한 처지에 놓인 제 형제 하나를 구제하는 일을 어찌 마다할 수 있겠나?\" 그러면서 동시에 호주머니에 손을 넣어, 그 가엾은 이에게 1실링을 건넸다.\n\n\"나리,\" 사내가 고맙다고 인사한 뒤 외쳤다. \"제 호주머니에 진기한 물건이 하나 있습니다요. 한 2마일쯤 떨어진 데서 주운 것인데, 나리께서 사 주실 마음이 있으시다면요. 아무한테나 꺼내 보일 물건은 아닙니다만, 나리께서 이리 훌륭하신 신사이시고, 가난한 사람에게도 이리 친절하시니, 그저 가난하다는 이유만으로 사람을 도둑으로 의심하지는 않으시겠지요.\" 그러고는 작은 금박 수첩 하나를 꺼내어 존스의 손에 건넸다.\n\n존스는 곧바로 수첩을 펼쳤고, 첫 장에서 (독자여, 그가 어떤 심정이었을지 짐작해 보라) 소피아 웨스턴이라는 글자가, 바로 그녀의 고운 손으로 쓰인 것을 보았다. 그 이름을 읽자마자 그는 수첩을 입술에 바싹 갖다 댔다. 일행이 곁에 있는데도 그는 자못 미친 듯한 황홀경에 빠져드는 것을 도무지 어쩌지 못했다. 하지만 어쩌면 그 황홀경 자체가, 자기가 혼자가 아니라는 사실을 잊게 만들었는지도 모른다.\n\n존스가 마치 입안에 노릇하게 구운 버터 바른 빵 껍질이라도 문 듯이, 혹은 정말로 책벌레라도 된 듯이, 아니면 제 작품 말고는 먹을 것이라곤 없는 작가라도 된 듯이 그 수첩에 입을 맞추고 우물거리는 동안, 종이 한 장이 책장 사이에서 땅으로 떨어졌다. 파트리지가 그것을 주워 존스에게 건넸고, 존스는 곧 그것이 은행권임을 알아보았다. 그것은 실로 웨스턴이 딸이 떠나기 전날 밤에 건넨 바로 그 어음이었다. 유대인이라면 100파운드에서 5실링 모자란 값에라도 냉큼 사들이려 덤볐을 물건이었다.\n\n존스가 이제 큰 소리로 알린 이 소식에 파트리지의 눈이 반짝였다. 그리고 (다소 다른 빛깔이긴 했으나) 그 수첩을 주운 가엾은 사내의 눈도 반짝였다. 그는 (정직함이라는 신조에서였기를 바라거니와) 수첩을 한 번도 펼쳐 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 여기서 다소 중요할 수 있는 한 가지 사정을 독자에게 알리지 않는다면 우리는 독자를 정직하게 대하는 것이 아닐 터이니, 곧 그 사내는 글을 읽을 줄 몰랐다는 사실이다.\n\n수첩을 찾은 일에서 순전한 기쁨과 환희밖에 느끼지 못했던 존스는, 이 새로운 발견에 한 가닥 근심이 뒤섞이는 것을 느꼈다. 그 어음의 주인이, 자기가 그것을 그녀에게 전해 줄 수 있게 되기도 전에 어쩌면 그것이 필요해질지 모른다는 생각이 곧장 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 수첩을 주운 사내에게, 이 수첩이 누구의 것인지 자기가 안다고, 되도록 빨리 그녀를 찾아내어 돌려주겠다고 일러 주었다.\n\n그 수첩은 얼마 전 웨스턴 부인이 조카딸에게 선물한 것이었다. 이름난 장신구 상인에게서 산 것으로 값이 25실링이었으나, 그 죔쇠에 들어간 은의 실제 가치는 1실링 6펜스쯤이었다. 그 장신구 상인이라면, 수첩이 처음 가게에서 나갔을 때와 다름없이 멀쩡하니, 지금도 그 값에는 도로 사들였을 것이다. 그러나 신중한 사람이라면 이 사내의 무지를 적절히 이용하여, 1실링, 어쩌면 6펜스 이상은 내놓지 않았을 것이다. 아니, 더러는 한 푼도 주지 않고 사내에게 동산 횡령 소송이나 걸어 보라고 내버려 두었을지도 모른다. 그런 처지에서 그가 과연 그 소송을 유지할 수 있었을지는, 학식 있는 법관 가운데 일부는 의심할지도 모를 일이지만.\n\n이와 반대로, 천성이 너그러움의 도를 넘어서서 어쩌면 헤픔이 지나치다는 의심을 받아도 그리 억울하지 않을 존스는, 망설임 없이 그 수첩 값으로 1기니를 내주었다. 오래도록 그만한 보물을 손에 쥐어 본 적이 없던 그 가엾은 사내는 존스에게 천 번이나 고맙다고 인사했으며, 앞서 존스가 소피아 웨스턴의 이름을 처음 읽었을 때 보였던 환희에 못지않은 기쁨을 제 얼굴 근육에 드러냈다.\n\n사내는 수첩을 주운 그 자리까지 우리 여행자들을 안내하겠다고 선뜻 응했다. 그리하여 그들은 곧장 그리로 함께 나아갔으나, 존스가 바라는 만큼 빠르지는 못했다. 안내인이 공교롭게도 절름발이여서 도무지 한 시간에 1마일 이상은 걸을 수 없었기 때문이다. 그 자리는 사내가 달리 말하긴 했어도 실은 3마일 넘게 떨어진 곳이었으니, 그곳까지 걸어가는 데 시간이 얼마나 걸렸을지는 독자에게 굳이 알릴 필요가 없겠다.\n\n존스는 걷는 동안 수첩을 백 번도 더 펼쳤고, 그만큼 자주 입을 맞추었으며, 혼잣말은 많이 하면서 일행에게는 거의 말을 건네지 않았다. 이 모든 광경에 안내인은 파트리지를 향해 놀라움의 기색을 내비쳤고, 파트리지는 몇 번이고 고개를 저으며 외쳤다. \"딱한 양반 같으니! 건강한 몸에 건강한 정신이 깃들기를 빌어야지.\"\n\n마침내 그들은 소피아가 불행히도 수첩을 떨어뜨린, 그리고 사내가 다행히도 그것을 주운 바로 그 자리에 이르렀다. 여기서 존스는 안내인과 작별하고 걸음을 재촉하려 했으나, 1기니를 처음 받았을 때 일었던 그 격렬한 놀라움과 기쁨이 이제 사뭇 가라앉고, 정신을 추스를 만한 시간을 충분히 가진 사내는 못마땅한 낯빛을 지으며 머리를 긁적이고는 말했다. \"나리께서 무언가 더 주셨으면 합니다요. 나리,\" 그가 말했다. \"한번 헤아려 주십시오. 제가 정직하지 않았더라면, 통째로 챙겨 버릴 수도 있었을 겁니다요.\" 그리고 실로 이 말은 독자도 사실임을 인정하지 않을 수 없으리라. \"거기 그 종이가,\" 그가 말했다. \"100파운드짜리라면, 그걸 주운 값으로 1기니는 너무 약소하지요. 게다가 나리께서 그 아씨를 끝내 못 만나서 못 돌려주실 수도 있는 일이고요. 나리께서 보기에도 말씀하시는 것도 영락없는 신사이시긴 합니다만, 그래도 제겐 나리의 말씀 한마디밖에 없는 셈이지요. 그리고 진짜 주인을 못 찾으면, 그건 다 처음 주운 사람 차지가 되는 게 분명합니다요. 나리께서 이 모든 사정을 헤아려 주셨으면 합니다. 저야 한낱 가난뱅이라 다 갖겠다는 게 아니라, 제 몫만큼은 받는 게 당연하지 않겠습니까. 나리께서는 좋은 분으로 보이시니, 부디 제 정직함을 생각해 주십시오. 저는 한 푼까지 다 챙기고도 아무도 모르게 할 수 있었으니까요.\" \"내 명예를 걸고 약속하건대,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"나는 진짜 주인을 알고 있고, 그녀에게 돌려줄 것이다.\" \"아닙니다요, 나리,\" 사내가 대답했다. \"그건 나리 좋으실 대로 하시지요. 그저 제 몫만 주시면, 그러니까 돈의 절반만 주시면, 나머지는 나리께서 가지셔도 좋습니다요.\" 그러고는 자못 격렬한 맹세를 내뱉으며, \"살아 있는 어느 누구에게도 이 일에 대해 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않겠노라\"고 다짐하며 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"이보게, 친구,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"진짜 주인은 잃은 것을 틀림없이 모두 되찾을 걸세. 그리고 그 이상의 사례로 말하자면, 지금 당장은 정말 줄 수가 없네. 다만 자네 이름과 사는 곳을 일러 주게. 그러면 자네가 오늘 아침의 이 일을 두고 앞으로 더욱 기뻐할 까닭이 생길 가능성이 충분하다네.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 생긴다는 건지 저는 통 모르겠습니다요,\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"나리께서 그 아씨한테 돈을 돌려주시든 말든, 제 운수만 내맡기게 생겼군요. 그래도 나리께서 좀 헤아려 주셨으면—\" \"자자,\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"나리께 자네 이름하고 어디로 가면 찾을 수 있는지나 일러 드리게. 돈을 나리 손에 맡긴 걸 결코 후회하는 일은 없을 게야.\" 사내는 수첩을 도로 손에 넣을 가망이 없음을 알고는, 마침내 자기 이름과 사는 곳을 대었다. 존스는 그것을 소피아의 연필로 종이 한 장에 적은 뒤, 그 종이를 그녀가 제 이름을 적어 둔 바로 그 책장에 끼워 넣고는 외쳤다. \"자, 친구, 자네는 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람일세. 내가 자네 이름을 천사의 이름 곁에 나란히 적어 주었으니.\" \"천사니 뭐니 하는 건 저는 모르겠습니다요,\" 사내가 대답했다. \"그저 저는 나리께서 돈을 조금만 더 주시거나, 아니면 수첩을 도로 돌려주셨으면 합니다요.\" 그러자 파트리지가 발끈 성을 냈다. 그는 그 가엾은 절름발이를 갖은 상스럽고 욕된 이름으로 불러 대며, 영락없이 매질이라도 할 기세였으나, 존스가 그런 짓은 결코 용납하지 않았다. 그러고는 사내에게 자기가 반드시 돕게 될 기회를 찾겠노라 이르고서, 존스는 두 발이 닿는 한 빠르게 그곳을 떠났다. 100파운드 생각에 새 기운이 솟은 파트리지도 제 주인을 뒤따랐다. 한편 뒤에 남을 수밖에 없게 된 사내는 그 두 사람은 물론 제 부모까지 싸잡아 저주하기 시작했다. \"그 양반들이,\" 그가 말했다. \"나를 자선 학교에라도 보내 글 읽고 쓰고 셈하는 법을 배우게 했더라면, 나도 남들처럼 이런 물건의 값어치쯤은 알았을 텐데.\"\n* 동산 횡령 소송(action of trover): 남의 동산을 부당하게 점유한 자를 상대로 그 반환이나 손해배상을 구하던 옛 영국 소송.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n존스 씨와 그의 일행이 길에서 겪은 더 많은 모험을 담은 장.\n\n이제 우리 여행자들은 어찌나 빨리 걸었던지, 이야기를 나눌 틈도 숨도 거의 없었다. 존스는 내내 소피아를 떠올리며 걸었고, 파트리지는 그 은행권 어음을 생각하며 걸었다. 어음은 그에게 어느 정도 기쁨을 안겨 주면서도, 동시에 운명을 원망하게 만들었다. 그가 평생 걸어 다닌 그 모든 길에서, 운명은 단 한 번도 자신의 정직함을 보일 이런 기회를 준 적이 없었기 때문이다. 두 사람이 삼 마일 남짓 나아갔을 무렵, 더는 존스를 따라잡을 수 없게 된 파트리지가 그를 부르며 부디 걸음을 조금만 늦춰 달라고 사정했다. 존스로서는 그 청을 더욱 선뜻 들어줄 만했다. 얼마 전부터 말발굽 자국을 놓쳤기 때문인데, 눈이 녹은 덕분에 몇 마일이나 따라올 수 있었던 그 자국이 이제는 사라졌고, 그는 여러 갈래 길이 나 있는 너른 공유지 위에 서 있었다.\n\n그래서 그는 이 길들 가운데 어느 쪽을 택해야 할지 헤아리려고 걸음을 멈추었다. 그때 갑자기 두 사람의 귀에 북소리가 들려왔는데, 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 나는 듯했다. 이 소리는 곧장 파트리지의 두려움을 일깨웠고, 그는 외쳤다. \"주여, 우리 모두에게 자비를 베푸소서. 저들이 분명 오고 있습니다!\" \"누가 온단 말인가?\" 존스가 물었다. 그의 마음속에서는 이미 오래전에 두려움이 더 부드러운 생각들에게 자리를 내준 터였다. 절름발이 사내와 겪은 일이 있은 뒤로, 그는 소피아를 뒤쫓는 데에만 온통 마음을 쏟았을 뿐, 적이라는 생각은 한 번도 품지 않았다. \"누구냐고요?\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"아니, 반란군 말입니다. 한데 제가 왜 저들을 반란군이라 부르는 걸까요? 어쩌면 아주 정직한 신사분들일지도 모르는데 말입니다, 제가 그렇지 않다고 단정할 근거도 없으니까요. 저들에게 모욕을 주는 자는 악마에게나 잡혀가라지요, 저는 그렇게 말하겠습니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 저들이 제게 할 말이 없다면 저도 저들에게 할 말이 없습니다, 그저 점잖게 말고는요. 제발 부탁입니다, 나리, 저들이 정말 오거든 모욕을 주지 마십시오. 그러면 어쩌면 우리에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않을 겁니다. 한데 저들이 다 지나갈 때까지 저쪽 덤불 속에 살그머니 숨어 있는 편이 더 현명하지 않겠습니까? 무장도 안 한 두 사람이 어쩌면 오만 명을 상대로 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까? 미친 사람이 아니고서야 누가 그러겠습니까. 나리께서 기분 상하지 않으셨으면 합니다만, 분명코 건강한 신체에 건전한 정신을 지닌 사람이라면 누구도——\" 여기서 존스가, 두려움이 불러일으킨 이 도도한 웅변의 물결을 가로막으며 말했다. \"북소리로 보아 어느 마을 가까이에 온 것 같군.\" 그러고는 곧장 그 소리가 나는 쪽으로 향하면서 파트리지에게 \"용기를 내게, 위험한 곳으로 끌고 가지는 않을 테니\" 하고 일렀고, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"반란군이 이렇게 가까이 있을 리가 없네.\"\n\n파트리지는 이 마지막 다짐에 조금 마음을 놓았다. 그래도 반대편으로 가는 편이 훨씬 더 좋았겠지만, 그는 가슴을 두근거리며 자기 길잡이를 따라갔다. 다만 그 두근거림은 영웅들이 북의 음악에 맞추어 뛰는 식의 것은 아니었다. 북소리는 두 사람이 공유지를 가로질러 좁은 샛길로 접어들 때까지 그치지 않았다.\n\n그리고 이제, 존스와 보조를 나란히 맞추어 가던 파트리지가 자기 앞 불과 몇 야드 거리에서 무언가 색칠된 것이 공중에 펄럭이는 것을 발견했다. 그것을 적의 군기라 여긴 그는 고래고래 소리치기 시작했다. \"오 주여, 나리, 저기 저들이 있습니다. 저기 왕관과 관이 보입니다. 오 주여! 저토록 끔찍한 것은 난생처음 봅니다. 게다가 우리는 이미 저들의 총 사정거리 안에 들어와 있습니다.\"\n\n존스가 눈을 들어 보자마자, 파트리지가 그토록 잘못 본 것이 무엇인지 또렷이 알아차렸다. \"파트리지,\" 그가 말했다. \"자네 혼자서도 이 군대 전체를 너끈히 상대할 수 있겠군. 저 군기를 보니, 아까 우리가 들은 북소리가 무엇이었는지 짐작이 가네. 저건 인형극에 사람을 끌어모으려고 두드리는 북소리일세.\"\n\n\"인형극이라고요!\" 파트리지가 더없이 들뜬 환희에 차서 대답했다. \"정말로 그것뿐이란 말입니까? 저는 이 세상 모든 소일거리 가운데서도 인형극을 제일 좋아합니다. 부디, 나리, 잠시 머물러 그것을 봅시다. 게다가 저는 배가 고파 죽을 지경입니다. 이제 거의 어두워졌는데, 새벽 세 시 이후로 한 입도 먹지 못했으니까요.\"\n\n이윽고 두 사람은 어느 여관에, 아니 실은 술집에 다다랐고, 존스는 이곳에 멈추자는 설득에 넘어갔다. 더구나 그는 자기가 바라던 길에 들어서 있다는 확신마저 더는 없던 터였다. 두 사람은 곧장 부엌으로 걸어 들어갔고, 거기서 존스는 아침에 이쪽 길로 지나간 부인네는 없었느냐고 묻기 시작했으며, 파트리지는 그에 못지않게 열심히 먹을거리의 형편을 살폈다. 그리고 사실 더 좋은 성과를 거둔 쪽은 파트리지의 탐문이었다. 존스는 소피아의 소식을 들을 수 없었지만, 파트리지는 매우 만족스럽게도 머지않아 김이 모락모락 나는 훌륭한 달걀과 베이컨 요리라는 반가운 광경을 보게 되리라 기대할 만한 충분한 근거를 찾아냈기 때문이다.\n\n튼튼하고 건강한 체질에서는, 사랑이 이 종족 가운데 허약한 부류에게 일으키는 것과는 사뭇 다른 효과를 낸다. 허약한 쪽에서는, 사랑이 대개 개체의 보존으로 향하는 모든 식욕을 무너뜨린다. 그러나 튼튼한 쪽에서는, 비록 사랑이 음식은 물론 다른 모든 것까지 잊고 소홀히 하게 만드는 일이 잦긴 해도, 배고픈 연인 앞에 밀가루를 잘 뿌린 좋은 살코기 한 점을 놓아 보라, 그는 좀처럼 자기 몫을 멋들어지게 해내지 않는 법이 없다. 지금 이 경우가 바로 그러했다. 존스는 어쩌면 누가 곁에서 부추겨 줄 필요가 있었는지도 모르고, 혼자였다면 빈속으로 훨씬 더 멀리까지 갔을지도 모른다. 그러나 베이컨과 달걀 앞에 앉자마자, 그는 파트리지 못지않게 왕성하고 게걸스럽게 먹어치우기 시작했다.\n\n우리 여행자들이 저녁 식사를 끝내기도 전에 밤이 찾아왔고, 달은 이미 보름을 지난 터라 사위가 몹시 어두웠다. 그리하여 파트리지는 막 시작되려는 인형극을 보고 가자고 존스를 설득했다. 그 인형극의 주인은 두 사람을 무척 열심히 불러들였는데, 자기 인형들이야말로 세상이 여태껏 만들어낸 것 중에 가장 빼어난 것이며, 잉글랜드의 어느 마을에서든 모든 지체 높은 분들께 큰 만족을 드렸노라고 큰소리쳤다.\n\n인형극은 자못 질서 정연하고 단정하게 펼쳐졌다. 그것은 《화난 남편》의 우아하고 진지한 대목이라 불리는 것이었는데, 과연 저속한 재치도 해학도 농담도 전혀 없는, 매우 점잖고 엄숙한 구경거리였다. 아니, 공정을 기하여 말하자면, 웃음을 자아낼 만한 것이라곤 아무것도 없는 구경거리였다. 관객은 모두 크게 흡족해했다. 어느 점잖은 부인은 주인에게, 당신이 천박한 것은 하나도 보여 주지 않으니 다음 날 밤에는 두 딸을 데려오겠노라고 말했다. 그리고 어느 변호사 서기와 어느 세무 관리는, 타운리 경과 타운리 부인의 인물됨이 잘 살아 있고 매우 자연스럽게 그려졌다고 입을 모았다. 파트리지 또한 이 의견에 동의했다.\n\n주인은 이 찬사들에 어찌나 의기양양해졌던지, 거기에 제 말까지 몇 마디 보태지 않고는 배기지 못했다. 그는 말했다. \"요즘 세상이 인형극만큼 크게 발전한 것이 없습니다. 펀치며 그의 아내 조앤이며 그런 시시껄렁한 것들을 내던져 버린 끝에, 마침내 인형극이 이성적인 구경거리가 되었거든요. 제가 기억하기로는,\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 처음 이 일에 발을 들였을 무렵에는, 사람들을 웃기기에 딱 좋은 저속한 것들이 잔뜩 있었지요. 하지만 그런 것은 결코 젊은이들의 품행을 바로잡는 데에 보탬이 되도록 짜인 적이 없었습니다. 품행을 바로잡는 일이야말로 어느 인형극에서나 마땅히 가장 으뜸으로 삼아야 할 목표인데도 말입니다. 어찌하여 선하고 교훈이 되는 가르침을 다른 어떤 방식 못지않게 이런 방식으로도 전할 수 없겠습니까? 제 인형들은 실물만큼 크고, 모든 세세한 데까지 인생을 고스란히 그려냅니다. 그러니 사람들이 제 작은 연극에서 일어설 때면, 저 거창한 연극에서 그러하듯 제법 품행이 나아져 일어선다고 저는 믿어 의심치 않습니다.\" \"당신 직업의 재간을 결코 깎아내릴 생각은 없소만,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"그래도 나는 옛 친구 펀치 선생을 한 번 봤으면 좋았겠소. 그리고 내 생각엔, 그와 그의 명랑한 아내 조앤을 빼버림으로써 인형극이 나아지기는커녕 도리어 망쳐 놓은 것 같소.\"\n\n인형 줄을 놀리던 사내는 이 말을 듣자 존스를 향해 대뜸 깊은 경멸을 품었다. 그러고는 얼굴 가득 멸시를 띤 채 대꾸했다. \"그게 손님 생각일 수야 있겠지요. 하지만 저는 가장 안목 높은 분들이 손님과 다르게 보신다는 걸 알고 있어 흐뭇할 따름입니다. 어차피 모든 입맛을 다 맞추기란 불가능한 노릇이지요. 솔직히 고백하건대, 두세 해 전에 바스의 지체 높은 분들 가운데 더러는 펀치를 다시 무대에 올리기를 무척이나 바라셨습니다. 그분들 뜻에 따르지 않은 탓에 돈을 좀 잃었을 겁니다. 하지만 남들이야 제멋대로 하라지요. 하찮은 이문 따위가 저를 매수해 제 직업을 욕보이게 하는 일은 결코 없을 것이며, 그런 저속한 것을 끌어들여 제 무대의 단정함과 질서를 망치는 데에 제 발로 동의하는 일도 결코 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"옳소, 친구,\" 서기가 외쳤다. \"참으로 지당한 말이오. 저속한 것은 언제나 피해야 하는 법이지요. 런던에 내가 아는 사람들 가운데 여럿이, 저속한 것은 무엇이든 무대에서 몰아내기로 단단히 작정하고 있다오.\" \"그보다 더 합당한 일은 없지요.\" 세무 관리가 입에서 파이프를 빼며 외쳤다. \"내가 기억하기로는,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"(그때 나는 우리 나리 댁에 살고 있었거든) 이 《화난 남편》이라는 연극이 처음 무대에 오른 날 밤, 나는 하인석 관람석에 있었소. 거기엔 의회 의원에 출마하려고 시골 신사 하나가 도시로 올라오는 이야기를 두고 저속한 것이 잔뜩 들어 있었지요. 그자의 하인들을 한 무리나 무대에 끌어내었는데, 특히 그의 마부가 기억나는구려. 한데 우리 관람석의 신사들은 그토록 저속한 것을 견디지 못해 그 대목에 야유를 퍼부었다오. 보아하니, 친구, 당신은 그 모든 대목을 다 빼버렸구려. 그건 칭찬받을 만한 일이오.\"\n\n\"아니오, 신사 여러분,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 많은 분을 상대로 내 의견을 끝까지 우길 수야 없지요. 정말이지, 관객 대다수가 그를 싫어한다면, 이 극을 이끄시는 학식 높은 신사분께서 펀치를 그 직무에서 내치신 것은 매우 잘하신 일일 수도 있겠소.\"\n\n그러자 인형극 주인은 두 번째 일장 연설을 시작하여, 본보기가 지닌 큰 힘에 관해, 그리고 못난 부류의 사람들이 윗사람들에게서 악덕이 얼마나 보기 흉한 것인지를 지켜봄으로써 얼마나 악덕을 멀리하게 되는지에 관해 장황하게 늘어놓았다. 그런데 그때 그는 어떤 사건에 그만 운수 사납게 가로막히고 말았다. 그 사건은 다른 때라면 어쩌면 우리가 생략했을지도 모르나, 지금으로서는 이야기하지 않을 수 없다. 다만 이 장에서는 아니다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n6장.\n이로써 가장 좋은 것들조차 오해받고 곡해되기 쉽다는 사실을 헤아릴 수 있다.\n\n이제 복도에서 한바탕 거센 소동이 일었다. 안주인이 하녀를 주먹과 입으로 한꺼번에 호되게 두들겨 패고 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 정말로 그 계집아이가 일터에서 사라진 것을 알아챘고, 잠시 찾아 헤맨 끝에 인형극 무대 위에서 어릿광대와 함께, 그리 점잖게 묘사하기 어려운 자세로 있는 것을 발견했다.\n\n그레이스(이것이 그 아이의 이름이었다)는 정숙함을 주장할 자격을 모조리 잃은 처지였으나, 그렇다고 막상 들킨 사실을 뻔뻔하게 부인할 만큼의 낯짝까지 갖추지는 못했다. 그래서 아이는 방향을 틀어, 그 잘못을 누그러뜨려 보려 했다. \"마님, 어째서 저를 이리 때리세요?\" 계집아이가 외쳤다. \"제가 하는 짓이 마음에 안 드시면, 그냥 내쫓으시면 되잖아요. 제가 화냥년이라면\"(안주인이 그 호칭을 아이에게 아낌없이 퍼부었던 것이다), \"저보다 잘난 분들도 저랑 매한가지지요. 방금 인형극에 나온 그 귀하신 마님은 뭐였답니까? 그분이 밤새 서방님을 떠나 밖에서 잔 게 괜히 그런 건 아니었겠지요.\"\n\n이제 안주인이 부엌으로 들이닥쳐, 제 남편과 그 가엾은 인형 부리는 사내에게 한꺼번에 화살을 퍼부었다. \"여보,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이런 자들을 집에 들이면 어찌 되는지 보라고요. 저들 덕분에 술이 좀 더 팔린다 한들, 저들이 어질러 놓는 꼴에 비하면 본전도 못 찾아요. 게다가 저런 이투성이 해충 같은 것들 때문에 우리 집이 갈봇집 꼴이 되다니. 한마디로, 내일 아침에는 떠나 주셨으면 해요. 더는 이런 짓거리를 봐줄 수 없으니까요. 이런 건 그저 하인들에게 게으름과 헛소리나 가르치는 길일 뿐이에요. 저런 허튼 구경거리에서 무슨 더 나은 걸 배우겠어요. 인형극이 옛날에 입다의 경솔한 맹세 같은, 그런 좋은 성경 이야기로 만들어지던 시절이 생각나네요. 못된 사람들이 마귀에게 끌려가던 그런 이야기들 말이에요. 그런 데는 그래도 무슨 뜻이라도 있었지요. 하지만 지난 일요일에 목사님이 말씀하셨듯, 요즘은 아무도 마귀 같은 건 믿지 않아요. 그런데 당신은 고작 가엾은 시골 계집애들 머리나 돌려놓으려고, 귀족 나리니 마님이니처럼 차려입힌 인형 떼거리를 끌고 다니는 거예요. 한번 그 머리가 거꾸로 뒤집히고 나면, 나머지도 죄다 그 꼴이 되는 게 당연하지요.\"\n\n내 기억으로는 베르길리우스가 이런 이야기를 들려준다. 군중이 난동을 부리며 시끌벅적하게 몰려들어 온갖 던질 무기가 사방으로 날아다닐 때, 위엄과 권위를 갖춘 사람이 그들 사이에 나타나면 소란은 이내 가라앉고, 한 덩어리로 뭉치면 영락없이 나귀에 견줄 만한 그 군중이, 위엄 있는 사람의 말 앞에서 긴 귀를 쫑긋 세운다는 것이다.\n\n반대로, 한 무리의 위엄 있는 사람들과 철학자들이 논쟁을 벌이고 있을 때, 말하자면 지혜의 여신 그 자신이 자리에 함께하여 논객들에게 논거를 대 준다고 여길 만한 그런 때에, 군중 사이에서 소란이 일거나, 혹은 그 자체로 거대한 군중에 맞먹는 소음을 내는 바가지 긁는 여인 하나가 그 철학자들 틈에 나타난다면, 그들의 논쟁은 순식간에 멎고, 지혜는 더 이상 제 직무를 수행하지 못하며, 모든 이의 주의는 즉시 그 바가지 긁는 여인 하나에게만 쏠려 버린다.\n\n이렇듯 앞서 말한 소동과 안주인의 등장은 인형극 주인을 잠잠하게 만들었고, 이미 독자에게 충분히 맛보여 드린 바 있는 그 위엄 있고 엄숙한 일장 연설에 빠르고도 결정적인 종지부를 찍어 버렸다. 실로 이 사고만큼 더없이 때를 못 맞춘 일도 없었을 것이다. 운명의 가장 짓궂은 악의라 한들, 그 가엾은 사내가 제 공연이 심어 주는 좋은 도덕에 대해 그토록 의기양양하게 늘어놓던 바로 그 순간에 그를 당황케 할 또 다른 술책을 꾸며 낼 수는 없었으리라. 이제 그의 입은, 마치 돌팔이 의사가 제 알약과 가루약의 위대한 효능을 일장 연설로 떠벌리던 한복판에, 그 솜씨의 증거랍시고 자기 손에 죽은 희생자의 시신이 들려 나와 무대 앞에 놓일 때 그 입이 막혀 버리는 것만큼이나 확실하게 봉해졌다.\n\n그래서 인형극 사내는 안주인에게 대꾸하는 대신, 제 어릿광대를 혼내 주러 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 이제 시인들이 이르는 대로 달이 은빛을 내뿜기 시작했는데(하기야 그때 달은 은보다 구릿빛 한 조각에 더 가까워 보였지만), 존스는 셈을 청한 뒤, 안주인이 막 깊은 낮잠에서 깨워 놓은 파트리지에게 여행을 채비하라 일렀다. 그러나 파트리지는 독자께서 앞서 보셨듯 최근 두 가지를 관철해 낸 터라, 대담해진 김에 세 번째까지 시도해 보려 했다. 그것은 곧 그날 저녁을 지금 머무는 이 집에서 묵도록 존스를 설득하는 일이었다. 그는 먼저 존스 씨가 자리를 옮기겠다고 밝힌 데 대해 짐짓 놀란 척하며 운을 떼고는, 거기에 반대하는 훌륭한 논거를 여럿 늘어놓은 끝에, 마침내 그것이 어떤 목적에도 도무지 닿을 수 없는 일이라고 강하게 주장했다. 존스가 그 부인이 어느 길로 갔는지 알지 못하는 한, 한 발짝 내디딜 때마다 그녀에게서 도리어 더 멀어지게 될지도 모른다는 것이었다. \"나리, 보십시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 집의 누구에게 물어봐도 그분이 이쪽으로 가지 않으셨다는 걸 알 수 있잖습니까. 그러니 날이 밝을 때까지 머무는 편이 얼마나 더 낫겠습니까. 그때라면 물어볼 만한 누군가를 만나리라 기대할 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n이 마지막 논거는 정말로 존스에게 얼마쯤 효력이 있었다. 그가 그것을 저울질하고 있는 동안, 주인장이 제가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 수사를 같은 저울판에 보태었다. \"암요, 나리,\" 그가 말했다. \"하인분께서 더없이 훌륭한 조언을 드리는 겁니다. 일 년 중 이런 철에 누가 밤길을 다니겠습니까?\" 그러고는 으레 하는 투로, 제 집이 제공하는 그 훌륭한 잠자리를 나팔 불듯 떠벌리기 시작했고, 안주인 또한 이때다 싶어 입을 열었다. 그러나 어느 집주인이든 안주인이든 다 하는 흔한 소리로 독자를 붙잡아 둘 것까지는 없으니, 결국 존스가 설득에 못 이겨 머무르며 몇 시간의 휴식으로 몸을 풀게 되었다는 것만 일러두면 충분하리라. 사실 그에게는 그 휴식이 무척이나 절실했다. 머리가 깨지는 사고가 일어났던 그 여관을 떠난 이후로 거의 눈을 붙이지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n존스는 그날 밤 더 나아가지 않기로 마음을 정하자마자, 곧 두 동침자, 곧 수첩과 토시를 데리고 잠자리에 들었다. 그러나 파트리지는 여러 차례 토막잠으로 몸을 추슬러 온 터라, 잠보다는 먹는 쪽에, 그리고 그 둘 어느 쪽보다도 마시는 쪽에 더 마음이 기울어 있었다.\n\n이제 그레이스가 일으킨 풍파도 가라앉고, 안주인도 인형 부리는 사내와 다시 화해했으며, 그 사내 또한 그 선량한 여인이 화김에 제 공연에 퍼부은 무례한 험담을 용서해 준 터라, 부엌에는 더없이 평화롭고 고요한 기운이 감돌았다. 그곳 난롯가에는 이 집의 주인 내외, 인형극 주인, 변호사 사무원, 소비세 징수원, 그리고 재간둥이 파트리지 씨가 빙 둘러앉아 있었으니, 다음 장에서 보게 될 그 유쾌한 담소가 이 사람들 사이에서 오갔다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n우리 자신의 소견 한두 가지와, 부엌에 모여 앉은 점잖은 무리의 더 많은 소견을 담고 있음.\n\n파트리지의 자존심이 자신을 하인이라 인정하는 데까지는 굽히지 않았으나, 그래도 그는 대개의 사소한 점에서 그 신분의 처신을 따라 하는 정도까지는 자신을 낮추었다. 그 한 예가, 자신이 동행이라 부르는 존스의 재산을 크게 부풀려 말한 일이었다. 이는 낯선 이들 사이에서 모든 하인이 흔히 보이는 습성이니, 어느 누구도 거지의 시중꾼으로 여겨지길 바라지 않기 때문이다. 주인의 처지가 높을수록, 그에 따라 자기 자신에 대한 하인의 자부심도 높아지는 법이다. 이 관찰의 진실함은 귀족들을 모시는 모든 종복의 행실에서 고스란히 드러난다.\n\n그러나 지위와 재산은 그 둘레의 모든 것에 광채를 나누어 주고, 신분 있고 재산 있는 사람의 종복은 자기 주인의 신분과 재산에 바쳐지는 존경의 일부를 누릴 자격이 있다고 여기지만, 덕과 식견에 관해서는 사정이 분명히 다르다. 이러한 장점은 철저히 그 사람 개인의 것이어서, 거기에 바쳐지는 존경을 자기 안으로 통째로 삼켜 버린다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그 존경이라는 것이 워낙 적어서, 다른 누구와 나눠 가질 여유조차 없다. 그러므로 이러한 것들이 하인에게는 아무런 영예도 비추어 주지 않듯, 주인에게 그 둘이 가장 한심할 만큼 결여되어 있다 해도 하인이 조금도 욕될 일은 없다. 다만 안주인에게 이른바 덕이라 부르는 것이 결여된 경우라면 사정이 다르니, 그 결과는 앞에서 이미 본 바와 같다. 이 불명예에는 일종의 전염성이 있어, 가난의 그것처럼 그것에 가까이 다가가는 모든 이에게 옮아붙기 때문이다.\n\n자, 이러한 이유들로 보아, 하인들이(나는 남자들만을 두고 하는 말이다) 주인의 재산에 대한 평판에는 그토록 큰 관심을 두면서도 다른 점에서의 인품에는 거의, 혹은 전혀 마음을 쓰지 않는다 해도, 또 거지의 종복이 되는 것은 부끄러워하면서도 악당이나 멍청이를 모시는 것은 부끄러워하지 않는다 해도, 그리고 그 결과 그런 주인의 죄악과 어리석음에 대한 소문을 거리낌 없이 가능한 한 멀리 퍼뜨리고, 그것도 흔히 큰 익살과 흥겨움을 곁들여 그리한다 해도, 우리는 놀랄 일이 아니다. 실로 종복이란, 자기가 걸친 제복의 주인 양반을 희생양 삼아, 멋쟁이일 뿐 아니라 곧잘 재치꾼이 되기도 하는 법이다.\n\n그리하여 파트리지는 존스 씨가 물려받을 막대한 재산에 대해 한껏 부풀려 늘어놓고 난 뒤, 전날부터 품기 시작한 어떤 우려를 아주 거리낌 없이 털어놓았다. 그 우려에 대해서는, 바로 그때 우리가 넌지시 일러둔 대로, 존스의 행동이 충분한 근거를 마련해 준 듯했다. 요컨대 그는 이제 자기 주인이 제정신이 아니라는 견해에 꽤나 굳게 확신을 갖게 되었고, 그 견해를 난롯가에 둘러앉은 점잖은 무리에게 아주 무뚝뚝하게 알려 주었다.\n\n이 의견에 인형극 사내가 곧바로 맞장구를 쳤다. \"솔직히 말해서,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 양반이 인형극에 대해 그렇게 터무니없는 소리를 늘어놓을 때 저는 어찌나 놀랐는지 모릅니다. 정말이지 제정신인 사람이 그토록 크게 잘못 알고 있으리라고는 도무지 믿기지가 않더군요. 지금 하신 말씀을 들으니 그 양반의 그 괴상한 생각들이 죄다 아주 잘 설명됩니다. 가엾은 양반 같으니! 저도 그 사람이 진심으로 딱하게 여겨집니다. 사실 그 양반 눈에는 뭔가 묘하게 사나운 기운이 있어요. 입 밖에 내진 않았어도 진작 눈여겨봐 두었답니다.\"\n\n주인장도 이 마지막 단언에 동의했고, 더불어 자기도 그것을 알아챘노라며 그 명민함을 자랑했다. \"그리고 분명히,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그게 틀림없을 겁니다. 미친 사람이 아니고서야 누가 그런 좋은 집을 놔두고 한밤중에 시골을 쏘다닐 생각을 하겠습니까.\"\n\n소비세 징수원이 입에서 담뱃대를 빼며 말했다. \"내 보기에도 그 양반이 좀 정신 나간 사람처럼 보이고 그렇게 말하는 것 같더이다.\" 그러고는 파트리지를 향해 돌아앉으며 말했다. \"만약 그 사람이 미치광이라면, 저렇게 시골을 떠돌아다니게 내버려두어선 안 됩니다. 무슨 사고라도 칠지 모르니까요. 붙잡아서 친척들에게 돌려보내지 못한 게 안타깝군요.\"\n\n그런데 이런 종류의 궁리는 파트리지의 마음속에서도 똬리를 틀고 있었다. 그는 이제 존스가 올워디 씨에게서 도망쳐 나왔다고 믿게 되었으므로, 무슨 수를 써서든 그를 도로 데려갈 수만 있다면 최고의 보상을 받으리라 혼자 기대를 부풀리고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 존스의 사나움과 힘에 대해서는 몇 차례 직접 보았고 또 실제로 몸소 겪기까지 했던 터라, 그 두려움 때문에 그런 계획은 도무지 실행 불가능한 일로 여겨졌고, 그래서 그는 그 목적을 위해 무슨 제대로 된 계획을 세워 볼 엄두조차 내지 못하고 있었다. 그러나 징수원의 의견을 듣자마자 그는 자기 생각을 밝힐 그 기회를 덥석 붙잡았고, 정말이지 그런 일이 성사되었으면 좋겠다는 간절한 바람을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"성사되었으면 좋겠다고요!\" 징수원이 말했다. \"아니, 그보다 쉬운 일이 어디 있습니까.\"\n\n\"아! 나리,\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"당신은 그 사람이 어떤 악마 같은 작자인지 모르셔서 그럽니다. 그 사람은 한 손으로 나를 번쩍 들어 창밖으로 내던질 수 있어요. 그리고 만약 그가 조금이라도 눈치챈다면, 정말로 그러고도 남을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"흥!\" 징수원이 말했다. \"내 생각에 나도 그 사람 못지않은 장정이오. 게다가 여기 우리가 다섯이나 되지 않소.\"\n\n\"무슨 다섯이라는 거예요,\" 안주인이 소리쳤다. \"내 남편은 이 일에 끼어들 일 없어요. 내 집에서 그 누구에게도 함부로 손을 댈 수 없고요. 그 젊은 양반은 내 평생 본 중에 가장 멀쩡한 젊은이고, 내 보기엔 우리 누구보다도 미치지 않았답니다. 그 사람 눈에 사나운 기운이 있다니, 그게 무슨 소리예요? 내 평생 본 중에 가장 고운 눈이고, 그 눈으로 짓는 표정도 더없이 곱답니다. 게다가 아주 점잖고 예의 바른 청년이고요. 저 구석에 있는 양반이 그 사람이 사랑에 어긋났다고 말해 준 뒤로 나는 진심으로 그 사람이 가엾어 죽겠어요. 그런 일을 겪으면 누구라도, 특히 그처럼 사랑스러운 젊은 양반이라면 전과는 좀 달라 보이기 마련이지요. 귀부인이라니, 참! 그렇게 잘생긴 데다 큰 재산까지 가진 남자보다 그 귀부인이 도대체 더 나은 걸 어디서 바라겠어요? 내 짐작에 그 여자는 당신들 말하는 그 지체 높은 양반네 중 하나, 어젯밤 인형극에서 본 그 타운리 부인 같은 여자일 거예요. 자기가 뭘 원하는지도 모르는 그런 부류 말이에요.\"\n\n변호사 사무원도 변호사의 조언 없이는 이 일에 관여하지 않겠노라고 밝혔다. \"가령,\" 그가 말했다. \"우리를 상대로 불법 감금 소송이 제기되면 우리가 무슨 변명을 할 수 있겠습니까? 배심원에게 광기를 입증할 증거로 무엇이 충분할지 누가 알겠어요? 하지만 이건 어디까지나 제 입장에서만 드리는 말씀입니다. 법률가가 이런 일에 끼어드는 건, 법률가로서가 아니고서는 모양새가 좋지 않거든요. 배심원들은 언제나 우리한테 다른 사람보다 덜 호의적이니까요. 그러니 톰슨 씨, 당신(징수원에게 한 말이다)이나 저 양반이나 다른 누구든 말리지는 않겠습니다.\"\n\n징수원은 이 말에 고개를 가로저었고, 인형극 사내가 말했다. \"광기란 때로 배심원이 판단하기 어려운 문제지요. 제가 기억하기로,\" 그가 말했다. \"한번은 어떤 광기 재판에 입회한 적이 있는데, 증인 스무 명이 그 사람은 삼월의 토끼처럼 미쳤다고 맹세했고, 또 다른 스무 명은 그가 영국의 어느 누구 못지않게 멀쩡하다고 맹세하더군요. 그리고 사실 대다수 사람들의 생각은, 그게 그 가엾은 사람의 권리를 가로채려는 친척들의 농간일 뿐이라는 거였답니다.\"\n\n\"충분히 그럴 만하죠!\" 안주인이 소리쳤다. \"나도 한 가엾은 양반을 알았는데, 그 사람은 가족 때문에 평생 정신병원에 갇혀 지냈고, 가족들은 그 재산을 누렸지요. 하지만 그게 그들에게 좋은 일이 되지는 못했어요. 법이 그걸 그들에게 주었어도, 그건 본디 다른 사람의 권리였으니까요.\"\n\n\"흥!\" 사무원이 크게 비웃으며 소리쳤다. \"법이 주는 것 말고 누가 무슨 권리를 가진단 말이오? 만약 법이 이 고장에서 가장 좋은 재산을 내게 준다면, 나는 누구한테 그 권리가 있는지 따위로 결코 골치를 썩이지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"그게 그렇다면,\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"펠릭스 쿠엠 파키운트 알리에나 페리쿨라 카우툼.\"*\n\n* 라틴어 격언: 남의 위험을 보고 조심하게 되는 사람은 행복하다.\n문간에 말 탄 사람이 도착하는 바람에 불려 나갔던 주인장이 이제 부엌으로 돌아오더니, 겁에 질린 얼굴로 소리쳤다. \"어떻게들 생각하시오, 여러분? 반란군이 공작을 따돌리고 거의 런던 코앞까지 들이닥쳤답니다. 분명한 사실이에요. 방금 말 탄 사람이 내게 그렇게 일러 주었으니까.\"\n\n\"그것참 진심으로 다행이군요,\" 파트리지가 소리쳤다. \"그럼 이 근방에서는 싸움이 없겠네요.\"\n\n\"나는 더 나은 이유에서 다행입니다,\" 사무원이 소리쳤다. \"나는 언제나 정당한 권리가 실현되기를 바라니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지만,\" 주인장이 대답했다. \"어떤 사람들은 그 사람에게 아무 권리도 없다고 말하는 걸 들었는데요.\"\n\n\"그 반대임을 당장 증명해 드리지요,\" 사무원이 소리쳤다. \"만약 내 아버지가 어떤 권리를 점유한 채로 죽는다면, 내 말 잘 들으시오, 권리를 점유한 채로라고 했소, 그 권리가 그 아들에게 상속되지 않겠소? 그리고 어느 권리든 다른 권리와 똑같이 상속되는 것 아니겠소?\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게 그 사람이 우리를 가톨릭교도로 만들 권리를 가질 수 있단 말이오?\" 주인장이 말했다.\n\n\"그건 걱정 마시오,\" 파트리지가 소리쳤다. \"권리 문제로 말하자면, 저 양반이 해처럼 환하게 증명해 보였지요. 그리고 종교 문제로 말하자면, 그건 아예 해당되지 않는 일이오. 가톨릭교도들 자신도 그런 일은 바라지 않으니까요. 내가 아주 잘 아는 어떤 가톨릭 사제는, 더없이 정직한 사람인데, 자기 신앙과 명예를 걸고 그들에게 그런 의도는 없다고 내게 말해 주었소.\"\n\n\"내가 아는 또 다른 사제도,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"내게 똑같은 말을 해 주었어요. 그런데 내 남편은 늘 가톨릭교도들을 그렇게 무서워한답니다. 나는 아주 정직한 부류의 가톨릭교도들을 많이 알아요. 돈도 아주 시원시원하게 쓰는 사람들이지요. 그리고 나한테는 늘 한 가지 격언이 있는데, 이 사람 돈이나 저 사람 돈이나 다 똑같이 좋다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"지당한 말씀입니다, 마님,\" 인형극 사내가 말했다. \"나는 어떤 종교가 들어오든 상관 안 합니다. 다만 장로교도들만 위에 올라서지 않으면 돼요. 그자들은 인형극의 적이거든요.\"\n\n\"그래서 당신은 당신 이익을 위해 종교를 팔아넘기겠다는 거로군요,\" 징수원이 소리쳤다. \"그리고 가톨릭이 들어오는 걸 보고 싶다는 거고, 그런 거요?\"\n\n\"천만에요, 정말로 아닙니다,\" 사내가 대답했다. \"나도 누구 못지않게 가톨릭을 싫어해요. 하지만 그래도, 그 밑에서나마 먹고살 수 있으리라는 건 위안이 되거든요. 장로교도들 사이에서는 그러지도 못할 테니까요. 확실히 사람은 누구나 제 밥줄을 가장 소중히 여기는 법, 그건 인정해야지요. 그리고 장담컨대, 당신도 솔직히 털어놓는다면 다른 무엇보다도 일자리 잃는 걸 가장 두려워하고 있을 거요. 하지만 걱정 마시오, 친구. 이 정부 아래서나 다른 정부 아래서나 소비세는 똑같이 있을 테니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 그야 물론,\" 징수원이 대꾸했다. \"내가 그 밥을 먹고 사는 임금님을 받들지 않는다면 나는 아주 못된 인간이겠지요. 그건 그저 당연한 일이오, 사람이 말하자면. 다른 정부 아래서 소비세 관청이 있은들 그게 내게 무슨 소용이겠소? 내 동료들은 다 쫓겨날 테고, 나도 그 뒤를 따라가는 것 말고는 더 나은 걸 기대할 수 없을 텐데 말이오. 아니, 아니지, 친구. 다른 정부 아래서 자리나 지키겠다는 기대 하나로 내 신앙을 속아 넘기는 일은 결코 없을 거요. 그래 봤자 나는 분명 조금도 나아지지 않을 테고, 십중팔구 더 나빠질 테니까.\"\n\n\"아, 그게 바로 내가 하는 말이오,\" 주인장이 소리쳤다. \"사람들이 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 아냐고 떠들어댈 때마다 말이오! 맙소사! 누군지도 모르는 사람한테, 어쩌면 도로 갚을지도 모른다는 이유 하나로 내 돈을 빌려준다면 그게 멍청이가 아니고 뭐겠소? 내 돈은 내 책상 서랍 안에 있는 게 안전하니, 거기다 그냥 둘 거요.\"\n\n변호사 사무원은 파트리지의 명민함에 크게 반해 버렸다. 이것이 사무원이 사물뿐 아니라 사람을 꿰뚫어 보는 그 뛰어난 안목에서 비롯된 것인지, 아니면 두 사람 마음 사이의 공감에서 생겨난 것인지는 모르겠으나, 두 사람 다 신념에서는 진짜배기 자코바이트였던 까닭에, 그들은 이제 진심으로 손을 맞잡고 독한 맥주를 가득 채워 어떤 이들의 건강을 위해 건배했는데, 우리는 그 이름을 망각 속에 묻어 두는 편이 옳다고 본다.\n\n이 축배는 뒤이어 그 자리에 있던 모두가, 심지어 주인장 자신조차도 마지못해 따라 들었다. 그러나 그는 만약 거절하면 다시는 이 집에 발을 들여놓지 않겠다고 맹세하는 사무원의 으름장을 도저히 당해 낼 수 없었다. 이때 들이켠 가득한 잔들이 곧 대화에 종지부를 찍었다. 그러니 우리도 여기서 이 장을 마치기로 하자.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n8장.\n이제껏 우리가 보아 온 것보다 운명의 여신이 존스에게 한결 너그러운 기분이었던 듯한 장.\n\n피로만큼 몸에 좋은 수면제는 없으며, 또한 그만큼 강력한 수면제도 드물 것이다. 존스는 이 약을 아주 큰 분량으로 들이켰다고 할 만했고, 그 효력은 그에게 아주 세차게 작용했다. 그는 이미 아홉 시간을 잤고, 어쩌면 더 오래 잤을지도 모른다. 만약 방문 앞에서 터져 나온 더없이 격렬한 소음, 곧 묵직한 매질 소리에 살인이라 외치는 고함이 잇따라 섞이는 그 소동에 깨어나지 않았더라면 말이다. 존스는 곧장 침대에서 뛰쳐나왔고, 그곳에서 인형극단의 단장이 자비도 절제도 없이 가엾은 어릿광대의 등과 옆구리를 두들겨 패고 있는 광경을 보았다.\n\n존스는 곧바로 매를 맞고 있는 쪽을 위해 끼어들어, 으스대던 그 정복자를 벽에 밀어붙여 꼼짝 못하게 했다. 가엾은 알록달록한 옷차림의 광대가 이 인형극단 단장과 겨룰 처지가 못 되었듯, 이 인형극단 단장 또한 존스와 맞붙을 처지가 못 되었던 것이다.\n\n하지만 그 어릿광대는 작은 사내였고 그리 힘세지도 않았으나, 그래도 나름의 노여움은 품고 있었다. 그래서 그는 적에게서 풀려나자마자, 자신이 그와 호각을 이룰 수 있는 유일한 무기로 그를 공격하기 시작했다. 우선 그는 두루뭉술한 욕설을 한바탕 퍼붓고, 거기서 나아가 몇 가지 구체적인 비난으로 옮겨 갔다. \"이 빌어먹을 놈아.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 너를 먹여 살렸을 뿐 아니라(네가 벌어들이는 돈은 다 내 덕이지) 교수대에서도 구해 줬어. 바로 어제, 여기 뒷골목에서 그 부인의 멋진 승마복을 빼앗으려 들지 않았느냐? 그 부인을 숲속에 혼자 끌고 가서 옷을 벗기고 싶어 했던 걸 부인할 수 있어? 세상에 보기 드물게 어여쁜 부인을 말이다! 그래 놓고도 너는, 나한테나 똑같이 마음 내켜 한 계집애한테 아무런 해도 끼치지 않은 나를, 그 애가 너보다 나를 더 좋아한다는 이유 하나로 덮쳐서 죽을 만큼 패 놓았어.\"\n\n존스는 이 말을 듣자마자 단장을 놓아주었는데, 그러면서도 어릿광대에게 더는 어떤 모욕도 가하지 말라고 더없이 단호하게 못 박아 두었다. 그러고 나서 그 가엾은 사내를 자기 방으로 데려가서는, 곧 소피아의 소식을 알아냈다. 그자는 전날 북을 치며 단장을 따라다니다가 그녀가 지나가는 것을 보았던 것이다. 존스는 어렵지 않게 그 청년을 구슬려 정확한 장소를 일러 받았고, 그런 다음 파트리지를 불러 더없이 서둘러 길을 떠났다.\n\n떠날 채비가 다 갖춰지기까지는 거의 여덟 시가 되었다. 파트리지가 도무지 서두르는 법이 없었고, 셈도 당장 치러지지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 이 두 가지가 다 정리되고 끝났을 때도, 존스는 단장과 그 사내 사이의 모든 다툼을 말끔히 화해시키기 전에는 그곳을 떠나려 하지 않았다.\n\n이것이 무사히 이루어지자 그는 길을 나섰고, 그 믿음직한 어릿광대의 안내를 받아 소피아가 지나간 지점에 이르렀다. 그러고는 안내인에게 후하게 사례한 뒤, 다시금 더없는 열의로 발걸음을 재촉했다. 소식을 얻게 된 그 남다른 경위에 무척이나 기분이 좋았던 것이다. 파트리지는 이 사정을 듣자마자 자못 진지하게 예언을 늘어놓기 시작하며, 존스에게 결국에는 반드시 좋은 결과가 따를 것이라고 장담했다. 그는 말했다. \"이렇게 두 번씩이나 우연이 일어나 도련님을 마님 뒤로 이끌었으니, 섭리가 끝내 두 분을 맺어 주려 작정한 게 아니라면 어찌 이런 일이 있겠습니까.\" 그리고 존스가 동행인의 미신 같은 소리에 처음으로 귀를 기울인 것이 바로 이때였다.\n\n그들이 채 이 마일도 가기 전에 사나운 빗줄기가 들이닥쳤다. 그리고 마침 같은 순간에 술집 하나가 눈에 들어온 터라, 파트리지는 간곡한 애원 끝에 존스를 안으로 들어가 비를 피하도록 설득해 냈다. 굶주림이란(그것을 적이라 부를 수 있다면 말이지만) 프랑스 사람의 기질보다는 영국 사람의 기질을 더 닮은 적이다. 아무리 자주 물리쳐도 때가 되면 늘 다시 전열을 가다듬으니 말이다. 파트리지도 마찬가지여서, 부엌에 발을 들여놓기가 무섭게 전날 밤 던졌던 것과 똑같은 물음들을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그 결과 훌륭한 차가운 등심 한 덩이가 식탁에 올라왔고, 파트리지뿐 아니라 존스 자신도 무척 든든하게 아침을 들었다. 다만 존스는 집안 사람들이 소피아에 관해 새 소식을 전혀 전해 주지 못하자 다시금 마음이 불안해지기 시작했다.\n\n식사가 끝나자 존스는 여전히 사나운 폭풍이 멎지 않았는데도 다시 길을 나설 채비를 했다. 그러나 파트리지가 한 잔만 더 마시자고 간절히 졸랐다. 그러다가 마침내 부엌에 들어와 난롯가에 서 있는 한 소년에게 눈길이 닿았는데, 그 소년 역시 바로 그 순간 그를 뚫어지게 쳐다보고 있었다. 파트리지는 별안간 존스를 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"도련님, 손 좀 주십시오. 이번 판에는 달랑 한 잔으로는 안 되겠습니다. 아, 소피아 마님의 소식이 또 들어왔지 뭡니까. 저기 난롯가에 서 있는 저 녀석이 바로 마님 앞에서 말을 달리던 그 아이입니다. 제가 저 얼굴에 붙여 준 고약을 두고 맹세할 수도 있습니다.\" \"어르신, 복 받으세요.\" 소년이 외쳤다. \"정말 어르신이 붙여 주신 그 고약이 맞아요. 저는 두고두고 어르신의 은혜를 잊지 못할 겁니다. 덕분에 거의 다 나았거든요.\"\n\n이 말에 존스는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 소년에게 즉시 따라오라 이르고는 부엌을 나서 호젓한 방으로 들어갔다. 소피아에 관한 한 그는 어찌나 조심스러웠던지, 여러 사람 앞에서는 결코 그녀의 이름을 함부로 입에 올리지 않았다. 비록 그가 가슴에서 흘러넘치는 마음을 어쩌지 못해, 결코 그녀가 누군지 알려질 리 없다고 여긴 장교들 앞에서 소피아를 축배의 이름으로 든 적은 있었지만, 그곳에서조차 그가 그녀의 성을 입에 올리도록 설득당하는 데 얼마나 애를 먹었는지는 독자도 기억할 것이다.\n\n그러니, 그가 그토록 차고 넘치게 지녔던 바로 그 조심스러움이 없었으리라는 오해 때문에 지금의 불행을 주로 겪게 되었다는 것은 참으로 야릇한 일이며, 어쩌면 식견 있는 많은 독자들의 눈에는 무척이나 터무니없고 괴이한 일로 비칠지도 모른다. 실은 소피아는, 지금 같은 처지의 그가 다른 여인의 몸을 두고 어떤 방종을 부렸든 그것보다도, 그가 자신의 이름과 인격을 함부로 다루었다고(그럴 만한 충분한 이유가 있었다) 여긴 그 무례함에 훨씬 더 마음이 상해 있었기 때문이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그토록 존중이 결여되어 있고, 위대하고 섬세한 마음이 품는 그 어떤 사랑이나 애틋함과도 도무지 어울리지 않는, 그의 처신에 담긴 그 두 가지 명백한 경박함의 본보기가 아니었다면, 어너가 아무리 졸랐어도 소피아가 존스를 만나 보지 않은 채 업튼을 떠나도록 설득해 낼 수는 없었으리라고 나는 믿는다.\n\n그러나 일이 그렇게 흘러갔고, 그러니 나는 그렇게 전할 수밖에 없다. 그리고 만약 어떤 독자가 이것이 부자연스럽게 보인다 하여 충격을 받는다면, 나로서도 어쩔 도리가 없다. 나는 그런 분들에게 일러두어야겠다. 나는 하나의 체계를 쓰는 것이 아니라 역사를 쓰는 것이며, 모든 사안을 진실과 자연에 관한 통념에 일일이 끼워 맞춰야 할 의무가 없다고. 그러나 설령 그렇게 맞추기가 아무리 쉽다 한들, 아마 나는 그것을 피하는 편이 더 분별 있는 일일 것이다. 이를테면 지금 우리 앞에 놓인 이 사실은, 내가 거기에 아무런 논평도 덧붙이지 않은 지금 이대로일 때, 비록 처음에는 어떤 독자들의 비위를 거스를지 몰라도, 더 무르익은 숙고를 거치고 나면 모두를 흡족하게 할 것이 분명하다. 현명하고 선한 사람들은 업튼에서 존스에게 닥친 일을 여자 문제에서 저지른 그의 못된 짓에 대한 정당한 벌로 여길 수 있는데, 그것은 실로 그 직접적인 결과였기 때문이다. 또 어리석고 못된 자들은 사람의 됨됨이가 덕보다는 우연에 달린 것이라고 제 마음을 다독이며 제 악덕 속에서 위안을 삼을 수도 있다. 그런데 어쩌면 우리가 이 자리에서 끌어내고 싶어질 성찰은 이 두 결론을 똑같이 반박할 것이며, 이 사건들이 다만, 이 작품 전체가 새겨 넣고자 하는 그 위대하고 유익하며 흔치 않은 가르침을 확증하는 데에만 이바지함을 보여 줄 것이다. 그리고 우리는 그 가르침을 자주 되풀이하느라 지면을 채워서는 안 된다. 마치 평범한 목사가 단락마다 끝에서 성경 구절을 되읊어 설교를 채우듯이 해서는 안 되는 것이다.\n\n우리는 이 한 가지가 분명히 드러나는 것으로 만족한다. 소피아가 존스에 대한 자신의 판단에서 아무리 불행하게 잘못 짚었다 해도, 그녀에게는 그렇게 판단할 충분한 이유가 있었다는 사실 말이다. 내가 믿기로는, 다른 어떤 젊은 아가씨라도 그녀의 처지였다면 똑같이 잘못 짚었을 것이다. 아니, 만약 그녀가 바로 이때 연인의 뒤를 좇아, 그가 떠난 바로 그 순간에 이 술집에 들어섰더라면, 그녀는 업튼의 계집이 그러했던 것만큼이나 이곳 주인 역시 자신의 이름과 인물을 훤히 알고 있음을 알게 되었으리라. 존스가 안쪽 방에서 소년을 속삭이며 캐묻는 동안, 그런 조심성이라곤 기질에 없는 파트리지가 부엌에서 피츠패트릭 부인을 모셨던 다른 안내인을 아주 대놓고 문답하듯 추궁하고 있었기 때문이다. 그 바람에, 이런 일에는 늘 귀를 활짝 열어 두는 주인은 소피아가 말에서 굴러떨어진 일이며, 제니 캐머런에 관한 오해며, 펀치주가 빚어낸 갖가지 결말이며, 한마디로, 우리가 두 부인과 마지막으로 작별하며 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차에 태워 떠나보냈던 그 여관에서 일어난 일이라면 거의 모두를 속속들이 알게 되었던 것이다.\n\n9장.\n별스러운 소견 몇 가지를 담은 것 외에는 별다른 내용이 없는 장.\n\n존스가 자리를 비운 지 꼬박 반 시간이 지났을 무렵, 그는 다급히 주방으로 돌아와 주인에게 당장 계산할 금액을 알려 달라고 청했다. 그리고 이제, 따뜻한 굴뚝 모퉁이와 더없이 좋은 술 한 잔을 두고 일어서야 하는 데서 파트리지가 느낀 서운함은, 더 이상 걸어서 갈 필요가 없다는 말을 듣고 어느 정도 위로를 받았다. 존스가 황금빛 논리로 마부 소년을 설득해, 전에 소피아를 데려다준 그 여관까지 자신을 다시 안내하도록 만들었던 것이다. 그러나 소년은 다른 안내인이 술집에서 자기를 기다려 준다는 조건으로만 이에 응했다. 어퍼턴의 여관 주인이 글로스터의 여관 주인과 친한 사이라, 자기 말들을 한 사람 이상에게 빌려주었다는 사실이 언젠가 후자의 귀에 들어갈 수도 있고, 그러면 소년이 슬쩍 제 주머니에 챙겨 넣을 작정이던 돈에 대해 추궁을 당할 수도 있었기 때문이다.\n\n사소해 보일지 몰라도 우리는 이 사정을 언급하지 않을 수 없었다. 이 일이 존스 씨의 출발을 상당히 지체시켰기 때문이다. 이 두 번째 소년의 정직함은 다소 높았던 것이다. 다시 말해 다소 비쌌고, 실로 존스에게 큰 대가를 치르게 했을 것이다. 앞서 말했듯 매우 약삭빠른 친구였던 파트리지가, 소년이 동료를 기다리는 동안 바로 그 술집에서 쓰라며 슬쩍 반 크라운을 던져 넣지 않았더라면 말이다. 여관 주인은 이 반 크라운의 냄새를 맡자마자, 어찌나 격렬하고 설득력 있는 외침으로 그것을 좇았던지, 소년은 곧 넘어가 자기가 머무는 값으로 반 크라운을 더 받기로 했다. 여기서 우리는 한마디 하지 않을 수 없다. 가장 낮은 삶 속에도 이토록 많은 처세술이 깃들어 있으니, 위대한 인물들은 흔히 자기들이 인간 가운데 가장 미천한 자들에게 도리어 능가당하는 그 속임수의 세련됨을 두고 스스로를 지나치게 높이 평가하곤 한다는 것이다.\n\n이제 말들이 끌려 나오자, 존스는 곧장 사랑하는 소피아가 탔던 옆안장에 뛰어올랐다. 소년은 매우 공손하게 자기 안장을 쓰라고 권했지만, 그는 옆안장을 택했다. 아마도 그쪽이 더 푹신했기 때문이리라. 그러나 존스 못지않게 여성스러운 파트리지는, 제 사내다움을 깎아내린다는 생각만은 견딜 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 소년의 제안을 받아들였다. 이리하여 존스는 소피아의 옆안장에 올라타고, 소년은 어너 부인의 안장에, 파트리지는 세 번째 말에 다리를 벌려 올라탄 채, 그들은 길을 떠나 네 시간 만에 독자가 이미 그토록 많은 시간을 보낸 그 여관에 도착했다. 파트리지는 가는 내내 기분이 무척 들떠 있었고, 최근에 자기를 도와준 앞날의 성공에 대한 여러 좋은 징조를 존스에게 자주 이야기했다. 그것들은 독자가 조금도 미신을 믿지 않는다 해도 유난히 운이 좋았다고 인정할 수밖에 없는 일들이었다. 게다가 파트리지는 동료가 지금 좇고 있는 일이, 전에 좇던 영광보다 더 마음에 들었다. 그리고 이 훈장에게 성공을 확신케 한 바로 그 징조들로부터, 그는 또한 존스와 소피아 사이의 연정에 대해 처음으로 분명한 생각을 얻게 되었다. 그는 전에는 거기에 거의 주의를 기울이지 않았는데, 애초에 존스가 떠난 이유를 두고 엉뚱한 냄새를 좇았던 까닭이었다. 그리고 어퍼턴에서 일어난 일로 말하자면, 그는 그곳을 떠나기 직전과 직후에 너무 겁에 질려 있어, 거기서 가엾은 존스가 영락없는 미치광이라는 것 말고는 다른 결론을 끌어낼 수가 없었다. 이런 짐작은, 그가 전부터 존스의 유별난 광기에 대해 품고 있던 견해와 조금도 어긋나지 않았다. 그는 글로스터를 떠날 때 보인 존스의 행동이야말로, 전에 들었던 온갖 소문을 그대로 입증해 준다고 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 이제 그는 지금의 이 여정에 꽤 만족했고, 이때부터 친구의 분별력에 대해 한결 더 후한 평가를 품기 시작했다.\n\n시계가 막 세 시를 쳤을 때 그들은 도착했고, 존스는 곧바로 역마를 청했다. 그러나 공교롭게도 그 마을 전체에 구할 수 있는 말이 한 마리도 없었다. 이 시기 온 나라가, 특히 이 지역이 얼마나 분주하게 돌아가고 있었는지, 그리고 밤낮 가리지 않고 매시간 급사들이 오가고 있었는지를 헤아려 본다면, 독자는 이를 조금도 이상하게 여기지 않을 것이다.\n\n존스는 전에 안내해 준 소년에게 코번트리까지 호위해 달라고 갖은 애를 썼지만, 소년은 요지부동이었다. 그가 여관 마당에서 소년과 실랑이를 벌이고 있을 때, 한 사람이 다가와 그의 이름을 부르며 인사하고는 서머싯셔의 그 좋은 가문 식구들이 모두 어떻게 지내느냐고 물었다. 이제 존스가 그 사람에게 눈길을 던지니, 곧 글로스터에서 함께 식사했던 변호사 다울링 씨임을 알아보고는, 매우 정중하게 인사를 되받았다.\n\n다울링은 존스 씨에게 그날 밤은 더 가지 말라고 무척 간곡히 권하며, 반박할 수 없는 여러 이유로 그 청을 거들었다. 이를테면 날이 거의 저물었다는 것, 길이 몹시 질척하다는 것, 날이 밝으면 훨씬 수월하게 갈 수 있으리라는 것, 그 밖에도 그에 못지않게 그럴듯한 이유들이 여럿이었는데, 그중 몇몇은 존스가 이미 스스로 떠올렸던 것일 터였다. 그러나 그때도 그 이유들이 소용없었듯, 지금도 마찬가지였다. 그는 설령 걸어서 출발해야 할지라도 자기 계획을 굽히지 않고 단호했다.\n\n이 선량한 변호사는 존스를 머물게 할 수 없음을 알자, 이번에는 마부 소년을 설득하는 데 그에 못지않게 힘을 쏟았다. 그는 이 짧은 여정을 맡도록 소년을 부추기는 여러 동기를 들이댔고, 마침내 이렇게 말로 끝맺었다. \"저 신사분이 자네 수고에 두둑이 보답하지 않을 성싶은가?\"\n\n둘이 하나를 상대하면 유리한 법인데, 이는 축구뿐 아니라 다른 모든 일에서도 그러하다. 그러나 이렇게 힘을 합쳤을 때 설득이나 간청에서 얻는 이점은 눈썰미 있는 관찰자라면 분명히 보아 왔을 것이다. 아버지든 주인이든 아내든, 권위 있는 그 누구든, 한 사람이 내놓을 수 있는 온갖 이유에 맞서 완강히 거절을 고수하다가도, 두 번째나 세 번째 사람이 그 일을 떠맡아 똑같은 말을 되풀이하면 — 그를 위해 새로운 것이라곤 아무것도 보태지 않았음에도 — 끝내 굴복하는 일을 자주 보았을 테니 말이다. 그리고 아마 여기서 어떤 주장이나 동의안을 \"재청한다\"는 말이 나왔을 것이며, 이것이 모든 공개 토론의 회합에서 큰 비중을 차지하는 까닭도 여기에 있을 것이다. 또한 우리 법정에서, 한 박식한 신사(대개는 변호사)가 바로 앞서 말한 다른 박식한 신사의 말을 한 시간 내내 되풀이하는 것을 우리가 자주 듣게 되는 것도, 아마 이 때문이리라.\n\n이를 설명하는 대신, 우리는 늘 하던 방식대로 앞서 언급한 소년의 행동으로 이를 예시해 보겠다. 소년은 다울링 씨의 설득에 넘어가, 다시 한번 존스를 자기 옆안장에 태우기로 약속했다. 다만 먼저 가엾은 짐승들에게 여물을 든든히 먹여야 한다고 우겼다. 녀석들이 먼 길을 왔고 몹시 호되게 몰렸다는 것이었다. 사실 소년의 이 조심성은 군더더기였다. 존스는 그토록 다급하고 안달이 났음에도, 누가 시키지 않아도 제 손으로 그렇게 시켰을 것이기 때문이다. 그는 동물을 한낱 기계로 여기는 자들의 생각에 결코 동의하지 않았으니, 그런 자들은 말의 배에 박차를 깊이 박으면서도 박차와 말이 똑같이 고통을 못 느낀다고 여기는 것이다.\n\n짐승들이 옥수수를 먹는 동안, 아니 차라리 먹는다고 여겨지는 동안(소년이 주방에서 제 몸을 챙기는 사이, 마구간지기는 그 옥수수가 마구간에서 축나지 않도록 단단히 챙겼으니 말이다), 존스 씨는 다울링 씨의 간곡한 청에 따라 그 신사를 따라 방으로 들어갔고, 거기서 두 사람은 포도주 한 병을 사이에 두고 마주 앉았다.\n\n존스 씨와 다울링 씨가 함께 술 한 병을 나누다.\n\n다울링 씨는 포도주를 한 잔 따르더니 선량한 올워디 영주의 건강을 위해 잔을 들자고 했다. 그러고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"괜찮으시다면, 선생, 그분의 조카이자 상속자인 젊은 영주의 건강도 함께 기리지요. 자, 선생, 블리필 씨를 위하여. 참으로 훤칠한 젊은 신사이고, 감히 장담하건대 앞으로 자기 고장에서 상당한 인물이 될 분입니다. 제 마음속에는 이미 그분을 위해 점찍어 둔 선거구까지 있답니다.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"당신이 나를 모욕할 뜻이 없다는 건 알겠으니, 화를 내지는 않겠습니다. 하지만 분명히 말하건대, 당신은 도저히 어울리지 않는 두 사람을 한자리에 묶어 놓았습니다. 한 사람은 인류의 영광이고, 다른 한 사람은 인간이라는 이름을 더럽히는 악당이니까요.\"\n\n다울링은 이 말에 눈이 휘둥그레졌다. 그가 말했다. \"저는 두 분 다 나무랄 데 없는 인품을 지닌 줄로 알았습니다만. 올워디 영주로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 그분을 직접 뵙는 행운을 한 번도 누리지 못했습니다. 그래도 세상 사람들이 모두 그분의 선량함을 입에 올리지요. 그리고 정말이지, 그 젊은 신사분은 딱 한 번 뵈었을 뿐입니다. 그분께 어머님이 돌아가셨다는 소식을 전하러 갔을 때였지요. 그때는 일이 어찌나 많았던지 정신없이 휘몰리고 쫓기고 찢겨서 그분과 이야기할 틈도 거의 없었습니다. 그래도 정말 정직한 신사처럼 보이셨고 행동도 어찌나 반듯하시던지, 단언컨대 저는 태어나서 그 어떤 신사에게서도 그토록 큰 기쁨을 느껴 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"놀랍지도 않군요,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"그토록 짧은 만남에서 그가 당신을 속였다는 게 말입니다. 그자는 악마 그 자체의 교활함을 지녔으니, 당신이 그와 여러 해를 함께 산다 해도 그 정체를 알아차리지 못할 겁니다. 나는 어릴 적부터 그와 함께 자랐고, 우리는 거의 떨어져 본 적이 없었습니다. 그런데도 그 안에 든 악행의 절반이나마 깨달은 것은 아주 최근의 일입니다. 솔직히 나는 한 번도 그를 그리 좋아하지 않았습니다. 그에게는 인간 본성의 위대하고 고귀한 모든 것의 든든한 토대가 되는 그 너그러운 정신이 없다고 생각했지요. 오래전부터 나는 그에게서 내가 경멸하던 이기심을 보아 왔습니다. 하지만 그가 가장 비열하고 가장 시커먼 음모까지 꾸밀 수 있는 자임을 알게 된 것은 아주, 정말 최근의 일입니다. 그렇습니다, 나는 마침내 알아냈습니다. 그자가 내 솔직한 성품을 이용해, 오랜 세월 사악한 술책을 한 줄로 엮어 가며 나를 파멸시킬 깊디깊은 계략을 짜 왔고, 결국 그것을 이루어 냈다는 것을요.\"\n\n\"이런! 이런!\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 단언컨대, 그런 자가 당신 백부 올워디 님의 그 큰 재산을 물려받는다는 건 참으로 안타까운 일이군요.\"\n\n\"아, 선생,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"당신은 제게 어울리지 않는 영예를 안겨 주시는군요. 사실 한때는 그분의 선량함 덕분에 제가 그분을 훨씬 가까운 이름으로 부를 자유를 누리기는 했습니다. 하지만 그것은 오로지 자발적인 선의의 행위였을 뿐이니, 그분이 그 영예를 제게서 거두는 것이 마땅하다 여기신다 해도 저는 어떤 부당함도 호소할 수 없습니다. 그 상실이 부당하다 한들, 애초에 그 선물이 받을 자격 없이 주어진 것보다 더 부당할 수는 없으니까요. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 선생, 저는 올워디 씨와 아무런 혈연도 아닙니다. 그러니 그분의 덕에 참된 가치를 매길 줄 모르는 세상 사람들이, 저를 대하는 그분의 처사를 두고 그분이 친척을 모질게 대했다고 여긴다면, 그것은 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 분께 부당한 짓을 하는 것입니다. 저로 말하자면—아니, 용서하십시오, 제 자신에 관한 자질구레한 사연으로 당신을 번거롭게 하지는 않겠습니다. 다만 당신이 저를 올워디 씨의 친척으로 여기시는 듯하기에, 그분께 비난이 쏟아질 수도 있는 일만큼은 바로잡아 두는 것이 옳다고 여겼을 뿐입니다. 단언컨대 저는 그런 빌미를 드리느니 차라리 제 목숨을 내놓겠습니다.\"\n\n\"단언컨대, 선생,\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"당신은 참으로 명예를 아는 분처럼 말씀하시는군요. 하지만 저를 번거롭게 하기는커녕, 정말이지 당신이 그분의 친척이 아니면서 어떻게 친척으로 여겨지게 되었는지 듣는다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다. 말들이 준비되려면 아직 반 시간은 남았고, 시간도 넉넉하니, 그 모든 일이 어찌 된 것인지 들려주시면 좋겠습니다. 단언컨대 친척도 아니면서 한 신사의 친척으로 통했다는 것이 참으로 놀랍게 여겨지니 말입니다.\"\n\n존스는 (분별력에서는 아니었으나) 사람 좋은 성품에서만큼은 사랑스러운 소피아를 조금 닮은 터라, 어렵지 않게 다울링 씨의 호기심을 채워 주기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그는 자기 출생과 성장의 내력을 이야기했는데, 마치 오셀로처럼 풀어놓았다.\n\n소년 시절부터 바로 그 이야기를 청해 들은 순간에 이르기까지.\n\n다울링은, 마치 데스데모나처럼, 그 이야기에 진지하게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n그는 맹세했네, 기이하다고, 더없이 기이하다고. 가엾다고, 한없이 가엾다고.\n\n다울링 씨는 실로 이 이야기에 크게 마음이 움직였다. 변호사가 되었다고 해서 그가 인간다움을 벗어 버린 것은 아니었기 때문이다. 사실 어떤 직업에 대한 우리의 편견을 사사로운 삶에까지 끌고 들어가, 그 사람의 생업에 대한 견해로 그 사람 자체를 단정하는 것만큼 부당한 일도 없다. 물론 습관이란 그 직업이 어쩔 수 없이 요구하고, 그래서 몸에 밴 행위들의 끔찍함을 무디게 만들기는 한다. 하지만 그 밖의 모든 경우에는, 자연은 어떤 직업의 사람에게서도 똑같이 작용한다. 아니, 어쩌면 평소 자기 생업을 좇느라 자연에게, 말하자면 휴일을 주는 사람들에게서 한층 더 강하게 작용할지도 모른다. 푸주한이라도 훌륭한 말을 도살하는 데에는 틀림없이 양심의 가책을 느낄 것이며, 외과의가 팔다리를 잘라 내며 아무 고통도 느끼지 않는다 해도, 나는 그가 통풍 발작으로 괴로워하는 사람을 가엾이 여기는 것을 본 적이 있다. 수백 명의 목을 늘여 놓은 평범한 교수형 집행인도, 처음 한 사람의 머리를 다룰 때에는 떨었다고 전해진다. 그리고 전쟁이라는 자기 생업 속에서 동료 군인은 물론이거니와 흔히 여자와 아이들까지 수천 명을 거리낌 없이 학살하는, 그야말로 사람 피 흘리는 일을 업으로 삼은 자들조차—그런 자들조차, 내 말하건대, 북과 나팔을 거두어 둔 평화로운 시절에는 흔히 그 모든 흉포함을 내려놓고 시민 사회의 더없이 온순한 일원이 된다. 마찬가지로 변호사도, 자기가 맞서야 할 상대가 아니기만 하다면, 같은 인간이 겪는 온갖 비참과 고통을 고스란히 느낄 수 있는 것이다.\n\n독자도 알다시피, 존스는 자신이 올워디 씨에게 얼마나 시커먼 빛깔로 그려져 있었는지를 아직 알지 못했다. 그리고 다른 일들에 대해서도 그는 가장 불리한 빛으로 드러내지는 않았다. 옛 벗이자 후원자에게 어떤 허물도 씌우고 싶지 않았던 한편으로, 그렇다고 너무 많은 짐을 제 자신에게 쌓아 올리고 싶지도 않았기 때문이다. 그래서 다울링은, 나름의 근거를 가지고, 누군가가 그에게 아주 못된 짓을 했음에 틀림없다고 보았다. \"분명히,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"영주께서 한낱 몇 가지 잘못만으로, 그것도 어느 젊은 신사라도 저질렀을 법한 잘못만으로 당신의 상속권을 박탈하셨을 리가 없습니다. 사실, 상속권을 박탈했다고 말하는 것도 온당치는 않지요. 법으로 따지면 당신이 상속인으로 권리를 주장할 수는 없으니까요. 그건 분명합니다. 그 점은 누구도 변호사를 찾아가 물어볼 필요조차 없는 일이지요. 그러나 한 신사분이 어떤 의미로든 당신을 친아들처럼 거두어 주었다면, 전부는 아닐지라도 상당히 큰 몫쯤은 기대하는 게 당연했을 겁니다. 아니, 당신이 전부를 기대했다 한들 저는 당신을 탓하지 않겠습니다. 분명 사람이란 누구나 가질 수 있는 만큼 가지려 하는 법이고, 그 때문에 탓할 일은 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"정말이지 당신은 저를 오해하고 계십니다,\" 존스가 말했다. \"저는 아주 적은 것으로도 만족했을 겁니다. 저는 단 한 번도 올워디 씨의 재산에 눈독을 들인 적이 없습니다. 아니, 진실로 말할 수 있건대, 그분이 제게 무엇을 줄 수 있고 무엇을 줄지를 단 한 번도 헤아려 본 적이 없습니다. 엄숙히 단언하건대, 만일 그분이 저를 위해 조카에게 손해를 끼치는 일을 하셨다면, 저는 그것을 도로 되돌려 놓았을 겁니다. 저는 남의 재산을 누리느니 차라리 제 마음을 누리는 편을 택하겠습니다. 웅장한 저택, 수많은 종복과 마차, 호화로운 식탁, 그 밖의 온갖 재산의 이점이나 겉모습에서 비롯되는 그 초라한 자만심이, 너그럽고 덕스럽고 고귀하고 자애로운 행위를 돌이켜 볼 때 선한 마음이 누리는 그 따뜻하고 단단한 만족, 부풀어 오르는 흡족함, 짜릿한 황홀, 의기양양한 승리감에 견줄 수나 있겠습니까? 저는 블리필이 누릴 재산의 전망을 부러워하지 않으며, 그가 그것을 손에 넣은들 부러워하지 않을 겁니다. 처지를 맞바꾸자고 단 반 시간이라도 제 자신을 악당이라 여기고 싶지 않습니다. 사실 블리필 씨는 제가 당신이 말한 그런 속셈을 품었다고 의심한 듯합니다. 그리고 짐작건대 그런 의심은 제 마음이 아니라 그 자신의 비열한 마음에서 비롯된 것이니, 그것이 또한 제게 가한 그자의 비열함의 원인이 되었겠지요. 하지만 하늘에 감사하게도, 저는 압니다, 저는 느낍니다—나는 내 결백을 느낍니다, 벗이여. 그리고 저는 그 느낌을 온 세상과도 바꾸지 않겠습니다. 제가 그 어떤 존재에게도 해를 끼친 적이 없고, 끼치려 마음먹은 적조차 없음을 아는 한 말입니다.\n\n포네 메 피그리스 우비 눌라 캄피스 아르보르 아이스티바 레크레아투르 아우라, 쿼드 라투스 문디 네불라이, 말루스퀘 유피테르 우르겟.\n\n포네 수브 쿠루 니미움 프로핀퀴 솔리스 인 테라 도미니부스 네가타; 둘체 리덴템 랄라겐 아마보, 둘체 로퀜템.[*]\n\n[*] 여름 산들바람도 흙을 풀어 주지 않고 나무를 데워 주지 않는 곳에 나를 두어라. 언제나 잔뜩 내려앉은 구름이 드리우고 성난 유피테르가 험한 계절을 망쳐 놓는 그곳에.\n\n타오르는 햇살 아래 나를 두어라, 대낮의 수레가 빠르게 굴러가는 그곳에. 사랑과 그 처녀가 내 노고를 어루만지리니, 달콤하게 말하고 달콤하게 미소 짓는 그 처녀가. 프랜시스 씨.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 포도주를 잔 가득 채워, 사랑하는 랄라게의 건강을 위해 단숨에 들이켰다. 그리고 다울링의 잔도 가장자리까지 가득 채워, 함께 잔을 들이켤 것을 권했다. \"그렇다면, 자, 진심으로 랄라게 아가씨의 건강을 위하여,\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"단언컨대 저는 그분을 위한 건배를 여러 번 들은 적이 있습니다. 직접 뵌 적은 없습니다만, 더없이 아리따운 분이라고들 하더군요.\"\n\n이 말 가운데 다울링이 온전히 알아듣지 못한 부분이 라틴어만은 아니었다. 그럼에도 그 안에는 그에게 아주 강한 인상을 남긴 무언가가 있었다. 그리고 그는 눈을 찡긋거리고, 고개를 끄덕이고, 비웃는 시늉을 하고, 히죽거리며 그 인상을 존스에게 감추려 애썼지만(우리는 그릇되게 생각할 때만큼이나 바르게 생각할 때에도 자주 부끄러워하는 법이니), 분명한 것은 그가 알아들은 만큼의 그 마음만은 속으로 깊이 수긍했고, 실로 그에게 강한 연민의 충동을 느꼈다는 사실이다. 하지만 이에 대해서는 다른 기회에 논평할 수도 있을 것이다. 특히 우리가 이야기를 풀어 가는 동안 다울링 씨를 다시 만나게 된다면 말이다. 지금으로서는 존스 씨를 본받아, 그 신사와 다소 갑작스럽게 작별할 수밖에 없다. 존스는 패트리지에게서 말이 준비되었다는 말을 듣자마자 셈을 치르고, 동행에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넨 뒤 말에 올라 코번트리를 향해 길을 나섰다. 밤은 어두웠고, 바로 그때 비가 몹시 거세게 내리기 시작했는데도 말이다.\n\n# Chapter xi.\n\n제일런에게는 코번트리로 가는 길보다 더 분명한 길이 없었다. 존스도, 파트리지도, 안내인도 그 길을 가 본 적은 없었지만, 앞 장의 끝에서 말한 두 가지 이유만 아니었다면 길을 잃기란 거의 불가능했을 것이다.\n\n그러나 불운하게도 이 두 가지 사정이 한꺼번에 끼어드는 바람에, 우리의 여행자들은 훨씬 사람의 발길이 드문 샛길로 빠지고 말았다. 그렇게 꼬박 육 마일을 달렸건만, 그들은 코번트리의 웅장한 첨탑에 다다르기는커녕 여전히 몹시 질척한 좁은 길 위에 있었고, 큰 도시의 변두리로 가까이 가고 있다는 어떤 기미도 보이지 않았다.\n\n이제 존스는 자기들이 분명 길을 잃었다고 단언했다. 그러나 안내인은 그럴 리 없다고 우겼다. \"그럴 리 없다\"는 말은 일상 대화에서 단지 일어날 법하지 않다는 뜻뿐 아니라, 흔히 실제로는 매우 그럼직한 일을, 때로는 이미 확실히 일어난 일까지도 가리키는 데 쓰인다. 이는 \"무한하다\"느니 \"영원하다\"느니 하는 말에 그토록 자주 가해지는 것과 같은 과장된 폭력이다. 앞의 말로는 흔히 반 야드의 거리를 나타내고, 뒤의 말로는 오 분의 길이를 나타내곤 하니 말이다. 그러니 이미 실제로 잃어버린 것을 두고 잃어버리기란 불가능하다고 우기는 것도 그만큼 흔한 일이다. 사실 지금이 바로 그런 경우였다. 소년이 아무리 자신만만하게 그 반대를 주장해도, 그들이 코번트리로 가는 옳은 길에 있는 것은, 사기 잘 치고 인색하며 잔인하고 위선적으로 입에 발린 말만 하는 구두쇠가 천국으로 가는 옳은 길에 있는 것만큼이나 어림없는 일이었다.\n\n이런 처지에 한 번도 놓여 본 적 없는 독자가, 밤중에 길을 잃은 사람들을 어둠과 비와 바람이 어떤 공포로 채우는지 상상하기란 아마 쉽지 않을 것이다. 그런 사람들에게는 따뜻한 불, 마른 옷, 그 밖의 위안거리가 마음을 받쳐 주며 모진 날씨와 싸우게 해 줄 흐뭇한 전망조차 없으니 말이다. 그러나 이 공포를 아주 어렴풋이만 떠올려 보아도, 지금 파트리지의 머릿속을 가득 채운 망상들을 설명하기에는 충분할 것이다. 우리는 이제 곧 그 망상을 펼쳐 보일 수밖에 없다.\n\n존스는 자기들이 길을 벗어났다고 점점 더 확신했다. 소년 자신도 마침내 코번트리로 가는 옳은 길에 있는 것 같지 않다고 인정했다. 그러면서도 동시에, 자기들이 길을 잘못 들었을 리는 없다고 우겼다. 그러나 파트리지의 생각은 달랐다. 그가 말했다. \"처음 길을 나설 때부터 저는 무슨 화든 닥칠 거라 짐작했습죠. 나리, 못 보셨습니까,\" 그가 존스에게 말했다, \"나리가 말에 오르실 바로 그때 문가에 서 있던 그 늙은 노파 말입니다. 정말이지 진심으로, 나리가 그 노파에게 푼돈이라도 좀 쥐여 주셨더라면 좋았을 것을요. 그때 노파가 후회하실 거라고 하지 않았습니까. 바로 그 순간 비가 내리기 시작했고, 바람은 그때부터 줄곧 거세지기만 했습죠. 어떤 사람들이야 뭐라 생각하든, 저는 마녀들이 마음만 먹으면 언제든 바람을 일으킬 힘이 있다고 굳게 믿습니다. 저는 살아오면서 그런 일을 아주 여러 번 보았습죠. 그리고 제 평생 마녀를 본 적이 있다면, 그 늙은 노파야말로 분명 마녀였습니다. 바로 그때 속으로 그렇게 생각했고, 만일 제 주머니에 반 페니라도 있었다면 좀 줬을 겁니다. 그런 부류의 사람들에게는 무슨 일이 생길지 모르니 늘 자비를 베푸는 게 좋은 법이거든요. 반 페니 아끼려다 가축을 잃은 사람이 한둘이 아닙니다.\"\n\n존스는 이 실수가 여정에 가져올 지체에 끔찍이도 속이 상했지만, 친구의 미신을 보고는 웃음을 참을 수 없었다. 그런데 마침 한 사고가 일어나 파트리지의 믿음을 크게 굳혀 주었다. 그것은 그가 말에서 굴러떨어진 일이었는데, 다행히 옷이 진흙을 뒤집어쓴 것 말고는 아무 부상도 입지 않았다.\n\n파트리지는 다리를 추스르기 무섭게, 자기가 넘어진 일이야말로 그동안 자기가 주장한 모든 것의 결정적 증거라며 들이댔다. 그러나 존스는 그가 다치지 않은 것을 보고 웃으며 대답했다. \"파트리지, 자네의 그 마녀란 게 참으로 배은망덕한 계집이로군. 보아하니 원한을 풀 때 제 편과 남을 가리지도 않으니 말이야. 그 노파가 자기를 무시했다고 나한테 화가 났다면, 자네가 그토록 깍듯이 떠받들었는데도 자네를 말에서 떨어뜨릴 까닭이 어디 있겠나.\"\n\n\"이런 일을 할 힘을 가진 사람들을 두고 농을 치는 건 좋지 않습니다,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"그런 자들은 흔히 아주 심술궂거든요. 어떤 편자장이가 마녀 하나를 화나게 한 일이 기억납니다. 악마와 흥정해 둔 기한이 언제 끝나느냐고 물어서 약을 올렸지요. 그런데 바로 그날부터 석 달도 채 안 되어 그의 가장 좋은 암소 한 마리가 물에 빠져 죽었습죠. 마녀는 그것으로도 성에 차지 않았습니다. 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 최고급 술 한 통을 잃었거든요. 그가 이웃 몇과 흥을 돋우려고 술통의 마개를 딴 바로 그 첫날 저녁에, 늙은 마녀가 그 꼭지를 뽑아 버려 술이 지하실 바닥에 죄다 흘러내리게 했으니까요. 한마디로 그 후로는 그에게 잘되는 일이 하나도 없었습죠. 마녀가 그 가엾은 사람을 어찌나 들들 볶았는지 그는 술독에 빠졌고, 한두 해 만에 가산을 압류당해 지금은 그와 그 식구들이 교구 구호 신세를 지고 있답니다.\"\n\n안내인은, 그리고 어쩌면 그의 말까지도 이 이야기에 어찌나 정신이 팔렸던지, 부주의 탓이든 마녀의 심술 탓이든 이제 둘 다 진흙탕 속에 큰대자로 나뒹굴고 말았다.\n\n파트리지는 자기가 넘어졌을 때와 마찬가지로, 이 추락도 전적으로 같은 원인 탓으로 돌렸다. 그는 존스에게 말했다. \"이제 분명 다음은 나리 차례일 겁니다.\" 그러면서 부디 되돌아가 그 노파를 찾아내 달래자고 간곡히 청했다. \"곧 여관에 닿을 겁니다,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"우리가 앞으로 나아온 것 같지만, 저는 한 시간 전에 있던 바로 그 자리에 우리가 여태 있다고 확신합니다. 감히 맹세하건대, 날이 밝았다면 우리가 출발한 그 여관이 지금 보일 겁니다.\"\n\n이 현명한 충고에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않은 채, 존스는 오로지 소년에게 일어난 일에만 정신을 쏟고 있었다. 소년은 앞서 파트리지가 당한 것 말고는 아무 상처도 입지 않았는데, 여러 해 동안 그런 일에 단련되어 온 그의 옷은 그 정도쯤 거뜬히 견뎌 냈다. 소년은 곧 자기 안장에 다시 올라앉았고, 자기 말에게 호되게 욕설을 퍼붓고 매질을 가하는 것으로, 아무 탈도 없음을 존스에게 금세 납득시켰다.\n\n# Chapter xii.\n\n존스 씨가 파트리지의 충고를 거스르고 여행을 계속한 일과, 그 자리에서 벌어진 일을 이야기하다.\n\n이윽고 두 사람은 멀찍이서 불빛 하나를 발견했는데, 이는 존스에게는 더없는 기쁨이었으나 파트리지에게는 적지 않은 공포였다. 그는 자신이 마법에 걸렸다고 굳게 믿고 있었고, 저 불빛이야말로 도깨비불이거나 어쩌면 그보다 더 짓궂은 무언가라고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그런데 이 불빛(이제는 여러 개의 불빛으로 보였다)에 점점 다가가면서 사람 목소리가 뒤섞인 소리, 노래하고 웃고 떠드는 소리가, 게다가 어떤 악기에서 나는 듯하면서도 도무지 음악이라 부르기 어려운 기묘한 소음까지 들려오자 그의 두려움이 얼마나 더 커졌겠는가! 실로 파트리지의 견해에 조금이나마 힘을 실어 주자면, 그것은 마법에 걸린 음악이라 불러도 좋을 만했다.\n\n지금 파트리지를 사로잡은 것보다 더한 공포는 상상하기 어려웠다. 그 공포의 전염은 마부에게까지 번졌으니, 마부는 파트리지가 내뱉은 여러 말을 줄곧 귀 기울여 듣고 있던 터였다. 이제 그도 존스에게 돌아가자고 함께 간청하며, 조금 전 파트리지가 한 말, 곧 말들이 앞으로 나아가는 듯 보여도 적어도 지난 반 시간 동안 단 한 걸음도 앞으로 움직이지 않았다는 말을 굳게 믿는다고 했다.\n\n존스는 짜증이 치미는 와중에도 이 가엾은 친구들의 두려움에 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었다. \"우리가 저 불빛을 향해 나아가고 있든지,\" 그가 말했다. \"아니면 저 불빛이 우리를 향해 다가왔든지 둘 중 하나일세. 이제 우리는 저것들과 아주 가까운 거리에 있으니 말이야. 그런데 그저 흥겹게 잔치를 벌이는 듯한 사람들을 보고 자네들 둘이 어찌 무서워할 수 있단 말인가?\"\n\n\"잔치라니요, 나리!\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"이런 한밤중에, 이런 곳에서, 이런 날씨에 누가 흥겨운 잔치를 벌이겠습니까? 저것들은 유령이나 마녀, 아니면 무슨 악령 같은 것일 수밖에 없습니다. 그건 분명해요.\"\n\n\"그것들이 무엇이든 간에,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"나는 저들에게 다가가 코번트리로 가는 길을 물어보기로 마음먹었네. 파트리지, 모든 마녀가 우리가 불행히도 지난번에 만난 그 노파처럼 심술궂은 것은 아닐세.\"\n\n\"오 맙소사, 나리,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"저들이 어떤 기분일지는 알 수가 없습니다. 물론 저들에게 공손하게 구는 것이 언제나 상책이지요. 하지만 만일 우리가 마녀보다 더 고약한 것, 바로 악령 그 자체를 만나기라도 하면 어쩝니까?——제발 나리, 제 말을 들으십시오. 제발요, 나리. 만일 나리께서 제가 이런 일들에 관해 읽은 그 숱한 끔찍한 이야기들을 읽으셨다면, 이렇게 무모하게 굴지는 않으실 겁니다.——주님만이 우리가 이미 어디까지 왔는지, 또 어디로 가고 있는지 아실 겁니다. 정말이지 이 땅 위에 이런 어둠은 일찍이 없었으니, 저세상이라 한들 이보다 더 어두울 수 있을지 의심스럽습니다.\"\n\n이 모든 암시와 경고에도 아랑곳없이 존스는 할 수 있는 한 빨리 앞으로 나아갔고, 가엾은 파트리지도 따라갈 수밖에 없었다. 앞으로 나아가기란 거의 엄두도 못 냈으나, 혼자 뒤에 처지는 것은 그보다 더 엄두가 나지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n마침내 그들은 불빛과 갖가지 소리가 새어 나오던 그곳에 다다랐다. 존스가 보니 그곳은 다름 아닌 헛간이었는데, 많은 남녀가 모여서 겉보기에도 무척 흥겹게 놀고 있었다.\n\n존스가 활짝 열린 헛간의 큰 문 앞에 모습을 드러내자마자, 안쪽에서 사내답고도 몹시 거친 목소리가 거기 누구냐고 물었다.——이에 존스는 부드럽게 친구라고 답하고는 곧바로 코번트리로 가는 길을 물었다.\n\n\"친구라면,\" 헛간 안의 다른 사내가 외쳤다. \"폭풍이 그칠 때까지 말에서 내리는 게 좋겠소.\" (실로 폭풍은 그 어느 때보다 거세졌던 것이다.) \"말은 얼마든지 들여놓으셔도 좋소. 헛간 끝에 그 녀석이 들어갈 자리가 넉넉하니 말이오.\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하시군요.\" 존스가 답했다. \"비가 계속되는 동안 잠깐 그 호의를 받아들이겠소. 그리고 여기 같은 호의를 반길 두 사람이 더 있소.\" 이 제안은 받아들여진 것보다 더 흔쾌히 베풀어진 것이었다. 파트리지로서는 자신이 도깨비라고 여긴 자들의 너그러움에 몸을 맡기느니 차라리 날씨의 모진 매서움에 굴복하는 편이 나았을 테고, 가엾은 마부도 이제 같은 불안에 물들어 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 두 사람 모두 존스의 본을 따를 수밖에 없었다. 한쪽은 말을 두고 갈 엄두가 나지 않아서였고, 다른 쪽은 혼자 남겨지는 것만큼 두려운 일이 없어서였다.\n\n이 이야기가 미신이 횡행하던 시절에 쓰였더라면, 나는 독자를 너무도 가엾이 여긴 나머지, 바알세불이나 사탄이 그 온갖 지옥의 무리를 거느리고 정말로 친히 모습을 드러내려는지를 두고 그토록 오래 마음 졸이게 두지는 않았을 것이다. 하지만 이런 교리들은 요즘 들어 무척이나 운이 다하여, 믿는 이가 있다 한들 거의 없는 형편이니, 나는 그런 공포를 전할 마음을 별로 두지 않았다. 사실대로 말하자면, 지옥 영역의 온갖 소품은 진작에 극장 운영자들이 가져다 써 버렸는데, 그들마저 요즘은 그것들을 위층 맨 꼭대기 관람석이나 흔들어 놓을 만한 쓰레기로 여겨 한쪽에 처박아 둔 듯하다. 그런데 그 자리에는 우리 독자 가운데 앉는 이가 거의 없다.\n\n그러나 이번 일로 굳이 큰 공포를 불러일으킬 생각은 없다 해도, 우리는 독자에게 다른 어떤 우려가 여기서 생겨날까 염려할 까닭이 있는데, 우리는 독자를 기꺼이 그런 우려에 빠뜨리고 싶지 않다. 다시 말해 우리가 요정 나라로 항해를 떠나, 어느 누구도 그토록 어린아이같이 믿은 적이 거의 없는—그러면서도 그들의 모험담을 쓰고 읽는 데 시간을 허비할 만큼 어리석은 이는 많았던—그런 존재의 무리를 우리 이야기 안에 끌어들이려 한다고 여길까 봐 하는 말이다.\n\n그러므로 오직 자연에서만 소재를 끌어온다고 표방하는 한 사가의 신용에 그토록 해가 될 이런 의심을 막기 위해, 우리는 이제 그 사람들이 누구였는지 독자에게 알려 주기로 하겠다. 그들이 느닷없이 나타나는 바람에 파트리지는 그토록 큰 공포에 사로잡혔고, 마부는 반 넘게 겁에 질렸으며, 심지어 존스 씨조차 조금은 놀랐던 것이다.\n\n그렇다면 이 헛간에 모인 사람들은 다름 아닌 이집트인 한 무리, 흔히들 집시라 부르는 사람들이었고, 그들은 지금 자기네 무리 가운데 한 사람의 결혼식을 축하하고 있는 참이었다.\n\n여기 한자리에 모인 이들보다 더 행복한 무리는 상상하기 어려웠다. 실로 더없는 흥겨움이 얼굴마다 드러나 있었다. 그렇다고 그들의 무도회에 질서와 예법이 통째로 빠져 있는 것도 아니었다. 어쩌면 시골 모임이 흔히 치러지는 것보다 더 갖추어져 있었는지도 모른다. 이 사람들은 자기네 나름의 격식 있는 통치와 법에 따르고 있었고, 모두가 한 사람의 위대한 통치자에게 복종을 바쳤는데, 그들은 그를 왕이라 불렀다.\n\n또한 이 헛간에 넘쳐나던 만큼 풍성한 광경도 어디서고 보기 어려웠다. 여기에는 실로 정교함도 우아함도 없었으나, 손님들의 날선 식욕은 그런 것을 필요로 하지도 않았다. 여기에는 베이컨과 가금과 양고기가 푸짐하게 갖추어져 있었고, 자리에 있는 누구나 가장 솜씨 좋고 값비싼 프랑스 요리사가 만들 수 있는 것보다 더 나은 양념을 제 스스로 마련해 두고 있었다.\n\n아이네이아스가 유노 신전에서 보였던 것보다 더한 경악에 휩싸여 있다고 묘사되지는 않았으니,\n\n망연자실하여 한곳에 시선을 못 박은 채 멍하니 서 있다,\n\n우리 주인공이 이 헛간에서 본 것에 사로잡힌 모습이야말로 그러했다. 그가 놀라움에 사방을 둘러보고 있을 때, 한 위엄 있는 인물이 정중하다 하기엔 다소 지나치게 푸근한 인사를 잔뜩 건네며 그에게 다가왔다. 이 사람이 바로 다름 아닌 집시들의 왕이었다. 그는 차림새로는 신하들과 거의 구별되지 않았고, 위엄을 떠받칠 만한 왕권의 표장도 지니지 않았다. 그런데도 (존스 씨의 말로는) 그의 풍모에는 권위를 나타내고 보는 이에게 경외와 존경의 관념을 불어넣는 무언가가 있는 듯했다. 물론 이 모든 것이 어쩌면 존스의 상상에 지나지 않았을 수도 있고, 실은 그런 관념이란 권력에 으레 따라붙는 것이어서 권력과 거의 떼어 놓을 수 없는 것인지도 모른다.\n\n존스의 트인 얼굴빛과 정중한 몸가짐에는 무언가가 있어서, 거기에 빼어난 풍채까지 어우러지니 첫눈에 보는 이마다 그에게 크게 호감을 품게 만들었다. 이번 경우에는 이런 점들이 어쩌면 조금 더 돋보였을 텐데, 그가 집시들의 왕에게 그 신분을 알게 된 순간 바친 깊은 존경 덕분이었다. 또한 그 존경은 집시 왕에게 한결 더 달콤했으니, 자기 신하 말고는 누구에게서도 그런 경의를 받아 본 적이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n왕은 그를 대접하려고 자기네 음식 가운데 가장 좋은 것으로 식탁을 차리라 명했다. 그러고는 그의 오른편에 자리를 잡고서, 폐하께서 우리 주인공에게 다음과 같이 이야기를 건네기 시작했다.——\n\n\"손님, 내 백성 가운데 몇몇을 자주 보셨을 게요, 그대들 말로 척후대라 부르는 자들 말이오. 그들은 어디든 돌아다니니까 말이오. 하지만 우리가 이만큼 대단한 무리인 줄은 미처 상상 못 하셨을 게요. 그리고 집시가 이 땅 위의 어느 백성 못지않게 질서 있고 잘 다스려지는 백성이라는 걸 들으면 더욱 놀라실지도 모르겠소.\n\n\"내가 말했듯, 나는 그들의 왕인 영예를 누리고 있는데, 어떤 군주도 이보다 더 충실하고 더 정 깊은 백성을 자랑할 수는 없을 게요. 내가 그들의 호의를 얼마나 받을 만한지는 말하지 않겠소. 다만 이 한 가지는 말할 수 있소. 나는 그들에게 좋은 일을 해 주는 것 말고는 결코 다른 무엇도 꾀한 적이 없다는 거요. 하지만 그것조차 자랑하지는 않겠소. 종일 돌아다니며 자기들이 얻은 것 가운데 늘 가장 좋은 것을 내게 가져다주는 저 가엾은 백성의 행복을 헤아리는 것 말고, 내가 달리 무엇을 할 수 있겠소? 그러니 그들은 나를 사랑하고 받드는 거요, 내가 그들을 사랑하고 보살피기 때문에 말이오. 그게 다요, 나는 다른 까닭을 알지 못하오.\n\n\"한 천 년인지 이천 년인지 전에—나는 글을 쓸 줄도 읽을 줄도 모르니 한두 해까지는 가릴 수 없소만—집시들 사이에 그대들 말로 큰 무어라 하던가, 그래 혁명이 있었소. 그 시절엔 집시 영주라는 게 있었거든. 그 영주들이 자리를 두고 서로 다투었지. 그런데 집시 왕이 그들을 모조리 무너뜨리고 모든 백성을 서로 평등하게 만들었소. 그 뒤로 그들은 아주 화목하게 지냈소. 왕이 될 생각을 하지 않으니 말이오. 어쩌면 지금 이대로가 그들에게 더 나을 게요. 내 장담하건대 왕 노릇을 하며 늘 공정함을 베푼다는 건 무척 골치 아픈 일이오. 내 사랑하는 벗이며 친척을 어쩔 수 없이 벌해야 할 때면, 나는 차라리 한낱 평범한 집시였으면 하고 자주 바랐소. 우리는 결코 사형에 처하지는 않지만, 우리 형벌은 무척 가혹하거든. 그것은 집시로 하여금 제 자신을 부끄럽게 만드는데, 그게 바로 끔찍한 형벌이오. 그렇게 벌받은 집시가 다시 잘못을 저지르는 건 내 일찍이 거의 본 적이 없소.\"\n\n이어 왕은 다른 나라의 통치에는 수치 같은 형벌이 없다는 데에 자못 놀라움을 드러냈다. 이에 존스는 그렇지 않다고 그를 일깨워 주었다. 영국 법으로도 수치를 형벌로 내리는 죄가 많고, 실로 수치는 모든 형벌에 따르는 한 가지 결과라는 것이었다. \"그것참 이상하구려,\" 왕이 말했다. \"나는 그대 백성들 틈에 살지는 않지만 그들에 대해 잘 알고 또 많이 듣소. 그런데 수치란 그대들이 내리는 숱한 상의 결과이자 또한 원인이기도 하다는 말을 자주 들었거든. 그렇다면 그대들의 상과 벌은 같은 것이오?\"\n\n폐하께서 이렇게 존스와 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 헛간 안에서 별안간 소동이 일었으니, 어찌 된 영문인고 하니 이러했다.——이 사람들의 친절함이 차츰 파트리지의 모든 불안을 걷어 내어, 그는 그들의 음식을 배불리 먹었을 뿐만 아니라 그들의 술까지 조금 맛보도록 구슬려졌던 것이다. 그 술은 차츰 그의 마음에서 두려움을 깡그리 몰아내고, 그 자리에 한결 더 흐뭇한 기분을 들여놓았다.\n\n미모보다는 재치로 더 눈에 띄는 한 젊은 집시 여자가 점을 봐 주겠다는 핑계로 이 순진한 사내를 한쪽으로 꾀어냈다. 그런데 그들이 헛간의 외진 구석에 단둘이 있게 되자—그것이 적당한 피로 뒤에 부절제한 욕정을 부추기는 데 그토록 잘 듣는 독한 술 탓이었는지, 아니면 그 어여쁜 집시 여자 스스로 제 성의 곱고 정숙한 태도를 내던지고 노골적인 수작으로 젊은 파트리지를 꾀어낸 것인지—아무튼 둘은 그 집시 여자의 남편에게 무척이나 망측한 꼴로 발각되고 말았다. 그 남편은 질투심에서였는지 제 아내를 줄곧 눈여겨 지켜보다가 그 자리까지 뒤쫓아 와서, 정부의 품에 안긴 아내를 찾아낸 것이었다.\n\n존스가 크게 당황한 가운데, 파트리지는 이제 왕 앞으로 황급히 끌려갔다. 왕은 고발을 듣고, 또한 죄인의 변명도 들었는데, 그 변명이란 실로 보잘것없는 것이었다. 가엾은 사내는 제게 불리하게 드러난 명백한 증거에 어쩔 줄 몰라 했고, 스스로 변호할 말이 거의 없었던 것이다. 그러자 폐하께서 존스를 향해 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"손님, 저들이 하는 말을 들으셨을 게요. 그대 사람이 어떤 벌을 받아 마땅하다고 생각하시오?\"\n\n존스가 답하기를, \"일어난 일이 유감스럽고, 파트리지가 그 남편에게 할 수 있는 한 모든 보상을 해야 마땅하다\"고 했다. 그러면서 \"지금 가진 돈이 무척 적다\"고 말하고는 주머니에 손을 넣어 그 사내에게 1기니를 내밀었다. 이에 사내는 곧바로 \"나리께서 설마 5기니보다 적게 주실 생각은 아니시겠지요\" 하고 답했다.\n\n이 액수는 얼마간 실랑이를 거쳐 2기니로 깎였다. 그래서 존스가 파트리지와 그 아내 둘 다에게 온전한 용서를 베푸는 조건을 내걸고 막 돈을 치르려 할 때, 폐하께서 그의 손을 막고는 증인을 향해 돌아서서 물었다. \"그대는 언제 이 죄인들을 발견했소?\" 이에 그는 답하기를, \"남편의 부탁을 받아, 아내가 그 낯선 자에게 처음 말을 걸 때부터 그 거동을 살폈고, 그 뒤로 죄가 저질러질 때까지 줄곧 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다\"고 했다. 왕이 또 물었다. \"그 내내 남편도 그대와 함께 숨어 있었소?\" 이에 그는 그렇다고 답했다. 그러자 이집트의 폐하께서는 남편을 향해 다음과 같이 말했다. \"제 아내의 명예를 돈에 팔 만큼 명예심이 없는 집시를 보다니 참으로 딱하오. 그대가 아내를 사랑했다면 이 일을 막았어야지, 아내를 들춰내려고 그녀를 창녀로 만들려 들어서는 안 되었소. 나는 그대에게 한 푼도 주지 말라 명하오. 그대는 상이 아니라 벌을 받아 마땅하니 말이오. 그러므로 나는 명하노니, 그대는 수치스러운 집시가 되어 한 달 동안 이마에 뿔 한 쌍을 달고 다니고, 그대 아내는 창녀라 불리며 그동안 내내 손가락질을 받을지어다. 그대는 수치스러운 집시이고, 그녀도 그에 못지않게 수치스러운 창녀이니 말이오.\"\n\n집시들은 곧바로 그 판결을 집행하러 나섰고, 존스와 파트리지만 폐하와 함께 남겨 두었다.\n\n존스는 그 판결의 공정함을 크게 칭송했다. 이에 왕이 그를 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"그대가 놀랐으리라 믿소. 그대는 내 백성을 무척 나쁘게 여길 테니 말이오. 그대는 우리를 모두 도둑이라 여길 테지요.\"\n\n\"솔직히 고백하건대, 폐하,\" 존스가 말했다. \"저는 그들이 받아 마땅해 보이는 것만큼 호의적인 평판을 들어 본 적은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"내 말해 주겠소,\" 왕이 말했다. \"그대들과 우리가 어떻게 다른지를 말이오. 내 백성은 그대 백성을 털지만, 그대 백성은 자기들끼리 서로를 터니 말이오.\"\n\n그 후 존스는 자못 진지하게, 그런 통치자 아래 사는 백성의 행복을 소리 높여 찬미하기 시작했다.\n\n실로 그들의 행복이 어찌나 완전해 보였던지, 우리는 훗날 전제 권력을 옹호하는 누군가가 이 사람들의 경우를 끌어다, 그 통치가 다른 무엇보다 우월하게 따라붙는 크나큰 이점의 한 사례로 들지나 않을까 염려스럽다.\n\n그래서 여기서 우리는, 어쩌면 우리에게서 기대하지 못했을 한 가지를 인정하고자 한다. 곧 어떤 제한된 형태의 통치도 이만큼의 완전함에 이르거나 사회에 이만큼의 이로움을 가져다줄 수는 없다는 것이다. 인류가 당시 알려진 세계의 가장 큰 부분이 단 한 사람의 주인 아래 있었을 때만큼 행복했던 적은 없었다. 그리고 그 복된 상태는 다섯 군주가 잇따라 다스리는 동안 줄곧 이어졌다.[*] 이때야말로 황금시대의 참된 연대였으니, 에덴에서 쫓겨난 이래 오늘에 이르기까지, 시인들의 따뜻한 상상 속에서가 아니고서는 실제로 존재한 적이 있었던 유일한 황금시대였다.\n\n[*] 네르바, 트라야누스, 하드리아누스, 그리고 두 안토니누스 황제.\n\n사실 나는 절대 군주제에 대해 단 하나의 묵직한 반론밖에 알지 못한다. 이 빼어난 정체에 있는 유일한 결함이란, 절대 군주의 직책에 걸맞은 사람을 도무지 찾기 어렵다는 점인 듯하다. 이 직책은 세 가지 자질을 반드시 요구하는데, 역사가 보여 주듯 그것은 군주의 천성에서 좀처럼 찾아보기 어려운 것들이다. 첫째, 군주에게 자신이 가질 수 있는 온갖 권력으로 만족할 만큼의 충분한 절제. 둘째, 자기 자신의 행복을 알아볼 만큼의 충분한 지혜. 셋째, 남의 행복이 제 행복과 양립할 뿐 아니라 제 행복에 보탬이 될 때 그것을 떠받쳐 줄 만큼의 충분한 선함이다.\n\n자, 만일 이 모든 위대하고 드문 자질을 갖춘 절대 군주가 사회에 더없이 큰 선을 베풀 수 있다고 인정한다면, 반대로 그 자질을 죄다 갖추지 못한 자의 손에 쥐어진 절대 권력은 그에 못지않게 큰 악을 동반하기 쉽다는 것 또한 분명 인정해야 하리라.\n\n요컨대, 우리 자신의 종교가 우리에게 절대 권력에 따를 수 있는 축복과 저주에 대한 충분한 관념을 마련해 준다. 천국과 지옥의 그림은 그 둘을 무척 생생한 영상으로 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐 보여 준다. 후자의 군주는 전자에 있는 전능하신 주권자에게서 본디 받은 것 말고는 아무런 권력도 가질 수 없지만, 그래도 성경에 분명히 나타나 있듯이 그 지옥의 영토 안에서의 절대 권력만큼은 그 악마 같은 통치자에게 허락되어 있는 것이다. 실로 이것이 성경에 따라 하늘로부터 받을 수 있는 유일한 절대 권력이다. 그러므로 만일 이 땅 위의 여러 폭정이 신성한 권위에 대한 무슨 자격이라도 입증할 수 있다면, 그것은 어둠의 군주에게 내려진 이 본래의 허락에서 비롯된 것일 수밖에 없다. 따라서 이 하급의 대리권들은 결국, 그것들이 그토록 또렷이 새겨 지니고 있는 그 도장의 주인에게서 곧바로 나온 것일 수밖에 없다.\n\n끝으로, 모든 시대의 사례가 우리에게 보여 주듯 인류는 대개 오로지 해를 끼치기 위해서만 권력을 바라며, 그것을 손에 넣으면 다른 어떤 목적에도 쓰지 않으니, 우리 두려움을 부추기는 천 가지 사례 가운데 고작 두셋의 예외로나 우리 희망이 가까스로 체면치레를 하는 마당에, 변혁을 무릅쓴다는 것은 일말의 분별에도 들어맞지 않는다. 이런 경우에는, 격정에 휩쓸려 활짝 열린 폭군의 귀에 호소하여 폐단을 바로잡으려 하기보다, 차라리 냉정하게 귀먹은 법에서 비롯되는 몇 가지 불편에 감수하는 편이 훨씬 더 지혜로울 것이다.\n\n또한 집시들의 사례는, 비록 그들이 이 통치 형태 아래에서 오래도록 행복했을지 모르나 여기에 끌어다 댈 수 없다. 우리는 그들이 다른 모든 사람과 다른 매우 중대한 점을, 어쩌면 이 행복이 전적으로 거기에 기대고 있을 그 점을 기억해야 하기 때문이다. 곧 그들 사이에는 거짓된 명예가 없으며, 그들은 수치를 이 세상에서 가장 견디기 힘든 형벌로 여긴다는 것이다.\n\n정직하게 자유를 사랑하는 이들이라면, 지난 장 끝머리에서 우리가 빠져들었던 그 긴 여담을 너그러이 보아 주리라 믿어 의심치 않는다. 그것은 다름 아니라, 사제의 술수가 일찍이 사악함이나 뻔뻔함을 무릅쓰고 설파했던 그 가장 해로운 교리에 우리 이야기가 악용되는 것을 막기 위함이었다.\n\n이제 우리는 다시 존스 씨에게로 돌아가자. 그는 소동이 가라앉자 집시 폐하께 정중한 대접과 친절한 환대에 거듭 감사를 표한 뒤 작별을 고하고, 코번트리를 향해 길을 나섰다. 아직 어두웠던 까닭에, 집시 한 사람이 그를 그곳까지 안내하라는 명을 받았다.\n\n존스는 길을 벗어난 탓에 육 마일이면 될 것을 열한 마일이나 돌아왔고, 그 길 대부분이 산파를 부르러 가는 길이라 해도 도무지 서두를 수 없을 만큼 끔찍한 길이었던 터라, 코번트리에 다다른 것은 거의 열두 시가 다 되어서였다. 게다가 다시 안장에 오른 것도 두 시가 지나서였다. 역마는 이제 구하기가 쉽지 않았고, 마부나 역참 소년이나 존스의 절반만큼도 서두르지 않았으며, 오히려 파트리지의 느긋한 성미를 본받는 편을 택했다. 파트리지로 말하자면, 잠이라는 자양분을 빼앗긴 처지였으므로 틈만 나면 그 자리를 온갖 다른 종류의 자양분으로 메우려 들었고, 여관에 도착했을 때만큼 기분 좋은 적이 없었으며, 다시 그곳을 떠나야만 할 때만큼 불만스러운 적도 없었다.\n\n이제 존스는 역마를 갈아타며 달렸다. 그러니 우리도 관례대로, 그리고 롱기누스의 법칙에 따라 같은 방식으로 그를 뒤따르자. 그는 코번트리에서 데이번트리에, 데이번트리에서 스트랫퍼드에, 스트랫퍼드에서 던스터블에 이르렀고, 그 이튿날 정오를 조금 지나, 소피아가 그곳을 떠난 지 몇 시간 뒤에 던스터블에 닿았다. 비록 그가 탈 역마에 대장장이가 자못 신중을 기하며 편자를 박는 동안 바라던 것보다 오래 머물러야 했지만, 그는 소피아가 세인트올번스를 떠나기 전에는 반드시 따라잡으리라 믿어 의심치 않았다. 그는 그곳에서 그 귀족 나리께서 멈춰 식사를 하리라고, 더없이 그럴듯하게 결론지었던 것이다.\n\n만일 이 짐작이 옳았다면, 그는 십중팔구 앞서 말한 그곳에서 자신의 천사를 따라잡았을 것이다. 그러나 불운하게도 나리께서는 런던 자택에 자신을 위한 만찬을 차려 두라 일러두었고, 제때에 그곳에 닿기 위해 세인트올번스에서 갈아탈 말을 미리 대기시켜 두라 명해 둔 터였다. 그리하여 존스가 그곳에 도착했을 때, 그는 여섯 마리가 끄는 마차가 두 시간 전에 이미 떠났다는 말을 들었다.\n\n지금 당장 새 역마가 준비되어 있었다면 모를까—실은 그렇지 못했다—마차가 런던에 닿기 전에 따라잡는다는 것은 누가 보아도 불가능해 보였으므로, 파트리지는 자기 벗이 까맣게 잊은 듯한 한 가지 문제를 일깨워 줄 마침맞은 기회가 왔다고 여겼다. 그것이 무엇인지는 독자도 짐작하리라. 존스가 소피아에게서 돌아오던 안내인을 처음 만났던 그 선술집을 떠난 이후로 수란 한 알밖에 먹지 않았다는 사실을 알려 드린다면 말이다. 집시들과 함께 있는 동안 그가 호사를 누린 것은 오직 그의 지성뿐이었으니까.\n\n여관 주인 또한 파트리지 씨의 의견에 어찌나 전적으로 동의했던지, 파트리지가 자기 벗에게 머물러 식사를 하라고 청하는 말을 듣자마자 선뜻 한마디 거들었다. 그러고는 즉시 말을 대령하겠노라던 앞선 약속을 슬쩍 거두며, 식사부터 차리는 데 한시도 지체하지 않겠노라 존스 씨에게 장담했다. 풀밭에 있는 말을 끌어와 곡물 사료를 먹여 여정에 대비시키는 것보다, 식사를 차리는 편이 더 빨리 될 수 있다는 것이었다.\n\n존스는 마침내 설득당했는데, 주로 여관 주인의 그 마지막 논거 때문이었다. 그리하여 양고기 한 덩이가 불 위에 올랐다. 그것이 익는 동안, 파트리지는 자기 벗 또는 주인과 같은 방에 들도록 허락받자 다음과 같이 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"확실히 나리, 만일 어떤 사내가 젊은 아가씨를 누릴 자격이 있다면, 나리야말로 젊은 웨스턴 아씨를 누릴 자격이 있습니다요. 나리처럼 다른 음식이라곤 한 입도 없이 오로지 사랑만으로 살아갈 수 있으려면 도대체 얼마나 어마어마한 양의 사랑을 품어야 한단 말입니까? 분명 저는 지난 스물네 시간 동안 나리보다 서른 배는 더 먹었는데도 거의 굶어 죽을 지경입니다. 사람을 그토록 허기지게 하는 건 여행만 한 게 없거든요. 더구나 이렇게 으슬으슬 추운 날씨에는요. 헌데 어찌 된 영문인지 나리께선 겉보기에 더없이 정정하시고, 평생에 이보다 더 좋아 보이고 생기 넘쳐 보인 적이 없으십니다요. 나리께서 양식으로 삼고 사는 건 틀림없이 사랑일 겝니다.\"\n\n\"그것도 아주 기름진 음식이지, 파트리지.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"한데 어제 운명의 여신이 내게 더없이 훌륭한 진미를 보내 주지 않았더냐? 내가 이 소중한 수첩 한 권으로 스물네 시간 넘게 못 버틸 성싶으냐?\"\n\n\"두말할 것도 없습죠.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"그 수첩 안에는 좋은 끼니를 여러 번 사고도 남을 만큼 들어 있으니까요. 운명의 여신이 그걸 나리께 보내 주신 게 지금 당장 쓰기에 참으로 때맞춤이었습니다. 나리 돈도 이맘때면 거의 바닥났을 테니 말입죠.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리냐?\" 존스가 대꾸했다. \"설마 내가, 설령 그게 웨스턴 양 말고 다른 누구의 것이라 한들, 그토록 부정직한 짓을 하리라고 여기는 건 아니겠지——\"\n\n\"부정직이라뇨!\" 파트리지가 응수했다. \"맙소사, 제가 나리께 그렇게까지 못된 누명을 씌울 리가요! 하지만 당장 쓰려고 조금 빌리는 게 무슨 부정직이란 말입니까. 어차피 나중에 아가씨께 갚을 능력이 넉넉하실 텐데요? 아니죠, 정말이지 형편 닿는 대로 어떻게든 다시 갚으셔야지요. 하지만 지금 필요하실 때 그걸 좀 쓰는 게 무슨 해가 된단 말입니까? 하기야 그게 가난한 사람의 것이라면 사정이 다르겠지만, 그토록 지체 높은 아가씨께서야 분명 그게 아쉬울 리 없지요. 더구나 지금은 어떤 나리와 함께 계시니, 그분이 아가씨께 필요한 건 무엇이든 대 주시리란 건 의심할 여지가 없고요. 게다가 아가씨께서 설사 조금 아쉬우시더라도 전부가 아쉬울 리는 없을 테니, 저라면 조금만 드리겠습니다요. 다만 처음부터 그걸 주웠다는 말을 입에 올리느니, 그리고 제 손에 돈이 좀 들어오기 전에 그러느니, 차라리 목매달리는 편을 택하겠습니다. 듣자 하니 런던이란 곳은 돈 없이 지내기엔 세상에서 가장 고약한 데라니까요. 사실 그게 누구 것인지 몰랐더라면, 저는 그걸 악마의 돈인 줄 알고 무서워서 못 썼을지도 모르지요. 하지만 나리께선 사정을 아시고, 떳떳하게 손에 넣으셨으니, 정작 가장 절실히 필요한 바로 이때에 그걸 도로 다 내주신다면 그거야말로 운명의 여신을 모욕하는 일입죠. 여신께서 또다시 그런 호의를 베푸시리라곤 좀처럼 기대하기 어려울 테니까요. 포르투나 눈콴 페르페투오 에스트 보나(운명의 여신은 결코 영원토록 좋게만 굴지 않는다)라 하지 않습니까. 제가 무슨 말을 하든 나리께선 마음대로 하시겠지요. 하지만 저로서는 그 문제를 한마디라도 입에 올리느니 차라리 목매달리는 편을 택하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"내가 보기에 말이다, 파트리지,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"목매다는 일이야말로 논 롱게 알리에눔 아 스카이볼라이 스투디이스(스카이볼라의 연구와 그리 동떨어진 것이 아닌)로구나.\" \"알리에누스라고 하셔야지요.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"그 구절을 기억합니다. 코무니스, 알리에누스, 임무니스, 바리이스 카시부스 세르비운트(communis, alienus, immunis는 여러 격을 취한다) 아래에 나오는 예문이지요.\" \"자네가 정녕 그걸 기억한다면,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"정작 그 뜻은 이해하지 못하는 모양일세. 허나 알기 쉬운 영어로 말해 주지. 남의 재물을 주워 놓고 그 주인이 누군지 뻔히 알면서도 짐짓 돌려주지 않고 쥐고 있는 자는, 포로 콘스키엔티아이(양심의 법정에서) 그것을 훔친 자와 다름없이 목매달릴 만하다는 걸세. 그리고 바로 이 어음으로 말하자면, 이건 내 천사의 재물이요 한때 그분의 소중한 손에 들려 있던 것이니, 나는 어떤 사정이 있더라도 그분 본인의 손 외에 다른 어떤 손에도 이걸 넘기지 않겠네. 그래, 설령 내가 자네처럼 굶주리고 이 들끓는 식욕을 채울 다른 방도가 전혀 없다 한들 말일세. 잠들기 전에 이 일을 해내고 싶네만, 혹 일이 그리되지 않더라도, 내 노여움을 영영 사고 싶지 않거든 그런 가증스러운 비열함을 입에 올려 다시는 나를 진저리치게 하지 말게.\"\n\n\"제 눈에 그렇게 보였다면 지금 그 말을 꺼내지도 않았을 겝니다.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"저도 어떤 못된 짓이든 남 못지않게 경멸한다고 자부하니까요. 헌데 어쩌면 나리께서 더 잘 아시겠지요. 그래도 제가 이 나이 먹도록 살고 그토록 오래 학교에서 가르쳐 왔으면서 파스 에트 네파스(옳음과 그름)도 가릴 줄 모를 리야 있겠습니까. 하지만 사람은 누구나 죽을 때까지 배워야 하나 봅니다. 굉장한 대학자셨던 제 옛 스승께서 곧잘 이르시던 말씀이 떠오르는군요. 폴리 마테테 크라이 타운 이스 마이 다스칼론. 그분이 일러 주시기로 그 영어 뜻은, 아이가 더러는 제 할미한테 달걀 빠는 법을 가르치려 든다는 것이었지요. 이 나이에 새삼 문법을 배워야 한다면, 참으로 제가 헛산 셈입니다요. 어쩌면, 젊은 도련님, 도련님도 제 나이쯤 사시면 생각이 바뀔지 모릅니다. 저도 스물한둘 풋내기 시절엔 지금만큼이나 제가 똑똑한 줄 알았던 게 기억나거든요. 분명 저는 늘 알리에누스라고 가르쳤고, 제 스승께서도 제 앞에서 그렇게 읽으셨습니다요.\"\n\n파트리지가 존스를 격분시킬 수 있는 경우는 그리 많지 않았고, 또 파트리지 자신이 존스를 향한 공경심을 잃고 발끈하게 되는 경우도 그리 많지 않았다. 그러나 불운하게도, 두 사람은 그 드문 경우 가운데 하나를 동시에 건드리고 만 것이다. 우리는 이미 파트리지가 자기 학식이 공격당하는 것을 견디지 못한다는 것을 보았고, 존스 또한 앞선 연설 속 이런저런 대목을 참아 넘길 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 자기 길동무를 경멸과 멸시 어린 낯빛으로(그로서는 좀처럼 없는 일이었다) 바라보며 외쳤다. \"파트리지, 보아하니 자넨 자만에 빠진 늙은 바보로군. 게다가 늙은 악당까지는 아니기를 바라네. 정말이지, 내가 후자를 전자만큼이나 확신하게 된다면, 자넨 더는 나와 동행하지 못할 걸세.\"\n\n이 현명한 훈장 나리는 이미 자기 분풀이를 어느 정도 쏟아낸 것으로 만족하고는, 흔한 속담대로, 곧장 뿔을 거두어들였다. 그는 노여움을 살 만한 말을 내뱉어 미안하다고, 결코 그럴 뜻은 아니었다고 했다. 그러나 네모 옴니부스 호리스 사피트(어느 누구도 늘 현명할 수는 없는 법)라는 것이었다.\n\n존스는 뜨거운 기질에서 비롯되는 악덕은 지녔으되, 차가운 기질에서 비롯되는 악덕에서는 전적으로 자유로웠다. 그의 벗들이라면 그의 성미가 좀 지나치게 쉬이 발끈한다는 점은 인정해야 했겠지만, 그의 적들이라도 동시에 그것이 또한 그만큼 빨리 가라앉는다는 점은 인정해야 했을 것이다. 그것은 폭풍이 잦아든 뒤의 일렁임이 폭풍 그 자체가 몰아칠 때보다 더 사납고 위태로운 바다와는 조금도 닮지 않았다. 그는 즉시 파트리지의 사과를 받아들이고 그의 손을 잡아 흔들며, 상상할 수 있는 가장 자애로운 낯빛으로 다정한 말을 스무 마디나 건넸고, 동시에 자신을 매우 호되게 나무랐다—비록 우리의 점잖은 독자 여러 분이 그를 나무랄 만한 정도의 절반에도 미치지 못했지만 말이다.\n\n이제 파트리지는 크게 위로받았다. 노여움을 샀을지 모른다는 두려움이 단숨에 사라졌고, 존스가 스스로 잘못을 인정한 덕분에 그의 자존심도 완전히 충족되었기 때문이다. 그는 그 사과를 당장 자신을 무엇보다 거슬리게 했던 대목에 갖다 붙이고는, 중얼거리는 목소리로 되뇌었다. \"그야 물론입죠, 나리, 어떤 일에서는 나리의 지식이 제 것보다 위에 계시겠지요. 하지만 문법으로 말할 것 같으면, 저는 살아 있는 어느 누구에게라도 한판 겨뤄 보자고 할 수 있을 듯합니다요. 적어도 그것만큼은 손끝에 훤히 꿰고 있다고 생각합니다.\"\n\n이 가련한 사내가 지금 누리는 만족에 무언가를 더 보탤 수 있는 것이 있었다면, 그는 바로 그것을 얻었다. 바로 그 순간 김을 모락모락 피우며 식탁에 오른 훌륭한 양 어깨살 덕분이었다. 두 사람은 그것으로 한껏 배불리 먹고는 다시 말에 올라 런던을 향해 길을 나섰다.\n\n세인트올번스를 떠난 길에서 존스 씨에게 일어난 일.\n\n일행이 바넷을 지나 두 마일쯤 갔을 무렵, 어느덧 저녁 어스름이 깔리고 있었다. 그때 점잖아 보이는 차림새의 한 남자가, 하지만 무척 초라한 말을 타고서 존스에게 다가와 런던으로 가는 길이냐고 물었다. 존스는 그렇다고 대답했다. 그 신사는 이렇게 말했다. \"선생, 동행을 받아주신다면 고맙겠습니다. 시간도 무척 늦었고, 저는 이 길이 낯선 사람이라서요.\" 존스는 흔쾌히 그 청을 받아들였고, 두 사람은 이런 경우에 흔히 나누는 그런 종류의 이야기를 주고받으며 함께 길을 갔다.\n\n실로 그중에서도 노상강도가 주된 화제였다. 그 이야기를 두고 낯선 남자는 큰 두려움을 드러냈으나, 존스는 자신은 잃을 것이 거의 없으니 두려워할 것도 그만큼 없다고 잘라 말했다. 이때 파트리지가 한마디 거들지 않을 수 없었다. \"나리께서야 별것 아니라 여기실지 모르지만요,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 생각엔, 만약 나리처럼 제 주머니에 백 파운드짜리 은행권이 있다면 그걸 잃는 게 무척 속상할 겁니다. 그래도 저로 말하자면 평생 이보다 덜 무서웠던 적이 없습니다. 우리가 넷이나 되고, 우리 모두가 서로 등을 맞대고 버티면 잉글랜드에서 제일가는 장사라도 우릴 털진 못할 테니까요. 설사 그자가 권총을 가졌대도, 그래봐야 우리 중 하나밖에 못 죽이고, 사람은 한 번밖에 못 죽으니까요. 그게 제 위안이지요, 사람은 한 번밖에 못 죽는다는 게.\"\n\n수적 우세에 대한 믿음—근대에 이르러 어느 한 민족을 드높은 영광의 경지로 끌어올린 그런 종류의 용맹—외에도, 파트리지가 지금 드러낸 이 유별난 담력에는 또 다른 이유가 있었다. 그는 지금 술기운이 베풀 수 있는 만큼의 그 담력을 한껏 지니고 있었던 것이다.\n\n일행이 이제 하이게이트에서 한 마일 안쪽까지 다다랐을 때, 그 낯선 남자가 별안간 존스에게 몸을 홱 돌리더니 권총을 꺼내 들고, 파트리지가 말했던 그 자그마한 은행권을 내놓으라고 요구했다.\n\n존스는 처음엔 이 뜻밖의 요구에 다소 충격을 받았다. 하지만 곧 정신을 가다듬고 노상강도에게, 주머니에 든 돈은 모두 기꺼이 그에게 내주겠다고 말했다. 그러면서 삼 기니가 넘는 돈을 꺼내 건네려 했으나, 상대는 욕설을 내뱉으며 그걸론 안 된다고 대답했다. 존스는 차분하게, 그것 참 안됐다고 말하고는 돈을 도로 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n그러자 노상강도는, 당장 은행권을 내놓지 않으면 쏘아 죽이겠다고 으름장을 놓으며 권총을 그의 가슴 바로 앞에 들이댔다. 존스는 즉시 그자의 손을 붙잡았는데, 그 손이 어찌나 떨리던지 권총을 제대로 쥐고 있기도 힘들 정도였다. 존스는 총구를 자기 쪽에서 돌려놓았다. 곧 몸싸움이 벌어졌고, 그 와중에 존스가 상대의 손에서 권총을 비틀어 빼앗았으며, 두 사람은 함께 말에서 땅바닥으로 굴러떨어졌다. 노상강도는 등을 대고 자빠졌고, 승리한 존스가 그 위에 올라탔다.\n\n이 가련한 자는 이제 정복자에게 자비를 빌기 시작했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 힘으로는 도무지 존스의 상대가 되지 못했던 것이다. \"정말입니다, 선생,\" 그가 말했다. \"전 선생을 쏠 생각이 조금도 없었습니다. 보시면 아시겠지만 권총엔 총알도 들어 있지 않았어요. 강도질이라곤 이게 난생처음이고, 저는 궁핍에 떠밀려 여기까지 온 겁니다.\"\n\n바로 이 순간, 백오십 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서 또 다른 사람이 땅바닥에 엎드린 채, 노상강도보다 훨씬 더 큰 소리로 자비를 부르짖고 있었다. 다름 아닌 파트리지였다. 그는 이 싸움에서 달아나려다 말에서 떨어져 얼굴을 땅에 처박은 채 납작 엎드려, 감히 고개를 들지도 못하고 시시각각 총에 맞을까 봐 떨고 있었다.\n\n그는 그런 자세로 엎드려 있었다. 마침내, 오로지 말들 걱정뿐이던 안내인이 비틀거리던 짐승을 붙잡아 둔 뒤 그에게 다가와, 주인 양반이 노상강도를 제압했다고 일러주었다.\n\n이 소식에 파트리지는 벌떡 일어나, 존스가 칼을 빼 들고 그 가련한 자를 지키고 서 있는 자리로 달려갔다. 그 광경을 보자마자 파트리지는 소리쳤다. \"그 악당을 죽이세요, 나리, 몸을 꿰뚫어 버리세요, 당장 죽이세요!\"\n\n하지만 다행히도 이 불쌍한 자는 좀 더 자비로운 손에 떨어진 셈이었다. 존스는 권총을 살펴보고 정말로 총알이 들어 있지 않음을 확인한 뒤, 파트리지가 오기 전에 그자가 했던 말을 모두 믿기 시작했던 것이다. 곧 그가 이 일에 풋내기이며, 자신이 말한 궁핍에 떠밀려 이 짓을 하게 되었다는 것이었다. 그 궁핍이란 실로 상상할 수 있는 가장 극심한 것으로, 배곯는 다섯 아이와, 더없는 궁색과 비참 속에서 여섯째를 낳고 누워 있는 아내가 있다는 것이었다. 노상강도는 이 모든 것이 사실이라고 더없이 격렬하게 주장하며, 수고스럽지만 자기 집까지 가주신다면—그곳은 두 마일도 채 안 되는 거리라며—그것을 존스 씨에게 납득시켜 드리겠노라 했다. 그는 \"다른 호의는 바라지 않으며, 다만 제가 한 말을 모두 증명한다는 조건에서만 바랄 뿐\"이라고 말했다.\n\n존스는 처음엔 그자의 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들여 함께 가겠노라 하는 체하며, 그의 운명은 전적으로 그 이야기의 진위에 달려 있노라고 못박았다. 이 말에 그 가련한 자가 즉시 어찌나 선뜻 응하는 기색을 보이던지, 존스는 그 진실함에 완전히 만족하여 이제 그에게 측은한 마음을 품기 시작했다. 그는 빈 권총을 그자에게 돌려주고, 궁핍을 면할 더 정직한 방도를 생각해 보라고 충고했으며, 아내와 식구를 당장 건사하라고 두 기니를 건넸다. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, \"자네를 위해 더 줄 수 있었으면 좋겠네만, 아까 말이 나온 그 백 파운드는 내 것이 아니라네\"라고 했다.\n\n아마 우리 독자들은 이 행동을 두고 의견이 갈릴 것이다. 어떤 이들은 더없이 인정 넘치는 행위라며 갈채를 보낼 테고, 좀 더 음울한 기질을 지닌 이들은 모든 사람이 제 나라에 마땅히 갚아야 할 정의를 소홀히 한 것이라 여길 것이다. 파트리지는 분명 그렇게 보았다. 그는 이 일에 큰 불만을 드러내며 옛 속담을 들먹였고, 그 악당이 런던에 닿기도 전에 다시 우리를 덮친다 해도 놀랄 일이 아니라고 말했다.\n\n노상강도는 고마움과 감사의 말을 쏟아냈다. 그는 실제로 눈물을 떨구거나, 그러는 척했다. 그는 곧장 집으로 돌아가 다시는 그런 죄를 저지르지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 그가 그 약속을 지켰는지 아닌지는 아마 나중에 드러날 것이다.\n\n우리의 여행자들은 다시 말에 올라, 새로운 변고 하나 없이 도시에 다다랐다. 길 위에서 존스와 파트리지 사이에는 방금 겪은 모험을 두고 유쾌한 이야기가 오갔다. 그 가운데 존스는, 어쩔 수 없는 궁핍에 떠밀려 말하자면 그런 불법의 길로 내몰리고, 그로 인해 대개 수치스러운 죽음에 이르고 마는 노상강도들에게 깊은 연민을 드러냈다. \"내가 말하는 건,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 죄가 강도질을 넘지 않는 자들, 그 누구에게도 잔혹하거나 모욕을 가하는 일이 없는 자들만을 두고 하는 말일세. 이건 우리나라의 명예를 위해 꼭 말해 두어야겠는데, 바로 그 점이 잉글랜드의 강도들을 다른 모든 나라의 강도들과 구별 짓는 점이지. 다른 나라에선 살인이 강도질과 거의 떼려야 뗄 수 없이 붙어 다니거든.\"\n\n\"두말할 것 없이,\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"남의 목숨을 빼앗는 것보다야 돈을 빼앗는 편이 낫지요. 그래도 정직한 사람들로선, 그런 악당들 때문에 위험에 처하지 않고서는 제 볼일 보러 다니지도 못한다는 게 무척 고달픈 노릇입니다. 그러니 정직한 사람 하나가 화를 입느니, 그런 악당들이 죄다 교수형에 처해져 사라지는 편이 분명 나을 겁니다. 저로 말하자면, 그자들 누구의 피든 제 손에 묻히고 싶진 않지만, 법이 그놈들을 다 매달아 버리는 건 지극히 마땅한 일이지요. 제가 주지도 않은 육 펜스를 대체 무슨 권리로 빼앗는단 말입니까? 그런 자에게 무슨 정직함이 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"그렇고말고,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그건 남의 마구간에서 말을 끌어내는 자나, 주운 돈의 임자가 누군지 뻔히 알면서도 제 것인 양 써버리는 자에게 정직함이 없는 것과 마찬가지지.\"\n\n이 말에 파트리지는 입을 다물었다. 그러고는 존스가 그의 비겁함을 두고 빈정대는 농담을 몇 마디 던지기 전까지 다시 입을 열지 않았다. 그러자 그는 화기(火器)가 공평치 못했다는 핑계로 자신을 변명하려 들며 이렇게 말했다. \"맨손인 사람 천 명도 권총 하나엔 아무것도 아니죠. 한 번 쏘면 한 명밖에 못 죽이는 게 사실이라지만, 그 한 명이 바로 저 자신이 아니라고 누가 장담하겠습니까?\"\n\n열이틀의 기간을 담은 권.\n\n영감을 청함.\n오라, 명예를 향한 빛나는 사랑이여, 달아오른 내 가슴에 영감을 불어넣어다오. 다만 내가 부르려는 것은, 피와 눈물의 부풀어 오르는 물결 위로 영웅을 영광으로 실어 나르며, 수백만의 한숨이 그 펼쳐진 돛을 밀어 주는 그런 사랑은 아니다. 오히려 내가 부르려는 것은, 행복한 님프 므네시스가 헤브루스 강가에서 처음 낳은, 아름답고 온화한 처녀 그대다. 마이오니아가 길렀고, 만투아가 매혹했으며, 브리튼의 도도한 수도를 굽어보는 저 아름다운 언덕 위에 그대의 밀턴과 더불어 앉아 영웅의 칠현금을 감미롭게 조율하던 그대여, 다가올 매혹의 시대에 대한 희망으로 황홀해진 내 상상을 가득 채워다오. 내게 예언해다오. 그 할머니가 아직 태어나지도 않은 어느 다정한 처녀가 훗날, 소피아라는 지어낸 이름 아래 한때 나의 샬럿에게 실재했던 참된 가치를 읽을 때, 그 공감 어린 가슴에서 들썩이는 한숨을 토해 내리라고. 그대는 내게 미래의 칭송을 내다보는 법뿐 아니라, 그것을 누리는 법, 아니 그것을 양식 삼아 살아가는 법까지 가르쳐다오. 엄숙한 확언으로 나를 위로해다오. 지금 이 순간 내가 앉아 있는 이 작은 거실이 더 초라하게 꾸며진 관(棺) 한 칸으로 줄어들 때에도, 나를 알지도 보지도 못했고, 나 또한 알지도 보지도 못할 사람들이 영예로이 나를 읽으리라고.\n\n그리고 그대, 한층 더 통통한 부인이여, 어떤 공기 같은 형상도 상상의 환영도 그대를 감싸지 못하고, 잘 양념된 소고기와 자두로 푸짐하게 물든 푸딩이 그대를 기쁘게 하는 그대여, 그대를 부른다. 어느 네덜란드 운하의 거룻배 안에서, 암스테르담의 유쾌한 상인에게 잉태된 살진 우프로 겔트가 낳은 그대여. 그럽 가(街)의 학교에서 그대는 학식의 기초를 빨아들였다. 여기서 그대는, 한층 무르익은 나이에 이르러, 시(詩)에게 상상이 아니라 후원자의 자만심을 간질이는 법을 가르쳤다. 희극은 그대에게서 근엄하고 엄숙한 태도를 배우고, 비극은 큰 소리로 폭풍처럼 휘몰아치며 그 천둥으로 겁먹은 극장들을 찢어 놓는다. 그대의 지친 사지를 잠으로 달래려, 시의원 역사 선생이 지루한 이야기를 늘어놓고, 다시 그대를 깨우려, 로맨스 선생이 놀라운 손재주의 묘기를 부린다. 잘 먹고 자란 그대의 서적상도 그대의 영향력에 못지않게 복종한다. 그대의 조언에 따라, 먼지 쌓인 선반 위에서 오래 졸고 있던, 읽히지 않은 그 묵직한 2절판 덩어리가 여러 분책으로 쪼개져 온 나라를 날렵하게 누빈다. 그대의 가르침을 받아, 어떤 책들은 돌팔이처럼 경이로움을 약속하며 세상을 속이고, 또 어떤 책들은 멋쟁이로 변신하여 제 모든 가치를 금박 입힌 겉모습에 내맡긴다. 오라, 빛나는 얼굴을 한 그대 유쾌한 실체여, 그대의 영감일랑 거두어 두고, 대신 그대의 솔깃한 보상을 내밀어 보여다오. 그대의 번쩍이며 짤랑대는 무더기, 눈에 보이지 않는 부로 잔뜩 부풀어 금세 현금으로 바꿀 수 있는 그대의 은행 어음, 끊임없이 값이 오르내리는 그대의 주식, 따뜻하고 안락한 집, 그리고 마지막으로, 흐르는 젖가슴으로 그 수많은 자식 모두에게 넘치도록 양식을 베푸는 그 너그러운 어머니의 — 다만 더러 너무 탐욕스럽고 방자하게 제 형제들을 젖꼭지에서 밀쳐 내지만 않았더라면 — 정당한 몫 한 자락을. 오라 그대여, 그리고 만일 내가 그대의 값진 보물을 너무도 분별없이 대한다면, 그것을 남들에게 전하리라는 황홀한 생각으로 내 마음을 데워다오. 내게 말해다오. 그대의 너그러움 덕분에, 그 천진한 놀이가 나의 노고에 자주 가로막혔던 종알대는 아기들이 언젠가 그 노고에 대해 넉넉히 보답받게 되리라고.\n\n그런데 이제, 이 어울리지 않게 한데 묶인 한 쌍, 이 야윈 그림자와 이 살진 실체가 나로 하여금 글을 쓰도록 부추겼으니, 내 펜을 이끌어 달라고 누구의 도움을 청해야 할까?\n\n먼저, 천재(天才)여, 그대 하늘의 선물이여. 그대의 도움 없이는 우리가 자연의 물줄기를 거슬러 헛되이 발버둥칠 따름이다. 그대는 너그러운 씨앗을 뿌리고, 기예가 그것을 길러 완성으로 이끈다. 부디 다정하게 내 손을 잡고, 자연의 온갖 미로, 그 구불구불한 미궁을 두루 지나도록 이끌어다오. 불경한 눈이 결코 본 적 없는 그 모든 신비 속으로 나를 들여보내다오. 그대에게는 조금도 어렵지 않을 일이니, 인류를 그들 자신이 아는 것보다 더 잘 아는 법을 내게 가르쳐다오. 인간의 지성을 흐리게 하는 저 안개를 걷어다오. 그 안개는 사람들로 하여금, 남을 속이는 자를 그 재주 때문에 떠받들거나 그 교활함 때문에 혐오하게 만들지만, 정작 그들은 제 자신을 속였다는 점에서 비웃음의 대상일 뿐이다. 자만심에서 지혜라는 얄팍한 가면을, 탐욕에서 풍요라는 가면을, 야망에서 영광이라는 가면을 벗겨다오. 오라, 그대의 아리스토파네스, 그대의 루키아노스, 그대의 세르반테스, 그대의 라블레, 그대의 몰리에르, 그대의 셰익스피어, 그대의 스위프트, 그대의 마리보에게 영감을 불어넣었던 그대여, 내 페이지를 해학으로 가득 채워다오. 마침내 인류가, 오직 남의 어리석음에만 웃을 만큼의 선한 마음과, 제 자신의 어리석음에는 슬퍼할 만큼의 겸손을 배우게 될 때까지.\n\n그리고 그대, 참된 천재에게 거의 변함없이 따라붙는 동반자, 인간애(人間愛)여, 그대의 온갖 다정한 감정을 모두 가져와다오. 만일 그대가 이미 그 감정들을 그대의 앨런과 그대의 리틀턴에게 죄다 나누어 주었다면, 잠시만 그들의 품에서 슬쩍 빌려와다오. 이런 감정 없이는 애틋한 장면이 그려지지 않는다. 이 감정에서만 고귀하고 사심 없는 우정, 녹아드는 사랑, 너그러운 마음씨, 뜨거운 감사, 부드러운 연민, 솔직한 견해가 우러나며, 또 촉촉이 젖은 눈에 눈물을 채우고, 달아오른 뺨에 피를 돌게 하며, 가슴을 슬픔과 기쁨과 자애의 물결로 부풀어 오르게 하는, 선한 마음의 그 모든 강렬한 힘이 솟아난다.\n\n그리고 그대, 오 학식(學識)이여! (그대의 도움 없이는 천재도 순수한 것, 올바른 것을 결코 만들어 내지 못하므로) 부디 내 펜을 인도해다오. 맑고 잔잔히 흐르는 템스강이 그대의 이튼 강기슭을 씻어 내리는 그대가 가장 아끼는 들판에서, 나는 어린 시절에 그대를 경배했다. 그대의 자작나무 제단 앞에서, 참된 스파르타식 헌신으로 나는 내 피를 바쳤다. 그러니 오라, 그리고 아득한 옛적부터 쌓아 올린 그대의 광대하고 풍요로운 곳간에서 그 풍성함을 쏟아부어다오. 그대의 마이오니아 궤와 만투아 궤를, 또 그리스 글자로든 로마 글자로든 그대가 새겨 넣기로 택한 그 묵직한 상자들이 품은, 그대의 철학적·시적·역사적 보물 무엇이든 모두 열어다오. 그대가 그대의 워버튼에게 맡겨 둔, 그대의 온갖 보물로 통하는 그 열쇠를 잠시만 내게 빌려다오.\n\n끝으로, 오라 경험이여, 지혜로운 자, 선한 자, 학식 있는 자, 세련된 자와 오래 어울려 온 그대여. 아니, 그들뿐 아니라 온갖 부류의 인물과도 어울려 온 그대여. 접견실의 대신에서부터 채무자 가둔 집의 집행관까지, 무도회의 공작 부인에서부터 술집 카운터 뒤의 여주인까지. 인류의 풍속은 오직 그대에게서만 알 수 있으니, 아무리 재능이 뛰어나고 학식이 넓다 한들, 세상과 등진 학자는 그것에 늘 낯선 이방인이었다.\n\n이들 모두 오라, 그리고 가능하다면 더 많이도 오라. 내가 떠맡은 일은 고된 것이어서, 그대들 모두의 도움 없이는 내가 떠받치기에 너무 무거우리라는 것을 나는 안다. 그러나 그대들 모두가 나의 노고에 미소 지어 준다면, 나는 그래도 이 일을 행복한 결말로 이끌어 가기를 바란다.\n\n런던에 도착한 존스에게 일어난 일.\n\n학식 깊은 미소뱅 박사는 자기 앞으로 오는 편지의 올바른 수신처는 \"세상에 계신 미소뱅 박사께\"라고 말하곤 했다. 세상에 자신의 대단한 명성을 모르는 사람이 거의 없다는 뜻이었다. 그리고 어쩌면, 이 문제를 아주 면밀히 따져 본다면, 우리는 이 사정이 위세가 누리는 그 숱한 축복 가운데서도 결코 작지 않은 몫을 차지함을 알게 될 것이다.\n\n후세에 이름이 알려지는 그 크나큰 행복, 앞 장에서 우리가 그 희망에 그토록 들떠 마지않던 그 행복은 오직 소수만이 누리는 몫이다. 시드넘이 표현한 대로, 우리 이름을 이루는 여러 낱낱의 글자들이 천 년 뒤에까지 되풀이되어 불리는 일이란, 지위와 부의 힘으로도 미치지 못하는 선물이며, 칼과 펜이 아니고서는 좀처럼 사들일 수 없는 것이다. 그러나 우리가 아직 살아 있는 동안에 \"아무도 모르는 자\"라는 수치스러운 낙인을 면하는 일은(덧붙이자면, 이는 호메로스 시절만큼이나 오래된 수치다) 명예든 재산이든 합법적 권리를 지닌 이들이 늘 부러움을 사며 누리는 몫이다.\n\n[*] 〈오디세이아〉 제2권, 175행을 보라.\n\n그러므로 소피아를 도시로 데려온 그 아일랜드 귀족이 이 이야기 속에서 이미 보여 준 그 위세로 미루어 보아, 독자는 분명 이렇게 결론지을 것이다. 그가 사는 거리나 광장을 따로 몰라도 런던에서 그의 저택을 찾아내기란 쉬운 일이었으리라고. 그야말로 \"누구나 아는 사람\" 중 하나였을 테니 말이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 위세 높은 이들의 영역을 드나드는 데 익숙한 상인들에게라면 정말 그러했을 것이다. 위세 높은 이들의 문이란, 들어가기가 어려운 만큼이나 찾아내기는 대체로 쉬운 법이니까. 그러나 존스는 파트리지와 마찬가지로 런던에서는 완전한 이방인이었다. 게다가 그는 하필 그 도시에서, 하노버 광장이나 그로스베너 광장의 저택 주인들과는 거의 왕래가 없는 사람들이 사는 구역에 먼저 들어서고 말았던 터라(그는 그레이스인 길을 통해 들어왔다), 운명이 미천한 무리로부터 떼어 따로 자리 잡게 해 놓은 그 행복한 저택들로 가는 길조차 찾기 전에 한참을 헤매고 돌아다녀야 했다. 그 저택의 주인들이란 고대 브리튼인, 색슨인, 데인인의 후예인 도량 넓은 영웅들이니, 그 조상들은 더 좋은 시절에 태어나 갖가지 공덕으로 후손에게 부와 명예를 물려준 이들이었다.\n\n마침내 그 지상의 엘리시온 들판에 다다른 존스는 이제 곧 귀족의 저택을 찾아낼 듯했다. 그러나 그 귀족은 운 나쁘게도 아일랜드로 떠날 때 옛집을 비웠고, 막 새집으로 들어선 참이어서 그 마차 행렬의 명성이 아직 이웃에 충분히 떨치지 못한 상태였다. 그리하여 시계가 열한 시를 칠 때까지 헛되이 수소문하던 존스는 끝내 파트리지의 권유에 따라 홀번에 있는 불 앤드 게이트로 물러났다. 그곳은 그가 처음 내렸던 여관이었고, 그는 그곳으로 물러나 자신과 같은 처지의 사람에게 으레 찾아드는 그런 종류의 휴식을 누리고자 했다.\n\n이튿날 이른 아침 그는 다시 소피아를 찾아 나섰고, 전과 다를 바 없이 헛수고로 끝나는 지친 걸음을 숱하게 옮겼다. 그러다 마침내, 운명이 마음을 누그러뜨렸는지, 아니면 더는 그를 실망시킬 힘이 남지 않았는지, 그는 바로 그 귀족의 저택이 영광을 더하고 있는 그 거리로 들어섰다. 그리고 그 집을 안내받아, 문을 한 번 가볍게 두드렸다.\n\n문지기는 그 조심스러운 노크 소리에서 다가온 사람을 그리 대단하게 여기지 않았는데, 존스 씨의 행색을 보고도 별반 나은 인상을 받지 못했다. 존스는 거친 무명천 옷을 입고 있었고, 옆구리에는 전에 하사관에게서 사들인 무기를 차고 있었는데, 칼날은 잘 벼린 강철로 되어 있었을지 몰라도 손잡이는 그저 놋쇠로, 그것도 그리 번쩍이지 않는 놋쇠로 만들어진 것이었다. 그래서 존스가 귀족과 함께 도시로 온 젊은 아가씨에 대해 묻자, 이 사내는 무뚝뚝하게 \"여기에는 아가씨 같은 분 안 계시오\" 하고 대답했다. 그러자 존스는 집주인을 만나기를 청했으나, 귀족께서는 오늘 아침 아무도 만나지 않으신다는 답을 들었다. 그래도 더 끈질기게 졸라대자 문지기가 말했다. \"아무도 들이지 말라는 분명한 분부를 받았소. 하지만 정 그러시다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"이름을 남겨 두시오. 내가 귀족께 여쭐 테니. 그리고 다음에 또 들르시면 언제 뵐 수 있을지 알려 드리리다.\"\n\n이제 존스는 이렇게 잘라 말했다. \"그 젊은 아가씨께 아주 긴한 용무가 있어서, 그분을 뵙지 않고는 떠날 수가 없소.\" 그러자 문지기는 그리 곱지 않은 목소리와 표정으로 잘라 말했다. \"이 집에는 젊은 아가씨 같은 분이 없으니, 당연히 만날 분도 없다지 않소.\" 그러고는 한마디 덧붙였다. \"참, 당신처럼 별난 사람은 내 평생 처음 봤소. 도무지 대답을 곧이듣지를 않으니 말이오.\"\n\n나는 종종 이런 생각을 해 보았다. 베르길리우스가 〈아이네이스〉 제6권에서 지옥의 문지기 케르베로스를 그토록 자세히 묘사한 것은, 어쩌면 자기 시대 권세가들의 문지기를 풍자하려는 뜻이었을지 모른다고. 적어도 그 묘사는 우리네 권세가의 문 앞을 지키는 영광을 누리는 자들과 꼭 닮았다. 자기 수위실에 앉은 문지기는 굴속의 케르베로스와 정확히 들어맞으며, 그처럼 미끼 한 점으로 달래야만 그 주인에게 다가갈 길이 열린다. 어쩌면 존스도 그를 그런 눈으로 보고서, 무녀가 아이네이아스를 들여보내려고 스틱스 강 길목의 문지기에게 그런 미끼를 바치는 대목을 떠올렸는지도 모른다. 존스 역시 그와 마찬가지로 이제 이 인간 케르베로스에게 뇌물을 내밀기 시작했는데, 이를 엿들은 하인 하나가 대뜸 나서서 이렇게 말했다. \"존스 씨가 제게 그 돈을 주신다면, 제가 아가씨께 안내해 드리겠습니다.\" 존스는 즉시 응했고, 곧바로 피츠패트릭 부인의 거처로 안내되었으니, 바로 전날 그 아가씨들을 그리로 모셔다 준 바로 그 사내가 안내자였다.\n\n바라던 것에 가까이 다가갔다 놓치는 것만큼 실패의 쓰라림을 더하는 것은 없다. 피케 노름에서 단 한 점 차로 판을 내준 노름꾼은, 애초에 이길 가망조차 보이지 않던 자보다 열 배는 더 제 불운을 한탄하는 법이다. 복권도 마찬가지여서, 대박이 터진 번호의 바로 옆 번호를 쥐고 있던 자는 함께 떨어진 다른 이들보다 자신이 훨씬 더 불운하다고 여기기 쉽다. 요컨대 이렇게 행복을 종이 한 장 차이로 놓치는 일은 운명의 모욕처럼 보인다. 운명이란 이렇듯 우리를 가지고 장난을 치며, 우리를 희생양 삼아 제멋대로 즐기는 존재라 여길 만하다.\n\n이미 한 번 이상 이 이교 여신의 짓궂은 변덕을 겪어 본 존스는, 이제 또다시 같은 식으로 애만 태우는 신세가 될 운명이었다. 그가 피츠패트릭 부인의 문 앞에 다다른 것은 소피아가 떠난 지 십 분쯤 지난 뒤였기 때문이다. 그는 이제 피츠패트릭 부인의 시중드는 여인에게 말을 건넸는데, 여인은 아가씨가 떠나셨다는 달갑지 않은 소식을 전하면서도 어디로 갔는지는 말해 주지 못했다. 그리고 나중에 피츠패트릭 부인 본인에게서도 똑같은 답을 들었다. 그 부인은 존스가 숙부 웨스턴이 딸을 쫓으라고 보낸 사람임이 틀림없다고 여겼던 터라, 너무도 너그러운 마음에 소피아를 팔아넘길 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n존스는 피츠패트릭 부인을 한 번도 본 적이 없었지만, 소피아의 사촌이 그 성을 쓰는 신사와 결혼했다는 이야기는 들은 적이 있었다. 그러나 지금처럼 마음이 들끓는 와중에는 그 일이 단 한 번도 기억에 떠오르지 않았다. 그러다 귀족의 집에서 그를 안내해 온 하인이 두 여인 사이가 무척 가깝고 서로를 사촌이라 부른다고 일러 주자, 그제야 그는 전에 들었던 그 결혼 이야기를 떠올렸다. 그리고 이 여인이 바로 그 여인임을 곧바로 확신하게 되자, 자신이 들은 대답에 더욱 놀라며 그 부인을 직접 뵙게 해 달라고 간곡히 청했다. 그러나 부인은 그 영예를 단호히 거절했다.\n\n존스는 궁정이라고는 한 번도 본 적 없었지만, 그곳을 드나드는 대다수보다 더 잘 자란 사람이어서, 숙녀에게 무례하거나 퉁명스럽게 구는 짓 따위는 할 줄 몰랐다. 그래서 단호한 거절을 받자, 그는 일단 물러나며 시중드는 여인에게 이렇게 말했다. \"지금이 마님을 뵙기에 적절치 않은 시각이라면, 오후에 다시 오겠소. 그때는 부디 뵙는 영광을 누렸으면 하오.\" 그가 이 말을 건넨 정중함에, 더없이 준수한 그의 용모까지 더해져 시중드는 여인에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 여인은 저도 모르게 대답하고 말았다. \"그러실 수도 있겠지요, 선생님.\" 그리고 실제로 그녀는 나중에 마님에게, 그 잘생긴 젊은 신사의(그녀는 그를 그렇게 불렀다) 방문을 받아들이도록 설득할 만하다 싶은 말은 죄다 늘어놓았다.\n\n존스는 사촌과 함께 있는 사람이 다름 아닌 소피아 자신이며, 그녀가 자기를 만나기를 거절한 것이라고 제법 날카롭게 짐작했다. 그는 그것을 업튼에서 벌어진 일에 대한 그녀의 노여움 탓으로 돌렸다. 그래서 파트리지에게는 묵을 곳을 구해 오라 보내 놓고, 자신은 종일 거리에 남아 자기의 천사가 숨어 있으리라 여긴 그 문을 지켜보았다. 그러나 그 집 하인 하나 말고는 누구도 나오는 것을 보지 못했고, 저녁이 되자 그는 다시 피츠패트릭 부인을 찾아갔다. 그제야 그 마음씨 고운 부인은 마침내 면회를 허락하는 아량을 베풀었다.\n\n타고난 기품이라는 것이 있어서, 그것은 옷차림으로 부여할 수도 없거니와 가릴 수도 없다. 앞서 넌지시 비친 대로, 존스 씨는 이 기품을 아주 두드러지게 지니고 있었다. 그래서 그는 그 부인에게서, 자기 옷차림이 마땅히 받아야 할 듯한 대접과는 사뭇 다른 응대를 받았다. 그리고 마땅한 예를 갖춰 인사를 올린 뒤, 자리에 앉으라는 권유를 받았다.\n\n독자는, 내 생각에, 이 대화의 시시콜콜한 내용을 다 알고 싶어 하지는 않을 것이다. 그것은 가엾은 존스에게는 거의 만족스럽지 못한 결말로 끝났으니 말이다. 피츠패트릭 부인은 이내 그가 연인임을 알아차렸지만(이런 문제에서는 모든 여인이 매의 눈을 지니고 있으니), 그래도 여전히 그가 마음 너그러운 벗이라면 아가씨를 팔아넘겨선 안 될 그런 부류의 연인이라고 여겼다. 요컨대 그녀는 이 사람이 바로 소피아가 달아나 온 그 블리필 씨라고 의심했다. 그리고 그녀가 올워디 씨 집안에 관해 존스에게서 교묘히 끌어낸 모든 대답이 이 생각을 더욱 굳혀 주었다. 그래서 그녀는 소피아가 어디로 갔는지에 대해 아는 바가 전혀 없다고 딱 잡아뗐다. 존스가 얻어 낸 것이라곤 이튿날 저녁에 다시 찾아와도 좋다는 허락뿐이었다.\n\n존스가 떠나자 피츠패트릭 부인은 블리필 씨에 대한 자신의 의심을 하녀에게 털어놓았다. 그러자 하녀가 대답했다. \"어머, 마님, 제가 보기엔 그분은 세상 어떤 여자라도 도망쳐 나오기엔 너무 잘생긴 분인걸요. 저라면 그분이 존스 씨라고 여기겠어요.\" \"존스 씨라고!\" 부인이 말했다. \"존스가 누구냐?\" 소피아는 두 사람이 나눈 모든 대화에서 그런 사람에 대해 털끝만큼도 내비친 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 어너 부인은 훨씬 입이 가벼워서, 자기 동무인 애비게일에게 존스의 사연을 죄다 들려준 터였고, 이제 하녀가 그것을 다시 마님에게 풀어놓은 것이었다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인은 이 이야기를 듣자마자 곧바로 하녀의 의견에 동의했다. 그리고 참으로 알 수 없는 일이지만, 박대받은 그 시골 지주에게서는 못 본 척 지나쳤던 매력을, 이 늠름하고 행복한 연인에게서는 발견하고 말았다. \"베티야,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네 말이 분명 옳다. 정말이지 아주 잘생긴 사내로구나. 내 사촌의 하녀가 너에게 많은 여자가 그에게 빠져 있다고 한 것도 놀랄 일이 아니다. 이제 와 보니 내 사촌이 어디 있는지 그에게 알려 주지 않은 것이 후회스럽구나. 그렇지만 네 말대로 그가 그토록 지독한 난봉꾼이라면, 사촌이 다시는 그를 보지 않는 편이 차라리 낫겠지. 아버지의 허락도 없이 난봉꾼에다 빈털터리와 결혼해서 무엇이 따르겠느냐, 신세를 망치는 일밖에 더 있겠느냐? 단언컨대, 그가 그 계집애가 네게 묘사한 대로의 사람이라면, 사촌을 그에게서 떼어 놓는 것이야말로 자비를 베푸는 일이다. 그리고 나로서는 그렇게 하지 않는다면 결코 용서받지 못할 일이지. 그런 결혼에 따르는 불행을 이토록 쓰라리게 맛본 내가 아니냐.\"\n\n이때 한 방문객이 찾아드는 바람에 그녀의 말은 끊기고 말았는데, 그가 바로 다름 아닌 그 귀족이었다. 그리고 이 방문에서는 새롭다거나 특별하다 할 일도, 이 이야기에 어떻게든 긴요한 일도 전혀 일어나지 않았으므로, 우리는 여기서 이 장을 끝맺기로 한다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인이 잠자리에 들었을 때, 그녀의 머릿속은 온통 사촌 소피아와 존스 씨로 가득 차 있었다. 사실 그녀는 소피아에게 조금 서운한 마음이 들었는데, 이제야 그 솔직하지 못한 면을 알아차렸기 때문이다. 그렇게 한참 생각에 잠겨 상상을 펼치던 차에, 문득 다음과 같은 생각이 떠올랐다. 만일 자신이 소피아를 이 남자에게서 지켜내어 아버지 품으로 돌려보내는 길잡이가 될 수 있다면, 그토록 큰 봉사를 집안에 베푼 셈이니, 인간의 짐작이 닿는 한, 자신은 외숙부와 외숙모 웨스턴 두 분 모두와 화해하게 되리라는 것이었다.\n\n이것은 그녀가 가장 바라 마지않던 소망 가운데 하나였고, 성공의 희망 또한 무척이나 그럴듯해 보였기에, 이제 남은 것이라곤 그 계획을 이룰 적당한 방법을 궁리하는 일뿐이었다. 소피아와 직접 이치를 따져 설득해 보는 것은 그런 방법 가운데 하나로 보이지 않았다. 베티가 어너 부인에게서 전해 들은 바로는 소피아가 존스에게 격렬한 마음을 품고 있다 했으니, 이 혼사에서 그녀를 떼어 놓으려 애쓰는 것은, 나방에게 촛불 속으로 날아들지 말라고 진심으로 간곡히 애원하는 것과 다를 바 없는 노릇이라 여겼던 것이다.\n\n소피아가 벨라스턴 부인과 알게 된 것이 웨스턴 부인의 집에서였고, 그 친분이 피츠패트릭 부인이 바로 이 웨스턴 부인과 함께 지내던 그 무렵에 깊어졌으리라는 사실을 독자가 부디 기억해 준다면, 피츠패트릭 부인 역시 그 부인과 알고 지냈으리라는 점은 굳이 일러 줄 필요도 없을 것이다. 게다가 두 사람 모두 똑같이 그 부인의 먼 친척이기도 했다.\n\n그리하여 많은 궁리 끝에 그녀는 이튿날 아침 일찍 그 부인을 찾아가, 소피아 모르게 그녀를 만나 이 모든 사정을 알리기로 결심했다. 평소 대화에서 낭만적인 사랑과 분별없는 혼인을 자주 비웃던 그 신중한 부인이라면, 이 혼사에 관한 자신의 생각에 흔쾌히 동의하고, 그것을 막는 데 온 힘을 보태 주리라는 것을 그녀는 조금도 의심하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n이 결심을 그녀는 곧바로 실행에 옮겼다. 이튿날 아침 해가 뜨기도 전에 부랴부랴 옷을 걸치고는, 격식에도 맞지 않고 철도 맞지 않으며 방문하기에도 마땅찮은 시각에 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아갔다. 그리하여 소피아에게는 조금도 알려지거나 의심받는 일 없이 그 부인을 만날 수 있었으니, 소피아는 그때 잠들지는 않았으나 어너가 곁에서 코를 골며 자는 가운데 침대에 누워 깨어 있었다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인은 이른 시각에 불쑥 찾아온 것을 두고 거듭 사과하며, 이런 시각에는 \"더없이 중대한 일이 아니고서야 부인께 폐를 끼칠 생각조차 하지 않았을 것\"이라고 말했다. 그러고는 이 모든 사정을 털어놓으며 베티에게서 들은 것을 죄다 전했고, 전날 저녁 존스가 자신을 찾아왔던 일도 빠뜨리지 않았다.\n\n벨라스턴 부인은 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"그러니까 부인께서 그 무서운 사내를 보셨다는 거군요. 그런데, 그 사람이 정말 소문대로 그토록 멋들어진 인물인가요? 에토프가 어젯밤에 거의 두 시간이나 그 사람 이야기로 저를 즐겁게 해 주었답니다. 그 계집애는 소문만 듣고도 그 사람한테 반한 것 같아요.\" 여기서 독자는 의아해할 법하다. 하지만 사실인즉, 벨라스턴 부인의 옷을 핀으로 여미고 풀어 주는 영예를 누리던 에토프 부인이 그 존스 씨에 관한 정보를 빠짐없이 입수해 두었다가, 어젯밤(아니, 정확히는 그날 새벽이라 해야겠다) 부인이 옷을 벗는 동안 그 이야기를 고스란히 전했던 것이다. 그런 까닭에 그녀는 한 시간 반이 넘도록 그 자리에서 붙들려 있었다.\n\n사실 그 부인은 그런 시각에 에토프 부인이 들려주는 이야기에 으레 흡족해하기는 했으나, 이번 존스에 관한 이야기에는 유난히 귀를 기울였다. 어너가 그를 무척 잘생긴 사내라고 묘사해 둔 데다, 에토프 부인이 흥에 겨워 자기 이야기 속에 그의 외모를 한껏 부풀려 보탰던 터라, 벨라스턴 부인은 그를 자연이 빚은 일종의 기적쯤으로 여기기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n자기 하녀가 불러일으킨 그 호기심은 이제 피츠패트릭 부인 덕분에 한층 더 부풀어 올랐다. 그녀는 앞서 존스의 출신과 인품과 재산을 헐뜯었던 만큼이나, 그의 사람됨에 대해서는 한껏 좋게 말했기 때문이다.\n\n벨라스턴 부인은 모든 이야기를 다 듣고 나서 진지하게 대답했다. \"정말이지, 부인, 이건 대단히 중대한 일이군요. 부인께서 하시는 일만큼 칭찬받아 마땅한 것도 없겠지요. 저 또한 이토록 훌륭하고, 제가 그토록 존중해 마지않는 젊은 아가씨를 지켜내는 데 한몫을 거들 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다.\"\n\n\"부인께서는 생각하지 않으세요.\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 간절하게 말했다. \"당장 외숙부께 편지를 써서, 제 사촌이 어디 있는지 알려 드리는 것이 가장 좋은 방법이라고요?\"\n\n부인은 이 말을 잠시 곰곰이 헤아리더니 이렇게 대답했다. \"글쎄요, 부인, 저는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요. 디 웨스턴이 자기 오라비를 어찌나 짐승 같은 사람으로 묘사하던지, 일단 그 손아귀에서 벗어난 여자를 다시 그 밑으로 들여보내는 데에는 도저히 찬성할 수가 없군요. 듣자 하니 그 사람은 제 아내한테도 괴물처럼 굴었다더군요. 우리 여자들을 짓밟을 권리라도 있는 줄 아는 그런 못된 작자 가운데 하나이니 말이에요. 그런 자들의 손아귀에 들어갈 불운에 처한 여자라면 누구든 구해 내는 것을, 저는 언제나 우리 여성 모두의 대의라고 여길 거예요. 여보세요, 사촌, 할 일이란 그저 웨스턴 양이 이 젊은 사내를 만나지 못하게 막는 것뿐이에요. 여기서 좋은 사람들을 만날 기회가 생길 테니, 그러다 보면 그 애의 마음도 더 올바른 쪽으로 돌아설 거예요.\"\n\n\"만일 그 사람이 그 애를 찾아낸다면요, 부인.\" 다른 쪽이 대답했다. \"장담하건대 그 사람은 그 애한테 다가가려고 무슨 짓이든 마다하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 부인.\" 부인이 응수했다. \"그 사람이 이 집에 들어오기란 불가능한 일이지요. 다만 그 애가 어디 있는지 어떻게든 정보를 얻어 내, 집 주변을 어슬렁거릴 수는 있겠군요. 그러니 저로서는 그 사람 얼굴을 알아 두었으면 싶어요.\n\n부인, 제가 그 사람을 한번 볼 방법이 없을까요? 그렇지 않으면, 아시다시피, 사촌, 그 애가 제 모르게 여기서 그 사람을 만날 궁리를 할 수도 있으니 말이에요.\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 대답하기를, \"그 사람이 오늘 오후에 다시 찾아오겠다고 자기를 으름장 놓았으니, 만일 부인께서 그때 자기를 찾아 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 여섯 시에서 일곱 시 사이에 그 사람을 보시기 어렵지 않을 거예요. 그리고 그 사람이 더 일찍 온다면, 어떻게든 부인이 오실 때까지 그를 붙들어 두겠어요.\" 벨라스턴 부인이 답했다. \"식사 자리에서 빠져나오는 대로 곧장 오겠어요. 아무리 늦어도 일곱 시까지는 일어날 수 있을 테니까요. 그 사람 얼굴을 알아 두는 것이 무엇보다 꼭 필요한 일이거든요. 정말이지, 부인.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이렇게 웨스턴 양을 돌봐 주시다니 참으로 갸륵한 일이에요. 하지만 우리 집안에 대한 마음은 둘째 치고, 사람으로서의 도리만 따져도 우리 둘 다 마땅히 해야 할 일이지요. 그건 정말이지 끔찍한 혼사가 될 테니까요.\"\n\n피츠패트릭 부인은 벨라스턴 부인이 자기 사촌에게 베푼 그 칭찬에 어김없이 합당한 답례를 올렸고, 이런저런 대수롭지 않은 이야기를 나눈 뒤 물러났다. 그러고는 소피아나 어너의 눈에 띄지 않게 부랴부랴 가마에 올라 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n방문으로 이루어진 장.\n\n존스는 온종일 어떤 문이 보이는 거리 안에서 서성거렸다. 그날은 일 년 중 가장 짧은 날 가운데 하나였건만, 그에게는 가장 긴 날 가운데 하나로 여겨졌다. 마침내 시계가 다섯 시를 알리자, 그는 피츠패트릭 부인에게로 돌아갔다. 점잖은 방문 시각보다 한 시간이나 이른 때였지만, 부인은 그를 매우 정중하게 맞이했다. 그러면서도 소피아에 관해서는 여전히 아무것도 모른다고 우겼다.\n\n존스는 자신의 천사를 찾으면서 사촌이라는 말을 무심코 흘렸고, 그러자 피츠패트릭 부인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 선생, 우리가 친척 사이라는 걸 아시겠군요. 사정이 그러하니, 제 사촌과 어떤 용건이 있으신지 그 세세한 내막을 캐물을 권리를 제게 허락하셔야겠어요.\" 여기서 존스는 한참을 머뭇거리다가 마침내 대답하기를, \"그녀의 상당한 액수의 돈을 자신이 맡아 가지고 있으며, 그것을 그녀에게 전해 주고 싶다\"고 했다. 그러고는 지갑을 꺼내어, 그 안에 든 것과 그것이 어떤 경위로 자신의 손에 들어오게 되었는지를 피츠패트릭 부인에게 알렸다. 그가 이야기를 채 끝내기도 전에, 더없이 사나운 소음이 온 집안을 뒤흔들었다. 그 소리를 들어 본 적 있는 사람들에게 그것을 묘사하려 드는 일은 부질없을 것이며, 그런 소리를 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 사람들에게 어떤 관념이나마 전하려 애쓰는 일은 더더욱 부질없을 것이다. 참으로 이렇게 말할 수 있으니—\n\n—날카롭게 울리는 키벨레 사제들의 청동도 이처럼 거듭 울리지는 않는다.\n\n요컨대, 하인 하나가 문을 두드렸다, 아니 천둥처럼 내리쳤다. 존스는 그 소리에 적잖이 놀랐다. 전에는 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 피츠패트릭 부인은 매우 차분하게, 손님이 오는 중이라 지금은 그에게 어떤 답도 해 줄 수 없으나, 만약 그들이 돌아갈 때까지 기다려 주겠다면 그에게 할 말이 있다고 넌지시 일렀다.\n\n이제 방문이 활짝 열리더니, 후프 치마를 옆으로 밀어 넣으며 벨라스턴 부인이 들어왔다. 그녀는 먼저 피츠패트릭 부인에게 매우 깊이 절을 하고, 존스에게도 그만큼 깊이 절을 한 뒤, 방의 상석으로 안내받았다.\n\n이런 사소한 일들을 굳이 적어 두는 것은, 우리가 아는 어떤 시골 부인들을 위해서다. 그들은 남자에게 무릎을 굽히는 것이 정숙함의 규칙에 어긋난다고 여기는 사람들이다.\n\n손님들이 채 자리를 제대로 잡기도 전에, 앞서 언급한 그 귀족의 도착이 새로운 소동을 일으켰고, 인사 절차가 다시 한 번 되풀이되었다.\n\n이것이 다 끝나자, 대화는 (흔히 말하듯) 더없이 빛나기 시작했다. 그러나 그 안에서는 이 이야기에 중요하다 할 만한 것도, 사실 그 자체로 그리 대단한 것도 오가지 않았으므로, 나는 그 내용을 옮기는 일을 생략하겠다. 더구나 나는 어떤 아주 멋지고 세련된 대화가 책에 옮겨지거나 무대 위에서 되풀이될 때 지독히도 따분해지는 것을 보아 왔기 때문이다. 사실 이런 정신의 만찬이란 하나의 진미여서, 세련된 모임에서 배제된 사람들은 그것을 모르는 채로 만족할 수밖에 없으니, 마치 오직 귀인의 식탁에만 오르는 프랑스 요리의 여러 진미를 모르는 채 지내야 하는 것과 같다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이 둘 다 누구의 입맛에나 맞는 것은 아니어서, 천한 사람들에게는 둘 다 종종 헛되이 버려지기 십상일 것이다.\n\n가엾은 존스는 이 우아한 장면 속에서 배우라기보다 차라리 구경꾼이었다. 귀족이 도착하기 전의 짧은 틈에는 처음에는 벨라스턴 부인이, 그다음에는 피츠패트릭 부인이 대화의 일부를 그에게 건네기도 했으나, 그 고귀한 귀족이 들어서자마자 두 부인의 모든 관심을 자기 혼자 독차지해 버렸기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 이따금 존스를 빤히 쳐다보는 것 말고는 마치 그런 사람이 그 자리에 없기라도 한 듯 그에게 더 이상 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않자, 두 부인도 그의 본을 따랐다.\n\n손님들이 이제 너무 오래 머무는 바람에, 피츠패트릭 부인은 그들 모두가 서로 끝까지 버티며 누가 더 오래 남나 겨루려 한다는 것을 빤히 알아차렸다. 그래서 부인은 존스를 떼어 버리기로 마음먹었으니, 그가 가장 격식을 차릴 필요가 적다고 여겨지는 방문객이었기 때문이다. 그리하여 잡담이 잠시 멎은 틈을 타, 부인은 그에게 진지하게 말을 건넸다. \"선생, 그 일에 관해서는 오늘 밤 도저히 답을 드릴 수가 없겠어요. 하지만 괜찮으시다면, 내일 어디로 사람을 보내면 될지 말을 남겨 주시겠어요?\"\n\n존스는 타고난 좋은 예법은 갖추었으나, 꾸며 낸 예법은 없었다. 그래서 자기 숙소의 비밀을 하인에게 알리는 대신, 부인에게 직접 그것을 자세히 일러 주고는, 곧이어 매우 격식 있게 물러났다.\n\n그가 떠나기가 무섭게, 그가 있을 때는 그에게 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않던 그 거물들은 그가 없는 자리에서 그를 한껏 입에 올리기 시작했다. 그러나 독자가 이미 이 대화의 더 빛나는 부분을 옮기는 일을 우리에게 면해 주었다면, 천한 험담이라 할 만한 것을 되풀이하는 일은 분명 기꺼이 면해 줄 것이다. 다만 어쩌면 우리 이야기에 중요할지 모를 한 가지, 곧 벨라스턴 부인의 한마디만은 언급해 둘 만하다. 부인은 존스가 떠나고 몇 분 뒤 자리를 뜨면서, 떠나는 길에 피츠패트릭 부인에게 이렇게 말했다. \"내 사촌 일은 이제 마음이 놓이는군요. 그 애가 저런 자에게서 위험에 처할 일은 없겠어요.\"\n\n우리의 이야기도 벨라스턴 부인의 본을 따라, 이제 두 사람으로 줄어든 지금의 일행에게 작별을 고하겠다. 두 사람 사이에는 우리나 우리 독자와 조금이라도 관계되는 일이 전혀 오가지 않았으니, 우리는 그것에 마음을 빼앗겨, 우리 주인공의 일에 조금이라도 관심을 둔 모든 이에게 더 중요하게 여겨질 만한 일들로부터 멀어지는 일이 없도록 하겠다.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n존스가 자기 숙소에서 겪은 모험, 그리고 그 집에 묵고 있던 한 젊은 신사와 집주인 여인, 그 두 딸에 관한 약간의 이야기.\n\n이튿날 아침, 예의에 어긋나지 않을 만큼 이른 시각이 되자 존스는 피츠패트릭 부인의 문 앞을 찾았으나, 부인은 집에 안 계신다는 대답을 들었다. 이 대답이 그를 더욱 놀라게 한 것은, 그가 동틀 무렵부터 그 거리를 이리저리 오가고 있었던 터라, 만일 부인이 외출했다면 분명 그 모습을 보았을 것이기 때문이었다. 그러나 그는 이 대답을 받아들일 수밖에 없었으니, 그것도 이번 한 번만이 아니라 그날 하루 부인을 다섯 차례나 찾아갔을 때마다 그러했다.\n\n독자에게 솔직히 말하자면, 그 고귀한 귀족은 어떤 이유에서인지, 어쩌면 부인의 명예를 염려해서인지, 부인이 천한 작자로밖에 보지 않던 존스 씨를 다시는 만나서는 안 된다고 강하게 못 박았던 것이다. 그리고 부인은 그 약속을 받아들였으니, 지금 우리가 보는 대로 그토록 철저히 지키고 있는 것이다.\n\n그러나 우리의 점잖은 독자께서는 어쩌면 부인보다 이 젊은 신사를 더 좋게 보고 계실지도 모르고, 또 그가 소피아와 이렇게 불행하게 떨어져 지내는 동안 여관이나 길거리에서 거처를 정한 것은 아닐까 하는 걱정마저 품으실지 모르니, 이제 그의 숙소에 관해 이야기해 보겠다. 그곳은 사실 매우 평판 좋은 집이었고, 시내에서도 아주 괜찮은 동네에 자리하고 있었다.\n\n존스 씨는 올워디 씨가 시내에 머물 때 늘 묵곤 하던 어느 부인에 관해 자주 들어 두었던 터였다. 존스 역시 알고 있었듯이 본드 거리에 사는 이 사람은 어느 성직자의 미망인으로, 남편이 세상을 떠나면서 그녀에게 두 딸과 직접 쓴 설교문 원고 한 묶음을 고스란히 남겼던 것이다.\n\n이 두 딸 가운데 언니 낸시는 이제 열일곱 살이 되었고, 동생 베티는 열 살이 되었다.\n\n존스는 이리로 파트리지를 먼저 보냈고, 이 집에서 자신은 삼층에 방 하나를, 파트리지는 오층에 방 하나를 마련했다.\n\n이층에는 지난 시대에 도시의 재치와 향락을 좇는 사람들이라 불리던 젊은 신사 가운데 하나가 살고 있었는데, 그 호칭은 꽤나 적절했다. 사람을 흔히 그가 하는 일이나 직업으로 일컫듯이, 운명이 모든 쓸모 있는 일거리를 불필요하게 만들어 준 이런 신사들에게는 향락이야말로 유일한 일이자 직업이었다고 말할 수 있기 때문이다. 극장과 커피점과 선술집이 그들이 만나는 무대였다. 재치와 해학은 한가로운 시간의 즐길 거리였고, 사랑은 좀 더 진지한 순간의 일거리였다. 술과 시의 여신들이 한데 어우러져 그들의 가슴속에 가장 환한 불꽃을 지폈으니, 그들은 단지 아름다움을 흠모하기만 한 것이 아니라, 더러는 흠모하는 아름다움을 노래로 기릴 줄도 알았고, 또 다들 그런 작품의 가치를 판단할 줄도 알았다.\n\n그러므로 그런 이들이야말로 마땅히 재치와 향락을 좇는 사람들이라 불릴 만했다. 그러나 우리 시대의 젊은 신사들, 곧 재능으로 두드러지려는 같은 야망을 품은 자들에게 똑같은 호칭을 똑같이 적절하게 붙일 수 있을지는 의문이다. 재치로 말하자면 그들은 분명 아무런 관계가 없다. 공정하게 말하자면, 그들은 선배들보다 한 단계 더 높이 날아올라 지혜와 베르튀(부디 미덕이라고 읽지 마시기를)를 좇는 사람들이라 불릴 만하다. 그리하여 앞서 말한 신사들이 한 여인의 매력에 술잔을 기울이거나 그녀를 기리는 소네트를 짓는 데, 극장의 연극이나 윌스나 버튼스에서 어느 시에 관한 의견을 내놓는 데 시간을 쓰던 그 나이에, 이 신사들은 어느 자치단체를 매수할 방법을 궁리하거나 하원에서 할 연설을, 아니 차라리 잡지에 실을 연설을 골똘히 생각한다. 그러나 무엇보다도 그들의 머릿속을 차지하는 것은 도박의 학문이다. 이것이 그들의 좀 더 진지한 시간에 매달리는 공부이고, 한편 그들이 즐기는 것으로는 감식안이라는 드넓은 세계, 곧 회화, 음악, 조각, 그리고 자연철학—아니 차라리 부자연철학이라 해야 할 것이니, 그것은 경이로운 것만을 다루고 자연에 대해서는 그 괴물과 결함밖에 알지 못한다—이 있다.\n\n존스가 하루 종일 피츠패트릭 부인을 헛되이 수소문하며 보낸 뒤, 마침내 풀이 죽은 채 자기 방으로 돌아왔다. 이곳에서 홀로 슬픔을 토해 내고 있는데, 아래층에서 요란한 소동이 들려왔다. 곧이어 한 여인의 목소리가 제발 내려와서 살인을 막아 달라고 그에게 애원했다. 어떤 경우에도 어려움에 빠진 사람을 돕는 일에 결코 뒤로 물러서는 법이 없는 존스는 즉시 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다. 그 모든 소리가 흘러나오던 식당으로 발을 들여놓자, 그는 앞서 말한 그 지혜와 베르튀를 좇는 젊은 신사가 하인에게 벽에 바짝 짓눌려 있는 것을 보았고, 그 곁에 한 젊은 여인이 서서 두 손을 비틀며 \"이 사람이 죽겠어요! 죽겠어요!\" 하고 외치고 있는 것을 보았다. 그리고 실로 그 가엾은 신사는 목이 졸려 죽을 위험에 처한 듯 보였는데, 존스가 급히 그를 도우러 날아들어, 그가 막 마지막 숨을 내쉬려는 찰나에 그 무자비한 적의 손아귀에서 그를 구해 냈다.\n\n이 작자는 힘보다 기개가 앞서던 그 작은 신사에게서 발길질과 주먹질을 여러 차례 당했음에도, 주인을 때리는 것만큼은 일종의 양심의 거리낌으로 삼아 그저 목을 조르는 것으로 만족하려던 참이었다. 그러나 존스에게는 그런 존중심을 품지 않았다. 그래서 새로 나타난 적수에게 다소 거칠게 다뤄지자마자, 그는 존스의 명치에 한 방을 먹였다. 그런 주먹질은 브로턴 원형 경기장의 구경꾼들에게는 더없는 기쁨을 안겨 주지만, 정작 맞는 사람에게는 즐거움이라곤 거의 전해 주지 못하는 법이다.\n\n이 한 방을 맞자마자 우리의 건장한 젊은이는 더없이 고마운 답례를 궁리했다. 이제 존스와 하인 사이에 한바탕 격투가 벌어졌는데, 그것은 매우 사납기는 했으나 짧았다. 이 작자는 앞서 그의 주인이 그를 당해 내지 못했던 것처럼, 존스를 도저히 당해 낼 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n이제 운명의 여신은 늘 그러하듯 형세를 뒤집어 놓았다. 좀 전의 승자는 숨도 못 쉬고 바닥에 나뒹굴었고, 패배했던 신사는 숨을 충분히 되찾아 때맞춰 도와준 존스 씨에게 고마움을 전했다. 그는 또한 그 자리에 있던 젊은 여인의 진심 어린 감사도 받았는데, 그 여인은 다름 아닌 그 집 맏딸 낸시 양이었다.\n\n이제 다리를 되찾은 하인은 존스를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 무언가 알겠다는 표정으로 외쳤다. \"에잇, 빌어먹을, 난 당신하곤 더 상대 안 하겠소. 당신 무대에 서 본 적이 있는 게 분명해, 아니면 내가 지독히 잘못 본 거고.\" 그리고 실로 우리는 그의 이 의심을 용서해 줄 만하다. 우리 주인공의 날렵함과 힘이 어찌나 대단했던지, 그는 어쩌면 일류 권투 선수와도 맞붙을 만했고, 브로턴 씨의 학교를 거친 솜장갑 낀 졸업생들쯤은 아주 손쉽게 모조리 때려눕힐 수 있었을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n* 솜장갑: 후세 사람들이 이 표현에 어리둥절해할까 싶어, 1747년 2월 1일에 실린 광고로 설명해 두는 것이 좋겠다. 주의—브로턴 씨는 적절한 도움을 받아, 헤이마켓에 있는 자기 집에 권투의 비법에 입문하고자 하는 이들을 가르칠 학교를 열고자 한다. 이곳에서는 그 진정한 영국식 기술의 이론과 실기 전반이, 격투에 따르는 온갖 막기, 치기, 엇걸어 메치기 등과 더불어 빠짐없이 가르쳐지고 설명될 것이다. 또한 지체 높고 명망 있는 분들이 이 강좌를 듣기를 꺼리지 않도록, 수강생의 몸과 체질의 섬세함을 최대한 부드럽게 배려하여 강의가 진행될 것이며, 그런 까닭에 솜장갑이 마련되어 있어, 멍든 눈과 부러진 턱과 코피의 불편함으로부터 그들을 확실히 지켜 줄 것이다.\n\n주인은 분노로 입에 거품을 물고 하인에게 당장 옷을 벗으라고 명했고, 하인은 밀린 품삯을 받는다는 조건으로 선뜻 응했다. 이 조건은 곧바로 받아들여졌고, 그 작자는 해고되었다.\n\n이제 나이팅게일이라는 이름의 젊은 신사는 자기를 구해 준 사람에게 함께 포도주 한 병을 나누자고 한사코 권했다. 존스는 여러 차례 청을 받고서야 응했는데, 마음이 내켜서라기보다는 그저 사양하기 어려워서였다. 이때 그의 마음이 너무도 편치 않아 대화를 나눌 기분이라곤 거의 들지 않았기 때문이다. 낸시 양도, 마침 어머니와 동생이 둘 다 연극을 보러 간 터라 그때 집 안에 있던 유일한 여자였는데, 기꺼이 함께 자리해 주는 호의를 베풀었다.\n\n술병과 잔이 식탁에 놓이자 신사는 앞서 벌어진 소동의 사연을 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"이런 일이 있었다고 해서,\" 그가 존스에게 말했다. \"선생, 부디 제가 하인을 때리는 것을 버릇처럼 한다고 여기지는 마십시오. 제 기억으로 이런 죄를 저지른 건 이번이 처음이라고 분명히 말씀드리며, 그 작자가 저를 이 지경까지 부추기기 전에 그가 저지른 화나는 잘못을 여러 번 그냥 넘겨주었답니다. 그러나 오늘 저녁 무슨 일이 있었는지 들으시면, 아마 저를 용서해 줄 만하다 여기실 겁니다. 제가 평소보다 몇 시간이나 일찍 집에 돌아왔더니, 성직자 옷을 입은 신사 네 명이 제 난롯가에서 휘스트를 치고 있더군요. 게다가 제 호일 책이, 선생, 일 기니나 주고 산 제일 좋은 호일 책이 펼쳐진 채 식탁에 놓여 있고, 그 책 전체에서 가장 중요한 책장 가운데 한 장에 흑맥주가 잔뜩 쏟아져 있지 뭡니까. 이건 화날 만했다고 인정하시겠지요. 그래도 저는 그 점잖은 일행이 다 갈 때까지 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 그 작자에게 부드럽게 꾸짖었습니다. 그런데 그자는 미안한 기색은커녕 건방진 대답을 내놓더군요. '하인도 남들처럼 즐길 거리가 있어야 하는 법이다, 책에 생긴 일은 안됐지만 자기 아는 사람 여럿이 똑같은 책을 일 실링이면 샀더라, 그러니 원하시면 품삯에서 그만큼 떼어 가시라'고요. 제가 이번엔 전보다 더 호되게 나무랐더니, 그 무뢰한이 뻔뻔하게도—요컨대, 제가 일찍 집에 온 것을 두고—요컨대, 비아냥거리는 말을 하더군요—그자가 어떤 젊은 숙녀의 이름을 입에 올리는데, 그 말투가—그 말투가 어찌나 저를 더는 참을 수 없게 만들던지, 그만 화가 치밀어 제가 그를 때리고 말았습니다.\"\n\n존스가 대답했다. \"살아 있는 누구도 선생을 탓하지 않으리라 믿습니다. 저로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다. \"솔직히 방금 말씀하신 그런 도발을 당했더라면 저도 똑같이 했을 겁니다.\"\n\n우리 일행이 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 연극을 보고 돌아온 어머니와 딸이 합류했다. 이제 그들은 다 함께 매우 즐거운 저녁을 보냈으니, 존스를 빼고는 모두 진심으로 흥겨워했고, 그조차도 애써 할 수 있는 한 즐거운 척했다. 사실 그의 타고난 활기와 쾌활함이 절반쯤만 살아나도, 그 부드러운 성품과 어우러져 더없이 사랑스러운 길동무가 되기에 충분했다. 마음이 무거웠음에도 그는 이날 자리에서 어찌나 유쾌하게 굴었던지, 자리가 파할 무렵 젊은 신사는 그와 더 가까이 사귀기를 간곡히 청했다. 낸시 양도 그를 무척 마음에 들어 했고, 새 하숙인에게 완전히 반한 미망인은 그와 또 다른 신사를 이튿날 아침 식사에 초대했다.\n\n존스 쪽에서도 못지않게 흡족했다. 낸시 양으로 말하자면, 몸집은 아주 작아도 더없이 예뻤고, 미망인은 쉰을 바라보는 여인을 꾸며 줄 수 있는 온갖 매력을 다 지니고 있었다. 그녀는 세상에서 가장 순진한 사람 가운데 하나인 만큼, 또한 가장 쾌활한 사람 가운데 하나였다. 그녀는 결코 나쁜 것을 생각하지도, 말하지도, 바라지도 않았으며, 늘 남을 기쁘게 하려는 마음을 품고 있었다. 그 마음은 모든 바람 가운데 가장 행복한 것이라 할 만한데, 가식으로 흠집 나지만 않는다면 그 목적을 이루지 못하는 일이 거의 없기 때문이다. 요컨대 그녀의 힘은 보잘것없었으나, 그 마음만큼은 더없이 따뜻한 벗 가운데 하나였다. 그녀는 더없이 다정한 아내였고, 더없이 자상하고 살뜰한 어머니였다. 우리 이야기는 신문처럼 전에 들어 본 적도 없고 앞으로도 들을 일 없는 사람들에게 거창한 인물됨을 부여하지는 않으니, 독자께서는 이로써 이 훌륭한 여인이 앞으로 우리 이야기에서 어느 정도 중요한 자리를 차지하리라 짐작하셔도 좋다.\n\n존스는 또한 자기가 그 포도주를 얻어 마신 그 젊은 신사 자신에게도 적잖이 호감을 느꼈다. 그는 그에게서 비록 도시풍의 멋부림에 다소 물들어 있긴 해도 상당한 분별력을 엿볼 수 있다고 여겼다. 그러나 무엇보다 존스의 마음을 끈 것은, 그에게서 이따금 흘러나오는 크나큰 너그러움과 인정 어린 마음씨였고, 특히 사랑의 문제에서 더없이 사심 없는 태도를 드러내는 여러 표현이었다. 이 주제에 관해 젊은 신사는 옛 아르카디아의 목동에게나 썩 어울릴 법한 말로 자신을 털어놓았는데, 그런 말이 현대의 멋쟁이 신사 입에서 나오니 무척이나 별스럽게 들렸다. 그러나 그는 그저 흉내로만 멋쟁이 신사였을 뿐, 본바탕은 훨씬 더 나은 인물로 타고난 사람이었다.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n일행은 어제 저녁 헤어질 때 서로에게 품었던 그 살가운 마음을 그대로 간직한 채 아침에 다시 모였다. 그러나 가엾은 존스만큼은 더없이 풀이 죽어 있었다. 방금 파트리지에게서 피츠패트릭 부인이 묵던 거처를 떠났으며, 그녀가 어디로 갔는지는 도무지 알아낼 수 없었다는 소식을 전해 들었기 때문이다. 이 소식은 그를 크게 괴롭혔고, 아무리 애써 감추려 해도 그의 표정이며 거동에는 마음이 헝클어졌다는 기색이 역력히 드러나고 말았다.\n\n지금 화제는 전과 마찬가지로 사랑으로 흘러갔다. 나이팅게일 씨는 이 주제를 두고 또다시 그 따뜻하고 너그럽고 사심 없는 감정을 여럿 늘어놓았는데, 지혜롭고 분별 있는 남자들은 이런 것을 두고 낭만적이라 부르지만, 지혜롭고 분별 있는 여자들은 대개 더 곱게 보아 준다. 밀러 부인(이 집 안주인의 이름이 그러했다)은 이런 감정에 크게 동의했다. 그러나 이 젊은 신사가 낸시 양에게 의견을 물었을 때, 그녀는 다만 이렇게만 답했다. \"가장 말을 적게 한 분이야말로 가장 깊이 느낄 줄 아는 분이라고 생각해요.\"\n\n이 찬사는 워낙 대놓고 존스를 겨눈 것이어서, 만일 그가 그것을 흘려보냈다면 우리로서는 무척 안타까웠을 것이다. 그는 실로 그녀에게 매우 정중하게 답했고, 끝에 가서는 그녀 자신의 침묵 역시 똑같은 의심을 살 만하다는 점을 에둘러 일러 주었다. 사실 그녀는 지금도, 어젯밤에도 거의 입을 열지 않았으니 말이다.\n\n\"잘됐구나, 내니.\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"이 신사분이 그 점을 짚어 주셨으니 말이다. 정말이지 나도 거의 같은 생각이란다. 대체 무슨 일이니, 얘야? 이렇게 변한 모습은 처음 본다. 그 명랑하던 기운은 다 어디로 갔니? 선생님, 믿어지세요? 제가 이 아이를 제 작은 수다쟁이라고 부르곤 했답니다. 그런데 이번 주에는 스무 마디도 안 했어요.\"\n\n여기서 둘의 대화는 한 하녀가 들어오는 바람에 끊겼다. 하녀는 손에 꾸러미 하나를 들고 와서, \"짐꾼이 존스 씨께 전하라며 두고 갔습니다\"라고 말했다. 그러고는 \"그 사람이 답장은 필요 없다는 말만 남기고 곧장 가 버렸어요\"라고 덧붙였다.\n\n존스는 이 일에 다소 놀라움을 드러내며 무슨 착오일 것이라고 단언했다. 그러나 하녀가 이름만큼은 분명하다고 거듭 우기는 통에, 여자들은 모두 어서 그 꾸러미를 풀어 보고 싶어 했다. 마침내 존스 씨의 허락을 받아 어린 베치가 그 일을 해냈고, 안에서 나온 것은 도미노 한 벌과 가면 하나, 그리고 가면무도회 입장권 한 장이었다.\n\n존스는 이제 그 어느 때보다도 단호하게, 이 물건들이 착오로 전해진 게 틀림없다고 주장했다. 밀러 부인 자신도 다소 미심쩍어하며 \"어찌 생각해야 할지 모르겠다\"고 말했다. 그러나 나이팅게일 씨에게 물으니, 그는 사뭇 다른 견해를 내놓았다. \"선생, 제가 여기서 끌어낼 수 있는 결론은 단 하나, 선생이 무척 운 좋은 사람이라는 것뿐입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"이건 분명 어느 부인이 보낸 것이고, 선생은 그 가면무도회에서 그분을 만나는 행복을 누리게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n존스에게는 그런 우쭐한 상상을 품을 만큼의 허영심이 없었다. 밀러 부인 자신도 나이팅게일 씨의 말에 그다지 동의하지 않았다. 그런데 낸시 양이 도미노를 들어 올리자 소매에서 카드 한 장이 떨어졌고, 거기에는 다음과 같이 적혀 있었다.\n존스 씨께.\n\n요정의 여왕이 그대에게 이것을 보내니, 그 호의를 함부로 쓰지 마시기를.\n\n이제 밀러 부인과 낸시 양도 둘 다 나이팅게일 씨의 말에 동의했다. 아니, 존스 자신마저 거의 같은 생각으로 기울 뻔했다. 그리고 자기 거처를 아는 부인은 피츠패트릭 부인밖에 없다고 여긴 그는, 이것이 그녀에게서 온 것이며 어쩌면 자신의 소피아를 만나게 될지도 모른다는 희망에 부풀기 시작했다. 이런 희망에는 분명 별다른 근거가 없었다. 그러나 피츠패트릭 부인이 약속대로 그를 만나 주지도 않고 거처마저 떠나 버린 그 행동이 워낙 기이하고 영문 모를 일이었던지라, 그는 어렴풋한 희망 하나를 품게 되었다. 곧, 예전부터 무척 변덕스럽다는 평을 들어 온 그녀가, 흔한 방식으로는 마다한 그 도움을 어쩌면 이런 별난 방식으로 베풀려는 것인지도 모른다는 희망이었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이토록 기이하고 흔치 않은 일에서는 무엇 하나 확실한 결론을 끌어낼 수 없었으니, 그는 도리어 마음 가는 대로 어떤 상상의 결론이든 그려 볼 더 넓은 여지를 얻은 셈이었다. 그리하여 천성이 낙천적이던 그는 이번 기회에 그 기질을 한껏 풀어놓았고, 그의 상상은 그날 저녁 사랑하는 소피아를 만나리라는 기대를 떠받치고 부풀리려 천 가지 망상을 지어냈다.\n\n독자여, 그대가 내게 무언가 좋은 마음을 품고 있다면, 나는 그대 또한 이런 낙천적 기질을 지니기를 바라는 것으로 그 마음을 온전히 갚으려 한다. 그토록 많은 위대한 펜들이 매달려 온 행복이라는 주제를 오래 읽고 길이 헤아려 본 끝에, 나는 행복이란 거의 이 기질을 지니는 데 있다고 여기는 쪽으로 기울었기 때문이다. 이 기질은 우리를 말하자면 운명의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 데려다 놓으며, 그녀의 도움 없이도 우리를 행복하게 해 준다. 실로 이 기질이 안기는 즐거움의 느낌은, 저 눈먼 여신이 베푸는 것보다 훨씬 한결같을 뿐 아니라 훨씬 더 날카롭다. 자연은 지혜롭게도, 우리가 누리는 모든 실제 즐거움에는 어느 정도의 물림과 나른함을 함께 붙여 두었으니, 우리가 그것에 너무 사로잡혀 더 나아갈 길을 멈추는 일이 없게 하려 함이다. 이런 눈으로 보자면, 이제 막 변호사로 불려 나온 상상 속의 미래 대법관이나, 검은 상복 같은 옷을 걸친 대주교나, 야당의 꽁무니에 붙은 수상이, 그 각각의 자리에 따르는 온갖 권세와 이익을 거머쥔 자들보다 도리어 더 참되게 행복하다는 것을 나는 조금도 의심하지 않는다.\n\n이제 존스 씨가 그날 저녁 가면무도회에 가기로 마음을 굳히자, 나이팅게일 씨는 자신이 그를 데려다주겠노라 나섰다. 그러면서 이 젊은 신사는 낸시 양과 그 어머니에게도 입장권을 권했다. 그러나 마음씨 고운 부인은 이를 받지 않았다. 그녀는 이렇게 말했다. \"어떤 이들이 가면무도회에 있다고 상상하는 그 해악을 저는 잘 모르겠어요. 다만 그렇게 분에 넘치는 놀이는 지체 높고 재산 있는 분들에게나 어울리지, 제 손으로 먹고살아야 하고 잘해야 착실한 장사꾼에게 시집가기를 바랄 수밖에 없는 처녀들에게 맞는 것은 아니지요.\"—\"장사꾼이라뇨!\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"제 낸시를 그렇게 낮춰 보시면 안 됩니다. 이 세상에 그녀의 됨됨이보다 나은 귀족은 없습니다.\" \"어머, 무슨 말씀을, 나이팅게일 씨.\" 밀러 부인이 답했다. \"그런 헛된 공상으로 아이 머릿속을 채우시면 안 돼요. 하지만\"—어머니는 빙긋 웃으며 말을 이었다—\"이 아이가 운이 좋아 선생님처럼 너그러운 마음을 지닌 신사를 만난다면, 분에 넘치는 즐거움에 마음을 내주는 것보다 그 너그러움에 더 나은 보답을 할 줄 알기를 바라요. 정말이지, 처녀가 제 손으로 큰 재산을 가져오는 경우라면 제 것을 쓰겠다고 고집할 권리쯤은 있겠지요. 그래서 신사분들이 하는 말을 들어 보면, 남자에게는 부유한 아내보다 가난한 아내가 때로 더 남는 흥정이라더군요.—하지만 제 딸들이 누구와 혼인하든, 저는 그 아이들이 남편에게 복이 되도록 힘쓸 거예요.—그러니 부디 가면무도회 이야기는 더 듣지 않았으면 합니다. 낸시는 분명 가고 싶다고 조를 만큼 철없는 아이가 아니에요. 작년에 선생님이 그 애를 거기 데려가셨을 때, 하마터면 정신이 다 나갈 뻔했던 일을 그 아이도 기억할 테니까요. 그 뒤로 한 달이 지나서야 겨우 제정신으로, 또 제 바느질거리로 돌아왔답니다.\"\n\n낸시의 가슴에서 가만히 새어 나온 부드러운 한숨이 이 말에 대한 어떤 은밀한 못마땅함을 드러내는 듯했으나, 그녀는 감히 드러내 놓고 맞서지는 못했다. 이 마음씨 고운 부인은 부모로서의 온갖 다정함을 지녔으되, 부모로서의 온갖 권위 또한 고스란히 지키고 있었기 때문이다. 자식들의 바람을 너그러이 받아 주는 그녀의 마음은 오직 그들의 안전과 앞날의 행복을 염려하는 마음에만 제동이 걸렸으니, 그런 염려에서 나온 분부만큼은 결코 거역당하거나 따져지는 일을 용납하지 않았다. 그리고 이 집에서 두 해를 묵어 온 이 젊은 신사는 그것을 너무도 잘 알았기에, 그 거절에 곧바로 수긍하고 말았다.\n\n나이팅게일 씨는 시시각각 존스를 더욱 아끼게 되어, 그날 선술집에서 함께 저녁을 들기를 몹시 바랐다. 거기서 그는 자기 지인 몇몇에게 존스를 소개하겠다고 했다. 그러나 존스는 \"아직 옷가지가 시내에 도착하지 않았다\"는 핑계로 양해를 구했다.\n\n사실대로 털어놓자면, 존스 씨는 이제 자기보다 훨씬 행색이 번듯한 젊은 신사들에게도 더러 닥치는 그런 처지에 놓여 있었다. 한마디로, 그의 주머니에는 단 한 푼도 없었다. 이런 처지는 롬바드 거리에 사는 현대의 현인들이나 화이트 초콜릿 하우스를 들락거리는 자들 사이에서보다, 옛 철학자들 사이에서 훨씬 더 높은 평판을 누렸다. 그리고 어쩌면, 저 철학자들이 빈 주머니에 돌린 그 큰 영예야말로, 앞서 말한 그 거리와 초콜릿 하우스에서 그들이 받는 그 깊은 멸시의 한 가지 까닭일는지도 모른다.\n\n자, 사람이 오직 덕만으로도 무척 편안히 살 수 있다는 옛 견해가, 방금 말한 현대의 현인들이 발견했노라 떠벌리듯, 명백한 오류라고 치자. 그렇다면 내가 보기에 그에 못지않게 거짓된 것이 바로, 사람이 오로지 사랑만으로 살 수 있다는 어떤 연애소설 작가들의 주장이다. 사랑이 우리의 어떤 감각이나 욕구에는 더없이 달콤한 만찬을 차려 줄지 몰라도, 다른 감각에는 아무것도 차려 주지 못한다는 것만큼은 가장 확실하기 때문이다. 그러니 그런 작가들을 지나치게 믿어 버린 이들은 너무 늦어서야 그 오류를 몸으로 겪었으며, 사랑이 허기를 달래 주는 데는, 장미가 귀를 즐겁게 하거나 바이올린이 코를 만족시키는 것만큼이나 무력하다는 것을 깨닫고 말았다.\n\n그리하여 사랑이 그의 앞에 차려 놓은 온갖 진미에도—다시 말해, 가면무도회에서 소피아를 만나리라는 희망, 그 상상이 아무리 근거가 빈약했을지언정 그날 하루 내내 그가 황홀히 누린 그 향연에도—저녁이 찾아오기 무섭게 존스 씨는 좀 더 거친 종류의 양식을 갈망하기 시작했다. 파트리지는 직감으로 이를 알아챘고, 이때를 틈타 은행권에 관해 몇 마디 에두른 암시를 던졌다. 그것이 경멸 속에 물리쳐지자, 그는 다시 한번 용기를 그러모아 올워디 씨에게로 돌아가는 일을 입에 올렸다.\n\n\"파트리지.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"내 처지를 자네가 나보다 더 절망적으로 볼 수는 없을 거야. 나도 그렇게 보고 있으니까. 그리고 자네가 자리 잡고 있던 곳을 떠나 나를 따라오도록 내버려 둔 것을, 나는 진심으로 뉘우치기 시작했네. 그러니 이제 자네에게 집으로 돌아가라고 강권하겠네. 그리고 자네가 나 때문에 그토록 고맙게도 들인 비용과 수고에 대해서는, 내가 자네에게 맡겨 두고 온 그 옷가지를 모두 자네 것으로 삼아 주게. 그 외에 달리 보답할 길이 없어 미안하네.\"\n\n그가 어찌나 애절한 어조로 이 말을 했던지, 그 악덕 가운데 심술이나 모진 마음만큼은 끼어 있지 않던 파트리지가 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다. 그는 어려움에 처한 그를 결코 떠나지 않겠다고 맹세한 뒤, 더없이 간절한 애원으로 집으로 돌아가시라고 채근하기 시작했다. \"제발 부탁입니다, 도련님.\" 그가 말했다. \"한번 생각해 보세요. 도련님이 대체 무엇을 하실 수 있겠습니까?—돈 한 푼 없이 이 도시에서 어떻게 사실 수 있단 말입니까? 뭘 하시든, 어디로 가시든, 저는 도련님을 버리지 않기로 마음먹었습니다. 하지만 제발, 도련님, 생각해 보세요.—제발, 도련님, 도련님 자신을 위해서, 부디 헤아려 보세요. 도련님의 그 분별력이라면 분명,\" 그가 말했다. \"집으로 돌아가라고 이르실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"내가 몇 번이나 말해야 알아듣겠나.\" 존스가 답했다. \"내게는 돌아갈 집이 없다고. 올워디 씨의 문이 나를 맞아 주려 열려 있으리란 희망이 조금이라도 있다면, 나를 떠밀 어떤 곤경도 필요치 않을 걸세.—아니, 이 세상에 그분 앞으로 날아가는 나를 한순간이라도 붙들어 둘 다른 이유란 없을 거야. 하지만, 아아! 나는 그곳에서 영원히 추방당한 몸일세. 그분의 마지막 말씀은—오, 파트리지, 그 말씀이 아직도 내 귓가에 울리네—그분의 마지막 말씀은, 내게 얼마간의 돈을 주시면서—그게 얼마였는지는 모르나 적지 않은 액수였던 건 분명하네—그 마지막 말씀은 이러했네. '나는 오늘 이후로 어떤 일이 있어도 다시는 너와 말을 섞지 않기로 굳게 마음먹었다.'\"\n\n여기서 격한 감정이 존스의 입을 막았고, 놀라움이 한순간 파트리지의 입도 막았다. 그러나 그는 곧 말문을 되찾았고, 자신은 본디 캐묻기 좋아하는 성미가 아니라고 짤막하게 운을 뗀 뒤, 존스가 말한 적지 않은 액수란 게 무슨 뜻이냐고—얼마인지는 모른다지만—그리고 그 돈은 어찌 되었느냐고 물었다.\n\n이 두 가지 점에 대해 그는 이제 충분한 답을 들었다. 그가 한참 거기에 토를 달려던 참에, 나이팅게일 씨가 보낸 전갈이 끼어들어 그를 막았다. 자기 방으로 주인을 모셨으면 한다는 청이었다.\n\n두 신사가 모두 가면무도회 차림을 갖추고, 나이팅게일 씨가 가마를 불러오라 일러 둔 뒤, 존스에게는 곤혹스러운 사정 하나가 떠올랐다. 내 독자들 가운데 많은 이에게는 이것이 무척 우스워 보일 것이다. 다름 아니라 어떻게 1실링을 마련하느냐 하는 것이었다. 그러나 그런 독자들이라도, 마음에 둔 어떤 계획을 이루려는데 1천 파운드가, 혹은 10파운드나 20파운드쯤이 없어 자신이 겪었던 심정을 잠시 떠올려 본다면, 이때 존스 씨가 느낀 바를 고스란히 헤아릴 수 있으리라. 그리하여 그는 이 돈을 파트리지에게 청했다. 그것은 그가 파트리지에게 내어놓도록 허락한 첫 번째 돈이었고, 또한 이 가엾은 사내가 자기를 위해 내어놓는 마지막 돈이 되게 하겠다고 마음먹은 것이었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 파트리지는 근래 들어 이런 식의 제안을 한 적이 없었다. 그것이 은행권을 헐어 쓰는 꼴을 보고 싶어서였는지, 아니면 곤궁함이 존스를 설득해 집으로 돌아가게 만들기를 바라서였는지, 그도 아니면 또 다른 어떤 동기에서 비롯된 것이었는지는 내가 단정하지 않겠다.\n\n가장무도회의 온갖 익살을 담은 장.\n\n우리의 기사들은 마침내 그 신전에 다다랐다. 위대한 쾌락의 중재자이자 환락의 대제사장인 하이데거가 주재하는 곳이었다. 그리고 그는 다른 이교의 사제들이 그러하듯, 실제로는 그곳에 어떤 신도 없건만 신이 임재한 척 꾸며 자신의 숭배자들을 속였다.\n\n나이팅게일 씨는 일행과 한두 바퀴를 돈 뒤 곧 그를 떠나 어떤 여인과 함께 걸어가 버리며 이렇게 말했다. \"이제 여기까지 왔으니, 자네는 알아서 자기 사냥감을 찾아 헤매 보게나.\"\n\n존스는 자신의 소피아가 그 자리에 와 있으리라는 강한 희망을 품기 시작했다. 그리고 이 희망은 불빛이나 음악이나 사람들보다 그에게 더 큰 활기를 불어넣었다. 물론 그런 것들도 우울을 다스리는 꽤 강력한 해독제이긴 했지만. 그는 이제 키나 몸매, 분위기가 자기 천사를 조금이라도 닮은 여인이 보일 때마다 말을 걸었다. 그런 여인들 모두에게 그는 어떻게든 재치 있는 말을 건네려 애썼는데, 대답을 끌어내어 결코 잘못 들을 리 없다고 여기는 그 목소리를 알아내려는 속셈이었다. 그중 몇몇은 새된 목소리로 되물었다. \"나 알아요?\" 훨씬 많은 수는 \"당신을 모르겠어요, 선생님\" 하고는 그뿐이었다. 어떤 이는 그를 무례한 작자라 불렀고, 어떤 이는 아예 대꾸도 하지 않았으며, 어떤 이는 \"정말이지 당신 목소리를 모르겠고, 당신과는 할 말이 없어요\" 하고 말했다. 또 많은 여인들이 그가 바랄 만큼 다정한 대답을 주었지만, 정작 그가 듣고 싶어 하는 그 목소리는 아니었다.\n\n그가 이 마지막 부류의 한 사람과(양치기 처녀 차림을 한 여인이었다) 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 도미노 차림의 한 부인이 그에게 다가와 어깨를 툭 치며 동시에 귓속말로 속삭였다. \"저 잡것과 좀 더 이야기를 나누신다면, 웨스턴 양에게 일러바치겠어요.\"\n\n존스는 그 이름을 듣자마자 곧장 앞서의 상대를 버려두고 도미노 차림의 부인에게 매달려, 만일 그분이 지금 이 방 안에 있다면 자신에게 보여 달라고 빌고 또 애원했다.\n\n가면을 쓴 여인은 말을 꺼내기 전에 가장 안쪽 방의 위편 끝으로 황급히 걸어갔다. 그러고는 그의 물음에 답하는 대신 자리에 앉아 피곤하다고 했다. 존스도 그 곁에 앉아 여전히 애원을 그치지 않았다. 마침내 부인이 차갑게 대답했다. \"나는 존스 씨가 좀 더 안목 있는 연인인 줄 알았어요. 변장 따위가 자기 연인을 가리도록 내버려 둘 분은 아닌 줄로요.\" \"그렇다면 그분이 여기 계십니까, 부인?\" 존스가 다소 격하게 응했다. 그러자 부인이 외쳤다. \"쉿, 선생님, 남들 눈에 띄겠어요. 내 명예를 걸고 약속하건대, 웨스턴 양은 여기 없어요.\"\n\n존스는 이제 가면 쓴 여인의 손을 잡고, 더없이 간곡한 태도로 소피아를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 알려 달라고 애원하기 시작했다. 그리고 어떤 직접적인 답도 얻을 수 없자, 어제 자기를 바람맞힌 일을 두고 부드럽게 책망하기 시작했다. 그러고는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"정말이지, 친애하는 요정의 여왕이시여, 목소리를 짐짓 꾸미셨지만 저는 폐하를 아주 잘 알아봅니다. 정말이지, 피츠패트릭 부인, 제 고통을 즐길 거리로 삼으시다니 좀 잔인하시군요.\"\n\n가면 쓴 여인이 대답했다. \"당신이 그렇게 영리하게 나를 알아보았다 해도, 나는 여전히 같은 목소리로 말해야 해요. 다른 사람들에게 들킬까 봐서요. 그리고 친애하는 선생님, 당신은 내가 사촌을 그토록 하찮게 여겨, 두 분의 정사를 거들기라도 하리라 생각하나요? 그 끝이 그 애의 파멸이면서 동시에 당신 자신의 파멸이 될 일인데요? 게다가 분명히 말하지만, 당신이 그토록 그 애의 적이 되어 부추긴다 한들, 내 사촌은 제 손으로 제 파멸에 동의할 만큼 미치지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"아아, 부인!\" 존스가 말했다. \"저를 소피아의 적이라 부르시다니, 부인은 제 마음을 너무도 모르십니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 누군가를 파멸시키는 일이란,\" 상대가 외쳤다. \"당신도 인정하겠지만 적이 하는 짓이에요. 그리고 같은 행동으로 당신 자신에게도 틀림없이, 그것도 알면서 파멸을 불러온다면, 그건 죄일 뿐 아니라 어리석음이나 광기 아닌가요? 자, 선생님, 내 사촌이 가진 것이라곤 그 애 아버지가 기꺼이 내주려는 것 말고는 거의 없어요. 그 애 신분의 여인치고는 너무도 적지요. 당신은 그분을 알고, 또 당신 자신의 처지도 알잖아요.\"\n\n존스는 소피아에게 그런 의도가 전혀 없노라 맹세했다. \"차라리 가장 끔찍한 죽음이라도 견딜지언정, 제 욕망을 위해 그분의 이익을 희생시키지는 않겠습니다.\" 그는 말했다. \"제가 어느 모로 보나 그분에게 어울리지 않는 사람임을 잘 알기에, 진작부터 그런 야심 어린 생각은 모두 버리기로 마음먹었습니다. 다만 어떤 이상한 사정들로 인해 그분을 단 한 번만 더 보고 싶어졌을 뿐이며, 그러고 나면 영영 작별하겠다고 약속드립니다. 아닙니다, 부인,\" 그가 끝맺었다. \"제 사랑은 사랑하는 대상에게 가장 소중한 것을 희생시키면서 제 만족을 구하는 그런 비천한 종류가 아닙니다. 저는 제 소피아를 얻기 위해서라면 모든 것을 희생할 수 있습니다. 다만 소피아 자신만은 예외입니다.\"\n\n독자는 이미 가면 쓴 부인의 덕성에 대해 그다지 고상한 관념을 품지 못했을 것이며, 어쩌면 그녀가 나중에 자기 성별 가운데 으뜸가는 인물에 걸맞지 않은 사람으로 드러날 수도 있다. 그럼에도 이 너그러운 마음씨가 그녀에게 강한 인상을 남겼고, 그녀가 우리의 젊은 주인공에게 전부터 품어 온 애정을 한층 더 깊게 했음은 분명하다.\n\n이제 부인은 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말했다. \"소피아를 향한 당신의 마음을 주제넘은 짓이라기보다는 분별없는 짓으로 본다\"고 했다. \"젊은 사내들은,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아무리 야심 찬 생각을 품어도 지나칠 게 없어요. 나는 젊은 남자의 야심을 좋아하고, 당신도 그것을 될 수 있는 한 키워 나가길 바라요. 어쩌면 당신은 재산이 한없이 더 나은 여인들과도 잘될지 몰라요. 아니, 나는 그런 여자들이 분명 있다고 확신해요... 하지만 존스 씨, 이렇게 거의 알지도 못하는 남자에게, 게다가 나를 대하는 태도로 보아 도무지 흡족할 까닭이 없는 남자에게 이런 충고를 늘어놓고 있으니, 나를 참 이상한 사람이라 여기지 않으세요?\"\n\n여기서 존스는 사과를 시작하며, 그녀의 사촌에 대해 자신이 한 말 가운데 무엇으로도 그녀의 기분을 상하게 하지 않았기를 바란다고 했다. 그러자 가면 쓴 여인이 대답했다. \"당신은 여인이라는 성별에 그토록 어두워서, 다른 여자를 향한 정열로 한 부인을 즐겁게 하는 것보다 더 큰 모욕을 안길 수 있다고 생각하나요? 만약 요정의 여왕이 당신의 정중함을 더 낫게 보지 않았다면, 가장무도회에서 자기를 만나자고 당신을 부르지도 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n존스는 지금만큼 정사에 마음이 없던 적이 없었다. 그러나 부인들에 대한 정중함은 그의 명예 원칙 가운데 하나였고, 그는 결투 신청을 받아들이는 것만큼이나 사랑의 도전을 받아들이는 것 또한 자기 의무라 여겼다. 아니, 소피아를 향한 바로 그 사랑 때문에라도 이 부인과 잘 지낼 필요가 있었으니, 그녀가 자신을 그 다른 여인의 앞으로 데려다줄 수 있으리라 조금도 의심하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 그가 그녀의 마지막 말에 아주 뜨거운 답을 막 건네려는 참에, 노파로 분장한 가면 하나가 두 사람에게 끼어들었다. 이 가면은 가장무도회에 오로지 심술을 풀려고 오는 부류의 여인들 가운데 하나였다. 사람들에게 무례한 진실을 들이대고, 흔히들 말하듯 가능한 한 흥을 깨려 애쓰는 부류였다. 그래서 이 갸륵한 부인은 존스와, 그녀가 잘 아는 그의 친구가 방 구석에서 은밀히 이야기를 나누는 것을 보고는, 그들을 방해하는 것보다 자기 심술을 풀기에 더 좋은 데가 없으리라 결론지었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그들을 공격해 곧 그 은신처에서 몰아냈다. 그것으로도 만족하지 못하고, 그들이 그녀를 피해 옮겨 가는 곳마다 쫓아다녔다. 마침내 나이팅게일 씨가 친구의 곤경을 보고 그를 구해 주었으니, 그 노파를 다른 추격거리에 붙들어 매어 놓았던 것이다.\n\n존스와 그의 가면 쓴 부인이 그 성가신 자를 떨쳐 내려고 함께 방을 거니는 동안, 그는 자기 부인이 여러 가면들에게 마치 맨얼굴로 만난 사이인 양 거리낌 없이 말을 거는 것을 보았다. 그는 이에 놀라움을 표하지 않을 수 없었다. \"정말이지, 부인, 온갖 변장 속에서도 사람을 알아보시다니, 한없는 안목을 지니셨나 봅니다.\" 그러자 부인이 대답했다. \"상류층 사람들에게는 가장무도회만큼 시시하고 유치한 것이 없답니다. 그들은 대개 회합이나 응접실에서 만날 때만큼이나 여기서도 서로를 잘 알아보거든요. 또 어떤 지체 있는 여인도 모르는 사람과는 대화하지 않아요. 한마디로, 여기 보이는 사람들 대부분은 다른 어느 곳에서보다 바로 이곳에서 시간을 죽인다고 하는 편이 더 옳을 거예요. 그리고 대개는 더없이 긴 설교를 듣고 나올 때보다 더 지친 채 여기서 물러간답니다. 사실대로 말하자면, 나 자신도 그런 처지가 되어 가기 시작했어요. 그리고 내게 눈치챌 재주가 조금이라도 있다면, 당신도 별로 더 즐거워 보이지 않는군요. 솔직히 당신을 위해서 집에 가 주는 게 거의 자선이 될 거예요.\" \"저는 그에 맞먹는 자선을 딱 하나밖에 모릅니다.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그건 제가 부인을 댁까지 모셔다 드리도록 허락해 주시는 겁니다.\" \"정말이지,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"당신은 나에 대해 참 이상한 생각을 품고 있군요. 이 정도 면식으로 이 야밤에 당신을 내 집 문 안으로 들여보내 주리라 상상하다니. 짐작건대 당신은 내가 사촌에게 보인 우정을 무슨 다른 속셈으로 돌리는 모양이군요. 솔직히 고백해 봐요. 당신은 이렇게 꾸며진 만남을 노골적인 밀회나 다름없는 것으로 여기는 거 아닌가요? 존스 씨, 당신은 이렇게 느닷없는 정복을 일삼는 사람인가요?\" \"저는, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이런 느닷없는 정복에 굴복하는 데에는 익숙하지 않습니다. 그러나 부인께서 제 마음을 불시에 사로잡으셨으니, 제 몸의 나머지도 따라갈 권리가 있지요. 그러니 부인이 어디로 가시든 따르기로 마음먹은 저를 용서하셔야 합니다.\" 그는 이 말에 적절한 행동을 곁들였다. 그러자 부인은 가볍게 나무라고 두 사람의 친밀함이 남들 눈에 띌 거라고 말하고는 그에게 일렀다. \"지인과 함께 야식을 들러 가는 길인데, 거기까지는 따라오지 않기를 바라요. 만약 따라온다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 영문 모를 여자로 여겨질 거예요. 하긴 내 친구가 그리 까다롭게 흠잡는 사람은 아니지만요. 그래도 따라오지 않기를 바라요. 정말이지 당신이 따라오면 나는 뭐라 말해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n부인은 곧이어 가장무도회장을 떠났고, 존스는 그토록 엄한 금지령을 받았음에도 감히 그녀를 따라나섰다. 그는 이제 앞서 말한 것과 똑같은 곤경에 빠져 있었다. 다름 아니라 일 실링이 없다는 것이었는데, 이번에는 전처럼 빌려서 해결할 수도 없었다. 그래서 그는 자기 부인이 탄 가마를 뒤따라 당당히 걸어갔고, 그 자리에 있던 모든 가마꾼들의 요란한 야유가 그를 쫓았다. 그들은 윗사람들이 두 발로 걷는 일이라면 무엇이든 못마땅하게 만들려고 영리하게도 갖은 애를 쓰는 자들이었다. 그러나 다행히도 오페라 극장에 대기하던 가마꾼들은 너무 바빠 제자리를 뜨지 못했고, 시간이 늦은 덕에 거리에서 그 동료들과 많이 마주치지도 않았다. 그리하여 그는 아무런 시달림 없이 나아갔으니, 다른 철이었다면 그 차림새가 틀림없이 그의 뒤꽁무니에 군중을 불러 모았을 터였다.\n\n부인은 하노버 광장에서 멀지 않은 어느 거리에서 내렸고, 그곳에서 문이 곧 열리자 안으로 옮겨졌다. 그리고 신사는 아무런 격식도 차리지 않고 그녀를 따라 안으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n이제 존스와 그의 동행은 가구가 아주 잘 갖춰지고 따뜻하게 데워진 방에 함께 있었다. 그때 여인이 여전히 가장무도회 때의 목소리로, 자기 친구에게 놀랐다며, 그가 약속을 까맣게 잊은 게 틀림없다고 말했다. 그러고는 한참 분을 터뜨린 끝에, 갑자기 존스를 두고 어떤 걱정을 내비치며, 이런 야밤에 한 집에 단둘이 있었다는 것을 세상이 어찌 여기겠느냐고 물었다. 그러나 그토록 중대한 물음에 곧장 답하는 대신, 존스는 부인에게 가면을 벗으라고 몹시 끈질기게 조르기 시작했다. 마침내 뜻을 이루고 보니, 거기 나타난 것은 피츠패트릭 부인이 아니라 다름 아닌 벨라스턴 부인 자신이었다.\n\n새벽 두 시부터 여섯 시까지 이어진 그 구체적인 대화를 일일이 옮기는 것은 따분할 것이다. 그것은 아주 흔하고 평범한 일들로 이루어져 있었으니 말이다. 이 이야기에 어떻게든 의미가 있는 부분만 들어 두는 것으로 충분하다. 그것은 부인이 소피아를 찾아내도록 애써 보겠으며, 며칠 안에 그를 그녀와 만나게 해 주겠다는 약속이었다. 단, 그때 그가 그녀와 작별한다는 조건이었다. 이것이 완전히 매듭지어지고, 저녁에 같은 장소에서 두 번째 만남이 정해지자, 둘은 헤어졌다. 부인은 자기 집으로 돌아갔고, 존스는 자기 숙소로 돌아갔다.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n존스는 몇 시간 눈을 붙여 몸을 추스른 뒤 파트리지를 불러들였다. 그러고는 50파운드짜리 은행권 한 장을 건네며 그것을 가서 잔돈으로 바꿔 오라고 일렀다. 파트리지는 두 눈을 반짝이며 그것을 받아 들었지만, 막상 더 곰곰이 생각해 보니 주인의 명예에 그리 이롭지 않은 의심이 슬그머니 고개를 들었다. 가면무도회에 대해 품고 있던 무시무시한 상상, 주인이 변장을 하고 나갔다가 변장한 채로 돌아온 일, 그리고 밤새 바깥에 나가 있었던 일이 그 의심을 부채질했다. 쉽게 말해, 이 은행권을 손에 넣은 경위를 그가 도무지 달리 설명할 길이 없었던 까닭은 강도질밖에 떠오르지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 솔직히 말하자면, 독자 역시 이것이 벨라스턴 부인의 너그러움 덕분이었으리라 짐작하지 않는 한, 다른 어떤 까닭도 떠올리기 어려울 것이다.\n\n그러니 존스의 명예를 깨끗이 해 두고, 또 그 부인의 후한 인심에 정당한 몫을 돌려주기 위해 밝혀 두자면, 그는 정말로 이 선물을 그녀에게서 받은 것이었다. 그녀는 병원 짓기 따위 그 시절의 흔해 빠진 자선에는 그다지 마음을 쓰지 않았으나, 그렇다고 그러한 기독교적 미덕이 아주 없는 사람은 아니었다. 그리고 그녀는 (내 생각에 아주 옳게도) 한 푼도 가진 것 없는 됨됨이 훌륭한 젊은이야말로 이 미덕을 베풀기에 부적절한 대상이 아니라고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n이날 존스와 나이팅게일은 밀러 부인의 집에서 함께 점심을 들기로 초대받은 터였다. 그리하여 약속한 시각에 두 젊은 신사는 두 아가씨와 더불어 거실에 모였고, 그곳에서 세 시부터 거의 다섯 시가 될 때까지 마음씨 고운 그 부인이 나타나기를 기다렸다. 그녀는 어느 친척을 보러 시외에 다녀온 길이었는데, 돌아와서는 다음과 같은 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n\"여러분, 이렇게 기다리시게 해서 정말 죄송해요. 사정을 아신다면 분명히 — 한 6마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 사는 사촌을 보러 다녀왔답니다. 지금 산후 조리 중이거든요. 이건 모든 사람에게 (그녀는 딸들을 바라보며 말했다) 경솔하게 결혼하는 일이 어떤 것인지 경고가 되어야 할 일이에요. 이 세상에 살림의 여유 없이는 행복이란 없어요. 오, 낸시! 네 가엾은 사촌이 처한 그 비참한 형편을 내가 어떻게 다 말로 그릴 수 있을까? 산후 일주일도 채 안 됐는데, 이 끔찍한 날씨에 침대에 휘장 하나 없는 차가운 방에 누워 있고, 불을 지필 석탄 한 가마니조차 집에 없더구나. 둘째 아들, 그 사랑스러운 어린 녀석은 후두염으로 어미와 같은 침대에 앓아 누워 있단다. 집에 다른 침대라곤 없으니까. 가엾은 어린 토미! 낸시야, 아무래도 네가 그토록 아끼던 그 아이를 다시는 못 볼 것 같구나. 정말이지 몹시 위중하거든. 나머지 아이들은 꽤 건강한 편이다만, 몰리는 제 몸을 상하게 할까 봐 걱정이야. 나이팅게일 씨, 그 애가 이제 겨우 열세 살인데, 내 평생 그렇게 잘 돌보는 간병인은 본 적이 없어요. 제 어미와 동생을 둘 다 보살피거든요. 게다가 그렇게 어린아이로서는 놀랍게도, 어미 앞에서는 세상 그 누구보다 밝은 얼굴을 보인답니다. 그런데 내가 봤어요 — 나이팅게일 씨, 그 가엾은 아이가 고개를 돌려 몰래 제 눈에서 눈물을 훔치는 걸 봤지 뭐예요.\" 여기서 밀러 부인은 북받치는 눈물에 말을 잇지 못했고, 그 자리에 있던 사람치고 함께 눈물짓지 않은 이는 아마 한 사람도 없었으리라. 이윽고 그녀는 조금 진정을 되찾고 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"이 모든 고생 속에서도 그 어미는 놀라우리만치 꿋꿋하게 마음을 다잡고 있어요. 아들이 위태로운 것이 제일 무겁게 그 가슴을 짓누르는데도, 남편을 생각해서 그 걱정마저 되도록 감추려 애쓴답니다. 그래도 슬픔이 이따금 그 모든 애씀을 이기고 마는 건, 그녀가 늘 이 아이를 끔찍이도 사랑했기 때문이에요. 정말이지 영민하고 마음씨 고운 아이거든요. 단언컨대 내 평생 그토록 가슴이 미어진 적이 없어요. 이제 겨우 일곱 살밖에 안 된 그 가엾은 어린것이, 제 어미가 눈물로 제 몸을 적시는 동안 어미더러 마음을 추스르라고 달래는 소리를 들었을 때 말이에요. '정말이에요, 엄마,' 그 아이가 외치더군요. '저는 안 죽어요. 전능하신 하느님께서 분명 토미를 데려가지 않으실 거예요. 천국이 아무리 좋은 곳이라도, 저는 거기 가느니 차라리 여기서 엄마랑 아빠랑 함께 굶는 편이 좋아요.' 여러분, 용서하세요. 어쩔 수가 없네요\" (그녀는 눈물을 닦으며 말했다). \"어린아이가 그토록 여린 마음과 정을 지녔으니. — 그래도 어쩌면 그 아이는 가장 덜 가엾은 처지일지도 몰라요. 하루이틀이면 십중팔구 인간 세상의 온갖 불행이 미치지 못하는 곳에 가 있을 테니까요. 정작 가장 가엾은 건 그 아비예요. 가엾은 사람, 그 얼굴이야말로 공포 그 자체의 그림 같아서, 산 사람이라기보다 죽은 사람처럼 보인답니다. 오, 맙소사! 내가 그 방에 처음 들어섰을 때 어떤 광경을 보았는지! 그 착한 사람이 베개받침 뒤에 누워서 제 아이와 아내를 한꺼번에 받쳐 안고 있더군요. 몸에는 얇은 조끼 하나밖에 걸치지 않았어요. 외투는 담요 대신 침대 위에 펼쳐 놓았거든요. — 내가 들어서자 그가 몸을 일으켰는데, 나는 그를 알아보기도 힘들었어요. 존스 씨, 보름 전만 해도 당신이 보신 어떤 이 못지않게 잘생긴 사람이었답니다. 나이팅게일 씨는 그를 본 적이 있지요. 두 눈은 움푹 꺼지고, 얼굴은 창백하고, 수염은 길게 자라 있었어요. 몸은 추위에 떨고, 게다가 굶주림에 야위어 있었지요. 사촌 말로는 그에게 도무지 무얼 먹게 할 수가 없다더군요. — 그가 직접 내게 속삭이듯 말했어요 — 그가 내게 말하기를 — 차마 옮길 수가 없네요 — 제 아이들에게 부족한 빵을 자기가 먹는 일을 견딜 수가 없다고 하더군요. 그런데 여러분, 믿어지세요? 이 모든 비참함 속에서도 그 아내만큼은 마치 더없이 풍족한 가운데 산후 조리를 하는 것처럼 좋은 caudle을 먹고 있더라니까요. 내가 맛을 봤는데, 그보다 나은 것을 맛본 적이 거의 없을 정도였어요. — 그가 말하길, 아내에게 이걸 마련해 줄 방도가 하늘에서 보낸 천사로부터 자기에게 온 것 같다고 하더군요. 무슨 뜻인지는 모르겠어요. 한 마디라도 물어볼 만한 기운이 내겐 없었으니까요.\n\n* caudle: 산모나 환자에게 먹이던, 포도주나 맥주에 달걀·곡물·향신료를 섞어 데운 따뜻한 음료.\n\"그 둘은 흔히들 말하는 연애결혼을 했어요. 양쪽 다 말이죠. 그러니까 거지 둘이 맺어진 셈이지요. 정말이지 그렇게 정다운 부부는 본 적이 없다고 말해야겠어요. 그런데 그 정다움이라는 게 서로를 괴롭히는 것 말고 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?\" \"정말이에요, 엄마,\" 낸시가 외쳤다. \"저는 늘 앤더슨 사촌 언니를\" (그것이 그녀의 이름이었다) \"세상에서 가장 행복한 여자 중 하나라고 여겼는데.\" \"분명한 건,\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"지금 형편은 그것과는 영 딴판이라는 거다. 서로의 고통을 애틋하게 헤아리는 마음이야말로 그 재난 가운데서도 남편에게나 아내에게나 가장 견디기 힘든 부분이라는 걸 누구라도 알아챘을 거야. 그에 비하면 굶주림과 추위는 제 한 몸에만 닥치는 것이니 고생 축에도 들지 못하지. 아니, 두 살도 안 된 막내만 빼면, 아이들조차 똑같이 그 마음을 느낀단다. 그 집은 더없이 정 많은 가족이라서, 살림에 최소한의 여유만 있었어도 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람들이었을 거야.\" \"저는 그 집에서 비참함의 기색이라곤 조금도 본 적이 없어요.\" 낸시가 대꾸했다. \"지금 엄마가 들려주신 이야기에 정말 가슴이 미어져요.\" — \"오, 얘야,\" 어미가 답했다. \"그 애는 늘 무슨 일이든 좋게 꾸려 보려고 애썼단다. 그들은 늘 큰 곤궁에 시달려 왔지. 하지만 정말이지, 이번의 이 완전한 파멸만큼은 남들이 그들에게 끼친 거란다. 그 가엾은 사람이 제 형제라는 악당의 보증을 섰거든. 그러다 한 일주일쯤 전, 바로 그 애가 몸을 풀기 전날, 그 집 살림살이가 모조리 차압당해 강제 집행으로 팔려 나갔지 뭐냐. 그가 집행관 한 사람 편에 그 일을 알리는 편지를 내게 보냈다는데, 그 악당이 그걸 끝내 전하지 않았던 거야. — 자기 소식을 듣기까지 내가 일주일이나 흘려보냈으니, 그가 나를 어떻게 생각했겠니?\"\n\n존스는 마른 눈으로 이 이야기를 들은 것이 아니었다. 이야기가 끝나자 그는 밀러 부인을 다른 방으로 따로 데려가, 50파운드가 든 자기 지갑을 건네며 그중 적당하다고 여겨지는 만큼을 그 가엾은 사람들에게 보내 달라고 청했다. 이때 밀러 부인이 존스에게 보낸 그 눈빛은 말로 그려 내기 쉽지 않다. 그녀는 일종의 벅찬 환희의 발작에 휩싸여 이렇게 외쳤다. \"맙소사! 세상에 이런 사람이 다 있다니!\" — 그러나 곧 정신을 추스르고 그녀는 말했다. \"사실 그런 분을 한 분 알고는 있어요. 하지만 또 한 분이 있을 수 있을까요?\" \"부인,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"흔한 인정을 지닌 사람이라면 많으리라 믿습니다. 동포가 겪는 그런 고통을 덜어 주는 것을 그 이상이라 부르기는 어려우니까요.\" 그러자 밀러 부인은 10기니를 받았는데, 그것이 그가 그녀에게 받게 할 수 있었던 최대한이었다. 그러고는 \"내일 아침 일찍 그것을 전할 방도를 찾아보겠노라\"고 말하며, \"자기도 그 가엾은 사람들을 위해 얼마간 손을 써 두어서, 처음 본 만큼 그렇게까지 비참한 처지에 그들을 내버려 두지는 않았다\"고 덧붙였다.\n\n그런 뒤 그들은 거실로 돌아왔고, 그곳에서 나이팅게일은 이 가엾은 이들의 끔찍한 형편에 큰 근심을 드러냈다. 실은 그도 그들을 알고 있었으니, 밀러 부인의 집에서 한두 번 이상 그들을 본 적이 있었던 것이다. 그는 남의 빚에 제 몸을 걸어 책임지는 어리석음을 강하게 성토했고, 그 형제를 향해 신랄한 저주를 숱하게 퍼부었으며, 그 불행한 가족을 위해 무언가 할 수 있으면 좋겠다는 바람으로 말을 맺었다. \"부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"그들을 올워디 씨께 천거해 보시면 어떨까요? 아니면 모금을 하는 건 어떻게 생각하세요? 저는 기꺼이 1기니를 내놓겠습니다.\"\n\n밀러 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그리고 어미가 존스의 너그러움을 귀엣말로 일러 준 낸시는 그 말에 얼굴이 창백해졌다. 하기야 두 사람 중 누구든 나이팅게일에게 화가 났다면, 그것은 분명 까닭 없는 노여움이었으리라. 설령 그가 존스의 후한 인심을 알았다 한들, 그것은 그가 따를 의무가 있는 본보기는 아니었으니 말이다. 게다가 반 푼도 내놓지 않을 사람이 수천 명은 될 것이며, 실은 그도 사실상 그러했다. 그는 무엇 하나 내밀지 않았고, 따라서 다른 이들이 굳이 청하지 않기로 한 이상, 그는 제 돈을 주머니에 그대로 간직했던 것이다.\n\n사실 나는 한 가지를 줄곧 눈여겨봐 왔는데, 지금보다 그 생각을 전하기에 더 좋은 기회는 다시없을 듯하다. 그것은 자선을 두고 세상이 대체로 정반대되는 두 가지 견해로 나뉜다는 점이다. 한쪽은 이런 종류의 행위는 모두 자발적인 선물로 여겨야 하며, 아무리 적게 준다 해도 (설령 그저 좋은 마음 하나뿐일지라도) 그렇게 함으로써 큰 공덕을 쌓는 것이라고 여기는 듯하다. 반대로 다른 쪽은, 선행이란 마땅히 해야 할 의무이며, 부유한 자가 가난한 이의 고통을 덜어 줄 자기 능력에 한참 못 미치게 베풀 때 그 알량한 베풂은 공덕은커녕 제 의무를 절반밖에 행하지 못한 것이고, 어떤 의미에서는 그 일을 아예 저버린 자들보다 더 경멸받을 만하다고 굳게 믿는 듯하다.\n\n이 서로 다른 견해를 화해시키는 일은 내 힘으로 될 바가 아니다. 다만 한 가지만 덧붙이자면, 베푸는 자들은 대체로 앞쪽 생각을 지니고, 받는 자들은 거의 예외 없이 뒤쪽으로 기운다는 것이다.\n\n# Chapter ix.\n\n앞 장에서 다룬 것과는 전혀 다른 종류의 문제들을 다루는 장.\n\n저녁에 존스는 다시 그 귀부인을 만났고, 두 사람 사이에는 또 한 차례 긴 대화가 이어졌다. 그러나 그 대화라는 것이 앞서와 똑같이 평범한 이야기들로만 이루어져 있었으므로, 우리는 그 세세한 내용을 언급하지 않으려 한다. 독자에게 즐거움을 줄 가망이 없다고 보기 때문이다. 다만 그 독자가, 가톨릭교도들이 성인들을 향해 그러하듯, 여성에 대한 헌신을 그림의 도움으로 북돋워야 하는 부류라면 모를까. 그러나 나는 그런 그림을 대중 앞에 내보이기를 바라기는커녕, 최근 어떤 프랑스 소설들에 펼쳐진 그런 장면들 위에 차라리 장막을 드리우고 싶다. 그것들의 솜씨 서툰 모작이 번역이라는 이름으로 이곳 우리에게 소개되어 왔으니 말이다.\n\n존스는 소피아를 보고 싶은 마음에 점점 더 안달이 났다. 그러나 벨라스턴 부인과 거듭 만남을 가져 보아도 그녀를 통해 그 뜻을 이룰 가망이 보이지 않자(오히려 부인은 소피아의 이름을 입에 올리는 것조차 노여움으로 대하기 시작했다), 그는 다른 방법을 시도해 보기로 마음먹었다. 그는 벨라스턴 부인이 자신의 천사가 어디 있는지 알고 있으리라고 의심치 않았고, 따라서 그녀의 하인들 가운데 누군가도 같은 비밀을 알고 있을 가능성이 가장 높다고 생각했다. 그리하여 파트리지가 그 하인들과 가까워져 그들에게서 이 비밀을 캐내는 임무를 맡게 되었다.\n\n그의 가엾은 주인이 지금 처한 처지보다 더 불편한 상황은 좀처럼 상상하기 어려울 것이다. 소피아를 찾아내는 데서 마주친 어려움 외에도, 자신이 그녀의 비위를 거슬렀을지 모른다는 두려움, 그리고 소피아가 그를 거부하기로 결심했으며 일부러 그에게서 몸을 숨겼다고 벨라스턴 부인에게서 들은 단언이 있었으니 말이다. 게다가 그 말이 사실일지도 모른다고 믿을 만한 충분한 이유가 그에게는 있었다. 그뿐만 아니라 그에게는 여전히 맞서 싸워야 할 또 하나의 난관이 있었는데, 그것은 그의 연인이 아무리 다정한 마음을 품었다 한들 그녀의 힘으로는 도저히 없앨 수 없는 것이었다. 바로 그녀를 아버지의 전 재산에서 상속받지 못하게 만드는 일이었다. 두 사람이 동의 없이 맺어지면 거의 피할 수 없는 결과가 그것이었고, 그 동의를 얻을 가망이라곤 그에게 전혀 없었다.\n\n여기에다, 이제 더는 감출 수 없게 된 그 격렬한 애정의 주인공 벨라스턴 부인이 그에게 베풀어 쌓아 올린 수많은 은혜까지 더해 보라. 그녀의 도움으로 그는 이제 런던에서 가장 옷을 잘 입는 남자 가운데 하나가 되었고, 앞서 언급한 그 우스꽝스러운 곤궁에서 벗어났을 뿐 아니라, 실로 일찍이 경험해 본 적 없는 풍족한 처지로까지 올라섰다.\n\n이제, 한 여인의 전 재산을 차지하고도 그녀에게 아무런 보답도 하지 않은 채 그것을 양심과 곧잘 화해시키는 신사들이 많기는 하다. 그러나 교수형에 처해 마땅하지 않을 정도의 양심을 지닌 사람에게는, 사랑을 오직 감사만으로 떠받치는 것보다 더 괴로운 일은 없으리라고 나는 믿는다. 더구나 마음이 정반대 쪽으로 끌릴 때는 말이다. 존스의 불행한 처지가 바로 그러했다. 그가 소피아에게 품은 그 정결한 사랑, 다른 어떤 여인을 향한 애정도 거의 남겨 두지 않은 그 사랑을 아예 제쳐 놓더라도, 그는 이 귀부인의 너그러운 정열에 결코 합당한 보답을 해 줄 수 없었을 것이다. 그녀는 한때 욕망의 대상이었던 것이 사실이나, 이제는 적어도 인생의 가을로 접어든 사람이었다. 비록 옷차림에서나 몸가짐에서나 여전히 젊음의 발랄함을 모두 걸치고 있었고, 심지어 두 뺨의 장밋빛까지 용케 유지하고 있었지만 말이다. 그러나 그 장밋빛은, 기술로 철 이르게 피워 낸 꽃처럼, 자연이 제때에 자기 작품을 단장할 때 입혀 주는 그 생기 있고 만발한 싱그러움을 조금도 지니지 못했다. 게다가 그녀에게는 어떤 결함이 하나 있었는데, 그것은 어떤 꽃들이 눈으로 보기에는 무척 아름다울지언정 향기로운 꽃밭 한가운데 두기에는 몹시 부적절하게, 그리고 무엇보다 사랑의 숨결에 가장 거슬리게 만드는 그런 종류의 것이었다.\n\n존스는 한편으로 이 모든 의기를 꺾는 사정들을 보면서도, 다른 한편으로는 자신이 진 은혜를 그에 못지않게 절절히 느꼈다. 또한 그 은혜가 흘러나온 그 뜨거운 정열을 그는 똑똑히 알아보았으니, 그 극도의 격렬함에 자신이 부응하지 못한다면 부인이 그를 배은망덕한 자로 여기리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 더 나쁜 것은, 그 자신도 스스로를 그렇게 여기게 되리라는 점이었다. 그는 그녀의 온갖 호의가 어떤 무언의 대가를 전제로 베풀어진 것인지 알고 있었다. 그리고 자신의 궁핍이 그것들을 받아들이도록 그를 몰아붙였듯이, 자신의 명예가 그 대가를 치르도록 자신을 강요한다고 결론지었다. 그리하여 그는 그 일을 하기로 마음먹었다. 그것이 자신에게 어떤 비참함을 안기든, 어떤 나라의 법이 달리 빚을 갚을 길이 없는 채무자로 하여금 채권자의 노예가 되도록 강제하는 그 위대한 정의의 원리에 따라, 그녀에게 자신을 바치기로 한 것이다.\n\n이런 문제들을 곰곰이 생각하고 있을 때, 그는 부인에게서 다음과 같은 쪽지를 받았다.\n\n\"우리가 마지막으로 만난 이후로 아주 어리석으면서도 아주 고약한 일이 하나 생겼는데, 그 때문에 늘 만나던 그곳에서 더는 당신을 보는 것이 마땅치 않게 되었습니다. 가능하다면 내일까지 다른 장소를 마련해 보겠어요. 그동안, 안녕히.\"\n\n이 실망이 그리 크지 않았으리라고 독자는 어쩌면 짐작할지도 모른다. 그러나 설령 컸다 한들 그는 곧 위안을 얻었다. 한 시간도 채 지나지 않아 같은 손에서 온 또 다른 쪽지가 그에게 전해졌는데, 그 내용은 이러했다.\n\n\"편지를 쓰고 나서 마음이 바뀌었습니다. 만일 당신이 모든 정열 가운데 가장 애틋한 그것을 모르는 사람이 아니라면, 이런 변덕에 놀라지 않으시겠지요. 이제 나는 무슨 일이 생기든 오늘 저녁 내 집에서 당신을 만나기로 결심했습니다. 정확히 일곱 시에 와 주세요. 나는 밖에서 저녁을 먹지만 그 시각까지는 집에 와 있을 거예요. 진심으로 사랑하는 이들에게는, 알고 보니 하루가 내 생각보다 더 길게 느껴지는군요.\n\n혹시라도 나보다 몇 분 일찍 도착하거든, 응접실로 안내해 달라고 이르세요.\"\n\n사실대로 고백하자면, 존스는 이 마지막 편지가 앞선 편지보다 덜 반가웠다. 이 편지 때문에 그는 나이팅게일 군의 간곡한 청을 들어줄 수 없게 되었기 때문이다. 그는 이제 나이팅게일과 꽤 깊은 친분과 우정을 맺은 사이였다. 그 청이란, 그 젊은 신사와 그 일행과 함께 그날 저녁 상연될 어느 새 연극을 보러 가자는 것이었다. 많은 무리가 그 연극을 야유로 망쳐 버리기로 합의해 두었는데, 작가에게 어떤 반감을 품었기 때문이었다. 그 작가는 나이팅게일 군의 지인 가운데 한 사람과 친구 사이였다. 그리고 이런 종류의 장난을, 부끄럽지만 고백하건대 우리의 주인공은 위에 말한 그 다정한 약속보다 기꺼이 더 좋아했을 것이다. 그러나 그의 명예가 그 마음을 눌러 이겼다.\n\n우리가 그를 따라 이 예정된 부인과의 만남으로 가기 전에, 앞의 두 쪽지에 대해 모두 설명을 해 두는 것이 마땅하리라 본다. 독자가 어쩌면 벨라스턴 부인의 경솔함에, 즉 자기 연인을 하필 자기 연적이 묵고 있는 바로 그 집으로 불러들인 일에 적잖이 놀랄지도 모르기 때문이다.\n\n먼저, 이 연인들이 지금까지 만나 왔던 그 집의 안주인은 여러 해 동안 그 부인에게서 연금을 받아 온 사람이었는데, 이제 감리교도가 되어 바로 그날 아침 부인을 찾아와 지난날의 삶을 몹시 호되게 꾸짖고는, 앞으로는 어떤 일이 있어도 부인의 어떤 정사든 거드는 도구가 되지 않겠다고 단호히 선언해 버린 것이다.\n\n이 사건이 부인을 몰아넣은 마음의 동요는, 그날 저녁 존스를 만날 다른 편의 장소를 도저히 찾을 수 없으리라는 절망에 빠지게 했다. 그러나 그 실망에서 비롯된 불안에서 조금씩 회복되기 시작하면서 그녀는 머리를 굴리기 시작했는데, 다행히도 소피아에게 연극을 보러 가자고 제안하자는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그 제안은 곧바로 받아들여졌고, 그녀의 동행으로 적당한 부인이 한 사람 마련되었다. 어너 부인 또한 같은 유흥의 심부름을 위해 에토프 부인과 함께 보내졌다. 이렇게 하여 그녀 자신의 집은 존스 군을 안전하게 맞아들이기에 마침맞게 비워졌고, 그녀는 저녁 식사를 한 곳에서 돌아온 뒤 그와 두세 시간 방해받지 않는 대화를 나누기를 기약했다. 저녁을 먹은 곳은 런던에서 꽤 멀리 떨어진 동네에 있는 한 친구의 집이었는데, 그곳은 예전의 밀회 장소 근처였다. 그녀는 옛 심복의 마음과 도덕에 일어난 그 변혁을 채 제대로 알기도 전에 그 약속을 잡아 두었던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n존스는 마침 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아뵙기 위해 옷을 다 차려입은 참이었는데, 그때 밀러 부인이 그의 방문을 두드렸다. 안으로 들어선 그녀는 아래층 응접실에서 함께 차를 마시자며 그의 동석을 간곡히 청했다.\n\n그가 방에 들어서자 그녀는 곧바로 한 사람을 그에게 소개하며 말했다. \"이 사람은 제 사촌이랍니다, 선생님. 선생님의 너그러우심에 큰 신세를 진 사람이라, 진심 어린 감사를 드리고 싶어 한답니다.\"\n\n밀러 부인이 그토록 다정하게 서두를 떼어 준 그 인사말을 그 사내가 채 시작하기도 전에, 존스와 그는 서로를 뚫어지게 바라보다가 동시에 더없는 놀라움의 기색을 드러냈다. 사내의 목소리는 당장에 떨리기 시작했고, 인사말을 끝맺기는커녕 의자에 털썩 주저앉으며 외쳤다. \"맞아, 틀림없어, 분명히 그래!\"\n\n\"이런! 이게 다 무슨 영문이람?\" 밀러 부인이 소리쳤다. \"어디 아픈 건 아니지요, 사촌? 물 좀, 어서 술 한 잔.\"\n\n\"놀라지 마십시오, 부인.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"저도 댁의 사촌 못지않게 술 한 잔이 절실합니다. 이 뜻밖의 만남에 우리 둘 다 똑같이 놀란 것이지요. 밀러 부인, 댁의 사촌은 제가 아는 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"아는 사람이라니!\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"오, 하느님!\"\n\n\"그렇소, 아는 사람이지요.\" 존스가 거듭 말했다. \"게다가 존경하는 사람이기도 합니다. 아내와 자식들을 당장의 파멸에서 지켜 내려 모든 것을 무릅쓰는 사람을 제가 사랑하고 존경하지 않는다면, 저 역시 역경 속에서 저를 모른 체할 수 있는 벗을 두어 마땅하겠지요!\"\n\n\"오, 선생님은 참으로 훌륭한 청년이세요.\" 밀러 부인이 외쳤다. \"그래요, 정말이지 가엾은 사람이랍니다! 이 사람은 모든 것을 무릅썼어요. 타고난 체질이 워낙 튼튼하지 않았더라면 그 일로 목숨을 잃었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"사촌,\" 이제 어지간히 정신을 차린 사내가 외쳤다. \"제가 말씀드린 하늘에서 내려온 천사가 바로 이분이에요. 제가 누이를 뵙기도 전에, 제 페기의 목숨을 지켜 준 분이 바로 이분입니다. 제가 그 사람을 위해 마련할 수 있었던 온갖 위안과 온갖 도움이 다 이분의 너그러움 덕분이었어요. 이분이야말로 정말이지 모든 인간 가운데 가장 가치 있고, 가장 용감하며, 가장 고귀한 분입니다. 오, 사촌, 제가 이분께 진 신세가 어떤 것이냐면!\"\n\n\"신세 이야기는 입에 올리지 마십시오.\" 존스가 다급히 외쳤다. \"한마디도, 부탁드리니 한마디도 마십시오.\" (강도 사건을 아무에게도 발설하지 않게 하려는 뜻이었으리라.) \"제게서 받은 그 하찮은 것으로 한 가정 전체를 지켜 낸 것이라면, 이토록 값싸게 기쁨을 산 적은 일찍이 없었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오, 선생님!\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"지금 당장 제 집을 보실 수 있다면 좋으련만. 선생님께서 말씀하신 그 기쁨을 누릴 자격이 있는 사람이 있다면, 그건 바로 선생님이라고 저는 확신합니다. 제 사촌이 그러더군요, 우리가 처한 그 곤경을 선생님께 알려 드렸다고요. 그 곤경이, 선생님, 이제는 거의 다 사라졌습니다. 무엇보다 선생님의 너그러우심 덕분에요. 이제 제 아이들은 누울 잠자리가 생겼고, 또 그 아이들은, 그 아이들은, 그 일로 영원한 축복이 선생님께 보답하기를! 그 아이들은 먹을 빵이 있습니다. 어린 아들은 다 나았고, 아내도 고비를 넘겼으며, 저는 행복합니다. 다, 다 선생님 덕분이에요, 선생님, 그리고 여기 계신 제 사촌, 더없이 훌륭한 여인 가운데 한 분인 이분 덕분입니다. 정말이지, 선생님, 저는 선생님을 제 집에서 꼭 뵙고 싶습니다. 제 아내도 꼭 선생님을 뵙고 감사드려야 합니다. 제 아이들도 그 고마움을 표해야 하고요. 정말이지, 선생님, 그 아이들도 자기들이 진 신세를 모르지 않습니다. 하지만 그 아이들이 지금 그 고마움을 표할 수 있게 된 것이 누구 덕분인지를 떠올리면, 제 가슴이 어떠하겠습니까. 오, 선생님, 선생님이 따뜻이 데워 주신 그 작은 가슴들이, 선생님의 도움이 없었더라면 지금쯤 얼음장처럼 차갑게 식어 있었을 겁니다.\"\n\n여기서 존스는 가엾은 사내가 더 말을 잇지 못하게 막으려 했다. 하지만 정작 사내 자신의 벅차오르는 가슴이 저절로 그 말문을 막아 버렸다. 그러자 이번에는 밀러 부인까지 감사의 말을 쏟아 내기 시작했으니, 자기 자신의 이름으로도, 또 사촌의 이름으로도 그러했다. 그리고 그녀는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"이런 선한 마음에는 틀림없이 영광스러운 보답이 따를 거예요.\"\n\n존스가 대답했다. \"저는 이미 충분히 보답받았습니다. 부인, 댁의 사촌이 들려준 이야기는,\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 일찍이 알지 못했던 더없이 흐뭇한 감정을 제게 안겨 주었습니다. 이런 이야기를 듣고도 마음이 움직이지 않는 자라면 비참한 인간일 테지요. 그러니 이 장면에서 다행히도 한몫을 해냈다는 생각이 얼마나 가슴 벅찬 것이겠습니까! 만일 남에게 행복을 베푸는 그 기쁨을 느끼지 못하는 사람이 있다면, 저는 진심으로 그들을 가엾게 여깁니다. 제 생각에 그것은 야심가도, 탐욕가도, 향락가도 결코 얻을 수 없는 더 큰 영예이자 더 높은 이익이며 더 달콤한 기쁨인데, 그들은 그 맛을 볼 수 없는 사람들이니까요.\"\n\n이제 약속한 시각이 되어 존스는 서둘러 자리를 떠야 했지만, 그 전에 진심으로 친구의 손을 굳게 잡고 되도록 빨리 다시 만나기를 청했다. 그리고 자기가 직접 첫 기회를 보아 그의 집을 찾아가겠노라 약속했다. 그런 다음 그는 가마에 올라타 벨라스턴 부인의 댁으로 향했는데, 이 가난한 가정에 마련해 준 행복에 크게 마음이 들떠 있었다. 또한 그는 큰길에서 습격을 당했을 때 자비의 목소리가 아니라 엄정한 정의의 목소리에 귀를 기울였더라면 그들에게 닥쳤을 끔찍한 결말을, 소름 끼치지 않고는 떠올릴 수 없었다.\n\n밀러 부인은 저녁 내내 존스를 찬양해 마지않았는데, 앤더슨 씨도 머무는 동안 어찌나 열렬히 그녀에게 맞장구를 쳤던지, 강도 사건의 정황을 입 밖에 낼 뻔한 적이 한두 번이 아니었다. 그러나 그는 다행히 정신을 차려, 그러잖아도 더 큰 경솔이 될 일을 피했다. 밀러 부인이 자기 원칙에 대해 지극히 엄격하고 까다로운 사람임을 그가 알고 있었던 만큼 더욱 그러했다. 또한 그는 이 부인이 말이 많다는 것도 익히 알고 있었다. 그런데도 그의 감사하는 마음이 어찌나 컸던지, 그것이 분별심과 수치심을 모두 거의 눌러 버려, 자기 은인에게 더없는 영예를 돌릴 수 있는 어떤 정황이라도 빠뜨리느니 차라리 제 평판을 더럽힐 그 일을 떠벌릴 뻔하게 만들었던 것이다.\n\n독자가 깜짝 놀라게 될 이야기.\n\n존스는 약속된 시각보다 다소 일찍 도착했고, 부인보다도 일찍 와 있었다. 부인의 도착이 늦어진 것은 그녀가 저녁을 먹은 곳이 멀어서이기도 했지만, 그녀처럼 심란한 처지에 놓인 사람에게는 몹시 성가신 다른 자잘한 사고들이 겹쳤기 때문이기도 했다. 그리하여 존스는 응접실로 안내되었는데, 그곳에 든 지 몇 분 지나지 않아 문이 열리고 들어선 사람은—다름 아닌 소피아 자신이었다. 그녀는 첫 막이 끝나기도 전에 연극을 떠나온 터였다. 앞서 이미 말했듯이 이 작품은 새로 올린 연극이어서, 두 패거리가 한쪽은 야유를 퍼붓고 다른 한쪽은 갈채를 보내려고 몰려들었던 것이다. 두 패 사이에 격렬한 소란과 충돌이 벌어지자 우리의 여주인공은 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지, 한 젊은 신사의 보호를 받으며 무사히 가마까지 데려다 받게 된 것을 다행으로 여겼다.\n\n벨라스턴 부인이 늦게야 돌아올 것이라 일러두었던 터라, 소피아는 방에 아무도 없으리라 여기고 급히 들어와, 거의 정면에 놓인 거울로 곧장 다가갔다. 방 위쪽 끝, 지금 존스가 조각상처럼 꼼짝 않고 서 있는 그곳은 한 번도 돌아보지 않은 채였다. 그녀가 자신의 사랑스러운 얼굴을 들여다보다가 바로 그 거울 속에서 그 조각상을 처음 발견하고는, 곧장 몸을 돌려 그 환영이 실제임을 알아차렸다. 그 순간 그녀는 날카로운 비명을 지르며 거의 까무러칠 뻔했으나, 존스가 그녀에게 다가가 두 팔로 부축할 때까지 가까스로 정신을 붙들었다.\n\n이 두 연인의 표정이나 생각을 그려내는 일은 내 능력 밖이다. 둘 다 말이 없었던 것으로 보아 그들의 감정이 스스로 입에 담기엔 너무 벅찼으리라 짐작되니, 내가 그것을 표현할 수 있으리라고는 도저히 생각할 수 없다. 게다가 안타까운 일은, 내 독자들 가운데 이때 그들의 가슴속에 오간 것을 자기 마음으로 느껴볼 만큼 사랑에 빠져본 이가 드물다는 점이다.\n\n잠깐의 침묵이 지난 뒤, 존스가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"보아하니, 아가씨, 놀라신 듯합니다.\" \"놀라다니요!\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"오, 맙소사! 정말이지, 저는 놀랐습니다. 당신이 정말 그 사람인지조차 의심스러울 지경이에요.\" \"정말입니다.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"나의 소피아—아가씨, 이번 한 번만은 그렇게 불러도 용서해 주십시오—저는 바로 그 가엾은 존스입니다. 그토록 많은 좌절을 겪은 끝에, 운명이 마침내 자애롭게도 저를 당신께로 이끌어 준 것입니다. 오! 나의 소피아, 이 길고 헛된 추적 속에서 제가 견뎌온 그 수천 가지 고통을 당신이 아신다면.\" \"누구를 추적했단 말인가요?\" 소피아는 다소 정신을 가다듬으며 새침한 태도를 지으며 말했다. \"어찌 그리 모질게 그런 질문을 하실 수 있습니까?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"당신을, 이라고 말할 필요가 있겠습니까?\" \"저를요!\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"그렇다면 존스 씨에게 저와 관련된 그토록 중대한 볼일이라도 있는 건가요?\" \"어떤 이들에게는, 아가씨,\" 존스가 외치며 그녀에게 수첩을 건넸다. \"이것이 중대한 볼일로 보일 수도 있겠지요. 부디, 아가씨, 잃어버렸을 때와 같은 값어치 그대로 들어 있기를 바랍니다.\" 소피아는 수첩을 받아 들고 막 입을 열려 했으나, 그가 이렇게 말을 가로막았다. \"부탁이니, 운명이 이토록 자애롭게 우리에게 보내준 이 귀한 순간을 단 한 순간도 헛되이 흘려보내지 맙시다. 오, 나의 소피아! 제게는 훨씬 더 중대한 볼일이 있습니다. 이렇게 무릎을 꿇고, 당신의 용서를 구하게 해 주십시오.\" \"용서를요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"분명, 선생님, 그간 있었던 일을 생각하면, 그리고 제가 들은 바를 생각하면, 그것을 바라실 수는 없겠지요.\" \"제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모르겠습니다.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"하늘에 맹세코! 당신이 저를 용서해 주기를 바라지도 못하겠습니다. 오, 나의 소피아! 이제부터 저 같은 가엾은 인간에게는 다시는 한 가닥 생각도 던지지 마십시오. 만약 저에 대한 어떤 기억이라도 끼어들어 그 여린 가슴에 한순간의 불편함이라도 안겨준다면, 제가 그럴 자격 없는 인간임을 떠올려 주십시오. 그리고 어프턴에서 있었던 일의 기억으로, 당신의 마음에서 저를 영원히 지워 버려 주십시오.\"\n\n소피아는 그동안 내내 떨며 서 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 눈보다 희었고, 가슴은 코르셋 너머로 두근거리고 있었다. 그러나 어프턴이 언급되자 두 뺨에 홍조가 솟았고, 그때까지 거의 들지도 않았던 두 눈이 경멸의 눈빛을 띤 채 존스를 향했다. 그는 이 말 없는 질책을 알아차리고 이렇게 응답했다. \"오, 나의 소피아! 나의 유일한 사랑! 그곳에서 일어난 일에 대해, 당신이 저를 미워하고 경멸하는 것보다 제가 저 자신을 더 미워하고 경멸합니다. 하지만 그래도 제게 한 가지 정당한 평가만은 내려 주십시오. 제 마음은 결코 당신께 불충하지 않았다는 것을요. 제가 저지른 그 어리석음에 마음만은 조금도 가담하지 않았습니다. 그때조차 제 마음은 변함없이 당신의 것이었습니다. 비록 당신을 차지하리라는 희망을, 아니, 다시 뵙는 일조차 거의 단념했지만, 저는 여전히 당신의 매혹적인 모습을 마음에 품고 그리워했으며, 다른 어떤 여인도 진심으로 사랑할 수 없었습니다. 그러나 설령 제 마음이 매여 있지 않았다 해도, 그 저주받은 곳에서 우연히 함께하게 된 그 여인은 진지한 사랑의 대상이 될 만한 사람이 아니었습니다. 믿어 주십시오, 나의 천사여, 저는 그날 이후 오늘까지 그녀를 한 번도 보지 않았으며, 다시 볼 생각도 바람도 없습니다.\" 소피아는 속으로 이 말을 듣고 무척 기뻤다. 그러나 지금까지 지어 보였던 것보다 더 차가운 기색을 억지로 얼굴에 띄우며 말했다. \"그런데, 존스 씨, 어찌하여 고발당하지도 않은 일에 대해 굳이 변명하는 수고를 하시나요? 만약 제가 당신을 고발할 만하다고 여겼다면, 정말이지 용서받지 못할 성질의 죄목이 하나 있긴 합니다만.\" \"그게 대체 무엇입니까, 제발?\" 존스는 떨며 창백해진 채 대답했다. 벨라스턴 부인과의 정사 이야기가 나올까 봐서였다. \"오,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어찌 그럴 수가! 모든 고귀한 것과 모든 비천한 것이 한 가슴속에 함께 깃들 수 있단 말인가요?\" 벨라스턴 부인과, 첩처럼 얹혀살았다는 그 치욕스러운 사정이 다시 그의 마음속에 떠올라, 어떤 대꾸도 못하도록 입을 막아 버렸다. \"제가,\" 소피아가 말을 이었다. \"당신에게서 이런 처사를 예상할 수 있었겠어요? 아니, 어떤 신사에게서, 명예를 아는 어떤 남자에게서라도요? 제 이름이 공개적으로, 여관에서, 가장 천한 무리들 사이에서 더럽혀지다니요! 경계심 없는 제 마음이 너무 가볍게 베풀어 버린 작은 호의들이 그런 데서 자랑거리가 되다니요! 아니, 심지어 당신이 제 사랑으로부터 도망쳐야만 했다는 말까지 듣다니요!\"\n\n소피아의 이 말에 존스가 느낀 놀라움은 무엇과도 견줄 수 없었다. 그러나 죄가 없는 그였기에, 만약 그녀가 그의 양심이 켕기던 그 여린 줄을 건드렸을 때보다 훨씬 덜 곤혹스럽게 자신을 변호할 수 있었다. 몇 마디 따져 물어본 끝에 그는, 그녀가 자신을 두고 사랑과 명예에 대한 그토록 충격적인 만행을 저질렀다고 여기게 된 것이, 전적으로 파트리지가 여관에서 주인장들과 하인들 앞에서 떠벌린 말 때문임을 곧 알게 되었다. 소피아가 바로 그들에게서 그 정보를 들었다고 그에게 털어놓았기 때문이다. 자신의 성품과는 동떨어진 그런 죄에 자신이 전혀 무고하다는 것을 그녀가 믿게 만드는 일은 그리 어렵지 않았다. 그러나 그가 당장 집으로 달려가 파트리지를 죽여 버리겠다고 한두 번이 아니게 맹세하는 것을 그녀가 말리느라 무척이나 애를 먹어야 했다. 이 점이 풀리고 나자, 두 사람은 이내 서로가 너무도 흡족해진 나머지, 존스는 자신을 향한 모든 생각을 단념해 달라고 간청하며 대화를 시작했던 것조차 까맣게 잊어버렸다. 그리고 그녀 역시 전혀 다른 성격의 청원에라도 귀를 기울일 만한 마음 상태였다. 미처 깨닫기도 전에 두 사람은 이미 너무 멀리 나아가, 그가 청혼처럼 들리는 몇 마디를 흘리고 말았기 때문이다. 이에 그녀는 이렇게 대답했다. \"아버지께 대한 도리가 제 마음 가는 대로 따르는 것을 막지만 않는다면, 다른 남자와 더없이 풍족한 재산을 누리는 것보다 당신과 함께 파멸하는 편이 제게는 더 반가울 거예요.\" 파멸이라는 말이 나오자 그는 흠칫하더니, 한동안 잡고 있던 그녀의 손을 놓고, 제 손으로 제 가슴을 치며 외쳤다. \"오, 소피아! 그렇다면 제가 당신을 파멸시킬 수 있겠습니까? 아니요, 하늘에 맹세코, 안 됩니다! 저는 결코 그런 비열한 짓은 하지 않겠습니다. 사랑하는 소피아, 어떤 대가를 치르더라도, 저는 당신을 단념하겠습니다. 당신을 놓아 드리겠습니다. 당신의 진정한 행복과 어긋나는 그런 온갖 희망을 제 가슴에서 모조리 뜯어내겠습니다. 제 사랑만은 영원히 간직하겠으나, 그것은 침묵 속에 있을 것입니다. 당신에게서 멀리 떨어진 곳에 있을 것이며, 어느 낯선 이국 땅에 있을 것입니다. 그곳에서는 제 절망의 어떤 목소리도, 어떤 한숨도 결코 당신의 귀에 닿아 당신을 어지럽히지 못할 것입니다. 그리고 제가 죽고 나면—\" 그는 말을 더 이으려 했으나, 소피아가 그의 가슴에 쏟아낸 눈물의 물결에 가로막혔다. 그녀는 그의 가슴에 기댄 채 한마디도 하지 못했다. 그는 그 눈물에 입을 맞춰 닦아냈고, 그녀는 한동안 아무 저항 없이 그가 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두었다. 그러나 이내 정신을 차리고 그의 두 팔에서 부드럽게 빠져나왔다. 그리고 너무도 여려서 도저히 감당할 수 없을 것 같은 화제에서 벗어나려고, 전에는 미처 물어볼 겨를이 없었던 질문 하나를 그에게 던질 생각을 해냈다. \"어떻게 이 방에 들어오게 되셨어요?\" 그가 더듬거리기 시작했고, 십중팔구 그가 막 내놓으려던 대답으로 그녀의 의심을 자아냈을 바로 그때, 별안간 문이 열리며 벨라스턴 부인이 들어왔다.\n\n몇 걸음 들어서다가 존스와 소피아가 함께 있는 것을 보고 그녀는 문득 멈춰 섰다. 그러더니 몇 순간의 침묵 끝에, 감탄할 만한 침착함으로 정신을 가다듬고 말했다—목소리와 표정 양쪽에 놀라움의 기색이 충분히 드러나 있긴 했지만. \"웨스턴 양, 연극을 보러 가 있는 줄 알았는데요?\"\n\n소피아는 존스가 어떤 방법으로 자신을 찾아냈는지 그에게서 들을 기회가 없었지만, 그 진짜 진상을, 혹은 존스와 벨라스턴 부인이 아는 사이라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았으므로 거의 당황하지 않았다. 더욱이 이 부인은 그 문제에 관한 모든 대화에서 그녀의 아버지에 맞서 전적으로 그녀의 편을 들어 왔던 터라 더욱 그러했다. 그래서 그녀는 별 망설임 없이 극장에서 일어난 일의 자초지종과 자신이 서둘러 돌아온 까닭을 죽 이야기했다.\n\n이 이야기가 긴 덕분에 벨라스턴 부인은 마음을 추스르고 어떻게 처신할지 궁리할 틈을 얻었다. 그리고 소피아의 태도로 보아 존스가 자신을 발설하지 않은 듯 희망이 생기자, 그녀는 쾌활한 기색을 띠며 말했다. \"웨스턴 양, 손님이 와 계신 줄 알았더라면 이렇게 불쑥 끼어들지 않았을 텐데요.\"\n\n벨라스턴 부인은 이 말을 하는 동안 소피아에게 시선을 고정하고 있었다. 그 가엾은 젊은 아가씨는 얼굴이 온통 홍조와 당혹으로 뒤덮인 채 더듬거리는 목소리로 대답했다. \"정말이지, 부인, 저는 부인의 자리에 함께해 주시는 영광을 언제나—\" \"적어도 제가,\" 벨라스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"무슨 볼일을 방해하는 건 아니길 바라요.\" \"아니에요, 부인,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"저희 볼일은 끝났어요. 부인께서도 기억하시겠지만, 제가 수첩을 잃어버렸다는 얘기를 여러 번 했었지요. 이분이 운 좋게도 그걸 주워서, 친절하게도 안에 든 어음과 함께 돌려주러 오신 거예요.\"\n\n존스는 벨라스턴 부인이 들어선 그 순간부터 두려움에 푹 가라앉을 지경이었다. 그는 발뒤꿈치를 까딱거리고 손가락을 만지작거리며, 가능하다면, 세련된 모임에 처음 끌려 나온 풋내기 시골 도령보다도 더 얼간이 같은 꼴로 앉아 있었다. 그러나 이제 그도 차츰 정신을 추스르기 시작했다. 그리고 벨라스턴 부인이 자기와 아는 사이임을 내세울 생각이 없음을 알아채고, 그 태도에서 단서를 얻어, 자기도 똑같이 처음 보는 사람인 척하기로 마음먹었다. 그는 말했다. \"수첩을 손에 넣은 뒤로, 그 안에 이름이 적힌 부인을 수소문하느라 무던히도 애를 썼습니다만, 오늘에야 비로소 운 좋게 그분을 찾아낼 수 있었습니다.\"\n\n소피아는 과연 수첩을 잃어버린 일을 벨라스턴 부인에게 말한 적이 있었다. 그러나 존스가 무슨 까닭에서인지 그것이 자기 손에 있다는 것을 그녀에게 한 번도 내비친 적이 없었으므로, 그녀는 지금 소피아가 하는 말을 한 음절도 믿지 않았고, 그토록 재빠르게 그런 핑계를 지어내는 이 젊은 아가씨의 비상한 기지에 그저 놀랄 따름이었다. 소피아가 극장을 떠난 이유 역시 그보다 나을 것 없는 신빙성을 얻었다. 비록 이 두 연인 사이의 만남을 설명할 길은 없었지만, 그녀는 그것이 우연이 아니라고 굳게 확신했다.\n\n그래서 그녀는 짐짓 꾸민 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"정말이지, 웨스턴 양, 돈을 되찾다니 운이 무척 좋았네요. 명예를 아는 신사분 손에 들어간 데다, 그분이 마침 그게 누구 것인지까지 알아내셨으니 말이에요. 양은 그걸 광고로 내는 데는 동의하지 않으셨을 것 같은데요.—선생님, 그 어음이 누구 것인지 알아내신 건 대단한 행운이었어요.\"\n\n\"오, 부인,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그건 수첩 안에 들어 있었고, 거기에 이 아가씨의 이름이 적혀 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"그거야말로 정말 다행이었네요.\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"그리고 웨스턴 양이 제 집에 있다는 걸 들으신 것도 그에 못지않게 다행이었고요. 이 사람은 아는 이가 거의 없거든요.\"\n\n존스는 마침내 완전히 평정을 되찾았다. 그리고 벨라스턴 부인이 들어오기 직전에 소피아가 던졌던 질문에 대해 그녀에게 해명할 기회가 지금 생겼다고 여기고는, 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"저, 부인,\" 그가 대답했다. \"상상할 수 있는 가장 운 좋은 우연으로 이걸 알아냈습니다. 며칠 전 밤 가면무도회에서 어느 부인께 제가 주운 물건과 그 주인의 이름을 말씀드렸더니, 그분이 웨스턴 양을 어디서 뵐 수 있을지 아실 것 같다며, 이튿날 아침 자기 집으로 오면 알려 주겠다고 하셨지요. 약속대로 갔습니다만, 그분은 집에 안 계셨습니다. 그 뒤로도 도무지 만날 수가 없다가, 오늘 아침에야 비로소 만나 부인 댁으로 가 보라고 일러 주셨습니다. 그래서 이리로 와서 부인을 뵙고자 청하는 영광을 누렸고, 제가 아주 특별한 볼일이 있다고 하자, 하인이 저를 이 방으로 안내했습니다. 그리고 그리 오래지 않아 이 아가씨가 연극에서 돌아오신 겁니다.\"\n\n가면무도회 이야기를 꺼내면서 그는 벨라스턴 부인을 아주 슬쩍 곁눈질했는데, 소피아에게 들킬까 봐 걱정할 필요는 전혀 없었다. 그녀는 보아하니 너무도 당황한 나머지 무엇 하나 눈여겨볼 처지가 아니었던 것이다. 이 암시에 부인은 다소 놀라 입을 다물었다. 그때 소피아의 마음이 동요하는 것을 본 존스는, 그녀를 풀어 줄 유일한 방법, 곧 물러나는 길을 택하기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 그렇게 하기 전에 그가 말했다. \"부인, 이런 경우에는 으레 얼마간 사례를 하는 것이 관례라고 알고 있습니다.—저는 제 정직함에 대해 아주 후한 보답을 한사코 청해야겠습니다.—그것은, 부인, 다름이 아니라, 이곳을 다시 한 번 방문하도록 허락받는 영광입니다.\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"당신이 신사라는 점은 조금도 의심치 않으며, 제 집 문은 지체 있는 분들께는 결코 닫혀 있지 않답니다.\"\n\n그리하여 존스는 격식에 맞는 예를 갖춘 뒤 떠났는데, 자신도 무척 만족스러웠고 소피아 또한 그에 못지않게 만족스러웠다. 그녀는 자신이 이미 너무도 잘 알고 있는 사실을 벨라스턴 부인이 알아챌까 봐 몹시 마음 졸이던 참이었던 것이다.\n\n계단에서 존스는 오랜 지인인 어너 부인과 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 그토록 그를 험담했으면서도 이제는 아주 예의 바르게 굴 만큼 처신이 능숙했다. 이 만남은 실로 운 좋은 일로 드러났는데, 존스가 자신이 묵고 있는 집을 그녀에게 알려 주었기 때문이다. 그 집을 소피아는 모르고 있었다.\n\n우아한 섀프츠베리 경은 어딘가에서 진실을 지나치게 많이 말하는 것에 반대한다. 이로 미루어 보면, 어떤 경우에는 거짓말이 단지 용서받을 만한 일일 뿐 아니라 칭찬받을 만한 일이기도 하다고 충분히 짐작할 수 있다.\n\n그리고 분명, 이처럼 칭찬받을 만한 진실로부터의 일탈을 누릴 권리를 가장 정당하게 주장할 수 있는 이들이라면, 사랑 문제에 놓인 젊은 여인들만 한 사람이 없을 것이다. 그들은 이를 위해 가르침과 교육을, 그리고 무엇보다 관습의 승인, 아니 관습의 필연성까지 내세울 수 있기 때문이다. 그 관습은 그들을 자연의 정직한 충동에 순응하지 못하도록 막는 것이 아니라(그런 금지란 어리석은 일일 테니), 그 충동을 인정하지 못하도록 막는 것이다.\n\n그러므로 우리는 이렇게 말하는 것을 부끄러워하지 않는다. 우리의 여주인공이 이제 앞서 말한 그 존경하는 철학자의 가르침을 좇았노라고. 그녀는 벨라스턴 부인이 존스라는 사람을 알아보지 못한다는 사실에 그때 완전히 안심하고 있었으므로, 약간의 거짓말을 치르더라도 부인을 그 무지 속에 그대로 두기로 마음먹었다.\n\n존스가 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않아 벨라스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 제법 잘생긴 젊은이로군요. 대체 누구일까요. 저 얼굴을 전에 본 기억이 도무지 없으니 말이에요.\"\n\n\"저도 그래요, 부인.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"제 쪽지 일에 관해서는 참 점잖게 처신했다고 해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"그래요. 게다가 아주 잘생긴 청년이지요.\" 부인이 말했다. \"그렇게 생각하지 않나요?\"\n\n\"별로 눈여겨보지 않았어요.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"다만 어딘가 어색하고 품위가 없어 보이던걸요.\"\n\n\"정말 옳은 말이에요.\" 벨라스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"그 태도만 봐도 좋은 사람들과 어울려 지내지 못했다는 걸 알 수 있지요. 아니, 쪽지를 돌려주고 사례를 거절했다고는 하지만, 저 사람이 과연 신사인지조차 의심스러워요. 저는 늘 보아 왔답니다. 잘 태어난 사람에게는 다른 이들이 결코 얻을 수 없는 무언가가 있다는 걸 말이에요. 저 사람에게는 집에 없다고 일러두라 명해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 부인, 정말이지.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"저 사람이 한 일을 보고서야 그렇게 의심할 수는 없지요. 게다가 부인께서 눈여겨보셨다면, 그의 말에는 우아함이, 섬세함이, 표현의 어떤 고운 맛이 있었어요. 그게, 그게……\"\n\n\"인정해요.\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"저 사람에게 말솜씨가 있긴 하지요. 그런데 정말이지, 소피아, 나를 용서해 줘야겠어요. 정말로요.\"\n\n\"제가 부인을 용서하다니요!\" 소피아가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 정말 그래 줘야 해요.\" 부인이 웃으며 대답했다. \"방에 처음 들어섰을 때 끔찍한 의심이 들었거든요. 정말이지 용서해 줘야 해요. 나는 저 사람이 바로 존스 씨 본인이 아닌가 의심했답니다.\"\n\n\"정말로요, 부인?\" 소피아가 얼굴을 붉히며, 짐짓 웃음을 지으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래요, 정말 그랬다니까요.\" 부인이 대답했다. \"어쩌다 그런 생각이 머릿속에 들어왔는지 모르겠어요. 저 사람의 공은 인정해 줘야지요. 옷차림이 점잖았으니까요. 그런데 사랑하는 소피, 그건 네 친구분께는 흔한 일이 아닐 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"이런 놀림은요, 부인.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"제가 부인께 약속을 드린 마당에 좀 매정하세요.\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않단다, 얘야.\" 부인이 말했다. \"그 전이었다면 매정했겠지. 하지만 아버지의 동의 없이는 결코 결혼하지 않겠다고 내게 약속한 다음이잖니. 너도 알다시피 거기엔 존스를 단념한다는 뜻이 담겨 있고 말이야. 시골의 어린 처녀에게는 충분히 용서받을 만했던 그 연정에 대해, 그리고 네가 완전히 이겨 냈다고 내게 말한 그 연정에 대해, 가벼운 놀림쯤은 견딜 수 있겠지. 사랑하는 소피, 그 사람 옷차림에 대한 작은 우스갯소리조차 견디지 못한다면 내가 무슨 생각을 해야겠니? 네가 정말로 깊이 빠져 있는 게 아닌가 두려워지기 시작하고, 네가 내게 솔직하게 대했는지조차 의심스러워질 거야.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 부인.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"제가 그 사람 때문에 무슨 마음이라도 쓴다고 여기신다면, 부인께서 저를 오해하시는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 사람 때문이라니!\" 부인이 대답했다. \"네가 나를 오해한 게 분명하구나. 나는 옷차림 이상은 말하지도 않았어. 그 밖의 어떤 비교로도 네 안목을 깎아내릴 생각은 없으니까. 사랑하는 소피, 네 존스 씨가 만일 이 사람 같은 자였다면, 나는 상상도……\"\n\n\"전 부인께서,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그분을 잘생겼다고 인정하신 줄 알았는데요……\"\n\n\"누구를 말이냐?\" 부인이 다급히 외쳤다.\n\n\"존스 씨요.\" 소피아가 대답했다. 그러고는 곧바로 정신을 차리며, \"존스 씨라니요! 아니, 아니에요. 죄송해요. 방금 여기 있던 그 신사분을 말한 거예요.\"\n\n\"오, 소피! 소피!\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"이 존스 씨가, 아무래도 여전히 네 머릿속을 맴돌고 있는 모양이구나.\"\n\n\"그러면, 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데요, 부인.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"존스 씨는 방금 우리 곁을 떠난 그 신사분만큼이나 제게는 아무런 의미가 없는 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"내 명예를 걸고,\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"그 말을 믿으마. 그러니 작은 악의 없는 놀림은 용서해 다오. 다시는 그 이름을 입에 올리지 않겠다고 약속할게.\"\n\n이제 두 여인은 헤어졌는데, 그것은 벨라스턴 부인보다 소피아에게 한없이 더 큰 기쁨이었다. 부인으로서는 더 중요한 볼일이 그녀를 불러내지만 않았더라면 기꺼이 연적을 좀 더 오래 괴롭혔을 터였다. 소피아로 말하자면, 처음으로 거짓을 행한 뒤 마음이 결코 편치 못했다. 방으로 물러난 그녀는 그 일을 더없는 불안과 부끄러운 양심으로 곱씹었다. 처지의 유난한 고달픔도, 그럴 수밖에 없었던 사정도, 자신의 행동을 마음과 화해시켜 주지는 못했다. 그녀의 마음은 너무도 섬세해서, 비록 상황 때문에 어쩔 수 없었다 해도 거짓을 저질렀다는 생각만큼은 견디지 못했던 것이다. 그리고 이 생각은 그날 밤 내내 한 번도 그녀가 눈을 붙이도록 내버려 두지 않았다.\n\n이틀 동안의 이야기를 담음.\n글을 쓰려는 주제에 대해 얼마간의 지식을 갖춘 작가가 더 잘 쓴다는 것을 증명하려는 시론.\n\n요즈음 여러 신사가 오로지 타고난 재능의 놀라운 힘만으로, 학문의 도움이라곤 조금도 받지 않고, 어쩌면 제대로 글을 읽을 줄도 모르면서도 문단에서 제법 이름을 떨치는지라, 들리는 바로는 근래의 비평가들이 이렇게 주장하기 시작했다고 한다. 온갖 종류의 학문은 작가에게 전혀 쓸모가 없으며, 실은 상상력의 타고난 발랄함과 활기를 옭아매는 일종의 족쇄에 지나지 않아서, 그로 인해 상상력이 짓눌려 본래라면 닿을 수 있었을 그 드높은 비상을 펼치지 못하게 된다는 것이다.\n\n이 학설은 지금 너무 멀리까지 끌고 간 것이 아닐까 싶다. 어찌하여 글쓰기만이 다른 모든 기예와 그토록 달라야 하는가? 춤 선생의 날렵함은 몸 놀리는 법을 배운다 해서 조금도 손상되지 않으며, 어떤 장인이든 연장 쓰는 법을 익혔다고 해서 그 연장을 더 서투르게 다루지는 않으리라 믿는다. 나로서는, 호메로스나 베르길리우스가 자기 시대의 온갖 학문에 통달한 대가가 아니라 오늘날의 작가 대부분처럼 무지했더라면 더 뜨거운 불꽃으로 글을 썼으리라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없다. 또한 피트의 그 모든 상상력과 열정과 판단력이라 한들, 만일 그가 데모스테네스와 키케로의 글을 깊이 읽어 그들의 정신을 통째로 자신의 연설 속에 옮겨 담고, 그 정신과 함께 그들의 지식까지 옮겨 오지 않았더라면, 우리 시대에 영국 의회를 웅변에서 그리스와 로마에 견줄 만한 곳으로 만든 그 연설들을 만들어낼 수 있었으리라고는 믿지 않는다.\n\n여기서 내가 동료 문인들 누구에게나 키케로가 웅변가를 길러내는 데 반드시 필요하다고 우리에게 설득하는 그만한 학식의 밑천을 요구한다고는 부디 오해하지 말기 바란다. 오히려 시인에게는 아주 약간의 독서면 충분하고, 비평가에게는 그보다 더 적게, 정치가에게는 그중에서도 가장 적게 필요하다고 나는 생각한다. 첫째 부류에게는 어쩌면 바이시의 《작시법》과 우리 현대 시인 몇 사람이면 족할 것이고, 둘째 부류에게는 그저 그런 희곡 한 무더기면 되며, 마지막 부류에게는 시원찮은 정치 잡지 모음 정도면 될 것이다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 나는 사람이 다루는 주제에 대해 얼마간의 작은 지식을 갖추기만을 바랄 뿐이니, 이는 \"각자 자신이 아는 기예 안에서 일하라\"는 옛 법언에 따른 것이다. 이것 하나만으로도 작가는 때때로 그런대로 잘해낼 수 있으며, 실로 이것이 없다면 세상의 다른 모든 학문도 그에게 별 도움이 되지 못할 것이다.\n\n이를테면 호메로스와 베르길리우스, 아리스토텔레스와 키케로, 투키디데스와 리비우스가 한자리에 모여 저마다의 재능을 한데 모아 춤의 기예에 관한 논고를 지었다고 가정해 보자. 그래도 그들이 에식스 씨가 그 주제에 관해 우리에게 내놓은 《품위 있는 교육의 기초》라는 저 빼어난 논고에는 미치지 못하리라는 데에 쉽게 동의하리라 믿는다. 그리고 만일 탁월한 브로턴 씨가 마음을 돌려 펜을 잡고, 위에서 말한 그 기초에 운동의 참된 원리를 덧붙여 완성해 준다면, 고금의 위대한 작가 가운데 누구도 그 고귀하고 유익한 기예를 다룬 적이 없음을 세상이 한탄할 까닭이 과연 있을지 의문이다.\n\n이토록 명백한 사안에 예를 잔뜩 늘어놓는 것을 피하고 단숨에 요점으로 가자면, 나는 많은 영국 작가가 상류 생활의 풍속을 묘사하는 데 완전히 실패한 한 가지 이유가, 실은 그들이 그것에 관해 아무것도 모르기 때문일 수 있다고 생각하는 편이다.\n\n이것은 불행히도 많은 작가가 얻을 수 있는 능력 밖의 지식이다. 책은 그것에 관해 매우 불완전한 관념밖에 주지 못하며, 무대도 그보다 그다지 나을 것이 없다. 앞의 것을 읽어 빚어진 멋진 신사는 거의 언제나 현학자로 드러나고, 뒤의 것을 본떠 자신을 빚은 자는 멋쟁이 허풍선이로 드러나기 마련이다.\n\n이러한 본보기에서 그려낸 인물들도 더 잘 떠받쳐지는 것은 아니다. 밴브루와 콩그리브는 자연을 본떴다. 그러나 그들을 본뜨는 자들은 마치 호가스가 야회나 무도회를 티치아노와 반다이크 시대의 의상으로 그린다면 그러할 만큼이나 오늘날의 모습과 동떨어진 그림을 그린다. 한마디로, 여기서는 모방이 일을 해내지 못한다. 그림은 반드시 자연 그 자체를 본떠야 한다. 세상에 대한 참된 앎은 오직 교제를 통해서만 얻어지며, 모든 계층의 풍속은 직접 보아야만 알 수 있는 법이다.\n\n그런데 마침 이 인류의 더 높은 계급은, 나머지 모든 인간 부류처럼 거리며 가게며 커피하우스에서 공짜로 볼 수 있는 것이 아니요, 짐승 중 상등품처럼 한 마리에 얼마씩 받고 구경시켜 주는 것도 아니다. 한마디로 이것은 다음 자격 가운데 하나라도 갖추지 못하면 아무도 들여보내 주지 않는 구경거리이니, 곧 가문이나 재산, 혹은 그 둘에 맞먹는 것으로서 노름꾼이라는 명예로운 직업이 그것이다. 그런데 세상으로서는 참으로 불운하게도, 그런 자격을 갖춘 사람들은 글쓰기라는 못난 장사를 스스로 떠맡으려 하는 일이 좀처럼 없다. 글쓰기란 흔히 더 낮고 가난한 부류가 발을 들이는 일인데, 밑천이라곤 한 푼도 들지 않고 차릴 수 있는 장사라고 많은 이가 여기기 때문이다.\n\n바로 여기서 저 기이한 괴물들이 나온다. 레이스와 수, 비단과 금란을 두르고 거대한 가발과 후프 치마를 걸친 채, 귀족과 귀부인이라는 이름으로 무대 위를 으스대며 거니는 자들 말이다. 이들은 일층 입석에 앉은 변호사와 그 서기들을, 위층 관람석에 앉은 시민과 그 도제들을 크게 기쁘게 하지만, 실제 삶에서는 켄타우로스나 키마이라, 혹은 순전히 지어낸 다른 어떤 피조물만큼이나 찾아볼 수 없는 것들이다. 그러나 독자에게 한 가지 비밀을 일러두자면, 상류 생활에 관한 이 지식은 실수를 막는 데는 매우 요긴하지만, 희극이나 혹은 지금 내가 쓰고 있는 이것처럼 희극 부류에 속하는 소설을 영역으로 삼은 작가에게는 그리 대단한 밑천은 못 된다.\n\n포프 씨가 여자들에 관해 한 말은 이 계급에 속한 대다수에게 그대로 들어맞는데, 그들은 실로 형식과 가식으로만 통째로 이루어져 있어 도무지 성격이라는 것이 없으며, 적어도 겉으로 드러나는 성격은 없다. 감히 말하건대 최상류 생활이야말로 단연 가장 따분하고, 해학이나 즐길 거리를 거의 내놓지 못한다. 더 낮은 영역의 갖가지 직업은 해학적인 인물의 큰 다양성을 빚어내지만, 이곳에서는 야심을 좇는 데 매달린 소수와 쾌락을 즐길 줄 아는 더더욱 적은 소수를 빼고 나면, 모든 것이 허영과 비굴한 모방뿐이다. 차려입기와 카드놀이, 먹고 마시기, 절하고 인사하기가 그들 삶의 일과를 이룬다.\n\n그러나 이 계급에도 더러는 정념이 폭정을 휘둘러, 예법이 정해 놓은 한계를 한참 넘어 그들을 내모는 자들이 있다. 그런 부류의 부인들은 그 당당한 대담함과, 평판에 대한 어떤 우월한 경멸로 인해 더 낮은 신분의 연약한 여자들과 뚜렷이 구별되는데, 이는 마치 신분 있는 정숙한 여인이 자영농이나 가게 주인의 정직한 아내와 그 정서의 우아함과 섬세함으로 구별되는 것과 같다. 벨라스턴 부인이 바로 이런 대담한 성격이었다. 그러나 시골의 독자들은 그녀를 보고 이것이 멋쟁이 여자들의 일반적인 행실이라거나, 우리가 그들을 그렇게 그리려 한다고 결론짓지 않기를 바란다. 그것은 마치 모든 성직자가 스왁컴으로 그려지고, 모든 군인이 노더턴 소위로 그려진다고 짐작하는 것이나 매한가지일 터이다.\n\n실로 어떤 무지한 풍자가들에게서 의견을 빌려 와 이 시대에 음탕함의 성격을 갖다 붙이는, 속된 무리 사이에 널리 퍼진 저 통념보다 더 큰 오류는 없다. 오히려 나는 신분 있는 사람들 사이에서 지금만큼 연애 음모가 적게 벌어진 적은 없었다고 확신한다. 오늘날의 여자들은 어머니에게서 오직 야심과 허영에만 마음을 두고 사랑의 즐거움은 거들떠볼 가치도 없는 것으로 멸시하도록 배웠으며, 그 후 그런 어머니들의 보살핌으로 남편이랄 것도 없이 결혼한 까닭에, 그러한 정서가 옳다는 확신을 꽤 단단히 굳힌 듯하다. 그리하여 그들은 따분하게 남은 평생을 좀 더 순진하지만 유감스럽게도 좀 더 유치한 오락을 좇는 일로 만족하니, 그것을 입에 올리는 것조차 이 이야기의 품위에 어울리지 않을 정도이다. 변변찮은 내 소견으로는, 오늘날 상류 사교계의 참된 특징은 악덕이라기보다 어리석음이며, 그것이 받아 마땅한 유일한 수식어는 경박함이라는 말뿐이다.\n\n연애에 따라붙기 마련인 편지와 그 밖의 일들에 관하여.\n\n존스가 집에 돌아온 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 그는 다음과 같은 편지 한 통을 받았다.\n\n“당신이 가버렸다는 걸 알았을 때만큼 놀란 적은 없었어요. 방을 나설 때, 나를 다시 보지도 않고 그 집을 떠나려 했으리라고는 조금도 짐작 못 했지요. 당신의 행동은 처음부터 끝까지 한결같아서, 백치 같은 여자에게 빠질 수 있는 마음을 내가 얼마나 경멸해야 마땅한지 새삼 일깨워 주는군요. 하기야 그 여자의 단순함보다는 차라리 그 교활함에 감탄해야 할지도 모르겠어요. 둘 다 놀랍긴 매한가지니까요! 우리 사이에 오간 말은 한마디도 못 알아들으면서도, 내 면전에서 당신을 안다는 것도, 전에 본 적이 있다는 것도 딱 잡아떼는 그 솜씨며 배짱이며, 그것을—뭐라고 불러야 할까요? 이건 둘이서 짜고 한 일인가요? 당신은 그토록 비열하게 나를 배신한 건가요? 오, 그 여자도, 당신도, 온 세상도 다 경멸스러워요. 무엇보다 내 자신이요! 왜냐하면—나중에 읽으면 미쳐버릴 말은 차마 쓰지 못하겠네요. 하지만 기억해 두세요. 나는 사랑했던 만큼이나 격렬하게 증오할 수도 있다는 걸요.”\n\n존스가 이 편지를 곱씹어볼 겨를도 거의 없이, 같은 손에서 온 두 번째 편지가 그에게 전해졌다. 이것 역시 우리는 그 글자 그대로 옮겨 적기로 한다.\n\n“내가 어떤 마음의 소용돌이 속에서 그 글을 썼을지 헤아려 본다면, 앞선 쪽지의 어떤 표현에도 당신은 놀라지 않을 거예요. 그래도 다시 생각해 보니, 좀 지나치게 격했던 듯싶네요. 적어도 나는, 가능하다면 모든 걸 그 끔찍한 극장 탓으로, 그리고 약속 시간을 넘기도록 나를 붙잡아 둔 어떤 어리석은 자의 뻔뻔함 탓으로 돌리고 싶어요. 사랑하는 사람에 대해 좋게 생각하기란 얼마나 쉬운지요! 어쩌면 당신은 내가 그렇게 생각하길 바라겠지요. 오늘 밤 당신을 보기로 마음먹었으니, 곧장 내게로 와요.\n\n추신. 당신 말고는 누구도 들이지 말라고 일러 두었어요.\n\n추신. 존스 씨는 내가 그의 변명을 거들어 주리라 짐작하겠지요. 내가 내 자신을 속이고 싶어 하는 만큼이나, 그가 나를 속이고 싶어 할 리는 없을 테니까요.\n\n추신. 어서 와요.”\n\n성난 편지와 다정한 편지 가운데 어느 쪽이 존스에게 더 큰 불편을 안겼는지는, 그 판단을 밀회에 능한 남자들에게 맡기겠다. 분명한 것은, 그가 그날 저녁 더 이상의 방문을 하고픈 마음이 조금도 없었다는 사실이다. 단 한 사람만은 예외였지만. 그렇지만 그는 자신의 명예가 걸려 있다고 여겼고, 설령 이것만으로는 충분한 동기가 못 되었더라도, 벨라스턴 부인의 성미를 자칫 불붙기 쉽다고 짐작되는 그 불길로 부채질하는 모험은 감히 하지 못했을 것이다. 그 결과가 소피아에게 들통나는 일로 이어질까 두려웠기 때문이다. 그래서 방 안을 못마땅한 듯 몇 차례 서성인 끝에 막 떠나려던 참에, 부인이 친절하게도 그를 가로막았다. 또 한 통의 편지가 아니라, 몸소 나타나서 말이다. 그녀는 옷매무새가 몹시 흐트러지고 낯빛도 매우 어지러운 채로 방에 들어와 의자에 몸을 던졌다. 그러고는 숨을 고른 뒤 입을 열었다. “보세요, 선생님. 여자란 한번 도를 넘어 멀리 가버리면, 어디서도 멈추지 못하는 법이지요. 일주일 전에 누군가가 이런 짓을 두고 내게 맹세했더라도, 나 자신이 그러리라고는 믿지 못했을 거예요.” “바라건대, 부인,” 존스가 말했다. “저의 매혹적인 벨라스턴 부인께서는, 자신이 베푼 그 많은 은혜를 이토록 잘 헤아리는 사람을 헐뜯는 어떤 말도 좀처럼 믿지 않으시겠지요.” “세상에!” 그녀가 말했다. “은혜를 헤아린다고요! 내가 존스 씨에게서 이런 싸늘한 말을 들으리라 기대했을까요?” “용서하세요, 나의 사랑스러운 천사여.” 그가 말했다. “받은 편지들 뒤로, 당신의 분노가 주는 두려움이라니요. 제가 어찌하여 그것을 받을 짓을 했는지는 모르겠습니다만.” “그러면 내가,” 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다. “그렇게나 성난 얼굴을 하고 있나요? 내가 정말로 꾸짖는 표정을 하고 왔던가요?” “남자에게 명예라는 것이 있다면,” 그가 말했다. “저는 당신의 분노를 살 만한 짓은 아무것도 하지 않았습니다. 당신이 보내신 약속을 기억하시지요. 저는 그에 따라 갔던 것입니다.” “제발,” 그녀가 외쳤다. “그 끔찍한 이야기를 처음부터 늘어놓지는 마세요. 한 가지 물음에만 답해 주면, 나는 마음을 놓겠어요. 당신은 그 여자에게 내 명예를 팔아넘기지 않았지요?” 존스가 무릎을 꿇고 더없이 격렬한 맹세를 쏟아내려는 순간, 파트리지가 기쁨에 취한 사람처럼 깡충거리고 까불며 방으로 뛰어들어 외쳤다. “찾았습니다요! 찾았어요! 여기, 도련님, 여기, 그분이 와 있어요. 어너 부인이 계단에 올라와 있습죠.” “잠깐 그녀를 붙잡아 둬라.” 존스가 외쳤다. “여기, 부인, 침대 뒤로 들어가세요. 당신을 숨길 다른 방도, 벽장도, 이 세상 어디에도 마땅한 자리가 없으니. 이렇게 빌어먹을 사달이 또 있을까요.” “정말 빌어먹을 일이군요!” 부인은 숨을 곳으로 가면서 말했다. 그리고 곧이어 어너 부인이 들어왔다. “어머나!” 그녀가 말했다. “존스 씨, 무슨 일이래요? 저 뻔뻔한 도련님 하인 녀석이 좀처럼 위층으로 올려 보내 주질 않으려 하지 뭐예요. 어퍼튼에서처럼 나를 도련님께 못 가게 막을 까닭이 또 있는 건 아니겠죠. 저를 보리라곤 별로 기대 안 하셨겠지만요. 도련님이 우리 아씨한테 분명 무슨 마법을 거셨나 봐요. 가엾고 사랑스러운 우리 아씨! 정말이지 친동생처럼 애틋하게 아끼는 분이거든요. 아씨한테 좋은 남편이 되어 주지 않으신다면, 하느님께서 자비를 베푸시기를요! 정말이지, 그러지 않으신다면 도련님한텐 아무리 끔찍한 벌이 내려도 모자랄 거예요.” 존스는 옆방에 죽어가는 부인이 한 분 계시니 제발 목소리를 낮춰 달라고 빌었다. “부인이라고요!” 그녀가 외쳤다. “옳거니, 도련님의 그 부인들 중 하나겠지요. 오, 존스 씨, 세상엔 그런 여자들이 너무 많다니까요. 우리가 그런 여자의 집에 들어온 게 아닌가 싶네요. 감히 말하건대 벨라스턴 부인이란 분도 행실이 그리 곱지는 않거든요.” “쉿! 쉿!” 존스가 외쳤다. “옆방에서 한마디도 빠짐없이 다 들린다.” “한 푼어치도 상관없어요.” 어너가 외쳤다. “저는 누구 험담도 안 해요. 하지만 정말이지 하인들은 거리낌도 없이들 말하던걸요. 그 마님이 딴 데서 남자들을 만난다고요. 어느 가난한 부인네 이름으로 되어 있는 집인데, 집세는 마님이 내고, 그 밖에도 그 부인네한테 좋은 것을 잔뜩 받는다고들 하더라고요.” 여기서 존스는 더없는 불편함을 드러내며 그녀의 입을 막으려 했다. “아니 이런! 아니 글쎄, 존스 씨, 말은 좀 하게 해 주세요. 저는 험담하는 게 아니에요. 그저 남들한테 들은 걸 옮길 뿐이라고요. 그러면서 속으로 생각하지요. 그 부인네가 그토록 사악한 방법으로 손에 넣은 재물이라면, 그게 그분한테 좋은 일이 될 게 뭐람, 하고요. 정말이지 가난해도 정직한 편이 낫지요.” “하인들은 악당이야.” 존스가 외쳤다. “자기네 마님을 부당하게 헐뜯는 게야.” “옳지요, 정말이지 하인이란 늘 악당이니까요. 우리 아씨도 그렇게 말씀하시면서 그런 소리는 한마디도 들으려 하지 않으셔요.” “그래, 나는 확신한다.” 존스가 말했다. “나의 소피아는 그런 비열한 험담에 귀 기울일 분이 아니지.” “아니 글쎄, 저도 그게 험담은 아니라고 봐요.” 어너가 외쳤다. “안 그러면 왜 딴 집에서 남자들을 만나겠어요? 절대 좋은 일일 리 없잖아요. 정식으로 구애를 받으려는 떳떳한 뜻이라면야—물론 어느 부인이든 그런 까닭으로 남자와 자리를 함께하는 거야 떳떳한 일이지만요—그렇다면 대체 무슨 까닭이겠어요?” “단언컨대,” 존스가 외쳤다. “그토록 명예로운 부인이자 소피아의 친척에 대해 이런 말을 다 듣고 있을 수는 없네. 게다가 자네는 옆방의 가엾은 부인을 미치게 만들 작정이군. 부디 나와 함께 아래층으로 내려가 주게.” “아니, 선생님, 말을 못 하게 하신다면 그만하지요. 여기, 우리 아씨가 보낸 편지가 있어요. 이걸 가지려고 어떤 남자들은 얼마를 내놓을까요? 그런데 존스 씨, 제가 보기에 도련님은 그다지 후한 편은 아니신 것 같네요. 게다가 어떤 하인들은 그러던데요—아무튼 분명 도련님은 제가 도련님 돈 빛깔이라곤 구경해 본 적도 없다는 걸 인정해 주시겠지요.” 여기서 존스는 황급히 편지를 받아 들고, 곧이어 그녀의 손에 다섯 닢을 슬쩍 쥐여 주었다. 그러고는 사랑하는 소피아에게 속삭이듯 천 번이고 감사를 보낸 뒤, 그 편지를 읽게 자기를 혼자 두고 가 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 그의 후한 인심에 깊은 고마움을 한껏 드러내며 곧 자리를 떴다.\n\n이제 벨라스턴 부인이 휘장 뒤에서 나왔다. 그녀의 격분을 내가 어찌 그려낼 수 있을까? 처음에는 혀가 말을 이루지 못했다. 그러나 두 눈에서는 불줄기가 쏘아져 나왔으니, 실로 그럴 만도 했다. 그녀의 가슴이 온통 불길에 휩싸여 있었으니 말이다. 그리고 마침내 목소리가 길을 찾자, 그녀는 어너나 자기 하인들을 향해 분노를 터뜨리는 대신, 가엾은 존스를 공격하기 시작했다. “보세요,” 그녀가 말했다. “내가 당신을 위해 무엇을 희생했는지를요. 내 평판도, 내 명예도—영영 사라져버렸어요! 그런데 내가 받은 보답이 뭐죠? 시골 계집애 따위, 백치 같은 여자 따위 때문에 외면당하고 멸시당하는 거잖아요.” “제가 무슨 외면을, 혹은 무슨 멸시를,” 존스가 외쳤다. “저질렀단 말입니까?” “존스 씨,” 그녀가 말했다. “시치미를 떼봐야 소용없어요. 나를 안심시키고 싶다면, 그 여자를 완전히 단념해야 해요. 그리고 당신의 진심을 보이는 증거로, 그 편지를 내게 보여 주세요.” “무슨 편지 말입니까, 부인?” 존스가 말했다. “아니 정말,” 그녀가 말했다. “설마 그 갈보 같은 계집 손에서 편지를 받은 걸 부인할 만큼 뻔뻔하지는 않겠지요.” “그러면 당신께서는,” 그가 외쳤다. “제가 명예를 내던지지 않고서는 허락할 수 없는 것을 제게 요구하시는 겁니까? 제가 당신께 그런 식으로 행동한 적이 있던가요? 제가 이 가엾고 죄 없는 처녀를 당신께 팔아넘기는 짓을 할 수 있다면, 제가 당신께도 똑같이 굴지 않으리라는 보장을 무엇으로 받으시겠습니까? 잠깐만 생각해 보시면, 한 부인의 비밀이 안전하게 지켜지지 못하는 남자야말로 가장 경멸스러운 자임을 분명 깨달으실 겁니다.” “좋아요,” 그녀가 말했다. “당신이 스스로의 눈에 그런 경멸스러운 자가 되라고 굳이 우기지는 않겠어요. 그 편지의 속내라야 내가 이미 아는 것 이상은 알려 주지 못할 테니까요. 당신이 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지 다 보여요.” 여기서 긴 대화가 이어졌으나, 너무 꼬치꼬치 캐묻지 않는 독자라면 그것을 길게 늘어놓지 않은 데 대해 내게 고마워할 것이다. 그러니 이쯤만 일러두는 것으로 충분하겠다. 벨라스턴 부인은 점점 누그러져, 마침내 그날 저녁 소피아와 마주친 것은 그저 우연일 뿐이라는 그의 항변과, 독자가 이미 아는 그 밖의 모든 사정을 믿게 되었거나, 적어도 믿는 척했다. 존스가 그 일들을 더없이 또렷하게 늘어놓고 보니, 그녀로서는 실상 그에게 화낼 까닭이 전혀 없었음이 분명해진 것이다.\n\n그렇지만 그녀의 마음속은, 편지를 보여 주기를 거절한 그의 태도에 온전히 만족하지는 못했다. 우리는 가장 명백한 이치라 해도 그것이 우리를 사로잡은 정념을 거스를 때면 그토록 귀를 닫아버리는 법이다. 그녀는 실로 소피아가 존스의 애정에서 첫 자리를 차지하고 있음을 잘 알고 있었다. 그런데도 거만하고 정욕에 들뜬 이 부인은, 끝내 두 번째 자리를 감수하기로 굽혔다. 좀 더 적절하게 법률 용어로 표현하자면, 다른 여자가 그 복귀권을 쥐고 있는 것을, 자신은 그 점유로 만족했던 것이다.\n\n마침내 존스가 앞으로는 그 집을 드나들기로 합의가 이루어졌다. 소피아도, 그녀의 하녀도, 모든 하인들도 이 방문을 소피아를 보러 온 것으로 여길 테고, 부인 자신은 속아 넘어가는 사람으로 비칠 터였기 때문이다.\n\n이 계략은 부인이 꾸민 것이었고, 존스도 크게 흡족해했다. 어떻게든 자신의 소피아를 볼 수 있는 가망이 생긴 것이 그로서는 진실로 반가웠던 까닭이다. 부인 또한 소피아를 속이는 일이 적잖이 즐거웠다. 존스가 자기 자신을 위해서라도 그 속임수를 소피아에게 발설할 리는 결코 없으리라 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n이튿날이 첫 방문 날로 정해졌고, 그런 다음 적절한 인사를 나눈 뒤 벨라스턴 부인은 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n여러 가지 일들을 담음.\n\n존스는 혼자 남게 되자마자 다급히 편지를 뜯어 다음과 같은 내용을 읽었다.\n\n\"선생님, 당신이 이 집을 떠난 뒤로 제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지는 말로 다 표현할 수가 없습니다. 당신이 다시 이곳에 오실 생각이라고 짐작할 만한 까닭이 있기에, 밤이 이렇게 늦었음에도 어너를 보냅니다. 그 아이가 당신의 거처를 안다고 하니, 당신을 막으려는 것입니다. 저를 아끼는 그 마음을 다해 당부드리오니, 부디 이곳을 찾아오실 생각은 하지 말아 주세요. 그러면 틀림없이 발각될 것입니다. 아니, 마님께서 흘리신 몇 마디로 미루어 보면, 그분이 벌써 어느 정도 의심을 품고 계신 것은 아닐까 싶기까지 합니다. 어쩌면 형편이 풀릴 일이 생길지도 모릅니다. 우리는 참고 기다려야 합니다. 그러나 다시 한 번 간청하오니, 제 마음의 평안을 조금이라도 헤아리신다면, 이곳으로 돌아오실 생각은 하지 말아 주세요.\"\n\n이 편지는 가엾은 존스에게, 옛날 욥이 친구들에게서 받았던 것과 똑같은 종류의 위로를 안겨 주었다. 소피아를 만나리라 스스로 약속했던 모든 희망이 무너진 것에 더하여, 그는 레이디 벨라스턴 문제에 관해서도 난처한 진퇴양난에 빠지고 말았다. 그가 잘 알고 있다시피, 세상에는 어겼을 때 어떤 변명도 좀처럼 통하지 않는 약속이라는 것이 있기 때문이다. 그렇다고 소피아의 그 단호한 금지가 있은 뒤에 그곳으로 가는 일은, 어떤 인간의 힘으로도 그를 떠밀 수 없는 일이었다. 마침내 그는, 그날 밤 잠을 대신했던 오랜 궁리 끝에, 자신이 병이 난 척하기로 마음을 정했다. 이것이야말로 레이디 벨라스턴의 노여움을 사지 않으면서 약속된 방문을 어길 수 있는 유일한 방편으로 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그가 그녀의 노여움을 피하고자 하는 데에는 한 가지 이상의 까닭이 있었다.\n\n그러나 아침에 그가 가장 먼저 한 일은 소피아에게 보낼 답장을 쓰는 것이었고, 그것을 어너에게 보내는 편지 안에 넣었다. 그런 다음 그는 앞서 말한 변명을 담은 또 다른 편지를 레이디 벨라스턴에게 부쳤다. 그리고 이에 대해 곧 다음과 같은 답을 받았다.\n\n\"오늘 오후에 여기서 당신을 볼 수 없다니 속상하지만, 그 사정이 더 마음에 걸립니다. 부디 몸을 잘 보살피시고 가장 좋은 의사의 진찰을 받으세요. 위험한 일은 없으리라 믿습니다. 저는 오늘 아침 내내 어리석은 자들에게 시달리느라 당신에게 편지 쓸 짬조차 거의 없습니다. 안녕히.\n\n추신. 오늘 저녁 아홉 시에 당신에게 들르도록 애써 보겠습니다. 반드시 혼자 계세요.\"\n\n이제 존스는 밀러 부인의 방문을 받았는데, 그녀는 격식 차린 인사를 몇 마디 건넨 뒤 다음과 같은 말을 시작했다. \"선생님, 이런 일로 찾아뵙게 되어 참으로 죄송합니다. 그렇지만 제 집이 한번이라도 평판 나쁜 집으로 입에 오르내리게 된다면, 제 가엾은 딸들의 명예에 얼마나 못된 결과가 미칠지 헤아려 주시기 바랍니다. 그러니 한밤중 그런 시각에 더 이상 부인들을 데려오지 말아 달라고 청하더라도, 제가 주제넘다고 여기지 말아 주세요. 어젯밤 한 분이 떠난 것이 시계가 두 시를 친 뒤였습니다.\" \"장담하건대, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"어젯밤 여기 와서 가장 늦게까지 머문 부인은—다른 한 사람은 제게 편지만 전했을 뿐입니다만—신분이 아주 높은 분이고, 저와 가까운 친척입니다.\" \"그분이 어떤 신분인지는 모릅니다.\" 밀러 부인이 대답했다. \"그렇지만 정말로 아주 가까운 친척이 아니고서야, 정숙한 여인이라면 밤 열 시에 젊은 신사를 찾아와 그 방에서 단둘이 네 시간이나 머무르지는 않을 겁니다. 게다가, 선생님, 그 부인의 가마꾼들이 보인 행실만 봐도 그분이 어떤 사람인지 알 만합니다. 그자들은 저녁 내내 현관에서 농지거리만 늘어놓더니, 제 하녀가 듣는 데서 파트리지 씨에게, 마님이 제 주인과 밤을 새울 작정이냐고 묻고는, 차마 옮길 수 없는 온갖 잡소리를 지껄였답니다. 저는 선생님을 정말로 존경합니다, 존스 씨, 선생님 그 자체로요. 아니, 제 사촌에게 베푸신 그 너그러움에 대해서는 더없이 큰 신세를 졌지요. 사실 저는 선생님이 그토록 선한 분인 줄을 최근에야 알았습니다. 그 가엾은 사람이 곤궁에 몰려 얼마나 끔찍한 길로 들어섰는지는 짐작도 못 했어요. 선생님이 제게 십 기니를 주셨을 때, 그것을 노상강도에게 건네주시는 거라고는 꿈에도 생각 못 했습니다! 오, 하느님! 선생님은 얼마나 큰 선을 베푸셨는지요! 이 집안을 어떻게 지켜 주셨는지요! 예전에 올워디 씨가 선생님에 대해 제게 들려주신 말씀이, 알고 보니 한 치도 틀림이 없었습니다. 그리고 정말이지, 설령 제가 선생님께 진 신세가 없다 하더라도, 그분께 진 신세가 워낙 크니, 그분을 보아서라도 제 힘닿는 한 최대의 존경을 선생님께 표해야 마땅하지요. 아니, 제 말을 믿어 주세요, 친애하는 존스 씨, 제 딸들과 제 자신의 평판이 걸린 문제가 아니라 하더라도, 선생님을 위해서라도 저는 이토록 잘생긴 젊은 신사가 그런 여자들과 어울리는 것이 안타까울 겁니다. 그러나 선생님이 기어이 그러시겠다면, 부디 다른 거처를 구해 달라고 청할 수밖에 없습니다. 저로서는 제 지붕 아래서 그런 일이 벌어지는 것을 좋아하지 않을뿐더러, 무엇보다 제 딸들 때문입니다. 하느님은 아시지만, 그 아이들에게는 명예 말고는 내세울 것이 거의 없으니까요.\" 존스는 올워디라는 이름에 흠칫 놀라며 안색이 변했다. \"정말이지, 밀러 부인,\" 그가 다소 격앙된 어조로 대답했다. \"이건 도무지 곱게 받아들일 수가 없군요. 저는 결코 부인의 집에 어떤 누명도 씌우지 않을 겁니다. 그렇지만 제 방에서 제가 원하는 손님을 만나는 것만큼은 양보할 수 없습니다. 그것이 부인의 비위를 거스른다면, 저는 형편이 되는 대로 다른 거처를 찾아보겠습니다.\" \"그러시다면 우리가 헤어져야 한다니 유감입니다, 선생님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 올워디 씨께서도, 제가 평판 나쁜 집을 꾸린다는 의심을 조금이라도 품으셨다면 결코 제 집 문턱을 넘지 않으셨으리라 확신합니다.\" \"아주 좋습니다, 부인.\" 존스가 말했다. \"선생님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"부디 노여워하지는 마세요. 저는 세상 무엇과도 바꿔서 올워디 씨 댁의 어느 누구에게도 결코 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않으니까요. 저는 이 일로 밤새 한숨도 못 잤답니다.\" \"부인의 안식을 어지럽혀 드려 죄송합니다, 부인.\" 존스가 말했다. \"하지만 파트리지를 즉시 제게 올려 보내 주시기를 청합니다.\" 그녀는 그러겠노라 약속한 뒤, 깍듯이 절을 하고 물러갔다.\n\n파트리지가 올라오자마자 존스는 더없이 사납게 그를 몰아세웠다. \"도대체,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 네 어리석음 때문에, 아니 그런 너를 데리고 있는 내 어리석음 때문에 몇 번이나 더 곤욕을 치러야 하느냐? 그 혓바닥이 기어이 나를 파멸시키기로 작정했느냐?\" \"제가 무엇을 했다고요, 나리?\" 겁에 질린 파트리지가 대답했다. \"그 강도 이야기를 입에 올리라고, 또 네가 여기서 본 사람이 바로 그자라고 떠벌리라고 누가 너에게 권한을 주더냐?\" \"제가요, 나리?\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"그걸 부인하는 거짓말까지 보태지는 마라.\" 존스가 말했다. \"제가 그런 이야기를 입에 올렸다 한들,\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"맹세코 나쁜 뜻은 없었습니다. 그분의 벗이며 친척 되는 분들에게가 아니었다면 입도 뻥긋하지 않았을 텐데, 그분들이라면 그 이야기를 더 멀리 옮기지는 않으리라 여겼던 겁니다.\" \"하지만 그보다 훨씬 무거운 죄목이 너에게 있다.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"내가 그토록 거듭 조심하라 일렀거늘, 이 집에서 감히 올워디 씨의 이름을 어떻게 입에 올렸느냐?\" 파트리지는 온갖 맹세를 늘어놓으며 결코 그런 적이 없다고 잡아뗐다. \"그게 아니라면,\" 존스가 말했다. \"밀러 부인이 그분과 나 사이에 어떤 연줄이 있다는 것을 어떻게 알았겠느냐? 게다가 바로 방금 그녀가 나에게, 그분을 보아 나를 존경한다고 말하던데.\" \"오, 맙소사, 나리.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"제발 끝까지 들어만 주십시오. 정말이지, 이렇게 불운한 일은 없었습니다. 끝까지 들어 보시면, 나리께서 저를 얼마나 억울하게 몰아세우셨는지 인정하실 겁니다. 어젯밤 어너 부인이 아래층으로 내려오다가 현관에서 저를 만나, 제 주인이 올워디 씨에게서 소식을 들은 게 언제냐고 물었습니다. 그리고 분명 밀러 부인이 그 말을 고스란히 들은 겁니다. 어너 마님이 가자마자, 그녀가 저를 거실로 불러들이더군요. '파트리지 씨,' 그녀가 말했습니다. '그 부인이 말한 올워디 씨가 어느 분이지요? 서머싯셔의 그 대단한 올워디 씨 말인가요?' '맹세코, 부인,' 제가 말했습니다. '저는 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다.' '설마,' 그녀가 말했습니다. '당신 주인이 올워디 씨가 이야기하시던 그 존스 씨는 아니겠지요?' '맹세코, 부인,' 제가 말했습니다. '저는 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다.' '그렇다면,' 그녀가 딸 낸시 쪽으로 돌아서며 말하더군요. '십 펜스를 걸어도 좋아, 바로 그 젊은 신사가 틀림없어. 영주님이 묘사하신 것과 한 치도 어김없이 들어맞잖니.' 도대체 누가 그녀에게 일러바쳤는지는 하늘이 아실 일입니다. 그게 제 입에서 나온 것이라면 저는 두 다리로 걸어 다니는 더없이 지독한 악당일 겁니다. 약속드리지만, 나리, 저는 비밀을 지키라는 부탁을 받으면 지킬 줄 압니다. 아니, 나리, 저는 그녀에게 올워디 씨에 관해 무언가를 일러 주기는커녕, 정반대로 말해 주었습니다. 그 순간에는 부인하지 않았지만, 흔히들 말하듯 두 번째 생각이 가장 낫다고 하니, 누군가가 그녀에게 알려 준 게 틀림없다는 데까지 생각이 미치자, 속으로 이 이야기에 끝을 내야겠다 마음먹었지요. 그래서 얼마 뒤에 거실로 다시 들어가 이렇게 말했습니다. 맹세코, 제가 말하기를, 이 신사가 존스 씨라고, 그러니까 이 존스 씨가 그 존스 씨라고 누가 부인께 일렀든 간에, 그건 새빨간 거짓말이라고 했습니다. 그리고 제가 청하기를, 부디 다시는 그런 이야기를 입에 올리지 말아 달라고 했습니다. 제 주인이, 제가 그렇게 일러바친 줄로 알 게 분명하기 때문이라고요. 그리고 이 집안의 누구든 제가 그런 말을 한 마디라도 했다고 말할 수 있으면 어디 해 보라고 했습니다. 정말이지, 나리, 참으로 신기한 일이라, 저는 그때부터 줄곧 도대체 그녀가 어떻게 알게 되었는지 속으로 곰곰이 생각해 왔습니다. 다만 일전에 여기 문간에서 동냥하는 노파를 하나 보았는데, 워릭셔에서 우리에게 그 모든 화를 불러왔던 그 노파와 어찌나 닮았던지요. 정말이지, 노파를 그냥 지나치면서 무언가를 쥐여 주지 않는 것은 결코 좋은 일이 아닙니다. 더구나 그 노파가 당신을 빤히 쳐다본다면 말이지요. 온 세상이 뭐라 해도, 저는 그들에게 화를 부르는 대단한 힘이 있다는 믿음을 절대 꺾지 않을 겁니다. 그리고 분명 저는 앞으로 노파를 볼 때마다 속으로 이렇게 되뇌겠지요. 인판둠, 레기나, 유베스 레노바레 돌로렘.\"*\n* 인판둠, 레기나, 유베스 레노바레 돌로렘: 베르길리우스의 〈아이네이스〉에 나오는 구절로, \"여왕이시여, 차마 말 못 할 슬픔을 다시 들추라 명하시는군요\"라는 뜻이다.\n\n파트리지의 그 순박함에 존스는 그만 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 그의 노여움도 끝내 사그라들고 말았다. 실은 그의 마음속에서 노여움이 오래 머무는 일은 좀처럼 없었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 파트리지의 변명을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하는 대신, 곧 이 거처를 떠날 작정이라 일러 주고는, 나가서 다른 거처를 구해 보라고 그에게 명했다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n파트리지가 존스 씨를 떠나기가 무섭게, 그동안 그와 무척 가까운 사이가 된 나이팅게일 씨가 그에게 다가왔다. 짧게 인사를 나눈 뒤 그가 말했다. \"그래, 톰, 어젯밤엔 꽤 늦게까지 손님이 있었다지. 정말이지 자넨 운 좋은 친구야. 이 도시에 온 지 보름도 안 됐는데, 새벽 두 시까지 문 앞에 가마를 세워 둘 수 있다니.\" 그러고는 그런 식의 흔해 빠진 농담을 한참 늘어놓았고, 마침내 존스가 말을 가로막으며 입을 열었다. \"그 얘긴 다 밀러 부인한테서 들은 모양이군. 조금 전에 여기 올라와서 내게 주의를 주고 갔거든. 그 착한 부인은 아무래도 딸들의 평판이 걱정인 모양이야.\" \"오! 그 점에 대해선 정말이지 깐깐하지.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"기억나나? 부인은 낸시를 우리와 함께 가면무도회에 보내려 하지 않았잖아.\" \"아니, 정말이지, 내 생각엔 부인이 옳아.\" 존스가 말했다. \"어쨌든 나는 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들여서, 파트리지를 보내 다른 하숙을 알아보게 했네.\" \"자네만 좋다면,\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"우리 또 함께 지낼 수도 있겠군. 사실 비밀을 하나 털어놓자면 — 이 집 식구들한테는 말하지 말아 주게 — 나도 오늘 이 집을 떠날 작정이거든.\" \"아니, 친구, 자네한테도 밀러 부인이 주의를 줬단 말인가?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"아니야.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"다만 방이 영 편치가 않아서 말이지. 게다가 이 동네에도 슬슬 싫증이 났어. 좀 더 놀거리 가까이에 있고 싶어서, 펠멜로 옮길 생각이네.\" \"그래서 떠나는 걸 비밀로 할 작정인가?\" 존스가 말했다. \"약속하지.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"하숙비를 떼먹으려는 건 아니야. 다만 정식으로 작별 인사를 하지 않으려는 데에는 나름의 사사로운 이유가 있어.\" \"그리 사사로운 이유도 아니지.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"분명히 말하는데, 나는 이 집에 온 둘째 날부터 그걸 다 보고 있었어. 자네가 떠나면 여기 눈물 적실 사람이 생기겠지. 가엾은 낸시, 정말이지 안됐어! 정말로, 잭, 자넨 그 처녀를 데리고 바보짓을 했어. 자네가 그 아이한테 갈망을 심어 놓았으니, 그건 아무것도 고쳐 주지 못할까 봐 두려워.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"대체 나더러 어쩌란 말인가? 그 아이를 고쳐 주겠다고 결혼이라도 하란 말이야?\" \"아니.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"자네가 내 눈앞에서 그토록 자주 했던 것처럼 그 아이한테 사랑을 속삭이지 말았어야 한다는 거야. 나는 그 어머니가 그걸 한 번도 못 보다니, 그 눈먼 짓에 어이가 없었네.\" \"흥, 그걸 봐?\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"대체 뭘 본단 말인가?\" \"뭘 보긴.\" 존스가 말했다. \"자네가 그 딸을 자네한테 정신없이 빠지게 만들어 놓은 걸 본단 말이지. 그 가엾은 처녀는 단 한순간도 그걸 숨기질 못해. 그 눈은 자네한테서 떠날 줄을 모르고, 자네가 방에 들어올 때마다 매번 얼굴을 붉히지. 정말이지 나는 그 아이가 진심으로 가엾어. 사람들 중에서도 가장 마음씨 곱고 정직한 축으로 보이거든.\" \"그러니까,\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"자네 말대로라면, 여자들이 우리한테 반할까 봐 두려워서 그저 흔한 가벼운 추파조차 즐겨서는 안 된다는 거로군.\" \"정말이지, 잭,\" 존스가 말했다. \"자넨 일부러 내 말을 잘못 알아듣는군. 나는 여자들이 그리 쉽게 사랑에 빠진다고 생각하진 않아. 다만 자네는 흔한 추파의 선을 한참 넘어섰단 말이지.\" \"뭐, 자넨 우리가 한 잠자리에 들기라도 했다고 생각하는 건가?\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"아니, 정말이지,\" 존스가 자못 진지하게 대답했다. \"자넬 그렇게까지 나쁘게 보진 않아. 아니, 한 걸음 더 나아가서 말하지. 나는 자네가 그 가엾은 어린것의 평온을 무너뜨리려고 미리 짜 놓은 계획을 세웠다거나, 그 결과를 내다보고 있었다고도 생각하지 않아. 자네가 무척 마음씨 좋은 친구라는 걸 잘 아니까. 그런 사람은 그런 식의 잔인한 짓을 저지를 리 없지. 하지만 그와 동시에, 자네는 이 가엾은 처녀가 그 제물로 바쳐지고 있다는 걸 헤아리지 않은 채 자기 허영심을 채운 거야. 그리고 그저 무료한 시간을 달래려는 마음밖에 없었으면서도, 실은 그 아이로 하여금 자네가 자기한테 더없이 진지한 뜻을 품고 있다고 자만하게 만들 빌미를 준 거지. 자, 잭, 솔직하게 대답해 보게. 격렬하고도 서로를 향한 애정에서 솟아나는 행복을, 그토록 우아하고 달콤하게 늘어놓은 그 모든 묘사가 다 무엇을 향한 거였나? 그 모든 따뜻한 다정함의 고백과, 너그럽고 사심 없는 사랑의 맹세는 다 무엇이었나? 자넨 그 아이가 그걸 제게 끌어다 새기지 않으리라고 생각했나? 아니면, 솔직히 말해 보게, 자넨 그 아이가 그러기를 바라지 않았던가?\" \"정말이지, 톰,\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"자네한테 이런 면이 있는 줄은 몰랐네. 자넨 훌륭한 목사가 되겠어. 그러니까 자네라면 이제 낸시가 허락한다 해도 그 아이와 잠자리에 들지 않겠다는 거지?\" \"안 들지.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"들면 내가 천벌을 받겠네.\" \"톰, 톰,\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"어젯밤 말이야, 어젯밤을 떠올려 보게——\n\n모든 눈이 감기고, 창백한 달과\n말 없는 별들만이, 그 은밀한 도둑질을 알고 빛나던 그때를.\"\n\n\"이보게, 나이팅게일 씨.\" 존스가 말했다. \"나는 입에 발린 위선자가 아니고, 이웃들보다 더 정결한 척하지도 않아. 나도 여자들과 죄를 지은 적이 있고, 그건 인정하네. 하지만 내가 누구를 해쳤다는 자각은 없어.—그리고 내 쾌락을 얻자고, 어떤 사람에게든 알면서도 불행의 원인이 되지는 않겠네.\"\n\n\"알았어, 알았어.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"자네 말을 믿네. 그리고 자네가 나를 그런 짓에서만큼은 무죄로 봐 준다고 확신하네.\"\n\n\"진심으로 그러네.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"그 아이를 농락했다는 점에서는 무죄로 보지만, 그 아이의 마음을 얻어 버린 일에 대해서는 아니야.\"\n\n\"내가 그랬다면,\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"그건 미안한 일이지. 하지만 시간과 떨어져 있음이 그런 인상쯤은 곧 지워 줄 거야. 그건 나 스스로도 받아들여야 할 처방이거든. 사실대로 털어놓자면—내 평생 어떤 처녀도 그 절반만큼도 좋아해 본 적이 없으니까. 하지만 톰, 자네한테 비밀을 전부 알려 줘야겠어. 우리 아버지가 나는 본 적도 없는 여자와 혼처를 정해 두셨어. 그 여자가 지금 도시로 올라오고 있는데, 내가 가서 구애를 하라는 거야.\"\n\n이 말에 존스가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리자, 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"아니, 제발, 날 비웃지 말게. 이 일로 내가 반쯤 미쳐 있지 않다면 내가 악마한테 끌려가도 좋아! 가엾은 내 낸시! 오! 존스, 존스, 내 손에 쥔 재산이 있었으면 좋겠네.\"\n\n\"나도 진심으로 자네한테 그런 게 있었으면 싶네.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"사정이 그렇다면, 나는 자네들 둘 다 진심으로 가엾게 여기네. 하지만 설마 그 아이한테 작별 인사도 없이 떠날 작정은 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"나는,\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"만 파운드를 준다 해도 작별 인사의 고통은 견디지 못하겠네. 게다가 나는, 그게 무슨 좋은 목적에 보탬이 되기는커녕 가엾은 내 낸시를 더욱 달아오르게 만들 뿐이라고 확신해. 그러니 부탁인데 오늘은 그 일에 대해 한마디도 꺼내지 말아 주게. 나는 저녁에, 아니면 내일 아침에 떠날 작정이야.\"\n\n존스는 그러지 않겠다고 약속했다. 그러고는 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 자네가 이미 마음을 정했고 또 그 아이를 떠나야만 하는 처지라면, 가장 신중한 방법을 택한 것 같다고 말했다. 이어 그는 나이팅게일에게 그와 같은 집에서 묵게 된다면 무척 기쁘겠다고 했고, 그리하여 두 사람 사이에서 나이팅게일이 그에게 일층이나 삼층 방 중 하나를 구해 주기로 합의가 이루어졌다. 그 사이 층은 이 젊은 신사 자신이 쓰기로 되어 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 나이팅게일은, 우리가 곧 그에 대해 조금 더 말하지 않을 수 없게 될 인물인데, 일상적인 거래에서는 엄격하게 명예를 지키는 사람이었고, 도시의 젊은 신사들 사이에서 더욱 드문 일로, 엄격하게 정직하기까지 한 사람이었다. 그럼에도 연애 문제에서는 그 도덕관념이 다소 헐거웠다. 그렇다고 이 점에서 그가 신사들이 더러 그러하고 더 자주 그런 척하는 것처럼 아예 원칙이라곤 없는 자였다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그가 여자들에게 변명의 여지가 없는 어떤 배신을 저질러 온 것만은 분명했고, 사랑을 한다고 불리는 어떤 알 수 없는 술수 속에서 숱한 기만을 부려 왔는데, 만약 그것을 장사에서 써먹었더라면 그는 세상에서 가장 지독한 악당으로 손꼽혔을 것이다.\n\n하지만 세상은, 무슨 까닭에서인지 나로서는 잘 모르겠으나, 이런 배신을 너그러운 눈으로 보아 주기로 합의해 둔 터라, 그는 이런 종류의 죄악을 부끄러워하기는커녕 도리어 자랑스러워했고, 여자를 손에 넣는 자신의 솜씨와 그 마음을 정복한 승리를 곧잘 떠벌리곤 했다. 이 때문에 그는 그 전에도 존스에게서 몇 차례 꾸지람을 들은 적이 있었으니, 존스는 이 종족의 아리따운 절반에게 가하는 어떤 못된 행실에 대해서도 늘 큰 쓴소리를 쏟아 냈다. 그의 말로는, 여자들이란 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 가장 소중한 벗이라는 관점에서 본다면 더없는 사랑과 다정함으로 보살피고 떠받들며 애지중지해야 할 존재이지만, 만약 적으로 여긴다면 그 정복이란 한 사내가 그것을 자랑으로 삼기보다 도리어 부끄러워해야 마땅한 것이라고 했다.\n\n밀러 부인의 내력에 관한 짧은 이야기.\n\n존스는 이날, 병자치고는 꽤 푸짐한 저녁을 먹었다. 다시 말해 양 어깨살 한 덩이의 절반이 넘는 양을 먹어치웠다. 오후에는 밀러 부인에게서 차를 함께 마시자는 청을 받았다. 마음씨 고운 이 부인은 파트리지를 통해서였는지, 아니면 자연스럽든 초자연적이든 다른 어떤 경로를 통해서였는지, 그가 올워디 씨와 인연이 있다는 사실을 알게 되자, 그를 불편한 마음으로 떠나보낸다는 생각을 도무지 견딜 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n존스는 그 청을 받아들였다. 찻주전자가 치워지고 딸들이 방에서 물러나자마자, 미망인은 별다른 서두도 없이 다음과 같이 이야기를 시작했다. \"글쎄요, 이 세상에는 참으로 놀라운 일들이 많이도 일어나지요. 하지만 올워디 씨의 친척분을 제 집에 모시고도 그 사실을 까맣게 모르고 있었다니, 정말이지 기막힌 노릇이 아닐 수 없어요. 아아! 선생님, 그 누구보다 훌륭한 그 신사분이 저와 제 식구들에게 얼마나 큰 은인이셨는지 선생님은 짐작도 못 하실 거예요. 그래요, 선생님, 저는 부끄러움 없이 인정하겠어요. 제가 오래전에 굶주려 죽지 않고, 의지할 데 없고 가엾고 친구 하나 없는 두 고아 자식을 세상의 보살핌이라기보다는 차라리 세상의 잔혹함에 내맡기고 떠나지 않을 수 있었던 것은, 모두가 그분의 자비 덕분이랍니다.\n\n\"선생님, 아셔야 할 것이, 제가 지금은 셋방을 놓아 입에 풀칠하는 처지로 전락했지만, 본래는 양갓집 규수로 나고 자랐답니다. 제 아버지는 군 장교셨고, 상당한 계급에 오른 채 돌아가셨어요. 하지만 봉급을 한 푼도 남김없이 다 쓰며 사셨고, 그 봉급이 아버지와 함께 끊어지자 그분이 돌아가셨을 때 우리 식구는 거지 신세가 되고 말았지요. 우리는 세 자매였어요. 그중 하나는 운 좋게도 얼마 안 가 천연두로 세상을 떠났고, 둘째는 어느 부인이 자비를 베푼다며 시중을 들이려고 데려갔지요. 그 부인의 어머니가 제 할머니의 하녀였는데, 전당포로 돈을 모은 자기 아버지에게서 막대한 재산을 물려받아 큰 영지를 가진 멋쟁이 신사와 결혼했더랬어요. 그 부인이 제 동생을 어찌나 야만스럽게 부려먹었는지, 걸핏하면 태생과 가난을 들먹이며 비웃듯 '양갓집 아가씨'라 불러댔으니, 결국 그 가엾은 아이의 가슴을 찢어놓고 만 거라고 저는 믿어요. 요컨대 그 애 역시 아버지가 돌아가신 지 한 해도 못 되어 세상을 떠났답니다. 운명은 제게는 더 나은 길을 마련해 주는 것이 옳다고 여겼는지, 아버지가 돌아가신 지 한 달 만에 저는 어느 목사님과 결혼했어요. 그분은 오래전부터 저를 사랑했고, 그 때문에 제 아버지에게 몹시 박대를 받았던 분이지요. 제 가엾은 아버지는 우리에게 단돈 한 푼 물려주지 못하면서도, 우리를 어찌나 곱게 길렀던지, 마치 우리가 세상에서 가장 부유한 상속녀라도 되는 양 우리를 대했고 우리 자신도 그렇게 여기기를 바라셨거든요. 하지만 사랑하는 제 남편은 그런 박대를 모두 잊고, 우리가 아버지를 여읜 그 순간 곧장 제게 어찌나 따뜻하게 다시 구애해 왔던지, 늘 그분을 좋아했고 이제는 그 어느 때보다 그분을 존경하게 된 저는 이내 마음을 허락했지요. 저는 그 더없이 훌륭한 분과 더없는 행복 속에서 오 년을 살았어요, 마침내, 그만—아아! 잔혹하고, 잔혹한 운명, 우리를 갈라놓고, 제게서 더없이 다정한 남편을, 제 가엾은 딸들에게서 더없이 자상한 아버지를 앗아간 그 운명—오, 가엾은 내 딸들아! 너희는 너희가 잃은 그 축복이 무엇인지조차 알지 못하는구나—존스 씨, 이런 여자다운 나약함이 부끄럽습니다만, 저는 그분을 입에 올릴 때마다 눈물 없이는 못 한답니다.\" \"오히려 제가 부끄러워해야지요, 부인.\" 존스가 말했다. \"제가 부인과 함께 눈물 흘리지 못하니 말입니다.\" \"그런데 선생님,\" 부인이 말을 이었다. \"저는 이제 두 번째로, 이전보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 처지에 놓이게 되었어요. 견뎌내야 할 그 끔찍한 슬픔에 더해, 이번에는 길러야 할 두 아이까지 있었고, 그러면서도 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 그 어느 때보다 무일푼이었으니까요. 바로 그때 제 남편과 약간의 친분이 있던 그 위대하고, 선량하고, 영광스러운 분, 올워디 씨가 우연히 제 곤경을 전해 듣고는 곧장 제게 이런 편지를 써 보내셨어요. 여기 있어요, 선생님, 여기. 선생님께 보여드리려고 주머니에 넣어 왔답니다. 이게 그 편지예요, 선생님. 꼭 읽어드려야겠어요, 그리고 그러고 싶어요.\n\n\"'부인께,\n\n\"'근래 겪으신 비통한 상실에 대해 진심으로 애도를 표합니다. 부인 자신의 훌륭한 분별력과, 더없이 훌륭하셨던 분께 배우셨을 그 귀한 가르침이 있으니, 제가 드릴 수 있는 그 어떤 충고보다도 부인께서 그 슬픔을 더 잘 견디게 해 줄 것입니다. 또한 더없이 다정한 어머니라고 익히 들어온 부인께서, 지나친 비탄에 빠진 나머지, 이제 부인의 다정함만을 오롯이 필요로 하는 그 가엾은 어린것들에 대한 의무를 저버리시는 일은 결코 없으리라 믿어 의심치 않습니다.\n\n\"'다만 지금 부인께서는 세상일을 두루 헤아릴 형편이 못 되시리라 짐작되니, 제가 사람을 시켜 부인을 찾아뵙고 이십 기니를 전해 드리도록 한 것을 너그러이 용서해 주십시오. 부디 받아주시기를 청하며, 부인을 직접 뵙는 기쁨을 누릴 때까지 기다리겠습니다. 부디 저를, 부인, 이러이러한 사람으로 여겨 주십시오.'\n\n\"이 편지를 저는, 앞서 말씀드린 그 돌이킬 수 없는 상실을 겪은 지 보름 만에 받았어요, 선생님. 그리고 또 보름 뒤에는 올워디 씨가—그 복되신 올워디 씨가 친히 저를 찾아오셔서, 선생님이 지금 저를 보고 계신 바로 이 집에 저를 들여앉히시고, 살림을 갖추라며 큰돈을 주시고, 해마다 오십 파운드의 연금을 정해 주셨답니다. 그 연금을 저는 그 후로 줄곧 한 번도 거르지 않고 받아왔지요. 그러니 헤아려 보세요, 존스 씨, 제 목숨과, 또 그 아이들이 있기에 비로소 제 목숨이 값진 것이 되는 그 사랑하는 자식들의 목숨까지 보전해 준 은인을 제가 어떤 마음으로 여겨야 하는지를요. 그러니 존스 씨, 제가 이 말씀을 드린다고 주제넘다 여기지 말아주세요(올워디 씨가 그토록 귀히 여기시는 분이니 저 또한 선생님을 귀히 여길 수밖에 없는지라). 부디 그 사악한 여자들과는 가까이하지 말아주시기를 청합니다. 선생님은 젊은 신사라, 그들의 교묘한 술책을 절반도 모르시잖아요. 제 집을 두고 제가 드린 말씀에 노여워하지 마세요, 선생님. 그것이 제 가엾고 사랑하는 딸들의 파멸이 되리라는 걸 선생님도 분명 아실 거예요. 게다가 선생님, 올워디 씨 본인께서도 제가 그런 일들을, 더구나 선생님과 관련하여 눈감아 주는 것을 결코 용서치 않으시리라는 걸 모르실 리 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"정말이지요, 부인.\" 존스가 말했다. \"더는 사과하실 것 없습니다. 또 부인께서 하신 말씀을 조금도 언짢게 여기지도 않고요. 다만, 올워디 씨를 저보다 더 귀히 여길 사람은 없을 터이니, 어쩌면 그분의 명예에 썩 좋지만은 않을 한 가지 오해에서 부인을 풀어드리고자 합니다. 단언컨대 저는 그분의 친척이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"아아! 선생님,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"친척이 아니라는 것, 저도 압니다. 선생님이 누구신지 아주 잘 알아요. 올워디 씨가 제게 다 말씀해 주셨거든요. 하지만 분명히 말씀드리지만, 선생님이 설령 스무 번 그분의 친아들이었다 한들, 제 앞에서 그분이 누차 보이신 그 마음보다 더한 애정을 보이실 수는 없었을 거예요. 선생님의 처지를 부끄러워하실 것 없어요. 약속하건대 어떤 선량한 사람도 그 때문에 선생님을 덜 귀하게 여기지는 않을 테니까요. 아니지요, 존스 씨, '떳떳지 못한 출생'이라는 말은, 제 사랑하고 사랑하는 남편이 늘 말했듯이, 헛소리일 뿐이에요. 그 '떳떳지 못한'이라는 말이 부모에게 붙는 것이라면 모를까요. 아이들이야, 자기들과는 전혀 무관한, 자기들이 아무 죄 없는 어떤 행위로부터 진짜 불명예를 떠안을 까닭이 없으니까요.\"\n\n여기서 존스는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 말했다. \"부인께서 저를 정말로 알고 계시고, 올워디 씨가 제 이름을 부인께 말씀하시는 것이 옳다고 여기셨음을 이제 알았으니, 또 부인께서 당신 자신의 사정을 제게 그토록 솔직히 털어놓으셨으니, 저도 제 자신에 관한 몇 가지 사정을 더 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 밀러 부인이 그 이야기를 듣고 싶다는 큰 바람과 호기심을 드러내자, 그는 이야기를 시작하여 자신의 내력을 처음부터 끝까지 들려주었다. 다만 소피아의 이름만은 단 한 번도 입에 올리지 않았다.\n\n정직한 마음들 사이에는 일종의 공감이 있어서, 그것을 통해 서로의 말을 쉽게 믿어준다. 밀러 부인은 존스가 들려준 이야기를 모두 사실로 믿었고, 그를 향해 깊은 연민과 걱정을 드러냈다. 그녀가 그 이야기에 대해 이런저런 말을 보태려는 참이었으나 존스가 그 말을 가로막았다. 약속한 시각이 이제 가까워졌기에, 그는 그날 저녁 그 부인과 한 번 더 만나게 해달라고 청하기 시작했던 것이다. 그러면서 이번이 그녀의 집에서 갖는 마지막 만남이 될 것이라 약속했고, 동시에 그 부인은 지체 높은 분이며 둘 사이에는 더없이 결백한 일 외에는 아무것도 오가지 않으리라고 맹세했다. 그리고 나는 그가 그 말을 지킬 작정이었음을 굳게 믿는다.\n\n밀러 부인은 마침내 설득되었고, 존스는 자기 방으로 물러갔다. 그곳에서 그는 열두 시까지 홀로 앉아 있었으나, 벨라스턴 부인은 끝내 나타나지 않았다.\n\n앞서 이 부인이 존스에게 깊은 애정을 품고 있다고 말했고, 또 실제로 그러했음이 분명히 드러났을 터이니, 독자는 어쩌면 그녀가 첫 약속부터 어긴 것을 의아하게 여길지도 모르겠다. 더구나 그녀는 그가 병으로 발이 묶여 있다고 짐작하고 있었는데, 그런 때야말로 우정이 그런 방문을 가장 절실히 요구하는 법이니 말이다. 그러므로 이 부인의 처신을 두고 더러는 부자연스럽다 탓할 수도 있겠다. 하지만 그것은 우리 잘못이 아니다. 우리의 소임은 다만 진실을 기록하는 것뿐이니까.\n\n# Chapter vi.\n\n모든 독자의 마음을 움직이리라 믿어 의심치 않는 한 장면을 담은 이야기.\n\n존스 씨는 그날 밤 초저녁 내내 한순간도 눈을 붙이지 못했다. 그러나 그것은 벨라스턴 부인에게 바람을 맞아 생긴 불편함 때문이 아니었다. 깨어 있던 시간의 대부분을 마땅히 그녀 몫으로 돌려야 할 소피아조차도, 이번만큼은 그의 잠을 쫓아낸 원인이 아니었다. 사실 가엾은 존스는 이 세상에 살아 있는 사람 가운데 가장 천성이 따뜻한 축에 드는 인물이었고, 연민이라 불리는 그 모든 나약함을 지니고 있었다. 그 나약함이야말로 이 불완전한 성품을, 마치 사람을 제 안으로 둥글게 말아 넣어 잘 닦인 공처럼 굴려, 남에게 닥치는 재앙에 단 한 번도 발이 묶이지 않은 채 세상을 굴러가게 하는 저 고결한 마음의 단단함과 구별 짓는 것이었다. 그러니 그는 가엾은 낸시의 처지를 동정하지 않을 수 없었다. 나이팅게일 씨를 향한 그녀의 사랑이 그에게는 너무도 뚜렷이 보였기에, 그는 그 어미의 눈먼 둔함에 놀랐다. 그 어미는 바로 전날 저녁만 해도 그에게 딸의 성정이 크게 변했다고 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 말했던 것이다. \"세상에서 가장 활기차고 명랑한 아이들 가운데 하나였던 애가,\" 그녀의 말로는, \"갑자기 온통 우울하고 침울한 아이가 되어 버렸다\"는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 마침내 잠은 모든 저항을 이겨 냈다. 그리고 이제, 옛사람들이 상상했듯 마치 그 자신이 이미 하나의 신, 그것도 노여워하는 신이라도 된 양, 그는 비싼 값을 치르고 얻은 그 승리를 음미하는 듯했다. 비유 없이 단순하게 말하자면, 존스 씨는 이튿날 아침 열한 시까지 잤고, 만일 격렬한 소란이 그를 깨우지 않았더라면 어쩌면 그 고요한 상태를 훨씬 더 오래 이어 갔을 것이다.\n\n이내 파트리지가 불려 왔고, 무슨 일이냐는 물음에 그는 이렇게 답했다. \"아래층에서 무시무시한 난리가 났습니다. 낸시 양은 발작을 일으켰고, 다른 자매와 어머니는 둘 다 그 곁에서 울며 슬퍼하고 있습니다.\" 존스는 이 소식에 깊은 걱정을 드러냈다. 파트리지는 그 걱정을 덜어 주려는 듯 빙그레 웃으며 말했다. \"그 아가씨가 죽을 위험에 처한 것은 아닌 듯합니다. 수전이\"—이것이 하녀의 이름이었다—\"제게 말해 준 바로는, 그저 흔해 빠진 일에 지나지 않는다고 하더군요. 한마디로,\" 그가 말했다, \"낸시 양은 제 어머니만큼이나 영리해지고 싶었던 모양입니다. 그게 다입니다. 보아하니 좀 시장하셨던지, 식전 기도도 올리기 전에 자리에 앉아 저녁을 잡수신 거지요. 그래서 업둥이 보호원으로 갈 아이가 하나 생기게 된 겁니다.\" \"제발 그 어리석은 농담 좀 그만두게,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"이 가엾은 사람들의 비참함이 우스갯거리란 말인가? 당장 밀러 부인께 가서 내가 청을 드린다고 전하게—아니, 기다리게, 자네는 또 무슨 실수를 저지를 게야. 내가 직접 가겠네. 부인께서 함께 아침을 들자고 청하셨으니.\" 그러고 나서 그는 일어나 할 수 있는 한 서둘러 옷을 입었다. 그가 옷을 입는 동안 파트리지는 호된 꾸지람을 여러 번 듣고도, 이런 판에 흔히 농담이라 불리는 어떤 야비한 말들을 던지는 것을 끝내 참지 못했다. 존스는 옷을 입자마자 아래층으로 내려가 문을 두드렸고, 곧 하녀의 안내를 받아 바깥 응접실로 들었다. 그곳은 손님이 없는 만큼이나 식사 채비라곤 아무것도 없이 텅 비어 있었다. 밀러 부인은 안쪽 방에서 딸과 함께 있었는데, 이내 하녀가 거기서 존스 씨에게 전갈을 가져왔다. \"마님께서 약속을 못 지키게 된 것을 부디 너그러이 용서해 주십사 하십니다. 뜻밖의 일이 생겨 오늘 아침은 함께 식사하는 기쁨을 누릴 수 없게 되었고, 더 일찍 기별을 올리지 못한 것을 거듭 사죄드린다 하십니다.\" 존스는 부탁했다. \"약속을 못 지킨 것 같은 사소한 일로는 조금도 마음 쓰지 마시라고 전해 주게. 나는 그 사연이 진심으로 안타깝네. 그리고 내가 부인께 무슨 도움이라도 될 수 있다면, 무엇이든 분부만 하시라 하게.\"\n\n그가 이 말을 채 마치기도 전에, 그 말을 모두 듣고 있던 밀러 부인이 갑자기 문을 활짝 열고 눈물을 쏟으며 그에게로 나와 말했다. \"오, 존스 씨! 당신은 분명 세상에 살아 있는 가장 훌륭한 젊은이 가운데 한 분이세요. 도움을 베풀어 주시겠다는 그 다정한 말씀에 천 번이라도 감사드립니다. 하지만, 아아! 선생님, 제 가엾은 딸을 구하는 것만은 선생님의 힘으로도 어쩌실 수가 없답니다. 오, 내 아이! 내 아이! 그 애는 끝장났어요, 영영 망가지고 말았어요!\" \"부디, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다, \"어떤 악당이……\" \"오, 존스 씨!\" 그녀가 말했다, \"어제 제 하숙을 떠난 그 악당이 제 가엾은 딸을 배신했어요. 그 애를 망쳐 놓았답니다. 저는 선생님이 명예를 아는 분임을 압니다. 선생님께는 선한, 고결한 마음이 있으세요, 존스 씨. 제가 직접 목격한 그 행동들은 다른 어떤 마음에서도 나올 수 없는 것이었지요. 모두 말씀드리겠어요. 아니, 사실 이렇게 일이 터진 마당에 비밀로 묻어 둘 수도 없는 노릇이지요. 그 나이팅게일, 그 잔인한 악당이 제 딸을 망쳐 놓았어요. 그 애는—그 애는—오! 존스 씨, 제 딸이 그자의 아이를 가졌답니다. 그래 놓고 그자는 그런 처지에 놓인 그 애를 내버린 거예요. 여기! 여기 있어요, 선생님, 이게 그자의 모진 편지예요. 읽어 보세요, 존스 씨, 그리고 이런 괴물이 또 어디 있을지 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n편지는 다음과 같았다.\n\n\"사랑하는 낸시,\n나로서는 도저히 입에 올릴 수 없는 일을 — 그대에게도 나 못지않게 충격일 것이 두려운 그 일을 — 전하기 위해 이 방법을 택했소. 내 아버지께서, 나를 위해 마련해 두신 재산 있는 어느 젊은 처자에게 내가 당장 구애에 나서야 한다고 고집하시오. 그 가증스러운 말을 굳이 적을 필요는 없겠지. 그대의 뛰어난 분별력이라면, 내가 이 복종에 얼마나 송두리째 매여 있는지 절감하게 될 거요. 그 복종으로 인해 나는 그대의 그리운 품에서 영영 떨어져 나가게 될 것이오. 그대 어머니의 다정함이라면, 우리 사랑의 그 불행한 결과를 그분께 털어놓도록 그대를 북돋워 줄지도 모르겠소. 그 일은 세상에 숨기기도 어렵지 않을 것이고, 나는 그대를 보살피듯 그 일도 책임지고 마련해 두겠소. 부디 이 일로 그대가 겪는 고통이 내가 겪은 것보다 덜하기를 바라오. 그러나 가진 모든 의연함을 끌어모아 그대 자신을 추스르고, 닥쳐올 파멸이 뻔히 보이지 않았던들 결코 이 편지를 쓰지 않았을 이 사내를 용서하고 잊어 주오. 나를 잊으라 하지만, 어디까지나 연인으로서만 잊으라는 뜻이오. 그러나 변함없는, 비록 불행한 그대의 벗으로서는, 그대가 언제까지나 가장 좋은 벗을 내게서 찾게 될 것이오.\n\nJ. N.\"\n존스가 이 편지를 다 읽고 나자, 두 사람은 잠시 동안 말없이 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다. 마침내 그가 입을 열었다. \"부인, 제가 읽은 것에 얼마나 큰 충격을 받았는지 이루 말로 다 할 수가 없습니다. 그래도 한 가지만은, 부디 이 편지를 쓴 자의 조언을 따르시기를 간청드립니다. 따님의 평판을 생각하셔야 합니다.\" \"그건 이미 떠나 버렸어요, 사라져 버렸답니다, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 외쳤다, \"그 애의 순결과 마찬가지로요. 그 애는 손님들로 가득 찬 방에서 그 편지를 받았고, 봉투를 뜯자마자 그 자리에서 까무러쳐서, 그 안에 담긴 내용이 거기 있던 사람들 모두에게 알려지고 말았어요. 하지만 평판을 잃은 것이, 그것이 아무리 끔찍해도, 가장 나쁜 일은 아니에요. 저는 제 아이를 잃게 될 거예요. 그 애는 벌써 두 번이나 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했어요. 지금까지는 가까스로 막았지만, 이 일을 안고서는 살아남지 않겠다고 맹세하고 있답니다. 그리고 저 자신도 그런 일을 당하고서는 살아남을 수가 없을 거예요. 그러면 제 어린 베치는, 의지할 데 없는 그 핏덩이 고아는 어찌 되겠어요? 그 가엾은 어린것은, 까닭도 모른 채 제 언니와 제가 슬픔에 미쳐 가는 그 비참한 꼴을 보다가, 제 생각엔, 가슴이 미어져 버릴 거예요. 오, 그 애는 더없이 영민하고 마음씨 고운 작은 아이랍니다! 그 야만스럽고 잔인한 자가 우리 모두를 망쳐 놓았어요. 오, 내 가엾은 아이들! 이것이 제 그 모든 정성의 보답인가요? 이것이 제 모든 바람의 열매인가요? 제가 어미의 그 모든 수고와 의무를 그토록 기꺼이 감당해 온 것이, 그 애들의 어린 시절을 그토록 애틋이 보살피고, 그 애들의 교육을 그토록 정성껏 챙긴 것이, 그 애들에게 변변찮으나마 먹을거리라도 마련해 주려고 제 한 몸 누릴 편의마저 마다하며 그 숱한 세월을 애써 온 것이, 결국 그 가운데 하나를, 아니 둘 다를 이런 식으로 잃기 위해서였단 말인가요?\" \"참으로, 부인,\" 존스가 두 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 말했다, \"저는 영혼 깊은 곳에서 부인을 가엾이 여깁니다.\" \"오! 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"저는 선생님의 마음이 얼마나 선한지 알지만, 선생님조차도 제가 지금 느끼는 것이 어떤 것인지는 짐작도 못 하실 거예요. 가장 착하고, 가장 다정하고, 가장 효성스러운 아이였답니다! 오, 내 가엾은 낸시, 내 영혼의 사랑이여! 내 두 눈의 기쁨이여! 내 가슴의 자랑이여! 정말이지 너무나 큰 제 자랑거리였어요. 그 애의 아름다움에서 비롯된 그 어리석고 욕심 사나운 기대들 때문에 제가 그 애를 망친 것이니까요. 아아! 저는 이 젊은이가 그 애에게 보이는 호감을 기쁘게 바라보았어요. 저는 그것이 떳떳한 애정이라 여겼고, 그 애가 그토록 격이 높은 사람과 혼인하는 것을 보게 되리라는 생각으로 제 어리석은 허영을 부풀렸지요. 그자는 제 앞에서 천 번도 넘게, 아니 선생님 앞에서도 종종, 사심 없는 사랑의 더없이 너그러운 말들로 이 기대를 어르고 북돋우려 했어요. 그 말들은 늘 제 가엾은 딸을 향한 것이었고, 그 애도 저도 그것을 진심이라 믿었답니다. 이것이 그저 제 아이의 순결을 배신하고 우리 모두를 파멸시키려고 놓아 둔 덫일 뿐이었다고, 제가 어찌 믿을 수 있었겠어요?\" 이 말이 끝날 즈음 어린 베치가 방으로 달려 들어오며 외쳤다. \"엄마, 제발 언니한테 와 주세요. 또 발작을 일으켜서 사촌 언니가 붙잡지를 못해요.\" 밀러 부인은 곧장 그 부름에 따랐으나, 먼저 베치에게는 존스 씨와 함께 있으라 이르고, 그에게 몇 분만 아이를 돌봐 달라 부탁하며 더없이 애처로운 목소리로 말했다. \"오, 하느님! 적어도 제 아이 하나만이라도 지켜 주소서.\"\n\n존스는 이 부탁에 따라, 비록 그 자신도 사실 밀러 부인의 사연에 무척이나 마음이 흔들리고 있었지만, 어린 소녀를 달래기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다했다. 그는 아이에게 말했다. \"네 언니는 곧 다시 좋아질 거란다. 네가 그렇게 슬퍼하면 언니를 더 나쁘게 할 뿐 아니라, 어머니까지 병나게 할 거야.\" \"정말이에요, 아저씨,\" 아이가 말했다, \"저는 무슨 일이 있어도 그분들을 아프게 할 짓은 하지 않을 거예요. 제가 우는 걸 그분들이 보느니 차라리 가슴이 터지는 게 나아요. 하지만 가엾은 우리 언니는 제가 우는 걸 볼 수가 없어요. 언니가 다시는 제가 우는 걸 볼 수 없게 될까 봐 무서워요. 정말이지 저는 언니랑 헤어질 수 없어요. 정말 그럴 수 없어요. 그리고 가엾은 엄마는 또 어찌 되시려고요? 엄마도 죽어서 저를 두고 떠나겠다고 하시는데, 저는 결코 혼자 남지 않을 거예요.\" \"그럼 너는 죽는 게 두렵지 않니, 우리 어린 베치?\" 존스가 말했다. \"두려워요,\" 아이가 대답했다, \"저는 늘 죽는 게 두려웠어요. 죽으면 엄마랑 언니를 두고 떠나야 하니까요. 하지만 제가 사랑하는 사람들과 함께라면 어디로 가든 두렵지 않아요.\"\n\n존스는 이 대답이 어찌나 흐뭇했던지 아이에게 열렬히 입을 맞추었다. 곧이어 밀러 부인이 돌아오며 말했다. \"이제 낸시가 정신을 차렸으니 하느님께 감사할 따름이에요. 자, 베치,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"너는 들어가 보렴. 언니가 좀 나아져서 너를 몹시 보고 싶어 하니까.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 존스에게로 돌아서서, 그에게 아침 식사를 대접하기로 한 약속을 못 지킨 것에 대해 다시금 사죄의 말을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"부인,\" 존스가 말했다, \"저는 부인께서 차려 주셨을 그 어떤 것보다 더 진귀한 한 끼를 누리게 될 듯합니다. 제가 이 작고 사랑스러운 가족에게 조금이라도 도움이 될 수 있다면, 분명 그렇게 되리라 단언합니다. 하지만 제 노력에 어떤 결과가 따르든, 저는 그 일을 시도해 보기로 마음먹었습니다. 만일, 이런 일이 벌어졌음에도, 나이팅게일 씨가 마음속 깊은 곳에 따뜻한 선의를, 그리고 따님을 향한 매우 격렬한 애정을 품고 있지 않다면, 그것은 제가 그 사람을 크게 잘못 본 것이겠지요. 만일 그렇다면, 제가 그 앞에 펼쳐 보일 이 그림이 그의 마음을 움직이리라 생각합니다. 부인, 부디 부인 자신과 낸시 양을 할 수 있는 한 추스르도록 애써 보십시오. 저는 당장 나이팅게일 씨를 찾아 나서겠습니다. 그리고 부인께 좋은 소식을 가져다드리기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n밀러 부인은 무릎을 꿇고 하늘의 온갖 축복이 존스 씨에게 내리기를 빌었으며, 거기에 더해 더없이 간절한 감사의 말을 쏟아 냈다. 그러고 나서 그는 나이팅게일 씨를 찾으러 떠났고, 그 착한 부인은 딸을 위로하러 돌아갔다. 딸은 어머니가 전해 준 말에 다소 기운을 차렸고, 두 사람은 함께 존스 씨에 대한 칭송을 거듭거듭 울려 퍼뜨렸다.\n\n우리가 남에게 베푸는 선이든 악이든, 그것은 흔히 우리 자신에게로 되돌아오는 법이라고 나는 믿는다. 너그러운 성품을 지닌 사람들이 선행의 대상이 되는 이들 못지않게 자신의 선행을 즐기는 것과 마찬가지로, 자기 동료에게 끼친 파멸에 대해 스스로 아무런 아픔도 치르지 않은 채 남을 해칠 수 있을 만큼 완전히 악마 같은 천성을 지닌 사람은 거의 없기 때문이다.\n\n적어도 나이팅게일은 그런 사람이 아니었다. 오히려 존스는 그가 새 거처에서 난롯가에 우울하게 앉아, 가엾은 낸시를 자신이 몰아넣은 그 불행한 처지를 말없이 슬퍼하고 있는 모습을 발견했다. 그는 친구가 나타난 것을 보자마자 황급히 일어나 맞이했고, 한참 반가운 인사를 나눈 뒤 이렇게 말했다. \"이렇게 고마운 방문이 이보다 더 때맞춰 올 수는 없었을 걸세. 평생 이렇게까지 울적했던 적이 없었거든.\"\n\n\"안타깝게도,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"내가 가져온 소식은 자네를 풀어 주기는커녕, 다른 무엇보다도 자네에게 가장 큰 충격을 줄 거라고 확신하는 그런 소식일세. 그래도 자네가 알아야 할 일이지. 그러니 더 길게 서두를 것 없이 말하겠네, 나이팅게일. 나는 자네가 비참과 파멸로 몰아넣은 어느 훌륭한 가족을 대신해 자네를 찾아온 걸세.\" 나이팅게일은 이 말에 안색이 변했다. 그러나 존스는 그것을 아랑곳하지 않고, 독자가 앞 장에서 익히 알게 된 그 비극적인 이야기를 더없이 생생한 어조로 그려 나갔다.\n\n나이팅게일은 여러 대목에서 격렬한 동요를 드러내면서도 한 번도 이야기를 끊지 않았다. 그러나 이야기가 끝나자 깊은 한숨을 내쉰 뒤 이렇게 말했다. \"자네가 들려준 이야기는, 친구여, 내 마음 가장 여린 곳을 찌르는군. 그 가엾은 아이가 내 편지를 들킨 것보다 더 저주스러운 사고는 없었을 걸세. 그것만 아니었다면 아이의 평판은 무사했을 테고, 이 일도 깊은 비밀로 남았을 텐데. 그랬다면 아이도 조금도 손해 보지 않고 시집갈 수 있었을 거야. 이 도시에선 그런 일이 흔하거든. 설령 남편이 너무 늦게라도 조금 의심을 품는다 해도, 그 의심을 아내한테나 세상한테나 감추는 게 그로선 더 현명한 처신일 테고.\"\n\n\"아닐세, 친구여,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"자네의 가엾은 낸시는 그럴 수 없는 처지일세. 자네가 그 아이의 마음을 너무도 완전히 사로잡아 버려서, 그 아이를 괴롭히는 건 평판을 잃는 것이 아니라 자네를 잃는 것이고, 그것이 결국 그 아이와 가족 모두의 파멸로 끝나고 말 거야.\" \"아니, 그 점이라면 내 장담하지,\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"그 아이가 내 마음을 어찌나 송두리째 차지하고 있는지, 장차 내 아내가 될 사람이 누구든 거기서 가질 몫은 아주 보잘것없을 걸세.\" \"그렇다면 어떻게,\" 존스가 말했다. \"그 아이를 저버릴 생각을 할 수 있단 말인가?\" \"그럼 내가 어쩌겠나?\" 상대가 대답했다. \"낸시 양에게 물어보게.\" 존스가 열을 띠며 대꾸했다. \"자네가 그 아이를 몰아넣은 처지를 생각하면, 나는 진심으로 자네가 어떤 보상을 해야 할지 그 아이가 정해야 한다고 보네. 자네의 이익이 아니라 오직 그 아이의 이익만이 자네가 고려해야 할 전부일세. 하지만 자네가 어떻게 해야 하느냐고 내게 묻는다면,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그 아이 가족의 기대와 그 아이 자신의 기대를 채워 주는 것보다 더 못한 일을 어떻게 할 수 있단 말인가? 아니, 솔직히 말하지. 자네 두 사람이 함께 있는 걸 처음 본 그 순간부터 그건 내 기대이기도 했네. 저 가엾은 사람들에게 측은한 마음이 동한 나머지, 자네가 베풀어 준 우정을 빌미로 주제넘게 구는 걸 용서해 주게. 그러나 자네 행실로 어머니까지, 딸까지 자네가 떳떳한 뜻을 품고 있다고 믿게 만들 작정은 결코 없었는지, 그건 자네 마음이 가장 잘 일러 줄 걸세. 만약 그랬다면, 비록 이 일에 결혼을 직접 약속한 적은 없다 하더라도, 자네가 어디까지 책임을 다해야 하는지는 자네의 분별에 맡기겠네.\"\n\n\"아니, 자네가 넌지시 비춘 것을 인정해야 할 뿐 아니라,\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"자네가 말한 바로 그 약속마저 내가 한 것 같아 두렵네.\" \"그걸 시인하고서도,\" 존스가 말했다. \"자네가 한순간이라도 망설일 수 있단 말인가?\" \"생각해 보게, 친구여,\" 상대가 대답했다. \"나는 자네가 명예를 아는 사람이며, 누구에게도 명예의 규칙에 어긋나게 행동하라 권하지 않을 사람임을 아네. 다른 반대 이유가 전혀 없다 해도, 그 아이의 수치가 이렇게 세상에 알려진 마당에, 내가 그런 결합을 명예롭게 여기며 받아들일 수 있겠나?\" \"물론이지,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"그리고 가장 훌륭하고 참된 명예, 곧 선함이야말로 자네에게 그것을 요구하네. 자네가 이런 종류의 거리낌을 입에 올렸으니, 그것을 한번 따져 보게 해 주게. 거짓된 구실로 한 젊은 여인과 그 가족을 속이고, 그 수법으로 배신스럽게 그녀의 순결을 앗아 가고도 명예로울 수 있겠나? 한 인간의 파멸을, 알면서, 일부러, 아니 교묘히 꾸며 낸 장본인이고도 명예로울 수 있겠나? 이 가여운 사람의 명예와 평안을, 아니 어쩌면 목숨과 영혼까지 무너뜨리고도 명예로울 수 있겠나? 이 가여운 사람이 여리고, 의지할 데 없고, 무방비한 젊은 여인이라는 생각을 명예가 견딜 수 있겠나? 자네를 사랑하고, 자네에게 빠져 있고, 자네 때문에 죽어 가며, 자네의 약속에 더할 나위 없는 신뢰를 걸고, 그 신뢰를 위해 자기에게 소중한 모든 것을 바친 젊은 여인 말일세. 명예가 이런 생각들을 한순간이라도 떠받칠 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"상식으로 따지면,\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"자네 말이 다 옳네. 하지만 세상의 통념이 그와는 정반대라는 것 또한 자네가 잘 알지 않나. 내가 비록 내 손으로 그렇게 만든 여자라 해도 화냥년을 아내로 맞는다면, 다시는 얼굴을 들고 다니기가 부끄러울 걸세.\"\n\n\"이런, 나이팅게일!\" 존스가 말했다. \"그 아이를 그렇게 야박한 이름으로 부르지 말게. 자네가 결혼을 약속한 순간 그 아이는 자네의 아내가 된 걸세. 그리고 그 아이가 죄를 지었다면 그것은 덕에 대해서라기보다 분별에 대해서네. 게다가 자네가 얼굴 들기 부끄러워하는 그 세상이라는 게 비열하고 어리석고 방탕한 자들 말고 또 무엇이란 말인가? 내 말을 용서하게만, 그런 부끄러움이란 거짓 겸손에서 나오는 것이 틀림없고, 그 거짓 겸손은 거짓 명예를 그림자처럼 늘 따라다니는 법일세. 그러나 나는 단언하네. 이 세상에 참된 분별과 선함을 지닌 사람치고 자네의 그 행동을 기리고 갈채하지 않을 자는 없을 걸세. 설령 다른 누구도 그러지 않는다 해도, 자네 자신의 마음만은, 친구여, 그것을 기리지 않겠나? 정직하고 고결하고 너그럽고 자애로운 행동을 했다는 의식에서 우러나는 그 뜨겁고 황홀한 감정이, 수백만 명의 받을 자격 없는 칭찬보다 우리 마음에 더 큰 기쁨을 가져다주지 않겠나? 두 갈래 길을 자네 눈앞에 공평하게 펼쳐 놓게. 한쪽에는 이 가엾고 불행하고 여리고 잘 믿는 아이가 비참한 어머니의 품에 안겨 마지막 숨을 거두는 모습이 있네. 단장의 고통 속에 자네 이름을 한숨으로 토해 내며, 자기를 파멸로 짓누르는 그 잔인함을 원망하기보다 슬퍼하는 그 무너지는 가슴 소리를 들어 보게. 사랑스러운 딸을 잃고 미쳐 버리거나, 어쩌면 죽음에 이르고 마는 그 애틋하고 절망에 빠진 어머니의 처지를 자네 상상 속에 그려 보게. 가엾고 의지할 데 없는 고아 아기를 떠올려 보게. 그리고 그런 광경에 자네 마음이 단 한순간만이라도 머물거든, 이 가엾고 작고 갸륵하고 무방비한 가족이 송두리째 무너진 그 모든 원인이 바로 자네임을 헤아려 보게. 다른 한쪽에는, 자네가 그들을 당장의 고통에서 건져 내는 모습을 떠올려 보게. 그 사랑스러운 아이가 얼마나 큰 기쁨과 환희로 자네 품에 달려들지 생각해 보게. 핏기가 그 창백한 뺨에 다시 돌고, 생기가 그 시든 눈에 다시 살아나며, 황홀함이 그 괴로워하던 가슴에 차오르는 모습을 보게. 그 어머니의 환희를, 모두의 행복을 헤아려 보게. 자네의 단 한 번의 행동으로 이 작은 가족이 더없이 행복해지는 광경을 그려 보게. 이 두 갈래 길을 떠올려 보게. 이 가엾은 이들을 영원히 가라앉혀 버릴지, 아니면 단 한 번의 너그럽고 고결한 결심으로 그들 모두를 비참과 절망의 벼랑 끝에서 인간 행복의 가장 높은 자리로 끌어올릴지, 그것을 정하는 데 오랜 숙고가 필요하다면 나는 내 친구를 잘못 본 것이 분명하네. 여기에 한 가지만 더 생각해 보태게. 그렇게 하는 것이 자네의 의무라는 사실 말일세. 자네가 이 가엾은 사람들을 건져 줄 그 비참함이란, 바로 자네 자신이 그들에게 일부러 안겨 준 비참함이라는 사실 말일세.\"\n\n\"오, 사랑하는 친구여!\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"나를 일깨우는 데 자네의 그 웅변까지는 필요치 않았네. 나는 가엾은 낸시를 진심으로 가엾이 여기며, 우리 사이에 그런 친밀한 일이 결코 없었던 일이 될 수만 있다면 내 힘닿는 무엇이라도 기꺼이 내놓겠네. 아니, 내 말을 믿게. 저 불행한 가족에 온갖 비참을 불러온 그 모진 편지를 쓰자고 스스로를 다잡기까지, 나는 내 욕정과 숱하게 싸웠네. 만약 내가 헤아려야 할 마음이 오직 내 마음뿐이라면, 당장 내일 아침에라도 그 아이와 결혼하겠네. 하늘에 맹세코 그러겠어! 하지만 우리 아버지가 그런 혼사에 동의하시도록 설득한다는 게 얼마나 불가능한 일인지는 자네도 쉽게 짐작할 걸세. 게다가 아버지는 나를 위해 다른 짝을 정해 두셨고, 내일은 그분의 엄한 분부에 따라 그 아가씨를 찾아뵙기로 되어 있네.\"\n\n\"나는 자네 부친을 알 영광을 갖지 못했네만,\" 존스가 말했다. \"혹 그분을 설득할 수 있다고 치면, 자네 자신은 이 가엾은 사람들을 구할 그 유일한 길에 동의하겠나?\" \"내 행복을 좇듯 간절히 그러겠네,\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"다른 어떤 여자한테서도 나는 결코 그 행복을 찾지 못할 테니까. 오, 사랑하는 친구여! 지난 열두 시간 동안 내가 그 가엾은 아이를 두고 어떤 심정이었는지 자네가 상상할 수만 있다면, 자네의 동정이 온통 그 아이한테만 쏠리지는 않으리라 확신하네. 내 마음은 오직 그 아이만을 향해 있어. 그리고 내게 명예에 대한 어리석은 거리낌이 조금이라도 있었다면, 자네가 그것을 말끔히 풀어 주었네. 아버지를 내 뜻에 따르도록 이끌 수만 있다면, 내 자신의 행복도, 내 낸시의 행복도 부족할 것이 없을 걸세.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 내가 그 일을 맡기로 마음먹었네,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이 일을 어떤 식으로 펼쳐 보일 필요가 생기든, 나한테 화를 내선 안 되네. 장담컨대 그렇게라도 하지 않으면 이 일이 그분께 오래도록 감춰질 수는 없으니까. 이런 종류의 일은 한번 밖으로 새어 나가면 순식간에 퍼지는 법이고, 안타깝게도 이미 그렇게 되어 버렸네. 게다가 당장 막지 않으면, 내 영혼을 걸고 두렵네만, 어떤 끔찍한 사고라도 뒤따를 경우 세상이 자네 이름을 입에 올려 떠들어 댈 텐데, 자네 부친께 사람으로서의 보통 인정이라도 있다면 그것이 그분을 분명 노엽게 할 걸세. 그러니 그 노신사를 어디서 뵐 수 있는지 일러 주면, 나는 한순간도 지체 않고 이 일에 나서겠네. 내가 그 일을 좇는 동안, 자네는 그 가엾은 아이를 찾아가 주는 것보다 더 너그러운 일을 할 수 없을 걸세. 그 가족의 비참함에 대해 내가 들려준 이야기에 조금도 과장이 없었음을 자네도 알게 될 거야.\"\n\n나이팅게일은 그 제안에 즉시 동의했다. 그러고는 존스에게 아버지의 거처와, 그를 가장 만나기 쉬울 커피점을 일러 준 뒤, 한순간 머뭇거리다가 이렇게 말했다. \"이보게 톰, 자네는 불가능한 일을 맡으려는 걸세. 우리 아버지를 알면 그분의 동의를 얻겠다는 생각은 아예 하지도 않을 거야. 잠깐, 한 가지 방법이 있네. 자네가 내가 이미 결혼했다고 그분께 말하면 어떻겠나. 일이 다 벌어진 뒤라면 그 사실을 받아들이도록 화해시키기가 더 쉬울지도 모르지. 그리고 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 나는 자네가 한 말에 어찌나 마음이 움직였는지, 그리고 내 낸시를 어찌나 뜨겁게 사랑하는지, 결과야 어찌 되든 거의 그 일이 다 벌어져 버렸으면 싶을 지경이네.\"\n\n존스는 그 묘안을 크게 반기며 그대로 따르겠노라 약속했다. 그런 다음 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 나이팅게일은 자신의 낸시를 찾아가러, 존스는 그 노신사를 찾으러.\n\n# Chapter viii.\n\n8장.\n존스와 늙은 나이팅게일 씨 사이에 오간 일과, 이 이야기에 아직 등장하지 않은 한 인물의 도래에 관하여.\n\n운명의 신성함을 부정한 로마 풍자시인의 견해, 그리고 같은 취지의 세네카의 의견에도 불구하고, 내 생각에 두 사람보다 더 현명한 인물이었던 키케로는 정반대의 입장을 분명히 견지한다. 또한 인생에는 너무도 기이하고 설명할 길 없는 사건들이 있어서, 그것을 빚어내는 데에 인간의 솜씨와 예지 이상의 무언가가 필요해 보인다는 것만큼은 분명한 사실이다.\n\n이번에 존스에게 일어난 일이 바로 그런 부류였다. 그는 늙은 나이팅게일 씨를 너무도 절묘한 순간에 만났으니, 만일 운명이 정말로 로마에서 받았던 그 모든 숭배를 받을 자격이 있었다 한들, 이런 또 하나의 상황을 꾸며낼 수는 없었을 것이다. 요컨대 이 노신사와, 그가 아들의 짝으로 점찍어 둔 젊은 처녀의 아버지는 여러 시간 동안 격렬하게 흥정을 벌였고, 후자는 방금 막 자리를 떴으며, 전자는 오랜 다툼에서 자신이 이겼다는 생각에 흡족해하며 남아 있었다. 그 다툼이란 장차 신부와 신랑이 될 두 집안의 두 아버지 사이에 벌어진 것으로, 둘 다 상대를 한 수 앞지르려 애썼고, 그런 경우 드물지 않게 일어나듯 양쪽 모두 승리를 거두었다고 더없이 만족하며 물러난 터였다.\n\n존스 씨가 이번에 방문한 이 신사는 사람들이 흔히 말하는 세상물정에 밝은 사람이었다. 다시 말해 이 세상 말고 다른 세상은 없다고 철석같이 믿고서, 이 세상을 최대한 활용하기로 작정한 사람처럼 처신하는 인물이었다. 젊은 시절 그는 장사를 배웠으나, 꽤 넉넉한 재산을 모은 뒤로는 최근 사업에서 손을 떼었다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하자면 물건을 거래하던 일을 오로지 돈만 거래하는 일로 바꾼 것이다. 그는 늘 손안에 풍족한 자금을 굴렸고, 그것으로 풍족한 이득을 챙기는 법을 아주 잘 알았다. 때로는 개인의 곤궁을 이용했고, 때로는 공공의 곤궁을 이용했다. 실로 그는 오로지 돈하고만 대화하며 살아온 터라, 이 세상에 정말로 존재하는 다른 무엇이 있다고 그가 상상이나 했을지 의심스러울 지경이다. 적어도 한 가지만큼은 확실히 단언할 수 있으니, 그는 돈 말고는 그 무엇에도 진정한 가치가 있다고는 굳게 믿지 않았다는 점이다.\n\n독자께서는, 운명이 존스 씨가 성공의 가망을 품고 들이대기에 이보다 더 부적절한 인물을 골라낼 수 없었으며, 이 변덕스러운 여신이 이 공략을 이보다 더 때 나쁜 시기로 잡을 수도 없었으리라는 데 동의하시리라 생각한다.\n\n이 신사의 머릿속에는 늘 돈이 가장 위에 있었던 만큼, 자기 집 문 안에 낯선 사람이 들어선 순간 곧바로 그의 상상에 떠오르는 생각은, 그 낯선 자가 돈을 가져다주러 왔거나 아니면 돈을 받아 가려고 왔다는 것이었다. 그리고 이 두 생각 가운데 어느 쪽이 우세하느냐에 따라, 그는 다가오는 인물에 대해 호의적인 인상 또는 비호의적인 인상을 품었다.\n\n존스에게는 불운하게도, 지금은 후자의 생각이 우세한 참이었다. 바로 전날 어떤 젊은 신사가 그의 아들이 진 노름빚 청구서를 들고 찾아왔던 터라, 그는 존스를 처음 본 순간 또 그런 용건으로 온 줄 알고 지레 짐작했다. 그리하여 존스가 아들 일로 찾아왔다고 말하기가 무섭게, 노신사는 자신의 의심이 확인된 양 버럭 소리를 질렀다. \"헛수고하러 오셨구먼.\" \"그렇다면, 선생님,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"제 용건을 짐작하실 수 있단 말씀입니까?\" \"내가 짐작한다면 말이지,\" 상대가 대꾸했다. \"다시 한 번 말하지만, 헛수고하시는 거요. 뭐, 짐작건대 당신도 내 아들을 그 모든 난동과 방탕의 자리로 끌어들여 결국 그 애를 파멸시킬 그 한량들 중 하나겠지. 하지만 분명히 말해 두는데, 나는 그 애 빚을 더는 한 푼도 갚지 않을 거요. 앞으로 그 애가 그런 무리와는 완전히 손을 끊기를 바라오. 그렇지 않을 거라 짐작했더라면 그 애 아내를 마련해 주지도 않았을 테니까. 나는 누구의 파멸에도 손을 보태고 싶지 않으니 말이오.\" \"어떻게 된 일입니까, 선생님,\" 존스가 말했다. \"그러면 그 아가씨는 선생님께서 마련해 주신 짝입니까?\" \"그게 말이오, 선생,\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"어째서 그게 당신이 상관할 일이 된단 말이오?\" \"아니, 선생님,\" 존스가 받았다. \"제가 그토록 크게 존경하고 아끼는 분, 곧 선생님 아드님의 행복에 관한 일에 관심을 두는 것을 노여워하지 말아 주십시오. 바로 그 일 때문에 선생님을 뵈러 온 것입니다. 방금 하신 말씀이 제게 얼마나 큰 만족을 주었는지 이루 다 말할 수 없습니다. 정말이지 선생님 아드님은 제가 더없이 높이 존경하는 분입니다. 아니, 선생님, 선생님께 품은 제 존경 또한 쉬이 표현하기 어렵습니다. 어쩌면 그토록 너그럽고, 그토록 선량하며, 그토록 다정하고, 그토록 아낌없이 아드님께 이런 짝을 마련해 주실 수 있단 말입니까. 제가 감히 맹세컨대, 그 여인은 아드님을 이 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람 중 하나로 만들어 줄 것입니다.\"\n\n사람을 우리 호감 속으로 그토록 행복하게 끌어들이는 것은, 처음 마주쳤을 때 그에게서 어떤 경계심을 품었던 경우만 한 게 거의 없다. 일단 그 불안이 사라지기 시작하면 우리는 곧 그것이 불러일으킨 두려움을 잊고, 처음에 우리의 두려움을 자아냈던 바로 그 사람들에게 지금의 안도를 빚지고 있다고 여기게 된다.\n\n나이팅게일에게도 바로 그런 일이 벌어졌다. 그는 존스가 자신이 의심했던 것처럼 자신에게 받아낼 게 없다는 것을 알아차리자마자 그의 존재가 반갑게 느껴지기 시작했다. \"자, 어서,\" 그가 말했다. \"앉으시구려. 전에 당신을 뵌 적은 없는 것 같소만, 당신이 내 아들의 친구이고 이 아가씨에 관해 할 말이 있다면 기꺼이 듣겠소. 그 아가씨가 그 애를 행복하게 해 줄지 어떨지로 말하자면, 그렇게 못 된다면 그건 순전히 그 애 잘못일 거요. 나는 가장 중요한 항목을 챙겨 둠으로써 내 도리를 다했소. 그 아가씨가 가져올 재산은 분별 있고 신중하며 절제할 줄 아는 사람이라면 누구라도 행복하게 만들기에 충분하니 말이오.\" \"틀림없습니다,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그 아가씨 자체가 하나의 재산이니까요. 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 기품 있고, 그토록 상냥하며, 그토록 잘 교육받았으니, 정말이지 더없이 교양 있는 아가씨입니다. 노래도 감탄스러우리만치 잘 부르고, 하프시코드를 다루는 손길도 더없이 섬세합니다.\" \"그런 건 하나도 몰랐소이다,\" 노신사가 대답했다. \"그 아가씨를 본 적이 없으니 말이오. 하지만 당신이 들려준 이야기 때문에 그 아가씨가 더 마음에 안 드는 것은 아니오. 그리고 그 아버지가 우리 흥정에서 그런 자질들을 조금도 내세우지 않은 점이 오히려 더 마음에 들었소. 나는 늘 그것을 그가 사리에 밝다는 증거로 여길 거요. 어리석은 자였다면 그런 항목들을 재산에 보태는 것으로 들먹였을 테지만, 공정하게 말하자면 그는 그런 따위는 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았소. 하기야 그런 게 여자에게 흠이 되는 일이야 분명 아니지만 말이오.\" \"정말이지, 선생님,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그 아가씨는 그 모든 자질을 더없이 빼어난 정도로 갖추고 있습니다. 솔직히 저는 선생님께서 혹시 좀 미적지근하시지 않을까, 이 혼사에 좀 덜 마음이 기우시지 않을까 걱정했습니다. 아드님이 제게 말하기를 선생님께서 그 아가씨를 본 적이 없다고 하셨거든요. 그래서 선생님, 그런 경우라면 제가 간청드리러, 아니 호소드리러 온 것이었습니다. 아드님의 행복을 소중히 여기신다면, 제가 말씀드린 그 모든 미덕뿐 아니라 그 이상을 갖춘 여인과의 혼사를 부디 마다하지 말아 달라고 말입니다.\" \"그게 당신 용건이었다면, 선생,\" 노신사가 말했다. \"우리 둘 다 당신에게 신세를 진 셈이오. 그리고 마음 푹 놓으시오. 내 장담컨대 나는 그 아가씨의 재산에 아주 흡족했으니까.\" \"선생님,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"저는 매 순간 선생님을 점점 더 존경하게 됩니다. 그토록 쉽게 만족하시고, 그 점에 대해 그토록 절도를 지키시는 것은 선생님의 분별이 건전하다는 증거인 동시에 마음이 고귀하다는 증거입니다.\" \"그리 절도 있는 건 아니오, 젊은이, 그리 절도 있는 건 아니라니까,\" 아버지가 대답했다. \"점점 더 고귀하십니다,\" 존스가 받았다. \"그리고 한마디 덧붙이자면, 사려 깊으시기도 합니다. 돈을 행복의 유일한 토대로 여기는 것이야말로 거의 광기에 가까운 일이니까요. 이런 여인이라면, 그 보잘것없는, 그 있으나 마나 한 재산을 가졌다 해도—\" \"보아하니,\" 노신사가 외쳤다. \"여보게, 자네는 돈에 대해 꽤 올바른 생각을 가졌거나, 아니면 그 아가씨의 형편보다 사람됨을 더 잘 아는 모양이군. 그래, 묻겠는데, 자네는 그 아가씨가 재산을 얼마나 가졌다고 생각하나?\" \"재산이요?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"글쎄요, 선생님 아드님 짝으로 입에 올리기엔 너무도 하찮은 액수지요.\" \"글쎄, 글쎄, 글쎄,\" 상대가 말했다. \"어쩌면 그 애가 더 나은 짝을 찾을 수도 있었겠지.\" \"그건 부정합니다,\" 존스가 말했다. \"그 아가씨는 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 여인 중 하나니까요.\" \"그래, 그래, 하지만 내 말은 재산 면에서 말이오,\" 상대가 대답했다. \"그건 그렇고, 자, 자네 친구가 얼마나 가졌으리라 생각하나?\" \"얼마라뇨?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"얼마요? 글쎄, 많아 봐야 아마 이백 파운드쯤이겠지요.\" \"지금 나를 놀리는 게요, 젊은이?\" 아버지가 약간 화를 내며 말했다. \"아닙니다, 맹세코,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"진심입니다. 아니, 오히려 한 푼까지 다 쳐서 최대한으로 말씀드린 것 같습니다. 제가 그 아가씨에게 누를 끼쳤다면 용서를 빌겠습니다.\" \"정말이지 누를 끼쳤소,\" 아버지가 외쳤다. \"내 분명히 아는데 그 아가씨는 그 액수의 쉰 배는 가졌고, 내 아들과 결혼하도록 내가 동의하기 전에 거기에 또 쉰 배를 내놓아야 할 거요.\" \"아니,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이제 와서 동의를 운운하기엔 너무 늦었습니다. 설령 그 아가씨가 쉰 파딩도 없다 한들, 선생님 아드님은 이미 결혼했으니까요.\" \"내 아들이 결혼을 해?\" 노신사가 놀라며 대답했다. \"아니,\" 존스가 말했다. \"저는 선생님께서 알고 계신 줄 알았습니다.\" \"내 아들이 해리스 양과 결혼을 해?\" 그가 다시 말했다. \"해리스 양이라뇨?\" 존스가 말했다. \"아닙니다, 선생님. 낸시 밀러 양과 결혼했습니다. 밀러 부인의 딸인데, 아드님이 그 댁에 하숙하고 있었지요. 그 아가씨는, 비록 그 어머니가 형편이 기울어 하숙을 치기는 하지만—\" \"지금 나를 놀리는 게요, 아니면 진심이오?\" 아버지가 더없이 엄숙한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 선생님,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"저는 남을 놀리는 자라는 평판을 경멸합니다. 저는 더없이 진지하게 진심으로 선생님을 찾아온 것입니다. 제 짐작이—그리고 그게 사실임을 알게 되었습니다만—아드님이 재산 면에서 자기보다 훨씬 못한 짝을 감히 선생님께 알리지 못했을 거라 여겼거든요. 다만 그 아가씨의 평판 때문에 이 일은 더 이상 비밀로 남아 있을 수 없게 되었습니다.\"\n\n아버지가 이 소식에 갑자기 벙어리라도 된 듯 멍하니 서 있는 동안, 한 신사가 방으로 들어와 그를 형제라 부르며 인사했다.\n\n하지만 이 두 사람은 혈연으로는 그토록 가까이 맺어졌어도, 기질에서는 거의 정반대였다. 방금 도착한 형제 역시 장사를 배워 자랐는데, 자기 재산이 육천 파운드에 이른 것을 보자마자 그 대부분으로 작은 영지를 사들이고 시골로 물러났다. 그곳에서 그는 봉록 없는 한 성직자의 딸과 결혼했는데, 그 아가씨는 미모도 재산도 없었으나, 자기가 매우 넉넉히 지닌 그 좋은 성품 하나만으로 온전히 그의 선택을 받기에 이르렀다.\n\n그는 이 여인과 더불어 이십오 년 동안, 오늘날의 풍속이 보여 주는 어떤 본보기보다도 어떤 시인들이 황금시대에 돌렸던 그 모형에 더 가까운 삶을 살았다. 그는 그녀에게서 네 아이를 두었으나, 딸 하나만 빼고는 누구도 성년에 이르지 못했다. 그 딸을, 속된 말로 하자면 그와 그의 아내는 응석받이로 키웠다. 다시 말해 더없는 다정함과 애정으로 길렀고, 딸 또한 그 사랑에 어찌나 크게 보답했던지, 부모와 떨어지는 것을 차마 견딜 수 없다는 이유로 마흔을 조금 넘긴 어느 신사와의 더없이 좋은 혼처를 실제로 거절하기까지 했다.\n\n나이팅게일 씨가 아들의 짝으로 점찍어 둔 그 처녀는 그의 형제와 가까운 이웃이자 그의 조카딸과 아는 사이였다. 사실 그가 지금 이 도시에 온 것도 바로 그 추진 중인 혼사 때문이었다. 다만 혼사를 거들기 위해서가 아니라, 자기 조카를 틀림없이 파멸시키리라 여기는 그 계획에서 형제를 만류하기 위해서였다. 그는 해리스 양과의 결합에서, 그녀의 막대한 재산에도 불구하고 다른 어떤 결말도 내다볼 수 없었다. 그녀의 외모도 마음씨도 그가 보기엔 그 어떤 부부의 행복도 약속해 주지 못할 것 같았기 때문이다. 그녀는 키가 무척 컸고, 무척 말랐으며, 무척 못생겼고, 무척 가식적이며, 무척 어리석고, 또 무척 심술궂었다.\n\n그러므로 그의 형제가 조카가 밀러 양과 결혼했다고 입에 올리기가 무섭게, 그는 더없는 만족을 드러냈다. 그리고 아버지가 아들을 호되게 욕하고 거지 신세가 될 거라며 선고를 내리자, 삼촌은 다음과 같이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"형님, 좀 진정하셨다면 저는 형님께 묻고 싶습니다. 형님이 아드님을 사랑하시는 건 그 애를 위해서입니까, 아니면 형님 자신을 위해서입니까? 그 애를 위해서라고 대답하시겠지요. 또 그렇게 여기고 계시리라 짐작합니다. 그리고 의심할 바 없이, 형님이 그 애에게 권하신 혼사에서 의도하신 것도 그 애의 행복이었을 겁니다.\n\n자, 형님, 남에게 행복의 규칙을 정해 주는 것은 제게 늘 무척 터무니없어 보였고, 그것을 기어이 강요하는 것은 무척 폭압적으로 여겨졌습니다. 흔한 착각이라는 건 압니다. 하지만 그래도 그것은 착각입니다. 그리고 다른 일에서도 그것이 터무니없다면, 결혼이라는 일에서는 더더욱 그렇습니다. 결혼의 행복은 두 당사자 사이에 깃든 애정에 전적으로 달려 있으니까요.\n\n그래서 저는 부모가 이런 일에서 자식을 대신해 짝을 고르려 드는 것이 늘 이치에 맞지 않다고 생각해 왔습니다. 애정을 억지로 강요하는 것은 불가능한 시도이니까요. 아니, 사랑이란 강요를 어찌나 질색하는지, 우리 본성에 깃든 어떤 불행하지만 고칠 수 없는 비뚤어짐 탓에, 사랑이 설득조차 견디지 못하는 건 아닌지 저로서는 모르겠습니다.\n\n하지만 부모가 현명하게 처신한다면 짝을 정해 주지는 않을지언정, 이런 일에서 의논 상대로는 마땅히 삼아야 한다는 것은 사실입니다. 그리고 엄밀히 말하자면, 아마 적어도 거부할 권리쯤은 가져야 할 것입니다. 그러니 제 조카가 형님의 조언을 구하지 않고 결혼한 것은, 인정컨대 잘못을 저지른 셈입니다. 하지만 솔직히 말해서, 형님, 형님께서 이 잘못을 조금은 부추기신 게 아닐까요? 이 문제에 관해 형님이 거듭 단언하신 말씀들이, 재산 면에서 조금이라도 부족함이 있는 경우엔 형님이 반드시 거절하시리라는 것을 그 애에게 거의 확신시켜 준 게 아닐까요? 아니, 지금 형님의 노여움도 오로지 그 부족함에서 비롯된 게 아닙니까? 그리고 그 애가 여기서 자기 도리를 다하지 못했다면, 형님 역시 권한을 그만큼 넘어선 게 아닙니까? 형님 자신도 본 적 없는 여자를, 그 애도 모르게 그 애를 두고 흥정까지 벌이셨으니 말입니다. 게다가 그 여자를 형님이 저만큼 보고 알았더라면, 그 여자를 가문에 들일 생각을 한 것 자체가 형님으로선 미친 짓이었을 겁니다.\n\n그래도 제 조카가 잘못을 저지른 건 인정합니다. 하지만 그것이 결코 용서 못 할 잘못은 아니지요. 그 애는 분명 형님의 동의를 구했어야 할 일에서 동의 없이 행동했습니다. 하지만 그건 무엇보다 그 애 자신의 이해가 걸린 일입니다. 형님 자신도 형님이 오로지 그 애의 이익만을 헤아렸노라고 인정하실 수밖에 없을 테고 또 인정하실 겁니다. 그런데 그 애가 불행히도 형님과 의견이 갈렸고, 행복에 대한 자기 생각에서 잘못 짚었다 한들, 형님, 만일 아드님을 사랑하신다면, 그 애를 핵심에서 더 멀리 끌고 가실 작정입니까? 그 애의 단순한 선택이 빚을 나쁜 결과를 더 키우시겠습니까? 어쩌다 비참한 일이 될지도 모를 그 결말을, 기어이 확실한 비참으로 만들려 애쓰시겠습니까? 한마디로, 형님, 그 애가 자기 형편을 형님이 바라시는 만큼 넉넉하게 만들 권한을 형님에게서 앗아갔다고 해서, 그 형편을 형님이 할 수 있는 한 최대로 곤궁하게 만드시겠습니까?\"\n\n참된 가톨릭 신앙의 힘으로 성 안토니우스는 물고기들의 마음을 얻었다. 오르페우스와 암피온은 한 걸음 더 나아가, 음악의 매력으로 한낱 생명 없는 것들마저 홀렸다. 둘 다 놀랍기 그지없다! 하지만 역사도 우화도, 논증과 이성의 힘으로 몸에 밴 탐욕을 이겨낸 사례만큼은 아직 감히 기록한 적이 없다.\n\n아버지 나이팅게일 씨는 형제에게 답하려 들기는커녕, 그저 자기네 둘이 자식 교육 문제에서 늘 생각이 달랐다는 점만 짚고 만족했다. \"내 바라건대,\" 그가 말했다. \"동생, 자네는 자네 딸이나 신경 쓸 것이지, 내 아들 일로는 결코 수고하지 말았어야 했네. 그 애는 자네의 가르침으로도, 자네의 본보기로도 거의 덕을 못 본 것 같으니 말일세.\" 젊은 나이팅게일은 삼촌의 대자였고, 아버지보다 삼촌과 더 많이 어울려 살았기 때문이다. 그래서 삼촌은 조카를 자기 친자식과 거의 똑같이 사랑한다고 곧잘 단언하곤 했다.\n\n존스는 이 선량한 신사에게 그만 마음을 빼앗겨 황홀해졌다. 그리고 한참을 설득했는데도 아버지가 누그러지기는커녕 점점 더 노여워하는 것을 알게 되자, 존스는 삼촌을 밀러 부인의 집에 있는 조카에게로 데려갔다.\n\n서로 다른 기이한 일들이 담긴 이야기.\n\n존스는 숙소로 돌아와, 자신이 떠날 때와는 상황이 크게 달라져 있음을 알게 되었다. 어머니와 두 딸, 그리고 젊은 나이팅게일 씨가 이제 함께 둘러앉아 저녁을 들고 있었는데, 그때 삼촌이 자신의 청에 따라 별다른 격식 없이 그 자리에 안내되어 들어왔다. 그곳에 있던 사람들은 모두 그를 잘 알고 있었으니, 그가 여러 차례 이 집으로 조카를 찾아온 적이 있었기 때문이다.\n\n노신사는 곧장 낸시 양에게 다가가 인사를 건네며 축하의 말을 전했고, 이어서 어머니와 다른 자매에게도 같은 인사를 건넸다. 그리고 마지막으로 조카에게도 합당한 덕담을 건넸는데, 마치 조카가 모든 사전 절차를 빠짐없이 마친 뒤 자신과 대등하거나 그보다 나은 재산을 가진 사람과 혼인한 것처럼, 더없이 너그럽고 정중한 태도였다.\n\n낸시 양과 그녀의 남편이라는 사람은 둘 다 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 이 자리에서 어쩐지 머쓱하기 짝이 없는 모습이었다. 그러나 밀러 부인은 기회를 보아 가장 먼저 자리를 빠져나왔고, 존스를 식당으로 불러들인 뒤 그의 발치에 몸을 던지고는 더없이 격정적인 눈물을 쏟으며 그를 자신의 착한 천사라, 가엾은 어린 가족의 구원자라 불렀다. 그 밖에도 온갖 정중하고도 애틋한 호칭으로 그를 부르며, 더없이 감사한 마음이 가장 큰 은혜로부터 끌어낼 수 있는 모든 감사의 표현을 그에게 바쳤다.\n\n격정의 첫 물결이 조금 가라앉은 뒤에, 그녀는 만일 이 감정을 쏟아내지 못했더라면 가슴이 터져버렸을 것이라고 말하고는, 나이팅게일 씨와 자기 딸 사이의 모든 일이 매듭지어졌으며 두 사람이 이튿날 아침에 혼인하기로 했다고 존스 씨에게 알렸다. 존스 씨가 이에 큰 기쁨을 드러내자, 가엾은 부인은 다시금 기쁨과 감사의 발작에 빠져들었고, 그는 가까스로 그녀를 진정시킨 끝에 자신과 함께 일행에게로 돌아가자고 설득했다. 두 사람이 돌아가 보니 일행은 떠날 때와 다름없이 화기애애한 분위기였다.\n\n이 작은 모임은 이제 두세 시간을 함께 아주 흐뭇하게 보냈는데, 그동안 술병을 무척이나 사랑하는 삼촌이 조카에게 어찌나 부지런히 술을 권했던지, 조카는 취하지는 않았으되 어느새 다소 얼근하게 달아오르기 시작했다. 그러자 나이팅게일 씨는 노신사를 데리고 이층으로 올라가 자신이 얼마 전까지 쓰던 방으로 안내한 뒤, 다음과 같이 속내를 털어놓았다.\n\n\"삼촌은 언제나 제게 더없이 좋고 다정한 분이셨고, 누가 보아도 다소 경솔하다 여길 만한 이 혼인을 용서해 주시는 데서도 비길 데 없는 너그러움을 보여 주셨으니, 만일 제가 무슨 일로든 삼촌을 속이려 한다면 저 자신을 결코 용서하지 못할 것입니다.\" 그러고 나서 그는 진실을 고백하며 사건의 전모를 털어놓았다.\n\n\"뭐라고, 잭?\" 노신사가 말했다. \"그러면 너는 정말로 이 젊은 여자와 혼인한 게 아니란 말이냐?\" \"네, 제 명예를 걸고요.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"있는 그대로의 진실을 말씀드린 겁니다.\" \"이 사랑스러운 녀석아.\" 삼촌이 그에게 입을 맞추며 외쳤다. \"그 말을 들으니 진심으로 기쁘구나. 내 평생 이보다 기뻤던 적이 없다. 만일 네가 이미 혼인을 했더라면, 나는 이 나쁜 일에서나마 최선을 끌어내려 내가 할 수 있는 힘껏 너를 도왔을 것이다. 하지만 이미 저질러져 돌이킬 수 없는 일을 따져보는 것과, 아직 하지 않은 일을 두고 따져보는 것 사이에는 큰 차이가 있는 법이지. 이성에게 공정한 기회를 주어 보거라, 잭. 그러면 너도 이 혼인이 얼마나 어리석고 터무니없는 것인지 알게 될 테니, 굳이 만류하는 말을 늘어놓을 필요조차 없을 게다.\" \"어째서요, 삼촌?\" 젊은 나이팅게일이 대꾸했다. \"어떤 일을 이미 저지른 것과, 명예를 걸고 그 일을 하기로 약속한 것 사이에 그런 차이가 있다는 말씀입니까?\" \"쳇!\" 삼촌이 말했다. \"명예라는 건 세상이 만들어낸 피조물이고, 세상은 그 위에 창조주와 같은 권능을 지녀서 제 마음대로 다스리고 휘두를 수 있다. 그런데 너도 잘 알다시피, 이런 약혼 파기쯤은 얼마나 사소하게 여겨지더냐. 가장 심한 경우라 해도 기껏 하루치 화젯거리나 놀라움이 될 뿐이지. 그 일이 있은 뒤에 제 누이나 딸을 너에게 주기를 더 꺼릴 남자가 있겠느냐? 아니면 너를 받아들이기를 더 꺼릴 누이나 딸이 있겠느냐? 명예는 이런 약속 따위와는 아무 상관이 없다.\" \"용서하십시오, 삼촌.\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"저는 결코 그렇게 생각할 수 없습니다. 명예뿐만 아니라 양심과 인정도 여기에 걸려 있습니다. 저는 분명히 확신합니다. 만일 제가 지금 저 젊은 사람을 저버린다면 그 끝은 그녀의 죽음일 것이고, 저는 저 자신을 그녀를 죽인 살인자로, 그것도 모든 방법 가운데 가장 잔인한 방법, 곧 그녀의 가슴을 찢어 죽인 살인자로 여기게 될 것입니다.\" \"가슴을 찢어 죽인다니, 원! 아니다, 아니야, 잭.\" 삼촌이 외쳤다. \"여자들의 가슴이란 그리 쉽게 찢어지는 게 아니다. 그것들은 질기고도 질기단다, 얘야, 질기고말고.\" \"하지만 삼촌.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"제 마음이 이미 그녀에게 매여 있어서, 다른 어떤 여자와도 결코 행복할 수가 없습니다. 자식들은 늘 제 짝을 스스로 고르게 해야 하며, 사촌 해리엇에게도 그리하도록 하겠노라고 삼촌께서 얼마나 자주 말씀하셨습니까?\" \"그래, 맞다.\" 노신사가 대꾸했다. \"그래, 나도 그러길 바란다. 다만 현명하게 고르길 바라는 게지. 정말이지, 잭, 너는 그 여자를 반드시 떠나야 하고, 떠나게 될 것이다.\" \"정말이지, 삼촌.\" 다른 쪽이 외쳤다. \"저는 반드시 그녀를 맞이해야 하고, 맞이하고야 말겠습니다.\" \"그러시겠다, 젊은 양반?\" 삼촌이 말했다. \"너에게서 그런 말을 들을 줄은 몰랐다. 너를 늘 개처럼 대하고, 폭군이 신하에게 두는 만큼의 거리를 두어 온 네 아비에게라면 그런 말투를 썼다 해도 이상할 게 없겠지. 하지만 너와 대등하게 지내 온 나로서는, 분명히 더 나은 대접을 기대할 만하지 않으냐. 허나 나는 이 모든 까닭을 안다. 죄다 너의 그 터무니없는 교육 탓이지. 그 교육에 내 몫이 너무 적었던 게야. 저기 내 딸을 보아라. 나는 그 아이를 벗처럼 길러서, 내 조언 없이는 아무 일도 하지 않고, 내가 조언을 줄 때 그것을 받아들이기를 마다하는 법도 없다.\" \"삼촌은 아직 이런 종류의 일에 대해서는 사촌에게 조언을 주신 적이 없으시지요.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"제가 사촌을 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 사촌은 제 마음을 버리는 일에서만큼은 삼촌의 가장 단호한 명령조차 선뜻 따르려 하지 않을 테니까요.\" \"내 딸을 함부로 말하지 마라.\" 노신사가 다소 격해진 어조로 대답했다. \"내 해리엇을 함부로 말하지 마. 나는 그 아이를 내 뜻과 어긋나는 마음을 품지 않도록 길러 왔다. 그 아이가 하고 싶은 대로 무엇이든 하게 내버려둠으로써, 나는 그 아이가 내가 원하는 것을 기꺼이 하는 버릇이 몸에 배도록 길들여 온 것이다.\" \"용서하십시오, 삼촌.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"저는 사촌을 두고 무어라 헐뜯을 생각은 조금도 없습니다. 저는 사촌을 더없이 존경하니까요. 게다가 저는 확신합니다. 삼촌께서는 사촌을 그렇게 가혹한 시험에 들게 하시거나, 제게 내리시는 것 같은 그런 모진 명령을 그 아이에게 내리시는 일은 결코 없으리라고요. 하지만 삼촌, 이제 일행에게로 돌아가시지요. 우리가 너무 오래 자리를 비우면 다들 불안해하기 시작할 테니까요. 사랑하는 삼촌께 한 가지 청을 드려야겠습니다. 부디 저 가엾은 아가씨나 그 어머니에게 충격이 될 만한 말씀은 하지 말아 주십시오.\" \"오! 그건 걱정하지 않아도 된다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"나는 여자에게 모욕을 줄 만큼 처신을 모르는 사람이 아니다. 그러니 그 청은 기꺼이 들어주마. 그 대신 나도 네게 한 가지를 청해야겠다.\" \"삼촌의 분부 가운데 제가 아주 기꺼이 따르지 않을 것은 별로 없습니다, 삼촌.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"아니다, 나는 별것을 청하는 게 아니다.\" 삼촌이 말했다. \"다만 네가 내 숙소까지 함께 가 주는 영광을 베풀어 주기를 바랄 뿐이다. 그곳에서 이 일을 좀 더 충분히 너와 따져보고 싶구나. 가능하다면 나는, 제 딴에는 세상에서 가장 현명한 사람이라 여기는 내 동생의 고집스러운 어리석음에도 불구하고, 우리 가문을 지켜냈다는 만족을 얻고 싶으니까.\"\n\n나이팅게일은 삼촌이 제 아비만큼이나 고집이 센 사람임을 익히 알고 있었기에, 그를 집까지 모시는 데 따르기로 했다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 함께 방으로 돌아갔고, 그곳에서 노신사는 앞서 지켜오던 것과 똑같은 예의를 갖추겠노라 약속했다.\n\n본권을 매듭짓는 짧은 장.\n\n삼촌과 조카가 그토록 오래 자리를 비운 탓에, 뒤에 남겨진 모든 이의 마음에는 적잖은 불안이 일었다. 더구나 앞선 대화가 오가는 동안 삼촌이 한두 번 목소리를 높여 아래층까지 들릴 정도였으니, 그 불안은 한층 커질 수밖에 없었다. 무슨 말을 하는지 또렷이 알아들을 수는 없었지만, 그 소리는 낸시와 그녀의 어머니에게, 그리고 실은 존스 자신에게까지 어떤 불길한 예감을 불러일으켰다.\n\n그리하여 이 정겨운 사람들이 다시 한자리에 모였을 때, 모두의 얼굴에는 눈에 띄는 변화가 있었다. 지난번 모임에서는 누구의 얼굴에나 한결같이 환하게 빛나던 그 좋은 기분이, 이제는 훨씬 덜 유쾌한 빛으로 바뀌어 있었다. 사실 그것은 이 나라의 날씨에 흔히 있는 변화였다. 햇살에서 구름으로, 유월에서 십이월로 옮겨 가는 그런 변화 말이다.\n\n그러나 이 변화를 자리에 있던 누구도 크게 알아차리지는 못했다. 모두가 저마다 제 속마음을 감추고 어떤 역할을 연기하느라, 그 장면에 너무 깊이 빠져들어 정작 그것을 지켜보는 구경꾼이 되지는 못했기 때문이다. 그래서 삼촌도 조카도 어머니나 딸에게서 의심의 기미를 알아채지 못했고, 어머니나 딸 역시 노인의 지나치게 과장된 다정함도, 젊은이의 얼굴에 어색하게 번지던 거짓 만족의 미소도 눈치채지 못했다.\n\n내 생각엔 이런 일이 종종 일어나는 듯하다. 두 친구의 온 신경이 서로를 속이려고 저마다 맡은 배역에 쏠려 있을 때, 누구도 자신을 겨눈 술책을 보지도 의심하지도 못하는 것이다. 그리하여 (이 자리에 어울리지 않을 리 없는 비유를 빌리자면) 두 사람의 찌르기가 똑같이 빗나가고 만다.\n\n같은 이유로, 거래에서 양쪽이 모두 속아 넘어가는 일도 드물지 않다. 물론 그 가운데 한쪽이 늘 더 크게 손해를 보기 마련이지만 말이다. 마치 눈먼 말을 팔고서 그 값으로 가짜 어음을 받은 사람처럼.\n\n우리의 일행은 반 시간쯤 지나 자리를 파했고, 삼촌은 조카를 데리고 떠났다. 하지만 그 전에 조카는 낸시 양에게 속삭이듯, 내일 아침 일찍 찾아와 약속한 모든 것을 지키겠노라고 다짐해 두었다.\n\n이 장면에서 가장 마음 쓸 일이 적었던 존스가, 정작 가장 많은 것을 보았다. 그는 사실의 진상을 의심하기까지 했다. 삼촌의 태도에 일어난 큰 변화, 그가 두른 거리감, 낸시 양에게 보인 지나치게 빳빳한 예의를 눈여겨본 데다, 한밤중 그 시각에 신랑을 신부에게서 떼어 데려가는 것은 너무도 별난 처사여서, 젊은 나이팅게일이 진실을 모조리 털어놓았다고밖에는 달리 설명할 길이 없었기 때문이다. 게다가 겉보기에 솔직한 그의 성미와 술기운에 들떠 있던 정황을 떠올리면, 그럴 가능성은 더욱 짙어 보였다.\n\n존스가 이 가엾은 사람들에게 자신의 의심을 알려야 할지 혼자 마음속으로 따져 보고 있을 때, 집안의 하녀가 어떤 부인이 그와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 알려 왔다. 그는 곧장 밖으로 나가 하녀에게서 촛불을 받아 든 뒤, 손님을 위층으로 안내했다. 그런데 그 손님은 다름 아닌 어너 부인이었고, 그녀가 그의 소피아에 관해 전한 끔찍한 소식에 그는 순식간에 다른 어떤 사람에 대한 배려도 모두 잊고 말았다. 그가 지녔던 동정심은 죄다, 제 자신의 비참함과 불운한 천사의 처지를 향한 상념 속으로 송두리째 삼켜져 버렸다.\n\n그 끔찍한 일이 대체 무엇이었는지는, 그 일을 빚어낸 앞선 여러 단계를 먼저 이야기한 뒤에야 독자에게 알려 드리겠다. 그리고 그 이야기야말로 다음 권에서 다룰 내용이 될 것이다.\n\n역사가 이틀쯤 나아가는 권.\n\n서문이 필요 없을 만큼 짧은 장.\n종교적인, 아니 좀 더 정확히 말하자면 도덕적인 글을 쓰는 한 무리의 작가들이 있다. 이들은 덕이 이 세상에서 행복으로 가는 확실한 길이며, 악덕은 불행으로 가는 길이라고 가르친다. 참으로 건전하고 위안이 되는 가르침이다. 다만 우리에게는 여기에 한 가지 반론이 있을 뿐이다. 그것이 사실이 아니라는 점 말이다.\n\n사실, 만일 이 작가들이 덕이라는 말로, 마치 알뜰한 주부처럼 집 안에 머물며 제 가족의 일만 챙기는 그 으뜸가는 덕목들의 실천을 뜻한다면, 나는 기꺼이 그 점을 인정하겠다. 그런 덕목들은 어김없이 행복에 보탬이 되고 행복으로 이끌기에, 나는 옛 현인과 오늘의 현인 모두의 뜻을 어기면서까지, 그것들을 차라리 덕이 아니라 지혜라는 이름으로 부르고 싶을 지경이다. 내가 보기에, 이 세상의 삶에 관한 한, 이 지혜야말로 으뜸가는 선을 이룬다고 여겼던 옛 에피쿠로스학파보다 더 현명한 사상은 일찍이 없었으며, 그와 정반대로 온갖 감각적 욕구를 마음껏 채우는 데 모든 행복이 있다고 보는 오늘날의 향락가들보다 더 어리석은 사상도 없었으니 말이다.\n\n그러나 만일 덕이라는 말이 (나는 거의 그래야 마땅하다고 생각하는데) 늘 바깥을 분주히 오가며, 제 이익만큼이나 남의 이익을 좇는 데에도 깊은 관심을 쏟는 듯한 어떤 관계적 성질을 뜻한다면, 나는 이것이 인간의 행복으로 가는 가장 확실한 길이라고 그리 쉽게 동의할 수가 없다. 그렇게 되면 우리는 행복이라는 관념 안에 가난과 멸시를, 그리고 험담과 시기와 배은망덕이 인류에게 끼칠 수 있는 온갖 해악까지 함께 넣어야 할까 봐 두렵기 때문이다. 아니, 때로는 어쩌면 그 행복을 모시고 감옥까지 따라 들어가야 할지도 모른다. 앞서 말한 그 덕 때문에 스스로 그곳에 이른 이들이 적지 않으니 말이다.\n\n지금 내게는 여기서 내 앞에 펼쳐지는 듯한 이토록 넓은 사색의 들판으로 들어설 여유가 없다. 내 뜻은 다만 내 길목에 놓인 한 가지 가르침을 닦아 내려는 것뿐이었다. 존스 씨가 동료 인간들을 파멸에서 구하려 애쓰며 상상할 수 있는 가장 덕스러운 역할을 하고 있는 동안, 악마가, 아니면 다른 어떤 악령이—어쩌면 인간의 살을 뒤집어쓴 자가—그를 그의 소피아의 파멸 속에서 완전히 비참하게 만들려고 한창 애쓰고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n따라서 이것은 앞서 말한 규칙에 대한 예외처럼 보일 것이다. 만일 그것이 정말 규칙이라면 말이다. 그러나 우리는 인생이라는 항해를 거치며 그에 대한 다른 예외들을 너무도 많이 보아 왔기에, 그 규칙이 딛고 선 가르침 자체를 따져 보기로 한다. 우리가 보기에 그것은 기독교적이지 않으며, 우리가 확신하건대 사실이 아니고, 실로 이성만으로 불멸을 믿게 해 줄 수 있는 가장 고귀한 논거 가운데 하나를 무너뜨리는 가르침이기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 이제 독자의 호기심이 (혹 그에게 호기심이 있다면) 깨어나 허기져 있을 터이니, 우리는 그것을 되도록 빨리 먹여 채울 채비를 하겠다.\n\n소피아를 겨눈 매우 음흉한 계략이 펼쳐지다.\n\n언젠가 한 지혜로운 노신사가 입버릇처럼 하던 말이 떠오른다. \"아이들이 아무것도 안 하고 있을 때, 그때가 바로 사고를 치고 있는 때다.\" 나는 이 익살스러운 말을 창조물 가운데 가장 아름다운 절반 전체에까지 넓혀 적용할 생각은 없다. 다만 이만큼은 허락해 주기를 바란다. 여자의 질투가 분노와 격노라는 제 본래의 빛깔로 드러내 놓고 나타나지 않을 때, 우리는 그 사악한 정념이 은밀히 일하고 있다고, 즉 지상에서 정면으로 공격하지 않는 것을 땅 밑에서 허물어뜨리려 시도하고 있다고 의심해도 좋다는 것이다.\n\n이런 모습이 벨라스턴 부인의 행동에서 그대로 드러났다. 그녀는 얼굴에 띤 온갖 미소 아래로 소피아를 향한 깊은 분노를 감추고 있었다. 그리고 이 젊은 아가씨가 자신과 욕망의 온전한 충족 사이를 가로막고 있음을 똑똑히 보았기에, 무슨 수를 써서라도 그녀를 없애 버리기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 이를 이룰 더없이 좋은 기회가 그녀에게 찾아오기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다.\n\n독자께서는 기억해 주시기 바란다. 소피아가 극장에서, 스스로를 '도회의 멋쟁이'라 칭하는 한 무리의 젊은 신사들이 부리는 재담과 익살에 그토록 큰 낭패와 당황에 빠졌을 때, 우리는 그녀가 어느 젊은 귀족의 보호 아래 몸을 맡겼고, 그 귀족이 그녀를 가마까지 무사히 데려다주었다고 알려 드린 바 있다.\n\n벨라스턴 부인을 자주 찾아오던 이 귀족은, 소피아가 상경한 이후로 그곳에서 그녀를 한두 번 본 적이 있었고, 그녀에게 큰 호감을 품게 되었다. 그런데 곤경에 처한 아름다움만큼 더없이 사랑스러워 보이는 것이 없는 법이라, 소피아는 이 소동 속에서 그 호감을 어찌나 키워 놓았던지, 이제 그는 큰 무리 없이 그녀에게 실제로 사랑에 빠졌다고 말해도 좋을 정도였다.\n\n사랑하는 대상과의 친분을 돈독히 할, 지금 눈앞에 펼쳐진 이토록 좋은 기회를, 그가 그냥 흘려보내지 않으리라는 것은 쉽게 믿을 수 있다. 설령 사랑이 아니라 단순한 예의범절만으로도 그녀를 찾아가 보라고 그를 부추겼을 만한 상황이었으니 말이다.\n\n그리하여 이 사고가 있은 다음 날 아침, 그는 소피아를 찾아가 으레 갖추는 인사를 건네며, 어젯밤의 소동으로 다친 데는 없으신지 바란다고 안부를 물었다.\n\n사랑은 불과 같아서 한번 완전히 불붙으면 이내 활활 타오르는 법이라, 소피아는 아주 짧은 시간 만에 그를 완전히 사로잡았다. 이제 시간은 알아차릴 새도 없이 흘러갔고, 이 고귀한 경께서는 이 아가씨와 함께한 지 두 시간이 지나서야 비로소 자신이 너무 오래 머물렀다는 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 이 사정 하나만으로도 소피아는 경계심을 품었을 터인데, 지금은 셈에 한결 밝아진 처지였다. 게다가 그녀는 연모하는 이의 눈빛으로부터 그의 가슴속에 무엇이 오가는지를 알려 주는 훨씬 더 분명한 증거를 얻었다. 아니, 그가 자신의 정념을 드러내 놓고 고백하지는 않았으나, 그의 말들 가운데 상당수는 단순한 정중함으로 돌리기에는 너무 뜨겁고 너무 다정했다. 그런 정중함이 유행하던 시절에 견주어도 그러했으니, 지금은 그와 정반대가 판을 치는 풍조임은 익히 알려진 바다.\n\n벨라스턴 부인은 경께서 도착하자마자 그의 방문을 전해 들었다. 그리고 그 방문이 길어지는 것을 보고, 일이 자기 바람대로, 아니 실은 이 젊은 두 사람이 함께 있는 것을 두 번째 보았을 때 이미 짐작한 대로 흘러가고 있음을 흡족히 확인했다. 이 일은, 내 생각에 그녀가 옳게 결론지었듯이, 둘이 함께 있을 때 자신이 끼어들어서는 결코 진전시킬 수 없는 노릇이었다. 그래서 그녀는 하인들에게, 경께서 떠나려 하시거든 자신이 한마디 나누고 싶어 한다고 전하라 일러 두었다. 그러고는 그사이의 시간을, 경께서 틀림없이 기꺼이 실행에 옮기리라 의심치 않는 어떤 계략을 어찌하면 가장 잘 이룰 수 있을지 궁리하는 데 썼다.\n\n펠라마 경(이것이 이 젊은 귀족의 작위였다)이 부인 앞으로 안내되자마자, 그녀는 이런 식으로 그를 공략하기 시작했다. \"어머나, 경, 아직 여기 계셨어요? 저는 제 하인들이 실수를 해서 경을 그냥 보내 드린 줄로만 알았어요. 제가 좀 중요한 일로 경을 뵙고 싶었거든요.\"—— \"정말이지, 벨라스턴 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 방문이 길어진 데 놀라시는 것도 무리가 아닙니다. 제가 두 시간 넘게 머물렀으면서도, 반 시간도 채 못 머문 줄로만 알았으니까요.\"—— \"그렇다면 저는 그걸 어떻게 받아들여야 할까요, 경?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"시간을 그토록 감쪽같이 흘려보내게 만들 만큼, 그 자리가 어지간히 즐거웠던 모양이지요.\"—— \"맹세코,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 평생 본 가장 즐거운 자리였습니다. 부디 말씀해 주십시오, 벨라스턴 부인. 갑자기 우리 모두 가운데 띄워 놓으신 이 눈부신 별은 대체 누구입니까?\"—— \"눈부신 별이라니요, 경?\" 그녀가 짐짓 놀란 척하며 말했다. \"제 말은,\" 그가 말했다. \"며칠 전 여기서 뵙고, 어젯밤 극장에서 제 품에 안았으며, 방금 그토록 분별없이 긴 방문을 한 그 아가씨 말입니다.\"—— \"오, 제 사촌 웨스턴 양 말이군요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"글쎄요, 경, 그 눈부신 별은 시골 촌뜨기 지주의 딸이고, 처음으로 상경한 지 보름쯤 된 처녀랍니다.\"—— \"맹세코,\" 그가 말했다. \"저라면 그녀가 궁정에서 자란 사람이라고 단언했을 겁니다. 미모는 말할 것도 없고, 그토록 우아하고, 그토록 분별 있고, 그토록 세련된 사람은 본 적이 없으니까요.\"—— \"어머, 대단하셔라!\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"보아하니 제 사촌이 경을 사로잡았군요.\"—— \"맹세코,\" 그가 대답했다. \"그녀가 정말 그래 주었으면 합니다. 저는 정신을 잃을 만큼 그녀에게 빠져 있으니까요.\"—— \"어머, 경,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그건 경 자신에게 그리 나쁜 바람도 아니에요. 그 처녀는 어마어마한 재산가거든요. 분명히 말씀드리는데 외동딸이고, 아버지의 영지는 연 3000파운드는 너끈히 되는 좋은 자리랍니다.\" \"그렇다면 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있겠습니다, 부인,\" 경이 대답했다. \"제 생각에 그녀는 잉글랜드에서 가장 좋은 혼처입니다.\" \"정말이지, 경,\" 그녀가 응했다. \"경께서 그녀를 마음에 들어 하신다면, 저는 진심으로 경께서 그녀를 차지하시기를 바랍니다.\" \"부인께서 저를 그토록 후하게 여겨 주신다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"그녀가 부인의 친척이시니, 부디 그녀의 아버지께 이 혼담을 꺼내 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\" \"그럼 정말로 진심이신 건가요?\" 부인이 짐짓 근엄한 척하며 외쳤다. \"부인,\" 그가 대답했다. \"이런 종류의 일을 두고 제가 부인을 상대로 농을 칠 사람이라고 여기실 만큼, 저를 낮게 보시지는 않으시리라 믿습니다.\" \"그렇다면 정말로,\" 부인이 말했다. \"저는 더없이 기꺼이 그녀의 아버지께 경을 천거해 드리겠어요. 그리고 제가 보기에 그분이 이 혼담을 기쁘게 받아들이시리라는 것도 장담할 수 있어요. 다만 한 가지 걸림돌이 있는데, 입에 올리기조차 부끄럽군요. 게다가 그건 경께서 결코 이겨 낼 수 없는 종류의 것이에요. 경께는 연적이 있어요. 그 연적은, 이름을 대기조차 낯이 뜨겁지만, 경도, 온 세상 누구도 결코 이겨 낼 수 없는 자랍니다.\" \"맹세코, 벨라스턴 부인,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"부인께서 제 가슴에 찬물을 끼얹으셨습니다. 저를 거의 숨도 못 쉬게 만드실 만큼이요.\" \"이런, 경,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 오히려 경께 불을 지폈기를 바라는데요. 사랑하는 사람이, 가슴이 찬물에 식었다는 말씀을 하시다니요! 저는 경께서 당장 연적과 겨룰 채비를 하시려고 그자의 이름부터 물으실 줄 알았는데요.\" \"장담합니다, 부인,\" 그가 대답했다. \"부인의 그 매혹적인 사촌을 위해서라면 제가 떠맡지 못할 일은 거의 없습니다. 한데 대체 그 행복한 사내가 누구입니까?\"—— \"글쎄요, 그자는,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"안타깝게도 우리네 가장 행복한 사내들이 흔히 그렇듯, 세상에서 가장 천한 부류의 인간이랍니다. 거지에다, 사생아에다, 업둥이에다, 경의 하인들 가운데 하나보다도 못한 처지의 사내예요.\" \"그게 가당키나 한 일입니까,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그토록 완벽한 젊은 처녀가 자신을 그토록 합당치 못한 곳에 내던질 생각을 한다는 것이 말입니다.\" \"아아! 경,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"시골을 생각해 보세요. 모든 젊은 여자를 망치는 독이 바로 시골이랍니다. 거기서 그들은 사랑이니 뭐니 하는 온갖 낭만적인 관념과, 또 무슨 어리석은 생각들을 배워 오는데, 이 도회와 좋은 사교계가 겨우내 매달려도 좀처럼 뿌리 뽑지 못할 정도예요.\" \"정말이지, 부인,\" 경이 응했다. \"부인의 사촌은 너무도 귀한 보배라 그렇게 내버려져서는 안 됩니다. 이런 파멸만큼은 막아야 합니다.\" \"아아!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"경, 어떻게 막을 수 있겠어요? 집안에서는 이미 할 수 있는 일을 다 했어요. 하지만 그 처녀는, 제가 보기엔 완전히 취해 버렸고, 파멸이 아니고서는 아무것도 그녀를 만족시키지 못할 거예요. 더 터놓고 말씀드리자면, 저는 날마다 그녀가 그자와 달아났다는 소식을 들을까 봐 마음을 졸이고 있답니다.\" \"벨라스턴 부인, 지금 하신 말씀은,\" 경께서 대답했다. \"제 마음을 더없이 아프게 합니다. 그리고 부인의 사촌에 대한 제 흠모를 줄이기는커녕 오히려 제 연민만 키우는군요. 그토록 값을 매길 수 없는 보석을 지킬 방도를 반드시 찾아야 합니다. 부인께서는 그녀를 타일러 보려 애써 보셨습니까?\" 여기서 부인은 짐짓 웃음을 지으며 외쳤다. \"친애하는 경, 설마 젊은 여자를 타일러서 그 마음을 돌려놓을 수 있다고 말씀하실 만큼, 우리 여자들을 모르시지는 않으시겠지요? 이 값진 보석들은 자기네가 걸친 보석만큼이나 귀가 먹었답니다. 시간이에요, 경, 시간만이 그들의 어리석음을 고칠 유일한 약이지요. 하지만 이 약을, 그 처녀는 결코 받아들이지 않을 것이 분명해요. 아니, 저는 그녀 때문에 시시각각 끔찍한 두려움 속에 살고 있답니다. 한마디로, 격렬한 수단이 아니고서는 안 되겠어요.\" \"어찌하면 좋겠습니까?\" 경께서 외쳤다. \"어떤 수단을 써야 합니까?—이 세상에 무슨 방도가 있기는 합니까?—오! 벨라스턴 부인! 그런 보상을 위해서라면 제가 떠맡지 못할 일은 아무것도 없습니다.\"—— \"저도 정말 모르겠어요,\" 부인이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 대답했다. 그러고는 다시 뜸을 들이다가 외쳤다.—— \"맹세코, 저는 이 처녀 일로 정말 어찌할 바를 모르겠어요.—그녀를 구할 수 있다면, 지금 당장 무언가를 해야 해요. 그리고 제가 말했듯, 격렬한 수단이 아니고서는 안 될 거예요.—만일 경께서 제 사촌에게 정말로 이만한 애착을 품고 계신다면(그리고 공정하게 말하자면, 머지않아 스스로 어리석음을 깨달을 이 한심한 마음만 빼면, 그녀는 어느 모로 보나 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 사람이에요), 한 가지 길이 있을 수도 있겠어요. 다만 정말이지 몹시 거북한 길이라, 떠올리기조차 거의 두려운 것이지요.—분명히 말씀드리는데, 그건 대단한 기개가 필요한 일이에요.\" \"부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 점에서라면 저는 제게 어떤 부족함도 없다고 자신합니다. 또 그런 의심을 받을 사람이라고도 생각지 않습니다. 이런 자리에서 저를 머뭇거리게 만들 수 있는 부족함이라면, 정말이지 어지간히 큰 것이어야 할 겁니다.\" \"아니에요, 경,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"저는 경을 의심하기는커녕, 오히려 제 자신의 용기를 의심하는 쪽이에요. 저야말로 엄청난 위험을 무릅써야 하니까요. 한마디로, 저는 경의 명예에 대해, 어떤 사정을 두고서도 현명한 여자라면 좀처럼 남자에게 두지 않을 만한 신뢰를 두어야 하는 거예요.\" 이 점에서도 경께서는 그녀를 흡족히 안심시켰다. 그의 평판은 더없이 깨끗했고, 세간의 소문도 그를 좋게 말하는 데 그저 마땅한 정도였으니 말이다. \"그럼, 좋아요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"경,—저는—맹세코, 그 일을 떠올리기만 해도 견딜 수가 없군요.—아니에요, 그래선 안 돼요.—적어도 다른 모든 방법을 먼저 시도해 봐야 해요. 약속들을 미루고, 오늘 여기서 식사하실 수 있겠어요? 경께서는 웨스턴 양을 조금 더 가까이서 뵐 기회를 얻으실 거예요.—분명히 말씀드리는데 우리에게는 허비할 시간이 없어요. 여기 베티 부인과, 이글 양과, 햄프스테드 대령과, 톰 에드워즈 말고는 아무도 없을 거예요. 그 사람들도 곧 다 갈 테고요—그러면 저는 누구에게도 자리를 비운 걸로 하겠어요. 그때 경께서는 조금 더 속내를 털어놓으실 수 있을 거예요. 아니, 제가 무슨 수를 써서라도 그녀가 이 사내에게 빠져 있다는 것을 경께 똑똑히 보여 드릴게요.\" 경께서는 적절한 인사를 건네고 초대를 받아들였다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 옷을 갈아입으러 헤어졌다. 때는 이미 새벽 세 시가 지난 시각, 옛 셈법으로 따지자면 오후였다.\n\n비록 독자께서는 진작부터 벨라스턴 부인을 상류 사회의 일원으로, 그것도 결코 하찮지 않은 일원으로 여겨 왔을지 모르나, 그녀는 사실 작은 사회의 매우 비중 있는 일원이었다. 이 작은 사회라는 이름으로 불리던 것은, 얼마 전까지 이 왕국에서 융성했던 어느 매우 훌륭하고 명예로운 모임을 가리킨다.\n\n이 모임이 세워진 여러 좋은 원칙 가운데, 유독 주목할 만한 것이 하나 있었다. 지난 전쟁이 끝날 무렵 모였던 어느 명예로운 영웅들의 클럽에 모든 회원은 날마다 적어도 한 번은 결투를 해야 한다는 규칙이 있었던 것처럼, 이 모임에는 모든 회원이 스물네 시간 안에 적어도 한 가지 유쾌한 거짓말을 지어내야 하며, 그것을 형제자매 회원 모두가 퍼뜨려야 한다는 규칙이 있었다.\n\n이 모임을 두고 한가한 이야기들이 숱하게 떠돌았는데, 그 이야기들이 지닌 어떤 성질로 미루어 보면 어쩌면 그 모임 사람들 자신에게서 나온 것이라 짐작해도 그리 부당하지는 않을 것이다. 이를테면 악마가 그 모임의 회장이라느니, 그가 몸소 식탁 상석의 팔걸이의자에 앉아 있다느니 하는 이야기들이다. 그러나 매우 엄밀히 알아본 결과, 나는 이런 이야기들 어디에도 티끌만 한 진실조차 없으며, 그 모임은 실은 매우 점잖은 부류의 사람들로 이루어졌고, 그들이 퍼뜨리던 거짓말도 해롭지 않은 종류여서 오로지 웃음과 좋은 기분을 자아내는 데 그쳤음을 알게 되었다.\n\n에드워즈 또한 이 익살스러운 모임의 일원이었다. 그래서 벨라스턴 부인은 그를 자기 목적에 알맞은 도구로 삼아, 그에게 거짓말 하나를 일러 주고는 자신이 신호를 보낼 때마다 그것을 풀어놓도록 했다. 그 신호는 저녁이 되기 전에는 보내지 않기로 했는데, 펠라마 경과 그를 제외한 손님들이 모두 돌아가고, 일행이 휘스트 한 판에 빠져 있을 때를 노린 것이었다.\n* 휘스트: 네 사람이 두 편으로 나뉘어 하는 카드놀이.\n\n그렇다면 이제 우리는 독자를 그 시각, 곧 저녁 일곱 시에서 여덟 시 사이로 모셔 가 보자. 벨라스턴 부인과 펠라마 경, 웨스턴 양과 톰이 휘스트에 빠져 마지막 판을 치르고 있을 때, 톰은 벨라스턴 부인에게서 신호를 받았다. 그 신호란 이런 말이었다. \"정말이지, 톰, 자네 요즘 못 봐주겠네그려. 예전엔 시내의 온갖 소식을 죄다 전해 주더니, 이제는 마치 세상 밖에 사는 사람처럼 아무것도 모르는구먼.\"\n\n그러자 에드워즈가 이렇게 입을 열었다. \"제 탓이 아닙니다, 부인. 그건 이 시대가 따분해서 입에 올릴 만한 일을 하나도 만들어 내지 못하는 탓이지요. 아, 참! 그러고 보니 가엾은 윌콕스 대령에게 끔찍한 일이 벌어졌답니다. 불쌍한 네드 말입니다. 경께서도 그를 아시지요, 다들 아는 사람 아닙니까. 정말이지! 저는 그가 너무도 안쓰럽습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 일인데 그래요?\" 벨라스턴 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"그게, 오늘 아침에 결투로 사람을 하나 죽였답니다. 그게 다예요.\"\n\n속사정을 모르는 경은 자못 엄숙하게 누구를 죽였느냐고 물었다. 이에 에드워즈가 대답했다. \"우리 가운데 아무도 모르는 젊은이입니다. 서머싯셔에서 막 상경한 청년인데, 이름이 존스라던가요. 경께서도 들어 보셨을 올워디라는 분의 가까운 친척이라더군요. 제가 그 청년이 어느 커피하우스에 죽어 누워 있는 걸 봤습니다. 맹세코, 제 평생 본 시신 중에 가장 잘생긴 시신이었어요!\"\n톰이 사람이 죽었다는 말을 꺼냈을 때 마침 카드를 돌리기 시작했던 소피아는 손을 멈추고 귀를 기울였다.(그런 종류의 이야기는 무엇이든 그녀의 마음을 흔들었으므로) 그러나 그가 이야기의 뒷부분에 이르자 그녀는 다시 카드를 돌리기 시작했고, 한 사람에게 석 장, 다른 사람에게 일곱 장, 또 한 사람에게 열 장을 돌리다가 끝내 남은 카드를 손에서 떨어뜨리고는 의자에 쓰러지듯 기대고 말았다.\n\n좌중은 이런 경우에 흔히 그러듯이 행동했다. 으레 있을 법한 소동이 뒤따랐고, 으레 부르는 도움이 불려 왔으며, 소피아는 마침내, 으레 그렇듯이 다시 정신을 차렸다. 그러고는 곧 자신의 간절한 청에 따라 자기 방으로 이끌려 갔다. 그곳에서 경의 부탁으로 벨라스턴 부인은 그녀에게 진실을 알려 주었는데, 그것을 자기가 꾸민 농담으로 둘러대려 애쓰면서, 경도 톰도—비록 그 이야기를 자기가 톰에게 일러 준 것이긴 하나—이 일의 진짜 속내는 모른다고 거듭 다짐하며 그녀를 달랬다.\n\n벨라스턴 부인이 이 사건을 자신에게 얼마나 정확히 일러 주었는지 펠라마 경을 납득시키는 데에는 더 이상의 증거가 필요하지 않았다. 그리하여 그녀가 방으로 돌아오자, 이 두 고귀한 인물 사이에 한 가지 계략이 짜였다. 그것이 경의 눈에는 그리 흉악한 일로 비치지 않았으나(그는 훗날 결혼으로 그 부인에게 자기 힘이 닿는 모든 보상을 해 주겠노라 진심으로 약속했고, 또 진심으로 그리 마음먹었으므로) 우리 독자들 가운데 많은 이는 분명 마땅한 혐오감으로 이 일을 바라보게 되리라는 것을 우리는 의심치 않는다.\n\n그 끔찍한 목적을 위해 이튿날 저녁 일곱 시가 정해졌다. 벨라스턴 부인은 그때 소피아를 혼자 있게 하고 경을 그녀에게 들여보내겠노라 맡았다. 온 집안이 그 목적에 맞게 단속될 것이며, 하인 대부분은 집 밖으로 내보낼 작정이었다. 그리고 의심을 사지 않도록 경이 올 때까지 안주인 곁에 남겨 둘 아너 부인은, 벨라스턴 부인이 몸소 나서서 그 못된 일이 벌어질 무대에서 되도록 멀리 떨어진 방으로, 소피아의 귀에 닿지 않는 곳으로 붙들어 두기로 했다.\n\n이렇게 일이 합의되자 경은 작별을 고했고, 부인은 쉬러 물러갔다. 그녀는 성공을 의심할 까닭이 조금도 없는 이 계획에 무척 흡족해했다. 이 계획은 소피아가 존스와의 자기 정사에 더는 걸림돌이 되지 못하도록 그토록 확실히 치워 줄 것을 약속했으며, 설령 그 일이 세상에 드러난다 해도 자신은 결코 죄를 지은 사람으로 보이지 않을 방도였다. 게다가 그것마저도, 그녀는 능욕당한 소피아가 쉽사리 동의하게 될 것이고 나머지 식구들 모두가 기뻐할 결혼을 서둘러 치러 버림으로써 막을 수 있으리라 믿어 의심치 않았다.\n그러나 또 한 사람의 공모자의 가슴속에서는 일이 그토록 잔잔하지가 못했다. 그의 마음은 셰익스피어가 그토록 고귀하게 그려 낸 그 어지러운 불안 속에 내던져져 있었다.\n\n\"끔찍한 일을 저지르기까지,\n그 첫 충동과 실행 사이의 모든 틈은\n\n허깨비 같고, 흉측한 꿈만 같아라.\n혼과 죽을 운명의 도구들이\n\n그때 회의를 열고, 사람의 처지란\n작은 왕국과도 같아, 그때 한바탕\n\n반란의 형국을 겪느니라.\"\n그의 격렬한 정념은 이 계획의 첫마디를 덥석 끌어안게 했고, 더욱이 그것이 그 부인의 친척에게서 나온 것이었기에 그러했다. 그러나 사색의 벗인 베개가 그 행위 자체를, 본디 지녔던 시커먼 빛깔 그대로, 반드시 뒤따를 결과와 어쩌면 뒤따를지도 모를 결과를 모두 그의 눈앞에 펼쳐 보이자, 그의 결심은 누그러지기 시작했다. 아니, 차라리 정반대 편으로 넘어가 버렸다. 그리고 명예와 욕망 사이에 밤새도록 이어진 기나긴 다툼 끝에 마침내 명예가 이겼고, 그는 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아가 그 계획을 단념하겠노라 마음먹었다.\n\n아침이 꽤 늦은 시각이었는데도 벨라스턴 부인은 아직 침대에 누워 있었고, 소피아는 그 머리맡에 앉아 있었다. 그때 하인이 펠라마 경이 아래층 응접실에 와 있다고 알렸다. 이에 부인은 그더러 기다리라 하고는 곧 만나러 가겠노라 했다. 그러나 하인이 물러가기가 무섭게 가엾은 소피아는 사촌에게 자기 때문에 그 가증스러운 경(그녀는 그를 그렇게 불렀으나, 조금은 억울한 표현이었다)의 방문을 부추기지 말아 달라고 애원하기 시작했다. \"저는 그 사람의 속셈을 알아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어제 아침에 저한테 대놓고 구애를 했거든요. 하지만 저는 절대로 그걸 받아들이지 않기로 마음먹었으니, 부디 다시는 저희 둘만 남겨 두지 말아 주시고, 하인들에게도 일러 두세요. 그가 저를 찾거든 늘 만날 수 없다고 돌려보내라고요.\"\n\n\"어머! 얘야.\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"너희 시골 처녀들은 머릿속에 사내 생각밖에 없구나. 너한테 친절하게 구는 남자는 죄다 구애를 하는 줄 아는구나. 그분은 이 도시에서 둘째가라면 서러운 멋쟁이 청년인데, 나는 그가 그저 가벼운 호의를 베푼 것에 지나지 않는다고 믿는다. 너한테 구애를 하다니! 진심으로 나야 그분이 그래 주기를 바란다만, 그분을 마다하다니 너는 영락없이 미친 여자임에 틀림없겠구나.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 분명 그 미친 여자가 될 테니,\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"그분의 방문이 제게 밀고 들어오지 않기를 바랄 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"오, 얘야!\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"그렇게 겁낼 것까지는 없단다. 네가 그 존스라는 사람과 달아나기로 작정한 거라면, 너를 막을 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없을 테니 말이다.\"\n\n\"제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리지만, 부인,\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"부인께서는 저를 모욕하고 계세요. 저는 결코 어떤 남자와도 달아나지 않을 것이며, 아버지의 뜻을 거슬러 혼인하지도 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"좋다, 웨스턴 양.\" 부인이 말했다. \"오늘 아침 손님을 맞을 기분이 아니라면, 네 방으로 물러가 있으렴. 나는 경이 두렵지 않으니, 그를 내 화장실로 불러올려야겠다.\"\n\n소피아는 부인에게 고마움을 표하고 물러갔다. 그리고 곧이어 펠라마 경이 위층으로 안내되어 들어왔다.\n\n# Chapter iv.\n\n어느 부인이 자신의 능변을 나쁜 목적에 쏟을 때 그 변호가 얼마나 위험한 것이 되는지를 보여 주는 장.\n\n벨라스턴 부인은 젊은 귀족이 망설이는 것을 듣자, 뉴게이트 변호사라 불리는 법조계의 그 현인들이 젊은 증인의 양심의 가책을 다루는 것과 똑같은 경멸로 그 망설임을 다루었다. \"친애하는 경이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"분명 강심제가 필요하신 모양이군요. 에지리 부인에게 사람을 보내 그 댁의 가장 좋은 술 한 잔을 가져오게 해야겠어요. 원, 부끄러운 줄 아셔야지요! 좀 더 결단력을 가지세요. 능욕이라는 말에 겁이 나시는 건가요? 아니면 무언가 걱정이 되시는——? 자! 헬레네의 이야기가 만일 요즘 일이었다면, 저는 그것을 부자연스럽다 여겼을 거예요. 제 말은 파리스의 행동을 두고 하는 말이지, 그 부인의 애틋한 마음을 두고 하는 말이 아니랍니다. 여자란 모두 기개 있는 남자를 사랑하니까요. 사비니 여인들에 관한 또 다른 이야기도 있지요——그리고 그것 또한, 다행히도, 아주 오래된 이야기예요. 경께서는 어쩌면 제 독서량에 감탄하실지 모르겠습니다만, 후크 씨가 우리에게 일러 주기로는, 그 여인들이 그 뒤로 제법 괜찮은 아내가 되었다더군요. 제가 아는 결혼한 부인들 가운데 자기 남편에게 능욕당한 이는 거의 없는 듯합니다.\" \"아니, 친애하는 벨라스턴 부인,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"저를 이런 식으로 조롱하지 마십시오.\" \"어머, 친절하신 경이시여,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"영국의 어떤 여자라도, 겉으로는 아무리 정숙한 척을 하더라도, 속으로는 경을 비웃지 않으리라 생각하시나요?——경께서는 저로 하여금 이상한 말투를 쓰게 만드시고, 제 성(性)을 가장 고약하게 배신하게 만드시는군요. 하지만 저는 제 의도가 선하다는 것을, 그리고 제가 제 사촌을 도우려 애쓰고 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로 만족합니다. 이런 일에도 불구하고 경께서 그 아이를 아내로 맞아 좋은 남편이 되어 주시리라 생각하기 때문이지요. 그렇지 않았다면, 맹세코, 저는 그 아이에게 속 빈 작위 하나에 자신을 내던지라고 권하지도 않았을 거예요. 그 아이가 훗날 저더러 기개 있는 남자를 놓치게 했다고 원망하는 일은 없어야 하니까요. 적들조차 이 가엾은 젊은이가 그런 사람임은 인정한답니다.\"\n\n아내나 정부의 입에서 이런 식의 빈정거림을 들어 본 만족을 누려 본 이들이라면, 그것이 여자의 혀에서 나온다 하여 조금이라도 달콤해지는지 어디 한번 말해 보라. 분명한 것은, 그 말이 데모스테네스나 키케로가 그 자리에서 무슨 말을 했더라도 그보다 더 깊이 그 귀족의 가슴속에 박혔다는 사실이다.\n\n벨라스턴 부인은 자신이 젊은 귀족의 자존심에 불을 붙였음을 알아채자, 이제 참된 웅변가답게 그 불길을 돕도록 다른 정념들을 일깨우기 시작했다. \"경이시여,\" 그녀가 한결 진중한 목소리로 말했다. \"이 일을 제게 먼저 꺼내신 분이 경이라는 사실을 부디 기억해 주세요. 저는 경께 제 사촌을 떠넘기려 애쓰는 사람처럼 비치고 싶지 않으니까요. 팔만 파운드라는 재산은 그것을 천거해 줄 변호인이 필요한 물건이 아니랍니다.\" \"웨스턴 양 또한,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 재산이 천거해 줄 필요가 없는 사람입니다. 제 생각으로는, 그 절반의 매력이라도 지녔던 여자조차 일찍이 없었으니까요.\" \"그럼요, 그럼요, 경이시여,\" 부인이 거울을 들여다보며 대답했다. \"그 아이의 매력의 절반 이상을 지녔던 여자들도 있었답니다, 정말이에요. 그렇다고 제가 그 때문에 그 아이를 깎아내릴 필요야 없겠지요. 더없이 사랑스러운 아이인 것만은 분명하니까요. 그리고 앞으로 몇 시간 안에 그 아이는 어느 남자의 품에 안기게 될 거예요. 그 남자가 그 아이를 받을 자격이 있다고는 결코 할 수 없겠지만, 그래도 공정하게 말하자면, 저는 그가 참으로 기개 있는 남자라고 믿는답니다.\"\n\n\"그렇기를 바랍니다, 부인,\" 귀족이 말했다. \"비록 그가 그녀를 받을 자격이 없다는 것은 저도 인정해야 하겠지만 말입니다. 하늘이나 부인께서 저를 실망시키지 않으신다면, 그 시간 안에 그녀는 제 품에 안기게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 말씀이세요, 경이시여,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"제 쪽에서는 어떤 실망도 일어나지 않으리라 약속드리지요. 그리고 이번 주 안에 저는 사람들 앞에서 경을 제 사촌이라 부르게 되리라 확신합니다.\"\n\n이 장면의 나머지는 온통 황홀한 찬사와 변명과 인사치레로 이루어졌으니, 당사자들에게서 직접 듣기에는 무척 즐거웠을 테지만, 남의 입을 거쳐 전해 들으니 어쩐지 따분하다. 그러니 우리는 여기서 이 대화를 끝맺고, 가엾은 소피아를 파멸시킬 모든 채비가 갖추어진 그 운명의 시각으로 서둘러 나아가기로 하자.\n\n다만 이것이 우리의 이야기 전체를 통틀어 가장 비극적인 사건인 만큼, 우리는 따로 한 장을 마련하여 다루기로 하겠다.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n독자의 마음을 흔들 만한 일들과, 독자를 놀라게 할 만한 일들을 담은 장.\n\n시계가 막 일곱 시를 알렸고, 가엾은 소피아는 홀로 우울에 잠긴 채 비극 한 편을 읽고 있었다. 그것은 《치명적인 결혼》이었고, 그녀는 마침 가엾고 비탄에 잠긴 이사벨라가 결혼반지를 처분하는 대목에 이르러 있었다.\n\n바로 그 순간 책이 손에서 미끄러져 떨어졌고, 한 줄기 눈물이 가슴께로 흘러내렸다. 그렇게 일 분쯤 있었을 때 문이 열리며 펠라마 경이 들어왔다. 소피아는 그가 들어서자 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰고, 그는 앞으로 나서며 깊이 허리를 숙여 인사하고는 말했다. \"웨스턴 양, 이렇게 갑작스레 찾아와 무례를 범하는 게 아닌가 두렵습니다.\" \"정말이지, 경께서,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 뜻밖의 방문에 제가 조금 놀랐음을 고백하지 않을 수 없군요.\" \"이 방문이 뜻밖이라면, 아가씨,\" 펠라마 경이 답했다. \"지난번 영광스럽게도 당신을 뵈었을 때, 제 두 눈이 제 마음을 너무도 미덥지 못하게 전한 셈이겠지요. 그렇지 않고서야 당신이, 제 마음을 당신 손안에 붙잡아 두고도 그 주인의 방문을 기대하지 않으셨을 리가 없으니 말입니다.\" 소피아는 당황한 와중에도 이 허풍에 (내 생각엔 매우 마땅하게도) 상상조차 할 수 없을 만큼 멸시 어린 눈빛으로 답했다. 그러자 그는 똑같은 투의, 더 긴 연설을 한 차례 더 늘어놓았다. 그 말에 소피아는 떨면서 말했다. \"정말로 경께서 제정신이 아니시라고 여겨야 하는 건가요? 분명, 경이시여, 그런 행동에는 달리 변명의 여지가 없을 텐데요.\" \"정말로, 아가씨, 저는 당신이 짐작하시는 그 상태에 빠져 있습니다.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그리고 분명 당신은, 바로 당신 자신이 불러일으킨 이 광기의 결과를 용서해 주시겠지요. 사랑이 제게서 이성을 완전히 앗아가 버려, 저는 제 어떤 행동에도 거의 책임을 질 수 없을 지경이니까요.\" \"맹세코 말씀드리지만, 경이시여,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"저는 당신의 말도 당신의 행동도 도무지 이해할 수 없어요.\" \"그렇다면 아가씨, 부디 허락해 주십시오.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신의 발치에서 그 둘을 다 설명하게 해 주십시오. 제 영혼을 당신께 활짝 열어 보이며, 제가 미칠 듯한 극한에 이르도록 당신을 흠모하고 있음을 고백하게 해 주십시오. 오, 더없이 사랑스럽고, 더없이 거룩한 분이시여! 어떤 말로 제 마음의 심정을 다 표현할 수 있겠습니까?\" \"분명히 말씀드리지만, 경이시여,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"저는 이런 말을 더는 듣고 있지 않겠어요.\" \"그러지 마십시오.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그렇게 잔인하게 저를 떠날 생각은 마십시오. 제가 느끼는 고통의 절반만이라도 아신다면, 그 부드러운 가슴이 저 두 눈이 불러온 일을 가엾이 여기실 텐데요.\" 그러더니 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 그녀의 손을 붙잡고는, 몇 분 동안 어떤 어조로 계속 말을 쏟아냈는데, 그것은 그 숙녀에게 못지않게 독자에게도 그리 즐겁지 않을 것이다. 그리고 마침내 이렇게 단언하며 끝을 맺었다. \"만일 제가 온 세상의 주인이라면, 그것을 당신 발치에 바치겠습니다.\" 그러자 소피아는 그의 손에서 자기 손을 억지로 빼내며 자못 기세 있게 답했다. \"분명히 말해 두지만, 선생님, 당신의 세상이든 그 주인이든, 저는 똑같이 경멸하며 내쳐 버릴 거예요.\" 그러고는 가려고 하자, 펠라마 경은 다시 그녀의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"용서하십시오, 사랑하는 나의 천사여, 절망이 아니었다면 결코 감히 저지르지 못했을 이 무례를. 믿어 주십시오, 만일 제 작위와 재산이 — 당신의 가치에 견주지 않는 한 둘 다 결코 하찮지 않은 것들이지만 — 받아들여지리라는 한 가닥 희망이라도 품을 수 있었다면, 저는 더없이 겸손하게 그것들을 당신 앞에 바쳤을 것입니다. 하지만 저는 당신을 잃을 수가 없습니다. 하늘에 맹세코, 차라리 제 영혼을 내놓겠습니다! 당신은, 당신은 반드시, 오직 저만의 사람이어야 합니다.\" \"경이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"부디 헛된 추구를 그만두시기를 간청합니다. 제 명예를 걸고, 저는 이 일에 관해 결코 당신의 말을 듣지 않겠어요. 제 손을 놓으세요, 경이시여. 저는 지금 이 순간 당신에게서 떠나기로 결심했으니까요. 그리고 다시는 당신을 보지 않겠어요.\" \"그렇다면, 아가씨,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"저는 이 순간을 최대한 활용해야겠습니다. 저는 당신 없이는 살 수 없고, 살지도 않을 테니까요.\" \"무슨 뜻이세요, 경이시여?\" 소피아가 말했다. \"제가 온 집안 사람을 불러 모으겠어요.\" \"저는 아무것도 두렵지 않습니다, 아가씨.\" 그가 답했다. \"당신을 잃는 것 말고는요. 그리고 그것만큼은, 절망이 제게 가리키는 단 하나의 방법으로라도 막기로 결심했습니다.\" 그러더니 그는 그녀를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안았다. 그 순간 그녀가 어찌나 크게 비명을 질렀던지, 벨라스턴 부인이 모든 귀를 미리 치워 두지 않았더라면 분명 누군가가 그녀를 도우러 달려왔을 것이다.\n\n그러나 가엾은 소피아에게는 더 다행스러운 일이 벌어졌다. 이제 또 다른 소란이 터져 나와 그녀의 비명을 거의 묻어 버릴 지경이었던 것이다. 온 집 안이 이런 고함으로 울렸다. \"그 애가 어디 있어? 빌어먹을, 당장 그 애를 굴에서 끌어내고 말겠다. 그 애 방을 보여 달란 말이야. 내 딸이 어디 있어? 그 애가 이 집에 있는 걸 다 알아. 땅 위에 살아 있다면 내가 기어이 보고 말겠어. 어디 있는지 보여 달라니까.\" 마지막 말과 함께 문이 벌컥 열리며, 웨스턴 영주가 자기 목사와 한 무리의 졸개들을 뒤에 거느리고 들어왔다.\n\n가엾은 소피아의 처지가 얼마나 비참했으면, 분노에 찬 아버지의 목소리가 그녀의 귀에 반갑게 들렸겠는가! 정말로 반가웠고, 그가 온 것은 더없이 다행스러운 일이었다. 그것이야말로, 그녀의 마음의 평화가 영영 부서져 버리는 것을 막아 줄 수 있었던, 이 세상 단 하나의 우연이었으니 말이다.\n\n소피아는 그 놀라움 속에서도 곧바로 아버지의 목소리를 알아챘고, 펠라마 경 역시 그 격정 속에서도 이성의 목소리를 알아들었으니, 그것은 지금이 그 악행을 저지를 때가 아님을 단호하게 그에게 일러 주었다. 그리하여 그 목소리가 가까이 다가오는 것을 듣고, 또 그것이 누구의 목소리인지도 듣고 나서 (영주가 딸이라는 말을 한 번 이상 고래고래 외쳐 댔듯, 소피아 역시 몸부림치는 와중에 아버지를 부르며 소리쳤으므로), 그는 자기 먹이를 놓아주는 것이 옳다고 여겼다. 다만 그녀의 손수건을 흐트러뜨리고, 그 무례한 입술로 그녀의 사랑스러운 목에 폭력을 가했을 뿐이었다.\n\n독자의 상상력이 나를 거들어 주지 않는다면, 웨스턴이 방 안으로 들어왔을 때 이 두 사람이 처한 상황을 나는 도저히 그려낼 수 없을 것이다. 소피아는 비틀거리며 의자에 주저앉았고, 거기서 흐트러진 채, 창백하고, 숨이 가쁘고, 펠라마 경에 대한 분노로 터질 듯한 모습으로 앉아 있었다. 아버지의 등장에 겁을 먹었으면서도, 그보다 더 기뻐하면서.\n\n펠라마 경은 그녀 가까이에 앉았는데, 가발의 머리 주머니가 한쪽 어깨 위로 늘어져 있었고, 나머지 차림새도 다소 흐트러진 데다, 평소보다 좀 많은 양의 속옷이 가슴께로 드러나 보였다. 그 밖의 점으로 말하자면, 그는 어리둥절하고, 겁먹고, 짜증나고, 부끄러워하고 있었다.\n\n웨스턴 영주로 말하자면, 그는 마침 이때 한 적에게 따라잡혀 있었는데, 그 적이란 이 왕국 시골 향신들 대부분을 매우 자주 쫓아다니며 좀처럼 따라잡는 데 실패하는 법이 없는 놈이었다. 그는 말 그대로 취해 있었다. 이 사정에 그의 타고난 불같은 성미까지 더해지자, 다른 어떤 결과도 나올 수 없었으니, 그는 곧장 딸에게 달려가 가장 끈질긴 방식으로 입으로 욕을 퍼부어 댔다. 아니, 목사가 끼어들지 않았더라면 아마 손으로도 폭력을 휘둘렀을 것이다. 목사는 이렇게 말했다. \"제발 부탁드립니다, 나리, 여기가 어느 지체 높은 부인의 댁임을 헤아리십시오. 분노를 누그러뜨리시기를 간청드립니다. 따님을 찾으셨으니 마땅히 그것으로 가득한 만족을 누리셔야 합니다. 복수로 말하자면, 그것은 우리에게 속한 것이 아니니까요. 저 젊은 아가씨의 얼굴에서 깊은 뉘우침이 보입니다. 확신컨대, 나리께서 용서해 주시면, 아가씨는 지난 모든 잘못을 회개하고 본분으로 돌아올 것입니다.\"\n\n목사의 팔 힘은 애초에 그의 수사학적 힘보다 더 쓸모가 있었다. 그래도 그의 마지막 말이 어느 정도 효과를 냈는지, 영주가 답했다. \"그놈만 받아들이면 용서해 주마. 그놈만 받으면, 소피, 다 용서해 줄게. 왜 말이 없어? 그놈을 받아야 해! 빌어먹을, 받아야 한다고! 왜 대답이 없어? 이렇게 고집 센 두꺼비 같은 게 또 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"부디 간청드립니다, 나리, 좀 더 누그러지십시오.\" 목사가 말했다. \"아가씨를 어찌나 겁주시는지, 입을 뗄 기력조차 다 앗아가고 계십니다.\"\n\n\"기력은 무슨 빌어먹을 기력.\" 영주가 답했다. \"그럼 자네는 저 애 편을 드는 거야, 응? 참 잘난 목사로군, 불효막심한 자식 편을 들다니! 그래, 그래, 빌어먹을 성직록을 자네한테 주지. 차라리 악마한테 주고 말지.\"\n\n\"황송하오나 용서를 빕니다.\" 목사가 말했다. \"나리께 맹세컨대 저는 그런 뜻이 전혀 아니었습니다.\"\n\n이제 벨라스턴 부인이 방으로 들어와 영주에게 다가왔다. 영주는 그녀를 보자마자, 누이의 당부를 따르기로 마음먹고 시골식으로 매우 정중하게 허리를 숙여 인사하고는, 자기가 할 수 있는 가장 그럴듯한 인사말 몇 마디를 건넸다. 그러고는 곧장 자기 하소연으로 넘어가 이렇게 말했다. \"자, 사촌 부인, 여기 세상에서 가장 불효막심한 자식이 서 있습니다. 거지 같은 불한당 놈을 못 잊어 안달하면서, 우리가 마련해 준 온 잉글랜드에서 손꼽히는 혼처 하나는 마다하지 뭡니까.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 웨스턴 사촌,\" 부인이 답했다. \"당신이 제 조카를 잘못 보신 게 분명해요. 분명 이 아이는 더 분별이 있답니다. 자기에게 그토록 큰 이득이 되는 일임을 알면서 거절할 리 없다고 저는 확신해요.\"\n\n이것은 벨라스턴 부인의 고의적인 착각이었다. 그녀는 웨스턴 씨가 누구를 가리키는지 잘 알고 있었으니 말이다. 다만 아마도 그녀는, 그가 펠라마 경의 청혼에 쉽게 마음을 돌리리라 여겼을 것이다.\n\n\"저기 좀 들어 봐라.\" 영주가 말했다. \"부인께서 하시는 말씀을 말이야. 네 집안 사람이 다 그 혼처를 찬성한다잖니. 자, 소피, 착한 딸이 되어 본분을 지키고, 네 아비를 기쁘게 해 다오.\"\n\n\"제 죽음이 아버지를 기쁘게 한다면, 아버지,\" 소피아가 답했다. \"곧 그렇게 되시겠지요.\"\n\n\"그건 거짓말이야, 소피. 빌어먹을 거짓말이고, 너도 그걸 알잖아.\" 영주가 말했다.\n\n\"정말이에요, 웨스턴 양,\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"당신은 아버지께 못할 짓을 하는 거예요. 이 혼사에서 아버지는 오직 당신의 이익만 바라보고 계세요. 그리고 저도, 당신의 모든 벗들도, 이 청혼이 당신 집안에 더없는 영광을 안겨 준 것임을 인정하지 않을 수 없답니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 우리 모두가 말이지.\" 영주가 말했다. \"아니, 그건 내가 꺼낸 청혼도 아니었어. 처음에 네 고모가 나한테 제안한 거라는 걸 저 애도 알아. 자, 소피, 다시 한번 부탁한다, 착한 딸이 되어서, 네 사촌 앞에서 승낙해 다오.\"\n\n\"제가 이 아이의 손을 그분께 건네드릴게요, 사촌.\" 부인이 말했다. \"요즘은 시간을 들이고 길게 구애하는 걸 생략하는 게 유행이랍니다.\"\n\n\"흥!\" 영주가 말했다. \"시간이 무슨 대수라고. 나중에 구애할 시간이야 얼마든지 있지 않겠어? 한 침대에 든 다음에도 사람들은 얼마든지 잘만 구애하는걸.\"\n\n펠라마 경은 벨라스턴 부인이 자기를 가리킨다는 것을 확실히 알았으므로, 또 블리필에 관해서는 한마디도 듣거나 의심해 본 적이 없었으므로, 아버지가 가리키는 사람도 자기라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 그리하여 영주에게 다가가 말했다. \"비록 나리, 제가 직접 인사를 드릴 영광은 갖지 못했으나, 제 청혼이 받아들여지는 행운을 얻은 듯하니, 나리, 저 젊은 아가씨를 위해 한마디 청을 드리겠습니다. 지금 이 순간만큼은 아가씨를 더 다그치지 마시기를요.\"\n\n\"자네가 청을 드린다고, 선생?\" 영주가 말했다. \"아니, 자넨 대체 누구야?\"\n\n\"나리, 저는 펠라마 경입니다.\" 그가 답했다. \"그리고 나리께서 영광스럽게도 사위로 받아들여 주셨기를 바라는, 그 행복한 사내입니다.\"\n\n\"넌 개자식이야.\" 영주가 받아쳤다. \"그 화려한 레이스 옷을 다 입었어도 말이지. 네가 내 사위라고, 빌어먹을 놈 같으니!\"\n\n\"나리, 다른 어떤 사람한테서보다 나리한테서는 더 많이 참겠습니다.\" 경이 답했다. \"하지만 분명히 말씀드려야겠습니다. 저는 그런 말을 듣고도 분개하지 않는 데에는 익숙하지 못합니다.\"\n\n\"분개는 무슨 빌어먹을 분개.\" 영주가 말했다. \"내가 너 따위를 무서워할 것 같으냐! 옆구리에 그 꼬챙이 하나 매달고 다닌다고 말이지. 그 꼬챙이일랑 치워 둬라, 그럼 네놈 일도 아닌 일에 끼어든 죗값을 내가 톡톡히 치르게 해 주마. 어디 내 장인 노릇을 하겠다고? 네 저고리를 흠씬 두들겨 주마.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 나리.\" 경이 말했다. \"저는 숙녀분들 앞에서 소동을 일으키지 않겠습니다. 저는 아주 만족합니다. 그럼 이만 물러가겠습니다, 나리. 벨라스턴 부인, 더없이 충실한 종이 인사 올립니다.\"\n\n경이 떠나기가 무섭게 벨라스턴 부인이 웨스턴 씨에게 다가와 말했다. \"맙소사, 사촌, 대체 무슨 짓을 하신 거예요? 당신이 누구한테 모욕을 준 건지 모르시는군요. 그분은 일등가는 지체와 재산을 가진 귀족인 데다, 어제 당신 따님께 청혼을 한 분이에요. 게다가 그 청혼이라는 게, 당신이 분명 더없는 기쁨으로 받아들여야 할 그런 것이라니까요.\"\n\n\"부인 일은 부인이 알아서 하시구려, 사촌 부인.\" 영주가 말했다. \"나는 당신네 귀족 나리들하고는 아무 상관도 하고 싶지 않소. 내 딸은 정직한 시골 향신과 짝지어 줄 거요. 내가 점찍어 둔 사람이 하나 있으니, 그 애는 그놈을 받아들일 거요. 부인께 끼친 폐는 진심으로 미안하게 생각하오.\" 벨라스턴 부인이 그 폐라는 말을 두고 정중한 인사말을 건네자, 영주가 답했다. \"허허, 친절하시구려. 나도 부인을 위해서라면 그만큼은 하지요. 암, 친척끼리는 서로 도와야지요. 그럼 부인께 편안한 밤 되시기를 비오. 자, 얘야, 너는 순순히 나하고 같이 가든지, 아니면 마차까지 들려 가든지 해라.\"\n\n소피아는 억지로 끌려가지 않고 따르겠노라 말했지만, 가마를 타고 가게 해 달라고 청했다. 다른 방법으로는 도저히 갈 수 없을 것 같다는 것이었다.\n\n\"이런,\" 영주가 외쳤다. \"설마 네가 마차를 못 탄다고 나를 구슬릴 셈은 아니겠지, 응? 그것참 가관이로구나! 안 돼, 안 돼, 네가 시집갈 때까지는 다시는 너를 내 눈앞에서 놓치지 않을 거다, 약속하마.\" 소피아는 아버지가 자기 가슴을 찢어 놓기로 작정하신 게 보인다고 말했다. \"오, 찢어지든 말든, 빌어먹을,\" 그가 말했다. \"좋은 남편이 네 가슴을 찢어 놓는다면야. 이 세상 어떤 불효막심한 계집이라도, 나는 놋쇠 동전 한 닢, 반 페니어치도 쳐주지 않는다.\" 그러더니 그는 그녀의 손을 거칠게 와락 붙잡았다. 그 순간 목사가 다시 한번 끼어들어, 부드러운 방법을 쓰시라고 그에게 간청했다. 그러자 영주는 욕설을 한바탕 우레처럼 퍼붓고는 목사에게 입을 다물라 명하며 말했다. \"지금 설교단에 서 있는 줄 아나? 자네가 거기 올라가 있을 땐 무슨 말을 하든 내가 신경도 안 쓰지. 하지만 나는 목사한테 끌려다니지도 않을 거고, 자네한테 처신하는 법을 배우지도 않을 거야. 부인께 편안한 밤 되시길 비오. 자, 가자, 소피. 착한 딸이 되면 다 잘될 거다. 그놈을 받아야 해, 빌어먹을, 받아야 한다고!\"\n\n아너 부인이 아래층에 나타나 영주에게 공손히 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 자기 여주인을 모시겠다고 나섰다. 그러나 영주는 그녀를 밀쳐 내며 말했다. \"그만, 이 사람아, 그만둬. 다시는 내 집 근처에 얼씬도 하지 마.\" \"제 하녀까지 빼앗아 가실 거예요?\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그럼, 정말이지, 그럴 거다.\" 영주가 외쳤다. \"하인 없이 지낼까 봐 걱정할 건 없다. 다른 하녀를 구해 줄 테니까. 이 계집보다 더 나은 하녀로 말이야. 이 계집은, 내 우리 할멈만큼도 처녀가 아니라는 데 5파운드 대 1크라운을 걸겠다. 안 돼, 안 돼, 소피, 이 계집이 더는 도망칠 궁리를 못 하게 하마, 약속하지.\" 그러고는 딸과 목사를 삯마차에 욱여넣고, 그 뒤에 자기도 올라타고는 자기 숙소로 몰라고 명했다. 그리로 가는 길에 그는 소피아를 가만 내버려 두었고, 목사에게 예절과 윗사람에 대한 올바른 처신에 관해 일장 훈계를 늘어놓으며 시간을 보냈다.\n\n만일 그 어진 부인이 소피아를 붙잡아 두고 싶어 했더라면, 영주가 벨라스턴 부인의 집에서 그토록 쉽게 딸을 데려가지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 실은, 부인은 소피아가 앞으로 들어가게 될 그 감금 신세가 적잖이 흡족했다. 게다가 펠라마 경과 함께 꾸민 자기 계략이 실패로 돌아간 마당이라, 이제 또 다른 사내를 위해 다른 거친 방법들이 동원되려는 것이 그녀로서는 못내 만족스러웠던 것이다.\n\n시골 지주가 어떤 경로로 딸을 찾아내게 되었는가.\n\n여러 이야기책에서 독자는 웨스턴 씨의 이번 경우보다 훨씬 더 설명하기 어려운 일들조차 아무런 해명 없이 그대로 삼켜야 할 때가 많다. 그러나 우리는 할 수 있는 한 언제든 독자를 기쁘게 해 드리는 것을 진심으로 좋아하므로, 이제 그 지주가 어떤 방법으로 딸이 있는 곳을 알아냈는지 보여 드리려 한다.\n\n그러니 앞선 권의 제3장에서 우리는 한 가지 단서를 흘려 두었다(우리는 어느 때든 그 상황에 꼭 필요한 것 이상은 펼쳐 보이지 않는 것이 관례이기 때문이다). 자기 외삼촌과 외숙모 웨스턴을 화해시키고 싶어 안달이 난 피츠패트릭 부인이, 소피아가 자신을 가족의 분노 속으로 끌어들였던 바로 그 죄를 저지르지 못하게 막아 주는 봉사를 통해 그럴듯한 기회를 잡았다고 여겼다는 단서 말이다. 그리하여 그녀는 오랜 고심 끝에 자기 외숙모 웨스턴에게 사촌이 어디 있는지 알리기로 결심했고, 그에 따라 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다. 우리는 한두 가지가 아닌 여러 이유로 이 편지를 독자에게 전문 그대로 보여 드리려 한다.\n\n“존경하는 외숙모님께,\n“이 글을 쓰게 된 사연이, 비록 다른 한 조카 때문에는 그러기를 바랄 만한 이유가 거의 없다 하더라도, 또 다른 한 조카를 위해서라면 어쩌면 제 편지를 사랑하는 외숙모님께 반갑게 만들어 드릴지도 모르겠습니다.\n\n“더 긴 사죄는 접어 두고 말씀드리겠습니다. 이 가엾은 몸을 외숙모님 발치에 던지러 가던 길에, 세상에서 가장 기이한 우연으로 저는 사촌 소피를 만났습니다. 그 아이의 사정은 저보다 외숙모님께서 더 잘 알고 계시지요. 그래도, 아, 저 역시 너무나 많은 것을 알고 있습니다. 정말이지 충분히 알고 있어서, 만약 지금 당장 막지 않으면 그 아이가 제가 어리석고 무지하게도 외숙모님의 더없이 현명하고 신중한 충고를 거절하여 불행히도 제 몸에 끌어들인 바로 그 치명적인 화 속으로 뛰어들 위험에 처해 있다고 확신하기에 족할 만큼 말입니다.\n\n“간단히 말씀드리면, 저는 그 남자를 보았습니다. 아니, 어제 거의 온종일 그 사람과 함께 있었답니다. 장담컨대 매력적인 젊은이더군요. 어떤 우연으로 그가 저와 알게 되었는지는 지금 말씀드리기엔 너무 장황합니다. 다만 저는 오늘 아침 그를 피하려고 거처를 옮겼습니다. 혹시라도 저를 통해 그가 제 사촌이 있는 곳을 알아낼까 두려워서지요. 그는 아직 그 아이가 어디 있는지 모르고, 외삼촌께서 그 아이를 단단히 붙드시기 전까지는 모르는 편이 좋습니다.——그러니 한시도 지체해서는 안 됩니다. 저는 다만 그 아이가 지금 벨라스턴 부인과 함께 있다는 것만 알려 드리면 됩니다. 저는 그분을 만나 보았는데, 제가 보기에 그분은 그 아이를 가족에게서 숨겨 둘 속셈을 품고 있는 듯합니다. 외숙모님께서도 아시다시피, 그분은 별난 여자입니다. 그러나 사실관계를 그저 알려 드리는 것 외에, 외숙모님처럼 큰 식견과 세상 물정에 밝으신 분께 감히 무언가를 넌지시 일러 드리려 드는 것보다 제게 더 어울리지 않는 일은 없을 것입니다.\n\n“외숙모님, 이번 일에서 제가 우리 가문의 안녕을 위해 보여 드린 정성이, 늘 우리 모두의 명예와 진정한 이익을 위해 그토록 열심을 다해 오신 부인의 호의를 제게 다시 얻게 해 주기를 바랍니다. 그리고 그것이, 제 지난날 행복의 그토록 큰 부분을 이루었고 앞으로의 행복에도 그토록 꼭 필요한 외숙모님의 우정을 제게 되돌려 주는 길이 되기를 바랍니다.\n“더없는 존경을 담아,\n\n존경하는 외숙모님의\n더없이 효심 깊고 신세 진 조카이자\n\n더없이 순종하는 미천한\n종,\n\n해리엇 피츠패트릭 올림.”\n웨스턴 부인은 이때 오라버니의 집에 머물고 있었는데, 소피아가 달아난 이후로 줄곧 그곳에 지내며 슬픔에 잠긴 가엾은 지주를 위로해 주고 있었다. 날마다 조금씩 배급하듯 나눠 주던 그 위로의 견본을 우리는 앞서 보여 드린 바 있다.\n\n그녀는 지금 난롯불을 등지고 서서, 손에 코담배 한 자밤을 쥔 채, 지주가 오후 담배를 피우는 동안 이 일일 위로 배급분을 풀어 놓고 있었다. 바로 그때 위의 편지를 받았는데, 다 읽기가 무섭게 그것을 오라버니에게 건네며 말했다. “자, 오라버니, 여기 잃어버린 양에 관한 소식이 있어요. 운명이 그 아이를 다시 오라버니께 돌려보내 주었으니, 제 충고에 따라 주시기만 한다면 어쩌면 아직 그 아이를 지켜 낼 수 있을지도 몰라요.”\n\n지주는 편지를 읽기가 무섭게 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 담뱃대를 불 속에 던져 넣고는 기쁨에 겨워 큰 소리로 만세를 외쳤다. 그러고는 하인들을 불러 장화를 가져오게 하고, 슈발리에와 다른 말 몇 필에 안장을 얹으라 명하며, 서플 목사를 즉시 불러오라 일렀다. 이 일을 마치고 그는 누이에게 돌아서서 그녀를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안고는 바짝 껴안으며 말했다. “이런! 너는 기뻐 보이질 않는구나. 내가 그 애를 찾은 걸 못마땅해하는 사람처럼 보일 지경이야.”\n\n“오라버니,” 그녀가 대답했다. “바닥까지 꿰뚫어 보는 가장 깊은 책략가들은, 수면 위에 떠 있는 것과는 사뭇 다른 일의 양상을 곧잘 알아채는 법이에요. 사실, 지금 사정이 예전 네덜란드에서 루이 14세가 암스테르담 성문 앞까지 닥쳐왔을 때보다는 그리 절망적으로 보이지 않는 건 맞아요. 그렇지만 이 일에는 섬세함이 필요한데, 오라버니, 용서하세요, 제가 보기엔 오라버니께 바로 그게 모자란 듯해요. 벨라스턴 부인 같은 지체 높은 여인을 대할 때는 마땅히 지켜야 할 예법이 있는데, 그건 세상 물정에 대한 지식을 요한답니다. 안타깝게도 오라버니의 것을 훨씬 웃도는 지식 말이에요.”\n\n“누이야,” 지주가 외쳤다. “네가 내 머리를 우습게 본다는 걸 잘 안다. 그렇지만 이번 일에서는 누가 바보인지 내가 보여 주마. 지식이라고, 흥! 내가 이렇게 오래 시골에 살면서 영장이며 이 나라 법에 대해 아무것도 모를 줄 아느냐. 내 것은 어디서 찾든 내가 가질 수 있다는 걸 안다. 내 친딸을 내 앞에 보여만 주면, 그 애한테 다가갈 방법을 내가 모른다면, 죽을 때까지 네가 나를 바보라 불러도 좋다. 런던에도 다른 곳들과 마찬가지로 치안판사들이 있단 말이다.”\n“정말이지,” 그녀가 외쳤다. “오라버니 때문에 이 일의 결말이 떨려요. 제 충고대로만 하시면 아주 좋은 결말로 이끌 수 있는 일인데 말이에요. 오라버니, 정말로 지체 높은 여인의 집을 영장과 무지막지한 치안판사들로 쳐들어갈 수 있다고 생각하세요? 어찌해야 하는지 제가 일러 드리지요. 도시에 도착하시는 대로, 우선 단정한 옷차림부터 갖추셔야 해요(정말이지 오라버니, 지금은 사람 앞에 나설 만한 옷이 한 벌도 없으니까요). 그런 다음 벨라스턴 부인께 인사를 전하고 찾아뵙기를 청하셔야 합니다. 그분 앞에 들게 되면—틀림없이 그리 될 텐데—오라버니의 사정을 말씀드리고 제 이름을 적절히 활용하세요(두 분은 친척이긴 해도 그저 얼굴만 아는 사이일 테니까요). 그러면 제 확신컨대 그분은 분명 자신을 속였을 제 조카에게서 보호를 거두실 거예요. 이것이 유일한 방법입니다.——치안판사라니, 원! 문명국에서 지체 높은 여인에게 그런 일이 일어날 수 있다고 생각하세요?”\n\n“그놈의 지체고 뭐고 다 빌어먹으라지,” 지주가 외쳤다. “참 대단한 문명국이로구나, 여자들이 법 위에 있다니. 그리고 친아비한테서 딸을 떼어 놓는 그 빌어먹을 화냥년한테 내가 인사치레 한 보따리를 보내며 굽실거려야 한다고? 누이야, 내가 네 생각만큼 그렇게 무지하진 않아.——나는 네가 여자들을 법 위에 두고 싶어 하는 걸 알지만, 그건 죄다 거짓말이야. 순회재판소에서 그분께서 말씀하시는 걸 내가 들었는데, 그 누구도 법 위에 있지 않다고 하셨다. 하지만 네 말은, 내 짐작에, 하노버 법이렷다.”\n\n“웨스턴 씨,” 그녀가 말했다. “오라버니는 날마다 무지가 늘어 가는 것 같군요.——정말이지 오라버니는 영락없는 곰이 다 되셨어요.”\n\n“너야말로 곰이지 나는 아니다, 누이 웨스턴,” 지주가 말했다.——“제기랄! 네 예의범절 타령은 마음껏 해라만, 내겐 한 번도 그런 걸 보여 준 적이 없잖느냐. 나는 곰이 아니야, 아니고말고, 개도 아니다. 그래도 ‘ㅁ’ 자로 시작하는 뭔가에 해당하는 누군가는 내가 알지만 말이다. 흥! 어쨌든 나도 어떤 사람들보다는 예의범절을 더 갖췄다는 걸 너한테 보여 주마.”\n\n* ‘ㅁ’ 자: 원문에서는 'b'로 시작하는 단어, 곧 'bitch(암캐)'를 빗댄 말장난이다.\n“웨스턴 씨,” 부인이 대답했다. “하고 싶은 말씀 마음껏 하세요. 저는 진심으로 당신을 경멸하니까요.* 그러니 저는 화내지 않겠어요.——게다가, 그 끔찍한 아일랜드식 성을 가진 제 조카가 옳게 말했듯이, 저는 우리 가문의 명예와 진정한 이익을 그토록 소중히 여기고, 그 가문의 일부인 제 조카를 그토록 염려하므로, 이번 일에 직접 도시로 올라가기로 결심했어요. 정말이지, 정말이지 오라버니, 오라버니는 세련된 궁정에 보낼 만한 적임의 사신이 못 됩니다.——그린란드, 그린란드야말로 그 산 너머 야만의 협상이 벌어질 무대로나 어울리지요.”\n\n* je vous mesprise de tout mon coeur: “저는 진심으로 당신을 경멸합니다”라는 뜻의 프랑스어.\n“하느님께 감사하지,” 지주가 외쳤다. “이번엔 네 말을 도통 알아듣지 못하겠으니 말이다. 또 그 하노버 말투가 나왔구나. 그래도 나도 예의에서 네게 뒤처지는 건 질색이라는 걸 보여 주마. 네가 내 말에 화내지 않았으니, 나도 네 말에 화내지 않으마. 정말이지 나는 늘 친척끼리 다투는 건 어리석은 짓이라 여겨 왔다. 어쩌다 한 번씩 성난 말이 오가더라도, 사람이란 주고받을 줄 알아야 하는 법이지. 나로 말하자면 결코 앙심을 품는 법이 없다. 그리고 네가 런던까지 올라가 준다니 참 고맙구나. 나는 평생 그곳에 두 번밖에 가 보질 못했고, 그때도 한 번에 보름 넘게 머문 적이 없으니, 그 짧은 동안에 그곳 거리며 사람들을 많이 알았을 리가 없지. 나는 네가 이런 일들을 나보다 더 잘 안다는 걸 한 번도 부인한 적이 없다. 내가 그걸 두고 따지는 건, 네가 나한테 개 떼 다루는 법이나 웅크린 토끼 찾아내는 법을 두고 따지는 거나 매한가지일 테니까.”——“그건,” 그녀가 말했다. “장담컨대 제가 결코 하지 않을 일이지요.”——“그래, 그럼 나도 장담하마,” 그가 응수했다. “나도 다른 쪽 일은 결코 따지지 않으마.”\n\n이로써 다투던 양편 사이에 (부인의 말투를 빌리자면) 동맹이 맺어졌다. 그러는 사이 목사가 도착하고 말들이 준비되자, 지주는 누이의 충고를 따르겠노라 약속하고 길을 떠났으며, 누이는 이튿날 그를 뒤따를 채비를 했다.\n\n그러나 길에서 이 일들을 목사에게 털어놓고 보니, 두 사람은 정해 놓은 격식 따위는 얼마든지 생략해도 좋겠다는 데 의견이 일치했다. 그리하여 지주는 마음을 바꾸어, 우리가 이미 본 그대로의 방식으로 일을 밀고 나갔다.\n\n가엾은 존스에게 갖가지 불행이 닥치는 이야기.\n\n앞서 묘사한 그런 상황에 일이 놓여 있을 때, 아너 부인이 밀러 부인의 집에 도착해, 우리가 앞서 본 대로 존스를 일행에게서 불러냈다. 그와 단둘이 있게 되자 부인은 이렇게 입을 열었다.\n\n\"오, 도련님! 무슨 기운으로 이 말을 전해야 할지 모르겠어요. 도련님은 끝장나셨고, 가엾은 우리 아가씨도 끝장나셨고, 저도 끝장났어요.\" \"소피아에게 무슨 일이라도 생긴 거요?\" 존스가 미친 사람처럼 눈을 부릅뜨며 외쳤다. \"나쁜 일이란 나쁜 일은 다요.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"오, 다시는 그런 아가씨를 못 모실 거예요! 오, 내가 살아서 이런 날을 보게 되다니!\" 이 말에 존스는 잿빛으로 새파랗게 질려 떨며 더듬거렸지만, 아너는 계속했다. \"오! 존스 도련님, 저는 아가씨를 영영 잃었어요.\" \"어떻게요? 뭐라고요! 제발, 말해줘요. 오, 내 사랑 소피아!\" \"그렇게 부르실 만하죠.\" 아너가 말했다. \"제게도 더없이 소중한 아가씨였으니까요. 다시는 그런 자리를 못 얻을 거예요.\" \"당신 자리 따위 빌어먹을!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"어디 있어요, 무슨, 내 소피아가 어떻게 됐냐고요?\" \"그럼요, 그러시겠죠.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"하인 따위야 빌어먹어도 되겠죠. 내쫓겨서 아주 신세를 망쳐도, 그들이 어떻게 되든 아무 상관없겠죠. 하긴 그들은 다른 사람들처럼 피와 살을 가진 존재가 아니니까요. 아무렴, 그들이 어떻게 되든 아무 상관없겠죠.\" \"조금이라도 가엾은 마음, 동정심이 있거든,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"제발 부탁이니 소피아에게 무슨 일이 생겼는지 당장 말해줘요.\" \"그럼요, 도련님이 저를 가엾어하시는 것보다야 제가 도련님을 더 가엾어하죠.\" 아너가 대답했다. \"저는 도련님이 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨를 잃었다고 해서 빌어먹으라고는 안 하잖아요. 정말이지 도련님은 동정받을 만하고, 저도 동정받을 만하죠. 왜냐하면, 정말이지, 세상에 좋은 안주인이 있었다면 바로—\" \"무슨 일이 생겼냐고요?\" 존스가 거의 발광하듯 외쳤다. \"뭐냐고요? 뭐냐고요?\" 아너가 말했다. \"그게요, 도련님께도 제게도 일어날 수 있는 가장 나쁜 일이죠. 아버님이 도시로 올라오셔서, 우리 둘 모두에게서 아가씨를 데려가 버리셨어요.\" 여기서 존스는 그보다 더 나쁜 일이 아니라는 데 감사하며 무릎을 꿇었다. \"더 나쁜 일이 아니라뇨!\" 아너가 되뇌었다. \"우리 둘 중 누구에게 이보다 더 나쁜 일이 있을 수 있겠어요? 그분은 아가씨를 블리필 도련님과 결혼시키겠다고 맹세하면서 데려가셨어요. 그게 도련님께 위로가 되겠네요. 그리고 가엾은 저는 문밖으로 쫓겨났고요.\" \"정말이지, 아너 부인,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"당신은 나를 정신이 나갈 만큼 겁먹게 했소. 나는 소피아에게 무슨 끔찍한 사고라도 갑자기 닥친 줄 알았소. 그에 비하면 그녀가 블리필과 결혼하는 걸 보는 것조차 사소한 일일 그런 사고 말이오. 하지만 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 희망은 있는 법이오, 아너. 이 자유의 나라에서는 여자가 실제로 짐승 같은 완력으로 결혼당할 수는 없으니까.\" \"그럼요, 도련님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그건 맞아요. 도련님께는 어떤 희망이 있을지도 모르죠. 하지만 어이쿠 맙소사! 가엾은 저에게는 무슨 희망이 있겠어요? 그리고 정말이지, 도련님, 제가 이 모든 걸 도련님 때문에 겪고 있다는 걸 아셔야 해요. 나리께서 제게 품으신 노여움이라는 게 다, 제가 그랬듯이 블리필 도련님에 맞서 도련님 편을 들었기 때문이거든요.\" \"정말이지, 아너 부인,\" 그가 대답했다. \"나는 당신에게 진 빚을 잘 알고 있으며, 내 힘이 닿는 한 무엇이든 다 해서 갚으리다.\" \"어이쿠, 도련님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하인에게 한 자리를 잃은 것을 갚아줄 수 있는 게 똑같이 좋은 다른 자리를 얻는 것 말고 뭐가 있겠어요?\" \"절망하지 마시오, 아너 부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"당신을 다시 그 자리에 복직시켜 줄 수 있기를 바라오.\" \"어이쿠 맙소사, 도련님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그게 불가능한 일인 줄 뻔히 아는데 어떻게 그런 희망으로 제 마음을 부풀리겠어요? 나리께서 저를 그토록 미워하시는데 말예요. 하지만, 만일 도련님이 언젠가 우리 아가씨를 차지하신다면—정말이지 이제 저는 진심으로 그러시길 바라요. 도련님은 너그럽고 마음씨 고운 신사분이시고, 분명 아가씨를 사랑하시잖아요. 그리고 아가씨도 제 영혼처럼 도련님을 끔찍이 사랑하시는 게 분명해요. 부인해봤자 헛일이죠. 왜냐하면 우리 아가씨를 조금이라도 아는 사람이라면 누구나 알 수밖에 없으니까요. 가엾고 고운 아가씨는 속을 감추질 못하시거든요. 서로 사랑하는 두 사람이 행복하지 못하다면, 대체 누가 행복해야 하겠어요? 행복이란 게 늘 가진 것에 달린 건 아니죠. 게다가 우리 아가씨는 두 분이 쓰고도 남을 만큼 가지셨고요. 그러니 정말이지, 말하자면, 그렇게 사랑하는 두 분을 떼어놓는다는 건 세상에 그보다 안타까운 일이 없을 거예요. 아니, 제 생각으로는, 결국 두 분이 만나게 되실 거라고 확신해요. 될 일이라면 막을 수가 없으니까요. 하늘에서 맺어진 혼인이라면, 땅 위의 어떤 치안판사도 그걸 깨뜨릴 수 없죠. 정말이지 저는 서플 목사님이 좀 더 기개가 있어서, 딸을 그 뜻과 어긋나게 억지로 시집보내려는 게 얼마나 못된 짓인지 나리께 말씀드렸으면 싶어요. 하지만 그분은 모든 게 나리께 매여 있으니까요. 그래서 그 가엾은 분은, 신앙심 깊고 좋은 분이라서 나리 등 뒤에서는 그런 짓이 나쁘다고 말씀하시면서도, 면전에서는 제 영혼이 제 것이라고 감히 말하지 못하시죠. 정말이지 저는 그분이 방금 전처럼 대담하게 구는 걸 한 번도 본 적이 없어요. 저는 나리께서 그분을 때리시는 줄 알았다니까요. 도련님, 우울해하시거나 절망하지 마세요. 아가씨를 확신하실 수 있는 한 일은 더 잘 풀릴 거예요. 그리고 그건 제가 분명히 장담하는데, 아가씨는 결코 다른 어떤 남자와 결혼하는 데 동의하지 않으실 테니까요. 정말이지 저는 나리께서 화김에 아가씨를 해코지하실까 봐 무섭기 짝이 없어요. 나리는 어마어마하게 격정적인 분이시거든요. 그리고 가엾은 아가씨가 상심으로 가슴이 찢어지실까 봐도 무서워요. 아가씨는 병아리처럼 여린 마음을 지니셨으니까요. 제 생각엔, 아가씨가 제 용기를 조금만 가지셨더라면 좋았을 텐데 안타까워요. 만일 제가 어떤 젊은 남자를 사랑하는데 아버지가 저를 가두려 든다면, 저는 그분 눈알을 후벼파서라도 그 남자에게 갈 거예요. 하지만 이 경우엔 큰 재산이 걸려 있고, 그걸 주고 안 주고는 아버님 손에 달려 있죠. 그게 정말이지 좀 사정을 다르게 만들 수도 있겠네요.\"\n\n존스가 앞서의 이 긴 사설에 줄곧 귀를 기울였는지, 아니면 그저 말 사이에 끼어들 틈이 없었던 탓인지는 내가 가늠할 수 없다. 다만 그는 단 한 번도 대꾸하려 들지 않았고, 그녀 역시 패트리지가 방으로 뛰어들어와 그 대단한 귀부인이 계단에 와 있다고 알릴 때까지 단 한 번도 말을 멈추지 않았다.\n\n이제 존스가 빠진 진퇴양난에 견줄 만한 것은 없었다. 아너는 그와 벨라스턴 귀부인 사이에 어떤 친분이 있는지 전혀 몰랐고, 그가 그 사실을 알리고 싶은 사람 가운데 그녀는 세상에서 거의 가장 마지막에 꼽힐 사람이었다. 이 다급함과 곤경 속에서 그는 (흔히 그렇듯이) 가장 나쁜 길을 택했다. 그녀를 귀부인에게 드러내는 편이—그래봤자 별일도 아니었을 텐데—나았으련만, 그는 귀부인을 그녀에게 드러내는 쪽을 골랐다. 그리하여 그는 아너를 숨기기로 마음먹고, 겨우 침대 뒤로 그녀를 밀어 넣어 휘장을 칠 시간밖에 없었다.\n\n가엾은 집주인 여자와 그 가족 때문에 존스가 하루 종일 분주했던 일, 아너 부인이 불러일으킨 공포, 그리고 벨라스턴 귀부인의 갑작스러운 도착으로 그가 빠진 혼란이, 그 모든 것이 합쳐져 이전의 생각들을 그의 머릿속에서 몰아내 버렸다. 그래서 병자 노릇을 해야 한다는 것이 그의 기억에 단 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다. 사실 그의 화사한 옷차림도, 생기 도는 얼굴빛도 그런 연기를 조금도 받쳐주지 못했을 테지만.\n\n그리하여 그는 그녀가 기대했던 것보다는 오히려 바랐던 모습에 더 가깝게 부인을 맞이했다. 얼굴에 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 좋은 기분을 띠고서, 조금도 흐트러진 기색을—실제로든 꾸며서든—드러내지 않은 채로 말이다.\n\n벨라스턴 귀부인은 방에 들어서기가 무섭게 침대에 털썩 주저앉았다. \"자, 내 사랑 존스,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이렇게 무엇도 나를 당신에게서 오래 떼어놓지 못한다는 걸 알겠죠. 어쩌면 당신에게 화를 내야 할지도 몰라요. 하루 종일 당신을 보지도, 소식을 듣지도 못했으니까요. 보아하니 당신의 병환쯤은 외출을 허락했을 텐데 말이에요. 아니, 출산 뒤 손님맞이하는 귀부인처럼 곱게 차려입고 하루 종일 방 안에 앉아 있지는 않았겠죠. 하지만, 그래도 내가 당신을 나무라려는 거라고는 생각 마세요. 나는 결코 아내의 심술을 부려서 남편의 차가운 행실에 핑계를 만들어주지는 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 벨라스턴 부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"부인께서 제가 그저 명령을 기다리고 있었을 뿐인데 직무를 게을리했다고 책망하지는 않으시리라 믿습니다. 내 사랑하는 그대여, 불평할 이유가 있는 쪽이 누구입니까? 어젯밤 약속을 어겨, 가엾은 사내를 기다리게 하고, 바라게 하고, 한숨짓게 하고, 애태우게 한 사람이 누구입니까?\"\n\n\"그 얘기는 꺼내지 마세요, 내 사랑 존스,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"그 사정을 안다면 당신은 나를 가엾어할 거예요. 한마디로, 신분 있는 여자들이 세상이라는 익살극을 이어가느라 어리석은 자들의 무례를 얼마나 견뎌야 하는지는 상상도 못 할 거예요. 하지만 당신이 그렇게 애태우고 바라느라 아무 탈도 나지 않아서 기뻐요. 당신은 평생 이보다 더 좋아 보인 적이 없으니까요. 정말이지! 존스, 당신은 지금 이 순간 아도니스의 초상화 모델로 앉아도 되겠어요.\"\n\n명예를 아는 사내들이 보기에, 어떤 도발의 말들은 오직 한 대 후려치는 것으로만 제대로 답할 수 있다고들 한다. 연인들 사이에서는 아마도 오직 입맞춤으로만 답할 수 있는 표현들이 있을 것이다. 그런데 지금 벨라스턴 귀부인이 존스에게 건넨 찬사가 바로 이런 부류에 속하는 듯했다. 더구나 그것은, 부인이 혀로 표현할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 부드러운 마음을 담은 눈길과 함께였으니 말이다.\n\n존스는 분명 이 순간 상상할 수 있는 가장 거북하고 곤혹스러운 처지 가운데 하나에 놓여 있었다. 앞서 우리가 썼던 비유를 이어가자면, 도발은 부인이 했으되, 존스는 제삼자가 있는 자리에서 그 만족을 받을 수도, 그것을 청하려 들 수조차 없었다. 이런 종류의 결투에서는 입회인을 두는 것이 무기의 법도에 어긋나기 때문이다. 자기 말고 다른 여자가 거기 있다는 걸 모르는 벨라스턴 귀부인에게는 이런 사정이 떠오르지 않았으므로, 그녀는 큰 놀라움 속에 한동안 존스의 대답을 기다렸다. 한편 존스는 자기가 우스꽝스러운 꼴이 되었음을 알고서 멀찍이 서 있었고, 마땅한 답을 감히 내놓지 못해 아예 아무 답도 하지 못했다. 만일 이 장면이 훨씬 더 오래 이어졌더라면, 이보다 더 희극적이면서도 동시에 더 비극적인 광경은 상상하기 어려웠을 것이다. 부인은 이미 두세 번 낯빛이 변했고, 침대에서 일어났다가 다시 앉기를 거듭했으며, 존스는 땅이 자기 발밑으로 꺼지거나 집이 머리 위로 무너지기를 바라고 있었다. 그때 묘한 사고가 일어나, 키케로의 웅변으로도 마키아벨리의 책략으로도 완전한 망신 없이는 그를 끌어낼 수 없었을 그 곤경에서 그를 구해 주었다.\n\n그것은 다름 아니라, 곤드레만드레 취한 젊은 나이팅게일의 등장이었다. 아니, 정확히 말하면, 이성은 못 쓰게 만들면서도 사지는 멀쩡히 쓰게 내버려 두는 그런 취기 상태로 말이다.\n\n밀러 부인과 딸들은 침대에 들어 있었고, 패트리지는 부엌 난롯가에서 담뱃대를 피우고 있었다. 그래서 그는 아무 제지도 받지 않고 존스의 방문 앞에 다다랐다. 그는 그 문을 박차고 열어젖히고는 거리낌 없이 들어오려 했는데, 그때 존스가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 그를 막으러 달려갔다. 어찌나 효과적으로 막았던지, 나이팅게일은 침대에 누가 앉아 있는지 볼 수 있을 만큼 문 안으로 들어오지조차 못했다.\n\n사실 나이팅게일은 존스의 방을 자기가 묵던 방으로 착각한 것이었다. 그래서 그는 들어가겠다고 한사코 우기며, 제 침대에서 자기를 떼어놓지 못한다고 거듭 맹세했다. 하지만 존스가 그를 이겨내, 계단의 소란에 곧 주인을 도우러 올라온 패트리지의 손에 그를 넘겼다.\n\n이제 존스는 마지못해 자기 방으로 돌아가야 했는데, 들어서는 바로 그 순간 그는 벨라스턴 귀부인이 그리 크지는 않은 탄성을 토해내는 소리를 들었다. 동시에 그녀가 몹시 동요한 채 의자에 몸을 내던지는 것을 보았으니, 여린 체질의 부인이었다면 그것은 히스테리 발작이었을 것이다.\n\n사실 부인은 두 사내의 몸싸움에 겁을 먹고서—그 결말이 어찌 될지 알 수 없었던 데다, 나이팅게일이 제 침대로 오겠다고 여러 번 맹세하는 것을 들은 터라—제가 아는 숨을 자리로 물러나려 했던 것이다. 그런데 거기가 이미 다른 사람에게 차지되어 있는 것을 발견하고는 크게 당황하고 말았다.\n\n\"존스 씨, 이런 대접을 참으란 말인가요?\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"사내 중에서도 가장 비열한 자여! 당신이 나를 드러내 보인 이 천한 것은 대체 누구죠?\" \"천한 것이라고요!\" 아너가 숨은 자리에서 격한 분노로 튀어나오며 외쳤다. \"원 참 어처구니가 없네! 천한 것이라뇨? 제가 아무리 가난한 천것이라도, 저는 정직해요. 이건 저보다 부유한 어떤 사람들은 못 하는 말이죠.\"\n\n존스는, 더 노련한 바람둥이였다면 그랬을 법하게 아너 부인의 분노를 누그러뜨리는 데 곧장 매달리는 대신, 제 운명의 별을 저주하며 자기야말로 세상에서 가장 불운한 사내라고 한탄하기 시작했다. 그러고는 이내 벨라스턴 귀부인을 향해, 자기는 결백하다는 매우 터무니없는 항변을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 이 무렵 부인은 이성의 쓸모를 되찾았는데—그녀는 세상 어느 여자 못지않게, 특히 이런 경우에 이성을 곧잘 부려 쓰는 사람이었다—차분히 대답했다. \"이봐요, 변명할 것 없어요. 이제 저 사람이 누군지 알겠으니까요. 처음엔 아너 부인을 못 알아봤어요. 하지만 이제 알겠으니, 그녀와 당신 사이에 잘못된 일이 있으리라고는 조금도 의심하지 않아요. 그리고 저 여자는 내가 당신을 찾아온 것을 두고 그릇된 해석을 할 만큼 분별없는 사람이 아닐 거예요. 나는 늘 저 여자의 친구였고, 앞으로는 훨씬 더 큰 힘이 되어줄 수 있을지도 모르죠.\"\n\n아너 부인은 성을 잘 내는 만큼이나 또 쉽게 풀어지는 사람이었다. 그래서 벨라스턴 귀부인이 부드러운 어조를 띠는 것을 듣자, 그녀 역시 제 어조를 누그러뜨렸다. \"정말이지, 마님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 늘 마님이 제게 베푸신 우정을 기꺼이 인정해 왔답니다. 정말이지 저는 마님 같은 좋은 친구를 가져본 적이 없어요. 그리고 정말이지, 제가 말씀드린 상대가 마님이라는 걸 이제 알고 나니, 너무 화가 나서 혀를 깨물고 싶을 지경이에요. 제가 마님을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 해석을 하다니요. 정말이지 저 같은 하인이 그런 대단한 귀부인에 대해 이러니저러니 생각하는 건 어울리지 않는 일이죠. 아니, 하인이었다는 말이에요. 사실 저는 이제 아무의 하인도 아니니까요. 그러니 제가 더 비참한 천것이죠. 저는 더없이 좋은 안주인을 잃었어요—\" 여기서 아너는 한바탕 눈물을 쏟아내는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼다. \"울지 마라, 얘야,\" 마음씨 좋은 귀부인이 말했다. \"어쩌면 네게 보상해 줄 방도를 찾을 수도 있을 거야. 내일 아침에 나를 찾아오너라.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 바닥에 떨어져 있던 부채를 집어 들고, 존스는 쳐다보지도 않은 채 매우 위엄 있게 방을 걸어 나갔다. 신분 있는 여자들의 그 뻔뻔함에는 일종의 위엄이 깃들어 있어서, 그보다 못한 자들은 이런 처지에서 그것을 흉내 내려 해봐야 헛수고일 뿐이다.\n\n존스는 계단을 내려가는 그녀를 뒤따르며 거듭 손을 내밀었으나, 그녀는 한사코 그 손을 마다했고, 그가 그녀 앞에 허리 굽혀 인사하며 서 있는 것도 아랑곳없이 가마에 올라탔다.\n\n그가 위층으로 돌아오자, 그와 아너 부인 사이에 긴 대화가 오갔다. 그녀가 겪은 소동 뒤 옷매무새를 가다듬는 동안에 말이다. 이 대화의 주제는 그가 젊은 아가씨에게 보인 부정(不貞)이었고, 그녀는 그것을 두고 몹시 신랄하게 늘어놓았다. 그러나 존스는 마침내 그녀를 달랠 방도를 찾아냈고, 그뿐만 아니라 결코 깨뜨리지 않겠다는 비밀 엄수의 약속과, 이튿날 아침 소피아를 찾아 나서서 나리의 동태에 관한 더 자세한 소식을 그에게 가져다주겠다는 약속까지 얻어냈다.\n\n이리하여 이 불운한 소동은 오직 아너 부인만 만족시킨 채 끝이 났다. 비밀이란 (내 독자 가운데 몇몇은 아마 경험으로 인정하겠지만) 흔히 매우 값진 재산이기 때문이다. 그리고 그것은 비밀을 충실히 지키는 사람에게만이 아니라, 때로는 그것을 여기저기 속삭이고 다녀 마침내, 누구나 다 아는 사실을 감춰준다는 명목으로 대가를 치르는 그 어수룩한 당사자만 빼고 모든 사람의 귀에 들어가게 만드는 사람에게도 그러하다.\n\n짧고도 흐뭇한 이야기.\n\n존스에게서 그토록 많은 은혜를 입었음에도, 밀러 부인은 이튿날 아침 전날 밤 그의 방에서 벌어진 한바탕 소동을 두고 부드러운 충고 몇 마디를 건네지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그 말은 어찌나 부드럽고 다정한지, 오직 존스 씨 자신의 진정한 행복만을 바라는 마음에서 나온 것임을 — 실제로도 그러했다 — 분명히 드러내고 있어서, 그는 기분이 상하기는커녕 그 선한 부인의 훈계를 고맙게 받아들였고, 지난 일에 깊은 유감을 표했으며, 할 수 있는 한 변명을 한 뒤, 다시는 그런 소란을 이 집에 끌어들이지 않겠노라 약속했다.\n\n하지만 밀러 부인이 처음 마주쳤을 때 단둘이서 짤막한 잔소리를 삼가지는 않았어도, 그날 아침 그가 아래층으로 불려 내려간 까닭은 훨씬 더 즐거운 종류의 것이었다. 다름 아니라 낸시 양에게 아버지 노릇을 대신 해 주고, 그녀를 나이팅게일 씨에게 시집보내는 일이었다. 나이팅게일 씨는 이제 옷을 말끔히 차려입은 채로, 이토록 분별없는 방식으로 아내를 맞아들이는 사내라면 응당 이래야 한다고 여길 만큼 — 내 독자들 가운데 상당수가 그렇게 생각하겠지만 — 충분히 말짱한 정신이었다.\n\n그리고 여기서 어쩌면, 이 젊은 신사가 어떻게 큰아버지에게서 빠져나왔는지, 또 우리가 전날 밤에 본 그 꼴로 나타나게 되었는지를 설명해 두는 것이 마땅하겠다.\n\n큰아버지가 조카를 데리고 자기 숙소에 이르렀을 때, 그는 한편으로는 제 취향을 마음껏 채우려고(그는 술병을 끔찍이도 사랑했으므로), 또 한편으로는 조카가 곧장 제 뜻을 실행에 옮기지 못하도록 만들려고, 식탁에 술을 내오라 일렀다. 그러고는 그것으로 어찌나 부지런히 젊은 신사를 들이켜게 했던지, 술에 그리 익숙하지는 않으나 거절함으로써 불복종이나 무례를 저지를 만큼 술을 싫어하지도 않던 조카는 이내 완전히 뻗어 버리고 말았다.\n\n큰아버지가 막 이 승리를 거두고 조카에게 잠자리를 마련해 주려던 참에, 한 전령이 도착해 어떤 소식을 전했는데, 그것이 어찌나 그를 당황시키고 충격에 빠뜨렸던지, 그는 순식간에 조카에 대한 온갖 신경을 잊어버렸고, 그의 온 마음은 오직 제 자신의 일로만 가득 차 버렸다.\n\n이 느닷없고도 비통한 소식이란 다름 아니라, 그의 딸이 거의 그가 자리를 비운 첫 순간을 틈타 이웃의 한 젊은 성직자와 달아나 버렸다는 것이었다. 그 사내에 대해 아버지가 내세울 만한 흠이라곤 단 하나, 곧 그가 빈털터리라는 것뿐이었음에도, 그녀는 그 아버지에게조차 자신의 연애를 한 번도 털어놓지 않는 편이 옳다고 여겼다. 게다가 어찌나 교묘하게 일을 꾸몄던지, 그 일이 이렇게 끝장을 보기 전까지는 누구도 단 한 번 의심해 본 적이 없었다.\n\n늙은 나이팅게일 씨는 이 소식을 듣자마자 극도의 혼란 속에서 곧장 역마차를 준비시키라 일렀고, 조카를 하인에게 맡겨 둔 채, 제가 무엇을 하는지도 어디로 가는지도 거의 알지 못한 채로 집을 나서 버렸다.\n\n이렇게 큰아버지가 떠나고, 하인이 조카를 잠자리로 모시러 와서 그 목적으로 그를 깨워, 마침내 큰아버지가 가 버렸음을 알아듣게 하자, 그는 자기에게 베풀어진 친절한 시중을 받아들이기는커녕 가마를 불러 달라고 우겼다. 그러자 그러지 말라는 엄한 지시를 받은 바 없던 하인은 선뜻 그 말을 따랐고, 그리하여 다시 밀러 부인의 집으로 인도된 그는, 앞서 이야기한 대로 비틀거리며 존스 씨의 방으로 올라갔던 것이다.\n\n이제 큰아버지라는 걸림돌이 치워졌으니(젊은 나이팅게일은 아직 그것이 어떤 식으로 그리되었는지는 몰랐지만), 그리고 모두가 금세 채비를 마치자, 어머니와 존스 씨, 나이팅게일 씨와 그의 연인은 삯마차에 올라 닥터스 코먼스로 향했다. 그곳에서 낸시 양은, 흔한 말로 하자면, 이내 정숙한 여인이 되었고, 가엾은 어머니는 그 말의 가장 순수한 뜻 그대로 온 인류 가운데 가장 행복한 사람 중 하나가 되었다.\n\n* 닥터스 코먼스: 당시 런던에서 혼인 허가증 등을 발급하던 교회법 관련 법조 기관.\n이제 존스 씨는 그 가엾은 여인과 그 가족에게 베푼 선행이 행복한 결말에 이른 것을 보고 나서, 자신의 일에 마음을 쏟기 시작했다. 그런데 여기서, 내 독자들 가운데 많은 이가 이렇게 남의 일로 제 몸을 수고롭게 한 그의 어리석음을 나무랄까 봐, 또 몇몇은 그가 실제보다 더 사심 없이 행동했다고 여길까 봐, 우리는 독자에게 이렇게 일러 두는 것이 마땅하다고 본다. 그는 이 일에 무심하기는커녕, 사실 그것을 이 마지막 결말로 이끄는 데 상당히 큰 이해관계를 지니고 있었다고 말이다.\n\n이 그럴듯한 역설을 단번에 풀어 보자면, 그는 테렌티우스의 작중 인물처럼 진심으로 이렇게 말할 수 있는 사람이었다. \"나는 인간이다. 인간의 일이라면 그 무엇도 나와 무관하다 여기지 않는다.\" 그는 그 누구의 불행이나 행복에 대해서도 결코 무심한 구경꾼인 적이 없었다. 그리고 그는 자기 자신이 그 어느 쪽에 보탬이 된 만큼 그 둘 중 하나를 크게 느꼈다. 그러므로 그는 한 가족 전체를 가장 비참한 밑바닥에서 가장 드높은 기쁨의 정점으로 끌어올리는 도구가 되면서, 제 자신에게도 크나큰 행복을 안기지 않을 수 없었다. 어쩌면 세상 사람들이 더없이 혹독한 수고를 견디고, 더러는 가장 깊은 죄악을 헤쳐 나가며 스스로 사들이는 것보다 더 큰 행복을 말이다.\n\n그와 같은 기질을 지닌 독자라면 어쩌면 이 짧은 장에 풍성한 내용이 담겨 있다고 여길 것이다. 반면 다른 이들은 아마, 이토록 짧긴 해도, 이 장이 본래의 큰 목적과는 무관하니 차라리 통째로 덜어 내는 편이 나았으리라 바랄지도 모른다. 내 짐작에 그들이 단정 짓는 그 목적이란, 존스 씨를 교수대로, 아니 될 수만 있다면 더 한심한 파국으로 끌고 가는 것일 테니 말이다.\n\n9장.\n여러 종류의 연애편지를 담은 장.\n\n존스는 집에 돌아와 책상 위에 놓인 다음의 편지들을 발견했는데, 다행히도 그것들을 보내진 순서대로 열어 보았다.\n첫째 편지.\n\n\"분명 저는 어떤 야릇한 홀림에 빠져 있나 봅니다. 아무리 굳게 다지고 아무리 옳게 세운 결심이라 해도 한순간도 지키지를 못하니 말이에요. 어젯밤 저는 다시는 당신을 보지 않겠다고 마음먹었습니다. 그런데 오늘 아침이 되니, 당신 말대로 이 일을 정말 해명할 수 있다면 그 말을 들어 보고 싶어집니다. 그러면서도 저는 그것이 불가능하다는 걸 알아요. 당신이 꾸며 낼 수 있는 말이라면 저는 이미 제 자신에게 죄다 해 보았으니까요.----어쩌면 아닐지도 모르죠. 어쩌면 당신의 둘러대는 재간이 더 셀지도 모르겠어요. 그러니 이 편지를 받는 순간 곧장 제게로 오세요. 당신이 변명을 지어낼 수 있다면, 저는 그것을 믿겠다고 거의 약속이라도 할 것 같아요. 배신까지 당하고도----더는 생각하지 않겠어요.----곧바로 제게로 오세요.----이것이 제가 쓴 세 번째 편지예요. 앞의 두 통은 태워 버렸어요----이것마저 태워 버리고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같아요----부디 제가 제정신을 잃지 않기를 바랄 뿐이에요.----어서 제게로 오세요.\"\n\n# LETTER II.\n\n편지 2.\n\"용서받기를, 아니 내 집 문 안에 한 발이라도 들이기를 바란다면, 지금 이 순간 당장 내게로 오시오.\"\n\n편지 3.\n\"이제야 알았습니다. 제 쪽지들이 당신 숙소에 닿았을 때 당신은 집에 없었더군요. 이 편지를 받는 즉시 나를 만나러 오세요. 나는 한 발짝도 밖으로 나가지 않을 것이며, 당신 말고는 그 누구도 들이지 않겠습니다. 분명 당신을 오래 붙들어 둘 일이란 없을 테지요.\"\n\n존스가 이 세 통의 짧은 편지를 막 다 읽었을 때, 나이팅게일 씨가 방으로 들어왔다. \"이보게, 톰.\" 그가 말했다. \"어젯밤 그 소동을 치르고 나서 벨라스턴 부인에게서 무슨 소식이라도 왔나?\" (이제 그 집 안에서는 그 부인이 누구인지 누구에게도 비밀이 아니었던 것이다.) \"벨라스턴 부인이라니?\" 존스가 자못 진지하게 되물었다. \"이런, 톰.\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"친구한테 그렇게 숨길 것까진 없잖나. 내가 어젯밤엔 너무 취해서 그분을 못 봤지만, 가면무도회에서는 봤다네. 자네는 내가 그 요정 나라의 여왕이 누구인지 모를 거라고 생각하나?\" \"그래서 자네는 정말로 가면무도회에서 그 부인을 알아본 건가?\" 존스가 말했다. \"그렇다네, 내 영혼을 걸고 알아봤지.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"그 후로도 자네에게 스무 번은 넌지시 운을 띄웠어. 다만 자네가 늘 그 대목에서 어찌나 예민하게 굴던지, 대놓고 말을 못 했을 뿐이야. 친구, 이 일에 대한 자네의 그 지나친 조심성을 보아하니, 자네는 그 부인의 사람됨보다는 외모 쪽을 더 잘 아는 모양일세. 화내지 말게, 톰. 하지만 내 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 자네가 그 부인이 망쳐 놓은 첫 번째 젊은이는 아니라네. 그분의 평판은 조금도 위태롭지 않으니, 내 말을 믿게.\"\n\n존스는 자신의 연애가 시작될 때 그 부인을 베스타의 처녀 같은 부류라고 상상할 만한 근거가 없었다. 그러나 그는 이 도시의 물정에 완전히 어두웠고 아는 이도 거의 없었던 터라, 흔히 데미렙이라 불리는 그런 사람됨에 대해서는 알지 못했다. 그것은 곧, 덕이라는 이름과 겉모습을 두르고서 마음에 드는 남자라면 누구와든 정을 통하는 여자를 일컫는 말이다. 지나치게 깔끔을 떠는 일부 부인들은 그런 여자와 함께 있는 모습을 보이려 하지 않지만, 그래도 (그들 표현을 빌리자면) 온 도시 사람들이 다 그 집을 드나든다. 한마디로, 누구나 그 정체를 알면서도 아무도 그 이름으로는 부르지 않는 여자다.\n\n* 베스타의 처녀: 고대 로마에서 화로의 여신 베스타를 섬기던, 순결을 지킨 여사제. 정숙한 처녀를 빗대는 표현.\n그리하여 존스는 나이팅게일이 자신의 정사를 속속들이 알고 있음을 알아차렸고, 지금껏 자신이 지켜 온 그 까다로운 조심성이 이 경우에는 그리 필요치 않았던 게 아닌가 의심하기 시작했다. 그래서 그는 친구의 혀에 자유를 허락하고, 그 부인에 대해 아는 것이나 들은 것이 있으면 숨김없이 말해 달라고 청했다.\n\n나이팅게일은 다른 여러 면에서 기질이 다소 여성스러운 데가 있었지만, 수다를 떠는 데에는 제법 강한 끌림을 지니고 있었다. 그리하여 존스에게서 마음껏 말해도 좋다는 허락을 받기가 무섭게, 그는 그 부인에 관한 긴 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그 이야기에는 그녀의 명예를 크게 떨어뜨릴 시시콜콜한 내용이 워낙 많았던지라, 우리는 지체 높은 모든 여인에 대해 너무나 큰 동정심을 품고 있는 터라 차마 그것을 옮기지 못하겠다. 우리는 훗날 우리 작품에 주석을 달 이들에게, 무언가 악의적인 해석을 갖다 붙이거나, 우리 머릿속에는 떠오른 적도 없는 추문의 작가로 우리를 본의 아니게 만들어 버릴 빌미를 주는 일을 조심스레 피하고자 한다.\n\n존스는 나이팅게일이 하는 말을 모두 아주 주의 깊게 듣고 나서 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그것을 본 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"아니 이런! 설마 자네 사랑에 빠진 건 아니겠지! 내 이야기들이 자네 마음을 이렇게 흔들 줄 알았더라면, 맹세코 자네는 결코 그것을 듣지 못했을 걸세.\" \"오, 친구!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"나는 그 여자에게 너무 얽혀 버려서, 어떻게 빠져나와야 할지 모르겠네. 사랑이라니, 그건 아닐세, 친구. 다만 나는 그분께 신세를 졌어. 그것도 아주 큰 신세를. 자네가 이미 그렇게 많이 아니, 나도 자네에게 아주 솔직히 털어놓겠네. 어쩌면 내가 지금까지 빵 한 조각 굶지 않은 것도 오로지 그분 덕분일지 몰라. 그런 여자를 내가 어떻게 저버릴 수 있겠나? 그러나 나는 그녀를 버려야만 하네. 안 그러면, 그녀보다 나에게 한없이 더 값진 사람에게 더없이 시커먼 배신을 저지르는 꼴이 되고 마네. 나이팅게일, 그건 좀처럼 누구도 상상하기 어려운 격정을 내가 품고 있는 여인일세. 나는 어찌 처신해야 할지 갈피를 못 잡고 반쯤 정신이 나가 있다네.\" \"그런데 말이지, 그 다른 여인은 떳떳한 정인인가?\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"떳떳하냐고!\" 존스가 답했다. \"지금껏 그 어떤 입김도 감히 그녀의 평판을 더럽히지 못했네. 가장 향기로운 바람도 그녀의 명예만큼 맑지 못하고, 가장 투명한 시냇물도 그만큼 깨끗하지 못해. 그녀는 마음에서나 몸에서나 온통 완벽함 그 자체일세. 이 우주에서 가장 아름다운 존재지. 그러면서도 그녀는 그토록 고귀하고 드높은 자질을 갖추고 있어서, 비록 그녀가 한순간도 내 생각에서 떠나지 않건만, 나는 그녀의 아름다움을 직접 눈으로 볼 때가 아니고서는 그 아름다움을 거의 떠올리지조차 않는다네.\" \"그렇다면 친구, 자네는 그런 인연을 손에 쥐고 있으면서, 그런 여자를 떠나는 일에 한순간이라도 망설일 수 있단 말인가, 그런—\" \"그만.\" 존스가 말했다. \"더는 그녀를 헐뜯지 말게. 나는 배은망덕하다는 생각만으로도 진저리가 나네.\" \"흥!\" 상대가 대꾸했다. \"그녀가 이런 식의 은혜를 베푼 사람이 자네가 처음은 아닐세. 그녀는 마음에 드는 사람에게는 놀랄 만큼 후하지. 하지만 내 말 좀 들어 보게, 그녀의 호의란 워낙 약삭빠르게 베풀어지는 것이라, 사람의 고마움이 아니라 차라리 허영심을 부추기는 쪽이라네.\" 요컨대 나이팅게일은 이 대목으로 한참을 더 나아가며, 자기가 진실이라 맹세하는 그 부인의 이야기를 친구에게 어찌나 많이 늘어놓았던지, 존스의 가슴에서 그녀에 대한 모든 존경심을 깡그리 걷어내고 말았다. 그리고 그의 고마움도 그에 비례하여 줄어들었다. 실로 그는 자기가 받은 모든 호의를 은혜라기보다는 차라리 품삯으로 여기기 시작했고, 이는 그녀뿐 아니라 자기 자신마저도 스스로의 눈에 깎아내리는 일이어서, 그는 두 사람 모두에 대해 잔뜩 기분이 상해 버렸다. 이 역겨움에서 그의 마음은 자연스러운 흐름을 타고 소피아에게로 향했다. 그녀의 덕성, 그녀의 순결함, 자신을 향한 그녀의 사랑, 자신 때문에 그녀가 겪은 고초가 온통 그의 생각을 가득 채웠고, 벨라스턴 부인과의 관계는 한층 더 혐오스럽게만 보이게 만들었다. 이 모든 것의 결말은 이러했다. 이제 그는 그녀와의 일을 일종의 고용살이로 보게 되었는데, 그 고용살이에서 스스로 발을 빼는 일은 곧 자기 빵줄을 잃는 일이었음에도, 그는 그럴듯한 구실만 찾을 수 있다면 그녀를 떠나기로 마음을 굳혔다. 이를 친구에게 털어놓자, 나이팅게일은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 이내 말했다. \"찾았네, 이 친구야! 확실한 방법을 알아냈어. 그녀에게 청혼을 하게. 그 성공에 내 목을 걸어도 좋네.\" \"청혼이라고?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그래, 청혼을 하란 말일세.\" 나이팅게일이 답했다. \"그러면 그녀는 단번에 발을 뺄 거야. 예전에 그녀가 데리고 있던 젊은이를 하나 알았는데, 그가 그녀에게 진심으로 청혼을 했다가, 그 수고의 대가로 곧장 쫓겨났다네.\"\n\n존스는 그런 시험을 감히 해 볼 수 없노라고 잘라 말했다. \"어쩌면,\" 그가 말했다. \"그녀가 다른 남자보다 내 입에서 나온 이 청혼에는 덜 충격을 받을지도 모르지. 그러다 그녀가 정말로 내 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들이면, 그땐 나는 어찌 되는 건가? 제 덫에 제가 걸려 영영 끝장나는 게 아닌가.\" \"아닐세.\" 나이팅게일이 답했다. \"내가 자네에게 언제든 그 덫에서 빠져나올 방책 하나를 일러 줄 수만 있다면 그럴 일은 없지.\" \"그게 대체 무슨 방책이란 말인가?\" 존스가 받아쳤다. \"이런 거라네.\" 나이팅게일이 답했다. \"내가 말한 그 젊은이는 세상에서 나와 가장 가까운 친구들 가운데 하나인데, 그 후로 그녀가 자기에게 못된 짓을 몇 가지 저지른 일로 단단히 화가 나 있어서, 분명 아무 어려움 없이 자네에게 그녀의 편지를 보여 줄 걸세. 그것을 빌미로 자네는 점잖게 그녀와 갈라설 수 있고, 만약 그녀가 정말로 그 매듭을 묶고 싶어 한다면—나는 그럴 리 없다고 확신하지만—그 매듭이 묶이기 전에 발을 뺄 수 있지.\"\n얼마간 망설인 끝에, 존스는 이 장담에 힘입어 동의했다. 그러나 그는 면전에서 그 일을 꺼낼 만한 배짱이 없다고 맹세했으므로, 나이팅게일이 불러 주는 대로 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다.\n\n\"부인,\n불행히도 밖에서 약속에 매여 있던 탓에, 부인의 분부가 당도한 바로 그 순간에 그 영광을 받들지 못한 것이 못내 마음에 걸립니다. 이제 와 부인께 제 처지를 변명해야 하는 이 지체가 그 불운을 더욱 무겁게 합니다. 오, 벨라스턴 부인! 이 짓궂은 사고들로 인해 부인의 평판이 행여 드러날까 제가 얼마나 두려움에 떨었는지요! 그것을 지킬 길은 오직 하나뿐입니다. 그것이 무엇인지 굳이 입에 올릴 필요는 없겠지요. 다만 이 말만은 하게 해 주십시오. 부인의 명예가 제 명예만큼이나 제게 소중하듯, 제 유일한 야심은 제 자유를 부인의 발치에 바치는 영광을 얻는 것뿐입니다. 그리고 제 말을 믿어 주십시오. 부인을 영원히 제 사람이라 부를 법적 권리를 부인께서 너그러이 베풀어 주시지 않는 한, 저는 결코 온전한 행복을 누릴 수 없으리라는 것을. 저는,\n\n부인의,\n더없이 깊은 존경을 담아,\n\n부인께 더없이 큰 신세를 진,\n순종하는 미천한 종,\n\n토머스 존스 올림.\"\n이에 그녀는 곧장 다음과 같은 답장을 보내왔다.\n\n\"선생,\n당신의 그 점잖은 편지를 다 읽고 나니, 그 냉랭함과 격식으로 미루어, 당신이 이미 말씀하신 그 법적 권리를 손에 쥐고 있다고, 아니 우리가 여러 해 동안 부부라는 그 괴물 같은 짐승을 이루고 살아왔다고 맹세라도 할 뻔했습니다. 그래, 당신은 정말로 나를 바보로 여기는 것입니까? 아니면 당신이 내 정신을 송두리째 홀려, 내가 내 전 재산을 당신 손아귀에 고스란히 넘겨, 당신이 내 돈으로 당신 쾌락이나 떠받치도록 거들게 만들 수 있다고 자신하는 것입니까? 이런 것이 내가 기대했던 사랑의 증거란 말입니까? 이것이 그 보답입니까—? 그러나 나는 당신을 나무라는 것조차 떳떳지 못하다 여기며, 당신의 그 깊디깊은 존경에는 그저 감탄해 마지않습니다.\n\n추신. 다시 고쳐 볼 겨를이 없습니다. 어쩌면 제 본심보다 더 많은 말을 했는지도 모르겠습니다. 오늘 저녁 여덟 시에 내게로 오세요.\"\n\n존스는 자신의 밀실 자문단의 조언을 받아 이렇게 답했다.\n\n\"부인,\n부인께서 저를 의심하신다는 사실에 제가 얼마나 큰 충격을 받았는지는 이루 말로 다 할 수 없습니다. 벨라스턴 부인께서 그토록 비열한 흉계를 품을 만한 사람이라 믿을 수 있는 자에게 은혜를 베푸셨겠습니까? 아니면 부인께서 사랑의 가장 엄숙한 맹세를 한낱 경멸로 대하실 수 있겠습니까? 부인, 한번 생각해 보십시오. 설령 방심한 한순간에 제 격정이 거세어져, 부인의 명예를 향한 제 애틋함을 이겨 버렸다 한들, 머지않아 세상의 이목을 결코 피할 수 없고, 발각되는 날에는 부인의 평판에 그토록 치명적일 수밖에 없는 그런 관계를 계속 이어 가며 제 욕심을 채울 생각을 하겠습니까? 만일 저를 그런 사람으로 보신다면, 저는 제가 불운하게도 부인의 손에서 받게 된 그 금전적 신세를 하루빨리 갚을 기회가 오기를 빌 따름입니다. 그리고 그보다 더 애틋한 종류의 신세에 대해서는, 저는 언제까지나 운운.\" 그러고는 앞서의 편지를 맺을 때 썼던 바로 그 말 그대로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n부인은 다음과 같이 답했다.\n\n\"이제 보니 당신은 악당이군요! 나는 영혼 깊은 곳에서부터 당신을 경멸합니다. 당신이 여기 온대도, 나는 집에 없을 것입니다.\"\n\n이런 속박에서 벗어난 것에 존스는 무척 흡족했지만—그것을 한 번이라도 겪어 본 사람이라면, 짐작건대 그 속박이 결코 가벼운 축에 들지 않음을 인정하리라—그렇다고 그의 마음이 온전히 편안했던 것은 아니었다. 이 계략 속에는 거짓이나 부정직함이라면 그 어떤 종류든 진저리 치게 싫어하는 사람을 흡족하게 하기에는 속임수가 너무 많이 들어 있었다. 그리고 실로 그가 이를 실행에 옮기는 데 응한 것도, 한쪽 부인이든 다른 쪽 여인이든, 어느 한 사람에게는 어떤 불명예를 저지를 수밖에 없는 괴로운 처지에 얽혀 있지 않았더라면 결코 없었을 일이다. 그리고 분명 독자도 인정하리라. 모든 선한 원칙이, 사랑과 더불어, 소피아 편에 서서 강력하게 호소하고 있었음을.\n\n나이팅게일은 자기 책략이 성공한 것에 크게 우쭐했고, 그 일로 친구에게서 많은 감사와 갈채를 받았다. 그가 대답했다. \"이보게 톰, 우리는 서로에게 사뭇 다른 신세를 진 셈일세. 자네는 자유를 되찾은 일을 내게 빚졌고, 나는 자유를 잃은 일을 자네에게 빚졌으니. 하지만 자네가 그 한쪽에서 내가 다른 쪽에서 누리는 만큼 행복하다면, 장담컨대 우리 둘은 잉글랜드에서 가장 행복한 사내들일세.\"\n\n이제 두 신사는 식사 자리로 불려 내려갔다. 그곳에서는 밀러 부인이 손수 요리사 노릇을 맡아, 딸의 혼례를 축하하느라 자신의 온 솜씨를 발휘하고 있었다. 그녀는 이 기쁜 경사를 무엇보다 존스의 다정한 행실 덕으로 돌렸고, 온 영혼이 그를 향한 고마움으로 불타올라, 그 눈빛이며 말이며 행동이 죄다 그것을 드러내느라 분주한 나머지, 정작 딸은, 심지어 새 사위마저도 그녀의 안중에는 거의 들어오지 않았다.\n\n식사가 막 끝났을 때 밀러 부인은 편지 한 통을 받았다. 그러나 이 장에서는 이미 편지가 차고도 넘쳤으니, 그 내용은 다음 장에서 전하기로 하겠다.\n\n# Chapter x.\n\n앞 장 끝머리에 도착한 그 편지는 올워디 씨가 보낸 것이었다. 내용인즉, 곧 조카 블리필을 데리고 런던으로 올라올 작정이니 늘 쓰던 숙소를 마련해 두기를 바란다는 것이었다. 그 숙소란 자신이 묵을 이 층과 조카가 묵을 삼 층을 말함이었다.\n\n이 가엾은 여인의 얼굴에 그전까지 떠올라 있던 밝은 빛이 이번 일로 조금 흐려졌다. 이 소식이 그녀를 적잖이 당황하게 만든 것은 사실이었다. 한편으로는, 딸과의 그토록 사심 없는 혼인을 갚는 대가로 이제 막 사위가 된 사람을 당장 문밖으로 내쫓는다는 것이 그녀에게는 도무지 정당화될 수 없는 일로 보였다. 다른 한편으로는, 올워디 씨에게서 그토록 많은 은혜를 입고서도 엄밀히 따지면 마땅히 그의 몫인 숙소를 빼앗는 일에 무슨 변명을 늘어놓을 생각을 차마 견딜 수가 없었다. 이 신사는 남에게 그 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 은혜를 베풀 때, 대다수의 너그러운 사람들이 행하는 방식과는 정반대의 원칙을 따랐기 때문이다. 그는 어떤 경우에든 자신의 선행을 세상으로부터는 물론이고 그 선행을 받는 당사자에게서까지 숨기려 들었다. 그는 \"주다\"라는 말 대신 한결같이 \"빌려주다\", \"갚다\"라는 말을 썼다. 또한 그가 생각해 낼 수 있는 온갖 다른 방법을 동원해, 두 손으로는 은혜를 산더미처럼 쌓아 올리면서도 입으로는 늘 그 은혜를 깎아내렸다. 그래서 밀러 부인에게 연 50파운드의 연금을 정해 줄 때에도, 그는 \"이건 내가 런던에 올라올 때 항상 그대의 이 층을 쓰는 값으로 치는 것이오(사실 그는 그럴 작정이 거의 없었다). 다만 다른 때에는 얼마든지 세를 놓아도 좋소. 내가 늘 한 달 전에 미리 기별을 보낼 테니 말이오\"라고 말했다. 그러나 이번에는 너무도 갑작스럽게 런던행을 서두르는 바람에 그런 기별을 보낼 틈이 없었다. 그리고 아마도 이 황망함 때문에, 숙소를 청하는 편지를 쓰면서 \"그때 비어 있다면\"이라는 단서를 덧붙이는 것을 잊었으리라. 그도 그럴 것이, 밀러 부인이 지금 내놓을 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 부족한 변명에도 그는 틀림없이 흔쾌히 그 숙소를 양보했을 사람이었으니 말이다.\n\n그러나 프라이어가 더없이 절묘하게 짚어 냈듯, 어떤 부류의 사람들은 자신의 행실을 무언가에 따라 다스린다.\n\n학교에서 가르치는 악덕과 미덕의 정해지고 굳어진 규칙을 넘어, 법의 문구를 넘어선 그 무언가에.\n\n이런 이들에게는, 자신의 변명이 올드 베일리 법정에서 무죄를 받아 낼 정도로는 결코 충분하지 않다. 그뿐 아니라 모든 재판관 가운데 가장 엄혹한 양심이 자신을 방면해 준다 해도 만족하지 못한다. 오직 공정하고 떳떳한 것만이 그 섬세한 마음을 흡족하게 할 수 있으니, 만일 그들의 어떤 행위가 이 기준에 조금이라도 못 미치면, 그들은 풀이 죽어 시들시들 앓으며, 유령이나 교수형 집행인을 두려워하는 살인자만큼이나 불안하고 안절부절못한다.\n\n밀러 부인이 바로 그런 사람 가운데 하나였다. 그녀는 이 편지를 받고 솟아오른 불안을 감추지 못했다. 좌중에게 편지 내용을 알리고 자신의 곤혹스러움을 넌지시 비치자마자, 그녀의 수호천사 같은 존스가 곧바로 그 근심을 덜어 주었다. \"저로 말하자면, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 숙소는 한마디 기별만 주시면 언제든 내드리겠습니다. 그리고 나이팅게일 씨도, 아직 부인을 맞이할 만한 집을 마련하지 못했으니, 분명 새 숙소로 돌아가는 데 동의할 것입니다. 나이팅게일 부인께서도 틀림없이 그곳으로 가는 데 응하실 테고요.\" 이 제안에 부부는 그 자리에서 모두 찬성했다.\n\n밀러 부인의 두 뺨이 존스를 향한 한층 깊어진 고마움으로 다시금 붉게 달아오르기 시작했으리라는 것은 독자도 쉬이 믿을 것이다. 그러나 존스 씨가 방금 한 말에서 그녀의 딸을 \"나이팅게일 부인\"이라 부른 일이(그 듣기 좋은 소리가 그녀의 귀에 닿은 것은 그때가 처음이었다), 눈앞의 근심을 풀어 준 것보다도 이 자애로운 어머니에게 더 큰 만족을 안기고 존스를 향한 그녀의 마음을 더 따뜻하게 데웠다는 사실은, 어쩌면 독자를 설득하기가 한결 더 어려울지도 모르겠다.\n\n그리하여 신혼부부와 존스 씨가 거처를 옮길 날로 이튿날이 정해졌다. 존스 역시 친구와 같은 집에 머물 수 있도록 마련될 참이었다. 이제 좌중의 평온함이 다시 회복되었고, 그들은 더없이 명랑하게 그 하루를 보냈다. 다만 존스만은 예외였다. 그는 겉으로는 나머지 사람들과 어울려 흥겨워했지만, 소피아를 생각하며 속으로 숱한 쓰라린 아픔을 느꼈다. 그 아픔은 블리필 씨가 런던에 온다는 소식에(그는 그 여행의 의도를 똑똑히 꿰뚫어 보았기에) 적잖이 더해졌다. 게다가 그의 근심을 한층 더 무겁게 한 것은, 소피아의 소식을 알아보고 이튿날 저녁 일찍 보고해 주겠다 약속했던 어너 부인이 그를 실망시켰다는 점이었다.\n\n이 무렵 그와 그의 연인이 처한 상황에서는, 그가 무슨 좋은 소식을 들으리라 바랄 만한 근거가 거의 없었다. 그런데도 그는 마치 어너 부인이 소피아가 보낸 밀회 약속이 담긴 편지라도 가져다줄 것처럼 그녀를 애타게 기다렸고, 그 기다림이 어긋난 것을 그만큼 견디기 힘들어했다. 이 조바심이, 인간의 마음에 본디 깃든 그 약점—최악을 알고 싶어 하고 불확실함을 가장 견디기 힘든 고통으로 여기게 만드는—에서 비롯된 것인지, 아니면 그가 여전히 어떤 은밀한 희망으로 스스로를 달래고 있었기 때문인지, 우리는 단정하지 않겠다. 그러나 그것이 후자일 수도 있다는 것은, 사랑해 본 사람이라면 누구나 모를 리 없다. 이 정념이 우리 마음에 휘두르는 온갖 힘 가운데, 가장 놀라운 것 하나가 바로 절망의 한가운데서도 희망을 떠받치는 힘이기 때문이다. 곤경도, 가망 없음도, 아니 불가능함조차도 이 정념 앞에서는 깡그리 무시되고 만다. 그리하여 사랑에 흠뻑 빠진 사람에게는, 애디슨이 카이사르를 두고 한 말을 그대로 갖다 붙일 수 있을 것이다.\n\n\"알프스도, 피레네도, 그의 앞에서는 무너져 내린다!\"\n\n그러나 이와 똑같이 참인 것은, 바로 그 정념이 때로는 두더지가 파 놓은 흙더미를 산으로 부풀려 놓고, 희망의 한복판에서 절망을 빚어내기도 한다는 사실이다. 다만 건강한 기질을 지닌 사람에게는 이런 싸늘한 발작이 오래가지 않는다. 존스가 지금 어느 쪽 기분에 잠겨 있었는지는, 정확한 정보가 없으니 독자의 짐작에 맡기겠다. 그러나 한 가지만은 분명하다. 그는 두 시간을 기다림 속에 보냈고, 마침내 더는 그 불안을 숨길 수 없게 되자 자기 방으로 물러났다. 그곳에서 근심이 그를 거의 미칠 지경으로 몰아갔을 무렵, 어너 부인에게서 다음과 같은 편지가 전해졌다. 우리는 그것을 독자 앞에 글자 그대로, 철자 그대로 옮겨 보이겠다.\n\"나으리,\n\n진작에 약속대로 차자 뵈었을 텐데 마님이 못 가게 부뜨신 탓이옵니다. 나으리도 잘 아시다십이, 사람은 누구나 제 앞가림부터 해야 하는 법이고, 또 이런 자리가 다시 올 리 만무하니, 마님이 그처럼 친절하시게도 제가 청하지도 안엇는데 당신 시녀로 삼아 주시겠다 하셨을 때 그걸 마다햇다면 저야말로 마땅이 욕을 먹을 일이엇겟지요. 마님은 참말로 세상에서 손꼽이는 좋은 마님이시고, 그와 반대로 말하는 사람들은 속마음이 아주 고약한 자들임에 틀임없습니다. 만일 제가 그런 말을 한 적이 있다면 그건 다 무지한 탓이엇고, 진심으로 뉘우치고 있사옵니다. 저는 나으리께서 더없는 명예와 정직을 갖추신 신사이심을 아오니, 설사 제가 그런 말을 한 적이 있더라도, 늘 나으리께 세상 무엇보다 큰 존경을 품어 온 가엾은 하인을 해치려고 그 말을 옴기실 분이 아니실 줄로 믿습니다. 참말로 사람은 제 혀를 이빨 안에 단단이 가둬 두어야 하는 법이옵니다. 앞으로 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겟습니까. 어제 누가 제게, 네가 내일 이처럼 좋은 자리에 있게 되리라 일러 주었다면 저는 믿지 안엇을 겁니다. 참말로 그런 일은 꿈에도 생각지 못햇고, 남의 자리를 노린 적도 결코 없었으니까요. 다만 마님이 그처럼 친절하시게 청하지도 안엇는데 손수 그 자리를 주시겟다 하니, 참말로 이토프 부인이든 그 누구든, 굴러든 복을 제가 받앗다고 저를 탓할 수는 없을 것이옵니다. 제가 한 말은 부디 한마디도 입에 올리지 마시기를 나으리께 비옵니다. 저는 나으리께 세상 모든 행운이 있기를 바라오며, 끝내 소피아 아씨를 차지하실 것을 조금도 의심치 안사옵니다. 다만 저로 말하자면, 나으리도 아시다십이 이제 다른 분의 명을 받는 처지이고 제 본 마님을 모시는 몸이 아니니, 그 일에 더는 도움이 되어 드릴 수가 없게 되엇습니다. 그러니 부디 지난 일은 아무 말씀도 마시기를 비오며, 죽는 날까지 나으리를 받드는 미천한 종임을 믿어 주시옵소서.\n\n어너 블랙모어 올림.\"\n존스는 벨라스턴 부인의 이 처사를 두고 갖가지 추측을 떠올렸다. 그러나 사실 그녀의 속셈은, 이미 진행된 것 이상으로는 결코 더 퍼지지 않기를 바라는 어떤 비밀의 저장고를 제 집 안에 단단히 가둬 두려는 것에 지나지 않았다. 무엇보다 그녀는 그 비밀이 소피아의 귀에 들어가지 않기를 바랐다. 그 젊은 아씨야말로 그 일을 결코 두 번 다시 입에 올리지 않을 거의 유일한 사람이었건만, 부인은 도무지 그렇게 믿을 수가 없었다. 이제 가엾은 소피아를 더없이 풀 길 없는 증오로 미워하게 된 터라, 우리 여주인공의 그 보드라운 가슴속에도 자신을 향한 똑같은 증오가 깃들어 있으리라 짐작했던 것이다. 정작 그곳에는 그런 정념이 단 한 번도 발을 들인 적이 없었는데도 말이다.\n\n존스가 어너의 출세 뒤에 깔려 있으리라 상상한 천 가지 무시무시한 음모와 깊은 책략을 떠올리며 스스로를 공포에 떨게 하고 있던 그때, 지금껏 그와 소피아의 혼인에 더할 나위 없는 적이었던 것만 같은 운명의 여신은, 그 혼인에 마지막 종지부를 찍으려 새로운 수를 시도했다. 곧, 그의 앞길에 하나의 유혹을 던져 놓은 것이다. 지금 그가 처한 그 절망적인 처지로 보아, 도저히 뿌리치지 못할 것만 같은 유혹을.\n\n기이하지만 전례가 없지는 않은 일에 관하여.\n\n헌트 부인이라 불리는 한 부인이 있었는데, 그녀는 존스가 묵고 있는 집에서 그를 여러 차례 본 적이 있었다. 그 집 여자들과 가깝게 지냈으며, 실로 밀러 부인과는 둘도 없는 벗이었기 때문이다. 그녀의 나이는 서른쯤이었으니, 본인은 스물여섯이라 주장했다. 얼굴과 몸매는 매우 훌륭했으나, 다만 살집이 조금 지나치게 붙는 쪽으로 기우는 것이 흠이라면 흠이었다. 그녀는 젊은 나이에 친척들의 손에 이끌려 나이 든 터키 무역상에게 시집갔는데, 그는 큰 재산을 모은 뒤 장사에서 손을 뗀 사람이었다. 그녀는 그와 함께 흠잡힐 일 없이, 그러나 고통이 없지는 않게, 큰 인내를 견디는 처지로 십이 년쯤을 살았다. 그리고 그녀의 정숙함은 그가 죽으면서 그녀에게 막대한 재산을 남겨 준 것으로 보답받았다. 과부살이의 첫해가 막 끝나려는 참이었는데, 그녀는 그 한 해를 상당히 은둔하며 보냈다. 가까운 벗 몇 사람만 만나고, 시간을 신앙 생활과 소설에 나누어 썼는데, 그녀는 늘 소설을 무척이나 좋아했다. 아주 좋은 건강과 매우 뜨거운 기질, 그리고 적지 않은 신앙심이 그녀로 하여금 다시 결혼하지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 그래서 그녀는 첫 번째 남편으로 친척들의 마음에 들었던 것처럼, 두 번째 남편으로는 자신의 마음에 드는 사람을 고르기로 결심했다. 그녀로부터 다음의 짧은 편지가 존스에게 전해졌다.\n“선생님께,\n\n“당신을 처음 본 그날부터, 제 눈이 당신께 너무도 또렷이 말해 버린 것은 아닐까 염려됩니다. 제가 당신께 무심하지 않다는 것을 말입니다. 그러나 만일 당신이 묵고 계신 집의 부인들이 제게 당신에 관해 그토록 좋은 평을 들려주지 않았더라면, 그리고 당신이 가장 호감 가는 사람일 뿐 아니라 가장 그럴 자격이 있는 사람임을 제게 확신시켜 줄 만큼 당신의 덕과 선함을 증명하는 이야기를 들려주지 않았더라면, 제 혀도 제 손도 결코 그것을 고백하지는 않았을 것입니다. 또한 저는 그분들로부터, 제 사람됨도, 식견도, 인품도 당신께 거슬리지 않는다는 말을 듣는 기쁨을 누렸습니다. 제게는 우리 두 사람을 다 행복하게 해 줄 만한 재산이 있으나, 그것도 당신 없이는 저를 행복하게 만들지 못합니다. 이렇게 제 한 몸을 내맡기는 일로 세상의 비난을 사게 되리라는 것을 저도 압니다. 그러나 만일 제가 세상을 두려워하는 것보다 당신을 더 사랑하지 않는다면, 저는 당신께 어울리는 사람이 못 될 것입니다. 다만 한 가지 어려움이 저를 멈춰 세웁니다. 듣자 하니 당신은 어느 지체 높은 여인과 연애 관계에 얽혀 있다고 하더군요. 만일 당신이 저를 차지하기 위해 그것을 희생할 만하다고 여기신다면, 저는 당신의 것입니다. 그렇지 않다면, 저의 약한 마음을 잊으시고, 이 일이 당신과 저 사이에 영원한 비밀로 남게 해 주십시오.\n\n“아라벨라 헌트 올림.”\n이 편지를 읽고 존스는 격한 동요에 휩싸였다. 그의 형편은 그때 몹시 바닥나 있었으니, 지금껏 그를 받쳐 주던 돈줄이 끊겨 버린 까닭이었다. 벨라스턴 부인에게서 받은 것 가운데 다섯 기니 남짓밖에 남지 않았는데, 바로 그날 아침에 한 상인이 그 두 배에 해당하는 금액을 갚으라며 그를 졸라 댔다. 그가 명예롭게 여기는 연인은 그녀의 아버지 손아귀에 들어가 있었고, 그녀를 다시 거기서 빼낼 가망은 거의 없었다. 그녀가 아버지와 별개로 지닌 그 작은 재산으로 그녀의 비용을 빌려 살아간다는 것은, 그의 자존심과 사랑의 섬세함 양쪽 모두에 크게 거슬리는 일이었다. 이 부인의 재산은 그에게 더없이 요긴했을 터였고, 그녀에 대해서는 어느 면으로 보아도 흠잡을 데가 없었다. 도리어 그는 소피아를 제외하면 어떤 여자 못지않게 그녀를 좋게 여겼다. 그러나 소피아를 저버리고 다른 여자와 결혼한다는 것, 그것은 불가능했다. 그는 어떤 이유로도 그것을 떠올릴 수가 없었다. 하지만 그녀가 자신의 사람이 될 수 없음이 분명한데, 어찌하여 그래서는 안 된단 말인가? 그녀를 가망 없는 사랑에 더 오래 묶어 두는 것보다는, 그렇게 하는 편이 오히려 그녀를 위하는 길이 아닐까? 그녀를 향한 우정으로 보더라도 마땅히 그렇게 해야 하는 것이 아닐까? 이런 생각이 한동안 그를 사로잡았고, 그는 드높은 명예심에서 그녀에게 거짓된 사람이 되기로 거의 마음을 굳힐 뻔했다. 그러나 그 고상한 논리는, 그러한 우정이야말로 사랑에 대한 배신이라고 그의 가슴속에서 외치는 본성의 목소리 앞에서 그리 오래 버티지 못했다. 마침내 그는 펜과 잉크와 종이를 가져오라 하여, 헌트 부인에게 다음과 같이 써 보냈다.\n\n“부인께,\n“당신을 차지하기 위해 어떤 연애를 희생한다 한들, 그것은 당신이 제게 베푸신 호의에 대한 변변찮은 보답에 지나지 않을 것입니다. 그리고 지금의 저처럼 그런 일에서 자유롭지 않은 처지였더라도, 저는 분명 그렇게 했을 것입니다. 하지만 만일 제 마음이 다른 이에게 매여 있다는 사실을 당신께 말씀드리지 않는다면, 저는 당신이 여기시는 그 정직한 사람이 못 될 것입니다. 그분은 덕 있는 여인이며, 비록 제가 그녀를 영영 차지하지 못할 가능성이 크다 해도, 결코 떠날 수 없는 사람입니다. 당신의 친절에 보답한답시고, 제 마음을 드릴 수 없으면서 제 손을 내드려 당신께 그런 해를 끼치는 일은 결단코 없어야 할 것입니다. 아닙니다. 그런 죄를 짓느니 저는 차라리 굶어 죽는 편을 택하겠습니다. 설령 제 연인이 다른 사람과 결혼한다 할지라도, 제 마음에서 그녀의 모든 흔적이 온전히 지워지지 않는 한 저는 당신과 결혼하지 않을 것입니다. 부디 믿어 주십시오. 당신의 비밀은 당신 자신의 가슴속에서보다, 당신께 더없는 은혜를 입고 깊이 감사하는 미천한 종의 가슴속에서 결코 덜 안전하지 않으리라는 것을요.\n\n“T. 존스 올림.”\n우리의 주인공은 이 편지를 다 쓰고 부친 뒤, 책상으로 가서 웨스턴 양의 토시를 꺼내어 여러 번 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 방 안을 몇 바퀴 거닐며 의기양양해했는데, 그 마음의 흡족함은 어떤 아일랜드 사람이 오만 파운드의 재산을 거머쥐고 달아날 때 느꼈을 것보다도 더한 것이었다.\n\n파트리지가 한 발견.\n\n존스가 자신의 결백을 의식하며 흐뭇해하고 있는 동안, 파트리지가 깡충거리며 방으로 들어왔다. 좋은 소식을 가져오거나, 가져왔다고 스스로 믿을 때면 늘 그러던 버릇이었다. 그날 아침 그는 주인의 명을 받고, 벨라스턴 부인의 하인들을 통해서든 다른 어떤 방법으로든 소피아가 어디로 옮겨졌는지 알아내려 길을 나섰던 터였다. 그리고 이제 돌아와, 기쁨에 찬 얼굴로 우리의 주인공에게 잃어버린 새를 찾았노라고 일러주었다. \"나리, 제가 사냥터지기인 블랙 조지를 보았습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그자는 지주 어른께서 함께 도시로 데려오신 하인들 가운데 하나입니다요. 몇 해째 보지 못했는데도 단번에 알아보았지요. 하지만 나리도 아시다시피 그자는 아주 눈에 띄는 사람입니다요. 아니, 더 고상하게 말하자면, 더없이 눈에 띄는 수염을 가졌지요. 제가 여태 본 가운데 가장 크고 가장 검은 수염입니다요. 다만 블랙 조지가 저를 알아보기까지는 한참이 걸렸습니다요.\"\n\"그래, 그런데 자네 좋은 소식이란 게 뭔가?\" 존스가 소리쳤다. \"내 소피아에 대해 뭘 알아냈느냔 말이야?\" \"곧 아시게 됩니다요, 나리.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"최대한 빨리 본론으로 가는 중입니다요. 나리께서는 어찌나 성급하신지, 명령법에 닿기도 전에 부정법부터 오시려 하시는군요. 말씀드렸다시피, 나리, 그자가 제 얼굴을 알아보기까지는 한참이 걸렸지요.\"—\"자네 얼굴 따위는 집어치우게!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"내 소피아는 어찌 됐단 말인가?\" \"아니, 나리.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"소피아 아씨에 대해서는 지금 말씀드리려는 것 말고는 아무것도 모릅니다요. 나리께서 말을 끊지만 않으셨어도 진작 다 말씀드렸을 겁니다요. 그런데 그렇게 화난 얼굴로 저를 노려보시면 죄다 제 머리에서, 아니 더 고상하게 말하자면 제 기억에서 놀라 달아나 버린답니다요. 업튼을 떠나던 날 이후로 나리께서 이토록 화난 얼굴을 하신 건 처음 봅니다요. 그날을 저는 천 년을 산다 해도 잊지 못할 겝니다요.\"—\"그래, 그럼 자네 좋을 대로 계속하게.\" 존스가 말했다. \"자네는 기어이 나를 미치게 만들 작정인 모양이군.\" \"천만의 말씀입니다요.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"그 일로는 이미 충분히 혼났는걸요. 말씀드렸듯이 살아 있는 동안 가장 긴 날까지도 두고두고 기억할 일이지요.\" \"그래, 그건 그렇고 블랙 조지는?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"예, 나리, 말씀드렸다시피 그자가 저를 알아보기까지 한참이 걸렸지요. 사실 그자를 본 이후로 저도 무척 변했으니까요. 옛날의 제가 아니지요. 세상살이에서 갖은 풍파를 겪었는데, 슬픔만큼 사람을 변하게 하는 건 없습니다요. 슬픔이 하룻밤 새 사람 머리칼 빛깔을 바꿔놓는다는 말도 들었습니다요. 어쨌든 끝내 그자가 저를 알아본 건 확실합니다요. 저희 둘은 동갑이고 같은 자선학교를 다녔거든요. 조지는 지독한 둔재였지만, 뭐 그건 상관없지요. 사람이 다 배운 만큼 세상에서 잘되는 건 아니니까요. 저야말로 그리 말할 만한 처지지요. 하지만 천 년 뒤면 다 매한가지일 겝니다요. 그래, 나리, 제가 어디까지 말했더라?—아, 그렇지—저희가 서로 알아보자마자, 손을 몇 번이고 정답게 맞잡고 나서 술집에 가 한잔하기로 했지요. 운 좋게도 그 맥주는 제가 도시에 온 이래 맛본 것 중에 으뜸가는 것이었답니다요. 자, 나리, 이제 본론으로 들어갑니다요. 제가 나리 성함을 입에 올리고, 나리와 제가 함께 도시로 와 그 뒤로 줄곧 같이 지내왔다고 말하자마자, 그자는 한 잔 더 가져오라 하고는 나리의 건강을 위해 마시겠다며 맹세했지요. 어찌나 진심으로 나리의 건강을 빌며 마시던지, 세상에 아직 그만한 고마움이 남아 있구나 싶어 저는 더없이 기뻤습니다요. 그 잔을 비우고 나서 저도 한잔 사겠다고 해서, 또 나리의 건강을 위해 한 잔을 마셨지요. 그러고는 이 소식을 전하려 부랴부랴 집으로 돌아온 겝니다요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소식 말인가?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"자네는 내 소피아에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았네!\" \"이런 맙소사! 그걸 잊을 뻔했군요. 사실 저희는 젊은 웨스턴 아씨에 대해서 꽤 많은 이야기를 나누었고, 조지가 죄다 일러주었습니다요. 블리필 도련님이 아씨와 혼인하러 도시로 올라온다는 겝니다요. 그럼 서둘러야겠구먼, 안 그러면 오기도 전에 누군가 아씨를 채갈 테니, 하고 제가 말했지요. 그러고는, 시그림 씨, 누군가 아씨를 차지하지 못한다면 그야말로 천만 번 안타까운 일일세, 하고 말했습죠. 그분이야말로 세상 어떤 여인보다 아씨를 사랑하시니까요. 저는 나리도 아씨도 알아주셨으면 합니다요. 그분이 아씨를 따르는 건 결코 재산 때문이 아니라는 걸 말입니다요. 그 점에 관해서라면 제가 장담할 수 있습죠. 아씨가 감히 견줄 수 없을 만큼 훨씬 높은 신분과 재산을 지닌 또 다른 부인이 계신데, 그 부인이 어떤 분에게 어찌나 푹 빠져 있는지 밤낮으로 그분을 쫓아다닐 정도이니 말입니다요.\"\n\n여기서 존스는 파트리지가 자기를 배신했다며 그에게 발끈 화를 냈다. 그러나 가엾은 친구는 자기는 이름을 입에 올린 적이 없다고 대답했다. \"게다가 나리.\" 그가 말했다. \"장담컨대 조지는 진심으로 나리의 벗입니다요. 한두 번이 아니라 블리필 도련님을 악마에게나 가버리라고 빌었는걸요. 아니, 나리를 위해서라면 이 땅에서 제 힘닿는 무슨 일이든 하겠다고까지 했습니다요. 그러니 저는 그자가 정말 그러리라 믿습니다요. 배신이라니, 당치도 않습니다요! 저를 빼면 이 땅에 조지보다 나은 벗이 나리께 또 있을까 싶고, 나리를 위해 그자보다 더 멀리 나설 사람이 또 있을까 싶은걸요.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 다소 누그러진 존스가 말했다. \"자네 말로는, 정말이지 내 벗이 되어줄 마음이 충분한 듯한 그 친구가, 소피아와 같은 집에 산다는 게로군?\"\n\n\"같은 집이라니요!\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"아니 나리, 그자는 그 댁 하인들 가운데 하나입니다요. 옷차림이 아주 번듯하다고 장담합니다요. 검은 수염만 아니라면 나리도 알아보기 어려우실 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 적어도 한 가지 일은 나를 위해 해줄 수 있겠군.\" 존스가 말했다. \"분명 그 친구라면 내 소피아에게 편지 한 통쯤은 틀림없이 전해줄 수 있을 테지.\"\n\n\"바로 정곡을 짚으셨습니다요, 한 치도 어김없이요!\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"어찌 제가 그 생각을 못 했을까요? 제가 한 번만 말을 꺼내면 그자가 그 일을 해내리라 장담합니다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 됐네.\" 존스가 말했다. \"지금은 자네는 나를 좀 혼자 두게. 내가 편지를 한 통 쓸 테니, 내일 아침 그 친구에게 전해주게. 자네는 그를 어디서 찾을지 알 테지.\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 나리.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"다시 찾고말고요. 그 점은 염려 마십시오. 술맛이 어찌나 좋은지 그자가 오래 발길을 끊을 리가 없습니다요. 그자가 도시에 머무는 동안에는 날마다 거기 나타나리라는 데 의심이 없습니다요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 자네는 내 소피아가 묵고 있는 거리도 모른단 말이로군?\" 존스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"천만에요, 나리, 압니다요.\" 파트리지가 말했다.\n\n\"그 거리 이름이 뭔가?\" 존스가 외쳤다.\n\n\"이름 말입니까요, 나리? 아니, 여기서, 나리, 바로 코앞입니다요.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"한두 골목 떨어졌을까 한 곳이지요. 사실 정확한 이름은 모릅니다요. 그자가 일러주지도 않았고, 제가 물었더라면, 아시다시피, 그자 머릿속에 의심을 심어줄 수도 있었을 테니까요. 아니지요, 아닙니다요, 나리, 그건 제게 맡겨두십시오. 그런 일에는 제가 너무 약아빠져서요, 장담합니다요.\"\n\n\"자네는 정말이지 놀랍도록 약아빠진 사람일세.\" 존스가 받아쳤다. \"어쨌든 내일 술집에서 그를 찾아낼 만큼은 자네가 영악하리라 믿으니, 나는 내 연인에게 편지나 쓰겠네.\"\n\n이제 영민한 파트리지를 내보낸 뒤, 존스 씨는 자리에 앉아 편지를 쓰기 시작했다. 그가 그 일에 매달려 있도록 잠시 그를 남겨두기로 하자. 그리고 여기서 우리는 제15권을 끝맺는다.\n\n닷새 동안의 일을 담은 권.\n1장.\n\n머리말에 관하여.\n어느 극작가가 이런 말을 하곤 했다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있다. 자신은 머리말 하나를 쓰느니 차라리 희곡 한 편을 쓰는 편이 낫겠다고 말이다. 나 역시 그와 비슷하게, 이 이야기의 본문 한 권을 쓰는 것이 각 권 앞에 붙이는 서두의 장 하나를 쓰는 것보다 수고가 덜하다고 느낀다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 처음으로 희곡 앞에 머리말이라 불리는 부분을 붙이는 방식을 만들어낸 그 작가의 머리 위로 진심 어린 저주가 적잖이 쏟아졌으리라 믿는다. 머리말이란 본래 작품 그 자체의 일부였으나, 근래에 이르러서는 그 앞에 놓인 극과 좀처럼 연결되는 데가 없어져서, 한 희곡의 머리말이 다른 어떤 희곡에 붙어도 무방할 지경이 되었다. 실로 더 근래에 쓰인 머리말들은 하나같이 똑같은 세 가지 주제 위에 쓰인 듯하다. 곧 도시 사람들의 취향에 대한 비난, 동시대 모든 작가에 대한 규탄, 그리고 막 상연될 작품에 대한 찬사가 그것이다. 이 모든 것에 담긴 생각은 거의 변하지 않으며, 또 변할 수도 없다. 같은 것을 표현하려고 그토록 다양한 어구를 찾아낼 줄 아는 작가들의 대단한 창의력에는 나도 종종 놀라곤 했다.\n\n이와 마찬가지로 나는 이렇게 짐작한다. 미래의 어느 역사가가 (혹 누군가 내 방식을 본떠 주는 영예를 베푼다면) 한참 머리를 긁적인 뒤, 이러한 여러 서두의 장을 처음으로 자리 잡게 한 나의 기억에 대고 몇 마디 좋은 축원을 보내리라고 말이다. 그 대부분은 근래의 머리말들과 같아서, 이 이야기의 다른 어느 권에 붙여도, 아니 다른 어떤 이야기에 붙여도, 정작 자기가 소개하는 권에 붙은 것만큼이나 잘 어울릴 것이다.\n\n하지만 작가들이 이 두 가지 발명 중 어느 쪽으로든 고생을 겪는다 해도, 관객이 오래전부터 머리말에서 충분한 이득을 누려 왔듯이, 독자도 이런 서두의 장에서 넉넉한 이득을 발견하게 될 것이다.\n\n첫째로, 머리말이 비평가에게 야유하는 솜씨를 시험하고 자기가 쓸 호각을 가장 유리하게 조율할 기회를 제공한다는 것은 익히 알려진 사실이다. 그 덕분에 나는 그런 악기들이 어찌나 잘 준비되어 있던지, 막이 처음 오르는 그 순간에 곧장 합주를 시작할 수 있는 광경까지 본 적이 있다.\n\n이런 서두의 장들에서도 똑같은 이점을 끌어낼 수 있다. 비평가는 여기서 자신의 고귀한 기개를 갈아 세울 숫돌이 되어 줄 무언가를 어김없이 만나게 될 테고, 그리하여 더 굶주린 식욕으로 이야기 자체를 향해 비난을 퍼부으며 달려들 수 있을 것이다. 게다가 그의 빈틈없는 통찰이 있으니, 이 장들이 그 훌륭한 목적을 위해 얼마나 교묘하게 짜였는지를 굳이 짚어 줄 필요도 없을 것이다. 왜냐하면 여기에는 그 비평의 기개를 날카롭게 벼리고 부추기려고, 우리가 늘 시큼하거나 신맛 나는 무언가를 군데군데 섞어 두었기 때문이다.\n\n또한 게으른 독자도 게으른 관객 못지않게 이 둘에서 큰 이득을 본다. 어느 쪽이든 머리말을 볼 의무도, 서두의 장을 읽을 의무도 없으니 말이다. 더구나 이렇게 해서 희곡도 책도 분량이 늘어난 덕분에, 관객은 머리말 덕에 저녁 식탁에 십오 분쯤 더 앉아 있을 여유를 얻고, 독자는 서두의 장 덕에 첫 쪽이 아니라 넷째나 다섯째 쪽에서부터 읽기 시작하는 이점을 누린다. 이것은 그저 읽었다고 말하기 위한 목적 말고는 다른 뜻 없이 책을 펼치는 사람들에게는 결코 사소한 일이 아니다. 그렇게 읽는 동기는 흔히 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 널리 퍼져 있으며, 그 동기 탓에 법률서나 좋은 책뿐 아니라 호메로스와 베르길리우스, 스위프트와 세르반테스의 책장도 자주 그저 넘겨지곤 한다.\n\n이 둘에서 비롯되는 이득은 그 밖에도 여럿이지만, 대부분 너무나 빤한 것이라 지금 일일이 늘어놓느라 머무르지는 않겠다. 더구나 머리말이든 서문이든 그 으뜸가는 미덕은 짧다는 데 있다는 사실이 마침 떠오르니 말이다.\n\n# Chapter ii.\n\n시골 향사에게 닥친 별난 소동, 그리고 소피아의 딱한 처지.\n\n이제 우리는 독자를 웨스턴 씨의 숙소로 안내해야겠다. 그곳은 피커딜리에 있었는데, 하이드파크 코너의 헤라클레스 기둥 여관 주인이 천거해 준 곳이었다. 그가 도시에 도착해 처음 본 그 여관에 말들을 맡겼고, 처음 소문을 들은 그 숙소에 자신의 몸을 부려 놓았기 때문이다.\n\n여기서, 소피아가 벨라스턴 부인의 집에서 그녀를 태우고 온 삯마차에서 내렸을 때, 그녀는 자기를 위해 마련된 방으로 물러가기를 청했다. 아버지는 선뜻 그러라 했고, 몸소 그녀를 데려다주었다. 그러고 나서 두 사람 사이에 짧은 대화가 오갔는데, 그리 중요하지도, 자세히 옮길 만큼 유쾌하지도 않은 내용이었다. 그는 며칠 안에 도시로 올 거라고 일러 둔 블리필과의 혼인에 동의하라고 그녀를 격렬하게 다그쳤다. 그러나 그녀는 따르기는커녕, 그 어느 때보다 단호하고 결연한 거절을 내놓았다. 이에 아버지는 어찌나 격노했던지, 네가 원하든 원치 않든 기어이 그놈과 짝지어 주겠노라고 모진 맹세를 거듭 퍼붓고는, 험한 말과 저주를 한가득 쏟아 내며 그녀 곁을 떠나 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n소피아가, 가장 엄중히 갇힌 죄수에게나 딸린 그 동무, 곧 난롯불과 촛불 말고는 아무 벗도 없이 남겨진 동안, 향사는 자기 목사와 헤라클레스 기둥 여관 주인을 데리고 포도주 한 병을 앞에 놓고 흥겹게 자리를 잡았다. 향사의 말로는, 여관 주인이야말로 셋째 사람으로 더없이 훌륭한 짝이며, 도시의 소식과 세상 돌아가는 형편을 일러 줄 수 있는 자였다. \"암, 그야 많이 알고말고.\" 그가 말했다. \"지체 높은 양반들 말이 숱하게 그자네 마구간에 묵으니까 말이야.\"\n\n이 즐거운 무리 속에서 웨스턴 씨는 그날 저녁과 이튿날 한나절을 보냈는데, 그동안 이 이야기에 자리를 차지할 만큼 중요한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 그 내내 소피아는 홀로 지냈다. 아버지가, 먼저 블리필과 혼인하기로 승낙하지 않는 한 살아서는 결코 그 방을 나오지 못하리라고 으름장을 놓았기 때문이다. 또한 음식을 들여보낼 때를 빼고는 문이 열리는 것을 결코 허락하지 않았고, 그럴 때면 늘 몸소 곁을 지켰다.\n\n도시에 도착한 지 이틀째 되는 아침, 그가 목사와 함께 토스트와 한 잔의 맥주로 아침을 들고 있는데, 어떤 신사가 아래층에서 그를 뵙고자 기다린다는 전갈이 왔다.\n\n\"신사라고?\" 향사가 말했다. \"대체 누구지? 박사 양반, 어디 내려가서 누군지 좀 보고 와요. 블리필 씨가 벌써 도시에 왔을 리는 없을 텐데.—내려가서 무슨 볼일인지 알아봐 줘요.\"\n\n목사가 돌아와 전하기를, 매우 잘 차려입은 사람인데 모자에 단 리본으로 보아 군 장교인 듯하며, 어떤 특별한 볼일이 있어 웨스턴 씨 본인 말고는 누구에게도 전할 수 없다고 했다는 것이었다.\n\n\"장교라!\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"그런 작자가 나랑 무슨 볼일이람? 짐수레 차출 명령서가 필요한 거라면, 난 여기 치안판사도 아니고 영장을 내줄 수도 없는데.—기어이 나한테 할 말이 있다면야 올려보내요.\"\n\n이윽고 아주 점잖은 사내가 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 향사에게 인사를 차린 뒤, 단둘이 있게 해 달라고 청하고는 이렇게 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"선생, 저는 펠라머 경의 명을 받들어 찾아뵈었습니다. 다만, 일전 그날 밤 일이 있은 뒤라 선생께서 짐작하실 법한 것과는 사뭇 다른 전갈을 가져왔습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 경이라고?\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"그런 이름은 들어 본 적도 없는데.\"\n\n\"경께서는,\" 사내가 말했다. \"모든 것을 술기운 탓으로 돌리실 뜻이시며, 그 정도의 사소한 인정 한마디면 모든 일이 바로잡힐 것입니다. 그분은 따님께 더없이 격렬한 애정을 품고 계셔서, 선생, 이 세상에서 모욕을 받고도 가장 노여워하지 않으실 마지막 분이 바로 선생이십니다. 또한 두 분께 다행스러운 것은, 그분이 이런 종류의 일을 명예에 한 점 흠 잡힐 위험 없이 덮어 둘 수 있을 만큼 용기를 이미 만천하에 증명해 보이셨다는 점입니다. 그러니 그분이 바라시는 것은 다만, 선생께서 제 앞에서 어떤 인정의 말을 해 주시는 것뿐입니다. 세상에서 가장 가벼운 한마디면 충분합니다. 그분은 오늘 오후 선생을 찾아뵙고, 연인의 자격으로 따님을 방문할 허락을 얻고자 하십니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 도통 모르겠소, 선생.\" 향사가 말했다. \"허나 내 딸 얘기를 하는 걸 보니, 이게 바로 내 사촌 벨라스턴 부인이 나한테 말했던 그 경인 모양이오. 부인이 내 딸한테 구애를 한다나 어쩐다나 하더군. 사정이 그렇다면야—경께 내 인사나 전하고, 그 애는 이미 임자가 정해졌다고 일러 주시오.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 사내가 말했다. \"아마 이 제안이 얼마나 대단한 것인지 충분히 헤아리지 못하신 듯합니다. 저런 인물에, 작위에, 재산이라면 어디서도 거절당할 리 없을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"이보시오, 선생.\" 향사가 답했다. \"아주 까놓고 말하리다. 내 딸은 벌써 정해진 데가 있소. 허나 설령 그렇지 않더라도, 난 무슨 일이 있어도 그 애를 경 따위한테 시집보내지 않을 거요. 난 경이란 작자들을 다 싫어해. 죄다 아첨꾼에 하노버 패거리들이고, 난 그런 자들하곤 아무 상관도 하기 싫소.\"\n\n\"그렇군요, 선생.\" 사내가 말했다. \"그것이 선생의 결심이라면, 제가 전해 드릴 전갈은 이렇습니다. 경께서 오늘 아침 하이드파크에서 선생과 한자리하기를 청하십니다.\"\n\n\"경한테 전하시오.\" 향사가 답했다. \"난 바빠서 갈 수가 없다고. 집안에 챙길 일이 산더미라 무슨 일이 있어도 밖에 나설 수가 없소.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 상대가 말했다. \"선생께서는 그런 전갈을 보낼 만큼 모자란 신사가 결코 아니실 겁니다. 고귀한 경 한 분을 모욕해 놓고 그분께 결투의 만족을 거절했다는 말이 선생을 두고 나돌게 두지는 않으시리라 확신합니다. 경께서는 따님을 어찌나 존중하시는지, 다른 방식으로 일을 매듭짓고자 하셨습니다. 그러나 선생을 장인으로 우러러보게 될 처지가 아니라면, 선생께서 분명 의식하셨을 그런 모욕을 그분의 명예가 덮어 두고 참아 줄 수는 없습니다.\"\n\n\"내가 모욕을 줬다고!\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"그건 빌어먹을 거짓말이오! 난 그자한테 아무것도 한 게 없소.\"\n\n이 말에 사내는 아주 짧은 말로 한마디 꾸짖었고, 동시에 손으로도 몇 차례 항의를 더했다. 그것이 웨스턴 씨의 귀에—아니 몸에—닿기가 무섭게, 그 점잖은 향사는 자기 날렵함을 구경하라고 더 많은 구경꾼이라도 불러 모으려는 듯, 있는 힘껏 고래고래 소리치며 방 안을 아주 활기차게 껑충껑충 뛰어다니기 시작했다.\n\n맥주잔을 절반이나 비우지 못한 채 자리를 뜬 목사는 멀리 가지 않은 터였다. 그래서 그는 향사의 고함 소리에 곧장 달려와 외쳤다. \"이런! 선생, 무슨 일입니까?\"—\"무슨 일이냐고!\" 향사가 말했다. \"여기 노상강도가 하나 있는데, 날 털고 죽이려 드는 모양이오.—내가 빌어먹을 놈이 되어도 좋으니, 손톱만큼도 자극한 적 없는데 손에 든 저 막대기로 날 덮쳤단 말이오.\"\n\n\"무슨 소립니까, 선생.\" 대위가 말했다. \"당신이 나한테 거짓말한다고 하지 않았소?\"\n\n\"아니, 내가 구원받기를 바라는 마음으로 맹세코,\" 향사가 답했다. \"—내가 경한테 무슨 모욕을 줬다는 게 거짓말이라고는 했을지 몰라도—'당신이 거짓말한다'라는 말은 결코 입에 올린 적 없소. 난 내 분수를 더 잘 아는 사람이고, 당신도 맨손인 사람한테 덤벼들지 않을 만큼은 제 분수를 알았어야지. 내 손에 막대기 하나만 있었어도 당신 따위 감히 날 못 쳤을 거요. 그 턱주가리를 귓등까지 날려 버렸을 텐데. 당장 마당으로 내려와, 막대기 한 대씩 주고받으며 머리통이 깨질 때까지 붙어 보자고, 정말이야. 아니면 빈방으로 들어가서 실컷 주먹다짐이라도 해 보든가. 당신은 절반쯤도 사내가 못 되는 작자야, 그래, 분명히.\"\n\n대위는 다소 분개하여 대꾸했다. \"보아하니, 선생, 당신은 내가 상대할 가치도 없는 사람이군. 경께도 당신이 그분 안중에 둘 가치도 없는 자라고 아뢰겠소. 당신 따위에 손가락을 더럽힌 게 유감이오.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 물러갔다. 목사가 끼어들어 향사가 그를 막지 못하게 했는데, 그 일은 쉽게 성공했다. 향사 쪽에서 막아 보려고 몇 번 애쓰는 시늉은 했어도, 정작 성공할 마음이 그리 간절해 보이지는 않았기 때문이다. 그래도 대위가 떠나자, 향사는 그 뒤에 대고 저주를 한가득, 으름장도 몇 마디 날렸다. 그러나 이 말들이 입에서 터져 나온 것은 장교가 이미 계단을 다 내려간 뒤였고, 그가 멀어질수록 점점 더 커졌으므로, 그의 귀에는 닿지도 않았고, 적어도 그의 발걸음을 늦추지는 못했다.\n\n그런데 갇혀 있던 가엾은 소피아는 처음부터 끝까지 아버지의 고함을 죄다 듣고 있었다. 그녀는 이제 처음으로 발을 쿵쿵 굴렀고, 이어 조금 전 노인이 했던 것만큼이나 크게—다만 훨씬 고운 목소리로—비명을 지르기 시작했다. 이 비명은 이내 향사를 잠잠하게 만들었고, 그의 온 마음을 딸에게로 돌려놓았다. 그는 딸을 어찌나 애틋이 사랑했던지, 그녀에게 무슨 해라도 닥칠까 하는 작은 두려움만으로도 곧장 고통 속에 빠져들었다. 그녀의 평생 행복이 걸린 그 단 하나의 일을 제외하면, 그녀는 그의 마음을 다스리는 더없는 여주인이었던 것이다.\n\n대위를 향한 분노를, 법으로 그를 손봐 주겠다는 맹세로 마무리한 향사는 이제 소피아에게로 계단을 올라갔다. 문을 열고 들어서자, 그는 딸이 새파랗게 질려 숨을 헐떡이는 것을 보았다. 그러나 아버지를 보는 순간, 그녀는 온 정신을 그러모아 그의 손을 붙잡고 격정에 차서 부르짖었다. \"오, 아버지, 무서워서 죽을 것만 같았어요! 부디 아버지께 아무 해도 없었기를 하늘에 빌어요.\" \"아니, 아니다.\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"큰 해는 없다. 그 못된 놈이 날 크게 다치게 하진 않았어. 허나 그놈을 법으로 가만두지 않으면 내가 사람이 아니다.\" \"제발 아버지,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"무슨 일인지 말씀해 주세요. 아버지를 모욕한 게 누구예요?\" \"그자 이름은 모른다.\" 웨스턴이 답했다. \"어떤 장교 나부랭이겠지. 우리한테 매를 맞고도 도리어 우리가 값을 치러야 하는 그런 부류 말이다. 허나 이번엔 내가 그놈한테 단단히 치르게 해 줄 테다, 그 못된 놈한테 뭐라도 있다면 말이지. 뭐 없을 것 같다만. 그렇게 곱게 차려입긴 했어도, 세상에 땅 한 뼘이나 가졌는지 의심스럽단 말이야.\" \"그런데 아버지,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"다툰 까닭이 뭐였어요?\" \"그게 뭐겠느냐, 소피.\" 향사가 답했다. \"다 너 때문이지, 소피! 내 모든 불행이 다 너 탓이다. 넌 결국 이 가엾은 아비를 죽이고 말 게야. 여기 어디 사는지도 모를 경이라는 작자가, 참 어이없게도! 너를 마음에 들어 한답시고, 내가 승낙을 안 해 줬다고 나한테 결투장을 보내왔지 뭐냐. 자, 착한 딸이 되어 주렴, 소피. 이 아비의 온갖 골칫거리를 끝내 다오. 자, 그놈한테 시집가겠다고 해 주렴. 그놈이 하루 이틀 안에 도시로 올 게다. 오는 대로 혼인하겠다고 이 아비한테 약속만 해 주면, 넌 날 세상에서 가장 행복한 사내로 만들어 주는 거고, 나도 널 가장 행복한 여인으로 만들어 주마. 런던에서 가장 고운 옷에, 가장 좋은 보석에, 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차까지 네 마음대로 부리게 해 주마. 난 벌써 올워디한테 내 재산 절반을 내주겠다고 약속했다—에라, 까짓것! 통째로 내준대도 그리 아깝지 않을 것 같구나.\" \"아버지께서는,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제 말도 좀 들어 주실 만큼 자상하신가요?\"—\"어찌 그런 걸 묻느냐, 소피?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내가 네 목소리를 영국에서 제일 좋은 사냥개 무리의 노랫소리보다도 더 듣고 싶어 한다는 걸 알면서.—네 말을 듣고말고, 내 어여쁜 딸아! 살아 있는 한 네 목소리를 들었으면 좋겠다. 만일 그 기쁨을 잃어버린다면, 난 단 한순간을 더 살자고 동전 한 닢도 내놓지 않을 게다. 정말이지, 소피, 넌 내가 널 얼마나 사랑하는지 모른다, 정말 몰라. 알았다면 결코 도망쳐서 이 가엾은 아비를 버려 두지 못했을 게야. 이 세상에 어린 소피 말고는 아무 기쁨도, 아무 위안도 없는 이 아비를 말이다.\" 이 말에 그의 눈에는 눈물이 고였다. 그러자 소피아도 (눈에서 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며) 답했다. \"정말이에요, 사랑하는 아버지. 아버지께서 저를 애틋이 사랑하신 걸 잘 알아요. 제가 그 사랑을 얼마나 진심으로 되돌려 드렸는지는 하늘이 증인이시고요. 이 사람의 품에 억지로 떠밀려 갈지 모른다는 두려움이 아니었다면, 그 어떤 것도 제가 이토록 격렬히 사랑하는 아버지에게서 달아나도록 몰아세우지 못했을 거예요. 아버지의 행복을 위해서라면 저는 기꺼이 제 목숨도 바칠 거예요. 아니, 저는 그보다 더한 일까지 하려고 스스로를 다잡으려 애썼고, 아버지의 뜻을 따르기 위해 세상에서 가장 비참한 삶을 견디겠다는 결심을 거의 세울 뻔했어요. 다만 제 마음을 도저히 끌고 갈 수 없었던 것이 바로 그 한 가지 결심이었어요. 앞으로도 결코 그러지 못할 거고요.\" 여기서 향사는 사나운 눈빛을 띠기 시작했고, 그 입가에는 거품이 일었다. 소피아는 그것을 보고 끝까지 들어 달라고 청한 뒤 말을 이었다. \"만일 아버지의 목숨이나 건강, 혹은 아버지의 어떤 진정한 행복이 걸린 일이라면, 여기 결심을 굳힌 따님이 있어요. 아버지를 지킬 수만 있다면 제가 못 견딜 비참함이 어디 있겠어요, 그렇지 못하다면 하늘이 저를 쳐 버려도 좋아요!—아니, 그 가장 혐오스럽고 가장 역겨운 운명까지도 저는 끌어안을 거예요. 아버지를 위해서라면 블리필에게 이 손을 내어 드리겠어요.\"—\"그게 바로 나를 지키는 거라니까.\" 아버지가 답했다. \"그게 나한테 건강과 행복과 목숨, 그 모든 걸 줄 게다.—맹세코, 네가 날 거절하면 난 죽고 말 게야. 가슴이 미어질 게다, 정말로, 맹세코.\" —\"그럴 수가 있나요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"절 비참하게 만들고 싶은 마음이 그토록 크실 수가.\" —\"아니라니까.\" 그가 큰 소리로 답했다. \"널 행복하게 만드는 일이라면 이 세상에서 내가 못 할 일은 없다, 빌어먹을!\" —\"그런데도 사랑하는 아버지께서는 무엇이 절 행복하게 할지에 대해 제가 조금도 알도록 허락해 주지 않으시는 거예요? 행복이 마음먹기에 달렸다는 게 참말이라면, 제가 스스로를 이 세상 모든 가련한 이들 중 가장 비참하다고 여기게 될 때, 제 처지는 대체 어떻게 되겠어요?\" \"그렇게 여기는 편이 낫지.\" 그가 말했다. \"가난뱅이 사생아 뜨내기 따위와 혼인해서 진짜로 그렇게 되는 것보다는.\" \"아버지께서 그것으로 흡족하시다면,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"더없이 엄숙한 약속을 드리겠어요. 아버지께서 살아 계신 동안에는 그 사람도, 다른 누구도, 아버지의 승낙 없이는 결코 혼인하지 않겠다고요. 제 평생을 아버지를 모시는 데 바치게 해 주세요. 다시 아버지의 가엾은 소피가 되게 해 주시고, 예전처럼 아버지를 기쁘게 하고 즐겁게 해 드리는 것을 제 온 일이자 기쁨으로 삼게 해 주세요.\" \"이봐라, 소피.\" 향사가 답했다. \"난 이런 식으로 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니다. 그랬다간 네 웨스턴 고모가 날 자기 생각대로 바보 천치라고 여길 빌미만 생기지. 안 돼, 안 돼, 소피, 똑똑히 알아 둬라. 난 사내가 걸린 일에서 여자의 말 따위를 곧이듣지 않을 만큼은 더 지혜롭고 세상 물정도 더 잘 안다.\" \"아버지, 제가 어떻게 이런 불신을 받을 짓을 했나요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 아버지께 한 약속을 단 하나라도 어긴 적 있나요? 아니면 요람에 있을 적부터 거짓말로 죄지은 적이 한 번이라도 있었나요?\" \"이봐라, 소피.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그건 이러쿵저러쿵할 일이 아니다. 난 이 혼인을 단단히 작정했고, 넌 그놈을 맞이하게 될 거다, 안 그러면 내가 사람이 아니다. 빌어먹을, 안 그러면 내가 사람이 아니라니까. 네가 그 이튿날 아침에 목을 매단다 해도 말이다.\" 이 말을 되뇌며 그는 주먹을 불끈 쥐고, 눈썹을 찌푸리고, 입술을 깨물며 어찌나 우렁차게 호통을 쳤던지, 가엾고 괴롭고 겁에 질린 소피아는 부들부들 떨며 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다. 그리고 눈물이 곧장 쏟아져 그녀를 풀어 주지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 더 나쁜 일이 뒤따랐을지도 모른다.\n\n웨스턴은 딸의 그 가련한 모습을 보면서도, 사형 선고를 받은 남편에게 마지막 작별을 고하는 다정한 아내의 고통을 바라보는 뉴게이트 간수가 느끼는 것만큼의 가책이나 회한조차 느끼지 않았다. 아니, 오히려 그는 정직하고 점잖은 상인이, 비록 정당한 빚이긴 하나 그 가련한 자가 차마 갚지 못하는 10파운드 때문에 채무자가 감옥으로 끌려가는 꼴을 바라볼 때 일어나는 그런 감정으로 딸을 내려다보았다. 아니, 더 꼭 들어맞게 말하자면, 그는 자기 손아귀에 옭아 넣은 어떤 가엾은 순진한 처녀가 이른바 손님맞이라는 것을 처음 제안받고 발작을 일으킬 때 뚜쟁이가 느끼는 것과 똑같은 양심의 가책을 느꼈다. 사실 이 비유는 꼭 들어맞을 뻔했으나, 다만 한 가지 차이가 있다. 뚜쟁이는 제가 하는 짓에서 이익을 챙기지만, 아버지는—비록 눈이 멀어 달리 여길지 몰라도—실은 딸을 거의 그에 맞먹는 매음으로 떠미는 데서 아무런 이익도 챙길 수 없다는 점이다.\n\n이런 상태로 그는 가엾은 소피아를 남겨 두고, 눈물의 효험에 대해 아주 천박한 한마디를 내뱉으며 방을 잠그고 떠나 목사에게로 돌아왔다. 목사는 그 처녀를 위해 차마 입에 담을 수 있는 말은 다 했는데, 비록 그것이 그의 본분이 요구하는 만큼에는 미치지 못했을지 모르나, 그래도 향사를 격렬한 분노에 빠뜨리고 성직자 전체에 대해 온갖 무례한 험담을 퍼붓게 만들기에는 충분했다. 그 험담들은 우리가 그 거룩한 직분을 너무도 존경하는 까닭에 차마 종이에 옮기지 않겠다.\n\n3장.\n소피아가 갇혀 지내는 동안 일어난 일.\n\n향사가 묵고 있던 집의 안주인은 일찌감치 자기 손님들에 대해 묘한 생각을 품기 시작했다. 그러나 그 향사가 어마어마한 재산을 가진 사람이라는 말을 들은 데다, 자기 방값을 유난히 비싸게 받아 두었던 터라, 굳이 비위를 거스를 마음은 없었다. 가엾은 소피아가 갇혀 있는 처지에 대해 다소 마음이 쓰이긴 했다. 집안의 하녀가 소피아의 더없이 다정한 성품과 상냥함을 좋게 전했고, 향사의 하인들도 모두 그 말을 거들었기 때문이다. 그래도 그녀는 자기 입으로 보기에 몹시 성마른 양반이라 여겨지는 사람을 자극하기에는, 제 잇속을 챙기는 마음이 훨씬 더 컸다.\n\n소피아는 거의 먹지 않았으나, 끼니는 빠짐없이 차려졌다. 사실 내 생각엔, 소피아가 무엇이든 귀한 음식을 먹고 싶어 했다면, 향사가 아무리 화가 나 있더라도 수고와 비용을 아끼지 않고 그것을 구해다 주었으리라. 일부 독자에게는 이상하게 보일지 모르나, 그는 진심으로 딸을 끔찍이 아꼈고, 딸에게 어떤 즐거움이든 주는 것이야말로 그의 인생에서 가장 큰 만족이었기 때문이다.\n\n저녁 식사 시간이 되자 검은 조지가 영계 한 마리를 올려다 갔고, 향사 자신은 (열쇠를 결코 내주지 않겠다고 맹세했으므로) 문간을 지켰다. 조지가 접시를 내려놓는 동안, 그와 소피아 사이에 몇 마디 인사가 오갔다 (그는 소피아가 시골을 떠난 뒤로 그녀를 본 적이 없었고, 소피아는 어떤 하인이든 어떤 이들이 조금 아랫사람을 대하는 것보다 훨씬 더 정중하게 대했다). 소피아는 영계를 도로 가져가라며, 먹을 수가 없다고 했다. 그러나 조지는 한번 들어 보시라고 청했고, 특히 그 안에 가득하다며 알을 권했다.\n\n이 모든 시간 동안 향사는 문간에서 기다리고 있었다. 그러나 조지는 주인의 큰 총애를 받는 자였다. 그의 일이라는 게 가장 중대한 일, 곧 사냥감을 다루는 일이었던지라, 그는 여러모로 제멋대로 굴어도 괜찮은 데 익숙해져 있었다. 그는 자기 아가씨를 몹시 보고 싶었노라며 일부러 나서서 식사를 올려다 갔고, 그래서 소피아와 정중한 말을 주고받느라 주인을 십 분 넘게 세워 두고도 거리낌이 없었다. 그가 돌아왔을 때 문간에서 받은 것이라곤 기분 좋은 가벼운 핀잔뿐이었다.\n\n영계며 자고새며 꿩 따위의 알이 소피아가 가장 좋아하는 별미라는 것을 조지는 잘 알고 있었다. 그러니 천성이 무척 선량한 그가, 집안의 하인들이 모두 아가씨가 굶어 죽지나 않을까 두려워하던 그 무렵에, 이런 종류의 진미를 그녀에게 챙겨다 준 것은 조금도 이상한 일이 아니었다. 소피아는 지난 마흔 시간 동안 음식 한 입 거의 삼키지 못했으니 말이다.\n\n번민이 모든 사람에게, 흔히 과부에게 미치는 것과 똑같은 작용을 하는 것은 아니다. 과부에게는 번민이 밴스테드 다운스나 솔즈베리 평원의 바람으로도 그렇게까지 돋울 수 없을 만큼 식욕을 날카롭게 만드는 일이 잦지만 말이다. 그래도 누가 뭐라 반대로 말하든, 가장 숭고한 슬픔이라 해도 결국에는 먹기 마련이다. 그리하여 소피아 자신도 잠시 망설인 끝에 그 새를 발라내기 시작했고, 조지가 전한 대로 정말 알이 가득 차 있음을 발견했다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 그 알에 기뻐했다 한들, 그 안에는 왕립학회를 훨씬 더 기쁘게 했을 무언가가 들어 있었다. 다리가 셋 달린 새가 — 어쩌면 세월이 그런 것을 천 마리쯤 만들어 냈을 텐데도 — 그토록 값을 매길 수 없는 진귀한 것이라면, 동물의 생리 법칙을 송두리째 거스르며 뱃속에 편지 한 장을 품은 새는 대체 얼마짜리로 쳐 주어야 할까? 오비디우스는 히아킨토스가 변신하여 된 어느 꽃에 대해, 그 잎에 글자가 적혀 있다고 전하며, 베르길리우스는 그것을 당대의 왕립학회에 기적이라 천거했다. 그러나 어느 시대 어느 나라도 모이주머니에 편지를 품은 새를 기록한 적은 없었다.\n\n그러나 이런 종류의 기적이라면 유럽의 모든 과학 아카데미(Académies des Sciences)를 끌어들였을 법하고, 어쩌면 아무 소득 없는 탐구로 이어졌을 법하지만, 독자는 존스 씨와 파트리지 씨 사이에 오간 지난번 대화를 떠올리기만 해도, 이 편지가 어디서 왔고 어떻게 그 새 속으로 들어가게 되었는지 아주 쉽게 납득할 것이다.\n\n소피아는 그토록 오래 굶었음에도, 또 가장 좋아하는 음식이 눈앞에 있었음에도, 편지를 보자마자 곧장 그것을 낚아채 뜯어 열고는 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n“아가씨,\n\n제가 누구에게 글을 쓰는 영광을 누리는지 모르는 사람이었다면, 아무리 어렵더라도 아너 부인이 제게 전한 소식에 제 마음이 얼마나 끔찍한 지경에 빠졌는지를 그려 보려 애썼을 것입니다. 그러나 오직 다정한 마음만이 다정한 마음이 느낄 수 있는 그 고통을 참되이 헤아릴 수 있는 법이니, 제 소피아가 누구보다도 가장 빼어나게 지닌 이 더없이 사랑스러운 자질이야말로, 이 슬픈 일을 두고 그대의 존스가 얼마나 괴로워했을지 그대에게 충분히 일러 줄 것입니다. 그대에게 닥친 어떤 불행을 전해 들을 때, 제 고뇌를 더욱 키울 만한 사정이 이 세상에 있을까요? 분명히 단 하나가 있고, 저는 바로 그것으로 저주받았습니다. 소피아여, 그것은 제 자신이 이 모든 비참함의 원인이라는 그 무서운 생각입니다. 어쩌면 제가 여기서 제게 너무 과한 영예를 돌리는지도 모르나, 이토록 값비싸게 치르는 영예라면 누구도 제게서 그것을 시샘하지는 않을 것입니다. 이 주제넘음을 용서해 주시고, 더 큰 주제넘음 또한 용서해 주십시오. 제 충고나, 제 도움이나, 제가 곁에 있는 것이나, 제가 떠나 있는 것이나, 제 죽음이나, 제 고통이 그대에게 조금이라도 위안이 될 수 있겠는지 여쭙는 이 일을 말입니다. 더없이 완벽한 흠모, 더없이 빈틈없는 보살핌, 더없이 뜨거운 사랑, 더없이 녹아드는 다정함, 그대의 뜻에 대한 더없이 순순한 복종이, 그대가 제 행복을 위해 희생하려는 것에 보답이 될 수 있겠습니까? 만일 그럴 수 있다면, 제 사랑스러운 천사여, 언제나 그대를 받아 안고 지켜 주려 열려 있는 이 두 팔로 날아오십시오. 그대가 그대 자신만을 데려오든, 세상의 온갖 재물을 함께 가져오든, 제 생각에는 따져 볼 가치도 없는 차이일 뿐입니다. 반대로, 만일 지혜가 우세하여, 가장 무르익은 숙고 끝에 그대에게 이 희생이 너무 크다고 일러 준다면, 그리고 저를 버리는 것 말고는 아버님과 화해하고 그대의 소중한 마음에 평안을 되찾아 줄 길이 달리 없다면, 부디 저를 영영 그대의 생각에서 몰아내고, 그대의 결단을 떨치십시오. 제 고통에 대한 어떤 연민도 그 다정한 가슴속에서 조금의 무게도 갖지 못하게 하십시오. 아가씨, 믿어 주십시오. 저는 제 자신보다 그대를 더 진심으로 사랑하기에, 저의 크고 으뜸가는 목적은 그대의 행복입니다. 저의 첫 번째 소망은 (어찌하여 운명은 제게 그것을 허락하지 않았을까요?) 그대가 매 순간 세상에서 가장 행복한 여인이 되는 모습을 보는 것이었고, 외람되나 말씀드리면 지금도 그러합니다. 저의 두 번째 소망은 그대가 그러하다는 소식을 듣는 것입니다. 그러나 그대가, 다음과 같은 사람에게 단 한 순간의 괴로움이라도 빚지고 있다고 제가 생각하는 한, 이 세상 어떤 비참함도 제 것에 견줄 수 없을 것입니다.\n아가씨,\n\n모든 의미에서, 그리고 모든 뜻에서,\n그대에게 헌신하는,\n\n토머스 존스 올림.”\n이 편지를 두고 소피아가 무어라 말했는지, 무엇을 했는지, 무슨 생각을 했는지, 그것을 몇 번이나 읽었는지, 한 번 넘게 읽었는지 어떤지는 모두 독자의 상상에 맡기겠다. 이 편지에 대한 답장은 어쩌면 나중에 보게 될지도 모르나, 지금은 아니다. 여러 까닭이 있지만 그중 하나는, 그녀가 지금은 답장을 쓰지 않았다는 것이며, 거기에는 몇 가지 그럴 만한 이유가 있는데, 그중 하나가 바로 그녀에게 종이도, 펜도, 잉크도 없었다는 것이다.\n\n저녁 무렵, 소피아가 받은 편지에 대해, 혹은 다른 무언가에 대해 골똘히 생각하고 있을 때, 아래층에서 들려온 요란한 소리가 그녀의 사색을 흩뜨렸다. 이 소리는 다름 아닌 두 사람 사이에 벌어진 한바탕 언쟁이었다. 다투는 이들 중 한 사람은 목소리로 보아 곧장 자기 아버지임을 알아차렸으나, 그보다 더 날카로운 음색이 자기 고모 웨스턴의 목청에서 나오는 것임은 그리 빨리 알아채지 못했다. 고모는 막 도시에 도착한 참이었는데, 헤라클레스 기둥 여관에 들른 자기 하인 하나를 통해 오라버니가 묵는 곳을 알아내고는, 곧장 그의 숙소로 마차를 몰아온 것이었다.\n\n그러므로 우리는 지금은 소피아와 작별하고, 여느 때의 예의 바른 태도로 그 마님을 모시기로 하자.\n\n소피아가 갇힘에서 풀려나는 이야기.\n\n지주와 목사는(여관 주인은 이제 다른 일에 매여 있었으므로) 함께 담배를 피우고 있었는데, 그때 부인이 도착했다는 소식이 처음 전해졌다. 지주는 누이의 이름을 듣자마자 곧장 아래로 달려 내려가 그녀를 위층으로 안내했다. 그는 이런 격식을 무척 따지는 사람이었고, 특히 누이에게는 더욱 그러했다. 그는 다른 어떤 사람보다도 누이를 어려워했으니, 본인은 결코 그것을 인정하려 들지 않았고 어쩌면 스스로도 알지 못했을 것이다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 식당에 들어서자마자 의자에 몸을 던지고는 이렇게 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했다. \"어휴, 정말이지 이렇게 견디기 힘든 여정을 겪은 사람은 없을 거예요. 통행세 법령이 그렇게 많이 생겼는데도 길은 전보다 더 나빠진 것 같아요. 어머나, 오라버니, 어쩌자고 이런 끔찍한 곳에 드신 거예요? 분명히 장담컨대, 지체 있는 사람치고 여기에 발을 들인 이는 없었을 거예요.\" \"글쎄다,\" 지주가 말했다. \"내 보기엔 그럭저럭 괜찮은데. 여관 주인이 추천한 거다. 그자가 상류층 사람들을 거의 다 안다기에, 그자라면 그런 사람들 틈에 끼는 법을 제일 잘 일러줄 거라고 생각했지.\" \"그래서, 내 조카는 어디 있죠?\" 부인이 말했다. \"벌써 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아뵙기는 했어요?\" \"그럼, 그럼,\" 지주가 말했다. \"네 조카는 아주 안전해. 위층 방에 있다.\" \"뭐라고요!\" 부인이 답했다. \"제 조카가 이 집에 있는데, 제가 여기 온 줄도 모른단 말이에요?\" \"아니, 아무도 그 애한테 함부로 못 가.\" 지주가 말했다. \"자물쇠로 채워 가뒀거든. 안전하게 데리고 있어. 내가 도시에 온 첫날 밤 사촌 마님한테서 데려와서, 그 뒤로 줄곧 잘 지켜왔다. 자루 속에 든 여우만큼이나 단단히 가둬뒀으니 걱정 마라.\" \"맙소사!\" 웨스턴 부인이 되받았다. \"이게 무슨 소리예요? 오라버니가 직접 도시로 올라오는 걸 제가 허락하면 무슨 꼴이 날지 짐작은 했지만요. 아니, 사실 그건 오라버니의 고집불통 뜻이었지, 제가 거기에 동의했다는 책임은 질 수 없어요. 오라버니, 이런 막무가내 수단은 쓰지 않겠다고 제게 약속하지 않았어요? 바로 그런 막무가내 수단 때문에 시골에서 조카가 오라버니한테서 도망친 거잖아요? 또 그런 짓을 하게 만들 작정이세요?\" \"빌어먹을, 제기랄!\" 지주가 담뱃대를 바닥에 내동댕이치며 소리쳤다. \"이런 소리를 들은 사람이 또 있겠나? 내가 한 일을 죄다 칭찬해 줄 줄 알았더니, 이런 식으로 몰아세우다니!\" \"아니, 오라버니!\" 부인이 말했다. \"제가 언제 딸을 가둔 일로 오라버니를 칭찬할 거라고 짐작할 만한 빌미를 조금이라도 준 적 있어요? 자유로운 나라에서 여자를 그런 제멋대로의 권력으로 다뤄선 안 된다고 제가 누차 말씀드리지 않았어요? 우리도 남자만큼 자유로워요. 그리고 진심으로 바라건대, 우리가 그 자유를 더 누릴 자격이 있다고 말하지 않을 수 있었으면 좋겠네요. 만일 제가 이 끔찍한 집에 한순간이라도 더 머물기를, 또 제가 오라버니를 다시 친척으로 인정하기를, 또 제가 오라버니 가족의 일에 다시 신경 쓰기를 바라신다면, 제 조카를 당장 이 자리에서 풀어줄 것을 요구해요.\" 그녀가 이렇게 말할 때, 등을 난로 쪽으로 두고 한 손은 뒤로 한 채 다른 손에는 코담배 한 줌을 쥐고 선 그 위풍당당한 기세란, 아마존 부대를 이끄는 탈레스트리스조차도 이보다 더 무시무시한 모습을 보인 적이 있었을까 싶을 정도였다. 그러니 가엾은 지주가 그녀가 자아내는 위압감을 당해내지 못한 것도 이상한 일이 아니었다. \"옜다,\" 그가 열쇠를 내던지며 소리쳤다. \"여기 있다, 마음대로 해라. 나는 그저 블리필이 도시에 올 때까지만 가둬둘 작정이었어. 그것도 곧이지. 그동안에 무슨 해라도 생기거든, 누구를 탓해야 하는지나 기억해 둬라.\"\n\n\"제 목숨을 걸고 책임지겠어요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 소리쳤다. \"하지만 한 가지 조건이 아니라면 저는 일절 끼어들지 않겠어요. 그 조건이란, 오라버니가 모든 일을 완전히 제 손에 맡기고, 제가 결국 오라버니더러 나서라고 정해주지 않는 한, 어떤 수단도 직접 취하지 않는 거예요. 오라버니, 만일 이 사전 조건을 받아들이신다면, 저는 그래도 오라버니 가문의 명예를 지키려 애써보겠어요. 그러지 않으신다면, 저는 중립을 지키겠어요.\"\n\n\"부탁드립니다, 어르신,\" 목사가 말했다. \"이번 한 번만은 부인의 충고를 받아들이도록 자신을 허락하십시오. 어쩌면 소피아 아가씨와 마음을 나누어, 더 엄한 수단으로 어르신께서 이루지 못하신 것보다 더 많은 것을 부인께서 해내실지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"뭐야, 네놈까지 나한테 입을 놀려?\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"그렇게 주절대기 시작하면 당장 채찍질을 해줄 테다.\"\n\n\"어머, 오라버니,\" 부인이 답했다. \"성직자한테 그게 무슨 말투예요? 서플 목사님은 분별 있는 분이고, 오라버니에게 가장 좋은 충고를 드리는 거예요. 온 세상이 그분의 의견에 동의할 거라고 저는 믿어요. 하지만 말해두는데, 저는 제 단도직입적인 제안에 즉답을 기대해요. 따님을 제 처분에 맡기시든지, 아니면 따님을 온전히 오라버니의 그 놀라운 분별에 맡기시든지요. 후자라면 저는 여기서 서플 목사님 앞에서 이 진지를 떠나, 오라버니와 오라버니 가족을 영영 등지겠어요.\"\n\n\"부디 제가 중재자가 되게 해주십시오.\" 목사가 소리쳤다. \"간청드립니다.\"\n\n\"이봐, 열쇠는 저기 탁자 위에 있다.\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"원하면 집어가면 될 거 아냐. 누가 막기라도 한대?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 오라버니,\" 부인이 답했다. \"저는 정식으로 그것이 제게 건네지기를, 그리고 약정된 모든 양보가 온전히 비준되기를 요구해요.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 너한테 건네주마. 옜다.\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"정말이지, 누이야, 내가 딸을 너한테 맡기길 거절한 적 있다고는 못 할 거다. 그 애는 너랑 꼬박 한 해도 넘게 살았어. 내가 코빼기도 못 본 채 말이야.\"\n\n\"그 애가 늘 저와 함께 살았더라면,\" 부인이 답했다. \"그 애에게는 행복한 일이었을 거예요. 제 눈앞에서라면 이런 일은 일어나지 않았을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇겠지.\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"잘못한 건 오로지 나뿐이지.\"\n\n\"그래요, 오라버니 잘못이에요.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"제가 누차 그렇게 말씀드릴 수밖에 없었고, 앞으로도 늘 그렇게 말씀드릴 수밖에 없을 거예요. 하지만 이제는 고치시길 바라요. 그리고 지난 잘못에서 충분한 교훈을 얻어, 오라버니의 실수로 제 가장 현명한 계략을 망치지나 마세요. 정말이지, 오라버니, 오라버니는 이런 교섭에 어울리는 사람이 아니에요. 오라버니의 책략이라는 게 통째로 틀려먹었어요. 그러니 다시 한번 요구해요. 끼어들지 마세요. 지난 일만 기억하세요.\"\n\n\"제기랄, 빌어먹을, 누이야,\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"나더러 무슨 말을 하란 말이냐? 너는 악마라도 화나게 할 사람이다.\"\n\n\"거 봐요, 또 저러시네.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"딱 옛날 버릇 그대로예요. 오라버니, 내가 보니 오라버니랑은 말이 안 통해요. 제가 사람을 화나게 할 만한 말을 한 마디라도 했는지, 분별 있는 분이신 서플 목사님께 여쭤보겠어요. 오라버니는 무슨 일에서나 그렇게 외고집이라니까요.\"\n\n\"제발 부탁드립니다, 부인,\" 목사가 말했다. \"어르신을 자극하지 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n\"자극이라뇨?\" 부인이 말했다. \"당신도 저 양반만큼이나 바보로군요. 좋아요, 오라버니, 끼어들지 않겠다고 약속하셨으니, 제가 다시 한번 조카 일을 맡겠어요. 주여, 남자들 손에 맡겨진 모든 일에 자비를 베푸소서! 여자 하나의 머리가 오라버니 같은 사람 천 명보다 나아요.\" 그러고는 하인을 불러 소피아에게 안내하게 한 뒤, 그녀는 열쇠를 챙겨 들고 나갔다.\n\n그녀가 나가자마자 지주는(먼저 문을 닫고는) 그녀를 향해 스무 번쯤 욕을 퍼붓고 그만큼 진심 어린 저주를 쏟아냈으며, 누이의 재산 따위에 마음을 두었던 자신도 봐주지 않았다. 그러나 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"이렇게 오래 노예 노릇을 했는데, 이제 와서 조금만 더 버티지 못하고 놓쳐버린다면 아까운 노릇이지. 저 망할 것이 영원히 살 수도 없는 노릇이고, 유언장에 내 몫으로 적혀 있는 걸 내가 아니까 말이야.\"\n\n목사는 이 결심을 크게 칭찬했다. 이윽고 지주는 술을 한 병 더 들이라 명했는데, 이는 기쁘거나 언짢은 일이 있을 때면 늘 쓰는 그의 방식이었다. 그러고는 이 약효 좋은 청량제를 넉넉히 들이켜 노여움을 깡그리 씻어냈으니, 웨스턴 부인이 소피아를 데리고 방으로 돌아왔을 때 그의 기분은 더없이 평온하고 잔잔해져 있었다. 젊은 아가씨는 모자와 두건 달린 외투를 걸치고 있었고, 고모는 웨스턴 씨에게 알렸다. \"조카를 제 거처로 데려갈 작정이에요. 정말이지, 오라버니,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이런 방들은 멀쩡한 기독교인이 머물 만한 곳이 못 돼요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 부인,\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"당신 뜻대로 하시구려. 그 애가 당신 손보다 더 좋은 손에 맡겨질 데가 어디 있겠소. 여기 목사도 증언해 줄 거요. 내가 당신 안 보는 데서 쉰 번도 넘게, 당신이 세상에서 가장 분별 있는 여자 중 하나라고 말했다는 걸 말이오.\"\n\n\"그것에 대해서는,\" 목사가 소리쳤다. \"제가 기꺼이 증언하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 오라버니,\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"저도 늘 오라버니를 그만큼 좋게 평했던 게 분명해요. 오라버니, 성미가 좀 지나치게 급한 건 인정하셔야 해요. 하지만 시간을 두고 곰곰이 생각하실 때면, 저는 오라버니보다 더 사리 밝은 분을 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 누이야, 네가 그리 생각한다면,\" 지주가 말했다. \"온 마음을 다해 네 건강을 위해 한잔하마. 나도 가끔은 좀 욱하긴 해도, 앙심을 품는 건 질색이다. 소피, 착한 아이가 되어, 고모가 시키는 건 뭐든지 하거라.\"\n\n\"그 애는 조금도 의심하지 않아요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 답했다. \"이미 그 애 눈앞에 본보기가 있었으니까요. 제 충고를 무시하다가 신세를 망친 그 못된 사촌 해리엇의 행실 말이에요. 아, 오라버니, 어떻게 생각하세요? 오라버니가 런던으로 떠나고서 소리가 채 닿지 않을 만큼 멀어졌나 싶을 때, 누가 들이닥쳤는지 아세요? 글쎄 그 뻔뻔한 작자, 그 끔찍한 아일랜드 이름을 가진—피츠패트릭 말이에요. 그자가 기별도 없이 불쑥 들이닥치는 바람에, 안 그랬으면 만나지도 않았을 거예요. 그자는 제 아내 얘기를 길게, 알아들을 수도 없게 늘어놓으면서 억지로 제게 들으라 강요하더군요. 하지만 저는 거의 대꾸도 하지 않고, 아내가 보낸 편지를 그자에게 건네면서 직접 답하라고 일렀어요. 그 못된 작자가 우리를 찾아내려 들겠지만, 부탁이니 그 애는 만나지 마세요. 저는 절대 만나지 않기로 마음먹었으니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 애를 만나!\" 지주가 답했다. \"그건 걱정 마라. 그런 불효막심한 계집애한테 부추길 일은 하지 않을 거다. 그 작자, 그 애 남편 말이다, 내가 집에 없었으니 그자한텐 다행이지. 빌어먹을, 못 연못에서 한바탕 헤엄을 치게 해줬을 텐데. 봐라, 소피, 불효가 사람들을 어떤 꼴로 만드는지. 네 집안에 본보기가 있잖느냐.\"\n\n\"오라버니,\" 고모가 소리쳤다. \"그렇게 끔찍한 얘기를 되풀이해서 조카에게 충격 줄 거 없어요. 어째서 모든 걸 전적으로 제게 맡기지 않으시는 거예요?\" \"알았다, 알았어. 그러마, 그러마.\" 지주가 말했다.\n\n그리고 이제 웨스턴 부인이, 소피아에게는 다행스럽게도, 가마를 불러오라 명하면서 대화를 끝맺었다. 다행이라고 한 까닭은, 만일 대화가 훨씬 더 길게 이어졌다면 오누이 사이에 십중팔구 새로운 다툼거리가 생겼을 것이기 때문이다. 둘 사이의 유일한 차이라곤 교육과 성별뿐이었다. 둘 다 똑같이 격렬하고 똑같이 막무가내였으며, 둘 다 소피아를 끔찍이 아끼면서도, 둘 다 서로를 더없이 경멸했으니 말이다.\n\n# Chapter v.\n\n존스가 소피아에게서 편지를 받고, 밀러 부인과 파트리지와 함께 연극을 보러 가다.\n\n블랙 조지가 도시에 도착했다는 소식, 그리고 그 고마운 사내가 옛 은인을 위해 해 주겠다고 약속한 호의는, 소피아 때문에 겪어 온 온갖 불안과 초조 한가운데 있던 존스에게 큰 위로가 되었다. 바로 그 조지를 통해 존스는 자기 편지에 대한 다음과 같은 답장을 받았다. 자유를 되찾으면서 펜과 잉크와 종이의 사용까지 함께 되돌려 받은 소피아가, 감금에서 풀려나던 바로 그날 저녁에 쓴 편지였다.\n\n\"선생님,\n\n당신이 쓰신 말씀의 진심을 의심하지 않기에, 제 고통 가운데 일부가 끝났다는 소식을 들으시면 기뻐하시리라 믿습니다. 웨스턴 고모님이 오신 덕분인데, 저는 지금 그분과 함께 지내며 제가 바랄 수 있는 모든 자유를 누리고 있습니다. 다만 고모님이 제게 한 가지 약속만은 꼭 하라고 고집하셨는데, 그분이 알지 못하고 동의하지 않은 어떤 사람도 만나거나 이야기를 나누지 않겠다는 것입니다. 이 약속을 저는 더없이 엄숙하게 드렸고, 더없이 어김없이 지킬 것입니다. 비록 고모님이 편지 쓰는 것을 분명히 금하지는 않으셨지만, 그것은 깜빡 잊으신 데서 온 누락일 뿐이거나, 어쩌면 '이야기를 나눈다'는 말 속에 이미 포함되어 있을 것입니다. 그러나 저로서는 이것을 제 명예에 대한 고모님의 너그러운 신뢰를 저버리는 일로 여기지 않을 수 없으니, 이후로 제가 그분 모르게 편지를 계속 쓰거나 편지를 받으리라고는 기대하지 마십시오. 제게 약속이란 매우 거룩한 것이어서, 말로 드러난 것뿐 아니라 거기서 미루어 짐작되는 모든 것에까지 미쳐야 합니다. 이 점을 곰곰이 생각해 보시면, 어쩌면 당신께 어떤 위안이 될지도 모르겠습니다. 그런데 제가 어찌하여 당신께 이런 종류의 위안을 말씀드릴까요. 비록 더없이 훌륭하신 아버지의 뜻을 결코 따를 수 없는 한 가지 일이 있긴 하나, 저는 결코 아버지를 거스르며 행동하거나, 그분의 동의 없이 중대한 일에 한 걸음도 내딛지 않으리라 굳게 결심했기 때문입니다. 이 점을 굳게 믿으신다면, 운명이 (어쩌면) 불가능하게 만들어 버린 것에서 마음을 거두는 법을 배우셔야 할 것입니다. 이것이 당신 자신의 이익이 권하는 바입니다. 또 이것이, 바라건대, 올워디 씨로 하여금 당신과 화해하시게 할 수 있습니다. 만일 그리되겠다면, 부디 그 길을 좇으시기를 제가 당부드립니다. 여러 일들이 제게 어떤 빚을 지웠고, 당신의 선한 마음은 아마도 더 많은 빚을 지웠습니다. 운명이 언젠가는 우리 두 사람 모두에게 지금보다 더 다정해질지도 모릅니다. 이것만은 믿어 주십시오. 저는 언제나 당신을, 당신이 마땅히 받으실 만하다고 제가 여기는 그대로 생각할 것이며, 또한 다음과 같은 사람임을.\n선생님,\n\n당신께 신세 진 미천한 종,\n소피아 웨스턴.\n\n당신께 부탁드리니 더는 제게 편지하지 마십시오. 적어도 지금은 말입니다. 그리고 이것을 받아 주십시오. 이제 제게는 아무 쓸모가 없으나 당신께는 분명 필요하리라 아는 것이며, 이 사소한 것은 다만 당신이 그것을 발견하게 한 그 운명에 빚진 것이라 여겨 주십시오.\"[*]\n\n[*] 아마 100파운드짜리 은행 어음을 말하는 듯하다.\n\n이제 막 글자를 깨친 아이라도, 존스가 이 편지를 읽는 데 들인 시간보다는 짧게 한 자 한 자 더듬어 읽었을 것이다. 이 편지가 불러일으킨 감정은 기쁨과 슬픔이 뒤섞인 것이었다. 마치 어떤 선한 사람이 세상을 떠난 벗의 유언장을 들여다보다가, 자기 곤궁함 때문에 더없이 반가운 큰 유산이 자신에게 남겨져 있음을 발견했을 때 그 마음을 갈라놓는 것과 비슷했다. 그러나 전체로 보면 그는 언짢기보다는 기뻤다. 사실 독자는 그가 조금이라도 언짢아했다는 사실에 의아해할지도 모른다. 하지만 독자는 가엾은 존스만큼 깊이 사랑에 빠져 있지는 않다. 사랑이란 하나의 병이어서, 어떤 경우에는 폐병(때로는 사랑이 폐병을 일으키기도 한다)과 닮았지만, 또 어떤 경우에는 그와 정반대로 나아간다. 특히 이 점에서 그러한데, 사랑은 결코 스스로를 다독여 위로하지 않으며, 어떤 징후도 좋게 보는 법이 없다.\n\n한 가지 점만은 그를 완전히 흡족하게 했으니, 곧 그의 연인이 자유를 되찾았고, 이제 적어도 점잖은 대접만은 스스로 확신할 수 있는 어느 부인과 함께 있다는 사실이었다. 또 하나 위로가 되는 정황은, 다른 어떤 남자와도 결코 결혼하지 않겠다는 그녀의 약속을 그녀가 언급했다는 점이었다. 자신의 연정이 아무리 사심 없는 것이라 여기더라도, 또 편지에서 베푼 그 온갖 너그러운 제안에도 불구하고, 그가 소피아가 다른 남자와 결혼했다는 소식보다 더 마음을 찢는 소식을 들을 수 있었을지 나는 몹시 의심스럽다. 설령 그 혼사가 더없이 훌륭하고, 그녀를 더없이 완전한 행복으로 이끌 가능성이 높았다 해도 말이다. 살에서 완전히 떨어져 나와, 실로 온전히 그리고 순수하게 정신적인, 그 세련된 경지의 플라톤적 애정이란 피조물 가운데 여성에게만 한정된 선물이다. 나는 그들 가운데 여럿이 (그리고 분명 매우 진실하게) 이렇게 단언하는 것을 들었다. 연인의 현세적 이익을 위해 그런 포기가 꼭 필요하다고 증명되면, 자신들은 더없이 기꺼이 연인을 연적에게 양보하겠노라고. 그러므로 나는 이런 애정이 자연 속에 실재한다고 결론짓는다. 다만 내가 그 실례를 본 적이 있다고는 감히 말하지 못하겠다.\n\n존스 씨는 앞서 말한 편지를 읽고 입 맞추는 데 세 시간을 보낸 뒤, 마침내 방금 말한 그 생각들 덕분에 기분이 좋아진 상태가 되자, 전에 잡아 두었던 약속 하나를 실행에 옮기기로 했다. 그것은 밀러 부인과 그 작은딸을 극장 일층 관람석으로 모시고 가는 일이었으며, 파트리지도 일행으로 끼워 주기로 한 것이었다. 사실 존스는 많은 사람들이 짐짓 흉내만 내는 그 해학에 대한 진짜 취향을 지니고 있었던 터라, 파트리지의 평을 들으며 큰 재미를 누리리라 기대했다. 그에게서 자연 그대로의 소박한 반응을 기대했던 것인데, 그것은 비록 기예로 다듬어지지 않았으나 또한 기예로 변질되지도 않은 것이었다.\n\n그리하여 일층 관람석 첫 줄에 존스 씨와 밀러 부인, 그 작은딸과 파트리지가 자리를 잡았다. 파트리지는 대뜸 이곳이 지금껏 와 본 곳 가운데 가장 멋진 곳이라고 선언했다. 첫 음악이 연주되자 그는 말했다. \"저렇게 많은 바이올린 연주자가 한꺼번에 켜는데 서로 헷갈리지 않다니, 참 신기한 노릇이오.\" 한 사내가 위쪽 촛불에 불을 붙이는 동안, 그는 밀러 부인에게 외쳤다. \"보세요, 보세요, 부인, 꼭 기도서 끝, 화약 음모 예배 앞에 나오는 그 사람 그림 같소.\" 또 촛불이 모두 켜지자, 그는 한숨을 내쉬며 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다. \"여기서 하룻밤에 태우는 촛불이면, 정직한 가난한 집안 하나가 일 년 내내 살 만하겠소.\"\n\n연극이 시작되자—그것은 《덴마크 왕자 햄릿》이었다—파트리지는 온통 집중했고, 유령이 등장할 때까지 침묵을 깨지 않았다. 유령이 나오자 그는 존스에게 물었다. \"저 이상한 옷차림을 한 사람은 누구요? 어쩐지,\" 그가 말했다, \"그림에서 본 것과 비슷한데. 설마 저게 갑옷은 아니겠지요?\" 존스가 답했다. \"저게 유령이오.\" 그 말에 파트리지는 빙긋 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"그렇게 믿게 해 보시구려, 선생, 할 수 있다면. 평생 유령을 실제로 본 적은 없다고 해도, 막상 보면 그게 유령인지 아닌지 그 정도는 똑똑히 알아볼 자신이 있소. 아니, 아니오, 선생, 유령은 저런 옷차림으로 나타나지 않는 법이오.\" 파트리지 주변에 큰 웃음을 일으킨 이 착각을, 그는 유령과 햄릿 사이의 장면이 나올 때까지 그대로 붙들고 있었다. 그 장면에서 파트리지는 존스에게는 인정하지 않았던 신뢰를 개릭 씨에게는 보냈으니, 어찌나 심하게 부들부들 떨었던지 무릎이 서로 맞부딪칠 지경이었다. 존스가 무슨 일이냐고, 무대 위의 저 전사가 무섭냐고 묻자, 그가 말했다. \"아이고, 선생, 이제 보니 선생이 말한 그대로구려. 나는 아무것도 무섭지 않소. 그저 연극인 줄 아니까. 설령 진짜 유령이라 해도, 이렇게 멀고 사람도 이리 많은데 사람한테 해를 끼칠 리 없지요. 그래도 내가 겁먹었다면, 겁먹은 게 나 혼자만은 아니오.\" \"아니,\" 존스가 외쳤다, \"여기서 너 말고 또 누가 그런 겁쟁이라는 거냐?\" \"아니, 날 겁쟁이라 부르고 싶으면 그러시구려. 하지만 저기 무대 위 저 작은 사람이 겁먹은 게 아니라면, 나는 평생 겁먹은 사람을 본 적이 없는 거요. 그래, 그래, 어서 가 보시구려. 그래, 정말이지! 그럼 누가 바보요? 가시겠다고? 저런 무모한 짓에 주여 자비를 베푸소서!—무슨 일이 닥치든, 그래도 싸오.—따라가라고? 차라리 악마를 따라가지. 아니, 어쩌면 저게 악마일지도 모르오—듣자 하니 악마는 제멋대로 아무 모습이나 꾸며 낼 수 있다고 하니까.—아! 또 나왔다.—더는 안 돼! 안 돼, 이미 충분히 멀리 갔소. 임금님의 온 영토를 다 준대도 내가 갈 만한 데보다 더 멀리 갔단 말이오.\" 존스가 무슨 말을 하려 하자, 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"쉿, 쉿! 선생, 저 소리 안 들리오?\" 그러고는 유령이 말하는 내내, 눈은 반쯤 유령에, 반쯤 햄릿에 고정한 채 입을 떡 벌리고 앉아 있었다. 햄릿에게서 차례로 일어나는 그 감정들이 그에게서도 똑같이 일어났던 것이다.\n\n그 장면이 끝나자 존스가 말했다. \"이런, 파트리지, 자네는 내 기대를 뛰어넘는군. 내가 가능하리라 여긴 것보다 훨씬 더 연극을 즐기고 있어.\" \"아니, 선생,\" 파트리지가 답했다, \"선생이 악마가 무섭지 않다면, 그거야 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이지요. 하지만 분명 그런 것들에 놀라는 건 사람으로서 자연스러운 일이오. 그 안에 아무것도 없다는 걸 알면서도 말이오. 사실 나를 놀라게 한 건 유령이 아니었소. 그거야 그저 이상한 옷차림을 한 사람일 뿐이라고 알아챘을 테니까. 다만 저 작은 사람이 그토록 겁먹는 걸 보니, 바로 그게 나를 사로잡은 거요.\" \"그럼 자네는,\" 존스가 외쳤다, \"저 사람이 정말로 겁먹었다고 생각하나, 파트리지?\" \"아니, 선생,\" 파트리지가 말했다, \"선생도 나중에 보지 않았소? 그게 자기 아버지의 혼령이고, 정원에서 어떻게 살해당했는지 알게 되자, 두려움이 차츰 가시고 슬픔에 그만 말문이 막혀 버린 것을. 꼭 내가 그 처지였다면 그랬을 것처럼 말이오.—그런데 쉿! 아이고! 저 소리는 뭐요? 또 나왔다.—거참, 그 안에 아무것도 없는 줄 알면서도, 저 사람들 있는 저 아래쪽에 내가 있지 않아서 다행이오.\" 그러고는 다시 눈을 햄릿에게 돌리며, \"그래, 칼을 뽑으시구려. 악마의 힘 앞에 칼이 무슨 소용이람?\"\n\n2막 동안 파트리지는 별다른 말을 거의 하지 않았다. 의상의 화려함에는 크게 감탄했고, 왕의 표정에 대해서도 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다. \"거참,\" 그가 말했다, \"사람이 얼굴에 얼마나 잘 속는지! '얼굴은 믿을 게 못 된다'(Nulla fides fronti)는 말이 참말이구려. 저 왕의 얼굴을 보고서, 저 사람이 살인을 저질렀으리라고 누가 생각하겠소?\" 그러고는 유령에 관해 물었으나, 그를 놀라게 할 작정이었던 존스는 다른 만족은 주지 않고 다만, \"어쩌면 곧 다시, 그것도 불길 속에서 그를 보게 될지도 모른다\"고만 했다.\n\n파트리지는 이것을 겁에 질려 기다리며 앉아 있었다. 그러다 유령이 다음번에 등장하자, 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"저기, 선생, 자 이제, 뭐라 하시겠소? 저 사람이 이제 겁먹은 거요, 아니오? 선생이 나더러 겁먹었다 한 만큼이나 겁먹었구려. 그래, 정말이지 사람이 좀 무서운 거야 어쩔 수 없지요. 나는 저기 저, 이름이 뭐더라, 햄릿 도령이 처한 저 딱한 꼴이 되느니 차라리 온 세상을 마다하겠소. 아이고! 혼령은 어디로 갔담? 살아 있는 내 영혼을 두고 맹세컨대, 저게 땅속으로 가라앉는 걸 본 것 같소.\" \"정말로 제대로 봤소,\" 존스가 답했다. \"그래, 그래,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다, \"그저 연극인 줄 아오. 게다가 이 모든 게 정말 무슨 일이라면 밀러 부인이 저렇게 웃으실 리 없지. 선생으로 말하자면, 악마가 직접 여기 와 있다 해도 무서워하지 않으리라 믿소만.—저기, 저기—그래, 저렇게 길길이 날뛰는 것도 무리가 아니지, 저 더럽고 사악한 계집을 박살을 내시구려. 저게 내 친어미라 해도 나는 똑같이 다루겠소. 그래, 저런 사악한 짓을 저질렀으면 어미에 대한 효도고 뭐고 다 끝장난 거지.—그래, 어서 네 볼일이나 보러 가라, 네 꼴도 보기 싫다.\"\n\n우리의 비평가는 이제 햄릿이 왕 앞에서 선보이는 극중극이 나올 때까지 꽤 잠잠했다. 그는 처음에는 이것을 이해하지 못했으나 존스가 설명해 주었고, 그 의미를 알아차리자마자 자기는 평생 살인을 저지른 적이 없다며 가슴을 쓸어내리기 시작했다. 그러고는 밀러 부인을 돌아보며 물었다. \"저 왕이 마음에 찔린 사람처럼 보이지 않으셨소? 비록,\" 그가 말했다, \"훌륭한 배우라 그걸 감추려고 갖은 애를 다 쓰긴 하지만 말이오. 거참, 나라면 저기 저 사악한 사람이 짊어진 만큼의 죄책을 짊어지느니, 차라리 그가 앉은 자리보다 훨씬 높은 자리에 앉기를 마다하겠소. 저 사람이 달아난 것도 무리가 아니지. 선생 덕분에 나는 다시는 순진한 얼굴은 믿지 않겠소.\"\n\n다음으로 무덤 파는 장면이 파트리지의 주의를 끌었는데, 그는 무대 위로 던져지는 해골의 수효에 크게 놀랐다. 그 말에 존스가 답했다. \"여기가 이 도시에서 가장 이름난 묘지 가운데 하나라오.\" \"그러면 그렇지,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다, \"그러니 이 자리에 귀신이 드는 게지. 그런데 저렇게 서툰 무덤지기는 평생 본 적이 없소. 내가 교회 서기였을 적에 데리고 있던 묘지기라면, 저 사람이 하나 파는 동안 셋은 팠을 거요. 저 사람이 삽을 다루는 꼴이라니, 꼭 평생 처음 삽을 손에 쥐어 본 사람 같구려. 그래, 그래, 노래나 부르시게. 일하느니 노래가 낫다, 이거겠지.\"—햄릿이 해골을 집어 들자, 그가 외쳤다. \"거참! 어떤 사람들은 어쩌면 저리도 겁이 없는지 신기하오. 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 죽은 사람한테 딸린 건 도무지 손도 못 댄단 말이오.—그런데 아까 유령 앞에서는 저 사람도 꽤 겁먹은 것 같던데. '아무도 매 순간 현명하지는 못한 법'(Nemo omnibus horis sapit)이지.\"\n\n연극이 진행되는 동안 더 기억할 만한 일은 별로 없었고, 연극이 끝나자 존스가 그에게 물었다. \"배우들 가운데 누가 제일 마음에 들었나?\" 이 물음에 그는 질문을 다소 못마땅해하는 기색으로 답했다. \"두말할 것 없이 왕이지요.\" \"이런, 파트리지 씨,\" 밀러 부인이 말했다, \"당신은 세상 사람들과 의견이 다르군요. 다들 햄릿을 연기한 이가 지금껏 무대에 선 배우 가운데 최고라는 데 의견이 일치하는데 말이에요.\" \"그 사람이 최고 배우라고요!\" 파트리지가 멸시 어린 비웃음을 띠며 외쳤다, \"원, 그 정도야 나도 그만큼은 하겠소. 분명 나도 유령을 봤다면 꼭 그와 똑같은 표정을 짓고, 그가 한 그대로 했을 거요. 그리고 또, 부인이 말한 그 장면, 그가 어머니와 함께 있던, 그가 그토록 멋지게 연기했다던 그 장면 말이오. 원, 맙소사, 어떤 사람이든, 그러니까 어떤 선량한 사람이든, 그런 어머니를 두었다면 누구나 똑같이 했을 거요. 부인이 나를 놀리는 줄 알지만, 정말이지, 부인, 비록 런던에서 연극을 본 적은 없어도 시골에서 연기라는 걸 본 적은 있소. 그러니 내 돈을 걸자면 왕이오. 그는 말을 죄다 또박또박하게, 다른 사람보다 곱절은 크게 하지 않소.—누가 봐도 저 사람이 진짜 배우인 게지.\"\n\n밀러 부인이 이렇게 파트리지와 이야기를 나누는 동안, 한 부인이 존스 씨에게 다가왔는데, 그는 곧 그 부인이 피츠패트릭 부인임을 알아보았다. 그녀는 관람석 다른 편에서 그를 보았으며, 그에게 큰 도움이 될 만한 할 이야기가 있어 이 기회에 말을 걸게 되었다고 했다. 그러고는 그에게 자기 숙소를 알려 주고 이튿날 아침에 만나기로 약속을 잡았는데, 생각해 보더니 곧 그것을 오후로 바꾸었고, 존스는 그때 찾아뵙겠다고 약속했다.\n\n이렇게 하여 극장에서의 모험은 끝났다. 그곳에서 파트리지는 존스와 밀러 부인뿐 아니라, 그의 말이 들리는 자리에 앉은 모든 이에게 큰 웃음을 안겨 주었으니, 그들은 무대에서 벌어지는 그 어떤 일보다 그가 하는 말에 더 귀를 기울였던 것이다.\n\n그날 밤 그는 유령이 무서워 도무지 잠자리에 들지 못했고, 그 뒤로도 여러 밤 동안 똑같은 두려움에 잠들기 전 두세 시간씩 식은땀을 흘렸으며, 몇 번이나 큰 공포에 휩싸여 잠에서 깨어 외쳤다. \"주여, 우리에게 자비를 베푸소서! 저기 있다.\"\n\n아무리 훌륭한 부모라도 제 자식들에게 한 치의 치우침 없는 공평함을 지키기란 거의 불가능한 일이다. 어느 한쪽이 더 뛰어나서 애정이 기울 까닭이 전혀 없을 때조차 그러하다. 그러나 바로 그 뛰어남 때문에 부모가 한 자식을 더 아끼게 되는 것이라면, 그 부모를 탓하기란 어려운 노릇이다.\n\n나는 이 이야기에 등장하는 모든 인물을 내 자식처럼 여기고 있으니, 소피아에게 똑같은 치우침의 마음이 있음을 고백하지 않을 수 없다. 그리고 그 점에 대해서는, 그녀의 인품이 남달리 뛰어나다는 이유로 독자께서도 내게 똑같은 변명을 허락해 주시리라 믿는다.\n\n내 여주인공을 향한 이 유별난 애틋함은, 그녀 곁을 오래 떠나 있는 것을 도무지 견디지 못하게 만든다. 그러니 나는 지금이라도 조바심을 내며 그녀에게로 돌아가, 아버지의 집을 떠난 뒤로 이 사랑스러운 사람에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알아보고 싶다. 다만 그 전에 블리필 군을 잠깐 찾아가 보지 않을 수 없다.\n\n웨스턴 씨는 딸에 관한 갑작스러운 소식을 듣고 마음이 처음 그 혼란에 빠졌을 때, 또 그녀를 뒤쫓아 가려는 첫 황급함 속에서, 그 사실을 블리필에게 알릴 생각은 한 번도 하지 못했다. 그러나 그리 멀리 가지 못해 정신을 차렸고, 가장 먼저 눈에 띈 여관에 멈추어 블리필에게 사람을 보냈다. 소피아를 찾았다는 것과, 그가 자기를 따라 시내로 올라온다면 즉시 그녀를 그에게 시집보내겠다는 굳은 결심을 전하기 위해서였다.\n\n블리필이 소피아에게 품은 사랑이란, 그녀의 재산을 잃는다든가 그런 종류의 사고가 아니고서는 결코 줄어들지 않는 그 격렬한 종류의 것이었으므로, 그녀가 달아났다 하여 이 혼사에 대한 그의 마음은 조금도 변하지 않았다. 비록 그 일을 제 탓으로 돌릴 수밖에 없는 처지였는데도 말이다. 그래서 그는 이 제안을 아주 기꺼이 받아들였다. 사실 그는 이제 이 아가씨와 혼인함으로써 탐욕 말고도 또 하나의 매우 강한 욕정을 채우려 했으니, 그것은 바로 증오였다. 혼인이란 증오든 사랑이든 어느 쪽이든 똑같이 만족시킬 기회를 준다고 그는 결론지었던 것이다. 그리고 이 견해는 숱한 경험으로 충분히 입증되는 듯하다. 솔직히 말해, 혼인한 사람들이 서로에게 보이는 흔한 행태로 미루어 판단한다면, 우리는 아마 이렇게 결론짓고 싶어질 것이다. 대다수가 마음만 빼고 모든 것을 하나로 합치는 그 결합 속에서, 오직 앞엣것의 욕정을 채우려 들 뿐이라고.\n\n그러나 그의 앞길에는 한 가지 어려움이 놓여 있었으니, 그것은 올워디 씨에게서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 선량한 사람은 소피아의 출분으로(그 일도, 그 까닭도 그에게 숨길 수는 없었으므로) 그녀가 자기 조카를 몹시 싫어한다는 것을 알게 되자, 자신이 속아 일을 이토록 멀리까지 끌고 왔다는 데 진심으로 마음이 무거워지기 시작했다. 그는 결코 그런 부모들의 견해에 동의하지 않았다. 그런 부모들은 혼인 문제에서 자식의 마음을 묻는 일을, 여행을 떠나려 할 때 하인의 기분을 살피는 것만큼이나 하찮게 여기며, 법이나 적어도 체면 때문에 절대적인 강요만은 못 하는 경우가 많은 자들이다. 오히려 그는 이 제도를 더없이 신성한 종류의 것으로 존중했기에, 그것을 거룩하고 침범할 수 없는 것으로 지키기 위해서는 사전의 온갖 신중함이 필요하다고 여겼다. 그리고 매우 현명하게도, 그것을 이루는 가장 확실한 길은 미리 싹튼 애정 위에 그 토대를 놓는 것이라고 결론지었다.\n\n블리필은 자기 또한 속았다는 숱한 맹세와 항변으로, 속임수 문제에 관한 한 삼촌의 노여움을 곧 말끔히 풀어 주었다. 웨스턴의 여러 진술도 그 말과 잘 들어맞았다. 그러나 이제 다시 구애를 시작하는 것에 올워디가 동의하도록 설득하는 일은 겉보기에도 워낙 어려워 보여서, 그 겉모습만으로도 덜 진취적인 사람이라면 단념하기에 충분했을 것이다. 그러나 이 젊은 신사는 제 재주를 너무도 잘 알았기에, 교활함의 영역에 속하는 일이라면 무엇이든 이루기 어렵게 보이지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 여기서 그는 제 애정의 격렬함과, 끈기로 아가씨의 반감을 누그러뜨리리라는 희망을 늘어놓았다. 그는 자신의 앞날의 평온이 모두 걸린 이 일에서, 적어도 성공을 위한 온갖 떳떳한 수단만은 자유로이 시도할 수 있게 해 달라고 간청했다. 그는 가장 부드러운 방법이 아닌 다른 수단으로 그녀를 차지할 생각은 결코 하지 않겠노라고, 하늘이 그것을 막아 주시기를 바란다고 말했다. \"게다가 삼촌,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 방법들이 실패하면, 그때 가서(분명 그래도 늦지 않을 겁니다) 동의를 거두시면 되지 않겠습니까.\" 그는 웨스턴 씨가 이 혼사를 얼마나 크고 간절하게 바라는지를 역설했다. 그리고 마지막으로 그는 존스의 이름을 크게 활용하여, 일어난 모든 일을 그에게 뒤집어씌웠고, 이토록 귀한 아가씨를 그자에게서 지키는 것이야말로 자선의 행위라고까지 말했다.\n\n이 모든 논변은 스왜컴이 든든히 거들어 주었는데, 그는 블리필 본인보다도 부모의 권위를 한층 강하게 강조했다. 그는 블리필이 취하고자 하는 조치들을 기독교적 동기로 돌렸다. \"그리고 비록,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 착한 젊은 신사가 자선을 맨 마지막에 들먹이긴 했습니다만, 저는 그것이야말로 그의 첫째가는 으뜸 고려라고 거의 확신합니다.\"\n\n스퀘어도, 만일 그 자리에 있었더라면, 가락은 다르되 같은 곡조를 노래했을 것이고, 이 일에서 도덕적으로 합당한 점을 많이 찾아냈을 것이다. 그러나 그는 이제 건강을 회복하러 바스로 가 있었다.\n\n올워디는 내키지 않으면서도 마침내 조카의 바람에 굴복했다. 그는 조카와 함께 런던으로 가겠다고, 그곳에서 조카가 아가씨의 마음을 얻기 위해 온갖 정직한 노력을 자유로이 기울여도 좋다고 말했다. \"허나 분명히 말해 두마.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 그 아이의 마음에 어떤 절대적인 강요를 가하는 데에는 결코 동의하지 않을 것이다. 그 아이가 스스로 우러나 응하지 않는 한, 너는 결코 그 아이를 얻지 못할 것이다.\"\n\n이렇게 하여 조카를 향한 올워디의 애정은, 뛰어난 분별이 못한 분별에게 압도당하도록 내어 주고 말았다. 그리고 이렇게, 가장 훌륭한 머리의 신중함은 흔히 가장 훌륭한 가슴의 다정함에게 패하고 마는 것이다.\n\n삼촌에게서 이 뜻밖의 묵인을 얻어 낸 블리필은, 제 목적을 실행에 옮기기 전까지는 한시도 가만히 있지 않았다. 올워디 씨가 시골에 있어야 할 급한 용무도 없었고, 사람이 여행을 떠나는 데에는 채비할 것도 별로 없었으므로, 두 사람은 바로 이튿날 길을 나서 그날 저녁 시내에 닿았다. 우리가 앞서 본 대로, 존스 씨가 파트리지와 함께 연극을 보며 즐기고 있던 바로 그 무렵이었다.\n\n블리필 씨는 도착한 다음 날 아침 웨스턴 씨를 찾아갔고, 그에게서 더없이 친절하고 자애로운 환대를 받았다. 또한 그에게서, 머지않아 소피아가 안겨 줄 수 있는 만큼 행복해지리라는 온갖 가능한 다짐을(어쩌면 가능한 것 이상의 다짐을) 받았다. 게다가 이 지주는 젊은 신사를 거의 그의 뜻을 거스르다시피 하여 제 누이에게로 데려가기 전까지는, 그를 삼촌에게 돌려보내려 하지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter vii.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 조카에게 신중함과 결혼의 정략에 관한 일장 훈계를 늘어놓고 있었다. 그때 오라비와 블리필이, 방문의 예법이 요구하는 것보다 한참 격식을 덜 차린 채 불쑥 들이닥쳤다. 소피아는 블리필을 보자마자 얼굴이 창백해지더니 거의 모든 감각을 잃을 지경이 되었다. 반면에 그녀의 고모는 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아올랐고, 자신의 모든 감각을 여전히 또렷이 부리며 향사를 향해 혀를 놀리기 시작했다.\n\n\"오라버니,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오라버니 행실에 정말이지 기가 막힙니다. 끝내 예의범절이라곤 배우지 못하실 작정인가요? 아직도 모든 방을 제 것으로, 아니면 시골 소작인 하나의 것쯤으로 여기시는 건가요? 지체 있는 여인들의 사적인 공간을 일말의 예의도, 기별도 없이 함부로 침범해도 된다고 생각하시는 건가요?\"——\"아니, 도대체 이번엔 또 무슨 난리야?\" 향사가 말했다. \"마치 내가 너를 무슨 현장에서 잡기라도——\"——\"제발 그 막돼먹은 소리 좀 그만두세요, 오라버니.\" 그녀가 대답했다.——\"오라버니가 가엾은 제 조카를 어찌나 놀라게 했는지, 저 애가 제 몸 하나 가누기도 힘들어하는 게 보이지 않나요.——가거라, 얘야, 물러가서 마음을 좀 추슬러 보렴. 너에게 그게 필요한 게 보이는구나.\" 이 말이 떨어지자, 이보다 더 반가운 명령을 받아 본 적 없던 소피아는 황급히 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"이거 참, 누이,\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"내가 저 애한테 청혼하라고 블리필 군을 여기까지 데려왔는데, 저 애를 쫓아내다니, 너 제정신이 아니구나.\"\n\n\"제정신이 아닌 건 오라버니죠,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"일이 지금 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지 뻔히 알면서도——블리필 군께는 정말 죄송합니다만, 그분도 이렇게 불쾌한 대접을 누구 탓으로 돌려야 할지 잘 아실 겁니다. 저로 말하자면, 블리필 군을 뵙는 것은 언제든 더없이 기쁜 일이지요. 하지만 오라버니가 그분을 억지로 떠밀지만 않았던들, 그분의 분별력으로 이렇게 불쑥 일을 밀어붙이는 짓은 하지 않으셨을 거예요.\"\n\n블리필은 허리를 굽혀 절하고 더듬거리며 바보처럼 서 있었다. 그러나 웨스턴은 그가 그럴듯한 말을 꾸며 낼 틈도 주지 않고 대답했다. \"그래, 그래, 정 그렇다면 내 탓이지, 늘 내 탓이고말고, 암. 그건 그렇고, 어서 그 애를 다시 데려오게 하든지, 아니면 블리필 군이 그 애한테 가게 하든지 하자고.——그가 일부러 올라온 마당에, 한시도 허비할 시간이 없단 말이다.\"\n\n\"오라버니,\" 웨스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"블리필 군은 분별 있는 분이니, 이런 일이 있은 마당에 오늘 아침에 제 조카를 다시 만날 생각 따위는 하지 않으실 거예요. 여자란 섬세한 결로 짜여 있는 존재라, 한번 마음이 흐트러지면 한순간에 다시 추슬러지는 게 아니랍니다. 만약 오라버니가 블리필 군더러 제 조카에게 안부 인사만 전하고 오후에 찾아뵐 영광을 청하도록 두셨더라면, 어쩌면 제가 그 애를 설득해 그분을 만나게 했을지도 모르지요. 하지만 이제는 그런 일을 성사시키기란 틀렸다고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"정말 죄송합니다, 부인,\" 블리필이 외쳤다. \"결코 다 갚을 수 없는 웨스턴 씨의 각별한 호의가 도리어 이런 일을 빚어내고 말았으니——\" \"정말이지, 선생,\" 그녀가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"사과하실 필요 없습니다. 우리 모두 제 오라버니를 워낙 잘 아니까요.\"\n\n\"누가 나를 어찌 알든 난 상관없어,\" 향사가 대답했다.——\"하지만 그가 언제 그 애를 보러 와야 하느냐 말이다. 생각 좀 해 봐라, 내 말하지 않느냐, 그가 일부러 올라왔고, 올워디도 마찬가지란 말이다.\"——\"오라버니,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"블리필 군이 제 조카에게 보내는 게 마땅하다고 여기는 전갈이라면 그게 무엇이든 그 애에게 전해질 거예요. 또 그 애가 합당한 답을 하는 데 무슨 지시 따위는 필요 없으리라 생각합니다. 적당한 때가 되면 그 애가 블리필 군 만나기를 거절하지 않으리라고 저는 확신해요.\"——\"거절하지 않는다고, 흥!\" 향사가 대답했다.——\"맙소사!——우리가 모를 줄 아느냐——나야 아무 말 안 하지만, 어떤 사람들은 온 세상 사람보다 더 똑똑하단 말이지.——내 뜻대로 할 수 있었다면 그 애가 진작에 도망치지도 않았을 게다. 그런데 이제는 그 애가 또 달아났다는 소리를 시시각각 듣게 되리라 싶구나. 어떤 사람들이 나를 천하의 바보로 여기지만, 나도 저 애가 누굴 끔찍이 싫어하는지는——\" \"그만하세요, 오라버니,\" 웨스턴 부인이 받아쳤다. \"제 조카가 헐뜯기는 소리는 듣지 않겠어요. 그건 우리 집안에 대한 모욕이에요. 그 애는 집안의 자랑이고, 앞으로도 그러할 거예요, 제가 장담합니다. 그 애의 처신에 제 세상 명예를 통째로 걸겠어요.——오후에 다시 뵙는 건 반갑겠어요, 오라버니. 긴히 말씀드릴 일이 좀 있으니까요.——지금은, 블리필 군도 그렇고 오라버니도, 양해해 주셔야겠어요. 옷매무새를 서둘러 갖춰야 해서요.\" \"그래, 그건 그렇고,\" 향사가 말했다. \"시간을 좀 정하란 말이다.\" \"정말이지,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"시간은 못 정해요. 오후에 뵙겠다고 말씀드렸잖아요.\"——\"대체 날더러 어쩌란 말이냐?\" 향사가 블리필 쪽으로 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"비글이 늙은 산토끼를 돌려세우지 못하듯, 나도 저 애를 도무지 돌려세울 수가 없네. 오후가 되면 어쩌면 기분이 좀 나아지겠지.\"——\"제가 보아하니, 선생, 저는 불운을 타고난 모양입니다,\" 블리필이 대답했다. \"하지만 선생께 진 신세만큼은 언제까지나 잊지 않겠습니다.\" 그러고 나서 그는 격식을 갖춰 웨스턴 부인에게 작별을 고했고, 부인 역시 자기 쪽에서 못지않게 격식을 차렸다. 그리하여 두 사람은 떠났는데, 향사는 오후에는 블리필이 제 딸을 보고야 말리라고 욕설을 섞어 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n웨스턴 씨가 이 만남을 별로 달가워하지 않았다면, 블리필은 더더욱 그러했다. 전자로 말하자면, 그는 누이의 그 모든 행동을 한낱 그녀의 변덕 탓으로, 그리고 방문의 예법이 빠진 것에 대한 불만 탓으로만 돌렸다. 그러나 블리필은 사정을 조금 더 깊이 꿰뚫어 보았다. 그는 부인의 입에서 흘러나온 두세 마디 말에서 무언가 더 중대한 낌새를 의심했다. 그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 그의 의심은 옳았으니, 이는 다음 장에 담길 여러 사정을 내가 펼쳐 보일 때 드러날 것이다.\n\n소피아를 파멸시키려는 벨라스턴 부인의 계략.\n\n펠라마 경의 마음속에 사랑은 너무 깊이 뿌리내려, 웨스턴 씨의 거친 손으로는 도저히 뽑아낼 수 없을 지경이었다. 분노가 끓어오르던 와중에 그는 실로 에글레인 대위에게 어떤 임무를 맡겼는데, 대위는 그 임무를 실행하면서 한참을 지나치게 넘어서고 말았다. 게다가 경께서 벨라스턴 부인을 만난 뒤—그것은 모욕을 당한 다음 날 오후의 일이었다—대위를 찾을 수만 있었더라면, 그 임무는 아예 실행되지조차 않았을 것이다. 그러나 대위는 자기 임무를 수행하는 데 어찌나 부지런했던지, 오랜 수소문 끝에 그날 저녁 매우 늦게야 향사의 숙소를 알아내고는, 아침에 향사를 놓치지 않으려고 어느 선술집에서 밤을 꼬박 새웠고, 그 바람에 경께서 자기 숙소로 보내신 명령 철회를 받아 보지 못했던 것이다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이, 소피아를 겁탈하려던 그 계획이 있었던 다음 날 오후, 경께서는 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아갔다. 부인은 향사의 성품을 어찌나 낱낱이 펼쳐 보였던지, 경은 그의 말 한마디에 노여움을 품었던 것이 얼마나 어처구니없는 짓이었는지를 분명히 깨달았다. 더구나 그 딸에게 그토록 떳떳한 뜻을 품고 있는 처지였으니 말이다. 그는 자신의 격렬한 연정을 벨라스턴 부인에게 털어놓았고, 부인은 선뜻 그 일을 떠맡았다. 그러고는 그에게, 집안의 모든 어른은 물론이요 술이 깨고 자기 딸에게 들어온 청혼이 어떤 것인지를 알게 되면 아버지 본인에게서도 더없이 호의적인 환대를 받으리라는 확실한 보장으로 그를 북돋아 주었다. 다만 한 가지 위험은, 전에 언급한 적 있는 그 사내에게 있다고 부인은 말했다. 거지에다 떠돌이 신세이면서도, 무슨 수를 썼는지 알 수 없으나 어쨌든 그럴듯한 옷을 마련해 신사 행세를 하고 다니는 자였다. \"그래서요,\" 부인이 말했다. \"제 사촌을 생각해서 이 사내의 뒤를 캐는 일을 떠맡았더니, 다행히 그자의 숙소까지 알아냈답니다.\" 그러고는 그 위치를 경께 일러 주었다. \"제 생각엔 말이죠, 경이시여,\" 부인이 덧붙였다. \"(이런 사내는 경께서 몸소 노여워하시기엔 너무 천한 자이니) 경께서 무슨 수를 짜내어 그자를 강제 징집해 배에 태워 보낼 수는 없을까요. 이 계획은 법으로도 양심으로도 막을 것이 없습니다. 단언컨대 그 사내는 아무리 잘 차려입었다 한들 떠돌이에 지나지 않으니, 거리의 어느 사내 못지않게 강제로 군역에 끌려갈 만한 자이지요. 양심에 관한 문제로 말하자면, 한 젊은 숙녀를 그런 파멸에서 지켜 내는 일이야말로 더없이 갸륵한 행위가 아니겠습니까. 아니, 그 사내 자신을 두고 보더라도, 제 사촌과 일이 잘 풀리지만 않는다면(하늘이 막아 주시기를) 이는 어쩌면 그자를 교수대에서 구해 주는 길이 될 수도 있고, 어쩌면 정직한 방식으로 그의 앞날을 열어 주는 수도 될 것입니다.\"\n\n펠라마 경은 부인이 이 일에 기꺼이 나서 준 것에 대해 진심으로 감사를 표했다. 그 성패에 자신의 앞날 행복이 송두리째 달려 있었기 때문이다. 그는 지금으로서는 강제 징집 계획에 아무런 이의도 없으며, 그것을 실행에 옮기는 일을 고려해 보겠다고 말했다. 그러고는 부인에게, 부디 곧바로 자기 청혼을 그 집안에 전해 주는 영예를 베풀어 달라고 더없이 간절히 청했다. 그는 그 집안에 백지 위임을 내놓겠으며, 그들이 요구하는 거의 어떤 방식으로든 자기 재산을 정해 주겠노라고 말했다. 그리고 소피아에 관해 온갖 황홀과 환희의 말을 쏟아 낸 뒤, 작별을 고하고 떠났다. 다만 떠나기 전에, 존스를 조심하고 한시도 지체 말고 그자의 신병을 확보하여, 다시는 그 젊은 숙녀를 파멸로 몰아넣을 어떤 시도도 할 수 없게 만들라는 더없이 단단한 당부를 받고서였다.\n\n웨스턴 부인이 숙소에 당도하자마자, 벨라스턴 부인에게 안부를 전하는 명함이 발송되었다. 그것을 받자마자 벨라스턴 부인은 연인의 조바심으로 사촌에게 한달음에 날아갔으니, 바라던 것 이상으로 찾아든 이 좋은 기회를 반긴 것이었다. 이 청혼을, 그가 호텐토트라는 별칭으로 영예롭게 불러 주던 신사에게 들이미는 것보다는, 세상 물정에 밝고 분별 있는 여인에게 들이미는 편이 훨씬 더 마음에 들었기 때문이다. 물론 그 신사에게서야 거절당할 염려는 전혀 하지 않았지만 말이다.\n\n두 부인이 만나, 짤막한 인사치레를 마치자 곧장 본론으로 들어갔으니, 그 본론은 시작되기가 무섭게 마무리되었다 해도 좋을 만했다. 웨스턴 부인은 펠라마 경의 이름을 듣자마자 두 뺨이 기쁨으로 달아올랐고, 그의 연정이 얼마나 간절한지, 그 청혼이 얼마나 진지한지, 그 제안이 얼마나 후한지를 듣고 나서는 더없이 또렷한 말로 흡족함을 가득 드러냈다.\n\n이야기가 흘러가는 가운데 화제는 존스에게로 돌아갔고, 두 사촌은 소피아가 그 젊은이에게 품은 불운한 애정을—둘 다 그렇다고 의견을 모았다—더없이 애처롭게 한탄했다. 웨스턴 부인은 그 일을 전적으로 오라비의 어리석은 처사 탓으로 돌렸다. 그러나 끝내는, 조카딸의 좋은 분별력에 대한 믿음을 밝히는 것으로 말을 맺었다. 그 아이가 블리필을 위해 자기 애정을 내놓지는 않으려 할지언정, \"내 장담하건대,\" 부인이 말했다. \"작위와 큰 영지를 함께 안겨 주는 훌륭한 신사의 구애 앞에서는 머지않아 그 한낱 끌림 따위는 희생하도록 설득될 거예요. 사실,\" 부인이 덧붙였다. \"소피에게 공정하게 말하자면, 이 블리필이라는 자가 보기 흉한 부류의 사내라는 점은 인정해야겠어요. 벨라스턴, 당신도 알다시피 시골 신사들이란 다 그렇잖아요. 게다가 그자는 재산 말고는 내세울 게 아무것도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"아니,\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 내 사촌이 그러는 것도 그리 놀랍지 않네요. 단언컨대 이 존스라는 자는 아주 호감 가는 사내이고, 남자들 말로는 우리 여자들에게 큰 호평거리가 된다는 한 가지 미덕도 갖추었거든요. 어떻게 생각하세요, 웨스턴 부인—분명 당신을 웃게 만들 거예요. 아니, 나조차 웃음을 참느라 말을 제대로 못 할 지경인데—그 사내가 뻔뻔스럽게도 내게 사랑을 고백했다면 믿으시겠어요? 혹시 못 믿겠다 싶으면, 여기 충분한 증거가 있답니다. 그자가 손수 쓴 글씨예요, 정말이라니까요.\" 그러고는 청혼이 담긴 그 편지를 사촌에게 건넸으니, 독자께서 그것을 보고 싶으시다면 이 이야기의 제15권에 이미 기록되어 있음을 확인하실 수 있을 것이다.\n\n\"맙소사, 정말 놀랍군요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"이거야말로 뻔뻔함의 걸작이로군요. 괜찮으시다면 이 편지를 어쩌면 좀 써먹을 수 있겠어요.\" \"얼마든지 마음대로,\" 벨라스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"당신 좋으실 대로 어떤 용도에든 쓰세요. 다만 웨스턴 양 말고는 누구에게도 보이지 마시고, 그 아이에게도 꼭 필요한 계기가 생기지 않는 한은 보이지 마세요.\" \"그래, 그 사내는 어떻게 대하셨어요?\" 웨스턴 부인이 되물었다. \"남편으로 대하진 않았지요.\" 부인이 말했다. \"단언컨대 난 재혼하지 않았어요, 이봐요. 벨, 당신도 알다시피 난 이미 한 번 그 위안이라는 걸 겪어 봤잖아요. 분별 있는 여자라면 한 번이면 족하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n벨라스턴 부인은 이 편지가 소피아의 마음속에서 존스에게 불리한 쪽으로 저울추를 확실히 기울게 하리라 여겼다. 그리고 그것을 선뜻 내준 것은, 한편으로는 존스가 곧장 처치되어 길에서 사라지리라는 기대 때문이었고, 다른 한편으로는 어너의 증언을 미리 확보해 둔 덕분이었다. 어너의 의중을 떠본 결과, 그녀가 무엇이든 원하는 대로 증언할 채비가 되어 있다고 짐작할 만한 충분한 근거를 보았던 것이다.\n\n그런데 어쩌면 독자께서는 의아해하실지도 모르겠다. 속으로는 소피아를 미워하는 벨라스턴 부인이 어째서 그 젊은 숙녀에게 그토록 이로운 혼사를 그리도 열심히 밀어붙이려 했을까 하고 말이다. 자, 나는 그런 독자께 인간 본성이라는 책을 거의 맨 마지막 쪽까지 찬찬히 들여다보시기를 권하고 싶다. 그러면 거기, 알아보기도 힘든 글씨로 이렇게 적혀 있음을 발견하시리라. 혼사를 두고 어머니와 이모 따위가 그토록 터무니없이 굴기는 해도, 여자들은 실상 자기 사랑의 끌림이 가로막히는 것을 너무도 큰 불행으로 여긴 나머지, 그런 좌절에 대해서만큼은 적의를 그 이상으로 끌어올려서는 결코 안 된다고 여긴다는 것을. 또한 그분은 거의 같은 자리에서 이렇게 적힌 것도 발견하시리라. 한 번이라도 어떤 남자를 차지하는 기쁨을 맛본 여자는, 다른 어떤 여자가 똑같은 기쁨을 누리지 못하도록 막기 위해서라면 지옥까지 가는 길의 절반은 넘어서까지 기꺼이 가리라는 것을.\n\n만일 그분이 이런 이유들로 만족하지 못하신다면, 솔직히 고백하건대 나로서는 그 부인의 행동에 다른 어떤 동기도 떠오르지 않는다. 펠라마 경에게 매수당했다고 여기지 않는 한에는 말이다. 하지만 나로서는 그것을 의심할 까닭이 도무지 보이지 않는다.\n\n바로 이것이 웨스턴 부인이 소피아에게 꺼내려고 준비하던 일이었다. 사랑의 어리석음과, 돈을 위해 법의 테두리 안에서 몸을 파는 일의 지혜로움에 관한 몇 마디 서두를 깔아 가며 말이다. 그런데 바로 그때 오라비와 블리필이 불쑥 들이닥쳤다. 그리하여 블리필을 대하는 부인의 그 모든 냉랭함이 생겨난 것이니, 향사는 평소 버릇대로 그것을 엉뚱한 원인 탓으로 돌렸으나, 블리필 본인은(그가 훨씬 더 약삭빠른 사람이었기에) 그 진짜 속내를 의심하게 되었던 것이다.\n\n존스가 피츠패트릭 부인을 방문하는 이야기.\n\n이제 독자께서는 우리와 함께 존스 씨에게로 돌아가기를 바라실지도 모르겠다. 그는 약속한 시각에 피츠패트릭 부인을 찾아갔다. 그러나 거기서 오간 대화를 전하기에 앞서, 우리가 늘 따라온 방식대로 잠시 뒤로 돌아가, 이 부인의 행동에 이토록 큰 변화가 생긴 까닭을 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠다. 처음에는 주로 존스 씨를 피하려고 거처를 옮겼던 그가, 이제는 앞서 본 대로 부지런히 이 만남을 청해 온 것이니 말이다.\n\n여기서는 다만 그 전날 일어난 일만 떠올리면 충분하다. 벨라스턴 부인에게서 웨스턴 씨가 도시에 도착했다는 소식을 들은 그는, 피카딜리에 있는 그의 거처로 문안을 드리러 갔다. 그러나 거기서 그는 차마 다시 옮기기 민망할 만큼 거칠고 천한 호칭을 잔뜩 들었고, 심지어 문밖으로 차내겠다는 위협까지 받았다. 그곳에서, 자기와 잘 아는 사이인 고모 웨스턴 댁의 늙은 하인이 그를 그 부인의 거처로 데려다주었다. 그러나 그 부인은 그를 더 친절하게 대한 것이 아니라 단지 더 정중하게 대했을 뿐이었다. 아니, 사실대로 말하자면 다른 방식으로 무례하게 대했다고 해야겠다. 요컨대 그는 양쪽 모두에게서 돌아오면서, 자신의 화해 계획이 수포로 돌아갔을 뿐 아니라 어떤 수단으로든 그것을 이루려는 생각은 영영 단념해야 한다는 것을 분명히 깨달았다. 이 순간부터 복수의 욕망만이 그의 마음을 가득 채웠다. 그리고 이런 심정으로 극장에서 존스를 마주쳤을 때, 그에게는 이 목적을 이룰 기회가 눈앞에 찾아온 것처럼 보였다.\n\n독자께서는 기억하실 것이다. 피츠패트릭 부인이 자기 사연을 이야기하며, 예전에 웨스턴 부인이 배스에서 피츠패트릭 씨에게 보였던 호감을 존스에게 들려준 것을 말이다. 그리고 그 호감이 어그러진 데서, 고모가 자신을 향해 드러낸 그 깊은 원한이 비롯되었음도 함께였다. 그러므로 그는, 그 인자한 부인이 예전에 다른 이의 구애에 그토록 쉽게 귀를 기울였던 것처럼, 존스 씨의 구애에도 그만큼 쉽게 응하리라고 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 매력의 우위로 말하자면 분명 존스 씨 쪽에 있었고, 게다가 그 사이 고모의 나이가 더 들었다는 사실 또한 (얼마나 정당한 판단인지는 말하지 않겠다) 자신의 계획에 불리하기보다 오히려 유리한 근거라고 그는 결론지었다.\n\n그리하여 존스가 찾아왔을 때, 그는 먼저 그를 돕고 싶다는 뜻을 밝혔는데, 그렇게 함으로써 소피아에게 얼마나 큰 도움이 될지 굳게 확신한다는 것이 그 이유였다. 그러고는 지난번 약속을 어긴 일에 대해 몇 마디 변명을 한 뒤, 존스 씨가 모르고 있으리라 여겨, 그의 연인이 누구의 보호 아래 있는지를 일러 주었다. 그런 다음 그는 자신의 계획을 아주 분명하게 그에게 털어놓으며, 어린 쪽에게 쉽게 다가가기 위해 늙은 부인에게 짐짓 구애하는 척하라고 권했다. 동시에 예전에 피츠패트릭 씨가 바로 이 똑같은 책략 덕분에 성공을 거두었던 일도 함께 알려 주었다.\n\n존스 씨는 부인이 자기를 향해 드러내고, 또 이 제안으로 실제로 입증해 보인 그 친절한 뜻에 깊은 감사를 표했다. 그러나 그는, 피츠패트릭 씨의 경우와는 달리 그 부인이 자신의 조카를 향한 자기 사랑을 이미 알고 있으니 성공이 어렵지 않겠느냐는 우려를 넌지시 비치면서, 웨스턴 양은 결코 이런 속임수에 응하지 않을 것 같다고 말했다. 모든 거짓을 철저히 혐오하는 마음에서도 그러하거니와, 고모를 향해 스스로 떳떳이 밝히는 효심 때문에도 그러하리라는 것이었다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인은 이 말에 조금 발끈했다. 사실 이것은, 말이 헛나간 것이라 부를 수 없다면, 존스로서는 예법에서 살짝 벗어난 실수였다. 그리고 소피아를 칭찬하는 데서 느낀 기쁨이 그를 온갖 분별 밖으로 떠밀지만 않았던들, 그가 좀처럼 저지르지 않았을 실수이기도 했다. 한쪽 사촌을 칭찬하는 이 말이, 다른 쪽 사촌에 대한 은근한 책망 이상이 되어 버렸으니 말이다.\n\n\"정말이지, 선생님,\" 부인이 약간 열을 올리며 대답했다. \"사랑한다고 떠벌리며 늙은 여자를 속이는 것보다 더 쉬운 일은 없다고 생각해요. 그 여자의 기질이 음탕하기만 하다면요. 그분이 제 고모이긴 하지만, 그분만큼 색을 밝히는 분도 없었다고 말씀드려야겠네요. 조카가 블리필과 약혼했기 때문에 조카를 차지하리란 절망이 당신 마음을 그분에게로 돌려놓았다고 둘러댈 수도 있잖아요? 제 사촌 소피아로 말하자면, 그 애가 이런 일에 조금이라도 거리낄 만큼, 또 이런 노파를 벌하는 데서 무슨 해악이라도 느낄 만큼 그렇게 어리석은 애라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없어요. 이런 노파들은 희비극적인 정념으로 집안에 온갖 풍파를 불러오는데, 그게 법으로 처벌되지 않는 게 안타까울 따름이지요. 저 자신은 그런 거리낌이 전혀 없었어요. 그래도 제 사촌 소피아가, 제가 이렇게 말한다고 해서 모욕으로 여기지 않기를 바라요. 그 애가 온갖 종류의 진짜 거짓을 혐오한다 해도, 사촌인 이 피츠패트릭보다 더 혐오하지는 못하리라는 거요. 고모에게는, 솔직히 저는 아무런 효심도 가장하지 않아요. 그분이 그럴 자격도 없고요. 어쨌든 선생님, 저는 충고를 드렸어요. 그걸 따르지 않으시겠다면, 저는 당신의 분별력을 그만큼 낮춰 볼 따름이에요. 그뿐이지요.\"\n\n이제 존스는 자기가 저지른 실수를 똑똑히 깨닫고, 그것을 바로잡으려 온 힘을 다했다. 그러나 그는 그저 더듬거리고 말을 흐리다가 헛소리와 모순 속으로 빠져들 뿐이었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 첫 실수를 바로잡으려 애쓰기보다 그 결과를 그냥 감내하는 편이 더 안전할 때가 많다. 그렇게 애쓰다 보면 대개 빠져나오기는커녕 더 깊이 가라앉게 마련이니 말이다. 또한 이런 경우에, 피츠패트릭 부인이 존스에게 보여 준 만큼의 너그러움을 지닌 이도 드물 것이다. 그는 미소 지으며 이렇게 말했다. \"더는 변명하실 것 없어요. 저는 진정한 연인이라면, 연인을 향한 애정에서 비롯된 일이라면 무엇이든 쉽게 용서할 수 있으니까요.\"\n\n그러고는 다시 자신의 제안을 꺼내 아주 열렬하게 권했다. 이 주제에 관해 자신의 머리가 떠올릴 수 있는 어떤 논거도 빠뜨리지 않았다. 그는 고모에게 어찌나 격렬하게 분이 치밀어 있던지, 그 고모의 약점을 들춰내는 일만큼 그에게 똑같은 기쁨을 줄 수 있는 것은 거의 없었다. 그리고 천생 여자답게, 그는 자기가 아끼는 계획을 실행하는 데 어떤 어려움도 보려 하지 않았다.\n\n그러나 존스는 그 일을 맡기를 한사코 거절했다. 사실 그 일은 성공할 가망이 조금도 없었다. 그는 피츠패트릭 부인이 자기 충고를 이토록 다급하게 들이미는 동기를 쉽게 알아챘다. 그는 소피아를 향한 자신의 다정하고 열렬한 마음을 부인하지는 않겠다고 말했다. 그러나 두 사람의 처지가 너무도 차이 난다는 것을 잘 알기에, 그토록 거룩한 아가씨가 이토록 보잘것없는 사내를 마음에 두어 줄 거라는 헛된 기대는 결코 품을 수 없다고 했다. 아니, 그는 그가 그러기를 바라는 마음조차 좀처럼 일으킬 수 없다고 단언했다. 그는 너그러운 심정을 토로하며 말을 맺었는데, 그 내용을 여기 옮길 겨를은 지금 우리에게 없다.\n\n세상에는 어떤 아름다운 여자들이 있는데(여기서 너무 일반화해 말할 엄두는 내지 못하겠다), 자아가 어찌나 압도적인지 어떤 화제에서도 그것을 떼어 놓지 못한다. 그들에게는 허영이 으뜸가는 원리여서, 마주치는 칭찬이라면 무엇이든 움켜쥐려 들고, 그것이 남의 것일지라도 제 것으로 가져다 쓴다. 이런 부인들과 함께 있을 때는, 다른 여자를 두고 무슨 멋진 말을 하든 그들이 그것을 자기 자신에게 갖다 붙이지 않을 수가 없다. 아니, 그들은 흔히 자기가 낚아챈 칭찬을 한술 더 떠 부풀린다. 이를테면, 저 여자의 아름다움과 재치, 우아함과 좋은 성품이 그만한 칭찬을 받을 만하다면, 그런 자질들을 훨씬 더 빼어나게 지닌 나는 대체 무엇을 받아 마땅하단 말인가, 하는 식으로.\n\n이런 부인들에게는, 사내가 다른 여자를 칭찬하는 동안에 도리어 자기 자신을 천거하게 되는 일이 흔하다. 그가 자기 연인을 향한 뜨거움과 너그러운 마음을 토로하는 동안, 그들은 이런 생각을 한다. 더 못한 정도의 가치를 지닌 여자에게도 이토록 다정할 수 있는 이 사내라면, 우리에게는 얼마나 매력적인 연인이 되어 줄까 하고. 이상하게 들릴지 모르나, 나는 이런 사례를 피츠패트릭 부인 말고도 여럿 보아 왔다. 그리고 이 모든 일이 실제로 일어난 피츠패트릭 부인은, 이제 존스 씨에게 어떤 감정을 느끼기 시작했는데, 그 징후를 그는 예전의 가엾은 소피아보다 훨씬 빨리 알아차렸다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 남녀 양쪽 모두에게 완벽한 아름다움이란 흔히들 여기는 것보다 훨씬 거역하기 어려운 대상이다. 우리 가운데 더러는 더 수수한 운명에 만족하며, (어린아이가 아무 뜻도 모르는 말을 따라 외우듯이) 겉모습을 멸시하고 더 단단한 매력을 더 높이 치라고 입에 익도록 배우지만, 그럼에도 나는 늘 보아 왔다. 더없이 빼어난 아름다움이 다가올 때면, 그 더 단단한 매력이란 것은 해가 뜬 뒤의 별들이 내는 그런 빛으로밖에 빛나지 못한다는 것을.\n\n존스가 그 감격에 찬 말들을 끝마쳤을 때(그 가운데 상당수는 오론다테스의 입에서 나왔어도 어울렸을 만한 것이었다), 피츠패트릭 부인은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러고는 한동안 존스에게 못 박혀 있던 눈길을 거두어 바닥으로 떨구며 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 존스 씨, 저는 당신이 가엾어요. 하지만 그런 다정함을 그것을 알아주지도 못하는 이들에게 헛되이 던져 버리는 것이야말로 그 다정함의 저주랍니다. 저는 당신보다 제 사촌을 더 잘 알아요, 존스 씨. 그래서 말씀드리는데, 이런 정념과 이런 사람에게 아무런 보답도 하지 않는 여자라면, 그 둘 어느 쪽에도 어울리지 않는 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"설마, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"그 말씀은 설마……\" \"설마라뇨!\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 외쳤다. \"저도 제가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 모르겠어요. 진정한 다정함에는 무언가 사람을 홀리는 게 있는 것 같아요. 사내에게서 그런 걸 만나는 여자는 드물고, 만난다 해도 그 값을 알아볼 줄 아는 여자는 더더욱 드물지요. 저는 이토록 진실로 고귀한 마음을 들어 본 적이 없어요. 어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만, 당신은 사람을 믿지 않을 수 없게 만드는군요. 이런 가치를 알아보지 못하는 여자라면, 분명 세상에서 가장 경멸할 만한 여자임에 틀림없어요.\"\n\n이 모든 말을 내뱉는 태도와 눈빛은 존스에게 어떤 의심을 불어넣었는데, 우리는 그것을 독자께 곧이곧대로 말씀드리고 싶지는 않다. 그는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않은 채 이렇게 말했다. \"부인, 제가 너무 지루하게 머물렀나 봅니다.\" 그러고는 작별을 고하려 했다.\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아요, 선생님.\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 대답했다. \"정말이지 당신이 가엾어요, 존스 씨. 정말이에요. 하지만 가시려거든, 제가 말씀드린 계획을 한번 곰곰이 생각해 보세요. 분명 마음에 드실 거예요. 그리고 되도록 빨리 다시 뵙도록 해요. 원하신다면 내일 아침에요, 아니면 적어도 내일 어느 때라도요. 저는 하루 종일 집에 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n그리하여 존스는 여러 차례 감사의 말을 한 뒤, 아주 공손하게 물러났다. 헤어질 때 피츠패트릭 부인은 그에게 눈길 하나를 선물하지 않을 수 없었는데, 만일 그가 그 눈길에서 아무것도 알아채지 못했다면, 그는 눈빛이라는 언어를 도무지 모르는 사람이었을 것이다. 사실 그 눈길은 다시는 그에게 돌아가지 않으리라는 그의 결심을 굳혀 주었다. 이 이야기에서 지금껏 허물 많은 사람으로 보였을지언정, 이제 그의 모든 생각은 오직 자신의 소피아에게만 매여 있어서, 세상 어떤 여자도 이제 그를 변심의 행위로 끌어들일 수는 없으리라고 나는 믿는다.\n\n그러나 그의 편이 아니었던 운명의 여신은, 그가 두 번째 기회를 주지 않으려 마음먹은 것을 알고는, 이 기회를 한껏 살려 보기로 작정했다. 그리하여 우리가 이제 슬픈 가락으로 적어 내려가야 할 그 비극적인 사건을 빚어내고 말았다.\n\n앞선 방문이 불러온 결과.\n\n피츠패트릭 씨는 앞서 말한 웨스턴 부인의 편지를 받고, 그 덕분에 아내가 몸을 피해 있는 장소를 알게 되자 곧장 배스로 돌아갔으며, 그 이튿날 거기서 런던으로 출발했다.\n\n독자는 이 신사의 질투심 많은 성미에 대해 이미 여러 차례 들어 알고 있을 것이다. 또한 그가 업튼에서 워터스 부인과 한 방에 있는 존스를 발견했을 때 품었던 의심도 기억해 주리라. 그 의심을 말끔히 풀어 줄 충분한 이유가 나중에 드러났음에도, 지금 아내가 쓴 편지에서 존스 씨에 대한 그토록 그럴듯한 평판을 읽고 나니, 그는 그녀 역시 같은 시간에 그 여관에 있었음을 떠올리게 되었고, 본래 그리 맑지도 못한 머릿속에서 온갖 정황을 뒤죽박죽 뒤섞어 버린 끝에, 그 전체가 셰익스피어가 비극 「오셀로」에서 언급한 그 초록 눈의 괴물을 빚어내고 말았다.\n\n그리고 지금, 그가 거리에서 아내의 거처를 물어 막 그 집 문까지 안내를 받은 참인데, 공교롭게도 존스 씨가 바로 그 문에서 나오고 있었다.\n\n피츠패트릭은 아직 존스의 얼굴을 기억해 내지 못했다. 그러나 잘 차려입은 젊은 사내가 자기 아내의 집에서 나오는 것을 보자 곧장 그에게로 다가가, 저 집에서 무슨 일을 하고 왔느냐고 물었다. \"분명히,\" 그가 말했다, \"당신은 저 안에 있었던 게 틀림없소. 거기서 나오는 걸 내가 봤으니 말이오.\"\n\n존스는 아주 겸손하게 대답했다. \"거기 있는 어느 부인을 찾아뵙고 오는 길입니다.\" 이에 피츠패트릭이 받아쳤다. \"그 부인과 당신이 무슨 볼일이 있단 말이오?\" 그러자 이제는 그 신사의 목소리와 이목구비, 실은 외투까지도 또렷이 기억해 낸 존스가 외쳤다. \"아니, 이 사람아! 손 좀 줘 보게. 그토록 오래전에 일어난 사소한 오해를 두고 우리 사이에 아직 나쁜 감정이 남아 있지는 않기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 선생,\" 피츠패트릭이 말했다, \"나는 당신 이름도 얼굴도 모르오.\" \"참으로, 선생,\" 존스가 말했다, \"저 역시 선생의 성함을 알게 되는 기쁨은 누리지 못했습니다만, 얼굴만큼은 전에 업튼에서 뵌 적이 있는 걸로 잘 기억하고 있습니다. 거기서 우리 사이에 어리석은 다툼이 있었지요. 만약 그 일이 아직 풀리지 않았다면, 이제 한잔 기울이며 풀어 봅시다.\"\n\n\"업튼에서라고!\" 상대가 외쳤다. \"하! 맹세코, 당신 이름이 존스 아니오?\" \"그렇습니다,\" 그가 대답했다, \"맞습니다.\" \"오! 맹세코,\" 피츠패트릭이 외쳤다, \"당신이야말로 내가 만나고 싶던 바로 그 작자로군. 맹세코 곧 당신과 한 병 비우겠소. 하지만 먼저 당신 정수리에 한 방 크게 먹여 주지. 자, 받아라, 이 악당아. 맹세코 당신이 그 일격에 대해 내게 명예를 회복시켜 주지 않으면, 한 방 더 먹여 줄 테다.\" 그러더니 그는 칼을 뽑아 방어 자세를 취했는데, 그것이 그가 아는 유일한 기술이었다.\n\n존스는 다소 뜻밖에 날아든 그 일격에 잠시 비틀거렸으나, 곧 정신을 차리고 자기도 칼을 뽑았다. 비록 검술이라곤 아무것도 몰랐지만, 어찌나 대담하게 피츠패트릭에게 밀어붙였던지 그의 방어를 뚫고 칼날의 절반을 그 신사의 몸에 박아 넣고 말았다. 그 신사는 칼을 받자마자 뒤로 한 걸음 물러나 칼끝을 떨어뜨리고는, 그 위에 몸을 기대며 외쳤다. \"이만하면 충분히 만족하오. 나는 죽은 목숨이오.\"\n\n\"그러지 않기를 바랍니다,\" 존스가 외쳤다, \"그러나 결과가 어찌 되든, 당신 스스로 자초한 일임은 분명히 아셔야 합니다.\" 바로 그 순간 한 무리의 사내들이 들이닥쳐 존스를 붙잡았고, 존스는 저항하지 않겠노라 말하며, 적어도 그들 중 몇은 부상당한 신사를 보살펴 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"아무렴,\" 사내들 중 하나가 외쳤다, \"부상당한 신사는 충분히 보살핌을 받을 거요. 살 시간이 몇 시간도 안 남은 것 같으니 말이오. 당신으로 말하자면, 선생, 적어도 한 달은 멀쩡히 더 살겠지.\" \"젠장, 잭,\" 다른 하나가 말했다, \"저 양반 항해는 끝났구먼. 이제 다른 항구로 가게 됐어.\" 이렇게 우리 가엾은 존스는 이 사내들의 온갖 농지거리의 대상이 되었으니, 사실 이들은 펠라마 경에게 고용된 패거리로, 존스를 미행하여 피츠패트릭 부인의 집까지 따라붙었고, 이 불운한 사고가 벌어졌을 때 길모퉁이에서 그를 기다리고 있던 것이었다.\n\n이 패거리를 지휘하던 자는 이제 자신이 할 일이 죄수를 민간 치안관의 손에 넘기는 것임을 매우 현명하게 판단했다. 그리하여 그는 존스를 어느 선술집으로 데려가게 한 뒤, 거기서 순경을 불러 그에게 신병을 인계했다.\n\n순경은 존스 씨가 아주 잘 차려입은 것을 보고, 또 그 사고가 결투에서 벌어진 일이라는 말을 듣고는, 죄수를 매우 정중하게 대했다. 그리고 존스의 청에 따라 부상당한 신사의 소식을 알아보러 사람을 보냈는데, 그 신사는 지금 어느 선술집에서 외과의의 손에 맡겨져 있었다. 돌아온 보고는, 상처가 분명 치명적이며 살아날 가망이 없다는 것이었다. 이에 순경은 존스에게 치안판사 앞으로 가야 한다고 알렸다. 그가 대답했다. \"어디든 좋을 대로 하시오. 내게 무슨 일이 닥치든 나는 개의치 않소. 법의 눈으로 보면 내가 살인죄를 저지른 것이 아님을 확신하지만, 그래도 피의 무게가 내 마음을 견딜 수 없이 짓누르는구려.\"\n\n이제 존스는 치안판사 앞으로 끌려갔고, 거기서 피츠패트릭 씨를 치료한 외과의가 나와 그 상처가 치명적이라고 믿는다고 진술했다. 이에 죄수는 게이트하우스 감옥에 수감되었다. 밤이 매우 깊은 시각이라 존스는 이튿날 아침까지는 파트리지를 부르지 않았다. 그리고 존스는 일곱 시까지 눈을 붙이지 못했으므로, 그토록 오래도록 주인의 소식을 듣지 못해 잔뜩 겁에 질려 있던 가엾은 파트리지가 그 소식을 듣자마자 거의 넋이 나갈 뻔한 전갈을 받은 것은 정오가 다 되어서였다.\n\n그는 무릎을 덜덜 떨고 가슴을 두근거리며 게이트하우스로 갔고, 존스 앞에 다다르자마자 그에게 닥친 불행을 두고 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 한탄했다. 그러면서도 내내 몹시 겁에 질려 사방을 두리번거렸으니, 피츠패트릭 씨가 죽었다는 소식이 이제 막 도착한 터라, 이 가엾은 사내는 그의 유령이 방 안으로 들어올까 봐 시시각각 두려워했던 것이다. 마침내 그는 하마터면 잊을 뻔했던 편지 한 통을 존스에게 건넸는데, 그것은 검둥이 조지의 손을 거쳐 소피아에게서 온 것이었다.\n\n존스는 즉시 모두를 방에서 내보낸 뒤, 다급히 편지를 뜯어 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\n\"제가 당신에게서 다시 소식을 듣게 된 것은, 솔직히 저로서도 놀라운 어떤 우연 덕분입니다. 방금 숙모께서 당신이 벨라스턴 부인에게 보낸 편지를 제게 보여 주셨는데, 거기에는 청혼이 담겨 있더군요. 그것이 당신의 친필임을 저는 확신합니다. 더욱 놀라운 것은, 그 편지의 날짜가 바로 당신이 저를 위해 그토록 애태우고 있다고 제게 믿게 하려던 그 시기로 되어 있다는 점입니다. 이 사실에 대한 해명은 당신께 맡기겠습니다. 제가 바라는 것은 오직 하나, 당신의 이름이 다시는 제 앞에서 거론되지 않는 것뿐입니다.\n\nS. W.\"\n존스 씨의 지금 심정과, 그가 이제 시달리고 있는 고통에 대해서는, 그의 비참함이 어찌나 컸던지 저 스왁컴조차 거의 그를 가엾이 여겼을 정도라고 말하는 것보다 독자에게 더 잘 전할 길이 없다. 그러나 그 처지가 아무리 가혹할지라도, 우리는 지금으로서는 그를 그 속에 그대로 두려 한다. 그의 수호신마저도 (정말 그런 것이 그에게 있었다면) 그리한 듯하니 말이다. 그리고 여기서 우리는 이 이야기의 열여섯 번째 권을 마친다.\n\n사흘간의 이야기.\n서두를 여는 글 한 토막.\n\n희극을 쓰는 작가가 주요 인물들을 더없이 행복하게 만들어 놓았을 때, 혹은 비극을 쓰는 작가가 그들을 인간이 겪을 수 있는 가장 비참한 밑바닥까지 끌고 내려갔을 때, 두 사람은 모두 제 할 일이 끝났으며 작품이 마침표에 이르렀다고 여긴다.\n\n만일 우리가 비극을 쓰는 사람이었다면, 독자는 이제 우리가 그 마침표에 거의 다다랐음을 인정해야 할 것이다. 악마든, 이 땅에 있는 그 어떤 악마의 대리인이든, 가엾은 존스를 위해 지난 장에서 우리가 그를 남겨 둔 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 고통을 꾸며 내기란 어려울 테니 말이다. 소피아로 말하자면, 마음씨 고운 여인이라면 연적에게라도 지금 그녀가 느끼고 있으리라 짐작되는 것 이상의 괴로움을 바라지는 않을 것이다. 그렇다면 이 비극을 완성하기 위해 남은 것이 살인 한두 건과 교훈적인 문장 몇 줄 말고 또 무엇이 있겠는가!\n\n그러나 우리의 사랑하는 인물들을 지금의 고뇌와 곤경에서 끌어내어, 마침내 행복의 기슭에 내려 주는 일은 훨씬 더 힘든 과업으로 보인다. 실로 어찌나 힘든 과업인지, 우리는 그것을 해내겠다고 떠맡지조차 못하겠다. 소피아에 관해서라면, 우리가 끝에 가서 어딘가에서 그녀에게 좋은 남편을 마련해 주리라는 것이 십중팔구일 것이다. 블리필이든, 그분 나리든, 아니면 다른 누군가든 말이다. 그러나 가엾은 존스로 말하면, 그가 제 경솔함 탓에 지금 휘말려 있는 재앙이 어찌나 깊은지, 그런 경솔함 때문에 사람이 세상을 향한 중죄인까지는 되지 않더라도 적어도 스스로를 해치는 자가 되고 만다. 지금 그는 벗 하나 없이 외롭고 적들에게 시달리고 있어서, 우리는 그를 어떤 좋은 결말로 이끄는 일을 거의 단념할 지경이다. 그러니 우리 독자가 사형 집행 구경을 즐기는 분이라면, 한시바삐 타이번으로 가서 맨 앞줄을 차지하는 편이 좋으리라 생각한다.\n\n* 타이번: 당시 런던의 공개 처형장.\n다만 이것만은 진심으로 약속하겠다. 불행히도 우리가 주인공으로 삼고 만 이 망나니에게 우리가 품고 있으리라 짐작되는 어떤 애정에도 불구하고, 우리는 그에게 그 초자연적인 도움의 손길을 조금도 빌려주지 않을 것이다. 그런 힘은 오직 매우 중대한 경우에만 쓰겠다는 조건 아래 우리에게 맡겨진 것이기 때문이다. 그러므로 그가 자신의 모든 곤경에서 정정당당하게 벗어날 어떤 자연스러운 방도를 찾아내지 못한다면, 우리는 그를 위해 역사의 진실과 품위를 함부로 해치지 않을 것이다. 우리로서는 우리의 진실성을 저버리거나 독자의 믿음에 충격을 주느니, 차라리 그가 타이번에서 교수형을 당했다고 적는 편이 낫겠다. 그리고 그렇게 될 공산이 다분하기도 하다.\n\n이 점에서는 옛사람들이 요즘 사람들보다 큰 이점을 누렸다. 그 시절 일반 백성들에게는 그들의 신화가 오늘날 어떤 종교보다도 더 굳게 믿어졌고, 그 신화 덕분에 작가들은 언제든 좋아하는 주인공을 구해 낼 기회를 가질 수 있었다. 그들의 신들은 작가가 어떤 목적이든 이루어 줄 채비를 갖추고 늘 작가 곁에 대기하고 있었으며, 그 꾸며 낸 이야기가 기상천외할수록 잘 속아 넘어가는 독자의 놀라움과 즐거움도 그만큼 컸다. 그런 작가들은 가엾은 처지에 매여 있는 오늘날의 작가가 주인공을 감옥에서 빼내는 것보다 훨씬 손쉽게, 주인공을 한 나라에서 다른 나라로, 아니 한 세계에서 다른 세계로 데려갔다가 다시 돌아오게 할 수 있었다.\n\n아랍인과 페르시아인들도 그들의 이야기를 쓸 때 정령과 요정 덕분에 똑같은 이점을 누렸다. 그들은 코란 그 자체의 권위에 기대어 이 정령과 요정을 신앙의 한 조목으로 믿었던 것이다. 그러나 우리에게는 이런 도움이 하나도 없다. 우리는 오로지 자연스러운 방도에만 매여 있다. 그러니 이 방도로 가엾은 존스를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 한번 시험해 보자. 솔직히 털어놓자면, 무언가가 내 귀에 속삭이기를, 그는 아직 제 운명의 가장 나쁜 대목을 알지 못하며, 지금껏 들은 그 어떤 소식보다 더 충격적인 소식이 아직 펼쳐지지 않은 운명의 책장 속에 그를 기다리고 있다고 한다.\n\n올워디 씨와 밀러 부인이 막 아침 식사를 하려고 자리에 앉았을 때, 그날 아침 아주 일찍 외출했던 블리필이 돌아와 그 자리에 합석했다.\n\n그는 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아 다음과 같이 말문을 열었다. \"맙소사! 사랑하는 외삼촌, 무슨 일이 있었는지 아십니까? 정말이지 이걸 말씀드리기가 두렵습니다. 그런 악당에게 친절을 베푸셨던 일을 떠올리시며 충격받으실까 봐서요.\" \"무슨 일이냐, 얘야?\" 외삼촌이 말했다. \"나도 살면서 한 번 이상은 자격 없는 자에게 친절을 베풀었을 게다. 그러나 자선이란 그 대상의 악덕까지 받아들이는 것은 아니지.\" \"오, 외삼촌!\" 블리필이 대답했다. \"받아들인다는 말씀을 하시는 것도 섭리의 은밀한 인도가 없지 않은가 봅니다. 외삼촌이 받아들이신 그 아들, 그 존스라는 자, 외삼촌이 품에 안아 길러 주신 그 비열한 놈이 이 세상에서 가장 큰 악당 중 하나임이 드러났습니다.\" \"신성한 모든 것을 걸고 말하건대, 그건 거짓이에요.\" 밀러 부인이 외쳤다. \"존스 씨는 악당이 아닙니다. 그는 숨 쉬는 사람 중에 가장 훌륭한 분이에요. 다른 사람이 그분을 악당이라 불렀다면, 저는 이 끓는 물을 죄다 그자 얼굴에 끼얹었을 거예요.\" 올워디 씨는 이런 행동에 몹시 놀란 표정을 지었다. 그러나 부인은 그가 말할 틈도 주지 않고 그를 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"제게 화내지 않으시기를 바랍니다. 세상을 다 준다 해도 어르신의 기분을 상하게 하고 싶지는 않아요. 하지만 정말이지 그분이 그렇게 불리는 것을 차마 들을 수가 없었어요.\" \"솔직히 말씀드리는데, 부인,\" 올워디가 아주 진지하게 말했다. \"잘 알지도 못하는 사내를 그토록 열을 올려 변호하시는 것을 들으니 조금 놀랍군요.\" \"오! 저는 그분을 알아요, 올워디 씨.\" 부인이 말했다. \"정말로 압니다. 그걸 부인한다면 저는 모든 사람 중에 가장 배은망덕한 자가 될 거예요. 오! 그분은 저와 제 어린 가족을 지켜 주셨어요. 우리 모두 살아 있는 동안 그분을 축복할 이유가 있답니다. 그리고 저는 하늘이 그분을 축복하시고 그분을 악의로 미워하는 적들의 마음을 돌이켜 주시기를 기도합니다. 그분에게 그런 적들이 있다는 걸 저는 알고, 느끼고, 또 봅니다.\" \"부인, 더더욱 놀랍군요.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"분명 다른 사람을 말씀하시는 게 틀림없습니다. 제 조카가 말한 그 사람에게 부인이 그런 은혜를 입었을 리가 없어요.\" \"너무도 분명히,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"저는 그분에게 가장 크고 가장 애틋한 은혜를 입었습니다. 그분은 저와 제 식구들을 지켜 주신 분이에요. 믿어 주세요, 어르신, 그분은 어르신께 모함을 당했어요, 지독한 모함을요. 저는 그렇다는 걸 압니다. 그렇지 않고서야, 온통 선함과 명예로 이루어진 분이신 어르신께서, 이 가엾고 의지가지없는 아이를 두고 그토록 다정하고 애틋하게 말씀하시던 것을 제가 여러 번 들었는데, 어떻게 그를 그렇게 멸시하듯 '사내'라 부르셨겠어요. 정말이지, 제 가장 좋은 벗이여, 어르신께서 그분이 어르신에 대해 하던 그 착하고 다정하고 고마워하는 말들을 들으셨다면, 그분은 어르신께 더 다정한 호칭을 받을 자격이 있는 분이에요. 그분은 어르신의 이름을 일종의 흠모 없이는 결코 입에 올리지 않으셨어요. 바로 이 방에서, 저는 그분이 무릎을 꿇고 하늘의 온갖 축복이 어르신의 머리 위에 내리기를 간청하는 모습을 보았답니다. 저기 저 아이를 사랑하는 마음도, 그분이 어르신을 사랑하는 마음만큼은 못 됩니다.\"\n\n\"이제 알겠습니다, 외삼촌.\" 블리필이, 악마가 자기가 가장 아끼는 자에게 새겨 넣는 그런 이죽거리는 비웃음 가운데 하나를 지으며 말했다. \"밀러 부인이 정말로 그를 아는군요. 짐작건대 그가 외삼촌의 험담을 늘어놓은 상대가 부인뿐만은 아니라는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다. 제 평판으로 말하자면, 부인이 흘린 몇 마디 말로 보아 그가 그것을 아주 거리낌 없이 다룬 모양입니다만, 저는 그를 용서합니다.\" \"그리고 주님께서 당신을 용서하시기를, 도련님!\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"우리 모두 그분의 용서가 필요할 만큼 죄가 많으니까요.\"\n\n\"솔직히 말하건대, 밀러 부인,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"제 조카에 대한 당신의 이런 태도를 곱게 받아들일 수가 없군요. 그리고 분명히 말씀드리는데, 당신이 그에게 던지는 어떤 비난도 오직 그 가장 사악한 인간에게서 나온 것일 수밖에 없으니, 그런 비난은 그저, 만일 그게 가능하다면, 그에 대한 제 분노를 더 키우는 데나 쓰일 따름입니다. 말씀드려야겠는데, 밀러 부인, 지금 당신 앞에 서 있는 이 젊은이야말로 당신이 편드는 그 배은망덕한 놈을 위해 늘 가장 열렬히 변호해 온 사람입니다. 이걸 제 입에서 직접 들으시면, 당신은 그토록 비열하고 배은망덕한 짓에 놀라시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"속으셨어요, 어르신.\" 밀러 부인이 대답했다. \"이게 제 입에서 나오는 마지막 말이 된다 해도, 저는 어르신이 속으셨다고 말하겠어요. 그리고 다시 한 번 말씀드립니다. 어르신을 속인 자들을 주님께서 용서하시기를! 그 젊은이가 허물이 없다고 우기는 게 아닙니다. 하지만 그것들은 모두 혈기와 젊음에서 나온 허물이에요. 그분이 버릴 수 있는, 아니, 제가 확신하건대 반드시 버리게 될 허물입니다. 설령 그러지 못한다 해도, 그 허물들은 사람이 복으로 받은 것 중 가장 인정 많고 다정하고 정직한 마음 하나에 의해 한참 상쇄되고도 남아요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 밀러 부인,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"이게 당신에 관한 이야기였다면, 저는 믿지 않았을 겁니다.\" \"정말이지, 어르신,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"어르신은 제가 한 모든 말을 믿게 되실 거예요. 분명히 그러시리라 믿습니다. 그리고 제가 들려드릴 이야기를 다 들으시고 나면 (전부 다 말씀드릴 테니까요), 어르신은 기분이 상하기는커녕, 오히려 인정하시게 될 거예요. (저는 어르신의 공정함을 잘 아니까요.) 제가 한 것과 다른 식으로 처신했다면 저야말로 가장 비열하고 가장 배은망덕한 자가 되었을 거라고요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 부인.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"솔직히 변명이 필요하다고 생각되는 그 태도에 대해, 무엇이든 그럴듯한 변명을 듣게 된다면 매우 기쁘겠습니다. 그런데 부인, 부디 제 조카가 끼어듦 없이 이야기를 계속하게 해 주시겠습니까. 그 아이가 사소한 일을 이런 서두로 꺼내지는 않았을 테니까요. 어쩌면 이 이야기가 당신의 오해를 고쳐 줄지도 모르겠군요.\"\n\n밀러 부인이 따르겠다는 뜻을 내비치자, 블리필 씨가 이렇게 말을 시작했다. \"분명히 말씀드리는데, 외삼촌, 밀러 부인의 무례한 처사에 분개하지 않으시겠다면, 저는 저 자신에게만 관계된 일이라면 쉽게 용서하겠습니다. 외삼촌의 선하심이 부인의 손에서 이런 모욕을 받을 만하지는 않다고 생각합니다만.\" \"좋다, 얘야.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그런데 이 새로운 사례라는 게 무엇이냐? 그가 최근에 무슨 짓을 했단 말이냐?\" \"무슨 짓이냐고요.\" 블리필이 외쳤다. \"밀러 부인이 그토록 말씀하셨음에도, 말씀드리기가 참으로 송구스럽습니다. 그리고 온 세상에 도저히 감출 수 없는 일이 아니었다면 제 입에서는 결코 들으실 일이 없었을 겁니다. 한마디로, 그가 사람을 죽였습니다. 살해했다고는 하지 않겠어요. 어쩌면 법적으로는 그렇게 해석되지 않을 수도 있으니까요. 그를 위해서라도 저는 최선의 결과를 바랍니다.\"\n\n올워디는 충격을 받은 표정으로 성호를 그었다. 그러고는 밀러 부인을 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"자, 부인, 이제 무슨 말씀을 하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 저는 이렇게 말하겠어요, 어르신.\" 부인이 대답했다. \"살면서 이보다 더 마음 아픈 일은 없었습니다. 하지만 그게 사실이라면, 저는 그 상대가 누구든 간에 잘못은 그쪽에 있었다고 확신해요. 하늘은 아십니다, 이 도시에는 젊은 신사들을 도발하는 것을 일삼는 악당들이 많다는 걸요. 더없이 큰 도발이 아니고서는 결코 그분을 자극할 수 없었을 거예요. 제 집에 묵었던 모든 신사 중에서, 저는 그분만큼 점잖고 다정한 성품을 가진 분을 본 적이 없으니까요. 그분은 집안의 모든 사람에게, 그리고 그 근처에 오는 모든 사람에게 사랑받았답니다.\"\n\n부인이 이렇게 거침없이 이야기를 이어 가는 동안, 문을 세차게 두드리는 소리가 그들의 대화를 가로막았고, 부인이 더 말을 잇거나 어떤 답을 듣는 것도 막아 버렸다. 부인은 이것이 올워디 씨를 찾아온 손님이리라 짐작하고는, 어린 딸을 데리고 황급히 물러났다. 그 아이의 두 눈은 존스에 관해 들은 서글픈 소식에 온통 눈물로 범벅이 되어 있었다. 존스는 그 아이를 자기의 어린 아내라 부르곤 했고, 여러 장난감을 주었을 뿐 아니라 몇 시간씩이나 손수 그 아이와 놀아 주던 사람이었다.\n\n어떤 독자들은 어쩌면 이런 자질구레한 정황을 즐거워할지도 모르겠다. 이런 것을 서술함에 있어 우리는 우리의 가장 훌륭한 동료 역사가 중 한 사람인 플루타르코스의 본을 따른다. 또 다른 독자들에게는 이런 것이 하찮아 보일지도 모르나, 우리는 이런 경우에 결코 장황하게 늘어놓는 법이 없으니, 부디 적어도 너그러이 보아 주기를 바란다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n웨스턴 씨가 도착했을 때 밀러 부인이 방을 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않았다. 다만 그가 방에 들기 전, 그와 그의 가마꾼들 사이에 작은 실랑이 한바탕이 벌어졌다. 헤라클레스 기둥에서 짐을 떠맡았던 이 사내들은 이 지주에게서 앞으로 좋은 단골이 되어 줄 가망을 조금도 품지 못한 데다, 그의 후한 인심에 한층 더 부추겨졌던 터였다(그가 제 손으로 삯보다 육 펜스를 더 얹어 주었으니 말이다). 그래서 그들은 아주 뻔뻔스럽게 일 실링을 더 내놓으라 요구했고, 이것이 지주를 어찌나 발끈하게 만들었던지, 그는 문간에서 그들에게 진심 어린 욕설을 한바탕 퍼부었을 뿐 아니라, 방에 들어온 뒤까지도 그 화를 거두지 못했다. 런던 사람들은 죄다 궁정 인간들과 똑같아서 시골 양반들 등쳐 먹을 궁리밖에 할 줄 모른다고 욕을 해댔다. \"제기랄,\" 그가 말했다. \"또다시 저놈들 손수레짝에 올라타느니 차라리 빗속을 걸어가고 말겠소. 저놈들이 일 마일 가는 동안 나를 흔들어댄 것이, 브라운 베스가 긴 여우 사냥 내내 흔드는 것보다 더하더이다.\"\n\n이 일로 인한 노여움이 조금 가라앉자, 그는 또 다른 일을 두고 똑같이 격앙된 어조를 다시 꺼냈다. \"이보시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 아주 볼만한 일이 벌어지고 있소. 사냥개들이 마침내 냄새를 바꿔 버렸지 뭐요. 우리가 여우를 상대하는 줄로만 알았더니, 빌어먹을, 끝에 가서 오소리로 드러났단 말이오!\"\n\n\"이보시오, 친애하는 이웃,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그 비유 좀 그만두고, 좀 더 알아듣게 말해 보시오.\" \"그럼,\" 지주가 말했다. \"솔직히 말해서, 우리는 이제껏 누군지도 모를 어떤 놈의 사생아, 그 매춘부 자식 놈을 두고 겁을 먹고 있었던 게요. 누구 자식인지 나야 알 게 뭐요. 그런데 이제 와서 여기 빌어먹을 귀족 나리 매춘부 자식 놈이 나타났단 말이오. 내가 알기로도 신경 쓰기로도 그놈 역시 사생아일지 모르오. 어쨌든 내 동의 아래 내 딸을 줄 일은 결코 없을 테니까. 그자들이 나라를 거덜 내긴 했어도, 나만은 결코 거덜 내지 못할 거요. 내 땅이 하노버로 넘어가는 일은 결코 없을 거란 말이오.\"\n\n\"참으로 놀랍구려, 친애하는 벗이여,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"허허, 이런! 나 자신도 놀랐소이다,\" 지주가 대답했다. \"어젯밤에 누이 웨스턴이 직접 잡아 둔 약속대로 그 사람을 보러 갔더니, 글쎄 여자들로 가득 찬 방으로 끌려 들어갔지 뭐요. 사촌 벨라스턴 마님이 계셨고, 베티 마님, 캐서린 마님, 그리고 누군지도 모를 마님까지 있더이다. 제기랄, 그 후프 페티코트 입은 암캐들 소굴에 또 나를 끼워 넣을 생각일랑 마시오! 빌어먹을, 차라리 내 사냥개들한테 쫓기는 편이 낫겠소. 옛날이야기 책에 나오는 그 액턴인가 하는 자처럼 말이오. 그자가 산토끼로 변해서 제 사냥개들한테 물려 죽고 잡아먹혔다지. 빌어먹을, 어느 인간도 그런 식으로 쫓겨 본 적은 없을 게요. 한쪽으로 피하면 한 여자가 나를 붙잡고, 되받아치려 들면 또 다른 여자가 나를 물어뜯었소. '오! 분명 영국에서 손꼽힐 만한 혼처지요.' 한 사촌이 그러더이다(여기서 그는 그들의 흉내를 내려 들었다). '참으로 더없이 유리한 제안이군요.' 또 다른 사촌이 소리치고요(그들이 죄다 내 사촌인 걸 알아 둬야 하오, 전엔 절반도 본 적 없는 자들인데도 말이오). '확실히 말씀드리지만,' 그 살집 두둑한 엉덩짝 암캐, 벨라스턴 마님이 그러더이다. '사촌, 그런 제안을 거절할 생각을 하다니, 제정신이 아니신 게 분명해요.'\"\n\n\"이제 좀 알겠소,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"누군가 웨스턴 양에게 청혼을 했고, 집안 부인들은 그것을 찬성하는데, 정작 그대 마음에는 들지 않는다는 거구려.\"\n\n\"내 마음이라!\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"그게 어떻게 마음에 들겠소? 내 말하지 않았소, 그자는 귀족이라고. 그리고 귀족이란, 내가 평생 상종하지 않기로 작정한 족속들임을 당신도 알지 않소. 한 귀족 놈이 제 사냥터로 삼고 싶어 했던 땅뙈기 하나를 두고, 사십 년치 임대료에 맞먹는 값을 내가 마다하지 않았소? 오로지 귀족들과는 거래를 안 하겠다는 이유 하나로 말이오. 그런데 내가 내 딸을 그런 놈에게 시집보낼 성싶소? 게다가 나는 이미 당신과 약조를 맺지 않았소? 내가 한번 약속한 흥정에서 발을 뺀 적이 있기나 하오?\"\n\n\"그 점에 관해서라면, 이웃이여,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"나는 그대를 어떤 약속에서도 온전히 풀어 드리겠소. 어떤 계약도, 맺을 당시에 그것을 맺을 온전한 권한이 없고, 그 후로도 그것을 이행할 권한을 얻지 못하는 당사자들 사이에서는 구속력을 가질 수 없는 법이오.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 웨스턴이 대답했다. \"내 말하건대, 나에게는 권한이 있고, 나는 그것을 이행하겠소. 당장 나와 함께 닥터스 코먼스로 갑시다. 내가 허가증을 받아 오겠소. 그리고 누이한테 가서 그 계집애를 힘으로 끌어내 오겠소. 그 애는 그자와 혼인하든가, 아니면 내가 그 애를 가둬 놓고 죽을 때까지 빵과 물만 먹이며 살게 하겠소.\"\n\n\"웨스턴 씨,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"이 일에 대한 나의 온전한 생각을 들어 봐 주시겠소?\"—\"들어 보라고? 아무렴, 듣고말고.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그럼, 그러지요.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"나는 그대에게도 그 젊은 아가씨에게도 빈말을 보태지 않고, 진심으로 말할 수 있소. 이 혼담이 처음 나왔을 때, 나는 두 분 모두에 대한 존중에서 그것을 아주 기꺼이, 진심으로 받아들였소. 이토록 가까운 이웃인 두 집안, 그리고 늘 서로 왕래하며 좋은 화목을 나누어 온 두 집안 사이의 결합이라니, 더없이 바람직한 일이라 여겼소. 그리고 그 아가씨로 말하자면, 그녀를 아는 모든 이의 한결같은 평판뿐 아니라 내 자신의 관찰만으로도, 그녀가 좋은 남편에게 더없이 값진 보배가 되리라 확신했소. 그녀의 빼어난 용모에 대해서는 더 말하지 않겠소. 그것은 분명 감탄할 만하니 말이오. 그녀의 선한 천성, 자애로운 마음씨, 정숙함은 너무도 잘 알려져 있어 따로 찬사가 필요치 않소. 그러나 그녀에게는 한 가지 자질이 있는데, 그것은 지금 천사들 중에서도 으뜸가는 자리에 있는 그 더없이 훌륭했던 여인에게 높은 정도로 깃들어 있던 것이오. 그것은 화려한 종류의 것이 아니어서, 흔히 사람들의 눈에 띄지 않고 지나치곤 하오. 실로 어찌나 드러나지 않는지, 나는 그것을 표현할 말조차 찾지 못하겠소. 이 자리에서는 부정의 방식으로 말할 수밖에 없겠소. 나는 그녀의 입에서 건방진 말이나, 이른바 재치 있는 응수라는 것을 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없소. 재기를 뽐내는 일도 없거니와, 오로지 깊은 학식과 경험에서만 나오는 그런 종류의 지혜를 내세우는 일은 더더욱 없소. 젊은 여인이 그런 것을 꾸며 보이는 일이란, 원숭이의 온갖 꾸밈만큼이나 우스꽝스러운 것이오. 단정 짓는 견해도, 재판관 같은 의견도, 심오한 비평도 없소. 내가 그녀를 남자들과 어울리는 자리에서 볼 때마다, 그녀는 늘 귀를 기울이는 모습이었소. 가르치려 드는 자의 주제넘음이 아니라, 배우는 자의 겸손함을 지니고 말이오. 이런 말을 하는 걸 용서하시오만, 나는 한번 그녀를 그저 시험해 보려고, 스웨컴 씨와 스퀘어 씨 사이에서 논쟁이 된 한 가지 문제에 대해 그녀의 의견을 청한 적이 있소. 그러자 그녀는 더없이 상냥하게 대답하더이다. '용서하세요, 친애하는 올워디 어른. 두 분 같은 신사분들이 의견을 달리하는 문제를 제가 가려낼 수 있으리라고는 진심으로 여기지 않으실 거예요.' 둘 다 똑같이 자기 쪽에 유리한 판정이 내려지리라 믿고 있던 스웨컴과 스퀘어가 내 청을 거들었소. 그녀는 똑같이 좋은 기색으로 대답하더이다. '저는 한사코 빠지겠어요. 어느 한 분 편을 들어 제 판단을 내놓아 다른 분을 모욕하는 일은 결코 하지 않겠어요.' 실로 그녀는 언제나 남자들의 식견에 더없는 경의를 보였소. 좋은 아내가 되는 데 절대적으로 필요한 자질이지요. 한 가지만 덧붙이겠소. 그녀에게는 어떤 꾸밈도 전혀 없음이 너무도 분명하니, 이 경의 역시 분명 진심에서 우러난 것임에 틀림없소.\"\n\n여기서 블리필이 비통하게 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러자 소피아에 대한 칭찬에 두 눈 가득 눈물이 고여 있던 웨스턴이 엉엉 울먹이며 내뱉었다. \"기죽지 말게, 자네가 그 애를 차지할 테니까, 빌어먹을, 그 애가 지금보다 스무 배 더 훌륭하더라도 자네가 차지할 거야.\"\n\n\"약속을 잊지 마시오, 선생.\" 올워디가 소리쳤다. \"내 말을 중간에 끊지 않기로 했잖소.\" \"알았소, 안 끊으리다.\" 지주가 대답했다. \"한마디도 더 안 하겠소.\"\n\n\"자, 친애하는 벗이여,\" 올워디가 말을 이었다. \"내가 이 아가씨의 미덕을 이토록 오래 늘어놓은 까닭은, 한편으로는 내가 정말로 그녀의 인품에 반했기 때문이고, 다른 한편으로는 내가 이 제안을 그토록 열렬히 받아들인 데에 재산이 주된 속셈이었다고 여겨지지 않게 하기 위함이오(그런 관점에서 보자면 이 혼담은 사실 내 조카 쪽에 유리한 것이니 말이오). 실로 나는 그토록 귀한 보석을 내 집안에 들이기를 진심으로 바랐소. 그러나 나는 좋은 것을 많이 바랄 수는 있어도, 그렇다고 그것을 훔치거나, 그것을 차지하기 위해 어떤 폭력이나 불의를 저지르지는 않겠소. 한 여인을 그 동의나 승낙에 어긋나게 혼인으로 몰아넣는 것은, 우리 나라 법이 그것을 막아 줄 수 있었으면 싶을 만큼 부당하고 억압적인 행위요. 그러나 선한 양심은 가장 형편없이 다스려지는 나라에서도 결코 무법이 아니어서, 입법자들의 태만이 미처 마련하지 못한 그 법들을 스스로 자기 안에 갖추는 법이오. 이것은 분명 그런 부류의 경우요. 한 여인을 제 뜻에 어긋나게 그 상태로 몰아넣는 것이, 어찌 잔인한 일이 아니겠소, 아니 불경한 일이 아니겠소? 그 상태에서의 처신에 대해 그녀는 가장 높고 가장 두려운 심판의 법정 앞에서 책임을 져야 하고, 제 영혼을 걸고 답해야 하거늘 말이오. 부부의 의무를 마땅한 방식으로 다해 내는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니오. 그런데 우리가 이 짐을 한 여인에게 지우면서, 동시에 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 있게 해 줄 모든 도움을 그녀에게서 빼앗는단 말이오? 온 마음을 다해도 감당하기 힘든 의무를 그녀에게 지우면서, 정작 그녀의 바로 그 마음을 가슴에서 뜯어낸단 말이오? 여기서는 아주 솔직히 말해야겠소. 이런 식으로 행동하는 부모들은, 그 자식들이 훗날 짓게 되는 모든 죄의 공범이며, 따라서 정의로운 심판자 앞에서 그 벌을 함께 나눠 받게 되리라 각오해야 마땅하오. 그러나 설령 그 벌을 피할 수 있다 한들, 오 하느님! 제 자식의 영원한 파멸에 일조했다는 생각을 견뎌 낼 수 있는 영혼이 어디 있겠소?\n\n\"이런 까닭으로, 더없이 좋은 이웃이여, 나는 이 아가씨의 마음이 더없이 불행하게도 내 조카에게서 등을 돌리고 있음을 보았기에, 그대가 그 애에게 베풀려 했던 영예에 대한 더 이상의 생각은 접어야겠소. 다만 그 영예에 대해서는 내가 언제나 더없이 고마운 마음을 간직하리라는 것만은 분명히 말해 두겠소.\"\n\n\"좋소, 선생.\" 웨스턴이 말했다(마개를 뽑자마자 거품이 터져 나오듯 말이 쏟아졌다). \"내가 당신 말을 끝까지 들어 줬다는 것만은 부인 못 할 거요. 이제 당신이 내 말을 들어 줄 차례요. 만일 내가 그 말 한 마디 한 마디에 답하지 못한다면, 그땐 이 일을 깨끗이 포기하는 데 동의하리다. 우선 한 가지만 대답해 보시오—내가 그 애를 낳지 않았소? 내가 그 애를 낳지 않았느냐 말이오? 그것부터 답해 보시오. 하긴 제 자식을 아는 아비야말로 현명한 아비라고들 하지요. 허나 나야말로 그 애에 대한 가장 떳떳한 권리를 가진 사람이오. 내가 그 애를 길러 냈으니까. 어쨌든 당신도 내가 그 애 아비라는 건 인정할 거요. 그럼 내가 아비라면, 내 자식을 내가 다스려야 하지 않겠소? 그걸 묻는 거요, 내 자식을 내가 다스리면 안 되는 거요? 다른 일들에서 그 애를 다스려야 한다면, 그 애에게 가장 중대한 이 일에서도 마땅히 내가 다스려야 하는 것 아니오? 게다가 그동안 내내 내가 바라는 게 대체 무엇이오? 내가 그 애더러 나를 위해 뭘 하라 하오? 나에게 뭘 달라 하오?—오히려 정반대요. 나는 그저 그 애더러 지금 내 재산의 절반을 가져가고, 나머지 절반은 내가 죽으면 가져가라는 것뿐이오. 그래, 이게 다 무엇을 위해서겠소? 다 그 애를 행복하게 해 주려는 것 아니오? 사람들 떠드는 소리를 듣고 있자면 미칠 노릇이오. 내가 만약 나 자신이 장가들려는 거라면, 그 애가 울고불고할 까닭이 있겠지. 허나 오히려 정반대로, 나는 내 땅을 묶어 놓아서 설령 내가 장가들고 싶어도 못 들게 하겠다고까지 했소. 이 땅 위에 나를 데려갈 여자가 어디 한 명이라도 있겠소만. 빌어먹을, 내가 그 이상 뭘 더 할 수 있단 말이오? 내가 그 애를 파멸로 몰아넣는다고!—이런 제기랄! 그 애의 새끼손가락 하나라도 다치게 하느니, 나는 차라리 온 세상이 다 망하는 꼴을 보겠소. 정말이지, 올워디 씨, 미안하오만, 당신이 그런 식으로 말하는 걸 들으니 놀랍기 그지없소. 그리고 어떻게 받아들이든 한마디 해야겠는데, 나는 당신이 좀 더 분별이 있는 사람인 줄 알았소.\"\n\n올워디는 이 빈정거림을 그저 미소로 받아넘겼다. 설령 그가 애를 썼다 한들, 그 미소에 악의나 멸시의 기색을 조금이라도 담아낼 수는 없었으리라. 어리석음을 향한 그의 미소란, 천사들이 인류의 터무니없는 짓들에 보낼 법한 바로 그런 미소였다.\n\n이제 블리필이 몇 마디 하게 해 달라고 청했다. \"그 아가씨에게 어떤 폭력을 쓰는 일이라면, 저는 결코 동의하지 않을 것입니다. 제 양심은 그 누구에게도 폭력을 쓰는 것을 허락하지 않으니, 하물며 한 아가씨에게야 더 말할 것도 없습니다. 그녀가 제게 아무리 모질게 군다 해도, 저는 그녀를 향해 언제나 더없이 순수하고 진실한 애정을 간직할 것입니다. 그렇지만 저는 여자들이 꾸준한 인내 앞에서는 좀처럼 버티지 못한다는 글을 읽은 적이 있습니다. 그러니 그런 꾸준함으로 마침내 그녀의 마음을 얻으리라 바라면 안 될 까닭이 무엇이겠습니까? 더욱이 앞으로는 제게 경쟁자가 없을지도 모르는 일이고요. 이 귀족으로 말하자면, 웨스턴 씨께서 저를 그자보다 더 아껴 주시니 말입니다. 그리고 분명, 어른, 이런 일에서 부모가 적어도 거부할 발언권만큼은 가진다는 것을 어른께서도 부인하지 않으실 겁니다. 아니, 저는 바로 그 아가씨 자신이 한두 번이 아니라 그렇게 말하는 것을 들은 적이 있습니다. 부모의 뜻에 정면으로 맞서 혼인하는 자식은 변명의 여지가 없다고 그녀가 단언했지요. 게다가 집안의 다른 부인들은 그 귀족의 청혼을 두둔하는 듯하나, 정작 그 아가씨 자신은 그자에게 조금의 호의도 보일 마음이 없어 보입니다. 아아! 저는 그녀가 그렇지 않음을 너무도 잘 알고 있습니다. 저 가장 사악한 사내가 그녀의 마음속에 여전히 가장 높은 자리를 차지하고 있음을, 저는 너무도 절절히 느끼고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 정말 그렇다네.\" 웨스턴이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 분명,\" 블리필이 말했다. \"그자가 저지른 이 살인 소식을 그녀가 듣게 되면, 설령 법이 그자의 목숨만은 살려 준다 하더라도——\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리냐?\" 웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"살인이라! 그놈이 살인을 저질렀단 말이냐, 그래서 그놈이 교수형 당하는 꼴을 볼 가망이 있단 말이냐?—툴 드 롤, 툴 롤 드 롤.\" 여기서 그는 노래를 부르며 방 안을 깡충깡충 뛰어다니기 시작했다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"너의 이 불행한 연정이 나를 더없이 괴롭게 하는구나. 나는 진심으로 너를 가엾이 여기고, 네가 뜻을 이루도록 정당한 일이라면 무엇이든 해 주고 싶다.\"\n\n\"그 이상은 바라지 않습니다.\" 블리필이 소리쳤다. \"친애하는 외숙께서 저를 좋게 보아 주시니, 제가 그 이상을 받아들이려 들 사람이라고는 여기지 않으실 줄 압니다.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그녀가 허락한다면 편지를 쓰고 찾아가는 것은 내가 허락하마—허나 폭력은 결코 생각도 말아야 한다. 가둬 두는 일도, 그런 종류의 어떤 시도도 나는 결단코 용납하지 않겠다.\"\n\n\"좋소, 좋아.\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"그런 짓은 결코 하지 않으리다. 좀 더 오래 점잖은 방법으로 어찌 되나 한번 해 봅시다. 그리고 이놈만 교수형으로 깨끗이 치워진다면—툴 롤 드 롤! 내 평생 이보다 더 반가운 소식은 들어 본 적이 없소—만사가 내 뜻대로 풀릴 게 분명하오.—자, 부디, 친애하는 올워디, 헤라클레스 기둥으로 가서 나와 함께 식사합시다. 양 어깨살 구이에다, 돼지 갈비, 닭고기에 달걀 소스까지 시켜 두었소. 우리 둘 말고는 아무도 없을 거요. 정 마음 내키면 주막 주인이나 불러도 좋고. 서플 목사는 내가 베이징스토크로 내려보냈소. 거기 어느 여관에 두고 온 내 담뱃갑을 가지러 말이오. 그건 무슨 일이 있어도 잃을 수 없소. 이십 년 넘게 함께한 오랜 벗이거든. 그 주막 주인이 아주 익살맞은 친구라, 당신도 그자를 무척 마음에 들어 할 거요.\"\n\n올워디 씨는 마침내 이 초대에 응했고, 곧이어 지주는 가엾은 존스의 빠른 비극적 최후를 보게 되리라는 희망에 노래를 부르고 깡충거리며 떠나갔다.\n\n그가 떠난 뒤, 올워디 씨는 앞서의 그 화제를 자못 진지하게 다시 꺼냈다. 그는 조카에게 말했다. \"나는 진심으로, 네가 이 연정을 이겨 내려 애써 주기를 바란다. 그것에 대해서는,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 너에게 성공할 어떤 희망도 빈말로 안겨 줄 수가 없구나. 꾸준함으로 여자의 반감을 이겨 낼 수 있다는 것은 분명 흔한 착각이다. 무관심이라면 어쩌면 이따금 거기에 굴복할지도 모르지. 그러나 연인이 꾸준함으로 거두는 통상의 승리란, 변덕이나 신중함, 꾸밈, 그리고 흔히는 도를 넘은 가벼움을 상대로 한 것이다. 천성이 그리 정열적이지 않은 여자들은 그런 가벼움 탓에, 설령 그 상대가 제법 마음에 들고 (애초에 마음을 정하기나 한다면) 끝내 그에게 보상해 주기로 마음먹은 경우에도, 구애의 기간을 질질 끌어 가며 제 허영을 부리려 들거든. 그러나 확고한 혐오는—이것이 바로 그런 경우일까 봐 나는 두렵다만—시간이 흐를수록 이겨지기는커녕 오히려 힘을 더해 가는 법이다. 게다가, 얘야, 나에게는 또 다른 염려가 있는데, 이건 부디 양해해 다오. 나는 네가 이 어여쁜 아가씨에게 품은 이 연정이 그녀의 아름다운 외모를 지나치게 그 대상으로 삼고 있어서, 부부의 행복의 유일한 토대인 그 사랑의 이름에 걸맞지 않은 것은 아닐까 두렵다. 아름다운 여인을 향해, 그녀가 우리에게 품은 마음 따위는 아랑곳없이, 감탄하고 좋아하고 그녀를 차지하기를 갈망하는 것—그것은 너무도 자연스러운 일일까 봐 나는 두렵구나. 그러나 사랑이란, 내가 믿기로는, 오직 사랑에서만 태어나는 자식이다. 적어도 우리를 미워한다고 확신되는 사람을 사랑하는 것은 인간의 본성에 없는 일이라고, 나는 꽤 확신한다. 그러니, 착한 아이야, 네 마음을 샅샅이 살펴보아라. 그리하여 살펴본 끝에 이런 종류의 의심이 조금이라도 든다면, 분명 너 자신의 덕과 신앙심이 그토록 사악한 연정을 네 가슴에서 몰아내도록 너를 떠밀 것이고, 너의 좋은 분별력은 머지않아 네가 그것을 고통 없이 해내게 해 줄 것이다.\"\n\n블리필의 대답은 독자도 어느 정도 짐작할 수 있으리라. 그러나 만일 독자가 짐작이 잘 안 되더라도, 지금 우리는 그것을 채워 줄 겨를이 없다. 우리 이야기는 이제 한층 더 중요한 문제들을 향해 서둘러 나아가고 있으며, 우리는 더 이상 소피아 곁을 떠나 있는 것을 견딜 수가 없기 때문이다.\n\n소피아와 그녀의 고모 사이에 벌어진 기이한 한 장면.\n\n음매 우는 암송아지와 매애 우는 암양은 떼를 지어 무리 속에서 안전하게, 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채로 목초지를 거닐 수 있다. 사실 이들도 훗날에는 인간의 먹잇감이 될 운명이지만, 그래도 여러 해 동안은 방해받지 않고 자유를 누리도록 허락받는다. 그러나 살집 좋은 암사슴 한 마리가 숲에서 빠져나와 어느 들판이나 작은 숲에서 쉬고 있는 것이 발각되면, 온 교구가 당장 발칵 뒤집힌다. 너 나 할 것 없이 모두가 사슴을 쫓아 개를 풀 준비를 한다. 그리고 만일 인심 좋은 지주가 그 사슴을 다른 이들로부터 지켜 준다면, 그것은 오직 자기가 잡아먹기 위해 따로 확보해 두려는 것일 뿐이다.\n\n나는 재산과 지체를 갖춘 매우 아리따운 젊은 여성이 양육이라는 울타리 밖으로 처음 길을 벗어난 모습을 볼 때면, 그녀가 이 암사슴과 거의 똑같은 처지에 놓여 있다고 자주 생각해 왔다. 도시는 곧장 떠들썩해진다. 그녀는 공원에서 연극장으로, 궁정에서 모임으로, 모임에서 다시 제 방으로 쫓겨 다니며, 어느 한 철도 이런저런 포식자의 아가리에서 좀처럼 벗어나지 못한다. 친구들이 어떤 자에게서 그녀를 지켜 준다 해도, 그것은 다만 그들 자신이 골라 둔 누군가에게 그녀를 넘겨주기 위함일 뿐이며, 그 상대는 흔히 다른 누구보다도 그녀에게 더 못마땅한 인물이다. 그러는 동안 다른 여자들은 떼를 지어 무리째 안전하게, 거의 눈길도 받지 않은 채 공원과 연극장과 오페라와 모임을 누비고 다닌다. 비록 적어도 대개는 그들도 결국에는 잡아먹히고 말지만, 그래도 오랫동안은 방해도 통제도 없이 자유를 마음껏 즐긴다.\n\n이 모든 본보기 같은 여인들 가운데, 가엾은 소피아만큼 이 박해를 더 톡톡히 맛본 이는 일찍이 없었다. 그녀의 불운한 별은 블리필 때문에 그녀가 겪은 그 모든 고초로도 만족하지 못하고, 이제 또 다른 추격자를 일으켜 세웠으니, 그자는 앞서의 인물 못지않게 그녀를 괴롭힐 듯이 보였다. 비록 고모는 덜 격렬했지만, 그녀를 들들 볶는 데 있어서만큼은 앞서 아버지가 그랬던 것에 조금도 뒤지지 않았다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나고 하인들이 물러가자마자, 이미 소피아에게 이 일을 꺼낸 적 있는 웨스턴 부인이 그녀에게 알렸다. \"바로 그날 오후에 백작께서 오시기를 기대하고 있으며, 첫 기회가 오는 대로 너를 그분과 단둘이 남겨 둘 작정이다.\" \"그러시면, 고모님,\" 소피아가 다소 기개 있게 대답했다. \"저는 첫 기회가 오는 대로 그분을 홀로 남겨 두고 자리를 뜨겠어요.\" \"뭐라고! 얘야!\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"내가 네 아버지 집에서 갇혀 지내던 너를 풀어 준 그 친절에 대해, 이게 네가 갚는 보답이냐?\" \"고모님도 아시잖아요,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그렇게 갇혀 지낸 까닭은, 제가 진저리 치는 남자를 받아들이라는 아버지의 뜻을 따르지 못하겠다고 거절했기 때문이에요. 그런데 그 괴로움에서 저를 구해 주신 사랑하는 고모님께서, 똑같이 나쁜 또 다른 괴로움 속으로 저를 밀어 넣으시려는 건가요?\" \"그러면 너는, 얘야,\" 웨스턴 부인이 대답했다. \"펠라마 백작님과 블리필 씨 사이에 아무 차이가 없다고 생각하는 거냐?\" \"제 생각엔 차이가 거의 없어요,\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"그리고 둘 중 하나를 받아들이도록 단죄받아야 한다면, 저는 분명 제 자신을 아버지의 만족을 위해 희생한다는 떳떳함이라도 가지겠어요.\" \"그러니까 내 만족 따위는,\" 고모가 말했다. \"네게 거의 무게가 없다는 거로구나. 하지만 그런 따위 생각은 나를 움직이지 못한다. 나는 더 고귀한 동기로 움직이는 사람이야. 우리 가문을 드높이고, 너 자신을 귀하게 만드는 것, 그것이 내가 나아가는 방향이다. 너는 야심이라는 감각이 전혀 없느냐? 네 마차 위에 백작 관(冠)을 얹는다는 생각에 아무런 매력도 느끼지 못한단 말이냐?\" \"조금도요, 제 명예를 걸고요,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"제 마차 위에는 바늘꽂이를 얹는 편이 차라리 똑같이 흡족하겠어요.\" \"명예 따위는 입에 올리지도 마라,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"너 같은 한심한 것의 입에는 어울리지 않는 말이다. 얘야, 네가 나로 하여금 이런 말을 쓰게 만드니 안타깝구나만, 나는 네 비굴한 성미를 도무지 참을 수가 없다. 네 안에는 웨스턴 가문의 피가 한 방울도 흐르지 않아. 하지만 네 생각이 아무리 천하고 비루하다 해도, 너 때문에 내 이름에 오점을 묻힐 수는 없다. 내가 잉글랜드에서 손꼽히는 최고의 혼처를 거절하도록 너를 부추겼다는 말이 세상에 돌게 둘 수는 없어. 그 혼처는 재산상의 이점은 말할 것도 없고, 거의 어느 가문에든 명예가 될 것이며, 작위에 있어서는 실로 우리 가문보다 한 수 위니 말이다.\" \"분명,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"저는 부족하게 태어난 모양이에요. 다른 사람들이 복으로 받은 그 감각을 저는 갖지 못한 거예요. 소리와 겉치레의 기쁨을 맛볼 줄 아는 어떤 감각이 분명 따로 있는데, 제게는 그게 없는 거지요. 그렇지 않고서야, 제 눈에 보이는 것처럼 그것이 모든 하찮은 것 가운데서도 가장 하찮게 보인다면, 사람들이 그것을 얻으려 그토록 애쓰고 그토록 많은 것을 희생하지도, 또 그것을 손에 넣었다고 그토록 우쭐대며 자랑스러워하지도 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"아니다, 아니야, 아가씨,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"너는 다른 사람들만큼이나 많은 감각을 갖고 태어났어. 다만 분명히 말해 두는데, 너는 나를 바보로 만들거나 내 처신을 세상에 웃음거리로 내놓을 만큼의 분별력을 갖고 태어나지는 못했다. 그러니 내 말로써 이것을 네게 선언하마. 그리고 내 결심이 얼마나 확고한지는 너도 알 거라 믿는다. 네가 오늘 오후에 백작님을 만나기로 동의하지 않는다면, 나는 내일 아침 내 손으로 직접 너를 내 오라비에게 넘겨줄 것이며, 이후로는 결코 네 일에 관여하지도, 다시는 네 얼굴을 보지도 않겠다.\" 이 말은 더없이 노엽고 단호한 어조로 내뱉어진 것이었는데, 소피아는 그 뒤로 잠시 말없이 서 있었다. 그러다 와락 눈물을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"고모님, 저를 마음대로 하세요. 저는 이 땅에서 가장 비참하고 망가진 신세예요. 사랑하는 고모님마저 저를 버리신다면, 저는 어디서 보호자를 찾아야 하나요?\" \"사랑하는 조카야,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"너는 백작님이라는 더없이 훌륭한 보호자를 얻게 될 거다. 저 비열한 작자 존스에 대한 미련이 아니고서는, 결코 마다할 까닭이 없는 보호자란 말이다.\" \"정말이지, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"고모님은 저를 잘못 보고 계세요. 고모님께서 제게 보여 주신 것들을 겪고도, 설령 제가 한때 그런 마음을 품었더라도 그것을 영영 떨쳐 버리지 않으리라고 어찌 짐작하실 수 있나요? 고모님께서 흡족하시다면, 다시는 그의 얼굴을 보지 않겠노라고 성찬을 받들어 맹세하겠어요.\" \"하지만 얘야, 사랑하는 얘야,\" 고모가 말했다. \"이치를 따져 보렴. 단 하나라도 반대할 까닭을 댈 수 있겠니?\" \"이미 충분한 반대 이유를 말씀드린 것 같은데요,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"뭐라고?\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"나는 아무것도 기억나지 않는다.\" \"분명히요, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그가 저를 가장 무례하고 비열한 방식으로 다뤘다고 말씀드렸잖아요.\" \"정말이지, 얘야,\" 고모가 대답했다. \"나는 들은 적이 없거나, 아니면 못 알아들었구나. 그런데 이 무례하고 비열한 방식이라는 게 무슨 뜻이냐?\" \"정말이지, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"말씀드리기가 거의 부끄러울 지경이에요. 그가 저를 팔로 붙잡아 긴 의자 위로 끌어 내리고는, 제 가슴팍에 손을 들이밀고 어찌나 거칠게 입을 맞췄던지, 지금 이 순간에도 왼쪽 가슴에 그 자국이 남아 있어요.\" \"정말이냐!\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"네, 정말이에요, 고모님,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"마침 그 순간에 아버지가 들어오셨기에 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 그가 어디까지 무례하게 굴 작정이었는지는 하늘만이 아실 거예요.\" \"기가 막히고 어이가 없구나,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"우리가 한 가문을 이룬 이래로 웨스턴이라는 이름을 가진 여자가 그런 취급을 받은 적은 없었다. 어떤 왕족이 내게 그런 무례를 시도했더라면, 나는 그자의 두 눈알을 뽑아 버렸을 거야. 그럴 리가 없어! 분명, 소피아, 네가 그자에 대한 내 분노를 돋우려고 이 일을 지어낸 게 틀림없다.\" \"고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"저를 거짓말이나 할 수 있는 아이로 여기실 만큼 저를 나쁘게 보지는 않으시리라 믿어요. 제 영혼을 걸고 사실이에요.\" \"내가 그 자리에 있었더라면, 나는 그자의 심장을 칼로 찔렀을 게다,\" 고모가 받아쳤다. \"그렇지만 분명 그에게 부정한 속셈은 있을 수 없어. 그럴 리가 없다! 그가 감히 그러진 못할 거야. 게다가 그의 청혼만 봐도 그렇지 않다는 게 드러나잖니. 그 청혼은 명예로울 뿐 아니라 너그럽기까지 하니 말이다. 모르겠구나. 요즘 세상은 너무 큰 방종을 허용하지. 나라면 예식 전에는 멀찍이서 인사하는 정도밖에 허락하지 않았을 거야. 나도 예전에 구애하는 이들이 있었단다, 그리 오래전 일도 아니야. 여럿 있었지. 비록 나는 결혼에는 결코 동의하지 않았고, 조금의 방종도 부추긴 적이 없지만 말이다. 그건 어리석은 풍습이고, 나라면 결코 받아들이지 않았을 일이야. 어떤 남자도 내 뺨 이상으로 입 맞춘 적은 없었다. 남편에게 입술을 내어 주는 것조차 사람이 스스로를 다잡아 겨우 할 수 있는 일이야. 그리고 사실, 내가 설령 결혼하도록 설득당했다 한들, 그렇게까지 견디는 일에 그리 쉽게 이르지는 못했을 게다.\" \"용서해 주세요, 사랑하는 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"한 가지만 짚어 보고 싶어요. 고모님께서는 구애하는 이들이 많았다고 스스로 인정하셨고, 설령 부인하셔도 세상이 다 아는 사실이지요. 고모님은 그들을 모두 거절하셨고, 저는 그중에 적어도 백작 관 하나쯤은 있었으리라 확신해요.\" \"맞는 말이다, 사랑하는 소피야,\" 고모가 대답했다. \"내게도 한때 작위를 주겠다는 제안이 있었지.\" \"그렇다면, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"어째서 저에게는 이번 한 번 거절하는 것을 허락하지 않으시나요?\" \"그건 그렇다, 얘야,\" 고모가 말했다. \"나는 작위 제안을 거절한 적이 있어. 하지만 그건 그리 좋은 제안이 아니었어. 그러니까, 그렇게나 아주, 아주 좋은 제안은 아니었단 말이다.\" \"그렇지요, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"하지만 고모님께는 어마어마한 재산을 가진 남자들로부터 굉장한 청혼들이 들어왔잖아요. 첫 번째도, 두 번째도, 세 번째 좋은 혼처도 처음으로 나타난 게 아니었고요.\" \"그건 인정한다,\" 고모가 말했다. \"그러면, 고모님,\" 소피아가 이어 갔다. \"어째서 저는 두 번째, 어쩌면 이번보다 더 나은 청혼을 기대하면 안 되나요? 고모님은 지금도 아직 젊은 여인이시고, 처음 나타난, 재산을 가진, 아니, 작위를 가진 구애자에게 굴복하겠다고 약속하지 않으실 거라고 저는 확신해요. 저는 아주 젊은 여자인데, 절망할 까닭이 없는 게 분명하잖아요.\" \"그래, 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 소피야,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"그러면 나더러 무슨 말을 하란 말이냐?\" \"그저, 적어도 오늘 저녁만큼은 제가 홀로 남겨지지 않게 해 주시기를 바랄 뿐이에요. 그것만 허락해 주시면, 지나간 일을 헤아려 보시고 제가 고모님과 함께 있는 자리에서 그분을 만나야 한다고 여기신다면, 저는 따르겠어요.\" \"그래, 그건 허락하마,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"소피야, 너도 알다시피 나는 너를 사랑하니, 네게 아무것도 거절할 수가 없구나. 내 천성이 무던하다는 건 너도 알지. 늘 이렇게 무던했던 건 아니야. 예전엔 모질다는 소리를 들었단다, 남자들한테서 말이야. 사람들은 나를 '모진 파르테니사'라고 불렀어. 나는 '모진 파르테니사'에게 바치는 시구가 적힌 창문을 여럿 깨뜨려 봤단다. 소피야, 나는 너만큼 어여뻤던 적은 없지만, 그래도 예전엔 네 모습 같은 데가 좀 있었어. 지금은 조금 변했다만. 키케로가 그의 서한에서 말했듯, 왕국이며 나라도 변화를 겪는 법이니, 인간의 모습이라고 변하지 않을 수 있겠니.\" 이렇게 그녀는 거의 반 시간 가까이 자기 자신과, 자신이 거둔 정복들과, 자신의 모짊에 대해 떠들어 댔다. 그러다 마침내 백작이 도착했는데, 그 더없이 지루한 방문 동안 웨스턴 부인은 단 한 번도 방을 비켜 줄 기색을 보이지 않았다. 그렇게 백작은 물러갔으니, 조카에게 그러했던 것 못지않게 고모에게도 그리 흡족하지는 못했다. 소피아가 고모를 어찌나 더없이 좋은 기분으로 돌려놓았던지, 고모는 조카가 하는 말이라면 거의 무엇이든 동의해 주었고, 그처럼 들이대는 구애자에게는 다소 거리를 두는 처신이 어쩌면 부적절하지 않으리라는 데에도 수긍해 주었던 것이다.\n\n이렇게 소피아는 조금의 잘 겨냥된 아첨으로—이를 두고 누구도 그녀를 탓하지는 않으리라—스스로에게 약간의 안도를 얻어 냈고, 적어도 그 끔찍한 날을 미루어 두었다. 자, 이제 우리는 우리의 여주인공이 오랫동안 겪어 온 처지보다 한결 나은 형편에 놓인 것을 보았으니, 우리가 가히 상상할 수 있는 가장 비참한 처지에 남겨 두고 떠나왔던 존스 씨를 잠시 살펴보기로 하자.\n\n밀러 부인과 나이팅게일 씨가 감옥으로 존스를 찾아오다.\n\n올워디 씨와 그 조카가 웨스턴 씨를 만나러 간 사이, 밀러 부인은 사위의 거처로 향했다. 친구 존스에게 닥친 사고를 사위에게 알리려는 것이었다. 그러나 사위는 이미 오래전에 파트리지에게서 그 소식을 들어 알고 있었다(존스는 밀러 부인의 집을 떠난 뒤 나이팅게일 씨와 같은 집에 방을 하나 얻어 머물고 있었기 때문이다). 이 선량한 부인은 딸이 존스 씨 일로 크게 상심해 있는 것을 보고, 할 수 있는 한 위로를 건넨 다음, 존스가 있다고 들은 게이트하우스로 길을 나섰다. 그곳에는 나이팅게일 씨가 부인보다 먼저 와 있었다.\n\n참된 벗의 굳건함과 한결같음은 어떤 종류의 고난에 빠진 사람에게든 더없이 큰 기쁨이어서, 그 고난이 단지 일시적이고 또 누그러질 여지가 있는 것이라면, 고난 그 자체가 이 위로를 함께 데려옴으로써 충분히 보상되고도 남는다. 그리고 이런 사례는, 어떤 피상적이고 부정확한 관찰자들이 전한 것만큼 그리 드물지 않다. 사실대로 말하자면, 동정심의 결핍은 우리의 일반적인 결점에 끼지 않는다. 우리의 성품을 더럽히는 검은 성분은 바로 시기심이다. 그래서 우리의 눈길은, 안타깝게도, 우리보다 분명히 더 위대하고 더 선하고 더 지혜롭고 더 행복한 이들을 향해 위로 들어 올려질 때면 으레 어느 정도의 악의를 품기 마련이다. 반면에 우리는 비천하고 가련한 이들을 내려다볼 때에는 흔히 넉넉한 자비와 연민을 보낸다. 실제로 나는, 내가 지켜본 우정들 속에서 드러난 결함의 대부분이 오로지 시기심에서 비롯되었음을 알아차렸다. 그것은 지옥에서 온 악덕이건만, 거기서 완전히 벗어난 사람은 내가 아는 한 극히 드물었다. 그러나 이 주제는 이쯤 해 두자. 더 파고들었다가는 너무 멀리 나가게 될 테니.\n\n운명의 여신은 존스가 역경의 무게에 눌려 가라앉아 버릴까, 그리하여 앞으로 그를 괴롭힐 기회를 잃을까 걱정했던 것인지, 아니면 정말로 그를 향한 가혹함을 다소 누그러뜨린 것인지 알 수 없지만, 그토록 충실한 두 벗의 동행을, 그리고 어쩌면 더더욱 드문 일이지만 충실한 하인 하나를 함께 보내 줌으로써 그 박해를 조금은 늦추어 준 듯 보였다. 파트리지로 말하자면, 흠은 많았어도 충직함만큼은 모자라지 않았다. 비록 두려움이 그로 하여금 주인을 위해 교수대에 오르게 할 만큼은 아니었으나, 온 세상이 뇌물을 준다 한들 그가 주인의 편을 저버리게 만들 수는 없었으리라고 나는 믿는다.\n\n존스가 벗들이 곁에 있어 큰 위안이 된다고 말하고 있을 때, 파트리지가 들어와 피츠패트릭 씨가 아직 살아 있다는 소식을 전했다. 다만 외과의는 가망이 거의 없다고 단언했다는 것이었다. 이 말에 존스가 깊은 한숨을 내쉬자, 나이팅게일이 그에게 말했다. \"여보게, 톰, 어째서 이런 사고로 그렇게 스스로를 괴롭히는가. 그 결과가 어떻든 자네에게는 아무런 위험도 닥치지 않을 일이고, 자네 양심으로도 조금의 책임조차 없다고 떳떳하지 않은가? 그자가 죽는다 한들, 자네가 한 일이라곤 자기 방어를 위해 한 불한당의 목숨을 거둔 것뿐일세. 검시 배심도 분명 그렇게 판정할 걸세. 그러면 자네는 쉽게 보석으로 풀려날 테고, 비록 재판이라는 형식은 거쳐야겠지만, 그건 많은 사람이 단돈 한 실링이면 기꺼이 자네 대신 받아 줄 만한 재판이라네.\" \"자, 자, 존스 씨,\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"기운을 내요. 나는 당신이 먼저 시비를 건 사람일 리 없다는 걸 잘 알아요. 그래서 올워디 씨께도 그렇게 말씀드렸고, 내가 그분과 이야기를 끝내기 전에 그분도 반드시 그걸 인정하시게 만들 거예요.\"\n\n존스는 무겁게 대답했다. \"제 운명이 어떻게 되든, 같은 인간의 피를 흘리게 했다는 사실만큼은 제게 닥칠 수 있는 가장 큰 불행 가운데 하나로 여기며 늘 한탄할 겁니다. 하지만 제게는 가장 애틋한 종류의 또 다른 불행이 있습니다——아! 밀러 부인, 저는 이 세상에서 가장 소중히 여기던 것을 잃고 말았습니다.\" \"그건 분명 사랑하는 사람 얘기겠지요,\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 자, 자, 나는 당신이 짐작하는 것보다 더 많이 알고 있어요\"(사실 파트리지가 죄다 떠벌려 놓았던 것이다). \"그리고 당신이 아는 것보다 더 많은 이야기를 들었답니다. 정말이지 일은 당신 생각보다 잘 풀려 가고 있어요. 블리필이 그 아가씨를 얻을 가망이라고는, 내가 보기엔 육 펜스 값어치도 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 부인,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"정말이지 부인께서는 제 슬픔의 까닭을 전혀 모르십니다. 그 사연을 아신다면, 제 처지에 어떤 위로도 통하지 않는다는 걸 인정하실 겁니다. 저는 블리필 따위는 조금도 두렵지 않아요. 제가 제 손으로 저를 망쳐 놓았습니다.\" \"절망하지 말아요,\" 밀러 부인이 받았다. \"여자가 무엇을 해낼 수 있는지 당신은 모를 거예요. 그러니 내 힘으로 할 수 있는 일이라면, 무엇이든 당신을 위해 하겠다고 약속할게요. 그게 내 도리니까요. 내 아들, 그러니까 사랑하는 나이팅게일도 같은 이유로 당신께 신세를 졌다고 친절히 말해 주는데, 그 아이도 이게 내 도리라는 걸 알아요. 내가 직접 그 아가씨를 찾아갈까요? 당신이 전하라는 말이라면 무엇이든 그대로 전해 주겠어요.\"\n\n\"세상에서 가장 훌륭한 부인이여,\" 존스가 부인의 손을 잡으며 외쳤다. \"제게 신세 졌다는 말씀은 마십시오.——하지만 부인께서 그렇게 친절히 말씀을 꺼내 주셨으니, 어쩌면 부인의 힘이 닿을지도 모를 부탁이 하나 있습니다. 보아하니 부인께서는 그 아가씨를 알고 계신 듯합니다(어떻게 그 소식을 들으셨는지는 저도 모르겠습니다만). 그분은 참으로 제 마음 깊은 곳에 자리한 분입니다. 이것을 그분께 전해 주실 방도를 마련해 주신다면(주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내 부인께 건네며), 저는 부인의 그 은혜를 영원히 잊지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이리 주세요,\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"잠들기 전에 이것이 그분 손에 들린 걸 보지 못한다면, 내 다음 잠이 마지막 잠이 되어도 좋아요! 마음을 편히 가져요, 착한 젊은이! 지난날의 어리석음에서 교훈을 얻을 만큼 현명해져요. 그러면 모든 일이 잘 풀릴 거라고 내가 장담하지요. 나는 머지않아 당신이 이 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨와 행복하게 지내는 모습을 보게 될 거예요. 누구에게 물어봐도 그분이 그런 분이라고 하니까요.\"\n\n\"부인, 제 말을 믿어 주십시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 저처럼 불행한 처지에 놓인 사람이 흔히 늘어놓는 빈말을 하는 게 아닙니다. 이 끔찍한 사고가 일어나기 전부터, 저는 그 사악함과 어리석음을 절감하게 된 그런 삶을 끊어 버리기로 결심했습니다. 진심으로 말씀드리지만, 불행히도 제가 부인 댁에서 일으킨 소동들에 대해서는 진정으로 용서를 빌면서도, 저는 자포자기한 난봉꾼은 아닙니다. 비록 악덕에 휩쓸려 떠밀려 오긴 했어도, 저는 악한 인간됨을 옳다고 여기지 않으며, 이 순간부터는 결코 그런 자라는 소리를 들을 짓을 하지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n밀러 부인은 이 다짐들에 크게 흡족해하며, 그 진실함을 온전히 믿는다고 단언했다. 이제 남은 대화는 그 선량한 부인과 나이팅게일 씨가 풀이 죽은 존스 씨의 기운을 북돋우려 함께 애쓰는 것으로 흘러갔다. 두 사람은 그를 어지간히 성공적으로 다독여, 처음 보았을 때보다 훨씬 위로받고 마음 편해진 상태로 그를 두고 나올 수 있었다. 이 기쁜 변화에 무엇보다 크게 보탬이 된 것은, 그가 도무지 이룰 방도를 찾지 못해 절망하던 일, 곧 그의 편지를 소피아에게 전해 주겠다는 밀러 부인의 다정한 약속이었다. 앞서 블랙 조지가 소피아의 마지막 편지를 가져왔을 때, 그는 파트리지에게, 아가씨가 답장을 가져오지 말라고, 만일 어긴다면 그 사실을 아버지께 알리겠다며 엄히 일러두었다고 전했던 터였다. 게다가 존스는, 이 선량한 부인이라는, 사실 세상에서 가장 가치 있는 사람 가운데 하나가 올워디 씨에게 직접 자기를 위해 그토록 열렬히 변호해 줄 사람이 되어 준다는 것을 알고 적잖이 기뻤다.\n\n부인이 한 시간쯤 머물다(나이팅게일은 그보다 훨씬 오래 함께 있었다) 두 사람은 곧 다시 오겠노라 약속하며 작별했다. 그 자리에서 밀러 부인은 그의 연인에게서 좋은 소식을 가져올 수 있기를 바란다고 했고, 나이팅게일 씨는 피츠패트릭 씨의 상처가 어떤 상태인지 알아보고, 아울러 그 싸움 자리에 있었던 사람들 가운데 몇몇을 찾아내 보겠다고 약속했다.\n\n이 가운데 앞엣사람, 곧 밀러 부인은 곧장 소피아를 찾아 나섰다. 이제 우리도 그 길을 따라 그녀와 함께 가 보기로 하자.\n\n밀러 부인이 소피아를 찾아간다.\n\n그 젊은 아가씨를 만나는 일은 결코 어렵지 않았다. 이제 소피아는 고모와 더없이 다정한 사이로 지내고 있었으므로, 마음에 드는 손님이라면 누구든 마음껏 맞이할 자유가 있었던 것이다.\n\n소피아가 한창 몸단장을 하고 있을 때, 아래층에 한 부인이 그녀를 뵙고자 기다린다는 전갈이 왔다. 같은 여인이라면 누구를 만나든 두려울 것도 부끄러울 것도 없었으므로, 밀러 부인은 곧바로 안으로 안내되었다.\n\n서로 낯선 여인들 사이에 오가는 인사와 으레 갖추는 예의가 끝나자, 소피아가 말했다. \"부인을 알게 되는 기쁨을 아직 누리지 못했군요.\" \"그렇습니다, 아가씨.\" 밀러 부인이 대답했다. \"이렇게 불쑥 찾아뵌 것을 용서해 주셔야겠습니다. 다만 무엇이 저로 하여금 이런 폐를 끼치게 했는지 아신다면, 부디……\" \"그런데 무슨 용건이신지요, 부인?\" 소피아가 약간 마음이 흔들린 채 물었다. \"아가씨, 우리 둘만 있는 것이 아니라서요.\" 밀러 부인이 나직한 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"베티, 나가 있거라.\" 소피아가 말했다.\n\n베티가 물러가자 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"아가씨, 무척 불행한 한 젊은 신사분의 간곡한 부탁으로 이 편지를 전해 드리러 왔습니다.\" 소피아는 겉봉을 보고 그 필체를 단번에 알아채고는 얼굴빛이 변했고, 잠시 머뭇거리다가 말했다. \"부인, 부인의 모습으로 보아서는 용건이 그런 종류의 것이라고는 생각지도 못했습니다. 그 편지를 누가 보냈든, 저는 열어 보지 않겠습니다. 누구에게든 부당한 의심을 품는 것은 저로서도 괴로운 일입니다만, 부인은 제게 전혀 낯선 분이니까요.\"\n\n\"아가씨, 조금만 참고 들어 주신다면,\" 밀러 부인이 대답했다. \"제가 누구인지, 또 그 편지를 어떻게 맡게 되었는지 말씀드리겠습니다.\" \"부인, 저는 무엇을 알고 싶은 호기심이 전혀 없습니다.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"다만 그 편지를 준 사람에게 도로 돌려주시기를 거듭 청합니다.\"\n\n그러자 밀러 부인은 무릎을 꿇고 더없이 간절한 말로 그녀의 자비를 빌었다. 소피아가 대답했다. \"정말이지, 부인, 부인께서 이 사람을 위해 이토록 강하게 마음을 쓰시는 것이 놀랍군요. 설마 부인이……\" \"아니요, 아가씨.\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"진실 말고는 어떤 것도 생각지 말아 주십시오. 제가 다 말씀드리겠으니, 제가 이렇게 마음을 쓰는 것을 이상히 여기지 않으실 겁니다. 그분은 세상에 태어난 누구보다도 천성이 착한 분이에요.\" 그러고는 앤더슨 씨의 이야기를 들려주기 시작했다. 그 이야기를 마치고 나서 그녀가 외쳤다. \"아가씨, 이것이, 이것이 바로 그분의 선함입니다. 하지만 저는 그분께 그보다 훨씬 더 애틋한 은혜를 입었습니다. 그분이 제 아이를 구해 주셨거든요.\" 그러고는 몇 방울 눈물을 흘린 뒤, 그 일에 관한 모든 것을 들려주었으되, 자기 딸에게 가장 누가 될 만한 정황만은 감추었다. 그리고 이렇게 말로 끝맺었다. \"자, 아가씨, 이토록 친절하고, 이토록 선하고, 이토록 너그러운 젊은이를 위해 제가 아무리 한들 지나치겠습니까. 정말이지 그분은 온 인류 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 값진 분입니다.\"\n\n여태까지 소피아의 얼굴빛 변화는 주로 그녀에게 불리한 쪽이었고, 안색이 너무 창백한 쪽으로 기울었었다. 그러나 이제는, 그럴 수만 있다면, 주홍보다도 더 붉어졌고, 그녀가 외쳤다. \"무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르겠군요. 감사하는 마음에서 우러난 일이라면 분명 나무랄 수 없겠지요. 하지만 제가 이 편지를 읽는 것이 부인의 벗에게 무슨 도움이 되겠습니까. 저는 결코……\" 밀러 부인이 다시금 애원하기 시작했고, 부디 용서해 달라고, 그러나 이것을 도로 가지고 갈 수는 없노라고 말했다. \"그렇다면, 부인,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"부인이 굳이 제게 떠맡기시겠다면 저로서도 어쩔 수 없지요. 제가 받든 안 받든, 부인이 두고 가실 수야 있겠지요.\" 소피아가 무슨 뜻으로 그리 말했는지, 아니 무슨 뜻이 있기나 했는지, 나로서는 감히 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 밀러 부인은 이 말을 하나의 암시로 알아듣고, 곧 편지를 탁자 위에 내려놓은 뒤 작별을 고했으며, 그에 앞서 다시 한번 소피아를 찾아뵙도록 허락해 달라 청했다. 그 청에는 승낙도 거절도 돌아오지 않았다.\n\n편지는 밀러 부인이 시야에서 사라질 때까지만 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다. 부인이 사라지자 소피아는 곧 그것을 열어 읽었으니 말이다.\n\n이 편지는 그의 처지에 그다지 도움이 되지 못했다. 거의 자신이 얼마나 보잘것없는 사람인지에 대한 고백과, 절망에 찬 쓰라린 한탄, 그리고 소피아를 향한 자신의 변치 않는 충정을 더없이 엄숙하게 다짐하는 말로 채워져 있을 뿐이었기 때문이다. 그는 만일 다시 한번 그녀 앞에 나설 영광을 얻는다면 그 충정을 그녀에게 납득시키기를 바란다고 적었고, 벨라스턴 부인에게 보낸 편지에 대해서도 비록 그것이 그녀의 용서를 받을 자격을 그에게 주지는 못하겠으나, 적어도 그녀의 자비로부터 용서만은 얻기를 바란다고 해명할 수 있다고 했다. 그리고 벨라스턴 부인과 결혼하는 것만큼 자기 생각에서 멀리 있는 일은 결코 없었노라 맹세하며 끝맺었다.\n\n소피아는 그 편지를 두 번이나 골똘히 읽었지만, 그의 뜻은 여전히 그녀에게 수수께끼로 남았다. 그녀의 머리로는 존스를 변명해 줄 어떤 방도도 떠오르지 않았다. 그녀는 분명 그에게 몹시 화가 나 있었으나, 실은 벨라스턴 부인이 그녀의 분노를 너무도 크게 차지하고 있어서, 그 온화한 마음에는 다른 사람에게 나눠 줄 분노가 거의 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n그 부인은 하필이면 바로 이날 웨스턴 고모와 함께 식사를 하기로 되어 있었고, 오후에는 약속대로 세 사람이 함께 오페라에 갔다가 거기서 다시 토머스 해칫 부인의 드럼 모임으로 가기로 되어 있었다. 소피아는 이 모든 것에서 기꺼이 빠지고 싶었으나 고모의 비위를 거스르려 하지 않았다. 그리고 병을 꾸며내는 재주로 말하자면, 그녀는 그런 것에 어찌나 생판 낯선 사람이었던지, 그런 생각이 머릿속에 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그리하여 단장을 마치자 그녀는 아래층으로 내려갔으니, 그날의 온갖 끔찍한 일들을 맞닥뜨리기로 마음먹은 것이었다. 그리고 그날은 과연 더없이 불쾌한 하루로 드러났다. 벨라스턴 부인이 기회 있을 때마다 매우 정중하면서도 교활하게 그녀를 모욕했기 때문이다. 그 모든 것에 대해 그녀는 가라앉은 마음 탓에 아무런 대꾸도 하지 못했다. 그리고 사실대로 고백하자면, 그녀는 아무리 잘해야 재치 있는 응수에는 그저 그런 솜씨밖에 지니지 못한 사람이었다.\n\n가엾은 소피아에게 닥친 또 하나의 불운은 펠라마 경과 한자리에 있게 된 것이었다. 그녀는 오페라에서 그를 만났고, 그는 드럼 모임까지 그녀를 따라왔다. 두 곳 다 너무 공개된 자리여서 어떤 사사로운 수작도 끼어들 틈이 없었고, 또 한 곳에서는 음악이, 다른 곳에서는 카드놀이가 그녀를 한결 덜어 주었지만, 그래도 그녀는 그와 함께 있는 자리를 즐길 수가 없었다. 여인에게는 어떤 섬세한 데가 있어서, 자기에게 마음이 있되 그 마음을 받아 줄 뜻이 없는 남자가 곁에 있으면 도무지 편안할 수가 없는 법이니 말이다.\n\n이 장에서 드럼이라는 말을 두 번이나 꺼냈는데, 우리 후손들은 이 말을 여기 쓰인 뜻으로는 부디 이해하지 않게 되기를 바라는 바이므로, 지금 우리가 서두르는 처지이긴 하나 잠시 멈추어 여기서 말하는 그 모임을 설명하고자 한다. 더구나 그것은 순식간에 설명할 수 있는 것이기에 더욱 그렇다.\n\n드럼이란, 그러니까, 잘 차려입은 남녀가 한데 모인 자리로, 그들 대부분은 카드놀이를 하고 나머지는 아무것도 하지 않으며, 그동안 그 집 안주인은 여관 여주인 노릇을 한다. 그리고 여관 여주인이 그러하듯, 손님 수가 많은 것을 자랑으로 여기되, 다만 여관 여주인과 달리 그것으로 늘 무언가를 벌어들이지는 못한다.\n\n그러니 이렇듯 따분한 자리에서 조금이나마 활기를 떠받치려면 그토록 많은 기운이 필요할 수밖에 없는 터라, 멋쟁이들이 기운이 없다고 끝없이 푸념하는 소리를 듣는 것도 놀랄 일이 아니다. 이 푸념은 오로지 상류 사회에만 국한된 것이다. 이 무렵 소피아에게 이 진절머리 나는 따분함의 순환이 얼마나 견디기 힘들었겠는가. 마음속에서는 더없이 애틋한 슬픔밖에 일러 주는 것이 없고, 온갖 생각마다 괴로운 상념으로 가득할 때, 즐거운 표정을 억지로 지어내기가 그녀에게 얼마나 어려웠겠는가!\n\n그러나 마침내 밤이 그녀를 베개로 돌려보내 주었으니, 그곳에 그녀를 남겨 두어 적어도 그 우수만은 달래게 하자. 비록 쉬기는 어려우리라 염려되지만 말이다. 우리는 우리의 이야기를 계속 좇아가려 하거니와, 무언가가 우리에게 속삭이기를, 이제 그 이야기는 어떤 커다란 사건의 전야에 다다랐다고 한다.\n\n올워디 씨와 밀러 부인 사이의 가슴 저린 한 장면.\n\n올워디 씨가 저녁 식사를 마치고 돌아왔을 때, 밀러 부인은 그와 긴 이야기를 나누었다. 그 자리에서 부인은 존스가 헤어질 무렵 올워디 씨가 기꺼이 베풀어 준 그 모든 것을 불운하게도 잃어버린 일이며, 그 상실 때문에 그가 빠지게 된 곤경을 그에게 알렸다. 이 모든 사정은 충실한 소식 전달자 파트리지에게서 낱낱이 전해 들은 것이었다. 이어서 부인은 자신이 존스에게 진 신세를 설명했다. 다만 자기 딸과 관련해서는 모든 것을 다 털어놓지는 않았다. 부인은 올워디 씨를 더없이 신뢰하고 있었고, 또 이미 여섯 사람도 넘는 이들이 안타깝게도 알고 있는 일을 비밀로 지킬 가망은 없었지만, 그래도 가엾은 낸시의 정절에 가장 누가 될 만한 그 정황만큼은 차마 입에 올릴 수가 없었던 것이다. 부인은 마치 판사 앞에 선 양, 그래서 그 딸이 지금 사생아 살해죄로 재판을 받고 있기라도 한 양, 자기 증언의 그 부분을 조심스레 덮어 두었다.\n\n올워디는 말하기를, 인간의 성품 가운데 선이라곤 티끌만큼도 섞이지 않은, 그토록 철저하게 악한 성품은 좀처럼 없다고 했다. \"하지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"그자가 아무리 나쁜 사람이라 해도, 부인이 그에게 어느 정도 신세를 졌다는 것만은 부인할 수 없겠지요. 그러니 이미 지나간 일은 너그러이 보아 드리겠습니다. 다만 다시는 내 앞에서 그의 이름을 입에 올리지 않으시기를 분명히 청합니다. 내가 지금까지 취한 조치들을 단행하기로 마음먹은 것은, 정말이지 더없이 충분하고 명백한 증거에 따른 것이었으니까요.\" \"글쎄요, 어르신,\" 부인이 말했다. \"저는 조금도 의심하지 않습니다. 세월이 흐르면 모든 일이 본디의 참된 빛깔을 드러낼 것이고, 이 가엾은 젊은이가 이름을 밝히지 않겠습니다만 어떤 다른 사람들보다 어르신께 훨씬 더 어울리는 사람이었음을 어르신께서도 납득하시게 되리라고요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 올워디가 약간 언짢아진 목소리로 외쳤다. \"내 조카를 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하는 말은 듣지 않겠습니다. 그런 말을 한 마디라도 더 하신다면, 나는 그 즉시 이 집을 떠나겠습니다. 그 아이는 더없이 훌륭하고 가장 선량한 사람입니다. 다시 한번 말씀드리지만, 그 아이는 가장 시커먼 사실들을 너무 오래 숨김으로써, 이 사내에 대한 우정을 나무랄 만한 지경까지 끌고 갔습니다. 이 못난 자가 그 착한 젊은이에게 보인 배은망덕이야말로 내가 가장 분개하는 바입니다. 부인, 나로서는 그자가 내 호의 속에서 조카의 자리를 빼앗고 그 아이의 상속권마저 박탈하려는 음모를 꾸몄다고 짐작할 만한 더없이 큰 이유가 있으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"확실히 말씀드리지만, 어르신,\" 밀러 부인이 조금 겁먹은 채 대답했다(올워디 씨는 미소 지을 때면 더없이 다정하고 자애로웠으나, 얼굴을 찌푸릴 때면 그 안에 큰 위엄이 서려 있었기 때문이다). \"어르신께서 좋게 보아 주시는 어느 신사분께든 저는 결코 거스르는 말을 하지 않을 것입니다. 정말이지, 어르신, 그런 처신은 제게 도무지 어울리지 않을 테지요. 더구나 그 신사분이 어르신의 가장 가까운 친척이시니 말입니다. 하지만, 어르신, 이 가엾은 못난이에게 제가 잘되기를 비는 마음만으로 저를 노여워하시면 안 됩니다. 정말로 그러시면 안 됩니다. 한때는 제가 그를 조금이라도 함부로 말하면 어르신께서 노여워하셨겠지만, 이제는 그렇게 불러도 되겠지요. 어르신께서 그를 아드님이라 부르시는 것을 제가 얼마나 자주 들었던가요? 어버이의 온갖 애틋함을 담아 그에 대해 제게 조잘조잘 이야기하시던 적이 또 얼마나 잦았던가요? 아니, 어르신, 저는 잊을 수가 없습니다. 그의 고운 용모며 재주며 덕에 대해, 또 그의 선한 성품과 너그러움에 대해 어르신께서 제게 들려주신 그 숱한 다정한 말씀과 그 많은 좋은 이야기들을 말입니다. 정말이지, 어르신, 저는 그것들을 잊을 수가 없습니다. 그 모두가 사실임을 제가 깨달았으니까요. 저는 제 일을 통해 그것들을 직접 겪었습니다. 그가 제 가족을 지켜 주었습니다. 제 눈물을 용서해 주십시오, 어르신, 정말로 용서해 주셔야 합니다. 제가 그토록 큰 신세를 진 이 가엾은 젊은이가 겪은 그 모진 운명의 뒤바뀜을 생각하면, 또 그가 제 목숨보다 더 소중히 여겼던 어르신의 호의를 잃은 것을 생각하면, 저는, 저는 그를 안타까워하지 않을 수가 없습니다. 설령 어르신께서 손에 단검을 쥐고 제 심장을 찌르려 하신다 해도, 저는 어르신께서 한때 사랑하셨고 제가 앞으로도 늘 사랑할 그 사람의 불행을 안타까워하지 않을 수 없을 것입니다.\"\n\n올워디는 이 말에 자못 마음이 움직였으나, 그것은 노여움 때문은 아닌 듯했다. 잠시 침묵하던 그는 밀러 부인의 손을 잡으며 더없이 다정하게 말했다. \"자, 부인, 우리 따님 일을 조금 헤아려 봅시다. 따님에게 이로울 듯한 혼사를 두고 부인이 기뻐하시는 것을 나무랄 수야 없지요. 하지만 부인도 아시다시피, 그 이로움은 상당 부분 아버지가 마음을 풀고 받아들이느냐에 달려 있습니다. 나는 나이팅게일 씨를 아주 잘 알고, 예전에 그와 거래한 일도 있습니다. 내가 그를 한번 찾아가, 이 일에서 부인을 도와 드리도록 애써 보겠습니다. 내 생각에 그는 세속적인 사람입니다. 그러나 외아들인 데다 이제는 돌이킬 수 없게 된 일이니, 어쩌면 시간이 지나면 이치를 받아들이게 될지도 모르지요. 부인을 위해 내가 할 수 있는 일은 다 하겠다고 약속드립니다.\"\n\n이 친절하고 너그러운 제안에 가엾은 부인이 올워디에게 건넨 감사의 말은 한두 마디가 아니었다. 또한 부인은 이 기회를 빌려 다시금 존스에 대한 고마움을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다. \"그분 덕분에,\" 부인이 말했다. \"지금 어르신께 이런 폐를 끼칠 기회를 얻은 것입니다.\" 올워디는 부드럽게 그 말을 막았다. 그러나 그는 지금 밀러 부인을 움직이는 그토록 고귀한 마음씨의 발로에 정말로 노여워하기에는 너무도 선량한 사람이었다. 사실, 이번의 새로운 일이 존스를 향한 그의 묵은 노여움에 다시 불을 지피지만 않았던들, 아무리 악의라 해도 결코 나쁜 동기에서 비롯되었다고 둘러댈 수 없는 그 선행의 이야기를 듣고, 그는 어쩌면 그에게 마음이 조금은 누그러졌을지도 모른다.\n\n올워디 씨와 밀러 부인이 한 시간 넘게 함께한 무렵, 그들의 대화는 블리필과 또 한 사람이 들이닥치는 바람에 끝나 버렸다. 그 또 한 사람이란 다름 아닌 변호사 다울링 씨였다. 그는 이제 블리필 씨의 큰 총애를 받는 인물이 되어 있었고, 올워디 씨가 조카의 바람대로 그를 자기 집사로 삼은 터였다. 또한 올워디 씨는 그를 웨스턴 씨에게도 천거하였는데, 다울링은 웨스턴 씨로부터 첫 자리가 비는 대로 같은 직책에 올려 주겠다는 약속을 받아 둔 상태였다. 그 사이에 그는 향사가 당시 런던에서 안고 있던 어떤 저당 관련 일을 처리하는 데 부려지고 있었다.\n\n이것이 바로 그때 다울링 씨를 런던으로 불러올린 주된 용건이었다. 그래서 그는 이 기회에 올워디 씨에게 전할 돈도 맡아 오고, 또 몇 가지 다른 일을 그에게 보고하기로 했다. 그 모든 일은 이 이야기에 자리할 만한 곳을 찾기에는 너무도 따분한 성격의 것이니, 우리는 이 일에 매인 숙부와 조카, 그리고 그들의 변호사를 그대로 두고 다른 이야기로 옮겨 가기로 하자.\n\n여러 가지 일들을 담은 장.\n\n존스 씨에게로 돌아가기에 앞서, 우리는 소피아를 한 번 더 들여다보기로 하자.\n\n비록 이 젊은 아가씨가 앞서 이야기한 그 부드러운 방법들로 고모의 기분을 무척 좋게 만들어 놓기는 했으나, 펠라마 경과의 혼사에 대한 고모의 열의만큼은 조금도 누그러뜨리지 못했다. 이 열의는 이제 벨라스턴 부인에 의해 더욱 활활 타올랐다. 부인은 그 전날 저녁 고모에게, 소피아의 행동과 그녀가 경을 대하는 태도를 보건대 어떤 지체도 위험할 것이라 충분히 확신한다고, 그리고 성공할 유일한 길은 그 젊은 아가씨가 곰곰이 생각할 틈조차 없도록 혼사를 빠르게 밀어붙여, 자기가 무슨 일을 하는지도 거의 모르는 사이에 승낙하지 않을 수 없게 만드는 것이라고 말했던 것이다. 부인의 말로는, 지체 있는 사람들 사이에서 이루어지는 혼인의 절반이 이런 식으로 성사된다고 했다. 아마도 사실일 법한 이야기이며, 짐작건대 그토록 많은 행복한 부부 사이에 뒷날 깃드는 그 서로에 대한 애틋함도 여기서 비롯되는 것이리라.\n\n같은 부류의 귀띔이 같은 부인에게서 펠라마 경에게도 건네졌다. 그리고 두 사람 모두 이 조언을 어찌나 기꺼이 받아들였던지, 경의 청에 따라 웨스턴 부인은 바로 다음 날을 두 젊은이의 단둘이만의 만남을 위한 날로 정했다. 이 일은 고모를 통해 소피아에게 전해졌고, 어찌나 단호한 말투로 강요되었던지, 소피아는 자기가 지어낼 수 있는 온갖 핑계를 다 들이대고도 조금의 효과조차 보지 못한 끝에, 결국 어떤 젊은 아가씨라도 보일 수 있는 최고의 순응을 보이는 데 동의하여, 경을 만나는 것을 승낙하고 말았다.\n\n이런 종류의 대화는 그리 큰 즐거움을 주지 못하니, 이 만남에서 오간 말을 전부 옮기는 일은 부디 면하게 해 주기를 바란다. 그 자리에서 경이 말없이 얼굴을 붉히는 소피아에게 더없이 순수하고 뜨거운 열정을 거듭 고백한 끝에, 소피아는 마침내 끌어올릴 수 있는 모든 기운을 그러모아 떨리는 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"경이시여, 예전에 저를 대하시던 행동이 지금 하시는 그 고백과 과연 부합하는지는 경 스스로가 잘 아시겠지요.\" \"제 광기를 속죄할 길이 정녕 없겠습니까?\" 그가 대답했다. \"제가 한 짓은, 사랑의 격정이 저에게서 분별을 앗아 갔음을 너무도 명백히 증명해 보였을까 두렵습니다.\" \"참으로, 경이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 훨씬 더 북돋우고 싶고, 또 거기에 더 큰 신세를 졌다고 여기고 싶은 그런 애정의 증거를 주시는 것은 경의 능력 안에 있습니다.\" \"말씀해 주십시오, 아가씨.\" 경이 무척 뜨겁게 말했다. \"경이시여,\" 그녀가 부채를 내려다보며 말했다. \"경께서도 이 거짓 열정이 저를 얼마나 괴롭혀 왔는지 틀림없이 느끼고 계실 것입니다.\" \"어찌 그토록 모질게 그것을 거짓이라 부르실 수 있습니까?\" 그가 말했다. \"그렇습니다, 경이시여,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"우리가 괴롭히는 이에게 바치는 사랑의 고백이란 모두 더없이 모욕적인 거짓일 뿐입니다. 경의 이 추적은 제게 더없이 잔인한 박해입니다. 아니, 그것은 저의 불행한 처지를 더없이 야박하게 이용하는 일입니다.\" \"더없이 사랑스럽고, 더없이 흠모스러운 매혹의 그대여, 저를 탓하지 마십시오.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"제 마음에는 오로지 그대의 명예와 이익으로만 향한 생각뿐이고, 저 자신과 명예와 재산과 모든 것을 그대의 발치에 던지는 것 외에는 어떤 속셈도, 어떤 희망도, 어떤 야심도 없는데, 제가 야박하게 이용한다고 탓하시다니요.\" \"경이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 한탄하는 그 우월함을 경께 안겨 준 것이 바로 그 재산과 그 명예입니다. 그것들은 제 친척들을 홀린 매력일 뿐, 제게는 아무래도 좋은 것들입니다. 경께서 정녕 제 감사를 받을 자격을 얻고자 하신다면, 길은 단 하나뿐입니다.\" \"용서하십시오, 신성한 그대여,\" 그가 말했다. \"그런 길은 있을 수 없습니다. 제가 그대를 위해 할 수 있는 모든 일은 마땅히 그대가 받아야 할 것이며, 제게는 너무도 큰 기쁨을 주는 일이니, 거기에 그대의 감사가 끼어들 자리란 없습니다.\" \"참으로, 경이시여,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"경께서는 제 감사를, 제 호의를, 제가 베풀 수 있는 온갖 다정한 생각과 바람을 얻으실 수 있습니다. 아니, 손쉽게 얻으실 수 있습니다. 너그러운 마음을 지닌 분이라면 제 청을 들어주는 일쯤은 분명 쉬울 테니까요. 그러니 부디 간청하건대, 결코 성공할 수 없는 이 추적을 그만두어 주십시오. 경 자신을 위해서도, 또 저를 위해서도 저는 이 호의를 애원합니다. 분명 경께서는 너무도 고귀하신 분이라, 한 불행한 사람을 괴롭히는 데서 어떤 기쁨도 느끼지 않으실 테니까요. 경께서 저를 어떤 곤경으로 내모신다 해도, 제 명예를 걸고, 제 영혼을 걸고, 경의 뜻이 저를 움직일 수 없고, 또 움직이게 두지도 않겠다는데, 이런 고집으로 경께서 스스로의 괴로움 말고 무엇을 얻으려 하십니까?\" 여기서 경은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬더니 말했다. \"그렇다면, 아가씨, 제가 그토록 불행하게도 그대의 혐오와 경멸의 대상이 되었다는 말씀입니까. 아니면 다른 누군가가 있지 않은가 의심하더라도 용서해 주시겠습니까?\" 여기서 그가 머뭇거리자, 소피아는 다소 기개 있게 대답했다. \"경이시여, 저는 제 행동의 까닭을 경께 일일이 해명할 의무가 없습니다. 경께서 베풀어 주신 너그러운 청혼에 대해서는 감사드립니다. 그것이 제 자격이나 기대를 넘어서는 것임은 인정합니다. 그러나 경이시여, 제가 받아들일 수 없노라 밝히는 마당에, 부디 그 까닭만은 캐묻지 말아 주시기를 바랍니다.\" 펠라마 경은 이에 많은 말을 되돌려 주었으나, 우리는 그것을 온전히 이해하지 못하겠고, 어쩌면 그 전부가 사리에든 문법에든 엄밀히 들어맞지도 않았을 것이다. 다만 그는 장광설을 늘어놓은 끝에 이렇게 맺었다. \"만일 그대가 어느 신사분께 이미 마음을 약속해 두었다면, 그것이 저를 아무리 불행하게 만든다 해도, 저는 명예를 걸고 물러나는 것이 마땅하다 여기겠습니다.\" 어쩌면 경이 신사라는 말에 지나치게 힘을 주었는지도 모른다. 그렇지 않고서는 그가 소피아에게 불러일으킨 분노를 달리 설명하기 어렵기 때문이다. 소피아는 그 대답에서, 그가 자신에게 안긴 어떤 모욕에 크게 분개하는 듯이 보였다.\n\n그녀가 평소보다 높아진 목소리로 말하고 있을 때, 웨스턴 부인이 두 뺨에 불길이 이글거리고 두 눈에서 불꽃이 터져 나오는 듯한 모습으로 방으로 들어왔다. \"경이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"경께서 받으신 이 대접이 부끄럽기 짝이 없습니다. 분명히 말씀드리지만 저희 모두 경께서 베풀어 주신 영광을 잘 알고 있습니다. 그리고 너에게 말해 두겠는데, 웨스턴 양, 집안은 너에게 이와는 다른 행동을 기대하고 있다.\" 여기서 경이 아가씨를 위해 끼어들었으나 아무 소용이 없었다. 고모는 계속 몰아붙였고, 마침내 소피아는 손수건을 꺼내 들고 의자에 몸을 던지더니 격렬한 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다.\n\n웨스턴 부인과 경 사이에 오간 나머지 대화는, 경이 물러갈 때까지, 그의 쪽에서는 쓰라린 한탄으로, 부인의 쪽에서는 조카딸이 그가 바라는 모든 것에 반드시 동의할 것이고 또 동의하게 되리라는 더없이 강한 다짐으로 이루어졌다. \"참으로, 경이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저 애는 어리석은 교육을 받았답니다. 제 재산에도, 제 집안에도 어울리지 않는 교육이지요. 유감스럽게도 그 애 아비가 모든 일의 잘못입니다. 그 애는 수줍음에 관한 어리석은 시골뜨기 생각을 품고 있어요. 다른 건 없습니다, 경이시여, 제 명예를 걸고요. 저는 그 애가 속으로는 좋은 분별을 지니고 있어서 곧 이치를 깨닫게 되리라 확신합니다.\"\n\n이 마지막 말은 소피아가 없는 자리에서 한 것이었다. 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 격한 감정을 드러내며 얼마 전에 방을 나가 버렸기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 경은 웨스턴 부인에게 거듭 감사의 말을 전하고, 무엇으로도 꺾을 수 없는 열정을 거듭 뜨겁게 고백하며, 끝까지 밀고 나가겠노라 거듭 다짐한 뒤―웨스턴 부인은 이를 한껏 부추겼다―이번에는 작별을 고했다.\n\n이제 웨스턴 부인과 소피아 사이에 오간 일을 이야기하기에 앞서, 한 가지 불운한 사건이 일어났음을 짚고 넘어가는 것이 마땅하겠다. 바로 그 일 때문에 우리가 본 대로 웨스턴 부인이 그토록 노발대발하며 돌아왔던 것이다.\n\n그러니 독자는 알아 두어야 한다. 지금 소피아의 시중을 들고 있는 하녀는 벨라스턴 부인이 천거한 사람으로, 한동안 부인의 곁에서 머리 손질하는 일을 맡아 살았던 처녀였다. 그녀는 무척 영리한 처녀였고, 자기가 모시는 젊은 아가씨를 아주 면밀히 감시하라는 더없이 엄한 지시를 받은 터였다. 유감스럽게도 이 지시는 어너 부인을 통해 그녀에게 전해진 것이었다. 벨라스턴 부인이 이제 어너 부인의 환심을 어찌나 잘 사 두었던지, 이 충직한 시녀가 예전에 소피아에게 품었던 그 격렬한 애정은 새 마님에게 쏟는 그 큰 애착에 완전히 지워져 버렸다.\n\n자, 밀러 부인이 떠나고 나자, 베티(그 처녀의 이름이 그러했다)가 자기 아가씨에게 돌아와 보니, 아가씨는 긴 편지 한 통을 무척 골똘히 읽고 있었다. 그때 아가씨가 내비친 뚜렷한 동요는 이 처녀가 품은 어떤 의심을 충분히 설명해 줄 만한 것이었으나, 실은 그 의심에는 더 단단한 근거가 있었다. 베티는 소피아와 밀러 부인 사이에 오간 장면 전부를 엿들었던 것이다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 베티에게서 이 모든 일을 전해 들었다. 베티는 그 충직함에 대해 많은 칭찬과 약간의 보상을 받은 뒤, 만일 편지를 가져온 그 여자가 다시 오거든 웨스턴 부인 본인에게 직접 데려오라는 명을 받았다.\n\n공교롭게도 밀러 부인은 소피아가 경과 함께 있던 바로 그때 다시 찾아왔다. 베티는 명령대로 그녀를 곧장 고모에게 안내했다. 전날 일어난 일에 관한 여러 정황을 꿰고 있던 고모는, 소피아가 그 일을 전부 자기에게 털어놓았다고 이 가엾은 여자가 믿도록 손쉽게 속여 넘겼고, 그리하여 편지에 관해서나 존스에 관해서나 그녀가 아는 모든 것을 죄다 캐냈다.\n\n이 가엾은 여자는 실로 순진함 그 자체라 불러도 좋을 만했다. 그녀는 남이 하는 말이라면 무엇이든 곧이곧대로 믿는 부류의 인간이었고, 자연이 기만의 공격용 무기도 방어용 무기도 베풀어 주지 않은 사람이었으며, 따라서 그 목적을 위해 약간의 거짓말을 들이는 수고만 마다하지 않는 자라면 누구에게든 쉽게 속아 넘어가기 마련인 사람이었다. 웨스턴 부인은 밀러 부인이 아는 것을 죄다 짜냈는데, 그것은 실로 얼마 되지 않았으나 고모로 하여금 많은 것을 의심하게 만들기에는 충분했다. 부인은 소피아가 그녀를 만나지 않을 것이며, 편지에 아무 답장도 보내지 않을 것이고, 앞으로도 결코 다른 편지를 받지 않을 것이라 단언하며 그녀를 돌려보냈다. 게다가 그녀를 그냥 보내지도 않고, 뚜쟁이라는 말 외에는 달리 더 나은 이름을 붙여 줄 수 없는 그 노릇의 됨됨이에 대해 한바탕 그럴듯한 훈계를 늘어놓았다. 이 발각은 부인의 심기를 크게 어지럽혀 놓았는데, 그러던 차에 연인들이 있는 방의 바로 옆방으로 들어서다가, 소피아가 경의 구애에 무척 격렬하게 항의하는 소리를 엿듣게 되었던 것이다. 그러자 이미 지펴진 분노가 활활 타올랐고, 부인은 우리가 이미 묘사한 대로 더없이 노발대발한 기세로 조카딸에게 달려들었으며, 그 뒤로 경이 떠날 때까지 벌어진 일도 앞서 본 그대로였다.\n\n펠라마 경이 떠나기가 무섭게 웨스턴 부인은 소피아에게로 돌아왔다. 부인은 자기가 베푼 신뢰를 그 애가 못되게 써먹었다며, 또 바로 전날 결코 다시는 어떤 말도 섞지 않겠노라 가장 엄숙한 맹세로 스스로를 묶겠다고까지 했던 그 사내와 이야기를 나눈 배신을 두고 더없이 쓰라린 말로 그녀를 나무랐다. 소피아는 그런 대화를 나눈 적이 없다고 항변했다. \"뭐라고, 뭐라고! 웨스턴 양,\" 고모가 말했다. \"어제 그자에게서 편지를 받은 일을 부인하겠다는 거냐?\" \"편지라뇨, 고모님!\" 소피아가 다소 놀라며 대답했다. \"내 말을 따라 되뇌는 건 그리 점잖은 짓이 아니다, 얘야.\" 고모가 받아쳤다. \"편지라고 했다. 그리고 당장 그걸 내게 보여 주기를 요구한다.\" \"저는 거짓말을 경멸합니다, 고모님.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"편지를 받은 것은 사실입니다. 하지만 그건 제가 바란 일도 아니었고, 정말이지 제 뜻을 거슬러 받은 것이라 해도 좋을 것입니다.\" \"정말이지, 정말이지, 얘야,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"그걸 받았다는 사실을 인정하는 것만으로도 부끄러워해야 마땅하다. 한데 그 편지는 어디 있느냐? 내가 봐야겠다.\"\n\n이 단호한 요구에 소피아는 한동안 말을 멈추었다가 대답을 내놓았는데, 결국에는 편지를 주머니에 지니고 있지 않다고 밝히는 것으로 변명을 대신했다. 사실 그것은 참말이었다. 그러자 고모는 온갖 인내심을 잃고 조카딸에게 이 짤막한 질문을 던졌다. 펠라마 경과 혼인할 결심이 섰느냐, 아니냐? 이에 부인은 더없이 단호한 부정의 대답을 들었다. 그러자 웨스턴 부인은 맹세, 혹은 거기에 무척 가까운 무엇과 함께, 다음 날 아침 일찍 너를 도로 네 아비 손에 넘겨주겠다고 받아쳤다.\n\n그러자 소피아는 다음과 같이 고모에게 따지기 시작했다. \"어찌하여, 고모님, 저는 기어이 혼인을 강요당해야만 하는 건가요? 고모님 자신의 일이었다면 그것을 얼마나 잔인하다 여기셨을지, 또 고모님을 자유롭게 내버려 두셨던 부모님이 얼마나 더 다정하셨을지 생각해 보세요. 제가 이 자유를 잃을 만한 무슨 짓을 했나요? 저는 결코 아버지의 승낙을 거슬러 혼인하지 않을 것이고, 고모님의 승낙을 구하지 않고서도 하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 제가 어느 한 분의 승낙이라도 부당하게 구하게 되거든, 그때 가서 제게 다른 혼인을 강요하셔도 늦지 않을 거예요.\" \"지금 주머니에 살인자가 보낸 편지를 지닌 계집애한테서,\" 웨스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"내가 이런 소릴 들어야 한단 말이냐?\" \"그런 편지는 지니고 있지 않아요, 약속드려요.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"그리고 그분이 살인자라면, 곧 고모님께 더 이상의 성가심을 끼칠 처지가 못 될 거예요.\" \"뭐라고, 웨스턴 양!\" 고모가 말했다. \"네가 감히 그자를 두고 이런 식으로 말하다니, 내 면전에서 그런 악당에게 품은 애정을 인정하다니?\" \"분명, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"고모님께서는 제 말을 무척 이상하게 해석하시는군요.\" \"정말이지, 웨스턴 양,\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"나는 이런 대접을 참지 않겠다. 너는 네 아비한테서 나를 이렇게 다루는 법을 배웠어. 그자가 너에게 나더러 거짓말쟁이라 하는 법을 가르친 게다. 그자는 이 그릇된 교육 방식으로 너를 완전히 망쳐 놓았어. 하느님의 뜻이라면, 그자는 그 열매가 주는 위안을 톡톡히 맛보게 될 게다. 다시 한 번 너에게 선언하건대, 내일 아침 나는 너를 도로 데려가겠다. 나는 전장에서 내 모든 병력을 거두어, 이제부터는 저 현명한 프로이센 왕처럼 완전한 중립 상태에 머물겠다. 너희 둘 다 내 방책에 따르기엔 너무도 영리하니 말이다. 그러니 채비를 해 두어라. 내일 아침 너는 이 집을 비워야 할 테니까.\"\n\n소피아는 할 수 있는 한 온갖 항변을 했지만, 고모는 그녀가 하는 말에 도무지 귀를 기울이지 않았다. 따라서 우리는 지금으로서는 그녀를 이 결심 속에 그대로 남겨 둘 수밖에 없다. 그 결심을 바꾸게 할 가망이라곤 없어 보이니 말이다.\n\n감옥에서 존스에게 일어난 일.\n\n존스는 나이팅게일이 돌아오기 전까지 스물네 시간 남짓을 홀로 우울하게 보냈다. 그나마 파트리지가 곁에 있어 줄 때만 그 외로움을 덜 수 있었다. 그렇다고 이 갸륵한 젊은이가 친구를 저버렸거나 잊은 것은 아니었다. 사실 그는 그 시간의 거의 대부분을 친구를 위한 일에 쏟고 있었다.\n\n그는 수소문 끝에, 그 불행한 충돌의 시작을 목격한 유일한 사람들이 그때 뎁트퍼드에 정박해 있던 군함 소속 선원들이라는 사실을 알아냈다. 그리하여 그는 그 선원들을 찾아 뎁트퍼드로 갔으나, 거기서 찾는 자들이 모두 뭍으로 올라갔다는 말을 들었다. 그는 이곳저곳으로 그들의 자취를 좇았고, 마침내 올더스게이트 근처의 어느 허름한 선술집에서 그들 가운데 둘이 또 다른 사람 하나와 함께 술을 마시고 있는 것을 찾아냈다.\n\n나이팅게일은 존스와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶다고 했다(그가 들어왔을 때 방 안에 파트리지가 있었기 때문이다). 둘만 남게 되자 나이팅게일은 존스의 손을 잡고 외쳤다. \"이보게, 나의 용감한 친구, 내가 이제부터 하려는 말에 너무 낙담하지 말게. 나쁜 소식을 전하는 사람이 나라는 게 미안하네. 그러나 자네에게 알리는 것이 내 도리라고 생각하네.\" \"그 나쁜 소식이 뭔지 벌써 짐작이 가네.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그 가엾은 신사가 죽었군.\" \"아니길 바라네.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"오늘 아침까지는 살아 있었네. 하지만 자네에게 듣기 좋은 말만 하지는 않겠네. 내가 알아낸 바로는 그의 상처가 치명적인 것 같아 두렵네. 그러나 일이 자네가 말한 그대로라면, 무슨 일이 일어나든 자네가 걱정해야 할 것은 자네 자신의 양심의 가책뿐일 걸세. 다만 용서하게, 나의 친애하는 톰, 친구들에게는 자네 이야기를 가장 나쁜 쪽으로 들려주라고 간청하는 것을 말일세. 만일 자네가 우리에게 무언가를 숨긴다면, 그것은 결국 자네 자신의 적이 되는 길일 뿐이라네.\"\n\n\"나의 친애하는 잭, 내가 언제 자네에게 그토록 잔인한 의심으로 나를 찌를 빌미를 준 적이 있던가?\" 존스가 말했다. \"참아 보게.\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"그러면 내가 다 말해 주겠네. 내가 할 수 있는 가장 부지런한 수소문 끝에, 마침내 이 불행한 사건 현장에 있었던 자들 가운데 둘을 만났네. 그런데 말하기 안타깝지만, 그들은 자네가 직접 들려준 것만큼 자네에게 유리하게 이야기를 전하지 않더군.\" \"아니, 그들이 뭐라고 하던가?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"사실, 자네에게 미칠 결과가 두려워 되풀이하기가 안타까운 말이네. 그들은 자기들이 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어서 자네들 사이에 오간 말은 한마디도 듣지 못했다고 하네. 그러나 둘 다 첫 일격을 가한 사람은 자네라고 입을 모으더군.\" \"그렇다면, 내 영혼을 걸고 말하지.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"그들은 나를 모함하는 걸세. 그는 나를 먼저 쳤을 뿐 아니라, 조금의 도발도 없는데 나를 친 거야. 무엇이 그 악당들로 하여금 나를 거짓으로 고발하게 만든단 말인가?\" \"글쎄, 그건 나도 짐작이 안 가네.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"그리고 자네 자신도, 그리고 진심으로 자네 편인 나도 그들이 왜 자네를 거짓으로 헐뜯어야 하는지 그 이유를 떠올리지 못한다면, 이해관계 없는 법정인들 그들의 말을 믿지 말아야 할 이유를 무엇으로 댈 수 있겠나? 나는 그 질문을 그들에게 여러 차례 되풀이했고, 그 자리에 있던 다른 신사 한 사람도 그렇게 했네. 내가 보기에 그는 뱃사람인 듯한데, 정말로 자네에게 더없이 우호적으로 행동해 주었네. 그는 이 일에 한 사람의 목숨이 걸려 있다는 것을 거듭 생각해 보라고 그들에게 간청했고, 확실하냐고 몇 번이나 물었네. 그런데 둘 다 확실하다고, 맹세하고서라도 자기들 증언을 지키겠다고 대답하더군. 제발, 나의 친애하는 친구여, 정신을 가다듬게. 만일 이것이 사실로 드러난다면, 자네는 때를 놓치지 말고 자네에게 이로운 방도를 최대한 마련할 궁리를 해야 할 걸세. 자네를 충격에 빠뜨리고 싶지는 않네. 그러나 자네에게 어떤 말로 된 도발이 있었든 간에, 법이 얼마나 가혹한지는 자네도 알고 있으리라 믿네.\" \"아아! 나의 친구여,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"나 같은 가련한 자에게 무슨 이로운 방도가 있겠나? 게다가, 내가 살인자라는 평판을 안고 살기를 바라기라도 할 것 같은가? 설령 내게 친구가 있다 한들(아아! 내겐 아무도 없지만), 인간 본성 가운데 가장 시커먼 죄로 단죄받은 사람을 위해 변호해 달라고 그들에게 청할 염치가 내게 있겠나? 믿어 주게, 내겐 그런 희망이 없네. 다만 나는 훨씬 더 높은 어떤 보좌에 약간의 의지를 두고 있을 뿐일세. 그분께서는, 분명히, 내가 받아 마땅한 모든 보호를 베풀어 주실 걸세.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 처음에 단언했던 것이 진실이라며, 엄숙하고 격렬한 항변을 거듭하는 것으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n흔들렸던 나이팅게일의 믿음은 이제 다시 흔들려, 친구의 말을 믿는 쪽으로 기울기 시작했다. 그때 밀러 부인이 나타나 자신이 맡았던 임무의 결과를 슬픈 어조로 전했다. 그 말을 들은 존스는 더없이 비장하게 외쳤다. \"좋소, 나의 친구여, 이제 나는 무슨 일이 일어나든 개의치 않겠소. 적어도 내 목숨에 관해서는 말이오. 그리고 내가 흘린 피를 내 목숨으로 속죄하는 것이 하늘의 뜻이라면, 신의 선하심이 언젠가 내 명예가 씻기는 것을 허락해 주시기를, 그리하여 적어도 죽어 가는 자의 말이, 그의 인품을 정당하게 밝혀 줄 만큼은 믿어지기를 바라오.\"\n\n이제 죄수와 그의 친구들 사이에 매우 애통한 장면이 펼쳐졌다. 그 자리에 함께 있고 싶어할 독자가 거의 없을 것이듯, 그것을 자세히 듣고 싶어할 독자도 거의 없으리라 믿는다. 그러니 우리는 간수가 들어오는 대목으로 넘어가기로 하자. 그는 존스에게, 밖에 한 부인이 와 있는데 그가 한가할 때 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 알렸다.\n\n존스는 이 전갈에 놀라움을 드러냈다. 그는 말했다. \"세상에 이런 곳에서 만날 거라고 기대할 만한 부인은 아무도 없는데.\" 그러나 어떤 사람이든 만나기를 거절할 이유가 없었으므로, 밀러 부인과 나이팅게일은 곧 작별을 고했고, 그는 그 부인을 들여보내라고 일렀다.\n\n존스가 한 부인의 방문 소식에 놀랐다면, 그 부인이 다름 아닌 워터스 부인임을 알았을 때 그가 얼마나 경악했겠는가! 그러니 우리는 그를 잠시 그 경악 속에 남겨 두고, 이 부인의 등장에 마찬가지로 적잖이 의아해할 독자의 놀라움부터 풀어 주기로 하자.\n\n이 워터스 부인이 누구인지는 독자가 제법 잘 알고 있다. 그녀가 어떤 사람인지에 대해서도 독자는 충분히 알고 있을 것이다. 그러니 부디 기억해 주기 바란다. 이 부인은 피츠패트릭 씨와 또 다른 아일랜드 신사와 같은 마차를 타고 업튼을 떠났고, 그들과 함께 배스까지 여행했다는 것을.\n\n그런데 그 무렵 피츠패트릭 씨가 내려 줄 수 있는 어떤 자리 하나가 비어 있었으니, 바로 아내라는 자리였다. 얼마 전까지 그 자리를 채우고 있던 부인이 사임했거나, 적어도 자신의 임무를 저버렸기 때문이다. 그리하여 피츠패트릭 씨는 길에서 워터스 부인을 철저히 살펴본 끝에 그녀가 그 자리에 더없이 적합하다는 것을 알게 되었고, 배스에 도착하자마자 곧 그 자리를 그녀에게 내주었으며, 그녀도 아무 거리낌 없이 받아들였다. 이 신사와 부인은 배스에 머무는 내내 부부로서 함께 지냈고, 부부로서 함께 도시에 도착했다.\n\n피츠패트릭 씨가 다른 하나를 확보하기 전에는 좋은 것 하나를 놓지 않을 만큼 영리한 사람이어서—지금으로서는 그 다른 하나를 되찾을 가망만 있었으니—그런 것인지, 아니면 워터스 부인이 그 직무를 어찌나 잘 수행했는지 그가 여전히 그녀를 정처로 두고, (흔히 그러하듯) 자기 아내는 그저 그녀의 대리쯤으로 삼으려 했던 것인지는 내가 말하지 않겠다. 그러나 분명한 것은, 그가 그녀에게 아내 이야기를 한 번도 꺼내지 않았고, 웨스턴 부인이 그에게 준 편지를 그녀에게 알리지도 않았으며, 아내를 되찾으려는 자신의 속셈을 단 한 번도 내비치지 않았다는 사실이다. 하물며 존스의 이름은 입에 올린 적조차 없었다. 왜냐하면 그는 어디서든 존스를 만나면 결투를 벌일 작정이었지만, 아내든 어머니든 누이든, 때로는 온 가족이든 이런 경우에 가장 안전한 결투 입회인이라고 여기는 그런 신중한 사람들을 본받지는 않았기 때문이다. 그러므로 그녀가 이 모든 일에 대해 처음 들은 이야기는, 그가 상처를 치료받았던 선술집에서 집으로 실려 온 뒤 그의 입에서 나온 것이었다.\n\n그러나 피츠패트릭 씨는 평소에도 이야기를 그다지 또렷하게 풀어내는 사람이 아니었고, 이번에는 어쩌면 평소보다 조금 더 정신이 혼미했던 터라, 그에게 이 상처를 입힌 신사가 다름 아닌 그녀의 마음에 상처를 입힌 바로 그 사람이라는 것을 그녀가 알아차리기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 그 상처는 치명적인 종류는 아니었으나 꽤 깊어서 상당한 흉터를 남겨 두고 간 것이었다. 그러나 이 추정상의 살인으로 게이트하우스에 수감된 사람이 바로 존스 씨라는 것을 알게 되자마자, 그녀는 첫 기회를 잡아 피츠패트릭 씨를 간병인의 손에 맡겨 두고, 그 승리자를 찾아 서둘러 길을 나섰다.\n\n그녀는 이제 명랑한 기색으로 방에 들어섰으나, 그 기색은 가엾은 존스의 우울한 모습에 곧장 꺾이고 말았다. 존스는 그녀를 보자 깜짝 놀라 성호를 그었다. 그러자 그녀가 말했다. \"아니, 당신이 놀라는 것도 무리는 아니죠. 당신은 저를 보리라고는 기대하지 못했을 테니까요. 이런 곳에서 어느 부인의 방문으로 신경 쓰이는 신사는 거의 없잖아요, 아내라면 모를까. 당신이 저에게 얼마나 큰 힘을 가졌는지 보세요, 존스 씨. 정말이지, 우리가 업튼에서 헤어질 때만 해도 다음번 만남이 이런 곳에서 이루어질 줄은 거의 생각지도 못했어요.\" \"정말이지, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이 방문을 친절로 여길 수밖에 없군요. 비참한 자를 따라와 주는 사람은 거의 없으니까요, 더구나 이런 음울한 거처까지는 말입니다.\" \"맹세컨대, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 업튼에서 보았던 그 유쾌한 사람과 같은 사람이라고는 도무지 믿기지 않네요. 아니, 당신 얼굴이 세상 어느 지하 감옥보다 더 비참해 보이는걸요. 대체 무슨 일이 있는 거예요?\" \"부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"제가 여기 있는 걸 아셨으니, 그 불행한 사연도 알고 계시리라 생각했습니다.\" \"쳇!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"결투에서 사람 하나 찔렀을 뿐이잖아요, 그게 다죠.\" 존스는 이런 가벼운 태도에 약간의 분개를 드러내며, 일어난 일에 대해 더없는 회한을 담아 말했다. 그러자 그녀가 대답했다. \"그럼, 좋아요, 선생님, 그 일을 그토록 마음에 두신다면 제가 시름을 덜어 드리죠. 그 신사는 죽지 않았어요. 그리고 저는 그가 죽을 위험은 없다고 꽤 확신해요. 사실 처음 그를 치료한 외과의는 젊은 친구였는데, 그를 고쳐서 더 큰 명예를 얻으려고 그의 상태를 되도록 나쁘게 보이려는 듯했어요. 하지만 그 후 왕실 외과의가 그를 진찰했고, 지금으로선 아무 증상도 없는 열병이라도 생기지 않는 한 목숨에 조금의 위험도 없다고 했답니다.\" 존스는 이 소식에 얼굴 가득 큰 만족을 드러냈다. 그러자 그녀는 그것이 사실임을 거듭 확언하며 덧붙였다. \"세상에서 가장 기막힌 우연으로, 제가 같은 집에 묵고 있어요. 그 신사도 만나 봤는데, 장담컨대 그는 당신에게 공정하게 말하더군요. 결과가 어찌 되든, 자기가 전적으로 먼저 덤빈 쪽이었고 당신에게는 조금도 잘못이 없다고 말이에요.\"\n\n존스는 워터스 부인이 전해 준 이야기에 더없는 만족을 드러냈다. 그러고 나서 그는 그녀가 이미 잘 알고 있는 여러 가지를 알려 주었으니, 피츠패트릭 씨가 누구인지, 그의 원한의 까닭이 무엇인지 따위였다. 또한 그녀가 모르고 있던 여러 사실도 들려주었는데, 토시 사건이라든가 그 밖의 자잘한 일들이었고, 다만 소피아의 이름만은 감추었다. 그런 다음 그는 자신이 저질러 온 어리석음과 악덕을 한탄했다. 그 하나하나가 모두 어찌나 나쁜 결과를 불러왔던지, 만일 그것을 교훈 삼아 앞으로 그 사악한 길을 버리지 않는다면 자신은 용서받지 못할 자라고 말했다. 끝으로 그는, 더 나쁜 일이 자신에게 닥치지 않도록 다시는 죄를 짓지 않겠다는 결심을 그녀에게 다짐하는 것으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n워터스 부인은 이 모든 것을 기분 가라앉음과 갇혀 있는 처지가 빚어낸 결과라며 더없이 익살스럽게 비웃었다. 그녀는 악마도 병들면 어쩌고 하는 우스갯소리 몇 마디를 늘어놓고는 그에게 말했다. \"당신이 머지않아 풀려나 예전처럼 활기찬 사람이 되는 걸 분명히 보게 될 거예요. 그리고 그때가 되면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"지금 그렇게 앓으며 품고 있는 그 모든 양심의 가책도 무사히 다 떨쳐 버릴 게 틀림없어요.\"\n\n그녀는 이런 식의 말을 훨씬 더 많이 늘어놓았는데, 그중 어떤 것들은 일부 독자가 보기에 기억해 둔다 해서 그녀에게 그리 명예로울 게 없을 것이다. 또한 존스가 한 대답들이 다른 독자들에게 조롱거리로 다루어지지 않으리라고 우리도 썩 자신하지는 못하겠다. 그러므로 우리는 이 대화의 나머지는 덮어 두기로 하고, 다만 그것이 끝내 완전무결한 순결함 속에서 마무리되었으며, 부인보다는 존스에게 훨씬 더 만족스럽게 끝났다는 점만 짚어 두겠다. 전자는 그녀가 가져온 소식에 크게 들떴지만, 후자는 처음 만났을 때 품었던 것과는 사뭇 다른 생각을 지금 품게 된 그 남자의 뉘우치는 행동이 그다지 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다.\n\n이리하여 나이팅게일의 전언이 불러온 우울은 제법 말끔히 지워졌다. 그러나 밀러 부인이 그를 빠뜨려 놓은 낙담만은 여전히 가시지 않았다. 그녀가 전한 이야기는 편지 속 소피아 자신의 말과 어찌나 잘 들어맞던지, 그는 그녀가 자기 편지를 숙모에게 털어놓았고 자신을 버리기로 굳게 마음먹었다는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 이 생각이 그에게 안긴 고통에 견줄 수 있는 것은 오직, 운명이 아직 그를 위해 마련해 둔 한 가지 소식뿐이었으니, 그것은 다음 권의 두 번째 장에서 전하기로 하겠다.\n\n엿새 남짓의 이야기를 담다.\n\n독자에게 고하는 작별.\n독자여, 우리는 이제 이 긴 여정의 마지막 구간에 다다랐다. 이렇게 그토록 많은 페이지를 함께 지나왔으니, 며칠을 한 마차 안에서 같이 보낸 역마차 길동무들처럼 서로를 대해 보자. 그들은 길 위에서 어떤 다툼이나 사소한 앙금이 있었더라도 결국에는 대개 다 풀어 버리고, 마지막으로 다시 한 번 명랑하고 흐뭇한 기분으로 마차에 오른다. 이 한 구간만 지나고 나면, 그들에게 흔히 그러하듯 우리에게도 다시는 만나지 못할 일이 일어날지 모르기 때문이다.\n\n이렇게 비유를 끄집어낸 김에, 그것을 조금만 더 끌고 가도록 허락해 다오. 그러니까 나는 이 마지막 권에서, 앞서 말한 그 좋은 길동무들이 마지막 여정에서 보이는 모습을 본떠 보려 한다. 자, 잘 알려져 있듯이 이때쯤이면 온갖 농담과 익살은 모두 거두어진다. 길 위에서 승객들 가운데 누군가가 우스개 삼아 꾸며 보이던 역할은 이제 다 벗어 던지고, 대화는 으레 담백하고 진지해진다.\n\n같은 방식으로, 만일 내가 이 작품을 써 오는 동안 때때로 너의 즐거움을 위해 어떤 익살을 부렸다면, 여기서는 그것을 내려놓겠다. 실로 이 권에 욱여넣어야 할 사건들이 워낙 다양하다 보니, 내가 다른 데서 늘어놓았던 그런 우스꽝스러운 논평들을 끼워 넣을 자리가 없을 것이다. 어쩌면 그런 논평들이, 너에게 슬그머니 졸음이 밀려들기 시작할 때 한숨 자려는 너를 막아 주기도 했을 텐데 말이다. 이 마지막 권에서 너는 그런 종류의 것을 전혀(많아야 아주 조금밖에) 찾지 못할 것이다. 모든 것이 오직 담백한 서술뿐이리라. 그리고 실로, 이 권이 펼쳐 놓을 그 숱한 큰 사건들을 다 읽고 나면, 너는 그 이야기를 다 들려주기에 여기 담긴 페이지 수가 도무지 모자라다고 여기게 될 것이다.\n\n그리고 이제, 나의 벗이여, 나는 이 기회를 빌려(달리 기회가 없을 터이니) 진심으로 너의 안녕을 빈다. 만일 내가 너에게 즐거운 길동무였다면, 분명히 말하건대 그것이야말로 내가 바라던 바였다. 만일 무엇으로든 내가 너의 기분을 상하게 했다면, 그것은 정말이지 아무 의도도 없이 그리된 것이다. 여기 쓰인 어떤 것들이 어쩌면 너나 너의 벗들을 찔렀을지도 모르겠다. 그러나 나는 더없이 엄숙하게 단언하건대, 그것들은 너나 그들을 겨냥한 것이 아니었다. 나에 관한 다른 여러 이야기 가운데, 네가 아주 입이 험한 자와 함께 여행하게 될 거라는 말을 들었을 법도 하다. 그러나 너에게 그리 말한 자가 누구든, 그는 나에게 해를 끼친 것이다. 나만큼 막말을 혐오하고 경멸하는 사람도 없으며, 그럴 만한 이유를 나보다 더 많이 가진 사람도 없다. 나만큼 그런 막말에 시달려 본 사람이 없기 때문이다. 게다가 참으로 가혹한 운명이라 할 것은, 바로 그자들이 다른 작품들에서 더없이 모질게 나를 욕해 놓고도, 그들이 쓴 욕설 가득한 글 가운데 일부가 도리어 내 것인 양 내게 떠넘겨졌다는 사실이다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 글들은, 이 페이지가 너의 눈앞에 펼쳐지기 훨씬 전에 이미 죽어 사라질 것이라고 나는 굳게 확신한다. 내 자신의 작품들이 누릴 수명이 아무리 짧다 한들, 그 글들은 십중팔구 그 병약한 저자보다도, 또 나를 헐뜯던 동시대인들의 그 가냘픈 작품들보다도 더 오래 살아남을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n매우 비극적인 사건을 담은 장.\n\n존스가 앞서 우리가 그를 두고 떠나올 때처럼 그 괴로운 상념에 빠져 스스로를 들볶고 있을 때, 파트리지가 비틀거리며 방으로 들어왔다. 얼굴은 잿빛보다 더 창백했고, 두 눈은 머리에 못 박힌 듯 굳어 있었으며, 머리카락은 곤두서고, 사지가 하나하나 떨리고 있었다. 한마디로, 유령을 본 사람, 아니 그 자신이 유령이라도 된 듯한 몰골이었다.\n\n좀처럼 겁을 먹는 법이 없는 존스도 이 갑작스러운 출현 앞에서는 다소 충격을 받지 않을 수 없었다. 실로 그 자신도 낯빛이 변했고, 무슨 일이냐고 물을 때 목소리가 조금 떨렸다.\n\n\"나리,\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"부디 저에게 노하지 말아 주십시오. 정말로 엿들은 것은 아닙니다만, 바깥방에 있을 수밖에 없었습니다. 제가 들은 그것을 듣느니, 차라리 백 마일은 떨어진 곳에 있었더라면 좋았을 것을, 정말이지 그렇게 바랐습니다.\" \"아니, 대체 무슨 일이냐?\" 존스가 말했다. \"무슨 일이냐고요, 나리? 오, 자비로운 하늘이시여!\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"방금 나간 저 여자가 어퍼튼에서 나리와 함께 있던 그 여자입니까?\" \"그렇다, 파트리지.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그러면 나리, 정말로 저 여자와 동침을 하셨습니까?\" 그가 떨면서 물었다. \"우리 사이에 있었던 일은 이제 비밀도 아닌 모양이로구나.\" 존스가 말했다. \"아니, 그래도 제발, 나리, 하늘에 맹세코, 나리, 대답해 주십시오.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"너도 알지 않느냐, 그렇다.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면, 주님께서 나리의 영혼에 자비를 베푸시고 용서해 주시기를.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"허나 제가 여기 이렇게 살아 서 있는 것만큼이나 분명하게, 나리께서는 친어머니와 잠자리를 같이하신 겁니다.\"\n\n이 말을 듣자 존스는 순식간에 파트리지보다도 더한 공포의 화신이 되고 말았다. 실로 그는 한동안 경악에 사로잡혀 입을 떼지 못했고, 두 사람은 서로를 미친 듯이 바라보며 서 있었다. 마침내 그의 입에서 말이 새어 나왔고, 끊어지는 목소리로 그가 말했다. \"뭐라고! 뭐라고! 그게 대체 무슨 소리냐?\" \"아니, 나리,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"지금은 다 말씀드릴 숨조차 남아 있지 않습니다만, 제가 한 말은 틀림없는 사실입니다. 방금 나간 저 여자가 나리의 친어머니입니다. 나리, 그때 제가 마침 그 여자를 보고 막아 드리지 못한 것이 얼마나 불운한 일이었는지요! 분명 악마가 직접 이 사악한 일을 꾸며 낸 게 틀림없습니다.\"\n\n\"분명,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"운명은 나를 미치게 몰아넣을 때까지 결코 나를 놓아주지 않을 것이다. 하지만 어찌하여 내가 운명을 탓하랴? 이 모든 비참함의 원인은 바로 나 자신이다. 내게 닥친 그 끔찍한 재앙들은 모두 나 자신의 어리석음과 악덕이 빚은 결과일 뿐이다. 파트리지, 네가 내게 한 말은 거의 나에게서 제정신을 앗아 가 버렸다! 그렇다면 워터스 부인이 정말로... 하지만 내가 어찌 묻겠는가? 너는 분명 그 여자를 알고 있을 테니. 만일 네게 나를 위하는 마음이 조금이라도 있다면, 아니, 네게 조금이라도 연민이 있다면, 부디 간청하노니 그 불쌍한 여자를 다시 내게 데려와 다오. 오, 자비로운 하늘이시여! 근친상간이라니... 어머니와! 나는 대체 무엇을 위해 남겨진 존재란 말인가!\" 그러고 나서 그는 더없이 격렬하고 광적인 슬픔과 절망의 고통 속으로 빠져들었고, 그 모습에 파트리지는 그를 결코 떠나지 않겠다고 다짐했다. 그러나 마침내 격정의 첫 격류를 다 쏟아 내고 나자 그는 조금씩 정신을 차렸다. 그러고는 파트리지에게, 상처 입은 신사가 묵고 있는 바로 그 집에서 이 가엾은 여자를 찾을 수 있을 것이라 일러 주고는 그녀를 찾으러 보냈다.\n\n독자께서 부디 제9권의 어퍼튼 장면으로 되돌아가 기억을 새롭게 해 주신다면, 워터스 부인이 존스 씨와 함께 그곳에서 하루를 온전히 보냈음에도 파트리지와 그녀의 만남을 공교롭게 가로막은 그 숱한 기묘한 우연들에 감탄하게 될 것이다. 이런 종류의 사례는 우리 삶에서도 흔히 볼 수 있다. 가장 큰 사건들이 자잘한 정황의 절묘한 연쇄로 빚어지는 경우 말이다. 그리고 이런 예는 우리의 이 이야기 속에서도 눈 밝은 이라면 한둘 넘게 찾아낼 수 있을 것이다.\n\n두세 시간을 헛되이 헤맨 끝에, 파트리지는 워터스 부인을 만나지 못한 채 주인에게로 돌아왔다. 그의 지체에 절망 상태에 빠져 있던 존스는, 그가 와서 사정을 전하자 거의 미쳐 날뛸 지경이었다. 그러나 그 상태가 오래가기 전에 그는 다음과 같은 편지를 받았다.\n\"나리께,\n\n당신과 헤어진 뒤 저는 한 신사를 만났고, 그에게서 당신에 관한 무언가를 알게 되었습니다. 그것은 저를 몹시 놀라게 하고 또 깊이 동요케 합니다. 그러나 지금은 그토록 중대한 일을 전할 겨를이 없으니, 당신의 궁금증은 우리가 다음에 만날 때까지 미루어 두셔야겠습니다. 그 만남은 제가 당신을 뵐 수 있게 되는 첫 순간이 될 것입니다. 오, 존스 씨, 제가 어퍼튼에서 그 행복한 하루를 보낼 때만 해도, 그토록 완전한 행복을 누가 제게 베풀어 준 것인지 저는 조금도 알지 못했습니다. 그날을 떠올리는 것이 앞으로의 제 모든 삶을 쓰디쓰게 만들 것만 같습니다. 저를 언제까지나 진심으로 당신의 불행한 사람으로 믿어 주십시오.\n\nJ. 워터스 올림.\"\n\"추신. 부디 되도록 마음을 위로하시기 바랍니다. 피츠패트릭 씨는 조금도 위험하지 않으니까요. 그러니 당신이 뉘우쳐야 할 다른 어떤 무거운 죄가 있다 하더라도, 피의 죄만큼은 그 가운데 들어 있지 않습니다.\"\n\n편지를 다 읽은 존스는 그것을 떨어뜨리고 말았다(편지를 쥐고 있을 수도 없었고, 실로 어느 한 가지 기능도 제대로 쓸 수 없는 지경이었으니까). 파트리지가 그것을 집어 들었고, 침묵으로 허락을 얻은 뒤 자신도 그것을 읽었다. 그 편지가 그에게 미친 충격 또한 결코 덜하지 않았다. 두 사람의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 공포는 펜이 아니라 화가의 붓으로나 그려 낼 수 있을 것이었다. 두 사람이 모두 말을 잃고 있을 때, 간수가 방으로 들어왔다. 그리고 두 사람의 얼굴에 충분히 드러나 있는 것을 조금도 알아채지 못한 채, 밖에 한 남자가 와서 그와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 존스에게 전했다. 그 사람이 곧 안으로 안내되어 들어왔는데, 다름 아닌 검둥이 조지였다.\n\n* 검둥이 조지: 'Black George'의 옮김으로, 사냥터지기 조지 시그림의 별명.\n공포의 광경이 간수에게는 익숙해도 조지에게는 그만큼 익숙한 것이 아니었던 터라, 그는 존스의 얼굴에 떠오른 큰 동요를 단번에 알아보았다. 그는 그것을, 웨스턴 씨 댁에서 더없이 나쁜 쪽으로 전해진 그 사건 탓으로 돌렸다. 그래서 그는 그 신사가 죽었으며, 존스 씨가 수치스러운 최후를 맞을 처지에 놓였다고 단정했다. 이 생각은 그를 몹시 불편하게 했다. 조지는 본디 인정 많은 성품이었던 데다, 비록 지나친 유혹에 넘어가 우정에 작은 금을 낸 일이 있긴 했어도, 근본은 예전에 존스 씨에게서 받은 은혜를 잊지 못하는 사람이었기 때문이다.\n\n그래서 그 가엾은 사내는 지금의 광경에 눈물을 가까스로 참았다. 그는 존스에게 그의 불운이 진심으로 안타깝다고 말하면서, 자신이 무슨 일로든 도움이 될 수 있을지 헤아려 봐 달라고 청했다. \"어쩌면 나리,\" 그가 말했다. \"이런 마당에 약간의 돈이 필요하실지도 모르겠습니다. 그러시다면, 나리, 제게 있는 얼마 안 되는 것이나마 진심으로 나리께 쓰십시오.\"\n\n존스는 그의 손을 더없이 정겹게 잡고는, 그가 베풀어 준 친절한 제안에 거듭 고마움을 표했다. 그러나 그런 종류의 도움은 조금도 필요하지 않다고 대답했다. 그러자 조지는 전보다 더 간곡하게 자기 도움을 받아 달라고 졸랐다. 존스는 다시 고마움을 표하면서, 살아 있는 그 누구의 힘으로도 줄 수 있는 것이라면 아무것도 필요하지 않다고 거듭 확언했다. \"자, 자, 우리 좋으신 주인 나리,\" 조지가 대답했다. \"그 일을 너무 가슴에 담아 두지 마십시오. 일이 생각하시는 것보다 잘 풀릴 수도 있습니다. 정말이지, 사람을 죽이고도 무사히 빠져나온 신사가 나리가 처음은 아니니까요.\" \"조지, 자네는 핵심을 한참 비껴갔네.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"그 신사는 죽지도 않았고, 죽을 것 같지도 않아. 지금은 우리 주인 나리를 어지럽히지 말게. 나리는 자네 힘으로는 조금도 도울 수 없는 일로 괴로워하고 계시니까.\" \"파트리지 씨, 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지는 당신이 모르는 일이지요.\" 조지가 대답했다. \"나리의 걱정이 우리 아가씨에 관한 것이라면, 제게 주인 나리께 전할 소식이 좀 있습니다.\" \"무슨 말인가, 조지?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"최근에 나의 소피아와 관련된 무슨 일이라도 있었단 말인가? 나의 소피아라니! 나 같은 못난 자가 어찌 감히 그분의 이름을 이리도 함부로 입에 올리는가.\" \"그분은 아직 나리의 사람이 될 것이라 저는 믿습니다.\" 조지가 대답했다. \"그렇고말고요, 나리, 그분에 관해 말씀드릴 게 좀 있습니다. 웨스턴 마님이 방금 소피아 아가씨를 집으로 데려오셨는데, 한바탕 끔찍한 소동이 벌어졌습니다. 그 자초지종을 제대로 알아내지는 못했습니다만, 우리 주인 나리는 굉장히 큰 노발대발이셨고, 웨스턴 마님도 그러셨습니다. 마님이 문밖으로 나가 가마에 오르시면서 다시는 주인 나리 댁에 발을 들이지 않겠다고 하시는 걸 제가 들었습니다. 무슨 영문인지는 저로서는 모르겠습니다만, 제가 나올 때는 모든 것이 아주 잠잠했습니다. 그런데 저녁 시중을 들던 로빈이 말하기를, 영주님이 그토록 오랫동안 젊은 아가씨에게 그렇게 기분 좋아하시는 모습은 처음 본다고 했습니다. 아가씨에게 여러 번 입을 맞추셨고, 아가씨더러 자기 자신의 주인이 되라 맹세하셨으며, 다시는 아가씨를 가두어 둘 생각을 하지 않겠다고 하셨답니다. 이 소식을 들으시면 나리께서 기뻐하시리라 생각해서, 이렇게 늦은 시각인데도 몰래 빠져나와 나리께 알려 드리러 온 것입니다.\" 존스는 조지에게, 그 소식이 자신을 참으로 기쁘게 한다고 확언했다. 비록 그 비할 데 없는 사람을 향해 다시는 감히 눈을 들지 못할 처지였지만, 그녀가 평안하다는 소식을 들으며 늘 누리게 될 그 만족만큼 그의 비참함을 덜어 줄 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없었기 때문이다.\n\n이 방문에서 오간 나머지 대화는 여기에 옮길 만큼 중요하지 않다. 따라서 독자께서는 우리가 이렇게 불쑥 이야기를 끊는 것을 너그러이 보아 주시고, 부디 영주가 딸에게 품은 이 큰 호의가 어떻게 빚어지게 되었는지 들어 주시기 바란다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 오라비의 처소에 막 도착하자마자, 펠라마 경과의 혼사가 가문에 가져다줄 크나큰 영예와 이익을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그것은 조카딸이 단호히 거절한 혼사였다. 그 거절을 두고 영주가 딸의 편을 들자, 부인은 즉시 더없이 격렬한 분노에 빠져들었고, 영주를 어찌나 약 올리고 들쑤셨는지, 영주의 인내심도 분별력도 더는 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 없었다. 그리하여 두 사람 사이에 어찌나 뜨거운 말다툼 한판이 벌어졌던지, 어쩌면 빌링스게이트 어시장의 욕설판도 그에 견줄 수 없었을 것이다. 이렇게 욕설이 한창 달아오른 와중에 웨스턴 부인이 떠나 버렸고, 그 바람에 그녀는 소피아가 받은 편지에 대해 오라비에게 알릴 겨를이 없었다. 그 편지를 알렸더라면 어쩌면 나쁜 결과를 낳았을지도 모른다. 그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 나는 그 일이 이때만큼은 단 한 번도 그녀의 기억에 떠오르지 않았으리라고 믿는다.\n* 빌링스게이트: 런던의 생선 시장으로, 상스러운 욕설로 악명이 높았다.\n\n웨스턴 부인이 떠나자, 그때까지 필요 때문이기도 하고 마음 때문이기도 하여 줄곧 입을 다물고 있던 소피아가, 아버지가 고모에 맞서 자기 편을 들어 준 그 호의에 보답하기 시작했다. 이번에는 그녀가 아버지 편을 들어 고모에게 맞선 것이다. 그녀가 그렇게 한 것은 이번이 처음이었고, 그것은 영주에게 더없이 흡족한 일이었다. 게다가 영주는, 올워디 씨가 모든 강압적인 수단을 완전히 포기하라고 강력히 요구했던 것을 떠올렸다. 그리고 실로 그는 존스가 교수형에 처해질 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았던 터라, 점잖은 방법으로 딸을 손에 넣는 데 성공하리라는 것 또한 추호도 의심하지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 이제 다시 한번 딸을 향한 타고난 애정을 마음껏 풀어놓았다. 그것이 효성스럽고 고마움을 아는, 다정하고 살가운 소피아의 마음에 어찌나 큰 영향을 미쳤던지, 만일 존스에게 바친 그녀의 정절과, 또 어쩌면 그와 관련된 다른 무언가가 제거되었더라면, 그녀가 아버지를 흡족하게 해 드리려고 마음에도 없는 남자에게 자신을 희생해 버리지 않았을지 나로서는 몹시 의심스럽다. 그녀는 아버지를 흡족하게 해 드리는 것을 평생의 유일한 과업으로 삼겠다고, 그의 동의 없이는 결코 어떤 남자와도 혼인하지 않겠다고 그에게 약속했다. 이 말은 노인을 더없는 행복의 문턱에까지 바짝 데려다 놓았고, 그리하여 그는 다음 단계로 나아가기로 작정하고는 완전히 곤드레만드레 취한 채 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n# Chapter iii.\n\n올워디가 나이팅게일 노인을 찾아가다. 그리고 그 자리에서 이루어진 기이한 발견에 관하여.\n\n이 모든 일이 벌어진 다음 날 아침, 올워디 씨는 약속대로 나이팅게일 노인을 찾아갔다. 그에 대한 올워디 씨의 영향력은 어찌나 컸던지, 세 시간을 함께 앉아 있은 끝에 마침내 그가 아들을 만나 보겠다고 동의하도록 설득해 냈다.\n\n여기서 참으로 기이한 종류의 사건이 하나 일어났다. 실로, 매우 선하고 진중한 사람들이, 신의 섭리가 가장 은밀한 악행을 들추어내는 데 종종 끼어든다고 결론짓게 한 그 이상한 우연들 가운데 하나였다. 사람들이 아무리 조심스레 악덕의 길을 디딘다 해도, 정직의 길에서 벗어나지 말라고 경계시키기 위함이라는 것이다.\n\n올워디 씨는 나이팅게일 씨의 집에 들어서면서 블랙 조지를 보았다. 그는 그를 아는 체하지 않았고, 블랙 조지 역시 그가 자신을 알아보았으리라고는 생각지 못했다.\n\n그러나 본론에 관한 대화가 끝나자, 올워디는 나이팅게일에게 조지 시그림이라는 자를 아는지, 또 무슨 일로 그가 집을 찾아왔는지 물었다. \"예.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"그자를 아주 잘 압니다. 정말이지 보통내기가 아닌 친구지요. 요즘 세상에, 일 년에 30파운드밖에 안 되는 아주 작은 땅을 부쳐서 500파운드나 모아 두었다니 말입니다.\" \"그게 그자가 당신에게 들려준 이야기입니까?\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"아니, 사실입니다. 장담하지요.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"지금 그 돈이 내 손에 있으니까요. 은행 어음 다섯 장으로 말입니다. 그것을 저당에 넣든지, 아니면 잉글랜드 북부에 무언가를 사들이는 데 쓰려고 합니다.\" 올워디의 청에 따라 그 은행 어음이 꺼내지자마자, 그는 그 발견의 기이함에 절로 성호를 그었다. 그는 곧 나이팅게일에게 이 은행 어음이 본래 자기 것이었다고 말하고는, 일의 전말을 모두 알려 주었다. 노상강도나 노름꾼, 그 밖의 그런 부류의 도둑들만큼 장사의 사기를 두고 불평하는 자들이 없듯이, 고리대금업자나 거간꾼, 그 밖의 이런 부류의 도둑들만큼 노름꾼 따위의 사기를 두고 모질게 떠들어 대는 자들도 없다. 한쪽의 속임수가 다른 쪽에게 불명예이거나 자기 모습을 비추는 거울이 되기 때문인지, 아니면 모든 사기꾼의 공통된 정부(情婦)인 돈이 그들로 하여금 서로를 경쟁자로 여기게 만들기 때문인지는 모르겠으나, 나이팅게일은 이 이야기를 듣자마자, 올워디의 정의로움과 정직함이 그자에게 베푼 것보다 훨씬 더 가혹한 말로 그를 욕했다.\n\n올워디는 나이팅게일에게, 다시 연락이 갈 때까지 그 돈도 비밀도 그대로 간직해 달라고 청했다. 또한 그사이에 그자를 보더라도, 자신이 알아낸 사실을 조금도 내색하지 말아 달라고 부탁했다. 그러고 나서 그는 자신의 숙소로 돌아왔는데, 그곳에서 밀러 부인이 사위에게서 들은 소식 때문에 몹시 풀이 죽어 있는 것을 보았다. 올워디 씨는 아주 밝은 얼굴로, 전해 줄 좋은 소식이 많다고 말했다. 그리고 별다른 서두 없이, 나이팅게일 씨가 아들을 만나 보기로 동의했으며 두 사람을 완전히 화해시킬 수 있으리라는 데 조금도 의심하지 않는다고 알렸다. 다만 그 집안에서 같은 종류의 또 다른 사건이 일어나는 바람에 아버지의 마음이 한층 더 틀어져 있더라는 말도 덧붙였다. 그러고는 삼촌의 딸이 달아난 일을 언급했는데, 이는 그 노신사에게서 들은 것으로, 밀러 부인과 그 사위는 아직 알지 못하는 일이었다.\n\n독자는 밀러 부인이 이 소식을 큰 감사와, 그에 못지않은 기쁨으로 받아들였으리라 짐작할 것이다. 그러나 존스를 향한 그녀의 우정이 어찌나 남다른지, 그를 위해 마음 아파하는 괴로움이 제 가족의 행복에 그토록 보탬이 되는 소식을 들은 기쁨을 능가하지 않았는지 나로서는 장담할 수 없다. 또한 이 소식이 그녀로 하여금 존스에게 진 빚을 떠올리게 한 만큼, 바로 이 소식조차 그녀를 기쁘게 한 동시에 아프게 하지 않았는지도 알 수 없다. 그녀의 고마운 마음이 그녀에게 이렇게 속삭였기 때문이다. \"내 가족은 행복한데, 우리가 이 모든 행복의 첫걸음을 빚진 그 너그러운 가엾은 사람은 얼마나 비참한가!\"\n\n올워디는 그녀가 이 첫 소식을 잠시 곱씹도록(이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 내버려 둔 뒤, 아직 전할 것이 더 남았으며 그녀를 기쁘게 하리라 믿는다고 말했다. \"내 생각에는,\" 그가 말했다. \"부인의 친구인 그 젊은이에게 속한 꽤 상당한 재산을 찾아낸 것 같습니다. 하지만 어쩌면, 사실, 그의 지금 처지가 그것이 그에게 아무 쓸모도 없을 그런 형편일지도 모르겠군요.\" 이 말의 뒷부분에서 밀러 부인은 그가 누구를 가리키는지 알아차렸고, 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다. \"그렇지 않기를 바랍니다, 어른.\" \"나도 그러기를 바랍니다.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"진심으로요. 하지만 내 조카가 오늘 아침 그 일에 관해 아주 나쁜 이야기를 들었다고 하더군요.\" \"맙소사! 어른.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"글쎄, 제가 입을 다물어야겠지만, 듣고서도 혀를 묶어 두어야 한다는 건 정말이지 견디기 힘든 일이에요.\" \"부인,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"하고 싶은 말은 무엇이든 하셔도 됩니다. 부인은 나를 잘 알지 않습니까. 내가 누구에게든 편견을 품을 사람이 아니라는 것을요. 그리고 그 젊은이로 말하자면, 그가 모든 일에서, 특히 이 슬픈 일에서 결백을 밝힐 수 있다면 진심으로 기쁘겠다고 분명히 말합니다. 부인은 내가 예전에 그를 얼마나 아꼈는지 증언해 줄 수 있을 겁니다. 세상이 내가 그를 너무 사랑한다고 나무란 것을 압니다. 나도 가장 정당한 이유가 있다고 여기지 않고서는 그 애정을 거두지 않았습니다. 나를 믿어 주십시오, 밀러 부인, 내가 잘못 알았던 것이기를 나도 바랍니다.\" 밀러 부인이 간절히 대답하려는데, 하인이 들어와 어떤 신사가 밖에서 그녀와 당장 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 알렸다. 그러자 올워디는 자기 조카를 찾았고, 조카가 늘 그를 찾아오던 신사와 함께 한동안 방에 있다는 말을 들었다. 올워디 씨는 그 신사를 다울링 씨라고 옳게 짐작하고는, 당장 그와 이야기하고 싶다고 청했다.\n\n다울링이 들어오자, 올워디는 이름은 일절 대지 않은 채 은행권에 관한 사정을 그에게 들려주고, 그런 자가 어떤 식으로 처벌받을 수 있느냐고 물었다. 이에 다울링이 대답했다. \"블랙 액트로 기소될 수 있을 듯합니다만, 다소 미묘한 문제이니 법률 자문을 구하는 것이 마땅하겠습니다. 마침 웨스턴 씨의 일로 곧 자문 변호인들을 만날 참인데, 올워디 씨께서 괜찮으시다면 이 사정도 그들 앞에 내놓아 보겠습니다.\" 그렇게 하기로 합의를 보았다. 그때 밀러 부인이 문을 열고 외쳤다. \"용서하세요, 손님이 계신 줄 몰랐어요.\" 그러나 올워디는 일이 끝났다며 그녀에게 들어오라고 청했다. 이에 다울링 씨가 물러나고, 밀러 부인은 올워디에게 베풂받은 큰 친절에 감사를 전하려는 젊은 나이팅게일 씨를 소개했다. 그러나 그녀는 그 젊은 신사가 인사를 마치도록 참아 줄 인내심도 없이 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"오, 어른! 나이팅게일 씨가 가엾은 존스 씨에 관한 굉장한 소식을 가져왔답니다. 그 다친 신사를 만나고 왔는데, 죽을 위험은 전혀 없고, 게다가 자기가 먼저 가엾은 존스 씨에게 달려들어 때렸노라고 밝혔다는군요. 분명히, 어른, 어른께서도 존스 씨가 겁쟁이이기를 바라지는 않으실 거예요. 제가 만일 남자였다면, 누군가 저를 친다면 분명 칼을 뽑았을 거예요. 자, 부탁이니, 이보게, 올워디 씨께 말씀드려요. 전부 자네가 직접 말씀드려요.\" 그러자 나이팅게일은 밀러 부인이 한 말을 확인해 주고, 존스에 관한 훌륭한 이야기를 잔뜩 늘어놓으며 끝맺었다. 그의 말에 따르면 존스는 세상에서 가장 천성이 착한 사람 가운데 하나이며, 다투기를 좋아하는 기색이라곤 조금도 없는 사람이라는 것이었다. 나이팅게일이 여기서 말을 그치려 하는데, 밀러 부인이 다시금, 그가 올워디 씨를 향해 쓰는 것을 들었다는 그 갖은 공손한 표현들을 모두 들려 달라고 청했다. \"올워디 씨에 관해 더없이 좋은 말을 한다는 건,\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"그저 엄정한 정의를 행하는 것일 뿐, 거기에 무슨 공이 있을 수는 없지요. 하지만 정말이지, 가엾은 존스만큼 이토록 훌륭한 분께 진 은혜를 절절히 느끼는 사람도 없으리라고 말하지 않을 수 없습니다. 정말로, 어른, 저는 어른의 노여움이라는 무게야말로 그가 짊어진 가장 무거운 짐이라고 확신합니다. 그는 자주 제게 그것을 한탄했고, 또 그만큼 자주, 더없이 엄숙한 태도로, 어른께 어떤 잘못도 고의로 저지른 적이 없노라고 단언했습니다. 아니, 그는 어른을 향해 무례하거나 배은망덕하거나 불충한 생각 하나라도 양심에 책잡히느니 차라리 천 번이라도 죽겠노라고 맹세했습니다. 하지만 용서하십시오, 어른, 이토록 민감한 대목에 제가 너무 깊이 끼어드는 것은 아닌지 두렵습니다.\" \"그리스도인으로서 마땅히 할 말 그 이상은 하지 않으셨어요.\" 밀러 부인이 외쳤다. \"참으로, 나이팅게일 씨.\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"당신의 그 너그러운 우정에 박수를 보냅니다. 부디 그가 당신에게 그런 우정을 받을 만한 사람이기를 바랍니다. 솔직히 이 불운한 젊은이에 관해 당신이 가져온 소식을 들으니 기쁩니다. 그리고 그 일이 당신이 그린 그대로 드러난다면(실로 당신의 말을 조금도 의심하지 않습니다만), 어쩌면 시간이 지나면 이 젊은이를 두고 요즘 품었던 것보다 더 좋게 생각하게 될지도 모르겠습니다. 여기 이 선량한 부인도, 아니, 나를 아는 모든 이가 증언해 줄 수 있듯이, 나는 그를 친아들이라도 되는 것처럼 끔찍이 사랑했으니까요. 정말로 나는 그를 운명이 내 보살핌에 맡긴 아이로 여겼습니다. 나는 그를 처음 발견했던 그 순진하고 무력한 모습을 아직도 기억합니다. 그 작은 손이 다정하게 쥐던 감촉이 이 순간에도 느껴집니다. 그는 내 귀염둥이였습니다, 정말로 그랬습니다.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 말을 멈추었고, 눈에는 눈물이 고였다.\n\n밀러 부인이 한 대답이 우리를 새로운 이야기로 이끌 수 있으니, 여기서 잠시 멈추어 올워디 씨의 마음에 눈에 띄게 일어난 변화와, 존스를 향한 노여움이 누그러진 까닭을 짚어 보기로 하자. 사실 이런 종류의 급변은 역사가나 극작가들의 글에 흔히 나타나는데, 다른 이유에서가 아니라 그저 그 역사나 극이 결말로 향하기 때문이며, 작가의 권위로 정당화되곤 한다. 그러나 우리는 어떤 작가 못지않은 권위를 주장하면서도, 이 권한만큼은 아주 아껴 쓸 것이며, 필요에 떠밀릴 때가 아니고서는 결코 휘두르지 않을 것이다. 그리고 이 작품에서 그런 일이 일어나리라고는 지금으로선 내다보지 않는다.\n\n그러니 올워디 씨의 마음에 일어난 이 변화는, 그가 방금 스퀘어 씨에게서 받은 한 통의 편지에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 편지를 우리는 다음 장의 첫머리에서 독자에게 보여 주려 한다.\n\n매우 다른 두 편지를 담은 장.\n\n존경하는 벗에게. 지난번 편지에서 저는, 광천수가 제 병의 증세를 덜어 주기는커녕 오히려 더 키운다는 사실이 경험으로 드러나, 그 사용이 금지되었음을 알려 드렸습니다. 이제 저는 한 가지 소식을 전하지 않을 수 없는데, 제 생각에 이 소식은 저를 괴롭히는 것보다 제 벗들을 더 괴롭게 할 것입니다. 해링턴 박사와 브루스터 박사가 제게, 제가 회복할 가망은 전혀 없다고 알려 주었습니다.\n\n저는 어디선가, 철학의 큰 쓸모란 죽는 법을 배우는 데 있다고 읽은 적이 있습니다. 그러니 제가 그토록 오래 익혀 온 것으로 여겨질 법한 가르침을 받으면서 새삼 놀라는 모습을 보여, 제 철학을 욕되게 하지는 않겠습니다. 그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 복음서의 한 페이지가 고금 철학자들의 모든 저서를 합친 것보다 이 가르침을 더 잘 일러 줍니다. 복음서가 우리에게 주는 또 다른 삶에 대한 확신은, 자연의 필연성에서, 이승에서 누리는 즐거움의 덧없음이나 물림에서, 혹은 그 밖의 온갖 변설의 화제에서 끌어낸 모든 위안보다, 선한 마음에 훨씬 더 든든한 버팀목이 됩니다. 그런 변설들은 때로 우리 마음을 무장시켜 죽음의 생각을 끈질긴 인내로 견디게 할 수는 있어도, 결코 그 마음을 들어 올려 죽음을 참으로 경멸하게 하지는 못하며, 하물며 죽음을 진정한 선으로 여기게 하는 일은 더더욱 못합니다. 여기서 저는 철학자라 불리는 모든 이에게 무신론이라는 끔찍한 비난을, 아니 불멸에 대한 절대적 부정이라는 비난을 던지려는 것으로 오해받고 싶지는 않습니다. 그 학파 가운데 고대와 근대를 막론하고 많은 이들이 이성의 빛으로부터 내세에 대한 어떤 희망을 발견하기도 했습니다. 그러나 실은 그 빛이 너무 희미하고 가물거렸으며 그 희망 또한 너무 불확실하고 위태로워, 그들의 믿음이 어느 쪽으로 기울었는지를 마땅히 의심할 만합니다. 플라톤조차 자신의 《파이돈》을 마무리하면서, 자기 최선의 논증이라야 겨우 개연성을 세우는 데 그친다고 선언했습니다. 키케로 또한 불멸의 교리에 대한 실제 믿음이라기보다는 믿고자 하는 마음의 기울임을 고백하는 데 가까워 보입니다. 저 자신으로 말하자면, 경께 아주 솔직히 털어놓건대, 제가 참된 그리스도인이 되기 전에는 이 믿음에 진지하게 매달린 적이 한 번도 없었습니다.\n\n경께서는 어쩌면 이 마지막 표현을 의아하게 여기실지도 모릅니다. 그러나 분명히 말씀드리건대, 제가 진실로 저 자신을 그리스도인이라 부를 수 있게 된 것은 아주 최근에 이르러서입니다. 철학의 오만이 제 이성을 취하게 했고, 모든 지혜 가운데 가장 숭고한 그 지혜가, 옛 그리스인들에게 그러했듯 제게도 어리석음으로만 보였습니다. 그러나 하느님께서는 너그러이도, 제가 영원한 어둠 속으로 완전히 가라앉기 전에 제 잘못을 제때 깨닫게 하시고 저를 진리의 길로 이끌어 주셨습니다.\n\n제 기운이 점점 빠지기 시작함을 느낍니다. 그러니 이 편지의 본래 목적으로 서둘러 들어가겠습니다.\n\n지난 제 삶의 행적들을 돌이켜 볼 때, 경의 양자인 그 가엾은 청년에게 제가 저지른 부당함만큼 제 양심을 무겁게 짓누르는 것이 없습니다. 저는 실로 남들의 악행을 못 본 척 눈감았을 뿐 아니라, 저 스스로도 그에게 부당한 일을 적극 행했습니다. 친애하는 벗이여, 죽어 가는 사람의 말로 제가 전하니 부디 믿어 주십시오. 그는 비열하게 해를 입었습니다. 그 잘못된 전언 때문에 경께서 그를 내치셨던 그 핵심 사건에 대하여, 저는 엄숙히 단언합니다. 그는 결백합니다. 경께서 임종하시리라 여겨졌던 그 자리에 누워 계셨을 때, 집 안에서 참된 근심을 드러낸 사람은 그가 유일했습니다. 그 뒤에 일어난 일은 경의 회복에 그가 미친 듯이 기뻐한 데서 비롯된 것이며, 말씀드리기 송구하오나, 또 다른 사람의 비열함에서 비롯된 것이기도 합니다(그러나 제 바람은 결백한 이를 변호하는 것이지, 누구를 고발하려는 것이 아닙니다). 벗이여, 부디 믿어 주십시오. 이 젊은이는 더없이 고결한 너그러움을, 더없이 완전한 우정의 자질을, 더없이 드높은 성실함을, 그리고 실로 사람을 고귀하게 만드는 온갖 덕을 지니고 있습니다. 그에게 허물이 없지는 않으나, 경께 대한 도리나 감사의 마음이 조금이라도 모자란 점만큼은 그 허물에 들지 않습니다. 오히려 저는 확신합니다. 경께서 그를 집에서 내치셨을 때, 그의 가슴은 자신을 위해서보다 경을 위해 더 피 흘렸으리라고요.\n\n세속적인 동기야말로 제가 이 사실을 그토록 오래 경께 숨겨 온 사악하고 비열한 이유였습니다. 이제 와 그것을 밝히는 데에는, 진리의 대의에 봉사하고, 결백한 이에게 정당함을 돌려주며, 지난날의 잘못을 제 힘이 닿는 한 모두 갚으려는 바람 외에 다른 어떤 동기도 있을 수 없습니다. 그러므로 저는 이 고백이 바라던 효과를 거두어, 이 마땅한 대접을 받아야 할 청년을 경의 총애 속으로 되돌려 놓기를 바랍니다. 제가 아직 살아 있는 동안 그 소식을 듣는 것이야말로, 다음과 같은 사람에게 더없는 위안이 될 것입니다.\n\n경이시여, 경께 더없이 큰 신세를 진, 순종하는 미천한 종, 토머스 스퀘어 올림.\n\n이 편지를 읽고 나면, 올워디 씨에게 그토록 뚜렷이 나타난 마음의 변화에 독자는 거의 놀라지 않을 것이다. 비록 그가 같은 우편으로 스왝컴에게서 사뭇 다른 종류의 또 다른 편지를 받았다 해도 말이다. 이제 그 편지를 여기 덧붙이는데, 어쩌면 우리가 그 신사의 이름을 입에 올릴 마지막 기회가 될지도 모르기 때문이다.\n\n경이시여,\n경의 훌륭하신 조카로부터, 무신론자 스퀘어 씨의 어린 제자가 저지른 악행의 새로운 사례를 전해 듣고도 저는 조금도 놀라지 않았습니다. 그가 앞으로 어떤 살인을 저지른다 해도 저는 놀라지 않을 것입니다. 그리고 경 자신의 핏줄이, 그를 통곡과 이 갈리는 곳으로 최종적으로 보내는 봉인이 되지 않기를 저는 진심으로 기도합니다.\n\n경께서 이 가련한 자에게 보이신 행동에서 드러난, 변명의 여지 없는 그 숱한 나약함들, 경 자신의 정당한 가문과 경의 명성에 그토록 해를 끼친 그 나약함들에 대해 회개할 충분한 이유가 없으실 리 없습니다. 다시 말해, 그것만으로도 지금 이 시기에 경의 양심을 찌르고 다그치기에 충분하다고 여길 만하나, 그럼에도 제가 경께 약간의 훈계를 베풀어 경으로 하여금 자신의 잘못을 마땅히 깨닫게 하기를 아낀다면, 저는 제 본분을 저버리는 셈이 될 것입니다. 그러므로 저는 경께 간곡히 청하오니, 이 사악한 악당에게 닥칠 법한 심판을 진지하게 헤아려 보십시오. 그리고 적어도 그것이 경께 하나의 경고가 되어, 경의 안녕을 위해 그토록 지칠 줄 모르고 기도하는 자의 충고를 앞으로는 업신여기지 않으시도록 하십시오.\n\n마땅한 징계를 가하려던 제 손이 묶이지만 않았던들, 저는 그 아이에게서 이 악마 같은 영혼을 매질하여 상당 부분 몰아냈을 것입니다. 어려서부터 저는 그 아이를 악마가 그토록 완전히 사로잡고 있음을 알아보았으니까요. 그러나 이런 회한도 이제는 너무 늦게 찾아왔습니다.\n\n경께서 웨스터턴의 성직록을 그토록 성급히 내어 주신 것이 안타깝습니다. 경께서 그것을 처분하기에 앞서 제게 알려 주지 않으시리라 알았더라면, 저는 그 기회에 진작 청을 넣었을 것입니다.―성직 겸임에 대한 경의 반대는 지나치게 의로운 처사입니다. 만일 그 관행에 무슨 죄가 있다면, 그토록 많은 경건한 분들이 그것에 동의하지는 않았을 것입니다. 올더그로브의 교구 목사가 죽기라도 한다면(듣자 하니 그가 쇠약해져 가고 있다 하니), 부디 저를 생각해 주시기 바랍니다. 경의 더없는 안녕에 대한 저의 더없이 진실한 헌신을 경께서 확신하시리라 저는 믿기 때문입니다.―그 안녕에 비하면 모든 세속적 고려란, 성경에 나오는 그 자잘한 십일조가 율법의 더 중대한 일들에 견주어 그러하듯, 하찮은 것에 지나지 않습니다.\n\n경이시여, 저는, 경의 충실하고 미천한 종, 로저 스왝컴 올림.\n\n스왝컴이 올워디에게 이렇게 권위적인 어투로 글을 쓴 것은 이번이 처음이었으며, 이에 대해 그는 뒷날 충분히 후회할 만한 이유를 갖게 되었으니, 이는 더없이 높은 정도의 선함을 더없이 낮은 정도의 나약함으로 착각한 자들의 경우와 같았다. 올워디는 사실 이 사내를 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없었다. 그는 이 사내가 오만하고 심술궂음을 알고 있었다. 또한 그의 신학 자체가 그의 성미에 물들어 있어, 여러 면에서 올워디 자신이 결코 인정할 수 없는 것임도 알고 있었다. 그러나 동시에 그는 뛰어난 학자였고, 두 소년을 가르치는 데 더없이 지칠 줄을 몰랐다. 거기에 더하여 그의 엄격한 생활과 몸가짐, 흠잡을 데 없는 정직함, 그리고 종교에 대한 더없이 경건한 헌신도 있었다. 그리하여 전체적으로 보아, 올워디는 이 사내를 존경하지도 사랑하지도 않았지만, 학식으로 보나 근면함으로 보나 그 직분에 더없이 합당한 소년들의 가정교사를 차마 내보낼 수 없었다. 그리고 그는, 소년들이 자기 집에서 자기 눈 아래 길러지고 있으니, 스왝컴의 가르침에서 잘못된 것은 무엇이든 자신이 바로잡을 수 있으리라 기대했다.\n\n올워디 씨는 방금 한 말 가운데 존스를 떠올리게 하는 어떤 애틋한 생각들이 되살아나, 이 선량한 사람의 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 그것을 알아챈 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"그래요, 그래요, 어른. 이 가엾은 청년에게 베푸신 어른의 자비는 아무리 감추려 애쓰셔도 다 알려져 있답니다. 하지만 그 악당들이 한 말에는 단 한 마디의 진실도 없어요. 나이팅게일 씨가 이제 일의 전모를 밝혀냈답니다. 보아하니 그 작자들은 가엾은 존스 씨의 연적인 어느 귀족에게 고용되어, 그를 배에 강제로 끌어다 태우려 했던 거예요. 정말이지, 그자들이 다음엔 또 누구를 잡아갈지 알 수가 없어요. 여기 이 나이팅게일 씨가 그 장교를 직접 만나 보았는데, 아주 점잖은 신사더래요. 그가 모든 걸 털어놓고는 자기가 맡았던 일을 몹시 뉘우치더랍니다. 존스 씨가 신사인 줄 알았더라면 결코 그런 일을 하지 않았을 텐데, 그가 흔해빠진 떠돌이 부랑자라는 말을 들었다지 뭐예요.\"\n\n올워디는 이 모든 말을 멍하니 바라보다가, 그녀가 한 말 한 마디 한 마디가 도무지 처음 듣는 이야기라고 잘라 말했다. \"그렇겠지요, 어른.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그러실 줄 알았어요. 아마 그 작자들이 그 변호사에게 한 이야기와는 사뭇 다른 이야기겠지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 변호사 말이오, 부인? 대체 무슨 소리요?\" 올워디가 물었다. \"어머나, 어머나,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이렇게 자기 자비를 한사코 부인하시는 게 꼭 어른답네요. 하지만 여기 나이팅게일 씨가 그를 봤다니까요.\" \"누구를 봤다는 거요, 부인?\" 그가 대답했다. \"아니, 어른의 변호사 말이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어른께서 그 일을 알아보라고 친절히 보내신 그 변호사 말이지요.\" \"맹세코, 나는 아직도 영문을 모르겠소.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그럼 어른, 직접 말씀해 보세요.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"사실은, 어른,\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"제가 이 방에 들어왔을 때 어른 곁에서 막 나가던 바로 그 변호사를, 올더스게이트의 어느 선술집에서 보았습니다. 펠라마 경에게 고용되어 존스 씨를 강제로 끌어가려 했던, 그래서 그와 피츠패트릭 씨 사이의 그 불행한 충돌 현장에 있었던 두 작자와 함께 있더군요.\" \"사실 어른,\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"이 신사분이 어른께 다가오는 걸 봤을 때, 저는 나이팅게일 씨에게 어른께서 일을 알아보라고 저 사람을 거기 보내신 모양이라고 말했답니다.\" 올워디는 이 소식에 얼굴 가득 놀라움을 드러냈고, 정말이지 이삼 분 동안은 그 충격에 말문이 막혔다. 마침내 그는 나이팅게일 씨를 향해 말했다. \"솔직히 말하건대, 선생, 나는 당신이 들려준 이야기에 내 평생 어떤 일을 두고도 느껴 본 적 없을 만큼 놀랐소. 그게 정말 그 신사가 틀림없소?\" \"틀림없습니다.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"올더스게이트에서 말이오?\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그리고 당신이 그 변호사와 두 작자와 함께 있었단 말이오?\" \"그렇습니다, 어른.\" 그가 말했다. \"거의 반 시간 가까이 있었지요.\" \"그래, 선생,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그래서 그 변호사는 어찌 행동했소? 그와 작자들 사이에 오간 말을 다 들었소?\" \"아닙니다, 어른.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"제가 가기 전에 이미 함께 있었던 모양입니다. 제 앞에서 변호사는 별말이 없었지요. 하지만 제가 그 작자들을 여러 차례 캐물어도, 그들이 존스 씨에게 들은 것과 정반대인 이야기를, 그러니까 피츠패트릭 씨 말로는 새빨간 거짓말인 이야기를 끝까지 고집하자, 그제야 변호사가 작자들에게 진실 아닌 것은 아무 말도 하지 말라고 당부하더군요. 그러면서 존스 씨를 어찌나 두둔하던지, 어른 곁에 있던 같은 사람을 봤을 때 저는 어른의 자비로운 마음이 그를 거기 보내신 모양이라고 결론지었던 겁니다.\" \"그럼 어른께서 그를 거기 보내신 게 아닌가요?\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"정말로 아니오.\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"이 순간까지 그가 그런 심부름을 갔다는 것조차 몰랐소.\" \"이제 다 알겠어요!\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"맹세코, 다 알겠다고요! 어쩐지 요즘 그렇게 둘이 바짝 붙어 쑥덕거리더라니. 나이팅게일, 부디 지금 당장 그 작자들을 잡으러 달려가 줘요. 땅 위에 있기만 하다면 꼭 찾아내요. 나도 직접 가겠어요.\" \"부인,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"진정하시오. 그리고 부디 하인을 위층에 올려 보내, 다울링 씨가 집 안에 있거든 이리 불러 주시오. 없거든 블리필 씨라도.\" 밀러 부인은 혼자 무어라 중얼거리며 나가더니, 이내 대답을 가지고 돌아왔다. \"다울링 씨는 떠났고, 그 사람은,\" 그녀는 블리필을 그렇게 불렀다. \"오는 중이라네요.\"\n\n올워디는 친구를 위해 온통 분기충천한 이 선량한 부인보다 한결 차분한 기질이었다. 그러나 그에게도 그녀의 것과 사촌쯤 되는 의심이 없지는 않았다. 블리필이 방에 들어오자, 그는 더없이 엄숙한 얼굴로, 그리고 일찍이 그에게 보낸 적 없는 덜 다정한 눈빛으로 물었다. \"다울링 씨가 존스와 어느 신사 사이의 결투 현장에 있었던 사람들 가운데 누구든 만났다는 일에 대해, 자네가 뭔가 아는 게 있는가?\"\n\n진실을 숨기거나 거짓을 변호하는 것이 본분인 사람에게 불시에 날아드는 질문만큼 위험한 것은 없다. 그런 까닭에, 올드베일리 법정에서 동족의 목숨을 구하는 고귀한 직무를 맡은 그 훌륭한 인물들은, 사전에 거듭 검토하여 재판 당일 의뢰인에게 던져질 만한 모든 질문을 미리 헤아려 두려 온갖 정성을 기울인다. 그래야만 적절하고 즉각적인 답변을 갖추어 둘 수 있으니, 아무리 비옥한 재간이라도 그 자리에서 즉석에 만들어 낼 수 없는 것이기 때문이다. 게다가 이런 불시의 충격이 피에 일으키는 갑작스럽고 격렬한 동요는, 흔히 얼굴빛을 크게 바꾸어 놓아, 그 사람이 자기에게 불리한 증거를 스스로 내놓게 만든다. 실로 이 갑작스러운 질문에 블리필의 얼굴빛이 겪은 변화가 어찌나 컸던지, 우리는 당장 \"맹세코 유죄예요! 영혼을 걸고 유죄라고요!\" 하고 외친 밀러 부인의 성급함을 차마 탓할 수 없을 정도다.\n\n올워디 씨는 그 성마름을 두고 그녀를 날카롭게 나무랐다. 그러고는 땅속으로 꺼져 들어갈 것만 같은 블리필을 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"왜 내게 대답하기를 망설이는가? 자네가 분명 그를 부린 게 틀림없네. 그가 제 발로, 더구나 내게 알리지도 않고 그런 심부름을 떠맡았을 리는 없으니 말일세.\"\n\n그러자 블리필이 대답했다. \"솔직히 말씀드리면, 어른, 제가 잘못을 저질렀습니다. 그래도 용서를 바랄 수 있을까요?\" \"용서라니.\" 올워디가 몹시 화를 내며 말했다. \"아니, 어른,\" 블리필이 대답했다. \"노하실 줄 알았습니다. 하지만 분명 사랑하는 외숙께서는 인간의 가장 사랑스러운 약점이 빚어낸 결과를 용서해 주시리라 믿습니다. 받을 자격 없는 자에 대한 동정이 죄임은 저도 압니다. 그러나 그것은 어른 자신도 온전히 벗어나 있지 못한 죄이기도 하지요. 저 역시 바로 이 사람에게 한두 번 그 죄를 저질렀음을 압니다. 그리고 솔직히 고백하건대, 제가 다울링 씨를 보낸 것은 헛되고 부질없는 조사를 위해서가 아니라, 증인들을 찾아내어 그 증언을 누그러뜨려 보려는 것이었습니다. 이것이, 어른, 진실입니다. 어른께 감추려 했던 것이지만, 부인하지는 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드리면,\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"그 신사분의 행동으로 보아 제게도 그렇게 비쳤습니다.\"\n\n\"자, 부인,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"이제 부인도 평생에 한 번쯤은 자신이 잘못된 의심을 품었음을 인정하고, 전처럼 내 조카에게 화내지 않게 되리라 믿소.\"\n\n밀러 부인은 입을 다물었다. 존스를 파멸시킨 장본인으로 여겨 온 블리필이 그리 선뜻 마음에 들 리야 없었지만, 적어도 이 일에서만큼은 그가 나머지 사람들과 더불어 그녀까지도 감쪽같이 속였던 것이다. 그토록 철저히 악마가 그의 편을 들어 주었던 셈이다. 사실 나는 \"악마는 흔히 제 친구를 저버리고 곤경에 빠뜨린다\"는 저 흔한 말이야말로, 그 신사의 인품을 크게 욕보이는 것이라 본다. 어쩌면 그는 한낱 술친구에 지나지 않는 자들이나, 기껏해야 절반쯤만 제 사람인 자들은 이따금 저버릴지도 모른다. 그러나 철두철미하게 제 종이 된 자들에게는 대개 끝까지 곁을 지키며, 그 거래의 기한이 다할 때까지 어떤 위기에서도 빠져나오도록 도와주는 법이다.\n\n진압된 반란이 정부를 더욱 굳건히 하듯, 또 어떤 병에서 회복되고 나면 건강이 한층 더 완전하게 자리 잡듯, 노여움도 가시고 나면 흔히 애정에 새 생명을 불어넣는다. 올워디 씨의 경우가 바로 그러했다. 블리필이 더 큰 의심을 씻어 내자, 스퀘어의 편지가 불러일으켰던 작은 의심은 당연히 가라앉아 잊히고 말았다. 그래서 그가 몹시 노여워하던 스왝컴이, 스퀘어가 존스의 적들에게 퍼부었던 온갖 비난을 혼자 고스란히 뒤집어쓰게 되었다.\n\n그 청년으로 말하자면, 올워디 씨의 원망은 점점 더 누그러져 갔다. 그는 블리필에게 말했다. \"자네의 그 유별난 선의의 노력을 용서할 뿐만 아니라, 자네에게 그 본을 따르는 기쁨까지 안겨 주겠네.\" 그러고는 천사에게나 어울릴 법한 미소를 띠고 밀러 부인을 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"어떻소, 부인? 우리 모두 삯마차를 잡아타고 함께 부인의 친구를 찾아가 보지 않겠소? 장담컨대, 내가 감옥을 찾아가는 것이 이번이 처음은 아니라오.\"\n\n독자라면 누구나 이 갸륵한 부인의 마음을 능히 헤아릴 수 있으리라 믿는다. 다만 이 자리에서 그녀가 느낀 바를 온전히 느끼려면, 더없이 너그러운 천성을 지니고 우정이 무엇인지를 깊이 아는 사람이라야 할 것이다. 한편 이제 블리필의 마음속을 스쳐 간 것을 느낄 수 있는 사람은 거의 없기를 바란다. 그러나 그것을 느낄 줄 아는 사람이라면, 그가 이 방문에 어떤 이의도 제기할 수 없었으리라는 점을 인정할 것이다. 그렇지만 운명이, 아니 앞서 말한 그 신사가 그의 편을 들어, 그가 그토록 큰 충격을 겪는 일을 막아 주었다. 바로 마차를 부르러 보낸 그 순간 파트리지가 도착하여, 밀러 부인을 사람들에게서 따로 불러내고는 최근에 드러난 그 끔찍한 사건을 알려 준 것이다. 그러고는 올워디 씨의 뜻을 듣자, 무슨 수를 써서라도 그를 말려 달라고 그녀에게 간청했다. \"왜냐하면,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 일은 무슨 일이 있어도 그분께는 비밀로 해야 합니다. 만일 지금 가신다면, 존스 씨와 그 어머니가, 제가 그를 떠날 때 막 도착한 그 어머니가, 자기들이 모르고 저지른 그 끔찍한 죄를 두고 서로 부둥켜안고 탄식하는 꼴을 보시게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n이 무시무시한 소식에 정신이 거의 나갈 지경이 된 가엾은 부인은, 지금만큼 꾀를 짜낼 형편이 못 되는 때가 없었다. 그러나 여자는 남자보다 이런 데 훨씬 재빠른 법이라, 그녀는 핑곗거리 하나를 떠올리고는 올워디에게 돌아가 말했다. \"어른, 방금 친절히 내주신 제안에 제가 이의를 다는 걸 들으시면 분명 놀라시겠지요. 그래도 그것을 당장 실행에 옮기면 그 결과가 어찌 될지 두렵습니다. 어른, 생각해 보세요. 요즈음 이 가엾은 청년에게 닥친 그 모든 재앙이 그를 더없이 깊은 실의에 빠뜨려 놓았을 게 틀림없습니다. 그런 마당에 우리가 느닷없이, 어른의 행차가 불러일으킬 것이 분명한 그토록 격렬한 기쁨의 발작 속으로 그를 내던진다면, 무슨 치명적인 화를 부를까 두렵습니다. 더구나 밖에 있는 그의 하인 말로는, 그가 도무지 성치 못하다고 하니 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그의 하인이 밖에 있소?\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"어서 이리 불러 주시오. 그 주인에 관해 몇 가지 물어봐야겠소.\"\n\n파트리지는 처음에는 올워디 씨 앞에 나서기를 두려워했다. 그러나 그의 입에서 그 사연 전부를 여러 차례 들어 온 밀러 부인이 그를 소개해 주겠노라 약속하자, 마침내 마음을 돌렸다.\n\n올워디는 그가 방에 들어서는 순간 파트리지를 알아보았다. 그를 본 지 여러 해가 지났는데도 그러했다. 그러니 밀러 부인은 여기서 격식 차린 일장 연설을 굳이 늘어놓지 않아도 좋았을 것이다. 실은 그 연설이 다소 장황하기도 했다. 독자라면 이미 알아챘으리라 믿거니와, 이 선량한 부인은 다른 여러 재주와 더불어, 친구를 위해서라면 언제든 술술 놀려 줄 혀를 지니고 있었으니 말이다.\n\n\"그래, 자네가,\" 올워디가 파트리지에게 말했다. \"존스 씨의 하인인가?\" \"글쎄요, 어른,\" 그가 대답했다. \"제가 어엿한 하인이라고는 못 하겠습니다만, 황송하게도 지금은 그분과 함께 지내고 있습죠. 논 숨 콸리스 에람, 어른께서도 익히 아시는 대로지요.\"\n\n* 논 숨 콸리스 에람(Non sum qualis eram): \"나는 예전의 내가 아니다\"라는 뜻의 라틴어.\n이어서 올워디 씨는 존스에 관해, 그의 건강이며 그 밖의 여러 일에 대해 그에게 숱한 질문을 던졌다. 파트리지는 그 모든 물음에 사실이 어떠한지는 조금도 개의치 않고, 오직 일들이 어찌 보이기를 바라는지만 헤아려 대답했다. 진실을 곧이곧대로 지키는 것은 이 정직한 친구의 도덕이나 신앙의 조목에 들어 있지 않았던 것이다.\n\n이 문답이 오가는 동안 나이팅게일 씨는 작별을 고했고, 곧이어 밀러 부인도 방을 나갔다. 그러자 올워디 역시 블리필을 내보냈다. 파트리지가 자기와 단둘이 있으면 여럿 앞에서보다 더 솔직히 털어놓으리라 짐작했기 때문이다. 둘만 따로 남게 되자마자 올워디는 다음 장에서와 같이 입을 열었다.\n\n“정말이지, 친구여,” 선한 노인이 말했다. “자네는 인간 가운데 가장 알 수 없는 사람일세. 예전에 거짓을 끝끝내 고집하다가 그렇게 고초를 겪고도, 지금까지도 그것을 끝까지 우기다니. 게다가 자기 친아들의 하인 행세를 하며 세상을 속이다니! 이 모든 일에서 자네가 얻을 이익이 무엇인가? 도대체 무슨 속셈인가?”\n\n“보아하니, 나리,” 파트리지가 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. “나리께서는 저를 미리 못 믿기로 작정하시고, 제가 무슨 말을 해도 곧이듣지 않으려 하시니, 제 항변이 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까? 그래도 위에 한 분 계시니, 그분만은 제가 이 청년의 아비가 아님을 아십니다.”\n\n“뭐라고!” 올워디가 말했다. “그토록 반박할 수 없고 그토록 명백한 증거로 예전에 유죄가 입증된 일을, 아직도 부인하려는가? 아니, 자네가 지금 바로 이 사람과 함께 있는 것 자체가, 이십 년 전 자네에게 불리하게 드러났던 모든 것을 다시금 확증하는 셈이 아닌가! 나는 자네가 이 고장을 떠난 줄로만 알았네! 아니, 오래전에 죽은 줄로만 여겼네. 그래, 자네가 이 청년에 대해 어떻게 알게 되었는가? 둘이 무슨 연락을 주고받지 않았다면, 어디서 그를 만났단 말인가? 그것을 부인하지 말게. 약속하건대, 자네 아들이 그토록 여러 해 동안 남몰래 제 아비를 보살펴 온 효심을 지녔다는 사실을 알게 되면, 그 아이에 대한 내 평가가 크게 올라갈 테니 말일세.”\n\n“나리께서 인내심을 갖고 제 말을 들어주신다면,” 파트리지가 말했다. “모두 말씀드리겠습니다.” 그리고 계속하라는 분부를 받고 이렇게 이어 갔다. “나리께서 저에게 그 노여움을 품으셨을 때, 그 일은 머지않아 저의 파멸로 끝났습니다. 저는 작은 학교를 잃었고, 목사님께서는, 제 짐작에 그것이 나리의 뜻에 맞으리라 여기셨던지, 저를 서기 자리에서도 내치셨습니다. 그래서 제게 기댈 것이라곤 이발소밖에 남지 않았는데, 그런 시골 동네에서야 그것으로는 입에 풀칠하기도 어려웠지요. 그러다 아내가 죽었습니다(그때까지는 어느 알 수 없는 손길에서 해마다 12파운드의 연금을 받았는데, 실은 그것이 나리께서 손수 베푸신 것이라 저는 믿습니다. 제가 들어 본 바로는 그런 일을 하는 분이 나리 말고는 달리 없으니까요). 어쨌든, 말씀드린 대로 아내가 죽자 그 연금도 끊겼습니다. 그래서 이제, 자질구레한 빚이 두세 건 있었는데 그것이 점점 골칫거리가 되기 시작했고, 특히 한 건은 어느 변호사가 소송 비용을 붙여 15실링짜리를 거의 30파운드 가까이로 불려 놓았으며, 게다가 늘 의지하던 생계 수단이 죄다 저를 저버린 것을 알게 되자, 저는 가진 보잘것없는 살림을 할 수 있는 만큼 챙겨 떠나 버렸습니다.\n\n* 이것은 내가 실제로 도싯셔의 어느 가난한 성직자에게 일어난 줄로 아는 사건이다. 어느 변호사의 악행 때문이었는데, 그는 단 한 건의 소송으로 그 가엾은 사람에게 터무니없는 비용을 물리고도 만족하지 못하고, 나중에는 이른바 판결을 빌미로 또 다른 소송을 걸었다. 이는 가난한 자를 억누르고 변호사들의 주머니를 채우는 데 흔히 쓰이는 수법으로, 법과 나라와 기독교에, 나아가 인간 본성 그 자체에까지 크나큰 수치를 안기는 일이다.\n\n“제가 처음 다다른 곳은 솔즈베리였습니다. 거기서 법조계에 몸담은 어느 신사의 시중을 들게 되었는데, 제가 아는 한 가장 훌륭한 신사 가운데 한 분이었습니다. 저에게 잘해 주셨을 뿐 아니라, 제가 모시는 동안 그분이 베푸신 선하고 자비로운 일을 천 가지는 알고 있으니까요. 더구나 일감이 옹졸하고 가혹한 것이면 마다하시는 모습도 여러 번 보았습니다.” “그렇게 시시콜콜 말할 것 없네,” 올워디가 말했다. “나도 그 신사를 알거니와, 참으로 훌륭한 사람이요 그 직업의 영예라 할 만하지.” “그렇습니다, 나리,” 파트리지가 계속했다. “거기서 저는 리밍턴으로 옮겨, 또 다른 변호사 밑에서 삼 년 남짓 일했습니다. 그분 역시 무척 좋은 분이었고, 확실히 잉글랜드에서 가장 유쾌한 신사 가운데 한 분이었지요. 자, 나리, 삼 년이 끝날 무렵 저는 작은 학교를 열었고, 더없이 불운한 사고만 아니었다면 다시 잘 풀릴 참이었습니다. 그때 저는 돼지를 한 마리 길렀는데, 어느 날 하필 운이 나쁘려고 그 돼지가 우리를 뛰쳐나가, 이웃 가운데 한 사람의 정원에 들어가 무슨 침입이라던가 하는 짓을 저질렀습니다. 그 이웃이라는 자가 거만하고 앙심 깊은 사람이라, 변호사를 한 명 고용했는데, 그자가, 그자가, 이름이 도무지 떠오르지 않습니다만, 어쨌든 그자가 저를 상대로 영장을 청구해 저를 순회 재판소에 끌어냈습니다. 제가 거기 갔을 때, 주여 저를 불쌍히 여기소서, 그 변호사들이 늘어놓는 말을 들어 보셨어야 합니다! 그중 하나는 판사 나리께 저에 대해 가장 어처구니없는 거짓말 보따리를 풀어 놓았습니다. 제가 늘 남의 정원으로 돼지를 몰아넣는다는 둥, 그밖에도 잔뜩 늘어놓더니, 마침내는 제가 드디어 돼지를 제값에 팔게 되기를 바란다고까지 했습니다. 정말이지, 그 말만 들으면 제가 가엾고 조그만 돼지 한 마리의 주인이 아니라 잉글랜드에서 가장 큰 돼지 상인이라도 되는 줄 알았을 겁니다. 그래서—” “제발,” 올워디가 말했다. “그렇게 시시콜콜 늘어놓지 말게. 나는 아직 자네 아들 이야기는 한마디도 듣지 못했네.” “아, 그건 아주 여러 해가 지난 뒤였습니다,” 파트리지가 대답했다. “나리께서 굳이 제 아들이라 부르시는 그 사람을 제가 본 것은요. 이 일이 있은 뒤 저는 아일랜드로 건너가 코크에서 학교를 가르쳤습니다(그 한 건의 소송이 저를 또 망쳐 놓아, 저는 윈체스터 감옥에서 칠 년을 살았으니까요).” “그래,” 올워디가 말했다. “그 대목은 건너뛰고 자네가 잉글랜드로 돌아온 때부터 말하게.” “그러면, 나리,” 그가 말했다. “반년쯤 전에 저는 브리스틀에 닿았고, 거기서 얼마간 머물렀습니다만, 그곳에서 일이 잘 풀리지 않았습니다. 그러다 그곳과 글로스터 사이 어느 마을에서 이발사가 막 죽었다는 소식을 듣고 그리로 갔지요. 거기서 두 달쯤 지냈을 무렵 존스 도련님이 그곳에 오신 것입니다.” 그러고 나서 그는 올워디에게 둘이 처음 만난 일과, 기억나는 한 그날부터 오늘까지 일어난 모든 일을 아주 시시콜콜 들려주었다. 이야기 사이사이 존스에 대한 찬사를 자주 끼워 넣었고, 자신이 올워디를 향해 품은 크나큰 사랑과 존경을 넌지시 비치는 것도 잊지 않았다. 그는 이렇게 끝맺었다. “자, 나리, 저는 나리께 모든 진실을 말씀드렸습니다.” 그러고는 더없이 엄숙한 항변을 되풀이했다. “제가 존스의 아비라면 로마 교황의 아비이기도 하다”고. 그리고 자기가 진실을 말하는 것이 아니라면 가장 모진 저주가 제 머리 위에 떨어지기를 빌었다.\n\n“이 일을 어떻게 받아들여야 한단 말인가?” 올워디가 외쳤다. “인정하는 편이 오히려 자네에게 이로울 듯한 사실을, 무슨 까닭으로 그토록 한사코 부인하는가?” “아니올시다, 나리,” 파트리지가 대답했다(더는 참을 수 없었기 때문이다). “나리께서 저를 믿지 않으시겠다면, 머잖아 충분히 납득하시게 될 것입니다. 나리께서 이 청년의 아비를 잘못 아신 것처럼, 그 어미도 잘못 아신 것이라면 좋으련만.” 그러자 무슨 뜻이냐는 물음을 받고, 그는 목소리에도 얼굴에도 온갖 공포의 빛을 띤 채, 조금 전 밀러 부인에게 나리께는 부디 숨겨 달라고 그토록 간곡히 청했던 그 이야기를 통째로 올워디에게 털어놓았다.\n\n올워디는 이 폭로에 거의, 파트리지가 그것을 이야기하는 동안 스스로 받았던 만큼이나 큰 충격을 받았다. “하느님 맙소사!” 그가 말했다. “악덕과 경솔함이 사람을 어떤 비참한 곤경으로 몰아넣는단 말인가! 사악함의 결과가 때로는 우리가 꾀한 것을 얼마나 멀리 넘어서 치닫는단 말인가!” 이 말을 채 마치기도 전에, 워터스 부인이 황급히, 그리고 느닷없이 방으로 들어왔다. 파트리지는 그녀를 보자마자 외쳤다. “여기, 나리, 바로 그 여인이 여기 있습니다. 이분이 바로 존스 도련님의 불행한 어머니입니다. 분명 이분이 나리 앞에서 저의 결백을 밝혀 주실 겁니다. 부탁입니다, 부인—”\n\n워터스 부인은 파트리지의 말에는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고, 거의 그를 거들떠보지도 않은 채 올워디 씨에게 다가갔다. “나리, 제가 나리를 뵙는 영광을 누린 지 너무 오래되어, 저를 기억하지 못하시리라 생각합니다.” “사실이오,” 올워디가 대답했다. “부인은 여러모로 너무나 변하셔서, 만일 이 사람이 부인이 누구신지 이미 일러 주지 않았더라면 곧바로 떠올리지는 못했을 것이오. 부인, 무슨 특별한 용무가 있어 나를 찾아오셨소?” 올워디는 이 말을 무척 조심스럽게 했다. 독자도 능히 짐작하겠지만, 그는 이 부인의 행실이 — 예전에 들었던 것이든 지금 파트리지가 전한 것이든 — 도무지 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다.\n\n워터스 부인이 대답했다. “나리, 실은 나리께 아주 특별한 용무가 있습니다. 그것도 오직 나리께만 전할 수 있는 일이지요. 그러니 청하건대, 나리와 단둘이 한마디 나눌 수 있는 은혜를 베풀어 주십시오. 제가 말씀드릴 것은 더없이 중대한 일임을 분명히 말씀드립니다.”\n\n이에 파트리지는 물러가라는 분부를 받았으나, 나가기 전에 그는 부인에게 자기가 완전히 결백하다는 것을 올워디 씨께 밝혀 달라고 간청했다. 그러자 그녀가 대답했다. “걱정하실 것 없습니다. 그 일에 관해서는 제가 올워디 나리께 아주 완벽하게 밝혀 드리겠습니다.”\n\n그러고 나서 파트리지가 물러갔고, 올워디 씨와 워터스 부인 사이에 오간 이야기는 다음 장에 적혀 있다.\n\n이야기의 계속.\n\n워터스 부인이 잠시 말없이 있자, 올워디 씨는 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"부인, 그 뒤로 제가 들은 바로 미루어, 부인께서 그토록 좋지 못한 데에 쓰셨다는 것을 알게 되어 유감스럽습니다——\" \"올워디 씨.\" 그녀가 그의 말을 가로채며 말했다. \"저도 제게 허물이 있음을 압니다. 하지만 당신께 은혜를 모르는 것만큼은 그 허물에 들지 않습니다. 저는 당신의 자비를 결코 잊을 수 없고, 잊지도 않을 것입니다. 그것을 받을 자격이 거의 없었음은 인정합니다. 다만 지금은 저를 나무라는 일은 모두 미뤄 두시기를 청합니다. 당신께서 제 처녀 적 이름인 존스를 붙여 주신 이 젊은이에 관하여, 너무도 중대한 일을 알려 드려야 하기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"방금 우리 곁을 떠난 그 사람을 두고, 죄 없는 사람을 모르고 벌한 것입니까? 그가 그 아이의 아버지가 아니었단 말입니까?\" \"정말이지 그는 아니었습니다.\" 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"기억하실지 모르겠습니다만, 선생님, 제가 예전에 언젠가는 아시게 될 것이라고 말씀드린 적이 있지요. 그리고 그것을 진작 밝히지 않은 잔인한 태만의 죄를 제가 저질렀음을 인정합니다. 사실 저는 그것이 얼마나 필요한 일인지 거의 알지 못했습니다.\" \"그럼, 부인.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"어서 계속하시지요.\" \"기억하셔야 합니다, 선생님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"서머라는 이름의 젊은이를 말입니다.\" \"잘 압니다.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그는 학식과 덕이 깊은 어느 성직자의 아들이었고, 저는 그분께 더없는 우정을 품고 있었지요.\" \"그러셨던 모양입니다, 선생님.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"당신께서 그 젊은이를 길러 주시고 대학까지 학비를 대 주신 것으로 압니다. 그가 당신 댁에 와서 지내게 되었을 무렵에는, 제 생각으로는 학업을 마쳤던 것 같습니다. 정말이지 그보다 더 잘난 사내에게 해가 비친 적은 없었다고 말해야겠습니다. 제가 본 가장 잘생긴 사람이었을 뿐 아니라, 어찌나 기품이 있고, 또 재치와 좋은 교양을 갖추었던지요.\" \"가엾은 사람.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그는 정말이지 때 이르게 빼앗기고 말았지요. 그가 이런 종류의 죄를 책임져야 하리라고는 조금도 생각지 못했습니다. 부인께서 그가 당신 아이의 아버지였다고 말하려 하심을 분명히 알겠으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 선생님.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그는 아니었습니다.\" \"어찌된 일입니까!\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그렇다면 이 모든 서두는 다 무엇을 향한 것입니까?\" \"한 이야기입니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신께 풀어 놓는 일이 제 몫으로 떨어진 것이 마음 아픈 그런 이야기지요. 오, 선생님! 당신을 놀라게 하고, 또 슬프게 할 무언가를 들으실 마음의 준비를 하십시오.\" \"말하시오.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"나는 어떤 죄도 짊어진 것이 없으니, 듣기를 두려워할 까닭이 없습니다.\" \"선생님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신 벗의 아들이요 당신의 비용으로 교육받았던 그 서머 씨, 마치 당신의 친아들처럼 일 년을 그 댁에서 살다가 그곳에서 천연두로 죽고, 당신께서 따뜻이 애도하시며 친아들처럼 묻어 주신 그 서머 씨, 그 사람이, 선생님, 바로 이 아이의 아버지였습니다.\" \"어찌된 일이오!\" 올워디가 말했다. \"당신은 스스로 모순되는 말을 하고 있소.\" \"그렇지 않습니다.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그는 정말로 이 아이의 아버지였으나, 저를 통해서가 아니었습니다.\" \"조심하시오, 부인.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"어떤 죄의 혐의를 피하려고 거짓을 짓는 죄를 범하지 마시오. 당신이 아무것도 숨길 수 없는 한 분이 계심을, 그리고 그분의 법정 앞에서는 거짓이 당신의 죄를 더 무겁게 할 뿐임을 기억하시오.\" \"정말이지, 선생님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 그의 어머니가 아닙니다. 또한 이제 와서 무슨 일이 있어도 제 자신을 그렇게 여기지는 않겠습니다.\" \"당신의 이유를 압니다.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그리고 사실이 다르다는 것을 알게 된다면 나도 당신만큼이나 기뻐할 것입니다. 그러나 당신 스스로 내 앞에서 그것을 인정했음을 기억해야 합니다.\" \"제가 인정한 것 가운데서 이만큼은 사실이었습니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"바로 이 두 손이 그 갓난아이를 당신의 침상으로 옮겼다는 것 말입니다. 그 아이의 어머니가 명한 대로 그곳에 옮겨 놓았고, 그 명령에 따라 나중에 제가 그 아이를 제 아이라 인정했으며, 그분의 너그러움 덕분에 제 침묵과 제 수치에 대해 모두 후하게 보상받았다고 여겼습니다.\" \"그 여인이 대체 누구였소?\" 올워디가 말했다. \"정말이지, 그분의 이름을 입에 올리기가 떨립니다.\" 워터스 부인이 대답했다. \"이 모든 준비를 보아하니, 그 여인이 내 친척이었다고 짐작해야겠군요.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"정말로 가까운 분이셨습니다.\" 그 말에 올워디가 흠칫 놀랐고, 그녀는 말을 이었다——\"당신께는 누이가 한 분 계셨지요, 선생님.\" \"누이라!\" 그가 아연실색한 얼굴로 되뇌었다.——\"하늘에 진실이 있는 것이 분명하듯이,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"당신의 누이가 바로 당신이 침상 시트 사이에서 발견하신 그 아이의 어머니였습니다.\" \"그게 가능한 일이오?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"맙소사!\" \"참으십시오, 선생님.\" 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"제가 그 모든 이야기를 풀어 드리겠습니다. 당신께서 런던으로 떠나신 직후, 어느 날 브리짓 아씨가 제 어머니의 집으로 오셨습니다. 그분은 제가 학식과, 그곳의 어느 젊은 여인들보다도 뛰어난 이해력으로 비범한 평판을 얻고 있다는 말을 들으셨다고, 친절히 말씀해 주셨습니다. 그러고는 큰 저택으로 자기를 찾아오라 하셨고, 제가 거기 들자 책을 읽어 달라 시키셨습니다. 그분은 제 낭독에 큰 만족을 보이시고, 제게 많은 친절을 베푸시며 여러 선물을 주셨습니다. 마침내 그분은 비밀을 지키는 일에 관해 저를 시험하듯 캐물으셨고, 제가 어찌나 흡족한 대답을 드렸던지, 결국에는 방문을 잠그시더니 저를 자신의 골방으로 데려가셨습니다. 그 방문마저 잠그신 다음, 그분은 자신의 명예가, 따라서 자신의 목숨이 걸린 비밀 하나를 털어놓음으로써 제 정직함에 얼마나 큰 의지를 두고 계신지 보여 주겠노라 하셨습니다. 그러고는 멈추셨고, 몇 분 동안의 침묵 끝에—그동안 자주 눈물을 훔치셨지요—제 어머니를 안심하고 믿어도 되겠느냐고 제게 물으셨습니다. 저는 어머니의 충직함에 제 목숨을 걸겠노라고 대답했습니다. 그러자 그분은 가슴속에서 진통처럼 끓던 그 크나큰 비밀을 제게 털어놓으셨는데, 제 생각에 그것은 그분이 나중에 해산할 때 겪으신 것보다 더한 고통과 함께 쏟아져 나온 것이었습니다. 그리하여 그 일이 있을 때에는 오직 제 어머니와 저만이 곁을 지키고, 윌킨스 부인은 멀리 떨어진 곳으로 보내기로 짜였습니다. 실제로 그분은 도싯셔의 가장 먼 곳까지, 어느 하인의 평판을 알아보라며 윌킨스 부인을 보내셨지요. 그 아씨는 석 달쯤 전에 자기 시녀를 내보내셨고, 그동안 내내, 아씨 말씀으로는 시험 삼아, 제가 그분의 시중을 들었습니다. 비록 나중에 그분께서는 제가 그 자리에 충분히 손이 야무지지 못하다고 말씀하셨지만요. 이 말이며, 그분이 저에 관해 늘 하시던 다른 여러 말들은, 나중에 제가 그 아이를 제 아이라 인정할 때 윌킨스가 품을지도 모를 의심을 미리 막으려고 던지신 것이었습니다. 그런 비밀을 맡긴 젊은 여인에게 굳이 해를 끼치려 들리라고는 누구도 결코 믿지 않으리라 여기셨기 때문이지요. 선생님, 분명히 말씀드리지만, 저는 이 모든 모욕에 대해 후하게 보상받았고, 그 모욕의 까닭까지 들어 알게 되었으니 매우 만족스러웠습니다. 사실 그 아씨는 다른 어느 누구보다 윌킨스 부인을 더 의심하셨습니다. 그 부인을 조금이라도 싫어하셔서가 아니라, 그 부인이 비밀을 지키지 못하는 사람, 특히 당신께는 더욱 그러한 사람이라고 여기셨기 때문입니다. 브리짓 아씨께서 자주 하시던 말씀이, 윌킨스 부인이 살인을 저질렀다 해도 결국 당신께 그 일을 일러바칠 것이라 하셨으니까요. 마침내 기다리던 날이 왔고, 일주일 동안 채비를 갖춘 채 대기하던 윌킨스 부인은, 너무 일찍 돌아오지 못하도록 이런저런 핑계로 거듭 출발이 미뤄지다가, 드디어 보내졌습니다. 그러고서 아이가 태어났는데, 오직 저와 제 어머니만이 곁에 있었고, 제 어머니가 그 아이를 자기 집으로 데려가 당신께서 돌아오시는 날 저녁까지 몰래 두었습니다. 그날 저녁 저는 브리짓 아씨의 명에 따라 그 아이를, 당신께서 발견하신 그 침상으로 옮겨 놓았습니다. 그리고 그 뒤로 모든 의심은, 당신 누이의 교묘한 처신으로 잠재워졌지요. 그 사내아이에게 짐짓 미운 마음을 품은 척하시고, 그 아이에게 보이신 어떤 보살핌도 다만 당신을 기쁘게 해 드리려는 것일 뿐인 양 꾸미셨으니까요.\"\n\n워터스 부인은 이어서 이 이야기가 진실임을 여러 차례 다짐했고, 이렇게 맺었다. \"이리하여, 선생님, 당신께서는 마침내 당신의 조카를 찾으신 것입니다. 분명 앞으로는 그를 그렇게 여기시게 되리라 믿으며, 그가 그 호칭 아래에서 당신께 명예이자 위안이 되리라는 것을 저는 의심치 않습니다.\"\n\n\"부인, 굳이,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"당신께서 들려주신 이야기에 대한 제 놀라움을 표현할 필요는 없겠지요. 그렇지만 분명히, 거짓을 증명하려고 이토록 많은 정황을 엮어 내려 하지도, 또 엮어 낼 수도 없었을 것입니다. 솔직히 말해, 그 서머에 관한 몇 가지 일들이 떠오릅니다. 예전에 제 누이가 그에게 호감을 품고 있는 것이 아닌가 하는 생각을 갖게 한 일들 말입니다. 저는 그것을 누이에게 말했습니다. 저는 그 젊은이를, 그 자신을 위해서나 그의 아버지를 위해서나 무척 아꼈으므로, 둘의 혼인을 기꺼이 허락했을 것이기 때문이지요. 그러나 누이는 자기가 말하는 그 '매정한 의심'에 대해 더없는 경멸을 드러냈고, 그래서 저는 그 이야기를 다시는 입에 올리지 않았습니다. 맙소사! 아! 주께서 모든 일을 정하시는구나.——그렇더라도, 누이가 이 비밀을 안고 세상을 떠난 것은 도무지 변명할 수 없는 처사였습니다.\" \"맹세코 말씀드립니다만, 선생님.\" 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"그분은 늘 그와 반대되는 뜻을 밝히셨고, 언젠가는 당신께 그것을 알려 드릴 작정이라고 제게 자주 말씀하셨습니다. 사실 그분은 자신의 계략이 그토록 잘 들어맞았다는 것과, 당신께서 스스로 그 아이를 그토록 어여삐 여기게 되셨다는 것이 무척 기쁘다고 하셨지요. 그러니 아직은 굳이 분명한 선언을 할 필요가 없다고 말입니다. 오! 선생님, 그 아씨가 살아 계셔서 이 가엾은 젊은이가 부랑자처럼 당신 댁에서 쫓겨나는 꼴을 보셨더라면. 아니, 선생님, 그가 저지르지도 않은 살인죄로 그를 기소하라고 당신께서 손수 변호사를 부리셨다는 말을 들으실 때까지 살아 계셨더라면——용서하십시오, 올워디 씨, 그것은 매정한 일이었다고 말해야겠습니다.——정말이지 당신께서는 속으셨던 것입니다. 그는 결코 당신께 그런 대접을 받을 사람이 아니었습니다.\" \"정말이지, 부인.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그렇게 당신께 말한 사람이 누구든, 저는 그자에게 속은 것입니다.\" \"아니요, 선생님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오해를 사고 싶지 않습니다. 당신께서 무슨 그릇된 짓을 저지르셨다고 감히 말씀드린 것은 아닙니다. 저를 찾아온 그 신사는 그런 식의 말은 하지 않았습니다. 그는 다만, 저를 피츠패트릭 씨의 부인으로 알고서, 만일 존스 씨가 제 남편을 살해했다면, 매우 훌륭한 어느 신사분의 도움으로 제가 기소를 진행하는 데 필요한 돈을 얼마든지 받게 될 것이라고 했을 뿐입니다. 그분은 제가 상대해야 할 자가 얼마나 악당인지 잘 알고 계시다고 그가 말하더군요. 제가 존스 씨가 누구인지 알아낸 것도 바로 이 사람을 통해서였습니다. 그리고 이 사람, 이름이 다울링이라는데, 존스 씨 말로는 당신의 집사라더군요. 저는 아주 묘한 우연으로 그의 이름을 알게 되었습니다. 그 자신은 제게 이름 밝히기를 거부했으나, 그가 제 숙소에 두 번째로 왔을 때 마주친 파트리지가 예전에 솔즈베리에서 그를 알고 있었기 때문이지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 이 다울링이라는 자가,\" 올워디가 얼굴에 큰 놀라움을 띤 채 말했다. \"내가 그 기소를 돕겠다고 당신께 말했단 말입니까?\"——\"아니요, 선생님.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그분께 부당한 혐의를 씌우지는 않겠습니다. 그는 제가 도움을 받게 될 것이라고만 했지, 어떤 이름도 입에 올리지 않았습니다. 그러나 용서하십시오, 선생님. 정황으로 미루어 다른 사람일 리 없다고 생각한 것이니까요.\"——\"정말이지, 부인.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"저야말로 정황으로 미루어, 그것이 다른 자였음을 너무도 확실히 믿게 되었습니다. 맙소사! 가장 검고 깊은 악행이 때로는 얼마나 놀라운 방법으로 드러나는가!——부인, 당신께서 말씀하신 그 사람이 올 때까지 머물러 주시기를 청해도 되겠습니까? 그를 시시각각 기다리고 있으니까요. 아니, 어쩌면 그가 이미 이 집 안에 와 있을지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n그러고서 올워디는 하인을 부르려고 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼는데, 그때 들어온 사람은 다울링 씨가 아니라, 다음 장에서 보게 될 그 신사였다.\n\n지금 도착한 신사는 다름 아닌 웨스턴 씨였다. 그는 올워디를 보자마자, 워터스 부인이 그 자리에 있다는 것은 조금도 개의치 않고 다음과 같이 고래고래 소리치기 시작했다. \"내 집에서 아주 볼 만한 일이 벌어졌네! 마침내 기막힌 꼴을 찾아냈어! 도대체 어떤 작자가 딸년 때문에 이렇게 시달려야 한단 말인가?\" \"무슨 일이오, 이웃 양반?\" 올워디가 물었다. \"무슨 일이라니, 일이 차고 넘치오.\" 웨스턴이 대답했다. \"이제 막 그년이 마음을 돌리는가 싶었을 때, 아니, 사실상 내 뜻대로 하겠노라 약속까지 한 마당에, 변호사를 불러 모든 걸 끝맺기만 하면 더 할 일이 없겠거니 바라고 있던 그 순간에, 내가 무얼 알아냈는지 아시오? 그 어린 계집년이 그동안 내내 나를 속여먹고, 당신네 그 사생아 놈과 편지질을 하고 있었다지 뭐요. 그년 때문에 다투기까지 한 웨스턴 누이가 내게 그 사실을 일러주었고, 나는 그년이 잠든 사이 호주머니를 뒤지게 했더니, 여기 그 매춘부 자식 놈 이름이 떡하니 서명된 편지를 손에 넣었소. 절반도 채 읽을 인내심이 없었는데, 서플 목사의 설교보다 더 길더란 말이오. 하지만 죄다 사랑 타령인 건 분명히 알겠더이다. 사실 그것 말고 뭐가 더 있겠소? 그년을 다시 방에 가둬놨고, 내일 아침엔 곧장 시골로 내려보낼 작정이오. 당장 결혼하겠다고 동의하지 않는 한 말이오. 거기서 평생 다락방에 갇혀 빵과 물만 먹고 살게 할 거요. 그런 계집년이 가슴이 미어져 일찍 죽으면 죽을수록 좋겠지만, 빌어먹을, 그년은 너무 질겨서 그럴 것 같지도 않소. 나를 괴롭히려고 아주 오래 살 게요.\" \"웨스턴 씨,\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"내가 늘 강압에는 반대해왔다는 걸 당신도 알지 않소. 그리고 당신 스스로도 어떤 강압도 쓰지 않겠다 동의했소.\" \"그야,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그건 그년이 강압 없이도 동의한다는 조건에서나 그랬던 거지. 빌어먹을 파우스트 박사를 두고 맹세하건대, 내 딸을 내 마음대로 못한단 말이오? 그것도 그년 자신의 행복 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않는 마당에?\" \"그럼, 이웃 양반,\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"내게 허락해주신다면, 내가 한번 그 아가씨와 이야기를 나눠보겠소.\" \"그래주시겠소?\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"그거 참 친절하고 이웃다운 일이오. 어쩌면 당신이 내가 그년한테 못한 일을 해낼지도 모르겠구려. 장담컨대 그년은 당신을 무척 좋게 여기니까.\" \"좋소, 그럼.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"댁으로 돌아가셔서 그 아가씨를 감금에서 풀어주시오. 내가 반 시간 안에 찾아뵙겠소.\" \"한데 그사이,\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"그년이 그놈과 도망쳐버리면 어쩐단 말이오? 다울링 변호사 말로는 결국 그놈을 교수형에 처할 가망은 없다는데. 그 사내가 살아 있고, 호전될 것 같다고 하더이다. 그러니 존스가 곧 다시 감옥에서 풀려날 거라는 게요.\" \"뭐라고요!\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그럼 당신이 그 사람을 시켜 그 문제를 알아보거나 무슨 일을 하게 했단 말이오?\" \"내가 그런 게 아니오.\" 웨스턴이 대답했다. \"방금 그 사람이 제 발로 내게 그 얘기를 꺼냈소.\" \"방금이라니!\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"아니, 그럼 그 사람을 어디서 보셨소? 나는 다울링 씨를 몹시 만나고 싶소만.\" \"원한다면 지금 당장 내 숙소에서 만날 수 있소. 오늘 아침 거기서 저당 잡힌 일로 변호사들 모임이 있을 참이니. 빌어먹을! 그 정직하신 신사 나이팅게일 씨 덕분에, 내 보아하니 이삼천 파운드는 날리게 생겼소이다.\" \"좋소, 그럼.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"반 시간 안에 댁으로 가겠소.\" \"그리고 이번 한 번만,\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"어리석은 사람 충고를 들으시오. 그년을 부드러운 방법으로 다룰 생각일랑 절대 마시오. 내 말 믿으시오, 그런 건 절대 안 통하오. 나는 그걸 충분히 오래 시도해봤소. 겁을 줘서 몰아넣어야 하오. 다른 길은 없소. 내가 제 아비라는 걸 일러주고, 불효라는 끔찍한 죄와 저세상에서 그 죄에 따를 무서운 벌을 말해주시오. 그러고 나서 이 세상에서는 평생 다락방에 갇혀 빵과 물만 먹고 산다는 것도 일러주시오.\" \"내 할 수 있는 건 다 하겠소.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"장담컨대, 이 사랑스러운 아이와 사돈을 맺는 것보다 내가 더 바라는 일은 없으니 말이오.\" \"원, 뭐 그런 거라면 그 아이도 그만하면 괜찮지.\" 향사가 외쳤다. \"더 멀리 찾아가봤자 더 못한 고기나 만날 뿐이오. 내 딸이긴 하다만 그 정도는 내 단언할 수 있소. 그년이 내게 순종하기만 한다면, 이 일대 백 마일 안에 나만큼 딸을 사랑하는 아비는 없을 게요. 한데 보아하니 당신은 여기 이 부인과 볼일이 있는 듯하니, 나는 집으로 가서 당신을 기다리리다. 그럼, 이만 실례하오.\"\n\n웨스턴 씨가 떠나자마자 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"보아하니, 어르신, 저 향사께서는 제 얼굴을 조금도 기억하지 못하시는군요. 제 생각엔, 올워디 어르신, 어르신께서도 저를 못 알아보셨을 거예요. 어르신께서 그토록 친절하게 충고를 해주셨던 그날 이후로 저는 무척이나 많이 변했답니다. 그 충고를 따랐더라면 저는 행복했을 텐데요.\" \"정말이지, 부인.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그 반대였다는 걸 처음 들었을 때 나는 무척 마음이 아팠소.\" \"사실은요, 어르신.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 아주 깊은 악당의 계략에 걸려 신세를 망쳤답니다. 그 내막을 아신다면, 비록 그것이 어르신의 눈에 저를 떳떳하게 해주리라고는 감히 생각지 않지만, 적어도 제 잘못을 덜어주고, 어르신께서 저를 가엾이 여기시도록 만들 거예요. 지금은 제 이야기를 전부 들으실 겨를이 없으시겠지요. 하지만 이것만은 분명히 말씀드릴게요. 저는 더없이 엄숙한 결혼 약속에 속아 넘어갔습니다. 아니, 하늘의 눈으로 보자면 저는 그와 결혼한 몸이었어요. 그 주제에 관해 많은 책을 읽고 나서, 저는 특정한 의식이란 결혼에 법적 효력을 부여하기 위해서만 필요한 것이며, 여자에게 아내의 권리를 주는 세속적인 쓸모만 있을 뿐이라고 확신하게 되었거든요. 엄숙한 사적 약혼을 한 뒤 한 남자에게 변함없이 지조를 지킨 여자라면, 세상이 그녀를 무어라 부르든, 제 양심에 떳떳지 못할 게 거의 없다고요.\" \"유감이오, 부인.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"당신이 학식을 그토록 그릇되게 썼다는 것이 말이오. 차라리 훨씬 더 많이 배웠거나, 아니면 아무것도 모르는 상태로 남아 있었더라면 좋았을 거요. 하지만 부인, 안타깝게도 당신에겐 이 죄말고도 책임져야 할 죄가 더 있는 것 같소만.\" \"그가 살아 있던 동안에는,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"십이 년 남짓 되는 그 세월 동안에는, 정말이지 엄숙하게 맹세하건대 그런 일이 없었어요. 그리고 어르신, 제 처지에서 헤아려주세요. 명예를 빼앗기고 빈털터리로 버려진 여자에게 무슨 힘이 있겠어요. 인심 좋은 세상이 그런 길 잃은 양 한 마리를, 설령 그녀가 아무리 간절히 바란다 한들, 덕의 길로 되돌아오게 내버려두겠어요? 그러니 단언컨대, 제게 그럴 힘이 있었다면 저는 그 길을 택했을 거예요. 하지만 궁핍이 저를 워터스 대위의 품으로 내몰았고, 그와는 여전히 정식으로 혼인하지 않은 채로 여러 해를 아내처럼 살며 그의 이름으로 통했답니다. 저는 우스터에서 그 신사와 헤어졌어요. 그가 반란군을 치러 진군하던 때였지요. 그리고 바로 그때 우연히 존스 씨를 만나게 되었는데, 그분이 어느 악당의 손아귀에서 저를 구해주셨어요. 정말이지 그분은 더없이 훌륭한 분이에요. 제 생각에 그 나이 또래의 젊은 신사 가운데 그분만큼 악덕에서 자유로운 이는 없고, 그분이 지닌 미덕의 이십 분의 일이라도 가진 이는 드물 거예요. 아니, 그분에게 어떤 악덕이 있었든 간에, 저는 그분이 이제 그것들을 버리기로 결심하셨다고 굳게 믿어요.\" \"나도 그러기를 바라오.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그 결심을 지켜가기를 바라오. 말해두건대, 나는 당신 자신에 대해서도 여전히 같은 희망을 품고 있소. 세상이 이런 경우에 너무 매정하게 구는 경향이 있다는 데는 나도 동의하오. 하지만 세월과 끈기는 결국 동정을 꺼리는, 말하자면 그 인색한 마음을 이겨낼 거요. 세상은 하늘처럼 뉘우치는 죄인을 선뜻 받아들이지는 않지만, 그래도 끊임없는 참회는 마침내 세상에서조차 자비를 얻어내게 될 테니 말이오. 이것만은 믿어도 좋소, 워터스 부인. 당신이 그러한 선한 뜻에 진실하다는 것을 내가 확인하는 날에는, 그것을 이루는 데 내 힘이 닿는 도움이라면 무엇 하나 부족함이 없을 거요.\"\n\n워터스 부인은 이제 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 그의 선량함에 대해 더없이 격정적인 감사의 말을 거듭 쏟아냈다. 그녀가 진심으로 말했듯이, 그 선량함은 인간의 본성보다는 신의 본성에 더 가까운 것이었다.\n\n올워디는 그녀를 일으켜 세우고는, 더없이 다정한 태도로 말하며, 그녀를 위로하기 위해 자신의 머리가 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 표현을 다 동원했다. 그때 다울링 씨가 도착하는 바람에 그의 말이 끊겼다. 다울링은 들어서자마자 워터스 부인을 보고 흠칫 놀라며 다소 당황한 기색을 보였으나, 곧 되도록 침착하게 자세를 가다듬고는, 웨스턴 씨의 숙소에서 변호사들과 만나야 해서 몹시 바쁘지만, 그래도 들러서 이전에 말씀드린 그 사건에 대한 법률 자문의 견해를 알려드리는 것이 자신의 도리라 여겼다고 말했다. 그 견해란, 그 경우 돈의 횡령은 형사 사건으로는 문제 삼을 수 없으나, 동산 점유 회복 소송을 제기할 수는 있으며, 만일 그 돈이 원고의 것임이 배심원에게 명백히 드러난다면, 원고가 그 가액에 대한 평결을 받아낼 수 있으리라는 것이었다.\n\n올워디는 이에 아무런 대답도 하지 않고 문을 걸어 잠그더니, 다울링에게 엄한 표정으로 다가서며 말했다. \"당신이 아무리 바쁘다 한들, 나는 먼저 몇 가지 질문에 대한 답을 들어야겠소. 이 부인을 아시오?\" \"그 부인 말씀입니까, 어르신?\" 다울링이 몹시 머뭇거리며 대답했다. 그러자 올워디는 더없이 엄숙한 목소리로 말했다. \"이보시오, 다울링 씨. 나의 호의를 소중히 여긴다면, 또 내 밑에서 단 한순간이라도 더 일하고 싶다면, 머뭇거리지도 둘러대지도 마시오. 내가 묻는 모든 질문에 거짓 없이 진실하게 답하시오. 이 부인을 아시오?\" \"압니다, 어르신.\" 다울링이 말했다. \"이 부인을 뵌 적이 있습니다.\" \"어디서 말이오?\" \"이분의 거처에서요.\" \"무슨 용건으로 거기 갔소? 그리고 누가 보냈소?\" \"저는, 어르신, 존스 씨에 관해 알아보러 갔습니다.\" \"그럼 누가 당신을 그 사람에 관해 알아보라고 보냈소?\" \"누구냐고요, 어르신? 그게, 어르신, 블리필 씨가 저를 보냈습니다.\" \"그럼 당신은 그 문제에 관해 이 부인에게 뭐라고 말했소?\" \"원, 어르신, 한마디 한마디 다 기억해내기란 불가능합니다.\" \"부인, 이분의 기억을 거들어주시겠소?\" \"그는 제게 이렇게 말했어요, 어르신.\" 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"만일 존스 씨가 제 남편을 살해했다면, 매우 훌륭한 어느 신사분이 소송을 진행하는 데 필요한 돈을 얼마든지 대주실 것이며, 그분은 제가 상대하는 자가 얼마나 악당인지 잘 알고 계신다고요. 이것이 그가 한 바로 그 말이었음을 저는 떳떳이 맹세할 수 있어요.\" \"이것이 당신이 한 말이오?\" 올워디가 말했다. \"정확히 기억해낼 수는 없습니다만,\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"그런 취지로 말한 것 같긴 합니다.\" \"그럼 블리필 씨가 그렇게 말하라고 시켰소?\" \"분명한 것은, 어르신, 제가 제 발로 가지는 않았을 것이며, 이런 종류의 일에서 제 권한을 일부러 넘어서지도 않았으리라는 점입니다. 제가 그렇게 말했다면, 블리필 씨의 지시를 그렇게 이해했음이 틀림없습니다.\" \"이보시오, 다울링 씨.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"이 부인 앞에서 약속하건대, 당신이 이 일에서 블리필 씨의 지시에 따라 한 것이라면 무엇이든 용서하겠소. 단, 지금 내게 엄정하게 진실을 말한다는 조건에서요. 나는 당신 말을 믿소. 당신이 이 일에서 제 발로, 그리고 권한도 없이 행동하지는 않았으리라는 말을 말이오. 그럼 블리필이 마찬가지로 당신을 보내 올더스게이트에 있는 두 사내를 심문하게 했소?\" \"그랬습니다, 어르신.\" \"좋소, 그럼 그가 그때 당신에게 어떤 지시를 내렸소? 되도록 잘 기억해내서, 그가 쓴 바로 그 말에 최대한 가깝게 내게 일러주시오.\" \"그게요, 어르신, 블리필 씨는 저를 보내 이 싸움을 목격한 사람들을 찾아내게 했습니다. 그는 그자들이 존스 씨나 그 친구들 가운데 누군가에게 매수당할까 봐 염려된다고 했습니다. 또 피는 피를 부른다고, 살인자를 숨기는 자들뿐 아니라 그를 법의 심판대에 세우기 위해 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 일을 빠뜨리는 자들도 모두 그 죄를 나눠 진다고 했습니다. 그는 어르신께서 그 악당이 법의 심판을 받기를 무척 바라시되, 다만 어르신께서 거기에 나서는 것은 온당치 않다고 여기시는 걸 알게 되었다고 말했습니다.\" \"그가 그렇게 말했단 말이오?\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그렇습니다, 어르신.\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"분명히 말씀드리건대, 저는 어르신이 아닌 다른 어떤 살아 있는 사람을 위해서라면 그렇게까지 멀리 나아가지 않았을 겁니다.\" \"그렇게까지라니, 무슨 말이오?\" 올워디가 말했다. \"아니, 어르신,\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"어르신께서 제가 무슨 일이 있어도 위증 교사 죄를 저지를 사람이라 여기시지는 않기를 바랍니다. 하지만 증언을 끌어내는 데는 두 가지 방법이 있지요. 그래서 저는 그자들에게, 만일 상대편에서 어떤 제안이 들어오더라도 거절하라고, 정직한 사람으로서 진실을 말한다고 해서 손해 볼 일은 결코 없을 거라고 일러주었습니다. 또 저는, 듣자 하니 존스 씨가 그 신사를 먼저 공격했다는데, 만일 그게 사실이라면 그렇게 진술하라고 말했고, 그자들에게 손해 보는 일은 없으리라는 언질을 좀 주었습니다.\" \"내 보기엔 당신이 정말로 멀리까지 나아갔구려.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"아니, 어르신,\" 다울링이 대답했다. \"저는 분명 그자들에게 거짓을 말하라고 요구하지는 않았습니다. 그리고 어르신을 위하는 일이 아니었다면 제가 한 그 말도 하지 않았을 겁니다.\" \"내 생각엔, 만일 이 존스 씨가 내 친조카라는 것을 당신이 알았더라면,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"나를 위하는 일이라고는 여기지 않았을 것 같소만.\" \"분명한 것은, 어르신,\" 그가 대답했다. \"어르신께서 감추고 싶어 하신다고 제가 여긴 일을 함부로 입에 올리는 것은 제 도리가 아니었다는 점입니다.\" \"뭐라고!\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그럼 그때 당신이 그 사실을 알고 있었단 말이오?\" \"아니, 어르신,\" 다울링이 대답했다. \"어르신께서 진실을 말하라 명하신다면, 저는 분명 그리하겠습니다. 사실 저는 그것을 알고 있었습니다. 그것은 블리필 마님께서 생전에 마지막으로 하신 말씀에 가까운 것이었는데, 제가 그분의 침상 곁에 홀로 서 있을 때 제게 일러주신 것입니다. 그분이 어르신께 전해 올리라며 제게 편지를 건네주실 때 말입니다.\" \"무슨 편지 말이오?\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그 편지요, 어르신.\" 다울링이 대답했다. \"제가 솔즈베리에서 가져와 블리필 씨의 손에 직접 건넨 그 편지 말입니다.\" \"오, 하늘이시여!\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그래, 그래서 무슨 말이었소? 내 누이가 당신에게 뭐라 했소?\" \"그분은 제 손을 잡으셨습니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그러고는 편지를 건네시며 말씀하셨습니다. '내가 무얼 썼는지 나도 잘 모르겠네. 내 오라비에게 전해주게. 존스 씨는 그의 조카라고. 그 아이는 내 아들일세. 그 애를 축복해주세요.' 그분은 이렇게 말씀하시고는, 마치 숨이 끊어지듯 뒤로 쓰러지셨습니다. 저는 곧장 사람들을 불러들였고, 그분은 제게 더는 아무 말씀도 못 하시고 몇 분 뒤에 숨을 거두셨습니다.\" 올워디는 잠시 말없이 선 채 눈을 들어 올렸다. 그러더니 다울링을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"어찌하여, 당신은 이 전언을 내게 전하지 않았소?\" \"어르신께서는,\" 그가 대답했다. \"그 무렵 병석에 누워 계셨음을 기억하실 겁니다. 그리고 저는 몹시 서두르던 참이라, 사실 늘 그렇긴 합니다만, 편지와 전언을 블리필 씨께 넘겼습니다. 그분은 두 가지 모두 어르신께 가져다드리겠다고 하셨고, 그 뒤에 제게 말하기를 정말로 그리했노라고, 그리고 어르신께서는 한편으로는 존스 씨에 대한 정 때문에, 또 한편으로는 누이분에 대한 정 때문에, 그 일이 결코 입에 오르내리는 것을 원치 않으시며 세상에 감추실 작정이라고 하셨습니다. 그러니, 어르신, 어르신께서 먼저 제게 이 일을 꺼내지 않으셨다면, 저는 분명 어르신께든 다른 누구에게든 이 일에 대해 무어라 말하는 것이 제 소관이라고는 결코 여기지 않았을 것입니다.\"\n\n우리는 이미 어딘가에서, 사람이 진실의 말로 거짓을 전할 수도 있다고 짚어둔 바 있다. 지금이 바로 그런 경우였다. 블리필은 실제로 다울링에게 그가 방금 전한 그대로 말했으나, 그를 속인 것은 아니었으며, 사실 자신에게 그럴 능력이 있다고 상상하지도 않았다. 실은 블리필이 다울링에게 했던 약속들이야말로 그를 입 다물게 만든 동기였다. 그리고 이제 블리필이 그 약속을 지킬 수 없으리라는 것을 아주 명백히 알아차린 다울링은, 지금이야말로 이렇게 털어놓을 때라고 여겼다. 용서해주겠다는 약속에 더해, 올워디의 위협과 목소리와 표정, 그리고 그가 앞서 밝혀낸 사실들이 다울링에게서 이 고백을 쥐어짜낸 것이었다. 게다가 그는 불시에 허를 찔린 터라, 빠져나갈 구실을 궁리할 겨를도 없었다.\n\n올워디는 이 진술에 꽤 만족한 듯 보였다. 그는 다울링에게 지나간 일에 대해 철저히 함구할 것을 단단히 일러둔 뒤, 그가 블리필과 마주치지 않도록 직접 그 신사를 문까지 배웅했다. 블리필은 자기 방으로 돌아가 있었는데, 거기서 숙부를 또 한 번 감쪽같이 속였다는 생각에 우쭐거리며, 그사이 아래층에서 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지는 조금도 의심하지 못하고 있었다.\n\n올워디가 자기 방으로 돌아가던 길에 현관에서 밀러 부인과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 새파랗게 질려 공포에 사로잡힌 얼굴로 그에게 말했다. \"오! 어르신, 보아하니 그 사악한 여자가 어르신과 함께 있었고, 어르신께서 모든 것을 알게 되신 모양이군요. 하지만 그렇다고 그 가엾은 젊은이를 버리지는 말아주세요. 헤아려주세요, 어르신. 그 사람은 자신의 친어머니인 줄 몰랐던 거예요. 그리고 그 사실을 알게 되는 것만으로도, 어르신의 매정함이 없더라도, 십중팔구 그의 가슴은 미어질 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"내가 들은 것에 너무도 놀란 나머지, 정말이지 당신에게 속 시원히 답해줄 수가 없구려. 그러니 나와 함께 내 방으로 갑시다. 정말이지, 밀러 부인, 나는 놀라운 사실들을 알아냈고, 당신도 곧 그것을 알게 될 거요.\"\n\n그 가엾은 여인은 떨면서 그를 따라갔다. 이제 올워디는 워터스 부인에게로 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡고는, 밀러 부인을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"이분이 내게 베푼 도움에 대해, 내가 무슨 보답을 해드려야 하겠소? 오! 밀러 부인, 당신은 내가 그 젊은이를, 당신이 그토록 충실한 벗이 되어주는 그 젊은이를, 내 아들이라 부르는 것을 수천 번도 더 들었지요. 그때 나는 그가 정말로 나와 핏줄로 이어져 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했소. 부인, 당신의 그 벗은 내 조카요. 그는 내가 그토록 오래 가슴에 품어 길러온 그 사악한 독사의 형제라오. 이분이 직접 당신에게 그 전말을, 그리고 어찌하여 그 젊은이가 자기 아들로 통하게 되었는지를 다 일러줄 거요. 정말이지, 밀러 부인, 나는 그가 부당한 일을 당했고, 내가 속아왔음을 확신하오. 당신이 너무도 정당하게 악당이라 의심했던 그자에게 속아온 것이오. 그자는 실로 악당 중에서도 가장 못된 악당이오.\"\n\n이제 밀러 부인이 느낀 기쁨은 그녀에게서 말할 힘을 앗아갔고, 만일 정다운 눈물이 때맞춰 흘러내려 그녀를 구해주지 않았더라면 어쩌면 그녀에게서 정신을, 아니 어쩌면 목숨까지 앗아갔을지도 모른다. 마침내 그녀는 황홀경에서 어느 정도 헤어나와 말할 수 있게 되자 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 제가 아끼는 존스 씨가 어르신의 조카이고, 이 부인의 아들이 아니란 말씀이세요? 그리고 어르신께서 마침내 그 사람을 향해 눈을 뜨신 거예요? 그리고 제가 그 사람이 마땅히 누릴 만큼 행복해지는 것을 살아서 보게 되는 건가요?\" \"그는 분명 내 조카요.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그리고 나머지도 다 그러기를 바라오.\" \"그럼 이 부인이 바로 그 다정하고 선량하신 분, 이 모든 것이 밝혀진 게 다 그분 덕분이라는 그분이세요?\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"정말 그렇소.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그렇다면,\" 밀러 부인이 무릎을 꿇고 외쳤다. \"하늘이시여, 이분의 머리 위에 가장 귀한 축복을 쏟아부어 주시고, 이 한 가지 선행으로 이분의 모든 죄를, 그것이 아무리 많을지라도 용서해주옵소서!\"\n\n그러자 워터스 부인은 존스가 머지않아 풀려나리라 믿는다고 그들에게 알려주었다. 외과의가 어느 귀족과 함께, 존스를 수감한 치안판사에게 가서, 피츠패트릭 씨가 온갖 위험에서 완전히 벗어났음을 증명하고 그 죄수에게 자유를 얻어주려 한다는 것이었다.\n\n올워디는 집에 돌아갔을 때 조카가 그곳에 와 있는 것을 보게 된다면 기쁘겠다고 말했다. 다만 자신은 지금 중요한 볼일이 있어 나가봐야 한다고 했다. 그러고는 하인을 불러 가마를 가져오게 하고, 곧 두 부인을 함께 남겨둔 채 자리를 떴다.\n\n블리필은 가마를 부르라는 소리를 듣고, 숙부의 시중을 들러 아래층으로 내려왔다. 그는 이런 의무를 다하는 일에는 결코 소홀한 적이 없었으니까. 그는 숙부에게 외출하시느냐고 물었는데, 이는 어디 가시느냐고 묻는 점잖은 방식이었다. 이에 상대가 아무 대답이 없자, 그는 다시 언제쯤 돌아오실 작정이시냐고 알려달라고 청했다. 올워디는 이 물음에도 아무 대답을 하지 않다가, 막 가마에 오르려는 순간 몸을 돌려 말했다. \"이보게, 자네는 내가 돌아오기 전에, 자네 어머니가 임종 자리에서 내게 보낸 그 편지를 찾아내게.\" 그러고 나서 올워디는 떠나갔고, 블리필은 막 교수대에 오르려는 사람이나 부러워할 만한 처지에 남겨졌다.\n\n올워디는 가마에 타고 있는 동안 틈을 내어, 웨스턴이 그에게 건넨 존스가 소피아에게 보낸 편지를 읽었다. 그 안에는 그 자신에 관한 몇몇 표현이 들어 있어 그의 눈에서 눈물이 흘러나오게 했다. 마침내 그는 웨스턴 씨의 집에 이르렀고, 소피아에게 안내되었다.\n\n처음의 인사치레가 끝나고 신사와 숙녀가 각자 의자에 자리를 잡자, 몇 분간 침묵이 이어졌다. 그동안 아버지로부터 이 방문에 대해 미리 들어 마음의 준비를 해 둔 소피아는 부채를 만지작거리며 앉아 있었고, 그녀의 표정과 몸짓 모두에 당혹스러움의 기색이 역력했다. 마침내 올워디도 자신 또한 다소 마음이 편치 않은 채로 이렇게 입을 열었다. \"웨스턴 양, 제 집안이 양에게 어떤 불편함을 끼친 빌미가 된 듯하여 송구합니다. 게다가 저 자신도, 본의는 아니었으나 의도했던 것보다 더 그 일에 한몫을 한 듯하여 두렵습니다. 부디 믿어 주십시오, 양. 처음부터 그 청혼이 얼마나 거슬리는 일인지 알았더라면, 양이 그토록 오래 시달리도록 두지 않았을 것입니다. 그러니 이 방문의 목적이 그런 종류의 간청으로 양을 더 괴롭히려는 것이 아니라, 오로지 그로부터 양을 풀어 드리려는 것임을 믿어 주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 소피아가 약간의 겸손한 망설임과 함께 말했다. \"이런 태도는 더없이 친절하고 너그러우시며, 오직 올워디 선생님께만 기대할 수 있는 것입니다. 다만 선생님께서 친히 이 일을 언급해 주셨으니, 솔직히 말씀드림을 용서해 주세요. 그것은 정말로 제게 큰 괴로움을 주었고, 그 불행한 일이 있기 전까지는 모든 부모 가운데 가장 다정하고 인자하셨던 아버지로부터 모진 대우를 받게 된 빌미가 되었습니다. 선생님, 저는 선생님께서 제가 조카분을 거절한 것을 노여워하시기에는 너무도 선하고 너그러운 분이심을 확신합니다. 마음이란 우리 뜻대로 되는 것이 아니며, 그분의 자질이 어떠하든 저는 제 마음을 그분께로 억지로 돌릴 수가 없습니다.\" \"장담하건대, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"설령 그 사람이 제 친아들이었고 제가 그에게 더없이 깊은 존경을 품고 있었다 해도, 저는 그런 노여움을 품을 수 없는 사람입니다. 양의 말씀이 옳으니까요. 우리는 우리 마음을 억지로 돌릴 수 없으며, 하물며 남이 그것을 좌우할 수는 더더욱 없는 법입니다.\" \"오! 선생님,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"선생님께서 하시는 말씀 한마디 한마디가, 온 세상이 인정하는 그 선하고 위대하며 자애로운 인품을 선생님께서 받으실 자격이 있음을 증명합니다. 장담하건대, 선생님, 장차 닥칠 불행이 확실히 내다보이지 않았다면 저는 결코 아버지의 명을 거역하지 못했을 것입니다.\" \"진심으로 양의 말을 믿습니다, 양.\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"그리고 양의 신중한 선견지명에 진심으로 축하를 드립니다. 그토록 정당한 거역으로 양은 참으로 불행을 피하셨으니까요!\" \"올워디 선생님,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"선생님께서는 이제 좀처럼 느끼지 못하는 사람이 많은 그 섬세함으로 말씀하시는군요! 하지만 분명, 제 생각으로는, 우리가 무심한 사람과 평생을 함께하는 것은 비참한 처지일 수밖에 없습니다.——어쩌면 그 비참함은, 우리가 마음을 줄 수 없는 상대의 미덕을 느끼는 만큼 오히려 더 커질지도 모릅니다. 만일 제가 블리필 씨와 결혼했더라면——\" \"말씀을 끊어 죄송합니다, 양,\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"하지만 그런 가정은 견딜 수가 없군요.——믿어 주십시오, 웨스턴 양, 저는 진심으로 기뻐하고 있습니다. 양이 벗어나신 것을 기뻐하고 있어요.——저는 양이 아버지로부터 이 모든 모진 횡포를 견디게 만든 그 사람이 악당임을 알아냈습니다.\" \"어떻게요, 선생님!\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"이 일이 저를 얼마나 놀라게 하는지 믿어 주셔야 합니다.\" \"그것은 저도 놀라게 했습니다, 양.\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"그리고 세상도 놀라게 할 것입니다.——하지만 저는 양께 있는 그대로의 진실을 알려 드린 것입니다.\" \"올워디 선생님의 입술에서는,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"진실 외에는 아무것도 나올 수 없음을 저는 확신합니다.——하지만 선생님, 이렇게 갑작스럽고 뜻밖의 소식이라니.——알아내셨다고요——악행이란 언제나 그런 것이겠지요!\" \"그 이야기는 머지않아 충분히 듣게 되실 겁니다.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"지금은 그토록 혐오스러운 이름은 입에 올리지 맙시다.——제게는 매우 중대한 또 다른 문제를 제안드릴 것이 있습니다.——오! 웨스턴 양, 저는 양의 크나큰 가치를 알기에, 그것과 인연을 맺고자 하는 바람을 그리 쉽게 접을 수가 없습니다.——제게는 가까운 친척이 하나 있습니다, 양. 그 젊은이의 됨됨이는, 제가 확신하건대, 그 악당과는 정반대이며, 저는 그의 재산을 본래 그 사람이 받았을 것과 똑같이 만들어 줄 것입니다. 양, 제가 그 젊은이의 방문을 양께서 허락해 주시리라 바라도 되겠습니까?\" 소피아는 잠시 침묵한 뒤 대답했다. \"올워디 선생님께는 더없이 진실하게 대하겠습니다. 선생님의 인품과, 방금 제가 선생님께 입은 은혜가 그것을 요구하니까요. 저는 지금으로서는 그 누구로부터도 그런 청혼에 귀 기울이지 않기로 결심했습니다. 제 유일한 바람은 아버지의 사랑을 되찾고, 다시 그 집안의 안주인이 되는 것입니다. 선생님, 이것을 선생님의 선의 어린 주선 덕에 이루기를 바랍니다. 부디 간청드립니다, 부디 빌건대, 저와 선생님을 아는 모든 이가 경험한 그 모든 선하심에 걸고, 저를 한 박해에서 풀어 주신 바로 그 순간에, 저를 또 다른 박해, 그만큼 비참하고 그만큼 헛된 박해에 옭아매지는 말아 주십시오.\" \"정말로, 웨스턴 양,\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"저는 그런 행동을 할 수 없는 사람입니다. 그리고 이것이 양의 결심이라면, 그가 그 아래에서 어떤 고통을 겪든 그 실망을 감수해야겠지요.\" \"이제는 웃을 수밖에 없군요, 올워디 선생님,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"선생님께서 제가 알지도 못하는 사람, 따라서 저와는 거의 면식이 없는 사람의 고통을 말씀하시니 말입니다.\" \"용서하십시오, 사랑스러운 아가씨,\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"이제 저는 그가 자신의 앞날의 평안을 위해서는 너무 많은 면식을 가진 것이 아닐까 두려워지기 시작합니다. 만일 어떤 사람이 진실하고 격렬하며 고귀한 정열을 품을 수 있다면, 저는 제 불행한 조카가 웨스턴 양께 품은 정열이 바로 그러하다고 확신하니까요.\" \"선생님의 조카라고요, 올워디 선생님!\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"정말 이상한 일이군요. 저는 그분에 대해 이전에 들어 본 적이 없습니다.\" \"사실, 양,\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"양께서 모르시는 것은 다만 그가 제 조카라는 사정뿐이며, 그것은 오늘까지 제게도 비밀이었습니다.——오래도록 양을 사랑해 온 존스 씨, 그가, 그가 제 조카입니다!\" \"존스 씨가 선생님의 조카라고요, 선생님!\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"그게 가능한 일인가요?\" \"정말로 그렇습니다, 양.\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"그는 제 친누이의 아들입니다.——그런 사람으로서 저는 그를 언제까지나 인정할 것이며, 그를 인정하는 것이 부끄럽지 않습니다. 저는 오히려 지난날 그에게 했던 제 처사가 훨씬 더 부끄럽습니다. 하지만 저는 그의 출생을 몰랐던 것만큼이나 그의 자질도 알지 못했습니다. 정말로, 웨스턴 양, 저는 그를 모질게 대했습니다.——정말 그랬습니다.\" 여기서 이 선한 사람은 눈물을 닦았고, 잠시 멈춘 뒤 말을 이었다. \"양의 도움 없이는 저는 결코 그가 겪은 고통을 보상해 줄 수 없을 것입니다.——믿어 주십시오, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨, 제가 양의 가치 앞에 바치는 이 제안을 저는 매우 귀하게 여기고 있습니다. 저는 그가 잘못을 저질러 온 것을 압니다. 하지만 그 밑바닥에는 크나큰 선한 마음이 있습니다. 믿어 주십시오, 양, 정말 그렇습니다.\" 여기서 그는 답을 기다리는 듯 말을 멈추었고, 이내 소피아로부터 답을 들었다. 그토록 이상하고 갑작스러운 소식에 휘말렸던 마음의 동요에서 조금 벗어난 뒤였다. \"선생님, 선생님께서 그토록 큰 만족을 느끼시는 것 같은 그 발견에 대해 진심으로 기쁘게 여깁니다. 선생님께서 그로부터 스스로 약속하실 수 있는 온갖 위안을 모두 누리시리라 믿어 의심치 않습니다. 그 젊은 신사분은 분명 수천 가지 좋은 자질을 지녔으니, 그런 큰아버지께 잘 처신하지 않을 수가 없겠지요.\" \"양,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"저는 그가 좋은 남편이 될 만한 그런 좋은 자질들을 지녔기를 바랍니다.——양처럼 가치 있는 숙녀께서 몸을 낮추어 주신다면, 그가 만일 잘못 처신한다면 그는 분명 모든 사람 가운데 가장 타락한 자일 것입니다——\" \"용서해 주셔야겠습니다, 올워디 선생님,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"저는 이런 종류의 제안에는 귀 기울일 수가 없습니다. 존스 씨가 많은 자질을 지녔음을 저는 확신합니다. 하지만 저는 결코 존스 씨를 제 남편이 될 사람으로 받아들이지 않을 것입니다——제 명예를 걸고, 결코 그러지 않을 것입니다.\" \"용서하십시오, 양,\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"제가 웨스턴 씨에게 들은 바가 있어서, 조금 놀랍기는 합니다.——그 불행한 젊은이가, 혹 한때 양의 호의를 누릴 영광을 가졌었다면, 그 호의를 잃을 만한 어떤 짓도 하지 않았기를 바랍니다.——어쩌면 그가 제게 그러했듯 양께도 잘못 전해졌을지 모릅니다. 같은 악행이 어디서나 그를 해쳤을 수 있으니까요.——장담하건대 그는 사람들이 일컫던 그런 살인자가 아닙니다.\" \"올워디 선생님,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"저는 제 결심을 말씀드렸습니다. 아버지께서 선생님께 무슨 말씀을 하셨든 저는 놀라지 않습니다. 다만, 그분의 염려나 두려움이 무엇이었든, 제 마음을 제가 안다면, 저는 그럴 빌미를 드린 적이 없습니다. 아버지의 동의 없이는 결코 결혼하지 않겠다는 것이 늘 제 확고한 신조였으니까요. 이것이야말로 자식이 부모께 마땅히 지켜야 할 도리라고 저는 생각하며, 어떤 것도 결코 저를 거기서 벗어나게 하지 못하리라 바랍니다. 다만 저는, 어떤 부모의 권위라 해도 우리의 마음에 정면으로 거스르는 결혼을 강요할 수는 없다고 생각합니다. 제가 의심할 만한 이유가 있었던 그런 강요를 피하기 위해 저는 아버지의 집을 떠나 다른 곳에서 보호를 구했습니다. 이것이 제 이야기의 진실이며, 만일 세상이나 아버지가 제 의도를 그 이상으로 몰아간다 해도, 제 양심만큼은 저를 무죄로 여길 것입니다.\" \"웨스턴 양,\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"저는 감탄하며 양의 말씀을 듣고 있습니다. 양의 감정이 지닌 올바름에 저는 감탄합니다. 하지만 분명 여기에는 그 이상의 무언가가 있습니다. 양을 거스르지 않으려 조심하고 있습니다만, 아가씨, 제가 지금까지 듣고 본 모든 것을 한낱 꿈으로만 여겨야 하는 것입니까? 그리고 양께서는, 늘 전혀 무심하기만 했던 한 남자 때문에 아버지로부터 그토록 모진 처사를 견디셨다는 말씀입니까?\" \"올워디 선생님, 부디,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"제 이유를 캐묻지는 말아 주세요.——그래요, 저는 정말로 고통받았습니다. 올워디 선생님, 숨기지 않겠습니다——선생님께 아주 솔직하게 말씀드리겠어요——저는 존스 씨를 매우 높이 평가했음을 인정합니다——제 생각으로는——아니, 저는 분명 그 평가 때문에 고통받았습니다——저는 아버지뿐 아니라 고모로부터도 모진 대우를 받았습니다. 하지만 그것은 이제 지난 일입니다——더는 다그치지 말아 주시기를 청합니다. 무슨 일이 있었든, 제 결심은 이제 확고하니까요. 선생님, 선생님의 조카분은 많은 미덕을 지녔습니다——크나큰 미덕을 지녔어요, 올워디 선생님. 저는 그분이 세상에서 선생님께 영예를 안기고, 선생님을 행복하게 해 드릴 것을 의심치 않습니다.\" \"그를 행복하게 해 줄 수 있다면 좋겠습니다, 양.\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"하지만 그것은 오직 양의 손에 달려 있음을 저는 확신합니다. 바로 그 확신이 저로 하여금 그를 위해 이토록 간곡히 청하게 만든 것입니다.\" \"선생님은 정말로 속고 계십니다. 속고 계세요.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그에게 속으신 것이 아니기를 바랍니다. 저 한 사람을 속인 것으로 충분합니다. 올워디 선생님, 이 일에 대해서는 더 이상 다그치지 말아 주시기를 간곡히 청합니다. 저는 안타까워해야겠지요——아니, 선생님 앞에서 그를 깎아내리지는 않겠습니다. 저는 존스 씨가 잘되기를 진심으로 바랍니다. 진심으로 그가 잘되기를 바랍니다. 그리고 다시 한번 말씀드리지만, 그가 제게 어떤 허물을 졌든, 저는 그가 많은 좋은 자질을 지녔음을 확신합니다. 저는 지난날의 제 생각을 부인하지는 않습니다. 하지만 어떤 것도 그것을 되돌릴 수는 없습니다. 지금 이 땅에서, 제가 존스 씨보다 더 단호하게 거절할 사람은 없으며, 블리필 씨 본인의 구애라 해도 제게 그보다 덜 달갑지는 않을 것입니다.\"\n\n웨스턴은 이 회담의 결과를 오래도록 애타게 기다리던 터라, 마침 그 순간 문 앞에 이르러 엿듣고 있었다. 그러다 딸의 마음속 마지막 심정을 듣고는 완전히 이성을 잃고, 화가 치밀어 문을 박차고 들어오며 외쳤다. \"거짓말이다! 빌어먹을 거짓말이야! 이게 다 그 빌어먹을 악당 존스 놈 때문이라고. 그놈한테 닿을 수만 있다면, 저 애는 하루 중 어느 때라도 그놈을 가지려 들 거란 말이다.\" 여기서 올워디가 끼어들어, 표정에 다소 노기를 띤 채 지주에게 말했다. \"웨스턴 씨, 당신은 제게 한 약속을 지키지 않으셨습니다. 모든 폭력적인 행동을 삼가겠다고 약속하셨잖습니까.\" \"아니, 나도 그랬소,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"할 수 있는 데까지는 말이오. 하지만 계집애가 저런 어처구니없는 거짓말을 늘어놓는 걸 듣고서야——제기랄! 다른 사람들은 다 바보로 만들 수 있다고, 나까지 바보로 만들 수 있다고 저 애가 생각하는 거요?——아니, 아니, 나는 당신보다 저 애를 더 잘 안단 말이오.\" \"선생, 말씀드리기 송구하나,\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"이 아가씨를 대하는 당신의 태도로 보아서는, 당신이 따님을 조금도 알지 못하시는 것 같습니다. 제 말을 용서해 주십시오. 하지만 우리의 친분, 당신 자신의 바람, 그리고 이 상황이 제게 그리 말할 자격을 준다고 봅니다. 그녀는 당신의 딸이며, 웨스턴 씨, 저는 그녀가 당신의 이름에 영예를 안긴다고 생각합니다. 만일 제가 시기심을 품을 줄 아는 사람이라면, 저는 이 일로 다른 그 누구보다 당신을 먼저 부러워할 것입니다.\" \"젠장맞을!\" 지주가 외쳤다. \"저 애가 진심으로 당신 것이었으면 좋겠소——그러면 저 애를 떠맡은 골칫거리에서 벗어나 곧 기뻐하게 될 테니.\" \"정말이지, 나의 좋은 벗이여,\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"당신이 한탄하는 그 모든 골칫거리의 원인은 바로 당신 자신입니다. 저 아가씨가 충분히 받을 자격이 있는 그 신뢰를 그녀에게 두십시오. 그러면 분명 당신은 이 땅에서 가장 행복한 아버지가 될 것입니다.\" \"내가 저 애를 신뢰하라고?\" 지주가 외쳤다. \"제기랄! 저 애가 내가 바라는 대로 하지 않는데, 내가 무슨 신뢰를 둘 수 있단 말이오? 내가 바라는 대로 결혼하겠다고 동의만 하면, 당신이 바라는 만큼 얼마든지 저 애를 신뢰하리다.\" \"이웃이여, 당신께는,\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"그런 동의를 강요할 권리가 없습니다. 따님은 당신께 거부권만은 허락하고 있으며, 하느님과 자연은 그 이상은 당신께 허락하는 것이 옳지 않다고 여기셨습니다.\" \"거부권이라!\" 지주가 외쳤다. \"그래! 그래! 내 거부권이 어떤 건지 보여 주리다.——가거라, 네 방으로 들어가, 가, 이 고집불통——\" \"정말이지, 웨스턴 씨,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"정말이지 당신은 따님을 모질게 대하고 계십니다——저는 이 광경을 차마 볼 수가 없습니다——당신은 그녀에게 더 다정하게 대하셔야 하고, 또 그러셔야만 합니다. 그녀는 더없이 좋은 대우를 받을 자격이 있습니다.\" \"그래, 그래,\" 지주가 말했다. \"저 애가 무슨 대우를 받아 마땅한지는 내가 알고 있소. 자, 이제 저 애가 갔으니, 저 애가 무슨 대우를 받아 마땅한지 보여 드리리다. 여기 보시오, 선생, 여기 내 사촌 벨라스턴 부인에게서 온 편지가 있소. 그 안에서 그녀는 친절하게도, 그 녀석이 다시 감옥에서 빠져나왔다는 걸 내게 알려 주었소. 그리고 여기서 저 계집애를 할 수 있는 한 단단히 단속하라고 충고하고 있단 말이오. 빌어먹을! 이웃 올워디, 당신은 딸자식을 다스린다는 게 어떤 건지 모르오.\"\n\n지주는 자신의 명민함에 대한 몇 마디 자화자찬으로 말을 맺었다. 그러자 올워디는 격식을 갖춘 서두를 단 뒤, 자신이 존스에 관해 알아낸 모든 사실과, 블리필에 대한 자신의 분노, 그리고 앞선 여러 장에서 독자에게 밝혀진 낱낱의 사정을 그에게 알려 주었다.\n\n기질이 지나치게 격한 사람들은 대개 그 기질만큼이나 변덕도 심한 법이다. 그리하여 웨스턴은 올워디 씨가 존스를 자신의 상속자로 삼으려 한다는 것을 알게 되자마자, 큰아버지에 못지않게 진심으로 조카에 대한 온갖 칭찬에 가담했고, 전에 딸을 블리필과 맺어 주려 안달했던 만큼이나 이제는 딸을 존스와 결혼시키려 열을 올렸다.\n\n여기서 올워디 씨는 다시금 끼어들어, 자신과 소피아 사이에 오간 일을 이야기해야 했고, 지주는 그 이야기에 크게 놀라움을 드러냈다.\n\n지주는 잠시 말이 없었고, 이 이야기에 어리둥절하여 멍하니 놀란 표정을 지었다.——마침내 그는 외쳤다. \"아니, 이게 도대체 무슨 영문이오, 이웃 올워디? 저 애가 그놈한테 푹 빠져 있었다는 건, 내가 맹세코 단언할 수 있소.——제기랄! 알아챘소. 총알처럼 확실하게, 내가 바로 그 진상을 알아챘단 말이오. 다 누이 때문이오. 저 계집애가 그 화냥년의 자식 같은 귀족 놈한테 마음을 두고 있는 거요. 내 사촌 벨라스턴 부인 댁에서 그 둘이 함께 있는 걸 내가 봤단 말이오. 그놈이 저 애 머리를 돌려놓은 게야, 그건 확실하오——하지만 빌어먹을, 그놈이 저 애를 갖게 두진 않겠소——내 집안에는 귀족이든 궁정 신하든 들이지 않을 거요.\"\n\n이제 올워디는 긴 연설을 했는데, 그 안에서 모든 폭력적인 수단을 피하겠다는 자신의 결심을 거듭 밝혔고, 따님에게 가장 잘 통할 수 있는 방법으로서 부드러운 방식을 웨스턴 씨에게 매우 간곡히 권했다. 그러고는 작별을 고하고 밀러 부인에게로 돌아갔으나, 그날 오후에 존스 씨를 데리고 와 그를 방문하게 하겠다는 약속을 하라는 지주의 간곡한 부탁에는 응할 수밖에 없었다. 지주의 말로는, \"그 젊은 신사와 모든 일을 매듭짓기\" 위해서였다. 올워디 씨가 떠날 때, 웨스턴은 소피아를 대하는 태도에서 그의 충고를 따르겠다고 약속하며 이렇게 말했다. \"어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠소만, 빌어먹을, 올워디, 당신은 늘 나를 당신 마음대로 하게 만든단 말이오. 그래도 나는 당신만큼이나 좋은 재산을 가졌고, 당신 못지않게 치안판사직도 맡고 있는 사람인데 말이오.\"\n\n역사가 결말을 향해 다가서기 시작하는 대목.\n\n올워디가 숙소로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 존스 씨가 자기보다 막 먼저 도착했다는 소식을 들었다. 그래서 그는 곧장 빈 방으로 서둘러 들어가, 존스 씨를 자기 앞에 홀로 데려오라고 일렀다.\n\n숙부와 조카 사이의 그 만남보다 더 다정하고 가슴 뭉클한 장면은 도무지 상상할 수 없을 것이다(독자도 능히 짐작하겠지만, 워터스 부인이 마지막 방문에서 그에게 출생의 비밀을 밝혀 주었던 것이다). 양쪽 모두가 느낀 그 첫 기쁨의 격정은 실로 내 필력으로 그려낼 수 있는 범위를 넘어선다. 그러니 나는 그것을 시도하지 않겠다. 올워디는 무릎 꿇고 엎드려 있던 존스를 일으켜 두 팔로 안고 나서 이렇게 외쳤다. \"오, 내 아들아! 내가 얼마나 잘못했더냐! 내가 너를 얼마나 해쳤더냐! 내가 품었던 그 매정하고 부당한 의심들, 그리고 그것이 네게 안긴 그 모든 고통에 대해, 내가 무엇으로 보상할 수 있겠느냐?\" \"이미 보상받지 않았습니까?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"제 고통이 설령 열 배는 더 컸더라도, 지금 이렇게 넘치도록 보답받지 않았겠습니까? 오, 그리운 숙부님, 이 자애로움, 이 다정함이 저를 압도하고, 무너뜨리고, 사라지게 합니다. 이토록 빠르게 밀려드는 환희를 저는 견뎌낼 수가 없습니다. 다시 숙부님 곁으로, 숙부님의 총애 안으로 돌아오다니. 위대하고 고귀하며 너그러우신 저의 은인께 이렇게 다시 한번 따뜻하게 받아들여지다니.\" \"정말이지, 아들아.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"나는 너를 모질게 대했구나.\" 그러고는 블리필의 모든 배신을 그에게 낱낱이 설명하고, 그 배신에 이끌려 그를 그토록 못되게 대한 것에 대해 더없는 비통함의 말을 거듭 되풀이했다. \"오, 그런 말씀 마십시오!\" 존스가 대답했다. \"정말이지, 숙부님, 숙부님은 저를 고결하게 대하셨습니다. 아무리 지혜로운 사람이라도 숙부님처럼 속을 수 있었을 것이며, 그런 기만 아래서라면 가장 훌륭한 사람도 꼭 숙부님처럼 행동했을 것입니다. 숙부님의 자애로움은 분노의 한복판에서도, 바로 그때조차 그렇게 보였듯 그 모습을 드러냈습니다. 저는 그 자애로움에 모든 것을 빚졌으며, 그러면서도 저는 그것을 누릴 자격이 가장 없는 사람이었습니다. 너그러운 마음을 너무 멀리까지 밀고 나가시어 저를 자책에 빠뜨리지 말아 주십시오. 아아! 숙부님, 저는 마땅히 받아야 할 것보다 더 큰 벌을 받은 것이 아니며, 앞으로 제 남은 삶의 모든 일은 숙부님께서 지금 베풀어 주시는 그 행복을 누릴 자격을 갖추는 데 바칠 것입니다. 그리운 숙부님, 믿어 주십시오. 제가 받은 벌은 헛되이 흘려보내지 않았습니다. 저는 큰 죄인이긴 했어도, 마음이 굳어버린 죄인은 아닙니다. 하늘에 감사하게도, 저는 지난 삶을 돌아볼 시간을 가졌습니다. 거기서 저는 비록 어떤 큰 악행으로 스스로를 책할 수는 없으나, 뉘우치고 부끄러워하기에 차고도 넘칠 만큼 많은 어리석음과 악덕을 분명히 알아보았습니다. 그 어리석음은 제 자신에게 끔찍한 결과를 가져왔고, 저를 파멸의 벼랑 끝까지 몰아갔습니다.\" \"기쁘구나, 내 아들아.\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"네가 이렇게 분별 있게 말하는 것을 들으니 기쁘다. 위선(맙소사! 내가 남들의 위선에 얼마나 속아 넘어갔던가!)은 결코 네 결점 가운데 있지 않았다고 나는 확신하기에, 네가 하는 말을 모두 기꺼이 믿을 수 있구나. 톰, 너는 이제 보았겠지. 경솔함 하나만으로도 덕이 어떤 위험에 놓일 수 있는지를(이제 나는 네가 덕을 크게 사랑한다고 확신한다). 신중함이란 실로 우리가 우리 자신에게 마땅히 져야 할 의무다. 우리가 그것을 소홀히 할 만큼 스스로의 적이 된다면, 세상이 우리에 대한 의무를 다하지 못한다 한들 놀랄 일이 아니지. 사람이 제 파멸의 기초를 스스로 놓으면, 안타깝게도 남들은 그 위에 너무도 쉽게 무언가를 쌓아 올리려 들기 마련이니까. 그러나 너는 네 잘못을 깨달았고, 그것을 고치겠노라 말하는구나. 나는 너를 굳게 믿는다, 내 아들아. 그러니 이 순간부터 나는 결코 그 잘못들을 다시 들먹여 네게 일깨우지 않겠다. 다만 너 스스로는, 앞으로 그것들을 더 잘 피하도록 일러줄 만큼만 기억해 두어라. 그러나 위안 삼아 이것만은 기억하여라. 너그러운 마음으로 보아 경솔함이라 해석될 수 있는 결점과, 오로지 악행에서 비롯되었다고밖에 볼 수 없는 결점 사이에는 이 큰 차이가 있다는 것을. 앞엣것은 어쩌면 사람을 파멸로 몰아넣는 데 더 쉬울지도 모른다. 그러나 그가 잘못을 고치면, 그의 명예는 마침내 온전히 회복될 것이다. 세상은 당장은 아닐지라도 시간이 지나면 그와 화해할 것이며, 그는 자신이 벗어난 위험을 돌아보며 어느 정도 즐거움이 섞인 회상을 할 수도 있을 것이다. 그러나 악행이란, 내 아이야, 일단 드러나면 돌이킬 수 없다. 그것이 남기는 얼룩은 어떤 세월로도 씻어낼 수 없다. 인류의 비난이 그 가련한 자를 쫓을 것이며, 그들의 경멸이 그를 사람들 앞에서 부끄럽게 할 것이다. 그리고 수치심이 그를 은둔으로 내몰면, 그는 도깨비를 무서워하는 지친 아이가 사람들 곁을 떠나 홀로 잠자리에 들 때 느끼는 그 모든 공포를 안고 그 은둔으로 들어갈 것이다. 그곳에서 그가 죽여버린 양심이 그를 따라다니며 괴롭힐 것이다. 안식은 거짓된 벗처럼 그에게서 달아날 것이다. 어디로 눈을 돌리든 공포가 그 앞에 나타날 것이다. 뒤를 돌아보면 아무 소용없는 후회가 발꿈치를 밟고 따라오고, 앞을 보면 고칠 길 없는 절망이 얼굴을 노려본다. 그리하여 그는 지하 감옥에 갇힌 사형수처럼 지금의 처지를 혐오하면서도, 그 처지에서 자신을 풀어줄 그 시간이 가져올 결말을 두려워하게 되지. 다시 말하건대, 내 아들아, 이것이 네 경우가 아님을 위안으로 삼아라. 그리고 네 잘못이, 그 잘못을 고집했다면 반드시 너를 이끌고 갔을 그 파멸을 가져오기 전에, 너로 하여금 잘못을 깨닫게 해주신 그분께 감사함으로 기뻐하여라. 너는 그 잘못들을 버렸고, 이제 네 앞에 펼쳐진 전망은 행복이 마치 네 손안에 있는 듯하구나.\" 이 말에 존스는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 올워디가 그것을 나무라자 그가 말했다. \"숙부님, 저는 숙부님께 아무것도 숨기지 않겠습니다. 제 악덕이 빚은 한 가지 결과만은 결코 되돌릴 수 없을까 두렵습니다. 오, 그리운 숙부님! 저는 보물을 잃었습니다.\" \"더 말할 것 없다.\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"내가 솔직히 말하마. 나는 네가 무엇을 한탄하는지 안다. 나는 그 젊은 아가씨를 만났고, 너에 관해 그녀와 이야기를 나누었다. 네가 한 모든 말의 진심과 네 결심의 굳건함을 증명하는 표시로서, 한 가지만은 나를 따라야 한다고 나는 단단히 일러두겠다. 그 결과가 네게 유리하든 아니든, 전적으로 그 젊은 아가씨의 결정에 따르는 것이다. 그녀는 이미 내가 생각하기조차 싫은 그 끈질긴 구애로 충분히 고통받았다. 그녀는 더 이상 내 집안 탓에 어떤 속박도 짊어져서는 안 된다. 나는 그녀의 아버지가 예전에 다른 사람을 두고 그러했듯, 이제는 너를 두고 그녀를 괴롭히려 들리라는 것을 안다. 그러나 나는 그녀가 더 이상 어떤 감금도, 어떤 폭력도, 어떤 괴로운 시간도 겪지 않게 하기로 굳게 마음먹었다.\" \"오, 그리운 숙부님!\" 존스가 대답했다. \"부디 청하오니, 따르는 데 제게 어떤 공이 돌아갈 만한 명령을 내려 주십시오. 믿어 주십시오, 숙부님, 제가 숙부님께 거역할 수 있는 유일한 경우란, 제 소피아에게 괴로운 한순간이라도 안기는 일뿐일 것입니다. 아닙니다, 숙부님, 만일 제가 너무도 비참하여 용서의 희망조차 모두 넘어설 만큼 그녀의 노여움을 샀다면, 그것 하나만으로도, 그리고 제가 그녀의 불행의 원인이 되었다는 그 끔찍한 생각만으로도 저를 무너뜨리기에 충분할 것입니다. 소피아를 제 사람이라 부르는 것이야말로 하늘이 베풀 수 있는 가장 크고, 이제는 유일하게 남은 추가의 축복입니다. 그러나 그것은 오로지 그녀에게서만 받을 수 있는 축복입니다.\" \"너에게 듣기 좋은 말은 하지 않으마, 아들아.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"네 처지가 절망적일까 두렵구나. 네 구애를 받아들이지 않겠다는 그녀의 격렬한 선언에서 드러난 것만큼, 그 어떤 사람에게서도 흔들림 없는 결심의 강한 표시를 본 적이 없다. 그 까닭은 어쩌면 나보다 네가 더 잘 설명할 수 있겠지.\" \"오, 숙부님! 저는 너무도 잘 설명할 수 있습니다.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"저는 그녀에게 용서받을 희망을 모두 넘어설 만큼 죄를 지었습니다. 게다가 죄인인 제 죄가, 불행히도 그녀의 눈에는 실제 빛깔보다 열 배는 더 시커멓게 비치고 있습니다. 오, 그리운 숙부님! 제 어리석음은 돌이킬 수 없는 것임을 깨닫습니다. 숙부님의 그 모든 자애로움으로도 저를 파멸에서 구해낼 수 없습니다.\"\n\n이때 하인이 웨스턴 씨가 아래층에 와 있다고 두 사람에게 알렸다. 존스를 보고 싶은 그의 조바심이 오후까지 기다릴 수 없었던 것이다. 그러자 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 존스는 자기가 조금 마음을 추스를 때까지 몇 분만 웨스턴을 상대해 달라고 숙부에게 청했다. 마음씨 좋은 그는 이에 응하여, 웨스턴 씨를 응접실로 안내하라 이른 뒤 그에게로 내려갔다.\n\n밀러 부인은 존스가 혼자 있다는 말을 듣자마자(그가 감옥에서 풀려난 뒤로 그녀는 아직 그를 만나지 못한 터였다) 다급히 방으로 들어와, 존스에게 다가가며 새로 찾은 숙부와 그 행복한 화해를 진심으로 축하해 주었다. 그러고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"다른 일로도 축하를 드릴 수 있으면 좋으련만, 내 사랑하는 도련님, 저토록 요지부동인 사람은 정말이지 본 적이 없답니다.\"\n\n존스는 다소 놀란 기색으로 그것이 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었다. \"그러니까요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 도련님의 아가씨를 찾아가서, 제 사위 나이팅게일이 제게 들려준 그대로 모든 일을 그녀에게 설명했답니다. 그녀는 이제 그 편지에 대해 더 이상 의심할 수 없을 거예요. 그건 제가 확신해요. 그녀가 원한다면 제 사위 나이팅게일이 그것이 모두 자기가 꾸며낸 것이며 편지도 자기가 지어낸 것이라고 맹세할 준비가 되어 있다고 제가 그녀에게 말했으니까요. 그 편지를 보낸 바로 그 이유야말로 도련님을 그녀에게 더 좋게 천거해 주어야 마땅하다고 제가 말했지요. 그것은 모두 그녀를 위한 일이었고, 도련님이 앞으로 모든 방탕을 끊겠다고 결심했다는 분명한 증거이며, 도련님이 시내에서 그녀를 만난 뒤로 단 한 번도 그녀에게 불충했던 적이 없다고 말이에요. 그 대목에서는 제가 너무 멀리 나갔나 봐요. 하지만 하늘이 용서하시기를! 앞으로의 도련님 행실이 저의 변명이 되어 주기를 바라요. 정말이지 저는 할 수 있는 말은 다 했답니다. 그러나 모두 헛수고였어요. 그녀는 요지부동이에요. 그녀가 말하길, 젊음을 봐서 여러 잘못은 용서했지만, 난봉꾼의 성품에 대해서는 어찌나 혐오를 드러내던지 저를 완전히 입 다물게 만들더군요. 저는 몇 번이나 도련님을 변호하려 했지만, 그녀의 비난이 너무도 정당해서 제 얼굴을 후려치는 듯했어요. 맹세코, 그녀는 사랑스러운 여인이고, 제가 본 가장 다정하고 분별 있는 사람 가운데 하나예요. 그녀가 한 어떤 말에는 거의 입을 맞추고 싶을 지경이었답니다. 세네카나 주교에게나 어울릴 만한 통찰이었거든요. 제가 '저는 한때 생각했습니다, 아가씨' 하고 말하니, 그녀가 이러더군요. '저는 존스 씨에게서 큰 마음의 선함을 발견했고, 그 점에 대해서는 진심으로 존경했음을 인정합니다. 그러나 행실이 송두리째 방탕에 빠지면 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 마음도 썩어버리는 법이며, 천성이 선한 난봉꾼이 바랄 수 있는 것이라고는 우리가 경멸과 혐오에 한 줌의 연민을 섞어 주는 정도뿐이지요.' 그녀는 천사 같은 사람이에요, 그게 진실이랍니다.\" \"오, 밀러 부인!\" 존스가 대답했다. \"제가 그런 천사를 잃었다는 생각을 어찌 견딜 수 있겠습니까?\" \"잃었다니요! 아니에요.\" 밀러 부인이 외쳤다. \"아직 그녀를 잃은 건 아니길 바라요. 그런 사악한 길을 떠나기로 결심하세요. 그러면 아직 희망이 있을 거예요. 아니, 설령 그녀가 끝내 요지부동이라 해도, 또 다른 젊은 아가씨가 있어요. 다정하고 어여쁜 데다 어마어마한 재산을 가진 아가씨가, 도련님을 향한 사랑에 완전히 애가 타고 있답니다. 바로 오늘 아침에 그 소식을 듣고 웨스턴 아가씨에게 일러주었어요. 아니, 제가 또 진실에서 조금 벗어났네요. 도련님이 그녀를 거절했다고 말했거든요. 하지만 도련님이 그녀를 거절하리라는 걸 저는 정말 알고 있었어요. 그리고 여기서 도련님에게 조금 위안을 드려야겠어요. 제가 그 젊은 아가씨의 이름을 댔을 때, 그 사람은 다름 아닌 어여쁜 과부 헌트였는데, 그녀의 얼굴이 창백해지는 것 같았어요. 그런데 도련님이 그녀를 거절했다고 하니, 맹세컨대 그녀의 얼굴이 순식간에 온통 진홍빛으로 물들더군요. 그리고 그녀가 한 말이 바로 이거예요. '그분이 저에게 어느 정도 애정을 품고 있다는 것은 부인하지 않겠어요.'\"\n\n여기서 대화는 웨스턴이 들이닥치는 바람에 끊기고 말았다. 그는 올워디 본인의 권위로도 더 이상 방 밖에 붙들어 둘 수가 없었다. 우리가 여러 번 보아 왔듯, 그 권위가 그에게는 놀라운 힘을 발휘했음에도 말이다.\n\n웨스턴은 곧장 존스에게 다가가며 외쳤다. \"내 오랜 친구 톰, 자넬 보니 진심으로 기쁘이! 지나간 일은 모두 잊어야지. 내가 자네한테 어떤 모욕을 줄 뜻은 있을 수 없었네. 여기 올워디도 알다시피, 아니, 자네 자신도 알다시피, 나는 자넬 딴 사람으로 여겼으니 말일세. 게다가 해칠 뜻이 없는데, 성급한 말 한두 마디가 무슨 대수란 말인가? 한 기독교도는 다른 기독교도를 잊고 또 용서해야 하는 법이지.\" \"어르신,\" 존스가 말했다. \"제가 어르신께 진 그 많은 은혜를 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다. 하지만 저를 향한 모욕에 대해서라면, 저는 전혀 모르는 일이라고 분명히 말씀드립니다.\" \"그렇구먼.\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"그럼 자네 주먹을 이리 내게. 자넨 이 왕국의 어느 누구 못지않게 씩씩하고 정직한 사내일세. 나랑 같이 가세. 지금 당장 자넬 자네 아가씨한테 데려다주겠네.\" 이때 올워디가 끼어들었다. 향사는 숙부에게도 조카에게도 뜻을 관철하지 못한 채, 옥신각신한 끝에 존스를 소피아에게 소개하는 일을 오후로 미루는 데 동의할 수밖에 없었다. 그 시각에 올워디는, 존스에 대한 연민에서나 웨스턴의 간절한 바람에 응하는 뜻에서나, 다과 자리에 함께하겠노라 약속하도록 설득당했다.\n\n뒤이어 오간 대화는 제법 유쾌했다. 만일 그것이 우리 이야기의 더 앞쪽에서 일어났더라면 우리는 그것으로 독자를 즐겁게 해드렸을 것이다. 그러나 이제는 정말로 중요한 일만 살필 여유밖에 없으니, 오후 방문에 관한 일이 모두 매듭지어진 뒤 웨스턴 씨가 다시 집으로 돌아갔다고만 말해 두면 충분하겠다.\n\n웨스턴 씨가 떠나자, 존스는 올워디 씨와 밀러 부인에게 자신의 석방이 두 귀족의 힘으로 이루어졌음을 알리기 시작했다. 그 두 귀족이 외과의 두 사람과 나이팅게일 씨의 친구 한 사람을 데리고, 자신을 수감했던 치안판사를 찾아갔으며, 외과의들이 다친 자가 그 상처로 인해 결코 어떤 위험에도 처해 있지 않다고 선서하자, 그 판사에게서 풀려나게 되었다는 것이다.\n\n그가 말하기를, 그 두 귀족 가운데 한 사람은 전에 본 적이 있는데 그것도 단 한 번뿐이었고, 다른 한 사람은 자신에게 저지른 잘못을 사과하며 자신을 크게 놀라게 했다는 것이었다. 그 잘못이란, 그 귀족의 말로는 자신이 누구인지를 전혀 몰랐던 데서 비롯된 것이라 했다.\n\n존스가 나중에야 알게 된 이 사건의 진상은 이러했다. 펠라마 경이 벨라스턴 부인의 조언에 따라 존스를 부랑자로 몰아 강제로 해군에 끌어넣으려고 부린 중위는, 우리가 앞서 본 그 사건의 결말을 보고하러 경에게 왔을 때, 모든 면에서 존스 씨의 처신을 매우 호의적으로 이야기했다. 그리고 자신이 사람을 잘못 보았던 게 틀림없다고, 존스는 분명 신사라고 그 경에게 단호히 장담했다. 그리하여 엄격하게 명예를 지키는 사람이며 세상이 두루 비난할 만한 행동은 결코 저지르려 하지 않았던 경은, 자신이 받아들인 그 조언을 두고 크게 마음을 졸이기 시작했다.\n\n이 일이 있은 지 하루 이틀 뒤, 펠라마 경은 마침 그 아일랜드 귀족과 함께 식사를 하게 되었는데, 그가 결투에 관한 이야기를 나누던 중 자리에 함께 있던 사람들에게 피츠패트릭의 인물됨을 알려 주었다. 다만 그는, 특히 그 부인에 관한 일에서는 사실 엄정한 평가를 내리지 못했다. 그는 그 부인이 살아 있는 여인 가운데 가장 결백하고 가장 부당하게 상처받은 사람이며, 자신은 오로지 연민에서 그녀의 편을 들기로 했다고 말했다. 그러고는 다음 날 아침 피츠패트릭의 거처를 찾아가, 가능하다면 그를 설득해 아내와의 별거에 동의하게 만들 생각이라고 밝혔다. 그 귀족의 말로는, 부인이 다시 남편의 손아귀에 들어가게 된다면 목숨을 잃을까 두려워하고 있다는 것이었다. 펠라마 경은 그와 함께 가기로 했다. 존스와 결투의 정황에 대해 좀 더 확실히 마음을 가다듬고 싶었기 때문이었다. 그는 자신이 한 짓을 두고 결코 마음이 편치 않았던 것이다. 경이 부인을 구해 내는 일을 돕겠다는 뜻을 슬쩍 비치는 순간, 다른 귀족은 그것을 덥석 받아들였다. 그는 펠라마 경의 권위에 크게 기대고 있었으니, 그것이 피츠패트릭을 위협해 순순히 따르게 하는 데 큰 보탬이 되리라 여겼던 것이다. 어쩌면 그 생각이 옳았다. 가엾은 아일랜드 사내는 이 고귀한 귀족들이 자기 아내의 편을 들고 나선 것을 보자마자 굴복했고, 별거 약정서가 곧 작성되어 양쪽이 서명을 마쳤기 때문이다.\n\n피츠패트릭은 어퍼튼에서 존스와 자기 아내 사이에 아무 일도 없었다는 점에 대해 워터스 부인에게서 충분히 만족스러운 해명을 들은 터였고, 아니면 어쩌면 다른 어떤 이유에서였는지, 이제는 그 문제에 너무도 무관심해져서 펠라마 경에게 존스를 한껏 두둔하며 모든 잘못을 자기 탓으로 돌렸다. 그러면서 상대방은 신사답게, 또 명예를 아는 사람답게 행동했다고 말했다. 그 경이 존스 씨에 관해 더 캐묻자, 피츠패트릭은 그가 대단히 지체 높고 재산도 많은 어느 신사의 조카라고 일러 주었는데, 이는 워터스 부인이 다울링과 만난 뒤 그에게 막 전해 준 이야기였다.\n\n이제 펠라마 경은 자신이 그토록 심하게 해를 끼친 신사에게 마땅히 할 수 있는 모든 일로 보상을 해야 한다고 여겼다. 그리고 연적 따위는 조금도 염두에 두지 않은 채(이제는 소피아에 대한 생각을 모두 접은 터였으므로), 피츠패트릭에게서나 그 외과의에게서나 상처가 치명적이지 않다는 확신을 얻고는 존스 씨의 석방을 도모하기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그는 아일랜드 귀족을 설득해 존스가 갇혀 있는 곳까지 함께 가게 되었고, 그곳에서 우리가 이미 이야기한 대로 존스를 대했던 것이다.\n\n올워디는 자신의 거처로 돌아오자마자 존스를 곧장 자기 방으로 데리고 들어가, 워터스 부인에게서 들은 것이며 다울링 씨에게서 알아낸 것이며 모든 일을 그에게 일러 주었다.\n\n존스는 이 이야기에 크게 놀라고 그에 못지않게 마음 쓰는 기색을 보였으나, 거기에 대해 아무 토도, 아무 견해도 달지 않았다. 그때 블리필 씨로부터, 외삼촌이 한가하신지 알고 싶다는, 찾아뵙고자 한다는 전갈이 왔다. 올워디는 흠칫하며 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 내가 짐작하기로는 전에 그가 한 번도 쓴 적 없을 만큼 격앙된 어조로, 하인에게 블리필에게 가서 자기는 그런 사람을 모른다고 전하라 일렀다. \"고정하십시오, 외삼촌.\" 존스가 떨리는 목소리로 외쳤다. \"이미 고심해 보았다.\" 올워디가 답했다. \"그러니 네가 직접 그 악당에게 내 전갈을 전해야겠다. 그자가 그토록 악랄하게 꾸민 파멸을 당할 뻔한 바로 그 사람만큼, 그 파멸의 선고를 마땅히 그에게 전할 자가 어디 있겠느냐.\" \"용서하십시오, 외삼촌.\" 존스가 말했다. \"잠깐만 곱씹어 보셔도 그 반대임을 분명 깨달으실 겁니다. 다른 사람의 입에서 나온다면 어쩌면 정당한 말일지라도, 제 입에서 나온다면 그것은 모욕이 될 것입니다. 게다가 누구에게 말입니까? 제 친형제이자 외삼촌의 조카에게요. 그가 저를 그토록 모질게 대한 것도 아니었습니다. 사실 그랬다면, 그것이야말로 그가 한 어떤 짓보다도 용서받기 힘든 일이었을 겁니다. 운명은 그리 나쁘지 않은 성품의 사람조차 불의로 꾈 수 있습니다. 하지만 모욕이란 오로지 검고 원한에 찬 마음에서만 나오며, 그것을 변명해 줄 유혹 같은 것도 없습니다. 부디 간청하건대, 지금 노여움이 한껏 솟구친 이때에는 그를 두고 아무 일도 하지 말아 주십시오. 헤아려 주십시오, 외삼촌. 저 역시 변명 한마디 듣지 못한 채 단죄당하지는 않았으니까요.\" 올워디는 한순간 말없이 서 있다가, 존스를 끌어안으며 두 눈에서 눈물을 쏟아 내며 말했다. \"오, 내 아이야! 내가 그토록 오래 어떤 선함에 눈이 멀어 있었단 말이냐!\"\n\n바로 그 순간 밀러 부인이, 미처 들리지 않은 가벼운 노크 끝에 방으로 들어와, 존스가 외삼촌의 품에 안겨 있는 것을 보았다. 가엾은 부인은 기쁨에 겨워 무릎을 꿇고는, 일어난 일에 대해 하늘을 향해 더없이 황홀한 감사의 기도를 터뜨렸다. 그러고는 존스에게 달려가 그를 와락 끌어안으며 외쳤다. \"내 더없이 소중한 벗이여, 이 복된 날을 천 번이고 만 번이고 축하드려요.\" 다음으로 올워디 씨 자신이 똑같은 축하를 받았다. 그는 이렇게 답했다. \"정말이지, 정말이지, 밀러 부인, 나는 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 행복합니다.\" 모두가 더 몇 마디 환희를 주고받은 뒤, 밀러 부인은 두 사람에게 응접실로 내려가 함께 식사하자고 청했다. 그곳에는 매우 행복한 사람들이 모여 있다는 것이었는데, 사실 그들은 다름 아닌 나이팅게일 씨와 그 신부, 그리고 그의 사촌 해리엇과 그녀의 신랑이었다.\n\n올워디는 함께 식사하는 것을 양해해 달라고 했다. 자기와 조카가 따로 의논할 사사로운 일이 많아, 자기 방에 두 사람을 위한 간단한 음식을 준비해 두라 일렀다는 것이었다. 다만 자신과 존스가 저녁 식사 자리만큼은 부인의 모임에 함께하겠노라는 약속만은 마다하지 않았다.\n\n그러자 밀러 부인은 블리필을 어찌할 작정이냐고 물었다. \"정말이지,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저런 악당이 제 집에 있는 한 저는 마음이 편할 수가 없어요.\" 올워디가 답했다. \"나 역시 같은 이유로 부인 못지않게 마음이 편치 않소.\" \"오!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 그 일은 저에게 맡겨 주세요. 제가 그자를 당장 문밖으로 내쫓아 보이겠어요, 장담합니다. 아래층에 건장한 사내가 두셋은 있으니까요.\" \"폭력 같은 건 전혀 필요 없을 거요.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"내 전갈만 그에게 전해 주면, 그는 분명 제 발로 떠날 거라 확신하오.\" \"제가요?\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"이보다 더 기꺼운 마음으로 한 일은 제 평생 없었어요.\" 여기서 존스가 끼어들어 말했다. \"그 문제를 좀 더 곰곰이 생각해 보았는데, 올워디 외삼촌만 허락하신다면 제가 직접 전령이 되고 싶습니다. 외삼촌의 뜻은,\" 그가 말했다. \"이미 충분히 알고 있으니, 제 말로써 그에게 그 뜻을 전하게 해 주십시오. 부디 간청하건대, 외삼촌.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그를 격렬하고 갑작스러운 절망으로 몰아넣었을 때의 끔찍한 결과를 헤아려 주십시오. 아아, 지금 같은 처지에서 죽음을 맞기에 저 가엾은 사람은 얼마나 준비가 안 되어 있겠습니까.\" 이 말은 밀러 부인에게는 조금도 효력을 미치지 못했다. 그녀는 방을 나서며 외쳤다. \"당신은 너무 착해요, 존스 씨, 이 세상에 살기엔 더없이 지나치게 착하시다고요.\" 하지만 그 말은 올워디에게는 더 깊은 인상을 남겼다. \"착한 내 아이야.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 네 마음의 선함과 네 분별의 민첩함에 똑같이 놀랐다. 정말이지 하늘이 막아 주시기를, 이 가엾은 자가 회개할 어떤 수단이나 시간도 빼앗기지 않기를! 그것은 참으로 충격적인 일일 테니 말이다. 그러니 그에게 가서 네 재량껏 하여라. 다만 내 용서를 바랄 어떤 희망으로도 그를 부추기지는 말아라. 나는 내 신앙이 명하는 그 이상으로는 결코 악행을 용서하지 않을 것이며, 그 신앙이라는 것도 우리의 베풂이나 교류에까지 미치지는 않으니까.\"\n\n존스는 블리필의 방으로 올라갔는데, 그곳에서 본 그의 모습은 자신의 연민을 자아냈으나, 많은 사람들의 눈에는 그보다 덜 너그러운 감정을 불러일으킬 만한 것이었다. 그는 침대에 몸을 던진 채 절망에 자신을 내맡기고 눈물에 잠겨 누워 있었다. 그러나 그것은 뉘우침에서 흘러나와, 타고난 성정을 거슬러 자기도 모르게 죄에 꾀이거나 휩쓸린 마음에서 죄를 씻어 내는 그런 눈물은 아니었다. 그런 눈물이라면 인간의 연약함 탓에 때로는 선한 사람에게서조차 흐르기도 하건만. 아니, 이 눈물은 겁에 질린 도둑이 호송 수레 위에서 흘리는 그런 눈물이었으니, 실은 아무리 야만적인 본성이라도 좀처럼 모자라는 법이 없는, 제 자신을 향한 그 근심의 결과일 뿐이었다.\n\n이 장면을 낱낱이 그려 내는 것은 불쾌하고도 지루한 일이 될 것이다. 다만 존스의 처신이 지나칠 만큼 다정했다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하리라. 그는 외삼촌의 결심, 곧 블리필이 그날 저녁 이 집을 떠나야 한다는 그 결정을 그에게 전하기에 앞서, 풀이 죽은 블리필의 기운을 북돋고 위로하기 위해 자신의 기지로 떠올릴 수 있는 모든 것을 빠짐없이 동원했다. 그는 그가 필요로 하는 돈은 얼마든 대 주겠노라 제안했고, 자신에게 저지른 모든 일을 진심으로 용서한다고 확언했으며, 앞으로는 그와 형제처럼 지내도록 애쓰겠고, 외삼촌과의 화해를 이루기 위해서라면 무엇이든 다 해 보겠다고 말했다.\n\n블리필은 처음에는 뚱하니 입을 다문 채, 아직도 모든 것을 부인할까 마음속으로 저울질했다. 그러나 마침내 자신에게 불리한 증거가 너무 확고함을 깨닫고는, 결국 고백에 의지하고 말았다. 그러고는 더없이 격렬한 태도로 형에게 용서를 빌며 땅바닥에 엎드려 그의 발에 입을 맞추었다. 요컨대 그는, 앞서 두드러지게 사악했던 만큼이나 이제는 두드러지게 비굴해졌다.\n\n존스는 이 극도의 비굴함 앞에서 자신의 경멸을 다 억누르지는 못해, 그것이 얼굴에 조금 드러나고 말았다. 그는 형제를 되도록 빨리 땅바닥에서 일으켜 세우고는, 좀 더 사내답게 고난을 견디라고 충고했다. 그러면서 동시에, 그 고난을 덜어 주기 위해 자신이 할 수 있는 모든 일을 하겠다는 약속을 거듭했다. 이에 블리필은 자신이 그럴 자격이 없노라고 여러 번 되뇌며 넘치도록 감사를 쏟아 냈다. 그러고는 그가 곧장 다른 거처로 떠나겠다고 밝히자, 존스는 외삼촌에게로 돌아갔다.\n\n다른 여러 일과 더불어, 올워디는 이제 5백 파운드짜리 은행권에 관해 자신이 알아낸 사실을 존스에게 일러 주었다. \"나는,\" 그가 말했다. \"이미 한 법률가에게 자문을 구했는데, 그자의 말로는 이런 종류의 사기에는 처벌이 없다는구나. 참으로 놀라운 일이지. 정말이지, 너를 향한 이 작자의 시커먼 배은망덕을 생각하면, 노상강도조차 그에 비하면 결백한 사람이라 여겨지는구나.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 존스가 말했다. \"그게 가능한 일입니까? 이 소식에 저는 한량없이 충격을 받았습니다. 저는 세상에 그보다 더 정직한 사람은 없을 거라 여겼는데요. 그만한 액수의 유혹은 그가 버텨 내기에 너무 컸던 거지요. 더 적은 액수는 그의 손을 거쳐 무사히 제게 닿곤 했으니까요. 정말이지, 외삼촌, 저는 그것을 배은망덕이라기보다 약함이라 부르도록 허락해 주셔야겠습니다. 저 가엾은 사람이 저를 사랑하며, 제가 결코 잊을 수 없는 친절을 여러 번 베풀었다고 저는 확신하니까요. 아니, 저는 그가 바로 이 짓을 두고 뉘우쳤으리라 믿습니다. 불과 하루 이틀 전, 제 형편이 가장 절망적으로 보이던 그때에도, 그는 갇혀 있던 저를 찾아와 제가 필요한 돈은 얼마든 대 주겠다고 했으니까요. 헤아려 주십시오, 외삼촌. 그토록 쓰라린 곤궁을 맛본 사람이, 자기와 가족을 앞으로 그런 고통을 다시 겪을 어떤 가능성에서도 영영 벗어나게 해 줄 큰돈을 손에 쥐고 있다는 것이, 그에게 얼마나 큰 유혹이었겠습니까.\"\n\n\"얘야.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"너는 이 용서하는 마음을 너무 멀리까지 끌고 가는구나. 그렇게 그릇된 자비는 약함일 뿐 아니라 불의에 맞닿아 있으며, 악을 부추기는 만큼 사회에 매우 해롭다. 이 작자의 부정직함이라면 어쩌면 내가 용서했을지도 모르겠다만, 그 배은망덕만큼은 결코 용서할 수 없다. 그리고 한마디 하게 해 다오. 어떤 유혹이 부정직함 자체를 갚아 주도록 우리가 받아들인다면, 그것은 우리가 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 공정하고 자비로운 것이다. 사실 나도 그만큼은 가 보았다. 나는 대배심에 앉아 있을 때 노상강도의 운명을 가엾이 여기곤 했고, 그 사정에 정상을 참작할 여지가 있는 자들을 위해서는 한두 번 이상 판사에게 청을 넣기도 했으니까. 하지만 부정직함이 잔혹함이나 살인, 배은망덕 따위의 더 시커먼 죄와 한데 얽힐 때면, 연민과 용서는 그때 잘못이 되고 만다. 나는 그 작자가 악당임을 확신하니, 그는 벌을 받을 것이다. 적어도 내가 벌할 수 있는 한도까지는 말이다.\"\n\n이 말은 너무도 준엄한 목소리로 내뱉어진지라, 존스는 어떤 대꾸도 하는 것이 마땅치 않다고 여겼다. 게다가 웨스턴 씨가 정해 둔 시각이 어느덧 가까워져, 옷을 차려입을 시간조차 빠듯하게 남아 있었다. 그리하여 지금의 대화는 여기서 끝났고, 존스는 다른 방으로 물러났다. 그곳에는 분부받은 대로 파트리지가 그의 옷가지를 들고 시중을 들고 있었다.\n\n파트리지는 그 행복한 발견 이후로 주인을 거의 보지 못한 터였다. 가엾은 사내는 자신의 환희를 가눌 수도, 다 드러낼 수도 없었다. 그는 미쳐 날뛰는 사람처럼 굴었고, 존스에게 옷을 입히면서 어찌나 많은 실수를 저질렀던지, 무대 위에서 제 옷을 입던 어릿광대가 저지르던 실수를 내가 본 것에 견줄 만했다.\n\n그러나 그의 기억력만큼은 조금도 부족함이 없었다. 그는 이제 이 행복한 사건의 수많은 전조와 징조를 떠올렸는데, 그중 몇 가지는 그때 그가 눈여겨봐 두었던 것이었고, 더 많은 것들은 이제야 기억해 낸 것이었다. 또한 존스를 만나기 전날 저녁에 꾸었던 꿈들도 빼놓지 않았다. 그러고는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"제가 늘 나리께 말씀드렸지요. 나리께서 언젠가 한 번쯤은 제 운을 틔워 주실 힘을 갖게 되리라는 무언가가 제 마음에 점쳐진다고 말입니다.\" 존스는 그에게, 다른 모든 전조가 자신에게 들어맞았던 것만큼이나 이 점괘도 그에게 틀림없이 이루어질 것이라고 확언해 주었다. 이 말은 가엾은 사내가 이미 주인을 두고 품고 있던 그 모든 환희에 적잖이 보탬이 되었다.\n\n결말에 한층 더 가까이 다가가다.\n\n이제 옷차림을 완전히 갖춘 존스는 숙부를 모시고 웨스턴 씨 댁으로 향했다. 그는 실로 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 빼어난 풍채를 지닌 사내였으며, 그 외모만으로도 세상 여인 대부분의 마음을 사로잡았을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 이 이야기를 통해 이미 드러났기를 바란다. 자연이 그를 빚을 때, 가끔 그러하듯 이 한 가지 장점에만 온전히 기대어 자신의 작품을 내세우려 하지는 않았다는 사실을 말이다.\n\n소피아는, 화가 났으면서도 그녀대로 가장 돋보이는 차림을 하고 있었는데—그 까닭은 여성 독자들의 헤아림에 맡기겠다—어찌나 곱고 아름다웠던지, 올워디조차 그녀를 보고는 웨스턴에게 그녀가 세상에서 가장 아리따운 사람이라고 속삭이지 않을 수 없었다. 이에 웨스턴은 속삭이듯 대답했으나 그 자리에 있던 모두가 다 들을 만한 소리로 말했다. \"그러니 톰한테는 더 잘된 일 아닌가.—제기랄, 저 녀석이 저 애를 끌어안고 뒹굴지 못하면 내가 사람이 아니지.\" 이 말에 소피아는 온통 새빨개졌고, 톰의 낯빛은 그와 반대로 온통 핏기가 가셔 의자에서 까무러칠 지경이었다.\n\n찻상이 채 치워지기도 전에 웨스턴은 올워디를 방 밖으로 끌어냈다. 긴히 전할 일이 있어 잊어버리기 전에 지금 당장 단둘이 이야기해야겠다는 것이었다.\n\n이제 두 연인만 남게 되었다. 위험과 어려움이 그들의 대화를 따라다닐 때에는 서로에게 그토록 할 말이 많았고, 그토록 많은 장애물이 가로놓여 있을 때에는 서로의 품으로 뛰어들고 싶어 그토록 안달하던 두 사람이, 이제 마음 놓고 무슨 말이든 무슨 일이든 자유로이 할 수 있게 되자 한동안 둘 다 말없이 꼼짝도 않고 앉아 있었다는 사실은—내 의심치 않건대—많은 독자에게 기이하게 비칠 것이다. 어찌나 그러했던지, 어지간한 눈치를 지닌 낯선 사람이라면 둘이 서로에게 무심하다고 결론지었을 법도 했다. 그러나 아무리 기이해 보여도 사정은 그러했다. 둘 다 눈을 내리깔고 바닥만 바라본 채 몇 분 동안 완전한 침묵을 이어갔다.\n\n그 사이 존스 씨는 한두 번 입을 열려 했으나 도무지 말이 나오지 않아, 그저 몇 마디 끊긴 말을 웅얼거리거나, 차라리 한숨처럼 내뱉을 뿐이었다. 그러다 마침내 소피아가, 한편으로는 그가 가여워서, 또 한편으로는 그가 꺼내려 애쓰고 있음을 빤히 아는 그 화제에서 이야기를 돌리려고 입을 열었다.\n\n\"분명히, 선생님, 이번에 모든 사실이 밝혀졌으니 선생님은 세상에서 가장 운이 좋은 분이시네요.\" \"그런데 정말로, 아가씨, 저를 그토록 운이 좋다고 여기실 수 있겠습니까,\" 존스가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"제가 당신의 노여움을 산 마당에 말입니다.\" \"글쎄요, 선생님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 점이라면 그럴 만한 짓을 하셨는지 아닌지는 선생님이 가장 잘 아시겠지요.\" \"정말이지, 아가씨,\" 그가 대답했다. \"제 허물이라면 당신께서도 죄다 알고 계시지 않습니까. 밀러 부인이 모든 진실을 다 일러드렸으니까요. 오! 나의 소피아, 저는 영영 용서를 바랄 수 없는 것입니까?\" \"제 생각엔, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 거의 선생님 자신의 공정함에 기댈 수 있을 듯하니, 선생님 자신의 행실에 대한 판결은 선생님 스스로에게 맡기겠어요.\" \"아아! 아가씨,\" 그가 대답했다. \"제가 당신 손에 비는 것은 공정함이 아니라 자비입니다. 공정함이라면 분명 저를 단죄할 수밖에 없음을 압니다.—그러나 제가 벨라스턴 부인에게 보낸 그 편지 때문은 아닙니다. 그 일에 관해서라면 제가 진실한 해명을 드렸음을 더없이 엄숙히 단언합니다.\" 그러고는 그는, 기대와 달리 부인이 자신의 청혼을 받아들였을 경우 깨끗이 발을 뺄 그럴듯한 구실을 나이팅게일이 보장해 주었음을 거듭 강조했다. 다만 그런 편지를 그녀의 손에 쥐여준 것은 자신의 큰 경솔함이었음을 인정하며 말했다. \"그 경솔함에 대해서는,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신에게 미친 그 결과로 톡톡히 대가를 치르고 있습니다.\" \"저는 그 편지를 두고 선생님이 바라시는 것과 다르게 믿지 않아요, 아니, 믿을 수가 없어요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제 행동을 보시면 제가 거기에 별것 없다고 여긴다는 걸 분명히 아실 거예요. 그렇지만, 존스 씨, 제가 분개할 까닭이 그뿐이겠어요? 어프턴에서 그 일이 있은 뒤, 그토록 금세 다른 여자와 새로운 연애에 빠지다니요. 그러는 동안에도 저는 선생님 가슴이 저 때문에 피 흘리고 있다 여겼고, 선생님은 그런 척하셨잖아요. 정말이지, 선생님은 이상하게 처신하셨어요. 제게 고백하신 그 열정이 진심이라고 제가 믿을 수 있겠어요? 설령 믿는다 한들, 그토록 변덕스러운 사람과 무슨 행복을 장담할 수 있겠어요?\" \"오! 나의 소피아,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"일찍이 인간의 가슴을 불태운 가장 순결한 열정의 진실함을 의심하지 마십시오. 가장 사랑스러운 분이시여, 제 불행한 처지를, 제 절망을 헤아려 주십시오. 나의 소피아여, 지금처럼 당신 발치에 이 몸을 던지도록 허락받으리라는 아주 먼 희망이라도 제가 품을 수 있었더라면, 그 어떤 다른 여인도 가장 엄격한 정절조차 단죄할 만한 생각을 제 안에 불러일으키지 못했을 것입니다. 당신에게 변심이라니요! 오 소피아! 지나간 일을 너그러이 용서해 주실 만큼 선량하시거든, 부디 잔인한 미래의 두려움 따위로 제게 베푸실 자비를 닫지 말아 주십시오. 이보다 더 진실한 뉘우침은 일찍이 없었습니다. 오! 이 사랑스러운 가슴 안에서 그 뉘우침이 저를 제 천국과 화해하게 해 주기를.\" \"진실한 뉘우침은, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"죄인의 용서를 얻어내겠지요. 그러나 그것은 그 진실함을 완벽하게 판별할 줄 아는 분에게서나 그러합니다. 인간의 마음은 속을 수 있으며, 그것을 막을 확실한 방법도 없으니까요. 그러니 알아 두세요. 제가 선생님의 뉘우침에 마음이 움직여 용서하게 되더라도, 적어도 그 진실함에 대한 가장 강력한 증거만큼은 반드시 요구하겠어요.\" \"제 힘으로 댈 수 있는 증거라면 무엇이든 말씀하십시오,\" 존스가 간절히 대답했다. \"시간이에요,\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"오직 시간만이, 존스 씨, 선생님이 참되이 뉘우치는 사람이며, 그 못된 행실을 끊어버리기로 결심했음을 제게 납득시켜 줄 수 있어요. 만약 선생님이 그런 행실을 계속할 수 있는 사람이라 여겨진다면, 저는 그 때문에 선생님을 혐오하게 될 거예요.\" \"그리 여기지 마십시오,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"무릎 꿇고 간청합니다, 부디 저를 믿어 주십시오. 그 믿음을 얻을 자격을 갖추는 것이야말로 제 평생의 과업이 되게 하겠습니다.\" \"그렇다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"선생님 생애의 얼마간을, 그 믿음을 받을 자격이 있음을 제게 보여 주는 일에 바치세요. 제가 충분히 분명하게 말씀드린 것 같은데요. 선생님이 제 믿음을 받을 만하다는 것을 제가 보게 되면, 그 믿음을 얻게 되실 거예요. 지나간 일이 있은 뒤에, 선생님, 제가 선생님 말만 곧이듣기를 바라실 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n그가 대답했다. \"제 말만으로 저를 믿지는 마십시오. 제겐 더 나은 담보가 있습니다. 제 한결같음에 대한 증표가요. 그것은 보고도 의심할 수 없는 것입니다.\" \"그게 무엇인데요?\" 소피아가 조금 놀라며 말했다. \"보여 드리지요, 나의 매혹적인 천사여,\" 존스가 외치며 그녀의 손을 잡아 거울 앞으로 데려갔다. \"자, 저기, 저 거울 속을 보십시오. 저 사랑스러운 자태를, 저 얼굴을, 저 몸매를, 저 눈을, 그리고 저 눈을 통해 빛나는 저 마음을 말입니다. 이것들을 차지하게 될 사내가 변심할 수 있겠습니까? 어림없습니다! 나의 소피아여, 저것들은 도리먼트도, 로체스터 경도 붙들어 매어 둘 것입니다. 당신 자신의 눈이 아닌 다른 눈으로 당신을 볼 수 있다면, 결코 그것을 의심하지 못하실 겁니다.\" 소피아는 얼굴을 붉히며 반쯤 미소를 지었다. 그러나 다시 억지로 이마를 찌푸리며 말했다. \"지난 일로 앞날을 가늠해야 한다면, 제가 방을 나가면 이 거울 속에 제 모습이 남지 않듯, 제가 선생님 눈에서 사라지면 제 모습도 더는 선생님 가슴에 남아 있지 않겠지요.\" \"하늘에 두고, 신성한 모든 것에 두고 맹세하건대!\" 존스가 말했다. \"당신은 단 한 번도 제 가슴을 떠난 적이 없습니다. 당신네 여성의 섬세함으로는 우리 남성의 거친 면모를, 또 한 종류의 연애가 마음과 얼마나 무관한지를 헤아릴 수 없을 것입니다.\" \"저는 결코,\" 소피아가 자못 진지하게 대답했다. \"그런 구별 따위는 저처럼 도무지 할 줄 모를 만큼 충분히 고상함을 익히지 못한 사람과는 결혼하지 않겠어요.\" \"그것을 배우겠습니다,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이미 배웠습니다. 나의 소피아가 제 아내가 될지도 모른다는 희망이 싹튼 그 첫 순간이 단번에 그것을 제게 가르쳐 주었습니다. 그 순간부터 나머지 모든 여성은 제 감각에 욕망의 대상이 되지도, 제 가슴에 정열의 대상이 되지도 못하게 되었습니다.\" \"좋아요,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그건 시간이 증명해 주어야겠지요. 선생님의 처지는, 존스 씨, 이제 바뀌었어요. 솔직히 저는 그 변화가 무척 흡족하답니다. 이제 선생님은 제 곁에 머물며 선생님 마음도 바뀌었음을 제게 납득시킬 기회가 부족하진 않으실 거예요.\" \"오! 나의 천사여,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"당신의 선량함에 제가 어찌 감사를 다 드릴 수 있겠습니까! 그리고 제가 잘되는 것이 흡족하다고 인정해 주실 만큼 그토록 너그러우신 것입니까?——믿어 주십시오, 믿어 주십시오, 아가씨, 그 행운에 운치를 더해 준 것은 오직 당신뿐입니다. 제가 그 덕에 그 소중한 희망을 품게 된 까닭이니까요——오! 나의 소피아여, 부디 그것이 먼 훗날의 희망이 되지 않게 해 주십시오.—저는 당신의 분부에 온전히 따르겠습니다. 당신이 허락하시는 것 이상으로는 감히 아무것도 재촉하지 않겠습니다. 그래도 부디 간청하건대, 짧은 시험 기간을 정해 주십시오. 오! 가장 엄숙하게 진실인 그것을 당신이 언제쯤 납득하시게 될지, 제게 일러 주십시오.\" \"제가 자진하여 여기까지 온 마당에, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"재촉받기를 바라진 않아요. 아니, 재촉당하지 않겠어요.\"—\"오! 그렇게 매정하게 보지 마십시오, 나의 소피아여,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"재촉하지 않겠습니다, 감히 재촉하지 못합니다.—그래도 적어도 한 번만 더, 그 기한을 정해 주시기를 청하게 해 주십시오. 오! 사랑의 조바심을 헤아려 주십시오.\"—\"한 일 년쯤일까요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오! 나의 소피아여,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신은 영원이나 다름없는 세월을 일러 주셨군요.\"—\"어쩌면 좀 더 빠를 수도 있겠지요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 졸림을 당하고 싶지 않아요. 저를 향한 선생님의 열정이 제가 바라는 그대로라면, 이제는 마음을 놓으셔도 될 듯한데요.\"—\"마음을 놓으라니요! 소피아, 저처럼 환희에 찬 행복을 그토록 차가운 말로 부르지 마십시오.——오! 가슴 벅찬 생각이여! 그 복된 날이 오리라는 것을, 당신을 제 사람이라 부를 그날이, 두려움이라곤 더는 없을 그날이, 나의 소피아를 행복하게 해 줄 그 소중하고도 광대하며 더없이 황홀한 환희를 누릴 그날이 오리라는 것을 제가 확신하지 않습니까?\"—\"정말이지, 선생님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그날은 선생님 손에 달려 있는걸요.\"—\"오! 나의 사랑스러운, 나의 거룩한 천사여,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그 말씀에 기뻐서 미칠 것만 같습니다.——그러나 제 더없는 복을 그토록 달콤하게 일러 준 그 사랑스러운 입술에 감사를 드려야겠습니다, 아니 드리겠습니다.\" 그러고는 그녀를 와락 품에 안고, 일찍이 한 번도 감히 보이지 못했던 뜨거움으로 입을 맞추었다.\n\n바로 그 순간, 한동안 문밖에서 엿듣고 서 있던 웨스턴이 방으로 뛰어들며 사냥터에서 쓰는 목청과 말투로 외쳤다. \"그 애한테 가, 이 녀석, 그 애한테! 가서 안아!——옳지, 요 귀여운 것들, 오 바로 그거야! 자! 어떻게 됐어, 다 끝난 거냐? 날은 잡았어, 이 녀석? 어때, 내일로 할까 모레로 할까? 모레보다 단 한순간도 미루는 일은 없을 거다, 내 그리 작정했으니.\" \"부디 청하건대, 어르신,\" 존스가 말했다. \"제가 빌미가 되어\"——\"청하긴 무슨 얼어죽을,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"나는 네놈을 처녀애 같은 잔꾀 따위에 굴복하는 위인보다는 좀 더 기개 있는 녀석으로 봤다.——내 말하건대 그건 죄다 헛수작이야. 빌어먹을! 저 애는 진심으로 오늘 밤에라도 혼례를 치르고 싶을 게다. 안 그러냐, 소피? 자, 솔직히 털어놓고 한 번쯤은 정직한 계집애가 되어 보거라. 뭐냐, 꿀먹은 벙어리냐? 어째서 말을 안 해?\" \"어째서 제가 털어놓아야 하나요, 아버지,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"아버지가 제 속마음을 그토록 잘 알고 계신 듯한데요?\"——\"착하다, 우리 딸,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그럼 승낙하는 거지?\" \"아니에요, 정말로, 아버지,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"저는 그런 승낙은 한 적 없어요.\"——\"그럼 내일도 모레도 저 녀석을 맞을 생각이 없단 말이냐?\" 웨스턴이 말했다.—\"정말로, 아버지,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 그럴 뜻이 없어요.\" \"허나 내 말해 주마,\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"어째서 그런지를. 그저 네가 거역하기를, 네 아비를 괴롭히고 속 썩이기를 좋아해서 그러는 게야.\" \"제발, 어르신,\" 존스가 끼어들며 말했다——\"내 말하건대 너는 풋내기다,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내가 저 애한테 안 된다 했을 때는 죄다 한숨에 우는소리에 애태우고 편지질이더니, 이제 내가 네 편을 드니까 저 애가 네 반대편으로 돌아서는구나. 죄다 청개구리 심보지, 그뿐이야. 제 아비한테 이끌리고 다스려지는 걸 못 견디는 게다, 그게 사실의 전부야. 그저 나를 거스르고 거역하려는 게지.\" \"아버지는 제가 어쩌기를 바라세요?\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"내가 너더러 어쩌라겠느냐?\" 그가 말했다. \"아니, 당장 저 녀석한테 네 손을 내주란 말이다.\"—\"좋아요, 아버지,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"분부에 따르겠어요.—여기 제 손이에요, 존스 씨.\" \"그래, 그럼 내일 아침에 저 녀석을 맞아들이는 데 승낙하는 거냐?\" 웨스턴이 말했다.—\"아버지께 순종하겠어요,\" 그녀가 외쳤다.—\"그럼 내일 아침을 그날로 잡자꾸나,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그럼 내일 아침을 그날로 하지요, 아버지, 아버지가 그토록 원하시니,\" 소피아가 말했다. 그러자 존스는 무릎을 꿇고 환희의 격정에 휩싸여 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었고, 그동안 웨스턴은 방 안을 폴짝폴짝 뛰며 춤을 추다가 이내 외쳤다.—\"대체 올워디는 어디 있는 거야? 다른 일에나 마음을 써야 할 판에, 지금 밖에서 그 빌어먹을 변호사 다울링이랑 노닥거리고 있구먼.\" 그러고는 그를 찾으러 뛰쳐나가, 마침맞게도 두 연인이 단둘이 몇 분간 다정한 시간을 누리도록 자리를 비워 주었다.\n\n그러나 그는 곧 올워디를 데리고 돌아와 말했다. \"내 말을 못 믿겠거든 자네가 직접 저 애한테 물어보게. 안 그러냐, 소피, 내일 혼인하는 데 승낙했지?\" \"그것이 아버지의 분부이니,\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"감히 거역하는 죄를 지을 수가 없지요.\" \"바라건대, 아가씨,\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"제 조카가 그토록 큰 호의를 받을 자격이 있는 사람이 되어, 아가씨가 우리 집안에 베푸신 크나큰 영광을 저처럼 늘 깊이 새기기를 바랍니다. 이토록 매혹적이고 이토록 훌륭한 아가씨와 인연을 맺는 것은 영국에서 가장 지체 높은 이에게도 참으로 영광일 것입니다.\" \"그렇지,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"허나 내가 저 애더러 이리 갈까 저리 갈까, 우물쭈물 미적거리도록 내버려 뒀더라면, 자네는 그 영광을 한동안 누리지 못했을 게야. 내가 부득이 아비로서의 권위를 좀 부려서 저 애를 끌어온 거라네.\" \"그러지 않았기를 바랍니다, 어르신,\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"조금의 강요도 없었기를 바랍니다.\" \"이런, 그렇다면,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"원한다면 저 애더러 한 말을 죄다 도로 무르라고 해 보게나. 네 약속을 진심으로 후회하느냐, 안 그러냐, 소피아?\" \"정말이지, 아버지,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"저는 후회하지 않아요. 존스 씨를 위한 약속이라면 그 어떤 것도 앞으로도 결코 후회하지 않으리라 믿어요.\" \"그렇다면, 조카야,\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"진심으로 축하한다. 너야말로 세상에서 가장 행복한 사내라 여겨지는구나. 그리고 아가씨, 이 기쁜 자리에서 제가 축하의 말을 올리도록 허락해 주십시오. 참으로, 저는 확신합니다. 아가씨는 당신의 크나큰 미덕을 알아볼 사람에게, 적어도 그 미덕을 받을 자격을 갖추고자 최선을 다할 사람에게 자신을 내어 주신 것이라고요.\" \"최선을 다한다고!\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"암, 그러고말고, 내 장담하지.——이보게, 올워디, 자네한테 5파운드 대 1크라운을 걸겠네. 오늘로부터 아홉 달 뒤엔 우리한테 사내아이가 생길 거야. 한데 어디 말해 보게, 뭘로 하려나! 부르고뉴로 할 텐가, 샹파뉴로 할 텐가, 아니면 뭘로? 주피터 신께 비노니, 우리 오늘 밤 진탕 마셔 보세나.\" \"참으로, 어르신,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"용서를 구해야겠습니다. 조카의 행복이 이토록 가까이 다가온 줄 짐작도 하기 전에, 저와 조카는 이미 다른 약속이 있었답니다.\"—\"약속이라!\" 지주가 말했다. \"그런 소리 말게.—오늘 밤만큼은 무슨 일이 있어도 자네를 보낼 수 없네. 여기서 저녁을 들어야지, 해리 영주님께 맹세코.\" \"용서해 주셔야겠습니다, 이웃 양반!\" 올워디가 대답했다. \"제가 엄숙히 약속한 일인데, 제가 약속을 깨는 법이 없음은 잘 아시지 않습니까.\" \"이런, 어디, 대체 누구와 약속했단 말인가?\" 지주가 외쳤다.——그러자 올워디는 그에게 사정을 알리고, 함께할 일행에 대해서도 일러 주었다.——\"이런 빌어먹을!\" 지주가 대답했다. \"내가 자네랑 함께 가지, 소피도 같이 가고! 오늘 밤만큼은 자네를 보낼 수 없으니 말이야. 게다가 톰이랑 저 애를 떼어 놓는 건 너무 야박한 짓이지.\" 이 제안을 올워디는 곧바로 받아들였고, 소피아도 동의했다. 다만 그녀는 먼저, 자신의 결혼에 관해서는 한 마디도 입에 올리지 않겠다는 약속을 아버지에게서 은밀히 받아 두었다.\n\n젊은 나이팅게일은 그날 오후 약속대로 아버지를 찾아뵈었는데, 아버지는 그가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 따뜻하게 그를 맞아 주었다. 그곳에서 그는 새로 결혼한 딸을 찾아 도시로 돌아온 숙부와도 마주쳤다.\n\n이 결혼은 이 젊은 신사에게 일어날 수 있었던 일 가운데 가장 운 좋은 사건이었다. 이 두 형제는 자식 교육 방식을 두고 늘 다투며 지냈으니, 서로가 택한 방법을 진심으로 경멸했던 것이다. 그러므로 이제 두 사람은 저마다 제 자식이 저지른 잘못은 되도록 감싸고, 상대 자식의 혼사는 되도록 깎아내리려 애썼다. 형제를 이겨 보고 싶은 이 욕망에, 올워디가 펼친 여러 논리까지 더해지자, 그 효과는 노신사에게 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 그는 미소 띤 얼굴로 아들을 맞이했고, 실제로 그날 저녁 밀러 부인 댁에서 아들과 함께 저녁을 들기로 응낙했다.\n\n다른 한쪽으로 말하자면, 딸을 더없이 지극한 애정으로 진심으로 사랑하던 그를 화해 쪽으로 기울게 하는 데에는 별 어려움이 없었다. 조카에게서 딸과 사위가 어디 있는지 듣자마자, 그는 당장 딸에게 가겠다고 선언했다. 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 딸이 자기 앞에 무릎을 꿇기도 전에 얼른 일으켜 세워, 보는 이 모두를 감동시킬 만큼 다정하게 끌어안았다. 그리고 채 십오 분도 지나지 않아, 마치 제 손으로 두 사람의 손을 맞잡아 준 것처럼 딸과 사위 모두와 깨끗이 화해해 버렸다.\n\n이런 상황일 때 올워디 씨와 그 일행이 도착해 밀러 부인의 행복을 완성해 주었다. 부인은 소피아를 보자마자 그동안 일어난 모든 일을 짐작했다. 존스를 향한 그녀의 우정은 어찌나 깊었던지, 제 딸의 행복에서 느끼던 환희에 적지 않은 기쁨이 더해졌다.\n\n내가 믿기로, 여러 사람이 한자리에 모여 저마다 이 일행만큼 완벽하게 행복했던 예는 그리 많지 않았을 것이다. 그 가운데 젊은 나이팅게일의 아버지가 가장 덜 완전한 만족을 누렸다. 아들을 향한 애정에도, 올워디의 권위와 논리에도, 앞서 말한 다른 동기까지 보태졌음에도, 그는 아들의 선택에 그토록 온전히 만족할 수는 없었던 것이다. 게다가 소피아가 그 자리에 있다는 사실 자체가 그의 근심을 조금쯤 키우고 돋우는 데 한몫했을지도 모른다. 아들이 저런 아가씨를, 혹은 그와 비슷한 어떤 아가씨를 아내로 맞을 수도 있었으련만 하는 생각이 이따금 고개를 들었기 때문이다. 소피아의 외모나 마음을 꾸미는 그 어떤 매력이 그를 불편하게 한 것은 아니었다. 그의 마음을 갈망으로 들끓게 한 것은 그녀 아버지의 금고에 담긴 내용물이었다. 아들이 밀러 부인의 딸을 위해 희생해 버린 것이 바로 그 매력이라고 생각하니, 그는 도무지 견딜 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n두 신부 모두 무척 예쁜 여인들이었다. 그러나 소피아의 아름다움에 너무도 완전히 빛을 잃어버린 탓에, 만약 이들이 세상에서 가장 마음씨 고운 처녀들 축에 들지 않았더라면 그들의 가슴속에 적잖은 질투가 일었을 법했다. 두 신랑 가운데 누구도 소피아에게서 오래 눈을 떼지 못했으니 말이다. 소피아는 신하의 경배를 받는 여왕처럼, 아니 차라리 주위 모두에게서 흠모를 받는 더 높은 존재처럼 식탁에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그것은 그들이 자진해 바치는 흠모였지, 그녀가 요구한 흠모가 아니었다. 그녀는 다른 모든 완벽함만큼이나 그 겸손과 상냥함으로도 돋보였기 때문이다.\n\n저녁 시간은 참된 흥겨움 속에서 흘러갔다. 모두가 행복했으나, 그 가운데서도 전에 가장 불행했던 이들이 가장 행복했다. 지난날의 고통과 두려움은 그들의 행복에 그런 깊은 맛을 더해 주었으니, 사랑과 행운이 아무리 가득 흘러넘친들 그런 대비의 도움 없이는 결코 줄 수 없는 맛이었다. 그러나 큰 기쁨이란, 특히 처지가 갑작스레 바뀌고 뒤집힌 뒤에는 으레 말이 없어지고, 혀보다는 가슴속에 머무는 법이라, 존스와 소피아는 모인 사람들 가운데 가장 덜 들떠 보였다. 웨스턴은 이를 몹시 답답해하며 두 사람에게 자주 외쳤다. \"이놈아, 왜 말이 없느냐? 어찌 그리 심각한 얼굴이냐? 얘야, 혀라도 잃어버렸느냐? 포도주 한 잔 더 들이켜라. 한 잔 더 마셔야지.\" 그리고 딸의 흥을 더 돋우려고 이따금 흥겨운 노래를 불렀는데, 결혼과 처녀성을 잃는 일에 얼마쯤 관련된 노래였다. 아니, 그는 그 주제를 어찌나 멀리 끌고 갔던지, 올워디 씨가 때로는 눈짓으로, 또 한두 번은 \"이런! 웨스턴 씨!\" 하고 제지하지 않았더라면 딸을 방에서 쫓아낼 뻔했다. 실제로 그는 한번은 그 문제를 따지고 들며, 제 딸에게 제 마음대로 말할 권리가 있노라 주장하기 시작했다. 그러나 아무도 그를 거들지 않았으므로, 그는 이내 잠잠해졌다.\n\n이런 자그마한 제지에도, 그는 일행의 쾌활함과 좋은 기분에 어찌나 흡족했던지 이튿날 자기 숙소에서 다시 모이자고 고집했다. 모두가 그렇게 했다. 그리고 이제 남몰래 신부가 된 사랑스러운 소피아가 의식의 안주인 노릇을, 점잖은 표현을 빌리자면 식탁의 접대를 맡았다. 그날 아침 그녀는 닥터스 커먼스의 예배당에서 존스에게 손을 내주었는데, 그 자리에 함께한 이는 올워디 씨와 웨스턴 씨, 그리고 밀러 부인뿐이었다.\n\n소피아는 그날 아버지와 함께 식사하기로 한 다른 일행에게는 자기 결혼을 알리지 말아 달라고 아버지에게 간곡히 청했다. 밀러 부인에게도 같은 비밀을 당부했고, 올워디 쪽은 존스가 맡았다. 이 덕분에, 제 뜻과는 크게 어긋났음에도 아버지의 바람을 좇아 어쩔 수 없이 나가야 했던 그 공개적인 잔치에 소피아의 까다로운 마음도 어느 정도 누그러졌다. 비밀이 지켜지리라는 믿음에 그녀는 하루를 그런대로 잘 버텼다. 그러나 이제 두 번째 병으로 넘어간 지주가 더는 기쁨을 누르지 못하고 잔을 가득 채워 신부의 건강을 위해 건배를 들고 말았다. 자리에 있던 모두가 곧장 그 건배에 응했고, 가엾게도 얼굴을 붉힌 우리 소피아는 크게 당황했으며, 존스도 그녀 때문에 크게 마음을 졸였다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이 폭로로 새로 알게 된 사람은 한 명도 없었다. 밀러 부인이 딸에게 귀띔했고, 딸은 남편에게, 남편은 누이에게, 누이는 나머지 모두에게 이미 전했기 때문이다.\n\n소피아는 이제 부인들과 함께 자리를 뜰 첫 기회를 잡았고, 지주는 술잔을 앞에 두고 눌러앉았다. 그러는 동안 일행은 하나둘 그를 떠났고, 끝까지 남은 이는 웨스턴 못지않게 술병을 사랑하는 젊은 나이팅게일의 숙부뿐이었다. 그리하여 이 두 사람은 저녁 내내 굳건히 술잔을 기울였으니, 매혹적인 소피아가 황홀해진 존스의 애타는 두 팔에 안기는 그 행복한 순간이 한참 지난 뒤까지 그러했다.\n\n이리하여 독자여, 우리는 마침내 우리 이야기를 결말에 이르게 했다. 어쩌면 그대의 기대와는 어긋날지 모르나, 우리로서는 더없이 기쁘게도, 그 결말에서 존스 씨는 온 인류 가운데 가장 행복한 사람으로 나타난다. 소피아 같은 여인을 차지하는 것에 견줄 만한 행복을 이 세상이 베푸는지, 나는 솔직히 고백하건대 아직껏 발견하지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n이 이야기에서 어느 정도 비중 있는 인물로 등장한 다른 이들에 대해서는, 그들에 관해 조금 더 알고 싶어 하는 분들이 있을지 모르니, 되도록 짧은 말로 그 궁금증을 풀어 드리고자 한다.\n\n올워디는 아직껏 블리필을 다시 만나 보라는 설득에 응하지 않았으나, 소피아의 지지를 등에 업은 존스의 끈질긴 간청에는 못 이겨 그에게 연 200파운드를 주기로 정했다. 거기에 존스는 남몰래 그 삼분의 일을 더 보탰다. 이 수입으로 그는 런던에서 약 200마일 떨어진 북부 어느 주에서 살며, 그 가운데 연 200파운드를 모아 두는데, 이는 이웃 자치구에서 다음 의회의 의석을 사들이려는 것으로, 그곳 변호사와 이미 흥정을 마쳐 두었다. 또 그는 최근 메소디스트로 개종했는데, 그 종파에 속한, 영지가 그 지방에 있는 어느 무척 부유한 과부와 결혼하려는 속셈에서였다.\n\n스퀘어는 앞서 말한 편지를 쓴 직후에 세상을 떠났다. 스왜컴으로 말하자면, 그는 여전히 자기 교구 목사 자리를 지키고 있다. 그는 올워디의 신임을 되찾거나 존스의 환심을 사려고 여러 번 헛된 시도를 했으니, 두 사람 모두에게 면전에서는 아첨하고 등 뒤에서는 욕을 해 댄다. 그러나 그 대신 올워디 씨는 최근 에이브러햄 애덤스 씨를 자기 집에 들였는데, 소피아는 그를 더없이 좋아하게 되어, 제 아이들의 교육을 그에게 맡기겠노라 선언한다.\n\n피츠패트릭 부인은 남편과 갈라서서 얼마 남지 않은 재산의 잔여분을 지니고 있다. 그녀는 도시의 점잖은 구역에서 평판을 누리며 사는데, 어찌나 살림 솜씨가 좋은지 빚 한 푼 지지 않으면서도 제 재산 수입의 세 배를 써 댄다. 그녀는 아일랜드 귀족의 부인과 더없이 친밀한 관계를 유지하며, 그 부인에게 우정을 베푸는 행위로써 제 남편에게 진 온갖 빚을 갚는다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 이내 조카딸 소피아와 화해하여, 시골에서 함께 두 달을 보냈다. 벨라스턴 부인은 소피아가 도시로 돌아오자 그녀를 격식 차려 방문했는데, 그 자리에서 존스에게는 생판 낯선 사람을 대하듯 굴며, 더없이 예의 바르게 그의 결혼을 축하했다.\n\n나이팅게일 씨는 아들을 위해 존스의 이웃에 영지를 사들였고, 그곳에서 젊은 신사와 그의 부인, 밀러 부인, 그리고 그 어린 딸이 함께 산다. 두 집안 사이에는 더없이 흐뭇한 왕래가 이어지고 있다.\n\n좀 더 미천한 이들로 말하자면, 워터스 부인은 시골로 돌아갔는데, 올워디 씨에게서 연 60파운드의 연금을 받게 되었고, 서플 목사와 결혼했다. 소피아의 청에 따라 웨스턴은 이 목사에게 제법 괜찮은 성직록을 베풀어 주었다.\n\n블랙 조지는 모든 일이 들통난 것을 알고 달아나, 그 뒤로는 소식을 들은 이가 없다. 존스는 그 돈을 그의 가족에게 나누어 주었으나 똑같이 나누지는 않았으니, 몰리가 단연 가장 많은 몫을 차지했다.\n\n파트리지로 말하자면, 존스는 그에게 연 50파운드를 정해 주었다. 그는 다시 학교를 열었는데, 예전보다 훨씬 나은 호응을 얻고 있다. 그리고 지금 그와 몰리 시그림 양 사이에 혼담이 오가고 있는데, 소피아의 중재로 성사될 듯하다.\n\n이제 우리는 존스 씨와 소피아에게 작별을 고하고자 한다. 두 사람은 결혼한 지 이틀 만에 웨스턴 씨와 올워디 씨를 모시고 시골로 내려갔다. 웨스턴은 집안의 본가와 영지의 대부분을 사위에게 물려주고, 사냥하기에 더 좋은, 그 고장 다른 곳에 있는 자기의 작은 집으로 물러났다. 실로 그는 존스 씨를 자주 찾아오는데, 존스는 딸과 마찬가지로 그를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 할 수 있는 모든 일을 다 하는 데서 더없는 즐거움을 느낀다. 그리고 이 두 사람의 정성은 어찌나 잘 통했던지, 노신사는 평생 이때만큼 행복한 적이 없었노라 선언한다. 그는 이곳에 거실 하나와 곁방 하나를 따로 두고, 마음에 드는 이 누구와든 그곳에서 술을 마신다. 또 딸은 예전과 다름없이 그가 바랄 때면 언제든 연주를 해 주는데, 존스가 그녀에게 이렇게 일러두었기 때문이다. 그녀를 기쁘게 하는 일 다음으로 자신의 가장 큰 만족 가운데 하나가 노인의 행복에 보탬이 되는 일이니, 그녀가 아버지에게 드러내고 또 다하는 그 갸륵한 효성이야말로, 자신에게 쏟는 사랑에 거의 맞먹을 만큼 그녀를 노인에게 소중한 사람으로 만들어 준다고.\n\n소피아는 벌써 그에게 잘생긴 아이 둘, 사내아이와 계집아이를 안겨 주었는데, 노신사는 이 아이들을 어찌나 사랑하는지 많은 시간을 아기방에서 보낸다. 그곳에서 그는, 한 살 반이 넘은 어린 손녀가 옹알이하는 소리야말로 잉글랜드에서 가장 훌륭한 사냥개들이 짖는 소리보다 더 감미로운 음악이라고 선언한다.\n\n올워디 역시 결혼에 즈음하여 존스에게 무척 후하게 베풀었으며, 그와 그의 부인에게 애정을 보일 기회를 하나도 빠뜨리지 않았으니, 두 사람은 그를 아버지처럼 사랑한다. 존스의 천성 가운데 악덕으로 기울 만한 점이 무엇이든, 이 선량한 분과 끊임없이 나눈 대화로, 그리고 사랑스럽고 정숙한 소피아와의 결합으로 바로잡혔다. 또한 그는 지난날의 어리석음을 돌이켜 봄으로써, 그처럼 활달한 기질을 지닌 사람에게서는 좀처럼 보기 드문 분별과 신중함을 갖추게 되었다.\n\n끝으로, 이 다정한 부부보다 더 훌륭한 남자와 여자를 찾아볼 수 없듯, 이들보다 더 행복한 이들 또한 상상할 수 없다. 그들은 서로를 향해 더없이 순수하고 다정한 애정을 간직하고 있으니, 그 애정은 서로를 아끼고 서로를 존중하는 가운데 날마다 자라고 굳어 간다. 친척과 벗을 대하는 그들의 태도 또한 서로를 대하는 태도 못지않게 사랑스럽다. 그리고 아랫사람들을 향한 그들의 겸손함과 너그러움, 그리고 자비로움이 어찌나 큰지, 이웃이든 소작인이든 하인이든, 존스 씨가 그의 소피아와 결혼한 그날을 더없이 감사하는 마음으로 축복하지 않는 이가 하나도 없다.\n끝.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-6593"]=[[["Sir,\nNotwithstanding your constant refusal, when I have asked leave to prefix your name to this dedication, I must still insist on my right to desire your protection of this work.", "이 헌사 앞머리에 귀하의 이름을 올려도 좋겠느냐고 청할 때마다 한결같이 거절하셨음에도, 저는 이 작품에 대한 귀하의 보호를 바랄 권리만큼은 여전히 굽히지 않겠습니다."]], [["To you, Sir, it is owing that this history was ever begun.", "경이시여, 이 이야기가 시작될 수 있었던 것 자체가 귀하 덕분입니다."], ["It was by your desire that I first thought of such a composition.", "제가 이런 작품을 처음 떠올린 것도 귀하의 권유 때문이었습니다."], ["So many years have since past, that you may have, perhaps, forgotten this circumstance: but your desires are to me in the nature of commands; and the impression of them is never to be erased from my memory.", "그 후로 어찌나 여러 해가 흘렀는지, 어쩌면 귀하께서는 이 일을 잊으셨을지도 모르겠습니다. 그러나 귀하의 바람은 제게 곧 명령과도 같은 것이어서, 그 자취는 제 기억에서 결코 지워지지 않을 것입니다."]], [["Again, Sir, without your assistance this history had never been completed.", "또한, 경이시여, 귀하의 도움이 없었다면 이 이야기는 결코 완성되지 못했을 것입니다. 이 단언에 놀라지는 마십시오."], ["Be not startled at the assertion.", "귀하를 두고 연애소설 작가라는 의심을 사게 할 뜻은 없습니다."], ["I do not intend to draw on you the suspicion of being a romance writer.", "제가 말하려는 것은 다만, 이 작품을 쓰는 데 들인 그 오랜 세월의 상당 부분 동안 제가 살아갈 수 있었던 것이 어느 정도 귀하 덕분이라는 사실입니다. 이것 또한 귀하께 일깨워 드릴 필요가 있을지 모르겠습니다."], ["I mean no more than that I partly owe to you my existence during great part of the time which I have employed in composing it: another matter which it may be necessary to remind you of; since there are certain actions of which you are apt to be extremely forgetful; but of these I hope I shall always have a better memory than yourself.", "귀하께서는 어떤 행위들에 대해서는 유난히 잘 잊으시는 경향이 있으시니 말입니다. 다만 그런 일들에 대해서만큼은, 제가 늘 귀하보다 더 또렷한 기억을 간직하기를 바랍니다."]], [["Lastly, It is owing to you that the history appears what it now is.", "끝으로, 이 이야기가 지금과 같은 모습으로 나올 수 있었던 것도 귀하 덕분입니다."], ["If there be in this work, as some have been pleased to say, a stronger picture of a truly benevolent mind than is to be found in any other, who that knows you, and a particular acquaintance of yours, will doubt whence that benevolence hath been copied?", "어떤 분들이 기꺼이 말씀해 주셨듯, 만일 이 작품 안에 다른 어떤 작품에서도 찾아보기 힘들 만큼 진정으로 자애로운 마음의 더 강렬한 그림이 담겨 있다면, 귀하를, 그리고 귀하와 가까운 어떤 분을 아는 이라면 그 자애로움이 어디서 본떠 온 것인지 누가 의심하겠습니까?"], ["The world will not, I believe, make me the compliment of thinking I took it from myself.", "세상이 그것을 제 자신에게서 가져왔다고 여겨 주는 칭찬을 제게 베풀지는 않으리라 믿습니다."], ["I care not: this they shall own, that the two persons from whom I have taken it, that is to say, two of the best and worthiest men in the world, are strongly and zealously my friends.", "저는 개의치 않습니다. 다만 세상이 인정하게 될 한 가지는, 제가 그것을 본떠 온 두 분, 다시 말해 이 세상에서 가장 훌륭하고 가치 있는 사람 가운데 두 분이 강렬하고 열렬하게 저의 벗이라는 사실입니다."], ["I might be contented with this, and yet my vanity will add a third to the number; and him one of the greatest and noblest, not only in his rank, but in every public and private virtue.", "저는 이것으로 만족할 수도 있겠지만, 그래도 저의 허영심이 그 수에 한 분을 더 보태려 합니다. 그분은 지위에서뿐 아니라 모든 공적·사적 덕에서도 가장 위대하고 고귀한 분 가운데 한 분이십니다."], ["But here, whilst my gratitude for the princely benefactions of the Duke of Bedford bursts from my heart, you must forgive my reminding you that it was you who first recommended me to the notice of my benefactor.", "그러나 베드퍼드 공작의 왕후 같은 후의에 대한 제 감사가 가슴에서 터져 나오는 이 자리에서, 제가 처음 그 은인의 눈에 띄도록 천거해 주신 분이 바로 귀하였음을 일깨워 드리는 것을 부디 용서해 주십시오."]], [["And what are your objections to the allowance of the honour which I have sollicited?", "그렇다면 제가 간청해 온 이 영예를 허락하지 못하시겠다는 귀하의 반대 이유는 무엇입니까?"], ["Why, you have commended the book so warmly, that you should be ashamed of reading your name before the dedication.", "글쎄요, 귀하께서 이 책을 어찌나 따뜻하게 칭찬하셨던지, 헌사 앞에 놓인 자신의 이름을 읽기가 부끄러우시다는 것입니다."], ["Indeed, sir, if the book itself doth not make you ashamed of your commendations, nothing that I can here write will, or ought.", "참으로, 경이시여, 책 그 자체가 귀하로 하여금 그 칭찬을 부끄러워하게 만들지 못한다면, 제가 여기에 무엇을 쓴들 그렇게 만들 수 없을 것이며, 또 그래서도 안 될 것입니다. 귀하께서 제 책을 칭찬하셨다 하여, 제가 귀하의 보호와 후원을 받을 권리를 포기할 수는 없습니다."], ["I am not to give up my right to your protection and patronage, because you have commended my book: for though I acknowledge so many obligations to you, I do not add this to the number; in which friendship, I am convinced, hath so little share: since that can neither biass your judgment, nor pervert your integrity.", "저는 귀하께 그토록 많은 신세를 졌음을 인정하지만, 이 칭찬만큼은 그 목록에 넣지 않겠습니다. 거기에는 우정이 끼어든 자리가 거의 없다고 확신하기 때문입니다."], ["An enemy may at any time obtain your commendation by only deserving it; and the utmost which the faults of your friends can hope for, is your silence; or, perhaps, if too severely accused, your gentle palliation.", "우정이란 귀하의 판단을 기울게 하지도, 귀하의 곧음을 비틀지도 못합니다. 적이라도 다만 그럴 자격만 갖춘다면 언제든 귀하의 칭찬을 얻을 수 있고, 벗의 허물이 바랄 수 있는 최대치란 귀하의 침묵, 혹 너무 가혹하게 비난받는 경우라면 어쩌면 귀하의 너그러운 두둔 정도입니다."]], [["In short, sir, I suspect, that your dislike of public praise is your true objection to granting my request.", "요컨대, 경이시여, 저는 귀하께서 공개적인 찬사를 꺼리시는 것이야말로 제 청을 들어주지 않으시는 진짜 이유라고 짐작합니다."], ["I have observed that you have, in common with my two other friends, an unwillingness to hear the least mention of your own virtues; that, as a great poet says of one of you, (he might justly have said it of all three), you", "저는 귀하께서 저의 다른 두 벗과 마찬가지로, 자신의 덕에 대한 한 마디 언급조차 듣기 싫어하심을 보아 왔습니다. 어느 위대한 시인이 귀하 가운데 한 분을 두고 (실은 세 분 모두에게 마땅히 그리 말할 수 있었을 텐데) 노래했듯이, 귀하께서는"]], [["Do good by stealth, and blush to find it fame.", "남몰래 선을 행하고, 그것이 명성이 됨을 부끄러워하시는 분입니다."]], [["If men of this disposition are as careful to shun applause, as others are to escape censure, how just must be your apprehension of your character falling into my hands; since what would not a man have reason to dread, if attacked by an author who had received from him injuries equal to my obligations to you!", "이런 성품의 사람들이 다른 이들이 비난을 피하려 애쓰는 만큼이나 갈채를 멀리하려 조심한다면, 귀하의 인품이 제 손에 들어가는 데 대한 두려움이 얼마나 당연하겠습니까. 제가 귀하께 진 신세만큼이나 큰 해를 그에게서 입은 작가에게 공격당한다면, 사람이 어찌 떨지 않을 까닭이 있겠습니까!"]], [["And will not this dread of censure increase in proportion to the matter which a man is conscious of having afforded for it?", "그리고 이 비난에 대한 두려움은, 사람이 스스로 그 빌미를 제공했음을 의식하는 정도에 비례하여 커지지 않겠습니까?"], ["If his whole life, for instance, should have been one continued subject of satire, he may well tremble when an incensed satirist takes him in hand.", "이를테면 어떤 이의 평생이 풍자의 끊임없는 소재였다면, 격분한 풍자가가 그를 다루기 시작할 때 그가 떨 만도 합니다."], ["Now, sir, if we apply this to your modest aversion to panegyric, how reasonable will your fears of me appear!", "자, 경이시여, 이것을 찬사에 대한 귀하의 겸손한 거부감에 적용해 본다면, 저를 향한 귀하의 두려움이 얼마나 이치에 맞아 보이겠습니까!"]], [["Yet surely you might have gratified my ambition, from this single confidence, that I shall always prefer the indulgence of your inclinations to the satisfaction of my own.", "하지만 분명 귀하께서는, 제가 언제나 제 자신의 만족보다 귀하의 뜻을 받드는 쪽을 택하리라는 이 한 가지 믿음만으로도 저의 야심을 채워 주실 수 있었을 것입니다."], ["A very strong instance of which I shall give you in this address, in which I am determined to follow the example of all other dedicators, and will consider not what my patron really deserves to have written, but what he will be best pleased to read.", "그 강력한 본보기를 저는 이 헌사에서 보여 드리려 합니다. 여기서 저는 다른 모든 헌사 작가의 본을 따르기로 마음먹었으니, 저의 후원자가 정말로 쓰일 자격이 있는 것이 무엇인지가 아니라, 그가 읽어서 가장 흡족할 만한 것이 무엇인지를 헤아릴 것입니다."]], [["Without further preface then, I here present you with the labours of some years of my life.", "그러니 더 이상의 서론 없이, 저는 여기 제 인생의 여러 해에 걸친 노고를 귀하께 바칩니다."], ["What merit these labours have is already known to yourself.", "이 노고에 어떤 가치가 있는지는 이미 귀하께서 알고 계십니다."], ["If, from your favourable judgment, I have conceived some esteem for them, it cannot be imputed to vanity; since I should have agreed as implicitly to your opinion, had it been given in favour of any other man's production.", "만일 귀하의 호의 어린 판단으로부터 제가 그 노고에 대해 어떤 자긍심을 품게 되었다 해도, 그것을 허영으로 돌릴 수는 없습니다. 귀하의 의견이 다른 어떤 사람의 작품을 두둔하는 쪽으로 내려졌더라도, 저는 그에 똑같이 고스란히 동의했을 것이기 때문입니다."], ["Negatively, at least, I may be allowed to say, that had I been sensible of any great demerit in the work, you are the last person to whose protection I would have ventured to recommend it.", "적어도 부정의 방식으로는 이렇게 말해도 좋을 듯합니다. 만일 제가 이 작품에 큰 흠이 있음을 느꼈다면, 귀하야말로 그 보호를 감히 청하려 할 마지막 사람이었으리라고요."]], [["From the name of my patron, indeed, I hope my reader will be convinced, at his very entrance on this work, that he will find in the whole course of it nothing prejudicial to the cause of religion and virtue, nothing inconsistent with the strictest rules of decency, nor which can offend even the chastest eye in the perusal.", "실로 저의 후원자의 이름만 보아도, 독자는 이 작품에 첫발을 들이는 바로 그 순간 확신하게 되리라 바랍니다. 그 전체 흐름 어디에서도 종교와 덕의 대의에 해로운 것, 가장 엄격한 예법의 규칙에 어긋나는 것, 읽는 동안 가장 순결한 눈조차 불쾌하게 할 만한 것은 찾지 못하리라고 말입니다."], ["On the contrary, I declare, that to recommend goodness and innocence hath been my sincere endeavour in this history.", "오히려 저는 선함과 순결함을 권하는 것이 이 이야기에서 제가 기울인 진실한 노력이었음을 분명히 밝힙니다. 귀하께서는 제가 이 정직한 목적을 이루었다고 기꺼이 여겨 주셨습니다."], ["This honest purpose you have been pleased to think I have attained: and to say the truth, it is likeliest to be attained in books of this kind; for an example is a kind of picture, in which virtue becomes, as it were, an object of sight, and strikes us with an idea of that loveliness, which Plato asserts there is in her naked charms.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이러한 종류의 책에서야말로 그 목적이 가장 이루어지기 쉽습니다. 본보기란 일종의 그림과 같아서, 그 안에서 덕은 말하자면 눈에 보이는 대상이 되며, 플라톤이 덕의 벌거벗은 매력 속에 있다고 단언한 그 사랑스러움의 관념으로 우리를 사로잡기 때문입니다."]], [["Besides displaying that beauty of virtue which may attract the admiration of mankind, I have attempted to engage a stronger motive to human action in her favour, by convincing men, that their true interest directs them to a pursuit of her. For this purpose I have shown that no acquisitions of guilt can compensate the loss of that solid inward comfort of mind, which is the sure companion of innocence and virtue; nor can in the least balance the evil of that horror and anxiety which, in their room, guilt introduces into our bosoms. And again, that as these acquisitions are in themselves generally worthless, so are the means to attain them not only base and infamous, but at best incertain, and always full of danger. Lastly, I have endeavoured strongly to inculcate, that virtue and innocence can scarce ever be injured but by indiscretion; and that it is this alone which often betrays them into the snares that deceit and villainy spread for them. A moral which I have the more industriously laboured, as the teaching it is, of all others, the likeliest to be attended with success; since, I believe, it is much easier to make good men wise, than to make bad men good.", "인류의 찬탄을 끌어낼 만한 덕의 아름다움을 보여 주는 것에 더하여, 저는 사람들에게 그들의 진정한 이익이 덕을 좇으라 이끈다는 것을 납득시킴으로써, 덕을 위한 인간 행동의 더 강력한 동기를 끌어내려 했습니다. 이를 위해 저는, 죄로 얻는 어떤 소득도 순결함과 덕에 늘 따라붙는 동반자인 그 단단한 내면의 평안을 잃은 것을 보상할 수 없으며, 죄가 그 자리에 우리 가슴속으로 들여놓는 공포와 불안의 해악과는 조금도 견줄 수 없음을 보여 주었습니다. 또한 그런 소득은 그 자체로 대개 무가치할 뿐 아니라, 그것을 얻는 수단 또한 비천하고 수치스러우며, 잘해야 불확실하고 언제나 위험으로 가득함을 보여 주었습니다. 끝으로 저는, 덕과 순결함은 경솔함이 아니고서는 거의 해를 입지 않으며, 기만과 악행이 그들을 노리고 쳐 놓은 덫으로 그들을 빠뜨리는 것도 흔히 바로 이 경솔함뿐이라는 점을 강하게 새기려 애썼습니다. 제가 이 교훈에 더욱 부지런히 공을 들인 까닭은, 그것을 가르치는 일이 다른 무엇보다 성공이 따르기 쉬운 일이기 때문입니다. 제 믿음으로는, 나쁜 사람을 선하게 만드는 것보다 선한 사람을 지혜롭게 만드는 편이 훨씬 쉬우니까요. 이러한 목적을 위해 저는 다음의 이야기에 제가 다룰 수 있는 온갖 재치와 해학을 쏟아부었습니다. 그 안에서 저는 인류가 아끼는 어리석음과 악덕을 웃음으로 떨쳐 내게 하려 애썼습니다. 이 갸륵한 시도에서 제가 얼마나 성공했는지는 공정한 독자의 판단에 맡기겠으며, 다만 두 가지만 청하고자 합니다. 첫째, 이 작품에서 완벽함을 기대하지 말아 주십시오. 둘째, 어떤 대목들이, 다른 대목들에서는 부디 드러나기를 바라는 그 작은 미덕에조차 미치지 못하더라도 너그러이 보아 주십시오."]], [["For these purposes I have employed all the wit and humour of which I am master in the following history; wherein I have endeavoured to laugh mankind out of their favourite follies and vices.", "경이시여, 더는 붙잡아 두지 않겠습니다. 실로 저는 헌사를 쓴다 공언해 놓고 어느새 서문으로 빠져들고 말았습니다."], ["How far I have succeeded in this good attempt, I shall submit to the candid reader, with only two requests: First, that he will not expect to find perfection in this work; and Secondly, that he will excuse some parts of it, if they fall short of that little merit which I hope may appear in others.", "하지만 달리 어쩌겠습니까? 저는 감히 귀하를 칭송하지 못하니, 귀하가 제 생각 속에 계실 때 그것을 피할 유일한 방법이란, 아예 입을 다물거나 아니면 제 생각을 다른 주제로 돌리는 것뿐임을 압니다."]], [["I will detain you, sir, no longer.", "그러니 이 서한에서 제가 한 말을, 귀하의 동의 없이 했을 뿐 아니라 명백히 그 뜻을 거슬러 한 것이니, 부디 용서해 주십시오."], ["Indeed I have run into a preface, while I professed to write a dedication. But how can it be otherwise?", "그리고 적어도 이렇게 공개적인 방식으로, 제가 더없는 존경과 감사를 담아 다음과 같은 사람임을 밝히는 것만은 허락해 주십시오."], ["I dare not praise you; and the only means I know of to avoid it, when you are in my thoughts, are either to be entirely silent, or to turn my thoughts to some other subject.", "경이시여,"]], [["Pardon, therefore, what I have said in this epistle, not only without your consent, but absolutely against it; and give me at least leave, in this public manner, to declare that I am, with the highest respect and gratitude,--", "귀하께 더없이 큰 신세를 진,\n순종하는 미천한 종,"]], [["Sir,\nYour most obliged,", "헨리 필딩 올림."]], [["Obedient, humble servant,", "업둥이 톰 존스의 이야기."]], [["# BOOK I.", "# BOOK I."]], [["CONTAINING AS MUCH OF THE BIRTH OF THE FOUNDLING AS IS NECESSARY OR PROPER TO ACQUAINT THE READER WITH IN THE BEGINNING OF THIS HISTORY.", "작가란 자기 자신을, 사사로이 혹은 자선 삼아 한 끼를 베푸는 신사가 아니라, 누구든 돈만 내면 환영받는 공중식당을 꾸려 가는 주인으로 여겨야 한다. 앞의 경우라면, 잘 알려진 대로 음식을 베푸는 이가 제 마음에 드는 요리를 마음껏 내놓으며, 설령 그 음식이 형편없고 손님들의 입맛에 도무지 맞지 않더라도 손님들은 흠을 잡아서는 안 된다."], ["Chapter i.", "아니, 오히려 예법이라는 것이 그들로 하여금 앞에 차려진 것이 무엇이든 겉으로는 인정하고 칭찬하도록 강요한다."], ["The introduction to the work, or bill of fare to the feast.", "그런데 식당 주인에게는 정반대의 일이 벌어진다. 먹는 값을 치르는 사람들은, 그 입맛이 제아무리 까다롭고 변덕스럽다 한들, 제 입을 만족시키라고 고집할 것이며, 모든 것이 제 입맛에 맞지 않으면 거리낌 없이 식사를 흠잡고, 욕하고, 저주할 권리를 내세울 것이다."]], [["An author ought to consider himself, not as a gentleman who gives a private or eleemosynary treat, but rather as one who keeps a public ordinary, at which all persons are welcome for their money. In the former case, it is well known that the entertainer provides what fare he pleases; and though this should be very indifferent, and utterly disagreeable to the taste of his company, they must not find any fault; nay, on the contrary, good breeding forces them outwardly to approve and to commend whatever is set before them. Now the contrary of this happens to the master of an ordinary. Men who pay for what they eat will insist on gratifying their palates, however nice and whimsical these may prove; and if everything is not agreeable to their taste, will challenge a right to censure, to abuse, and to d--n their dinner without controul. To prevent, therefore, giving offence to their customers by any such disappointment, it hath been usual with the honest and well-meaning host to provide a bill of fare which all persons may peruse at their first entrance into the house; and having thence acquainted themselves with the entertainment which they may expect, may either stay and regale with what is provided for them, or may depart to some other ordinary better accommodated to their taste.", "그러므로 이런 실망으로 손님의 기분을 상하게 하는 일을 막기 위해, 정직하고 선의를 품은 주인이라면 으레 차림표를 마련해 두는 법이다. 누구든 가게에 들어서는 순간 그것을 죽 훑어보고, 자신이 기대할 수 있는 음식을 미리 알아챈 다음, 머물러 차려진 음식을 즐기거나, 아니면 제 입맛에 더 잘 맞는 다른 식당으로 떠나면 되는 것이다."]], [["As we do not disdain to borrow wit or wisdom from any man who is capable of lending us either, we have condescended to take a hint from these honest victuallers, and shall prefix not only a general bill of fare to our whole entertainment, but shall likewise give the reader particular bills to every course which is to be served up in this and the ensuing volumes.", "우리는 재치든 지혜든, 그것을 빌려줄 능력이 있는 사람에게서라면 누구에게든 빌리기를 부끄러워하지 않으므로, 이 정직한 음식 장수들에게서 한 가지 착상을 기꺼이 받아들이기로 했다. 그리하여 우리의 향연 전체에 대한 총괄 차림표를 앞머리에 내걸 뿐 아니라, 이 책과 뒤이은 권들에서 차려 낼 코스마다 독자에게 따로따로 차림표를 마련해 드리고자 한다."]], [["The provision, then, which we have here made is no other than Human Nature.", "그렇다면 우리가 여기 마련한 음식은 다름 아닌 인간 본성이다."], ["Nor do I fear that my sensible reader, though most luxurious in his taste, will start, cavil, or be offended, because I have named but one article.", "비록 내가 단 한 가지 품목만을 이름 지어 내놓았다 해서, 입맛이 더없이 호사스러운 분별 있는 독자라 한들 놀라거나, 트집을 잡거나, 기분이 상하지는 않으리라 믿는다. 거북은—먹는 일에 두루 통달한 브리스틀의 시참사회원이 풍부한 경험으로 아는 바와 같이—그 맛깔진 등딱지 살과 배딱지 살 말고도 여러 가지 다른 먹을거리를 품고 있다."], ["The tortoise--as the alderman of Bristol, well learned in eating, knows by much experience--besides the delicious calipash and calipee, contains many different kinds of food; nor can the learned reader be ignorant, that in human nature, though here collected under one general name, is such prodigious variety, that a cook will have sooner gone through all the several species of animal and vegetable food in the world, than an author will be able to exhaust so extensive a subject.", "또한 학식 있는 독자라면 모르지 않을 것이다. 인간 본성이란, 여기서는 하나의 두루뭉술한 이름 아래 모아 두었으나, 그 안에 어찌나 엄청난 다양함이 들어 있는지, 요리사가 세상의 온갖 동물성·식물성 먹을거리를 죄다 거쳐 가는 것이, 작가가 이토록 광대한 주제를 다 길어 올리는 것보다 차라리 빠르다는 것을."]], [["An objection may perhaps be apprehended from the more delicate, that this dish is too common and vulgar; for what else is the subject of all the romances, novels, plays, and poems, with which the stalls abound?", "어쩌면 더 까다로운 분들에게서, 이 요리는 너무 흔하고 천하다는 이의가 나올지도 모르겠다. 좌판마다 넘쳐 나는 그 숱한 로맨스며 소설, 희곡, 시들이 다루는 것이 이것 말고 또 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["Many exquisite viands might be rejected by the epicure, if it was a sufficient cause for his contemning of them as common and vulgar, that something was to be found in the most paltry alleys under the same name.", "미식가가 어떤 진귀한 음식을 두고, 가장 누추한 골목에서도 같은 이름의 무언가를 찾아볼 수 있다는 이유만으로 그것을 천하다 업신여겨도 좋다면, 수많은 절묘한 요리들이 퇴짜를 맞을 것이다."], ["In reality, true nature is as difficult to be met with in authors, as the Bayonne ham, or Bologna sausage, is to be found in the shops.", "사실, 작가들에게서 진짜 본성을 만나기란, 가게에서 바욘 햄이나 볼로냐 소시지를 찾기만큼이나 어려운 일이다."]], [["But the whole, to continue the same metaphor, consists in the cookery of the author; for, as Mr Pope tells us--", "그러나 같은 비유를 이어 가자면, 모든 것은 작가의 요리 솜씨에 달려 있다. 포프 씨가 우리에게 일러 주듯이—"]], [["“True wit is nature to advantage drest; What oft was thought, but ne'er so well exprest.”", "\"참된 재치란 본성을 한껏 돋보이게 차려 낸 것, 누구나 늘 생각했으되 이토록 잘 표현된 적은 없던 것.\""]], [["The same animal which hath the honour to have some part of his flesh eaten at the table of a duke, may perhaps be degraded in another part, and some of his limbs gibbeted, as it were, in the vilest stall in town.", "공작의 식탁에서 그 살의 어느 부위가 먹히는 영예를 누리는 바로 그 짐승이, 다른 부위에서는 격이 떨어져, 그 사지의 일부가 마치 효수당하듯 읍내에서 가장 천한 좌판에 내걸릴 수도 있다."], ["Where, then, lies the difference between the food of the nobleman and the porter, if both are at dinner on the same ox or calf, but in the seasoning, the dressing, the garnishing, and the setting forth?", "그렇다면 귀족의 음식과 짐꾼의 음식 사이에 무슨 차이가 있겠는가. 둘 다 같은 소나 송아지로 저녁을 든다면, 그 차이란 간을 맞추고, 손질하고, 곁들임을 더하고, 차려 내는 솜씨에 있을 뿐이다."], ["Hence the one provokes and incites the most languid appetite, and the other turns and palls that which is the sharpest and keenest.", "그리하여 한쪽은 가장 시들한 식욕마저 자극해 돋우는데, 다른 한쪽은 가장 날카롭고 예리한 식욕마저 돌려세우고 김빠지게 만든다."]], [["In like manner, the excellence of the mental entertainment consists less in the subject than in the author's skill in well dressing it up.", "이와 마찬가지로, 정신의 향연이 지닌 빼어남도 주제 자체보다는 그것을 잘 차려 내는 작가의 솜씨에 달려 있다."], ["How pleased, therefore, will the reader be to find that we have, in the following work, adhered closely to one of the highest principles of the best cook which the present age, or perhaps that of Heliogabalus, hath produced.", "그러니 독자께서는, 다음의 작품에서 우리가 이 시대—어쩌면 헬리오가발루스의 시대까지 통틀어—가 배출한 최고 요리사의 가장 드높은 원칙 하나를 충실히 따랐음을 알고 얼마나 흐뭇해하실 것인가."], ["This great man, as is well known to all lovers of polite eating, begins at first by setting plain things before his hungry guests, rising afterwards by degrees as their stomachs may be supposed to decrease, to the very quintessence of sauce and spices.", "이 위대한 인물은, 우아한 미식을 사랑하는 이라면 누구나 잘 알듯이, 처음에는 굶주린 손님들 앞에 담백한 음식부터 내놓고, 그 뒤로 손님들의 위장이 차츰 줄어들리라 짐작되는 만큼, 소스와 향신료의 정수에 이르기까지 단계를 밟아 올라간다."], ["In like manner, we shall represent human nature at first to the keen appetite of our reader, in that more plain and simple manner in which it is found in the country, and shall hereafter hash and ragoo it with all the high French and Italian seasoning of affectation and vice which courts and cities afford.", "우리도 이와 같이, 처음에는 우리 독자의 날카로운 식욕에 시골에서 볼 수 있는 그 한결 꾸밈없고 소박한 모습으로 인간 본성을 내놓고, 그다음에는 궁정과 도시가 제공하는 온갖 거창한 프랑스풍·이탈리아풍 양념인 허식과 악덕을 곁들여 그것을 잘게 다지고 졸여 낼 것이다."], ["By these means, we doubt not but our reader may be rendered desirous to read on for ever, as the great person just above-mentioned is supposed to have made some persons eat.", "이런 방법으로, 방금 말한 그 위대한 인물이 어떤 이들에게 끝없이 먹게 만들었다 전해지듯, 우리 독자께서도 영영 읽어 나가고 싶어지시리라 믿어 의심치 않는다."]], [["Having premised thus much, we will now detain those who like our bill of fare no longer from their diet, and shall proceed directly to serve up the first course of our history for their entertainment.", "이만큼 미리 일러 두었으니, 우리의 차림표가 마음에 드는 분들을 더는 식사에서 붙잡아 두지 않겠다. 이제 곧장 우리 이야기의 첫 번째 코스를 그분들의 즐거움을 위해 차려 내는 일로 나아가겠다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["A short description of squire Allworthy, and a fuller account of Miss Bridget Allworthy, his sister.", "올워디 영주에 대한 짧은 소개, 그리고 그의 누이 브리짓 올워디 양에 대한 좀 더 자세한 이야기."]], [["In that part of the western division of this kingdom which is commonly called Somersetshire, there lately lived, and perhaps lives still, a gentleman whose name was Allworthy, and who might well be called the favourite of both nature and fortune; for both of these seem to have contended which should bless and enrich him most. In this contention, nature may seem to some to have come off victorious, as she bestowed on him many gifts, while fortune had only one gift in her power; but in pouring forth this, she was so very profuse, that others perhaps may think this single endowment to have been more than equivalent to all the various blessings which he enjoyed from nature. From the former of these, he derived an agreeable person, a sound constitution, a solid understanding, and a benevolent heart; by the latter, he was decreed to the inheritance of one of the largest estates in the county.", "흔히 서머싯셔라고 불리는, 이 왕국 서부 지역의 어느 한구석에 얼마 전까지 한 신사가 살고 있었다. 어쩌면 지금도 살아 있을지 모른다. 그의 이름은 올워디였으며, 자연과 운명 양쪽 모두의 총애를 받은 사람이라 불러도 좋을 만했다. 이 둘은 누가 그를 더 많이 축복하고 더 풍요롭게 해줄지 서로 겨루기라도 한 듯했기 때문이다. 어떤 이들은 이 다툼에서 자연이 승리했다고 볼지도 모른다. 자연은 그에게 여러 가지 선물을 베푼 반면, 운명은 단 하나의 선물만을 손에 쥐고 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 운명은 그 하나를 쏟아붓는 데 어찌나 아낌이 없었던지, 어쩌면 어떤 이들은 이 단 하나의 선물이 그가 자연으로부터 누린 온갖 축복을 다 합친 것보다 더 값지다고 여길지도 모른다. 자연으로부터 그는 호감 가는 풍채와 튼튼한 체질, 견실한 분별력, 그리고 자애로운 마음을 물려받았다. 운명으로부터는 이 고장에서 손꼽히게 큰 영지 가운데 하나를 상속받도록 점지받았다."]], [["This gentleman had in his youth married a very worthy and beautiful woman, of whom he had been extremely fond: by her he had three children, all of whom died in their infancy. He had likewise had the misfortune of burying this beloved wife herself, about five years before the time in which this history chuses to set out. This loss, however great, he bore like a man of sense and constancy, though it must be confest he would often talk a little whimsically on this head; for he sometimes said he looked on himself as still married, and considered his wife as only gone a little before him, a journey which he should most certainly, sooner or later, take after her; and that he had not the least doubt of meeting her again in a place where he should never part with her more--sentiments for which his sense was arraigned by one part of his neighbours, his religion by a second, and his sincerity by a third.", "이 신사는 젊은 시절에 매우 훌륭하고 아름다운 여인과 혼인했고, 그녀를 끔찍이도 아꼈다. 그녀와의 사이에서 세 아이를 두었으나, 모두 어린 나이에 세상을 떠났다. 게다가 그는 이 이야기가 막을 올리기로 한 때로부터 약 오 년 전에, 사랑하던 아내마저 땅에 묻는 불행을 겪었다. 이 상실은 아무리 컸어도, 그는 분별 있고 한결같은 사람답게 견뎌냈다. 다만 이 대목에서만큼은 그가 종종 좀 별난 소리를 하곤 했음을 인정해야겠다. 그는 이따금 자신이 아직도 혼인한 몸이라 여긴다고 말하면서, 아내는 단지 자기보다 조금 먼저 떠났을 뿐이며, 그 길은 자신도 머지않아 틀림없이 그녀를 따라 떠날 여정이라고 생각한다고 했다. 그리고 다시는 헤어지지 않을 어느 곳에서 아내와 다시 만나리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않는다고도 했다. 이런 생각을 두고 이웃 가운데 일부는 그의 분별력을, 다른 일부는 그의 신앙심을, 또 다른 일부는 그의 진심을 도마 위에 올렸다."]], [["He now lived, for the most part, retired in the country, with one sister, for whom he had a very tender affection.", "그는 이제 대부분의 시간을 시골에 은거하며 지냈는데, 누이 한 사람과 함께였고, 그녀에게 매우 다정한 애정을 품고 있었다."], ["This lady was now somewhat past the age of thirty, an aera at which, in the opinion of the malicious, the title of old maid may with no impropriety be assumed.", "이 부인은 당시 서른을 조금 넘긴 나이였으니, 심술궂은 이들의 눈에는 노처녀라는 호칭을 갖다 붙여도 별 무리가 없을 시기였다."], ["She was of that species of women whom you commend rather for good qualities than beauty, and who are generally called, by their own sex, very good sort of women--as good a sort of woman, madam, as you would wish to know.", "그녀는 아름다움보다는 좋은 품성으로 칭찬받는 부류의 여인이었으며, 같은 여자들 사이에서 흔히 \"참 좋은 사람\"이라고 불리는 그런 여인이었다. \"부인, 알아두면 좋을 만큼 참 좋은 사람이지요\""], ["Indeed, she was so far from regretting want of beauty, that she never mentioned that perfection, if it can be called one, without contempt; and would often thank God she was not as handsome as Miss Such-a-one, whom perhaps beauty had led into errors which she might have otherwise avoided.", "하는 식으로 말이다. 사실 그녀는 미모가 없음을 아쉬워하기는커녕, 그것을 미덕이라 부를 수 있다면 그 미덕을 언급할 때마다 늘 경멸을 담았다."], ["Miss Bridget Allworthy (for that was the name of this lady) very rightly conceived the charms of person in a woman to be no better than snares for herself, as well as for others; and yet so discreet was she in her conduct, that her prudence was as much on the guard as if she had all the snares to apprehend which were ever laid for her whole sex.", "그리고 자신이 아무개 양만큼 곱지 않은 것을 자주 하느님께 감사하곤 했는데, 아무개 양은 어쩌면 그 미모 탓에, 미모만 없었다면 피했을 잘못의 길로 빠져들었을지도 모른다는 것이었다. 브리짓 올워디 양은—이 부인의 이름이 바로 그러했다—여인의 외모가 지닌 매력이란 남에게나 자신에게나 한낱 덫에 지나지 않는다고 아주 옳게 여겼다."], ["Indeed, I have observed, though it may seem unaccountable to the reader, that this guard of prudence, like the trained bands, is always readiest to go on duty where there is the least danger.", "그러면서도 그녀의 처신은 어찌나 신중했던지, 마치 온 여성을 노려 놓인 모든 덫을 자기가 다 경계해야 하는 사람처럼 빈틈없이 조심했다. 사실 나는, 독자에게는 영문 모를 일로 보일지 모르나, 이 신중함이라는 경비병이 마치 훈련된 민병대처럼 위험이 가장 적은 곳에 가장 먼저 달려가 보초를 선다는 것을 눈여겨봐 왔다."], ["It often basely and cowardly deserts those paragons for whom the men are all wishing, sighing, dying, and spreading every net in their power; and constantly attends at the heels of that higher order of women for whom the other sex have a more distant and awful respect, and whom (from despair, I suppose, of success) they never venture to attack.", "그것은 남자들이 한결같이 갈망하고 한숨짓고 애태우며 온갖 그물을 다 펼쳐대는 그런 절세의 미인들은 비겁하고 야비하게 저버리기 일쑤다. 그러면서도 다른 성이 좀 더 멀찍이서 두려운 경의를 품을 뿐, (아마도 성공할 가망이 없다는 절망 때문에) 감히 공략할 엄두조차 내지 못하는, 그 한 단계 높은 부류의 여인들 발치에는 늘 따라붙어 시중을 든다."]], [["Reader, I think proper, before we proceed any farther together, to acquaint thee that I intend to digress, through this whole history, as often as I see occasion, of which I am myself a better judge than any pitiful critic whatever; and here I must desire all those critics to mind their own business, and not to intermeddle with affairs or works which no ways concern them; for till they produce the authority by which they are constituted judges, I shall not plead to their jurisdiction.", "독자여, 우리가 더 멀리 함께 나아가기 전에, 나는 이 이야기 전체에 걸쳐 적당하다고 여겨질 때마다 곁길로 새겠다는 뜻을 그대에게 알려두는 것이 옳다고 생각한다. 그 적당함을 판단하는 데는 그 어떤 가련한 비평가보다도 나 자신이 더 나은 판관이다. 그러니 여기서 나는 그 모든 비평가에게 부디 제 일이나 신경 쓰고, 자기와는 아무 상관도 없는 일이나 작품에 간섭하지 말라고 당부해야겠다. 그들이 무슨 권위로 판관 자리에 앉게 되었는지 그 근거를 내놓기 전까지는, 나는 그들의 재판 관할권을 인정하여 변론에 나서지 않을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["An odd accident which befel Mr Allworthy at his return home.", "올워디 씨가 집으로 돌아왔을 때 그에게 일어난 기묘한 사건."], ["The decent behaviour of Mrs Deborah Wilkins, with some proper animadversions on bastards.", "데버러 윌킨스 부인의 점잖은 처신, 그리고 사생아에 관한 몇 가지 적절한 논평."]], [["I have told my reader, in the preceding chapter, that Mr Allworthy inherited a large fortune; that he had a good heart, and no family.", "앞 장에서 나는 독자에게 올워디 씨가 막대한 재산을 물려받았고, 선한 마음씨를 지녔으며, 가족이 없다는 것을 이야기했다. 그러니 많은 이들이 틀림없이 이런 결론을 내릴 것이다."], ["Hence, doubtless, it will be concluded by many that he lived like an honest man, owed no one a shilling, took nothing but what was his own, kept a good house, entertained his neighbours with a hearty welcome at his table, and was charitable to the poor, i.e. to those who had rather beg than work, by giving them the offals from it; that he died immensely rich and built an hospital.", "그는 정직한 사람답게 살았고, 누구에게도 한 푼 빚지지 않았으며, 제 것이 아닌 것은 결코 취하지 않았고, 좋은 집을 건사하며 이웃을 식탁으로 맞아 진심으로 환대했고, 가난한 이들에게—말하자면 일하기보다 구걸하기를 택하는 자들에게—식탁에서 남은 음식을 베풀어 자선을 행했으며, 마침내 어마어마한 부자로 죽어 병원을 하나 세웠으리라고 말이다."]], [["And true it is that he did many of these things; but had he done nothing more I should have left him to have recorded his own merit on some fair freestone over the door of that hospital.", "그가 이 일들 가운데 많은 것을 실제로 행한 것은 사실이다. 그러나 그것 말고 한 일이 아무것도 없었다면, 나는 그를 그 병원 문 위에 놓인 어느 매끈한 사암 비석에다 제 공덕이나 새기도록 내버려 두었을 것이다."], ["Matters of a much more extraordinary kind are to be the subject of this history, or I should grossly mis-spend my time in writing so voluminous a work; and you, my sagacious friend, might with equal profit and pleasure travel through some pages which certain droll authors have been facetiously pleased to call The History of England.", "이 이야기의 주제가 되어야 할 것은 그보다 훨씬 비범한 종류의 일들이다. 그렇지 않다면 나는 이토록 방대한 작품을 쓰느라 시간을 형편없이 허비하는 셈이 될 것이며, 명민한 벗이여, 그대 또한 어떤 익살스러운 작가들이 우스갯소리로 《영국사》라 부르기를 즐겨 온 몇몇 책장을 넘기는 편이 똑같은 이득과 즐거움을 줄 것이다."]], [["Mr Allworthy had been absent a full quarter of a year in London, on some very particular business, though I know not what it was; but judge of its importance by its having detained him so long from home, whence he had not been absent a month at a time during the space of many years.", "올워디 씨는 매우 특별한 용무로 런던에 꼬박 석 달을 머물러 집을 비웠는데, 그 용무가 무엇이었는지 나는 알지 못한다. 다만 여러 해 동안 한 번에 한 달 이상 집을 비운 적이 없던 그를 그토록 오래 붙들어 둔 것을 보면, 그 중요성을 짐작할 수 있으리라."], ["He came to his house very late in the evening, and after a short supper with his sister, retired much fatigued to his chamber.", "그는 저녁 매우 늦은 시각에 집에 도착했고, 누이와 짧게 저녁을 든 뒤 몹시 지친 몸으로 침실로 물러났다."], ["Here, having spent some minutes on his knees--a custom which he never broke through on any account--he was preparing to step into bed, when, upon opening the cloathes, to his great surprize he beheld an infant, wrapt up in some coarse linen, in a sweet and profound sleep, between his sheets.", "거기서 그는 무릎을 꿇고 몇 분을 보낸 뒤—어떤 일이 있어도 거르지 않는 습관이었다—막 잠자리에 들려던 참이었다. 그런데 이불을 들치자, 그는 크게 놀랍게도 거친 아마포에 싸인 채 시트 사이에서 달고 깊은 잠에 빠져 있는 한 갓난아이를 보게 되었다."], ["He stood some time lost in astonishment at this sight; but, as good nature had always the ascendant in his mind, he soon began to be touched with sentiments of compassion for the little wretch before him.", "그는 이 광경에 넋을 잃고 한동안 서 있었다. 그러나 그의 마음에서는 늘 선한 천성이 우위를 차지하고 있던 터라, 그는 곧 눈앞의 가엾은 어린 것에게 연민의 정을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He then rang his bell, and ordered an elderly woman-servant to rise immediately, and come to him; and in the meantime was so eager in contemplating the beauty of innocence, appearing in those lively colours with which infancy and sleep always display it, that his thoughts were too much engaged to reflect that he was in his shirt when the matron came in.", "그는 종을 울려 나이 든 하녀에게 당장 일어나 자기에게 오라고 일렀다. 그러는 동안 그는 갓난아이와 잠이 언제나 드러내 보이는 그 생생한 빛깔로 나타난 순결의 아름다움을 들여다보는 데 너무도 골몰한 나머지, 나이 든 하녀가 들어왔을 때 자신이 속옷 바람이라는 사실을 미처 떠올리지 못할 만큼 생각에 깊이 빠져 있었다."], ["She had indeed given her master sufficient time to dress himself; for out of respect to him, and regard to decency, she had spent many minutes in adjusting her hair at the looking-glass, notwithstanding all the hurry in which she had been summoned by the servant, and though her master, for aught she knew, lay expiring in an apoplexy, or in some other fit.", "사실 그 하녀는 주인에게 옷을 갖춰 입을 시간을 넉넉히 주었다. 주인에 대한 존경과 예법을 지키려는 마음에서, 그녀는 하인이 그토록 다급하게 불러댔음에도, 또 그녀가 아는 한 주인이 어쩌면 졸중이나 다른 발작으로 숨을 거두고 있을지도 모르는 와중에도, 거울 앞에서 머리를 매만지느라 여러 분을 보냈으니 말이다."]], [["It will not be wondered at that a creature who had so strict a regard to decency in her own person, should be shocked at the least deviation from it in another.", "제 몸가짐에 그토록 엄격하게 예법을 지키는 사람이 남에게서 그 예법이 조금이라도 어긋나는 것을 보고 충격을 받는다 한들 놀랄 일은 아니다. 그리하여 그녀는 문을 열고 주인이 속옷 바람으로 손에 촛불을 든 채 침대 곁에 서 있는 것을 보자마자, 끔찍한 공포에 사로잡혀 뒷걸음질을 쳤고, 만일 그가 그제야 자신이 옷을 벗고 있었음을 떠올리고서, 등에 옷가지를 걸쳐 데버러 윌킨스 부인의 순결한 눈에 더는 충격을 줄 수 없는 상태가 될 때까지 문밖에서 기다려 달라고 청해 그녀의 공포를 가라앉히지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 그대로 기절해 버렸을지도 모른다."], ["She therefore no sooner opened the door, and saw her master standing by the bedside in his shirt, with a candle in his hand, than she started back in a most terrible fright, and might perhaps have swooned away, had he not now recollected his being undrest, and put an end to her terrors by desiring her to stay without the door till he had thrown some cloathes over his back, and was become incapable of shocking the pure eyes of Mrs Deborah Wilkins, who, though in the fifty-second year of her age, vowed she had never beheld a man without his coat.", "그녀는 비록 쉰두 살의 나이였으나, 외투를 걸치지 않은 남자를 한 번도 본 적이 없노라고 맹세하던 사람이었다. 비웃기 좋아하는 자들과 신성을 모독하는 익살꾼들은 어쩌면 그녀의 첫 번째 공포를 두고 웃을지도 모른다."], ["Sneerers and prophane wits may perhaps laugh at her first fright; yet my graver reader, when he considers the time of night, the summons from her bed, and the situation in which she found her master, will highly justify and applaud her conduct, unless the prudence which must be supposed to attend maidens at that period of life at which Mrs Deborah had arrived, should a little lessen his admiration.", "그러나 좀 더 진지한 내 독자라면, 한밤중이라는 시각과, 잠자리에서 불려 나온 정황과, 주인이 처해 있던 상태를 헤아릴 때, 그녀의 처신을 크게 정당하다 여기며 갈채를 보낼 것이다. 다만 데버러 부인이 다다른 그 나이의 처녀들에게 으레 따른다고 여겨지는 신중함이, 그 감탄을 조금 누그러뜨리지 않는다면 말이다."]], [["When Mrs Deborah returned into the room, and was acquainted by her master with the finding the little infant, her consternation was rather greater than his had been; nor could she refrain from crying out, with great horror of accent as well as look, “My good sir!", "데버러 부인이 방으로 돌아와 주인에게서 그 어린 갓난아이를 발견한 일을 전해 듣자, 그녀의 경악은 오히려 주인의 그것보다 더했다. 그녀는 말투에서나 표정에서나 큰 공포를 담아 이렇게 외치지 않을 수 없었다."], ["what's to be done?”", "\"나리! 이를 어쩌면 좋답니까?\""], ["Mr Allworthy answered, she must take care of the child that evening, and in the morning he would give orders to provide it a nurse.", "올워디 씨는 그날 저녁은 그녀가 아이를 돌보아야 하며, 아침이 되면 유모를 구하도록 지시하겠노라고 대답했다. \"예, 나리.\""], ["“Yes, sir,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she; “and I hope your worship will send out your warrant to take up the hussy its mother, for she must be one of the neighbourhood; and I should be glad to see her committed to Bridewell, and whipt at the cart's tail.", "\"그리고 부디 나리께서 영장을 내리시어 이 아이의 어미인 그 화냥년을 잡아들이시기를 바랍니다. 분명 이 근방 사람일 테니까요."], ["Indeed, such wicked sluts cannot be too severely punished.", "저는 그 계집이 브라이드웰 감화원에 끌려가 짐수레 꽁무니에 묶인 채 채찍질당하는 꼴을 봤으면 좋겠습니다. 정말이지, 이런 사악한 잡것들은 아무리 호되게 벌해도 지나치지 않지요."], ["I'll warrant 'tis not her first, by her impudence in laying it to your worship.”", "감히 그걸 나리께 뒤집어씌우는 뻔뻔함을 보면, 이번이 처음이 아닐 거라고 장담합니다.\" \"나에게 뒤집어씌우다니, 데버러!\""], ["“In laying it to me, Deborah!”", "올워디가 대답했다. \"그 여자에게 그런 속셈이 있다고는 생각되지 않네."], ["answered Allworthy: “I can't think she hath any such design.", "내 짐작으로는 그저 제 아이를 거둘 방도로 이런 방법을 택한 것일 뿐이고, 솔직히 그보다 더 나쁜 짓을 하지 않은 것이 다행스럽군.\" \"이보다 더 나쁜 것이 무엇인지 저는 모르겠습니다.\""], ["I suppose she hath only taken this method to provide for her child; and truly I am glad she hath not done worse.”", "데버러가 외쳤다. \"이런 사악한 갈보들이 제 죄를 정직한 사람의 문간에 떠넘기는 것보다 말입니다."], ["“I don't know what is worse,”", "나리께서야 당신의 결백을 아시지만, 세상 사람들은 험구가 심하지요. 정직한 사람이 자기가 낳지도 않은 아이의 아비로 몰리는 일은 흔하디흔합니다."], ["cries Deborah, “than for such wicked strumpets to lay their sins at honest men's doors; and though your worship knows your own innocence, yet the world is censorious; and it hath been many an honest man's hap to pass for the father of children he never begot; and if your worship should provide for the child, it may make the people the apter to believe; besides, why should your worship provide for what the parish is obliged to maintain?", "게다가 나리께서 그 아이를 거두시면, 사람들이 그 말을 더 곧이듣기 십상이지요. 그리고 어차피 교구가 떠맡아 기를 수밖에 없는 것을 어찌하여 나리께서 거두려 하십니까?"], ["For my own part, if it was an honest man's child, indeed--but for my own part, it goes against me to touch these misbegotten wretches, whom I don't look upon as my fellow-creatures.", "제 생각으로는, 정녕 정직한 사람의 아이라면 또 모르겠으나—제 생각으로는, 저는 이 잘못 태어난 가엾은 것들을 손대는 것조차 거슬립니다. 저는 이런 것들을 같은 사람으로 여기지도 않으니까요."], ["Faugh!", "아유!"], ["how it stinks!", "어찌나 고약한 냄새가 나는지!"], ["It doth not smell like a Christian.", "기독교인의 냄새 같지가 않아요."], ["If I might be so bold to give my advice, I would have it put in a basket, and sent out and laid at the churchwarden's door.", "제가 감히 한 말씀 올려도 된다면, 저는 이 아이를 바구니에 담아 교구위원의 문간에 내다 놓겠습니다. 오늘 밤은 좋은 편이고, 다만 비가 조금 오고 바람이 좀 불 뿐이지요."], ["It is a good night, only a little rainy and windy; and if it was well wrapt up, and put in a warm basket, it is two to one but it lives till it is found in the morning.", "잘 싸서 따뜻한 바구니에 담아 두면, 아침에 발견될 때까지 살아 있을 확률이 두 배는 됩니다. 설령 그러지 못한다 해도, 우리는 마땅한 보살핌을 베풀어 우리의 도리를 다한 셈이지요."], ["But if it should not, we have discharged our duty in taking proper care of it; and it is, perhaps, better for such creatures to die in a state of innocence, than to grow up and imitate their mothers; for nothing better can be expected of them.”", "그리고 이런 것들은 자라나서 제 어미를 흉내 내느니, 차라리 순결한 상태에서 죽는 편이 어쩌면 더 나을 겁니다. 그것들에게서 그보다 나은 것을 기대할 수는 없으니까요.\""]], [["There were some strokes in this speech which perhaps would have offended Mr Allworthy, had he strictly attended to it; but he had now got one of his fingers into the infant's hand, which, by its gentle pressure, seeming to implore his assistance, had certainly out-pleaded the eloquence of Mrs Deborah, had it been ten times greater than it was.", "이 말 가운데에는 올워디 씨가 만일 귀 기울여 들었더라면 아마 기분이 상했을 대목이 더러 있었다. 그러나 그는 어느새 손가락 하나를 갓난아이의 손에 쥐여 주고 있었는데, 그 아이가 부드럽게 손가락을 쥐며 마치 그의 도움을 간청하는 듯한 그 몸짓은, 데버러 부인의 웅변이 설령 지금보다 열 배는 더 대단했다 한들 그것을 능히 눌러 이겼을 것이 분명했다."], ["He now gave Mrs Deborah positive orders to take the child to her own bed, and to call up a maid-servant to provide it pap, and other things, against it waked.", "그는 이제 데버러 부인에게, 아이를 그녀 자신의 침대로 데려가고 하녀 하나를 깨워 아이가 깰 때를 대비해 미음과 다른 것들을 마련해 두라고 단호히 명했다."], ["He likewise ordered that proper cloathes should be procured for it early in the morning, and that it should be brought to himself as soon as he was stirring.", "또한 아침 일찍 아이에게 알맞은 옷을 구하도록 하고, 자신이 일어나는 대로 곧장 아이를 데려오라고 일렀다."]], [["Such was the discernment of Mrs Wilkins, and such the respect she bore her master, under whom she enjoyed a most excellent place, that her scruples gave way to his peremptory commands; and she took the child under her arms, without any apparent disgust at the illegality of its birth; and declaring it was a sweet little infant, walked off with it to her own chamber.", "윌킨스 부인의 분별이 그러했고, 또 더없이 좋은 자리를 누리게 해 주는 주인을 향한 그녀의 존경이 그러했기에, 그녀의 거리낌은 그의 단호한 명령 앞에 물러서고 말았다. 그녀는 아이의 출생이 떳떳지 못하다는 것에 겉으로는 아무런 혐오도 내비치지 않은 채 아이를 두 팔에 안아 들었고, 사랑스러운 어린 갓난아이라고 말하며 아이를 안고 제 방으로 걸어갔다."]], [["Allworthy here betook himself to those pleasing slumbers which a heart that hungers after goodness is apt to enjoy when thoroughly satisfied.", "여기서 올워디는, 선함을 갈망하는 마음이 흡족하게 채워졌을 때 누리기 쉬운 그 흐뭇한 잠 속으로 빠져들었다."], ["As these are possibly sweeter than what are occasioned by any other hearty meal, I should take more pains to display them to the reader, if I knew any air to recommend him to for the procuring such an appetite.", "이런 잠은 어쩌면 그 어떤 푸짐한 식사에서 비롯된 잠보다도 달콤할 터이니, 만일 그런 식욕을 돋우려면 어떤 곳의 공기를 마시라고 독자에게 권할지를 내가 알았더라면, 나는 그 잠을 독자에게 보여 주려고 더 많은 공을 들였을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["The reader's neck brought into danger by a description; his escape; and the great condescension of Miss Bridget Allworthy.", "묘사 하나로 독자의 목이 위태로워지나, 무사히 빠져나오고, 브리짓 올워디 양이 보여 준 크나큰 너그러움에 관하여."]], [["The Gothic stile of building could produce nothing nobler than Mr Allworthy's house.", "고딕 양식의 건축물이 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 고귀한 것이 있다면, 그것은 바로 올워디 씨의 저택이었다."], ["There was an air of grandeur in it that struck you with awe, and rivalled the beauties of the best Grecian architecture; and it was as commodious within as venerable without.", "거기에는 보는 이를 경외감으로 사로잡는 웅장한 기운이 서려 있어, 가장 빼어난 그리스 건축의 아름다움에 견줄 만했다. 밖으로는 위엄이 넘쳤고, 안으로는 그만큼 편안하고 쓰임새가 좋았다."]], [["It stood on the south-east side of a hill, but nearer the bottom than the top of it, so as to be sheltered from the north-east by a grove of old oaks which rose above it in a gradual ascent of near half a mile, and yet high enough to enjoy a most charming prospect of the valley beneath.", "저택은 어느 언덕의 남동쪽 비탈에 서 있었는데, 꼭대기보다는 기슭에 더 가까운 자리였다. 그래서 거의 반 마일에 걸쳐 완만하게 솟아오른 오래된 떡갈나무 숲이 저택 위로 우거져 북동풍을 막아 주었고, 그러면서도 아래로 펼쳐진 골짜기의 더없이 매혹적인 경관을 즐길 만큼은 높은 자리였다."]], [["In the midst of the grove was a fine lawn, sloping down towards the house, near the summit of which rose a plentiful spring, gushing out of a rock covered with firs, and forming a constant cascade of about thirty feet, not carried down a regular flight of steps, but tumbling in a natural fall over the broken and mossy stones till it came to the bottom of the rock, then running off in a pebly channel, that with many lesser falls winded along, till it fell into a lake at the foot of the hill, about a quarter of a mile below the house on the south side, and which was seen from every room in the front. Out of this lake, which filled the center of a beautiful plain, embellished with groups of beeches and elms, and fed with sheep, issued a river, that for several miles was seen to meander through an amazing variety of meadows and woods till it emptied itself into the sea, with a large arm of which, and an island beyond it, the prospect was closed.", "그 숲 한가운데에는 저택 쪽으로 비스듬히 기운 고운 잔디밭이 있었고, 그 꼭대기 가까이에는 전나무로 뒤덮인 바위에서 물이 콸콸 솟아 나오는 풍성한 샘이 있었다. 샘은 약 삼십 피트에 이르는 끊임없는 폭포를 이루었는데, 가지런히 다듬은 계단을 따라 흘러내리는 것이 아니라, 깨지고 이끼 낀 돌들 위로 자연스럽게 떨어지며 굴러내려 바위 밑동에 다다랐다. 그러고는 자갈 깔린 물길을 따라 흘러가며 크고 작은 여러 폭포를 이루어 굽이굽이 돌아, 마침내 언덕 기슭의 호수로 떨어졌다. 그 호수는 저택 남쪽으로 사 분의 일 마일쯤 아래에 있었고, 정면의 모든 방에서 내다보였다. 너도밤나무와 느릅나무 무리가 곳곳을 수놓고 양 떼가 풀을 뜯는 아름다운 평원의 한가운데를 채운 이 호수에서 강 하나가 흘러나왔다. 그 강은 몇 마일에 걸쳐 놀랍도록 다채로운 목초지와 숲 사이를 굽이쳐 흐르다가 마침내 바다로 흘러들었으니, 바다의 너른 한 줄기와 그 너머의 섬이 이 경관의 끝을 닫아 주었다."]], [["On the right of this valley opened another of less extent, adorned with several villages, and terminated by one of the towers of an old ruined abby, grown over with ivy, and part of the front, which remained still entire.", "이 골짜기의 오른쪽으로는 좀 더 작은 또 하나의 골짜기가 열렸는데, 여러 마을이 점점이 그곳을 꾸미고 있었고, 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮인 채 정면 일부만이 아직 온전히 남은 어느 무너진 옛 수도원의 탑 하나가 그 끝을 맺고 있었다."]], [["The left-hand scene presented the view of a very fine park, composed of very unequal ground, and agreeably varied with all the diversity that hills, lawns, wood, and water, laid out with admirable taste, but owing less to art than to nature, could give.", "왼쪽 풍경은 무척 빼어난 정원의 모습을 보여 주었다. 높낮이가 고르지 않은 땅으로 이루어져 있었고, 언덕과 잔디밭, 숲과 물이 어우러져 감탄스러운 취향으로 배치된, 그러나 인공보다는 자연의 손길에 더 많이 기댄 온갖 변화로 보기 좋게 다채로웠다."], ["Beyond this, the country gradually rose into a ridge of wild mountains, the tops of which were above the clouds.", "그 너머로는 들판이 차츰 솟아올라 험준한 산줄기를 이루었는데, 그 봉우리들은 구름 위로 솟아 있었다."]], [["It was now the middle of May, and the morning was remarkably serene, when Mr Allworthy walked forth on the terrace, where the dawn opened every minute that lovely prospect we have before described to his eye; and now having sent forth streams of light, which ascended the blue firmament before him, as harbingers preceding his pomp, in the full blaze of his majesty rose the sun, than which one object alone in this lower creation could be more glorious, and that Mr Allworthy himself presented--a human being replete with benevolence, meditating in what manner he might render himself most acceptable to his Creator, by doing most good to his creatures.", "때는 바야흐로 오월 중순이었고, 아침은 유난히 맑고 고요했다. 올워디 씨가 테라스로 걸어 나오자, 동틀 녘의 빛은 우리가 앞서 묘사한 그 사랑스러운 경관을 시시각각 그의 눈앞에 펼쳐 보였다. 이윽고 빛줄기들이 그의 앞에서 푸른 창공으로 솟아오르며 그 위풍에 앞서가는 선구자처럼 길을 트자, 위엄을 한껏 내뿜으며 태양이 떠올랐다. 그런데 이 낮은 세상의 피조물 가운데 그 태양보다 더 영광스러울 수 있는 대상이 단 하나 있었으니, 바로 올워디 씨 자신이 그것을 보여 주었다. 자애로움으로 가득 찬 한 인간이, 제 피조물들에게 가장 큰 선을 베풂으로써 어떻게 하면 창조주의 마음에 가장 흡족한 사람이 될 수 있을까를 깊이 헤아리고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Reader, take care.", "독자여, 조심하시라."], ["I have unadvisedly led thee to the top of as high a hill as Mr Allworthy's, and how to get thee down without breaking thy neck, I do not well know.", "나는 경솔하게도 그대를 올워디 씨의 저택만큼이나 높은 언덕 꼭대기로 이끌고 말았으니, 그대의 목이 부러지지 않게끔 어떻게 내려보내야 할지 나로서도 도무지 알 수가 없다. 하지만 그래도 함께 미끄러져 내려가 보기로 하자."], ["However, let us e'en venture to slide down together; for Miss Bridget rings her bell, and Mr Allworthy is summoned to breakfast, where I must attend, and, if you please, shall be glad of your company.", "브리짓 양이 종을 울리고 있고, 올워디 씨가 아침 식사 자리로 불려 가고 있으니 말이다. 나도 그 자리에 가 봐야 하는데, 괜찮으시다면 그대가 함께해 주신다면 더없이 기쁘겠다."]], [["The usual compliments having past between Mr Allworthy and Miss Bridget, and the tea being poured out, he summoned Mrs Wilkins, and told his sister he had a present for her, for which she thanked him--imagining, I suppose, it had been a gown, or some ornament for her person.", "올워디 씨와 브리짓 양 사이에 으레 오가는 인사말이 끝나고 차가 따라지자, 그는 윌킨스 부인을 불러들이고는 누이에게 선물을 하나 준비해 두었다고 말했다. 누이는 그 말에 고맙다고 했는데, 짐작건대 가운이나 몸을 꾸밀 무슨 장신구쯤으로 여긴 모양이었다."], ["Indeed, he very often made her such presents; and she, in complacence to him, spent much time in adorning herself.", "사실 그는 누이에게 그런 선물을 꽤 자주 했고, 누이도 그를 흡족하게 해 주려는 마음에서 자신을 단장하는 데 적잖은 시간을 들였다."], ["I say in complacence to him, because she always exprest the greatest contempt for dress, and for those ladies who made it their study.", "굳이 그를 흡족하게 해 주려는 마음이라 말하는 까닭은, 누이가 늘 옷차림과 그것에 골몰하는 부인네들을 더없이 경멸한다고 입버릇처럼 말해 왔기 때문이다."]], [["But if such was her expectation, how was she disappointed when Mrs Wilkins, according to the order she had received from her master, produced the little infant?", "그런데 만일 그것이 누이의 기대였다면, 윌킨스 부인이 주인에게서 받은 지시대로 그 자그마한 아기를 내놓았을 때 누이가 얼마나 실망했겠는가? 앞서 말한 대로, 큰 놀라움은 흔히 침묵을 부르는 법이다."], ["Great surprizes, as hath been observed, are apt to be silent; and so was Miss Bridget, till her brother began, and told her the whole story, which, as the reader knows it already, we shall not repeat.", "브리짓 양도 마찬가지여서, 오라비가 입을 열어 자초지종을 다 들려줄 때까지 잠자코 있었다. 그 이야기는 독자가 이미 알고 있으니, 여기서 되풀이하지는 않겠다."]], [["Miss Bridget had always exprest so great a regard for what the ladies are pleased to call virtue, and had herself maintained such a severity of character, that it was expected, especially by Wilkins, that she would have vented much bitterness on this occasion, and would have voted for sending the child, as a kind of noxious animal, immediately out of the house; but, on the contrary, she rather took the good-natured side of the question, intimated some compassion for the helpless little creature, and commended her brother's charity in what he had done.", "브리짓 양은 부인네들이 즐겨 정숙함이라 부르는 것에 늘 대단한 존중을 드러냈고, 스스로도 그토록 엄격한 품성을 지켜 왔던 터라, 특히 윌킨스로서는 이번 일에 누이가 모진 말을 잔뜩 쏟아내며 그 아이를 무슨 해로운 짐승이라도 되는 양 당장 집 밖으로 내보내자고 할 줄로 예상했다. 그런데 정반대로, 누이는 오히려 너그러운 쪽으로 마음을 기울여, 이 가엾고 무력한 작은 생명에게 얼마간 측은한 마음을 내비치고, 오라비가 베푼 그 자비로운 처사를 칭찬하기까지 했다."]], [["Perhaps the reader may account for this behaviour from her condescension to Mr Allworthy, when we have informed him that the good man had ended his narrative with owning a resolution to take care of the child, and to breed him up as his own; for, to acknowledge the truth, she was always ready to oblige her brother, and very seldom, if ever, contradicted his sentiments. She would, indeed, sometimes make a few observations, as that men were headstrong, and must have their own way, and would wish she had been blest with an independent fortune; but these were always vented in a low voice, and at the most amounted only to what is called muttering.", "어쩌면 독자는 누이의 이런 태도를 올워디 씨에 대한 순종에서 비롯된 것으로 여길지도 모르겠다. 이 선량한 양반이 자신의 이야기를 마무리하면서, 그 아이를 거두어 친자식처럼 길러 내겠다는 결심을 털어놓았다는 사실을 우리가 일러둔다면 말이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 누이는 늘 오라비의 뜻을 기꺼이 받들 채비가 되어 있었고, 그의 생각에 거스르는 일이란 좀처럼, 아니 거의 없다시피 했다. 다만 이따금 몇 마디 한마디씩 덧붙이기는 했는데, 이를테면 사내들이란 고집이 세어 제 멋대로 해야 직성이 풀린다느니, 자기에게도 남에게 기대지 않을 만한 재산이 있었더라면 좋았을 것을, 하는 식이었다. 그러나 이런 말들은 늘 낮은 목소리로 흘러나왔고, 기껏해야 흔히 중얼거림이라 부르는 정도에 그쳤다."]], [["However, what she withheld from the infant, she bestowed with the utmost profuseness on the poor unknown mother, whom she called an impudent slut, a wanton hussy, an audacious harlot, a wicked jade, a vile strumpet, with every other appellation with which the tongue of virtue never fails to lash those who bring a disgrace on the sex.", "그렇지만 누이는 아기에게 아끼던 그 모진 말들을, 이름도 모르는 가엾은 어미에게는 더없이 아낌없이 퍼부었다. 뻔뻔한 계집이니, 음탕한 년이니, 대담한 화냥년이니, 못된 잡년이니, 비천한 갈보니 하며, 정숙함의 혀가 제 성을 욕보인 자들을 후려칠 때 결코 빼놓는 법이 없는 온갖 호칭을 죄다 갖다 붙였다."]], [["A consultation was now entered into how to proceed in order to discover the mother.", "이제 어미를 찾아내려면 어떻게 해야 할지 의논이 시작되었다."], ["A scrutiny was first made into the characters of the female servants of the house, who were all acquitted by Mrs Wilkins, and with apparent merit; for she had collected them herself, and perhaps it would be difficult to find such another set of scarecrows.", "먼저 집안 여자 하인들의 행실을 살펴보았는데, 이들은 모두 윌킨스 부인의 명백한 보증으로 혐의를 벗었다. 하인들은 부인이 손수 골라 모은 이들이었고, 어쩌면 그렇게 못생긴 허수아비 같은 무리를 또 한 패 찾아내기란 쉽지 않았을 것이다."]], [["The next step was to examine among the inhabitants of the parish; and this was referred to Mrs Wilkins, who was to enquire with all imaginable diligence, and to make her report in the afternoon.", "다음 단계는 교구 주민들 사이를 캐 보는 일이었는데, 이는 윌킨스 부인에게 맡겨졌다. 부인은 온갖 정성을 다해 알아본 뒤 오후에 그 결과를 보고하기로 했다."]], [["Matters being thus settled, Mr Allworthy withdrew to his study, as was his custom, and left the child to his sister, who, at his desire, had undertaken the care of it.", "이렇게 일이 정해지자 올워디 씨는 늘 하던 대로 서재로 물러갔고, 아이는 누이에게 맡겨 두었다. 누이는 그의 부탁에 따라 아이를 돌보는 일을 떠맡았던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["Containing a few common matters, with a very uncommon observation upon them.", "주인이 자리를 뜨자, 데버라 부인은 브리짓 아가씨의 신호를 기다리며 말없이 서 있었다."], ["When her master was departed, Mrs Deborah stood silent, expecting her cue from Miss Bridget; for as to what had past before her master, the prudent housekeeper by no means relied upon it, as she had often known the sentiments of the lady in her brother's absence to differ greatly from those which she had expressed in his presence.", "주인 앞에서 오갔던 말에 대해서는, 신중한 가정부는 결코 그것을 믿지 않았으니, 오라비가 없을 때의 아가씨 속마음이 그가 있는 자리에서 내뱉은 말과 크게 어긋나는 경우를 여러 번 겪어 보았기 때문이다. 그러나 브리짓 아가씨는 그녀를 그 미심쩍은 처지에 오래 두지는 않았다."], ["Miss Bridget did not, however, suffer her to continue long in this doubtful situation; for having looked some time earnestly at the child, as it lay asleep in the lap of Mrs Deborah, the good lady could not forbear giving it a hearty kiss, at the same time declaring herself wonderfully pleased with its beauty and innocence.", "데버라 부인의 무릎 위에서 잠든 아이를 한동안 진지하게 들여다보던 마음씨 고운 아가씨는, 끝내 아이에게 다정한 입맞춤을 하지 않고는 견딜 수 없었고, 동시에 그 아름다움과 천진함이 더없이 마음에 든다고 선언했다."], ["Mrs Deborah no sooner observed this than she fell to squeezing and kissing, with as great raptures as sometimes inspire the sage dame of forty and five towards a youthful and vigorous bridegroom, crying out, in a shrill voice, “O, the dear little creature!--The dear, sweet, pretty creature!", "이를 본 데버라 부인은 곧장 아이를 끌어안고 입을 맞추기 시작했는데, 그 황홀함이란 마흔다섯 먹은 점잖은 부인이 젊고 혈기왕성한 신랑을 향해 이따금 품는 그것에 못지않았으니, 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"어머, 이 귀여운 것!—사랑스럽고 어여쁜 것!"], ["Well, I vow it is as fine a boy as ever was seen!”", "정말이지, 이렇게 잘생긴 사내아이는 난생처음 본다니까요!\""]], [["These exclamations continued till they were interrupted by the lady, who now proceeded to execute the commission given her by her brother, and gave orders for providing all necessaries for the child, appointing a very good room in the house for his nursery.", "이 감탄은 아가씨가 끼어들 때까지 이어졌다. 이제 아가씨는 오라비가 맡긴 임무를 수행하기 시작하여, 아이에게 필요한 온갖 것들을 마련하라 일렀고, 집 안의 아주 좋은 방 하나를 아이의 육아실로 정해 주었다."], ["Her orders were indeed so liberal, that, had it been a child of her own, she could not have exceeded them; but, lest the virtuous reader may condemn her for showing too great regard to a base-born infant, to which all charity is condemned by law as irreligious, we think proper to observe that she concluded the whole with saying, “Since it was her brother's whim to adopt the little brat, she supposed little master must be treated with great tenderness.", "그 분부가 어찌나 후하던지, 만일 제 친자식이었다 한들 그 이상 베풀 수는 없었을 것이다. 다만 덕망 높은 독자께서, 천출 아이에게—법으로 보면 그런 아이에게 베푸는 모든 자선은 불경한 짓으로 단죄되거니와—지나친 관심을 보였다 하여 그녀를 나무라실까 하여, 우리는 그녀가 이 모든 일을 이런 말로 마무리했음을 일러두는 것이 마땅하다고 본다."], ["For her part, she could not help thinking it was an encouragement to vice; but that she knew too much of the obstinacy of mankind to oppose any of their ridiculous humours.”", "\"오라비가 이 어린 것을 거두겠다는 변덕을 부린 이상, 도련님을 더없이 살뜰히 다뤄야겠지요. 나로서야 이게 악덕을 부추기는 짓이 아닌가 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없지만, 사람이란 고집이 어떤 것인지 너무도 잘 아는 터라, 그 우스꽝스러운 변덕에 대놓고 맞설 마음은 없어요.\""]], [["With reflections of this nature she usually, as has been hinted, accompanied every act of compliance with her brother's inclinations; and surely nothing could more contribute to heighten the merit of this compliance than a declaration that she knew, at the same time, the folly and unreasonableness of those inclinations to which she submitted.", "앞서 넌지시 비친 대로, 그녀는 오라비의 뜻을 따르는 행위 하나하나에 으레 이런 식의 토를 곁들이곤 했다. 그리고 자기가 굴복하는 그 뜻이 어리석고 사리에 맞지 않음을 동시에 알고 있노라 선언하는 것만큼, 이 순종의 공덕을 한층 더 돋보이게 하는 것도 분명 없을 것이다."], ["Tacit obedience implies no force upon the will, and consequently may be easily, and without any pains, preserved; but when a wife, a child, a relation, or a friend, performs what we desire, with grumbling and reluctance, with expressions of dislike and dissatisfaction, the manifest difficulty which they undergo must greatly enhance the obligation.", "말없는 복종이란 의지에 아무런 강제도 없음을 뜻하며, 따라서 별다른 수고 없이 손쉽게 지킬 수 있다. 그러나 아내가, 자식이, 친척이, 혹은 벗이 우리가 바라는 바를 투덜대며 마지못해, 싫고 못마땅하다는 기색을 내비치며 행한다면, 그들이 겪는 그 뚜렷한 괴로움이야말로 우리가 진 은혜를 크게 더해 줄 수밖에 없다."]], [["As this is one of those deep observations which very few readers can be supposed capable of making themselves, I have thought proper to lend them my assistance; but this is a favour rarely to be expected in the course of my work.", "이것은 스스로 떠올릴 수 있는 독자가 극히 드물 만한 깊은 통찰 가운데 하나이므로, 나는 독자에게 내 도움의 손길을 빌려주는 것이 마땅하다 여겼다. 다만 이런 호의는 이 작품을 읽어 나가는 동안 좀처럼 기대하기 어려운 것이다."], ["Indeed, I shall seldom or never so indulge him, unless in such instances as this, where nothing but the inspiration with which we writers are gifted, can possibly enable any one to make the discovery.", "실로 나는 이런 경우가 아니고서는 좀처럼, 아니 거의 결코 그를 그렇게 떠받들지 않을 것이다. 우리 작가들에게 주어진 영감이 아니고서는 누구도 도무지 그런 발견에 이를 수 없는, 바로 이런 경우가 아니라면 말이다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["Mrs Deborah is introduced into the parish with a simile.", "데버라 부인이 비유와 함께 이 교구로 등장하다."], ["A short account of Jenny Jones, with the difficulties and discouragements which may attend young women in the pursuit of learning.", "제니 존스에 대한 짤막한 소개와, 배움을 좇는 젊은 여인들에게 따라붙기 마련인 어려움과 낙담들."]], [["Mrs Deborah, having disposed of the child according to the will of her master, now prepared to visit those habitations which were supposed to conceal its mother.", "주인의 뜻에 따라 아이를 처리한 데버라 부인은, 이제 그 아이의 어미를 숨기고 있으리라 여겨지는 집들을 찾아 나설 채비를 했다."]], [["Not otherwise than when a kite, tremendous bird, is beheld by the feathered generation soaring aloft, and hovering over their heads, the amorous dove, and every innocent little bird, spread wide the alarm, and fly trembling to their hiding-places.", "무시무시한 새인 솔개가 하늘 높이 솟아올라 머리 위를 맴도는 모습을 깃털 달린 무리가 바라볼 때와 다를 바 없었다. 사랑에 빠진 비둘기며 죄 없는 작은 새들은 저마다 사방으로 경보를 퍼뜨리고, 벌벌 떨며 숨을 곳으로 날아든다."], ["He proudly beats the air, conscious of his dignity, and meditates intended mischief.", "솔개는 제 위엄을 의식한 채 의기양양하게 허공을 치며, 마음먹은 악행을 곰곰이 궁리한다."]], [["So when the approach of Mrs Deborah was proclaimed through the street, all the inhabitants ran trembling into their houses, each matron dreading lest the visit should fall to her lot.", "그리하여 데버라 부인이 다가온다는 소문이 거리에 퍼지자, 모든 주민이 벌벌 떨며 제 집으로 달려 들어갔고, 안주인마다 그 방문이 제 차례로 떨어질까 두려워했다."], ["She with stately steps proudly advances over the field: aloft she bears her towering head, filled with conceit of her own pre-eminence, and schemes to effect her intended discovery.", "그녀는 위풍당당한 걸음으로 의기양양하게 벌판을 가로질러 나아간다. 제 자신의 탁월함에 대한 자만으로 가득 찬 그 우뚝 솟은 머리를 높이 쳐들고서, 마음먹은 발각을 이뤄 낼 책략을 짠다."]], [["The sagacious reader will not from this simile imagine these poor people had any apprehension of the design with which Mrs Wilkins was now coming towards them; but as the great beauty of the simile may possibly sleep these hundred years, till some future commentator shall take this work in hand, I think proper to lend the reader a little assistance in this place.", "눈치 빠른 독자라면 이 비유를 보고, 이 가엾은 사람들이 윌킨스 부인이 지금 자신들을 향해 다가오는 그 속셈을 무슨 낌새라도 챘으리라고 상상하지는 않을 것이다. 다만 이 비유의 빼어난 아름다움이라는 것이, 훗날 어느 주석가가 이 작품을 손에 들 때까지 어쩌면 백 년쯤 잠들어 있을지도 모르는 노릇이니, 나는 이 대목에서 독자에게 약간의 도움을 빌려주는 것이 마땅하다고 본다."]], [["It is my intention, therefore, to signify, that, as it is the nature of a kite to devour little birds, so is it the nature of such persons as Mrs Wilkins to insult and tyrannize over little people.", "그러므로 내가 일러두고자 하는 바는 이렇다. 작은 새들을 잡아먹는 것이 솔개의 본성이듯, 작은 사람들을 모욕하고 짓누르며 군림하는 것이 윌킨스 부인 같은 부류의 본성이라는 것이다."], ["This being indeed the means which they use to recompense to themselves their extreme servility and condescension to their superiors; for nothing can be more reasonable, than that slaves and flatterers should exact the same taxes on all below them, which they themselves pay to all above them.", "이것이야말로 그들이 윗사람들에게 바친 극도의 굽실거림과 비굴함을 스스로에게 보상하는 수단이다. 노예와 아첨꾼이 윗사람 모두에게 바치는 것과 똑같은 세금을 제 아랫사람 모두에게서 거둬들이는 것보다 더 이치에 맞는 일은 없으니 말이다."]], [["Whenever Mrs Deborah had occasion to exert any extraordinary condescension to Mrs Bridget, and by that means had a little soured her natural disposition, it was usual with her to walk forth among these people, in order to refine her temper, by venting, and, as it were, purging off all ill humours; on which account she was by no means a welcome visitant: to say the truth, she was universally dreaded and hated by them all.", "데버라 부인은 브리짓 마님에게 유난히 굽실거려야 할 일이 생겨 그 바람에 타고난 성미가 좀 시큼해졌다 싶을 때면, 이 사람들 사이로 나가 거니는 것이 버릇이었다. 묵은 짜증을 죄다 쏟아 내고, 말하자면 깨끗이 비워 냄으로써 제 기분을 다스리기 위함이었다. 그러니 그녀는 결코 반가운 손님일 수 없었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그녀는 그들 모두에게 두루 두려움과 미움을 받았다."]], [["On her arrival in this place, she went immediately to the habitation of an elderly matron; to whom, as this matron had the good fortune to resemble herself in the comeliness of her person, as well as in her age, she had generally been more favourable than to any of the rest.", "이곳에 도착한 그녀는 곧장 어느 나이 든 안주인의 집으로 갔다. 이 안주인은 다행히도 나이뿐 아니라 인물의 곱상함에서도 자신을 닮은 데가 있어, 데버라 부인은 평소 다른 누구보다 이 여자에게 호의를 베풀어 왔다."], ["To this woman she imparted what had happened, and the design upon which she was come thither that morning.", "그녀는 이 여자에게 무슨 일이 있었는지, 그리고 그날 아침 무슨 목적으로 여기까지 왔는지를 털어놓았다."], ["These two began presently to scrutinize the characters of the several young girls who lived in any of those houses, and at last fixed their strongest suspicion on one Jenny Jones, who, they both agreed, was the likeliest person to have committed this fact.", "두 사람은 이내 그 집들 가운데 어디든 살고 있는 젊은 처녀들 하나하나의 평판을 샅샅이 따지기 시작했고, 마침내 제니 존스라는 처녀 하나에게 가장 강한 의심을 두었다. 두 사람 모두 그녀야말로 이 일을 저질렀을 법한 가장 유력한 인물이라는 데 뜻을 같이했다."]], [["This Jenny Jones was no very comely girl, either in her face or person; but nature had somewhat compensated the want of beauty with what is generally more esteemed by those ladies whose judgment is arrived at years of perfect maturity, for she had given her a very uncommon share of understanding. This gift Jenny had a good deal improved by erudition. She had lived several years a servant with a schoolmaster, who, discovering a great quickness of parts in the girl, and an extraordinary desire of learning--for every leisure hour she was always found reading in the books of the scholars--had the good-nature, or folly--just as the reader pleases to call it--to instruct her so far, that she obtained a competent skill in the Latin language, and was, perhaps, as good a scholar as most of the young men of quality of the age. This advantage, however, like most others of an extraordinary kind, was attended with some small inconveniences: for as it is not to be wondered at, that a young woman so well accomplished should have little relish for the society of those whom fortune had made her equals, but whom education had rendered so much her inferiors; so is it matter of no greater astonishment, that this superiority in Jenny, together with that behaviour which is its certain consequence, should produce among the rest some little envy and ill-will towards her; and these had, perhaps, secretly burnt in the bosoms of her neighbours ever since her return from her service.", "이 제니 존스는 얼굴이든 몸매든 그리 곱상한 처녀는 아니었다. 그러나 자연은 그 미모의 부족을, 판단력이 완전히 무르익을 나이에 이른 부인들이 흔히 더 높이 치는 그 무엇으로 어느 정도 채워 주었다. 다름 아니라 자연이 그녀에게 보기 드물 만큼 넉넉한 분별력을 내려 준 것이다. 이 천부의 재능을 제니는 학식으로 한층 갈고닦았다. 그녀는 여러 해 동안 어느 학교 선생 밑에서 하녀로 살았는데, 그 선생은 처녀에게서 재주의 뛰어난 민첩함과 배움에 대한 남다른 열망을 알아보고는 — 틈만 나면 늘 학생들의 책을 읽고 있는 모습이 눈에 띄었던 것이다 — 그녀를 가르칠 만큼 마음씨가 좋았던 것이다. 아니, 독자가 부르고 싶은 대로라면 어리석었던 것이다. 그 덕에 그녀는 라틴어에 제법 능숙해졌고, 어쩌면 그 시대의 웬만한 양갓집 젊은이들 못지않은 학자였을 것이다. 그러나 이 강점도 유별난 종류의 다른 강점들이 대개 그렇듯, 자잘한 불편을 몇 가지 데리고 다녔다. 이토록 뛰어난 소양을 갖춘 젊은 여인이, 운명은 자신과 같은 처지로 만들었으나 교육은 자신보다 훨씬 못한 처지로 만든 이들과 어울리는 데 흥이 나지 않는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니듯, 제니의 이 우월함이, 그리고 그 우월함에 으레 따라붙는 처신이 다른 이들 사이에 그녀를 향한 자잘한 시샘과 악의를 빚어내는 것 또한 더 놀랄 일이 못 된다. 어쩌면 이런 감정은 그녀가 하녀살이에서 돌아온 이래로 줄곧 이웃들의 가슴속에서 남몰래 타오르고 있었으리라."]], [["Their envy did not, however, display itself openly, till poor Jenny, to the surprize of everybody, and to the vexation of all the young women in these parts, had publickly shone forth on a Sunday in a new silk gown, with a laced cap, and other proper appendages to these.", "그러나 그들의 시샘은 겉으로 드러나지 않다가, 마침내 가엾은 제니가 모두를 놀라게 하고 이 일대 모든 젊은 여인들을 분하게 하면서, 어느 일요일에 새 비단 가운에 레이스 달린 모자며 거기에 걸맞은 갖은 부속물까지 갖추고 사람들 앞에서 광채를 발하자 비로소 터져 나왔다."]], [["The flame, which had before lain in embryo, now burst forth.", "전에는 알 속에 잠들어 있던 그 불길이 이제 와락 타올랐다."], ["Jenny had, by her learning, increased her own pride, which none of her neighbours were kind enough to feed with the honour she seemed to demand; and now, instead of respect and adoration, she gained nothing but hatred and abuse by her finery.", "제니는 학식으로 제 자긍심을 한껏 키워 놓았건만, 이웃 가운데 누구 하나 그녀가 요구하는 듯한 그 명예로 그 자긍심을 먹여 줄 만큼 친절하지 않았다. 그리하여 이제 그녀는 그 호사스러운 차림으로 존경과 흠모는커녕 미움과 욕설만 얻었다."], ["The whole parish declared she could not come honestly by such things; and parents, instead of wishing their daughters the same, felicitated themselves that their children had them not.", "온 교구가 그녀더러 그런 것들을 정직하게 얻었을 리 없다고 떠들어 댔고, 부모들은 제 딸도 그런 것을 가지기를 바라기는커녕, 제 자식이 그런 것을 가지지 않았음을 다행으로 여겼다."]], [["Hence, perhaps, it was, that the good woman first mentioned the name of this poor girl to Mrs Wilkins; but there was another circumstance that confirmed the latter in her suspicion; for Jenny had lately been often at Mr Allworthy's house. She had officiated as nurse to Miss Bridget, in a violent fit of illness, and had sat up many nights with that lady; besides which, she had been seen there the very day before Mr Allworthy's return, by Mrs Wilkins herself, though that sagacious person had not at first conceived any suspicion of her on that account: for, as she herself said, “She had always esteemed Jenny as a very sober girl (though indeed she knew very little of her), and had rather suspected some of those wanton trollops, who gave themselves airs, because, forsooth, they thought themselves handsome.”", "아마도 그래서, 앞서 말한 그 마음씨 좋은 여자가 이 가엾은 처녀의 이름을 윌킨스 부인에게 처음 입에 올렸을 것이다. 그러나 윌킨스 부인의 의심을 굳히게 한 또 다른 사정이 있었다. 제니가 근래에 올워디 씨 댁을 자주 드나들었던 것이다. 그녀는 브리짓 아가씨가 심한 병을 앓을 때 간병인 노릇을 하며 여러 밤을 그 아가씨 곁에서 지새웠고, 그뿐 아니라 올워디 씨가 돌아오기 바로 전날에도 그곳에 있는 모습을 다름 아닌 윌킨스 부인 자신이 보았던 것이다. 다만 그 눈치 빠른 인물도 처음에는 그 일로 그녀를 의심할 생각을 품지 않았다. 그녀 자신이 말했듯, \"나는 늘 제니를 아주 얌전한 처녀로 여겨 왔거든요(사실 그녀에 대해 아는 거라곤 거의 없었지만요). 그보다는 잘난 척 거들먹대는 그 음탕한 계집애들 가운데 하나가 아닐까 의심했죠. 제깟 것들이 어디 예쁘다고 그리 으스대니 말이에요.\" 하고 말이다."]], [["Jenny was now summoned to appear in person before Mrs Deborah, which she immediately did.", "이제 제니는 데버라 부인 앞에 직접 출두하라는 호출을 받았고, 곧바로 그리했다."], ["When Mrs Deborah, putting on the gravity of a judge, with somewhat more than his austerity, began an oration with the words, “You audacious strumpet!” in which she proceeded rather to pass sentence on the prisoner than to accuse her.", "그러자 데버라 부인은 재판관의 근엄함에다 그 이상의 매서움까지 얹어, \"이 뻔뻔한 갈보 같으니!\"라는 말로 일장 연설을 시작했는데, 그 연설에서 그녀는 죄수를 고발한다기보다 차라리 선고를 내리는 쪽으로 나아갔다."]], [["Though Mrs Deborah was fully satisfied of the guilt of Jenny, from the reasons above shewn, it is possible Mr Allworthy might have required some stronger evidence to have convicted her; but she saved her accusers any such trouble, by freely confessing the whole fact with which she was charged.", "데버라 부인은 앞서 보인 까닭들로 제니의 죄를 굳게 확신하고 있었지만, 올워디 씨라면 그녀에게 유죄를 선고하기 위해 좀 더 확실한 증거를 요구했을지도 모른다. 그러나 제니는 고발당한 그 일 전부를 순순히 자백함으로써, 자신을 단죄하려는 이들의 그러한 수고를 덜어 주었다."]], [["This confession, though delivered rather in terms of contrition, as it appeared, did not at all mollify Mrs Deborah, who now pronounced a second judgment against her, in more opprobrious language than before; nor had it any better success with the bystanders, who were now grown very numerous. Many of them cried out, “They thought what madam's silk gown would end in;” others spoke sarcastically of her learning. Not a single female was present but found some means of expressing her abhorrence of poor Jenny, who bore all very patiently, except the malice of one woman, who reflected upon her person, and tossing up her nose, said, “The man must have a good stomach who would give silk gowns for such sort of trumpery!” Jenny replied to this with a bitterness which might have surprized a judicious person, who had observed the tranquillity with which she bore all the affronts to her chastity; but her patience was perhaps tired out, for this is a virtue which is very apt to be fatigued by exercise.", "이 자백은 보아하니 차라리 뉘우치는 말투로 전해졌건만, 데버라 부인의 마음을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 못했다. 그녀는 이제 전보다 더 모욕적인 말투로 두 번째 선고를 그녀에게 내렸다. 어느덧 매우 많아진 구경꾼들에게도 그 자백은 더 나은 효과를 거두지 못했다. 그들 중 많은 이가 \"마님 비단 가운이 어떻게 끝장날지 다 알아봤다니까.\" 하고 소리쳤고, 더러는 그녀의 학식을 두고 비아냥거렸다. 거기 있던 여자치고 가엾은 제니를 향한 혐오를 어떻게든 드러내지 않는 이가 없었다. 제니는 그 모든 것을 아주 참을성 있게 견뎌 냈지만, 한 여자의 악의만은 예외였다. 그 여자는 그녀의 인물을 들먹이며 콧대를 치켜세우고는 \"이런 시시한 것한테 비단 가운을 내주는 걸 보면, 그 사내는 어지간히 비위가 좋은 모양이지!\" 하고 말했다. 이에 제니는 신중한 사람이라면 놀랄 만한 신랄함으로 받아쳤다. 자신의 정조를 향한 온갖 모욕을 그토록 태연하게 견뎌 내던 그 모습을 지켜본 사람이라면 말이다. 하지만 그녀의 인내심이 어쩌면 바닥났던 것이리라. 인내란 쓰면 쓸수록 쉬이 지치는 미덕이니 말이다."]], [["Mrs Deborah having succeeded beyond her hopes in her inquiry, returned with much triumph, and, at the appointed hour, made a faithful report to Mr Allworthy, who was much surprized at the relation; for he had heard of the extraordinary parts and improvements of this girl, whom he intended to have given in marriage, together with a small living, to a neighbouring curate.", "데버라 부인은 그 탐문에서 기대 이상의 성과를 거두고 의기양양하게 돌아와, 정해진 시각에 올워디 씨에게 충실히 보고를 올렸다. 그는 그 이야기에 몹시 놀랐다."], ["His concern, therefore, on this occasion, was at least equal to the satisfaction which appeared in Mrs Deborah, and to many readers may seem much more reasonable.", "그 처녀의 남다른 재주와 향상에 대해 익히 들어 알고 있었던 데다, 그녀를 이웃의 어느 부목사에게 자그마한 성직록과 함께 시집보낼 작정이었던 까닭이다. 그러니 이 일을 두고 그가 느낀 근심은 적어도 데버라 부인에게서 엿보인 흡족함만큼은 되었고, 많은 독자에게는 그쪽이 훨씬 더 이치에 맞아 보일 것이다."]], [["Miss Bridget blessed herself, and said, “For her part, she should never hereafter entertain a good opinion of any woman.”", "브리짓 아가씨는 성호를 그으며 말했다. \"나로서는, 이제부터 어떤 여자에 대해서도 두 번 다시 좋게 보지 않을 거예요.\""], ["For Jenny before this had the happiness of being much in her good graces also.", "제니는 이 일이 있기 전까지 그녀의 총애 또한 듬뿍 받는 행운을 누렸던 것이다."]], [["The prudent housekeeper was again dispatched to bring the unhappy culprit before Mr Allworthy, in order, not as it was hoped by some, and expected by all, to be sent to the house of correction, but to receive wholesome admonition and reproof; which those who relish that kind of instructive writing may peruse in the next chapter.", "신중한 가정부는 다시 한번 그 가엾은 죄인을 올워디 씨 앞으로 데려오라는 명을 받고 떠났다. 다만 더러는 바라고 모두는 으레 그러리라 짐작한 대로 교화소로 보내기 위함이 아니라, 유익한 훈계와 꾸지람을 받게 하기 위함이었다. 그런 종류의 교훈적인 글을 즐기는 이라면, 다음 장에서 그것을 찬찬히 읽어 볼 수 있을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["Containing such grave matter, that the reader cannot laugh once through the whole chapter, unless peradventure he should laugh at the author.", "너무도 무거운 내용을 담고 있어, 독자는 이 장을 통틀어 한 번도 웃을 수 없을 것이다. 혹 작가를 비웃는다면 또 모를까."]], [["When Jenny appeared, Mr Allworthy took her into his study, and spoke to her as follows: “You know, child, it is in my power as a magistrate, to punish you very rigorously for what you have done; and you will, perhaps, be the more apt to fear I should execute that power, because you have in a manner laid your sins at my door.", "제니가 모습을 나타내자, 올워디 씨는 그녀를 자기 서재로 데려가 다음과 같이 말했다. \"얘야, 너도 알겠지만, 나는 치안판사로서 네가 저지른 일에 대해 너를 아주 엄하게 벌할 권한을 쥐고 있다. 그리고 너는 어쩌면 내가 그 권한을 실제로 행사하지 않을까 더 두려워할지도 모르겠구나. 어떤 의미에서는 네가 네 죄를 내 문 앞에 갖다 놓은 셈이니 말이다."]], [["“But, perhaps, this is one reason which hath determined me to act in a milder manner with you: for, as no private resentment should ever influence a magistrate, I will be so far from considering your having deposited the infant in my house as an aggravation of your offence, that I will suppose, in your favour, this to have proceeded from a natural affection to your child, since you might have some hopes to see it thus better provided for than was in the power of yourself, or its wicked father, to provide for it. I should indeed have been highly offended with you had you exposed the little wretch in the manner of some inhuman mothers, who seem no less to have abandoned their humanity, than to have parted with their chastity. It is the other part of your offence, therefore, upon which I intend to admonish you, I mean the violation of your chastity;--a crime, however lightly it may be treated by debauched persons, very heinous in itself, and very dreadful in its consequences.", "\"하지만 어쩌면 바로 그 점이, 내가 너를 좀 더 너그럽게 대하기로 마음먹은 한 가지 까닭일지도 모른다. 사사로운 원한이 치안판사를 좌우해서는 결코 안 되는 법이니, 나는 네가 그 아이를 내 집에 두고 간 일을 네 죄를 무겁게 하는 사유로 여기기는커녕, 오히려 네게 유리하게 헤아릴 생각이다. 즉 그것이 네 아이에 대한 자연스러운 정에서 비롯된 것이라 짐작하겠다는 말이다. 너 자신이나 그 못된 아비가 마련해 줄 수 있는 것보다 이렇게 하는 편이 아이를 더 잘 보살펴 줄 수 있으리라는 어떤 희망을 품었을 테니 말이다. 만일 네가 그 가엾은 것을, 정조를 버린 것 못지않게 인간다움마저 저버린 듯 보이는 어떤 비정한 어미들처럼 내다 버렸더라면, 나는 진실로 너에게 크게 노여워했을 것이다. 그러니 내가 너에게 타이르려는 것은 네 죄의 다른 한 부분, 다시 말해 네 정조를 더럽힌 일이다. 방탕한 자들은 그것을 아무리 가볍게 여길지 몰라도, 그 자체로 몹시 흉악하며 그 결과 또한 몹시 무서운 죄다."]], [["“The heinous nature of this offence must be sufficiently apparent to every Christian, inasmuch as it is committed in defiance of the laws of our religion, and of the express commands of Him who founded that religion.", "\"이 죄의 흉악한 본질은 모든 그리스도인에게 충분히 분명할 것이다. 그것은 우리 종교의 율법을, 그리고 그 종교를 세우신 분의 명백한 계명을 거역하여 저지른 일이기 때문이다."]], [["“And here its consequences may well be argued to be dreadful; for what can be more so, than to incur the divine displeasure, by the breach of the divine commands; and that in an instance against which the highest vengeance is specifically denounced?", "\"그리고 여기서 그 결과가 무섭다고 충분히 논할 수 있다. 신의 계명을 어겨 신의 노여움을 사는 것보다 더 무서운 일이 어디 있겠느냐. 더구나 가장 준엄한 응징이 콕 집어 선포된 그런 일에서라면 말이다."]], [["“But these things, though too little, I am afraid, regarded, are so plain, that mankind, however they may want to be reminded, can never need information on this head.", "\"하지만 이런 것들은, 안타깝게도 너무 가볍게 여겨지긴 해도 워낙 명백한 일이라, 사람들이 다시 일깨워질 필요는 있을지언정 새로 알아야 할 것은 없다."], ["A hint, therefore, to awaken your sense of this matter, shall suffice; for I would inspire you with repentance, and not drive you to desperation.", "그러니 이 일에 대한 네 분별을 일깨우기 위한 한마디면 충분하겠다. 나는 네게 회개의 마음을 불어넣으려는 것이지, 절망으로 내몰려는 것이 아니니까."]], [["“There are other consequences, not indeed so dreadful or replete with horror as this; and yet such, as, if attentively considered, must, one would think, deter all of your sex at least from the commission of this crime.", "\"이 죄에는 또 다른 결과들도 있다. 앞서 말한 것만큼 무섭거나 공포로 가득하지는 않으나, 그래도 곰곰이 헤아려 보면 적어도 너희 여인들이라면 누구라도 이 죄를 저지르지 못하게 막아 줄 만한 것들이다."]], [["“For by it you are rendered infamous, and driven, like lepers of old, out of society; at least, from the society of all but wicked and reprobate persons; for no others will associate with you.", "\"이 죄로 인해 너는 오명을 뒤집어쓰고, 옛날의 나병 환자들처럼 사회 밖으로 내쫓긴다. 적어도 사악하고 타락한 자들을 빼고는 그 누구와도 어울리지 못하게 되는 것이다. 다른 이들은 결코 너와 함께하려 하지 않을 테니 말이다."]], [["“If you have fortunes, you are hereby rendered incapable of enjoying them; if you have none, you are disabled from acquiring any, nay almost of procuring your sustenance; for no persons of character will receive you into their houses. Thus you are often driven by necessity itself into a state of shame and misery, which unavoidably ends in the destruction of both body and soul.", "\"재산이 있다 한들, 너는 그것을 누릴 자격을 잃는다. 재산이 없다면, 새로 얻을 길조차 막힌다. 아니, 입에 풀칠할 양식을 구하는 것마저 거의 불가능해진다. 평판 있는 사람이라면 누구도 너를 제 집에 들이지 않을 테니까. 그리하여 너는 흔히 그 궁핍 자체에 떠밀려 수치와 비참의 처지에 빠지고, 그것은 결국 몸과 영혼 둘 다의 파멸로 어쩔 수 없이 끝나고 만다."]], [["“Can any pleasure compensate these evils?", "\"어떤 쾌락이 이런 해악들을 보상할 수 있겠느냐?"], ["Can any temptation have sophistry and delusion strong enough to persuade you to so simple a bargain?", "어떤 유혹이 이토록 어리석은 거래를 받아들이도록 너를 구슬릴 만큼 강한 궤변과 기만을 갖출 수 있겠느냐?"], ["Or can any carnal appetite so overpower your reason, or so totally lay it asleep, as to prevent your flying with affright and terror from a crime which carries such punishment always with it?", "또 어떤 육체의 욕망이 너의 이성을 그토록 압도하거나 그토록 깊이 잠재울 수 있어, 이런 벌을 늘 달고 다니는 죄로부터 네가 놀라 두려워하며 달아나는 것을 막을 수 있겠느냐?"]], [["“How base and mean must that woman be, how void of that dignity of mind, and decent pride, without which we are not worthy the name of human creatures, who can bear to level herself with the lowest animal, and to sacrifice all that is great and noble in her, all her heavenly part, to an appetite which she hath in common with the vilest branch of the creation!", "\"가장 천한 짐승과 자신을 같은 자리에 끌어내리고, 그 안의 위대하고 고귀한 모든 것, 그 천상의 부분 전부를, 창조물 가운데 가장 비천한 무리와 똑같이 지닌 한낱 욕망에 제물로 바칠 수 있는 여인이라면, 그 여인은 얼마나 비루하고 천박하며, 마음의 그 품위와 단정한 자긍심—그것이 없다면 우리는 인간이라 불릴 자격조차 없는—이 얼마나 텅 비어 있겠느냐!"], ["For no woman, sure, will plead the passion of love for an excuse.", "정녕 어떤 여인도 사랑의 정열을 핑계로 삼지는 못하리라."], ["This would be to own herself the mere tool and bubble of the man.", "그것은 곧 자신이 남자의 한낱 도구이자 거품에 지나지 않음을 자인하는 꼴이니까."], ["Love, however barbarously we may corrupt and pervert its meaning, as it is a laudable, is a rational passion, and can never be violent but when reciprocal; for though the Scripture bids us love our enemies, it means not with that fervent love which we naturally bear towards our friends; much less that we should sacrifice to them our lives, and what ought to be dearer to us, our innocence.", "사랑이란, 우리가 아무리 야만스럽게 그 뜻을 더럽히고 비틀어 놓는다 해도, 칭송받을 만한 것인 동시에 이성적인 정열이며, 서로 주고받는 사이가 아니고서는 결코 격렬할 수 없는 것이다. 성경이 원수를 사랑하라 이른다 해도, 그것은 우리가 벗에게 자연스레 품는 그 뜨거운 사랑을 뜻하는 것이 아니며, 하물며 그들을 위해 우리 목숨을, 또 우리에게 그보다 더 귀해야 할 우리의 순결을 제물로 바치라는 뜻은 더더욱 아니다."], ["Now in what light, but that of an enemy, can a reasonable woman regard the man who solicits her to entail on herself all the misery I have described to you, and who would purchase to himself a short, trivial, contemptible pleasure, so greatly at her expense!", "그러니 분별 있는 여인이라면, 내가 너에게 들려준 그 모든 비참을 스스로 짊어지도록 꾀어내고, 그토록 큰 그녀의 대가를 치르게 하여 자신은 짧고 하찮고 경멸스러운 쾌락을 사들이려는 남자를, 원수가 아니고서 무엇으로 여길 수 있겠느냐!"], ["For, by the laws of custom, the whole shame, with all its dreadful consequences, falls intirely upon her.", "관습의 법도에 따르면, 그 모든 무서운 결과를 동반한 수치는 오롯이 여인에게만 떨어지기 때문이다."], ["Can love, which always seeks the good of its object, attempt to betray a woman into a bargain where she is so greatly to be the loser?", "언제나 그 대상의 이로움을 구하는 사랑이, 여인이 이토록 크게 손해 볼 거래로 그녀를 배신해 빠뜨리려 들 수 있겠느냐?"], ["If such corrupter, therefore, should have the impudence to pretend a real affection for her, ought not the woman to regard him not only as an enemy, but as the worst of all enemies, a false, designing, treacherous, pretended friend, who intends not only to debauch her body, but her understanding at the same time?”", "그러니 그런 타락한 자가 그녀에게 진정한 애정을 품은 척 뻔뻔하게 굴거든, 여인은 그를 한낱 원수로뿐만 아니라 모든 원수 가운데 가장 못된 원수, 곧 거짓되고 음흉하며 배신을 일삼는, 벗인 척하는 자로 여겨야 하지 않겠느냐? 그자는 그녀의 몸뿐 아니라 그녀의 분별까지 동시에 망쳐 놓으려는 자이니 말이다.\""]], [["Here Jenny expressing great concern, Allworthy paused a moment, and then proceeded: “I have talked thus to you, child, not to insult you for what is past and irrevocable, but to caution and strengthen you for the future. Nor should I have taken this trouble, but from some opinion of your good sense, notwithstanding the dreadful slip you have made; and from some hopes of your hearty repentance, which are founded on the openness and sincerity of your confession. If these do not deceive me, I will take care to convey you from this scene of your shame, where you shall, by being unknown, avoid the punishment which, as I have said, is allotted to your crime in this world; and I hope, by repentance, you will avoid the much heavier sentence denounced against it in the other. Be a good girl the rest of your days, and want shall be no motive to your going astray; and, believe me, there is more pleasure, even in this world, in an innocent and virtuous life, than in one debauched and vicious.", "여기서 제니가 깊은 괴로움을 내비치자, 올워디는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 다시 이어 갔다. \"얘야, 내가 네게 이렇게 말한 것은, 이미 지나가 돌이킬 수 없는 일로 너를 모욕하려는 게 아니라, 앞날을 위해 너를 일깨우고 굳세게 하려는 것이다. 또 네가 저지른 그 무서운 잘못에도 불구하고 너의 분별을 어느 정도 믿는 마음이 없었다면, 그리고 너의 솔직하고 진실한 고백에 바탕을 둔, 네가 진심으로 뉘우치리라는 어떤 희망이 없었다면, 나는 이런 수고를 하지도 않았을 것이다. 만일 이런 믿음이 나를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 나는 네가 이 수치의 무대를 벗어나도록 손써 줄 것이다. 그곳에서 너는 아무에게도 알려지지 않은 채, 앞서 말했듯 이 세상에서 네 죄에 매겨진 벌을 피할 수 있을 것이다. 그리고 회개를 통해, 저세상에서 그 죄에 선포된 훨씬 더 무거운 형벌도 면하기를 바란다. 남은 평생 착하게 살아가거라. 그러면 궁핍이 너를 옆길로 빠지게 할 빌미가 되지 않을 것이다. 그리고 내 말을 믿어라. 이 세상에서조차, 순결하고 덕스러운 삶에는 방탕하고 사악한 삶보다 더 큰 즐거움이 있느니라."]], [["“As to your child, let no thoughts concerning it molest you; I will provide for it in a better manner than you can ever hope.", "\"네 아이에 대해서는, 어떤 근심도 너를 괴롭히지 못하게 하여라. 내가 네가 바랄 수 있는 것보다 더 나은 방식으로 그 아이를 보살펴 주마."], ["And now nothing remains but that you inform me who was the wicked man that seduced you; for my anger against him will be much greater than you have experienced on this occasion.”", "이제 남은 일이라곤, 너를 유혹한 그 못된 자가 누구인지 내게 알려 주는 것뿐이다. 그자를 향한 나의 노여움은 네가 이 일로 겪은 것보다 훨씬 클 테니 말이다.\""]], [["Jenny now lifted her eyes from the ground, and with a modest look and decent voice thus began:--", "이제 제니는 땅에 떨군 눈을 들어 올리고, 조신한 표정과 단정한 목소리로 이렇게 입을 열었다."]], [["“To know you, sir, and not love your goodness, would be an argument of total want of sense or goodness in any one.", "\"나리를 알면서도 그 선하심을 사랑하지 않는다면, 그것은 누구에게든 분별이나 선함이 송두리째 결여되어 있다는 증거일 것입니다. 제 경우라면 그것은 더없는 배은망덕이 되겠지요."], ["In me it would amount to the highest ingratitude, not to feel, in the most sensible manner, the great degree of goodness you have been pleased to exert on this occasion.", "이번 일에서 나리께서 기꺼이 베풀어 주신 그 크나큰 선하심을 가장 절실한 마음으로 느끼지 못한다면 말입니다. 지나간 일에 대한 제 뉘우침에 관해서는, 나리께서 제가 다시 얼굴 붉히지 않도록 되풀이를 아껴 주시리라 압니다."], ["As to my concern for what is past, I know you will spare my blushes the repetition.", "앞으로의 제 행실이, 지금 제가 입으로 늘어놓는 어떤 다짐보다도 제 마음을 훨씬 잘 드러내 줄 것입니다."], ["My future conduct will much better declare my sentiments than any professions I can now make.", "나리, 감히 한 말씀 올리자면, 저는 나리께서 말씀 끝에 베풀어 주신 그 너그러운 제안보다도 나리의 충고를 훨씬 고맙게 받아들이고 있습니다. 나리께서 친히 말씀하셨듯, 그것은 제 분별을 인정해 주시는 표시이기 때문입니다.\""], ["I beg leave to assure you, sir, that I take your advice much kinder than your generous offer with which you concluded it; for, as you are pleased to say, sir, it is an instance of your opinion of my understanding.”--Here her tears flowing apace, she stopped a few moments, and then proceeded thus:--“Indeed, sir, your kindness overcomes me; but I will endeavour to deserve this good opinion: for if I have the understanding you are so kindly pleased to allow me, such advice cannot be thrown away upon me.", "여기서 그녀의 눈물이 빠르게 흘러내려, 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이렇게 이어 갔다. \"참으로, 나리, 나리의 인자하심이 저를 압도합니다."], ["I thank you, sir, heartily, for your intended kindness to my poor helpless child: he is innocent, and I hope will live to be grateful for all the favours you shall show him.", "하지만 저는 이 좋은 평가를 받을 자격을 갖추도록 애쓰겠습니다. 나리께서 그토록 다정하게 인정해 주시는 그 분별이 제게 있다면, 그런 충고가 제게 헛되이 던져질 리 없으니까요."], ["But now, sir, I must on my knees entreat you not to persist in asking me to declare the father of my infant.", "제 가엾고 의지할 데 없는 아이에게 베풀고자 하시는 인자하심에 대해, 나리, 진심으로 감사드립니다. 그 아이는 죄가 없으며, 살아남아 나리께서 베풀어 주실 모든 은혜에 감사할 줄 아는 사람이 되기를 바랍니다."], ["I promise you faithfully you shall one day know; but I am under the most solemn ties and engagements of honour, as well as the most religious vows and protestations, to conceal his name at this time.", "하지만 이제, 나리, 무릎 꿇고 간청드리니, 제 아이의 아비가 누구인지 밝히라고 계속 다그치지는 말아 주십시오. 언젠가는 반드시 나리께서 아시게 되리라 굳게 약속드립니다."], ["And I know you too well, to think you would desire I should sacrifice either my honour or my religion.”", "그러나 저는 지금 그 이름을 감추기로, 가장 엄숙한 명예의 약속과 맹세에, 또한 가장 경건한 서원과 다짐에 묶여 있습니다. 그리고 저는 나리를 너무도 잘 알기에, 나리께서 제 명예든 제 신앙이든 제물로 바치기를 바라시리라고는 생각하지 않습니다.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy, whom the least mention of those sacred words was sufficient to stagger, hesitated a moment before he replied, and then told her, she had done wrong to enter into such engagements to a villain; but since she had, he could not insist on her breaking them.", "그 거룩한 말이 조금만 언급되어도 마음이 흔들리고 마는 올워디 씨는 대답하기 전에 잠시 머뭇거리더니, 이윽고 그녀에게 말했다. 악당에게 그런 약속을 한 것은 잘못한 일이지만, 이미 그리했으니 그것을 깨라고 우길 수는 없다고."], ["He said, it was not from a motive of vain curiosity he had inquired, but in order to punish the fellow; at least, that he might not ignorantly confer favours on the undeserving.", "그가 물은 것은 부질없는 호기심에서가 아니라 그 사내를 벌하기 위함이며, 적어도 자격 없는 자에게 모르고 은혜를 베푸는 일이 없도록 하기 위함이라고 말했다."]], [["As to these points, Jenny satisfied him by the most solemn assurances, that the man was entirely out of his reach; and was neither subject to his power, nor in any probability of becoming an object of his goodness.", "이 점들에 관하여, 제니는 가장 엄숙한 다짐으로 그를 안심시켰다. 그 남자는 그가 닿을 수 있는 범위에서 완전히 벗어나 있으며, 그의 권한에 매여 있지도 않고, 그의 선의의 대상이 될 가망도 전혀 없다고 말이다."]], [["The ingenuity of this behaviour had gained Jenny so much credit with this worthy man, that he easily believed what she told him; for as she had disdained to excuse herself by a lie, and had hazarded his further displeasure in her present situation, rather than she would forfeit her honour or integrity by betraying another, he had but little apprehensions that she would be guilty of falsehood towards himself.", "이 솔직한 태도 덕분에 제니는 이 훌륭한 사람에게 큰 신뢰를 얻었으므로, 그는 그녀가 한 말을 어렵잖게 믿어 주었다. 그녀가 거짓말로 자신을 변명하기를 떳떳하게 거부하고, 또 다른 이를 팔아 자기 명예나 정직을 저버리기보다는 차라리 지금 처지에서 그의 노여움을 더 사는 위험을 무릅썼으니, 그녀가 그 자신에게 거짓을 저지르지는 않으리라는 우려가 그에게는 거의 없었던 것이다."]], [["He therefore dismissed her with assurances that he would very soon remove her out of the reach of that obloquy she had incurred; concluding with some additional documents, in which he recommended repentance, saying, “Consider, child, there is one still to reconcile yourself to, whose favour is of much greater importance to you than mine.”", "그리하여 그는, 그녀가 뒤집어쓴 그 오명이 닿지 못할 곳으로 그녀를 머잖아 옮겨 주겠노라 다짐하며 그녀를 돌려보냈다. 끝으로 그는 회개를 권하는 몇 마디를 덧붙이며 이렇게 마무리했다. \"얘야, 생각해 보아라. 너에게는 아직 화해해야 할 또 다른 분이 계시니, 그분의 은총은 나의 것보다 너에게 훨씬 더 중요하니라.\""]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["A dialogue between Mesdames Bridget and Deborah; containing more amusement, but less instruction, than the former.", "브리짓과 데버러 두 부인 사이의 대화. 앞 장보다 재미는 더하나 교훈은 덜한 내용을 담음."]], [["When Mr Allworthy had retired to his study with Jenny Jones, as hath been seen, Mrs Bridget, with the good housekeeper, had betaken themselves to a post next adjoining to the said study; whence, through the conveyance of a keyhole, they sucked in at their ears the instructive lecture delivered by Mr Allworthy, together with the answers of Jenny, and indeed every other particular which passed in the last chapter.", "앞서 본 대로 올워디 씨가 제니 존스를 데리고 서재로 물러간 사이, 브리짓 부인은 마음씨 좋은 가정부와 함께 바로 그 서재에 잇닿은 자리로 가 진을 쳤다. 거기서 두 사람은 열쇠 구멍이 전해 주는 소리를 따라, 올워디 씨가 베푼 그 교훈적인 훈계며 제니의 대답이며, 실로 앞 장에서 오간 온갖 자질구레한 이야기까지 귀로 빨아들였다."]], [["This hole in her brother's study-door was indeed as well known to Mrs Bridget, and had been as frequently applied to by her, as the famous hole in the wall was by Thisbe of old.", "오라비의 서재 문에 난 이 구멍은 브리짓 부인에게 너무도 익숙한 것이어서, 옛날 티스베가 그 유명한 담벼락 구멍을 드나든 만큼이나 자주 부인이 이용해 온 터였다."], ["This served to many good purposes.", "이 구멍은 여러모로 쓸모가 많았다."], ["For by such means Mrs Bridget became often acquainted with her brother's inclinations, without giving him the trouble of repeating them to her.", "이런 수단을 통해 브리짓 부인은 오라비가 굳이 거듭 일러 주는 수고를 끼치지 않고도 그의 생각을 자주 알아챌 수 있었으니까."], ["It is true, some inconveniences attended this intercourse, and she had sometimes reason to cry out with Thisbe, in Shakspeare, “O, wicked, wicked wall!”", "물론 이 통로에는 더러 불편이 따랐고, 부인은 셰익스피어에 나오는 티스베처럼 \"오, 못된, 못된 담벼락아!\"라고 외치고 싶을 때도 있었다."], ["For as Mr Allworthy was a justice of peace, certain things occurred in examinations concerning bastards, and such like, which are apt to give great offence to the chaste ears of virgins, especially when they approach the age of forty, as was the case of Mrs Bridget.", "올워디 씨가 치안판사였던 까닭에, 사생아 따위에 관한 심문 자리에서는 처녀의 정숙한 귀를 몹시 거스를 만한 일들이 더러 벌어지곤 했기 때문이다. 더구나 그 처녀가 마흔 줄에 가까워지는 경우라면 더욱 그러한데, 바로 브리짓 부인이 그러했다."], ["However, she had, on such occasions, the advantage of concealing her blushes from the eyes of men; and De non apparentibus, et non existentibus eadem est ratio--in English, “When a woman is not seen to blush, she doth not blush at all.”", "그렇지만 이런 때 부인에게는 남자들의 눈을 피해 자신의 홍조를 감출 수 있다는 이점이 있었다. 그리고 \"나타나지 않는 것과 존재하지 않는 것은 같은 이치로 다룬다\"는 법언이 있으니, 우리말로 옮기면 \"여자가 얼굴 붉히는 것을 들키지 않으면, 그 여자는 아예 붉히지 않은 것이다\"라는 뜻이다."]], [["Both the good women kept strict silence during the whole scene between Mr Allworthy and the girl; but as soon as it was ended, and that gentleman was out of hearing, Mrs Deborah could not help exclaiming against the clemency of her master, and especially against his suffering her to conceal the father of the child, which she swore she would have out of her before the sun set.", "올워디 씨와 그 처녀 사이의 장면이 이어지는 내내 두 마음씨 좋은 여인은 입을 꼭 다물고 있었다. 그러나 그 장면이 끝나고 그 신사가 더는 들을 수 없는 거리로 사라지자마자, 데버러 부인은 주인의 너그러움에 대해, 특히 아이 아비를 감추도록 내버려 둔 처사에 대해 불평을 터뜨리지 않을 수 없었다. 부인은 해가 지기 전에 그 처녀에게서 기어이 아비의 이름을 캐내고야 말겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."]], [["At these words Mrs Bridget discomposed her features with a smile (a thing very unusual to her).", "이 말에 브리짓 부인은 얼굴을 풀며 미소를 지었다(그로서는 무척 드문 일이었다)."], ["Not that I would have my reader imagine, that this was one of those wanton smiles which Homer would have you conceive came from Venus, when he calls her the laughter-loving goddess; nor was it one of those smiles which Lady Seraphina shoots from the stage-box, and which Venus would quit her immortality to be able to equal.", "그렇다고 독자께서 이것을 호메로스가 베누스를 두고 웃음을 사랑하는 여신이라 부를 때 떠올리게 하려던 그런 음탕한 미소 가운데 하나라고 여기시지는 않기를 바란다. 또한 세라피나 부인이 무대 곁 특별석에서 쏘아 보내는, 베누스가 그것에 견줄 수만 있다면 제 불멸성마저 내놓을 법한 그런 미소 가운데 하나도 아니었다."], ["No, this was rather one of those smiles which might be supposed to have come from the dimpled cheeks of the august Tisiphone, or from one of the misses, her sisters.", "아니, 이것은 차라리 위엄 어린 티시포네의 보조개 팬 뺨에서나, 아니면 그 자매인 처녀들 가운데 하나에게서 나왔으리라 짐작할 만한 미소였다."]], [["With such a smile then, and with a voice sweet as the evening breeze of Boreas in the pleasant month of November, Mrs Bridget gently reproved the curiosity of Mrs Deborah; a vice with which it seems the latter was too much tainted, and which the former inveighed against with great bitterness, adding, “That, among all her faults, she thanked Heaven her enemies could not accuse her of prying into the affairs of other people.”", "그러니까 그런 미소를 띠고서, 그리고 화창한 십일월에 부는 보레아스의 저녁 산들바람처럼 달콤한 목소리로, 브리짓 부인은 데버러 부인의 호기심을 부드럽게 나무랐다. 아무래도 데버러 부인은 그 악덕에 너무 깊이 물든 듯했고, 브리짓 부인은 그것을 몹시 신랄하게 성토하면서 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"내게 온갖 허물이 있다 해도, 적들조차 내가 남의 일을 캐고 다닌다고는 탓하지 못할 테니, 그 점만은 하느님께 감사한다오.\""]], [["She then proceeded to commend the honour and spirit with which Jenny had acted.", "이어서 부인은 제니가 보여 준 명예로움과 기개를 칭찬하기 시작했다. 자기 고백의 진실함과 연인에 대한 충직함에 어떤 갸륵함이 있다는 오라비의 말에 동의하지 않을 수 없다고 했다."], ["She said, she could not help agreeing with her brother, that there was some merit in the sincerity of her confession, and in her integrity to her lover: that she had always thought her a very good girl, and doubted not but she had been seduced by some rascal, who had been infinitely more to blame than herself, and very probably had prevailed with her by a promise of marriage, or some other treacherous proceeding.", "부인은 늘 제니를 아주 착한 처녀로 여겨 왔으며, 분명 어떤 못된 놈에게 꾐을 당했을 것이고, 그놈이 제니보다 한없이 더 큰 잘못을 저질렀으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다고 했다. 또 십중팔구 그놈이 결혼하겠다는 약속이나 그밖의 무슨 음흉한 수작으로 제니를 넘어가게 했을 거라고 했다."]], [["This behaviour of Mrs Bridget greatly surprised Mrs Deborah; for this well-bred woman seldom opened her lips, either to her master or his sister, till she had first sounded their inclinations, with which her sentiments were always consonant.", "브리짓 부인의 이런 태도에 데버러 부인은 크게 놀랐다. 이 예의 바른 여인은 주인에게든 그 누이에게든 먼저 그들의 속내를 떠보지 않고는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않았고, 자기 생각은 늘 그들의 생각과 한목소리였기 때문이다."], ["Here, however, she thought she might have launched forth with safety; and the sagacious reader will not perhaps accuse her of want of sufficient forecast in so doing, but will rather admire with what wonderful celerity she tacked about, when she found herself steering a wrong course.", "그런데 이번만큼은 마음 놓고 거침없이 떠들어도 되겠다고 여겼던 것이다. 그렇다고 눈치 빠른 독자께서 데버러 부인이 그렇게 한 것을 두고 미리 헤아리는 분별이 모자랐다고 탓하지는 않으실 테고, 오히려 자기가 엉뚱한 방향으로 키를 잡았음을 깨달았을 때 얼마나 놀라운 민첩함으로 뱃머리를 돌리는지를 감탄하실 것이다."]], [["“Nay, madam,”", "\"아니죠, 마님.\""], ["said this able woman, and truly great politician, “I must own I cannot help admiring the girl's spirit, as well as your ladyship.", "이 유능한 여인, 참으로 위대한 책략가가 말했다. \"솔직히 말씀드려, 저도 마님처럼 그 처녀의 기개를 칭찬하지 않을 수 없네요."], ["And, as your ladyship says, if she was deceived by some wicked man, the poor wretch is to be pitied.", "그리고 마님 말씀대로, 그 애가 어떤 못된 사내에게 속았다면, 그 가엾은 것이 딱하기 짝이 없지요."], ["And to be sure, as your ladyship says, the girl hath always appeared like a good, honest, plain girl, and not vain of her face, forsooth, as some wanton husseys in the neighbourhood are.”", "그리고 정말이지, 마님 말씀대로, 그 처녀는 늘 착하고 정직하고 수수한 아이로 보였지, 이 동네 어떤 음탕한 계집들처럼 제 얼굴을 뽐내거나 하지는 않았으니까요.\""]], [["“You say true, Deborah,”", "\"네 말이 맞다, 데버러.\""], ["said Miss Bridget.", "브리짓 아가씨가 말했다."], ["“If the girl had been one of those vain trollops, of which we have too many in the parish, I should have condemned my brother for his lenity towards her.", "\"그 애가 우리 교구에 너무도 많은 그런 허영 떠는 잡것들 가운데 하나였다면, 나는 그 애를 너그럽게 대한 오라비를 나무랐을 게다. 며칠 전 교회에서 목덜미를 드러낸 농부 딸 둘을 보았는데, 정말이지 그 꼴에 충격을 받았다."], ["I saw two farmers' daughters at church, the other day, with bare necks.", "계집들이 사내를 낚으려고 미끼를 내거는 짓을 한다면, 그들이 어떤 험한 꼴을 당하든 알 바 아니지."], ["I protest they shocked me.", "나는 그런 것들이 끔찍하게 싫다."], ["If wenches will hang out lures for fellows, it is no matter what they suffer.", "차라리 그 얼굴이 천연두 자국으로 얽었더라면 그들에게 훨씬 나았을 게다. 하지만 솔직히 말해, 가엾은 제니에게서는 그런 음탕한 행실을 단 한 번도 본 적이 없다."], ["I detest such creatures; and it would be much better for them that their faces had been seamed with the smallpox; but I must confess, I never saw any of this wanton behaviour in poor Jenny: some artful villain, I am convinced, hath betrayed, nay perhaps forced her; and I pity the poor wretch with all my heart.”", "분명 어떤 교활한 악당이 그 애를 배신했거나, 어쩌면 강제로 욕보였을 게야. 나는 그 가엾은 것이 진심으로 가엾다.\""]], [["Mrs Deborah approved all these sentiments, and the dialogue concluded with a general and bitter invective against beauty, and with many compassionate considerations for all honest plain girls who are deluded by the wicked arts of deceitful men.", "데버러 부인은 이 모든 생각에 맞장구를 쳤고, 두 사람의 대화는 아름다움을 싸잡아 신랄하게 헐뜯는 것으로, 그리고 기만하는 사내들의 못된 술수에 속아 넘어가는 모든 정직하고 수수한 처녀들을 향한 갖은 가엾어하는 마음으로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["Containing matters which will surprize the reader.", "독자를 놀라게 할 일들을 담은 장."]], [["Jenny returned home well pleased with the reception she had met with from Mr Allworthy, whose indulgence to her she industriously made public; partly perhaps as a sacrifice to her own pride, and partly from the more prudent motive of reconciling her neighbours to her, and silencing their clamours.", "제니는 올워디 씨에게서 받은 대접에 흡족해하며 집으로 돌아왔고, 그가 자신에게 베푼 너그러움을 부지런히 사방에 알리고 다녔다. 어쩌면 그것은 한편으로는 자기 자존심을 채우려는 제물이었고, 다른 한편으로는 이웃들의 마음을 누그러뜨려 그들의 떠들썩한 비난을 잠재우려는 더 영리한 속셈이었을 것이다."]], [["But though this latter view, if she indeed had it, may appear reasonable enough, yet the event did not answer her expectation; for when she was convened before the justice, and it was universally apprehended that the house of correction would have been her fate, though some of the young women cryed out “It was good enough for her,” and diverted themselves with the thoughts of her beating hemp in a silk gown; yet there were many others who began to pity her condition: but when it was known in what manner Mr Allworthy had behaved, the tide turned against her. One said, “I'll assure you, madam hath had good luck.” A second cryed, “See what it is to be a favourite!” A third, “Ay, this comes of her learning.” Every person made some malicious comment or other on the occasion, and reflected on the partiality of the justice.", "그러나 만일 그녀가 정말 이 후자의 속셈을 품었다면 그것은 제법 이치에 맞아 보일지 몰라도, 막상 일은 그녀의 기대대로 풀리지 않았다. 그녀가 치안판사 앞에 불려 나갔을 때, 사람들은 누구나 그녀의 운명이 교화소행이리라 짐작했다. 몇몇 젊은 여자들은 \"그년한테 딱 알맞은 꼴이지\" 하고 소리치며, 비단 가운을 걸친 채 삼을 두드리는 그녀의 모습을 상상하며 즐거워했다. 그래도 그녀의 처지를 가엾게 여기기 시작한 사람들도 많았다. 하지만 올워디 씨가 어떻게 처신했는지가 알려지자, 분위기는 그녀에게 등을 돌렸다. 한 사람은 이렇게 말했다. \"정말이지, 마님께서는 운도 참 좋으셔.\" 두 번째 사람은 외쳤다. \"총애받는다는 게 어떤 건지 보라고!\" 세 번째 사람은 말했다. \"그럼, 이게 다 그년이 글 배운 덕이지.\" 사람들은 저마다 이 일을 두고 짓궂은 말을 한마디씩 보태며, 치안판사의 편파성을 비꼬았다."]], [["The behaviour of these people may appear impolitic and ungrateful to the reader, who considers the power and benevolence of Mr Allworthy.", "올워디 씨의 권세와 자비로움을 떠올리는 독자에게는, 이 사람들의 행태가 분별없고 배은망덕해 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 그의 권세로 말하자면, 그는 결코 그것을 휘두른 적이 없었다."], ["But as to his power, he never used it; and as to his benevolence, he exerted so much, that he had thereby disobliged all his neighbours; for it is a secret well known to great men, that, by conferring an obligation, they do not always procure a friend, but are certain of creating many enemies.", "또 그의 자비로움으로 말하자면, 그는 그것을 어찌나 많이 베풀었던지 그 바람에 이웃들 모두의 비위를 거슬러 놓고 말았다. 위대한 사람들이라면 잘 아는 한 가지 비밀이 있으니, 은혜를 베푼다고 해서 늘 벗을 얻는 것은 아니며 오히려 숱한 적을 만드는 일만큼은 틀림없다는 것이다."]], [["Jenny was, however, by the care and goodness of Mr Allworthy, soon removed out of the reach of reproach; when malice being no longer able to vent its rage on her, began to seek another object of its bitterness, and this was no less than Mr Allworthy, himself; for a whisper soon went abroad, that he himself was the father of the foundling child.", "그러나 제니는 올워디 씨의 보살핌과 호의 덕분에 머잖아 비난의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 옮겨졌다. 그리하여 악의가 더는 그녀에게 분풀이를 할 수 없게 되자, 그것은 또 다른 분풀이 대상을 찾기 시작했다. 그 대상은 다름 아닌 올워디 씨 본인이었다. 그가 바로 그 업둥이 아이의 아버지라는 수군거림이 곧 퍼져 나갔던 것이다."]], [["This supposition so well reconciled his conduct to the general opinion, that it met with universal assent; and the outcry against his lenity soon began to take another turn, and was changed into an invective against his cruelty to the poor girl.", "이 추측은 그의 처신을 사람들의 통념과 어찌나 그럴듯하게 맞아떨어지게 했는지, 너나없이 그 말에 동조했다. 그의 관대함을 향하던 비난은 곧 방향을 틀어, 가엾은 처녀를 향한 그의 잔인함을 성토하는 쪽으로 바뀌었다."], ["Very grave and good women exclaimed against men who begot children, and then disowned them.", "점잖고 선량한 부인네들은 아이를 낳아 놓고 모른 척하는 사내들을 두고 목청을 높였다."], ["Nor were there wanting some, who, after the departure of Jenny, insinuated that she was spirited away with a design too black to be mentioned, and who gave frequent hints that a legal inquiry ought to be made into the whole matter, and that some people should be forced to produce the girl.", "게다가 제니가 떠난 뒤로는, 그녀가 입에 담기조차 너무 시커먼 음모에 휘말려 어디론가 감쪽같이 빼돌려졌다고 넌지시 흘리는 자들도 있었고, 이 모든 일을 두고 법으로 조사를 벌여야 마땅하며 어떤 사람들은 그 처녀를 내놓도록 강제되어야 한다고 자꾸만 운을 떼는 자들도 있었다."]], [["These calumnies might have probably produced ill consequences, at the least might have occasioned some trouble, to a person of a more doubtful and suspicious character than Mr Allworthy was blessed with; but in his case they had no such effect; and, being heartily despised by him, they served only to afford an innocent amusement to the good gossips of the neighbourhood.", "이런 비방들은 올워디 씨가 복으로 지닌 성품보다 더 미덥지 못하고 의심받기 쉬운 사람에게라면 아마 나쁜 결과를 빚어냈을지도, 적어도 어떤 골칫거리쯤은 일으켰을지도 모른다. 그러나 그의 경우에는 그런 영향이 전혀 없었다. 그가 그것들을 진심으로 우습게 여겼기에, 비방들은 그저 동네의 선량한 수다쟁이들에게 죄 없는 심심풀이 거리나 되어 주었을 뿐이다."]], [["But as we cannot possibly divine what complection our reader may be of, and as it will be some time before he will hear any more of Jenny, we think proper to give him a very early intimation, that Mr Allworthy was, and will hereafter appear to be, absolutely innocent of any criminal intention whatever.", "그런데 우리로서는 독자가 어떤 기질의 사람인지 도무지 짐작할 길이 없고, 또 그가 제니에 관한 이야기를 다시 듣기까지는 한참이 걸릴 터이므로, 여기서 미리 일러두는 편이 좋겠다. 올워디 씨는 그 어떤 죄스러운 의도와도 전혀 무관했으며, 앞으로도 그러하리라는 점을 말이다."], ["He had indeed committed no other than an error in politics, by tempering justice with mercy, and by refusing to gratify the good-natured disposition of the mob,[*] with an object for their compassion to work on in the person of poor Jenny, whom, in order to pity, they desired to have seen sacrificed to ruin and infamy, by a shameful correction in Bridewell.", "그가 저지른 잘못이라곤 정치적인 실책 하나뿐이었으니, 정의에 자비를 섞었다는 것, 그리고 군중*의 그 선량한 기질을 만족시켜 주기를 거부했다는 것이다. 군중은 가엾은 제니를 동정의 대상으로 삼아 그 연민을 마음껏 풀고 싶어 했고, 정작 그녀를 가엾이 여기기 위해서는 그녀가 브라이드웰에서 수치스러운 교화를 받아 파멸과 오욕으로 내던져지는 꼴을 보고 싶어 했던 것이다."]], [["[*]Whenever this word occurs in our writings, it intends persons without virtue or sense, in all stations; and many of the highest rank are often meant by it.", "* 군중: 이 단어가 우리 글에 나올 때면, 그것은 지위 고하를 막론하고 덕도 분별도 없는 사람들을 가리키며, 흔히 가장 높은 신분의 사람들 가운데 상당수를 두고 하는 말이다."]], [["So far from complying with this their inclination, by which all hopes of reformation would have been abolished, and even the gate shut against her if her own inclinations should ever hereafter lead her to chuse the road of virtue, Mr Allworthy rather chose to encourage the girl to return thither by the only possible means; for too true I am afraid it is, that many women have become abandoned, and have sunk to the last degree of vice, by being unable to retrieve the first slip. This will be, I am afraid, always the case while they remain among their former acquaintance; it was therefore wisely done by Mr Allworthy, to remove Jenny to a place where she might enjoy the pleasure of reputation, after having tasted the ill consequences of losing it.", "이런 그들의 바람을 따랐더라면 그녀가 새사람이 될 모든 희망은 사라졌을 것이고, 훗날 그녀 자신의 마음이 덕의 길을 택하도록 이끈다 해도 그 문조차 닫혀 버렸을 것이다. 그러나 올워디 씨는 그러기는커녕, 그 처녀가 그 길로 되돌아오도록 단 하나 가능한 방법으로 북돋아 주는 쪽을 택했다. 안타깝지만 너무도 참된 사실은, 숱한 여인들이 첫 번째 실수를 만회하지 못한 탓에 타락하여 악의 마지막 나락까지 떨어지고 만다는 것이다. 그리고 안타깝게도 이런 일은, 그들이 옛 지인들 사이에 머무는 한 언제까지나 되풀이될 것이다. 그러므로 올워디 씨가 제니를, 평판을 잃은 쓰라린 결과를 맛본 뒤에라도 좋은 평판이라는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있는 곳으로 옮겨 준 것은 참으로 현명한 처사였다."]], [["To this place therefore, wherever it was, we will wish her a good journey, and for the present take leave of her, and of the little foundling her child, having matters of much higher importance to communicate to the reader.", "그러니 그곳이 어디든, 우리는 그녀가 그곳으로 무사히 가기를 빌어 주며, 지금으로서는 그녀와, 그녀의 아이인 그 어린 업둥이에게 작별을 고하기로 하자. 우리에게는 독자에게 전할, 훨씬 더 중대한 일들이 있으니 말이다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["The hospitality of Allworthy; with a short sketch of the characters of two brothers, a doctor and a captain, who were entertained by that gentleman.", "올워디 씨의 손님 대접, 그리고 그에게 환대를 받은 두 형제, 곧 한 사람은 의사이고 다른 한 사람은 대위인 두 인물에 대한 짧은 소묘."]], [["Neither Mr Allworthy's house, nor his heart, were shut against any part of mankind, but they were both more particularly open to men of merit.", "올워디 씨의 집도, 그의 마음도 그 누구에게든 닫혀 있지 않았으나, 특히 덕망 있는 사람들에게는 더욱 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["To say the truth, this was the only house in the kingdom where you was sure to gain a dinner by deserving it.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이 나라 안에서 그럴 자격을 갖춘 것만으로 식사 한 끼를 확실히 얻을 수 있는 집은 이곳뿐이었다."]], [["Above all others, men of genius and learning shared the principal place in his favour; and in these he had much discernment: for though he had missed the advantage of a learned education, yet, being blest with vast natural abilities, he had so well profited by a vigorous though late application to letters, and by much conversation with men of eminence in this way, that he was himself a very competent judge in most kinds of literature.", "무엇보다도 천재성과 학식을 갖춘 사람들이 그의 호의 가운데 가장 으뜸가는 자리를 차지했다. 그리고 이런 부류를 가려내는 데에 그는 안목이 상당했다. 비록 그는 정식 학문 교육의 혜택을 누리지 못했으나, 타고난 재능이 워낙 풍부한 데다, 뒤늦게나마 글공부에 힘차게 매진하고 또 이 방면에서 뛰어난 인물들과 자주 대화를 나눈 덕분에 큰 이득을 보아, 그 자신이 대부분의 문학 갈래에서 꽤나 유능한 감식가가 되어 있었다."]], [["It is no wonder that in an age when this kind of merit is so little in fashion, and so slenderly provided for, persons possessed of it should very eagerly flock to a place where they were sure of being received with great complaisance; indeed, where they might enjoy almost the same advantages of a liberal fortune as if they were entitled to it in their own right; for Mr Allworthy was not one of those generous persons who are ready most bountifully to bestow meat, drink, and lodging on men of wit and learning, for which they expect no other return but entertainment, instruction, flattery, and subserviency; in a word, that such persons should be enrolled in the number of domestics, without wearing their master's cloathes, or receiving wages.", "이런 종류의 재능이 거의 유행을 타지 못하고 또 그에 대한 대접도 변변치 못한 시대에, 그것을 지닌 사람들이 큰 호의로 맞아 줄 것이 분명한 곳으로 앞다투어 몰려드는 것은 조금도 이상한 일이 아니었다. 실로 그곳에서 그들은, 마치 자기 권리로 누릴 자격이 있기라도 한 것처럼, 넉넉한 재산이 주는 거의 같은 혜택을 누릴 수 있었기 때문이다. 올워디 씨는, 재치와 학식을 갖춘 사람들에게 고기와 술과 잠자리를 더없이 후하게 베풀면서도 그 대가로 오락과 가르침과 아첨과 굴종 말고는 아무것도 기대하지 않는 척하는, 그런 부류의 너그러운 사람이 아니었다. 한마디로, 그런 사람들이 주인의 옷을 입지도 않고 삯을 받지도 않으면서 하인의 명부에 이름을 올리게 만드는 그런 사람이 아니었던 것이다."]], [["On the contrary, every person in this house was perfect master of his own time: and as he might at his pleasure satisfy all his appetites within the restrictions only of law, virtue, and religion; so he might, if his health required, or his inclination prompted him to temperance, or even to abstinence, absent himself from any meals, or retire from them, whenever he was so disposed, without even a sollicitation to the contrary: for, indeed, such sollicitations from superiors always savour very strongly of commands. But all here were free from such impertinence, not only those whose company is in all other places esteemed a favour from their equality of fortune, but even those whose indigent circumstances make such an eleemosynary abode convenient to them, and who are therefore less welcome to a great man's table because they stand in need of it.", "오히려 이 집에서는 누구나 자기 시간의 완전한 주인이었다. 그래서 그는 법과 덕과 종교라는 제약 안에서만큼은 마음껏 자기 욕구를 채울 수 있었고, 또 건강이 요구하거나 절제를, 심지어 금욕을 마음먹게 되면, 언제든 내키는 대로 어떤 식사 자리든 빠지거나 도중에 물러날 수 있었다. 그러지 말라는 권유조차 받지 않으면서 말이다. 실로 윗사람의 그런 권유에는 늘 명령의 냄새가 짙게 배어 있는 법이다. 그러나 이곳에서는 모두가 그런 무례함에서 자유로웠다. 다른 어떤 곳에서라면 비슷한 재산 덕에 그 동석 자체가 호의로 여겨질 사람들뿐 아니라, 가난한 형편 탓에 이런 자선의 거처가 편리하기에, 바로 그 필요 때문에 도리어 대갓집 식탁에서 덜 환영받기 마련인 사람들까지도 그러했다."]], [["Among others of this kind was Dr Blifil, a gentleman who had the misfortune of losing the advantage of great talents by the obstinacy of a father, who would breed him to a profession he disliked. In obedience to this obstinacy the doctor had in his youth been obliged to study physic, or rather to say he studied it; for in reality books of this kind were almost the only ones with which he was unacquainted; and unfortunately for him, the doctor was master of almost every other science but that by which he was to get his bread; the consequence of which was, that the doctor at the age of forty had no bread to eat.", "이런 부류 가운데 블리필 박사라는 신사가 있었다. 그는 자기가 싫어하는 직업을 억지로 시키려 든 아버지의 고집 때문에, 큰 재능이 주는 이점을 잃어버리는 불운을 겪은 사람이었다. 그 고집에 복종하느라 박사는 젊은 시절 의학을 공부해야만 했다. 아니, 공부했다고 말해야 한다고 하는 편이 옳겠다. 실은 이런 종류의 책이야말로 그가 거의 유일하게 친숙하지 못한 분야였기 때문이다. 그에게 불행하게도 박사는 정작 밥벌이로 삼아야 할 그 학문만 빼고는 거의 모든 다른 학문에 통달해 있었다. 그 결과 박사는 마흔이 되도록 먹을 빵 한 조각 없는 처지가 되고 말았다."]], [["Such a person as this was certain to find a welcome at Mr Allworthy's table, to whom misfortunes were ever a recommendation, when they were derived from the folly or villany of others, and not of the unfortunate person himself.", "이런 사람이라면 올워디 씨의 식탁에서 환영받을 것이 분명했다. 올워디 씨에게는, 불운이 그 불운한 사람 자신의 어리석음이나 악행이 아니라 남들의 어리석음이나 악행에서 비롯된 것이기만 하다면, 언제나 그것이 곧 추천장이었기 때문이다."], ["Besides this negative merit, the doctor had one positive recommendation;--this was a great appearance of religion.", "이런 소극적인 미덕 말고도, 박사에게는 한 가지 적극적인 추천 거리가 있었다. 바로 신앙심이 깊어 보인다는 점이었다."], ["Whether his religion was real, or consisted only in appearance, I shall not presume to say, as I am not possessed of any touchstone which can distinguish the true from the false.", "그의 신앙이 진짜인지, 아니면 겉모습뿐인지는 내가 감히 말하지 않겠다. 참된 것과 거짓된 것을 가려낼 만한 시금석을 나는 가지고 있지 않으니 말이다."]], [["If this part of his character pleased Mr Allworthy, it delighted Miss Bridget.", "그의 인품 가운데 이런 면이 올워디 씨를 흡족하게 했다면, 브리짓 양은 아예 기쁘게 했다."], ["She engaged him in many religious controversies; on which occasions she constantly expressed great satisfaction in the doctor's knowledge, and not much less in the compliments which he frequently bestowed on her own.", "그녀는 그를 끌어들여 종교 논쟁을 여러 차례 벌였는데, 그럴 때마다 그녀는 박사의 해박함에 늘 큰 만족을 드러냈고, 또 그가 자신의 해박함에 자주 보내 주는 찬사에도 그에 못지않게 만족스러워했다."], ["To say the truth, she had read much English divinity, and had puzzled more than one of the neighbouring curates.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그녀는 영국 신학 서적을 많이 읽어, 이웃의 보좌 사제들 가운데 한둘이 아니라 여럿을 쩔쩔매게 한 바 있었다."], ["Indeed, her conversation was so pure, her looks so sage, and her whole deportment so grave and solemn, that she seemed to deserve the name of saint equally with her namesake, or with any other female in the Roman kalendar.", "실로 그녀의 대화는 어찌나 정결하고, 그 표정은 어찌나 현숙하며, 몸가짐 전체가 어찌나 진중하고 엄숙했던지, 같은 이름을 지닌 성녀와, 아니 로마 성인력에 오른 그 어떤 여인과도 똑같이 성녀라는 이름을 받을 만해 보였다."]], [["As sympathies of all kinds are apt to beget love, so experience teaches us that none have a more direct tendency this way than those of a religious kind between persons of different sexes.", "온갖 종류의 공감이 사랑을 낳기 쉽듯이, 서로 다른 성별의 사람들 사이에서 종교적 공감만큼 이쪽으로 곧장 기우는 것이 없다는 사실을 경험은 우리에게 일러 준다."], ["The doctor found himself so agreeable to Miss Bridget, that he now began to lament an unfortunate accident which had happened to him about ten years before; namely, his marriage with another woman, who was not only still alive, but, what was worse, known to be so by Mr Allworthy.", "박사는 자신이 브리짓 양에게 무척 마음에 드는 사람이라는 것을 알아채고는, 이제 한 십 년쯤 전에 자기에게 일어난 불운한 사건 하나를 한탄하기 시작했다. 다름 아니라 다른 여자와 결혼한 일이었는데, 그 여자는 아직 살아 있었을 뿐 아니라, 더 고약하게도 올워디 씨가 그 사실을 알고 있다는 점이었다."], ["This was a fatal bar to that happiness which he otherwise saw sufficient probability of obtaining with this young lady; for as to criminal indulgences, he certainly never thought of them.", "이것은, 그렇지 않았다면 이 젊은 아가씨와 더불어 얻을 가능성이 충분해 보이던 그 행복을 가로막는 치명적인 장벽이었다. 떳떳치 못한 방종으로 말하자면, 그는 분명 그런 것은 결코 생각조차 하지 않았으니까."], ["This was owing either to his religion, as is most probable, or to the purity of his passion, which was fixed on those things which matrimony only, and not criminal correspondence, could put him in possession of, or could give him any title to.", "이것은 아마도 그의 신앙심 덕분이었거나, 아니면 그의 열정이 정결한 까닭이었을 것이다. 그 열정은 오직 결혼만이 손에 넣게 해 주거나 권리를 부여할 수 있는 것, 떳떳치 못한 관계로는 결코 얻을 수 없는 것에 단단히 고정되어 있었다."]], [["He had not long ruminated on these matters, before it occurred to his memory that he had a brother who was under no such unhappy incapacity.", "이런 문제들을 곰곰이 곱씹은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 그의 기억 속에 자기에게 그런 불행한 결격 사유가 전혀 없는 동생이 하나 있다는 사실이 떠올랐다. 이 동생이라면 틀림없이 성공하리라고 그는 의심치 않았다."], ["This brother he made no doubt would succeed; for he discerned, as he thought, an inclination to marriage in the lady; and the reader perhaps, when he hears the brother's qualifications, will not blame the confidence which he entertained of his success.", "그가 보기에 아가씨에게 결혼할 마음이 있어 보였기 때문이다. 그리고 독자도 아마 그 동생의 자질을 듣고 나면, 그가 동생의 성공을 굳게 믿은 것을 나무라지 않으리라."]], [["This gentleman was about thirty-five years of age.", "이 신사는 나이가 서른다섯쯤이었다."], ["He was of a middle size, and what is called well-built.", "중간 키에, 이른바 체격이 다부진 사람이었다."], ["He had a scar on his forehead, which did not so much injure his beauty as it denoted his valour (for he was a half-pay officer).", "이마에는 흉터가 하나 있었는데, 그것은 그의 잘생긴 외모를 해친다기보다는 오히려 그의 용맹함을 드러내 주었다(그는 반액 봉급을 받는 퇴역 장교였으니까)."], ["He had good teeth, and something affable, when he pleased, in his smile; though naturally his countenance, as well as his air and voice, had much of roughness in it: yet he could at any time deposit this, and appear all gentleness and good-humour.", "치아가 좋았고, 마음만 내키면 미소에 어딘가 상냥한 데가 있었다. 비록 타고난 얼굴 생김은 물론 분위기와 목소리에도 거친 구석이 많았으나, 그는 언제든 그것을 거두어들이고 더없이 부드럽고 유쾌한 모습으로 나타날 줄 알았다."], ["He was not ungenteel, nor entirely devoid of wit, and in his youth had abounded in sprightliness, which, though he had lately put on a more serious character, he could, when he pleased, resume.", "그는 천박하지도 않았고, 재치가 아주 없는 것도 아니었으며, 젊은 시절에는 발랄함이 넘쳤다. 비록 근래에는 더 진지한 성품을 두르고 있었으나, 마음만 먹으면 언제든 그 발랄함을 되살릴 수 있었다."]], [["He had, as well as the doctor, an academic education; for his father had, with the same paternal authority we have mentioned before, decreed him for holy orders; but as the old gentleman died before he was ordained, he chose the church military, and preferred the king's commission to the bishop's.", "그도 박사와 마찬가지로 대학 교육을 받았다. 그의 아버지가 앞서 말한 그 똑같은 아버지의 권위로 그를 성직자로 정해 두었기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 서품을 받기 전에 노신사가 세상을 떠나자, 그는 교회 대신 군대를 택했고, 주교의 성직 임명보다 국왕의 임관을 더 좋아했다."]], [["He had purchased the post of lieutenant of dragoons, and afterwards came to be a captain; but having quarrelled with his colonel, was by his interest obliged to sell; from which time he had entirely rusticated himself, had betaken himself to studying the Scriptures, and was not a little suspected of an inclination to methodism.", "그는 용기병 중위 자리를 사들였고, 나중에는 대위까지 올랐다. 그러나 자기 연대장과 다툰 끝에, 그 사람의 입김으로 자리를 팔아야만 했다. 그때부터 그는 완전히 시골에 파묻혀 지내며 성경 공부에 매달렸고, 감리교에 마음이 기울어 있다는 의심을 적잖이 받았다."]], [["It seemed, therefore, not unlikely that such a person should succeed with a lady of so saint-like a disposition, and whose inclinations were no otherwise engaged than to the marriage state in general; but why the doctor, who certainly had no great friendship for his brother, should for his sake think of making so ill a return to the hospitality of Allworthy, is a matter not so easy to be accounted for.", "그러니 이런 사람이라면, 그토록 성녀 같은 기질을 지녔고 마음이 어느 특정한 상대가 아니라 그저 결혼이라는 상태 자체에 쏠려 있을 뿐인 아가씨에게서 성공을 거두는 것도 그리 가망 없는 일은 아니어 보였다. 하지만 분명 동생과 그리 깊은 우애가 있는 것도 아닌 박사가, 어째서 동생을 위해 올워디의 환대에 이토록 못된 보답을 할 생각을 하게 되었는지는 그리 쉽게 설명할 수 있는 문제가 아니다."]], [["Is it that some natures delight in evil, as others are thought to delight in virtue?", "어떤 본성은 악을 즐기는 것일까, 다른 본성이 덕을 즐긴다고 여겨지듯이?"], ["Or is there a pleasure in being accessory to a theft when we cannot commit it ourselves?", "아니면 우리 자신이 직접 저지를 수 없을 때 도둑질의 공범이 되는 데에 어떤 쾌감이 있는 것일까?"], ["Or lastly (which experience seems to make probable), have we a satisfaction in aggrandizing our families, even though we have not the least love or respect for them?", "아니면 마지막으로(경험이 그럴듯하다고 일러 주는 바이지만), 우리는 자기 가문에 손톱만큼의 애정이나 존경도 품고 있지 않으면서도, 그 가문을 번성시키는 데에서 만족을 느끼는 것일까?"]], [["Whether any of these motives operated on the doctor, we will not determine; but so the fact was.", "이런 동기들 가운데 어느 것이 박사에게 작용했는지는 단정하지 않겠다. 어쨌든 사실은 이러했다."], ["He sent for his brother, and easily found means to introduce him at Allworthy's as a person who intended only a short visit to himself.", "그는 동생을 불러들였고, 어렵지 않게 방도를 찾아내어, 그저 자기를 잠깐 찾아온 손님인 것처럼 동생을 올워디의 집에 소개했다."]], [["The captain had not been in the house a week before the doctor had reason to felicitate himself on his discernment.", "대위가 그 집에 든 지 일주일도 채 되지 않아, 박사는 자기 안목을 자축할 만한 이유를 얻었다."], ["The captain was indeed as great a master of the art of love as Ovid was formerly.", "실로 대위는 옛날의 오비디우스 못지않게 사랑의 기술에 통달한 대가였다."], ["He had besides received proper hints from his brother, which he failed not to improve to the best advantage.", "게다가 그는 형으로부터 적절한 귀띔까지 받았고, 그것을 더없이 유리하게 살려 내는 데에 빈틈이 없었다."]], [["# Chapter xi.", "# Chapter xi."]], [["Containing many rules, and some examples, concerning falling in love: descriptions of beauty, and other more prudential inducements to matrimony.", "이 사랑에 빠지는 일에 관하여 많은 규칙과 몇 가지 사례를 담고 있다. 또한 아름다움에 대한 묘사와, 결혼으로 이끄는 그 밖의 좀 더 타산적인 유인들도 함께 다룬다."]], [["It hath been observed, by wise men or women, I forget which, that all persons are doomed to be in love once in their lives. No particular season is, as I remember, assigned for this; but the age at which Miss Bridget was arrived, seems to me as proper a period as any to be fixed on for this purpose: it often, indeed, happens much earlier; but when it doth not, I have observed it seldom or never fails about this time. Moreover, we may remark that at this season love is of a more serious and steady nature than what sometimes shows itself in the younger parts of life. The love of girls is uncertain, capricious, and so foolish that we cannot always discover what the young lady would be at; nay, it may almost be doubted whether she always knows this herself.", "현명한 남자였는지 여자였는지는 잊어버렸으나, 어떤 이가 이렇게 말한 적이 있다. 모든 사람은 평생에 한 번은 사랑에 빠질 운명을 타고난다고. 내 기억으로는 그 일에 정해진 특정한 시기는 없다. 그러나 브리짓 양이 이른 그 나이야말로 이 목적을 위해 정해 둘 수 있는 어느 시기 못지않게 적절해 보인다. 실제로 그 일은 훨씬 더 이른 나이에 일어나는 경우가 많다. 그러나 그렇지 않을 때라면, 내가 보아 온 바로는 대개 이맘때를 거의 거르는 법이 없다. 더구나 우리는 이 시기의 사랑이 인생의 더 젊은 시절에 이따금 모습을 드러내는 사랑보다 훨씬 진지하고 한결같은 성질을 지닌다는 점을 짚어 둘 만하다. 소녀들의 사랑은 종잡을 수 없고, 변덕스러우며, 어찌나 어리석은지 그 아가씨가 도대체 무엇을 원하는지 우리가 늘 알아챌 수가 없다. 아니, 그녀 자신조차 늘 그것을 알고 있는지 의심스러울 지경이다."]], [["Now we are never at a loss to discern this in women about forty; for as such grave, serious, and experienced ladies well know their own meaning, so it is always very easy for a man of the least sagacity to discover it with the utmost certainty.", "그런데 마흔쯤 된 여인에게서는 우리가 이를 알아보는 데 조금도 어려움이 없다. 그처럼 점잖고 진중하며 경험 많은 부인들은 자기 마음이 무엇을 뜻하는지 잘 알고 있으니, 웬만큼만 눈치가 있는 남자라면 누구든 그것을 더없이 확실하게 알아채기란 늘 아주 쉬운 일이다."]], [["Miss Bridget is an example of all these observations.", "브리짓 양은 이 모든 관찰의 본보기였다."], ["She had not been many times in the captain's company before she was seized with this passion.", "그녀는 대위와 자리를 함께한 지 얼마 되지 않아 이 격정에 사로잡히고 말았다. 그렇다고 자기 병을 알지도 못하는 가냘프고 어리석은 소녀처럼 집 안을 시름시름 풀 죽어 돌아다니지도 않았다."], ["Nor did she go pining and moping about the house, like a puny, foolish girl, ignorant of her distemper: she felt, she knew, and she enjoyed, the pleasing sensation, of which, as she was certain it was not only innocent but laudable, she was neither afraid nor ashamed.", "그녀는 그 기분 좋은 감각을 느꼈고, 알았으며, 즐겼다. 그리고 그것이 죄 없는 것일 뿐 아니라 칭찬받을 만한 것임을 확신했기에, 두려워하지도 부끄러워하지도 않았다."]], [["And to say the truth, there is, in all points, great difference between the reasonable passion which women at this age conceive towards men, and the idle and childish liking of a girl to a boy, which is often fixed on the outside only, and on things of little value and no duration; as on cherry-cheeks, small, lily-white hands, sloe-black eyes, flowing locks, downy chins, dapper shapes; nay, sometimes on charms more worthless than these, and less the party's own; such are the outward ornaments of the person, for which men are beholden to the taylor, the laceman, the periwig-maker, the hatter, and the milliner, and not to nature. Such a passion girls may well be ashamed, as they generally are, to own either to themselves or others.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이 나이의 여인들이 남자를 향해 품는 이치에 맞는 격정과, 소녀가 소년에게 품는 부질없고 유치한 호감 사이에는 모든 면에서 큰 차이가 있다. 후자는 흔히 겉모습에만, 그것도 별 가치도 없고 오래가지도 못하는 것들에만 매달린다. 이를테면 앵두 같은 뺨, 작고 백합처럼 흰 손, 자두처럼 까만 눈, 흘러내리는 머리카락, 솜털 보송한 턱, 날렵한 몸매 같은 것들이다. 아니, 때로는 이보다 더 하잘것없고, 그나마 본인의 것도 아닌 매력에 빠지기도 한다. 그런 것은 사람 겉을 꾸미는 장식들로, 남자들이 그 덕을 입는 것은 자연이 아니라 재단사, 레이스 장수, 가발 장인, 모자 장수, 그리고 여성용 장신구 상인이다. 소녀들이 그런 격정을 자기 자신에게든 남에게든 인정하기를 부끄러워하는 것은 당연한 일이며, 실제로 대개 그렇게 부끄러워한다."]], [["The love of Miss Bridget was of another kind.", "브리짓 양의 사랑은 그런 종류가 아니었다."], ["The captain owed nothing to any of these fop-makers in his dress, nor was his person much more beholden to nature.", "대위는 옷차림에서 그런 멋쟁이 제조업자들에게 신세 진 것이 하나도 없었고, 그의 몸도 자연에게 그리 많은 신세를 진 편이 아니었다."], ["Both his dress and person were such as, had they appeared in an assembly or a drawing-room, would have been the contempt and ridicule of all the fine ladies there.", "그의 옷차림과 몸이란, 무도회나 응접실에 나타났더라면 그곳에 모인 멋쟁이 부인들 모두에게 멸시와 비웃음을 샀을 만한 것이었다."], ["The former of these was indeed neat, but plain, coarse, ill-fancied, and out of fashion.", "그의 옷은 과연 단정하기는 했으나, 수수하고 거칠며, 멋이라곤 없고 유행에도 뒤떨어진 것이었다."], ["As for the latter, we have expressly described it above.", "그의 몸으로 말하자면, 우리는 이미 앞에서 또렷이 묘사한 바 있다."], ["So far was the skin on his cheeks from being cherry-coloured, that you could not discern what the natural colour of his cheeks was, they being totally overgrown by a black beard, which ascended to his eyes.", "그의 뺨 살갗은 앵두 빛깔과는 거리가 멀어도 한참 멀어서, 뺨의 본래 색이 무엇인지조차 분간할 수 없을 지경이었다. 눈가까지 뻗쳐 올라온 시커먼 수염이 온통 뒤덮고 있었기 때문이다."], ["His shape and limbs were indeed exactly proportioned, but so large that they denoted the strength rather of a ploughman than any other.", "그의 체격과 팔다리는 과연 정확히 균형이 잡혀 있었으나, 어찌나 큼지막한지 다른 무엇보다도 쟁기질하는 농부의 힘을 떠올리게 했다."], ["His shoulders were broad beyond all size, and the calves of his legs larger than those of a common chairman.", "그의 어깨는 가늠하기 어려울 만큼 떡 벌어졌고, 종아리는 흔한 가마꾼의 것보다도 굵었다."], ["In short, his whole person wanted all that elegance and beauty which is the very reverse of clumsy strength, and which so agreeably sets off most of our fine gentlemen; being partly owing to the high blood of their ancestors, viz., blood made of rich sauces and generous wines, and partly to an early town education.", "한마디로 그의 온몸에는, 우람한 힘과는 정반대되는 그 우아함과 아름다움이 전혀 없었다. 그 우아함과 아름다움이란 우리네 멋쟁이 신사들 대부분을 그토록 보기 좋게 돋보이게 하는 것으로, 한편으로는 그 조상들의 고귀한 핏줄, 다시 말해 진한 소스와 좋은 포도주로 빚어진 피 덕분이요, 다른 한편으로는 어려서부터 받은 도회적 교육 덕분이다."]], [["Though Miss Bridget was a woman of the greatest delicacy of taste, yet such were the charms of the captain's conversation, that she totally overlooked the defects of his person.", "브리짓 양은 더없이 섬세한 안목을 지닌 여인이었지만, 대위의 대화가 지닌 매력이 어찌나 대단했던지 그녀는 그의 외모의 결점을 깡그리 못 본 척했다."], ["She imagined, and perhaps very wisely, that she should enjoy more agreeable minutes with the captain than with a much prettier fellow; and forewent the consideration of pleasing her eyes, in order to procure herself much more solid satisfaction.", "그녀는 훨씬 잘생긴 사내와 함께할 때보다 대위와 함께할 때 더 기분 좋은 시간을 누리리라 생각했고, 어쩌면 그것은 무척 현명한 판단이었다. 그래서 그녀는 눈을 즐겁게 하는 일은 제쳐 두고, 그 대신 훨씬 더 단단한 만족을 자기 것으로 삼고자 했다."]], [["The captain no sooner perceived the passion of Miss Bridget, in which discovery he was very quick-sighted, than he faithfully returned it.", "대위는 이런 일을 알아채는 데 눈이 무척 밝았던지라, 브리짓 양의 격정을 알아채기가 무섭게 그것에 성실히 화답했다. 이 부인 역시 그녀의 연인 못지않게 미모로 이름난 사람은 아니었다."], ["The lady, no more than her lover, was remarkable for beauty.", "그녀의 모습을 그려 보고 싶지만, 그 일은 이미 더 솜씨 좋은 거장, 다름 아닌 호가스 씨께서 해 놓으셨다."], ["I would attempt to draw her picture, but that is done already by a more able master, Mr Hogarth himself, to whom she sat many years ago, and hath been lately exhibited by that gentleman in his print of a winter's morning, of which she was no improper emblem, and may be seen walking (for walk she doth in the print) to Covent Garden church, with a starved foot-boy behind carrying her prayer-book.", "그녀는 여러 해 전 그 앞에 모델로 앉았고, 근래에 그 신사가 〈겨울 아침〉이라는 판화로 세상에 내놓았으니, 그녀야말로 그 그림의 더없이 어울리는 상징이라 할 만했다. 그림 속에서 그녀는 코번트 가든 교회로 걸어가는(과연 그림 속에서 그녀는 걷고 있다) 모습으로, 뒤에는 비쩍 곯은 사동 하나가 그녀의 기도서를 들고 따라가고 있는 것을 볼 수 있다."]], [["The captain likewise very wisely preferred the more solid enjoyments he expected with this lady, to the fleeting charms of person.", "대위 또한 매우 현명하게도, 덧없는 외모의 매력보다 이 부인에게서 기대되는 더 단단한 즐거움을 택했다."], ["He was one of those wise men who regard beauty in the other sex as a very worthless and superficial qualification; or, to speak more truly, who rather chuse to possess every convenience of life with an ugly woman, than a handsome one without any of those conveniences.", "그는 이성의 아름다움을 지극히 하찮고 겉만 번지르르한 자질로 여기는 그런 현명한 사내들 가운데 하나였다. 좀 더 정확히 말하자면, 그런 편의는 하나도 없는 미인보다는 못생긴 여인이라도 인생의 온갖 편의를 다 갖춘 쪽을 차라리 택하는 부류였다."], ["And having a very good appetite, and but little nicety, he fancied he should play his part very well at the matrimonial banquet, without the sauce of beauty.", "게다가 그는 식욕이 무척 왕성하고 입맛은 그리 까다롭지 않은 사람이었던지라, 아름다움이라는 소스가 없어도 결혼이라는 잔치에서 제 몫을 톡톡히 해내리라 여겼다."]], [["To deal plainly with the reader, the captain, ever since his arrival, at least from the moment his brother had proposed the match to him, long before he had discovered any flattering symptoms in Miss Bridget, had been greatly enamoured; that is to say, of Mr Allworthy's house and gardens, and of his lands, tenements, and hereditaments; of all which the captain was so passionately fond, that he would most probably have contracted marriage with them, had he been obliged to have taken the witch of Endor into the bargain.", "독자에게 솔직히 털어놓자면, 대위는 도착한 이래로, 적어도 그의 형이 그에게 이 혼사를 제안한 그 순간부터, 브리짓 양에게서 무슨 호의의 기미를 알아채기 한참 전부터, 이미 단단히 반해 있었다. 다시 말해 올워디 씨의 저택과 정원, 그의 토지와 가옥, 그리고 상속 재산에 반해 있었던 것이다. 대위는 이 모든 것을 어찌나 열렬히 사랑했던지, 거래 조건에 엔도르의 마녀까지 끼워 받아야 하는 처지였더라도 십중팔구 그것들과 혼인 계약을 맺었을 것이다."]], [["As Mr Allworthy, therefore, had declared to the doctor that he never intended to take a second wife, as his sister was his nearest relation, and as the doctor had fished out that his intentions were to make any child of hers his heir, which indeed the law, without his interposition, would have done for him; the doctor and his brother thought it an act of benevolence to give being to a human creature, who would be so plentifully provided with the most essential means of happiness.", "그리하여 올워디 씨가 의사에게 다시는 두 번째 아내를 맞을 뜻이 없노라 밝힌 바 있고, 누이가 그의 가장 가까운 혈육이었으며, 또 의사가 그의 속내가 누이에게서 난 어떤 아이든 자신의 상속자로 삼는 데 있음을 슬쩍 캐낸 터였으니—사실 이는 그가 끼어들지 않아도 법이 알아서 해 줄 일이었다—의사와 그의 동생은, 행복의 가장 본질적인 수단을 그토록 넉넉하게 갖추게 될 한 인간 존재를 세상에 태어나게 하는 것이야말로 자비로운 행위라고 여겼다."], ["The whole thoughts, therefore, of both the brothers were how to engage the affections of this amiable lady.", "그리하여 두 형제의 온 생각은 오로지 이 사랑스러운 부인의 마음을 어떻게 사로잡느냐에 쏠려 있었다."]], [["But fortune, who is a tender parent, and often doth more for her favourite offspring than either they deserve or wish, had been so industrious for the captain, that whilst he was laying schemes to execute his purpose, the lady conceived the same desires with himself, and was on her side contriving how to give the captain proper encouragement, without appearing too forward; for she was a strict observer of all rules of decorum.", "그러나 운명은 다정한 어버이여서, 곧잘 자기가 아끼는 자식을 위해 그들이 받을 자격이 있거나 바라는 것보다 더 많은 일을 해 주는 법인데, 그 운명이 대위를 위해 어찌나 부지런을 떨었던지, 그가 자기 목적을 이루려 계략을 짜고 있는 동안 부인 쪽에서도 그와 똑같은 욕망을 품게 되어, 자기 편에서 어떻게 하면 대위에게 너무 들이대는 기색 없이 적절한 격려를 보낼까 궁리하고 있었다. 그녀는 온갖 예법의 규칙을 엄격히 지키는 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["In this, however, she easily succeeded; for as the captain was always on the look-out, no glance, gesture, or word escaped him.", "그러나 이 일에서 그녀는 손쉽게 성공했다. 대위가 늘 빈틈없이 살피고 있었던 까닭에, 눈짓 하나, 몸짓 하나, 말 한마디도 그를 비껴가지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["The satisfaction which the captain received from the kind behaviour of Miss Bridget, was not a little abated by his apprehensions of Mr Allworthy; for, notwithstanding his disinterested professions, the captain imagined he would, when he came to act, follow the example of the rest of the world, and refuse his consent to a match so disadvantageous, in point of interest, to his sister. From what oracle he received this opinion, I shall leave the reader to determine: but however he came by it, it strangely perplexed him how to regulate his conduct so as at once to convey his affection to the lady, and to conceal it from her brother. He at length resolved to take all private opportunities of making his addresses; but in the presence of Mr Allworthy to be as reserved and as much upon his guard as was possible; and this conduct was highly approved by the brother.", "브리짓 양의 다정한 태도에서 대위가 얻는 만족은, 올워디 씨에 대한 그의 염려 탓에 적잖이 깎여 나갔다. 올워디 씨가 사심 없음을 입버릇처럼 내세우기는 했어도, 막상 행동에 나설 때가 되면 세상 사람들이 다 그러듯 누이에게 이익 면에서 그토록 불리한 혼사에는 동의하지 않으리라고 대위는 짐작했던 것이다. 그가 이런 생각을 어느 신탁에서 받아 왔는지는 독자의 판단에 맡기겠다. 그러나 어찌하여 그런 생각을 품게 되었든, 그것은 그를 묘하게도 곤혹스럽게 만들었다. 어떻게 처신해야 부인에게는 자기 애정을 전하는 동시에 그녀의 오라비에게는 그것을 감출 수 있을지 말이다. 그는 마침내 이렇게 마음먹었다. 사사로이 둘만의 기회가 닿을 때마다 구애를 펼치되, 올워디 씨 앞에서는 되도록 말을 아끼고 한껏 경계를 늦추지 않기로. 그리고 이런 처신을 그 형은 크게 흡족해했다."]], [["He soon found means to make his addresses, in express terms, to his mistress, from whom he received an answer in the proper form, viz.: the answer which was first made some thousands of years ago, and which hath been handed down by tradition from mother to daughter ever since.", "그는 곧 자기 연인에게 분명한 말로 구애를 펼칠 방도를 찾아냈고, 그녀에게서 격식에 맞는 대답을 받았다. 다시 말해 수천 년 전에 처음 행해진 이래로 어머니에게서 딸로 면면히 전해 내려온 바로 그 대답이었다."], ["If I was to translate this into Latin, I should render it by these two words, Nolo Episcopari: a phrase likewise of immemorial use on another occasion.", "이것을 라틴어로 옮긴다면, 나는 두 마디, 즉 놀로 에피스코파리(Nolo Episcopari)로 번역하겠다. 이 또한 또 다른 경우에 아득한 옛날부터 쓰여 온 문구다."]], [["The captain, however he came by his knowledge, perfectly well understood the lady, and very soon after repeated his application with more warmth and earnestness than before, and was again, according to due form, rejected; but as he had increased in the eagerness of his desires, so the lady, with the same propriety, decreased in the violence of her refusal.", "대위는 어디서 그런 식견을 얻었든 간에 이 부인의 마음을 완벽하게 헤아렸고, 머지않아 전보다 더한 열기와 간절함으로 다시 청을 넣었다가, 또 한 번 마땅한 격식에 따라 거절당했다. 그러나 그의 욕망이 점점 더 간절해진 만큼, 부인 또한 그에 걸맞은 절도로 거절의 강도를 점점 누그러뜨렸다."]], [["Not to tire the reader, by leading him through every scene of this courtship (which, though in the opinion of a certain great author, it is the pleasantest scene of life to the actor, is, perhaps, as dull and tiresome as any whatever to the audience), the captain made his advances in form, the citadel was defended in form, and at length, in proper form, surrendered at discretion.", "이 구애의 장면 하나하나를 다 거치게 하여 독자를 지치게 하지는 않겠다. 어느 위대한 작가의 견해로는 그것이 그것을 펼치는 당사자에게는 인생에서 가장 즐거운 장면이라지만, 구경하는 이에게는 아마 그 무엇 못지않게 따분하고 진력나는 장면일 테니 말이다. 어쨌든 대위는 격식에 맞게 진격했고, 성채는 격식에 맞게 방어되었으며, 마침내 마땅한 격식에 따라 항복하고 말았다."]], [["During this whole time, which filled the space of near a month, the captain preserved great distance of behaviour to his lady in the presence of the brother; and the more he succeeded with her in private, the more reserved was he in public.", "거의 한 달에 이르는 이 기간 내내 대위는 형이 보는 앞에서는 부인에게 한껏 거리를 두는 태도를 유지했다. 사사로운 자리에서 그녀와 잘 풀려 갈수록, 사람들 앞에서는 그만큼 더 말을 아꼈다."], ["And as for the lady, she had no sooner secured her lover than she behaved to him before company with the highest degree of indifference; so that Mr Allworthy must have had the insight of the devil (or perhaps some of his worse qualities) to have entertained the least suspicion of what was going forward.", "부인으로 말하자면, 연인을 손에 넣자마자 사람들 앞에서는 더없이 무관심한 태도로 그를 대했다. 그리하여 올워디 씨가 그 사이에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 털끝만큼이라도 의심을 품으려면, 악마의 통찰력(혹은 어쩌면 악마의 더 고약한 자질 몇 가지)이라도 지녀야 했을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter xii.", "# Chapter xii."]], [["Containing what the reader may, perhaps, expect to find in it. In all bargains, whether to fight or to marry, or concerning any other such business, little previous ceremony is required to bring the matter to an issue when both parties are really in earnest.", "싸움이든 결혼이든, 아니면 그 비슷한 어떤 일에 관한 것이든, 모든 흥정에서 양쪽 당사자가 진정으로 진지하기만 하다면 일을 매듭짓는 데에 미리 갖춰야 할 격식 따위는 거의 필요가 없다."], ["This was the case at present, and in less than a month the captain and his lady were man and wife.", "지금이 바로 그러한 경우였으니, 한 달도 채 지나기 전에 대위와 그의 여인은 부부가 되었다."]], [["The great concern now was to break the matter to Mr Allworthy; and this was undertaken by the doctor.", "이제 가장 큰 걱정거리는 이 일을 올워디 씨에게 어떻게 알리느냐 하는 것이었다. 그리고 그 일은 의사가 맡았다."]], [["One day, then, as Allworthy was walking in his garden, the doctor came to him, and, with great gravity of aspect, and all the concern which he could possibly affect in his countenance, said, “I am come, sir, to impart an affair to you of the utmost consequence; but how shall I mention to you what it almost distracts me to think of!”", "그리하여 어느 날, 올워디가 자기 정원을 거닐고 있을 때, 의사가 그에게 다가와 더없이 근엄한 표정으로, 얼굴에 띄울 수 있는 온갖 근심을 끌어모아 이렇게 말했다. \"선생님, 저는 더할 나위 없이 중대한 일을 알려 드리려고 왔습니다."], ["He then launched forth into the most bitter invectives both against men and women; accusing the former of having no attachment but to their interest, and the latter of being so addicted to vicious inclinations that they could never be safely trusted with one of the other sex.", "그러나 생각만 해도 거의 정신이 나갈 지경인 이 일을 어떻게 입에 올려야 할지요!\" 그러고는 남자와 여자 모두를 향해 더없이 신랄한 비난을 쏟아붓기 시작했다."], ["“Could I,” said he, “sir, have suspected that a lady of such prudence, such judgment, such learning, should indulge so indiscreet a passion!", "남자는 제 이익에만 매달릴 뿐 다른 애착이라곤 없다고 몰아붙였고, 여자는 사악한 성향에 어찌나 빠져 있는지 도무지 다른 성(性)의 사람과 안심하고 맺어 줄 수가 없다고 했다."], ["or could I have imagined that my brother--why do I call him so?", "\"선생님,\" 그가 말했다, \"그토록 신중하고, 그토록 분별 있고, 그토록 학식 있는 부인이 그처럼 경솔한 정념에 자신을 내맡기리라고 제가 어찌 의심이나 했겠습니까!"], ["he is no longer a brother of mine----”", "아니면 제 동생이—아니, 어찌 제가 그를 그렇게 부르겠습니까? 그는 더 이상 제 동생이 아닙니다——\""]], [["“Indeed but he is,”", "\"아니, 그는 분명 자네 동생일세,\""], ["said Allworthy, “and a brother of mine too.”", "올워디가 말했다, \"그리고 내 동생이기도 하지.\""]], [["“Bless me, sir!”", "\"맙소사, 선생님!\""], ["said the doctor, “do you know the shocking affair?”", "의사가 말했다, \"이 충격적인 일을 알고 계신 겁니까?\""]], [["“Look'ee, Mr Blifil,”", "\"이보게, 블리필 씨,\""], ["answered the good man, “it hath been my constant maxim in life to make the best of all matters which happen.", "선량한 신사가 대답했다, \"일어나는 모든 일을 가장 좋은 쪽으로 받아들이는 것이 내 평생 한결같은 신조였네."], ["My sister, though many years younger than I, is at least old enough to be at the age of discretion.", "내 누이는 나보다 여러 해 어리긴 하지만, 적어도 분별이 설 나이는 충분히 되었지. 만일 그가 아이를 속였다면 나도 더 용서하기 어려웠을 걸세."], ["Had he imposed on a child, I should have been more averse to have forgiven him; but a woman upwards of thirty must certainly be supposed to know what will make her most happy.", "그러나 서른이 넘은 여자라면 무엇이 자신을 가장 행복하게 해 줄지는 분명히 안다고 보아야 하지 않겠나. 그녀는 한 신사와 결혼했네."], ["She hath married a gentleman, though perhaps not quite her equal in fortune; and if he hath any perfections in her eye which can make up that deficiency, I see no reason why I should object to her choice of her own happiness; which I, no more than herself, imagine to consist only in immense wealth.", "비록 재산에서는 그녀와 어울리는 짝이 아닐지 모르나, 그 부족함을 메워 줄 만한 어떤 미덕이 그녀의 눈에 그에게 있다면, 그녀가 자신의 행복을 스스로 고른 것에 내가 반대할 이유를 나는 도무지 찾지 못하겠네. 나도 그녀와 마찬가지로, 행복이 오직 엄청난 재산에만 있다고 여기지는 않으니까."], ["I might, perhaps, from the many declarations I have made of complying with almost any proposal, have expected to have been consulted on this occasion; but these matters are of a very delicate nature, and the scruples of modesty, perhaps, are not to be overcome.", "거의 어떤 제안이든 따르겠다고 내가 누차 밝혀 온 터라, 이번 일에 대해서는 어쩌면 내게 상의가 있으리라 기대했을지도 모르지. 그러나 이런 일이란 매우 미묘한 성질의 것이고, 부끄러움이라는 망설임은 어쩌면 이겨 내기 어려운 법일세."], ["As to your brother, I have really no anger against him at all.", "자네 동생으로 말하자면, 나는 그에게 정말이지 조금의 노여움도 없네."], ["He hath no obligations to me, nor do I think he was under any necessity of asking my consent, since the woman is, as I have said, sui juris, and of a proper age to be entirely answerable only, to herself for her conduct.”", "그는 내게 빚진 것이 없으며, 또 내 동의를 구해야 할 어떤 필요도 없었다고 생각하네. 그 여인은, 내가 말했듯, 제 권리를 가진 사람(sui juris)이요, 자기 행실에 대해 오직 자기 자신에게만 책임지면 되는 적절한 나이이니 말일세.\""]], [["The doctor accused Mr Allworthy of too great lenity, repeated his accusations against his brother, and declared that he should never more be brought either to see, or to own him for his relation.", "의사는 올워디 씨가 너무 관대하다고 나무라며, 자기 동생을 향한 비난을 거듭하고는, 다시는 그를 만나지도, 친척으로 인정하지도 않겠다고 선언했다."], ["He then launched forth into a panegyric on Allworthy's goodness; into the highest encomiums on his friendship; and concluded by saying, he should never forgive his brother for having put the place which he bore in that friendship to a hazard.", "그러고는 올워디의 선량함을 향한 찬사로 말문을 열어, 그의 우정에 대한 더없이 높은 칭송을 늘어놓더니, 끝으로 자기 동생이 그 우정 속에서 자신이 차지하고 있던 자리를 위태롭게 만든 일을 결코 용서하지 않겠다고 맺었다."]], [["Allworthy thus answered: “Had I conceived any displeasure against your brother, I should never have carried that resentment to the innocent: but I assure you I have no such displeasure. Your brother appears to me to be a man of sense and honour. I do not disapprove the taste of my sister; nor will I doubt but that she is equally the object of his inclinations. I have always thought love the only foundation of happiness in a married state, as it can only produce that high and tender friendship which should always be the cement of this union; and, in my opinion, all those marriages which are contracted from other motives are greatly criminal; they are a profanation of a most holy ceremony, and generally end in disquiet and misery: for surely we may call it a profanation to convert this most sacred institution into a wicked sacrifice to lust or avarice: and what better can be said of those matches to which men are induced merely by the consideration of a beautiful person, or a great fortune?", "이에 올워디가 이렇게 답했다. \"내가 자네 동생에게 어떤 불쾌함을 품었다 한들, 죄 없는 사람에게까지 그 원망을 옮기는 일은 결코 없었을 걸세. 하지만 분명히 말하건대, 나는 그런 불쾌함이 전혀 없네. 자네 동생은 내가 보기에 분별 있고 명예로운 사람일세. 나는 내 누이의 안목을 못마땅히 여기지 않으며, 그녀 또한 그의 마음을 똑같이 끄는 대상임을 의심하지 않겠네. 나는 늘 사랑이야말로 결혼 생활에서 행복의 유일한 토대라고 생각해 왔네. 사랑만이 이 결합의 접착제가 되어야 할 그 높고 다정한 우정을 길러 낼 수 있으니까. 그리고 내 생각에는, 다른 동기에서 맺어지는 모든 결혼은 큰 죄악일세. 그것은 더없이 거룩한 의식을 모독하는 일이며, 대개는 불화와 비참으로 끝나고 마네. 이 가장 신성한 제도를 정욕이나 탐욕에 바치는 사악한 제물로 바꾸는 것을 모독이라 부르지 않을 수 있겠나? 그리고 단지 아름다운 외모나 막대한 재산을 헤아려 사람을 결혼으로 끌어들이는 짝짓기를 두고, 그보다 나은 말을 무엇이라 할 수 있겠나?"]], [["“To deny that beauty is an agreeable object to the eye, and even worthy some admiration, would be false and foolish.", "\"아름다움이 눈에 흐뭇한 대상이며, 심지어 어느 정도 찬탄을 받을 만하다는 것을 부정하는 일은 거짓되고 어리석은 짓일세."], ["Beautiful is an epithet often used in Scripture, and always mentioned with honour.", "'아름답다'는 말은 성경에서도 자주 쓰이며, 언제나 명예로운 뜻으로 언급되지."], ["It was my own fortune to marry a woman whom the world thought handsome, and I can truly say I liked her the better on that account.", "나 또한 세상이 미인이라 여긴 여인과 결혼하는 행운을 누렸고, 그 점 때문에 그녀를 더 좋아했다고 진실로 말할 수 있네."], ["But to make this the sole consideration of marriage, to lust after it so violently as to overlook all imperfections for its sake, or to require it so absolutely as to reject and disdain religion, virtue, and sense, which are qualities in their nature of much higher perfection, only because an elegance of person is wanting: this is surely inconsistent, either with a wise man or a good Christian.", "그러나 이것을 결혼의 유일한 고려 사항으로 삼아, 그것을 위해 온갖 결함을 못 본 척할 만큼 격렬하게 갈망하거나, 단지 외모의 우아함이 모자란다는 이유만으로—본디 훨씬 더 높은 완전함을 지닌 자질인 신앙과 덕과 분별을 물리치고 멸시할 만큼 그것을 절대적으로 요구한다면, 이는 분명 지혜로운 사람에게도, 선량한 그리스도인에게도 어울리지 않는 일일세."], ["And it is, perhaps, being too charitable to conclude that such persons mean anything more by their marriage than to please their carnal appetites; for the satisfaction of which, we are taught, it was not ordained.", "그리고 그런 사람들이 자기 결혼으로 육욕을 채우는 것 이상의 무엇을 뜻한다고 결론짓는 것은, 어쩌면 지나치게 너그러운 일일 걸세. 그것을 채우라고 결혼이 정해진 것은 아니라고 우리는 배웠으니 말일세."]], [["“In the next place, with respect to fortune.", "\"다음으로 재산에 관해서네."], ["Worldly prudence, perhaps, exacts some consideration on this head; nor will I absolutely and altogether condemn it.", "세속의 분별은 어쩌면 이 점에 대해 어느 정도의 고려를 요구하지. 나도 그것을 완전히, 송두리째 비난하지는 않겠네."], ["As the world is constituted, the demands of a married state, and the care of posterity, require some little regard to what we call circumstances.", "세상이 이루어진 모양새가 그러하니, 결혼 생활의 요구와 후손을 돌보는 일은 우리가 형편이라 부르는 것에 어느 정도의 관심을 기울이게 만들지."], ["Yet this provision is greatly increased, beyond what is really necessary, by folly and vanity, which create abundantly more wants than nature.", "그러나 이 대비책은 실제로 필요한 정도를 훨씬 넘어, 어리석음과 허영 때문에 크게 부풀려지네. 그것들은 자연이 요구하는 것보다 훨씬 많은 욕구를 만들어 내니까."], ["Equipage for the wife, and large fortunes for the children, are by custom enrolled in the list of necessaries; and to procure these, everything truly solid and sweet, and virtuous and religious, are neglected and overlooked.", "아내를 위한 마차며 자식들을 위한 큰 재산이 관습에 따라 필수품의 목록에 올라 있고, 이것들을 마련하느라 진실로 단단하고 달콤하며, 덕스럽고 신앙심 깊은 모든 것이 소홀히 여겨지고 외면당하지."]], [["“And this in many degrees; the last and greatest of which seems scarce distinguishable from madness;--I mean where persons of immense fortunes contract themselves to those who are, and must be, disagreeable to them--to fools and knaves--in order to increase an estate already larger even than the demands of their pleasures.", "\"그리고 이것은 여러 단계로 나뉘는데, 그 마지막이자 가장 심한 단계는 광기와 거의 구별되지 않는 듯하네.—내가 말하는 것은, 막대한 재산을 가진 사람들이 자신에게 못마땅하고 또 못마땅할 수밖에 없는 자들에게—어리석은 자들과 악당들에게—이미 자기 즐거움의 요구를 넘어설 만큼 큰 재산을 더 불리려고 스스로를 묶어 버리는 경우일세."], ["Surely such persons, if they will not be thought mad, must own, either that they are incapable of tasting the sweets of the tenderest friendship, or that they sacrifice the greatest happiness of which they are capable to the vain, uncertain, and senseless laws of vulgar opinion, which owe as well their force as their foundation to folly.”", "분명 그런 사람들은, 미치광이로 여겨지기를 바라지 않는다면, 둘 중 하나는 인정해야 할 걸세. 그들이 가장 다정한 우정의 달콤함을 맛볼 줄 모르는 무능한 자들이라는 것, 아니면 자신이 누릴 수 있는 가장 큰 행복을, 어리석음에서 그 힘과 토대를 똑같이 빌려 온 저 헛되고 불확실하며 무의미한 세속 통념의 법칙에 제물로 바치고 있다는 것을 말이야.\""]], [["Here Allworthy concluded his sermon, to which Blifil had listened with the profoundest attention, though it cost him some pains to prevent now and then a small discomposure of his muscles.", "여기서 올워디는 자신의 설교를 마쳤고, 블리필은 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 그것을 들었다. 비록 이따금 근육이 슬며시 흐트러지려는 것을 막느라 적잖이 애를 먹기는 했지만 말이다."], ["He now praised every period of what he had heard with the warmth of a young divine, who hath the honour to dine with a bishop the same day in which his lordship hath mounted the pulpit.", "그는 이제 자기가 들은 그 모든 대목을 젊은 성직자의 열성으로 칭송했다. 마치 주교가 설교단에 오른 바로 그날, 그분과 함께 식사하는 영광을 누린 젊은 성직자처럼."]], [["# Chapter xiii.", "# Chapter xiii."]], [["Which concludes the first book; with an instance of ingratitude, which, we hope, will appear unnatural.", "제1권을 마무리하는 장. 부디 부자연스러워 보이기를 바라는, 배은망덕의 한 사례를 담아."]], [["The reader, from what hath been said, may imagine that the reconciliation (if indeed it could be so called) was only matter of form; we shall therefore pass it over, and hasten to what must surely be thought matter of substance.", "지금까지 한 이야기로 미루어, 독자는 그 화해가(과연 그것을 화해라 부를 수 있다면) 형식에 불과한 것이었음을 짐작할 것이다. 그러니 우리는 그 대목을 건너뛰고, 분명 알맹이가 있는 일이라 여겨질 만한 쪽으로 서둘러 가보자."]], [["The doctor had acquainted his brother with what had past between Mr Allworthy and him; and added with a smile, “I promise you I paid you off; nay, I absolutely desired the good gentleman not to forgive you: for you know after he had made a declaration in your favour, I might with safety venture on such a request with a person of his temper; and I was willing, as well for your sake as for my own, to prevent the least possibility of a suspicion.”", "의사는 자기 형에게 올워디 씨와 자신 사이에 오간 일을 알려주고, 빙긋 웃으며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"장담하건대 내가 형 빚을 톡톡히 갚았소. 아니, 나는 그 선량한 분께 형을 용서하지 말아달라고 아예 못박아 부탁했단 말이오. 그분이 이미 형에게 호의를 표명하신 뒤였으니, 그런 성품의 분에게 그런 청쯤은 마음 놓고 던져볼 수 있었지. 게다가 나는 형을 위해서나 나 자신을 위해서나, 조금이라도 의심받을 여지를 막아두고 싶었던 거요.\""]], [["Captain Blifil took not the least notice of this, at that time; but he afterwards made a very notable use of it.", "블리필 대위는 그때만큼은 이 말에 조금도 내색하지 않았다. 그러나 나중에 그는 이것을 아주 요긴하게 써먹었다."]], [["One of the maxims which the devil, in a late visit upon earth, left to his disciples, is, when once you are got up, to kick the stool from under you.", "악마가 얼마 전 지상을 다녀가며 제 제자들에게 남긴 격언 중 하나는, 일단 올라서고 나면 발밑의 발판을 걷어차버리라는 것이다."], ["In plain English, when you have made your fortune by the good offices of a friend, you are advised to discard him as soon as you can.", "쉬운 우리말로 풀자면, 친구의 호의 덕에 출세를 이루었거든 가능한 한 빨리 그를 내쳐버리라는 충고다."]], [["Whether the captain acted by this maxim, I will not positively determine: so far we may confidently say, that his actions may be fairly derived from this diabolical principle; and indeed it is difficult to assign any other motive to them: for no sooner was he possessed of Miss Bridget, and reconciled to Allworthy, than he began to show a coldness to his brother which increased daily; till at length it grew into rudeness, and became very visible to every one.", "대위가 이 격언대로 행동했는지는 단정해 말하지 않겠다. 다만 그의 행동이 이 악마적인 원리에서 비롯되었다고 봐도 무리가 없다는 것만큼은 자신 있게 말할 수 있다. 실로 그 행동에 다른 어떤 동기를 갖다 붙이기란 어려운 노릇이다. 그는 브리짓 양을 손에 넣고 올워디와도 화해하자마자, 곧바로 동생에게 냉랭한 기색을 드러내기 시작했고, 그 냉기는 날이 갈수록 심해지다가 마침내 무례함으로까지 번져 누구의 눈에도 훤히 보일 지경이 되었다."]], [["The doctor remonstrated to him privately concerning this behaviour, but could obtain no other satisfaction than the following plain declaration: “If you dislike anything in my brother's house, sir, you know you are at liberty to quit it.”", "의사는 이 처사에 대해 사사로이 그에게 따져 물었으나, 다음과 같은 노골적인 선언 말고는 어떤 만족스러운 대답도 얻지 못했다. \"내 형님 집에서 마음에 안 드는 게 있거든, 언제든 떠나도 좋다는 걸 알아두게.\""], ["This strange, cruel, and almost unaccountable ingratitude in the captain, absolutely broke the poor doctor's heart; for ingratitude never so thoroughly pierces the human breast as when it proceeds from those in whose behalf we have been guilty of transgressions.", "대위의 이 기이하고 잔인하며 거의 설명조차 되지 않는 배은망덕은 가엾은 의사의 가슴을 완전히 무너뜨렸다. 배은망덕이란, 우리가 그자를 위해 죄까지 저질러준 바로 그런 이들에게서 나올 때 인간의 가슴을 가장 사무치게 후벼파는 법이니까."], ["Reflections on great and good actions, however they are received or returned by those in whose favour they are performed, always administer some comfort to us; but what consolation shall we receive under so biting a calamity as the ungrateful behaviour of our friend, when our wounded conscience at the same time flies in our face, and upbraids us with having spotted it in the service of one so worthless!", "위대하고 선한 행동을 떠올리는 일은, 그 덕을 입은 자들이 그것을 어떻게 받아들이고 어떻게 갚든 간에 늘 우리에게 얼마간의 위안을 준다. 그러나 친구의 배은망덕이라는 이토록 쓰라린 재앙 아래에서, 동시에 우리의 상처 입은 양심마저 우리 얼굴에 달려들어 그토록 하잘것없는 자를 위해 양심에 얼룩을 묻혔다고 꾸짖는다면, 우리는 대체 어디서 위로를 받을 수 있겠는가!"]], [["Mr Allworthy himself spoke to the captain in his brother's behalf, and desired to know what offence the doctor had committed; when the hard-hearted villain had the baseness to say that he should never forgive him for the injury which he had endeavoured to do him in his favour; which, he said, he had pumped out of him, and was such a cruelty that it ought not to be forgiven.", "올워디 씨도 직접 대위에게 그 동생을 두둔하는 말을 건네며, 의사가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀느냐고 물었다. 그러자 그 매정한 악당은 비열하게도 이렇게 대답했다. 동생이 자기를 위한답시고 자기에게 해를 끼치려 했던 그 일만큼은 결코 용서할 수 없다고. 그것은 자기가 동생에게서 캐낸 일이며, 도저히 용서받아서는 안 될 잔인한 짓이었다는 것이다."]], [["Allworthy spoke in very high terms upon this declaration, which, he said, became not a human creature.", "올워디는 이 선언을 두고 아주 준엄한 말로 꾸짖으며, 그것은 인간된 자에게 어울리지 않는 태도라고 했다."], ["He expressed, indeed, so much resentment against an unforgiving temper, that the captain at last pretended to be convinced by his arguments, and outwardly professed to be reconciled.", "실로 그는 용서할 줄 모르는 성정에 대해 어찌나 큰 분노를 드러냈던지, 대위는 마침내 그의 논리에 설득당한 척하며 겉으로는 화해했노라고 공언했다."]], [["As for the bride, she was now in her honeymoon, and so passionately fond of her new husband that he never appeared to her to be in the wrong; and his displeasure against any person was a sufficient reason for her dislike to the same.", "신부로 말하자면, 그녀는 이제 한창 신혼이라 새 남편에게 어찌나 열렬히 빠져 있던지, 남편이 잘못했다고는 결코 보이지 않았다. 그리고 남편이 누군가에게 품은 불쾌감은, 그녀 역시 그 사람을 싫어할 충분한 이유가 되었다."]], [["The captain, at Mr Allworthy's instance, was outwardly, as we have said, reconciled to his brother; yet the same rancour remained in his heart; and he found so many opportunities of giving him private hints of this, that the house at last grew insupportable to the poor doctor; and he chose rather to submit to any inconveniences which he might encounter in the world, than longer to bear these cruel and ungrateful insults from a brother for whom he had done so much.", "앞서 말했듯 대위는 올워디 씨의 권유로 겉으로는 동생과 화해했다. 그러나 마음속에는 여전히 똑같은 원한이 도사리고 있었고, 그는 그 원한을 동생에게 은밀히 내비칠 기회를 어찌나 자주 찾아냈던지, 가엾은 의사에게 그 집은 마침내 견딜 수 없는 곳이 되고 말았다. 그래서 그는 그토록 많은 것을 베풀어준 형에게서 이런 잔인하고 배은망덕한 모욕을 더 견디느니, 차라리 세상에 나가 부딪칠 온갖 불편을 감수하는 편을 택했다."]], [["He once intended to acquaint Allworthy with the whole; but he could not bring himself to submit to the confession, by which he must take to his share so great a portion of guilt.", "그는 한때 올워디에게 그 전말을 모조리 알려줄까도 생각했다. 그러나 그렇게 고백하려면 죄의 큰 몫을 제 몫으로 떠안아야 하니, 도저히 그 일에 마음을 굽힐 수가 없었다."], ["Besides, by how much the worse man he represented his brother to be, so much the greater would his own offence appear to Allworthy, and so much the greater, he had reason to imagine, would be his resentment.", "게다가 형을 더 나쁜 사람으로 그려낼수록, 올워디 앞에서 자기 잘못도 그만큼 더 커 보일 테고, 또 그만큼 올워디의 분노도 더 커지리라고 짐작할 만한 이유가 있었다."]], [["He feigned, therefore, some excuse of business for his departure, and promised to return soon again; and took leave of his brother with so well-dissembled content, that, as the captain played his part to the same perfection, Allworthy remained well satisfied with the truth of the reconciliation.", "그리하여 그는 떠난다는 핑계로 무슨 볼일이 있는 척 둘러대고는, 곧 다시 돌아오겠노라 약속했다. 그러고는 어찌나 흡족한 표정을 그럴듯하게 꾸며 형에게 작별을 고했던지, 대위 또한 자기 역할을 똑같이 완벽하게 해낸 터라, 올워디는 그 화해가 진심이라고 철석같이 믿게 되었다."]], [["The doctor went directly to London, where he died soon after of a broken heart; a distemper which kills many more than is generally imagined, and would have a fair title to a place in the bill of mortality, did it not differ in one instance from all other diseases--viz., that no physician can cure it.", "의사는 곧장 런던으로 갔고, 그곳에서 얼마 안 가 상심병으로 세상을 떠났다. 이 병은 흔히 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 사람을 죽음으로 몰아가니, 한 가지 점에서만 다른 모든 병과 구별되지 않았다면 사망자 명부에 당당히 한 자리를 차지할 자격이 있을 것이다. 그 한 가지란, 어떤 의사도 이 병만은 고치지 못한다는 점이다."]], [["Now, upon the most diligent enquiry into the former lives of these two brothers, I find, besides the cursed and hellish maxim of policy above mentioned, another reason for the captain's conduct: the captain, besides what we have before said of him, was a man of great pride and fierceness, and had always treated his brother, who was of a different complexion, and greatly deficient in both these qualities, with the utmost air of superiority. The doctor, however, had much the larger share of learning, and was by many reputed to have the better understanding. This the captain knew, and could not bear; for though envy is at best a very malignant passion, yet is its bitterness greatly heightened by mixing with contempt towards the same object; and very much afraid I am, that whenever an obligation is joined to these two, indignation and not gratitude will be the product of all three.", "자, 이 두 형제의 지난 삶을 아무리 부지런히 캐어 보아도, 나는 위에서 말한 그 저주받고 지옥 같은 처세의 격언 말고도 대위의 행동을 설명할 또 다른 이유를 하나 더 찾아냈다. 앞서 그에 대해 말한 것 외에도, 대위는 자존심이 세고 사나운 사람이어서, 자기와는 기질이 다르고 그 두 가지 성품이 한참 모자랐던 동생을 늘 더없이 우월한 태도로 대해왔다. 그러나 학식으로 말하자면 의사가 훨씬 더 깊었고, 많은 이들이 그가 더 나은 분별을 지녔다고 평했다. 대위는 이 사실을 알고 있었고, 그것을 견딜 수 없어 했다. 시기심이란 그 자체로도 아주 악독한 감정이지만, 같은 대상을 향한 경멸이 거기에 뒤섞이면 그 쓴맛은 한층 더 짙어지는 법이다. 그리고 나는 적이 염려스럽다. 이 두 가지에 신세까지 더해질 때면, 그 셋이 함께 빚어내는 것은 감사가 아니라 분노이리라는 점이."]], [["# BOOK II.", "# BOOK II."]], [["CONTAINING SCENES OF MATRIMONIAL FELICITY IN DIFFERENT DEGREES OF LIFE; AND VARIOUS OTHER TRANSACTIONS DURING THE FIRST TWO YEARS AFTER THE MARRIAGE BETWEEN CAPTAIN BLIFIL AND MISS BRIDGET ALLWORTHY. Chapter i.", "서로 다른 처지의 사람들에게서 펼쳐지는 결혼 생활의 여러 단계의 행복, 그리고 블리필 대위와 브리짓 올워디 양의 혼인 이후 첫 두 해 동안에 벌어진 갖가지 일들을 담은 권."]], [["Showing what kind of a history this is; what it is like, and what it is not like.", "이 이야기가 어떤 종류의 이야기인지, 무엇과 닮았고 무엇과는 닮지 않았는지를 보여 주는 장."]], [["Though we have properly enough entitled this our work, a history, and not a life; nor an apology for a life, as is more in fashion; yet we intend in it rather to pursue the method of those writers, who profess to disclose the revolutions of countries, than to imitate the painful and voluminous historian, who, to preserve the regularity of his series, thinks himself obliged to fill up as much paper with the detail of months and years in which nothing remarkable happened, as he employs upon those notable aeras when the greatest scenes have been transacted on the human stage.", "우리는 이 작품을 한 사람의 일생도 아니고, 요즘 더 유행하는 일생에 대한 변명도 아닌, 마땅히 하나의 역사라고 이름 붙였다. 그렇지만 우리는 이 안에서, 어느 한 시대의 규칙적인 흐름을 지킨다는 명목으로, 인간이라는 무대 위에서 가장 위대한 장면들이 펼쳐진 저 주목할 만한 시대에 들이는 만큼의 종이를, 아무 일도 일어나지 않은 달과 해의 세세한 기록으로 채워야 한다고 여기는 그 고통스럽고 장황한 역사가를 본뜨기보다는, 차라리 여러 나라의 흥망을 밝히겠노라 공언하는 작가들의 방식을 따르려 한다."]], [["Such histories as these do, in reality, very much resemble a newspaper, which consists of just the same number of words, whether there be any news in it or not.", "이런 종류의 역사책은 사실상, 그 안에 소식이 있든 없든 늘 똑같은 분량의 글자로 채워지는 신문과 무척 닮았다. 또한 이런 책은 텅 빈 채로든 가득 찬 채로든 한결같이 같은 노선을 달리는 역마차에 빗댈 수도 있다."], ["They may likewise be compared to a stage coach, which performs constantly the same course, empty as well as full.", "실로 그 작가는, 자신이 그 비서 노릇을 하는 시간과 똑같은 보폭을 유지해야 한다고 여기는 듯하다."], ["The writer, indeed, seems to think himself obliged to keep even pace with time, whose amanuensis he is; and, like his master, travels as slowly through centuries of monkish dulness, when the world seems to have been asleep, as through that bright and busy age so nobly distinguished by the excellent Latin poet--", "그래서 마치 제 주인을 본받듯, 세상이 잠든 것만 같았던 수도사들의 그 둔중한 세월을 지날 때나, 저 빼어난 라틴 시인이 그토록 고결하게 노래한 밝고 분주한 시대를 지날 때나 한결같이 더디게 흘러간다. 사방에서 카르타고인들이 싸우러 몰려오니,"]], [["Ad confligendum venientibus undique poenis, Omnia cum belli trepido concussa tumultu Horrida contremuere sub altis aetheris auris; In dubioque fuit sub utrorum regna cadendum Omnibus humanis esset, terraque marique.", "온 세상이 전쟁의 떨리는 소란에 뒤흔들리고\n높은 하늘의 바람 아래 무섭게 진동하였도다."]], [["Of which we wish we could give our readers a more adequate translation than that by Mr Creech--", "바다에서나 땅에서나 모든 인간이\n어느 편의 통치 아래 떨어질지 알 수 없었느니라."]], [["When dreadful Carthage frighted Rome with arms, And all the world was shook with fierce alarms; Whilst undecided yet, which part should fall, Which nation rise the glorious lord of all.", "이 구절을 우리는 크리치 씨의 번역보다 더 어울리는 번역으로 독자들에게 전해 줄 수 있었으면 한다. 무서운 카르타고가 무기로 로마를 위협하고,"]], [["Now it is our purpose, in the ensuing pages, to pursue a contrary method. When any extraordinary scene presents itself (as we trust will often be the case), we shall spare no pains nor paper to open it at large to our reader; but if whole years should pass without producing anything worthy his notice, we shall not be afraid of a chasm in our history; but shall hasten on to matters of consequence, and leave such periods of time totally unobserved.", "온 세상이 사나운 경보에 뒤흔들리던 때,\n아직 어느 편이 쓰러질지, 어느 나라가 일어나"]], [["These are indeed to be considered as blanks in the grand lottery of time. We therefore, who are the registers of that lottery, shall imitate those sagacious persons who deal in that which is drawn at Guildhall, and who never trouble the public with the many blanks they dispose of; but when a great prize happens to be drawn, the newspapers are presently filled with it, and the world is sure to be informed at whose office it was sold: indeed, commonly two or three different offices lay claim to the honour of having disposed of it; by which, I suppose, the adventurers are given to understand that certain brokers are in the secrets of Fortune, and indeed of her cabinet council.", "온 세상의 영광스러운 주인이 될지 정해지지 않았던 그때. 자, 이제 이어지는 글에서 우리가 뜻하는 바는 그와는 정반대의 방식을 따르는 것이다. 어떤 특별한 장면이 나타날 때면(자주 그러하리라 믿는다), 우리는 그것을 독자에게 한껏 펼쳐 보이기 위해 수고도 종이도 아끼지 않을 것이다. 그러나 독자가 눈여겨볼 만한 일을 아무것도 빚어내지 못한 채 여러 해가 그냥 흘러간다면, 우리는 이야기에 빈틈이 생기는 것을 두려워하지 않을 것이다. 오히려 우리는 중요한 일들로 서둘러 나아가며, 그런 세월은 아예 거들떠보지도 않은 채 남겨 둘 것이다."]], [["My reader then is not to be surprized, if, in the course of this work, he shall find some chapters very short, and others altogether as long; some that contain only the time of a single day, and others that comprise years; in a word, if my history sometimes seems to stand still, and sometimes to fly. For all which I shall not look on myself as accountable to any court of critical jurisdiction whatever: for as I am, in reality, the founder of a new province of writing, so I am at liberty to make what laws I please therein. And these laws, my readers, whom I consider as my subjects, are bound to believe in and to obey; with which that they may readily and cheerfully comply, I do hereby assure them that I shall principally regard their ease and advantage in all such institutions: for I do not, like a jure divino tyrant, imagine that they are my slaves, or my commodity. I am, indeed, set over them for their own good only, and was created for their use, and not they for mine. Nor do I doubt, while I make their interest the great rule of my writings, they will unanimously concur in supporting my dignity, and in rendering me all the honour I shall deserve or desire.", "실로 이런 시간은 거대한 시간의 복권에서 꽝으로 여겨야 마땅하다. 그러므로 그 복권의 기록자인 우리는, 길드홀에서 추첨되는 복권을 다루며 자기들이 팔아 치운 그 숱한 꽝으로는 결코 대중을 성가시게 하지 않는 저 약삭빠른 사람들을 본받으려 한다. 그러나 큰 상이 당첨되기라도 하면, 신문들은 곧장 그 소식으로 가득 차고, 세상은 어김없이 그것이 어느 가게에서 팔렸는지를 알게 된다. 실로 보통은 두세 군데의 서로 다른 가게가 저마다 그것을 팔았다는 영예를 주장하는데, 짐작건대 이로써 복권을 산 사람들은 어떤 중개인들이 운명의 비밀, 아니 운명의 내각 회의에까지 통해 있다고 믿게 되는 것이다. 그러니 이 작품을 읽어 나가다가, 어떤 장은 무척 짧고 어떤 장은 그만큼 길며, 어떤 장은 단 하루의 시간만 담고 있는가 하면 어떤 장은 여러 해를 아우르고 있다 하더라도, 한마디로 내 이야기가 때로는 멈춰 선 듯하고 때로는 날아가는 듯하더라도, 독자는 놀라서는 안 된다. 이 모든 것에 대해 나는 그 어떤 비평의 법정에도 책임질 까닭이 없다고 여긴다. 나는 실로 글쓰기의 새로운 영토를 일군 창건자이니, 그 안에서 마음껏 법을 만들 자유가 있기 때문이다. 그리고 나의 백성으로 여기는 내 독자들은, 이 법들을 믿고 또 따라야 할 의무가 있다. 다만 그들이 기꺼이, 그리고 흔쾌히 그 법을 따를 수 있도록, 나는 이로써 그들에게 약속하노니, 그러한 모든 제도에서 나는 무엇보다 그들의 편안함과 이익을 먼저 헤아릴 것이다. 나는 결코 왕권신수설을 내세우는 폭군처럼 그들을 내 노예나 내 소유물로 여기지 않기 때문이다. 실로 나는 오직 그들 자신의 이로움을 위해 그들 위에 세워졌으며, 그들을 위해 지어졌을 뿐 그들이 나를 위해 지어진 것이 아니다. 또한 나는 그들의 이익을 내 글쓰기의 가장 큰 준칙으로 삼는 한, 그들이 한마음으로 나의 위엄을 떠받치고, 내가 마땅히 누리거나 바라는 모든 영예를 내게 돌려주리라는 것을 의심치 않는다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["Religious cautions against showing too much favour to bastards; and a great discovery made by Mrs Deborah Wilkins.", "서출 자식에게 지나친 호의를 베푸는 것에 대한 종교적 경계, 그리고 데버러 윌킨스 부인이 이룬 위대한 발견."]], [["Eight months after the celebration of the nuptials between Captain Blifil and Miss Bridget Allworthy, a young lady of great beauty, merit, and fortune, was Miss Bridget, by reason of a fright, delivered of a fine boy.", "블리필 대위와 브리짓 올워디 양의 혼례를 치른 지 여덟 달 만에, 빼어난 미모와 덕성과 재산을 두루 갖춘 이 처녀, 곧 브리짓 양은 어떤 놀란 일을 겪은 탓에 잘생긴 사내아이를 낳았다."], ["The child was indeed to all appearances perfect; but the midwife discovered it was born a month before its full time.", "그 아이는 겉보기에는 흠 하나 없이 멀쩡했으나, 산파는 그 아이가 달을 한 달 채우기 전에 태어났음을 알아차렸다."]], [["Though the birth of an heir by his beloved sister was a circumstance of great joy to Mr Allworthy, yet it did not alienate his affections from the little foundling, to whom he had been godfather, had given his own name of Thomas, and whom he had hitherto seldom failed of visiting, at least once a day, in his nursery.", "사랑하는 누이가 상속자를 낳은 것은 올워디 씨에게 더없이 큰 기쁨이었으나, 그렇다고 그 작은 업둥이를 향한 그의 애정이 식은 것은 아니었다. 그는 그 아이의 대부였고, 자기 이름인 토머스를 그 아이에게 지어 주었으며, 지금껏 하루에 적어도 한 번은 거의 빠짐없이 그 아이의 방을 찾아가 돌보아 왔다."]], [["He told his sister, if she pleased, the new-born infant should be bred up together with little Tommy; to which she consented, though with some little reluctance: for she had truly a great complacence for her brother; and hence she had always behaved towards the foundling with rather more kindness than ladies of rigid virtue can sometimes bring themselves to show to these children, who, however innocent, may be truly called the living monuments of incontinence.", "그는 누이에게, 원한다면 갓 태어난 이 아기를 어린 토미와 함께 길러도 좋겠다고 말했고, 누이는 약간 마지못한 기색이긴 했으나 이에 동의했다. 누이는 실로 오라비를 무척이나 흡족하게 여겼으며, 그런 까닭에 그동안 그 업둥이를 대할 때도, 엄격한 덕을 지닌 부인들이 이런 아이들—아무리 죄가 없다 한들 방종이 남긴 살아 있는 기념물이라 부를 만한 아이들—에게 좀처럼 보이기 어려운 친절함을 늘 베풀어 왔던 것이다."]], [["The captain could not so easily bring himself to bear what he condemned as a fault in Mr Allworthy.", "대위는 올워디 씨의 허물이라 단죄하는 그 일을 그리 쉽게 견딜 수가 없었다. 그는 죄의 열매를 거두어 기르는 것은 곧 죄를 두둔하는 것이라는 뜻을 자주 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["He gave him frequent hints, that to adopt the fruits of sin, was to give countenance to it.", "그는 여러 성경 구절을 인용했는데(그는 성경에 밝았다), 이를테면 \"그분은 아비의 죄를 그 자식에게 갚으시며, 아비가 신 포도를 먹었으니 그 자식의 이가 시리다\" 같은 구절들이었다."], ["He quoted several texts (for he was well read in Scripture), such as, He visits the sins of the fathers upon the children; and the fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge,&c.", "이로부터 그는 어버이의 죄를 서출에게 벌하는 것이 정당하다고 주장했다. 그는 이렇게 말했다."], ["Whence he argued the legality of punishing the crime of the parent on the bastard.", "\"법이 그런 천한 출생의 아이들을 죽이는 것을 명시적으로 허락하지는 않으나, 그래도 그들을 누구의 자식도 아닌 자로 본다."], ["He said, “Though the law did not positively allow the destroying such base-born children, yet it held them to be the children of nobody; that the Church considered them as the children of nobody; and that at the best, they ought to be brought up to the lowest and vilest offices of the commonwealth.”", "교회 또한 그들을 누구의 자식도 아닌 자로 여긴다. 그리고 잘해야 그들은 나라에서 가장 낮고 천한 일에나 길러져야 마땅하다.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy answered to all this, and much more, which the captain had urged on this subject, “That, however guilty the parents might be, the children were certainly innocent: that as to the texts he had quoted, the former of them was a particular denunciation against the Jews, for the sin of idolatry, of relinquishing and hating their heavenly King; and the latter was parabolically spoken, and rather intended to denote the certain and necessary consequences of sin, than any express judgment against it. But to represent the Almighty as avenging the sins of the guilty on the innocent, was indecent, if not blasphemous, as it was to represent him acting against the first principles of natural justice, and against the original notions of right and wrong, which he himself had implanted in our minds; by which we were to judge not only in all matters which were not revealed, but even of the truth of revelation itself. He said he knew many held the same principles with the captain on this head; but he was himself firmly convinced to the contrary, and would provide in the same manner for this poor infant, as if a legitimate child had had fortune to have been found in the same place.”", "이 모든 말, 그리고 대위가 이 문제를 두고 들이댄 그 밖의 숱한 말에 대해 올워디 씨는 이렇게 답했다. \"어버이가 아무리 죄가 있다 한들 그 자식들은 분명 죄가 없습니다. 그리고 그대가 인용한 구절들로 말하자면, 앞엣것은 유대인들이 우상을 섬긴 죄, 곧 하늘에 계신 자신들의 왕을 저버리고 미워한 죄에 대한 특정한 단죄였습니다. 뒤엣것은 비유로 말한 것이어서, 죄에 대한 어떤 명시적인 심판이라기보다는 죄가 빚는 확실하고도 필연적인 결과를 나타내려는 뜻이었습니다. 그러나 전능하신 분께서 죄 있는 자의 죄를 죄 없는 자에게 갚아 보복하시는 분으로 그리는 것은, 신성을 모독하는 짓까지는 아니더라도 온당치 못한 일입니다. 그것은 그분이 자연적 정의의 제일 원리에 어긋나게, 또 그분 자신이 우리 마음속에 심어 두신 옳고 그름에 대한 본래의 관념에 어긋나게 행하시는 것으로 그리는 셈이기 때문입니다. 우리는 바로 그 관념으로써, 계시되지 않은 모든 일을 판단할 뿐 아니라 계시 그 자체의 참됨까지도 판단하는 것입니다.\" 그는 또 이 문제에서 대위와 같은 원칙을 지닌 이가 많다는 것을 안다고 했다. 그러나 자신은 그와 정반대로 굳게 확신하며, 만일 적법한 자식이 운 좋게 같은 자리에서 발견되었더라도 했을 것과 똑같은 방식으로 이 가엾은 아기를 보살피겠노라고 했다."]], [["While the captain was taking all opportunities to press these and such like arguments, to remove the little foundling from Mr Allworthy's, of whose fondness for him he began to be jealous, Mrs Deborah had made a discovery, which, in its event, threatened at least to prove more fatal to poor Tommy than all the reasonings of the captain.", "대위가 틈만 나면 이러한 논리, 그리고 이와 비슷한 논리를 내세워 그 작은 업둥이를 올워디 씨에게서 떼어 놓으려 애쓰는 동안—그는 올워디 씨가 그 아이에게 쏟는 정에 슬슬 시샘이 일기 시작했던 것이다—데버러 부인은 한 가지 발견을 했고, 그 결과는 적어도 대위의 온갖 논변보다 가엾은 토미에게 더 치명적인 것으로 드러날 조짐을 보였다."]], [["Whether the insatiable curiosity of this good woman had carried her on to that business, or whether she did it to confirm herself in the good graces of Mrs Blifil, who, notwithstanding her outward behaviour to the foundling, frequently abused the infant in private, and her brother too, for his fondness to it, I will not determine; but she had now, as she conceived, fully detected the father of the foundling.", "이 선량한 부인의 채울 길 없는 호기심이 그 일에 그녀를 떠밀었는지, 아니면 블리필 부인의 환심을 더 굳히려고 그리한 것인지—블리필 부인은 업둥이를 향한 겉치레 행동과는 달리, 사사로이는 그 아기를 자주 욕했고, 그 아이에게 정을 쏟는 오라비마저 욕하곤 했다—나는 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 이제, 스스로 생각하기에, 그 업둥이의 아비를 완전히 찾아낸 셈이었다."]], [["Now, as this was a discovery of great consequence, it may be necessary to trace it from the fountain-head.", "자, 이는 자못 중대한 발견이었으니, 그 일을 근원에서부터 더듬어 보는 것이 필요하겠다."], ["We shall therefore very minutely lay open those previous matters by which it was produced; and for that purpose we shall be obliged to reveal all the secrets of a little family with which my reader is at present entirely unacquainted; and of which the oeconomy was so rare and extraordinary, that I fear it will shock the utmost credulity of many married persons.", "그러므로 우리는 그 발견을 낳은 앞선 사정들을 아주 세세하게 펼쳐 보일 것이다. 그러기 위해 우리는 지금 독자가 전혀 알지 못하는 어느 작은 집안의 온갖 비밀을 낱낱이 드러내야만 할 텐데, 그 집안의 살림살이가 어찌나 진귀하고 별난지, 결혼한 많은 이들의 더없는 곧이곧대로 믿는 마음마저 충격에 빠뜨리지 않을까 염려스럽다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["The description of a domestic government founded upon rules directly contrary to those of Aristotle.", "아리스토텔레스의 가르침과는 정반대되는 규칙 위에 세워진 한 가정 통치를 묘사함."]], [["My reader may please to remember he hath been informed that Jenny Jones had lived some years with a certain schoolmaster, who had, at her earnest desire, instructed her in Latin, in which, to do justice to her genius, she had so improved herself, that she was become a better scholar than her master.", "독자께서는 제니 존스가 어느 학교 선생과 몇 해를 함께 지냈으며, 그 선생이 그녀의 간곡한 청에 따라 라틴어를 가르쳐 주었다는 이야기를 앞서 들으셨음을 기억해 주시기 바란다. 그녀의 재능을 공정히 말하자면, 그녀는 그 라틴어 실력을 어찌나 키웠던지 스승보다 더 나은 학자가 되어 있었다."]], [["Indeed, though this poor man had undertaken a profession to which learning must be allowed necessary, this was the least of his commendations.", "사실, 이 가엾은 사내는 학식이 반드시 필요하다고 여겨지는 직업을 택하긴 했으나, 학식이란 그가 내세울 수 있는 장점 중 가장 사소한 것이었다."], ["He was one of the best-natured fellows in the world, and was, at the same time, master of so much pleasantry and humour, that he was reputed the wit of the country; and all the neighbouring gentlemen were so desirous of his company, that as denying was not his talent, he spent much time at their houses, which he might, with more emolument, have spent in his school.", "그는 세상에서 가장 마음씨 좋은 사람 가운데 하나였고, 동시에 익살과 해학에 어찌나 능했던지 그 고장의 재담꾼으로 이름이 나 있었다. 그래서 이웃의 신사들이 모두 그와 함께 어울리기를 바랐는데, 거절이란 그의 재주가 아니었던 까닭에, 그는 자기 학교에서 보냈더라면 더 많은 벌이가 되었을 시간을 그들의 집에서 흘려보냈다."]], [["It may be imagined that a gentleman so qualified and so disposed, was in no danger of becoming formidable to the learned seminaries of Eton or Westminster.", "이만한 자질을 갖추고 또 이만한 성품을 지닌 신사라면, 이튼이나 웨스트민스터 같은 명문 학당에 위협이 될 염려는 조금도 없었으리라 짐작할 만하다. 솔직히 말하자면, 그의 학생들은 두 부류로 나뉘어 있었다."], ["To speak plainly, his scholars were divided into two classes: in the upper of which was a young gentleman, the son of a neighbouring squire, who, at the age of seventeen, was just entered into his Syntaxis; and in the lower was a second son of the same gentleman, who, together with seven parish-boys, was learning to read and write.", "윗반에는 이웃 지주의 아들인 한 젊은 신사가 있었는데, 그는 열일곱 나이에 이제 막 구문론에 들어선 참이었다. 그리고 아랫반에는 같은 신사의 둘째 아들이 있었는데, 이 아이는 마을 소년 일곱 명과 함께 읽기와 쓰기를 배우고 있었다."]], [["The stipend arising hence would hardly have indulged the schoolmaster in the luxuries of life, had he not added to this office those of clerk and barber, and had not Mr Allworthy added to the whole an annuity of ten pounds, which the poor man received every Christmas, and with which he was enabled to cheer his heart during that sacred festival.", "여기서 나오는 보수만으로는 이 학교 선생이 삶의 호사를 누리기 어려웠을 것이다. 다만 그가 이 직책에 교회 서기와 이발사 노릇을 보탰고, 또 올워디 씨가 이 모든 것에 더해 해마다 십 파운드의 연금을 주었기에 사정이 달랐다. 가엾은 사내는 그 돈을 매년 성탄절마다 받았고, 그 덕분에 그 거룩한 명절 동안 마음을 흐뭇하게 다독일 수 있었다."]], [["Among his other treasures, the pedagogue had a wife, whom he had married out of Mr Allworthy's kitchen for her fortune, viz., twenty pounds, which she had there amassed.", "이 훈장에게는 여러 보물 가운데 아내가 하나 있었다. 그는 그녀의 재산, 곧 그녀가 그곳에서 모은 이십 파운드를 보고 올워디 씨의 주방에서 그녀를 데려와 혼인했던 것이다."]], [["This woman was not very amiable in her person.", "이 여인은 생김새가 그리 곱지는 않았다."], ["Whether she sat to my friend Hogarth, or no, I will not determine; but she exactly resembled the young woman who is pouring out her mistress's tea in the third picture of the Harlot's Progress.", "그녀가 내 벗 호가스의 그림 모델로 앉았는지 아닌지는 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 〈매춘부의 행로〉 세 번째 그림에서 여주인의 차를 따르고 있는 젊은 여자와 똑 닮아 있었다."], ["She was, besides, a profest follower of that noble sect founded by Xantippe of old; by means of which she became more formidable in the school than her husband; for, to confess the truth, he was never master there, or anywhere else, in her presence.", "게다가 그녀는 옛적 크산티페가 세운 그 고귀한 종파의 공공연한 추종자였다. 그 덕분에 그녀는 학교에서 남편보다 더 무서운 존재가 되었는데, 사실대로 고백하자면 남편은 그곳에서든 다른 어디에서든 그녀가 곁에 있는 한 결코 주인 노릇을 한 적이 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Though her countenance did not denote much natural sweetness of temper, yet this was, perhaps, somewhat soured by a circumstance which generally poisons matrimonial felicity; for children are rightly called the pledges of love; and her husband, though they had been married nine years, had given her no such pledges; a default for which he had no excuse, either from age or health, being not yet thirty years old, and what they call a jolly brisk young man.", "그녀의 얼굴이 천성적으로 다정한 기질을 그리 드러내 보이지는 않았지만, 그 다정함마저도 부부의 행복을 흔히 망쳐 놓는 어떤 사정 탓에 다소 시어졌는지도 모른다. 아이들은 응당 사랑의 증표라 불리는데, 그녀의 남편은 결혼한 지 아홉 해가 되도록 그녀에게 그런 증표를 하나도 주지 못했던 것이다. 이 결함에 대해 그는 나이로도 건강으로도 변명할 거리가 없었으니, 아직 서른도 되지 않은 데다 사람들이 말하는 이른바 혈기 왕성하고 활달한 젊은이였기 때문이다."]], [["Hence arose another evil, which produced no little uneasiness to the poor pedagogue, of whom she maintained so constant a jealousy, that he durst hardly speak to one woman in the parish; for the least degree of civility, or even correspondence, with any female, was sure to bring his wife upon her back, and his own.", "여기서 또 다른 화근이 생겨났는데, 이는 가엾은 훈장에게 적잖은 고통을 안겨 주었다. 그녀가 그를 어찌나 한결같이 질투하던지, 그는 교구 안의 어떤 여자에게도 감히 말을 건네지 못할 지경이었다. 어떤 여자에게든 조금이라도 예의를 차리거나, 심지어 편지 한 통이라도 주고받는 일이 있으면, 그것은 영락없이 아내를 그 여자에게, 그리고 자기 자신에게 달려들게 만들었기 때문이다."]], [["In order to guard herself against matrimonial injuries in her own house, as she kept one maid-servant, she always took care to chuse her out of that order of females whose faces are taken as a kind of security for their virtue; of which number Jenny Jones, as the reader hath been before informed, was one.", "제 집 안에서 부부 사이의 해를 막고자, 그녀는 하녀를 하나만 두면서도 늘 신경 써서, 그 얼굴이 곧 정조의 일종의 담보로 통하는 그런 부류의 여자들 중에서 골랐다. 그리고 앞서 독자께서 들으신 대로, 제니 존스도 바로 그런 부류에 속하는 여자였다."]], [["As the face of this young woman might be called pretty good security of the before-mentioned kind, and as her behaviour had been always extremely modest, which is the certain consequence of understanding in women; she had passed above four years at Mr Partridge's (for that was the schoolmaster's name) without creating the least suspicion in her mistress.", "이 젊은 여자의 얼굴은 앞서 말한 종류의 담보로는 꽤 든든하다 할 만했고, 또 그녀의 행실은 늘 더없이 정숙했으니—이는 여자에게 있어 분별의 확실한 결과다—그래서 그녀는 파트리지 씨(이것이 그 학교 선생의 이름이다)의 집에서 네 해 넘게 지내는 동안 안주인에게 추호의 의심도 사지 않았다."], ["Nay, she had been treated with uncommon kindness, and her mistress had permitted Mr Partridge to give her those instructions which have been before commemorated.", "아니, 오히려 그녀는 남다른 친절을 받았고, 안주인은 파트리지 씨가 앞서 이야기한 그 가르침을 그녀에게 베푸는 것까지 허락했던 것이다."]], [["But it is with jealousy as with the gout: when such distempers are in the blood, there is never any security against their breaking out; and that often on the slightest occasions, and when least suspected.", "그러나 질투란 통풍과도 같다. 그런 병이 핏속에 들어 있으면 언제 터져 나올지 결코 안심할 수 없으며, 더구나 그것은 흔히 가장 사소한 계기에, 가장 의심하지 않을 때 터져 나온다."]], [["Thus it happened to Mrs Partridge, who had submitted four years to her husband's teaching this young woman, and had suffered her often to neglect her work, in order to pursue her learning. For, passing by one day, as the girl was reading, and her master leaning over her, the girl, I know not for what reason, suddenly started up from her chair: and this was the first time that suspicion ever entered into the head of her mistress. This did not, however, at that time discover itself, but lay lurking in her mind, like a concealed enemy, who waits for a reinforcement of additional strength before he openly declares himself and proceeds upon hostile operations: and such additional strength soon arrived to corroborate her suspicion; for not long after, the husband and wife being at dinner, the master said to his maid, Da mihi aliquid potum: upon which the poor girl smiled, perhaps at the badness of the Latin, and, when her mistress cast her eyes on her, blushed, possibly with a consciousness of having laughed at her master. Mrs Partridge, upon this, immediately fell into a fury, and discharged the trencher on which she was eating, at the head of poor Jenny, crying out, “You impudent whore, do you play tricks with my husband before my face?” and at the same instant rose from her chair with a knife in her hand, with which, most probably, she would have executed very tragical vengeance, had not the girl taken the advantage of being nearer the door than her mistress, and avoided her fury by running away: for, as to the poor husband, whether surprize had rendered him motionless, or fear (which is full as probable) had restrained him from venturing at any opposition, he sat staring and trembling in his chair; nor did he once offer to move or speak, till his wife, returning from the pursuit of Jenny, made some defensive measures necessary for his own preservation; and he likewise was obliged to retreat, after the example of the maid.", "파트리지 부인에게도 바로 이런 일이 일어났다. 그녀는 네 해 동안 남편이 이 젊은 여자를 가르치는 일을 묵묵히 받아들였고, 그녀가 공부를 좇느라 제 일을 게을리하는 것도 여러 번 눈감아 주었다. 그런데 어느 날 그 곁을 지나가던 차에, 그 처녀가 글을 읽고 있고 선생이 그녀 위로 몸을 기울이고 있는데, 무슨 까닭에서인지 처녀가 별안간 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다. 안주인의 머릿속에 의심이 처음으로 깃든 것은 바로 이때였다. 그러나 그 의심은 그때 곧장 모습을 드러내지는 않고, 마치 숨어 있는 적이 더 큰 힘의 증원을 기다렸다가 그제야 공공연히 정체를 드러내고 적대 행위에 나서듯, 그녀의 마음속에 도사리고 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 의심을 단단히 굳혀 줄 그 증원군이 머지않아 당도했다. 얼마 지나지 않은 어느 날, 부부가 함께 식사를 하던 중에 선생이 하녀에게 \"다 미히 알리퀴드 포툼\"이라고 말했던 것이다. 그러자 가엾은 처녀가 빙긋 웃었는데, 어쩌면 그 라틴어가 형편없어서였을 것이다. 그리고 안주인이 자기에게 눈길을 던지자, 처녀는 얼굴을 붉혔으니, 아마도 주인을 비웃었다는 자각 때문이었으리라. 이에 파트리지 부인은 그 즉시 격분하여, 자기가 먹고 있던 나무 접시를 가엾은 제니의 머리를 향해 내던지며 소리쳤다. \"이 뻔뻔한 화냥년아, 내 눈앞에서 내 남편이랑 수작을 부려?\" 그러고는 같은 순간에 칼을 손에 들고 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰는데, 이는 십중팔구 비극적인 복수를 실행에 옮길 작정이었을 것이다. 다만 처녀가 안주인보다 문에 더 가까이 있는 이점을 살려 달아남으로써 그 분노를 피한 덕분이었다. 가엾은 남편으로 말하자면, 놀라움에 몸이 굳었는지, 아니면 (이쪽이 훨씬 더 그럴듯한데) 두려움에 어떤 맞섬도 감히 시도하지 못했는지, 그저 의자에 앉은 채 눈만 휘둥그레 뜨고 벌벌 떨고 있었다. 그는 한 번도 움직이려 하거나 입을 떼려 하지 않았으니, 마침내 아내가 제니를 쫓다가 돌아와 그가 제 한 몸을 지키기 위해 방어 조치를 취하지 않을 수 없게 만들 때까지 그러했다. 그리하여 그 역시 하녀의 본을 따라 물러나야만 했다. 이 선량한 여인은 오셀로와 마찬가지로,"]], [["This good woman was, no more than Othello, of a disposition", "질투로 한평생을 살아가며\n달이 바뀔 때마다 한결같이"]], [["To make a life of jealousy And follow still the changes of the moon With fresh suspicions--", "새로운 의심을 좇는—\n그런 기질의 사람은 아니었다. 오셀로가 그러했듯 그녀에게도,"]], [["With her, as well as him,", "—한번 의심한다는 것은"]], [["--To be once in doubt, Was once to be resolvd--", "곧 단번에 마음을 정하는 것이었으니—"]], [["she therefore ordered Jenny immediately to pack up her alls and begone, for that she was determined she should not sleep that night within her walls.", "그래서 그녀는 제니에게 당장 제 짐을 죄다 꾸려서 떠나라고 명했다. 그날 밤 그 처녀가 결단코 자기 집 담장 안에서 잠들게 두지 않으리라 마음먹은 것이다."]], [["Mr Partridge had profited too much by experience to interpose in a matter of this nature.", "파트리지 씨는 경험으로 너무 많은 것을 배운 터라 이런 일에 끼어들지 않았다."], ["He therefore had recourse to his usual receipt of patience, for, though he was not a great adept in Latin, he remembered, and well understood, the advice contained in these words", "그리하여 그는 늘 쓰던 처방인 인내에 의지했으니, 그가 라틴어의 대가는 아니었지만 다음 말에 담긴 충고만큼은 기억하고 또 잘 헤아리고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["--Leve fit quod bene fertur onus", "—레베 피트 쿼드 베네 페르투르 오누스"]], [["in English:", "영어로 하면 이렇다."]], [["A burden becomes lightest when it is well borne--", "짐이란 잘 짊어질 때 가장 가벼워진다—"]], [["which he had always in his mouth; and of which, to say the truth, he had often occasion to experience the truth.", "그는 이 말을 늘 입에 달고 살았으며, 사실대로 말하면 그 말의 참뜻을 몸소 겪을 일이 자주 있었다."]], [["Jenny offered to make protestations of her innocence; but the tempest was too strong for her to be heard.", "제니는 자기 결백을 항변하려 했으나, 폭풍이 너무 거세어 그녀의 말은 들리지조차 않았다."], ["She then betook herself to the business of packing, for which a small quantity of brown paper sufficed, and, having received her small pittance of wages, she returned home.", "그래서 그녀는 짐을 꾸리는 일에 매달렸는데, 거기에는 갈색 종이 한 줌이면 충분했다. 그러고 나서 쥐꼬리만 한 품삯을 받아 들고 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["The schoolmaster and his consort passed their time unpleasantly enough that evening, but something or other happened before the next morning, which a little abated the fury of Mrs Partridge; and she at length admitted her husband to make his excuses: to which she gave the readier belief, as he had, instead of desiring her to recall Jenny, professed a satisfaction in her being dismissed, saying, she was grown of little use as a servant, spending all her time in reading, and was become, moreover, very pert and obstinate; for, indeed, she and her master had lately had frequent disputes in literature; in which, as hath been said, she was become greatly his superior. This, however, he would by no means allow; and as he called her persisting in the right, obstinacy, he began to hate her with no small inveteracy.", "학교 선생과 그의 배우자는 그날 저녁을 자못 언짢게 보냈다. 그러나 다음 날 아침이 되기 전에 무슨 일인가가 일어나 파트리지 부인의 분노를 조금 누그러뜨렸고, 마침내 그녀는 남편이 변명할 기회를 허락했다. 그녀가 그 말을 한결 선뜻 믿어 준 것은, 남편이 제니를 다시 불러들이자고 청하기는커녕 오히려 그녀가 쫓겨난 것이 흡족하다고 공언했기 때문이다. 그의 말로는, 제니는 하녀로서 별 쓸모가 없어졌으니 온종일 책만 읽고, 게다가 몹시 건방지고 고집스러워졌다는 것이었다. 실은 근래 들어 그녀와 그 스승은 학문을 두고 자주 다투었는데, 앞서 말했듯이 그 다툼에서 그녀는 그를 크게 앞지른 처지가 되어 있었다. 그러나 그는 이 점을 결코 인정하려 들지 않았고, 그녀가 옳은 편을 고집하는 것을 두고 고집불통이라 부르며, 적잖은 앙심을 품고 그녀를 미워하기 시작했다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["Containing one of the most bloody battles, or rather duels, that were ever recorded in domestic history. For the reasons mentioned in the preceding chapter, and from some other matrimonial concessions, well known to most husbands, and which, like the secrets of freemasonry, should be divulged to none who are not members of that honourable fraternity, Mrs Partridge was pretty well satisfied that she had condemned her husband without cause, and endeavoured by acts of kindness to make him amends for her false suspicion.", "앞 장에서 말한 여러 이유들, 그리고 대부분의 남편들에게는 잘 알려져 있되 프리메이슨의 비밀처럼 그 명예로운 결사의 회원이 아닌 이에게는 결코 누설되어서는 안 될 다른 몇 가지 부부 사이의 양보들 덕분에, 파트리지 부인은 자신이 까닭 없이 남편을 단죄했음을 어지간히 깨닫게 되었고, 잘못된 의심을 보상하고자 다정한 행동으로 그에게 갚으려 애썼다."], ["Her passions were indeed equally violent, whichever way they inclined; for as she could be extremely angry, so could she be altogether as fond.", "사실 그녀의 격정은 어느 쪽으로 기울든 똑같이 사나웠다. 더없이 화를 낼 수 있는 만큼이나, 또한 더없이 살갑게 굴 수도 있었으니 말이다."]], [["But though these passions ordinarily succeed each other, and scarce twenty-four hours ever passed in which the pedagogue was not, in some degree, the object of both; yet, on extraordinary occasions, when the passion of anger had raged very high, the remission was usually longer: and so was the case at present; for she continued longer in a state of affability, after this fit of jealousy was ended, than her husband had ever known before: and, had it not been for some little exercises, which all the followers of Xantippe are obliged to perform daily, Mr Partridge would have enjoyed a perfect serenity of several months.", "그러나 이 두 격정이 보통은 번갈아 찾아들어, 이 학자 양반이 어느 정도는 그 둘 모두의 대상이 되지 않고 지나가는 날이 거의 하루도 없었음에도, 특별한 경우, 곧 분노의 격정이 몹시 거세게 들끓고 난 뒤에는 그 가라앉는 기간이 대개 더 길었다. 이번이 바로 그러했다. 이 질투의 발작이 끝난 뒤, 그녀는 남편이 일찍이 겪어 본 그 어느 때보다도 오래도록 다정한 상태에 머물렀다. 그래서 크산티페의 모든 추종자들이 날마다 행하지 않을 수 없는 그 자잘한 운동들만 아니었던들, 파트리지 씨는 여러 달에 걸친 더없는 평온을 누렸을 것이다."]], [["Perfect calms at sea are always suspected by the experienced mariner to be the forerunners of a storm, and I know some persons, who, without being generally the devotees of superstition, are apt to apprehend that great and unusual peace or tranquillity will be attended with its opposite.", "바다에서의 완벽한 잔잔함을, 노련한 뱃사람은 언제나 폭풍의 전조로 의심하게 마련이다. 또 나는 평소 미신을 떠받드는 부류가 아니면서도, 크고 별난 평화나 고요가 그 반대의 것을 동반하리라 짐작하는 사람들을 몇 알고 있다."], ["For which reason the antients used, on such occasions, to sacrifice to the goddess Nemesis, a deity who was thought by them to look with an invidious eye on human felicity, and to have a peculiar delight in overturning it.", "바로 그 까닭에, 옛사람들은 그런 경우에 네메시스 여신에게 제물을 바치곤 했다. 그들은 이 여신이 인간의 행복을 시샘하는 눈으로 바라보며, 그것을 뒤엎는 데 유난한 기쁨을 느낀다고 여겼다."]], [["As we are very far from believing in any such heathen goddess, or from encouraging any superstition, so we wish Mr John Fr----, or some other such philosopher, would bestir himself a little, in order to find out the real cause of this sudden transition from good to bad fortune, which hath been so often remarked, and of which we shall proceed to give an instance; for it is our province to relate facts, and we shall leave causes to persons of much higher genius.", "우리는 그런 이교의 여신을 믿는다거나 어떤 미신을 부추기는 것과는 거리가 멀기에, 부디 존 프 씨, 아니면 그런 부류의 다른 어떤 철학자가 조금이나마 분발하여, 그토록 자주 목격되어 온, 또 우리가 이제부터 한 사례를 들어 보이려 하는, 좋은 운에서 나쁜 운으로의 이 갑작스러운 전환의 진짜 원인을 밝혀내 주기를 바랄 따름이다. 사실을 이야기하는 것이 우리의 소관이요, 원인은 우리보다 훨씬 뛰어난 천재들에게 맡겨 두려 하니 말이다."]], [["Mankind have always taken great delight in knowing and descanting on the actions of others.", "인류는 언제나 남들의 행실을 알고 그것을 두고 떠드는 데서 큰 즐거움을 누려 왔다."], ["Hence there have been, in all ages and nations, certain places set apart for public rendezvous, where the curious might meet and satisfy their mutual curiosity.", "그리하여 어느 시대 어느 나라에나, 호기심 많은 이들이 모여 서로의 궁금증을 채울 수 있는, 공공의 만남을 위해 따로 마련된 특정한 장소들이 있어 왔다."], ["Among these, the barbers' shops have justly borne the pre-eminence.", "그중에서도 이발소가 단연 으뜸의 자리를 차지해 온 것은 마땅한 일이다."], ["Among the Greeks, barbers' news was a proverbial expression; and Horace, in one of his epistles, makes honourable mention of the Roman barbers in the same light.", "그리스인들 사이에서 '이발소 소식'은 속담처럼 쓰이는 표현이었고, 호라티우스도 그의 한 서간에서 로마의 이발사들을 같은 맥락으로 명예롭게 언급한 바 있다."]], [["Those of England are known to be no wise inferior to their Greek or Roman predecessors.", "잉글랜드의 이발소들이 그 그리스나 로마의 선배들에 결코 뒤지지 않음은 익히 알려진 사실이다."], ["You there see foreign affairs discussed in a manner little inferior to that with which they are handled in the coffee-houses; and domestic occurrences are much more largely and freely treated in the former than in the latter.", "거기서 여러분은 커피하우스에서 다뤄지는 것에 거의 뒤지지 않는 솜씨로 외국 정세가 논의되는 광경을 볼 수 있고, 집안 안팎의 사건들은 후자보다 전자에서 훨씬 폭넓고 거리낌 없이 다뤄진다."], ["But this serves only for the men.", "그러나 이는 어디까지나 남자들에게만 해당하는 이야기다."], ["Now, whereas the females of this country, especially those of the lower order, do associate themselves much more than those of other nations, our polity would be highly deficient, if they had not some place set apart likewise for the indulgence of their curiosity, seeing they are in this no way inferior to the other half of the species.", "그런데 이 나라의 여인들, 특히 하층 신분의 여인들은 다른 나라 여인들보다 훨씬 자주 어울려 모이는 만큼, 만약 그녀들의 호기심을 마음껏 채울 장소가 따로 마련되어 있지 않다면 우리의 사회 제도는 크게 모자란 것이 되리라. 이 점에서 그녀들은 인류의 나머지 절반에 조금도 뒤지지 않으니 말이다."]], [["In enjoying, therefore, such place of rendezvous, the British fair ought to esteem themselves more happy than any of their foreign sisters; as I do not remember either to have read in history, or to have seen in my travels, anything of the like kind.", "그러므로 그러한 만남의 장소를 누린다는 점에서, 영국의 미인들은 그 어느 외국 자매들보다도 스스로를 더 행복하다 여겨야 마땅하다. 나로서는 역사책에서 읽은 적도, 여행 중에 본 적도, 그와 비슷한 무엇을 기억하지 못하니 말이다."]], [["This place then is no other than the chandler's shop, the known seat of all the news; or, as it is vulgarly called, gossiping, in every parish in England.", "그렇다면 그 장소란 다름 아닌 잡화상, 곧 온갖 소식이 모여드는 익히 알려진 자리요, 잉글랜드의 모든 교구에서 흔히 '수다 떨기'라 불리는 바로 그곳이다."]], [["Mrs Partridge being one day at this assembly of females, was asked by one of her neighbours, if she had heard no news lately of Jenny Jones?", "어느 날 파트리지 부인이 이 여인들의 모임에 와 있는데, 이웃 가운데 한 사람이 요즘 제니 존스의 소식을 들은 것이 없느냐고 물었다."], ["To which she answered in the negative.", "부인은 없다고 답했다."], ["Upon this the other replied, with a smile, That the parish was very much obliged to her for having turned Jenny away as she did.", "그러자 상대는 미소를 띠며, 그렇게 제니를 내쫓아 준 덕에 교구가 부인께 크게 신세를 졌노라고 말했다."]], [["Mrs Partridge, whose jealousy, as the reader well knows, was long since cured, and who had no other quarrel to her maid, answered boldly, She did not know any obligation the parish had to her on that account; for she believed Jenny had scarce left her equal behind her.", "독자도 잘 알다시피 질투가 이미 오래전에 나은 데다, 하녀와 달리 다툴 거리가 없던 파트리지 부인은 거리낌 없이 대답했다. 그 일로 교구가 자기한테 무슨 신세를 졌다는 건지 모르겠노라고, 자기가 보기엔 제니가 자기만 한 사람을 뒤에 남겨 두고 가지도 못했을 거라고 말이다."]], [["“No, truly,”", "\"아무렴, 그렇고말고요.\""], ["said the gossip, “I hope not, though I fancy we have sluts enow too.", "수다쟁이가 말했다. \"그러지 않았기를 바라죠."], ["Then you have not heard, it seems, that she hath been brought to bed of two bastards?", "하긴 우리 동네에도 헤픈 계집이야 차고 넘치지만요. 그럼 그 소식도 못 들으셨나 보군요?"], ["but as they are not born here, my husband and the other overseer says we shall not be obliged to keep them.”", "그 애가 사생아 둘을 낳고 산후조리 중이라는 거 말이에요. 하지만 여기서 난 게 아니니, 우리 남편이랑 다른 빈민감독관 말로는 우리가 걔들을 떠맡을 의무는 없다더라고요.\""]], [["“Two bastards!”", "\"사생아 둘이라니!\""], ["answered Mrs Partridge hastily: “you surprize me!", "파트리지 부인이 황급히 받았다. \"놀랍군요!"], ["I don't know whether we must keep them; but I am sure they must have been begotten here, for the wench hath not been nine months gone away.”", "우리가 그 애들을 떠맡아야 하는지 어떤지는 모르겠지만, 분명 여기서 잉태된 게 틀림없어요. 그 계집이 떠난 지 아홉 달도 채 안 됐으니까요.\""]], [["Nothing can be so quick and sudden as the operations of the mind, especially when hope, or fear, or jealousy, to which the two others are but journeymen, set it to work.", "마음의 작용만큼 빠르고 돌연한 것은 없다. 더구나 희망이나 두려움, 또는 그 두 가지를 한낱 졸개로 부리는 질투가 마음을 부려 댈 때면 더욱 그러하다."], ["It occurred instantly to her, that Jenny had scarce ever been out of her own house while she lived with her.", "제니가 함께 사는 동안 제집 밖을 거의 나간 적이 없었다는 사실이 그녀에게 단번에 떠올랐다."], ["The leaning over the chair, the sudden starting up, the Latin, the smile, and many other things, rushed upon her all at once.", "의자에 기대 몸을 숙이던 일, 화들짝 일어서던 일, 그 라틴어, 그 미소, 그 밖의 숱한 일들이 한꺼번에 그녀에게 밀려들었다."], ["The satisfaction her husband expressed in the departure of Jenny, appeared now to be only dissembled; again, in the same instant, to be real; but yet to confirm her jealousy, proceeding from satiety, and a hundred other bad causes.", "제니가 떠날 때 남편이 내비친 만족스러움이 이제는 한낱 꾸민 것으로만 보였다가, 같은 순간에 다시 진심으로 보였다가, 그러나 그것이 물림과 그 밖의 백 가지 못된 까닭에서 비롯된 것이라며 도리어 질투를 굳혀 주었다."], ["In a word, she was convinced of her husband's guilt, and immediately left the assembly in confusion.", "한마디로, 그녀는 남편의 죄를 확신했고, 곧장 혼란에 빠진 채 모임을 떠났다."]], [["As fair Grimalkin, who, though the youngest of the feline family, degenerates not in ferocity from the elder branches of her house, and though inferior in strength, is equal in fierceness to the noble tiger himself, when a little mouse, whom it hath long tormented in sport, escapes from her clutches for a while, frets, scolds, growls, swears; but if the trunk, or box, behind which the mouse lay hid be again removed, she flies like lightning on her prey, and, with envenomed wrath, bites, scratches, mumbles, and tears the little animal.", "아름다운 그리말킨은 고양잇과 중 가장 막내이면서도 그 집안 손위 가지들에 못지않은 사나움을 지녔고, 힘은 뒤질지언정 그 흉포함만큼은 고귀한 호랑이 그 자체에 견줄 만한지라, 오래도록 장난삼아 괴롭혀 온 어린 생쥐가 잠시 그 발톱에서 빠져나가면 안달하고 호통치고 으르렁대고 욕설을 퍼붓다가도, 생쥐가 숨었던 궤짝이나 상자가 다시 치워지면 번개처럼 먹잇감에게 달려들어 독기 어린 분노로 그 작은 짐승을 물어뜯고 할퀴고 우물거리고 갈가리 찢는다."]], [["Not with less fury did Mrs Partridge fly on the poor pedagogue.", "그에 못지않은 사나움으로 파트리지 부인은 가엾은 학자 양반에게 달려들었다."], ["Her tongue, teeth, and hands, fell all upon him at once.", "혀와 이와 손이 한꺼번에 그에게 쏟아졌다."], ["His wig was in an instant torn from his head, his shirt from his back, and from his face descended five streams of blood, denoting the number of claws with which nature had unhappily armed the enemy.", "가발은 순식간에 머리에서 뜯겨 나갔고, 셔츠는 등에서 찢겨 나갔으며, 얼굴에서는 다섯 줄기 피가 흘러내려, 자연이 불행하게도 이 적에게 쥐여 준 발톱의 수를 일러 주었다."]], [["Mr Partridge acted for some time on the defensive only; indeed he attempted only to guard his face with his hands; but as he found that his antagonist abated nothing of her rage, he thought he might, at least, endeavour to disarm her, or rather to confine her arms; in doing which her cap fell off in the struggle, and her hair being too short to reach her shoulders, erected itself on her head; her stays likewise, which were laced through one single hole at the bottom, burst open; and her breasts, which were much more redundant than her hair, hung down below her middle; her face was likewise marked with the blood of her husband: her teeth gnashed with rage; and fire, such as sparkles from a smith's forge, darted from her eyes. So that, altogether, this Amazonian heroine might have been an object of terror to a much bolder man than Mr Partridge.", "파트리지 씨는 한동안 방어만 했다. 사실 그는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리려 애쓸 뿐이었다. 그러나 적수가 분노를 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않는 것을 보고는, 적어도 무장을 풀어 놓거나, 아니 차라리 그 두 팔을 붙들어 두려고는 해 봐야겠다 싶었다. 그러는 사이 몸싸움 끝에 그녀의 모자가 벗겨졌고, 어깨에 닿기에는 너무 짧은 머리카락이 머리 위로 곤두섰다. 또한 밑단의 구멍 하나로만 끈을 꿴 그녀의 코르셋이 터져 벌어지면서, 머리카락보다 훨씬 풍성한 가슴이 허리 아래까지 늘어졌다. 얼굴에는 남편의 피가 묻어 있었고, 이는 분노로 뿌득뿌득 갈렸으며, 대장간 화덕에서 튀는 듯한 불꽃이 두 눈에서 쏟아졌다. 그러니 이 모든 것을 합쳐 놓고 보면, 이 아마존 여전사는 파트리지 씨보다 훨씬 대담한 남자에게조차 공포의 대상이 되고도 남았을 것이다."]], [["He had, at length, the good fortune, by getting possession of her arms, to render those weapons which she wore at the ends of her fingers useless; which she no sooner perceived, than the softness of her sex prevailed over her rage, and she presently dissolved in tears, which soon after concluded in a fit.", "마침내 그는 운 좋게도 그녀의 두 팔을 붙잡아, 손끝에 달린 그 무기들을 무용지물로 만들었다. 그러자 그녀는 그것을 알아채기가 무섭게, 여인 특유의 여린 천성이 분노를 누르고 이내 눈물로 녹아내리더니, 곧 그 끝에 발작으로 이어졌다."]], [["That small share of sense which Mr Partridge had hitherto preserved through this scene of fury, of the cause of which he was hitherto ignorant, now utterly abandoned him.", "이 분노의 한바탕 소동 내내 그 까닭조차 알지 못한 채 파트리지 씨가 그나마 지켜 온 그 얼마 안 되는 분별마저, 이제는 그를 완전히 저버렸다."], ["He ran instantly into the street, hallowing out that his wife was in the agonies of death, and beseeching the neighbours to fly with the utmost haste to her assistance.", "그는 곧장 거리로 뛰쳐나가, 아내가 죽음의 고통 속에 있다고 고래고래 외치며, 이웃들에게 한시바삐 달려와 그녀를 도와 달라고 애원했다."], ["Several good women obeyed his summons, who entering his house, and applying the usual remedies on such occasions, Mrs Partridge was at length, to the great joy of her husband, brought to herself.", "몇몇 마음씨 좋은 아낙들이 그 부름에 응해 그의 집으로 들어와, 이런 경우에 흔히 쓰는 처방을 베푸니, 마침내 파트리지 부인은 남편의 크나큰 기쁨 속에 정신을 차렸다."]], [["As soon as she had a little recollected her spirits, and somewhat composed herself with a cordial, she began to inform the company of the manifold injuries she had received from her husband; who, she said, was not contented to injure her in her bed; but, upon her upbraiding him with it, had treated her in the cruelest manner imaginable; had tore her cap and hair from her head, and her stays from her body, giving her, at the same time, several blows, the marks of which she should carry to the grave.", "기력을 조금 추스르고 강심제로 어느 정도 마음을 가라앉히자, 그녀는 남편에게서 받은 갖가지 해코지를 좌중에게 일러바치기 시작했다. 그녀의 말인즉, 남편은 잠자리에서 자기를 욕보이는 것으로 모자라, 그 일을 두고 따져 묻자 상상할 수 있는 가장 잔인한 방식으로 자기를 다루었으며, 모자와 머리카락을 머리에서 쥐어뜯고 몸에서 코르셋을 잡아 뜯었을 뿐 아니라, 그러는 와중에 여러 차례 매질까지 해 그 자국을 무덤에까지 지고 가게 만들었다는 것이었다."]], [["The poor man, who bore on his face many more visible marks of the indignation of his wife, stood in silent astonishment at this accusation; which the reader will, I believe, bear witness for him, had greatly exceeded the truth; for indeed he had not struck her once; and this silence being interpreted to be a confession of the charge by the whole court, they all began at once, una voce, to rebuke and revile him, repeating often, that none but a coward ever struck a woman.", "얼굴에 아내의 분노가 남긴 자국을 그보다 훨씬 또렷이 달고 있던 이 가엾은 남자는, 이 고발 앞에 말없이 아연실색하여 서 있었다. 독자도 그를 위해 증언해 주리라 믿거니와, 그 고발은 진실을 한참 넘어선 것이었다. 실로 그는 그녀를 단 한 차례도 때린 적이 없었으니 말이다. 그런데 이 침묵이 온 법정에 의해 혐의를 인정하는 것으로 풀이되니, 그녀들은 일제히 한목소리로 그를 나무라고 욕하기 시작하여, 여자에게 손찌검하는 자는 비겁한 놈밖에 없다고 거듭거듭 되뇌었다."]], [["Mr Partridge bore all this patiently; but when his wife appealed to the blood on her face, as an evidence of his barbarity, he could not help laying claim to his own blood, for so it really was; as he thought it very unnatural, that this should rise up (as we are taught that of a murdered person often doth) in vengeance against him.", "파트리지 씨는 이 모든 것을 꾹 참고 견뎠다. 그러나 아내가 자기 얼굴의 피를 그의 야만성의 증거랍시고 들이대자, 그는 그것이 제 피임을 주장하지 않을 수 없었다. 실제로도 그것은 그의 피였으니, 살해당한 자의 피가 흔히 그러하다고들 배워 온 것처럼 이 피가 자기에게 복수하려 들고일어나는 것은 참으로 가당찮다고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["To this the women made no other answer, than that it was a pity it had not come from his heart, instead of his face; all declaring, that, if their husbands should lift their hands against them, they would have their hearts'", "이에 아낙들은 다른 대꾸 없이, 그 피가 얼굴이 아니라 그의 심장에서 나왔더라면 좋았을 것을 안타깝다고만 했다."], ["bloods out of their bodies.", "그러면서 하나같이, 만약 자기 남편이 자기들에게 손을 쳐든다면 그놈 몸에서 심장의 피를 뽑아내고 말겠다고 큰소리쳤다."]], [["After much admonition for what was past, and much good advice to Mr Partridge for his future behaviour, the company at length departed, and left the husband and wife to a personal conference together, in which Mr Partridge soon learned the cause of all his sufferings.", "지난 일에 대한 숱한 훈계와, 앞으로의 처신에 대해 파트리지 씨에게 건네는 숱한 좋은 충고가 오간 끝에, 마침내 좌중은 흩어지고 부부만 남아 둘만의 담판을 벌이게 되었다. 그 자리에서 파트리지 씨는 곧 자신이 겪은 그 모든 고초의 원인을 알게 되었다."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["Containing much matter to exercise the judgment and reflection of the reader.", "독자의 판단과 성찰을 일깨우는 많은 내용을 담은 장."]], [["I believe it is a true observation, that few secrets are divulged to one person only; but certainly, it would be next to a miracle that a fact of this kind should be known to a whole parish, and not transpire any farther.", "비밀이 단 한 사람에게만 새어 나가는 일은 드물다는 말은 참된 관찰이라고 나는 믿는다. 그러나 이런 종류의 사실이 교구 하나 전체에 알려지고도 그 이상으로 퍼지지 않는다면, 그것이야말로 기적에 버금가는 일이 분명할 것이다."]], [["And, indeed, a very few days had past, before the country, to use a common phrase, rung of the schoolmaster of Little Baddington; who was said to have beaten his wife in the most cruel manner.", "실제로 며칠 지나지 않아, 흔한 표현을 빌리자면, 리틀 배딩턴의 학교 선생 이야기로 온 동네가 떠들썩해졌다. 그가 아내를 더없이 잔인하게 때렸다는 것이었다."], ["Nay, in some places it was reported he had murdered her; in others, that he had broke her arms; in others, her legs: in short, there was scarce an injury which can be done to a human creature, but what Mrs Partridge was somewhere or other affirmed to have received from her husband.", "아니, 어떤 곳에서는 그가 아내를 죽였다는 소문이 돌았고, 다른 곳에서는 팔을 부러뜨렸다고, 또 다른 곳에서는 다리를 부러뜨렸다고들 했다. 한마디로, 인간이라는 피조물에게 가해질 수 있는 상해 가운데 파트리지 부인이 남편에게서 당했다고 어딘가에서 단언되지 않은 것은 거의 없을 지경이었다."]], [["The cause of this quarrel was likewise variously reported; for as some people said that Mrs Partridge had caught her husband in bed with his maid, so many other reasons, of a very different kind, went abroad.", "이 다툼의 원인 또한 가지각색으로 전해졌다. 어떤 이들은 파트리지 부인이 남편이 하녀와 한 침대에 있는 것을 붙잡았다고 했고, 또 사뭇 다른 종류의 다른 이유들도 여럿 나돌았다."], ["Nay, some transferred the guilt to the wife, and the jealousy to the husband.", "아니, 어떤 이들은 죄를 아내에게로 돌리고 질투를 남편에게로 돌리기까지 했다."]], [["Mrs Wilkins had long ago heard of this quarrel; but, as a different cause from the true one had reached her ears, she thought proper to conceal it; and the rather, perhaps, as the blame was universally laid on Mr Partridge; and his wife, when she was servant to Mr Allworthy, had in something offended Mrs Wilkins, who was not of a very forgiving temper.", "윌킨스 부인은 이 다툼에 대해 오래전에 들어 알고 있었다. 그러나 진짜 원인과는 다른 사연이 그녀의 귀에 들어왔기에, 그녀는 그것을 덮어 두는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼다. 어쩌면 그 비난이 한결같이 파트리지 씨에게 쏟아지고 있었기 때문에 더욱 그러했는지도 모른다. 게다가 그의 아내는 올워디 씨의 하녀로 있던 시절 무언가로 윌킨스 부인의 비위를 거슬렀던 적이 있었는데, 윌킨스 부인은 그리 너그럽게 용서하는 성품이 아니었다."]], [["But Mrs Wilkins, whose eyes could see objects at a distance, and who could very well look forward a few years into futurity, had perceived a strong likelihood of Captain Blifil's being hereafter her master; and as she plainly discerned that the captain bore no great goodwill to the little foundling, she fancied it would be rendering him an agreeable service, if she could make any discoveries that might lessen the affection which Mr Allworthy seemed to have contracted for this child, and which gave visible uneasiness to the captain, who could not entirely conceal it even before Allworthy himself; though his wife, who acted her part much better in public, frequently recommended to him her own example, of conniving at the folly of her brother, which, she said, she at least as well perceived, and as much resented, as any other possibly could.", "그러나 멀리 있는 대상까지 내다보는 눈을 지녔고, 몇 해 앞의 미래까지도 능히 헤아릴 줄 알았던 윌킨스 부인은, 블리필 대위가 머지않아 자신의 주인이 될 가능성이 다분함을 알아차렸다. 그리고 대위가 그 어린 업둥이에게 별다른 호의를 품고 있지 않음을 분명히 간파했기에, 올워디 씨가 이 아이에게 품은 듯한 애정—대위에게는 눈에 띄게 거북함을 주었고, 그래서 그가 올워디 씨 앞에서조차 온전히 감추지 못했던 그 애정—을 덜어 줄 만한 무언가를 캐낼 수 있다면, 그것이 대위에게 흡족한 봉사가 되리라고 생각했다. 그의 아내는 사람들 앞에서 자기 역할을 훨씬 능란하게 해내며, 남편에게 자신의 본보기를 자주 권했다. 즉 자기 오라비의 어리석음을 못 본 척 눈감아 주라는 것이었는데, 그녀는 자기야말로 그 어리석음을 누구 못지않게 잘 알아채고 누구 못지않게 못마땅해한다고 말하곤 했다."]], [["Mrs Wilkins having therefore, by accident, gotten a true scent of the above story,--though long after it had happened, failed not to satisfy herself thoroughly of all the particulars; and then acquainted the captain, that she had at last discovered the true father of the little bastard, which she was sorry, she said, to see her master lose his reputation in the country, by taking so much notice of.", "그리하여 윌킨스 부인은 우연히 위 이야기의 참된 냄새를 맡게 되자—일이 벌어지고 한참이 지난 뒤이긴 했으나—온갖 자세한 사정을 빠짐없이 확인하는 일을 게을리하지 않았다. 그러고는 대위에게, 마침내 그 어린 사생아의 진짜 아비를 알아냈노라고, 주인께서 그 아이를 그토록 마음에 두시는 탓에 시골에서 명성을 잃으시는 것이 안타깝다고 아뢰었다."]], [["The captain chid her for the conclusion of her speech, as an improper assurance in judging of her master's actions: for if his honour, or his understanding, would have suffered the captain to make an alliance with Mrs Wilkins, his pride would by no means have admitted it.", "대위는 그녀가 말끝에 덧붙인 결론을 꾸짖었다. 주인의 행동을 함부로 판단하는 주제넘은 짓이라는 것이었다."], ["And to say the truth, there is no conduct less politic, than to enter into any confederacy with your friend's servants against their master: for by these means you afterwards become the slave of these very servants; by whom you are constantly liable to be betrayed.", "설령 그의 명예나 분별이 대위로 하여금 윌킨스 부인과 한패가 되도록 허락했다 하더라도, 그의 자존심은 결코 그것을 받아들이지 않았을 것이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 벗의 하인들과 손잡고 그 주인에게 맞서는 것만큼 분별없는 처사도 없다."], ["And this consideration, perhaps it was, which prevented Captain Blifil from being more explicit with Mrs Wilkins, or from encouraging the abuse which she had bestowed on Allworthy.", "그렇게 하다가는 나중에 바로 그 하인들의 종이 되고 말며, 그들에게 언제든 배신당할 위험에 놓이기 때문이다. 어쩌면 바로 이런 고려 때문에 블리필 대위는 윌킨스 부인에게 더 속을 터놓지 못했고, 그녀가 올워디에게 퍼부은 험담을 부추기지도 않았을 것이다."]], [["But though he declared no satisfaction to Mrs Wilkins at this discovery, he enjoyed not a little from it in his own mind, and resolved to make the best use of it he was able.", "그러나 그는 이 발견에 대해 윌킨스 부인에게 만족하는 기색을 전혀 내비치지 않았으면서도, 속으로는 그것을 적잖이 즐겼고, 그것을 할 수 있는 한 최대로 활용하기로 작정했다."]], [["He kept this matter a long time concealed within his own breast, in hopes that Mr Allworthy might hear it from some other person; but Mrs Wilkins, whether she resented the captain's behaviour, or whether his cunning was beyond her, and she feared the discovery might displease him, never afterwards opened her lips about the matter.", "그는 올워디 씨가 다른 누군가에게서 이 일을 듣게 되기를 바라며, 이 문제를 오랫동안 제 가슴속에만 묻어 두었다. 그러나 윌킨스 부인은, 대위의 처신에 앙심을 품었기 때문인지, 아니면 그의 교활함이 자기 머리 위에 있어 그 발설이 그의 비위를 거스를까 두려웠기 때문인지, 그 후로는 이 일에 대해 결코 입을 열지 않았다."]], [["I have thought it somewhat strange, upon reflection, that the housekeeper never acquainted Mrs Blifil with this news, as women are more inclined to communicate all pieces of intelligence to their own sex, than to ours.", "돌이켜 생각해 보니, 여자들은 모든 소식을 우리 남자보다는 자기와 같은 성에게 전하려는 성향이 더 강한 법인데도, 그 가정부가 이 소식을 블리필 부인에게 한 번도 알리지 않았다는 것이 다소 이상하게 여겨졌다."], ["The only way, as it appears to me, of solving this difficulty, is, by imputing it to that distance which was now grown between the lady and the housekeeper: whether this arose from a jealousy in Mrs Blifil, that Wilkins showed too great a respect to the foundling; for while she was endeavouring to ruin the little infant, in order to ingratiate herself with the captain, she was every day more and more commending it before Allworthy, as his fondness for it every day increased.", "내가 보기에 이 난점을 풀 유일한 길은, 그것을 이제 부인과 가정부 사이에 자라난 그 거리감 탓으로 돌리는 것이다. 이 거리감이 윌킨스가 업둥이에게 지나치게 큰 존중을 보인 데서 비롯된 블리필 부인의 질투에서 생겨난 것인지는 모르겠으나, 윌킨스는 대위의 환심을 사기 위해 그 어린 갓난것을 망치려 애쓰는 한편으로, 올워디의 그 아이에 대한 애정이 날로 커져 가자 그 앞에서는 날마다 점점 더 그 아이를 칭찬했던 것이다."], ["This, notwithstanding all the care she took at other times to express the direct contrary to Mrs Blifil, perhaps offended that delicate lady, who certainly now hated Mrs Wilkins; and though she did not, or possibly could not, absolutely remove her from her place, she found, however, the means of making her life very uneasy.", "다른 때에는 블리필 부인에게 정반대를 표하려 그토록 조심했음에도, 이 일은 어쩌면 그 까다로운 부인의 비위를 거슬렀고, 부인은 이제 분명 윌킨스 부인을 미워하게 되었다. 그리고 부인은 그녀를 그 자리에서 완전히 내쫓지는 않았거나, 어쩌면 내쫓을 수 없었으나, 어쨌든 그녀의 삶을 몹시 고달프게 만드는 방법은 찾아냈다."], ["This Mrs Wilkins, at length, so resented, that she very openly showed all manner of respect and fondness to little Tommy, in opposition to Mrs Blifil.", "윌킨스 부인은 마침내 이에 분개한 나머지, 블리필 부인에게 보란 듯이 어린 토미에게 온갖 존중과 애정을 아주 공공연히 드러냈다."]], [["The captain, therefore, finding the story in danger of perishing, at last took an opportunity to reveal it himself.", "그리하여 대위는 그 이야기가 사그라들 위험에 처한 것을 알고, 마침내 기회를 잡아 스스로 그것을 폭로하기에 이르렀다."]], [["He was one day engaged with Mr Allworthy in a discourse on charity: in which the captain, with great learning, proved to Mr Allworthy, that the word charity in Scripture nowhere means beneficence or generosity.", "어느 날 그는 올워디 씨와 자선에 관한 담론에 빠져 있었는데, 거기서 대위는 대단한 학식을 동원하여, 성경에서 자선이라는 말이 어디에서도 선행이나 너그러움을 뜻하지 않는다는 것을 올워디 씨에게 입증해 보였다."]], [["“The Christian religion,”", "\"기독교는,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “was instituted for much nobler purposes, than to enforce a lesson which many heathen philosophers had taught us long before, and which, though it might perhaps be called a moral virtue, savoured but little of that sublime, Christian-like disposition, that vast elevation of thought, in purity approaching to angelic perfection, to be attained, expressed, and felt only by grace.", "\"수많은 이교 철학자들이 우리에게 이미 오래전에 가르쳐 준 교훈을 강요하려고 세워진 것이 아니라, 그보다 훨씬 더 고귀한 목적을 위해 세워진 것입니다. 그 교훈이란 것은, 어쩌면 도덕적 미덕이라 불릴 수는 있겠으나, 오직 은총으로만 이르고, 표현하고, 느낄 수 있는 그 숭고하고 기독교다운 성품, 천사의 완전함에 순결함으로 다가가는 그 드높은 사고의 고양과는 거의 닮은 데가 없습니다."], ["Those,”", "그 말을 너그러움, 곧 우리 형제들에 대해 선의의 견해를 품고 그들의 행동에 호의적인 판단을 내리는 것으로 이해한 이들이야말로 성경의 뜻에 더 가까이 다가간 것입니다."], ["he said, “came nearer to the Scripture meaning, who understood by it candour, or the forming of a benevolent opinion of our brethren, and passing a favourable judgment on their actions; a virtue much higher, and more extensive in its nature, than a pitiful distribution of alms, which, though we would never so much prejudice, or even ruin our families, could never reach many; whereas charity, in the other and truer sense, might be extended to all mankind.”", "이것은 그 본성에서 보잘것없는 적선의 분배보다 훨씬 더 높고 훨씬 더 폭넓은 미덕입니다. 적선이란, 우리가 아무리 우리 가족을 해치거나 심지어 파산시키더라도, 결코 많은 이에게 미칠 수 없는 것이지만, 다른 의미, 곧 더 참된 의미의 자선은 온 인류에게 미칠 수 있는 것이니까요.\""]], [["He said, “Considering who the disciples were, it would be absurd to conceive the doctrine of generosity, or giving alms, to have been preached to them.", "그가 말했다. \"그 제자들이 어떤 사람들이었는지를 생각하면, 너그러움이나 적선의 교리가 그들에게 설파되었으리라 여기는 것은 터무니없는 일입니다."], ["And, as we could not well imagine this doctrine should be preached by its Divine Author to men who could not practise it, much less should we think it understood so by those who can practise it, and do not.", "또한 이 교리가 그것을 실천할 수 없는 사람들에게 그 거룩하신 창시자에 의해 설파되었다고 상상하기 어려운 만큼, 그것을 실천할 수 있으면서도 하지 않는 사람들이 그렇게 이해했으리라고는 더더욱 생각해서는 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“But though,” continued he, “there is, I am afraid, little merit in these benefactions, there would, I must confess, be much pleasure in them to a good mind, if it was not abated by one consideration. I mean, that we are liable to be imposed upon, and to confer our choicest favours often on the undeserving, as you must own was your case in your bounty to that worthless fellow Partridge: for two or three such examples must greatly lessen the inward satisfaction which a good man would otherwise find in generosity; nay, may even make him timorous in bestowing, lest he should be guilty of supporting vice, and encouraging the wicked; a crime of a very black dye, and for which it will by no means be a sufficient excuse, that we have not actually intended such an encouragement; unless we have used the utmost caution in chusing the objects of our beneficence. A consideration which, I make no doubt, hath greatly checked the liberality of many a worthy and pious man.”", "\"그러나,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"이런 자선 행위에는 유감스럽게도 공덕이 거의 없을지언정, 한 가지 고려 사항만 없다면 선한 마음에는 거기서 큰 기쁨이 있으리라는 것을 저도 인정하지 않을 수 없습니다. 제 말은, 우리가 속아 넘어가, 우리의 가장 귀한 호의를 자격 없는 자에게 베푸는 일이 흔하다는 것입니다. 저 변변찮은 파트리지에게 베푸신 후의가 바로 그런 경우였음을 어르신께서도 인정하셔야 할 것입니다. 그런 사례가 두세 번만 있어도, 선한 사람이 너그러움 속에서 으레 찾을 그 내면의 만족이 크게 줄어들 수밖에 없습니다. 아니, 그것은 그가 베푸는 일조차 두려워하게 만들 수도 있습니다. 악덕을 떠받치고 사악한 자를 부추기는 죄를 짓게 될까 봐서요. 그것은 그 빛깔이 아주 시커먼 죄이며, 우리가 실제로 그런 부추김을 의도하지는 않았다는 변명만으로는 결코 충분치 못합니다. 우리가 선행의 대상을 고르는 데 더없는 신중함을 기하지 않았다면 말이지요. 의심할 바 없이 이 고려 사항은 수많은 가치 있고 경건한 사람의 베풂을 크게 위축시켜 왔을 것입니다.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy answered, “He could not dispute with the captain in the Greek language, and therefore could say nothing as to the true sense of the word which is translated charity; but that he had always thought it was interpreted to consist in action, and that giving alms constituted at least one branch of that virtue.", "올워디 씨가 대답했다. \"나는 그리스어를 두고 대위와 다툴 수가 없으니, 자선으로 번역되는 그 말의 참뜻에 대해서는 아무 말도 할 수 없네. 다만 나는 그것이 늘 행동에 있다고 풀이된다고 생각해 왔고, 적선이야말로 적어도 그 미덕의 한 갈래를 이룬다고 여겨 왔네.\""]], [["“As to the meritorious part,”", "\"공덕의 측면에 관해서는,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “he readily agreed with the captain; for where could be the merit of barely discharging a duty?", "\"기꺼이 대위의 의견에 동의하네. 한낱 의무를 다하는 데 무슨 공덕이 있겠는가?"], ["which,” he said, “let the word charity have what construction it would, it sufficiently appeared to be from the whole tenor of the New Testament.", "자선이라는 말이 어떤 해석을 가지든, 그것이 의무임은 신약 전체의 흐름으로 보아 충분히 드러난다고 보네."], ["And as he thought it an indispensable duty, enjoined both by the Christian law, and by the law of nature itself; so was it withal so pleasant, that if any duty could be said to be its own reward, or to pay us while we are discharging it, it was this.", "또한 나는 그것이 기독교의 율법으로나 자연의 법칙 그 자체로나 명해진, 없어서는 안 될 의무라고 생각하는데, 그러면서도 그것은 어찌나 즐거운 일인지, 만약 어떤 의무가 그 자체로 보상이 된다거나 우리가 그것을 행하는 동안 우리에게 보답한다고 말할 수 있다면, 그것이 바로 이 의무일 것이네.\""]], [["“To confess the truth,”", "\"솔직히 털어놓자면,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “there is one degree of generosity (of charity I would have called it), which seems to have some show of merit, and that is, where, from a principle of benevolence and Christian love, we bestow on another what we really want ourselves; where, in order to lessen the distresses of another, we condescend to share some part of them, by giving what even our own necessities cannot well spare.", "\"한 가지 정도의 너그러움(나는 그것을 자선이라 부르고 싶네만)은 얼마간 공덕의 빛을 띠는 듯하네. 곧 선의와 기독교적 사랑의 원리에서, 우리 자신에게 정말로 필요한 것을 남에게 베푸는 경우, 곧 남의 고통을 덜어 주기 위해 우리 자신의 궁핍으로도 도저히 내어 줄 형편이 못 되는 것을 내어 줌으로써 그 고통의 일부를 기꺼이 나누어 지는 경우 말이네."], ["This is, I think, meritorious; but to relieve our brethren only with our superfluities; to be charitable (I must use the word) rather at the expense of our coffers than ourselves; to save several families from misery rather than hang up an extraordinary picture in our houses or gratify any other idle ridiculous vanity--this seems to be only being human creatures.", "이것은, 내 생각에, 공덕이 있는 일이지. 그러나 우리의 남아도는 것으로만 형제들을 돕는 것, 우리 자신을 축내기보다는 우리 금고를 축내어 자선을 베푸는 것(이 말을 꼭 써야겠군), 집에 비범한 그림 한 점을 걸어 두거나 그 밖의 헛되고 우스꽝스러운 허영을 채우기보다는 차라리 여러 가정을 비참에서 구해 내는 것—이것은 그저 인간다운 피조물이 되는 것에 지나지 않는 듯하네."], ["Nay, I will venture to go farther, it is being in some degree epicures: for what could the greatest epicure wish rather than to eat with many mouths instead of one?", "아니, 나는 더 나아가 감히 말하겠네. 그것은 어느 정도 미식가가 되는 것이라고."], ["which I think may be predicated of any one who knows that the bread of many is owing to his own largesses.", "가장 대단한 미식가라도 입 하나가 아니라 여러 입과 함께 먹는 것보다 더 바랄 게 무엇이겠는가? 여러 사람의 빵이 자기 베풂 덕분임을 아는 자라면 누구에게나 이렇게 말할 수 있다고 나는 생각하네.\""]], [["“As to the apprehension of bestowing bounty on such as may hereafter prove unworthy objects, because many have proved such; surely it can never deter a good man from generosity.", "\"앞으로 자격 없는 대상으로 드러날지도 모를 자에게 후의를 베풀까 봐 염려하는 것에 대해서는, 많은 이가 그렇게 드러났다는 이유로 그러한 것일 텐데, 그것이 선한 사람을 너그러움에서 물러서게 할 수는 결코 없을 것이네."], ["I do not think a few or many examples of ingratitude can justify a man's hardening his heart against the distresses of his fellow-creatures; nor do I believe it can ever have such effect on a truly benevolent mind.", "나는 배은망덕의 사례가 적든 많든, 그것이 사람이 자기 동료 피조물의 고통 앞에서 마음을 굳게 닫는 일을 정당화할 수 있다고는 생각하지 않으며, 진실로 선의 어린 마음에 그런 영향을 미칠 수 있으리라고도 믿지 않네."], ["Nothing less than a persuasion of universal depravity can lock up the charity of a good man; and this persuasion must lead him, I think, either into atheism, or enthusiasm; but surely it is unfair to argue such universal depravity from a few vicious individuals; nor was this, I believe, ever done by a man, who, upon searching his own mind, found one certain exception to the general rule.”", "만인이 타락했다는 확신만이 선한 사람의 자선을 닫아걸 수 있을 것이며, 그 확신은, 내 생각에, 그를 무신론이나 광신으로 이끌 수밖에 없을 것이네. 그러나 몇몇 사악한 개인들을 두고 그런 만인의 타락을 논하는 것은 분명 온당치 못한 일이며, 자기 마음을 들여다보아 그 일반 법칙에 대해 단 하나라도 확실한 예외를 찾아낸 사람이라면, 결코 그런 짓을 하지 않으리라고 나는 믿네.\""], ["He then concluded by asking, “who that Partridge was, whom he had called a worthless fellow?”", "그러고서 그는 이렇게 물으며 말을 맺었다. \"그런데 자네가 변변찮은 자라 부른 그 파트리지란 누구인가?\""]], [["“I mean,”", "\"제가 말씀드리는 건,\""], ["said the captain, “Partridge the barber, the schoolmaster, what do you call him?", "대위가 말했다. \"이발사 파트리지, 그 학교 선생, 뭐라고 부르시던가요?"], ["Partridge, the father of the little child which you found in your bed.”", "파트리지 말입니다, 어르신께서 침대에서 발견하신 그 어린아이의 아비 말이지요.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy exprest great surprize at this account, and the captain as great at his ignorance of it; for he said he had known it above a month: and at length recollected with much difficulty that he was told it by Mrs Wilkins.", "올워디 씨는 이 이야기에 크게 놀라움을 드러냈고, 대위는 그가 그것을 모르고 있었다는 데에 그만큼이나 크게 놀랐다. 자기는 그 일을 한 달도 더 전부터 알고 있었노라고 했기 때문이다. 그러고는 한참을 어렵사리 떠올린 끝에, 그것을 윌킨스 부인에게서 들었노라고 했다."]], [["Upon this, Wilkins was immediately summoned; who having confirmed what the captain had said, was by Mr Allworthy, by and with the captain's advice, dispatched to Little Baddington, to inform herself of the truth of the fact: for the captain exprest great dislike at all hasty proceedings in criminal matters, and said he would by no means have Mr Allworthy take any resolution either to the prejudice of the child or its father, before he was satisfied that the latter was guilty; for though he had privately satisfied himself of this from one of Partridge's neighbours, yet he was too generous to give any such evidence to Mr Allworthy.", "이에 윌킨스가 즉시 불려 왔다. 그녀가 대위의 말을 확인해 주자, 올워디 씨는 대위의 조언에 따라 그녀를 리틀 배딩턴으로 보내 그 사실의 진위를 직접 알아 오게 했다. 대위가 형사적인 문제에서 성급하게 처리하는 것을 몹시 꺼리는 기색을 보이며, 후자가 죄를 지었음을 확실히 하기 전에는 올워디 씨가 그 아이나 그 아비에게 불리한 어떤 결정도 결코 내리지 않으셨으면 한다고 말했기 때문이다. 사실 그는 파트리지의 이웃 가운데 하나에게서 이 일을 사사로이 확인해 두었으면서도, 너무도 너그러운 나머지 그런 증거를 올워디 씨에게 내놓지 않았던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["The trial of Partridge, the schoolmaster, for incontinency; the evidence of his wife; a short reflection on the wisdom of our law; with other grave matters, which those will like best who understand them most.", "교사 파트리지가 부정을 저질렀다는 죄목으로 받는 재판. 그의 아내가 내놓은 증언. 우리 법의 지혜에 대한 짧은 단상. 그리고 가장 잘 이해하는 이들이 가장 좋아할 다른 묵직한 문제들."]], [["It may be wondered that a story so well known, and which had furnished so much matter of conversation, should never have been mentioned to Mr Allworthy himself, who was perhaps the only person in that country who had never heard of it.", "이토록 널리 알려져 그 많은 입길에 오르내린 이야기가, 어째서 정작 올워디 씨 본인에게는 한 번도 전해지지 않았는지 의아할 법하다. 그 고장에서 이 일을 들어 본 적 없는 사람은 아마 그가 유일했을 테니 말이다."]], [["To account in some measure for this to the reader, I think proper to inform him, that there was no one in the kingdom less interested in opposing that doctrine concerning the meaning of the word charity, which hath been seen in the preceding chapter, than our good man.", "독자에게 이 사정을 어느 정도 풀어 드리고자, 나는 이렇게 일러두는 편이 옳다고 본다. 앞 장에서 다룬바, 자선이라는 낱말의 뜻을 둘러싼 그 논리에 맞설 마음이 이 나라에서 우리의 선량한 분만큼 적은 이도 없었다고 말이다."], ["Indeed, he was equally intitled to this virtue in either sense; for as no man was ever more sensible of the wants, or more ready to relieve the distresses of others, so none could be more tender of their characters, or slower to believe anything to their disadvantage.", "실로 그는 어느 쪽 의미로 보든 이 미덕을 똑같이 누릴 자격이 있었다. 남의 궁핍을 누구보다 잘 헤아렸고 남의 곤경을 누구보다 기꺼이 덜어 주었듯, 남의 평판에 누구보다 너그러웠으며 그들에게 불리한 일이라면 무엇이든 누구보다 더디게 믿었기 때문이다."]], [["Scandal, therefore, never found any access to his table; for as it hath been long since observed that you may know a man by his companions, so I will venture to say, that, by attending to the conversation at a great man's table, you may satisfy yourself of his religion, his politics, his taste, and indeed of his entire disposition: for though a few odd fellows will utter their own sentiments in all places, yet much the greater part of mankind have enough of the courtier to accommodate their conversation to the taste and inclination of their superiors.", "그러니 험담은 결코 그의 식탁에 발붙이지 못했다. 사람을 그 벗으로 알 수 있다는 말이 예부터 있어 왔듯, 나 또한 감히 이렇게 말해 두겠다. 위대한 인물의 식탁에서 오가는 대화에 귀를 기울이면, 그 사람의 신앙과 정치 성향, 취향, 나아가 됨됨이 전체를 어렵잖이 가늠할 수 있다고. 어디서든 제 생각을 거리낌 없이 내뱉는 별난 자들이 몇 있긴 하지만, 사람들 대다수는 윗사람의 취향과 기호에 제 대화를 맞출 만큼의 아첨기는 충분히 지니고 있으니 말이다."]], [["But to return to Mrs Wilkins, who, having executed her commission with great dispatch, though at fifteen miles distance, brought back such a confirmation of the schoolmaster's guilt, that Mr Allworthy determined to send for the criminal, and examine him viva voce. Mr Partridge, therefore, was summoned to attend, in order to his defence (if he could make any) against this accusation.", "하지만 윌킨스 부인에게로 돌아가 보자. 그녀는 십오 마일이나 떨어진 곳에서도 임무를 어찌나 빠르게 처리했던지, 교사가 죄를 지었다는 사실을 확실히 뒷받침할 증거를 가지고 돌아왔다. 그리하여 올워디 씨는 그 죄인을 불러들여 직접 입으로 묻기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 파트리지 씨는 이 고발에 맞서 (할 수 있다면) 변론하라는 소환을 받았다. * 직접 입으로 묻기: 원문은 라틴어 viva voce, 곧 구두로 직접 심문한다는 뜻."]], [["At the time appointed, before Mr Allworthy himself, at Paradise-hall, came as well the said Partridge, with Anne, his wife, as Mrs Wilkins his accuser.", "정해진 시각에 파라다이스홀의 올워디 씨 앞으로, 앞서 말한 파트리지가 아내 앤과 함께, 그리고 그를 고발한 윌킨스 부인이 나란히 나왔다."]], [["And now Mr Allworthy being seated in the chair of justice, Mr Partridge was brought before him.", "이제 올워디 씨가 재판관의 자리에 앉자, 파트리지 씨가 그 앞에 끌려 나왔다."], ["Having heard his accusation from the mouth of Mrs Wilkins, he pleaded not guilty, making many vehement protestations of his innocence.", "윌킨스 부인의 입에서 자기 죄목을 듣고 난 그는 결백을 주장하며, 자신은 무죄라고 격렬하게 거듭 항변했다."]], [["Mrs Partridge was then examined, who, after a modest apology for being obliged to speak the truth against her husband, related all the circumstances with which the reader hath already been acquainted; and at last concluded with her husband's confession of his guilt.", "다음으로 파트리지 부인이 심문을 받았다. 그녀는 남편에게 불리한 진실을 말할 수밖에 없게 된 처지를 겸손하게 변명한 뒤, 독자가 이미 알고 있는 정황을 빠짐없이 늘어놓았고, 마침내 남편이 제 죄를 자백했다는 말로 끝맺었다."]], [["Whether she had forgiven him or no, I will not venture to determine; but it is certain she was an unwilling witness in this cause; and it is probable from certain other reasons, would never have been brought to depose as she did, had not Mrs Wilkins, with great art, fished all out of her at her own house, and had she not indeed made promises, in Mr Allworthy's name, that the punishment of her husband should not be such as might anywise affect his family.", "그녀가 그를 용서했는지 어떤지는 내가 감히 단정하지 못하겠다. 다만 이 사건에서 그녀가 마지못해 나선 증인이었다는 것만큼은 분명하다. 또 다른 몇 가지 이유로 미루어 보건대, 윌킨스 부인이 큰 수완을 발휘해 그녀의 집에서 모든 이야기를 낚아내지 않았더라면, 그리고 남편에 대한 처벌이 그 가족에게는 결코 해가 미치지 않게 하겠다고 올워디 씨의 이름을 빌려 약속해 주지 않았더라면, 그녀는 결코 그렇게 증언하도록 끌려 나오지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Partridge still persisted in asserting his innocence, though he admitted he had made the above-mentioned confession; which he however endeavoured to account for, by protesting that he was forced into it by the continued importunity she used: who vowed, that, as she was sure of his guilt, she would never leave tormenting him till he had owned it; and faithfully promised, that, in such case, she would never mention it to him more. Hence, he said, he had been induced falsely to confess himself guilty, though he was innocent; and that he believed he should have confest a murder from the same motive.", "파트리지는 앞서 말한 자백을 한 적이 있음은 인정하면서도 여전히 자신의 결백을 주장했다. 다만 그는 그 자백을 이렇게 해명하려 했다. 아내가 끊임없이 들볶는 통에 어쩔 수 없이 자백하게 되었노라고. 아내는 그가 죄를 지은 것이 틀림없으니, 그가 인정할 때까지 절대 괴롭히기를 멈추지 않겠다고 다짐했고, 일단 인정만 하면 다시는 그 일을 입에 올리지 않겠노라 굳게 약속했다는 것이다. 그래서 그는 결백한데도 거짓으로 죄를 인정하게 되었으며, 같은 까닭이라면 살인이라도 자백했을 것이라고 했다."]], [["Mrs Partridge could not bear this imputation with patience; and having no other remedy in the present place but tears, she called forth a plentiful assistance from them, and then addressing herself to Mr Allworthy, she said (or rather cried), “May it please your worship, there never was any poor woman so injured as I am by that base man; for this is not the only instance of his falsehood to me.", "파트리지 부인은 이 비난을 참고 견딜 수가 없었다. 이 자리에서 그녀가 기댈 수단이라곤 눈물뿐이었으므로, 그녀는 눈물을 펑펑 불러내 도움을 청한 다음 올워디 씨를 향해 이렇게 말했다(아니, 차라리 울부짖었다)."], ["No, may it please your worship, he hath injured my bed many's the good time and often.", "\"나리, 어디 저 비열한 인간한테 저처럼 당한 가엾은 계집이 또 있겠습니까."], ["I could have put up with his drunkenness and neglect of his business, if he had not broke one of the sacred commandments.", "저 인간이 저를 속인 게 어디 이번 한 번뿐이겠어요. 아닙니다요, 나리, 저 인간은 좋은 시절 내내 몇 번이고 제 잠자리를 더럽혔답니다."], ["Besides, if it had been out of doors I had not mattered it so much; but with my own servant, in my own house, under my own roof, to defile my own chaste bed, which to be sure he hath, with his beastly stinking whores.", "술주정에 일까지 내팽개치는 거야 그러려니 참아 줄 수도 있었어요, 신성한 계명 하나만 깨뜨리지 않았다면요. 게다가 그게 바깥에서 있었던 일이라면 그렇게까지 마음 쓰지도 않았을 텐데, 제 집 종년이랑, 제 집에서, 제 지붕 아래서, 제 정숙한 잠자리를 더럽히다니요."], ["Yes, you villain, you have defiled my own bed, you have; and then you have charged me with bullocking you into owning the truth.", "정말이지 저 인간이 그 짐승 같고 구린내 나는 갈보들이랑 그 짓을 했단 말이에요. 그래, 이 악당아, 네가 내 잠자리를 더럽혀 놓고선, 이제 와서 내가 너를 윽박질러 사실을 인정하게 했다고 뒤집어씌워?"], ["It is very likely, an't please your worship, that I should bullock him?", "나리, 제가 저 인간을 윽박지를 만하게 생겼습니까요?"], ["I have marks enow about my body to show of his cruelty to me.", "저 인간이 저한테 얼마나 모질게 굴었는지 제 몸뚱이에 흔적이 차고 넘칩니다."], ["If you had been a man, you villain, you would have scorned to injure a woman in that manner.", "이 악당아, 네가 사내였다면 그런 식으로 계집을 괴롭히는 짓은 부끄러워서라도 못 했을 거다."], ["But you an't half a man, you know it.", "하지만 넌 반쪽짜리 사내도 못 돼, 너도 알잖아."], ["Nor have you been half a husband to me.", "나한테 반쪽짜리 남편 노릇도 못 했고."], ["You need run after whores, you need, when I'm sure--And since he provokes me, I am ready, an't please your worship, to take my bodily oath that I found them a-bed together.", "갈보들 꽁무니나 쫓아다녀야겠지, 그래야겠지, 내가 분명히 아는데—저 인간이 저를 이렇게 부추기니, 나리, 저는 두 사람이 한 침대에 같이 누워 있는 걸 제 눈으로 봤다고 이 몸에 걸고 맹세할 준비가 돼 있습니다."], ["What, you have forgot, I suppose, when you beat me into a fit, and made the blood run down my forehead, because I only civilly taxed you with adultery!", "뭐야, 잊어버린 모양인데, 내가 그저 점잖게 간통을 따졌다는 이유로 네가 나를 두들겨 패서 발작을 일으키게 하고 이마에서 피가 흐르게 한 일은 어쩌고!"], ["but I can prove it by all my neighbours.", "그건 이웃 사람 누구한테 물어봐도 다 증명할 수 있다고."], ["You have almost broke my heart, you have, you have.”", "네가 내 가슴을 거의 찢어 놓은 거야, 네가, 네가.\""]], [["Here Mr Allworthy interrupted, and begged her to be pacified, promising her that she should have justice; then turning to Partridge, who stood aghast, one half of his wits being hurried away by surprize and the other half by fear, he said he was sorry to see there was so wicked a man in the world.", "여기서 올워디 씨가 말을 끊고는, 정의는 반드시 세워질 테니 부디 진정하라고 그녀를 달랬다. 그러고는 파트리지에게로 몸을 돌렸는데, 그는 넋이 반쯤 나가 서 있었다."], ["He assured him that his prevaricating and lying backward and forward was a great aggravation of his guilt; for which the only atonement he could make was by confession and repentance.", "정신의 반은 놀라움에, 나머지 반은 두려움에 휩쓸려 가 버린 꼴이었다. 올워디 씨는 세상에 이토록 사악한 자가 있는 것을 보니 안타깝다고 말했다."], ["He exhorted him, therefore, to begin by immediately confessing the fact, and not to persist in denying what was so plainly proved against him even by his own wife.", "그리고 이리저리 말을 둘러대며 거짓을 늘어놓는 것은 그 죄를 한층 무겁게 만드는 일이며, 그에 대해 할 수 있는 유일한 속죄란 자백과 회개뿐이라고 단언했다. 그러므로 그는 파트리지에게, 우선 곧장 사실을 자백하는 것부터 시작하고, 제 아내까지 나서서 이토록 명백히 증명한 일을 끝까지 부인하지 말라고 권고했다."]], [["Here, reader, I beg your patience a moment, while I make a just compliment to the great wisdom and sagacity of our law, which refuses to admit the evidence of a wife for or against her husband.", "여기서, 독자여, 잠시만 인내해 주시기를 청한다. 그동안 나는 우리 법의 위대한 지혜와 분별력에 마땅한 찬사를 보내려 한다."], ["This, says a certain learned author, who, I believe, was never quoted before in any but a law-book, would be the means of creating an eternal dissension between them.", "우리 법은 남편을 위해서든 그에게 불리하게든 아내의 증언을 받아들이기를 거부하니 말이다. 어느 학식 높은 저자의 말을 빌리자면(내 생각에 이 사람의 글이 법률서가 아닌 데서 인용된 적은 여태 한 번도 없을 것이다), 그렇지 않으면 부부 사이에 끝없는 불화의 씨앗을 뿌리는 셈이 된다고 한다."], ["It would, indeed, be the means of much perjury, and of much whipping, fining, imprisoning, transporting, and hanging.", "실로 그것은 숱한 위증을 낳고, 숱한 채찍질과 벌금형, 투옥과 유배, 그리고 교수형을 불러오는 빌미가 될 것이다."]], [["Partridge stood a while silent, till, being bid to speak, he said he had already spoken the truth, and appealed to Heaven for his innocence, and lastly to the girl herself, whom he desired his worship immediately to send for; for he was ignorant, or at least pretended to be so, that she had left that part of the country.", "파트리지는 한동안 말없이 서 있다가, 말하라는 명을 받자 자신은 이미 진실을 말했노라고 했다. 그러고는 자신의 결백을 두고 하늘에 호소했고, 마지막으로는 그 처녀 본인에게 호소하며, 부디 나리께서 당장 그녀를 불러 주십사 청했다. 그녀가 이미 그 고장을 떠난 줄을 그는 모르고 있었거나, 적어도 모르는 척하고 있었다."]], [["Mr Allworthy, whose natural love of justice, joined to his coolness of temper, made him always a most patient magistrate in hearing all the witnesses which an accused person could produce in his defence, agreed to defer his final determination of this matter till the arrival of Jenny, for whom he immediately dispatched a messenger; and then having recommended peace between Partridge and his wife (though he addressed himself chiefly to the wrong person), he appointed them to attend again the third day; for he had sent Jenny a whole day's journey from his own house.", "올워디 씨는 타고난 정의감에 차분한 성품까지 더해진 덕에, 피고가 제 변론을 위해 내세울 수 있는 증인이라면 누구든 끝까지 참을성 있게 들어 주는 더없이 인내심 깊은 재판관이었다. 그리하여 그는 제니가 도착할 때까지 이 사건의 최종 판결을 미루기로 했고, 곧장 그녀를 데려올 심부름꾼을 보냈다. 그런 다음 파트리지와 그 아내 사이에 화목하기를 권한 뒤(다만 그는 주로 엉뚱한 쪽을 향해 그 말을 했다), 사흘째 되는 날 다시 출두하라고 두 사람에게 명했다. 제니를 자기 집에서 꼬박 하루 거리나 떨어진 곳으로 보냈던 까닭이다."]], [["At the appointed time the parties all assembled, when the messenger returning brought word, that Jenny was not to be found; for that she had left her habitation a few days before, in company with a recruiting officer.", "정해진 시각에 당사자들이 모두 모였는데, 심부름꾼이 돌아와 전한 말은 제니를 찾을 수 없다는 것이었다. 그녀가 며칠 전에 어느 모병 장교와 함께 살던 집을 떠나 버렸다는 것이다."]], [["Mr Allworthy then declared that the evidence of such a slut as she appeared to be would have deserved no credit; but he said he could not help thinking that, had she been present, and would have declared the truth, she must have confirmed what so many circumstances, together with his own confession, and the declaration of his wife that she had caught her husband in the fact, did sufficiently prove.", "그러자 올워디 씨는, 보아하니 그런 헤픈 계집의 증언이라면 애초에 믿을 만한 가치도 없었을 것이라고 잘라 말했다. 다만 그녀가 그 자리에 있어 진실을 밝혔더라면, 그토록 많은 정황과 더불어 그 자신의 자백, 그리고 남편이 현장에서 붙잡힌 것을 보았다는 아내의 진술이 충분히 증명한 바를 그녀 역시 확인해 주었을 것이 틀림없다고 생각하지 않을 수 없다고 했다."], ["He therefore once more exhorted Partridge to confess; but he still avowing his innocence, Mr Allworthy declared himself satisfied of his guilt, and that he was too bad a man to receive any encouragement from him.", "그래서 그는 파트리지에게 다시 한번 자백하라고 권했으나, 그가 여전히 결백을 주장하자, 올워디 씨는 그의 죄를 확신한다고, 그리고 그는 자신에게서 어떤 격려도 받기에는 너무 나쁜 인간이라고 선언했다."], ["He therefore deprived him of his annuity, and recommended repentance to him on account of another world, and industry to maintain himself and his wife in this.", "그리하여 그에게서 연금을 거두어들이고는, 저세상을 생각하여 회개하고 이 세상에서는 부지런히 일해 제 한 몸과 아내를 건사하라고 권했다."]], [["There were not, perhaps, many more unhappy persons than poor Partridge.", "가엾은 파트리지보다 더 불행한 사람은 아마 그리 많지 않았을 것이다."], ["He had lost the best part of his income by the evidence of his wife, and yet was daily upbraided by her for having, among other things, been the occasion of depriving her of that benefit; but such was his fortune, and he was obliged to submit to it.", "그는 아내의 증언 탓에 수입의 가장 큰 몫을 잃었으면서도, 다른 일들과 더불어 바로 그 연금까지 빼앗기게 만든 장본인이라는 이유로 날마다 아내에게 들볶였다. 하지만 그것이 그의 팔자였고, 그는 그것을 받아들이는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Though I called him poor Partridge in the last paragraph, I would have the reader rather impute that epithet to the compassion in my temper than conceive it to be any declaration of his innocence.", "앞 문단에서 그를 가엾은 파트리지라 불렀지만, 독자는 그 수식어를 그의 결백을 선언하는 말로 받아들이기보다 내 천성에 깃든 연민에서 나온 것으로 여겨 주기를 바란다."], ["Whether he was innocent or not will perhaps appear hereafter; but if the historic muse hath entrusted me with any secrets, I will by no means be guilty of discovering them till she shall give me leave.", "그가 결백했는지 아닌지는 어쩌면 이후에 드러날 것이다. 다만 역사의 뮤즈가 내게 어떤 비밀을 맡겨 두었다 해도, 그녀가 허락을 내리기 전까지는 그것을 누설하는 죄를 결코 짓지 않겠다."]], [["Here therefore the reader must suspend his curiosity. Certain it is that, whatever was the truth of the case, there was evidence more than sufficient to convict him before Allworthy; indeed, much less would have satisfied a bench of justices on an order of bastardy; and yet, notwithstanding the positiveness of Mrs Partridge, who would have taken the sacrament upon the matter, there is a possibility that the schoolmaster was entirely innocent: for though it appeared clear on comparing the time when Jenny departed from Little Baddington with that of her delivery that she had there conceived this infant, yet it by no means followed of necessity that Partridge must have been its father; for, to omit other particulars, there was in the same house a lad near eighteen, between whom and Jenny there had subsisted sufficient intimacy to found a reasonable suspicion; and yet, so blind is jealousy, this circumstance never once entered into the head of the enraged wife.", "그러니 여기서 독자는 잠시 호기심을 미뤄 두어야 한다. 분명한 것은, 이 사건의 진실이 무엇이었든 간에 올워디 앞에서 그를 유죄로 단정할 증거가 차고 넘쳤다는 사실이다. 실로 사생아 부양 명령을 내리는 치안판사석이라면 이보다 훨씬 적은 증거로도 만족했을 것이다. 그런데도, 이 일을 두고 성찬에 걸고서라도 맹세하려 들었을 파트리지 부인의 단호함에도 불구하고, 그 교사가 완전히 결백했을 가능성은 여전히 있다. 제니가 리틀 배딩턴을 떠난 시점과 그녀가 아이를 낳은 시점을 견주어 보면 그녀가 그곳에서 이 아이를 잉태했다는 것이 분명해 보이긴 했으나, 그렇다고 해서 파트리지가 반드시 그 아비여야만 한다는 결론이 필연적으로 따라 나오는 것은 결코 아니었다. 다른 자잘한 정황은 접어 두더라도, 같은 집에 열여덟 살 가까운 사내아이가 하나 있었는데, 그와 제니 사이에는 합당한 의심을 품을 만한 친밀함이 충분히 있었던 것이다. 그런데도 질투란 워낙 눈먼 것이어서, 격분한 아내의 머릿속에는 이런 정황이 단 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다."]], [["Whether Partridge repented or not, according to Mr Allworthy's advice, is not so apparent.", "파트리지가 올워디 씨의 충고에 따라 회개했는지 어떤지는 그리 분명치 않다."], ["Certain it is that his wife repented heartily of the evidence she had given against him: especially when she found Mrs Deborah had deceived her, and refused to make any application to Mr Allworthy on her behalf.", "분명한 것은, 그의 아내가 남편에게 불리하게 한 증언을 진심으로 후회했다는 사실이다. 특히 데버라 부인이 자기를 속였으며, 자기를 위해 올워디 씨에게 어떤 청도 넣어 주지 않으려 한다는 것을 알았을 때 더욱 그러했다."], ["She had, however, somewhat better success with Mrs Blifil, who was, as the reader must have perceived, a much better-tempered woman, and very kindly undertook to solicit her brother to restore the annuity; in which, though good-nature might have some share, yet a stronger and more natural motive will appear in the next chapter.", "그래도 블리필 부인에게는 다소 나은 결과를 거두었는데, 독자도 익히 짐작했겠지만 그녀는 훨씬 너그러운 성품의 여인이어서, 연금을 되돌려 주도록 제 오라비에게 청을 넣어 주는 일을 아주 친절히 떠맡았다. 여기에 선한 마음도 얼마쯤 몫을 했겠지만, 더 강하고 자연스러운 동기가 무엇이었는지는 다음 장에서 드러날 것이다."]], [["These solicitations were nevertheless unsuccessful: for though Mr Allworthy did not think, with some late writers, that mercy consists only in punishing offenders; yet he was as far from thinking that it is proper to this excellent quality to pardon great criminals wantonly, without any reason whatever.", "그럼에도 이런 청탁은 끝내 받아들여지지 않았다. 올워디 씨는 자비란 오로지 죄인을 벌하는 데 있다고 본 요즘 어떤 저술가들과 생각을 달리하긴 했으나, 그렇다고 아무런 까닭도 없이 큰 죄인을 함부로 용서하는 것이 그 훌륭한 덕성에 걸맞은 일이라고 여기는 것과는 한참 거리가 멀었다."], ["Any doubtfulness of the fact, or any circumstance of mitigation, was never disregarded: but the petitions of an offender, or the intercessions of others, did not in the least affect him.", "사실 관계에 의심스러운 점이 있거나 정상을 참작할 만한 사정이 있다면 그는 결코 그것을 무시하지 않았다. 그러나 죄인 본인의 탄원이나 다른 이들의 중재는 그의 마음을 조금도 움직이지 못했다."], ["In a word, he never pardoned because the offender himself, or his friends, were unwilling that he should be punished.", "한마디로 그는 죄인 본인이나 그 벗들이 처벌을 원치 않는다는 이유만으로 누군가를 용서한 적이 없었다."]], [["Partridge and his wife were therefore both obliged to submit to their fate; which was indeed severe enough: for so far was he from doubling his industry on the account of his lessened income, that he did in a manner abandon himself to despair; and as he was by nature indolent, that vice now increased upon him, by which means he lost the little school he had; so that neither his wife nor himself would have had any bread to eat, had not the charity of some good Christian interposed, and provided them with what was just sufficient for their sustenance.", "그리하여 파트리지와 그 아내는 둘 다 제 운명에 굴복하는 수밖에 없었으니, 그 운명이란 실로 가혹하기 그지없는 것이었다. 수입이 줄어든 만큼 부지런함을 곱절로 늘리기는커녕, 그는 어느새 절망에 제 몸을 내맡겨 버렸다. 게다가 그는 천성이 게을렀던지라 이제 그 악덕이 더욱 심해졌고, 그 탓에 가지고 있던 자그마한 학교마저 잃고 말았다. 그래서 어느 마음씨 고운 기독교인의 자선이 끼어들어 겨우 끼니나 이을 만큼을 마련해 주지 않았더라면, 그의 아내도 그 자신도 입에 풀칠할 빵 한 조각조차 없었을 것이다."]], [["As this support was conveyed to them by an unknown hand, they imagined, and so, I doubt not, will the reader, that Mr Allworthy himself was their secret benefactor; who, though he would not openly encourage vice, could yet privately relieve the distresses of the vicious themselves, when these became too exquisite and disproportionate to their demerit. In which light their wretchedness appeared now to Fortune herself; for she at length took pity on this miserable couple, and considerably lessened the wretched state of Partridge, by putting a final end to that of his wife, who soon after caught the small-pox, and died.", "이 도움이 누군지 모를 손길을 통해 그들에게 전해졌으므로, 그들은 올워디 씨 본인이 자기들의 은밀한 은인이리라 짐작했고, 독자 역시 그러하리라고 나는 의심치 않는다. 그분은 드러내 놓고 악을 두둔하지는 않으려 했으나, 악한 자들이 겪는 곤경이 그 죗값에 견주어 지나치게 혹독하고 어울리지 않을 만큼 커지면 남몰래 그것을 덜어 줄 수는 있는 분이었다. 이제 운명의 여신마저 그들의 비참함을 바로 그런 시선으로 바라보았던 모양이다. 여신은 마침내 이 가련한 부부를 가엾이 여겨, 파트리지의 비참한 처지를 적잖이 덜어 주었으니, 곧 아내의 비참함에 종지부를 찍어 준 것이다. 아내는 얼마 지나지 않아 천연두에 걸려 세상을 떠났다."]], [["The justice which Mr Allworthy had executed on Partridge at first met with universal approbation; but no sooner had he felt its consequences, than his neighbours began to relent, and to compassionate his case; and presently after, to blame that as rigour and severity which they before called justice.", "올워디 씨가 파트리지에게 내린 처분은 처음에는 모두의 찬동을 얻었다. 그러나 사람들이 그 결과를 직접 보게 되자마자, 이웃들은 마음이 누그러져 그의 처지를 가엾게 여기기 시작했고, 이내 전에는 정의라 부르던 것을 이제 와 가혹하고 모진 처사라고 탓하기 시작했다."], ["They now exclaimed against punishing in cold blood, and sang forth the praises of mercy and forgiveness.", "이제 그들은 냉혹하게 벌을 내리는 짓을 두고 소리 높여 비난하면서, 자비와 용서를 찬양하는 노래를 불러 댔다."]], [["These cries were considerably increased by the death of Mrs Partridge, which, though owing to the distemper above mentioned, which is no consequence of poverty or distress, many were not ashamed to impute to Mr Allworthy's severity, or, as they now termed it, cruelty.", "이 아우성은 파트리지 부인의 죽음으로 한층 거세졌다. 그 죽음이 가난이나 곤경과는 아무 상관도 없는 앞서 말한 그 돌림병 탓이었는데도, 많은 이들이 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 그것을 올워디 씨의 모진 처사, 아니 그들이 이제 부르는 말로 하자면 잔인함의 탓으로 돌렸다."]], [["Partridge having now lost his wife, his school, and his annuity, and the unknown person having now discontinued the last-mentioned charity, resolved to change the scene, and left the country, where he was in danger of starving, with the universal compassion of all his neighbours.", "이제 아내도, 학교도, 연금도 다 잃은 데다, 그 정체 모를 사람마저 앞서 말한 자선을 끊어 버리자, 파트리지는 사는 무대를 바꾸기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 굶어 죽을 위기에 놓인 그 고장을, 이웃 모두의 한결같은 동정을 받으며 떠나갔다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["A short sketch of that felicity which prudent couples may extract from hatred: with a short apology for those people who overlook imperfections in their friends.", "분별 있는 부부가 증오에서 길어 올릴 수 있는 그 행복을 짧게 그려 보임. 더불어 벗의 결점을 못 본 척 넘기는 사람들을 위한 짤막한 변호도 곁들임."]], [["Though the captain had effectually demolished poor Partridge, yet had he not reaped the harvest he hoped for, which was to turn the foundling out of Mr Allworthy's house.", "대위는 가엾은 파트리지를 완전히 무너뜨리는 데에는 성공했지만, 정작 그가 바라던 수확, 곧 그 업둥이를 올워디 씨의 집에서 내쫓는 일만큼은 거두지 못했다."]], [["On the contrary, that gentleman grew every day fonder of little Tommy, as if he intended to counterbalance his severity to the father with extraordinary fondness and affection towards the son.", "오히려 그 신사는 날이 갈수록 어린 토미를 더욱 어여삐 여겼으니, 마치 아버지에게 베푼 가혹함을 아들에게 베푸는 유별난 애정과 다정함으로 상쇄하려는 듯했다."]], [["This a good deal soured the captain's temper, as did all the other daily instances of Mr Allworthy's generosity; for he looked on all such largesses to be diminutions of his own wealth.", "이것은 대위의 심사를 적잖이 뒤틀어 놓았고, 올워디 씨가 날마다 보이는 다른 모든 너그러움 또한 마찬가지였다. 그는 그러한 모든 베풂을 제 몫이 될 재산이 줄어드는 것으로 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["In this, we have said, he did not agree with his wife; nor, indeed, in anything else: for though an affection placed on the understanding is, by many wise persons, thought more durable than that which is founded on beauty, yet it happened otherwise in the present case.", "이 점에서 그는 아내와 뜻이 맞지 않았다고 우리는 이미 말한 바 있는데, 사실 다른 어떤 일에서도 마찬가지였다. 지성에 둔 애정이 아름다움에 토대를 둔 애정보다 더 오래간다고 많은 현명한 이들이 여기지만, 이번 경우만큼은 그렇지 못했다."], ["Nay, the understandings of this couple were their principal bone of contention, and one great cause of many quarrels, which from time to time arose between them; and which at last ended, on the side of the lady, in a sovereign contempt for her husband; and on the husband's, in an utter abhorrence of his wife.", "아니, 이 부부의 지성이야말로 다툼의 으뜸가는 빌미였고, 때때로 둘 사이에서 일어나던 숱한 언쟁의 큰 원인이었으니, 그 언쟁은 마침내 아내 쪽에서는 남편을 향한 더없는 경멸로, 남편 쪽에서는 아내를 향한 철저한 혐오로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["As these had both exercised their talents chiefly in the study of divinity, this was, from their first acquaintance, the most common topic of conversation between them.", "두 사람 모두 신학 연구에 주로 재능을 쏟아 왔던 터라, 이것은 처음 알게 된 무렵부터 둘 사이에서 가장 흔한 대화 주제였다."], ["The captain, like a well-bred man, had, before marriage, always given up his opinion to that of the lady; and this, not in the clumsy awkward manner of a conceited blockhead, who, while he civilly yields to a superior in an argument, is desirous of being still known to think himself in the right.", "대위는 예의 바른 사람답게 결혼 전에는 늘 제 의견을 아내의 의견에 양보했는데, 그것도 잘난 척하는 멍청이가 흔히 하는 그 서툴고 어색한 방식—토론에서 윗사람에게 점잖게 굽히면서도 속으로는 여전히 제가 옳다고 여기는 줄을 남이 알아주기를 바라는 방식—은 아니었다."], ["The captain, on the contrary, though one of the proudest fellows in the world, so absolutely yielded the victory to his antagonist, that she, who had not the least doubt of his sincerity, retired always from the dispute with an admiration of her own understanding and a love for his.", "오히려 대위는 세상에서 가장 자존심 센 사내 가운데 하나였음에도, 상대에게 승리를 어찌나 깔끔하게 내주었던지, 그의 진심을 조금도 의심치 않던 아내는 언제나 제 지성에 대한 감탄과 그의 지성에 대한 사랑을 품은 채 논쟁에서 물러나곤 했다."]], [["But though this complacence to one whom the captain thoroughly despised, was not so uneasy to him as it would have been had any hopes of preferment made it necessary to show the same submission to a Hoadley, or to some other of great reputation in the science, yet even this cost him too much to be endured without some motive.", "그러나 대위가 속으로 철저히 경멸하던 사람에게 보인 이 너그러운 굴종은, 가령 승진의 기대 때문에 호들리 같은 이나 그 학문에서 큰 명성을 떨치는 다른 누군가에게 똑같은 복종을 보여야 했을 때만큼 그에게 거북한 일은 아니었다 해도, 그래도 어떤 동기 없이 견디기에는 너무 큰 대가를 치르는 일이었다."], ["Matrimony, therefore, having removed all such motives, he grew weary of this condescension, and began to treat the opinions of his wife with that haughtiness and insolence, which none but those who deserve some contempt themselves can bestow, and those only who deserve no contempt can bear.", "그러니 결혼이 그러한 모든 동기를 없애 버리자, 그는 이 굽힘에 싫증을 느끼기 시작했고, 아내의 의견을 그 거만하고 무례한 태도로 대하기에 이르렀다. 그것은 제 자신이 얼마간 경멸받아 마땅한 자들만이 베풀 수 있고, 조금도 경멸받을 일 없는 자들만이 견뎌 낼 수 있는 그런 태도였다."]], [["When the first torrent of tenderness was over, and when, in the calm and long interval between the fits, reason began to open the eyes of the lady, and she saw this alteration of behaviour in the captain, who at length answered all her arguments only with pish and pshaw, she was far from enduring the indignity with a tame submission.", "다정함의 첫 격류가 잦아들고, 발작과 발작 사이의 그 잔잔하고 긴 틈에서 이성이 아내의 눈을 뜨게 하기 시작하여, 마침내 자신의 모든 논변에 그저 흥, 칫 따위로만 답하는 대위의 달라진 행실을 그녀가 보게 되었을 때, 그녀는 그 모욕을 고분고분 받아들이며 견딜 사람이 결코 아니었다. 실로 그 일은 처음에는 그녀를 어찌나 격하게 자극했던지, 만일 더 해롭지 않은 쪽으로 방향을 틀지 않았더라면 어떤 비극적 사건을 낳았을지도 모른다."], ["Indeed, it at first so highly provoked her, that it might have produced some tragical event, had it not taken a more harmless turn, by filling her with the utmost contempt for her husband's understanding, which somewhat qualified her hatred towards him; though of this likewise she had a pretty moderate share.", "그 방향이란, 남편의 지성을 향한 더없는 경멸로 그녀의 가슴을 가득 채우는 것이었으니, 이것이 남편을 향한 증오를 어느 정도 누그러뜨려 주었다. 비록 그 증오 또한 제법 적당한 몫만큼은 그녀 안에 있었지만 말이다."]], [["The captain's hatred to her was of a purer kind: for as to any imperfections in her knowledge or understanding, he no more despised her for them, than for her not being six feet high. In his opinion of the female sex, he exceeded the moroseness of Aristotle himself: he looked on a woman as on an animal of domestic use, of somewhat higher consideration than a cat, since her offices were of rather more importance; but the difference between these two was, in his estimation, so small, that, in his marriage contracted with Mr Allworthy's lands and tenements, it would have been pretty equal which of them he had taken into the bargain. And yet so tender was his pride, that it felt the contempt which his wife now began to express towards him; and this, added to the surfeit he had before taken of her love, created in him a degree of disgust and abhorrence, perhaps hardly to be exceeded.", "대위가 아내에게 품은 증오는 좀 더 순수한 종류였다. 그녀의 지식이나 지성에 어떤 결함이 있다 해서 그가 그녀를 경멸한 것은 아니었으니, 그것은 그녀의 키가 육 척이 못 된다 해서 경멸하지 않는 것과 다를 바 없었다. 여성을 보는 그의 견해는 아리스토텔레스 자신의 까탈스러움마저 능가했다. 그는 여자를 집안일에 쓰이는 짐승쯤으로 여겼는데, 고양이보다는 조금 더 쳐줄 만하다 했으니, 그 맡은 일이 좀 더 중요하기 때문이었다. 그러나 그 둘 사이의 차이란 그의 평가로는 어찌나 작던지, 올워디 씨의 토지와 가옥을 노리고 맺은 그 결혼에서, 둘 중 어느 쪽을 덤으로 떠안았든 거의 매한가지였을 정도였다. 그러면서도 그의 자존심은 어찌나 여리던지, 이제 아내가 그를 향해 드러내기 시작한 경멸을 고스란히 느꼈다. 그리고 이것은, 전에 그녀의 사랑에 물리도록 시달린 경험과 더해져, 그의 안에 어쩌면 더 넘어설 수 없을 만큼의 역겨움과 혐오를 만들어 냈다."]], [["One situation only of the married state is excluded from pleasure: and that is, a state of indifference: but as many of my readers, I hope, know what an exquisite delight there is in conveying pleasure to a beloved object, so some few, I am afraid, may have experienced the satisfaction of tormenting one we hate.", "결혼 생활에서 즐거움이 배제되는 처지는 단 하나뿐이니, 그것은 곧 무관심의 상태다. 그러나 내 독자들 가운데 많은 분은 사랑하는 대상에게 기쁨을 전하는 일에 얼마나 절묘한 즐거움이 있는지 아시리라 믿는 한편, 안타깝게도 몇몇 분은 미워하는 자를 괴롭히는 데서 오는 만족을 겪어 보셨을지도 모른다."], ["It is, I apprehend, to come at this latter pleasure, that we see both sexes often give up that ease in marriage which they might otherwise possess, though their mate was never so disagreeable to them.", "내가 짐작건대, 바로 이 후자의 즐거움에 다다르려고, 우리는 남녀 양쪽이 결혼 생활에서 누릴 수도 있었을 그 편안함을 곧잘 내던지는 것을 본다. 비록 상대가 아무리 마음에 들지 않았다 하더라도 말이다."], ["Hence the wife often puts on fits of love and jealousy, nay, even denies herself any pleasure, to disturb and prevent those of her husband; and he again, in return, puts frequent restraints on himself, and stays at home in company which he dislikes, in order to confine his wife to what she equally detests.", "그리하여 아내는 흔히 사랑이며 질투의 발작을 꾸며 내고, 아니 제 즐거움마저 마다하면서까지 남편의 즐거움을 어지럽히고 막으려 든다. 그리고 남편은 또 그 보답으로, 제 자신에게 자주 제약을 가하며, 마음에 들지 않는 사람들과 더불어 집에 머무르니, 이는 아내를 그녀가 똑같이 질색하는 일에 붙잡아 두기 위함이다."], ["Hence, too, must flow those tears which a widow sometimes so plentifully sheds over the ashes of a husband with whom she led a life of constant disquiet and turbulency, and whom now she can never hope to torment any more.", "또한 여기서, 끊임없는 불안과 소란으로 점철된 삶을 함께한 남편의 유골 위에 과부가 때때로 그토록 펑펑 쏟아 내는 그 눈물도 흘러나올 수밖에 없으니, 이제는 그를 더 괴롭힐 길이 영영 사라졌기 때문이다."]], [["But if ever any couple enjoyed this pleasure, it was at present experienced by the captain and his lady.", "그러나 어떤 부부가 이 즐거움을 누린 적이 있다면, 지금 바로 대위와 그 부인이 그것을 겪고 있었다."], ["It was always a sufficient reason to either of them to be obstinate in any opinion, that the other had previously asserted the contrary.", "어느 한쪽에게는, 다른 한쪽이 앞서 그 반대를 주장했다는 사실만으로도 어떤 의견에든 고집을 부릴 충분한 이유가 되었다."], ["If the one proposed any amusement, the other constantly objected to it: they never loved or hated, commended or abused, the same person.", "한쪽이 무슨 놀이를 제안하면 다른 쪽은 어김없이 반대했다. 두 사람은 같은 사람을 사랑하거나 미워하는 일도, 칭찬하거나 헐뜯는 일도 결코 없었다."], ["And for this reason, as the captain looked with an evil eye on the little foundling, his wife began now to caress it almost equally with her own child.", "그리고 바로 이런 까닭에, 대위가 그 어린 업둥이를 못마땅한 눈으로 보았으므로, 이제 아내는 거의 제 자식과 다름없을 만큼 그 아이를 어루만지기 시작했다."]], [["The reader will be apt to conceive, that this behaviour between the husband and wife did not greatly contribute to Mr Allworthy's repose, as it tended so little to that serene happiness which he had designed for all three from this alliance; but the truth is, though he might be a little disappointed in his sanguine expectations, yet he was far from being acquainted with the whole matter; for, as the captain was, from certain obvious reasons, much on his guard before him, the lady was obliged, for fear of her brother's displeasure, to pursue the same conduct. In fact, it is possible for a third person to be very intimate, nay even to live long in the same house, with a married couple, who have any tolerable discretion, and not even guess at the sour sentiments which they bear to each other: for though the whole day may be sometimes too short for hatred, as well as for love; yet the many hours which they naturally spend together, apart from all observers, furnish people of tolerable moderation with such ample opportunity for the enjoyment of either passion, that, if they love, they can support being a few hours in company without toying, or if they hate, without spitting in each other's faces.", "독자는 부부 사이의 이러한 행실이 올워디 씨의 평온에 그다지 보탬이 되지 않았으리라 짐작하기 쉬울 것이다. 그가 이 혼인으로 세 사람 모두에게 마련해 주려 했던 그 잔잔한 행복에 거의 이바지하지 못했으니 말이다. 그러나 사실인즉, 그가 부푼 기대에서 조금 실망했을지언정, 그 일의 전말을 죄다 알고 있었던 것과는 거리가 멀었다. 대위는 몇 가지 뻔한 이유로 그 앞에서 무던히도 몸을 사렸고, 아내 또한 오라비의 노여움이 두려워 같은 처신을 따를 수밖에 없었기 때문이다. 실로, 웬만큼이라도 분별을 갖춘 부부와 더불어 제삼자가 아주 가깝게 지내며, 아니 같은 집에서 오래 산다 해도, 그들이 서로에게 품은 그 시큰둥한 감정을 짐작조차 못 하는 일은 얼마든지 가능하다. 하루가 통째로 증오에는, 사랑에 그렇듯이, 때로 너무 짧다 한들, 아무도 보는 이 없이 둘이 자연스레 함께 보내는 그 숱한 시간은, 웬만큼 절제할 줄 아는 사람들에게 둘 중 어느 감정이든 누릴 넉넉한 기회를 마련해 주기에, 그들이 사랑한다면 몇 시간쯤 시시덕거리지 않고도 함께 있을 수 있고, 미워한다면 서로의 얼굴에 침을 뱉지 않고도 함께 있을 수 있는 것이다."]], [["It is possible, however, that Mr Allworthy saw enough to render him a little uneasy; for we are not always to conclude, that a wise man is not hurt, because he doth not cry out and lament himself, like those of a childish or effeminate temper.", "그렇긴 해도, 올워디 씨가 자신을 조금쯤 불편하게 할 만큼은 보았을 수도 있다. 우리는 현명한 사람이 어린아이 같거나 여린 기질의 사람들처럼 소리쳐 울며 신세 한탄을 하지 않는다 해서, 그가 상처받지 않았다고 늘 단정해서는 안 되니까."], ["But indeed it is possible he might see some faults in the captain without any uneasiness at all; for men of true wisdom and goodness are contented to take persons and things as they are, without complaining of their imperfections, or attempting to amend them.", "그러나 실로 그는 아무런 불편함 없이도 대위의 어떤 허물을 보았을 수 있다. 참된 지혜와 선함을 갖춘 사람들은 사람이든 사물이든 있는 그대로 받아들이는 데 만족하며, 그 결함을 두고 불평하거나 그것을 고치려 들지 않기 때문이다."], ["They can see a fault in a friend, a relation, or an acquaintance, without ever mentioning it to the parties themselves, or to any others; and this often without lessening their affection.", "그들은 벗이나 친척, 또는 아는 이의 허물을 보고도 그 당사자에게든 다른 누구에게든 결코 그것을 입에 올리지 않으며, 그러면서도 흔히 애정이 줄어드는 일도 없다. 실로 빼어난 분별력이 이 못 본 척 넘기는 성향으로 다스려지지 않는 한, 우리는 우리가 속여 넘길 만큼의 어리석음을 지닌 사람과나 우정을 맺어야 할 것이다."], ["Indeed, unless great discernment be tempered with this overlooking disposition, we ought never to contract friendship but with a degree of folly which we can deceive; for I hope my friends will pardon me when I declare, I know none of them without a fault; and I should be sorry if I could imagine I had any friend who could not see mine.", "내 벗들이 부디 용서해 주기를 바라며 나는 분명히 말하건대, 나는 그들 가운데 허물 없는 이를 하나도 알지 못한다. 또한 내 허물을 보지 못할 벗이 내게 있다고 여겨야 한다면 나는 서글플 것이다."], ["Forgiveness of this kind we give and demand in turn.", "이런 종류의 용서를 우리는 번갈아 주고받는다."], ["It is an exercise of friendship, and perhaps none of the least pleasant.", "그것은 우정의 한 행함이요, 어쩌면 그중에서도 가장 즐거운 축에 든다."], ["And this forgiveness we must bestow, without desire of amendment.", "그리고 이 용서를 우리는 고치라는 바람 없이 베풀어야 한다."], ["There is, perhaps, no surer mark of folly, than an attempt to correct the natural infirmities of those we love.", "우리가 사랑하는 이들의 타고난 약점을 고쳐 보려는 시도보다 더 확실한 어리석음의 표시는 아마 없을 것이다."], ["The finest composition of human nature, as well as the finest china, may have a flaw in it; and this, I am afraid, in either case, is equally incurable; though, nevertheless, the pattern may remain of the highest value.", "인간 본성의 가장 빼어난 됨됨이도, 가장 빼어난 도자기와 마찬가지로 흠이 하나쯤 있을 수 있고, 안타깝게도 이것은 어느 쪽이든 똑같이 고칠 수 없는 법이다. 그럼에도 그 본은 여전히 더없이 값진 채로 남을 수 있지만 말이다."]], [["Upon the whole, then, Mr Allworthy certainly saw some imperfections in the captain; but as this was a very artful man, and eternally upon his guard before him, these appeared to him no more than blemishes in a good character, which his goodness made him overlook, and his wisdom prevented him from discovering to the captain himself. Very different would have been his sentiments had he discovered the whole; which perhaps would in time have been the case, had the husband and wife long continued this kind of behaviour to each other; but this kind Fortune took effectual means to prevent, by forcing the captain to do that which rendered him again dear to his wife, and restored all her tenderness and affection towards him.", "요컨대 그렇다면, 올워디 씨는 분명 대위에게서 몇 가지 결함을 보았다. 그러나 대위는 워낙 교활한 사람이라 그 앞에서 끝없이 몸을 사렸으므로, 그 결함들은 올워디 씨에게 한낱 훌륭한 인품에 박힌 티끌쯤으로만 비쳤고, 그의 선함은 그것을 못 본 척 넘기게 했으며, 그의 지혜는 그것을 대위 본인에게 들춰내지 못하게 막았다. 만일 그가 전말을 죄다 알아챘더라면 그의 감정은 사뭇 달랐을 것이다. 그리고 만일 부부가 서로에 대한 이런 식의 행실을 오래도록 이어 갔더라면, 어쩌면 머지않아 그리되었을 터다. 그러나 그런 친절을 운명은 효과적으로 막아 버렸으니, 대위로 하여금 그를 다시금 아내에게 사랑스러운 존재로 만들고, 그를 향한 그녀의 모든 다정함과 애정을 되살려 놓을 어떤 일을 하도록 떠밀었던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["A receipt to regain the lost affections of a wife, which hath never been known to fail in the most desperate cases.", "아내의 잃어버린 애정을 되찾는 처방, 가장 절망적인 경우에도 한 번도 실패한 적이 없다고 알려진 비법."]], [["The captain was made large amends for the unpleasant minutes which he passed in the conversation of his wife (and which were as few as he could contrive to make them), by the pleasant meditations he enjoyed when alone.", "대위는 아내와의 대화 속에서 보낸 그 불쾌한 순간들을 — 그는 그 시간을 가능한 한 짧게 줄이려 애썼다 — 혼자 있을 때 즐기는 흐뭇한 사색으로 넉넉히 보상받았다."]], [["These meditations were entirely employed on Mr Allworthy's fortune; for, first, he exercised much thought in calculating, as well as he could, the exact value of the whole: which calculations he often saw occasion to alter in his own favour: and, secondly and chiefly, he pleased himself with intended alterations in the house and gardens, and in projecting many other schemes, as well for the improvement of the estate as of the grandeur of the place: for this purpose he applied himself to the studies of architecture and gardening, and read over many books on both these subjects; for these sciences, indeed, employed his whole time, and formed his only amusement. He at last completed a most excellent plan: and very sorry we are, that it is not in our power to present it to our reader, since even the luxury of the present age, I believe, would hardly match it. It had, indeed, in a superlative degree, the two principal ingredients which serve to recommend all great and noble designs of this nature; for it required an immoderate expense to execute, and a vast length of time to bring it to any sort of perfection. The former of these, the immense wealth of which the captain supposed Mr Allworthy possessed, and which he thought himself sure of inheriting, promised very effectually to supply; and the latter, the soundness of his own constitution, and his time of life, which was only what is called middle-age, removed all apprehension of his not living to accomplish.", "이 사색은 오로지 올워디 씨의 재산에만 쏠려 있었다. 우선 그는 그 전체의 정확한 가치를 가능한 한 정밀하게 셈하느라 적잖은 생각을 쏟았는데, 이 계산은 종종 그 자신에게 유리한 쪽으로 고칠 구실을 발견하곤 했다. 그리고 둘째로, 또 무엇보다도, 그는 저택과 정원을 어떻게 바꿀지 마음속으로 그려 보며, 그 영지의 가치를 높이는 것은 물론 그 장소의 웅장함을 더할 여러 계획을 세우는 일로 스스로를 즐겁게 했다. 이를 위해 그는 건축과 조경 공부에 매달려, 이 두 주제를 다룬 책을 여러 권 통독했다. 사실 이 학문들은 그의 시간을 통째로 차지했고, 그의 유일한 소일거리가 되었다. 그는 마침내 더없이 훌륭한 설계도를 완성했다. 그러나 우리로서는 그것을 독자에게 보여 드릴 능력이 없으니 참으로 안타까울 뿐이다. 내 생각으로는 지금 이 시대의 사치조차 거기에는 좀처럼 견주지 못할 것이기 때문이다. 그 설계도는 실로, 이런 종류의 위대하고 고귀한 구상을 추천할 만하게 만드는 두 가지 핵심 요소를 더할 나위 없이 갖추고 있었다. 곧 그것을 실행하려면 엄청난 비용이 들었고, 어느 정도라도 완성에 이르게 하려면 까마득한 세월이 필요했다. 이 가운데 앞의 것은, 대위가 올워디 씨가 지녔다고 짐작하며 또 자신이 틀림없이 물려받으리라 여긴 그 막대한 재산이 아주 효과적으로 채워 줄 듯했다. 그리고 뒤의 것은, 그 자신의 튼튼한 체질과, 이른바 중년이라 불리는 정도에 지나지 않는 그의 나이가, 그것을 다 이루기 전에 죽지 않을까 하는 모든 염려를 거두어 주었다."]], [["Nothing was wanting to enable him to enter upon the immediate execution of this plan, but the death of Mr Allworthy; in calculating which he had employed much of his own algebra, besides purchasing every book extant that treats of the value of lives, reversions, &c.", "이 계획을 당장이라도 실행에 옮길 수 있도록 하는 데 필요한 것은 오직 올워디 씨의 죽음 하나뿐이었다. 그는 이를 계산하느라 자신의 대수학을 적잖이 동원했고, 그 밖에도 수명과 상속권의 가치 따위를 다룬 책이라면 시중에 나와 있는 것을 모조리 사들였다."], ["From all which he satisfied himself, that as he had every day a chance of this happening, so had he more than an even chance of its happening within a few years.", "이 모든 것을 통해 그는 스스로 이렇게 만족스럽게 결론지었다. 그 일이 일어날 가능성이 날마다 있으니, 몇 해 안에 그것이 일어날 확률 또한 절반을 넘는다고 말이다."]], [["But while the captain was one day busied in deep contemplations of this kind, one of the most unlucky as well as unseasonable accidents happened to him.", "그러나 어느 날 대위가 이런 종류의 깊은 명상에 잠겨 있던 바로 그때, 그에게 더없이 불운하고도 더없이 때를 잘못 만난 사고가 닥쳤다."], ["The utmost malice of Fortune could, indeed, have contrived nothing so cruel, so mal-a-propos, so absolutely destructive to all his schemes.", "실로 운명이 아무리 짓궂은 악의를 짜낸들, 이토록 잔인하고, 이토록 엉뚱하며, 그의 모든 계획을 이토록 송두리째 무너뜨릴 일을 꾸며 내지는 못했을 것이다."], ["In short, not to keep the reader in long suspense, just at the very instant when his heart was exulting in meditations on the happiness which would accrue to him by Mr Allworthy's death, he himself--died of an apoplexy.", "요컨대, 독자를 오래 애태우지 않고 말하자면, 올워디 씨의 죽음으로 자신에게 굴러들 행복을 떠올리며 그의 가슴이 한껏 우쭐대던 바로 그 순간에, 정작 그 자신이 — 뇌졸중으로 죽고 말았다."]], [["This unfortunately befel the captain as he was taking his evening walk by himself, so that nobody was present to lend him any assistance, if indeed, any assistance could have preserved him. He took, therefore, measure of that proportion of soil which was now become adequate to all his future purposes, and he lay dead on the ground, a great (though not a living) example of the truth of that observation of Horace:", "이 불행은 대위가 홀로 저녁 산책을 하고 있을 때 닥쳤다. 그래서 곁에는 그를 도와줄 사람이 아무도 없었다. 설령 어떤 도움이 그를 살릴 수 있었다 한들 말이다. 그리하여 그는 이제 자신의 모든 미래 계획에 꼭 맞는 만큼의 흙뙈기를 자기 몸으로 재고 말았으며, 땅바닥에 죽은 채 누워, 호라티우스의 다음 관찰이 참됨을 보여 주는 거대한 본보기가 되었다(살아 있는 본보기는 아니었지만). 너는 장례를 코앞에 두고도"]], [["Tu secanda marmora Locas sub ipsum funus; et sepulchri Immemor, struis domos.", "대리석을 잘라 오게 하고, 무덤은\n까맣게 잊은 채 집을 짓는구나."]], [["Which sentiment I shall thus give to the English reader: “You provide the noblest materials for building, when a pickaxe and a spade are only necessary: and build houses of five hundred by a hundred feet, forgetting that of six by two.”", "이 뜻을 나는 영국 독자에게 이렇게 옮겨 드리겠다. \"너는 곡괭이와 삽 한 자루면 족할 때에 가장 고귀한 건축 자재를 마련하고, 가로 육 척에 세로 이 척짜리 집은 잊은 채 가로 백 척에 세로 오백 척짜리 집을 짓는구나.\""]], [["# Chapter ix.", "# Chapter ix."]], [["A proof of the infallibility of the foregoing receipt, in the lamentations of the widow; with other suitable decorations of death, such as physicians, &c., and an epitaph in the true stile.", "앞서 말한 처방의 틀림없음을 증명하는 장. 과부의 비탄과 함께, 의사들 따위 죽음에 어울리는 여러 장식들, 그리고 참된 문체로 쓴 비문을 곁들임."]], [["Mr Allworthy, his sister, and another lady, were assembled at the accustomed hour in the supper-room, where, having waited a considerable time longer than usual, Mr Allworthy first declared he began to grow uneasy at the captain's stay (for he was always most punctual at his meals); and gave orders that the bell should be rung without the doors, and especially towards those walks which the captain was wont to use.", "올워디 씨와 그의 누이, 그리고 또 한 부인이 여느 때처럼 저녁 식탁이 차려지는 방에 모여 있었다. 평소보다 한참을 더 기다린 끝에, 올워디 씨가 먼저 대위가 늦는 것이 슬슬 마음에 걸린다고 입을 열었다. 대위는 늘 식사 시간만큼은 누구보다 정확한 사람이었기 때문이다. 그는 문밖에 종을 울리라고, 특히 대위가 즐겨 거닐던 산책로 쪽을 향해 울리라고 일렀다."]], [["All these summons proving ineffectual (for the captain had, by perverse accident, betaken himself to a new walk that evening), Mrs Blifil declared she was seriously frightened.", "이 모든 부름이 아무 소용이 없자(대위는 짓궂은 우연으로 그날 저녁 새로운 산책로로 발길을 돌렸던 것이다), 블리필 부인은 정말 무서워졌다고 말했다."], ["Upon which the other lady, who was one of her most intimate acquaintance, and who well knew the true state of her affections, endeavoured all she could to pacify her, telling her--To be sure she could not help being uneasy; but that she should hope the best.", "그러자 그녀의 가장 가까운 친구 가운데 한 사람이자 그녀의 속마음을 잘 아는 또 한 부인이 온갖 말로 그녀를 달래려 애썼다. 물론 불안하지 않을 수야 없겠지만 그래도 가장 좋은 쪽으로 생각하시라고, 어쩌면 저녁 공기가 너무 좋아서 대위가 평소보다 멀리까지 걸었거나, 이웃집에 붙잡혀 있는 것인지도 모른다고 말이다."], ["That, perhaps the sweetness of the evening had inticed the captain to go farther than his usual walk: or he might be detained at some neighbour's.", "블리필 부인은 아니라고, 분명 무슨 사고를 당한 것이라고 대답했다."], ["Mrs Blifil answered, No; she was sure some accident had befallen him; for that he would never stay out without sending her word, as he must know how uneasy it would make her.", "그이는 자기가 얼마나 마음 졸일지 잘 알기에, 한마디 전갈도 없이 밖에 머무를 사람이 결코 아니라는 것이었다."], ["The other lady, having no other arguments to use, betook herself to the entreaties usual on such occasions, and begged her not to frighten herself, for it might be of very ill consequence to her own health; and, filling out a very large glass of wine, advised, and at last prevailed with her to drink it.", "더 댈 말이 없어진 그 부인은 이런 때 으레 쓰는 간청에 매달려, 부디 그렇게 스스로를 무섭게 몰아가지 마시라고, 그러다 정작 본인 건강을 크게 해칠 수 있다고 빌었다. 그러고는 포도주를 한 잔 가득 따라 권했고, 마침내 그녀로 하여금 그것을 마시게 했다."]], [["Mr Allworthy now returned into the parlour; for he had been himself in search after the captain. His countenance sufficiently showed the consternation he was under, which, indeed, had a good deal deprived him of speech; but as grief operates variously on different minds, so the same apprehension which depressed his voice, elevated that of Mrs Blifil. She now began to bewail herself in very bitter terms, and floods of tears accompanied her lamentations; which the lady, her companion, declared she could not blame, but at the same time dissuaded her from indulging; attempting to moderate the grief of her friend by philosophical observations on the many disappointments to which human life is daily subject, which, she said, was a sufficient consideration to fortify our minds against any accidents, how sudden or terrible soever. She said her brother's example ought to teach her patience, who, though indeed he could not be supposed as much concerned as herself, yet was, doubtless, very uneasy, though his resignation to the Divine will had restrained his grief within due bounds.", "이제 올워디 씨가 거실로 돌아왔다. 그 자신이 직접 대위를 찾아 나섰던 터였다. 그의 얼굴빛은 그가 얼마나 큰 충격에 빠져 있는지를 충분히 보여 주었고, 실로 그 충격은 그에게서 말까지 거의 앗아 가 버렸다. 그러나 슬픔이 사람마다 다르게 작용하듯, 그의 목소리를 짓눌렀던 바로 그 두려움이 블리필 부인의 목소리는 오히려 높여 놓았다. 그녀는 이제 더없이 쓰라린 말로 스스로를 한탄하기 시작했고, 그 비탄에는 눈물의 홍수가 뒤따랐다. 그녀의 동무인 부인은 그것을 나무랄 수는 없다면서도, 동시에 너무 그러지 말라고 만류했다. 그러면서 인간의 삶이 날마다 겪는 숱한 좌절에 관한 철학적인 견해로 친구의 슬픔을 누그러뜨리려 했다. 그런 좌절들을 헤아려 두는 것이야말로, 아무리 갑작스럽고 끔찍한 일이 닥치더라도 우리 마음을 단단히 지켜 줄 충분한 근거가 된다는 것이었다. 그녀는 오라버니의 본을 보고 인내를 배워야 한다고 말했다. 오라버니가 부인만큼 깊이 마음 쓸 처지는 아니라 하더라도 분명 몹시 괴로워하고 있으며, 다만 하느님의 뜻에 순종하는 마음이 그의 슬픔을 마땅한 한도 안에 붙들어 두고 있을 뿐이라고 했다."]], [["“Mention not my brother,”", "\"제 오라버니 얘기는 꺼내지 마세요.\""], ["said Mrs Blifil; “I alone am the object of your pity.", "블리필 부인이 말했다. \"지금 가엾이 여겨야 할 사람은 오직 저뿐이에요."], ["What are the terrors of friendship to what a wife feels on these occasions?", "이런 일에 아내가 느끼는 것에 비하면, 우정의 두려움 따위가 다 무어란 말인가요? 오, 그이는 죽었어요!"], ["Oh, he is lost!", "누군가 그이를 죽인 거예요."], ["Somebody hath murdered him--I shall never see him more!”--Here a torrent of tears had the same consequence with what the suppression had occasioned to Mr Allworthy, and she remained silent.", "저는 다시는 그이를 볼 수 없을 거예요!\" 여기서 눈물이 한바탕 쏟아져, 올워디 씨에게는 말을 억누른 것이 가져온 것과 똑같은 결과를 그녀에게 안겨, 그녀는 입을 다물고 말았다."]], [["At this interval a servant came running in, out of breath, and cried out, The captain was found; and, before he could proceed farther, he was followed by two more, bearing the dead body between them.", "바로 그때 하인 하나가 숨이 턱에 차서 뛰어 들어와 외쳤다. 대위를 찾았다는 것이었다. 그가 더 말을 잇기도 전에, 둘이서 시신을 마주 들고 옮기는 하인 둘이 그 뒤를 따라 들어왔다."]], [["Here the curious reader may observe another diversity in the operations of grief: for as Mr Allworthy had been before silent, from the same cause which had made his sister vociferous; so did the present sight, which drew tears from the gentleman, put an entire stop to those of the lady; who first gave a violent scream, and presently after fell into a fit.", "여기서 호기심 많은 독자는 슬픔이 작용하는 또 다른 차이를 눈여겨볼 수 있을 것이다. 누이를 그토록 큰 소리로 울게 만든 바로 그 원인이 올워디 씨는 앞서 입을 다물게 했듯이, 이번에는 그 신사에게서 눈물을 자아낸 그 광경이 부인의 눈물은 뚝 멈추게 했다. 그녀는 먼저 날카로운 비명을 한 번 지르더니, 곧이어 발작을 일으키며 쓰러졌다."]], [["The room was soon full of servants, some of whom, with the lady visitant, were employed in care of the wife; and others, with Mr Allworthy, assisted in carrying off the captain to a warm bed; where every method was tried, in order to restore him to life.", "방은 이내 하인들로 가득 찼다. 그 가운데 일부는 방문한 부인과 함께 아내를 돌보는 데 매달렸고, 나머지는 올워디 씨와 함께 대위를 따뜻한 침상으로 옮기는 일을 거들었다. 거기서 그를 되살리려고 온갖 방법이 시도되었다."]], [["And glad should we be, could we inform the reader that both these bodies had been attended with equal success; for those who undertook the care of the lady succeeded so well, that, after the fit had continued a decent time, she again revived, to their great satisfaction: but as to the captain, all experiments of bleeding, chafing, dropping, &c., proved ineffectual.", "이 두 몸이 똑같은 성과를 거두었다고 독자에게 알릴 수 있다면 기쁘겠다. 부인을 돌본 이들은 큰 성공을 거두어, 발작이 점잖게 적당한 시간 동안 이어진 뒤에 그녀가 다시 깨어났고, 그들은 크게 만족했다."], ["Death, that inexorable judge, had passed sentence on him, and refused to grant him a reprieve, though two doctors who arrived, and were fee'd at one and the same instant, were his counsel.", "그러나 대위에 관해서는 피를 빼고, 문지르고, 약을 떨구는 등 온갖 시도가 다 헛수고였다. 죽음이라는 그 가차 없는 재판관이 이미 그에게 선고를 내려 형의 유예를 거절했으니, 한 순간에 동시에 도착해 똑같이 진료비를 받은 두 의사가 그의 변호인이었음에도 그러했다."]], [["These two doctors, whom, to avoid any malicious applications, we shall distinguish by the names of Dr Y.", "이 두 의사를, 행여 악의적인 추측을 살까 하여, 우리는 Y 박사와 Z 박사라는 이름으로 구별하겠다."], ["and Dr Z., having felt his pulse; to wit, Dr Y. his right arm, and Dr Z.", "그들이 그의 맥을 짚었는데, Y 박사는 오른팔을, Z 박사는 왼팔을 짚었다."], ["his left; both agreed that he was absolutely dead; but as to the distemper, or cause of his death, they differed; Dr Y.", "둘 다 그가 완전히 숨졌다는 데에는 의견을 같이했으나, 그 병이나 사인에 관해서는 견해가 갈렸다."], ["holding that he died of an apoplexy, and Dr Z. of an epilepsy.", "Y 박사는 그가 뇌졸중으로 죽었다고 했고, Z 박사는 간질로 죽었다고 했다."]], [["Hence arose a dispute between the learned men, in which each delivered the reasons of their several opinions.", "여기서 두 학식 있는 사람 사이에 논쟁이 벌어졌고, 저마다 자기 견해의 근거를 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["These were of such equal force, that they served both to confirm either doctor in his own sentiments, and made not the least impression on his adversary.", "그 근거들이 어찌나 힘이 팽팽했던지, 양쪽 모두 자기 생각을 굳히는 데에만 보탬이 되었을 뿐, 상대에게는 조금의 자국도 남기지 못했다."]], [["To say the truth, every physician almost hath his favourite disease, to which he ascribes all the victories obtained over human nature.", "사실대로 말하자면, 거의 모든 의사에게는 저마다 아끼는 병이 하나씩 있어서, 인간의 몸을 이긴 모든 승리를 그 병의 탓으로 돌린다."], ["The gout, the rheumatism, the stone, the gravel, and the consumption, have all their several patrons in the faculty; and none more than the nervous fever, or the fever on the spirits.", "통풍, 류머티즘, 결석, 요사, 폐병에는 모두 의료계에 저마다의 후원자가 있는데, 그중에서도 신경열, 곧 정신에 깃드는 열만큼 후원자가 많은 병은 없다."], ["And here we may account for those disagreements in opinion, concerning the cause of a patient's death, which sometimes occur, between the most learned of the college; and which have greatly surprized that part of the world who have been ignorant of the fact we have above asserted.", "바로 여기서, 환자의 사인을 두고 그 학식 높은 대가들 사이에서 이따금 벌어지는 그 의견 불일치를 설명할 수 있다. 우리가 위에서 말한 사실을 몰랐던 세상 사람들은 그런 불일치에 크게 놀라곤 했던 것이다."]], [["The reader may perhaps be surprized, that, instead of endeavouring to revive the patient, the learned gentlemen should fall immediately into a dispute on the occasion of his death; but in reality all such experiments had been made before their arrival: for the captain was put into a warm bed, had his veins scarified, his forehead chafed, and all sorts of strong drops applied to his lips and nostrils.", "독자는 어쩌면, 환자를 되살리려 애쓰기는커녕 이 학식 있는 신사들이 곧장 사인을 두고 논쟁에 빠져든 것이 의아할지도 모른다. 그러나 사실 그런 온갖 처치는 그들이 도착하기 전에 이미 다 행해져 있었다. 대위는 따뜻한 침상에 뉘여졌고, 혈관을 칼로 째어 피를 뺐으며, 이마를 문질렀고, 온갖 독한 물약을 그의 입술과 콧구멍에 발랐던 것이다."]], [["The physicians, therefore, finding themselves anticipated in everything they ordered, were at a loss how to apply that portion of time which it is usual and decent to remain for their fee, and were therefore necessitated to find some subject or other for discourse; and what could more naturally present itself than that before mentioned?", "그리하여 의사들은 자기들이 지시할 만한 모든 것이 이미 앞질러 행해진 것을 알고는, 진료비를 받기 위해 머무는 것이 으레 점잖게 여겨지는 그 얼마간의 시간을 어찌 메워야 할지 난감해졌다. 그래서 무엇이든 이야깃거리를 찾을 수밖에 없었으니, 앞서 말한 그 주제보다 더 자연스레 떠오를 것이 무엇이었겠는가?"]], [["Our doctors were about to take their leave, when Mr Allworthy, having given over the captain, and acquiesced in the Divine will, began to enquire after his sister, whom he desired them to visit before their departure.", "우리의 의사들이 막 작별을 고하려던 참에, 대위를 단념하고 하느님의 뜻에 따른 올워디 씨가 누이의 안부를 묻기 시작하더니, 떠나기 전에 그녀를 한 번 살펴봐 달라고 청했다."]], [["This lady was now recovered of her fit, and, to use the common phrase, as well as could be expected for one in her condition.", "이 부인은 이제 발작에서 깨어나, 흔히 쓰는 말마따나 그녀의 처지에서 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 회복되어 있었다."], ["The doctors, therefore, all previous ceremonies being complied with, as this was a new patient, attended, according to desire, and laid hold on each of her hands, as they had before done on those of the corpse.", "그리하여 의사들은, 새 환자인 만큼 앞서의 모든 격식을 다 갖춘 뒤, 청에 따라 그녀를 돌보러 가서, 앞서 시신에게 했던 그대로 그녀의 양손을 각각 하나씩 잡았다."]], [["The case of the lady was in the other extreme from that of her husband: for as he was past all the assistance of physic, so in reality she required none.", "부인의 경우는 남편의 경우와 정반대 끝에 놓여 있었다. 남편은 의술의 어떤 도움도 받을 수 없는 지경이었던 반면, 그녀는 사실 아무런 도움도 필요치 않았던 것이다."]], [["There is nothing more unjust than the vulgar opinion, by which physicians are misrepresented, as friends to death.", "세상에 떠도는 속된 견해만큼 부당한 것도 없으니, 그 견해는 의사들을 마치 죽음의 친구인 양 왜곡한다."], ["On the contrary, I believe, if the number of those who recover by physic could be opposed to that of the martyrs to it, the former would rather exceed the latter.", "오히려 나는 이렇게 믿는다. 의술로 회복한 사람의 수를 그 의술의 희생자가 된 사람의 수와 견주어 본다면, 앞쪽이 오히려 뒤쪽을 웃돌 것이라고."], ["Nay, some are so cautious on this head, that, to avoid a possibility of killing the patient, they abstain from all methods of curing, and prescribe nothing but what can neither do good nor harm.", "아니, 어떤 의사들은 이 점에서 어찌나 조심스러운지, 환자를 죽일 가능성을 피하려고 아예 모든 치료법을 삼가고, 득도 해도 끼치지 못할 것 외에는 아무것도 처방하지 않는다."], ["I have heard some of these, with great gravity, deliver it as a maxim, “That Nature should be left to do her own work, while the physician stands by as it were to clap her on the back, and encourage her when she doth well.”", "나는 이런 의사들 가운데 몇이 자못 진지하게 다음과 같은 금언을 내놓는 것을 들은 적이 있다. \"자연이 제 할 일을 하도록 내버려 두어야 하며, 의사는 그저 곁에 서서, 자연이 일을 잘 해낼 때 그 등을 두드리며 북돋아 주기만 하면 된다.\""]], [["So little then did our doctors delight in death, that they discharged the corpse after a single fee; but they were not so disgusted with their living patient; concerning whose case they immediately agreed, and fell to prescribing with great diligence.", "이렇듯 우리 의사들은 죽음을 그리 반기지 않았기에 진료비 한 번만 받고 시신을 놓아주었다. 그러나 살아 있는 환자에게는 그만큼 싫증을 내지 않아서, 그녀의 경우에 대해서는 곧바로 의견을 모으고 자못 부지런히 처방에 매달렸다."]], [["Whether, as the lady had at first persuaded her physicians to believe her ill, they had now, in return, persuaded her to believe herself so, I will not determine; but she continued a whole month with all the decorations of sickness.", "부인이 처음에 자기 의사들로 하여금 그녀가 아프다고 믿게 만들었던 것이, 이제 그 보답으로 의사들이 그녀로 하여금 스스로 아프다고 믿게 만든 것인지 아닌지는 내가 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 꼬박 한 달 동안 병의 온갖 장식을 두른 채 지냈다."], ["During this time she was visited by physicians, attended by nurses, and received constant messages from her acquaintance to enquire after her health.", "그동안 그녀는 의사들의 방문을 받고, 간병인의 시중을 받았으며, 안부를 묻는 지인들의 전갈을 끊임없이 받았다."]], [["At length the decent time for sickness and immoderate grief being expired, the doctors were discharged, and the lady began to see company; being altered only from what she was before, by that colour of sadness in which she had dressed her person and countenance.", "마침내 병과 지나친 슬픔에 어울리는 점잖은 기간이 다 지나자, 의사들은 물러났고, 부인은 손님을 맞기 시작했다. 다만 예전과 달라진 점이 있다면, 그녀가 제 몸과 얼굴에 둘러 입은 그 슬픔의 빛깔뿐이었다."]], [["The captain was now interred, and might, perhaps, have already made a large progress towards oblivion, had not the friendship of Mr Allworthy taken care to preserve his memory, by the following epitaph, which was written by a man of as great genius as integrity, and one who perfectly well knew the captain.", "대위는 이제 땅에 묻혔고, 어쩌면 이미 망각을 향해 한참을 나아갔을지도 모른다. 만일 올워디 씨의 우정이 다음과 같은 비문으로 그의 기억을 지켜 두려 애쓰지 않았더라면 말이다. 이 비문은 정직함만큼이나 큰 재능을 지녔으며 대위를 더없이 잘 알았던 한 사람이 지은 것이었다."]], [["# CAPTAIN JOHN BLIFIL.", "# CAPTAIN JOHN BLIFIL."]], [["LONDON HAD THE HONOUR OF HIS BIRTH, OXFORD OF HIS EDUCATION.", "여기, 기쁜 부활을 기다리며, 한 사람의 육신이 잠들다. 존 블리필 대위."]], [["# LONDON HAD THE HONOUR OF HIS BIRTH, OXFORD OF HIS EDUCATION.", "# LONDON HAD THE HONOUR OF HIS BIRTH, OXFORD OF HIS EDUCATION."]], [["HIS PARTS WERE AN HONOUR TO HIS PROFESSION AND TO HIS COUNTRY: HIS LIFE, TO HIS RELIGION AND HUMAN NATURE.", "런던은 그가 태어난 영광을 누렸고, 옥스퍼드는 그가 배운 영광을 누렸다. 그의 재능은 그의 직업과 그의 조국에 영예였으며, 그의 삶은 그의 종교와 인간 본성에 영예였다."], ["HE WAS A DUTIFUL SON, A TENDER HUSBAND, AN AFFECTIONATE FATHER, A MOST KIND BROTHER, A SINCERE FRIEND, A DEVOUT CHRISTIAN, AND A GOOD MAN.", "그는 효성스러운 아들이요, 다정한 남편이요, 자애로운 아버지요, 더없이 친절한 형제요, 진실한 벗이요, 독실한 그리스도교인이요, 선한 사람이었다."]], [["HIS INCONSOLABLE WIDOW HATH ERECTED THIS STONE, THE MONUMENT OF HIS VIRTUES AND OF HER AFFECTION.", "위로받을 길 없는 그의 과부가 이 돌을 세우니, 그의 덕과 그녀의 사랑을 기리는 비석이라."]], [["# BOOK III.", "# BOOK III."]], [["CONTAINING THE MOST MEMORABLE TRANSACTIONS WHICH PASSED IN THE FAMILY OF MR ALLWORTHY, FROM THE TIME WHEN TOMMY JONES ARRIVED AT THE AGE OF FOURTEEN, TILL HE ATTAINED THE AGE OF NINETEEN.", "토미 존스가 열네 살에 이른 때부터 열아홉 살에 이르기까지, 올워디 씨 집안에서 일어난 가장 기억할 만한 일들을 담음."], ["IN THIS BOOK THE READER MAY PICK UP SOME HINTS CONCERNING THE EDUCATION OF CHILDREN. Chapter i.", "이 권에서 독자는 아이들의 교육에 관한 몇 가지 단서를 주워 모을 수 있을 것이다."]], [["Containing little or nothing.", "거의 또는 전혀 아무것도 담지 않은 장."]], [["The reader will be pleased to remember, that, at the beginning of the second book of this history, we gave him a hint of our intention to pass over several large periods of time, in which nothing happened worthy of being recorded in a chronicle of this kind.", "독자께서는 이 이야기 제2권의 첫머리에서, 우리가 이런 종류의 연대기에 기록할 만한 일이 아무것도 일어나지 않은 여러 큰 시간의 토막들을 건너뛰겠노라 넌지시 알려 드렸음을 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다."]], [["In so doing, we do not only consult our own dignity and ease, but the good and advantage of the reader: for besides that by these means we prevent him from throwing away his time, in reading without either pleasure or emolument, we give him, at all such seasons, an opportunity of employing that wonderful sagacity, of which he is master, by filling up these vacant spaces of time with his own conjectures; for which purpose we have taken care to qualify him in the preceding pages.", "그렇게 하는 것은 우리 자신의 품위와 편안함을 헤아린 것일 뿐 아니라, 독자의 이로움과 이익을 헤아린 일이기도 하다. 이렇게 함으로써 우리는 독자가 즐거움도 소득도 없는 글을 읽느라 시간을 허비하는 것을 막아 드릴 뿐 아니라, 그런 모든 대목마다 독자가 지닌 그 놀라운 통찰력을 발휘할 기회를 드리는 셈이기 때문이다. 다시 말해 이 비어 있는 시간의 공간들을 독자 자신의 추측으로 채워 보게 하려는 것인데, 우리는 바로 이 목적을 위해 앞선 여러 쪽에서 독자가 그럴 자격을 갖추도록 미리 정성을 들여 두었다."]], [["For instance, what reader but knows that Mr Allworthy felt, at first, for the loss of his friend, those emotions of grief, which on such occasions enter into all men whose hearts are not composed of flint, or their heads of as solid materials?", "예컨대, 올워디 씨가 처음에 벗을 잃고서 슬픔의 감정에 휩싸였으리라는 것을 모를 독자가 어디 있겠는가? 그런 일을 당했을 때, 가슴이 부싯돌로 빚어지지 않았거나 머리가 그만큼 단단한 재료로 이루어지지 않은 사람이라면 누구나 그러한 감정에 잠기는 법이다."], ["Again, what reader doth not know that philosophy and religion in time moderated, and at last extinguished, this grief?", "또한 철학과 종교가 시간이 흐르면서 이 슬픔을 누그러뜨리고 마침내 꺼뜨려 버렸다는 것을 모를 독자가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["The former of these teaching the folly and vanity of it, and the latter correcting it as unlawful, and at the same time assuaging it, by raising future hopes and assurances, which enable a strong and religious mind to take leave of a friend, on his deathbed, with little less indifference than if he was preparing for a long journey; and, indeed, with little less hope of seeing him again.", "앞의 것은 슬픔의 어리석음과 헛됨을 가르치고, 뒤의 것은 그것을 옳지 못한 것으로 바로잡는 한편, 앞날의 희망과 확신을 일으켜 그것을 달래 준다. 그 덕에 굳건하고 신실한 마음을 가진 이는, 마치 긴 여행을 떠날 채비를 하는 사람을 대하듯, 임종을 맞은 벗과도 그에 못지않은 담담함으로 작별할 수 있으며, 실로 그를 다시 만나리라는 희망 또한 그에 못지않게 품을 수 있는 것이다."]], [["Nor can the judicious reader be at a greater loss on account of Mrs Bridget Blifil, who, he may be assured, conducted herself through the whole season in which grief is to make its appearance on the outside of the body, with the strictest regard to all the rules of custom and decency, suiting the alterations of her countenance to the several alterations of her habit: for as this changed from weeds to black, from black to grey, from grey to white, so did her countenance change from dismal to sorrowful, from sorrowful to sad, and from sad to serious, till the day came in which she was allowed to return to her former serenity.", "또한 분별 있는 독자라면 브리짓 블리필 부인에 대해서도 그리 크게 헤맬 까닭이 없을 것이다. 그녀가 슬픔이 몸 바깥으로 드러나야 하는 그 모든 기간 내내, 관습과 예법의 온갖 규칙을 가장 엄격하게 지키며 처신했음을, 그리하여 자기 얼굴빛의 변화를 옷차림의 갖가지 변화에 맞추어 갔음을 독자는 믿어도 좋다. 그녀의 옷이 상복에서 검은색으로, 검은색에서 회색으로, 회색에서 흰색으로 바뀌어 감에 따라, 그녀의 얼굴빛 또한 침울함에서 비통함으로, 비통함에서 서글픔으로, 서글픔에서 진지함으로 바뀌어 갔으니, 마침내 그녀가 예전의 평온함으로 돌아가도 좋다고 허락된 날이 올 때까지 그러하였다."]], [["We have mentioned these two, as examples only of the task which may be imposed on readers of the lowest class.", "우리가 이 두 사람을 언급한 것은, 다만 가장 낮은 등급의 독자에게 부과될 법한 과제의 본보기로서일 뿐이다."], ["Much higher and harder exercises of judgment and penetration may reasonably be expected from the upper graduates in criticism.", "비평에서 더 높은 학위를 받은 독자들에게는, 이보다 훨씬 높고 까다로운 판단력과 통찰력의 연습을 마땅히 기대할 만하다. 우리가 건너뛰는 편이 좋겠다고 여긴 그 모든 세월 동안, 우리의 가치 있는 인물의 집안에서 일어난 일들에 관하여, 그런 독자들이 주목할 만한 발견을 많이 해내리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다."], ["Many notable discoveries will, I doubt not, be made by such, of the transactions which happened in the family of our worthy man, during all the years which we have thought proper to pass over: for though nothing worthy of a place in this history occurred within that period, yet did several incidents happen of equal importance with those reported by the daily and weekly historians of the age; in reading which great numbers of persons consume a considerable part of their time, very little, I am afraid, to their emolument.", "그 기간 안에 이 이야기에 자리를 차지할 만한 일이라곤 아무것도 일어나지 않았으되, 그래도 이 시대의 일간·주간 사료 기록자들이 보도하는 사건들 못지않게 중요한 사건들이 여럿 일어나기는 했으니 말이다. 수많은 사람이 그런 글을 읽느라 자기 시간의 상당 부분을 써 버리지만, 유감스럽게도 그들에게 돌아가는 소득은 거의 없는 듯하다."], ["Now, in the conjectures here proposed, some of the most excellent faculties of the mind may be employed to much advantage, since it is a more useful capacity to be able to foretel the actions of men, in any circumstance, from their characters, than to judge of their characters from their actions.", "자, 여기서 우리가 권하는 추측 속에서라면, 마음의 가장 뛰어난 능력 가운데 몇 가지를 크게 이롭게 부려 볼 수 있을 것이다. 어떤 상황에서든 사람들의 성격으로부터 그들의 행동을 미리 내다보는 능력은, 그들의 행동으로부터 그 성격을 판단하는 능력보다 더 쓸모 있는 재간이기 때문이다."], ["The former, I own, requires the greater penetration; but may be accomplished by true sagacity with no less certainty than the latter.", "인정하건대 앞의 것이 더 큰 통찰력을 요구한다. 하지만 참된 명민함만 있다면 뒤의 것에 못지않은 확실함으로 이루어 낼 수 있다."]], [["As we are sensible that much the greatest part of our readers are very eminently possessed of this quality, we have left them a space of twelve years to exert it in; and shall now bring forth our heroe, at about fourteen years of age, not questioning that many have been long impatient to be introduced to his acquaintance.", "우리 독자의 대부분이 이 자질을 대단히 두드러지게 갖추고 있음을 잘 알기에, 우리는 그것을 발휘하시라고 열두 해의 공간을 남겨 드렸다. 그리고 이제 우리의 주인공을 열네 살쯤 된 모습으로 내보내려 하는데, 많은 분이 그와 알고 지내게 되기를 오래도록 애타게 기다려 오셨으리라는 것을 우리는 조금도 의심하지 않는다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["The heroe of this great history appears with very bad omens.", "이 위대한 이야기의 주인공이 매우 불길한 징조와 더불어 등장한다."], ["A little tale of so LOW a kind that some may think it not worth their notice.", "어떤 이들은 눈여겨볼 가치조차 없다고 여길 만큼 너무나 저속한 작은 일화 하나."], ["A word or two concerning a squire, and more relating to a gamekeeper and a schoolmaster.", "어느 시골 지주에 관한 한두 마디와, 사냥터지기와 학교 선생에 관한 좀 더 많은 이야기."]], [["As we determined, when we first sat down to write this history, to flatter no man, but to guide our pen throughout by the directions of truth, we are obliged to bring our heroe on the stage in a much more disadvantageous manner than we could wish; and to declare honestly, even at his first appearance, that it was the universal opinion of all Mr Allworthy's family that he was certainly born to be hanged.", "이 이야기를 쓰려고 처음 자리에 앉았을 때 우리는 그 누구에게도 아첨하지 않고, 오직 진실의 인도를 따라 펜을 움직이기로 마음먹었다. 그러니 우리는 바라는 것보다 훨씬 불리한 방식으로 주인공을 무대에 올릴 수밖에 없으며, 그가 처음 등장하는 바로 그 순간조차 정직하게 밝혀야만 한다. 올워디 씨 댁의 모든 사람이 한결같이 품은 의견은, 이 아이가 틀림없이 교수대에 매달리려고 태어났다는 것이었다고."]], [["Indeed, I am sorry to say there was too much reason for this conjecture; the lad having from his earliest years discovered a propensity to many vices, and especially to one which hath as direct a tendency as any other to that fate which we have just now observed to have been prophetically denounced against him: he had been already convicted of three robberies, viz., of robbing an orchard, of stealing a duck out of a farmer's yard, and of picking Master Blifil's pocket of a ball.", "실로 유감스럽게도 이 추측에는 너무도 많은 근거가 있었음을 말해 두어야겠다. 이 소년은 아주 어린 시절부터 여러 악덕에 기우는 성향을 드러냈으며, 특히 우리가 방금 그에게 예언처럼 선고되었다고 말한 그 운명으로 곧장 이끄는, 다른 무엇 못지않게 직접적인 성향 하나를 보였던 것이다. 그는 이미 세 건의 도둑질로 유죄가 입증된 바 있었으니, 곧 과수원을 턴 것, 어느 농부의 마당에서 오리를 한 마리 훔친 것, 그리고 블리필 도련님의 주머니에서 공을 슬쩍한 것이었다."]], [["The vices of this young man were, moreover, heightened by the disadvantageous light in which they appeared when opposed to the virtues of Master Blifil, his companion; a youth of so different a cast from little Jones, that not only the family but all the neighbourhood resounded his praises. He was, indeed, a lad of a remarkable disposition; sober, discreet, and pious beyond his age; qualities which gained him the love of every one who knew him: while Tom Jones was universally disliked; and many expressed their wonder that Mr Allworthy would suffer such a lad to be educated with his nephew, lest the morals of the latter should be corrupted by his example.", "게다가 이 어린아이의 악덕은, 그의 동무인 블리필 도련님의 미덕과 견주어졌을 때 한층 불리한 빛 속에 도드라져 보였다. 블리필은 어린 존스와는 너무도 다른 부류의 소년이어서, 집안뿐 아니라 온 이웃이 그의 칭찬을 울려 퍼뜨렸다. 그는 참으로 남다른 기질의 소년이었다. 제 나이를 넘어설 만큼 차분하고 신중하며 경건했으니, 이런 자질들은 그를 아는 모든 이의 사랑을 얻게 했다. 반면 톰 존스는 누구에게나 미움을 받았으며, 많은 이들이 올워디 씨가 어찌하여 저런 아이를 자기 조카와 함께 교육받도록 내버려 두는지 의아해했다. 조카의 품행이 그 본보기 때문에 더럽혀질까 염려했던 것이다."]], [["An incident which happened about this time will set the characters of these two lads more fairly before the discerning reader than is in the power of the longest dissertation.", "이즈음에 일어난 한 사건은, 아무리 긴 논설로도 못할 만큼 이 두 소년의 성격을 분별 있는 독자 앞에 더 공정하게 펼쳐 보여 줄 것이다."]], [["Tom Jones, who, bad as he is, must serve for the heroe of this history, had only one friend among all the servants of the family; for as to Mrs Wilkins, she had long since given him up, and was perfectly reconciled to her mistress.", "톰 존스는, 비록 그 행실이 나쁘다 해도 어쨌든 이 이야기의 주인공 노릇을 해야 할 인물인데, 집안의 모든 하인 가운데 단 한 명의 벗밖에 없었다. 윌킨스 부인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 오래전에 그를 단념하고 제 여주인과 완전히 화해한 터였기 때문이다."], ["This friend was the gamekeeper, a fellow of a loose kind of disposition, and who was thought not to entertain much stricter notions concerning the difference of meum and tuum than the young gentleman himself.", "그 벗이란 바로 사냥터지기였으니, 그는 헐겁고 헤픈 기질의 사내로서, '내 것(meum)'과 '네 것(tuum)'의 구별에 관해 그 젊은 도련님 자신보다 별로 더 엄격한 관념을 품고 있지 않다고 여겨지는 자였다."], ["And hence this friendship gave occasion to many sarcastical remarks among the domestics, most of which were either proverbs before, or at least are become so now; and, indeed, the wit of them all may be comprised in that short Latin proverb, “Noscitur a socio;”", "그리하여 이 우정은 하인들 사이에서 많은 빈정거림의 빌미가 되었는데, 그 대부분은 이미 속담이었거나, 적어도 이제는 속담이 되었다. 그리고 사실 그 모든 재담의 묘미는 짧은 라틴어 속담 하나에 다 담을 수 있으니, 곧 \"노스키투르 아 소키오(Noscitur a socio)\"다."], ["which, I think, is thus expressed in English, “You may know him by the company he keeps.”", "내 생각에 이것은 영어로 \"그가 어울리는 무리를 보면 그를 알 수 있다\"로 옮겨진다."]], [["To say the truth, some of that atrocious wickedness in Jones, of which we have just mentioned three examples, might perhaps be derived from the encouragement he had received from this fellow, who, in two or three instances, had been what the law calls an accessary after the fact: for the whole duck, and great part of the apples, were converted to the use of the gamekeeper and his family; though, as Jones alone was discovered, the poor lad bore not only the whole smart, but the whole blame; both which fell again to his lot on the following occasion.", "사실대로 말하자면, 우리가 방금 세 가지 예를 든 존스의 그 끔찍한 못된 짓 가운데 얼마간은, 어쩌면 이 사내에게서 받은 부추김에서 비롯된 것일지도 모른다. 그는 두세 차례에 걸쳐, 법에서 이른바 사후 종범(從犯)이라 부르는 역할을 했던 것이다. 오리 한 마리는 통째로, 사과는 그 태반이 사냥터지기와 그 식구들의 몫으로 돌아갔으니 말이다. 그런데도 존스 혼자만 발각되었기에, 가엾은 소년은 온갖 매질뿐 아니라 온갖 비난까지 도맡았다. 그리고 그 둘은 다음과 같은 사건에서 또다시 그의 차지가 되었다."]], [["Contiguous to Mr Allworthy's estate was the manor of one of those gentlemen who are called preservers of the game. This species of men, from the great severity with which they revenge the death of a hare or partridge, might be thought to cultivate the same superstition with the Bannians in India; many of whom, we are told, dedicate their whole lives to the preservation and protection of certain animals; was it not that our English Bannians, while they preserve them from other enemies, will most unmercifully slaughter whole horse-loads themselves; so that they stand clearly acquitted of any such heathenish superstition.", "올워디 씨의 영지에 잇닿아, 이른바 사냥감 보호자라 불리는 신사들 가운데 한 사람의 영지가 있었다. 이런 부류의 사람들은 산토끼나 자고새의 죽음을 어찌나 가혹하게 앙갚음하는지, 인도의 바니안교도와 똑같은 미신을 떠받든다고 여겨질 만하다. 우리가 듣기로 그들 중 많은 이들은 어떤 동물들의 보존과 보호에 평생을 바친다는 것이다. 다만 우리 잉글랜드의 바니안교도들은 그 짐승들을 다른 적들로부터 지켜 주면서도, 정작 저희가 말 등에 가득 실을 만큼을 더없이 무자비하게 도살해 버린다는 점이 다를 뿐이다. 그러니 그들은 그런 이교도적 미신의 혐의에서만큼은 깨끗이 벗어난 셈이다."]], [["I have, indeed, a much better opinion of this kind of men than is entertained by some, as I take them to answer the order of Nature, and the good purposes for which they were ordained, in a more ample manner than many others.", "실로 나는 이런 부류의 사람들에 대해 어떤 이들이 품은 것보다 훨씬 나은 견해를 지니고 있다. 그들이야말로 자연의 질서와, 그들이 마련된 그 선한 목적에 다른 많은 이들보다 더 충실하게 부응한다고 보기 때문이다."], ["Now, as Horace tells us that there are a set of human beings", "자, 호라티우스가 우리에게 일러 주기를, 인간들 가운데는 한 부류가 있어\n프루게스 콘수메레 나티(Fruges consumere nati),"]], [["“Born to consume the fruits of the earth;”", "곧 \"땅의 열매를 소비하려고 태어난 자들\"이 있다 하니, 나는 또 다른 부류가 있음을 조금도 의심하지 않는다."], ["so I make no manner of doubt but that there are others", "페라스 콘수메레 나티(Feras consumere nati),"]], [["Feras consumere nati,\n“Born to consume the beasts of the field;”", "곧 \"들판의 짐승을 소비하려고 태어난 자들\", 흔히 이르는 대로 사냥감을 소비하려 태어난 자들 말이다."], ["or, as it is commonly called, the game; and none, I believe, will deny but that those squires fulfil this end of their creation.", "그리고 저 시골 지주들이야말로 그들이 창조된 이 목적을 충실히 이룬다는 데에는 아무도 토를 달지 않으리라 믿는다."]], [["Little Jones went one day a shooting with the gamekeeper; when happening to spring a covey of partridges near the border of that manor over which Fortune, to fulfil the wise purposes of Nature, had planted one of the game consumers, the birds flew into it, and were marked (as it is called) by the two sportsmen, in some furze bushes, about two or three hundred paces beyond Mr Allworthy's dominions.", "어린 존스는 어느 날 사냥터지기와 함께 사냥을 나갔다. 그러다 자고새 떼를 그 영지의 경계 가까이에서 푸드덕 날려 보내게 되었는데, 그곳은 운명이 자연의 현명한 목적을 이루고자 사냥감 소비자 한 사람을 심어 둔 땅이었다. 새들은 그쪽으로 날아들었고, 두 사냥꾼은 올워디 씨 영토를 이삼백 보쯤 넘어선 어느 가시금작화 덤불 속에서 그 새들이 내려앉은 자리를 (이른바) 점찍어 두었다."]], [["Mr Allworthy had given the fellow strict orders, on pain of forfeiting his place, never to trespass on any of his neighbours; no more on those who were less rigid in this matter than on the lord of this manor. With regard to others, indeed, these orders had not been always very scrupulously kept; but as the disposition of the gentleman with whom the partridges had taken sanctuary was well known, the gamekeeper had never yet attempted to invade his territories. Nor had he done it now, had not the younger sportsman, who was excessively eager to pursue the flying game, over-persuaded him; but Jones being very importunate, the other, who was himself keen enough after the sport, yielded to his persuasions, entered the manor, and shot one of the partridges.", "올워디 씨는 그 사내에게, 자리를 잃는 벌을 무릅쓰더라도 결코 이웃의 어느 땅에도 함부로 들어가지 말라고 엄히 명해 두었다. 이 일에 덜 엄격한 자들의 땅이든, 이 영지의 주인 같은 자의 땅이든 가리지 말라는 것이었다. 실로 다른 이들에 관해서라면 이 명령이 늘 그렇게 꼼꼼히 지켜지지는 않았다. 그러나 자고새들이 피신처로 삼은 그 신사의 기질이 워낙 잘 알려져 있었기에, 사냥터지기는 그때껏 그의 영역을 침범하려 시도한 적이 한 번도 없었다. 이번에도 마찬가지였을 것이다. 날아가는 사냥감을 좇으려고 안달이 난 어린 사냥꾼이 그를 한사코 설득하지만 않았더라면 말이다. 그러나 존스가 어찌나 졸라 대던지, 저 역시 사냥에 충분히 열을 올리던 사내는 그 설득에 넘어가 영지에 들어갔고, 자고새 한 마리를 쏘아 잡았다."]], [["The gentleman himself was at that time on horse-back, at a little distance from them; and hearing the gun go off, he immediately made towards the place, and discovered poor Tom; for the gamekeeper had leapt into the thickest part of the furze-brake, where he had happily concealed himself.", "그 신사 본인이 마침 그때 그들에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에서 말을 타고 있었다. 그는 총소리를 듣자 곧장 그 자리로 향했고, 가엾은 톰을 찾아냈다. 사냥터지기는 가시금작화 덤불의 가장 빽빽한 속으로 뛰어들어 다행히 몸을 숨긴 터였기 때문이다."]], [["The gentleman having searched the lad, and found the partridge upon him, denounced great vengeance, swearing he would acquaint Mr Allworthy.", "그 신사는 소년의 몸을 뒤져 자고새를 찾아내고는, 올워디 씨에게 알리겠노라 맹세하며 단단히 앙갚음하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."], ["He was as good as his word: for he rode immediately to his house, and complained of the trespass on his manor in as high terms and as bitter language as if his house had been broken open, and the most valuable furniture stole out of it.", "그는 제 말을 어김없이 지켰다. 곧장 말을 달려 올워디 씨 댁으로 가서, 마치 제 집이 부서져 열리고 가장 값진 가구들이 도둑맞기라도 한 것처럼, 더없이 거센 어조와 더없이 신랄한 말로 제 영지를 침범당한 일을 하소연한 것이다."], ["He added, that some other person was in his company, though he could not discover him; for that two guns had been discharged almost in the same instant.", "그는 덧붙이기를, 일행 가운데 또 다른 누군가가 있었으나 미처 찾아내지는 못했다고 했다. 거의 같은 순간에 총이 두 발 발사되었기 때문이라는 것이었다."], ["And, says he, “We have found only this partridge, but the Lord knows what mischief they have done.”", "그러고는 이렇게 말했다. \"우리는 이 자고새 한 마리밖에 못 찾았소만, 그놈들이 무슨 못된 짓을 저질렀는지는 하느님만 아실 거요.\""]], [["At his return home, Tom was presently convened before Mr Allworthy.", "집에 돌아오자 톰은 곧바로 올워디 씨 앞으로 불려 나왔다."], ["He owned the fact, and alledged no other excuse but what was really true, viz., that the covey was originally sprung in Mr Allworthy's own manor.", "그는 그 일을 시인했고, 정말로 사실인 것 말고는 다른 어떤 변명도 늘어놓지 않았다. 곧 그 새 떼가 본디 올워디 씨 자신의 영지에서 날아오른 것이라는 사실이었다."]], [["Tom was then interrogated who was with him, which Mr Allworthy declared he was resolved to know, acquainting the culprit with the circumstance of the two guns, which had been deposed by the squire and both his servants; but Tom stoutly persisted in asserting that he was alone; yet, to say the truth, he hesitated a little at first, which would have confirmed Mr Allworthy's belief, had what the squire and his servants said wanted any further confirmation.", "이어 톰은 누구와 함께 있었느냐는 신문을 받았다. 올워디 씨는 기어이 알아내고야 말겠다고 선언하며, 그 지주와 그의 두 하인이 진술한 대로 총이 두 발 발사되었다는 정황을 이 죄인에게 일러 주었다. 그러나 톰은 자기 혼자였다고 한사코 우겨 댔다. 다만 사실대로 말하자면, 처음에 그는 잠깐 머뭇거렸으니, 만일 그 지주와 하인들이 한 말에 더 보탤 확증이 필요했더라면 이 머뭇거림이 올워디 씨의 믿음을 굳혀 주었을 것이다."]], [["The gamekeeper, being a suspected person, was now sent for, and the question put to him; but he, relying on the promise which Tom had made him, to take all upon himself, very resolutely denied being in company with the young gentleman, or indeed having seen him the whole afternoon.", "이제 의심을 받던 사냥터지기가 불려 와 같은 질문을 받았다. 그러나 그는 모든 것을 제가 뒤집어쓰겠다던 톰의 약속을 믿고서, 그 젊은 도련님과 함께 있었다는 것은 물론, 실은 그날 오후 내내 그를 본 적조차 없다고 아주 단호하게 부인했다."]], [["Mr Allworthy then turned towards Tom, with more than usual anger in his countenance, and advised him to confess who was with him; repeating, that he was resolved to know.", "그러자 올워디 씨는 평소보다 더한 노여움을 얼굴에 띤 채 톰 쪽으로 돌아서서, 누구와 함께 있었는지 실토하라고 타이르며 기어이 알아내고야 말겠다는 말을 거듭했다."], ["The lad, however, still maintained his resolution, and was dismissed with much wrath by Mr Allworthy, who told him he should have to the next morning to consider of it, when he should be questioned by another person, and in another manner.", "그러나 소년은 여전히 제 결심을 굽히지 않았고, 올워디 씨는 단단히 진노하여 그를 물러가게 했다. 그러면서 이튿날 아침까지 곰곰이 생각해 둘 시간을 주겠노라고, 그때는 다른 사람이 다른 방식으로 너를 신문하게 될 것이라고 일렀다."]], [["Poor Jones spent a very melancholy night; and the more so, as he was without his usual companion; for Master Blifil was gone abroad on a visit with his mother. Fear of the punishment he was to suffer was on this occasion his least evil; his chief anxiety being, lest his constancy should fail him, and he should be brought to betray the gamekeeper, whose ruin he knew must now be the consequence.", "가엾은 존스는 몹시도 울적한 밤을 보냈다. 더욱이 늘 함께하던 동무마저 곁에 없었으니 그 울적함은 한층 더했다. 블리필 도련님은 어머니와 함께 어디론가 방문을 떠나 있었던 것이다. 이번 경우에 그가 받게 될 벌에 대한 두려움은 그에게 가장 작은 근심거리였다. 그의 가장 큰 걱정은, 혹여 제 굳건함이 무너져 사냥터지기를 배신하게 되지나 않을까 하는 것이었으니, 이제 그 결과가 그 사내의 파멸일 수밖에 없음을 알았기 때문이다."]], [["Nor did the gamekeeper pass his time much better.", "사냥터지기 또한 그보다 그리 나은 시간을 보내지는 못했다."], ["He had the same apprehensions with the youth; for whose honour he had likewise a much tenderer regard than for his skin.", "그는 소년과 똑같은 불안을 품고 있었으니, 제 가죽보다도 소년의 명예를 한결 더 애틋하게 여겼던 것이다."]], [["In the morning, when Tom attended the reverend Mr Thwackum, the person to whom Mr Allworthy had committed the instruction of the two boys, he had the same questions put to him by that gentleman which he had been asked the evening before, to which he returned the same answers.", "아침이 되어 톰이 트와컴 목사 앞에 나아갔을 때 — 그는 올워디 씨가 두 소년의 가르침을 맡긴 사람이었다 — 그 신사는 전날 저녁에 받았던 것과 똑같은 질문들을 그에게 던졌고, 톰은 똑같은 대답으로 응했다."], ["The consequence of this was, so severe a whipping, that it possibly fell little short of the torture with which confessions are in some countries extorted from criminals.", "그 결과는 어찌나 혹독한 매질이었던지, 어쩌면 어떤 나라들에서 죄인에게서 자백을 쥐어짜 내는 그 고문에 거의 못지않았을 정도였다."]], [["Tom bore his punishment with great resolution; and though his master asked him, between every stroke, whether he would not confess, he was contented to be flead rather than betray his friend, or break the promise he had made.", "톰은 굳은 결심으로 제 벌을 견뎌 냈다. 매를 한 대씩 칠 때마다 선생이 그에게 이래도 실토하지 않겠느냐고 물었지만, 그는 제 벗을 배신하거나 제가 한 약속을 어기느니 차라리 살가죽이 벗겨지는 편을 달게 받아들였다."]], [["The gamekeeper was now relieved from his anxiety, and Mr Allworthy himself began to be concerned at Tom's sufferings: for besides that Mr Thwackum, being highly enraged that he was not able to make the boy say what he himself pleased, had carried his severity much beyond the good man's intention, this latter began now to suspect that the squire had been mistaken; which his extreme eagerness and anger seemed to make probable; and as for what the servants had said in confirmation of their master's account, he laid no great stress upon that. Now, as cruelty and injustice were two ideas of which Mr Allworthy could by no means support the consciousness a single moment, he sent for Tom, and after many kind and friendly exhortations, said, “I am convinced, my dear child, that my suspicions have wronged you; I am sorry that you have been so severely punished on this account.” And at last gave him a little horse to make him amends; again repeating his sorrow for what had past.", "이제 사냥터지기는 불안에서 놓여났고, 올워디 씨 자신도 톰의 고초를 마음에 걸려 하기 시작했다. 트와컴 씨가 그 소년에게서 제가 듣고 싶은 말을 끌어내지 못한 데 잔뜩 격분한 나머지 그 가혹함을 이 선량한 분의 본뜻을 훨씬 넘어서까지 몰고 갔다는 점은 차치하더라도, 올워디 씨는 이제 그 지주가 잘못 안 것이 아닐까 의심하기 시작했던 것이다. 그 지주의 지나친 열기와 노여움이 그럴 법하게 보이도록 만들었기 때문이다. 그리고 하인들이 제 주인의 말을 뒷받침하느라 한 진술에 관해서는, 그는 그것에 별로 무게를 두지 않았다. 자, 잔혹함과 부당함이란 올워디 씨로서는 단 한순간도 그 의식을 도저히 견뎌 낼 수 없는 두 관념이었으므로, 그는 톰을 불러들였다. 그러고는 다정하고 정겨운 훈계를 잔뜩 늘어놓은 끝에 이렇게 말했다. \"얘야, 내 의심이 너를 억울하게 했다는 것을 이제 확신하게 되었구나. 이 일로 네가 그토록 모질게 벌을 받았으니 참으로 안타깝다.\" 그러고는 마침내 그에게 보상으로 작은 망아지 한 마리를 주면서, 지나간 일에 대한 자신의 안타까움을 거듭 되뇌었다."]], [["Tom's guilt now flew in his face more than any severity could make it.", "이제 톰의 죄책감은 어떤 가혹함도 못 미칠 만큼 그의 얼굴을 후려쳤다."], ["He could more easily bear the lashes of Thwackum, than the generosity of Allworthy.", "그는 트와컴의 채찍질보다 올워디의 너그러움을 더 견디기 어려웠다."], ["The tears burst from his eyes, and he fell upon his knees, crying, “Oh, sir, you are too good to me.", "두 눈에서 눈물이 터져 나왔고, 그는 무릎을 꿇으며 울부짖었다. \"오, 어르신, 어르신은 제게 너무 잘해 주십니다."], ["Indeed you are.", "정말로 그러십니다."], ["Indeed I don't deserve it.”", "정말이지 저는 그럴 자격이 없습니다.\""], ["And at that very instant, from the fulness of his heart, had almost betrayed the secret; but the good genius of the gamekeeper suggested to him what might be the consequence to the poor fellow, and this consideration sealed his lips.", "그리고 바로 그 순간, 벅차오르는 가슴에 못 이겨 그는 비밀을 거의 털어놓을 뻔했다. 그러나 사냥터지기의 선한 수호신이 그에게, 그 가엾은 사내에게 어떤 결과가 닥칠지를 일깨워 주었고, 이 생각이 그의 입술을 봉해 버렸다."]], [["Thwackum did all he could to persuade Allworthy from showing any compassion or kindness to the boy, saying, “He had persisted in an untruth;”", "트와컴은 올워디가 그 소년에게 어떤 동정이나 친절도 베풀지 못하도록 설득하느라 갖은 애를 썼다. \"그 아이는 거짓을 끝까지 우겨 댔습니다\""], ["and gave some hints, that a second whipping might probably bring the matter to light.", "하고 말하면서, 두 번째 매질이라면 아마도 이 일의 진상을 밝혀낼 수 있을 것이라는 암시를 흘리기도 했다."]], [["But Mr Allworthy absolutely refused to consent to the experiment.", "그러나 올워디 씨는 그 시도에 동의하기를 단호히 거부했다."], ["He said, the boy had suffered enough already for concealing the truth, even if he was guilty, seeing that he could have no motive but a mistaken point of honour for so doing.", "그는 말하기를, 설령 그 아이가 죄가 있다 한들 진실을 숨긴 것만으로도 이미 충분히 고초를 겪었으며, 그렇게 한 데에는 잘못 짚은 명예의 관념 말고는 다른 어떤 동기도 있을 수 없었을 것이라 했다."]], [["“Honour!”", "\"명예라니요!\""], ["cryed Thwackum, with some warmth, “mere stubbornness and obstinacy!", "트와컴이 다소 발끈하며 외쳤다. \"그저 고집이요 외고집일 뿐입니다!"], ["Can honour teach any one to tell a lie, or can any honour exist independent of religion?”", "명예가 누구에게 거짓말을 하라고 가르칠 수 있단 말입니까, 또 종교와 동떨어진 채로 무슨 명예가 존재할 수 있단 말입니까?\""]], [["This discourse happened at table when dinner was just ended; and there were present Mr Allworthy, Mr Thwackum, and a third gentleman, who now entered into the debate, and whom, before we proceed any further, we shall briefly introduce to our reader's acquaintance.", "이 대화는 막 저녁 식사가 끝났을 무렵 식탁에서 오간 것이었다. 그 자리에는 올워디 씨와 트와컴 씨, 그리고 이제 이 논쟁에 끼어든 세 번째 신사가 함께 있었다. 우리는 더 나아가기에 앞서 그를 독자에게 간략히 소개해 두려 한다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["The character of Mr Square the philosopher, and of Mr Thwackum the divine; with a dispute concerning----", "철학자 스퀘어 씨와 신학자 스왜컴 씨의 성격, 그리고 ――에 관한 논쟁."]], [["The name of this gentleman, who had then resided some time at Mr Allworthy's house, was Mr Square.", "그 무렵 올워디 씨의 저택에 한동안 머물고 있던 이 신사의 이름은 스퀘어 씨였다."], ["His natural parts were not of the first rate, but he had greatly improved them by a learned education.", "타고난 재능은 일류라 할 만한 것은 아니었으나, 그는 학문적 교육으로 그것을 크게 갈고닦은 사람이었다."], ["He was deeply read in the antients, and a profest master of all the works of Plato and Aristotle.", "그는 고대 저술가들을 깊이 읽었으며, 플라톤과 아리스토텔레스의 모든 저작에 통달했다고 자처했다."], ["Upon which great models he had principally formed himself; sometimes according with the opinion of the one, and sometimes with that of the other.", "그는 이 위대한 두 본보기를 바탕으로 주로 자신을 빚어 갔으니, 어떤 때는 한 사람의 의견에 따르고 또 어떤 때는 다른 사람의 의견에 따랐다."], ["In morals he was a profest Platonist, and in religion he inclined to be an Aristotelian.", "도덕에서는 자처하는 플라톤주의자였고, 종교에서는 아리스토텔레스주의 쪽으로 기울었다."]], [["But though he had, as we have said, formed his morals on the Platonic model, yet he perfectly agreed with the opinion of Aristotle, in considering that great man rather in the quality of a philosopher or a speculatist, than as a legislator.", "그러나 앞서 말했듯 그가 자신의 도덕을 플라톤의 본보기 위에 세웠음에도, 그 위대한 인물을 입법자라기보다는 차라리 철학자나 사변가의 자질로 여기는 점에서는 아리스토텔레스의 의견에 완전히 동의했다. 이러한 생각을 그는 꽤나 멀리까지 밀고 나갔으니, 실로 모든 덕을 이론의 문제일 뿐이라고 여기는 데까지 이르렀다."], ["This sentiment he carried a great way; indeed, so far, as to regard all virtue as matter of theory only.", "사실 그가 이런 말을 누구에게 단언했다는 이야기는, 내가 들은 바로는 없다."], ["This, it is true, he never affirmed, as I have heard, to any one; and yet upon the least attention to his conduct, I cannot help thinking it was his real opinion, as it will perfectly reconcile some contradictions which might otherwise appear in his character.", "그렇지만 그의 행실을 조금만 눈여겨보면, 나는 그것이 그의 진짜 생각이었다고 여기지 않을 수 없다. 그래야만 그의 성격에서 달리 보면 모순으로 보일 법한 몇 가지가 말끔히 풀리기 때문이다."]], [["This gentleman and Mr Thwackum scarce ever met without a disputation; for their tenets were indeed diametrically opposite to each other.", "이 신사와 스왜컴 씨는 만나기만 하면 거의 빠짐없이 논쟁을 벌였다. 두 사람의 신조가 정말이지 정반대로 어긋나 있었기 때문이다."], ["Square held human nature to be the perfection of all virtue, and that vice was a deviation from our nature, in the same manner as deformity of body is.", "스퀘어는 인간 본성이야말로 모든 덕의 완성이며, 악덕이란 몸의 기형이 그렇듯 우리 본성에서 벗어난 일탈이라고 주장했다."], ["Thwackum, on the contrary, maintained that the human mind, since the fall, was nothing but a sink of iniquity, till purified and redeemed by grace.", "반대로 스왜컴은, 인간의 정신이란 타락 이후로 은총에 의해 정화되고 구원받기 전까지는 죄악의 시궁창에 지나지 않는다고 고집했다."], ["In one point only they agreed, which was, in all their discourses on morality never to mention the word goodness.", "두 사람이 의견을 같이하는 점은 단 하나뿐이었으니, 도덕을 두고 논할 때 결코 선함이라는 말을 입에 올리지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["The favourite phrase of the former, was the natural beauty of virtue; that of the latter, was the divine power of grace.", "전자가 즐겨 쓰는 표현은 덕의 자연스러운 아름다움이었고, 후자가 즐겨 쓰는 표현은 은총의 신성한 권능이었다."], ["The former measured all actions by the unalterable rule of right, and the eternal fitness of things; the latter decided all matters by authority; but in doing this, he always used the scriptures and their commentators, as the lawyer doth his Coke upon Lyttleton, where the comment is of equal authority with the text.", "전자는 모든 행위를 변치 않는 옳음의 규칙과 사물의 영원한 합당함으로 가늠했고, 후자는 모든 문제를 권위로 판가름했다. 다만 그렇게 할 때면 그는 늘 성경과 그 주석가들을 끌어다 썼으니, 마치 법률가가 자신의 《라이틀턴 주해 코크》를 인용할 때 주석이 본문과 똑같은 권위를 지니는 것과 같았다."]], [["After this short introduction, the reader will be pleased to remember, that the parson had concluded his speech with a triumphant question, to which he had apprehended no answer; viz., Can any honour exist independent on religion?", "이 짧은 소개를 마쳤으니, 독자는 그 목사가 자신의 말을 의기양양한 물음으로 끝맺었으며, 거기에 어떤 대답도 돌아오지 않으리라 짐작했다는 사실을 떠올려 주기 바란다. 그 물음이란 곧, 종교와 무관하게 명예라는 것이 존재할 수 있느냐는 것이었다."]], [["To this Square answered; that it was impossible to discourse philosophically concerning words, till their meaning was first established: that there were scarce any two words of a more vague and uncertain signification, than the two he had mentioned; for that there were almost as many different opinions concerning honour, as concerning religion.", "이에 스퀘어가 대답했다. 어떤 말이든 그 뜻이 먼저 정해지기 전에는 철학적으로 논할 수 없는데, 그가 입에 올린 두 단어만큼 막연하고 불확실한 의미를 지닌 말도 좀처럼 없다고 했다."], ["“But,”", "명예에 관한 견해가 종교에 관한 견해 못지않게 제각각이기 때문이라는 것이었다."], ["says he, “if by honour you mean the true natural beauty of virtue, I will maintain it may exist independent of any religion whatever.", "\"하지만,\" 그가 말했다, \"만약 명예라는 말로 그대가 덕의 참된 자연스러운 아름다움을 뜻하는 것이라면, 나는 그것이 그 어떤 종교와도 무관하게 존재할 수 있다고 주장하겠소."], ["Nay,”", "아니,\""], ["added he, “you yourself will allow it may exist independent of all but one: so will a Mahometan, a Jew, and all the maintainers of all the different sects in the world.”", "그가 덧붙였다, \"그대 자신도 그것이 단 하나를 뺀 모든 종교와 무관하게 존재할 수 있음을 인정할 것이오. 마호메트교도도, 유대인도, 세상에 있는 온갖 다른 교파를 지지하는 사람들도 다 그러할 것이오.\""]], [["Thwackum replied, this was arguing with the usual malice of all the enemies to the true Church.", "스왜컴이 받아쳤다. 이는 참된 교회의 모든 적들이 으레 부리는 악의로 논쟁하는 짓이라고 말이다."], ["He said, he doubted not but that all the infidels and hereticks in the world would, if they could, confine honour to their own absurd errors and damnable deceptions; “but honour,”", "그는, 세상의 모든 무신론자와 이단자들이 할 수만 있다면 명예라는 것을 자기네의 터무니없는 오류와 저주받을 기만 속에 가두어 두려 들리라는 점을 의심치 않는다고 했다. \"하지만 명예가,\""], ["says he, “is not therefore manifold, because there are many absurd opinions about it; nor is religion manifold, because there are various sects and heresies in the world.", "그가 말했다, \"그것에 관한 어리석은 견해가 많다고 해서 여러 갈래가 되는 것은 아니오. 종교 또한 세상에 갖가지 교파와 이단이 있다고 해서 여러 갈래가 되는 것은 아니오."], ["When I mention religion, I mean the Christian religion; and not only the Christian religion, but the Protestant religion; and not only the Protestant religion, but the Church of England.", "내가 종교를 입에 올릴 때 내가 뜻하는 것은 기독교요. 그저 기독교만이 아니라 프로테스탄트 종교이며, 그저 프로테스탄트 종교만이 아니라 잉글랜드 교회요."], ["And when I mention honour, I mean that mode of Divine grace which is not only consistent with, but dependent upon, this religion; and is consistent with and dependent upon no other.", "그리고 내가 명예를 입에 올릴 때 내가 뜻하는 것은, 이 종교와 어긋나지 않을 뿐 아니라 이 종교에 의지하는, 또 다른 어떤 종교와도 양립하지 않고 의지하지도 않는 그런 신성한 은총의 양식이오."], ["Now to say that the honour I here mean, and which was, I thought, all the honour I could be supposed to mean, will uphold, much less dictate an untruth, is to assert an absurdity too shocking to be conceived.”", "그러니 내가 여기서 뜻하는 명예, 또 내가 뜻할 법한 명예라곤 그것뿐이라 여겼던 그 명예가 거짓을 떠받친다, 하물며 거짓을 명령한다고 말하는 것은, 차마 떠올리기조차 끔찍한 터무니없는 소리를 내뱉는 것이오.\""]], [["“I purposely avoided,”", "\"나는 일부러,\""], ["says Square, “drawing a conclusion which I thought evident from what I have said; but if you perceived it, I am sure you have not attempted to answer it.", "스퀘어가 말했다, \"내가 한 말에서 분명히 끌어낼 수 있다고 여긴 결론을 끄집어내지 않았소. 그런데 그대가 그것을 알아챘다면, 분명 그대는 거기에 답하려 들지 못했을 거요."], ["However, to drop the article of religion, I think it is plain, from what you have said, that we have different ideas of honour; or why do we not agree in the same terms of its explanation?", "어쨌든 종교라는 항목은 접어 두고 말하자면, 그대가 한 말로 미루어 우리가 명예에 대해 서로 다른 관념을 지녔다는 것이 명백한 듯하오. 그렇지 않다면 어찌 우리가 그 풀이의 똑같은 말에서 의견이 갈리겠소?"], ["I have asserted, that true honour and true virtue are almost synonymous terms, and they are both founded on the unalterable rule of right, and the eternal fitness of things; to which an untruth being absolutely repugnant and contrary, it is certain that true honour cannot support an untruth.", "나는 참된 명예와 참된 덕이 거의 같은 뜻의 말이며, 둘 다 변치 않는 옳음의 규칙과 사물의 영원한 합당함 위에 세워져 있다고 주장했소. 거짓은 그것과 절대로 어긋나고 반대되는 것이니, 참된 명예가 거짓을 떠받칠 수 없다는 것은 분명하오."], ["In this, therefore, I think we are agreed; but that this honour can be said to be founded on religion, to which it is antecedent, if by religion be meant any positive law--”", "그러므로 이 점에서는 우리가 의견이 같다고 생각하오. 다만 이 명예가 종교 위에 세워져 있다고 말할 수는 있다 한들, 명예란 종교보다 앞서는 것이니, 만약 종교라는 말로 어떤 실정법을 뜻하는 것이라면――\""]], [["“I agree,”", "\"내가 동의할 줄 아시오,\""], ["answered Thwackum, with great warmth, “with a man who asserts honour to be antecedent to religion!", "스왜컴이 잔뜩 열을 올리며 대답했다, \"명예가 종교보다 앞선다고 주장하는 사람에게 말이오!"], ["Mr Allworthy, did I agree--?”", "올워디 씨, 제가 동의했습니까――?\""]], [["He was proceeding when Mr Allworthy interposed, telling them very coldly, they had both mistaken his meaning; for that he had said nothing of true honour.--It is possible, however, he would not have easily quieted the disputants, who were growing equally warm, had not another matter now fallen out, which put a final end to the conversation at present.", "그가 말을 이어 가려는데 올워디 씨가 끼어들어, 두 사람 다 자기 말뜻을 잘못 알아들었다고 아주 차갑게 일렀다. 자기는 참된 명예에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다는 것이었다. 그러나 두 논쟁자가 똑같이 달아오르고 있던 터라, 이제 또 다른 일이 벌어져 이 대화에 당장 종지부를 찍지 않았더라면, 올워디 씨도 그들을 쉽사리 가라앉히지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["Containing a necessary apology for the author; and a childish incident, which perhaps requires an apology likewise.", "4장. 작가를 위한 부득이한 변명, 그리고 어쩌면 그것대로 변명이 필요할 어린아이다운 사건을 담은 장."]], [["Before I proceed farther, I shall beg leave to obviate some misconstructions into which the zeal of some few readers may lead them; for I would not willingly give offence to any, especially to men who are warm in the cause of virtue or religion.", "더 나아가기에 앞서, 몇몇 독자의 열정이 그들을 빠뜨릴지도 모를 오해를 미리 바로잡아 두고자 한다. 나는 어느 누구에게도, 특히 덕이나 종교의 대의에 뜨겁게 헌신하는 이들에게는 결코 기꺼이 거슬리고 싶지 않기 때문이다."]], [["I hope, therefore, no man will, by the grossest misunderstanding or perversion of my meaning, misrepresent me, as endeavouring to cast any ridicule on the greatest perfections of human nature; and which do, indeed, alone purify and ennoble the heart of man, and raise him above the brute creation.", "그러므로 바라건대, 어떤 사람도 내 뜻을 더없이 거칠게 곡해하거나 비틀어, 내가 인간 본성의 가장 위대한 완성—실로 인간의 마음을 정화하고 고귀하게 만들며, 인간을 짐승의 무리 위로 끌어올리는 그 유일한 것—을 조롱하려 든다고 그릇되이 전하지 말기를 바란다."], ["This, reader, I will venture to say (and by how much the better man you are yourself, by so much the more will you be inclined to believe me), that I would rather have buried the sentiments of these two persons in eternal oblivion, than have done any injury to either of these glorious causes.", "독자여, 나는 감히 이렇게 말하겠다(그리고 그대 자신이 더 훌륭한 사람일수록 그만큼 더 내 말을 믿으려 하리라). 나는 차라리 이 두 인물의 생각을 영원한 망각 속에 묻어 버릴지언정, 이 영광스러운 두 대의 가운데 어느 하나에도 해를 끼치고 싶지는 않았다."]], [["On the contrary, it is with a view to their service, that I have taken upon me to record the lives and actions of two of their false and pretended champions. A treacherous friend is the most dangerous enemy; and I will say boldly, that both religion and virtue have received more real discredit from hypocrites than the wittiest profligates or infidels could ever cast upon them: nay, farther, as these two, in their purity, are rightly called the bands of civil society, and are indeed the greatest of blessings; so when poisoned and corrupted with fraud, pretence, and affectation, they have become the worst of civil curses, and have enabled men to perpetrate the most cruel mischiefs to their own species.", "오히려 나는 그 대의에 봉사할 뜻으로, 그것을 거짓되이 자처하며 위선적으로 옹호하는 두 사람의 삶과 행적을 기록하는 일을 떠맡은 것이다. 배신하는 벗이야말로 가장 위험한 적이다. 그리고 나는 대담하게 말하겠다. 종교와 덕은 가장 재치 있는 난봉꾼이나 불신자가 던질 수 있는 그 어떤 것보다도, 위선자들로부터 더 큰 실질적 불명예를 입어 왔다고. 아니, 한 걸음 더 나아가, 이 둘은 순수한 상태에서는 마땅히 시민 사회의 끈이라 불리며 실로 가장 큰 축복이지만, 기만과 가식과 허세로 오염되고 부패할 때에는 시민 사회의 가장 나쁜 저주가 되어, 인간으로 하여금 제 동족에게 가장 잔혹한 해악을 저지르게 만들었다."]], [["Indeed, I doubt not but this ridicule will in general be allowed: my chief apprehension is, as many true and just sentiments often came from the mouths of these persons, lest the whole should be taken together, and I should be conceived to ridicule all alike.", "실로 나는 이 조롱이 대체로 받아들여지리라는 것을 의심하지 않는다. 다만 내가 가장 염려하는 바는, 이 인물들의 입에서 참되고 올바른 생각이 적잖이 흘러나왔기에, 그 전체가 한데 뭉뚱그려져 내가 그 모두를 똑같이 조롱한다고 여겨지지나 않을까 하는 점이다."], ["Now the reader will be pleased to consider, that, as neither of these men were fools, they could not be supposed to have holden none but wrong principles, and to have uttered nothing but absurdities; what injustice, therefore, must I have done to their characters, had I selected only what was bad!", "그러니 독자는 부디 이렇게 헤아려 주기 바란다. 이 두 사람 중 어느 쪽도 바보는 아니었으니, 그릇된 원칙만을 품고 터무니없는 말만 늘어놓았다고 볼 수는 없다."], ["And how horribly wretched and maimed must their arguments have appeared!", "그렇다면 만일 내가 그들에게서 나쁜 것만을 골라냈다면, 그들의 인물 됨됨이에 얼마나 큰 부당함을 저질렀겠는가! 또 그들의 논변이 얼마나 끔찍하게 초라하고 불구처럼 보였겠는가!"]], [["Upon the whole, it is not religion or virtue, but the want of them, which is here exposed.", "요컨대 여기서 드러나는 것은 종교나 덕이 아니라, 그것의 결핍이다."], ["Had not Thwackum too much neglected virtue, and Square, religion, in the composition of their several systems, and had not both utterly discarded all natural goodness of heart, they had never been represented as the objects of derision in this history; in which we will now proceed.", "스왝컴이 제 나름의 체계를 꾸리면서 덕을 지나치게 등한시하지 않았더라면, 또 스퀘어가 종교를 그리하지 않았더라면, 그리고 두 사람이 마음의 모든 천성적인 선함을 송두리째 내버리지 않았더라면, 그들은 이 이야기 속에서 결코 조롱의 대상으로 그려지지 않았을 것이다. 이제 우리는 그 이야기를 이어 가 보자."]], [["This matter then, which put an end to the debate mentioned in the last chapter, was no other than a quarrel between Master Blifil and Tom Jones, the consequence of which had been a bloody nose to the former; for though Master Blifil, notwithstanding he was the younger, was in size above the other's match, yet Tom was much his superior at the noble art of boxing.", "앞 장에서 말한 논쟁을 끝맺게 한 그 일이란, 다름 아닌 블리필 도련님과 톰 존스 사이의 다툼이었다. 그 결과로 전자는 코피를 흘리게 되었다. 블리필 도련님은 비록 나이는 더 어렸지만 몸집은 톰을 웃돌았는데도, 권투라는 고귀한 기술에서는 톰이 그보다 훨씬 뛰어났기 때문이다."]], [["Tom, however, cautiously avoided all engagements with that youth; for besides that Tommy Jones was an inoffensive lad amidst all his roguery, and really loved Blifil, Mr Thwackum being always the second of the latter, would have been sufficient to deter him.", "그러나 톰은 그 소년과의 모든 충돌을 조심스레 피해 왔다. 토미 존스는 온갖 장난기에도 불구하고 남에게 해를 끼치지 않는 아이였으며 진심으로 블리필을 좋아했던 데다, 스왝컴 선생이 늘 블리필 편에 서는 후원자였으니 그것만으로도 그를 단념시키기에 충분했던 것이다."]], [["But well says a certain author, No man is wise at all hours; it is therefore no wonder that a boy is not so.", "하지만 어느 작가가 잘 말했듯, 어느 누구도 매 순간 현명할 수는 없다. 그러니 한 소년이 그렇지 못하다 한들 이상할 것이 없다."], ["A difference arising at play between the two lads, Master Blifil called Tom a beggarly bastard.", "놀이를 하던 중 두 소년 사이에 다툼이 일어나자, 블리필 도련님이 톰을 거지 같은 사생아라고 불렀다."], ["Upon which the latter, who was somewhat passionate in his disposition, immediately caused that phenomenon in the face of the former, which we have above remembered.", "그러자 다소 격한 기질을 지닌 톰은 곧바로, 앞서 우리가 떠올린 그 현상을 전자의 얼굴에 만들어 냈다."]], [["Master Blifil now, with his blood running from his nose, and the tears galloping after from his eyes, appeared before his uncle and the tremendous Thwackum.", "이제 블리필 도련님은 코에서 피를 흘리고 눈에서는 그 뒤를 쫓듯 눈물이 줄달음치는 모습으로, 자신의 외삼촌과 무시무시한 스왝컴 앞에 나타났다."], ["In which court an indictment of assault, battery, and wounding, was instantly preferred against Tom; who in his excuse only pleaded the provocation, which was indeed all the matter that Master Blifil had omitted.", "이 법정에서 톰을 상대로 폭행과 구타, 상해의 죄목이 즉각 제기되었다. 톰은 변명으로 다만 도발이 있었다는 점만을 내세웠는데, 그것이야말로 블리필 도련님이 쏙 빼놓은 사정이었다."]], [["It is indeed possible that this circumstance might have escaped his memory; for, in his reply, he positively insisted, that he had made use of no such appellation; adding, “Heaven forbid such naughty words should ever come out of his mouth!”", "실로 이 사정이 그의 기억에서 빠져나갔을 수도 있다. 답변에서 그는 자신이 그런 호칭을 쓴 적이 결코 없다고 단호히 우기며 이렇게 덧붙였으니 말이다. \"그런 못된 말이 제 입에서 나오다니, 하늘이 막아 주시기를!\""]], [["Tom, though against all form of law, rejoined in affirmance of the words.", "톰은, 비록 모든 법도에 어긋나는 일이었지만, 그 말이 있었다고 거듭 주장했다."], ["Upon which Master Blifil said, “It is no wonder.", "그러자 블리필 도련님이 말했다. \"당연하지."], ["Those who will tell one fib, will hardly stick at another.", "거짓말 하나를 하는 사람은 또 다른 거짓말도 거리낌 없이 하는 법이니까."], ["If I had told my master such a wicked fib as you have done, I should be ashamed to show my face.”", "만약 내가 너처럼 그런 못된 거짓말을 선생님께 했다면, 나는 부끄러워서 얼굴도 못 들 거야.\""]], [["“What fib, child?”", "\"무슨 거짓말 말이냐, 얘야?\""], ["cries Thwackum pretty eagerly.", "스왝컴이 꽤나 다급하게 소리쳤다."]], [["“Why, he told you that nobody was with him a shooting when he killed the partridge; but he knows”", "\"저, 쟤가 자고새를 잡을 때 사냥에 아무도 같이 가지 않았다고 선생님께 말했잖아요. 그런데 쟤는 알아요.\""], ["(here he burst into a flood of tears), “yes, he knows, for he confessed it to me, that Black George the gamekeeper was there.", "(여기서 그는 왈칵 눈물을 쏟았다.) \"네, 쟤는 알아요. 저한테 직접 털어놓았으니까요."], ["Nay, he said--yes you did--deny it if you can, that you would not have confest the truth, though master had cut you to pieces.”", "사냥터지기 검둥이 조지가 거기 있었다는 걸요. 아니, 쟤가 그랬어요—맞아, 너 그랬잖아—할 수 있으면 부인해 봐, 선생님이 너를 갈가리 찢어 놓는다 해도 진실을 털어놓지 않았을 거라고 말이야.\""]], [["At this the fire flashed from Thwackum's eyes, and he cried out in triumph--“Oh!", "이 말에 스왝컴의 눈에서 불꽃이 번뜩였고, 그는 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"오!"], ["ho!", "호!"], ["this is your mistaken notion of honour!", "이게 바로 네가 떠받드는 그 잘못된 명예라는 거지!"], ["This is the boy who was not to be whipped again!”", "이놈이 바로 다시는 매를 맞아선 안 된다던 그 녀석이야!\""], ["But Mr Allworthy, with a more gentle aspect, turned towards the lad, and said, “Is this true, child?", "그러나 올워디 씨는 좀 더 부드러운 낯빛으로 소년을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"이게 사실이냐, 얘야?"], ["How came you to persist so obstinately in a falsehood?”", "어쩌다 그렇게 고집스레 거짓을 우기게 되었느냐?\""]], [["Tom said, “He scorned a lie as much as any one: but he thought his honour engaged him to act as he did; for he had promised the poor fellow to conceal him: which,”", "톰이 말했다. 자기도 누구 못지않게 거짓말을 경멸하지만, 자신의 명예가 그리하도록 자신을 묶었다고 여겼다는 것이었다."], ["he said, “he thought himself farther obliged to, as the gamekeeper had begged him not to go into the gentleman's manor, and had at last gone himself, in compliance with his persuasions.”", "그 가엾은 사람을 숨겨 주겠다고 약속했기 때문이라 했다. 그리고 사냥터지기가 신사 양반의 영지로는 들어가지 말라고 자신에게 사정했는데도 끝내 자신의 설득에 따라 함께 가 준 처지였으니, 그 약속을 더더욱 지켜야 한다고 생각했노라고 했다."], ["He said, “This was the whole truth of the matter, and he would take his oath of it;”", "그는 \"이것이 이 일의 진실 전부이며, 자기는 그것을 두고 맹세라도 하겠다\"고 말했다. 그러고는 올워디 씨에게 \"그 가엾은 사람의 가족을 불쌍히 여겨 달라\"고 몹시 간절히 빌며 끝맺었다."], ["and concluded with very passionately begging Mr Allworthy “to have compassion on the poor fellow's family, especially as he himself only had been guilty, and the other had been very difficultly prevailed on to do what he did.", "더욱이 죄는 오직 자기 혼자 지었을 뿐이고, 그 사람은 한 일조차 몹시 힘겹게 설득당해 한 것이라 했다. \"정말입니다, 어르신.\""], ["Indeed, sir,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “it could hardly be called a lie that I told; for the poor fellow was entirely innocent of the whole matter.", "\"제가 한 말은 거짓말이라고 하기도 어렵습니다. 그 가엾은 사람은 이 일 전체에 아무 죄도 없으니까요."], ["I should have gone alone after the birds; nay, I did go at first, and he only followed me to prevent more mischief.", "새를 쫓아간 건 저 혼자였어야 했고, 아니, 처음엔 저 혼자 갔고, 그는 그저 더 큰 사달이 나지 않게 하려고 저를 따라온 것뿐입니다. 부디, 어르신, 저를 벌해 주십시오."], ["Do, pray, sir, let me be punished; take my little horse away again; but pray, sir, forgive poor George.”", "제 작은 말을 다시 빼앗아 가셔도 좋습니다. 하지만 제발, 어르신, 가엾은 조지만은 용서해 주십시오.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy hesitated a few moments, and then dismissed the boys, advising them to live more friendly and peaceably together.", "올워디 씨는 잠시 머뭇거리다가 두 소년을 물러가게 하며, 앞으로는 좀 더 사이좋고 평화롭게 지내라고 타일렀다."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["The opinions of the divine and the philosopher concerning the two boys; with some reasons for their opinions, and other matters.", "두 소년에 관한 신학자와 철학자의 견해, 그리고 그 견해의 몇 가지 근거와 그 밖의 일들."]], [["It is probable, that by disclosing this secret, which had been communicated in the utmost confidence to him, young Blifil preserved his companion from a good lashing; for the offence of the bloody nose would have been of itself sufficient cause for Thwackum to have proceeded to correction; but now this was totally absorbed in the consideration of the other matter; and with regard to this, Mr Allworthy declared privately, he thought the boy deserved reward rather than punishment, so that Thwackum's hand was withheld by a general pardon.", "어린 블리필이 이 비밀을 털어놓은 덕분에—더없이 굳은 신뢰 속에 자기에게 전해진 비밀이었지만—제 동무는 흠씬 매 맞는 일을 면했을 가능성이 크다. 코피를 터뜨린 잘못만으로도 스웨컴이 매질에 나설 충분한 빌미가 되었겠지만, 이제 그 잘못은 다른 사안을 헤아리는 가운데 완전히 묻혀 버렸기 때문이다. 그리고 이 사안에 관해 올워디 씨는, 그 아이가 벌이 아니라 오히려 상을 받아야 마땅하다고 생각한다는 뜻을 사사로이 밝혔다. 그리하여 스웨컴의 손은 전반적인 사면으로 묶이고 말았다."]], [["Thwackum, whose meditations were full of birch, exclaimed against this weak, and, as he said he would venture to call it, wicked lenity.", "머릿속이 자작나무 회초리 생각으로 가득했던 스웨컴은, 이런 나약한—그리고 그가 감히 그렇게 부르겠노라 말했듯이—사악한 관대함을 큰 소리로 성토했다."], ["To remit the punishment of such crimes was, he said, to encourage them.", "그런 죄에 대한 벌을 면제해 주는 것은 그 죄를 부추기는 짓이라고 그는 말했다."], ["He enlarged much on the correction of children, and quoted many texts from Solomon, and others; which being to be found in so many other books, shall not be found here.", "그는 아이들을 매로 다스리는 일에 대해 장황하게 늘어놓았고, 솔로몬을 비롯한 여러 사람의 글귀를 숱하게 인용했는데, 그것들은 다른 책에 워낙 많이 실려 있으니 여기서는 굳이 옮기지 않겠다."], ["He then applied himself to the vice of lying, on which head he was altogether as learned as he had been on the other.", "그러고 나서 그는 거짓말이라는 악덕으로 화제를 옮겼는데, 이 대목에서도 앞서의 주제 못지않게 박식했다."]], [["Square said, he had been endeavouring to reconcile the behaviour of Tom with his idea of perfect virtue, but could not.", "스퀘어는, 톰의 행동을 자기가 품은 완전한 덕의 관념과 어떻게든 조화시켜 보려 애썼으나 끝내 그럴 수 없었다고 말했다."], ["He owned there was something which at first sight appeared like fortitude in the action; but as fortitude was a virtue, and falsehood a vice, they could by no means agree or unite together.", "그는 그 행위에 언뜻 보기에 굳건한 용기 같은 무언가가 있음을 인정했다. 그러나 용기는 덕이요 거짓은 악덕이니, 둘은 결코 어울리거나 하나로 합쳐질 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["He added, that as this was in some measure to confound virtue and vice, it might be worth Mr Thwackum's consideration, whether a larger castigation might not be laid on upon the account.", "그는 덧붙이기를, 이 일이 어느 정도는 덕과 악덕을 뒤섞어 놓는 셈이므로, 그 점을 고려해 더 큰 매질을 가하는 편이 낫지 않을지 스웨컴 씨가 한번 헤아려 볼 만하다고 했다."]], [["As both these learned men concurred in censuring Jones, so were they no less unanimous in applauding Master Blifil.", "이 두 학식 있는 사람이 존스를 나무라는 데 한뜻이었던 만큼, 블리필 도련님을 칭찬하는 데에서도 그에 못지않게 일치를 보였다."], ["To bring truth to light, was by the parson asserted to be the duty of every religious man; and by the philosopher this was declared to be highly conformable with the rule of right, and the eternal and unalterable fitness of things.", "진실을 환히 드러내는 일이야말로 신앙심 있는 모든 사람의 의무라고 목사는 단언했고, 철학자는 이것이 올바름의 규칙, 그리고 사물의 영원하고 변치 않는 합당함에 더없이 부합한다고 천명했다."]], [["All this, however, weighed very little with Mr Allworthy.", "그러나 이 모든 말도 올워디 씨에게는 거의 무게가 없었다."], ["He could not be prevailed on to sign the warrant for the execution of Jones.", "그는 끝내 존스를 벌하라는 명령서에 서명하도록 설득당하지 않았다."], ["There was something within his own breast with which the invincible fidelity which that youth had preserved, corresponded much better than it had done with the religion of Thwackum, or with the virtue of Square.", "그의 가슴속에는, 그 소년이 지켜 낸 그 꺾이지 않는 의리와 훨씬 잘 들어맞는 무언가가 있었으니, 그것은 스웨컴의 신앙이나 스퀘어의 덕과 들어맞던 것보다 한결 잘 어울렸다."], ["He therefore strictly ordered the former of these gentlemen to abstain from laying violent hands on Tom for what had past.", "그리하여 그는 앞의 신사에게, 지난 일을 두고 톰에게 손찌검을 하지 말라고 엄히 명했다."], ["The pedagogue was obliged to obey those orders; but not without great reluctance, and frequent mutterings that the boy would be certainly spoiled.", "그 훈장은 그 명령에 따를 수밖에 없었으나, 큰 마지못함이 없지 않았고, 저 아이는 분명 버릇이 잘못 들고 말 거라고 자주 투덜거렸다."]], [["Towards the gamekeeper the good man behaved with more severity.", "사냥터지기를 향해서는 이 선한 분도 한층 매섭게 굴었다."], ["He presently summoned that poor fellow before him, and after many bitter remonstrances, paid him his wages, and dismist him from his service; for Mr Allworthy rightly observed, that there was a great difference between being guilty of a falsehood to excuse yourself, and to excuse another.", "그는 곧장 그 가엾은 사내를 불러들여, 모진 말로 한참 나무란 끝에 품삯을 치러 주고는 일자리에서 내보냈다. 올워디 씨가 옳게 보았듯이, 자기 자신을 변명하려고 거짓을 저지르는 것과 남을 변명하려고 거짓을 저지르는 것 사이에는 큰 차이가 있기 때문이었다."], ["He likewise urged, as the principal motive to his inflexible severity against this man, that he had basely suffered Tom Jones to undergo so heavy a punishment for his sake, whereas he ought to have prevented it by making the discovery himself.", "그는 또한 이 사내에게 가한 굽힐 줄 모르는 엄벌의 주된 동기로, 그가 비열하게도 톰 존스로 하여금 자기 대신 그토록 무거운 벌을 받게 내버려 두었으며, 마땅히 스스로 진실을 밝혀 그것을 막았어야 했다는 점을 들었다."]], [["When this story became public, many people differed from Square and Thwackum, in judging the conduct of the two lads on the occasion.", "이 이야기가 널리 알려지자, 그 일에서 두 소년이 보인 행동을 두고 많은 사람이 스퀘어나 스웨컴과는 다른 판단을 내렸다. 블리필 도련님은 대체로 비실비실 알랑대는 놈이니, 옹졸하고 좀스러운 자식이니 하는, 그와 비슷한 말로 불렸다."], ["Master Blifil was generally called a sneaking rascal, a poor-spirited wretch, with other epithets of the like kind; whilst Tom was honoured with the appellations of a brave lad, a jolly dog, and an honest fellow.", "반면 톰은 용감한 아이, 호탕한 녀석, 정직한 사내라는 칭호로 떠받들어졌다. 실로 블랙 조지를 대한 그의 행동은 하인들 모두의 호감을 크게 샀다."], ["Indeed, his behaviour to Black George much ingratiated him with all the servants; for though that fellow was before universally disliked, yet he was no sooner turned away than he was as universally pitied; and the friendship and gallantry of Tom Jones was celebrated by them all with the highest applause; and they condemned Master Blifil as openly as they durst, without incurring the danger of offending his mother.", "그 사내는 전에는 누구에게나 미움받던 자였으나, 쫓겨나자마자 그만큼 누구에게나 동정을 받게 되었다. 그리고 톰 존스의 우정과 의협심은 그들 모두에게서 더없이 높은 갈채로 기려졌으며, 그들은 블리필의 어머니의 심기를 거스를 위험을 무릅쓰지 않는 선에서, 감히 할 수 있는 한 드러내 놓고 블리필 도련님을 욕했다."], ["For all this, however, poor Tom smarted in the flesh; for though Thwackum had been inhibited to exercise his arm on the foregoing account, yet, as the proverb says, It is easy to find a stick, &c.", "그러나 이 모든 일에도 불구하고, 가엾은 톰은 살갗에 매를 맞아야 했다. 스웨컴이 앞서의 일을 두고는 팔을 휘두르는 일을 금지당했지만, 속담에도 있듯, 매를 들 핑계는 찾기 쉬운 법이니, 회초리 또한 찾기 쉬웠다."], ["So was it easy to find a rod; and, indeed, the not being able to find one was the only thing which could have kept Thwackum any long time from chastising poor Jones.", "사실 회초리를 찾지 못하는 것만이 스웨컴으로 하여금 가엾은 존스를 오래도록 매질하지 못하게 막을 유일한 길이었으리라."]], [["Had the bare delight in the sport been the only inducement to the pedagogue, it is probable Master Blifil would likewise have had his share; but though Mr Allworthy had given him frequent orders to make no difference between the lads, yet was Thwackum altogether as kind and gentle to this youth, as he was harsh, nay even barbarous, to the other. To say the truth, Blifil had greatly gained his master's affections; partly by the profound respect he always showed his person, but much more by the decent reverence with which he received his doctrine; for he had got by heart, and frequently repeated, his phrases, and maintained all his master's religious principles with a zeal which was surprizing in one so young, and which greatly endeared him to the worthy preceptor.", "만일 이 훈장을 부추긴 것이 오로지 매질의 즐거움뿐이었다면, 블리필 도련님 역시 제 몫을 받았을 가능성이 크다. 그러나 올워디 씨가 두 아이를 차별하지 말라고 거듭 명했음에도, 스웨컴은 한 소년에게 그토록 매섭게, 아니 야만스럽기까지 했던 것만큼이나, 다른 소년에게는 다정하고 부드러웠다. 사실대로 말하자면, 블리필은 스승의 애정을 크게 사로잡았다. 한편으로는 늘 그 인품에 깊은 존경을 보인 까닭이었으나, 그보다 훨씬 더 큰 이유는 스승의 가르침을 받아들이는 그 단정한 공경심에 있었다. 그는 스승의 어구들을 외워 두었다가 자주 되뇌었고, 스승의 종교적 신조를 하나같이 옹호하기를 그토록 어린 나이에서는 놀라울 만큼 열성적으로 했으니, 그 점이 그 갸륵한 스승에게 그를 더없이 사랑스럽게 만들었다."]], [["Tom Jones, on the other hand, was not only deficient in outward tokens of respect, often forgetting to pull off his hat, or to bow at his master's approach; but was altogether as unmindful both of his master's precepts and example.", "반면 톰 존스는 겉으로 드러내는 존경의 표시에 모자랐을 뿐 아니라—스승이 다가올 때 모자를 벗거나 절을 하는 것을 잊기 일쑤였다—스승의 가르침이나 본보기 또한 도무지 마음에 두지 않았다."], ["He was indeed a thoughtless, giddy youth, with little sobriety in his manners, and less in his countenance; and would often very impudently and indecently laugh at his companion for his serious behaviour.", "그는 실로 생각 없고 들뜬 소년이라, 몸가짐에 진중함이 거의 없었고, 표정에는 그보다 더 없었다. 게다가 그 진지한 행실을 두고 제 동무를 아주 뻔뻔스럽고 무례하게 비웃곤 했다."]], [["Mr Square had the same reason for his preference of the former lad; for Tom Jones showed no more regard to the learned discourses which this gentleman would sometimes throw away upon him, than to those of Thwackum.", "스퀘어 씨도 앞의 소년을 더 아끼는 까닭은 같았다. 톰 존스는 이 신사가 이따금 허비하듯 그에게 들려주는 학식 있는 설교에도, 스웨컴의 설교에 보이던 것 이상의 관심을 두지 않았기 때문이다."], ["He once ventured to make a jest of the rule of right; and at another time said, he believed there was no rule in the world capable of making such a man as his father (for so Mr Allworthy suffered himself to be called).", "그는 한번은 감히 올바름의 규칙을 두고 농을 던졌고, 또 한번은 자기 아버지(올워디 씨가 그렇게 불리는 것을 허락했다) 같은 사람을 만들어 낼 수 있는 규칙이란 세상에 없을 거라고 말하기도 했다."]], [["Master Blifil, on the contrary, had address enough at sixteen to recommend himself at one and the same time to both these opposites.", "이와 달리 블리필 도련님은 열여섯 나이에 이미, 이 상반된 두 사람의 마음을 동시에 사로잡을 만한 수완을 갖추고 있었다."], ["With one he was all religion, with the other he was all virtue.", "한 사람에게는 온통 신앙으로, 다른 한 사람에게는 온통 덕으로 대했다."], ["And when both were present, he was profoundly silent, which both interpreted in his favour and in their own.", "그리고 두 사람이 함께 있을 때는 깊이 입을 다물었는데, 둘 다 그것을 자기에게 유리하게, 또한 제 편으로 해석했다."]], [["Nor was Blifil contented with flattering both these gentlemen to their faces; he took frequent occasions of praising them behind their backs to Allworthy; before whom, when they two were alone, and his uncle commended any religious or virtuous sentiment (for many such came constantly from him) he seldom failed to ascribe it to the good instructions he had received from either Thwackum or Square; for he knew his uncle repeated all such compliments to the persons for whose use they were meant; and he found by experience the great impressions which they made on the philosopher, as well as on the divine: for, to say the truth, there is no kind of flattery so irresistible as this, at second hand.", "블리필은 이 두 신사를 면전에서 추켜세우는 것으로 만족하지 않았다. 그는 등 뒤에서도 올워디에게 그들을 칭찬할 기회를 자주 만들었다. 외삼촌과 단둘이 있을 때, 외삼촌이 어떤 종교적이거나 덕스러운 생각을 칭찬하면(그런 말이 그에게서 끊임없이 나왔다), 그는 그것을 스웨컴이나 스퀘어에게서 받은 좋은 가르침 덕으로 돌리는 일을 거의 빠뜨리지 않았다. 외삼촌이 그런 모든 칭찬을 그 말이 가리키는 당사자들에게 그대로 옮긴다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 그것이 신학자에게나 철학자에게나 얼마나 큰 인상을 남기는지를 경험으로 알아냈다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이렇게 한 다리 건너 전해지는 아첨만큼 뿌리치기 어려운 아첨은 없으니 말이다."]], [["The young gentleman, moreover, soon perceived how extremely grateful all those panegyrics on his instructors were to Mr Allworthy himself, as they so loudly resounded the praise of that singular plan of education which he had laid down; for this worthy man having observed the imperfect institution of our public schools, and the many vices which boys were there liable to learn, had resolved to educate his nephew, as well as the other lad, whom he had in a manner adopted, in his own house; where he thought their morals would escape all that danger of being corrupted to which they would be unavoidably exposed in any public school or university.", "게다가 이 젊은 신사는, 자기 스승들을 향한 그 모든 찬사가 올워디 씨 자신에게 얼마나 지극히 흐뭇한 일인지를 곧 알아차렸다. 그것이 그가 세워 둔 그 독특한 교육 계획에 대한 칭찬을 그토록 우렁차게 울려 주었기 때문이다. 이 갸륵한 분은 우리네 공립학교의 불완전한 제도와, 그곳에서 소년들이 익히기 쉬운 숱한 악덕을 눈여겨본 끝에, 자기 조카와, 어느 모로는 양자처럼 거둔 다른 소년을 모두 자기 집에서 가르치기로 결심했었다. 그곳이라면 어느 공립학교나 대학에서든 피할 수 없이 노출될 그 타락의 위험에서 두 아이의 품행이 모두 벗어나리라 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Having, therefore, determined to commit these boys to the tuition of a private tutor, Mr Thwackum was recommended to him for that office, by a very particular friend, of whose understanding Mr Allworthy had a great opinion, and in whose integrity he placed much confidence.", "그리하여 이 소년들을 사사로운 가정교사의 가르침에 맡기기로 정했을 때, 스웨컴 씨가 그 자리에 천거되었다. 올워디 씨가 그 식견을 매우 높이 사고 그 성실함을 크게 신뢰하던 어느 각별한 벗이 추천한 것이었다."], ["This Thwackum was fellow of a college, where he almost entirely resided; and had a great reputation for learning, religion, and sobriety of manners.", "이 스웨컴은 어느 대학의 특별연구원으로, 거의 내내 그곳에 머물렀으며, 학식과 신앙과 단정한 몸가짐으로 큰 명성을 누리고 있었다."], ["And these were doubtless the qualifications by which Mr Allworthy's friend had been induced to recommend him; though indeed this friend had some obligations to Thwackum's family, who were the most considerable persons in a borough which that gentleman represented in parliament.", "올워디 씨의 벗이 그를 추천하게 된 것도 분명 이런 자질들 때문이었으리라. 다만 실은 이 벗이 스웨컴 집안에 어느 정도 신세를 진 바가 있었으니, 그 집안은 이 신사가 의회에서 대표하던 어느 자치구에서 가장 유력한 인사들이었다."]], [["Thwackum, at his first arrival, was extremely agreeable to Allworthy; and indeed he perfectly answered the character which had been given of him. Upon longer acquaintance, however, and more intimate conversation, this worthy man saw infirmities in the tutor, which he could have wished him to have been without; though as those seemed greatly overbalanced by his good qualities, they did not incline Mr Allworthy to part with him: nor would they indeed have justified such a proceeding; for the reader is greatly mistaken, if he conceives that Thwackum appeared to Mr Allworthy in the same light as he doth to him in this history; and he is as much deceived, if he imagines that the most intimate acquaintance which he himself could have had with that divine, would have informed him of those things which we, from our inspiration, are enabled to open and discover. Of readers who, from such conceits as these, condemn the wisdom or penetration of Mr Allworthy, I shall not scruple to say, that they make a very bad and ungrateful use of that knowledge which we have communicated to them.", "스웨컴은 처음 도착했을 때 올워디에게 더없이 흡족했고, 실로 그에게 주어졌던 평판에 완벽하게 들어맞았다. 그러나 더 오래 사귀고 더 가까이 이야기를 나누면서, 이 갸륵한 분은 그 가정교사에게서 없었으면 싶은 결점들을 보게 되었다. 다만 그 결점들이 그의 장점들에 크게 가려지는 듯했으므로, 올워디 씨로서는 그를 내보낼 마음이 들지 않았다. 사실 그런 처사가 정당화되지도 않았을 것이다. 만일 독자가, 이 이야기 속에서 스웨컴이 독자에게 비치는 것과 똑같은 모습으로 올워디 씨에게도 비쳤으리라 여긴다면, 그것은 크나큰 오해다. 또 만일 독자가, 자신이 그 신학자와 아무리 가깝게 사귀었던들 우리가 영감을 통해 능히 드러내고 밝혀낼 수 있는 그런 것들까지 알게 되었으리라 상상한다면, 그것도 똑같이 잘못된 생각이다. 이런 식의 억측에서 올워디 씨의 지혜나 통찰을 나무라는 독자들을 두고, 나는 서슴없이 이렇게 말하겠다. 그들은 우리가 그들에게 전해 준 그 지식을 아주 나쁘고 배은망덕하게 쓰고 있는 것이라고."]], [["These apparent errors in the doctrine of Thwackum served greatly to palliate the contrary errors in that of Square, which our good man no less saw and condemned.", "스웨컴의 교리에 드러난 이런 결함들은, 우리의 선한 분이 못지않게 보고 또 나무랐던 스퀘어의 교리 속 상반된 결함들을 크게 덜어 주는 구실을 했다."], ["He thought, indeed, that the different exuberancies of these gentlemen would correct their different imperfections; and that from both, especially with his assistance, the two lads would derive sufficient precepts of true religion and virtue.", "그는 실로, 이 두 신사의 서로 다른 지나침이 그들의 서로 다른 불완전함을 바로잡아 주리라 생각했고, 또한 두 사람 모두에게서, 특히 자신의 도움이 더해진다면, 두 소년이 참된 신앙과 덕의 가르침을 넉넉히 얻어 가리라 여겼다. 만일 그 결과가 그의 기대와 어긋나게 나타났다면, 그것은 어쩌면 그 계획 자체에 어떤 흠이 있었던 탓일 것이다."], ["If the event happened contrary to his expectations, this possibly proceeded from some fault in the plan itself; which the reader hath my leave to discover, if he can: for we do not pretend to introduce any infallible characters into this history; where we hope nothing will be found which hath never yet been seen in human nature.", "그 흠이 무엇인지는, 독자가 찾아낼 수 있다면 찾아내도 좋다. 우리는 이 이야기에 어떤 흠 없는 인물을 들여놓는 척하지 않으니, 여기에서는 일찍이 인간 본성에서 보인 적 없는 것이 하나도 발견되지 않기를 바라는 바이다."]], [["To return therefore: the reader will not, I think, wonder that the different behaviour of the two lads above commemorated, produced the different effects of which he hath already seen some instance; and besides this, there was another reason for the conduct of the philosopher and the pedagogue; but this being matter of great importance, we shall reveal it in the next chapter.", "그러니 본론으로 돌아가자. 앞서 적은 두 소년의 서로 다른 행동이, 독자가 이미 그 한 사례를 본 그 서로 다른 결과를 낳았다 한들, 독자는 그리 놀라지 않으리라 생각한다. 그리고 이것 말고도, 철학자와 훈장의 행동에는 또 다른 까닭이 있었다. 그러나 이는 매우 중요한 사안이니, 다음 장에서 밝히기로 하겠다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["Containing a better reason still for the before-mentioned opinions.", "앞에서 다룬 그 두 학식 있는 인물이 이 이야기의 무대에 최근 제법 비중 있게 등장했다는 사실은 이미 알려진 바와 같다."], ["It is to be known then, that those two learned personages, who have lately made a considerable figure on the theatre of this history, had, from their first arrival at Mr Allworthy's house, taken so great an affection, the one to his virtue, the other to his religion, that they had meditated the closest alliance with him.", "그런데 그들은 올워디 씨의 집에 처음 도착한 순간부터, 한 사람은 그의 덕을, 다른 한 사람은 그의 신앙심을 어찌나 깊이 사모하게 되었던지, 그와 더없이 긴밀한 결속을 맺으리라 마음속으로 궁리해 왔다."]], [["For this purpose they had cast their eyes on that fair widow, whom, though we have not for some time made any mention of her, the reader, we trust, hath not forgot.", "이를 위해 그들은 어느 아름다운 미망인에게 눈길을 두었다. 한동안 그녀를 입에 올리지 않았으나, 독자께서는 부디 잊지 않으셨으리라 믿는다."], ["Mrs Blifil was indeed the object to which they both aspired.", "두 사람이 한결같이 흠모한 대상은 다름 아닌 블리필 부인이었다."]], [["It may seem remarkable, that, of four persons whom we have commemorated at Mr Allworthy's house, three of them should fix their inclinations on a lady who was never greatly celebrated for her beauty, and who was, moreover, now a little descended into the vale of years; but in reality bosom friends, and intimate acquaintance, have a kind of natural propensity to particular females at the house of a friend--viz., to his grandmother, mother, sister, daughter, aunt, niece, or cousin, when they are rich; and to his wife, sister, daughter, niece, cousin, mistress, or servant-maid, if they should be handsome.", "올워디 씨의 집에서 우리가 기록해 온 네 사람 가운데 셋이, 미모로 크게 이름을 떨친 적도 없거니와 이제는 나이의 골짜기로 조금 접어든 한 부인에게 마음을 두었다는 것은 어쩌면 눈여겨볼 만한 일로 보일 수도 있다. 하지만 실상 가슴 깊이 사귄 벗이나 가까운 지인이란, 친구의 집에 있는 특정한 여인들에게 일종의 자연스러운 끌림을 품기 마련이다. 곧 부유하다면 그 친구의 할머니, 어머니, 누이, 딸, 고모, 조카딸, 사촌에게 끌리고, 미모가 빼어나다면 그 친구의 아내, 누이, 딸, 조카딸, 사촌, 정부, 혹은 하녀에게 끌리는 법이다."]], [["We would not, however, have our reader imagine, that persons of such characters as were supported by Thwackum and Square, would undertake a matter of this kind, which hath been a little censured by some rigid moralists, before they had thoroughly examined it, and considered whether it was (as Shakespear phrases it) “Stuff o' th'", "그렇지만 우리는 독자께서, 스왁컴과 스퀘어 같은 인물이 어떤 엄격한 도덕가들에게 다소 비난을 사 온 이런 종류의 일을, 철저히 따져 보지도 않고 그것이 (셰익스피어의 말마따나) \"양심에 걸리는 것\"인지 아닌지 헤아려 보지도 않은 채 덥석 벌이리라고는 상상하지 않으시기를 바란다."], ["conscience,” or no.", "스왁컴은 이웃의 누이를 탐하는 것은 어디에서도 금지되어 있지 않다는 점을 곱씹으며 이 일에 용기를 얻었다."], ["Thwackum was encouraged to the undertaking by reflecting that to covet your neighbour's sister is nowhere forbidden: and he knew it was a rule in the construction of all laws, that “Expressum facit cessare tacitum.”", "그는 모든 법을 해석할 때 \"명시된 것은 침묵하는 것을 멈추게 한다(Expressum facit cessare tacitum)\"라는 원칙이 있음을 알고 있었다."], ["The sense of which is, “When a lawgiver sets down plainly his whole meaning, we are prevented from making him mean what we please ourselves.”", "그 뜻인즉, \"입법자가 자신의 뜻 전부를 분명히 적어 놓았을 때, 우리는 그가 우리 멋대로 바라는 바를 의미하게 만들 수 없다\"는 것이다."], ["As some instances of women, therefore, are mentioned in the divine law, which forbids us to covet our neighbour's goods, and that of a sister omitted, he concluded it to be lawful.", "이웃의 재물을 탐하지 말라 금하는 신의 율법에 몇몇 여인들이 거명되어 있으되 누이는 빠져 있으므로, 그는 그것이 적법하다고 결론지었다."], ["And as to Square, who was in his person what is called a jolly fellow, or a widow's man, he easily reconciled his choice to the eternal fitness of things.", "그리고 스퀘어로 말하자면, 그는 흔히 말하는 쾌활한 사내, 곧 미망인에게 어울리는 부류의 사람이었던 터라, 자신의 선택을 사물의 영원한 합당함과 어렵잖게 화해시켰다."]], [["Now, as both of these gentlemen were industrious in taking every opportunity of recommending themselves to the widow, they apprehended one certain method was, by giving her son the constant preference to the other lad; and as they conceived the kindness and affection which Mr Allworthy showed the latter, must be highly disagreeable to her, they doubted not but the laying hold on all occasions to degrade and vilify him, would be highly pleasing to her; who, as she hated the boy, must love all those who did him any hurt.", "그런데 이 두 신사는 미망인에게 자신을 좋게 보이게 할 기회라면 무엇이든 부지런히 붙잡았고, 그 확실한 방법 하나가 그녀의 아들을 다른 소년보다 늘 앞세워 주는 것이라 여겼다. 또한 올워디 씨가 그 다른 소년에게 보이는 친절과 애정이 그녀에게 몹시 거슬릴 것이라 짐작했으므로, 그 아이를 깎아내리고 욕보일 기회를 빠짐없이 붙잡는 것이야말로 그녀를 크게 흡족케 하리라 믿어 의심치 않았다."], ["In this Thwackum had the advantage; for while Square could only scarify the poor lad's reputation, he could flea his skin; and, indeed, he considered every lash he gave him as a compliment paid to his mistress; so that he could, with the utmost propriety, repeat this old flogging line, “Castigo te non quod odio habeam, sed quod AMEM.", "그 아이를 미워하는 그녀라면, 그에게 해를 끼치는 자라면 누구든 사랑할 수밖에 없을 테니 말이다. 이 점에서는 스왁컴이 유리했다."], ["I chastise thee not out of hatred, but out of love.”", "스퀘어가 가엾은 소년의 평판에 생채기를 낼 수 있을 뿐이라면, 그는 아이의 살가죽을 벗길 수 있었으니 말이다. 실로 그는 아이에게 매질 한 번을 할 때마다 그것을 자신의 연인에게 바치는 찬사로 여겼다."], ["And this, indeed, he often had in his mouth, or rather, according to the old phrase, never more properly applied, at his fingers'", "그리하여 그는 더없이 그럴듯하게 이 오래된 매질의 구절을 읊을 수 있었다. \"내가 너를 벌하는 것은 미움 때문이 아니라 사랑 때문이다(Castigo te non quod odio habeam, sed quod amem).\""], ["ends.", "실로 그는 이 말을 입에 달고 살았으니, 옛말을 빌리자면 이보다 더 적절히 쓰인 적이 없을 만큼 손끝에 익히고 있었다."]], [["For this reason, principally, the two gentlemen concurred, as we have seen above, in their opinion concerning the two lads; this being, indeed, almost the only instance of their concurring on any point; for, beside the difference of their principles, they had both long ago strongly suspected each other's design, and hated one another with no little degree of inveteracy.", "주로 이런 까닭에, 위에서 본 대로 두 신사는 두 소년에 관한 의견에서 한목소리를 냈다. 사실 이것은 그들이 어떤 점에서든 의견을 같이한 거의 유일한 경우였다. 원칙이 서로 다른 데다, 두 사람은 이미 오래전부터 서로의 속셈을 강하게 의심해 왔고, 적잖이 뿌리 깊은 증오로 서로를 미워하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["This mutual animosity was a good deal increased by their alternate successes; for Mrs Blifil knew what they would be at long before they imagined it; or, indeed, intended she should: for they proceeded with great caution, lest she should be offended, and acquaint Mr Allworthy.", "이 서로에 대한 적개심은 번갈아 거둔 성공으로 한층 부풀어 올랐다. 블리필 부인은 그들이 무엇을 노리는지를, 그들이 짐작하기 훨씬 전부터, 아니 그녀가 알아채기를 그들이 바라기도 훨씬 전부터 이미 꿰뚫고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But they had no reason for any such fear; she was well enough pleased with a passion, of which she intended none should have any fruits but herself.", "그들은 그녀의 비위를 거슬러 올워디 씨에게 일러바칠까 두려워 매사 무척 조심스럽게 움직였다. 하지만 그런 두려움을 품을 까닭은 없었다."], ["And the only fruits she designed for herself were, flattery and courtship; for which purpose she soothed them by turns, and a long time equally.", "그녀는 오직 자신만이 그 열매를 거두기로 작정한 그 연정을 자못 흡족해하고 있었던 것이다. 그녀가 스스로를 위해 작정한 열매란 다름 아닌 아첨과 구애였으니, 이를 위해 그녀는 두 사람을 번갈아, 그것도 오랫동안 똑같이 어르고 달랬다."], ["She was, indeed, rather inclined to favour the parson's principles; but Square's person was more agreeable to her eye, for he was a comely man; whereas the pedagogue did in countenance very nearly resemble that gentleman, who, in the Harlot's Progress, is seen correcting the ladies in Bridewell.", "실은 그녀는 목사의 원칙 쪽으로 다소 마음이 기울어 있었으나, 스퀘어의 외양이 그녀의 눈에는 더 흡족했다. 그는 풍채 좋은 사내였던 반면, 그 훈장의 얼굴은 〈창부 일대기〉에서 브라이드웰 감화원의 여인들을 매질하는 모습으로 그려진 그 신사와 거의 빼닮았기 때문이다."]], [["Whether Mrs Blifil had been surfeited with the sweets of marriage, or disgusted by its bitters, or from what other cause it proceeded, I will not determine; but she could never be brought to listen to any second proposals.", "블리필 부인이 결혼의 단맛에 물려 버린 것인지, 그 쓴맛에 신물이 난 것인지, 아니면 또 다른 무슨 까닭에서인지 나는 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 두 번째 청혼이라면 어느 것에도 끝내 귀를 기울이려 하지 않았다."], ["However, she at last conversed with Square with such a degree of intimacy that malicious tongues began to whisper things of her, to which, as well for the sake of the lady, as that they were highly disagreeable to the rule of right and the fitness of things, we will give no credit, and therefore shall not blot our paper with them.", "그렇지만 그녀는 마침내 스퀘어와 어찌나 친밀하게 어울렸던지, 짓궂은 입들이 그녀를 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 수군대기 시작했다. 하지만 그것은 부인을 위해서나, 올바름의 규칙과 사물의 합당함에 몹시 어긋나는 것이기에 우리는 믿지 않을 것이며, 따라서 그런 말로 우리의 지면을 더럽히지 않겠다."], ["The pedagogue, 'tis certain, whipped on, without getting a step nearer to his journey's end.", "다만 그 훈장이 여정의 끝에는 한 걸음도 다가서지 못한 채 매질만 계속했다는 것은 분명하다."]], [["Indeed he had committed a great error, and that Square discovered much sooner than himself.", "실로 그는 큰 실수를 저질렀고, 그것을 스퀘어는 그 자신보다 훨씬 일찍 알아챘다. 블리필 부인은 (어쩌면 독자께서 진작 짐작하셨을지 모르나) 남편의 처신을 그다지 마뜩잖아했다."], ["Mrs Blifil (as, perhaps, the reader may have formerly guessed) was not over and above pleased with the behaviour of her husband; nay, to be honest, she absolutely hated him, till his death at last a little reconciled him to her affections.", "아니, 솔직히 말하자면 그녀는 그를 철저히 미워했고, 끝내 그의 죽음에 이르러서야 그가 그녀의 애정과 조금이나마 화해할 수 있었다. 그러니 그녀가 그에게서 얻은 자식에게 그리 격렬한 애정을 두지 않았다 한들 크게 놀랄 일은 아니다."], ["It will not be therefore greatly wondered at, if she had not the most violent regard to the offspring she had by him.", "사실 그녀에게 그런 정은 어찌나 적었던지, 아이가 갓난아기였을 적에 그녀는 아들을 거의 들여다보지도, 아랑곳하지도 않았다. 그런 까닭에 그녀는 다소 마지못해하다가도, 올워디 씨가 그 업둥이에게 쏟아붓는 온갖 은혜를 잠자코 받아들였다."], ["And, in fact, she had so little of this regard, that in his infancy she seldom saw her son, or took any notice of him; and hence she acquiesced, after a little reluctance, in all the favours which Mr Allworthy showered on the foundling; whom the good man called his own boy, and in all things put on an entire equality with Master Blifil.", "선량한 그는 그 아이를 제 친아들이라 불렀고 매사에 블리필 도련님과 완전히 똑같이 대했다. 블리필 부인의 이런 묵인을 이웃들과 집안 사람들은 오라비의 기분을 맞춰 주려는 양보의 표시로 여겼고, 스왁컴과 스퀘어를 비롯한 다른 모든 이들도 그녀가 속으로는 그 업둥이를 미워한다고 짐작했다."], ["This acquiescence in Mrs Blifil was considered by the neighbours, and by the family, as a mark of her condescension to her brother's humour, and she was imagined by all others, as well as Thwackum and Square, to hate the foundling in her heart; nay, the more civility she showed him, the more they conceived she detested him, and the surer schemes she was laying for his ruin: for as they thought it her interest to hate him, it was very difficult for her to persuade them she did not.", "아니, 그녀가 그에게 예의를 차릴수록 사람들은 그녀가 그를 더더욱 혐오하며, 그의 파멸을 위해 더욱 확실한 계략을 꾸미고 있으리라 믿었다. 그를 미워하는 것이 그녀에게 이익이라 여겼던 터라, 그렇지 않다고 그들을 납득시키기란 그녀로서는 무척 어려운 일이었다."]], [["Thwackum was the more confirmed in his opinion, as she had more than once slily caused him to whip Tom Jones, when Mr Allworthy, who was an enemy to this exercise, was abroad; whereas she had never given any such orders concerning young Blifil.", "스왁컴은 자신의 생각을 한층 굳혔으니, 이 매질을 적대시하던 올워디 씨가 집을 비웠을 때 그녀가 한두 번이 아니게 슬그머니 톰 존스에게 매질을 하도록 그를 부추겼기 때문이다. 반면 그녀는 어린 블리필에 관해서는 그런 명령을 내린 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["And this had likewise imposed upon Square.", "그리고 이는 스퀘어마저 속아 넘어가게 했다."], ["In reality, though she certainly hated her own son--of which, however monstrous it appears, I am assured she is not a singular instance--she appeared, notwithstanding all her outward compliance, to be in her heart sufficiently displeased with all the favour shown by Mr Allworthy to the foundling.", "실상 그녀는 분명 제 친아들을 미워하기는 했으나 — 아무리 괴이쩍게 보일지라도, 그녀가 그런 유일무이한 본보기는 아니라고 나는 확신한다 — 겉으로 온갖 순응을 보이면서도 속으로는 올워디 씨가 업둥이에게 베푸는 온갖 호의를 충분히 못마땅해하는 듯했다."], ["She frequently complained of this behind her brother's back, and very sharply censured him for it, both to Thwackum and Square; nay, she would throw it in the teeth of Allworthy himself, when a little quarrel, or miff, as it is vulgarly called, arose between them.", "그녀는 오라비 등 뒤에서 이를 두고 자주 불평했고, 스왁컴과 스퀘어 앞에서 그를 몹시 날카롭게 나무랐다. 아니, 두 사람 사이에 자그마한 다툼, 흔히 말하는 토라짐이 생기면 올워디 본인의 면전에다 대고 그 말을 쏘아붙이기도 했다."]], [["However, when Tom grew up, and gave tokens of that gallantry of temper which greatly recommends men to women, this disinclination which she had discovered to him when a child, by degrees abated, and at last she so evidently demonstrated her affection to him to be much stronger than what she bore her own son, that it was impossible to mistake her any longer. She was so desirous of often seeing him, and discovered such satisfaction and delight in his company, that before he was eighteen years old he was become a rival to both Square and Thwackum; and what is worse, the whole country began to talk as loudly of her inclination to Tom, as they had before done of that which she had shown to Square: on which account the philosopher conceived the most implacable hatred for our poor heroe.", "그렇지만 톰이 자라나, 남자를 여자에게 크게 매력적으로 보이게 하는 그 호방한 기질의 낌새를 드러내자, 어릴 적 그에게 보였던 이 거부감은 차츰 누그러졌다. 그리하여 마침내 그녀는 제 친아들에게 품은 것보다 그에게 품은 애정이 훨씬 더 강하다는 것을 어찌나 뚜렷이 드러냈던지, 더는 그 마음을 오해할 수 없을 지경이 되었다. 그녀는 그를 자주 보고 싶어 안달했고, 그와 함께 있는 데서 어찌나 큰 만족과 기쁨을 드러냈던지, 그가 열여덟이 되기도 전에 그는 스퀘어와 스왁컴 두 사람 모두의 연적이 되어 있었다. 게다가 더 고약한 것은, 온 고장이 예전에 그녀가 스퀘어에게 보인 끌림을 두고 떠들어대던 만큼이나 이제는 톰을 향한 그녀의 끌림을 큰 소리로 입에 올리기 시작했다는 점이다. 이런 까닭에 그 철학자는 우리의 가엾은 주인공에게 도무지 풀 길 없는 증오를 품게 되었다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["In which the author himself makes his appearance on the stage.", "지은이가 손수 무대에 모습을 드러내는 장."]], [["Though Mr Allworthy was not of himself hasty to see things in a disadvantageous light, and was a stranger to the public voice, which seldom reaches to a brother or a husband, though it rings in the ears of all the neighbourhood; yet was this affection of Mrs Blifil to Tom, and the preference which she too visibly gave him to her own son, of the utmost disadvantage to that youth.", "올워디 씨는 본디 무슨 일이든 불리한 쪽으로 성급하게 보는 사람이 아니었고, 세상의 입소문—이웃 모두의 귀에는 쩌렁쩌렁 울려도 정작 형제나 남편에게는 좀체 닿지 않는 그 소문—과도 거리가 먼 사람이었다. 그럼에도 블리필 부인이 톰에게 보이는 이 애정, 그리고 친아들보다 톰을 더 위하는 그 너무도 빤한 편애는, 그 소년에게는 더없이 큰 손해였다."]], [["For such was the compassion which inhabited Mr Allworthy's mind, that nothing but the steel of justice could ever subdue it.", "올워디 씨의 마음속에는 깊은 연민이 자리잡고 있어서, 오직 정의라는 강철만이 그것을 눌러 이길 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["To be unfortunate in any respect was sufficient, if there was no demerit to counterpoise it, to turn the scale of that good man's pity, and to engage his friendship and his benefaction.", "어떤 점에서든 불행한 처지에 놓인 것만으로도—그 불행을 상쇄할 만한 잘못이 없는 한—이 선한 사람의 동정심을 그쪽으로 기울게 하고, 그의 우정과 자선을 끌어내기에 충분했다."]], [["When therefore he plainly saw Master Blifil was absolutely detested (for that he was) by his own mother, he began, on that account only, to look with an eye of compassion upon him; and what the effects of compassion are, in good and benevolent minds, I need not here explain to most of my readers.", "그리하여 블리필 도련님이 다름 아닌 친어머니에게 철저히 미움을 받고 있음을(실제로 그러했다) 똑똑히 알아차리자, 올워디 씨는 오직 그 이유 하나만으로 그를 연민의 눈으로 바라보기 시작했다. 그리고 선하고 자애로운 마음속에서 연민이 어떤 결과를 낳는지는, 여기서 내가 독자 대부분에게 굳이 설명할 필요가 없으리라."]], [["Henceforward he saw every appearance of virtue in the youth through the magnifying end, and viewed all his faults with the glass inverted, so that they became scarce perceptible. And this perhaps the amiable temper of pity may make commendable; but the next step the weakness of human nature alone must excuse; for he no sooner perceived that preference which Mrs Blifil gave to Tom, than that poor youth (however innocent) began to sink in his affections as he rose in hers. This, it is true, would of itself alone never have been able to eradicate Jones from his bosom; but it was greatly injurious to him, and prepared Mr Allworthy's mind for those impressions which afterwards produced the mighty events that will be contained hereafter in this history; and to which, it must be confest, the unfortunate lad, by his own wantonness, wildness, and want of caution, too much contributed.", "이때부터 그는 소년에게서 보이는 온갖 미덕의 기미는 확대경으로 들여다보듯 크게 보았고, 그의 결점은 거꾸로 뒤집은 유리로 보아서 거의 눈에 띄지 않을 만큼 작게 보았다. 어쩌면 이런 일은 연민이라는 정다운 성정 덕에 칭찬받을 만한 것일지도 모른다. 그러나 그다음 단계만큼은 오직 인간 본성의 나약함으로만 변명할 수 있을 뿐이다. 블리필 부인이 톰에게 주는 그 편애를 알아차리자마자, 그 가엾은 소년은(아무 죄가 없는데도) 부인의 마음속에서 떠오르는 만큼 올워디 씨의 마음속에서는 가라앉기 시작했으니 말이다. 사실 이것만으로는 결코 존스를 그의 가슴에서 뿌리째 뽑아내지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 이는 소년에게 큰 해를 끼쳤고, 훗날 이 이야기 안에 담길 그 엄청난 사건들을 빚어낼 어떤 인상들을 받아들이도록 올워디 씨의 마음을 미리 길들여 놓았다. 그리고 솔직히 말하건대, 그 불행한 소년은 제 방종과 거침, 그리고 조심성 없음으로 그 일에 너무 많이 한몫을 거들고 말았다."]], [["In recording some instances of these, we shall, if rightly understood, afford a very useful lesson to those well-disposed youths who shall hereafter be our readers; for they may here find, that goodness of heart, and openness of temper, though these may give them great comfort within, and administer to an honest pride in their own minds, will by no means, alas!", "이러한 일들의 몇몇 사례를 적어 나가면서, 우리가 제대로만 이해된다면, 앞으로 우리의 독자가 될 마음씨 고운 젊은이들에게 무척 쓸모 있는 교훈을 건넬 수 있을 것이다. 그들은 여기서 이런 것을 깨닫게 될 테니 말이다."], ["do their business in the world. Prudence and circumspection are necessary even to the best of men.", "따뜻한 마음과 솔직한 성품은 비록 속으로는 큰 위안을 주고 제 안에 정직한 자긍심을 심어 줄지언정, 아아, 세상살이에서는 결코 제 몫을 해내지 못한다는 것을."], ["They are indeed, as it were, a guard to Virtue, without which she can never be safe.", "분별과 신중함은 아무리 훌륭한 사람에게도 반드시 필요하다. 그것은 실로 덕을 지키는 호위병과도 같아서, 그것이 없으면 덕은 결코 안전할 수 없다."], ["It is not enough that your designs, nay, that your actions, are intrinsically good; you must take care they shall appear so.", "그대의 의도가, 아니 그대의 행동이 본래 선한 것만으로는 충분치 않다. 그것이 선하게 보이도록 마음을 써야 한다."], ["If your inside be never so beautiful, you must preserve a fair outside also.", "그대의 속이 아무리 아름다워도, 겉모습 또한 곱게 간수해야 한다."], ["This must be constantly looked to, or malice and envy will take care to blacken it so, that the sagacity and goodness of an Allworthy will not be able to see through it, and to discern the beauties within.", "이를 끊임없이 살피지 않으면, 악의와 시기가 어떻게든 그 겉을 새카맣게 칠해 놓을 것이고, 그리되면 올워디 같은 이의 명민함과 선량함으로도 그 너머를 꿰뚫어 속에 든 아름다움을 알아보지 못하게 된다."], ["Let this, my young readers, be your constant maxim, that no man can be good enough to enable him to neglect the rules of prudence; nor will Virtue herself look beautiful, unless she be bedecked with the outward ornaments of decency and decorum.", "나의 젊은 독자들이여, 이것을 그대들의 변치 않는 좌우명으로 삼으라. 어떤 사람도 분별의 규칙을 무시해도 좋을 만큼 선할 수는 없으며, 덕 자신조차도 예의와 품위라는 바깥의 장식으로 단장하지 않고서는 결코 아름다워 보이지 않는다는 것을."], ["And this precept, my worthy disciples, if you read with due attention, you will, I hope, find sufficiently enforced by examples in the following pages.", "그리고 이 가르침을, 나의 갸륵한 제자들이여, 마땅한 주의를 기울여 읽는다면, 뒤이은 페이지들에서 여러 사례로 충분히 뒷받침되고 있음을 발견하게 되리라 믿는다."]], [["I ask pardon for this short appearance, by way of chorus, on the stage.", "코러스 삼아 이렇게 잠깐 무대에 나선 것을 용서해 주기 바란다."], ["It is in reality for my own sake, that, while I am discovering the rocks on which innocence and goodness often split, I may not be misunderstood to recommend the very means to my worthy readers, by which I intend to show them they will be undone.", "사실 이것은 나 자신을 위해서다. 순결과 선함이 흔히 부딪혀 깨지는 그 바위들을 들춰 보이는 동안, 정작 내가 갸륵한 독자들에게 파멸로 이르는 길임을 보여 주려는 바로 그 수단을 도리어 권하는 것으로 오해받지 않으려는 것이다."], ["And this, as I could not prevail on any of my actors to speak, I myself was obliged to declare.", "그리고 이 말은, 내 배우들 가운데 누구에게도 입을 떼게 할 수 없었던 터라, 나 스스로 직접 밝힐 수밖에 없었다."]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["A childish incident, in which, however, is seen a good-natured disposition in Tom Jones.", "어린아이다운 한 사건. 그러나 그 안에서 톰 존스의 선량한 마음씨를 엿볼 수 있다."]], [["The reader may remember that Mr Allworthy gave Tom Jones a little horse, as a kind of smart-money for the punishment which he imagined he had suffered innocently.", "독자께서는 올워디 씨가 톰 존스에게 작은 말 한 마리를 주었던 일을 기억하실 것이다. 죄 없이 벌을 받았다고 여긴 그 일에 대한 일종의 위자료 같은 것이었다."]], [["This horse Tom kept above half a year, and then rode him to a neighbouring fair, and sold him.", "톰은 이 말을 반년 남짓 데리고 있다가, 이웃 마을 장터로 끌고 가 팔아 버렸다."]], [["At his return, being questioned by Thwackum what he had done with the money for which the horse was sold, he frankly declared he would not tell him.", "돌아온 톰에게 트와컴은 말을 판 돈을 어떻게 했느냐고 캐물었다. 톰은 말하지 않겠노라고 거리낌 없이 잘라 말했다."]], [["“Oho!”", "\"오호!\""], ["says Thwackum, “you will not!", "트와컴이 말했다. \"말을 안 하겠다고!"], ["then I will have it out of your br--h;”", "그렇다면 네 엉덩이에서 그걸 뽑아내 주마.\""], ["that being the place to which he always applied for information on every doubtful occasion.", "의심스러운 일이 생길 때마다 그가 진실을 캐내는 곳은 늘 그곳이었다."]], [["Tom was now mounted on the back of a footman, and everything prepared for execution, when Mr Allworthy, entering the room, gave the criminal a reprieve, and took him with him into another apartment; where, being alone with Tom, he put the same question to him which Thwackum had before asked him.", "이제 톰은 하인의 등에 업혔고, 형 집행을 위한 모든 준비가 갖추어졌다. 그때 올워디 씨가 방으로 들어와 죄인에게 집행 유예를 내리고는, 그를 데리고 다른 방으로 들어갔다. 톰과 단둘이 남게 되자 그는 조금 전 트와컴이 물었던 것과 똑같은 질문을 던졌다."]], [["Tom answered, he could in duty refuse him nothing; but as for that tyrannical rascal, he would never make him any other answer than with a cudgel, with which he hoped soon to be able to pay him for all his barbarities.", "톰은 대답했다. 도리로 보아 어르신께는 아무것도 숨길 수 없으나, 그 폭군 같은 악당에게만큼은 몽둥이가 아니고서는 다른 어떤 대답도 결코 하지 않겠다고. 그 몽둥이로 머지않아 그가 저지른 온갖 잔혹한 짓을 죄다 갚아 주기를 바란다고."]], [["Mr Allworthy very severely reprimanded the lad for his indecent and disrespectful expressions concerning his master; but much more for his avowing an intention of revenge.", "올워디 씨는 자기 스승을 두고 그처럼 무례하고 막된 말을 내뱉은 소년을 몹시 엄하게 꾸짖었으나, 복수하겠다는 마음을 드러낸 것에 대해서는 한층 더 호되게 나무랐다."], ["He threatened him with the entire loss of his favour, if he ever heard such another word from his mouth; for, he said, he would never support or befriend a reprobate.", "다시 그런 말이 입에서 나오는 것을 듣는다면 자신의 호의를 완전히 거두겠노라고 으름장을 놓았다. 자신은 결코 패륜아를 거두거나 벗으로 삼지 않겠다는 것이었다."], ["By these and the like declarations, he extorted some compunction from Tom, in which that youth was not over-sincere; for he really meditated some return for all the smarting favours he had received at the hands of the pedagogue.", "이런저런 말로 그는 톰에게서 약간의 뉘우침을 끌어냈으나, 그 소년의 마음은 그리 진실하지 못했다. 사실 톰은 그 훈장에게서 받은 따끔따끔한 은혜를 어떻게든 되갚아 줄 궁리를 하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["He was, however, brought by Mr Allworthy to express a concern for his resentment against Thwackum; and then the good man, after some wholesome admonition, permitted him to proceed, which he did as follows:--", "그래도 올워디 씨는 트와컴에 대한 원한을 후회한다는 말을 톰의 입에서 끌어냈고, 그런 다음 그 선량한 사람은 몇 마디 유익한 훈계를 덧붙인 뒤 이야기를 계속하라고 허락했다. 톰은 다음과 같이 말했다."]], [["“Indeed, my dear sir, I love and honour you more than all the world: I know the great obligations I have to you, and should detest myself if I thought my heart was capable of ingratitude.", "\"정말이지, 어르신, 저는 이 세상 누구보다 어르신을 사랑하고 존경합니다. 제가 어르신께 얼마나 큰 신세를 졌는지 잘 알고 있으며, 만일 제 마음이 은혜를 모를 수 있는 그런 마음이라고 생각된다면 저는 제 자신을 혐오하고 말 것입니다."], ["Could the little horse you gave me speak, I am sure he could tell you how fond I was of your present; for I had more pleasure in feeding him than in riding him.", "어르신께서 주신 그 작은 말이 입을 열 수 있다면, 제가 그 선물을 얼마나 아꼈는지 분명히 어르신께 말해 줄 것입니다. 저는 그 말을 타는 것보다 먹이를 주는 일이 더 즐거웠으니까요."], ["Indeed, sir, it went to my heart to part with him; nor would I have sold him upon any other account in the world than what I did.", "정말로, 어르신, 그 말과 헤어지는 일은 제 가슴을 후벼 팠습니다. 그리고 제가 한 그 까닭이 아니었다면, 이 세상 어떤 이유로도 저는 그 말을 팔지 않았을 것입니다."], ["You yourself, sir, I am convinced, in my case, would have done the same: for none ever so sensibly felt the misfortunes of others.", "어르신, 만일 제 처지셨다면 어르신께서도 똑같이 하셨으리라 저는 확신합니다. 어르신만큼 남의 불행을 그토록 절절히 느끼시는 분은 일찍이 없었으니까요."], ["What would you feel, dear sir, if you thought yourself the occasion of them?", "그런데, 어르신, 만일 그 불행의 원인이 바로 자기 자신이라고 생각하신다면 어떤 심정이시겠습니까?"], ["Indeed, sir, there never was any misery like theirs.”", "정말로, 어르신, 그들의 비참함 같은 것은 어디에도 없었습니다.\""]], [["“Like whose, child?”", "\"그들이라니, 누구의 비참함 말이냐?\""], ["says Allworthy: “What do you mean?”", "올워디 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 말이냐?\""]], [["“Oh, sir!” answered Tom, “your poor gamekeeper, with all his large family, ever since your discarding him, have been perishing with all the miseries of cold and hunger: I could not bear to see these poor wretches naked and starving, and at the same time know myself to have been the occasion of all their sufferings. I could not bear it, sir; upon my soul, I could not.” [Here the tears ran down his cheeks, and he thus proceeded.] “It was to save them from absolute destruction I parted with your dear present, notwithstanding all the value I had for it: I sold the horse for them, and they have every farthing of the money.”", "\"오, 어르신!\" 톰이 대답했다. \"어르신의 그 가엾은 사냥터지기 말입니다. 어르신께서 그를 내치신 뒤로, 그 많은 식구를 거느린 그가 추위와 굶주림의 온갖 고통 속에 죽어 가고 있었습니다. 저는 그 불쌍한 사람들이 헐벗고 굶주리는 꼴을 차마 볼 수가 없었습니다. 더구나 그 모든 고통의 원인이 바로 저였다는 것을 알면서요. 저는 견딜 수가 없었습니다, 어르신. 맹세코, 견딜 수가 없었습니다.\" (이 대목에서 톰의 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내렸고, 그는 이어서 말했다.) \"그들이 영영 무너지는 것을 막으려고, 저는 그토록 소중히 여기던 어르신의 선물과 헤어졌던 것입니다. 그들을 위해 말을 팔았고, 그 돈은 한 푼도 빠짐없이 그들에게 갔습니다.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy now stood silent for some moments, and before he spoke the tears started from his eyes.", "올워디 씨는 한동안 말없이 서 있었고, 입을 열기에 앞서 두 눈에서 눈물이 솟구쳤다."], ["He at length dismissed Tom with a gentle rebuke, advising him for the future to apply to him in cases of distress, rather than to use extraordinary means of relieving them himself.", "그는 마침내 부드러운 꾸지람과 함께 톰을 돌려보내며, 앞으로는 곤경에 처한 이들을 직접 비상한 방법으로 구하려 들기보다 자신에게 의논하라고 일러 주었다."]], [["This affair was afterwards the subject of much debate between Thwackum and Square.", "이 일은 그 뒤 트와컴과 스퀘어 사이에서 한참 논쟁거리가 되었다. 트와컴은 이것이 올워디 씨의 뜻을 정면으로 거스른 짓이라고 주장했다."], ["Thwackum held, that this was flying in Mr Allworthy's face, who had intended to punish the fellow for his disobedience.", "올워디 씨는 그 녀석의 불복종을 벌하려 했다는 것이었다. 그는 말하기를, 어떤 경우에는 세상이 자선이라 부르는 것이 자기 눈에는 전능하신 분의 뜻을 거스르는 것으로 보인다고 했다."], ["He said, in some instances, what the world called charity appeared to him to be opposing the will of the Almighty, which had marked some particular persons for destruction; and that this was in like manner acting in opposition to Mr Allworthy; concluding, as usual, with a hearty recommendation of birch.", "그분께서는 어떤 특정한 사람들을 멸망하도록 점지해 두셨다는 것이다. 그러니 이것 역시 마찬가지로 올워디 씨의 뜻을 거스르는 행동이라고 했고, 늘 그렇듯 자작나무 회초리를 진심으로 권하는 것으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["Square argued strongly on the other side, in opposition perhaps to Thwackum, or in compliance with Mr Allworthy, who seemed very much to approve what Jones had done.", "스퀘어는 그 반대편에서 강하게 논변했는데, 어쩌면 트와컴에게 맞서기 위해서였거나, 아니면 존스가 한 일을 무척 흡족해하는 듯 보이던 올워디 씨의 비위를 맞추기 위해서였을 것이다."], ["As to what he urged on this occasion, as I am convinced most of my readers will be much abler advocates for poor Jones, it would be impertinent to relate it.", "이 자리에서 그가 펼친 주장에 대해서는, 내 독자 대부분이 가엾은 존스를 위해 훨씬 더 훌륭한 변호인이 되어 주리라 확신하기에, 굳이 늘어놓는 것은 주제넘은 일이 될 것이다."], ["Indeed it was not difficult to reconcile to the rule of right an action which it would have been impossible to deduce from the rule of wrong.", "사실 그릇됨의 잣대로는 도무지 끌어낼 수 없었을 행동을 올바름의 잣대와 화해시키는 일은 그리 어렵지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter ix.", "# Chapter ix."]], [["Containing an incident of a more heinous kind, with the comments of Thwackum and Square.", "9장. 트와컴과 스퀘어의 논평과 더불어, 한층 더 흉악한 종류의 사건을 담은 이야기."]], [["It hath been observed by some man of much greater reputation for wisdom than myself, that misfortunes seldom come single. An instance of this may, I believe, be seen in those gentlemen who have the misfortune to have any of their rogueries detected; for here discovery seldom stops till the whole is come out. Thus it happened to poor Tom; who was no sooner pardoned for selling the horse, than he was discovered to have some time before sold a fine Bible which Mr Allworthy gave him, the money arising from which sale he had disposed of in the same manner. This Bible Master Blifil had purchased, though he had already such another of his own, partly out of respect for the book, and partly out of friendship to Tom, being unwilling that the Bible should be sold out of the family at half-price. He therefore deposited the said half-price himself; for he was a very prudent lad, and so careful of his money, that he had laid up almost every penny which he had received from Mr Allworthy.", "나보다 지혜로 훨씬 더 명성이 높은 어떤 분이 일찍이 말씀하신 바 있다. 불행이란 좀처럼 혼자 오지 않는 법이라고. 이 말의 한 본보기는, 제 못된 짓거리 가운데 하나가 발각되는 불행을 겪은 사람들에게서 찾아볼 수 있으리라 믿는다. 그런 경우에는 한 번 발각이 시작되면 모든 것이 다 드러날 때까지 좀처럼 멈추지 않으니 말이다. 가엾은 톰에게도 꼭 그런 일이 일어났다. 말을 판 일로 막 용서를 받자마자, 얼마 전에 올워디 씨가 준 좋은 성경책 한 권을 팔아버린 사실이 드러난 것이다. 그 책을 판 돈 역시 같은 방식으로 써버린 터였다. 이 성경책은 블리필 도련님이 사들였는데, 이미 똑같은 자기 책을 한 권 가지고 있었음에도 그리한 것은 한편으로는 그 책에 대한 존중에서, 한편으로는 톰을 향한 우정에서 비롯된 일이었다. 성경책이 반값에 집안 밖으로 팔려 나가는 것을 못마땅하게 여겼기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 그 반값을 손수 치렀다. 그는 매우 신중한 소년이었고 돈을 어찌나 알뜰히 챙겼던지, 올워디 씨에게서 받은 돈은 거의 한 푼도 빠짐없이 모아두고 있었다."]], [["Some people have been noted to be able to read in no book but their own.", "세상에는 자기 책이 아니면 어떤 책도 읽지 못하는 사람들이 있다고들 한다."], ["On the contrary, from the time when Master Blifil was first possessed of this Bible, he never used any other.", "그런데 블리필 도련님은 그와 정반대였다. 이 성경책이 처음 제 손에 들어온 그때부터, 그는 다른 책은 결코 쓰지 않았다."], ["Nay, he was seen reading in it much oftener than he had before been in his own.", "아니, 오히려 전에 자기 책을 읽던 것보다 훨씬 더 자주 이 책을 들여다보는 모습이 눈에 띄었다."], ["Now, as he frequently asked Thwackum to explain difficult passages to him, that gentleman unfortunately took notice of Tom's name, which was written in many parts of the book.", "그런데 그가 어려운 구절을 풀어 달라고 트와컴에게 자주 청하다 보니, 그 책 여러 군데에 적혀 있던 톰의 이름을 그 양반이 그만 발견하고 만 것이다."], ["This brought on an inquiry, which obliged Master Blifil to discover the whole matter.", "이 일로 추궁이 시작되었고, 블리필 도련님은 어쩔 수 없이 모든 사정을 털어놓아야 했다."]], [["Thwackum was resolved a crime of this kind, which he called sacrilege, should not go unpunished.", "트와컴은 자신이 신성모독이라 부르는 이런 종류의 죄가 벌을 받지 않고 넘어가서는 안 된다고 단단히 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그는 곧장 매질에 들어갔다."], ["He therefore proceeded immediately to castigation: and not contented with that he acquainted Mr Allworthy, at their next meeting, with this monstrous crime, as it appeared to him: inveighing against Tom in the most bitter terms, and likening him to the buyers and sellers who were driven out of the temple.", "그것으로도 모자라, 다음번 만남에서 올워디 씨에게 자기 눈에 비친 이 끔찍한 죄에 대해 일러바쳤다. 더없이 신랄한 말로 톰을 헐뜯으며, 그를 성전에서 쫓겨난 장사꾼들에 빗대기까지 했다."]], [["Square saw this matter in a very different light.", "스퀘어는 이 일을 사뭇 다른 시각으로 보았다."], ["He said, he could not perceive any higher crime in selling one book than in selling another.", "그는 책 한 권을 파는 데 다른 책을 파는 것보다 더 큰 죄가 있다고는 도무지 볼 수 없다고 했다."], ["That to sell Bibles was strictly lawful by all laws both Divine and human, and consequently there was no unfitness in it.", "성경책을 파는 일은 신의 법으로 보나 인간의 법으로 보나 엄연히 합법이며, 따라서 거기에 부적절한 구석이라곤 없다는 것이었다."], ["He told Thwackum, that his great concern on this occasion brought to his mind the story of a very devout woman, who, out of pure regard to religion, stole Tillotson's Sermons from a lady of her acquaintance.", "그는 트와컴에게, 이번 일로 그가 보인 그 대단한 노심초사를 보니 어느 무척 독실한 여인의 이야기가 떠오른다고 말했다. 그 여인은 순전히 종교를 향한 마음에서, 아는 어느 부인에게서 틸럿슨의 설교집을 훔쳤다는 것이었다."]], [["This story caused a vast quantity of blood to rush into the parson's face, which of itself was none of the palest; and he was going to reply with great warmth and anger, had not Mrs Blifil, who was present at this debate, interposed. That lady declared herself absolutely of Mr Square's side. She argued, indeed, very learnedly in support of his opinion; and concluded with saying, if Tom had been guilty of any fault, she must confess her own son appeared to be equally culpable; for that she could see no difference between the buyer and the seller; both of whom were alike to be driven out of the temple.", "이 이야기에 목사의 얼굴로 엄청난 양의 피가 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그 얼굴이 본디 창백한 축도 아니었는데 말이다. 그는 잔뜩 열을 올리며 노여움에 차 받아치려던 참이었으나, 이 논쟁 자리에 함께 있던 블리필 부인이 끼어드는 바람에 그러지 못했다. 그 부인은 자신이 전적으로 스퀘어 씨 편이라고 밝혔다. 실로 그녀는 그의 의견을 떠받치며 매우 박식하게 논변을 펼쳤다. 그리고 끝으로, 만일 톰에게 잘못이 있다면 자기 아들도 똑같이 죄가 있다고 인정할 수밖에 없다고 말했다. 사는 자와 파는 자 사이에 아무런 차이도 볼 수 없으며, 둘 다 똑같이 성전에서 쫓겨나야 마땅하다는 것이었다."]], [["Mrs Blifil having declared her opinion, put an end to the debate.", "블리필 부인이 자기 의견을 밝히자 논쟁은 끝이 났다."], ["Square's triumph would almost have stopt his words, had he needed them; and Thwackum, who, for reasons before-mentioned, durst not venture at disobliging the lady, was almost choaked with indignation.", "스퀘어는 굳이 말이 더 필요했더라도 승리감에 겨워 말문이 막힐 지경이었다. 그리고 앞서 말한 까닭들로 감히 그 부인의 비위를 거스를 엄두를 내지 못한 트와컴은 분노로 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["As to Mr Allworthy, he said, since the boy had been already punished he would not deliver his sentiments on the occasion; and whether he was or was not angry with the lad, I must leave to the reader's own conjecture.", "올워디 씨로 말하자면, 아이가 이미 벌을 받았으니 이 일에 대해 자기 견해를 내놓지는 않겠다고 했다. 그가 이 소년에게 화가 났는지 안 났는지는, 독자 여러분의 짐작에 맡길 수밖에 없겠다."]], [["Soon after this, an action was brought against the gamekeeper by Squire Western (the gentleman in whose manor the partridge was killed), for depredations of the like kind. This was a most unfortunate circumstance for the fellow, as it not only of itself threatened his ruin, but actually prevented Mr Allworthy from restoring him to his favour: for as that gentleman was walking out one evening with Master Blifil and young Jones, the latter slily drew him to the habitation of Black George; where the family of that poor wretch, namely, his wife and children, were found in all the misery with which cold, hunger, and nakedness, can affect human creatures: for as to the money they had received from Jones, former debts had consumed almost the whole.", "이 일이 있고 얼마 지나지 않아, 웨스턴 지주(자고새가 잡혔던 영지의 주인 양반)가 사냥터지기를 상대로 비슷한 종류의 침해 행위를 걸어 소송을 제기했다. 이는 그 사내에게 더없이 불운한 일이었다. 그 자체로 그를 파멸로 몰아넣을 위협이었을 뿐 아니라, 실제로 올워디 씨가 그를 다시 거두어 주는 것을 가로막았기 때문이다. 어느 날 저녁 그 양반이 블리필 도련님과 어린 존스를 데리고 산책을 나갔을 때, 존스가 슬쩍 그를 블랙 조지의 거처로 이끌었다. 그곳에서는 그 가엾은 사내의 식구들, 곧 그의 아내와 아이들이 추위와 굶주림과 헐벗음이 인간을 짓누를 수 있는 온갖 비참함 속에 놓여 있었다. 존스에게서 받은 돈으로 말하자면, 이전의 빚이 거의 다 삼켜버린 터였다."]], [["Such a scene as this could not fail of affecting the heart of Mr Allworthy.", "이러한 광경이 올워디 씨의 마음을 움직이지 못할 리 없었다."], ["He immediately gave the mother a couple of guineas, with which he bid her cloath her children.", "그는 곧바로 어미에게 1기니 금화 두 닢을 건네며, 그것으로 아이들에게 옷을 입히라고 일렀다."], ["The poor woman burst into tears at this goodness, and while she was thanking him, could not refrain from expressing her gratitude to Tom; who had, she said, long preserved both her and hers from starving.", "가엾은 여인은 이 자비로움에 그만 눈물을 터뜨렸고, 그에게 고마움을 전하면서도 톰을 향한 감사의 마음을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀의 말로는, 톰이 오래도록 자기와 식구들을 굶주림에서 지켜 주었다는 것이었다."], ["“We have not,”", "\"저희는,\""], ["says she, “had a morsel to eat, nor have these poor children had a rag to put on, but what his goodness hath bestowed on us.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"그분의 자비가 베풀어 주신 것 말고는 한 입 먹을 것도 없었고, 이 가엾은 아이들도 몸에 걸칠 천 쪼가리 하나 없었답니다.\""], ["For, indeed, besides the horse and the Bible, Tom had sacrificed a night-gown, and other things, to the use of this distressed family.", "실로 톰은 말과 성경책 말고도, 잠옷이며 다른 물건들까지 이 곤궁한 식구들을 위해 내어주었던 것이다."]], [["On their return home, Tom made use of all his eloquence to display the wretchedness of these people, and the penitence of Black George himself; and in this he succeeded so well, that Mr Allworthy said, he thought the man had suffered enough for what was past; that he would forgive him, and think of some means of providing for him and his family.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에, 톰은 이 사람들의 비참함과 블랙 조지 자신의 뉘우침을 드러내 보이려 온갖 말솜씨를 동원했다. 그리고 이 일에 어찌나 잘 성공했던지, 올워디 씨는 그 사내가 지난 일에 대해 충분히 고생했다고 여겨진다고 말했다. 그를 용서하고, 그와 그 식구들을 돌볼 어떤 방도를 궁리해 보겠다는 것이었다."]], [["Jones was so delighted with this news, that, though it was dark when they returned home, he could not help going back a mile, in a shower of rain, to acquaint the poor woman with the glad tidings; but, like other hasty divulgers of news, he only brought on himself the trouble of contradicting it: for the ill fortune of Black George made use of the very opportunity of his friend's absence to overturn all again.", "존스는 이 소식에 어찌나 기뻤던지, 집에 돌아왔을 때는 이미 날이 어두웠는데도 쏟아지는 빗속을 1마일이나 되짚어 달려가 그 가엾은 여인에게 기쁜 소식을 알리지 않고는 못 배겼다. 그러나 성급하게 소식을 퍼뜨리는 다른 이들과 마찬가지로, 그는 제 입으로 한 말을 도로 뒤집어야 하는 곤란만 자초하고 말았다. 블랙 조지의 불운이 바로 그 친구가 자리를 비운 틈을 타, 모든 것을 다시 뒤엎어 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["In which Master Blifil and Jones appear in different lights.", "블리필 도련님과 존스가 서로 다른 빛으로 비치는 이야기."]], [["Master Blifil fell very short of his companion in the amiable quality of mercy; but he as greatly exceeded him in one of a much higher kind, namely, in justice: in which he followed both the precepts and example of Thwackum and Square; for though they would both make frequent use of the word mercy, yet it was plain that in reality Square held it to be inconsistent with the rule of right; and Thwackum was for doing justice, and leaving mercy to heaven. The two gentlemen did indeed somewhat differ in opinion concerning the objects of this sublime virtue; by which Thwackum would probably have destroyed one half of mankind, and Square the other half.", "블리필 도련님은 자비라는 사랑스러운 덕에서는 동무에게 한참 못 미쳤다. 그러나 그보다 훨씬 높은 부류의 덕, 곧 정의에서는 그를 크게 앞질렀다. 이 점에서 그는 스웨컴과 스퀘어의 가르침과 본보기를 그대로 따랐다. 두 사람 모두 자비라는 말을 입에 자주 올리기는 했으나, 실제로는 스퀘어가 그것을 옳음의 규칙과 어긋나는 것으로 여겼음이 분명했고, 스웨컴은 정의를 행하고 자비는 하늘에 맡기자는 쪽이었다. 다만 두 신사는 이 숭고한 덕의 대상이 누구인가를 두고는 다소 의견이 갈렸으니, 이 셈법대로라면 스웨컴은 아마 인류의 절반을, 스퀘어는 나머지 절반을 멸했을 것이다."]], [["Master Blifil then, though he had kept silence in the presence of Jones, yet, when he had better considered the matter, could by no means endure the thought of suffering his uncle to confer favours on the undeserving.", "그러니 블리필 도련님은 존스 앞에서는 입을 다물고 있었지만, 그 일을 곰곰이 더 따져 본 뒤로는 자기 외삼촌이 받을 자격 없는 자에게 은혜를 베푸는 꼴을 도저히 두고 볼 수 없었다."], ["He therefore resolved immediately to acquaint him with the fact which we have above slightly hinted to the readers.", "그리하여 그는 우리가 앞서 독자들에게 슬쩍 비쳐 두었던 그 사실을 곧장 외삼촌에게 알리기로 마음먹었다."], ["The truth of which was as follows:", "그 진상은 이러했다."]], [["The gamekeeper, about a year after he was dismissed from Mr Allworthy's service, and before Tom's selling the horse, being in want of bread, either to fill his own mouth or those of his family, as he passed through a field belonging to Mr Western espied a hare sitting in her form.", "그 사냥터지기는 올워디 씨의 일자리에서 쫓겨난 지 한 해쯤 지난 뒤, 그리고 톰이 말을 팔기 전, 제 입이든 식구들의 입이든 채울 빵이 없어 궁하던 차에, 웨스턴 씨 소유의 어느 밭을 지나다가 보금자리에 웅크려 앉은 산토끼 한 마리를 발견했다."], ["This hare he had basely and barbarously knocked on the head, against the laws of the land, and no less against the laws of sportsmen.", "그는 이 토끼를 비열하고 무자비하게 머리를 후려쳐 잡았으니, 나라의 법에 어긋났을 뿐 아니라 사냥꾼의 법도에도 못지않게 어긋나는 짓이었다."]], [["The higgler to whom the hare was sold, being unfortunately taken many months after with a quantity of game upon him, was obliged to make his peace with the squire, by becoming evidence against some poacher.", "그 토끼를 사 갔던 행상은 불행히도 몇 달 뒤에 사냥감을 잔뜩 지닌 채 붙잡혔고, 어느 밀렵꾼에게 불리한 증언을 하는 조건으로 향사와 화해할 수밖에 없게 되었다. 그리하여 그가 지목한 사람이 바로 블랙 조지였으니, 이미 웨스턴 씨의 눈 밖에 난 자인 데다 동네에서 평판도 좋지 못한 인물이었기 때문이다."], ["And now Black George was pitched upon by him, as being a person already obnoxious to Mr Western, and one of no good fame in the country.", "게다가 그는 행상이 내놓을 수 있는 가장 마땅한 제물이기도 했다. 그 뒤로 그에게 사냥감을 대 준 적이 없었던 까닭이다."], ["He was, besides, the best sacrifice the higgler could make, as he had supplied him with no game since; and by this means the witness had an opportunity of screening his better customers: for the squire, being charmed with the power of punishing Black George, whom a single transgression was sufficient to ruin, made no further enquiry.", "이런 식으로 증인은 더 좋은 단골들을 감싸 줄 기회를 얻었다. 향사는 단 한 번의 위법으로도 충분히 파멸시킬 수 있는 블랙 조지를 벌하는 힘에 흡족해하여 더는 캐묻지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Had this fact been truly laid before Mr Allworthy, it might probably have done the gamekeeper very little mischief.", "이 사실이 올워디 씨 앞에 있는 그대로 진술되었다면, 아마 사냥터지기에게 이렇다 할 화는 미치지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 죄인을 향해 정의를 사랑하는 마음에서 타오르는 열성만큼 눈먼 열성도 없다."], ["But there is no zeal blinder than that which is inspired with the love of justice against offenders.", "블리필 도련님은 그 일이 일어난 시점이 얼마나 오래전이었는지를 잊고 있었다. 게다가 사실을 전하는 방식에서도 어긋났으니, 'S'라는 글자 하나를 성급히 덧붙임으로써 이야기를 상당히 바꿔 놓고 말았다."], ["Master Blifil had forgot the distance of the time.", "조지가 산토끼들에게 올가미를 놓았다고 말한 것이다."], ["He varied likewise in the manner of the fact: and by the hasty addition of the single letter S he considerably altered the story; for he said that George had wired hares.", "이런 왜곡들은 아마 바로잡힐 수도 있었으련만, 불운하게도 블리필 도련님이 이 일을 털어놓기에 앞서 올워디 씨에게 비밀을 지키겠다는 약속부터 받아 둔 것이 문제였다."], ["These alterations might probably have been set right, had not Master Blifil unluckily insisted on a promise of secrecy from Mr Allworthy before he revealed the matter to him; but by that means the poor gamekeeper was condemned without having an opportunity to defend himself: for as the fact of killing the hare, and of the action brought, were certainly true, Mr Allworthy had no doubt concerning the rest.", "그 탓에 가엾은 사냥터지기는 스스로를 변호할 기회조차 갖지 못한 채 단죄당하고 말았다. 토끼를 잡은 사실과 소송이 제기된 사실은 분명히 참이었으니, 올워디 씨는 나머지에 대해서도 의심을 품지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Short-lived then was the joy of these poor people; for Mr Allworthy the next morning declared he had fresh reason, without assigning it, for his anger, and strictly forbad Tom to mention George any more: though as for his family, he said he would endeavour to keep them from starving; but as to the fellow himself, he would leave him to the laws, which nothing could keep him from breaking.", "그러니 이 가난한 사람들의 기쁨은 오래가지 못했다. 올워디 씨가 이튿날 아침, 까닭은 밝히지 않은 채 자신의 노여움에 새로운 이유가 생겼다고 선언하며, 톰에게 다시는 조지의 이름을 입에 올리지 말라고 엄히 금했기 때문이다. 다만 그 식구들에 대해서는 굶어 죽지 않도록 애써 보겠노라 했으나, 정작 그 사내만큼은 법에 맡겨 두겠으며, 그 무엇으로도 그가 법을 어기는 것을 막을 수 없으리라 했다."]], [["Tom could by no means divine what had incensed Mr Allworthy, for of Master Blifil he had not the least suspicion.", "톰은 무엇이 올워디 씨를 그토록 격분케 했는지 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었다. 블리필 도련님에 대해서는 털끝만큼의 의심도 없었기 때문이다."], ["However, as his friendship was to be tired out by no disappointments, he now determined to try another method of preserving the poor gamekeeper from ruin.", "그러나 그의 우정은 어떤 실망으로도 지칠 줄 모르는 것이었기에, 그는 이제 가엾은 사냥터지기를 파멸에서 구할 또 다른 방법을 시도해 보기로 했다."]], [["Jones was lately grown very intimate with Mr Western.", "존스는 근래에 웨스턴 씨와 무척 가까워져 있었다."], ["He had so greatly recommended himself to that gentleman, by leaping over five-barred gates, and by other acts of sportsmanship, that the squire had declared Tom would certainly make a great man if he had but sufficient encouragement.", "그는 다섯 칸짜리 울타리 문을 훌쩍 뛰어넘는다든가 하는 여러 사냥 솜씨로 그 신사의 마음에 어찌나 들었던지, 향사는 톰이 충분한 격려만 받는다면 틀림없이 대단한 인물이 되리라고 단언하기에 이르렀다."], ["He often wished he had himself a son with such parts; and one day very solemnly asserted at a drinking bout, that Tom should hunt a pack of hounds for a thousand pound of his money, with any huntsman in the whole country.", "그는 자기에게 그런 재능을 지닌 아들이 있었으면 좋겠다고 자주 바랐고, 어느 날은 술자리에서 자못 엄숙하게, 톰이라면 자기 돈 천 파운드를 걸고 이 고장의 어떤 사냥꾼과 견주어도 한 무리의 사냥개를 거뜬히 몰 것이라고 장담했다."]], [["By such kind of talents he had so ingratiated himself with the squire, that he was a most welcome guest at his table, and a favourite companion in his sport: everything which the squire held most dear, to wit, his guns, dogs, and horses, were now as much at the command of Jones, as if they had been his own.", "이런 재주들 덕분에 그는 향사에게 어찌나 환심을 샀던지, 그의 식탁에서 더없이 반가운 손님이자 그의 사냥에서 가장 아끼는 길동무가 되었다. 향사가 가장 귀히 여기는 것, 다시 말해 그의 총과 개와 말은 이제 마치 제 것이기라도 한 듯 존스의 뜻대로 부릴 수 있었다."], ["He resolved therefore to make use of this favour on behalf of his friend Black George, whom he hoped to introduce into Mr Western's family, in the same capacity in which he had before served Mr Allworthy.", "그리하여 그는 이 호의를 벗 블랙 조지를 위해 써먹기로 마음먹었으니, 전에 올워디 씨를 모시던 바로 그 자리에 조지를 웨스턴 씨 집안으로 들여보내고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["The reader, if he considers that this fellow was already obnoxious to Mr Western, and if he considers farther the weighty business by which that gentleman's displeasure had been incurred, will perhaps condemn this as a foolish and desperate undertaking; but if he should totally condemn young Jones on that account, he will greatly applaud him for strengthening himself with all imaginable interest on so arduous an occasion.", "독자는, 이 사내가 이미 웨스턴 씨의 눈 밖에 나 있었다는 점을 헤아리고, 더 나아가 그 신사의 노여움을 산 그 묵직한 사연까지 떠올린다면, 어쩌면 이 일을 어리석고 무모한 짓이라 나무랄지도 모른다. 그러나 그 까닭으로 젊은 존스를 통째로 단죄하기보다는, 이토록 험난한 마당에서 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 연줄로 제 입지를 다지려 한 그를 크게 칭찬하게 될 것이다."]], [["For this purpose, then, Tom applied to Mr Western's daughter, a young lady of about seventeen years of age, whom her father, next after those necessary implements of sport just before mentioned, loved and esteemed above all the world.", "그리하여 이 목적을 위해 톰은 웨스턴 씨의 딸에게 도움을 청했다. 열일곱 살쯤 된 젊은 아가씨로, 그녀의 아버지는 방금 말한 그 사냥에 꼭 필요한 도구들 다음으로 세상 그 무엇보다 그녀를 사랑하고 아꼈다."], ["Now, as she had some influence on the squire, so Tom had some little influence on her.", "자, 그녀가 향사에게 어느 정도 영향을 미쳤듯이, 톰도 그녀에게 작으나마 어느 정도 영향을 미치고 있었다."], ["But this being the intended heroine of this work, a lady with whom we ourselves are greatly in love, and with whom many of our readers will probably be in love too, before we part, it is by no means proper she should make her appearance at the end of a book.", "그러나 이 사람이야말로 이 작품의 여주인공으로 점지된 아가씨이며, 우리 자신이 깊이 사랑하는 이요, 헤어지기 전에 우리 독자들 가운데 많은 이도 아마 사랑하게 될 그 아가씨이니, 그녀가 한 권의 끝자락에서 모습을 드러내는 것은 결코 온당치 않다."]], [["Containing five pages of paper.", "일 년이라는 시간을 담은 권."]], [["As truth distinguishes our writings from those idle romances which are filled with monsters, the productions, not of nature, but of distempered brains; and which have been therefore recommended by an eminent critic to the sole use of the pastry-cook; so, on the other hand, we would avoid any resemblance to that kind of history which a celebrated poet seems to think is no less calculated for the emolument of the brewer, as the reading it should be always attended with a tankard of good ale--", "다섯 쪽 분량의 종이를 담은 장. 진실이야말로 우리의 글을, 괴물들로 가득 찬 저 한가한 연애담들과 갈라놓는다. 그런 이야기들은 자연이 빚은 것이 아니라 열에 들뜬 머릿속에서 나온 산물이며, 그래서 어느 저명한 비평가가 오직 빵 굽는 사람에게나 쓰라고 권한 것들이다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 우리는 어느 이름난 시인이 양조업자의 잇속을 채우기에 꼭 알맞다고 여긴 듯한 그런 종류의 역사와도 닮기를 피하고자 한다. 그런 역사는 읽을 때 늘 질 좋은 맥주 한 잔이 곁들여져야 한다는 것이니—"]], [["While--history with her comrade ale, Soothes the sad series of her serious tale", "역사가 그 동무인 맥주와 더불어, 자신의 그 엄숙한 이야기의 서글픈 줄거리를 달래는 동안"]], [["For as this is the liquor of modern historians, nay, perhaps their muse, if we may believe the opinion of Butler, who attributes inspiration to ale, it ought likewise to be the potation of their readers, since every book ought to be read with the same spirit and in the same manner as it is writ. Thus the famous author of Hurlothrumbo told a learned bishop, that the reason his lordship could not taste the excellence of his piece was, that he did not read it with a fiddle in his hand; which instrument he himself had always had in his own, when he composed it.", "이것이 근대 역사가들의 음료요, 아니 어쩌면 그들의 시신(詩神)이기도 하니—맥주에 영감을 돌린 버틀러의 견해를 믿어도 좋다면—마땅히 그 독자들의 음료이기도 해야 한다. 무릇 모든 책은 쓰인 것과 같은 기운으로, 같은 방식으로 읽혀야 하기 때문이다. 그래서 《허를로트럼보》의 그 유명한 작가는 어느 학식 깊은 주교에게 이렇게 말했다. 주교 각하께서 제 작품의 뛰어남을 음미하지 못하시는 까닭은, 그것을 손에 바이올린을 들고 읽지 않으셨기 때문이라고 말이다. 작가 자신은 그 곡을 지을 때 늘 그 악기를 손에 쥐고 있었다는 것이다."]], [["That our work, therefore, might be in no danger of being likened to the labours of these historians, we have taken every occasion of interspersing through the whole sundry similes, descriptions, and other kind of poetical embellishments.", "그러므로 우리의 작품이 이런 역사가들의 노고와 견주어질 위험에 조금도 빠지지 않도록, 우리는 글 전체에 걸쳐 갖가지 비유와 묘사, 그 밖의 시적인 꾸밈들을 군데군데 끼워 넣을 기회를 빠짐없이 잡아 왔다."], ["These are, indeed, designed to supply the place of the said ale, and to refresh the mind, whenever those slumbers, which in a long work are apt to invade the reader as well as the writer, shall begin to creep upon him.", "이것들은 사실 앞서 말한 그 맥주의 자리를 대신하고, 긴 작품에서는 독자에게나 작가에게나 슬며시 찾아들기 마련인 그 졸음이 독자에게 스멀스멀 기어들기 시작할 때마다 그 마음을 새롭게 깨우려는 것이다."], ["Without interruptions of this kind, the best narrative of plain matter of fact must overpower every reader; for nothing but the ever lasting watchfulness, which Homer has ascribed only to Jove himself, can be proof against a newspaper of many volumes.", "이런 종류의 끼어듦이 없다면, 평범한 사실을 다룬 아무리 훌륭한 서술이라도 모든 독자를 짓눌러 버리고 말 것이다. 호메로스가 오직 유피테르 자신에게만 돌린 그 영원한 깨어 있음이 아니고서는, 여러 권에 이르는 신문을 견뎌 낼 길이 없으니 말이다."]], [["We shall leave to the reader to determine with what judgment we have chosen the several occasions for inserting those ornamental parts of our work.", "우리가 작품의 이 장식적인 부분들을 끼워 넣을 여러 자리를 어떤 판단으로 골랐는지는, 독자의 헤아림에 맡겨 두기로 하자."], ["Surely it will be allowed that none could be more proper than the present, where we are about to introduce a considerable character on the scene; no less, indeed, than the heroine of this heroic, historical, prosaic poem.", "분명 지금 이 자리야말로 더없이 알맞은 곳임은 인정받으리라. 지금 우리는 한 비중 있는 인물을 무대에 올리려 하는 참이니, 그 인물은 실로 이 영웅적이고 역사적이며 산문적인 시의 여주인공에 다름 아니다."], ["Here, therefore, we have thought proper to prepare the mind of the reader for her reception, by filling it with every pleasing image which we can draw from the face of nature.", "그러므로 우리는 자연의 얼굴에서 길어 올릴 수 있는 온갖 즐거운 이미지로 독자의 마음을 가득 채워, 그녀를 맞이할 채비를 갖추게 하는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다."], ["And for this method we plead many precedents.", "그리고 이 방법에 대해서는 여러 선례를 들어 변호하겠다."], ["First, this is an art well known to, and much practised by, our tragick poets, who seldom fail to prepare their audience for the reception of their principal characters.", "첫째로, 이것은 우리의 비극 시인들이 익히 알고 즐겨 쓰는 기술로서, 그들은 자신들의 주요 인물을 맞아들이도록 관객을 채비시키는 일을 좀처럼 빠뜨리지 않는다."]], [["Thus the heroe is always introduced with a flourish of drums and trumpets, in order to rouse a martial spirit in the audience, and to accommodate their ears to bombast and fustian, which Mr Locke's blind man would not have grossly erred in likening to the sound of a trumpet.", "그리하여 영웅은 언제나 북과 나팔의 우렁찬 울림과 함께 등장하는데, 이는 관객의 가슴에 무인의 기개를 불러일으키고, 허풍스럽고 과장된 대사에 그들의 귀를 길들이기 위함이다. 로크 씨의 그 눈먼 사람이 그런 대사를 나팔 소리에 빗대었다 한들 그리 크게 틀린 말은 아니었으리라."], ["Again, when lovers are coming forth, soft music often conducts them on the stage, either to soothe the audience with the softness of the tender passion, or to lull and prepare them for that gentle slumber in which they will most probably be composed by the ensuing scene.", "또한 연인들이 나설 때면, 흔히 부드러운 음악이 그들을 무대로 이끌어 들인다. 이는 그 애틋한 사랑의 부드러움으로 관객을 어루만지거나, 아니면 뒤이어 펼쳐질 장면이 십중팔구 그들을 잠재울 그 잔잔한 졸음으로 그들을 다독여 채비시키기 위함이다."]], [["And not only the poets, but the masters of these poets, the managers of playhouses, seem to be in this secret; for, besides the aforesaid kettle-drums, &c., which denote the heroe's approach, he is generally ushered on the stage by a large troop of half a dozen scene-shifters; and how necessary these are imagined to his appearance, may be concluded from the following theatrical story:--", "그리고 비단 시인들뿐 아니라, 그 시인들의 주인 격인 극장 운영자들 또한 이 비밀을 알고 있는 듯하다. 앞서 말한 영웅의 다가옴을 알리는 큰북 따위 말고도, 영웅은 대개 무대장치 옮기는 일꾼 대여섯 명으로 이루어진 큰 무리의 안내를 받으며 무대에 오르기 때문이다. 그리고 이들이 영웅의 등장에 얼마나 필수적이라 여겨졌는지는, 다음의 극장 일화에서 능히 짐작할 수 있다—"]], [["King Pyrrhus was at dinner at an ale-house bordering on the theatre, when he was summoned to go on the stage.", "피로스 왕이 극장에 잇닿은 어느 맥줏집에서 저녁을 들고 있는데, 무대에 오르라는 부름을 받았다."], ["The heroe, being unwilling to quit his shoulder of mutton, and as unwilling to draw on himself the indignation of Mr Wilks (his brother-manager) for making the audience wait, had bribed these his harbingers to be out of the way.", "영웅은 양고기 다리를 놓아두기도 싫었고, 그렇다고 관객을 기다리게 해서 동료 운영자인 윌크스 씨의 분노를 사기도 싫었던지라, 자신의 그 길잡이들을 매수해 자리를 비우게 해 두었다."], ["While Mr Wilks, therefore, was thundering out, “Where are the carpenters to walk on before King Pyrrhus?”", "그래서 윌크스 씨가 \"피로스 왕 앞에서 걸어 나갈 목수들은 어디 있느냐?\""], ["that monarch very quietly eat his mutton, and the audience, however impatient, were obliged to entertain themselves with music in his absence.", "하고 우레같이 호통치는 동안, 그 군주는 아주 태연하게 양고기를 먹었고, 관객들은 제아무리 애가 탔어도 그가 없는 사이 음악으로나마 스스로를 달랠 수밖에 없었다."]], [["To be plain, I much question whether the politician, who hath generally a good nose, hath not scented out somewhat of the utility of this practice.", "솔직히 말해, 나는 대체로 코가 밝은 정치가가 이런 관행의 유용함을 어느 정도 냄새 맡아 내지 않았을까 자못 의심스럽다."], ["I am convinced that awful magistrate my lord-mayor contracts a good deal of that reverence which attends him through the year, by the several pageants which precede his pomp.", "저 위엄 있는 행정관, 우리의 시장 각하께서 일 년 내내 따라다니는 그 존경을 적잖이 거둬들이는 것도, 그의 화려한 행차에 앞서는 여러 가두 행렬 덕분임을 나는 확신한다."], ["Nay, I must confess, that even I myself, who am not remarkably liable to be captivated with show, have yielded not a little to the impressions of much preceding state.", "아니, 솔직히 고백하건대, 겉치레에 좀처럼 사로잡히지 않는 나 자신조차도 한껏 앞세운 위풍의 인상에 적잖이 굴복하고 만 적이 있다."], ["When I have seen a man strutting in a procession, after others whose business was only to walk before him, I have conceived a higher notion of his dignity than I have felt on seeing him in a common situation.", "어떤 사람이 그저 그의 앞에서 걸어가는 것이 본분인 다른 이들의 뒤를 따라 행렬 속에서 거드름을 피우며 걷는 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 그를 예사로운 자리에서 보았을 때보다 그의 위엄을 더 높이 여기곤 했다."], ["But there is one instance, which comes exactly up to my purpose.", "그러나 내 뜻에 꼭 들어맞는 한 가지 사례가 있다."], ["This is the custom of sending on a basket-woman, who is to precede the pomp at a coronation, and to strew the stage with flowers, before the great personages begin their procession.", "대관식 때 그 화려한 행렬에 앞서 나아가, 위대한 인물들이 행진을 시작하기 전에 무대에 꽃을 흩뿌리는 바구니 든 여인을 내보내는 관습이 그것이다."], ["The antients would certainly have invoked the goddess Flora for this purpose, and it would have been no difficulty for their priests, or politicians to have persuaded the people of the real presence of the deity, though a plain mortal had personated her and performed her office.", "옛사람들이라면 분명 이 일을 위해 꽃의 여신 플로라를 불러냈을 것이며, 그들의 사제나 정치가가 백성에게 그 여신이 실제로 거기 임하셨다고 설득하기란 그리 어렵지 않았으리라. 비록 한낱 평범한 인간이 그 여신을 흉내 내어 그 역할을 대신했을 뿐이라 해도 말이다."], ["But we have no such design of imposing on our reader; and therefore those who object to the heathen theology, may, if they please, change our goddess into the above-mentioned basket-woman.", "그러나 우리에게는 독자를 그렇게 속이려는 의도가 없으니, 이교의 신학에 거부감을 느끼는 이들은 원한다면 우리의 여신을 앞서 말한 그 바구니 든 여인으로 바꾸어 두어도 좋다."], ["Our intention, in short, is to introduce our heroine with the utmost solemnity in our power, with an elevation of stile, and all other circumstances proper to raise the veneration of our reader.--Indeed we would, for certain causes, advise those of our male readers who have any hearts, to read no farther, were we not well assured, that how amiable soever the picture of our heroine will appear, as it is really a copy from nature, many of our fair countrywomen will be found worthy to satisfy any passion, and to answer any idea of female perfection which our pencil will be able to raise.", "요컨대 우리의 의도는, 우리가 부릴 수 있는 최대한의 장엄함과 격조 높은 문체, 그리고 독자의 경외심을 자아낼 만한 그 밖의 온갖 정황을 갖추어 우리의 여주인공을 소개하는 것이다.—실로 우리는 어떤 이유들로 인해, 가슴이라는 것을 지닌 우리의 남성 독자들에게 더 이상 읽지 말라고 권하고 싶을 지경이다. 다만 우리의 여주인공의 그림이 제아무리 사랑스럽게 보일지라도, 그것이 실은 자연을 본뜬 모사인 만큼, 우리의 붓이 그려 낼 수 있는 그 어떤 정념이든 채워 주고 여성의 완전함에 대한 그 어떤 관념에든 부응할 만한 우리 고운 동포 여인들이 많이 있으리라 우리가 굳게 확신하지 않았던들 그리했을 것이다."]], [["And now, without any further preface, we proceed to our next chapter.", "자, 이제 더 이상의 머리말 없이, 우리는 다음 장으로 나아가기로 한다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["A short hint of what we can do in the sublime, and a description of Miss Sophia Western.", "거친 숨결은 모두 잠재워라."], ["Hushed be every ruder breath. May the heathen ruler of the winds confine in iron chains the boisterous limbs of noisy Boreas, and the sharp-pointed nose of bitter-biting Eurus.", "바람을 다스리는 이교의 신이여, 시끄러운 북풍 보레아스의 우악스러운 팔다리를, 그리고 매섭게 쏘아붙이는 동풍 에우루스의 뾰족한 콧날을 쇠사슬로 묶어 가두소서."], ["Do thou, sweet Zephyrus, rising from thy fragrant bed, mount the western sky, and lead on those delicious gales, the charms of which call forth the lovely Flora from her chamber, perfumed with pearly dews, when on the 1st of June, her birth-day, the blooming maid, in loose attire, gently trips it over the verdant mead, where every flower rises to do her homage, till the whole field becomes enamelled, and colours contend with sweets which shall ravish her most.", "그대 다정한 서풍 제피루스여, 향기로운 잠자리에서 일어나 서쪽 하늘로 올라, 저 감미로운 산들바람을 이끌어 오라. 그 산들바람의 매력이 사랑스러운 꽃의 여신 플로라를 진주 같은 이슬로 향기 어린 그녀의 방에서 불러내니, 6월 초하루 그녀의 생일에, 활짝 핀 이 처녀가 헐렁한 옷차림으로 푸른 풀밭 위를 사뿐히 거닐면, 송이마다 꽃들이 일어나 그녀에게 경의를 표하고, 마침내 들판 전체가 칠보처럼 빛나며, 빛깔과 향기가 서로 그녀를 누가 더 사로잡을지 다투는 것이다."]], [["So charming may she now appear!", "그러니 그녀가 지금 이토록 매혹적으로 나타나리라!"], ["and you the feathered choristers of nature, whose sweetest notes not even Handel can excell, tune your melodious throats to celebrate her appearance.", "그리고 그대들, 헨델조차 능가하지 못할 가장 감미로운 가락을 지닌 자연의 깃털 입은 합창대여, 가락 고운 목청을 가다듬어 그녀의 등장을 노래하라."], ["From love proceeds your music, and to love it returns.", "그대들의 음악은 사랑에서 비롯되어, 다시 사랑으로 돌아간다."], ["Awaken therefore that gentle passion in every swain: for lo!", "그러니 모든 젊은 양치기의 가슴에 그 부드러운 정념을 일깨워라."], ["adorned with all the charms in which nature can array her; bedecked with beauty, youth, sprightliness, innocence, modesty, and tenderness, breathing sweetness from her rosy lips, and darting brightness from her sparkling eyes, the lovely Sophia comes!", "보라! 자연이 그녀를 꾸밀 수 있는 온갖 매력으로 단장하고, 아름다움과 젊음, 발랄함, 순수함, 정숙함, 그리고 다정함으로 치장한 채, 장밋빛 입술에서 감미로움을 내뿜고 반짝이는 두 눈에서 광채를 쏘아 보내며, 사랑스러운 소피아가 오고 있다!"]], [["Reader, perhaps thou hast seen the statue of the Venus de Medicis.", "독자여, 그대는 어쩌면 메디치의 비너스 조각상을 본 적이 있을지 모른다."], ["Perhaps, too, thou hast seen the gallery of beauties at Hampton Court.", "어쩌면 햄프턴 코트의 미녀 화랑도 보았을지 모른다."], ["Thou may'st remember each bright Churchill of the galaxy, and all the toasts of the Kit-cat.", "그대는 그 화려한 무리 가운데 빛나던 처칠가의 여인들을 하나하나 기억할 것이고, 키트캣 클럽에서 건배의 주인공이 된 미녀들도 모두 떠올릴 것이다."], ["Or, if their reign was before thy times, at least thou hast seen their daughters, the no less dazzling beauties of the present age; whose names, should we here insert, we apprehend they would fill the whole volume.", "혹 그들의 전성기가 그대의 시대보다 앞섰다면, 적어도 그들의 딸들, 다시 말해 그에 못지않게 눈부신 이 시대의 미녀들은 보았을 것이다. 그 이름들을 여기에 다 적는다면 책 한 권이 통째로 채워지지 않을까 싶다."]], [["Now if thou hast seen all these, be not afraid of the rude answer which Lord Rochester once gave to a man who had seen many things.", "자, 그대가 이들을 모두 보았다면, 일찍이 로체스터 경이 온갖 것을 다 보았다는 어떤 사내에게 던진 그 거친 대답 같은 것은 두려워하지 말라."], ["No.", "아니다."], ["If thou hast seen all these without knowing what beauty is, thou hast no eyes; if without feeling its power, thou hast no heart.", "만일 그대가 이들을 다 보고도 아름다움이 무엇인지 모른다면, 그대에겐 눈이 없는 것이다. 그 힘을 느끼지 못한 채 보았다면, 그대에겐 가슴이 없는 것이다."]], [["Yet is it possible, my friend, that thou mayest have seen all these without being able to form an exact idea of Sophia; for she did not exactly resemble any of them.", "하지만 벗이여, 그대가 이들을 모두 보았다 해도 소피아의 모습을 정확히 그려 내지는 못할 수도 있다. 그녀는 그 누구와도 똑같이 닮지는 않았기 때문이다."], ["She was most like the picture of Lady Ranelagh: and, I have heard, more still to the famous dutchess of Mazarine; but most of all she resembled one whose image never can depart from my breast, and whom, if thou dost remember, thou hast then, my friend, an adequate idea of Sophia.", "그녀는 래닐러 부인의 초상화와 가장 비슷했고, 내가 듣기로는 그 유명한 마자랭 공작부인과 더 닮았다고 한다. 그러나 무엇보다도, 그 모습이 결코 내 가슴에서 떠나지 않는 어떤 이를 가장 닮았으니, 벗이여, 그대가 그이를 기억한다면 그대는 소피아에 대한 마땅한 그림을 이미 지닌 셈이다."]], [["But lest this should not have been thy fortune, we will endeavour with our utmost skill to describe this paragon, though we are sensible that our highest abilities are very inadequate to the task.", "그러나 그런 행운이 그대에게 없었을 경우를 대비하여, 우리는 우리의 가장 뛰어난 솜씨로 이 본보기 같은 여인을 그려 내려 애써 보겠다. 비록 우리의 최고 역량으로도 이 일에는 턱없이 모자란다는 것을 잘 알고 있지만 말이다."]], [["Sophia, then, the only daughter of Mr Western, was a middle-sized woman; but rather inclining to tall.", "자, 웨스턴 씨의 외동딸인 소피아는 중간 키였으나, 오히려 큰 편에 가까웠다."], ["Her shape was not only exact, but extremely delicate: and the nice proportion of her arms promised the truest symmetry in her limbs.", "그녀의 몸매는 정확할 뿐 아니라 더없이 섬세했고, 균형 잡힌 두 팔은 사지의 더할 나위 없는 조화를 약속하고 있었다."], ["Her hair, which was black, was so luxuriant, that it reached her middle, before she cut it to comply with the modern fashion; and it was now curled so gracefully in her neck, that few could believe it to be her own.", "검은 머리카락은 어찌나 풍성했던지, 요즘 유행을 따르려 자르기 전에는 허리께까지 닿았다. 지금은 목덜미에서 어찌나 우아하게 곱슬져 있던지, 그것이 본래 그녀의 머리라고는 믿기 어려운 이가 많을 정도였다."], ["If envy could find any part of the face which demanded less commendation than the rest, it might possibly think her forehead might have been higher without prejudice to her.", "시기심이 그녀의 얼굴에서 다른 부분만큼 찬사받을 자격이 덜한 데를 찾으려 든다면, 어쩌면 이마가 조금 더 높았어도 흠이 되지 않았으리라 여길지 모른다."], ["Her eyebrows were full, even, and arched beyond the power of art to imitate.", "그녀의 두 눈썹은 짙고 고르며, 기교로는 흉내 낼 수 없을 만큼 활처럼 휘어 있었다."], ["Her black eyes had a lustre in them, which all her softness could not extinguish.", "검은 눈동자에는 광채가 어려 있어, 그 모든 부드러움으로도 그 빛을 끌 수 없었다."], ["Her nose was exactly regular, and her mouth, in which were two rows of ivory, exactly answered Sir John Suckling's description in those lines:--", "코는 더없이 반듯했고, 상아 같은 두 줄의 이가 들어찬 입은 존 서클링 경의 시구에 그려진 묘사와 정확히 들어맞았다. 그녀의 입술은 붉었고, 그 가운데 하나는"]], [["Her lips were red, and one was thin, Compar'd to that was next her chin. Some bee had stung it newly.", "턱에 가까운 아랫입술에 견주어 얇았으니,\n어느 벌이 갓 쏘아 놓은 듯하였다."]], [["Her cheeks were of the oval kind; and in her right she had a dimple, which the least smile discovered.", "그녀의 두 뺨은 갸름한 달걀형이었고, 오른쪽 뺨에는 보조개가 있어 아주 옅은 미소에도 드러났다."], ["Her chin had certainly its share in forming the beauty of her face; but it was difficult to say it was either large or small, though perhaps it was rather of the former kind.", "턱 역시 분명 그녀 얼굴의 아름다움을 이루는 데 제 몫을 했으나, 크다고도 작다고도 말하기 어려웠다. 다만 어쩌면 큰 편에 가까웠다고 할 수 있겠다."], ["Her complexion had rather more of the lily than of the rose; but when exercise or modesty increased her natural colour, no vermilion could equal it.", "그녀의 살빛은 장미보다는 백합 쪽에 가까웠다. 그러나 운동이나 수줍음으로 타고난 혈색이 짙어질 때면, 그 어떤 진홍빛도 그에 견줄 수 없었다."], ["Then one might indeed cry out with the celebrated Dr Donne:", "그럴 때면 우리는 정녕 저 이름난 던 박사처럼 외칠 만하다. ―그녀의 맑고도 웅변하는 피가"]], [["--Her pure and eloquent blood Spoke in her cheeks, and so distinctly wrought That one might almost say her body thought.", "두 뺨에서 말을 하니, 그 결이 어찌나 또렷하던지\n그녀의 몸이 생각을 한다고 말할 수 있을 정도였다."]], [["Her neck was long and finely turned: and here, if I was not afraid of offending her delicacy, I might justly say, the highest beauties of the famous Venus de Medicis were outdone.", "그녀의 목은 길고 곱게 빠졌다. 여기서, 그녀의 섬세함을 거스를까 두렵지만 않다면, 나는 저 유명한 메디치의 비너스가 지닌 최고의 아름다움마저 능가했다고 떳떳이 말할 수 있으리라."], ["Here was whiteness which no lilies, ivory, nor alabaster could match.", "거기엔 어떤 백합도, 상아도, 설화석고도 견줄 수 없는 흰빛이 있었다."], ["The finest cambric might indeed be supposed from envy to cover that bosom which was much whiter than itself.--It was indeed,", "더없이 고운 무명천조차 시기심에서 자기보다 훨씬 흰 그 가슴을 가리려 든다고 여길 만했다. 그것은 실로,"]], [["Nitor splendens Pario marmore purius.", "니토르 스플렌덴스 파리오 마르모레 푸리우스."]], [["A gloss shining beyond the purest brightness of Parian marble.", "파로스 대리석의 가장 순결한 빛마저 넘어서는 광택."]], [["Such was the outside of Sophia; nor was this beautiful frame disgraced by an inhabitant unworthy of it.", "이것이 소피아의 겉모습이었다. 그리고 이 아름다운 틀은 그에 어울리지 않는 자가 깃들어 그 품격을 떨어뜨리는 일도 없었다."], ["Her mind was every way equal to her person; nay, the latter borrowed some charms from the former; for when she smiled, the sweetness of her temper diffused that glory over her countenance which no regularity of features can give.", "그녀의 마음은 모든 면에서 그 몸에 견줄 만했다. 아니, 몸은 오히려 마음에서 얼마간 매력을 빌려 왔으니, 그녀가 미소 지을 때면 그 온화한 성품의 감미로움이 얼굴 위로 영광을 퍼뜨려, 아무리 반듯한 이목구비로도 줄 수 없는 빛을 더했기 때문이다."], ["But as there are no perfections of the mind which do not discover themselves in that perfect intimacy to which we intend to introduce our reader with this charming young creature, so it is needless to mention them here: nay, it is a kind of tacit affront to our reader's understanding, and may also rob him of that pleasure which he will receive in forming his own judgment of her character.", "그러나 우리가 이 매혹적인 젊은 여인을 통해 독자를 이끌어 들이려는 그 완전한 친밀함 속에서 절로 드러나지 않을 마음의 미덕이란 없으니, 여기서 그것들을 일일이 언급할 필요는 없다. 아니, 그렇게 하는 것은 독자의 이해력에 대한 일종의 은근한 모욕이며, 또한 독자가 그녀의 성격을 스스로 판단하며 누릴 그 즐거움을 빼앗는 일이 될 수도 있다."]], [["It may, however, be proper to say, that whatever mental accomplishments she had derived from nature, they were somewhat improved and cultivated by art: for she had been educated under the care of an aunt, who was a lady of great discretion, and was thoroughly acquainted with the world, having lived in her youth about the court, whence she had retired some years since into the country. By her conversation and instructions, Sophia was perfectly well bred, though perhaps she wanted a little of that ease in her behaviour which is to be acquired only by habit, and living within what is called the polite circle. But this, to say the truth, is often too dearly purchased; and though it hath charms so inexpressible, that the French, perhaps, among other qualities, mean to express this, when they declare they know not what it is; yet its absence is well compensated by innocence; nor can good sense and a natural gentility ever stand in need of it.", "다만 이것만은 말해 두는 편이 좋겠다. 그녀가 천성에서 물려받은 어떤 정신적 자질이 무엇이든, 그것은 기예에 의해 어느 정도 다듬어지고 가꾸어졌다는 점이다. 그녀는 한 이모의 보살핌 아래 자랐는데, 이 부인은 분별이 깊은 데다 세상 물정에 두루 밝았으니, 젊은 시절 궁정 언저리에서 지내다가 몇 해 전 시골로 물러난 분이었다. 이모의 대화와 가르침 덕분에 소피아는 더없이 훌륭한 교양을 갖추게 되었다. 다만 몸가짐에서는, 오직 습관과 이른바 사교계 안에서의 생활을 통해서만 얻을 수 있는 그 여유로움이 어쩌면 조금 부족했을지 모른다. 그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 그런 여유로움은 흔히 너무 비싼 값을 치르고 얻는 것이다. 또 그것은 이루 말로 다 할 수 없는 매력을 지녀서, 어쩌면 프랑스인들이 다른 여러 자질과 더불어 \"그것이 무엇인지 우리도 모르겠다\"고 단언할 때 표현하려는 것이 바로 이것인지도 모른다. 그렇지만 그것이 없더라도 순수함이 충분히 그 자리를 메워 주며, 또 분별 있는 양식과 타고난 기품이 있는 이라면 그것이 결코 필요치 않다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["Wherein the history goes back to commemorate a trifling incident that happened some years since; but which, trifling as it was, had some future consequences.", "3장. 이야기가 잠시 거슬러 올라가, 몇 해 전에 일어난 사소한 사건 하나를 되짚는다. 비록 사소하기 짝이 없었으나, 훗날 적잖은 결과를 낳은 일이다."]], [["The amiable Sophia was now in her eighteenth year, when she is introduced into this history.", "사랑스러운 소피아가 이 이야기에 등장하는 지금, 그녀는 열여덟 살이 되어 있었다."], ["Her father, as hath been said, was fonder of her than of any other human creature.", "앞서 말했듯, 그녀의 아버지는 이 세상 그 누구보다 그녀를 아꼈다."], ["To her, therefore, Tom Jones applied, in order to engage her interest on the behalf of his friend the gamekeeper.", "그래서 톰 존스는 자기 벗인 사냥터지기를 위해 그녀의 마음을 얻으려고 그녀를 찾아갔다."]], [["But before we proceed to this business, a short recapitulation of some previous matters may be necessary.", "하지만 이 일로 넘어가기 전에, 앞서 있었던 몇 가지 일을 짧게 되짚어 두는 편이 좋겠다."]], [["Though the different tempers of Mr Allworthy and of Mr Western did not admit of a very intimate correspondence, yet they lived upon what is called a decent footing together; by which means the young people of both families had been acquainted from their infancy; and as they were all near of the same age, had been frequent playmates together.", "올워디 씨와 웨스턴 씨는 기질이 서로 달라 그리 가까운 왕래까지는 없었으나, 그래도 점잖다 할 만한 사이로는 지내고 있었다. 그 덕분에 두 집안의 아이들은 어릴 적부터 서로 알고 지냈고, 모두 또래였던 터라 함께 자주 어울려 놀던 사이였다."]], [["The gaiety of Tom's temper suited better with Sophia, than the grave and sober disposition of Master Blifil.", "쾌활한 톰의 기질은, 진중하고 무던한 블리필 도련님의 성품보다 소피아와 더 잘 맞았다."], ["And the preference which she gave the former of these, would often appear so plainly, that a lad of a more passionate turn than Master Blifil was, might have shown some displeasure at it.", "그리고 그녀가 두 사람 가운데 톰을 더 좋아한다는 사실은 자주 너무도 또렷하게 드러나서, 블리필 도련님보다 성정이 격한 소년이었다면 그에 대해 얼마간 언짢음을 내비쳤을 법도 했다."]], [["As he did not, however, outwardly express any such disgust, it would be an ill office in us to pay a visit to the inmost recesses of his mind, as some scandalous people search into the most secret affairs of their friends, and often pry into their closets and cupboards, only to discover their poverty and meanness to the world.", "그러나 그는 그런 불쾌함을 겉으로 드러내지 않았으니, 우리가 그의 마음 가장 깊은 구석까지 들여다보려 드는 것은 못된 짓이 될 것이다. 마치 어떤 입방아 찧기 좋아하는 자들이 벗의 가장 은밀한 일까지 캐고 다니며, 오직 그 가난과 옹졸함을 세상에 까발리려고 옷장과 찬장까지 들춰 보는 것처럼 말이다."]], [["However, as persons who suspect they have given others cause of offence, are apt to conclude they are offended; so Sophia imputed an action of Master Blifil to his anger, which the superior sagacity of Thwackum and Square discerned to have arisen from a much better principle.", "하지만 남에게 노여움을 살 빌미를 주었다고 의심하는 사람일수록 상대가 화가 났으리라 지레짐작하기 마련이다. 그래서 소피아도 블리필 도련님의 어떤 행동을 그의 노여움 탓으로 돌렸으나, 트와컴과 스퀘어의 더 뛰어난 통찰력은 그 행동이 훨씬 더 갸륵한 원리에서 비롯되었다고 보았다."]], [["Tom Jones, when very young, had presented Sophia with a little bird, which he had taken from the nest, had nursed up, and taught to sing.", "톰 존스는 아주 어렸을 적에 둥지에서 꺼내 직접 길러 노래까지 가르친 작은 새 한 마리를 소피아에게 선물한 적이 있었다."]], [["Of this bird, Sophia, then about thirteen years old, was so extremely fond, that her chief business was to feed and tend it, and her chief pleasure to play with it.", "그때 열세 살쯤이던 소피아는 이 새를 어찌나 끔찍이 아꼈던지, 모이를 주고 보살피는 것이 그녀의 가장 큰 일이요, 함께 노는 것이 가장 큰 즐거움이었다. 그렇게 지내다 보니 어린 토미는 — 그 새의 이름이 그러했다 — 아주 길이 잘 들어서, 안주인의 손에서 모이를 받아먹고, 손가락에 내려앉으며, 그녀의 품에 안겨 만족스레 누워 있곤 했다."], ["By these means little Tommy, for so the bird was called, was become so tame, that it would feed out of the hand of its mistress, would perch upon the finger, and lie contented in her bosom, where it seemed almost sensible of its own happiness; though she always kept a small string about its leg, nor would ever trust it with the liberty of flying away.", "마치 제 행복을 거의 알아차리기라도 하는 듯했다. 다만 그녀는 늘 새의 다리에 가느다란 끈을 매어 두었고, 날아가 버릴 자유만큼은 결코 허락하지 않았다."]], [["One day, when Mr Allworthy and his whole family dined at Mr Western's, Master Blifil, being in the garden with little Sophia, and observing the extreme fondness that she showed for her little bird, desired her to trust it for a moment in his hands.", "어느 날, 올워디 씨가 온 식구를 데리고 웨스턴 씨 댁에서 만찬을 들 때였다. 블리필 도련님은 어린 소피아와 정원에 있다가, 그녀가 작은 새를 끔찍이 아끼는 것을 보고는 잠깐만 새를 자기 손에 맡겨 달라고 청했다."], ["Sophia presently complied with the young gentleman's request, and after some previous caution, delivered him her bird; of which he was no sooner in possession, than he slipt the string from its leg and tossed it into the air.", "소피아는 곧바로 도련님의 청을 들어주었고, 미리 몇 마디 당부를 한 뒤 새를 건넸다. 그런데 그는 새를 손에 쥐자마자 다리에 매인 끈을 풀어 공중으로 휙 날려 보냈다."]], [["The foolish animal no sooner perceived itself at liberty, than forgetting all the favours it had received from Sophia, it flew directly from her, and perched on a bough at some distance.", "그 어리석은 짐승은 제가 자유로워진 것을 알아채자마자, 소피아에게서 받은 온갖 은혜를 다 잊고 곧장 그녀에게서 날아가 조금 떨어진 나뭇가지에 내려앉았다."]], [["Sophia, seeing her bird gone, screamed out so loud, that Tom Jones, who was at a little distance, immediately ran to her assistance.", "새가 날아가 버린 것을 본 소피아가 어찌나 크게 비명을 질렀던지, 멀지 않은 곳에 있던 톰 존스가 곧장 그녀를 도우러 달려왔다."]], [["He was no sooner informed of what had happened, than he cursed Blifil for a pitiful malicious rascal; and then immediately stripping off his coat he applied himself to climbing the tree to which the bird escaped.", "그는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 듣자마자 블리필을 두고 비열하고 심술궂은 악당이라며 욕을 퍼부었고, 곧바로 웃옷을 벗어 던지고는 새가 달아난 나무로 기어오르기 시작했다."]], [["Tom had almost recovered his little namesake, when the branch on which it was perched, and that hung over a canal, broke, and the poor lad plumped over head and ears into the water.", "톰이 제 이름을 딴 그 작은 새를 거의 다 붙잡았을 무렵, 새가 앉아 있던 가지가 — 그것이 운하 위로 뻗어 있었는데 — 그만 부러지는 바람에, 가엾은 소년은 머리끝까지 물속으로 풍덩 빠지고 말았다."]], [["Sophia's concern now changed its object.", "이제 소피아의 걱정은 대상을 바꾸었다."], ["And as she apprehended the boy's life was in danger, she screamed ten times louder than before; and indeed Master Blifil himself now seconded her with all the vociferation in his power.", "소년의 목숨이 위태롭다고 여긴 그녀는 아까보다 열 배는 더 크게 비명을 질렀다. 그리고 정말로, 이번에는 블리필 도련님 자신도 있는 힘껏 목청을 보태어 함께 소리쳤다."]], [["The company, who were sitting in a room next the garden, were instantly alarmed, and came all forth; but just as they reached the canal, Tom (for the water was luckily pretty shallow in that part) arrived safely on shore.", "정원 옆방에 앉아 있던 사람들이 곧장 놀라 모두 뛰쳐나왔다. 그러나 그들이 운하에 막 다다랐을 때, 톰은 — 다행히 그 부분의 물이 꽤 얕았던 덕분에 — 무사히 뭍으로 올라온 참이었다."]], [["Thwackum fell violently on poor Tom, who stood dropping and shivering before him, when Mr Allworthy desired him to have patience; and turning to Master Blifil, said, “Pray, child, what is the reason of all this disturbance?”", "트와컴이 물을 뚝뚝 흘리며 그 앞에서 덜덜 떨고 선 가엾은 톰에게 사납게 달려들려 하자, 올워디 씨가 좀 참으라고 이른 뒤 블리필 도련님 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"얘야, 이 소동이 다 어찌 된 영문인지 말해 보아라.\""]], [["Master Blifil answered, “Indeed, uncle, I am very sorry for what I have done; I have been unhappily the occasion of it all. I had Miss Sophia's bird in my hand, and thinking the poor creature languished for liberty, I own I could not forbear giving it what it desired; for I always thought there was something very cruel in confining anything. It seemed to be against the law of nature, by which everything hath a right to liberty; nay, it is even unchristian, for it is not doing what we would be done by; but if I had imagined Miss Sophia would have been so much concerned at it, I am sure I never would have done it; nay, if I had known what would have happened to the bird itself: for when Master Jones, who climbed up that tree after it, fell into the water, the bird took a second flight, and presently a nasty hawk carried it away.”", "블리필 도련님이 대답했다. \"정말이지 외삼촌, 제가 한 일이 너무도 후회됩니다. 불행히도 제가 이 모든 일의 빌미가 되고 말았어요. 제 손에 소피아 양의 새가 있었는데, 그 가엾은 것이 자유를 그리워하며 시들어 가는 것 같아서, 솔직히 그것이 바라는 바를 주지 않을 수가 없었습니다. 저는 무언가를 가두는 데에는 늘 몹시 잔인한 구석이 있다고 여겨 왔거든요. 그것은 만물이 자유에 대한 권리를 지니는 자연의 법칙에 어긋나는 일처럼 보였습니다. 아니, 그것은 그리스도인답지 않은 일이기까지 합니다. 우리가 남에게 대접받고자 하는 대로 남을 대접하는 일이 아니니까요. 하지만 소피아 양이 이토록 마음 아파할 줄 알았더라면, 결단코 그러지 않았을 겁니다. 아니, 새 자신에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지 알았더라면요. 존스 도련님이 새를 잡으러 저 나무에 올라갔다가 물에 빠지자, 새는 다시 한 번 날아올랐고, 곧바로 흉측한 매 한 마리가 그것을 채 가 버렸으니까요.\""]], [["Poor Sophia, who now first heard of her little Tommy's fate (for her concern for Jones had prevented her perceiving it when it happened), shed a shower of tears.", "이제야 어린 토미의 운명을 처음 듣게 된 가엾은 소피아는 — 존스를 걱정하느라 그 일이 벌어졌을 때는 미처 알아채지 못했던 것이다 — 눈물을 비 오듯 쏟았다."], ["These Mr Allworthy endeavoured to assuage, promising her a much finer bird: but she declared she would never have another.", "올워디 씨는 훨씬 더 고운 새를 구해 주겠다고 약속하며 그 눈물을 달래려 했으나, 그녀는 다시는 다른 새를 갖지 않겠다고 잘라 말했다."], ["Her father chid her for crying so for a foolish bird; but could not help telling young Blifil, if he was a son of his, his backside should be well flead.", "그녀의 아버지는 한낱 어리석은 새 따위로 그리 운다고 딸을 나무라면서도, 어린 블리필을 향해 만일 네가 내 아들이었다면 엉덩이 가죽이 벗겨지도록 흠씬 두들겨 맞았을 거라는 말만큼은 끝내 참지 못했다."]], [["Sophia now returned to her chamber, the two young gentlemen were sent home, and the rest of the company returned to their bottle; where a conversation ensued on the subject of the bird, so curious, that we think it deserves a chapter by itself.", "이윽고 소피아는 자기 방으로 돌아갔고, 두 도련님은 집으로 보내졌으며, 나머지 사람들은 다시 술병 앞으로 돌아왔다. 거기서 그 새를 두고 대화가 오갔는데, 그것이 어찌나 흥미로웠던지 우리는 따로 한 장을 할애할 만하다고 여기는 바이다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["Containing such very deep and grave matters, that some readers, perhaps, may not relish it.", "스퀘어는 파이프에 불을 붙이기가 무섭게 올워디 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 이렇게 말문을 열었다."], ["Square had no sooner lighted his pipe, than, addressing himself to Allworthy, he thus began: “Sir, I cannot help congratulating you on your nephew; who, at an age when few lads have any ideas but of sensible objects, is arrived at a capacity of distinguishing right from wrong.", "\"선생, 댁의 조카를 두고 축하의 말씀을 드리지 않을 수가 없군요. 또래의 아이들 대부분이 감각으로 느낄 수 있는 것들 말고는 아무 관념도 없을 나이에, 그 아이는 벌써 옳고 그름을 분별하는 능력에 이르렀으니 말입니다."], ["To confine anything, seems to me against the law of nature, by which everything hath a right to liberty.", "무엇이든 가두어 두는 것은 제가 보기에 자연의 법칙에 어긋나는 일입니다. 그 법칙에 따르면 모든 것은 자유를 누릴 권리를 지니고 있지요."], ["These were his words; and the impression they have made on me is never to be eradicated.", "이것이 그 아이가 한 말입니다. 그 말이 제게 남긴 인상은 결코 지워지지 않을 것입니다."], ["Can any man have a higher notion of the rule of right, and the eternal fitness of things?", "어느 누가 옳음의 법칙과 사물의 영원한 합당함에 대해 이보다 더 높은 관념을 품을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["I cannot help promising myself, from such a dawn, that the meridian of this youth will be equal to that of either the elder or the younger Brutus.”", "저는 이런 새벽빛을 보고서, 이 젊은이의 한낮이 형 브루투스든 동생 브루투스든 그 어느 쪽에도 못지않으리라 자신하지 않을 수 없습니다.\""]], [["Here Thwackum hastily interrupted, and spilling some of his wine, and swallowing the rest with great eagerness, answered, “From another expression he made use of, I hope he will resemble much better men.", "여기서 스웨컴이 황급히 끼어들어, 포도주를 조금 흘리고 나머지는 단숨에 들이켜고는 대답했다. \"그 아이가 쓴 또 다른 표현으로 미루어, 저는 그 아이가 훨씬 더 훌륭한 사람들을 닮기를 바랍니다."], ["The law of nature is a jargon of words, which means nothing.", "자연의 법칙이란 아무 뜻도 없는 말장난에 지나지 않습니다."], ["I know not of any such law, nor of any right which can be derived from it.", "저는 그런 법칙도, 거기서 끌어낼 수 있는 어떤 권리도 알지 못합니다."], ["To do as we would be done by, is indeed a Christian motive, as the boy well expressed himself; and I am glad to find my instructions have borne such good fruit.”", "남이 내게 해주기를 바라는 대로 남에게 행하라는 것이야말로 참으로 기독교의 동기이며, 그 아이도 그것을 훌륭히 말로 옮겼지요. 제 가르침이 이토록 좋은 열매를 맺은 것을 확인하니 기쁩니다.\""]], [["“If vanity was a thing fit,”", "\"허영이 합당한 것이라면\" 하고 스퀘어가 말했다."], ["says Square, “I might indulge some on the same occasion; for whence only he can have learnt his notions of right or wrong, I think is pretty apparent.", "\"저도 같은 일을 두고 어느 정도 허영을 부려도 좋을 듯합니다. 그 아이가 옳고 그름에 관한 관념을 대체 어디서 배웠는지는 꽤 분명해 보이니까요."], ["If there be no law of nature, there is no right nor wrong.”", "자연의 법칙이 없다면, 옳음도 그름도 없습니다.\""]], [["“How!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["says the parson, “do you then banish revelation?", "하고 목사가 말했다. \"그러면 댁은 계시를 내쳐 버리는 겁니까?"], ["Am I talking with a deist or an atheist?”", "내가 지금 이신론자나 무신론자와 이야기하고 있는 것입니까?\""]], [["“Drink about,”", "\"한 잔씩들 드시오.\""], ["says Western.", "하고 웨스턴이 말했다."], ["“Pox of your laws of nature!", "\"당신네 자연의 법칙 따위 염병할!"], ["I don't know what you mean, either of you, by right and wrong.", "옳음이니 그름이니 하는 게 두 분 다 무슨 소린지 나는 도무지 모르겠소."], ["To take away my girl's bird was wrong, in my opinion; and my neighbour Allworthy may do as he pleases; but to encourage boys in such practices, is to breed them up to the gallows.”", "내 딸의 새를 빼앗아 간 건 내 생각에는 잘못된 일이오. 이웃인 올워디 양반이야 마음대로 하시면 그만이지만, 사내아이들에게 그런 짓을 부추기는 건 그놈들을 교수대로 키우는 것이나 다름없소.\""]], [["Allworthy answered, “That he was sorry for what his nephew had done, but could not consent to punish him, as he acted rather from a generous than unworthy motive.”", "올워디는 이렇게 대답했다. 조카가 한 일에 대해서는 자기도 유감스럽게 여기지만, 그 아이가 비열한 동기에서가 아니라 오히려 너그러운 동기에서 한 일이니 벌하는 데에는 동의할 수 없다고."], ["He said, “If the boy had stolen the bird, none would have been more ready to vote for a severe chastisement than himself; but it was plain that was not his design:”", "그는 말했다. \"만일 그 아이가 새를 훔쳤다면 저보다 더 앞장서서 엄한 매질에 찬성할 사람은 없었을 겁니다."], ["and, indeed, it was as apparent to him, that he could have no other view but what he had himself avowed.", "하지만 그것이 그 아이의 의도가 아니었음은 분명합니다.\" 실로 그에게는, 그 아이가 스스로 고백한 것 말고는 다른 어떤 속셈도 있을 수 없다는 것이 자명해 보였다."], ["(For as to that malicious purpose which Sophia suspected, it never once entered into the head of Mr Allworthy.) He at length concluded with again blaming the action as inconsiderate, and which, he said, was pardonable only in a child.", "(소피아가 의심했던 그 악의에 찬 목적은 올워디의 머릿속에 단 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었던 것이다.) 그는 마침내 그 행동을 다시 한번 경솔했다고 나무라며 말을 맺었는데, 그런 일은 아이에게서나 너그러이 보아 넘길 수 있는 일이라고 했다."]], [["Square had delivered his opinion so openly, that if he was now silent, he must submit to have his judgment censured.", "스퀘어는 자기 의견을 워낙 거리낌 없이 내놓았던 터라, 이제 와서 잠자코 있다가는 자신의 판단이 비난받는 것을 감수해야 할 처지였다."], ["He said, therefore, with some warmth, “That Mr Allworthy had too much respect to the dirty consideration of property.", "그래서 그는 다소 열을 올리며 말했다. \"올워디 선생은 재산이라는 그 천박한 고려에 너무 무게를 두십니다."], ["That in passing our judgments on great and mighty actions, all private regards should be laid aside; for by adhering to those narrow rules, the younger Brutus had been condemned of ingratitude, and the elder of parricide.”", "위대하고 장엄한 행위를 두고 판단을 내릴 때에는 모든 사사로운 고려를 한쪽으로 치워 두어야 마땅합니다. 그런 옹졸한 규칙에 매달린 탓에 동생 브루투스는 배은망덕하다 단죄받았고, 형 브루투스는 친족 살해범으로 몰렸으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“And if they had been hanged too for those crimes,”", "\"그런 죄로 두 사람이 교수형을 당했더라도\""], ["cried Thwackum, “they would have had no more than their deserts.", "하고 스웨컴이 외쳤다. \"그건 제 죗값을 받은 것에 지나지 않았을 겁니다."], ["A couple of heathenish villains!", "한 쌍의 이교도 악당들이지요!"], ["Heaven be praised we have no Brutuses now-a-days!", "요즘 세상에 브루투스 같은 자들이 없는 것을 하늘에 감사할 따름입니다!"], ["I wish, Mr Square, you would desist from filling the minds of my pupils with such antichristian stuff; for the consequence must be, while they are under my care, its being well scourged out of them again.", "스퀘어 선생, 제발 제 제자들의 머릿속을 그런 반기독교적인 것들로 채우는 일은 그만두시면 좋겠습니다. 그 결과란, 그 아이들이 제 손아래 있는 한, 그것을 다시 호되게 매질로 뽑아내는 일이 될 수밖에 없으니까요."], ["There is your disciple Tom almost spoiled already.", "댁의 제자 톰만 해도 벌써 거의 망쳐졌습니다."], ["I overheard him the other day disputing with Master Blifil that there was no merit in faith without works.", "일전에 그 아이가 블리필 도련님과 다투면서, 행위가 따르지 않는 믿음에는 아무 공덕이 없다고 하는 것을 제가 엿들었습니다."], ["I know that is one of your tenets, and I suppose he had it from you.”", "그게 댁의 신조 가운데 하나인 줄 알고 있으며, 그 아이도 댁에게서 배운 것이리라 짐작합니다.\""]], [["“Don't accuse me of spoiling him,”", "\"그 아이를 망쳤다고 저를 탓하지 마십시오.\""], ["says Square.", "하고 스퀘어가 말했다."], ["“Who taught him to laugh at whatever is virtuous and decent, and fit and right in the nature of things?", "\"덕스럽고 점잖으며, 사물의 본성에 합당하고 옳은 것이라면 무엇이든 비웃도록 그 아이를 가르친 게 누굽니까?"], ["He is your own scholar, and I disclaim him.", "그 아이는 댁이 가르친 학생이니, 저는 그 아이와 인연을 끊겠습니다."], ["No, no, Master Blifil is my boy.", "아니지요, 아닙니다. 블리필 도련님이야말로 제 아이입니다."], ["Young as he is, that lad's notions of moral rectitude I defy you ever to eradicate.”", "어린 나이임에도 그 아이가 지닌 도덕적 올바름의 관념만큼은, 댁이 결코 뽑아낼 수 없으리라 장담합니다.\""]], [["Thwackum put on a contemptuous sneer at this, and replied, “Ay, ay, I will venture him with you.", "스웨컴은 이 말에 경멸 어린 비웃음을 띠며 대꾸했다. \"암, 그렇고말고요."], ["He is too well grounded for all your philosophical cant to hurt.", "그 아이라면 댁에게 맡겨 봐도 괜찮습니다. 댁의 그 온갖 철학적 헛소리로도 해칠 수 없을 만큼 단단히 다져진 아이니까요."], ["No, no, I have taken care to instil such principles into him--”", "아니지요, 아닙니다. 저는 그 아이에게 그런 원칙들을 단단히 새겨 넣어 두는 데 정성을 들였단 말입니다—\""]], [["“And I have instilled principles into him too,”", "\"저도 그 아이에게 원칙들을 새겨 넣었습니다.\""], ["cries Square.", "하고 스퀘어가 외쳤다."], ["“What but the sublime idea of virtue could inspire a human mind with the generous thought of giving liberty?", "\"자유를 베푼다는 너그러운 생각을 사람의 마음속에 불어넣을 수 있는 것이, 덕이라는 숭고한 관념 말고 또 무엇이 있겠습니까?"], ["And I repeat to you again, if it was a fit thing to be proud, I might claim the honour of having infused that idea.”--", "다시 한번 말씀드리지만, 자랑하는 것이 합당한 일이라면 저는 그 관념을 불어넣은 영예를 제 것이라 주장할 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“And if pride was not forbidden,”", "\"그리고 자랑이 금지된 것이 아니라면\""], ["said Thwackum, “I might boast of having taught him that duty which he himself assigned as his motive.”", "하고 스웨컴이 말했다. \"저는 그 아이가 스스로 자기 동기로 내세운 바로 그 의무를 가르쳐 준 일을 뽐낼 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["“So between you both,”", "\"그러니 두 분 덕에\""], ["says the squire, “the young gentleman hath been taught to rob my daughter of her bird.", "하고 향사가 말했다. \"그 도련님이 내 딸의 새를 훔치는 법을 배운 셈이로군."], ["I find I must take care of my partridge-mew.", "아무래도 내 자고새 사육장을 단속해야겠소."], ["I shall have some virtuous religious man or other set all my partridges at liberty.”", "어떤 덕스럽고 신앙심 깊은 양반이 와서 내 자고새들을 죄다 풀어놓을 판이니 말이오.\""], ["Then slapping a gentleman of the law, who was present, on the back, he cried out, “What say you to this, Mr Counsellor?", "그러고는 그 자리에 있던 법률가 한 사람의 등을 철썩 치며 외쳤다. \"이 일에 대해 어찌 생각하시오, 변호사 양반?"], ["Is not this against law?”", "이건 법에 어긋나는 일이 아니오?\""]], [["The lawyer with great gravity delivered himself as follows:--", "법률가는 자못 엄숙하게 다음과 같이 견해를 밝혔다."]], [["“If the case be put of a partridge, there can be no doubt but an action would lie; for though this be ferae naturae, yet being reclaimed, property vests: but being the case of a singing bird, though reclaimed, as it is a thing of base nature, it must be considered as nullius in bonis.", "\"만일 자고새의 경우라면, 소송이 성립하리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없습니다. 이는 본래 야생의 것이긴 하나, 길들여졌으니 소유권이 귀속되기 때문이지요."], ["In this case, therefore, I conceive the plaintiff must be non-suited; and I should disadvise the bringing any such action.”", "그러나 노래하는 새의 경우라면, 비록 길들여졌다 하더라도 천한 본성을 지닌 것이므로 누구의 소유물도 아닌 것으로 보아야 합니다. 따라서 이 경우 원고는 패소할 수밖에 없다고 사료되며, 저로서는 그러한 소송을 제기하지 않으시기를 권하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋소,\" 하고 향사가 말했다."], ["says the squire, “if it be nullus bonus, let us drink about, and talk a little of the state of the nation, or some such discourse that we all understand; for I am sure I don't understand a word of this.", "\"그것이 누구의 것도 아니라면, 한 잔씩들 들면서 나라 형편이나 우리 모두가 알아들을 만한 그런 이야기나 좀 나눕시다. 나는 이 말은 한 마디도 못 알아듣겠으니 말이오."], ["It may be learning and sense for aught I know: but you shall never persuade me into it.", "내가 모르는 학식이고 분별일 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 댁들이 나를 거기에 끌어들이지는 못할 거요."], ["Pox!", "염병할!"], ["you have neither of you mentioned a word of that poor lad who deserves to be commended: to venture breaking his neck to oblige my girl was a generous-spirited action: I have learning enough to see that.", "두 분 다 칭찬받아 마땅한 그 가엾은 아이에 대해서는 한 마디도 꺼내지 않으셨소. 내 딸의 비위를 맞추려고 제 목이 부러질 위험을 무릅쓴 건 너그러운 기상에서 나온 행동이오."], ["D--n me, here's Tom's health!", "그 정도는 나도 알아볼 학식은 되오."], ["I shall love the boy for it the longest day I have to live.”", "빌어먹을, 자, 톰의 건강을 위하여! 나는 살아 있는 마지막 날까지 그 일로 그 아이를 아낄 거요.\""]], [["Thus was the debate interrupted; but it would probably have been soon resumed, had not Mr Allworthy presently called for his coach, and carried off the two combatants.", "이렇게 해서 그 논쟁은 중단되었다. 하지만 올워디가 곧바로 마차를 부르며 두 논객을 데리고 가지 않았더라면 아마 머지않아 다시 이어졌을 것이다."]], [["Such was the conclusion of this adventure of the bird, and of the dialogue occasioned by it; which we could not help recounting to our reader, though it happened some years before that stage or period of time at which our history is now arrived.", "새를 둘러싼 이 사건과 그로 말미암아 벌어진 대화는 이렇게 끝을 맺었다. 비록 이 일이 우리 이야기가 지금 이른 그 단계나 시점보다 몇 해 전에 일어난 일이긴 하나, 우리는 이를 독자 여러분께 들려드리지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Containing matter accommodated to every taste.", "모든 취향에 두루 맞는 이야기를 담다."]], [["“Parva leves capiunt animos--Small things affect light minds,” was the sentiment of a great master of the passion of love.", "“Parva leves capiunt animos—사소한 것들이 가벼운 마음을 사로잡는다”라는 말은, 사랑이라는 정념을 다루는 어느 위대한 거장의 생각이었다."], ["And certain it is, that from this day Sophia began to have some little kindness for Tom Jones, and no little aversion for his companion.", "그리고 분명한 사실은, 이날부터 소피아가 톰 존스에게 작은 호의를 품기 시작했고, 그의 동무에게는 결코 작지 않은 반감을 갖게 되었다는 것이다."]], [["Many accidents from time to time improved both these passions in her breast; which, without our recounting, the reader may well conclude, from what we have before hinted of the different tempers of these lads, and how much the one suited with her own inclinations more than the other. To say the truth, Sophia, when very young, discerned that Tom, though an idle, thoughtless, rattling rascal, was nobody's enemy but his own; and that Master Blifil, though a prudent, discreet, sober young gentleman, was at the same time strongly attached to the interest only of one single person; and who that single person was the reader will be able to divine without any assistance of ours.", "여러 가지 일들이 때때로 그녀 가슴속에서 이 두 감정을 모두 키워 갔다. 굳이 우리가 일일이 들려주지 않더라도, 앞서 이 두 소년의 서로 다른 기질에 대해, 그리고 그중 하나가 다른 하나보다 얼마나 더 그녀 자신의 성향에 들어맞았는지에 대해 넌지시 일러둔 바를 떠올리면, 독자는 능히 짐작할 수 있을 것이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 소피아는 아주 어린 나이에 이미 알아챘다. 톰은 비록 게으르고 생각 없고 수다스러운 개구쟁이였지만 자기 자신 말고는 누구의 적도 아니었고, 블리필 도련님은 비록 신중하고 분별 있고 진중한 젊은 신사였지만 동시에 오직 단 한 사람의 이익에만 강하게 매여 있었다는 것을. 그 한 사람이 누구인지는, 우리가 거들지 않아도 독자가 능히 짐작해 낼 수 있으리라."]], [["These two characters are not always received in the world with the different regard which seems severally due to either; and which one would imagine mankind, from self-interest, should show towards them.", "이 두 부류의 사람은, 저마다 응당 받아 마땅해 보이는, 그리고 인류가 제 잇속을 따져서라도 베풀 법한 그 서로 다른 대우를 세상에서 늘 받는 것은 아니다."], ["But perhaps there may be a political reason for it: in finding one of a truly benevolent disposition, men may very reasonably suppose they have found a treasure, and be desirous of keeping it, like all other good things, to themselves.", "하지만 거기에는 어쩌면 어떤 영리한 까닭이 있는지도 모른다. 사람들은 진정으로 자애로운 성품의 사람을 하나 발견하면, 보물을 찾았다고 여기는 것도 무리가 아니어서, 다른 온갖 좋은 것들과 마찬가지로 그것을 자기들끼리만 간직하고 싶어 할 법하다."], ["Hence they may imagine, that to trumpet forth the praises of such a person, would, in the vulgar phrase, be crying Roast-meat, and calling in partakers of what they intend to apply solely to their own use.", "그래서 그들은, 그런 사람의 미덕을 나팔 불어 떠벌리는 일이 흔한 말로 하자면 \"고기 구워졌다고 외쳐\" 자기들만 쓰려고 마음먹은 것에 동참할 이들을 불러 모으는 격이라고 여길지도 모른다."], ["If this reason does not satisfy the reader, I know no other means of accounting for the little respect which I have commonly seen paid to a character which really does great honour to human nature, and is productive of the highest good to society.", "만일 이 설명이 독자를 흡족하게 하지 못한다면, 나로서는 인간 본성에 참으로 큰 영광을 안기고 사회에 더없이 큰 이로움을 낳는 그런 성품에, 내가 흔히 보아 온 대로 어찌 그리 작은 존경밖에 돌아가지 않는지를 달리 설명할 길을 알지 못한다."], ["But it was otherwise with Sophia.", "그러나 소피아는 달랐다."], ["She honoured Tom Jones, and scorned Master Blifil, almost as soon as she knew the meaning of those two words.", "그녀는 그 두 단어의 뜻을 알게 되자마자 거의 곧바로 톰 존스를 우러르고 블리필 도련님을 업신여겼다."]], [["Sophia had been absent upwards of three years with her aunt; during all which time she had seldom seen either of these young gentlemen.", "소피아는 삼 년 남짓 고모와 함께 떨어져 지냈는데, 그동안 이 두 젊은 신사를 거의 본 적이 없었다. 다만 한 번, 고모와 함께 올워디 씨 댁에서 식사를 한 적이 있었다."], ["She dined, however, once, together with her aunt, at Mr Allworthy's.", "그것은 앞서 말한 자고 사건이 있고 며칠 뒤의 일이었다. 소피아는 식탁에서 그 이야기를 처음부터 끝까지 들었으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["This was a few days after the adventure of the partridge, before commemorated.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에도 고모는 그녀에게서 별다른 말을 끌어내지 못했다."], ["Sophia heard the whole story at table, where she said nothing: nor indeed could her aunt get many words from her as she returned home; but her maid, when undressing her, happening to say, “Well, miss, I suppose you have seen young Master Blifil to-day?”", "그런데 그녀의 시중을 들던 하녀가 옷을 벗기다가 무심코 \"아씨, 오늘 어린 블리필 도련님을 뵈었겠네요?\" 하고 말하자, 그녀는 몹시 격하게 대답했다."], ["she answered with much passion, “I hate the name of Master Blifil, as I do whatever is base and treacherous: and I wonder Mr Allworthy would suffer that old barbarous schoolmaster to punish a poor boy so cruelly for what was only the effect of his good-nature.”", "\"나는 블리필 도련님이라는 이름을, 비열하고 음흉한 모든 것을 미워하듯 미워해. 그리고 올워디 씨가 어찌 그 늙고 잔인한 선생을 그냥 두어, 그저 착한 마음에서 한 일 때문에 가엾은 아이를 그토록 모질게 매질하게 했는지 정말 모르겠어.\""], ["She then recounted the story to her maid, and concluded with saying, “Don't you think he is a boy of noble spirit?”", "그러고 나서 그녀는 하녀에게 그 이야기를 들려주고는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"그 아이가 고결한 기상을 지닌 소년이라고 생각하지 않니?\""]], [["This young lady was now returned to her father; who gave her the command of his house, and placed her at the upper end of his table, where Tom (who for his great love of hunting was become a great favourite of the squire) often dined.", "이제 이 젊은 아가씨는 아버지 곁으로 돌아왔다. 아버지는 그녀에게 집안의 살림을 맡기고, 식탁 윗자리에 앉혔는데, 그 자리에는 (사냥을 무척 좋아한 덕에 지주의 큰 총애를 받게 된) 톰이 자주 와서 식사를 했다."], ["Young men of open, generous dispositions are naturally inclined to gallantry, which, if they have good understandings, as was in reality Tom's case, exerts itself in an obliging complacent behaviour to all women in general.", "솔직하고 너그러운 성품의 젊은이는 본디 여인을 정중히 대하는 쪽으로 기울기 마련인데, 만일 분별까지 갖추었다면—실제로 톰이 그러했다—그 마음씨는 모든 여인을 두루 살뜰히 배려하는 상냥한 태도로 드러난다."], ["This greatly distinguished Tom from the boisterous brutality of mere country squires on the one hand, and from the solemn and somewhat sullen deportment of Master Blifil on the other; and he began now, at twenty, to have the name of a pretty fellow among all the women in the neighbourhood.", "이 점이 톰을, 한편으로는 그저 시골 지주들의 거칠고 무뚝뚝한 됨됨이로부터, 다른 한편으로는 블리필 도련님의 엄숙하고 다소 시무룩한 태도로부터 크게 돋보이게 했다. 그리하여 그는 이제 스무 살에 이르러, 이웃의 모든 여인들 사이에서 멋진 청년이라는 이름을 얻기 시작했다."]], [["Tom behaved to Sophia with no particularity, unless perhaps by showing her a higher respect than he paid to any other. This distinction her beauty, fortune, sense, and amiable carriage, seemed to demand; but as to design upon her person he had none; for which we shall at present suffer the reader to condemn him of stupidity; but perhaps we shall be able indifferently well to account for it hereafter.", "톰은 소피아를 대할 때 특별한 기색을 내비치지 않았다. 다만 다른 누구에게보다 더 높은 존경을 보였다는 점만은 예외라면 예외였을 것이다. 이런 차이는 그녀의 아름다움과 재산과 분별, 그리고 사랑스러운 몸가짐이 마땅히 요구하는 것처럼 보였다. 그러나 그녀의 몸을 노린 어떤 속셈도 그에게는 없었으니, 이 점에서 우리는 당장은 독자가 그를 어리석다고 나무라도록 내버려 두겠다. 다만 어쩌면 나중에 그 까닭을 그럭저럭 잘 설명할 수 있을 것이다."]], [["Sophia, with the highest degree of innocence and modesty, had a remarkable sprightliness in her temper. This was so greatly increased whenever she was in company with Tom, that had he not been very young and thoughtless, he must have observed it: or had not Mr Western's thoughts been generally either in the field, the stable, or the dog-kennel, it might have perhaps created some jealousy in him: but so far was the good gentleman from entertaining any such suspicions, that he gave Tom every opportunity with his daughter which any lover could have wished; and this Tom innocently improved to better advantage, by following only the dictates of his natural gallantry and good-nature, than he might perhaps have done had he had the deepest designs on the young lady.", "소피아는 더없이 순결하고 정숙하면서도, 그 기질에 두드러진 발랄함이 있었다. 그리고 그 발랄함은 톰과 함께 있을 때면 어찌나 크게 부풀어 올랐던지, 만일 톰이 그토록 어리고 생각이 없지만 않았다면 분명 그것을 알아챘으리라. 또 만일 웨스턴 씨의 머릿속이 늘 사냥터나 마구간이나 개집에 가 있지만 않았다면, 어쩌면 그의 마음에 어떤 질투를 일으켰을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 선량한 양반은 그런 의심을 품기는커녕, 어떤 연인이라도 바랄 법한 온갖 기회를 톰에게 제 딸과 더불어 마련해 주었다. 그리고 톰은 그저 타고난 정중함과 착한 마음씨가 시키는 대로만 따랐는데도, 이 기회를 천진하게 잘 살려 냈으니, 어쩌면 그 아가씨에게 깊은 속셈을 품었더라면 오히려 그만큼 잘 해내지 못했을지도 모른다."]], [["But indeed it can occasion little wonder that this matter escaped the observation of others, since poor Sophia herself never remarked it; and her heart was irretrievably lost before she suspected it was in danger.", "그러나 실로 이 일이 다른 사람들의 눈을 벗어났다는 것은 그리 놀랄 일이 아니다. 가엾은 소피아 자신조차도 결코 그것을 알아채지 못했으며, 그녀의 마음은 위태로운 줄 의심해 보기도 전에 이미 돌이킬 수 없이 빼앗기고 말았으니 말이다."]], [["Matters were in this situation, when Tom, one afternoon, finding Sophia alone, began, after a short apology, with a very serious face, to acquaint her that he had a favour to ask of her which he hoped her goodness would comply with.", "일이 이런 형편에 놓여 있던 어느 날 오후, 톰은 소피아가 혼자 있는 것을 보고는, 짧게 양해를 구한 뒤 자못 진지한 얼굴로 그녀에게 청할 부탁이 하나 있는데 그녀의 너그러움으로 들어주기를 바란다고 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["Though neither the young man's behaviour, nor indeed his manner of opening this business, were such as could give her any just cause of suspecting he intended to make love to her; yet whether Nature whispered something into her ear, or from what cause it arose I will not determine; certain it is, some idea of that kind must have intruded itself; for her colour forsook her cheeks, her limbs trembled, and her tongue would have faltered, had Tom stopped for an answer; but he soon relieved her from her perplexity, by proceeding to inform her of his request; which was to solicit her interest on behalf of the gamekeeper, whose own ruin, and that of a large family, must be, he said, the consequence of Mr Western's pursuing his action against him.", "그 젊은이의 태도도, 또 이 용건을 꺼내는 방식도, 그가 그녀에게 사랑을 고백하려 한다고 의심할 만한 정당한 빌미를 줄 만한 것은 아니었다. 그런데도 자연이 그녀의 귀에 무언가를 속삭였던 것인지, 아니면 어떤 까닭에서 비롯되었는지는 내가 단정하지 않겠으나, 분명한 것은 그런 종류의 어떤 생각이 그녀에게 슬며시 끼어들었으리라는 점이다. 그녀의 뺨에서 핏기가 가시고, 사지가 떨렸으며, 만일 톰이 대답을 기다리며 말을 멈추기라도 했다면 혀까지 더듬거렸으리라. 그러나 그는 곧 자신의 청을 마저 알림으로써 그녀를 그 당혹에서 풀어 주었다. 그 청이란, 웨스턴 씨가 사냥터지기를 상대로 소송을 계속 밀어붙인다면 그 결과 본인은 물론 대식구가 통째로 파멸할 수밖에 없으니, 그를 위해 아버지께 힘써 달라는 것이었다."]], [["Sophia presently recovered her confusion, and, with a smile full of sweetness, said, “Is this the mighty favour you asked with so much gravity?", "소피아는 이내 혼란에서 벗어나, 더없이 달콤한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그토록 엄숙하게 청하신 그 대단한 부탁이 고작 이거였나요?"], ["I will do it with all my heart.", "기꺼이 들어드리겠어요."], ["I really pity the poor fellow, and no longer ago than yesterday sent a small matter to his wife.”", "저는 정말로 그 가엾은 사람이 딱해서, 바로 어제도 그 아내에게 변변찮은 것이나마 보냈답니다.\""], ["This small matter was one of her gowns, some linen, and ten shillings in money, of which Tom had heard, and it had, in reality, put this solicitation into his head.", "이 변변찮은 것이란, 그녀의 옷 한 벌과 약간의 아마포, 그리고 돈 십 실링이었는데, 톰은 이 일을 전해 듣고 있었고, 실은 바로 그것이 이 청을 그의 머릿속에 떠올려 준 것이었다."]], [["Our youth, now, emboldened with his success, resolved to push the matter farther, and ventured even to beg her recommendation of him to her father's service; protesting that he thought him one of the honestest fellows in the country, and extremely well qualified for the place of a gamekeeper, which luckily then happened to be vacant.", "이제 성공에 한껏 용기를 얻은 우리의 젊은이는, 일을 한 걸음 더 밀고 나가기로 마음먹고, 감히 그를 아버지의 일꾼으로 천거해 달라는 부탁까지 내놓았다. 그는 그 사람이야말로 이 고장에서 가장 정직한 사람 가운데 하나이며, 마침 그때 운 좋게 비어 있던 사냥터지기 자리에 더없이 잘 어울린다고 힘주어 말했다."]], [["Sophia answered, “Well, I will undertake this too; but I cannot promise you as much success as in the former part, which I assure you I will not quit my father without obtaining. However, I will do what I can for the poor fellow; for I sincerely look upon him and his family as objects of great compassion. And now, Mr Jones, I must ask you a favour.”", "소피아가 대답했다. \"좋아요, 이 일도 맡아 보겠어요. 다만 앞서 부탁만큼 성공을 장담할 수는 없네요. 앞 부탁이라면 분명 아버지께 허락을 얻기 전에는 물러나지 않으리라 약속드릴 수 있지만요. 그래도 그 가엾은 사람을 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 해 보겠어요. 저는 진심으로 그와 그 식구들을 크게 가엾이 여기고 있으니까요. 그런데 존스 씨, 이제는 제가 당신께 한 가지 부탁을 드려야겠네요.\""]], [["“A favour, madam!”", "\"부탁이라니요, 아가씨!\" 톰이 외쳤다."], ["cries Tom: “if you knew the pleasure you have given me in the hopes of receiving a command from you, you would think by mentioning it you did confer the greatest favour on me; for by this dear hand I would sacrifice my life to oblige you.”", "\"아가씨께 분부를 받게 되리라는 기대만으로 제게 얼마나 큰 기쁨을 주셨는지 아신다면, 그 말씀을 꺼내신 것만으로도 제게 더없이 큰 은혜를 베푸신 셈임을 아실 겁니다. 이 사랑스러운 손에 두고 맹세컨대, 아가씨를 기쁘게 해 드리기 위해서라면 저는 제 목숨도 바치겠습니다.\""]], [["He then snatched her hand, and eagerly kissed it, which was the first time his lips had ever touched her.", "그러고는 그녀의 손을 덥석 잡아 열렬히 입을 맞추었으니, 그의 입술이 그녀에게 닿은 것은 이때가 처음이었다."], ["The blood, which before had forsaken her cheeks, now made her sufficient amends, by rushing all over her face and neck with such violence, that they became all of a scarlet colour.", "조금 전에 뺨을 떠났던 피가 이제는 얼굴과 목 전체로 어찌나 거세게 몰려들어 충분히 보상을 했던지, 온통 새빨갛게 물들고 말았다."], ["She now first felt a sensation to which she had been before a stranger, and which, when she had leisure to reflect on it, began to acquaint her with some secrets, which the reader, if he doth not already guess them, will know in due time.", "그녀는 이때 처음으로, 전에는 낯설기만 했던 어떤 감각을 느꼈는데, 그것은 그녀가 한가로이 곱씹어 볼 여유가 생기자 어떤 비밀들을 일러 주기 시작했다. 그 비밀이 무엇인지, 독자가 아직 짐작하지 못했다면 머지않아 알게 될 것이다."]], [["Sophia, as soon as she could speak (which was not instantly), informed him that the favour she had to desire of him was, not to lead her father through so many dangers in hunting; for that, from what she had heard, she was terribly frightened every time they went out together, and expected some day or other to see her father brought home with broken limbs.", "소피아는 말을 할 수 있게 되자마자(이는 곧바로는 아니었다), 자기가 그에게 청하려는 부탁이란 다름 아니라, 사냥할 때 아버지를 그토록 많은 위험 속으로 끌고 다니지 말아 달라는 것이라고 그에게 알렸다. 들은 바에 따르면, 두 사람이 함께 나갈 때마다 그녀는 끔찍하게 겁이 나서, 언젠가는 아버지가 팔다리가 부러진 채 실려 오는 모습을 보게 되지나 않을까 마음을 졸인다는 것이었다."], ["She therefore begged him, for her sake, to be more cautious; and as he well knew Mr Western would follow him, not to ride so madly, nor to take those dangerous leaps for the future.", "그러니 부디 자기를 봐서라도 좀 더 조심해 달라고, 또 웨스턴 씨가 으레 그를 뒤따른다는 것을 그가 잘 아는 만큼, 앞으로는 그렇게 무모하게 말을 몰지도, 그런 위험한 도약도 하지 말아 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["Tom promised faithfully to obey her commands; and after thanking her for her kind compliance with his request, took his leave, and departed highly charmed with his success.", "톰은 그녀의 분부를 충실히 따르겠노라 약속했다. 그리고 자신의 청을 너그러이 들어준 데 감사를 표한 뒤 작별을 고하고, 자신의 성공에 더없이 들뜬 채 떠났다."]], [["Poor Sophia was charmed too, but in a very different way.", "가엾은 소피아 역시 마음이 들떴으나, 그 결은 사뭇 달랐다."], ["Her sensations, however, the reader's heart (if he or she have any) will better represent than I can, if I had as many mouths as ever poet wished for, to eat, I suppose, those many dainties with which he was so plentifully provided.", "그러나 그녀의 감정이 어떠했는지는, 설령 내가 옛 시인이 그토록 바라던 만큼 많은 입을 가졌다 한들—아마 그토록 푸짐하게 차려진 그 온갖 진미를 먹기 위해서였겠지—내가 그려 내는 것보다, 독자의 마음이(그나 그녀에게 마음이라는 것이 있다면) 더 잘 그려 낼 것이다."]], [["It was Mr Western's custom every afternoon, as soon as he was drunk, to hear his daughter play on the harpsichord; for he was a great lover of music, and perhaps, had he lived in town, might have passed for a connoisseur; for he always excepted against the finest compositions of Mr Handel.", "웨스턴 씨에게는 매일 오후, 술이 거나하게 오르자마자 딸이 하프시코드를 타는 것을 듣는 버릇이 있었다. 그는 음악을 무척 사랑하는 사람이어서, 어쩌면 도시에 살았더라면 감식가로 통했을지도 모른다."], ["He never relished any music but what was light and airy; and indeed his most favourite tunes were Old Sir Simon the King, St George he was for England, Bobbing Joan, and some others.", "그도 그럴 것이, 그는 헨델 씨의 더없이 빼어난 곡들마저 늘 못마땅해했으니 말이다. 그는 가볍고 경쾌한 음악이 아니면 결코 즐기지 않았으며, 실로 그가 가장 좋아하는 곡들은 〈늙은 시몬 왕〉, 〈성 조지는 잉글랜드 편이라네〉, 〈까딱거리는 조앤〉, 그리고 그 밖의 몇 곡이었다."]], [["His daughter, though she was a perfect mistress of music, and would never willingly have played any but Handel's, was so devoted to her father's pleasure, that she learnt all those tunes to oblige him.", "그의 딸은 음악에 완벽히 통달했고, 마음대로 할 수만 있다면 결코 헨델의 곡 말고는 타려 하지 않았겠지만, 아버지의 즐거움에 어찌나 헌신적이었던지 그를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 그 모든 곡을 익혔다."], ["However, she would now and then endeavour to lead him into her own taste; and when he required the repetition of his ballads, would answer with a “Nay, dear sir;”", "그래도 그녀는 이따금 아버지를 자기 취향 쪽으로 이끌어 보려 애썼고, 아버지가 그 발라드들을 다시 한 번 더 청하면 \"아니에요, 아버지\""], ["and would often beg him to suffer her to play something else.", "하고 대답하며, 부디 다른 것을 타게 해 달라고 종종 졸랐다."]], [["This evening, however, when the gentleman was retired from his bottle, she played all his favourites three times over without any solicitation.", "그러나 이날 저녁, 그 양반이 술병에서 물러나자 그녀는 조르지도 않았는데 그가 좋아하는 곡들을 죄다 세 번씩 거푸 타 주었다."], ["This so pleased the good squire, that he started from his couch, gave his daughter a kiss, and swore her hand was greatly improved.", "이에 어찌나 흡족했던지 선량한 지주는 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 딸에게 입을 맞추고는, 솜씨가 부쩍 늘었다고 맹세하듯 칭찬했다."], ["She took this opportunity to execute her promise to Tom; in which she succeeded so well, that the squire declared, if she would give him t'other bout of Old Sir Simon, he would give the gamekeeper his deputation the next morning.", "그녀는 이 기회를 틈타 톰에게 한 약속을 실행에 옮겼는데, 그 일이 어찌나 잘 풀렸던지 지주는, 〈늙은 시몬 왕〉을 한 번만 더 타 주면 이튿날 아침에 사냥터지기에게 임명장을 내주겠노라고 선언했다."], ["Sir Simon was played again and again, till the charms of the music soothed Mr Western to sleep.", "〈시몬 왕〉은 거듭거듭 연주되었고, 마침내 그 음악의 매력에 달래진 웨스턴 씨는 스르르 잠이 들었다."], ["In the morning Sophia did not fail to remind him of his engagement; and his attorney was immediately sent for, ordered to stop any further proceedings in the action, and to make out the deputation.", "아침이 되자 소피아는 그 약속을 일깨우는 것을 잊지 않았다. 그러자 곧바로 그의 변호사가 불려 와, 그 소송의 모든 후속 절차를 중단하고 임명장을 작성하라는 명을 받았다."]], [["Tom's success in this affair soon began to ring over the country, and various were the censures passed upon it; some greatly applauding it as an act of good nature; others sneering, and saying, “No wonder that one idle fellow should love another.”", "이 일에서 거둔 톰의 성공은 머지않아 온 고장에 울려 퍼지기 시작했고, 그에 대한 평은 가지각색이었다. 어떤 이들은 착한 마음에서 나온 행동이라며 크게 칭찬했고, 또 어떤 이들은 비웃으며 \"게으른 녀석이 게으른 녀석을 좋아하는 거야 놀랄 것도 없지\""], ["Young Blifil was greatly enraged at it.", "하고 말했다. 어린 블리필은 그 일에 몹시 격분했다."], ["He had long hated Black George in the same proportion as Jones delighted in him; not from any offence which he had ever received, but from his great love to religion and virtue;--for Black George had the reputation of a loose kind of a fellow.", "그는 존스가 블랙 조지를 좋아하는 꼭 그만큼 오랫동안 그를 미워해 왔는데, 자신이 그에게서 어떤 모욕을 받아서가 아니라 종교와 덕을 향한 자신의 크나큰 사랑 때문이었다. 블랙 조지는 행실이 헤픈 부류라는 평판이 있었기 때문이다."], ["Blifil therefore represented this as flying in Mr Allworthy's face; and declared, with great concern, that it was impossible to find any other motive for doing good to such a wretch.", "그래서 블리필은 이 일을 두고 올워디 씨의 얼굴에 정면으로 침을 뱉는 짓이라고 몰아세우며, 그런 못된 자에게 선을 베풀 다른 동기란 도무지 찾을 수 없다고 자못 근심스러운 듯 단언했다."]], [["Thwackum and Square likewise sung to the same tune.", "스왜컴과 스퀘어 역시 같은 가락을 읊어 댔다."], ["They were now (especially the latter) become greatly jealous of young Jones with the widow; for he now approached the age of twenty, was really a fine young fellow, and that lady, by her encouragements to him, seemed daily more and more to think him so.", "그들은 이제(특히 후자가) 그 과부를 두고 어린 존스를 몹시 질투하게 되었다. 그도 그럴 것이 그는 이제 스무 살에 가까워졌고, 정말이지 훤칠한 젊은이가 되어 있었으며, 그 부인 또한 그를 부추기는 태도로 보아 날이 갈수록 그를 점점 더 그렇게 여기는 듯했기 때문이다."]], [["Allworthy was not, however, moved with their malice.", "그러나 올워디는 그들의 악의에 마음이 흔들리지 않았다."], ["He declared himself very well satisfied with what Jones had done.", "그는 존스가 한 일에 매우 만족한다고 밝혔다."], ["He said the perseverance and integrity of his friendship was highly commendable, and he wished he could see more frequent instances of that virtue.", "그 우정의 한결같음과 곧음이 크게 칭찬받아 마땅하며, 그런 미덕의 본보기를 더 자주 볼 수 있으면 좋겠다고 그는 말했다."]], [["But Fortune, who seldom greatly relishes such sparks as my friend Tom, perhaps because they do not pay more ardent addresses to her, gave now a very different turn to all his actions, and showed them to Mr Allworthy in a light far less agreeable than that gentleman's goodness had hitherto seen them in.", "그러나 운명의 여신은, 어쩌면 그런 이들이 자기에게 더 열렬히 구애를 바치지 않는다는 이유에서인지, 내 벗 톰 같은 멋쟁이들을 좀처럼 그리 달갑게 여기지 않는데, 이제 그의 모든 행동에 사뭇 다른 방향으로 비틀어 놓았고, 그것들을 올워디 씨에게, 그 양반의 선량함이 그때껏 보아 온 것보다 훨씬 덜 흐뭇한 빛으로 비춰 보이기 시작했다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["An apology for the insensibility of Mr Jones to all the charms of the lovely Sophia; in which possibly we may, in a considerable degree, lower his character in the estimation of those men of wit and gallantry who approve the heroes in most of our modern comedies.", "아름다운 소피아의 그 모든 매력 앞에서도 무덤덤했던 존스 씨를 위한 변명. 여기서 우리는, 우리 시대 희극 대부분에 등장하는 주인공을 흡족해하는 재치와 풍류의 신사들이 보기에, 그의 인물됨을 상당히 깎아내리게 될지도 모르겠다."]], [["There are two sorts of people, who, I am afraid, have already conceived some contempt for my heroe, on account of his behaviour to Sophia.", "두 부류의 사람들이, 소피아를 대하는 그의 처신을 두고 이미 내 주인공에게 얼마간 경멸을 품었을까 봐 나는 염려스럽다."], ["The former of these will blame his prudence in neglecting an opportunity to possess himself of Mr Western's fortune; and the latter will no less despise him for his backwardness to so fine a girl, who seemed ready to fly into his arms, if he would open them to receive her.", "앞쪽 부류는 웨스턴 씨의 재산을 차지할 기회를 흘려보낸 그의 분별없음을 탓할 것이고, 뒤쪽 부류는 그토록 어여쁜 처녀, 그가 팔만 벌리면 곧장 그 품으로 날아들 것 같던 처녀에게 그가 보인 머뭇거림을 못지않게 깔볼 것이다."]], [["Now, though I shall not perhaps be able absolutely to acquit him of either of these charges (for want of prudence admits of no excuse; and what I shall produce against the latter charge will, I apprehend, be scarce satisfactory); yet, as evidence may sometimes be offered in mitigation, I shall set forth the plain matter of fact, and leave the whole to the reader's determination.", "자, 비록 내가 이 두 가지 비난 가운데 어느 쪽으로부터도 그를 온전히 벗겨 줄 수는 없겠지만(분별없음에는 변명의 여지가 없고, 뒤쪽 비난에 맞서 내가 내놓을 만한 것도 좀처럼 흡족하지 않으리라 짐작되기에), 그래도 증거란 때로 형량을 덜어 달라는 뜻으로 제출될 수도 있는 법이니, 나는 있는 그대로의 사실만 펼쳐 보이고 나머지 전부는 독자의 판단에 맡기겠다."]], [["Mr Jones had somewhat about him, which, though I think writers are not thoroughly agreed in its name, doth certainly inhabit some human breasts; whose use is not so properly to distinguish right from wrong, as to prompt and incite them to the former, and to restrain and withhold them from the latter.", "존스 씨에게는 무언가가 깃들어 있었는데, 작가들이 그 이름에 대해 온전히 합의를 보지는 못한 듯해도, 그것이 어떤 사람들의 가슴속에 분명히 자리 잡고 있다는 점만은 확실하다. 그것의 쓰임새는 옳고 그름을 가려내는 데 있다기보다는, 앞의 것으로 사람을 부추기고 떠밀며, 뒤의 것으로부터는 사람을 붙잡아 멈춰 세우는 데 있다."]], [["This somewhat may be indeed resembled to the famous trunk-maker in the playhouse; for, whenever the person who is possessed of it doth what is right, no ravished or friendly spectator is so eager or so loud in his applause: on the contrary, when he doth wrong, no critic is so apt to hiss and explode him.", "이 무언가는 사실 극장의 그 유명한 궤짝 두드리는 사내에 견줄 만하다. 그것을 지닌 사람이 옳은 일을 할 때면, 넋을 잃고 박수를 보내는 다정한 관객조차 그토록 열렬하거나 그토록 소리 높여 갈채를 보내지는 못하기 때문이다. 반대로 그가 그릇된 일을 할 때면, 어떤 비평가도 그처럼 거세게 야유하고 그를 무대에서 끌어내리려 들지는 못한다."]], [["To give a higher idea of the principle I mean, as well as one more familiar to the present age; it may be considered as sitting on its throne in the mind, like the Lord High Chancellor of this kingdom in his court; where it presides, governs, directs, judges, acquits, and condemns according to merit and justice, with a knowledge which nothing escapes, a penetration which nothing can deceive, and an integrity which nothing can corrupt.", "* 궤짝 두드리는 사내: 당시 극장에서 마음에 드는 장면마다 궤짝을 막대기로 요란하게 두드려 갈채를 보낸 것으로 유명한 관객을 가리킨다. 내가 말하려는 이 원리에 대해 더 높은 관념을, 또한 지금 이 시대에 더 친숙한 관념을 떠올리게 하자면, 그것은 마음속 옥좌에 앉아 있는 것으로 여길 수 있겠다. 마치 이 왕국의 대법관이 자기 법정에 앉아 있듯이 말이다. 그곳에서 그것은 좌장이 되어 다스리고, 이끌고, 판결하며, 공로와 정의에 따라 무죄를 선고하고 유죄를 선고한다. 그 무엇도 빠져나가지 못하는 지식과, 그 무엇도 속일 수 없는 통찰과, 그 무엇도 더럽힐 수 없는 청렴함을 지닌 채로."]], [["This active principle may perhaps be said to constitute the most essential barrier between us and our neighbours the brutes; for if there be some in the human shape who are not under any such dominion, I choose rather to consider them as deserters from us to our neighbours; among whom they will have the fate of deserters, and not be placed in the first rank.", "이 능동적인 원리야말로 어쩌면 우리와 우리의 이웃인 짐승들 사이에 놓인 가장 본질적인 울타리라 할 수 있을 것이다. 사람의 형상을 하고도 그런 다스림 아래 있지 않은 자들이 있다면, 나는 차라리 그들을 우리에게서 우리 이웃 쪽으로 넘어간 탈영병으로 여기겠다. 그곳에서 그들은 탈영병의 운명을 맞아, 결코 첫째 줄에는 끼지 못할 것이다."]], [["Our heroe, whether he derived it from Thwackum or Square I will not determine, was very strongly under the guidance of this principle; for though he did not always act rightly, yet he never did otherwise without feeling and suffering for it.", "우리의 주인공은, 이 원리를 스왝검에게서 물려받았는지 스퀘어에게서 물려받았는지 나는 단정하지 않겠지만, 그 인도 아래 무척이나 강하게 놓여 있었다. 그가 늘 옳게만 행동한 것은 아니었으나, 그릇되게 행동할 때면 결코 그것을 느끼고 괴로워하지 않은 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["It was this which taught him, that to repay the civilities and little friendships of hospitality by robbing the house where you have received them, is to be the basest and meanest of thieves.", "바로 이것이 그에게 가르쳐 주었다. 환대가 베푼 친절과 작은 우정에, 그 친절을 받았던 그 집을 터는 것으로 갚는 짓이야말로 도둑 중에서도 가장 비열하고 가장 천한 도둑이 되는 일임을."], ["He did not think the baseness of this offence lessened by the height of the injury committed; on the contrary, if to steal another's plate deserved death and infamy, it seemed to him difficult to assign a punishment adequate to the robbing a man of his whole fortune, and of his child into the bargain.", "그는 저질러진 해악의 크기가 크다고 해서 이 죄의 비열함이 덜어진다고는 여기지 않았다. 오히려 남의 은접시를 훔치는 것이 죽음과 오명을 받아 마땅하다면, 한 사람에게서 그의 재산 전부를, 게다가 그의 딸까지 통째로 앗아 가는 짓에 걸맞은 벌을 정하기란 그로서는 어려운 일로 보였다."]], [["This principle, therefore, prevented him from any thought of making his fortune by such means (for this, as I have said, is an active principle, and doth not content itself with knowledge or belief only).", "그러므로 이 원리는 그가 그런 수단으로 제 재산을 일굴 생각조차 못 하게 막아 주었다(앞서 말했듯 이것은 능동적인 원리여서, 아는 것이나 믿는 것만으로는 결코 만족하지 않으니까). 만일 그가 소피아에게 깊이 빠져 있었다면 어쩌면 다르게 생각했을지도 모른다."], ["Had he been greatly enamoured of Sophia, he possibly might have thought otherwise; but give me leave to say, there is great difference between running away with a man's daughter from the motive of love, and doing the same thing from the motive of theft.", "하지만 한마디 하게 해 달라. 사랑이라는 동기에서 남의 딸을 데리고 달아나는 것과, 도둑질이라는 동기에서 똑같은 짓을 하는 것 사이에는 큰 차이가 있다."]], [["Now, though this young gentleman was not insensible of the charms of Sophia; though he greatly liked her beauty, and esteemed all her other qualifications, she had made, however, no deep impression on his heart; for which, as it renders him liable to the charge of stupidity, or at least of want of taste, we shall now proceed to account.", "자, 이 젊은 신사가 소피아의 매력에 무감각했던 것은 아니다. 그녀의 아름다움을 무척 좋아했고 그녀의 다른 모든 자질도 높이 샀지만, 그래도 그녀는 그의 마음에 깊은 자국을 남기지는 못했다. 이 점은 그를 우둔하다는, 적어도 안목이 없다는 비난에 노출시키니, 우리는 이제 그 까닭을 밝히는 데로 나아가겠다."]], [["The truth then is, his heart was in the possession of another woman. Here I question not but the reader will be surprized at our long taciturnity as to this matter; and quite at a loss to divine who this woman was, since we have hitherto not dropt a hint of any one likely to be a rival to Sophia; for as to Mrs Blifil, though we have been obliged to mention some suspicions of her affection for Tom, we have not hitherto given the least latitude for imagining that he had any for her; and, indeed, I am sorry to say it, but the youth of both sexes are too apt to be deficient in their gratitude for that regard with which persons more advanced in years are sometimes so kind to honour them.", "사실인즉, 그의 마음은 다른 여인이 차지하고 있었다. 이 대목에서 독자가 우리가 이 문제에 대해 그토록 오래 입을 다물고 있었던 것에 놀라리라는 점, 그리고 이 여인이 누구인지 도무지 가늠하지 못하리라는 점을 나는 의심하지 않는다. 지금까지 우리는 소피아의 연적이 될 만한 누군가에 대해 단 한 마디 귀띔조차 흘리지 않았으니 말이다. 블리필 부인으로 말하자면, 톰을 향한 그녀의 애정에 얼마간 의심을 품고 있다는 사실을 언급하지 않을 수 없었으나, 지금껏 그가 그녀에게 어떤 애정이라도 품었다고 상상할 만한 여지는 조금도 주지 않았다. 그리고 참으로, 이런 말을 하기는 안타깝지만, 남녀를 막론하고 젊은이들은, 나이가 더 든 사람들이 때로 그토록 다정하게 베풀어 주는 그 마음에 대해 감사할 줄 모르기가 너무도 쉽다."]], [["That the reader may be no longer in suspense, he will be pleased to remember, that we have often mentioned the family of George Seagrim (commonly called Black George, the gamekeeper), which consisted at present of a wife and five children.", "독자가 더 이상 마음을 졸이지 않도록 하기 위해, 우리가 조지 시그림(흔히 사냥터지기 검은 조지라 불리는)의 가족을 자주 언급해 왔음을 떠올려 주기 바란다. 그 가족은 지금 아내와 다섯 아이로 이루어져 있었다."]], [["The second of these children was a daughter, whose name was Molly, and who was esteemed one of the handsomest girls in the whole country.", "이 아이들 가운데 둘째는 딸이었는데, 이름은 몰리였고, 그 고장 전체에서 가장 어여쁜 처녀 가운데 하나로 꼽혔다."]], [["Congreve well says there is in true beauty something which vulgar souls cannot admire; so can no dirt or rags hide this something from those souls which are not of the vulgar stamp.", "콩그리브가 잘 말했듯, 진정한 아름다움에는 속된 영혼이 도무지 우러러볼 수 없는 무언가가 있다. 마찬가지로 어떤 흙먼지나 누더기도, 속된 틀에서 빚어지지 않은 영혼들에게서 이 무언가를 가릴 수는 없다."]], [["The beauty of this girl made, however, no impression on Tom, till she grew towards the age of sixteen, when Tom, who was near three years older, began first to cast the eyes of affection upon her.", "그러나 이 처녀의 아름다움은 그녀가 열여섯에 가까워질 무렵까지는 톰에게 아무런 자국도 남기지 못하다가, 마침내 그녀보다 세 살 가까이 위였던 톰이 비로소 그녀에게 애정 어린 눈길을 보내기 시작했다."], ["And this affection he had fixed on the girl long before he could bring himself to attempt the possession of her person: for though his constitution urged him greatly to this, his principles no less forcibly restrained him.", "그리고 그는 차마 그녀의 몸을 차지하려 시도하기로 마음먹기 훨씬 전부터 이 애정을 그 처녀에게 붙박아 두고 있었다. 그의 타고난 기질은 그를 이쪽으로 무던히도 떠밀었으나, 그의 원리 또한 그에 못지않게 강하게 그를 붙들었기 때문이다."], ["To debauch a young woman, however low her condition was, appeared to him a very heinous crime; and the good-will he bore the father, with the compassion he had for his family, very strongly corroborated all such sober reflections; so that he once resolved to get the better of his inclinations, and he actually abstained three whole months without ever going to Seagrim's house, or seeing his daughter.", "처지가 아무리 미천한 처녀라도 그녀를 유혹해 타락시키는 일은 그에게 매우 가증스러운 죄로 비쳤고, 그가 그 아버지에게 품은 호의와 그 가족에게 느끼는 연민이 이런 진중한 성찰을 모두 무척 강하게 떠받쳐 주었다. 그리하여 그는 한때 자기 욕망을 이겨 내기로 결심했고, 실제로 꼬박 석 달 동안이나 시그림의 집에 가지도, 그 딸을 보지도 않으며 자제했다."]], [["Now, though Molly was, as we have said, generally thought a very fine girl, and in reality she was so, yet her beauty was not of the most amiable kind.", "자, 앞서 말했듯 몰리는 대체로 매우 어여쁜 처녀로 여겨졌고 실제로도 그러했지만, 그녀의 아름다움은 가장 사랑스러운 종류는 아니었다."], ["It had, indeed, very little of feminine in it, and would have become a man at least as well as a woman; for, to say the truth, youth and florid health had a very considerable share in the composition.", "사실 거기에는 여성스러운 구석이 거의 없어서, 적어도 여자에게 어울리는 만큼은 남자에게도 어울렸을 법했다. 솔직히 말하자면 젊음과 혈색 좋은 건강이 그 아름다움을 이루는 데 상당히 큰 몫을 했기 때문이다."]], [["Nor was her mind more effeminate than her person.", "그녀의 마음도 그 몸뚱이보다 더 여성스럽지는 않았다."], ["As this was tall and robust, so was that bold and forward.", "몸이 키 크고 다부졌듯, 마음도 대담하고 거침이 없었다."], ["So little had she of modesty, that Jones had more regard for her virtue than she herself.", "그녀에게는 정숙함이라곤 어찌나 적었던지, 존스가 그녀 자신보다도 그녀의 정조를 더 아꼈을 정도였다. 그리고 십중팔구 그녀가 톰을 좋아하는 만큼 톰도 그녀를 좋아했으니, 그녀는 그의 머뭇거림을 알아채자 그에 비례하여 스스로 더 적극적으로 나섰다."], ["And as most probably she liked Tom as well as he liked her, so when she perceived his backwardness she herself grew proportionably forward; and when she saw he had entirely deserted the house, she found means of throwing herself in his way, and behaved in such a manner that the youth must have had very much or very little of the heroe if her endeavours had proved unsuccessful.", "그리고 그가 그 집을 아예 발길을 끊은 것을 보자, 그녀는 그의 앞길에 자신을 내던질 방도를 찾아내어, 만일 그녀의 그 노력이 실패로 돌아갔다면 그 젊은이는 영웅 기질이 너무 넘치거나 너무 모자랐다고 할 수밖에 없을 만큼 그렇게 처신했다. 한마디로, 그녀는 이내 존스의 그 모든 정숙한 결심을 무너뜨리고 승리를 거두었다."], ["In a word, she soon triumphed over all the virtuous resolutions of Jones; for though she behaved at last with all decent reluctance, yet I rather chuse to attribute the triumph to her, since, in fact, it was her design which succeeded.", "끝에 가서는 그녀가 자못 점잖게 마지못한 척 굴긴 했지만, 나는 차라리 그 승리를 그녀에게 돌리겠다. 사실 성공한 것은 그녀의 계략이었으니까."]], [["In the conduct of this matter, I say, Molly so well played her part, that Jones attributed the conquest entirely to himself, and considered the young woman as one who had yielded to the violent attacks of his passion.", "이 일의 처리에서, 다시 말하건대, 몰리는 제 역할을 어찌나 잘 해냈던지, 존스는 그 정복을 온전히 자기 공으로 돌렸고, 이 젊은 처녀를 제 격정의 거센 공세에 굴복한 사람으로 여겼다. 그는 또한 그녀의 굴복을 그를 향한 그녀의 걷잡을 수 없는 사랑의 힘 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["He likewise imputed her yielding to the ungovernable force of her love towards him; and this the reader will allow to have been a very natural and probable supposition, as we have more than once mentioned the uncommon comeliness of his person: and, indeed, he was one of the handsomest young fellows in the world.", "그리고 우리가 그의 빼어난 풍채를 한두 번이 아니게 언급해 왔으니, 독자는 이것이 매우 자연스럽고 그럴듯한 짐작이었음을 인정할 것이다. 실로 그는 세상에서 가장 잘생긴 젊은이 가운데 하나였으니까."]], [["As there are some minds whose affections, like Master Blifil's, are solely placed on one single person, whose interest and indulgence alone they consider on every occasion; regarding the good and ill of all others as merely indifferent, any farther than as they contribute to the pleasure or advantage of that person: so there is a different temper of mind which borrows a degree of virtue even from self-love.", "블리필 도련님처럼 그 애정이 오로지 단 한 사람에게만 쏠려, 매사에 오직 그 사람의 이익과 안락만을 헤아리고, 다른 모든 이의 좋고 나쁨은 그 사람의 즐거움이나 이익에 보탬이 되는 한도에서만 따질 뿐 그 너머로는 그저 무관심하게 여기는 그런 마음들이 있는가 하면, 자기애에서조차 얼마간의 미덕을 빌려 오는 다른 기질의 마음도 있다."], ["Such can never receive any kind of satisfaction from another, without loving the creature to whom that satisfaction is owing, and without making its well-being in some sort necessary to their own ease.", "이런 마음은 누군가에게서 어떤 종류의 만족을 받든, 그 만족을 베푼 상대를 사랑하지 않고서는, 또 그 상대의 안녕을 어느 정도 제 자신의 평안에 꼭 필요한 것으로 삼지 않고서는 결코 그 만족을 받아들이지 못한다."]], [["Of this latter species was our heroe.", "우리의 주인공은 바로 이 뒤쪽 부류였다."], ["He considered this poor girl as one whose happiness or misery he had caused to be dependent on himself.", "그는 이 가엾은 처녀를, 그 행복과 불행을 자기 손에 매달려 있게 만든 사람으로 여겼다."], ["Her beauty was still the object of desire, though greater beauty, or a fresher object, might have been more so; but the little abatement which fruition had occasioned to this was highly overbalanced by the considerations of the affection which she visibly bore him, and of the situation into which he had brought her.", "그녀의 아름다움은 여전히 욕망의 대상이었으나, 더 빼어난 아름다움이나 더 싱싱한 대상이었다면 더더욱 그러했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 욕망이 충족됨으로써 이 아름다움에 생겨난 작은 시들함은, 그녀가 그에게 눈에 보이게 품고 있는 애정에 대한 헤아림과, 그가 그녀를 몰아넣은 처지에 대한 헤아림으로 넉넉히 메워지고도 남았다."], ["The former of these created gratitude, the latter compassion; and both, together with his desire for her person, raised in him a passion which might, without any great violence to the word, be called love; though, perhaps, it was at first not very judiciously placed.", "앞의 것은 감사를, 뒤의 것은 연민을 자아냈고, 이 둘이 그녀의 몸을 향한 그의 욕망과 더불어 그의 안에 하나의 격정을 일으켰으니, 그 말을 크게 비틀지 않고도 사랑이라 부를 만한 것이었다. 비록 처음에는 어쩌면 그리 분별 있게 자리 잡은 사랑은 아니었을지라도 말이다."]], [["This, then, was the true reason of that insensibility which he had shown to the charms of Sophia, and that behaviour in her which might have been reasonably enough interpreted as an encouragement to his addresses; for as he could not think of abandoning his Molly, poor and destitute as she was, so no more could he entertain a notion of betraying such a creature as Sophia.", "바로 이것이, 그가 소피아의 매력 앞에서 보인 그 무덤덤함의, 그리고 그를 향한 구애를 부추기는 것으로 충분히 그럴듯하게 풀이될 만했던 그녀의 그 처신 앞에서 보인 무덤덤함의 진짜 까닭이었다. 그는 자기 몰리를, 그토록 가난하고 의지가지없는 그녀를 저버린다는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었고, 마찬가지로 소피아 같은 사람을 배신한다는 관념도 품을 수 없었다."], ["And surely, had he given the least encouragement to any passion for that young lady, he must have been absolutely guilty of one or other of those crimes; either of which would, in my opinion, have very justly subjected him to that fate, which, at his first introduction into this history, I mentioned to have been generally predicted as his certain destiny.", "그리고 만일 그가 그 젊은 아가씨를 향한 어떤 격정에 조금이라도 부채질을 했다면, 그는 그 두 죄 가운데 하나를 어김없이 저지른 셈이 되었을 것이다. 내 생각에는 그 어느 쪽이든, 이 이야기에 그가 처음 등장할 때 내가 그의 확정된 운명으로 흔히 예언되었다고 말했던 바로 그 신세를, 그에게 매우 정당하게 안겨 주었을 것이다."]], [["Being the shortest chapter in this book.", "이 책에서 가장 짧은 장."]], [["Her mother first perceived the alteration in the shape of Molly; and in order to hide it from her neighbours, she foolishly clothed her in that sack which Sophia had sent her; though, indeed, that young lady had little apprehension that the poor woman would have been weak enough to let any of her daughters wear it in that form.", "몰리의 몸매가 달라진 것을 가장 먼저 알아챈 사람은 그녀의 어머니였다. 이웃들의 눈을 피하려고 그녀는 어리석게도 소피아가 보내 준 그 자루 같은 드레스를 몰리에게 입혔다. 사실 소피아라는 젊은 아가씨는 그 가엾은 여인이 자기 딸 가운데 누구에게라도 그 옷을 그런 모양으로 입히게 할 만큼 분별이 없으리라고는 거의 짐작조차 하지 못했다."]], [["Molly was charmed with the first opportunity she ever had of showing her beauty to advantage; for though she could very well bear to contemplate herself in the glass, even when dressed in rags; and though she had in that dress conquered the heart of Jones, and perhaps of some others; yet she thought the addition of finery would much improve her charms, and extend her conquests.", "몰리는 난생처음으로 자신의 아름다움을 한껏 돋보이게 할 기회를 얻은 것에 들떴다. 누더기를 걸치고 있을 때조차도 거울에 비친 자신을 흐뭇하게 바라볼 수 있었고, 또 그 누더기 차림으로 존스의 마음을, 어쩌면 다른 몇 사람의 마음까지도 사로잡은 그녀였지만, 그래도 고운 옷을 더하면 자신의 매력이 한결 빛나고 정복의 범위도 넓어지리라 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Molly, therefore, having dressed herself out in this sack, with a new laced cap, and some other ornaments which Tom had given her, repairs to church with her fan in her hand the very next Sunday.", "그리하여 몰리는 이 자루 같은 드레스에, 톰이 준 새 레이스 모자며 몇 가지 다른 장신구를 더해 차려입고는, 바로 다음 일요일에 부채를 손에 들고 교회로 향했다."], ["The great are deceived if they imagine they have appropriated ambition and vanity to themselves.", "지체 높은 이들이 야심과 허영을 저희만의 것으로 여긴다면 큰 착각이다."], ["These noble qualities flourish as notably in a country church and churchyard as in the drawing-room, or in the closet.", "이 고귀한 성정들은 응접실이나 골방에서만큼이나 시골 교회와 그 묘지에서도 두드러지게 무성히 피어난다."], ["Schemes have indeed been laid in the vestry which would hardly disgrace the conclave.", "실로 교회 제의실에서도, 추기경 회의에 견주어 부끄럽지 않을 만한 음모들이 꾸며진 적이 있다."], ["Here is a ministry, and here is an opposition.", "여기에도 집권 세력이 있고, 여기에도 반대파가 있다."], ["Here are plots and circumventions, parties and factions, equal to those which are to be found in courts.", "여기에도 궁정에서나 볼 법한 책략과 술수, 당파와 파벌이 고스란히 있다."]], [["Nor are the women here less practised in the highest feminine arts than their fair superiors in quality and fortune.", "여기 여인들이라고 해서 지체와 재산에서 자기보다 나은 귀부인들에 비해 그 지극한 여성의 기예에 덜 능숙한 것도 아니다."], ["Here are prudes and coquettes.", "여기에도 새침데기와 요염한 여자가 있다."], ["Here are dressing and ogling, falsehood, envy, malice, scandal; in short, everything which is common to the most splendid assembly, or politest circle.", "여기에도 단장과 추파, 거짓과 시기, 악의와 험담이 있으니, 한마디로 가장 화려한 모임이나 가장 세련된 사교계에 흔한 것이라면 무엇이든 다 있다."], ["Let those of high life, therefore, no longer despise the ignorance of their inferiors; nor the vulgar any longer rail at the vices of their betters.", "그러니 높은 자리에 있는 이들이여, 더는 아랫사람들의 무지를 업신여기지 말 것이며, 또 천한 이들이여, 더는 윗사람들의 악덕을 욕하지 말 일이다."]], [["Molly had seated herself some time before she was known by her neighbours.", "몰리는 한동안 자리에 앉아 있었으나 이웃들은 그녀를 알아보지 못했다."], ["And then a whisper ran through the whole congregation, “Who is she?”", "그러다 이윽고 회중 전체에 \"저 여자가 누구지?\" 하는 수군거림이 번졌다."], ["but when she was discovered, such sneering, gigling, tittering, and laughing ensued among the women, that Mr Allworthy was obliged to exert his authority to preserve any decency among them.", "그러나 그녀의 정체가 드러나자, 여인들 사이에서 어찌나 비웃고 킥킥대고 낄낄대고 깔깔대는 소리가 이어졌던지, 올워디 씨가 직접 위엄을 내세워 그 자리에 그나마 체면이라도 지키게 해야 할 지경이었다."]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["A battle sung by the muse in the Homerican style, and which none but the classical reader can taste.", "호메로스풍으로 뮤즈가 노래하는 한바탕 전투, 고전에 밝은 독자가 아니고서는 그 맛을 알 수 없는 이야기."]], [["Mr Western had an estate in this parish; and as his house stood at little greater distance from this church than from his own, he very often came to Divine Service here; and both he and the charming Sophia happened to be present at this time.", "웨스턴 씨는 이 교구에 영지를 두고 있었다. 그의 저택이 자기 집 교회보다 이 교회에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 있었던 까닭에, 그는 이곳으로 예배를 보러 오는 일이 잦았다. 그리고 마침 이날, 그와 사랑스러운 소피아가 함께 자리하고 있었다."]], [["Sophia was much pleased with the beauty of the girl, whom she pitied for her simplicity in having dressed herself in that manner, as she saw the envy which it had occasioned among her equals.", "소피아는 그 처녀의 아름다움에 무척 마음이 끌렸고, 동시에 그토록 차려입은 그 단순함을 가엾이 여겼다. 그것이 또래들 사이에 불러일으킨 시샘을 보았기 때문이다."], ["She no sooner came home than she sent for the gamekeeper, and ordered him to bring his daughter to her; saying she would provide for her in the family, and might possibly place the girl about her own person, when her own maid, who was now going away, had left her.", "그녀는 집에 돌아오자마자 사냥터지기를 불러, 딸을 데려오라 일렀다. 집안에서 그 아이를 거두어 주겠노라고, 지금 떠나려 하는 자기 하녀가 나가고 나면 어쩌면 그 처녀를 자기 곁에 둘지도 모르겠다고 말하면서."]], [["Poor Seagrim was thunderstruck at this; for he was no stranger to the fault in the shape of his daughter.", "가엾은 시그림은 이 말에 벼락을 맞은 듯 얼어붙었다. 그는 딸의 몸매에 생긴 허물을 모르는 바가 아니었던 것이다."], ["He answered, in a stammering voice, “That he was afraid Molly would be too awkward to wait on her ladyship, as she had never been at service.”", "그는 더듬거리는 목소리로 대답했다. \"몰리가 한 번도 일을 해 본 적이 없으니 아씨를 시중들기엔 너무 서투를까 두렵습니다.\""], ["“No matter for that,”", "\"그런 건 상관없네.\""], ["says Sophia; “she will soon improve.", "소피아가 말했다. \"곧 나아질 게야."], ["I am pleased with the girl, and am resolved to try her.”", "나는 그 아이가 마음에 드니, 한번 써 보기로 작정했네.\""]], [["Black George now repaired to his wife, on whose prudent counsel he depended to extricate him out of this dilemma; but when he came thither he found his house in some confusion.", "블랙 조지는 이제 아내에게로 발길을 돌렸다. 이 곤경에서 자기를 빼내 줄 아내의 슬기로운 조언에 기대를 걸었던 것이다."], ["So great envy had this sack occasioned, that when Mr Allworthy and the other gentry were gone from church, the rage, which had hitherto been confined, burst into an uproar; and, having vented itself at first in opprobrious words, laughs, hisses, and gestures, betook itself at last to certain missile weapons; which, though from their plastic nature they threatened neither the loss of life or of limb, were however sufficiently dreadful to a well-dressed lady.", "그러나 막상 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 집안이 어수선해진 것을 보았다. 그 화려한 드레스가 어찌나 큰 시샘을 불러일으켰던지, 올워디 씨와 다른 양반들이 교회를 떠나자, 그때까지 억눌려 있던 노여움이 한바탕 소동으로 터져 나왔던 것이다."], ["Molly had too much spirit to bear this treatment tamely.", "그 노여움은 처음에는 욕설과 웃음과 야유와 손짓으로 분출되더니, 마침내는 어떤 던지는 무기들에까지 손을 뻗쳤다. 그것들은 무른 성질을 지닌 탓에 목숨이나 사지를 잃을 위협까지는 아니었으나, 잘 차려입은 처녀에게는 충분히 끔찍한 것이었다."], ["Having therefore--but hold, as we are diffident of our own abilities, let us here invite a superior power to our assistance.", "몰리는 이런 대접을 고분고분 견디기엔 너무도 기가 셌다. 그리하여 그녀는 ─ 그러나 잠깐, 우리 자신의 재주가 미덥지 못하니, 여기서는 우리보다 한 수 위인 어떤 힘을 불러 도움을 청하기로 하자."]], [["Ye Muses, then, whoever ye are, who love to sing battles, and principally thou who whilom didst recount the slaughter in those fields where Hudibras and Trulla fought, if thou wert not starved with thy friend Butler, assist me on this great occasion.", "오, 뮤즈들이여, 그대들이 누구든, 전투를 노래하기 좋아하는 이들이여, 특히 일찍이 후디브라스와 트룰라가 싸우던 그 들판의 살육을 읊었던 그대여, 만일 그대가 벗 버틀러와 함께 굶주려 죽지 않았다면, 이 위대한 자리에서 나를 도우라."], ["All things are not in the power of all.", "모든 것이 모든 이의 능력 안에 있는 것은 아니니."]], [["As a vast herd of cows in a rich farmer's yard, if, while they are milked, they hear their calves at a distance, lamenting the robbery which is then committing, roar and bellow; so roared forth the Somersetshire mob an hallaloo, made up of almost as many squalls, screams, and other different sounds as there were persons, or indeed passions among them: some were inspired by rage, others alarmed by fear, and others had nothing in their heads but the love of fun; but chiefly Envy, the sister of Satan, and his constant companion, rushed among the crowd, and blew up the fury of the women; who no sooner came up to Molly than they pelted her with dirt and rubbish.", "부유한 농부의 마당에 모인 한 무리의 소 떼가, 젖을 짜이는 동안 멀리서 자기 송아지가 그 빼앗김을 슬퍼하며 우는 소리를 듣고는 으르렁대고 울부짖듯이, 그렇게 서머싯셔의 군중도 함성을 내질렀다. 그 안에 모인 사람의 수만큼이나, 아니 그들 속에 든 감정의 수만큼이나 많은 비명과 고함과 갖가지 소리가 뒤섞인 함성이었다. 어떤 이들은 노여움에 사로잡혔고, 어떤 이들은 두려움에 떨었으며, 또 어떤 이들은 그저 재미를 좇는 마음뿐 머릿속에 다른 것이라곤 없었다. 그러나 무엇보다도 사탄의 누이이자 그의 한결같은 동무인 시샘이 군중 속으로 뛰어들어, 여자들의 광기를 부채질했다. 그들은 몰리에게 다가서기가 무섭게 진흙과 쓰레기를 퍼부었다."]], [["Molly, having endeavoured in vain to make a handsome retreat, faced about; and laying hold of ragged Bess, who advanced in the front of the enemy, she at one blow felled her to the ground.", "몰리는 보기 좋게 물러나 보려 했으나 헛수고였고, 마침내 돌아서서 맞섰다. 그녀는 적의 선두에서 나아오던 누더기 베스를 붙잡아, 단 한 방에 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["The whole army of the enemy (though near a hundred in number), seeing the fate of their general, gave back many paces, and retired behind a new-dug grave; for the churchyard was the field of battle, where there was to be a funeral that very evening.", "적의 온 군대는 (그 수가 백에 가까웠음에도) 자기네 장수의 운명을 보고는 여러 걸음 물러나, 새로 판 무덤 뒤로 후퇴했다. 교회 묘지가 바로 전쟁터였던 것인데, 그날 저녁에 그곳에서 장례가 치러질 참이었다."], ["Molly pursued her victory, and catching up a skull which lay on the side of the grave, discharged it with such fury, that having hit a taylor on the head, the two skulls sent equally forth a hollow sound at their meeting, and the taylor took presently measure of his length on the ground, where the skulls lay side by side, and it was doubtful which was the more valuable of the two.", "몰리는 승세를 몰아, 무덤가에 놓여 있던 해골 하나를 집어 들어 어찌나 사납게 내던졌던지, 그것이 한 재단사의 머리에 맞으면서 두 해골이 부딪치며 똑같이 텅 빈 소리를 울려 냈다. 재단사는 그 자리에서 땅바닥에 제 키를 쟀고, 두 해골은 나란히 누워 둘 중 어느 것이 더 값진지 가늠하기 어려웠다."], ["Molly then taking a thigh-bone in her hand, fell in among the flying ranks, and dealing her blows with great liberality on either side, overthrew the carcass of many a mighty heroe and heroine.", "그러고 나서 몰리는 넓적다리뼈 하나를 손에 쥐고 달아나는 대열 한복판으로 뛰어들어, 양옆으로 후하게 매질을 베풀며 숱한 용맹한 영웅과 여걸의 몸뚱이를 거꾸러뜨렸다."]], [["Recount, O Muse, the names of those who fell on this fatal day.", "오, 뮤즈여, 이 운명의 날에 쓰러진 자들의 이름을 읊어 다오."], ["First, Jemmy Tweedle felt on his hinder head the direful bone.", "먼저 제미 트위들이 뒤통수에 그 무시무시한 뼈를 맞았다."], ["Him the pleasant banks of sweetly-winding Stour had nourished, where he first learnt the vocal art, with which, wandering up and down at wakes and fairs, he cheered the rural nymphs and swains, when upon the green they interweaved the sprightly dance; while he himself stood fiddling and jumping to his own music.", "그를 길러 낸 것은 굽이굽이 다정하게 흐르는 스타우어 강의 정겨운 강둑이었으니, 그가 노래의 재주를 처음 배운 곳이 바로 거기였다. 그는 그 재주를 들고 밤샘 잔치와 장터를 이리저리 떠돌며, 시골 처녀와 총각들이 풀밭 위에서 흥겨운 춤을 엮어 갈 때 그들을 즐겁게 해 주었다."], ["How little now avails his fiddle!", "그러는 동안 그 자신은 제 가락에 맞추어 바이올린을 켜고 폴짝거렸다."], ["He thumps the verdant floor with his carcass.", "이제 그의 바이올린이 무슨 소용이랴!"], ["Next, old Echepole, the sowgelder, received a blow in his forehead from our Amazonian heroine, and immediately fell to the ground.", "그는 제 몸뚱이로 푸른 바닥을 쿵 두드린다. 다음으로 늙은 돼지 거세업자 에키폴이 우리의 여전사 같은 여걸에게 이마를 한 대 맞고 곧장 땅에 고꾸라졌다."], ["He was a swinging fat fellow, and fell with almost as much noise as a house.", "그는 살집이 출렁이는 뚱뚱한 사내라, 집채만 한 소리를 내며 쓰러졌다."], ["His tobacco-box dropped at the same time from his pocket, which Molly took up as lawful spoils.", "동시에 그의 담배통이 주머니에서 떨어졌는데, 몰리는 그것을 정당한 전리품으로 챙겼다."], ["Then Kate of the Mill tumbled unfortunately over a tombstone, which catching hold of her ungartered stocking inverted the order of nature, and gave her heels the superiority to her head.", "그다음에는 방앗간의 케이트가 운 나쁘게도 묘비에 걸려 나뒹굴었으니, 그 묘비가 대님 풀린 그녀의 양말을 붙잡는 바람에 자연의 질서가 뒤집혀, 발꿈치가 머리보다 위로 솟구쳤다."], ["Betty Pippin, with young Roger her lover, fell both to the ground; where, oh perverse fate!", "베티 피핀은 젊은 연인 로저와 함께 둘 다 땅에 쓰러졌는데, 아, 짓궂은 운명이여!"], ["she salutes the earth, and he the sky.", "그녀는 땅에 인사하고 그는 하늘에 인사했다."], ["Tom Freckle, the smith's son, was the next victim to her rage.", "대장장이의 아들 톰 프레클이 그녀의 노여움에 쓰러진 다음 제물이었다."], ["He was an ingenious workman, and made excellent pattens; nay, the very patten with which he was knocked down was his own workmanship.", "그는 솜씨 좋은 장인이라 훌륭한 나막신을 만들었으니, 더구나 그를 때려눕힌 바로 그 나막신이 제 손으로 만든 것이었다."], ["Had he been at that time singing psalms in the church, he would have avoided a broken head.", "그 시각에 교회에서 시편을 부르고 있었더라면 머리가 깨지는 일은 면했으련만."], ["Miss Crow, the daughter of a farmer; John Giddish, himself a farmer; Nan Slouch, Esther Codling, Will Spray, Tom Bennet; the three Misses Potter, whose father keeps the sign of the Red Lion; Betty Chambermaid, Jack Ostler, and many others of inferior note, lay rolling among the graves.", "농부의 딸 크로 양, 그 자신이 농부인 존 기디시, 낸 슬라우치, 에스더 코들링, 윌 스프레이, 톰 베닛, 붉은 사자 간판을 내건 아버지를 둔 포터가의 세 자매, 하녀 베티, 마부 잭, 그 밖에도 이름이 덜 알려진 숱한 이들이 무덤들 사이에 나뒹굴었다."]], [["Not that the strenuous arm of Molly reached all these; for many of them in their flight overthrew each other.", "물론 몰리의 억센 팔이 이들 모두에게 닿은 것은 아니었다. 그들 가운데 많은 이가 달아나다가 서로 걸려 넘어졌으니 말이다."]], [["But now Fortune, fearing she had acted out of character, and had inclined too long to the same side, especially as it was the right side, hastily turned about: for now Goody Brown--whom Zekiel Brown caressed in his arms; nor he alone, but half the parish besides; so famous was she in the fields of Venus, nor indeed less in those of Mars. The trophies of both these her husband always bore about on his head and face; for if ever human head did by its horns display the amorous glories of a wife, Zekiel's did; nor did his well-scratched face less denote her talents (or rather talons) of a different kind.", "그러나 이제 운명의 여신은, 자기가 본분에 어긋나게 행동했다 싶고, 너무 오래 한쪽 편으로만 기울었다 ─ 더구나 그것이 옳은 편이었으니 ─ 싶어, 황급히 방향을 틀었다. 바야흐로 구디 브라운이 나선 것이다. 지키엘 브라운이 두 팔로 어루만지던 여인, 아니 그뿐 아니라 교구의 절반이 어루만지던 여인이었으니, 그만큼 그녀는 비너스의 들판에서 이름이 높았고, 마르스의 들판에서도 못지않았다. 그 두 들판에서 얻은 전리품을 그녀의 남편은 늘 제 머리와 얼굴에 달고 다녔다. 일찍이 사람의 머리가 그 뿔로 아내의 음탕한 영광을 드러낸 적이 있다면, 지키엘의 머리가 바로 그러했고, 잔뜩 할퀴어진 그의 얼굴 또한 그녀의 또 다른 종류의 재능을 (아니, 차라리 발톱이라 해야 할까) 적잖이 일러 주었다."]], [["No longer bore this Amazon the shameful flight of her party.", "이 여전사는 제 편의 수치스러운 패주를 더는 두고 볼 수 없었다."], ["She stopt short, and, calling aloud to all who fled, spoke as follows: “Ye Somersetshire men, or rather ye Somersetshire women, are ye not ashamed thus to fly from a single woman?", "그녀는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 달아나는 모든 이를 향해 큰 소리로 이렇게 외쳤다. \"이 서머싯셔 사내들아, 아니 차라리 서머싯셔 아낙들아, 너희는 이렇게 계집 하나한테서 달아나는 것이 부끄럽지도 않으냐?"], ["But if no other will oppose her, I myself and Joan Top here will have the honour of the victory.”", "허나 아무도 저 계집을 막지 않겠다면, 나와 여기 이 조앤 톱이 승리의 영예를 차지하겠다.\""], ["Having thus said, she flew at Molly Seagrim, and easily wrenched the thigh-bone from her hand, at the same time clawing off her cap from her head.", "이렇게 말하고 나서 그녀는 몰리 시그림에게 달려들어, 손쉽게 그 손에서 넓적다리뼈를 비틀어 빼앗고, 동시에 그녀의 머리에서 모자를 낚아챘다."], ["Then laying hold of the hair of Molly with her left hand, she attacked her so furiously in the face with the right, that the blood soon began to trickle from her nose.", "그러고는 왼손으로 몰리의 머리채를 움켜쥐고, 오른손으로 어찌나 사납게 얼굴을 후려치는지, 이내 코에서 피가 줄줄 흘러내리기 시작했다."], ["Molly was not idle this while.", "그러는 동안 몰리도 가만있지 않았다."], ["She soon removed the clout from the head of Goody Brown, and then fastening on her hair with one hand, with the other she caused another bloody stream to issue forth from the nostrils of the enemy.", "그녀는 곧 구디 브라운의 머리에서 두건을 벗겨 내고, 한 손으로 머리채를 거머쥔 채 다른 손으로 적의 콧구멍에서도 또 한 줄기 핏줄기가 터져 나오게 했다."]], [["When each of the combatants had borne off sufficient spoils of hair from the head of her antagonist, the next rage was against the garments.", "두 싸움꾼이 각자 상대의 머리에서 머리카락을 넉넉히 전리품으로 뜯어낸 다음, 그다음 분노는 옷가지를 향했다."], ["In this attack they exerted so much violence, that in a very few minutes they were both naked to the middle.", "이 공격에서 그들은 어찌나 거센 힘을 쏟았던지, 불과 몇 분도 채 안 되어 둘 다 허리까지 알몸이 되었다."]], [["It is lucky for the women that the seat of fistycuff war is not the same with them as among men; but though they may seem a little to deviate from their sex, when they go forth to battle, yet I have observed, they never so far forget, as to assail the bosoms of each other; where a few blows would be fatal to most of them.", "여자들에게 다행스러운 점은, 주먹다짐 전쟁의 요새가 그들에게는 남자들과 같지 않다는 것이다. 비록 싸움터에 나설 때면 그들이 제 성별에서 조금 벗어난 듯 보일지라도, 내가 보아 온 바로는, 서로의 가슴팍을 공격할 만큼 그렇게까지 잊는 법은 결코 없다."], ["This, I know, some derive from their being of a more bloody inclination than the males.", "그곳이라면 몇 대만 맞아도 대개는 치명적일 텐데 말이다. 어떤 이들은 이를 두고, 여자들이 남자들보다 피를 더 즐기는 기질을 타고난 탓이라 말하기도 한다."], ["On which account they apply to the nose, as to the part whence blood may most easily be drawn; but this seems a far-fetched as well as ill-natured supposition.", "그래서 피가 가장 쉽게 흐르는 부위인 코를 노린다는 것이다. 그러나 이는 억지스러운 데다 심술궂은 추측으로 보인다."]], [["Goody Brown had great advantage of Molly in this particular; for the former had indeed no breasts, her bosom (if it may be so called), as well in colour as in many other properties, exactly resembling an antient piece of parchment, upon which any one might have drummed a considerable while without doing her any great damage.", "구디 브라운은 이 점에서 몰리보다 크게 유리했다. 전자에게는 실로 젖가슴이라 할 것이 없었으니, 그 가슴팍은 (그렇게 부를 수 있다면 말이다) 빛깔로 보나 다른 여러 성질로 보나 오래된 양피지 한 조각을 꼭 빼닮아서, 누구든 거기를 한참을 두드려 대도 그녀에게 별 손상을 입히지 못했을 것이다."]], [["Molly, beside her present unhappy condition, was differently formed in those parts, and might, perhaps, have tempted the envy of Brown to give her a fatal blow, had not the lucky arrival of Tom Jones at this instant put an immediate end to the bloody scene.", "몰리는 지금 처한 딱한 형편은 그렇다 치더라도, 그 부위가 사뭇 다르게 빚어져 있었으니, 어쩌면 브라운의 시샘을 자극해 치명적인 일격을 부르고 말았을지도 모른다. 다행히 바로 이 순간 톰 존스가 운 좋게 당도하여 이 피비린내 나는 장면에 즉각 종지부를 찍지 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["This accident was luckily owing to Mr Square; for he, Master Blifil, and Jones, had mounted their horses, after church, to take the air, and had ridden about a quarter of a mile, when Square, changing his mind (not idly, but for a reason which we shall unfold as soon as we have leisure), desired the young gentlemen to ride with him another way than they had at first purposed.", "이 우연한 등장은 운 좋게도 스퀘어 씨 덕분이었다. 그와 블리필 도련님과 존스는 예배가 끝난 뒤 바람을 쐬러 말에 올라 사 분의 일 마일쯤 달려갔는데, 그때 스퀘어가 마음을 바꾸어 (괜한 변덕이 아니라 한 가지 이유가 있었으니, 그것은 우리에게 틈이 나는 대로 곧 펼쳐 보이겠다) 두 젊은이에게 처음 작정했던 길과는 다른 길로 함께 가자고 청했던 것이다."], ["This motion being complied with, brought them of necessity back again to the churchyard.", "이 청이 받아들여진 탓에 그들은 어쩔 수 없이 다시 교회 묘지로 되돌아오게 되었다."]], [["Master Blifil, who rode first, seeing such a mob assembled, and two women in the posture in which we left the combatants, stopt his horse to enquire what was the matter.", "앞장서 말을 달리던 블리필 도련님은 그렇게 모여든 군중과, 우리가 떠나올 때의 그 자세로 있는 두 여인을 보고는, 무슨 일인가 물어보려 말을 세웠다. 한 시골뜨기가 머리를 긁적이며 그에게 대답했다."], ["A country fellow, scratching his head, answered him: “I don't know, measter, un't I; an't please your honour, here hath been a vight, I think, between Goody Brown and Moll Seagrim.”", "\"지가 알게 뭡니까요, 나리. 황송하오나, 여기서 한바탕 싸움이 벌어진 모양인데요, 구디 브라운하고 몰 시그림 사이에 말입죠.\""]], [["“Who, who?”", "\"누구, 누구라고?\""], ["cries Tom; but without waiting for an answer, having discovered the features of his Molly through all the discomposure in which they now were, he hastily alighted, turned his horse loose, and, leaping over the wall, ran to her.", "톰이 외쳤다. 그러나 대답을 기다리지도 않고, 그는 지금 온통 흐트러진 와중에도 제 몰리의 얼굴 생김새를 알아보고는, 황급히 말에서 내려 말을 풀어놓고 담장을 뛰어넘어 그녀에게 달려갔다."], ["She now first bursting into tears, told him how barbarously she had been treated.", "이제야 비로소 그녀는 울음을 터뜨리며, 자기가 얼마나 야만스러운 대접을 받았는지 그에게 일러 주었다."], ["Upon which, forgetting the sex of Goody Brown, or perhaps not knowing it in his rage--for, in reality, she had no feminine appearance but a petticoat, which he might not observe--he gave her a lash or two with his horsewhip; and then flying at the mob, who were all accused by Moll, he dealt his blows so profusely on all sides, that unless I would again invoke the muse (which the good-natured reader may think a little too hard upon her, as she hath so lately been violently sweated), it would be impossible for me to recount the horse-whipping of that day.", "이에 그는 구디 브라운이 여자라는 사실을 잊고서, 아니 어쩌면 노여움에 사로잡혀 그것을 알아채지 못하고서 ─ 사실 그녀에게는 페티코트 말고는 여자다운 데라곤 없었는데, 그마저도 그는 눈에 들어오지 않았을 것이다 ─ 그녀에게 말채찍으로 한두 대를 후려쳤다. 그러고는 몰이 죄다 지목한 군중에게 달려들어, 사방으로 어찌나 거침없이 매질을 퍼부었던지, 내가 또다시 뮤즈를 불러내지 않고서는 (마음씨 고운 독자는 뮤즈가 방금 그토록 호되게 땀을 뺀 터라 이를 좀 가혹하다 여길지도 모르겠지만) 그날의 채찍질을 도무지 다 읊을 수 없을 지경이었다."]], [["Having scoured the whole coast of the enemy, as well as any of Homer's heroes ever did, or as Don Quixote or any knight-errant in the world could have done, he returned to Molly, whom he found in a condition which must give both me and my reader pain, was it to be described here.", "호메로스의 어느 영웅 못지않게, 또 돈키호테나 이 세상의 어느 편력 기사 못지않게 적의 온 진영을 말끔히 쓸어 버린 뒤, 그는 몰리에게로 돌아왔다. 그녀가 처한 형편은, 여기서 묘사한다면 나와 독자 모두에게 고통을 안길 만한 것이었다."], ["Tom raved like a madman, beat his breast, tore his hair, stamped on the ground, and vowed the utmost vengeance on all who had been concerned.", "톰은 미친 사람처럼 길길이 날뛰며, 제 가슴을 치고, 머리를 쥐어뜯고, 땅을 구르며, 이 일에 가담한 모든 자에게 끝까지 복수하겠노라 맹세했다."], ["He then pulled off his coat, and buttoned it round her, put his hat upon her head, wiped the blood from her face as well as he could with his handkerchief, and called out to the servant to ride as fast as possible for a side-saddle, or a pillion, that he might carry her safe home.", "그러고는 제 외투를 벗어 그녀에게 입혀 단추를 채우고, 제 모자를 그녀의 머리에 씌우고, 손수건으로 그녀의 얼굴에서 피를 되도록 깨끗이 닦아 낸 다음, 하인에게 외쳐 되도록 빨리 말을 달려 옆안장이나 뒷안장을 구해 오라 일렀다. 그녀를 무사히 집으로 데려가기 위해서였다."]], [["Master Blifil objected to the sending away the servant, as they had only one with them; but as Square seconded the order of Jones, he was obliged to comply.", "블리필 도련님은 하인을 보내는 데 반대했다. 그들에게 하인이 하나밖에 없었던 것이다. 그러나 스퀘어가 존스의 명령을 거들어 주는 바람에, 그는 따를 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The servant returned in a very short time with the pillion, and Molly, having collected her rags as well as she could, was placed behind him.", "하인은 아주 짧은 시간 만에 뒷안장을 가지고 돌아왔고, 몰리는 제 누더기를 되도록 잘 추슬러 그의 뒤에 앉혀졌다."], ["In which manner she was carried home, Square, Blifil, and Jones attending.", "그렇게 그녀는 집으로 실려 갔으며, 스퀘어와 블리필과 존스가 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Here Jones having received his coat, given her a sly kiss, and whispered her, that he would return in the evening, quitted his Molly, and rode on after his companions.", "이때 존스는 제 외투를 돌려받고, 그녀에게 슬그머니 입맞춤을 한 뒤, 저녁에 다시 오겠노라 속삭이고는 제 몰리와 작별하고 일행을 뒤쫓아 말을 달려갔다."]], [["Containing matter of no very peaceable colour.", "9장. 그다지 평화롭다고는 할 수 없는 빛깔의 일을 담은 장."]], [["Molly had no sooner apparelled herself in her accustomed rags, than her sisters began to fall violently upon her, particularly her eldest sister, who told her she was well enough served.", "몰리가 늘 입던 누더기로 다시 갈아입기가 무섭게 언니들이 사납게 그녀에게 달려들었다. 특히 맏언니가 그러했는데, 꼴좋다고 쏘아붙였다."], ["“How had she the assurance to wear a gown which young Madam Western had given to mother!", "\"어디서 감히 어머니한테 웨스턴 댁 아씨가 주신 가운을 입을 생각을 한 거야!"], ["If one of us was to wear it, I think,”", "우리 중 누가 그걸 입어야 한다면 말이야,\" 맏언니가 말했다."], ["says she, “I myself have the best right; but I warrant you think it belongs to your beauty.", "\"내가 제일 자격이 있다고 생각해. 그런데 넌 그게 네 미모한테나 어울린다고 여기겠지."], ["I suppose you think yourself more handsomer than any of us.”--“Hand her down the bit of glass from over the cupboard,”", "우리 중에 네가 제일 곱다고 생각하는 모양이지.\"—\"찬장 위에 있는 거울 조각이나 갖다줘,\" 다른 언니가 소리쳤다."], ["cries another; “I'd wash the blood from my face before I talked of my beauty.”--“You'd better have minded what the parson says,”", "\"나 같으면 미모 운운하기 전에 얼굴에 묻은 피부터 씻겠다.\"—\"넌 목사님 말씀이나 새겨들었어야지,\" 맏언니가 외쳤다."], ["cries the eldest, “and not a harkened after men voke.”--“Indeed, child, and so she had,”", "\"사내들 꽁무니나 좇지 말고.\"—\"정말이지, 얘야, 그랬어야 했어,\""], ["says the mother, sobbing: “she hath brought a disgrace upon us all.", "어머니가 흐느끼며 말했다. \"이 애가 우리 모두에게 망신을 안겼어."], ["She's the vurst of the vamily that ever was a whore.”", "우리 집안에서 갈보가 된 건 얘가 처음이라고.\""]], [["“You need not upbraid me with that, mother,”", "\"그걸로 저를 나무라실 건 없잖아요, 어머니,\""], ["cries Molly; “you yourself was brought-to-bed of sister there, within a week after you was married.”", "몰리가 외쳤다. \"어머니도 결혼한 지 일주일도 안 돼서 저기 언니를 낳으셨으면서.\""]], [["“Yes, hussy,” answered the enraged mother, “so I was, and what was the mighty matter of that? I was made an honest woman then; and if you was to be made an honest woman, I should not be angry; but you must have to doing with a gentleman, you nasty slut; you will have a bastard, hussy, you will; and that I defy any one to say of me.”", "\"그래, 이 못된 것아,\" 격분한 어머니가 대꾸했다. \"그랬다, 그래서 그게 무슨 대단한 일이라도 되더냐? 난 그때 떳떳한 여자가 됐어. 너도 떳떳한 여자가 된다면야 화도 안 내지. 그런데 넌 하필 신사 나리랑 놀아나야겠더냐, 이 더러운 계집년아. 넌 사생아를 배게 될 거다, 이것아, 그렇고말고. 그런 소리는 누구도 나한테는 못 한다, 어디 해 봐라.\""]], [["In this situation Black George found his family, when he came home for the purpose before mentioned.", "바로 이런 판국에 블랙 조지가 앞서 말한 용건을 가지고 집에 돌아와 가족을 마주했다."], ["As his wife and three daughters were all of them talking together, and most of them crying, it was some time before he could get an opportunity of being heard; but as soon as such an interval occurred, he acquainted the company with what Sophia had said to him.", "아내와 세 딸이 한꺼번에 떠들어 대고, 그중 대부분이 울고 있었던 탓에, 그가 자기 말을 들려줄 틈을 얻기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 그러나 그런 틈이 생기자마자, 그는 소피아가 자기에게 한 말을 식구들에게 전했다."]], [["Goody Seagrim then began to revile her daughter afresh.", "그러자 시그림 아주머니가 다시금 딸을 욕하기 시작했다."], ["“Here,”", "\"거 봐라,\""], ["says she, “you have brought us into a fine quandary indeed.", "그녀가 말했다. \"네가 우릴 아주 고약한 곤경에 빠뜨렸어."], ["What will madam say to that big belly?", "그 불룩한 배를 보면 아씨가 뭐라고 하시겠냐?"], ["Oh that ever I should live to see this day!”", "아이고, 이 꼴을 보자고 내가 여태 살았더란 말이냐!\""]], [["Molly answered with great spirit, “And what is this mighty place which you have got for me, father?”", "몰리가 기죽지 않고 대꾸했다. \"그래서 아버지가 저한테 구해 주셨다는 그 대단한 자리란 게 뭔데요?\""], ["(for he had not well understood the phrase used by Sophia of being about her person).", "(소피아가 쓴 '몸종으로 곁에 두겠다'는 말의 뜻을 그가 제대로 알아듣지 못했던 까닭이다.) \"요리사 밑이겠죠, 뭐."], ["“I suppose it is to be under the cook; but I shan't wash dishes for anybody.", "하지만 난 누구를 위해서도 설거지 따위 안 할 거예요."], ["My gentleman will provide better for me.", "우리 나리께서 더 잘 챙겨 주실 테니까."], ["See what he hath given me this afternoon.", "오늘 오후에 나한테 뭘 주셨는지 보세요."], ["He hath promised I shall never want money; and you shan't want money neither, mother, if you will hold your tongue, and know when you are well.”", "나리는 내가 평생 돈 걱정 안 하게 해 주겠다고 약속하셨어요. 그리고 어머니도 입만 다물고 가만히 분수를 지키면, 돈 걱정 안 하실 거예요.\""], ["And so saying, she pulled out several guineas, and gave her mother one of them.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 기니 금화 몇 닢을 꺼내 어머니에게 한 닢을 건넸다."]], [["The good woman no sooner felt the gold within her palm, than her temper began (such is the efficacy of that panacea) to be mollified.", "착한 아주머니는 손바닥 안에 금화의 감촉을 느끼기가 무섭게 (그 만병통치약의 효험이란 이런 것이다) 성미가 누그러지기 시작했다."], ["“Why, husband,”", "\"아니, 영감,\""], ["says she, “would any but such a blockhead as you not have enquired what place this was before he had accepted it?", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신처럼 멍청한 사람 말고 누가, 받아들이기 전에 그게 무슨 자리인지부터 물어보지 않았겠어요?"], ["Perhaps, as Molly says, it may be in the kitchen; and truly I don't care my daughter should be a scullion wench; for, poor as I am, I am a gentlewoman.", "어쩌면 몰리 말마따나 부엌일일지도 모르지. 솔직히 난 내 딸이 부엌데기 노릇 하는 건 싫어요."], ["And thof I was obliged, as my father, who was a clergyman, died worse than nothing, and so could not give me a shilling of potion, to undervalue myself by marrying a poor man; yet I would have you to know, I have a spirit above all them things.", "내 비록 가난하지만 양가집 여자라고요. 목사였던 우리 아버지가 빈털터리만도 못한 채로 돌아가시는 바람에 내게 한 푼 지참금도 못 물려주셨고, 그래서 나도 가난한 남자랑 결혼해 스스로를 낮출 수밖에 없었지만, 그래도 알아 두세요, 난 그런 모든 것보다 높은 기개를 가진 사람이에요."], ["Marry come up!", "원, 별꼴이야!"], ["it would better become Madam Western to look at home, and remember who her own grandfather was.", "웨스턴 아씨야말로 제 집안이나 돌아보고, 제 할아버지가 누구였는지나 기억하는 게 좋을걸."], ["Some of my family, for aught I know, might ride in their coaches, when the grandfathers of some voke walked a-voot.", "내가 알기로 우리 집안 사람들 중엔, 어떤 이들 할아버지가 두 발로 걸어 다닐 때 마차를 타고 다닌 사람들도 있었다고요."], ["I warrant she fancies she did a mighty matter, when she sent us that old gownd; some of my family would not have picked up such rags in the street; but poor people are always trampled upon.--The parish need not have been in such a fluster with Molly.", "그 낡은 가운 한 벌 보내 주고선 무슨 대단한 일이라도 한 줄 아나 본데, 우리 집안 사람들 같으면 그런 누더기는 길에 떨어져 있어도 줍지 않았을 거예요. 그저 가난한 사람들은 늘 짓밟히기 마련이지.—교구 사람들도 몰리 일로 그렇게 호들갑 떨 것 없었어."], ["You might have told them, child, your grandmother wore better things new out of the shop.”", "얘야, 너도 그 사람들한테 말해 줬어야지. 네 할머니는 그보다 더 좋은 옷을 가게에서 새것으로 사 입었다고 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, but consider,”", "\"아 그래, 그건 그렇다 치고,\""], ["cried George, “what answer shall I make to madam?”", "조지가 외쳤다. \"내가 아씨한테 무슨 대답을 해야 하느냐고.\""]], [["“I don't know what answer,”", "\"무슨 대답이고 뭐고 난 모르겠소,\""], ["says she; “you are always bringing your family into one quandary or other.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 늘 이렇게 식구들을 이런저런 곤경에 몰아넣는다니까."], ["Do you remember when you shot the partridge, the occasion of all our misfortunes?", "당신이 자고새를 쏴서 우리 모든 불행의 발단을 만든 거 기억나요?"], ["Did not I advise you never to go into Squire Western's manor?", "내가 웨스턴 나리 영지엔 절대 들어가지 말라고 충고하지 않았어요?"], ["Did not I tell you many a good year ago what would come of it?", "그 일이 어떻게 될지 내가 몇 해 전부터 누누이 말하지 않았느냐고요?"], ["But you would have your own headstrong ways; yes, you would, you villain.”", "그런데도 당신은 기어이 그 고집대로만 했지. 그래, 그랬어, 이 못된 사람아.\""]], [["Black George was, in the main, a peaceable kind of fellow, and nothing choleric nor rash; yet did he bear about him something of what the antients called the irascible, and which his wife, if she had been endowed with much wisdom, would have feared.", "블랙 조지는 대체로 평화로운 부류의 사내여서, 성을 잘 내지도 경솔하지도 않았다. 그러나 그에게도 옛사람들이 '발끈하는 기질'이라 부르던 무언가가 깃들어 있었으니, 만일 그의 아내에게 지혜가 넉넉했더라면 그것을 두려워했을 것이다."], ["He had long experienced, that when the storm grew very high, arguments were but wind, which served rather to increase, than to abate it.", "그는 오래도록 겪어 보아 알고 있었다. 폭풍이 거세게 일 때 논리란 그저 바람일 뿐이어서, 그것을 가라앉히기는커녕 오히려 부추긴다는 것을."], ["He was therefore seldom unprovided with a small switch, a remedy of wonderful force, as he had often essayed, and which the word villain served as a hint for his applying.", "그래서 그는 작은 회초리 하나쯤은 늘 지니고 다녔는데, 여러 차례 써 본 결과 그것은 놀라운 효력을 지닌 처방이었으며, '못된 사람'이라는 말이 그 처방을 쓰라는 신호 노릇을 했다."]], [["No sooner, therefore, had this symptom appeared, than he had immediate recourse to the said remedy, which though, as it is usual in all very efficacious medicines, it at first seemed to heighten and inflame the disease, soon produced a total calm, and restored the patient to perfect ease and tranquillity.", "그러므로 이 증상이 나타나기가 무섭게, 그는 즉시 앞서 말한 처방에 손을 댔다. 매우 효험 좋은 모든 약이 다 그러하듯 처음에는 병세를 한층 돋우고 들끓게 하는 듯 보였으나, 이내 완전한 평온을 가져와 환자를 더없는 안락과 고요로 되돌려 놓았다."]], [["This is, however, a kind of horse-medicine, which requires a very robust constitution to digest, and is therefore proper only for the vulgar, unless in one single instance, viz., where superiority of birth breaks out; in which case, we should not think it very improperly applied by any husband whatever, if the application was not in itself so base, that, like certain applications of the physical kind which need not be mentioned, it so much degrades and contaminates the hand employed in it, that no gentleman should endure the thought of anything so low and detestable.", "하지만 이것은 일종의 말에게나 쓰는 독한 약이어서, 받아 삭이려면 아주 튼튼한 체질이 필요하고, 따라서 오직 천한 사람들에게나 맞는 처방이다. 다만 한 가지 경우만은 예외인데, 곧 태생의 우월함이 불쑥 튀어나오는 경우다. 그럴 때라면 어떤 남편이 그 처방을 쓴들 그리 잘못이라 여기지 않을 법도 하다. 단, 그 행위 자체가 워낙 비천한 것이어서, 굳이 입에 올릴 것도 없는 어떤 의술적 처치들처럼, 그 일에 쓰인 손을 너무도 깎아내리고 더럽히는 까닭에, 점잖은 신사라면 그토록 낮고 가증스러운 짓은 떠올리는 것조차 견디지 못할 것이다."]], [["The whole family were soon reduced to a state of perfect quiet; for the virtue of this medicine, like that of electricity, is often communicated through one person to many others, who are not touched by the instrument.", "온 가족은 곧 완전한 평정 상태로 돌아갔다. 이 약의 효능이란 전기의 그것과 같아서, 흔히 한 사람을 통해 그 도구에 닿지도 않은 여러 사람에게로 전해지기 때문이다."], ["To say the truth, as they both operate by friction, it may be doubted whether there is not something analogous between them, of which Mr Freke would do well to enquire, before he publishes the next edition of his book.", "사실대로 말하자면, 둘 다 마찰로 작용하는 것이니, 그 사이에 어떤 유사한 점이 있지 않을까 의심해 볼 만하다. 프리크 씨가 자기 책의 다음 판을 내기 전에 한번 잘 살펴보면 좋을 일이다."]], [["A council was now called, in which, after many debates, Molly still persisting that she would not go to service, it was at length resolved, that Goody Seagrim herself should wait on Miss Western, and endeavour to procure the place for her eldest daughter, who declared great readiness to accept it: but Fortune, who seems to have been an enemy of this little family, afterwards put a stop to her promotion.", "이제 회의가 소집되었고, 여러 논의 끝에, 몰리가 여전히 하녀로는 가지 않겠다고 버티는 통에, 마침내 시그림 아주머니가 직접 웨스턴 아가씨를 찾아뵙고 맏딸을 위해 그 자리를 얻어 보기로 결정되었다. 맏딸은 기꺼이 받아들이겠노라 밝혔다. 그러나 이 작은 가족에게 적이었던 듯한 운명의 여신은, 그 후 그녀의 출세에 제동을 걸고 말았다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["A story told by Mr Supple, the curate. The penetration of Squire Western. His great love for his daughter, and the return to it made by her.", "이튿날 아침 톰 존스는 웨스턴 씨와 함께 사냥을 나갔고, 돌아오는 길에 그 신사로부터 만찬에 초대받았다."]], [["The next morning Tom Jones hunted with Mr Western, and was at his return invited by that gentleman to dinner.", "사랑스러운 소피아는 그날따라 여느 때보다 한층 밝고 생기 넘치는 모습으로 빛났다."], ["The lovely Sophia shone forth that day with more gaiety and sprightliness than usual.", "그녀의 포화는 분명 우리의 주인공을 겨누고 있었다."], ["Her battery was certainly levelled at our heroe; though, I believe, she herself scarce yet knew her own intention; but if she had any design of charming him, she now succeeded.", "비록 그녀 자신조차 아직 제 속내를 잘 알지 못했으리라 믿지만, 그를 매혹할 의도가 조금이라도 있었다면, 이날 그녀는 보기 좋게 성공한 셈이었다."]], [["Mr Supple, the curate of Mr Allworthy's parish, made one of the company.", "올워디 씨 교구의 부목사인 서플 씨도 그 자리에 함께한 사람 중 하나였다."], ["He was a good-natured worthy man; but chiefly remarkable for his great taciturnity at table, though his mouth was never shut at it.", "그는 천성이 너그럽고 훌륭한 사람이었으나, 무엇보다 식탁에서 유난히 말이 없는 것으로 이름났다. 물론 그렇다고 그 자리에서 입이 닫혀 있는 것은 결코 아니었다."], ["In short, he had one of the best appetites in the world.", "한마디로 그는 세상에서 손꼽히게 식욕이 좋은 사람이었다."], ["However, the cloth was no sooner taken away, than he always made sufficient amends for his silence: for he was a very hearty fellow; and his conversation was often entertaining, never offensive.", "그러나 식탁보가 걷히기가 무섭게 그는 늘 자신의 침묵을 충분히 보상하고도 남았다. 워낙 마음이 따뜻한 친구였고, 그의 이야기는 종종 즐거웠으되 결코 거슬리는 법이 없었다."]], [["At his first arrival, which was immediately before the entrance of the roast-beef, he had given an intimation that he had brought some news with him, and was beginning to tell, that he came that moment from Mr Allworthy's, when the sight of the roast-beef struck him dumb, permitting him only to say grace, and to declare he must pay his respect to the baronet, for so he called the sirloin.", "그가 처음 도착한 것은 구운 쇠고기가 들어오기 바로 직전이었는데, 그는 가져온 소식이 있노라고 넌지시 말을 꺼내며, 방금 올워디 씨 댁에서 오는 길이라고 이야기를 시작하려던 참이었다. 그러나 구운 쇠고기를 보는 순간 그는 입이 딱 막혀, 겨우 식전 기도를 올리고는 저 남작께 경의를 표해야겠다고 한마디 했을 뿐이었다. 그가 남작이라 부른 것은 다름 아닌 등심 고기였다."]], [["When dinner was over, being reminded by Sophia of his news, he began as follows: “I believe, lady, your ladyship observed a young woman at church yesterday at even-song, who was drest in one of your outlandish garments; I think I have seen your ladyship in such a one. However, in the country, such dresses are", "만찬이 끝나자 소피아가 그의 소식을 다시 일깨워 주었고, 그는 다음과 같이 입을 열었다. \"마님, 어제 저녁 기도 때 교회에서 젊은 여인 하나를 보셨을 줄 압니다. 마님께서나 입으실 법한, 별스러운 옷을 차려입고 있었지요. 마님께서 그런 옷을 입으신 걸 본 적이 있는 것 같습니다. 그렇지만 시골에서는 그런 차림이란,"]], [["Rara avis in terris, nigroque simillima cygno.", "라라 아비스 인 테르리스, 니그로퀘 시밀리마 키그노."]], [["That is, madam, as much as to say, 'A rare bird upon the earth, and very like a black swan.'", "라는 거지요. 부인, 풀어 말하자면 '땅 위의 진귀한 새요, 검은 백조와 매우 흡사하다'는 뜻입니다."], ["The verse is in Juvenal.", "유베날리스의 시구지요."], ["But to return to what I was relating.", "그러나 다시 제가 하던 이야기로 돌아가자면, 그런 옷차림은 시골에서 보기 드문 광경이라는 말이었습니다."], ["I was saying such garments are rare sights in the country; and perchance, too, it was thought the more rare, respect being had to the person who wore it, who, they tell me, is the daughter of Black George, your worship's gamekeeper, whose sufferings, I should have opined, might have taught him more wit, than to dress forth his wenches in such gaudy apparel.", "게다가 어쩌면 그 옷을 입은 사람으로 인해 더욱 진귀하게 여겨졌는지도 모릅니다. 사람들이 말하기를 그 여인은 나리의 사냥터지기인 검은 조지의 딸이라더군요."], ["She created so much confusion in the congregation, that if Squire Allworthy had not silenced it, it would have interrupted the service: for I was once about to stop in the middle of the first lesson.", "제 생각으로는, 그가 겪은 고초가 그에게 더 분별을 가르쳐 주어, 제 딸년들을 그토록 화려한 옷으로 치장하지 않게 했어야 마땅했을 텐데 말입니다. 그 여인이 회중 가운데 어찌나 큰 소란을 일으켰던지, 만약 올워디 나리께서 잠재우지 않으셨다면 예배가 중단될 뻔했습니다."], ["Howbeit, nevertheless, after prayer was over, and I was departed home, this occasioned a battle in the churchyard, where, amongst other mischief, the head of a travelling fidler was very much broken.", "저 자신도 첫 번째 성경 봉독 중간에 한 번은 멈추려 했을 정도였으니까요. 그렇긴 하나, 어쨌든 기도가 끝나고 제가 집으로 떠난 뒤에, 이 일이 교회 묘지에서 한바탕 싸움을 일으켰고, 그 와중에 다른 험한 일들 가운데 떠돌이 바이올린 악사 하나가 머리가 몹시 깨지는 일까지 있었습니다."], ["This morning the fidler came to Squire Allworthy for a warrant, and the wench was brought before him.", "오늘 아침 그 악사가 올워디 나리께 영장을 청하러 왔고, 그 여인도 나리 앞으로 끌려왔지요."], ["The squire was inclined to have compounded matters; when, lo!", "나리께서는 일을 원만히 무마하려 하셨습니다. 그런데, 보십시오!"], ["on a sudden the wench appeared (I ask your ladyship's pardon) to be, as it were, at the eve of bringing forth a bastard.", "갑자기 그 여인이 (마님, 송구합니다) 말하자면 사생아를 막 낳을 임박에 이른 것으로 드러나는 게 아니겠습니까."], ["The squire demanded of her who was the father?", "나리께서 그 아비가 누구냐고 물으셨지요."], ["But she pertinaciously refused to make any response.", "그러나 그 여인은 한사코 아무 대답도 하지 않으려 했습니다."], ["So that he was about to make her mittimus to Bridewell when I departed.”", "그래서 제가 떠날 무렵 나리께서는 그 여인을 브라이드웰 감화원으로 보내는 수감 영장을 발부하려던 참이었습니다.\""]], [["“And is a wench having a bastard all your news, doctor?”", "\"아니, 계집애가 사생아 가진 게 자네 소식의 전부란 말인가, 박사 양반?\""], ["cries Western; “I thought it might have been some public matter, something about the nation.”", "웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"난 무슨 공적인 일이라도, 나라에 관한 무슨 일이라도 되는 줄 알았네.\""]], [["“I am afraid it is too common, indeed,”", "\"그게 너무 흔한 일이긴 합니다, 정말로요.\""], ["answered the parson; “but I thought the whole story altogether deserved commemorating.", "목사가 대답했다. \"하지만 이 이야기 전체가 한번 두루 곱씹어 볼 만하다고 여겼지요."], ["As to national matters, your worship knows them best.", "나랏일로 말하자면 나리께서 가장 잘 아시지 않습니까."], ["My concerns extend no farther than my own parish.”", "제 소관은 제 교구 너머로는 미치지 못합니다.\""]], [["“Why, ay,”", "\"하긴, 그렇지.\""], ["says the squire, “I believe I do know a little of that matter, as you say.", "지주가 말했다. \"자네 말마따나 그 일이라면 나도 좀 안다고 할 수 있지."], ["But, come, Tommy, drink about; the bottle stands with you.”", "자, 그건 그렇고, 토미, 한 잔 돌리게. 술병이 자네 앞에 있구먼.\""]], [["Tom begged to be excused, for that he had particular business; and getting up from table, escaped the clutches of the squire, who was rising to stop him, and went off with very little ceremony.", "톰은 따로 볼일이 있다며 양해를 구하고는, 식탁에서 일어나 자기를 붙들려고 몸을 일으키는 지주의 손아귀를 빠져나가, 거의 예의도 차리지 않고 떠나 버렸다."]], [["The squire gave him a good curse at his departure; and then turning to the parson, he cried out, “I smoke it: I smoke it.", "지주는 그가 떠날 때 한바탕 욕을 퍼붓고는, 목사를 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"감 잡았네, 감 잡았어."], ["Tom is certainly the father of this bastard.", "톰이 분명 이 사생아의 아비야."], ["Zooks, parson, you remember how he recommended the veather o' her to me.", "이런, 목사 양반, 그놈이 그 계집의 아비를 내게 천거하던 거 기억나지 않나."], ["D--n un, what a sly b--ch 'tis.", "빌어먹을, 그 음흉한 놈 같으니."], ["Ay, ay, as sure as two-pence, Tom is the veather of the bastard.”", "그래, 그래, 두 푼짜리만큼이나 틀림없이 톰이 그 사생아의 아비야.\""]], [["“I should be very sorry for that,”", "\"그렇다면 참으로 안타까운 일이겠습니다.\""], ["says the parson.", "목사가 말했다."]], [["“Why sorry,”", "\"안타깝긴 왜?\""], ["cries the squire: “Where is the mighty matter o't?", "지주가 소리쳤다. \"그게 무슨 대단한 일이라고?"], ["What, I suppose dost pretend that thee hast never got a bastard?", "뭐, 자네는 사생아 한 번 안 가져 봤다고 시치미라도 떼려는가? 빌어먹을!"], ["Pox! more good luck's thine?", "그럼 자네 운이 더 좋은 게지."], ["for I warrant hast a done a therefore many's the good time and often.”", "장담컨대 자네도 그런 짓을 좋은 시절에 여러 번, 그것도 자주 해 봤을 테니까.\""]], [["“Your worship is pleased to be jocular,” answered the parson; “but I do not only animadvert on the sinfulness of the action--though that surely is to be greatly deprecated--but I fear his unrighteousness may injure him with Mr Allworthy. And truly I must say, though he hath the character of being a little wild, I never saw any harm in the young man; nor can I say I have heard any, save what your worship now mentions. I wish, indeed, he was a little more regular in his responses at church; but altogether he seems", "\"나리께서 농을 즐기시는군요.\" 목사가 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 단지 그 행실의 죄스러움만을 나무라는 게 아닙니다. 물론 그것이야 분명 크게 경계해야 마땅한 일이지요. 다만 저는 그의 그 옳지 못한 행실이 올워디 씨와의 사이에서 그에게 해가 되지 않을까 염려하는 겁니다. 그리고 정말이지 말씀드리건대, 그가 좀 거칠다는 평판이야 있지만, 저는 그 젊은이에게서 어떤 해악도 본 적이 없습니다. 게다가 나리께서 지금 말씀하시는 것 말고는 그런 이야기를 들은 적도 없다 하겠습니다. 다만 그가 교회에서 응답할 때 조금 더 또박또박했으면 하는 바람이야 있지요. 그러나 두루 보아 그는,"]], [["Ingenui vultus puer ingenuique pudoris.", "인게누이 불투스 푸에르 인게누이퀘 푸도리스."]], [["That is a classical line, young lady; and, being rendered into English, is, `a lad of an ingenuous countenance, and of an ingenuous modesty;'", "한 듯합니다. 아가씨, 이건 고전의 한 구절입니다."], ["for this was a virtue in great repute both among the Latins and Greeks.", "영어로 옮기면 '천진한 얼굴빛에 천진한 부끄러움을 지닌 소년'이라는 뜻이지요. 이것이 라틴인들과 그리스인들 사이에서 크게 칭송받던 미덕이었으니까요."], ["I must say, the young gentleman (for so I think I may call him, notwithstanding his birth) appears to me a very modest, civil lad, and I should be sorry that he should do himself any injury in Squire Allworthy's opinion.”", "제가 말씀드리건대 그 젊은 신사는 (출생이야 어떻든 저는 그렇게 불러도 좋다고 생각합니다) 제게는 무척 겸손하고 예의 바른 젊은이로 보입니다. 그러니 그가 올워디 나리의 평가에 스스로 해를 입히는 일이 생긴다면 안타까울 따름입니다.\""]], [["“Poogh!”", "\"체!\""], ["says the squire: “Injury, with Allworthy!", "지주가 말했다. \"올워디한테 해를 입어?"], ["Why, Allworthy loves a wench himself.", "아니, 올워디 자신도 계집이라면 좋아하는 사람이야."], ["Doth not all the country know whose son Tom is?", "톰이 누구 아들인지 온 고을이 다 모른단 말인가?"], ["You must talk to another person in that manner.", "그딴 얘기는 다른 사람한테나 하게."], ["I remember Allworthy at college.”", "난 대학 시절의 올워디를 기억하거든.\""]], [["“I thought,”", "\"저는,\""], ["said the parson, “he had never been at the university.”", "목사가 말했다. \"그분이 대학에 다니신 적이 없는 줄 알았습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, he was,”", "\"아니야, 아니, 다녔다네.\""], ["says the squire: “and many a wench have we two had together.", "지주가 말했다. \"우리 둘이서 계집도 숱하게 데리고 놀았지."], ["As arrant a whore-master as any within five miles o'un.", "오 마일 안에서 둘째가라면 서러울 만큼 지독한 오입쟁이였어."], ["No, no.", "아니지, 아니야."], ["It will do'n no harm with he, assure yourself; nor with anybody else.", "그 일이 올워디한테는 아무 해도 안 될 걸세, 장담하지. 다른 누구한테도 마찬가지고."], ["Ask Sophy there--You have not the worse opinion of a young fellow for getting a bastard, have you, girl?", "저기 소피한테 물어보게. 너는 사생아 하나 가졌다고 젊은 녀석을 더 나쁘게 보지는 않겠지, 안 그러냐, 얘야?"], ["No, no, the women will like un the better for't.”", "아니, 아니지, 여자들은 그래서 그놈을 더 좋아할 게다.\""]], [["This was a cruel question to poor Sophia.", "이것은 가엾은 소피아에게 잔인한 질문이었다."], ["She had observed Tom's colour change at the parson's story; and that, with his hasty and abrupt departure, gave her sufficient reason to think her father's suspicion not groundless.", "그녀는 목사의 이야기에 톰의 얼굴빛이 변하는 것을 보았고, 그것이 그의 황급하고 돌연한 떠남과 더불어, 아버지의 의심이 근거 없는 것이 아니라고 여길 충분한 이유가 되었다. 이제 그녀의 마음은, 그토록 오랫동안 조금씩 조금씩 드러내 오던 그 커다란 비밀을 단번에 그녀에게 펼쳐 보였고, 그녀는 자신이 이 일에 깊이 마음 쓰고 있음을 깨달았다."], ["Her heart now at once discovered the great secret to her which it had been so long disclosing by little and little; and she found herself highly interested in this matter.", "그런 처지에서 아버지의 무례한 질문이 별안간 그녀에게 들이닥치자, 의심 많은 마음이라면 능히 놀랐을 만한 몇 가지 기색이 드러났다. 그러나 지주를 위해 공정히 말하자면, 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니었다."], ["In such a situation, her father's malapert question rushing suddenly upon her, produced some symptoms which might have alarmed a suspicious heart; but, to do the squire justice, that was not his fault.", "그래서 그녀가 의자에서 일어나, 아버지가 한마디 넌지시 비치기만 해도 늘 물러나기에 충분하다고 말하자, 그는 그녀를 방에서 나가게 두었다. 그러고는 무척 근엄한 얼굴로 한마디 했다."], ["When she rose therefore from her chair, and told him a hint from him was always sufficient to make her withdraw, he suffered her to leave the room, and then with great gravity of countenance remarked, “That it was better to see a daughter over-modest than over-forward;”--a sentiment which was highly applauded by the parson.", "\"딸자식이란 지나치게 앞서는 것보다 지나치게 얌전한 편을 보는 게 낫지.\" 이 말에 목사는 크게 갈채를 보냈다."]], [["There now ensued between the squire and the parson a most excellent political discourse, framed out of newspapers and political pamphlets; in which they made a libation of four bottles of wine to the good of their country: and then, the squire being fast asleep, the parson lighted his pipe, mounted his horse, and rode home.", "이제 지주와 목사 사이에는, 신문과 정치 소책자에서 짜낸 더없이 훌륭한 정치 담론이 이어졌다. 그 담론 속에서 그들은 나라의 안녕을 위해 포도주 네 병을 헌주로 바쳤다. 그러고는 지주가 곤히 잠들자, 목사는 파이프에 불을 붙이고 말에 올라 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["When the squire had finished his half-hour's nap, he summoned his daughter to her harpsichord; but she begged to be excused that evening, on account of a violent head-ache. This remission was presently granted; for indeed she seldom had occasion to ask him twice, as he loved her with such ardent affection, that, by gratifying her, he commonly conveyed the highest gratification to himself. She was really, what he frequently called her, his little darling, and she well deserved to be so; for she returned all his affection in the most ample manner. She had preserved the most inviolable duty to him in all things; and this her love made not only easy, but so delightful, that when one of her companions laughed at her for placing so much merit in such scrupulous obedience, as that young lady called it, Sophia answered, “You mistake me, madam, if you think I value myself upon this account; for besides that I am barely discharging my duty, I am likewise pleasing myself. I can truly say I have no delight equal to that of contributing to my father's happiness; and if I value myself, my dear, it is on having this power, and not on executing it.”", "지주가 반 시간의 낮잠을 끝내고 나서 딸을 불러 하프시코드 앞에 앉히려 했으나, 그녀는 심한 두통을 이유로 그날 저녁만은 양해를 구했다. 이 면제는 곧바로 허락되었다. 사실 그녀가 그에게 두 번 청해야 할 일은 좀처럼 없었으니, 그는 그녀를 어찌나 뜨거운 애정으로 사랑했던지, 그녀를 흡족하게 해 주는 일이 곧 자기 자신에게 더없는 흡족함을 안겨 주는 일이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 그가 자주 부르던 대로 정말이지 그의 작은 사랑이었고, 또한 그렇게 불릴 자격이 충분했다. 그녀는 그의 모든 애정에 더없이 넘치는 방식으로 보답했기 때문이다. 그녀는 모든 일에서 그에게 더없이 흠 없는 효성을 지켜 왔으며, 이 효성을 그녀의 사랑은 쉬운 일로 만들었을 뿐 아니라 무척 즐거운 일로 만들어 주었다. 그래서 한번은 그녀의 벗 하나가, 그 젊은 아가씨의 표현을 빌리자면 그런 까다로운 순종에 그토록 큰 가치를 둔다며 그녀를 비웃었을 때, 소피아는 이렇게 대답했다. \"제가 이 일로 스스로를 대단하게 여긴다고 생각하신다면, 저를 잘못 아신 거예요. 저는 그저 제 도리를 다하고 있을 뿐인 데다, 동시에 저 자신을 기쁘게 하고 있는 거니까요. 정말이지 저는 아버지의 행복에 보탬이 되는 것만큼 큰 기쁨을 알지 못해요. 그리고 제가 스스로를 대단히 여긴다면, 그건 그렇게 할 수 있는 힘을 가졌다는 점에서지, 그것을 실행한다는 점에서가 아니에요.\""]], [["This was a satisfaction, however, which poor Sophia was incapable of tasting this evening.", "그러나 이것은 가엾은 소피아가 이날 저녁만은 맛볼 수 없는 만족이었다."], ["She therefore not only desired to be excused from her attendance at the harpsichord, but likewise begged that he would suffer her to absent herself from supper.", "그래서 그녀는 하프시코드 앞에서 시중드는 일을 면해 달라고 청했을 뿐 아니라, 저녁 식사에도 빠지게 해 달라고 부탁했다."], ["To this request likewise the squire agreed, though not without some reluctance; for he scarce ever permitted her to be out of his sight, unless when he was engaged with his horses, dogs, or bottle.", "이 청에도 지주는 동의했으나, 다소 마지못한 기색이 없지는 않았다. 그가 말이나 개나 술병에 빠져 있을 때를 빼고는, 그녀를 자기 눈앞에서 벗어나게 두는 일이 좀처럼 없었기 때문이다."], ["Nevertheless he yielded to the desire of his daughter, though the poor man was at the same time obliged to avoid his own company (if I may so express myself), by sending for a neighbouring farmer to sit with him.", "그럼에도 그는 딸의 바람에 굽혔다. 다만 그 가엾은 사람은 동시에, (이렇게 표현해도 좋다면) 자기 자신과 단둘이 있는 것을 피하기 위해, 이웃 농부를 불러다 함께 앉아 있어야 했다."]], [["# Chapter xi.", "# Chapter xi."]], [["The narrow escape of Molly Seagrim, with some observations for which we have been forced to dive pretty deep into nature.", "훨씬 깊이 인간의 본성 속으로 잠수해 들어가야만 했던 몇 가지 관찰과 더불어, 몰리 시그림이 가까스로 위기를 모면한 일."]], [["Tom Jones had ridden one of Mr Western's horses that morning in the chase; so that having no horse of his own in the squire's stable, he was obliged to go home on foot: this he did so expeditiously that he ran upwards of three miles within the half-hour.", "톰 존스는 그날 아침 사냥에서 웨스턴 씨의 말 가운데 한 필을 타고 다녔다. 그래서 자기 말은 그 지주의 마구간에 없었던 터라, 집까지는 걸어서 돌아가는 수밖에 없었다. 그는 어찌나 부지런히 걸었던지, 반시간 만에 삼 마일 남짓을 내달렸다."]], [["Just as he arrived at Mr Allworthy's outward gate, he met the constable and company with Molly in their possession, whom they were conducting to that house where the inferior sort of people may learn one good lesson, viz., respect and deference to their superiors; since it must show them the wide distinction Fortune intends between those persons who are to be corrected for their faults, and those who are not; which lesson if they do not learn, I am afraid they very rarely learn any other good lesson, or improve their morals, at the house of correction.", "올워디 씨의 바깥 대문에 막 다다랐을 때, 그는 몰리를 붙잡아 끌고 가는 치안관 일행과 마주쳤다. 그들이 그녀를 데려가던 곳은, 신분이 낮은 부류의 사람들이 한 가지 좋은 교훈을 배울 수 있는 집, 곧 윗사람에 대한 존경과 공경을 깨우치는 곳이었다. 그곳은 운명이 정해 둔 커다란 차이를, 즉 자신의 잘못 때문에 교정을 받아야 하는 사람들과 그렇지 않은 사람들 사이의 차이를 그들에게 똑똑히 보여 줄 것이기 때문이다. 만일 그 교훈마저 배우지 못한다면, 그들은 교정의 집에서 다른 어떤 좋은 교훈도 좀처럼 배우지 못하고, 자기 품행을 나아지게 하는 일도 거의 없으리라 나는 생각한다."]], [["A lawyer may perhaps think Mr Allworthy exceeded his authority a little in this instance.", "법률가라면 어쩌면 올워디 씨가 이 일에서 자기 권한을 다소 넘어섰다고 여길지도 모른다."], ["And, to say the truth, I question, as here was no regular information before him, whether his conduct was strictly regular.", "그리고 솔직히 말하자면, 그의 앞에 정식 고발 절차가 없었던 만큼, 그의 처사가 엄밀하게 적법한 것이었는지는 나로서도 의문이다."], ["However, as his intention was truly upright, he ought to be excused in foro conscientiae; since so many arbitrary acts are daily committed by magistrates who have not this excuse to plead for themselves.", "그러나 그의 의도가 참으로 올곧았으니, 양심의 법정에서는 마땅히 용서받아야 할 것이다. 이만한 변명조차 내세울 수 없는 치안판사들이 날마다 그토록 많은 자의적 행위를 저지르고 있으니 말이다."]], [["Tom was no sooner informed by the constable whither they were proceeding (indeed he pretty well guessed it of himself), than he caught Molly in his arms, and embracing her tenderly before them all, swore he would murder the first man who offered to lay hold of her.", "톰은 치안관에게서 그들이 어디로 가는 길인지 듣자마자(사실 그는 혼자서도 거의 짐작하고 있었다), 곧장 몰리를 두 팔로 끌어안고는 모두가 보는 앞에서 그녀를 다정하게 껴안으며, 누구든 그녀에게 손대려 하는 자가 있으면 첫째로 그를 죽여 버리겠다고 맹세했다."], ["He bid her dry her eyes and be comforted; for, wherever she went, he would accompany her.", "그는 그녀에게 눈물을 닦고 마음을 놓으라고 일렀다. 그녀가 어디로 가든 자기가 함께 가겠노라고."], ["Then turning to the constable, who stood trembling with his hat off, he desired him, in a very mild voice, to return with him for a moment only to his father (for so he now called Allworthy); for he durst, he said, be assured, that, when he had alledged what he had to say in her favour, the girl would be discharged.", "그러고는 모자를 벗어 든 채 부들부들 떨며 서 있는 치안관을 돌아보며, 아주 부드러운 목소리로, 잠시만이라도 자기와 함께 아버지에게(이제 그는 올워디를 그렇게 불렀다) 돌아가 달라고 청했다. 그는 자신이 그녀를 위해 해야 할 말을 다 아뢰고 나면, 그 처녀가 분명히 풀려나리라고 장담했다."]], [["The constable, who, I make no doubt, would have surrendered his prisoner had Tom demanded her, very readily consented to this request.", "톰이 요구했다면 죄인을 순순히 내주었을 게 틀림없는 치안관은, 이 청에 아주 선뜻 응했다. 그리하여 그들은 모두 올워디 씨의 홀로 되돌아갔다."], ["So back they all went into Mr Allworthy's hall; where Tom desired them to stay till his return, and then went himself in pursuit of the good man.", "거기서 톰은 자기가 돌아올 때까지 그들에게 기다려 달라고 한 뒤, 직접 그 선량한 사람을 찾아 나섰다."], ["As soon as he was found, Tom threw himself at his feet, and having begged a patient hearing, confessed himself to be the father of the child of which Molly was then big.", "그를 찾아내자마자 톰은 그의 발치에 몸을 던졌고, 끝까지 들어 달라고 간청한 뒤, 몰리가 지금 배고 있는 아이의 아버지가 바로 자기라고 고백했다."], ["He entreated him to have compassion on the poor girl, and to consider, if there was any guilt in the case, it lay principally at his door.", "그는 그 가엾은 처녀를 불쌍히 여겨 달라고, 그리고 이 일에 무슨 죄가 있다면 그 죄는 주로 자기 쪽에 있음을 헤아려 달라고 애원했다."]], [["“If there is any guilt in the case!”", "\"이 일에 무슨 죄가 있다면이라니!\""], ["answered Allworthy warmly: “Are you then so profligate and abandoned a libertine to doubt whether the breaking the laws of God and man, the corrupting and ruining a poor girl be guilt?", "올워디가 발끈하여 대답했다. \"그래, 너는 하나님과 인간의 법을 어기는 것이, 가엾은 처녀를 타락시키고 파멸로 몰아넣는 것이 죄인지 아닌지를 의심할 만큼 그토록 방탕하고 막돼먹은 난봉꾼이란 말이냐?"], ["I own, indeed, it doth lie principally upon you; and so heavy it is, that you ought to expect it should crush you.”", "그래, 정말이지 그 죄는 주로 너에게 있다. 게다가 어찌나 무거운지, 너는 그것이 너를 짓눌러 으스러뜨릴 것을 각오해야 마땅하다.\""]], [["“Whatever may be my fate,”", "\"제 운명이 어찌 되든,\""], ["says Tom, “let me succeed in my intercessions for the poor girl.", "톰이 말했다. \"이 가엾은 처녀를 위한 제 간청만은 들어주십시오."], ["I confess I have corrupted her!", "제가 그녀를 타락시킨 것은 인정합니다!"], ["but whether she shall be ruined, depends on you.", "하지만 그녀가 파멸하느냐 마느냐는 어르신께 달려 있습니다."], ["For Heaven's sake, sir, revoke your warrant, and do not send her to a place which must unavoidably prove her destruction.”", "제발 부탁드립니다, 어르신, 영장을 거두어 주십시오. 그녀를 보내면 틀림없이 파멸하고 말 그런 곳으로는 보내지 말아 주십시오.\""]], [["Allworthy bid him immediately call a servant.", "올워디는 그에게 당장 하인을 부르라고 일렀다. 톰은 그럴 필요가 없다고 대답했다."], ["Tom answered there was no occasion; for he had luckily met them at the gate, and relying upon his goodness, had brought them all back into his hall, where they now waited his final resolution, which upon his knees he besought him might be in favour of the girl; that she might be permitted to go home to her parents, and not be exposed to a greater degree of shame and scorn than must necessarily fall upon her.", "다행히 대문에서 그들과 마주쳤고, 그의 선량함을 믿고서 그들을 모두 홀로 데려와 두었으니, 이제 그들이 거기서 그분의 최종 결정을 기다리고 있다는 것이었다. 그는 무릎을 꿇은 채, 부디 그 결정이 처녀에게 유리한 쪽이기를, 그녀가 부모에게 돌아가도록 허락받기를, 그리고 그녀에게 어쩔 수 없이 떨어질 수치와 멸시보다 더 큰 수치와 멸시에 내몰리지 않기를 빌었다."], ["“I know,”", "\"압니다,\""], ["said he, “that is too much.", "그가 말했다. \"그것조차 지나친 청인 줄 압니다."], ["I know I am the wicked occasion of it.", "제가 그 사악한 빌미를 만들었음을 압니다."], ["I will endeavour to make amends, if possible; and if you shall have hereafter the goodness to forgive me, I hope I shall deserve it.”", "가능하다면 보상하려 애쓰겠습니다. 그리고 언젠가 어르신께서 너그러이 저를 용서해 주신다면, 저는 그 용서를 받을 만한 사람이 되기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Allworthy hesitated some time, and at last said, “Well, I will discharge my mittimus.--You may send the constable to me.”", "올워디는 한동안 머뭇거리다가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"좋다, 구금 영장을 거두마."], ["He was instantly called, discharged, and so was the girl.", "치안관을 내게 보내거라.\" 곧 그가 불려 왔고, 임무가 해제되었으며, 그 처녀도 함께 풀려났다."]], [["It will be believed that Mr Allworthy failed not to read Tom a very severe lecture on this occasion; but it is unnecessary to insert it here, as we have faithfully transcribed what he said to Jenny Jones in the first book, most of which may be applied to the men, equally with the women.", "올워디 씨가 이 일을 두고 톰에게 아주 호된 훈계를 빼놓지 않았으리라는 점은 짐작이 갈 것이다. 다만 그것을 여기 굳이 옮길 필요는 없다."], ["So sensible an effect had these reproofs on the young man, who was no hardened sinner, that he retired to his own room, where he passed the evening alone, in much melancholy contemplation.", "우리가 첫째 권에서 그가 제니 존스에게 한 말을 충실히 옮겨 적어 두었으니, 그 대부분은 여자에게나 남자에게나 똑같이 적용될 수 있기 때문이다. 굳은살 박인 죄인이 아니었던 그 젊은이에게 이 꾸지람은 자못 크게 와닿아, 그는 자기 방으로 물러가 그날 저녁을 홀로 깊은 우울에 잠긴 채 보냈다."]], [["Allworthy was sufficiently offended by this transgression of Jones; for notwithstanding the assertions of Mr Western, it is certain this worthy man had never indulged himself in any loose pleasures with women, and greatly condemned the vice of incontinence in others. Indeed, there is much reason to imagine that there was not the least truth in what Mr Western affirmed, especially as he laid the scene of those impurities at the university, where Mr Allworthy had never been. In fact, the good squire was a little too apt to indulge that kind of pleasantry which is generally called rhodomontade: but which may, with as much propriety, be expressed by a much shorter word; and perhaps we too often supply the use of this little monosyllable by others; since very much of what frequently passes in the world for wit and humour, should, in the strictest purity of language, receive that short appellation, which, in conformity to the well-bred laws of custom, I here suppress.", "올워디는 존스의 이 잘못에 적잖이 마음이 상했다. 웨스턴 씨가 뭐라고 단언했든 간에, 이 훌륭한 사람이 여자와 어떤 방탕한 쾌락에 빠져 본 적이 한 번도 없으며, 남들의 음란한 악덕을 몹시 나무라곤 했다는 것은 분명한 사실이기 때문이다. 실로 웨스턴 씨가 단언한 말에는 손톱만큼의 진실도 없었으리라 짐작할 만한 근거가 충분하다. 더구나 그는 그 부정한 짓들의 무대를 대학으로 설정했는데, 올워디 씨는 대학 근처에도 가 본 적이 없었으니 말이다. 사실 그 선량한 지주는, 흔히 허풍이라 불리는 그런 농담을 조금 지나치게 즐기는 버릇이 있었다. 그런데 그 농담은 훨씬 더 짧은 한 마디로도 적절히 표현될 수 있는 것이다. 어쩌면 우리는 그 짧은 한 음절의 자리를 다른 말들로 너무 자주 메우고 있는지도 모른다. 세상에서 흔히 재치와 해학으로 통하는 것 가운데 상당수는, 언어의 가장 엄정한 순수성으로 따지자면 마땅히 그 짧은 명칭을 받아야 할 것인데, 나는 여기서 점잖은 관습의 법도를 좇아 그 말을 삼간다."]], [["But whatever detestation Mr Allworthy had to this or to any other vice, he was not so blinded by it but that he could discern any virtue in the guilty person, as clearly indeed as if there had been no mixture of vice in the same character.", "그러나 올워디 씨가 이 악덕이나 다른 어떤 악덕을 아무리 혐오한다 해도, 그 혐오에 눈이 멀어 죄지은 자에게 깃든 미덕을 못 알아볼 정도는 아니었다. 실로 그는, 같은 사람 안에 악덕이 조금도 섞여 있지 않을 때만큼이나 또렷이 그 미덕을 분간해 냈다."], ["While he was angry therefore with the incontinence of Jones, he was no less pleased with the honour and honesty of his self-accusation.", "그러므로 그는 존스의 음란함에는 노여워하면서도, 스스로를 고발해 보인 그 명예롭고 정직한 태도에는 그에 못지않게 흐뭇해했다."], ["He began now to form in his mind the same opinion of this young fellow, which, we hope, our reader may have conceived.", "그는 이제 이 젊은이에 대해, 우리 독자가 마음속에 품었기를 바라는 그런 의견을 마음속에 그리기 시작했다."], ["And in balancing his faults with his perfections, the latter seemed rather to preponderate.", "그리고 그의 결점을 그의 장점과 저울질해 보니, 오히려 장점 쪽으로 저울이 기우는 듯했다."]], [["It was to no purpose, therefore, that Thwackum, who was immediately charged by Mr Blifil with the story, unbended all his rancour against poor Tom.", "그러므로 블리필 씨에게서 곧장 이 이야기를 전해 들은 스왜컴이 가엾은 톰에게 온갖 앙심을 다 쏟아부은 것도 아무 소용이 없었다. 올워디는 그들의 험담을 끈기 있게 다 들어주고 나서 냉랭하게 대답했다."], ["Allworthy gave a patient hearing to their invectives, and then answered coldly: “That young men of Tom's complexion were too generally addicted to this vice; but he believed that youth was sincerely affected with what he had said to him on the occasion, and he hoped he would not transgress again.”", "\"톰 같은 기질의 젊은이들이 대체로 이런 악덕에 너무 쉽게 빠지는 법이오. 하지만 나는 그 젊은이가 이번 일로 내가 한 말에 진심으로 마음이 움직였다고 믿으며, 다시는 잘못을 저지르지 않으리라 바라오.\""], ["So that, as the days of whipping were at an end, the tutor had no other vent but his own mouth for his gall, the usual poor resource of impotent revenge.", "그리하여 매질의 시절은 이미 끝난 터라, 이 가정교사는 자기 입 말고는 쓸개즙을 풀 다른 출구가 없었으니, 그것은 무력한 복수가 흔히 기대는 가련한 수단이었다."]], [["But Square, who was a less violent, was a much more artful man; and as he hated Jones more perhaps than Thwackum himself did, so he contrived to do him more mischief in the mind of Mr Allworthy.", "그런데 스퀘어는 덜 거칠었던 대신 훨씬 더 교활한 사람이었다. 그가 어쩌면 스왜컴보다도 더 존스를 미워했던 만큼, 그는 올워디 씨의 마음속에서 톰에게 더 큰 해를 끼칠 궁리를 했다."]], [["The reader must remember the several little incidents of the partridge, the horse, and the Bible, which were recounted in the second book.", "독자는 둘째 권에서 이야기된 자고새와 말과 성경에 얽힌 자질구레한 몇 가지 사건을 기억할 것이다."], ["By all which Jones had rather improved than injured the affection which Mr Allworthy was inclined to entertain for him.", "그 모든 일을 통해 존스는, 올워디 씨가 그에게 품으려 하던 애정을 해치기보다는 오히려 키워 놓았다."], ["The same, I believe, must have happened to him with every other person who hath any idea of friendship, generosity, and greatness of spirit, that is to say, who hath any traces of goodness in his mind.", "우정과 너그러움과 큰 도량의 관념을 조금이라도 지닌 사람, 다시 말해 마음속에 선함의 자취를 조금이라도 지닌 사람이라면 누구에게나 그와 똑같은 일이 일어났으리라고 나는 믿는다."]], [["Square himself was not unacquainted with the true impression which those several instances of goodness had made on the excellent heart of Allworthy; for the philosopher very well knew what virtue was, though he was not always perhaps steady in its pursuit; but as for Thwackum, from what reason I will not determine, no such thoughts ever entered into his head: he saw Jones in a bad light, and he imagined Allworthy saw him in the same, but that he was resolved, from pride and stubbornness of spirit, not to give up the boy whom he had once cherished; since by so doing, he must tacitly acknowledge that his former opinion of him had been wrong.", "스퀘어 자신도, 그 여러 선행의 사례가 올워디의 그 빼어난 마음에 남긴 참된 인상을 모르지 않았다. 이 철학자는 비록 그것을 추구하는 데 늘 한결같지는 못했을지라도, 미덕이 무엇인지는 아주 잘 알고 있었던 것이다. 하지만 스왜컴으로 말하자면, 무슨 까닭에서인지는 단정하지 않겠으나, 그런 생각이라곤 그의 머릿속에 한 번도 들어선 적이 없었다. 그는 존스를 나쁜 쪽으로만 보았고, 올워디도 그를 같은 눈으로 보고 있으면서, 다만 자존심과 고집 때문에 한때 아꼈던 소년을 차마 내치지 못하는 것이라고 상상했다. 그렇게 하는 것은 곧 그 소년에 대한 자신의 예전 판단이 틀렸음을 슬며시 인정하는 셈이 되기 때문이라는 것이었다."]], [["Square therefore embraced this opportunity of injuring Jones in the tenderest part, by giving a very bad turn to all these before-mentioned occurrences.", "그리하여 스퀘어는 이 기회를 붙잡아, 앞서 말한 그 모든 사건들을 아주 나쁜 쪽으로 비틀어 풀이함으로써 존스의 가장 여린 부분을 찔러 상처를 입혔다."], ["“I am sorry, sir,”", "\"어르신,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “to own I have been deceived as well as yourself.", "\"유감스럽게도 저 역시 어르신과 마찬가지로 속았음을 고백하지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["I could not, I confess, help being pleased with what I ascribed to the motive of friendship, though it was carried to an excess, and all excess is faulty and vicious: but in this I made allowance for youth.", "솔직히 말씀드려, 저는 그것을 우정이라는 동기에서 비롯된 것으로 여겨 흐뭇해하지 않을 수 없었습니다. 비록 그것이 지나친 데까지 치달았고, 모든 지나침이란 결함이요 악덕이긴 하지만 말입니다."], ["Little did I suspect that the sacrifice of truth, which we both imagined to have been made to friendship, was in reality a prostitution of it to a depraved and debauched appetite.", "다만 이 점에서는 젊은 나이를 참작했지요. 진실을 희생한 그 행위가, 우리 둘 다 우정을 위한 희생이라 짐작했던 그 행위가, 실은 타락하고 방탕한 욕정에 진실을 팔아넘긴 짓이었으리라고는 꿈에도 의심치 못했습니다."], ["You now plainly see whence all the seeming generosity of this young man to the family of the gamekeeper proceeded.", "이제 어르신께서는 이 젊은이가 사냥터지기 가족에게 베푼 그 겉보기 너그러움이 다 어디서 비롯되었는지를 똑똑히 보실 것입니다."], ["He supported the father in order to corrupt the daughter, and preserved the family from starving, to bring one of them to shame and ruin.", "그는 딸을 타락시키기 위해 아비를 거두었고, 그 가족 가운데 하나를 수치와 파멸로 몰아넣으려고 그 집안을 굶주림에서 건져 낸 것입니다."], ["This is friendship!", "이것이 우정이라니!"], ["this is generosity!", "이것이 너그러움이라니!"], ["As Sir Richard Steele says, `Gluttons who give high prices for delicacies, are very worthy to be called generous.'", "리처드 스틸 경이 말한 대로, '진미에 비싼 값을 치르는 식충이들이야말로 너그럽다고 불릴 자격이 충분하다'는 격이지요."], ["In short I am resolved, from this instance, never to give way to the weakness of human nature more, nor to think anything virtue which doth not exactly quadrate with the unerring rule of right.”", "요컨대 저는 이 일을 계기로, 다시는 인간 본성의 나약함에 굴복하지 않으리라, 또 어김없는 올바름의 규칙에 정확히 들어맞지 않는 것은 그 무엇도 미덕이라 여기지 않으리라 결심했습니다.\""]], [["The goodness of Allworthy had prevented those considerations from occurring to himself; yet were they too plausible to be absolutely and hastily rejected, when laid before his eyes by another.", "올워디의 선량함은 그런 생각들이 스스로 떠오르는 것을 막아 주었다. 그러나 다른 사람이 그의 눈앞에 그것을 펼쳐 놓고 보니, 그 생각들은 단번에 또 성급히 물리쳐 버리기에는 너무나 그럴듯했다."], ["Indeed what Square had said sunk very deeply into his mind, and the uneasiness which it there created was very visible to the other; though the good man would not acknowledge this, but made a very slight answer, and forcibly drove off the discourse to some other subject.", "실로 스퀘어가 한 말은 그의 마음속에 아주 깊이 파고들었고, 그것이 거기 일으킨 불편함은 상대방의 눈에도 또렷이 보일 정도였다. 그 선량한 사람은 이것을 인정하려 들지 않고 아주 시큰둥하게 대답하며, 애써 화제를 다른 데로 돌려 버리긴 했지만 말이다."], ["It was well perhaps for poor Tom, that no such suggestions had been made before he was pardoned; for they certainly stamped in the mind of Allworthy the first bad impression concerning Jones.", "어쩌면 가엾은 톰으로서는, 그가 용서받기 전에 그런 말이 나오지 않았던 것이 다행이었는지도 모른다. 그 말은 분명 올워디의 마음속에 존스에 대한 첫 번째 나쁜 인상을 새겨 놓았으니 말이다."]], [["# Chapter xii.", "# Chapter xii."]], [["Containing much clearer matters; but which flowed from the same fountain with those in the preceding chapter.", "앞 장보다 훨씬 분명한 이야기를 담고 있으나, 그 앞 장의 사연과 같은 샘에서 흘러나온 것들에 관하여."]], [["The reader will be pleased, I believe, to return with me to Sophia.", "독자께서는 저와 함께 소피아에게로 돌아가 주시면 기쁘겠다."], ["She passed the night, after we saw her last, in no very agreeable manner.", "우리가 마지막으로 그녀를 본 뒤, 그녀는 그리 편안하지 못한 밤을 보냈다."], ["Sleep befriended her but little, and dreams less.", "잠은 그녀에게 좀처럼 벗이 되어 주지 않았고, 꿈은 더더욱 그러했다."], ["In the morning, when Mrs Honour, her maid, attended her at the usual hour, she was found already up and drest.", "아침에 시녀 어너 부인이 평소의 시각에 그녀를 시중들러 왔을 때, 그녀는 이미 일어나 옷까지 차려입은 채였다."]], [["Persons who live two or three miles' distance in the country are considered as next-door neighbours, and transactions at the one house fly with incredible celerity to the other.", "시골에서 이삼 마일 떨어져 사는 사람들은 바로 옆집 이웃으로 여겨지며, 한 집에서 일어난 일은 믿기 어려울 만큼 빠른 속도로 다른 집까지 날아간다."], ["Mrs Honour, therefore, had heard the whole story of Molly's shame; which she, being of a very communicative temper, had no sooner entered the apartment of her mistress, than she began to relate in the following manner:--", "그러니 어너 부인은 몰리의 수치에 얽힌 이야기를 죄다 들어 알고 있었다. 본디 입이 가벼운 성품인 그녀는 안주인의 방에 들어서기가 무섭게 다음과 같이 이야기를 풀어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“La, ma'am, what doth your la'ship think?", "\"어머, 아씨, 글쎄 아씨께선 어떻게 생각하세요?"], ["the girl that your la'ship saw at church on Sunday, whom you thought so handsome; though you would not have thought her so handsome neither, if you had seen her nearer, but to be sure she hath been carried before the justice for being big with child.", "일요일에 교회에서 아씨께서 보시고 그렇게 곱다고 여기셨던 그 처녀 말이에요. 하기야 가까이서 보셨더라면 그리 곱다고는 못 하셨겠지만요."], ["She seemed to me to look like a confident slut: and to be sure she hath laid the child to young Mr Jones.", "아무튼 그 애가 배가 불러서 치안판사 앞에 끌려갔다지 뭐예요. 제 눈엔 영 뻔뻔스러운 계집으로 보이더라니까요."], ["And all the parish says Mr Allworthy is so angry with young Mr Jones, that he won't see him.", "그러더니 그 애가 그 아이를 젊은 존스 도련님한테 떠넘겼답니다. 온 교구가 다 말하기를, 올워디 나리께서 젊은 존스 도련님한테 어찌나 화가 나셨는지 얼굴도 안 보려 하신대요."], ["To be sure, one can't help pitying the poor young man, and yet he doth not deserve much pity neither, for demeaning himself with such kind of trumpery.", "그야 그 가엾은 젊은 분이 딱하긴 하지요. 그래도 그런 천한 것과 어울려 제 몸을 그리 떨어뜨렸으니 동정받을 만한 처지도 못 되긴 하지요."], ["Yet he is so pretty a gentleman, I should be sorry to have him turned out of doors.", "그래도 워낙 잘생긴 신사분이라, 문밖으로 쫓겨나신다면 제가 다 안타까울 거예요. 장담컨대 그 계집도 도련님 못지않게 마음이 동했을 거예요."], ["I dares to swear the wench was as willing as he; for she was always a forward kind of body.", "늘 사내한테 들이대는 부류였으니까요. 계집들이 그렇게 먼저 달려드는데야 젊은 사내들을 그리 탓할 것도 없지요."], ["And when wenches are so coming, young men are not so much to be blamed neither; for to be sure they do no more than what is natural.", "그야 본성대로 행동한 것뿐일 테니까요. 사실 그런 더럽고 질질 끌리는 치마짜리들과 얽히는 건 도련님 체면을 깎는 일이지요."], ["Indeed it is beneath them to meddle with such dirty draggle-tails; and whatever happens to them, it is good enough for them.", "그런 것들이야 무슨 일을 당해도 싸요. 그래도 정작 가장 잘못한 건 그 천한 계집들이라니까요."], ["And yet, to be sure, the vile baggages are most in fault.", "진심으로 그것들을 수레 꽁무니에 매달아 채찍질이라도 해 줬으면 좋겠어요."], ["I wishes, with all my heart, they were well to be whipped at the cart's tail; for it is pity they should be the ruin of a pretty young gentleman; and nobody can deny but that Mr Jones is one of the most handsomest young men that ever----”", "잘생긴 젊은 신사분을 망쳐 놓다니 그게 어디 될 말이에요. 존스 도련님이 여태껏 본 가장 잘생긴 젊은 사내 가운데 한 분이란 건 누구도 부정 못 할……\""]], [["She was running on thus, when Sophia, with a more peevish voice than she had ever spoken to her in before, cried, “Prithee, why dost thou trouble me with all this stuff?", "이렇게 줄줄 늘어놓고 있을 때, 소피아가 여태껏 그녀에게 써 본 적 없는 한결 짜증 섞인 목소리로 외쳤다. \"제발, 어쩌자고 그런 시시한 이야기로 나를 성가시게 하는 거냐?"], ["What concern have I in what Mr Jones doth?", "존스 씨가 무얼 하든 그게 나와 무슨 상관이라고?"], ["I suppose you are all alike.", "내 보기엔 너희는 다 한통속이야."], ["And you seem to me to be angry it was not your own case.”", "게다가 너는 정작 네 일이 아니어서 분한 모양이로구나.\""]], [["“I, ma'am!”", "\"제가요, 아씨!\""], ["answered Mrs Honour, “I am sorry your ladyship should have such an opinion of me.", "어너 부인이 대꾸했다. \"아씨께서 저를 그리 보신다니 서운하옵니다."], ["I am sure nobody can say any such thing of me.", "정말이지 누구도 저를 두고 그런 말은 못 할 거예요."], ["All the young fellows in the world may go to the divil for me.", "세상 젊은 사내들이 죄다 악마한테 가 버려도 저는 상관없어요. 제가 그분더러 잘생겼다고 한 게 잘못인가요?"], ["Because I said he was a handsome man?", "그건 저뿐 아니라 다들 하는 말이에요."], ["Everybody says it as well as I.", "정말이지 젊은 사내더러 잘생겼다고 하는 게 무슨 흉이 될 줄은 미처 몰랐어요."], ["To be sure, I never thought as it was any harm to say a young man was handsome; but to be sure I shall never think him so any more now; for handsome is that handsome does.", "하지만 이제는 결코 그분을 그리 여기지 않겠어요. 잘생겼다는 건 행실이 잘생긴 걸 두고 하는 말이니까요."], ["A beggar wench!--”", "거지 계집 따위가!\""]], [["“Stop thy torrent of impertinence,”", "\"그 무엄한 말의 봇물을 멈추거라.\""], ["cries Sophia, “and see whether my father wants me at breakfast.”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"그리고 아버님께서 아침 식사 자리에 나를 찾으시는지 가서 보거라.\""]], [["Mrs Honour then flung out of the room, muttering much to herself, of which “Marry come up, I assure you,”", "그러자 어너 부인은 혼잣말을 잔뜩 중얼거리며 방을 박차고 나갔는데, 그중 또렷이 알아들을 수 있었던 것이라곤 \"흥, 정말 너무하시네, 참\""], ["was all that could be plainly distinguished.", "하는 말뿐이었다."]], [["Whether Mrs Honour really deserved that suspicion, of which her mistress gave her a hint, is a matter which we cannot indulge our reader's curiosity by resolving.", "어너 부인이 정말로 그 안주인이 넌지시 비친 의심을 받을 만했는지는, 그 답을 밝혀 독자의 호기심을 채워 드릴 수 없는 일이다."], ["We will, however, make him amends in disclosing what passed in the mind of Sophia.", "그래도 소피아의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 오갔는지를 드러냄으로써 그 보상은 해 드리겠다."]], [["The reader will be pleased to recollect, that a secret affection for Mr Jones had insensibly stolen into the bosom of this young lady.", "독자께서는, 존스 씨를 향한 은밀한 애정이 어느새 이 젊은 아가씨의 가슴속으로 슬며시 스며들었다는 것을 떠올려 주시면 기쁘겠다."], ["That it had there grown to a pretty great height before she herself had discovered it.", "그리고 그 애정은 그녀 자신이 미처 알아차리기도 전에 이미 제법 높은 데까지 자라 있었다는 것도."], ["When she first began to perceive its symptoms, the sensations were so sweet and pleasing, that she had not resolution sufficient to check or repel them; and thus she went on cherishing a passion of which she never once considered the consequences.", "처음 그 징후를 느끼기 시작했을 때, 그 감정이 어찌나 달콤하고 흐뭇했던지 그녀는 그것을 억누르거나 물리칠 만한 결심을 차마 내지 못했다. 그렇게 그녀는 그 끝이 어찌 될지 단 한 번도 헤아려 보지 않은 채 그 연정을 고이 품어 왔던 것이다."]], [["This incident relating to Molly first opened her eyes.", "몰리에 얽힌 이 사건이 처음으로 그녀의 눈을 뜨게 했다."], ["She now first perceived the weakness of which she had been guilty; and though it caused the utmost perturbation in her mind, yet it had the effect of other nauseous physic, and for the time expelled her distemper.", "그제야 그녀는 비로소 자신이 저지른 마음의 약함을 깨달았다. 그것이 그녀의 마음에 이루 말할 수 없는 동요를 일으키기는 했으나, 한편으로는 여느 역겨운 약과 같은 효력을 발휘하여 한동안 그 병을 몰아내 주었다."], ["Its operation indeed was most wonderfully quick; and in the short interval, while her maid was absent, so entirely removed all symptoms, that when Mrs Honour returned with a summons from her father, she was become perfectly easy, and had brought herself to a thorough indifference for Mr Jones.", "그 약효는 실로 놀랍도록 빨라서, 시녀가 자리를 비운 그 짧은 사이에 모든 징후를 어찌나 말끔히 걷어 갔던지, 어너 부인이 아버지의 부름을 전하러 돌아왔을 무렵에는 그녀가 완전히 마음이 편안해져, 존스 씨에 대해 철저한 무관심에 이르러 있었다."]], [["The diseases of the mind do in almost every particular imitate those of the body.", "마음의 병은 거의 모든 면에서 몸의 병을 그대로 흉내 낸다."], ["For which reason, we hope, that learned faculty, for whom we have so profound a respect, will pardon us the violent hands we have been necessitated to lay on several words and phrases, which of right belong to them, and without which our descriptions must have been often unintelligible.", "그런 까닭에, 우리가 그토록 깊이 존경하는 그 박식한 의학계 여러분께서, 본디 그분들의 것인 여러 낱말과 표현에 우리가 어쩔 수 없이 함부로 손을 댄 것을 부디 용서해 주시기를 바란다. 그런 말들이 없었다면 우리의 묘사는 흔히 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 것이 되고 말았을 테니 말이다."]], [["Now there is no one circumstance in which the distempers of the mind bear a more exact analogy to those which are called bodily, than that aptness which both have to a relapse.", "그런데 마음의 병이 이른바 몸의 병과 가장 정확히 닮은 점이 있다면, 그것은 양쪽 다 재발하기 쉽다는 그 성질이다."], ["This is plain in the violent diseases of ambition and avarice.", "야망과 탐욕이라는 격렬한 병에서 이 점은 분명히 드러난다."], ["I have known ambition, when cured at court by frequent disappointments (which are the only physic for it), to break out again in a contest for foreman of the grand jury at an assizes; and have heard of a man who had so far conquered avarice, as to give away many a sixpence, that comforted himself, at last, on his deathbed, by making a crafty and advantageous bargain concerning his ensuing funeral, with an undertaker who had married his only child.", "나는 궁정에서 거듭된 좌절(그것이야말로 야망의 유일한 약이다)로 야망이 다 나았던 사람이, 순회재판소에서 대배심 배심장 자리를 두고 다투며 그 병이 다시 도지는 것을 본 적이 있다. 또 탐욕을 어찌나 잘 다스렸던지 6펜스짜리 동전을 숱하게 베풀던 어떤 사람이, 끝내 임종의 자리에서, 자기 외동자식과 혼인한 어느 장의사와 자기 장례에 관하여 교활하고도 잇속 밝은 흥정을 맺으며 스스로를 위로했다는 이야기도 들은 바 있다."]], [["In the affair of love, which, out of strict conformity with the Stoic philosophy, we shall here treat as a disease, this proneness to relapse is no less conspicuous.", "사랑이라는 일에 있어서도 ─ 스토아 철학에 엄격히 따라 우리는 이것을 여기서 하나의 병으로 다루겠다 ─ 이 재발하기 쉬운 성질은 조금도 덜 뚜렷하지 않다."], ["Thus it happened to poor Sophia; upon whom, the very next time she saw young Jones, all the former symptoms returned, and from that time cold and hot fits alternately seized her heart.", "가엾은 소피아에게도 바로 그런 일이 일어났다. 그녀가 젊은 존스를 다시 본 바로 그 순간, 이전의 모든 징후가 되살아났고, 그때부터 차가운 발작과 뜨거운 발작이 번갈아 그녀의 가슴을 사로잡았다."]], [["The situation of this young lady was now very different from what it had ever been before.", "이제 이 젊은 아가씨의 처지는 이전 그 어느 때와도 사뭇 달라져 있었다."], ["That passion which had formerly been so exquisitely delicious, became now a scorpion in her bosom.", "한때 그토록 더없이 달콤하던 그 연정이 이제는 그녀의 가슴속 전갈이 되었다."], ["She resisted it therefore with her utmost force, and summoned every argument her reason (which was surprisingly strong for her age) could suggest, to subdue and expel it.", "그래서 그녀는 온 힘을 다해 그것에 맞섰고, 그것을 억누르고 몰아내기 위해 자신의 이성(나이에 비해 놀랍도록 강했다)이 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 논리를 끌어모았다."], ["In this she so far succeeded, that she began to hope from time and absence a perfect cure.", "이 일에서 그녀는 어느 정도 성공을 거두어, 시간과 떨어져 지냄으로써 완전히 나으리라는 희망을 품기 시작했다."], ["She resolved therefore to avoid Tom Jones as much as possible; for which purpose she began to conceive a design of visiting her aunt, to which she made no doubt of obtaining her father's consent.", "그리하여 그녀는 되도록 톰 존스를 피하기로 마음먹었고, 이를 위해 고모를 찾아뵙겠다는 계획을 세우기 시작했는데, 아버지의 승낙을 얻는 일이야 의심할 바 없다고 여겼다."]], [["But Fortune, who had other designs in her head, put an immediate stop to any such proceeding, by introducing an accident, which will be related in the next chapter.", "그러나 머릿속에 다른 속셈을 품고 있던 운명의 여신은, 한 가지 사건을 끼워 넣음으로써 그런 일을 당장에 가로막아 버렸다. 그 사건은 다음 장에서 이야기하겠다."]], [["# Chapter xiii.", "# Chapter xiii."]], [["A dreadful accident which befel Sophia.", "끔찍한 사고가 소피아에게 닥치다."], ["The gallant behaviour of Jones, and the more dreadful consequence of that behaviour to the young lady; with a short digression in favour of the female sex.", "존스의 용감한 행동, 그리고 그 행동이 이 젊은 아가씨에게 불러온 한층 더 끔찍한 결과. 더불어 여성을 옹호하는 짧은 여담."]], [["Mr Western grew every day fonder and fonder of Sophia, insomuch that his beloved dogs themselves almost gave place to her in his affections; but as he could not prevail on himself to abandon these, he contrived very cunningly to enjoy their company, together with that of his daughter, by insisting on her riding a hunting with him.", "웨스턴 씨는 날이 갈수록 소피아를 더욱 아끼게 되었고, 급기야 그토록 사랑하던 사냥개들조차 그의 애정 속에서 그녀에게 거의 자리를 내줄 지경이었다. 그러나 그는 차마 개들을 포기할 수 없었던 터라, 아주 교묘한 꾀를 내어, 딸에게 함께 사냥을 나가 말을 타라고 고집함으로써 개들과 딸의 곁을 한꺼번에 누리려 했다."]], [["Sophia, to whom her father's word was a law, readily complied with his desires, though she had not the least delight in a sport, which was of too rough and masculine a nature to suit with her disposition.", "아버지의 말이라면 곧 법이었던 소피아는, 자신의 성정에는 너무 거칠고 사내답기만 한 그 놀이에 조금의 즐거움도 느끼지 못했지만, 순순히 그 뜻을 따랐다."], ["She had however another motive, beside her obedience, to accompany the old gentleman in the chase; for by her presence she hoped in some measure to restrain his impetuosity, and to prevent him from so frequently exposing his neck to the utmost hazard.", "그러나 그녀가 노신사를 따라 사냥에 나선 데에는 순종 말고도 또 다른 까닭이 있었다. 자신이 곁에 있으면 어느 정도나마 아버지의 무모함을 억누를 수 있고, 그토록 자주 목숨을 위태로움 한복판에 내던지는 일을 막을 수 있으리라 바랐던 것이다."]], [["The strongest objection was that which would have formerly been an inducement to her, namely, the frequent meeting with young Jones, whom she had determined to avoid; but as the end of the hunting season now approached, she hoped, by a short absence with her aunt, to reason herself entirely out of her unfortunate passion; and had not any doubt of being able to meet him in the field the subsequent season without the least danger.", "가장 큰 걸림돌은, 예전 같았으면 도리어 그녀를 끌어당겼을 바로 그것, 곧 그녀가 피하기로 마음먹은 젊은 존스와 자주 마주치게 되는 일이었다. 그러나 이제 사냥철이 끝나갈 무렵이었기에, 그녀는 고모와 함께 잠시 떠나 있는 동안 자신의 불행한 연정을 이성으로 온전히 떨쳐 내기를 바랐다. 그리고 다음 철에 들판에서 그를 다시 만나더라도 조금의 위험도 없으리라 의심치 않았다."]], [["On the second day of her hunting, as she was returning from the chase, and was arrived within a little distance from Mr Western's house, her horse, whose mettlesome spirit required a better rider, fell suddenly to prancing and capering in such a manner that she was in the most imminent peril of falling.", "사냥에 나선 둘째 날, 그녀가 사냥을 마치고 돌아오다가 웨스턴 씨의 집에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 이르렀을 때였다. 더 나은 기수가 다루어야 마땅했을 만큼 혈기 넘치는 그녀의 말이 갑자기 껑충거리며 날뛰기 시작했고, 그녀는 당장이라도 떨어질 듯한 절박한 위험에 빠졌다."], ["Tom Jones, who was at a little distance behind, saw this, and immediately galloped up to her assistance.", "조금 뒤처져 있던 톰 존스가 이를 보고는 곧장 말을 몰아 그녀를 도우러 달려왔다."], ["As soon as he came up, he leapt from his own horse, and caught hold of hers by the bridle.", "다가오자마자 그는 자신의 말에서 뛰어내려 그녀의 말 고삐를 붙잡았다."], ["The unruly beast presently reared himself an end on his hind legs, and threw his lovely burthen from his back, and Jones caught her in his arms.", "그 사나운 짐승은 이내 뒷다리로 곧추서며 그 사랑스러운 짐을 등에서 내던졌고, 존스가 그녀를 두 팔로 받아 안았다."]], [["She was so affected with the fright, that she was not immediately able to satisfy Jones, who was very solicitous to know whether she had received any hurt.", "그녀는 놀란 가슴에 어찌나 충격을 받았던지, 혹 다친 데는 없는지 몹시 알고 싶어 하던 존스에게 곧바로 대답조차 하지 못했다."], ["She soon after, however, recovered her spirits, assured him she was safe, and thanked him for the care he had taken of her.", "그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 정신을 가다듬고, 무사하다고 그를 안심시키며, 자신을 돌봐 준 데 대해 고마움을 전했다. 존스가 대답했다."], ["Jones answered, “If I have preserved you, madam, I am sufficiently repaid; for I promise you, I would have secured you from the least harm at the expense of a much greater misfortune to myself than I have suffered on this occasion.”", "\"제가 아가씨를 구했다면, 그것으로 저는 충분히 보답받은 것입니다. 약속드리건대, 이번에 제가 겪은 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 불행을 무릅쓰고서라도 아가씨를 조금의 해도 입지 않게 지켰을 테니까요.\""]], [["“What misfortune?”", "\"무슨 불행 말씀이세요?\""], ["replied Sophia eagerly; “I hope you have come to no mischief?”", "소피아가 다급히 되물었다. \"설마 어디 다치신 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“Be not concerned, madam,”", "\"염려 마십시오, 아가씨.\""], ["answered Jones.", "존스가 대답했다."], ["“Heaven be praised you have escaped so well, considering the danger you was in.", "\"아가씨가 처했던 위험을 생각하면, 이렇게 무사하시니 하늘에 감사할 따름입니다."], ["If I have broke my arm, I consider it as a trifle, in comparison of what I feared upon your account.”", "제가 팔이 부러졌다 한들, 아가씨 일로 두려워하던 것에 견주면 그건 하찮은 일에 지나지 않습니다.\""]], [["Sophia then screamed out, “Broke your arm!", "그러자 소피아가 비명을 질렀다. \"팔이 부러지셨다고요!"], ["Heaven forbid.”", "어찌 그런 일이.\""]], [["“I am afraid I have, madam,”", "\"아무래도 그런 듯합니다, 아가씨.\""], ["says Jones: “but I beg you will suffer me first to take care of you.", "존스가 말했다. \"하지만 우선 제가 아가씨를 보살피게 해 주십시오."], ["I have a right hand yet at your service, to help you into the next field, whence we have but a very little walk to your father's house.”", "아직 성한 오른손이 있으니, 다음 들판까지는 아가씨를 도와 드릴 수 있습니다. 거기서 아버님 댁까지는 잠깐만 걸으면 되니까요.\""]], [["Sophia seeing his left arm dangling by his side, while he was using the other to lead her, no longer doubted of the truth.", "소피아는 그가 한쪽 손으로 자신을 이끄는 동안 다른 한 팔, 곧 왼팔이 옆구리에 축 늘어져 흔들리는 것을 보고는 더 이상 그 말을 의심하지 않았다. 그녀는 조금 전 자신을 향한 두려움으로 질렸던 것보다 훨씬 더 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["She now grew much paler than her fears for herself had made her before.", "온몸이 떨려 와, 존스가 그녀를 부축하기도 버거울 지경이었다."], ["All her limbs were seized with a trembling, insomuch that Jones could scarce support her; and as her thoughts were in no less agitation, she could not refrain from giving Jones a look so full of tenderness, that it almost argued a stronger sensation in her mind, than even gratitude and pity united can raise in the gentlest female bosom, without the assistance of a third more powerful passion.", "마음 또한 그에 못지않게 요동쳤던 터라, 그녀는 존스에게 너무도 다정함이 가득한 눈빛을 보내고야 말았다. 그 눈빛은, 가장 강력한 제3의 감정의 도움 없이는 감사와 연민이 한데 합쳐진다 해도 가장 부드러운 여인의 가슴속에 불러일으킬 수 있는 것보다 더 강한 무언가가 그녀의 마음속에 일고 있음을 거의 드러내고 있었다."]], [["Mr Western, who was advanced at some distance when this accident happened, was now returned, as were the rest of the horsemen.", "이 사고가 일어났을 때 얼마간 앞서 나가 있던 웨스턴 씨가 이제 돌아왔고, 다른 기수들도 마찬가지였다."], ["Sophia immediately acquainted them with what had befallen Jones, and begged them to take care of him.", "소피아는 곧바로 그들에게 존스에게 일어난 일을 알리고, 그를 잘 돌봐 달라고 청했다."], ["Upon which Western, who had been much alarmed by meeting his daughter's horse without its rider, and was now overjoyed to find her unhurt, cried out, “I am glad it is no worse.", "그러자 기수 없이 돌아온 딸의 말을 보고 몹시 놀랐다가, 이제 딸이 무사한 것을 알고 한껏 기뻐하던 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"이만하길 다행이로구나."], ["If Tom hath broken his arm, we will get a joiner to mend un again.”", "톰이 팔이 부러졌다면, 목수를 불러 다시 붙여 주면 될 일이지.\""]], [["The squire alighted from his horse, and proceeded to his house on foot, with his daughter and Jones.", "향사는 말에서 내려, 딸과 존스와 함께 걸어서 집으로 향했다."], ["An impartial spectator, who had met them on the way, would, on viewing their several countenances, have concluded Sophia alone to have been the object of compassion: for as to Jones, he exulted in having probably saved the life of the young lady, at the price only of a broken bone; and Mr Western, though he was not unconcerned at the accident which had befallen Jones, was, however, delighted in a much higher degree with the fortunate escape of his daughter.", "길에서 그들과 마주친 공정한 구경꾼이 있었다면, 그 세 사람의 표정을 살펴보고는 동정을 받아야 할 사람은 오직 소피아뿐이라고 결론지었을 것이다. 존스로 말하자면, 그는 겨우 뼈 하나가 부러진 대가로 어쩌면 이 젊은 아가씨의 목숨을 구했다는 사실에 의기양양했고, 웨스턴 씨는 존스에게 닥친 사고를 무심히 넘긴 것은 아니었으나, 딸이 운 좋게 화를 면한 것에 훨씬 더 높은 정도로 기뻐하고 있었으니 말이다."]], [["The generosity of Sophia's temper construed this behaviour of Jones into great bravery; and it made a deep impression on her heart: for certain it is, that there is no one quality which so generally recommends men to women as this; proceeding, if we believe the common opinion, from that natural timidity of the sex, which is, says Mr Osborne, “so great, that a woman is the most cowardly of all the creatures God ever made;”--a sentiment more remarkable for its bluntness than for its truth. Aristotle, in his Politics, doth them, I believe, more justice, when he says, “The modesty and fortitude of men differ from those virtues in women; for the fortitude which becomes a woman, would be cowardice in a man; and the modesty which becomes a man, would be pertness in a woman.” Nor is there, perhaps, more of truth in the opinion of those who derive the partiality which women are inclined to show to the brave, from this excess of their fear. Mr Bayle (I think, in his article of Helen) imputes this, and with greater probability, to their violent love of glory; for the truth of which, we have the authority of him who of all others saw farthest into human nature, and who introduces the heroine of his Odyssey, the great pattern of matrimonial love and constancy, assigning the glory of her husband as the only source of her affection towards him.[*]", "너그러운 소피아의 천성은 존스의 이 행동을 대단한 용기로 받아들였고, 그것은 그녀의 가슴에 깊은 자국을 남겼다. 이것만큼 남자를 여자에게 널리 추천해 주는 자질은 달리 없는 것이 분명하니 말이다. 흔한 의견을 믿자면, 이는 여성의 타고난 소심함에서 비롯하는 것인데, 오즈번 씨의 말로는 그 소심함이 \"어찌나 큰지, 여자는 하느님이 지으신 모든 피조물 가운데 가장 겁 많은 존재\"라는 것이다. 그러나 이는 진실보다는 그 무뚝뚝함으로 더 두드러지는 견해다. 아리스토텔레스는 그의 《정치학》에서, 내 생각으로는 여성에게 좀 더 공정하게 말하고 있다. \"남자의 정숙함과 굳셈은 여자에게서의 그 덕목들과 다르다. 여자에게 어울리는 굳셈은 남자에게서는 비겁함이 될 것이며, 남자에게 어울리는 정숙함은 여자에게서는 건방짐이 될 것이다.\" 또한 여자들이 용감한 자에게 보이는 편애를, 이 지나친 두려움에서 끌어내는 사람들의 견해에도 더 많은 진실이 담겨 있는 것 같지는 않다. 베일 씨는 (내 기억으로는 헬레네에 관한 항목에서) 이를, 한층 더 그럴듯하게, 여자들의 격렬한 영예욕에서 비롯한 것으로 본다. 그 진실성에 대해서는, 누구보다도 인간 본성을 가장 멀리까지 꿰뚫어 본 그 시인의 권위가 우리 곁에 있다. 그는 자신의 《오디세이아》의 여주인공, 부부간 사랑과 정절의 위대한 본보기로 하여금, 남편을 향한 자신의 애정의 유일한 원천으로 남편의 영예를 꼽게 하고 있으니 말이다.[*]"]], [["[*] The English reader will not find this in the poem; for the sentiment is entirely left out in the translation.", "[*] 영어권 독자는 이 대목을 그 시에서 찾지 못할 것이다. 이 정서는 번역본에서 통째로 빠져 있기 때문이다."]], [["However this be, certain it is that the accident operated very strongly on Sophia; and, indeed, after much enquiry into the matter, I am inclined to believe, that, at this very time, the charming Sophia made no less impression on the heart of Jones; to say truth, he had for some time become sensible of the irresistible power of her charms.", "어찌 되었든, 이 사고가 소피아에게 매우 강하게 작용한 것만은 분명하다. 그리고 사실, 이 문제를 한참 캐어 본 끝에 나는 이렇게 믿게 되었다. 바로 이때, 어여쁜 소피아 또한 존스의 마음에 그에 못지않은 자국을 남겼으리라고. 사실대로 말하자면, 그는 얼마 전부터 그녀의 매력이 지닌 거역할 수 없는 힘을 느끼고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter xiv.", "# Chapter xiv."]], [["The arrival of a surgeon.--His operations, and a long dialogue between Sophia and her maid.", "외과의가 도착하다. 그의 처치, 그리고 소피아와 하녀 사이의 긴 대화."]], [["When they arrived at Mr Western's hall, Sophia, who had tottered along with much difficulty, sunk down in her chair; but by the assistance of hartshorn and water, she was prevented from fainting away, and had pretty well recovered her spirits, when the surgeon who was sent for to Jones appeared.", "웨스턴 씨의 저택 현관에 다다랐을 때, 어렵사리 비틀거리며 걸어온 소피아는 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 그러나 사슴뿔 정기와 물의 도움으로 기절은 면했고, 존스에게 부르러 보냈던 외과의가 나타났을 무렵에는 꽤 정신을 차린 상태였다."], ["Mr Western, who imputed these symptoms in his daughter to her fall, advised her to be presently blooded by way of prevention.", "딸의 이런 증세를 낙마 탓으로 돌린 웨스턴 씨는, 예방 삼아 당장 피를 빼는 게 좋겠다고 권했다."], ["In this opinion he was seconded by the surgeon, who gave so many reasons for bleeding, and quoted so many cases where persons had miscarried for want of it, that the squire became very importunate, and indeed insisted peremptorily that his daughter should be blooded.", "이 의견에 외과의도 거들었는데, 그는 사혈을 해야 하는 이유를 어찌나 많이 늘어놓고, 사혈을 하지 않아 잘못된 사례들을 어찌나 많이 인용했던지, 향사는 몹시 끈질겨졌으며, 실로 딸이 반드시 피를 빼야 한다고 단호하게 고집했다."]], [["Sophia soon yielded to the commands of her father, though entirely contrary to her own inclinations, for she suspected, I believe, less danger from the fright, than either the squire or the surgeon.", "소피아는 자신의 뜻과는 전혀 어긋났지만 곧 아버지의 명령에 따랐다. 내 생각에 그녀는 이 충격에서 비롯될 위험을 향사나 외과의보다 더 작게 여겼던 것 같다."], ["She then stretched out her beautiful arm, and the operator began to prepare for his work.", "그녀는 이윽고 아름다운 팔을 내밀었고, 시술자는 작업 채비를 시작했다."]], [["While the servants were busied in providing materials, the surgeon, who imputed the backwardness which had appeared in Sophia to her fears, began to comfort her with assurances that there was not the least danger; for no accident, he said, could ever happen in bleeding, but from the monstrous ignorance of pretenders to surgery, which he pretty plainly insinuated was not at present to be apprehended.", "하인들이 분주히 도구를 마련하는 동안, 외과의는 소피아가 보인 머뭇거림을 두려움 탓으로 돌리고는, 조금도 위험할 일이 없다고 거듭 안심시키며 그녀를 달래기 시작했다. 그의 말로는, 사혈에서 사고가 생기는 일이란 외과술을 흉내 내는 자들의 끔찍한 무지에서나 비롯될 뿐인데, 지금 이 자리에서는 그런 걱정을 할 필요가 없다는 것을 그는 꽤 노골적으로 내비쳤다."], ["Sophia declared she was not under the least apprehension; adding, “If you open an artery, I promise you I'll forgive you.”", "소피아는 자신은 조금도 두렵지 않다고 밝히며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"혹 동맥을 잘못 여신다 해도, 약속하는데 용서해 드리겠어요.\""], ["“Will you?”", "\"용서한다고?\""], ["cries Western: “D--n me, if I will.", "웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"제기랄, 나는 안 그래."], ["If he does thee the least mischief, d--n me if I don't ha' the heart's blood o'un out.”", "이자가 너에게 조금이라도 해를 끼친다면, 빌어먹을, 이자의 심장 피를 다 뽑아내고 말겠다.\""], ["The surgeon assented to bleed her upon these conditions, and then proceeded to his operation, which he performed with as much dexterity as he had promised; and with as much quickness: for he took but little blood from her, saying, it was much safer to bleed again and again, than to take away too much at once.", "외과의는 이런 조건 아래 사혈하기로 동의하고는 시술에 들어갔는데, 약속한 대로 능숙하게, 또 그만큼 신속하게 해냈다. 그는 그녀에게서 피를 조금만 뽑으며, 한꺼번에 너무 많이 빼는 것보다 거듭거듭 빼는 편이 훨씬 안전하다고 말했다."]], [["Sophia, when her arm was bound up, retired: for she was not willing (nor was it, perhaps, strictly decent) to be present at the operation on Jones.", "팔을 동여매고 나자 소피아는 자리를 떴다. 존스에게 시술하는 자리에 함께 있고 싶지 않았기 때문이다(어쩌면 엄밀히 따져 그게 예법에 맞지도 않았을 것이다)."], ["Indeed, one objection which she had to bleeding (though she did not make it) was the delay which it would occasion to setting the broken bone.", "사실 그녀가 사혈을 꺼린 한 가지 이유는(입 밖에 내지는 않았지만) 그것이 부러진 뼈를 맞추는 일을 늦추리라는 점이었다."], ["For Western, when Sophia was concerned, had no consideration but for her; and as for Jones himself, he “sat like patience on a monument smiling at grief.”", "소피아와 관련된 일에서라면 웨스턴은 오직 딸 생각뿐이었고, 존스 본인으로 말하자면 그는 \"묘비 위에 앉은 인내처럼 슬픔을 향해 미소 짓고\" 있었다."], ["To say the truth, when he saw the blood springing from the lovely arm of Sophia, he scarce thought of what had happened to himself.", "사실대로 말하자면, 소피아의 사랑스러운 팔에서 피가 솟구치는 것을 보았을 때 그는 자기 몸에 닥친 일은 거의 떠올리지도 못했다."]], [["The surgeon now ordered his patient to be stript to his shirt, and then entirely baring the arm, he began to stretch and examine it, in such a manner that the tortures he put him to caused Jones to make several wry faces; which the surgeon observing, greatly wondered at, crying, “What is the matter, sir?", "이제 외과의는 환자에게 셔츠만 남기고 옷을 벗기라 명하고는, 팔을 완전히 드러낸 다음 그것을 잡아당기고 살피기 시작했는데, 그 통증이 어찌나 심했던지 존스는 몇 번이나 얼굴을 일그러뜨렸다. 이를 지켜보던 외과의는 몹시 의아해하며 외쳤다."], ["I am sure it is impossible I should hurt you.”", "\"왜 그러십니까, 선생? 제가 선생을 아프게 했을 리가 없는데요.\""], ["And then holding forth the broken arm, he began a long and very learned lecture of anatomy, in which simple and double fractures were most accurately considered; and the several ways in which Jones might have broken his arm were discussed, with proper annotations showing how many of these would have been better, and how many worse than the present case.", "그러고는 부러진 팔을 들어 올린 채, 길고도 무척이나 학식 있는 해부학 강의를 시작했다. 단순 골절과 복합 골절을 더없이 정밀하게 따지고, 존스가 팔을 부러뜨릴 수 있었던 여러 경우를 논하면서, 그 가운데 몇 가지는 지금보다 나았을 것이고 몇 가지는 더 나빴을 것이라는 적절한 주석까지 곁들였다."]], [["Having at length finished his laboured harangue, with which the audience, though it had greatly raised their attention and admiration, were not much edified, as they really understood not a single syllable of all he had said, he proceeded to business, which he was more expeditious in finishing, than he had been in beginning.", "마침내 그 공들인 일장연설을 끝냈을 때—듣는 이들의 주의와 감탄은 크게 끌어올렸으나, 그가 한 말이라곤 단 한 음절도 정작 알아듣지 못했으니 별로 깨우친 바는 없었다—그는 본업에 들어갔고, 시작할 때보다는 마무리하는 데 훨씬 더 빨랐다."]], [["Jones was then ordered into a bed, which Mr Western compelled him to accept at his own house, and sentence of water-gruel was passed upon him.", "이윽고 존스는 침대로 옮겨지라는 명을 받았는데, 웨스턴 씨가 자기 집에서 그것을 받아들이라고 강권한 것이었다. 그리고 그에게는 물죽이라는 처분이 내려졌다."]], [["Among the good company which had attended in the hall during the bone-setting, Mrs Honour was one; who being summoned to her mistress as soon as it was over, and asked by her how the young gentleman did, presently launched into extravagant praises on the magnanimity, as she called it, of his behaviour, which, she said, “was so charming in so pretty a creature.” She then burst forth into much warmer encomiums on the beauty of his person; enumerating many particulars, and ending with the whiteness of his skin.", "뼈를 맞추는 동안 현관에 모여 있던 점잖은 사람들 가운데 어너 부인도 있었다. 일이 끝나자마자 그녀는 안주인에게 불려 갔고, 안주인이 그 젊은 신사가 어떠냐고 묻자 곧장 그의 행동이 보인 의연함—그녀의 표현이었다—을 두고 과장된 찬사를 쏟아냈다. 그녀의 말로는 \"그토록 잘생긴 사람에게 그런 의연함이 어찌나 매력적인지\" 모른다는 것이었다. 그러더니 그의 외모가 지닌 아름다움에 대해 한층 더 뜨거운 칭송을 터뜨리며, 이런저런 특징을 일일이 열거하다가 끝내 그 살결의 흰빛으로 마무리했다."]], [["This discourse had an effect on Sophia's countenance, which would not perhaps have escaped the observance of the sagacious waiting-woman, had she once looked her mistress in the face, all the time she was speaking: but as a looking-glass, which was most commodiously placed opposite to her, gave her an opportunity of surveying those features, in which, of all others, she took most delight; so she had not once removed her eyes from that amiable object during her whole speech.", "이 이야기는 소피아의 낯빛에 어떤 변화를 일으켰는데, 만일 그 영민한 시중꾼이 말하는 내내 한 번이라도 안주인의 얼굴을 마주 보았더라면 그 변화를 놓치지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 맞은편 더없이 편리한 자리에 놓인 거울이, 그녀가 무엇보다 가장 흐뭇해하는 그 이목구비—다름 아닌 자기 자신의 얼굴—를 들여다볼 기회를 주었던 터라, 그녀는 말을 하는 동안 단 한 번도 그 사랑스러운 대상에서 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["Mrs Honour was so intirely wrapped up in the subject on which she exercised her tongue, and the object before her eyes, that she gave her mistress time to conquer her confusion; which having done, she smiled on her maid, and told her, “she was certainly in love with this young fellow.”--“I in love, madam!” answers she: “upon my word, ma'am, I assure you, ma'am, upon my soul, ma'am, I am not.”--“Why, if you was,” cries her mistress, “I see no reason that you should be ashamed of it; for he is certainly a pretty fellow.”--“Yes, ma'am,” answered the other, “that he is, the most handsomest man I ever saw in my life. Yes, to be sure, that he is, and, as your ladyship says, I don't know why I should be ashamed of loving him, though he is my betters. To be sure, gentlefolks are but flesh and blood no more than us servants. Besides, as for Mr Jones, thof Squire Allworthy hath made a gentleman of him, he was not so good as myself by birth: for thof I am a poor body, I am an honest person's child, and my father and mother were married, which is more than some people can say, as high as they hold their heads. Marry, come up! I assure you, my dirty cousin! thof his skin be so white, and to be sure it is the most whitest that ever was seen, I am a Christian as well as he, and nobody can say that I am base born: my grandfather was a clergyman,[*] and would have been very angry, I believe, to have thought any of his family should have taken up with Molly Seagrim's dirty leavings.”", "어너 부인은 자신의 혀를 놀리는 주제와 눈앞의 대상에 어찌나 완전히 빠져 있었던지, 안주인이 당혹감을 다스릴 시간을 주고 말았다. 마음을 추스른 소피아는 하녀를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다. \"너 분명 이 젊은이를 사랑하고 있구나.\" \"제가 사랑한다고요, 아씨!\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"맹세코, 아씨, 정말 아니에요, 아씨, 제 영혼을 걸고요, 아씨, 절대 아닙니다.\" \"왜, 설령 그렇다 해도,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"부끄러워할 이유는 없어 보이는데. 정말 잘생긴 청년이니까.\" \"맞아요, 아씨,\" 다른 쪽이 대답했다. \"정말 그래요, 제 평생 본 사람 중에 가장 잘생겼지요. 네, 정말 그렇고요, 아씨 말씀처럼 그를 사랑한다 해도 부끄러워할 까닭이 없지요, 비록 저보다 윗분이긴 해도요. 하긴, 양반네라고 해 봤자 우리 같은 하인들과 똑같이 피와 살로 된 사람일 뿐인걸요. 게다가 존스 씨로 말하면, 올워디 향사께서 그를 신사로 만들어 주셨다지만, 태생으로 따지면 저만도 못했어요. 저야 가난한 몸이지만 그래도 정직한 사람의 자식이고, 제 아버지와 어머니는 혼인한 사이였으니까요. 그건 아무리 콧대 높이 쳐든 사람이라도 다 할 수 있는 말은 아니잖아요. 흥, 어디 한번 잘난 척해 보시지! 정말이지, 이 더러운 친척아! 그 살결이 그토록 희다지만, 하긴 여태 본 중에 가장 흰 살결이긴 하지만, 저도 그 사람만큼이나 떳떳한 기독교인이고, 누구도 저를 천하게 태어났다고는 못 해요. 제 할아버지는 성직자셨고요, 당신네 집안 누군가가 몰리 시그림이 버린 더러운 찌꺼기를 주워 든다는 걸 아셨다면 무척이나 노여워하셨을 거예요.\""]], [["[*] This is the second person of low condition whom we have recorded in this history to have sprung from the clergy.", "[*] 이 이야기에서 우리가 성직자 출신으로 기록한 신분 낮은 인물로는 이번이 두 번째다."], ["It is to be hoped such instances will, in future ages, when some provision is made for the families of the inferior clergy, appear stranger than they can be thought at present.", "바라건대 훗날, 하급 성직자의 가족에게 어떤 생계 대책이 마련되는 시대가 오면, 이런 사례가 지금 여겨질 수 있는 것보다 한층 더 기이하게 보이기를 바란다."]], [["Perhaps Sophia might have suffered her maid to run on in this manner, from wanting sufficient spirits to stop her tongue, which the reader may probably conjecture was no very easy task; for certainly there were some passages in her speech which were far from being agreeable to the lady.", "어쩌면 소피아는 하녀의 혀를 멈출 만한 기력이 모자라 그녀가 이런 식으로 떠들게 내버려 두었는지도 모른다. 독자도 짐작하겠지만, 그 혀를 멈추는 일은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었으니까."], ["However, she now checked the torrent, as there seemed no end of its flowing.", "분명 그녀의 말 가운데에는 아씨에게 도무지 달갑지 않은 대목들이 있었다. 그러나 이제 소피아는 그 봇물을 막아섰다."], ["“I wonder,”", "흐르는 데 끝이 없어 보였기 때문이다. \"참 놀랍구나,\""], ["says she, “at your assurance in daring to talk thus of one of my father's friends.", "그녀가 말했다. \"어찌 그리 뻔뻔하게 우리 아버지의 친구 한 분을 두고 이렇게 함부로 떠들 수 있는지."], ["As to the wench, I order you never to mention her name to me.", "그리고 그 계집애로 말하자면, 다시는 내 앞에서 그 이름을 입에 올리지 말거라."], ["And with regard to the young gentleman's birth, those who can say nothing more to his disadvantage, may as well be silent on that head, as I desire you will be for the future.”", "또 그 젊은 신사의 출생에 관해서라면, 그 점 말고는 그를 깎아내릴 거리가 없는 자들은 그 일에 대해 차라리 입을 다무는 편이 낫지. 너도 앞으로는 그렇게 하기를 바란다.\""]], [["“I am sorry I have offended your ladyship,”", "\"아씨를 노엽게 해 드려 죄송합니다,\" 어너 부인이 대답했다."], ["answered Mrs Honour.", "\"저도 몰리 시그림이라면 아씨 못지않게 미워한다고요. 또 존스 도련님을 욕하다니요, 집안의 하인들을 다 증인으로 부를 수 있어요."], ["“I am sure I hate Molly Seagrim as much as your ladyship can; and as for abusing Squire Jones, I can call all the servants in the house to witness, that whenever any talk hath been about bastards, I have always taken his part; for which of you, says I to the footmen, would not be a bastard, if he could, to be made a gentleman of?", "사생아 이야기가 나올 때마다 저는 늘 그분 편을 들었거든요. 제가 하인들한테 그랬어요, 너희 중에 누가 할 수만 있다면 신사로 키워지려고 사생아가 되지 않겠느냐고요?"], ["And, says I, I am sure he is a very fine gentleman; and he hath one of the whitest hands in the world; for to be sure so he hath: and, says I, one of the sweetest temperedest, best naturedest men in the world he is; and, says I, all the servants and neighbours all round the country loves him.", "그리고 제가 그랬죠, 그분은 정말 훌륭한 신사고, 세상에서 가장 흰 손을 가진 분 중 하나라고요. 정말 그러니까요."], ["And, to be sure, I could tell your ladyship something, but that I am afraid it would offend you.”--“What could you tell me, Honour?”", "또 제가 그랬어요, 세상에서 가장 성품 곱고, 가장 마음씨 좋은 분 중 하나라고요. 또 제가 그랬죠, 온 동네 하인들과 이웃들이 모두 그분을 좋아한다고요."], ["says Sophia.", "그리고 정말이지, 아씨께 한 가지 말씀드릴 게 있긴 한데, 노여워하실까 봐 두렵네요.\" \"무슨 말을 할 수 있다는 거냐, 어너?\""], ["“Nay, ma'am, to be sure he meant nothing by it, therefore I would not have your ladyship be offended.”--“Prithee tell me,”", "소피아가 물었다. \"아니에요, 아씨, 정말이지 그분은 아무 뜻 없이 그러신 거예요, 그러니 아씨께서 노여워하지 않으셨으면 해서요.\""], ["says Sophia; “I will know it this instant.”--“Why, ma'am,”", "\"어서 말해 보거라,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["answered Mrs Honour, “he came into the room one day last week when I was at work, and there lay your ladyship's muff on a chair, and to be sure he put his hands into it; that very muff your ladyship gave me but yesterday.", "\"지금 당장 알아야겠다.\" \"그게요, 아씨,\""], ["La!", "어너 부인이 대답했다."], ["says I, Mr Jones, you will stretch my lady's muff, and spoil it: but he still kept his hands in it: and then he kissed it--to be sure I hardly ever saw such a kiss in my life as he gave it.”--“I suppose he did not know it was mine,”", "\"지난주 어느 날 제가 일을 하고 있는데 그분이 방에 들어오셨어요. 그때 의자 위에 아씨의 토시가 놓여 있었는데, 정말이지 그분이 거기에 두 손을 넣으셨지 뭐예요."], ["replied Sophia.", "바로 어제 아씨께서 제게 주신 그 토시 말이에요. 어머!"], ["“Your ladyship shall hear, ma'am.", "제가 그랬죠, 존스 씨, 우리 아씨 토시가 늘어나서 못 쓰게 돼요. 그런데도 그분은 손을 그 안에 그대로 두시더니, 거기에 입을 맞추셨어요."], ["He kissed it again and again, and said it was the prettiest muff in the world.", "정말이지 제 평생 그런 입맞춤은 거의 본 적이 없을 정도였어요.\" \"그게 내 것인 줄 몰랐겠지,\""], ["La!", "소피아가 받았다."], ["sir, says I, you have seen it a hundred times.", "\"아씨, 끝까지 들어 보세요. 그분은 그 토시에 몇 번이고 입을 맞추시며, 세상에서 가장 예쁜 토시라고 하셨어요."], ["Yes, Mrs Honour, cried he; but who can see anything beautiful in the presence of your lady but herself?--Nay, that's not all neither; but I hope your ladyship won't be offended, for to be sure he meant nothing.", "어머나! 제가 그랬죠, 그건 백 번도 더 보신 거잖아요."], ["One day, as your ladyship was playing on the harpsichord to my master, Mr Jones was sitting in the next room, and methought he looked melancholy.", "그래요, 어너 부인, 그분이 외치셨어요. 하지만 그대의 아씨가 계신 자리에서, 그분 자신 말고 누가 무슨 아름다운 것을 볼 수 있겠소?"], ["La!", "아니, 그게 다가 아니에요."], ["says I, Mr Jones, what's the matter?", "그래도 아씨께서 노여워하지 않으셨으면 해요, 정말 아무 뜻 없이 하신 말이니까요. 어느 날 아씨께서 주인어른께 하프시코드를 연주해 드리고 계실 때, 존스 씨는 옆방에 앉아 계셨는데, 제 눈엔 그분이 우울해 보였어요."], ["a penny for your thoughts, says I.", "어머! 제가 그랬죠, 존스 씨, 무슨 일이세요?"], ["Why, hussy, says he, starting up from a dream, what can I be thinking of, when that angel your mistress is playing?", "무슨 생각을 그리 하세요, 라고요. 아니, 이 아가씨야, 그분이 꿈에서 깨어나듯 벌떡 일어나며 말씀하셨어요, 저 천사 같은 그대의 아씨가 연주하고 계신데 내가 무슨 다른 생각을 할 수 있겠소?"], ["And then squeezing me by the hand, Oh!", "그러고는 제 손을 꼭 쥐며, 아! 어너 부인, 그분이 말씀하셨어요, 저 사람을 차지하는 남자는 얼마나 행복할까!"], ["Mrs Honour, says he, how happy will that man be!--and then he sighed.", "그러고는 한숨을 쉬셨어요. 정말이지 그분의 숨결은 꽃다발처럼 향긋했답니다."], ["Upon my troth, his breath is as sweet as a nosegay.--But to be sure he meant no harm by it.", "하지만 정말 아무 나쁜 뜻은 없으셨어요. 그러니 아씨께서 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않으셨으면 해요."], ["So I hope your ladyship will not mention a word; for he gave me a crown never to mention it, and made me swear upon a book, but I believe, indeed, it was not the Bible.”", "그분이 이 일을 절대 말하지 말라고 제게 한 닢을 주시고, 책에 대고 맹세까지 시키셨거든요. 하지만 사실 그건 성경은 아니었던 것 같아요.\""]], [["Till something of a more beautiful red than vermilion be found out, I shall say nothing of Sophia's colour on this occasion. “Ho--nour,” says she, “I--if you will not mention this any more to me--nor to anybody else, I will not betray you--I mean, I will not be angry; but I am afraid of your tongue. Why, my girl, will you give it such liberties?”--“Nay, ma'am,” answered she, “to be sure, I would sooner cut out my tongue than offend your ladyship. To be sure I shall never mention a word that your ladyship would not have me.”--“Why, I would not have you mention this any more,” said Sophia, “for it may come to my father's ears, and he would be angry with Mr Jones; though I really believe, as you say, he meant nothing. I should be very angry myself, if I imagined--“--“Nay, ma'am,” says Honour, “I protest I believe he meant nothing. I thought he talked as if he was out of his senses; nay, he said he believed he was beside himself when he had spoken the words. Ay, sir, says I, I believe so too. Yes, says he, Honour.--But I ask your ladyship's pardon; I could tear my tongue out for offending you.” “Go on,” says Sophia; “you may mention anything you have not told me before.”--“Yes, Honour, says he (this was some time afterwards, when he gave me the crown), I am neither such a coxcomb, or such a villain, as to think of her in any other delight but as my goddess; as such I will always worship and adore her while I have breath.--This was all, ma'am, I will be sworn, to the best of my remembrance. I was in a passion with him myself, till I found he meant no harm.”--“Indeed, Honour,” says Sophia, “I believe you have a real affection for me. I was provoked the other day when I gave you warning; but if you have a desire to stay with me, you shall.”--“To be sure, ma'am,” answered Mrs Honour, “I shall never desire to part with your ladyship. To be sure, I almost cried my eyes out when you gave me warning. It would be very ungrateful in me to desire to leave your ladyship; because as why, I should never get so good a place again. I am sure I would live and die with your ladyship; for, as poor Mr Jones said, happy is the man----”", "주홍보다 더 고운 붉은빛이 발견되기 전까지는, 이 순간 소피아의 낯빛에 대해 나는 아무 말도 하지 않으련다. \"어—너,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나, 만일 네가 이 일을 다시는 내게도, 다른 누구에게도 입에 올리지 않는다면, 나도 너를 일러바치지 않으마. 그러니까, 화내지 않겠다는 말이다. 다만 네 혀가 두렵구나. 얘야, 어찌 혀에 그런 자유를 주려 하느냐?\" \"아니에요, 아씨,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"정말이지, 아씨를 노엽게 하느니 차라리 제 혀를 잘라 버리겠어요. 정말로 아씨께서 원치 않으실 말은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않겠습니다.\" \"그래, 나는 네가 이 일을 더는 입에 올리지 않았으면 한다,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그게 아버지 귀에 들어가면 존스 씨에게 노여워하실 테니까. 비록 네 말처럼 그분이 아무 뜻 없이 그러셨다고 정말 믿기는 하지만 말이다. 만일 내가 다른 뜻이 있었다고 여겼다면, 나 자신이 무척 화가 났을 거다—\" \"아니에요, 아씨,\" 어너가 말했다. \"맹세코 저는 그분이 아무 뜻 없으셨다고 믿어요. 그분은 마치 제정신이 아닌 사람처럼 말씀하셨거든요. 아니, 그분 스스로도 그 말을 하고 나서 자기가 정신이 나갔던 것 같다고 하셨어요. 네, 도련님, 제가 그랬죠, 저도 그런 것 같다고요. 그래, 어너, 그분이 말씀하셨어요. 아, 아씨, 용서하세요. 아씨를 노엽게 했다니 제 혀를 뽑아내고 싶을 지경이에요.\" \"계속해 보거라,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"전에 내게 하지 않은 이야기라면 무엇이든 해도 좋다.\" \"그래, 어너, 그분이 말씀하셨어요(이건 얼마 뒤, 그분이 제게 한 닢을 주실 때 일이에요). 나는 그분을 나의 여신으로 여기는 것 말고 다른 어떤 기쁨으로도 떠올릴 만큼 그런 멋쟁이도, 그런 악당도 아니오. 여신으로서 나는 숨이 붙어 있는 한 언제나 그분을 경배하고 흠모할 것이오. 이게 다였어요, 아씨, 제 기억이 닿는 한 맹세할 수 있어요. 저도 처음엔 그분에게 화가 났다가, 아무 나쁜 뜻이 없다는 걸 알고 나서야 풀렸답니다.\" \"정말이지, 어너,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"너는 나를 진심으로 아끼는 것 같구나. 며칠 전 네게 해고를 통고했을 때는 내가 화가 나 있었지. 하지만 네가 내 곁에 남고 싶다면, 그리하려무나.\" \"물론이죠, 아씨,\" 어너 부인이 대답했다. \"저는 결코 아씨 곁을 떠나고 싶지 않아요. 정말이지 아씨께서 해고를 말씀하셨을 때 저는 눈이 빠지도록 울었답니다. 아씨 곁을 떠나고 싶어 한다면 그건 정말 배은망덕한 일이지요. 왜냐하면요, 다시는 이렇게 좋은 자리를 얻지 못할 테니까요. 정말이지 저는 아씨와 함께 살다 함께 죽겠어요. 가엾은 존스 씨가 말씀하셨듯이, 행복한 자는—\""]], [["Here the dinner bell interrupted a conversation which had wrought such an effect on Sophia, that she was, perhaps, more obliged to her bleeding in the morning, than she, at the time, had apprehended she should be.", "여기서 저녁 식사를 알리는 종이 울리며 대화가 끊겼다. 그 대화는 소피아에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤던지, 어쩌면 그녀는 그 순간 자신이 짐작했던 것보다 아침의 사혈에 더 큰 신세를 진 셈이었다."], ["As to the present situation of her mind, I shall adhere to a rule of Horace, by not attempting to describe it, from despair of success.", "지금 그녀의 마음 상태로 말하자면, 나는 호라티우스의 한 규칙을 좇아, 성공할 가망이 없으니 그것을 묘사하려는 시도조차 하지 않으련다."], ["Most of my readers will suggest it easily to themselves; and the few who cannot, would not understand the picture, or at least would deny it to be natural, if ever so well drawn.", "내 독자 대부분은 손쉽게 스스로 그것을 떠올릴 것이다. 그러지 못하는 몇몇은, 아무리 잘 그려 낸다 한들 그 그림을 이해하지 못하거나, 적어도 그것이 자연스럽다는 것을 인정하려 들지 않을 테니까."]], [["# BOOK V.", "# BOOK V."]], [["CONTAINING A PORTION OF TIME SOMEWHAT LONGER THAN HALF A YEAR. Chapter i.", "혹여 이 방대한 작품 가운데, 읽는 독자에게 가장 적은 즐거움을 주는 부분이, 정작 글쓴이가 짓느라 가장 큰 수고를 들인 부분일지도 모르겠다."], ["Of the SERIOUS in writing, and for what purpose it is introduced.", "그 가운데 아마도, 우리가 각 권에 담긴 이야기 앞머리에 붙여 둔 저 서두의 논설들이 꼽힐 것이다. 우리 스스로 그 선두에 서기로 한 이런 종류의 글쓰기에, 이 논설들이 본질적으로 꼭 필요하다고 우리는 단정해 두었다."]], [["Peradventure there may be no parts in this prodigious work which will give the reader less pleasure in the perusing, than those which have given the author the greatest pains in composing.", "이런 단정에 대해, 우리는 굳이 그 이유를 대야 한다고 생각하지 않는다."], ["Among these probably may be reckoned those initial essays which we have prefixed to the historical matter contained in every book; and which we have determined to be essentially necessary to this kind of writing, of which we have set ourselves at the head.", "산문이자 희극이자 서사시인 이 글쓰기 전반에서 반드시 지켜야 할 규칙으로 우리가 그것을 못 박아 두었다는 것만으로도 차고 넘치게 충분하기 때문이다."], ["For this our determination we do not hold ourselves strictly bound to assign any reason; it being abundantly sufficient that we have laid it down as a rule necessary to be observed in all prosai-comi-epic writing.", "시간과 장소의 그 엄정한 일치—오늘날 극시에 그토록 본질적이라고 자리 잡은 그 규칙—의 근거를 누가 따져 물은 적이 있던가? 어느 비평가가, 어째서 한 편의 극이 하루뿐 아니라 이틀을 담아서는 안 되느냐는 질문을 받아 본 적이 있던가?"], ["Who ever demanded the reasons of that nice unity of time or place which is now established to be so essential to dramatic poetry?", "또는 어째서 관객이 (마치 유권자들처럼 아무 비용 없이 이동만 한다면) 오 마일이 아니라 오십 마일을 실려 가서는 안 되느냐고?"], ["What critic hath been ever asked, why a play may not contain two days as well as one? Or why the audience (provided they travel, like electors, without any expense) may not be wafted fifty miles as well as five?", "어느 주석가가, 어떤 옛 비평가가 극에 그어 놓은 한계—그가 다섯 막을 더도 덜도 아니게 담아야 한다고 우긴 그 한계—를 제대로 설명해 낸 적이 있던가?"], ["Hath any commentator well accounted for the limitation which an antient critic hath set to the drama, which he will have contain neither more nor less than five acts?", "또는 살아 있는 누군가가, 우리 극장의 현대 심판관들이 저 \"저급하다\"는 말로 무엇을 뜻하는지 풀어 보려 한 적이 있던가?"], ["Or hath any one living attempted to explain what the modern judges of our theatres mean by that word low; by which they have happily succeeded in banishing all humour from the stage, and have made the theatre as dull as a drawing-room!", "그들은 그 말 덕분에 무대에서 모든 해학을 몰아내는 데 멋지게 성공했고, 극장을 응접실만큼이나 따분하게 만들어 버렸다! 이 모든 경우에 세상은 우리 법조계의 한 격언을 받아들인 듯하다."], ["Upon all these occasions the world seems to have embraced a maxim of our law, viz., cuicunque in arte sua perito credendum est: for it seems perhaps difficult to conceive that any one should have had enough of impudence to lay down dogmatical rules in any art or science without the least foundation.", "곧 \"각자 제 기술에 정통한 이의 말은 믿어야 한다\"는 것이다. 어떤 사람이 아무런 근거도 없이 어느 기술이나 학문에 독단적인 규칙을 세울 만큼 뻔뻔할 수 있으리라고는, 어쩌면 상상하기 어렵기 때문이리라."], ["In such cases, therefore, we are apt to conclude there are sound and good reasons at the bottom, though we are unfortunately not able to see so far.", "그러니 그런 경우에 우리는, 비록 안타깝게도 우리가 거기까지 내다보지는 못할지언정, 그 밑바닥에는 건전하고 훌륭한 이유가 깔려 있으리라고 결론짓기 십상이다."]], [["Now, in reality, the world have paid too great a compliment to critics, and have imagined them men of much greater profundity than they really are.", "그러나 실은, 세상은 비평가들에게 지나친 찬사를 바쳐 왔으며, 그들을 실제보다 훨씬 더 깊이 있는 사람들로 여겨 왔다."], ["From this complacence, the critics have been emboldened to assume a dictatorial power, and have so far succeeded, that they are now become the masters, and have the assurance to give laws to those authors from whose predecessors they originally received them.", "이런 너그러운 호의에 힘입어 비평가들은 대담하게도 군림하는 권력을 떠맡았고, 끝내는 주인이 되기에 이르렀다. 그래서 이제 그들은, 본래 자신들의 선배들로부터 법을 받았던 바로 그 작가들에게 도리어 법을 내릴 만큼 뻔뻔해졌다."]], [["The critic, rightly considered, is no more than the clerk, whose office it is to transcribe the rules and laws laid down by those great judges whose vast strength of genius hath placed them in the light of legislators, in the several sciences over which they presided.", "비평가란, 제대로 따져 보면 한낱 서기에 지나지 않는다. 그의 직분이란, 천재의 막대한 힘 덕분에 자신이 다스리는 여러 학문에서 입법자의 자리에 올라선 저 위대한 심판관들이 세운 규칙과 법을 베껴 적는 일이다."], ["This office was all which the critics of old aspired to; nor did they ever dare to advance a sentence, without supporting it by the authority of the judge from whence it was borrowed.", "옛 비평가들이 바라던 것은 오로지 이 직분뿐이었다. 그들은 어떤 판단을 내릴 때마다, 그것을 빌려 온 심판관의 권위로 떠받치지 않고서는 감히 한 마디도 앞세우지 못했다."]], [["But in process of time, and in ages of ignorance, the clerk began to invade the power and assume the dignity of his master.", "그러나 세월이 흐르고 무지의 시대가 닥치자, 서기는 제 주인의 권력을 침범하고 그 위엄을 떠맡기 시작했다."], ["The laws of writing were no longer founded on the practice of the author, but on the dictates of the critic.", "글쓰기의 법칙은 더 이상 작가의 실제 작업이 아니라 비평가의 명령 위에 세워졌다."], ["The clerk became the legislator, and those very peremptorily gave laws whose business it was, at first, only to transcribe them.", "서기가 입법자가 되었고, 처음에는 그저 그것을 베껴 적는 것이 일이었던 자들이 이제는 더없이 단호하게 법을 내렸다."]], [["Hence arose an obvious, and perhaps an unavoidable error; for these critics being men of shallow capacities, very easily mistook mere form for substance.", "여기서 명백한, 어쩌면 피할 수 없는 오류가 생겨났다. 이 비평가들이 얕은 그릇의 사람들이었던 탓에, 그들은 한낱 형식을 실체로 너무도 쉽게 착각해 버린 것이다."], ["They acted as a judge would, who should adhere to the lifeless letter of law, and reject the spirit.", "그들은 법조문의 생명 없는 글자에만 매달리고 그 정신을 내치는 심판관처럼 굴었다."], ["Little circumstances, which were perhaps accidental in a great author, were by these critics considered to constitute his chief merit, and transmitted as essentials to be observed by all his successors.", "위대한 작가에게서는 어쩌면 우연이었을 사소한 정황들을, 이 비평가들은 그의 으뜸가는 장점이라 여겼고, 그의 모든 후예가 지켜야 할 본질로 물려주었다. 이러한 월권에 대해, 사기를 떠받치는 두 기둥인 세월과 무지가 권위를 실어 주었다."], ["To these encroachments, time and ignorance, the two great supporters of imposture, gave authority; and thus many rules for good writing have been established, which have not the least foundation in truth or nature; and which commonly serve for no other purpose than to curb and restrain genius, in the same manner as it would have restrained the dancing-master, had the many excellent treatises on that art laid it down as an essential rule that every man must dance in chains.", "그리하여 진실에도 자연에도 손톱만큼의 근거가 없는, 좋은 글쓰기를 위한 수많은 규칙이 자리 잡았다. 그것들은 흔히 천재를 옭아매고 억누르는 것 말고는 아무 쓸모가 없으니, 마치 춤에 관한 그 숱한 훌륭한 논문들이, 사람은 누구나 쇠사슬을 차고 춤춰야 한다는 것을 본질적인 규칙으로 못 박아 두어 무용 선생을 옭아맸을 법한 것과 똑같다."]], [["To avoid, therefore, all imputation of laying down a rule for posterity, founded only on the authority of ipse dixit--for which, to say the truth, we have not the profoundest veneration--we shall here waive the privilege above contended for, and proceed to lay before the reader the reasons which have induced us to intersperse these several digressive essays in the course of this work.", "그러니 오로지 \"그분이 그리 말씀하셨다\"는 권위에만 기대어 후대를 위한 규칙을 세웠다는 비난—사실대로 말하자면 우리는 그런 권위에 깊은 경의를 품고 있지 않다—을 모조리 피하기 위해, 우리는 앞서 다투어 주장한 그 특권을 여기서 내려놓겠다. 그리고 이 작품의 흐름 곳곳에 이 여러 군데 곁가지 논설을 끼워 넣게 된 까닭을 독자 앞에 펼쳐 보이겠다."]], [["And here we shall of necessity be led to open a new vein of knowledge, which if it hath been discovered, hath not, to our remembrance, been wrought on by any antient or modern writer.", "여기서 우리는 어쩔 수 없이 새로운 지식의 광맥을 열어야 할 처지에 놓인다. 설령 누군가 그것을 발견했더라도, 우리 기억으로는 옛 작가든 오늘날의 작가든 그것을 캐낸 이는 없었다."], ["This vein is no other than that of contrast, which runs through all the works of the creation, and may probably have a large share in constituting in us the idea of all beauty, as well natural as artificial: for what demonstrates the beauty and excellence of anything but its reverse?", "이 광맥이란 다름 아닌 대조의 광맥이다. 그것은 창조된 만물을 두루 꿰뚫고 흐르며, 자연의 것이든 인공의 것이든 우리 안에 온갖 아름다움의 관념을 빚어내는 데 큰 몫을 하리라."], ["Thus the beauty of day, and that of summer, is set off by the horrors of night and winter.", "무엇이든 그 아름다움과 빼어남을 드러내 주는 것은 바로 그 반대편 아니던가?"], ["And, I believe, if it was possible for a man to have seen only the two former, he would have a very imperfect idea of their beauty.", "그리하여 낮의 아름다움과 여름의 아름다움은 밤과 겨울의 끔찍함에 의해 돋보인다. 또 내 믿기로는, 만일 사람이 앞의 두 가지만 본다는 것이 가능했다면, 그는 그 아름다움에 대해 아주 불완전한 관념밖에 갖지 못했을 것이다."]], [["But to avoid too serious an air; can it be doubted, but that the finest woman in the world would lose all benefit of her charms in the eye of a man who had never seen one of another cast? The ladies themselves seem so sensible of this, that they are all industrious to procure foils: nay, they will become foils to themselves; for I have observed (at Bath particularly) that they endeavour to appear as ugly as possible in the morning, in order to set off that beauty which they intend to show you in the evening.", "그러나 지나치게 진지한 태도는 피하기로 하자. 세상에서 가장 아름다운 여인이라도, 다른 부류의 여인을 한 번도 본 적 없는 남자의 눈에는 그 매력의 덕을 모조리 잃고 마리라는 것을, 누가 의심할 수 있겠는가? 여인들 자신도 이를 잘 아는 듯하다. 그래서 그들은 하나같이 자신을 돋보이게 할 들러리를 마련하느라 분주하다. 아니, 그들은 스스로 제 자신의 들러리가 되기도 한다. 나는 (특히 배스에서) 그들이 아침에는 될 수 있는 한 못나 보이려고 애쓰는 것을 보았는데, 이는 저녁에 보여 줄 작정인 그 아름다움을 한층 돋보이게 하기 위함이었다."]], [["Most artists have this secret in practice, though some, perhaps, have not much studied the theory.", "대다수 예술가는 이 비결을 실천하고 있다. 다만 그 이론까지 깊이 연구한 이는 어쩌면 많지 않을 것이다."], ["The jeweller knows that the finest brilliant requires a foil; and the painter, by the contrast of his figures, often acquires great applause.", "보석 세공인은 가장 빼어난 광채의 보석에 받침이 필요함을 안다. 화가는 인물들을 대조시킴으로써 흔히 큰 갈채를 얻는다."]], [["A great genius among us will illustrate this matter fully.", "우리 가운데 한 위대한 천재가 이 문제를 남김없이 밝혀 줄 것이다."], ["I cannot, indeed, range him under any general head of common artists, as he hath a title to be placed among those", "사실 나는 그를 흔한 예술가의 어떤 일반 항목 아래 묶을 수가 없다. 그는 다음과 같은 이들 사이에 놓일 자격이 있는 까닭이다."]], [["Inventas qui vitam excoluere per artes.", "발명한 기술로써 삶을 가꾸어 낸 이들."], ["Who by invented arts have life improved.", "* 라틴어 원문: \"Inventas qui vitam excoluere per artes.\""]], [["I mean here the inventor of that most exquisite entertainment, called the English Pantomime.", "내가 여기서 말하는 이는, 영국 팬터마임이라 불리는 그 더없이 절묘한 오락을 발명한 사람이다."]], [["This entertainment consisted of two parts, which the inventor distinguished by the names of the serious and the comic.", "이 오락은 두 부분으로 이루어졌는데, 발명자는 그것들을 진지극과 희극이라는 이름으로 구분했다."], ["The serious exhibited a certain number of heathen gods and heroes, who were certainly the worst and dullest company into which an audience was ever introduced; and (which was a secret known to few) were actually intended so to be, in order to contrast the comic part of the entertainment, and to display the tricks of harlequin to the better advantage.", "진지극은 이교의 신들과 영웅들을 얼마간 등장시켰으니, 그들은 관객이 여태껏 마주한 가운데 가장 고약하고 가장 따분한 패거리였음이 분명하다. 그리고 (이는 아는 이가 거의 없던 비밀인데) 사실 일부러 그렇게 만든 것이었으니, 오락의 희극 부분을 대조하고 어릿광대의 익살을 한층 돋보이게 드러내기 위함이었다."]], [["This was, perhaps, no very civil use of such personages: but the contrivance was, nevertheless, ingenious enough, and had its effect. And this will now plainly appear, if, instead of serious and comic, we supply the words duller and dullest; for the comic was certainly duller than anything before shown on the stage, and could be set off only by that superlative degree of dulness which composed the serious. So intolerably serious, indeed, were these gods and heroes, that harlequin (though the English gentleman of that name is not at all related to the French family, for he is of a much more serious disposition) was always welcome on the stage, as he relieved the audience from worse company.", "이는 어쩌면 그런 인물들을 그리 정중하게 다룬 처사는 아니었다. 그러나 그 궁리는 그럼에도 제법 기발했고, 효과도 있었다. 진지극과 희극이라는 말 대신에 따분한 쪽과 가장 따분한 쪽이라는 말을 넣어 보면 이 점이 이제 분명히 드러난다. 희극은 분명 여태껏 무대에 오른 그 무엇보다 따분했고, 진지극을 이루는 저 최상급의 따분함으로만 겨우 돋보일 수 있었기 때문이다. 실로 이 신들과 영웅들이 어찌나 견디기 힘들 만큼 진지했던지, 어릿광대는 (이 이름을 가진 영국 신사는 프랑스 가문과는 전혀 친척이 아니다. 그는 훨씬 더 진지한 기질의 소유자이니까) 무대에 나올 때마다 늘 환영을 받았다. 그가 관객을 더 고약한 패거리로부터 구해 주었기 때문이다."]], [["Judicious writers have always practised this art of contrast with great success.", "분별 있는 작가들은 늘 이 대조의 기술을 큰 성공과 함께 실천해 왔다."], ["I have been surprized that Horace should cavil at this art in Homer; but indeed he contradicts himself in the very next line:", "나는 호라티우스가 호메로스에게서 이 기술을 트집 잡았다는 것에 놀랐다. 그러나 실은 그가 바로 다음 줄에서 스스로 모순에 빠진다."]], [["Indignor quandoque bonus dormitat Homerus; Verum opere in longo fas est obrepere somnum.", "\"Indignor quandoque bonus dormitat Homerus; Verum opere in longo fas est obrepere somnum.\""]], [["I grieve if e'er great Homer chance to sleep, Yet slumbers on long works have right to creep.", "위대한 호메로스가 어쩌다 잠드는 일이 있으면 나는 슬프다. 허나 긴 작품에서는 졸음이 슬며시 기어드는 것도 마땅하다."]], [["For we are not here to understand, as perhaps some have, that an author actually falls asleep while he is writing.", "여기서 우리는, 어쩌면 더러 그리 여겼을 법하지만, 작가가 글을 쓰는 동안 정말로 잠들어 버린다는 뜻으로 이해해서는 안 된다."], ["It is true, that readers are too apt to be so overtaken; but if the work was as long as any of Oldmixon, the author himself is too well entertained to be subject to the least drowsiness.", "독자가 그렇게 졸음에 사로잡히기 쉬운 것은 사실이다. 그러나 작품이 올드믹슨의 어느 것만큼이나 길다 한들, 작가 자신은 너무도 흥겹게 빠져 있어 조금도 졸음에 빠질 까닭이 없다."], ["He is, as Mr Pope observes,", "그는, 포프 씨가 일렀듯이,"]], [["Sleepless himself to give his readers sleep.", "스스로는 잠 못 이루며 제 독자에게 잠을 안긴다."]], [["To say the truth, these soporific parts are so many scenes of serious artfully interwoven, in order to contrast and set off the rest; and this is the true meaning of a late facetious writer, who told the public that whenever he was dull they might be assured there was a design in it.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이 졸음을 부르는 부분들이란, 나머지를 대조하고 돋보이게 하려고 솜씨껏 짜 넣은 진지함의 장면들에 다름 아니다. 그리고 이것이야말로, 자신이 따분할 때면 거기에 반드시 의도가 있는 줄 알아도 좋다고 대중에게 일러둔 어느 근래의 익살스러운 작가가 남긴 말의 참뜻이다."]], [["In this light, then, or rather in this darkness, I would have the reader to consider these initial essays.", "그러니 이 빛 아래에서, 아니 차라리 이 어둠 속에서, 독자가 이 서두의 논설들을 헤아려 주기를 나는 바란다."], ["And after this warning, if he shall be of opinion that he can find enough of serious in other parts of this history, he may pass over these, in which we profess to be laboriously dull, and begin the following books at the second chapter.", "그리고 이렇게 미리 일러두었으니, 만일 그가 이 이야기의 다른 부분에서 진지함을 넉넉히 찾을 수 있겠다고 여긴다면, 우리가 애써 따분하기를 자처하는 이 부분은 건너뛰고 다음 권들을 둘째 장부터 시작해도 좋다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["In which Mr Jones receives many friendly visits during his confinement; with some fine touches of the passion of love, scarce visible to the naked eye.", "존스 씨가 요양하는 동안 여러 다정한 문병을 받게 되는 이야기. 더불어 맨눈으로는 좀처럼 보이지 않는 사랑이라는 감정의 미묘한 결들도 함께 그려진다."]], [["Tom Jones had many visitors during his confinement, though some, perhaps, were not very agreeable to him.", "톰 존스는 요양하는 동안 많은 문병객을 맞았으나, 그중 더러는 그에게 그리 달갑지 않은 손님이었을 것이다. 올워디 씨는 거의 매일 그를 보러 왔다."], ["Mr Allworthy saw him almost every day; but though he pitied Tom's sufferings, and greatly approved the gallant behaviour which had occasioned them; yet he thought this was a favourable opportunity to bring him to a sober sense of his indiscreet conduct; and that wholesome advice for that purpose could never be applied at a more proper season than at the present, when the mind was softened by pain and sickness, and alarmed by danger; and when its attention was unembarrassed with those turbulent passions which engage us in the pursuit of pleasure.", "다만 그는 톰의 고통을 가엾이 여기고, 그 고통을 불러온 용감한 행동을 크게 칭찬하면서도, 지금이야말로 톰을 자신의 경솔했던 처신에 대해 차분히 돌아보게 만들 좋은 기회라고 여겼다. 그리고 그런 목적에 쓰일 유익한 충고는, 마음이 아픔과 병으로 누그러지고 위험으로 놀라 있으며, 우리를 쾌락의 추구로 끌어들이는 그 사나운 열정들로부터 그 주의가 흐트러지지 않은 지금보다 더 알맞은 때에 베풀어질 수 없으리라 생각했다."]], [["At all seasons, therefore, when the good man was alone with the youth, especially when the latter was totally at ease, he took occasion to remind him of his former miscarriages, but in the mildest and tenderest manner, and only in order to introduce the caution which he prescribed for his future behaviour; “on which alone,”", "그리하여 이 선량한 사람은 그 청년과 단둘이 있을 때마다, 특히 청년이 더없이 편안해 보일 때를 골라, 지난날의 그릇된 행실을 일깨워 주었다. 그러나 그것은 더없이 부드럽고 다정한 방식이었으며, 오직 앞으로의 처신을 위해 그가 일러 주는 경계의 말을 꺼내기 위한 것일 뿐이었다."], ["he assured him, “would depend his own felicity, and the kindness which he might yet promise himself to receive at the hands of his father by adoption, unless he should hereafter forfeit his good opinion: for as to what had past,”", "그는 톰에게 이렇게 다짐했다. \"오직 그 처신에 너 자신의 행복이 달려 있고, 앞으로 네가 나의 양아버지로서 베풀 호의를 받을지 여부도 거기에 달려 있다."], ["he said, “it should be all forgiven and forgotten.", "다만 네가 이후에 나의 좋은 평가를 스스로 잃어버리지만 않는다면 말이다. 지나간 일에 대해서라면,\""], ["He therefore advised him to make a good use of this accident, that so in the end it might prove a visitation for his own good.”", "그는 말했다, \"모두 용서하고 잊어 줄 것이다.\" 그러면서 그는 톰에게, 이 사고를 잘 활용하여 결국에는 그것이 너 자신을 위한 시련이었음이 드러나도록 하라고 권했다."]], [["Thwackum was likewise pretty assiduous in his visits; and he too considered a sick-bed to be a convenient scene for lectures.", "스왁검도 못지않게 부지런히 문병을 왔는데, 그 역시 병상이야말로 설교를 늘어놓기에 안성맞춤인 무대라고 여겼다. 다만 그의 어조는 올워디 씨보다 훨씬 가혹했다."], ["His stile, however, was more severe than Mr Allworthy's: he told his pupil, “That he ought to look on his broken limb as a judgment from heaven on his sins.", "그는 자기 제자에게 이렇게 말했다. \"너는 부러진 그 팔다리를 네 죄에 대한 하늘의 심판으로 여겨야 마땅하다."], ["That it would become him to be daily on his knees, pouring forth thanksgivings that he had broken his arm only, and not his neck; which latter,”", "매일 무릎 꿇고, 목이 아니라 겨우 팔만 부러진 것에 감사를 쏟아 올려야 옳다. 그 목이라는 것은,\""], ["he said, “was very probably reserved for some future occasion, and that, perhaps, not very remote.", "그는 말했다, \"필시 훗날의 어떤 기회를 위해 남겨져 있을 터이며, 어쩌면 그리 먼 날의 일도 아닐 것이다. 나로서는,\""], ["For his part,”", "그가 말했다, \"진작에 어떤 심판이 너를 덮치지 않은 것이 늘 의아했다."], ["he said, “he had often wondered some judgment had not overtaken him before; but it might be perceived by this, that Divine punishments, though slow, are always sure.”", "그러나 이로써 알 수 있는 것이, 신의 벌이란 더디기는 해도 언제나 어김없다는 사실이다.\" 그리하여 그는 또한 이렇게 충고했다."], ["Hence likewise he advised him, “to foresee, with equal certainty, the greater evils which were yet behind, and which were as sure as this of overtaking him in his state of reprobacy.", "\"아직 남아 있는 더 큰 재앙들도 그만큼 확실하게 내다보아라. 그것들은 이번 일과 마찬가지로, 네가 버림받은 죄인의 처지에 있는 한 어김없이 너를 덮칠 것이다."], ["These are,”", "그것들은,\""], ["said he, “to be averted only by such a thorough and sincere repentance as is not to be expected or hoped for from one so abandoned in his youth, and whose mind, I am afraid, is totally corrupted.", "그가 말했다, \"오직 철저하고 진실한 회개로만 피할 수 있는데, 그런 회개란 어릴 때부터 그토록 타락한 자, 게다가 그 마음이 송두리째 썩어 버렸을까 두려운 자에게서는 기대하거나 바랄 수 없는 것이다. 그러나 너에게 이 회개를 권하는 것이 내 의무다."], ["It is my duty, however, to exhort you to this repentance, though I too well know all exhortations will be vain and fruitless.", "그 모든 권면이 헛되고 보람 없으리라는 것을 너무도 잘 알고는 있지만 말이다. 그래도 나는 내 영혼만큼은 자유롭게 했다(liberavi animam meam)."], ["But liberavi animam meam.", "내 양심에는 어떤 태만의 죄도 물을 수 없다."], ["I can accuse my own conscience of no neglect; though it is at the same time with the utmost concern I see you travelling on to certain misery in this world, and to as certain damnation in the next.”", "비록 그와 동시에, 네가 이 세상에서는 확실한 비참을 향해, 다음 세상에서는 그만큼 확실한 저주를 향해 걸어가는 것을 더없는 근심으로 지켜보고 있지만 말이다.\""]], [["Square talked in a very different strain; he said, “Such accidents as a broken bone were below the consideration of a wise man. That it was abundantly sufficient to reconcile the mind to any of these mischances, to reflect that they are liable to befal the wisest of mankind, and are undoubtedly for the good of the whole.” He said, “It was a mere abuse of words to call those things evils, in which there was no moral unfitness: that pain, which was the worst consequence of such accidents, was the most contemptible thing in the world;” with more of the like sentences, extracted out of the second book of Tully's Tusculan questions, and from the great Lord Shaftesbury. In pronouncing these he was one day so eager, that he unfortunately bit his tongue; and in such a manner, that it not only put an end to his discourse, but created much emotion in him, and caused him to mutter an oath or two: but what was worst of all, this accident gave Thwackum, who was present, and who held all such doctrine to be heathenish and atheistical, an opportunity to clap a judgment on his back. Now this was done with so malicious a sneer, that it totally unhinged (if I may so say) the temper of the philosopher, which the bite of his tongue had somewhat ruffled; and as he was disabled from venting his wrath at his lips, he had possibly found a more violent method of revenging himself, had not the surgeon, who was then luckily in the room, contrary to his own interest, interposed and preserved the peace.", "스퀘어는 전혀 다른 가락으로 이야기했다. 그는 말했다. \"뼈가 부러진 것 따위의 사고는 현명한 사람이 마음 쓸 만한 것이 못 된다. 이런 불운을 마음에 받아들이기에는, 그것이 인류 가운데 가장 지혜로운 자에게도 닥칠 수 있는 일이며, 의심할 바 없이 전체의 선을 위한 것임을 헤아리는 것만으로 충분하고도 남는다.\" 그는 말했다. \"도덕적 부적합함이 전혀 없는 것들을 두고 악이라 부르는 것은 그저 말의 오용일 뿐이다. 이런 사고의 가장 나쁜 결과인 고통이라는 것도, 세상에서 가장 하찮은 것에 지나지 않는다.\" 이와 비슷한 문구들이 더 이어졌는데, 그것들은 툴리의 《투스쿨룸 담론》 제2권과 저 위대한 섀프츠베리 경에게서 따온 것이었다. 그런데 어느 날 그는 이런 말들을 어찌나 열을 올려 내뱉던지, 불행히도 자기 혀를 깨물고 말았다. 그것도 어찌나 심하게 깨물었던지, 말이 끊겼을 뿐 아니라 그의 마음에 큰 동요가 일어 욕설을 한두 마디 중얼거리기까지 했다. 그러나 무엇보다도 고약했던 것은, 그 자리에 있으면서 그런 학설을 죄다 이교도적이고 무신론적인 것으로 여기던 스왁검에게, 이 사고가 그의 등에 심판 한 방을 먹일 기회를 주었다는 점이다. 그런데 그것이 어찌나 짓궂은 비웃음을 곁들여 행해졌던지, 혀를 깨물어 가뜩이나 흐트러져 있던 이 철학자의 평정심을 (이렇게 말해도 된다면) 완전히 무너뜨리고 말았다. 그는 입술로 분노를 풀 수 없게 된 터라, 어쩌면 자신을 위해 더 난폭한 복수의 방법을 찾아냈을지도 모른다. 마침 그때 운 좋게도 방 안에 있던 외과의가, 자기 잇속과는 반대로 끼어들어 평화를 지켜 주지 않았다면 말이다."]], [["Mr Blifil visited his friend Jones but seldom, and never alone.", "블리필 씨는 친구 존스를 찾아오는 일이 드물었고, 결코 혼자 오는 법이 없었다."], ["This worthy young man, however, professed much regard for him, and as great concern at his misfortune; but cautiously avoided any intimacy, lest, as he frequently hinted, it might contaminate the sobriety of his own character: for which purpose he had constantly in his mouth that proverb in which Solomon speaks against evil communication.", "그래도 이 갸륵한 청년은 그에게 큰 관심을 표하고 그 불행에 깊은 염려를 나타냈으나, 어떤 친밀함이라도 조심스레 피했으니, 자신이 자주 넌지시 비치곤 했듯이 그것이 제 인품의 단정함을 더럽힐까 염려해서였다. 그런 까닭에 그는 솔로몬이 나쁜 교제를 두고 한 잠언을 입에 달고 다녔다."], ["Not that he was so bitter as Thwackum; for he always expressed some hopes of Tom's reformation; “which,”", "그렇다고 그가 스왁검만큼 신랄했던 것은 아니다. 그는 늘 톰이 개과천선하리라는 약간의 희망을 내비쳤으니, \"이번 일에 숙부께서 보여 주신 그 견줄 데 없는 자애로움이라면,\""], ["he said, “the unparalleled goodness shown by his uncle on this occasion, must certainly effect in one not absolutely abandoned:”", "그는 말했다, \"아주 못 쓰게 버려진 사람이 아닌 다음에야 반드시 그런 변화를 일으킬 것\"이라고 했다. 그러나 이렇게 매듭지었다."], ["but concluded, “if Mr Jones ever offends hereafter, I shall not be able to say a syllable in his favour.”", "\"만약 존스 씨가 이후에라도 또 잘못을 저지른다면, 나는 그를 두둔하는 말을 단 한마디도 할 수 없을 것입니다.\""]], [["As to Squire Western, he was seldom out of the sick-room, unless when he was engaged either in the field or over his bottle.", "웨스턴 영주로 말하자면, 그는 들판에 나가 있거나 술병을 끼고 있을 때가 아니면 좀처럼 병실을 떠나지 않았다. 아니, 그는 가끔 맥주를 마시러 일부러 이 방으로 물러나오기까지 했고, 존스에게도 억지로 맥주를 마시게 하려는 것을 말리느라 적잖이 애를 먹어야 했다."], ["Nay, he would sometimes retire hither to take his beer, and it was not without difficulty that he was prevented from forcing Jones to take his beer too: for no quack ever held his nostrum to be a more general panacea than he did this; which, he said, had more virtue in it than was in all the physic in an apothecary's shop.", "어떤 돌팔이도 제 묘약을 이만큼 만병통치약이라 여기지는 못했을 만큼, 그는 이 맥주를 떠받들었으니, 약방의 온갖 약을 다 합친 것보다 더 큰 효험이 이 안에 들어 있다고 했다. 그래도 그는 거듭된 간청에 못 이겨 이 약을 들이대는 것만큼은 삼가기로 했다."], ["He was, however, by much entreaty, prevailed on to forbear the application of this medicine; but from serenading his patient every hunting morning with the horn under his window, it was impossible to withhold him; nor did he ever lay aside that hallow, with which he entered into all companies, when he visited Jones, without any regard to the sick person's being at that time either awake or asleep.", "그러나 사냥하는 아침마다 창문 밑에서 나팔로 환자에게 노래를 불러 주는 것만은 도무지 말릴 수가 없었다. 또 그는 존스를 찾아올 때면 환자가 그 시각에 깨어 있는지 잠들어 있는지는 아랑곳없이, 어느 자리에 들어설 때나 내지르던 그 우렁찬 고함을 한 번도 거두는 법이 없었다."]], [["This boisterous behaviour, as it meant no harm, so happily it effected none, and was abundantly compensated to Jones, as soon as he was able to sit up, by the company of Sophia, whom the squire then brought to visit him; nor was it, indeed, long before Jones was able to attend her to the harpsichord, where she would kindly condescend, for hours together, to charm him with the most delicious music, unless when the squire thought proper to interrupt her, by insisting on Old Sir Simon, or some other of his favourite pieces.", "이런 떠들썩한 행동은 악의가 없었던 만큼 다행히 아무런 해도 끼치지 않았고, 존스가 일어나 앉을 수 있게 되자마자 영주가 데려온 소피아의 동행으로 넉넉히 보상받았다. 게다가 존스가 그녀를 따라 하프시코드 곁으로 갈 수 있게 되기까지는 정말이지 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그곳에서 그녀는 친절하게도 몸을 낮추어, 몇 시간이고 더없이 감미로운 음악으로 그를 사로잡아 주곤 했다. 다만 영주가 〈올드 사이먼 경〉이나 자기가 좋아하는 다른 곡들을 연주하라고 우겨 그녀를 가로막는 것이 마땅하다고 여길 때만은 예외였다."]], [["Notwithstanding the nicest guard which Sophia endeavoured to set on her behaviour, she could not avoid letting some appearances now and then slip forth: for love may again be likened to a disease in this, that when it is denied a vent in one part, it will certainly break out in another.", "소피아는 자기 행동에 더없이 섬세한 경계를 두려 애썼지만, 그래도 이따금 어떤 기색이 새어 나오는 것을 막을 수는 없었다. 사랑이란 이런 점에서 다시 한번 병에 견줄 만한데, 한쪽에서 빠져나갈 길이 막히면 반드시 다른 쪽에서 터져 나오기 때문이다."], ["What her lips, therefore, concealed, her eyes, her blushes, and many little involuntary actions, betrayed.", "그러므로 그녀의 입술이 감춘 것을, 그녀의 눈과 붉어진 뺨과 자신도 모르게 나오는 자잘한 몸짓들이 들통내고 말았다."]], [["One day, when Sophia was playing on the harpsichord, and Jones was attending, the squire came into the room, crying, “There, Tom, I have had a battle for thee below-stairs with thick parson Thwackum.", "어느 날, 소피아가 하프시코드를 연주하고 존스가 그 곁에서 듣고 있는데, 영주가 방으로 들어오며 소리쳤다. \"이봐, 톰, 내가 아래층에서 자네를 위해 저 둔한 목사 스왁검과 한바탕 싸우고 왔네."], ["He hath been a telling Allworthy, before my face, that the broken bone was a judgment upon thee.", "그자가 내 면전에서 올워디에게, 그 부러진 뼈가 자네에 대한 심판이라고 떠들어 대지 뭔가."], ["D--n it, says I, how can that be?", "빌어먹을, 내가 말했지, 그게 어떻게 그렇소?"], ["Did he not come by it in defence of a young woman?", "그 친구가 젊은 여자를 지키려다 그리된 거 아니오?"], ["A judgment indeed!", "심판은 무슨 놈의 심판!"], ["Pox, if he never doth anything worse, he will go to heaven sooner than all the parsons in the country.", "망할, 이보다 더 나쁜 짓만 안 한다면, 그 친구는 이 고장 어느 목사보다도 먼저 천국에 갈 거요. 부끄러워할 게 아니라 오히려 자랑할 만한 일이라고.\""], ["He hath more reason to glory in it than to be ashamed of it.”--“Indeed, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 어르신,\" 존스가 말했다, \"저로서는 자랑할 일도 부끄러워할 일도 아닙니다."], ["says Jones, “I have no reason for either; but if it preserved Miss Western, I shall always think it the happiest accident of my life.”--“And to gu,”", "다만 그 일로 웨스턴 양을 지킬 수 있었다면, 저는 그것을 언제까지나 제 인생에서 가장 행복한 사고였다고 여길 것입니다.\" \"그런데 그걸로,\""], ["said the squire, “to zet Allworthy against thee vor it!", "영주가 말했다, \"올워디를 자네에게 등 돌리게 하려 들다니!"], ["D--n un, if the parson had unt his petticuoats on, I should have lent un o flick; for I love thee dearly, my boy, and d--n me if there is anything in my power which I won't do for thee.", "빌어먹을, 그 목사가 사제복만 안 걸치고 있었어도 내가 한 대 갈겨 줬을 거야. 나는 자넬 끔찍이도 아끼니까 말이지, 이 사람아."], ["Sha't take thy choice of all the horses in my stable to-morrow morning, except only the Chevalier and Miss Slouch.”", "자네를 위해서라면 내 힘닿는 일은 무엇이든 안 할 게 없다네. 내일 아침에 내 마구간의 말 가운데 아무거나 마음대로 골라 가게."], ["Jones thanked him, but declined accepting the offer.", "슈발리에랑 미스 슬라우치만 빼고 말이야.\" 존스는 고마워하면서도 그 제안을 받기를 사양했다."], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["added the squire, “sha't ha the sorrel mare that Sophy rode.", "영주가 덧붙였다, \"소피가 타던 그 적갈색 암말을 갖게."], ["She cost me fifty guineas, and comes six years old this grass.”", "그놈을 사는 데 오십 기니가 들었고, 이번 봄에 여섯 살이 된다네.\""], ["“If she had cost me a thousand,”", "\"그 말이 천 기니를 줬다 한들,\""], ["cries Jones passionately, “I would have given her to the dogs.”", "존스가 격하게 외쳤다, \"저는 그놈을 개들에게나 던져 줬을 겁니다.\""], ["“Pooh! pooh!”", "\"쯧, 쯧!\""], ["answered Western; “what!", "웨스턴이 대꾸했다, \"이런!"], ["because she broke thy arm?", "그놈이 자네 팔을 부러뜨려서 그러나?"], ["Shouldst forget and forgive.", "잊고 용서해야지. 나는 자네가 말 못 하는 짐승에게 앙심을 품을 만큼 옹졸한 사람은 아니라고 여겼는데.\""], ["I thought hadst been more a man than to bear malice against a dumb creature.”--Here Sophia interposed, and put an end to the conversation, by desiring her father's leave to play to him; a request which he never refused.", "여기서 소피아가 끼어들어, 아버지께 한 곡 연주해 드리게 해 달라고 청하며 이야기를 끝냈다. 그것은 그가 결코 거절하는 법이 없는 부탁이었다."]], [["The countenance of Sophia had undergone more than one change during the foregoing speeches; and probably she imputed the passionate resentment which Jones had expressed against the mare, to a different motive from that from which her father had derived it.", "앞서 오간 이야기들이 이어지는 동안 소피아의 얼굴빛은 한 번 이상 바뀌었다. 그리고 존스가 그 암말에 대해 드러낸 격한 분노를, 그녀는 아마도 자기 아버지가 짐작한 것과는 다른 동기에서 비롯된 것으로 풀이했을 것이다."], ["Her spirits were at this time in a visible flutter; and she played so intolerably ill, that had not Western soon fallen asleep, he must have remarked it.", "이때 그녀의 마음은 눈에 띄게 떨리고 있었고, 연주가 어찌나 견딜 수 없을 만큼 형편없던지, 웨스턴이 곧 잠들어 버리지 않았더라면 그도 분명 그것을 알아챘을 것이다. 그러나 충분히 깨어 있던 존스는, 눈이 없지 않은 것과 마찬가지로 귀도 없지 않았던지라 몇 가지를 알아차렸다."], ["Jones, however, who was sufficiently awake, and was not without an ear any more than without eyes, made some observations; which being joined to all which the reader may remember to have passed formerly, gave him pretty strong assurances, when he came to reflect on the whole, that all was not well in the tender bosom of Sophia; an opinion which many young gentlemen will, I doubt not, extremely wonder at his not having been well confirmed in long ago.", "그리고 그것을, 독자가 앞서 일어났던 일들로 기억할 만한 모든 정황과 함께 엮어 전체를 곰곰이 되짚어 보니, 소피아의 여린 가슴속에 무언가 심상치 않은 것이 있다는 꽤 강한 확신이 들었다. 많은 젊은 신사들은 그가 이 사실을 진작에 단단히 확인해 두지 못한 것을 몹시 의아해하리라는 데 나는 의심이 없다."], ["To confess the truth, he had rather too much diffidence in himself, and was not forward enough in seeing the advances of a young lady; a misfortune which can be cured only by that early town education, which is at present so generally in fashion.", "사실대로 고백하자면, 그는 자기 자신을 좀 지나치게 미더워하지 못했고, 젊은 아가씨가 보내는 호의의 신호를 알아채는 데도 그리 민첩하지 못했다. 이것은 요즘 그토록 널리 유행하는 이른 도회지 교육으로만 고칠 수 있는 결함이었다."]], [["When these thoughts had fully taken possession of Jones, they occasioned a perturbation in his mind, which, in a constitution less pure and firm than his, might have been, at such a season, attended with very dangerous consequences.", "이런 생각들이 존스의 마음을 온통 사로잡자, 그것은 그의 마음에 동요를 불러일으켰다. 그의 것보다 덜 순수하고 덜 굳건한 체질이었다면, 그런 시기에 그 동요는 자칫 매우 위험한 결과로 이어졌을지도 모른다."], ["He was truly sensible of the great worth of Sophia.", "그는 소피아의 큰 가치를 진정으로 느끼고 있었다."], ["He extremely liked her person, no less admired her accomplishments, and tenderly loved her goodness.", "그녀의 자태를 더없이 좋아했고, 그 못지않게 그녀의 재주에 감탄했으며, 그 선량함을 애틋하게 사랑했다."], ["In reality, as he had never once entertained any thought of possessing her, nor had ever given the least voluntary indulgence to his inclinations, he had a much stronger passion for her than he himself was acquainted with.", "사실 그는 그녀를 차지하겠다는 생각을 단 한 번도 품어 본 적이 없었고, 자기 마음에 스스로 조금이라도 고삐를 늦춰 준 적도 없었기에, 정작 그 자신이 알고 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 강한 열정을 그녀에게 품고 있었다."], ["His heart now brought forth the full secret, at the same time that it assured him the adorable object returned his affection.", "이제 그의 가슴은 그 온전한 비밀을 끄집어냈고, 그와 동시에 그 사랑스러운 대상이 자신의 애정에 화답하고 있음을 그에게 확신시켜 주었다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["Which all who have no heart will think to contain much ado about nothing.", "마음이 없는 이들은 모두 이 장이 별것 아닌 일로 호들갑을 떤다고 여길 것이다."]], [["The reader will perhaps imagine the sensations which now arose in Jones to have been so sweet and delicious, that they would rather tend to produce a chearful serenity in the mind, than any of those dangerous effects which we have mentioned; but in fact, sensations of this kind, however delicious, are, at their first recognition, of a very tumultuous nature, and have very little of the opiate in them.", "독자는 어쩌면 존스의 가슴속에 지금 막 피어오른 그 느낌들이 너무도 달콤하고 황홀해서, 우리가 앞서 말한 그 위험한 결과들보다는 차라리 마음에 잔잔한 평온을 가져다주리라 짐작할지도 모른다. 그러나 사실 이런 종류의 느낌은 아무리 황홀하다 해도 처음 깨닫는 순간에는 몹시 소란스러운 성질을 지니며, 사람을 잠재우는 진정제 같은 구석은 거의 없다."], ["They were, moreover, in the present case, embittered with certain circumstances, which being mixed with sweeter ingredients, tended altogether to compose a draught that might be termed bitter-sweet; than which, as nothing can be more disagreeable to the palate, so nothing, in the metaphorical sense, can be so injurious to the mind.", "더구나 지금의 경우 그 느낌들은 어떤 사정들로 인해 쓰디쓰게 물들어 있었고, 그 사정들이 더 달콤한 재료와 뒤섞이면서 결국 쓴맛과 단맛이 함께 도는 음료라 부를 만한 것을 빚어냈다. 그런데 입에 이보다 더 거슬리는 것이 없듯, 비유적인 의미로도 이보다 마음에 더 해로운 것은 없다."]], [["For first, though he had sufficient foundation to flatter himself in what he had observed in Sophia, he was not yet free from doubt of misconstruing compassion, or at best, esteem, into a warmer regard.", "먼저, 그는 소피아에게서 본 것들을 근거로 스스로를 기쁘게 부풀릴 만한 충분한 토대를 갖고 있었지만, 그래도 자신이 연민을, 혹은 잘해야 호감을, 더 따뜻한 마음으로 잘못 읽고 있는 것은 아닐까 하는 의심에서 아직 자유롭지 못했다."], ["He was far from a sanguine assurance that Sophia had any such affection towards him, as might promise his inclinations that harvest, which, if they were encouraged and nursed, they would finally grow up to require.", "그는 소피아가 자기에게 그런 애정을 품고 있어서, 자기 마음이 자라나고 보살핌을 받으면 마침내 요구하게 될 그 수확을 약속해 주리라고 낙관할 만큼 확신에 차 있지 못했다."], ["Besides, if he could hope to find no bar to his happiness from the daughter, he thought himself certain of meeting an effectual bar in the father; who, though he was a country squire in his diversions, was perfectly a man of the world in whatever regarded his fortune; had the most violent affection for his only daughter, and had often signified, in his cups, the pleasure he proposed in seeing her married to one of the richest men in the county.", "게다가 설령 딸 쪽에서는 행복을 가로막는 장애가 없으리라 바랄 수 있다 해도, 아버지 쪽에서는 확실한 장벽을 만나리라고 그는 생각했다. 그 아버지는 오락을 즐길 때는 시골 지주였으나, 재산과 관련된 일에서는 완벽하게 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이었고, 외동딸에게 더없이 격렬한 애정을 품고 있었으며, 술잔을 기울일 때면 딸이 이 고장에서 가장 부유한 남자 가운데 하나와 결혼하는 것을 보는 즐거움을 종종 내비치곤 했다."], ["Jones was not so vain and senseless a coxcomb as to expect, from any regard which Western had professed for him, that he would ever be induced to lay aside these views of advancing his daughter.", "존스는 웨스턴이 자기에게 보여 온 호의를 빌미로, 그가 딸을 출세시키려는 이 계획을 언젠가 접게 되리라 기대할 만큼 허영심 많고 분별없는 멋쟁이는 아니었다."], ["He well knew that fortune is generally the principal, if not the sole, consideration, which operates on the best of parents in these matters: for friendship makes us warmly espouse the interest of others; but it is very cold to the gratification of their passions.", "그는 이런 일에서 가장 훌륭한 부모조차 재산이야말로 유일하지는 않더라도 주된 고려 대상이 됨을 잘 알고 있었다. 우정은 우리로 하여금 남의 이익을 따뜻하게 편들게 하지만, 남의 욕망을 채워 주는 일에는 몹시 차갑기 때문이다."], ["Indeed, to feel the happiness which may result from this, it is necessary we should possess the passion ourselves.", "사실 그 욕망에서 비롯될 행복을 느끼려면, 우리 자신이 그 욕망을 직접 지니고 있어야 한다."], ["As he had therefore no hopes of obtaining her father's consent; so he thought to endeavour to succeed without it, and by such means to frustrate the great point of Mr Western's life, was to make a very ill use of his hospitality, and a very ungrateful return to the many little favours received (however roughly) at his hands.", "그러니 그는 아버지의 동의를 얻을 가망이 없는 만큼, 동의 없이 일을 이루어 보려 애쓸까도 생각했지만, 그런 식으로 웨스턴 씨의 일생일대의 큰 목표를 좌절시킨다는 것은 그의 환대를 몹시 못되게 이용하는 짓이요, 그의 손에서 받은(아무리 거칠게 받았다 해도) 숱한 자잘한 호의에 대한 더없이 배은망덕한 보답이었다. 그런 결과를 그가 두려움과 경멸로 바라보았다면, 올워디 씨와 관련된 일에는 얼마나 더 큰 충격을 받았겠는가."], ["If he saw such a consequence with horror and disdain, how much more was he shocked with what regarded Mr Allworthy; to whom, as he had more than filial obligations, so had he for him more than filial piety!", "그는 올워디 씨에게 자식 이상의 의무를 지고 있었으니, 그분께 자식 이상의 효심을 품고 있었던 것이다! 그는 그 선한 분의 천성이 어떤 비열함이나 배신에도 너무나 질색하는지라, 그런 종류의 시도가 조금이라도 있으면 그 죄지은 자의 모습이 그분의 눈에 영영 가증스러워지고, 그 이름이 그분의 귀에 혐오스러운 소리가 되리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He knew the nature of that good man to be so averse to any baseness or treachery, that the least attempt of such a kind would make the sight of the guilty person for ever odious to his eyes, and his name a detestable sound in his ears.", "이렇게 넘을 수 없는 난관들의 모습만으로도, 그의 소망이 아무리 뜨거웠다 한들 그를 절망에 빠뜨리기에 충분했다. 그러나 이런 난관들조차 또 다른 여인에 대한 연민으로 다스려지고 있었다."], ["The appearance of such unsurmountable difficulties was sufficient to have inspired him with despair, however ardent his wishes had been; but even these were contruoled by compassion for another woman.", "사랑스러운 몰리의 모습이 이제 그의 앞을 비집고 들어왔다. 그는 그녀의 품에서 영원한 변치 않음을 맹세했고, 그녀도 그가 자기를 버리면 결코 살아남지 않겠노라 그만큼이나 자주 다짐했었다."], ["The idea of lovely Molly now intruded itself before him.", "그는 이제 그녀가 가장 끔찍한 죽음의 온갖 자세로 누워 있는 모습을 보았다."], ["He had sworn eternal constancy in her arms, and she had as often vowed never to out-live his deserting her.", "아니, 그녀가 빠지게 될 매춘의 온갖 비참함까지 떠올렸으니, 그 비참함의 원인은 두 곱절로 그 자신이었다."], ["He now saw her in all the most shocking postures of death; nay, he considered all the miseries of prostitution to which she would be liable, and of which he would be doubly the occasion; first by seducing, and then by deserting her; for he well knew the hatred which all her neighbours, and even her own sisters, bore her, and how ready they would all be to tear her to pieces.", "먼저 그녀를 유혹했고, 그다음에 그녀를 버린 까닭이다. 그는 그녀의 이웃들이, 심지어 그녀 자신의 자매들까지도 그녀에게 품은 미움을, 그리고 그들 모두가 얼마나 기꺼이 그녀를 갈가리 찢어 놓으려 들지를 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Indeed, he had exposed her to more envy than shame, or rather to the latter by means of the former: for many women abused her for being a whore, while they envied her her lover, and her finery, and would have been themselves glad to have purchased these at the same rate.", "실로 그는 그녀를 수치보다는 시샘에 더 내몰았으니, 아니 차라리 시샘을 통해 수치에 내몬 셈이었다. 많은 여자들이 그녀를 창녀라 욕하면서도 그녀의 연인과 그녀의 고운 옷을 부러워했고, 자기들도 같은 대가를 치르고 그것들을 손에 넣을 수 있었다면 기뻐했을 테니 말이다."], ["The ruin, therefore, of the poor girl must, he foresaw, unavoidably attend his deserting her; and this thought stung him to the soul.", "그러므로 자기가 그녀를 버리면 그 가엾은 처녀의 파멸이 피할 수 없이 뒤따르리라는 것을 그는 내다보았고, 이 생각이 그의 영혼을 찔렀다."], ["Poverty and distress seemed to him to give none a right of aggravating those misfortunes.", "가난과 곤궁이 누군가에게 그런 불행을 더 무겁게 얹을 권리를 준다고는 그에게 결코 여겨지지 않았다."], ["The meanness of her condition did not represent her misery as of little consequence in his eyes, nor did it appear to justify, or even to palliate, his guilt, in bringing that misery upon her.", "그녀의 처지가 미천하다 해서 그녀의 비참함이 그의 눈에 하찮게 보이지도 않았고, 그 비참함을 그녀에게 안긴 자기 죄가 정당화되거나 조금이라도 가벼워지는 것 같지도 않았다."], ["But why do I mention justification?", "그러나 내가 어찌 정당화를 입에 올리겠는가?"], ["His own heart would not suffer him to destroy a human creature who, he thought, loved him, and had to that love sacrificed her innocence.", "그의 마음 자체가, 그가 생각하기에 자기를 사랑했고 그 사랑을 위해 순결을 바친 한 인간을 파멸시키는 일을 차마 견디지 못했다."], ["His own good heart pleaded her cause; not as a cold venal advocate, but as one interested in the event, and which must itself deeply share in all the agonies its owner brought on another.", "그의 선한 마음이 그녀를 변호했다. 돈에 매수된 차가운 변호인으로서가 아니라, 그 결말에 직접 얽혀 있어, 자기 주인이 남에게 안긴 모든 고통을 스스로도 깊이 나누어 가질 수밖에 없는 자로서였다."]], [["When this powerful advocate had sufficiently raised the pity of Jones, by painting poor Molly in all the circumstances of wretchedness; it artfully called in the assistance of another passion, and represented the girl in all the amiable colours of youth, health, and beauty; as one greatly the object of desire, and much more so, at least to a good mind, from being, at the same time, the object of compassion.", "이 강력한 변호인이 가엾은 몰리를 비참함의 온갖 정경 속에 그려 보임으로써 존스의 연민을 충분히 끌어올리자, 그것은 교묘하게 또 다른 정념의 도움을 불러들여, 그 처녀를 젊음과 건강과 아름다움의 온갖 사랑스러운 빛깔로 그려 냈다. 그녀를 크나큰 욕망의 대상으로, 그리고 적어도 선한 마음에는, 동시에 연민의 대상이기도 하다는 점에서 한층 더한 욕망의 대상으로 비추어 보인 것이다."]], [["Amidst these thoughts, poor Jones passed a long sleepless night, and in the morning the result of the whole was to abide by Molly, and to think no more of Sophia.", "이런 생각들 가운데서 가엾은 존스는 잠 못 이루는 긴 밤을 보냈고, 아침이 되자 그 모든 것의 결론은 몰리 곁에 머무르고 소피아는 더 이상 생각하지 않겠다는 것이었다."]], [["In this virtuous resolution he continued all the next day till the evening, cherishing the idea of Molly, and driving Sophia from his thoughts; but in the fatal evening, a very trifling accident set all his passions again on float, and worked so total a change in his mind, that we think it decent to communicate it in a fresh chapter.", "그는 이 갸륵한 결심을 이튿날 저녁이 될 때까지 온종일 이어 가며, 몰리에 대한 생각을 소중히 품고 소피아를 마음에서 몰아냈다. 그러나 그 운명의 저녁, 아주 사소한 사건 하나가 그의 모든 정념을 다시금 일렁이게 했고, 그의 마음에 어찌나 완전한 변화를 일으켰던지, 우리는 그것을 새로운 장에서 전하는 편이 마땅하다고 여긴다."]], [["A little chapter, in which is contained a little incident.", "짧은 장. 그 안에 자그마한 사건 하나가 담겨 있다."]], [["Among other visitants, who paid their compliments to the young gentleman in his confinement, Mrs Honour was one.", "감금된 처지의 이 젊은 신사에게 문안 인사를 건넨 여러 방문객 가운데 어너 부인도 있었다. 독자께서는, 앞서 그녀의 입에서 흘러나온 몇몇 말들을 떠올리며, 어쩌면 그녀 자신이 존스 씨에게 아주 각별한 애정을 품고 있었으리라 짐작할지도 모른다."], ["The reader, perhaps, when he reflects on some expressions which have formerly dropt from her, may conceive that she herself had a very particular affection for Mr Jones; but, in reality, it was no such thing.", "그러나 실상은 전혀 그렇지 않았다. 톰은 잘생긴 젊은이였고, 어너 부인은 그런 부류의 남자들에게 약간의 호감을 두고 있었다."], ["Tom was a handsome young fellow; and for that species of men Mrs Honour had some regard; but this was perfectly indiscriminate; for having being crossed in the love which she bore a certain nobleman's footman, who had basely deserted her after a promise of marriage, she had so securely kept together the broken remains of her heart, that no man had ever since been able to possess himself of any single fragment.", "다만 그 호감은 누구에게나 똑같이 향하는 것이어서, 따로 가리는 법이 없었다. 어느 귀족의 하인에게 품었던 사랑이 어긋난 뒤로—그 사내는 결혼을 약속해 놓고 비열하게 그녀를 버렸다—그녀는 깨어진 마음의 잔해를 어찌나 단단히 한데 그러모아 두었던지, 그 후로는 어떤 남자도 그 조각 하나조차 차지하지 못했다."], ["She viewed all handsome men with that equal regard and benevolence which a sober and virtuous mind bears to all the good.", "그녀는 모든 잘생긴 남자를, 차분하고 정숙한 마음이 모든 선한 것을 대하듯 한결같은 호의와 너그러움으로 바라보았다. 실로 그녀는 인간을 사랑하는 사람이라 불릴 만했으니, 소크라테스가 인류를 사랑한 것과 같았다."], ["She might indeed be called a lover of men, as Socrates was a lover of mankind, preferring one to another for corporeal, as he for mental qualifications; but never carrying this preference so far as to cause any perturbation in the philosophical serenity of her temper.", "다만 소크라테스가 정신의 자질을 두고 한 사람을 다른 사람보다 더 좋아했다면, 그녀는 육체의 자질을 두고 그리했을 뿐이다. 그러나 그 선호를 결코 지나치게 끌고 가는 법이 없어, 철학자다운 그녀 기질의 고요함을 흩뜨리는 일은 없었다."]], [["The day after Mr Jones had that conflict with himself which we have seen in the preceding chapter, Mrs Honour came into his room, and finding him alone, began in the following manner:--“La, sir, where do you think I have been?", "존스 씨가 앞 장에서 우리가 본 그 내면의 갈등을 겪은 다음 날, 어너 부인이 그의 방으로 들어왔다. 그가 혼자 있는 것을 보고는 이렇게 말문을 열었다."], ["I warrants you, you would not guess in fifty years; but if you did guess, to be sure I must not tell you neither.”--“Nay, if it be something which you must not tell me,”", "\"어머, 나리, 제가 어디 다녀왔는지 아세요? 장담컨대 오십 년을 맞혀 보셔도 못 맞히실 거예요."], ["said Jones, “I shall have the curiosity to enquire, and I know you will not be so barbarous to refuse me.”--“I don't know,”", "그렇지만 맞히신다 해도, 그건 정말 말씀드리면 안 되는 거랍니다.\" \"허, 내게 말해선 안 되는 것이라면,\""], ["cries she, “why I should refuse you neither, for that matter; for to be sure you won't mention it any more.", "존스가 받았다. \"더더욱 궁금해서 캐묻고 싶어지는걸."], ["And for that matter, if you knew where I have been, unless you knew what I have been about, it would not signify much.", "그리고 자네가 나를 그렇게 매정하게 거절하지는 않으리라는 걸 나는 알지.\" \"글쎄요,\""], ["Nay, I don't see why it should be kept a secret for my part; for to be sure she is the best lady in the world.”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"사실 거절할 이유도 없긴 하죠."], ["Upon this, Jones began to beg earnestly to be let into this secret, and faithfully promised not to divulge it.", "나리께서 두 번 다시 입 밖에 안 내실 게 분명하니까요. 게다가 제가 어디 다녀왔는지 아신들, 거기서 무얼 했는지 모르시면 별 의미도 없잖아요."], ["She then proceeded thus:--“Why, you must know, sir, my young lady sent me to enquire after Molly Seagrim, and to see whether the wench wanted anything; to be sure, I did not care to go, methinks; but servants must do what they are ordered.--How could you undervalue yourself so, Mr Jones?--So my lady bid me go and carry her some linen, and other things.", "아니, 제 입장에선 굳이 비밀로 할 까닭도 없죠. 그분이야말로 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 아가씨시니까요.\""], ["She is too good.", "이 말에 존스는 그 비밀을 알려 달라고 간곡히 청하기 시작했고, 절대 발설하지 않겠노라 굳게 약속했다. 그러자 그녀가 이렇게 이어 갔다."], ["If such forward sluts were sent to Bridewell, it would be better for them.", "\"그러니까 말이죠, 나리, 우리 아가씨께서 저더러 몰리 시그림의 안부를 알아보고, 그 계집애가 뭐 필요한 게 없는지 살펴보고 오라 하셨어요. 솔직히 저야 가고 싶지 않았죠, 정말로요."], ["I told my lady, says I, madam, your la'ship is encouraging idleness.”--“And was my Sophia so good?”", "하지만 하인은 시키는 대로 해야 하는 법이니까요. 나리, 어쩌자고 스스로를 그렇게 값싸게 굴리셨어요?"], ["says Jones.", "아무튼 아가씨께서 가서 그 애한테 속옷이며 이것저것 갖다주라 하셨어요."], ["“My Sophia!", "우리 아가씨는 너무 마음이 고우셔서 탈이에요."], ["I assure you, marry come up,”", "그런 헤픈 계집들은 교화소로 보내는 편이 차라리 그 애들한테도 나을 텐데요. 제가 아가씨께 그랬어요, 마님, 마님께서는 게으름을 부추기고 계신 거예요, 하고요.\""], ["answered Honour.", "\"그래서 우리 소피아가 그렇게 마음씨가 고우셨다고?\" 존스가 말했다."], ["“And yet if you knew all--indeed, if I was as Mr Jones, I should look a little higher than such trumpery as Molly Seagrim.”", "\"우리 소피아라뇨! 어머나, 별말씀을 다 하시네.\""], ["“What do you mean by these words,”", "어너가 대꾸했다. \"그래도 나리께서 전부 다 아신다면 말이죠."], ["replied Jones, “if I knew all?”", "정말이지, 제가 만약 존스 나리라면 몰리 시그림 같은 하찮은 것보다는 좀 더 높은 데를 바라볼 거예요.\" \"다 안다면이라니, 그게 무슨 말인가?\""], ["“I mean what I mean,”", "존스가 되물었다. \"제 말은 제가 뜻하는 그대로지요,\""], ["says Honour.", "어너가 말했다. \"언젠가 나리께서 우리 아가씨 토시 속에 손을 넣으셨던 일, 기억 안 나세요?"], ["“Don't you remember putting your hands in my lady's muff once?", "아가씨가 절대 못 들으실 게 확실하다면, 정말이지 다 털어놓고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같은데요.\" 그러자 존스가 엄숙하게 여러 번 다짐을 했다."], ["I vow I could almost find in my heart to tell, if I was certain my lady would never come to the hearing on't.”", "어너가 말을 이었다. \"그러니까 말이죠, 아가씨께서 그 토시를 제게 주셨어요."], ["Jones then made several solemn protestations.", "그런데 나중에 나리께서 하신 일을 듣고 나서는—\" \"그럼 자네가 내가 한 일을 아가씨께 말씀드렸단 말인가?\""], ["And Honour proceeded--“Then to be sure, my lady gave me that muff; and afterwards, upon hearing what you had done”--“Then you told her what I had done?”", "존스가 끼어들었다. \"설령 그랬다 해도요, 나리,\""], ["interrupted Jones.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"저한테 화내실 필요는 없어요."], ["“If I did, sir,”", "자기들이 그런 줄 알았다면 우리 아가씨께 일러바칠 기회를 얻으려고 목이라도 내놓았을 사내가 한둘이 아닐 테니까요. 정말이지, 이 나라에서 제일가는 귀족이라도 우쭐할 만한 일이거든요."], ["answered she, “you need not be angry with me.", "그렇지만, 맹세코, 저는 나리께 말씀 안 드릴 작정이 굴뚝같아요.\" 존스가 사정사정하자, 그녀는 곧 마음이 풀려 이렇게 말을 이어 갔다."], ["Many's the man would have given his head to have had my lady told, if they had known,--for, to be sure, the biggest lord in the land might be proud--but, I protest, I have a great mind not to tell you.”", "\"그러니까 나리, 아가씨께서 그 토시를 제게 주셨다고 했잖아요. 그런데 제가 그 얘기를 말씀드린 지 하루 이틀쯤 지나서, 아가씨가 새 토시에 트집을 잡으시는 거예요."], ["Jones fell to entreaties, and soon prevailed on her to go on thus.", "그게 정말이지 여태껏 본 것 중에 제일 예쁜 토시인데도요. 어너야, 하시더니, 이 토시는 영 보기 싫구나."], ["“You must know then, sir, that my lady had given this muff to me; but about a day or two after I had told her the story, she quarrels with her new muff, and to be sure it is the prettiest that ever was seen.", "내겐 너무 커서 못 끼겠다. 다른 걸 구할 때까지 옛것을 도로 내게 다오, 그러면 그 대신 이걸 네가 가지렴, 하시는 거예요."], ["Honour, says she, this is an odious muff; it is too big for me, I can't wear it: till I can get another, you must let me have my old one again, and you may have this in the room on't--for she's a good lady, and scorns to give a thing and take a thing, I promise you that.", "우리 아가씨는 정말 마음이 고우셔서, 줬다 뺐는 짓 같은 건 경멸하시거든요, 그건 제가 장담해요. 그래서 제가 그걸 도로 갖다 드렸죠."], ["So to be sure I fetched it her back again, and, I believe, she hath worn it upon her arm almost ever since, and I warrants hath given it many a kiss when nobody hath seen her.”", "그런데 아가씨는 그 후로 거의 줄곧 그 토시를 팔에 끼고 계셨던 것 같아요. 그리고 분명히 아무도 안 볼 때 거기다 입맞춤도 숱하게 하셨을 거예요.\""]], [["Here the conversation was interrupted by Mr Western himself, who came to summon Jones to the harpsichord; whither the poor young fellow went all pale and trembling. This Western observed, but, on seeing Mrs Honour, imputed it to a wrong cause; and having given Jones a hearty curse between jest and earnest, he bid him beat abroad, and not poach up the game in his warren.", "여기서 대화는 웨스턴 씨가 몸소 들이닥치는 바람에 끊기고 말았다. 그는 존스를 하프시코드 앞으로 불러내러 온 것이었다. 가엾은 젊은이는 새파랗게 질려 부들부들 떨며 그리로 갔다. 웨스턴도 그 모습을 보았으나, 어너 부인이 있는 것을 보고는 엉뚱한 데로 까닭을 돌렸다. 그러고는 농담 반 진담 반으로 존스에게 걸쭉한 욕설을 한바탕 퍼붓더니, 다른 데 가서나 쏘다니지 제 사냥터에서 사냥감을 슬쩍 가로채지는 말라고 일렀다."]], [["Sophia looked this evening with more than usual beauty, and we may believe it was no small addition to her charms, in the eye of Mr Jones, that she now happened to have on her right arm this very muff.", "이날 저녁 소피아는 여느 때보다도 더 아름다워 보였다. 그리고 존스 씨의 눈에는, 그녀가 마침 그 오른팔에 바로 그 토시를 끼고 있다는 사실이 그 매력에 적지 않게 보태졌으리라 믿어도 좋을 것이다."]], [["She was playing one of her father's favourite tunes, and he was leaning on her chair, when the muff fell over her fingers, and put her out.", "소피아가 아버지가 좋아하는 곡 가운데 하나를 연주하고 있을 때, 그는 그녀의 의자에 기대어 서 있었다. 그런데 그 토시가 그녀의 손가락 위로 흘러내려 연주를 그르치게 했다."], ["This so disconcerted the squire, that he snatched the muff from her, and with a hearty curse threw it into the fire.", "이에 향사는 어찌나 짜증이 났던지 그녀에게서 토시를 낚아채더니, 걸쭉한 욕설과 함께 불 속으로 던져 버렸다."], ["Sophia instantly started up, and with the utmost eagerness recovered it from the flames.", "소피아는 즉시 벌떡 일어나, 더없이 다급한 손길로 불길 속에서 그것을 건져 냈다."]], [["Though this incident will probably appear of little consequence to many of our readers; yet, trifling as it was, it had so violent an effect on poor Jones, that we thought it our duty to relate it.", "이 사건이 우리 독자들 가운데 많은 분께는 아마 대수롭지 않게 보일 것이다. 그러나 그토록 하찮은 일이 가엾은 존스에게는 어찌나 격렬한 영향을 미쳤던지, 우리는 이를 전하는 것이 우리의 의무라 여겼다."], ["In reality, there are many little circumstances too often omitted by injudicious historians, from which events of the utmost importance arise.", "사실, 분별없는 기록자들이 너무도 자주 빠뜨리는 자그마한 정황들 가운데서 더없이 중대한 사건들이 솟아나는 법이다."], ["The world may indeed be considered as a vast machine, in which the great wheels are originally set in motion by those which are very minute, and almost imperceptible to any but the strongest eyes.", "세상이란 실로 거대한 기계와 같다고 할 수 있다. 그 안에서 큰 톱니바퀴들은 본디 아주 미세하여, 어지간히 날카로운 눈이 아니고서는 거의 알아챌 수 없는 작은 톱니바퀴들에 의해 비로소 움직이기 시작하는 것이다."]], [["Thus, not all the charms of the incomparable Sophia; not all the dazzling brightness, and languishing softness of her eyes; the harmony of her voice, and of her person; not all her wit, good-humour, greatness of mind, or sweetness of disposition, had been able so absolutely to conquer and enslave the heart of poor Jones, as this little incident of the muff.", "이리하여 견줄 데 없는 소피아의 그 모든 매력도, 그녀 눈동자의 그 눈부신 광채와 애틋한 부드러움도, 그 목소리와 자태의 조화도, 그 모든 재치와 쾌활함과 드넓은 마음과 다정한 성품도, 이 토시라는 자그마한 사건만큼 가엾은 존스의 마음을 그토록 완전히 정복하고 사로잡지는 못했다."], ["Thus the poet sweetly sings of Troy--", "시인이 트로이를 두고 감미롭게 노래한 그대로다—\n속임수에 사로잡히고 눈물에 무릎 꿇었으니,"]], [["--Captique dolis lachrymisque coacti Quos neque Tydides, nec Larissaeus Achilles, Non anni domuere decem, non mille Carinae.", "튀데우스의 아들도, 라리사의 아킬레우스도,\n십 년 세월도, 천 척의 배도 이루지 못한 것을."]], [["What Diomede or Thetis' greater son, A thousand ships, nor ten years' siege had done False tears and fawning words the city won.", "디오메데스도, 테티스의 더 위대한 아들도,\n천 척의 배도, 십 년의 포위도 못 해낸 일을,"]], [["The citadel of Jones was now taken by surprize.", "거짓 눈물과 아첨하는 말이 그 도성을 빼앗았네."], ["All those considerations of honour and prudence which our heroe had lately with so much military wisdom placed as guards over the avenues of his heart, ran away from their posts, and the god of love marched in, in triumph.", "이제 존스의 성채는 기습으로 함락되었다. 우리의 주인공이 얼마 전 그토록 군인다운 지혜로 제 마음의 길목마다 파수꾼으로 세워 두었던 명예와 신중함의 그 모든 헤아림은 제자리를 버리고 달아났고, 사랑의 신이 의기양양하게 진군해 들어왔다."]], [["A very long chapter, containing a very great incident.", "매우 긴 장으로, 매우 중대한 사건을 담고 있다."]], [["But though this victorious deity easily expelled his avowed enemies from the heart of Jones, he found it more difficult to supplant the garrison which he himself had placed there.", "그러나 이 승리의 신이 존스의 가슴에서 공공연한 적들을 손쉽게 몰아내기는 했어도, 정작 자기 손으로 그곳에 세워 두었던 수비대를 밀어내는 일은 한결 어렵게 느껴졌다."], ["To lay aside all allegory, the concern for what must become of poor Molly greatly disturbed and perplexed the mind of the worthy youth.", "비유는 모두 접어 두고 말하자면, 가엾은 몰리가 앞으로 어찌 될 것인가 하는 걱정이 이 마음씨 고운 청년의 머릿속을 크게 어지럽히고 괴롭혔던 것이다."], ["The superior merit of Sophia totally eclipsed, or rather extinguished, all the beauties of the poor girl; but compassion instead of contempt succeeded to love.", "소피아의 빼어난 미덕은 이 가련한 처녀의 온갖 아름다움을 완전히 가렸다기보다 아예 꺼뜨려 버렸다. 그러나 사랑이 떠난 자리를 차지한 것은 경멸이 아니라 연민이었다."], ["He was convinced the girl had placed all her affections, and all her prospect of future happiness, in him only.", "그는 그 처녀가 자신의 온 사랑과, 앞날의 행복에 대한 모든 기대를 오로지 자기 한 사람에게만 걸어 두었음을 확신하고 있었다."], ["For this he had, he knew, given sufficient occasion, by the utmost profusion of tenderness towards her: a tenderness which he had taken every means to persuade her he would always maintain.", "그가 그녀에게 더할 나위 없이 넘치도록 애정을 쏟아부어, 그렇게 만들 충분한 빌미를 스스로 제공했다는 사실을 그는 알고 있었다. 게다가 그 애정을 언제까지나 지켜 나가겠노라고 온갖 방법을 다해 그녀에게 믿게 만들었던 것이다."], ["She, on her side, had assured him of her firm belief in his promise, and had with the most solemn vows declared, that on his fulfilling or breaking these promises, it depended, whether she should be the happiest or most miserable of womankind.", "그녀 또한 그의 약속을 굳게 믿는다고 다짐했고, 그가 그 약속을 지키느냐 깨느냐에 따라 자신이 여인들 가운데 가장 행복한 사람이 될지, 아니면 가장 비참한 사람이 될지가 달려 있다고 더없이 엄숙한 맹세로 선언했었다."], ["And to be the author of this highest degree of misery to a human being, was a thought on which he could not bear to ruminate a single moment.", "한 인간에게 이처럼 극심한 불행을 안겨 준 장본인이 된다는 생각은, 그가 단 한순간도 곱씹어 견딜 수 없는 것이었다."], ["He considered this poor girl as having sacrificed to him everything in her little power; as having been at her own expense the object of his pleasure; as sighing and languishing for him even at that very instant.", "그는 이 가엾은 처녀가 자신의 보잘것없는 힘이 미치는 모든 것을 자기에게 바쳤으며, 제 스스로를 희생하면서까지 자기 쾌락의 대상이 되어 주었고, 바로 지금 이 순간에도 자기를 그리며 한숨짓고 애태우고 있으리라 여겼다."], ["Shall then, says he, my recovery, for which she hath so ardently wished; shall my presence, which she hath so eagerly expected, instead of giving her that joy with which she hath flattered herself, cast her at once down into misery and despair?", "그렇다면, 하고 그는 혼잣말을 했다, 그녀가 그토록 간절히 바라던 나의 회복이, 그녀가 그토록 애타게 기다리던 나의 출현이, 그녀가 스스로 부풀려 온 그 기쁨을 안겨 주기는커녕 도리어 그녀를 단숨에 비참과 절망 속으로 떨어뜨린단 말인가?"], ["Can I be such a villain?", "내가 그런 악당이 될 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Here, when the genius of poor Molly seemed triumphant, the love of Sophia towards him, which now appeared no longer dubious, rushed upon his mind, and bore away every obstacle before it.", "이렇게 가엾은 몰리의 수호신이 승리를 거두는 듯하던 바로 그 순간, 이제 더는 의심할 여지가 없어 보이는 소피아를 향한 그의 사랑이 그의 마음속으로 밀려들어, 앞을 가로막는 온갖 장애물을 모조리 쓸어 가 버렸다."]], [["At length it occurred to him, that he might possibly be able to make Molly amends another way; namely, by giving her a sum of money.", "마침내 그의 머릿속에 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. 어쩌면 다른 방식으로 몰리에게 보상해 줄 수 있을지도 모른다는 것, 즉 그녀에게 한 뭉치의 돈을 쥐여 주는 것이었다."], ["This, nevertheless, he almost despaired of her accepting, when he recollected the frequent and vehement assurances he had received from her, that the world put in balance with him would make her no amends for his loss.", "그러나 세상을 그와 저울에 올려 견준다 해도 그를 잃은 것을 결코 메워 줄 수 없으리라고, 그녀가 자주 그리고 격렬하게 다짐했던 말을 떠올리자, 그녀가 그것을 받아들이리라는 데에는 거의 절망스러운 마음이 들었다."], ["However, her extreme poverty, and chiefly her egregious vanity (somewhat of which hath been already hinted to the reader), gave him some little hope, that, notwithstanding all her avowed tenderness, she might in time be brought to content herself with a fortune superior to her expectation, and which might indulge her vanity, by setting her above all her equals.", "그럼에도 그녀의 극심한 가난, 그리고 무엇보다도 그녀의 유별난 허영심이(이에 대해서는 독자에게 이미 어느 정도 귀띔한 바 있다) 그에게 약간의 희망을 안겨 주었다. 비록 그녀가 그토록 애정을 떠벌렸다 해도, 자신의 기대를 뛰어넘는 재물, 게다가 그녀를 또래 누구보다도 위에 올려놓아 그 허영심을 채워 줄 재물이라면, 머지않아 그것으로 만족할 마음이 생길지도 모른다는 희망이었다."], ["He resolved therefore to take the first opportunity of making a proposal of this kind.", "그리하여 그는 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 이런 제안을 꺼내 보기로 결심했다."]], [["One day, accordingly, when his arm was so well recovered that he could walk easily with it slung in a sash, he stole forth, at a season when the squire was engaged in his field exercises, and visited his fair one.", "그래서 어느 날, 그의 팔이 어지간히 회복되어 띠로 둘러 멘 채로도 수월히 걸을 수 있게 되자, 향사가 들에서 자신의 운동에 한창 빠져 있는 틈을 타 그는 슬며시 빠져나가 어여쁜 자기 여인을 찾아갔다. 차를 마시고 있던 그녀의 어머니와 자매들은 그에게 우선 몰리가 집에 없다고 알렸다."], ["Her mother and sisters, whom he found taking their tea, informed him first that Molly was not at home; but afterwards the eldest sister acquainted him, with a malicious smile, that she was above stairs a-bed.", "그러나 곧이어 맏언니가 짓궂은 미소를 지으며, 그녀가 위층에서 잠자리에 누워 있다고 일러 주었다. 톰은 자기 연인이 그런 자세로 있다는 데 아무런 이의가 없었으므로, 곧장 그녀의 침실로 이어지는 사다리를 올라갔다."], ["Tom had no objection to this situation of his mistress, and immediately ascended the ladder which led towards her bed-chamber; but when he came to the top, he, to his great surprize, found the door fast; nor could he for some time obtain any answer from within; for Molly, as she herself afterwards informed him, was fast asleep.", "그러나 꼭대기에 다다랐을 때, 그는 크게 놀랍게도 문이 단단히 잠겨 있는 것을 발견했다. 한참 동안이나 안에서는 아무런 대답도 들려오지 않았으니, 몰리가, 그녀가 나중에 직접 그에게 일러 준 바로는, 깊이 잠들어 있었기 때문이었다."]], [["The extremes of grief and joy have been remarked to produce very similar effects; and when either of these rushes on us by surprize, it is apt to create such a total perturbation and confusion, that we are often thereby deprived of the use of all our faculties.", "슬픔과 기쁨의 극단은 매우 비슷한 결과를 낳는다고들 한다. 이 둘 가운데 어느 하나가 느닷없이 우리에게 밀어닥치면, 그것은 온통 마음을 뒤흔들고 어지럽혀 우리가 가진 모든 능력을 쓸 수 없게 만들어 버리기 일쑤다."], ["It cannot therefore be wondered at, that the unexpected sight of Mr Jones should so strongly operate on the mind of Molly, and should overwhelm her with such confusion, that for some minutes she was unable to express the great raptures, with which the reader will suppose she was affected on this occasion.", "그러니 존스 씨의 뜻밖의 모습이 몰리의 마음에 그토록 강하게 작용해, 그녀를 그런 혼란으로 압도해 버린 것도 이상한 일은 아니다. 그래서 몇 분 동안 그녀는, 이 자리에서 그녀가 휩싸였으리라고 독자가 짐작할 그 크나큰 환희를 미처 표현하지 못했다."], ["As for Jones, he was so entirely possessed, and as it were enchanted, by the presence of his beloved object, that he for a while forgot Sophia, and consequently the principal purpose of his visit.", "존스로 말하자면, 그는 사랑하는 대상의 출현에 완전히 사로잡혀, 이를테면 홀린 듯했던 까닭에, 한동안 소피아를, 따라서 자신이 이곳을 찾은 본래의 목적까지도 까맣게 잊고 있었다."]], [["This, however, soon recurred to his memory; and after the first transports of their meeting were over, he found means by degrees to introduce a discourse on the fatal consequences which must attend their amour, if Mr Allworthy, who had strictly forbidden him ever seeing her more, should discover that he still carried on this commerce.", "그러나 이내 그것이 그의 기억 속에 되살아났다. 만남의 첫 격정이 가라앉고 나자, 그는 조금씩 화제를 끌어내어, 자신에게 그녀를 다시는 만나지 말라고 엄히 금하셨던 올워디 씨가 자기가 여전히 이런 왕래를 이어 가고 있음을 알아채기라도 한다면, 그들의 정사에 어떤 끔찍한 결과가 뒤따를 수밖에 없는지를 이야기하기 시작했다."], ["Such a discovery, which his enemies gave him reason to think would be unavoidable, must, he said, end in his ruin, and consequently in hers.", "그런 발각은, 자기의 적들이 미루어 보건대 피할 수 없으리라 여겨질 만한 일이며, 그렇게 되면 결국 자신의 파멸로, 따라서 그녀의 파멸로 끝날 수밖에 없다고 그는 말했다. 그러니 모진 운명이 두 사람을 갈라놓아야 한다고 정해 놓은 이상, 그녀에게 그것을 의연히 견뎌 달라고 그는 당부했다."], ["Since therefore their hard fates had determined that they must separate, he advised her to bear it with resolution, and swore he would never omit any opportunity, through the course of his life, of showing her the sincerity of his affection, by providing for her in a manner beyond her utmost expectation, or even beyond her wishes, if ever that should be in his power; concluding at last, that she might soon find some man who would marry her, and who would make her much happier than she could be by leading a disreputable life with him.", "그리고 자기 평생에 걸쳐, 만약 언젠가 그럴 힘이 자기에게 생긴다면, 그녀의 더없는 기대를, 아니 그녀의 바람마저도 뛰어넘는 방식으로 그녀를 보살펴 줌으로써 자기 애정의 진실함을 보여 줄 기회를 결코 놓치지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 끝으로 그는, 그녀가 머지않아 자신과 결혼해 줄 어떤 남자를, 그것도 그녀가 그와 떳떳지 못한 삶을 이어 가는 것보다 훨씬 더 행복하게 해 줄 남자를 만날 수 있을 거라고 마무리 지었다."]], [["Molly remained a few moments in silence, and then bursting into a flood of tears, she began to upbraid him in the following words: “And this is your love for me, to forsake me in this manner, now you have ruined me!", "몰리는 잠시 말없이 가만히 있더니, 이윽고 눈물을 펑펑 쏟으며 다음과 같은 말로 그를 나무라기 시작했다. \"이게 저를 향한 당신의 사랑인가요?"], ["How often, when I have told you that all men are false and perjury alike, and grow tired of us as soon as ever they have had their wicked wills of us, how often have you sworn you would never forsake me!", "이제 저를 망쳐 놓고서, 이런 식으로 저를 버리는 게요! 남자란 다 거짓되고 위증하기는 매한가지라서, 우리에게서 못된 욕심을 채우자마자 우리한테 싫증을 낸다고 제가 말할 때마다, 당신은 얼마나 자주 저를 결코 버리지 않겠다고 맹세했던가요!"], ["And can you be such a perjury man after all?", "그래 놓고 결국 그런 거짓 맹세나 일삼는 사람일 수가 있나요?"], ["What signifies all the riches in the world to me without you, now you have gained my heart, so you have--you have--?", "이제 당신이 제 마음을 다 가져갔는데, 정말 그래 놓고서, 당신 없는 세상의 온갖 재물이 제게 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?"], ["Why do you mention another man to me?", "어째서 제게 다른 남자 이야기를 꺼내는 거예요?"], ["I can never love any other man as long as I live.", "저는 살아 있는 한 다른 어떤 남자도 사랑할 수 없어요."], ["All other men are nothing to me.", "다른 남자들은 다 제겐 아무것도 아니에요."], ["If the greatest squire in all the country would come a suiting to me to-morrow, I would not give my company to him.", "이 고장에서 제일가는 향사가 내일 저한테 청혼하러 온다 해도, 저는 그에게 곁을 내주지 않을 거예요."], ["No, I shall always hate and despise the whole sex for your sake.”--", "아니, 저는 당신 때문에라도 그 종자 전체를 늘 미워하고 경멸할 거라고요.\""]], [["She was proceeding thus, when an accident put a stop to her tongue, before it had run out half its career.", "이렇게 말을 이어 가던 그녀의 혀가, 미처 그 절반도 달리기 전에 한 사건으로 인해 뚝 멈춰 버렸다."], ["The room, or rather garret, in which Molly lay, being up one pair of stairs, that is to say, at the top of the house, was of a sloping figure, resembling the great Delta of the Greeks.", "몰리가 누워 있던 방은, 아니 다락방이라고 해야겠지만, 계단 하나를 올라간 곳, 다시 말해 집의 맨 꼭대기에 자리하고 있어, 그리스 문자의 커다란 델타를 닮은 비스듬한 형상이었다."], ["The English reader may perhaps form a better idea of it, by being told that it was impossible to stand upright anywhere but in the middle.", "영국 독자라면, 한가운데 말고는 어디서도 똑바로 설 수 없었다는 말을 들으면 그 모양을 더 잘 떠올릴 수 있으리라."], ["Now, as this room wanted the conveniency of a closet, Molly had, to supply that defect, nailed up an old rug against the rafters of the house, which enclosed a little hole where her best apparel, such as the remains of that sack which we have formerly mentioned, some caps, and other things with which she had lately provided herself, were hung up and secured from the dust.", "그런데 이 방에는 벽장이라는 편의 시설이 없었으므로, 몰리는 그 결함을 메우려고 집의 서까래에 낡은 깔개 하나를 못으로 박아 두었다. 그것이 작은 구멍 하나를 가려 주고 있었는데, 그 안에는 그녀의 가장 좋은 옷가지들, 이를테면 앞서 언급한 적이 있는 그 자루 같은 드레스의 남은 부분이며, 두건 몇 개, 그리고 그녀가 최근에 마련해 둔 다른 물건들이 먼지를 타지 않게 걸려 보관되어 있었다."]], [["This enclosed place exactly fronted the foot of the bed, to which, indeed, the rug hung so near, that it served in a manner to supply the want of curtains.", "이 가려진 자리는 침대 발치 바로 맞은편에 있었고, 사실 그 깔개가 침대에 어찌나 가까이 드리워져 있었던지, 어떤 의미에서는 커튼이 없는 것을 대신해 주는 노릇까지 했다. 그런데 몰리가 분노의 격정 속에서 발로 이 깔개를 밀쳤는지, 아니면 존스가 그것에 손을 댔는지, 그것도 아니면 핀이나 못이 저 혼자 빠진 것인지 나로서는 확실치 않다."], ["Now, whether Molly, in the agonies of her rage, pushed this rug with her feet; or Jones might touch it; or whether the pin or nail gave way of its own accord, I am not certain; but as Molly pronounced those last words, which are recorded above, the wicked rug got loose from its fastening, and discovered everything hid behind it; where among other female utensils appeared--(with shame I write it, and with sorrow will it be read)--the philosopher Square, in a posture (for the place would not near admit his standing upright) as ridiculous as can possibly be conceived.", "다만 몰리가 위에 적어 둔 그 마지막 말을 내뱉는 순간, 고약한 깔개가 그 못질에서 풀려나 그 뒤에 감춰져 있던 모든 것을 드러내고 말았다. 거기에는 다른 여인네의 살림 도구들 틈에 끼여 ― 부끄러움을 무릅쓰고 적거니와, 또한 슬픔에 잠겨 읽힐 일이거니와 ― 철학자 스퀘어가, 도무지 떠올릴 수 없을 만큼 우스꽝스러운 자세로(그 자리는 그가 똑바로 서는 것을 도저히 허락하지 않았으므로) 모습을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["The posture, indeed, in which he stood, was not greatly unlike that of a soldier who is tied neck and heels; or rather resembling the attitude in which we often see fellows in the public streets of London, who are not suffering but deserving punishment by so standing.", "실로 그가 서 있던 자세는, 목과 발꿈치가 한데 묶인 병사의 꼴과 그리 다르지 않았다. 아니, 그보다는 런던의 큰길거리에서 우리가 흔히 보는 사내들, 즉 그렇게 서서 형벌을 받고 있는 것이 아니라 마땅히 그런 형벌을 받아야 할 자들의 모양새에 더 가까웠다."], ["He had a nightcap belonging to Molly on his head, and his two large eyes, the moment the rug fell, stared directly at Jones; so that when the idea of philosophy was added to the figure now discovered, it would have been very difficult for any spectator to have refrained from immoderate laughter.", "그는 몰리의 것인 잠자리 두건을 머리에 쓰고 있었고, 깔개가 떨어지는 순간 그의 커다란 두 눈은 곧장 존스를 향해 부릅떠졌다. 그리하여 이제 막 드러난 그 꼴에 철학이라는 관념까지 더해지고 보니, 어떤 구경꾼이라도 터져 나오는 웃음을 참기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었을 것이다."]], [["I question not but the surprize of the reader will be here equal to that of Jones; as the suspicions which must arise from the appearance of this wise and grave man in such a place, may seem so inconsistent with that character which he hath, doubtless, maintained hitherto, in the opinion of every one.", "이 대목에서 독자의 놀라움도 존스의 놀라움 못지않으리라는 데 나는 의심이 없다. 이 현명하고 근엄한 사내가 그런 곳에 나타난 데서 솟아날 수밖에 없는 의혹들이, 그가 여태껏 모든 이의 눈에 의심할 바 없이 지켜 왔을 그 성품과 너무도 어긋나 보일 테니 말이다."]], [["But to confess the truth, this inconsistency is rather imaginary than real.", "그러나 사실대로 고백하자면, 이 어긋남이란 실제라기보다는 차라리 상상 속의 것이다. 철학자들 또한 다른 인간 피조물들과 마찬가지로 살과 피로 이루어져 있다."], ["Philosophers are composed of flesh and blood as well as other human creatures; and however sublimated and refined the theory of these may be, a little practical frailty is as incident to them as to other mortals.", "그들의 이론이 아무리 고상하고 정련되어 있다 한들, 약간의 실천적 나약함이란 다른 평범한 인간들에게 그러하듯 그들에게도 흔히 따라붙는 법이다. 앞서 넌지시 비친 바와 같이, 그 차이라는 것은 실로 이론에만 있을 뿐 실천에 있는 것이 아니다."], ["It is, indeed, in theory only, and not in practice, as we have before hinted, that consists the difference: for though such great beings think much better and more wisely, they always act exactly like other men.", "그런 위대한 존재들은 훨씬 더 훌륭하고 더 지혜롭게 생각하기는 해도, 행동은 언제나 다른 사람들과 똑같이 하기 때문이다. 그들은 온갖 식욕과 정념을 굴복시키고 고통도 쾌락도 모두 경멸하는 법을 아주 잘 알고 있으며, 이 앎은 그들에게 더없이 즐거운 사색거리를 안겨 주고 또 손쉽게 익혀진다."], ["They know very well how to subdue all appetites and passions, and to despise both pain and pleasure; and this knowledge affords much delightful contemplation, and is easily acquired; but the practice would be vexatious and troublesome; and, therefore, the same wisdom which teaches them to know this, teaches them to avoid carrying it into execution.", "그러나 그것을 실천하기란 성가시고 골치 아픈 일이다. 그러므로 이를 알도록 가르쳐 주는 바로 그 지혜가, 그것을 굳이 실행에 옮기는 일은 피하라고도 그들에게 가르치는 것이다."]], [["Mr Square happened to be at church on that Sunday, when, as the reader may be pleased to remember, the appearance of Molly in her sack had caused all that disturbance.", "스퀘어 씨는 마침 그 일요일에 교회에 와 있었다. 독자가 기억해 주시면 좋겠지만, 자루 같은 드레스를 입은 몰리의 등장이 그 온갖 소동을 불러일으켰던 바로 그날이다."], ["Here he first observed her, and was so pleased with her beauty, that he prevailed with the young gentlemen to change their intended ride that evening, that he might pass by the habitation of Molly, and by that means might obtain a second chance of seeing her.", "그는 여기서 처음으로 그녀를 눈여겨보았고, 그 미모에 어찌나 흡족했던지 그날 저녁 젊은 신사들을 구슬려 본래 예정했던 승마 길을 바꾸게 했다. 그래야 몰리의 거처를 지나치면서 그녀를 한 번 더 볼 기회를 얻을 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["This reason, however, as he did not at that time mention to any, so neither did we think proper to communicate it then to the reader.", "그러나 이 까닭을 그가 그 당시 누구에게도 말하지 않았으므로, 우리 또한 그때 독자에게 굳이 알리는 것이 옳다고 여기지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Among other particulars which constituted the unfitness of things in Mr Square's opinion, danger and difficulty were two.", "스퀘어 씨가 보기에 사물의 부적합함을 이루는 여러 요소 가운데, 위험과 어려움이 그 둘이었다."], ["The difficulty therefore which he apprehended there might be in corrupting this young wench, and the danger which would accrue to his character on the discovery, were such strong dissuasives, that it is probable he at first intended to have contented himself with the pleasing ideas which the sight of beauty furnishes us with.", "그러므로 이 젊은 처녀를 타락시키는 데 있을지 모를 어려움, 그리고 발각될 경우 자기 평판에 미칠 위험이 어찌나 강한 만류의 힘이 되었던지, 아마 처음에는 미모를 바라보는 데서 얻어지는 그 즐거운 상념들로 만족하려 했던 듯하다."], ["These the gravest men, after a full meal of serious meditation, often allow themselves by way of dessert: for which purpose, certain books and pictures find their way into the most private recesses of their study, and a certain liquorish part of natural philosophy is often the principal subject of their conversation.", "더없이 근엄한 사내들도 진지한 명상을 한바탕 배불리 하고 난 뒤에는, 그런 상념들을 후식 삼아 종종 스스로에게 허락하곤 한다. 바로 그 목적으로, 어떤 책이며 그림 따위가 그들 서재의 가장 은밀한 구석까지 흘러들고, 자연철학의 어떤 음탕한 대목이 흔히 그들 대화의 으뜸가는 주제가 되는 것이다."]], [["But when the philosopher heard, a day or two afterwards, that the fortress of virtue had already been subdued, he began to give a larger scope to his desires.", "그러나 이 철학자가 하루 이틀 뒤에 정조의 요새가 이미 함락되었다는 소식을 듣자, 그는 자신의 욕망을 더 넓게 풀어놓기 시작했다. 그의 식욕은, 누가 이미 맛보았다 하여 별미를 입에 대지 못하는 그런 까다로운 부류가 아니었다."], ["His appetite was not of that squeamish kind which cannot feed on a dainty because another hath tasted it.", "한마디로, 그는 그녀가 정조를 잃었다는 바로 그 이유로 그녀가 더욱 마음에 들었다."], ["In short, he liked the girl the better for the want of that chastity, which, if she had possessed it, must have been a bar to his pleasures; he pursued and obtained her.", "만약 그녀가 그 정조를 지니고 있었다면, 그것은 그의 쾌락을 가로막는 장애가 되었을 테니 말이다. 그는 그녀를 쫓아다녔고, 끝내 손에 넣었다."]], [["The reader will be mistaken, if he thinks Molly gave Square the preference to her younger lover: on the contrary, had she been confined to the choice of one only, Tom Jones would undoubtedly have been, of the two, the victorious person. Nor was it solely the consideration that two are better than one (though this had its proper weight) to which Mr Square owed his success: the absence of Jones during his confinement was an unlucky circumstance; and in that interval some well-chosen presents from the philosopher so softened and unguarded the girl's heart, that a favourable opportunity became irresistible, and Square triumphed over the poor remains of virtue which subsisted in the bosom of Molly.", "만약 몰리가 자기보다 어린 연인보다 스퀘어를 더 좋아했다고 독자가 여긴다면, 그것은 오해다. 오히려 그녀가 단 한 사람만을 골라야 하는 처지였다면, 그 둘 가운데 의심할 바 없이 톰 존스가 승자가 되었을 것이다. 또한 스퀘어가 성공을 거둔 까닭이 오로지 하나보다 둘이 낫다는 셈속(물론 이것도 나름의 무게를 지니기는 했다) 때문만도 아니었다. 존스가 몸져누워 있는 동안 자리를 비웠던 것이 불운한 사정이었고, 그 틈에 철학자가 골라 보낸 몇 가지 선물이 처녀의 마음을 어찌나 누그러뜨리고 무방비로 만들어 놓았던지, 절호의 기회가 거역할 수 없는 것이 되어 버렸다. 그리하여 스퀘어는 몰리의 가슴속에 남아 있던 정조의 가련한 잔재마저 짓밟고 승리를 거두었다."]], [["It was now about a fortnight since this conquest, when Jones paid the above-mentioned visit to his mistress, at a time when she and Square were in bed together.", "이 정복이 있은 지 어느덧 두 주쯤 지났을 무렵, 존스가 앞서 말한 대로 자기 연인을 찾아갔던 것이니, 바로 그녀와 스퀘어가 한 침대에 누워 있던 때였다. 우리가 본 대로 어머니가 그녀를 없다고 둘러댄 진짜 이유가 바로 이것이었다."], ["This was the true reason why the mother denied her as we have seen; for as the old woman shared in the profits arising from the iniquity of her daughter, she encouraged and protected her in it to the utmost of her power; but such was the envy and hatred which the elder sister bore towards Molly, that, notwithstanding she had some part of the booty, she would willingly have parted with this to ruin her sister and spoil her trade.", "그 늙은 여인은 딸의 부정에서 생기는 이익을 나누어 가졌던 까닭에, 제 힘이 닿는 데까지 딸을 부추기고 또 감싸 주었던 것이다. 그러나 맏언니가 몰리에게 품은 시샘과 미움이 어찌나 컸던지, 자기도 그 노획물의 한몫을 차지하고 있었음에도, 동생을 망쳐 그 장사를 망쳐 놓을 수만 있다면 기꺼이 그 몫을 내던질 작정이었다."], ["Hence she had acquainted Jones with her being above-stairs in bed, in hopes that he might have caught her in Square's arms.", "그래서 그녀는 혹시 존스가 스퀘어의 품에 안긴 동생을 덮칠 수도 있으리라는 속셈으로, 동생이 위층에서 잠자리에 누워 있다고 그에게 일러 주었던 것이다."], ["This, however, Molly found means to prevent, as the door was fastened; which gave her an opportunity of conveying her lover behind that rug or blanket where he now was unhappily discovered.", "그러나 문이 잠겨 있던 덕에 몰리는 이를 막아 낼 방도를 찾았고, 그 틈을 이용해 자기 연인을 그 깔개 혹은 담요 뒤로 숨겨 두었던 것이다. 그가 이제 거기서 불행히도 발각된 바로 그 자리 말이다."]], [["Square no sooner made his appearance than Molly flung herself back in her bed, cried out she was undone, and abandoned herself to despair.", "스퀘어가 모습을 드러내기가 무섭게 몰리는 침대에 벌렁 뒤로 자빠지며 자기는 끝장났다고 부르짖고는, 절망에 온몸을 내맡겨 버렸다."], ["This poor girl, who was yet but a novice in her business, had not arrived to that perfection of assurance which helps off a town lady in any extremity; and either prompts her with an excuse, or else inspires her to brazen out the matter with her husband, who, from love of quiet, or out of fear of his reputation--and sometimes, perhaps, from fear of the gallant, who, like Mr Constant in the play, wears a sword--is glad to shut his eyes, and content to put his horns in his pocket.", "이 가엾은 처녀는 아직 자기 장사에서 풋내기에 지나지 않았던지라, 어떤 위기에서도 도시의 귀부인을 척척 빠져나가게 해 주는 그 완벽한 뻔뻔함의 경지에는 미처 이르지 못했던 것이다. 그 뻔뻔함이란, 변명을 일러 주든가, 아니면 남편 앞에서 끝까지 시치미를 떼고 버티도록 부추겨 주는 것이다."], ["Molly, on the contrary, was silenced by this evidence, and very fairly gave up a cause which she had hitherto maintained with so many tears, and with such solemn and vehement protestations of the purest love and constancy.", "그 남편이란 자는 평온을 사랑하는 마음에서, 혹은 제 평판이 두려워서 ― 그리고 더러는 어쩌면 연극 속의 콘스탄트 씨처럼 칼을 차고 있는 정부가 무서워서 ― 기꺼이 두 눈을 질끈 감고, 제 뿔을 호주머니에 넣고 다니는 데 만족하는 법이다. 그러나 이와는 달리 몰리는 이 증거 앞에서 입이 막혀 버렸고, 여태껏 그토록 많은 눈물과, 더없이 순수한 사랑과 한결같음에 대한 그토록 엄숙하고 격렬한 항변으로 지켜 오던 명분을 깨끗이 포기하고 말았다."]], [["As to the gentleman behind the arras, he was not in much less consternation.", "휘장 뒤의 그 신사로 말하자면, 그 또한 못지않게 큰 낭패에 빠져 있었다."], ["He stood for a while motionless, and seemed equally at a loss what to say, or whither to direct his eyes.", "그는 한동안 꼼짝도 못 하고 서서, 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 또 눈길을 어디로 돌려야 할지조차 똑같이 막막해하는 듯했다. 존스는 셋 가운데 아마 가장 놀랐을 터인데도, 가장 먼저 입을 열었다."], ["Jones, though perhaps the most astonished of the three, first found his tongue; and being immediately recovered from those uneasy sensations which Molly by her upbraidings had occasioned, he burst into a loud laughter, and then saluting Mr Square, advanced to take him by the hand, and to relieve him from his place of confinement.", "그리고 몰리의 나무람이 자아냈던 그 거북한 심사에서 곧바로 벗어나, 그는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 이어 스퀘어 씨에게 인사를 건네고는, 그의 손을 잡아 그 갇힌 자리에서 풀어 주려고 다가갔다."]], [["Square being now arrived in the middle of the room, in which part only he could stand upright, looked at Jones with a very grave countenance, and said to him, “Well, sir, I see you enjoy this mighty discovery, and, I dare swear, take great delight in the thoughts of exposing me; but if you will consider the matter fairly, you will find you are yourself only to blame.", "스퀘어는 이제 방 한가운데, 즉 똑바로 설 수 있는 그 부분에 이르러서는, 자못 근엄한 낯빛으로 존스를 바라보며 이렇게 말했다. \"이보게, 자네가 이 대단한 발견을 즐기고 있다는 걸 알겠네."], ["I am not guilty of corrupting innocence.", "그리고 단언컨대, 나를 폭로할 생각에 크게 흐뭇해하고 있겠지. 하지만 이 일을 공정하게 따져 본다면, 탓할 사람은 오로지 자네 자신뿐임을 알게 될 걸세."], ["I have done nothing for which that part of the world which judges of matters by the rule of right, will condemn me.", "나는 순결을 더럽힌 죄를 짓지 않았네. 옳고 그름의 잣대로 일을 판단하는 그런 부류의 사람들이 나를 단죄할 만한 짓은 하나도 하지 않았어."], ["Fitness is governed by the nature of things, and not by customs, forms, or municipal laws.", "적합함이란 사물의 본성에 의해 다스려지는 것이지, 관습이나 형식, 또는 시정의 법률에 의해 다스려지는 게 아닐세. 자연스럽지 않은 것이 아니라면, 실로 그 어떤 것도 부적합하지 않으니까.\""], ["Nothing is indeed unfit which is not unnatural.”--“Well reasoned, old boy,”", "\"참 그럴듯한 논리로군, 이 친구야.\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones; “but why dost thou think that I should desire to expose thee?", "\"한데 어째서 내가 자넬 폭로하고 싶어 하리라 여기는 건가? 정말이지, 난 평생 자네가 지금처럼 마음에 든 적이 없었네."], ["I promise thee, I was never better pleased with thee in my life; and unless thou hast a mind to discover it thyself, this affair may remain a profound secret for me.”--“Nay, Mr Jones,”", "그러니 자네 스스로 이 일을 드러낼 작정이 아니라면, 이 일은 내게는 깊은 비밀로 남아 있을 걸세.\" \"아닐세, 존스 군.\""], ["replied Square, “I would not be thought to undervalue reputation.", "스퀘어가 받아 말했다. \"내가 평판을 하찮게 여긴다고는 생각지 말게."], ["Good fame is a species of the Kalon, and it is by no means fitting to neglect it.", "좋은 평판이란 '칼론'의 한 종류이니, 그것을 소홀히 하는 것은 결코 적합한 일이 아니지. 게다가 제 평판을 죽이는 것은 일종의 자살이며, 가증스럽고 혐오스러운 악덕일세."], ["Besides, to murder one's own reputation is a kind of suicide, a detestable and odious vice.", "그러니 만약 자네가 나의 어떤 약점을(나도 그런 게 있을 수 있지, 완전무결한 사람은 없으니 말일세) 기꺼이 감춰 줄 마음이 있다면, 나도 내 입으로 나를 들춰내지 않겠다고 약속하겠네. 행하기에는 적합하나 자랑하기에는 적합지 않은 일들이 있는 법이니까."], ["If you think proper, therefore, to conceal any infirmity of mine (for such I may have, since no man is perfectly perfect), I promise you I will not betray myself.", "세상의 비뚤어진 판단으로 말미암아, 사실은 죄가 없을 뿐 아니라 칭찬받아 마땅한 일이 흔히 비난의 대상이 되곤 하니 말일세.\" \"옳거니!\""], ["Things may be fitting to be done, which are not fitting to be boasted of; for by the perverse judgment of the world, that often becomes the subject of censure, which is, in truth, not only innocent but laudable.”--“Right!”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"자연스러운 식욕을 채우는 것보다 더 죄 없는 일이 어디 있겠나?"], ["cries Jones: “what can be more innocent than the indulgence of a natural appetite?", "또 우리 종족을 퍼뜨리는 것보다 더 칭찬받을 만한 일이 어디 있겠어?\" \"진지하게 말하자면,\""], ["or what more laudable than the propagation of our species?”--“To be serious with you,”", "스퀘어가 대답했다, \"내게는 그것이 늘 그렇게 보였다고 고백하네.\" \"한데도,\""], ["answered Square, “I profess they always appeared so to me.”--“And yet,”", "존스가 말했다, \"이 처녀와의 내 일이 처음 드러났을 때는 자네 생각이 달랐잖나.\" \"글쎄, 인정하지 않을 수 없군.\""], ["said Jones, “you was of a different opinion when my affair with this girl was first discovered.”--“Why, I must confess,”", "스퀘어가 말했다. \"그 일이 저 스왜컴 목사 녀석을 통해 내게 잘못 전해진 탓에, 나는 순결을 더럽힌 짓을 단죄했을지도 모르네."], ["says Square, “as the matter was misrepresented to me, by that parson Thwackum, I might condemn the corruption of innocence: it was that, sir, it was that--and that--: for you must know, Mr Jones, in the consideration of fitness, very minute circumstances, sir, very minute circumstances cause great alteration.”--“Well,”", "그게, 이보게, 바로 그것 때문이었어 ― 그리고 또 그것 ―. 알다시피, 존스 군, 적합함을 헤아릴 때에는 아주 사소한 정황, 이보게, 아주 사소한 정황이 큰 차이를 빚어내는 법이거든.\""], ["cries Jones, “be that as it will, it shall be your own fault, as I have promised you, if you ever hear any more of this adventure.", "\"좋아.\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["Behave kindly to the girl, and I will never open my lips concerning the matter to any one.", "\"그건 그렇다 치고, 내가 약속한 대로, 자네가 이 사건에 대해 다시 무슨 말을 듣게 된다면 그건 오로지 자네 자신의 탓일 걸세. 이 처녀에게 다정하게 대해 주게."], ["And, Molly, do you be faithful to your friend, and I will not only forgive your infidelity to me, but will do you all the service I can.”", "그러면 나는 이 일에 관해 누구에게도 결코 입을 열지 않겠네. 그리고 몰리, 너는 네 벗에게 충실해 다오."], ["So saying, he took a hasty leave, and, slipping down the ladder, retired with much expedition.", "그러면 나는 네가 내게 보인 부정을 용서할 뿐 아니라, 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 도움을 다 베풀어 주마.\" 이렇게 말하고서 그는 서둘러 작별을 고하고는, 사다리를 미끄러져 내려가 부리나케 물러갔다."]], [["Square was rejoiced to find this adventure was likely to have no worse conclusion; and as for Molly, being recovered from her confusion, she began at first to upbraid Square with having been the occasion of her loss of Jones; but that gentleman soon found the means of mitigating her anger, partly by caresses, and partly by a small nostrum from his purse, of wonderful and approved efficacy in purging off the ill humours of the mind, and in restoring it to a good temper.", "스퀘어는 이 사건이 더 나쁜 결말로 치닫지는 않을 듯하자 무척 기뻐했다. 몰리로 말하자면, 혼란에서 벗어나자 처음에는 자기가 존스를 잃은 빌미가 되었다고 스퀘어를 나무라기 시작했다. 그러나 그 신사는 곧 그녀의 노여움을 누그러뜨릴 방도를 찾아냈으니, 한편으로는 어루만짐으로, 또 한편으로는 제 지갑에서 꺼낸 작은 묘약으로였다. 마음의 나쁜 기운을 씻어 내고 그것을 다시 좋은 기분으로 되돌려 놓는 데 놀랍도록 효험이 입증된 묘약 말이다."]], [["She then poured forth a vast profusion of tenderness towards her new lover; turned all she had said to Jones, and Jones himself, into ridicule; and vowed, though he once had the possession of her person, that none but Square had ever been master of her heart.", "그러자 그녀는 새 연인을 향해 엄청난 애정을 한껏 쏟아부었다. 자기가 존스에게 했던 말이며 존스라는 사람 자체를 죄다 비웃음거리로 돌려 버렸고, 비록 한때 그가 자기 몸을 차지한 적은 있을지언정, 자기 마음의 주인이 되었던 사람은 오직 스퀘어뿐이라고 맹세했다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["By comparing which with the former, the reader may possibly correct some abuse which he hath formerly been guilty of in the application of the word love.", "앞 장과 견주어 보면, 독자는 자신이 전에 '사랑'이라는 말을 잘못 적용해 저질렀을지도 모를 어떤 오용을 바로잡게 될지도 모른다."]], [["The infidelity of Molly, which Jones had now discovered, would, perhaps, have vindicated a much greater degree of resentment than he expressed on the occasion; and if he had abandoned her directly from that moment, very few, I believe, would have blamed him.", "이제 존스가 알아챈 몰리의 부정(不貞)은, 그가 그 자리에서 드러낸 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 분노를 정당화할 만한 것이었으리라. 그가 바로 그 순간 그녀를 곧장 내쳤다 한들, 그를 탓할 사람은 거의 없었으리라 나는 믿는다."]], [["Certain, however, it is, that he saw her in the light of compassion; and though his love to her was not of that kind which could give him any great uneasiness at her inconstancy, yet was he not a little shocked on reflecting that he had himself originally corrupted her innocence; for to this corruption he imputed all the vice into which she appeared now so likely to plunge herself.", "그러나 분명한 것은, 그가 그녀를 연민의 눈으로 바라보았다는 사실이다. 그녀를 향한 그의 사랑은 그녀의 변심에 크게 괴로워할 만한 종류의 것이 아니었지만, 그래도 그는 애초에 자신이 그녀의 순결을 더럽혔다는 데 생각이 미치자 적잖이 충격을 받았다. 이제 그녀가 그토록 빠져들 것만 같은 온갖 악덕을, 그는 모두 이 타락의 탓으로 돌렸던 것이다."]], [["This consideration gave him no little uneasiness, till Betty, the elder sister, was so kind, some time afterwards, entirely to cure him by a hint, that one Will Barnes, and not himself, had been the first seducer of Molly; and that the little child, which he had hitherto so certainly concluded to be his own, might very probably have an equal title, at least, to claim Barnes for its father.", "이 생각은 그에게 적잖은 괴로움을 안겨 주었으나, 얼마 후 언니인 베티가 친절하게도 한 가지 귀띔으로 그를 말끔히 낫게 해 주었다. 몰리를 처음 유혹한 자는 그 자신이 아니라 윌 반스라는 사내였으며, 지금껏 그가 그토록 확신하며 제 자식이라 단정해 온 그 어린아이도 적어도 반스를 아비로 주장할 동등한 권리쯤은 충분히 가졌을 법하다는 것이었다."]], [["Jones eagerly pursued this scent when he had first received it; and in a very short time was sufficiently assured that the girl had told him truth, not only by the confession of the fellow, but at last by that of Molly herself.", "존스는 그 단서를 처음 받아 들자마자 열심히 그 자취를 좇았다. 그리고 머지않아 그 아가씨가 진실을 말했음을 충분히 확신하게 되었는데, 그 사내의 자백뿐 아니라 마침내 몰리 자신의 입을 통해서도 그러했다."]], [["This Will Barnes was a country gallant, and had acquired as many trophies of this kind as any ensign or attorney's clerk in the kingdom.", "이 윌 반스는 시골의 바람둥이로, 이런 종류의 전리품이라면 왕국의 어느 소위나 변호사 서기 못지않게 많이 거둬들인 자였다."], ["He had, indeed, reduced several women to a state of utter profligacy, had broke the hearts of some, and had the honour of occasioning the violent death of one poor girl, who had either drowned herself, or, what was rather more probable, had been drowned by him.", "실제로 그는 여러 여인을 완전한 타락의 구렁으로 몰아넣었고, 몇몇의 가슴을 찢어 놓았으며, 한 가엾은 처녀의 비참한 죽음을 불러일으키는 '영예'까지 누렸다. 그 처녀는 스스로 물에 빠져 죽었거나, 그보다 더 그럴듯하게는 그가 빠뜨려 죽인 것이었다."]], [["Among other of his conquests, this fellow had triumphed over the heart of Betty Seagrim.", "이 사내는 다른 여러 정복담 가운데서도 베티 시그림의 마음을 손에 넣어 의기양양했었다."], ["He had made love to her long before Molly was grown to be a fit object of that pastime; but had afterwards deserted her, and applied to her sister, with whom he had almost immediate success.", "그는 몰리가 그런 놀이의 알맞은 상대가 되기 한참 전부터 그녀에게 구애했으나, 그 뒤에 그녀를 버리고 동생에게 들러붙었으며, 거기서는 거의 단번에 성공을 거두었다."], ["Now Will had, in reality, the sole possession of Molly's affection, while Jones and Square were almost equally sacrifices to her interest and to her pride.", "사실 윌이야말로 몰리의 애정을 독차지하고 있었고, 존스와 스퀘어는 그녀의 잇속과 허영에 거의 똑같이 바쳐진 제물에 지나지 않았다."]], [["Hence had grown that implacable hatred which we have before seen raging in the mind of Betty; though we did not think it necessary to assign this cause sooner, as envy itself alone was adequate to all the effects we have mentioned.", "우리가 앞서 베티의 마음속에서 들끓는 것을 본 그 누그러질 줄 모르는 증오는 바로 여기서 자라난 것이었다. 다만 우리는 그 까닭을 더 일찍 밝힐 필요까지는 없다고 여겼는데, 질투 하나만으로도 우리가 언급한 모든 결과를 빚어내기에 충분했기 때문이다."]], [["Jones was become perfectly easy by possession of this secret with regard to Molly; but as to Sophia, he was far from being in a state of tranquillity; nay, indeed, he was under the most violent perturbation; his heart was now, if I may use the metaphor, entirely evacuated, and Sophia took absolute possession of it. He loved her with an unbounded passion, and plainly saw the tender sentiments she had for him; yet could not this assurance lessen his despair of obtaining the consent of her father, nor the horrors which attended his pursuit of her by any base or treacherous method.", "존스는 몰리에 관한 한 이 비밀을 손에 쥐고 나서 마음이 완전히 편안해졌다. 그러나 소피아에 관해서라면 결코 평온한 상태가 아니었으니, 아니, 실로 그는 더없이 격렬한 동요에 휩싸여 있었다. 비유를 써도 좋다면, 이제 그의 마음은 완전히 비워졌고 소피아가 그곳을 송두리째 차지해 버렸다. 그는 끝 모를 열정으로 그녀를 사랑했고, 그녀가 자신에게 품은 다정한 감정을 또렷이 알아보았다. 그러나 이 확신도 그녀 아버지의 동의를 얻으리라는 절망을 덜어 주지는 못했고, 어떤 비열하거나 배신적인 방법으로 그녀를 좇는 데 따르는 공포 또한 가시게 해 주지 못했다."]], [["The injury which he must thus do to Mr Western, and the concern which would accrue to Mr Allworthy, were circumstances that tormented him all day, and haunted him on his pillow at night.", "그리하여 그가 웨스턴 씨에게 끼치게 될 해악과, 올워디 씨에게 미칠 근심은 온종일 그를 괴롭히고 밤이면 베갯머리에서 그를 따라다니며 시달리게 하는 사정들이었다."], ["His life was a constant struggle between honour and inclination, which alternately triumphed over each other in his mind.", "그의 삶은 명예와 욕망 사이의 끊임없는 다툼이었고, 둘은 그의 마음속에서 번갈아 서로를 이겼다."], ["He often resolved, in the absence of Sophia, to leave her father's house, and to see her no more; and as often, in her presence, forgot all those resolutions, and determined to pursue her at the hazard of his life, and at the forfeiture of what was much dearer to him.", "소피아가 곁에 없을 때면 그는 종종 그녀 아버지의 집을 떠나 다시는 그녀를 보지 않으리라 결심했고, 그녀가 곁에 있을 때면 그 모든 결심을 잊은 채 목숨을 걸고, 또 그에게 목숨보다 훨씬 더 소중한 것마저 잃을 각오로 그녀를 좇으리라 마음먹기 일쑤였다."]], [["This conflict began soon to produce very strong and visible effects: for he lost all his usual sprightliness and gaiety of temper, and became not only melancholy when alone, but dejected and absent in company; nay, if ever he put on a forced mirth, to comply with Mr Western's humour, the constraint appeared so plain, that he seemed to have been giving the strongest evidence of what he endeavoured to conceal by such ostentation.", "이 갈등은 곧 매우 강하고 또렷한 결과를 빚어내기 시작했다. 그는 평소의 활기와 쾌활한 기질을 모두 잃었고, 혼자 있을 때 우울할 뿐 아니라 사람들 틈에서도 풀이 죽고 넋을 놓았다. 아니, 웨스턴 씨의 비위를 맞추려 억지 흥겨움을 꾸며 낼 때조차 그 부자연스러움이 너무도 빤히 드러나, 그렇게 과시하면서 감추려 애쓰던 바로 그것을 도리어 가장 확실하게 증명해 보이는 듯했다."]], [["It may, perhaps, be a question, whether the art which he used to conceal his passion, or the means which honest nature employed to reveal it, betrayed him most: for while art made him more than ever reserved to Sophia, and forbad him to address any of his discourse to her, nay, to avoid meeting her eyes, with the utmost caution; nature was no less busy in counterplotting him.", "어쩌면 이런 의문이 들지도 모른다. 그가 자신의 열정을 감추려 쓴 기교와, 정직한 자연이 그것을 드러내려 동원한 수단 가운데 무엇이 그를 더 많이 배신했을까?"], ["Hence, at the approach of the young lady, he grew pale; and if this was sudden, started.", "기교가 그로 하여금 어느 때보다도 소피아에게 더 조심스럽게 굴게 하고, 그녀에게 어떤 말도 건네지 못하게 막았으며, 아니, 그녀의 눈길과 마주치는 것조차 더없이 신중하게 피하도록 했지만, 그러는 동안 자연도 못지않게 분주히 그를 거슬러 일을 꾸몄다."], ["If his eyes accidentally met hers, the blood rushed into his cheeks, and his countenance became all over scarlet.", "그리하여 그 아가씨가 다가올 때면 그는 창백해졌고, 그것이 갑작스러우면 흠칫 놀랐다. 그의 눈이 우연히 그녀의 눈과 마주치면 피가 두 뺨으로 솟구쳐 얼굴이 온통 새빨개졌다."], ["If common civility ever obliged him to speak to her, as to drink her health at table, his tongue was sure to falter.", "흔한 예의상 그녀에게 말을 건네야 할 때면, 이를테면 식탁에서 그녀의 건강을 비는 잔을 들 때면 그의 혀는 어김없이 떨렸다."], ["If he touched her, his hand, nay his whole frame, trembled.", "그녀에게 손이 닿으면 그의 손이, 아니 온몸이 떨렸다."], ["And if any discourse tended, however remotely, to raise the idea of love, an involuntary sigh seldom failed to steal from his bosom.", "그리고 어떤 이야기가 아무리 멀게나마 사랑의 관념을 불러일으키는 쪽으로 흐르면, 저도 모르게 새어 나오는 한숨이 그의 가슴에서 빠져나오지 않는 일이 드물었다."], ["Most of which accidents nature was wonderfully industrious to throw daily in his way.", "그리고 이런 일들의 대부분을 자연은 날마다 그의 앞길에 던져 놓으려 놀랍도록 부지런히 애를 썼다."]], [["All these symptoms escaped the notice of the squire: but not so of Sophia.", "이 모든 징후가 지주의 눈은 피해 갔으나, 소피아의 눈은 그러지 못했다."], ["She soon perceived these agitations of mind in Jones, and was at no loss to discover the cause; for indeed she recognized it in her own breast.", "그녀는 곧 존스의 이런 심란함을 알아챘고, 그 까닭을 찾아내는 데 조금도 어려움이 없었다. 실로 그녀는 자기 가슴속에서 바로 그것을 알아보았던 것이다."], ["And this recognition is, I suppose, that sympathy which hath been so often noted in lovers, and which will sufficiently account for her being so much quicker-sighted than her father.", "그리고 이 알아봄이야말로, 내 생각으로는, 연인들에게서 그토록 자주 언급되어 온 그 공감이며, 그녀가 아버지보다 훨씬 빨리 눈치챈 까닭을 충분히 설명해 줄 것이다."]], [["But, to say the truth, there is a more simple and plain method of accounting for that prodigious superiority of penetration which we must observe in some men over the rest of the human species, and one which will serve not only in the case of lovers, but of all others.", "그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 어떤 사람들이 나머지 인류보다 월등히 뛰어난 통찰력을 지닌 것을 우리가 보게 될 때, 그것을 설명하는 더 단순하고 명백한 방법이 하나 있으니, 이는 연인의 경우뿐 아니라 다른 모든 경우에도 들어맞는다."], ["From whence is it that the knave is generally so quick-sighted to those symptoms and operations of knavery, which often dupe an honest man of a much better understanding?", "어찌하여 악당은 자신보다 훨씬 더 분별 있는 정직한 사람조차 곧잘 속아 넘어가는 그 악행의 징후와 작용을, 대개 그토록 재빠르게 알아보는 것일까?"], ["There surely is no general sympathy among knaves; nor have they, like freemasons, any common sign of communication.", "악당들 사이에 무슨 보편적 공감 같은 것이 있을 리 없고, 프리메이슨처럼 서로 통하는 공통의 신호가 있는 것도 아니다."], ["In reality, it is only because they have the same thing in their heads, and their thoughts are turned the same way.", "실은 다만 그들의 머릿속에 같은 것이 들어 있고, 그들의 생각이 같은 방향으로 향해 있기 때문일 따름이다."], ["Thus, that Sophia saw, and that Western did not see, the plain symptoms of love in Jones can be no wonder, when we consider that the idea of love never entered into the head of the father, whereas the daughter, at present, thought of nothing else.", "그러니 소피아는 보았고 웨스턴은 보지 못했던 존스의 빤한 사랑의 징후도, 그 아비의 머릿속에는 사랑의 관념이라곤 들어선 적이 없었던 반면 그 딸은 지금 다른 것은 아무것도 생각하지 않았다는 점을 헤아려 보면, 조금도 이상할 게 없다."]], [["When Sophia was well satisfied of the violent passion which tormented poor Jones, and no less certain that she herself was its object, she had not the least difficulty in discovering the true cause of his present behaviour.", "가엾은 존스를 괴롭히는 격렬한 열정을 소피아가 충분히 납득하고, 또 그 대상이 다름 아닌 자기 자신임을 못지않게 확신하게 되자, 그녀는 지금 그의 행동의 참된 까닭을 알아내는 데 조금도 어려움이 없었다."], ["This highly endeared him to her, and raised in her mind two of the best affections which any lover can wish to raise in a mistress--these were, esteem and pity--for sure the most outrageously rigid among her sex will excuse her pitying a man whom she saw miserable on her own account; nor can they blame her for esteeming one who visibly, from the most honourable motives, endeavoured to smother a flame in his own bosom, which, like the famous Spartan theft, was preying upon and consuming his very vitals.", "이로 인해 그는 그녀에게 더없이 사랑스러운 존재가 되었고, 어떤 연인이 연인의 마음속에 불러일으키기를 바랄 수 있는 가장 좋은 두 가지 감정을 그녀의 마음에 일으켰으니, 바로 존경과 연민이었다. 자기 때문에 비참해진 한 남자를 가엾이 여겼다 하여, 그녀의 성(性) 가운데 아무리 모질게 엄격한 이라도 그녀를 용서하지 않을 수 없을 것이다."], ["Thus his backwardness, his shunning her, his coldness, and his silence, were the forwardest, the most diligent, the warmest, and most eloquent advocates; and wrought so violently on her sensible and tender heart, that she soon felt for him all those gentle sensations which are consistent with a virtuous and elevated female mind.", "또한 더없이 명예로운 동기에서, 자신의 가슴속에서 타오르는 불꽃—저 유명한 스파르타 소년의 도둑질처럼 바로 그의 오장육부를 파먹고 좀먹어 들어가던 그 불꽃—을 눈에 띄게 억누르려 애쓰는 남자를 존경했다 하여 그녀를 탓할 수도 없으리라. 이리하여 그의 머뭇거림, 그녀를 피하는 것, 그의 냉담함, 그의 침묵이야말로 가장 적극적이고 가장 부지런하며 가장 따뜻하고 가장 웅변적인 변호인이 되어, 예민하고 다정한 그녀의 마음에 어찌나 격렬히 작용했던지, 그녀는 곧 정숙하고 고결한 여인의 마음에 어울리는 그 모든 부드러운 정을 그에게 느끼게 되었다."], ["In short, all which esteem, gratitude, and pity, can inspire in such towards an agreeable man--indeed, all which the nicest delicacy can allow.", "요컨대, 존경과 감사와 연민이 그런 마음으로 하여금 호감 가는 남자를 향해 불러일으킬 수 있는 모든 것, 실로 더없이 섬세한 정결함이 허락할 수 있는 모든 것이었다."], ["In a word, she was in love with him to distraction.", "한마디로, 그녀는 정신을 잃을 만큼 그를 사랑하고 있었다."]], [["One day this young couple accidentally met in the garden, at the end of the two walks which were both bounded by that canal in which Jones had formerly risqued drowning to retrieve the little bird that Sophia had there lost.", "어느 날 이 젊은 두 사람은 정원에서 우연히 마주쳤다. 두 산책로의 끝자락이었는데, 그 두 길은 모두 저 운하에 닿아 있었으니, 바로 존스가 전에 소피아가 잃어버린 작은 새를 되찾으려 익사할 위험까지 무릅썼던 그 운하였다."]], [["This place had been of late much frequented by Sophia.", "이곳은 근래 소피아가 자주 찾는 곳이 되어 있었다."], ["Here she used to ruminate, with a mixture of pain and pleasure, on an incident which, however trifling in itself, had possibly sown the first seeds of that affection which was now arrived to such maturity in her heart.", "그녀는 여기서, 그 자체로는 아무리 사소할지언정 어쩌면 지금 그녀의 마음속에서 이토록 무르익은 그 애정의 첫 씨앗을 뿌렸을지도 모를 한 사건을, 괴로움과 즐거움이 뒤섞인 심정으로 곱씹곤 했다."]], [["Here then this young couple met.", "그리하여 이곳에서 이 젊은 두 사람이 마주쳤다."], ["They were almost close together before either of them knew anything of the other's approach.", "둘은 서로 다가오는 것을 알아채기도 전에 거의 코앞에 다다라 있었다."], ["A bystander would have discovered sufficient marks of confusion in the countenance of each; but they felt too much themselves to make any observation.", "곁에서 지켜보는 이라면 둘의 얼굴에서 당혹의 빛을 넉넉히 알아챘을 테지만, 정작 두 사람은 자기 감정에 너무 휩싸여 무엇을 살펴볼 겨를이 없었다."], ["As soon as Jones had a little recovered his first surprize, he accosted the young lady with some of the ordinary forms of salutation, which she in the same manner returned; and their conversation began, as usual, on the delicious beauty of the morning.", "존스는 처음의 놀람에서 조금 정신을 차리자마자 흔한 인사말 몇 마디로 그 아가씨에게 말을 건넸고, 그녀도 같은 식으로 답했다. 그리고 둘의 대화는 늘 그렇듯 아침의 황홀한 아름다움에서 시작되었다."], ["Hence they past to the beauty of the place, on which Jones launched forth very high encomiums.", "거기서 그들은 이 장소의 아름다움으로 옮겨 갔는데, 존스는 거기에 더없이 드높은 찬사를 늘어놓았다."], ["When they came to the tree whence he had formerly tumbled into the canal, Sophia could not help reminding him of that accident, and said, “I fancy, Mr Jones, you have some little shuddering when you see that water.”--“I assure you, madam,”", "그가 전에 운하로 곤두박질쳤던 그 나무에 이르자, 소피아는 그 사고를 떠올려 주지 않을 수 없어 이렇게 말했다. \"존스 씨, 저 물을 보시면 조금은 몸서리가 쳐지지 않을까 싶네요.\"—\"맹세컨대, 아가씨,\""], ["answered Jones, “the concern you felt at the loss of your little bird will always appear to me the highest circumstance in that adventure.", "존스가 답했다. \"그 모험에서 제게 가장 큰 일은 언제까지나, 작은 새를 잃고 아가씨께서 느끼신 그 안타까움일 겁니다."], ["Poor little Tommy!", "가엾은 토미!"], ["there is the branch he stood upon.", "저기 저 가지가 그 녀석이 앉아 있던 곳이지요."], ["How could the little wretch have the folly to fly away from that state of happiness in which I had the honour to place him?", "그 작은 가엾은 것이 어쩌자고 제가 영광스럽게 마련해 준 그 행복한 처지에서 날아가 버리는 어리석음을 저질렀을까요?"], ["His fate was a just punishment for his ingratitude.”--“Upon my word, Mr Jones,”", "그 운명은 녀석의 배은망덕에 대한 마땅한 벌이었습니다.\"—\"정말이지, 존스 씨,\""], ["said she, “your gallantry very narrowly escaped as severe a fate.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신의 그 용맹도 똑같이 가혹한 운명을 아슬아슬하게 면했지요."], ["Sure the remembrance must affect you.”--“Indeed, madam,”", "그 일을 떠올리면 분명 마음이 흔들리실 텐데요.\"—\"실로, 아가씨,\" 그가 답했다."], ["answered he, “if I have any reason to reflect with sorrow on it, it is, perhaps, that the water had not been a little deeper, by which I might have escaped many bitter heart-aches that Fortune seems to have in store for me.”--“Fie, Mr Jones!”", "\"제가 그 일을 슬픔으로 돌이켜 볼 까닭이 있다면, 그것은 어쩌면 그 물이 조금만 더 깊지 않았다는 점일 겁니다. 그랬다면 운명이 제게 마련해 둔 듯한 그 숱한 쓰라린 마음앓이를 면했을 테니까요.\"—\"어머, 존스 씨!\""], ["replied Sophia; “I am sure you cannot be in earnest now.", "소피아가 받아쳤다. \"지금 진심이실 리 없어요."], ["This affected contempt of life is only an excess of your complacence to me.", "이렇게 삶을 짐짓 가벼이 여기시는 건 그저 제게 베푸시는 지나친 호의일 뿐이에요."], ["You would endeavour to lessen the obligation of having twice ventured it for my sake.", "저를 위해 두 번이나 목숨을 거셨다는 그 빚의 무게를 덜어 보려 하시는 거지요."], ["Beware the third time.”", "세 번째는 조심하세요.\""], ["She spoke these last words with a smile, and a softness inexpressible.", "그녀는 이 마지막 말을, 형언할 수 없는 미소와 부드러움을 담아 했다."], ["Jones answered with a sigh, “He feared it was already too late for caution:”", "존스는 한숨으로 답했다. \"조심하기엔 이미 너무 늦은 것 같아 두렵습니다.\""], ["and then looking tenderly and stedfastly on her, he cried, “Oh, Miss Western!", "그러고는 그녀를 다정하고도 한결같이 바라보며 외쳤다. \"오, 웨스턴 아가씨!"], ["can you desire me to live?", "제게 살라고 하실 수 있습니까?"], ["Can you wish me so ill?”", "제게 그토록 모진 것을 바라실 수 있습니까?\""], ["Sophia, looking down on the ground, answered with some hesitation, “Indeed, Mr Jones, I do not wish you ill.”--“Oh, I know too well that heavenly temper,”", "소피아는 땅을 내려다보며 다소 머뭇거리는 목소리로 답했다. \"정말이지, 존스 씨, 저는 당신께 모진 것을 바라지 않아요.\"—\"오, 저는 그 천국 같은 마음씨를 너무도 잘 압니다,\""], ["cries Jones, “that divine goodness, which is beyond every other charm.”--“Nay, now,”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"다른 어떤 매력보다도 뛰어난 그 거룩한 선함을요.\"—\"아니에요, 이제,\""], ["answered she, “I understand you not.", "그녀가 답했다. \"저는 당신을 못 알아듣겠어요."], ["I can stay no longer.”--“I--I would not be understood!”", "더는 머물 수가 없네요.\"—\"저는… 저는 알아들으시지 않기를 바랐습니다!\""], ["cries he; “nay, I can't be understood.", "그가 외쳤다. \"아니, 알아들으실 수가 없지요."], ["I know not what I say.", "제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모르겠습니다."], ["Meeting you here so unexpectedly, I have been unguarded: for Heaven's sake pardon me, if I have said anything to offend you.", "여기서 이토록 뜻밖에 아가씨를 뵙고는 그만 마음을 놓아 버렸습니다. 제발, 제가 아가씨를 거스를 말을 했다면 용서해 주십시오."], ["I did not mean it.", "그럴 뜻은 없었습니다."], ["Indeed, I would rather have died--nay, the very thought would kill me.”--“You surprize me,”", "실로 저는 차라리 죽는 편이 나았을 겁니다. 아니, 그런 생각만으로도 저는 죽을 것 같습니다.\"—\"놀랍군요,\""], ["answered she.", "그녀가 답했다."], ["“How can you possibly think you have offended me?”--“Fear, madam,”", "\"어떻게 당신이 저를 거슬렀다고 생각하실 수 있나요?\"—\"두려움이란, 아가씨,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “easily runs into madness; and there is no degree of fear like that which I feel of offending you.", "\"쉬이 광기로 치닫는 법이지요. 그리고 아가씨를 거스를까 하는 두려움만 한 두려움은 없습니다."], ["How can I speak then?", "그러니 제가 어찌 말을 할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Nay, don't look angrily at me: one frown will destroy me.", "아니, 노여운 눈으로 저를 보지 말아 주십시오. 한 번의 찡그림이 저를 무너뜨릴 겁니다."], ["I mean nothing.", "저는 아무 뜻도 없습니다."], ["Blame my eyes, or blame those beauties.", "제 눈을 탓하시든지, 아니면 저 아름다움을 탓하십시오."], ["What am I saying?", "제가 무슨 말을 하는 걸까요?"], ["Pardon me if I have said too much.", "너무 많은 말을 했다면 용서해 주십시오."], ["My heart overflowed.", "제 마음이 넘쳐흘렀습니다."], ["I have struggled with my love to the utmost, and have endeavoured to conceal a fever which preys on my vitals, and will, I hope, soon make it impossible for me ever to offend you more.”", "저는 제 사랑과 끝까지 싸워 왔고, 제 오장육부를 파먹는 이 열병을 감추려 애써 왔습니다. 바라건대 그 열병이 머지않아, 제가 다시는 아가씨를 거스를 수 없게 만들어 줄 것입니다.\""]], [["Mr Jones now fell a trembling as if he had been shaken with the fit of an ague.", "이제 존스는 학질 발작에 흔들리기라도 하듯 부들부들 떨기 시작했다. 그와 그리 다르지 않은 처지에 놓인 소피아는 이렇게 답했다."], ["Sophia, who was in a situation not very different from his, answered in these words: “Mr Jones, I will not affect to misunderstand you; indeed, I understand you too well; but, for Heaven's sake, if you have any affection for me, let me make the best of my way into the house.", "\"존스 씨, 저는 당신을 못 알아들은 척하지 않겠어요. 실은 너무도 잘 알아들었으니까요."], ["I wish I may be able to support myself thither.”", "하지만 제발, 제게 조금이라도 정이 있으시다면, 제가 무사히 집으로 들어가게 해 주세요. 거기까지 몸을 가눌 수 있으면 좋으련만.\""]], [["Jones, who was hardly able to support himself, offered her his arm, which she condescended to accept, but begged he would not mention a word more to her of this nature at present. He promised he would not; insisting only on her forgiveness of what love, without the leave of his will, had forced from him: this, she told him, he knew how to obtain by his future behaviour; and thus this young pair tottered and trembled along, the lover not once daring to squeeze the hand of his mistress, though it was locked in his.", "자기 몸조차 가누기 힘들던 존스는 그녀에게 팔을 내밀었고, 그녀는 황송하게도 그것을 받아들였다. 다만 지금은 이런 종류의 말을 한마디도 더 꺼내지 말아 달라고 청했다. 그는 그러겠노라 약속하며, 다만 자기 의지의 허락도 없이 사랑이 억지로 끌어낸 그 말을 용서해 달라고만 거듭 졸랐다. 그녀는 앞으로의 처신으로 그 용서를 얻는 법을 그가 알고 있을 거라고 말했다. 그리하여 이 젊은 한 쌍은 비틀거리고 떨면서 걸어갔는데, 연인은 자기 손에 그녀의 손이 맞물려 있었는데도 단 한 번도 감히 그 손을 꼭 쥐어 보지 못했다."]], [["Sophia immediately retired to her chamber, where Mrs Honour and the hartshorn were summoned to her assistance.", "소피아는 곧장 자기 방으로 물러갔고, 그곳으로 어너 부인과 사슴뿔 정기(精氣)가 그녀를 돕도록 불려 왔다."], ["As to poor Jones, the only relief to his distempered mind was an unwelcome piece of news, which, as it opens a scene of different nature from those in which the reader hath lately been conversant, will be communicated to him in the next chapter.", "가엾은 존스로 말하자면, 병든 그의 마음에 유일한 위안이 된 것은 달갑지 않은 한 가지 소식이었다. 이 소식은 독자가 근래 접해 온 것과는 사뭇 다른 성격의 장면을 여는 것이므로, 다음 장에서 그에게 전하기로 하겠다."]], [["In which Mr Allworthy appears on a sick-bed.", "올워디 씨가 병상에 누운 이야기."]], [["Mr Western was become so fond of Jones that he was unwilling to part with him, though his arm had been long since cured; and Jones, either from the love of sport, or from some other reason, was easily persuaded to continue at his house, which he did sometimes for a fortnight together without paying a single visit at Mr Allworthy's; nay, without ever hearing from thence.", "웨스턴 씨는 어느새 존스를 무척 좋아하게 되어, 그의 팔이 다 나은 지 오래였는데도 그를 떠나보내려 하지 않았다. 존스 또한 사냥을 좋아해서였는지 아니면 다른 어떤 이유에서였는지, 그의 집에 계속 머물러 달라는 청에 쉽게 넘어갔다. 그래서 가끔은 보름씩 내리 그 집에 머무르며 올워디 씨 댁에 한 번도 들르지 않았고, 더구나 그곳 소식조차 듣지 못한 채 지내곤 했다."]], [["Mr Allworthy had been for some days indisposed with a cold, which had been attended with a little fever. This he had, however, neglected; as it was usual with him to do all manner of disorders which did not confine him to his bed, or prevent his several faculties from performing their ordinary functions;--a conduct which we would by no means be thought to approve or recommend to imitation; for surely the gentlemen of the Aesculapian art are in the right in advising, that the moment the disease has entered at one door, the physician should be introduced at the other: what else is meant by that old adage, Venienti occurrite morbo? “Oppose a distemper at its first approach.” Thus the doctor and the disease meet in fair and equal conflict; whereas, by giving time to the latter, we often suffer him to fortify and entrench himself, like a French army; so that the learned gentleman finds it very difficult, and sometimes impossible, to come at the enemy. Nay, sometimes by gaining time the disease applies to the French military politics, and corrupts nature over to his side, and then all the powers of physic must arrive too late. Agreeable to these observations was, I remember, the complaint of the great Doctor Misaubin, who used very pathetically to lament the late applications which were made to his skill, saying, “Bygar, me believe my pation take me for de undertaker, for dey never send for me till de physicion have kill dem.”", "올워디 씨는 며칠 전부터 감기로 몸이 편치 않았는데, 거기에 가벼운 열까지 따라붙었다. 그러나 그는 이것을 대수롭지 않게 여겼다. 자신을 침대에 눕게 만들거나 여러 기능을 평소처럼 발휘하지 못하게 가로막지 않는 종류의 병이라면 모두 그렇게 무심히 넘기는 것이 그의 버릇이었던 까닭이다. 우리는 이런 처신을 결코 옳다 여기거나 본받으라 권할 마음은 없다. 의술을 다루는 신사 분들이 권하는 말, 곧 병이 한쪽 문으로 들어오는 그 순간 의사를 다른 쪽 문으로 들여보내야 한다는 충고야말로 분명 지당한 것이기 때문이다. 저 오래된 격언, \"베니엔티 오쿠리테 모르보(Venienti occurrite morbo)\"가 달리 무엇을 뜻하겠는가? \"병이 처음 다가올 때 맞서 싸우라\"는 말이다. 그리하면 의사와 병은 공정하고 대등한 싸움에서 맞붙게 된다. 그러나 병에게 시간을 내어주면, 우리는 흔히 그것이 프랑스 군대처럼 스스로를 굳건히 다지고 참호를 파게 내버려두는 셈이 된다. 그래서 그 박식한 신사 분이 적에게 다가가기가 무척 어려워지고, 때로는 아예 불가능해진다. 아니, 때로는 병이 시간을 벌어 프랑스식 군사 책략을 끌어들이고, 자연마저 제 편으로 매수해 버린다. 그러면 의술의 온갖 힘이 도착해도 이미 너무 늦은 것이다. 이런 관찰과 잘 들어맞는 것이, 내가 기억하기로, 저 위대한 미소뱅 박사의 한탄이다. 그는 자신의 솜씨를 너무 늦게 찾는 일을 무척 애처롭게 탄식하며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"맙소사, 내 환자들은 나를 장의사로 아는 모양이야. 의사가 저들을 죽여 놓기 전에는 결코 나를 부르지 않으니 말이지.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy's distemper, by means of this neglect, gained such ground, that, when the increase of his fever obliged him to send for assistance, the doctor at his first arrival shook his head, wished he had been sent for sooner, and intimated that he thought him in very imminent danger.", "올워디 씨의 병은 이렇게 방치된 탓에 어찌나 깊어졌던지, 열이 더 심해져 마침내 도움을 청해야 했을 때, 의사는 도착하자마자 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 좀 더 일찍 불렀어야 했다고 아쉬워했고, 환자가 매우 위태로운 지경에 이른 것 같다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["Mr Allworthy, who had settled all his affairs in this world, and was as well prepared as it is possible for human nature to be for the other, received this information with the utmost calmness and unconcern.", "올워디 씨는 이 세상의 일을 모두 정리해 두었고, 인간의 본성이 다른 세상을 향해 준비할 수 있는 한 가장 잘 준비되어 있던 터라, 이 소식을 더없이 차분하고 태연하게 받아들였다."], ["He could, indeed, whenever he laid himself down to rest, say with Cato in the tragical poem--", "실로 그는 잠자리에 누울 때마다 저 비극 시 속의 카토처럼 이렇게 말할 수 있었다."]], [["Let guilt or fear Disturb man's rest: Cato knows neither of them; Indifferent in his choice to sleep or die.", "죄나 두려움일랑 사람의 안식을 어지럽히게 두라. 카토는 그 둘을 다 모른다. 잠들든 죽든, 그 선택에 마음이 흔들리지 않으니."]], [["In reality, he could say this with ten times more reason and confidence than Cato, or any other proud fellow among the antient or modern heroes; for he was not only devoid of fear, but might be considered as a faithful labourer, when at the end of harvest he is summoned to receive his reward at the hands of a bountiful master.", "사실 그는 카토보다, 아니 고금의 어떤 자긍심 높은 영웅들보다 열 배는 더 떳떳하고 확신에 차서 이렇게 말할 수 있었다. 그는 두려움이 없을 뿐 아니라, 추수가 끝날 무렵 후한 주인의 손에서 삯을 받으라 불려가는 충실한 일꾼에 견줄 만한 사람이었기 때문이다."]], [["The good man gave immediate orders for all his family to be summoned round him.", "이 선량한 분은 곧바로 온 가족을 자기 곁으로 불러 모으라 명했다."], ["None of these were then abroad, but Mrs Blifil, who had been some time in London, and Mr Jones, whom the reader hath just parted from at Mr Western's, and who received this summons just as Sophia had left him.", "그때 집을 떠나 있던 사람은 한동안 런던에 가 있던 블리필 부인과, 독자가 방금 웨스턴 씨 댁에서 작별한 존스 씨뿐이었는데, 존스는 소피아가 막 그의 곁을 떠난 바로 그 순간에 이 부름을 받았다."]], [["The news of Mr Allworthy's danger (for the servant told him he was dying) drove all thoughts of love out of his head.", "올워디 씨가 위독하다는 소식은(하인이 그에게 주인이 죽어 간다고 전했으므로) 사랑에 관한 온갖 생각을 그의 머릿속에서 몰아내 버렸다."], ["He hurried instantly into the chariot which was sent for him, and ordered the coachman to drive with all imaginable haste; nor did the idea of Sophia, I believe, once occur to him on the way.", "그는 자신을 데리러 온 마차에 곧장 뛰어올라 마부에게 상상할 수 있는 한 가장 빠르게 몰아 달라고 명했다. 그리고 가는 길에 소피아의 모습은, 내 생각으로는, 단 한 번도 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다."]], [["And now the whole family, namely, Mr Blifil, Mr Jones, Mr Thwackum, Mr Square, and some of the servants (for such were Mr Allworthy's orders) being all assembled round his bed, the good man sat up in it, and was beginning to speak, when Blifil fell to blubbering, and began to express very loud and bitter lamentations.", "이제 온 가족, 곧 블리필 씨, 존스 씨, 스웨컴 씨, 스퀘어 씨, 그리고 하인 몇몇이(올워디 씨가 그렇게 명했던 까닭이다) 모두 그의 침대 둘레에 모이자, 이 선량한 분은 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 막 말을 꺼내려 했다. 그때 블리필이 흐느끼기 시작하더니 아주 크고 비통한 탄식을 쏟아내기 시작했다."], ["Upon this Mr Allworthy shook him by the hand, and said, “Do not sorrow thus, my dear nephew, at the most ordinary of all human occurrences.", "그러자 올워디 씨는 그의 손을 잡아 흔들며 말했다. \"사랑하는 조카야, 인간에게 일어나는 일 가운데 가장 흔한 일을 두고 그리 슬퍼하지 마라."], ["When misfortunes befal our friends we are justly grieved; for those are accidents which might often have been avoided, and which may seem to render the lot of one man more peculiarly unhappy than that of others; but death is certainly unavoidable, and is that common lot in which alone the fortunes of all men agree: nor is the time when this happens to us very material.", "벗에게 불운이 닥칠 때 우리가 슬퍼하는 것은 마땅하다. 그런 것은 흔히 피할 수도 있었던 사고요, 한 사람의 처지를 다른 이들보다 유난히 더 불행하게 만드는 것처럼 보이는 일이니까."], ["If the wisest of men hath compared life to a span, surely we may be allowed to consider it as a day.", "그러나 죽음은 분명 피할 수 없는 것이며, 모든 사람의 운명이 오직 그 한 가지에서만은 똑같아지는, 그 공통된 몫이다. 또한 이 일이 우리에게 언제 닥치느냐는 그리 중요하지 않다."], ["It is my fate to leave it in the evening; but those who are taken away earlier have only lost a few hours, at the best little worth lamenting, and much oftener hours of labour and fatigue, of pain and sorrow.", "가장 지혜로운 사람이 인생을 한 뼘에 견주었다면, 우리야 그것을 하루에 견주어도 좋으리라. 저녁에 그 자리를 떠나는 것이 내 운명이다."], ["One of the Roman poets, I remember, likens our leaving life to our departure from a feast;--a thought which hath often occurred to me when I have seen men struggling to protract an entertainment, and to enjoy the company of their friends a few moments longer.", "그러나 더 일찍 불려 간 이들은 고작 몇 시간을 잃었을 뿐이니, 그 몇 시간이란 잘해야 애석해할 만한 가치도 별로 없고, 훨씬 더 흔하게는 노고와 피로, 고통과 슬픔의 시간일 따름이다. 내 기억으로는 로마의 한 시인이 우리가 삶을 떠나는 것을 잔치 자리에서 물러나는 것에 견주었더구나."], ["Alas! how short is the most protracted of such enjoyments!", "이것은 사람들이 즐거운 자리를 조금이라도 더 끌어 보려, 벗들과 함께하는 시간을 잠시라도 더 누려 보려 애쓰는 것을 볼 때마다 내게 자주 떠오르곤 하던 생각이었다."], ["how immaterial the difference between him who retires the soonest, and him who stays the latest!", "아아! 그런 즐거움이란 아무리 길게 끈다 한들 얼마나 짧은가!"], ["This is seeing life in the best view, and this unwillingness to quit our friends is the most amiable motive from which we can derive the fear of death; and yet the longest enjoyment which we can hope for of this kind is of so trivial a duration, that it is to a wise man truly contemptible.", "가장 먼저 자리를 뜨는 사람과 가장 늦게까지 남는 사람 사이의 차이가 얼마나 하찮은가! 이것이 삶을 가장 좋게 보는 길이며, 벗들과 헤어지기 싫어하는 이 마음이야말로 우리가 죽음에 대한 두려움을 길어 올릴 수 있는 가장 사랑스러운 동기다."], ["Few men, I own, think in this manner; for, indeed, few men think of death till they are in its jaws.", "그러나 우리가 이런 종류의 즐거움으로 바랄 수 있는 가장 긴 누림조차 그 지속이 너무나 보잘것없어서, 지혜로운 사람에게는 참으로 하찮게 여겨질 따름이다. 나도 인정하건대, 이렇게 생각하는 이는 드물다."], ["However gigantic and terrible an object this may appear when it approaches them, they are nevertheless incapable of seeing it at any distance; nay, though they have been ever so much alarmed and frightened when they have apprehended themselves in danger of dying, they are no sooner cleared from this apprehension than even the fears of it are erased from their minds.", "실로 사람들은 죽음의 아가리에 들어서기 전에는 죽음을 생각하지 않으니까. 죽음이 다가올 때 그 대상이 아무리 거대하고 무섭게 보일지라도, 사람들은 멀찍이 떨어진 거리에서는 그것을 보지 못한다."], ["But, alas!", "아니, 죽을 위험에 처했다고 느꼈을 때 제아무리 놀라고 겁에 질렸던 사람이라도, 그 두려움에서 벗어나기가 무섭게 죽음에 대한 공포마저 마음에서 깨끗이 지워 버린다. 그러나 아아!"], ["he who escapes from death is not pardoned; he is only reprieved, and reprieved to a short day.", "죽음에서 벗어난 자는 사면받은 것이 아니다. 그저 집행이 미뤄졌을 뿐이며, 그것도 잠깐 동안 미뤄졌을 따름이다."]], [["“Grieve, therefore, no more, my dear child, on this occasion: an event which may happen every hour; which every element, nay, almost every particle of matter that surrounds us is capable of producing, and which must and will most unavoidably reach us all at last, ought neither to occasion our surprize nor our lamentation.", "\"그러니 사랑하는 아이야, 이 일로 더는 슬퍼하지 마라. 시시각각 일어날 수 있는 일, 우리를 둘러싼 모든 원소가, 아니 거의 모든 물질의 알갱이 하나하나가 능히 일으킬 수 있는 일, 그리고 끝내는 더없이 피할 길 없이 우리 모두에게 반드시 닥치고야 말 일이라면, 그것은 우리의 놀람도 우리의 탄식도 불러일으켜서는 안 되는 것이다."]], [["“My physician having acquainted me (which I take very kindly of him) that I am in danger of leaving you all very shortly, I have determined to say a few words to you at this our parting, before my distemper, which I find grows very fast upon me, puts it out of my power.", "\"내 의사가 내게 알려 주기를(이는 그의 친절로 고맙게 여긴다), 내가 머지않아 너희 모두를 떠날 위험에 처했다 하니, 나는 이 작별의 자리에서 너희에게 몇 마디 말을 남기기로 마음먹었다. 내가 보기에 무척 빠르게 깊어 가는 이 병이 그럴 힘마저 앗아가 버리기 전에 말이다."]], [["“But I shall waste my strength too much.", "\"하지만 내 기력을 너무 많이 소진하겠구나. 나는 내 유언에 대해 말하려 했다."], ["I intended to speak concerning my will, which, though I have settled long ago, I think proper to mention such heads of it as concern any of you, that I may have the comfort of perceiving you are all satisfied with the provision I have there made for you.", "비록 오래전에 다 정해 둔 것이지만, 너희 가운데 누구와든 관련된 대목만은 짚어 두는 것이 옳다고 본다. 그래야 내가 거기에 너희를 위해 마련해 둔 몫에 너희 모두가 흡족해하는 것을 보는 위안을 누릴 수 있을 테니."]], [["“Nephew Blifil, I leave you the heir to my whole estate, except only £500 a-year, which is to revert to you after the death of your mother, and except one other estate of £500 a-year, and the sum of £6000, which I have bestowed in the following manner:", "\"조카 블리필, 나는 너를 내 전 재산의 상속자로 삼는다. 다만 연 500파운드는 예외로, 이는 네 어머니가 세상을 떠난 뒤에 네게 돌아갈 것이며, 또 연 500파운드의 다른 재산 한 몫과 6000파운드의 돈도 예외인데, 이것들은 내가 다음과 같이 나누어 주었다."]], [["“The estate of £500 a-year I have given to you, Mr Jones: and as I know the inconvenience which attends the want of ready money, I have added £1000 in specie. In this I know not whether I have exceeded or fallen short of your expectation. Perhaps you will think I have given you too little, and the world will be as ready to condemn me for giving you too much; but the latter censure I despise; and as to the former, unless you should entertain that common error which I have often heard in my life pleaded as an excuse for a total want of charity, namely, that instead of raising gratitude by voluntary acts of bounty, we are apt to raise demands, which of all others are the most boundless and most difficult to satisfy.--Pardon me the bare mention of this; I will not suspect any such thing.”", "\"연 500파운드의 그 재산은 그대, 존스 군에게 주었네. 그리고 나는 당장 쓸 현금이 없을 때 따르는 불편을 잘 알기에, 거기에 현금 1000파운드를 보탰네. 이 일로 내가 그대의 기대를 넘었는지 모자랐는지는 나도 모르겠네. 어쩌면 그대는 내가 너무 적게 주었다 여길지도 모르고, 세상은 또 내가 너무 많이 주었다며 나를 나무라려 들겠지. 그러나 뒤의 비난이라면 나는 경멸하네. 앞의 것에 대해 말하자면, 그대가 내가 살면서 자주 들어 온 그 흔한 잘못된 생각을 품지만 않는다면 말일세. 곧 자발적인 은혜의 베풂으로 감사를 일으키기는커녕, 도리어 요구를 키우기 쉽다는 생각, 그 요구란 무엇보다 끝이 없고 무엇보다 채우기 어렵다는 그 생각 말이네. 자선이 전혀 없는 것을 변명하는 핑계로 자주 들먹여지던 그 말 말일세. 이런 것을 입에 올린 것조차 용서하게. 나는 그대에게 그런 일은 조금도 의심하지 않겠네.\""]], [["Jones flung himself at his benefactor's feet, and taking eagerly hold of his hand, assured him his goodness to him, both now and all other times, had so infinitely exceeded not only his merit but his hopes, that no words could express his sense of it.", "존스는 자기 은인의 발치에 몸을 던지고 그의 손을 간절히 부여잡으며, 이번에도 그렇고 다른 모든 때에도 그렇고, 자신에게 베풀어 주신 그 자애가 자신의 공로는 물론 자신의 바람조차 한없이 넘어선 것이어서 어떤 말로도 그 고마움을 다 표현할 수 없다고 단언했다. \"그리고 분명히 말씀드리지만, 어르신,\""], ["“And I assure you, sir,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “your present generosity hath left me no other concern than for the present melancholy occasion.", "\"지금 베풀어 주신 이 너그러움은 제게, 오직 이 슬픈 자리에 대한 근심 말고는 어떤 다른 근심도 남겨 두지 않았습니다."], ["Oh, my friend, my father!”", "오, 나의 벗이여, 나의 아버지여!\""], ["Here his words choaked him, and he turned away to hide a tear which was starting from his eyes.", "여기서 말이 그의 목에 메었고, 그는 눈에서 솟아나는 눈물을 감추려 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["Allworthy then gently squeezed his hand, and proceeded thus: “I am convinced, my child, that you have much goodness, generosity, and honour, in your temper: if you will add prudence and religion to these, you must be happy; for the three former qualities, I admit, make you worthy of happiness, but they are the latter only which will put you in possession of it.", "그러자 올워디는 그의 손을 부드럽게 꼭 쥐고는 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"내 확신하네, 아이야, 그대의 천성에는 선함과 너그러움과 명예가 많이 깃들어 있어. 여기에 신중함과 신앙심까지 보탠다면 그대는 반드시 행복할 것이네. 인정하건대 앞의 세 가지 자질은 그대를 행복을 누릴 자격이 있는 사람으로 만들어 주지만, 그 행복을 실제로 손에 쥐게 해 주는 것은 오직 뒤의 두 가지뿐이라네."]], [["“One thousand pound I have given to you, Mr Thwackum; a sum I am convinced which greatly exceeds your desires, as well as your wants.", "\"그대, 스웨컴 씨에게는 1000파운드를 주었네. 이는 그대의 바람은 물론 그대의 필요까지도 크게 넘어서는 액수라고 나는 확신하네."], ["However, you will receive it as a memorial of my friendship; and whatever superfluities may redound to you, that piety which you so rigidly maintain will instruct you how to dispose of them.", "그래도 내 우정의 정표로 받아 주게. 그리고 그대에게 넘쳐 남는 것이 무엇이든, 그대가 그토록 엄격히 지켜 온 그 경건함이 그것을 어떻게 처분할지 일러 줄 것이네."]], [["“A like sum, Mr Square, I have bequeathed to you.", "\"스퀘어 씨, 그대에게도 같은 액수를 남겼네."], ["This, I hope, will enable you to pursue your profession with better success than hitherto.", "이것이 그대로 하여금 지금까지보다 더 나은 성취를 거두며 그대의 직업을 이어 가게 해 주기를 바라네."], ["I have often observed with concern, that distress is more apt to excite contempt than commiseration, especially among men of business, with whom poverty is understood to indicate want of ability.", "나는 곤궁이 동정보다 경멸을 불러일으키기 더 쉽다는 것을, 특히 사업하는 사람들 사이에서 그러하다는 것을 자주 안타깝게 지켜보았네. 그들에게 가난이란 능력이 모자란다는 표시로 통하니 말일세."], ["But the little I have been able to leave you will extricate you from those difficulties with which you have formerly struggled; and then I doubt not but you will meet with sufficient prosperity to supply what a man of your philosophical temper will require.", "그러나 내가 그대에게 남길 수 있었던 이 얼마 안 되는 것이, 그대가 전에 씨름하던 그 어려움에서 그대를 건져 줄 것이네. 그러고 나면 그대 같은 철학자 기질의 사람이 필요로 하는 것을 채워 줄 만한 넉넉한 번영을 그대가 분명 누리게 되리라 나는 의심치 않네."]], [["“I find myself growing faint, so I shall refer you to my will for my disposition of the residue.", "\"기운이 점점 빠져 가는구나. 그러니 나머지를 어떻게 처분했는지는 내 유언장을 보아 다오."], ["My servants will there find some tokens to remember me by; and there are a few charities which, I trust, my executors will see faithfully performed.", "내 하인들도 거기서 나를 기억할 만한 정표 몇 가지를 찾게 될 것이며, 또 몇 가지 자선이 있으니, 내 유언 집행인들이 그것을 충실히 이행해 주리라 믿는다. 너희 모두에게 축복이 있기를."], ["Bless you all. I am setting out a little before you.”--", "나는 너희보다 조금 앞서 길을 떠나는 것이란다.\""]], [["Here a footman came hastily into the room, and said there was an attorney from Salisbury who had a particular message, which he said he must communicate to Mr Allworthy himself: that he seemed in a violent hurry, and protested he had so much business to do, that, if he could cut himself into four quarters, all would not be sufficient.", "이때 한 하인이 황급히 방으로 들어와, 솔즈베리에서 온 변호사가 특별한 전갈을 가지고 왔으며 그것을 반드시 올워디 씨께 직접 전해야 한다고 한다고 알렸다. 그가 몹시 다급해 보였으며, 처리할 일이 어찌나 많은지 자기 몸을 넷으로 쪼갠다 해도 다 못 할 지경이라고 큰소리를 치더라는 것이었다."]], [["“Go, child,”", "\"가 보아라, 아이야,\""], ["said Allworthy to Blifil, “see what the gentleman wants.", "올워디가 블리필에게 말했다. \"그 사람이 무엇을 원하는지 보고 오너라."], ["I am not able to do any business now, nor can he have any with me, in which you are not at present more concerned than myself.", "나는 지금 어떤 일도 처리할 수 없거니와, 그가 나와 볼일이 있다 한들 그 일은 지금 나보다 네가 더 깊이 관련된 일일 테니."], ["Besides, I really am--I am incapable of seeing any one at present, or of any longer attention.”", "게다가 나는 정말이지 지금은 누구도 만날 수 없고, 더 오래 무언가에 마음을 쏟을 수도 없구나.\""], ["He then saluted them all, saying, perhaps he should be able to see them again, but he should be now glad to compose himself a little, finding that he had too much exhausted his spirits in discourse.", "그러고는 그들 모두에게 작별 인사를 건네며, 어쩌면 다시 그들을 볼 수 있을지도 모르겠으나 지금은 좀 마음을 가라앉히고 싶다고, 이야기에 너무 많은 기력을 쏟은 것 같다고 말했다."]], [["Some of the company shed tears at their parting; and even the philosopher Square wiped his eyes, albeit unused to the melting mood.", "자리에 있던 몇몇은 작별하며 눈물을 흘렸다. 좀처럼 마음이 녹는 일이 없던 철학자 스퀘어조차 눈가를 훔쳤다."], ["As to Mrs Wilkins, she dropt her pearls as fast as the Arabian trees their medicinal gums; for this was a ceremonial which that gentlewoman never omitted on a proper occasion.", "윌킨스 부인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 아라비아의 나무들이 약재로 쓰이는 진을 뚝뚝 흘리듯 진주 같은 눈물방울을 줄줄 떨구었다. 이는 그 부인이 마땅한 자리에서라면 결코 빠뜨리는 법이 없는 의식이었던 까닭이다."]], [["After this Mr Allworthy again laid himself down on his pillow, and endeavoured to compose himself to rest.", "이런 일이 있은 뒤 올워디 씨는 다시 베개에 몸을 누이고, 마음을 가라앉혀 쉬어 보려 애썼다."]], [["Containing matter rather natural than pleasing.", "여덟째 장. 유쾌하다기보다는 자연스러운 일을 담은 장."]], [["Besides grief for her master, there was another source for that briny stream which so plentifully rose above the two mountainous cheek-bones of the housekeeper.", "주인을 향한 슬픔 말고도, 가정부의 두 산봉우리 같은 광대뼈 위로 그토록 넘치게 솟아오른 그 짭짤한 물줄기에는 또 다른 원천이 있었다."], ["She was no sooner retired, than she began to mutter to herself in the following pleasant strain: “Sure master might have made some difference, methinks, between me and the other servants.", "그녀는 방에서 물러나오자마자 다음과 같이 흥겨운 가락으로 혼자 중얼거리기 시작했다. “정말이지, 주인 나리께서 나하고 다른 하인들을 좀 구별해 주실 만도 했을 텐데."], ["I suppose he hath left me mourning; but, i'fackins!", "내게 상복 값이나 남겨 주신 모양이지. 하지만 어머나!"], ["if that be all, the devil shall wear it for him, for me.", "그게 다라면, 그건 나리를 위해 악마더러 입으라지, 난 안 입을 테야."], ["I'd have his worship know I am no beggar.", "나리께서 알아 두셨으면 좋겠네, 나는 거지가 아니라는 걸."], ["I have saved five hundred pound in his service, and after all to be used in this manner.--It is a fine encouragement to servants to be honest; and to be sure, if I have taken a little something now and then, others have taken ten times as much; and now we are all put in a lump together.", "나리를 모시면서 오백 파운드나 모았는데, 결국 이런 대접을 받다니. 하인더러 정직하게 살라고 부추기는 데 참 좋은 본보기로다."], ["If so be that it be so, the legacy may go to the devil with him that gave it.", "하긴, 내가 가끔 이것저것 좀 챙겼다 쳐도, 다른 이들은 그 열 배는 챙겼지. 그런데 이제 우리를 죄다 한 덩어리로 뭉뚱그려 놓았으니."], ["No, I won't give it up neither, because that will please some folks.", "정 그렇다면, 그 유산은 그걸 준 사람과 함께 악마한테나 가 버려라."], ["No, I'll buy the gayest gown I can get, and dance over the old curmudgeon's grave in it.", "아니, 그래도 포기는 안 할 테야. 그러면 어떤 작자들이 좋아할 테니까."], ["This is my reward for taking his part so often, when all the country have cried shame of him, for breeding up his bastard in that manner; but he is going now where he must pay for all.", "아니, 구할 수 있는 가장 화려한 가운을 사서, 그걸 입고 그 노랑이의 무덤 위에서 춤을 출 테야. 온 고장 사람들이 그 사생아를 그런 식으로 키운다고 부끄러운 줄 알라며 손가락질할 때마다 그렇게도 자주 나리 편을 들어 준 보답이 이거라니."], ["It would have become him better to have repented of his sins on his deathbed, than to glory in them, and give away his estate out of his own family to a misbegotten child.", "하지만 이제 나리는 모든 걸 갚아야 할 곳으로 가시는 길이지. 임종 자리에서 죄를 뉘우치는 편이 훨씬 어울렸을 텐데, 도리어 그 죄를 자랑하며 제 집안 식구도 아닌 천출의 자식에게 재산을 떼어 주다니."], ["Found in his bed, forsooth!", "침대에서 주웠다고?"], ["a pretty story!", "참, 그럴듯한 이야기로군!"], ["ay, ay, those that hide know where to find.", "흥, 감추는 자들이 어디서 찾을지 아는 법이지."], ["Lord forgive him!", "주님, 저분을 용서하소서!"], ["I warrant he hath many more bastards to answer for, if the truth was known.", "진실이 밝혀진다면, 책임져야 할 사생아가 더 많이 있을 게 분명해."], ["One comfort is, they will all be known where he is a going now.--`The servants will find some token to remember me by.'", "한 가지 위안이라면, 나리께서 이제 가시는 곳에서는 그것들이 죄다 밝혀지리란 거지."], ["Those were the very words; I shall never forget them, if I was to live a thousand years.", "‘하인들에게도 나를 기억할 만한 표시를 남겨 두겠노라.’ 바로 그 말씀이었어. 천 년을 산다 해도 나는 그 말을 잊지 못할 거야."], ["Ay, ay, I shall remember you for huddling me among the servants.", "흥, 흥, 나를 하인들 틈에 욱여넣으신 걸 똑똑히 기억해 드리지."], ["One would have thought he might have mentioned my name as well as that of Square; but he is a gentleman forsooth, though he had not cloths on his back when he came hither first.", "스퀘어의 이름을 부르신 것처럼 내 이름도 부를 만하셨을 텐데. 하지만 그자는 신사이시란다, 처음 여기 왔을 적엔 등에 걸칠 옷 한 벌 없었으면서."], ["Marry come up with such gentlemen!", "그런 신사 나리들과는 어디 한번 어울려 보시지!"], ["though he hath lived here this many years, I don't believe there is arrow a servant in the house ever saw the colour of his money.", "그자가 여기서 그 여러 해를 살았어도, 이 집 하인 중에 그자 돈 색깔이라도 본 사람이 있을 성싶지 않아."], ["The devil shall wait upon such a gentleman for me.”", "그런 신사 나리는 악마더러나 모시라지.”"], ["Much more of the like kind she muttered to herself; but this taste shall suffice to the reader.", "그녀는 이런 식의 말을 훨씬 더 많이 혼자 중얼거렸지만, 이쯤이면 독자에게 그 맛보기로 충분할 것이다."]], [["Neither Thwackum nor Square were much better satisfied with their legacies.", "스왜컴도 스퀘어도 제 유산에 그다지 만족하지 못하기는 마찬가지였다."], ["Though they breathed not their resentment so loud, yet from the discontent which appeared in their countenances, as well as from the following dialogue, we collect that no great pleasure reigned in their minds.", "두 사람 다 그 원망을 그토록 큰 소리로 토해 내지는 않았으나, 얼굴에 떠오른 불만으로 보나 다음에 이어지는 대화로 보나, 우리는 그들의 마음속에 대단한 흡족함이 깃들지는 않았음을 짐작하게 된다."]], [["About an hour after they had left the sick-room, Square met Thwackum in the hall and accosted him thus: “Well, sir, have you heard any news of your friend since we parted from him?”--“If you mean Mr Allworthy,”", "병실을 나선 지 한 시간쯤 지나, 스퀘어는 현관 홀에서 스왜컴과 마주치자 이렇게 말을 건넸다. “자, 선생, 우리가 그분 곁을 떠난 뒤로 댁의 벗에 관한 소식을 좀 들으셨소?”"], ["answered Thwackum, “I think you might rather give him the appellation of your friend; for he seems to me to have deserved that title.”--“The title is as good on your side,”", "“올워디 씨를 두고 하는 말씀이라면,” 스왜컴이 대꾸했다."], ["replied Square, “for his bounty, such as it is, hath been equal to both.”--“I should not have mentioned it first,”", "“차라리 그분을 댁의 벗이라 부르는 편이 맞을 듯하오. 내가 보기엔 그분이 그 칭호를 받을 자격이 있는 듯하니 말이오.”"], ["cries Thwackum, “but since you begin, I must inform you I am of a different opinion.", "“그 칭호라면 댁 쪽에도 똑같이 합당하오.” 스퀘어가 받아쳤다."], ["There is a wide distinction between voluntary favours and rewards.", "“그분의 그 후한 베풂이라는 것이, 그 정도가 어떻든 우리 둘에게 똑같았으니 말이오.” “먼저 꺼낼 생각은 없었소만,”"], ["The duty I have done in his family, and the care I have taken in the education of his two boys, are services for which some men might have expected a greater return.", "스왜컴이 외쳤다. “댁이 시작했으니 알려 드려야겠소, 나는 생각이 다르오."], ["I would not have you imagine I am therefore dissatisfied; for St Paul hath taught me to be content with the little I have.", "자발적인 호의와 보상 사이에는 큰 차이가 있소. 내가 그 집안에서 해 온 직무와, 두 소년의 교육에 기울인 정성은, 어떤 이들이라면 더 큰 보답을 기대했을 만한 봉사요."], ["Had the modicum been less, I should have known my duty.", "그렇다고 내가 불만스러워한다고 여기지는 마시오. 성 바울께서 내게 작은 것에 만족하라 가르치셨으니."], ["But though the Scriptures obliges me to remain contented, it doth not enjoin me to shut my eyes to my own merit, nor restrain me from seeing when I am injured by an unjust comparison.”--“Since you provoke me,”", "설령 그 몫이 더 적었다 한들, 나는 내 본분을 알았을 거요. 그러나 성경이 내게 만족하라 명하신다 하여, 내 공적에 눈을 감으라 명하시는 것은 아니며, 부당한 비교로 손해를 입었을 때 그것을 알아차리는 일까지 막으시는 것도 아니오.”"], ["returned Square, “that injury is done to me; nor did I ever imagine Mr Allworthy had held my friendship so light, as to put me in balance with one who received his wages.", "“댁이 나를 자극하니 하는 말인데,” 스퀘어가 응수했다."], ["I know to what it is owing; it proceeds from those narrow principles which you have been so long endeavouring to infuse into him, in contempt of everything which is great and noble.", "“그 손해를 입은 것은 바로 나요. 올워디 씨가 내 우정을 그토록 가벼이 여겨, 품삯을 받는 자와 나를 저울에 나란히 올리리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했소."], ["The beauty and loveliness of friendship is too strong for dim eyes, nor can it be perceived by any other medium than that unerring rule of right, which you have so often endeavoured to ridicule, that you have perverted your friend's understanding.”--“I wish,”", "어쩌다 그리 되었는지 나는 알고 있소. 그것은 댁이 그토록 오랫동안 그분께 불어넣으려 애써 온 그 옹졸한 원칙들에서 비롯된 것이오."], ["cries Thwackum, in a rage, “I wish, for the sake of his soul, your damnable doctrines have not perverted his faith.", "위대하고 고귀한 모든 것을 업신여기는 그 원칙 말이오. 우정의 아름다움과 사랑스러움은 흐린 눈으로 보기엔 너무도 강렬해서, 댁이 그토록 자주 비웃으려 들던 그 어김없는 옳음의 척도가 아니고서는 어떤 매개로도 알아볼 수가 없소."], ["It is to this I impute his present behaviour, so unbecoming a Christian.", "댁은 그것을 비웃은 끝에, 댁의 벗의 분별력마저 비틀어 놓고 만 거요.” “나는 바라오,”"], ["Who but an atheist could think of leaving the world without having first made up his account?", "스왜컴이 격분하여 외쳤다. “그분의 영혼을 위해 바라건대, 댁의 그 저주받을 교리가 그분의 믿음마저 비틀어 놓지 않았기를 말이오."], ["without confessing his sins, and receiving that absolution which he knew he had one in the house duly authorized to give him?", "나는 그분의 지금 행실을, 그리스도인답지 않은 그 행실을 바로 거기에 돌리는 바요. 무신론자가 아니고서야 그 누가, 먼저 제 셈을 다 치르지도 않고서 이 세상을 떠날 생각을 하겠소?"], ["He will feel the want of these necessaries when it is too late, when he is arrived at that place where there is wailing and gnashing of teeth.", "죄를 고백하지도 않고, 집안에 그것을 베풀 권한을 정당히 받은 사람이 하나 있는 줄 알면서도 그 사죄를 받지 않고서 말이오? 그분은 너무 늦었을 때에야 이 필수품들의 부재를 느끼게 될 거요."], ["It is then he will find in what mighty stead that heathen goddess, that virtue, which you and all other deists of the age adore, will stand him.", "통곡과 이 가는 소리가 있는 그곳에 다다랐을 때에 말이오. 바로 그때 그분은, 댁과 이 시대의 다른 모든 이신론자들이 떠받드는 그 이교의 여신, 그 덕이라는 것이 자기에게 얼마나 큰 도움이 되는지 깨닫게 될 거요."], ["He will then summon his priest, when there is none to be found, and will lament the want of that absolution, without which no sinner can be safe.”--“If it be so material,”", "그때 그분은 사제를 부를 테지만 찾을 수 없을 것이고, 그것 없이는 어떤 죄인도 안전할 수 없는 그 사죄의 부재를 한탄하게 될 거요.” “그것이 그토록 긴요하다면,”"], ["says Square, “why don't you present it him of your own accord?”", "스퀘어가 말했다. “어찌하여 댁이 자청해서 그분께 베풀지 않으시오?”"], ["“It hath no virtue,”", "“그것은,” 스왜컴이 외쳤다."], ["cries Thwackum, “but to those who have sufficient grace to require it.", "“그것을 청할 만한 충분한 은총을 지닌 자에게가 아니고서는 아무 효력이 없소. 한데 내가 어쩌자고 이교도이자 불신자에게 이런 말을 하고 있는가?"], ["But why do I talk thus to a heathen and an unbeliever?", "그분께 이 가르침을 일러 준 것은 바로 댁이오. 그 대가로 댁은 이 세상에서 두둑한 보답을 받았으니, 댁의 제자가 머지않아 저세상에서도 그러하리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않소.”"], ["It is you that taught him this lesson, for which you have been well rewarded in this world, as I doubt not your disciple will soon be in the other.”--“I know not what you mean by reward,”", "“보답이라는 말로 무엇을 뜻하는지 모르겠소.” 스퀘어가 말했다."], ["said Square; “but if you hint at that pitiful memorial of our friendship, which he hath thought fit to bequeath me, I despise it; and nothing but the unfortunate situation of my circumstances should prevail on me to accept it.”", "“하지만 그분이 우리 우정의 기념이랍시고 내게 남겨 주기로 한 그 보잘것없는 표시를 빗대는 거라면, 나는 그것을 경멸하오. 내 처지가 불운하지만 않았던들, 그 무엇도 나로 하여금 그것을 받아들이게 하지 못했을 거요.”"]], [["The physician now arrived, and began to inquire of the two disputants, how we all did above-stairs?", "이제 의사가 도착하여, 위층 우리 모두의 안부가 어떠한지 두 논객에게 묻기 시작했다."], ["“In a miserable way,”", "“비참한 지경이오.”"], ["answered Thwackum.", "스왜컴이 대답했다."], ["“It is no more than I expected,”", "“내가 짐작한 그대로로군요.” 의사가 외쳤다."], ["cries the doctor: “but pray what symptoms have appeared since I left you?”--“No good ones, I am afraid,”", "“한데 부디 알려 주시오, 내가 자리를 비운 사이에 어떤 증세가 나타났는지요?” “좋은 것은 없는 듯하오.”"], ["replied Thwackum: “after what past at our departure, I think there were little hopes.”", "스왜컴이 대꾸했다. “우리가 떠날 적에 오간 일을 보면, 거의 희망이 없는 듯하오.”"], ["The bodily physician, perhaps, misunderstood the curer of souls; and before they came to an explanation, Mr Blifil came to them with a most melancholy countenance, and acquainted them that he brought sad news, that his mother was dead at Salisbury; that she had been seized on the road home with the gout in her head and stomach, which had carried her off in a few hours.", "육신의 의사는 아마도 영혼의 치유자를 잘못 알아들은 모양이었다. 한데 두 사람이 그 뜻을 풀어 밝히기도 전에, 블리필 씨가 더없이 침울한 얼굴로 다가와, 슬픈 소식을 가져왔노라 알렸다."], ["“Good-lack-a-day!” says the doctor.", "그의 어머니가 솔즈베리에서 세상을 떠났다는 것이었다."], ["“One cannot answer for events; but I wish I had been at hand, to have been called in.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에 머리와 위장에 통풍이 덮쳐, 몇 시간 만에 그분을 데려가 버렸다고 했다. “저런, 이를 어쩌나!”"], ["The gout is a distemper which it is difficult to treat; yet I have been remarkably successful in it.”", "의사가 말했다. “이런 일이야 누구도 장담할 수 없는 노릇이지만, 그래도 내가 곁에 있어 불려 갈 수 있었더라면 좋았을 것을."], ["Thwackum and Square both condoled with Mr Blifil for the loss of his mother, which the one advised him to bear like a man, and the other like a Christian.", "통풍은 다스리기 까다로운 병이지요. 그래도 나는 그 병에 유달리 성과가 좋았소.”"], ["The young gentleman said he knew very well we were all mortal, and he would endeavour to submit to his loss as well as he could.", "스왜컴과 스퀘어는 둘 다 블리필 씨에게 어머니를 여읜 일에 조의를 표했는데, 한 사람은 그에게 사내답게 견디라 충고했고, 다른 한 사람은 그리스도인답게 견디라 충고했다. 젊은 신사는, 우리 모두가 죽을 운명임을 자기도 잘 알고 있으며, 이 상실에 되도록 잘 순응하도록 애써 보겠노라 말했다."], ["That he could not, however, help complaining a little against the peculiar severity of his fate, which brought the news of so great a calamity to him by surprize, and that at a time when he hourly expected the severest blow he was capable of feeling from the malice of fortune.", "다만 자기 운명의 유별난 가혹함에 대해서는 조금 한탄하지 않을 수 없노라고 했다. 그토록 큰 재앙의 소식이 불시에 자기에게 닥쳐왔으며, 그것도 운명의 악의로부터 감당할 수 있는 가장 모진 일격을 시시각각 기다리던 바로 그 시점에 그리되었다는 것이었다."], ["He said, the present occasion would put to the test those excellent rudiments which he had learnt from Mr Thwackum and Mr Square; and it would be entirely owing to them, if he was enabled to survive such misfortunes.", "그는 말하기를, 지금 이 일이야말로 자기가 스왜컴 씨와 스퀘어 씨에게서 배운 그 훌륭한 기초 가르침들을 시험대에 올리게 될 것이며, 만일 자기가 이런 불행들을 견뎌 낼 수 있다면 그것은 전적으로 그 가르침 덕분일 것이라고 했다."]], [["It was now debated whether Mr Allworthy should be informed of the death of his sister.", "이제 올워디 씨에게 누이의 죽음을 알려야 할지 말지를 두고 의논이 벌어졌다."], ["This the doctor violently opposed; in which, I believe, the whole college would agree with him: but Mr Blifil said, he had received such positive and repeated orders from his uncle, never to keep any secret from him for fear of the disquietude which it might give him, that he durst not think of disobedience, whatever might be the consequence.", "의사는 이를 격렬히 반대했는데, 내 생각으로는 의학계 전체가 그와 뜻을 같이했을 것이다. 그러나 블리필 씨는, 어떤 비밀이든 그것이 줄지도 모를 불안을 염려하여 결코 자기에게 숨기지 말라는 분부를 숙부로부터 그토록 단호하고 거듭 받아 왔으므로, 그 결과가 어떠하든 감히 거역할 생각은 못 하겠노라 말했다."], ["He said, for his part, considering the religious and philosophic temper of his uncle, he could not agree with the doctor in his apprehensions.", "그는 말하기를, 자기로서는 숙부의 신앙심 깊고 철학적인 기질을 헤아릴 때, 의사의 그 염려에 동의할 수 없다고 했다."], ["He was therefore resolved to communicate it to him: for if his uncle recovered (as he heartily prayed he might) he knew he would never forgive an endeavour to keep a secret of this kind from him.", "그리하여 그는 그것을 숙부에게 전하기로 결심했다. 만일 숙부가 회복하신다면(그는 그리되시기를 진심으로 기도했다) 이런 종류의 비밀을 자기에게 숨기려 한 일을 결코 용서치 않으시리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다."]], [["The physician was forced to submit to these resolutions, which the two other learned gentlemen very highly commended.", "의사는 이 결심에 따를 수밖에 없었고, 다른 두 학식 있는 신사는 그 결심을 매우 높이 칭찬했다. 그리하여 블리필 씨와 의사는 함께 병실 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼고, 거기서 의사가 먼저 들어가 침상에 다가가 환자의 맥을 짚었다."], ["So together moved Mr Blifil and the doctor toward the sick-room; where the physician first entered, and approached the bed, in order to feel his patient's pulse, which he had no sooner done, than he declared he was much better; that the last application had succeeded to a miracle, and had brought the fever to intermit: so that, he said, there appeared now to be as little danger as he had before apprehended there were hopes.", "맥을 짚자마자 그는 환자가 훨씬 나아졌노라 선언했다. 마지막 처방이 기적처럼 효험을 보여 열이 한풀 꺾였으니, 앞서 그가 희망이 있다고 본 만큼이나 이제는 위험이 거의 없어 보인다고 말했다."]], [["To say the truth, Mr Allworthy's situation had never been so bad as the great caution of the doctor had represented it: but as a wise general never despises his enemy, however inferior that enemy's force may be, so neither doth a wise physician ever despise a distemper, however inconsiderable.", "사실대로 말하자면, 올워디 씨의 상태는 의사의 그 대단한 신중함이 묘사했던 만큼 나빴던 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그러나 지혜로운 장수가 적의 병력이 아무리 보잘것없을지라도 결코 그 적을 얕보지 않듯이, 지혜로운 의사 또한 병세가 아무리 대수롭지 않을지라도 결코 그 병을 얕보지 않는 법이다."], ["As the former preserves the same strict discipline, places the same guards, and employs the same scouts, though the enemy be never so weak; so the latter maintains the same gravity of countenance, and shakes his head with the same significant air, let the distemper be never so trifling.", "앞의 장수가 적이 아무리 약하더라도 똑같이 엄격한 군기를 유지하고, 똑같은 경계병을 세우며, 똑같은 정찰병을 부리듯이, 뒤의 의사는 병세가 아무리 하찮을지라도 똑같이 무거운 표정을 짓고, 똑같이 의미심장한 태도로 고개를 가로젓는다."], ["And both, among many other good ones, may assign this solid reason for their conduct, that by these means the greater glory redounds to them if they gain the victory, and the less disgrace if by any unlucky accident they should happen to be conquered.", "그리고 두 사람은, 그 밖의 여러 그럴듯한 이유들 가운데서도 자기네 처신에 대해 이 단단한 이유 하나를 댈 수 있다. 곧, 이런 방식으로 하면 승리를 거둘 경우 더 큰 영광이 자기에게 돌아오고, 어쩌다 불운한 사고로 패하게 되더라도 망신은 덜하다는 것이다."]], [["Mr Allworthy had no sooner lifted up his eyes, and thanked Heaven for these hopes of his recovery, than Mr Blifil drew near, with a very dejected aspect, and having applied his handkerchief to his eye, either to wipe away his tears, or to do as Ovid somewhere expresses himself on another occasion", "올워디 씨는 눈을 떠 회복의 희망을 두고 하늘에 감사를 올리기가 무섭게, 블리필 씨가 몹시 풀죽은 낯빛으로 가까이 다가와, 눈물을 닦으려는 것인지 아니면 오비디우스가 다른 자리에서 어디선가 표현한 대로 하려는 것인지, 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다."]], [["Si nullus erit, tamen excute nullum,", "Si nullus erit, tamen excute nullum,"]], [["If there be none, then wipe away that none,", "설령 눈물이 없을지라도, 그 없는 눈물을 닦아 내라,"]], [["he communicated to his uncle what the reader hath been just before acquainted with.", "그러고는 독자가 바로 앞서 알게 된 그 일을 숙부에게 전했다."]], [["Allworthy received the news with concern, with patience, and with resignation.", "올워디는 그 소식을 근심과 인내와 체념으로 받아들였다."], ["He dropt a tender tear, then composed his countenance, and at last cried, “The Lord's will be done in everything.”", "그는 부드러운 눈물 한 방울을 떨구고는, 곧 낯빛을 가다듬고, 마침내 외쳤다. “모든 일에 주님의 뜻이 이루어지이다.”"]], [["He now enquired for the messenger; but Blifil told him it had been impossible to detain him a moment; for he appeared by the great hurry he was in to have some business of importance on his hands; that he complained of being hurried and driven and torn out of his life, and repeated many times, that if he could divide himself into four quarters, he knew how to dispose of every one.", "이제 그는 전령을 찾았으나, 블리필은 그를 한순간도 붙들어 둘 수가 없었노라 말했다. 그가 어찌나 몹시 서두르던지 무슨 중대한 볼일을 손에 쥐고 있는 듯 보였다는 것이었다. 그는 정신없이 쫓기고 내몰려 죽을 지경이라 투덜대며, 자기 몸을 네 조각으로 나눌 수만 있다면 그 하나하나를 어디다 써야 할지 안다고 여러 번 되뇌었다고 했다."]], [["Allworthy then desired Blifil to take care of the funeral.", "그러자 올워디는 블리필에게 장례를 맡아 달라 부탁했다."], ["He said, he would have his sister deposited in his own chapel; and as to the particulars, he left them to his own discretion, only mentioning the person whom he would have employed on this occasion.", "그는 누이를 자기 집안 예배당에 안치하고 싶다고 말했고, 세부적인 일들은 블리필의 재량에 맡기되, 다만 이 일에 부려 쓰고 싶은 사람만을 따로 일러두었다."]], [["# Chapter ix.", "# Chapter ix."]], [["Which, among other things, may serve as a comment on that saying of Aeschines, that “drunkenness shows the mind of a man, as a mirrour reflects his person.”", "이 장은 여러 가지 가운데서도, “술취함은 거울이 사람의 모습을 비추듯 그 마음을 드러낸다”는 아이스키네스의 말에 대한 주석으로 읽힐 수 있을 것이다."]], [["The reader may perhaps wonder at hearing nothing of Mr Jones in the last chapter.", "독자는 지난 장에서 존스 씨에 관한 이야기가 전혀 없었던 것을 의아해할지도 모른다."], ["In fact, his behaviour was so different from that of the persons there mentioned, that we chose not to confound his name with theirs.", "사실 그의 행동은 거기 언급된 사람들의 그것과 너무도 달라서, 우리는 그의 이름을 그들의 이름과 뒤섞고 싶지 않았다."]], [["When the good man had ended his speech, Jones was the last who deserted the room. Thence he retired to his own apartment, to give vent to his concern; but the restlessness of his mind would not suffer him to remain long there; he slipped softly therefore to Allworthy's chamber-door, where he listened a considerable time without hearing any kind of motion within, unless a violent snoring, which at last his fears misrepresented as groans. This so alarmed him, that he could not forbear entering the room; where he found the good man in the bed, in a sweet composed sleep, and his nurse snoring in the above mentioned hearty manner, at the bed's feet. He immediately took the only method of silencing this thorough bass, whose music he feared might disturb Mr Allworthy; and then sitting down by the nurse, he remained motionless till Blifil and the doctor came in together and waked the sick man, in order that the doctor might feel his pulse, and that the other might communicate to him that piece of news, which, had Jones been apprized of it, would have had great difficulty of finding its way to Mr Allworthy's ear at such a season.", "그 선량한 사람이 연설을 마쳤을 때, 그 방을 가장 마지막으로 떠난 이는 존스였다. 그는 거기서 자기 방으로 물러나 마음속 근심을 풀어내려 했다. 그러나 마음의 들끓음이 그를 오래 그곳에 머물게 두지 않았다. 그래서 그는 살그머니 올워디의 침실 문 앞으로 미끄러져 가서, 안에서 어떤 기척도 들리지 않는 가운데 한참을 귀 기울였는데, 다만 사나운 코 고는 소리만은 들렸고, 끝내 그의 두려움은 그것을 신음 소리로 잘못 알아들었다. 이것이 그를 어찌나 놀라게 했던지, 그는 방으로 들어가지 않을 수 없었다. 거기서 그는 선량한 사람이 침대에 누워 달콤하고 평온한 잠에 빠져 있는 것을, 그리고 그를 돌보는 간병인이 침대 발치에서 앞서 말한 그 우렁찬 방식으로 코를 골고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 곧바로 이 깊은 저음을 잠재울 유일한 방법을 택했다. 그 소리가 올워디 씨의 잠을 깨우지 않을까 두려웠기 때문이다. 그러고는 간병인 곁에 앉아 꼼짝도 하지 않고 있었는데, 마침내 블리필과 의사가 함께 들어와 환자를 깨웠다. 의사가 맥을 짚기 위해서였고, 다른 한 사람은 환자에게 그 소식을 전하기 위해서였는데, 만일 존스가 그것을 미리 알았더라면, 그 소식은 이런 때에 올워디 씨의 귀에 닿는 데 큰 어려움을 겪었을 것이다."]], [["When he first heard Blifil tell his uncle this story, Jones could hardly contain the wrath which kindled in him at the other's indiscretion, especially as the doctor shook his head, and declared his unwillingness to have the matter mentioned to his patient.", "블리필이 삼촌에게 그 이야기를 하는 것을 처음 들었을 때, 존스는 상대의 분별없음에 끓어오르는 분노를 좀처럼 참기 어려웠다. 더구나 의사가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 그 일을 환자에게 언급하는 것을 꺼린다고 말한 터라 더욱 그러했다."], ["But as his passion did not so far deprive him of all use of his understanding, as to hide from him the consequences which any violent expression towards Blifil might have on the sick, this apprehension stilled his rage at the present; and he grew afterwards so satisfied with finding that this news had, in fact, produced no mischief, that he suffered his anger to die in his own bosom, without ever mentioning it to Blifil.", "그러나 그의 격정이 이성을 모두 앗아갈 만큼은 아니어서, 블리필을 향한 어떤 격렬한 표현이든 환자에게 미칠 결과가 그의 눈앞에 가려지지는 않았다. 이 염려가 당장은 그의 분노를 가라앉혔고, 그 뒤로 그 소식이 사실 아무런 해를 끼치지 않았음을 알게 되자 그는 무척 흡족해져서, 자신의 분노가 블리필에게 한마디도 내비치지 않은 채 제 가슴속에서 사그라지도록 내버려두었다."]], [["The physician dined that day at Mr Allworthy's; and having after dinner visited his patient, he returned to the company, and told them, that he had now the satisfaction to say, with assurance, that his patient was out of all danger: that he had brought his fever to a perfect intermission, and doubted not by throwing in the bark to prevent its return.", "그 의사는 그날 올워디 씨 댁에서 식사를 했다. 식사 후 환자를 살펴본 뒤 그는 다시 좌중으로 돌아와, 이제 자신의 환자가 모든 위험에서 벗어났음을 확신을 가지고 말할 수 있게 되어 만족스럽다고 전했다. 그는 환자의 열을 완전한 소강 상태로 끌어내렸으며, 키니네 껍질을 처방해 두면 그 재발을 막을 수 있으리라 의심치 않는다고 했다."]], [["This account so pleased Jones, and threw him into such immoderate excess of rapture, that he might be truly said to be drunk with joy--an intoxication which greatly forwards the effects of wine; and as he was very free too with the bottle on this occasion (for he drank many bumpers to the doctor's health, as well as to other toasts) he became very soon literally drunk.", "이 소식은 존스를 어찌나 기쁘게 했던지, 그를 도를 넘는 황홀경에 빠뜨려, 그가 정말로 기쁨에 취했다고 말해도 좋을 정도였다. 이러한 도취는 술의 효과를 크게 부추기는 법이다. 게다가 이번 자리에서 그는 술병을 아주 거리낌 없이 다루었으니 (의사의 건강을 위해, 또 다른 여러 축배를 위해 가득 채운 잔을 여러 차례 비웠으므로), 그는 머지않아 말 그대로 취하고 말았다."]], [["Jones had naturally violent animal spirits: these being set on float and augmented by the spirit of wine, produced most extravagant effects.", "존스는 천성적으로 격렬한 혈기를 지니고 있었다. 이것이 술기운에 둥둥 떠올라 더욱 부풀어 오르니, 더없이 터무니없는 결과를 빚어냈다."], ["He kissed the doctor, and embraced him with the most passionate endearments; swearing that next to Mr Allworthy himself, he loved him of all men living.", "그는 의사에게 입을 맞추고, 더없이 열렬한 애정 표현으로 그를 끌어안으며, 올워디 씨 본인 다음으로 살아 있는 모든 사람 가운데 그를 가장 사랑한다고 맹세했다. “선생,”"], ["“Doctor,”", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["added he, “you deserve a statue to be erected to you at the public expense, for having preserved a man, who is not only the darling of all good men who know him, but a blessing to society, the glory of his country, and an honour to human nature.", "“선생은 공금을 들여서라도 동상을 세워 마땅한 분이오. 한 사람을 살려냈으니 말이오."], ["D--n me if I don't love him better than my own soul.”", "그분은 그를 아는 모든 선한 이들의 사랑을 받는 분일 뿐 아니라, 사회의 축복이요, 조국의 영광이요, 인류의 명예이기도 하오. 내가 그분을 내 영혼보다 더 사랑하지 않는다면 빌어먹을 놈이라 불러도 좋소.”"]], [["“More shame for you,”", "“그건 자네에게 더욱 수치스러운 일이로군.”"], ["cries Thwackum.", "스왜컴이 소리쳤다."], ["“Though I think you have reason to love him, for he hath provided very well for you.", "“하기야 자네가 그분을 사랑할 만한 이유는 있다고 생각하네. 그분이 자네를 위해 아주 잘 마련해 두셨으니 말이야."], ["And perhaps it might have been better for some folks that he had not lived to see just reason of revoking his gift.”", "그리고 어쩌면 어떤 사람들에게는, 그분이 그 선물을 거두실 정당한 이유를 보실 때까지 살아 계시지 않는 편이 더 나았을지도 모르지.”"]], [["Jones now looking on Thwackum with inconceivable disdain, answered, “And doth thy mean soul imagine that any such considerations could weigh with me? No, let the earth open and swallow her own dirt (if I had millions of acres I would say it) rather than swallow up my dear glorious friend.”", "존스는 이제 형언할 수 없는 경멸의 눈으로 스왜컴을 바라보며 대답했다. “그래, 자네의 비천한 영혼은 그따위 따짐이 내게 조금이라도 무게가 나가리라 상상하는가? 천만에. 차라리 땅이 열려 제 흙을 집어삼키게 하라 (내게 수백만 에이커가 있다 해도 나는 그렇게 말하겠네). 내 소중하고 영광스러운 벗을 집어삼키느니 말일세.”"]], [["Quis desiderio sit pudor aut modus Tam chari capitis?[*]", "그토록 소중한 분을 향한 그리움에 어찌 부끄러움이나 절도가 있으랴?"]], [["[*] “What modesty or measure can set bounds to our desire of so dear a friend?”", "[*] “그토록 소중한 벗을 향한 우리의 갈망에 어떤 절제나 한도가 한계를 지을 수 있으랴?”"], ["The word desiderium here cannot be easily translated.", "여기 쓰인 desiderium이라는 말은 쉽게 옮기기 어렵다."], ["It includes our desire of enjoying our friend again, and the grief which attends that desire.", "그것은 우리의 벗을 다시 누리고자 하는 갈망과, 그 갈망에 따르는 슬픔을 함께 담고 있다."]], [["The doctor now interposed, and prevented the effects of a wrath which was kindling between Jones and Thwackum; after which the former gave a loose to mirth, sang two or three amorous songs, and fell into every frantic disorder which unbridled joy is apt to inspire; but so far was he from any disposition to quarrel, that he was ten times better humoured, if possible, than when he was sober.", "이제 의사가 끼어들어, 존스와 스왜컴 사이에 타오르던 분노의 결과를 막았다. 그 후 존스는 흥겨움에 몸을 맡겨 사랑 노래 두세 곡을 부르고, 고삐 풀린 기쁨이 으레 불러일으키는 온갖 광란의 무질서에 빠져들었다. 그러나 다툴 기색은 조금도 없었으니, 가능하다면 술 깬 때보다 열 배는 더 기분이 좋았다."]], [["To say truth, nothing is more erroneous than the common observation, that men who are ill-natured and quarrelsome when they are drunk, are very worthy persons when they are sober: for drink, in reality, doth not reverse nature, or create passions in men which did not exist in them before.", "사실대로 말하자면, 술에 취하면 심술궂고 시비를 거는 사람이 술이 깨면 매우 훌륭한 사람이라는 흔한 통념만큼 잘못된 것도 없다. 술은 실상 사람의 본성을 뒤집거나, 전에 없던 격정을 그에게서 새로 만들어내는 것이 아니기 때문이다."], ["It takes away the guard of reason, and consequently forces us to produce those symptoms, which many, when sober, have art enough to conceal.", "술은 이성의 파수병을 거두어가며, 따라서 많은 이들이 멀쩡할 때는 능숙하게 감추던 그 징후들을 우리로 하여금 드러내게 만든다."], ["It heightens and inflames our passions (generally indeed that passion which is uppermost in our mind), so that the angry temper, the amorous, the generous, the good-humoured, the avaricious, and all other dispositions of men, are in their cups heightened and exposed.", "술은 우리의 격정을(대개는 우리 마음속에서 가장 윗자리를 차지한 격정을) 부추기고 불타오르게 하니, 화 잘 내는 기질, 정 많은 기질, 너그러운 기질, 쾌활한 기질, 인색한 기질, 그 밖의 온갖 사람의 성향들이 술잔 속에서 한껏 부풀려지고 까발려지는 것이다."]], [["And yet as no nation produces so many drunken quarrels, especially among the lower people, as England (for indeed, with them, to drink and to fight together are almost synonymous terms), I would not, methinks, have it thence concluded, that the English are the worst-natured people alive.", "그렇지만 어떤 나라도 영국만큼 술취한 다툼을, 특히 하층민들 사이에서 많이 빚어내지 않는다 해서 (사실 그들에게 함께 마시는 것과 함께 싸우는 것은 거의 같은 뜻의 말이다), 거기서 영국인이 세상에서 가장 심성 고약한 사람들이라는 결론을 끌어내고 싶지는 않다. 어쩌면 그 바탕에는 오직 명예에 대한 사랑이 깔려 있을지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps the love of glory only is at the bottom of this; so that the fair conclusion seems to be, that our countrymen have more of that love, and more of bravery, than any other plebeians.", "그러니 공정한 결론은, 우리 동포가 그 어떤 다른 평민들보다도 그 사랑을, 그리고 더 많은 용맹을 지니고 있다는 것이리라. 더욱이 그러한 자리에서 너그럽지 못하거나 부당하거나 심술궂은 짓이 행해지는 일은 드물다는 점이 이를 뒷받침한다."], ["And this the rather, as there is seldom anything ungenerous, unfair, or ill-natured, exercised on these occasions: nay, it is common for the combatants to express good-will for each other even at the time of the conflict; and as their drunken mirth generally ends in a battle, so do most of their battles end in friendship.", "아니, 싸움이 벌어지는 그 와중에도 서로에게 호의를 내비치는 것이 그들에게는 흔한 일이다. 또한 그들의 취한 흥이 대개 한바탕 싸움으로 끝나듯, 그들의 싸움도 대개는 우정으로 끝나는 법이다."]], [["But to return to our history.", "그러나 우리의 이야기로 돌아가자."], ["Though Jones had shown no design of giving offence, yet Mr Blifil was highly offended at a behaviour which was so inconsistent with the sober and prudent reserve of his own temper.", "존스가 모욕을 줄 의도를 전혀 보이지 않았는데도, 블리필 씨는 자기 기질의 차분하고 신중한 절제와 너무도 어긋나는 그 행동에 몹시 기분이 상했다."], ["He bore it too with the greater impatience, as it appeared to him very indecent at this season; “When,”", "그는 그것을 한층 더 참기 어려워했는데, 그에게는 그것이 이런 때에 매우 무례하게 비쳤기 때문이다."], ["as he said, “the house was a house of mourning, on the account of his dear mother; and if it had pleased Heaven to give him some prospect of Mr Allworthy's recovery, it would become them better to express the exultations of their hearts in thanksgiving, than in drunkenness and riots; which were properer methods to encrease the Divine wrath, than to avert it.”", "그가 말하기를, “지금 이 집은 사랑하는 어머니 때문에 상중에 있는 집이거늘, 만일 하늘이 기뻐하시어 올워디 씨의 회복에 어떤 희망을 주셨다면, 우리가 마음의 환희를 표하는 데에는 술취함과 소란보다 감사 기도가 더 어울리는 법이오. 술취함과 소란은 하늘의 진노를 돌리기보다 오히려 키우는 방법에 더 가깝소.”"], ["Thwackum, who had swallowed more liquor than Jones, but without any ill effect on his brain, seconded the pious harangue of Blifil; but Square, for reasons which the reader may probably guess, was totally silent.", "존스보다 더 많은 술을 들이켰으나 머리에는 아무 해도 없던 스왜컴이 블리필의 경건한 일장 연설을 거들었다. 그러나 스퀘어는, 독자가 아마 짐작할 만한 이유로, 완전히 입을 다물고 있었다."]], [["Wine had not so totally overpowered Jones, as to prevent his recollecting Mr Blifil's loss, the moment it was mentioned.", "술이 존스를 완전히 압도한 것은 아니어서, 블리필 씨의 상실이 언급되는 순간 그는 그것을 떠올렸다. 그래서, 자신의 잘못을 인정하고 책망하는 데 그보다 더 선뜻한 사람도 없었기에, 그는 블리필 씨에게 악수를 청하며 용서를 빌었다."], ["As no person, therefore, was more ready to confess and condemn his own errors, he offered to shake Mr Blifil by the hand, and begged his pardon, saying, “His excessive joy for Mr Allworthy's recovery had driven every other thought out of his mind.”", "“올워디 씨의 회복에 대한 지나친 기쁨이 내 마음에서 다른 모든 생각을 몰아내고 말았소.” 그가 말했다."]], [["Blifil scornfully rejected his hand; and with much indignation answered, “It was little to be wondered at, if tragical spectacles made no impression on the blind; but, for his part, he had the misfortune to know who his parents were, and consequently must be affected with their loss.”", "블리필은 경멸하듯 그의 손을 뿌리치고, 분노에 차서 대답했다. “비극적인 광경이 눈먼 자에게 아무런 인상을 남기지 않는다 해도 그리 놀랄 일은 아니지. 하지만 나로 말하자면, 내 부모가 누구인지 아는 불행을 지녔으니, 그분들을 잃은 슬픔에 마음이 흔들리는 것이 당연하오.”"]], [["Jones, who, notwithstanding his good humour, had some mixture of the irascible in his constitution, leaped hastily from his chair, and catching hold of Blifil's collar, cried out, “D--n you for a rascal, do you insult me with the misfortune of my birth?” He accompanied these words with such rough actions, that they soon got the better of Mr Blifil's peaceful temper; and a scuffle immediately ensued, which might have produced mischief, had it not been prevented by the interposition of Thwackum and the physician; for the philosophy of Square rendered him superior to all emotions, and he very calmly smoaked his pipe, as was his custom in all broils, unless when he apprehended some danger of having it broke in his mouth.", "존스는 비록 쾌활한 기질이긴 했어도 그 천성에 다소 노여움을 잘 타는 면이 섞여 있던 터라, 황급히 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 블리필의 멱살을 움켜쥐며 소리쳤다. “이 악당 놈, 빌어먹을, 내 출생의 불행으로 나를 모욕하려는 거냐?” 그는 이 말에 어찌나 거친 행동을 곁들였던지, 그것이 이내 블리필 씨의 평화로운 기질마저 압도하고 말았다. 곧바로 한바탕 몸싸움이 벌어졌고, 만일 스왜컴과 의사가 끼어들어 말리지 않았다면 사달이 났을 것이다. 스퀘어로 말하자면, 그의 철학이 그를 모든 감정 위에 올려놓은지라, 그는 모든 난투극에서 그러하듯 매우 침착하게 파이프 담배를 피우고 있었다. 다만 입에 물린 파이프가 부러질 위험이 있다고 느껴질 때만은 예외였다."]], [["The combatants being now prevented from executing present vengeance on each other, betook themselves to the common resources of disappointed rage, and vented their wrath in threats and defiance.", "이제 서로에게 당장의 복수를 실행하지 못하게 된 두 싸움꾼은 좌절된 분노가 으레 기대는 수단으로 돌아서서, 위협과 도전으로 화를 풀어냈다."], ["In this kind of conflict, Fortune, which, in the personal attack, seemed to incline to Jones, was now altogether as favourable to his enemy.", "이런 종류의 다툼에서는, 몸으로 맞붙었을 때 존스 쪽으로 기우는 듯하던 운수가 이제는 그의 적에게 못지않게 우호적이었다."]], [["A truce, nevertheless, was at length agreed on, by the mediation of the neutral parties, and the whole company again sat down at the table; where Jones being prevailed on to ask pardon, and Blifil to give it, peace was restored, and everything seemed in statu quo.", "그렇지만 결국 중립을 지킨 이들의 중재로 휴전이 합의되었고, 좌중은 다시 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 거기서 존스는 용서를 구하도록, 블리필은 그것을 받아들이도록 설득되어, 평화가 회복되었고 모든 것이 본래의 상태로 돌아간 듯 보였다."]], [["But though the quarrel was, in all appearance, perfectly reconciled, the good humour which had been interrupted by it, was by no means restored.", "그러나 다툼은 겉보기에 완전히 화해되었으되, 그 다툼으로 끊겼던 흥겨움은 결코 되살아나지 않았다."], ["All merriment was now at an end, and the subsequent discourse consisted only of grave relations of matters of fact, and of as grave observations upon them; a species of conversation, in which, though there is much of dignity and instruction, there is but little entertainment.", "모든 즐거움이 이제 끝나버렸고, 뒤이은 대화는 다만 사실에 관한 진지한 이야기와, 그에 못지않게 진지한 논평으로만 채워졌다. 이런 종류의 대화에는 품위와 교훈은 많을지언정 재미는 거의 없는 법이다."], ["As we presume therefore to convey only this last to the reader, we shall pass by whatever was said, till the rest of the company having by degrees dropped off, left only Square and the physician together; at which time the conversation was a little heightened by some comments on what had happened between the two young gentlemen; both of whom the doctor declared to be no better than scoundrels; to which appellation the philosopher, very sagaciously shaking his head, agreed.", "그러므로 우리는 그 마지막 것만을 독자에게 전하려 하니, 좌중의 나머지 사람들이 하나둘 자리를 뜨고 마침내 스퀘어와 의사만 남을 때까지 오간 말은 모두 건너뛰겠다. 그때쯤 두 젊은이 사이에 벌어진 일에 대한 몇 마디 논평으로 대화가 조금 달아올랐는데, 의사는 두 사람 다 악당에 지나지 않는다고 단언했고, 그 호칭에 철학자도 자못 현명하게 고개를 끄덕이며 동의했다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["Showing the truth of many observations of Ovid, and of other more grave writers, who have proved beyond contradiction, that wine is often the forerunner of incontinency.", "오비디우스를 비롯해 더 근엄한 여러 저술가들의 관찰이 참되다는 것을 보여 주는 장. 그들은 술이 흔히 음란함의 앞잡이가 된다는 사실을, 반박할 여지 없이 증명해 보였다."]], [["Jones retired from the company, in which we have seen him engaged, into the fields, where he intended to cool himself by a walk in the open air before he attended Mr Allworthy. There, whilst he renewed those meditations on his dear Sophia, which the dangerous illness of his friend and benefactor had for some time interrupted, an accident happened, which with sorrow we relate, and with sorrow doubtless will it be read; however, that historic truth to which we profess so inviolable an attachment, obliges us to communicate it to posterity.", "존스는 우리가 앞서 그가 함께 있는 모습을 보았던 무리에서 빠져나와 들판으로 나갔다. 올워디 씨를 모시러 가기 전에 탁 트인 바깥 공기 속을 거닐며 머리를 식힐 작정이었다. 거기서 그는, 벗이자 은인의 위태로운 병환으로 한동안 끊겨 있던, 사랑하는 소피아에 대한 사색을 다시 이어 갔다. 그런데 그때 한 사건이 일어났다. 우리는 슬픔에 잠겨 이를 전하며, 읽는 이들 또한 분명 슬픔에 잠겨 읽게 될 것이다. 그러나 우리가 그토록 어길 수 없이 떠받든다고 공언한 역사적 진실이, 이 일을 후세에 전하도록 우리를 떠밀고 있다."]], [["It was now a pleasant evening in the latter end of June, when our heroe was walking in a most delicious grove, where the gentle breezes fanning the leaves, together with the sweet trilling of a murmuring stream, and the melodious notes of nightingales, formed altogether the most enchanting harmony.", "때는 6월 말의 어느 흐뭇한 저녁이었고, 우리의 주인공은 더없이 감미로운 숲속을 거닐고 있었다. 그곳에서는 부드러운 산들바람이 나뭇잎을 어루만지고, 졸졸 흐르는 시냇물의 달콤한 떨림과 밤꾀꼬리의 가락 고운 노랫소리가 한데 어우러져 더없이 황홀한 화음을 이루고 있었다."], ["In this scene, so sweetly accommodated to love, he meditated on his dear Sophia.", "사랑에 그토록 달콤하게 어울리는 이 정경 속에서 그는 사랑하는 소피아를 떠올렸다."], ["While his wanton fancy roamed unbounded over all her beauties, and his lively imagination painted the charming maid in various ravishing forms, his warm heart melted with tenderness; and at length, throwing himself on the ground, by the side of a gently murmuring brook, he broke forth into the following ejaculation:", "그의 들뜬 공상이 그녀의 온갖 아름다움 위를 거침없이 떠돌고, 그의 생생한 상상이 그 매혹적인 처녀를 갖가지 황홀한 모습으로 그려 내자, 그의 뜨거운 가슴은 애틋함으로 녹아내렸다. 마침내 그는 잔잔히 속삭이는 시냇가에 몸을 던지듯 누워, 이렇게 마음을 토해 냈다."]], [["“O Sophia, would Heaven give thee to my arms, how blest would be my condition!", "\"오, 소피아여, 하늘이 그대를 내 품에 안겨 준다면 내 처지가 얼마나 축복받은 것이랴!"], ["Curst be that fortune which sets a distance between us.", "우리 사이에 거리를 둔 저 운명에 저주가 있을지어다."], ["Was I but possessed of thee, one only suit of rags thy whole estate, is there a man on earth whom I would envy!", "그대만 내 것이 된다면, 누더기 한 벌이 그대 재산의 전부라 한들, 이 세상에 내가 부러워할 사람이 어디 있겠는가!"], ["How contemptible would the brightest Circassian beauty, drest in all the jewels of the Indies, appear to my eyes!", "인도의 온갖 보석으로 치장한 가장 빛나는 키르카시아의 미인인들 내 눈엔 얼마나 하찮아 보이랴!"], ["But why do I mention another woman?", "한데 내가 어찌 다른 여인을 입에 올리는가?"], ["Could I think my eyes capable of looking at any other with tenderness, these hands should tear them from my head.", "이 눈이 다른 누군가를 애틋한 눈길로 바라볼 수 있다 여겨진다면, 이 손으로 그 눈을 머리에서 뽑아 버리리라."], ["No, my Sophia, if cruel fortune separates us for ever, my soul shall doat on thee alone.", "아니, 나의 소피아여, 모진 운명이 우리를 영영 갈라놓더라도, 내 영혼은 오직 그대만을 흠모하리라."], ["The chastest constancy will I ever preserve to thy image.", "그대의 모습에 가장 순결한 절개를 길이 지키리라."], ["Though I should never have possession of thy charming person, still shalt thou alone have possession of my thoughts, my love, my soul.", "내 비록 그대의 매혹적인 몸을 결코 차지하지 못할지라도, 그대만은 여전히 나의 생각과 사랑과 영혼을 차지하리라."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["my fond heart is so wrapt in that tender bosom, that the brightest beauties would for me have no charms, nor would a hermit be colder in their embraces.", "나의 애틋한 가슴은 그 부드러운 품에 어찌나 휘감겨 있는지, 가장 빛나는 미인들도 내겐 아무 매력이 없고, 그들의 품에 안긴 은수자라도 나보다 더 차갑지는 않으리라."], ["Sophia, Sophia alone shall be mine.", "소피아, 오직 소피아만이 내 것이 되리라."], ["What raptures are in that name!", "그 이름 속에 깃든 황홀함이여!"], ["I will engrave it on every tree.”", "나는 그 이름을 나무란 나무마다 새기리라.\""]], [["At these words he started up, and beheld--not his Sophia--no, nor a Circassian maid richly and elegantly attired for the grand Signior's seraglio.", "이 말과 함께 그는 벌떡 일어나, 바라보았다. 그러나 그것은 그의 소피아가 아니었다."], ["No; without a gown, in a shift that was somewhat of the coarsest, and none of the cleanest, bedewed likewise with some odoriferous effluvia, the produce of the day's labour, with a pitchfork in her hand, Molly Seagrim approached.", "아니, 술탄의 후궁을 위해 화려하고 우아하게 차려입은 키르카시아 처녀도 아니었다. 천만에."], ["Our hero had his penknife in his hand, which he had drawn for the before-mentioned purpose of carving on the bark; when the girl coming near him, cryed out with a smile, “You don't intend to kill me, squire, I hope!”--“Why should you think I would kill you?”", "겉옷도 걸치지 않은 채, 다소 거칠고 그다지 깨끗하지도 않은 속옷 차림에, 게다가 하루 일의 산물인 향긋하달 수 없는 냄새까지 풍기며, 손에 쇠스랑을 든 몰리 시그림이 다가오고 있었다. 우리의 주인공은 앞서 말한 대로 나무껍질에 이름을 새기려고 펼쳐 둔 주머니칼을 손에 쥐고 있었는데, 그 처녀가 가까이 다가오며 미소 띤 얼굴로 소리쳤다."], ["answered Jones.", "\"도련님, 설마 절 죽이실 작정은 아니시겠죠!\" \"내가 왜 너를 죽일 거라 생각하느냐?\""], ["“Nay,”", "존스가 대답했다. \"원,\""], ["replied she, “after your cruel usage of me when I saw you last, killing me would, perhaps, be too great kindness for me to expect.”", "그녀가 받아쳤다. \"지난번에 절 만나셨을 때 그토록 모질게 대하셨으니, 절 죽여 주시는 거라면 어쩌면 제가 바라기엔 너무 큰 친절이겠죠.\""]], [["Here ensued a parley, which, as I do not think myself obliged to relate it, I shall omit.", "여기서 한바탕 옥신각신이 이어졌는데, 굳이 그것을 전할 의무가 있다고 여기지 않으므로 나는 생략하겠다."], ["It is sufficient that it lasted a full quarter of an hour, at the conclusion of which they retired into the thickest part of the grove.", "그것이 꼬박 십오 분이나 이어졌고, 그 끝에 두 사람이 숲에서 가장 우거진 곳으로 물러갔다는 것만으로 충분하다."]], [["Some of my readers may be inclined to think this event unnatural.", "내 독자 가운데 더러는 이 사건이 부자연스럽다고 여기고픈 마음이 들지도 모르겠다. 그러나 사실은 사실이다."], ["However, the fact is true; and perhaps may be sufficiently accounted for by suggesting, that Jones probably thought one woman better than none, and Molly as probably imagined two men to be better than one.", "그리고 어쩌면 이렇게 짚어 주면 충분히 설명이 될 것이다. 존스는 아마 여자가 하나라도 있는 편이 아예 없는 것보다 낫다고 여겼을 테고, 몰리 또한 사내가 하나보다는 둘인 편이 낫다고 짐작했을 것이라고."], ["Besides the before-mentioned motive assigned to the present behaviour of Jones, the reader will be likewise pleased to recollect in his favour, that he was not at this time perfect master of that wonderful power of reason, which so well enables grave and wise men to subdue their unruly passions, and to decline any of these prohibited amusements.", "앞서 존스의 이번 행동에 부여한 동기 외에도, 독자께서는 그에게 유리하게 다음을 떠올려 주시면 기쁘겠다. 그는 이때 그 놀라운 이성의 힘을 온전히 다스리는 처지가 아니었다는 것을."], ["Wine now had totally subdued this power in Jones.", "근엄하고 지혜로운 사람들이 그 힘 덕분에 다스릴 길 없는 정념을 잘도 억누르고, 이런 금지된 즐길 거리들을 마다하게 되는, 바로 그 힘 말이다. 이제 술이 존스의 그 힘을 완전히 꺾어 버렸다."], ["He was, indeed, in a condition, in which, if reason had interposed, though only to advise, she might have received the answer which one Cleostratus gave many years ago to a silly fellow, who asked him, if he was not ashamed to be drunk?", "그는 실로, 이성이 끼어든다 한들, 그저 충고만 하려 들더라도, 옛날 클레오스트라투스가 어느 어리석은 자에게 돌려준 그 대답을 받기 십상인 처지였다. 그 어리석은 자가 그에게 술 취한 게 부끄럽지 않으냐고 묻자, 클레오스트라투스는 이렇게 말했다."], ["“Are not you,”", "\"그대는 술 취한 사람에게 훈계하는 게 부끄럽지 않은가?\""], ["said Cleostratus, “ashamed to admonish a drunken man?”--To say the truth, in a court of justice drunkenness must not be an excuse, yet in a court of conscience it is greatly so; and therefore Aristotle, who commends the laws of Pittacus, by which drunken men received double punishment for their crimes, allows there is more of policy than justice in that law.", "사실대로 말하자면, 법정에서야 만취가 변명이 되어서는 안 되겠으나, 양심의 법정에서는 크게 변명이 된다. 그래서 아리스토텔레스는 피타쿠스의 법, 곧 술 취한 자가 저지른 죄에 갑절의 벌을 내리던 그 법을 칭찬하면서도, 그 법에는 정의보다 정략이 더 많이 담겼음을 인정한다."], ["Now, if there are any transgressions pardonable from drunkenness, they are certainly such as Mr Jones was at present guilty of; on which head I could pour forth a vast profusion of learning, if I imagined it would either entertain my reader, or teach him anything more than he knows already.", "자, 만취 때문에 용서받을 만한 잘못이 있다면, 그것은 분명 지금 존스 씨가 저지른 것과 같은 종류의 잘못이다. 이 대목을 두고 나는 방대한 학식을 한껏 쏟아부을 수도 있겠으나, 그것이 내 독자를 즐겁게 하거나 그가 이미 아는 것 이상을 가르쳐 주리라 여겨지지 않는다."], ["For his sake therefore I shall keep my learning to myself, and return to my history.", "그러니 독자를 위해 나는 내 학식을 혼자 간직하고, 다시 이야기로 돌아가겠다."]], [["It hath been observed, that Fortune seldom doth things by halves.", "운명의 여신은 좀처럼 일을 어중간하게 하는 법이 없다는 말이 있다."], ["To say truth, there is no end to her freaks whenever she is disposed to gratify or displease.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그녀가 누군가를 흡족하게 하거나 언짢게 하기로 마음먹으면 그 변덕에는 끝이 없다."], ["No sooner had our heroe retired with his Dido, but", "우리의 주인공이 자신의 디도와 함께 물러나기가 무섭게,"]], [["Speluncam Blifil dux et divinus eandem Deveniunt--", "같은 동굴로 블리필 대장과 거룩한 점쟁이가\n이르렀으니—"]], [["the parson and the young squire, who were taking a serious walk, arrived at the stile which leads into the grove, and the latter caught a view of the lovers just as they were sinking out of sight.", "진지하게 산책을 하던 목사와 젊은 도련님이 숲으로 통하는 층계 울짱에 다다랐고, 그 가운데 도련님이 연인들이 막 시야에서 사라지려는 바로 그 순간 그들의 모습을 흘끗 보았던 것이다."]], [["Blifil knew Jones very well, though he was at above a hundred yards' distance, and he was as positive to the sex of his companion, though not to the individual person.", "블리필은 백 야드 넘게 떨어져 있었음에도 존스를 아주 잘 알아보았고, 그 동행이 어느 성별인지도, 비록 누구인지까지는 아니어도, 그만큼 확신했다."], ["He started, blessed himself, and uttered a very solemn ejaculation.", "그는 흠칫 놀라 성호를 긋고는, 자못 엄숙하게 한마디를 내뱉었다."]], [["Thwackum expressed some surprize at these sudden emotions, and asked the reason of them.", "스웨컴은 이 느닷없는 동요에 다소 놀라며 그 까닭을 물었다. 블리필은 이렇게 대답했다."], ["To which Blifil answered, “He was certain he had seen a fellow and wench retire together among the bushes, which he doubted not was with some wicked purpose.”", "\"사내 하나와 계집 하나가 함께 덤불 속으로 물러가는 걸 분명히 보았는데, 무슨 못된 속셈이 있는 게 틀림없습니다.\""], ["As to the name of Jones, he thought proper to conceal it, and why he did so must be left to the judgment of the sagacious reader; for we never chuse to assign motives to the actions of men, when there is any possibility of our being mistaken.", "다만 존스의 이름은 감추는 편이 낫다고 여겼는데, 그가 왜 그랬는지는 명민한 독자의 판단에 맡겨 두는 수밖에 없다. 우리가 틀릴 가능성이 조금이라도 있을 때에는 결코 사람들의 행동에 동기를 함부로 갖다 붙이지 않기로 했으니 말이다."]], [["The parson, who was not only strictly chaste in his own person, but a great enemy to the opposite vice in all others, fired at this information.", "목사는 자신이 몸가짐에서 엄격히 정결할 뿐 아니라 다른 모든 이의 그 반대 악덕에도 큰 적이었던 터라, 이 소식에 발끈했다. 그는 블리필 씨에게 당장 그곳으로 안내하라 청했고, 다가가는 내내 한탄을 섞어 복수의 숨결을 토해 냈다."], ["He desired Mr Blifil to conduct him immediately to the place, which as he approached he breathed forth vengeance mixed with lamentations; nor did he refrain from casting some oblique reflections on Mr Allworthy; insinuating that the wickedness of the country was principally owing to the encouragement he had given to vice, by having exerted such kindness to a bastard, and by having mitigated that just and wholesome rigour of the law which allots a very severe punishment to loose wenches.", "또한 올워디 씨를 향해 비스듬한 비난을 던지는 것도 마다하지 않았다. 이 고장의 사악함은 주로 그가 사생아에게 그토록 친절을 베풀고, 헤픈 계집들에게 아주 혹독한 벌을 매기는 그 정당하고 유익한 법의 엄정함을 누그러뜨려, 악덕을 부추긴 탓이라고 넌지시 빗대었다."]], [["The way through which our hunters were to pass in pursuit of their game was so beset with briars, that it greatly obstructed their walk, and caused besides such a rustling, that Jones had sufficient warning of their arrival before they could surprize him; nay, indeed, so incapable was Thwackum of concealing his indignation, and such vengeance did he mutter forth every step he took, that this alone must have abundantly satisfied Jones that he was (to use the language of sportsmen) found sitting.", "우리의 사냥꾼들이 사냥감을 쫓아 지나가야 할 길은 가시덤불이 어찌나 무성하던지 걸음을 크게 가로막았고, 게다가 어찌나 부스럭 소리를 냈던지, 존스는 그들이 그를 덮치기도 전에 충분히 그 도착을 미리 알아챘다. 아니, 실로 스웨컴은 자신의 분노를 감추는 데 어찌나 서툴렀던지, 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 어찌나 복수를 중얼거렸던지, 이것만으로도 존스는 (사냥꾼들의 말투를 빌리자면) 자신이 앉아 있는 채로 발각되었음을 충분히 알아챘을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter xi.", "# Chapter xi."]], [["In which a simile in Mr Pope's period of a mile introduces as bloody a battle as can possibly be fought without the assistance of steel or cold iron.", "파이프 씨의 시 한 구절에 나오는 비유가 강철이나 차디찬 쇠붙이의 도움 없이 벌어질 수 있는 가장 피비린내 나는 전투를 끌어들이는 장."]], [["As in the season of rutting (an uncouth phrase, by which the vulgar denote that gentle dalliance, which in the well-wooded[*] forest of Hampshire, passes between lovers of the ferine kind), if, while the lofty-crested stag meditates the amorous sport, a couple of puppies, or any other beasts of hostile note, should wander so near the temple of Venus Ferina that the fair hind should shrink from the place, touched with that somewhat, either of fear or frolic, of nicety or skittishness, with which nature hath bedecked all females, or hath at least instructed them how to put it on; lest, through the indelicacy of males, the Samean mysteries should be pryed into by unhallowed eyes: for, at the celebration of these rites, the female priestess cries out with her in Virgil (who was then, probably, hard at work on such celebration),", "발정기에는—이 거친 말은 햄프셔의 우거진[*] 숲에서 야성을 지닌 연인들 사이에 오가는 그 점잖은 희롱을 두고 속된 무리가 쓰는 표현이다—만일 높다란 뿔을 곤두세운 수사슴이 그 사랑의 유희를 궁리하는 동안, 강아지 두 마리나 그 밖에 적의를 품은 어떤 짐승이 야성의 비너스 신전 가까이 어슬렁거려, 아름다운 암사슴이 자연이 모든 암컷에게 입혀 두었거나 적어도 어떻게 두를지 일러 둔 그 무엇—두려움이든 장난기든, 까다로움이든 변덕이든—에 닿아 그 자리에서 움찔 물러난다면, 그것은 수컷들의 무례함으로 인해 사메의 신비가 신성치 못한 눈에 엿보이지나 않을까 함이다. 이 의식을 거행할 때, 여사제는 베르길리우스에 나오는 그녀와 더불어 (그 시인은 아마 그때 그런 거행에 한창 몰두해 있었으리라) 이렇게 외치기 때문이다. * 사메의 신비: 고대 밀교의 비의(秘儀)를 가리킨다."]], [["--Procul, o procul este, profani; Proclamat vates, totoque absistite luco.", "—멀리, 오 멀리 물러가라, 부정한 자들이여,\n무녀가 외치니, 온 숲에서 떠나라."]], [["--Far hence be souls profane, The sibyl cry'd, and from the grove abstain.--DRYDEN.", "—부정한 영혼들아 여기서 멀리 떠나라,\n무녀가 외쳤으니, 그 숲에서 물러나라.—드라이든."]], [["[*] This is an ambiguous phrase, and may mean either a forest well cloathed with wood, or well stript of it.", "[*] 이것은 뜻이 두 갈래인 표현으로, 나무로 잘 뒤덮인 숲을 뜻할 수도 있고, 나무가 말끔히 베어져 헐벗은 숲을 뜻할 수도 있다."]], [["If, I say, while these sacred rites, which are in common to genus omne animantium, are in agitation between the stag and his mistress, any hostile beasts should venture too near, on the first hint given by the frighted hind, fierce and tremendous rushes forth the stag to the entrance of the thicket; there stands he centinel over his love, stamps the ground with his foot, and with his horns brandished aloft in air, proudly provokes the apprehended foe to combat.", "내 말인즉, 모든 살아 있는 것에 공통된 이 신성한 의식이 수사슴과 그 애인 사이에서 한창 무르익는 동안, 어떤 적대적인 짐승이 너무 가까이 다가오는 모험을 감행한다면, 겁먹은 암사슴이 처음 신호를 보내기가 무섭게 수사슴은 사납고 무시무시하게 덤불 어귀로 뛰쳐나간다. 거기서 그는 제 사랑을 지키는 보초로 서서, 발로 땅을 구르고, 뿔을 허공 높이 휘두르며, 다가오는 적에게 당당히 싸움을 걸어 도발하는 것이다."]], [["Thus, and more terrible, when he perceived the enemy's approach, leaped forth our heroe.", "이처럼, 아니 그보다 더 무섭게, 적이 다가오는 것을 알아챈 우리의 주인공은 펄쩍 뛰쳐나왔다."], ["Many a step advanced he forwards, in order to conceal the trembling hind, and, if possible, to secure her retreat.", "그는 떨고 있는 암사슴을 감추고, 될 수 있다면 그녀가 물러날 길을 지켜 주려고 앞으로 여러 걸음 나아갔다."], ["And now Thwackum, having first darted some livid lightning from his fiery eyes, began to thunder forth, “Fie upon it!", "그러자 스왜컴은 먼저 그 불타는 두 눈에서 시퍼런 번갯불을 쏘아 보내더니, 천둥처럼 호통치기 시작했다. \"이런 망측한!"], ["Fie upon it!", "망측한 일이로다!"], ["Mr Jones.", "존스 군."], ["Is it possible you should be the person?”--“You see,”", "자네가 정말 그 장본인일 수 있단 말인가?\"—\"보시다시피,\""], ["answered Jones, “it is possible I should be here.”--“And who,”", "존스가 대답했다. \"제가 여기 있을 수도 있는 일이지요.\"—\"그리고 자네와 함께 있는 저 고약한 계집은 누구냐?\""], ["said Thwackum, “is that wicked slut with you?”--“If I have any wicked slut with me,”", "스왜컴이 말했다.—\"제게 무슨 고약한 계집이 있다 한들,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “it is possible I shall not let you know who she is.”--“I command you to tell me immediately,”", "\"그게 누구인지 선생님께 알려 드리지 않을 수도 있지요.\"—\"당장 말하라고 명한다,\" 스왜컴이 말했다."], ["says Thwackum: “and I would not have you imagine, young man, that your age, though it hath somewhat abridged the purpose of tuition, hath totally taken away the authority of the master.", "\"그리고 젊은이, 자네 나이가 비록 가르침의 목적을 다소 줄여 놓았다 해서, 스승의 권위마저 송두리째 앗아 갔다고는 생각지 말게. 스승과 제자의 관계는 지워지지 않는 것이야."], ["The relation of the master and scholar is indelible; as, indeed, all other relations are; for they all derive their original from heaven.", "실로 다른 모든 관계가 그러하듯이 말일세. 그 모든 관계는 하늘에서 그 근원을 받아 왔으니까."], ["I would have you think yourself, therefore, as much obliged to obey me now, as when I taught you your first rudiments.”--“I believe you would,”", "그러니 자네는 내가 처음 자네에게 기초를 가르치던 때와 똑같이, 지금도 나에게 복종할 의무가 있다고 여겨야 할 게야.\"—\"그러시겠지요,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones; “but that will not happen, unless you had the same birchen argument to convince me.”--“Then I must tell you plainly,”", "\"하지만 저를 납득시킬 그 자작나무 회초리의 논거를 똑같이 갖추지 않으신 이상, 그런 일은 없을 겁니다.\"—\"그렇다면 분명히 말해 두겠네,\" 스왜컴이 말했다."], ["said Thwackum, “I am resolved to discover the wicked wretch.”--“And I must tell you plainly,”", "\"나는 그 고약한 년의 정체를 기어이 밝혀내고야 말겠다.\"—\"그럼 저도 분명히 말씀드리지요,\""], ["returned Jones, “I am resolved you shall not.”", "존스가 받아쳤다. \"저는 기어이 그러지 못하게 하겠습니다.\""], ["Thwackum then offered to advance, and Jones laid hold of his arms; which Mr Blifil endeavoured to rescue, declaring, “he would not see his old master insulted.”", "그러자 스왜컴이 앞으로 나아가려 했고, 존스는 그의 팔을 붙잡았다. 블리필이 이를 떼어 놓으려 애쓰며, \"내 옛 스승이 모욕당하는 꼴은 두고 볼 수 없다\"고 외쳤다."]], [["Jones now finding himself engaged with two, thought it necessary to rid himself of one of his antagonists as soon as possible.", "이제 둘을 한꺼번에 상대하게 된 존스는, 가능한 한 빨리 적 가운데 하나를 처치하는 것이 필요하겠다고 판단했다."], ["He therefore applied to the weakest first; and, letting the parson go, he directed a blow at the young squire's breast, which luckily taking place, reduced him to measure his length on the ground.", "그래서 그는 먼저 가장 약한 쪽을 공략하기로 하고, 목사를 놓아 준 다음 젊은 지주의 가슴을 향해 한 방을 날렸다. 그 일격이 운 좋게 제대로 들어가, 블리필은 땅바닥에 제 키만큼 길게 뻗고 말았다."]], [["Thwackum was so intent on the discovery, that, the moment he found himself at liberty, he stept forward directly into the fern, without any great consideration of what might in the meantime befal his friend; but he had advanced a very few paces into the thicket, before Jones, having defeated Blifil, overtook the parson, and dragged him backward by the skirt of his coat.", "스왜컴은 정체를 밝히는 데 어찌나 골몰해 있었던지, 자유로워졌다 싶은 순간 그새 제 친구에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지는 별로 생각지도 않고 곧장 양치식물 덤불 속으로 발을 들였다. 그러나 그가 덤불 안으로 몇 걸음 채 나아가기도 전에, 블리필을 쓰러뜨린 존스가 목사를 따라잡아 그의 외투 자락을 잡고 뒤로 끌어당겼다."]], [["This parson had been a champion in his youth, and had won much honour by his fist, both at school and at the university.", "이 목사는 젊은 시절에 한가락 하던 투사로, 학교에서나 대학에서나 주먹으로 적잖은 명예를 얻은 사람이었다."], ["He had now indeed, for a great number of years, declined the practice of that noble art; yet was his courage full as strong as his faith, and his body no less strong than either.", "물론 이제는 오랜 세월 그 고귀한 기술의 연마를 게을리해 왔다. 그래도 그의 용기는 그의 믿음만큼이나 굳셌고, 그의 몸뚱이도 그 둘 못지않게 단단했다."], ["He was moreover, as the reader may perhaps have conceived, somewhat irascible in his nature.", "게다가 독자도 이미 짐작했을 테지만, 그는 성미가 다소 발끈하는 편이었다."], ["When he looked back, therefore, and saw his friend stretched out on the ground, and found himself at the same time so roughly handled by one who had formerly been only passive in all conflicts between them (a circumstance which highly aggravated the whole), his patience at length gave way; he threw himself into a posture of offence; and collecting all his force, attacked Jones in the front with as much impetuosity as he had formerly attacked him in the rear.", "그러니 그가 뒤를 돌아보아 친구가 땅바닥에 뻗어 있는 것을 보고, 동시에 자기가 예전 둘 사이의 모든 다툼에서 늘 당하기만 하던 자에게 이토록 거칠게 다루어지고 있음을 깨달았을 때 (이 사정이 사태 전체를 한층 더 부아 나게 했다), 마침내 그의 인내심도 무너지고 말았다. 그는 공격 자세를 취하더니, 온 힘을 그러모아 예전에 뒤에서 존스를 덮쳤던 그 기세 그대로 이번에는 정면에서 존스에게 달려들었다."]], [["Our heroe received the enemy's attack with the most undaunted intrepidity, and his bosom resounded with the blow.", "우리의 주인공은 더없이 의연한 담대함으로 적의 공격을 받아 냈고, 그 일격에 그의 가슴이 쿵 하고 울렸다."], ["This he presently returned with no less violence, aiming likewise at the parson's breast; but he dexterously drove down the fist of Jones, so that it reached only his belly, where two pounds of beef and as many of pudding were then deposited, and whence consequently no hollow sound could proceed.", "그는 즉시 못지않은 사나움으로 되갚으며 역시 목사의 가슴을 노렸지만, 목사가 능숙하게 존스의 주먹을 아래로 쳐 내는 바람에 주먹은 그의 배에만 닿고 말았다. 그 배에는 그때 쇠고기 두 파운드와 푸딩 두 파운드가 들어앉아 있던 터라, 당연히 빈 소리 따위는 울려 나올 리 없었다."], ["Many lusty blows, much more pleasant as well as easy to have seen, than to read or describe, were given on both sides: at last a violent fall, in which Jones had thrown his knees into Thwackum's breast, so weakened the latter, that victory had been no longer dubious, had not Blifil, who had now recovered his strength, again renewed the fight, and by engaging with Jones, given the parson a moment's time to shake his ears, and to regain his breath.", "양쪽에서 우람한 주먹질이 숱하게 오갔으니, 그것은 읽거나 묘사하기보다 직접 보는 편이 훨씬 흥겹고 또한 수월한 광경이었다. 마침내 존스가 무릎을 스왜컴의 가슴팍에 처박는 격렬한 한판이 벌어져 목사를 어찌나 약하게 만들었던지, 이제 막 기운을 되찾은 블리필이 다시 싸움에 끼어들어 존스와 맞붙어 줌으로써 목사에게 귀를 털고 숨을 고를 잠깐의 틈을 주지 않았더라면, 승부는 더 이상 의심할 여지가 없었을 것이다."]], [["And now both together attacked our heroe, whose blows did not retain that force with which they had fallen at first, so weakened was he by his combat with Thwackum; for though the pedagogue chose rather to play solos on the human instrument, and had been lately used to those only, yet he still retained enough of his antient knowledge to perform his part very well in a duet.", "이제 두 사람이 한꺼번에 우리의 주인공을 공격해 왔는데, 스왜컴과의 싸움으로 어찌나 지쳐 있었던지 존스의 주먹은 처음 내리꽂힐 때의 그 힘을 더는 간직하지 못했다. 비록 이 훈장은 인간이라는 악기로 독주를 켜는 쪽을 더 즐겨, 근래에는 오직 그것에만 익숙해져 있었으나, 그래도 이중주에서 자기 몫을 썩 잘 해낼 만큼의 옛 솜씨는 여태 지니고 있었던 것이다."]], [["The victory, according to modern custom, was like to be decided by numbers, when, on a sudden, a fourth pair of fists appeared in the battle, and immediately paid their compliments to the parson; and the owner of them at the same time crying out, “Are not you ashamed, and be d--n'd to you, to fall two of you upon one?”", "요즘 풍습대로 승부는 머릿수로 결판이 날 듯하던 차에, 별안간 네 번째 주먹 한 쌍이 싸움판에 나타나 곧장 목사에게 인사를 차렸다. 그 주먹의 임자는 그와 동시에 이렇게 고함쳤다. \"둘이서 하나한테 달려들다니, 이 빌어먹을 놈들, 부끄럽지도 않으냐?\""]], [["The battle, which was of the kind that for distinction's sake is called royal, now raged with the utmost violence during a few minutes; till Blifil being a second time laid sprawling by Jones, Thwackum condescended to apply for quarter to his new antagonist, who was now found to be Mr Western himself; for in the heat of the action none of the combatants had recognized him.", "이제 그 싸움은—구별을 위해 난전(亂戰)이라 부르는 종류였는데—몇 분 동안 더없이 격렬하게 타올랐다. 그러다 블리필이 두 번째로 존스에게 나가떨어지자, 스왜컴은 자기 새 적수에게 항복을 청하기에 이르렀다. 그 적수가 다름 아닌 웨스턴 씨 본인임이 그제야 밝혀졌으니, 격렬한 난투 와중에 어느 싸움꾼도 그를 알아보지 못했던 것이다."]], [["In fact, that honest squire, happening, in his afternoon's walk with some company, to pass through the field where the bloody battle was fought, and having concluded, from seeing three men engaged, that two of them must be on a side, he hastened from his companions, and with more gallantry than policy, espoused the cause of the weaker party.", "사실인즉, 이 정직한 지주는 그날 오후 몇몇 일행과 산책을 하다가 마침 그 피비린내 나는 싸움이 벌어진 들판을 지나게 되었는데, 세 사람이 엉겨 붙어 있는 것을 보고는 그중 둘은 한편일 것이라고 단정 짓고는, 일행에게서 부리나케 달려와 분별보다는 객기로 더 약한 쪽 편을 들었던 것이다."], ["By which generous proceeding he very probably prevented Mr Jones from becoming a victim to the wrath of Thwackum, and to the pious friendship which Blifil bore his old master; for, besides the disadvantage of such odds, Jones had not yet sufficiently recovered the former strength of his broken arm.", "이 너그러운 행동 덕분에 그는 십중팔구 존스가 스왜컴의 분노와, 블리필이 옛 스승에게 품은 그 경건한 우정의 제물이 되는 것을 막아 준 셈이었다. 그런 수적 열세의 불리함은 차치하고라도, 존스는 부러졌던 팔의 예전 힘을 아직 충분히 되찾지 못한 터였기 때문이다."], ["This reinforcement, however, soon put an end to the action, and Jones with his ally obtained the victory.", "어쨌든 이 원군이 가세하자 싸움은 이내 끝이 났고, 존스는 자기 동맹과 더불어 승리를 거두었다."]], [["# Chapter xii.", "# Chapter xii."]], [["In which is seen a more moving spectacle than all the blood in the bodies of Thwackum and Blifil, and of twenty other such, is capable of producing.", "써그럼과 블리필을 비롯해 그런 무리 스무 명의 몸속에 흐르는 피를 다 합친 것보다도 더 가슴을 뭉클하게 하는 광경이 펼쳐지는 장."]], [["The rest of Mr Western's company were now come up, being just at the instant when the action was over.", "이제 웨스턴 씨 일행의 나머지 사람들이 막 도착했는데, 마침 싸움이 끝나는 바로 그 순간이었다."], ["These were the honest clergyman, whom we have formerly seen at Mr Western's table; Mrs Western, the aunt of Sophia; and lastly, the lovely Sophia herself.", "이들은 앞서 웨스턴 씨의 식탁에서 본 적 있는 그 정직한 목사와, 소피아의 고모인 웨스턴 부인, 그리고 마지막으로 사랑스러운 소피아 본인이었다."]], [["At this time, the following was the aspect of the bloody field.", "이때 피로 물든 그 전장의 모습은 다음과 같았다."], ["In one place lay on the ground, all pale, and almost breathless, the vanquished Blifil.", "한쪽에는 패배한 블리필이 온통 창백한 얼굴로, 거의 숨도 쉬지 못한 채 땅바닥에 쓰러져 있었다."], ["Near him stood the conqueror Jones, almost covered with blood, part of which was naturally his own, and part had been lately the property of the Reverend Mr Thwackum.", "그 곁에는 승리자 존스가 거의 온몸을 피로 뒤덮은 채 서 있었는데, 그 피의 일부는 당연히 그 자신의 것이었고, 일부는 얼마 전까지 써그럼 목사의 것이었다."], ["In a third place stood the said Thwackum, like King Porus, sullenly submitting to the conqueror.", "세 번째 자리에는 그 써그럼이 마치 포루스 왕처럼 시무룩하게 정복자에게 굴복한 채 서 있었다."], ["The last figure in the piece was Western the Great, most gloriously forbearing the vanquished foe.", "이 그림의 마지막 인물은 위대한 웨스턴으로, 패배한 적을 더없이 영광스럽게 너그러이 봐주고 있었다."]], [["Blifil, in whom there was little sign of life, was at first the principal object of the concern of every one, and particularly of Mrs Western, who had drawn from her pocket a bottle of hartshorn, and was herself about to apply it to his nostrils, when on a sudden the attention of the whole company was diverted from poor Blifil, whose spirit, if it had any such design, might have now taken an opportunity of stealing off to the other world, without any ceremony.", "생기라곤 거의 보이지 않던 블리필이 처음에는 모두의 관심을 한 몸에 받는 주된 대상이었고, 특히 웨스턴 부인이 그러했다. 부인은 주머니에서 각성제 한 병을 꺼내 직접 그의 콧구멍에 갖다 대려던 참이었다. 그런데 갑자기 일행 전체의 주의가 가엾은 블리필에게서 멀어지고 말았으니, 만일 그의 혼이 그럴 작정이었다면 이 틈을 타 아무런 격식도 없이 저세상으로 슬쩍 빠져나갈 기회를 얻었을지도 모른다."]], [["For now a more melancholy and a more lovely object lay motionless before them.", "이제 더 애처롭고 더 사랑스러운 대상이 그들 앞에 꼼짝없이 누워 있었기 때문이다."], ["This was no other than the charming Sophia herself, who, from the sight of blood, or from fear for her father, or from some other reason, had fallen down in a swoon, before any one could get to her assistance.", "다름 아닌 매혹적인 소피아 본인이었다. 그녀는 피를 본 탓인지, 아버지에 대한 두려움 탓인지, 아니면 또 다른 어떤 까닭인지, 누구도 손을 쓸 새도 없이 그만 정신을 잃고 쓰러져 버린 것이었다."]], [["Mrs Western first saw her and screamed.", "웨스턴 부인이 먼저 그녀를 보고 비명을 질렀다."], ["Immediately two or three voices cried out, “Miss Western is dead.”", "곧바로 두세 사람의 목소리가 외쳤다. \"웨스턴 양이 죽었어요.\""], ["Hartshorn, water, every remedy was called for, almost at one and the same instant.", "각성제, 물, 온갖 처방이 거의 동시에 한꺼번에 불려 나왔다."]], [["The reader may remember, that in our description of this grove we mentioned a murmuring brook, which brook did not come there, as such gentle streams flow through vulgar romances, with no other purpose than to murmur.", "독자는 우리가 이 숲을 묘사하면서 졸졸 흐르는 시냇물을 언급했던 것을 기억할 것이다. 그 시냇물은 흔해 빠진 연애소설 속에서 그저 졸졸거리는 것 말고는 아무 목적 없이 흘러가는 그런 잔잔한 물줄기로 거기 있었던 것이 아니다."], ["No!", "아니다!"], ["Fortune had decreed to ennoble this little brook with a higher honour than any of those which wash the plains of Arcadia ever deserved.", "운명은 이 작은 시냇물에게, 아르카디아의 들판을 적시는 어떤 시내가 누렸던 것보다도 더 높은 영예를 안겨 주기로 정해 두었던 것이다."]], [["Jones was rubbing Blifil's temples, for he began to fear he had given him a blow too much, when the words, Miss Western and Dead, rushed at once on his ear. He started up, left Blifil to his fate, and flew to Sophia, whom, while all the rest were running against each other, backward and forward, looking for water in the dry paths, he caught up in his arms, and then ran away with her over the field to the rivulet above mentioned; where, plunging himself into the water, he contrived to besprinkle her face, head, and neck very plentifully.", "존스는 블리필의 관자놀이를 문지르고 있었다. 한 대를 너무 세게 친 게 아닌가 슬슬 겁이 나기 시작했던 것이다. 그때 \"웨스턴 양\"과 \"죽었다\"는 말이 한꺼번에 그의 귀로 쏟아져 들어왔다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 블리필을 그의 운명에 내맡긴 채 소피아에게로 날아갔다. 다른 사람들이 모두 앞으로 뒤로 서로 부딪쳐 가며 메마른 길에서 물을 찾고 있는 동안, 그는 소피아를 두 팔로 안아 올린 뒤, 앞서 말한 그 시냇물을 향해 들판을 가로질러 달려갔다. 거기서 물속에 몸을 던져 가며, 그녀의 얼굴과 머리와 목에 물을 흠뻑 끼얹는 데 성공했다."]], [["Happy was it for Sophia that the same confusion which prevented her other friends from serving her, prevented them likewise from obstructing Jones.", "소피아에게는 다행스러운 일이었다. 다른 벗들이 그녀를 돕지 못하게 만든 바로 그 혼란이, 그들이 존스를 방해하지 못하게도 막아 주었으니 말이다."], ["He had carried her half ways before they knew what he was doing, and he had actually restored her to life before they reached the waterside.", "사람들이 그가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알아채기도 전에 그는 이미 그녀를 절반쯤 데려가 있었고, 그들이 물가에 닿기도 전에 그는 정말로 그녀를 되살려 놓았다."], ["She stretched out her arms, opened her eyes, and cried, “Oh!", "그녀는 두 팔을 뻗고 눈을 떠 \"오!"], ["heavens!”", "맙소사!\""], ["just as her father, aunt, and the parson came up.", "하고 외쳤는데, 마침 그녀의 아버지와 고모와 목사가 다가온 바로 그 순간이었다."]], [["Jones, who had hitherto held this lovely burthen in his arms, now relinquished his hold; but gave her at the same instant a tender caress, which, had her senses been then perfectly restored, could not have escaped her observation.", "그때까지 이 사랑스러운 짐을 두 팔에 안고 있던 존스는 이제 손을 놓았다. 하지만 그와 동시에 그녀에게 다정한 손길로 한 번 어루만져 주었으니, 만일 그녀가 그때 정신을 온전히 차린 상태였다면 결코 그 손길을 알아채지 못했을 리 없었다."], ["As she expressed, therefore, no displeasure at this freedom, we suppose she was not sufficiently recovered from her swoon at the time.", "그런데 그녀가 이 무례함에 아무런 불쾌함도 드러내지 않았으니, 우리는 그녀가 그 순간 아직 기절에서 충분히 깨어나지 못했던 것이라 짐작한다."]], [["This tragical scene was now converted into a sudden scene of joy.", "이 비극적인 장면은 이제 별안간 기쁨의 장면으로 바뀌었다. 여기서 우리의 주인공이야말로 단연 주연 인물이었다."], ["In this our heroe was certainly the principal character; for as he probably felt more ecstatic delight in having saved Sophia than she herself received from being saved, so neither were the congratulations paid to her equal to what were conferred on Jones, especially by Mr Western himself, who, after having once or twice embraced his daughter, fell to hugging and kissing Jones.", "그는 아마도 소피아를 구한 데서, 그녀 자신이 구원받은 데서 느낀 것보다 더 황홀한 기쁨을 맛보았을 것이고, 그녀에게 쏟아진 축하의 말 또한 존스에게 바쳐진 것에는 미치지 못했으니 말이다. 특히 웨스턴 씨 본인이 그러했는데, 그는 딸을 한두 번 끌어안고 나더니 이내 존스를 부둥켜안고 입을 맞추기 시작했다."], ["He called him the preserver of Sophia, and declared there was nothing, except her, or his estate, which he would not give him; but upon recollection, he afterwards excepted his fox-hounds, the Chevalier, and Miss Slouch (for so he called his favourite mare).", "그는 존스를 소피아의 구원자라 부르며, 딸과 자기 영지를 빼고는 무엇이든 그에게 주지 못할 것이 없노라고 선언했다. 하지만 다시 곰곰 생각해 보더니, 나중에 가서는 자기 폭스하운드와 슈발리에와 슬라우치 양(그가 자기 애마를 그렇게 불렀다)만은 예외로 두었다."]], [["All fears for Sophia being now removed, Jones became the object of the squire's consideration.--“Come, my lad,” says Western, “d'off thy quoat and wash thy feace; for att in a devilish pickle, I promise thee. Come, come, wash thyself, and shat go huome with me; and we'l zee to vind thee another quoat.”", "소피아에 대한 모든 걱정이 이제 사라지자, 존스가 지주의 관심거리가 되었다. \"이리 와라, 얘야.\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"웃옷을 벗고 얼굴을 좀 씻어라. 정말이지 꼴이 말이 아니구나. 어서, 어서, 몸을 씻고 나하고 같이 집으로 가자. 다른 웃옷을 한 벌 마련해 주마.\""]], [["Jones immediately complied, threw off his coat, went down to the water, and washed both his face and bosom; for the latter was as much exposed and as bloody as the former.", "존스는 곧바로 따랐다. 웃옷을 벗어 던지고 물가로 내려가 얼굴과 가슴을 둘 다 씻었으니, 가슴 역시 얼굴만큼이나 다 드러나 있고 피투성이였기 때문이다."], ["But though the water could clear off the blood, it could not remove the black and blue marks which Thwackum had imprinted on both his face and breast, and which, being discerned by Sophia, drew from her a sigh and a look full of inexpressible tenderness.", "그러나 물로 피는 씻어 낼 수 있었어도, 써그럼이 그의 얼굴과 가슴에 둘 다 새겨 놓은 시퍼런 멍자국까지 지울 수는 없었다. 그 자국을 알아본 소피아는 한숨과 함께, 이루 말할 수 없는 애틋함이 가득 담긴 눈길을 보냈다."]], [["Jones received this full in his eyes, and it had infinitely a stronger effect on him than all the contusions which he had received before.", "존스는 이 눈길을 두 눈으로 고스란히 받아들였는데, 그것은 그가 앞서 입은 그 모든 타박상보다도 한없이 더 강한 영향을 그에게 미쳤다."], ["An effect, however, widely different; for so soft and balmy was it, that, had all his former blows been stabs, it would for some minutes have prevented his feeling their smart.", "다만 그 영향은 사뭇 다른 종류의 것이었으니, 어찌나 부드럽고 향기로웠던지, 만일 그가 앞서 맞은 모든 타격이 칼에 찔린 상처였다 해도 몇 분 동안은 그 쓰라림을 느끼지 못하게 했을 정도였다."]], [["The company now moved backwards, and soon arrived where Thwackum had got Mr Blifil again on his legs.", "이제 일행은 뒤로 발길을 돌렸고, 곧 써그럼이 블리필 씨를 다시 일으켜 세워 놓은 곳에 이르렀다."], ["Here we cannot suppress a pious wish, that all quarrels were to be decided by those weapons only with which Nature, knowing what is proper for us, hath supplied us; and that cold iron was to be used in digging no bowels but those of the earth.", "여기서 우리는 한 가지 경건한 소망을 억누를 수가 없다. 모든 싸움이 오직, 무엇이 우리에게 알맞은지를 아는 자연이 우리에게 마련해 준 그 무기로만 결판났으면, 그리고 차가운 쇠붙이는 오직 땅의 창자를 파헤치는 데에만 쓰였으면 하는 소망이다."], ["Then would war, the pastime of monarchs, be almost inoffensive, and battles between great armies might be fought at the particular desire of several ladies of quality; who, together with the kings themselves, might be actual spectators of the conflict.", "그렇게만 된다면 군주들의 소일거리인 전쟁도 거의 해롭지 않은 것이 될 테고, 거대한 군대들 사이의 전투도 몇몇 지체 높은 부인들의 특별한 청에 따라 벌어질 수 있으리라. 그러면 부인들은 왕들과 더불어 그 싸움을 실제로 구경하는 관객이 될 수도 있을 것이다."], ["Then might the field be this moment well strewed with human carcasses, and the next, the dead men, or infinitely the greatest part of them, might get up, like Mr Bayes's troops, and march off either at the sound of a drum or fiddle, as should be previously agreed on.", "또한 들판은 이 순간 인간의 시체로 흥건히 뒤덮였다가, 다음 순간에는 죽은 자들이, 아니 그 압도적인 대다수가, 베이즈 씨의 군대처럼 벌떡 일어나, 미리 합의한 대로 북소리든 바이올린 소리든 그 소리에 맞추어 행진해 나갈 수도 있으리라."]], [["I would avoid, if possible, treating this matter ludicrously, lest grave men and politicians, whom I know to be offended at a jest, may cry pish at it; but, in reality, might not a battle be as well decided by the greater number of broken heads, bloody noses, and black eyes, as by the greater heaps of mangled and murdered human bodies? Might not towns be contended for in the same manner? Indeed, this may be thought too detrimental a scheme to the French interest, since they would thus lose the advantage they have over other nations in the superiority of their engineers; but when I consider the gallantry and generosity of that people, I am persuaded they would never decline putting themselves upon a par with their adversary; or, as the phrase is, making themselves his match.", "나는 가능하다면 이 문제를 우스갯거리로 다루지 않으려 한다. 농담이라면 발끈하는 줄 내가 잘 아는 근엄한 분들과 정치가들이 그것을 보고 \"쳇\" 하며 콧방귀를 뀔까 봐 말이다. 하지만 사실, 전투라는 것이 갈가리 찢기고 살해당한 인간의 몸뚱이를 더 높이 쌓아 올리는 쪽으로 결판나는 것 못지않게, 깨진 머리와 피투성이 코와 시퍼런 눈두덩이를 더 많이 만드는 쪽으로 결판날 수도 있지 않겠는가? 도시도 같은 방식으로 다투어 차지할 수 있지 않겠는가? 하기야 이것은 프랑스의 이익에는 너무 손해가 큰 계획이라 여겨질 법도 하다. 그렇게 되면 그들이 다른 나라들에 대해 가진 우위, 즉 뛰어난 공병들의 우월함이라는 이점을 잃게 될 테니 말이다. 그러나 그 국민의 용맹함과 너그러움을 헤아려 보건대, 나는 그들이 적과 대등한 처지에 서는 것을, 즉 흔한 표현대로 적의 호적수가 되기를 결코 마다하지 않으리라 확신한다."]], [["But such reformations are rather to be wished than hoped for: I shall content myself, therefore, with this short hint, and return to my narrative.", "하지만 그러한 개혁이란 바라는 것이지 기대할 것은 못 된다. 그러니 나는 이 짤막한 암시 하나로 만족하고, 다시 내 이야기로 돌아가겠다."]], [["Western began now to inquire into the original rise of this quarrel. To which neither Blifil nor Jones gave any answer; but Thwackum said surlily, “I believe the cause is not far off; if you beat the bushes well you may find her.”--“Find her?” replied Western: “what! have you been fighting for a wench?”--“Ask the gentleman in his waistcoat there,” said Thwackum: “he best knows.” “Nay then,” cries Western, “it is a wench certainly.--Ah, Tom, Tom, thou art a liquorish dog. But come, gentlemen, be all friends, and go home with me, and make final peace over a bottle.” “I ask your pardon, sir,” says Thwackum: “it is no such slight matter for a man of my character to be thus injuriously treated, and buffeted by a boy, only because I would have done my duty, in endeavouring to detect and bring to justice a wanton harlot; but, indeed, the principal fault lies in Mr Allworthy and yourself; for if you put the laws in execution, as you ought to do, you will soon rid the country of these vermin.”", "이제 웨스턴은 이 싸움이 애당초 어떻게 일어난 것인지 캐묻기 시작했다. 그런데 거기에 블리필도 존스도 아무런 대답을 하지 않았고, 다만 써그럼이 뚱하게 말했다. \"그 까닭이라면 멀지 않은 데 있을 겁니다. 덤불을 잘 뒤져 보면 그 계집을 찾을 수 있을 테지요.\" \"그 계집을 찾는다?\" 웨스턴이 받아쳤다. \"뭐라고! 자네들이 계집 하나를 두고 싸웠단 말이야?\" \"저기 조끼 차림인 신사 양반한테 물어보시지요.\" 써그럼이 말했다. \"그자가 제일 잘 알 테니.\" \"허, 그렇다면,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"틀림없이 계집 때문이로군. 아, 톰, 톰, 너 이 색 밝히는 놈 같으니. 하지만 자, 여러분, 모두 친구가 되어 나하고 같이 집으로 가서, 술 한 병 놓고 화해를 매듭짓읍시다.\" \"송구하오나, 선생,\" 써그럼이 말했다. \"저 같은 신분의 사람이 이렇게 모욕적인 대접을 받고, 그것도 한낱 풋내기에게 두들겨 맞다니, 결코 그리 가볍게 넘길 일이 아닙니다. 그저 음란한 계집을 적발해 처벌받게 하려고 제 본분을 다하려 했을 뿐인데 말입니다. 하지만 사실, 가장 큰 잘못은 올워디 씨와 선생 본인께 있습니다. 두 분이 마땅히 하셔야 할 대로 법을 집행하신다면, 이내 이 고장에서 그런 해충 떼를 쓸어버릴 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“I would as soon rid the country of foxes,”", "\"그런 거라면 차라리 이 고장에서 여우를 쓸어버리는 게 낫겠소.\""], ["cries Western.", "웨스턴이 외쳤다."], ["“I think we ought to encourage the recruiting those numbers which we are every day losing in the war.--But where is she?", "\"내 생각엔 우리가 전쟁으로 날마다 잃어 가는 그 머릿수를 다시 채우는 일을 오히려 장려해야 할 것 같은데. 한데 그 계집은 어디 있나?"], ["Prithee, Tom, show me.”", "자, 톰, 어디 보여 다오.\""], ["He then began to beat about, in the same language and in the same manner as if he had been beating for a hare; and at last cried out, “Soho!", "그러더니 그는 마치 산토끼를 몰아내듯 똑같은 말투에 똑같은 몸짓으로 이리저리 덤불을 두들기기 시작하더니, 마침내 외쳤다. \"어이!"], ["Puss is not far off.", "토끼가 멀지 않은 데 있군."], ["Here's her form, upon my soul; I believe I may cry stole away.”", "여기 그 잠자리 자국이 있어, 정말이지. 이쯤이면 '도망쳤다!'고 외쳐도 되겠는걸.\""], ["And indeed so he might; for he had now discovered the place whence the poor girl had, at the beginning of the fray, stolen away, upon as many feet as a hare generally uses in travelling.", "과연 그렇게 외칠 만했다. 그 가엾은 처녀가 싸움이 시작되던 무렵에, 산토끼가 줄행랑칠 때 흔히 놀리는 만큼이나 부지런히 발을 놀려 슬그머니 빠져나간 바로 그 자리를 이제 막 찾아낸 참이었으니 말이다."]], [["Sophia now desired her father to return home; saying she found herself very faint, and apprehended a relapse.", "이제 소피아가 아버지에게 집으로 돌아가자고 청했다. 몸이 몹시 어지럽고, 또 혼절이 도질까 걱정이 된다는 것이었다."], ["The squire immediately complied with his daughter's request (for he was the fondest of parents).", "지주는 즉시 딸의 청에 따랐다(그는 더없이 자식을 끔찍이 아끼는 아비였으니까). 그는 일행 전부에게 자기 집에 가서 함께 저녁을 먹자고 간곡히 권했다."], ["He earnestly endeavoured to prevail with the whole company to go and sup with him: but Blifil and Thwackum absolutely refused; the former saying, there were more reasons than he could then mention, why he must decline this honour; and the latter declaring (perhaps rightly) that it was not proper for a person of his function to be seen at any place in his present condition.", "그러나 블리필과 써그럼은 한사코 거절했다. 블리필은 지금 그 자리에서 다 말할 수 없는 여러 이유로 이 영광을 사양할 수밖에 없다고 했고, 써그럼은 (아마 옳은 말이었겠지만) 자기 같은 직분을 가진 사람이 지금 이런 꼴로 어디든 모습을 드러내는 것은 온당치 못하다고 잘라 말했다."]], [["Jones was incapable of refusing the pleasure of being with his Sophia; so on he marched with Squire Western and his ladies, the parson bringing up the rear. This had, indeed, offered to tarry with his brother Thwackum, professing his regard for the cloth would not permit him to depart; but Thwackum would not accept the favour, and, with no great civility, pushed him after Mr Western.", "존스는 자기 소피아와 함께 있는 기쁨을 차마 마다할 수가 없었다. 그리하여 그는 웨스턴 지주와 그 부인들과 함께 발걸음을 옮겼고, 목사가 그 뒤를 따라왔다. 사실 이 목사는 자기 형제뻘인 써그럼 곁에 남겠다고 나섰더랬다. 같은 성직자에 대한 도리상 차마 떠날 수 없다는 것이었다. 그러나 써그럼은 그 호의를 받아들이지 않고, 그다지 정중하달 것도 없이 그를 웨스턴 씨 뒤로 떠밀어 버렸다."]], [["Thus ended this bloody fray; and thus shall end the fifth book of this history.", "이렇게 이 피비린내 나는 싸움은 끝이 났다. 그리고 이렇게 이 이야기의 다섯 번째 권도 끝을 맺으리라."]], [["In our last book we have been obliged to deal pretty much with the passion of love; and in our succeeding book shall be forced to handle this subject still more largely.", "약 3주간의 이야기를 담음."], ["It may not therefore in this place be improper to apply ourselves to the examination of that modern doctrine, by which certain philosophers, among many other wonderful discoveries, pretend to have found out, that there is no such passion in the human breast.", "사랑에 관하여."]], [["Whether these philosophers be the same with that surprising sect, who are honourably mentioned by the late Dr Swift, as having, by the mere force of genius alone, without the least assistance of any kind of learning, or even reading, discovered that profound and invaluable secret that there is no God; or whether they are not rather the same with those who some years since very much alarmed the world, by showing that there were no such things as virtue or goodness really existing in human nature, and who deduced our best actions from pride, I will not here presume to determine. In reality, I am inclined to suspect, that all these several finders of truth, are the very identical men who are by others called the finders of gold. The method used in both these searches after truth and after gold, being indeed one and the same, viz., the searching, rummaging, and examining into a nasty place; indeed, in the former instances, into the nastiest of all places, A BAD MIND.", "우리는 앞선 권에서 사랑이라는 정념을 꽤 많이 다룰 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 이어질 권에서는 이 주제를 한층 더 폭넓게 다룰 수밖에 없을 것이다. 그러니 이 자리에서, 어떤 철학자들이 수많은 놀라운 발견들 가운데 하나로, 인간의 가슴속에는 그런 정념 따위가 존재하지 않는다는 것을 알아냈노라 자처하는 그 현대의 학설을 한번 살펴보는 것도 그리 부적절하지는 않을 것이다. 이 철학자들이, 고(故) 스위프트 박사가 명예롭게 언급한 바 있는 그 놀라운 무리—오로지 타고난 천재성의 힘만으로, 어떤 종류의 학식이나 심지어 독서의 도움조차 조금도 받지 않고서, 신이란 존재하지 않는다는 그 심오하고도 값진 비밀을 발견해 냈다는 무리—와 같은 자들인지, 아니면 몇 해 전 인간 본성 속에는 덕이나 선이라 할 만한 것이 실제로는 전혀 존재하지 않으며 우리의 가장 훌륭한 행위조차 자만심에서 비롯된 것이라고 주장하여 세상을 적잖이 놀라게 한 그 자들과 차라리 같은 부류인지, 나는 여기서 함부로 단정하지 않으련다. 사실대로 말하자면, 나는 이 여러 진리의 발견자들이라는 자들이, 다른 이들이 황금을 캐는 자라 부르는 바로 그 똑같은 인간들이 아닐까 의심하는 쪽으로 기운다. 진리를 캐는 일과 황금을 캐는 일에 쓰이는 방법이란 실은 하나요 같은 것이니, 곧 더러운 곳을 뒤지고, 헤집고, 살피는 일이다. 더구나 앞의 경우에는 모든 더러운 곳 가운데서도 가장 더러운 곳, 곧 나쁜 마음을 파헤치는 것이다."]], [["But though in this particular, and perhaps in their success, the truth-finder and the gold-finder may very properly be compared together; yet in modesty, surely, there can be no comparison between the two; for who ever heard of a gold-finder that had the impudence or folly to assert, from the ill success of his search, that there was no such thing as gold in the world?", "그러나 이 점에서, 그리고 어쩌면 그 성과에서도 진리를 캐는 자와 황금을 캐는 자를 나란히 견주는 것이 제법 그럴듯하다 하더라도, 겸손함에서만큼은 분명 둘 사이에 견줄 거리가 없다. 황금을 캐는 자가 자기 탐색이 신통치 못했다 하여 세상에 황금 따위는 존재하지 않는다고 뻔뻔하거나 어리석게 주장했다는 이야기를, 일찍이 누가 들어 본 적이 있던가?"], ["whereas the truth-finder, having raked out that jakes, his own mind, and being there capable of tracing no ray of divinity, nor anything virtuous or good, or lovely, or loving, very fairly, honestly, and logically concludes that no such things exist in the whole creation.", "그런데 진리를 캐는 자는 제 마음이라는 그 뒷간을 박박 긁어 보고도 거기서 신성의 빛줄기 하나, 덕스럽거나 선하거나 사랑스럽거나 사랑하는 것 하나 찾아내지 못하자, 아주 공정하고 정직하며 논리적으로, 그런 것들은 온 피조물 가운데 어디에도 존재하지 않는다고 결론짓는 것이다."]], [["To avoid, however, all contention, if possible, with these philosophers, if they will be called so; and to show our own disposition to accommodate matters peaceably between us, we shall here make them some concessions, which may possibly put an end to the dispute.", "하지만 가능하다면 이 철학자들—그렇게 불리고자 한다면—과의 온갖 다툼을 피하고, 우리 사이의 문제를 평화롭게 매듭짓고자 하는 우리 자신의 뜻을 보이기 위해, 여기서 우리는 그들에게 몇 가지 양보를 해 보려 한다. 어쩌면 이로써 이 논쟁이 끝날지도 모를 일이다."]], [["First, we will grant that many minds, and perhaps those of the philosophers, are entirely free from the least traces of such a passion.", "첫째, 우리는 많은 마음이, 그리고 어쩌면 그 철학자들의 마음이, 그러한 정념의 흔적이라곤 조금도 없이 완전히 깨끗하다는 것을 인정하겠다."]], [["Secondly, that what is commonly called love, namely, the desire of satisfying a voracious appetite with a certain quantity of delicate white human flesh, is by no means that passion for which I here contend.", "둘째, 흔히 사랑이라 불리는 것, 곧 일정한 분량의 보드랍고 하얀 인육으로 게걸스러운 식욕을 채우려는 욕망은, 내가 여기서 옹호하려는 그 정념과는 결코 같지 않다는 것을 인정하겠다."], ["This is indeed more properly hunger; and as no glutton is ashamed to apply the word love to his appetite, and to say he LOVES such and such dishes; so may the lover of this kind, with equal propriety, say, he HUNGERS after such and such women.", "그런 것은 실은 더 정확히 말해 허기다. 어떤 대식가도 제 식욕에다 사랑이라는 말을 붙여 이러저러한 요리를 사랑한다고 말하기를 부끄러워하지 않듯이, 이런 부류의 연인도 똑같이 적절하게, 자신이 이러저러한 여인을 향해 허기진다고 말할 수 있을 것이다."]], [["Thirdly, I will grant, which I believe will be a most acceptable concession, that this love for which I am an advocate, though it satisfies itself in a much more delicate manner, doth nevertheless seek its own satisfaction as much as the grossest of all our appetites.", "셋째, 나는, 아마도 가장 흔쾌히 받아들여질 양보가 되리라 믿는바, 내가 옹호하는 이 사랑이 비록 훨씬 더 섬세한 방식으로 스스로를 만족시킬지언정, 그럼에도 우리의 모든 식욕 가운데 가장 천박한 것에 못지않게 제 나름의 만족을 구한다는 것을 인정하겠다."]], [["And, lastly, that this love, when it operates towards one of a different sex, is very apt, towards its complete gratification, to call in the aid of that hunger which I have mentioned above; and which it is so far from abating, that it heightens all its delights to a degree scarce imaginable by those who have never been susceptible of any other emotions than what have proceeded from appetite alone.", "그리고 끝으로, 이 사랑이 다른 성(性)의 누군가를 향해 작용할 때에는, 그 만족을 온전히 이루기 위해 앞서 말한 그 허기의 도움을 불러들이기 십상이라는 것, 그리고 이 사랑은 그 허기를 누그러뜨리기는커녕 오히려 그 모든 기쁨을, 오로지 식욕에서만 비롯된 감정 말고는 다른 어떤 감정도 느껴 본 적 없는 자들로서는 좀처럼 상상조차 할 수 없는 정도로까지 드높여 준다는 것을 인정하겠다."]], [["In return to all these concessions, I desire of the philosophers to grant, that there is in some (I believe in many) human breasts a kind and benevolent disposition, which is gratified by contributing to the happiness of others.", "이 모든 양보의 대가로, 나는 철학자들에게 다음의 것들을 인정해 달라고 청한다. 어떤(내 믿기로는 많은) 인간의 가슴속에는, 남들의 행복에 보탬이 됨으로써 흡족해지는 너그럽고 자애로운 성향이 자리하고 있다는 것."], ["That in this gratification alone, as in friendship, in parental and filial affection, as indeed in general philanthropy, there is a great and exquisite delight.", "우정에서, 부모와 자식 간의 정에서, 그리고 실로 널리 인류를 사랑하는 마음에서와 마찬가지로, 바로 이 흡족함 그 자체 속에 크고도 정묘한 기쁨이 깃들어 있다는 것."], ["That if we will not call such disposition love, we have no name for it.", "만약 우리가 그런 성향을 사랑이라 부르지 않겠다면, 그것을 가리킬 다른 이름이 우리에게는 없다는 것."], ["That though the pleasures arising from such pure love may be heightened and sweetened by the assistance of amorous desires, yet the former can subsist alone, nor are they destroyed by the intervention of the latter.", "그리고 그런 순수한 사랑에서 솟아나는 기쁨이 비록 연정 어린 욕망의 도움으로 한층 드높아지고 달콤해질 수 있다 해도, 앞의 것은 홀로도 능히 존립할 수 있으며, 뒤의 것이 끼어든다 하여 무너지는 것도 아니라는 것."], ["Lastly, that esteem and gratitude are the proper motives to love, as youth and beauty are to desire, and, therefore, though such desire may naturally cease, when age or sickness overtakes its object; yet these can have no effect on love, nor ever shake or remove, from a good mind, that sensation or passion which hath gratitude and esteem for its basis.", "끝으로, 젊음과 아름다움이 욕망의 마땅한 동기이듯, 존경과 감사는 사랑의 마땅한 동기라는 것. 그러므로 그 대상에 늙음이나 병이 닥치면 그런 욕망은 자연히 사그라질 수 있을지언정, 이런 것들은 사랑에는 아무런 영향도 미치지 못하며, 감사와 존경을 그 바탕으로 삼는 그 감각이나 정념을 선한 마음에서 결코 흔들거나 몰아내지 못한다는 것이다."]], [["To deny the existence of a passion of which we often see manifest instances, seems to be very strange and absurd; and can indeed proceed only from that self-admonition which we have mentioned above: but how unfair is this!", "우리가 그 뚜렷한 사례들을 자주 보게 되는 정념을 두고 그 존재를 부정한다는 것은 참으로 기이하고 터무니없는 일로 보인다. 그리고 그것은 실로 앞서 말한 그 자기 본위의 잣대에서만 비롯될 수 있다."], ["Doth the man who recognizes in his own heart no traces of avarice or ambition, conclude, therefore, that there are no such passions in human nature?", "그러나 이것이 얼마나 부당한가! 제 가슴속에서 탐욕이나 야심의 흔적을 찾지 못한다 하여, 인간 본성에는 그런 정념이 존재하지 않는다고 결론짓는 사람이 어디 있는가?"], ["Why will we not modestly observe the same rule in judging of the good, as well as the evil of others?", "어찌하여 우리는, 남들의 악을 헤아릴 때와 마찬가지로 남들의 선을 헤아릴 때에도 그 같은 규칙을 겸손하게 따르려 하지 않는가?"], ["Or why, in any case, will we, as Shakespear phrases it, “put the world in our own person?”", "또 어찌하여 우리는, 어떤 경우에든 셰익스피어의 표현처럼 \"온 세상을 제 한 몸으로 빗대려\" 드는가?"]], [["Predominant vanity is, I am afraid, too much concerned here.", "아무래도 여기에는 우리를 지배하는 허영심이 너무 깊이 끼어 있는 듯하다."], ["This is one instance of that adulation which we bestow on our own minds, and this almost universally.", "이는 우리가 우리 자신의 마음에 바치는 그 아첨의 한 본보기이며, 그것도 거의 누구에게나 보편적인 본보기다."], ["For there is scarce any man, how much soever he may despise the character of a flatterer, but will condescend in the meanest manner to flatter himself.", "아첨꾼이라는 인물을 제아무리 경멸하는 사람이라 할지라도, 가장 비루한 방식으로 자기 자신에게는 기꺼이 아첨하려 들지 않는 이가 거의 없으니 말이다."]], [["To those therefore I apply for the truth of the above observations, whose own minds can bear testimony to what I have advanced.", "그러므로 위의 관찰들이 참임을 가려 줄 일에 대해, 나는 자기 마음이 내가 펼친 주장을 증언해 줄 수 있는 이들에게 호소하는 바이다."]], [["Examine your heart, my good reader, and resolve whether you do believe these matters with me. If you do, you may now proceed to their exemplification in the following pages: if you do not, you have, I assure you, already read more than you have understood; and it would be wiser to pursue your business, or your pleasures (such as they are), than to throw away any more of your time in reading what you can neither taste nor comprehend. To treat of the effects of love to you, must be as absurd as to discourse on colours to a man born blind; since possibly your idea of love may be as absurd as that which we are told such blind man once entertained of the colour scarlet; that colour seemed to him to be very much like the sound of a trumpet: and love probably may, in your opinion, very greatly resemble a dish of soup, or a surloin of roast-beef.", "착한 독자여, 그대의 마음을 한번 살펴보고, 그대가 나와 더불어 이런 일들을 믿는지 결단해 보라. 만약 믿는다면, 이제 이어지는 책장들에서 그 실례를 보러 나아가도 좋다. 만약 믿지 않는다면, 분명히 말해 두건대 그대는 이미 자신이 이해한 것보다 더 많이 읽은 것이니, 맛볼 수도 깨달을 수도 없는 글을 읽느라 더는 시간을 허비하기보다, 차라리 그대의 일이나 (그것이 무엇이든) 그대의 즐거움을 좇는 편이 더 지혜로울 것이다. 그대에게 사랑의 효력을 논한다는 것은, 날 때부터 눈먼 사람에게 색깔을 늘어놓는 것만큼이나 터무니없는 일임에 틀림없다. 그도 그럴 것이, 사랑에 대한 그대의 관념이란, 어떤 눈먼 사람이 진홍빛에 대해 한때 품었다고 전해지는 그 관념만큼이나 터무니없을지도 모르기 때문이다. 그 사람에게는 진홍빛이 나팔 소리와 무척 비슷하게 여겨졌다 한다. 그러니 그대 생각에 사랑이란, 어쩌면 수프 한 접시나 등심 구운 쇠고기 한 덩이와 아주 흡사한 것일지도 모를 일이다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["The character of Mrs Western.", "웨스턴 부인의 인물됨."], ["Her great learning and knowledge of the world, and an instance of the deep penetration which she derived from those advantages.", "그녀의 대단한 학식과 세상물정에 대한 식견, 그리고 그러한 강점에서 비롯된 깊은 통찰력의 한 사례."]], [["The reader hath seen Mr Western, his sister, and daughter, with young Jones, and the parson, going together to Mr Western's house, where the greater part of the company spent the evening with much joy and festivity. Sophia was indeed the only grave person; for as to Jones, though love had now gotten entire possession of his heart, yet the pleasing reflection on Mr Allworthy's recovery, and the presence of his mistress, joined to some tender looks which she now and then could not refrain from giving him, so elevated our heroe, that he joined the mirth of the other three, who were perhaps as good-humoured people as any in the world.", "독자께서는 웨스턴 씨와 그 누이, 그리고 그 딸이 젊은 존스, 목사와 함께 웨스턴 씨의 집으로 향하는 것을 이미 보았다. 그곳에서 일행의 대부분은 무척이나 흥겹고 떠들썩하게 저녁을 보냈다. 진지한 표정을 짓고 있는 사람은 사실 소피아뿐이었다. 존스로 말하자면, 이제 사랑이 그의 가슴을 온전히 사로잡고 있었음에도, 올워디 씨가 회복되었다는 흐뭇한 생각과 사랑하는 여인이 곁에 있다는 사실, 게다가 그녀가 이따금 그에게 던지지 않고는 못 배기던 다정한 눈길까지 더해져, 우리의 주인공은 한껏 기분이 들떠 나머지 세 사람의 즐거움에 함께 어울렸다. 어쩌면 그 세 사람은 세상에서 둘도 없이 쾌활한 이들이었으리라."]], [["Sophia retained the same gravity of countenance the next morning at breakfast; whence she retired likewise earlier than usual, leaving her father and aunt together.", "소피아는 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서도 똑같이 굳은 얼굴을 하고 있었고, 그래서 평소보다 일찍 자리를 떠 아버지와 고모를 단둘이 남겨 두었다. 지주 양반은 딸의 기분에 일어난 이 변화를 전혀 알아차리지 못했다."], ["The squire took no notice of this change in his daughter's disposition.", "사실을 말하자면, 그는 다소간 정치가 흉내를 내는 사람이었고 선거 때 시골 진영의 후보로 두 번이나 나선 적도 있었지만, 관찰력이 그리 뛰어난 인물은 아니었다. 그의 누이는 결이 다른 부인이었다."], ["To say the truth, though he was somewhat of a politician, and had been twice a candidate in the country interest at an election, he was a man of no great observation.", "그녀는 궁정 근처에서 지내며 세상을 두루 보아 왔다. 그래서 이른바 그 세상이 으레 전해 주는 온갖 지식을 익혔고, 예법과 관습, 의례와 유행에 통달한 완벽한 안주인이었다."], ["His sister was a lady of a different turn.", "그녀의 박식함은 거기서 그치지 않았다."], ["She had lived about the court, and had seen the world.", "그녀는 공부로 상당히 마음을 갈고닦았다."], ["Hence she had acquired all that knowledge which the said world usually communicates; and was a perfect mistress of manners, customs, ceremonies, and fashions. Nor did her erudition stop here.", "현대의 희곡과 오페라, 오라토리오, 시, 연애소설을 죄다 읽었을 뿐 아니라—이 모든 분야에서 그녀는 평론가였다—라팽의 《영국사》, 에처드의 《로마사》, 그리고 수많은 프랑스어 《역사에 이바지하기 위한 회고록》까지 두루 섭렵했다."], ["She had considerably improved her mind by study; she had not only read all the modern plays, operas, oratorios, poems, and romances--in all which she was a critic; but had gone through Rapin's History of England, Eachard's Roman History, and many French Mémoires pour servir à l'Histoire: to these she had added most of the political pamphlets and journals published within the last twenty years.", "여기에 더해 지난 이십 년 동안 출간된 정치 소책자와 잡지의 대부분도 읽어 두었다. 이로써 그녀는 정치에 제법 능숙한 솜씨를 얻었고, 유럽 정세를 무척 유식하게 논할 수 있었다."], ["From which she had attained a very competent skill in politics, and could discourse very learnedly on the affairs of Europe.", "게다가 그녀는 연애의 이치에도 훤히 통달해, 누구와 누가 그렇고 그런 사이인지를 누구보다 잘 알고 있었다. 이런 지식은 그만큼 더 쉽게 얻은 것이었으니, 그것을 좇는 데 자기 자신의 연애사가 끼어들어 방해한 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이다."], ["She was, moreover, excellently well skilled in the doctrine of amour, and knew better than anybody who and who were together; a knowledge which she the more easily attained, as her pursuit of it was never diverted by any affairs of her own; for either she had no inclinations, or they had never been solicited; which last is indeed very probable; for her masculine person, which was near six foot high, added to her manner and learning, possibly prevented the other sex from regarding her, notwithstanding her petticoats, in the light of a woman.", "그녀에게는 그런 마음이 아예 없었거나, 아니면 그런 청을 받아 본 적이 없었던 것이다. 후자가 사실 매우 그럴듯하다."], ["However, as she had considered the matter scientifically, she perfectly well knew, though she had never practised them, all the arts which fine ladies use when they desire to give encouragement, or to conceal liking, with all the long appendage of smiles, ogles, glances, &c., as they are at present practised in the beau-monde.", "키가 거의 육 척에 이르는 그 사내 같은 체구에다 몸가짐과 학식까지 더해지니, 치마를 두르고 있음에도 다른 성(性)이 그녀를 여자로 여기지 못하게 막았을 법하기 때문이다. 그러나 이 문제를 학문적으로 헤아려 온 터라, 그녀는 비록 한 번도 직접 써먹어 본 적은 없을지언정, 우아한 부인들이 호감을 부추기거나 좋아하는 마음을 감추려 할 때 쓰는 온갖 기교를, 그리고 오늘날 상류 사교계에서 행해지는 미소와 추파, 곁눈질 따위의 그 길고 긴 부속물 일체를 완벽히 꿰고 있었다."], ["To sum the whole, no species of disguise or affectation had escaped her notice; but as to the plain simple workings of honest nature, as she had never seen any such, she could know but little of them.", "한마디로, 어떤 종류의 가식이나 위장도 그녀의 눈을 피하지 못했다. 다만 정직한 본성이 빚어내는 꾸밈없는 단순한 움직임에 대해서라면, 그런 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없었던 만큼, 거의 아는 바가 없었다."]], [["By means of this wonderful sagacity, Mrs Western had now, as she thought, made a discovery of something in the mind of Sophia.", "이 놀라운 통찰력 덕분에 웨스턴 부인은 이제, 적어도 자기 생각으로는, 소피아의 마음속에서 무언가를 발견해 냈다."], ["The first hint of this she took from the behaviour of the young lady in the field of battle; and the suspicion which she then conceived, was greatly corroborated by some observations which she had made that evening and the next morning.", "이에 대한 첫 실마리는 그 싸움터에서 보였던 조카딸의 거동에서 잡아낸 것이었고, 그때 품은 의심은 그날 저녁과 이튿날 아침에 그녀가 해 둔 몇 가지 관찰로 한층 굳어졌다."], ["However, being greatly cautious to avoid being found in a mistake, she carried the secret a whole fortnight in her bosom, giving only some oblique hints, by simpering, winks, nods, and now and then dropping an obscure word, which indeed sufficiently alarmed Sophia, but did not at all affect her brother.", "그러나 잘못 짚은 것으로 드러나지 않으려 무척이나 조심했던 까닭에, 그녀는 그 비밀을 꼬박 두 주 동안 가슴속에 묻어 둔 채, 다만 능청스러운 웃음과 윙크, 고갯짓, 그리고 이따금 알쏭달쏭한 말 한마디를 흘려 에둘러 암시만 던졌다. 그것은 소피아를 충분히 불안하게 만들었으되, 그녀의 오라비에게는 아무런 영향도 미치지 못했다."]], [["Being at length, however, thoroughly satisfied of the truth of her observation, she took an opportunity, one morning, when she was alone with her brother, to interrupt one of his whistles in the following manner:--", "그러나 마침내 자신의 관찰이 옳다는 것을 철저히 확신하게 된 그녀는, 어느 날 아침 오라비와 단둘이 있을 때를 노려, 그가 한창 휘파람을 불고 있는 것을 다음과 같이 끊고 들어갔다."]], [["“Pray, brother, have you not observed something very extraordinary in my niece lately?”--“No, not I,”", "\"오라버니, 요즘 제 조카에게서 뭔가 아주 심상찮은 점을 못 보셨어요?\" \"아니, 못 봤는데.\""], ["answered Western; “is anything the matter with the girl?”--“I think there is,”", "웨스턴이 대답했다. \"그 애한테 무슨 일이라도 있나?\""], ["replied she; “and something of much consequence too.”--“Why, she doth not complain of anything,”", "\"있는 것 같아요.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다."], ["cries Western; “and she hath had the small-pox.”--“Brother,”", "\"그것도 아주 중대한 일이요.\" \"허, 그 애는 아무 데도 아프다고 한 적이 없는데.\""], ["returned she, “girls are liable to other distempers besides the small-pox, and sometimes possibly to much worse.”", "웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"게다가 천연두도 이미 앓았고.\""], ["Here Western interrupted her with much earnestness, and begged her, if anything ailed his daughter, to acquaint him immediately; adding, “she knew he loved her more than his own soul, and that he would send to the world's end for the best physician to her.”", "\"오라버니,\" 그녀가 되받았다."], ["“Nay, nay,”", "\"처녀들이 천연두 말고도 걸리는 병이 있고, 때로는 어쩌면 훨씬 더 고약한 병도 있답니다.\""], ["answered she, smiling, “the distemper is not so terrible; but I believe, brother, you are convinced I know the world, and I promise you I was never more deceived in my life, if my niece be not most desperately in love.”--“How!", "여기서 웨스턴은 무척 다급하게 그녀의 말을 끊고, 딸에게 무슨 탈이라도 있다면 당장 알려 달라고 간청했다. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, \"그 애를 제 목숨보다 더 사랑한다는 걸 너도 알지 않느냐, 그 애를 위해서라면 세상 끝까지라도 사람을 보내 가장 용한 의사를 데려오겠다.\""], ["in love!”", "했다."], ["cries Western, in a passion; “in love, without acquainting me!", "\"아니, 아니에요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["I'll disinherit her; I'll turn her out of doors, stark naked, without a farthing.", "\"그 병은 그렇게 무서운 게 아니랍니다. 하지만 오라버니, 제가 세상물정을 안다는 건 오라버니도 인정하시잖아요."], ["Is all my kindness vor 'ur, and vondness o'ur come to this, to fall in love without asking me leave?”--“But you will not,”", "그래서 약속하건대, 제 조카가 지독하게 사랑에 빠진 게 아니라면, 제 평생 이렇게까지 속아 본 적은 없을 거예요.\" \"뭐라고!"], ["answered Mrs Western, “turn this daughter, whom you love better than your own soul, out of doors, before you know whether you shall approve her choice.", "사랑에 빠져?\" 웨스턴이 발끈하며 소리쳤다."], ["Suppose she should have fixed on the very person whom you yourself would wish, I hope you would not be angry then?”--“No, no,”", "\"사랑에 빠졌다고, 그것도 나한테 알리지도 않고! 그 애를 상속에서 빼 버리겠다."], ["cries Western, “that would make a difference.", "실오라기 하나 걸치지 못하게 한 푼도 없이 문밖으로 내쫓아 버리겠어. 내가 그 애를 그토록 아끼고 위해 준 게 고작 이 꼴을 보자고 한 짓이었나, 내 허락도 구하지 않고 사랑에 빠지다니?\""], ["If she marries the man I would ha'", "\"하지만 오라버니,\" 웨스턴 부인이 대답했다."], ["her, she may love whom she pleases, I shan't trouble my head about that.”", "\"그 애가 고른 상대를 오라버니가 마음에 들어 할지 어떨지 알기도 전에, 제 목숨보다 더 사랑한다는 그 딸을 문밖으로 내쫓진 않으시겠죠. 만약 그 애가 오라버니 자신이 바라던 바로 그 사람을 점찍어 두었다면, 그래도 화를 내시겠어요?\""], ["“That is spoken,”", "\"아니, 아니지.\" 웨스턴이 소리쳤다."], ["answered the sister, “like a sensible man; but I believe the very person she hath chosen would be the very person you would choose for her.", "\"그건 얘기가 다르지. 내가 점찍어 둔 사내와 결혼만 한다면야, 그 애가 누굴 사랑하든 그건 마음대로 하라지."], ["I will disclaim all knowledge of the world, if it is not so; and I believe, brother, you will allow I have some.”--“Why, lookee, sister,”", "그런 것까지 내가 머리 싸맬 일은 아니고.\" \"그건 분별 있는 사람다운 말씀이네요.\""], ["said Western, “I do believe you have as much as any woman; and to be sure those are women's matters.", "누이가 대답했다. \"하지만 그 애가 고른 바로 그 사람이, 오라버니가 그 애를 위해 고를 바로 그 사람일 거라고 저는 믿어요."], ["You know I don't love to hear you talk about politics; they belong to us, and petticoats should not meddle: but come, who is the man?”--“Marry!”", "만약 그렇지 않다면, 제가 세상물정을 안다는 걸 깡그리 부인하겠어요. 그리고 오라버니, 제게 그쯤 되는 식견은 있다는 걸 인정하실 테죠.\""], ["said she, “you may find him out yourself if you please.", "\"허, 들어 보게, 누이.\" 웨스턴이 말했다."], ["You, who are so great a politician, can be at no great loss.", "\"나도 자네가 여느 여자만큼은 식견이 있다고 믿네. 게다가 그런 건 확실히 여자들 일이고."], ["The judgment which can penetrate into the cabinets of princes, and discover the secret springs which move the great state wheels in all the political machines of Europe, must surely, with very little difficulty, find out what passes in the rude uninformed mind of a girl.”--“Sister,”", "자네도 알다시피 나는 자네가 정치 얘기 하는 건 듣기 싫어하네. 정치는 우리 남자들 몫이고, 치마 두른 사람은 끼어들 게 아니지."], ["cries the squire, “I have often warn'd you not to talk the court gibberish to me.", "자, 그래서 그 사내가 누군가?\" \"원, 세상에!\""], ["I tell you, I don't understand the lingo: but I can read a journal, or the London Evening Post.", "그녀가 말했다. \"굳이 원하신다면 직접 알아내실 수 있을 텐데요."], ["Perhaps, indeed, there may be now and tan a verse which I can't make much of, because half the letters are left out; yet I know very well what is meant by that, and that our affairs don't go so well as they should do, because of bribery and corruption.”--“I pity your country ignorance from my heart,”", "그토록 대단한 정치가이신 오라버니라면 그리 크게 헤매실 일도 아니죠. 군주들의 내실을 꿰뚫어 보고, 유럽의 온갖 정치 기계 속에서 거대한 국가의 바퀴를 돌리는 은밀한 태엽을 찾아내는 그 판단력이라면, 처녀의 거칠고 어수룩한 마음속에 무슨 일이 벌어지는지쯤이야 거의 힘들이지 않고 알아내실 게 틀림없잖아요.\""], ["cries the lady.--“Do you?”", "\"누이,\" 지주가 소리쳤다."], ["answered Western; “and I pity your town learning; I had rather be anything than a courtier, and a Presbyterian, and a Hanoverian too, as some people, I believe, are.”--“If you mean me,”", "\"그 궁정식 횡설수설일랑 내게 늘어놓지 말라고 몇 번이나 일렀잖나. 다시 말하지만, 나는 그 외국말 같은 건 알아듣지 못해."], ["answered she, “you know I am a woman, brother; and it signifies nothing what I am.", "하지만 잡지나 《런던 이브닝 포스트》쯤은 읽을 줄 안다네. 하긴 가끔 글자가 절반쯤 빠져 있어서 도무지 알아먹기 힘든 구절이 끼어 있긴 하지."], ["Besides--“--“I do know you are a woman,”", "그래도 그게 무슨 뜻인지는 아주 잘 알아."], ["cries the squire, “and it's well for thee that art one; if hadst been a man, I promise thee I had lent thee a flick long ago.”--“Ay, there,”", "뇌물과 부패 때문에 우리 나라 일이 마땅히 돌아가야 할 만큼 잘 돌아가지 않는다는 뜻이지.\" \"그 시골뜨기 무지를 진심으로 가엾게 여겨요.\""], ["said she, “in that flick lies all your fancied superiority.", "부인이 소리쳤다. \"그래?\""], ["Your bodies, and not your brains, are stronger than ours.", "웨스턴이 대답했다. \"나는 자네의 그 도시물 든 학식이 가엾네."], ["Believe me, it is well for you that you are able to beat us; or, such is the superiority of our understanding, we should make all of you what the brave, and wise, and witty, and polite are already--our slaves.”--“I am glad I know your mind,”", "나는 어떤 사람이 되든 좋지만, 궁정 아첨꾼이나 장로교도, 거기다 어떤 자들이 그렇듯 하노버 지지자만큼은 되기 싫네.\" \"저를 두고 하시는 말씀이라면,\""], ["answered the squire.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"아시다시피 저는 여자예요, 오라버니."], ["“But we'll talk more of this matter another time.", "제가 어떤 사람인지는 아무 상관이 없죠. 게다가―\""], ["At present, do tell me what man is it you mean about my daughter?”--“Hold a moment,”", "\"자네가 여자인 줄은 잘 알지.\" 지주가 소리쳤다."], ["said she, “while I digest that sovereign contempt I have for your sex; or else I ought to be angry too with you.", "\"여자인 게 자네한테는 다행인 줄 알게. 사내였다면, 진작 한 대 후려쳐 줬을 테니까.\""], ["There--I have made a shift to gulp it down.", "\"옳거니, 바로 그거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["And now, good politic sir, what think you of Mr Blifil?", "\"오라버니가 잘난 척하는 그 우월함이라는 게 죄다 그 한 대에 들어 있는 거죠. 우리보다 강한 건 오라버니들의 머리가 아니라 몸뚱이일 뿐이라니까."], ["Did she not faint away on seeing him lie breathless on the ground?", "정말이지, 오라버니가 우리를 두들겨 팰 수 있다는 게 다행인 줄 아세요. 안 그랬다면, 우리 지력이 워낙 뛰어나서, 용감하고 지혜롭고 재치 있고 세련된 자들이 이미 그렇게 되어 있듯, 오라버니들 모두를 우리 노예로 만들어 버렸을 테니까요.\""], ["Did she not, after he was recovered, turn pale again the moment we came up to that part of the field where he stood?", "\"자네 속마음을 알게 되어 다행이군.\" 지주가 대답했다."], ["And pray what else should be the occasion of all her melancholy that night at supper, the next morning, and indeed ever since?”--“'Fore George!”", "\"하지만 이 얘기는 다음에 더 하기로 하고. 지금은 내 딸 일로 자네가 말하는 그 사내가 누군지나 어서 말해 보게.\""], ["cries the squire, “now you mind me on't, I remember it all.", "\"잠깐만요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["It is certainly so, and I am glad on't with all my heart.", "\"당신네 성(性)에 대한 이 지독한 경멸을 좀 삭이고요. 안 그러면 저도 오라버니한테 화를 내야 할 판이니까."], ["I knew Sophy was a good girl, and would not fall in love to make me angry.", "자―어찌어찌 꿀꺽 삼켰네요. 자, 그럼 훌륭하신 정치가 양반, 블리필 씨를 어떻게 생각하세요?"], ["I was never more rejoiced in my life; for nothing can lie so handy together as our two estates.", "그 애가 그분이 땅바닥에 숨이 끊긴 듯 누워 있는 걸 보고 까무러치지 않았나요? 그분이 깨어나신 뒤에도, 우리가 그분이 서 있던 그 들판으로 다가가는 순간 그 애가 다시 새파랗게 질리지 않던가요?"], ["I had this matter in my head some time ago: for certainly the two estates are in a manner joined together in matrimony already, and it would be a thousand pities to part them.", "그렇다면 그날 밤 저녁 식사 자리에서, 또 이튿날 아침에, 아니 사실 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 애가 보인 우울이 대체 무엇 때문이겠어요?\" \"맙소사!\""], ["It is true, indeed, there be larger estates in the kingdom, but not in this county, and I had rather bate something, than marry my daughter among strangers and foreigners.", "지주가 소리쳤다. \"자네가 그렇게 짚어 주니, 이제야 죄다 떠오르는군."], ["Besides, most o'", "분명 그런 거야, 나는 진심으로 기쁘기 그지없네. 우리 소피가 착한 애라서 날 화나게 할 사랑에는 빠지지 않을 줄 알았지."], ["zuch great estates be in the hands of lords, and I heate the very name of themmun.", "내 평생 이보다 더 기뻤던 적이 없네. 우리 두 영지만큼 딱 들어맞게 붙어 있는 게 또 어디 있겠나."], ["Well but, sister, what would you advise me to do; for I tell you women know these matters better than we do?”--“Oh, your humble servant, sir,”", "사실 나도 얼마 전부터 이 일을 머릿속에 두고 있었네. 두 영지가 어떤 의미에서는 이미 혼인으로 한데 묶여 있는 셈이니, 그걸 갈라놓는다면 천 번 만 번 애석한 일이지."], ["answered the lady: “we are obliged to you for allowing us a capacity in anything.", "하긴 이 나라에 더 큰 영지들이 있는 건 사실이지만, 이 고을 안에는 없으니까. 게다가 나는 차라리 조금 손해를 보더라도, 내 딸을 낯선 이방인들 틈에 시집보내고 싶지는 않네."], ["Since you are pleased, then, most politic sir, to ask my advice, I think you may propose the match to Allworthy yourself.", "더구나 그런 큰 영지는 대개 귀족 나리들 손에 들어 있는데, 나는 그 양반들 이름만 들어도 진저리가 나거든. 자, 그런데 누이, 내가 어찌하면 좋을지 좀 일러 주겠나."], ["There is no indecorum in the proposal's coming from the parent of either side.", "다시 말하지만, 이런 일은 자네들 여자가 우리보다 더 잘 아니까.\" \"오, 황송하옵니다, 나리.\""], ["King Alcinous, in Mr Pope's Odyssey, offers his daughter to Ulysses.", "부인이 대답했다. \"우리한테 무슨 일에든 능력이 있다고 인정해 주시니 그저 송구할 따름이죠."], ["I need not caution so politic a person not to say that your daughter is in love; that would indeed be against all rules.”--“Well,”", "그럼 그토록 능란하신 정치가 양반께서 기꺼이 제 조언을 구하시니, 제 생각에는 오라버니가 직접 올워디 씨에게 혼담을 꺼내시는 게 좋겠어요. 어느 쪽 부모가 먼저 청혼을 내놓는다 해도 예법에 어긋날 건 없으니까요."], ["said the squire, “I will propose it; but I shall certainly lend un a flick, if he should refuse me.”", "포프 씨의 《오디세이아》에서도 알키노오스 왕이 자기 딸을 율리시스에게 내주겠다고 청하잖아요. 그토록 능란한 분께 굳이 일러 둘 필요야 없겠지만, 따님이 사랑에 빠졌다는 말씀만은 하지 마세요."], ["“Fear not,” cries Mrs Western; “the match is too advantageous to be refused.”", "그건 정말이지 모든 법도에 어긋나는 일이니까요.\""], ["“I don't know that,”", "\"좋아.\" 지주가 말했다."], ["answered the squire: “Allworthy is a queer b--ch, and money hath no effect o'un.”", "\"내가 청해 보지. 하지만 그가 날 퇴짜 놓으면, 분명 한 대 후려쳐 줄 테야.\""], ["“Brother,”", "\"걱정 마세요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 소리쳤다."], ["said the lady, “your politics astonish me.", "\"이 혼사는 너무나 유리해서 거절당할 리가 없어요.\" \"그건 모르는 일이지.\""], ["Are you really to be imposed on by professions?", "지주가 대답했다. \"올워디는 별난 작자라서, 돈 따위는 먹히질 않거든.\""], ["Do you think Mr Allworthy hath more contempt for money than other men because he professes more?", "\"오라버니,\" 부인이 말했다."], ["Such credulity would better become one of us weak women, than that wise sex which heaven hath formed for politicians.", "\"그 정치 식견이라는 게 정말 놀랍네요. 정말로 입에 발린 말에 그렇게 넘어가실 작정이세요?"], ["Indeed, brother, you would make a fine plenipo to negotiate with the French.", "올워디 씨가 남들보다 돈을 더 멸시한다고 떠들어 댄다고 해서 정말 더 멸시하는 줄 아세요? 그런 잘 믿는 버릇은, 하늘이 정치가가 되라고 빚으신 그 지혜로운 성(性)보다는, 우리처럼 연약한 여자들에게 더 어울리는 거랍니다."], ["They would soon persuade you, that they take towns out of mere defensive principles.”", "정말이지 오라버니, 오라버니가 프랑스와 협상하는 전권대사로 나선다면 볼만하겠어요. 그쪽 사람들은 자기네가 순전히 방어 원칙에서 도시들을 빼앗는 거라고 금세 오라버니를 설득해 버릴 테니까요.\""], ["“Sister,”", "\"누이,\""], ["answered the squire, with much scorn, “let your friends at court answer for the towns taken; as you are a woman, I shall lay no blame upon you; for I suppose they are wiser than to trust women with secrets.”", "지주가 잔뜩 비웃으며 대답했다. \"함락된 도시들에 대해서는 자네 궁정 친구들더러 책임지라고 하게."], ["He accompanied this with so sarcastical a laugh, that Mrs Western could bear no longer.", "자네는 여자니까 나는 아무 탓도 않겠네. 그자들도 여자한테 비밀을 맡길 만큼 어리석진 않을 만큼은 영리할 테니까.\""], ["She had been all this time fretted in a tender part (for she was indeed very deeply skilled in these matters, and very violent in them), and therefore, burst forth in a rage, declared her brother to be both a clown and a blockhead, and that she would stay no longer in his house.", "그러고는 어찌나 빈정대며 웃어 댔던지, 웨스턴 부인은 더 참을 수가 없었다. 그녀는 줄곧 가장 예민한 데를 찔려 속이 부글거리던 터였고(사실 그녀는 이런 문제에 매우 깊이 통달해 있었고, 또 그만큼 격렬하기도 했다), 그리하여 분통을 터뜨리며 오라비를 두고 촌놈에다 멍청이라고 못 박더니, 이런 집에는 더 이상 머물지 않겠다고 선언했다."]], [["The squire, though perhaps he had never read Machiavel, was, however, in many points, a perfect politician.", "지주는 비록 마키아벨리는 읽어 본 적이 없을지 몰라도, 많은 점에서 완벽한 정치가였다."], ["He strongly held all those wise tenets, which are so well inculcated in that Politico-Peripatetic school of Exchange-alley.", "그는 익스체인지 골목의 그 정치적 소요학파에서 그토록 잘 가르치는 온갖 현명한 신조를 굳게 신봉했다."], ["He knew the just value and only use of money, viz., to lay it up.", "그는 돈의 정당한 가치와 유일한 쓰임새, 곧 차곡차곡 쌓아 두는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He was likewise well skilled in the exact value of reversions, expectations, &c., and had often considered the amount of his sister's fortune, and the chance which he or his posterity had of inheriting it.", "또한 그는 상속권과 기대 재산 따위의 정확한 가치에도 능통해 있어서, 누이의 재산이 얼마나 되는지, 그리고 그것을 자신이나 자손이 물려받을 가망이 얼마나 되는지를 곧잘 따져 보곤 했다. 이것을 하찮은 분풀이 따위에 내던지기에는 그는 한없이 영리했다."], ["This he was infinitely too wise to sacrifice to a trifling resentment.", "그래서 일을 너무 멀리 끌고 갔다 싶자, 그는 둘 사이를 화해시킬 궁리를 하기 시작했다."], ["When he found, therefore, he had carried matters too far, he began to think of reconciling them; which was no very difficult task, as the lady had great affection for her brother, and still greater for her niece; and though too susceptible of an affront offered to her skill in politics, on which she much valued herself, was a woman of a very extraordinary good and sweet disposition.", "그것은 그리 어려운 일이 아니었으니, 부인은 오라비를 무척 아꼈고, 조카딸은 그보다 더 아꼈기 때문이다. 또한 그녀가 스스로 무척 자부하던 정치 식견을 모욕당하는 데는 지나치게 발끈하긴 했어도, 본바탕은 더없이 착하고 다정한 성품의 여자였던 것이다."]], [["Having first, therefore, laid violent hands on the horses, for whose escape from the stable no place but the window was left open, he next applied himself to his sister; softened and soothed her, by unsaying all he had said, and by assertions directly contrary to those which had incensed her. Lastly, he summoned the eloquence of Sophia to his assistance, who, besides a most graceful and winning address, had the advantage of being heard with great favour and partiality by her aunt.", "그리하여 그는 우선 말들에게 우격다짐을 가했다. 말들이 마구간에서 빠져나갈 통로라고는 창문밖에 열려 있지 않았던 것이다. 그러고는 누이에게로 향해, 자신이 했던 말을 죄다 주워 담고 그녀를 분노케 했던 주장과 정반대되는 말들을 늘어놓으며 그녀를 누그러뜨리고 달랬다. 마지막으로 그는 소피아의 말솜씨를 거들미로 불러들였다. 소피아는 더없이 우아하고 사람을 끄는 화술뿐 아니라, 고모가 큰 호의와 편애로 자기 말에 귀 기울여 준다는 이점까지 누리고 있었다."]], [["The result of the whole was a kind smile from Mrs Western, who said, “Brother, you are absolutely a perfect Croat; but as those have their use in the army of the empress queen, so you likewise have some good in you. I will therefore once more sign a treaty of peace with you, and see that you do not infringe it on your side; at least, as you are so excellent a politician, I may expect you will keep your leagues, like the French, till your interest calls upon you to break them.”", "이 모든 일의 결말은 웨스턴 부인의 다정한 미소였다. 그녀가 말했다. \"오라버니, 오라버니는 영락없이 완벽한 크로아트 용병이네요. 하지만 그런 자들도 황후 폐하의 군대에서는 나름의 쓸모가 있듯, 오라버니에게도 좋은 구석은 있는 셈이죠. 그러니 한 번 더 오라버니와 평화 조약에 서명하겠어요. 다만 오라버니 쪽에서 그걸 어기지 않도록 잘 지켜보겠어요. 적어도, 오라버니가 그토록 뛰어난 정치가이시니, 프랑스 사람들처럼 자기 이해관계가 깨뜨리라고 부추기기 전까지는 동맹을 지키시리라 기대해도 되겠죠.\""]], [["Containing two defiances to the critics.", "비평가들에게 보내는 두 가지 도전장을 담은 장."]], [["The squire having settled matters with his sister, as we have seen in the last chapter, was so greatly impatient to communicate the proposal to Allworthy, that Mrs Western had the utmost difficulty to prevent him from visiting that gentleman in his sickness, for this purpose.", "앞 장에서 보았듯 누이와 일을 매듭지은 향사는 그 제안을 올워디 씨에게 전하고 싶어 안달이 어찌나 심했던지, 웨스턴 부인은 그가 병중인 그분을 이 일로 찾아가는 것을 막느라 그야말로 진땀을 빼야 했다."]], [["Mr Allworthy had been engaged to dine with Mr Western at the time when he was taken ill.", "올워디 씨는 자신이 병으로 쓰러질 무렵, 웨스턴 씨와 식사를 함께하기로 약속이 잡혀 있었다."], ["He was therefore no sooner discharged out of the custody of physic, but he thought (as was usual with him on all occasions, both the highest and the lowest) of fulfilling his engagement.", "그래서 그는 약물의 손아귀에서 풀려나기가 무섭게, (가장 큰일에서든 가장 사소한 일에서든 늘 그러했듯이) 그 약속을 지킬 생각을 했다."]], [["In the interval between the time of the dialogue in the last chapter, and this day of public entertainment, Sophia had, from certain obscure hints thrown out by her aunt, collected some apprehension that the sagacious lady suspected her passion for Jones.", "앞 장의 대화가 오간 때와 이 공식 만찬의 날 사이의 그 짬에, 소피아는 고모가 흘린 어떤 모호한 암시들로 미루어, 그 빈틈없는 부인이 자신이 존스를 향해 품은 연정을 의심하고 있다는 약간의 불안을 모으게 되었다."], ["She now resolved to take this opportunity of wiping out all such suspicion, and for that purpose to put an entire constraint on her behaviour.", "그녀는 이제 이 기회를 빌려 그런 의심을 모조리 지워 버리기로, 그리고 그 목적을 위해 자신의 행동에 완전한 굴레를 씌우기로 마음먹었다."]], [["First, she endeavoured to conceal a throbbing melancholy heart with the utmost sprightliness in her countenance, and the highest gaiety in her manner.", "먼저 그녀는 두근대는 우울한 마음을 더없이 발랄한 표정과 더없이 명랑한 태도 뒤에 숨기려 애썼다."], ["Secondly, she addressed her whole discourse to Mr Blifil, and took not the least notice of poor Jones the whole day.", "둘째로, 그녀는 자신의 모든 이야기를 블리필 씨에게 건넸고, 가엾은 존스에게는 하루 종일 눈길 한 번 주지 않았다."]], [["The squire was so delighted with this conduct of his daughter, that he scarce eat any dinner, and spent almost his whole time in watching opportunities of conveying signs of his approbation by winks and nods to his sister; who was not at first altogether so pleased with what she saw as was her brother.", "향사는 딸의 이런 처신이 어찌나 흐뭇했던지 식사를 거의 입에 대지도 못하고, 거의 온 시간을 누이에게 윙크와 끄덕임으로 흡족함의 신호를 보낼 틈을 엿보는 데 썼다. 그런데 누이는 처음에는 자신이 본 것이 오라비만큼 그렇게 마냥 흡족하지만은 않았다."]], [["In short, Sophia so greatly overacted her part, that her aunt was at first staggered, and began to suspect some affectation in her niece; but as she was herself a woman of great art, so she soon attributed this to extreme art in Sophia. She remembered the many hints she had given her niece concerning her being in love, and imagined the young lady had taken this way to rally her out of her opinion, by an overacted civility: a notion that was greatly corroborated by the excessive gaiety with which the whole was accompanied. We cannot here avoid remarking, that this conjecture would have been better founded had Sophia lived ten years in the air of Grosvenor Square, where young ladies do learn a wonderful knack of rallying and playing with that passion, which is a mighty serious thing in woods and groves an hundred miles distant from London.", "요컨대 소피아가 자신의 배역을 어찌나 과하게 연기했던지, 고모는 처음에는 흠칫하며 조카에게 어떤 꾸밈이 있는 것은 아닌가 의심하기 시작했다. 그러나 그녀 자신이 술수에 능한 여인이었던 터라, 곧 이것을 소피아의 극도의 술수 탓으로 돌렸다. 그녀는 조카가 사랑에 빠졌다는 것을 두고 자신이 던졌던 숱한 암시를 떠올리고는, 이 처녀가 과장된 예의로 자신을 놀려 그 짐작을 물리치려고 이 방법을 택한 것이라고 상상했다. 이 짐작은 그 모든 처신에 곁들여진 지나친 명랑함으로 인해 한층 더 굳어졌다. 우리는 여기서 한 가지 짚고 넘어가지 않을 수 없다. 만일 소피아가 그로스브너 광장의 공기 속에서 십 년을 살았더라면 이 추측은 한결 근거가 탄탄했으리라는 점이다. 그곳의 젊은 처녀들은 런던에서 백 마일이나 떨어진 숲과 작은 숲에서는 무척이나 진지한 일인 그 연정을 놀리고 가지고 노는 놀라운 재주를 정말로 익히기 때문이다."]], [["To say the truth, in discovering the deceit of others, it matters much that our own art be wound up, if I may use the expression, in the same key with theirs: for very artful men sometimes miscarry by fancying others wiser, or, in other words, greater knaves, than they really are.", "사실대로 말하자면, 남의 속임수를 알아채는 데 있어서는, 이렇게 표현해도 좋다면, 우리 자신의 술수가 그들의 것과 같은 가락에 맞춰 감겨 있는 것이 무척 중요하다. 매우 술수에 능한 사람들도 때로는 남들이 실제보다 더 현명하다고, 다시 말해 더 큰 악당이라고 지레짐작하다가 일을 그르치기 때문이다."], ["As this observation is pretty deep, I will illustrate it by the following short story.", "이 통찰이 제법 깊은 만큼, 나는 이를 다음의 짧은 이야기로 풀어 보이겠다. 시골 사람 셋이 윌트셔 출신 도둑 하나를 쫓아 브렌트퍼드를 지나고 있었다."], ["Three countrymen were pursuing a Wiltshire thief through Brentford.", "그중 가장 단순한 자가 어느 간판 아래 \"윌트셔 하우스\"라고 쓰인 것을 보고는, 들어가 보자고 동료들에게 권했다."], ["The simplest of them seeing “The Wiltshire House,”", "거기라면 십중팔구 자기네 고향 사람을 찾을 수 있으리라는 것이었다."], ["written under a sign, advised his companions to enter it, for there most probably they would find their countryman.", "좀 더 영리한 둘째는 이 어수룩함을 비웃었다. 그러나 한층 더 영리한 셋째가 대답했다."], ["The second, who was wiser, laughed at this simplicity; but the third, who was wiser still, answered, “Let us go in, however, for he may think we should not suspect him of going amongst his own countrymen.”", "\"그래도 들어가 보세. 그자는 우리가 설마 제 고향 사람들 틈에 끼어 있으리라고는 의심하지 않을 거라 여길지도 모르니.\""], ["They accordingly went in and searched the house, and by that means missed overtaking the thief, who was at that time but a little way before them; and who, as they all knew, but had never once reflected, could not read.", "그래서 그들은 들어가 그 집을 뒤졌고, 그 통에 도둑을 따라잡을 기회를 놓쳤다. 그 도둑은 그때 그들 바로 앞쪽 멀지 않은 곳에 있었는데, 그들 모두가 알면서도 단 한 번도 떠올리지 못했던 사실이지만, 글을 읽을 줄 모르는 자였던 것이다."]], [["The reader will pardon a digression in which so invaluable a secret is communicated, since every gamester will agree how necessary it is to know exactly the play of another, in order to countermine him.", "독자는 이토록 값진 비밀이 전해지는 이 여담을 너그러이 보아 줄 것이다. 어떤 노름꾼이든, 상대의 패를 거꾸로 무너뜨리려면 그 수를 정확히 아는 것이 얼마나 긴요한지 동의할 테니 말이다."], ["This will, moreover, afford a reason why the wiser man, as is often seen, is the bubble of the weaker, and why many simple and innocent characters are so generally misunderstood and misrepresented; but what is most material, this will account for the deceit which Sophia put on her politic aunt.", "더구나 이는 흔히 보듯 더 영리한 자가 어째서 더 어수룩한 자에게 속아 넘어가는지, 또 어째서 그토록 많은 단순하고 순진한 사람들이 그렇게나 흔히 오해받고 잘못 비쳐지는지에 대한 까닭도 제공해 줄 것이다. 그러나 무엇보다 중요한 것은, 이것이 소피아가 자신의 빈틈없는 고모에게 씌운 그 속임수를 설명해 준다는 점이다."]], [["Dinner being ended, and the company retired into the garden, Mr Western, who was thoroughly convinced of the certainty of what his sister had told him, took Mr Allworthy aside, and very bluntly proposed a match between Sophia and young Mr Blifil.", "식사가 끝나고 일행이 정원으로 물러나자, 누이가 들려준 말이 틀림없다고 철석같이 믿은 웨스턴 씨는 올워디 씨를 한쪽으로 데려가, 소피아와 젊은 블리필 씨의 혼사를 아주 무뚝뚝하게 제안했다."]], [["Mr Allworthy was not one of those men whose hearts flutter at any unexpected and sudden tidings of worldly profit.", "올워디 씨는 세속적 이익에 대한 예기치 못한 갑작스러운 소식에 가슴이 뛰는 그런 부류의 사람이 아니었다."], ["His mind was, indeed, tempered with that philosophy which becomes a man and a Christian.", "그의 마음은 실로 한 인간으로서, 또 한 그리스도인으로서 어울리는 그 철학으로 다스려져 있었다."], ["He affected no absolute superiority to all pleasure and pain, to all joy and grief; but was not at the same time to be discomposed and ruffled by every accidental blast, by every smile or frown of fortune.", "그는 모든 쾌락과 고통, 모든 기쁨과 슬픔 위에 완전히 군림하는 척하지는 않았다. 그러나 동시에 우연히 부는 바람마다, 운명의 미소나 찡그림마다 흔들리고 헝클어질 사람도 아니었다."], ["He received, therefore, Mr Western's proposal without any visible emotion, or without any alteration of countenance.", "그리하여 그는 웨스턴 씨의 제안을 겉으로 드러나는 동요 하나 없이, 표정의 변화 하나 없이 받아들였다."], ["He said the alliance was such as he sincerely wished; then launched forth into a very just encomium on the young lady's merit; acknowledged the offer to be advantageous in point of fortune; and after thanking Mr Western for the good opinion he had professed of his nephew, concluded, that if the young people liked each other, he should be very desirous to complete the affair.", "그는 이 혼인이 자신이 진심으로 바라던 바라고 말했고, 이어 그 처녀의 덕에 대한 매우 정당한 찬사를 늘어놓았다. 또 재산의 측면에서 이 제안이 유리함을 인정했고, 자신의 조카를 좋게 보아 준 데 대해 웨스턴 씨에게 감사를 표한 뒤, 만일 젊은이들이 서로 마음에 들어 한다면 자신도 이 일을 매듭짓기를 몹시 바란다고 결론지었다."]], [["Western was a little disappointed at Mr Allworthy's answer, which was not so warm as he expected. He treated the doubt whether the young people might like one another with great contempt, saying, “That parents were the best judges of proper matches for their children: that for his part he should insist on the most resigned obedience from his daughter: and if any young fellow could refuse such a bed-fellow, he was his humble servant, and hoped there was no harm done.”", "웨스턴은 올워디 씨의 대답이 자신이 기대한 만큼 따뜻하지 않아 다소 실망했다. 그는 젊은이들이 서로 마음에 들어 할지 어떨지에 대한 그 의문을 크게 깔보며 이렇게 말했다. \"자식에게 알맞은 혼처를 가리는 데는 부모가 가장 훌륭한 판관이지요. 나로 말하자면 딸에게 더없이 고분고분한 복종을 요구할 작정입니다. 그리고 어떤 젊은 녀석이든 그런 잠자리 동무를 마다할 수 있다면야, 나야 그저 그의 미천한 종이외다, 부디 아무 탈도 없기를 바랄 뿐이오.\""]], [["Allworthy endeavoured to soften this resentment by many eulogiums on Sophia, declaring he had no doubt but that Mr Blifil would very gladly receive the offer; but all was ineffectual; he could obtain no other answer from the squire but--“I say no more--I humbly hope there's no harm done--that's all.” Which words he repeated at least a hundred times before they parted.", "올워디는 소피아에 대한 숱한 찬사로 이 노여움을 누그러뜨리려 애쓰며, 블리필 씨가 이 제안을 기꺼이 받아들이리라는 데 한 점 의심도 없다고 단언했다. 그러나 모두 헛수고였다. 그는 향사에게서 이런 대답밖에 얻어 내지 못했다. \"더는 말하지 않겠소. 그저 아무 탈도 없기를 삼가 바랄 뿐이오. 그게 다요.\" 그는 이 말을 헤어지기 전에 적어도 백 번은 되풀이했다."]], [["Allworthy was too well acquainted with his neighbour to be offended at this behaviour; and though he was so averse to the rigour which some parents exercise on their children in the article of marriage, that he had resolved never to force his nephew's inclinations, he was nevertheless much pleased with the prospect of this union; for the whole country resounded the praises of Sophia, and he had himself greatly admired the uncommon endowments of both her mind and person.", "올워디는 이 이웃을 너무도 잘 아는지라 이런 처신에 기분이 상하지는 않았다. 또 그는 혼인 문제에서 어떤 부모들이 자식에게 휘두르는 그 가혹함을 몹시 못마땅히 여겨, 조카의 마음을 결코 억지로 꺾지 않으리라 다짐해 둔 터였음에도, 이 혼사의 전망에 무척 흐뭇해했다. 온 고장이 소피아의 칭송으로 떠들썩했고, 그 자신도 그녀의 마음과 인물 양쪽에 깃든 보기 드문 자질을 크게 흠모해 왔던 까닭이다."]], [["To which I believe we may add, the consideration of her vast fortune, which, though he was too sober to be intoxicated with it, he was too sensible to despise.", "여기에 우리는 그녀의 막대한 재산에 대한 고려도 보탤 수 있으리라 생각한다. 그는 그 재산에 도취되기에는 너무 진중했으나, 또한 그것을 가벼이 여기기에는 너무 분별 있는 사람이었다."]], [["And here, in defiance of all the barking critics in the world, I must and will introduce a digression concerning true wisdom, of which Mr Allworthy was in reality as great a pattern as he was of goodness.", "그리고 여기서, 세상의 짖어 대는 모든 비평가에게 맞서, 나는 참된 지혜에 관한 여담을 기어이 끌어들이지 않을 수 없다. 올워디 씨는 실로 선함의 본보기였던 만큼이나 이 참된 지혜의 본보기이기도 했으니 말이다."]], [["True wisdom then, notwithstanding all which Mr Hogarth's poor poet may have writ against riches, and in spite of all which any rich well-fed divine may have preached against pleasure, consists not in the contempt of either of these.", "참된 지혜란, 호가스 씨가 그린 가난한 시인이 부에 맞서 무어라 써 갈겼든, 또 잘 먹고 사는 어떤 부유한 성직자가 쾌락에 맞서 무어라 설교했든, 이 둘 중 어느 것을 경멸하는 데 있지 않다."], ["A man may have as much wisdom in the possession of an affluent fortune, as any beggar in the streets; or may enjoy a handsome wife or a hearty friend, and still remain as wise as any sour popish recluse, who buries all his social faculties, and starves his belly while he well lashes his back.", "사람은 넉넉한 재산을 누리면서도 거리의 어느 거지 못지않은 지혜를 지닐 수 있고, 또 어여쁜 아내나 마음 든든한 벗을 누리면서도, 자신의 모든 사교적 능력을 묻어 버리고 등을 호되게 매질하며 배를 굶기는 그 어떤 시큰둥한 가톨릭 은둔자 못지않게 여전히 지혜로울 수 있다."]], [["To say truth, the wisest man is the likeliest to possess all worldly blessings in an eminent degree; for as that moderation which wisdom prescribes is the surest way to useful wealth, so can it alone qualify us to taste many pleasures.", "사실대로 말하자면, 가장 지혜로운 사람이야말로 세상의 온갖 복을 가장 높은 경지로 누릴 가능성이 크다. 지혜가 일러 주는 그 절제야말로 쓸모 있는 부에 이르는 가장 확실한 길이듯, 오직 그 절제만이 우리로 하여금 숱한 쾌락을 맛볼 자격을 갖추게 하기 때문이다."], ["The wise man gratifies every appetite and every passion, while the fool sacrifices all the rest to pall and satiate one.", "지혜로운 사람은 온갖 욕구와 온갖 열정을 두루 채우지만, 어리석은 자는 단 하나를 물리도록 채우고 물려 버리느라 나머지 전부를 희생한다."]], [["It may be objected, that very wise men have been notoriously avaricious.", "매우 지혜로운 사람들이 소문난 탐욕가였던 적이 있다고 반박할지도 모른다. 나는 답한다."], ["I answer, Not wise in that instance.", "그 점에서는 지혜롭지 않았던 것이라고."], ["It may likewise be said, That the wisest men have been in their youth immoderately fond of pleasure.", "또 가장 지혜로운 사람들이 젊은 시절 도를 넘게 쾌락을 탐했다고도 할지 모른다. 나는 답한다."], ["I answer, They were not wise then.", "그때 그들은 지혜롭지 않았던 것이라고."]], [["Wisdom, in short, whose lessons have been represented as so hard to learn by those who never were at her school, only teaches us to extend a simple maxim universally known and followed even in the lowest life, a little farther than that life carries it.", "요컨대 지혜란, 한 번도 그 학교에 다녀 본 적 없는 자들이 배우기 너무 어려운 것인 양 떠들어 댔지만, 다만 가장 비천한 삶에서조차 누구나 알고 따르는 단순한 격언 하나를, 그 삶이 데려가는 데서 조금 더 멀리까지 늘이는 법을 가르칠 뿐이다."], ["And this is, not to buy at too dear a price.", "그 격언이란 바로, 너무 비싼 값을 치르고 사지 말라는 것이다."]], [["Now, whoever takes this maxim abroad with him into the grand market of the world, and constantly applies it to honours, to riches, to pleasures, and to every other commodity which that market affords, is, I will venture to affirm, a wise man, and must be so acknowledged in the worldly sense of the word; for he makes the best of bargains, since in reality he purchases everything at the price only of a little trouble, and carries home all the good things I have mentioned, while he keeps his health, his innocence, and his reputation, the common prices which are paid for them by others, entire and to himself.", "이제 누구든 이 격언을 품고 세상이라는 거대한 장터로 나아가, 명예에, 부에, 쾌락에, 그리고 그 장터가 내놓는 그 밖의 온갖 물건에 끊임없이 이를 적용하는 사람이라면, 나는 감히 단언하건대 지혜로운 사람이며, 세속적인 의미에서도 마땅히 그렇게 인정받아야 한다. 그는 가장 좋은 흥정을 해내는 셈이기 때문이다. 실로 그는 모든 것을 그저 약간의 수고라는 값만으로 사들이며, 내가 앞서 말한 온갖 좋은 것들을 집으로 가져가면서도, 남들이 그것들에 흔히 치르는 값인 자신의 건강과 순결과 명성을 고스란히 제 것으로 온전히 지켜 내기 때문이다."]], [["From this moderation, likewise, he learns two other lessons, which complete his character.", "이 절제에서 그는 또한 자신의 인격을 완성해 주는 다른 두 가지 가르침도 배운다."], ["First, never to be intoxicated when he hath made the best bargain, nor dejected when the market is empty, or when its commodities are too dear for his purchase.", "첫째, 가장 좋은 흥정을 해냈을 때 결코 도취되지 않는 것, 그리고 장터가 비었거나 그 물건들이 제 주머니로 사기에 너무 비쌀 때 결코 풀이 죽지 않는 것이다."]], [["But I must remember on what subject I am writing, and not trespass too far on the patience of a good-natured critic.", "그러나 나는 내가 무엇을 주제로 글을 쓰고 있는지 잊지 말아야 하고, 마음씨 좋은 비평가의 인내심을 너무 멀리까지 침범해서도 안 되겠다."], ["Here, therefore, I put an end to the chapter.", "그러니 여기서 나는 이 장을 끝맺는다."]], [["Containing sundry curious matters.", "여러 가지 흥미로운 사정이 담긴 장."]], [["As soon as Mr Allworthy returned home, he took Mr Blifil apart, and after some preface, communicated to him the proposal which had been made by Mr Western, and at the same time informed him how agreeable this match would be to himself.", "올워디 씨는 집에 돌아오자마자 블리필 씨를 따로 불러, 몇 마디 운을 뗀 뒤 웨스턴 씨가 내놓은 제안을 그에게 전했다. 그러면서 동시에 이 혼사가 자신에게 얼마나 흡족한 일인지도 일러 주었다."]], [["The charms of Sophia had not made the least impression on Blifil; not that his heart was pre-engaged; neither was he totally insensible of beauty, or had any aversion to women; but his appetites were by nature so moderate, that he was able, by philosophy, or by study, or by some other method, easily to subdue them: and as to that passion which we have treated of in the first chapter of this book, he had not the least tincture of it in his whole composition.", "소피아의 매력은 블리필에게 손톱만큼의 인상도 남기지 못했다. 그의 마음이 이미 다른 데 가 있어서가 아니었다. 아름다움에 전혀 둔감하다거나 여자를 싫어해서도 아니었다. 다만 그의 욕망이 본래부터 어찌나 절제되어 있던지, 그는 철학으로든 학문으로든 아니면 다른 어떤 방법으로든 그것을 손쉽게 억누를 수 있었다. 그리고 이 책 제1장에서 우리가 다룬 바 있는 그 정념으로 말하자면, 그의 됨됨이 전체를 통틀어 그 기색이라곤 티끌만큼도 없었다."]], [["But though he was so entirely free from that mixed passion, of which we there treated, and of which the virtues and beauty of Sophia formed so notable an object; yet was he altogether as well furnished with some other passions, that promised themselves very full gratification in the young lady's fortune.", "하지만 우리가 거기서 다루었던, 소피아의 덕과 아름다움이 그토록 빼어난 대상을 이루었던 그 뒤섞인 정념으로부터는 완전히 자유로웠을지언정, 그는 또 다른 정념들만큼은 똑같이 넉넉히 갖추고 있었다. 그 정념들은 이 젊은 아가씨의 재산에서 더없이 충만한 만족을 약속받았다."], ["Such were avarice and ambition, which divided the dominion of his mind between them.", "바로 탐욕과 야심이었으니, 이 둘은 그의 마음의 지배권을 사이좋게 나눠 가지고 있었다."], ["He had more than once considered the possession of this fortune as a very desirable thing, and had entertained some distant views concerning it; but his own youth, and that of the young lady, and indeed principally a reflection that Mr Western might marry again, and have more children, had restrained him from too hasty or eager a pursuit.", "그는 이 재산을 손에 넣는 일을 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번이나 무척 탐나는 것으로 여겨 왔고, 그에 관해 막연한 속셈을 품기도 했다. 그러나 자기 자신의 젊음과 그 아가씨의 어린 나이, 그리고 무엇보다도 웨스턴 씨가 재혼하여 자식을 더 둘지도 모른다는 생각이 그를 너무 성급하거나 조급한 추구에서 붙들어 두었다."]], [["This last and most material objection was now in great measure removed, as the proposal came from Mr Western himself.", "이 마지막이자 가장 중대한 걸림돌이 이제 상당 부분 사라졌으니, 제안이 다름 아닌 웨스턴 씨 본인에게서 나왔기 때문이다."], ["Blifil, therefore, after a very short hesitation, answered Mr Allworthy, that matrimony was a subject on which he had not yet thought; but that he was so sensible of his friendly and fatherly care, that he should in all things submit himself to his pleasure.", "그리하여 블리필은 아주 잠깐 망설인 뒤 올워디 씨에게 대답하기를, 혼인이란 자기가 아직 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없는 주제이지만, 숙부의 다정하고도 아버지 같은 보살핌을 너무도 깊이 느끼고 있으니 모든 일에서 그분의 뜻에 따르겠노라고 했다."]], [["Allworthy was naturally a man of spirit, and his present gravity arose from true wisdom and philosophy, not from any original phlegm in his disposition; for he had possessed much fire in his youth, and had married a beautiful woman for love. He was not therefore greatly pleased with this cold answer of his nephew; nor could he help launching forth into the praises of Sophia, and expressing some wonder that the heart of a young man could be impregnable to the force of such charms, unless it was guarded by some prior affection.", "올워디는 본디 혈기 있는 사람이었고, 지금의 진중함도 천성에 깃든 무덤덤함에서가 아니라 참된 지혜와 철학에서 비롯된 것이었다. 젊은 시절에는 가슴속에 불길을 많이 품었던 사람이었고, 사랑으로 아름다운 여인과 결혼하기도 했으니 말이다. 그래서 그는 조카의 이런 미적지근한 대답이 그리 흡족하지 않았다. 그는 소피아에 대한 칭찬을 늘어놓지 않을 수 없었고, 젊은이의 마음이 그런 매력의 힘에도 끄떡없다는 것이 좀 의아하다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 다만 그 마음이 이미 다른 정으로 단단히 지켜지고 있는 게 아니라면 말이다."]], [["Blifil assured him he had no such guard; and then proceeded to discourse so wisely and religiously on love and marriage, that he would have stopt the mouth of a parent much less devoutly inclined than was his uncle.", "블리필은 자기에게 그런 방패 따위는 없다고 숙부를 안심시켰다. 그러고는 사랑과 결혼에 관하여 어찌나 지혜롭고 경건하게 늘어놓았던지, 자기 숙부보다 신앙심이 훨씬 옅은 부모라 해도 그 입을 다물게 할 정도였다."], ["In the end, the good man was satisfied that his nephew, far from having any objections to Sophia, had that esteem for her, which in sober and virtuous minds is the sure foundation of friendship and love.", "결국 이 선량한 분은 흡족한 마음으로 이렇게 믿게 되었다. 조카는 소피아에게 아무런 거부감도 없을뿐더러, 오히려 그녀를 깍듯이 존경하는데, 이런 존경이야말로 진중하고 덕 있는 마음에서는 우정과 사랑의 든든한 주춧돌이라고 말이다."], ["And as he doubted not but the lover would, in a little time, become altogether as agreeable to his mistress, he foresaw great happiness arising to all parties by so proper and desirable an union.", "또한 그는 머지않아 이 구혼자가 그 연인에게도 똑같이 마음에 드는 사람이 되리라 의심치 않았기에, 이토록 합당하고 바람직한 결합으로 모든 이에게 큰 행복이 찾아오리라 내다보았다."], ["With Mr Blifil's consent therefore he wrote the next morning to Mr Western, acquainting him that his nephew had very thankfully and gladly received the proposal, and would be ready to wait on the young lady, whenever she should be pleased to accept his visit.", "그리하여 블리필 씨의 동의를 얻어 이튿날 아침 웨스턴 씨에게 편지를 썼다. 조카가 그 제안을 무척 고맙고 기쁘게 받아들였으며, 아가씨가 그의 방문을 기꺼이 받아들이고자 하실 때면 언제든 찾아뵐 준비가 되어 있다는 내용이었다."]], [["Western was much pleased with this letter, and immediately returned an answer; in which, without having mentioned a word to his daughter, he appointed that very afternoon for opening the scene of courtship.", "웨스턴은 이 편지에 무척 기뻐하며 곧바로 답장을 보냈다. 그 답장에서 그는 딸에게는 한마디 말도 꺼내지 않은 채, 바로 그날 오후를 구애의 막을 여는 시각으로 정해 버렸다."]], [["As soon as he had dispatched this messenger, he went in quest of his sister, whom he found reading and expounding the Gazette to parson Supple.", "이 심부름꾼을 떠나보내자마자 그는 누이를 찾아 나섰는데, 마침 누이는 서플 목사에게 《가제트》를 읽어 주며 풀이해 주고 있었다."], ["To this exposition he was obliged to attend near a quarter of an hour, though with great violence to his natural impetuosity, before he was suffered to speak.", "그는 입을 열기까지 그 풀이를 거의 십오 분이나 들어 주어야 했으니, 본래 다혈질인 그에게는 여간 고역이 아니었다."], ["At length, however, he found an opportunity of acquainting the lady, that he had business of great consequence to impart to her; to which she answered, “Brother, I am entirely at your service.", "그래도 마침내 그는 누이에게 더없이 중대한 용건이 있노라 알릴 기회를 잡았고, 그러자 누이가 대답했다. \"오라버니, 저는 온전히 오라버니께 마음을 쏟고 있어요."], ["Things look so well in the north, that I was never in a better humour.”", "북쪽 정세가 어찌나 좋게 돌아가는지, 제 기분이 이보다 더 좋았던 적이 없답니다.\""]], [["The parson then withdrawing, Western acquainted her with all which had passed, and desired her to communicate the affair to Sophia, which she readily and chearfully undertook; though perhaps her brother was a little obliged to that agreeable northern aspect which had so delighted her, that he heard no comment on his proceedings; for they were certainly somewhat too hasty and violent.", "그러자 목사가 물러갔고, 웨스턴은 누이에게 그간의 일을 모조리 알린 뒤 그 일을 소피아에게 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 누이는 기꺼이, 그리고 흔쾌히 그 일을 맡았다. 어쩌면 그 오라버니는 누이를 그토록 들뜨게 한 그 흐뭇한 북쪽 정세 덕을 조금 본 셈이었으니, 덕분에 그는 자기 처사에 대한 잔소리를 한마디도 듣지 않을 수 있었다. 그의 처사란 분명 다소 지나치게 성급하고 거칠었으니 말이다."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["In which is related what passed between Sophia and her aunt. Sophia was in her chamber, reading, when her aunt came in.", "소피아는 자기 방에서 책을 읽고 있었는데, 그때 고모가 들어왔다."], ["The moment she saw Mrs Western, she shut the book with so much eagerness, that the good lady could not forbear asking her, What book that was which she seemed so much afraid of showing?", "웨스턴 부인을 보는 순간 소피아가 어찌나 황급히 책을 덮던지, 마음씨 고운 부인은 그토록 보여 주기를 꺼리는 듯한 그 책이 무엇이냐고 묻지 않을 수 없었다. \"정말이지, 고모님,\""], ["“Upon my word, madam,”", "소피아가 대답했다."], ["answered Sophia, “it is a book which I am neither ashamed nor afraid to own I have read.", "\"이 책은 제가 읽었다는 걸 부끄러워하지도, 두려워하지도 않을 책이에요."], ["It is the production of a young lady of fashion, whose good understanding, I think, doth honour to her sex, and whose good heart is an honour to human nature.”", "한 젊은 숙녀가 쓴 작품인데, 제 생각엔 그분의 빼어난 식견은 여성에게 영예가 되고, 그분의 어진 마음은 인간 본성에 영예가 됩니다.\""], ["Mrs Western then took up the book, and immediately after threw it down, saying--“Yes, the author is of a very good family; but she is not much among people one knows.", "그러자 웨스턴 부인이 책을 집어 들었다가 곧바로 내던지며 말했다. \"그래, 작가는 아주 좋은 집안 출신이지."], ["I have never read it; for the best judges say, there is not much in it.”--“I dare not, madam, set up my own opinion,”", "하지만 우리가 아는 사람들 사이에는 별로 끼지 못하는 사람이야. 나는 읽어 본 적이 없어."], ["says Sophia, “against the best judges, but there appears to me a great deal of human nature in it; and in many parts so much true tenderness and delicacy, that it hath cost me many a tear.”--“Ay, and do you love to cry then?”", "안목 있는 사람들 말로는 별 내용이 없다더구나.\" \"고모님, 저는 감히 안목 있는 분들에 맞서 제 의견을 내세울 수는 없어요.\""], ["says the aunt. “I love a tender sensation,”", "소피아가 말했다."], ["answered the niece, “and would pay the price of a tear for it at any time.”--“Well, but show me,”", "\"하지만 제게는 그 안에 인간 본성이 가득해 보였고, 여러 대목에서 어찌나 진실한 다정함과 섬세함이 깃들어 있던지, 그 때문에 여러 번 눈물을 흘렸어요.\" \"아니, 그래 너는 우는 걸 좋아하느냐?\""], ["said the aunt, “what was you reading when I came in; there was something very tender in that, I believe, and very loving too.", "고모가 말했다. \"저는 마음을 울리는 느낌을 사랑해요,\""], ["You blush, my dear Sophia. Ah!", "조카가 대답했다."], ["child, you should read books which would teach you a little hypocrisy, which would instruct you how to hide your thoughts a little better.”--“I hope, madam,”", "\"그걸 위해서라면 언제든 눈물 한 방울쯤은 기꺼이 치르겠어요.\" \"그래, 그건 그렇고, 어디 보자,\""], ["answered Sophia, “I have no thoughts which I ought to be ashamed of discovering.”--“Ashamed!", "고모가 말했다. \"내가 들어왔을 때 읽고 있던 게 뭐였니."], ["no,” cries the aunt, “I don't think you have any thoughts which you ought to be ashamed of; and yet, child, you blushed just now when I mentioned the word loving.", "거기엔 무언가 아주 다정한 게 있었을 것 같구나."], ["Dear Sophy, be assured you have not one thought which I am not well acquainted with; as well, child, as the French are with our motions, long before we put them in execution.", "그리고 아주 애틋한 것도 말이야. 얼굴을 붉히는구나, 소피아야."], ["Did you think, child, because you have been able to impose upon your father, that you could impose upon me?", "아! 얘야, 너는 위선을 좀 가르쳐 줄 만한 책을, 네 속마음을 조금 더 잘 감추는 법을 일러 줄 책을 읽어야겠다.\""], ["Do you imagine I did not know the reason of your overacting all that friendship for Mr Blifil yesterday?", "\"고모님, 저는 드러내기 부끄러워해야 할 생각 따위는 없는 것 같아요.\""], ["I have seen a little too much of the world, to be so deceived.", "소피아가 대답했다. \"부끄러워해?"], ["Nay, nay, do not blush again.", "아니지,\" 고모가 외쳤다."], ["I tell you it is a passion you need not be ashamed of.", "\"나는 네게 부끄러워해야 할 생각이 있다고는 보지 않아."], ["It is a passion I myself approve, and have already brought your father into the approbation of it.", "그런데도, 얘야, 방금 내가 '애틋하다'는 말을 꺼냈을 때 너는 얼굴을 붉혔지. 소피야, 명심하렴."], ["Indeed, I solely consider your inclination; for I would always have that gratified, if possible, though one may sacrifice higher prospects.", "내가 훤히 알지 못하는 생각은 네게 하나도 없단다. 우리가 어떤 일을 실행에 옮기기 한참 전부터 프랑스 사람들이 우리 움직임을 다 알고 있는 것만큼이나 환히 알고 있어."], ["Come, I have news which will delight your very soul.", "얘야, 네가 아버지는 속일 수 있었다고 해서 나까지 속일 수 있을 거라 생각했니?"], ["Make me your confident, and I will undertake you shall be happy to the very extent of your wishes.” “La, madam,”", "어제 네가 블리필 씨에게 그 우정을 그토록 과장해 보인 까닭을 내가 모를 줄 알았어?"], ["says Sophia, looking more foolishly than ever she did in her life, “I know not what to say--why, madam, should you suspect?”--“Nay, no dishonesty,” returned Mrs Western.", "나는 세상을 너무 많이 봐서 그렇게 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니란다."], ["“Consider, you are speaking to one of your own sex, to an aunt, and I hope you are convinced you speak to a friend.", "아니, 아니, 또 얼굴 붉히지 마라. 그건 부끄러워할 필요 없는 감정이라고 내가 말하잖니."], ["Consider, you are only revealing to me what I know already, and what I plainly saw yesterday, through that most artful of all disguises, which you had put on, and which must have deceived any one who had not perfectly known the world.", "그건 나 자신도 인정하는 감정이고, 이미 네 아버지도 그걸 인정하도록 끌어들였단다. 정말이지, 나는 오로지 네 마음만을 헤아리고 있어."], ["Lastly, consider it is a passion which I highly approve.” “La, madam,”", "가능하기만 하다면 나는 언제나 그것을 채워 주고 싶거든."], ["says Sophia, “you come upon one so unawares, and on a sudden.", "비록 더 높은 가망을 희생하는 한이 있더라도 말이야."], ["To be sure, madam, I am not blind--and certainly, if it be a fault to see all human perfections assembled together--but is it possible my father and you, madam, can see with my eyes?” “I tell you,”", "자, 네 영혼을 더없이 기쁘게 할 소식이 있단다."], ["answered the aunt, “we do entirely approve; and this very afternoon your father hath appointed for you to receive your lover.”", "나를 네 속내를 털어놓을 사람으로 삼으렴. 그러면 네가 바라는 그 끝까지 행복해지도록 내가 책임지마.\""], ["“My father, this afternoon!”", "\"어머, 고모님,\""], ["cries Sophia, with the blood starting from her face.--“Yes, child,”", "소피아가 평생 어느 때보다도 어리석어 보이는 표정으로 말했다."], ["said the aunt, “this afternoon.", "\"뭐라 말해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["You know the impetuosity of my brother's temper.", "어째서, 고모님, 무얼 의심하시는 거예요?\" \"아니, 거짓은 안 된다.\""], ["I acquainted him with the passion which I first discovered in you that evening when you fainted away in the field.", "웨스턴 부인이 받아쳤다. \"생각해 보렴, 너는 같은 여자에게, 고모에게 말하고 있어."], ["I saw it in your fainting. I saw it immediately upon your recovery.", "그리고 나는 네가 벗에게 말하고 있다는 걸 확신하길 바란다."], ["I saw it that evening at supper, and the next morning at breakfast (you know, child, I have seen the world). Well, I no sooner acquainted my brother, but he immediately wanted to propose it to Allworthy.", "생각해 보렴, 너는 내가 이미 알고 있는 것을, 어제 네가 둘러쓴 그 온갖 위장 중에서도 가장 교묘한 위장을 꿰뚫어 내가 똑똑히 본 것을 내게 털어놓고 있을 뿐이야."], ["He proposed it yesterday, Allworthy consented (as to be sure he must with joy), and this afternoon, I tell you, you are to put on all your best airs.”", "세상을 완벽하게 알지 못하는 사람이라면 누구라도 속아 넘어갔을 그런 위장 말이다. 끝으로, 그것이 내가 크게 인정하는 감정이라는 걸 생각하렴.\""], ["“This afternoon!”", "\"어머, 고모님,\""], ["cries Sophia.", "소피아가 말했다."], ["“Dear aunt, you frighten me out of my senses.” “O, my dear,”", "\"이렇게 갑작스럽게, 느닷없이 들이대시니."], ["said the aunt, “you will soon come to yourself again; for he is a charming young fellow, that's the truth on't.”", "분명, 고모님, 저도 눈이 멀지는 않았어요. 그리고 정말로, 인간의 온갖 완벽함이 한데 모인 것을 보는 게 잘못이라면 모르겠지만, 아버지와 고모님이, 정말 제 눈으로 보시는 것과 같이 보실 수 있을까요?\""], ["“Nay, I will own,”", "\"내 말하잖니,\""], ["says Sophia, “I know none with such perfections.", "고모가 대답했다. \"우리는 전적으로 인정한다고."], ["So brave, and yet so gentle; so witty, yet so inoffensive; so humane, so civil, so genteel, so handsome!", "그리고 바로 오늘 오후로 네 아버지가 네가 네 연인을 맞이할 시간을 정해 두셨어.\""], ["What signifies his being base born, when compared with such qualifications as these?”", "\"아버지가, 오늘 오후에요!\" 소피아가 외쳤다."], ["“Base born? What do you mean?”", "그 얼굴에서 핏기가 확 가셨다."], ["said the aunt, “Mr Blifil base born!”", "\"그래, 얘야,\" 고모가 말했다."], ["Sophia turned instantly pale at this name, and faintly repeated it.", "\"오늘 오후란다. 너도 내 오라버니의 급한 성미를 알잖니."], ["Upon which the aunt cried, “Mr Blifil--ay, Mr Blifil, of whom else have we been talking?” “Good heavens,”", "네가 들판에서 기절했던 그날 저녁, 내가 네게서 처음 발견한 그 감정을 오라버니에게 알려 드렸지."], ["answered Sophia, ready to sink, “of Mr Jones, I thought; I am sure I know no other who deserves--” “I protest,”", "나는 네가 기절할 때 그걸 봤어."], ["cries the aunt, “you frighten me in your turn.", "네가 정신을 차리자마자 곧바로 봤지."], ["Is it Mr Jones, and not Mr Blifil, who is the object of your affection?” “Mr Blifil!”", "그날 저녁 만찬 자리에서도 봤고, 이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서도 봤단다(얘야, 나는 세상을 봐 온 사람이라니까)."], ["repeated Sophia. “Sure it is impossible you can be in earnest; if you are, I am the most miserable woman alive.”", "그래, 내가 오라버니에게 알리자마자 곧장 올워디 씨에게 그 얘기를 꺼내려 하셨지."], ["Mrs Western now stood a few moments silent, while sparks of fiery rage flashed from her eyes.", "어제 그걸 제안했고, 올워디 씨가 승낙했어(물론 기쁘게 그러셨을 게 분명하지)."], ["At length, collecting all her force of voice, she thundered forth in the following articulate sounds:", "그러니 오늘 오후, 내 말하잖니, 너는 네 가장 멋진 자태를 다 갖추어 입어야 한단다.\""]], [["“And is it possible you can think of disgracing your family by allying yourself to a bastard? Can the blood of the Westerns submit to such contamination? If you have not sense sufficient to restrain such monstrous inclinations, I thought the pride of our family would have prevented you from giving the least encouragement to so base an affection; much less did I imagine you would ever have had the assurance to own it to my face.” “Madam,” answered Sophia, trembling, “what I have said you have extorted from me. I do not remember to have ever mentioned the name of Mr Jones with approbation to any one before; nor should I now had I not conceived he had your approbation. Whatever were my thoughts of that poor, unhappy young man, I intended to have carried them with me to my grave--to that grave where only now, I find, I am to seek repose.” Here she sunk down in her chair, drowned in her tears, and, in all the moving silence of unutterable grief, presented a spectacle which must have affected almost the hardest heart.", "\"오늘 오후에요!\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"고모님, 저를 정신이 나가도록 겁주시네요.\" \"오, 얘야,\" 고모가 말했다. \"너는 곧 다시 제정신으로 돌아올 거란다. 그는 매력적인 젊은이거든, 그게 사실이니까.\" \"아니, 인정해요,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그만한 완벽함을 갖춘 사람은 저도 알지 못해요. 그렇게 용감하면서도 그렇게 부드럽고, 그렇게 재치 있으면서도 그렇게 남의 마음 상하게 하지 않고, 그렇게 인정 많고, 그렇게 예의 바르고, 그렇게 품위 있고, 그렇게 잘생겼으니! 이런 자질들에 견주면 그가 천하게 태어났다는 게 무슨 대수겠어요?\" \"천하게 태어나? 그게 무슨 말이냐?\" 고모가 말했다. \"블리필 씨가 천하게 태어나다니!\" 소피아는 그 이름에 순간 새파랗게 질려 가냘프게 그 이름을 되뇌었다. 그러자 고모가 외쳤다. \"블리필 씨 말이다. 그래, 블리필 씨, 우리가 그 말고 누구 얘기를 하고 있었겠니?\" \"맙소사,\" 소피아가 금방이라도 쓰러질 듯이 대답했다. \"저는 존스 씨 얘긴 줄 알았어요. 그만한 자격을 갖춘 다른 사람은 정말 알지 못하는걸요.\" \"맙소사,\" 고모가 외쳤다. \"이번엔 네가 나를 겁주는구나. 네 애정의 대상이 블리필 씨가 아니라 존스 씨란 말이냐?\" \"블리필 씨라고요!\" 소피아가 되뇌었다. \"정말 진심이실 리는 없겠죠. 만약 진심이시라면, 저는 살아 있는 여자 중에 가장 비참한 여자예요.\" 웨스턴 부인은 이제 몇 순간 말없이 서 있었고, 그동안 두 눈에서는 불같은 분노의 불꽃이 번쩍였다. 마침내 목소리의 온 힘을 끌어모아 다음과 같이 또렷한 소리로 우레같이 내뱉었다."]], [["All this tender sorrow, however, raised no compassion in her aunt.", "\"그래, 네가 사생아 따위와 짝을 맺어 네 가문을 욕보일 생각을 할 수 있단 말이냐? 웨스턴 가의 핏줄이 그런 오염을 받아들일 수 있어?"], ["On the contrary, she now fell into the most violent rage.--“And I would rather,”", "그런 끔찍한 마음을 억누를 만한 분별이 네게 없다 하더라도, 나는 우리 가문의 자긍심만큼은 네가 그토록 천한 애정에 손톱만큼의 부추김도 주지 못하게 막아 주리라 여겼다."], ["she cried, in a most vehement voice, “follow you to your grave, than I would see you disgrace yourself and your family by such a match.", "더더욱 네가 그것을 내 면전에 대고 인정할 만한 뻔뻔함을 가지리라고는 상상조차 못 했어.\" \"고모님,\""], ["O Heavens!", "소피아가 떨면서 대답했다."], ["could I have ever suspected that I should live to hear a niece of mine declare a passion for such a fellow?", "\"제가 말씀드린 건 고모님이 제게서 억지로 끌어내신 거예요. 저는 전에 누구에게도 존스 씨의 이름을 좋게 입에 올린 기억이 없어요."], ["You are the first--yes, Miss Western, you are the first of your name who ever entertained so grovelling a thought.", "그리고 그분이 고모님의 인정을 받고 있다고 여기지 않았더라면 지금도 그러지 않았을 거예요. 그 가엾고 불행한 젊은이에 대한 제 마음이 어떠했든, 저는 그것을 무덤까지 가지고 갈 작정이었어요."], ["A family so noted for the prudence of its women”--here she ran on a full quarter of an hour, till, having exhausted her breath rather than her rage, she concluded with threatening to go immediately and acquaint her brother.", "이제 보니 제가 안식을 찾아야 할 곳은 오직 그 무덤뿐이군요.\" 여기서 그녀는 눈물에 잠긴 채 의자에 무너지듯 주저앉았고, 말로 다 할 수 없는 슬픔이 자아내는 그 가슴 저미는 침묵 속에서, 거의 가장 모진 마음이라도 움직였을 광경을 자아냈다."]], [["Sophia then threw herself at her feet, and laying hold of her hands, begged her with tears to conceal what she had drawn from her; urging the violence of her father's temper, and protesting that no inclinations of hers should ever prevail with her to do anything which might offend him. Mrs Western stood a moment looking at her, and then, having recollected herself, said, “That on one consideration only she would keep the secret from her brother; and this was, that Sophia should promise to entertain Mr Blifil that very afternoon as her lover, and to regard him as the person who was to be her husband.”", "그러나 이 애틋한 슬픔도 고모에게는 아무런 연민을 불러일으키지 못했다. 오히려 그녀는 이제 더없이 격렬한 분노에 빠져들었다. \"나라면 차라리,\" 그녀가 더없이 사나운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"네가 그런 혼인으로 너 자신과 가문을 욕보이는 꼴을 보느니, 너를 무덤까지 따라가는 편을 택하겠다. 오 하늘이시여! 내 조카가 그런 작자에게 연정을 품었노라 선언하는 걸 들으려고 내가 이렇게 살아 있었다니, 내 어찌 짐작이나 했겠느냐? 네가 처음이야. 그래, 웨스턴 아가씨, 네가 그 성을 가진 사람 중에 그토록 비천한 생각을 품은 첫 번째 사람이다. 여인들의 분별로 그토록 이름난 가문에서.\" 여기서 그녀는 족히 십오 분을 내리 떠들어 댔고, 마침내 분노가 아니라 숨이 다 빠져 버린 끝에, 당장 가서 오라버니에게 알리겠다는 위협으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["Poor Sophia was too much in her aunt's power to deny her anything positively; she was obliged to promise that she would see Mr Blifil, and be as civil to him as possible; but begged her aunt that the match might not be hurried on. She said, “Mr Blifil was by no means agreeable to her, and she hoped her father would be prevailed on not to make her the most wretched of women.”", "그러자 소피아는 고모의 발치에 몸을 던지고는 그 손을 붙잡고, 자기에게서 끌어낸 그것을 부디 숨겨 달라고 눈물로 빌었다. 그녀는 아버지 성미의 격렬함을 들어 호소하며, 자기 마음이 어떻든 결코 아버지를 화나게 할 어떤 일도 하도록 이끌지 못할 거라고 다짐했다. 웨스턴 부인은 잠시 서서 그녀를 바라보다가, 마음을 추스른 뒤 이렇게 말했다. \"오직 한 가지 조건이라면 이 비밀을 오라버니에게 알리지 않겠다. 그 조건이란, 소피아가 바로 오늘 오후에 블리필 씨를 연인으로 맞이하고, 그를 장차 자기 남편이 될 사람으로 여기겠노라 약속하는 것이다.\""]], [["Mrs Western assured her, “That the match was entirely agreed upon, and that nothing could or should prevent it. I must own,” said she, “I looked on it as on a matter of indifference; nay, perhaps, had some scruples about it before, which were actually got over by my thinking it highly agreeable to your own inclinations; but now I regard it as the most eligible thing in the world: nor shall there be, if I can prevent it, a moment of time lost on the occasion.”", "가엾은 소피아는 고모의 손아귀에 너무 깊이 잡혀 있어 어떤 것도 딱 잘라 거절할 수가 없었다. 그녀는 블리필 씨를 만나고 가능한 한 그에게 예의 바르게 대하겠노라 약속할 수밖에 없었다. 다만 혼사를 서두르지는 말아 달라고 고모에게 빌었다. 그녀가 말했다. \"블리필 씨는 제게 결코 마음에 드는 사람이 아니에요. 그리고 아버지가 저를 세상에서 가장 비참한 여자로 만들지 않도록 설득되시길 바라요.\" 웨스턴 부인은 그녀에게 단언했다. \"이 혼사는 완전히 합의된 거고, 어떤 것도 그것을 막을 수 없고 막아서도 안 된다. 나도 인정하마,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 이걸 대수롭지 않은 일로 봤어. 아니, 어쩌면 전에는 약간 꺼림칙한 데가 있었지만, 이게 네 마음에도 무척 흡족하리라 여기게 되면서 실은 그게 다 가셨단다. 하지만 이제는 이걸 세상에서 가장 바람직한 일로 여긴다. 그리고 내가 막을 수 있는 한, 이 일에는 한 순간도 허비되지 않을 거야.\""]], [["Sophia replied, “Delay at least, madam, I may expect from both your goodness and my father's.", "소피아가 대답했다. \"적어도 미루는 것만은, 고모님, 고모님의 인자함과 아버지의 인자함 양쪽에서 기대할 수 있겠지요."], ["Surely you will give me time to endeavour to get the better of so strong a disinclination as I have at present to this person.”", "분명 제게 시간을 주셔서, 지금 이 사람에게 품고 있는 이토록 강한 거부감을 이겨 내도록 애써 볼 수 있게 해 주실 거예요.\""]], [["The aunt answered, “She knew too much of the world to be so deceived; that as she was sensible another man had her affections, she should persuade Mr Western to hasten the match as much as possible.", "고모가 대답했다. \"나는 세상을 너무 많이 알아서 그렇게 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니다."], ["It would be bad politics, indeed,”", "다른 남자가 네 애정을 차지하고 있는 걸 아는 이상, 나는 웨스턴 씨를 설득해 혼사를 최대한 서두르게 하겠다. 정말이지,\""], ["added she, “to protract a siege when the enemy's army is at hand, and in danger of relieving it.", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"적군이 코앞에 와서 포위를 풀어 줄 위험이 있는데 공성을 질질 끄는 건 어리석은 전략이지."], ["No, no, Sophy,”", "안 돼, 안 돼, 소피야,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “as I am convinced you have a violent passion which you can never satisfy with honour, I will do all I can to put your honour out of the care of your family: for when you are married those matters will belong only to the consideration of your husband.", "\"네가 명예롭게는 결코 채울 수 없는 격렬한 정념을 품고 있다고 확신하니, 나는 네 명예를 가문의 근심거리에서 벗어나게 하려고 할 수 있는 건 다 하겠다. 네가 결혼하고 나면 그런 일들은 오로지 네 남편이 헤아릴 몫이 될 테니 말이야."], ["I hope, child, you will always have prudence enough to act as becomes you; but if you should not, marriage hath saved many a woman from ruin.”", "얘야, 나는 네가 언제나 너답게 처신할 만큼 분별을 갖추길 바란다. 하지만 설령 그러지 못하더라도, 결혼은 숱한 여인을 파멸에서 구해 왔단다.\""]], [["Sophia well understood what her aunt meant; but did not think proper to make her an answer.", "소피아는 고모의 뜻을 잘 알아들었지만, 거기에 답하는 것이 적절치 않다고 여겼다."], ["However, she took a resolution to see Mr Blifil, and to behave to him as civilly as she could, for on that condition only she obtained a promise from her aunt to keep secret the liking which her ill fortune, rather than any scheme of Mrs Western, had unhappily drawn from her.", "그렇지만 그녀는 블리필 씨를 만나, 할 수 있는 한 예의 바르게 그를 대하기로 마음을 굳혔다. 오직 그 조건 아래서만, 웨스턴 부인의 무슨 계략이라기보다 자신의 불운이 불행히도 그녀에게서 끌어낸 그 호감을 비밀로 지켜 주겠다는 약속을 고모에게서 얻어 냈기 때문이다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["Containing a dialogue between Sophia and Mrs Honour, which may a little relieve those tender affections which the foregoing scene may have raised in the mind of a good-natured reader.", "소피아와 아너 부인 사이의 대화를 담은 장으로, 앞 장면이 마음씨 고운 독자의 마음에 불러일으켰을 애틋한 감정을 조금이나마 누그러뜨려 줄지도 모른다."]], [["Mrs Western having obtained that promise from her niece which we have seen in the last chapter, withdrew; and presently after arrived Mrs Honour.", "웨스턴 부인은 앞 장에서 본 대로 조카에게서 약속을 받아낸 뒤 물러갔고, 곧이어 아너 부인이 들어왔다."], ["She was at work in a neighbouring apartment, and had been summoned to the keyhole by some vociferation in the preceding dialogue, where she had continued during the remaining part of it.", "그녀는 옆방에서 일을 하고 있었는데, 앞선 대화에서 터져 나온 고함 소리에 이끌려 열쇠 구멍으로 불려갔고, 그 대화가 끝날 때까지 거기에 붙어 있었던 것이다. 방으로 들어선 그녀는 소피아가 눈에서 눈물을 뚝뚝 흘리며 꼼짝 않고 서 있는 모습을 보았다."], ["At her entry into the room, she found Sophia standing motionless, with the tears trickling from her eyes.", "그러자 그녀는 곧장 자기 눈에도 알맞은 양의 눈물을 불러내고는 입을 열었다."], ["Upon which she immediately ordered a proper quantity of tears into her own eyes, and then began, “O Gemini, my dear lady, what is the matter?”--“Nothing,”", "\"어머나 세상에, 아씨, 무슨 일이세요?\"—\"아무것도 아니야.\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["cries Sophia. “Nothing!", "\"아무것도 아니라니요!"], ["O dear Madam!”", "아이고 아씨!\""], ["answers Honour, “you must not tell me that, when your ladyship is in this taking, and when there hath been such a preamble between your ladyship and Madam Western.”--“Don't teaze me,”", "아너가 대답했다. \"아씨께서 이 지경이 되시고, 아씨와 웨스턴 마님 사이에 그런 한바탕 말이 오간 마당에, 그런 말씀으로 저를 속이시면 안 되지요.\"—\"성가시게 굴지 마.\""], ["cries Sophia; “I tell you nothing is the matter.", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"아무 일도 없다니까."], ["Good heavens!", "아, 하느님!"], ["why was I born?”--“Nay, madam,”", "나는 왜 태어났을까?\"—\"아니에요, 아씨.\""], ["says Mrs Honour, “you shall never persuade me that your la'ship can lament yourself so for nothing.", "아너 부인이 말했다. \"아씨께서 아무 까닭도 없이 그렇게 슬퍼하신다고는 저를 결코 설득하지 못하실 거예요."], ["To be sure I am but a servant; but to be sure I have been always faithful to your la'ship, and to be sure I would serve your la'ship with my life.”--“My dear Honour,”", "물론 저야 한낱 하인에 불과하지만, 그래도 저는 늘 아씨께 충성을 다해 왔고, 또 제 목숨을 바쳐서라도 아씨를 모시고 싶은 사람이에요.\"—\"내 사랑하는 아너야.\""], ["says Sophia, “'tis not in thy power to be of any service to me.", "소피아가 말했다. \"네가 나에게 도움이 될 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없단다."], ["I am irretrievably undone.”--“Heaven forbid!”", "나는 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장이 났어.\"—\"맙소사, 그런 말씀 마세요!\""], ["answered the waiting-woman; “but if I can't be of any service to you, pray tell me, madam--it will be some comfort to me to know--pray, dear ma'am, tell me what's the matter.”--“My father,”", "시중드는 여인이 대답했다. \"하지만 제가 아씨께 아무 도움이 못 된다 하더라도, 부디 말씀해 주세요, 아씨—사연을 아는 것만으로도 저에게는 조금이나마 위로가 될 테니—제발, 아씨, 무슨 일인지 말씀해 주세요.\"—\"아버지가.\""], ["cries Sophia, “is going to marry me to a man I both despise and hate.”--“O dear, ma'am,”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"내가 경멸하고 또 증오하는 남자에게 나를 시집보내려 하셔.\"—\"아이고, 아씨.\""], ["answered the other, “who is this wicked man?", "다른 여인이 대답했다. \"그 못된 남자가 대체 누구예요?"], ["for to be sure he is very bad, or your la'ship would not despise him.”--“His name is poison to my tongue,”", "분명 아주 나쁜 사람일 테지요. 안 그러면 아씨께서 그를 경멸하실 리가 없으니까요.\"—\"그 이름은 내 혀에 독이나 마찬가지야.\""], ["replied Sophia: “thou wilt know it too soon.”", "소피아가 받았다. \"너도 머지않아 알게 될 테지.\""], ["Indeed, to confess the truth, she knew it already, and therefore was not very inquisitive as to that point.", "사실 솔직히 말하자면 그녀는 이미 그 이름을 알고 있었고, 그래서 그 점에 대해서는 그리 캐묻지 않았다."], ["She then proceeded thus: “I don't pretend to give your la'ship advice, whereof your la'ship knows much better than I can pretend to, being but a servant; but, i-fackins!", "그러고는 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"한낱 하인 주제에 아씨께 무슨 충고를 드리겠다는 건 아니에요."], ["no father in England should marry me against my consent.", "그런 거야 아씨께서 저보다 훨씬 잘 아시니까요. 하지만, 정말이지!"], ["And, to be sure, the 'squire is so good, that if he did but know your la'ship despises and hates the young man, to be sure he would not desire you to marry him.", "영국의 어떤 아버지도 제 동의 없이 저를 시집보낼 수는 없을 거예요. 또 분명 나리께서는 워낙 인자하신 분이라, 아씨가 그 젊은이를 경멸하고 증오한다는 걸 아시기만 한다면, 분명 아씨더러 그 사람과 결혼하라고 바라지 않으실 거예요."], ["And if your la'ship would but give me leave to tell my master so.", "아씨께서 제게 그 말씀을 나리께 전하라고 허락만 해 주신다면요."], ["To be sure, it would be more properer to come from your own mouth; but as your la'ship doth not care to foul your tongue with his nasty name--“--“You are mistaken, Honour,”", "물론 아씨 입으로 직접 말씀하시는 게 더 마땅하겠지만, 아씨께서 그 더러운 이름으로 혀를 더럽히고 싶어 하지 않으시니—\"—\"네가 잘못 알고 있어, 아너야.\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["says Sophia; “my father was determined before he ever thought fit to mention it to me.”--“More shame for him,”", "\"아버지는 그 이야기를 나에게 꺼내기로 마음먹기도 전에 이미 결심을 굳히셨어.\"—\"그렇다면 더더욱 부끄러운 일이지요.\" 아너가 외쳤다."], ["cries Honour: “you are to go to bed to him, and not master: and thof a man may be a very proper man, yet every woman mayn't think him handsome alike.", "\"잠자리를 함께해야 하는 건 나리가 아니라 아씨인데 말이에요. 어떤 남자가 아무리 번듯한 사람이라 한들, 여자라고 다들 그를 똑같이 잘생겼다 여기는 건 아니잖아요."], ["I am sure my master would never act in this manner of his own head.", "분명 우리 나리께서 제 머리로 혼자 이런 식으로 일을 처리하실 분은 아니에요. 어떤 사람들은 제 일에만 신경을 쓰면 좋으련만."], ["I wish some people would trouble themselves only with what belongs to them; they would not, I believe, like to be served so, if it was their own case; for though I am a maid, I can easily believe as how all men are not equally agreeable.", "제 처지였다면 그렇게 당하는 걸 좋아하지 않을 텐데 말이지요. 저야 비록 처녀지만, 모든 남자가 다 똑같이 마음에 드는 건 아니라는 걸 쉽게 헤아릴 수 있거든요."], ["And what signifies your la'ship having so great a fortune, if you can't please yourself with the man you think most handsomest?", "아씨께서 가장 잘생겼다 여기는 남자로 마음을 채울 수 없다면, 그 큰 재산이 다 무슨 소용이겠어요? 글쎄요, 저야 아무 말 않겠어요."], ["Well, I say nothing; but to be sure it is a pity some folks had not been better born; nay, as for that matter, I should not mind it myself; but then there is not so much money; and what of that?", "하지만 어떤 사람들이 더 좋은 집안에서 태어나지 못한 게 안타까운 일이긴 하지요. 아니, 사실 그 점이라면 저 같으면 개의치 않겠어요."], ["your la'ship hath money enough for both; and where can your la'ship bestow your fortune better?", "다만 그러면 돈이 그리 많지 않다는 게 문제인데, 그게 또 뭐 어때서요? 아씨께는 두 사람 몫의 재산이 넉넉히 있으니, 그 재산을 어디다 그보다 더 잘 쓰시겠어요?"], ["for to be sure every one must allow that he is the most handsomest, charmingest, finest, tallest, properest man in the world.”--“What do you mean by running on in this manner to me?”", "분명 누구나 인정할 거예요. 그분이야말로 세상에서 가장 잘생기고, 가장 매력 있고, 가장 멋지고, 가장 키 크고, 가장 번듯한 남자라고요.\"—\"네가 나에게 무슨 뜻으로 그렇게 떠들어 대는 거니?\""], ["cries Sophia, with a very grave countenance.", "소피아가 자못 엄숙한 얼굴로 외쳤다."], ["“Have I ever given any encouragement for these liberties?”--“Nay, ma'am, I ask pardon; I meant no harm,”", "\"내가 언제 너한테 이런 무례를 부려도 좋다고 허락이라도 했더냐?\"—\"아니에요, 아씨, 용서하세요. 나쁜 뜻은 없었어요.\""], ["answered she; “but to be sure the poor gentleman hath run in my head ever since I saw him this morning.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 정말이지, 오늘 아침에 그 가엾은 분을 본 뒤로 줄곧 그분이 제 머릿속을 떠나지 않았어요."], ["To be sure, if your la'ship had but seen him just now, you must have pitied him.", "분명 아씨께서도 방금 그분을 보셨더라면 가엾게 여기실 수밖에 없으셨을 거예요. 가엾은 분!"], ["Poor gentleman!", "그분께 무슨 불행한 일이라도 생기지 않았으면 좋겠어요."], ["I wishes some misfortune hath not happened to him; for he hath been walking about with his arms across, and looking so melancholy, all this morning: I vow and protest it made me almost cry to see him.”--“To see whom?”", "오늘 아침 내내 팔짱을 끼고 이리저리 거닐면서 어찌나 우울한 얼굴을 하고 계셨던지요. 정말이지, 그분을 보고 있자니 저도 거의 울 뻔했다니까요.\"—\"누구를 봤다는 거니?\""], ["says Sophia.", "소피아가 말했다."], ["“Poor Mr Jones,”", "\"가엾은 존스 씨요.\""], ["answered Honour.", "아너가 대답했다."], ["“See him!", "\"그분을 봤다고!"], ["why, where did you see him?”", "아니, 어디서 봤는데?\""], ["cries Sophia.", "소피아가 외쳤다."], ["“By the canal, ma'am,”", "\"운하 옆에서요, 아씨.\""], ["says Honour.", "아너가 말했다."], ["“There he hath been walking all this morning, and at last there he laid himself down: I believe he lies there still.", "\"오늘 아침 내내 거기를 거닐다가, 마침내 거기에 몸을 누이셨어요. 아마 지금도 거기 누워 계실 거예요."], ["To be sure, if it had not been for my modesty, being a maid, as I am, I should have gone and spoke to him.", "정말이지, 제가 처녀로서의 부끄러움만 없었다면, 가서 말이라도 걸었을 거예요."], ["Do, ma'am, let me go and see, only for a fancy, whether he is there still.”--“Pugh!”", "아씨, 제가 가서 그분이 아직도 거기 계신지, 그냥 궁금해서 한번 보고 와도 될까요?\"—\"흥!\""], ["says Sophia. “There!", "소피아가 말했다."], ["no, no: what should he do there?", "\"거기라니! 안 돼, 안 돼."], ["He is gone before this time, to be sure.", "그 사람이 거기서 뭘 한다고? 분명 지금쯤은 벌써 갔을 거야."], ["Besides, why--what--why should you go to see?", "게다가, 왜—뭐 하러—네가 왜 가서 본다는 거니?"], ["besides, I want you for something else.", "게다가 너한테 다른 시킬 일이 있어."], ["Go, fetch me my hat and gloves.", "가서 내 모자와 장갑을 가져오너라."], ["I shall walk with my aunt in the grove before dinner.”", "저녁 식사 전에 고모님과 함께 숲을 거닐어야겠다.\" 아너는 시킨 대로 곧장 했고, 소피아는 모자를 썼다."], ["Honour did immediately as she was bid, and Sophia put her hat on; when, looking in the glass, she fancied the ribbon with which her hat was tied did not become her, and so sent her maid back again for a ribbon of a different colour; and then giving Mrs Honour repeated charges not to leave her work on any account, as she said it was in violent haste, and must be finished that very day, she muttered something more about going to the grove, and then sallied out the contrary way, and walked, as fast as her tender trembling limbs could carry her, directly towards the canal.", "그러다 거울을 들여다보니 모자를 묶은 리본이 자기에게 어울리지 않는 것 같아, 하녀를 다시 보내 다른 색깔의 리본을 가져오게 했다. 그러고는 아너 부인에게, 그 일이 무척 급하니 무슨 일이 있어도 손을 놓아서는 안 되며 바로 그날 안에 끝내야 한다고 거듭 당부한 뒤, 숲으로 간다는 둥 무언가를 더 중얼거리고는, 그와는 반대 방향으로 빠져나가, 가냘프게 떨리는 다리가 데려다줄 수 있는 한 빠른 걸음으로 곧장 운하를 향해 걸어갔다."]], [["Jones had been there as Mrs Honour had told her; he had indeed spent two hours there that morning in melancholy contemplation on his Sophia, and had gone out from the garden at one door the moment she entered it at another.", "존스는 아너 부인이 말한 대로 거기에 있었다. 사실 그는 그날 아침 그곳에서 소피아를 생각하며 우울한 명상에 잠겨 두 시간을 보냈고, 그녀가 한쪽 문으로 정원에 들어선 바로 그 순간 다른 쪽 문으로 정원을 빠져나가 버린 것이다."], ["So that those unlucky minutes which had been spent in changing the ribbons, had prevented the lovers from meeting at this time;--a most unfortunate accident, from which my fair readers will not fail to draw a very wholesome lesson.", "그리하여 리본을 바꾸느라 흘려보낸 그 불운한 몇 분이, 이번에는 두 연인이 만나는 것을 막아 버리고 말았다. 참으로 불행한 우연이니, 나의 아리따운 여성 독자들이라면 여기서 더없이 유익한 교훈 하나를 어김없이 길어 올릴 것이다."], ["And here I strictly forbid all male critics to intermeddle with a circumstance which I have recounted only for the sake of the ladies, and upon which they only are at liberty to comment.", "그리고 여기서 나는 모든 남성 비평가들에게, 오직 숙녀들을 위해서만 이야기했고 또 오직 숙녀들만이 마음대로 논평할 수 있는 이 정황에 함부로 끼어드는 것을 엄히 금하는 바이다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["A picture of formal courtship in miniature, as it always ought to be drawn, and a scene of a tenderer kind painted at full length.", "의례적인 구애를 작게 축소하여 마땅히 그려져야 할 모습대로 그린 그림 한 폭, 그리고 한층 부드러운 종류의 한 장면을 전신상으로 그린 그림."]], [["It was well remarked by one (and perhaps by more), that misfortunes do not come single.", "불행은 홀로 오지 않는다고 어떤 이가(어쩌면 여러 사람이) 절묘하게 말한 바 있다."], ["This wise maxim was now verified by Sophia, who was not only disappointed of seeing the man she loved, but had the vexation of being obliged to dress herself out, in order to receive a visit from the man she hated.", "이 지혜로운 격언은 이제 소피아에게서 그대로 확인되었으니, 그녀는 사랑하는 남자를 만나리라는 기대가 어긋났을 뿐 아니라, 미워하는 남자의 방문을 맞기 위해 차려입어야 하는 곤욕까지 치르게 되었던 것이다."]], [["That afternoon Mr Western, for the first time, acquainted his daughter with his intention; telling her, he knew very well that she had heard it before from her aunt.", "그날 오후, 웨스턴 씨는 처음으로 딸에게 자신의 뜻을 알렸다. 그러면서 이미 누이에게서 그 이야기를 들었으리라는 것을 잘 안다고 말했다."], ["Sophia looked very grave upon this, nor could she prevent a few pearls from stealing into her eyes.", "이 말에 소피아는 몹시 어두운 얼굴을 했고, 진주알 같은 눈물 몇 방울이 슬며시 눈에 고이는 것을 막을 수가 없었다. \"자, 자,\""], ["“Come, come,”", "웨스턴이 말했다. \"처녀티 내는 짓은 그만두려무나."], ["says Western, “none of your maidenish airs; I know all; I assure you sister hath told me all.”", "나는 다 안다. 단언컨대 누이가 내게 전부 다 말해 주었으니까.\""]], [["“Is it possible,”", "\"그럼 정말,\""], ["says Sophia, “that my aunt can have betrayed me already?”--“Ay, ay,”", "소피아가 말했다. \"고모께서 벌써 저를 일러바치셨단 말씀인가요?\"—\"그래, 그렇다.\""], ["says Western; “betrayed you!", "웨스턴이 말했다. \"일러바쳤다고!"], ["ay.", "그래."], ["Why, you betrayed yourself yesterday at dinner.", "아니, 너는 어제 저녁 식사 자리에서 네 스스로 다 드러내지 않았느냐."], ["You showed your fancy very plainly, I think.", "내가 보기엔 네 마음을 아주 또렷이 보여 주었어."], ["But you young girls never know what you would be at.", "하지만 너희 젊은 처녀들이란 자기가 무엇을 바라는지조차 모른단 말이지."], ["So you cry because I am going to marry you to the man you are in love with!", "그래, 사랑하는 남자에게 너를 시집보내려 하니까 운다는 게냐! 내 기억에 네 어미도 꼭 이런 식으로 훌쩍이고 칭얼댔지."], ["Your mother, I remember, whimpered and whined just in the same manner; but it was all over within twenty-four hours after we were married: Mr Blifil is a brisk young man, and will soon put an end to your squeamishness.", "하지만 우리가 혼인하고 스물네 시간도 안 되어 다 끝나 버렸다. 블리필 군은 활기찬 청년이니 네 까탈스러움쯤은 금세 끝장내 줄 게야."], ["Come, chear up, chear up; I expect un every minute.”", "자, 기운 내라, 기운 내. 나는 그가 곧 올 줄 알고 있다.\""]], [["Sophia was now convinced that her aunt had behaved honourably to her: and she determined to go through that disagreeable afternoon with as much resolution as possible, and without giving the least suspicion in the world to her father.", "소피아는 이제 고모가 자신에게 명예롭게 처신했음을 확신하게 되었다. 그래서 그 불쾌한 오후를 되도록 굳센 마음으로 견뎌 내되, 아버지에게 추호의 의심도 사지 않으리라 마음먹었다."]], [["Mr Blifil soon arrived; and Mr Western soon after withdrawing, left the young couple together.", "블리필 씨가 곧 도착했고, 얼마 지나지 않아 웨스턴 씨가 물러나며 젊은 두 사람만 함께 남겨 두었다."]], [["Here a long silence of near a quarter of an hour ensued; for the gentleman who was to begin the conversation had all the unbecoming modesty which consists in bashfulness.", "여기서 거의 십오 분에 가까운 긴 침묵이 이어졌다. 대화를 시작해야 할 신사가 부끄럼이라는, 어울리지 않는 그 수줍음을 온통 짊어지고 있었기 때문이다."], ["He often attempted to speak, and as often suppressed his words just at the very point of utterance.", "그는 여러 번 입을 떼려 했고, 그때마다 말이 막 터져 나오려는 바로 그 순간 다시 삼켜 버렸다."], ["At last out they broke in a torrent of far-fetched and high-strained compliments, which were answered on her side by downcast looks, half bows, and civil monosyllables.", "마침내 억지스럽고 과장된 찬사가 봇물 터지듯 쏟아져 나왔고, 그녀 쪽에서는 내리뜬 눈길과 어정쩡한 목례, 그리고 예의 바른 단음절로 그것에 응했다."], ["Blifil, from his inexperience in the ways of women, and from his conceit of himself, took this behaviour for a modest assent to his courtship; and when, to shorten a scene which she could no longer support, Sophia rose up and left the room, he imputed that, too, merely to bashfulness, and comforted himself that he should soon have enough of her company.", "블리필은 여자를 다루는 법에 미숙한 데다 제 자신에 대한 자만이 가득했던 터라, 이 태도를 자신의 구애에 대한 정숙한 동의로 받아들였다. 그래서 더는 견딜 수 없는 그 자리를 줄이려고 소피아가 일어나 방을 나가자, 그것마저도 한낱 수줍음 탓으로 돌리며, 곧 그녀와 충분히 함께할 수 있으리라 스스로를 위안했다."]], [["He was indeed perfectly well satisfied with his prospect of success; for as to that entire and absolute possession of the heart of his mistress which romantic lovers require, the very idea of it never entered his head.", "실제로 그는 자신의 성공 전망에 더없이 만족하고 있었다. 낭만적인 연인들이 요구하는, 연인의 마음을 온전히 절대적으로 차지한다는 것 따위는 애초에 그의 머릿속에 떠오른 적도 없었다."], ["Her fortune and her person were the sole objects of his wishes, of which he made no doubt soon to obtain the absolute property; as Mr Western's mind was so earnestly bent on the match; and as he well knew the strict obedience which Sophia was always ready to pay to her father's will, and the greater still which her father would exact, if there was occasion.", "그가 바라는 것은 오로지 그녀의 재산과 그녀의 몸뿐이었으며, 그 절대적 소유권을 곧 얻으리라는 데 그는 조금의 의심도 없었다. 웨스턴 씨의 마음이 이 혼사에 그토록 간절히 기울어 있었기 때문이요, 또 소피아가 늘 아버지의 뜻에 기꺼이 바치는 그 엄정한 복종, 그리고 필요하다면 아버지가 강요해 낼 더욱 큰 복종을 그가 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["This authority, therefore, together with the charms which he fancied in his own person and conversation, could not fail, he thought, of succeeding with a young lady, whose inclinations were, he doubted not, entirely disengaged.", "따라서 이 권위에 더하여, 자기 자신의 인품과 말솜씨에 있다고 믿는 매력까지 곁들여진다면, 그 마음이 전적으로 자유롭다고 의심치 않는 젊은 숙녀에게 성공하지 못할 리 없다고 그는 생각했다."]], [["Of Jones he certainly had not even the least jealousy; and I have often thought it wonderful that he had not.", "존스에 대해서는 그가 분명 일말의 질투조차 품지 않았으니, 나는 그가 그러지 않았다는 것이 종종 놀랍게 여겨졌다."], ["Perhaps he imagined the character which Jones bore all over the country (how justly, let the reader determine), of being one of the wildest fellows in England, might render him odious to a lady of the most exemplary modesty.", "어쩌면 그는 존스가 온 고장에 떨치고 있는 평판—잉글랜드에서 가장 거친 사내 가운데 하나라는—이(그것이 얼마나 정당한지는 독자의 판단에 맡긴다) 더없이 모범적으로 정숙한 숙녀에게 그를 혐오스러운 존재로 만들리라 상상했는지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps his suspicions might be laid asleep by the behaviour of Sophia, and of Jones himself, when they were all in company together.", "어쩌면 그들이 다 함께 자리할 때 보였던 소피아와 존스 자신의 태도가 그의 의심을 잠재웠는지도 모른다."], ["Lastly, and indeed principally, he was well assured there was not another self in the case.", "마지막으로, 그리고 사실 무엇보다도, 그는 이 일에 또 다른 자아 같은 것은 없다고 굳게 믿었다."], ["He fancied that he knew Jones to the bottom, and had in reality a great contempt for his understanding, for not being more attached to his own interest.", "그는 존스를 속속들이 안다고 여겼으며, 사실 그의 분별력을 크게 경멸하고 있었다. 자기 자신의 이익에 더 집착하지 않는다는 이유에서였다."], ["He had no apprehension that Jones was in love with Sophia; and as for any lucrative motives, he imagined they would sway very little with so silly a fellow.", "그는 존스가 소피아를 사랑한다는 우려는 전혀 하지 않았고, 어떤 잇속을 노린 동기로 말하자면, 그토록 어리석은 녀석에게는 그런 것이 거의 영향을 미치지 못하리라 상상했다. 게다가 블리필은 몰리 시그림과의 정사가 아직 계속되고 있으며, 실은 그것이 결혼으로 끝나리라고까지 믿고 있었다."], ["Blifil, moreover, thought the affair of Molly Seagrim still went on, and indeed believed it would end in marriage; for Jones really loved him from his childhood, and had kept no secret from him, till his behaviour on the sickness of Mr Allworthy had entirely alienated his heart; and it was by means of the quarrel which had ensued on this occasion, and which was not yet reconciled, that Mr Blifil knew nothing of the alteration which had happened in the affection which Jones had formerly borne towards Molly.", "존스는 어릴 적부터 진심으로 그를 좋아했고, 올워디 씨가 병들었을 때 보인 그의 행실이 그의 마음을 완전히 멀어지게 하기 전까지는 그에게 어떤 비밀도 숨기지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 바로 이 일을 계기로 벌어진, 아직 화해되지 못한 그 다툼 탓에, 블리필 씨는 존스가 예전에 몰리에게 품었던 애정에 일어난 변화를 전혀 알지 못했던 것이다."]], [["From these reasons, therefore, Mr Blifil saw no bar to his success with Sophia.", "그러므로 이러한 이유들에서, 블리필 씨는 소피아와의 일에 어떤 걸림돌도 보지 못했다."], ["He concluded her behaviour was like that of all other young ladies on a first visit from a lover, and it had indeed entirely answered his expectations.", "그는 그녀의 태도가 연인의 첫 방문을 받은 여느 젊은 숙녀들과 다를 바 없다고 결론지었으며, 그것은 과연 그의 기대에 온전히 들어맞았다."]], [["Mr Western took care to way-lay the lover at his exit from his mistress.", "웨스턴 씨는 연인이 자신의 연모하는 이에게서 나오는 길목을 지켜 잡으려고 신경 썼다."], ["He found him so elevated with his success, so enamoured with his daughter, and so satisfied with her reception of him, that the old gentleman began to caper and dance about his hall, and by many other antic actions to express the extravagance of his joy; for he had not the least command over any of his passions; and that which had at any time the ascendant in his mind hurried him to the wildest excesses.", "그는 그가 성공에 한껏 들떠 있고, 딸에게 흠뻑 빠져 있으며, 그녀의 환대에 너무도 만족해하는 것을 보고는, 이 노신사는 자기 집 큰 방을 이리저리 깡충거리며 춤추기 시작했고, 그 밖에도 여러 우스꽝스러운 동작으로 주체할 수 없는 기쁨을 드러냈다. 그는 자신의 어떤 감정도 조금도 다스리지 못하는 사람이었고, 어느 때든 그의 마음을 사로잡은 감정은 그를 가장 거친 극단으로 몰아갔던 것이다."]], [["As soon as Blifil was departed, which was not till after many hearty kisses and embraces bestowed on him by Western, the good squire went instantly in quest of his daughter, whom he no sooner found than he poured forth the most extravagant raptures, bidding her chuse what clothes and jewels she pleased; and declaring that he had no other use for fortune but to make her happy.", "블리필이 떠나자—그것도 웨스턴이 그에게 진심 어린 입맞춤과 포옹을 숱하게 퍼붓고 나서야 떠났는데—이 선량한 지주는 곧장 딸을 찾아 나섰다. 그리고 딸을 발견하자마자 더없이 과장된 환희를 쏟아 내며, 마음에 드는 옷이든 보석이든 무엇이든 고르라 일렀고, 자신에게 재산이란 오로지 딸을 행복하게 해 주기 위한 것일 뿐 다른 쓸모는 없다고 선언했다."], ["He then caressed her again and again with the utmost profusion of fondness, called her by the most endearing names, and protested she was his only joy on earth.", "그러고는 더없이 흘러넘치는 다정함으로 그녀를 거듭거듭 어루만지며, 가장 사랑스러운 이름들로 부르고, 그녀야말로 이 세상에서 자신의 유일한 기쁨이라고 단언했다."]], [["Sophia perceiving her father in this fit of affection, which she did not absolutely know the reason of (for fits of fondness were not unusual to him, though this was rather more violent than ordinary), thought she should never have a better opportunity of disclosing herself than at present, as far at least as regarded Mr Blifil; and she too well foresaw the necessity which she should soon be under of coming to a full explanation.", "소피아는 아버지가 이런 애정의 발작에 사로잡힌 것을 보았으나, 그 까닭을 정확히 알지는 못했다(이런 다정함의 발작이 그에게 드문 일은 아니었지만, 이번 것은 여느 때보다 다소 격렬했다). 그녀는 적어도 블리필 씨에 관한 한 지금보다 더 좋은 기회는 결코 없으리라 생각했고, 또 머지않아 자신이 모든 것을 낱낱이 털어놓아야 할 처지에 놓이리라는 것도 너무도 잘 내다보고 있었다."], ["After having thanked the squire, therefore, for all his professions of kindness, she added, with a look full of inexpressible softness, “And is it possible my papa can be so good to place all his joy in his Sophy's happiness?”", "그래서 지주의 그 모든 다정한 말들에 감사를 드린 뒤, 형언할 수 없이 부드러운 표정으로 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그럼 정말 우리 아버지께서 당신의 모든 기쁨을 이 소피의 행복에 두실 만큼 그렇게 자애로우실 수가 있을까요?\""], ["which Western having confirmed by a great oath, and a kiss; she then laid hold of his hand, and, falling on her knees, after many warm and passionate declarations of affection and duty, she begged him “not to make her the most miserable creature on earth by forcing her to marry a man whom she detested.", "웨스턴이 굳은 맹세와 입맞춤으로 그것을 확인해 주자, 그녀는 그의 손을 붙잡고 무릎을 꿇으며, 애정과 효성을 담은 뜨겁고 간절한 다짐을 여러 번 늘어놓은 끝에 그에게 애원했다. \"제가 몹시 싫어하는 남자와 억지로 혼인하게 하시어 저를 이 세상에서 가장 비참한 존재로 만들지 말아 주세요."], ["This I entreat of you, dear sir,”", "사랑하는 아버지, 이것을 제가 간청드립니다.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “for your sake, as well as my own, since you are so very kind to tell me your happiness depends on mine.”--“How!", "\"저 자신을 위해서이기도 하지만 아버지를 위해서이기도 합니다. 아버지의 행복이 제 행복에 달려 있다고 그토록 다정히 말씀해 주셨으니까요.\"—\"뭐라고!"], ["what!”", "무엇이!\""], ["says Western, staring wildly.", "웨스턴이 사납게 눈을 부릅뜨며 말했다. \"오!"], ["“Oh! sir,”", "아버지,\""], ["continued she, “not only your poor Sophy's happiness; her very life, her being, depends upon your granting her request.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"가엾은 소피의 행복만이 아니라, 그 애의 목숨, 그 존재 자체가 아버지께서 이 청을 들어주시는 데 달려 있습니다."], ["I cannot live with Mr Blifil.", "저는 블리필 씨와는 살 수 없습니다."], ["To force me into this marriage would be killing me.”--“You can't live with Mr Blifil?”", "이 혼인을 제게 강요하시는 것은 저를 죽이시는 일이 될 것입니다.\"—\"네가 블리필 씨와 살 수 없다고?\""], ["says Western.", "웨스턴이 말했다."], ["“No, upon my soul I can't,”", "\"네, 맹세코 저는 그럴 수 없습니다.\""], ["answered Sophia.", "소피아가 대답했다."], ["“Then die and be d--d,”", "\"그럼 죽어라, 빌어먹을,\""], ["cries he, spurning her from him.", "그가 외치며 그녀를 밀쳐 냈다. \"오!"], ["“Oh! sir,”", "아버지,\""], ["cries Sophia, catching hold of the skirt of his coat, “take pity on me, I beseech you.", "소피아가 그의 외투 자락을 붙들며 외쳤다. \"부디 저를 가엾이 여겨 주세요, 간청드립니다."], ["Don't look and say such cruel--Can you be unmoved while you see your Sophy in this dreadful condition?", "그렇게 보시며 그토록 잔인한 말씀을—이 끔찍한 처지에 빠진 소피를 보시면서도 마음이 흔들리지 않으실 수 있나요?"], ["Can the best of fathers break my heart?", "더없이 훌륭하신 아버지께서 제 가슴을 찢으실 수 있나요?"], ["Will he kill me by the most painful, cruel, lingering death?”--“Pooh!", "가장 고통스럽고 잔인하며 더디게 끌리는 죽음으로 저를 죽이시려는 건가요?\"—\"쳇!"], ["pooh!”", "쳇!\""], ["cries the squire; “all stuff and nonsense; all maidenish tricks.", "지주가 외쳤다. \"다 헛소리에 쓸데없는 소리다."], ["Kill you, indeed!", "죄다 처녀티 내는 수작이지."], ["Will marriage kill you?”--“Oh!", "죽긴 무얼 죽어! 혼인이 너를 죽이기라도 한단 말이냐?\"—\"오!"], ["sir,”", "아버지,\""], ["answered Sophia, “such a marriage is worse than death.", "소피아가 대답했다. \"그런 혼인은 죽음보다 더 나쁩니다."], ["He is not even indifferent; I hate and detest him.”--“If you detest un never so much,”", "그는 제게 무덤덤한 정도조차 아니에요. 저는 그를 미워하고 혐오합니다.\"—\"네가 그를 아무리 혐오한다 해도,\""], ["cries Western, “you shall ha'un.”", "웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"너는 그를 갖게 될 게다.\""], ["This he bound by an oath too shocking to repeat; and after many violent asseverations, concluded in these words: “I am resolved upon the match, and unless you consent to it I will not give you a groat, not a single farthing; no, though I saw you expiring with famine in the street, I would not relieve you with a morsel of bread.", "그는 여기에 차마 옮기기조차 끔찍한 맹세를 덧붙였고, 격렬한 단언을 숱하게 늘어놓은 끝에 이런 말로 끝맺었다. \"나는 이 혼사를 굳게 작정했다."], ["This is my fixed resolution, and so I leave you to consider on it.”", "네가 거기에 동의하지 않는다면 단 한 푼도, 단 한 닢도 네게 주지 않겠다. 그래, 네가 거리에서 굶주려 숨이 끊어지는 꼴을 본다 해도, 빵 한 조각으로도 너를 구해 주지 않을 테다."], ["He then broke from her with such violence, that her face dashed against the floor; and he burst directly out of the room, leaving poor Sophia prostrate on the ground.", "이것이 내 확고한 결심이니, 그 일을 곰곰이 생각해 보라고 너를 두고 가마.\" 그러고는 어찌나 사납게 그녀에게서 떨쳐 나왔는지, 그녀의 얼굴이 마룻바닥에 부딪혔고, 그는 가엾은 소피아를 바닥에 엎드린 채 남겨 둔 채 곧장 방을 박차고 나갔다."]], [["When Western came into the hall, he there found Jones; who seeing his friend looking wild, pale, and almost breathless, could not forbear enquiring the reason of all these melancholy appearances.", "웨스턴이 큰 방으로 들어섰을 때, 그곳에서 그는 존스를 마주쳤다. 존스는 자기 벗이 사납고 창백하며 거의 숨이 넘어갈 듯한 모습인 것을 보고는, 이 모든 침울한 기색의 까닭을 묻지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Upon which the squire immediately acquainted him with the whole matter, concluding with bitter denunciations against Sophia, and very pathetic lamentations of the misery of all fathers who are so unfortunate to have daughters.", "그러자 지주는 즉시 그에게 일의 전말을 들려주었으며, 소피아를 향한 모진 저주의 말과, 딸을 둔 불운을 안은 모든 아비들의 비참함에 대한 더없이 애처로운 한탄으로 이야기를 끝맺었다."]], [["Jones, to whom all the resolutions which had been taken in favour of Blifil were yet a secret, was at first almost struck dead with this relation; but recovering his spirits a little, mere despair, as he afterwards said, inspired him to mention a matter to Mr Western, which seemed to require more impudence than a human forehead was ever gifted with.", "블리필에게 유리하게 내려진 그 모든 결정이 아직 비밀로 남아 있던 존스는, 처음 이 이야기를 듣고 거의 죽을 듯이 충격을 받았다. 그러나 정신을 조금 추스르고 나자, 훗날 그 자신이 말했듯 순전한 절망감이 그를 부추겨, 인간의 낯짝에 일찍이 주어진 적 없는 뻔뻔함이 필요해 보이는 일 하나를 웨스턴 씨에게 꺼내게 했다."], ["He desired leave to go to Sophia, that he might endeavour to obtain her concurrence with her father's inclinations.", "그는 소피아에게 가서, 그녀가 아버지의 뜻에 따르도록 설득해 보겠다며 허락을 청한 것이다."]], [["If the squire had been as quicksighted as he was remarkable for the contrary, passion might at present very well have blinded him.", "만일 지주가 둔하기로 그토록 이름났던 것과는 반대로 눈치가 빨랐더라면, 지금쯤 격정이 그의 눈을 가렸을 법도 했다."], ["He thanked Jones for offering to undertake the office, and said, “Go, go, prithee, try what canst do;”", "그는 그 일을 맡아 주겠다는 존스의 제안에 고마워하며 말했다. \"가게, 가, 부디 자네가 할 수 있는 걸 한번 해 보게.\""], ["and then swore many execrable oaths that he would turn her out of doors unless she consented to the match.", "그러고는 그녀가 이 혼사에 동의하지 않으면 그녀를 집 밖으로 내쫓겠다며 차마 입에 담기 어려운 저주의 맹세를 숱하게 퍼부었다."]], [["The meeting between Jones and Sophia.", "존스와 소피아의 만남."]], [["Jones departed instantly in quest of Sophia, whom he found just risen from the ground, where her father had left her, with the tears trickling from her eyes, and the blood running from her lips.", "존스는 곧장 소피아를 찾아 떠났고, 아버지가 그녀를 두고 간 그 자리에서 막 일어선 그녀를 발견했다. 두 눈에서는 눈물이 흘러내리고, 입술에서는 피가 배어 나오고 있었다."], ["He presently ran to her, and with a voice full at once of tenderness and terrour, cried, “O my Sophia, what means this dreadful sight?”", "그는 곧바로 그녀에게 달려가, 다정함과 두려움이 한꺼번에 가득 담긴 목소리로 외쳤다. \"오, 나의 소피아, 이 끔찍한 광경은 대체 무엇이오?\""], ["She looked softly at him for a moment before she spoke, and then said, “Mr Jones, for Heaven's sake how came you here?--Leave me, I beseech you, this moment.”--“Do not,”", "그녀는 입을 열기 전 잠시 그를 부드럽게 바라보더니 말했다. \"존스 씨, 제발 부탁이에요, 어떻게 여기까지 오신 거예요?—제발 부탁드려요, 지금 당장 저를 떠나 주세요.\"—\"그런,\""], ["says he, “impose so harsh a command upon me--my heart bleeds faster than those lips.", "그가 말했다, \"그토록 모진 명령을 제게 내리지 마시오—내 가슴은 저 입술보다 더 빨리 피를 흘리고 있소. 오, 소피아, 그 소중한 피 한 방울을 지킬 수 있다면 내 핏줄을 다 비워 낸들 어찌 어렵겠소.\"—\"이미 당신께 너무 많은 은혜를 입었어요,\""], ["O Sophia, how easily could I drain my veins to preserve one drop of that dear blood.”--“I have too many obligations to you already,”", "그녀가 대답했다, \"분명 당신은 그것을 은혜로 베푸셨겠지요.\" 여기서 그녀는 거의 일 분 가까이 그를 다정하게 바라보더니, 이내 격한 슬픔에 북받쳐 외쳤다."], ["answered she, “for sure you meant them such.”", "\"오, 존스 씨, 어째서 제 목숨을 구하셨나요?"], ["Here she looked at him tenderly almost a minute, and then bursting into an agony, cried, “Oh, Mr Jones, why did you save my life?", "제가 죽었더라면 우리 둘 모두에게 더 행복했을 텐데요.\"—\"우리 둘 모두에게 더 행복하다니!\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["my death would have been happier for us both.”--“Happier for us both!” cried he.", "\"고문대나 형차인들 소피아의—그 무서운 말을 차마 견딜 수가 없소."], ["“Could racks or wheels kill me so painfully as Sophia's--I cannot bear the dreadful sound.", "내가 그녀 말고 무엇을 위해 산단 말이오?\" 이 말을 할 때 그의 목소리와 표정에는 말로 다 할 수 없는 다정함이 가득했다."], ["Do I live but for her?”", "그러면서 그는 그녀의 손을 부드럽게 잡았고, 그녀는 그것을 빼지 않았다."], ["Both his voice and looks were full of inexpressible tenderness when he spoke these words; and at the same time he laid gently hold on her hand, which she did not withdraw from him; to say the truth, she hardly knew what she did or suffered.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그녀는 자신이 무엇을 하고 무엇을 겪고 있는지조차 거의 알지 못했다. 이제 이 연인들 사이에 잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그의 두 눈은 간절하게 소피아에게 못 박혀 있었으며, 그녀의 눈은 땅을 향해 내리깔렸다."], ["A few moments now passed in silence between these lovers, while his eyes were eagerly fixed on Sophia, and hers declining towards the ground: at last she recovered strength enough to desire him again to leave her, for that her certain ruin would be the consequence of their being found together; adding, “Oh, Mr Jones, you know not, you know not what hath passed this cruel afternoon.”--“I know all, my Sophia,”", "마침내 그녀는 다시 그에게 떠나 달라고 청할 만큼의 기운을 되찾았다. 두 사람이 함께 있다가 들키면 자신의 파멸이 분명한 결과로 닥칠 것이기 때문이었다."], ["answered he; “your cruel father hath told me all, and he himself hath sent me hither to you.”--“My father sent you to me!”", "그녀는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"오, 존스 씨, 당신은 모르세요, 이 잔인한 오후에 무슨 일이 있었는지 정말 모르세요.\"—\"나는 다 알고 있소, 나의 소피아,\""], ["replied she: “sure you dream.”--“Would to Heaven,”", "그가 대답했다, \"당신의 모진 아버님이 내게 모두 말씀하셨고, 그분이 직접 나를 당신에게 보내셨소.\"—\"아버지가 당신을 제게 보내셨다고요!\" 그녀가 되받았다."], ["cries he, “it was but a dream!", "\"분명 꿈을 꾸고 계신 거예요.\"—\"하늘에 비노니,\""], ["Oh, Sophia, your father hath sent me to you, to be an advocate for my odious rival, to solicit you in his favour.", "그가 외쳤다, \"이것이 한낱 꿈이라면 얼마나 좋겠소! 오, 소피아, 당신의 아버님이 나를 당신에게 보내신 것은, 내 가증스러운 연적을 대신해 변호하라고, 그를 위해 당신을 설득하라고 하신 것이오."], ["I took any means to get access to you.", "나는 당신에게 다가갈 수만 있다면 어떤 수단이든 가리지 않았소."], ["O speak to me, Sophia!", "오, 내게 말해 주오, 소피아!"], ["comfort my bleeding heart.", "피 흘리는 내 가슴을 달래 주오."], ["Sure no one ever loved, ever doated like me.", "분명 나처럼 사랑한, 나처럼 애틋이 흠모한 이는 일찍이 없었을 것이오."], ["Do not unkindly withhold this dear, this soft, this gentle hand--one moment, perhaps, tears you for ever from me--nothing less than this cruel occasion could, I believe, have ever conquered the respect and awe with which you have inspired me.”", "이 소중한, 이 부드러운, 이 가녀린 손을 매정하게 거두지 마오—어쩌면 한순간이 당신을 내게서 영원히 앗아 갈지도 모르오—이토록 잔인한 사정이 아니고서는, 당신이 내게 불어넣은 그 존경과 경외를 무엇으로도 이겨 낼 수 없었을 것이오.\" 그녀는 잠시 말없이 서서 당혹감에 휩싸여 있었다."], ["She stood a moment silent, and covered with confusion; then lifting up her eyes gently towards him, she cried, “What would Mr Jones have me say?”--“O do but promise,”", "그러더니 부드럽게 눈을 들어 그를 향해 외쳤다. \"존스 씨는 제가 무슨 말을 하기를 바라시나요?\"—\"오, 다만 약속만이라도 해 주오,\""], ["cries he, “that you never will give yourself to Blifil.”--“Name not,”", "그가 외쳤다, \"결코 당신 자신을 블리필에게 내주지 않겠다고.\"—\"그 혐오스러운 이름은,\""], ["answered she, “the detested sound.", "그녀가 대답했다, \"입에 올리지도 마세요."], ["Be assured I never will give him what is in my power to withhold from him.”--“Now then,”", "분명히 말씀드리지만, 제가 거절할 수 있는 한 결코 그에게 저를 내주지 않겠어요.\"—\"그렇다면 이제,\""], ["cries he, “while you are so perfectly kind, go a little farther, and add that I may hope.”--“Alas!”", "그가 외쳤다, \"이토록 더없이 다정하신 김에 한 걸음만 더 나아가, 제가 희망을 품어도 된다고 덧붙여 주오.\"—\"아아!\""], ["says she, “Mr Jones, whither will you drive me?", "그녀가 말했다, \"존스 씨, 저를 어디로 몰아가시려는 거예요?"], ["What hope have I to bestow?", "제가 무슨 희망을 드릴 수 있겠어요?"], ["You know my father's intentions.”--“But I know,”", "아버지의 뜻을 당신도 알고 계시잖아요.\"—\"하지만 나는 알고 있소,\""], ["answered he, “your compliance with them cannot be compelled.”--“What,”", "그가 대답했다, \"당신이 그 뜻에 따르도록 강요할 수는 없다는 것을.\"—\"제가 거역하면,\""], ["says she, “must be the dreadful consequence of my disobedience?", "그녀가 말했다, \"그 끔찍한 결과가 어떻겠어요?"], ["My own ruin is my least concern.", "제 자신의 파멸은 가장 작은 걱정이에요."], ["I cannot bear the thoughts of being the cause of my father's misery.”--“He is himself the cause,”", "제가 아버지를 불행하게 만드는 원인이 된다는 생각만은 견딜 수가 없어요.\"—\"그분 스스로가 원인이오,\""], ["cries Jones, “by exacting a power over you which Nature hath not given him.", "존스가 외쳤다, \"자연이 그분께 주지 않은 권력을 당신에게 휘두르려 하시니 말이오."], ["Think on the misery which I am to suffer if I am to lose you, and see on which side pity will turn the balance.”--“Think of it!”", "만일 내가 당신을 잃어야 한다면 내가 겪어야 할 그 비참함을 생각해 보오, 그리고 동정이 어느 쪽으로 저울을 기울일지 헤아려 보오.\"—\"그걸 생각해 보라고요!\""], ["replied she: “can you imagine I do not feel the ruin which I must bring on you, should I comply with your desire?", "그녀가 되받았다. \"제가 당신의 바람을 따른다면 당신께 끼치게 될 그 파멸을 제가 느끼지 못한다고 여기시는 거예요?"], ["It is that thought which gives me resolution to bid you fly from me for ever, and avoid your own destruction.”--“I fear no destruction,”", "바로 그 생각이, 당신께 영원히 제게서 달아나 당신 자신의 파멸을 피하라고 말할 결심을 제게 주는 거예요.\"—\"나는 어떤 파멸도 두렵지 않소,\""], ["cries he, “but the loss of Sophia.", "그가 외쳤다, \"소피아를 잃는 것 말고는."], ["If you would save me from the most bitter agonies, recall that cruel sentence.", "가장 쓰라린 고통에서 나를 구하고 싶다면, 그 잔인한 선고를 거두어 주오."], ["Indeed, I can never part with you, indeed I cannot.”", "정말이지, 나는 결코 당신과 헤어질 수 없소, 정말로 그럴 수 없소.\""]], [["The lovers now stood both silent and trembling, Sophia being unable to withdraw her hand from Jones, and he almost as unable to hold it; when the scene, which I believe some of my readers will think had lasted long enough, was interrupted by one of so different a nature, that we shall reserve the relation of it for a different chapter.", "이제 두 연인은 모두 말없이 떨며 서 있었다. 소피아는 존스에게서 손을 빼낼 수가 없었고, 그 역시 거의 그 손을 잡고 있을 수 없을 지경이었다. 그때, 내 독자들 가운데 더러는 이미 충분히 길었다고 여길 법한 이 장면이, 사뭇 다른 성질의 또 다른 장면에 의해 끊기고 말았다. 그 이야기는 다른 장으로 미루어 두기로 하겠다."]], [["Being of a much more tempestuous kind than the former.", "9장. 앞 장보다 훨씬 더 격렬한 종류의 이야기."]], [["Before we proceed with what now happened to our lovers, it may be proper to recount what had past in the hall during their tender interview.", "지금 우리 연인들에게 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지로 넘어가기 전에, 그 애틋한 만남이 이어지는 동안 거실에서 어떤 일이 오갔는지를 먼저 들려주는 편이 좋겠다."]], [["Soon after Jones had left Mr Western in the manner above mentioned, his sister came to him, and was presently informed of all that had passed between her brother and Sophia relating to Blifil.", "존스가 앞서 이야기한 대로 웨스턴 씨를 떠난 직후, 그의 누이가 그에게 왔고, 곧 블리필을 둘러싸고 오라비와 소피아 사이에 오간 모든 일을 전해 듣게 되었다."]], [["This behaviour in her niece the good lady construed to be an absolute breach of the condition on which she had engaged to keep her love for Mr Jones a secret.", "이 선량한 부인은 조카딸의 이런 처신을, 자신이 존스 씨를 향한 그 애정을 비밀에 부쳐 두기로 약속했던 조건을 완전히 깨뜨린 것으로 받아들였다."], ["She considered herself, therefore, at full liberty to reveal all she knew to the squire, which she immediately did in the most explicit terms, and without any ceremony or preface.", "그래서 그녀는 자신이 아는 모든 것을 지주에게 털어놓을 완전한 자유가 있다고 여겼고, 곧장 아무런 격식이나 서두도 없이 더없이 분명한 말로 그렇게 했다."]], [["The idea of a marriage between Jones and his daughter, had never once entered into the squire's head, either in the warmest minutes of his affection towards that young man, or from suspicion, or on any other occasion.", "존스와 자기 딸의 결혼이라는 발상은, 그 젊은이를 향한 애정이 가장 뜨겁던 순간에도, 의심에서든, 그 밖의 어떤 경우에든, 지주의 머릿속에 단 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["He did indeed consider a parity of fortune and circumstances to be physically as necessary an ingredient in marriage, as difference of sexes, or any other essential; and had no more apprehension of his daughter's falling in love with a poor man, than with any animal of a different species.", "그는 재산과 처지가 엇비슷한 것이야말로 결혼에서 남녀가 서로 다른 성이라는 점이나 그 밖의 어떤 본질적 요소만큼이나 물리적으로 빠질 수 없는 성분이라고 여겼다. 그리하여 그는 자기 딸이 가난한 사내와 사랑에 빠지는 일을, 종이 다른 어떤 짐승과 사랑에 빠지는 일만큼이나 도무지 있을 수 없는 일로 보았다."]], [["He became, therefore, like one thunderstruck at his sister's relation.", "그러니 그는 누이의 이야기에 벼락이라도 맞은 사람처럼 굳어 버렸다."], ["He was, at first, incapable of making any answer, having been almost deprived of his breath by the violence of the surprize.", "처음에는 어떤 대답도 할 수 없었으니, 놀라움의 격렬함에 숨이 거의 막혀 버린 탓이었다."], ["This, however, soon returned, and, as is usual in other cases after an intermission, with redoubled force and fury.", "그러나 숨은 이내 돌아왔고, 다른 경우들에서도 으레 그렇듯, 한차례 멎었다가 갑절의 힘과 분노로 되살아났다."]], [["The first use he made of the power of speech, after his recovery from the sudden effects of his astonishment, was to discharge a round volley of oaths and imprecations.", "그가 그 갑작스러운 경악의 충격에서 깨어나 말할 힘을 되찾은 뒤 가장 먼저 한 일은, 욕설과 저주를 한바탕 무더기로 쏟아내는 것이었다."], ["After which he proceeded hastily to the apartment where he expected to find the lovers, and murmured, or rather indeed roared forth, intentions of revenge every step he went.", "그러고는 연인들이 있으리라 짐작되는 방으로 황급히 나아가며, 걸음을 옮길 때마다 복수의 뜻을 중얼거리듯, 아니 사실은 으르렁대듯 토해 냈다."]], [["As when two doves, or two wood-pigeons, or as when Strephon and Phyllis (for that comes nearest to the mark) are retired into some pleasant solitary grove, to enjoy the delightful conversation of Love, that bashful boy, who cannot speak in public, and is never a good companion to more than two at a time; here, while every object is serene, should hoarse thunder burst suddenly through the shattered clouds, and rumbling roll along the sky, the frightened maid starts from the mossy bank or verdant turf, the pale livery of death succeeds the red regimentals in which Love had before drest her cheeks, fear shakes her whole frame, and her lover scarce supports her trembling tottering limbs.", "두 마리 비둘기가, 혹은 두 마리 산비둘기가, 아니면 스트레폰과 필리스가 (이쪽이 더 들어맞겠다) 사랑이라는 그 수줍은 소년의 달콤한 속삭임을 즐기려고 어느 한적하고 아늑한 숲으로 물러나 있을 때— 그 소년은 사람들 앞에서는 입을 떼지 못하고, 한 번에 두 사람 넘게는 결코 좋은 동무가 되어 주지 못하는 법이다—그렇게 온갖 것이 고요하기만 한데, 별안간 갈라진 구름을 뚫고 쉰 목소리의 천둥이 터져 나와 우르릉 하늘을 따라 굴러간다면, 놀란 처녀는 이끼 낀 둑이나 푸른 잔디밭에서 화들짝 일어나고, 조금 전 사랑이 그 뺨에 입혀 두었던 붉은 군복 같은 빛 대신 죽음의 창백한 제복이 들어서며, 두려움이 그녀의 온몸을 떨게 하고, 연인은 떨며 휘청대는 그녀의 사지를 간신히 떠받친다."]], [["Or as when two gentlemen, strangers to the wondrous wit of the place, are cracking a bottle together at some inn or tavern at Salisbury, if the great Dowdy, who acts the part of a madman as well as some of his setters-on do that of a fool, should rattle his chains, and dreadfully hum forth the grumbling catch along the gallery; the frighted strangers stand aghast; scared at the horrid sound, they seek some place of shelter from the approaching danger; and if the well-barred windows did admit their exit, would venture their necks to escape the threatening fury now coming upon them.", "또는 그 고장의 놀라운 익살을 알지 못하는 두 신사가 솔즈베리의 어느 여관이나 선술집에서 함께 술병을 비우고 있을 때, 그 부추기는 패거리 가운데 누구는 바보 역을 곧잘 해내듯이 미치광이 역을 곧잘 해내는 그 거대한 다우디가 회랑을 따라 제 쇠사슬을 절그럭거리며 투덜대는 가락을 무시무시하게 흥얼거린다면, 겁에 질린 두 손님은 그 끔찍한 소리에 얼이 빠져 멍하니 서 있다가, 다가오는 위험을 피할 은신처를 찾고, 만일 단단히 빗장 지른 창문이 빠져나갈 틈이라도 내어 준다면 닥쳐오는 그 협박조의 광기에서 벗어나려 제 목숨조차 내걸 것이다."]], [["So trembled poor Sophia, so turned she pale at the noise of her father, who, in a voice most dreadful to hear, came on swearing, cursing, and vowing the destruction of Jones.", "가엾은 소피아도 그렇게 떨었고, 아버지의 고함 소리에 그렇게 얼굴이 새하얗게 질렸다. 아버지는 듣기에 더없이 무시무시한 목소리로 욕하고, 저주하고, 존스를 끝장내겠노라 맹세하며 다가오고 있었다."], ["To say the truth, I believe the youth himself would, from some prudent considerations, have preferred another place of abode at this time, had his terror on Sophia's account given him liberty to reflect a moment on what any otherways concerned himself, than as his love made him partake whatever affected her.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그 젊은이 자신도 어떤 신중한 셈속에서라면 이 순간만큼은 다른 거처를 더 바랐으리라고 나는 믿는다. 다만 소피아를 향한 공포가, 그녀에게 닥치는 모든 일을 사랑 때문에 함께 나누어 짊어지게 하는 것 말고 달리 제 자신과 관계된 무엇을 단 한순간이라도 헤아릴 겨를을 그에게 허락하지 않았을 뿐이다."]], [["And now the squire, having burst open the door, beheld an object which instantly suspended all his fury against Jones; this was the ghastly appearance of Sophia, who had fainted away in her lover's arms. This tragical sight Mr Western no sooner beheld, than all his rage forsook him; he roared for help with his utmost violence; ran first to his daughter, then back to the door calling for water, and then back again to Sophia, never considering in whose arms she then was, nor perhaps once recollecting that there was such a person in the world as Jones; for indeed I believe the present circumstances of his daughter were now the sole consideration which employed his thoughts.", "그리하여 지주는 문을 박차고 들어서다가, 존스를 향한 모든 분노를 그 자리에서 멎게 하는 한 광경을 마주했다. 그것은 연인의 품 안에서 정신을 잃은 소피아의 핏기 없는 모습이었다. 이 비극적인 장면을 보자마자 웨스턴 씨의 분노는 온데간데없이 사라졌다. 그는 있는 힘껏 사람을 부르며 울부짖었고, 먼저 딸에게 달려갔다가, 다시 문 쪽으로 돌아가 물을 외쳤다가, 또다시 소피아에게로 되돌아왔다. 그녀가 그때 누구의 품에 안겨 있는지는 전혀 헤아리지 못했고, 어쩌면 존스라는 사람이 이 세상에 있다는 사실조차 단 한 번도 떠올리지 못했다. 실로 그때 그의 머릿속을 채운 단 하나의 생각은 딸의 지금 처지뿐이었다고 나는 믿는다."]], [["Mrs Western and a great number of servants soon came to the assistance of Sophia with water, cordials, and everything necessary on those occasions.", "이내 웨스턴 부인과 많은 하인들이 물과 강심제, 그리고 그런 경우에 필요한 온갖 것들을 들고 소피아를 도우러 달려왔다."], ["These were applied with such success, that Sophia in a very few minutes began to recover, and all the symptoms of life to return.", "그것들이 어찌나 효험을 발휘했던지, 소피아는 채 몇 분도 지나지 않아 깨어나기 시작했고, 살아 있는 모든 기색이 되돌아왔다."], ["Upon which she was presently led off by her own maid and Mrs Western: nor did that good lady depart without leaving some wholesome admonitions with her brother, on the dreadful effects of his passion, or, as she pleased to call it, madness.", "그러자 그녀는 곧 자기 하녀와 웨스턴 부인의 부축을 받아 그 자리를 떠났다. 그 선량한 부인은 자기 격정의, 아니 그녀가 즐겨 부르듯 광기의 무시무시한 결과에 대해 오라비에게 몇 마디 유익한 충고를 남겨 두고서야 자리를 떴다."]], [["The squire, perhaps, did not understand this good advice, as it was delivered in obscure hints, shrugs, and notes of admiration: at least, if he did understand it, he profited very little by it; for no sooner was he cured of his immediate fears for his daughter, than he relapsed into his former frenzy, which must have produced an immediate battle with Jones, had not parson Supple, who was a very strong man, been present, and by mere force restrained the squire from acts of hostility.", "지주는 이 좋은 충고를 알아듣지 못했을지도 모른다. 그것이 알쏭달쏭한 암시와 어깨짓과 탄식 섞인 가락으로 전해진 까닭이다. 적어도 설령 알아들었다 해도, 거기서 얻은 것은 거의 없었다. 딸을 향한 당장의 두려움에서 풀려나기가 무섭게 그는 곧장 이전의 광란으로 되돌아갔으니, 만일 매우 기운이 센 사내인 서플 목사가 그 자리에 있어 순전한 힘으로 지주의 적대 행위를 막지 않았다면 그 즉시 존스와 한판 싸움이 벌어졌을 것이다."]], [["The moment Sophia was departed, Jones advanced in a very suppliant manner to Mr Western, whom the parson held in his arms, and begged him to be pacified; for that, while he continued in such a passion, it would be impossible to give him any satisfaction.", "소피아가 떠나가자마자, 존스는 더없이 애원하는 태도로 웨스턴 씨에게 다가갔다. 목사가 그를 양팔로 붙들고 있었는데, 존스는 부디 노여움을 가라앉혀 달라고 빌었다. 그가 그토록 격분해 있는 한 그에게 어떤 사죄도 드릴 수 없을 것이기 때문이었다."]], [["“I wull have satisfaction o' thee,”", "\"내 너헌테 만족을 받아낼 테다.\""], ["answered the squire; “so doff thy clothes.", "지주가 대꾸했다. \"그러니 옷을 벗어라."], ["At unt half a man, and I'll lick thee as well as wast ever licked in thy life.”", "넌 사내 반푼어치도 안 되는 놈이니, 네 평생 맞아 본 것 중에 제일 흠씬 두들겨 패 주마.\""], ["He then bespattered the youth with abundance of that language which passes between country gentlemen who embrace opposite sides of the question; with frequent applications to him to salute that part which is generally introduced into all controversies that arise among the lower orders of the English gentry at horse-races, cock-matches, and other public places.", "그러고는 그 젊은이에게, 시골 신사들이 견해를 달리하여 서로 맞설 때 주고받는 그런 말들을 잔뜩 퍼부었다. 게다가 영국 하급 향신 계층이 경마장이며 닭싸움판이며 그 밖의 공공장소에서 벌이는 온갖 다툼에 으레 끼어드는 그 신체 부위에 입을 맞추라는 요구까지 빈번히 덧붙였다."], ["Allusions to this part are likewise often made for the sake of the jest.", "이 부위에 빗댄 말은 농지거리 삼아서도 자주 나오는 법이다."], ["And here, I believe, the wit is generally misunderstood.", "그런데 여기서 나는 그 익살이 흔히 잘못 이해된다고 믿는다."], ["In reality, it lies in desiring another to kiss your a-- for having just before threatened to kick his; for I have observed very accurately, that no one ever desires you to kick that which belongs to himself, nor offers to kiss this part in another.", "실상 그 묘미는, 방금 상대의 엉덩이를 걷어차겠노라 위협해 놓고는 도리어 제 엉덩이에 입을 맞추라 청하는 데에 있다. 나는 아주 면밀히 관찰한 바, 누구도 제게 속한 것을 걷어차 달라 청하는 법이 없고, 또 남의 그 부위에 입 맞추겠노라 나서는 일도 없으니 말이다."]], [["It may likewise seem surprizing that in the many thousand kind invitations of this sort, which every one who hath conversed with country gentlemen must have heard, no one, I believe, hath ever seen a single instance where the desire hath been complied with;--a great instance of their want of politeness; for in town nothing can be more common than for the finest gentlemen to perform this ceremony every day to their superiors, without having that favour once requested of them.", "또 한 가지 놀라운 일이 있다. 시골 신사들과 말을 섞어 본 사람이라면 누구나 수천 번은 들어 보았을 이런 종류의 다정한 초대 가운데, 그 청이 실제로 받아들여진 사례를 단 한 번이라도 본 사람은 없으리라 믿는다는 것이다. 이는 그들이 예의를 갖추지 못했음을 보여 주는 큰 증거다. 도회지에서야 더없이 세련된 신사들이 윗사람에게 매일같이 이 의식을 베푸는 일이, 그것도 그런 호의를 한 번 청 받은 적도 없이 베푸는 일이 흔하디흔하니 말이다."]], [["To all such wit, Jones very calmly answered, “Sir, this usage may perhaps cancel every other obligation you have conferred on me; but there is one you can never cancel; nor will I be provoked by your abuse to lift my hand against the father of Sophia.”", "이 모든 익살에 대해 존스는 아주 차분하게 대답했다. \"어르신, 이런 대우가 어쩌면 어르신께서 제게 베푸신 다른 모든 은혜를 다 지워 버릴지도 모릅니다. 하지만 결코 지우실 수 없는 은혜가 하나 있습니다. 또한 저는 어르신의 모욕에 자극받아 소피아의 아버지께 손을 들어 올리는 일은 하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["At these words the squire grew still more outrageous than before; so that the parson begged Jones to retire; saying, “You behold, sir, how he waxeth wrath at your abode here; therefore let me pray you not to tarry any longer.", "이 말에 지주는 전보다 더욱 길길이 날뛰었고, 그래서 목사는 존스에게 물러가 달라 청했다. \"보다시피 그대가 여기 머무는 것에 저분이 노여움을 더 키우고 있소."], ["His anger is too much kindled for you to commune with him at present.", "그러니 부디 더는 지체하지 마시오."], ["You had better, therefore, conclude your visit, and refer what matters you have to urge in your behalf to some other opportunity.”", "지금은 분노가 너무 거세어 그대가 저분과 말을 나눌 형편이 못 되오. 그러니 방문을 이만 마무리하고, 그대를 위해 호소할 일들은 다른 기회로 미루는 편이 좋겠소.\""]], [["Jones accepted this advice with thanks, and immediately departed.", "존스는 고마운 마음으로 이 충고를 받아들이고 곧 떠났다. 그제야 지주는 두 팔의 자유를 되찾았고, 마음도 어지간히 가라앉아 자신에게 가해졌던 그 제지에 대해 어느 정도 흡족함을 내비쳤다."], ["The squire now regained the liberty of his hands, and so much temper as to express some satisfaction in the restraint which had been laid upon him; declaring that he should certainly have beat his brains out; and adding, “It would have vexed one confoundedly to have been hanged for such a rascal.”", "그러지 않았다면 틀림없이 그 녀석의 골통을 박살내고 말았으리라 하면서, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그런 놈팡이 때문에 교수형이라도 당했더라면 그야말로 분통이 터질 노릇이었지.\""]], [["The parson now began to triumph in the success of his peace-making endeavours, and proceeded to read a lecture against anger, which might perhaps rather have tended to raise than to quiet that passion in some hasty minds.", "이제 목사는 화해를 이끌어 낸 자기 노력의 성과에 으쓱해하기 시작했고, 분노를 경계하는 일장 훈계를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 다만 그 훈계는 성마른 어떤 이들에게는 오히려 그 격정을 가라앉히기보다 부추기는 쪽으로 작용했을지도 모른다."], ["This lecture he enriched with many valuable quotations from the antients, particularly from Seneca; who hath indeed so well handled this passion, that none but a very angry man can read him without great pleasure and profit.", "그는 이 훈계를 고대 사람들에게서, 특히 세네카에게서 끌어온 값진 인용으로 풍성하게 꾸몄다. 세네카는 실로 이 격정을 어찌나 능란하게 다루었던지, 어지간히 성난 사람이 아니고서는 누구라도 그를 읽으며 큰 즐거움과 유익을 얻지 않을 수 없을 정도다."], ["The doctor concluded this harangue with the famous story of Alexander and Clitus; but as I find that entered in my common-place under title Drunkenness, I shall not insert it here.", "목사는 이 일장 연설을 알렉산드로스와 클리투스의 그 유명한 이야기로 마무리했다. 그러나 그 이야기는 내 비망록의 \"취중(醉中)\"이라는 항목 아래 적혀 있으니, 여기에는 옮기지 않겠다."]], [["The squire took no notice of this story, nor perhaps of anything he said; for he interrupted him before he had finished, by calling for a tankard of beer; observing (which is perhaps as true as any observation on this fever of the mind) that anger makes a man dry.", "지주는 이 이야기에 아무런 관심도 두지 않았고, 어쩌면 목사가 한 어떤 말에도 마찬가지였다. 그가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에 맥주 한 잔을 가져오라 외치며 끊어 버렸으니 말이다. 그러면서 그는 (마음의 이 열병에 관한 어떤 관찰만큼이나 어쩌면 참된 관찰을) 이렇게 한마디 했다. 화가 나면 사람은 목이 마르는 법이라고."]], [["No sooner had the squire swallowed a large draught than he renewed the discourse on Jones, and declared a resolution of going the next morning early to acquaint Mr Allworthy.", "지주는 큼직한 한 모금을 들이켜기가 무섭게 다시 존스 이야기로 되돌아갔고, 이튿날 아침 일찌감치 올워디 씨에게 알리러 가겠노라 결심을 밝혔다."], ["His friend would have dissuaded him from this, from the mere motive of good-nature; but his dissuasion had no other effect than to produce a large volley of oaths and curses, which greatly shocked the pious ears of Supple; but he did not dare to remonstrate against a privilege which the squire claimed as a freeborn Englishman.", "그의 벗은 순전히 선량한 마음에서 그를 만류하려 했으나, 그 만류는 욕설과 저주를 한바탕 무더기로 끌어낸 것 말고는 아무 효과도 없었다. 그 욕설과 저주는 서플의 경건한 귀를 크게 거슬렸으나, 자유민으로 태어난 영국인으로서 지주가 누리노라 주장하는 특권에 그는 감히 항의하지 못했다."], ["To say truth, the parson submitted to please his palate at the squire's table, at the expense of suffering now and then this violence to his ears.", "사실대로 말하자면, 목사는 지주의 식탁에서 입의 즐거움을 누리는 대가로 이따금 이런 폭언에 귀를 내맡기는 일을 감수했다."], ["He contented himself with thinking he did not promote this evil practice, and that the squire would not swear an oath the less, if he never entered within his gates.", "그는 자신이 이 못된 습관을 부추긴 것은 아니며, 설령 자신이 그 집 대문 안에 발 한 번 들이지 않았더라도 지주가 욕설을 한 마디라도 덜 했을 리 없다고 생각하며 스스로를 위안했다. 다만 남의 집에서 신사를 꾸짖는 무례를 저지르지는 않았으되, 그는 설교단에서 그를 에둘러 갚아 주었다."], ["However, though he was not guilty of ill manners by rebuking a gentleman in his own house, he paid him off obliquely in the pulpit: which had not, indeed, the good effect of working a reformation in the squire himself; yet it so far operated on his conscience, that he put the laws very severely in execution against others, and the magistrate was the only person in the parish who could swear with impunity.", "그것이 실로 지주 본인을 바로잡는 좋은 효험을 내지는 못했으나, 그래도 그의 양심에 어느 정도는 작용하여, 그는 다른 사람들에게는 이 법을 아주 엄하게 집행했다. 그리하여 그 교구에서 아무 벌도 받지 않고 욕설을 할 수 있는 사람은 오직 그 치안판사 한 명뿐이었다."]], [["In which Mr Western visits Mr Allworthy.", "웨스턴 씨는 조카가 소피아를 찾아간 일이 성공적이었다는 보고에 흡족해하며 함께 아침 식사를 마치고 막 자리에서 물러난 참이었다(올워디 씨가 이 혼사를 그토록 바란 것은 처녀의 재산보다도 그녀의 인품 때문이었다)."], ["Mr Allworthy was now retired from breakfast with his nephew, well satisfied with the report of the young gentleman's successful visit to Sophia (for he greatly desired the match, more on account of the young lady's character than of her riches), when Mr Western broke abruptly in upon them, and without any ceremony began as follows:--", "그때 웨스턴 씨가 느닷없이 두 사람 사이로 뛰어들더니, 예의고 뭐고 없이 다짜고짜 이렇게 입을 열었다."]], [["“There, you have done a fine piece of work truly!", "\"자, 댁이 참으로 훌륭한 일을 해 놓으셨소그래! 그 사생아 녀석을 잘도 키워 놓으셨더군."], ["You have brought up your bastard to a fine purpose; not that I believe you have had any hand in it neither, that is, as a man may say, designedly: but there is a fine kettle-of-fish made on't up at our house.”", "그렇다고 댁이 거기에 손을 댔다는 건 아니오, 그러니까 말하자면 일부러 그랬다는 건 아니라는 말이지. 하지만 우리 집은 지금 아주 난장판이 되어 버렸소이다.\""], ["“What can be the matter, Mr Western?”", "\"대체 무슨 일입니까, 웨스턴 씨?\""], ["said Allworthy.", "올워디가 물었다."], ["“O, matter enow of all conscience: my daughter hath fallen in love with your bastard, that's all; but I won't ge her a hapeny, not the twentieth part of a brass varden.", "\"오, 양심에 비춰 봐도 일은 차고도 넘치오. 내 딸년이 댁의 사생아 놈한테 홀딱 빠져 버렸단 말이오, 그게 다요."], ["I always thought what would come o'", "하지만 내 그년한테는 반 푼도 안 줄 거요, 놋쇠 동전 한 닢의 스무 분의 일도 안 줄 거란 말이오."], ["breeding up a bastard like a gentleman, and letting un come about to vok's houses.", "사생아를 신사처럼 길러서 남의 집을 들락거리게 하면 무슨 꼴이 날지 내 진작에 알아봤지. 그놈 손에 못 닿은 게 천만다행이지."], ["It's well vor un I could not get at un: I'd a lick'd un; I'd a spoil'd his caterwauling; I'd a taught the son of a whore to meddle with meat for his master.", "안 그랬으면 흠씬 두들겨 패서 그 발정 난 울음소리를 못 내게 만들어 놨을 거요. 그 화냥년의 자식한테 제 주인의 고깃점에 함부로 손대면 어찌 되는지 단단히 가르쳐 줬을 텐데."], ["He shan't ever have a morsel of meat of mine, or a varden to buy it: if she will ha un, one smock shall be her portion.", "그놈은 내 고기 한 점도, 그걸 살 동전 한 닢도 절대 못 가질 거요. 그년이 기어이 그놈을 갖겠다면, 입은 속치마 한 장이 그년 몫의 전부일 거란 말이오."], ["I'd sooner ge my esteate to the zinking fund, that it may be sent to Hanover to corrupt our nation with.”", "차라리 내 재산을 감채기금에 넘겨서 하노버로 보내 우리 나라를 썩히는 데 쓰게 하는 편이 낫겠소.\" \"진심으로 유감입니다.\""], ["“I am heartily sorry,”", "올워디가 외쳤다."], ["cries Allworthy. “Pox o'", "\"유감이고 나발이고,\""], ["your sorrow,”", "웨스턴이 말했다."], ["says Western; “it will do me abundance of good when I have lost my only child, my poor Sophy, that was the joy of my heart, and all the hope and comfort of my age; but I am resolved I will turn her out o'", "\"내가 하나뿐인 자식을, 내 가엾은 소피를, 내 가슴의 기쁨이자 늘그막의 온갖 희망이요 위안이던 그 아이를 잃고 나면, 댁의 유감이 내게 무슨 큰 도움이 되겠소. 하지만 내 그년을 문밖으로 내쫓기로 작심했소."], ["doors; she shall beg, and starve, and rot in the streets.", "빌어먹게 하고, 굶기고, 길바닥에서 썩게 할 거요. 단 반 푼도, 반 푼도 그년은 절대 못 받을 거란 말이오."], ["Not one hapeny, not a hapeny shall she ever hae o' mine.", "그 개자식은 늘 토끼가 웅크린 자리를 잘도 찾아냈지, 썩어 문드러질 놈 같으니."], ["The son of a bitch was always good at finding a hare sitting, an be rotted to'n: I little thought what puss he was looking after; but it shall be the worst he ever vound in his life.", "그놈이 노리던 게 어떤 토끼였는지 내 미처 몰랐던 거요. 하지만 이번 토끼는 그놈 평생에 잡은 것 중 가장 고약한 놈이 될 거요."], ["She shall be no better than carrion: the skin o'er is all he shall ha, and zu you may tell un.”", "그년은 짐승 시체나 다름없는 신세가 될 테고, 그놈이 가질 수 있는 건 그 껍데기뿐일 거란 말이오. 그러니 그렇게 전해도 좋소.\""], ["“I am in amazement,”", "\"도무지 영문을 모르겠습니다.\""], ["cries Allworthy, “at what you tell me, after what passed between my nephew and the young lady no longer ago than yesterday.”", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"바로 어제, 내 조카와 그 처녀 사이에 오간 일을 생각하면 더더욱 그렇습니다.\""], ["“Yes, sir,”", "\"그렇소, 선생,\""], ["answered Western, “it was after what passed between your nephew and she that the whole matter came out.", "웨스턴이 대답했다. \"댁의 조카와 그년 사이에 그 일이 오간 다음에야 모든 게 들통났단 말이오."], ["Mr Blifil there was no sooner gone than the son of a whore came lurching about the house.", "저기 블리필 씨가 떠나기가 무섭게 그 화냥년의 자식이 집 주변을 어슬렁거렸지."], ["Little did I think when I used to love him for a sportsman that he was all the while a poaching after my daughter.”", "사냥꾼이랍시고 그놈을 아끼던 시절에, 그놈이 내내 내 딸년을 노리고 밀렵을 하고 있었을 줄 누가 알았겠소.\" \"사실은,\""], ["“Why truly,”", "올워디가 말했다. \"그에게 그 아이와 함께할 기회를 그토록 많이 주지 않으셨으면 했습니다."], ["says Allworthy, “I could wish you had not given him so many opportunities with her; and you will do me the justice to acknowledge that I have always been averse to his staying so much at your house, though I own I had no suspicion of this kind.”", "그리고 내가 늘 그가 댁의 집에 그토록 오래 머무는 걸 탐탁지 않아 했다는 건 댁도 인정해 주시리라 믿습니다. 다만 이런 종류의 의심은 전혀 품지 못했음을 고백합니다.\""], ["“Why, zounds,”", "\"원, 제기랄,\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다."], ["cries Western, “who could have thought it?", "\"누가 그런 걸 생각이나 했겠소? 그놈이 그년하고 무슨 상관이 있었겠냔 말이오?"], ["What the devil had she to do wi'n?", "그놈은 그년한테 구애하러 온 게 아니라, 나하고 사냥하러 온 거였소.\" \"하지만 그럴 수가 있습니까,\""], ["He did not come there a courting to her; he came there a hunting with me.”", "올워디가 말했다. \"그토록 자주 둘이 함께 있는 걸 보시면서 그들 사이의 사랑의 기색을 단 한 번도 알아채지 못하셨다니 말입니다.\""], ["“But was it possible,”", "\"맹세코 내 평생 단 한 번도 없었소,\""], ["says Allworthy, “that you should never discern any symptoms of love between them, when you have seen them so often together?”", "웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"그놈이 그년한테 입 맞추는 걸 내 평생 한 번도 본 적이 없단 말이오."], ["“Never in my life, as I hope to be saved,”", "게다가 구애는커녕, 그놈은 그년이 같이 있을 때면 다른 때보다 오히려 더 말이 없었소."], ["cries Western: “I never so much as zeed him kiss her in all my life; and so far from courting her, he used rather to be more silent when she was in company than at any other time; and as for the girl, she was always less civil to'n than to any young man that came to the house.", "그리고 그년으로 말하자면, 집에 오는 어떤 젊은 놈한테보다도 그놈한테 늘 더 쌀쌀맞게 굴었단 말이오. 그런 일에 관해서라면 나도 남들 못지않게 잘 속아 넘어가지 않는 사람이오."], ["As to that matter, I am not more easy to be deceived than another; I would not have you think I am, neighbour.”", "내가 그렇다고 여기진 마시오, 이웃 양반.\" 올워디는 이 말에 웃음을 참기 어려웠으나 억지로 자신을 다잡았다."], ["Allworthy could scarce refrain laughter at this; but he resolved to do a violence to himself; for he perfectly well knew mankind, and had too much good-breeding and good-nature to offend the squire in his present circumstances.", "그는 인간이라는 존재를 너무도 잘 알았고, 또 지금 같은 처지의 지주를 언짢게 하기에는 너무나 교양 있고 천성이 선량한 사람이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 웨스턴에게 이번 일에 자기가 어찌해 주기를 바라느냐고 물었다."], ["He then asked Western what he would have him do upon this occasion.", "이에 상대는 이렇게 답했다. \"그 망나니 놈을 자기 집에 얼씬도 못 하게 막아 주었으면 좋겠소."], ["To which the other answered, “That he would have him keep the rascal away from his house, and that he would go and lock up the wench; for he was resolved to make her marry Mr Blifil in spite of her teeth.”", "그리고 자기는 가서 그 계집애를 가둬 놓겠소. 이를 악물고 버티더라도 기어이 블리필 씨와 혼인시킬 작정이니까 말이오.\""], ["He then shook Blifil by the hand, and swore he would have no other son-in-law.", "그러고는 블리필의 손을 흔들며, 다른 사위는 절대 두지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 곧이어 그는 작별을 고하며, 집안이 워낙 엉망이 되어 서둘러 돌아가 딸년이 빠져나가지 못하도록 단속해야 한다고 했다."], ["Presently after which he took his leave; saying his house was in such disorder that it was necessary for him to make haste home, to take care his daughter did not give him the slip; and as for Jones, he swore if he caught him at his house, he would qualify him to run for the geldings' plate.", "그리고 존스로 말하자면, 만일 자기 집에서 그놈을 붙잡으면 거세마 경주에나 나가도록 손을 봐 주겠노라 맹세했다."]], [["When Allworthy and Blifil were again left together, a long silence ensued between them; all which interval the young gentleman filled up with sighs, which proceeded partly from disappointment, but more from hatred; for the success of Jones was much more grievous to him than the loss of Sophia.", "올워디와 블리필만 다시 남게 되자, 두 사람 사이에는 긴 침묵이 이어졌다. 그동안 내내 젊은 신사는 한숨으로 그 틈을 메웠는데, 그 한숨은 일부는 실망에서, 그러나 더 많게는 증오에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그에게는 소피아를 잃은 것보다 존스가 성공을 거둔 것이 훨씬 더 가슴 쓰린 일이었기 때문이다."]], [["At length his uncle asked him what he was determined to do, and he answered in the following words:--“Alas!", "마침내 그의 숙부가 어찌하기로 마음먹었느냐고 묻자, 그는 다음과 같은 말로 대답했다. \"아아!"], ["sir, can it be a question what step a lover will take, when reason and passion point different ways?", "숙부님, 이성과 정념이 서로 다른 길을 가리킬 때, 사랑에 빠진 자가 어느 쪽으로 걸음을 옮길지가 과연 물어볼 일이겠습니까? 그런 갈림길에서 그가 언제나 후자를 따르리라는 것은 너무도 분명한 듯하여 두렵습니다."], ["I am afraid it is too certain he will, in that dilemma, always follow the latter.", "이성은 제게 다른 사람에게 마음을 둔 여인에 대한 생각일랑 전부 버리라고 명합니다. 그러나 제 정념은 그녀가 언젠가 마음을 돌려 제게로 기울지도 모른다는 희망을 품으라 합니다."], ["Reason dictates to me, to quit all thoughts of a woman who places her affections on another; my passion bids me hope she may in time change her inclinations in my favour.", "다만 여기서 한 가지 반론이 제기될 수 있다고 봅니다. 만일 그 반론에 충분히 답할 수 없다면, 저는 더 이상의 추구를 완전히 단념하게 될 것입니다."], ["Here, however, I conceive an objection may be raised, which, if it could not fully be answered, would totally deter me from any further pursuit.", "곧, 이미 다른 사람이 차지한 듯한 마음에서 그를 밀어내려 애쓰는 일의 부당함을 말하는 것입니다. 하지만 웨스턴 씨의 단호한 결심으로 보건대, 이 경우에는 제가 그렇게 함으로써 오히려 모든 당사자의 행복을 증진하게 됩니다."], ["I mean the injustice of endeavouring to supplant another in a heart of which he seems already in possession; but the determined resolution of Mr Western shows that, in this case, I shall, by so doing, promote the happiness of every party; not only that of the parent, who will thus be preserved from the highest degree of misery, but of both the others, who must be undone by this match.", "그리하여 더없이 깊은 비참함에서 구원받게 될 그 아버지의 행복뿐 아니라, 이 혼사로 반드시 파멸하고 말 다른 두 사람의 행복까지도 말입니다. 그 처녀는 분명 모든 의미에서 신세를 망치게 될 것입니다."], ["The lady, I am sure, will be undone in every sense; for, besides the loss of most part of her own fortune, she will be not only married to a beggar, but the little fortune which her father cannot withhold from her will be squandered on that wench with whom I know he yet converses.", "자기 재산의 대부분을 잃을 뿐 아니라, 거지에게 시집가는 셈이 되고, 게다가 그녀의 아버지가 차마 떼어 놓지 못할 그 얼마 안 되는 재산마저도, 제가 알기로 그가 아직도 만나고 다니는 그 계집에게 탕진되고 말 테니까요. 아니, 그것은 사소한 일입니다."], ["Nay, that is a trifle; for I know him to be one of the worst men in the world; for had my dear uncle known what I have hitherto endeavoured to conceal, he must have long since abandoned so profligate a wretch.”", "저는 그가 세상에서 가장 못된 사람 중 하나임을 알고 있으니까요. 만일 제가 지금껏 숨기려 애써 온 일을 사랑하는 숙부님께서 아셨더라면, 진작에 그토록 방탕한 망나니를 내치셨을 것입니다.\""], ["“How!”", "\"뭐라!\""], ["said Allworthy; “hath he done anything worse than I already know?", "올워디가 말했다. \"그가 내가 이미 아는 것보다 더 못된 짓을 저질렀단 말이냐?"], ["Tell me, I beseech you?”", "부디 말해 다오.\" \"아닙니다,\""], ["“No,”", "블리필이 대답했다."], ["replied Blifil; “it is now past, and perhaps he may have repented of it.”", "\"이제 다 지난 일이고, 어쩌면 그가 뉘우쳤을지도 모릅니다.\" \"네 도리를 걸고 명하노니,\""], ["“I command you, on your duty,”", "올워디가 말했다. \"네가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 내게 말하거라.\""], ["said Allworthy, “to tell me what you mean.”", "\"아시다시피, 숙부님,\" 블리필이 말했다."], ["“You know, sir,”", "\"저는 한 번도 숙부님의 뜻을 거스른 적이 없습니다. 하지만 이 일을 입에 올린 것이 후회스럽습니다."], ["says Blifil, “I never disobeyed you; but I am sorry I mentioned it, since it may now look like revenge, whereas, I thank Heaven, no such motive ever entered my heart; and if you oblige me to discover it, I must be his petitioner to you for your forgiveness.”", "이제 와서는 마치 복수처럼 보일 수도 있으니까요. 그러나 하늘에 감사하게도, 제 마음에는 그런 동기가 단 한 번도 들어온 적이 없습니다."], ["“I will have no conditions,”", "그러니 숙부님께서 굳이 제게 이 일을 밝히라 하신다면, 저는 그를 위해 숙부님께 용서를 비는 탄원자가 되어야겠습니다.\" \"나는 어떤 조건도 받아들이지 않겠다,\""], ["answered Allworthy; “I think I have shown tenderness enough towards him, and more perhaps than you ought to thank me for.”", "올워디가 대답했다. \"나는 그에게 충분히, 어쩌면 네가 내게 고마워해야 할 것보다도 더 많은 너그러움을 베풀어 왔다고 생각한다.\""], ["“More, indeed, I fear, than he deserved,”", "\"정말로, 그가 받을 자격이 있는 것보다 더 많이 베푸셨을까 두렵습니다,\""], ["cries Blifil; “for in the very day of your utmost danger, when myself and all the family were in tears, he filled the house with riot and debauchery.", "블리필이 외쳤다. \"숙부님께서 가장 위태로우셨던 바로 그날, 저와 온 집안이 눈물에 잠겨 있을 때, 그는 온 집 안을 떠들썩한 소동과 방탕으로 가득 채웠으니까요."], ["He drank, and sung, and roared; and when I gave him a gentle hint of the indecency of his actions, he fell into a violent passion, swore many oaths, called me rascal, and struck me.”", "술을 마시고, 노래를 부르고, 고래고래 소리를 질렀습니다. 그래서 제가 그 행동이 얼마나 무례한지 점잖게 일러 주었더니, 그는 불같이 화를 내며 온갖 욕설을 퍼붓고, 저를 망나니라 부르며 저를 때렸습니다.\""], ["“How!”", "\"뭐라!\""], ["cries Allworthy; “did he dare to strike you?”", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"그가 감히 너를 때렸단 말이냐?\""], ["“I am sure,”", "\"분명히,\""], ["cries Blifil, “I have forgiven him that long ago.", "블리필이 외쳤다. \"그 일은 제가 오래전에 용서했습니다."], ["I wish I could so easily forget his ingratitude to the best of benefactors; and yet even that I hope you will forgive him, since he must have certainly been possessed with the devil: for that very evening, as Mr Thwackum and myself were taking the air in the fields, and exulting in the good symptoms which then first began to discover themselves, we unluckily saw him engaged with a wench in a manner not fit to be mentioned.", "그가 가장 훌륭한 은인께 저지른 그 배은망덕도 그렇게 쉽게 잊을 수 있다면 좋으련만. 하지만 그것마저도 숙부님께서 부디 용서해 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["Mr Thwackum, with more boldness than prudence, advanced to rebuke him, when (I am sorry to say it) he fell upon the worthy man, and beat him so outrageously that I wish he may have yet recovered the bruises.", "그는 분명 악마에 씌어 있었을 테니까요. 바로 그날 저녁, 스왁검 선생님과 제가 들판으로 바람을 쐬러 나가, 그제야 처음으로 나타나기 시작한 좋은 차도에 기뻐하고 있을 때, 불행히도 우리는 그가 차마 입에 올릴 수 없는 모양새로 어떤 계집과 엉켜 있는 것을 보고 말았습니다."], ["Nor was I without my share of the effects of his malice, while I endeavoured to protect my tutor; but that I have long forgiven; nay, I prevailed with Mr Thwackum to forgive him too, and not to inform you of a secret which I feared might be fatal to him.", "스왁검 선생님은 신중함보다 대담함이 앞서 그를 꾸짖으러 다가가셨는데, (말씀드리기 송구하오나) 그가 그 훌륭하신 분께 달려들어 어찌나 모질게 두들겨 팼던지, 그 멍이 아직도 다 나았을지 모르겠습니다. 또한 제가 제 스승을 지켜 드리려 애쓰는 동안 저 역시 그의 악의가 미친 영향에서 무사하지 못했습니다."], ["And now, sir, since I have unadvisedly dropped a hint of this matter, and your commands have obliged me to discover the whole, let me intercede with you for him.”", "하지만 그것은 제가 오래전에 용서했고, 아니, 스왁검 선생님께도 그를 용서하시고 그에게 치명적일까 두려운 그 비밀을 숙부님께 알리지 않으시도록 제가 권했습니다. 그런데 이제, 제가 경솔하게 이 일의 단서를 흘리고 말았고, 숙부님의 명에 못 이겨 모든 것을 밝히게 되었으니, 부디 그를 위해 제가 숙부님께 간청하도록 허락해 주십시오.\""], ["“O child!”", "\"오, 얘야!\""], ["said Allworthy, “I know not whether I should blame or applaud your goodness, in concealing such villany a moment: but where is Mr Thwackum?", "올워디가 말했다. \"그런 악행을 한순간이라도 숨긴 네 착한 마음을 두고, 내가 너를 나무라야 할지 칭찬해야 할지 모르겠구나."], ["Not that I want any confirmation of what you say; but I will examine all the evidence of this matter, to justify to the world the example I am resolved to make of such a monster.”", "그런데 스왁검 선생은 어디 계시냐? 네 말에 무슨 확증이 필요해서가 아니라, 이 일의 모든 증거를 살펴, 그런 괴물을 본보기로 삼으려는 내 결심을 세상에 정당하게 밝히려는 것이다.\""]], [["Thwackum was now sent for, and presently appeared.", "곧 스왁검이 불려 왔고, 이내 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He corroborated every circumstance which the other had deposed; nay, he produced the record upon his breast, where the handwriting of Mr Jones remained very legible in black and blue.", "그는 상대가 진술한 모든 정황을 뒷받침했다. 아니, 그는 자신의 가슴에 새겨진 증거까지 내보였는데, 거기에는 존스 씨의 필적이 시퍼렇게 멍든 채로 또렷이 남아 있었다."], ["He concluded with declaring to Mr Allworthy, that he should have long since informed him of this matter, had not Mr Blifil, by the most earnest interpositions, prevented him.", "그는 올워디 씨에게, 블리필 씨가 더없이 간곡하게 만류하지만 않았더라면 진작에 이 일을 알려 드렸을 것이라고 밝히며 말을 맺었다. \"그 사람은,\""], ["“He is,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “an excellent youth: though such forgiveness of enemies is carrying the matter too far.”", "\"참으로 훌륭한 젊은이입니다. 다만 원수를 그렇게까지 용서하는 것은 일을 너무 지나치게 끌고 가는 것이지요.\""]], [["In reality, Blifil had taken some pains to prevail with the parson, and to prevent the discovery at that time; for which he had many reasons.", "사실 블리필은 그때 이 일이 발각되지 않도록 목사를 구슬리는 데 적잖이 공을 들였는데, 거기에는 여러 이유가 있었다."], ["He knew that the minds of men are apt to be softened and relaxed from their usual severity by sickness.", "그는 사람의 마음이 병을 앓고 나면 평소의 엄격함이 누그러지고 풀어지기 쉽다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Besides, he imagined that if the story was told when the fact was so recent, and the physician about the house, who might have unravelled the real truth, he should never be able to give it the malicious turn which he intended.", "게다가 그는, 만일 사건이 일어난 지 얼마 안 되어, 진상을 밝혀냈을지도 모를 의사가 집에 머무는 동안 이 이야기를 했다가는, 자신이 의도한 악의적인 방향으로 그것을 비틀어 놓을 수 없으리라 짐작했다. 또한 그는 존스의 경솔함이 새로운 불만거리를 보태 줄 때까지 이 일을 묵혀 두기로 마음먹었다."], ["Again, he resolved to hoard up this business, till the indiscretion of Jones should afford some additional complaints; for he thought the joint weight of many facts falling upon him together, would be the most likely to crush him; and he watched, therefore, some such opportunity as that with which fortune had now kindly presented him.", "여러 사실이 한꺼번에 그에게 떨어져 그 무게가 합쳐질 때, 그를 짓뭉개기에 가장 좋으리라 여겼기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 지금 운명이 친절하게 마련해 준 것과 같은 그런 기회를 노리고 있었던 것이다."], ["Lastly, by prevailing with Thwackum to conceal the matter for a time, he knew he should confirm an opinion of his friendship to Jones, which he had greatly laboured to establish in Mr Allworthy.", "끝으로, 스왁검을 구슬려 한동안 이 일을 숨기게 함으로써, 그는 자신이 올워디 씨에게 심어 두려고 그토록 애써 온 존스에 대한 우정의 인상을 더욱 굳히게 되리라는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter xi.", "# Chapter xi."]], [["A short chapter; but which contains sufficient matter to affect the good-natured reader.", "짧은 장이지만, 마음씨 고운 독자의 가슴을 울리기에 충분한 이야기를 담고 있다."]], [["It was Mr Allworthy's custom never to punish any one, not even to turn away a servant, in a passion.", "올워디 씨는 화가 난 상태에서는 누구도, 심지어 하인 한 사람조차도 결코 벌하지 않는 것을 원칙으로 삼고 있었다."], ["He resolved therefore to delay passing sentence on Jones till the afternoon.", "그래서 그는 존스에 대한 선고를 오후로 미루기로 마음먹었다."]], [["The poor young man attended at dinner, as usual; but his heart was too much loaded to suffer him to eat. His grief too was a good deal aggravated by the unkind looks of Mr Allworthy; whence he concluded that Western had discovered the whole affair between him and Sophia; but as to Mr Blifil's story, he had not the least apprehension; for of much the greater part he was entirely innocent; and for the residue, as he had forgiven and forgotten it himself, so he suspected no remembrance on the other side. When dinner was over, and the servants departed, Mr Allworthy began to harangue. He set forth, in a long speech, the many iniquities of which Jones had been guilty, particularly those which this day had brought to light; and concluded by telling him, “That unless he could clear himself of the charge, he was resolved to banish him his sight for ever.”", "가엾은 젊은이는 여느 때처럼 만찬 자리에 함께했다. 그러나 그의 가슴은 너무도 무거워 무엇 하나 먹을 수가 없었다. 게다가 그의 슬픔은 올워디 씨의 차가운 눈길에 적잖이 더해졌다. 그 눈길을 보고 그는 웨스턴이 자신과 소피아 사이의 모든 일을 알아챘다고 결론지었다. 그러나 블리필 씨가 늘어놓은 이야기에 대해서만큼은 조금도 짐작하지 못했다. 그 대부분에 대해 그는 전적으로 결백했고, 나머지 부분에 대해서도, 그 자신이 이미 용서하고 잊어버렸기에 상대편 역시 기억하고 있으리라고는 의심조차 하지 않았던 것이다. 만찬이 끝나고 하인들이 물러가자, 올워디 씨가 일장 훈계를 시작했다. 그는 긴 연설을 통해 존스가 저지른 수많은 악행을, 특히 바로 이날 드러난 일들을 낱낱이 펼쳐 보였다. 그러고는 이렇게 말하며 끝을 맺었다. \"이 혐의에서 스스로를 깨끗이 벗기지 못한다면, 나는 너를 영원히 내 눈앞에서 쫓아낼 작정이다.\""]], [["Many disadvantages attended poor Jones in making his defence; nay, indeed, he hardly knew his accusation; for as Mr Allworthy, in recounting the drunkenness, &c., while he lay ill, out of modesty sunk everything that related particularly to himself, which indeed principally constituted the crime; Jones could not deny the charge. His heart was, besides, almost broken already; and his spirits were so sunk, that he could say nothing for himself; but acknowledged the whole, and, like a criminal in despair, threw himself upon mercy; concluding, “That though he must own himself guilty of many follies and inadvertencies, he hoped he had done nothing to deserve what would be to him the greatest punishment in the world.”", "가엾은 존스가 자신을 변호하는 데에는 여러 불리한 사정이 따랐다. 아니, 사실 그는 자신에게 씌워진 죄목조차 제대로 알지 못했다. 올워디 씨가 자신이 앓아누웠을 때의 만취 등을 늘어놓으면서도, 겸손한 마음에 자기 자신과 직접 관련된 부분—실은 바로 그것이 죄의 핵심을 이루고 있었는데—은 모조리 덮어 두었기 때문이다. 그러니 존스로서는 혐의를 부인할 수가 없었다. 게다가 그의 가슴은 이미 거의 무너져 있었고, 기력은 한껏 가라앉아 자신을 위해 아무 말도 할 수가 없었다. 그저 모든 것을 인정하고는, 절망에 빠진 죄인처럼 자비에 몸을 던질 뿐이었다. 그러면서 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"제가 수많은 어리석음과 부주의를 저질렀음은 인정할 수밖에 없지만, 저로서는 이 세상에서 가장 큰 벌이 될 일을 받을 만한 짓은 결코 하지 않았다고 믿습니다.\""]], [["Allworthy answered, “That he had forgiven him too often already, in compassion to his youth, and in hopes of his amendment: that he now found he was an abandoned reprobate, and such as it would be criminal in any one to support and encourage.", "올워디는 이렇게 답했다. \"나는 너의 어림을 가엾이 여겨, 또 네가 고쳐지기를 바라며 이미 너무 자주 너를 용서해 왔다."], ["Nay,”", "이제 보니 너는 구제할 길 없는 망나니이며, 누구든 너를 떠받치고 부추기는 것 자체가 죄가 될 자다. 아니,\""], ["said Mr Allworthy to him, “your audacious attempt to steal away the young lady, calls upon me to justify my own character in punishing you.", "올워디 씨가 그에게 말했다. \"그 아가씨를 몰래 빼돌리려 한 너의 뻔뻔한 시도는, 너를 벌함으로써 내 자신의 명예를 지키지 않을 수 없게 만든다."], ["The world who have already censured the regard I have shown for you may think, with some colour at least of justice, that I connive at so base and barbarous an action--an action of which you must have known my abhorrence: and which, had you had any concern for my ease and honour, as well as for my friendship, you would never have thought of undertaking.", "내가 너에게 베푼 관심을 두고 이미 세상은 나를 비난해 왔다. 그런 마당에 이토록 비열하고 야만스러운 행위를—네가 내 혐오를 분명히 알고 있었을 그런 행위를—내가 눈감아 준다고 여긴다면, 적어도 어느 정도는 정당한 명분이 있다고 세상이 생각할 만하지 않겠느냐."], ["Fie upon it, young man!", "만일 네가 나의 우정뿐 아니라 나의 평안과 명예를 조금이라도 헤아렸다면, 너는 결코 그런 일을 꾀할 생각조차 하지 않았을 것이다."], ["indeed there is scarce any punishment equal to your crimes, and I can scarce think myself justifiable in what I am now going to bestow on you.", "부끄러운 줄 알아라, 젊은이! 참으로 너의 죄에 걸맞은 벌이라고는 거의 없으며, 내가 지금 너에게 베풀려는 것조차 과연 정당한 일인지 나 스스로도 의심스럽다."], ["However, as I have educated you like a child of my own, I will not turn you naked into the world.", "그러나 나는 너를 내 친자식처럼 길러 왔으니, 네 몸뚱이 하나 벌거숭이로 세상에 내던지지는 않겠다. 그러니 이 종이를 펼쳐 보면, 부지런히 애쓴다면 너로 하여금 떳떳한 생계를 꾸려 갈 수 있게 해 줄 무언가를 발견하게 될 것이다."], ["When you open this paper, therefore, you will find something which may enable you, with industry, to get an honest livelihood; but if you employ it to worse purposes, I shall not think myself obliged to supply you farther, being resolved, from this day forward, to converse no more with you on any account.", "하지만 그것을 더 나쁜 데에 쓴다면, 나는 더 이상 너를 도울 의무가 있다고 여기지 않을 것이다. 오늘 이날부터, 어떤 이유로도 다시는 너와 말을 섞지 않기로 결심했기 때문이다."], ["I cannot avoid saying, there is no part of your conduct which I resent more than your ill-treatment of that good young man (meaning Blifil) who hath behaved with so much tenderness and honour towards you.”", "그리고 이 말만은 하지 않을 수 없구나. 너의 모든 행실 가운데, 너에게 그토록 다정하고 명예롭게 대해 준 저 착한 젊은이(블리필을 가리킨 것이다)를 네가 모질게 대한 일만큼 내가 분개하는 일은 없다.\""]], [["These last words were a dose almost too bitter to be swallowed.", "이 마지막 말은 삼키기에 너무도 쓴 약이었다."], ["A flood of tears now gushed from the eyes of Jones, and every faculty of speech and motion seemed to have deserted him.", "이제 존스의 두 눈에서 눈물이 홍수처럼 쏟아졌고, 말하고 움직이는 모든 능력이 그를 저버린 듯했다. 그가 떠나라는 올워디의 단호한 명령에 따를 수 있기까지는 얼마간의 시간이 걸렸다."], ["It was some time before he was able to obey Allworthy's peremptory commands of departing; which he at length did, having first kissed his hands with a passion difficult to be affected, and as difficult to be described.", "그는 마침내 그렇게 했는데, 먼저 올워디의 두 손에 입을 맞추었다. 꾸며 내기도 어렵고, 또 그만큼 묘사하기도 어려운 격정을 담아서."]], [["The reader must be very weak, if, when he considers the light in which Jones then appeared to Mr Allworthy, he should blame the rigour of his sentence.", "그때 존스가 올워디 씨에게 어떤 모습으로 비쳤는지를 헤아려 본다면, 그 선고의 엄격함을 탓하는 독자가 있다면 그는 어지간히 모자란 사람일 것이다."], ["And yet all the neighbourhood, either from this weakness, or from some worse motive, condemned this justice and severity as the highest cruelty.", "그런데도 인근의 온 동네는, 그런 모자람 때문이든 아니면 더 못된 속셈 때문이든, 이 정의롭고 준엄한 처사를 더없이 잔인한 짓이라며 비난했다."], ["Nay, the very persons who had before censured the good man for the kindness and tenderness shown to a bastard (his own, according to the general opinion), now cried out as loudly against turning his own child out of doors.", "아니, 전에는 그 선량한 분이 사생아(세간의 통설로는 그 자신의 자식이라던)에게 베푼 친절과 다정함을 두고 흉보던 바로 그 사람들이, 이제는 그가 제 자식을 집 밖으로 내쫓는다며 한층 더 목청을 높였다."], ["The women especially were unanimous in taking the part of Jones, and raised more stories on the occasion than I have room, in this chapter, to set down.", "특히 여인네들은 한목소리로 존스의 편을 들었고, 이 일을 두고 이 장에 다 적을 수 없을 만큼 많은 이야기를 지어냈다."]], [["One thing must not be omitted, that, in their censures on this occasion, none ever mentioned the sum contained in the paper which Allworthy gave Jones, which was no less than five hundred pounds; but all agreed that he was sent away penniless, and some said naked, from the house of his inhuman father.", "한 가지 빠뜨려서는 안 될 것이 있다. 이 일을 두고 비난을 퍼붓는 가운데, 올워디가 존스에게 건넨 그 종이에 담긴 액수—그것은 무려 오백 파운드나 되었다—를 입에 올리는 사람은 아무도 없었다는 점이다. 다들 입을 모아, 그가 무자비한 아버지의 집에서 한 푼도 없이 쫓겨났다고 했고, 더러는 벌거숭이로 내쫓겼다고까지 말했다."]], [["Containing love-letters, &c.", "연애편지 따위가 담긴 장."]], [["Jones was commanded to leave the house immediately, and told, that his clothes and everything else should be sent to him whithersoever he should order them.", "존스는 즉시 그 집을 떠나라는 명을 받았다. 옷가지며 그 밖의 모든 것은, 그가 어디로든 일러두기만 하면 그리로 보내 주겠다는 말도 함께 들었다."]], [["He accordingly set out, and walked above a mile, not regarding, and indeed scarce knowing, whither he went. At length a little brook obstructing his passage, he threw himself down by the side of it; nor could he help muttering with some little indignation, “Sure my father will not deny me this place to rest in!”", "그리하여 그는 길을 나섰고, 1마일 남짓을 걸었다. 어디로 가는지 마음에 두지도 않았고, 사실상 어디로 가는지조차 거의 알지 못한 채였다. 마침내 작은 시냇물이 앞을 가로막자, 그는 그 곁에 몸을 던지듯 주저앉았다. 그리고 약간의 분함을 담아 이렇게 중얼거리지 않을 수 없었다. \"설마 아버지께서 이렇게 쉴 자리 하나야 마다하시지는 않으시겠지!\""]], [["Here he presently fell into the most violent agonies, tearing his hair from his head, and using most other actions which generally accompany fits of madness, rage, and despair.", "여기서 그는 곧 더없이 격렬한 고뇌에 빠져들어, 머리카락을 쥐어뜯고, 광기와 분노와 절망의 발작에 흔히 뒤따르는 온갖 몸짓을 거듭했다."]], [["When he had in this manner vented the first emotions of passion, he began to come a little to himself.", "이런 식으로 격정의 첫 동요를 쏟아낸 뒤에야, 그는 조금씩 제정신을 되찾기 시작했다."], ["His grief now took another turn, and discharged itself in a gentler way, till he became at last cool enough to reason with his passion, and to consider what steps were proper to be taken in his deplorable condition.", "이제 그의 슬픔은 다른 방향으로 흘러, 한결 부드러운 방식으로 풀려나갔다. 마침내 그는 자신의 격정과 이치를 따져 볼 만큼 차분해졌고, 이 한심한 처지에서 어떤 조치를 취하는 것이 마땅한지를 헤아릴 수 있게 되었다."]], [["And now the great doubt was, how to act with regard to Sophia. The thoughts of leaving her almost rent his heart asunder; but the consideration of reducing her to ruin and beggary still racked him, if possible, more; and if the violent desire of possessing her person could have induced him to listen one moment to this alternative, still he was by no means certain of her resolution to indulge his wishes at so high an expense. The resentment of Mr Allworthy, and the injury he must do to his quiet, argued strongly against this latter; and lastly, the apparent impossibility of his success, even if he would sacrifice all these considerations to it, came to his assistance; and thus honour at last backed with despair, with gratitude to his benefactor, and with real love to his mistress, got the better of burning desire, and he resolved rather to quit Sophia, than pursue her to her ruin.", "이제 가장 큰 고민은, 소피아를 두고 어떻게 처신할 것인가였다. 그녀를 떠난다는 생각만으로도 가슴이 거의 둘로 찢기는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀를 파멸과 거지 신세로 떨어뜨리게 된다는 생각은, 가능하다면, 그를 한층 더 고통스럽게 짓눌렀다. 설령 그녀를 차지하고 싶은 격렬한 욕망이 잠시나마 그로 하여금 이 다른 길에 귀 기울이게 만들 수 있었다 해도, 그토록 큰 대가를 치르면서까지 그녀가 그의 바람을 들어줄 결심을 하리라고는 결코 확신할 수 없었다. 올워디 씨의 노여움, 그리고 그분의 평온함에 끼칠 수밖에 없는 상처가 이 후자의 길을 강하게 가로막았다. 끝으로, 설령 그가 이 모든 고려를 다 희생한다 해도 그 일이 성공할 가망이 거의 없다는 명백한 사실이 그를 거들었다. 이리하여 마침내 절망이, 그리고 은인을 향한 감사와 연인을 향한 진실한 사랑이 뒤를 받쳐 준 명예가, 타오르는 욕망을 이겨 냈다. 그는 소피아를 파멸로 몰아가며 좇느니 차라리 그녀를 단념하기로 결심했다."]], [["It is difficult for any who have not felt it, to conceive the glowing warmth which filled his breast on the first contemplation of this victory over his passion. Pride flattered him so agreeably, that his mind perhaps enjoyed perfect happiness; but this was only momentary: Sophia soon returned to his imagination, and allayed the joy of his triumph with no less bitter pangs than a good-natured general must feel, when he surveys the bleeding heaps, at the price of whose blood he hath purchased his laurels; for thousands of tender ideas lay murdered before our conqueror.", "이런 격정을 이겨 낸 이 승리를 처음 곱씹을 때 그의 가슴을 채운 그 뜨거운 온기를, 그것을 느껴 본 적 없는 사람이 떠올리기란 어려운 일이다. 자긍심이 어찌나 기분 좋게 그를 다독여 주던지, 그의 마음은 어쩌면 완전한 행복을 누렸는지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은 한순간뿐이었다. 곧 소피아가 그의 상상 속으로 되돌아와, 그 승리의 기쁨을 못지않게 쓰라린 아픔으로 가라앉혀 버렸다. 마치 마음씨 고운 장군이, 그 피를 대가로 자신의 월계관을 사들인 피투성이 시신 더미를 둘러볼 때 느낄 법한 그 아픔처럼. 우리의 정복자 앞에는 수천 가지 애틋한 생각들이 살해당한 채 누워 있었으니 말이다."]], [["Being resolved, however, to pursue the paths of this giant honour, as the gigantic poet Lee calls it, he determined to write a farewel letter to Sophia; and accordingly proceeded to a house not far off, where, being furnished with proper materials, he wrote as follows:--", "그러나 거인 같은 시인 리가 이름 붙였듯 이 '거인 같은 명예'의 길을 걷기로 결심한 그는, 소피아에게 작별의 편지를 쓰기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 멀지 않은 어느 집으로 가서, 알맞은 필기도구를 갖춘 뒤 다음과 같이 써 내려갔다. \"아가씨,"]], [["“When you reflect on the situation in which I write, I am sure your good-nature will pardon any inconsistency or absurdity which my letter contains; for everything here flows from a heart so full, that no language can express its dictates.", "제가 이 글을 쓰고 있는 처지를 헤아려 주신다면, 이 편지에 담긴 어떤 모순이나 터무니없음도 당신의 고운 마음씨가 너그러이 용서해 주시리라 믿습니다. 여기 적힌 모든 것은 가득 차오른 가슴에서 흘러나온 것이라, 어떤 말로도 그 가슴이 일러 주는 바를 다 담아낼 수 없기 때문입니다."]], [["“I have resolved, madam, to obey your commands, in flying for ever from your dear, your lovely sight.", "아가씨, 저는 당신의 명을 따르기로, 당신의 그 사랑스러운 모습으로부터 영원히 달아나기로 결심했습니다."], ["Cruel indeed those commands are; but it is a cruelty which proceeds from fortune, not from my Sophia.", "그 명은 참으로 가혹합니다. 그러나 그것은 운명에서 비롯된 가혹함일 뿐, 제 소피아에게서 나온 것이 아닙니다."], ["Fortune hath made it necessary, necessary to your preservation, to forget there ever was such a wretch as I am.", "운명은, 저 같은 가련한 인간이 이 세상에 있었다는 사실조차 잊는 것을, 당신을 지키기 위한 필연으로 만들어 버렸습니다."]], [["“Believe me, I would not hint all my sufferings to you, if I imagined they could possibly escape your ears.", "믿어 주십시오. 제 모든 고통이 어떻게든 당신의 귀에 닿으리라 생각했다면, 저는 그것을 당신께 한 마디도 비치지 않았을 것입니다."], ["I know the goodness and tenderness of your heart, and would avoid giving you any of those pains which you always feel for the miserable.", "저는 당신 마음의 선함과 다정함을 압니다. 그래서 당신이 늘 불행한 이들을 보며 느끼는 그 아픔을 조금도 드리고 싶지 않습니다."], ["O let nothing, which you shall hear of my hard fortune, cause a moment's concern; for, after the loss of you, everything is to me a trifle.", "부디 제 모진 운명에 관해 들으시는 어떤 것도, 단 한순간의 근심거리조차 되지 않게 해 주십시오. 당신을 잃은 뒤로는, 제게 그 어떤 것도 하찮은 일에 지나지 않으니까요."]], [["“O Sophia! it is hard to leave you; it is harder still to desire you to forget me; yet the sincerest love obliges me to both. Pardon my conceiving that any remembrance of me can give you disquiet; but if I am so gloriously wretched, sacrifice me every way to your relief. Think I never loved you; or think truly how little I deserve you; and learn to scorn me for a presumption which can never be too severely punished.--I am unable to say more.--May guardian angels protect you for ever!”", "오, 소피아! 당신을 떠나는 일은 견디기 어렵습니다. 당신께 저를 잊어 달라고 청하는 일은 더더욱 견디기 어렵습니다. 그러나 가장 진실한 사랑이 저로 하여금 그 두 가지를 다 하지 않을 수 없게 합니다. 저에 대한 어떤 기억이라도 당신께 불안을 안길 수 있으리라 여기는 저를 용서해 주십시오. 그러나 제가 이토록 영광스럽게 비참한 존재라면, 부디 당신의 위안을 위해 저를 모든 방식으로 희생물로 삼아 주십시오. 제가 한 번도 당신을 사랑하지 않았다고 여기십시오. 아니면, 제가 당신을 받을 자격이 얼마나 없는지를 있는 그대로 헤아리십시오. 그리고 아무리 혹독하게 벌해도 모자랄 이 주제넘음을 두고 저를 경멸하는 법을 배우십시오. 더는 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 수호천사들이 영원토록 당신을 지켜 주기를!\""]], [["He was now searching his pockets for his wax, but found none, nor indeed anything else, therein; for in truth he had, in his frantic disposition, tossed everything from him, and amongst the rest, his pocket-book, which he had received from Mr Allworthy, which he had never opened, and which now first occurred to his memory.", "이제 그는 봉랍을 찾으려 주머니를 뒤졌으나, 봉랍은커녕 그 안에 아무것도 없었다. 사실 그는 정신없이 날뛰던 와중에 모든 것을 내던져 버렸는데, 그중에는 올워디 씨에게서 받았으나 한 번도 열어 보지 않은 지갑도 있었다. 그 지갑 생각이 이제야 비로소 그의 머리에 떠올랐다."]], [["The house supplied him with a wafer for his present purpose, with which, having sealed his letter, he returned hastily towards the brook side, in order to search for the things which he had there lost.", "그 집에서 지금 쓸 봉함지 한 장을 얻은 그는, 그것으로 편지를 봉한 뒤, 거기서 잃어버린 물건들을 찾으러 서둘러 시냇가 쪽으로 되돌아갔다."], ["In his way he met his old friend Black George, who heartily condoled with him on his misfortune; for this had already reached his ears, and indeed those of all the neighbourhood.", "가는 길에 그는 옛 친구 블랙 조지를 만났는데, 조지는 그의 불운을 진심으로 위로해 주었다. 그 소식은 이미 그의 귀에, 아니 사실 온 동네 사람들의 귀에 닿아 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Jones acquainted the gamekeeper with his loss, and he as readily went back with him to the brook, where they searched every tuft of grass in the meadow, as well where Jones had not been as where he had been; but all to no purpose, for they found nothing; for, indeed, though the things were then in the meadow, they omitted to search the only place where they were deposited; to wit, in the pockets of the said George; for he had just before found them, and being luckily apprized of their value, had very carefully put them up for his own use.", "존스는 사냥터지기에게 자신이 물건을 잃어버렸다는 사정을 알렸고, 조지도 선뜻 그와 함께 시냇가로 돌아갔다. 그들은 존스가 다녀온 곳뿐 아니라 다녀오지 않은 곳까지, 풀밭의 풀포기 하나하나를 다 뒤졌다. 그러나 모두 헛수고였으니, 아무것도 찾지 못했기 때문이다. 사실 그 물건들이 그때 그 풀밭에 있기는 했으나, 그들은 그것들이 들어 있는 단 한 군데를 뒤지지 않았다. 다름 아닌 바로 그 조지의 주머니 속이었다. 그는 조금 전에 이미 그것들을 발견했고, 다행스럽게도 그 값어치를 알아챈 터라, 제 몫으로 쓰려고 아주 조심스레 챙겨 둔 것이었다."]], [["The gamekeeper having exerted as much diligence in quest of the lost goods, as if he had hoped to find them, desired Mr Jones to recollect if he had been in no other place: “For sure,” said he, “if you had lost them here so lately, the things must have been here still; for this is a very unlikely place for any one to pass by.” And indeed it was by great accident that he himself had passed through that field, in order to lay wires for hares, with which he was to supply a poulterer at Bath the next morning.", "사냥터지기는 마치 그것들을 정말로 찾아 주기를 바라는 사람처럼 잃어버린 물건을 뒤지는 데 온 부지런을 떨고 나서, 존스 씨에게 혹시 다른 곳에 들른 적은 없는지 잘 떠올려 보라고 청했다. \"분명히,\" 그가 말했다. \"이렇게 얼마 전에 여기서 잃어버리셨다면, 물건들은 아직 여기 그대로 있어야 할 겁니다요. 이런 데는 누가 지나다닐 만한 곳이 도무지 아니니까요.\" 사실 그 자신이 그 들판을 지나게 된 것도 큰 우연 덕분이었다. 다음 날 아침 배스의 어느 닭장수에게 댈 산토끼를 잡으려고 올가미를 놓으러 온 길이었던 것이다."]], [["Jones now gave over all hopes of recovering his loss, and almost all thoughts concerning it, and turning to Black George, asked him earnestly if he would do him the greatest favour in the world?", "이제 존스는 잃어버린 것을 되찾을 모든 희망을, 아니 그에 관한 거의 모든 생각을 접었다. 그러고는 블랙 조지를 돌아보며, 세상에서 가장 큰 부탁 하나를 들어주겠느냐고 간곡히 물었다."]], [["George answered with some hesitation, “Sir, you know you may command me whatever is in my power, and I heartily wish it was in my power to do you any service.” In fact, the question staggered him; for he had, by selling game, amassed a pretty good sum of money in Mr Western's service, and was afraid that Jones wanted to borrow some small matter of him; but he was presently relieved from his anxiety, by being desired to convey a letter to Sophia, which with great pleasure he promised to do. And indeed I believe there are few favours which he would not have gladly conferred on Mr Jones; for he bore as much gratitude towards him as he could, and was as honest as men who love money better than any other thing in the universe, generally are.", "조지는 다소 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"나리, 제 힘이 닿는 일이라면 무엇이든 분부만 하시면 되는 거 아시잖습니까요. 저야 진심으로 나리께 무슨 도움이라도 되었으면 좋겠습니다요.\" 사실 그 물음은 그를 흠칫하게 했다. 그는 웨스턴 씨 밑에서 사냥감을 팔아 제법 두둑한 돈을 모아 둔 터라, 존스가 자기에게 얼마간 돈을 꾸려는 것이 아닐까 겁이 났던 것이다. 그러나 소피아에게 편지 한 통을 전해 달라는 부탁임을 알자 그는 곧 그 불안에서 풀려났고, 큰 기쁨으로 그러겠노라 약속했다. 사실 나는 그가 존스 씨에게 기꺼이 베풀지 않았을 부탁이란 거의 없으리라 믿는다. 그는 자기 깜냥껏 그에게 감사하는 마음을 품고 있었고, 이 우주의 그 무엇보다 돈을 더 사랑하는 사람들이 대개 그러한 만큼은 정직했으니 말이다."]], [["Mrs Honour was agreed by both to be the proper means by which this letter should pass to Sophia.", "이 편지를 소피아에게 전할 알맞은 통로로는 어너 부인이 적임이라는 데 둘 다 뜻이 모였다."], ["They then separated; the gamekeeper returned home to Mr Western's, and Jones walked to an alehouse at half a mile's distance, to wait for his messenger's return.", "그런 다음 그들은 헤어졌다. 사냥터지기는 웨스턴 씨 댁으로 돌아갔고, 존스는 심부름꾼이 돌아오기를 기다리려고 반 마일 떨어진 어느 선술집으로 걸어갔다."]], [["George no sooner came home to his master's house than he met with Mrs Honour; to whom, having first sounded her with a few previous questions, he delivered the letter for her mistress, and received at the same time another from her, for Mr Jones; which Honour told him she had carried all that day in her bosom, and began to despair of finding any means of delivering it.", "조지는 주인집에 막 돌아오자마자 어너 부인을 만났다. 그는 먼저 몇 가지 미리 던지는 물음으로 그녀의 속을 떠본 뒤, 그녀의 아가씨에게 전할 편지를 건넸고, 동시에 그녀에게서 존스 씨에게 전할 또 다른 편지 한 통을 받았다. 어너는 그 편지를 온종일 가슴속에 품고 다녔으나 전할 길을 도무지 찾지 못해 단념하려던 참이었다고 그에게 말했다."]], [["The gamekeeper returned hastily and joyfully to Jones, who, having received Sophia's letter from him, instantly withdrew, and eagerly breaking it open, read as follows:--", "사냥터지기는 서둘러, 그리고 기쁜 마음으로 존스에게 돌아왔다. 그에게서 소피아의 편지를 받아 든 존스는 곧장 자리를 물러나, 안달하며 봉투를 뜯어 다음과 같이 읽었다. \"존스 씨,"]], [["“It is impossible to express what I have felt since I saw you.", "당신을 본 뒤로 제가 무엇을 느꼈는지는 말로 다 표현할 길이 없습니다."], ["Your submitting, on my account, to such cruel insults from my father, lays me under an obligation I shall ever own.", "저 때문에 아버지로부터 그토록 모진 모욕을 감내하신 일은, 제가 평생 두고 갚아야 할 빚으로 남겨 두었습니다. 당신은 아버지의 성미를 아시니, 부디 저를 위해서라도 아버지를 피해 주시기를 청합니다."], ["As you know his temper, I beg you will, for my sake, avoid him.", "당신께 보내 드릴 무슨 위안거리라도 있으면 좋으련만."], ["I wish I had any comfort to send you; but believe this, that nothing but the last violence shall ever give my hand or heart where you would be sorry to see them bestowed.”", "다만 이것만은 믿어 주십시오. 더없이 극단적인 강압이 아니고서는, 당신이 보고 슬퍼하실 자리에 제 손과 마음을 결코 내어주지 않으리라는 것을요.\""]], [["Jones read this letter a hundred times over, and kissed it a hundred times as often.", "존스는 이 편지를 백 번도 더 거듭 읽었고, 그만큼 백 번도 더 거기에 입을 맞췄다. 이제 그의 격정은 온갖 애틋한 욕망을 다시 그의 마음속으로 불러들였다."], ["His passion now brought all tender desires back into his mind.", "그는 앞서 우리가 본 그런 식으로 소피아에게 편지를 쓴 것을 후회했다."], ["He repented that he had writ to Sophia in the manner we have seen above; but he repented more that he had made use of the interval of his messenger's absence to write and dispatch a letter to Mr Allworthy, in which he had faithfully promised and bound himself to quit all thoughts of his love.", "그러나 그보다 더 후회스러운 것은, 심부름꾼이 자리를 비운 그 사이를 틈타 올워디 씨에게 편지를 써 보낸 일이었다. 그 편지에서 그는 자신의 사랑에 대한 모든 생각을 단념하겠노라고 진심으로 약속하고 스스로를 묶어 두었던 것이다."], ["However, when his cool reflections returned, he plainly perceived that his case was neither mended nor altered by Sophia's billet, unless to give him some little glimpse of hope, from her constancy, of some favourable accident hereafter.", "그러나 차분한 성찰이 되돌아오자, 그는 소피아의 짧은 편지로 자신의 처지가 나아지지도 달라지지도 않았음을 또렷이 깨달았다. 다만 그녀의 한결같음 덕분에, 훗날 어떤 좋은 일이라도 생기리라는 작은 희망의 빛 한 줄기를 얻었을 뿐이었다."], ["He therefore resumed his resolution, and taking leave of Black George, set forward to a town about five miles distant, whither he had desired Mr Allworthy, unless he pleased to revoke his sentence, to send his things after him.", "그리하여 그는 결심을 다시 다잡았고, 블랙 조지에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 5마일쯤 떨어진 어느 읍내를 향해 길을 떠났다. 그는 올워디 씨에게, 처분을 거두실 뜻이 없으시다면 자신의 물건을 그곳으로 부쳐 달라고 청해 둔 터였다."]], [["# Chapter xiii.", "# Chapter xiii."]], [["The behaviour of Sophia on the present occasion; which none of her sex will blame, who are capable of behaving in the same manner.", "소피아가 이번 일을 두고 보인 처신에 관하여. 같은 처지에서 똑같이 행동할 줄 아는 여인이라면 그 누구도 이를 탓하지 못할 것이다."], ["And the discussion of a knotty point in the court of conscience.", "그리고 양심의 법정에서 다루어진 한 까다로운 쟁점에 관한 논의."]], [["Sophia had passed the last twenty-four hours in no very desirable manner.", "소피아는 지난 스물네 시간을 그리 달갑지 않은 모습으로 보냈다."], ["During a large part of them she had been entertained by her aunt with lectures of prudence, recommending to her the example of the polite world, where love (so the good lady said) is at present entirely laughed at, and where women consider matrimony, as men do offices of public trust, only as the means of making their fortunes, and of advancing themselves in the world.", "그 시간의 상당 부분 동안 그녀는 고모로부터 분별에 관한 설교를 들어야 했는데, 고모는 상류 사교계의 본을 따르라 권했다. 그 점잖은 부인의 말씀에 따르면, 그 세계에서는 사랑이란 지금 완전히 비웃음거리가 되어 버렸고, 여인들은 결혼을 마치 남자들이 공직을 대하듯 여겨, 오로지 재산을 불리고 세상에서 자기 자리를 끌어올리는 수단으로만 본다는 것이었다."], ["In commenting on which text Mrs Western had displayed her eloquence during several hours.", "이 본문을 풀이하며 웨스턴 부인은 몇 시간에 걸쳐 자신의 웅변을 펼쳐 보였다."]], [["These sagacious lectures, though little suited either to the taste or inclination of Sophia, were, however, less irksome to her than her own thoughts, that formed the entertainment of the night, during which she never once closed her eyes.", "이런 약삭빠른 설교들은 소피아의 취향이나 마음에 도무지 맞지 않았지만, 그래도 밤 내내 그녀를 사로잡은 자신의 생각보다는 덜 괴로웠다. 그날 밤 그녀는 단 한 번도 눈을 붙이지 못했다."]], [["But though she could neither sleep nor rest in her bed, yet, having no avocation from it, she was found there by her father at his return from Allworthy's, which was not till past ten o'clock in the morning.", "그러나 잠도 못 자고 침대에서 쉬지도 못했으나, 달리 거기서 빠져나갈 일도 없었던 터라, 올워디 댁에서 돌아온 아버지는 침대에 누운 그녀를 발견했다. 아버지가 돌아온 것은 아침 열 시가 넘어서였다."], ["He went directly up to her apartment, opened the door, and seeing she was not up, cried, “Oh!", "그는 곧장 그녀의 방으로 올라가 문을 열었고, 그녀가 아직 일어나지 않은 것을 보고는 외쳤다. \"오!"], ["you are safe then, and I am resolved to keep you so.”", "그래 넌 무사하구나. 그럼 이대로 무사하게 지켜 주마.\""], ["He then locked the door, and delivered the key to Honour, having first given her the strictest charge, with great promises of rewards for her fidelity, and most dreadful menaces of punishment in case she should betray her trust.", "그러고는 문을 잠그고 그 열쇠를 어너에게 건넸다. 먼저 그녀에게 가장 엄한 분부를 내리며, 충실히 지키면 큰 상을 주겠다 약속하는 한편, 만일 그 소임을 저버리면 더없이 끔찍한 벌을 내리겠노라 으름장을 놓았다."]], [["Honour's orders were, not to suffer her mistress to come out of her room without the authority of the squire himself, and to admit none to her but him and her aunt; but she was herself to attend her with whatever Sophia pleased, except only pen, ink, and paper, of which she was forbidden the use.", "어너가 받은 명령은 이러했다. 영주 본인의 허락 없이는 아씨를 방에서 나오지 못하게 할 것, 그리고 영주와 고모 외에는 그 누구도 들이지 말 것. 다만 어너 자신은 소피아가 원하는 무엇이든 곁에서 시중들되, 오직 펜과 잉크와 종이만은 예외로 하여 그 사용을 금했다."]], [["The squire ordered his daughter to dress herself and attend him at dinner; which she obeyed; and having sat the usual time, was again conducted to her prison.", "영주는 딸에게 옷을 차려입고 만찬에 자신을 모시라 일렀고, 그녀는 그대로 따랐다. 그리고 늘 그렇듯 만찬 자리에 앉아 있다가, 다시 자신의 감옥으로 끌려갔다."]], [["In the evening the gaoler Honour brought her the letter which she received from the gamekeeper.", "저녁이 되자 간수 어너가 사냥터지기에게서 받은 편지를 그녀에게 가져왔다."], ["Sophia read it very attentively twice or thrice over, and then threw herself upon the bed, and burst into a flood of tears.", "소피아는 그 편지를 두세 번 아주 꼼꼼히 읽고는 침대에 몸을 던지며 눈물을 왈칵 쏟았다."], ["Mrs Honour expressed great astonishment at this behaviour in her mistress; nor could she forbear very eagerly begging to know the cause of this passion.", "어너 부인은 아씨의 이런 모습에 크게 놀라움을 드러냈고, 이 격정의 까닭을 못내 간절히 알고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["Sophia made her no answer for some time, and then, starting suddenly up, caught her maid by the hand, and cried, “O Honour!", "소피아는 한동안 아무 대답도 하지 않다가, 갑자기 벌떡 일어나 하녀의 손을 붙잡고 외쳤다. \"오 어너!"], ["I am undone.”", "난 끝장났어.\""], ["“Marry forbid,”", "\"원, 천만에요.\""], ["cries Honour: “I wish the letter had been burnt before I had brought it to your la'ship.", "어너가 외쳤다. \"그 편지를 아씨께 가져다드리기 전에 차라리 태워 버렸더라면 좋았을 것을."], ["I'm sure I thought it would have comforted your la'ship, or I would have seen it at the devil before I would have touched it.”", "정말이지 그 편지가 아씨를 위로해 드릴 줄 알았어요. 아니었다면 손도 대기 전에 차라리 마귀에게나 줘 버렸을 거예요.\""], ["“Honour,”", "\"어너.\""], ["says Sophia, “you are a good girl, and it is vain to attempt concealing longer my weakness from you; I have thrown away my heart on a man who hath forsaken me.”", "소피아가 말했다."], ["“And is Mr Jones,”", "\"넌 착한 아이야."], ["answered the maid, “such a perfidy man?”", "더 이상 너에게 내 약함을 감추려 해도 소용없구나. 나는 나를 버린 남자에게 내 마음을 던져 버렸어.\""], ["“He hath taken his leave of me,”", "\"그럼 존스 도련님이,\" 하녀가 대답했다."], ["says Sophia, “for ever in that letter.", "\"그렇게 신의 없는 분이란 말씀이세요?\""], ["Nay, he hath desired me to forget him.", "\"그이는 저 편지로 나에게 영영 작별을 고했어.\""], ["Could he have desired that if he had loved me?", "소피아가 말했다. \"아니, 나더러 자기를 잊으라고까지 했어."], ["Could he have borne such a thought?", "나를 사랑했다면 그런 걸 바랄 수 있었겠니?"], ["Could he have written such a word?”", "그런 생각을 견딜 수 있었겠어? 그런 말을 쓸 수 있었겠어?\""], ["“No, certainly, ma'am,”", "\"없고말고요, 아씨.\""], ["cries Honour; “and to be sure, if the best man in England was to desire me to forget him, I'd take him at his word.", "어너가 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 잉글랜드에서 제일 잘난 남자가 저더러 자기를 잊으라 한다면, 저는 그 말 그대로 받아들이겠어요."], ["Marry, come up!", "원, 어림없는 소리!"], ["I am sure your la'ship hath done him too much honour ever to think on him;--a young lady who may take her choice of all the young men in the country.", "정말이지 아씨께서는 그분을 한 번이라도 마음에 두신 것만으로도 너무 과한 영예를 베푸신 거예요. 이 고장 젊은 남자들 가운데 누구든 골라잡으실 수 있는 아씨가 말이에요."], ["And to be sure, if I may be so presumptuous as to offer my poor opinion, there is young Mr Blifil, who, besides that he is come of honest parents, and will be one of the greatest squires all hereabouts, he is to be sure, in my poor opinion, a more handsomer and a more politer man by half; and besides, he is a young gentleman of a sober character, and who may defy any of the neighbours to say black is his eye; he follows no dirty trollops, nor can any bastards be laid at his door.", "그리고 정말이지, 외람되이 제 변변찮은 의견을 말씀드려도 된다면, 젊은 블리필 도련님이 계시잖아요. 정직한 부모에게서 나신 데다 이 근방에서 제일가는 영주가 되실 분이고, 게다가 제 변변찮은 눈에는 절반은 더 잘생기고 절반은 더 점잖으신 분이에요."], ["Forget him, indeed!", "더구나 품행이 점잖은 젊은 신사라서, 이웃 누구도 그분에게 흠 하나 잡지 못할 거예요."], ["I thank Heaven I myself am not so much at my last prayers as to suffer any man to bid me forget him twice.", "지저분한 들병이를 쫓아다니지도 않고, 그분 문 앞에 갖다 놓을 사생아 하나 없으니까요. 잊으라니, 참!"], ["If the best he that wears a head was for to go for to offer to say such an affronting word to me, I would never give him my company afterwards, if there was another young man in the kingdom.", "저로 말하자면, 하느님 덕분에 두 번씩이나 잊으라는 소리를 들을 만큼 막바지 기도나 올리는 신세는 아니랍니다. 머리 달린 사내 가운데 제일가는 자가 저에게 그런 무례한 말을 감히 입에 올린다면, 이 나라에 다른 젊은이가 또 있는 한 다시는 그자와 어울려 주지 않을 거예요."], ["And as I was a saying, to be sure, there is young Mr Blifil.”", "그러니 아까 말씀드린 대로, 정말이지 젊은 블리필 도련님이 계시잖아요.\""], ["“Name not his detested name,”", "\"그 끔찍한 이름은 입에 올리지도 마.\""], ["cries Sophia.", "소피아가 외쳤다."], ["“Nay, ma'am,”", "\"아니, 아씨.\""], ["says Honour, “if your la'ship doth not like him, there be more jolly handsome young men that would court your la'ship, if they had but the least encouragement.", "어너가 말했다. \"아씨께서 그분이 싫으시다면, 조금만 부추겨 주셔도 아씨께 구애할 잘나고 흥겨운 젊은이들이야 얼마든지 더 있는걸요."], ["I don't believe there is arrow young gentleman in this county, or in the next to it, that if your la'ship was but to look as if you had a mind to him, would not come about to make his offers directly.”", "이 고장이든 옆 고장이든, 아씨께서 마음 있는 듯한 눈빛만 보내셔도 곧장 청혼하러 달려오지 않을 젊은 신사는 하나도 없을 거예요.\""], ["“What a wretch dost thou imagine me,”", "\"넌 나를 어떤 못된 계집으로 여기기에,\""], ["cries Sophia, “by affronting my ears with such stuff!", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"그런 소리로 내 귀를 모욕하는 거니!"], ["I detest all mankind.”", "나는 사내란 사내는 다 진저리가 나.\""], ["“Nay, to be sure, ma'am,”", "\"아니, 정말이지요, 아씨.\""], ["answered Honour, “your la'ship hath had enough to give you a surfeit of them.", "어너가 대답했다. \"아씨께서야 사내라면 물릴 만큼 겪으셨지요."], ["To be used ill by such a poor, beggarly, bastardly fellow.”--“Hold your blasphemous tongue,”", "그런 가난하고 거지 같고 사생아 같은 자에게 함부로 대접받으셨으니.\" \"그 망측한 혀를 거두지 못할까.\""], ["cries Sophia: “how dare you mention his name with disrespect before me?", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"감히 내 앞에서 그이의 이름을 함부로 입에 담다니?"], ["He use me ill?", "그이가 나를 함부로 대했다고?"], ["No, his poor bleeding heart suffered more when he writ the cruel words than mine from reading them.", "아니야, 그 잔인한 말을 쓸 때 그이의 가엾고 피 흘리는 가슴이, 그것을 읽은 내 가슴보다 더 아팠을 거야. 오!"], ["O! he is all heroic virtue and angelic goodness.", "그이는 영웅 같은 덕과 천사 같은 선함 그 자체야."], ["I am ashamed of the weakness of my own passion, for blaming what I ought to admire.", "나는 내 격정의 나약함이 부끄러워. 마땅히 우러러야 할 것을 탓했으니."], ["O, Honour!", "오, 어너!"], ["it is my good only which he consults.", "그이가 헤아리는 것은 오직 나의 행복뿐이야."], ["To my interest he sacrifices both himself and me.", "나의 이익을 위해 그이는 자기 자신과 나를 함께 희생한 거야."], ["The apprehension of ruining me hath driven him to despair.”", "나를 망칠지 모른다는 두려움이 그이를 절망으로 내몬 거라고.\" \"참 다행이에요.\""], ["“I am very glad,”", "어너가 말했다."], ["says Honour, “to hear your la'ship takes that into your consideration; for to be sure, it must be nothing less than ruin to give your mind to one that is turned out of doors, and is not worth a farthing in the world.”", "\"아씨께서 그것까지 헤아리신다니요. 정말이지, 집에서 쫓겨나 세상에 한 푼어치도 못 되는 사람에게 마음을 주는 건 신세를 망치는 일밖에 안 되니까요.\""], ["“Turned out of doors!”", "\"쫓겨났다고!\""], ["cries Sophia hastily: “how!", "소피아가 다급히 외쳤다. \"뭐라고!"], ["what dost thou mean?”", "그게 무슨 말이니?\""], ["“Why, to be sure, ma'am, my master no sooner told Squire Allworthy about Mr Jones having offered to make love to your la'ship than the squire stripped him stark naked, and turned him out of doors!”", "\"원, 정말이지요, 아씨. 우리 주인 나리께서 존스 도련님이 아씨께 사랑을 고백하려 했다는 걸 올워디 나리께 말씀드리자마자, 올워디 나리께서 그분을 발가벗겨 알몸으로 문밖에 내쫓으셨답니다!\""], ["“Ha!”", "\"아!\""], ["says Sophia, “I have been the cursed, wretched cause of his destruction!", "소피아가 말했다. \"내가 바로 그이를 파멸시킨 저주받고 가련한 원인이로구나!"], ["Turned naked out of doors!", "발가벗겨 문밖에 쫓겨나다니!"], ["Here, Honour, take all the money I have; take the rings from my fingers.", "자, 어너, 내가 가진 돈을 다 가져가. 내 손가락에서 반지도 빼 가."], ["Here, my watch: carry him all.", "자, 내 시계도. 그이에게 다 가져다줘."], ["Go find him immediately.”", "어서 가서 그이를 찾아.\""], ["“For Heaven's sake, ma'am,”", "\"제발, 아씨.\" 어너 부인이 대답했다."], ["answered Mrs Honour, “do but consider, if my master should miss any of these things, I should be made to answer for them.", "\"한번 생각해 보세요. 주인 나리께서 이 물건들 가운데 하나라도 없어진 걸 아시면, 그 책임이 저에게 돌아온답니다."], ["Therefore let me beg your la'ship not to part with your watch and jewels.", "그러니 부디 시계와 보석만은 내놓지 마시기를 빌게요."], ["Besides, the money, I think, is enough of all conscience; and as for that, my master can never know anything of the matter.”", "게다가 돈이라면, 제 양심에 거리낌 없이 말씀드려도 충분하고도 남아요. 그리고 그 돈이야 주인 나리께서 결코 아실 수 없는 일이고요.\""], ["“Here, then,”", "\"그럼 여기,\""], ["cries Sophia, “take every farthing I am worth, find him out immediately, and give it him.", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"내가 가진 마지막 한 푼까지 가져가서 당장 그이를 찾아내 건네줘."], ["Go, go, lose not a moment.”", "가, 가, 한순간도 지체하지 마.\""]], [["Mrs Honour departed according to orders, and finding Black George below-stairs, delivered him the purse, which contained sixteen guineas, being, indeed, the whole stock of Sophia; for though her father was very liberal to her, she was much too generous to be rich.", "어너 부인은 분부대로 길을 나섰고, 아래층에서 블랙 조지를 발견하고는 그에게 지갑을 건넸다. 그 안에는 열여섯 기니가 들어 있었는데, 사실 그것이 소피아가 가진 전 재산이었다. 아버지가 그녀에게 무척 후하긴 했으나, 그녀는 부자가 되기에는 너무도 베푸는 마음이 컸던 것이다."]], [["Black George having received the purse, set forward towards the alehouse; but in the way a thought occurred to him, whether he should not detain this money likewise.", "지갑을 받아 든 블랙 조지는 술집을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그런데 가는 길에 문득 이 돈도 슬쩍 가로채 버릴까 하는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["His conscience, however, immediately started at this suggestion, and began to upbraid him with ingratitude to his benefactor.", "그러나 그의 양심이 곧장 이 부추김에 펄쩍 뛰며, 은인에게 배은망덕하다고 그를 나무라기 시작했다."], ["To this his avarice answered, That his conscience should have considered the matter before, when he deprived poor Jones of his £500.", "이에 그의 탐욕이 응수하기를, 양심이라면 진작에, 다시 말해 가엾은 존스에게서 오백 파운드를 가로챘을 때 이 문제를 따졌어야 했다고 했다."], ["That having quietly acquiesced in what was of so much greater importance, it was absurd, if not downright hypocrisy, to affect any qualms at this trifle.", "그보다 훨씬 더 중한 일에는 잠자코 눈감아 놓고, 이런 하찮은 일에 새삼 거리끼는 척하는 것이야말로 어처구니없는 일이며, 노골적인 위선이나 다름없다는 것이었다."], ["In return to which, Conscience, like a good lawyer, attempted to distinguish between an absolute breach of trust, as here, where the goods were delivered, and a bare concealment of what was found, as in the former case.", "이에 맞서 양심은 마치 노련한 변호사처럼, 이번처럼 물건이 정식으로 전달된 경우의 명백한 신의 위반과, 앞서처럼 그저 주운 것을 숨기기만 한 경우 사이를 구분 지으려 했다."], ["Avarice presently treated this with ridicule, called it a distinction without a difference, and absolutely insisted that when once all pretensions of honour and virtue were given up in any one instance, that there was no precedent for resorting to them upon a second occasion.", "탐욕은 이를 곧장 비웃음으로 받아치며, 차이도 없는 구분이라 일컫고는, 일단 어느 한 경우에 명예니 덕이니 하는 핑계를 모조리 내던진 마당에, 두 번째 경우에 그것들을 다시 끌어다 쓸 전례란 없다고 딱 잘라 우겼다. 요컨대 가엾은 양심은 그 논쟁에서 영락없이 패하고 말았을 텐데, 마침 두려움이 끼어들어 양심을 도왔다."], ["In short, poor Conscience had certainly been defeated in the argument, had not Fear stept in to her assistance, and very strenuously urged that the real distinction between the two actions, did not lie in the different degrees of honour but of safety: for that the secreting the £500 was a matter of very little hazard; whereas the detaining the sixteen guineas was liable to the utmost danger of discovery.", "두려움은 두 행위의 진짜 차이가 명예의 정도가 아니라 안전의 정도에 있다고 아주 힘주어 주장했다. 오백 파운드를 숨기는 것은 위험이 거의 없는 일이었던 반면, 열여섯 기니를 가로채는 것은 발각될 위험이 더없이 크다는 것이었다."]], [["By this friendly aid of Fear, Conscience obtained a compleat victory in the mind of Black George, and, after making him a few compliments on his honesty, forced him to deliver the money to Jones.", "이렇게 두려움이 우정 어린 도움을 베푼 덕분에, 양심은 블랙 조지의 마음속에서 완벽한 승리를 거두었다. 그리고 그의 정직함에 몇 마디 치하를 건넨 뒤, 그에게 그 돈을 존스에게 건네도록 만들었다."]], [["# Chapter xiv.", "# Chapter xiv."]], [["A short chapter, containing a short dialogue between Squire Western and his sister.", "웨스턴 영주와 그의 누이 사이에 오간 짧은 대화를 담은 짧은 장."]], [["Mrs Western had been engaged abroad all that day.", "웨스턴 부인은 그날 하루 종일 바깥일로 나가 있었다."], ["The squire met her at her return home; and when she enquired after Sophia, he acquainted her that he had secured her safe enough.", "영주는 그녀가 집에 돌아왔을 때 그녀를 맞이했다. 그녀가 소피아의 안부를 묻자, 그는 그녀를 단단히 가둬 두었다고 일러 주었다."], ["“She is locked up in chamber,”", "\"방 안에 자물쇠로 걸어 잠가 놨고,\""], ["cries he, “and Honour keeps the key.”", "그가 외쳤다, \"열쇠는 어너가 가지고 있소.\""], ["As his looks were full of prodigious wisdom and sagacity when he gave his sister this information, it is probable he expected much applause from her for what he had done; but how was he disappointed when, with a most disdainful aspect, she cried, “Sure, brother, you are the weakest of all men.", "그가 누이에게 이 소식을 전할 때 그의 얼굴은 엄청난 지혜와 명민함으로 가득 차 있었으니, 아마도 그는 자기가 한 일에 대해 그녀에게서 큰 갈채를 기대했을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 더없이 경멸하는 표정으로 이렇게 외쳤을 때 그가 얼마나 실망했겠는가."], ["Why will you not confide in me for the management of my niece?", "\"정말이지, 오라버니, 오라버니는 모든 남자 중에서도 가장 무르고 어리석은 사람이에요. 어째서 조카딸을 다루는 일을 저한테 맡기지 않으시는 거예요?"], ["Why will you interpose?", "어째서 끼어드시는 거냐고요?"], ["You have now undone all that I have been spending my breath in order to bring about.", "제가 그 애 마음에 심어 주려고 그토록 숨이 차도록 애써 온 모든 것을, 오라버니가 지금 죄다 망쳐 놓으셨어요."], ["While I have been endeavouring to fill her mind with maxims of prudence, you have been provoking her to reject them.", "제가 그 애 마음을 신중함의 격언으로 채우려 애쓰는 동안, 오라버니는 그 애가 그것들을 내팽개치도록 자극하고 계셨던 거예요."], ["English women, brother, I thank heaven, are no slaves.", "오라버니, 하늘에 감사하게도, 영국 여자들은 노예가 아니에요."], ["We are not to be locked up like the Spanish and Italian wives.", "우리는 스페인이나 이탈리아 아내들처럼 가둬 둘 수 있는 존재가 아니라고요."], ["We have as good a right to liberty as yourselves.", "우리에게도 오라버니들 못지않게 자유에 대한 떳떳한 권리가 있어요."], ["We are to be convinced by reason and persuasion only, and not governed by force.", "우리는 오직 이성과 설득으로 납득시켜야 할 존재이지, 힘으로 다스릴 대상이 아니에요."], ["I have seen the world, brother, and know what arguments to make use of; and if your folly had not prevented me, should have prevailed with her to form her conduct by those rules of prudence and discretion which I formerly taught her.”", "오라버니, 저는 세상을 두루 보아 왔고, 어떤 논리를 써야 하는지 알아요. 그러니 오라버니의 어리석음이 저를 가로막지만 않았더라면, 제가 예전에 그 애에게 가르쳤던 그 신중함과 분별의 규칙에 따라 처신하도록 그 애를 설득해 냈을 거예요.\""], ["“To be sure,”", "\"그렇겠지,\""], ["said the squire, “I am always in the wrong.”", "영주가 말했다, \"어차피 잘못은 늘 내 쪽이니까.\""], ["“Brother,”", "\"오라버니,\""], ["answered the lady, “you are not in the wrong, unless when you meddle with matters beyond your knowledge.", "부인이 대답했다, \"오라버니가 잘못된 게 아니에요, 오라버니의 식견을 벗어난 일에 참견할 때만 빼고요."], ["You must agree that I have seen most of the world; and happy had it been for my niece if she had not been taken from under my care.", "제가 세상을 누구보다 많이 보아 왔다는 건 인정하셔야 해요. 그 애가 제 보살핌에서 벗어나지만 않았더라면 얼마나 좋았을까요."], ["It is by living at home with you that she hath learnt romantic notions of love and nonsense.”", "그 애가 사랑이니 뭐니 하는 그 허무맹랑하고 낭만적인 관념을 배운 건 다 오라버니랑 집에서 지냈기 때문이에요.\" \"설마,\""], ["“You don't imagine, I hope,” cries the squire, “that I have taught her any such things.”", "영주가 외쳤다, \"내가 그 애한테 그런 걸 가르쳤다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지.\""], ["“Your ignorance, brother,”", "\"오라버니의 그 무지는,\""], ["returned she, “as the great Milton says, almost subdues my patience.”[*] “D--n Milton!”", "그녀가 받아쳤다, \"위대한 밀턴이 말했듯이, 제 인내심을 거의 무너뜨리는군요.\"[*] \"밀턴이고 뭐고!\""], ["answered the squire: “if he had the impudence to say so to my face, I'd lend him a douse, thof he was never so great a man.", "영주가 대답했다. \"그놈이 뻔뻔하게 내 면전에 대고 그런 소릴 했다면, 제아무리 대단한 인물이라 해도 한 대 갈겨 줬을 거야."], ["Patience!", "인내심이라고!"], ["An you come to that, sister, I have more occasion of patience, to be used like an overgrown schoolboy, as I am by you.", "그 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 누이, 인내심이 더 필요한 건 오히려 나야. 누이한테 다 큰 학생 녀석 취급이나 당하고 있으니 말이지."], ["Do you think no one hath any understanding, unless he hath been about at court.", "궁정 같은 데나 드나들지 않으면 아무도 식견이란 게 없다고 생각하는 거야? 빌어먹을!"], ["Pox! the world is come to a fine pass indeed, if we are all fools, except a parcel of round-heads and Hanover rats.", "머리 깎은 청교도 놈들이랑 하노버 쥐새끼들 몇 마리 빼고는 우리가 죄다 바보라면, 세상 참 꼴 좋게 돌아가는 거지."], ["Pox!", "빌어먹을!"], ["I hope the times are a coming when we shall make fools of them, and every man shall enjoy his own.", "우리가 그놈들을 바보로 만들고, 사람마다 제 몫을 누리는 시절이 오기를 바라네."], ["That's all, sister; and every man shall enjoy his own.", "그게 다야, 누이. 사람마다 제 몫을 누리는 거 말이지."], ["I hope to zee it, sister, before the Hanover rats have eat up all our corn, and left us nothing but turneps to feed upon.”--“I protest, brother,”", "하노버 쥐새끼들이 우리 곡식을 죄다 갉아먹고 순무밖에 먹을 게 안 남게 되기 전에, 그날을 보고 싶구먼, 누이.\" \"이건 분명히 말하지만, 오라버니,\""], ["cries she, “you are now got beyond my understanding.", "그녀가 외쳤다, \"이젠 제 식견을 한참 넘어선 얘기를 하고 계시네요."], ["Your jargon of turneps and Hanover rats is to me perfectly unintelligible.”--“I believe,”", "순무니 하노버 쥐새끼니 하는 그 횡설수설은 저로선 도무지 알아들을 수가 없어요.\" \"암,\""], ["cries he, “you don't care to hear o'em; but the country interest may succeed one day or other for all that.”--“I wish,”", "그가 외쳤다, \"그런 얘기 듣고 싶지 않은 거겠지. 하지만 그래도 시골 토박이들의 뜻이 언젠가는 이루어질 날이 올 거야.\""], ["answered the lady, “you would think a little of your daughter's interest; for, believe me, she is in greater danger than the nation.”--“Just now,”", "\"제 바람은,\" 부인이 대답했다, \"오라버니가 따님 일을 조금이라도 생각해 주셨으면 하는 거예요."], ["said he, “you chid me for thinking on her, and would ha'", "정말이지, 그 애는 나라보다 더 큰 위험에 처해 있으니까요.\""], ["her left to you.”--“And if you will promise to interpose no more,”", "\"방금 전엔,\" 그가 말했다, \"내가 그 애 생각한다고 나무라더니, 그 애를 누이한테 맡기라며.\""], ["answered she, “I will, out of my regard to my niece, undertake the charge.”--“Well, do then,”", "\"오라버니가 더는 끼어들지 않겠다고 약속만 하신다면,\" 그녀가 대답했다, \"제가 조카딸을 아끼는 마음으로 그 일을 맡겠어요.\""], ["said the squire, “for you know I always agreed, that women are the properest to manage women.”", "\"그럼 그러구려,\" 영주가 말했다, \"여자 다루는 데는 여자가 제일 적격이라는 데에 나도 늘 동의했으니 말이오.\""]], [["[*] The reader may, perhaps, subdue his own patience, if he searches for this in Milton.]", "[*] 독자께서 이 구절을 밀턴에게서 찾으려 든다면, 아마 자신의 인내심부터 무너뜨리게 될지도 모른다."]], [["Mrs Western then departed, muttering something with an air of disdain, concerning women and management of the nation.", "그러고 나서 웨스턴 부인은 여자들과 나라를 다스리는 일에 관해 경멸 어린 기색으로 뭔가를 중얼거리며 자리를 떴다."], ["She immediately repaired to Sophia's apartment, who was now, after a day's confinement, released again from her captivity.", "그녀는 곧장 소피아의 방으로 향했다. 소피아는 하루 동안의 감금 끝에 다시 갇힌 신세에서 풀려난 참이었다."]], [["A comparison between the world and the stage.", "세상은 흔히 극장에 비유되어 왔다."], ["The world hath been often compared to the theatre; and many grave writers, as well as the poets, have considered human life as a great drama, resembling, in almost every particular, those scenical representations which Thespis is first reported to have invented, and which have been since received with so much approbation and delight in all polite countries.", "시인들뿐 아니라 점잖은 글쟁이들도 인간의 삶을 하나의 거대한 연극으로 여겨 왔으니, 거의 모든 면에서 테스피스가 처음 고안했다고 전해지는, 그리고 그 후로 교양 있는 모든 나라에서 그토록 큰 호평과 즐거움을 받아 온 저 무대 위 재현과 닮았다는 것이다."]], [["This thought hath been carried so far, and is become so general, that some words proper to the theatre, and which were at first metaphorically applied to the world, are now indiscriminately and literally spoken of both; thus stage and scene are by common use grown as familiar to us, when we speak of life in general, as when we confine ourselves to dramatic performances: and when transactions behind the curtain are mentioned, St James's is more likely to occur to our thoughts than Drury-lane.", "이 생각은 어찌나 멀리까지 나아갔는지, 또 어찌나 널리 퍼졌는지, 본래 극장에만 어울리던 몇몇 낱말, 처음에는 비유적으로 세상에 갖다 붙였던 그 말들이 이제는 양쪽 모두에 가리지 않고 글자 그대로 쓰이게 되었다. 그리하여 무대니 장면이니 하는 말은, 우리가 삶 전반을 이야기할 때나 연극 공연만을 두고 말할 때나 똑같이 익숙해졌다. 그리고 막 뒤에서 벌어지는 일을 입에 올리면, 우리 머릿속에는 드루어리 레인보다 세인트 제임스 궁이 떠오르기 십상이다."]], [["It may seem easy enough to account for all this, by reflecting that the theatrical stage is nothing more than a representation, or, as Aristotle calls it, an imitation of what really exists; and hence, perhaps, we might fairly pay a very high compliment to those who by their writings or actions have been so capable of imitating life, as to have their pictures in a manner confounded with, or mistaken for, the originals.", "이 모든 것을 설명하기란 제법 쉬워 보일지도 모른다. 연극 무대란 실제로 존재하는 것의 재현, 아리스토텔레스의 말을 빌리자면 모방에 지나지 않는다는 점을 떠올리면 되니까. 그러니 어쩌면 우리는, 글이나 행동으로 삶을 어찌나 잘 흉내 냈던지 그 그림이 원본과 뒤섞이거나 원본으로 오인될 지경에 이른 이들에게, 마땅히 아주 높은 찬사를 보낼 만도 하다."]], [["But, in reality, we are not so fond of paying compliments to these people, whom we use as children frequently do the instruments of their amusement; and have much more pleasure in hissing and buffeting them, than in admiring their excellence.", "하지만 사실 우리는 이런 사람들에게 찬사를 보내기를 그리 좋아하지 않는다. 오히려 아이들이 제 놀잇감을 다루듯 그들을 대하니, 그들의 뛰어남에 감탄하기보다는 야유하고 두들기는 데서 훨씬 큰 즐거움을 얻는다."], ["There are many other reasons which have induced us to see this analogy between the world and the stage.", "우리가 세상과 무대 사이의 이 유사함을 보게 된 데에는 그 밖에도 여러 까닭이 있다."]], [["Some have considered the larger part of mankind in the light of actors, as personating characters no more their own, and to which in fact they have no better title, than the player hath to be in earnest thought the king or emperor whom he represents.", "어떤 이들은 인류의 대다수를 배우로 여겨 왔다. 그들이 자기 것도 아니고, 실은 그것에 대한 정당한 자격이라곤 배우가 자신이 연기하는 왕이나 황제로 진지하게 여겨질 자격만큼밖에 없는 인물을 흉내 내고 있다는 것이다."], ["Thus the hypocrite may be said to be a player; and indeed the Greeks called them both by one and the same name.", "그러니 위선자도 배우라 할 만하다. 실제로 그리스인들은 그 둘을 똑같은 하나의 이름으로 불렀다."]], [["The brevity of life hath likewise given occasion to this comparison.", "삶의 덧없음 또한 이 비유가 나오게 된 빌미였다."], ["So the immortal Shakespear--", "그리하여 불멸의 셰익스피어는 이렇게 노래했다—"]], [["--Life's a poor player, That struts and frets his hour upon the stage, And then is heard no more.", "—삶이란 가련한 배우라, 무대 위에서 한때를 으스대고 안달하다가, 이내 더는 들리지 않게 되는구나."]], [["For which hackneyed quotation I will make the reader amends by a very noble one, which few, I believe, have read.", "이 닳고 닳은 인용에 대한 보상으로, 나는 독자에게 아주 고귀한 시 한 편을 들려주려 한다. 아마 읽어 본 이가 거의 없으리라 믿는다."], ["It is taken from a poem called the Deity, published about nine years ago, and long since buried in oblivion; a proof that good books, no more than good men, do always survive the bad.", "그것은 아홉 해쯤 전에 출간되었다가 오래전 망각 속에 묻힌 《신성》이라는 시에서 따온 것이다. 좋은 사람이 늘 나쁜 사람보다 오래 살아남지 않듯, 좋은 책도 늘 살아남는 것은 아님을 보여 주는 증거이다."]], [["From Thee[*] all human actions take their springs, The rise of empires and the fall of kings!", "당신*에게서 모든 인간의 행위가 그 샘을 길어 올리나니, 제국의 흥기도, 임금의 몰락도 그러하도다! 펼쳐진 저 거대한 시간의 극장을 보라, 잇따라 나타난 영웅들이 무대 위를 밟고 지나가는 것을!"], ["See the vast Theatre of Time display'd, While o'er the scene succeeding heroes tread!", "빛나는 형상들이 위풍당당하게 뒤를 이으니, 어떤 장수는 개선하고, 어떤 군주는 피를 흘리는가!"], ["With pomp the shining images succeed, What leaders triumph, and what monarchs bleed!", "당신의 섭리가 맡긴 배역을 그들은 연기하나니, 그들의 자만도, 그들의 정념도 당신의 목적을 향해 기울도다."], ["Perform the parts thy providence assign'd, Their pride, their passions, to thy ends inclin'd: Awhile they glitter in the face of day, Then at thy nod the phantoms pass away; No traces left of all the busy scene, But that remembrance says--The things have been!", "한동안 그들은 대낮의 얼굴 앞에서 반짝이다가, 당신의 고갯짓 한 번에 그 허깨비들은 스러져 가도다. 그 분주하던 무대의 자취란 하나도 남지 않고, 다만 기억만이 말하는도다—그런 것들이 있었노라고!"]], [["[*] The Deity.", "* 《신성》."]], [["In all these, however, and in every other similitude of life to the theatre, the resemblance hath been always taken from the stage only.", "그러나 이 모든 비유에서도, 그리고 삶을 극장에 빗댄 다른 어떤 비유에서도, 그 닮은 점은 언제나 무대에서만 끌어 왔을 뿐이다."], ["None, as I remember, have at all considered the audience at this great drama.", "내 기억으로는, 이 거대한 연극의 관객을 헤아려 본 이는 아무도 없었다."]], [["But as Nature often exhibits some of her best performances to a very full house, so will the behaviour of her spectators no less admit the above-mentioned comparison than that of her actors.", "하지만 자연이 흔히 만석의 객석을 앞에 두고 제 최고의 공연을 펼쳐 보이듯, 그 구경꾼들의 행동 또한 배우들의 행동 못지않게 앞서 말한 비유에 들어맞는다. 이 거대한 시간의 극장에는 벗과 비평가가 나란히 앉아 있다."], ["In this vast theatre of time are seated the friend and the critic; here are claps and shouts, hisses and groans; in short, everything which was ever seen or heard at the Theatre-Royal.", "여기에는 박수와 환호가 있고, 야유와 신음이 있다. 한마디로, 왕립극장에서 보고 들을 수 있던 온갖 것이 다 있다."]], [["Let us examine this in one example; for instance, in the behaviour of the great audience on that scene which Nature was pleased to exhibit in the twelfth chapter of the preceding book, where she introduced Black George running away with the £500 from his friend and benefactor.", "이것을 한 가지 예로 따져 보자. 이를테면, 자연이 앞 권 열두째 장에서 흔쾌히 펼쳐 보인 그 장면, 곧 검은 조지가 자기 벗이자 은인에게서 오백 파운드를 들고 달아나는 장면을 두고 그 거대한 관객이 보인 행동을 살펴보자."]], [["Those who sat in the world's upper gallery treated that incident, I am well convinced, with their usual vociferation; and every term of scurrilous reproach was most probably vented on that occasion.", "세상의 맨 위층 관람석에 앉은 이들은, 내가 확신하건대 그 사건을 언제나처럼 떠들썩하게 받아들였을 것이다. 아마도 그 자리에서 입에 담을 수 있는 온갖 상스러운 욕설이 터져 나왔을 것이다."]], [["If we had descended to the next order of spectators, we should have found an equal degree of abhorrence, though less of noise and scurrility; yet here the good women gave Black George to the devil, and many of them expected every minute that the cloven-footed gentleman would fetch his own.", "그다음 등급의 구경꾼들에게로 내려가 보면, 똑같은 정도의 혐오를 발견하되 소란과 상스러움은 덜했을 것이다. 그렇더라도 여기서는 마음씨 고운 부인네들이 검은 조지를 악마에게나 가 버리라고 빌어 댔고, 그들 가운데 많은 이가 갈라진 발굽의 신사가 당장에라도 제 것을 거두러 오려니 하고 시시각각 기다렸을 것이다."]], [["The pit, as usual, was no doubt divided; those who delight in heroic virtue and perfect character objected to the producing such instances of villany, without punishing them very severely for the sake of example.", "일층 평토석은 늘 그렇듯 의견이 갈렸을 것이 분명하다. 영웅적인 덕과 완벽한 인물을 좋아하는 이들은, 이런 악행의 본보기를 내놓으면서 본보기 삼아 아주 호되게 벌하지도 않는다고 트집을 잡았다."], ["Some of the author's friends cryed, “Look'e, gentlemen, the man is a villain, but it is nature for all that.”", "작가의 벗 가운데 몇몇은 이렇게 외쳤다. \"보십시오, 여러분, 이 사람이 악당이긴 하나, 그래도 이것이 인간의 본성이올시다.\""], ["And all the young critics of the age, the clerks, apprentices, &c., called it low, and fell a groaning.", "그리고 당대의 젊은 비평가라는 자들, 곧 점원이며 견습공 따위는 그것을 천박하다 일컫고는 일제히 신음을 토했다."]], [["As for the boxes, they behaved with their accustomed politeness.", "특별석으로 말하자면, 그들은 늘 그렇듯 점잖게 굴었다."], ["Most of them were attending to something else.", "대부분은 딴 데 정신이 팔려 있었다."], ["Some of those few who regarded the scene at all, declared he was a bad kind of man; while others refused to give their opinion, till they had heard that of the best judges.", "그나마 장면에 조금이라도 눈길을 준 몇 안 되는 이들 가운데 더러는 그가 나쁜 부류의 사람이라 단언했고, 다른 이들은 가장 식견 있는 사람들의 의견을 들어 보기 전까지는 제 견해를 내놓기를 마다했다."]], [["Now we, who are admitted behind the scenes of this great theatre of Nature (and no author ought to write anything besides dictionaries and spelling-books who hath not this privilege), can censure the action, without conceiving any absolute detestation of the person, whom perhaps Nature may not have designed to act an ill part in all her dramas; for in this instance life most exactly resembles the stage, since it is often the same person who represents the villain and the heroe; and he who engages your admiration to-day will probably attract your contempt to-morrow. As Garrick, whom I regard in tragedy to be the greatest genius the world hath ever produced, sometimes condescends to play the fool; so did Scipio the Great, and Laelius the Wise, according to Horace, many years ago; nay, Cicero reports them to have been “incredibly childish.” These, it is true, played the fool, like my friend Garrick, in jest only; but several eminent characters have, in numberless instances of their lives, played the fool egregiously in earnest; so far as to render it a matter of some doubt whether their wisdom or folly was predominant; or whether they were better intitled to the applause or censure, the admiration or contempt, the love or hatred, of mankind.", "자, 이 자연이라는 거대한 극장의 막 뒤로 들여보내진 우리는(이런 특권을 누리지 못하는 자라면 사전이나 철자 교본 말고는 아무것도 쓰지 말아야 마땅하다), 그 행위를 나무라되 그 사람 자체를 절대적으로 혐오하지는 않을 수 있다. 어쩌면 자연은 그를 자신의 모든 연극에서 악역만 맡도록 점지하지는 않았을지 모르니 말이다. 바로 이 점에서 삶은 무대와 더없이 똑같이 닮았다. 악당과 영웅을 같은 한 사람이 연기하는 일이 잦으며, 오늘 당신의 감탄을 자아낸 자가 내일이면 당신의 경멸을 끌어내기 십상이기 때문이다. 내가 비극에서만큼은 세상이 낳은 가장 위대한 천재로 꼽는 개릭이 이따금 어릿광대 노릇으로 몸을 낮추듯, 호라티우스에 따르면 위대한 스키피오와 현명한 라일리우스도 오래전에 그러했다. 아니, 키케로는 그들이 \"믿기 어려울 만큼 아이 같았다\"고 전한다. 하기야 이들은 내 벗 개릭처럼 그저 장난삼아 어릿광대 노릇을 한 것이 사실이다. 하지만 적잖은 저명한 인물들은 제 삶의 무수한 대목에서 진지하게, 그것도 어처구니없을 만큼 어릿광대짓을 했으니, 그들의 지혜가 우세한지 어리석음이 우세한지, 또 그들이 인류의 갈채와 비난, 감탄과 경멸, 사랑과 미움 가운데 어느 쪽을 받을 자격이 더 있는지가 자못 의심스러울 지경이다."]], [["Those persons, indeed, who have passed any time behind the scenes of this great theatre, and are thoroughly acquainted not only with the several disguises which are there put on, but also with the fantastic and capricious behaviour of the Passions, who are the managers and directors of this theatre (for as to Reason, the patentee, he is known to be a very idle fellow and seldom to exert himself), may most probably have learned to understand the famous nil admirari of Horace, or in the English phrase, to stare at nothing.", "실로, 이 거대한 극장의 막 뒤에서 얼마간 시간을 보낸 사람들, 그곳에서 걸치는 갖가지 변장뿐 아니라 이 극장의 지배인이자 연출가인 정념들의 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수 없는 행태에까지 훤히 정통한 이들은(특허권자인 이성으로 말하자면, 그는 지독히 게으른 자라 좀처럼 제 힘을 쓰지 않기로 소문이 났으니), 호라티우스의 그 유명한 닐 아드미라리, 우리말로 옮기자면 무엇에도 놀라 입을 벌리지 않는다는 말을 십중팔구 깨우쳤을 법하다."]], [["A single bad act no more constitutes a villain in life, than a single bad part on the stage.", "단 한 번의 못된 행위가 삶에서 악당을 만들지 못하는 것은, 무대에서 단 한 번의 못된 배역이 그러하지 못하는 것과 같다."], ["The passions, like the managers of a playhouse, often force men upon parts without consulting their judgment, and sometimes without any regard to their talents.", "정념은 극장의 지배인처럼 사람의 판단을 묻지도 않고, 때로는 재능을 헤아리지도 않은 채 곧잘 어떤 배역으로 사람을 떠민다."], ["Thus the man, as well as the player, may condemn what he himself acts; nay, it is common to see vice sit as awkwardly on some men, as the character of Iago would on the honest face of Mr William Mills.", "그러니 배우와 마찬가지로 사람도 자신이 연기하는 것을 스스로 못마땅해할 수 있다. 아니, 어떤 사람들에게 악덕이 어색하게 들러붙은 꼴이란, 마치 이아고 역이 윌리엄 밀스 씨의 정직한 얼굴에 들러붙은 것만큼이나 흔히 볼 수 있는 광경이다."]], [["Upon the whole, then, the man of candour and of true understanding is never hasty to condemn.", "그러니 통틀어 말하건대, 마음이 너그럽고 참된 식견을 갖춘 사람은 결코 섣불리 단죄하지 않는다."], ["He can censure an imperfection, or even a vice, without rage against the guilty party.", "그는 죄지은 당사자를 향해 분노를 터뜨리지 않고도 결함을, 심지어 악덕까지도 나무랄 수 있다."], ["In a word, they are the same folly, the same childishness, the same ill-breeding, and the same ill-nature, which raise all the clamours and uproars both in life and on the stage.", "한마디로, 삶에서나 무대에서나 온갖 아우성과 소란을 일으키는 것은 바로 그 똑같은 어리석음, 그 똑같은 유치함, 그 똑같은 무례함, 그 똑같은 심술이다."], ["The worst of men generally have the words rogue and villain most in their mouths, as the lowest of all wretches are the aptest to cry out low in the pit.", "가장 못된 자들이 으레 악당이니 불한당이니 하는 말을 입에 가장 많이 달고 다니는 법이니, 모든 가련한 자 가운데 가장 천한 이들이 평토석에서 천박하다고 외쳐 대기에 가장 능한 것과 같다."]], [["Containing a conversation which Mr Jones had with himself.", "존스가 자기 자신과 나눈 대화에 관하여."]], [["Jones received his effects from Mr Allworthy's early in the morning, with the following answer to his letter:--", "존스는 이른 아침 올워디 씨 댁에서 자신의 소지품을 돌려받았는데, 그가 보낸 편지에 대한 답장이 함께 딸려 있었다. “귀하,"]], [["“I am commanded by my uncle to acquaint you, that as he did not proceed to those measures he had taken with you, without the greatest deliberation, and after the fullest evidence of your unworthiness, so will it be always out of your power to cause the least alteration in his resolution.", "“숙부님의 명을 받아 알려드립니다. 숙부님께서 귀하에게 취하신 그 조치들은 더없이 깊은 숙고를 거쳐, 그리고 귀하의 부덕함이 가장 명백히 드러난 뒤에야 비로소 내리신 것이니만큼, 귀하가 그 결심에 조금이라도 변화를 일으키는 일은 영원히 불가능할 것입니다."], ["He expresses great surprize at your presumption in saying you have resigned all pretensions to a young lady, to whom it is impossible you should ever have had any, her birth and fortune having made her so infinitely your superior.", "숙부님께서는 귀하가 어느 젊은 숙녀에 대한 모든 미련을 단념했다고 말한 그 주제넘음에 크게 놀라움을 표하셨습니다. 그 숙녀의 태생과 재산이 귀하보다 한없이 위에 있으니, 귀하가 애초에 그런 미련을 품는 일 자체가 가당치 않았기 때문입니다."], ["Lastly, I am commanded to tell you, that the only instance of your compliance with my uncle's inclinations which he requires, is, your immediately quitting this country.", "끝으로, 숙부님께서 귀하에게 요구하시는 그분 뜻에 대한 단 하나의 순종은, 귀하가 즉시 이 고장을 떠나는 것임을 전하라 명하셨습니다."], ["I cannot conclude this without offering you my advice, as a Christian, that you would seriously think of amending your life.", "한 사람의 그리스도인으로서 귀하에게 충고 한마디를 드리지 않고는 이 글을 맺을 수 없으니, 부디 귀하의 삶을 고치는 일을 진지하게 생각해보시기 바랍니다."], ["That you may be assisted with grace so to do, will be always the prayer of", "귀하가 그리할 수 있도록 은총의 도움을 받기를, 늘 다음과 같은 사람이 기도하겠습니다. “귀하의 미천한 종,"]], [["“Your humble servant,", "“W. 블리필 올림.”"]], [["Many contending passions were raised in our heroe's mind by this letter; but the tender prevailed at last over the indignant and irascible, and a flood of tears came seasonably to his assistance, and possibly prevented his misfortunes from either turning his head, or bursting his heart.", "이 편지는 우리 주인공의 마음속에 서로 다투는 여러 격정을 불러일으켰다. 그러나 결국 부드러운 감정이 분노하고 노여워하는 감정을 이겨냈고, 때맞춰 한바탕 쏟아진 눈물이 그를 도와주었으며, 어쩌면 그 불행이 그의 정신을 무너뜨리거나 그의 가슴을 터뜨리는 일을 막아주었는지도 모른다."]], [["He grew, however, soon ashamed of indulging this remedy; and starting up, he cried, “Well, then, I will give Mr Allworthy the only instance he requires of my obedience.", "그러나 그는 곧 이 위안에 빠져 있는 것이 부끄러워졌다. 그래서 벌떡 일어나며 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["I will go this moment--but whither?--why, let Fortune direct; since there is no other who thinks it of any consequence what becomes of this wretched person, it shall be a matter of equal indifference to myself.", "“좋아, 그렇다면 올워디 씨가 요구하시는 그 단 하나의 순종을 보여드리겠다. 지금 이 순간 떠나겠다."], ["Shall I alone regard what no other--Ha!", "하지만 어디로? 그래, 운명이 이끄는 대로 가자."], ["have I not reason to think there is another?--one whose value is above that of the whole world!--I may, I must imagine my Sophia is not indifferent to what becomes of me.", "이 비참한 자가 어찌 되든 그것이 무슨 대수냐고 여길 사람이 달리 아무도 없으니, 나 자신에게도 똑같이 아무래도 좋은 일이 되게 하자. 다른 누구도 마음 쓰지 않는 것을 나 혼자 마음 쓸 까닭이 있을까."], ["Shall I then leave this only friend--and such a friend?", "아, 그런데 마음 써줄 사람이 또 하나 있다고 여길 만한 근거가 내게 없는 것은 아니지 않은가."], ["Shall I not stay with her?--Where--how can I stay with her?", "온 세상을 다 합친 것보다 값진 사람이! 나의 소피아만큼은 내가 어찌 되든 무심하지 않으리라고, 나는 그렇게 상상할 수 있다, 아니 상상해야만 한다."], ["Have I any hopes of ever seeing her, though she was as desirous as myself, without exposing her to the wrath of her father, and to what purpose?", "그렇다면 이 유일한 벗을, 그것도 그런 벗을 떠나도 되는가. 그녀 곁에 머물러서는 안 되는가."], ["Can I think of soliciting such a creature to consent to her own ruin?", "어디서, 어떻게 그녀 곁에 머문단 말인가. 설령 그녀가 나만큼 간절히 바란다 해도, 그녀를 아버지의 노여움에 내맡기지 않고서 그녀를 다시 볼 희망이 내게 있기나 한가."], ["Shall I indulge any passion of mine at such a price?", "게다가 그래봤자 무슨 소용인가. 그런 사람더러 제 파멸에 스스로 동의하라고 졸라댈 생각을 할 수 있겠는가."], ["Shall I lurk about this country like a thief, with such intentions?--No, I disdain, I detest the thought.", "그런 대가를 치르면서까지 내 격정 하나를 채워야 하겠는가. 그런 속셈을 품고 도둑처럼 이 고장을 어슬렁거려야 하겠는가."], ["Farewel, Sophia; farewel, most lovely, most beloved--”", "아니다, 그런 생각은 경멸스럽다, 혐오스럽다. 잘 있어요, 소피아."], ["Here passion stopped his mouth, and found a vent at his eyes.", "잘 있어요, 더없이 사랑스럽고 더없이 사랑하는 사람이여.” 여기서 격정이 그의 입을 막아버렸고, 그 격정은 그의 두 눈으로 출구를 찾았다."]], [["And now having taken a resolution to leave the country, he began to debate with himself whither he should go.", "이제 이 고장을 떠나기로 결심한 그는, 어디로 가야 할지를 스스로 따져보기 시작했다."], ["The world, as Milton phrases it, lay all before him; and Jones, no more than Adam, had any man to whom he might resort for comfort or assistance.", "밀턴의 표현을 빌리자면, 세상이 온통 그의 앞에 펼쳐져 있었다. 그리고 존스에게는, 아담이 그러했듯, 위로나 도움을 청하러 갈 만한 사람이 하나도 없었다."], ["All his acquaintance were the acquaintance of Mr Allworthy; and he had no reason to expect any countenance from them, as that gentleman had withdrawn his favour from him.", "그가 아는 사람이라고는 모두 올워디 씨가 아는 사람들이었고, 그 신사가 그에게서 호의를 거두어들인 마당에 그들에게서 어떤 두둔을 기대할 까닭도 없었다."], ["Men of great and good characters should indeed be very cautious how they discard their dependents; for the consequence to the unhappy sufferer is being discarded by all others.", "실로 위대하고 선한 인품을 지닌 사람들은 자기에게 딸린 이들을 내칠 때 매우 신중해야 마땅하다. 그 결과란 불행한 당사자가 다른 모든 사람에게서도 내쳐지는 일이기 때문이다."]], [["What course of life to pursue, or to what business to apply himself, was a second consideration: and here the prospect was all a melancholy void.", "어떤 삶의 길을 좇을지, 또 어떤 일에 자신을 바칠지가 두 번째 고민거리였다. 그리고 여기서는 그 앞날이 온통 음울한 공허였다."], ["Every profession, and every trade, required length of time, and what was worse, money; for matters are so constituted, that “nothing out of nothing”", "어떤 직업이든, 어떤 장사든 오랜 시간이 필요했고, 더 나쁜 것은 돈이 필요하다는 사실이었다."], ["is not a truer maxim in physics than in politics; and every man who is greatly destitute of money, is on that account entirely excluded from all means of acquiring it.", "세상의 이치란 이렇게 짜여 있어서, “무에서는 아무것도 나오지 않는다”는 격언은 자연의 이치에서만큼이나 세상살이의 이치에서도 참되며, 돈이 몹시 궁한 사람은 바로 그 이유로 돈을 얻을 모든 수단에서 완전히 배제되고 마는 것이다."]], [["At last the Ocean, that hospitable friend to the wretched, opened her capacious arms to receive him; and he instantly resolved to accept her kind invitation.", "마침내, 비참한 자들에게 너그러운 벗인 저 대양이 그를 받아들이려 너른 품을 활짝 벌렸다. 그리고 그는 즉시 그 다정한 초대를 받아들이기로 마음먹었다."], ["To express myself less figuratively, he determined to go to sea.", "좀 덜 비유적으로 말하자면, 그는 바다로 나가기로 결심한 것이다."]], [["This thought indeed no sooner suggested itself, than he eagerly embraced it; and having presently hired horses, he set out for Bristol to put it in execution.", "실로 이 생각은 떠오르기가 무섭게 그의 마음을 사로잡았다. 그는 곧장 말을 빌려, 그것을 실행에 옮기고자 브리스틀을 향해 길을 나섰다."]], [["But before we attend him on this expedition, we shall resort awhile to Mr Western's, and see what further happened to the charming Sophia.", "그러나 이 여정에 그를 따라나서기 전에, 우리는 잠시 웨스턴 씨 댁으로 돌아가, 사랑스러운 소피아에게 그 뒤로 어떤 일이 더 일어났는지 살펴보기로 하자."]], [["Containing several dialogues.", "여러 대화를 담은 장."]], [["The morning in which Mr Jones departed, Mrs Western summoned Sophia into her apartment; and having first acquainted her that she had obtained her liberty of her father, she proceeded to read her a long lecture on the subject of matrimony; which she treated not as a romantic scheme of happiness arising from love, as it hath been described by the poets; nor did she mention any of those purposes for which we are taught by divines to regard it as instituted by sacred authority; she considered it rather as a fund in which prudent women deposit their fortunes to the best advantage, in order to receive a larger interest for them than they could have elsewhere.", "존스 씨가 떠난 그날 아침, 웨스턴 부인은 소피아를 자기 방으로 불러들였다. 부인은 우선 소피아의 아버지로부터 그녀를 풀어 줄 자유를 얻어 냈다고 알린 다음, 결혼이라는 주제로 긴 강연을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 부인은 결혼을 시인들이 그려 온 것처럼 사랑에서 솟아나는 낭만적인 행복의 구상으로 다루지도 않았고, 신학자들이 가르치는바 신성한 권위로 제정된 것이라 여길 만한 어떤 목적도 입에 올리지 않았다. 오히려 부인은 결혼을, 분별 있는 여자들이 다른 어디서보다 더 큰 이자를 받기 위해 자신의 재산을 가장 유리하게 맡겨 두는 일종의 기금쯤으로 여겼다."]], [["When Mrs Western had finished, Sophia answered, “That she was very incapable of arguing with a lady of her aunt's superior knowledge and experience, especially on a subject which she had so very little considered, as this of matrimony.”", "웨스턴 부인이 말을 마치자 소피아가 대답했다. \"고모님처럼 뛰어난 지식과 경험을 갖추신 분과 논쟁할 만한 능력이 제게는 도무지 없습니다. 더구나 이 결혼이라는 문제처럼 제가 거의 생각해 본 적 없는 주제라면 더욱 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Argue with me, child!”", "\"나하고 논쟁이라니, 얘야!\""], ["replied the other; “I do not indeed expect it.", "부인이 받아쳤다. \"나도 그런 걸 바라는 건 정말 아니란다."], ["I should have seen the world to very little purpose truly, if I am to argue with one of your years.", "내가 네 또래의 아이와 논쟁이나 벌일 거라면, 그동안 세상을 헛산 셈이겠지."], ["I have taken this trouble, in order to instruct you.", "내가 이 수고를 들이는 건 너를 가르치기 위해서야."], ["The antient philosophers, such as Socrates, Alcibiades, and others, did not use to argue with their scholars.", "소크라테스나 알키비아데스 같은 옛 철학자들은 제자들과 논쟁하는 법이 없었어."], ["You are to consider me, child, as Socrates, not asking your opinion, but only informing you of mine.”", "얘야, 너는 나를 소크라테스라고 여기면 된다. 네 의견을 묻는 게 아니라, 다만 내 의견을 일러 줄 뿐이니까.\""], ["From which last words the reader may possibly imagine, that this lady had read no more of the philosophy of Socrates, than she had of that of Alcibiades; and indeed we cannot resolve his curiosity as to this point.", "이 마지막 말로 미루어 독자는 어쩌면, 이 부인이 알키비아데스의 철학에 대해 아는 만큼이나 소크라테스의 철학에 대해서도 읽은 바가 없으리라 짐작할지도 모른다. 그리고 솔직히 우리로서도 이 점에 관한 독자의 궁금증을 풀어 줄 길이 없다."]], [["“Madam,”", "\"고모님.\" 소피아가 외쳤다."], ["cries Sophia, “I have never presumed to controvert any opinion of yours; and this subject, as I said, I have never yet thought of, and perhaps never may.”", "\"저는 고모님의 어떤 의견에도 감히 맞서려 한 적이 없습니다. 그리고 이 주제로 말하자면, 말씀드렸듯이 아직 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없고, 어쩌면 앞으로도 영영 그러지 않을지 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Indeed, Sophy,”", "\"정말이지, 소피야.\""], ["replied the aunt, “this dissimulation with me is very foolish.", "고모가 대꾸했다. \"내 앞에서 그렇게 시치미를 떼는 건 무척 어리석은 짓이다."], ["The French shall as soon persuade me that they take foreign towns in defence only of their own country, as you can impose on me to believe you have never yet thought seriously of matrimony.", "프랑스인들이 오로지 제 나라를 지키기 위해 외국 도시들을 점령한다고 나를 설득할 수 없듯이, 네가 결혼을 진지하게 생각해 본 적이 한 번도 없다고 나를 믿게 만들 수도 없어. 네가 어떤 사람과 인연을 맺고 싶어 하는지를 내가 이미 다 알고 있는데, 어찌 너는 그런 결합을 궁리한 적 없다고 시치미를 뗄 수가 있느냐?"], ["How can you, child, affect to deny that you have considered of contracting an alliance, when you so well know I am acquainted with the party with whom you desire to contract it?--an alliance as unnatural, and contrary to your interest, as a separate league with the French would be to the interest of the Dutch!", "그 인연이라는 것이 얼마나 부자연스럽고 네 이익에 어긋나는지, 마치 네덜란드인들에게 프랑스와의 단독 동맹이 그들의 이익에 어긋나는 것과 똑같은데 말이다! 하지만 설령 네가 지금껏 이 문제를 생각해 본 적이 없다 해도, 분명히 말해 두마, 이제는 그럴 때가 무르익었다."], ["But however, if you have not hitherto considered of this matter, I promise you it is now high time, for my brother is resolved immediately to conclude the treaty with Mr Blifil; and indeed I am a sort of guarantee in the affair, and have promised your concurrence.”", "네 아버지가 블리필 씨와의 혼담을 당장 매듭짓기로 결심하셨으니까. 사실 나도 이 일에서 일종의 보증인 노릇을 하고 있고, 네가 응하리라고 약속까지 해 두었다.\""]], [["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"고모님.\""], ["cries Sophia, “this is the only instance in which I must disobey both yourself and my father.", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"이 일만큼은, 고모님과 아버지 두 분 모두의 뜻을 거역할 수밖에 없는 유일한 경우입니다."], ["For this is a match which requires very little consideration in me to refuse.”", "이 혼사는 거절하는 데 제가 거의 고민할 필요조차 없는 일이니까요.\""]], [["“If I was not as great a philosopher as Socrates himself,”", "\"내가 소크라테스 본인만큼이나 대단한 철학자가 아니었다면,\""], ["returned Mrs Western, “you would overcome my patience.", "웨스턴 부인이 응수했다. \"너는 내 인내심을 다 바닥내고도 남았을 게다."], ["What objection can you have to the young gentleman?”", "그 젊은 신사에게 무슨 트집을 잡을 수 있단 말이냐?\""]], [["“A very solid objection, in my opinion,”", "\"제 생각으로는 아주 확실한 트집이 있습니다.\""], ["says Sophia--“I hate him.”", "소피아가 말했다. \"저는 그분이 싫습니다.\""]], [["“Will you never learn a proper use of words?”", "\"너는 끝내 말을 제대로 쓰는 법을 못 배우겠느냐?\""], ["answered the aunt.", "고모가 대꾸했다."], ["“Indeed, child, you should consult Bailey's Dictionary.", "\"정말이지, 얘야, 너는 베일리 사전이라도 들춰 봐야겠다."], ["It is impossible you should hate a man from whom you have received no injury.", "네게 아무 해도 끼친 적 없는 남자를 미워한다는 건 있을 수 없는 일이야."], ["By hatred, therefore, you mean no more than dislike, which is no sufficient objection against your marrying of him.", "그러니 네가 미움이라고 하는 건 그저 싫음일 뿐이고, 그건 그와 결혼하지 않을 이유로는 충분치 않다."], ["I have known many couples, who have entirely disliked each other, lead very comfortable genteel lives.", "나는 서로를 완전히 싫어하면서도 아주 편안하고 점잖은 삶을 누리는 부부를 여럿 보아 왔다."], ["Believe me, child, I know these things better than you.", "내 말을 믿으렴, 얘야, 이런 일들은 내가 너보다 더 잘 안다."], ["You will allow me, I think, to have seen the world, in which I have not an acquaintance who would not rather be thought to dislike her husband than to like him.", "너도 내가 세상을 좀 보아 왔다는 건 인정하겠지. 그 세상에서 내가 아는 여자들 가운데, 제 남편을 좋아한다고 여겨지기보다 싫어한다고 여겨지길 바라지 않는 이는 하나도 없다."], ["The contrary is such out-of-fashion romantic nonsense, that the very imagination of it is shocking.”", "그 반대로 행동하는 건 한물간 낭만적 허튼소리라서, 상상만 해도 끔찍할 지경이야.\""]], [["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"고모님.\""], ["replied Sophia, “I shall never marry a man I dislike.", "소피아가 대답했다. \"저는 결코 싫어하는 남자와 결혼하지 않을 겁니다."], ["If I promise my father never to consent to any marriage contrary to his inclinations, I think I may hope he will never force me into that state contrary to my own.”", "제가 아버지께 그분의 뜻에 어긋나는 결혼에는 절대 동의하지 않겠다고 약속드린다면, 아버지께서도 제 뜻에 어긋나게 저를 그런 처지로 몰아넣지는 않으시리라 바랄 수 있다고 생각합니다.\""]], [["“Inclinations!”", "\"뜻이라고!\""], ["cries the aunt, with some warmth.", "고모가 다소 발끈하여 외쳤다."], ["“Inclinations!", "\"뜻이라니!"], ["I am astonished at your assurance.", "네 그 당돌함에 기가 막히는구나."], ["A young woman of your age, and unmarried, to talk of inclinations!", "네 나이의 처녀가, 그것도 미혼인 처녀가, 뜻 운운하다니!"], ["But whatever your inclinations may be, my brother is resolved; nay, since you talk of inclinations, I shall advise him to hasten the treaty.", "하지만 네 뜻이 무엇이든, 내 동생은 이미 결심했다. 아니, 네가 뜻을 들먹이니, 나는 그더러 혼담을 서두르라고 권하겠다."], ["Inclinations!”", "뜻이라니!\""]], [["Sophia then flung herself upon her knees, and tears began to trickle from her shining eyes.", "그러자 소피아는 무릎을 꿇고 엎드렸고, 빛나는 두 눈에서 눈물이 방울방울 흘러내리기 시작했다."], ["She entreated her aunt, “to have mercy upon her, and not to resent so cruelly her unwillingness to make herself miserable;”", "그녀는 고모에게 \"자기를 가엾이 여겨 주시고, 스스로 비참해지기를 꺼리는 마음을 그토록 모질게 책망하지 말아 달라\"고 애원했다."], ["often urging, “that she alone was concerned, and that her happiness only was at stake.”", "그러면서 \"이 일에 걸린 것은 오직 자기 하나뿐이며, 위태로운 것은 다른 누구도 아닌 자기 자신의 행복뿐\"이라고 거듭 호소했다."]], [["As a bailiff, when well authorized by his writ, having possessed himself of the person of some unhappy debtor, views all his tears without concern; in vain the wretched captive attempts to raise compassion; in vain the tender wife bereft of her companion, the little prattling boy, or frighted girl, are mentioned as inducements to reluctance.", "집행관이 영장으로 제대로 권한을 부여받아 어느 가엾은 채무자의 몸을 붙들고 나면, 그자의 눈물을 죄다 무심히 바라보는 것처럼. 그 비참한 포로가 동정을 자아내려 애써 본들 헛일이요, 동무를 빼앗긴 가련한 아내며, 재잘대는 어린 사내아이며, 겁에 질린 계집아이를 차마 떼어 놓지 못할 이유로 들먹여 본들 헛일이다."], ["The noble bumtrap, blind and deaf to every circumstance of distress, greatly rises above all the motives to humanity, and into the hands of the gaoler resolves to deliver his miserable prey.", "그 고귀한 포박꾼은 온갖 비탄의 정경에 눈멀고 귀먹은 채, 인간다운 마음을 자극하는 모든 것을 한참 넘어서서, 그 가엾은 먹잇감을 옥졸의 손에 넘겨주기로 굳게 마음먹는다."]], [["Not less blind to the tears, or less deaf to every entreaty of Sophia was the politic aunt, nor less determined was she to deliver over the trembling maid into the arms of the gaoler Blifil.", "수완 좋은 그 고모도 소피아의 눈물에 못지않게 눈멀어 있었고, 그녀의 어떤 애원에도 못지않게 귀먹어 있었으며, 떨고 있는 처녀를 옥졸 블리필의 품에 넘겨주려는 결심 또한 못지않게 단호했다. 부인은 격하게 대답했다."], ["She answered with great impetuosity, “So far, madam, from your being concerned alone, your concern is the least, or surely the least important.", "\"이 일에 걸린 게 너 하나뿐이라니, 천만의 말씀이다. 네 일은 가장 사소한, 아니 분명 가장 덜 중요한 것이야."], ["It is the honour of your family which is concerned in this alliance; you are only the instrument.", "이 혼사에 걸린 것은 네 가문의 명예다. 너는 그저 도구일 뿐이야."], ["Do you conceive, mistress, that in an intermarriage between kingdoms, as when a daughter of France is married into Spain, the princess herself is alone considered in the match?", "얘야, 너는 왕국과 왕국 사이의 혼인, 이를테면 프랑스의 공주가 에스파냐로 시집갈 때, 그 혼사에서 공주 자신만이 고려되리라 생각하느냐? 아니지!"], ["No! it is a match between two kingdoms, rather than between two persons.", "그건 두 사람 사이의 혼인이라기보다 두 왕국 사이의 혼인이야."], ["The same happens in great families such as ours.", "우리 같은 대갓집에서도 마찬가지다."], ["The alliance between the families is the principal matter.", "가문과 가문 사이의 결합이야말로 으뜸가는 문제다."], ["You ought to have a greater regard for the honour of your family than for your own person; and if the example of a princess cannot inspire you with these noble thoughts, you cannot surely complain at being used no worse than all princesses are used.”", "너는 네 한 몸보다 네 가문의 명예를 더 귀히 여겨야 한다. 그리고 공주의 본보기로도 너에게 이런 고귀한 생각을 불어넣을 수 없다면, 적어도 모든 공주가 겪는 것보다 조금도 더 나쁘게 대접받는 게 아니니 불평할 수는 없겠지.\""]], [["“I hope, madam,”", "\"고모님.\" 소피아가 목소리를 조금 높여 외쳤다."], ["cries Sophia, with a little elevation of voice, “I shall never do anything to dishonour my family; but as for Mr Blifil, whatever may be the consequence, I am resolved against him, and no force shall prevail in his favour.”", "\"저는 제 가문을 욕되게 할 짓은 결코 하지 않겠습니다. 하지만 블리필 씨로 말하자면, 그 결과가 어떻든 간에 저는 그분을 거절하기로 마음먹었고, 어떤 힘으로도 그분을 위해 제 뜻을 꺾을 수는 없습니다.\""]], [["Western, who had been within hearing during the greater part of the preceding dialogue, had now exhausted all his patience; he therefore entered the room in a violent passion, crying, “D--n me then if shatunt ha'un, d--n me if shatunt, that's all--that's all; d--n me if shatunt.”", "앞선 대화의 거의 대부분을 들을 수 있는 곳에 있던 웨스턴은 이제 인내심이 바닥나고 말았다. 그리하여 그는 사납게 격분한 채 방으로 들어서며 외쳤다. \"그럼 빌어먹을, 네가 그놈을 안 받겠다면 가만두지 않겠다, 빌어먹을, 가만두지 않아, 그게 다야, 그게 다라고. 빌어먹을, 안 받겠다면 가만두지 않겠다.\""]], [["Mrs Western had collected a sufficient quantity of wrath for the use of Sophia; but she now transferred it all to the squire.", "웨스턴 부인은 소피아에게 쓰려고 충분한 분량의 노여움을 그러모아 두었지만, 이제 그것을 죄다 향사에게 쏟아부었다."], ["“Brother,”", "\"동생아.\""], ["said she, “it is astonishing that you will interfere in a matter which you had totally left to my negotiation.", "부인이 말했다. \"전적으로 내 교섭에 맡겨 둔 일에 끼어들다니, 어이가 없구나."], ["Regard to my family hath made me take upon myself to be the mediating power, in order to rectify those mistakes in policy which you have committed in your daughter's education.", "가문을 생각하는 마음에 내가 중재자 노릇을 자청한 것은, 네가 딸아이 교육에서 저지른 그 처신의 실책을 바로잡기 위해서였다. 동생아, 그 애의 여린 마음에 내가 전에 뿌려 둔 씨앗을 죄다 뽑아 버린 게 바로 너, 바로 너의 그 어처구니없는 행실이란 말이다."], ["For, brother, it is you--it is your preposterous conduct which hath eradicated all the seeds that I had formerly sown in her tender mind.", "그 애에게 불복종을 가르친 것이 다름 아닌 너 자신이야.\" \"제기랄!\""], ["It is you yourself who have taught her disobedience.”--“Blood!”", "향사가 입에 거품을 물고 외쳤다. \"누님은 악마의 인내심마저 거덜 낼 사람이오!"], ["cries the squire, foaming at the mouth, “you are enough to conquer the patience of the devil!", "내가 언제 내 딸에게 불복종을 가르쳤단 말이오? 여기 그 애가 서 있으니, 정직하게 말해 보아라, 얘야, 내가 언제 너더러 나를 거역하라 시킨 적이 있느냐?"], ["Have I ever taught my daughter disobedience?--Here she stands; speak honestly, girl, did ever I bid you be disobedient to me?", "내가 네 비위를 맞추고 너를 흡족하게 해 주고 너를 내게 순종하게 만들려고 안 해 본 일이 있더냐? 누님이 그 애를 떠맡아 머릿속에 한 보따리 궁정 사상이나 가득 채워 그 애를 망쳐 놓기 전, 어린 계집애였을 적엔 나한테 아주 잘만 순종했단 말이오."], ["Have not I done everything to humour and to gratify you, and to make you obedient to me?", "아니, 아니, 누님이 그 애더러 공주처럼 처신해야 한다고 말하는 걸 내가 엿듣지 않았소? 누님이 그 계집애를 휘그당원으로 만들어 놓은 거요."], ["And very obedient to me she was when a little child, before you took her in hand and spoiled her, by filling her head with a pack of court notions.", "그러니 제 아비든 다른 누구든 그 애에게서 어떻게 순종을 바랄 수 있겠소?\" \"동생아.\""], ["Why--why--why--did I not overhear you telling her she must behave like a princess?", "웨스턴 부인이 더없이 경멸하는 기색으로 대답했다. \"너의 그 온갖 정치 따위에 내가 품은 경멸은 이루 말로 다 할 수가 없구나."], ["You have made a Whig of the girl; and how should her father, or anybody else, expect any obedience from her?”--“Brother,”", "하지만 그 애에게 내가 불복종의 원리를 조금이라도 가르친 적이 있는지, 이 아가씨 본인에게도 똑같이 물어보마. 오히려, 조카야, 내가 너에게 인간이라는 존재가 사회 속에서 맺는 여러 관계에 대한 참된 관념을 불어넣으려 애쓰지 않았더냐?"], ["answered Mrs Western, with an air of great disdain, “I cannot express the contempt I have for your politics of all kinds; but I will appeal likewise to the young lady herself, whether I have ever taught her any principles of disobedience.", "자식이 부모에게 도리를 다하는 것을 자연의 법칙이 명한다는 것을, 내가 한없는 공을 들여 보여 주지 않았더냐? 그 주제에 대해 플라톤이 뭐라고 했는지 일러 주지 않았더냐?"], ["On the contrary, niece, have I not endeavoured to inspire you with a true idea of the several relations in which a human creature stands in society?", "네가 처음 내 손에 맡겨졌을 때 그 주제에 대해 어찌나 형편없이 무지했던지, 나는 정말이지 네가 딸과 아버지 사이의 관계조차 모르는 줄 알았다.\" \"거짓말이오.\""], ["Have I not taken infinite pains to show you, that the law of nature hath enjoined a duty on children to their parents?", "웨스턴이 대답했다. \"그 애가 그렇게 바보는 아니어서, 열한 살이 되도록 제가 제 아비의 혈육이라는 것도 모르고 살았을 리는 없소.\""], ["Have I not told you what Plato says on that subject?--a subject on which you was so notoriously ignorant when you came first under my care, that I verily believe you did not know the relation between a daughter and a father.”--“'Tis a lie,”", "\"오! 고트족도 무색할 무지로구나.\""], ["answered Western.", "부인이 대답했다."], ["“The girl is no such fool, as to live to eleven years old without knowing that she was her father's relation.”--“O!", "\"그리고 동생아, 너의 그 버르장머리로 말할 것 같으면, 회초리를 맞아 마땅하다고 일러둬야겠다.\" \"그럼 누님이 할 수 있겠다 싶거든 나한테 한번 휘둘러 보시구려.\""], ["more than Gothic ignorance,”", "향사가 외쳤다. \"아니, 거기 누님 조카가 기꺼이 거들어 줄 것 같소만.\""], ["answered the lady.", "\"동생아.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“And as for your manners, brother, I must tell you, they deserve a cane.”--“Why then you may gi'", "\"내 너를 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 경멸하지만, 그래도 너의 그 무례를 더는 견디지 못하겠다. 그러니 당장 내 마차를 채비해 다오."], ["it me, if you think you are able,”", "나는 바로 오늘 아침에 이 집을 떠나기로 마음먹었으니까.\" \"그것참 잘됐구려.\""], ["cries the squire; “nay, I suppose your niece there will be ready enough to help you.”--“Brother,”", "그가 대답했다. \"이왕 그렇게 됐으니, 나도 누님의 무례를 더는 못 견디겠소."], ["said Mrs Western, “though I despise you beyond expression, yet I shall endure your insolence no longer; so I desire my coach may be got ready immediately, for I am resolved to leave your house this very morning.”--“And a good riddance too,”", "제기랄! 누님이 시도 때도 없이 나더러 경멸한다고 떠들어 대는 걸 들으면, 그것만으로도 내 딸이 제 아비의 분별을 깔보게 만들기에 충분하단 말이오.\""], ["answered he; “I can bear your insolence no longer, an you come to that.", "\"그럴 리가, 그럴 리가.\""], ["Blood!", "고모가 외쳤다."], ["it is almost enough of itself to make my daughter undervalue my sense, when she hears you telling me every minute you despise me.”--“It is impossible, it is impossible,”", "\"이런 촌뜨기를 깔볼 사람은 아무도 없다.\" \"촌돼지라니,\""], ["cries the aunt; “no one can undervalue such a boor.”--“Boar,”", "향사가 대답했다. \"나는 돼지가 아니오."], ["answered the squire, “I am no boar; no, nor ass; no, nor rat neither, madam.", "아니, 당나귀도 아니고, 쥐새끼도 아니오, 누님. 그것만은 기억해 두시오."], ["Remember that--I am no rat.", "나는 쥐새끼가 아니란 말이오."], ["I am a true Englishman, and not of your Hanover breed, that have eat up the nation.”--“Thou art one of those wise men,”", "나는 진짜 잉글랜드인이지, 나라를 거덜 낸 하노버 종자가 아니오.\" \"너야말로 그 잘난 식자들 가운데 하나지.\""], ["cries she, “whose nonsensical principles have undone the nation; by weakening the hands of our government at home, and by discouraging our friends and encouraging our enemies abroad.”--“Ho!", "부인이 외쳤다. \"그 터무니없는 신조로 나라를 망쳐 놓은 자들 말이다."], ["are you come back to your politics?”", "안으로는 우리 정부의 손발을 묶고, 밖으로는 우리 벗들의 기를 꺾고 적들의 기를 살려 주면서.\" \"허!"], ["cries the squire: “as for those I despise them as much as I do a f--t.”", "또 정치 타령으로 돌아왔구려?\" 향사가 외쳤다."], ["Which last words he accompanied and graced with the very action, which, of all others, was the most proper to it.", "\"그딴 것들로 말하자면, 나는 방귀만큼이나 우습게 여기오.\" 그러고는 이 마지막 말에다, 무엇보다도 그 말에 가장 어울리는 바로 그 행동을 곁들여 멋을 더했다."], ["And whether it was this word or the contempt exprest for her politics, which most affected Mrs Western, I will not determine; but she flew into the most violent rage, uttered phrases improper to be here related, and instantly burst out of the house.", "그리고 웨스턴 부인의 심기를 가장 거스른 것이 그 말이었는지, 아니면 자신의 정치에 대해 드러낸 그 경멸이었는지는 내가 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 부인은 더없이 격렬한 분노에 휩싸여, 여기 옮기기에 마땅찮은 말들을 내뱉고는, 곧장 집을 박차고 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Nor did her brother or her niece think proper either to stop or to follow her; for the one was so much possessed by concern, and the other by anger, that they were rendered almost motionless.", "동생도 조카도 그녀를 붙잡거나 뒤따라가는 것이 옳다고 여기지 않았다. 한 사람은 근심에, 다른 한 사람은 분노에 어찌나 사로잡혀 있던지, 거의 옴짝달싹 못 할 지경이었으니까."]], [["The squire, however, sent after his sister the same holloa which attends the departure of a hare, when she is first started before the hounds.", "그래도 향사는 누이의 뒤를 향해, 사냥개들 앞에서 산토끼가 처음 튀어나와 달아날 때 따라붙는 바로 그 함성을 내질렀다."], ["He was indeed a great master of this kind of vociferation, and had a holla proper for most occasions in life.", "그는 실로 이런 종류의 고함에 통달한 명수였고, 인생의 거의 모든 경우에 알맞은 함성을 하나씩 갖추고 있었다."]], [["Women who, like Mrs Western, know the world, and have applied themselves to philosophy and politics, would have immediately availed themselves of the present disposition of Mr Western's mind, by throwing in a few artful compliments to his understanding at the expense of his absent adversary; but poor Sophia was all simplicity. By which word we do not intend to insinuate to the reader, that she was silly, which is generally understood as a synonymous term with simple; for she was indeed a most sensible girl, and her understanding was of the first rate; but she wanted all that useful art which females convert to so many good purposes in life, and which, as it rather arises from the heart than from the head, is often the property of the silliest of women.", "웨스턴 부인처럼 세상을 알고 철학과 정치에 몰두해 온 여자들이라면, 자리에 없는 적수를 깎아내리는 대가로 웨스턴 씨의 분별을 추켜세우는 교묘한 찬사 몇 마디를 슬쩍 던져, 지금 그의 마음 상태를 곧장 이용해 먹었을 것이다. 하지만 가엾은 소피아는 온통 순박함 그 자체였다. 이 말로써 우리가 독자에게 그녀가 어리석었다고 넌지시 일러 두려는 것은 아니다. 흔히 어리석음이 순박함과 같은 뜻으로 통하긴 하지만 말이다. 사실 그녀는 더없이 사리 분별이 밝은 처녀였고, 그 명석함은 일류였으니까. 다만 그녀에게는, 여자들이 살아가며 그토록 많은 좋은 목적에 써먹는 그 쓸모 있는 술수가 도무지 없었다. 그리고 그 술수란 머리보다는 가슴에서 비롯되는 것이라, 흔히 여자들 가운데 가장 어리석은 이들의 몫인 경우가 많다."]], [["A picture of a country gentlewoman taken from the life.", "실생활에서 그려 낸 시골 귀부인의 초상."]], [["Mr Western having finished his holla, and taken a little breath, began to lament, in very pathetic terms, the unfortunate condition of men, who are, says he, “always whipt in by the humours of some d--n'd b-- or other. I think I was hard run enough by your mother for one man; but after giving her a dodge, here's another b-- follows me upon the foil; but curse my jacket if I will be run down in this manner by any o'um.”", "웨스턴 씨는 사냥꾼 특유의 함성을 마저 내지르고 잠시 숨을 고른 뒤, 자못 처량한 어조로 남자라는 존재의 가련한 처지를 한탄하기 시작했다. 그의 말인즉, 남자란 \"늘 어떤 빌어먹을 망할 계집의 변덕에 이리저리 휘둘리며 산다\"는 것이었다. \"내 생각엔 한 사내가 감당하기에 자네 어머니 하나만으로도 충분히 호되게 시달렸지. 그런데 겨우 그 여자를 한번 따돌려 놓았더니, 이번엔 또 다른 계집이 냄새를 맡고 내 뒤를 쫓아온단 말이야. 하지만 내 윗도리를 걸고 맹세하건대, 누가 이런 식으로 날 몰아세우게 두지는 않겠어.\""]], [["Sophia never had a single dispute with her father, till this unlucky affair of Blifil, on any account, except in defence of her mother, whom she had loved most tenderly, though she lost her in the eleventh year of her age.", "소피아는 이 불운한 블리필 사건이 있기 전까지, 어머니를 변호하는 일을 빼고는 아버지와 단 한 번도 다툰 적이 없었다. 소피아는 열한 살 되던 해에 어머니를 잃었지만, 그토록 애틋하게 사랑했던 어머니였다."], ["The squire, to whom that poor woman had been a faithful upper-servant all the time of their marriage, had returned that behaviour by making what the world calls a good husband.", "결혼 생활 내내 그 가엾은 여인은 향사에게 충실한 상급 하녀나 다름없었고, 향사는 세상에서 흔히 말하는 좋은 남편 노릇을 해 줌으로써 그 처신에 보답했다."], ["He very seldom swore at her (perhaps not above once a week) and never beat her; she had not the least occasion for jealousy, and was perfect mistress of her time; for she was never interrupted by her husband, who was engaged all the morning in his field exercises, and all the evening with bottle companions.", "그는 아내에게 욕설을 퍼붓는 일이 좀처럼 없었고(아마 일주일에 한 번을 넘지 않았으리라) 손찌검은 결코 하지 않았다. 아내는 질투할 만한 일이라곤 조금도 없었고, 자기 시간을 온전히 주무를 수 있었다."], ["She scarce indeed ever saw him but at meals; where she had the pleasure of carving those dishes which she had before attended at the dressing.", "남편이 그녀를 방해하는 일이 전혀 없었기 때문이다. 그는 오전 내내 들판에서 사냥에 빠져 있었고, 저녁 내내 술친구들과 어울렸다."], ["From these meals she retired about five minutes after the other servants, having only stayed to drink “the king over the water.”", "사실 아내는 식사 때 말고는 남편을 거의 본 적이 없었는데, 식탁에서 그녀가 누리는 즐거움이란 미리 손질을 거든 음식을 직접 썰어 나누는 것 정도였다."], ["Such were, it seems, Mr Western's orders; for it was a maxim with him, that women should come in with the first dish, and go out after the first glass.", "그녀는 이런 식사 자리에서 다른 하인들보다 오 분쯤 늦게 물러났는데, 그저 \"바다 건너 계신 임금님\"을 위한 축배를 들기 위해 남아 있었을 뿐이다. 듣자 하니 그것이 웨스턴 씨의 분부였다."], ["Obedience to these orders was perhaps no difficult task; for the conversation (if it may be called so) was seldom such as could entertain a lady.", "그에게는 여자란 첫 요리와 함께 들어와 첫 잔을 마신 뒤 나가야 한다는 신조가 있었기 때문이다. 이 분부를 따르는 일은 아마 그리 어렵지 않았을 것이다."], ["It consisted chiefly of hallowing, singing, relations of sporting adventures, b--d--y, and abuse of women, and of the government.", "그 자리의 대화란(그것을 대화라 부를 수 있다면) 귀부인을 즐겁게 할 만한 것이 좀처럼 못 되었으니 말이다. 그것은 주로 고함치기, 노래 부르기, 사냥 무용담 늘어놓기, 음담패설, 그리고 여자와 정부(政府)에 대한 욕설로 이루어져 있었다."]], [["These, however, were the only seasons when Mr Western saw his wife; for when he repaired to her bed, he was generally so drunk that he could not see; and in the sporting season he always rose from her before it was light. Thus was she perfect mistress of her time, and had besides a coach and four usually at her command; though unhappily, indeed, the badness of the neighbourhood, and of the roads, made this of little use; for none who had set much value on their necks would have passed through the one, or who had set any value on their hours, would have visited the other. Now to deal honestly with the reader, she did not make all the return expected to so much indulgence; for she had been married against her will by a fond father, the match having been rather advantageous on her side; for the squire's estate was upward of £3000 a year, and her fortune no more than a bare £8000. Hence perhaps she had contracted a little gloominess of temper, for she was rather a good servant than a good wife; nor had she always the gratitude to return the extraordinary degree of roaring mirth, with which the squire received her, even with a good-humoured smile. She would, moreover, sometimes interfere with matters which did not concern her, as the violent drinking of her husband, which in the gentlest terms she would take some of the few opportunities he gave her of remonstrating against. And once in her life she very earnestly entreated him to carry her for two months to London, which he peremptorily denied; nay, was angry with his wife for the request ever after, being well assured that all the husbands in London are cuckolds.", "그렇지만 웨스턴 씨가 아내를 보는 때라곤 이런 자리뿐이었다. 그가 아내의 침상에 들 무렵이면 으레 너무 취해서 앞이 보이지 않았고, 사냥철이면 동도 트기 전에 자리를 떴기 때문이다. 이리하여 그녀는 자기 시간을 온전히 누렸고, 게다가 보통은 마음대로 부릴 수 있는 사두마차까지 거느렸다. 다만 안타깝게도 동네가 험하고 길이 나빠서 이것이 별 쓸모가 없었다. 제 목숨을 조금이라도 아끼는 사람이라면 그 길을 지나려 하지 않았을 테고, 제 시간을 조금이라도 아끼는 사람이라면 그런 동네를 찾아오려 하지 않았을 테니 말이다. 자, 독자에게 솔직히 털어놓자면, 그녀는 이토록 넘치는 너그러움에 기대되는 만큼의 보답을 다 하지는 못했다. 그녀는 다정한 아버지의 뜻에 떠밀려 마음에도 없이 결혼했는데, 따지고 보면 이 혼사는 오히려 그녀 쪽에 득이 되는 것이었다. 향사의 재산은 한 해에 삼천 파운드가 넘었던 반면, 그녀의 지참금은 고작 팔천 파운드에 불과했기 때문이다. 그래서인지 그녀는 약간 우울한 기질을 품게 된 듯했다. 그녀는 좋은 아내라기보다는 차라리 좋은 하녀에 가까웠고, 향사가 떠들썩한 환희로 맞아 줄 때조차 그것에 고마운 미소 한 번으로 화답할 만한 마음의 여유가 늘 있었던 것도 아니다. 게다가 그녀는 이따금 자신과 상관없는 일에 끼어들곤 했는데, 이를테면 남편의 지나친 음주 같은 것이었다. 그녀는 남편이 드물게 내어 주는 몇 안 되는 기회를 틈타, 더없이 부드러운 말로 그것을 나무라곤 했다. 그리고 평생 단 한 번, 그녀는 두 달만 런던에 데려가 달라고 진심으로 간청한 적이 있었는데, 남편은 단호히 거절했다. 아니, 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그는 그런 청을 했다는 이유만으로 줄곧 아내에게 화를 냈다. 런던의 남편이란 죄다 오쟁이 진 자들이라고 굳게 믿었기 때문이다."]], [["For this last, and many other good reasons, Western at length heartily hated his wife; and as he never concealed this hatred before her death, so he never forgot it afterwards; but when anything in the least soured him, as a bad scenting day, or a distemper among his hounds, or any other such misfortune, he constantly vented his spleen by invectives against the deceased, saying, “If my wife was alive now, she would be glad of this.”", "이 마지막 이유에다 다른 여러 그럴듯한 이유까지 더해져, 웨스턴은 마침내 진심으로 아내를 미워하게 되었다. 그는 아내가 죽기 전에도 이 증오를 결코 숨기지 않았거니와, 죽은 뒤에도 결코 잊지 않았다. 냄새가 잘 풍기지 않는 날이라든가 사냥개들 사이에 병이 돈다든가 그 밖의 비슷한 불운처럼, 조금이라도 그의 비위를 거스르는 일이 생기면 그는 어김없이 죽은 아내를 향한 독설로 울분을 풀어냈다. \"내 마누라가 지금 살아 있었다면 이걸 보고 좋아 죽었을 거다\"라고 말이다."]], [["These invectives he was especially desirous of throwing forth before Sophia; for as he loved her more than he did any other, so he was really jealous that she had loved her mother better than him. And this jealousy Sophia seldom failed of heightening on these occasions; for he was not contented with violating her ears with the abuse of her mother, but endeavoured to force an explicit approbation of all this abuse; with which desire he never could prevail upon her by any promise or threats to comply.", "이런 독설을 그는 특히 소피아 앞에서 내뱉고 싶어 안달했다. 그는 다른 누구보다 소피아를 사랑했던 만큼, 딸이 자기보다 어미를 더 사랑했다는 사실에 정말로 질투를 느꼈기 때문이다. 그리고 이런 자리에서 소피아는 그 질투를 부추기지 않는 법이 거의 없었다. 그는 어미를 욕하는 말로 딸의 귀를 더럽히는 것만으로는 성에 차지 않아, 그 모든 욕설에 딸이 노골적으로 동조하도록 억지로 강요하려 들었다. 하지만 그 어떤 약속이나 협박으로도 딸을 그 뜻에 따르게 할 수는 없었다."]], [["Hence some of my readers will, perhaps, wonder that the squire had not hated Sophia as much as he had hated her mother; but I must inform them, that hatred is not the effect of love, even through the medium of jealousy.", "그래서 내 독자 가운데 어떤 이들은 어쩌면 의아해할지도 모르겠다. 향사가 어째서 소피아를 그 어미만큼 미워하지 않았는가 하고 말이다."], ["It is, indeed, very possible for jealous persons to kill the objects of their jealousy, but not to hate them.", "그러나 이것만은 일러두어야겠다. 증오란 질투라는 매개를 거친다 해도 사랑의 산물은 아니라는 것을."], ["Which sentiment being a pretty hard morsel, and bearing something of the air of a paradox, we shall leave the reader to chew the cud upon it to the end of the chapter.", "질투에 사로잡힌 사람이 그 질투의 대상을 죽이는 일은 얼마든지 있을 수 있으나, 그 대상을 미워하는 일은 그렇지 않다. 이 견해는 제법 씹어 삼키기 어려운 한 덩어리인 데다 어딘가 역설의 분위기를 풍기니, 독자가 이 장이 끝날 때까지 그것을 곱씹어 보도록 맡겨 두기로 하자."]], [["The generous behaviour of Sophia towards her aunt.", "소피아가 고모에게 보인 너그러운 태도."]], [["Sophia kept silence during the foregoing speech of her father, nor did she once answer otherwise than with a sigh; but as he understood none of the language, or, as he called it, lingo of the eyes, so he was not satisfied without some further approbation of his sentiments, which he now demanded of his daughter; telling her, in the usual way, “he expected she was ready to take the part of everybody against him, as she had always done that of the b-- her mother.”", "소피아는 아버지가 앞서 늘어놓은 말이 이어지는 동안 줄곧 입을 다물었고, 한숨 말고는 단 한 번도 다른 대꾸를 하지 않았다. 그러나 아버지는 눈빛이라는 언어를, 그가 부르는 말로 하자면 그 \"눈짓\"이라는 것을 전혀 알아듣지 못하는 사람이었기에, 자기 생각에 대한 좀 더 분명한 동의를 얻지 못하고는 만족하지 못했다."], ["Sophia remaining still silent, he cryed out, “What, art dumb?", "그래서 그는 이제 딸에게 그것을 요구하며, 평소 하던 식으로 일러두었다. \"너는 늘 그 빌어먹을 네 어미 편을 들었던 것처럼, 이제 나를 거슬러 누구 편이든 들 채비가 되어 있겠지.\""], ["why dost unt speak?", "소피아가 여전히 잠자코 있자, 그는 버럭 소리쳤다."], ["Was not thy mother a d--d b-- to me?", "\"뭐냐, 벙어리라도 됐냐? 왜 말을 안 해?"], ["answer me that.", "네 어미가 나한테 빌어먹을 화냥년이 아니었더냐?"], ["What, I suppose you despise your father too, and don't think him good enough to speak to?”", "그것부터 대답해 봐라. 뭐냐, 너도 이 아비를 깔보고, 말 한마디 섞기에도 모자란 인간이라 여기는 게로구나?\""]], [["“For Heaven's sake, sir,” answered Sophia, “do not give so cruel a turn to my silence. I am sure I would sooner die than be guilty of any disrespect towards you; but how can I venture to speak, when every word must either offend my dear papa, or convict me of the blackest ingratitude as well as impiety to the memory of the best of mothers; for such, I am certain, my mamma was always to me?”", "\"제발요, 아버지.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"제 침묵을 그토록 모질게 받아들이지 말아 주세요. 저는 아버지께 조금이라도 무례를 저지르느니 차라리 죽는 편이 낫다고 정말로 생각해요. 하지만 제가 어찌 감히 입을 열 수 있겠어요? 한마디 한마디가 사랑하는 아버지의 마음을 상하게 하거나, 아니면 가장 훌륭한 어머니의 추억에 대해 가장 새카만 배은망덕과 불효를 스스로 인정하는 꼴이 될 텐데요. 제게 어머니는 언제나 그런 분이셨다고 저는 확신하니까요.\""]], [["“And your aunt, I suppose, is the best of sisters too!”", "\"그리고 네 고모도 가장 훌륭한 누이라는 말이겠지!\""], ["replied the squire.", "지주가 받아쳤다."], ["“Will you be so kind as to allow that she is a b--?", "\"그래, 고맙게도 그 사람이 화냥년이라는 것 정도는 인정해 주겠느냐?"], ["I may fairly insist upon that, I think?”", "그 정도는 내가 떳떳이 우겨도 되겠지, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 아버지.\""], ["says Sophia, “I have great obligations to my aunt.", "소피아가 말했다. \"저는 고모께 큰 신세를 졌어요."], ["She hath been a second mother to me.”", "제게는 두 번째 어머니나 다름없는 분이셨지요.\""]], [["“And a second wife to me too,”", "\"나한테는 두 번째 아내이기도 했지.\""], ["returned Western; “so you will take her part too!", "웨스턴이 응수했다. \"그러니 너는 그 사람 편도 들겠다는 거로구나!"], ["You won't confess that she hath acted the part of the vilest sister in the world?”", "그 여자가 세상에서 가장 못된 누이 노릇을 했다는 것조차 인정 못 하겠다 이 말이지?\""]], [["“Upon my word, sir,” cries Sophia, “I must belie my heart wickedly if I did. I know my aunt and you differ very much in your ways of thinking; but I have heard her a thousand times express the greatest affection for you; and I am convinced, so far from her being the worst sister in the world, there are very few who love a brother better.”", "\"맹세코, 아버지.\" 소피아가 외쳤다. \"그렇게 말한다면 저는 제 마음을 사악하게 속이는 거예요. 고모와 아버지의 생각하시는 방식이 무척 다르다는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 저는 고모가 아버지를 향해 더없이 깊은 애정을 표하시는 걸 천 번도 더 들었어요. 그리고 저는 확신해요. 고모는 세상에서 가장 못된 누이이기는커녕, 오라버니를 그보다 더 사랑하는 누이는 거의 없다고요.\""]], [["“The English of all which is,”", "\"그 말을 알아듣기 쉽게 풀면,\""], ["answered the squire, “that I am in the wrong.", "지주가 대답했다. \"결국 내가 틀렸다는 소리로구나."], ["Ay, certainly.", "그래, 분명히 그렇지."], ["Ay, to be sure the woman is in the right, and the man in the wrong always.”", "암, 틀림없이 여자가 옳고, 남자는 언제나 그른 법이렷다.\""]], [["“Pardon me, sir,”", "\"용서하세요, 아버지.\""], ["cries Sophia.", "소피아가 외쳤다."], ["“I do not say so.”", "\"저는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요.\""]], [["“What don't you say?”", "\"뭘 말하지 않았다는 거냐?\""], ["answered the father: “you have the impudence to say she's in the right: doth it not follow then of course that I am in the wrong?", "아버지가 대꾸했다. \"네가 뻔뻔스럽게도 그 사람이 옳다고 했으니, 그러면 당연히 내가 그르다는 결론이 따라 나오는 게 아니냐?"], ["And perhaps I am in the wrong to suffer such a Presbyterian Hanoverian b-- to come into my house.", "하기야, 그런 장로교도에 하노버 패거리인 화냥년을 내 집에 들인 내가 그른 건지도 모르지."], ["She may 'dite me of a plot for anything I know, and give my estate to the government.”", "내가 알 게 뭐냐, 그 여자가 나를 음모죄로 고발해서 내 영지를 정부에 넘겨 버릴지도 모르는 일이야.\""]], [["“So far, sir, from injuring you or your estate,”", "\"아버지를 해치거나 영지를 가로채기는커녕요, 아버지.\""], ["says Sophia, “if my aunt had died yesterday, I am convinced she would have left you her whole fortune.”", "소피아가 말했다. \"고모가 어제 돌아가셨다면, 저는 고모가 자기 재산을 몽땅 아버지께 남기셨으리라고 확신해요.\""]], [["Whether Sophia intended it or no, I shall not presume to assert; but certain it is, these last words penetrated very deep into the ears of her father, and produced a much more sensible effect than all she had said before.", "소피아가 그것을 의도했는지 아닌지는 내가 함부로 단언하지 않겠다. 그러나 이 마지막 말이 아버지의 귀에 아주 깊숙이 파고들어, 앞서 그녀가 한 어떤 말보다 훨씬 더 뚜렷한 효과를 냈다는 것만은 분명하다."], ["He received the sound with much the same action as a man receives a bullet in his head.", "그는 머리에 총알을 맞은 사람과 거의 똑같은 동작으로 그 소리를 받아들였다."], ["He started, staggered, and turned pale.", "그는 흠칫 놀라 비틀거리며 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["After which he remained silent above a minute, and then began in the following hesitating manner: “Yesterday!", "그러고는 일 분 넘게 입을 다물고 있다가, 이윽고 다음과 같이 더듬거리며 입을 뗐다. \"어제라고!"], ["she would have left me her esteate yesterday! would she?", "그 여자가 어제 나한테 영지를 남겼을 거라고!"], ["Why yesterday, of all the days in the year?", "그랬을 거란 말이지? 하필이면 일 년 중에 왜 어제냐?"], ["I suppose if she dies to-morrow, she will leave it to somebody else, and perhaps out of the vamily.”--“My aunt, sir,”", "내 짐작에는 그 여자가 내일 죽으면, 영지를 딴 사람한테 남길 테고, 어쩌면 집안 밖으로 흘려보낼지도 모르겠구나.\" \"고모는요, 아버지.\""], ["cries Sophia, “hath very violent passions, and I can't answer what she may do under their influence.”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"성정이 무척 격하셔서, 그 격정에 휩싸이면 무슨 일을 하실지 저로서는 장담할 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“You can't!”", "\"장담할 수가 없다고!\""], ["returned the father: “and pray who hath been the occasion of putting her into those violent passions?", "아버지가 받아쳤다. \"그래, 도대체 그 여자를 그런 격정에 빠뜨린 빌미를 댄 게 누구냐?"], ["Nay, who hath actually put her into them?", "아니, 실제로 그 여자를 그 지경으로 몰아넣은 게 누구냔 말이다?"], ["Was not you and she hard at it before I came into the room?", "내가 방에 들어오기도 전에 너와 그 여자가 한창 맞붙어 있지 않았더냐?"], ["Besides, was not all our quarrel about you?", "게다가 우리 싸움이 죄다 너 때문에 벌어진 게 아니더냐?"], ["I have not quarrelled with sister this many years but upon your account; and now you would throw the whole blame upon me, as thof I should be the occasion of her leaving the esteate out o'", "내가 누이와 다툰 게 이 여러 해 동안 죄다 너 때문이었는데, 이제 와서 너는 그 모든 허물을 나한테 뒤집어씌우려 하는구나. 마치 그 여자가 영지를 집안 밖으로 빼돌리게 만든 빌미가 나라는 듯이 말이야."], ["the vamily.", "하기야 더 나은 걸 바랄 수도 없었지."], ["I could have expected no better indeed; this is like the return you make to all the rest of my fondness.”", "이게 내가 너한테 쏟은 그 온갖 애정에 대해 네가 돌려주는 보답이로구나.\""]], [["“I beseech you then,”", "\"그러면 제발 부탁드려요.\" 소피아가 외쳤다."], ["cries Sophia, “upon my knees I beseech you, if I have been the unhappy occasion of this difference, that you will endeavour to make it up with my aunt, and not suffer her to leave your house in this violent rage of anger: she is a very good-natured woman, and a few civil words will satisfy her.", "\"무릎 꿇고 비옵나니, 만일 제가 이 불화의 불행한 빌미가 되었다면, 부디 고모와 화해를 시도하시고, 고모가 이렇게 격한 노여움에 사로잡힌 채 아버지 집을 떠나시게 두지 말아 주세요. 고모는 본디 마음씨가 무척 너그러운 분이라, 정중한 말 몇 마디면 풀어지실 거예요."], ["Let me entreat you, sir.”", "부디 청을 들어주세요, 아버지.\""]], [["“So I must go and ask pardon for your fault, must I?”", "\"그래서 내가 가서 네 잘못을 대신 빌어야 한다 이 말이냐?\""], ["answered Western.", "웨스턴이 대답했다."], ["“You have lost the hare, and I must draw every way to find her again?", "\"네가 토끼를 놓쳤는데, 그걸 다시 찾겠다고 내가 사방으로 그물을 끌고 다녀야 한단 말이지? 하기야, 내가 확신만 든다면야……\""], ["Indeed, if I was certain”--Here he stopt, and Sophia throwing in more entreaties, at length prevailed upon him; so that after venting two or three bitter sarcastical expressions against his daughter, he departed as fast as he could to recover his sister, before her equipage could be gotten ready.", "여기서 그는 말을 멈췄고, 소피아가 더욱 간곡히 매달리자 마침내 그를 설득해 냈다. 그리하여 그는 딸을 향해 신랄하고 비꼬는 말을 두세 마디 내뱉은 뒤, 누이의 마차가 채비되기 전에 그녀를 붙잡으려고 할 수 있는 한 빠르게 자리를 떴다."]], [["Sophia then returned to her chamber of mourning, where she indulged herself (if the phrase may be allowed me) in all the luxury of tender grief. She read over more than once the letter which she had received from Jones; her muff too was used on this occasion; and she bathed both these, as well as herself, with her tears. In this situation the friendly Mrs Honour exerted her utmost abilities to comfort her afflicted mistress. She ran over the names of many young gentlemen: and having greatly commended their parts and persons, assured Sophia that she might take her choice of any. These methods must have certainly been used with some success in disorders of the like kind, or so skilful a practitioner as Mrs Honour would never have ventured to apply them; nay, I have heard that the college of chambermaids hold them to be as sovereign remedies as any in the female dispensary; but whether it was that Sophia's disease differed inwardly from those cases with which it agreed in external symptoms, I will not assert; but, in fact, the good waiting-woman did more harm than good, and at last so incensed her mistress (which was no easy matter) that with an angry voice she dismissed her from her presence.", "소피아는 그러고 나서 슬픔에 잠긴 자기 방으로 돌아갔고, 거기서 (이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 애틋한 비탄의 온갖 호사를 마음껏 누렸다. 그녀는 존스에게서 받은 편지를 한 번 넘게 거듭 읽었다. 토시 역시 이 자리에 동원되었다. 그녀는 그 둘을, 그리고 자기 자신까지도 눈물로 흠뻑 적셨다. 이런 처지에서 다정한 어너 부인은 비탄에 빠진 안주인을 위로하려 온갖 재주를 다 부렸다. 그녀는 여러 젊은 신사들의 이름을 줄줄이 읊었고, 그들의 재능과 외모를 한껏 추켜세운 뒤, 소피아에게 그중 누구든 마음대로 고를 수 있다고 장담했다. 이런 방법들은 분명 비슷한 종류의 병에 어느 정도 성공적으로 쓰였을 것이 틀림없다. 그렇지 않고서야 어너 부인처럼 노련한 시술자가 감히 이를 처방하려 들지는 않았을 테니까. 아니, 내가 듣기로는 하녀들의 학계에서 이 방법들을 여성용 약전에 실린 그 어떤 약 못지않은 만병통치약으로 여긴다고 한다. 다만 소피아의 병이 겉으로 드러난 증상은 그런 사례들과 일치하면서도 속으로는 달랐던 탓인지 어떤지, 나는 단언하지 않겠다. 그러나 사실은, 이 착한 시녀는 득보다 해를 더 끼쳤고, 끝내는 안주인의 화를 어찌나 돋우었던지 (이는 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었다) 안주인이 성난 목소리로 그녀를 제 앞에서 물러가게 하고 말았다."]], [["Containing great variety of matter.", "다양한 사건을 담은 장."]], [["The squire overtook his sister just as she was stepping into the coach, and partly by force, and partly by solicitations, prevailed upon her to order her horses back into their quarters.", "지주는 누이가 막 마차에 오르려는 찰나에 그녀를 따라잡았고, 반은 힘으로, 반은 간청으로 설득하여 말들을 다시 마구간으로 돌려보내라고 명하게 만들었다."], ["He succeeded in this attempt without much difficulty; for the lady was, as we have already hinted, of a most placable disposition, and greatly loved her brother, though she despised his parts, or rather his little knowledge of the world.", "이 시도는 큰 어려움 없이 성공했다. 우리가 이미 넌지시 일러두었듯, 그녀는 더없이 누그러지기 쉬운 성품이었고, 비록 그의 재능을, 아니 그보다는 세상사에 대한 얕은 식견을 업신여기기는 했어도 오라비를 몹시 아꼈기 때문이다."]], [["Poor Sophia, who had first set on foot this reconciliation, was now made the sacrifice to it.", "가엾은 소피아는 이 화해의 물꼬를 처음 튼 장본인이었으나, 이제 도리어 그 화해의 제물이 되고 말았다. 두 사람은 그녀의 행실을 한목소리로 나무랐고, 합심하여 그녀에게 선전포고를 했으며, 곧장 의논에 들어가 어떻게 하면 이 일을 가장 힘차게 밀어붙일 수 있을지를 짰다."], ["They both concurred in their censures on her conduct; jointly declared war against her, and directly proceeded to counsel, how to carry it on in the most vigorous manner.", "이를 위해 웨스턴 부인은 올워디와의 협약을 즉각 매듭지을 뿐 아니라, 그 즉시 실행에 옮기자고 제안했다. 그녀의 말은 이러했다."], ["For this purpose, Mrs Western proposed not only an immediate conclusion of the treaty with Allworthy, but as immediately to carry it into execution; saying, “That there was no other way to succeed with her niece, but by violent methods, which she was convinced Sophia had not sufficient resolution to resist.", "\"조카아이를 다룰 길은 격렬한 방법 말고는 없어요. 소피아에게는 그것을 버텨 낼 만한 결단력이 없다고 나는 확신해요."], ["By violent,”", "격렬하다는 건,\""], ["says she, “I mean rather, hasty measures; for as to confinement or absolute force, no such things must or can be attempted.", "그녀가 말했다, \"차라리 다급하다는 뜻이에요. 감금이나 노골적인 완력 같은 건 시도해서도 안 되고, 시도할 수도 없으니까요."], ["Our plan must be concerted for a surprize, and not for a storm.”", "우리 계획은 기습을 위한 것이어야지, 정면 돌격을 위한 것이어선 안 돼요.\""]], [["These matters were resolved on, when Mr Blifil came to pay a visit to his mistress.", "이런 일들이 결정되었을 무렵, 블리필 씨가 자기 정인을 찾아 방문하러 왔다."], ["The squire no sooner heard of his arrival, than he stept aside, by his sister's advice, to give his daughter orders for the proper reception of her lover: which he did with the most bitter execrations and denunciations of judgment on her refusal.", "지주는 그가 도착했다는 말을 듣자마자 누이의 조언에 따라 한쪽으로 비켜서서, 딸에게 구혼자를 제대로 맞이하라는 명을 내렸다. 그는 딸이 거절할 경우를 두고 더없이 모진 악담과 천벌의 선고를 퍼부으며 그 명을 내렸다."]], [["The impetuosity of the squire bore down all before him; and Sophia, as her aunt very wisely foresaw, was not able to resist him.", "지주의 격정은 앞을 가로막는 모든 것을 휩쓸어 버렸고, 소피아는 그녀의 고모가 매우 현명하게 내다보았던 대로 그를 당해 낼 수 없었다."], ["She agreed, therefore, to see Blifil, though she had scarce spirits or strength sufficient to utter her assent.", "그리하여 그녀는 블리필을 만나는 데 동의했지만, 그 승낙을 입 밖에 낼 기운도 힘도 거의 남아 있지 않았다."], ["Indeed, to give a peremptory denial to a father whom she so tenderly loved, was no easy task.", "사실, 그토록 애틋하게 사랑하는 아버지에게 단호한 거절을 들이대는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었다."], ["Had this circumstance been out of the case, much less resolution than what she was really mistress of, would, perhaps, have served her; but it is no unusual thing to ascribe those actions entirely to fear, which are in a great measure produced by love.", "만일 이 사정만 빠져 있었더라면, 그녀가 실제로 지닌 결단력보다 훨씬 적은 것으로도 아마 충분했을 것이다. 그러나 대체로 사랑에서 비롯된 행동을 전적으로 두려움 탓으로 돌리는 일은 그리 드물지 않다."]], [["In pursuance, therefore, of her father's peremptory command, Sophia now admitted Mr Blifil's visit.", "그리하여 아버지의 단호한 명에 따라, 소피아는 이제 블리필 씨의 방문을 허락했다. 이런 장면들은, 우리가 이미 짚었듯이, 장황하게 그려 놓으면 독자에게 거의 즐거움을 주지 못한다."], ["Scenes like this, when painted at large, afford, as we have observed, very little entertainment to the reader.", "그러므로 여기서 우리는 호라티우스의 한 가지 법칙을 엄격히 따르려 한다. 그 법칙은 작가들에게, 빛나게 그려 낼 가망이 없는 모든 소재는 건너뛰라고 일러 준다."], ["Here, therefore, we shall strictly adhere to a rule of Horace; by which writers are directed to pass over all those matters which they despair of placing in a shining light;--a rule, we conceive, of excellent use as well to the historian as to the poet; and which, if followed, must at least have this good effect, that many a great evil (for so all great books are called) would thus be reduced to a small one.", "우리가 생각건대 이는 시인에게뿐 아니라 역사가에게도 더없이 쓸모 있는 법칙이며, 이를 따른다면 적어도 이런 좋은 효과는 있을 것이다. 많은 거대한 악(큰 책을 두고들 그렇게 부르니 말이다)이 이로써 작은 악으로 줄어들리라는 것이다."]], [["It is possible the great art used by Blifil at this interview would have prevailed on Sophia to have made another man in his circumstances her confident, and to have revealed the whole secret of her heart to him; but she had contracted so ill an opinion of this young gentleman, that she was resolved to place no confidence in him; for simplicity, when set on its guard, is often a match for cunning.", "이 면담에서 블리필이 구사한 대단한 기술이 만일 같은 처지의 다른 남자였다면 소피아로 하여금 그를 속내를 털어놓을 상대로 삼고 마음속 비밀을 모두 드러내게 했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀는 이 젊은 신사에 대해 이미 너무도 나쁜 인상을 품고 있던 터라, 그에게 어떤 신뢰도 두지 않기로 마음먹은 뒤였다."], ["Her behaviour to him, therefore, was entirely forced, and indeed such as is generally prescribed to virgins upon the second formal visit from one who is appointed for their husband.", "순박함이란 일단 경계 태세를 갖추고 나면 흔히 교활함과도 맞설 만하기 때문이다. 그래서 그를 대하는 그녀의 태도는 온통 억지스러운 것이었고, 실로 장차 남편이 될 사람의 두 번째 공식 방문을 받을 때 처녀들에게 으레 권해지는 그런 식이었다."]], [["But though Blifil declared himself to the squire perfectly satisfied with his reception; yet that gentleman, who, in company with his sister, had overheard all, was not so well pleased.", "그러나 블리필이 지주에게 자신이 받은 응대에 더없이 만족한다고 떠벌렸음에도, 누이와 함께 그 모든 것을 엿들었던 그 신사는 그다지 흡족하지 않았다. 그는 그 현명한 부인의 조언에 따라 일을 되도록 빨리 밀어붙이기로 작정하고, 사위가 될 사람을 향해 사냥꾼들이 쓰는 말투로 큰 소리로 \"워어!\""], ["He resolved, in pursuance of the advice of the sage lady, to push matters as forward as possible; and addressing himself to his intended son-in-law in the hunting phrase, he cried, after a loud holla, “Follow her, boy, follow her; run in, run in; that's it, honeys.", "외친 뒤 이렇게 외쳤다. \"쫓아라, 이 사람아, 쫓아가!"], ["Dead, dead, dead.", "달려들어, 달려들라고."], ["Never be bashful, nor stand shall I, shall I?", "그렇지, 옳거니. 잡았다, 잡았어, 잡았다고."], ["Allworthy and I can finish all matters between us this afternoon, and let us ha'", "절대 수줍어하지 말고, '할까 말까' 하며 머뭇대지도 말게."], ["the wedding to-morrow.”", "올워디와 나는 오늘 오후에 우리끼리 모든 일을 매듭지을 수 있으니, 혼례는 내일 치르세.\""]], [["Blifil having conveyed the utmost satisfaction into his countenance, answered, “As there is nothing, sir, in this world which I so eagerly desire as an alliance with your family, except my union with the most amiable and deserving Sophia, you may easily imagine how impatient I must be to see myself in possession of my two highest wishes.", "블리필은 더할 나위 없는 만족을 얼굴 가득 띄우며 대답했다. \"어르신, 이 세상에서 제가 그토록 간절히 바라는 것은 어르신 가문과 맺어지는 일뿐이고, 그에 견줄 만한 건 오직 더없이 사랑스럽고 가치 있는 소피아 양과 한 몸이 되는 일뿐입니다."], ["If I have not therefore importuned you on this head, you will impute it only to my fear of offending the lady, by endeavouring to hurry on so blessed an event faster than a strict compliance with all the rules of decency and decorum will permit.", "그러니 저의 두 가지 가장 큰 소원을 제 손안에 거머쥐는 그 순간을 얼마나 애타게 기다리고 있을지 쉬이 짐작하실 겁니다. 제가 이 일을 두고 어르신을 졸라 대지 않은 까닭이 있다면, 그것은 오직 이 더없이 복된 일을 예법과 격식의 온갖 규칙이 허락하는 것보다 더 빨리 서둘러 몰아붙이다가 소피아 양의 마음을 상하게 할까 두려웠던 탓이라 여겨 주십시오."], ["But if, by your interest, sir, she might be induced to dispense with any formalities--”", "하지만 어르신의 힘으로 그녀가 어떤 격식 따위는 면제하도록 이끌어질 수만 있다면—\""]], [["“Formalities! with a pox!”", "\"격식이라니, 빌어먹을!\""], ["answered the squire.", "지주가 대꾸했다."], ["“Pooh, all stuff and nonsense!", "\"쳇, 다 부질없는 헛소리야!"], ["I tell thee, she shall ha'", "내 자네에게 이르노니, 그애는 내일 자네를 맞을 걸세."], ["thee to-morrow: you will know the world better hereafter, when you come to my age.", "자네도 내 나이쯤 되면 세상을 더 잘 알게 될 게야. 이 사람아, 여자란 어떻게든 피할 수만 있으면 결코 승낙 같은 건 하지 않는 법이야."], ["Women never gi'", "그게 세상 이치라네."], ["their consent, man, if they can help it, 'tis not the fashion.", "만일 내가 그애 어미의 승낙을 기다렸더라면, 나는 오늘까지도 홀아비 신세였을 게야."], ["If I had stayed for her mother's consent, I might have been a batchelor to this day.--To her, to her, co to her, that's it, you jolly dog.", "어서 그애한테 가게, 가, 가라니까, 그렇지, 이 유쾌한 친구야."], ["I tell thee shat ha' her to-morrow morning.”", "내 자네에게 이르노니, 내일 아침에 그애를 맞게 될 걸세.\""]], [["Blifil suffered himself to be overpowered by the forcible rhetoric of the squire; and it being agreed that Western should close with Allworthy that very afternoon, the lover departed home, having first earnestly begged that no violence might be offered to the lady by this haste, in the same manner as a popish inquisitor begs the lay power to do no violence to the heretic delivered over to it, and against whom the church hath passed sentence.", "블리필은 지주의 거센 언변에 못 이기는 척 자신을 내맡겼다. 그리하여 웨스턴이 바로 그날 오후에 올워디와 합의를 매듭짓기로 정해지자, 이 구혼자는 집으로 떠났다. 다만 떠나기에 앞서, 이렇게 서두르다가 소피아 양에게 어떤 폭력도 가해지지 않기를 간곡히 빌었다. 그것은 마치 교황파의 종교재판관이, 교회가 이미 판결을 내린 이단자를 세속 권력에 넘기면서 그에게 어떤 폭력도 가하지 말라고 비는 것과 똑같은 모양새였다."]], [["And, to say the truth, Blifil had passed sentence against Sophia; for, however pleased he had declared himself to Western with his reception, he was by no means satisfied, unless it was that he was convinced of the hatred and scorn of his mistress: and this had produced no less reciprocal hatred and scorn in him.", "사실대로 말하자면, 블리필은 소피아에게 이미 판결을 내린 셈이었다. 그가 웨스턴에게 자신이 받은 응대에 만족한다고 아무리 떠벌렸어도, 그가 흡족했던 단 한 가지는 자기 정인이 자신을 미워하고 경멸한다는 사실을 확신했다는 것뿐이었다."], ["It may, perhaps, be asked, Why then did he not put an immediate end to all further courtship?", "그리고 이것은 그의 안에서도 똑같은 미움과 경멸을 자아냈다. 그렇다면 어째서 그는 더 이상의 구애를 곧장 끝내지 않았느냐고 물을지도 모르겠다."], ["I answer, for that very reason, as well as for several others equally good, which we shall now proceed to open to the reader.", "나는 답하노니, 바로 그 이유 때문이며, 그에 못지않게 그럴듯한 여러 다른 이유 때문이기도 하다. 이제 그것을 독자에게 펼쳐 보이려 한다."]], [["Though Mr Blifil was not of the complexion of Jones, nor ready to eat every woman he saw; yet he was far from being destitute of that appetite which is said to be the common property of all animals.", "블리필 씨는 존스 같은 기질을 타고나지도 않았고, 눈에 띄는 여자마다 집어삼킬 준비가 되어 있는 위인도 아니었다. 그러나 모든 동물의 공통된 속성이라 일컬어지는 그 식욕만큼은 그에게도 결코 모자라지 않았다."], ["With this, he had likewise that distinguishing taste, which serves to direct men in their choice of the object or food of their several appetites; and this taught him to consider Sophia as a most delicious morsel, indeed to regard her with the same desires which an ortolan inspires into the soul of an epicure.", "게다가 그는 사람들이 저마다의 식욕을 채울 대상이나 먹잇감을 고르도록 이끌어 주는 그 분별의 취향 또한 지니고 있었다. 그리고 이 취향이 그로 하여금 소피아를 더없이 입맛 도는 한 점 진미로 여기게 했으니, 실로 그는 식도락가의 영혼을 사로잡는 멧새 한 마리를 바라보는 것과 똑같은 욕망으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Now the agonies which affected the mind of Sophia, rather augmented than impaired her beauty; for her tears added brightness to her eyes, and her breasts rose higher with her sighs.", "그런데 소피아의 마음을 괴롭히던 고통은 도리어 그녀의 아름다움을 깎아내리기는커녕 더 돋우어 놓았다. 눈물은 그녀의 눈에 광채를 더했고, 한숨에 그녀의 가슴은 더 높이 부풀어 올랐다."], ["Indeed, no one hath seen beauty in its highest lustre who hath never seen it in distress.", "실로 아름다움이 고뇌에 잠긴 모습을 한 번도 본 적 없는 사람은, 그 아름다움이 가장 눈부신 광채를 발하는 모습을 본 적이 없는 셈이다."], ["Blifil therefore looked on this human ortolan with greater desire than when he viewed her last; nor was his desire at all lessened by the aversion which he discovered in her to himself.", "그리하여 블리필은 이 인간 멧새를 지난번 보았을 때보다 한층 더한 욕망으로 바라보았다. 그가 그녀에게서 발견한 자신에 대한 혐오도 그 욕망을 조금도 줄이지 못했다."], ["On the contrary, this served rather to heighten the pleasure he proposed in rifling her charms, as it added triumph to lust; nay, he had some further views, from obtaining the absolute possession of her person, which we detest too much even to mention; and revenge itself was not without its share in the gratifications which he promised himself.", "오히려 그것은 정욕에 승리감마저 보태었으니, 그녀의 매력을 노략질하는 데서 그가 기대한 쾌락을 한층 더 부추기는 구실을 했을 뿐이다. 아니, 그는 그녀의 몸을 완전히 손에 넣음으로써 얻고자 한 또 다른 속셈까지 품고 있었으나, 그것은 입에 올리기조차 너무도 역겨운 것이다."], ["The rivalling poor Jones, and supplanting him in her affections, added another spur to his pursuit, and promised another additional rapture to his enjoyment.", "그리고 그가 스스로에게 약속한 그 만족들 가운데에는 복수심 또한 한몫을 차지하고 있었다. 가엾은 존스의 연적이 되어 그녀의 애정에서 그를 밀어내는 일은 그의 추격에 또 하나의 박차를 더했고, 그의 향락에 또 하나의 황홀을 약속해 주었다."]], [["Besides all these views, which to some scrupulous persons may seem to savour too much of malevolence, he had one prospect, which few readers will regard with any great abhorrence.", "엄격한 이들에게는 지나치게 악의의 냄새를 풍기는 듯 보일 이 모든 속셈 말고도, 그에게는 또 한 가지 전망이 있었는데, 이것만큼은 어떤 독자도 그리 크게 혐오하지는 않을 것이다."], ["And this was the estate of Mr Western; which was all to be settled on his daughter and her issue; for so extravagant was the affection of that fond parent, that, provided his child would but consent to be miserable with the husband he chose, he cared not at what price he purchased him.", "다름 아닌 웨스턴 씨의 재산이었다. 그 재산은 모두 그의 딸과 그 자손에게 물려질 참이었으니, 그 자애로운 아비의 애정이 어찌나 분에 넘쳤던지, 제 자식이 자기가 고른 남편과 함께 불행해지는 데 승낙만 해 준다면, 그 남편을 어떤 값에 사들이든 그는 개의치 않았던 것이다."]], [["For these reasons Mr Blifil was so desirous of the match that he intended to deceive Sophia, by pretending love to her; and to deceive her father and his own uncle, by pretending he was beloved by her.", "이런 이유들로 블리필 씨는 이 혼사를 어찌나 바랐던지, 소피아에게는 사랑하는 척하여 그녀를 속이고, 그녀의 아버지와 자신의 숙부에게는 그녀에게 사랑받는 척하여 그들을 속이려 들었다."], ["In doing this he availed himself of the piety of Thwackum, who held, that if the end proposed was religious (as surely matrimony is), it mattered not how wicked were the means.", "이렇게 하면서 그는 스왜컴의 신앙심을 끌어다 썼는데, 그 신앙심인즉 내세운 목적이 종교적인 것이라면(혼인이야 분명 그러하니), 그 수단이 아무리 사악하든 상관없다는 것이었다."], ["As to other occasions, he used to apply the philosophy of Square, which taught, that the end was immaterial, so that the means were fair and consistent with moral rectitude.", "그 밖의 경우에는 스퀘어의 철학을 갖다 댔는데, 그 철학인즉 수단이 정당하고 도덕적 올바름에 어긋나지 않기만 하면 목적은 아무래도 좋다는 것이었다."], ["To say truth, there were few occurrences in life on which he could not draw advantage from the precepts of one or other of those great masters.", "사실대로 말하면, 인생에서 일어나는 일 가운데 이 두 위대한 스승 중 하나의 가르침에서 이득을 끌어낼 수 없는 경우란 거의 없었다."]], [["Little deceit was indeed necessary to be practised on Mr Western; who thought the inclinations of his daughter of as little consequence as Blifil himself conceived them to be; but as the sentiments of Mr Allworthy were of a very different kind, so it was absolutely necessary to impose on him. In this, however, Blifil was so well assisted by Western, that he succeeded without difficulty; for as Mr Allworthy had been assured by her father that Sophia had a proper affection for Blifil, and that all which he had suspected concerning Jones was entirely false, Blifil had nothing more to do than to confirm these assertions; which he did with such equivocations, that he preserved a salvo for his conscience; and had the satisfaction of conveying a lie to his uncle, without the guilt of telling one. When he was examined touching the inclinations of Sophia by Allworthy, who said, “He would on no account be accessary to forcing a young lady into a marriage contrary to her own will;” he answered, “That the real sentiments of young ladies were very difficult to be understood; that her behaviour to him was full as forward as he wished it, and that if he could believe her father, she had all the affection for him which any lover could desire. As for Jones,” said he, “whom I am loth to call villain, though his behaviour to you, sir, sufficiently justifies the appellation, his own vanity, or perhaps some wicked views, might make him boast of a falsehood; for if there had been any reality in Miss Western's love to him, the greatness of her fortune would never have suffered him to desert her, as you are well informed he hath. Lastly, sir, I promise you I would not myself, for any consideration, no, not for the whole world, consent to marry this young lady, if I was not persuaded she had all the passion for me which I desire she should have.”", "웨스턴 씨에게는 실로 속임수를 부릴 필요가 거의 없었다. 그는 제 딸의 마음 따위를 블리필 자신이 여기는 것만큼이나 하찮게 여겼기 때문이다. 그러나 올워디 씨의 생각은 전혀 다른 종류의 것이었으니, 그를 속이는 일만큼은 반드시 필요했다. 다만 이 일에서 블리필은 웨스턴의 도움을 어찌나 잘 받았던지 어려움 없이 성공을 거두었다. 올워디 씨는 소피아의 아버지로부터 그녀가 블리필에게 마땅한 애정을 품고 있으며, 존스에 관해 자신이 의심했던 모든 것이 전적으로 거짓이라는 다짐을 이미 받은 터였으므로, 블리필이 할 일이라곤 그저 이 단언들을 굳혀 주는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 그는 그것을 얼마나 애매한 말로 해냈던지, 제 양심을 위한 빠져나갈 구실을 마련해 두는 한편, 거짓말을 했다는 죄책감 없이 숙부에게 거짓을 전하는 만족까지 누렸다. 올워디가 소피아의 마음에 관해 그를 캐물으며 \"나는 결코 어린 처녀를 제 뜻에 어긋나는 혼인으로 몰아넣는 일에 가담하지 않겠다\"고 말하자, 그는 이렇게 대답했다. \"어린 처녀들의 진짜 속마음이란 도무지 헤아리기가 어렵습니다. 다만 그녀가 저를 대하는 태도는 제가 바라는 만큼이나 적극적이었고, 그녀의 아버지 말을 믿어도 좋다면, 그녀는 어떤 연인이라도 바랄 법한 온갖 애정을 제게 품고 있다 합니다. 존스로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다, \"그가 어르신께 보인 행실로 보면 그 이름값을 충분히 하고도 남지만, 저는 차마 그를 악당이라 부르고 싶지 않습니다. 그의 허영심이, 어쩌면 무슨 사악한 속셈이 그로 하여금 거짓을 떠벌리게 했을지도 모릅니다. 만일 웨스턴 양이 그를 향해 품은 사랑에 조금이라도 진실이 있었더라면, 그토록 막대한 그녀의 재산을 두고 그가 그녀를 저버리는 일은 결코 없었을 테니까요. 어르신께서도 익히 들어 아시듯 그는 정녕 그녀를 저버렸지 않습니까. 끝으로 어르신, 제가 어르신께 약속드리건대, 만일 이 처녀가 제가 바라는 만큼의 온 애정을 제게 품고 있다고 확신하지 못한다면, 저 자신은 어떤 대가를 치른다 해도, 아니 온 세상을 다 준다 해도 결코 이 처녀와 혼인하는 데 동의하지 않을 것입니다.\""]], [["This excellent method of conveying a falsehood with the heart only, without making the tongue guilty of an untruth, by the means of equivocation and imposture, hath quieted the conscience of many a notable deceiver; and yet, when we consider that it is Omniscience on which these endeavour to impose, it may possibly seem capable of affording only a very superficial comfort; and that this artful and refined distinction between communicating a lie, and telling one, is hardly worth the pains it costs them.", "거짓을 오로지 마음으로만 전하면서, 애매한 표현과 사기로써 혀로는 거짓말의 죄를 짓지 않는 이 절묘한 수법은 적잖은 이름난 사기꾼들의 양심을 잠재워 왔다. 그러나 이런 자들이 속이려 드는 대상이 다름 아닌 전지(全知)하신 분임을 헤아려 보면, 그것은 아마도 더없이 얄팍한 위안밖에 주지 못하는 것일 터다. 그리고 거짓을 전하는 것과 거짓말을 하는 것 사이의 이 교묘하고 정교한 구분이란, 그것을 짜내는 데 드는 수고만큼의 값어치도 좀체 못 되는 것이다."]], [["Allworthy was pretty well satisfied with what Mr Western and Mr Blifil told him: and the treaty was now, at the end of two days, concluded.", "올워디는 웨스턴 씨와 블리필 씨가 일러 준 말에 제법 만족했고, 협약은 이제 이틀 만에 매듭지어졌다."], ["Nothing then remained previous to the office of the priest, but the office of the lawyers, which threatened to take up so much time, that Western offered to bind himself by all manner of covenants, rather than defer the happiness of the young couple.", "그리하여 사제의 직무에 앞서 남은 것이라곤 법률가들의 직무뿐이었는데, 이것이 어찌나 많은 시간을 잡아먹을 듯 위협했던지, 웨스턴은 차라리 온갖 종류의 서약으로 스스로를 옭아맬지언정 이 젊은 한 쌍의 행복을 미루지는 않겠다고 나섰다. 실로 그가 어찌나 열성적이고 다급했던지, 무심한 사람이라면 그가 실제보다 이 혼사에서 더 주된 당사자라고 여겼을 법했다."], ["Indeed, he was so very earnest and pressing, that an indifferent person might have concluded he was more a principal in this match than he really was; but this eagerness was natural to him on all occasions: and he conducted every scheme he undertook in such a manner, as if the success of that alone was sufficient to constitute the whole happiness of his life.", "그러나 이런 열성은 그가 무슨 일에서나 으레 드러내던 것이었다. 그는 자신이 손댄 모든 계획을, 마치 오직 그 계획의 성공만으로 제 인생의 온 행복이 이루어지기라도 하는 양 그렇게 밀어붙였다."]], [["The joint importunities of both father and son-in-law would probably have prevailed on Mr Allworthy, who brooked but ill any delay of giving happiness to others, had not Sophia herself prevented it, and taken measures to put a final end to the whole treaty, and to rob both church and law of those taxes which these wise bodies have thought proper to receive from the propagation of the human species in a lawful manner.", "아버지와 사위 될 사람의 합세한 성화에 올워디 씨는 아마 넘어가고 말았을 것이다. 그는 남에게 행복을 베푸는 일이 조금이라도 미뤄지는 것을 좀체 견디지 못하는 사람이었으니 말이다."], ["Of which in the next chapter.", "그러나 소피아 자신이 그것을 막아섰고, 이 모든 협약에 마침내 종지부를 찍을 방책을 강구했으니, 이로써 인류를 합법적인 방식으로 번식시키는 데서 그 세금을 거두어들이는 것이 마땅하다고 여겨 온 그 현명한 두 기관, 곧 교회와 법으로부터 그 세금을 가로채 버린 셈이었다. 그 이야기는 다음 장에서 다루기로 하자."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["A strange resolution of Sophia, and a more strange stratagem of Mrs Honour.", "7장. 소피아의 기이한 결심, 그리고 아너 부인의 더더욱 기이한 책략."]], [["Though Mrs Honour was principally attached to her own interest, she was not without some little attachment to Sophia.", "아너 부인은 무엇보다 제 잇속에 충실한 사람이었지만, 소피아를 향한 작은 애정마저 없는 것은 아니었다."], ["To say truth, it was very difficult for any one to know that young lady without loving her.", "사실을 말하자면, 그 젊은 아가씨를 알게 된 사람치고 그녀를 사랑하지 않기란 무척 어려운 일이었다."], ["She no sooner therefore heard a piece of news, which she imagined to be of great importance to her mistress, than, quite forgetting the anger which she had conceived two days before, at her unpleasant dismission from Sophia's presence, she ran hastily to inform her of the news.", "그래서 그녀는 자기 안주인에게 매우 중대하다고 여겨지는 소식 하나를 듣자마자, 이틀 전 소피아 앞에서 불쾌하게 물러나게 된 일로 품었던 노여움 따위는 까맣게 잊고, 그 소식을 알리려 허둥지둥 달려갔다."]], [["The beginning of her discourse was as abrupt as her entrance into the room.", "그녀의 말은 방으로 들어서는 모양새만큼이나 다짜고짜 시작되었다. \"어머나, 아가씨!\""], ["“O dear ma'am!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"아가씨께서는 어찌 생각하실까요?"], ["says she, “what doth your la'ship think?", "정말이지 저는 넋이 나갈 만큼 놀랐답니다. 그래도 아가씨께 말씀드리는 것이 제 도리라고 여겼어요."], ["To be sure I am frightened out of my wits; and yet I thought it my duty to tell your la'ship, though perhaps it may make you angry, for we servants don't always know what will make our ladies angry; for, to be sure, everything is always laid to the charge of a servant.", "어쩌면 아가씨를 화나게 할지도 모르지만요. 우리 같은 하인들은 아가씨들을 무엇이 화나게 할지 늘 알지 못하니까요."], ["When our ladies are out of humour, to be sure we must be scolded; and to be sure I should not wonder if your la'ship should be out of humour; nay, it must surprize you certainly, ay, and shock you too.”--“Good Honour, let me know it without any longer preface,”", "정말이지, 무슨 일이든 죄다 하인 탓으로 돌려지는 법이잖아요. 아가씨들이 기분이 언짢으시면, 우리야 꾸지람을 들어야 하는 게 당연하고요."], ["says Sophia; “there are few things, I promise you, which will surprize, and fewer which will shock me.”--“Dear ma'am,”", "또 아가씨께서 기분이 상하신대도 저는 조금도 이상하지 않을 거예요. 아니, 분명 놀라실 거예요."], ["answered Honour, “to be sure, I overheard my master talking to parson Supple about getting a licence this very afternoon; and to be sure I heard him say, your la'ship should be married to-morrow morning.”", "그래요, 충격까지 받으실 거고요.\"—\"착한 아너, 더 이상 서론 늘어놓지 말고 어서 말해 보거라.\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["Sophia turned pale at these words, and repeated eagerly, “To-morrow morning!”--“Yes, ma'am,”", "\"장담하건대 나를 놀라게 할 일은 거의 없고, 충격을 줄 일은 더더욱 적단다.\"—\"아가씨,\" 아너가 대답했다."], ["replied the trusty waiting-woman, “I will take my oath I heard my master say so.”--“Honour,”", "\"정말이지, 바로 오늘 오후에 나리께서 서플 목사님과 혼인 허가증을 받는 일을 두고 이야기하시는 걸 제가 엿들었답니다. 그리고 분명히, 나리께서 아가씨를 내일 아침에 혼인시키겠다고 말씀하시는 걸 들었어요.\""], ["says Sophia, “you have both surprized and shocked me to such a degree that I have scarce any breath or spirits left.", "소피아는 이 말에 얼굴이 창백해지며 다급하게 되물었다. \"내일 아침이라고!\"—\"네, 아가씨.\""], ["What is to be done in my dreadful situation?”--“I wish I was able to advise your la'ship,”", "믿음직한 시녀가 대답했다. \"맹세코, 나리께서 그리 말씀하시는 걸 들었어요.\"—\"아너,\""], ["says she.", "소피아가 말했다."], ["“Do advise me,”", "\"너는 나를 놀라게도 하고 충격에 빠뜨리기도 해서, 이제 숨도 정신도 거의 남지 않았구나. 이 끔찍한 처지에서 어찌해야 한단 말이냐?\"—\"제가 아가씨께 조언을 드릴 수 있다면 좋으련만.\""], ["cries Sophia; “pray, dear Honour, advise me.", "그녀가 말했다. \"조언을 해 다오.\""], ["Think what you would attempt if it was your own case.”--“Indeed, ma'am,”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"부디, 착한 아너, 나에게 조언을 해 다오."], ["cries Honour, “I wish your la'ship and I could change situations; that is, I mean without hurting your la'ship; for to be sure I don't wish you so bad as to be a servant; but because that if so be it was my case, I should find no manner of difficulty in it; for, in my poor opinion, young Squire Blifil is a charming, sweet, handsome man.”--“Don't mention such stuff,”", "만일 네 일이라면 무엇을 시도하겠는지 생각해 보거라.\"—\"정말이지, 아가씨.\" 아너가 외쳤다."], ["cries Sophia.", "\"아가씨와 제가 처지를 바꿀 수 있다면 좋겠어요. 그러니까, 아가씨를 해코지하자는 뜻이 아니라요."], ["“Such stuff!”", "아가씨가 하인이 되시기를 바랄 만큼 못된 마음은 절대 아니에요. 다만 만일 그게 제 일이라면, 저는 조금도 어려울 게 없으리라는 뜻이지요."], ["repeated Honour; “why, there.", "제 변변찮은 소견으로는, 젊은 블리필 도련님은 매력 있고, 다정하고, 잘생긴 분이니까요.\"—\"그런 소리는 입에 담지도 마라.\" 소피아가 외쳤다."], ["Well, to be sure, what's one man's meat is another man's poison, and the same is altogether as true of women.”--“Honour,”", "\"그런 소리라고요!\" 아너가 되받았다."], ["says Sophia, “rather than submit to be the wife of that contemptible wretch, I would plunge a dagger into my heart.”--“O lud!", "\"원, 저런. 그래요, 한 사람의 진수성찬이 다른 사람에겐 독이라더니, 여자들에게도 똑같이 들어맞는 말인가 봐요.\"—\"아너,\""], ["ma'am!”", "소피아가 말했다. \"저 경멸스러운 인간의 아내가 되느니, 차라리 내 가슴에 단검을 꽂겠다.\"—\"어머나!"], ["answered the other, “I am sure you frighten me out of my wits now.", "아가씨!\" 상대가 대답했다."], ["Let me beseech your la'ship not to suffer such wicked thoughts to come into your head.", "\"정말이지 이제는 아가씨께서 저를 넋 나가게 하시네요. 부디 그런 못된 생각이 머릿속에 들어오지 못하게 하시기를 간청드려요."], ["O lud!", "아이고! 정말이지 제 온몸이 떨려요."], ["to be sure I tremble every inch of me.", "아가씨, 생각해 보세요, 기독교식 매장을 거부당하고, 시신이 큰길에 묻혀, 옥스 크로스에서 농부 하프페니가 당한 것처럼 말뚝이 몸을 꿰뚫고 박힌다는 게 어떤 일인지. 그리고 정말이지, 그 후로 그의 유령이 거기를 떠돈다지요."], ["Dear ma'am, consider, that to be denied Christian burial, and to have your corpse buried in the highway, and a stake drove through you, as farmer Halfpenny was served at Ox Cross; and, to be sure, his ghost hath walked there ever since, for several people have seen him.", "여러 사람이 그를 봤다니까요. 정말이지, 누구의 머릿속에 그런 못된 생각을 집어넣는 건 악마밖에 없을 거예요."], ["To be sure it can be nothing but the devil which can put such wicked thoughts into the head of anybody; for certainly it is less wicked to hurt all the world than one's own dear self; and so I have heard said by more parsons than one.", "분명히, 자기 자신을 해치는 것보다는 온 세상을 해치는 것이 덜 사악한 일이니까요. 한두 분 목사님께 들은 게 아니라고요."], ["If your la'ship hath such a violent aversion, and hates the young gentleman so very bad, that you can't bear to think of going into bed to him; for to be sure there may be such antipathies in nature, and one had lieverer touch a toad than the flesh of some people.”--", "만일 아가씨께서 그토록 격렬한 거부감을 품으시고, 그 젊은 도련님을 너무도 끔찍이 싫어하셔서 그와 한 침대에 든다는 생각조차 견딜 수 없으시다면요. 정말이지 천성에 그런 반감이 있을 수도 있고, 어떤 사람의 살갗을 만지느니 두꺼비를 만지는 편이 낫다고 여기는 이도 있으니까요.\"—"]], [["Sophia had been too much wrapt in contemplation to pay any great attention to the foregoing excellent discourse of her maid; interrupting her therefore, without making any answer to it, she said, “Honour, I am come to a resolution. I am determined to leave my father's house this very night; and if you have the friendship for me which you have often professed, you will keep me company.”--“That I will, ma'am, to the world's end,” answered Honour; “but I beg your la'ship to consider the consequence before you undertake any rash action. Where can your la'ship possibly go?”--“There is,” replied Sophia, “a lady of quality in London, a relation of mine, who spent several months with my aunt in the country; during all which time she treated me with great kindness, and expressed so much pleasure in my company, that she earnestly desired my aunt to suffer me to go with her to London. As she is a woman of very great note, I shall easily find her out, and I make no doubt of being very well and kindly received by her.”--“I would not have your la'ship too confident of that,” cries Honour; “for the first lady I lived with used to invite people very earnestly to her house; but if she heard afterwards they were coming, she used to get out of the way. Besides, though this lady would be very glad to see your la'ship, as to be sure anybody would be glad to see your la'ship, yet when she hears your la'ship is run away from my master--” “You are mistaken, Honour,” says Sophia: “she looks upon the authority of a father in a much lower light than I do; for she pressed me violently to go to London with her, and when I refused to go without my father's consent, she laughed me to scorn, called me silly country girl, and said, I should make a pure loving wife, since I could be so dutiful a daughter. So I have no doubt but she will both receive me and protect me too, till my father, finding me out of his power, can be brought to some reason.”", "소피아는 너무도 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있어, 하녀의 앞선 그 훌륭한 일장 연설에 별 관심을 기울이지 못했다. 그래서 그녀는 거기에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 말을 끊으며 이렇게 말했다. \"아너, 나는 결심을 굳혔다. 바로 오늘 밤 아버지의 집을 떠나기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 네가 늘 입버릇처럼 말하던 그 우정을 나에게 품고 있다면, 나와 동행해 줄 것이다.\"—\"그럼요, 아가씨, 세상 끝까지라도 따르겠어요.\" 아너가 대답했다. \"하지만 부디, 어떤 무모한 행동을 벌이시기 전에 그 결과를 헤아려 보시기를 청해요. 아가씨께서 대체 어디로 가실 수 있겠어요?\"—\"런던에,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"지체 높은 부인 한 분이 계신데, 나의 친척이란다. 시골에서 여러 달을 내 고모와 함께 보내셨지. 그동안 내내 나에게 무척 다정하게 대해 주셨고, 내 곁에 있는 것을 어찌나 즐거워하셨던지, 나를 런던으로 데려가게 해 달라고 고모에게 간곡히 청하실 정도였단다. 워낙 이름난 분이시니, 나는 쉽게 찾아낼 수 있을 테고, 그분께 아주 따뜻하고 친절하게 맞아들여지리라는 데 조금도 의심이 없다.\"—\"아가씨, 그 일에 너무 자신만만하지는 마세요.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"제가 처음 모셨던 마님은 사람들을 자기 집으로 무척 간곡히 초대하시곤 했지만, 나중에 정작 그들이 온다는 말을 들으면 슬그머니 자리를 피하시곤 했답니다. 게다가, 그 부인이 아가씨를 뵙는 거야 무척 반가워하시겠지요. 정말이지 누구라도 아가씨를 뵈면 반가워할 테니까요. 그렇더라도 아가씨께서 나리에게서 도망쳐 나오셨다는 말을 들으시면—\" \"네가 잘못 알고 있다, 아너.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"그분은 아버지의 권위라는 것을 나보다 훨씬 가볍게 여기시거든. 그분은 나에게 런던으로 함께 가자고 거세게 졸라 대셨고, 내가 아버지의 허락 없이는 안 가겠다고 했더니, 나를 비웃으며 어리석은 시골 처녀라 부르시고는, 이토록 효성스러운 딸이니 분명 더없이 다정한 아내가 되겠다고 하셨단다. 그러니 그분이 나를 받아 주시고, 또 보호해 주시리라는 것을 의심하지 않는다. 아버지가 내가 당신 손아귀를 벗어난 것을 깨닫고 어느 정도 이치에 닿게 마음을 돌릴 때까지 말이다.\""]], [["“Well, but, ma'am,” answered Honour, “how doth your la'ship think of making your escape? Where will you get any horses or conveyance? For as for your own horse, as all the servants know a little how matters stand between my master and your la'ship, Robin will be hanged before he will suffer it to go out of the stable without my master's express orders.” “I intend to escape,” said Sophia, “by walking out of the doors when they are open. I thank Heaven my legs are very able to carry me. They have supported me many a long evening”--“Yes, to be sure,” cries Honour, “I will follow your la'ship through the world; but your la'ship had almost as good be alone: for I should not be able to defend you, if any robbers, or other villains, should meet with you. Nay, I should be in as horrible a fright as your la'ship; for to be certain, they would ravish us both. Besides, ma'am, consider how cold the nights are now; we shall be frozen to death.”--“A good brisk pace,” answered Sophia, “will preserve us from the cold; and if you cannot defend me from a villain, Honour, I will defend you; for I will take a pistol with me. There are two always charged in the hall.”--“Dear ma'am, you frighten me more and more,” cries Honour: “sure your la'ship would not venture to fire it off! I had rather run any chance than your la'ship should do that.”--“Why so?” says Sophia, smiling; “would not you, Honour, fire a pistol at any one who should attack your virtue?”--“To be sure, ma'am,” cries Honour, “one's virtue is a dear thing, especially to us poor servants; for it is our livelihood, as a body may say: yet I mortally hate fire-arms; for so many accidents happen by them.”--“Well, well,” says Sophia, “I believe I may ensure your virtue at a very cheap rate, without carrying any arms with us; for I intend to take horses at the very first town we come to, and we shall hardly be attacked in our way thither. Look'ee, Honour, I am resolved to go; and if you will attend me, I promise you I will reward you to the very utmost of my power.”", "\"그럼, 그런데 아가씨,\" 아너가 대답했다. \"어떻게 도망칠 생각이세요? 말이나 탈것은 어디서 구하시려고요? 아가씨 말로 말씀드리자면, 하인들이 다들 나리와 아가씨 사이가 어떤지 어느 정도 알고 있으니, 로빈은 나리의 명백한 분부 없이는 말을 마구간에서 내보내느니 차라리 목매달리는 편을 택할 거예요.\" \"나는,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"문이 열려 있을 때 그냥 걸어 나가서 도망칠 작정이다. 하늘에 감사하게도 내 다리는 나를 충분히 데려갈 수 있단다. 여러 긴 저녁 동안 나를 지탱해 주었으니 말이다.\"—\"네, 정말이지요.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"저는 세상 끝까지 아가씨를 따르겠어요. 하지만 아가씨께서는 차라리 혼자 가시는 편이 나을지도 몰라요. 도둑이나 다른 악당들이 아가씨와 마주치면, 저는 아가씨를 지켜 드릴 수 없을 테니까요. 아니, 저야말로 아가씨만큼이나 끔찍하게 겁에 질릴 거예요. 분명히 그자들은 우리 둘 다 욕보일 테니까요. 게다가 아가씨, 요즘 밤이 얼마나 추운지 생각해 보세요. 우리는 얼어 죽고 말 거예요.\"—\"빠르고 활기차게 걸으면,\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"추위는 막아 줄 게다. 그리고 네가 악당으로부터 나를 지켜 줄 수 없다면, 아너, 내가 너를 지켜 주마. 권총을 가지고 갈 테니까. 홀에 늘 장전된 채로 두 자루가 있단다.\"—\"아가씨, 점점 더 무섭게 하시네요.\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"설마 아가씨께서 그걸 발사하실 작정은 아니시지요! 아가씨께서 그런 일을 하시느니, 저는 차라리 어떤 위험이든 무릅쓰겠어요.\"—\"왜 그러느냐?\" 소피아가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아너, 네 정조를 노리고 덤벼드는 자가 있다면 너는 권총을 쏘지 않겠느냐?\"—\"정말이지요, 아가씨,\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"정조란 소중한 것이지요. 특히 우리 같은 가난한 하인들에게는요. 말하자면 그게 우리 밥줄이니까요. 그래도 저는 총칼을 죽도록 싫어해요. 그것 때문에 사고가 어찌나 많이 일어나는지요.\"—\"그래, 그래.\" 소피아가 말했다. \"내 생각엔 우리가 무기를 지니지 않아도 네 정조쯤은 아주 헐값에 지켜 줄 수 있을 듯하구나. 처음 닿는 마을에서 곧장 말을 구할 작정이고, 거기까지 가는 동안에 우리가 공격당하는 일은 거의 없을 테니까. 보거라, 아너, 나는 가기로 결심했다. 그리고 네가 나를 따라 준다면, 내 힘이 닿는 끝까지 너에게 보답하리라 약속하마.\""]], [["This last argument had a stronger effect on Honour than all the preceding. And since she saw her mistress so determined, she desisted from any further dissuasions. They then entered into a debate on ways and means of executing their project. Here a very stubborn difficulty occurred, and this was the removal of their effects, which was much more easily got over by the mistress than by the maid; for when a lady hath once taken a resolution to run to a lover, or to run from him, all obstacles are considered as trifles. But Honour was inspired by no such motive; she had no raptures to expect, nor any terrors to shun; and besides the real value of her clothes, in which consisted a great part of her fortune, she had a capricious fondness for several gowns, and other things; either because they became her, or because they were given her by such a particular person; because she had bought them lately, or because she had had them long; or for some other reasons equally good; so that she could not endure the thoughts of leaving the poor things behind her exposed to the mercy of Western, who, she doubted not, would in his rage make them suffer martyrdom.", "이 마지막 말은 앞선 그 어떤 논리보다 아너에게 더 강한 영향을 미쳤다. 그리고 자기 안주인이 그토록 단단히 결심한 것을 보자, 그녀는 더 이상의 만류를 그만두었다. 두 사람은 곧 계획을 실행할 방법과 수단을 두고 의논에 들어갔다. 여기서 매우 까다로운 난관 하나가 불거졌으니, 바로 짐을 옮기는 문제였다. 이는 안주인 쪽보다 하녀 쪽에게 훨씬 더 넘기 어려운 일이었다. 한 아가씨가 연인에게 달려가거나 그에게서 도망치기로 일단 마음을 정하고 나면, 모든 장애물은 하찮은 것으로 여겨지는 법이니까. 그러나 아너에게는 그런 동기가 없었다. 기대할 황홀함도, 피해야 할 두려움도 없었던 것이다. 게다가 자기 재산의 상당 부분을 이루는 옷가지의 실제 값어치는 차치하고라도, 그녀는 여러 벌의 드레스와 다른 물건들에 변덕스러운 애착을 품고 있었다. 그것이 자기에게 잘 어울려서든, 아니면 어떤 특별한 사람에게서 받은 것이어서든, 최근에 산 것이어서든, 오래도록 지녀 온 것이어서든, 그밖에 그만큼이나 그럴듯한 갖가지 이유로 말이다. 그래서 그녀는 그 가엾은 물건들을 뒤에 남겨, 웨스턴의 자비에 내맡긴다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없었다. 그가 분노에 차서 그것들을 순교의 고통에 처하게 하리라는 것을 그녀는 의심치 않았으니까."]], [["The ingenious Mrs Honour having applied all her oratory to dissuade her mistress from her purpose, when she found her positively determined, at last started the following expedient to remove her clothes, viz., to get herself turned out of doors that very evening. Sophia highly approved this method, but doubted how it might be brought about. “O, ma'am,” cries Honour, “your la'ship may trust that to me; we servants very well know how to obtain this favour of our masters and mistresses; though sometimes, indeed, where they owe us more wages than they can readily pay, they will put up with all our affronts, and will hardly take any warning we can give them; but the squire is none of those; and since your la'ship is resolved upon setting out to-night, I warrant I get discharged this afternoon.” It was then resolved that she should pack up some linen and a night-gown for Sophia, with her own things; and as for all her other clothes, the young lady abandoned them with no more remorse than the sailor feels when he throws over the goods of others, in order to save his own life.", "재간 있는 아너 부인은 안주인의 결심을 단념시키려 온갖 언변을 다 동원했지만, 그녀가 단호하게 마음을 굳힌 것을 보고는, 마침내 자기 옷가지를 옮길 다음과 같은 방책을 떠올렸다. 바로 그날 저녁에 자기 자신이 집에서 쫓겨나는 것이었다. 소피아는 이 방법을 크게 찬성했으나, 어찌 그 일을 꾸밀 수 있을지 미심쩍어했다. \"오, 아가씨,\" 아너가 외쳤다. \"그 일은 저에게 맡기셔도 돼요. 우리 같은 하인들은 주인 나리나 마님께 이런 혜택을 얻어 내는 법을 아주 잘 안답니다. 물론 때로는, 그분들이 우리에게 곧장 치를 수 없을 만큼 밀린 품삯이 있을 때면, 우리의 온갖 무례를 다 참아 넘기시고, 우리가 아무리 으름장을 놓아도 좀처럼 알아듣지 못하시지만요. 그러나 나리께서는 그런 분이 아니시지요. 아가씨께서 오늘 밤에 떠나기로 결심하셨으니, 제가 오늘 오후에 해고당하는 걸 틀림없이 해 보이겠어요.\" 그리하여 그녀가 소피아를 위해 속옷 몇 벌과 잠옷 한 벌을 자기 물건과 함께 꾸리기로 정해졌다. 그리고 자기의 다른 모든 옷가지에 대해서는, 젊은 아가씨는 자기 목숨을 구하려 남의 짐을 바다에 던져 버리는 뱃사람만큼이나 거리낌 없이 그것들을 단념했다."]], [["Containing scenes of altercation, of no very uncommon kind.", "그다지 드물지 않은 종류의 말다툼 장면들을 담은 장."]], [["Mrs Honour had scarce sooner parted from her young lady, than something (for I would not, like the old woman in Quevedo, injure the devil by any false accusation, and possibly he might have no hand in it)--but something, I say, suggested itself to her, that by sacrificing Sophia and all her secrets to Mr Western, she might probably make her fortune.", "아너 부인은 젊은 아가씨와 헤어지기가 무섭게, 무언가가(나는 케베도의 그 노파처럼 거짓 고발로 악마를 모함하고 싶지는 않으며, 어쩌면 이번 일에는 악마가 손을 대지 않았을지도 모르니) 떠올랐는데, 다시 말해 무언가가 그녀에게 이런 생각을 불어넣었다. 소피아와 그녀의 온갖 비밀을 웨스턴 씨에게 팔아넘긴다면, 자신의 팔자를 고칠 수 있으리라는 생각이었다."], ["Many considerations urged this discovery.", "여러 가지 고려가 이 폭로를 부추겼다."], ["The fair prospect of a handsome reward for so great and acceptable a service to the squire, tempted her avarice; and again, the danger of the enterprize she had undertaken; the uncertainty of its success; night, cold, robbers, ravishers, all alarmed her fears.", "지주에게 그토록 크고 반가운 봉사를 해 준 대가로 두둑한 보상을 받을 거라는 화사한 전망이 그녀의 탐욕을 자극했고, 또 한편으로는 자신이 맡은 이 모험의 위험, 그 성공이 불확실하다는 점, 밤, 추위, 강도, 겁탈자—이 모두가 그녀의 두려움을 일깨웠다."], ["So forcibly did all these operate upon her, that she was almost determined to go directly to the squire, and to lay open the whole affair.", "이 모든 것이 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 그녀는 곧장 지주에게 가서 사정을 죄다 털어놓기로 거의 결심할 뻔했다."], ["She was, however, too upright a judge to decree on one side, before she had heard the other.", "그러나 그녀는 한쪽 말만 듣고 한쪽 편을 들기에는 너무나 올곧은 재판관이었다."], ["And here, first, a journey to London appeared very strongly in support of Sophia.", "그리고 여기서, 먼저 런던으로 가는 여정이 소피아 편을 매우 강하게 뒷받침했다."], ["She eagerly longed to see a place in which she fancied charms short only of those which a raptured saint imagines in heaven.", "그녀는 황홀경에 든 성인이 천국에서나 상상할 법한 그것에 조금 못 미칠 뿐인 매력으로 가득하리라고 마음속에 그리던 그곳을 간절히 보고 싶어 했다."], ["In the next place, as she knew Sophia to have much more generosity than her master, so her fidelity promised her a greater reward than she could gain by treachery.", "다음으로, 그녀는 소피아가 자기 주인보다 훨씬 더 너그럽다는 것을 알았기에, 충실함을 지키는 편이 배신으로 얻을 수 있는 것보다 더 큰 보상을 약속해 주었다."], ["She then cross-examined all the articles which had raised her fears on the other side, and found, on fairly sifting the matter, that there was very little in them.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 반대편에서 자신의 두려움을 키웠던 항목들을 하나하나 따져 보았고, 문제를 공정하게 추려 보니 거기에는 별것 없다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["And now both scales being reduced to a pretty even balance, her love to her mistress being thrown into the scale of her integrity, made that rather preponderate, when a circumstance struck upon her imagination which might have had a dangerous effect, had its whole weight been fairly put into the other scale.", "이제 양쪽 저울이 거의 평형에 이르렀고, 여주인을 향한 사랑이 그녀의 충직함 쪽 저울판에 얹히자 그쪽이 다소 무겁게 기울었다. 그때 한 가지 사정이 그녀의 상상을 때렸으니, 만일 그 무게가 고스란히 반대쪽 저울판에 실렸더라면 위험한 결과를 낳았을 만한 것이었다."], ["This was the length of time which must intervene before Sophia would be able to fulfil her promises; for though she was intitled to her mother's fortune at the death of her father, and to the sum of £3000 left her by an uncle when she came of age; yet these were distant days, and many accidents might prevent the intended generosity of the young lady; whereas the rewards she might expect from Mr Western were immediate.", "그것은 소피아가 약속을 지킬 수 있게 되기까지 흘러야 할 세월의 길이였다. 비록 소피아는 아버지가 죽으면 어머니의 재산을 물려받을 권리가 있고, 성년이 되면 어느 삼촌이 남긴 3천 파운드의 돈도 받을 권리가 있었지만, 그날들은 아직 멀었고, 또 많은 일이 끼어들어 이 젊은 아가씨가 베풀려는 너그러움을 가로막을 수도 있었다."], ["But while she was pursuing this thought the good genius of Sophia, or that which presided over the integrity of Mrs Honour, or perhaps mere chance, sent an accident in her way, which at once preserved her fidelity, and even facilitated the intended business.", "반면에 웨스턴 씨에게서 기대할 수 있는 보상은 당장의 것이었다. 그러나 그녀가 이 생각을 좇고 있을 때, 소피아의 선한 수호신이, 혹은 아너 부인의 충직함을 다스리는 그 무엇이, 어쩌면 그저 우연이 그녀의 길에 한 사건을 보내 주었으니, 그것이 단번에 그녀의 충실함을 지켜 주었을 뿐 아니라 의도된 일까지 수월하게 만들어 주었다."]], [["Mrs Western's maid claimed great superiority over Mrs Honour on several accounts.", "웨스턴 부인의 하녀는 여러 가지 이유로 아너 부인보다 자신이 훨씬 위라고 주장했다."], ["First, her birth was higher; for her great-grandmother by the mother's side was a cousin, not far removed, to an Irish peer.", "첫째, 그녀의 출생이 더 높았다. 어머니 쪽 증조할머니가 어느 아일랜드 귀족과 그리 멀지 않은 사촌뻘이었기 때문이다."], ["Secondly, her wages were greater.", "둘째, 그녀의 급료가 더 많았다."], ["And lastly, she had been at London, and had of consequence seen more of the world.", "그리고 마지막으로, 그녀는 런던에 가 본 적이 있었고, 그 덕분에 세상을 더 많이 본 셈이었다."], ["She had always behaved, therefore, to Mrs Honour with that reserve, and had always exacted of her those marks of distinction, which every order of females preserves and requires in conversation with those of an inferior order.", "그래서 그녀는 늘 아너 부인을 대할 때 그런 거리를 두었고, 모든 계급의 여인이 아랫계급의 여인과 대화할 때 지키고 또 요구하는 그 차별의 표시를 늘 강요했다."], ["Now as Honour did not at all times agree with this doctrine, but would frequently break in upon the respect which the other demanded, Mrs Western's maid was not at all pleased with her company; indeed, she earnestly longed to return home to the house of her mistress, where she domineered at will over all the other servants.", "그런데 아너는 이 교리에 언제나 동의하지는 않았고, 상대가 요구하는 존경을 곧잘 깨뜨리곤 했으므로, 웨스턴 부인의 하녀는 그녀와 함께 있는 것을 전혀 달가워하지 않았다. 실로 그녀는 자기 여주인의 저택으로 돌아가기를 간절히 바랐는데, 그곳에서는 다른 하인들 위에 마음껏 군림할 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["She had been greatly, therefore, disappointed in the morning, when Mrs Western had changed her mind on the very point of departure; and had been in what is vulgarly called a glouting humour ever since.", "그래서 그녀는 아침에, 웨스턴 부인이 막 떠나려던 바로 그 순간에 마음을 바꾸었을 때 크게 실망했고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 흔히 말하는 부루퉁한 기분에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["In this humour, which was none of the sweetest, she came into the room where Honour was debating with herself in the manner we have above related.", "이런 기분으로, 그것도 결코 곱지 않은 기분으로, 그녀는 아너가 위에서 이야기한 식으로 혼자 곰곰이 따지고 있던 방으로 들어왔다. 아너는 그녀를 보자마자 다음과 같이 상냥한 말투로 인사를 건넸다."], ["Honour no sooner saw her, than she addressed her in the following obliging phrase: “Soh, madam, I find we are to have the pleasure of your company longer, which I was afraid the quarrel between my master and your lady would have robbed us of.”--“I don't know, madam,”", "\"어머, 마님, 댁의 동행을 더 오래 누리는 즐거움을 갖게 됐네요. 제 주인 어른과 댁의 아씨 사이의 다툼 때문에 그 즐거움을 빼앗기지나 않을까 걱정했는데 말이에요.\""], ["answered the other, “what you mean by we and us.", "\"마님이라뇨, 저는,\" 상대가 대답했다, \"'우리'니 '우리네'니 하는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠군요."], ["I assure you I do not look on any of the servants in this house to be proper company for me.", "분명히 말해 두는데, 저는 이 집의 어떤 하인도 저와 어울릴 만한 동행이라고 보지 않아요. 저는 일주일 내내 그들보다 윗사람들과 어울릴 자격이 있다고 생각하거든요."], ["I am company, I hope, for their betters every day in the week.", "아너 부인, 당신을 두고 하는 말은 아니에요. 당신은 교양 있는 젊은 여자니까요."], ["I do not speak on your account, Mrs Honour; for you are a civilized young woman; and when you have seen a little more of the world, I should not be ashamed to walk with you in St James's Park.”--“Hoity toity!”", "세상을 조금만 더 겪으면, 세인트 제임스 공원을 당신과 함께 거니는 것도 부끄럽지 않을 거예요.\" \"어머나 세상에!\""], ["cries Honour, “madam is in her airs, I protest.", "아너가 외쳤다, \"마님께서 거드름을 피우시네요, 정말. '아너 부인'이라니, 원!"], ["Mrs Honour, forsooth!", "마님, 제 성으로 불러 주셔도 될 텐데요."], ["sure, madam, you might call me by my sir-name; for though my lady calls me Honour, I have a sir-name as well as other folks.", "우리 아씨가 저를 아너라고 부르시긴 하지만, 저도 다른 사람들처럼 성이 있다고요. 저와 함께 걷기가 부끄럽다니, 참!"], ["Ashamed to walk with me, quotha!", "어머, 저도 당신만큼은 된답니다.\" \"내 예의에 그렇게 갚는다면,\""], ["marry, as good as yourself, I hope.”--“Since you make such a return to my civility,”", "상대가 말했다, \"아너 부인, 분명히 말해 두지만, 당신은 나만큼 못해요. 시골에서야 별의별 잡것들과 다 어울려야 하지만, 도시에서 저는 지체 높은 부인네들만 찾아뵙거든요."], ["said the other, “I must acquaint you, Mrs Honour, that you are not so good as me.", "정말이지, 아너 부인, 당신과 저 사이에는 어느 정도 차이가 있다고 생각해요.\" \"저도 그렇게 생각해요,\""], ["In the country, indeed, one is obliged to take up with all kind of trumpery; but in town I visit none but the women of women of quality.", "아너가 대꾸했다. \"우리 나이에 어느 정도 차이가 있고, 또—제 생각엔 우리 인물에도요.\""], ["Indeed, Mrs Honour, there is some difference, I hope, between you and me.”--“I hope so too,”", "마지막 말을 내뱉으면서 그녀는 더없이 약을 올리는 경멸의 태도로 웨스턴 부인의 하녀 곁을 으스대며 지나갔다. 코를 치켜들고, 고개를 까딱 젖히고, 자기 치마 버팀살로 경쟁자의 버팀살을 거칠게 쓸어 넘기면서."], ["answered Honour: “there is some difference in our ages, and--I think in our persons.”", "상대 여인은 더없이 악의에 찬 비웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"이런 것이!"], ["Upon speaking which last words, she strutted by Mrs Western's maid with the most provoking air of contempt; turning up her nose, tossing her head, and violently brushing the hoop of her competitor with her own.", "너는 내 화를 받을 가치도 없어. 그런 뻔뻔하고 건방진 잡년에게 험한 말을 주고받는 것조차 내 격에 안 맞지."], ["The other lady put on one of her most malicious sneers, and said, “Creature!", "하지만 이년아, 분명히 말해 두는데, 네 행실은 네 출생의 천함과 더불어 네 교육의 천함까지 그대로 드러내는구나. 둘 다 너를 어느 시골 계집의 천한 하녀 노릇이나 하기에 딱 알맞게 만들어 놓았어.\""], ["you are below my anger; and it is beneath me to give ill words to such an audacious saucy trollop; but, hussy, I must tell you, your breeding shows the meanness of your birth as well as of your education; and both very properly qualify you to be the mean serving-woman of a country girl.”--“Don't abuse my lady,”", "\"우리 아씨를 욕하지 마요,\" 아너가 외쳤다."], ["cries Honour: “I won't take that of you; she's as much better than yours as she is younger, and ten thousand times more handsomer.”", "\"그건 못 참아요. 우리 아씨는 댁의 아씨보다 젊은 만큼 훨씬 나으시고, 만 배는 더 아름다우시다고요.\""]], [["Here ill luck, or rather good luck, sent Mrs Western to see her maid in tears, which began to flow plentifully at her approach; and of which being asked the reason by her mistress, she presently acquainted her that her tears were occasioned by the rude treatment of that creature there--meaning Honour.", "여기서 불운이, 아니 오히려 행운이 웨스턴 부인을 보내, 자기 하녀가 눈물 흘리는 모습을 보게 했다."], ["“And, madam,”", "그 눈물은 부인이 다가오자 펑펑 쏟아지기 시작했다."], ["continued she, “I could have despised all she said to me; but she hath had the audacity to affront your ladyship, and to call you ugly--Yes, madam, she called you ugly old cat to my face.", "여주인이 그 까닭을 묻자, 하녀는 곧바로 자신의 눈물이 저기 저 것이—아너를 가리키는 말이었다—거칠게 굴어서 나온 것이라고 일러바쳤다. \"그리고 마님,\""], ["I could not bear to hear your ladyship called ugly.”--“Why do you repeat her impudence so often?”", "그녀가 말을 이었다, \"저한테 한 말은 다 무시할 수 있었어요. 그런데 저 여자가 뻔뻔하게도 마님을 모욕하면서 마님을 못생겼다고 했지 뭐예요—네, 마님, 제 면전에서 마님을 못생긴 늙은 고양이라고 불렀어요."], ["said Mrs Western.", "마님이 못생겼다는 말을 듣고는 도저히 견딜 수가 없었어요.\" \"그 무엄한 말을 왜 자꾸 되풀이하느냐?\""], ["And then turning to Mrs Honour, she asked her “How she had the assurance to mention her name with disrespect?”--“Disrespect, madam!”", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다. 그러고 나서 아너 부인 쪽으로 돌아서서, \"어떻게 감히 내 이름을 함부로 입에 올렸느냐?\"고 물었다."], ["answered Honour; “I never mentioned your name at all: I said somebody was not as handsome as my mistress, and to be sure you know that as well as I.”--“Hussy,”", "\"함부로라뇨, 마님!\" 아너가 대답했다, \"저는 마님 이름을 입에 올린 적이 전혀 없어요."], ["replied the lady, “I will make such a saucy trollop as yourself know that I am not a proper subject of your discourse.", "그저 누군가가 우리 아씨만큼 아름답지 못하다고 했을 뿐이고, 그건 분명히 마님도 저만큼 잘 아실 텐데요.\" \"이년아,\""], ["And if my brother doth not discharge you this moment, I will never sleep in his house again.", "부인이 받아쳤다, \"너 같은 건방진 잡년에게, 내가 네 입에 오르내릴 만한 사람이 아니라는 걸 톡톡히 알게 해 주마. 내 동생이 지금 당장 너를 내쫓지 않는다면, 나는 다시는 이 집에서 잠자지 않을 테다."], ["I will find him out, and have you discharged this moment.”--“Discharged!”", "그를 찾아내서, 너를 이 순간 당장 쫓아내게 하겠다.\" \"쫓겨난다고요!\""], ["cries Honour; “and suppose I am: there are more places in the world than one.", "아너가 외쳤다, \"그렇게 된다 해도, 세상에 일자리가 한 군데뿐인 줄 아세요. 다행히도, 좋은 하인은 일자리가 모자랄 일이 없어요."], ["Thank Heaven, good servants need not want places; and if you turn away all who do not think you handsome, you will want servants very soon; let me tell you that.”", "그리고 마님을 아름답다고 여기지 않는 사람을 죄다 내보내신다면, 곧 부릴 하인이 모자랄 거예요. 분명히 말씀드리는데요.\""]], [["Mrs Western spoke, or rather thundered, in answer; but as she was hardly articulate, we cannot be very certain of the identical words; we shall therefore omit inserting a speech which at best would not greatly redound to her honour.", "웨스턴 부인은 대답으로 말을 했다, 아니 오히려 천둥을 쳤다. 그러나 거의 알아들을 수 없는 소리였으므로, 우리는 그 똑같은 말이 무엇이었는지 확실히 알 수 없다."], ["She then departed in search of her brother, with a countenance so full of rage, that she resembled one of the furies rather than a human creature.", "따라서 우리는 잘해 봐야 그녀의 명예에 별로 보탬이 되지 않을 그 말을 옮겨 적는 일은 생략하겠다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 동생을 찾아 나섰는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 분노로 가득했던지, 인간이라기보다 복수의 여신 가운데 하나를 닮아 있었다."]], [["The two chambermaids being again left alone, began a second bout at altercation, which soon produced a combat of a more active kind.", "두 하녀가 다시 단둘이 남게 되자, 두 번째 말다툼이 시작되었고, 그것은 이내 더 격렬한 종류의 싸움으로 번졌다."], ["In this the victory belonged to the lady of inferior rank, but not without some loss of blood, of hair, and of lawn and muslin.", "이 싸움에서 승리는 신분이 낮은 쪽 여인에게 돌아갔으나, 피와 머리카락, 그리고 고운 아마포와 모슬린의 손실이 없지는 않았다."]], [["# Chapter ix.", "# Chapter ix."]], [["The wise demeanour of Mr Western in the character of a magistrate.", "9장. 치안판사 노릇을 맡은 웨스턴 씨의 현명한 처신."], ["A hint to justices of peace, concerning the necessary qualifications of a clerk; with extraordinary instances of paternal madness and filial affection.", "치안판사들에게 서기에게 꼭 필요한 자질에 관해 던지는 한마디 조언. 그리고 아비의 광기와 딸의 효심에 관한 유별난 사례들."]], [["Logicians sometimes prove too much by an argument, and politicians often overreach themselves in a scheme.", "논리학자들은 때때로 하나의 논증으로 너무 많은 것을 증명하려 들고, 정치가들은 곧잘 책략을 부리다가 제 꾀에 넘어간다. 어너 부인에게도 하마터면 그런 일이 벌어질 뻔했다."], ["Thus had it like to have happened to Mrs Honour, who, instead of recovering the rest of her clothes, had like to have stopped even those she had on her back from escaping; for the squire no sooner heard of her having abused his sister, than he swore twenty oaths he would send her to Bridewell.", "그녀는 남은 옷가지를 되찾기는커녕, 도리어 지금 등에 걸치고 있는 옷마저 빼앗길 뻔했다. 어너가 누이를 욕보였다는 말을 듣자마자, 지주는 스무 번도 넘게 욕설을 퍼부으며 그녀를 브라이드웰로 보내 버리겠다고 맹세했기 때문이다."]], [["Mrs Western was a very good-natured woman, and ordinarily of a forgiving temper. She had lately remitted the trespass of a stage-coachman, who had overturned her post-chaise into a ditch; nay, she had even broken the law, in refusing to prosecute a highwayman who had robbed her, not only of a sum of money, but of her ear-rings; at the same time d--ning her, and saying, “Such handsome b--s as you don't want jewels to set them off, and be d--n'd to you.” But now, so uncertain are our tempers, and so much do we at different times differ from ourselves, she would hear of no mitigation; nor could all the affected penitence of Honour, nor all the entreaties of Sophia for her own servant, prevail with her to desist from earnestly desiring her brother to execute justiceship (for it was indeed a syllable more than justice) on the wench.", "웨스턴 양은 본래 마음씨가 무척 너그러운 여인으로, 평소에는 잘 용서하는 성품이었다. 얼마 전에는 자신의 마차를 도랑에 처박은 역마차 마부의 잘못도 눈감아 주었다. 아니, 심지어 그녀에게서 돈뿐 아니라 귀고리까지 털어 간 노상강도를 고발하지 않음으로써 법을 어기기까지 했다. 그 강도는 그녀에게 욕을 퍼부으며 이렇게 말했던 것이다. \"당신처럼 예쁜 ××년은 보석 따위로 꾸밀 필요가 없으니, 빌어먹을, 그런 줄이나 아쇼.\" 그러나 지금은—사람의 성미란 이토록 종잡을 수 없고, 우리는 때에 따라 이렇게도 제 자신과 달라지는 법이라—그녀는 어떤 감형의 말도 들으려 하지 않았다. 어너가 아무리 뉘우치는 척해 보아도, 소피아가 제 하녀를 위해 아무리 간청해 보아도, 그녀가 동생더러 저 계집에게 정의를(아니, 사실은 정의보다 한 음절 더한 무엇을) 집행하라고 간절히 졸라 대는 것을 멈추게 할 수는 없었다."]], [["But luckily the clerk had a qualification, which no clerk to a justice of peace ought ever to be without, namely, some understanding in the law of this realm.", "그러나 다행히도 그 서기에게는 치안판사의 서기라면 결코 없어서는 안 될 자질이 하나 있었으니, 다름 아니라 이 나라 법에 대한 얼마간의 식견이었다."], ["He therefore whispered in the ear of the justice that he would exceed his authority by committing the girl to Bridewell, as there had been no attempt to break the peace; “for I am afraid, sir,”", "그리하여 그는 판사의 귀에 대고 속삭이기를, 평화를 깨뜨리려는 시도가 전혀 없었으니 저 처녀를 브라이드웰에 가두는 것은 권한을 넘어서는 일이라고 했다. \"송구하오나 나리,\""], ["says he, “you cannot legally commit any one to Bridewell only for ill-breeding.”", "그가 말했다. \"그저 버르장머리가 없다는 이유만으로는 누구도 합법적으로 브라이드웰에 가둘 수 없습니다.\""]], [["In matters of high importance, particularly in cases relating to the game, the justice was not always attentive to these admonitions of his clerk; for, indeed, in executing the laws under that head, many justices of peace suppose they have a large discretionary power, by virtue of which, under the notion of searching for and taking away engines for the destruction of the game, they often commit trespasses, and sometimes felony, at their pleasure.", "매우 중대한 일, 특히 사냥감에 관한 사건에서는 판사가 늘 서기의 이런 충고에 귀를 기울이는 것은 아니었다. 사실 그 항목의 법을 집행할 때면, 많은 치안판사들이 자신에게 폭넓은 재량권이 있다고 여겨, 사냥감을 잡아 없애는 도구를 수색하고 압수한다는 명목 아래 제멋대로 불법 침입을, 때로는 중죄까지도 저지르곤 했다."]], [["But this offence was not of quite so high a nature, nor so dangerous to the society.", "하지만 이번 죄는 그리 무거운 성질의 것이 아니었고, 사회에 그리 위험한 것도 아니었다."], ["Here, therefore, the justice behaved with some attention to the advice of his clerk; for, in fact, he had already had two informations exhibited against him in the King's Bench, and had no curiosity to try a third.", "그러므로 이번만큼은 판사도 서기의 조언에 어느 정도 귀를 기울였다. 실은 이미 왕좌재판소에 그를 고발하는 진정이 두 건이나 들어와 있었던 터라, 세 번째를 시험해 볼 호기심 따위는 없었던 것이다."]], [["The squire, therefore, putting on a most wise and significant countenance, after a preface of several hums and hahs, told his sister, that upon more mature deliberation, he was of opinion, that “as there was no breaking up of the peace, such as the law,”", "그리하여 지주는 더없이 현명하고 의미심장한 표정을 짓고서, 흠흠 하하 하며 몇 번 운을 뗀 뒤, 누이에게 말하기를, 더 무르익게 곰곰이 따져 본 결과 자기 생각으로는 이러하다는 것이었다."], ["says he, “calls breaking open a door, or breaking a hedge, or breaking a head, or any such sort of breaking, the matter did not amount to a felonious kind of a thing, nor trespasses, nor damages, and, therefore, there was no punishment in the law for it.”", "\"법에서 평화를 깨뜨렸다고 부르는 일, 그러니까 문을 부수어 연다거나, 울타리를 부순다거나, 머리통을 깬다거나, 뭐 그런 종류의 깨뜨림이 없었으니, 이 일은 중죄에 해당하는 것도 아니고, 불법 침입도 아니고, 손해를 끼친 것도 아니라네. 그러니 법에 이걸 벌할 처벌이 없단 말일세.\""]], [["Mrs Western said, “she knew the law much better; that she had known servants very severely punished for affronting their masters;”", "웨스턴 양은 \"자기가 법을 훨씬 더 잘 안다\"고 말했다."], ["and then named a certain justice of the peace in London, “who,” she said, “would commit a servant to Bridewell at any time when a master or mistress desired it.”", "\"주인을 모욕했다는 이유로 하인이 아주 호되게 벌받는 걸 본 적이 있다\"고 하면서, 런던의 어느 치안판사를 콕 집어 말하기를, \"그분은 주인이나 안주인이 원하기만 하면 언제든 하인을 브라이드웰에 가둬 버린다\"는 것이었다."]], [["“Like enough,”", "\"그럴 법도 하지,\""], ["cries the squire; “it may be so in London; but the law is different in the country.”", "지주가 외쳤다. \"런던에선 그럴지 모르나, 시골에선 법이 다르거든.\""], ["Here followed a very learned dispute between the brother and sister concerning the law, which we would insert, if we imagined many of our readers could understand it.", "이어서 남매 사이에 법을 둘러싼 매우 학식 깊은 논쟁이 벌어졌는데, 우리 독자들 가운데 그것을 알아들을 만한 이가 많으리라 여겨졌다면 여기 옮겨 적었을 것이다."], ["This was, however, at length referred by both parties to the clerk, who decided it in favour of the magistrate; and Mrs Western was, in the end, obliged to content herself with the satisfaction of having Honour turned away; to which Sophia herself very readily and cheerfully consented.", "어쨌든 이 논쟁은 마침내 양쪽 모두의 합의로 서기에게 판가름을 맡겼고, 서기는 치안판사 쪽에 유리하게 결론을 내렸다. 그리하여 웨스턴 양은 결국 어너를 내쫓는 것으로 만족할 수밖에 없었으니, 이에는 소피아 자신도 아주 선뜻, 기꺼이 동의했다."]], [["Thus Fortune, after having diverted herself, according to custom, with two or three frolicks, at last disposed all matters to the advantage of our heroine; who indeed succeeded admirably well in her deceit, considering it was the first she had ever practised.", "이렇게 운명의 여신은 늘 하던 대로 두세 차례 장난을 치며 즐긴 끝에, 마침내 모든 일을 우리 여주인공에게 유리하게 매듭지어 주었다. 사실 소피아는 난생처음 부려 본 속임수치고는 놀랍도록 훌륭하게 성공한 셈이었다."], ["And, to say the truth, I have often concluded, that the honest part of mankind would be much too hard for the knavish, if they could bring themselves to incur the guilt, or thought it worth their while to take the trouble.", "그리고 솔직히 말하자면, 나는 이런 결론을 자주 내리곤 했다. 만약 정직한 사람들이 죄를 무릅쓸 마음을 먹거나 그만한 수고를 들일 가치가 있다고 여기기만 한다면, 그들은 교활한 자들보다 훨씬 더 능히 이길 것이라고."]], [["Honour acted her part to the utmost perfection.", "어너는 제 역할을 더없이 완벽하게 해냈다."], ["She no sooner saw herself secure from all danger of Bridewell, a word which had raised most horrible ideas in her mind, than she resumed those airs which her terrors before had a little abated; and laid down her place, with as much affectation of content, and indeed of contempt, as was ever practised at the resignation of places of much greater importance.", "그녀의 마음속에 더없이 끔찍한 상상을 불러일으켰던 그 말, 브라이드웰의 위험에서 벗어났음을 알아차리자마자, 그녀는 조금 전 공포에 눌려 잠시 수그러들었던 그 거드름을 도로 되찾았다. 그러고는 자기 자리를 내놓으면서, 훨씬 더 높은 자리를 내려놓을 때에나 어울릴 만큼 만족한 척, 아니 경멸하는 척까지 부려 가며 굴었다."], ["If the reader pleases, therefore, we chuse rather to say she resigned--which hath, indeed, been always held a synonymous expression with being turned out, or turned away.", "그러니 독자께서 괜찮으시다면, 우리는 차라리 그녀가 \"사임했다\"고 말하기로 하겠다. 이 말은 사실 늘 쫓겨났다거나 내쫓겼다는 말과 같은 뜻으로 통해 왔으니 말이다."]], [["Mr Western ordered her to be very expeditious in packing; for his sister declared she would not sleep another night under the same roof with so impudent a slut. To work therefore she went, and that so earnestly, that everything was ready early in the evening; when, having received her wages, away packed bag and baggage, to the great satisfaction of every one, but of none more than of Sophia; who, having appointed her maid to meet her at a certain place not far from the house, exactly at the dreadful and ghostly hour of twelve, began to prepare for her own departure.", "웨스턴 씨는 그녀더러 짐을 어서 싸라고 명했다. 누이가 그런 뻔뻔한 계집과 한 지붕 아래서 하룻밤도 더 자지 않겠다고 선언했기 때문이다. 그리하여 어너는 일에 매달렸고, 어찌나 부지런히 했던지 저녁 일찍 모든 채비가 끝났다. 품삯을 받고 나자 그녀는 봇짐이며 짐가지를 챙겨 떠나갔는데, 이는 모든 이의 큰 만족이었으나 그 누구보다 소피아의 만족이 가장 컸다. 소피아는 하녀에게 집에서 그리 멀지 않은 어느 장소에서 정확히 으스스하고 귀신 나올 법한 자정에 만나기로 일러 두고는, 자신의 출발 채비를 시작했다."]], [["But first she was obliged to give two painful audiences, the one to her aunt, and the other to her father. In these Mrs Western herself began to talk to her in a more peremptory stile than before: but her father treated her in so violent and outrageous a manner, that he frightened her into an affected compliance with his will; which so highly pleased the good squire, that he changed his frowns into smiles, and his menaces into promises: he vowed his whole soul was wrapt in hers; that her consent (for so he construed the words, “You know, sir, I must not, nor can, refuse to obey any absolute command of yours”) had made him the happiest of mankind. He then gave her a large bank-bill to dispose of in any trinkets she pleased, and kissed and embraced her in the fondest manner, while tears of joy trickled from those eyes which a few moments before had darted fire and rage against the dear object of all his affection.", "그러나 그에 앞서 그녀는 두 차례 괴로운 면담을 견뎌야 했으니, 하나는 고모와의 면담이요 다른 하나는 아버지와의 면담이었다. 이 자리에서 웨스턴 양은 전보다 더 단호한 어조로 그녀에게 말하기 시작했다. 그러나 아버지는 어찌나 사납고 무지막지하게 그녀를 다루었던지, 그녀를 겁에 질리게 하여 그 뜻을 따르는 척하게 만들고 말았다. 이에 마음씨 좋은 지주는 어찌나 흡족해했던지, 찌푸린 얼굴을 미소로 바꾸고 으름장을 약속으로 바꾸었다. 그는 자기 온 영혼이 딸의 영혼에 휘감겨 있노라 맹세했고, 딸의 승낙이(그는 \"아버님, 아시다시피 저는 아버님의 절대적인 명령이라면 무엇이든 거역해서도 안 되고 거역할 수도 없습니다\"라는 말을 그렇게 해석했던 것이다) 자신을 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람으로 만들었노라 했다. 그러고는 그녀가 원하는 패물이라면 무엇이든 사라며 큼직한 은행권을 건네주고, 더없이 다정하게 입 맞추고 끌어안았다. 그러는 동안 기쁨의 눈물이 그 두 눈에서 흘러내렸으니, 바로 그 눈이 몇 분 전만 해도 자신의 온 애정이 쏠린 그 사랑스러운 대상을 향해 불길과 분노를 내쏘았던 것이다."]], [["Instances of this behaviour in parents are so common, that the reader, I doubt not, will be very little astonished at the whole conduct of Mr Western.", "부모에게서 이런 행동이 나타나는 사례는 워낙 흔한지라, 독자께서도 웨스턴 씨의 이 모든 처신에 그리 크게 놀라지는 않으시리라 믿는다."], ["If he should, I own I am not able to account for it; since that he loved his daughter most tenderly, is, I think, beyond dispute.", "만약 놀라신다면, 솔직히 나로서는 그것을 설명할 길이 없음을 고백하겠다. 그가 딸을 더없이 살뜰하게 사랑한 것만큼은 다툼의 여지가 없다고 생각하기 때문이다."], ["So indeed have many others, who have rendered their children most completely miserable by the same conduct; which, though it is almost universal in parents, hath always appeared to me to be the most unaccountable of all the absurdities which ever entered into the brain of that strange prodigious creature man.", "실로 다른 많은 이들도 그러했으니, 그들은 바로 이 똑같은 행동으로 제 자식을 더없이 비참하게 만들어 버렸다. 이런 행동은 부모들에게 거의 보편적이긴 하나, 나에게는 늘 인간이라는 저 기이하고 놀라운 피조물의 머릿속에 떠오른 온갖 부조리 가운데서도 가장 설명하기 어려운 것으로 보여 왔다."]], [["The latter part of Mr Western's behaviour had so strong an effect on the tender heart of Sophia, that it suggested a thought to her, which not all the sophistry of her politic aunt, nor all the menaces of her father, had ever once brought into her head.", "웨스턴 씨가 보인 처신의 뒷부분은 소피아의 여린 마음에 어찌나 강하게 와닿았던지, 그녀에게 한 가지 생각을 불러일으켰다. 그것은 약삭빠른 고모의 온갖 궤변으로도, 아버지의 온갖 으름장으로도 단 한 번도 그녀의 머릿속에 떠올려 놓지 못했던 생각이었다."], ["She reverenced her father so piously, and loved him so passionately, that she had scarce ever felt more pleasing sensations, than what arose from the share she frequently had of contributing to his amusement, and sometimes, perhaps, to higher gratifications; for he never could contain the delight of hearing her commended, which he had the satisfaction of hearing almost every day of her life.", "그녀는 아버지를 어찌나 경건하게 우러르고 어찌나 열렬하게 사랑했던지, 그분의 즐거움에—때로는 어쩌면 더 큰 만족에—자주 한몫 보태는 데서 우러나는 것보다 더 기쁜 감정을 거의 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 아버지는 딸이 칭찬받는 소리를 들을 때면 그 기쁨을 도무지 억누르지 못했고, 그 흐뭇한 소리를 거의 매일같이 듣는 행복을 누렸기 때문이다."], ["The idea, therefore, of the immense happiness she should convey to her father by her consent to this match, made a strong impression on her mind.", "그러니 이 혼사에 승낙함으로써 아버지에게 안겨 드릴 더없이 큰 행복을 떠올리자, 그것이 그녀의 마음에 깊은 자국을 남겼다."], ["Again, the extreme piety of such an act of obedience worked very forcibly, as she had a very deep sense of religion.", "또한 그처럼 순종하는 행위에 깃든 더없는 경건함이 무척이나 강하게 작용했으니, 그녀는 신앙심이 아주 깊었기 때문이다."], ["Lastly, when she reflected how much she herself was to suffer, being indeed to become little less than a sacrifice, or a martyr, to filial love and duty, she felt an agreeable tickling in a certain little passion, which though it bears no immediate affinity either to religion or virtue, is often so kind as to lend great assistance in executing the purposes of both.", "끝으로, 자신이 효성과 도리를 위한 제물, 아니 순교자나 다름없는 처지가 되어 얼마나 큰 고통을 겪어야 할지를 곱씹어 보았을 때, 그녀는 어떤 작은 열정 속에서 흐뭇한 간지러움을 느꼈다. 그 열정은 신앙이나 덕과 직접적인 친연이라고는 없으면서도, 곧잘 친절을 베풀어 그 둘의 목적을 이루는 데 큰 도움을 주곤 하는 것이다."]], [["Sophia was charmed with the contemplation of so heroic an action, and began to compliment herself with much premature flattery, when Cupid, who lay hid in her muff, suddenly crept out, and like Punchinello in a puppet-show, kicked all out before him.", "소피아는 그토록 영웅적인 행동을 떠올리는 데 흠뻑 취해, 너무 이른 자화자찬으로 스스로를 추어올리기 시작했다. 그런데 그때, 그녀의 토시 속에 숨어 있던 큐피드가 별안간 기어 나오더니, 인형극의 펀치넬로처럼 앞에 놓인 것을 죄다 걷어차 버렸다."], ["In truth (for we scorn to deceive our reader, or to vindicate the character of our heroine by ascribing her actions to supernatural impulse) the thoughts of her beloved Jones, and some hopes (however distant) in which he was very particularly concerned, immediately destroyed all which filial love, piety, and pride had, with their joint endeavours, been labouring to bring about.", "사실(우리는 독자를 속이거나, 우리 여주인공의 행동을 초자연적인 충동 탓으로 돌려 그녀의 됨됨이를 변호할 마음 따위는 추호도 없으니), 사랑하는 존스에 대한 생각과, 그가 아주 각별히 얽혀 있는 어떤 희망이(아무리 아득한 것일지라도) 곧장, 효성과 경건함과 자존심이 힘을 합쳐 애써 이뤄 놓으려던 그 모든 것을 산산이 무너뜨려 버렸다."]], [["But before we proceed any farther with Sophia, we must now look back to Mr Jones.", "그러나 소피아의 이야기를 더 이어 가기에 앞서, 이제 우리는 존스 씨에게로 눈길을 돌려야겠다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["Containing several matters, natural enough perhaps, but low.", "독자께서는 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다."], ["The reader will be pleased to remember, that we left Mr Jones, in the beginning of this book, on his road to Bristol; being determined to seek his fortune at sea, or rather, indeed, to fly away from his fortune on shore.", "우리는 이 권의 첫머리에서 존스 씨를 브리스틀로 가는 길 위에 두고 떠났다. 그는 바다에서 운을 찾아보겠다고, 아니 사실은 뭍에서의 자기 운명으로부터 달아나겠다고 마음을 굳혔던 것이다."]], [["It happened (a thing not very unusual), that the guide who undertook to conduct him on his way, was unluckily unacquainted with the road; so that having missed his right track, and being ashamed to ask information, he rambled about backwards and forwards till night came on, and it began to grow dark. Jones suspecting what had happened, acquainted the guide with his apprehensions; but he insisted on it, that they were in the right road, and added, it would be very strange if he should not know the road to Bristol; though, in reality, it would have been much stranger if he had known it, having never past through it in his life before.", "그런데 (그리 드문 일은 아니지만) 마침 그를 안내하기로 한 길잡이가 공교롭게도 그 길을 잘 알지 못했다. 그래서 옳은 길을 놓치고도 부끄러워 차마 물어보지 못한 채 앞으로 갔다 뒤로 갔다 헤매다 보니 어느새 밤이 되어 사방이 어두워지기 시작했다. 존스는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 짐작하고는 길잡이에게 제 걱정을 털어놓았다. 그러나 길잡이는 우리가 바른 길에 있다고 우겼고, 자기가 브리스틀로 가는 길을 모른다면 그것이야말로 참 이상한 일이라고 덧붙였다. 사실은, 평생 그 길을 한 번도 지나본 적이 없었으니, 그가 그 길을 안다면 그게 훨씬 더 이상한 일이었겠지만 말이다."]], [["Jones had not such implicit faith in his guide, but that on their arrival at a village he inquired of the first fellow he saw, whether they were in the road to Bristol. “Whence did you come?” cries the fellow. “No matter,” says Jones, a little hastily; “I want to know if this be the road to Bristol?”--“The road to Bristol!” cries the fellow, scratching his head: “why, measter, I believe you will hardly get to Bristol this way to-night.”--“Prithee, friend, then,” answered Jones, “do tell us which is the way.”--“Why, measter,” cries the fellow, “you must be come out of your road the Lord knows whither; for thick way goeth to Glocester.”--“Well, and which way goes to Bristol?” said Jones. “Why, you be going away from Bristol,” answered the fellow. “Then,” said Jones, “we must go back again?”--“Ay, you must,” said the fellow. “Well, and when we come back to the top of the hill, which way must we take?”--“Why, you must keep the strait road.”--“But I remember there are two roads, one to the right and the other to the left.”--“Why, you must keep the right-hand road, and then gu strait vorwards; only remember to turn vurst to your right, and then to your left again, and then to your right, and that brings you to the squire's; and then you must keep strait vorwards, and turn to the left.”", "존스는 길잡이를 그렇게까지 곧이곧대로 믿지는 않았기에, 어느 마을에 다다르자 처음 마주친 사내에게 여기가 브리스틀로 가는 길이 맞느냐고 물었다. \"어디서 오셨수?\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"그건 됐고,\" 존스가 조금 성급하게 말했다. \"이 길이 브리스틀로 가는 길인지 알고 싶을 뿐이오.\" \"브리스틀로 가는 길!\" 사내가 머리를 긁적이며 외쳤다. \"아니, 나리, 이 길로는 오늘 밤 브리스틀에 가기 어려우실 거구먼유.\" \"그럼 친구여,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"어느 쪽이 길인지 좀 일러주게.\" \"그게 말이유, 나리,\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"하느님만 아실 어딘가로 길을 잘못 드신 게 분명해유. 저쪽 길은 글로스터로 가는 길이니께유.\" \"그래, 그럼 어느 쪽이 브리스틀로 가는가?\" 존스가 말했다. \"아니, 나리는 지금 브리스틀에서 멀어지고 계시잖유.\" 사내가 대답했다. \"그럼,\" 존스가 말했다. \"우리가 다시 되돌아가야 한다는 말인가?\" \"아믄유, 그래야쥬.\" 사내가 말했다. \"좋아, 그럼 언덕 꼭대기로 되돌아가면 어느 길로 가야 하나?\" \"그야 곧장 난 길로 가셔야쥬.\" \"한데 내 기억엔 길이 둘이던데, 하나는 오른쪽, 하나는 왼쪽으로.\" \"그야 오른쪽 길로 가셔서 곧장 앞으로 가셔유. 다만 첨엔 오른쪽으로 돌고, 그담엔 또 왼쪽으로, 그담엔 오른쪽으로 돌면, 그러면 향사 댁이 나와유. 거기서 또 곧장 앞으로 가다가 왼쪽으로 도시면 돼유.\""]], [["Another fellow now came up, and asked which way the gentlemen were going; of which being informed by Jones, he first scratched his head, and then leaning upon a pole he had in his hand, began to tell him, “That he must keep the right-hand road for about a mile, or a mile and a half, or such a matter, and then he must turn short to the left, which would bring him round by Measter Jin Bearnes's.”--“But which is Mr John Bearnes's?”", "이제 또 다른 사내가 다가오더니 신사분들이 어느 쪽으로 가시느냐고 물었다. 존스에게서 그 말을 듣자 그는 먼저 머리를 긁적이고는, 손에 들고 있던 막대기에 몸을 기대고 이렇게 일러주기 시작했다."], ["says Jones.", "\"오른쪽 길로 한 일 마일, 아니 일 마일 반쯤, 뭐 그쯤 가셔야 해유. 그담에 왼쪽으로 바짝 꺾으면, 진 베언스 나리 댁을 빙 돌아 나오게 돼유.\""], ["“O Lord!”", "\"한데 존 베언스 씨가 누구인가?\" 존스가 말했다."], ["cries the fellow, “why, don't you know Measter Jin Bearnes?", "\"이런 세상에!\" 사내가 외쳤다."], ["Whence then did you come?”", "\"아니, 진 베언스 나리를 모르신단 말이유? 그럼 대체 어디서 오신 거유?\""]], [["These two fellows had almost conquered the patience of Jones, when a plain well-looking man (who was indeed a Quaker) accosted him thus: “Friend, I perceive thou hast lost thy way; and if thou wilt take my advice, thou wilt not attempt to find it to-night.", "이 두 사내는 존스의 인내심을 거의 바닥내고 말았는데, 그때 행색이 단정하고 사람 좋아 보이는 한 남자가(실은 퀘이커교도였다) 그에게 이렇게 말을 걸었다. \"벗이여, 보아하니 길을 잃으신 모양이오."], ["It is almost dark, and the road is difficult to hit; besides, there have been several robberies committed lately between this and Bristol.", "내 충고를 들으시려거든, 오늘 밤은 그 길을 찾으려 들지 마시오. 거의 어두워졌고, 길은 찾기가 어렵소."], ["Here is a very creditable good house just by, where thou may'st find good entertainment for thyself and thy cattle till morning.”", "게다가 요즈음 여기서 브리스틀 사이에 노상강도가 여럿 일어났소이다. 바로 곁에 아주 믿을 만한 좋은 집이 있으니, 거기서 아침까지 그대와 그대의 말이 편히 묵을 수 있을 것이오.\""], ["Jones, after a little persuasion, agreed to stay in this place till the morning, and was conducted by his friend to the public-house.", "존스는 조금 설득당한 끝에 아침까지 이곳에 머물기로 했고, 그 벗의 안내를 받아 여관으로 갔다."]], [["The landlord, who was a very civil fellow, told Jones, “He hoped he would excuse the badness of his accommodation; for that his wife was gone from home, and had locked up almost everything, and carried the keys along with her.” Indeed the fact was, that a favourite daughter of hers was just married, and gone that morning home with her husband; and that she and her mother together had almost stript the poor man of all his goods, as well as money; for though he had several children, this daughter only, who was the mother's favourite, was the object of her consideration; and to the humour of this one child she would with pleasure have sacrificed all the rest, and her husband into the bargain.", "여관 주인은 무척 점잖은 사람이었는데, 존스에게 이렇게 말했다. \"변변치 못한 잠자리를 부디 너그러이 봐주시길 바랍니다요. 안사람이 집을 비웠는데, 거의 모든 걸 잠가놓고 열쇠를 죄다 가져가버렸지 뭡니까요.\" 사실인즉, 그의 아끼는 딸이 막 혼인을 해서 그날 아침 남편을 따라 집으로 떠났던 것이다. 그리고 딸과 그 어미가 함께 이 가엾은 남자에게서 돈은 물론이요 세간까지 거의 다 벗겨가다시피 한 터였다. 그에게는 자식이 여럿 있었지만, 어미가 마음에 두는 것은 오직 그가 가장 사랑하는 이 딸 하나뿐이었으니, 이 한 자식의 비위를 맞추기 위해서라면 나머지 자식들은 물론이고 남편까지 덤으로 기꺼이 희생시켰을 사람이었다."]], [["Though Jones was very unfit for any kind of company, and would have preferred being alone, yet he could not resist the importunities of the honest Quaker; who was the more desirous of sitting with him, from having remarked the melancholy which appeared both in his countenance and behaviour; and which the poor Quaker thought his conversation might in some measure relieve.", "존스는 누구와도 어울릴 처지가 못 되어 차라리 혼자 있고 싶었지만, 그래도 이 정직한 퀘이커교도의 끈질긴 청을 뿌리칠 수가 없었다. 퀘이커교도는 존스의 얼굴과 거동 모두에 깃든 우울을 알아차렸기에 더욱 그와 함께 앉아 있고 싶어했고, 가엾게도 자신의 이야기가 그 우울을 얼마쯤 덜어주지 않을까 여겼던 것이다."]], [["After they had past some time together, in such a manner that my honest friend might have thought himself at one of his silent meetings, the Quaker began to be moved by some spirit or other, probably that of curiosity, and said, “Friend, I perceive some sad disaster hath befallen thee; but pray be of comfort.", "그들이 한동안 함께 앉아 있었는데, 그 모양새가 어찌나 조용했던지 나의 이 정직한 벗은 자기네 침묵 집회 가운데 하나에 와 있다고 여겼을 법했다. 그러던 차에 퀘이커교도는 어떤 영(靈)인가에, 아마도 호기심의 영에 이끌려 입을 열었다."], ["Perhaps thou hast lost a friend.", "\"벗이여, 보아하니 그대에게 무슨 슬픈 재난이 닥친 모양이오."], ["If so, thou must consider we are all mortal.", "허나 부디 마음을 다잡으시오. 어쩌면 벗을 잃으신 게로군요."], ["And why shouldst thou grieve, when thou knowest thy grief will do thy friend no good?", "그렇다면, 우리 모두 언젠가 죽을 몸임을 생각하셔야 하오. 그대의 슬픔이 그대의 벗에게 아무 도움도 되지 못함을 알면서, 어찌하여 슬퍼하시오?"], ["We are all born to affliction.", "우리는 다 고난을 짊어지고 태어났소."], ["I myself have my sorrows as well as thee, and most probably greater sorrows.", "나 또한 그대 못지않은 슬픔을 안고 있으며, 아마 그대보다 더 큰 슬픔일 게요."], ["Though I have a clear estate of £100 a year, which is as much as I want, and I have a conscience, I thank the Lord, void of offence; my constitution is sound and strong, and there is no man can demand a debt of me, nor accuse me of an injury; yet, friend, I should be concerned to think thee as miserable as myself.”", "비록 나는 한 해에 백 파운드씩 나오는 깨끗한 재산이 있어 모자람이 없고, 주님께 감사하게도 거리낌 없는 양심을 지녔으며, 몸도 튼튼하고 건강하여 내게 빚을 갚으라 할 사람도, 내가 해를 끼쳤다고 탓할 사람도 없소이다. 그런데도 벗이여, 그대가 나만큼이나 비참하다고 생각하니 마음이 아프오.\""]], [["Here the Quaker ended with a deep sigh; and Jones presently answered, “I am very sorry, sir, for your unhappiness, whatever is the occasion of it.”--“Ah! friend,” replied the Quaker, “one only daughter is the occasion; one who was my greatest delight upon earth, and who within this week is run away from me, and is married against my consent. I had provided her a proper match, a sober man and one of substance; but she, forsooth, would chuse for herself, and away she is gone with a young fellow not worth a groat. If she had been dead, as I suppose thy friend is, I should have been happy.”--“That is very strange, sir,” said Jones. “Why, would it not be better for her to be dead, than to be a beggar?” replied the Quaker: “for, as I told you, the fellow is not worth a groat; and surely she cannot expect that I shall ever give her a shilling. No, as she hath married for love, let her live on love if she can; let her carry her love to market, and see whether any one will change it into silver, or even into halfpence.”--“You know your own concerns best, sir,” said Jones. “It must have been,” continued the Quaker, “a long premeditated scheme to cheat me: for they have known one another from their infancy; and I always preached to her against love, and told her a thousand times over it was all folly and wickedness. Nay, the cunning slut pretended to hearken to me, and to despise all wantonness of the flesh; and yet at last broke out at a window two pair of stairs: for I began, indeed, a little to suspect her, and had locked her up carefully, intending the very next morning to have married her up to my liking. But she disappointed me within a few hours, and escaped away to the lover of her own chusing; who lost no time, for they were married and bedded and all within an hour. But it shall be the worst hour's work for them both that ever they did; for they may starve, or beg, or steal together, for me. I will never give either of them a farthing.” Here Jones starting up cried, “I really must be excused: I wish you would leave me.”--“Come, come, friend,” said the Quaker, “don't give way to concern. You see there are other people miserable besides yourself.”--“I see there are madmen, and fools, and villains in the world,” cries Jones. “But let me give you a piece of advice: send for your daughter and son-in-law home, and don't be yourself the only cause of misery to one you pretend to love.”--“Send for her and her husband home!” cries the Quaker loudly; “I would sooner send for the two greatest enemies I have in the world!”--“Well, go home yourself, or where you please,” said Jones, “for I will sit no longer in such company.”--“Nay, friend,” answered the Quaker, “I scorn to impose my company on any one.” He then offered to pull money from his pocket, but Jones pushed him with some violence out of the room.", "여기서 퀘이커교도는 깊은 한숨으로 말을 맺었고, 존스는 곧바로 대답했다. \"선생, 무엇이 그 까닭이든 선생의 불행이 참으로 안타깝습니다.\" \"아아! 벗이여,\" 퀘이커교도가 답했다. \"그 까닭은 외동딸 하나라오. 이 세상에서 내 가장 큰 기쁨이던 그 아이가, 이 한 주 안에 내게서 달아나 내 뜻을 거스르고 혼인을 해버렸소. 나는 그 아이에게 마땅한 짝을 정해두었으니, 점잖고 살림도 넉넉한 사람이었소. 그런데 그 아이는, 어이없게도, 제 멋대로 고르겠다며 한 푼어치도 안 되는 젊은 녀석과 함께 떠나버렸소. 만일 그 아이가, 짐작건대 그대의 벗처럼, 차라리 죽었더라면 나는 행복했을 거요.\" \"그것 참 이상한 말씀이군요, 선생.\" 존스가 말했다. \"아니, 거지가 되느니 차라리 죽는 편이 그 아이에게 낫지 않겠소?\" 퀘이커교도가 대답했다. \"내 말했듯이, 그 녀석은 한 푼어치도 안 되는 자요. 그러니 내가 그 아이에게 한 닢이라도 줄 거라곤 기대할 수 없겠지요. 안 되오, 사랑을 좇아 혼인을 했으니, 할 수 있거든 사랑으로 먹고살라지요. 사랑을 장에 들고 나가서, 누가 그걸 은화로, 아니 반 푼짜리 동전으로라도 바꿔주는지 어디 보라지요.\" \"선생의 일은 선생이 가장 잘 아시겠지요.\" 존스가 말했다. \"이건 분명,\" 퀘이커교도가 말을 이었다. \"나를 속이려고 오래전부터 꾸민 흉계였을 게요. 그 둘은 어릴 적부터 서로 알고 지냈으니까요. 나는 늘 그 아이에게 사랑이란 걸 멀리하라 타일렀고, 그건 다 어리석고 사악한 짓이라고 수천 번도 더 일러주었소. 아니, 그 교활한 계집애는 내 말을 새겨듣는 척, 육신의 온갖 음탕함을 경멸하는 척하더니, 결국엔 이 층 창문으로 뛰어내려 달아나버렸소. 실은 내가 그 아이를 조금 의심하기 시작해서, 바로 다음 날 아침에 내 마음에 드는 짝과 혼인시킬 작정으로 단단히 가둬두었던 거요. 그런데 몇 시간도 못 되어 그 아이는 나를 저버리고, 제가 고른 그 정인에게로 달아나버렸소. 그 녀석도 꾸물대지 않아서, 그 둘은 한 시간 안에 혼인하고 잠자리까지 다 치러버렸소. 허나 그건 그 둘이 평생 한 일 중에 가장 못된 한 시간의 짓거리가 될 게요. 나로 말하자면, 그 둘이 함께 굶든, 빌어먹든, 도둑질을 하든 상관없소. 나는 결코 그 둘 중 누구에게도 한 푼도 주지 않을 테니.\" 여기서 존스가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"정말이지 양해를 구해야겠소. 나를 좀 혼자 있게 해주시오.\" \"이런, 이런, 벗이여,\" 퀘이커교도가 말했다. \"근심에 그리 휩쓸리지 마시오. 보다시피 이 세상엔 그대 말고도 비참한 사람들이 있다오.\" \"이 세상엔 미친 자들과, 바보들과, 악당들이 있다는 건 알겠소.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"허나 내 한마디 충고하겠소. 따님과 사위를 집으로 불러들이시오. 그리고 사랑한다 말하는 사람에게 비참함을 안기는 유일한 장본인이 선생 자신이 되지 마시오.\" \"그 아이와 그 남편을 집으로 불러들이라니!\" 퀘이커교도가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"그럴 바엔 차라리 이 세상에서 가장 미워하는 두 원수를 부르고 말겠소!\" \"그렇다면 선생이나 집으로 가시오, 아니면 가고 싶은 데로 가시든가.\" 존스가 말했다. \"나는 더 이상 그런 사람과 함께 앉아 있지 않겠소.\" \"아니, 벗이여,\" 퀘이커교도가 대답했다. \"내 함께 있어달라고 누군가에게 떼쓰는 건 질색이오.\" 그러고는 주머니에서 돈을 꺼내려 했으나, 존스가 다소 거칠게 그를 방 밖으로 떠밀어냈다."]], [["The subject of the Quaker's discourse had so deeply affected Jones, that he stared very wildly all the time he was speaking.", "퀘이커교도가 늘어놓은 이야기의 주제가 존스의 마음을 어찌나 깊이 흔들어놓았던지, 그는 상대가 말하는 내내 사뭇 사납게 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었다."], ["This the Quaker had observed, and this, added to the rest of his behaviour, inspired honest Broadbrim with a conceit, that his companion was in reality out of his senses.", "퀘이커교도는 이를 알아차렸고, 거기에 더해 그의 나머지 거동까지 겹쳐 보고는, 이 정직한 챙 넓은 모자 양반은 제 길동무가 정말로 제정신이 아니라는 생각에 사로잡히고 말았다."], ["Instead of resenting the affront, therefore, the Quaker was moved with compassion for his unhappy circumstances; and having communicated his opinion to the landlord, he desired him to take great care of his guest, and to treat him with the highest civility.", "그래서 그 모욕에 분개하기는커녕, 퀘이커교도는 그 불행한 처지를 가엾이 여겨 마음이 움직였다. 그리하여 자신의 견해를 여관 주인에게 일러주며, 손님을 각별히 잘 보살피고 더없이 정중하게 대접하라고 당부했다."]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"천만에요,\" 여관 주인이 말했다."], ["says the landlord, “I shall use no such civility towards him; for it seems, for all his laced waistcoat there, he is no more a gentleman than myself, but a poor parish bastard, bred up at a great squire's about thirty miles off, and now turned out of doors (not for any good to be sure).", "\"그자한테 그런 정중함 따윈 베풀지 않을 겁니다요. 보아하니 저기 레이스 달린 조끼를 걸쳤어도, 저자는 나보다 나을 게 없는 신사기는커녕, 한 삼십 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 대단한 향사 댁에서 자라난 가난한 교구 사생아일 뿐이고, 지금은 (틀림없이 좋은 일로는 아닐 테고) 집에서 쫓겨난 신세랍니다요."], ["I shall get him out of my house as soon as possible.", "되도록 빨리 저자를 내 집에서 내보내야겠어요."], ["If I do lose my reckoning, the first loss is always the best.", "숙박비를 떼인다 해도, 첫 손해가 늘 가장 나은 손해인 법이니까요."], ["It is not above a year ago that I lost a silver spoon.”", "일 년도 채 안 됐어요, 내가 은수저 하나를 잃어버린 게.\""]], [["“What dost thou talk of a parish bastard, Robin?”", "\"교구 사생아라니 무슨 소릴 하는 게요, 로빈?\""], ["answered the Quaker.", "퀘이커교도가 대답했다."], ["“Thou must certainly be mistaken in thy man.”", "\"분명 사람을 잘못 본 게요.\""]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"천만에요,\""], ["replied Robin; “the guide, who knows him very well, told it me.”", "로빈이 대답했다. \"저자를 아주 잘 아는 길잡이가 내게 일러준 겁니다요.\""], ["For, indeed, the guide had no sooner taken his place at the kitchen fire, than he acquainted the whole company with all he knew or had ever heard concerning Jones.", "사실 그 길잡이는 부엌 화롯가에 자리를 잡자마자, 존스에 관해 자기가 알거나 들어본 모든 것을 거기 모인 사람들에게 죄다 늘어놓았던 것이다."]], [["The Quaker was no sooner assured by this fellow of the birth and low fortune of Jones, than all compassion for him vanished; and the honest plain man went home fired with no less indignation than a duke would have felt at receiving an affront from such a person.", "이 사내에게서 존스의 출생과 보잘것없는 처지를 확인하기가 무섭게, 퀘이커교도의 가엾어하던 마음은 죄다 사라져버렸다. 그리고 이 정직하고 소박한 남자는, 공작이 그런 사람에게서 모욕을 당했을 때 느꼈을 법한 노여움 못지않은 분노에 불타올라 집으로 돌아가버렸다."]], [["The landlord himself conceived an equal disdain for his guest; so that when Jones rung the bell in order to retire to bed, he was acquainted that he could have no bed there.", "여관 주인 또한 자기 손님에게 똑같은 멸시를 품게 되었다. 그래서 존스가 잠자리에 들려고 종을 울리자, 여기엔 내줄 잠자리가 없다는 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["Besides disdain of the mean condition of his guest, Robin entertained violent suspicion of his intentions, which were, he supposed, to watch some favourable opportunity of robbing the house.", "손님의 비천한 처지를 멸시하는 데다, 로빈은 그자의 속셈에 대해서도 격한 의심을 품고 있었다. 그자가 집을 털 만한 좋은 기회를 노리고 있다고 짐작한 것이다."], ["In reality, he might have been very well eased of these apprehensions, by the prudent precautions of his wife and daughter, who had already removed everything which was not fixed to the freehold; but he was by nature suspicious, and had been more particularly so since the loss of his spoon.", "사실 그는 이런 걱정을 충분히 덜 수도 있었을 것이다. 아내와 딸이 신중하게도 부동산에 붙박이로 딸린 것 말고는 이미 모조리 치워버렸으니 말이다."], ["In short, the dread of being robbed totally absorbed the comfortable consideration that he had nothing to lose.", "하지만 그는 천성이 의심이 많았고, 은수저를 잃은 뒤로는 유독 더 그러했다. 한마디로, 도둑맞을지 모른다는 두려움이, 사실은 잃을 것이 아무것도 없다는 마음 편한 생각을 송두리째 집어삼켜버린 것이다."]], [["Jones being assured that he could have no bed, very contentedly betook himself to a great chair made with rushes, when sleep, which had lately shunned his company in much better apartments, generously paid him a visit in his humble cell.", "존스는 잠자리를 얻을 수 없다는 말을 듣고서, 골풀로 엮은 커다란 의자에 아주 흡족하게 몸을 맡겼다. 그러자 요즘 들어 훨씬 더 좋은 방에서도 그를 피하던 잠이, 이 초라한 골방으로 너그러이 그를 찾아와주었다."]], [["As for the landlord, he was prevented by his fears from retiring to rest.", "여관 주인으로 말하자면, 그는 두려움 탓에 잠자리에 들지 못했다."], ["He returned therefore to the kitchen fire, whence he could survey the only door which opened into the parlour, or rather hole, where Jones was seated; and as for the window to that room, it was impossible for any creature larger than a cat to have made his escape through it.", "그래서 부엌 화롯가로 돌아갔는데, 거기서는 존스가 앉아 있는 거실로, 아니 거실이라기보다는 굴이라 할 만한 방으로 통하는 단 하나뿐인 문을 지켜볼 수 있었다. 그 방의 창문으로 말하자면, 고양이보다 큰 짐승이라면 도저히 그리로 빠져나갈 수 없을 만큼 작았다."]], [["The adventure of a company of soldiers.", "군인 무리에 얽힌 모험."]], [["The landlord having taken his seat directly opposite to the door of the parlour, determined to keep guard there the whole night.", "여관 주인은 곧장 응접실 문 맞은편에 자리를 잡고는, 밤새도록 그곳에서 보초를 서기로 마음먹었다."], ["The guide and another fellow remained long on duty with him, though they neither knew his suspicions, nor had any of their own.", "길잡이와 또 다른 사내 하나가 그와 함께 오래도록 그 임무에 붙어 있었으나, 두 사람은 주인의 의심을 알지도 못했고 제 나름의 의심을 품은 것도 아니었다."], ["The true cause of their watching did, indeed, at length, put an end to it; for this was no other than the strength and goodness of the beer, of which having tippled a very large quantity, they grew at first very noisy and vociferous, and afterwards fell both asleep.", "사실 그들이 자리를 지킨 진짜 이유는 끝내 그 보초 서기를 끝장내고 말았으니, 그 이유란 다름 아닌 맥주의 힘과 그 맛이 좋았다는 데 있었다. 어마어마한 양을 들이켠 두 사람은 처음에는 몹시 시끄럽고 떠들썩해지더니, 이윽고 둘 다 곯아떨어지고 말았다."]], [["But it was not in the power of liquor to compose the fears of Robin. He continued still waking in his chair, with his eyes fixed stedfastly on the door which led into the apartment of Mr Jones, till a violent thundering at his outward gate called him from his seat, and obliged him to open it; which he had no sooner done, than his kitchen was immediately full of gentlemen in red coats, who all rushed upon him in as tumultuous a manner as if they intended to take his little castle by storm.", "그러나 술의 힘으로도 로빈의 두려움만은 잠재울 수 없었다. 그는 여전히 의자에 앉은 채 깨어 있었고, 두 눈을 존스 씨의 방으로 통하는 문에 똑바로 못 박고 있었다. 그러던 차에 바깥문을 거세게 두드리는 소리가 그를 자리에서 불러냈고, 문을 열지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 그가 문을 열기가 무섭게, 그의 부엌은 곧장 붉은 군복을 입은 신사들로 가득 찼다. 그들은 마치 그의 작은 성을 기습으로 함락시키려는 듯, 한꺼번에 우르르 그에게 들이닥쳤다."]], [["The landlord was now forced from his post to furnish his numerous guests with beer, which they called for with great eagerness; and upon his second or third return from the cellar, he saw Mr Jones standing before the fire in the midst of the soldiers; for it may easily be believed, that the arrival of so much good company should put an end to any sleep, unless that from which we are to be awakened only by the last trumpet.", "이제 여관 주인은 자기 자리를 떠나, 그 숱한 손님들에게 맥주를 내올 수밖에 없었다. 손님들은 무척이나 다급하게 맥주를 외쳐 댔다. 그가 두 번째인지 세 번째인지 지하 저장고에서 돌아왔을 때, 그는 군인들 한가운데 불 앞에 서 있는 존스 씨를 보았다. 이렇게 좋은 일행이 잔뜩 들이닥쳤으니, 어지간한 잠은 다 달아나기 마련이라는 것은 쉬이 믿을 만한 일이다. 마지막 나팔 소리에야 비로소 깨어날 그런 잠이 아니라면 말이다."]], [["The company having now pretty well satisfied their thirst, nothing remained but to pay the reckoning, a circumstance often productive of much mischief and discontent among the inferior rank of gentry, who are apt to find great difficulty in assessing the sum, with exact regard to distributive justice, which directs that every man shall pay according to the quantity which he drinks. This difficulty occurred upon the present occasion; and it was the greater, as some gentlemen had, in their extreme hurry, marched off, after their first draught, and had entirely forgot to contribute anything towards the said reckoning.", "이제 일행은 갈증을 어지간히 푼 터라, 남은 일이라곤 셈을 치르는 것뿐이었다. 그런데 이것은 신분이 낮은 축의 신사들 사이에서 흔히 적잖은 말썽과 불만을 낳는 일이다. 그들은 분배의 정의에 정확히 맞추어 액수를 매기는 데 큰 어려움을 겪기 일쑤니, 그 정의란 사람마다 자기가 마신 양에 따라 값을 치러야 한다고 일러 주는 법이다. 이번에도 그 어려움이 닥쳤다. 게다가 어떤 신사들은 어찌나 급했던지 첫 잔을 들이켠 뒤 곧장 행군해 가 버려서, 그 셈에 한 푼 보태는 일조차 까맣게 잊은 터라 어려움은 한층 더 컸다."]], [["A violent dispute now arose, in which every word may be said to have been deposed upon oath; for the oaths were at least equal to all the other words spoken.", "이제 격렬한 다툼이 일어났는데, 그 자리에서 오간 모든 말은 가히 선서를 곁들여 진술된 것이라 해도 좋았다. 그도 그럴 것이, 욕설이 적어도 다른 모든 말의 수만큼은 되었기 때문이다."], ["In this controversy the whole company spoke together, and every man seemed wholly bent to extenuate the sum which fell to his share; so that the most probable conclusion which could be foreseen was, that a large portion of the reckoning would fall to the landlord's share to pay, or (what is much the same thing) would remain unpaid.", "이 입씨름에서 일행은 모두가 한꺼번에 떠들어 댔고, 저마다 제 몫으로 떨어지는 액수를 깎아 보려고 온통 안간힘을 쓰는 듯했다. 그러니 미루어 짐작할 수 있는 가장 그럴듯한 결말이란, 셈의 상당 부분이 여관 주인의 몫으로 떨어져 그가 갚게 되거나, 아니면 (사실상 같은 말이지만) 끝내 받지 못한 채 남게 되리라는 것이었다."]], [["All this while Mr Jones was engaged in conversation with the serjeant; for that officer was entirely unconcerned in the present dispute, being privileged by immemorial custom from all contribution.", "이러는 내내 존스 씨는 하사와 대화를 나누고 있었다. 그 하사관은 지금의 다툼과는 전혀 무관했으니, 아득한 옛 관습에 따라 어떤 셈에서도 면제받는 특권을 누렸기 때문이다."]], [["The dispute now grew so very warm that it seemed to draw towards a military decision, when Jones, stepping forward, silenced all their clamours at once, by declaring that he would pay the whole reckoning, which indeed amounted to no more than three shillings and fourpence.", "다툼이 어찌나 뜨거워졌던지, 이제 막 무력으로 판가름이 날 지경에 이르렀다. 그때 존스가 앞으로 나서서, 자기가 셈을 전부 치르겠노라 선언하자 그들의 떠들썩한 소동이 단번에 잠잠해졌다. 그 셈이란 실은 통틀어 3실링 4펜스밖에 되지 않았다."]], [["This declaration procured Jones the thanks and applause of the whole company.", "이 선언으로 존스는 일행 전부의 고마움과 갈채를 한 몸에 받았다."], ["The terms honourable, noble, and worthy gentleman, resounded through the room; nay, my landlord himself began to have a better opinion of him, and almost to disbelieve the account which the guide had given.", "명예로운 분, 고귀한 분, 훌륭한 신사라는 말들이 방 안에 울려 퍼졌다. 아니, 여관 주인 자신마저 그를 더 좋게 보기 시작했고, 길잡이가 들려준 이야기를 거의 믿지 않게 될 지경이었다."]], [["The serjeant had informed Mr Jones that they were marching against the rebels, and expected to be commanded by the glorious Duke of Cumberland.", "하사는 존스 씨에게, 자기들이 반란군을 토벌하러 행군하는 길이며, 영광스러운 컴벌랜드 공작의 지휘를 받게 되리라 기대한다고 일러 주었다. 이로써 독자는 (지금까지 굳이 알릴 필요가 없다고 여겼던 한 가지 사정을) 알아챌 수 있을 것이다."], ["By which the reader may perceive (a circumstance which we have not thought necessary to communicate before) that this was the very time when the late rebellion was at the highest; and indeed the banditti were now marched into England, intending, as it was thought, to fight the king's forces, and to attempt pushing forward to the metropolis.", "즉 이때야말로 지난날의 그 반란이 한창 절정에 이르렀던 바로 그 무렵이었던 것이다. 실로 그 도적 떼는 이제 잉글랜드 안으로 진군해 들어와, 사람들 짐작으로는 국왕의 군대와 맞서 싸우고 수도까지 밀고 나아가려는 참이었다."]], [["Jones had some heroic ingredients in his composition, and was a hearty well-wisher to the glorious cause of liberty, and of the Protestant religion.", "존스의 됨됨이에는 영웅다운 기질이 얼마간 섞여 있었고, 그는 자유와 신교라는 영광스러운 대의를 진심으로 빌어 주는 사람이었다."], ["It is no wonder, therefore, that in circumstances which would have warranted a much more romantic and wild undertaking, it should occur to him to serve as a volunteer in this expedition.", "그러니 훨씬 더 무모하고 거친 일을 벌인다 해도 정당하다 할 만한 이런 상황에서, 그가 이번 원정에 의용병으로 나서 볼 생각을 떠올린 것도 이상한 일은 아니다."]], [["Our commanding officer had said all in his power to encourage and promote this good disposition, from the first moment he had been acquainted with it.", "우리의 지휘관은 그 좋은 마음가짐을 알게 된 첫 순간부터, 그것을 북돋우고 부추기느라 할 수 있는 말은 다 했다."], ["He now proclaimed the noble resolution aloud, which was received with great pleasure by the whole company, who all cried out, “God bless King George and your honour;”", "그는 이제 그 갸륵한 결심을 큰 소리로 알렸고, 일행 모두가 이를 크게 기뻐하며 받아들여 한목소리로 외쳤다. \"조지 국왕 폐하와 나리께 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\""], ["and then added, with many oaths, “We will stand by you both to the last drops of our blood.”", "그러고는 숱한 욕설을 곁들여 덧붙였다. \"우리는 마지막 피 한 방울이 다할 때까지 두 분 곁을 지키겠습니다!\""]], [["The gentleman who had been all night tippling at the alehouse, was prevailed on by some arguments which a corporal had put into his hands, to undertake the same expedition.", "밤새도록 술집에서 진탕 마셔 댄 그 신사는, 한 상병이 그의 손에 쥐여 준 몇 마디 설득에 넘어가 같은 원정에 나서기로 했다. 그리고 이제 존스 씨의 여행 가방이 짐수레에 실리고, 부대가 막 앞으로 움직이려던 참이었다."], ["And now the portmanteau belonging to Mr Jones being put up in the baggage-cart, the forces were about to move forwards; when the guide, stepping up to Jones, said, “Sir, I hope you will consider that the horses have been kept out all night, and we have travelled a great ways out of our way.”", "그때 길잡이가 존스에게 다가와 말했다. \"나리, 말들이 밤새도록 바깥에 매여 있었고, 우리가 길을 한참이나 벗어나 멀리 왔다는 점을 헤아려 주셨으면 합니다.\""], ["Jones was surprized at the impudence of this demand, and acquainted the soldiers with the merits of his cause, who were all unanimous in condemning the guide for his endeavours to put upon a gentleman.", "존스는 이 뻔뻔한 요구에 어이가 없어, 군인들에게 자기 사정의 정당함을 일러 주었다. 그러자 그들은 신사를 등쳐 먹으려 든 길잡이를 한목소리로 나무랐다."], ["Some said, he ought to be tied neck and heels; others that he deserved to run the gantlope; and the serjeant shook his cane at him, and wished he had him under his command, swearing heartily he would make an example of him.", "누구는 그를 목과 발을 한데 묶어 두어야 한다고 했고, 또 누구는 태형의 늘어선 매를 맞아야 마땅하다고 했다. 하사는 그에게 지팡이를 흔들어 보이며, 그가 제 휘하에 있었으면 좋겠다고, 본때를 보여 주겠노라 한껏 욕설을 퍼부었다."]], [["Jones contented himself however with a negative punishment, and walked off with his new comrades, leaving the guide to the poor revenge of cursing and reviling him; in which latter the landlord joined, saying, “Ay, ay, he is a pure one, I warrant you.", "그러나 존스는 벌을 주지 않는 것으로 만족하고는, 새로 사귄 동료들과 함께 떠나며, 길잡이에게는 욕하고 헐뜯는 가련한 앙갚음이나 하도록 내버려 두었다. 그 헐뜯는 일에는 여관 주인도 끼어들어 말했다."], ["A pretty gentleman, indeed, to go for a soldier!", "\"그렇고말고, 저자는 아주 별난 작자지, 내 장담하리다. 군인이 되겠다는 어지간한 신사 나리께서 말이오!"], ["He shall wear a laced wastecoat truly.", "정말이지 화려하게 수놓은 조끼나 차려입겠지."], ["It is an old proverb and a true one, all is not gold that glisters.", "옛 속담에 이런 말이 있는데, 다 옳은 말이라오. 번쩍인다고 다 금은 아니다."], ["I am glad my house is well rid of him.”", "저자가 우리 집에서 깨끗이 떠나 주니 속이 다 시원하구먼.\""]], [["All that day the serjeant and the young soldier marched together; and the former, who was an arch fellow, told the latter many entertaining stories of his campaigns, though in reality he had never made any; for he was but lately come into the service, and had, by his own dexterity, so well ingratiated himself with his officers, that he had promoted himself to a halberd; chiefly indeed by his merit in recruiting, in which he was most excellently well skilled.", "그날 내내 하사와 젊은 신병은 나란히 행군했다. 능청스러운 위인이던 하사는 신병에게 자기 출정 시절의 재미난 이야기를 잔뜩 들려주었다. 하지만 사실 그는 출정 따위는 한 번도 해 본 적이 없었다. 군에 들어온 지도 얼마 되지 않았는데, 제 잔꾀로 상관들의 비위를 어찌나 잘 맞추었던지 스스로 미늘창을 든 하사 자리에까지 올라간 것이다. 실은 주로 신병을 끌어모으는 솜씨 덕이었으니, 그 일에 있어서는 더없이 능란했다."]], [["Much mirth and festivity passed among the soldiers during their march.", "행군하는 동안 군인들 사이에는 흥겨움과 떠들썩함이 넘쳐흘렀다."], ["In which the many occurrences that had passed at their last quarters were remembered, and every one, with great freedom, made what jokes he pleased on his officers, some of which were of the coarser kind, and very near bordering on scandal.", "그 와중에 지난 숙영지에서 있었던 숱한 일들이 떠올랐고, 저마다 거리낌 없이 제 상관들을 두고 마음껏 농을 쳐 댔다. 그중에는 다소 거친 것도 있어, 거의 험담에 가까울 지경이었다."], ["This brought to our heroe's mind the custom which he had read of among the Greeks and Romans, of indulging, on certain festivals and solemn occasions, the liberty to slaves, of using an uncontrouled freedom of speech towards their masters.", "이 광경은 우리 주인공의 머릿속에, 책에서 읽은 그리스인과 로마인의 풍습을 떠올리게 했다. 어떤 축제와 엄숙한 행사 때면 노예들에게도, 주인을 향해 거침없이 말할 자유를 허락하던 그 풍습 말이다."]], [["Our little army, which consisted of two companies of foot, were now arrived at the place where they were to halt that evening.", "두 개의 보병 중대로 이루어진 우리의 작은 군대는 이제 그날 저녁 머물기로 한 곳에 다다랐다. 하사는 곧 지휘관인 자기 중위에게, 그날 행군 중에 사내 둘을 거두어들였노라 일러 주었다."], ["The serjeant then acquainted his lieutenant, who was the commanding officer, that they had picked up two fellows in that day's march, one of which, he said, was as fine a man as ever he saw (meaning the tippler), for that he was near six feet, well proportioned, and strongly limbed; and the other (meaning Jones) would do well enough for the rear rank.", "그중 하나는 (술꾼을 두고 한 말이었다) 여태 본 적 없이 잘생긴 사내라고 했으니, 키가 6피트에 가깝고 몸의 균형이 잘 잡혔으며 팔다리가 다부지다는 것이었다. 그리고 다른 하나는 (존스를 두고 한 말이었다) 후열에 세우기에 그런대로 괜찮을 것이라 했다."]], [["The new soldiers were now produced before the officer, who having examined the six-feet man, he being first produced, came next to survey Jones: at the first sight of whom, the lieutenant could not help showing some surprize; for besides that he was very well dressed, and was naturally genteel, he had a remarkable air of dignity in his look, which is rarely seen among the vulgar, and is indeed not inseparably annexed to the features of their superiors.", "이제 새 병사들이 장교 앞에 불려 나왔다. 먼저 불려 나온 6피트 사내를 살펴본 장교는, 다음으로 존스를 훑어보러 왔다. 그를 처음 본 순간, 중위는 적잖은 놀라움을 감추지 못했다. 그가 옷차림이 무척 단정하고 타고나기를 기품 있었던 데다, 그의 표정에는 좀처럼 천한 무리에서는 볼 수 없는, 그렇다고 그들의 윗사람들 이목구비에 반드시 따라붙는 것도 아닌, 도드라진 위엄의 기운이 서려 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\" 중위가 말했다."], ["said the lieutenant, “my serjeant informed me that you are desirous of enlisting in the company I have at present under my command; if so, sir, we shall very gladly receive a gentleman who promises to do much honour to the company by bearing arms in it.”", "\"내 하사가 일러 주기로, 선생께서 지금 내 휘하에 있는 중대에 입대하고 싶어 한다고 하더이다. 그렇다면, 선생, 우리 중대에서 무기를 들어 큰 명예를 더해 주겠다 약속하는 신사를 우리는 무척 기쁘게 맞이하겠소.\""]], [["Jones answered: “That he had not mentioned anything of enlisting himself; that he was most zealously attached to the glorious cause for which they were going to fight, and was very desirous of serving as a volunteer;”", "존스는 이렇게 답했다. \"저는 입대에 관해서는 한마디도 한 적이 없습니다."], ["concluding with some compliments to the lieutenant, and expressing the great satisfaction he should have in being under his command.", "다만 여러분이 싸우러 나가는 그 영광스러운 대의에 더없이 열렬히 마음이 끌려, 의용병으로 섬기기를 간절히 바랄 뿐입니다.\" 그러고는 중위에게 몇 마디 칭송의 말을 곁들이며, 그의 지휘 아래 있게 되어 무척 흡족하다는 뜻을 밝히는 것으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["The lieutenant returned his civility, commended his resolution, shook him by the hand, and invited him to dine with himself and the rest of the officers.", "중위는 그의 정중함에 화답하며 그 결심을 치하했고, 그와 악수를 나눈 뒤, 자기와 다른 장교들과 함께 식사하자고 청했다."]], [["The adventure of a company of officers. The lieutenant, whom we mentioned in the preceding chapter, and who commanded this party, was now near sixty years of age.", "전 장에서 언급했던, 이 부대를 지휘하던 그 중위는 이제 나이가 예순에 가까웠다."], ["He had entered very young into the army, and had served in the capacity of an ensign at the battle of Tannieres; here he had received two wounds, and had so well distinguished himself, that he was by the Duke of Marlborough advanced to be a lieutenant, immediately after that battle.", "그는 아주 젊어서 군에 들어가 타니에르 전투에서 소위로 복무했는데, 거기서 두 군데 부상을 입었고 어찌나 두드러지게 공을 세웠던지, 그 전투가 끝나자마자 말버러 공작의 손으로 중위로 진급했다."]], [["In this commission he had continued ever since, viz., near forty years; during which time he had seen vast numbers preferred over his head, and had now the mortification to be commanded by boys, whose fathers were at nurse when he first entered into the service.", "그는 그 이후로, 그러니까 거의 사십 년 동안이나 줄곧 이 직위에 머물러 있었다. 그동안 그는 자기보다 위로 올라가는 사람을 수도 없이 보아 왔으며, 이제는 자신이 처음 군에 들어왔을 무렵 아버지가 아직 젖먹이였던 애송이들의 지휘를 받는 굴욕까지 겪고 있었다."]], [["Nor was this ill success in his profession solely owing to his having no friends among the men in power.", "그가 직업에서 이렇게 출세하지 못한 것이 오로지 권력자들 사이에 벗 하나 두지 못한 탓만은 아니었다."], ["He had the misfortune to incur the displeasure of his colonel, who for many years continued in the command of this regiment.", "그는 불운하게도 자기 연대장의 미움을 사고 말았는데, 그 연대장은 여러 해 동안 이 연대의 지휘를 맡고 있었다. 게다가 이자가 그에게 품은 그 누그러질 줄 모르는 악의는 장교로서의 어떤 태만이나 부족함에서, 아니 사실 그 자신의 어떤 허물에서 비롯된 것도 아니었다."], ["Nor did he owe the implacable ill-will which this man bore him to any neglect or deficiency as an officer, nor indeed to any fault in himself; but solely to the indiscretion of his wife, who was a very beautiful woman, and who, though she was remarkably fond of her husband, would not purchase his preferment at the expense of certain favours which the colonel required of her.", "그것은 오로지 그의 아내가 보인 분별없음 때문이었다. 그녀는 무척 아름다운 여인이었고, 남편을 유난히 사랑하면서도, 연대장이 요구하는 어떤 호의들을 대가로 치르면서까지 남편의 진급을 사들이려 하지는 않았던 것이다."]], [["The poor lieutenant was more peculiarly unhappy in this, that while he felt the effects of the enmity of his colonel, he neither knew, nor suspected, that he really bore him any; for he could not suspect an ill-will for which he was not conscious of giving any cause; and his wife, fearing what her husband's nice regard to his honour might have occasioned, contented herself with preserving her virtue without enjoying the triumphs of her conquest.", "가엾은 중위가 이 일에서 유독 더 불행했던 것은, 그가 연대장의 적의가 빚어낸 결과를 몸소 겪으면서도 연대장이 정말로 자기에게 그런 마음을 품고 있다는 것을 알지도, 의심하지도 못했다는 점이다. 자신이 빌미를 준 기억이 전혀 없는 악의를 그가 어찌 의심할 수 있었겠는가. 그리고 그의 아내는, 명예에 까다로운 남편의 성정이 어떤 일을 빚어낼지 두려워한 나머지, 자신의 정조를 지킨 것에 만족할 뿐 그 승리의 개가는 누리려 하지 않았다."]], [["This unfortunate officer (for so I think he may be called) had many good qualities besides his merit in his profession; for he was a religious, honest, good-natured man; and had behaved so well in his command, that he was highly esteemed and beloved not only by the soldiers of his own company, but by the whole regiment.", "이 불운한 장교는(그를 이렇게 불러도 좋으리라 생각한다) 직업적인 공로 말고도 좋은 자질을 많이 갖추고 있었다. 그는 신앙심 깊고 정직하며 마음씨 고운 사람이었고, 지휘하는 일에서도 어찌나 처신이 훌륭했던지 자기 중대의 병사들뿐 아니라 연대 전체로부터 높이 존경받고 사랑받았다."]], [["The other officers who marched with him were a French lieutenant, who had been long enough out of France to forget his own language, but not long enough in England to learn ours, so that he really spoke no language at all, and could barely make himself understood on the most ordinary occasions.", "그와 함께 행군하던 다른 장교들로는, 프랑스를 떠난 지 너무 오래되어 제 나라 말은 잊어버렸으되 영국에 머문 지는 우리말을 익히기엔 짧았던 어느 프랑스인 중위가 있었다. 그래서 그는 사실상 어떤 언어도 제대로 구사하지 못했고, 가장 평범한 상황에서조차 겨우 뜻을 통하게 할 정도였다."], ["There were likewise two ensigns, both very young fellows; one of whom had been bred under an attorney, and the other was son to the wife of a nobleman's butler.", "또한 둘 다 아주 젊은 소위가 있었는데, 하나는 변호사 밑에서 자랐고, 다른 하나는 어느 귀족의 집사 아내가 낳은 아들이었다."]], [["As soon as dinner was ended, Jones informed the company of the merriment which had passed among the soldiers upon their march; “and yet,”", "저녁 식사가 끝나자마자 존스는 행군 중에 병사들 사이에서 벌어졌던 흥겨운 일을 일행에게 이야기해 주었다. \"그렇지만,\""], ["says he, “notwithstanding all their vociferation, I dare swear they will behave more like Grecians than Trojans when they come to the enemy.”--“Grecians and Trojans!”", "그가 말했다. \"그렇게들 떠들썩하게 소리를 질러대도, 막상 적과 맞닥뜨리면 저들은 트로이 사람들보다는 그리스 사람들처럼 처신할 거라고 장담하겠소.\""], ["says one of the ensigns, “who the devil are they?", "\"그리스 사람과 트로이 사람이라!\" 소위 가운데 하나가 말했다."], ["I have heard of all the troops in Europe, but never of any such as these.”", "\"그게 대체 누구요? 나는 유럽의 군대란 군대는 다 들어봤지만, 그런 자들 얘긴 한 번도 못 들어봤소.\""]], [["“Don't pretend to more ignorance than you have, Mr Northerton,”", "\"노더턴 군, 자네가 가진 것 이상으로 무식한 척은 하지 말게.\""], ["said the worthy lieutenant.", "그 훌륭한 중위가 말했다."], ["“I suppose you have heard of the Greeks and Trojans, though perhaps you never read Pope's Homer; who, I remember, now the gentleman mentions it, compares the march of the Trojans to the cackling of geese, and greatly commends the silence of the Grecians.", "\"포프의 호메로스는 읽은 적이 없을지 몰라도, 그리스 사람과 트로이 사람 이야기쯤은 들어봤을 테지. 이제 이 신사분이 말을 꺼내니 생각나는데, 포프는 트로이 사람들의 행군을 거위 떼 꽥꽥대는 소리에 견주고, 그리스 사람들의 침묵을 크게 칭송했지."], ["And upon my honour there is great justice in the cadet's observation.”", "그리고 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 이 젊은이의 관찰에는 큰 일리가 있네.\""]], [["“Begar, me remember dem ver well,”", "\"그렇지, 나도 그자들 아주 잘 기억하지.\" 프랑스인 중위가 말했다."], ["said the French lieutenant: “me ave read them at school in dans Madam Daciere, des Greek, des Trojan, dey fight for von woman--ouy, ouy, me ave read all dat.”", "\"나도 학교에서 다시에 부인 책으로 그거 읽었어, 그리스 사람들, 트로이 사람들, 그들이 여자 하나 때문에 싸웠지. 그래, 그래, 나도 그거 다 읽었어.\""]], [["“D--n Homo with all my heart,”", "\"호모인지 뭔지, 진심으로 빌어먹으라지.\""], ["says Northerton; “I have the marks of him on my a-- yet.", "노더턴이 말했다. \"나는 아직도 그놈 자국이 내 엉덩이에 남아 있단 말이오."], ["There's Thomas, of our regiment, always carries a Homo in his pocket; d--n me, if ever I come at it, if I don't burn it.", "우리 연대에 토머스란 자가 있는데, 늘 호모를 호주머니에 넣고 다닌다오. 빌어먹을, 내 손에 그게 들어오기만 해 봐, 태워버리지 않으면 사람이 아니지."], ["And there's Corderius, another d--n'd son of a whore, that hath got me many a flogging.”", "그리고 코르데리우스라는 또 다른 후레자식 놈도 있는데, 그놈 때문에 내가 매를 어찌나 많이 맞았는지.\""]], [["“Then you have been at school, Mr Northerton?”", "\"그러면 노더턴 군, 자네도 학교를 다녔다는 말인가?\""], ["said the lieutenant.", "중위가 말했다."]], [["“Ay, d--n me, have I,” answered he; “the devil take my father for sending me thither! The old put wanted to make a parson of me, but d--n me, thinks I to myself, I'll nick you there, old cull; the devil a smack of your nonsense shall you ever get into me. There's Jemmy Oliver, of our regiment, he narrowly escaped being a pimp too, and that would have been a thousand pities; for d--n me if he is not one of the prettiest fellows in the whole world; but he went farther than I with the old cull, for Jimmey can neither write nor read.”", "\"그렇소, 빌어먹게도 다녔지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"나를 거기 보낸 우리 아버지를 악마가 잡아갔으면! 그 영감탱이가 나를 목사로 만들려고 했단 말이오. 하지만 빌어먹을, 나는 속으로 생각했지, 영감, 내가 거기서 한 방 먹여 주마, 당신의 그 헛소리는 손톱만큼도 내 머릿속에 안 들어오게 해 주지. 우리 연대에 제미 올리버란 자가 있는데, 그자도 하마터면 뚜쟁이가 될 뻔했지 뭐요. 그랬다면 천만 번 아까운 일이었을 거요. 빌어먹을, 그자야말로 온 세상에서 가장 멋진 친구 가운데 하나니까. 하지만 그자는 나보다 더 멀리까지 그 영감탱이를 골탕 먹였소, 지미는 글을 쓸 줄도 읽을 줄도 모르니 말이오.\""]], [["“You give your friend a very good character,”", "\"자네 친구에게 아주 좋은 인물평을 해 주는군.\""], ["said the lieutenant, “and a very deserved one, I dare say.", "중위가 말했다. \"그리고 아주 마땅한 평이겠지."], ["But prithee, Northerton, leave off that foolish as well as wicked custom of swearing; for you are deceived, I promise you, if you think there is wit or politeness in it.", "하지만 노더턴 군, 부탁이니 그 어리석으면서도 고약한 욕설 버릇은 좀 버리게. 거기에 무슨 재치나 품위가 있다고 여긴다면, 내 분명히 말해두는데 그건 자네가 속고 있는 걸세."], ["I wish, too, you would take my advice, and desist from abusing the clergy.", "그리고 내 충고를 받아들여, 성직자들을 헐뜯는 짓도 그만두었으면 하네. 어떤 무리에게든 망신스러운 이름을 붙이고 비난을 퍼붓는 것은 늘 부당한 일이지만, 그토록 거룩한 직분을 두고 그러는 것은 더욱이나 그러하네."], ["Scandalous names, and reflections cast on any body of men, must be always unjustifiable; but especially so, when thrown on so sacred a function; for to abuse the body is to abuse the function itself; and I leave to you to judge how inconsistent such behaviour is in men who are going to fight in defence of the Protestant religion.”", "그 직분에 종사하는 무리를 욕보이는 것은 곧 그 직분 자체를 욕보이는 일이니까. 그리고 신교의 신앙을 지키려 싸우러 가는 사람들이 그런 짓을 하는 것이 얼마나 앞뒤가 맞지 않는지는 자네 판단에 맡기겠네.\""]], [["Mr Adderly, which was the name of the other ensign, had sat hitherto kicking his heels and humming a tune, without seeming to listen to the discourse; he now answered, “O, Monsieur, on ne parle pas de la religion dans la guerre.”--“Well said, Jack,” cries Northerton: “if la religion was the only matter, the parsons should fight their own battles for me.”", "다른 소위의 이름은 애덜리였는데, 그는 그때까지 발뒤꿈치를 까딱거리고 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 이야기에는 귀를 기울이지 않는 듯이 앉아 있었다. 이제 그가 대답했다. \"오, 무슈, 전쟁터에서는 종교 이야기는 하지 않는 법이오.\" \"잘 말했네, 잭.\" 노더턴이 외쳤다. \"종교가 유일한 문제라면, 목사 놈들더러 제 싸움은 제가 하라지, 나는 빠지겠소.\""]], [["“I don't know, gentlemen,” said Jones, “what may be your opinion; but I think no man can engage in a nobler cause than that of his religion; and I have observed, in the little I have read of history, that no soldiers have fought so bravely as those who have been inspired with a religious zeal: for my own part, though I love my king and country, I hope, as well as any man in it, yet the Protestant interest is no small motive to my becoming a volunteer in the cause.”", "\"여러분 생각이 어떤지는 모르겠소만,\" 존스가 말했다. \"내 생각에는 사람이 제 신앙을 위한 것보다 더 고귀한 대의에 몸을 던질 수는 없을 듯하오. 그리고 내가 역사를 얼마간 읽어 본 바로는, 신앙의 열의에 고무된 병사들만큼 용감하게 싸운 병사는 없었소. 나로 말하자면, 비록 이 나라의 어느 누구 못지않게 내 임금과 나라를 사랑한다고 믿지만, 그래도 신교의 이익이야말로 내가 이 대의에 지원병으로 나선 데 적잖은 동기가 되었소.\""]], [["Northerton now winked on Adderly, and whispered to him slily, “Smoke the prig, Adderly, smoke him.” Then turning to Jones, said to him, “I am very glad, sir, you have chosen our regiment to be a volunteer in; for if our parson should at any time take a cup too much, I find you can supply his place. I presume, sir, you have been at the university; may I crave the favour to know what college?”", "이제 노더턴은 애덜리에게 눈짓을 보내며 슬며시 속삭였다. \"저 잘난 척하는 녀석을 골려 주자고, 애덜리, 골려 줘.\" 그러고는 존스에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"선생, 우리 연대를 지원병으로 택해 주시니 참 기쁘오. 우리 목사가 어쩌다 술을 한 잔 과하게 들이켤 때면, 보아하니 선생이 그 자리를 메워 줄 수 있겠구려. 짐작건대 선생은 대학을 다녔겠지요. 어느 학료였는지 여쭈어도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “so far from having been at the university, I have even had the advantage of yourself, for I was never at school.”", "\"나는 대학을 다니기는커녕, 오히려 선생보다 한 수 위라오. 나는 학교라는 데를 다녀 본 적조차 없으니 말이오.\""]], [["“I presumed,”", "\"내 짐작은,\""], ["cries the ensign, “only upon the information of your great learning.”--“Oh!", "소위가 외쳤다. \"그저 선생의 대단한 학식에 근거한 것이었소만.\""], ["sir,”", "\"오! 선생,\""], ["answered Jones, “it is as possible for a man to know something without having been at school, as it is to have been at school and to know nothing.”", "존스가 대답했다. \"학교를 다녔으면서도 아무것도 모를 수 있는 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 학교를 다니지 않고도 무언가를 알 수 있는 법이라오.\""]], [["“Well said, young volunteer,”", "\"잘 말했네, 젊은 지원병.\""], ["cries the lieutenant.", "중위가 외쳤다."], ["“Upon my word, Northerton, you had better let him alone; for he will be too hard for you.”", "\"정말이지 노더턴 군, 자네는 저 사람을 그냥 두는 편이 낫겠어. 자네에겐 너무 벅찬 상대일 테니까.\""]], [["Northerton did not very well relish the sarcasm of Jones; but he thought the provocation was scarce sufficient to justify a blow, or a rascal, or scoundrel, which were the only repartees that suggested themselves.", "노더턴은 존스의 비꼬는 말이 영 입맛에 맞지 않았다. 하지만 그 도발이 주먹질을 정당화하기에도, 그렇다고 머릿속에 떠오른 유일한 응수인 '악당'이나 '비열한 놈'"], ["He was, therefore, silent at present; but resolved to take the first opportunity of returning the jest by abuse.", "같은 말을 내뱉기에도 모자란다고 여겼다. 그래서 그는 당장은 입을 다물었으나, 첫 기회가 오는 대로 욕설로 그 농담을 되갚아 주리라 마음먹었다."]], [["It now came to the turn of Mr Jones to give a toast, as it is called; who could not refrain from mentioning his dear Sophia.", "이제 흔히 말하는 건배사를 할 차례가 존스에게로 돌아왔고, 그는 사랑하는 소피아를 입에 올리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["This he did the more readily, as he imagined it utterly impossible that any one present should guess the person he meant.", "자기가 가리키는 사람이 누구인지 그 자리의 어느 누구도 절대 알아맞히지 못하리라 여겼기에, 그는 더욱 거리낌 없이 그렇게 했다."]], [["But the lieutenant, who was the toast-master, was not contented with Sophia only.", "그러나 건배 진행을 맡은 중위는 소피아라는 이름만으로는 성에 차지 않았다."], ["He said, he must have her sir-name; upon which Jones hesitated a little, and presently after named Miss Sophia Western.", "그는 성까지 대야 한다고 했고, 그러자 존스는 잠시 머뭇거리다가 곧 소피아 웨스턴 아가씨라고 이름을 댔다."], ["Ensign Northerton declared he would not drink her health in the same round with his own toast, unless somebody would vouch for her.", "노더턴 소위는 누군가가 그녀의 신원을 보증해 주지 않는 한, 자기 건배 상대와 같은 잔으로 그녀의 건강을 위해 마시지는 않겠다고 선언했다. \"내가 소피 웨스턴이란 여자를 하나 알았는데,\""], ["“I knew one Sophy Western,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “that was lain with by half the young fellows at Bath; and perhaps this is the same woman.”", "\"배스에서 젊은 사내들 절반과 동침한 여자였소. 어쩌면 같은 여자일지도 모르지.\""], ["Jones very solemnly assured him of the contrary; asserting that the young lady he named was one of great fashion and fortune.", "존스는 자못 엄숙하게 그렇지 않다고 그에게 단언하며, 자기가 말한 그 아가씨는 대단한 지체와 재산을 갖춘 분이라고 못 박았다. \"그래, 그래,\""], ["“Ay, ay,”", "소위가 말했다."], ["says the ensign, “and so she is: d--n me, it is the same woman; and I'll hold half a dozen of Burgundy, Tom French of our regiment brings her into company with us at any tavern in Bridges-street.”", "\"그 여자도 바로 그렇소. 빌어먹을, 같은 여자가 틀림없소."], ["He then proceeded to describe her person exactly (for he had seen her with her aunt), and concluded with saying, “that her father had a great estate in Somersetshire.”", "우리 연대의 톰 프렌치가 브리지스가의 어느 술집에서든 그 여자를 우리 자리로 데려온다는 데 부르고뉴 포도주 여섯 병을 걸겠소.\" 그러고는 그녀의 외모를 정확하게 묘사하기 시작하더니(그는 그녀가 이모와 함께 있는 것을 본 적이 있었다), \"그 여자 아버지가 서머싯셔에 큰 영지를 가지고 있다\"는 말로 끝맺었다."]], [["The tenderness of lovers can ill brook the least jesting with the names of their mistresses.", "연인의 애틋한 마음은 제 연인의 이름을 두고 조금이라도 농을 거는 것을 도무지 견디지 못한다."], ["However, Jones, though he had enough of the lover and of the heroe too in his disposition, did not resent these slanders as hastily as, perhaps, he ought to have done.", "그렇지만 존스는 천성에 연인의 기질도 영웅의 기질도 충분히 갖추고 있었으면서도, 어쩌면 마땅히 그래야 했을 만큼 성급하게 이 비방에 발끈하지는 않았다."], ["To say the truth, having seen but little of this kind of wit, he did not readily understand it, and for a long time imagined Mr Northerton had really mistaken his charmer for some other.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그는 이런 종류의 재담을 별로 접해 본 적이 없어 얼른 알아듣지 못했고, 한참 동안은 노더턴이 자기 연인을 다른 누군가와 정말로 착각한 줄로만 알았다. 그러나 이제 험악한 낯빛으로 소위를 돌아보며 그가 말했다."], ["But now, turning to the ensign with a stern aspect, he said, “Pray, sir, chuse some other subject for your wit; for I promise you I will bear no jesting with this lady's character.”", "\"선생, 부탁이니 그 재치는 다른 주제를 골라 부려 주시오. 분명히 말해두는데, 이 아가씨의 인격을 두고 농을 거는 것은 결코 참아주지 않겠소.\""], ["“Jesting!”", "\"농이라!\""], ["cries the other, “d--n me if ever I was more in earnest in my life.", "상대가 외쳤다. \"빌어먹을, 내 평생 이보다 더 진지했던 적이 없소."], ["Tom French of our regiment had both her and her aunt at Bath.”", "우리 연대의 톰 프렌치가 배스에서 그 여자도 그 이모도 둘 다 데리고 있었단 말이오.\""], ["“Then I must tell you in earnest,”", "\"그렇다면 나도 진지하게 말해야겠군.\""], ["cries Jones, “that you are one of the most impudent rascals upon earth.”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"당신은 이 세상에서 가장 뻔뻔한 악당 가운데 하나요.\""]], [["He had no sooner spoken these words, than the ensign, together with a volley of curses, discharged a bottle full at the head of Jones, which hitting him a little above the right temple, brought him instantly to the ground.", "이 말을 내뱉기가 무섭게 소위는 욕설을 한바탕 퍼부으며 술병을 가득 채운 채로 존스의 머리를 향해 던졌고, 그것이 오른쪽 관자놀이 조금 위를 맞히는 바람에 존스는 즉각 바닥에 쓰러지고 말았다."]], [["The conqueror perceiving the enemy to lie motionless before him, and blood beginning to flow pretty plentifully from his wound, began now to think of quitting the field of battle, where no more honour was to be gotten; but the lieutenant interposed, by stepping before the door, and thus cut off his retreat.", "승자는 제 앞에 적이 꼼짝 않고 누워 있고 상처에서 피가 제법 많이 흐르기 시작하는 것을 보고는, 이제 더 얻을 명예도 없는 이 싸움터를 뜨려고 생각하기 시작했다. 그러나 중위가 문 앞을 가로막고 나서며 그의 퇴로를 끊어버렸다."]], [["Northerton was very importunate with the lieutenant for his liberty; urging the ill consequences of his stay, asking him, what he could have done less?", "노더턴은 풀어달라고 중위를 몹시 졸라댔다. 자기가 머물러 있으면 좋지 않은 결과가 따를 거라며, 자기가 이보다 덜 어쩔 수 있었겠느냐고 따져 물었다."], ["“Zounds!”", "\"이런 빌어먹을!\""], ["says he, “I was but in jest with the fellow.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 그저 그 친구한테 농담을 했을 뿐이오."], ["I never heard any harm of Miss Western in my life.”", "웨스턴 아가씨에 관한 나쁜 소문 따위 내 평생 들어본 적도 없소.\" \"들어본 적이 없다고?\""], ["“Have not you?”", "중위가 말했다. \"그렇다면 자네는 그런 농을 던진 죄로나, 그런 흉기를 쓴 죄로나 교수형에 처해 마땅하군."], ["said the lieutenant; “then you richly deserve to be hanged, as well for making such jests, as for using such a weapon: you are my prisoner, sir; nor shall you stir from hence till a proper guard comes to secure you.”", "자네는 내 포로일세. 자네를 가둘 적절한 호위병이 올 때까지 여기서 한 발짝도 못 움직일 줄 알게.\""]], [["Such an ascendant had our lieutenant over this ensign, that all that fervency of courage which had levelled our poor heroe with the floor, would scarce have animated the said ensign to have drawn his sword against the lieutenant, had he then had one dangling at his side: but all the swords being hung up in the room, were, at the very beginning of the fray, secured by the French officer.", "우리 중위는 이 소위에게 그토록 위세가 있었으니, 우리의 가엾은 영웅을 바닥에 눕혀버린 그 끓어오르던 용기조차도, 설령 그때 그의 옆구리에 칼이 한 자루 달려 있었다 한들, 이 소위로 하여금 중위를 향해 칼을 빼들도록 부추기기엔 어림없었을 것이다. 그러나 방 안에 걸려 있던 칼은 죄다 다툼이 시작되자마자 그 프랑스인 장교가 챙겨버린 뒤였다."], ["So that Mr Northerton was obliged to attend the final issue of this affair.", "그리하여 노더턴은 이 사건이 어떻게 결말지어질지 잠자코 기다릴 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The French gentleman and Mr Adderly, at the desire of their commanding officer, had raised up the body of Jones, but as they could perceive but little (if any) sign of life in him, they again let him fall, Adderly damning him for having blooded his wastecoat; and the Frenchman declaring, “Begar, me no tush the Engliseman de mort: me have heard de Englise ley, law, what you call, hang up de man dat tush him last.”", "그 프랑스인 신사와 애덜리는 지휘관의 분부에 따라 존스의 몸을 일으켜 세웠으나, 그에게서 살아 있는 기색이 거의(아니 조금도) 보이지 않자 다시 그를 떨어뜨렸다. 애덜리는 그가 자기 조끼에 피를 묻혔다고 욕을 퍼부었고, 프랑스인은 이렇게 선언했다. \"빌어먹을, 나는 이 죽은 영국 사람 안 만지겠어. 나는 영국 법, 법인지 율인지, 뭐라고 부르든, 맨 나중에 만진 사람을 목매단다는 얘기 들었어.\""]], [["When the good lieutenant applied himself to the door, he applied himself likewise to the bell; and the drawer immediately attending, he dispatched him for a file of musqueteers and a surgeon.", "그 선량한 중위는 문에 매달리는 동시에 종에도 매달렸다. 술 시중꾼이 곧바로 달려오자 그는 그를 보내 머스킷 총병 한 분대와 외과의를 불러오게 했다."], ["These commands, together with the drawer's report of what he had himself seen, not only produced the soldiers, but presently drew up the landlord of the house, his wife, and servants, and, indeed, every one else who happened at that time to be in the inn.", "이 명령에 더해 그 시중꾼이 자기가 직접 본 것을 떠벌리는 통에, 병사들이 나타났을 뿐 아니라 어느새 그 집 주인과 그 아내며 하인들, 그리고 사실상 그때 마침 여관 안에 있던 사람 모두가 우르르 몰려들었다."]], [["To describe every particular, and to relate the whole conversation of the ensuing scene, is not within my power, unless I had forty pens, and could, at once, write with them all together, as the company now spoke.", "이어진 장면의 자초지종을 낱낱이 묘사하고 거기 오간 대화를 통째로 옮기는 일은, 내게 펜이 마흔 자루쯤 있어서 사람들이 지금처럼 동시에 떠들어대듯 그 모두로 한꺼번에 쓸 수 있지 않은 한, 내 능력 밖의 일이다."], ["The reader must, therefore, content himself with the most remarkable incidents, and perhaps he may very well excuse the rest.", "그러니 독자는 가장 눈여겨볼 만한 사건들만으로 만족해야 하리라. 어쩌면 나머지는 너그러이 넘겨주어도 좋을 것이다."]], [["The first thing done was securing the body of Northerton, who being delivered into the custody of six men with a corporal at their head, was by them conducted from a place which he was very willing to leave, but it was unluckily to a place whither he was very unwilling to go.", "가장 먼저 한 일은 노더턴의 몸을 단속하는 것이었다. 그는 상등병을 우두머리로 한 여섯 사람의 손에 넘겨졌고, 그들에게 이끌려 그가 몹시 떠나고 싶어 하던 곳을 떠나게 되었으나, 공교롭게도 그것은 그가 몹시 가기 싫어하던 곳으로의 길이었다."], ["To say the truth, so whimsical are the desires of ambition, the very moment this youth had attained the above-mentioned honour, he would have been well contented to have retired to some corner of the world, where the fame of it should never have reached his ears.", "사실대로 말하자면, 야심의 욕망이란 어찌나 변덕스러운지, 이 젊은이는 앞서 말한 그 명예를 손에 넣은 바로 그 순간, 그 소문이 결코 제 귀에 닿지 못할 세상 어느 구석으로든 물러나 있었으면 좋겠다고 흔쾌히 여겼을 것이다."]], [["It surprizes us, and so perhaps, it may the reader, that the lieutenant, a worthy and good man, should have applied his chief care, rather to secure the offender, than to preserve the life of the wounded person.", "훌륭하고 선량한 사람인 중위가 부상자의 목숨을 살리는 일보다 가해자를 단속하는 일에 주된 신경을 쏟았다는 것이 우리를 놀라게 하며, 어쩌면 독자도 그러할 것이다. 우리가 이 점을 짚는 것은 그토록 별난 처신을 굳이 변명해 보겠다는 뜻에서가 아니라, 훗날 어느 비평가가 그것을 발견했다고 으스댈까 봐서다."], ["We mention this observation, not with any view of pretending to account for so odd a behaviour, but lest some critic should hereafter plume himself on discovering it.", "우리는 그 양반들에게 알려주고 싶다. 우리도 그들 못지않게 인물 속의 별난 구석을 볼 줄 안다는 것을."], ["We would have these gentlemen know we can see what is odd in characters as well as themselves, but it is our business to relate facts as they are; which, when we have done, it is the part of the learned and sagacious reader to consult that original book of nature, whence every passage in our work is transcribed, though we quote not always the particular page for its authority.", "다만 우리가 할 일은 사실을 있는 그대로 전하는 것이며, 그 일을 마쳤거든, 우리 작품의 모든 대목이 베껴진 그 자연이라는 원전을 들여다보는 것은 박식하고 슬기로운 독자의 몫이다. 비록 우리가 그 전거로서 정확한 쪽수까지 늘 인용하지는 않을지라도 말이다."]], [["The company which now arrived were of a different disposition.", "이제 도착한 사람들은 기질이 사뭇 달랐다."], ["They suspended their curiosity concerning the person of the ensign, till they should see him hereafter in a more engaging attitude.", "그들은 소위라는 인물에 대한 호기심은 훗날 그가 좀 더 그럴듯한 자세를 취한 모습을 보게 될 때까지 미루어 두었다."], ["At present, their whole concern and attention were employed about the bloody object on the floor; which being placed upright in a chair, soon began to discover some symptoms of life and motion.", "지금 그들의 온 관심과 주의는 바닥에 쓰러진 그 피투성이 대상에게로 쏠려 있었다. 그를 일으켜 의자에 똑바로 앉히자, 그는 곧 살아 있다는, 움직인다는 몇 가지 기미를 보이기 시작했다."], ["These were no sooner perceived by the company (for Jones was at first generally concluded to be dead) than they all fell at once to prescribing for him (for as none of the physical order was present, every one there took that office upon him).", "사람들이 이를 알아채자마자(처음에는 다들 존스가 죽었다고 단정하던 터였다), 그들은 일제히 그에게 처방을 내리기 시작했다(의술에 몸담은 이가 하나도 없던 자리라, 거기 있던 사람마다 그 일을 제 소임으로 떠맡았던 것이다)."]], [["Bleeding was the unanimous voice of the whole room; but unluckily there was no operator at hand; every one then cried, “Call the barber;”", "피를 뽑아야 한다는 것이 방 안 모두의 한목소리였다. 그러나 공교롭게도 그 일을 해낼 사람이 곁에 없었다."], ["but none stirred a step.", "그러자 너나없이 외쳤다. \"이발사를 불러와!\""], ["Several cordials was likewise prescribed in the same ineffective manner; till the landlord ordered up a tankard of strong beer, with a toast, which he said was the best cordial in England.", "하지만 한 발짝도 움직이는 이가 없었다. 여러 가지 강심제도 마찬가지로 아무런 효험 없이 처방되었고, 마침내 여관 주인이 독한 맥주 한 잔에 토스트를 곁들여 가져오게 하면서, 그것이야말로 영국에서 가장 좋은 강심제라고 말했다."]], [["The person principally assistant on this occasion, indeed the only one who did any service, or seemed likely to do any, was the landlady: she cut off some of her hair, and applied it to the wound to stop the blood; she fell to chafing the youth's temples with her hand; and having exprest great contempt for her husband's prescription of beer, she despatched one of her maids to her own closet for a bottle of brandy, of which, as soon as it was brought, she prevailed on Jones, who was just returned to his senses, to drink a very large and plentiful draught.", "이 자리에서 주로 거든 사람, 아니 사실 무언가 쓸모 있는 일을 했거나 할 성싶은 유일한 사람은 안주인이었다. 그녀는 제 머리카락을 얼마간 잘라 상처에 대어 피를 멎게 했고, 손으로 젊은이의 관자놀이를 문지르기 시작했다. 그리고 맥주를 처방하는 남편을 몹시 업신여기며, 하녀 하나를 자기 골방으로 보내 브랜디 한 병을 가져오게 하더니, 그것이 오자마자 막 정신을 차린 존스를 구슬려 아주 크고 넉넉한 한 모금을 들이켜게 했다."]], [["Soon afterwards arrived the surgeon, who having viewed the wound, having shaken his head, and blamed everything which was done, ordered his patient instantly to bed; in which place we think proper to leave him some time to his repose, and shall here, therefore, put an end to this chapter.", "곧이어 외과의가 도착했는데, 그는 상처를 살펴보고 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 여태 행해진 일을 죄다 나무라더니, 환자를 당장 잠자리에 들게 하라고 일렀다. 우리는 그를 거기 그 자리에 얼마간 두어 편히 쉬게 하는 것이 마땅하리라 여기며, 그리하여 이쯤에서 이 장을 끝맺으려 한다."]], [["# Chapter xiii.", "# Chapter xiii."]], [["Containing the great address of the landlady, the great learning of a surgeon, and the solid skill in casuistry of the worthy lieutenant.", "여관 안주인의 빼어난 말솜씨와, 어느 외과의의 대단한 학식과, 갸륵한 중위의 결의론에 관한 단단한 솜씨를 담은 장."]], [["When the wounded man was carried to his bed, and the house began again to clear up from the hurry which this accident had occasioned, the landlady thus addressed the commanding officer: “I am afraid, sir,” said she, “this young man did not behave himself as well as he should do to your honours; and if he had been killed, I suppose he had but his desarts: to be sure, when gentlemen admit inferior parsons into their company, they oft to keep their distance; but, as my first husband used to say, few of 'em know how to do it. For my own part, I am sure I should not have suffered any fellows to include themselves into gentlemen's company; but I thoft he had been an officer himself, till the serjeant told me he was but a recruit.”", "부상당한 사내가 침대로 옮겨지고, 이 사고로 들썩이던 집안이 다시 진정되기 시작하자, 안주인은 지휘관에게 이렇게 말을 건넸다. \"나리, 죄송한 말씀이오나,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 젊은이가 나리들께 마땅히 갖춰야 할 처신을 못한 것 같습니다. 만약 죽임을 당했다 한들, 제 생각엔 자업자득이지요. 정말이지, 신사 양반들이 자기보다 못한 작자들을 자기 무리에 끼워 줄 적엔 거리를 두어야 마땅한데, 제 첫 남편 말마따나 그걸 할 줄 아는 자가 드물지요. 저로 말씀드리면, 어떤 작자도 신사분들 자리에 자기를 끼워 넣게 그냥 두진 않았을 겁니다. 그런데 저 사람도 장교인 줄로만 알았지 뭡니까, 하사가 그저 신병일 뿐이라고 일러 줄 때까진요.\""]], [["“Landlady,”", "\"안주인,\""], ["answered the lieutenant, “you mistake the whole matter.", "중위가 대답했다. \"당신은 일의 전말을 완전히 잘못 알고 있소."], ["The young man behaved himself extremely well, and is, I believe, a much better gentleman than the ensign who abused him.", "저 젊은이는 더없이 훌륭하게 처신했고, 내가 보기엔 그를 모욕한 소위보다 훨씬 나은 신사요. 만약 저 젊은이가 죽는다면, 그를 친 자가 가장 후회하게 될 거요."], ["If the young fellow dies, the man who struck him will have most reason to be sorry for it: for the regiment will get rid of a very troublesome fellow, who is a scandal to the army; and if he escapes from the hands of justice, blame me, madam, that's all.”", "연대로서는 군의 수치인 골칫덩어리 하나를 떨어내는 셈이 될 테니까. 그리고 그자가 법의 손아귀에서 빠져나간다면, 부인, 그땐 나를 탓하시오, 그뿐이오.\""]], [["“Ay! ay!", "\"아이고!"], ["good lack-a-day!”", "아이고! 저런 변이 있나!\""], ["said the landlady; “who could have thoft it?", "안주인이 말했다. \"누가 그런 생각을 했겠어요?"], ["Ay, ay, ay, I am satisfied your honour will see justice done; and to be sure it oft to be to every one.", "그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고, 나리께서 정의가 이뤄지게 하실 줄 알았습니다. 정말이지 누구에게나 그래야 마땅하지요."], ["Gentlemen oft not to kill poor folks without answering for it.", "신사 양반들이 가난한 사람을 죽여 놓고 책임도 안 진대서야 쓰겠습니까."], ["A poor man hath a soul to be saved, as well as his betters.”", "가난한 사람도 윗사람들과 똑같이 구원받을 영혼이 있는걸요.\""]], [["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"정말이오, 부인,\" 중위가 말했다."], ["said the lieutenant, “you do the volunteer wrong: I dare swear he is more of a gentleman than the officer.”", "\"당신은 저 지원병을 잘못 보고 있소. 단언컨대 그는 저 장교보다 훨씬 신사다운 사람이오.\""]], [["“Ay!”", "\"아이고!\""], ["cries the landlady; “why, look you there, now: well, my first husband was a wise man; he used to say, you can't always know the inside by the outside.", "안주인이 외쳤다. \"그것 보세요, 자, 그러니까요."], ["Nay, that might have been well enough too; for I never saw'd him till he was all over blood.", "글쎄, 제 첫 남편이 지혜로운 사람이었지요. 늘 그러더군요, 겉만 보고 속을 다 알 순 없다고요."], ["Who would have thoft it?", "하긴 그것도 그럴 만했지요."], ["mayhap, some young gentleman crossed in love.", "저는 그가 온통 피투성이가 되고 나서야 봤으니까요. 누가 그런 생각을 했겠어요?"], ["Good lack-a-day, if he should die, what a concern it will be to his parents!", "아마, 사랑에 가로막힌 어느 젊은 신사 양반인지도 모르지요. 저런, 만약 그가 죽기라도 한다면 그 부모에게 얼마나 큰 걱정거리겠어요!"], ["why, sure the devil must possess the wicked wretch to do such an act.", "어휴, 정말이지 그런 짓을 하다니 그 사악한 작자에게 악마가 씌었던 게 분명해요. 정말이지, 나리 말씀처럼 그는 군의 수치예요."], ["To be sure, he is a scandal to the army, as your honour says; for most of the gentlemen of the army that ever I saw, are quite different sort of people, and look as if they would scorn to spill any Christian blood as much as any men: I mean, that is, in a civil way, as my first husband used to say.", "제가 여태 본 군의 신사 양반들은 대부분 영 다른 사람들이라서, 그 누구 못지않게 기독교인의 피 흘리는 걸 경멸할 것처럼 보이는걸요. 제 말은, 그러니까, 제 첫 남편 말마따나 점잖은 방식으로 말씀드리는 거예요."], ["To be sure, when they come into the wars, there must be bloodshed: but that they are not to be blamed for.", "물론 전쟁에 나가면 피를 보는 일이야 있어야겠지요. 하지만 그건 탓할 일이 아니지요."], ["The more of our enemies they kill there, the better: and I wish, with all my heart, they could kill every mother's son of them.”", "거기서 우리 적을 많이 죽일수록 좋은 거고요. 저는 진심으로, 그들이 적이란 적은 어미가 낳은 아들놈 하나 남김없이 다 죽여 주었으면 합니다.\""]], [["“O fie, madam!”", "\"아이고, 저런, 부인!\""], ["said the lieutenant, smiling; “all is rather too bloody-minded a wish.”", "중위가 웃으며 말했다. \"하나 남김없이라니, 그건 좀 너무 살벌한 바람이오.\""]], [["“Not at all, sir,”", "\"천만에요, 나리,\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["answered she; “I am not at all bloody-minded, only to our enemies; and there is no harm in that.", "\"저는 조금도 살벌하지 않아요, 다만 우리 적들한테만 그렇지요. 그게 무슨 나쁜 일이겠어요."], ["To be sure it is natural for us to wish our enemies dead, that the wars may be at an end, and our taxes be lowered; for it is a dreadful thing to pay as we do.", "정말이지 우리 적이 죽기를 바라는 건 당연한 일이에요. 그래야 전쟁이 끝나고 세금도 내려갈 테니까요."], ["Why now, there is above forty shillings for window-lights, and yet we have stopt up all we could; we have almost blinded the house, I am sure.", "우리처럼 세금을 내야 한다는 건 끔찍한 일이거든요. 글쎄, 지금 창문세만 해도 사십 실링이 넘는다니까요, 그것도 막을 수 있는 데는 다 막아 놨는데 말이에요."], ["Says I to the exciseman, says I, I think you oft to favour us; I am sure we are very good friends to the government: and so we are for sartain, for we pay a mint of money to 'um.", "정말이지 집을 거의 눈멀게 만들어 놨다고요. 제가 세무관한테 그랬어요, 제가 그랬죠, 제 생각엔 당신네가 우리를 좀 봐줘야 할 것 같다고요."], ["And yet I often think to myself the government doth not imagine itself more obliged to us, than to those that don't pay 'um a farthing.", "우리야말로 정부에 아주 좋은 친구가 아니냐고요. 정말로 그렇잖아요, 우리가 그들한테 엄청난 돈을 내는걸요."], ["Ay, ay, it is the way of the world.”", "그런데도 저는 가끔 혼자 생각해요, 정부는 우리한테 빚진 게 한 푼도 안 내는 사람들보다 더 많다고는 여기지 않을 거라고요. 그렇죠, 그렇죠, 그게 세상 이치인걸요.\""]], [["She was proceeding in this manner when the surgeon entered the room. The lieutenant immediately asked how his patient did. But he resolved him only by saying, “Better, I believe, than he would have been by this time, if I had not been called; and even as it is, perhaps it would have been lucky if I could have been called sooner.”--“I hope, sir,” said the lieutenant, “the skull is not fractured.”--“Hum,” cries the surgeon: “fractures are not always the most dangerous symptoms. Contusions and lacerations are often attended with worse phaenomena, and with more fatal consequences, than fractures. People who know nothing of the matter conclude, if the skull is not fractured, all is well; whereas, I had rather see a man's skull broke all to pieces, than some contusions I have met with.”--“I hope,” says the lieutenant, “there are no such symptoms here.”--“Symptoms,” answered the surgeon, “are not always regular nor constant. I have known very unfavourable symptoms in the morning change to favourable ones at noon, and return to unfavourable again at night. Of wounds, indeed, it is rightly and truly said, Nemo repente fuit turpissimus. I was once, I remember, called to a patient who had received a violent contusion in his tibia, by which the exterior cutis was lacerated, so that there was a profuse sanguinary discharge; and the interior membranes were so divellicated, that the os or bone very plainly appeared through the aperture of the vulnus or wound. Some febrile symptoms intervening at the same time (for the pulse was exuberant and indicated much phlebotomy), I apprehended an immediate mortification. To prevent which, I presently made a large orifice in the vein of the left arm, whence I drew twenty ounces of blood; which I expected to have found extremely sizy and glutinous, or indeed coagulated, as it is in pleuretic complaints; but, to my surprize, it appeared rosy and florid, and its consistency differed little from the blood of those in perfect health. I then applied a fomentation to the part, which highly answered the intention; and after three or four times dressing, the wound began to discharge a thick pus or matter, by which means the cohesion--But perhaps I do not make myself perfectly well understood?”--“No, really,” answered the lieutenant, “I cannot say I understand a syllable.”--“Well, sir,” said the surgeon, “then I shall not tire your patience; in short, within six weeks my patient was able to walk upon his legs as perfectly as he could have done before he received the contusion.”--“I wish, sir,” said the lieutenant, “you would be so kind only to inform me, whether the wound this young gentleman hath had the misfortune to receive, is likely to prove mortal.”--“Sir,” answered the surgeon, “to say whether a wound will prove mortal or not at first dressing, would be very weak and foolish presumption: we are all mortal, and symptoms often occur in a cure which the greatest of our profession could never foresee.”--“But do you think him in danger?” says the other.--“In danger! ay, surely,” cries the doctor: “who is there among us, who, in the most perfect health, can be said not to be in danger? Can a man, therefore, with so bad a wound as this be said to be out of danger? All I can say at present is, that it is well I was called as I was, and perhaps it would have been better if I had been called sooner. I will see him again early in the morning; and in the meantime let him be kept extremely quiet, and drink liberally of water-gruel.”--“Won't you allow him sack-whey?” said the landlady.--“Ay, ay, sack-whey,” cries the doctor, “if you will, provided it be very small.”--“And a little chicken broth too?” added she.--“Yes, yes, chicken broth,” said the doctor, “is very good.”--“Mayn't I make him some jellies too?” said the landlady.--“Ay, ay,” answered the doctor, “jellies are very good for wounds, for they promote cohesion.” And indeed it was lucky she had not named soup or high sauces, for the doctor would have complied, rather than have lost the custom of the house.", "그녀가 이런 식으로 이야기를 이어 가고 있을 때 외과의가 방으로 들어왔다. 중위는 곧바로 환자가 어떠냐고 물었다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 대답할 뿐이었다. \"내가 불려 오지 않았다면 지금쯤 됐을 것보다는 나은 것 같소. 그리고 이만한 것도, 좀 더 일찍 불려 올 수 있었더라면 어쩌면 다행이었을 거요.\" \"선생,\" 중위가 말했다. \"두개골이 골절되지는 않았기를 바라오.\" \"흠,\" 외과의가 외쳤다. \"골절이 늘 가장 위험한 증상은 아니라오. 타박상이나 열상이 골절보다 더 나쁜 현상을 동반하고 더 치명적인 결과를 부르는 경우가 흔하지요. 이 일을 전혀 모르는 사람들은 두개골만 부러지지 않으면 다 괜찮다고 단정하지만, 나라면 내가 봐 온 어떤 타박상들보다는 차라리 두개골이 산산조각 나는 편을 보겠소.\" \"여기엔 그런 증상이 없기를 바라오.\" 중위가 말했다. \"증상이란,\" 외과의가 대답했다. \"늘 규칙적이지도 한결같지도 않소. 나는 아침엔 아주 불길하던 증상이 정오엔 좋은 쪽으로 바뀌었다가 밤엔 다시 불길해지는 경우를 본 적이 있소. 상처에 관해서라면 참으로 옳고 진실하게 일컫는 말이 있지요. '네모 레펜테 푸이트 투르피시무스'라고. 한번은 기억건대, 정강이뼈에 심한 타박상을 입은 환자에게 불려 간 적이 있소. 그로 인해 바깥쪽 피부가 찢겨서 출혈이 심했고, 안쪽 막들이 어찌나 찢겨 나갔던지 그 상처, 곧 불누스라는 그 틈으로 오스, 곧 뼈가 아주 또렷이 드러나 보였소. 그와 동시에 몇 가지 열성 증상이 끼어들었는데(맥박이 넘쳐서 많은 사혈이 필요함을 가리켰소), 나는 당장 괴저가 올까 걱정했소. 그걸 막으려고 나는 즉시 왼팔 정맥에 큰 구멍을 내어 거기서 피 스무 온스를 뽑았소. 나는 그 피가, 늑막염을 앓는 사람의 것처럼 몹시 끈적하고 들러붙거나, 실은 응고된 상태일 거라 예상했소. 그런데 놀랍게도 그 피는 장밋빛으로 생기 있게 나타났고, 그 농도가 더없이 건강한 사람의 피와 거의 다르지 않았소. 그래서 나는 그 부위에 찜질을 했고, 그것이 의도한 바에 톡톡히 부응했지요. 서너 차례 처치를 한 뒤로 그 상처가 짙은 고름, 곧 농을 흘려보내기 시작했고, 그 덕분에 유착이—한데 혹시 내 말을 제대로 못 알아듣고 계시는 건 아니오?\" \"아니, 정말이지,\" 중위가 대답했다. \"한 마디도 알아듣고 있다고는 말 못 하겠소.\" \"그럼, 선생,\" 외과의가 말했다. \"당신 인내심을 더는 시험하지 않겠소. 요컨대, 여섯 주 만에 내 환자는 타박상을 입기 전 그대로 두 다리로 걸어 다닐 수 있게 되었소.\" \"선생,\" 중위가 말했다. \"부디 친절을 베풀어 이 한 가지만 알려 주시오. 이 젊은 신사가 불행히도 입은 이 상처가 치명상이 될 것 같소?\" \"선생,\" 외과의가 대답했다. \"첫 처치에서 상처가 치명적일지 아닐지를 말한다는 건 참으로 어리석고 경솔한 억측이오. 우리는 모두 죽을 몸이고, 치료 과정에서는 우리 직업의 가장 위대한 자라도 결코 예견 못 할 증상이 흔히 생기니까.\" \"하지만 그가 위험하다고 보시오?\" 상대가 물었다. \"위험하냐고! 그야 물론이지요,\" 의사가 외쳤다. \"우리 가운데 누가, 더없이 완벽하게 건강한 상태인들 위험하지 않다고 할 수 있겠소? 그러니 이렇게 나쁜 상처를 입은 사람을 두고 위험에서 벗어났다고 할 수 있겠소? 지금 내가 할 수 있는 말은 다만, 내가 그때 불려 온 게 다행이었고, 어쩌면 좀 더 일찍 불려 왔더라면 더 나았으리란 것뿐이오. 내일 아침 일찍 다시 그를 보러 오겠소. 그동안엔 그를 더없이 조용히 두고, 물죽을 넉넉히 마시게 하시오.\" \"백포도주 유장은 안 되나요?\" 안주인이 말했다. \"그래요, 그래요, 백포도주 유장도,\" 의사가 외쳤다. \"원한다면, 아주 묽게 한다면 괜찮소.\" \"닭고기 국물도 조금?\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그래요, 그래요, 닭고기 국물은,\" 의사가 말했다. \"아주 좋소.\" \"젤리도 좀 만들어 주면 안 될까요?\" 안주인이 말했다. \"그럼요, 그럼요,\" 의사가 대답했다. \"젤리는 상처에 아주 좋소, 유착을 돕거든.\" 그리고 정말이지, 그녀가 수프나 진한 소스를 들먹이지 않은 것이 다행이었다. 의사는 이 집의 단골을 잃느니 차라리 거기에도 응했을 테니까."]], [["The doctor was no sooner gone, than the landlady began to trumpet forth his fame to the lieutenant, who had not, from their short acquaintance, conceived quite so favourable an opinion of his physical abilities as the good woman, and all the neighbourhood, entertained (and perhaps very rightly); for though I am afraid the doctor was a little of a coxcomb, he might be nevertheless very much of a surgeon.", "의사가 자리를 뜨기가 무섭게 안주인은 중위에게 그의 명성을 나팔 불기 시작했다. 중위는 짧은 만남으로는 이 착한 여인이며 동네 사람들 모두가 품은(어쩌면 아주 옳게도) 만큼이나 그의 의술에 호의적인 의견을 갖지는 못했다. 비록 그 의사가 다소 멋부리는 허풍쟁이였던 건 사실일까 봐 걱정되지만, 그럼에도 그는 꽤 솜씨 있는 외과의였을 수도 있으니 말이다."]], [["The lieutenant having collected from the learned discourse of the surgeon that Mr Jones was in great danger, gave orders for keeping Mr Northerton under a very strict guard, designing in the morning to attend him to a justice of peace, and to commit the conducting the troops to Gloucester to the French lieutenant, who, though he could neither read, write, nor speak any language, was, however, a good officer.", "중위는 외과의의 그 학식 있는 이야기에서 존스 씨가 큰 위험에 처해 있음을 헤아리고, 노더턴 씨를 아주 엄중한 감시 아래 두라고 명령했다. 그러고는 아침에 그를 치안판사에게 데려가고, 글로스터로 가는 병력의 인솔은 프랑스인 중위에게 맡길 작정이었다. 이 프랑스인은 어떤 언어도 읽지도, 쓰지도, 말하지도 못했지만 그래도 훌륭한 장교였다."]], [["In the evening, our commander sent a message to Mr Jones, that if a visit would not be troublesome, he would wait on him. This civility was very kindly and thankfully received by Jones, and the lieutenant accordingly went up to his room, where he found the wounded man much better than he expected; nay, Jones assured his friend, that if he had not received express orders to the contrary from the surgeon, he should have got up long ago; for he appeared to himself to be as well as ever, and felt no other inconvenience from his wound but an extreme soreness on that side of his head.", "저녁이 되자 우리의 지휘관은 존스 씨에게 전갈을 보냈다. 방문이 폐가 되지 않는다면 한번 들르겠노라고. 이 정중함을 존스는 무척 다정하고 고맙게 받아들였고, 그리하여 중위는 그의 방으로 올라갔다. 거기서 그는 부상당한 사내가 예상보다 훨씬 나아진 것을 보았다. 아니, 존스는 친구에게, 외과의로부터 그러지 말라는 분명한 지시를 받지 않았더라면 진작에 일어났을 거라고 장담했다. 자기가 보기엔 여느 때처럼 멀쩡한 것 같았고, 머리 한쪽이 몹시 쓰라린 것 말고는 상처로 인한 다른 불편은 느끼지 못한다는 것이었다."]], [["“I should be very glad,” quoth the lieutenant, “if you was as well as you fancy yourself, for then you could be able to do yourself justice immediately; for when a matter can't be made up, as in case of a blow, the sooner you take him out the better; but I am afraid you think yourself better than you are, and he would have too much advantage over you.”", "\"내가 무척 기쁘겠소,\" 중위가 말했다. \"당신이 스스로 여기는 만큼 정말 멀쩡하다면 말이오. 그러면 곧장 명예를 회복할 수 있을 테니까. 한 대 얻어맞은 경우처럼 일을 무마할 수 없을 적엔, 그자를 빨리 불러내 결판 짓는 게 좋소. 다만 당신이 실제보다 자기를 더 멀쩡하다 여기는 게 아닐까 걱정이오. 그러면 그자가 당신보다 너무 유리해질 테니까.\""]], [["“I'll try, however,”", "\"그래도 한번 해 보겠소,\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “if you please, and will be so kind to lend me a sword, for I have none here of my own.”", "\"괜찮으시다면, 부디 친절을 베풀어 칼을 한 자루 빌려 주시오. 여기엔 제 칼이 없으니 말이오.\""]], [["“My sword is heartily at your service, my dear boy,” cries the lieutenant, kissing him; “you are a brave lad, and I love your spirit; but I fear your strength; for such a blow, and so much loss of blood, must have very much weakened you; and though you feel no want of strength in your bed, yet you most probably would after a thrust or two. I can't consent to your taking him out tonight; but I hope you will be able to come up with us before we get many days' march advance; and I give you my honour you shall have satisfaction, or the man who hath injured you shan't stay in our regiment.”", "\"내 칼은 기꺼이 자네를 위해 쓰이겠네, 사랑스러운 친구,\" 중위가 그에게 입을 맞추며 외쳤다. \"자네는 용감한 젊은이고, 나는 그 기개가 마음에 드네. 다만 자네 기력이 걱정이야. 그만한 일격에 그만큼 피를 흘렸으니 자네는 분명 무척 약해졌을 거야. 침대에 누워서는 기력이 모자란 줄 못 느낄지 몰라도, 칼을 한두 번 찌르고 나면 십중팔구 느끼게 될 걸세. 오늘 밤 그자를 불러내는 데는 동의할 수 없네. 다만 우리가 며칠 행군해 나아가기 전에 자네가 따라잡을 수 있기를 바라네. 그리고 내 명예를 걸고 약속하지, 자네는 반드시 명예 회복의 기회를 얻을 걸세. 아니면 자네를 해친 그자가 우리 연대에 남아 있지 못하게 하겠네.\""]], [["“I wish,”", "\"바라기는,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “it was possible to decide this matter to-night: now you have mentioned it to me, I shall not be able to rest.”", "\"이 일을 오늘 밤 결판 지을 수 있었으면 좋겠소. 이제 당신이 그 얘기를 꺼냈으니, 나는 도저히 쉬지 못할 거요.\""]], [["“Oh, never think of it,”", "\"오, 그런 생각일랑 마시게,\""], ["returned the other: “a few days will make no difference.", "상대가 대꾸했다. \"며칠 늦어진다고 달라질 건 없네."], ["The wounds of honour are not like those in your body: they suffer nothing by the delay of cure.", "명예의 상처는 자네 몸의 상처와는 다르거든. 치료가 늦어진다고 덧나는 법이 없지."], ["It will be altogether as well for you to receive satisfaction a week hence as now.”", "지금 명예를 회복하든 일주일 뒤에 회복하든 자네에겐 매한가지일 걸세.\""]], [["“But suppose,”", "\"하지만 만약,\""], ["says Jones, “I should grow worse, and die of the consequences of my present wound?”", "존스가 말했다. \"내가 더 나빠져서, 지금 이 상처의 후유증으로 죽기라도 한다면 어쩌오?\""]], [["“Then your honour,”", "\"그럼 자네 명예는,\""], ["answered the lieutenant, “will require no reparation at all.", "중위가 대답했다. \"아무런 보상도 필요로 하지 않을 걸세."], ["I myself will do justice to your character, and testify to the world your intention to have acted properly, if you had recovered.”", "내가 직접 자네의 인격을 위해 정의를 세우고, 자네가 회복했더라면 마땅히 처신했으리란 그 뜻을 세상에 증언해 주겠네.\""]], [["“Still,”", "\"그래도,\""], ["replied Jones, “I am concerned at the delay.", "존스가 대답했다. \"나는 이 지체가 마음에 걸리오."], ["I am almost afraid to mention it to you who are a soldier; but though I have been a very wild young fellow, still in my most serious moments, and at the bottom, I am really a Christian.”", "군인인 당신께 이 얘기를 꺼내기가 거의 두렵소만, 비록 내가 무척 방종한 젊은이로 살아오긴 했어도, 가장 진지한 순간엔, 그리고 마음 밑바닥에선, 나는 정말로 기독교인이오.\""]], [["“So am I too, I assure you,”", "\"나 또한 그렇다네, 정말이야,\" 장교가 말했다."], ["said the officer; “and so zealous a one, that I was pleased with you at dinner for taking up the cause of your religion; and I am a little offended with you now, young gentleman, that you should express a fear of declaring your faith before any one.”", "\"그것도 어찌나 열심인지, 저녁 식사 때 자네가 자기 종교의 편을 드는 걸 보고 흐뭇했다네. 그런데 젊은 신사 양반, 자네가 누구 앞에서든 자기 신앙을 밝히기를 두려워하는 기색을 보이니 지금은 조금 언짢구먼.\""]], [["“But how terrible must it be,”", "\"하지만 얼마나 끔찍한 일이겠소,\""], ["cries Jones, “to any one who is really a Christian, to cherish malice in his breast, in opposition to the command of Him who hath expressly forbid it?", "존스가 외쳤다. \"정말로 기독교인인 사람이, 그것을 명백히 금하신 그분의 명령을 거스르며 가슴속에 원한을 품는다는 것이?"], ["How can I bear to do this on a sick-bed?", "병상에서 어떻게 내가 이 일을 견딜 수 있겠소?"], ["Or how shall I make up my account, with such an article as this in my bosom against me?”", "아니, 가슴속에 이런 죄목을 나 자신을 거스르는 채로 품고서 어떻게 내 셈을 치를 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Why, I believe there is such a command,”", "\"글쎄, 그런 명령이 있다는 건 나도 믿네,\""], ["cries the lieutenant; “but a man of honour can't keep it.", "중위가 외쳤다. \"하지만 명예를 아는 사람은 그걸 지킬 수가 없어."], ["And you must be a man of honour, if you will be in the army.", "그리고 군에 몸담으려면 자네는 명예를 아는 사람이어야만 하네."], ["I remember I once put the case to our chaplain over a bowl of punch, and he confessed there was much difficulty in it; but he said, he hoped there might be a latitude granted to soldiers in this one instance; and to be sure it is our duty to hope so; for who would bear to live without his honour?", "언젠가 펀치 한 사발을 놓고 우리 군목에게 이 문제를 던진 적이 있는데, 그도 거기에 어려움이 많다는 건 인정하더군. 다만 이 한 가지 경우만큼은 군인에게 좀 너그러운 여지가 주어질 수 있기를 바란다고 하더라고."], ["No, no, my dear boy, be a good Christian as long as you live; but be a man of honour too, and never put up an affront; not all the books, nor all the parsons in the world, shall ever persuade me to that.", "그리고 정말이지 우리야 그러기를 바라는 게 마땅하지. 명예 없이 살아가는 걸 누가 견디겠나?"], ["I love my religion very well, but I love my honour more.", "아니지, 아니야, 사랑스러운 친구, 살아 있는 동안 좋은 기독교인이 되게. 하지만 명예를 아는 사람도 되게."], ["There must be some mistake in the wording the text, or in the translation, or in the understanding it, or somewhere or other.", "결코 모욕을 그냥 넘기지 말게. 세상의 그 어떤 책도, 세상의 그 어떤 목사도 나를 그렇게 설득하진 못할 거야."], ["But however that be, a man must run the risque, for he must preserve his honour.", "나는 내 종교를 무척 사랑하지만, 내 명예는 더 사랑하거든. 그 성경 구절의 표현에든, 번역에든, 아니면 그걸 이해하는 데든, 어디엔가 무슨 착오가 있는 게 분명해."], ["So compose yourself to-night, and I promise you you shall have an opportunity of doing yourself justice.”", "하지만 그게 어떻든 사람은 위험을 무릅써야 하네, 명예는 지켜야 하니까. 그러니 오늘 밤은 마음을 가라앉히게."], ["Here he gave Jones a hearty buss, shook him by the hand, and took his leave.", "자네가 스스로 명예를 회복할 기회를 갖게 되리라 약속하네.\" 여기서 그는 존스에게 다정하게 입을 맞추고, 손을 잡아 흔들고는 작별했다."]], [["But though the lieutenant's reasoning was very satisfactory to himself, it was not entirely so to his friend.", "그러나 중위의 그 논리는 자신에게는 무척 만족스러웠을지언정, 그의 친구에게는 온전히 그렇지가 못했다."], ["Jones therefore, having revolved this matter much in his thoughts, at last came to a resolution, which the reader will find in the next chapter.", "그리하여 존스는 이 문제를 머릿속에서 한참 곱씹은 끝에, 마침내 한 가지 결심에 이르렀다. 그 결심이 무엇인지는 독자가 다음 장에서 보게 될 것이다."]], [["# Chapter xiv.", "# Chapter xiv."]], [["A most dreadful chapter indeed; and which few readers ought to venture upon in an evening, especially when alone.", "참으로 무시무시한 장이다. 어지간한 독자라면 저녁 시간에, 그것도 혼자 있을 때에는 함부로 들어서지 않는 편이 좋을 것이다."]], [["Jones swallowed a large mess of chicken, or rather cock, broth, with a very good appetite, as indeed he would have done the cock it was made of, with a pound of bacon into the bargain; and now, finding in himself no deficiency of either health or spirit, he resolved to get up and seek his enemy.", "존스는 닭, 아니 정확히 말하면 수탉으로 끓인 죽을 큰 그릇으로 아주 맛있게 들이켰다. 사실 그 죽의 재료가 된 수탉을 통째로, 거기에 베이컨 한 근을 곁들여서라도 먹어 치웠을 만한 식욕이었다. 이제 몸 상태에도 기력에도 모자람이 없음을 느낀 그는, 자리를 털고 일어나 적을 찾아 나서기로 마음먹었다."]], [["But first he sent for the serjeant, who was his first acquaintance among these military gentlemen.", "그러나 그는 먼저 중사를 불러오게 했다. 이 군인 양반들 가운데 처음으로 안면을 튼 사람이 바로 그였기 때문이다."], ["Unluckily that worthy officer having, in a literal sense, taken his fill of liquor, had been some time retired to his bolster, where he was snoring so loud that it was not easy to convey a noise in at his ears capable of drowning that which issued from his nostrils.", "그런데 공교롭게도 그 갸륵한 장교는 말 그대로 술을 잔뜩 퍼마신 뒤 한참 전부터 베개에 머리를 누이고 있었는데, 어찌나 크게 코를 골던지 그 콧구멍에서 새어 나오는 소리를 덮을 만한 소음을 그의 귀에 들이밀기란 결코 쉬운 일이 아니었다."]], [["However, as Jones persisted in his desire of seeing him, a vociferous drawer at length found means to disturb his slumbers, and to acquaint him with the message.", "그래도 존스가 그를 보겠다고 고집을 부리자, 목청 좋은 술집 종업원이 마침내 그의 잠을 흔들어 깨우고 전갈을 전할 방도를 찾아냈다."], ["Of which the serjeant was no sooner made sensible, than he arose from his bed, and having his clothes already on, immediately attended.", "그 전갈을 알아듣자마자 중사는 침상에서 일어났고, 옷을 이미 걸친 채였던 터라 곧장 달려왔다. 존스는 자신의 계획을 중사에게 알리는 것이 적절치 않다고 여겼다."], ["Jones did not think fit to acquaint the serjeant with his design; though he might have done it with great safety, for the halberdier was himself a man of honour, and had killed his man.", "사실 알려도 큰 탈은 없었을 것이다. 그 미늘창 든 사내 자신이 명예를 아는 사람이었고, 이미 한 사람을 베어 죽인 적도 있었으니 말이다."], ["He would therefore have faithfully kept this secret, or indeed any other which no reward was published for discovering.", "그러니 그는 이런 비밀쯤은, 아니 발설에 현상금이 걸리지 않은 어떤 비밀이든 충실히 지켜 주었을 것이다."], ["But as Jones knew not those virtues in so short an acquaintance, his caution was perhaps prudent and commendable enough.", "그러나 존스는 그토록 짧은 사귐만으로 그의 그런 됨됨이를 알 길이 없었으니, 그의 조심성은 어쩌면 사려 깊고 칭찬할 만한 것이었으리라."]], [["He began therefore by acquainting the serjeant, that as he was now entered into the army, he was ashamed of being without what was perhaps the most necessary implement of a soldier; namely, a sword; adding, that he should be infinitely obliged to him, if he could procure one. “For which,” says he, “I will give you any reasonable price; nor do I insist upon its being silver-hilted; only a good blade, and such as may become a soldier's thigh.”", "그리하여 그는 먼저 이렇게 말문을 열었다. 이제 군에 들어온 마당에, 군인에게 어쩌면 가장 필요한 도구라 할 것, 곧 칼 한 자루가 없는 처지가 부끄럽다고. 그러면서 만일 한 자루 구해 줄 수 있다면 더없이 고맙겠노라 덧붙였다. \"그 값으로는,\" 그가 말했다, \"합당한 만큼 얼마든 치르겠소. 자루를 은으로 두른 것까지는 바라지 않소. 그저 좋은 칼날, 군인의 허리에 어울릴 만한 것이면 되오.\""]], [["The serjeant, who well knew what had happened, and had heard that Jones was in a very dangerous condition, immediately concluded, from such a message, at such a time of night, and from a man in such a situation, that he was light-headed.", "무슨 일이 있었는지 빤히 알고 있었고, 존스가 매우 위태로운 상태라는 말도 들었던 중사는, 이런 한밤중에, 이런 처지의 사람에게서 이런 전갈을 받자, 곧 그가 정신이 오락가락한다고 단정해 버렸다."], ["Now as he had his wit (to use that word in its common signification) always ready, he bethought himself of making his advantage of this humour in the sick man.", "한데 그는 늘 재치를(이 말을 흔히 쓰는 뜻으로 쓰자면) 갖추고 있던 자라, 이 환자의 헛소리를 제 잇속에 이용할 궁리를 떠올렸다. \"나리,\""], ["“Sir,” says he, “I believe I can fit you.", "그가 말했다, \"제가 딱 맞춰 드릴 수 있을 것 같습니다요."], ["I have a most excellent piece of stuff by me.", "마침 더없이 훌륭한 물건을 하나 가지고 있지요."], ["It is not indeed silver-hilted, which, as you say, doth not become a soldier; but the handle is decent enough, and the blade one of the best in Europe.", "은으로 두른 자루는 아닙니다만, 나리 말씀대로 그건 군인에게 어울리지도 않고요. 그래도 손잡이가 제법 점잖고, 칼날로 말하자면 유럽에서 손꼽히는 물건입니다."], ["It is a blade that--a blade that--in short, I will fetch it you this instant, and you shall see it and handle it.", "이게 바로, 이게 바로 그러니까, 당장 가져다 드리겠습니다, 직접 보시고 손에 쥐어 보십쇼."], ["I am glad to see your honour so well with all my heart.”", "나리께서 이리 정정하신 걸 뵈니 진심으로 기쁩니다요.\""]], [["Being instantly returned with the sword, he delivered it to Jones, who took it and drew it; and then told the serjeant it would do very well, and bid him name his price.", "그는 곧장 칼을 들고 돌아와 존스에게 건넸다. 존스는 그것을 받아 칼집에서 뽑아 보고는, 아주 쓸 만하다고 중사에게 말한 뒤 값을 불러 보라 했다."]], [["The serjeant now began to harangue in praise of his goods.", "이제 중사는 제 물건을 칭찬하는 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["He said (nay he swore very heartily), “that the blade was taken from a French officer, of very high rank, at the battle of Dettingen.", "그는 말했다(아니, 아주 거창하게 맹세까지 했다). \"이 칼날은 데팅겐 전투에서 지위가 아주 높은 프랑스 장교한테서 빼앗은 겁니다."], ["I took it myself,”", "제가 직접,\""], ["says he, “from his side, after I had knocked him o'", "그가 말했다, \"그놈 대가리를 후려친 뒤에 옆구리에서 뽑아낸 거지요."], ["the head. The hilt was a golden one.", "자루는 황금으로 된 거였는데, 그건 우리 멋쟁이 양반들 중 하나한테 팔아넘겼습니다."], ["That I sold to one of our fine gentlemen; for there are some of them, an't please your honour, who value the hilt of a sword more than the blade.”", "나리, 황송합니다만 그런 양반들 중에는 칼날보다 칼자루를 더 치는 자들이 더러 있거든요.\""]], [["Here the other stopped him, and begged him to name a price.", "여기서 존스가 그의 말을 자르고 값이나 부르라고 청했다."], ["The serjeant, who thought Jones absolutely out of his senses, and very near his end, was afraid lest he should injure his family by asking too little.", "존스가 완전히 제정신이 아닌 데다 죽음이 코앞에 닥쳤다고 여긴 중사는, 너무 적게 불러 제 식구들에게 손해를 끼치지나 않을까 겁이 났다."], ["However, after a moment's hesitation, he contented himself with naming twenty guineas, and swore he would not sell it for less to his own brother.", "그래도 잠시 머뭇거린 끝에, 스무 기니를 부르는 것으로 만족하고는, 친형제에게라도 이보다 싸게는 못 판다고 맹세했다."]], [["“Twenty guineas!”", "\"스무 기니라!\""], ["says Jones, in the utmost surprize: “sure you think I am mad, or that I never saw a sword in my life.", "존스가 더없이 놀라 소리쳤다. \"내가 미쳤거나, 평생 칼이라곤 구경도 못 해 본 줄 아는 게로군."], ["Twenty guineas, indeed!", "스무 기니라니, 참!"], ["I did not imagine you would endeavour to impose upon me.", "네가 나를 속여 먹으려 들 줄은 몰랐다. 자, 칼 도로 가져가라."], ["Here, take the sword--No, now I think on't, I will keep it myself, and show it your officer in the morning, acquainting him, at the same time, what a price you asked me for it.”", "아니, 가만 생각해 보니 내가 직접 가지고 있다가 아침에 네 상관에게 보여 주겠다. 네가 이걸 얼마에 부르더라고 함께 일러 주면서 말이지.\""]], [["The serjeant, as we have said, had always his wit (in sensu praedicto) about him, and now plainly saw that Jones was not in the condition he had apprehended him to be; he now, therefore, counterfeited as great surprize as the other had shown, and said, “I am certain, sir, I have not asked you so much out of the way.", "앞서 말했듯 중사는 늘 재치를(앞서 말한 그 뜻으로) 곁에 지니고 있던 자라, 이제 존스가 자기가 짐작했던 그런 상태가 아님을 똑똑히 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 존스 못지않게 놀란 척을 꾸며 보이며 말했다."], ["Besides, you are to consider, it is the only sword I have, and I must run the risque of my officer's displeasure, by going without one myself.", "\"나리, 제가 정말이지 그렇게 터무니없이 부른 건 아닙니다. 게다가 헤아려 주십쇼, 이게 제가 가진 단 하나뿐인 칼이라, 저 자신이 칼도 없이 다니면서 상관의 노여움을 살 위험까지 무릅써야 하는 겁니다."], ["And truly, putting all this together, I don't think twenty shillings was so much out of the way.”", "정말이지 이걸 다 합쳐 놓고 보면, 스무 실링이 그렇게 터무니없는 값은 아닐 듯싶습니다요.\""]], [["“Twenty shillings!”", "\"스무 실링이라!\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones; “why, you just now asked me twenty guineas.”--“How!”", "\"이놈, 방금은 스무 기니라 부르지 않았더냐.\"—\"아니!\" 중사가 외쳤다."], ["cries the serjeant, “sure your honour must have mistaken me: or else I mistook myself--and indeed I am but half awake.", "\"나리께서 잘못 들으셨거나, 아니면 제가 잘못 말했나 봅니다. 사실 제가 아직 잠이 덜 깨서요."], ["Twenty guineas, indeed!", "스무 기니라니, 참!"], ["no wonder your honour flew into such a passion.", "나리께서 그리 발끈하신 것도 무리가 아니지요."], ["I say twenty guineas too.", "제가 스무 기니라 했다니."], ["No, no, I mean twenty shillings, I assure you.", "아니, 아닙니다, 스무 실링이라 한 겁니다, 정말입니다요."], ["And when your honour comes to consider everything, I hope you will not think that so extravagant a price.", "나리께서 이것저것 다 헤아려 보시면, 그게 그렇게 과한 값이라곤 여기지 않으실 줄 압니다."], ["It is indeed true, you may buy a weapon which looks as well for less money.", "하기야 이만큼 그럴듯해 보이는 무기를 더 싸게 살 수야 있겠지요."], ["But----”", "허나—\""]], [["Here Jones interrupted him, saying, “I will be so far from making any words with you, that I will give you a shilling more than your demand.”", "여기서 존스가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"너와 입씨름할 생각이 조금도 없으니, 네가 부른 값보다 한 실링 더 얹어 주마.\""], ["He then gave him a guinea, bid him return to his bed, and wished him a good march; adding, he hoped to overtake them before the division reached Worcester.", "그러고는 그에게 1기니를 건네며 가서 자라 이르고, 행군에 행운을 빌어 주었다. 그러면서 부대가 우스터에 닿기 전에 따라잡았으면 좋겠다고 덧붙였다."]], [["The serjeant very civilly took his leave, fully satisfied with his merchandize, and not a little pleased with his dexterous recovery from that false step into which his opinion of the sick man's light-headedness had betrayed him.", "중사는 아주 공손하게 작별을 고했다. 제 장사에 흡족했을 뿐 아니라, 환자가 헛소리를 한다고 넘겨짚었다가 빠질 뻔한 그 헛디딤에서 솜씨 좋게 벗어난 것에도 적잖이 흐뭇해하면서 말이다."]], [["As soon as the serjeant was departed, Jones rose from his bed, and dressed himself entirely, putting on even his coat, which, as its colour was white, showed very visibly the streams of blood which had flowed down it; and now, having grasped his new-purchased sword in his hand, he was going to issue forth, when the thought of what he was about to undertake laid suddenly hold of him, and he began to reflect that in a few minutes he might possibly deprive a human being of life, or might lose his own.", "중사가 떠나가자마자 존스는 침상에서 일어나 옷을 빠짐없이 갖춰 입었다. 윗옷까지 걸쳤는데, 그것이 흰색이었던 탓에 흘러내린 핏줄기가 아주 또렷이 드러나 보였다."], ["“Very well,” said he, “and in what cause do I venture my life?", "그러고는 새로 산 칼을 손에 거머쥐고 막 나서려는데, 자기가 벌이려는 일이 어떤 일인지 하는 생각이 문득 그를 사로잡았다."], ["Why, in that of my honour.", "그는 몇 분 안에 어쩌면 한 인간의 목숨을 앗을 수도, 자신의 목숨을 잃을 수도 있음을 곰곰 곱씹기 시작했다."], ["And who is this human being?", "\"좋다,\" 그가 말했다, \"한데 나는 무엇을 위해 목숨을 거는가?"], ["A rascal who hath injured and insulted me without provocation.", "그야 내 명예를 위해서다. 그러면 그 인간이란 누구인가?"], ["But is not revenge forbidden by Heaven?", "까닭도 없이 나를 해치고 모욕한 악당이다. 하지만 복수는 하늘이 금하지 않으셨던가?"], ["Yes, but it is enjoined by the world.", "그렇다, 그러나 세상은 그것을 명한다."], ["Well, but shall I obey the world in opposition to the express commands of Heaven?", "그럼 나는 하늘의 분명한 명을 거스르면서까지 세상에 따라야 하는가? 신의 노여움을 사느니, 차라리—흥—겁쟁이니, 비겁한 놈이니 하는 소리를 들으란 말인가?"], ["Shall I incur the Divine displeasure rather than be called--ha--coward--scoundrel?--I'll think no more; I am resolved, and must fight him.”", "더는 생각지 않겠다. 나는 결심했고, 그와 싸워야만 한다.\""]], [["The clock had now struck twelve, and every one in the house were in their beds, except the centinel who stood to guard Northerton, when Jones softly opening his door, issued forth in pursuit of his enemy, of whose place of confinement he had received a perfect description from the drawer.", "이제 시계가 자정을 알렸고, 노더턴을 지키느라 서 있는 보초를 빼고는 집 안의 모두가 잠자리에 들어 있었다. 존스가 가만히 문을 열고 적을 쫓아 나섰을 때 말이다."], ["It is not easy to conceive a much more tremendous figure than he now exhibited.", "그가 갇힌 곳에 대해서는 술집 종업원에게 완벽한 설명을 들어 둔 터였다."], ["He had on, as we have said, a light-coloured coat, covered with streams of blood.", "이때 그가 드러낸 것보다 더 끔찍한 형상을 떠올리기란 쉽지 않을 것이다."], ["His face, which missed that very blood, as well as twenty ounces more drawn from him by the surgeon, was pallid.", "앞서 말했듯 그는 핏줄기가 줄줄이 묻은 옅은 색 윗옷을 걸치고 있었다. 바로 그 피를, 게다가 외과의가 더 뽑아낸 스무 온스의 피까지 잃은 그의 얼굴은 핏기가 가셔 창백했다."], ["Round his head was a quantity of bandage, not unlike a turban.", "머리 둘레에는 붕대가 잔뜩 감겨, 흡사 터번 같았다."], ["In the right hand he carried a sword, and in the left a candle.", "오른손에는 칼을, 왼손에는 촛불을 들고 있었다."], ["So that the bloody Banquo was not worthy to be compared to him.", "그러니 피투성이 뱅코의 유령조차 그에 견줄 자격이 없었다."], ["In fact, I believe a more dreadful apparition was never raised in a church-yard, nor in the imagination of any good people met in a winter evening over a Christmas fire in Somersetshire.", "사실, 이보다 더 무시무시한 망령이 묘지에 불려 나온 적도, 서머싯셔의 어느 겨울 저녁 크리스마스 모닥불가에 둘러앉은 선량한 사람들의 상상 속에 떠오른 적도 없었으리라 나는 믿는다."]], [["When the centinel first saw our heroe approach, his hair began gently to lift up his grenadier cap; and in the same instant his knees fell to blows with each other.", "보초가 우리 주인공이 다가오는 것을 처음 본 순간, 그의 머리카락이 슬그머니 척탄병 모자를 들어 올리기 시작했다. 그와 동시에 두 무릎이 서로 부딪치며 싸움을 벌였다."], ["Presently his whole body was seized with worse than an ague fit.", "이내 그의 온몸이 학질 발작보다 더한 떨림에 사로잡혔다."], ["He then fired his piece, and fell flat on his face.", "그러더니 그는 총을 발사하고는 얼굴을 땅에 박은 채 풀썩 쓰러졌다."]], [["Whether fear or courage was the occasion of his firing, or whether he took aim at the object of his terror, I cannot say.", "그가 총을 쏜 것이 두려움 탓이었는지 용기 탓이었는지, 아니면 그 공포의 대상을 정말로 겨냥했던 것인지는 나도 알 수 없다."], ["If he did, however, he had the good fortune to miss his man.", "다만 겨냥했다 하더라도, 그는 다행히 상대를 빗맞히는 행운을 누렸다."]], [["Jones seeing the fellow fall, guessed the cause of his fright, at which he could not forbear smiling, not in the least reflecting on the danger from which he had just escaped.", "그 사내가 쓰러지는 것을 본 존스는 그가 겁에 질린 까닭을 짐작하고는 웃음을 참지 못했는데, 정작 자신이 방금 벗어난 위험에 대해서는 조금도 생각하지 않았다."], ["He then passed by the fellow, who still continued in the posture in which he fell, and entered the room where Northerton, as he had heard, was confined.", "그러고는 쓰러진 그 자세 그대로 엎어져 있는 사내를 지나쳐, 들은 대로 노더턴이 갇혀 있다는 방으로 들어섰다. 그곳, 호젓한 그 자리에서 그가 발견한 것은—탁자 위에 놓인 빈 한 쿼트들이 술잔이었다."], ["Here, in a solitary situation, he found--an empty quart pot standing on the table, on which some beer being spilt, it looked as if the room had lately been inhabited; but at present it was entirely vacant.", "거기 맥주가 좀 엎질러져 있어, 방금까지 누가 머물렀던 듯 보였다. 그러나 지금은 텅 비어 있었다."]], [["Jones then apprehended it might lead to some other apartment; but upon searching all round it, he could perceive no other door than that at which he entered, and where the centinel had been posted. He then proceeded to call Northerton several times by his name; but no one answered; nor did this serve to any other purpose than to confirm the centinel in his terrors, who was now convinced that the volunteer was dead of his wounds, and that his ghost was come in search of the murderer: he now lay in all the agonies of horror; and I wish, with all my heart, some of those actors who are hereafter to represent a man frighted out of his wits had seen him, that they might be taught to copy nature, instead of performing several antic tricks and gestures, for the entertainment and applause of the galleries.", "그래서 존스는 이 방이 다른 방으로 이어질지도 모른다고 생각했다. 그러나 사방을 샅샅이 뒤져 보아도, 자신이 들어온 문, 곧 보초가 서 있던 그 문 말고는 다른 문을 찾을 수 없었다. 그는 이어 노더턴의 이름을 몇 번이고 불러 보았다. 그러나 아무도 대답하지 않았다. 그리고 이것은 보초의 공포를 더욱 굳히는 것 말고는 아무 소용이 없었으니, 보초는 이제 그 지원병이 상처로 죽었으며 그 유령이 자기를 죽인 자를 찾아온 것이라고 확신해 버렸다. 그는 이제 공포의 온갖 고통 속에 누워 있었다. 나는 진심으로 바라거니와, 앞으로 혼이 빠지도록 겁에 질린 사람을 연기할 배우들 가운데 누군가가 그를 보았더라면 좋았을 것이다. 그랬다면 그들이, 객석의 흥과 갈채를 노린 우스꽝스러운 재주와 몸짓이나 부려 대는 대신, 자연 그대로를 본뜨는 법을 배웠을 테니 말이다."]], [["Perceiving the bird was flown, at least despairing to find him, and rightly apprehending that the report of the firelock would alarm the whole house, our heroe now blew out his candle, and gently stole back again to his chamber, and to his bed; whither he would not have been able to have gotten undiscovered, had any other person been on the same staircase, save only one gentleman who was confined to his bed by the gout; for before he could reach the door to his chamber, the hall where the centinel had been posted was half full of people, some in their shirts, and others not half drest, all very earnestly enquiring of each other what was the matter.", "새가 이미 날아가 버렸음을, 적어도 그를 찾기란 글렀음을 깨닫고, 또 총소리가 온 집안을 깨우리라는 것을 옳게 알아챈 우리 주인공은, 이제 촛불을 끄고 가만히 제 방으로, 제 침상으로 살그머니 되돌아갔다. 만일 같은 계단에 다른 사람이 하나라도 있었더라면 들키지 않고 거기까지 가지는 못했을 것이나, 마침 그 계단에는 통풍으로 침상에 누워 지내는 신사 한 사람뿐이었다. 그가 미처 제 방 문에 닿기도 전에, 보초가 서 있던 그 홀은 셔츠 바람인 사람, 옷도 제대로 못 걸친 사람으로 이미 절반쯤 들어찼고, 다들 무슨 일이냐고 서로에게 다급히 캐묻고 있었으니 말이다."]], [["The soldier was now found lying in the same place and posture in which we just now left him.", "이제 그 병사는 우리가 방금 그를 두고 온 바로 그 자리, 그 자세 그대로 누워 있는 채로 발견되었다."], ["Several immediately applied themselves to raise him, and some concluded him dead; but they presently saw their mistake, for he not only struggled with those who laid their hands on him, but fell a roaring like a bull.", "여럿이 곧장 달려들어 그를 일으키려 했고, 더러는 그가 죽었다고 단정했다. 그러나 그들은 이내 자기들이 잘못 봤음을 알았으니, 그는 자기 몸에 손을 대는 자들과 몸부림치며 다툴 뿐 아니라, 황소처럼 고래고래 울부짖기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["In reality, he imagined so many spirits or devils were handling him; for his imagination being possessed with the horror of an apparition, converted every object he saw or felt into nothing but ghosts and spectres.", "사실 그는 수많은 혼령이나 악마들이 자기를 붙잡고 있다고 여기고 있었다. 망령의 공포에 사로잡힌 그의 상상이, 보거나 만지는 모든 것을 한낱 귀신이며 유령으로 바꿔 놓았던 것이다."]], [["At length he was overpowered by numbers, and got upon his legs; when candles being brought, and seeing two or three of his comrades present, he came a little to himself; but when they asked him what was the matter?", "마침내 그는 머릿수에 눌려 두 다리로 일어섰다. 촛불이 들어오고 동료 두세 명이 곁에 있는 것을 보자, 그는 조금 정신을 차렸다."], ["he answered, “I am a dead man, that's all, I am a dead man, I can't recover it, I have seen him.”", "그러나 사람들이 무슨 일이냐고 묻자, 그는 이렇게 대답했다. \"나는 죽은 목숨이야, 그뿐이지, 나는 죽은 목숨이라고, 도저히 못 살아나, 내가 그를 봤단 말이야.\""], ["“What hast thou seen, Jack?”", "\"잭, 뭘 봤다는 거야?\""], ["says one of the soldiers.", "병사 중 하나가 물었다."], ["“Why, I have seen the young volunteer that was killed yesterday.”", "\"어제 죽은 그 젊은 지원병을 봤다고.\""], ["He then imprecated the most heavy curses on himself, if he had not seen the volunteer, all over blood, vomiting fire out of his mouth and nostrils, pass by him into the chamber where Ensign Northerton was, and then seizing the ensign by the throat, fly away with him in a clap of thunder.", "그러고는 만일 자신이 그 지원병을 본 게 아니라면 가장 무거운 저주가 제게 떨어져도 좋다고 퍼부었다. 온몸이 피투성이인 채로 입과 콧구멍에서 불을 토해 내며 자기 곁을 지나 노더턴 소위가 있던 방으로 들어가더니, 소위의 멱을 거머쥐고는 우레 같은 소리와 함께 그를 데리고 날아가 버렸다는 것이었다."]], [["This relation met with a gracious reception from the audience.", "이 이야기는 청중에게서 너그러운 환대를 받았다."], ["All the women present believed it firmly, and prayed Heaven to defend them from murder.", "그 자리의 여인들은 모두 그것을 굳게 믿고는, 부디 살해당하는 일에서 자기들을 지켜 달라고 하늘에 기도했다. 남자들 중에도 이 이야기를 믿는 이가 많았다."], ["Amongst the men too, many had faith in the story; but others turned it into derision and ridicule; and a serjeant who was present answered very coolly, “Young man, you will hear more of this, for going to sleep and dreaming on your post.”", "그러나 더러는 그것을 비웃음과 조롱거리로 돌렸고, 그 자리에 있던 한 중사는 아주 냉랭하게 대꾸했다. \"젊은 친구, 보초를 서다 잠이 들어 꿈이나 꾼 일로 자네는 두고두고 시달리게 될 걸세.\""]], [["The soldier replied, “You may punish me if you please; but I was as broad awake as I am now; and the devil carry me away, as he hath the ensign, if I did not see the dead man, as I tell you, with eyes as big and as fiery as two large flambeaux.”", "병사가 대꾸했다. \"벌을 주시려거든 얼마든지 주십쇼. 하지만 저는 지금처럼 또렷이 깨어 있었습니다. 소위를 데려갔듯이 악마가 저를 데려가도 좋으니, 제가 말씀드린 대로 그 죽은 사람을, 두 자루 커다란 횃불처럼 크고 시뻘건 눈을 한 그 사람을 정녕 보지 않았다면 말입니다.\""]], [["The commander of the forces, and the commander of the house, were now both arrived; for the former being awake at the time, and hearing the centinel fire his piece, thought it his duty to rise immediately, though he had no great apprehensions of any mischief; whereas the apprehensions of the latter were much greater, lest her spoons and tankards should be upon the march, without having received any such orders from her.", "이제 병력의 지휘관과 이 집의 안주인이 둘 다 당도했다. 전자는 그때 깨어 있다가 보초가 총 쏘는 소리를 듣고는, 무슨 변고가 있으리라 크게 걱정한 것은 아니었으나 즉시 일어나는 것이 제 도리라 여겼던 것이다. 반면 후자의 걱정은 훨씬 더 컸으니, 제 숟가락이며 술잔들이 자기로부터 그런 명령을 받은 바도 없이 행군길에 오르지나 않았을까 해서였다."]], [["Our poor centinel, to whom the sight of this officer was not much more welcome than the apparition, as he thought it, which he had seen before, again related the dreadful story, and with many additions of blood and fire; but he had the misfortune to gain no credit with either of the last-mentioned persons: for the officer, though a very religious man, was free from all terrors of this kind; besides, having so lately left Jones in the condition we have seen, he had no suspicion of his being dead. As for the landlady, though not over religious, she had no kind of aversion to the doctrine of spirits; but there was a circumstance in the tale which she well knew to be false, as we shall inform the reader presently.", "우리의 가엾은 보초에게는, 앞서 보았다고 여긴 그 망령보다 이 장교의 모습이 그리 더 반가울 것도 없었지만, 그는 또 한 번 그 끔찍한 이야기를, 피와 불을 잔뜩 보태어 들려주었다. 그러나 그는 방금 말한 두 사람 중 누구의 믿음도 얻지 못하는 불운을 겪었다. 장교는 매우 신앙심 깊은 사람이었으되 이런 종류의 공포에서는 자유로웠고, 게다가 우리가 본 그 상태로 존스를 떠나온 지 얼마 되지도 않은 터라 그가 죽었으리라곤 조금도 의심하지 않았기 때문이다. 안주인으로 말하자면, 그리 신앙심이 깊지는 않았으나 혼령에 관한 가르침을 딱히 꺼리지도 않았다. 다만 이 이야기에는 그녀가 거짓임을 빤히 아는 한 대목이 있었으니, 그것은 곧 독자에게 알려 주겠다."]], [["But whether Northerton was carried away in thunder or fire, or in whatever other manner he was gone, it was now certain that his body was no longer in custody.", "그러나 노더턴이 우레에 실려 갔든 불에 실려 갔든, 아니면 그 밖의 어떤 식으로 사라졌든 간에, 이제 그의 몸이 더는 감금 상태에 있지 않다는 것만큼은 분명했다."], ["Upon this occasion the lieutenant formed a conclusion not very different from what the serjeant is just mentioned to have made before, and immediately ordered the centinel to be taken prisoner.", "이 일을 두고 중위는 앞서 그 중사가 내렸다고 방금 말한 것과 그리 다르지 않은 결론에 이르렀고, 즉시 보초를 잡아 가두라 명했다."], ["So that, by a strange reverse of fortune (though not very uncommon in a military life), the guard became the guarded.", "그리하여 운명의 기묘한 뒤집힘으로(군대 생활에서 그리 드문 일은 아니지만), 지키던 자가 지킴을 당하는 신세가 되고 말았다."]], [["The conclusion of the foregoing adventure.", "앞서 벌어진 모험의 결말."]], [["Besides the suspicion of sleep, the lieutenant harboured another and worse doubt against the poor centinel, and this was, that of treachery; for as he believed not one syllable of the apparition, so he imagined the whole to be an invention formed only to impose upon him, and that the fellow had in reality been bribed by Northerton to let him escape.", "잠들었다는 의심 말고도, 중위는 가엾은 보초병을 향해 또 하나의, 그보다 더 고약한 의심을 품고 있었다. 바로 배신이라는 의심이었다."], ["And this he imagined the rather, as the fright appeared to him the more unnatural in one who had the character of as brave and bold a man as any in the regiment, having been in several actions, having received several wounds, and, in a word, having behaved himself always like a good and valiant soldier.", "그는 유령 이야기 따위는 단 한 마디도 믿지 않았기에, 그 모든 것이 자기를 속이려고 꾸며낸 지어낸 이야기일 뿐이며, 실은 이 사내가 노더턴에게 매수되어 그를 도망치게 놓아준 것이라고 짐작했다. 더욱이 그가 이렇게 의심한 까닭은, 연대에서 누구 못지않게 용감하고 대담하다는 평을 듣던 자, 여러 차례 전투를 겪고 여러 군데 부상을 입었으며, 한마디로 늘 훌륭하고 용맹한 병사답게 처신해 온 자에게 그런 공포란 더욱 부자연스러워 보였기 때문이다."]], [["That the reader, therefore, may not conceive the least ill opinion of such a person, we shall not delay a moment in rescuing his character from the imputation of this guilt.", "그러니 독자가 이런 사람에 대해 조금이라도 나쁜 인상을 품지 않도록, 우리는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 그의 인품을 이 죄의 혐의에서 구해 내려 한다."]], [["Mr Northerton then, as we have before observed, was fully satisfied with the glory which he had obtained from this action.", "그런데 노더턴 씨는, 앞서 말했듯이 이번 일로 얻은 영광에 더없이 흡족해하고 있었다."], ["He had perhaps seen, or heard, or guessed, that envy is apt to attend fame.", "그는 어쩌면 명성에는 시기가 따라붙기 마련이라는 것을 보았거나, 들었거나, 짐작했는지도 모른다."], ["Not that I would here insinuate that he was heathenishly inclined to believe in or to worship the goddess Nemesis; for, in fact, I am convinced he never heard of her name.", "그렇다고 여기서 내가, 그가 이교도처럼 복수의 여신 네메시스를 믿거나 떠받드는 쪽으로 기울었다는 뜻을 넌지시 비치려는 것은 아니다."], ["He was, besides, of an active disposition, and had a great antipathy to those close quarters in the castle of Gloucester, for which a justice of peace might possibly give him a billet.", "사실 나는 그가 그 여신의 이름조차 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없으리라 확신한다. 게다가 그는 활동적인 기질이었던지라, 치안판사가 그에게 숙소를 마련해 줄지도 모를 글로스터 성의 그 비좁은 감방을 몹시 꺼렸다."], ["Nor was he moreover free from some uneasy meditations on a certain wooden edifice, which I forbear to name, in conformity to the opinion of mankind, who, I think, rather ought to honour than to be ashamed of this building, as it is, or at least might be made, of more benefit to society than almost any other public erection.", "또한 그는, 내가 차마 이름을 입에 올리지 않으려는 어떤 나무로 된 구조물에 대해서도 불안한 상념을 떨치지 못하고 있었다. 이 구조물은 다른 거의 모든 공공 건축물보다 사회에 더 유익한 것이거나 적어도 그렇게 만들 수 있는 것이니, 내 생각에 사람들은 그것을 부끄러워하기보다는 오히려 기려야 마땅하건만, 나는 세상 사람들의 통념에 따라 그 이름을 삼가려 한다."], ["In a word, to hint at no more reasons for his conduct, Mr Northerton was desirous of departing that evening, and nothing remained for him but to contrive the quomodo, which appeared to be a matter of some difficulty.", "한마디로, 그의 행동에 대해 더는 이유를 들먹이지 않더라도, 노더턴 씨는 그날 저녁에 떠나고 싶어 했고, 그에게 남은 일이라곤 그 방법을 궁리하는 것뿐이었는데, 그것이 자못 어려운 문제로 보였다."]], [["Now this young gentleman, though somewhat crooked in his morals, was perfectly straight in his person, which was extremely strong and well made.", "그런데 이 젊은 신사는, 도덕은 다소 비뚤어졌을지언정 몸만큼은 더할 나위 없이 곧았으니, 매우 튼튼하고 잘 빠진 체격이었다."], ["His face too was accounted handsome by the generality of women, for it was broad and ruddy, with tolerably good teeth.", "게다가 그의 얼굴은 대다수 여인들에게 잘생겼다는 평을 받았는데, 널찍하고 혈색이 좋은 데다 치아도 그런대로 가지런했기 때문이다."], ["Such charms did not fail making an impression on my landlady, who had no little relish for this kind of beauty.", "이런 매력은 이런 부류의 미남을 적잖이 즐기던 우리 안주인에게 인상을 남기지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She had, indeed, a real compassion for the young man; and hearing from the surgeon that affairs were like to go ill with the volunteer, she suspected they might hereafter wear no benign aspect with the ensign.", "실로 그녀는 이 젊은이를 진심으로 가엾게 여겼고, 외과의로부터 지원병 쪽 형편이 좋지 않을 듯하다는 말을 듣자, 앞으로 소위에게도 사정이 결코 순탄하지 않으리라 짐작했다. 그리하여 그를 찾아가도 좋다는 허락을 받아 낸 뒤, 몹시 우울한 기색에 잠긴 그를 보고서, 지원병의 목숨이 거의 가망 없다는 말로 그 우울을 한층 부추긴 다음, 그녀는 슬며시 몇 마디 운을 띄우기 시작했다."], ["Having obtained, therefore, leave to make him a visit, and finding him in a very melancholy mood, which she considerably heightened by telling him there were scarce any hopes of the volunteer's life, she proceeded to throw forth some hints, which the other readily and eagerly taking up, they soon came to a right understanding; and it was at length agreed that the ensign should, at a certain signal, ascend the chimney, which communicating very soon with that of the kitchen, he might there again let himself down; for which she would give him an opportunity by keeping the coast clear.", "상대가 그것을 선뜻, 또 열렬히 받아들이니, 두 사람은 이내 제대로 뜻이 통했고, 마침내 이렇게 합의를 보았다. 소위가 어떤 신호에 맞추어 굴뚝을 타고 올라가면, 그 굴뚝이 곧장 부엌 굴뚝과 통하니 거기로 다시 내려올 수 있고, 그녀가 사람들이 다니지 않도록 길을 비워 그 틈을 마련해 준다는 것이었다."]], [["But lest our readers, of a different complexion, should take this occasion of too hastily condemning all compassion as a folly, and pernicious to society, we think proper to mention another particular which might possibly have some little share in this action.", "하지만 기질이 다른 독자가 이 일을 빌미로, 모든 연민을 어리석음이요 사회에 해로운 것이라고 너무 성급히 단죄하지 않도록, 우리는 이 행동에 어쩌면 조금쯤 한몫했을 또 다른 사정을 밝혀 두는 편이 옳다고 본다."], ["The ensign happened to be at this time possessed of the sum of fifty pounds, which did indeed belong to the whole company; for the captain having quarrelled with his lieutenant, had entrusted the payment of his company to the ensign.", "마침 이때 소위는 오십 파운드의 돈을 지니고 있었는데, 이 돈은 실은 중대 전체의 것이었다. 대위가 자기 중위와 다툰 터라 중대의 급료 지급을 소위에게 맡겼던 것이다."], ["This money, however, he thought proper to deposit in my landlady's hand, possibly by way of bail or security that he would hereafter appear and answer to the charge against him; but whatever were the conditions, certain it is, that she had the money and the ensign his liberty.", "그런데 그는 이 돈을 우리 안주인의 손에 맡겨 두는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼으니, 아마도 앞으로 출두하여 자신에게 걸린 혐의에 답하겠다는 보석금이나 담보 삼아 그리한 것이리라. 그러나 조건이 무엇이었든 간에, 확실한 것은 그녀는 돈을 손에 넣었고 소위는 자유를 얻었다는 사실이다."]], [["The reader may perhaps expect, from the compassionate temper of this good woman, that when she saw the poor centinel taken prisoner for a fact of which she knew him innocent, she should immediately have interposed in his behalf; but whether it was that she had already exhausted all her compassion in the above-mentioned instance, or that the features of this fellow, though not very different from those of the ensign, could not raise it, I will not determine; but, far from being an advocate for the present prisoner, she urged his guilt to his officer, declaring, with uplifted eyes and hands, that she would not have had any concern in the escape of a murderer for all the world.", "독자는 어쩌면 이 마음씨 고운 여인의 동정심 많은 성품으로 미루어, 죄가 없는 줄 뻔히 아는 일로 가엾은 보초병이 잡혀가는 것을 보았으니 곧장 그를 위해 나서 주었으리라 기대할지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀가 앞서 말한 일에서 이미 연민을 모조리 써 버린 탓인지, 아니면 이 사내의 이목구비가 소위와 그리 다르지 않았는데도 그 연민을 자아내지 못한 탓인지는 내가 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀는 지금 잡힌 이 죄수를 두둔하기는커녕, 오히려 그가 죄인이라고 장교에게 다그쳐 말하면서, 눈과 두 손을 치켜든 채 이 세상을 다 준대도 살인자를 도망치게 하는 일에는 끼어들지 않았을 것이라고 단언했다."]], [["Everything was now once more quiet, and most of the company returned again to their beds; but the landlady, either from the natural activity of her disposition, or from her fear for her plate, having no propensity to sleep, prevailed with the officers, as they were to march within little more than an hour, to spend that time with her over a bowl of punch.", "이제 모든 것이 다시 한번 잠잠해졌고, 사람들 대부분은 도로 잠자리로 돌아갔다. 그러나 안주인은 타고난 부산한 기질 탓인지, 아니면 자기 은그릇이 걱정된 탓인지 도무지 잠이 오지 않아, 한 시간 남짓 뒤면 행군해야 할 장교들을 구슬려 그 시간을 자기와 함께 펀치 한 사발을 들며 보내게 했다."]], [["Jones had lain awake all this while, and had heard great part of the hurry and bustle that had passed, of which he had now some curiosity to know the particulars.", "존스는 그동안 내내 깨어 누운 채 그 소동과 부산함의 상당 부분을 들었던 터라, 이제 그 자초지종이 궁금해졌다. 그래서 종을 당겼는데, 적어도 스무 번은 울려 댔건만 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["He therefore applied to his bell, which he rung at least twenty times without any effect: for my landlady was in such high mirth with her company, that no clapper could be heard there but her own; and the drawer and chambermaid, who were sitting together in the kitchen (for neither durst he sit up nor she lie in bed alone), the more they heard the bell ring the more they were frightened, and as it were nailed down in their places.", "안주인이 일행과 어찌나 흥겹게 떠들썩한지, 그곳에서는 그녀 자신의 입놀림 말고는 어떤 종소리도 들리지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 부엌에 함께 앉아 있던 사환과 하녀로 말하자면(사환은 혼자 깨어 앉아 있기가 무서웠고 하녀는 혼자 잠자리에 들기가 무서웠던 까닭이다), 종이 울리는 소리를 들을수록 더욱 겁에 질려, 마치 제자리에 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝도 하지 못했다."]], [["At last, at a lucky interval of chat, the sound reached the ears of our good landlady, who presently sent forth her summons, which both her servants instantly obeyed.", "마침내 잡담이 운 좋게 끊긴 틈을 타, 그 소리가 우리 마음씨 좋은 안주인의 귀에 닿았고, 그녀는 곧장 두 하인을 불러댔으니, 둘 다 즉시 그 부름에 따랐다."], ["“Joe,”", "\"조,\""], ["says the mistress, “don't you hear the gentleman's bell ring?", "안주인이 말했다. \"저 신사분 종소리 안 들려?"], ["Why don't you go up?”--“It is not my business,”", "왜 안 올라가는 게냐?\"—\"방 시중드는 건,\" 사환이 대답했다."], ["answered the drawer, “to wait upon the chambers--it is Betty Chambermaid's.”--“If you come to that,”", "\"제 일이 아닙니다요. 그건 하녀 베티 일이지요.\"—\"그렇게 따질 거면,\""], ["answered the maid, “it is not my business to wait upon gentlemen.", "하녀가 받아쳤다. \"신사분 시중드는 것도 제 일이 아니에요."], ["I have done it indeed sometimes; but the devil fetch me if ever I do again, since you make your preambles about it.”", "가끔 해 드린 적이야 있지만, 네가 그렇게 토를 다는 마당이니 다시 하면 악마가 날 잡아가도 좋아요.\""], ["The bell still ringing violently, their mistress fell into a passion, and swore, if the drawer did not go up immediately, she would turn him away that very morning.", "종이 여전히 사납게 울려 대자, 안주인은 발끈하여, 사환이 당장 올라가지 않으면 바로 그날 아침에 내쫓아 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. \"그러시면, 마님,\""], ["“If you do, madam,”", "사환이 말했다."], ["says he, “I can't help it.", "\"저도 어쩔 수 없지요."], ["I won't do another servant's business.”", "다른 하인 일은 안 합니다요.\""], ["She then applied herself to the maid, and endeavoured to prevail by gentle means; but all in vain: Betty was as inflexible as Joe.", "그러자 그녀는 하녀에게로 돌아서서 부드러운 말로 구슬려 보려 했으나, 모두 헛수고였다. 베티는 조만큼이나 요지부동이었다."], ["Both insisted it was not their business, and they would not do it.", "둘 다 그건 제 일이 아니라며 우겨 댔고, 절대 하지 않겠다고 버텼다."]], [["The lieutenant then fell a laughing, and said, “Come, I will put an end to this contention;”", "그러자 중위가 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"자, 내가 이 다툼을 끝내 주지.\""], ["and then turning to the servants, commended them for their resolution in not giving up the point; but added, he was sure, if one would consent to go the other would.", "그러고는 두 하인을 돌아보며, 끝까지 고집을 꺾지 않은 그 결의를 칭찬하더니, 다만 한쪽이 가는 데 동의하면 다른 쪽도 갈 것이 분명하다고 덧붙였다."], ["To which proposal they both agreed in an instant, and accordingly went up very lovingly and close together.", "이 제안에 둘은 그 자리에서 곧바로 동의했고, 그리하여 아주 다정하게 바싹 붙은 채 함께 올라갔다."], ["When they were gone, the lieutenant appeased the wrath of the landlady, by satisfying her why they were both so unwilling to go alone.", "둘이 가고 나자, 중위는 어째서 둘 다 혼자 가기를 그토록 꺼렸는지 까닭을 일러 주어 안주인의 노여움을 가라앉혔다."]], [["They returned soon after, and acquainted their mistress, that the sick gentleman was so far from being dead, that he spoke as heartily as if he was well; and that he gave his service to the captain, and should be very glad of the favour of seeing him before he marched.", "둘은 곧 돌아와, 그 아픈 신사가 죽기는커녕 멀쩡한 사람처럼 기운차게 말하더라고, 그리고 대위께 안부를 전하며 행군하기 전에 한번 만나 뵐 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘겠다고 하더라고 안주인에게 알렸다."]], [["The good lieutenant immediately complied with his desires, and sitting down by his bed-side, acquainted him with the scene which had happened below, concluding with his intentions to make an example of the centinel.", "마음씨 좋은 중위는 곧바로 그 청에 응하여, 침상 곁에 앉아 아래층에서 벌어진 광경을 일러 주고는, 그 보초병을 본보기로 삼겠다는 자신의 뜻으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["Upon this Jones related to him the whole truth, and earnestly begged him not to punish the poor soldier, “who, I am confident,”", "이에 존스는 그에게 모든 진실을 들려주고, 그 가엾은 병사를 벌하지 말아 달라고 간곡히 청했다. \"저는 확신합니다,\""], ["says he, “is as innocent of the ensign's escape, as he is of forging any lie, or of endeavouring to impose on you.”", "그가 말했다. \"그자가 소위의 도주에 아무 죄가 없는 것은, 거짓말을 지어내거나 당신을 속이려 든 일이 전혀 없는 것과 마찬가지입니다.\""]], [["The lieutenant hesitated a few moments, and then answered: “Why, as you have cleared the fellow of one part of the charge, so it will be impossible to prove the other, because he was not the only centinel.", "중위는 잠깐 머뭇거리다가 이렇게 답했다. \"흠, 자네가 그 친구의 혐의 한쪽을 벗겨 주었으니, 다른 한쪽도 입증하기란 불가능하겠군."], ["But I have a good mind to punish the rascal for being a coward.", "보초가 그자 혼자만은 아니었으니 말이야. 하지만 그 비겁한 죄만큼은 그 녀석을 벌하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같아."], ["Yet who knows what effect the terror of such an apprehension may have?", "그래도 그런 체포의 공포가 어떤 효과를 낼지 누가 알겠나?"], ["and, to say the truth, he hath always behaved well against an enemy.", "게다가 솔직히 말하면, 그자는 적을 상대로는 늘 잘 처신해 왔거든."], ["Come, it is a good thing to see any sign of religion in these fellows; so I promise you he shall be set at liberty when we march.", "자, 이런 친구들에게서 신앙의 기미라도 보이는 건 좋은 일이지. 그러니 약속하지, 우리가 행군할 때 그자를 풀어 주겠네."], ["But hark, the general beats.", "그런데 들어 보게, 집합 북이 울리는군."], ["My dear boy, give me another buss.", "사랑하는 친구여, 입맞춤 한 번 더 해 주게."], ["Don't discompose nor hurry yourself; but remember the Christian doctrine of patience, and I warrant you will soon be able to do yourself justice, and to take an honourable revenge on the fellow who hath injured you.”", "마음을 어지럽히거나 서두르지 말고, 인내라는 기독교의 가르침을 기억하게. 그러면 머잖아 자네 손으로 정의를 세우고, 자네에게 상처를 입힌 그자에게 떳떳한 복수를 할 수 있게 되리라 내 장담하네.\""], ["The lieutenant then departed, and Jones endeavoured to compose himself to rest.", "그러고 나서 중위는 떠나갔고, 존스는 마음을 가라앉혀 쉬어 보려 애썼다."]], [["A wonderful long chapter concerning the marvellous; being much the longest of all our introductory chapters.", "약 이틀간의 이야기. 경이로움에 관한, 놀랍도록 긴 장. 우리의 모든 서론 격 장들 가운데 단연 가장 길다."]], [["As we are now entering upon a book in which the course of our history will oblige us to relate some matters of a more strange and surprizing kind than any which have hitherto occurred, it may not be amiss, in the prolegomenous or introductory chapter, to say something of that species of writing which is called the marvellous.", "이제 우리는 지금까지 등장한 그 어떤 것보다도 더 기이하고 놀라운 일들을 들려주지 않을 수 없는 책으로 들어서려 하므로, 이 머리말 격 서론 장에서 이른바 경이로움이라 불리는 글쓰기의 한 갈래에 대해 몇 마디 해 두는 것도 나쁘지 않을 듯하다."], ["To this we shall, as well for the sake of ourselves as of others, endeavour to set some certain bounds, and indeed nothing can be more necessary, as critics[*] of different complexions are here apt to run into very different extremes; for while some are, with M.", "우리는 우리 자신을 위해서나 남들을 위해서나 여기에 어떤 분명한 한계를 그어 보려 애쓸 것이며, 실로 이보다 더 필요한 일도 없다. 이 대목에서는 서로 다른 기질의 비평가들이 매우 다른 양극단으로 치닫기 쉽기 때문이다."], ["Dacier, ready to allow, that the same thing which is impossible may be yet probable,[**] others have so little historic or poetic faith, that they believe nothing to be either possible or probable, the like to which hath not occurred to their own observation.", "어떤 이들은 다시에 씨처럼, 불가능한 바로 그것이 그럼에도 개연성을 지닐 수 있다고 선뜻 인정하는가 하면, 다른 이들은 역사적 믿음도 시적 믿음도 너무나 박약하여, 자기 눈으로 직접 보지 못한 것과 비슷한 일이라면 가능하지도 개연성도 없다고 여긴다."]], [["[*] By this word here, and in most other parts of our work, we mean every reader in the world.", "[*] 여기서, 그리고 우리 작품의 거의 모든 다른 대목에서 이 말은 세상의 모든 독자를 뜻한다."], ["[**] It is happy for M. Dacier that he was not an Irishman.", "[**] 다시에 씨가 아일랜드 사람이 아니었던 것은 다행이다."]], [["First, then, I think it may very reasonably be required of every writer, that he keeps within the bounds of possibility; and still remembers that what it is not possible for man to perform, it is scarce possible for man to believe he did perform.", "그러니 먼저, 나는 모든 작가에게 가능성의 한계 안에 머물 것을 요구하는 것이 매우 합당하다고 생각한다. 그리고 인간이 행할 수 없는 일이라면, 인간이 그것을 행했다고 믿기란 거의 불가능하다는 점을 늘 기억해야 한다."], ["This conviction perhaps gave birth to many stories of the antient heathen deities (for most of them are of poetical original).", "어쩌면 이러한 확신이 옛 이교 신들에 관한 숱한 이야기를 낳았는지도 모른다(그 대부분이 시적 기원을 지니기 때문이다)."], ["The poet, being desirous to indulge a wanton and extravagant imagination, took refuge in that power, of the extent of which his readers were no judges, or rather which they imagined to be infinite, and consequently they could not be shocked at any prodigies related of it.", "시인은 제멋대로 분방한 상상을 마음껏 펼치고 싶은 나머지, 그 위력의 정도를 독자들이 판가름할 수 없는, 아니 오히려 독자들이 무한하다고 상상하는 그런 힘에 기댔다. 그리하여 독자들은 그 힘에 관해 어떤 기적이 이야기되더라도 충격받을 일이 없었던 것이다."], ["This hath been strongly urged in defence of Homer's miracles; and it is perhaps a defence; not, as Mr Pope would have it, because Ulysses told a set of foolish lies to the Phaeacians, who were a very dull nation; but because the poet himself wrote to heathens, to whom poetical fables were articles of faith.", "이는 호메로스의 기적들을 옹호하는 데 강력하게 내세워져 왔으며, 어쩌면 변호가 될 만하다. 다만 포프 씨가 주장하듯, 율리시스가 매우 아둔한 민족이던 파이아키아인들에게 어리석은 거짓말 한 보따리를 늘어놓았기 때문이 아니라, 시인 자신이 이교도들을 위해 글을 썼고 그들에게는 시적 우화가 신앙의 조항이었기 때문이다."], ["For my own part, I must confess, so compassionate is my temper, I wish Polypheme had confined himself to his milk diet, and preserved his eye; nor could Ulysses be much more concerned than myself, when his companions were turned into swine by Circe, who showed, I think, afterwards, too much regard for man's flesh to be supposed capable of converting it into bacon.", "내 자신으로 말하자면, 내 성정이 워낙 측은지심이 많은지라, 폴리페모스가 우유 식단에 그쳐 제 눈을 보전했더라면 좋았겠다 싶다고 고백하지 않을 수 없다. 또한 키르케가 율리시스의 동료들을 돼지로 둔갑시켰을 때 율리시스가 느낀 것 못지않게 나도 마음이 쓰였는데, 키르케는 내 생각에 나중에 사람의 살에 너무 큰 애착을 보였으니 그것을 베이컨으로 만들 수 있는 사람이라고는 도저히 여겨지지 않는다."], ["I wish, likewise, with all my heart, that Homer could have known the rule prescribed by Horace, to introduce supernatural agents as seldom as possible.", "마찬가지로 나는 진심으로, 호메로스가 초자연적 행위자를 되도록 드물게 등장시키라는 호라티우스의 규칙을 알았더라면 좋았겠다 싶다."], ["We should not then have seen his gods coming on trivial errands, and often behaving themselves so as not only to forfeit all title to respect, but to become the objects of scorn and derision.", "그랬다면 우리는 그의 신들이 하찮은 심부름이나 다니고, 흔히 존경받을 자격을 죄다 잃을 뿐 아니라 조롱과 비웃음의 대상이 될 만큼 처신하는 꼴을 보지 않았을 것이다. 이런 행태는 경건하고 사려 깊은 이교도의 믿음조차 충격에 빠뜨렸을 것이며, 결코 변호될 수 없는 것이었다."], ["A conduct which must have shocked the credulity of a pious and sagacious heathen; and which could never have been defended, unless by agreeing with a supposition to which I have been sometimes almost inclined, that this most glorious poet, as he certainly was, had an intent to burlesque the superstitious faith of his own age and country.", "다만 내가 이따금 거의 기울곤 했던 어떤 가정에 동의한다면 모를까 말이다. 즉 이 더없이 영광스러운 시인이—실제로 그러했지만—제 시대와 나라의 미신적 신앙을 풍자하려는 의도를 품었으리라는 가정이다."]], [["But I have rested too long on a doctrine which can be of no use to a Christian writer; for as he cannot introduce into his works any of that heavenly host which make a part of his creed, so it is horrid puerility to search the heathen theology for any of those deities who have been long since dethroned from their immortality.", "하지만 나는 기독교 작가에게는 아무 쓸모도 없는 교리에 너무 오래 머물렀다. 그는 자기 신조의 일부를 이루는 저 천상의 무리를 작품에 끌어들일 수 없거니와, 그렇다고 오래전에 불멸의 자리에서 끌어내려진 신들을 이교 신학에서 뒤지는 것 또한 끔찍한 유치함이기 때문이다."], ["Lord Shaftesbury observes, that nothing is more cold than the invocation of a muse by a modern; he might have added, that nothing can be more absurd.", "섀프츠베리 경은 현대인이 뮤즈를 불러내는 것만큼 싸늘한 것도 없다고 지적했는데, 거기에 그것만큼 터무니없는 것도 없다고 덧붙일 수도 있었으리라."], ["A modern may with much more elegance invoke a ballad, as some have thought Homer did, or a mug of ale, with the author of Hudibras; which latter may perhaps have inspired much more poetry, as well as prose, than all the liquors of Hippocrene or Helicon.", "현대인이라면 어떤 이들이 호메로스가 그랬으리라 여겼듯 발라드를 불러내거나, 《휴디브라스》의 작가처럼 에일 한 잔을 불러내는 편이 훨씬 더 우아할 것이다. 어쩌면 이 에일 한 잔이 히포크레네나 헬리콘의 온갖 샘물을 다 합친 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 시와 산문에 영감을 불어넣었을지도 모른다."]], [["The only supernatural agents which can in any manner be allowed to us moderns, are ghosts; but of these I would advise an author to be extremely sparing.", "우리 현대인에게 어떤 식으로든 허용될 수 있는 유일한 초자연적 행위자는 유령뿐이다. 하지만 이마저도 작가에게는 극도로 아껴 쓰라고 권하고 싶다."], ["These are indeed, like arsenic, and other dangerous drugs in physic, to be used with the utmost caution; nor would I advise the introduction of them at all in those works, or by those authors, to which, or to whom, a horse-laugh in the reader would be any great prejudice or mortification.", "이것들은 실로 의약에서의 비소나 그 밖의 위험한 약물처럼 더없이 조심스럽게 써야 하는 법이다. 또한 독자의 폭소가 작품이나 작가에게 큰 흠이나 망신이 될 만한 그런 작품, 그런 작가의 경우에는 아예 유령을 끌어들이지 말라고 권하고 싶다."]], [["As for elves and fairies, and other such mummery, I purposely omit the mention of them, as I should be very unwilling to confine within any bounds those surprizing imaginations, for whose vast capacity the limits of human nature are too narrow; whose works are to be considered as a new creation; and who have consequently just right to do what they will with their own.", "요정이며 선녀며 그 밖의 그런 익살극에 대해서는, 나는 일부러 언급을 빼겠다. 인간 본성의 한계로는 너무 좁아 다 담을 수 없을 만큼 광대한 그릇을 지닌 저 놀라운 상상력을 어떤 한계 안에 가두는 것이 나로서는 영 내키지 않기 때문이다. 그들의 작품은 하나의 새로운 창조로 여겨져야 하며, 따라서 그들은 제 것을 가지고 제 마음대로 할 정당한 권리를 지닌다."]], [["Man therefore is the highest subject (unless on very extraordinary occasions indeed) which presents itself to the pen of our historian, or of our poet; and, in relating his actions, great care is to be taken that we do not exceed the capacity of the agent we describe.", "그러므로 인간이야말로(정말로 매우 특별한 경우가 아니고서는) 우리 역사가나 우리 시인의 붓 앞에 놓이는 가장 높은 소재이다. 그리고 그의 행동을 들려줄 때는, 우리가 묘사하는 행위자의 능력 범위를 넘어서지 않도록 크게 주의해야 한다."]], [["Nor is possibility alone sufficient to justify us; we must keep likewise within the rules of probability.", "또한 가능성만으로 우리가 정당화되는 것도 아니다. 우리는 마찬가지로 개연성의 규칙 안에도 머물러야 한다."], ["It is, I think, the opinion of Aristotle; or if not, it is the opinion of some wise man, whose authority will be as weighty when it is as old, “That it is no excuse for a poet who relates what is incredible, that the thing related is really matter of fact.”", "이는 내 생각에 아리스토텔레스의 견해이거나, 설령 아니더라도 어떤 현인의 견해이다. 그 권위는 세월이 지나 오래될수록 그만큼 묵직해질 것이다."], ["This may perhaps be allowed true with regard to poetry, but it may be thought impracticable to extend it to the historian; for he is obliged to record matters as he finds them, though they may be of so extraordinary a nature as will require no small degree of historical faith to swallow them.", "\"믿기 어려운 것을 들려주는 시인에게, 들려준 그것이 실제로 사실이라는 점은 변명이 되지 못한다.\" 이는 어쩌면 시에 관해서는 옳다고 인정할 만하지만, 역사가에게까지 넓히기란 실현 불가능하다고 여겨질 수 있다."], ["Such was the successless armament of Xerxes described by Herodotus, or the successful expedition of Alexander related by Arrian.", "역사가는 자기가 발견한 그대로 일들을 기록해야 하기 때문이다. 비록 그것들이 삼키려면 적잖은 역사적 믿음이 필요할 만큼 비범한 성질의 것이라 해도 말이다."], ["Such of later years was the victory of Agincourt obtained by Harry the Fifth, or that of Narva won by Charles the Twelfth of Sweden.", "헤로도토스가 묘사한 크세르크세스의 실패한 군사 원정이나, 아리아노스가 들려준 알렉산드로스의 성공한 원정이 그러하다. 후대로 와서는 헨리 5세가 거둔 아쟁쿠르의 승리, 혹은 스웨덴의 카를 12세가 거둔 나르바의 승리가 그러하다."], ["All which instances, the more we reflect on them, appear still the more astonishing.", "이 모든 사례는 곱씹어 볼수록 한층 더 놀랍게만 보인다."]], [["Such facts, however, as they occur in the thread of the story, nay, indeed, as they constitute the essential parts of it, the historian is not only justifiable in recording as they really happened, but indeed would be unpardonable should he omit or alter them.", "그러나 그러한 사실들은 이야기의 실타래 속에 나타나는 한, 아니 실로 이야기의 본질적인 부분을 이루는 한, 역사가는 그것들을 실제로 일어난 그대로 기록하는 것이 정당할 뿐 아니라, 만일 그것들을 빠뜨리거나 고친다면 도리어 용서받지 못할 것이다."], ["But there are other facts not of such consequence nor so necessary, which, though ever so well attested, may nevertheless be sacrificed to oblivion in complacence to the scepticism of a reader.", "하지만 그만큼 중대하지도 않고 그만큼 꼭 필요하지도 않은 다른 사실들이 있는데, 이런 것들은 아무리 잘 입증되었다 한들 독자의 회의(懷疑)에 영합하여 망각 속에 묻어 버려도 무방하다."], ["Such is that memorable story of the ghost of George Villiers, which might with more propriety have been made a present of to Dr Drelincourt, to have kept the ghost of Mrs Veale company, at the head of his Discourse upon Death, than have been introduced into so solemn a work as the History of the Rebellion.", "조지 빌리어스의 유령이라는 저 유명한 이야기가 그러하니, 그것은 《반란의 역사》처럼 그토록 엄숙한 저작에 끌어들여지기보다는, 드렐랭쿠르 박사에게 선사되어 그의 《죽음에 관한 논고》 머리에 실린 빌 부인의 유령과 짝을 이루게 했더라면 훨씬 더 어울렸을 것이다."]], [["To say the truth, if the historian will confine himself to what really happened, and utterly reject any circumstance, which, though never so well attested, he must be well assured is false, he will sometimes fall into the marvellous, but never into the incredible.", "사실대로 말하자면, 역사가가 실제로 일어난 일에만 자신을 한정하고, 아무리 잘 입증되었다 해도 거짓임이 분명한 정황이라면 무엇이든 단호히 물리친다면, 그는 이따금 경이로움에 빠질지언정 결코 믿을 수 없는 것에 빠지지는 않을 것이다."], ["He will often raise the wonder and surprize of his reader, but never that incredulous hatred mentioned by Horace.", "그는 독자의 놀라움과 경탄을 자주 불러일으키겠지만, 호라티우스가 말한 저 못 믿겠다는 식의 혐오는 결코 일으키지 않을 것이다."], ["It is by falling into fiction, therefore, that we generally offend against this rule, of deserting probability, which the historian seldom, if ever, quits, till he forsakes his character and commences a writer of romance.", "그러므로 우리가 이 규칙, 곧 개연성을 저버리는 일을 어기는 것은 대체로 허구에 빠질 때이며, 역사가는 제 본분을 버리고 연애소설 작가로 나서기 전까지는 좀처럼, 아니 결코 이 개연성을 떠나지 않는다."], ["In this, however, those historians who relate public transactions, have the advantage of us who confine ourselves to scenes of private life.", "다만 이 점에서, 공적 사건을 들려주는 역사가들은 사사로운 삶의 장면에 자신을 한정하는 우리보다 유리하다."], ["The credit of the former is by common notoriety supported for a long time; and public records, with the concurrent testimony of many authors, bear evidence to their truth in future ages.", "전자의 신빙성은 널리 알려진 사실에 의해 오래도록 떠받쳐지며, 공적 기록과 여러 저자의 일치하는 증언이 훗날의 시대에까지 그 진실성을 증언해 준다."], ["Thus a Trajan and an Antoninus, a Nero and a Caligula, have all met with the belief of posterity; and no one doubts but that men so very good, and so very bad, were once the masters of mankind.", "그리하여 트라야누스와 안토니누스, 네로와 칼리굴라가 모두 후세의 믿음을 얻었으니, 그토록 선한 사람들과 그토록 악한 사람들이 한때 인류의 주인이었음을 아무도 의심하지 않는다."]], [["But we who deal in private character, who search into the most retired recesses, and draw forth examples of virtue and vice from holes and corners of the world, are in a more dangerous situation.", "그러나 사사로운 인물을 다루고, 가장 외진 구석을 뒤지며, 세상의 구멍과 모퉁이에서 미덕과 악덕의 본보기를 끄집어내는 우리는 한층 위태로운 처지에 놓인다."], ["As we have no public notoriety, no concurrent testimony, no records to support and corroborate what we deliver, it becomes us to keep within the limits not only of possibility, but of probability too; and this more especially in painting what is greatly good and amiable.", "우리에게는 널리 알려진 명성도, 일치하는 증언도, 우리가 전하는 바를 떠받치고 입증해 줄 기록도 없으니, 우리는 가능성의 한계뿐 아니라 개연성의 한계 안에도 머물러야 마땅하다. 특히 크게 선하고 사랑스러운 것을 그려 낼 때는 더욱 그러하다."], ["Knavery and folly, though never so exorbitant, will more easily meet with assent; for ill-nature adds great support and strength to faith.", "악행과 어리석음은 아무리 터무니없어도 한결 쉽게 수긍을 얻는다. 심술궂은 본성이 믿음에 큰 버팀목과 힘을 보태 주기 때문이다."]], [["Thus we may, perhaps, with little danger, relate the history of Fisher; who having long owed his bread to the generosity of Mr Derby, and having one morning received a considerable bounty from his hands, yet, in order to possess himself of what remained in his friend's scrutore, concealed himself in a public office of the Temple, through which there was a passage into Mr Derby's chambers.", "그리하여 우리는 어쩌면 별 위험 없이 피셔의 이야기를 들려줄 수 있을 것이다. 그는 오랫동안 더비 씨의 관대함에 빌붙어 빵을 얻어먹어 왔고, 어느 날 아침 그의 손에서 적잖은 후한 선물을 받았으면서도, 친구의 서랍장에 남은 것을 제 것으로 삼으려고 템플에 있는 한 공용 사무실에 몸을 숨겼다."], ["Here he overheard Mr Derby for many hours solacing himself at an entertainment which he that evening gave his friends, and to which Fisher had been invited.", "그곳을 통하면 더비 씨의 방으로 통하는 통로가 있었던 것이다. 그는 여기서 여러 시간 동안 더비 씨가 그날 저녁 친구들에게 베푼 연회—피셔도 초대받았던 그 연회—에서 즐거워하는 소리를 엿들었다."], ["During all this time, no tender, no grateful reflections arose to restrain his purpose; but when the poor gentleman had let his company out through the office, Fisher came suddenly from his lurking-place, and walking softly behind his friend into his chamber, discharged a pistol-ball into his head.", "그 내내 그의 목적을 가로막을 어떤 다정한 생각도, 어떤 고마운 회상도 떠오르지 않았다. 그러나 가엾은 신사가 손님들을 그 사무실을 통해 배웅하고 나자, 피셔는 숨어 있던 곳에서 불쑥 나와, 친구의 뒤를 따라 살금살금 그의 방으로 걸어 들어가서는, 그의 머리에 권총 탄환을 발사했다."], ["This may be believed when the bones of Fisher are as rotten as his heart.", "이런 일은 피셔의 뼈가 그의 심장만큼이나 썩어 없어졌을 무렵에야 믿어질 만한 것이다. 아니, 어쩌면 이런 것도 믿어질 것이다."], ["Nay, perhaps, it will be credited, that the villain went two days afterwards with some young ladies to the play of Hamlet; and with an unaltered countenance heard one of the ladies, who little suspected how near she was to the person, cry out, “Good God!", "그 악당이 이틀 뒤에 몇몇 젊은 숙녀들과 함께 《햄릿》 연극을 보러 갔고, 그 숙녀들 중 하나가—자기가 그 장본인 곁에 얼마나 가까이 있는지는 꿈에도 모른 채—\"맙소사! 더비 씨를 죽인 그 사람이 지금 여기 있다면!\""], ["if the man that murdered Mr Derby was now present!”", "하고 외치는 것을 낯빛 하나 변치 않고 들었다는 것이다. 이로써 그는 네로보다도 더 무뎌지고 굳어 버린 양심을 드러냈으니, 네로에 관해서는 수에토니우스가 이렇게 전한다."], ["manifesting in this a more seared and callous conscience than even Nero himself; of whom we are told by Suetonius, “that the consciousness of his guilt, after the death of his mother, became immediately intolerable, and so continued; nor could all the congratulations of the soldiers, of the senate, and the people, allay the horrors of his conscience.”", "\"어머니가 죽은 뒤 자기 죄에 대한 의식이 곧바로 견딜 수 없게 되었고, 그대로 계속되었다. 군대와 원로원과 백성의 온갖 축하도 그의 양심의 공포를 가라앉히지 못했다.\""]], [["But now, on the other hand, should I tell my reader, that I had known a man whose penetrating genius had enabled him to raise a large fortune in a way where no beginning was chaulked out to him; that he had done this with the most perfect preservation of his integrity, and not only without the least injustice or injury to any one individual person, but with the highest advantage to trade, and a vast increase of the public revenue; that he had expended one part of the income of this fortune in discovering a taste superior to most, by works where the highest dignity was united with the purest simplicity, and another part in displaying a degree of goodness superior to all men, by acts of charity to objects whose only recommendations were their merits, or their wants; that he was most industrious in searching after merit in distress, most eager to relieve it, and then as careful (perhaps too careful) to conceal what he had done; that his house, his furniture, his gardens, his table, his private hospitality, and his public beneficence, all denoted the mind from which they flowed, and were all intrinsically rich and noble, without tinsel, or external ostentation; that he filled every relation in life with the most adequate virtue; that he was most piously religious to his Creator, most zealously loyal to his sovereign; a most tender husband to his wife, a kind relation, a munificent patron, a warm and firm friend, a knowing and a chearful companion, indulgent to his servants, hospitable to his neighbours, charitable to the poor, and benevolent to all mankind. Should I add to these the epithets of wise, brave, elegant, and indeed every other amiable epithet in our language, I might surely say,", "그러나 이제 반대로, 만일 내가 독자에게 이런 사람을 알고 있다고 말한다면 어떨까. 그의 통찰력 있는 천재성은, 아무런 출발선도 그어져 있지 않은 곳에서 막대한 재산을 일구도록 그를 이끌었으며, 그는 그 일을 자신의 정직함을 더없이 완벽하게 지켜 가며 해냈고, 어느 한 개인에게도 티끌만 한 부당함이나 해를 끼치지 않았을 뿐 아니라 오히려 상업에 더없는 이익을 안기고 공공 세입을 막대하게 늘렸다. 또한 그는 이 재산에서 나오는 수입의 한 부분을, 더없이 높은 품위가 더없이 순수한 소박함과 어우러진 작품들을 통해 대다수 사람들보다 뛰어난 안목을 드러내는 데 썼고, 또 다른 한 부분은, 오로지 그 가치나 궁핍만이 추천장이 되는 대상들에게 자선을 베풂으로써 모든 사람보다 뛰어난 선함을 펼쳐 보이는 데 썼다. 그는 곤경에 빠진 가치를 찾아내는 데 더없이 부지런했고, 그것을 구제하는 데 더없이 열심이었으며, 그러고 나서는 자기가 한 일을 감추는 데 (어쩌면 지나치다 싶을 만큼) 조심스러웠다. 그의 집, 그의 가구, 그의 정원, 그의 식탁, 그의 사사로운 환대와 공적인 선행, 이 모든 것이 그것들이 흘러나온 마음을 드러냈으며, 번지르르한 겉치레나 바깥으로의 과시 없이도 하나같이 본질에서 풍요롭고 고귀했다. 그는 인생의 모든 관계를 더없이 합당한 미덕으로 채웠다. 그는 창조주를 향해 더없이 경건하게 신앙심이 깊었고, 군주를 향해 더없이 열렬하게 충성스러웠으며, 아내에게는 더없이 다정한 남편이요, 친절한 친족이며, 후한 후원자요, 따뜻하고 굳건한 벗이며, 식견 있고 유쾌한 동무이고, 하인들에게는 너그럽고, 이웃에게는 후하며, 가난한 이에게는 자비롭고, 온 인류에게 자애로웠다. 여기에 더해 내가 현명하고, 용감하고, 우아하다는, 아니 실로 우리말의 다른 온갖 사랑스러운 수식어를 다 붙인다 한들, 나는 분명 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없으리라."]], [["--Quis credet?", "누가 믿겠는가? 맹세코 아무도!"], ["nemo Hercule! nemo; Vel duo, vel nemo;", "아무도 없으리니, 혹 둘이거나, 아니면 아무도 없으리."]], [["and yet I know a man who is all I have here described.", "그럼에도 나는 여기 묘사한 그 모든 것을 갖춘 사람을 한 명 알고 있다."], ["But a single instance (and I really know not such another) is not sufficient to justify us, while we are writing to thousands who never heard of the person, nor of anything like him.", "하지만 단 하나의 사례(실은 그와 같은 다른 사례를 나는 정말로 알지 못한다)는, 그 사람도 그 비슷한 무엇도 들어 본 적 없는 수천 명을 향해 글을 쓰는 우리를 정당화하기에는 충분하지 않다."], ["Such rarae aves should be remitted to the epitaph writer, or to some poet who may condescend to hitch him in a distich, or to slide him into a rhime with an air of carelessness and neglect, without giving any offence to the reader.", "그러한 진귀한 새는 묘비명 작가에게나, 혹은 그를 이행시 한 짝에 슬쩍 끼워 넣거나 무심하고 소홀한 척하며 운율 속에 살그머니 밀어 넣어 독자에게 아무런 거슬림도 주지 않을 만한 어떤 시인에게나 넘겨 버려야 마땅하다."]], [["In the last place, the actions should be such as may not only be within the compass of human agency, and which human agents may probably be supposed to do; but they should be likely for the very actors and characters themselves to have performed; for what may be only wonderful and surprizing in one man, may become improbable, or indeed impossible, when related of another.", "마지막으로, 행동이란 인간의 능력 범위 안에 있고 인간 행위자가 능히 할 법한 것이어야 할 뿐 아니라, 바로 그 행위자나 인물 자신이 행했음 직한 것이어야 한다. 한 사람에게는 그저 놀랍고 경이로울 뿐인 일이, 다른 사람에 관해 이야기될 때는 개연성이 없거나 실로 불가능한 일이 될 수도 있기 때문이다."]], [["This last requisite is what the dramatic critics call conversation of character; and it requires a very extraordinary degree of judgment, and a most exact knowledge of human nature.", "이 마지막 요건이 바로 극 비평가들이 인물의 일관성이라 부르는 것인데, 이는 매우 비범한 정도의 판단력과 인간 본성에 대한 더없이 정확한 지식을 요구한다."]], [["It is admirably remarked by a most excellent writer, that zeal can no more hurry a man to act in direct opposition to itself, than a rapid stream can carry a boat against its own current.", "어느 더없이 빼어난 작가가 감탄스럽게 지적한 바, 빠른 물살이 제 흐름을 거슬러 배를 실어 갈 수 없듯, 열정도 사람을 제 자신과 정면으로 어긋나게 행동하도록 몰아갈 수 없다."], ["I will venture to say, that for a man to act in direct contradiction to the dictates of his nature, is, if not impossible, as improbable and as miraculous as anything which can well be conceived.", "나는 감히 말하건대, 사람이 제 본성의 명령과 정면으로 모순되게 행동한다는 것은, 불가능하지는 않더라도, 능히 떠올릴 수 있는 그 무엇 못지않게 개연성 없고 기적 같은 일이다."], ["Should the best parts of the story of M. Antoninus be ascribed to Nero, or should the worst incidents of Nero's life be imputed to Antoninus, what would be more shocking to belief than either instance?", "만일 안토니누스의 이야기에서 가장 훌륭한 부분이 네로에게 돌려진다거나, 네로의 삶에서 가장 고약한 사건들이 안토니누스에게 씌워진다면, 그 어느 쪽인들 믿음에 이보다 더 충격적인 것이 있겠는가?"], ["whereas both these being related of their proper agent, constitute the truly marvellous.", "반면 이 둘이 각기 제 마땅한 행위자에 관해 이야기될 때, 그것이야말로 참된 경이로움을 이룬다."]], [["Our modern authors of comedy have fallen almost universally into the error here hinted at; their heroes generally are notorious rogues, and their heroines abandoned jades, during the first four acts; but in the fifth, the former become very worthy gentlemen, and the latter women of virtue and discretion: nor is the writer often so kind as to give himself the least trouble to reconcile or account for this monstrous change and incongruity. There is, indeed, no other reason to be assigned for it, than because the play is drawing to a conclusion; as if it was no less natural in a rogue to repent in the last act of a play, than in the last of his life; which we perceive to be generally the case at Tyburn, a place which might indeed close the scene of some comedies with much propriety, as the heroes in these are most commonly eminent for those very talents which not only bring men to the gallows, but enable them to make an heroic figure when they are there.", "우리 현대 희극 작가들은 여기서 넌지시 일러둔 오류에 거의 너 나 할 것 없이 빠져들었다. 그들의 주인공은 처음 네 막 동안 대개 소문난 악당이고, 그들의 여주인공은 막돼먹은 계집이다. 그러나 다섯째 막에 이르면 전자는 매우 훌륭한 신사가 되고, 후자는 미덕과 분별을 갖춘 여인이 된다. 게다가 작가는 이 괴이한 변화와 모순을 짜 맞추거나 설명하려고 조금이라도 수고를 들일 만큼 친절한 경우가 드물다. 실로 거기에는 연극이 결말로 치닫고 있다는 것 말고는 달리 댈 만한 이유가 없으니, 마치 악당이 연극의 마지막 막에서 뉘우치는 것이 제 삶의 마지막 순간에 뉘우치는 것 못지않게 자연스럽기라도 한 듯하다. 우리는 그런 일이 타이번에서 흔히 벌어짐을 보거니와, 그곳이야말로 실로 어떤 희극의 장면을 매우 어울리게 닫을 만한 자리이다. 이런 희극의 주인공들은 사람을 교수대로 끌고 갈 뿐 아니라 막상 거기 섰을 때 영웅적인 모습을 꾸며 내게도 해 주는 바로 그 재능으로 가장 흔히 이름을 떨치기 때문이다."]], [["Within these few restrictions, I think, every writer may be permitted to deal as much in the wonderful as he pleases; nay, if he thus keeps within the rules of credibility, the more he can surprize the reader the more he will engage his attention, and the more he will charm him.", "이 몇 가지 제약 안에서라면, 내 생각에 어떤 작가든 제 마음껏 경이로움을 다루도록 허락받을 수 있다. 아니, 이렇게 신빙성의 규칙 안에 머문다면, 독자를 놀라게 하면 할수록 그만큼 더 독자의 주의를 사로잡고, 그만큼 더 독자를 매혹할 것이다."], ["As a genius of the highest rank observes in his fifth chapter of the Bathos, “The great art of all poetry is to mix truth with fiction, in order to join the credible with the surprizing.”", "더없이 높은 반열의 한 천재가 《바토스》 제5장에서 지적했듯, \"모든 시의 위대한 기술은 진실을 허구와 섞어, 믿을 만한 것을 놀라운 것과 결합하는 데 있다.\""]], [["For though every good author will confine himself within the bounds of probability, it is by no means necessary that his characters, or his incidents, should be trite, common, or vulgar; such as happen in every street, or in every house, or which may be met with in the home articles of a newspaper.", "무릇 훌륭한 작가라면 누구나 개연성의 한계 안에 자신을 가두겠지만, 그렇다고 그의 인물이나 사건이 진부하거나 흔하거나 천박해야 할 까닭은 결코 없다. 거리마다, 집집마다 벌어지는 일이라든가 신문의 국내 단신에서 마주칠 법한 일 같은 것 말이다."], ["Nor must he be inhibited from showing many persons and things, which may possibly have never fallen within the knowledge of great part of his readers.", "또한 그가 독자 대다수의 식견 안에 한 번도 들어온 적 없을 법한 숱한 인물과 사물을 보여 주는 일을 금해서도 안 된다."], ["If the writer strictly observes the rules above-mentioned, he hath discharged his part; and is then intitled to some faith from his reader, who is indeed guilty of critical infidelity if he disbelieves him.", "작가가 앞서 말한 규칙들을 엄격히 지킨다면, 그는 제 몫을 다한 것이며, 그렇다면 독자로부터 어느 정도의 믿음을 받을 자격이 있다. 그를 못 믿겠다고 한다면, 독자야말로 실로 비평적 불신의 죄를 저지르는 셈이다."]], [["For want of a portion of such faith, I remember the character of a young lady of quality, which was condemned on the stage for being unnatural, by the unanimous voice of a very large assembly of clerks and apprentices; though it had the previous suffrages of many ladies of the first rank; one of whom, very eminent for her understanding, declared it was the picture of half the young people of her acquaintance.", "이런 믿음이 얼마간 모자란 탓에, 나는 어느 지체 높은 젊은 숙녀의 인물이 무대에서 부자연스럽다는 이유로, 사무원과 도제들이 잔뜩 모인 자리에서 만장일치의 목소리로 비난받았던 일을 떠올린다. 그 인물은 앞서 일류 가문의 여러 숙녀에게서 찬성표를 받은 터였고, 그중 식견으로 매우 이름난 한 분은 그것이 자기가 아는 젊은이의 절반쯤을 그려 낸 초상이라고 단언했는데도 말이다."]], [["In which the landlady pays a visit to Mr Jones.", "주막 안주인이 존스를 찾아오다."]], [["When Jones had taken leave of his friend the lieutenant, he endeavoured to close his eyes, but all in vain; his spirits were too lively and wakeful to be lulled to sleep.", "존스는 친구인 중위와 작별한 뒤 눈을 붙여 보려 애썼지만 모두 헛수고였다. 마음이 너무 또렷하고 말짱해서 도무지 잠으로 달랠 수가 없었다."], ["So having amused, or rather tormented, himself with the thoughts of his Sophia till it was open daylight, he called for some tea; upon which occasion my landlady herself vouchsafed to pay him a visit.", "그렇게 날이 훤히 밝을 때까지 소피아 생각으로 스스로를 달래었다기보다는 차라리 괴롭히고 나서, 그는 차를 좀 가져다 달라고 청했다. 바로 그 기회에 안주인이 친히 그를 찾아오는 영광을 베풀었다."]], [["This was indeed the first time she had seen him, or at least had taken any notice of him; but as the lieutenant had assured her that he was certainly some young gentleman of fashion, she now determined to show him all the respect in her power; for, to speak truly, this was one of those houses where gentlemen, to use the language of advertisements, meet with civil treatment for their money.", "사실 안주인이 그를 본 것은, 아니 적어도 그에게 조금이라도 관심을 둔 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그러나 중위가 그를 두고 틀림없이 어느 귀한 집안의 젊은 신사라고 장담해 두었던 터라, 그녀는 이제 자기가 할 수 있는 모든 예우를 그에게 베풀기로 작정했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 이곳은 광고 문구를 빌려 표현하자면 신사들이 제 돈값만큼 정중한 대접을 받는 그런 집들 가운데 하나였으니까."]], [["She had no sooner begun to make his tea, than she likewise began to discourse:--“La!", "그녀는 그에게 차를 우려내기 시작하자마자 이야기도 함께 풀어놓기 시작했다. \"어머나!"], ["sir,”", "나리,\""], ["said she, “I think it is great pity that such a pretty young gentleman should under-value himself so, as to go about with these soldier fellows.", "그녀가 말했다. \"이렇게 잘생긴 젊은 신사분이 저런 군인 나부랭이들이랑 어울려 다니느라 스스로를 그리 낮추시다니, 참으로 안타까운 일이지 뭐예요."], ["They call themselves gentlemen, I warrant you; but, as my first husband used to say, they should remember it is we that pay them.", "그자들이야 제 입으로 신사라 떠들고 다니겠지만, 우리 첫 남편이 늘 하던 말마따나, 그자들 봉급을 대는 게 바로 우리라는 걸 잊지 말아야지요."], ["And to be sure it is very hard upon us to be obliged to pay them, and to keep 'um too, as we publicans are.", "게다가 우리 같은 술장수들은 그자들한테 돈을 치를 뿐 아니라 먹여 재우기까지 해야 하니, 정말이지 죽을 노릇이에요."], ["I had twenty of 'um last night, besides officers: nay, for matter o'", "어젯밤만 해도 장교들 말고도 스무 명이나 받았다니까요. 아니, 그런 걸로 치자면 차라리 장교보다 사병들이 나아요."], ["that, I had rather have the soldiers than officers: for nothing is ever good enough for those sparks; and I am sure, if you was to see the bills; la!", "그 멋쟁이 양반들은 뭘 갖다 줘도 성에 차는 법이 없거든요. 그 계산서들을 보셨다면, 어머나!"], ["sir, it is nothing.", "나리, 정말 가관이라니까요."], ["I have had less trouble, I warrant you, with a good squire's family, where we take forty or fifty shillings of a night, besides horses.", "장담컨대 점잖은 시골 지주 식구들을 치를 때가 훨씬 수월하답니다. 그런 분들은 말 값 말고도 하룻밤에 사십, 오십 실링은 너끈히 내고 가시거든요."], ["And yet I warrants me, there is narrow a one of those officer fellows but looks upon himself to be as good as arrow a squire of £500 a year.", "그런데도 장담하건대, 저 장교 나부랭이들은 하나같이 제 깐엔 연 오백 파운드짜리 지주 어른 못지않다고 여긴다니까요. 그자들 졸병들이 졸졸 따라다니면서 '나리, 나리'"], ["To be sure it doth me good to hear their men run about after 'um, crying your honour, and your honour.", "하고 외쳐대는 꼴을 보면 정말 속이 다 후련해요. 거참, 나리 좋아하시네, 머리 하나에 한 실링짜리 정식이나 처먹는 주제에 말이죠."], ["Marry come up with such honour, and an ordinary at a shilling a head.", "게다가 그자들끼리 욕지거리를 어찌나 해대는지, 정말이지 무서워서 정신이 다 나갈 지경이에요. 저런 못된 인간들한테 좋은 일이 생길 리가 있나 싶다니까요."], ["Then there's such swearing among 'um, to be sure it frightens me out o'", "그런데 그 가운데 하나가 나리한테 그토록 야만스럽게 굴었으니. 나머지 작자들이 그놈을 어떻게든 빼돌릴 거라고 진작에 짐작했지요."], ["my wits: I thinks nothing can ever prosper with such wicked people.", "그자들은 죄다 한통속이거든요. 나리께서 목숨이 위태로우셨더라도—다행히 그렇지 않으셔서 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라요—저런 못된 인간들한테야 다 매한가지였을 거예요."], ["And here one of 'um has used you in so barbarous a manner.", "살인자를 그냥 놓아주었겠지요. 하느님, 저들에게 자비를 베푸소서."], ["I thought indeed how well the rest would secure him; they all hang together; for if you had been in danger of death, which I am glad to see you are not, it would have been all as one to such wicked people.", "저라면 온 세상을 다 준대도 그런 죄를 짊어지진 않겠어요. 하지만 나리께서는 다행히도 신의 가호로 회복하실 듯하니, 그놈한테는 아직 법이 남아 있답니다."], ["They would have let the murderer go.", "스몰 변호사를 쓰시면, 장담컨대 그 작자를 이 나라에서 도망치게 만들어 줄 거예요."], ["Laud have mercy upon 'um; I would not have such a sin to answer for, for the whole world.", "하긴 어쩌면 그놈은 벌써 줄행랑을 쳤을지도 모르지요. 그런 부류는 오늘 여기 있다가도 내일이면 사라지는 법이니까요."], ["But though you are likely, with the blessing, to recover, there is laa for him yet; and if you will employ lawyer Small, I darest be sworn he'll make the fellow fly the country for him; though perhaps he'll have fled the country before; for it is here to-day and gone to-morrow with such chaps.", "하지만 앞으로는 부디 더 분별을 차리셔서 친지분들 곁으로 돌아가시길 바라요. 장담컨대 나리를 잃고 다들 애가 타고 계실 거예요."], ["I hope, however, you will learn more wit for the future, and return back to your friends; I warrant they are all miserable for your loss; and if they was but to know what had happened--La, my seeming!", "무슨 일이 있었는지 그분들이 아시기라도 하면—어머나, 세상에! 그분들이 아시는 건 정말이지 천만의 말씀이에요."], ["I would not for the world they should.", "자, 자, 우리도 사정이야 다 알지요."], ["Come, come, we know very well what all the matter is; but if one won't, another will; so pretty a gentleman need never want a lady.", "하지만 한 사람이 마다하면 또 다른 사람이 있는 법, 이렇게 잘생긴 신사분이야 여인이 모자랄 일이 결코 없으시지요. 정말이지 제가 나리라면, 어떤 여자 때문에 군인이 되느니 차라리 세상에서 제일 고운 여자가 목매달리는 꼴을 보고 말겠어요."], ["I am sure, if I was you, I would see the finest she that ever wore a head hanged, before I would go for a soldier for her.--Nay, don't blush so”", "아니, 그렇게 얼굴 붉히지 마세요\"(실제로 그는 격하게 얼굴을 붉혔던 것이다). \"어머, 나리는 제가 그 소피아 아씨 일을 아무것도 모르는 줄 아셨나 본데, 장담컨대 다 알고 있다니까요.\""], ["(for indeed he did to a violent degree).", "\"뭐라고요,\" 존스가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."], ["“Why, you thought, sir, I knew nothing of the matter, I warrant you, about Madam Sophia.”--“How,”", "\"당신이 내 소피아를 안다고요?\""], ["says Jones, starting up, “do you know my Sophia?”--“Do I!", "\"알다마다요! 아믄요,\""], ["ay marry,”", "안주인이 외쳤다."], ["cries the landlady; “many's the time hath she lain in this house.”--“With her aunt, I suppose,”", "\"그 아씨가 이 집에 묵으신 게 한두 번이 아닌걸요.\" \"이모님하고 함께였겠지요,\""], ["says Jones.", "존스가 말했다."], ["“Why, there it is now,”", "\"거 봐요, 바로 그거예요,\""], ["cries the landlady.", "안주인이 외쳤다."], ["“Ay, ay, ay, I know the old lady very well.", "\"그래요, 그래요, 그 노부인도 제가 아주 잘 알지요."], ["And a sweet young creature is Madam Sophia, that's the truth on't.”--“A sweet creature,”", "그리고 소피아 아씨는 참으로 사랑스러운 젊은 분이세요, 그건 정말이에요.\" \"사랑스러운 분이고말고요,\""], ["cries Jones; “O heavens!”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"오, 하늘이여!\""]], [["Angels are painted fair to look like her.", "천사들도 그녀를 닮으라고 곱게 그려졌도다."], ["There's in her all that we believe of heav'n, Amazing brightness, purity, and truth, Eternal joy and everlasting love.", "그녀 안에는 우리가 천국이라 믿는 모든 것이 깃들어 있으니, 눈부신 광채와 순결함과 진실함, 영원한 기쁨과 끝없는 사랑이로다."]], [["“And could I ever have imagined that you had known my Sophia!”--“I wish,” says the landlady, “you knew half so much of her. What would you have given to have sat by her bed-side? What a delicious neck she hath! Her lovely limbs have stretched themselves in that very bed you now lie in.”--“Here!” cries Jones: “hath Sophia ever laid here?”--“Ay, ay, here; there, in that very bed,” says the landlady; “where I wish you had her this moment; and she may wish so too for anything I know to the contrary, for she hath mentioned your name to me.”--“Ha!” cries he; “did she ever mention her poor Jones? You flatter me now: I can never believe so much.”--“Why, then,” answered she, “as I hope to be saved, and may the devil fetch me if I speak a syllable more than the truth, I have heard her mention Mr Jones; but in a civil and modest way, I confess; yet I could perceive she thought a great deal more than she said.”--“O my dear woman!” cries Jones, “her thoughts of me I shall never be worthy of. Oh, she is all gentleness, kindness, goodness! Why was such a rascal as I born, ever to give her soft bosom a moment's uneasiness? Why am I cursed? I, who would undergo all the plagues and miseries which any daemon ever invented for mankind, to procure her any good; nay, torture itself could not be misery to me, did I but know that she was happy.”--“Why, look you there now,” says the landlady; “I told her you was a constant lovier.”--“But pray, madam, tell me when or where you knew anything of me; for I never was here before, nor do I remember ever to have seen you.”--“Nor is it possible you should,” answered she; “for you was a little thing when I had you in my lap at the squire's.”--“How, the squire's?” says Jones: “what, do you know that great and good Mr Allworthy then?”--“Yes, marry, do I,” says she: “who in the country doth not?”--“The fame of his goodness indeed,” answered Jones, “must have extended farther than this; but heaven only can know him--can know that benevolence which it copied from itself, and sent upon earth as its own pattern. Mankind are as ignorant of such divine goodness, as they are unworthy of it; but none so unworthy of it as myself. I, who was raised by him to such a height; taken in, as you must well know, a poor base-born child, adopted by him, and treated as his own son, to dare by my follies to disoblige him, to draw his vengeance upon me. Yes, I deserve it all; for I will never be so ungrateful as ever to think he hath done an act of injustice by me. No, I deserve to be turned out of doors, as I am. And now, madam,” says he, “I believe you will not blame me for turning soldier, especially with such a fortune as this in my pocket.” At which words he shook a purse, which had but very little in it, and which still appeared to the landlady to have less.", "\"당신이 내 소피아를 알고 있었으리라곤 정말 상상도 못 했어요!\" \"저는요,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"나리가 그 절반만큼이라도 그 아씨를 아셨으면 좋겠네요. 그 아씨 침상 곁에 앉아 보셨다면 무엇인들 내놓지 않으셨겠어요? 그 목덜미가 얼마나 고운지! 그 어여쁜 팔다리가 바로 지금 나리께서 누워 계신 그 침대에 길게 뉘어졌었다니까요.\" \"여기요!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"소피아가 여기 누운 적이 있다고요?\" \"그럼요, 그럼요, 바로 여기요. 거기, 바로 그 침대에요,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"지금 이 순간 나리께서 그 아씨를 거기 모셨으면 얼마나 좋을까요. 그리고 그 아씨도 어쩌면 그러길 바라실지 누가 알겠어요. 제가 아는 바로는 그렇지 않다 할 까닭이 없거든요. 그 아씨가 제게 나리 이름을 말씀하셨으니 말이에요.\" \"아!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그녀가 정말로 가엾은 이 존스를 입에 올렸단 말이오? 지금 나를 추켜세우는 거지요. 도저히 그렇게까지는 믿을 수가 없소.\" \"그럼요, 그러니까요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"제가 구원받기를 바라는 만큼 진실인데요, 제가 진실보다 한 마디라도 더 보탰다면 악마가 저를 잡아가도 좋아요. 그 아씨가 존스 나리 얘기를 하시는 걸 제가 들었답니다. 하지만 점잖고 정숙하게 말씀하신 거예요, 그건 인정해요. 그래도 입 밖에 낸 것보다 훨씬 많은 생각을 품고 계신 걸 저는 알아챌 수 있었지요.\" \"오, 친절한 분이여!\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그녀가 나를 어떻게 생각하든 나는 결코 그럴 자격이 없는 사람이오. 오, 그녀는 온통 부드러움이요, 다정함이요, 선함 그 자체인데! 어찌하여 나 같은 망나니가 태어나, 그 보드라운 가슴을 단 한 순간이라도 불편하게 한단 말이오? 어찌하여 나는 저주받은 몸인가? 그녀에게 작은 행복 하나라도 가져다줄 수만 있다면, 어떤 악마가 인류를 위해 고안한 온갖 재앙과 고통이라도 기꺼이 견딜 이 몸인데. 아니, 그녀가 행복하다는 것만 알 수 있다면 고문조차 내게는 고통이 아닐 텐데.\" \"거 봐요, 보세요,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"제가 그 아씨한테 나리는 한결같은 연인이라고 했다니까요.\" \"한데 부인, 부디 말해 주시오, 당신이 나에 관해 무엇을 알게 된 게 언제, 어디서요? 나는 여기 와 본 적이 한 번도 없고, 당신을 본 기억도 없으니 말이오.\" \"그러실 만도 하지요,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"제가 지주 어른 댁에서 나리를 무릎에 안고 있을 때 나리는 코딱지만 한 어린애였으니까요.\" \"뭐라고요, 지주 댁이라니요?\" 존스가 말했다. \"아니, 그럼 그 위대하고 훌륭하신 올워디 어른을 아신단 말이오?\" \"그럼요, 아믄요, 알다마다요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 고장에서 그분을 모르는 사람이 어디 있겠어요?\" \"그분의 선하심에 대한 명성이야,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"분명 여기보다 훨씬 멀리까지 뻗어 나갔을 거요. 하지만 오직 하늘만이 그분을 알 수 있소—하늘이 제 자신에게서 본떠 와 그 본보기로 삼아 땅으로 내려보낸 그 자애로움을 알 수 있는 건 오직 하늘뿐이오. 인류는 그런 신성한 선함을 누릴 자격이 없는 만큼이나 그것을 알지도 못하오. 하지만 그 누구도 나만큼 그럴 자격이 없는 사람은 없을 거요. 그분 손에 그토록 높이 들어 올려진 나, 당신도 잘 알다시피 가엾은 사생아로 거두어져 그분의 양자가 되고 친아들처럼 길러진 이 몸이, 감히 내 어리석음으로 그분의 노여움을 사고 그 진노를 내게 끌어들였으니. 그렇소, 나는 그 모든 걸 받아 마땅하오. 나는 그분이 내게 부당한 짓을 하셨다고는 결코 배은망덕하게 생각하지 않을 거니까. 아니오, 나는 지금 이 꼴처럼 문밖으로 내쫓겨 마땅한 사람이오. 그러니 이제, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 군인이 되기로 한 걸 탓하지는 않으시겠지요. 더구나 내 주머니에 이만한 재산이 들었으니 말이오.\" 그 말과 함께 그는 지갑을 흔들어 보였는데, 그 안에는 아주 적은 돈밖에 들어 있지 않았고, 안주인 눈에는 그보다도 더 적어 보였다."]], [["My good landlady was (according to vulgar phrase) struck all of a heap by this relation.", "마음씨 좋은 안주인은 이 이야기를 듣고 (속된 표현을 빌리자면) 한 방에 폭삭 주저앉고 말았다."], ["She answered coldly, “That to be sure people were the best judges what was most proper for their circumstances.", "그녀는 쌀쌀맞게 대답했다. \"뭐, 자기 형편에 뭐가 가장 알맞은지는 본인이 제일 잘 아는 법이지요."], ["But hark,”", "그런데 어머,\""], ["says she, “I think I hear somebody call.", "그녀가 말했다. \"누가 부르는 소리가 들리는 것 같네요."], ["Coming!", "가요!"], ["coming!", "가요!"], ["the devil's in all our volk; nobody hath any ears.", "우리 집 사람들은 죄다 귀신이 씌었나, 아무도 귀가 없다니까."], ["I must go down-stairs; if you want any more breakfast the maid will come up.", "저는 아래층으로 내려가 봐야겠어요. 아침을 더 드시려거든 하녀를 올려보낼게요."], ["Coming!”", "가요!\""], ["At which words, without taking any leave, she flung out of the room; for the lower sort of people are very tenacious of respect; and though they are contented to give this gratis to persons of quality, yet they never confer it on those of their own order without taking care to be well paid for their pains.", "그 말과 함께 그녀는 작별 인사도 없이 방을 휙 나가 버렸다. 신분이 낮은 부류의 사람들은 대접받는 일에 무척 까다로운 법이라, 지체 높은 분들에게는 기꺼이 공짜로 예우를 바치면서도 제 또래의 사람들에게는 그 수고에 대한 값을 단단히 받아 챙기기 전에는 결코 예우를 베푸는 법이 없으니 말이다."]], [["In which the surgeon makes his second appearance.", "외과의가 두 번째로 등장하는 장."]], [["Before we proceed any farther, that the reader may not be mistaken in imagining the landlady knew more than she did, nor surprized that she knew so much, it may be necessary to inform him that the lieutenant had acquainted her that the name of Sophia had been the occasion of the quarrel; and as for the rest of her knowledge, the sagacious reader will observe how she came by it in the preceding scene. Great curiosity was indeed mixed with her virtues; and she never willingly suffered any one to depart from her house, without enquiring as much as possible into their names, families, and fortunes.", "더 나아가기 전에, 독자가 여관 안주인이 실제 아는 것보다 더 많이 안다고 착각하거나, 혹은 그녀가 그렇게나 많이 안다는 데 놀라는 일이 없도록, 다음 사실을 일러두는 편이 좋겠다. 중위가 그녀에게 소피아라는 이름이 그 싸움의 발단이었음을 알려 주었던 것이다. 그리고 나머지 그녀가 아는 바에 대해서는, 눈치 빠른 독자라면 그녀가 앞 장면에서 그것을 어떻게 알아냈는지 알아차렸을 것이다. 실로 그녀의 미덕에는 큰 호기심이 뒤섞여 있었다. 그녀는 누구든 자기 집을 떠나려 할 때, 그 이름과 가문과 재산에 대해 될 수 있는 대로 캐묻지 않고는 결코 순순히 보내지 않았다."]], [["She was no sooner gone than Jones, instead of animadverting on her behaviour, reflected that he was in the same bed which he was informed had held his dear Sophia.", "그녀가 나가자마자 존스는 그녀의 행동을 따져 보기는커녕, 자신이 누워 있는 이 침대가 바로 사랑하는 소피아를 품었었다고 들은 그 침대임을 떠올렸다."], ["This occasioned a thousand fond and tender thoughts, which we would dwell longer upon, did we not consider that such kind of lovers will make a very inconsiderable part of our readers.", "이 생각은 천 가지 애틋하고 다정한 상념을 불러일으켰는데, 우리가 여기에 더 오래 머물고 싶기는 하나, 이런 부류의 연인들이 우리 독자 가운데 지극히 작은 일부일 뿐임을 헤아리지 않을 수 없다. 외과의가 상처를 치료하러 왔을 때 존스는 이런 상태에 있었다."], ["In this situation the surgeon found him, when he came to dress his wound.", "의사는 진찰을 해 보고 그의 맥이 흐트러져 있음을 알아채고, 또 그가 잠을 자지 못했다는 말을 듣고는, 그가 큰 위험에 처해 있다고 단언했다."], ["The doctor perceiving, upon examination, that his pulse was disordered, and hearing that he had not slept, declared that he was in great danger; for he apprehended a fever was coming on, which he would have prevented by bleeding, but Jones would not submit, declaring he would lose no more blood; “and, doctor,”", "그는 열이 오르려 한다고 보았고, 사혈로 그것을 막으려 했으나, 존스는 응하지 않으며 더는 피를 흘리지 않겠다고 잘라 말했다. \"그리고 선생님,\""], ["says he, “if you will be so kind only to dress my head, I have no doubt of being well in a day or two.”", "그가 말했다. \"친절을 베푸셔서 제 머리만 싸매 주신다면, 하루 이틀이면 나으리라는 데에 의심이 없습니다.\""]], [["“I wish,”", "\"제가,\" 외과의가 대답했다."], ["answered the surgeon, “I could assure your being well in a month or two.", "\"한두 달 안에 나으시리라 장담할 수 있다면 좋겠군요. 나아요, 그것 참!"], ["Well, indeed!", "아니, 아니지요. 이런 타박상은 그리 빨리 낫는 게 아닙니다."], ["No, no, people are not so soon well of such contusions; but, sir, I am not at this time of day to be instructed in my operations by a patient, and I insist on making a revulsion before I dress you.”", "그런데 선생, 나는 이 나이가 되도록 환자에게서 내 시술법을 배우는 처지는 아니올시다. 그러니 치료에 앞서 반드시 반동 요법(피를 다른 곳으로 돌리는 처치)을 행하겠소.\""]], [["Jones persisted obstinately in his refusal, and the doctor at last yielded; telling him at the same time that he would not be answerable for the ill consequence, and hoped he would do him the justice to acknowledge that he had given him a contrary advice; which the patient promised he would.", "존스가 한사코 거절을 고집하자, 의사는 마침내 물러섰다. 다만 그러면서도 그 나쁜 결과에 대해서는 책임지지 않겠노라고, 자신이 그와 반대되는 조언을 했음을 환자가 정당하게 인정해 주기를 바란다고 일러두었다. 환자는 그리하겠노라 약속했다."]], [["The doctor retired into the kitchen, where, addressing himself to the landlady, he complained bitterly of the undutiful behaviour of his patient, who would not be blooded, though he was in a fever.", "의사는 부엌으로 물러나 안주인에게 다가가, 열이 있는데도 사혈을 받지 않으려는 환자의 고분고분하지 못한 태도를 쓴소리로 늘어놓았다."]], [["“It is an eating fever then,”", "\"그렇다면 먹는 열인가 보군요.\""], ["says the landlady; “for he hath devoured two swinging buttered toasts this morning for breakfast.”", "안주인이 말했다. \"오늘 아침 식사로 버터 바른 두툼한 토스트를 두 장이나 먹어 치웠으니까요.\""]], [["“Very likely,” says the doctor: “I have known people eat in a fever; and it is very easily accounted for; because the acidity occasioned by the febrile matter may stimulate the nerves of the diaphragm, and thereby occasion a craving which will not be easily distinguishable from a natural appetite; but the aliment will not be concreted, nor assimilated into chyle, and so will corrode the vascular orifices, and thus will aggravate the febrific symptoms. Indeed, I think the gentleman in a very dangerous way, and, if he is not blooded, I am afraid will die.”", "\"그럴 법하지요.\" 의사가 말했다. \"열이 났는데도 먹는 사람을 본 적이 있소. 그건 아주 쉽게 설명이 됩니다. 열을 일으키는 물질에서 생긴 산기(酸氣)가 횡격막의 신경을 자극하여, 그로 인해 타고난 식욕과 좀처럼 구별되지 않는 갈망이 생기는 것이지요. 그러나 그 음식은 엉기지도 않고 유미(乳糜, 소화된 영양액)로 흡수되지도 않으니, 결국 혈관의 입구를 갉아 들어가 열병의 증세를 더 악화시킬 것이오. 정말이지 저 신사는 매우 위태로운 지경에 있다고 봅니다. 사혈을 받지 않으면 죽지 않을까 걱정스럽소.\""]], [["“Every man must die some time or other,”", "\"사람은 누구나 언젠가 한 번은 죽게 마련이지요.\""], ["answered the good woman; “it is no business of mine.", "마음씨 좋은 여인이 대답했다. \"제 알 바 아니랍니다."], ["I hope, doctor, you would not have me hold him while you bleed him.", "선생님, 설마 사혈하시는 동안 제가 저이를 붙들고 있길 바라시는 건 아니겠지요."], ["But, hark'ee, a word in your ear; I would advise you, before you proceed too far, to take care who is to be your paymaster.”", "그런데 말이에요, 귀에 한마디 넣어 드릴게요. 너무 깊이 손을 대시기 전에, 누가 선생님께 값을 치를 사람인지부터 챙기시라고 권하고 싶군요.\""]], [["“Paymaster!”", "\"값을 치를 사람이라!\""], ["said the doctor, staring; “why, I've a gentleman under my hands, have I not?”", "의사가 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했다. \"아니, 내 손 안에 신사 한 분이 있지 않소, 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["“I imagined so as well as you,”", "\"저도 선생님처럼 그리 여겼지요.\""], ["said the landlady; “but, as my first husband used to say, everything is not what it looks to be.", "안주인이 말했다. \"하지만, 제 첫 남편이 늘 하던 말마따나, 모든 것이 겉보기 그대로는 아니랍니다."], ["He is an arrant scrub, I assure you.", "정말이지 저이는 영락없는 거렁뱅이예요."], ["However, take no notice that I mentioned anything to you of the matter; but I think people in business oft always to let one another know such things.”", "그래도 제가 이 일을 입에 올렸다는 티는 내지 마세요. 다만 장사하는 사람들끼리는 이런 일을 서로 알려 주는 게 으레 도리라고 생각해서요.\""]], [["“And have I suffered such a fellow as this,”", "\"그래서 내가 이런 작자한테,\""], ["cries the doctor, in a passion, “to instruct me?", "의사가 발끈하여 외쳤다. \"가르침을 받았단 말이오?"], ["Shall I hear my practice insulted by one who will not pay me?", "값도 치르지 않을 자에게 내 의술이 모욕당하는 꼴을 들어야 한단 말이오?"], ["I am glad I have made this discovery in time.", "때맞춰 이 사실을 알게 되어 다행이군."], ["I will see now whether he will be blooded or no.”", "이제 저이가 사혈을 받나 안 받나 두고 봅시다.\""], ["He then immediately went upstairs, and flinging open the door of the chamber with much violence, awaked poor Jones from a very sound nap, into which he was fallen, and, what was still worse, from a delicious dream concerning Sophia.", "그러고는 곧장 위층으로 올라가 방문을 거칠게 열어젖혀, 막 깊은 단잠에 빠져 있던 가엾은 존스를 깨우고 말았다. 게다가 더 고약하게도, 소피아에 관한 달콤한 꿈에서 그를 끌어낸 것이었다."]], [["“Will you be blooded or no?”", "\"사혈을 받겠소, 안 받겠소?\""], ["cries the doctor, in a rage.", "의사가 노발대발하며 외쳤다."], ["“I have told you my resolution already,”", "\"이미 제 결심을 말씀드렸습니다.\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “and I wish with all my heart you had taken my answer; for you have awaked me out of the sweetest sleep which I ever had in my life.”", "\"제 대답을 받아들여 주셨더라면 진심으로 좋았을 텐데요. 제 평생 가장 단꿈 같은 잠에서 저를 깨우셨으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Ay, ay,”", "\"옳지, 옳지.\""], ["cries the doctor; “many a man hath dozed away his life.", "의사가 외쳤다. \"많은 이들이 그렇게 잠에 빠져 목숨을 흘려보냈소."], ["Sleep is not always good, no more than food; but remember, I demand of you for the last time, will you be blooded?”--“I answer you for the last time,”", "잠이라고 늘 좋은 게 아니지, 음식과 마찬가지로 말이오. 하지만 기억하시오, 마지막으로 다시 묻겠소."], ["said Jones, “I will not.”--“Then I wash my hands of you,”", "사혈을 받겠소?\" \"마지막으로 답하겠습니다.\""], ["cries the doctor; “and I desire you to pay me for the trouble I have had already.", "존스가 말했다. \"받지 않겠습니다.\""], ["Two journeys at 5s.", "\"그렇다면 나는 당신한테서 손을 떼겠소.\""], ["each, two dressings at 5s.", "의사가 외쳤다. \"그리고 이미 내가 들인 수고에 대한 값을 치러 주시오."], ["more, and half a crown for phlebotomy.”--“I hope,”", "왕진 두 차례에 한 번에 5실링씩, 치료 두 차례에 또 5실링씩, 그리고 사혈에 반 크라운이오.\" \"설마,\""], ["said Jones, “you don't intend to leave me in this condition.”--“Indeed but I shall,”", "존스가 말했다. \"저를 이런 상태로 내버려 두실 작정은 아니겠지요.\""], ["said the other.", "\"아니, 그럴 작정이올시다.\" 상대가 말했다."], ["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Jones, “you have used me rascally, and I will not pay you a farthing.”--“Very well,”", "존스가 말했다. \"당신은 저를 악당처럼 다룬 셈이니, 한 푼도 치르지 않겠습니다.\""], ["cries the doctor; “the first loss is the best.", "\"아주 좋소.\" 의사가 외쳤다."], ["What a pox did my landlady mean by sending for me to such vagabonds!”", "\"맨 처음 손해가 제일 나은 손해라더니. 대체 우리 안주인은 무슨 심사로 이런 부랑배들한테 나를 불러댔단 말인가!\""], ["At which words he flung out of the room, and his patient turning himself about soon recovered his sleep; but his dream was unfortunately gone.", "이 말을 내뱉으며 그는 방을 박차고 나갔고, 환자는 몸을 돌려 누워 이내 잠을 되찾았다. 그러나 그의 꿈은 불행히도 사라지고 없었다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["In which is introduced one of the pleasantest barbers that was ever recorded in history, the barber of Bagdad, or he in Don Quixote, not excepted.", "바그다드의 이발사도, 돈키호테에 나오는 그 이발사도 따라올 수 없는, 역사에 기록된 가장 유쾌한 이발사 한 사람을 소개하는 이야기."]], [["The clock had now struck five when Jones awaked from a nap of seven hours, so much refreshed, and in such perfect health and spirits, that he resolved to get up and dress himself; for which purpose he unlocked his portmanteau, and took out clean linen, and a suit of cloaths; but first he slipt on a frock, and went down into the kitchen to bespeak something that might pacify certain tumults he found rising within his stomach.", "시계가 막 다섯 시를 알렸을 때 존스는 일곱 시간의 단잠에서 깨어났다. 어찌나 개운하고 건강과 기운이 완전히 돌아왔던지, 그는 자리에서 일어나 옷을 차려입기로 마음먹었다. 그 채비로 그는 여행 가방을 열어 깨끗한 속옷 한 벌과 옷 한 벌을 꺼냈다. 그러나 먼저 겉옷을 걸치고 부엌으로 내려갔는데, 뱃속에서 일어나기 시작한 어떤 소란을 가라앉혀 줄 무언가를 주문하기 위해서였다."]], [["Meeting the landlady, he accosted her with great civility, and asked, “What he could have for dinner?”--“For dinner!” says she; “it is an odd time a day to think about dinner. There is nothing drest in the house, and the fire is almost out.”--“Well, but,” says he, “I must have something to eat, and it is almost indifferent to me what; for, to tell you the truth, I was never more hungry in my life.”--“Then,” says she, “I believe there is a piece of cold buttock and carrot, which will fit you.”--“Nothing better,” answered Jones; “but I should be obliged to you, if you would let it be fried.” To which the landlady consented, and said, smiling, “she was glad to see him so well recovered;” for the sweetness of our heroe's temper was almost irresistible; besides, she was really no ill-humoured woman at the bottom; but she loved money so much, that she hated everything which had the semblance of poverty.", "여주인을 마주친 그는 무척 정중하게 인사를 건네며 \"저녁으로 무엇을 먹을 수 있겠느냐\"고 물었다. \"저녁이라뇨!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저녁을 생각하시기엔 참 어중간한 시간이네요. 집에 차려 놓은 음식이라곤 하나도 없고, 불도 거의 다 꺼졌는걸요.\" \"그래도,\" 그가 말했다. \"뭐든 먹을 게 있어야겠소. 무엇이든 거의 상관없으니 말이오. 솔직히 말하자면, 평생 이렇게 배고팠던 적이 없거든.\" \"그러시다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"차게 식힌 소 엉덩잇살 한 점과 당근이 있는데, 손님께 맞으실 것 같네요.\" \"더할 나위 없소.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"다만 그것을 기름에 좀 구워 주신다면 고맙겠소.\" 여주인은 그러마 하고, 미소 지으며 \"이렇게 건강을 잘 회복하신 걸 보니 기쁘다\"고 말했다. 우리 주인공의 다정한 성품은 거의 거역할 수 없는 것이었기 때문이다. 게다가 그녀는 본바탕이 결코 심술궂은 여인은 아니었다. 다만 돈을 어찌나 사랑했던지, 가난의 기미가 비치는 것이라면 무엇이든 미워했을 따름이다."]], [["Jones now returned in order to dress himself, while his dinner was preparing, and was, according to his orders, attended by the barber.", "이제 존스는 저녁이 준비되는 동안 옷을 차려입으려고 돌아갔고, 자신이 시킨 대로 이발사의 시중을 받았다."]], [["This barber, who went by the name of Little Benjamin, was a fellow of great oddity and humour, which had frequently let him into small inconveniencies, such as slaps in the face, kicks in the breech, broken bones, &c.", "이 이발사는 꼬마 벤저민이라는 이름으로 통했는데, 별나고 익살스럽기 그지없는 사내였다. 그 성미 탓에 그는 종종 자잘한 곤욕을 치르곤 했으니, 이를테면 따귀를 맞거나, 엉덩이를 걷어차이거나, 뼈가 부러지거나 하는 식이었다."], ["For every one doth not understand a jest; and those who do are often displeased with being themselves the subjects of it.", "누구나 농담을 알아듣는 것은 아니며, 알아듣는 이들조차 정작 자신이 농담의 표적이 되는 것은 못마땅해하는 법이니까."], ["This vice was, however, incurable in him; and though he had often smarted for it, yet if ever he conceived a joke, he was certain to be delivered of it, without the least respect of persons, time, or place.", "그러나 이 버릇은 그에게 고칠 수 없는 것이었다. 그 때문에 여러 번 호되게 당하고도, 일단 농담거리가 떠오르면 그는 상대가 누구든, 때가 언제든, 장소가 어디든 조금도 가리지 않고 기어이 그것을 입 밖에 내고야 말았다."]], [["He had a great many other particularities in his character, which I shall not mention, as the reader will himself very easily perceive them, on his farther acquaintance with this extraordinary person.", "그의 성격에는 이 밖에도 별난 구석이 무척 많았는데, 그것들은 따로 언급하지 않겠다. 독자가 이 비범한 인물과 좀 더 친해지다 보면 스스로 아주 쉽게 알아차릴 테니 말이다."]], [["Jones being impatient to be drest, for a reason which may be easily imagined, thought the shaver was very tedious in preparing his suds, and begged him to make haste; to which the other answered with much gravity, for he never discomposed his muscles on any account, “Festina lente, is a proverb which I learned long before I ever touched a razor.”--“I find, friend, you are a scholar,” replied Jones. “A poor one,” said the barber, “non omnia possumus omnes.”--“Again!” said Jones; “I fancy you are good at capping verses.”--“Excuse me, sir,” said the barber, “non tanto me dignor honore.” And then proceeding to his operation, “Sir,” said he, “since I have dealt in suds, I could never discover more than two reasons for shaving; the one is to get a beard, and the other to get rid of one. I conjecture, sir, it may not be long since you shaved from the former of these motives. Upon my word, you have had good success; for one may say of your beard, that it is tondenti gravior.”--“I conjecture,” says Jones, “that thou art a very comical fellow.”--“You mistake me widely, sir,” said the barber: “I am too much addicted to the study of philosophy; hinc illae lacrymae, sir; that's my misfortune. Too much learning hath been my ruin.”--“Indeed,” says Jones, “I confess, friend, you have more learning than generally belongs to your trade; but I can't see how it can have injured you.”--“Alas! sir,” answered the shaver, “my father disinherited me for it. He was a dancing-master; and because I could read before I could dance, he took an aversion to me, and left every farthing among his other children.--Will you please to have your temples--O la! I ask your pardon, I fancy there is hiatus in manuscriptis. I heard you was going to the wars; but I find it was a mistake.”--“Why do you conclude so?” says Jones. “Sure, sir,” answered the barber, “you are too wise a man to carry a broken head thither; for that would be carrying coals to Newcastle.”", "쉽게 짐작이 갈 만한 이유로 어서 옷을 입고 싶어 안달이 난 존스는, 이 면도꾼이 비누 거품을 푸는 데 너무 굼뜨다고 여겨 좀 서둘러 달라고 청했다. 그러자 상대는 무슨 일이 있어도 결코 표정을 흩뜨리는 법이 없었으므로, 자못 진지하게 대답했다. \"'천천히 서둘러라'(Festina lente)는, 제가 면도칼을 잡기 훨씬 전부터 배워 둔 격언입지요.\" \"이보게, 자네는 학식이 있는 사람이로군.\" 존스가 응수했다. \"변변찮습니다요.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"'우리 모두가 모든 것을 할 수 있는 것은 아니지요'(non omnia possumus omnes).\" \"또 나오는군!\" 존스가 말했다. \"자네는 시구 받아 잇기에 능한 모양이야.\" \"용서하십시오, 나리.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"'저는 그만한 영예를 누릴 자격이 못 됩니다'(non tanto me dignor honore).\" 그러고는 일을 계속하면서 그가 말했다. \"나리, 제가 비누 거품을 다루기 시작한 이래로, 면도를 하는 까닭이라곤 두 가지밖에는 도무지 찾아낼 수가 없더군요. 하나는 수염을 기르려는 것이고, 다른 하나는 수염을 없애려는 것입지요. 제 짐작으로는, 나리께서 앞쪽 까닭으로 면도를 하신 지가 그리 오래되지 않은 듯합니다. 정말이지 그 결과가 아주 좋으십니다. 나리의 수염을 두고 이렇게 말할 만하니까요. '깎는 자에게 꽤나 묵직하다'(tondenti gravior)고요.\" \"내 짐작으로는,\" 존스가 말했다. \"자네는 아주 익살맞은 친구로군.\" \"크게 잘못 보셨습니다, 나리.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"저는 철학 공부에 너무 빠진 사람입니다. '여기서 그 눈물이 나오는 것이지요'(hinc illae lacrymae), 나리. 그것이 제 불운입니다. 지나친 학식이 저를 망쳤지요.\" \"이런,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이보게, 자네가 그 직업에 흔히 따르는 것보다 훨씬 많은 학식을 지녔다는 건 인정하겠네. 하지만 그것이 어떻게 자네를 망쳤다는 건지는 모르겠군.\" \"아아! 나리.\" 면도꾼이 대답했다. \"제 아버지는 그 때문에 저를 상속에서 내치셨답니다. 아버지는 무용 선생이셨는데, 제가 춤을 추기도 전에 글을 읽을 줄 알았다는 이유로 저를 미워하셔서, 한 푼도 남김없이 다른 자식들에게 다 물려주셨지요. 관자놀이 쪽도 손봐 드릴깝쇼ㅡ아이고! 죄송합니다, 아무래도 '원고에 빈틈이 있는'(hiatus in manuscriptis) 모양입니다. 나리께서 전쟁터로 가신다는 말을 들었습니다만, 잘못 안 것 같군요.\" \"어째서 그렇게 단정하나?\" 존스가 물었다. \"분명히, 나리.\" 이발사가 대답했다. \"나리께선 깨진 머리를 그곳까지 들고 가실 만큼 어리석은 분은 아니실 테니까요. 그건 '뉴캐슬에 석탄을 나르는' 격이 될 테니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Upon my word,” cries Jones, “thou art a very odd fellow, and I like thy humour extremely; I shall be very glad if thou wilt come to me after dinner, and drink a glass with me; I long to be better acquainted with thee.”", "\"정말이지,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"자네는 아주 별난 친구일세. 자네의 익살이 마음에 쏙 드는군. 저녁을 먹고 난 뒤에 내게 와서 나와 한잔 나눠 준다면 무척 기쁘겠네. 자네와 좀 더 가까워지고 싶구먼.\""]], [["“O dear sir!” said the barber, “I can do you twenty times as great a favour, if you will accept of it.”--“What is that, my friend?” cries Jones. “Why, I will drink a bottle with you if you please; for I dearly love good-nature; and as you have found me out to be a comical fellow, so I have no skill in physiognomy, if you are not one of the best-natured gentlemen in the universe.” Jones now walked downstairs neatly drest, and perhaps the fair Adonis was not a lovelier figure; and yet he had no charms for my landlady; for as that good woman did not resemble Venus at all in her person, so neither did she in her taste. Happy had it been for Nanny the chambermaid, if she had seen with the eyes of her mistress, for that poor girl fell so violently in love with Jones in five minutes, that her passion afterwards cost her many a sigh. This Nanny was extremely pretty, and altogether as coy; for she had refused a drawer, and one or two young farmers in the neighbourhood, but the bright eyes of our heroe thawed all her ice in a moment.", "\"오, 그러신다면 나리!\" 이발사가 말했다. \"받아 주시기만 한다면, 그보다 스무 배는 더 큰 호의를 베풀어 드릴 수 있습니다요.\" \"그게 무엇인가, 친구?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그게요, 원하신다면 제가 나리와 한 병을 비워 드리겠습니다. 저는 좋은 마음씨를 진정으로 사랑하거든요. 나리께서 저를 익살맞은 친구라고 알아보셨으니, 만일 나리께서 이 세상에서 가장 마음씨 고운 신사 가운데 한 분이 아니시라면, 제겐 관상 보는 재주가 영 없는 셈이겠지요.\" 이제 말끔히 차려입은 존스는 아래층으로 내려왔는데, 아마 저 아름다운 아도니스라도 그보다 더 사랑스러운 모습은 아니었을 것이다. 그런데도 우리 여주인에게는 그가 아무런 매력도 없었다. 그 마음씨 좋은 여인이 생김새에서 비너스를 조금도 닮지 않았듯이, 취향에서도 마찬가지였기 때문이다. 객실 하녀 내니가 제 안주인의 눈으로 그를 보았더라면 차라리 다행이었을 것을. 그 가엾은 처녀는 단 오 분 만에 존스에게 어찌나 격렬하게 빠져 버렸던지, 그 연정이 훗날 그녀에게 숱한 한숨을 치르게 했으니 말이다. 이 내니는 무척 예뻤고, 또 그만큼 도도했다. 술 따르는 사내며 동네의 젊은 농부 한둘을 마다한 적도 있었으니까. 하지만 우리 주인공의 빛나는 눈동자는 한순간에 그녀의 얼음을 죄다 녹여 버렸다."]], [["When Jones returned to the kitchen, his cloth was not yet laid; nor indeed was there any occasion it should, his dinner remaining in statu quo, as did the fire which was to dress it.", "존스가 부엌으로 돌아왔을 때, 식탁보는 아직 깔려 있지 않았다. 사실 그럴 필요도 없었으니, 그의 저녁은 여전히 '있던 그대로'(in statu quo)였고, 그것을 익혀 줄 불 또한 마찬가지였다."], ["This disappointment might have put many a philosophical temper into a passion; but it had no such effect on Jones.", "이런 낭패라면 어지간한 철학자의 기질도 발끈하게 만들 만했지만, 존스에게는 그런 효과가 없었다."], ["He only gave the landlady a gentle rebuke, saying, “Since it was so difficult to get it heated he would eat the beef cold.”", "그는 다만 여주인에게 부드럽게 한마디 나무라며 말했다. \"데우기가 그렇게 어렵다면, 고기를 차게 그냥 먹겠소.\""], ["But now the good woman, whether moved by compassion, or by shame, or by whatever other motive, I cannot tell, first gave her servants a round scold for disobeying the orders which she had never given, and then bidding the drawer lay a napkin in the Sun, she set about the matter in good earnest, and soon accomplished it.", "그러나 이번에는 그 마음씨 좋은 여인이, 측은한 마음에서였는지, 부끄러움에서였는지, 아니면 다른 어떤 까닭에서였는지 나로서는 알 수 없으나, 먼저 자신이 내린 적도 없는 명령을 어겼다며 하인들을 한바탕 호되게 꾸짖고는, 술 따르는 사내에게 '해의 방'에 냅킨을 깔라고 이른 뒤, 진심으로 일에 매달려 곧 그것을 해냈다."]], [["This Sun, into which Jones was now conducted, was truly named, as lucus a non lucendo; for it was an apartment into which the sun had scarce ever looked.", "존스가 이제 안내받아 들어간 이 '해의 방'은 참으로 '빛이 들지 않는데도 숲이라 불리는'(lucus a non lucendo) 식의 이름이었다. 해라곤 거의 들여다본 적이 없는 방이었기 때문이다."], ["It was indeed the worst room in the house; and happy was it for Jones that it was so.", "사실 그 집에서 가장 형편없는 방이었는데, 존스로서는 그게 차라리 다행이었다."], ["However, he was now too hungry to find any fault; but having once satisfied his appetite, he ordered the drawer to carry a bottle of wine into a better room, and expressed some resentment at having been shown into a dungeon.", "어쨌든 그는 지금 너무 배가 고팠던 터라 흠을 잡을 겨를이 없었다. 그러나 일단 허기를 채우고 나자, 그는 술 따르는 사내에게 포도주 한 병을 더 나은 방으로 가져오라 이르며, 자신을 토굴 같은 데로 안내한 것에 약간의 불만을 내비쳤다."]], [["The drawer having obeyed his commands, he was, after some time, attended by the barber, who would not indeed have suffered him to wait so long for his company had he not been listening in the kitchen to the landlady, who was entertaining a circle that she had gathered round her with the history of poor Jones, part of which she had extracted from his own lips, and the other part was her own ingenious composition; for she said “he was a poor parish boy, taken into the house of Squire Allworthy, where he was bred up as an apprentice, and now turned out of doors for his misdeeds, particularly for making love to his young mistress, and probably for robbing the house; for how else should he come by the little money he hath; and this,” says she, “is your gentleman, forsooth!”--“A servant of Squire Allworthy!” says the barber; “what's his name?”--“Why he told me his name was Jones,” says she: “perhaps he goes by a wrong name. Nay, and he told me, too, that the squire had maintained him as his own son, thof he had quarrelled with him now.”--“And if his name be Jones, he told you the truth,” said the barber; “for I have relations who live in that country; nay, and some people say he is his son.”--“Why doth he not go by the name of his father?”--“I can't tell that,” said the barber; “many people's sons don't go by the name of their father.”--“Nay,” said the landlady, “if I thought he was a gentleman's son, thof he was a bye-blow, I should behave to him in another guess manner; for many of these bye-blows come to be great men, and, as my poor first husband used to say, never affront any customer that's a gentleman.”", "술 따르는 사내가 그 분부를 따른 뒤, 얼마쯤 지나 이발사가 그의 시중을 들러 왔다. 사실 이발사는 존스를 이렇게 오래 기다리게 두지 않았을 테지만, 부엌에서 여주인의 이야기에 귀를 기울이느라 그리된 것이었다. 여주인은 자기 둘레에 사람들을 한 무리 그러모아 놓고 가엾은 존스의 내력을 들려주고 있었는데, 그 일부는 존스 본인의 입에서 캐낸 것이었고, 나머지는 그녀 자신이 기발하게 지어낸 것이었다. 그녀의 말인즉, \"그는 가엾은 교구 출신 소년인데, 올워디 영주님 댁에 들어가 거기서 도제로 길러지다가, 이제 못된 짓 때문에 쫓겨난 거라우. 특히 젊은 안주인께 연정을 품은 죄가 크고, 아마도 그 집을 턴 죄도 있을 거유. 안 그러면 저 사람이 가진 그 푼돈이 대체 어디서 나왔겠수. 그러니,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저게 바로 당신들이 말하는 그 신사 양반이라우!\" \"올워디 영주님 댁 하인이라고요!\" 이발사가 말했다. \"그자 이름이 뭐랍니까?\" \"글쎄, 제 입으로는 존스라고 합디다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어쩌면 가짜 이름으로 행세하는 걸지도 모르지요. 게다가 그러더군요, 영주님이 자길 친아들처럼 거두어 주셨는데, 이제 그분과 틀어졌다고 말이우.\" \"이름이 존스라면, 당신한테 사실대로 말한 거요.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"나도 그 고장에 사는 친척이 있는데, 사람들 중에는 그가 영주님의 아들이라고 말하는 이들도 있으니까요.\" \"그럼 어째서 아버지 이름을 쓰지 않는답니까?\" \"그건 나도 모르겠소.\" 이발사가 말했다. \"많은 사람들의 아들이 제 아버지 이름을 쓰지 않고 살지요.\" \"하긴,\" 여주인이 말했다. \"그가 비록 사생아라 해도 신사의 아들이라고 생각했더라면, 내가 그를 사뭇 다르게 대접했을 텐데. 그런 사생아들 중에 큰 인물이 되는 이가 한둘이 아니거든. 그리고 내 가엾은 첫 남편이 늘 하던 말마따나, 신사인 손님은 절대 함부로 대하면 안 되는 법이니까.\""]], [["A dialogue between Mr Jones and the barber.", "존스 씨와 이발사가 나눈 대화."]], [["This conversation passed partly while Jones was at dinner in his dungeon, and partly while he was expecting the barber in the parlour. And, as soon as it was ended, Mr Benjamin, as we have said, attended him, and was very kindly desired to sit down. Jones then filling out a glass of wine, drank his health by the appellation of doctissime tonsorum. “Ago tibi gratias, domine” said the barber; and then looking very steadfastly at Jones, he said, with great gravity, and with a seeming surprize, as if he had recollected a face he had seen before, “Sir, may I crave the favour to know if your name is not Jones?” To which the other answered, “That it was.”--“Proh deum atque hominum fidem!” says the barber; “how strangely things come to pass! Mr Jones, I am your most obedient servant. I find you do not know me, which indeed is no wonder, since you never saw me but once, and then you was very young. Pray, sir, how doth the good Squire Allworthy? how doth ille optimus omnium patronus?”--“I find,” said Jones, “you do indeed know me; but I have not the like happiness of recollecting you.”--“I do not wonder at that,” cries Benjamin; “but I am surprized I did not know you sooner, for you are not in the least altered. And pray, sir, may I, without offence, enquire whither you are travelling this way?”--“Fill the glass, Mr Barber,” said Jones, “and ask no more questions.”--“Nay, sir,” answered Benjamin, “I would not be troublesome; and I hope you don't think me a man of an impertinent curiosity, for that is a vice which nobody can lay to my charge; but I ask pardon; for when a gentleman of your figure travels without his servants, we may suppose him to be, as we say, in casu incognito, and perhaps I ought not to have mentioned your name.”--“I own,” says Jones, “I did not expect to have been so well known in this country as I find I am; yet, for particular reasons, I shall be obliged to you if you will not mention my name to any other person till I am gone from hence.”--“Pauca verba,” answered the barber;” and I wish no other here knew you but myself; for some people have tongues; but I promise you I can keep a secret. My enemies will allow me that virtue.”--“And yet that is not the characteristic of your profession, Mr Barber,” answered Jones. “Alas! sir,” replied Benjamin, “Non si male nunc et olim sic erit. I was not born nor bred a barber, I assure you. I have spent most of my time among gentlemen, and though I say it, I understand something of gentility. And if you had thought me as worthy of your confidence as you have some other people, I should have shown you I could have kept a secret better. I should not have degraded your name in a public kitchen; for indeed, sir, some people have not used you well; for besides making a public proclamation of what you told them of a quarrel between yourself and Squire Allworthy, they added lies of their own, things which I knew to be lies.”--“You surprize me greatly,” cries Jones. “Upon my word, sir,” answered Benjamin, “I tell the truth, and I need not tell you my landlady was the person. I am sure it moved me to hear the story, and I hope it is all false; for I have a great respect for you, I do assure you I have, and have had ever since the good-nature you showed to Black George, which was talked of all over the country, and I received more than one letter about it. Indeed, it made you beloved by everybody. You will pardon me, therefore; for it was real concern at what I heard made me ask many questions; for I have no impertinent curiosity about me: but I love good-nature and thence became amoris abundantia erga te.”", "이 대화는 일부는 존스가 자신의 토굴 같은 방에서 저녁을 먹는 동안에, 또 일부는 그가 응접실에서 이발사를 기다리는 동안에 오갔다. 그리고 그 대화가 끝나자마자, 앞서 말했듯이 벤저민 씨가 그를 시중들러 왔고, 존스는 매우 친절하게 그에게 앉으라고 청했다. 그러고 나서 존스는 포도주 한 잔을 따라, \"도시시메 톤소룸(이발사 중에 가장 박식하신 분)\"이라는 호칭으로 그의 건강을 위해 잔을 비웠다. \"아고 티비 그라티아스, 도미네(감사드립니다, 나리).\" 이발사가 말했다. 그러고는 존스를 아주 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 마치 전에 본 적 있는 얼굴을 떠올리기라도 한 듯, 자못 진지하고도 놀란 기색으로 이렇게 말했다. \"나리, 외람되오나 혹 성함이 존스가 아니신지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\" 이에 상대는 \"그렇소\"라고 답했다. \"프로 데움 아트퀘 호미눔 피뎀(신과 인간의 신의를 두고)!\" 이발사가 말했다. \"참으로 묘하게도 일이 이렇게 풀리는군요! 존스 나리, 저는 나리의 더없이 순종하는 종입니다. 저를 못 알아보시는 듯한데, 하기야 당연한 일이지요. 단 한 번 저를 보셨을 뿐이고, 그때 나리께서는 무척 어리셨으니까요. 그래, 나리, 그 어지신 올워디 나리께서는 어떻게 지내십니까? 일레 옵티무스 옴니움 파트로누스(모든 후원자 가운데 가장 훌륭하신 그분)께서는 어떻게 지내시는지요?\" 존스가 말했다. \"보아하니 그대가 정말 나를 아는 모양이오. 하나 나는 그대를 떠올리는 그런 기쁨은 누리지 못하는구려.\" \"그건 이상할 게 없습니다.\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"다만 제가 나리를 더 빨리 알아보지 못한 게 놀라울 따름이지요. 조금도 변하지 않으셨으니까요. 그런데 나리, 결례가 안 된다면, 이 길로 어디를 가시는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\" \"잔이나 채우시오, 이발사 양반.\" 존스가 말했다. \"그리고 더는 묻지 마시오.\" \"아닙니다, 나리.\" 벤저민이 답했다. \"저는 성가시게 굴 생각이 없습니다. 또 부디 저를 무례하게 캐묻기나 하는 사람으로 여기지는 말아 주십시오. 그건 누구도 제게 씌울 수 없는 흠이니까요. 하지만 용서를 빕니다. 나리 같은 풍모의 신사분이 하인도 없이 여행을 하실 때면, 흔히 말하듯 인 카수 인코그니토(신분을 숨긴 채) 다니시는 것으로 짐작해야 마땅하니, 어쩌면 제가 나리의 성함을 입에 올리지 말았어야 했는지도 모르겠습니다.\" \"고백하건대,\" 존스가 말했다. \"내가 이 지방에서 이렇게까지 잘 알려져 있을 줄은 몰랐소. 그러나 특별한 사정이 있으니, 내가 이곳을 떠날 때까지 다른 누구에게도 내 이름을 말하지 않는다면 고맙겠소.\" \"파우카 베르바(말은 적게).\" 이발사가 답했다. \"그리고 저 말고는 이곳의 누구도 나리를 알지 못했으면 좋겠습니다. 입이 가벼운 사람들이 더러 있으니까요. 하지만 저는 비밀을 지킬 수 있다고 약속드립니다. 제 원수라도 그 미덕만큼은 제게 인정해 줄 것입니다.\" \"하나 그것이 그대 직업의 특징은 아니지 않소, 이발사 양반.\" 존스가 답했다. \"아아! 나리.\" 벤저민이 대꾸했다. \"논 시 말레 눙크 에트 올림 시크 에리트(지금 형편이 나쁘다고 늘 그러하지는 않으리라). 분명히 말씀드리지만, 저는 이발사로 태어나지도 길러지지도 않았습니다. 저는 대부분의 세월을 신사분들 사이에서 보냈고, 제 입으로 말하긴 뭣하지만, 점잖음이 무엇인지도 어느 정도 압니다. 만일 나리께서 다른 어떤 사람들에게 그러셨던 만큼 저를 신뢰할 만하다고 여기셨더라면, 제가 비밀을 더 잘 지킬 수 있는 사람임을 보여 드렸을 것입니다. 저라면 공개된 부엌에서 나리의 성함을 욕보이지는 않았을 테니까요. 실로, 나리, 어떤 사람들은 나리를 곱게 대하지 않았습니다. 나리와 올워디 나리 사이의 다툼에 관해 나리께서 말씀하신 것을 온 동네에 떠벌렸을 뿐 아니라, 거기에 제 멋대로 거짓말까지 보탰으니까요. 제가 거짓인 줄 아는 것들 말입니다.\" \"참으로 놀라운 말이오.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"맹세코, 나리.\" 벤저민이 답했다. \"저는 진실을 말씀드리는 것이며, 그게 제 안주인이었다는 사실은 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요. 그 이야기를 듣고 마음이 흔들린 건 분명하고, 부디 그게 다 거짓이기를 바랍니다. 저는 나리를 무척 존경하니까요, 정말로 그렇습니다. 그리고 나리께서 블랙 조지에게 베푸신 그 인정 어린 처사 이래로 줄곧 그래 왔습니다. 그 일은 온 동네에 회자되었고, 저도 그에 관한 편지를 한 통 넘게 받았습니다. 실로 그 일로 나리는 모두에게 사랑받게 되셨지요. 그러니 용서해 주십시오. 제가 이것저것 많이 여쭌 것은 제가 들은 일에 대한 진심 어린 걱정 때문이었습니다. 저에게는 무례하게 캐묻는 호기심 따위는 없습니다. 다만 저는 인정 많은 마음을 사랑하고, 그리하여 아모리스 아분단티아 에르가 테(나리를 향한 넘치는 애정)에 이르게 된 것이지요.\""]], [["Every profession of friendship easily gains credit with the miserable; it is no wonder therefore, if Jones, who, besides his being miserable, was extremely open-hearted, very readily believed all the professions of Benjamin, and received him into his bosom. The scraps of Latin, some of which Benjamin applied properly enough, though it did not savour of profound literature, seemed yet to indicate something superior to a common barber; and so indeed did his whole behaviour. Jones therefore believed the truth of what he had said, as to his original and education; and at length, after much entreaty, he said, “Since you have heard, my friend, so much of my affairs, and seem so desirous to know the truth, if you will have patience to hear it, I will inform you of the whole.”--“Patience!” cries Benjamin, “that I will, if the chapter was never so long; and I am very much obliged to you for the honour you do me.”", "우정의 고백이란 비참한 처지에 놓인 이에게는 쉽사리 믿음을 얻기 마련이다. 그러니 비참할 뿐 아니라 천성이 더없이 솔직하기까지 했던 존스가 벤저민의 모든 고백을 선뜻 믿고 그를 가슴으로 받아들인 것도 이상할 게 없다. 라틴어 토막들은, 비록 깊은 학식이 배어 있지는 않았으나 그중 더러는 벤저민이 제법 적절하게 가져다 쓴 터라, 평범한 이발사 이상의 무언가를 일러 주는 듯했다. 실로 그의 행동거지 전체가 그러했다. 그리하여 존스는 그의 출신과 교육에 관해 그가 한 말이 사실이라고 믿었다. 그리고 마침내, 여러 번 간청을 받은 끝에 그는 말했다. \"벗이여, 그대가 내 사정을 그토록 많이 들었고, 또 진실을 알고 싶어 그토록 안달하는 듯하니, 끝까지 참고 들어 줄 수 있다면 내가 모든 것을 일러 주겠소.\" \"참으라고요!\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"이야기가 아무리 길어도 그러고말고요. 그리고 제게 베풀어 주시는 이 영광에 깊이 감사드립니다.\""]], [["Jones now began, and related the whole history, forgetting only a circumstance or two, namely, everything which passed on that day in which he had fought with Thwackum; and ended with his resolution to go to sea, till the rebellion in the North had made him change his purpose, and had brought him to the place where he then was.", "이제 존스는 이야기를 시작했고, 자초지종을 모두 들려주었다. 다만 한두 가지 정황만은 빼놓았으니, 곧 스왜컴과 싸웠던 그날에 있었던 일은 모조리 빠뜨린 것이다. 그리고 바다로 나가려 했던 결심에서, 북부의 반란이 그 작정을 바꾸어 놓아 그가 지금 있는 이곳까지 오게 된 데까지 이야기를 마쳤다."]], [["Little Benjamin, who had been all attention, never once interrupted the narrative; but when it was ended he could not help observing, that there must be surely something more invented by his enemies, and told Mr Allworthy against him, or so good a man would never have dismissed one he had loved so tenderly, in such a manner.", "줄곧 온 신경을 기울이고 있던 꼬마 벤저민은 단 한 번도 그 이야기를 가로막지 않았다. 그러나 이야기가 끝나자, 분명 나리의 원수들이 무언가를 더 지어내어 올워디 씨에게 일러바쳤음이 틀림없다는 말을 하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["To which Jones answered, “He doubted not but such villanous arts had been made use of to destroy him.”", "그렇지 않고서야 그토록 어진 분이 그토록 다정하게 아끼던 사람을 그런 식으로 내쫓았을 리 없다는 것이었다. 이에 존스는 \"그를 파멸시키려고 그런 악랄한 술책이 쓰였음을 의심치 않는다\"고 답했다."]], [["And surely it was scarce possible for any one to have avoided making the same remark with the barber, who had not indeed heard from Jones one single circumstance upon which he was condemned; for his actions were not now placed in those injurious lights in which they had been misrepresented to Allworthy; nor could he mention those many false accusations which had been from time to time preferred against him to Allworthy: for with none of these he was himself acquainted.", "실로 이발사와 똑같은 말을 하지 않을 사람은 거의 없었을 것이다. 그는 존스에게서, 그가 단죄받은 근거가 된 정황을 단 하나도 듣지 못했으니 말이다."], ["He had likewise, as we have observed, omitted many material facts in his present relation.", "그의 행동은 이제 올워디에게 왜곡되어 전해졌던 그 해로운 빛깔로 놓여 있지 않았고, 또 존스는 그동안 올워디에게 거듭 제기되었던 그 숱한 거짓 고발들을 입에 올릴 수도 없었으니, 그 자신이 그 가운데 어느 것도 알지 못했기 때문이다."], ["Upon the whole, indeed, everything now appeared in such favourable colours to Jones, that malice itself would have found it no easy matter to fix any blame upon him.", "게다가 앞서 보았듯이, 그는 지금의 이야기에서 중요한 사실들을 여럿 빠뜨리기도 했다. 요컨대 실로 이제 모든 일이 존스에게 너무도 유리한 빛깔로 비쳤으므로, 악의 그 자체라도 그에게 어떤 허물을 씌우기란 결코 쉽지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Not that Jones desired to conceal or to disguise the truth; nay, he would have been more unwilling to have suffered any censure to fall on Mr Allworthy for punishing him, than on his own actions for deserving it; but, in reality, so it happened, and so it always will happen; for let a man be never so honest, the account of his own conduct will, in spite of himself, be so very favourable, that his vices will come purified through his lips, and, like foul liquors well strained, will leave all their foulness behind. For though the facts themselves may appear, yet so different will be the motives, circumstances, and consequences, when a man tells his own story, and when his enemy tells it, that we scarce can recognise the facts to be one and the same.", "존스가 진실을 숨기거나 꾸미려 했다는 말은 아니다. 오히려 그는 자신이 벌받아 마땅한 행동을 했다는 비난을 받기보다, 자신을 벌한 일로 올워디 씨가 어떤 비난이라도 받는 것을 더 견디지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 실제로는 그렇게 되었고, 또 늘 그렇게 되기 마련이다. 사람이 아무리 정직하다 해도, 제 행실에 관한 이야기는 본인의 의도와는 상관없이 너무도 유리하게 흘러가서, 그의 악덕은 입술을 거치며 정화되어 나오고, 잘 거른 탁한 물처럼 그 모든 더러움을 뒤에 남겨 두고 만다. 사실 자체는 드러난다 해도, 사람이 제 이야기를 할 때와 원수가 그 이야기를 할 때, 그 동기와 정황과 결과가 어찌나 다른지, 우리는 그것이 하나의 똑같은 사실임을 좀처럼 알아보지 못하기 때문이다."]], [["Though the barber had drank down this story with greedy ears, he was not yet satisfied.", "이발사는 이 이야기를 게걸스러운 귀로 들이켰지만, 그래도 아직 만족하지 못했다."], ["There was a circumstance behind which his curiosity, cold as it was, most eagerly longed for.", "그 뒤에는 그의 호기심이, 차분한 척하면서도 가장 간절히 갈망하는 한 가지 정황이 남아 있었다."], ["Jones had mentioned the fact of his amour, and of his being the rival of Blifil, but had cautiously concealed the name of the young lady.", "존스는 자신의 연애 사실과, 자신이 블리필의 연적이라는 사실은 언급했으나, 그 처녀의 이름만은 조심스레 숨겨 두었던 것이다."], ["The barber, therefore, after some hesitation, and many hums and hahs, at last begged leave to crave the name of the lady, who appeared to be the principal cause of all this mischief.", "그리하여 이발사는 얼마간 머뭇거리고 헛기침을 여러 번 한 끝에, 마침내 이 모든 화근의 주된 원인으로 보이는 그 숙녀의 이름을 알려 달라고 청했다."], ["Jones paused a moment, and then said, “Since I have trusted you with so much, and since, I am afraid, her name is become too publick already on this occasion, I will not conceal it from you.", "존스는 잠시 멈칫하더니 이렇게 말했다. \"내가 그대에게 이미 그토록 많은 것을 털어놓았고, 또 이 일로 그녀의 이름이 벌써 너무 널리 알려졌을까 두려우니, 그대에게 숨기지 않겠소."], ["Her name is Sophia Western.”", "그녀의 이름은 소피아 웨스턴이오.\""]], [["“Proh deum atque hominum fidem!", "\"프로 데움 아트퀘 호미눔 피뎀(신과 인간의 신의를 두고)!"], ["Squire Western hath a daughter grown a woman!”--“Ay, and such a woman,”", "웨스턴 나리께 여인이 된 딸이 있다니!\" \"그렇소, 그것도 어떤 여인이냐 하면,\""], ["cries Jones, “that the world cannot match.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"세상에 견줄 이가 없는 여인이오."], ["No eye ever saw anything so beautiful; but that is her least excellence.", "그토록 아름다운 것을 본 눈은 없었지. 하나 그건 그녀의 가장 작은 미덕에 지나지 않소."], ["Such sense!", "그 분별력이라니!"], ["such goodness!", "그 선함이라니! 오, 영원히 칭송해도 그녀의 미덕은 절반도 다 못 말할 거요!\""], ["Oh, I could praise her for ever, and yet should omit half her virtues!”--“Mr Western a daughter grown up!”", "\"웨스턴 씨에게 다 큰 딸이 있다니!\" 이발사가 외쳤다."], ["cries the barber: “I remember the father a boy; well, Tempus edax rerum.”", "\"저는 그 아버지가 소년이던 시절을 기억하는데요. 허, 템푸스 에닥스 레룸(세월은 만물을 삼키는 것)이로군요.\""]], [["The wine being now at an end, the barber pressed very eagerly to be his bottle; but Jones absolutely refused, saying, “He had already drank more than he ought: and that he now chose to retire to his room, where he wished he could procure himself a book.”--“A book!” cries Benjamin; “what book would you have? Latin or English? I have some curious books in both languages; such as Erasmi Colloquia, Ovid de Tristibus, Gradus ad Parnassum; and in English I have several of the best books, though some of them are a little torn; but I have a great part of Stowe's Chronicle; the sixth volume of Pope's Homer; the third volume of the Spectator; the second volume of Echard's Roman History; the Craftsman; Robinson Crusoe; Thomas a Kempis; and two volumes of Tom Brown's Works.”", "이제 포도주가 다 떨어지자 이발사는 자기가 한 병 내겠다고 자못 열을 올리며 권했다. 그러나 존스는 한사코 거절하며 말했다. \"이미 마실 것보다 더 마셨소. 이제 내 방으로 물러가고 싶은데, 거기서 책이라도 한 권 구할 수 있으면 좋겠소.\" \"책이라고요!\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"어떤 책을 원하십니까? 라틴어 책입니까, 영어 책입니까? 저에게는 두 언어로 된 진귀한 책이 더러 있습니다. 이를테면 에라스무스의 《대화집》, 오비디우스의 《비가》, 《파르나소스로 오르는 계단》 같은 것들이지요. 영어로도 가장 훌륭한 책을 여러 권 갖고 있습니다. 더러 좀 찢어지긴 했지만요. 스토의 《연대기》 상당 부분, 포프의 《호메로스》 제6권, 《스펙테이터》 제3권, 에처드의 《로마사》 제2권, 《크래프츠맨》, 《로빈슨 크루소》, 토마스 아 켐피스, 그리고 톰 브라운의 《작품집》 두 권이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Those last,”", "\"마지막 그것은,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “are books I never saw, so if you please lend me one of those volumes.”", "\"내가 한 번도 본 적 없는 책이오. 그러니 괜찮다면 그 가운데 한 권을 빌려 주시오.\""], ["The barber assured him he would be highly entertained, for he looked upon the author to have been one of the greatest wits that ever the nation produced.", "이발사는 더없이 즐거우실 거라고 장담하며, 그 작가야말로 이 나라가 배출한 가장 위대한 재사 가운데 하나라고 여긴다고 했다. 그러고는 바로 가까이 있던 자기 집으로 건너가더니 곧 돌아왔다."], ["He then stepped to his house, which was hard by, and immediately returned; after which, the barber having received very strict injunctions of secrecy from Jones, and having sworn inviolably to maintain it, they separated; the barber went home, and Jones retired to his chamber.", "그 후, 이발사가 존스에게서 비밀을 지키라는 엄중한 다짐을 받고, 그것을 어김없이 지키겠노라 맹세한 다음, 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 이발사는 집으로 돌아갔고, 존스는 자기 방으로 물러갔다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["In which more of the talents of Mr Benjamin will appear, as well as who this extraordinary person was.", "6장. 여기서 벤저민 씨의 또 다른 재주가 드러나며, 이 별난 인물이 대체 누구인지도 밝혀진다."]], [["In the morning Jones grew a little uneasy at the desertion of his surgeon, as he apprehended some inconvenience, or even danger, might attend the not dressing his wound; he enquired of the drawer, what other surgeons were to be met with in that neighbourhood.", "아침이 되자 존스는 외과의가 사라진 것이 조금 마음에 걸렸다. 상처를 치료하지 않고 두면 어떤 불편이, 어쩌면 위험까지도 따를지 모른다는 염려가 들었기 때문이다."], ["The drawer told him, there was one not far off; but he had known him often refuse to be concerned after another had been sent before him; “but, sir,”", "그는 술집 종업원에게 이 근방에서 만날 수 있는 다른 외과의가 누가 있느냐고 물었다."], ["says he, “if you will take my advice, there is not a man in the kingdom can do your business better than the barber who was with you last night.", "종업원은 멀지 않은 곳에 한 사람이 있긴 하다고 일러 주었지만, 그가 다른 사람이 먼저 불려 온 뒤에 끼어드는 일을 자주 마다하더라고 했다. \"그렇지만 손님,\""], ["We look upon him to be one of the ablest men at a cut in all this neighbourhood.", "그가 말했다. \"제 말을 들으실 거라면, 손님 일을 어젯밤에 함께 계셨던 그 이발사보다 더 잘 처리할 사람은 이 나라 어디에도 없습니다."], ["For though he hath not been her above three months, he hath done several great cures.”", "저희는 그분을 이 일대에서 칼 다루는 데 가장 솜씨 좋은 사람 가운데 하나로 보고 있지요. 이곳에 온 지 석 달밖에 안 됐는데도, 벌써 큰 병을 여럿 고쳤거든요.\""]], [["The drawer was presently dispatched for Little Benjamin, who being acquainted in what capacity he was wanted, prepared himself accordingly, and attended; but with so different an air and aspect from that which he wore when his basin was under his arm, that he could scarce be known to be the same person.", "종업원은 곧장 리틀 벤저민을 데리러 보내졌고, 벤저민은 자신이 어떤 자격으로 불려 가는지를 알고는 그에 맞게 채비를 갖춘 뒤 찾아왔다. 그런데 그 모습이 옆구리에 대야를 끼고 있을 때와는 어찌나 다른 분위기와 표정이었던지, 같은 사람이라고 알아보기 힘들 지경이었다."]], [["“So, tonsor,”", "\"이런, 이발사 양반,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["says Jones, “I find you have more trades than one; how came you not to inform me of this last night?”--“A surgeon,”", "\"보아하니 한 가지 직업만 가진 게 아니로군. 어째서 어젯밤엔 이걸 알려 주지 않았소?\""], ["answered Benjamin, with great gravity, “is a profession, not a trade.", "\"외과란,\" 벤저민이 자못 근엄하게 대답했다."], ["The reason why I did not acquaint you last night that I professed this art, was, that I then concluded you was under the hands of another gentleman, and I never love to interfere with my brethren in their business.", "\"직업이 아니라 하나의 전문 학문입니다. 제가 어젯밤에 이 의술을 익혔다는 사실을 알려 드리지 않은 까닭은, 그때 손님께서 다른 신사분의 손에 맡겨져 계신 줄 알았기 때문입니다."], ["Ars omnibus communis.", "저는 한 번도 동업자의 일에 끼어드는 걸 좋아한 적이 없거든요. 의술은 모두에게 공통된 것이니까요."], ["But now, sir, if you please, I will inspect your head, and when I see into your skull, I will give my opinion of your case.”", "자, 이제 손님, 괜찮으시다면 머리를 살펴보겠습니다. 두개골 속까지 들여다본 뒤에 손님 상태에 대한 제 소견을 말씀드리지요.\""]], [["Jones had no great faith in this new professor; however, he suffered him to open the bandage and to look at his wound; which as soon as he had done, Benjamin began to groan and shake his head violently.", "존스는 이 새 전문가를 그리 믿지는 않았으나, 그래도 붕대를 풀고 상처를 들여다보도록 내버려 두었다. 벤저민은 그렇게 하자마자 신음 소리를 내며 머리를 세차게 가로젓기 시작했다."], ["Upon which Jones, in a peevish manner, bid him not play the fool, but tell him in what condition he found him.", "그러자 존스는 짜증 섞인 투로, 어릿광대 짓 그만하고 자기가 어떤 상태인지나 말해 보라고 했다."], ["“Shall I answer you as a surgeon, or a friend?”", "\"외과의로서 답해 드릴까요, 아니면 친구로서 답해 드릴까요?\""], ["said Benjamin.", "벤저민이 물었다."], ["“As a friend, and seriously,”", "\"친구로서, 그리고 진지하게.\""], ["said Jones.", "존스가 말했다."], ["“Why then, upon my soul,”", "\"그렇다면, 정말이지,\" 벤저민이 외쳤다."], ["cries Benjamin, “it would require a great deal of art to keep you from being well after a very few dressings; and if you will suffer me to apply some salve of mine, I will answer for the success.”", "\"치료를 몇 번만 하고 나면 손님이 나아 버리는 걸 막는 데도 오히려 대단한 기술이 필요할 겁니다. 그러니 제 연고를 좀 발라 드리게만 허락해 주시면, 그 결과는 제가 책임지겠습니다.\""], ["Jones gave his consent, and the plaister was applied accordingly.", "존스가 승낙하자, 그에 따라 고약이 발라졌다."]], [["“There, sir,”", "\"자, 손님,\" 벤저민이 외쳤다."], ["cries Benjamin: “now I will, if you please, resume my former self; but a man is obliged to keep up some dignity in his countenance whilst he is performing these operations, or the world will not submit to be handled by him.", "\"이제 괜찮으시다면, 저는 본래의 제 모습으로 돌아오겠습니다. 다만 이런 시술을 하는 동안에는 사람이 얼굴에 어느 정도 위엄을 갖추지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["You can't imagine, sir, of how much consequence a grave aspect is to a grave character.", "안 그러면 세상 사람들이 그런 사람의 손에 몸을 맡기려 하질 않거든요. 손님은 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다, 근엄한 인물에게 근엄한 표정이 얼마나 중요한지를요."], ["A barber may make you laugh, but a surgeon ought rather to make you cry.”", "이발사는 손님을 웃게 만들어도 되지만, 외과의라면 차라리 손님을 울려야 마땅하지요.\""]], [["“Mr Barber, or Mr Surgeon, or Mr Barber-surgeon,” said Jones. “O dear sir!” answered Benjamin, interrupting him, “Infandum, regina, jubes renovare dolorem. You recall to my mind that cruel separation of the united fraternities, so much to the prejudice of both bodies, as all separations must be, according to the old adage, Vis unita fortior; which to be sure there are not wanting some of one or of the other fraternity who are able to construe. What a blow was this to me, who unite both in my own person!” “Well, by whatever name you please to be called,” continued Jones, “you certainly are one of the oddest, most comical fellows I ever met with, and must have something very surprizing in your story, which you must confess I have a right to hear.”--“I do confess it,” answered Benjamin, “and will very readily acquaint you with it, when you have sufficient leisure, for I promise you it will require a good deal of time.” Jones told him, he could never be more at leisure than at present. “Well, then,” said Benjamin, “I will obey you; but first I will fasten the door, that none may interrupt us.” He did so, and then advancing with a solemn air to Jones, said: “I must begin by telling you, sir, that you yourself have been the greatest enemy I ever had.” Jones was a little startled at this sudden declaration. “I your enemy, sir!” says he, with much amazement, and some sternness in his look. “Nay, be not angry,” said Benjamin, “for I promise you I am not. You are perfectly innocent of having intended me any wrong; for you was then an infant: but I shall, I believe, unriddle all this the moment I mention my name. Did you never hear, sir, of one Partridge, who had the honour of being reputed your father, and the misfortune of being ruined by that honour?” “I have, indeed, heard of that Partridge,” says Jones, “and have always believed myself to be his son.” “Well, sir,” answered Benjamin, “I am that Partridge; but I here absolve you from all filial duty, for I do assure you, you are no son of mine.” “How!” replied Jones, “and is it possible that a false suspicion should have drawn all the ill consequences upon you, with which I am too well acquainted?” “It is possible,” cries Benjamin, “for it is so: but though it is natural enough for men to hate even the innocent causes of their sufferings, yet I am of a different temper. I have loved you ever since I heard of your behaviour to Black George, as I told you; and I am convinced, from this extraordinary meeting, that you are born to make me amends for all I have suffered on that account. Besides, I dreamt, the night before I saw you, that I stumbled over a stool without hurting myself; which plainly showed me something good was towards me: and last night I dreamt again, that I rode behind you on a milk-white mare, which is a very excellent dream, and betokens much good fortune, which I am resolved to pursue unless you have the cruelty to deny me.”", "\"이발사 양반, 아니 외과의 양반, 아니 이발외과의 양반이라고 해야 하나.\" 존스가 말했다. \"아아, 손님!\" 벤저민이 말을 가로채며 대답했다. \"여왕이여, 차마 입에 올릴 수 없는 슬픔을 되살리라 명하시는군요. 손님께서는 제게 그 잔인한 분리를 떠올리게 하셨습니다. 하나로 묶여 있던 두 조합이 갈라져, 양쪽 단체 모두에게 그토록 큰 손해를 입힌 그 일 말이지요. 모든 분리란 본래 그런 법입니다. 옛 격언에 이르기를, 힘은 합쳐질 때 더 강해진다 하지 않습니까. 물론 어느 한쪽 조합이든 이 말을 풀이할 줄 아는 이가 없지는 않겠지요. 두 가지를 제 한 몸에 합쳐 지닌 저로서는, 그것이 얼마나 큰 타격이었겠습니까!\" \"뭐, 당신이 어떤 이름으로 불리고 싶든 간에,\" 존스가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 분명 내가 여태 만나 본 사람 가운데 가장 별나고 가장 우스꽝스러운 친구요. 그러니 당신 사연에는 무언가 아주 놀라운 게 틀림없이 있을 거요. 그리고 그걸 들을 권리가 내게 있다는 건 당신도 인정해야 할 거요.\" \"인정합니다.\" 벤저민이 대답했다. \"그리고 손님께서 넉넉히 짬이 나실 때 기꺼이 들려 드리지요. 장담컨대 시간이 꽤 걸릴 이야기거든요.\" 존스는 지금보다 더 한가한 때는 없을 거라고 말했다. \"그럼 좋습니다,\" 벤저민이 말했다. \"분부에 따르지요. 다만 먼저 문을 걸어 잠그겠습니다. 누구도 우리를 방해하지 못하도록요.\" 그는 그렇게 하고 나서, 엄숙한 태도로 존스에게 다가오며 말했다. \"먼저 이 말씀부터 드려야겠습니다, 손님. 손님이야말로 제가 여태 가진 가장 큰 원수였답니다.\" 존스는 이 느닷없는 선언에 적잖이 놀랐다. \"내가 당신 원수라고!\" 그가 몹시 놀라며, 표정에 다소 매서운 기색까지 띤 채 말했다. \"아니, 노여워 마십시오.\" 벤저민이 말했다. \"맹세코 저는 노여워하지 않으니까요. 손님은 제게 어떤 해를 끼치려는 뜻이 전혀 없었습니다. 그때 손님은 갓난아기였으니까요. 다만 제 이름을 대는 순간, 이 모든 수수께끼가 풀리리라 믿습니다. 손님, 혹시 패트리지라는 사람 이야기를 들어 보신 적 없습니까? 손님의 아버지라는 평판을 얻는 영예를 누렸으나, 바로 그 영예 때문에 신세를 망치는 불운을 겪은 사람 말입니다.\" \"그 패트리지 이야기라면 정말 들어 보았소.\" 존스가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 늘 내가 그의 아들이라고 믿어 왔소.\" \"그렇습니다, 손님.\" 벤저민이 대답했다. \"제가 바로 그 패트리지입니다. 하지만 저는 이 자리에서 손님을 모든 자식 된 도리에서 풀어 드리겠습니다. 단언컨대, 손님은 결코 제 아들이 아니니까요.\" \"어떻게!\" 존스가 받았다. \"그럼 잘못된 의심 하나가, 내가 너무도 잘 아는 그 모든 불행한 결과를 당신에게 몽땅 끌어왔다는 게 가능한 일이오?\" \"가능합니다.\" 벤저민이 외쳤다. \"실제로 그러했으니까요. 하지만 사람이 제 고통의 죄 없는 원인조차 미워하는 건 충분히 자연스러운 일이라 해도, 저는 기질이 좀 다릅니다. 아까 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 손님이 블랙 조지에게 보여 준 처신을 전해 들은 그 순간부터 손님을 사랑해 왔지요. 그리고 이 뜻밖의 만남을 통해 확신하게 되었습니다. 손님은 그 일로 제가 겪은 모든 고통을 제게 갚아 주려고 태어난 분이라고요. 게다가 손님을 만나기 전날 밤에는 발판에 걸려 넘어졌는데도 다치지 않는 꿈을 꾸었답니다. 좋은 일이 제게 다가오고 있다는 분명한 표시였지요. 그리고 어젯밤에는 또 우윳빛처럼 흰 암말을 타고 손님 뒤에 앉아 가는 꿈을 꾸었습니다. 이건 아주 훌륭한 꿈이라, 큰 행운을 점치는 징조랍니다. 손님이 잔인하게 거절만 하지 않으신다면, 저는 그 행운을 꼭 좇기로 마음먹었습니다.\""]], [["“I should be very glad, Mr Partridge,”", "\"나는 정말 기쁘겠소, 패트리지 씨.\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “to have it in my power to make you amends for your sufferings on my account, though at present I see no likelihood of it; however, I assure you I will deny you nothing which is in my power to grant.”", "\"비록 지금은 그럴 가망이 도무지 보이지 않지만, 나 때문에 당신이 겪은 고통을 갚아 줄 힘이 내게 있다면 말이오. 어쨌든 약속하건대, 내 힘으로 들어줄 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 거절하지 않겠소.\""]], [["“It is in your power sure enough,”", "\"분명히 손님의 힘으로 들어주실 수 있는 일입니다.\""], ["replied Benjamin; “for I desire nothing more than leave to attend you in this expedition.", "벤저민이 받았다. \"저는 이번 여정에 손님을 모시고 따라가도 좋다는 허락 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않으니까요."], ["Nay, I have so entirely set my heart upon it, that if you should refuse me, you will kill both a barber and a surgeon in one breath.”", "아니, 저는 여기에 어찌나 마음을 단단히 쏟았던지, 손님이 저를 거절하신다면 한숨에 이발사 하나와 외과의 하나를 함께 죽이시는 셈이 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Jones answered, smiling, that he should be very sorry to be the occasion of so much mischief to the public.", "존스는 웃으며, 자기가 세상에 그토록 큰 손실을 끼치는 빌미가 된다면 무척 안타까울 거라고 대답했다."], ["He then advanced many prudential reasons, in order to dissuade Benjamin (whom we shall hereafter call Partridge) from his purpose; but all were in vain.", "그러고 나서 그는 벤저민(이제부터 우리는 그를 패트리지라 부르기로 하자)이 그 뜻을 단념하도록 신중한 이유를 여럿 늘어놓았으나, 모두 헛수고였다."], ["Partridge relied strongly on his dream of the milk-white mare.", "패트리지는 우윳빛 흰 암말 꿈에 단단히 기대고 있었다. \"게다가 손님,\""], ["“Besides, sir,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “I promise you I have as good an inclination to the cause as any man can possibly have; and go I will, whether you admit me to go in your company or not.”", "\"장담컨대 저는 그 대의에 대해 누구 못지않은 좋은 뜻을 품고 있습니다. 그러니 손님이 동행으로 받아 주시든 안 받아 주시든, 저는 기어이 가고 말 겁니다.\""]], [["Jones, who was as much pleased with Partridge as Partridge could be with him, and who had not consulted his own inclination but the good of the other in desiring him to stay behind, when he found his friend so resolute, at last gave his consent; but then recollecting himself, he said, “Perhaps, Mr Partridge, you think I shall be able to support you, but I really am not;”", "존스도 패트리지가 자기를 좋아하는 만큼이나 패트리지가 마음에 들었고, 또 그에게 뒤에 남으라고 했던 것도 자기 좋자고가 아니라 상대의 이익을 헤아려서였던 터라, 친구가 그토록 굳게 결심한 것을 보고는 마침내 승낙하고 말았다. 하지만 곧 정신을 차리고 그는 말했다."], ["and then taking out his purse, he told out nine guineas, which he declared were his whole fortune.", "\"패트리지 씨, 어쩌면 당신은 내가 당신을 거둘 수 있으리라 여기는 모양인데, 실은 정말 그럴 형편이 못 되오.\" 그러고는 지갑을 꺼내 아홉 기니를 세어 보이며, 이것이 자기 전 재산이라고 밝혔다."]], [["Partridge answered, “That his dependence was only on his future favour; for he was thoroughly convinced he would shortly have enough in his power. At present, sir,” said he, “I believe I am rather the richer man of the two; but all I have is at your service, and at your disposal. I insist upon your taking the whole, and I beg only to attend you in the quality of your servant; Nil desperandum est Teucro duce et auspice Teucro”: but to this generous proposal concerning the money, Jones would by no means submit.", "패트리지가 대답했다. \"제가 기대는 건 오직 앞으로 베풀어 주실 호의뿐입니다. 저는 손님이 머지않아 손안에 넉넉한 재물을 쥐게 되시리라 철석같이 믿고 있으니까요. 지금으로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다. \"둘 중에서는 오히려 제가 더 가진 사람이지 싶습니다. 하지만 제가 가진 것은 모두 손님의 것이요, 손님 뜻대로 쓰실 것입니다. 부디 전부 받아 주십시오. 저는 그저 손님의 하인 자격으로 모시고 따라가기만을 청합니다. 토이케르가 이끌고 토이케르가 굽어보는 한, 결코 절망할 것이 없습니다.\" 그러나 돈에 관한 이 너그러운 제안만큼은 존스가 결코 받아들이려 하지 않았다."]], [["It was resolved to set out the next morning, when a difficulty arose concerning the baggage; for the portmanteau of Mr Jones was too large to be carried without a horse.", "이튿날 아침에 길을 떠나기로 정해졌는데, 그때 짐 문제로 한 가지 난관이 생겼다. 존스 씨의 여행 가방이 너무 커서 말 없이는 운반할 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["“If I may presume to give my advice,”", "\"제가 감히 한 말씀 드려도 된다면,\""], ["says Partridge, “this portmanteau, with everything in it, except a few shirts, should be left behind.", "패트리지가 말했다. \"셔츠 몇 벌만 빼고, 이 여행 가방은 안에 든 것까지 통째로 두고 가시는 게 좋겠습니다."], ["Those I shall be easily able to carry for you, and the rest of your cloaths will remain very safe locked up in my house.”", "그 셔츠 몇 벌이야 제가 손님을 위해 거뜬히 들고 갈 수 있고, 나머지 옷가지는 제 집에 단단히 잠가 두면 아주 안전하게 보관될 테니까요.\""]], [["This method was no sooner proposed than agreed to; and then the barber departed, in order to prepare everything for his intended expedition.", "이 방안은 제안되기가 무섭게 합의되었다. 그러고 나서 이발사는 떠나기로 한 여정을 위해 만반의 준비를 갖추러 자리를 떴다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["Containing better reasons than any which have yet appeared for the conduct of Partridge; an apology for the weakness of Jones; and some further anecdotes concerning my landlady.", "파트리지의 처신에 대해 지금까지 나온 어떤 것보다 더 그럴듯한 이유들을 담고, 존스의 약점에 대한 변명과, 우리 안주인에 관한 몇 가지 일화를 더 보탠다."]], [["Though Partridge was one of the most superstitious of men, he would hardly perhaps have desired to accompany Jones on his expedition merely from the omens of the joint-stool and white mare, if his prospect had been no better than to have shared the plunder gained in the field of battle.", "파트리지가 세상에서 손꼽히게 미신을 신봉하는 사람이긴 했지만, 만일 그가 바랄 수 있는 전망이라는 게 고작 전쟁터에서 얻은 노획물을 나눠 갖는 정도였다면, 그저 다리 셋 달린 의자와 흰 암말의 징조만 보고 존스의 원정에 따라나서고 싶어 하지는 않았을 것이다. 사실 파트리지가 존스에게서 들은 이야기를 곰곰이 되씹어 보니, 자기가 들은 그 어떤 이유를 들이대더라도 올워디 씨가 자기 아들을(그는 존스를 누구보다 굳게 그렇게 믿고 있었다) 집 밖으로 내쫓았다는 것이 도무지 납득되지 않았다."], ["In fact, when Partridge came to ruminate on the relation he had heard from Jones, he could not reconcile to himself that Mr Allworthy should turn his son (for so he most firmly believed him to be) out of doors, for any reason which he had heard assigned.", "그래서 그는 그 모든 이야기가 꾸며 낸 것이고, 자기 통신원들에게서 그 누구보다 거친 사람이라는 평판을 자주 들어 온 존스가 실은 아버지에게서 도망쳐 나온 것이라고 결론지었다. 그러자 그의 머릿속에 이런 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["He concluded, therefore, that the whole was a fiction, and that Jones, of whom he had often from his correspondents heard the wildest character, had in reality run away from his father.", "만일 자기가 이 젊은 신사를 설득해 아버지에게 돌아가게 할 수만 있다면, 그렇게 함으로써 올워디에게 큰 도움을 베푸는 셈이 되어 그동안의 노여움을 모조리 씻어 낼 수 있으리라고. 아니, 실은 그 노여움조차 거짓으로 꾸민 것이고, 올워디가 자기 명예를 지키려고 그를 희생양으로 삼은 것이라고 그는 생각했다."], ["It came into his head, therefore, that if he could prevail with the young gentleman to return back to his father, he should by that means render a service to Allworthy, which would obliterate all his former anger; nay, indeed, he conceived that very anger was counterfeited, and that Allworthy had sacrificed him to his own reputation.", "그리고 이 의심에는 그 나름대로 충분한 근거가 있었다. 그 훌륭한 분이 업둥이 아이에게 보인 다정한 태도, 자기는 결백함을 알기에 다른 사람이 자기를 죄인으로 여길 수 있다는 걸 상상조차 못 했던 파트리지 자신에게 보인 그 모진 엄격함, 그리고 마지막으로, 그에게 주던 연금이 공개적으로 끊긴 한참 뒤에도 은밀히 받아 온 그 보조금이 그것이었다."], ["And this suspicion indeed he well accounted for, from the tender behaviour of that excellent man to the foundling child; from his great severity to Partridge, who, knowing himself to be innocent, could not conceive that any other should think him guilty; lastly, from the allowance which he had privately received long after the annuity had been publickly taken from him, and which he looked upon as a kind of smart-money, or rather by way of atonement for injustice; for it is very uncommon, I believe, for men to ascribe the benefactions they receive to pure charity, when they can possibly impute them to any other motive.", "그는 그 보조금을 일종의 위자료, 아니 오히려 부당함에 대한 속죄의 표시쯤으로 여겼다. 내 생각에, 사람이란 자기가 받은 호의를 다른 어떤 동기로든 돌릴 수 있을 때에는 그것을 순수한 자선으로 돌리는 경우가 매우 드무니 말이다."], ["If he could by any means therefore persuade the young gentleman to return home, he doubted not but that he should again be received into the favour of Allworthy, and well rewarded for his pains; nay, and should be again restored to his native country; a restoration which Ulysses himself never wished more heartily than poor Partridge.", "그러므로 만일 어떤 수단으로든 이 젊은 신사를 설득해 집으로 돌려보낼 수만 있다면, 자기가 다시 올워디의 총애를 받아 그 수고에 대해 후한 보답을 얻으리라는 것, 아니 그뿐 아니라 자기 고향 땅으로 다시 돌아가게 되리라는 것을 그는 의심치 않았다. 그 귀향이라면, 가엾은 파트리지가 율리시스 못지않게 간절히 바라던 것이었다."]], [["As for Jones, he was well satisfied with the truth of what the other had asserted, and believed that Partridge had no other inducements but love to him, and zeal for the cause; a blameable want of caution and diffidence in the veracity of others, in which he was highly worthy of censure. To say the truth, there are but two ways by which men become possessed of this excellent quality. The one is from long experience, and the other is from nature; which last, I presume, is often meant by genius, or great natural parts; and it is infinitely the better of the two, not only as we are masters of it much earlier in life, but as it is much more infallible and conclusive; for a man who hath been imposed on by ever so many, may still hope to find others more honest; whereas he who receives certain necessary admonitions from within, that this is impossible, must have very little understanding indeed, if he ever renders himself liable to be once deceived. As Jones had not this gift from nature, he was too young to have gained it by experience; for at the diffident wisdom which is to be acquired this way, we seldom arrive till very late in life; which is perhaps the reason why some old men are apt to despise the understandings of all those who are a little younger than themselves.", "* 율리시스: 호메로스의 서사시 속 인물로, 오랜 방랑 끝에 고향으로 돌아가기를 애타게 갈망한 영웅. 한편 존스로 말하자면, 그는 상대가 한 말이 모두 진실이라고 흡족하게 믿었고, 파트리지에게 자기에 대한 애정과 대의를 향한 열의 말고는 다른 속셈이 없다고 여겼다. 이는 남의 진실성을 믿는 데서 경계심과 의심이 부족했던 탓인데, 이 점에서 그는 크게 나무람을 받아 마땅했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 사람이 이 빼어난 자질을 갖추게 되는 길은 두 가지뿐이다. 하나는 오랜 경험에서 오는 것이고, 다른 하나는 타고나는 것이다. 후자는 흔히 천재성, 또는 위대한 천부의 재능이라 불리는 것을 가리키지 싶다. 그리고 이 둘 가운데 후자가 비할 데 없이 낫다. 인생에서 훨씬 일찍 그것을 손에 넣을 수 있을 뿐 아니라, 훨씬 더 어김없고 확실하기 때문이다. 아무리 많은 사람에게 속아 넘어간 사람이라도 여전히 더 정직한 이를 만나기를 바랄 수 있지만, 그것이 불가능하다는 어떤 긴요한 깨우침을 마음속에서 받는 사람은, 만일 단 한 번이라도 속을 처지에 빠진다면 정말이지 이해력이 너무도 모자란 사람일 테니 말이다. 존스는 이 자질을 타고나지 못했고, 경험으로 얻기에는 너무 젊었다. 이런 식으로 얻게 되는, 의심할 줄 아는 지혜에는 우리가 인생의 아주 늦은 시기에 이르러서야 겨우 다다르기 때문이다. 어쩌면 이것이, 어떤 노인들이 자기보다 조금이라도 어린 사람들의 이해력을 죄다 업신여기기 쉬운 까닭일 것이다."]], [["Jones spent most part of the day in the company of a new acquaintance.", "존스는 그날 하루의 대부분을 새로 사귄 사람과 함께 보냈다."], ["This was no other than the landlord of the house, or rather the husband of the landlady.", "그 사람은 다름 아닌 이 집의 주인, 아니 더 정확히 말하면 안주인의 남편이었다."], ["He had but lately made his descent downstairs, after a long fit of the gout, in which distemper he was generally confined to his room during one half of the year; and during the rest, he walked about the house, smoaked his pipe, and drank his bottle with his friends, without concerning himself in the least with any kind of business.", "그는 오랜 통풍 발작을 앓다가 얼마 전에야 겨우 아래층으로 내려왔는데, 이 병 때문에 그는 한 해의 절반은 대개 방 안에 갇혀 지냈고, 나머지 절반 동안은 집 안을 어슬렁거리며 담배를 피우고 벗들과 술병을 비우면서, 그 어떤 일에도 도통 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 그는 이른바 신사로 길러졌다."], ["He had been bred, as they call it, a gentleman; that is, bred up to do nothing; and had spent a very small fortune, which he inherited from an industrious farmer his uncle, in hunting, horse-racing, and cock-fighting, and had been married by my landlady for certain purposes, which he had long since desisted from answering; for which she hated him heartily.", "다시 말해, 아무 일도 하지 않도록 길러졌다는 뜻이다. 그는 부지런한 농사꾼이던 삼촌에게서 물려받은 얼마 안 되는 재산을 사냥과 경마와 닭싸움에 다 써 버렸고, 우리 안주인이 어떤 목적을 위해 그와 결혼했으나, 그는 이미 오래전부터 그 목적에 부응하기를 그만둔 터라 안주인은 그를 진심으로 미워했다."], ["But as he was a surly kind of fellow, so she contented herself with frequently upbraiding him by disadvantageous comparisons with her first husband, whose praise she had eternally in her mouth; and as she was for the most part mistress of the profit, so she was satisfied to take upon herself the care and government of the family, and, after a long successless struggle, to suffer her husband to be master of himself.", "그러나 그가 워낙 무뚝뚝한 사내였던 까닭에, 안주인은 그를 입에 달고 칭송하던 첫 남편과 불리하게 견주어 가며 자주 면박을 주는 것으로 만족했다. 또 대부분의 수입을 안주인이 쥐고 있었던 만큼, 그녀는 집안의 살림과 관리를 스스로 떠맡는 것으로 흡족해했고, 오랫동안 보람 없는 다툼 끝에 결국 남편이 제 한 몸의 주인 노릇이나 하도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["In the evening, when Jones retired to his room, a small dispute arose between this fond couple concerning him:--“What,” says the wife, “you have been tippling with the gentleman, I see?”--“Yes,” answered the husband, “we have cracked a bottle together, and a very gentlemanlike man he is, and hath a very pretty notion of horse-flesh. Indeed, he is young, and hath not seen much of the world; for I believe he hath been at very few horse-races.”--“Oho! he is one of your order, is he?” replies the landlady: “he must be a gentleman to be sure, if he is a horse-racer. The devil fetch such gentry! I am sure I wish I had never seen any of them. I have reason to love horse-racers truly!”--“That you have,” says the husband; “for I was one, you know.”--“Yes,” answered she, “you are a pure one indeed. As my first husband used to say, I may put all the good I have ever got by you in my eyes, and see never the worse.”--“D--n your first husband!” cries he. “Don't d--n a better man than yourself,” answered the wife: “if he had been alive, you durst not have done it.”--“Then you think,” says he, “I have not so much courage as yourself; for you have d--n'd him often in my hearing.”--“If I did,” says she, “I have repented of it many's the good time and oft. And if he was so good to forgive me a word spoken in haste or so, it doth not become such a one as you to twitter me. He was a husband to me, he was; and if ever I did make use of an ill word or so in a passion, I never called him rascal; I should have told a lie, if I had called him rascal.” Much more she said, but not in his hearing; for having lighted his pipe, he staggered off as fast as he could. We shall therefore transcribe no more of her speech, as it approached still nearer and nearer to a subject too indelicate to find any place in this history.", "저녁이 되어 존스가 자기 방으로 물러가자, 이 살가운 부부 사이에 그를 두고 자그마한 말다툼이 벌어졌다. \"뭐예요,\" 아내가 말했다. \"보아하니 그 신사 양반이랑 한잔 걸치셨구먼?\" \"그래,\" 남편이 대꾸했다. \"한 병 같이 비웠지. 아주 신사다운 사람이고, 말에 대해서도 제법 안목이 있더군. 하기야 아직 젊고 세상 구경을 많이 못 해서 그런지, 경마장에는 거의 가 본 적이 없는 모양이야.\" \"오호! 그럼 당신 같은 부류 사람이로구먼?\" 안주인이 받아쳤다. \"경마꾼이라면 분명 신사일 테지. 그런 양반들은 악마나 데려가라지! 정말이지, 그런 작자들 따위 평생 안 봤더라면 좋았을걸. 내가 경마꾼들을 사랑할 만한 까닭이 참 많기도 하다!\" \"암, 많고말고,\" 남편이 말했다. \"내가 바로 그 경마꾼이었으니까 말이야.\" \"그래요,\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"당신 참 순수한 경마꾼이지. 첫 남편이 늘 하던 말마따나, 내가 당신한테서 얻은 좋은 거라곤 몽땅 눈에 넣어도 끄떡없을 만큼 쥐뿔도 없을 거예요.\" \"당신 첫 남편 같은 건 빌어먹을!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신보다 나은 사람을 두고 빌어먹으라 하지 마세요,\" 아내가 받아쳤다. \"그이가 살아 있었다면 당신은 감히 그런 말 못 했을 테니.\" \"그러니까 당신 생각엔,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 당신만큼도 배짱이 없다 이거지. 당신이야말로 내 앞에서 그 사람을 노상 빌어먹으라 욕했잖아.\" \"내가 그랬다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여러 번 두고두고 뉘우쳤어요. 그이가 마음 너그러워서 홧김에 내뱉은 말 한마디쯤 용서해 줬다 한들, 당신 같은 사람이 그걸 가지고 나를 놀려 댈 자격은 없어요. 그이는 내게 진정한 남편이었어요, 정말로. 그리고 내가 설령 발끈해서 험한 말 한마디를 했더라도, 그이를 두고 망나니라고는 한 번도 안 불렀어요. 그이를 망나니라고 불렀다면 그건 거짓말이었을 테니까.\" 그녀는 훨씬 더 많은 말을 늘어놓았으나, 남편이 듣는 데서는 아니었다. 남편은 담뱃대에 불을 붙이고는 비척비척 부리나케 자리를 떠 버렸기 때문이다. 그러므로 우리는 그녀의 말을 더 옮겨 적지 않겠다. 그것이 이 이야기에 도무지 끼워 넣을 수 없을 만큼 점잖지 못한 주제로 점점 더 가까이 다가갔기 때문이다."]], [["Early in the morning Partridge appeared at the bedside of Jones, ready equipped for the journey, with his knapsack at his back.", "이른 아침, 파트리지가 등에 배낭을 멘 채 여행 채비를 단단히 갖추고 존스의 침대 머리맡에 나타났다. 이 배낭은 그가 손수 만든 것이었다."], ["This was his own workmanship; for besides his other trades, he was no indifferent taylor.", "그는 다른 여러 생업 말고도 솜씨가 제법인 재단사였기 때문이다. 그는 이미 셔츠 넉 장으로 된 자기 옷가지 전부를 그 안에 챙겨 넣었고, 거기에 이제 존스 씨의 셔츠 여덟 장을 더 보탰다."], ["He had already put up his whole stock of linen in it, consisting of four shirts, to which he now added eight for Mr Jones; and then packing up the portmanteau, he was departing with it towards his own house, but was stopt in his way by the landlady, who refused to suffer any removals till after the payment of the reckoning.", "그러고는 여행 가방을 꾸려 그것을 들고 자기 집을 향해 떠나려 했으나, 가는 길에 안주인에게 가로막혔다. 안주인은 셈을 치르기 전에는 어떤 물건도 옮겨 나가게 둘 수 없다고 버텼다."]], [["The landlady was, as we have said, absolute governess in these regions; it was therefore necessary to comply with her rules; so the bill was presently writ out, which amounted to a much larger sum than might have been expected, from the entertainment which Jones had met with.", "앞서 말했듯, 안주인은 이 영역의 절대적인 지배자였다. 그러니 그녀의 규칙을 따를 수밖에 없었다."], ["But here we are obliged to disclose some maxims, which publicans hold to be the grand mysteries of their trade.", "그리하여 계산서가 곧 작성되었는데, 그 액수는 존스가 받은 대접에 견주어 예상보다 훨씬 큰 금액에 이르렀다."], ["The first is, If they have anything good in their house (which indeed very seldom happens) to produce it only to persons who travel with great equipages.", "그러나 여기서 우리는 술집 주인들이 자기네 장사의 비법으로 떠받드는 몇 가지 격언을 밝히지 않을 수 없다. 첫째, 만일 집 안에 무언가 좋은 것이 있거든(실은 그런 일이 아주 드물게 일어나지만), 그것을 큰 마차 행렬을 거느리고 여행하는 손님들에게만 내놓을 것."], ["2dly, To charge the same for the very worst provisions, as if they were the best.", "둘째, 가장 형편없는 음식을 가장 좋은 음식인 양 똑같은 값에 매길 것."], ["And lastly, If any of their guests call but for little, to make them pay a double price for everything they have; so that the amount by the head may be much the same.", "마지막으로, 어느 손님이 조금밖에 시키지 않거든, 그가 먹은 모든 것에 두 배의 값을 물려서, 머릿수로 따진 총액이 엇비슷하도록 맞출 것."]], [["The bill being made and discharged, Jones set forward with Partridge, carrying his knapsack; nor did the landlady condescend to wish him a good journey; for this was, it seems, an inn frequented by people of fashion; and I know not whence it is, but all those who get their livelihood by people of fashion, contract as much insolence to the rest of mankind, as if they really belonged to that rank themselves.", "계산서가 작성되고 셈이 치러지자, 존스는 배낭을 멘 파트리지와 함께 길을 나섰다. 안주인은 그에게 좋은 여행 되라는 인사 한마디조차 베풀려 하지 않았다. 보아하니 이 여관은 상류층 사람들이 즐겨 찾는 곳이었던 모양인데, 어찌 된 영문인지는 모르겠으나, 상류층 사람들 덕에 생계를 잇는 자들은 하나같이 마치 자기네가 정말로 그 계급에 속하기라도 하는 양, 나머지 사람들에게는 그만큼 거만하게 굴기 마련이다."]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["Jones arrives at Gloucester, and goes to the Bell; the character of that house, and of a petty-fogger which he there meets with.", "존스가 글로스터에 도착해 벨 여관으로 들어간다. 그 여관의 됨됨이와, 그곳에서 만난 어느 삼류 변호사의 됨됨이."]], [["Mr Jones and Partridge, or Little Benjamin (which epithet of Little was perhaps given him ironically, he being in reality near six feet high), having left their last quarters in the manner before described, travelled on to Gloucester without meeting any adventure worth relating.", "존스 씨와 파트리지, 다시 말해 꼬마 벤저민(이 ‘꼬마’라는 별명은 어쩌면 비꼬는 뜻으로 붙은 듯하다. 그는 실은 키가 6피트에 가까웠으니 말이다)은 앞서 묘사한 방식으로 마지막 거처를 떠난 뒤, 이렇다 할 이야깃거리가 될 만한 사건 하나 만나지 않고 글로스터까지 걸어왔다."]], [["Being arrived here, they chose for their house of entertainment the sign of the Bell, an excellent house indeed, and which I do most seriously recommend to every reader who shall visit this antient city.", "이곳에 다다른 두 사람은 묵을 곳으로 벨이라는 간판을 단 여관을 골랐다. 참으로 훌륭한 여관이라, 나는 이 유서 깊은 도시를 찾을 모든 독자에게 진심으로 이곳을 권하는 바이다."], ["The master of it is brother to the great preacher Whitefield; but is absolutely untainted with the pernicious principles of Methodism, or of any other heretical sect.", "주인은 저 위대한 설교가 화이트필드의 형제이지만, 감리교의 해로운 교리나 그 밖의 어떤 이단 교파의 물도 전혀 들지 않은 사람이다. 그는 실로 매우 정직하고 소박한 사람이며, 내 생각으로는 교회에서든 나라에서든 어떤 소동도 일으킬 만한 위인이 아니다."], ["He is indeed a very honest plain man, and, in my opinion, not likely to create any disturbance either in church or state.", "그의 아내는 내가 보기에 한때 미모를 꽤나 자랑할 만했고, 지금도 여전히 무척 고운 여인이다. 그녀의 자태와 몸가짐이라면 가장 세련된 모임에서도 빛나는 인물이 될 만했을 것이다."], ["His wife hath, I believe, had much pretension to beauty, and is still a very fine woman.", "그러나 그녀는 이 점과 그 밖의 여러 미덕을 분명 의식하고 있을 텐데도, 자신에게 주어진 그 삶의 자리에 더없이 만족하며 순순히 따르는 듯 보인다. 이 순응은 전적으로 그녀의 신중하고 슬기로운 성품 덕분이다."], ["Her person and deportment might have made a shining figure in the politest assemblies; but though she must be conscious of this and many other perfections, she seems perfectly contented with, and resigned to, that state of life to which she is called; and this resignation is entirely owing to the prudence and wisdom of her temper; for she is at present as free from any Methodistical notions as her husband: I say at present; for she freely confesses that her brother's documents made at first some impression upon her, and that she had put herself to the expense of a long hood, in order to attend the extraordinary emotions of the Spirit; but having found, during an experiment of three weeks, no emotions, she says, worth a farthing, she very wisely laid by her hood, and abandoned the sect.", "지금의 그녀는 남편만큼이나 감리교적 관념에서 자유롭기 때문이다. ‘지금’이라 말하는 까닭은, 그녀가 솔직히 털어놓기를, 처음에는 오라비의 가르침이 자기에게 얼마간 인상을 남겨서, 성령의 특별한 감동을 받아 보려고 긴 두건을 사 입느라 돈까지 들였노라 하기 때문이다."], ["To be concise, she is a very friendly good-natured woman; and so industrious to oblige, that the guests must be of a very morose disposition who are not extremely well satisfied in her house.", "그러나 삼 주에 걸친 시험 끝에, 단돈 한 푼어치도 될 만한 감동이 없음을 깨닫고는, 아주 현명하게도 그 두건을 치워 두고 그 교파를 버렸다고 한다. 요컨대 그녀는 무척 다정하고 마음씨 좋은 여인이며, 손님을 받드는 데 어찌나 부지런한지, 그녀의 여관에서 더없이 흡족하지 못한 손님이 있다면 그건 성미가 몹시 까다로운 사람일 수밖에 없다."]], [["Mrs Whitefield happened to be in the yard when Jones and his attendant marched in.", "존스와 그의 일행이 들어설 때, 마침 화이트필드 부인은 마당에 있었다."], ["Her sagacity soon discovered in the air of our heroe something which distinguished him from the vulgar.", "그녀의 밝은 눈썰미는 우리 주인공의 분위기에서 무언가 범상한 사람들과는 다른 점을 곧 알아챘다. 그리하여 그녀는 하인들에게 즉시 그를 방으로 안내하라 이르고는, 이내 그를 자기와 함께 식사하자고 청했다."], ["She ordered her servants, therefore, immediately to show him into a room, and presently afterwards invited him to dinner with herself; which invitation he very thankfully accepted; for indeed much less agreeable company than that of Mrs Whitefield, and a much worse entertainment than she had provided, would have been welcome after so long fasting and so long a walk.", "존스는 그 청을 무척 고맙게 받아들였다. 실로 그토록 오래 굶고 그토록 오래 걸은 뒤라면, 화이트필드 부인보다 훨씬 덜 유쾌한 벗이라도, 또 그녀가 차린 것보다 훨씬 못한 음식이라도 반가웠을 테니 말이다."]], [["Besides Mr Jones and the good governess of the mansion, there sat down at table an attorney of Salisbury, indeed the very same who had brought the news of Mrs Blifil's death to Mr Allworthy, and whose name, which I think we did not before mention, was Dowling: there was likewise present another person, who stiled himself a lawyer, and who lived somewhere near Linlinch, in Somersetshire. This fellow, I say, stiled himself a lawyer, but was indeed a most vile petty-fogger, without sense or knowledge of any kind; one of those who may be termed train-bearers to the law; a sort of supernumeraries in the profession, who are the hackneys of attorneys, and will ride more miles for half-a-crown than a postboy.", "존스 씨와 이 여관의 어진 안주인 외에도, 식탁에는 솔즈베리에서 온 한 변호사가 앉아 있었다. 실은 블리필 부인의 부고를 올워디 씨에게 전했던 바로 그 사람으로, 이름은 앞서 언급하지 않았던 듯한데, 다울링이라 했다. 또 한 사람이 함께 있었으니, 스스로를 법률가라 일컫는 자로, 서머싯셔의 린린치 부근 어딘가에 사는 인물이었다. 이 작자는 제 입으로 법률가라 했지만, 실은 무슨 분별이나 지식이라곤 한 톨도 없는 더없이 천한 삼류 변호사였다. 법의 들러리라 부를 만한 부류, 곧 그 직업의 잉여 인력 같은 자들로, 변호사들의 삯말 노릇을 하며, 반 크라운만 받으면 우편배달 소년보다도 더 먼 거리를 마다 않고 달리는 그런 위인이었다."]], [["During the time of dinner, the Somersetshire lawyer recollected the face of Jones, which he had seen at Mr Allworthy's; for he had often visited in that gentleman's kitchen.", "식사가 이어지는 동안, 서머싯셔의 그 법률가는 존스의 얼굴을 기억해 냈다. 올워디 씨 댁에서 본 적이 있었던 것이다."], ["He therefore took occasion to enquire after the good family there with that familiarity which would have become an intimate friend or acquaintance of Mr Allworthy; and indeed he did all in his power to insinuate himself to be such, though he had never had the honour of speaking to any person in that family higher than the butler.", "그는 그 신사의 부엌을 곧잘 드나들었기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 올워디 씨와 가까운 벗이나 지인에게나 어울릴 법한 허물없는 태도로, 그 댁의 안부를 묻는 기회를 잡았다."], ["Jones answered all his questions with much civility, though he never remembered to have seen the petty-fogger before; and though he concluded, from the outward appearance and behaviour of the man, that he usurped a freedom with his betters, to which he was by no means intitled.", "실로 그는 자신이 그런 사이인 양 보이려고 온갖 수를 다 썼으나, 그 집안에서 집사보다 높은 사람과는 한마디 나눠 본 영광조차 없는 자였다. 존스는 그 삼류 변호사를 전에 본 기억이 도무지 없었고, 또 그자의 겉모습과 행실로 미루어 제 분수에 넘치는 허물없음을 윗사람에게 함부로 부린다고 결론지었으면서도, 그의 모든 물음에 무척 정중하게 답해 주었다."]], [["As the conversation of fellows of this kind is of all others the most detestable to men of any sense, the cloth was no sooner removed than Mr Jones withdrew, and a little barbarously left poor Mrs Whitefield to do a penance, which I have often heard Mr Timothy Harris, and other publicans of good taste, lament, as the severest lot annexed to their calling, namely, that of being obliged to keep company with their guests.", "이런 부류의 작자들과 나누는 대화란, 조금이라도 분별 있는 사람에게는 무엇보다도 역겨운 법이라, 식탁보가 걷히기가 무섭게 존스 씨는 자리를 떴다. 그리하여 가엾은 화이트필드 부인을 다소 매정하게 남겨 두고 떠난 셈인데, 나는 티머시 해리스 씨를 비롯해 안목 있는 여러 여관 주인들이, 손님과 어울려 앉아 있어야 하는 것이야말로 자기네 생업에 따라붙은 가장 가혹한 형벌이라고 한탄하는 소리를 곧잘 들어 왔다."]], [["Jones had no sooner quitted the room, than the petty-fogger, in a whispering tone, asked Mrs Whitefield, “If she knew who that fine spark was?” She answered, “She had never seen the gentleman before.”--“The gentleman, indeed!” replied the petty-fogger; “a pretty gentleman, truly! Why, he's the bastard of a fellow who was hanged for horse-stealing. He was dropt at Squire Allworthy's door, where one of the servants found him in a box so full of rain-water, that he would certainly have been drowned, had he not been reserved for another fate.”--“Ay, ay, you need not mention it, I protest: we understand what that fate is very well,” cries Dowling, with a most facetious grin.--“Well,” continued the other, “the squire ordered him to be taken in; for he is a timbersome man everybody knows, and was afraid of drawing himself into a scrape; and there the bastard was bred up, and fed, and cloathified all to the world like any gentleman; and there he got one of the servant-maids with child, and persuaded her to swear it to the squire himself; and afterwards he broke the arm of one Mr Thwackum a clergyman, only because he reprimanded him for following whores; and afterwards he snapt a pistol at Mr Blifil behind his back; and once, when Squire Allworthy was sick, he got a drum, and beat it all over the house to prevent him from sleeping; and twenty other pranks he hath played, for all which, about four or five days ago, just before I left the country, the squire stripped him stark naked, and turned him out of doors.”", "존스가 방을 나서기가 무섭게, 삼류 변호사는 속삭이는 목소리로 화이트필드 부인에게 물었다. “저 멀끔한 멋쟁이가 누군지 아시오?” 그녀는 “저 신사분은 전에 본 적이 없다”고 답했다. “신사라니, 원!” 삼류 변호사가 받았다. “참으로 그럴듯한 신사로군! 아니, 저자는 말 도둑질로 교수형 당한 놈의 사생아요. 올워디 나리 댁 문 앞에 버려진 것을, 하인 하나가 어찌나 빗물이 가득 찬 상자 속에서 찾아냈던지, 다른 운명을 위해 남겨진 게 아니었다면 영락없이 빠져 죽었을 거요.” “아, 아, 굳이 말 안 해도 됩니다. 정말이지, 그 운명이 뭔지야 우리도 잘 아니까요.” 다울링이 더없이 익살스러운 히죽임을 지으며 외쳤다. “그래서,” 다른 자가 말을 이었다. “나리께서 그놈을 거두라 명하셨소. 다들 알다시피 나리는 마음 약한 양반이라, 괜한 곤경에 휘말릴까 두려웠던 게지. 그렇게 해서 그 사생아 놈은 어엿한 신사처럼 길러지고, 먹여지고, 옷도 떡 벌어지게 입혀졌다오. 그러더니 거기서 하녀 하나를 임신시키고는, 그게 나리의 아이라고 맹세하라고 꼬드겼지. 그다음엔 색시질을 나무랐다는 이유 하나로 색이라는 목사 양반의 팔을 부러뜨렸고, 그 뒤엔 블리필 씨 등 뒤에서 권총을 들이댔다오. 또 한번은 올워디 나리가 병이 나자, 북을 구해다가 잠 못 들게 하려고 온 집안을 돌며 둥둥 쳐 댔지. 그 밖에도 스무 가지 장난을 쳤으니, 그 모든 일 때문에 나흘이나 닷새 전쯤, 마침 내가 고향을 떠나기 직전에, 나리께서 그놈을 발가벗겨 문밖으로 내쫓아 버리셨소.”"]], [["“And very justly too, I protest,”", "“그것도 지극히 마땅한 일이고말고요, 정말이지.”"], ["cries Dowling; “I would turn my own son out of doors, if he was guilty of half as much.", "다울링이 외쳤다. “내 아들이라도 그 절반만 저질렀어도 문밖으로 내쫓았을 거요."], ["And pray what is the name of this pretty gentleman?”", "한데 그 멀끔한 신사분 이름이 대체 뭐랍니까?”"]], [["“The name o' un?”", "“그놈 이름 말이오?”"], ["answered Petty-fogger; “why, he is called Thomas Jones.”", "삼류 변호사가 답했다. “아, 토머스 존스라고 한다오.”"]], [["“Jones!”", "“존스라고요!” 다울링이 다소 다급하게 받았다."], ["answered Dowling a little eagerly; “what, Mr Jones that lived at Mr Allworthy's?", "“뭐, 올워디 씨 댁에 살던 그 존스 씨 말이오? 우리와 함께 식사한 그 신사가 그 사람이었소?”"], ["was that the gentleman that dined with us?”--“The very same,”", "“바로 그 사람이오.” 다른 자가 말했다."], ["said the other.", "“그 신사 얘기야 자주 들었지요.” 다울링이 외쳤다."], ["“I have heard of the gentleman,”", "“하지만 그에 대한 나쁜 평판은 한 번도 들은 적이 없는데.” “정말이지,”"], ["cries Dowling, “often; but I never heard any ill character of him.”--“And I am sure,”", "화이트필드 부인이 말했다. “이분이 하신 말씀 가운데 절반만 사실이라 해도, 존스 씨는 내 평생 본 가운데 가장 속을 알 수 없는 얼굴을 지닌 사람이네요."], ["says Mrs Whitefield, “if half what this gentleman hath said be true, Mr Jones hath the most deceitful countenance I ever saw; for sure his looks promise something very different; and I must say, for the little I have seen of him, he is as civil a well-bred man as you would wish to converse with.”", "그 인상은 분명 그와는 전혀 다른 무언가를 약속하는 듯하니 말이에요. 또 내가 잠깐 본 바로는, 그는 누구라도 함께 이야기 나누고 싶을 만큼 점잖고 예의 바른 사람이라고 말할 수밖에 없네요.”"]], [["Petty-fogger calling to mind that he had not been sworn, as he usually was, before he gave his evidence, now bound what he had declared with so many oaths and imprecations that the landlady's ears were shocked, and she put a stop to his swearing, by assuring him of her belief.", "삼류 변호사는, 평소 증언하기 전에 늘 하던 것처럼 선서를 하지 않았다는 사실이 문득 떠오르자, 이제 자신이 한 말에 어찌나 많은 맹세와 저주를 갖다 붙여 못을 박았던지, 안주인이 그 소리에 질겁하여, 그를 믿는다고 다짐해 줌으로써 그의 맹세를 멈추게 했다. 그러자 그가 말했다."], ["Upon which he said, “I hope, madam, you imagine I would scorn to tell such things of any man, unless I knew them to be true.", "“부인, 내가 사실이라 알지 못하는 일을 두고 어느 누구에 대해서든 이런 얘기를 떠벌릴 만큼 비열한 사람이라 여기진 않으시겠지요."], ["What interest have I in taking away the reputation of a man who never injured me?", "내게 해코지 한 번 한 적 없는 사람의 명예를 깎아내려서 내가 얻을 이익이 뭐가 있겠소?"], ["I promise you every syllable of what I have said is fact, and the whole country knows it.”", "약속하건대 내가 한 말은 한 음절도 빠짐없이 사실이며, 온 고을이 다 아는 일이오.”"]], [["As Mrs Whitefield had no reason to suspect that the petty-fogger had any motive or temptation to abuse Jones, the reader cannot blame her for believing what he so confidently affirmed with many oaths.", "화이트필드 부인으로서는 그 삼류 변호사가 존스를 헐뜯을 만한 동기나 유혹을 가졌으리라 의심할 까닭이 없었으니, 독자도 그가 그토록 자신만만하게 숱한 맹세까지 곁들여 단언한 말을 그녀가 믿었다 하여 탓할 수는 없으리라."], ["She accordingly gave up her skill in physiognomy, and hence-forwards conceived so ill an opinion of her guest, that she heartily wished him out of her house.", "그리하여 그녀는 자신의 관상 솜씨를 접어 두고, 이때부터 그 손님에 대해 어찌나 나쁜 견해를 품었던지, 그가 자기 여관에서 나가 버리기를 진심으로 바라게 되었다."]], [["This dislike was now farther increased by a report which Mr Whitefield made from the kitchen, where Partridge had informed the company, “That though he carried the knapsack, and contented himself with staying among servants, while Tom Jones (as he called him) was regaling in the parlour, he was not his servant, but only a friend and companion, and as good a gentleman as Mr Jones himself.”", "이 반감은 이제 화이트필드 씨가 부엌에서 전해 온 이야기로 한층 더 커졌다. 거기서 파트리지가 사람들에게 일러바치기를, “비록 자기가 배낭을 메고, 톰 존스(그는 그를 이렇게 불렀다)가 응접실에서 융숭히 대접받는 동안 하인들 틈에 끼어 있는 것으로 만족하고는 있지만, 자기는 그의 하인이 아니라 그저 벗이자 길동무일 뿐이며, 존스 씨 못지않게 어엿한 신사”라 했다는 것이다."]], [["Dowling sat all this while silent, biting his fingers, making faces, grinning, and looking wonderfully arch; at last he opened his lips, and protested that the gentleman looked like another sort of man.", "다울링은 이 내내 말없이 앉아, 손가락을 깨물고, 얼굴을 찡그리고, 히죽거리며, 놀랍도록 능청스러운 낯빛을 지었다. 마침내 그가 입을 열어, 그 신사는 그와는 전혀 다른 부류의 사람처럼 보이더라고 단언했다."], ["He then called for his bill with the utmost haste, declared he must be at Hereford that evening, lamented his great hurry of business, and wished he could divide himself into twenty pieces, in order to be at once in twenty places.", "그러고는 부랴부랴 계산서를 청하고, 그날 저녁 안에 헤리퍼드에 닿아야 한다며, 일이 너무 바빠 죽겠다고 한탄하고는, 스무 군데에 한꺼번에 있을 수 있도록 제 몸을 스무 조각으로 나눌 수 있었으면 좋겠다고 했다."]], [["The petty-fogger now likewise departed, and then Jones desired the favour of Mrs Whitefield's company to drink tea with him; but she refused, and with a manner so different from that with which she had received him at dinner, that it a little surprized him.", "이제 삼류 변호사도 마찬가지로 떠나갔고, 그러자 존스는 화이트필드 부인에게 함께 차나 한잔하자고 청하는 호의를 베풀어 달라 했다. 그러나 그녀는 거절했는데, 그 태도가 식사 자리에서 그를 맞이하던 것과 어찌나 다르던지 그는 다소 놀랐다."], ["And now he soon perceived her behaviour totally changed; for instead of that natural affability which we have before celebrated, she wore a constrained severity on her countenance, which was so disagreeable to Mr Jones, that he resolved, however late, to quit the house that evening.", "이제 그는 그녀의 행동이 완전히 달라졌음을 곧 알아챘다. 앞서 우리가 기렸던 그 천성적인 상냥함 대신, 그녀는 얼굴에 억지스러운 엄격함을 띠고 있었는데, 그것이 존스 씨에게는 어찌나 거북하던지, 그는 아무리 늦더라도 그날 저녁 안에 이 여관을 떠나기로 마음먹었다."]], [["He did indeed account somewhat unfairly for this sudden change; for besides some hard and unjust surmises concerning female fickleness and mutability, he began to suspect that he owed this want of civility to his want of horses; a sort of animals which, as they dirty no sheets, are thought in inns to pay better for their beds than their riders, and are therefore considered as the more desirable company; but Mrs Whitefield, to do her justice, had a much more liberal way of thinking. She was perfectly well-bred, and could be very civil to a gentleman, though he walked on foot. In reality, she looked on our heroe as a sorry scoundrel, and therefore treated him as such, for which not even Jones himself, had he known as much as the reader, could have blamed her; nay, on the contrary, he must have approved her conduct, and have esteemed her the more for the disrespect shown towards himself. This is indeed a most aggravating circumstance, which attends depriving men unjustly of their reputation; for a man who is conscious of having an ill character, cannot justly be angry with those who neglect and slight him; but ought rather to despise such as affect his conversation, unless where a perfect intimacy must have convinced them that their friend's character hath been falsely and injuriously aspersed.", "사실 그는 이 갑작스러운 변화를 다소 부당하게 해석했다. 여자의 변덕스러움과 변하기 쉬운 마음에 대한 가혹하고 부당한 억측은 차치하고서라도, 그는 이 무례함이 자기에게 말이 없는 탓이라 의심하기 시작했다. 말이라는 짐승은 침대보를 더럽히지 않기에, 여관에서는 그 타는 사람보다 잠자리 값을 더 잘 치른다고 여겨지고, 그래서 더 반가운 손님으로 대접받는 법이니 말이다. 그러나 화이트필드 부인은, 공정하게 말하자면, 훨씬 더 너그러운 사고방식을 지닌 여인이었다. 그녀는 더없이 예의 바른 사람이라, 비록 걸어서 온 신사라 해도 무척 정중하게 대할 줄 알았다. 실은 그녀가 우리 주인공을 형편없는 악당으로 보았기에 그렇게 대했던 것이며, 만일 존스 자신도 독자만큼 사정을 알았더라면 그조차 그녀를 탓할 수 없었으리라. 아니, 오히려 그는 그녀의 처신을 옳다 여기고, 자기에게 보인 그 무례함 때문에 그녀를 더욱 높이 샀을 것이다. 이것이야말로 사람의 명예를 부당하게 앗아 가는 일에 따라붙는 가장 통탄할 사정이다. 자신이 나쁜 평판을 지녔음을 의식하는 사람은, 자기를 소홀히 하고 업신여기는 이들에게 정당하게 화를 낼 수 없으며, 도리어 자기와 어울리려 드는 이들을 경멸해 마땅하다. 다만 더없이 깊은 친분이 있어 그 벗의 평판이 거짓되고 해롭게 더럽혀졌음을 그들에게 확신시킨 경우라면 모를 일이다."]], [["This was not, however, the case of Jones; for as he was a perfect stranger to the truth, so he was with good reason offended at the treatment he received.", "그러나 이는 존스의 경우는 아니었다. 그는 진실을 전혀 모르는 처지였으니, 자기가 받은 대접에 화를 낸 것도 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었던 것이다."], ["He therefore paid his reckoning and departed, highly against the will of Mr Partridge, who having remonstrated much against it to no purpose, at last condescended to take up his knapsack and to attend his friend.", "그리하여 그는 셈을 치르고 떠났다. 파트리지 씨의 뜻에는 몹시 거슬리는 일이었는데, 그는 이에 대해 한참을 항변했으나 아무 소용이 없자, 마침내 마지못해 제 배낭을 둘러메고 벗을 따라나섰다."]], [["# Chapter ix.", "# Chapter ix."]], [["Containing several dialogues between Jones and Partridge, concerning love, cold, hunger, and other matters; with the lucky and narrow escape of Partridge, as he was on the very brink of making a fatal discovery to his friend.", "사랑과 추위와 굶주림을 비롯한 여러 문제에 관해 존스와 파트리지가 나눈 몇 차례의 대화를 담았으며, 아울러 파트리지가 자신의 벗에게 치명적인 비밀을 폭로하기 직전까지 갔다가 운 좋게 가까스로 빠져나온 이야기를 담았다."]], [["The shadows began now to descend larger from the high mountains; the feathered creation had betaken themselves to their rest.", "이제 높은 산들로부터 그림자가 점점 길게 내려오기 시작했고, 깃털 달린 무리들은 저마다 보금자리로 돌아갔다."], ["Now the highest order of mortals were sitting down to their dinners, and the lowest order to their suppers.", "바야흐로 가장 높은 신분의 인간들은 만찬 자리에 앉고, 가장 낮은 신분의 인간들은 저녁 끼니 앞에 앉을 무렵이었다. 한마디로, 존스 씨가 글로스터에 작별을 고하던 바로 그때 시계가 다섯 시를 쳤다."], ["In a word, the clock struck five just as Mr Jones took his leave of Gloucester; an hour at which (as it was now mid-winter) the dirty fingers of Night would have drawn her sable curtain over the universe, had not the moon forbid her, who now, with a face as broad and as red as those of some jolly mortals, who, like her, turn night into day, began to rise from her bed, where she had slumbered away the day, in order to sit up all night.", "한겨울인 지금이라면 밤의 더러운 손가락이 그 검은 장막을 온 세상 위로 드리웠을 시각이었으나, 달이 그것을 막았다. 달은 마치 자기처럼 밤을 낮으로 바꾸며 사는 어느 유쾌한 인간들의 얼굴마냥 넓고 붉은 얼굴을 하고서, 하루 종일 잠들어 빈둥거리던 잠자리에서 이제 일어나, 밤새 깨어 있으려고 몸을 일으키기 시작했다."], ["Jones had not travelled far before he paid his compliments to that beautiful planet, and, turning to his companion, asked him if he had ever beheld so delicious an evening?", "존스는 멀리 가기도 전에 그 아름다운 별에게 경의를 표했고, 동행을 돌아보며 이토록 감미로운 저녁을 본 적이 있느냐고 물었다. 파트리지가 그 물음에 선뜻 대답을 내놓지 못하자, 그는 달의 아름다움에 대해 한마디 덧붙이고는, 천상의 빛나는 것들을 묘사하는 데 있어 다른 어떤 시인보다 단연 뛰어났던 밀턴의 시구 몇 구절을 읊었다."], ["Partridge making no ready answer to his question, he proceeded to comment on the beauty of the moon, and repeated some passages from Milton, who hath certainly excelled all other poets in his description of the heavenly luminaries.", "그러고는 파트리지에게 《스펙테이터》에 실린 두 연인의 이야기를 들려주었다. 두 사람은 서로 멀리 떨어져 있을 때, 정해진 어느 시각에 함께 달을 바라보기로 약속함으로써 스스로를 달래기로 했다는 것이다."], ["He then told Partridge the story from the Spectator, of two lovers who had agreed to entertain themselves when they were at a great distance from each other, by repairing, at a certain fixed hour, to look at the moon; thus pleasing themselves with the thought that they were both employed in contemplating the same object at the same time.", "같은 시각에 둘 다 같은 대상을 바라보고 있다는 생각으로 서로 위안을 삼은 셈이었다. \"그 연인들은,\""], ["“Those lovers,”", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["added he, “must have had souls truly capable of feeling all the tenderness of the sublimest of all human passions.”--“Very probably,”", "\"인간의 모든 정념 가운데 가장 숭고한 그 정념의 온갖 애틋함을 진정으로 느낄 수 있는 영혼을 지녔던 게 분명해.\"—\"아마 그렇겠지요.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["cries Partridge: “but I envy them more, if they had bodies incapable of feeling cold; for I am almost frozen to death, and am very much afraid I shall lose a piece of my nose before we get to another house of entertainment.", "\"하지만 저는 그들이 추위를 느끼지 못하는 몸을 가졌다면 더 부럽겠습니다. 저는 지금 거의 얼어 죽을 지경이라, 다음 여관에 닿기도 전에 코끝이 한 조각 떨어져 나가지 않을까 몹시 두렵거든요."], ["Nay, truly, we may well expect some judgment should happen to us for our folly in running away so by night from one of the most excellent inns I ever set my foot into.", "아니, 사실, 제가 평생 발을 들여본 곳 가운데 가장 훌륭한 여관 하나를 밤에 이렇게 박차고 나온 우리의 어리석음에 대해서는 어떤 천벌이 내려도 마땅합니다."], ["I am sure I never saw more good things in my life, and the greatest lord in the land cannot live better in his own house than he may there.", "정말이지 저는 그렇게 좋은 것들을 평생 본 적이 없습니다. 이 나라에서 제일가는 영주라 해도 제 집에서 그곳보다 더 잘 지내지는 못할 겁니다."], ["And to forsake such a house, and go a rambling about the country, the Lord knows whither, per devia rura viarum, I say nothing for my part; but some people might not have charity enough to conclude we were in our sober senses.”--“Fie upon it, Mr Partridge!”", "그런데 그런 곳을 버려두고, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채, 들길을 헤매며 시골을 떠돌다니, 저야 입을 다물겠습니다만, 어떤 이들은 우리가 제정신이었다고 너그러이 봐줄 만큼 인정이 넉넉하지는 않을 겁니다.\"—\"저런, 파트리지 씨!\" 존스가 말했다."], ["says Jones, “have a better heart; consider you are going to face an enemy; and are you afraid of facing a little cold?", "\"기운을 좀 내요. 당신은 적과 맞서러 가는 길임을 생각해요."], ["I wish, indeed, we had a guide to advise which of these roads we should take.”--“May I be so bold,”", "그런 당신이 고작 약간의 추위와 맞서기를 두려워한단 말이오? 정말이지 어느 길로 가야 할지 일러줄 안내인이라도 하나 있었으면 좋겠소.\"—\"제가 외람되이,\""], ["says Partridge, “to offer my advice?", "파트리지가 말했다. \"제 의견을 좀 아뢰어도 될까요?"], ["Interdum stultus opportuna loquitur”--“Why, which of them,”", "때로는 어리석은 자도 적절한 말을 하는 법이지요.\"—\"그래, 그 길들 중에서,\""], ["cries Jones, “would you recommend?”--“Truly neither of them,”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"어느 쪽을 권하겠소?\"—\"실은 어느 쪽도 권하지 않습니다.\""], ["answered Partridge.", "파트리지가 대답했다."], ["“The only road we can be certain of finding, is the road we came.", "\"우리가 확실히 찾을 수 있는 유일한 길은 우리가 왔던 길입니다. 부지런히 한 시간만 걸으면 글로스터로 되돌아갈 수 있지요."], ["A good hearty pace will bring us back to Gloucester in an hour; but if we go forward, the Lord Harry knows when we shall arrive at any place; for I see at least fifty miles before me, and no house in all the way.”--“You see, indeed, a very fair prospect,”", "하지만 앞으로 나아가면, 우리가 어딘가에 닿기는 할지 하느님만 아실 일입니다. 제 앞으로는 적어도 오십 마일은 펼쳐져 있는데, 그 길 어디에도 집 한 채 보이지 않으니까요.\"—\"과연 매우 멋진 경치가 보이는군.\""], ["says Jones, “which receives great additional beauty from the extreme lustre of the moon.", "존스가 말했다. \"더없이 환한 달빛 덕분에 한층 더 아름다워 보이고 말이오."], ["However, I will keep the left-hand track, as that seems to lead directly to those hills, which we were informed lie not far from Worcester.", "그래도 나는 왼쪽 길을 택하겠소. 그쪽이 우스터에서 멀지 않다고 들은 저 언덕들로 곧장 이어지는 듯하니 말이오."], ["And here, if you are inclined to quit me, you may, and return back again; but for my part, I am resolved to go forward.”", "자, 나를 떠나고 싶거든 그래도 좋소, 돌아가도 되오. 하지만 나로 말하면, 앞으로 나아가기로 굳게 마음먹었소.\""]], [["“It is unkind in you, sir,”", "\"나리, 제가 그런 마음을 품었으리라 의심하시다니 인정머리 없으십니다.\""], ["says Partridge, “to suspect me of any such intention.", "파트리지가 말했다."], ["What I have advised hath been as much on your account as on my own: but since you are determined to go on, I am as much determined to follow.", "\"제가 드린 의견은 제 자신을 위한 것만큼이나 나리를 위한 것이었습니다. 하지만 나리께서 나아가기로 결심하셨으니, 저도 따르기로 그만큼 굳게 결심했습니다."], ["I prae sequar te.”", "먼저 가십시오, 제가 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["They now travelled some miles without speaking to each other, during which suspense of discourse Jones often sighed, and Benjamin groaned as bitterly, though from a very different reason.", "이제 두 사람은 서로 한마디도 나누지 않은 채 몇 마일을 걸었는데, 그렇게 말이 끊긴 사이 존스는 자주 한숨을 내쉬었고, 벤저민 역시 못지않게 쓰라리게 끙끙거렸으나 그 까닭은 사뭇 달랐다. 마침내 존스가 우뚝 멈춰 서더니 돌아서며 외쳤다."], ["At length Jones made a full stop, and turning about, cries, “Who knows, Partridge, but the loveliest creature in the universe may have her eyes now fixed on that very moon which I behold at this instant?”", "\"누가 알겠나, 파트리지. 이 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 사람이 지금 바로 내가 이 순간 바라보는 저 달에 눈을 두고 있을지도 모르지 않나?\""], ["“Very likely, sir,”", "\"그럴 법도 합니다, 나리.\""], ["answered Partridge; “and if my eyes were fixed on a good surloin of roast beef, the devil might take the moon and her horns into the bargain.”", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"그리고 제 눈이 잘 구운 소 등심 한 덩이에 가 있다면, 달이고 그 뿔이고 죄다 악마가 가져가 버려도 좋겠습니다.\""], ["“Did ever Tramontane make such an answer?”", "\"산 너머 촌사람이라 한들 그런 대꾸를 한 자가 있었겠나?\""], ["cries Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["“Prithee, Partridge, wast thou ever susceptible of love in thy life, or hath time worn away all the traces of it from thy memory?”", "\"이보게 파트리지, 자네는 평생 사랑이란 걸 느껴본 적이 있기는 한가, 아니면 세월이 자네 기억에서 그 흔적을 죄다 닳아 없애버린 건가?\" \"아아, 한심한 노릇이지요!\""], ["“Alack-a-day!”", "파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["cries Partridge, “well would it have been for me if I had never known what love was.", "\"차라리 사랑이 뭔지 영영 몰랐더라면 제게 좋았을 겁니다."], ["Infandum regina jubes renovare dolorem.", "여왕이시여, 말로 못할 슬픔을 다시 들추라 명하시는군요."], ["I am sure I have tasted all the tenderness, and sublimities, and bitternesses of the passion.”", "저야말로 그 정념의 온갖 애틋함과 숭고함과 쓰디씀을 죄다 맛본 사람이라고 확신합니다.\""], ["“Was your mistress unkind, then?”", "\"그렇다면 자네 연인이 매정했단 말인가?\""], ["says Jones.", "존스가 말했다."], ["“Very unkind, indeed, sir,”", "\"정말이지 무척이나 매정했지요, 나리.\" 파트리지가 대답했다."], ["answered Partridge; “for she married me, and made one of the most confounded wives in the world.", "\"저와 결혼해서 세상에서 가장 지긋지긋한 아내 노릇을 했으니까요. 그래도, 하느님 감사하게도, 그 사람은 갔습니다."], ["However, heaven be praised, she's gone; and if I believed she was in the moon, according to a book I once read, which teaches that to be the receptacle of departed spirits, I would never look at it for fear of seeing her; but I wish, sir, that the moon was a looking-glass for your sake, and that Miss Sophia Western was now placed before it.”", "그리고 만약 그 사람이 달 속에 있다고 믿었다면, 언젠가 읽은 어느 책이 달을 떠난 영혼들이 머무는 곳이라 가르치더이다만, 저는 그 사람을 볼까 두려워 결코 달을 쳐다보지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 나리, 나리를 위해서라면 달이 거울이 되어, 소피아 웨스턴 아가씨가 지금 그 앞에 놓여 있으면 좋겠습니다.\""], ["“My dear Partridge,”", "\"이보게 파트리지,\""], ["cries Jones, “what a thought was there!", "존스가 외쳤다. \"거 참 기막힌 생각이로군!"], ["A thought which I am certain could never have entered into any mind but that of a lover.", "사랑하는 자의 마음이 아니고서는 결코 떠오를 수 없는 생각이야."], ["O Partridge!", "오 파트리지!"], ["could I hope once again to see that face; but, alas!", "그 얼굴을 다시 한 번 볼 수만 있다면. 하지만, 아아!"], ["all those golden dreams are vanished for ever, and my only refuge from future misery is to forget the object of all my former happiness.”", "그 황금빛 꿈들은 모두 영영 사라져버렸고, 앞으로의 비참함에서 내가 피할 유일한 길이란 지난날의 모든 행복의 대상을 잊는 것뿐이라네.\""], ["“And do you really despair of ever seeing Miss Western again?”", "\"정말로 웨스턴 아가씨를 다시 볼 수 없으리라 절망하시는 겁니까?\""], ["answered Partridge; “if you will follow my advice I will engage you shall not only see her but have her in your arms.”", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"제 충고를 따르신다면, 나리께서 그분을 다시 보실 뿐 아니라 품에 안으시도록 제가 책임지겠습니다.\""], ["“Ha!", "\"하!"], ["do not awaken a thought of that nature,”", "그런 종류의 생각일랑 일깨우지 말게나.\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones: “I have struggled sufficiently to conquer all such wishes already.”", "\"나는 이미 그런 온갖 바람을 누르려고 충분히 애써왔다네.\" \"원, 참,\""], ["“Nay,”", "파트리지가 대답했다."], ["answered Partridge, “if you do not wish to have your mistress in your arms you are a most extraordinary lover indeed.”", "\"연인을 품에 안기를 바라지 않으신다면, 나리야말로 참으로 별난 연인이십니다.\" \"알았네, 알았어.\""], ["“Well, well,”", "존스가 말했다. \"이 이야기는 그만두세."], ["says Jones, “let us avoid this subject; but pray what is your advice?”", "그런데 자네 충고가 무엇인지나 말해보게.\" \"그렇다면 군대식으로 말씀드리지요.\""], ["“To give it you in the military phrase, then,”", "파트리지가 말했다. \"우리도 병사이니, '뒤로 돌아.'"], ["says Partridge, “as we are soldiers, `To the right about.'", "왔던 길로 돌아갑시다. 늦더라도 오늘 밤 안에 글로스터에 닿을 수 있을 겁니다."], ["Let us return the way we came; we may yet reach Gloucester to-night, though late; whereas, if we proceed, we are likely, for aught I see, to ramble about for ever without coming either to house or home.”", "반면에 계속 나아가면, 제가 보기엔 집도 절도 못 만난 채 영영 떠돌게 될 듯합니다.\" \"내 결심은 나아가는 것이라고 이미 말했네.\""], ["“I have already told you my resolution is to go on,”", "존스가 대답했다. \"다만 자네는 돌아갔으면 하네."], ["answered Jones; “but I would have you go back.", "여기까지 함께해준 것은 고마우이. 부디 내 작은 감사의 표시로 이 일 기니를 받아주게."], ["I am obliged to you for your company hither; and I beg you to accept a guinea as a small instance of my gratitude.", "아니, 자네를 더 멀리 끌고 가는 건 내가 잔인한 짓이 될 거야. 솔직히 털어놓자면, 나의 가장 큰 목적이자 바람은 임금과 나라를 위해 싸우다 영광스럽게 죽는 것이거든.\""], ["Nay, it would be cruel in me to suffer you to go any farther; for, to deal plainly with you, my chief end and desire is a glorious death in the service of my king and country.”", "\"돈에 관해서라면,\" 파트리지가 대꾸했다."], ["“As for your money,”", "\"나리, 부디 도로 넣어두십시오. 이번엔 한 푼도 받지 않겠습니다."], ["replied Partridge, “I beg, sir, you will put it up; I will receive none of you at this time; for at present I am, I believe, the richer man of the two.", "지금으로선 둘 중에 제가 더 가진 사람일 테니까요. 그리고 나리의 결심이 나아가는 것이듯, 제 결심은 나리께서 가시면 따르는 것입니다."], ["And as your resolution is to go on, so mine is to follow you if you do.", "아니, 이제는 나리를 보살피기 위해 제가 곁에 있는 것이 반드시 필요해 보입니다. 나리의 작정이 그토록 무모하니 말입니다."], ["Nay, now my presence appears absolutely necessary to take care of you, since your intentions are so desperate; for I promise you my views are much more prudent; as you are resolved to fall in battle if you can, so I am resolved as firmly to come to no hurt if I can help it.", "분명히 말씀드리지만 제 속셈은 훨씬 더 신중하지요. 나리께서 할 수만 있다면 싸우다 쓰러지기로 작정하셨듯, 저는 할 수만 있다면 조금도 다치지 않기로 그만큼 굳게 작정했으니까요."], ["And, indeed, I have the comfort to think there will be but little danger; for a popish priest told me the other day the business would soon be over, and he believed without a battle.”", "그리고 사실, 위험은 거의 없으리라 생각하니 마음이 놓입니다. 며칠 전 어느 가톨릭 신부가 제게 말하기를, 이 일은 곧 끝날 거고, 자기 생각엔 싸움도 없이 끝나리라더군요.\""], ["“A popish priest!”", "\"가톨릭 신부라!\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “I have heard is not always to be believed when he speaks in behalf of his religion.”", "\"내가 듣기로 그런 자들은 제 종교를 위해 말할 때 늘 믿을 만한 건 아니라던데.\" \"예, 하지만 그 점으로 말하면,\""], ["“Yes, but so far,”", "상대가 대답했다."], ["answered the other, “from speaking in behalf of his religion, he assured me the Catholicks did not expect to be any gainers by the change; for that Prince Charles was as good a Protestant as any in England; and that nothing but regard to right made him and the rest of the popish party to be Jacobites.”--“I believe him to be as much a Protestant as I believe he hath any right,”", "\"그 신부는 자기 종교를 위해 말하기는커녕, 가톨릭 신도들은 이번 변화로 무얼 얻으리라 기대하지도 않는다고 확언했습니다. 찰스 왕자가 잉글랜드의 어느 누구 못지않게 훌륭한 신교도이고, 그분과 가톨릭 일파가 자코바이트가 된 것은 오로지 정당한 권리를 존중해서라는 겁니다.\"—\"내가 그자에게 무슨 권리가 있다고 믿는 만큼만 그가 신교도라고 믿겠네.\""], ["says Jones; “and I make no doubt of our success, but not without a battle.", "존스가 말했다. \"우리가 이기리라는 것은 의심치 않지만, 싸움 없이는 아니야."], ["So that I am not so sanguine as your friend the popish priest.”", "그러니 나는 자네 친구인 그 가톨릭 신부만큼 낙관하지는 않네.\" \"아니, 정말입니다, 나리.\""], ["“Nay, to be sure, sir,”", "파트리지가 대답했다."], ["answered Partridge, “all the prophecies I have ever read speak of a great deal of blood to be spilt in the quarrel, and the miller with three thumbs, who is now alive, is to hold the horses of three kings, up to his knees in blood.", "\"제가 읽어본 예언이란 예언은 죄다 이 싸움에서 엄청난 피가 흐를 거라고 말하더군요. 지금 살아 있는 엄지가 셋 달린 방앗간 주인이 무릎까지 피에 잠긴 채 세 임금의 말고삐를 잡게 된다지요."], ["Lord, have mercy upon us all, and send better times!”", "주여, 우리 모두를 불쌍히 여기시고, 더 좋은 시절을 보내주소서!\""], ["“With what stuff and nonsense hast thou filled thy head!”", "\"어떻게 그런 헛소리며 말도 안 되는 것들로 머릿속을 채웠나!\""], ["answered Jones: “this too, I suppose, comes from the popish priest.", "존스가 대답했다. \"이것도 그 가톨릭 신부한테서 나온 거겠지."], ["Monsters and prodigies are the proper arguments to support monstrous and absurd doctrines.", "괴물이며 흉조 따위는 괴물 같고 터무니없는 교설을 떠받치기에 딱 맞는 근거니까."], ["The cause of King George is the cause of liberty and true religion.", "조지 왕의 대의는 자유와 참된 종교의 대의일세."], ["In other words, it is the cause of common sense, my boy, and I warrant you will succeed, though Briarius himself was to rise again with his hundred thumbs, and to turn miller.”", "다시 말해, 이 사람아, 그건 상식의 대의야. 브리아레우스 본인이 엄지 백 개를 달고 되살아나 방앗간 주인 노릇을 한다 해도, 우리가 이길 거라고 내가 장담하지.\""], ["Partridge made no reply to this.", "파트리지는 여기에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["He was, indeed, cast into the utmost confusion by this declaration of Jones.", "실은 존스의 이 선언에 그는 극도의 혼란에 빠지고 말았다."], ["For, to inform the reader of a secret, which he had no proper opportunity of revealing before, Partridge was in truth a Jacobite, and had concluded that Jones was of the same party, and was now proceeding to join the rebels.", "독자에게 한 가지 비밀을 알려드리자면—여태 적절히 밝힐 기회가 없었던 비밀인데—파트리지는 사실 자코바이트였고, 존스도 같은 일파이며 지금 반군에 합류하러 가는 길이라고 결론지었던 것이다."], ["An opinion which was not without foundation.", "그런 생각에는 근거가 없지도 않았다."], ["For the tall, long-sided dame, mentioned by Hudibras--that many-eyed, many-tongued, many-mouthed, many-eared monster of Virgil, had related the story of the quarrel between Jones and the officer, with the usual regard to truth.", "허디브라스가 언급한 그 키 크고 옆구리 긴 여인—눈도 많고, 혀도 많고, 입도 많고, 귀도 많은 베르길리우스의 그 괴물이, 존스와 그 장교 사이에 벌어진 다툼 이야기를, 진실에 대한 여느 때만큼의 존중을 담아 퍼뜨렸던 것이다. 그 여인은 실로 소피아라는 이름을 왕위 참칭자의 이름으로 바꿔치기했고, 존스가 얻어맞은 것은 그자의 건강을 위해 축배를 든 탓이라고 전했다."], ["She had, indeed, changed the name of Sophia into that of the Pretender, and had reported, that drinking his health was the cause for which Jones was knocked down.", "이 이야기를 파트리지는 들었고, 더없이 굳게 믿었다. 그러니 그가 거기서 존스에 대해 앞서 말한 그런 생각을 품게 된 것도 이상할 게 없으며, 자기 착각을 깨닫기 전까지 하마터면 그 생각을 존스에게 들킬 뻔했던 것이다."], ["This Partridge had heard, and most firmly believed.", "이에 대해서도 독자는 덜 놀라게 될 것이다. 존스가 자신의 결심을 파트리지 씨에게 처음 알릴 때 썼던 그 모호한 표현을 떠올려본다면 말이다."], ["'Tis no wonder, therefore, that he had thence entertained the above-mentioned opinion of Jones; and which he had almost discovered to him before he found out his own mistake.", "사실, 그 말이 덜 애매했더라도 파트리지는 자기 식대로 해석했을 법하다. 그는 온 나라가 마음속으로는 같은 성향이라고 굳게 믿고 있었으니까."], ["And at this the reader will be the less inclined to wonder, if he pleases to recollect the doubtful phrase in which Jones first communicated his resolution to Mr Partridge; and, indeed, had the words been less ambiguous, Partridge might very well have construed them as he did; being persuaded as he was that the whole nation were of the same inclination in their hearts; nor did it stagger him that Jones had travelled in the company of soldiers; for he had the same opinion of the army which he had of the rest of the people.", "존스가 병사들과 함께 여행했다는 사실도 그의 믿음을 흔들지 못했다. 그는 군대에 대해서도 나머지 백성에 대해 가진 것과 똑같은 생각을 품고 있었던 것이다."]], [["But however well affected he might be to James or Charles, he was still much more attached to Little Benjamin than to either; for which reason he no sooner discovered the principles of his fellow-traveller than he thought proper to conceal and outwardly give up his own to the man on whom he depended for the making his fortune, since he by no means believed the affairs of Jones to be so desperate as they really were with Mr Allworthy; for as he had kept a constant correspondence with some of his neighbours since he left that country, he had heard much, indeed more than was true, of the great affection Mr Allworthy bore this young man, who, as Partridge had been instructed, was to be that gentleman's heir, and whom, as we have said, he did not in the least doubt to be his son.", "그러나 그가 제임스나 찰스에게 아무리 호의를 품고 있었다 한들, 그는 그 둘 중 누구보다 어린 벤저민에게 훨씬 더 애착이 있었다. 그런 까닭에 그는 동행자의 신조를 알아채자마자, 자기 출세를 의탁한 그 사람 앞에서 제 신조를 감추고 겉으로는 내려놓는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼다. 그도 그럴 것이 그는 존스의 처지가 올워디 씨와의 관계에서 실제만큼 절망적이라고는 결코 믿지 않았던 것이다. 그 고장을 떠난 뒤로도 몇몇 이웃과 꾸준히 편지를 주고받아 온 터라, 그는 올워디 씨가 이 젊은이에게 품은 깊은 애정에 관해 많은 이야기를, 실로 사실보다 더 부풀려진 이야기를 들어왔다. 파트리지가 전해 들은 바로는 그 젊은이가 그 신사의 상속자가 될 사람이었고, 또 앞서 말했듯 파트리지는 그가 자기 아들임을 추호도 의심하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["He imagined therefore that whatever quarrel was between them, it would be certainly made up at the return of Mr Jones; an event from which he promised great advantages, if he could take this opportunity of ingratiating himself with that young gentleman; and if he could by any means be instrumental in procuring his return, he doubted not, as we have before said, but it would as highly advance him in the favour of Mr Allworthy.", "그러므로 그는 두 사람 사이에 어떤 다툼이 있었든, 존스 씨가 돌아가면 반드시 화해가 이루어지리라 생각했다. 그리고 그 일에서 큰 이득을 기대했으니, 이번 기회에 그 젊은 신사의 환심을 살 수 있다면, 그리고 어떻게든 그가 돌아가게 하는 데 한몫할 수 있다면, 앞서 말한 대로 그것이 올워디 씨의 호의 속에서 자신을 그만큼 높여주리라 의심치 않았던 것이다."]], [["We have already observed, that he was a very good-natured fellow, and he hath himself declared the violent attachment he had to the person and character of Jones; but possibly the views which I have just before mentioned, might likewise have some little share in prompting him to undertake this expedition, at least in urging him to continue it, after he had discovered that his master and himself, like some prudent fathers and sons, though they travelled together in great friendship, had embraced opposite parties.", "우리는 이미 그가 매우 마음씨 고운 사람임을 살펴보았고, 그 자신도 존스의 인품과 사람됨에 대한 격렬한 애착을 털어놓은 바 있다. 하지만 어쩌면 내가 방금 언급한 그 속셈들 역시, 그로 하여금 이 원정에 나서도록—적어도 주인과 자신이, 신중한 어떤 아버지와 아들들처럼, 더없이 다정하게 함께 길을 가면서도 서로 반대 당파를 끌어안고 있음을 알아챈 뒤에도 그것을 계속하도록 다그치는 데—작으나마 한몫했을 것이다."], ["I am led into this conjecture, by having remarked, that though love, friendship, esteem, and such like, have very powerful operations in the human mind; interest, however, is an ingredient seldom omitted by wise men, when they would work others to their own purposes.", "내가 이런 추측에 이르게 된 것은, 사랑이며 우정이며 존경이며 그 비슷한 것들이 인간의 마음속에서 매우 강력하게 작용하기는 하나, 지혜로운 자들이 남을 제 뜻대로 부리려 할 때 좀처럼 빠뜨리지 않는 재료가 바로 이익이라는 점을 눈여겨보았기 때문이다."], ["This is indeed a most excellent medicine, and, like Ward's pill, flies at once to the particular part of the body on which you desire to operate, whether it be the tongue, the hand, or any other member, where it scarce ever fails of immediately producing the desired effect.", "이것이야말로 실로 더없이 탁월한 약이어서, 워드의 환약처럼, 혀든 손이든 그 밖의 어느 부위든 당신이 작용시키고 싶은 바로 그 신체 부위로 단번에 날아가, 거기서 원하는 효과를 즉각 내는 데 좀처럼 실패하는 법이 없다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["In which our travellers meet with a very extraordinary adventure.", "우리의 여행자들이 매우 기이한 모험을 만나는 이야기."]], [["Just as Jones and his friend came to the end of their dialogue in the preceding chapter, they arrived at the bottom of a very steep hill. Here Jones stopt short, and directing his eyes upwards, stood for a while silent. At length he called to his companion, and said, “Partridge, I wish I was at the top of this hill; it must certainly afford a most charming prospect, especially by this light; for the solemn gloom which the moon casts on all objects, is beyond expression beautiful, especially to an imagination which is desirous of cultivating melancholy ideas.”--“Very probably,” answered Partridge; “but if the top of the hill be properest to produce melancholy thoughts, I suppose the bottom is the likeliest to produce merry ones, and these I take to be much the better of the two. I protest you have made my blood run cold with the very mentioning the top of that mountain; which seems to me to be one of the highest in the world. No, no, if we look for anything, let it be for a place under ground, to screen ourselves from the frost.”--“Do so,” said Jones; “let it be but within hearing of this place, and I will hallow to you at my return back.”--“Surely, sir, you are not mad,” said Partridge.--“Indeed, I am,” answered Jones, “if ascending this hill be madness; but as you complain so much of the cold already, I would have you stay below. I will certainly return to you within an hour.”--“Pardon me, sir,” cries Partridge; “I have determined to follow you wherever you go.” Indeed he was now afraid to stay behind; for though he was coward enough in all respects, yet his chief fear was that of ghosts, with which the present time of night, and the wildness of the place, extremely well suited.", "앞 장에서 존스와 그의 벗이 막 대화를 끝낼 무렵, 두 사람은 몹시 가파른 언덕 기슭에 다다랐다. 여기서 존스는 우뚝 걸음을 멈추고는 눈을 위로 향한 채 한동안 말없이 서 있었다. 마침내 그는 동행을 불러 이렇게 말했다. \"파트리지, 이 언덕 꼭대기에 올라가 보고 싶군. 거기서는 분명 더없이 아름다운 경치가 펼쳐질 거야. 특히 이런 달빛 아래서는 말이지. 달이 모든 사물 위에 드리우는 그 엄숙한 어스름은 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 아름다워. 더구나 우울한 상념을 가꾸고 싶어 하는 상상력에는 더더욱 그렇지.\" \"그럴 수도 있겠지요.\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"하지만 언덕 꼭대기가 우울한 생각을 자아내기에 가장 알맞은 곳이라면, 그 아래쪽은 즐거운 생각을 자아내기에 가장 알맞은 곳일 테고, 둘 중에는 단연 이쪽이 훨씬 낫지 않겠습니까. 솔직히 말씀드리면 그 산꼭대기 이야기를 꺼내는 것만으로도 제 피가 다 식어 버렸습니다. 제 눈에는 세상에서 가장 높은 산 중 하나처럼 보이는걸요. 아니, 아닙니다. 무언가를 찾으시려거든, 차라리 이 추위를 피할 땅 밑 자리나 찾아보시지요.\" \"그렇게 하게.\" 존스가 말했다. \"다만 이곳에서 부르면 들릴 만한 곳에 있게나. 내가 돌아오는 길에 자네를 부를 테니.\" \"이런, 나리, 정신이 나가신 건 아니시지요.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"정말로 그렇다네.\" 존스가 대답했다. \"이 언덕을 오르는 게 미친 짓이라면 말일세. 하지만 자네가 벌써부터 그토록 추위를 호소하니, 아래에 남아 있게나. 한 시간 안에 반드시 자네에게 돌아오겠네.\" \"용서하십시오, 나리.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"저는 나리가 어디를 가시든 따라가기로 마음먹었습니다.\" 사실 그는 이제 뒤에 남는 게 두려웠다. 그는 모든 면에서 충분히 겁쟁이였지만, 그가 가장 두려워하는 것은 유령이었고, 지금 이 밤 시각과 황량한 장소는 그 두려움과 더없이 잘 어울렸기 때문이다."]], [["At this instant Partridge espied a glimmering light through some trees, which seemed very near to them.", "바로 그 순간, 파트리지는 나무들 사이로 가물거리는 불빛 하나를 발견했는데, 그것은 그들에게 아주 가까이 있는 듯했다."], ["He immediately cried out in a rapture, “Oh, sir!", "그는 곧장 환희에 차서 외쳤다. \"오, 나리!"], ["Heaven hath at last heard my prayers, and hath brought us to a house; perhaps it may be an inn.", "마침내 하늘이 제 기도를 들으시고 우리를 어느 집으로 이끄셨습니다. 어쩌면 여관일지도 모르지요."], ["Let me beseech you, sir, if you have any compassion either for me or yourself, do not despise the goodness of Providence, but let us go directly to yon light.", "부디 간청드립니다, 나리, 저든 나리 자신이든 조금이라도 가엾이 여기는 마음이 있으시거든, 하늘의 은혜를 저버리지 마시고 저 불빛 쪽으로 곧장 가십시다."], ["Whether it be a public-house or no, I am sure if they be Christians that dwell there, they will not refuse a little house-room to persons in our miserable condition.”", "그곳이 술집이든 아니든, 거기 사는 이들이 그리스도인이라면 우리처럼 비참한 처지에 놓인 사람들에게 잠시 집 한 귀퉁이쯤 내어 주기를 거절하지는 않을 겁니다.\""], ["Jones at length yielded to the earnest supplications of Partridge, and both together made directly towards the place whence the light issued.", "존스는 마침내 파트리지의 간곡한 애원에 굴복했고, 두 사람은 함께 불빛이 새어 나오는 곳을 향해 곧장 나아갔다."]], [["They soon arrived at the door of this house, or cottage, for it might be called either, without much impropriety.", "그들은 곧 이 집, 아니 오두막이라 해도 좋을, 어느 쪽으로 불러도 크게 어긋나지 않을 그곳의 문 앞에 다다랐다. 여기서 존스는 여러 번 문을 두드렸으나 안에서는 아무런 대답도 없었다."], ["Here Jones knocked several times without receiving any answer from within; at which Partridge, whose head was full of nothing but of ghosts, devils, witches, and such like, began to tremble, crying, “Lord, have mercy upon us!", "그러자 머릿속이 유령이며 악마며 마녀 같은 것들로 가득 차 있던 파트리지가 떨기 시작하며 외쳤다. \"주여, 저희에게 자비를 베푸소서!"], ["surely the people must be all dead.", "분명 사람들이 다 죽은 게 틀림없습니다. 이제는 불빛도 보이지 않는걸요."], ["I can see no light neither now, and yet I am certain I saw a candle burning but a moment before.--Well!", "그런데도 저는 바로 조금 전에 촛불이 타는 걸 분명히 보았단 말입니다. 아이고!"], ["I have heard of such things.”--“What hast thou heard of?”", "저는 이런 일에 대해 들어 본 적이 있습니다.\" \"무엇에 대해 들었다는 건가?\""], ["said Jones.", "존스가 말했다."], ["“The people are either fast asleep, or probably, as this is a lonely place, are afraid to open their door.”", "\"사람들이 깊이 잠들었거나, 아니면 여기가 외진 곳이니 아마도 문을 여는 게 무서운 거겠지.\""], ["He then began to vociferate pretty loudly, and at last an old woman, opening an upper casement, asked, Who they were, and what they wanted?", "그러고는 꽤 큰 소리로 외치기 시작했고, 마침내 한 늙은 여인이 위층 여닫이창을 열고 누구냐고, 무엇을 원하느냐고 물었다."], ["Jones answered, They were travellers who had lost their way, and having seen a light in the window, had been led thither in hopes of finding some fire to warm themselves.", "존스가 대답하기를, 자기들은 길을 잃은 여행자들인데, 창문에 불빛이 보이기에 몸을 녹일 불이라도 찾을까 하는 마음에 이리로 이끌려 왔노라고 했다. \"당신들이 누구든,\""], ["“Whoever you are,”", "여인이 외쳤다. \"여기 볼일은 없을 텐데."], ["cries the woman, “you have no business here; nor shall I open the door to any one at this time of night.”", "이런 밤 시각에 누구에게도 문을 열어 줄 수는 없소.\" 사람의 목소리를 듣고 겁에서 어느 정도 벗어난 파트리지는, 잠깐만이라도 불 곁에 들여 달라며 더없이 간곡하게 애원하기 시작했다."], ["Partridge, whom the sound of a human voice had recovered from his fright, fell to the most earnest supplications to be admitted for a few minutes to the fire, saying, he was almost dead with the cold; to which fear had indeed contributed equally with the frost.", "추위로 거의 죽을 지경이라는 것이었는데, 사실 그 추위에는 두려움도 서리만큼이나 한몫하고 있었다. 그는 자기에게 말을 건 신사가 이 고장에서 손꼽히는 대지주 중 한 분이라고 그녀에게 장담했고, 단 하나만 빼고 온갖 논리를 다 동원했다."], ["He assured her that the gentleman who spoke to her was one of the greatest squires in the country; and made use of every argument, save one, which Jones afterwards effectually added; and this was, the promise of half-a-crown;--a bribe too great to be resisted by such a person, especially as the genteel appearance of Jones, which the light of the moon plainly discovered to her, together with his affable behaviour, had entirely subdued those apprehensions of thieves which she had at first conceived.", "그 하나는 나중에 존스가 효과적으로 보탰으니, 바로 반 크라운을 주겠다는 약속이었다. 그런 사람에게는 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 뇌물이었다."], ["She agreed, therefore, at last, to let them in; where Partridge, to his infinite joy, found a good fire ready for his reception.", "더구나 달빛이 그녀에게 또렷이 드러내 준 존스의 점잖은 차림새와 그의 상냥한 태도가, 처음에 그녀가 품었던 도둑에 대한 경계심을 완전히 가라앉힌 터였다. 그리하여 그녀는 마침내 두 사람을 들이기로 했고, 파트리지는 자기를 맞이할 준비가 된 따뜻한 불을 보고 더없이 기뻐했다."]], [["The poor fellow, however, had no sooner warmed himself, than those thoughts which were always uppermost in his mind, began a little to disturb his brain.", "그러나 이 가엾은 사내는 몸을 녹이자마자, 늘 머릿속을 가장 크게 차지하고 있던 생각들이 다시 그의 머리를 조금씩 어지럽히기 시작했다."], ["There was no article of his creed in which he had a stronger faith than he had in witchcraft, nor can the reader conceive a figure more adapted to inspire this idea, than the old woman who now stood before him.", "그의 신조 가운데 마법보다 더 굳게 믿는 항목은 없었으니, 지금 그의 앞에 서 있는 늙은 여인보다 그런 생각을 불러일으키기에 더 알맞은 형상은 독자도 떠올리기 어려울 것이다."], ["She answered exactly to that picture drawn by Otway in his Orphan.", "그녀는 오트웨이가 그의 작품 《고아》에서 그린 그 그림에 정확히 들어맞았다."], ["Indeed, if this woman had lived in the reign of James the First, her appearance alone would have hanged her, almost without any evidence.", "실로 이 여인이 제임스 1세 치세에 살았더라면, 그 생김새 하나만으로도 거의 아무런 증거 없이 교수형에 처해졌을 것이다."]], [["Many circumstances likewise conspired to confirm Partridge in his opinion.", "여러 정황 또한 파트리지의 생각을 굳히는 데 한몫했다."], ["Her living, as he then imagined, by herself in so lonely a place; and in a house, the outside of which seemed much too good for her, but its inside was furnished in the most neat and elegant manner.", "그가 그때 짐작하기로 그녀는 이토록 외진 곳에서 홀로 살고 있었고, 게다가 겉모습은 그녀에게 어울리지 않게 너무 좋아 보이는 집인데도 그 안은 더없이 정갈하고 우아하게 꾸며져 있었다."], ["To say the truth, Jones himself was not a little surprized at what he saw; for, besides the extraordinary neatness of the room, it was adorned with a great number of nicknacks and curiosities, which might have engaged the attention of a virtuoso.", "사실대로 말하자면 존스 자신도 눈앞의 광경에 적잖이 놀랐다. 방이 유난히 단정한 데다, 애호가의 관심을 끌 만한 수많은 진기한 물건과 골동품으로 장식되어 있었기 때문이다."]], [["While Jones was admiring these things, and Partridge sat trembling with the firm belief that he was in the house of a witch, the old woman said, “I hope, gentlemen, you will make what haste you can; for I expect my master presently, and I would not for double the money he should find you here.”--“Then you have a master?” cried Jones. “Indeed, you will excuse me, good woman, but I was surprized to see all those fine things in your house.”--“Ah, sir,” said she, “if the twentieth part of these things were mine, I should think myself a rich woman. But pray, sir, do not stay much longer, for I look for him in every minute.”--“Why, sure he would not be angry with you,” said Jones, “for doing a common act of charity?”--“Alack-a-day, sir!” said she, “he is a strange man, not at all like other people. He keeps no company with anybody, and seldom walks out but by night, for he doth not care to be seen; and all the country people are as much afraid of meeting him; for his dress is enough to frighten those who are not used to it. They call him, the Man of the Hill (for there he walks by night), and the country people are not, I believe, more afraid of the devil himself. He would be terribly angry if he found you here.”--“Pray, sir,” says Partridge, “don't let us offend the gentleman; I am ready to walk, and was never warmer in my life. Do pray, sir, let us go. Here are pistols over the chimney: who knows whether they be charged or no, or what he may do with them?”--“Fear nothing, Partridge,” cries Jones; “I will secure thee from danger.”--“Nay, for matter o' that, he never doth any mischief,” said the woman; “but to be sure it is necessary he should keep some arms for his own safety; for his house hath been beset more than once; and it is not many nights ago that we thought we heard thieves about it: for my own part, I have often wondered that he is not murdered by some villain or other, as he walks out by himself at such hours; but then, as I said, the people are afraid of him; and besides, they think, I suppose, he hath nothing about him worth taking.”--“I should imagine, by this collection of rarities,” cries Jones, “that your master had been a traveller.”--“Yes, sir,” answered she, “he hath been a very great one: there be few gentlemen that know more of all matters than he. I fancy he hath been crost in love, or whatever it is I know not; but I have lived with him above these thirty years, and in all that time he hath hardly spoke to six living people.” She then again solicited their departure, in which she was backed by Partridge; but Jones purposely protracted the time, for his curiosity was greatly raised to see this extraordinary person. Though the old woman, therefore, concluded every one of her answers with desiring him to be gone, and Partridge proceeded so far as to pull him by the sleeve, he still continued to invent new questions, till the old woman, with an affrighted countenance, declared she heard her master's signal; and at the same instant more than one voice was heard without the door, crying, “D--n your blood, show us your money this instant. Your money, you villain, or we will blow your brains about your ears.”", "존스가 이 물건들에 감탄하고, 파트리지가 자기가 마녀의 집에 있다고 굳게 믿으며 떨고 앉아 있는 동안, 늙은 여인이 말했다. \"신사 양반들, 부디 되도록 서둘러 주셨으면 합니다. 곧 주인 어른이 오실 텐데, 두 배 돈을 준다 해도 이분이 여러분을 여기서 보시게 하고 싶지는 않거든요.\" \"그러면 주인이 있단 말이오?\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"이거 정말 미안하오, 착한 부인. 하지만 댁에 저 좋은 물건들이 다 있는 걸 보고 놀랐던 거요.\" \"아이고, 나리.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 물건들의 스무 분의 일이라도 제 것이라면 저는 제가 부자라고 여길 겁니다. 하지만 부디, 나리, 너무 오래 머무르지 마세요. 매 순간 그분이 오시기를 기다리는 처지니까요.\" \"아니, 설마 흔한 자선을 베푼 일로 부인에게 화를 내지는 않을 텐데.\" 존스가 말했다. \"아이고 맙소사, 나리!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그분은 이상한 분이라, 다른 사람들과는 전혀 다릅니다. 누구와도 어울리지 않고, 좀처럼 밖에 나가는 일도 없으며, 나가더라도 밤에만 나가시지요. 남에게 보이는 걸 싫어하시니까요. 그리고 온 고장 사람들도 그분과 마주치는 걸 그만큼 두려워합니다. 그분의 차림새는 익숙하지 않은 이들을 겁먹게 하기에 충분하거든요. 사람들은 그분을 '언덕의 사나이'라고 부른답니다(밤이면 거기를 거니시니까요). 제 생각에 고장 사람들은 악마 그 자체보다 그분을 덜 두려워하지는 않을 거예요. 여러분을 여기서 보시면 무섭게 화를 내실 겁니다.\" \"제발, 나리,\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"저 어른의 심기를 거스르지 맙시다. 저는 걸을 채비가 되어 있고, 평생 이보다 더 따뜻했던 적이 없습니다. 제발, 나리, 갑시다. 저기 굴뚝 위에 권총이 있습니다. 장전이 되어 있는지 아닌지 누가 알겠으며, 그분이 그걸로 무슨 짓을 할지 누가 알겠습니까?\" \"아무것도 두려워 말게, 파트리지.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"내가 자네를 위험으로부터 지켜 주겠네.\" \"아니, 그 일이라면, 그분이 누구를 해코지하는 일은 결코 없답니다.\" 여인이 말했다. \"하지만 분명 자기 안전을 위해 무기를 좀 갖추어 둘 필요가 있긴 하지요. 이 집이 한두 번 습격당한 게 아니거든요. 도둑이 집 주변을 어슬렁거린다고 우리가 여긴 게 불과 며칠 밤 전 일입니다. 저로 말하자면, 그분이 그런 시각에 홀로 거닐면서도 어떤 악당에게든 살해당하지 않은 게 종종 신기할 따름이에요. 하지만 아까 말씀드렸듯이, 사람들이 그분을 무서워하는 데다, 짐작건대 그분에게 가져갈 만한 물건이 없다고들 여기는 거겠지요.\" \"이 진기한 물건들로 미루어 보건대,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"댁의 주인은 여행을 많이 다닌 분인가 보군.\" \"그렇습니다, 나리.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그분은 아주 대단한 여행가셨지요. 온갖 일에 그분보다 더 많이 아는 신사는 드물 겁니다. 제 짐작으로는 사랑에 어긋난 일이 있었거나 뭐 그런 게 아닌가 싶은데, 무엇인지는 저도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 저는 이분과 삼십 년 넘게 함께 살아왔는데, 그 긴 세월 동안 그분이 살아 있는 사람 여섯 명과도 채 말을 나누지 않았답니다.\" 그러고서 그녀는 다시 그들의 출발을 재촉했고, 파트리지가 거기에 가세했다. 그러나 존스는 일부러 시간을 끌었으니, 이 비범한 사람을 보고 싶은 호기심이 크게 일었기 때문이다. 그리하여 늙은 여인은 대답마다 끝에 어서 가 달라는 말을 붙였고, 파트리지는 그의 소매를 잡아당기는 데까지 이르렀지만, 그는 여전히 새로운 질문을 짜내며 머물렀다. 마침내 늙은 여인이 겁에 질린 얼굴로 주인의 신호 소리가 들린다고 말했고, 바로 그 순간 문밖에서 한 사람 이상의 목소리가 들려왔다. \"이 피도 안 마른 놈아, 당장 돈을 내놔. 돈을 내놓으라고, 이 악당아, 안 그러면 네 골통을 날려 버릴 테다.\""]], [["“O, good heaven!”", "\"오, 맙소사!\""], ["cries the old woman, “some villains, to be sure, have attacked my master.", "늙은 여인이 외쳤다. \"분명 어떤 악당들이 주인 어른을 덮친 거예요."], ["O la!", "아이고!"], ["what shall I do?", "어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아?\""], ["what shall I do?”--“How!”", "\"어떻게!\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “how!--Are these pistols loaded?”--“O, good sir, there is nothing in them, indeed.", "\"어떻게! 이 권총들은 장전되어 있소?\""], ["O pray don't murder us, gentlemen!”", "\"오, 착하신 나리, 정말 그 안엔 아무것도 없어요."], ["(for in reality she now had the same opinion of those within as she had of those without).", "오, 제발 저희를 죽이지 마세요, 나리들!\"(사실 그녀는 이제 안에 있는 자들에 대해서도 밖에 있는 자들에 대해 품었던 것과 똑같은 생각을 하고 있었던 것이다.) 존스는 그녀에게 대꾸도 하지 않고, 방에 걸려 있던 낡은 폭검 하나를 낚아채서는 곧장 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 거기서 그는 늙은 신사가 두 악당과 몸싸움을 벌이며 자비를 구걸하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Jones made her no answer; but snatching an old broad sword which hung in the room, he instantly sallied out, where he found the old gentleman struggling with two ruffians, and begging for mercy.", "존스는 아무것도 묻지 않고 폭검을 들고 어찌나 날쌔게 일에 나섰던지, 그자들은 곧장 붙잡았던 손을 놓았다. 그리고 우리의 주인공을 감히 공격하려 들지도 못한 채 줄행랑을 쳐 달아났다."], ["Jones asked no questions, but fell so briskly to work with his broad sword, that the fellows immediately quitted their hold; and without offering to attack our heroe, betook themselves to their heels and made their escape; for he did not attempt to pursue them, being contented with having delivered the old gentleman; and indeed he concluded he had pretty well done their business, for both of them, as they ran off, cried out with bitter oaths that they were dead men.", "존스는 그들을 뒤쫓으려 하지 않았으니, 늙은 신사를 구해 낸 것으로 만족했기 때문이다. 실로 그는 그자들을 꽤 단단히 손봐 주었다고 결론지었는데, 둘 다 달아나면서 자기들은 죽은 목숨이라고 모진 욕설과 함께 외쳐 댔기 때문이다."]], [["Jones presently ran to lift up the old gentleman, who had been thrown down in the scuffle, expressing at the same time great concern lest he should have received any harm from the villains. The old man stared a moment at Jones, and then cried, “No, sir, no, I have very little harm, I thank you. Lord have mercy upon me!”--“I see, sir,” said Jones, “you are not free from apprehensions even of those who have had the happiness to be your deliverers; nor can I blame any suspicions which you may have; but indeed you have no real occasion for any; here are none but your friends present. Having mist our way this cold night, we took the liberty of warming ourselves at your fire, whence we were just departing when we heard you call for assistance, which, I must say, Providence alone seems to have sent you.”--“Providence, indeed,” cries the old gentleman, “if it be so.”--“So it is, I assure you,” cries Jones. “Here is your own sword, sir; I have used it in your defence, and I now return it into your hand.” The old man having received the sword, which was stained with the blood of his enemies, looked stedfastly at Jones during some moments, and then with a sigh cried out, “You will pardon me, young gentleman; I was not always of a suspicious temper, nor am I a friend to ingratitude.”", "존스는 곧장 달려가 몸싸움 중에 쓰러졌던 늙은 신사를 일으켜 세우면서, 혹시 그가 악당들에게 무슨 해라도 입지 않았을까 깊은 염려를 드러냈다. 노인은 한순간 존스를 빤히 쳐다보더니 외쳤다. \"아니오, 선생, 아니오, 다친 데는 거의 없소이다, 고맙소. 주여, 제게 자비를 베푸소서!\" \"보아하니, 선생,\" 존스가 말했다. \"당신을 구해 드리는 행운을 누린 이들에 대해서조차 경계를 풀지 못하시는군요. 당신이 품으실 어떤 의심도 나무랄 수는 없습니다만, 사실 그러실 실제 까닭은 전혀 없습니다. 여기 있는 이들은 당신의 벗들뿐이니까요. 이 추운 밤에 길을 잃은 우리가 외람되게도 당신의 불 곁에서 몸을 녹이고 막 떠나려던 참에, 당신이 도움을 청하는 소리를 들었습니다. 분명히 말씀드리건대, 그 외침은 오직 하늘의 섭리가 당신에게 보낸 것 같습니다.\" \"섭리라, 정말이지,\" 늙은 신사가 외쳤다. \"그게 사실이라면 말이오.\" \"사실입니다, 분명히 말씀드리지요.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"여기 당신의 검을 받으십시오, 선생. 당신을 지키는 데 썼으니, 이제 다시 당신 손에 돌려드립니다.\" 노인은 적들의 피로 얼룩진 검을 받아 들고는 한동안 존스를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 한숨을 내쉬며 외쳤다. \"용서하시오, 젊은 양반. 나도 늘 의심 많은 성미였던 건 아니며, 배은망덕을 가까이하는 사람도 아니라오.\""]], [["“Be thankful then,”", "\"그렇다면 감사하십시오,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “to that Providence to which you owe your deliverance: as to my part, I have only discharged the common duties of humanity, and what I would have done for any fellow-creature in your situation.”--“Let me look at you a little longer,”", "\"당신의 구원을 빚진 그 섭리에 말입니다. 제 몫으로 말하자면, 저는 인간으로서의 흔한 도리를 다했을 뿐이며, 당신과 같은 처지의 어떤 동료에게라도 했을 일을 했을 따름입니다.\""], ["cries the old gentleman.", "\"당신을 좀 더 보게 해 주시오,\""], ["“You are a human creature then?", "늙은 신사가 외쳤다. \"그러면 당신은 사람이란 말이오?"], ["Well, perhaps you are.", "음, 그럴지도 모르겠구려."], ["Come pray walk into my little hutt.", "자, 부디 내 작은 오두막으로 들어오시오."], ["You have been my deliverer indeed.”", "당신은 정말로 나의 구원자였소이다.\""]], [["The old woman was distracted between the fears which she had of her master, and for him; and Partridge was, if possible, in a greater fright.", "늙은 여인은 주인에 대한 두려움과 주인을 위한 두려움 사이에서 정신이 없었고, 파트리지는 그게 가능하다면 그보다 더 큰 공포에 빠져 있었다. 그러나 늙은 여인은 주인이 존스에게 다정하게 말하는 것을 듣고 무슨 일이 있었는지 알아차리자 다시 제정신을 차렸다."], ["The former of these, however, when she heard her master speak kindly to Jones, and perceived what had happened, came again to herself; but Partridge no sooner saw the gentleman, than the strangeness of his dress infused greater terrors into that poor fellow than he had before felt, either from the strange description which he had heard, or from the uproar which had happened at the door.", "하지만 파트리지는 그 신사를 보자마자, 그 차림새의 기이함이 이 가엾은 사내에게 앞서 느꼈던 것보다 더 큰 공포를 불어넣었다. 들었던 그 기이한 묘사로 인한 것이든, 문 앞에서 벌어진 그 소동으로 인한 것이든 말이다."]], [["To say the truth, it was an appearance which might have affected a more constant mind than that of Mr Partridge.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그것은 파트리지보다 훨씬 더 굳건한 마음이라도 동요시켰을 법한 행색이었다."], ["This person was of the tallest size, with a long beard as white as snow.", "이 사람은 키가 더없이 컸고, 눈처럼 새하얀 긴 수염을 기르고 있었다."], ["His body was cloathed with the skin of an ass, made something into the form of a coat.", "그의 몸은 나귀 가죽으로 만든, 외투 비슷한 형태로 다듬은 것을 걸치고 있었다."], ["He wore likewise boots on his legs, and a cap on his head, both composed of the skin of some other animals.", "또한 그는 다리에 장화를 신고 머리에 모자를 썼는데, 둘 다 어떤 다른 짐승의 가죽으로 지은 것이었다."]], [["As soon as the old gentleman came into his house, the old woman began her congratulations on his happy escape from the ruffians.", "늙은 신사가 집 안으로 들어서자마자, 늙은 여인은 그가 악당들에게서 무사히 벗어난 것을 축하하기 시작했다. \"그래,\""], ["“Yes,”", "그가 외쳤다."], ["cried he, “I have escaped, indeed, thanks to my preserver.”--“O the blessing on him!”", "\"정말로 벗어났지, 나를 구해 준 분 덕분에.\" \"오, 그분에게 축복이 있기를!\""], ["answered she: “he is a good gentleman, I warrant him.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"장담컨대 좋은 신사 양반이에요."], ["I was afraid your worship would have been angry with me for letting him in; and to be certain I should not have done it, had not I seen by the moon-light, that he was a gentleman, and almost frozen to death.", "그분을 들였다고 나리께서 제게 화를 내시지 않을까 걱정했답니다. 분명 저도 그러지 않았을 거예요."], ["And to be certain it must have been some good angel that sent him hither, and tempted me to do it.”", "달빛에 그분이 신사인 데다 추위에 거의 얼어 죽을 지경인 걸 보지 못했더라면요. 그리고 분명 그분을 이리로 보내고 제가 그러도록 이끈 건 어떤 착한 천사였을 거예요.\""]], [["“I am afraid, sir,”", "\"미안하오, 선생,\" 늙은 신사가 존스에게 말했다."], ["said the old gentleman to Jones, “that I have nothing in this house which you can either eat or drink, unless you will accept a dram of brandy; of which I can give you some most excellent, and which I have had by me these thirty years.”", "\"이 집에는 당신이 먹거나 마실 만한 게 아무것도 없소이다. 브랜디 한 잔이라도 받아 주신다면 모를까."], ["Jones declined this offer in a very civil and proper speech, and then the other asked him, “Whither he was travelling when he mist his way?”", "더없이 훌륭한 걸로 좀 드릴 수 있는데, 삼십 년이나 곁에 두어 온 것이라오.\" 존스는 매우 정중하고 적절한 말로 이 제안을 사양했다."], ["saying, “I must own myself surprized to see such a person as you appear to be, journeying on foot at this time of night.", "그러자 노인은 그에게 길을 잃었을 때 어디로 가는 길이었느냐고 물으며 말했다. \"당신 같은 사람이 이런 밤 시각에 걸어서 여행하는 걸 보니 솔직히 놀랐다고 인정하지 않을 수 없구려."], ["I suppose, sir, you are a gentleman of these parts; for you do not look like one who is used to travel far without horses?”", "짐작건대, 선생, 당신은 이 근방의 신사겠지요. 말도 없이 먼 길을 다니는 데 익숙한 사람으로는 보이지 않으니 말이오.\""]], [["“Appearances,”", "\"겉모습이란,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cried Jones, “are often deceitful; men sometimes look what they are not.", "\"흔히 사람을 속이는 법이지요. 사람은 때로 실제와 다른 모습으로 보이니까요."], ["I assure you I am not of this country; and whither I am travelling, in reality I scarce know myself.”", "분명히 말씀드리지만 저는 이 고장 사람이 아닙니다. 그리고 제가 어디로 가고 있는지는, 실은 저 자신도 거의 알지 못합니다.\""]], [["“Whoever you are, or whithersoever you are going,”", "\"당신이 누구든, 어디로 가고 있든,\""], ["answered the old man, “I have obligations to you which I can never return.”", "노인이 대답했다. \"나는 당신에게 결코 갚을 수 없는 신세를 졌소이다.\""]], [["“I once more,” replied Jones, “affirm that you have none; for there can be no merit in having hazarded that in your service on which I set no value; and nothing is so contemptible in my eyes as life.”", "\"다시 한 번 말씀드리지만,\" 존스가 답했다. \"당신은 제게 아무 신세도 지지 않으셨습니다. 제가 가치를 두지 않는 것을 당신을 위해 걸었다고 해서 무슨 공이 있을 수 있겠습니까. 그리고 제 눈에 목숨만큼 하찮은 것은 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I am sorry, young gentleman,”", "\"안타깝구려, 젊은 양반,\""], ["answered the stranger, “that you have any reason to be so unhappy at your years.”", "낯선 이가 대답했다. \"그 나이에 그토록 불행할 까닭이 있다니 말이오.\""]], [["“Indeed I am, sir,”", "\"정말 그렇습니다, 선생.\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “the most unhappy of mankind.”--“Perhaps you have had a friend, or a mistress?”", "\"저는 인류 가운데 가장 불행한 사람입니다.\" \"혹시 벗이나 연인이 있었던 게요?\""], ["replied the other.", "상대가 물었다. \"어떻게,\""], ["“How could you,”", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “mention two words sufficient to drive me to distraction?”--“Either of them are enough to drive any man to distraction,”", "\"저를 미칠 지경으로 몰아넣기에 충분한 두 마디를 입에 올리실 수 있단 말입니까?\" \"그 둘 중 어느 하나라도 어떤 사람이든 미칠 지경으로 몰아넣기에 충분하지요.\""], ["answered the old man.", "노인이 대답했다."], ["“I enquire no farther, sir; perhaps my curiosity hath led me too far already.”", "\"더는 묻지 않겠소, 선생. 아마 내 호기심이 이미 너무 멀리 나아간 듯하구려.\""]], [["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 선생,\""], ["cries Jones, “I cannot censure a passion which I feel at this instant in the highest degree.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"저는 바로 이 순간 더없이 강하게 느끼고 있는 그 마음을 나무랄 수 없습니다."], ["You will pardon me when I assure you, that everything which I have seen or heard since I first entered this house hath conspired to raise the greatest curiosity in me.", "이 집에 처음 들어선 뒤로 제가 보고 들은 모든 것이 제 안에 가장 큰 호기심을 불러일으켰다고 분명히 말씀드리면, 당신은 저를 용서해 주실 겁니다."], ["Something very extraordinary must have determined you to this course of life, and I have reason to fear your own history is not without misfortunes.”", "무언가 매우 비범한 일이 당신을 이런 삶의 방식으로 이끌었을 게 틀림없고, 저는 당신 자신의 이야기에도 불행이 없지 않을 거라 짐작할 까닭이 있습니다.\""]], [["Here the old gentleman again sighed, and remained silent for some minutes: at last, looking earnestly on Jones, he said, “I have read that a good countenance is a letter of recommendation; if so, none ever can be more strongly recommended than yourself. If I did not feel some yearnings towards you from another consideration, I must be the most ungrateful monster upon earth; and I am really concerned it is no otherwise in my power than by words to convince you of my gratitude.”", "여기서 늙은 신사는 다시 한숨을 내쉬며 몇 분간 말이 없었다. 마침내 그는 존스를 진지하게 바라보며 말했다. \"선한 인상은 추천장과 같다고 어디선가 읽은 적이 있소. 만일 그렇다면, 당신만큼 강력하게 추천받을 만한 사람은 없을 게요. 만약 내가 또 다른 까닭으로 당신에게 어떤 끌림을 느끼지 못한다면, 나는 이 땅에서 가장 배은망덕한 괴물일 테지요. 그리고 내가 말 외의 다른 방법으로는 당신에게 내 감사를 보일 길이 없다는 게 정말이지 마음에 걸리는구려.\""]], [["Jones, after a moment's hesitation, answered, “That it was in his power by words to gratify him extremely.", "존스는 잠시 망설이다가 대답하기를, 말로도 자기를 더없이 흡족하게 해 주실 수 있다고 했다."], ["I have confest a curiosity,”", "\"저는 호기심을 고백했습니다, 선생.\""], ["said he, “sir; need I say how much obliged I should be to you, if you would condescend to gratify it?", "그가 말했다. \"만일 당신이 황송하게도 그것을 풀어 주신다면 제가 얼마나 감사하겠는지, 굳이 말씀드릴 필요가 있겠습니까?"], ["Will you suffer me therefore to beg, unless any consideration restrains you, that you would be pleased to acquaint me what motives have induced you thus to withdraw from the society of mankind, and to betake yourself to a course of life to which it sufficiently appears you were not born?”", "그러니 어떤 사정이 당신을 막지 않는다면, 부디 청하건대, 무슨 동기로 이렇게 인류 사회에서 물러나 보아하니 당신이 타고나지 않은 게 분명한 삶의 방식에 몸을 맡기게 되셨는지 제게 알려 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I scarce think myself at liberty to refuse you anything after what hath happened,”", "\"그동안 있었던 일을 생각하면, 당신에게 무엇이든 거절할 자유가 내게 있다고는 여기기 어렵구려.\" 노인이 대답했다."], ["replied the old man.", "\"그러니 불행한 한 사람의 이야기를 듣고 싶다면, 당신에게 들려드리리다."], ["“If you desire therefore to hear the story of an unhappy man, I will relate it to you.", "실로 당신의 판단이 옳소. 사회에서 달아나는 자들의 운명에는 흔히 무언가 비범한 게 있다고 생각하다니 말이오."], ["Indeed you judge rightly, in thinking there is commonly something extraordinary in the fortunes of those who fly from society; for however it may seem a paradox, or even a contradiction, certain it is, that great philanthropy chiefly inclines us to avoid and detest mankind; not on account so much of their private and selfish vices, but for those of a relative kind; such as envy, malice, treachery, cruelty, with every other species of malevolence.", "역설처럼, 아니 모순처럼 보일지 몰라도, 분명한 사실은 위대한 박애야말로 무엇보다 우리를 인류로부터 멀어지고 그들을 혐오하게 만든다는 것이오. 그것은 그들의 사사롭고 이기적인 악덕 때문이라기보다는, 관계에서 비롯되는 종류의 악덕, 이를테면 시기, 악의, 배신, 잔인함을 비롯한 온갖 다른 종류의 악의 때문이오."], ["These are the vices which true philanthropy abhors, and which rather than see and converse with, she avoids society itself.", "이것들이야말로 참된 박애가 혐오하는 악덕이며, 박애는 그것들을 보고 더불어 이야기하느니 차라리 사회 그 자체를 피해 버린다오. 하지만 당신에게 빈말을 하려는 게 아니라, 내 보기에 당신은 내가 피하거나 혐오할 사람들 가운데 하나가 아닌 듯하오."], ["However, without a compliment to you, you do not appear to me one of those whom I should shun or detest; nay, I must say, in what little hath dropt from you, there appears some parity in our fortunes: I hope, however, yours will conclude more successfully.”", "아니, 말하건대, 당신에게서 흘러나온 그 얼마 안 되는 말 속에 우리 둘의 운명에 어떤 닮은 데가 있어 보이는구려. 다만 당신의 운명은 더 성공적으로 끝맺기를 바라오.\""]], [["Here some compliments passed between our heroe and his host, and then the latter was going to begin his history, when Partridge interrupted him.", "여기서 우리의 주인공과 그의 주인 사이에 몇 마디 인사치레가 오갔고, 그런 뒤 주인이 막 자기 이야기를 시작하려는데 파트리지가 그를 가로막았다."], ["His apprehensions had now pretty well left him, but some effects of his terrors remained; he therefore reminded the gentleman of that excellent brandy which he had mentioned.", "그의 두려움은 이제 어지간히 가셨으나, 그 공포의 여운이 얼마간 남아 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 그는 신사에게 아까 언급했던 그 훌륭한 브랜디를 일깨워 주었다."], ["This was presently brought, and Partridge swallowed a large bumper.", "브랜디는 곧 나왔고, 파트리지는 큰 잔으로 가득 들이켰다."]], [["The gentleman then, without any farther preface, began as you may read in the next chapter.", "그러고서 신사는 더 이상의 서두 없이, 다음 장에서 읽게 될 대로 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["In which the Man of the Hill begins to relate his history.", "산속 사람이 자신의 이야기를 들려주기 시작하다."]], [["“I was born in a village of Somersetshire, called Mark, in the year 1657.", "\"나는 1657년, 서머싯셔의 마크라는 마을에서 태어났습니다."], ["My father was one of those whom they call gentlemen farmers.", "아버지는 사람들이 흔히 신사 농부라 부르는 부류였지요."], ["He had a little estate of about £300 a year of his own, and rented another estate of near the same value.", "연 300파운드쯤 되는 자그마한 토지를 직접 소유하고, 거기에 거의 같은 값어치의 다른 토지를 빌려 농사를 지었습니다."], ["He was prudent and industrious, and so good a husbandman, that he might have led a very easy and comfortable life, had not an arrant vixen of a wife soured his domestic quiet.", "신중하고 부지런했으며, 농사 솜씨가 어찌나 좋았던지, 지독한 암여우 같은 아내가 집안의 평온을 들쑤시지만 않았다면 아주 편안하고 안락한 삶을 누릴 수 있었을 겁니다."], ["But though this circumstance perhaps made him miserable, it did not make him poor; for he confined her almost entirely at home, and rather chose to bear eternal upbraidings in his own house, than to injure his fortune by indulging her in the extravagancies she desired abroad.", "그러나 이런 사정이 아버지를 비참하게 만들었을지언정 가난하게 만들지는 않았습니다. 아버지는 어머니를 거의 집 안에만 붙들어 두었고, 어머니가 바깥에서 부리고 싶어 하던 사치에 재산을 축내느니 차라리 집 안에서 끝없는 잔소리를 견디는 쪽을 택했으니까요."]], [["“By this Xanthippe” (so was the wife of Socrates called, said Partridge)--“by this Xanthippe he had two sons, of which I was the younger. He designed to give us both good education; but my elder brother, who, unhappily for him, was the favourite of my mother, utterly neglected his learning; insomuch that, after having been five or six years at school with little or no improvement, my father, being told by his master that it would be to no purpose to keep him longer there, at last complied with my mother in taking him home from the hands of that tyrant, as she called his master; though indeed he gave the lad much less correction than his idleness deserved, but much more, it seems, than the young gentleman liked, who constantly complained to his mother of his severe treatment, and she as constantly gave him a hearing.”", "\"이 크산티페에게서—\" (소크라테스의 아내가 그렇게 불렸지요, 하고 파트리지가 끼어들었다) \"—이 크산티페에게서 아버지는 두 아들을 두었는데, 내가 동생이었습니다. 아버지는 우리 둘 다 좋은 교육을 시키려 했지요. 그러나 형은, 자기에게는 불행하게도 어머니의 총애를 한 몸에 받던 터라, 공부를 아예 내팽개치고 말았습니다. 어찌나 그랬던지, 대여섯 해를 학교에 다니고도 거의 늘지를 않으니, 결국 선생이 더 붙들어 두어 봐야 소용없다고 아버지에게 말하기에 이르렀습니다. 마침내 아버지는 어머니의 뜻에 따라 형을 그 폭군의 손에서—어머니는 선생을 그렇게 불렀지요—집으로 데려왔습니다. 사실 그 선생은 형의 게으름이 마땅히 받아야 할 것보다 훨씬 덜 매를 들었는데도, 정작 도련님 입맛에는 너무 가혹했던 모양이라, 형은 늘 가혹한 대우를 받는다며 어머니에게 일러바쳤고, 어머니는 또 한결같이 그 말을 들어주곤 했습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,” cries Partridge, “I have seen such mothers; I have been abused myself by them, and very unjustly; such parents deserve correction as much as their children.”", "\"맞아요, 맞아.\" 하고 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"나도 그런 어머니들을 본 적이 있지요. 나 자신도 그런 이들에게 부당하게 욕을 먹은 적이 있고요. 그런 부모는 제 자식만큼이나 매를 맞아 마땅합니다.\""]], [["Jones chid the pedagogue for his interruption, and then the stranger proceeded.", "존스가 끼어든다고 선생을 나무라자, 낯선 이는 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“My brother now, at the age of fifteen, bade adieu to all learning, and to everything else but to his dog and gun; with which latter he became so expert, that, though perhaps you may think it incredible, he could not only hit a standing mark with great certainty, but hath actually shot a crow as it was flying in the air.", "\"이제 형은 열다섯 나이에 모든 배움에, 아니 개와 총 말고는 다른 모든 것에 작별을 고했습니다. 그중 총 솜씨는 어찌나 능숙해졌던지, 어쩌면 믿기 어려우실지 모르겠습니다만, 멈춰 있는 표적을 아주 정확히 맞히는 것은 물론이요, 공중을 날아가는 까마귀를 실제로 쏘아 떨어뜨릴 정도였습니다."], ["He was likewise excellent at finding a hare sitting, and was soon reputed one of the best sportsmen in the country; a reputation which both he and his mother enjoyed as much as if he had been thought the finest scholar.", "또 앉아 있는 토끼를 찾아내는 데도 능했고, 머지않아 이 고장에서 손꼽히는 사냥꾼으로 이름을 날렸습니다. 형도 어머니도 그 명성을, 마치 형이 가장 빼어난 학자로 여겨지기라도 한 양 한껏 즐겼지요."]], [["“The situation of my brother made me at first think my lot the harder, in being continued at school: but I soon changed my opinion; for as I advanced pretty fast in learning, my labours became easy, and my exercise so delightful, that holidays were my most unpleasant time; for my mother, who never loved me, now apprehending that I had the greater share of my father's affection, and finding, or at least thinking, that I was more taken notice of by some gentlemen of learning, and particularly by the parson of the parish, than my brother, she now hated my sight, and made home so disagreeable to me, that what is called by school-boys Black Monday, was to me the whitest in the whole year.", "\"형의 처지를 보면서 처음에는 학교에 계속 매여 있는 내 신세가 더 고달프다고 여겼습니다. 그러나 곧 생각이 바뀌었지요. 나는 배움에서 제법 빠르게 나아갔기에 공부가 수월해졌고, 그 익힘이 어찌나 즐거웠던지 도리어 방학이 가장 괴로운 때가 되었으니까요. 나를 한 번도 사랑한 적 없던 어머니는, 이제 아버지의 애정을 내가 더 많이 차지한다고 여기고, 학식 있는 몇몇 신사들, 특히 교구 목사가 형보다 나를 더 눈여겨본다는 사실을 알게 되자—적어도 그렇게 생각하게 되자—내 얼굴 보는 것조차 미워하게 되었고, 집을 어찌나 견디기 싫은 곳으로 만들었던지, 학생들이 흔히 '검은 월요일'이라 부르는 그날이 내게는 한 해 통틀어 가장 하얗고 반가운 날이 되었습니다."]], [["“Having at length gone through the school at Taunton, I was thence removed to Exeter College in Oxford, where I remained four years; at the end of which an accident took me off entirely from my studies; and hence, I may truly date the rise of all which happened to me afterwards in life.", "\"마침내 톤턴의 학교 과정을 모두 마치자, 나는 거기서 옥스퍼드의 엑서터 칼리지로 옮겨가 네 해를 머물렀습니다. 그 끝에 한 사건이 나를 학업에서 완전히 떼어 놓았지요. 그러니 그 후 내 인생에 일어난 모든 일의 시작을, 나는 진정 그때로 잡을 수 있습니다."]], [["“There was at the same college with myself one Sir George Gresham, a young fellow who was intitled to a very considerable fortune, which he was not, by the will of his father, to come into full possession of till he arrived at the age of twenty-five.", "\"나와 같은 칼리지에 조지 그레셤 경이라는 젊은이가 있었습니다. 상당한 재산을 물려받을 자격이 있었지만, 아버지의 유언에 따라 스물다섯이 되기 전에는 그 재산을 온전히 손에 넣을 수 없는 사람이었지요."], ["However, the liberality of his guardians gave him little cause to regret the abundant caution of his father; for they allowed him five hundred pounds a year while he remained at the university, where he kept his horses and his whore, and lived as wicked and as profligate a life as he could have done had he been never so entirely master of his fortune; for besides the five hundred a year which he received from his guardians, he found means to spend a thousand more.", "그렇지만 후견인들의 후한 씀씀이 덕에, 그는 아버지가 베푼 그 넘치는 신중함을 아쉬워할 까닭이 거의 없었습니다. 후견인들은 그가 대학에 머무는 동안 연 500파운드를 내주었고, 그는 그 돈으로 말과 정부를 거느리며, 제 재산을 완전히 제 마음대로 주물렀더라도 더 나빴을 리 없을 만큼 사악하고 방탕한 삶을 살았으니까요."], ["He was above the age of twenty-one, and had no difficulty in gaining what credit he pleased.", "후견인들에게서 받는 연 500파운드 말고도, 그는 천 파운드를 더 쓸 방도를 찾아냈습니다. 스물한 살이 넘은 나이라, 마음껏 외상을 얻는 데에도 아무 어려움이 없었지요."]], [["“This young fellow, among many other tolerable bad qualities, had one very diabolical.", "\"이 젊은이는 그럭저럭 봐줄 만한 여러 악덕 가운데, 그야말로 악마 같은 하나를 지니고 있었습니다."], ["He had a great delight in destroying and ruining the youth of inferior fortune, by drawing them into expenses which they could not afford so well as himself; and the better, and worthier, and soberer any young man was, the greater pleasure and triumph had he in his destruction.", "자기보다 형편이 못한 젊은이들을, 감당할 수 없는 씀씀이로 끌어들여 망치고 파멸시키는 데서 큰 기쁨을 느꼈던 것이지요. 그리고 어떤 젊은이가 더 착하고, 더 훌륭하고, 더 절제할수록, 그자는 그를 파멸시키는 데서 더 큰 쾌감과 승리감을 맛보았습니다."], ["Thus acting the character which is recorded of the devil, and going about seeking whom he might devour.", "그야말로 악마에 관해 기록된 그 모습 그대로, 누구를 삼킬까 찾아 헤매며 돌아다닌 셈이지요."]], [["“It was my misfortune to fall into an acquaintance and intimacy with this gentleman.", "\"이 신사와 알고 지내며 가까워진 것이 내 불행이었습니다. 학업에 부지런하다는 내 평판이 도리어 그의 못된 의도에 더없이 탐나는 먹잇감이 되게 했고, 내 천성 또한 그가 뜻을 이루기에 어렵잖게 만들어 주었지요."], ["My reputation of diligence in my studies made me a desirable object of his mischievous intention; and my own inclination made it sufficiently easy for him to effect his purpose; for though I had applied myself with much industry to books, in which I took great delight, there were other pleasures in which I was capable of taking much greater; for I was high-mettled, had a violent flow of animal spirits, was a little ambitious, and extremely amorous.", "나는 책에 열심히 매달렸고 거기서 큰 즐거움을 얻기도 했지만, 그보다 훨씬 더 큰 즐거움을 누릴 수 있는 다른 쾌락들도 있었으니까요. 나는 혈기왕성하고, 동물적 기운이 거세게 솟구쳤으며, 야심이 조금 있었고, 무엇보다 욕정이 지독했습니다."]], [["“I had not long contracted an intimacy with Sir George before I became a partaker of all his pleasures; and when I was once entered on that scene, neither my inclination nor my spirit would suffer me to play an under part. I was second to none of the company in any acts of debauchery; nay, I soon distinguished myself so notably in all riots and disorders, that my name generally stood first in the roll of delinquents; and instead of being lamented as the unfortunate pupil of Sir George, I was now accused as the person who had misled and debauched that hopeful young gentleman; for though he was the ringleader and promoter of all the mischief, he was never so considered. I fell at last under the censure of the vice-chancellor, and very narrowly escaped expulsion.", "\"조지 경과 가까워진 지 얼마 되지 않아, 나는 그의 모든 쾌락에 함께하는 처지가 되었습니다. 그리고 일단 그 무대에 발을 들이고 나니, 내 성정도 내 기개도 결코 조연 노릇은 견디지 못했습니다. 어떤 방탕한 짓에서도 나는 그 무리 가운데 누구에게도 뒤지지 않았지요. 아니, 머지않아 온갖 난동과 소란에서 어찌나 두드러지게 이름을 떨쳤던지, 비행자 명단에는 늘 내 이름이 맨 앞에 올랐습니다. 그래서 조지 경의 불운한 제자라며 동정받기는커녕, 이제 나는 그 장래가 촉망되던 젊은 신사를 잘못 이끌어 타락시킨 장본인으로 고발당하는 처지가 되었습니다. 정작 모든 못된 짓의 우두머리이자 부추긴 자는 그였건만, 그렇게 여겨지는 일은 한 번도 없었지요. 나는 결국 부총장의 견책을 받았고, 퇴학을 아슬아슬하게 면했습니다."]], [["“You will easily believe, sir, that such a life as I am now describing must be incompatible with my further progress in learning; and that in proportion as I addicted myself more and more to loose pleasure, I must grow more and more remiss in application to my studies.", "\"이런 삶, 지금 내가 늘어놓고 있는 이런 삶이 내 학업의 더 나은 진척과 양립할 수 없다는 것을, 선생도 쉬이 믿으시겠지요. 그리고 내가 헤픈 쾌락에 점점 더 빠져들수록, 학업에 쏟는 정성도 점점 더 게을러질 수밖에 없었다는 것도요."], ["This was truly the consequence; but this was not all.", "정말 그런 결과가 따랐습니다. 그러나 그뿐이 아니었지요."], ["My expenses now greatly exceeded not only my former income, but those additions which I extorted from my poor generous father, on pretences of sums being necessary for preparing for my approaching degree of batchelor of arts.", "이제 내 씀씀이는 예전 수입은 물론, 머지않은 문학사 학위 준비에 돈이 필요하다는 핑계로 가엾고 너그러운 아버지에게서 뜯어낸 그 보탬마저도 훌쩍 넘어섰습니다. 그런데 이 요구가 마침내 어찌나 잦고 터무니없어졌던지, 아버지는 차츰 여러 군데서 들려오는 내 요즘 행실에 관한 이야기에 귀를 열기 시작했습니다."], ["These demands, however, grew at last so frequent and exorbitant, that my father by slow degrees opened his ears to the accounts which he received from many quarters of my present behaviour, and which my mother failed not to echo very faithfully and loudly; adding, `Ay, this is the fine gentleman, the scholar who doth so much honour to his family, and is to be the making of it.", "어머니는 그 이야기를 아주 충실하고 요란하게 되울리는 일을 빠뜨리지 않았지요. 거기에 이렇게 덧붙이면서요."], ["I thought what all this learning would come to.", "'암, 이게 바로 그 잘난 신사, 가문에 그토록 영광을 안기고 가문을 일으켜 세울 그 학자님이시지. 이 배움이란 게 다 어디로 갈지 내 진작 알아봤다니까."], ["He is to be the ruin of us all, I find, after his elder brother hath been denied necessaries for his sake, to perfect his education forsooth, for which he was to pay us such interest: I thought what the interest would come to,'", "결국 우리 모두를 망쳐 놓을 게야. 보아하니, 큰애가 동생 교육 마무리해 준답시고 필요한 것조차 못 받고 지냈는데, 그 대가로 우리한테 그런 이자를 갚아 준다더니, 그 이자란 게 어떻게 될지 내 진작 알아봤지.'"], ["with much more of the same kind; but I have, I believe, satisfied you with this taste.", "이런 식의 말이 훨씬 더 많았지만, 이 정도면 충분히 짐작하셨으리라 믿습니다."]], [["“My father, therefore, began now to return remonstrances instead of money to my demands, which brought my affairs perhaps a little sooner to a crisis; but had he remitted me his whole income, you will imagine it could have sufficed a very short time to support one who kept pace with the expenses of Sir George Gresham.", "\"그리하여 아버지는 이제 내 요구에 돈 대신 훈계를 돌려보내기 시작했고, 그 때문에 내 형편이 어쩌면 조금 더 일찍 위기로 치달았는지도 모릅니다. 하지만 설령 아버지가 자기 수입을 몽땅 내게 부쳐 주었더라도, 조지 그레셤 경의 씀씀이를 따라가는 사람을 지탱하기에는 아주 짧은 동안밖에 못 버텼으리라는 걸 짐작하시겠지요."]], [["“It is more than possible that the distress I was now in for money, and the impracticability of going on in this manner, might have restored me at once to my senses and to my studies, had I opened my eyes before I became involved in debts from which I saw no hopes of ever extricating myself.", "\"빚더미에 깊이 휘말려 도무지 빠져나올 가망이 보이지 않게 되기 전에 눈을 떴더라면, 지금 처한 이 돈에 쪼들리는 곤경과 이런 식으로는 더 못 버틴다는 막막함이 어쩌면 나를 단번에 제정신으로, 또 학업으로 되돌려 놓았을 법도 합니다."], ["This was indeed the great art of Sir George, and by which he accomplished the ruin of many, whom he afterwards laughed at as fools and coxcombs, for vying, as he called it, with a man of his fortune.", "사실 이것이야말로 조지 경의 대단한 수법이었고, 그것으로 그는 숱한 이들을 파멸시킨 뒤, 그의 표현을 빌리자면 자기 같은 재력가와 겨루었다며 그들을 바보요 멋쟁이 허풍선이라고 비웃었지요."], ["To bring this about, he would now and then advance a little money himself, in order to support the credit of the unfortunate youth with other people; till, by means of that very credit, he was irretrievably undone.", "이 일을 꾸미려고 그는 이따금 자기 돈을 조금 내주어, 그 불운한 젊은이의 신용을 남들 앞에서 떠받쳐 주곤 했습니다. 그러다 바로 그 신용 때문에 젊은이는 돌이킬 수 없이 파멸하고 마는 것이지요."]], [["“My mind being by these means grown as desperate as my fortune, there was scarce a wickedness which I did not meditate, in order for my relief. Self-murder itself became the subject of my serious deliberation; and I had certainly resolved on it, had not a more shameful, though perhaps less sinful, thought expelled it from my head.”--Here he hesitated a moment, and then cried out, “I protest, so many years have not washed away the shame of this act, and I shall blush while I relate it.” Jones desired him to pass over anything that might give him pain in the relation; but Partridge eagerly cried out, “Oh, pray, sir, let us hear this; I had rather hear this than all the rest; as I hope to be saved, I will never mention a word of it.” Jones was going to rebuke him, but the stranger prevented it by proceeding thus: “I had a chum, a very prudent, frugal young lad, who, though he had no very large allowance, had by his parsimony heaped up upwards of forty guineas, which I knew he kept in his escritore. I took therefore an opportunity of purloining his key from his breeches-pocket, while he was asleep, and thus made myself master of all his riches: after which I again conveyed his key into his pocket, and counterfeiting sleep--though I never once closed my eyes, lay in bed till after he arose and went to prayers--an exercise to which I had long been unaccustomed.", "\"이런 식으로 내 마음이 내 형편만큼이나 절망에 빠지자, 거기서 벗어나 보려고 궁리하지 않은 악행이 거의 없을 지경이었습니다. 자살마저도 진지한 숙고의 대상이 되었고, 더 부끄러운, 그러나 어쩌면 덜 죄스러운 생각이 그것을 머릿속에서 몰아내지 않았다면 나는 분명 그렇게 작정했을 겁니다.\" 여기서 그는 잠시 머뭇거리더니 이윽고 외쳤다. \"단언컨대, 이토록 여러 해가 흘렀어도 이 짓의 수치만은 씻기지 않아서, 이야기하는 동안 얼굴이 붉어질 겁니다.\" 존스는 이야기하기에 괴로운 대목은 그냥 건너뛰라고 청했지만, 파트리지는 안달하며 외쳤다. \"오, 제발, 그건 꼭 들려주십시오. 나머지 다 합친 것보다 이게 더 듣고 싶습니다. 구원받기를 바라는 마음을 걸고, 절대 한마디도 발설하지 않겠습니다.\" 존스가 그를 나무라려는데, 낯선 이가 이렇게 이야기를 이어 가며 막았다. \"나에게는 한 방을 쓰는 동무가 있었는데, 아주 신중하고 검소한 젊은이였습니다. 그리 넉넉한 용돈을 받지도 않았건만, 알뜰함으로 마흔 기니가 넘는 돈을 모아, 책상 서랍에 넣어 둔 걸 내가 알고 있었지요. 그래서 나는 그가 잠든 틈을 타 바지 주머니에서 열쇠를 슬쩍 빼내, 그렇게 그의 모든 재물의 주인이 되었습니다. 그러고는 열쇠를 다시 그의 주머니에 넣어 두고 잠든 척했지요. 사실 나는 한순간도 눈을 붙이지 못했으면서도, 그가 일어나 기도하러 갈 때까지—오랫동안 손을 놓고 지내던 그 기도 말입니다—침대에 누워 있었습니다."]], [["“Timorous thieves, by extreme caution, often subject themselves to discoveries, which those of a bolder kind escape.", "\"겁 많은 도둑은 지나친 조심 탓에, 도리어 더 대담한 부류라면 피해 갈 발각을 자초하는 일이 많습니다. 내게도 바로 그런 일이 벌어졌지요."], ["Thus it happened to me; for had I boldly broke open his escritore, I had, perhaps, escaped even his suspicion; but as it was plain that the person who robbed him had possessed himself of his key, he had no doubt, when he first missed his money, but that his chum was certainly the thief.", "만약 그의 책상을 대담하게 부수고 열었더라면, 어쩌면 의심조차 사지 않았을지 모릅니다. 그러나 자기 물건을 턴 자가 열쇠를 손에 넣었던 것이 분명했으니, 돈이 없어진 걸 처음 알아챘을 때 그는 한방 동무가 틀림없는 도둑이라고 의심치 않았던 것이지요."], ["Now as he was of a fearful disposition, and much my inferior in strength, and I believe in courage, he did not dare to confront me with my guilt, for fear of worse bodily consequences which might happen to him.", "그런데 그는 겁이 많은 성미인 데다, 힘으로는 나보다 한참 못했고, 내 생각엔 용기에서도 그러했기에, 더 나쁜 몸싸움이라도 벌어질까 두려워 감히 죄를 들이대며 나와 맞서지는 못했습니다."], ["He repaired therefore immediately to the vice-chancellor, and upon swearing to the robbery, and to the circumstances of it, very easily obtained a warrant against one who had now so bad a character through the whole university.", "그래서 그는 곧장 부총장에게 달려가, 도둑맞은 사실과 그 정황을 두고 맹세하고는, 이제 온 대학에 걸쳐 그토록 평판이 나빠진 사람을 잡을 영장을 아주 쉽게 받아냈습니다."]], [["“Luckily for me, I lay out of the college the next evening; for that day I attended a young lady in a chaise to Witney, where we staid all night, and in our return, the next morning, to Oxford, I met one of my cronies, who acquainted me with sufficient news concerning myself to make me turn my horse another way.”", "\"다행스럽게도, 나는 그다음 날 저녁 칼리지 밖에서 묵었습니다. 그날 한 젊은 아가씨를 마차에 태우고 위트니까지 모셔 갔다가 거기서 밤을 지냈으니까요. 그리고 이튿날 아침 옥스퍼드로 돌아오는 길에 친구 하나를 만났는데, 그가 나에 관한 소식을 충분히 알려준 덕에 나는 말 머리를 다른 쪽으로 돌리게 되었습니다.\""]], [["“Pray, sir, did he mention anything of the warrant?”", "\"여쭙는데, 그가 영장에 관한 이야기는 좀 했습니까?\""], ["said Partridge.", "하고 파트리지가 물었다."], ["But Jones begged the gentleman to proceed without regarding any impertinent questions; which he did as follows:--", "그러나 존스는 신사에게 그런 주제넘은 질문일랑 개의치 말고 계속하라고 청했고, 그는 이렇게 이어 갔다."]], [["“Having now abandoned all thoughts of returning to Oxford, the next thing which offered itself was a journey to London.", "\"이제 옥스퍼드로 돌아갈 생각은 죄다 접고 나니, 다음으로 떠오른 것이 런던으로 가는 길이었습니다."], ["I imparted this intention to my female companion, who at first remonstrated against it; but upon producing my wealth, she immediately consented.", "나는 이 뜻을 내 여자 동행에게 알렸는데, 그녀는 처음엔 반대했지만, 내가 가진 재물을 꺼내 보이자 곧바로 동의하더군요."], ["We then struck across the country, into the great Cirencester road, and made such haste, that we spent the next evening, save one, in London.", "그래서 우리는 들판을 가로질러 사이런세스터로 이어지는 큰길로 나섰고, 어찌나 서둘렀던지 그다음다음 날 저녁을 런던에서 맞았습니다."]], [["“When you consider the place where I now was, and the company with whom I was, you will, I fancy, conceive that a very short time brought me to an end of that sum of which I had so iniquitously possessed myself.", "\"내가 지금 와 있던 곳이 어떤 데였는지, 또 함께 어울리던 무리가 어떤 자들이었는지를 헤아려 보시면, 그토록 사악하게 손에 넣은 그 돈이 머지않아 바닥나고 말았으리라는 걸 짐작하시겠지요."]], [["“I was now reduced to a much higher degree of distress than before: the necessaries of life began to be numbered among my wants; and what made my case still the more grievous was, that my paramour, of whom I was now grown immoderately fond, shared the same distresses with myself. To see a woman you love in distress; to be unable to relieve her, and at the same time to reflect that you have brought her into this situation, is perhaps a curse of which no imagination can represent the horrors to those who have not felt it.”--“I believe it from my soul,” cries Jones, “and I pity you from the bottom of my heart:” he then took two or three disorderly turns about the room, and at last begged pardon, and flung himself into his chair, crying, “I thank Heaven, I have escaped that!”", "\"이제 나는 전보다 훨씬 더 깊은 곤경에 빠졌습니다. 살아가는 데 꼭 필요한 것들마저 내가 갖지 못한 것들 축에 들기 시작했지요. 그리고 내 처지를 한층 더 비참하게 만든 것은, 이제 도가 지나치도록 빠져 버린 내 연인이 나와 똑같은 곤경을 함께 겪고 있다는 사실이었습니다. 사랑하는 여인이 곤경에 처한 것을 보면서도 그녀를 구해 줄 수 없고, 게다가 그녀를 그 처지로 몰아넣은 것이 바로 자신이라는 걸 곱씹어야 하는 것—이것은 아마, 겪어 보지 못한 사람에게는 그 끔찍함을 어떤 상상으로도 그려 보일 수 없는 저주일 겁니다.\" \"진심으로 그 말을 믿습니다.\" 하고 존스가 외쳤다. \"그리고 마음 깊은 곳에서부터 당신이 가엾습니다.\" 그러고는 방 안을 두세 차례 어지러이 서성이다가, 마침내 용서를 구하며 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지고는 외쳤다. \"하늘에 감사할 일이지요, 나는 그것만은 면했으니!\""]], [["“This circumstance,”", "\"이 사정이,\""], ["continued the gentleman, “so severely aggravated the horrors of my present situation, that they became absolutely intolerable.", "하고 신사가 이어 갔다. \"지금 내 처지의 끔찍함을 어찌나 모질게 더해 놓았던지, 도무지 견딜 수 없는 지경이 되었습니다."], ["I could with less pain endure the raging in my own natural unsatisfied appetites, even hunger or thirst, than I could submit to leave ungratified the most whimsical desires of a woman on whom I so extravagantly doated, that, though I knew she had been the mistress of half my acquaintance, I firmly intended to marry her.", "굶주림이든 목마름이든, 채워지지 않은 내 천성의 욕구가 날뛰는 것을 견디는 편이, 그토록 터무니없이 떠받들던 여인의 그 변덕스럽기 짝이 없는 욕망 하나를 채워 주지 못하고 두는 것보다 차라리 덜 괴로웠으니까요. 그녀가 내 지인 절반의 정부였다는 걸 알면서도, 나는 그녀와 결혼하겠다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었습니다."], ["But the good creature was unwilling to consent to an action which the world might think so much to my disadvantage.", "그러나 이 착한 여인은, 세상이 내게 너무도 불리하다 여길 그런 행동에 선뜻 동의하려 하지 않았습니다."], ["And as, possibly, she compassionated the daily anxieties which she must have perceived me suffer on her account, she resolved to put an end to my distress.", "그리고 어쩌면 자기 때문에 내가 날마다 시달리는 그 불안을 가엾이 여겼던지, 내 고통을 끝내 주기로 마음먹었지요. 정말이지 그녀는 곧 나를 이 성가시고 뒤엉킨 처지에서 풀어 줄 방도를 찾아냈습니다."], ["She soon, indeed, found means to relieve me from my troublesome and perplexed situation; for while I was distracted with various inventions to supply her with pleasures, she very kindly--betrayed me to one of her former lovers at Oxford, by whose care and diligence I was immediately apprehended and committed to gaol.", "내가 그녀에게 어떤 즐거움을 마련해 줄까 온갖 궁리로 정신이 없을 때, 그녀가 아주 친절하게도—나를 옥스퍼드의 옛 정부 가운데 하나에게 팔아넘긴 것이지요. 그자의 정성과 부지런함 덕에 나는 곧장 붙잡혀 감옥에 갇혔습니다."]], [["“Here I first began seriously to reflect on the miscarriages of my former life; on the errors I had been guilty of; on the misfortunes which I had brought on myself; and on the grief which I must have occasioned to one of the best of fathers.", "\"여기서 나는 비로소 지난 삶의 잘못들을, 내가 저지른 과오들을, 스스로 불러들인 불행들을, 그리고 더없이 훌륭한 아버지 가운데 한 분에게 안겼을 그 슬픔을 진지하게 곱씹기 시작했습니다."], ["When I added to all these the perfidy of my mistress, such was the horror of my mind, that life, instead of being longer desirable, grew the object of my abhorrence; and I could have gladly embraced death as my dearest friend, if it had offered itself to my choice unattended by shame.", "여기에 내 정부의 배신까지 보태고 나니, 내 마음의 끔찍함이 어찌나 컸던지, 삶이 더는 바랄 만한 것이 아니라 도리어 혐오의 대상이 되었습니다. 그래서 만약 죽음이 수치를 동반하지 않은 채 내 앞에 골라잡으라 내밀어졌다면, 나는 그것을 가장 소중한 벗으로 기꺼이 끌어안았을 겁니다."]], [["“The time of the assizes soon came, and I was removed by habeas corpus to Oxford, where I expected certain conviction and condemnation; but, to my great surprize, none appeared against me, and I was, at the end of the sessions, discharged for want of prosecution. In short, my chum had left Oxford, and whether from indolence, or from what other motive I am ignorant, had declined concerning himself any farther in the affair.”", "\"순회재판의 때가 곧 다가왔고, 나는 인신보호영장에 따라 옥스퍼드로 옮겨졌습니다. 거기서 나는 영락없이 유죄 판결과 단죄를 받으리라 여겼지요. 그런데 놀랍게도 나를 고발하러 나서는 자가 아무도 없었고, 나는 회기가 끝날 무렵 기소가 없다는 이유로 풀려났습니다. 요컨대, 내 한방 동무가 옥스퍼드를 떠났던 것이지요. 게으름 탓이었는지 다른 무슨 까닭이었는지는 모르겠으나, 그는 그 일에 더는 끼어들기를 그만두었던 겁니다.\""]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"아마,\""], ["cries Partridge, “he did not care to have your blood upon his hands; and he was in the right on't.", "하고 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"그자는 당신 피를 제 손에 묻히고 싶지 않았던 게지요."], ["If any person was to be hanged upon my evidence, I should never be able to lie alone afterwards, for fear of seeing his ghost.”", "그게 옳았고요. 만약 내 증언으로 누가 교수형에 처해진다면, 나는 그 귀신이 보일까 무서워서 그 뒤로 다시는 혼자서는 못 누워 잘 겁니다.\""]], [["“I shall shortly doubt, Partridge,”", "\"파트리지,\" 하고 존스가 말했다."], ["says Jones, “whether thou art more brave or wise.”--“You may laugh at me, sir, if you please,”", "\"이제 곧 네가 더 용감한 자인지 더 지혜로운 자인지 헷갈리겠구나.\" \"정 그러시면 비웃으셔도 좋습니다, 도련님.\""], ["answered Partridge; “but if you will hear a very short story which I can tell, and which is most certainly true, perhaps you may change your opinion.", "하고 파트리지가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 제가 들려드릴 수 있는, 아주 짧고 또 틀림없이 사실인 이야기를 들으시면, 어쩌면 생각이 바뀌실지도 모르지요."], ["In the parish where I was born--”", "제가 태어난 교구에서는—\""], ["Here Jones would have silenced him; but the stranger interceded that he might be permitted to tell his story, and in the meantime promised to recollect the remainder of his own.", "여기서 존스가 그를 입 다물게 하려 했으나, 낯선 이가 그에게 이야기를 마저 하도록 허락해 주자고 거들며, 그동안 자신은 나머지 이야기를 가다듬어 두겠노라 약속했다."]], [["Partridge then proceeded thus: “In the parish where I was born, there lived a farmer whose name was Bridle, and he had a son named Francis, a good hopeful young fellow: I was at the grammar-school with him, where I remember he was got into Ovid's Epistles, and he could construe you three lines together sometimes without looking into a dictionary.", "그리하여 파트리지는 이렇게 이어 갔다. \"제가 태어난 교구에는 브라이들이라는 이름의 농부가 살았는데, 그에게 프랜시스라는 아들이 있었습니다."], ["Besides all this, he was a very good lad, never missed church o'", "장래가 촉망되는 착한 젊은이였지요. 저는 그와 같이 문법학교에 다녔는데, 거기서 그가 오비디우스의 《서한집》까지 나아간 게 기억나고, 때로는 사전 한번 안 들여다보고도 세 줄을 잇달아 해석해 내기도 했지요."], ["Sundays, and was reckoned one of the best psalm-singers in the whole parish.", "게다가 아주 착한 녀석이라, 일요일에 교회를 거른 적이 없고, 온 교구를 통틀어 손꼽히는 시편 찬송가 잘하는 이로 쳤습니다."], ["He would indeed now and then take a cup too much, and that was the only fault he had.”--“Well, but come to the ghost,”", "정말이지 이따금 술을 한 잔 과하게 들이켜는 게, 그가 가진 단 하나뿐인 흠이었지요.\" \"그래, 알았으니 귀신 이야기로 넘어가게.\""], ["cries Jones.", "하고 존스가 외쳤다."], ["“Never fear, sir; I shall come to him soon enough,”", "\"걱정 마십시오, 도련님. 곧 그자에게 이를 테니까요.\""], ["answered Partridge.", "하고 파트리지가 대답했다."], ["“You must know, then, that farmer Bridle lost a mare, a sorrel one, to the best of my remembrance; and so it fell out that this young Francis shortly afterward being at a fair at Hindon, and as I think it was on--, I can't remember the day; and being as he was, what should he happen to meet but a man upon his father's mare.", "\"그러니까 말입니다, 농부 브라이들이 암말 한 마리를 잃었는데, 제 기억으론 밤색 말이었지요. 한데 마침 이 젊은 프랜시스가 얼마 안 가 힌든의 장에 갔는데, 제 생각엔 그게—날짜는 기억이 안 납니다만, 아무튼 거기서 무슨 일이 있었는고 하니, 글쎄 제 아버지 암말을 탄 사내를 떡하니 마주친 게 아니겠습니까."], ["Frank called out presently, Stop thief; and it being in the middle of the fair, it was impossible, you know, for the man to make his escape.", "프랭크는 대뜸 '도둑 잡아라!' 하고 외쳤고, 장 한복판이었던지라 그 사내는 도무지 달아날 수가 없었지요, 아시다시피요."], ["So they apprehended him and carried him before the justice: I remember it was Justice Willoughby, of Noyle, a very worthy good gentleman; and he committed him to prison, and bound Frank in a recognisance, I think they call it--a hard word compounded of re and cognosco; but it differs in its meaning from the use of the simple, as many other compounds do.", "그래서 사람들이 그를 붙잡아 치안판사 앞으로 데려갔습니다. 노일의 윌러비 판사였던 게 기억나는데, 아주 훌륭하고 좋은 신사였지요."], ["Well, at last down came my Lord Justice Page to hold the assizes; and so the fellow was had up, and Frank was had up for a witness.", "그가 그 사내를 감옥에 가두고, 프랭크에게는—그걸 뭐라더라, 출두서약이라고 하던가요—그런 걸 받았습니다. '레(re)'와 '코그노스코(cognosco)'를 합친 어려운 낱말인데, 다른 여러 합성어가 그렇듯 그 뜻이 단순한 본디말 쓰임과는 다르지요."], ["To be sure, I shall never forget the face of the judge, when he began to ask him what he had to say against the prisoner.", "자, 마침내 페이지 판사 나리께서 순회재판을 열러 내려오셨고, 그래서 그 사내가 끌려 나오고 프랭크는 증인으로 불려 나왔습니다. 확실히 저는 판사가 그에게 죄수에 대해 할 말이 무엇이냐고 묻기 시작했을 때 그 얼굴을 영영 못 잊을 겁니다."], ["He made poor Frank tremble and shake in his shoes.", "가엾은 프랭크는 신발 속에서 다리가 다 후들거리고 떨렸지요. '자, 이보게,'"], ["`Well you, fellow,'", "하고 나리가 말합니다."], ["says my lord, `what have you to say?", "'할 말이 무엇인가? 우물쭈물 더듬거리지 말고 똑똑히 말하게.'"], ["Don't stand humming and hawing, but speak out.'", "하지만 그러고는 곧 프랭크에게는 더없이 정중하게 굴면서, 그 사내를 향해 천둥같이 호통치기 시작했습니다. 그리고 사내에게 변명할 말이 있느냐고 묻자, 사내는 말을 주웠노라고 했지요."], ["But, however, he soon turned altogether as civil to Frank, and began to thunder at the fellow; and when he asked him if he had anything to say for himself, the fellow said, he had found the horse.", "'허!' 하고 판사가 답했습니다."], ["`Ay!'", "'운도 좋은 놈이로구나."], ["answered the judge, `thou art a lucky fellow: I have travelled the circuit these forty years, and never found a horse in my life: but I'll tell thee what, friend, thou wast more lucky than thou didst know of; for thou didst not only find a horse, but a halter too, I promise thee.'", "나는 이 순회재판 길을 사십 년이나 다녔어도 평생 말 한 마리 주워 본 적이 없거늘. 한데 친구, 내 일러두지."], ["To be sure, I shall never forget the word.", "자네는 자네가 안 것보다 더 운이 좋았어. 말만 주운 게 아니라 올가미도 같이 주웠으니 말일세, 내 장담하지.'"], ["Upon which everybody fell a laughing, as how could they help it?", "정말이지 저는 그 말을 영영 못 잊을 겁니다. 그 말에 다들 웃음보를 터뜨렸지요."], ["Nay, and twenty other jests he made, which I can't remember now.", "어떻게 안 웃을 수가 있겠습니까? 아니, 그 밖에도 우스갯소리를 스무 가지는 더 했는데 지금은 다 기억나지 않습니다."], ["There was something about his skill in horse-flesh which made all the folks laugh.", "그가 말고기 보는 안목에 관해 뭐라 한 게 있어서 사람들이 다 웃었지요. 확실히 그 판사는 학식이 깊은 분일 뿐 아니라, 아주 담대한 분이었음에 틀림없습니다."], ["To be certain, the judge must have been a very brave man, as well as a man of much learning.", "정말이지 목숨이 걸린 재판을 듣는 건 더없이 흥미진진한 구경거리지요. 한 가지, 제가 좀 가혹하다 여긴 게 있는데, 죄수의 변호인이 그를 위해 변론하는 게 허락되지 않았다는 겁니다."], ["It is indeed charming sport to hear trials upon life and death.", "그저 아주 짧은 한마디만 들어 달라 청했는데도, 나리께서는 들으려 하지 않으셨지요. 정작 그를 반박하는 변호인이 반 시간 넘게 떠드는 건 그냥 두셨으면서요."], ["One thing I own I thought a little hard, that the prisoner's counsel was not suffered to speak for him, though he desired only to be heard one very short word, but my lord would not hearken to him, though he suffered a counsellor to talk against him for above half-an-hour.", "솔직히 좀 가혹하다 여겼습니다, 그런 자들이 그렇게나 많다는 게요. 나리에, 법정에, 배심원에, 변호인들에, 증인들까지, 죄다 가엾은 한 사람한테 달려드는데, 게다가 그 사람은 쇠사슬까지 차고 있었으니까요."], ["I thought it hard, I own, that there should be so many of them; my lord, and the court, and the jury, and the counsellors, and the witnesses, all upon one poor man, and he too in chains.", "자, 그 사내는 교수형을 당했지요. 어차피 달리될 수가 없었으니까요."], ["Well, the fellow was hanged, as to be sure it could be no otherwise, and poor Frank could never be easy about it.", "그리고 가엾은 프랭크는 그 일로 도무지 마음이 편할 수가 없었습니다. 어두운 데 혼자 있기만 하면 그 사내의 혼령이 보이는 것 같았으니까요.\""], ["He never was in the dark alone, but he fancied he saw the fellow's spirit.”--“Well, and is this thy story?”", "\"그래, 이게 자네 이야기인가?\" 하고 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones.", "\"아니요, 아니요.\" 하고 파트리지가 답했다."], ["“No, no,”", "\"오, 주여, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서! 이제 막 본론으로 들어가려는 참입니다."], ["answered Partridge.", "어느 날 밤, 술집에서 돌아오는데, 길고 좁고 어두운 골목길에서 그가 그 사내와 정통으로 맞부딪쳤지 뭡니까. 그 혼령은 온통 흰옷을 두르고 프랭크에게 덤벼들었고, 다부진 녀석이었던 프랭크도 그 혼령에게 맞서 덤볐지요."], ["“O Lord have mercy upon me!", "그래서 둘이 거기서 한바탕 드잡이를 벌였는데, 가엾은 프랭크는 끔찍하게 두들겨 맞았습니다. 정말이지 그는 겨우겨우 기다시피 해서 집에 당도했지요."], ["I am just now coming to the matter; for one night, coming from the alehouse, in a long, narrow, dark lane, there he ran directly up against him; and the spirit was all in white, and fell upon Frank; and Frank, who was a sturdy lad, fell upon the spirit again, and there they had a tussel together, and poor Frank was dreadfully beat: indeed he made a shift at last to crawl home; but what with the beating, and what with the fright, he lay ill above a fortnight; and all this is most certainly true, and the whole parish will bear witness to it.”", "한데 두들겨 맞은 데다 놀라기까지 한 탓에, 보름 넘게 앓아누웠습니다. 이게 다 틀림없는 사실이고, 온 교구가 증인이 되어 줄 겁니다.\""]], [["The stranger smiled at this story, and Jones burst into a loud fit of laughter; upon which Partridge cried, “Ay, you may laugh, sir; and so did some others, particularly a squire, who is thought to be no better than an atheist; who, forsooth, because there was a calf with a white face found dead in the same lane the next morning, would fain have it that the battle was between Frank and that, as if a calf would set upon a man. Besides, Frank told me he knew it to be a spirit, and could swear to him in any court in Christendom; and he had not drank above a quart or two or such a matter of liquor, at the time. Lud have mercy upon us, and keep us all from dipping our hands in blood, I say!”", "낯선 이는 이 이야기에 빙그레 웃었고, 존스는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러자 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"암요, 웃으셔도 좋습니다, 도련님. 다른 몇몇도 그랬으니까요. 특히 어떤 향사 하나는, 무신론자나 다름없다고들 하는 자인데, 글쎄 그 이튿날 아침 같은 골목에서 흰 얼굴을 한 송아지 한 마리가 죽어 있는 게 발견됐다는 이유로, 그 싸움이 프랭크와 그 송아지 사이에 벌어진 거라고 우겨 댔지 뭡니까. 마치 송아지가 사람한테 덤비기라도 한다는 듯이요. 게다가 프랭크는 제게, 그게 혼령인 줄 똑똑히 알았고 기독교 세계 어느 법정에서든 그걸 두고 맹세할 수 있다고 했습니다. 게다가 그때 그가 들이켠 술이라곤 한두 쿼트 남짓, 그 정도밖에 안 됐고요. 주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기시고, 우리 모두가 피에 손 담그는 일이 없게 지켜 주소서, 저는 그렇게 빕니다!\""]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"자, 그럼,\""], ["said Jones to the stranger, “Mr Partridge hath finished his story, and I hope will give you no future interruption, if you will be so kind to proceed.”", "하고 존스가 낯선 이에게 말했다. \"파트리지가 이야기를 끝냈으니, 부디 친절히 이어 가 주신다면 앞으로는 더 끼어들지 않으리라 믿습니다.\""], ["He then resumed his narration; but as he hath taken breath for a while, we think proper to give it to our reader, and shall therefore put an end to this chapter.", "그러자 그는 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러나 그가 잠시 숨을 고른 만큼, 우리도 독자에게 그 틈을 드리는 것이 마땅하다고 여겨, 이로써 이 장을 마치고자 한다."]], [["In which the Man of the Hill continues his history.", "「이제 나는 자유를 되찾았소.」"], ["“I had now regained my liberty,”", "낯선 사내가 말했다."], ["said the stranger; “but I had lost my reputation; for there is a wide difference between the case of a man who is barely acquitted of a crime in a court of justice, and of him who is acquitted in his own heart, and in the opinion of the people.", "「하지만 평판은 잃어버렸지요. 법정에서 겨우 무죄 판결을 받은 사람과, 제 양심 속에서 그리고 사람들의 평가 속에서 무죄가 된 사람 사이에는 커다란 차이가 있으니 말이오."], ["I was conscious of my guilt, and ashamed to look any one in the face; so resolved to leave Oxford the next morning, before the daylight discovered me to the eyes of any beholders.", "나는 내 죄를 스스로 의식하고 있었고, 누구의 얼굴도 똑바로 볼 면목이 없었소. 그래서 이튿날 아침, 날이 밝아 누구의 눈에라도 띄기 전에 옥스퍼드를 떠나기로 마음먹었소."]], [["“When I had got clear of the city, it first entered into my head to return home to my father, and endeavour to obtain his forgiveness; but as I had no reason to doubt his knowledge of all which had past, and as I was well assured of his great aversion to all acts of dishonesty, I could entertain no hopes of being received by him, especially since I was too certain of all the good offices in the power of my mother; nay, had my father's pardon been as sure, as I conceived his resentment to be, I yet question whether I could have had the assurance to behold him, or whether I could, upon any terms, have submitted to live and converse with those who, I was convinced, knew me to have been guilty of so base an action.", "「도시를 벗어나자 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각은, 집으로 돌아가 아버지께 용서를 구해 보자는 것이었소. 그러나 그동안 일어난 모든 일을 아버지가 모르실 리 없다고 여길 만했고, 아버지가 온갖 부정한 행위를 몹시 혐오하신다는 것 또한 잘 알고 있던 터라, 그분께 받아들여지리라는 희망은 도무지 품을 수 없었소. 더욱이 어머니의 힘이 미치는 온갖 술책을 너무도 확신하고 있었으니 말이오. 아니, 설령 아버지의 용서가 내가 짐작한 그분의 노여움만큼이나 확실했다 하더라도, 과연 내가 그분의 얼굴을 마주할 만한 뻔뻔함을 지닐 수 있었을지, 혹은 어떤 조건으로든, 내가 그토록 비열한 짓을 저질렀음을 알고 있다고 내가 확신하는 사람들과 함께 살며 이야기를 나누는 데 굴복할 수 있었을지 의문이오."]], [["“I hastened therefore back to London, the best retirement of either grief or shame, unless for persons of a very public character; for here you have the advantage of solitude without its disadvantage, since you may be alone and in company at the same time; and while you walk or sit unobserved, noise, hurry, and a constant succession of objects, entertain the mind, and prevent the spirits from preying on themselves, or rather on grief or shame, which are the most unwholesome diet in the world; and on which (though there are many who never taste either but in public) there are some who can feed very plentifully and very fatally when alone.", "「그래서 나는 슬픔이든 수치든 가장 잘 숨어들 수 있는 곳, 런던으로 서둘러 돌아갔소. 다만 지극히 공적인 인물이 아닌 한에서 말이오. 이곳에서는 고독의 이점을 누리되 그 단점은 피할 수 있으니, 혼자이면서 동시에 무리 속에 있을 수 있기 때문이오. 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 걷거나 앉아 있는 동안에도, 소음과 분주함, 그리고 끊임없이 이어지는 볼거리가 마음을 즐겁게 해 주어, 정신이 스스로를 갉아먹지 않게, 아니 차라리 슬픔이나 수치를 갉아먹지 않게 막아 주오. 그것들은 세상에서 가장 해로운 양식이라, (사람들 앞에서가 아니고는 결코 그것을 맛보지 않는 이들도 많지만) 혼자 있을 때면 아주 배불리, 아주 치명적으로 그것을 먹어 치우는 이들도 있는 법이오."]], [["“But as there is scarce any human good without its concomitant evil, so there are people who find an inconvenience in this unobserving temper of mankind; I mean persons who have no money; for as you are not put out of countenance, so neither are you cloathed or fed by those who do not know you.", "「하지만 어떤 인간의 좋음이든 거기에 따라붙는 나쁨이 없는 경우란 거의 없으니, 인간이 지닌 이 무관심한 기질에서 불편을 느끼는 사람들도 있소. 다름 아니라 돈이 없는 사람들 말이오."], ["And a man may be as easily starved in Leadenhall-market as in the deserts of Arabia.", "남의 눈에 띄어 무안해질 일은 없지만, 그렇다고 당신을 모르는 사람들이 당신에게 옷을 입혀 주거나 먹여 주지도 않기 때문이오. 그러니 사람은 아라비아 사막에서나 리든홀 시장에서나 똑같이 쉽게 굶어 죽을 수 있는 법이오."]], [["“It was at present my fortune to be destitute of that great evil, as it is apprehended to be by several writers, who I suppose were overburthened with it, namely, money.”--“With submission, sir,”", "「당시 나는 그 크나큰 악, 다시 말해 돈이라는 것을 갖지 못하는 처지였소. 여러 작가들이 그것을 악이라 여겼는데, 짐작건대 그들은 그것에 너무 짓눌려 있었던 게지요.」—「외람되오나, 선생,」"], ["said Partridge, “I do not remember any writers who have called it malorum; but irritamenta malorum.", "파트리지가 말했다. 「저는 그것을 말로룸이라 부른 작가는 기억하지 못하옵고, 다만 이리타멘타 말로룸이라 했을 뿐이지요."], ["Effodiuntur opes, irritamenta malorum”--“Well, sir,”", "에포디운투르 오페스, 이리타멘타 말로룸.」*—「자, 선생,」 낯선 사내가 말을 이었다."], ["continued the stranger, “whether it be an evil, or only the cause of evil, I was entirely void of it, and at the same time of friends, and, as I thought, of acquaintance; when one evening, as I was passing through the Inner Temple, very hungry, and very miserable, I heard a voice on a sudden hailing me with great familiarity by my Christian name; and upon turning about, I presently recollected the person who so saluted me to have been my fellow-collegiate; one who had left the university above a year, and long before any of my misfortunes had befallen me.", "「그것이 악이든, 아니면 한낱 악의 원인일 뿐이든, 나는 그것이 완전히 비어 있었고, 동시에 벗도 없었으며, 내 생각으로는 아는 사람조차 없었소. 그러던 어느 날 저녁, 몹시 허기지고 몹시 비참한 채로 이너 템플을 지나고 있을 때, 별안간 어떤 목소리가 내 세례명을 아주 친근하게 부르며 나를 불러 세우는 것이었소."], ["This gentleman, whose name was Watson, shook me heartily by the hand; and expressing great joy at meeting me, proposed our immediately drinking a bottle together.", "돌아보니, 그렇게 인사를 건넨 사람이 내 대학 동기였음을 곧바로 알아보았소. 대학을 떠난 지 일 년이 넘은 사람으로, 내게 어떤 불행이 닥치기 훨씬 전에 떠난 친구였소."], ["I first declined the proposal, and pretended business, but as he was very earnest and pressing, hunger at last overcame my pride, and I fairly confessed to him I had no money in my pocket; yet not without framing a lie for an excuse, and imputing it to my having changed my breeches that morning.", "왓슨이라는 이름의 이 신사는 내 손을 진심으로 굳게 잡으며, 나를 만난 큰 기쁨을 표하더니, 당장 함께 술 한 병을 마시자고 청했소. 나는 처음엔 그 제안을 사양하며 볼일이 있는 척했으나, 그가 워낙 간곡하고 끈질기게 권하는 통에, 결국 허기가 내 자존심을 이겼소."], ["Mr Watson answered, `I thought, Jack, you and I had been too old acquaintance for you to mention such a matter.'", "그래서 나는 주머니에 한 푼도 없다고 솔직히 고백했는데, 다만 변명 삼아 거짓말 하나를 지어내어, 그날 아침 바지를 갈아입은 탓이라 둘러댔소. 왓슨 군이 대답하길, '이보게 잭, 자네와 나는 그런 일을 입에 올리기엔 너무 오랜 친구 사이라고 여겼는데.'"], ["He then took me by the arm, and was pulling me along; but I gave him very little trouble, for my own inclinations pulled me much stronger than he could do.", "그러더니 내 팔을 붙들고 끌고 가는 것이었소. 하지만 나는 그를 별로 힘들게 하지 않았으니, 내 마음이 그가 끄는 것보다 훨씬 더 세게 나를 끌어당기고 있었기 때문이오."]], [["“We then went into the Friars, which you know is the scene of all mirth and jollity.", "「그러고 나서 우리는 프라이어스로 들어갔는데, 아시다시피 그곳은 온갖 흥과 환락의 무대지요."], ["Here, when we arrived at the tavern, Mr Watson applied himself to the drawer only, without taking the least notice of the cook; for he had no suspicion but that I had dined long since.", "술집에 다다랐을 때 왓슨 군은 요리사에게는 눈길 한 번 주지 않고 술 따르는 사람에게만 말을 걸었소. 내가 한참 전에 저녁을 먹었으리라 의심치 않았던 까닭이오."], ["However, as the case was really otherwise, I forged another falsehood, and told my companion I had been at the further end of the city on business of consequence, and had snapt up a mutton-chop in haste; so that I was again hungry, and wished he would add a beef-steak to his bottle.”--“Some people,”", "그러나 실상은 그렇지 않았으므로, 나는 또 하나의 거짓을 꾸며 내어, 동료에게 도시 저편 끝에서 중대한 볼일을 보다가 양고기 한 조각을 급히 먹었을 뿐이라, 다시 허기가 진다며 술에 비프스테이크를 곁들여 주면 좋겠다고 말했소.」—「어떤 사람들은,」 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["cries Partridge, “ought to have good memories; or did you find just money enough in your breeches to pay for the mutton-chop?”--“Your observation is right,”", "「기억력이 좋아야 하는 법이지요. 아니면 그 양고기 값을 치를 만큼의 돈이 바지에 딱 들어 있기라도 했더이까?」—「자네 지적이 옳네.」"], ["answered the stranger, “and I believe such blunders are inseparable from all dealing in untruth.--But to proceed--I began now to feel myself extremely happy.", "낯선 사내가 대답했다. 「거짓말을 다루다 보면 그런 실수가 따라붙기 마련이라고 믿네.—하지만 이야기를 계속하자면—나는 이제 더없이 행복해지기 시작했소."], ["The meat and wine soon revived my spirits to a high pitch, and I enjoyed much pleasure in the conversation of my old acquaintance, the rather as I thought him entirely ignorant of what had happened at the university since his leaving it.", "고기와 술이 곧 내 기운을 한껏 되살려 주었고, 옛 친구와 나누는 대화에서 큰 즐거움을 누렸소. 더욱이 그가 떠난 뒤 대학에서 일어난 일을 그가 전혀 모르고 있다고 여겼던 터라 더욱 그러했소."]], [["“But he did not suffer me to remain long in this agreeable delusion; for taking a bumper in one hand, and holding me by the other, `Here, my boy,' cries he, `here's wishing you joy of your being so honourably acquitted of that affair laid to your charge.' I was thunderstruck with confusion at those words, which Watson observing, proceeded thus: `Nay, never be ashamed, man; thou hast been acquitted, and no one now dares call thee guilty; but, prithee, do tell me, who am thy friend--I hope thou didst really rob him? for rat me if it was not a meritorious action to strip such a sneaking, pitiful rascal; and instead of the two hundred guineas, I wish you had taken as many thousand. Come, come, my boy, don't be shy of confessing to me: you are not now brought before one of the pimps. D--n me if I don't honour you for it; for, as I hope for salvation, I would have made no manner of scruple of doing the same thing.'", "「하지만 그는 내가 이 흐뭇한 착각 속에 오래 머물도록 내버려 두지 않았소. 한 손에는 술잔을 가득 채워 들고, 다른 손으로는 나를 붙든 채 외치길, '자, 이보게,' 하더니, '자네에게 씌워졌던 그 일에서 그토록 명예롭게 무죄를 받은 것을 축하하며 한잔하세.' 그 말에 나는 어쩔 줄 모르고 벼락 맞은 듯 굳어 버렸소. 그걸 알아챈 왓슨이 이렇게 이어 말했소. '아니, 부끄러워할 것 없네, 이 사람아. 자네는 무죄를 받았고, 이제 누구도 감히 자네를 죄인이라 부르지 못해. 그러니 부디 자네 친구인 내게만은 말해 주게—정말로 그자를 털어먹은 게지? 그런 비굴하고 한심한 악당의 옷을 벗긴 건 칭찬받을 만한 일이거든. 이백 기니가 아니라 그만큼의 천 기니라도 챙겼더라면 좋았을 것을. 자, 자, 이 사람아, 내게 고백하는 걸 수줍어 말게. 자넨 지금 그 뚜쟁이 같은 자들 앞에 끌려 나온 게 아니잖나. 망할, 나는 그 일로 자네를 오히려 우러러본다네. 구원을 바라는 마음으로 맹세컨대, 나라도 똑같이 했을 때 조금도 거리낌이 없었을 테니.'"]], [["“This declaration a little relieved my abashment; and as wine had now somewhat opened my heart, I very freely acknowledged the robbery, but acquainted him that he had been misinformed as to the sum taken, which was little more than a fifth part of what he had mentioned.", "「이 말이 나의 무안함을 조금 덜어 주었소. 그리고 이제 술기운에 마음이 다소 풀린 터라, 나는 그 강도질을 아주 거리낌 없이 인정했소. 다만 그에게 알려 주기를, 빼앗은 액수에 관해서는 잘못 전해 들었으며, 실은 그가 말한 것의 오분의 일이 조금 넘는 정도였다고 했소."]], [["“`I am sorry for it with all my heart,'", "「'그것 참 진심으로 안타깝군,'"], ["quoth he, `and I wish thee better success another time.", "그가 말하길, '다음번엔 더 잘되길 바라네."], ["Though, if you will take my advice, you shall have no occasion to run any such risque.", "하기야 내 충고를 받아들인다면, 그런 위험을 무릅쓸 일은 없을 걸세."], ["Here,'", "자,'"], ["said he, taking some dice out of his pocket, `here's the stuff.", "하더니 그는 주머니에서 주사위 몇 개를 꺼내며, '여기 물건이 있네."], ["Here are the implements; here are the little doctors which cure the distempers of the purse.", "여기 도구가 있어. 여기, 지갑의 병을 고쳐 주는 작은 의사 양반들이 있다네."], ["Follow but my counsel, and I will show you a way to empty the pocket of a queer cull without any danger of the nubbing cheat.'”", "내 조언만 따르면, 교수대에 걸릴 위험 하나 없이 어수룩한 봉의 주머니를 비우는 길을 보여 주지.'」"]], [["“Nubbing cheat!”", "「교수대라!」"], ["cries Partridge: “pray, sir, what is that?”", "파트리지가 외쳤다. 「선생, 그게 대체 무엇이옵니까?」"]], [["“Why that, sir,”", "「그게 말이오, 선생,」 낯선 사내가 말했다."], ["says the stranger, “is a cant phrase for the gallows; for as gamesters differ little from highwaymen in their morals, so do they very much resemble them in their language.", "「교수대를 가리키는 은어요. 노름꾼들은 도덕에서 노상강도와 별반 다를 게 없으니, 말투에서도 그들과 무척 닮았지요."]], [["“We had now each drank our bottle, when Mr Watson said, the board was sitting, and that he must attend, earnestly pressing me at the same time to go with him and try my fortune.", "「우리는 이제 각자 한 병씩 비웠는데, 그때 왓슨 군이 노름판이 열렸으니 가 봐야 한다며, 동시에 나더러 함께 가서 운을 시험해 보자고 간곡히 졸랐소. 나는 주머니가 비었다고 이미 일러 준 터라, 지금은 그럴 형편이 못 됨을 그도 알지 않느냐고 대답했소."], ["I answered he knew that was at present out of my power, as I had informed him of the emptiness of my pocket.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그가 그토록 우정을 강하게 표하던 터라, 그런 목적으로 약간의 돈을 빌려 주겠노라 나설 줄 믿어 의심치 않았소."], ["To say the truth, I doubted not from his many strong expressions of friendship, but that he would offer to lend me a small sum for that purpose, but he answered, `Never mind that, man; e'en boldly run a levant'", "그러나 그는 이렇게 대답하더이다. '그런 건 신경 쓰지 말게, 이 사람아."], ["[Partridge was going to inquire the meaning of that word, but Jones stopped his mouth]: `but be circumspect as to the man.", "그냥 대담하게 레반트를 한 판 뛰어 보게.' [파트리지가 그 말의 뜻을 물으려 했으나, 존스가 그의 입을 막았다.] '다만 상대만은 신중히 고르게."], ["I will tip you the proper person, which may be necessary, as you do not know the town, nor can distinguish a rum cull from a queer one.”", "적당한 사람을 내가 슬쩍 점찍어 줄 테니. 자넨 이 도시를 모르고, 멀쩡한 봉과 어수룩한 봉을 구별하지도 못하니, 그게 필요할 걸세.'"]], [["“The bill was now brought, when Watson paid his share, and was departing.", "「이제 계산서가 나오자, 왓슨은 제 몫을 치르고 떠나려 했소."], ["I reminded him, not without blushing, of my having no money.", "나는 얼굴을 붉히며, 내게 돈이 없음을 그에게 상기시켰소."], ["He answered, `That signifies nothing; score it behind the door, or make a bold brush and take no notice.--Or--stay,'", "그가 대답하길, '그건 아무것도 아닐세. 문 뒤에다 외상으로 달아 놓든가, 아니면 대담하게 슬쩍 나가 버리고 모른 척하게.—아니—잠깐만,'"], ["says he; `I will go down-stairs first, and then do you take up my money, and score the whole reckoning at the bar, and I will wait for you at the corner.'", "하더니, '내가 먼저 아래층으로 내려갈 테니, 자네는 내 돈을 집어 들고, 계산은 전부 카운터에 외상으로 달아 두게. 나는 모퉁이에서 기다리고 있겠네.'"], ["I expressed some dislike at this, and hinted my expectations that he would have deposited the whole; but he swore he had not another sixpence in his pocket.", "나는 이게 다소 못마땅하다는 기색을 내비치며, 그가 전부 내놓을 줄 알았다고 넌지시 말했소. 그러나 그는 주머니에 육 펜스 한 닢도 더는 없다고 맹세하더이다."]], [["“He then went down, and I was prevailed on to take up the money and follow him, which I did close enough to hear him tell the drawer the reckoning was upon the table.", "「그러더니 그는 아래층으로 내려갔고, 나는 떠밀리듯 그 돈을 집어 들고 그를 뒤따랐소. 어찌나 바싹 따라붙었던지, 그가 술 따르는 사람에게 계산은 탁자 위에 있다고 말하는 것까지 들렸소."], ["The drawer past by me up-stairs; but I made such haste into the street, that I heard nothing of his disappointment, nor did I mention a syllable at the bar, according to my instructions.", "그 사람은 내 곁을 지나 위층으로 올라갔지만, 나는 어찌나 서둘러 거리로 나섰던지 그가 실망하는 소리는 듣지도 못했고, 일러 준 대로 카운터에서는 한마디도 꺼내지 않았소."]], [["“We now went directly to the gaming-table, where Mr Watson, to my surprize, pulled out a large sum of money and placed it before him, as did many others; all of them, no doubt, considering their own heaps as so many decoy birds, which were to intice and draw over the heaps of their neighbours.", "「우리는 이제 곧장 노름판으로 갔는데, 그곳에서 왓슨 군은 놀랍게도 큰돈을 꺼내 제 앞에 늘어놓았고, 다른 많은 이들도 그리했소. 그들 모두 분명, 제 돈 무더기를 일종의 미끼 새로 여겨, 그것으로 이웃의 돈 무더기를 꾀어내 끌어오려는 속셈이었던 게지요."]], [["“Here it would be tedious to relate all the freaks which Fortune, or rather the dice, played in this her temple.", "「이곳에서 운명, 아니 차라리 주사위가 제 신전에서 부린 온갖 변덕을 죄다 늘어놓자면 지루할 따름이오."], ["Mountains of gold were in a few moments reduced to nothing at one part of the table, and rose as suddenly in another.", "산더미 같던 금화가 탁자 한쪽에서는 순식간에 흔적도 없이 사라졌다가, 다른 한쪽에서는 또 그만큼 갑작스레 솟아올랐소."], ["The rich grew in a moment poor, and the poor as suddenly became rich; so that it seemed a philosopher could nowhere have so well instructed his pupils in the contempt of riches, at least he could nowhere have better inculcated the incertainty of their duration.", "부자가 한순간에 가난뱅이가 되고, 가난뱅이가 또 그만큼 갑작스레 부자가 되니, 어느 철학자라도 부의 덧없음을 그곳에서보다 더 잘 제자들에게 가르칠 곳은 없을 듯했소. 적어도 부가 지속되는 것의 불확실함을 그곳에서보다 더 잘 새겨 줄 곳은 없었을 게요."]], [["“For my own part, after having considerably improved my small estate, I at last entirely demolished it.", "「나로 말하자면, 내 작은 밑천을 상당히 불린 끝에, 결국 그것을 통째로 거덜내고 말았소."], ["Mr Watson too, after much variety of luck, rose from the table in some heat, and declared he had lost a cool hundred, and would play no longer.", "왓슨 군 역시 운이 갖가지로 오락가락하더니, 다소 열이 오른 채 탁자에서 일어나, 백 파운드를 고스란히 잃었으니 더는 안 하겠다고 선언했소."], ["Then coming up to me, he asked me to return with him to the tavern; but I positively refused, saying, I would not bring myself a second time into such a dilemma, and especially as he had lost all his money and was now in my own condition.", "그러더니 내게 다가와, 함께 술집으로 돌아가자고 청했소. 그러나 나는 단호히 거절하며, 또다시 그런 곤경에 빠지지 않겠다고, 더구나 그도 돈을 죄다 잃어 이제 나와 같은 처지가 아니냐고 말했소."], ["`Pooh!'", "'쳇!'"], ["says he, `I have just borrowed a couple of guineas of a friend, and one of them is at your service.'", "그가 말하길, '내 방금 친구한테서 두어 기니를 빌렸는데, 그중 하나는 자네 쓰라고 주지.'"], ["He immediately put one of them into my hand, and I no longer resisted his inclination.", "그러고는 즉시 그중 하나를 내 손에 쥐여 주었고, 나는 더는 그의 뜻을 거스르지 않았소."]], [["“I was at first a little shocked at returning to the same house whence we had departed in so unhandsome a manner; but when the drawer, with very civil address, told us, `he believed we had forgot to pay our reckoning,'", "「처음엔 그토록 떳떳치 못하게 빠져나왔던 바로 그 집으로 돌아가는 게 다소 꺼림칙했소. 그러나 술 따르는 사람이 아주 공손한 말씨로 우리에게 '계산을 깜빡 잊고 가신 듯합니다'"], ["I became perfectly easy, and very readily gave him a guinea, bid him pay himself, and acquiesced in the unjust charge which had been laid on my memory.", "하고 말하자, 나는 완전히 마음이 놓여, 선뜻 그에게 일 기니를 건네며 알아서 받으라 일렀고, 내 기억력에 씌워진 부당한 혐의를 잠자코 받아들였소."]], [["“Mr Watson now bespoke the most extravagant supper he could well think of; and though he had contented himself with simple claret before, nothing now but the most precious Burgundy would serve his purpose.", "「이제 왓슨 군은 그가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 사치스러운 저녁을 주문했소. 그리고 아까는 소박한 클라레로 만족하던 그가, 이제는 더없이 값진 부르고뉴가 아니고선 성에 차지 않는 것이었소."]], [["“Our company was soon encreased by the addition of several gentlemen from the gaming-table; most of whom, as I afterwards found, came not to the tavern to drink, but in the way of business; for the true gamesters pretended to be ill, and refused their glass, while they plied heartily two young fellows, who were to be afterwards pillaged, as indeed they were without mercy.", "「우리 자리에는 곧 노름판에서 온 여러 신사들이 더해져 일행이 불어났소. 나중에 알게 된 일이지만, 그들 대부분은 술을 마시러 술집에 온 게 아니라 장사 삼아 온 것이었소."], ["Of this plunder I had the good fortune to be a sharer, though I was not yet let into the secret.", "진짜 노름꾼들은 몸이 아픈 척하며 술잔을 마다하면서, 나중에 털어먹을 두 젊은이에게는 부지런히 술을 권했고, 과연 그 둘은 인정사정없이 털리고 말았소. 이 노략질에서 나도 운 좋게 한몫을 챙겼으나, 아직 그 속내까지는 알지 못했소."]], [["“There was one remarkable accident attended this tavern play; for the money by degrees totally disappeared; so that though at the beginning the table was half covered with gold, yet before the play ended, which it did not till the next day, being Sunday, at noon, there was scarce a single guinea to be seen on the table; and this was the stranger as every person present, except myself, declared he had lost; and what was become of the money, unless the devil himself carried it away, is difficult to determine.”", "「이 술집 노름에는 한 가지 묘한 일이 따라붙었소. 돈이 점차 깡그리 사라져 버린 것이오. 처음엔 탁자가 금화로 절반쯤 덮여 있었건만, 이튿날, 곧 일요일 정오까지 이어진 그 노름이 끝날 무렵에는 탁자 위에서 기니 한 닢조차 거의 볼 수 없었소. 게다가 더 이상한 것은, 나를 뺀 그 자리의 모든 사람이 저마다 잃었노라 단언했다는 점이오. 악마 자신이 그 돈을 가져가지 않은 한, 도대체 그것이 어디로 갔는지는 가늠하기 어려운 일이오.」"]], [["“Most certainly he did,”", "「틀림없이 악마가 가져갔지요.」 파트리지가 말했다."], ["says Partridge, “for evil spirits can carry away anything without being seen, though there were never so many folk in the room; and I should not have been surprized if he had carried away all the company of a set of wicked wretches, who were at play in sermon time.", "「악령은 방 안에 사람이 아무리 많아도 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않고 무엇이든 가져갈 수 있으니까요. 설교 시간에 노름을 하던 그 못된 무리를 통째로 데려갔대도 저는 놀라지 않았을 겁니다."], ["And I could tell you a true story, if I would, where the devil took a man out of bed from another man's wife, and carried him away through the keyhole of the door.", "그리고 마음만 먹으면 실화 하나를 들려드릴 수도 있는데요, 악마가 어떤 남자를 남의 아내 곁 잠자리에서 끌어내어 문 열쇠 구멍으로 데려가 버린 이야기랍니다. 그 일이 벌어진 바로 그 집을 제 눈으로 봤는데, 지난 삼십 년 동안 아무도 그 집에 살지 않았지요.」"], ["I've seen the very house where it was done, and nobody hath lived in it these thirty years.”", "존스는 파트리지의 주제넘은 말에 다소 언짢았으나, 그 순박함에 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었다. 낯선 사내도 마찬가지였고, 그러고 나서 다음 장에서 보게 될 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["Though Jones was a little offended by the impertinence of Partridge, he could not however avoid smiling at his simplicity.", "* 라틴어: \"재물이 캐어지나니, 그것이 악을 부추기는 자극제로다.\""], ["The stranger did the same, and then proceeded with his story, as will be seen in the next chapter.", "(오비디우스의 시구로, 돈을 악 그 자체가 아니라 악을 자극하는 것이라 한 표현.)"]], [["In which the foregoing story is farther continued.", "앞의 이야기가 계속 이어진다."]], [["“My fellow-collegiate had now entered me in a new scene of life. I soon became acquainted with the whole fraternity of sharpers, and was let into their secrets; I mean, into the knowledge of those gross cheats which are proper to impose upon the raw and unexperienced; for there are some tricks of a finer kind, which are known only to a few of the gang, who are at the head of their profession; a degree of honour beyond my expectation; for drink, to which I was immoderately addicted, and the natural warmth of my passions, prevented me from arriving at any great success in an art which requires as much coolness as the most austere school of philosophy.", "“이제 그 대학 동무가 나를 전혀 새로운 인생의 무대로 끌어들였습니다. 나는 곧 사기꾼 무리 전체와 안면을 트게 되었고, 그들의 비밀까지 알게 되었습니다. 다시 말해, 풋내기와 경험 없는 자들을 속이기에 알맞은, 그 조잡한 속임수들의 지식 속으로 들어선 것이지요. 더 정교한 부류의 술수들도 있는데, 그것은 이 직업의 정점에 선 몇몇 두목 패거리만이 아는 것이었습니다. 그건 내가 기대했던 것 이상의 영예였지요. 술이라면 내가 도를 넘게 빠져 있었고, 또 타고난 격정의 뜨거움 탓에, 가장 엄격한 철학 학파만큼이나 냉정함을 요하는 그 기술에서 나는 큰 성공에 이르지 못했으니까요."]], [["“Mr Watson, with whom I now lived in the closest amity, had unluckily the former failing to a very great excess; so that instead of making a fortune by his profession, as some others did, he was alternately rich and poor, and was often obliged to surrender to his cooler friends, over a bottle which they never tasted, that plunder that he had taken from culls at the public table.", "“이제 더없이 가까운 우정을 나누며 함께 지내던 왓슨 씨는 불행하게도 앞서 말한 그 결함, 즉 술에 지나치게 빠져 있었습니다. 그래서 어떤 자들처럼 그 직업으로 재산을 모으기는커녕, 부유했다가 가난했다가를 번갈아 오갔고, 공개 도박판에서 호구들에게서 뜯어낸 그 노획물을, 자신들은 입도 대지 않는 술병을 앞에 두고 더 냉정한 동료들에게 자주 내놓아야 했지요."]], [["“However, we both made a shift to pick up an uncomfortable livelihood; and for two years I continued of the calling; during which time I tasted all the varieties of fortune, sometimes flourishing in affluence, and at others being obliged to struggle with almost incredible difficulties.", "“그래도 우리 둘은 그럭저럭 불편한 생계를 꾸려 나갔습니다. 그렇게 나는 이 년 동안 그 노릇을 이어 갔지요."], ["To-day wallowing in luxury, and to-morrow reduced to the coarsest and most homely fare.", "그동안 나는 온갖 종류의 운명을 다 맛보았습니다. 어느 때는 풍족함 속에서 번성하다가, 어느 때는 거의 믿기 힘든 곤경과 씨름해야 했지요."], ["My fine clothes being often on my back in the evening, and at the pawn-shop the next morning.", "오늘은 사치 속에 뒹굴다가, 내일은 가장 거칠고 초라한 끼니로 내몰리는 식이었습니다. 저녁이면 등에 걸쳐져 있던 멋진 옷이, 이튿날 아침이면 전당포에 가 있는 일이 잦았지요."]], [["“One night, as I was returning pennyless from the gaming-table, I observed a very great disturbance, and a large mob gathered together in the street.", "“어느 날 밤, 도박판에서 한 푼 없이 빈손으로 돌아오던 길에, 나는 큰 소동이 일어나고 거리에 많은 군중이 몰려 있는 것을 보았습니다. 나로서는 소매치기에게 잃을 것이 없었으므로, 나는 인파 속으로 과감히 끼어들었습니다."], ["As I was in no danger from pickpockets, I ventured into the croud, where upon enquiry I found that a man had been robbed and very ill used by some ruffians.", "그러고 물어보니, 어떤 사람이 강도질을 당하고 불한당들에게 심하게 당했다는 것이었습니다. 그 다친 사람은 피투성이로 보였고, 제 다리로 몸을 지탱하기조차 힘들어 보였습니다."], ["The wounded man appeared very bloody, and seemed scarce able to support himself on his legs.", "지금의 내 삶과 어울림이 정직함도 수치심도 거의 남겨 놓지 않았지만, 그래도 인간애만큼은 앗아 가지 않았던 터라, 나는 즉시 그 불행한 사람에게 도움을 내밀었습니다."], ["As I had not therefore been deprived of my humanity by my present life and conversation, though they had left me very little of either honesty or shame, I immediately offered my assistance to the unhappy person, who thankfully accepted it, and, putting himself under my conduct, begged me to convey him to some tavern, where he might send for a surgeon, being, as he said, faint with loss of blood.", "그는 고맙게 그것을 받아들이며 내 인도에 몸을 맡겼고, 어느 선술집으로든 자신을 데려가 외과의를 부를 수 있게 해 달라고 청했습니다. 피를 너무 흘려 정신이 아득하다는 것이었지요."], ["He seemed indeed highly pleased at finding one who appeared in the dress of a gentleman; for as to all the rest of the company present, their outside was such that he could not wisely place any confidence in them.", "그는 신사 차림으로 보이는 사람을 만난 것을 참으로 기뻐하는 듯했습니다. 그 자리에 있던 나머지 사람들로 말하자면, 겉모습이 그러해서 그가 도무지 분별 있게 믿음을 둘 수 없었으니까요."]], [["“I took the poor man by the arm, and led him to the tavern where we kept our rendezvous, as it happened to be the nearest at hand.", "“나는 그 가엾은 사람의 팔을 부축하여, 마침 가장 가까이에 있던, 우리가 모이던 그 선술집으로 데려갔습니다."], ["A surgeon happening luckily to be in the house, immediately attended, and applied himself to dressing his wounds, which I had the pleasure to hear were not likely to be mortal.", "운 좋게도 외과의 한 사람이 그 집에 있어 곧바로 와서, 그의 상처를 손보는 일에 매달렸습니다. 그리고 나는 그 상처가 목숨을 앗아 갈 것 같지는 않다는 말을 들어 기뻤지요."]], [["“The surgeon having very expeditiously and dextrously finished his business, began to enquire in what part of the town the wounded man lodged; who answered, `That he was come to town that very morning; that his horse was at an inn in Piccadilly, and that he had no other lodging, and very little or no acquaintance in town.'", "“외과의가 아주 신속하고 능숙하게 일을 마치고 나서, 다친 사람에게 도시의 어느 동네에 묵고 있느냐고 묻기 시작했습니다. 그는 ‘바로 그날 아침에 도시에 막 도착했고, 말은 피카딜리의 어느 여관에 매어 두었으며, 달리 묵을 곳도 없고, 도시에 아는 사람도 거의 또는 전혀 없다’고 대답했습니다."]], [["“This surgeon, whose name I have forgot, though I remember it began with an R, had the first character in his profession, and was serjeant-surgeon to the king.", "“이 외과의는—이름은 잊었지만 R로 시작했던 것은 기억합니다—그 직업에서 으뜸가는 명성을 지녔고, 국왕의 시의(侍醫)였습니다."], ["He had moreover many good qualities, and was a very generous good-natured man, and ready to do any service to his fellow-creatures.", "게다가 그는 좋은 자질을 많이 갖춘, 매우 너그럽고 마음씨 고운 사람이어서, 동료 인간에게 무슨 도움이든 기꺼이 베푸는 사람이었지요."], ["He offered his patient the use of his chariot to carry him to his inn, and at the same time whispered in his ear, `That if he wanted any money, he would furnish him.'", "그는 자신의 환자에게 여관까지 데려다주도록 자기 마차를 쓰라고 권하면서, 동시에 그의 귀에 대고 ‘혹시 돈이 필요하면 마련해 주겠다’고 속삭였습니다."]], [["“The poor man was not now capable of returning thanks for this generous offer; for having had his eyes for some time stedfastly on me, he threw himself back in his chair, crying, `Oh, my son!", "“그 가엾은 사람은 이제 이 너그러운 제안에 감사를 표할 처지가 못 되었습니다. 한동안 나에게 눈길을 고정한 채 바라보고 있더니, 의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히며 ‘오, 내 아들!"], ["my son!' and then fainted away.", "내 아들!’ 하고 외치고는, 그대로 기절해 버렸으니까요."]], [["“Many of the people present imagined this accident had happened through his loss of blood; but I, who at the same time began to recollect the features of my father, was now confirmed in my suspicion, and satisfied that it was he himself who appeared before me.", "“그 자리에 있던 사람들 가운데 많은 이가 이 일이 피를 흘린 탓에 생긴 것이라 여겼습니다. 그러나 그와 동시에 아버지의 모습을 떠올리기 시작한 나는, 이제 내 의심이 확실해졌고, 내 앞에 나타난 이가 바로 아버지 그분이라는 것을 알게 되었습니다."], ["I presently ran to him, raised him in my arms, and kissed his cold lips with the utmost eagerness.", "나는 곧장 그에게 달려가 두 팔로 안아 일으키고, 더없이 간절하게 그 차가운 입술에 입을 맞추었습니다. 여기서 나는 차마 묘사할 수 없는 한 장면 위로 장막을 드리워야겠습니다."], ["Here I must draw a curtain over a scene which I cannot describe; for though I did not lose my being, as my father for a while did, my senses were however so overpowered with affright and surprize, that I am a stranger to what passed during some minutes, and indeed till my father had again recovered from his swoon, and I found myself in his arms, both tenderly embracing each other, while the tears trickled a-pace down the cheeks of each of us.", "아버지가 한동안 그러셨듯 정신을 잃지는 않았으나, 내 정신도 두려움과 놀라움에 너무나 짓눌려, 그 몇 분 동안 무슨 일이 있었는지 나로서는 알 길이 없으니까요. 정말이지 아버지가 다시 혼절에서 깨어나, 우리 둘이 서로의 품에 안겨 다정히 끌어안고, 두 사람의 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내리는 그 순간에 이르러서야 나는 정신을 차렸습니다."]], [["“Most of those present seemed affected by this scene, which we, who might be considered as the actors in it, were desirous of removing from the eyes of all spectators as fast as we could; my father therefore accepted the kind offer of the surgeon's chariot, and I attended him in it to his inn.", "“그 자리에 있던 사람들 대부분이 이 장면에 감동한 듯했습니다. 그 안의 배우라 할 만한 우리는, 가능한 한 빨리 모든 구경꾼의 눈에서 벗어나고 싶었지요. 그래서 아버지는 외과의의 친절한 마차 제안을 받아들였고, 나는 그 안에 함께 타 아버지를 여관까지 모셨습니다."]], [["“When we were alone together, he gently upbraided me with having neglected to write to him during so long a time, but entirely omitted the mention of that crime which had occasioned it.", "“우리 둘만 남게 되자, 아버지는 그토록 오랜 세월 동안 편지 한 통 쓰지 않고 소홀히 했다며 나를 부드럽게 나무라셨지만, 그 까닭이 된 그 죄에 대해서는 한마디도 꺼내지 않으셨습니다."], ["He then informed me of my mother's death, and insisted on my returning home with him, saying, `That he had long suffered the greatest anxiety on my account; that he knew not whether he had most feared my death or wished it, since he had so many more dreadful apprehensions for me.", "그러고는 어머니의 별세를 알려 주시며, 함께 집으로 돌아가자고 고집하셨지요. ‘오랫동안 나 때문에 더없는 근심에 시달렸으며, 나에 대해 그토록 더 끔찍한 걱정들을 품고 있었던 터라, 내가 죽기를 가장 두려워했는지 아니면 차라리 바랐는지 자신도 모르겠다’고 하셨습니다."], ["At last, he said, a neighbouring gentleman, who had just recovered a son from the same place, informed him where I was; and that to reclaim me from this course of life was the sole cause of his journey to London.'", "마침내, 같은 곳에서 막 아들 하나를 되찾은 이웃 신사가 내가 어디 있는지 알려 주었고, 이 방탕한 삶에서 나를 되돌려 오는 것만이 런던으로 온 유일한 이유라고 하셨습니다."], ["He thanked Heaven he had succeeded so far as to find me out by means of an accident which had like to have proved fatal to him; and had the pleasure to think he partly owed his preservation to my humanity, with which he profest himself to be more delighted than he should have been with my filial piety, if I had known that the object of all my care was my own father.", "아버지는 하마터면 목숨을 잃을 뻔했던 사고를 통해서나마 나를 찾아내게 된 것을 하늘에 감사하셨고, 자신이 목숨을 부지한 데에 부분적으로 내 인간애의 덕을 입었다고 생각하는 것을 기뻐하셨습니다. 그러면서, 만일 내가 그토록 보살핀 대상이 바로 내 아버지인 줄 알았더라면 효심에서 그리한 것이겠지만, 그것을 모른 채 베푼 인간애가 오히려 더 기쁘다고 하셨지요."]], [["“Vice had not so depraved my heart as to excite in it an insensibility of so much paternal affection, though so unworthily bestowed.", "“악덕이 내 마음을 그토록 더럽혀, 비록 받을 자격 없이 베풀어진 것일지언정 그토록 큰 부정(父情)에 무감각하도록 만들지는 못했습니다."], ["I presently promised to obey his commands in my return home with him, as soon as he was able to travel, which indeed he was in a very few days, by the assistance of that excellent surgeon who had undertaken his cure.", "나는 곧, 여행이 가능해지는 대로 함께 집으로 돌아가라는 아버지의 명을 따르겠다고 약속했습니다. 실제로 아버지는, 그의 치료를 맡았던 그 훌륭한 외과의의 도움으로 며칠 만에 여행할 수 있게 되었지요."]], [["“The day preceding my father's journey (before which time I scarce ever left him), I went to take my leave of some of my most intimate acquaintance, particularly of Mr Watson, who dissuaded me from burying myself, as he called it, out of a simple compliance with the fond desires of a foolish old fellow.", "“아버지가 길을 떠나기 전날(그때까지는 나는 아버지 곁을 거의 떠나지 않았습니다), 나는 가장 가까운 몇몇 지인에게, 특히 왓슨 씨에게 작별을 고하러 갔습니다. 그는, 그가 부르던 표현대로, 어리석은 늙은이의 어리석은 소망에 그저 순순히 따르느라 나 자신을 파묻어 버리지 말라며 만류했습니다."], ["Such sollicitations, however, had no effect, and I once more saw my own home.", "그러나 그런 권유는 아무 소용이 없었고, 나는 다시 한번 내 집을 보게 되었지요."], ["My father now greatly sollicited me to think of marriage; but my inclinations were utterly averse to any such thoughts.", "아버지는 이제 내게 결혼을 생각해 보라고 간곡히 권하셨습니다. 하지만 내 마음은 그런 생각이라면 도무지 내키지 않았습니다."], ["I had tasted of love already, and perhaps you know the extravagant excesses of that most tender and most violent passion.”--Here the old gentleman paused, and looked earnestly at Jones; whose countenance, within a minute's space, displayed the extremities of both red and white.", "나는 이미 사랑을 맛본 적이 있었고, 어쩌면 당신도 그 더없이 부드럽고 더없이 격렬한 정념의 무절제한 극단을 아실 테지요.”—여기서 노신사는 말을 멈추고 존스를 진지하게 바라보았다. 존스의 얼굴은 한순간에 새빨개졌다가 새하얘지는 양극단을 드러냈다."], ["Upon which the old man, without making any observations, renewed his narrative.", "그러나 노인은 아무 말도 덧붙이지 않고 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["“Being now provided with all the necessaries of life, I betook myself once again to study, and that with a more inordinate application than I had ever done formerly.", "“이제 생활에 필요한 온갖 것을 갖추게 된 나는, 다시 한번 학문에 몰두했습니다. 그것도 예전 그 어느 때보다 더 지나치리만치 열심이었지요."], ["The books which now employed my time solely were those, as well antient as modern, which treat of true philosophy, a word which is by many thought to be the subject only of farce and ridicule.", "이제 오로지 내 시간을 채운 책들은, 고대의 것이든 근대의 것이든, 참된 철학을 다룬 것들이었습니다. 많은 이가 그저 익살극과 조롱의 소재로나 여기는 그 ‘철학’ 말이지요."], ["I now read over the works of Aristotle and Plato, with the rest of those inestimable treasures which antient Greece had bequeathed to the world.", "나는 이제 아리스토텔레스와 플라톤의 저작들을, 그리고 고대 그리스가 세상에 물려준 그 헤아릴 수 없는 보배들의 나머지를 두루 읽었습니다."]], [["“These authors, though they instructed me in no science by which men may promise to themselves to acquire the least riches or worldly power, taught me, however, the art of despising the highest acquisitions of both.", "“이 저자들은, 비록 사람이 조금이라도 부나 세속의 권력을 얻으리라 기대할 만한 어떤 학문도 가르쳐 주지는 않았지만, 그 둘의 가장 높은 성취조차 경멸하는 기술만큼은 가르쳐 주었습니다."], ["They elevate the mind, and steel and harden it against the capricious invasions of fortune.", "그것들은 마음을 드높이고, 운명의 변덕스러운 침입에 맞서 마음을 단련하고 굳세게 합니다."], ["They not only instruct in the knowledge of Wisdom, but confirm men in her habits, and demonstrate plainly, that this must be our guide, if we propose ever to arrive at the greatest worldly happiness, or to defend ourselves, with any tolerable security, against the misery which everywhere surrounds and invests us.", "그것들은 지혜의 지식을 가르쳐 줄 뿐 아니라, 사람을 그 지혜의 습관 안에 단단히 세워 주며, 만일 우리가 가장 큰 세속의 행복에 이르고자 하거나, 어디서나 우리를 에워싸고 둘러싼 그 불행에 맞서 그런대로 안전하게 자신을 지키고자 한다면, 바로 이 지혜야말로 우리의 인도자가 되어야 함을 또렷이 일러 줍니다."]], [["“To this I added another study, compared to which, all the philosophy taught by the wisest heathens is little better than a dream, and is indeed as full of vanity as the silliest jester ever pleased to represent it.", "“여기에 나는 또 하나의 공부를 더했는데, 이에 비하면 가장 지혜로운 이교도들이 가르친 온갖 철학도 한낱 꿈만큼이나 못한 것이며, 실로 더없이 어리석은 익살꾼이 멋대로 그려 보인 것만큼이나 허망함으로 가득 찬 것입니다. 그것은 바로 성경 안에서만 찾을 수 있는 신성한 지혜입니다."], ["This is that Divine wisdom which is alone to be found in the Holy Scriptures; for they impart to us the knowledge and assurance of things much more worthy our attention than all which this world can offer to our acceptance; of things which Heaven itself hath condescended to reveal to us, and to the smallest knowledge of which the highest human wit unassisted could never ascend.", "성경은 이 세상이 우리에게 받아들이라 내놓을 수 있는 그 모든 것보다 훨씬 더 주목할 가치가 있는 것들의 지식과 확신을 우리에게 전해 주니까요. 그것은 하늘 그 자신이 우리에게 굽어살피사 드러내 주신 것들이며, 그 가장 작은 지식조차 도움 없는 가장 높은 인간의 지혜로는 결코 다다를 수 없는 것들입니다."], ["I began now to think all the time I had spent with the best heathen writers was little more than labour lost: for, however pleasant and delightful their lessons may be, or however adequate to the right regulation of our conduct with respect to this world only; yet, when compared with the glory revealed in Scripture, their highest documents will appear as trifling, and of as little consequence, as the rules by which children regulate their childish little games and pastime.", "나는 이제, 최고의 이교도 저자들과 보낸 그 모든 시간이 헛수고나 다름없었다고 여기기 시작했습니다. 그들의 가르침이 아무리 즐겁고 유쾌하다 한들, 또 오직 이 세상에 관한 우리 행동을 바르게 다스리는 데 아무리 알맞다 한들, 성경에 계시된 영광에 견주면, 그들의 가장 드높은 가르침조차 아이들이 어린애 같은 놀이와 소일을 정하는 규칙만큼이나 하찮고 보잘것없어 보일 테니까요."], ["True it is, that philosophy makes us wiser, but Christianity makes us better men.", "참으로, 철학은 우리를 더 지혜롭게 하지만, 기독교는 우리를 더 나은 사람으로 만듭니다."], ["Philosophy elevates and steels the mind, Christianity softens and sweetens it.", "철학은 마음을 드높이고 단련하지만, 기독교는 마음을 부드럽게 하고 다정하게 합니다."], ["The former makes us the objects of human admiration, the latter of Divine love.", "전자는 우리를 인간의 찬탄의 대상으로 만들지만, 후자는 신의 사랑의 대상으로 만들지요."], ["That insures us a temporal, but this an eternal happiness.--But I am afraid I tire you with my rhapsody.”", "전자는 우리에게 한때의 행복을 보장하지만, 후자는 영원한 행복을 보장합니다.—하지만 내 이 장광설로 당신을 지치게 하는 건 아닌지 모르겠군요.”"]], [["“Not at all,”", "“전혀 아닙니다,”"], ["cries Partridge; “Lud forbid we should be tired with good things!”", "파트리지가 외쳤다. “좋은 이야기에 우리가 지치다니, 그런 일이 있어서야 되겠습니까!”"]], [["“I had spent,”", "“나는,”"], ["continued the stranger, “about four years in the most delightful manner to myself, totally given up to contemplation, and entirely unembarrassed with the affairs of the world, when I lost the best of fathers, and one whom I so entirely loved, that my grief at his loss exceeds all description.", "낯선 이가 말을 이었다, “약 사 년을, 나로서는 더없이 즐거운 방식으로 보냈습니다. 온전히 사색에 몸을 맡기고, 세상사에 조금도 시달리지 않은 채로 말이지요."], ["I now abandoned my books, and gave myself up for a whole month to the effects of melancholy and despair.", "그러던 차에 나는 더없이 훌륭한 아버지를, 그토록 온 마음으로 사랑했던 그분을 잃었습니다. 그 상실의 슬픔은 도무지 무어라 말로 다 할 수가 없습니다."], ["Time, however, the best physician of the mind, at length brought me relief.”--“Ay, ay; Tempus edax rerum”", "나는 이제 책을 내던지고, 꼬박 한 달을 우울과 절망의 손아귀에 나를 내맡겼지요. 그러나 마음의 가장 좋은 의사인 시간이 마침내 내게 위안을 가져다주었습니다.”—“그렇지요, 그렇고말고요."], ["said Partridge.--“I then,”", "‘템푸스 에닥스 레룸(시간은 만물을 삼키는 것).’” 파트리지가 말했다.—“그래서 나는,”"], ["continued the stranger, “betook myself again to my former studies, which I may say perfected my cure; for philosophy and religion may be called the exercises of the mind, and when this is disordered, they are as wholesome as exercise can be to a distempered body.", "낯선 이가 말을 이었다, “다시 예전의 공부로 돌아갔고, 그것이 나를 온전히 낫게 했다고 할 수 있습니다. 철학과 종교는 마음의 운동이라 부를 수 있는데, 마음이 흐트러졌을 때 그것들은 병든 몸에 운동이 그러하듯 이롭기 때문이지요."], ["They do indeed produce similar effects with exercise; for they strengthen and confirm the mind, till man becomes, in the noble strain of Horace--", "그것들은 실로 운동과 비슷한 효과를 냅니다. 마음을 튼튼히 하고 굳건히 다져, 마침내 사람이 호라티우스의 그 고귀한 가락처럼 되게 하니까요—"]], [["Fortis, et in seipso totus teres atque rotundus, Externi ne quid valeat per laeve morari; In quem manca ruit semper Fortuna.“[*]", "포르티스, 에트 인 세입소 토투스 테레스 아트퀘 로툰두스,\n엑스테르니 네 퀴드 발레아트 페르 라이베 모라리;"]], [["[*] Firm in himself, who on himself relies, Polish'd and round, who runs his proper course And breaks misfortunes with superior force.--MR FRANCIS.", "인 퀘엠 만카 루이트 셈페르 포르투나.”[*]\n[*] 스스로 안에 굳세게 서서, 자신에게 기대고,"]], [["Here Jones smiled at some conceit which intruded itself into his imagination; but the stranger, I believe, perceived it not, and proceeded thus:--", "반들반들 둥글게 다듬어져, 제 길을 달리며\n남보다 큰 힘으로 불운을 부수는 사람.—프랜시스 씨 역."]], [["“My circumstances were now greatly altered by the death of that best of men; for my brother, who was now become master of the house, differed so widely from me in his inclinations, and our pursuits in life had been so very various, that we were the worst of company to each other: but what made our living together still more disagreeable, was the little harmony which could subsist between the few who resorted to me, and the numerous train of sportsmen who often attended my brother from the field to the table; for such fellows, besides the noise and nonsense with which they persecute the ears of sober men, endeavour always to attack them with affront and contempt. This was so much the case, that neither I myself, nor my friends, could ever sit down to a meal with them without being treated with derision, because we were unacquainted with the phrases of sportsmen. For men of true learning, and almost universal knowledge, always compassionate the ignorance of others; but fellows who excel in some little, low, contemptible art, are always certain to despise those who are unacquainted with that art.", "여기서 존스는 제 상상 속으로 비집고 든 어떤 익살스러운 생각에 빙긋 웃었다. 그러나 낯선 이는 그것을 눈치채지 못한 듯, 이렇게 이야기를 이어 갔다.—\n“그 더없이 훌륭한 분의 죽음으로 내 형편은 이제 크게 달라졌습니다. 이제 그 집의 주인이 된 형은 성향이 나와 너무나 달랐고, 우리의 인생 행로가 워낙 판이했던 탓에, 우리는 서로에게 더없이 못된 길동무였으니까요. 그런데 함께 사는 것을 한층 더 거북하게 만든 것은, 나를 찾아오는 몇 안 되는 이들과, 들판에서 식탁까지 형을 자주 따라다니는 그 수많은 사냥꾼 무리 사이에 도무지 화합이라곤 있을 수 없다는 점이었습니다. 그런 자들은 진중한 이들의 귀를 시끄러움과 헛소리로 괴롭히는 것도 모자라, 늘 모욕과 경멸로 그들을 공격하려 들었으니까요. 그 정도가 어찌나 심했던지, 나든 내 벗들이든 그들과 한 끼라도 함께 앉으면 으레 조롱을 당하기 일쑤였습니다. 우리가 사냥꾼들의 은어를 모른다는 이유에서였지요. 참된 학식과 거의 두루 통하는 지식을 갖춘 사람은 늘 남의 무지를 가엾이 여기는 법이지만, 어떤 자질구레하고 천하며 하찮은 기술에 뛰어난 자들은 영락없이 그 기술을 모르는 이들을 업신여기게 마련이니까요."]], [["“In short, we soon separated, and I went, by the advice of a physician, to drink the Bath waters; for my violent affliction, added to a sedentary life, had thrown me into a kind of paralytic disorder, for which those waters are accounted an almost certain cure.", "“요컨대, 우리는 곧 갈라섰고, 나는 한 의사의 권유에 따라 바스의 광천수를 마시러 갔습니다. 격렬한 비탄에 앉아만 지내는 생활까지 더해져, 나는 일종의 마비성 질환에 걸렸는데, 그 광천수가 거의 ‘확실한’ 치료제로 통했기 때문이지요."], ["The second day after my arrival, as I was walking by the river, the sun shone so intensely hot (though it was early in the year), that I retired to the shelter of some willows, and sat down by the river side.", "도착한 이튿날, 강가를 거닐고 있는데 (아직 한 해의 이른 철이었음에도) 햇볕이 어찌나 뜨겁게 내리쬐던지, 나는 버드나무 그늘로 물러나 강가에 앉았습니다."], ["Here I had not been seated long before I heard a person on the other side of the willows sighing and bemoaning himself bitterly.", "거기 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 버드나무 저편에서 누군가가 한숨을 쉬며 비통하게 신세를 한탄하는 소리가 들렸습니다."], ["On a sudden, having uttered a most impious oath, he cried, `I am resolved to bear it no longer,' and directly threw himself into the water.", "그러더니 갑자기 더없이 불경한 욕설을 내뱉고는, ‘더는 못 견디겠다’고 외치며 곧장 물속으로 몸을 던지는 것이었습니다."], ["I immediately started, and ran towards the place, calling at the same time as loudly as I could for assistance.", "나는 즉시 벌떡 일어나 그 자리로 달려가면서, 동시에 있는 힘껏 큰 소리로 도움을 청했습니다."], ["An angler happened luckily to be a-fishing a little below me, though some very high sedge had hid him from my sight.", "운 좋게도 내 조금 아래쪽에서 한 낚시꾼이 낚시를 하고 있었는데, 무성히 높은 사초가 그를 내 시야에서 가리고 있었지요."], ["He immediately came up, and both of us together, not without some hazard of our lives, drew the body to the shore.", "그가 곧장 올라와, 우리 둘이 함께, 적잖이 목숨을 건 끝에 그 몸을 강가로 끌어냈습니다."], ["At first we perceived no sign of life remaining; but having held the body up by the heels (for we soon had assistance enough), it discharged a vast quantity of water at the mouth, and at length began to discover some symptoms of breathing, and a little afterwards to move both its hands and its legs.", "처음에는 생명의 기색이 조금도 남아 있지 않은 듯했지만, 발꿈치를 잡아 몸을 거꾸로 들어 올리니(곧 충분히 도울 손길이 모였으니까요), 입에서 엄청난 양의 물이 쏟아져 나왔고, 마침내 숨을 쉬는 기미가 보이기 시작하더니, 조금 뒤에는 두 손과 두 다리를 움직이기 시작했습니다."]], [["“An apothecary, who happened to be present among others, advised that the body, which seemed now to have pretty well emptied itself of water, and which began to have many convulsive motions, should be directly taken up, and carried into a warm bed.", "“마침 다른 이들 틈에 있던 한 약제사가, 이제 물을 거의 다 토해 낸 듯하고 경련하듯 몸을 자주 움직이기 시작한 그 사람을 곧장 들어 따뜻한 침상으로 옮기는 것이 좋겠다고 일러 주었습니다."], ["This was accordingly performed, the apothecary and myself attending.", "그래서 약제사와 내가 그를 돌보며 그렇게 했지요."]], [["“As we were going towards an inn, for we knew not the man's lodgings, luckily a woman met us, who, after some violent screaming, told us that the gentleman lodged at her house.", "“우리는 그 사람의 숙소를 몰랐던 터라 어느 여관 쪽으로 향하고 있었는데, 운 좋게도 한 여인이 우리를 만났습니다. 그녀는 한바탕 요란하게 비명을 지른 뒤, 그 신사가 자기 집에 묵고 있다고 알려 주었지요."]], [["“When I had seen the man safely deposited there, I left him to the care of the apothecary; who, I suppose, used all the right methods with him, for the next morning I heard he had perfectly recovered his senses.", "“나는 그 사람이 거기 무사히 눕는 것을 보고 나서, 약제사의 손에 그를 맡기고 자리를 떴습니다. 약제사가 그에게 온갖 알맞은 처치를 했으리라 짐작합니다. 이튿날 아침 그가 정신을 완전히 되찾았다는 소식을 들었으니까요."]], [["“I then went to visit him, intending to search out, as well as I could, the cause of his having attempted so desperate an act, and to prevent, as far as I was able, his pursuing such wicked intentions for the future.", "“그래서 나는 그를 찾아갔습니다. 그토록 절망적인 짓을 저지르려 한 까닭을 가능한 한 캐어내고, 또 앞으로 그런 사악한 의도를 좇지 못하도록 내 힘닿는 데까지 막아 보려는 마음에서였지요."], ["I was no sooner admitted into his chamber, than we both instantly knew each other; for who should this person be but my good friend Mr Watson!", "그의 방으로 들어서기가 무섭게, 우리 둘은 단번에 서로를 알아보았습니다. 그 사람이 다름 아닌 나의 좋은 벗 왓슨 씨였으니까요!"], ["Here I will not trouble you with what past at our first interview; for I would avoid prolixity as much as possible.”--“Pray let us hear all,”", "여기서 우리가 처음 마주했을 때 오간 이야기로 당신을 번거롭게 하지는 않겠습니다. 되도록 장황함은 피하고 싶으니까요.”—“부디 다 들려주십시오,”"], ["cries Partridge; “I want mightily to know what brought him to Bath.”", "파트리지가 외쳤다. “그가 무슨 일로 바스에 왔는지 너무도 알고 싶습니다.”"]], [["“You shall hear everything material,”", "“중요한 것은 모두 들려드리지요,”"], ["answered the stranger; and then proceeded to relate what we shall proceed to write, after we have given a short breathing time to both ourselves and the reader.", "낯선 이가 대답했다. 그러고는 그가 이야기하려던 바를 풀어놓기 시작했는데, 그것은 우리가 우리 자신과 독자에게 잠시 숨 돌릴 틈을 준 뒤에 이어서 쓰기로 하겠다."]], [["In which the Man of the Hill concludes his history.", "「언덕 위의 사내」가 자신의 이야기를 마무리하는 장."]], [["“Mr Watson,”", "\"왓슨 씨는,\""], ["continued the stranger, “very freely acquainted me, that the unhappy situation of his circumstances, occasioned by a tide of ill luck, had in a manner forced him to a resolution of destroying himself.", "낯선 사내가 말을 이었다. \"거듭된 불운의 물결로 형편이 더없이 비참해진 나머지, 어떻게 보면 스스로 목숨을 끊겠다는 결심에까지 떠밀리고 말았노라고 아주 거리낌 없이 털어놓더이다."]], [["“I now began to argue very seriously with him, in opposition to this heathenish, or indeed diabolical, principle of the lawfulness of self-murder; and said everything which occurred to me on the subject; but, to my great concern, it seemed to have very little effect on him.", "\"나는 이 이교도적인, 아니 실로 악마적인 자살 정당화의 원리에 맞서 그와 매우 진지하게 따져 묻기 시작했고, 그 문제에 관해 떠오르는 것은 무엇이든 다 말했소이다. 그러나 매우 안타깝게도, 그것은 그에게 거의 아무런 영향도 미치지 못하는 듯했소."], ["He seemed not at all to repent of what he had done, and gave me reason to fear he would soon make a second attempt of the like horrible kind.", "그는 자기가 저지른 일을 조금도 뉘우치는 기색이 없었고, 머지않아 같은 끔찍한 짓을 또다시 시도하리라는 두려움을 내게 안겨 주었소."]], [["“When I had finished my discourse, instead of endeavouring to answer my arguments, he looked me stedfastly in the face, and with a smile said, `You are strangely altered, my good friend, since I remember you. I question whether any of our bishops could make a better argument against suicide than you have entertained me with; but unless you can find somebody who will lend me a cool hundred, I must either hang, or drown, or starve; and, in my opinion, the last death is the most terrible of the three.'", "\"내 이야기를 끝맺었을 때, 그는 내 논리에 대답하려 애쓰는 대신, 나를 뚫어지게 바라보더니 미소를 지으며 이렇게 말하더이다. '여보게, 좋은 친구, 내가 기억하던 자네와는 참 묘하게 달라졌군. 자살을 반대하는 논리라면, 우리 주교들 가운데 누구라도 자네가 내게 들려준 것보다 더 나은 걸 펼칠 수 있을지 의문일세. 하지만 내게 선뜻 백 파운드를 빌려줄 누군가를 찾아내지 못하는 한, 나는 목을 매든, 물에 빠지든, 굶어 죽든 해야 할 처지일세. 그리고 내 생각엔 그 세 가지 중에서 마지막 죽음이 가장 끔찍하지.'"]], [["“I answered him very gravely that I was indeed altered since I had seen him last.", "\"나는 매우 엄숙하게 대답했소. 지난번에 본 이후로 나는 정말 달라졌노라고."], ["That I had found leisure to look into my follies and to repent of them.", "내 어리석음을 들여다보고 그것을 뉘우칠 여유를 얻었노라고."], ["I then advised him to pursue the same steps; and at last concluded with an assurance that I myself would lend him a hundred pound, if it would be of any service to his affairs, and he would not put it into the power of a die to deprive him of it.", "그러고는 그에게도 똑같은 길을 걸으라고 권했고, 마침내 그의 일에 조금이나마 보탬이 된다면, 그리고 그가 그것을 주사위 한 번에 빼앗기는 처지로 내몰지만 않는다면, 내가 직접 백 파운드를 빌려주겠노라는 다짐으로 말을 맺었소."]], [["“Mr Watson, who seemed almost composed in slumber by the former part of my discourse, was roused by the latter.", "\"내 이야기의 앞부분에서는 거의 졸음에 잠긴 듯 보이던 왓슨 씨가, 뒷부분에서는 정신이 번쩍 들었던 모양이오."], ["He seized my hand eagerly, gave me a thousand thanks, and declared I was a friend indeed; adding that he hoped I had a better opinion of him than to imagine he had profited so little by experience, as to put any confidence in those damned dice which had so often deceived him.", "그는 내 손을 와락 붙잡고는 천 번이나 고맙다 하며 내가 진정한 친구라고 외쳤소. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, 자기가 그토록 자주 자기를 속여 먹은 그 빌어먹을 주사위 따위에 다시 믿음을 둘 만큼 경험에서 배운 게 없는 인간이라고 여길 정도로, 내가 자기를 그리 못나게 보지는 않으리라 믿는다고 했소."], ["`No, no,'", "'아니, 아니지.'"], ["cries he; `let me but once handsomely be set up again, and if ever Fortune makes a broken merchant of me afterwards, I will forgive her.'", "그가 외쳤소. '한 번만 그럴듯하게 다시 일어설 밑천만 마련된다면, 그 뒤에 운명이 또다시 나를 망한 장사꾼으로 만든다 해도 나는 그 운명을 용서하겠네.'"]], [["“I very well understood the language of setting up, and broken merchant. I therefore said to him, with a very grave face, Mr Watson, you must endeavour to find out some business or employment, by which you may procure yourself a livelihood; and I promise you, could I see any probability of being repaid hereafter, I would advance a much larger sum than what you have mentioned, to equip you in any fair and honourable calling; but as to gaming, besides the baseness and wickedness of making it a profession, you are really, to my own knowledge, unfit for it, and it will end in your certain ruin.", "\"나는 '밑천을 세운다'느니 '망한 장사꾼'이라느니 하는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 잘 알고 있었소. 그래서 나는 아주 엄숙한 얼굴로 그에게 말했소. 왓슨 씨, 당신은 어떻게든 생계를 꾸릴 만한 사업이나 일자리를 찾아내도록 애써야 하오. 그리고 약속하건대, 훗날 되돌려 받을 가망이 조금이라도 보인다면, 나는 당신이 정직하고 떳떳한 어떤 일에든 발을 들이도록 당신이 말한 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 액수라도 대 주겠소. 허나 노름으로 말하자면, 그것을 직업으로 삼는 일의 비천함과 사악함은 차치하더라도, 내가 직접 아는 바로는 당신은 정말로 노름에 맞지 않는 사람이고, 그것은 당신의 확실한 파멸로 끝날 것이오."]], [["“`Why now, that's strange,' answered he; `neither you, nor any of my friends, would ever allow me to know anything of the matter, and yet I believe I am as good a hand at every game as any of you all; and I heartily wish I was to play with you only for your whole fortune: I should desire no better sport, and I would let you name your game into the bargain: but come, my dear boy, have you the hundred in your pocket?”", "\"'아니, 그것 참 이상하군.' 그가 대답했소. '자네도, 내 친구들 가운데 누구도, 내가 그 일에 대해 뭔가 안다는 걸 도무지 인정해 주질 않으려 하지. 하지만 내 생각엔 어떤 노름에서든 나는 자네들 누구 못지않게 솜씨가 좋은 사람일세. 자네의 전 재산을 걸고 나하고만 한판 붙어 보면 좋으련만. 그보다 더 좋은 놀이는 바라지도 않을 테고, 게다가 종목은 자네더러 정하라 하겠네. 한데 여보게, 친구, 그 백 파운드는 주머니에 있는가?'"]], [["“I answered I had only a bill for £50, which I delivered him, and promised to bring him the rest next morning; and after giving him a little more advice, took my leave.", "\"나는 50파운드짜리 어음 한 장밖에 없다고 대답하고는 그것을 그에게 건넸으며, 나머지는 다음 날 아침에 가져다주겠노라 약속했소. 그러고는 충고를 몇 마디 더 해 준 뒤에 작별을 고했소."]], [["“I was indeed better than my word; for I returned to him that very afternoon.", "\"실은 나는 약속보다 더 잘 지킨 셈이오. 바로 그날 오후에 그에게 다시 돌아갔으니 말이오."], ["When I entered the room, I found him sitting up in his bed at cards with a notorious gamester.", "방에 들어서니, 그는 침대에 일어나 앉아 악명 높은 노름꾼 하나와 카드놀이를 하고 있었소."], ["This sight, you will imagine, shocked me not a little; to which I may add the mortification of seeing my bill delivered by him to his antagonist, and thirty guineas only given in exchange for it.", "짐작하겠지만 이 광경에 나는 적잖이 충격을 받았소. 게다가 내 어음이 그의 손에서 상대편에게 건네지고, 그 대가로 고작 30기니만 받는 꼴을 본 굴욕까지 더해졌소."]], [["“The other gamester presently quitted the room, and then Watson declared he was ashamed to see me; `but,'", "\"상대 노름꾼은 곧 방을 나갔고, 그러자 왓슨은 나를 볼 면목이 없다고 했소. '하지만,'"], ["says he, `I find luck runs so damnably against me, that I will resolve to leave off play for ever.", "그가 말하더이다. '운이 어찌나 지독하게 나를 거스르는지, 이제 노름이라면 영영 손을 떼기로 결심하겠네."], ["I have thought of the kind proposal you made me ever since, and I promise you there shall be no fault in me, if I do not put it in execution.'", "자네가 내게 해 준 친절한 제안을 그 뒤로 줄곧 생각해 왔는데, 약속하건대 그것을 실행에 옮기지 못한다면 그건 내 탓이 아닐 걸세.'"]], [["“Though I had no great faith in his promises, I produced him the remainder of the hundred in consequence of my own; for which he gave me a note, which was all I ever expected to see in return for my money.", "\"그의 약속에 큰 믿음은 없었지만, 나는 내 약속에 따라 백 파운드의 나머지를 그에게 내주었소. 그는 그 대가로 차용증 한 장을 써 주었는데, 그것이 내 돈의 대가로 내가 보게 되리라 기대한 전부였소."]], [["“We were prevented from any further discourse at present by the arrival of the apothecary; who, with much joy in his countenance, and without even asking his patient how he did, proclaimed there was great news arrived in a letter to himself, which he said would shortly be public, `That the Duke of Monmouth was landed in the west with a vast army of Dutch; and that another vast fleet hovered over the coast of Norfolk, and was to make a descent there, in order to favour the duke's enterprize with a diversion on that side.'", "\"우리는 약제사가 들이닥치는 바람에 더 이상의 이야기를 나누지 못했소. 그는 얼굴에 가득한 기쁨을 띠고, 환자에게 안부조차 묻지 않은 채, 자기 앞으로 온 편지에 굉장한 소식이 도착했다고 떠벌렸소. 곧 세상에 알려질 일이라면서 말이오. '몬머스 공작이 네덜란드의 대군을 이끌고 서부에 상륙했으며, 또 다른 대규모 함대가 노퍽 해안 위를 맴돌다가 그곳에 상륙하여, 그쪽에서의 양동작전으로 공작의 거사를 거들 것이다.'"]], [["“This apothecary was one of the greatest politicians of his time.", "\"이 약제사는 당대 최고의 정치통 가운데 하나였소."], ["He was more delighted with the most paultry packet, than with the best patient, and the highest joy he was capable of, he received from having a piece of news in his possession an hour or two sooner than any other person in the town.", "그는 더없이 보잘것없는 우편물 하나에도 최고의 환자보다 더 기뻐했고, 그가 누릴 수 있는 가장 큰 기쁨이란 어떤 소식을 마을의 다른 누구보다 한두 시간 일찍 손에 넣는 데서 오는 것이었소."], ["His advices, however, were seldom authentic; for he would swallow almost anything as a truth--a humour which many made use of to impose upon him.", "하지만 그의 정보는 미덥기 어려운 것이어서, 그는 거의 무엇이든 진실이라고 덥석 삼켰소. 많은 이들이 그 버릇을 이용해 그를 속여 먹었소."]], [["“Thus it happened with what he at present communicated; for it was known within a short time afterwards that the duke was really landed, but that his army consisted only of a few attendants; and as to the diversion in Norfolk, it was entirely false.", "\"이번에 그가 전한 소식도 그러했소. 머지않아 알려진 바에 따르면, 공작이 실제로 상륙한 것은 사실이었으나 그의 군대라고는 고작 몇 안 되는 수행원들뿐이었고, 노퍽에서의 양동작전에 관해서는 완전히 거짓이었소."]], [["“The apothecary staid no longer in the room than while he acquainted us with his news; and then, without saying a syllable to his patient on any other subject, departed to spread his advices all over the town.", "\"약제사는 우리에게 소식을 전하는 동안만 방에 머물렀다가, 환자에게 다른 어떤 화제에 관해서도 한마디 건네지 않은 채, 자기 정보를 온 마을에 퍼뜨리러 떠났소."]], [["“Events of this nature in the public are generally apt to eclipse all private concerns.", "\"이런 종류의 공적인 사건들은 대체로 온갖 사사로운 관심사를 무색하게 만들기 마련이오. 그래서 우리의 이야기도 이제 완전히 정치적인 것이 되었소.[*] 나로 말하자면, 가톨릭교도 군주 아래에서 신교가 그토록 뚜렷이 위험에 노출되어 있는 것에 한동안 매우 진지하게 마음을 쓰고 있었고, 그 우려 하나만으로도 그 봉기를 정당화하기에 충분하다고 여겼소."], ["Our discourse therefore now became entirely political.[*] For my own part, I had been for some time very seriously affected with the danger to which the Protestant religion was so visibly exposed under a Popish prince, and thought the apprehension of it alone sufficient to justify that insurrection; for no real security can ever be found against the persecuting spirit of Popery, when armed with power, except the depriving it of that power, as woeful experience presently showed.", "권력으로 무장한 가톨릭의 박해 정신에 맞설 진정한 안전이란, 곧 드러난 비참한 경험이 보여 주었듯, 그 권력을 빼앗는 것 말고는 결코 찾을 수 없으니 말이오. 제임스 왕이 이 시도를 제압한 뒤에 어떻게 처신했는지, 자기 왕의 말이나 대관식 서약이나 백성의 자유와 권리를 얼마나 하찮게 여겼는지는 그대도 알 것이오."], ["You know how King James behaved after getting the better of this attempt; how little he valued either his royal word, or coronation oath, or the liberties and rights of his people.", "그러나 모두가 처음부터 이를 내다볼 분별을 지녔던 것은 아니오. 그래서 몬머스 공작은 변변치 못한 지지를 받았소."], ["But all had not the sense to foresee this at first; and therefore the Duke of Monmouth was weakly supported; yet all could feel when the evil came upon them; and therefore all united, at last, to drive out that king, against whose exclusion a great party among us had so warmly contended during the reign of his brother, and for whom they now fought with such zeal and affection.”", "허나 화가 닥치자 모두가 느낄 수 있었고, 그리하여 마침내 모두가 한데 뭉쳐 그 왕을 몰아냈소. 그의 형이 다스리던 시절에 우리 가운데 큰 무리가 그토록 열렬히 그의 왕위 계승 배제에 맞서 싸웠던 바로 그 왕, 그들이 이제 그토록 열의와 애정으로 옹호하며 싸웠던 바로 그 왕을 말이오.\""]], [["“What you say,”", "\"하시는 말씀이,\" 존스가 끼어들었다."], ["interrupted Jones, “is very true; and it has often struck me, as the most wonderful thing I ever read of in history, that so soon after this convincing experience which brought our whole nation to join so unanimously in expelling King James, for the preservation of our religion and liberties, there should be a party among us mad enough to desire the placing his family again on the throne.”", "\"참으로 옳습니다. 그리고 제가 역사책에서 읽은 것 가운데 가장 놀라운 일이라고 늘 여겨 온 것이 있는데, 우리 종교와 자유를 지키기 위해 온 나라가 그토록 한마음으로 제임스 왕을 몰아내는 데 함께 나서게 한 그 명백한 경험 직후에, 우리 가운데 그의 가문을 다시 왕좌에 앉히고 싶어 할 만큼 미친 무리가 있을 수 있다는 사실입니다.\""], ["“You are not in earnest!”", "\"진심으로 하는 말은 아니겠지!\" 노인이 대답했다."], ["answered the old man; “there can be no such party.", "\"그런 무리가 있을 리 없네. 내가 인류를 아무리 나쁘게 본다 해도, 그들이 그 정도까지 정신이 홀렸으리라고는 믿을 수 없네."], ["As bad an opinion as I have of mankind, I cannot believe them infatuated to such a degree.", "사제들에게 이끌려 이 무모한 대의에 뛰어들어 그것을 성전으로 여기는 머리 뜨거운 가톨릭교도 몇몇이야 있을 수 있겠지. 허나 신교도들이, 그것도 영국 국교회의 신자들이 그런 배교자, 그런 자살자가 되리라고는 도무지 믿을 수 없네."], ["There may be some hot-headed Papists led by their priests to engage in this desperate cause, and think it a holy war; but that Protestants, that are members of the Church of England, should be such apostates, such felos de se, I cannot believe it; no, no, young man, unacquainted as I am with what has past in the world for these last thirty years, I cannot be so imposed upon as to credit so foolish a tale; but I see you have a mind to sport with my ignorance.”--“Can it be possible,”", "아니, 아니야, 젊은이, 내가 지난 삼십 년 동안 세상에서 일어난 일에 어둡기는 하다만, 그토록 어리석은 이야기를 곧이듣도록 속아 넘어갈 수는 없네. 보아하니 그대는 내 무지를 가지고 장난을 칠 작정인 게로군.\"—\"그토록 세상을 벗어나 사신 나머지,\""], ["replied Jones, “that you have lived so much out of the world as not to know that during that time there have been two rebellions in favour of the son of King James, one of which is now actually raging in the very heart of the kingdom.”", "존스가 대꾸했다. \"그동안 제임스 왕의 아들을 옹립하려는 반란이 두 차례나 있었고, 그 가운데 하나가 지금 실제로 이 왕국의 한복판에서 맹렬히 타오르고 있다는 것을 모르신다는 게 가당한 일입니까?\""], ["At these words the old gentleman started up, and in a most solemn tone of voice, conjured Jones by his Maker to tell him if what he said was really true; which the other as solemnly affirming, he walked several turns about the room in a profound silence, then cried, then laughed, and at last fell down on his knees, and blessed God, in a loud thanksgiving prayer, for having delivered him from all society with human nature, which could be capable of such monstrous extravagances.", "이 말에 노신사는 벌떡 일어나, 더없이 엄숙한 목소리로 그가 한 말이 정말 사실이냐고 창조주의 이름으로 존스에게 다짐을 받으려 했다. 상대방이 똑같이 엄숙하게 그것을 확인해 주자, 그는 깊은 침묵 속에 방 안을 몇 바퀴 거닐더니, 울다가 웃다가 하다가 마침내 무릎을 꿇고는, 그토록 괴이한 광기를 부릴 수 있는 인간 본성과의 온갖 교제로부터 자신을 건져 주신 것에 대해 큰 소리로 감사 기도를 올리며 하느님을 찬미했다."], ["After which, being reminded by Jones that he had broke off his story, he resumed it again in this manner:--", "그 뒤에 존스로부터 이야기가 끊겼다는 것을 일깨움받고는, 그는 다시 이런 식으로 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“As mankind, in the days I was speaking of, was not yet arrived at that pitch of madness which I find they are capable of now, and which, to be sure, I have only escaped by living alone, and at a distance from the contagion, there was a considerable rising in favour of Monmouth; and my principles strongly inclining me to take the same part, I determined to join him; and Mr Watson, from different motives concurring in the same resolution (for the spirit of a gamester will carry a man as far upon such an occasion as the spirit of patriotism), we soon provided ourselves with all necessaries, and went to the duke at Bridgewater.", "\"내가 말하던 그 시절에는 인류가, 지금은 그들이 능히 부릴 수 있다고 내가 깨닫게 된 그런 광기의 절정에는 아직 이르지 못했었소. 물론 나야 홀로, 그 전염에서 멀찍이 떨어져 살았던 덕에 그것을 면했을 뿐이오만. 어쨌든 그때 몬머스를 옹호하는 상당한 봉기가 일어났고, 내 신념도 같은 편에 서도록 나를 강하게 기울게 한 터라, 나는 그에게 합류하기로 결심했소. 그리고 왓슨 씨도 다른 동기에서 같은 결심에 뜻을 같이하니(노름꾼의 기질이란 그런 경우에 애국심의 기질만큼이나 멀리까지 사람을 이끄는 법이오), 우리는 곧 필요한 것들을 모두 갖추고 브리지워터에 있는 공작에게로 갔소."]], [["“The unfortunate event of this enterprize, you are, I conclude, as well acquainted with as myself.", "\"이 거사의 불행한 결말은, 짐작건대 그대도 나만큼 잘 알고 있을 것이오."], ["I escaped, together with Mr Watson, from the battle at Sedgemore, in which action I received a slight wound.", "나는 왓슨 씨와 함께 세지무어 전투에서 빠져나왔는데, 그 싸움에서 나는 가벼운 상처를 입었소."], ["We rode near forty miles together on the Exeter road, and then abandoning our horses, scrambled as well as we could through the fields and bye-roads, till we arrived at a little wild hut on a common, where a poor old woman took all the care of us she could, and dressed my wound with salve, which quickly healed it.”", "우리는 함께 엑서터로 가는 길을 40마일 가까이 말을 달렸고, 그런 다음 말을 버리고 들판과 샛길을 헤치며 갈 수 있는 데까지 기어가다시피 하여, 마침내 어느 공유지에 있는 자그마한 외딴 오두막에 다다랐소. 거기서 가난한 노파 하나가 우리를 정성껏 보살펴 주었고, 내 상처에 연고를 발라 주니 금세 아물었소.\""]], [["“Pray, sir, where was the wound?”", "\"실례지만, 어르신, 상처는 어디였습니까?\""], ["says Partridge.", "파트리지가 물었다."], ["The stranger satisfied him it was in his arm, and then continued his narrative.", "낯선 사내는 팔이었다고 일러 그를 만족시켜 준 다음, 이야기를 이어 갔다. \"바로 여기서,\""], ["“Here, sir,”", "그가 말했다. \"왓슨 씨는 이튿날 아침에 나를 떠났소."], ["said he, “Mr Watson left me the next morning, in order, as he pretended, to get us some provision from the town of Collumpton; but--can I relate it, or can you believe it?--this Mr Watson, this friend, this base, barbarous, treacherous villain, betrayed me to a party of horse belonging to King James, and at his return delivered me into their hands.", "그는 우리가 먹을 식량을 콜럼튼 마을에서 좀 구해 오겠다는 핑계를 댔소. 그러나—내가 이 이야기를 할 수 있을까, 또 그대가 믿을 수 있을까?—이 왓슨 씨가, 이 친구가, 이 비열하고 야만스럽고 배신을 일삼는 악당이, 나를 제임스 왕에게 속한 기병 한 무리에게 팔아넘기고는, 돌아와서 나를 그들의 손에 넘겨주었소."]], [["“The soldiers, being six in number, had now seized me, and were conducting me to Taunton gaol; but neither my present situation, nor the apprehensions of what might happen to me, were half so irksome to my mind as the company of my false friend, who, having surrendered himself, was likewise considered as a prisoner, though he was better treated, as being to make his peace at my expense. He at first endeavoured to excuse his treachery; but when he received nothing but scorn and upbraiding from me, he soon changed his note, abused me as the most atrocious and malicious rebel, and laid all his own guilt to my charge, who, as he declared, had solicited, and even threatened him, to make him take up arms against his gracious as well as lawful sovereign.", "\"여섯 명으로 이루어진 그 병사들은 이제 나를 붙잡아 톤턴 감옥으로 끌고 갔소. 허나 나의 지금 처지도, 내게 닥칠지 모를 일에 대한 두려움도, 그 거짓된 친구와 한자리에 있는 것의 절반만큼도 내 마음을 괴롭히지는 못했소. 그는 스스로 항복했기에 마찬가지로 포로로 간주되었으나, 나를 희생시켜 제 안위를 도모할 자였기에 더 나은 대접을 받았소. 그는 처음에는 자기 배신을 변명하려 들었지만, 내게서 멸시와 책망 말고는 아무것도 돌아오지 않자, 금세 어조를 바꾸어 나를 더없이 흉악하고 악의에 찬 반역자라고 욕하며, 자기 죄를 모조리 내게 뒤집어씌웠소. 그의 말로는, 자비롭고도 합법적인 군주에게 맞서 무기를 들도록 내가 그를 꾀고 심지어 협박까지 했다는 것이었소."]], [["“This false evidence (for in reality he had been much the forwarder of the two) stung me to the quick, and raised an indignation scarce conceivable by those who have not felt it.", "\"이 거짓 증언은(실은 둘 중에 훨씬 더 앞장섰던 것은 바로 그였소만) 나를 뼛속까지 찔렀고, 그것을 겪어 보지 못한 이들은 좀처럼 헤아릴 수 없을 분노를 불러일으켰소. 그러나 마침내 운명이 내게 연민을 베풀었소."], ["However, fortune at length took pity on me; for as we were got a little beyond Wellington, in a narrow lane, my guards received a false alarm, that near fifty of the enemy were at hand; upon which they shifted for themselves, and left me and my betrayer to do the same.", "우리가 웰링턴을 조금 지나 좁은 샛길에 이르렀을 때, 내 호위병들에게 적군 쉰 명 가까이가 코앞에 있다는 거짓 경보가 들어왔소. 그러자 그들은 제 한 몸 챙기기에 바빠, 나와 나를 배신한 자를 내버려 두고 각자 알아서 하게 했소."], ["That villain immediately ran from me, and I am glad he did, or I should have certainly endeavoured, though I had no arms, to have executed vengeance on his baseness.", "그 악당은 즉시 내게서 달아났는데, 차라리 잘된 일이오. 그러지 않았다면 나는 무기 하나 없는 처지였음에도 분명 그의 비열함에 복수하려 들었을 테니 말이오."]], [["“I was now once more at liberty; and immediately withdrawing from the highway into the fields, I travelled on, scarce knowing which way I went, and making it my chief care to avoid all public roads and all towns--nay, even the most homely houses; for I imagined every human creature whom I saw desirous of betraying me.", "\"이제 나는 다시 한 번 자유의 몸이 되었소. 그래서 곧장 큰길에서 벗어나 들판으로 들어가, 어느 쪽으로 가는지도 거의 모른 채 계속 걸었고, 모든 큰길과 모든 마을을—아니, 심지어 가장 허름한 집까지도—피하는 것을 가장 큰 일로 삼았소. 내 눈에 띄는 사람이라면 누구나 나를 팔아넘기려 한다고 여겼기 때문이오."]], [["“At last, after rambling several days about the country, during which the fields afforded me the same bed and the same food which nature bestows on our savage brothers of the creation, I at length arrived at this place, where the solitude and wildness of the country invited me to fix my abode. The first person with whom I took up my habitation was the mother of this old woman, with whom I remained concealed till the news of the glorious revolution put an end to all my apprehensions of danger, and gave me an opportunity of once more visiting my own home, and of enquiring a little into my affairs, which I soon settled as agreeably to my brother as to myself; having resigned everything to him, for which he paid me the sum of a thousand pounds, and settled on me an annuity for life.", "\"마침내 며칠을 시골 일대를 떠돌며—그동안 들판은 조물주가 미물인 우리 형제들에게 베푸는 것과 똑같은 잠자리와 똑같은 먹을거리를 내게 주었소—나는 이윽고 이곳에 다다랐소. 이 고장의 한적함과 거친 정취가 나더러 거처를 정하라 손짓했소. 내가 처음 거처를 함께한 사람은 이 노파의 어머니였는데, 나는 영광스러운 혁명의 소식이 위험에 대한 내 모든 두려움을 끝내 주고, 다시 한 번 내 고향 집을 찾아가 내 일을 조금 알아볼 기회를 줄 때까지 그녀와 함께 숨어 지냈소. 그 일은 곧 형에게나 내게나 흡족하게 매듭지어졌소. 나는 모든 것을 형에게 넘겼고, 그 대가로 형은 내게 천 파운드의 돈을 치르고 평생 받을 연금을 정해 주었소."]], [["“His behaviour in this last instance, as in all others, was selfish and ungenerous.", "\"이 마지막 경우에도, 다른 모든 경우와 마찬가지로 그의 처신은 이기적이고 인색했소. 나는 그를 내 벗으로 여길 수 없었고, 사실 그 또한 내가 그러기를 바라지 않았소."], ["I could not look on him as my friend, nor indeed did he desire that I should; so I presently took my leave of him, as well as of my other acquaintance; and from that day to this, my history is little better than a blank.”", "그래서 나는 곧 그에게도, 또 다른 지인들에게도 작별을 고했소. 그날 이후 오늘에 이르기까지 내 이야기는 백지나 다름없소.\""]], [["“And is it possible, sir,”", "\"그러면 어르신,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “that you can have resided here from that day to this?”--“O no, sir,”", "\"그날부터 지금까지 이곳에서 줄곧 지내신 게 가능한 일입니까?\"—\"오, 아니라네.\" 신사가 대답했다."], ["answered the gentleman; “I have been a great traveller, and there are few parts of Europe with which I am not acquainted.”", "\"나는 대단한 여행가였고, 내가 모르는 유럽 땅이라곤 거의 없네.\" \"지금 어르신께 그것을 청할 만큼,\""], ["“I have not, sir,”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"뻔뻔하지는 못하겠습니다."], ["cried Jones, “the assurance to ask it of you now; indeed it would be cruel, after so much breath as you have already spent: but you will give me leave to wish for some further opportunity of hearing the excellent observations which a man of your sense and knowledge of the world must have made in so long a course of travels.”--“Indeed, young gentleman,”", "정말이지 이미 그토록 많은 기운을 쓰셨는데 그것을 청한다면 너무 가혹한 일이겠지요. 다만 어르신처럼 분별 있고 세상 물정에 밝으신 분이 그토록 오랜 여정 동안 하셨을 훌륭한 견문을 들을 기회를 다음에 또 갖기를 바라는 것만은 허락해 주십시오.\"—\"정말이지, 젊은 신사 양반,\""], ["answered the stranger, “I will endeavour to satisfy your curiosity on this head likewise, as far as I am able.”", "낯선 사내가 대답했다. \"그 점에 관해서도 내 능력이 닿는 한 그대의 호기심을 채워 주도록 애써 보겠네.\""], ["Jones attempted fresh apologies, but was prevented; and while he and Partridge sat with greedy and impatient ears, the stranger proceeded as in the next chapter.", "존스는 새삼 사양의 말을 꺼내려 했으나 가로막혔다. 그가 파트리지와 함께 탐욕스럽고 안달 난 귀를 기울이고 앉아 있는 동안, 낯선 사내는 다음 장에서와 같이 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["[*] The rest of this paragraph and the two following paragraphs in the first edition were as follows:", "[*] 초판에서는 이 문단의 나머지 부분과 뒤이은 두 문단이 다음과 같았다."]], [["“For my own part, I had been for some time very seriously affected with the danger to which the Protestant religion was so visibly exposed, that nothing but the immediate interposition of Providence seemed capable of preserving it; for King James had indeed declared war against the Protestant cause.", "\"나로 말하자면, 신교가 그토록 뚜렷이 위험에 노출되어 있어 오직 섭리의 즉각적인 개입 말고는 그것을 지켜 낼 길이 없어 보이는 것에 한동안 매우 진지하게 마음을 쓰고 있었소. 제임스 왕은 실로 신교의 대의에 전쟁을 선포한 셈이었으니 말이오."], ["He had brought known papists into the army and attempted to bring them into the Church and into the University.", "그는 알려진 가톨릭교도들을 군대에 들여놓았고, 그들을 교회와 대학에까지 들여놓으려 했소."], ["Popish priests swarmed through the nation, appeared publicly in their habits, and boasted that they should shortly walk in procession through the streets.", "가톨릭 사제들이 온 나라에 들끓었고, 자기네 사제복을 입고 공공연히 나돌았으며, 머지않아 거리를 행렬 지어 행진하게 되리라고 떠벌렸소."], ["Our own clergy were forbid to preach against popery, and bishops were ordered to supend those who did; and to do the business at once an illegal ecclesiastical commission was erected, little inferior to an inquisition, of which, probably, it was intended to be the ringleader.", "우리 성직자들은 가톨릭을 반박하는 설교가 금지되었고, 주교들은 그런 설교를 하는 자들을 정직시키라는 명령을 받았소. 그리고 이 일을 단번에 해치우려고, 종교재판소에 거의 못지않은 불법적인 교회위원회가 세워졌는데, 아마도 그것이 그 우두머리 노릇을 하도록 의도된 것이었소."], ["Thus, as our duty to the king can never be called more than our second duty, he had discharged us from this by making it incompatible with our preserving the first, which is surely to heaven.", "이리하여 왕에 대한 우리의 의무란 결코 우리의 두 번째 의무 이상으로 불릴 수 없는 것인데, 그는 그것을 우리의 첫 번째 의무, 곧 하늘을 향한 의무를 지키는 일과 양립할 수 없게 만듦으로써 우리를 그 의무에서 풀어 준 셈이었소. 이뿐 아니라 그는 자신의 대관식 서약을 깨뜨림으로써 백성들을 그 충성의 의무에서 풀어 주었으니, 백성들의 충성이란 바로 그 서약에 매여 있는 것이었소."], ["Besides this, he had dissolved his subjects from their allegiance by breaking his Coronation Oath, to which their allegiance is annexed; for he had imprisoned bishops because they would not give up their religion, and turned out judges because they would not absolutely surrender the law into his hands; nay, he seized this himself, and when he claimed a dispensing power, he declared himself, in fact, as absolute as any tyrant ever was or can be.", "그는 주교들이 자기네 종교를 포기하려 하지 않는다는 이유로 그들을 가두었고, 법을 자기 손에 고스란히 넘겨주려 하지 않는다는 이유로 판사들을 내쫓았소. 아니, 그는 법마저 스스로 거머쥐었고, 법을 면제할 권한을 주장했을 때, 그는 실로 일찍이 있었거나 있을 수 있는 그 어떤 폭군 못지않게 자신이 절대적임을 선언한 셈이었소."], ["I have recapitulated these matters in full lest some of them should have been omitted in history; and I think nothing less than such provocations as I have here mentioned, nothing less than certain and imminent danger to their religion and liberties, can justify or even mitigate the dreadful sin of rebellion in any people.”", "이런 일들을 내가 낱낱이 되짚어 본 것은 그 가운데 일부가 역사에 빠졌을까 염려해서요. 그리고 내 생각에, 여기서 내가 말한 것과 같은 도발, 곧 자기네 종교와 자유에 닥친 분명하고도 임박한 위험에 못 미치는 그 어떤 것으로도, 그 어떤 백성에게든 반란이라는 무서운 죄를 정당화하거나 심지어 덜어 줄 수조차 없을 것이오.\""]], [["“I promise you, sir,”", "\"장담하건대, 어르신,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["says Jones, “all these facts, and more, I have read in history, but I will tell you a fact which is not yet recorded and of which I suppose you are ignorant.", "\"이 모든 사실은, 아니 그 이상의 것까지도 저는 역사책에서 읽었습니다. 허나 아직 기록되지 않은, 그래서 어르신께서도 모르시리라 짐작되는 한 가지 사실을 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["There is actually now a rebellion on foot in this kingdom in favour of the son of that very King James, a professed papist, more bigoted, if possible, than his father, and this carried on by Protestants against a king who hath never in one single instance made the least invasion on our liberties.”", "지금 이 왕국에는 바로 그 제임스 왕의 아들을 옹립하려는 반란이 실제로 일고 있는데, 그는 공언된 가톨릭교도로서 가능하다면 제 아비보다 더 광신적인 자입니다. 그리고 이 반란을 신교도들이, 우리의 자유에 단 한 차례도 털끝만 한 침해조차 가한 적 없는 왕에게 맞서 벌이고 있다는 것입니다.\""]], [["“Prodigious indeed!”", "\"실로 어처구니없는 일이로군!\""], ["answered the stranger.", "낯선 사내가 대답했다."], ["“You tell me what would be incredible of a nation which did not deserve the character that Virgil gives of a woman, varium et mutabile semper.", "\"베르길리우스가 여인을 두고 한 평, 곧 '언제나 변덕스럽고 변하기 쉽다'는 그 평에 합당하지 않은 나라라면, 그대가 하는 말은 믿을 수 없는 일이었을 게요."], ["Surely this is to be unworthy of the care which Providence seems to have taken of us in the preservation of our religion against the powerful designs and constant machinations of Popery, a preservation so strange and unaccountable that I almost think we may appeal to it as to a miracle for the proof of its holiness.", "분명 이는, 섭리가 가톨릭의 강력한 책략과 끊임없는 음모에 맞서 우리 종교를 지켜 주려 기울인 듯한 그 보살핌을 받을 자격이 없는 짓이오. 그토록 기이하고 헤아릴 수 없는 그 지켜 줌이란, 그 거룩함의 증거로서 거의 기적에 호소해도 좋으리라고 내가 여길 정도인데 말이오."], ["Prodigious indeed!", "실로 어처구니없는 일이로군!"], ["A Protestant rebellion in favour of a popish prince!", "가톨릭 군주를 옹립하려는 신교도의 반란이라니!"], ["The folly of mankind is as wonderful as their knavery--But to conclude my story: I resolved to take arms in defence of my country, of my religion, and my liberty, and Mr. Watson joined in the same resolution.", "인류의 어리석음은 그들의 간악함만큼이나 놀랍구려.—허나 내 이야기를 마무리하자면, 나는 내 나라와 내 종교와 내 자유를 지키기 위해 무기를 들기로 결심했고, 왓슨 씨도 같은 결심에 뜻을 같이했소."], ["We soon provided ourselves with an necessaries and joined the Duke at Bridgewater.”", "우리는 곧 필요한 것들을 모두 갖추고 브리지워터에 있는 공작에게 합류했소.\""]], [["“The unfortunate event of this enterprise you are perhaps better acquainted with than myself.", "\"이 거사의 불행한 결말은 어쩌면 그대가 나보다 더 잘 알고 있을 것이오."], ["I escaped together with Mr. Watson from the battle at Sedgemore,...", "나는 왓슨 씨와 함께 세지무어 전투에서 빠져나왔는데,……\""]], [["# Chapter xv.", "# Chapter xv."]], [["A brief history of Europe; and a curious discourse between Mr Jones and the Man of the Hill.", "유럽에 관한 짤막한 역사, 그리고 존스 씨와 산속 노인 사이에 오간 흥미로운 대화."]], [["“In Italy the landlords are very silent.", "\"이탈리아에서는 여관 주인들이 무척 말이 없습니다."], ["In France they are more talkative, but yet civil.", "프랑스에서는 좀 더 수다스럽긴 해도 그래도 예의는 차립니다."], ["In Germany and Holland they are generally very impertinent.", "독일과 네덜란드에서는 대개 아주 무례하지요."], ["And as for their honesty, I believe it is pretty equal in all those countries.", "그리고 정직함으로 말하자면, 이 모든 나라가 거의 엇비슷하다고 봅니다."], ["The laquais à louange are sure to lose no opportunity of cheating you; and as for the postilions, I think they are pretty much alike all the world over.", "삯을 받고 알랑거리는 하인 놈들은 당신을 속일 기회를 절대 놓치지 않을 게 분명하고, 마부들로 말하자면 세상 어디를 가나 다 거기서 거기인 것 같더군요."], ["These, sir, are the observations on men which I made in my travels; for these were the only men I ever conversed with.", "선생, 이것이 제가 여행하면서 사람들에 대해 얻은 관찰입니다. 제가 말을 섞어 본 인간이라곤 그들뿐이었으니까요."], ["My design, when I went abroad, was to divert myself by seeing the wondrous variety of prospects, beasts, birds, fishes, insects, and vegetables, with which God has been pleased to enrich the several parts of this globe; a variety which, as it must give great pleasure to a contemplative beholder, so doth it admirably display the power, and wisdom, and goodness of the Creator.", "제가 외국으로 떠났을 때의 의도는, 하느님께서 기꺼이 이 지구의 여러 곳을 풍요롭게 채워 주신 그 경이로운 갖가지 풍경과 짐승, 새, 물고기, 곤충, 식물을 보면서 스스로를 즐겁게 하려는 것이었습니다. 그 다양함은 사색하는 구경꾼에게 큰 기쁨을 줄 뿐 아니라, 창조주의 권능과 지혜와 선하심을 더없이 훌륭하게 드러내 보여 주지요."], ["Indeed, to say the truth, there is but one work in his whole creation that doth him any dishonour, and with that I have long since avoided holding any conversation.”", "사실 진실을 말하자면, 그분의 온 창조물 가운데 그분께 욕을 돌리는 작품은 단 하나뿐인데, 저는 오래전부터 그것과는 어떤 대화도 나누기를 피해 왔습니다.\""]], [["“You will pardon me,” cries Jones; “but I have always imagined that there is in this very work you mention as great variety as in all the rest; for, besides the difference of inclination, customs and climates have, I am told, introduced the utmost diversity into human nature.”", "\"용서하십시오.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"하지만 저는 늘 생각해 왔습니다. 어르신께서 말씀하시는 바로 그 작품 안에도 다른 모든 것 못지않은 큰 다양함이 있다고요. 성향의 차이뿐 아니라, 풍습과 기후 또한 인간 본성에 더없이 큰 다채로움을 끌어들였다고 들었으니까요.\""]], [["“Very little indeed,”", "\"실은 거의 없습니다.\" 다른 이가 대답했다."], ["answered the other: “those who travel in order to acquaint themselves with the different manners of men might spare themselves much pains by going to a carnival at Venice; for there they will see at once all which they can discover in the several courts of Europe.", "\"사람들의 서로 다른 풍속을 알고자 여행하는 자들은 베네치아의 사육제에 한 번 가 보는 것만으로도 많은 수고를 덜 수 있을 겁니다. 거기서 그들은 유럽 여러 궁정을 돌며 발견할 수 있는 것을 한꺼번에 다 보게 될 테니까요."], ["The same hypocrisy, the same fraud; in short, the same follies and vices dressed in different habits.", "똑같은 위선, 똑같은 사기, 한마디로 똑같은 어리석음과 악덕이 그저 다른 옷을 걸치고 있을 뿐입니다."], ["In Spain, these are equipped with much gravity; and in Italy, with vast splendor.", "스페인에서는 그것들이 무척 진중한 차림을 하고, 이탈리아에서는 굉장한 화려함으로 치장하지요."], ["In France, a knave is dressed like a fop; and in the northern countries, like a sloven.", "프랑스에서는 악당이 멋쟁이처럼 차려입고, 북쪽 나라들에서는 칠칠치 못한 게으름뱅이처럼 입습니다."], ["But human nature is everywhere the same, everywhere the object of detestation and scorn.", "그러나 인간 본성은 어디서나 똑같고, 어디서나 혐오와 경멸의 대상입니다."]], [["“As for my own part, I past through all these nations as you perhaps may have done through a croud at a shew-jostling to get by them, holding my nose with one hand, and defending my pockets with the other, without speaking a word to any of them, while I was pressing on to see what I wanted to see; which, however entertaining it might be in itself, scarce made me amends for the trouble the company gave me.”", "\"저로 말하자면, 저는 이 모든 나라를 거쳐 가기를, 마치 당신이 구경거리를 보러 가는 군중 속을 헤쳐 나가듯 했습니다. 그들을 지나치려 떠밀며, 한 손으로는 코를 막고 다른 손으로는 주머니를 지키면서, 누구에게도 한마디 건네지 않은 채 제가 보고 싶은 것을 향해 밀고 나아갔지요. 그런데 그 보고 싶던 것이 제아무리 그 자체로 즐거웠다 한들, 그 무리가 제게 끼친 성가심을 보상해 주기에는 턱없이 부족했습니다.\""]], [["“Did not you find some of the nations among which you travelled less troublesome to you than others?”", "\"여행하신 나라들 가운데, 다른 곳보다 덜 성가신 곳도 있지 않으셨습니까?\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones.", "\"오, 있고말고요.\""], ["“O yes,”", "노인이 대답했다."], ["replied the old man: “the Turks were much more tolerable to me than the Christians; for they are men of profound taciturnity, and never disturb a stranger with questions.", "\"튀르크 사람들이 기독교도들보다 제게는 훨씬 견딜 만했습니다. 그들은 깊은 과묵함을 지닌 자들이라, 낯선 이를 질문으로 괴롭히는 법이 없거든요."], ["Now and then indeed they bestow a short curse upon him, or spit in his face as he walks the streets, but then they have done with him; and a man may live an age in their country without hearing a dozen words from them.", "물론 이따금 짤막한 욕을 던지거나, 거리를 걷는 그의 얼굴에 침을 뱉기도 하지만, 그러고 나면 그걸로 끝입니다. 그 나라에서는 한평생을 살아도 그들에게서 열두 마디조차 듣지 못할 수 있습니다."], ["But of all the people I ever saw, heaven defend me from the French!", "그러나 제가 본 모든 민족 가운데, 부디 하늘이 저를 프랑스인들로부터 지켜 주시기를!"], ["With their damned prate and civilities, and doing the honour of their nation to strangers (as they are pleased to call it), but indeed setting forth their own vanity; they are so troublesome, that I had infinitely rather pass my life with the Hottentots than set my foot in Paris again.", "그 빌어먹을 수다와 인사치레, 그리고 이방인에게 제 나라의 명예를 베푼다면서 (그들 좋을 대로 그렇게 부릅니다만) 실은 제 허영을 늘어놓는 그 짓이라니. 어찌나 성가신지, 저는 파리에 다시 발을 들이느니 차라리 호텐토트인들과 평생을 보내는 편이 한없이 낫겠습니다."], ["They are a nasty people, but their nastiness is mostly without; whereas, in France, and some other nations that I won't name, it is all within, and makes them stink much more to my reason than that of Hottentots does to my nose.", "호텐토트인들은 더러운 족속이지만, 그들의 더러움은 대개 겉에 있습니다. 반면 프랑스나, 굳이 이름을 대지 않을 몇몇 다른 나라에서는 그 더러움이 죄다 속에 있어서, 호텐토트인의 냄새가 제 코를 찌르는 것보다 그자들이 제 이성을 훨씬 더 고약하게 찌릅니다."]], [["“Thus, sir, I have ended the history of my life; for as to all that series of years during which I have lived retired here, it affords no variety to entertain you, and may be almost considered as one day.[*] The retirement has been so compleat, that I could hardly have enjoyed a more absolute solitude in the deserts of the Thebais than here in the midst of this populous kingdom.", "\"이리하여, 선생, 저는 제 인생의 이야기를 마쳤습니다. 제가 이곳에 은거하며 살아온 그 모든 세월로 말하자면, 당신을 즐겁게 할 만한 변화라곤 없으니, 거의 하루로 쳐도 될 정도지요."], ["As I have no estate, I am plagued with no tenants or stewards: my annuity is paid me pretty regularly, as indeed it ought to be; for it is much less than what I might have expected in return for what I gave up.", "이 은거는 어찌나 완벽했던지, 테바이스의 사막 한가운데에서라 한들 이 인구 많은 왕국 한복판에서 누리는 것보다 더 완전한 고독을 누리지는 못했을 겁니다. 저는 재산이 없으니, 소작인이나 집사 따위로 시달릴 일이 없습니다."], ["Visits I admit none; and the old woman who keeps my house knows that her place entirely depends upon her saving me all the trouble of buying the things that I want, keeping off all sollicitation or business from me, and holding her tongue whenever I am within hearing.", "제 연금은 꽤 꼬박꼬박 지급되는데, 사실 그래야 마땅하지요. 제가 내놓은 것에 대한 대가로 기대할 수 있었던 것보다 훨씬 적은 액수니까요."], ["As my walks are all by night, I am pretty secure in this wild unfrequented place from meeting any company.", "방문은 일절 받지 않습니다. 제 집을 돌보는 노파는, 자기 자리가 전적으로 제게 달려 있음을 압니다."], ["Some few persons I have met by chance, and sent them home heartily frighted, as from the oddness of my dress and figure they took me for a ghost or a hobgoblin.", "즉 제가 필요한 물건을 사는 온갖 수고를 대신 덜어 주고, 모든 청탁이나 용무를 제게서 막아 주고, 제 귀에 들릴 만한 자리에서는 입을 다무는 일에 달려 있다는 것을요. 제 산책은 모두 밤에 이루어지므로, 이 인적 드문 황량한 곳에서 누군가와 마주칠 걱정은 거의 없습니다."], ["But what has happened to-night shows that even here I cannot be safe from the villany of men; for without your assistance I had not only been robbed, but very probably murdered.”", "몇몇 사람을 우연히 만나기는 했는데, 제 옷차림과 모습이 하도 괴이해서 저를 유령이나 도깨비로 여기는 바람에 혼비백산하여 집으로 돌려보냈지요. 그러나 오늘 밤 일어난 일은, 이곳에서조차 제가 인간의 악행으로부터 안전할 수 없음을 보여 줍니다."], ["[*] the rest of this paragraph is omitted in the third edition", "당신의 도움이 없었다면 저는 강도를 당했을 뿐 아니라, 십중팔구 살해당했을 테니까요.\""]], [["Jones thanked the stranger for the trouble he had taken in relating his story, and then expressed some wonder how he could possibly endure a life of such solitude; “in which,”", "존스는 이방인이 자기 이야기를 들려주느라 애써 준 것에 감사한 뒤, 그가 어떻게 그토록 고독한 삶을 견뎌 낼 수 있었는지 다소 놀라움을 표했다. \"그런 삶에서는,\""], ["says he, “you may well complain of the want of variety.", "그가 말했다. \"변화가 없다고 하소연하실 만도 합니다."], ["Indeed I am astonished how you have filled up, or rather killed, so much of your time.”", "정말이지 저는 어르신께서 그 많은 시간을 어떻게 채우셨는지, 아니 차라리 어떻게 죽이셨는지 놀라울 따름입니다.\""]], [["“I am not at all surprized,”", "\"전혀 놀랄 일이 아닙니다.\" 다른 이가 대답했다."], ["answered the other, “that to one whose affections and thoughts are fixed on the world my hours should appear to have wanted employment in this place: but there is one single act, for which the whole life of man is infinitely too short: what time can suffice for the contemplation and worship of that glorious, immortal, and eternal Being, among the works of whose stupendous creation not only this globe, but even those numberless luminaries which we may here behold spangling all the sky, though they should many of them be suns lighting different systems of worlds, may possibly appear but as a few atoms opposed to the whole earth which we inhabit?", "\"애정과 생각이 이 세상에 붙박여 있는 사람에게야, 이런 곳에서 제 시간이 할 일을 잃은 듯 보이는 게 당연하지요. 하지만 단 한 가지 일이 있으니, 인간의 한평생을 다 바쳐도 한없이 모자란 일입니다."], ["Can a man who by divine meditations is admitted as it were into the conversation of this ineffable, incomprehensible Majesty, think days, or years, or ages, too long for the continuance of so ravishing an honour?", "저 영광스럽고 불멸하며 영원하신 존재를 묵상하고 경배하는 데에 무슨 시간인들 넉넉하겠습니까? 그분의 그 어마어마한 창조물 가운데서는, 이 지구는 물론이거니와 우리가 여기서 온 하늘에 반짝이는 것으로 바라보는 저 헤아릴 수 없는 빛들조차도—그 가운데 상당수가 서로 다른 세계의 체계를 비추는 태양일 수 있음에도—우리가 사는 이 온 지구에 견주면 한낱 몇 개의 티끌처럼 보일 따름일 텐데 말입니다."], ["Shall the trifling amusements, the palling pleasures, the silly business of the world, roll away our hours too swiftly from us; and shall the pace of time seem sluggish to a mind exercised in studies so high, so important, and so glorious?", "거룩한 묵상으로 말미암아 이 말로 다할 수 없고 헤아릴 수 없는 위엄과의 대화에 이를테면 받아들여진 사람이, 그토록 황홀한 영예가 이어지는 데에 며칠이든, 몇 년이든, 몇 세기든 너무 길다고 여기겠습니까? 하찮은 오락, 물리도록 시들한 쾌락, 세상의 어리석은 용무 따위가 우리에게서 시간을 너무 빨리 굴려 가 버리는데, 그토록 높고, 그토록 중대하며, 그토록 영광스러운 연구에 힘쓰는 정신에게 시간의 걸음이 굼떠 보이겠습니까?"], ["As no time is sufficient, so no place is improper, for this great concern.", "어떤 시간도 넉넉하지 않듯, 이 위대한 관심사에 어울리지 않는 장소도 없습니다. 우리가 눈길을 던지는 그 어떤 대상이 그분의 권능과 지혜와 선하심에 대한 생각을 우리에게 불어넣지 않겠습니까?"], ["On what object can we cast our eyes which may not inspire us with ideas of his power, of his wisdom, and of his goodness?", "떠오르는 해가 동녘 지평선 위로 그 불타는 영광을 쏘아 올릴 필요도 없고, 사나운 바람이 동굴에서 뛰쳐나와 우뚝한 숲을 뒤흔들 필요도 없으며, 갈라진 구름이 들판에 홍수를 쏟아부을 필요도 없습니다. 다시 말하건대, 이 가운데 어느 것도 그분의 위엄을 선포할 필요가 없습니다."], ["It is not necessary that the rising sun should dart his fiery glories over the eastern horizon; nor that the boisterous winds should rush from their caverns, and shake the lofty forest; nor that the opening clouds should pour their deluges on the plains: it is not necessary, I say, that any of these should proclaim his majesty: there is not an insect, not a vegetable, of so low an order in the creation as not to be honoured with bearing marks of the attributes of its great Creator; marks not only of his power, but of his wisdom and goodness.", "창조물 가운데 아무리 낮은 등급의 곤충이라도, 식물이라도, 그 위대한 창조주의 속성을 새긴 표지를 영광스럽게 지니지 않은 것은 없습니다. 그분의 권능뿐 아니라 지혜와 선하심의 표지를 말입니다."], ["Man alone, the king of this globe, the last and greatest work of the Supreme Being, below the sun; man alone hath basely dishonoured his own nature; and by dishonesty, cruelty, ingratitude, and treachery, hath called his Maker's goodness in question, by puzzling us to account how a benevolent being should form so foolish and so vile an animal.", "오직 인간만이, 이 지구의 왕이요, 태양 아래에서 지존하신 존재의 마지막이자 가장 위대한 작품인 인간만이, 비열하게도 제 본성에 욕을 돌렸습니다. 그리고 부정직과 잔인함, 배은망덕과 배반으로써 제 창조주의 선하심을 의문에 부쳤으니, 자애로운 존재가 어찌하여 이토록 어리석고 비천한 짐승을 빚으셨는지를 헤아리느라 우리를 곤혹스럽게 만든 것입니다."], ["Yet this is the being from whose conversation you think, I suppose, that I have been unfortunately restrained, and without whose blessed society, life, in your opinion, must be tedious and insipid.”", "그런데도 이것이 바로, 제가 안타깝게도 그와의 대화로부터 차단되어 있고, 또 그 복된 무리와 함께하지 못하니 당신 생각으로는 제 삶이 따분하고 무미건조할 수밖에 없다고 여기시는, 바로 그 존재로군요.\""]], [["“In the former part of what you said,” replied Jones, “I most heartily and readily concur; but I believe, as well as hope, that the abhorrence which you express for mankind in the conclusion, is much too general. Indeed, you here fall into an error, which in my little experience I have observed to be a very common one, by taking the character of mankind from the worst and basest among them; whereas, indeed, as an excellent writer observes, nothing should be esteemed as characteristical of a species, but what is to be found among the best and most perfect individuals of that species. This error, I believe, is generally committed by those who from want of proper caution in the choice of their friends and acquaintance, have suffered injuries from bad and worthless men; two or three instances of which are very unjustly charged on all human nature.”", "\"방금 말씀하신 앞부분에는,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"제가 진심으로 또 기꺼이 동의합니다. 그러나 결론에서 인류를 향해 드러내신 그 혐오는, 너무 지나치게 일반화된 것이라고 저는 믿고 또 바랍니다. 정말이지, 어르신께서는 여기서 한 가지 오류에 빠지셨는데, 제 짧은 경험으로 보아도 매우 흔한 오류입니다. 인류 가운데 가장 나쁘고 가장 비열한 자들에게서 인류의 성격을 끌어내신 것이지요. 어느 훌륭한 작가가 말했듯, 사실 어떤 종(種)의 특징으로 삼아야 할 것은, 그 종 가운데 가장 뛰어나고 가장 완전한 개체들에게서 찾아볼 수 있는 것뿐인데 말입니다. 이 오류는, 제 믿음으로는, 벗이나 지인을 고를 때 마땅한 신중함이 부족하여 나쁘고 보잘것없는 자들에게서 해를 입은 이들이 흔히 저지르는 것입니다. 그런 두세 가지 사례를 두고 인간 본성 전체에 부당하게 죄를 씌우는 것이지요.\""]], [["“I think I had experience enough of it,”", "\"저는 그것을 충분히 겪어 봤다고 생각합니다.\""], ["answered the other: “my first mistress and my first friend betrayed me in the basest manner, and in matters which threatened to be of the worst of consequences--even to bring me to a shameful death.”", "다른 이가 대답했다. \"제 첫 연인과 제 첫 벗이 더없이 비열한 방식으로 저를 배신했고, 그것도 최악의 결과로 이어질 뻔한 일들에서—심지어 저를 수치스러운 죽음으로 몰아갈 뻔한 일들에서 그리했습니다.\""]], [["“But you will pardon me,”", "\"하지만 용서하십시오.\""], ["cries Jones, “if I desire you to reflect who that mistress and who that friend were.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"그 연인이 누구였고 그 벗이 누구였는지 한번 돌이켜 보시기를 청하고 싶습니다."], ["What better, my good sir, could be expected in love derived from the stews, or in friendship first produced and nourished at the gaming-table?", "어르신, 매음굴에서 비롯된 사랑이나, 도박판에서 처음 생겨나 자라난 우정에서 그보다 더 나은 무엇을 기대할 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["To take the characters of women from the former instance, or of men from the latter, would be as unjust as to assert that air is a nauseous and unwholesome element, because we find it so in a jakes.", "앞의 사례에서 여자의 성격을 끌어내거나 뒤의 사례에서 남자의 성격을 끌어내는 것은, 변소에서 그렇다는 이유로 공기가 역겹고 해로운 원소라고 단언하는 것만큼이나 부당한 일입니다."], ["I have lived but a short time in the world, and yet have known men worthy of the highest friendship, and women of the highest love.”", "저는 이 세상에서 짧은 시간밖에 살지 않았지만, 그래도 더없이 높은 우정을 받을 만한 남자들과, 더없이 높은 사랑을 받을 만한 여자들을 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["young man,”", "젊은이,\" 이방인이 대답했다."], ["answered the stranger, “you have lived, you confess, but a very short time in the world: I was somewhat older than you when I was of the same opinion.”", "\"당신 스스로 고백했듯이, 당신은 이 세상에서 아주 짧은 시간밖에 살지 않았습니다. 저도 당신과 같은 생각을 품었을 때는 당신보다 다소 나이가 위였지요.\""]], [["“You might have remained so still,” replies Jones, “if you had not been unfortunate, I will venture to say incautious, in the placing your affections.", "\"어르신께서 애정을 두는 일에서 불운하지만 않으셨다면—감히 말하건대 부주의하지만 않으셨다면—지금도 여전히 그 생각을 간직하고 계셨을 겁니다.\""], ["If there was, indeed, much more wickedness in the world than there is, it would not prove such general assertions against human nature, since much of this arrives by mere accident, and many a man who commits evil is not totally bad and corrupt in his heart.", "존스가 대답했다. \"설령 세상에 지금보다 훨씬 많은 악이 있다 한들, 그것이 인간 본성에 대한 그런 일반적 단언을 입증해 주지는 못할 것입니다."], ["In truth, none seem to have any title to assert human nature to be necessarily and universally evil, but those whose own minds afford them one instance of this natural depravity; which is not, I am convinced, your case.”", "그 악의 상당 부분은 그저 우연으로 일어나는 것이고, 악을 저지르는 많은 사람도 그 마음속까지 온통 나쁘고 타락한 것은 아니니까요. 사실, 인간 본성이 필연적이고 보편적으로 악하다고 단언할 자격을 가진 자는, 오직 제 마음속에서 그 타고난 사악함의 한 사례를 찾아내는 자들뿐인데, 그것이 어르신의 경우가 아님을 저는 확신합니다.\""]], [["“And such,”", "\"그런 자들이야말로,\""], ["said the stranger, “will be always the most backward to assert any such thing.", "이방인이 말했다. \"언제나 그런 말을 입에 올리기를 가장 꺼리는 법이지요."], ["Knaves will no more endeavour to persuade us of the baseness of mankind, than a highwayman will inform you that there are thieves on the road.", "악당들이 인류의 비열함을 우리에게 납득시키려 애쓰지 않는 것은, 노상강도가 길에 도둑이 있다고 당신에게 일러 주지 않는 것과 같습니다."], ["This would, indeed, be a method to put you on your guard, and to defeat their own purposes.", "그렇게 한다면 당신을 경계하게 만들어 제 목적을 그르치는 셈이 될 테니까요."], ["For which reason, though knaves, as I remember, are very apt to abuse particular persons, yet they never cast any reflection on human nature in general.”", "그런 까닭에, 제가 기억하기로 악당들은 특정한 개인을 헐뜯기는 무척 좋아해도, 인간 본성 일반에 대해서는 결코 어떤 비난도 던지지 않습니다.\""], ["The old gentleman spoke this so warmly, that as Jones despaired of making a convert, and was unwilling to offend, he returned no answer.", "노신사가 어찌나 열을 올려 이 말을 하던지, 존스는 그를 설득해 생각을 바꾸게 하기를 단념했고 또 그의 비위를 거스르고 싶지도 않아서 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["The day now began to send forth its first streams of light, when Jones made an apology to the stranger for having staid so long, and perhaps detained him from his rest.", "이제 날이 밝아 첫 빛줄기를 내보내기 시작하자, 존스는 너무 오래 머무른 것에 대해, 또 어쩌면 그가 쉬는 것을 방해했을지도 모르는 일에 대해 이방인에게 사과했다."], ["The stranger answered, “He never wanted rest less than at present; for that day and night were indifferent seasons to him; and that he commonly made use of the former for the time of his repose and of the latter for his walks and lucubrations.", "이방인이 대답하기를, \"지금처럼 휴식이 필요 없을 때도 없습니다. 제게는 낮과 밤이 아무런 차이도 없는 시간이며, 보통 낮은 휴식의 시간으로, 밤은 산책과 밤늦은 사색의 시간으로 쓰니까요."], ["However,”", "그건 그렇고,\""], ["said he, “it is now a most lovely morning, and if you can bear any longer to be without your own rest or food, I will gladly entertain you with the sight of some very fine prospects which I believe you have not yet seen.”", "그가 말했다. \"지금 무척 사랑스러운 아침이 밝았으니, 당신이 휴식이나 음식 없이 조금 더 버틸 수 있다면, 아직 못 보셨을 듯한 아주 멋진 풍경 몇 가지를 보여 드리며 기꺼이 대접하겠습니다.\""]], [["Jones very readily embraced this offer, and they immediately set forward together from the cottage.", "존스는 이 제안을 흔쾌히 받아들였고, 둘은 곧장 함께 오두막에서 길을 나섰다."], ["As for Partridge, he had fallen into a profound repose just as the stranger had finished his story; for his curiosity was satisfied, and the subsequent discourse was not forcible enough in its operation to conjure down the charms of sleep.", "파트리지로 말하자면, 이방인이 이야기를 막 끝냈을 때 깊은 잠에 빠져 있었다. 그의 호기심은 충족되었고, 뒤이은 담론은 그 잠의 마력을 물리칠 만큼 강한 힘을 부리지 못했던 것이다."], ["Jones therefore left him to enjoy his nap; and as the reader may perhaps be at this season glad of the same favour, we will here put an end to the eighth book of our history.", "그래서 존스는 그가 낮잠을 즐기도록 내버려 두었다. 그리고 독자께서도 이쯤에서 어쩌면 같은 호의를 반기실지 모르니, 우리는 이 이야기의 여덟 번째 권을 여기서 마치기로 하자."]], [["Of those who lawfully may, and of those who may not, write such histories as this.", "열두 시간을 담은 이야기. 이런 이야기를 쓸 자격이 있는 자와 없는 자에 대하여."]], [["Among other good uses for which I have thought proper to institute these several introductory chapters, I have considered them as a kind of mark or stamp, which may hereafter enable a very indifferent reader to distinguish what is true and genuine in this historic kind of writing, from what is false and counterfeit. Indeed, it seems likely that some such mark may shortly become necessary, since the favourable reception which two or three authors have lately procured for their works of this nature from the public, will probably serve as an encouragement to many others to undertake the like. Thus a swarm of foolish novels and monstrous romances will be produced, either to the great impoverishing of booksellers, or to the great loss of time and depravation of morals in the reader; nay, often to the spreading of scandal and calumny, and to the prejudice of the characters of many worthy and honest people.", "내가 이 여러 머리말 장을 따로 두는 것이 마땅하다고 여긴 데에는 여러 가지 좋은 쓰임새가 있는데, 그중 하나로 나는 이것들을 일종의 표지나 낙인쯤으로 생각해 왔다. 앞으로 어지간히 무딘 독자라도 이런 역사류 글에서 무엇이 참되고 진짜인지, 무엇이 거짓이고 가짜인지를 가려낼 수 있게 해 주는 표지 말이다. 실로 이런 표지가 머지않아 필요해질 듯도 하다. 요즘 두세 작가가 이런 종류의 작품으로 세상의 호의적인 환대를 얻은 일이, 아마도 다른 많은 이들에게 같은 일에 손대 보라고 부추기는 계기가 될 테니 말이다. 그리하여 어리석은 소설과 괴상한 연애담이 떼 지어 쏟아져 나올 것이다. 그것은 서적상을 크게 가난하게 만들거나, 아니면 독자의 시간을 크게 허비하게 하고 그 도덕을 타락시킬 것이다. 아니, 흔히는 추문과 비방을 퍼뜨려, 점잖고 정직한 많은 사람들의 명예를 해치는 데까지 이를 것이다."]], [["I question not but the ingenious author of the Spectator was principally induced to prefix Greek and Latin mottos to every paper, from the same consideration of guarding against the pursuit of those scribblers, who having no talents of a writer but what is taught by the writing-master, are yet nowise afraid nor ashamed to assume the same titles with the greatest genius, than their good brother in the fable was of braying in the lion's skin.", "나는 그 재기 넘치는 《스펙테이터》의 저자가 글마다 그리스어와 라틴어 제사(題詞)를 앞머리에 붙인 것도 주로 같은 고려에서 비롯되었으리라 의심치 않는다. 즉, 작가로서의 재능이라고는 글씨 선생에게 배운 것밖에 없으면서도, 가장 위대한 천재와 똑같은 이름을 감히 내거는 데 두려움도 부끄러움도 없는 그런 끄적이는 무리들의 추격을 막으려는 것이었다. 마치 우화 속 그 어리석은 형제가 사자 가죽을 뒤집어쓰고 당나귀 울음을 우는 데 거리낌이 없었던 것처럼 말이다."]], [["By the device therefore of his motto, it became impracticable for any man to presume to imitate the Spectators, without understanding at least one sentence in the learned languages.", "그리하여 그는 제사라는 장치 덕분에, 적어도 그 학식 있는 언어로 된 한 문장은 이해하지 못하고서는 누구도 감히 《스펙테이터》를 흉내 내려 들 수 없게 만들었다."], ["In the same manner I have now secured myself from the imitation of those who are utterly incapable of any degree of reflection, and whose learning is not equal to an essay.", "같은 방식으로 나도 이제, 조금의 성찰조차 도무지 해낼 줄 모르고 그 학식이 한 편의 에세이에도 못 미치는 자들의 모방으로부터 나 자신을 지켜 둔 셈이다."]], [["I would not be here understood to insinuate, that the greatest merit of such historical productions can ever lie in these introductory chapters; but, in fact, those parts which contain mere narrative only, afford much more encouragement to the pen of an imitator, than those which are composed of observation and reflection.", "여기서 내가, 이런 역사류 작품의 가장 큰 가치가 이 머리말 장들에 있다고 넌지시 말하는 것으로 오해되지는 않기를 바란다. 사실 단순한 서사만 담은 부분이야말로, 관찰과 성찰로 이루어진 부분보다 모방자의 붓에 훨씬 더 큰 부추김을 준다."], ["Here I mean such imitators as Rowe was of Shakespear, or as Horace hints some of the Romans were of Cato, by bare feet and sour faces.", "여기서 내가 말하는 모방자란, 로(Rowe)가 셰익스피어를 흉내 낸 것 같은 부류, 혹은 호라티우스가 넌지시 일렀듯 어떤 로마인들이 맨발과 신 얼굴만으로 카토를 흉내 낸 것 같은 부류를 뜻한다."]], [["To invent good stories, and to tell them well, are possibly very rare talents, and yet I have observed few persons who have scrupled to aim at both: and if we examine the romances and novels with which the world abounds, I think we may fairly conclude, that most of the authors would not have attempted to show their teeth (if the expression may be allowed me) in any other way of writing; nor could indeed have strung together a dozen sentences on any other subject whatever.", "좋은 이야기를 지어내는 것과 그것을 잘 들려주는 것은 어쩌면 아주 드문 재능일 텐데, 그런데도 나는 이 두 가지 모두를 겨냥하는 데 거리낌을 느끼는 사람을 거의 보지 못했다. 그리고 세상에 넘쳐 나는 연애담과 소설들을 살펴본다면, 우리는 이렇게 단언해도 무방하리라 본다. 그 작가 대부분은 다른 어떤 종류의 글쓰기로는 (이런 표현을 허락해 준다면) 이빨을 드러내 보일 엄두조차 내지 못했으리라고. 아니, 실은 다른 어떤 주제로도 열두 문장을 이어 엮어 낼 수조차 없었으리라고."]], [["Scribimus indocti doctique passim,[*]", "배운 자나 못 배운 자나 가리지 않고 우리는 쓴다,[*]"]], [["[*] --Each desperate blockhead dares to write: Verse is the trade of every living wight.--FRANCIS.", "[*] 그 어떤 못난 멍청이도 감히 글을 쓰려 든다. 운문은 살아 있는 모든 자의 생업이로다. — 프랜시스."]], [["may be more truly said of the historian and biographer, than of any other species of writing; for all the arts and sciences (even criticism itself) require some little degree of learning and knowledge. Poetry, indeed, may perhaps be thought an exception; but then it demands numbers, or something like numbers: whereas, to the composition of novels and romances, nothing is necessary but paper, pens, and ink, with the manual capacity of using them. This, I conceive, their productions show to be the opinion of the authors themselves: and this must be the opinion of their readers, if indeed there be any such.", "이 말은 다른 어떤 종류의 글쓰기보다도 역사가와 전기 작가에게 더 진실하게 들어맞는다. 모든 기예와 학문은 (비평조차도) 약간의 배움과 지식을 요하기 때문이다. 시(詩)는 어쩌면 예외라 여겨질지도 모르겠다. 하지만 시는 운율, 혹은 운율 비슷한 무언가를 요구한다. 반면 소설과 연애담을 짓는 데는 종이와 펜, 잉크, 그리고 그것들을 손으로 부릴 능력 외에는 아무것도 필요치 않다. 이것이 작가들 스스로의 견해임을 그들의 작품이 보여 준다고 나는 생각한다. 그리고 그 독자라는 것이 정말 존재한다면, 이것이 그들 독자의 견해이기도 할 것이다."]], [["Hence we are to derive that universal contempt which the world, who always denominate the whole from the majority, have cast on all historical writers who do not draw their materials from records.", "여기서 우리는, 기록에서 그 재료를 길어 내지 않는 모든 역사 저술가들에게 세상이 던지는 그 보편적인 경멸이 어디서 비롯되는지를 짐작할 수 있다. 세상이란 늘 다수를 가지고 전체를 일컫는 법이니까."], ["And it is the apprehension of this contempt that hath made us so cautiously avoid the term romance, a name with which we might otherwise have been well enough contented.", "그리고 우리가 그토록 조심스레 '연애담'이라는 말을 피해 온 것도 바로 이 경멸이 두려워서다. 안 그랬다면 우리는 그 이름에 그런대로 만족했을지도 모른다."], ["Though, as we have good authority for all our characters, no less indeed than the vast authentic doomsday-book of nature, as is elsewhere hinted, our labours have sufficient title to the name of history.", "하지만 다른 곳에서 넌지시 일렀듯, 우리는 우리의 모든 인물에 대해 든든한 전거(典據)를 갖고 있으니, 그것은 다름 아닌 자연이라는 저 방대하고도 진실한 심판의 장부이다. 그러니 우리의 노고는 '역사'라는 이름을 얻을 충분한 자격이 있다."], ["Certainly they deserve some distinction from those works, which one of the wittiest of men regarded only as proceeding from a pruritus, or indeed rather from a looseness of the brain.", "분명 우리의 노고는, 가장 재치 있는 어떤 사람이 그저 가려움증, 아니 차라리 두뇌의 헐거움에서 나온 것으로만 여겼던 그런 작품들과는 얼마쯤 구별될 자격이 있다."]], [["But besides the dishonour which is thus cast on one of the most useful as well as entertaining of all kinds of writing, there is just reason to apprehend, that by encouraging such authors we shall propagate much dishonour of another kind; I mean to the characters of many good and valuable members of society; for the dullest writers, no more than the dullest companions, are always inoffensive.", "그러나 이렇게 하여, 모든 글쓰기 가운데 가장 유익하면서도 가장 재미있는 종류의 하나에 불명예가 씌워지는 것 외에도, 또 다른 종류의 불명예를 우리가 퍼뜨리게 되리라는 걱정에는 그만한 까닭이 있다. 내가 말하는 것은 사회의 점잖고 값진 많은 구성원의 명예를 두고 하는 말이다."], ["They have both enough of language to be indecent and abusive.", "가장 둔한 글쟁이도 가장 둔한 동석자와 다를 바 없이 늘 무해하지만은 않기 때문이다."], ["And surely if the opinion just above cited be true, we cannot wonder that works so nastily derived should be nasty themselves, or have a tendency to make others so.", "둘 다 무례하고 험한 말을 늘어놓을 만큼의 언어는 충분히 갖추고 있다. 그리고 앞서 인용한 그 견해가 참이라면, 그토록 추잡한 데서 비롯된 작품이 그 자체로 추잡하거나 남들을 추잡하게 만드는 경향이 있다 한들 놀랄 일이 못 된다."]], [["To prevent therefore, for the future, such intemperate abuses of leisure, of letters, and of the liberty of the press, especially as the world seems at present to be more than usually threatened with them, I shall here venture to mention some qualifications, every one of which are in a pretty high degree necessary to this order of historians.", "그러므로 앞으로 이런 무절제한 여가의 남용, 글의 남용, 출판 자유의 남용을 막기 위해 — 특히 지금 세상이 평소보다 더 그런 위협에 시달리는 듯하니 — 나는 여기서 감히 몇 가지 자질을 들어 보려 한다. 그 하나하나가 이 부류의 역사가에게 상당히 높은 정도로 꼭 필요한 것들이다."]], [["The first is, genius, without a full vein of which no study, says Horace, can avail us. By genius I would understand that power or rather those powers of the mind, which are capable of penetrating into all things within our reach and knowledge, and of distinguishing their essential differences. These are no other than invention and judgment; and they are both called by the collective name of genius, as they are of those gifts of nature which we bring with us into the world. Concerning each of which many seem to have fallen into very great errors; for by invention, I believe, is generally understood a creative faculty, which would indeed prove most romance writers to have the highest pretensions to it; whereas by invention is really meant no more (and so the word signifies) than discovery, or finding out; or to explain it at large, a quick and sagacious penetration into the true essence of all the objects of our contemplation. This, I think, can rarely exist without the concomitancy of judgment; for how we can be said to have discovered the true essence of two things, without discerning their difference, seems to me hard to conceive. Now this last is the undisputed province of judgment, and yet some few men of wit have agreed with all the dull fellows in the world in representing these two to have been seldom or never the property of one and the same person.", "첫째는 천부의 재능이다. 호라티우스의 말마따나, 이것이 풍부히 흐르지 않고서는 어떤 공부도 우리에게 소용이 없다. 천부의 재능이라 함은, 우리의 손과 지식이 닿는 범위 안의 모든 것을 꿰뚫어 보고 그것들의 본질적인 차이를 분별해 낼 수 있는 정신의 힘, 아니 차라리 그 여러 힘을 뜻한다. 이는 다름 아닌 발상과 판단이다. 그리고 이 둘은, 우리가 세상에 태어날 때 지니고 나오는 자연의 선물이라는 점에서, 천부의 재능이라는 한 이름으로 통틀어 불린다. 그런데 그 각각에 대해 많은 사람이 매우 큰 오류에 빠진 듯하다. 발상이라 하면 흔히 창조하는 능력으로 이해되는데, 만일 그렇다면 대부분의 연애담 작가야말로 그 능력을 가장 높이 주장할 자격이 있는 셈이 될 것이다. 그러나 발상이 정말로 뜻하는 바는 (이 말이 본디 그러하듯) 발견, 즉 찾아내는 것에 지나지 않는다. 좀 더 풀어 말하자면, 우리가 사색하는 모든 대상의 참된 본질을 빠르고 슬기롭게 꿰뚫어 보는 것이다. 이것은 판단이 함께하지 않고서는 좀처럼 존재할 수 없다고 나는 생각한다. 두 사물의 차이를 가려내지 않고서 어떻게 그 둘의 참된 본질을 발견했다 말할 수 있겠는가, 내게는 그것이 도무지 그려지지 않는다. 그런데 바로 이 후자야말로 누구도 다투지 않는 판단의 영역이다. 그런데도 재치 있는 몇몇 사람은 세상의 모든 둔한 자들과 똑같이, 이 둘이 한 사람 안에 같이 깃든 적이 거의, 아니 전혀 없다고 말하는 데 뜻을 같이했다."]], [["But though they should be so, they are not sufficient for our purpose, without a good share of learning; for which I could again cite the authority of Horace, and of many others, if any was necessary to prove that tools are of no service to a workman, when they are not sharpened by art, or when he wants rules to direct him in his work, or hath no matter to work upon. All these uses are supplied by learning; for nature can only furnish us with capacity; or, as I have chose to illustrate it, with the tools of our profession; learning must fit them for use, must direct them in it, and, lastly, must contribute part at least of the materials. A competent knowledge of history and of the belles-lettres is here absolutely necessary; and without this share of knowledge at least, to affect the character of an historian, is as vain as to endeavour at building a house without timber or mortar, or brick or stone. Homer and Milton, who, though they added the ornament of numbers to their works, were both historians of our order, were masters of all the learning of their times.", "그러나 설령 이 둘을 다 갖추었다 해도, 상당한 배움이 더해지지 않고서는 우리의 목적에 충분치 않다. 이 점에 대해서도 호라티우스를 비롯해 많은 이들의 권위를 다시 끌어다 댈 수 있겠지만, 그럴 필요까지 있을까. 연장이란 기예로 날을 세우지 않으면, 또는 일을 이끌어 줄 규칙이 없거나 손댈 재료가 없으면, 일꾼에게 아무 쓸모가 없다는 것을 증명하는 데 말이다. 이 모든 쓰임은 배움이 채워 준다. 자연은 우리에게 그저 역량만을, 혹은 내가 즐겨 비유했듯이 우리 직업의 연장만을 마련해 줄 뿐이다. 배움은 그것들을 쓸 수 있게 다듬어 주고, 쓰는 길을 일러 주며, 끝으로 적어도 재료의 일부는 보태 주어야 한다. 여기서는 역사와 순수문학에 대한 충분한 지식이 절대로 필요하다. 적어도 이만한 지식 없이 역사가의 자격을 흉내 내려는 것은, 목재도 회반죽도 벽돌도 돌도 없이 집을 지으려 애쓰는 것만큼이나 헛된 일이다. 호메로스와 밀턴은, 비록 자기네 작품에 운율이라는 장식을 더하긴 했어도 둘 다 우리와 같은 부류의 역사가였는데, 그들은 자기 시대의 모든 학식의 대가였다."]], [["Again, there is another sort of knowledge, beyond the power of learning to bestow, and this is to be had by conversation.", "또한 배움의 힘으로는 베풀 수 없는, 또 다른 종류의 지식이 있다. 이것은 사람들과 어울려 대화하는 가운데 얻어진다."], ["So necessary is this to the understanding the characters of men, that none are more ignorant of them than those learned pedants whose lives have been entirely consumed in colleges, and among books; for however exquisitely human nature may have been described by writers, the true practical system can be learnt only in the world.", "이것은 사람들의 성격을 이해하는 데 어찌나 긴요한지, 평생을 대학과 책 사이에서만 보낸 그 학식 있는 책상물림들이야말로 사람의 성격에 가장 어두운 자들이다. 작가들이 인간 본성을 아무리 절묘하게 묘사해 놓았다 해도, 그 참된 실천적 체계는 오직 세상 속에서만 배울 수 있는 까닭이다."], ["Indeed the like happens in every other kind of knowledge.", "실로 다른 모든 종류의 지식에서도 마찬가지다."], ["Neither physic nor law are to be practically known from books.", "의술도 법률도 책만으로는 실제로 익힐 수 없다."], ["Nay, the farmer, the planter, the gardener, must perfect by experience what he hath acquired the rudiments of by reading.", "아니, 농부도, 농장주도, 정원사도, 읽어서 그 기초를 익힌 것을 경험으로 완성해야 한다."], ["How accurately soever the ingenious Mr Miller may have described the plant, he himself would advise his disciple to see it in the garden.", "그 솜씨 좋은 밀러 씨가 식물을 아무리 정확히 묘사했다 한들, 그조차도 제 제자에게 정원에 나가 직접 보라고 권할 것이다."], ["As we must perceive, that after the nicest strokes of a Shakespear or a Jonson, of a Wycherly or an Otway, some touches of nature will escape the reader, which the judicious action of a Garrick, of a Cibber, or a Clive,[*] can convey to him; so, on the real stage, the character shows himself in a stronger and bolder light than he can be described.", "셰익스피어나 존슨, 위철리나 오트웨이의 더없이 섬세한 필치 뒤에도 자연의 어떤 손길은 독자를 비껴가기 마련이며, 그것을 독자에게 전해 주는 것은 개릭이나 시버, 혹은 클라이브[*]의 빈틈없는 연기임을 우리가 알아야 하듯이, 실제 무대 위에서 인물은 글로 그려질 수 있는 것보다 더 강렬하고 더 또렷한 빛으로 제 모습을 드러낸다."], ["And if this be the case in those fine and nervous descriptions which great authors themselves have taken from life, how much more strongly will it hold when the writer himself takes his lines not from nature, but from books?", "위대한 작가들 스스로가 삶에서 길어 낸 그 정교하고 힘찬 묘사에서도 사정이 이러할진대, 작가가 그 글줄을 자연이 아니라 책에서 끌어왔을 때는 이 말이 얼마나 더 강하게 들어맞겠는가?"], ["Such characters are only the faint copy of a copy, and can have neither the justness nor spirit of an original.", "그런 인물들은 베낀 것을 다시 베낀 흐릿한 모사에 지나지 않으니, 원본의 정확함도 그 생기도 가질 수 없다."]], [["[*] There is a peculiar propriety in mentioning this great actor, and these two most justly celebrated actresses, in this place, as they have all formed themselves on the study of nature only, and not on the imitation of their predecessors.", "[*] 이 위대한 배우와, 더없이 마땅한 명성을 누리는 이 두 여배우를 이 자리에서 언급하는 데에는 각별한 이유가 있다. 이들은 모두 선배들의 모방이 아니라 오직 자연을 연구하는 데서 스스로를 빚어냈기 때문이다."], ["Hence they have been able to excel all who have gone before them; a degree of merit which the servile herd of imitators can never possibly arrive at.", "그리하여 이들은 앞서간 모든 이를 능가할 수 있었으니, 그것은 노예처럼 따라 하는 모방자의 무리는 결코 다다를 수 없는 경지의 가치다."]], [["Now this conversation in our historian must be universal, that is, with all ranks and degrees of men; for the knowledge of what is called high life will not instruct him in low; nor, e converso, will his being acquainted with the inferior part of mankind teach him the manners of the superior. And though it may be thought that the knowledge of either may sufficiently enable him to describe at least that in which he hath been conversant, yet he will even here fall greatly short of perfection; for the follies of either rank do in reality illustrate each other. For instance, the affectation of high life appears more glaring and ridiculous from the simplicity of the low; and again, the rudeness and barbarity of this latter, strikes with much stronger ideas of absurdity, when contrasted with, and opposed to, the politeness which controuls the former. Besides, to say the truth, the manners of our historian will be improved by both these conversations; for in the one he will easily find examples of plainness, honesty, and sincerity; in the other of refinement, elegance, and a liberality of spirit; which last quality I myself have scarce ever seen in men of low birth and education.", "그런데 우리 역사가의 이 대화는 두루 걸쳐야 한다. 다시 말해 온갖 신분과 계층의 사람들과 두루 나누는 것이라야 한다. 이른바 상류층의 삶을 안다고 해서 하류층을 알게 되는 것은 아니며, 거꾸로 인류의 낮은 부류와 친숙하다 해서 높은 이들의 풍속을 배우게 되는 것도 아니기 때문이다. 어느 한쪽을 아는 것만으로도 적어도 자기가 익숙한 그 부류만큼은 충분히 그려낼 수 있으리라 여길지도 모르겠으나, 그렇더라도 여기서마저 완벽에는 크게 못 미칠 것이다. 두 신분의 어리석음은 실은 서로를 비춰 주기 때문이다. 이를테면 상류층의 허세는 하류층의 소박함과 견줄 때 한층 더 두드러지고 우스꽝스러워 보인다. 또 거꾸로, 후자의 거칠고 야만스러움은 전자를 다스리는 그 세련됨과 대비되어 맞세워질 때 훨씬 더 강렬한 부조리의 인상으로 다가온다. 게다가 솔직히 말하면, 우리 역사가의 몸가짐도 이 두 가지 사귐을 통해 나아질 것이다. 한쪽에서는 꾸밈없음과 정직함과 진실함의 본보기를 손쉽게 발견할 것이요, 다른 쪽에서는 세련됨과 우아함, 그리고 너그러운 기품을 발견할 것이기 때문이다. 그런데 이 마지막 자질만큼은, 낮은 태생과 교육을 받은 사람들에게서 나 스스로 본 적이 거의 없다."]], [["Nor will all the qualities I have hitherto given my historian avail him, unless he have what is generally meant by a good heart, and be capable of feeling.", "그러나 내가 지금껏 우리 역사가에게 부여한 모든 자질도, 그가 흔히 말하는 따뜻한 마음을 지니고 또 느낄 줄 알지 못한다면 그에게 아무 소용이 없을 것이다."], ["The author who will make me weep, says Horace, must first weep himself.", "호라티우스의 말마따나, 나를 울리려는 작가는 먼저 제가 울어야 한다."], ["In reality, no man can paint a distress well which he doth not feel while he is painting it; nor do I doubt, but that the most pathetic and affecting scenes have been writ with tears.", "실로 어떤 사람도 자기가 그려 내는 동안 스스로 느끼지 못하는 고통을 잘 그려 낼 수는 없다. 또한 가장 애틋하고 가슴을 울리는 장면들이 눈물로 쓰였다는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다."], ["In the same manner it is with the ridiculous.", "우스운 것에 대해서도 마찬가지다. 나는 확신한다."], ["I am convinced I never make my reader laugh heartily but where I have laughed before him; unless it should happen at any time, that instead of laughing with me he should be inclined to laugh at me.", "내가 독자보다 먼저 웃어 본 대목이 아니고서는 결코 독자를 진심으로 웃기지 못했음을. 다만 어쩌다 가끔, 독자가 나와 함께 웃는 대신 나를 비웃고 싶어지는 일이 생길 수는 있겠다."], ["Perhaps this may have been the case at some passages in this chapter, from which apprehension I will here put an end to it.", "어쩌면 이 장의 어떤 대목들에서 바로 그런 일이 벌어졌을지도 모르겠다. 그런 걱정이 들기에, 나는 여기서 이 장을 끝맺으려 한다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["Containing a very surprizing adventure indeed, which Mr Jones met with in his walk with the Man of the Hill.", "참으로 매우 놀라운 모험을 담은 장. 존스가 산속 노인과 함께 걷던 길에서 마주친 일이라."]], [["Aurora now first opened her casement, Anglice the day began to break, when Jones walked forth in company with the stranger, and mounted Mazard Hill; of which they had no sooner gained the summit than one of the most noble prospects in the world presented itself to their view, and which we would likewise present to the reader, but for two reasons: first, we despair of making those who have seen this prospect admire our description; secondly, we very much doubt whether those who have not seen it would understand it.", "이제 막 오로라가 자기 창문을 처음 열어젖혔을 때, 영어로 옮기자면 날이 막 밝아 오기 시작했을 때, 존스는 낯선 노인과 함께 길을 나서 매자드 언덕에 올랐다. 두 사람이 그 꼭대기에 막 다다르자마자 세상에서 가장 장엄한 풍경 가운데 하나가 그들의 눈앞에 펼쳐졌으니, 우리 또한 그것을 독자에게 펼쳐 보이고 싶지만 두 가지 이유로 그러지 못한다. 첫째, 이 풍경을 직접 본 이들이 우리의 묘사에 감탄하도록 만들 자신이 도무지 없기 때문이고, 둘째, 그것을 보지 못한 이들이 우리의 묘사를 이해할 수 있을지 매우 의심스럽기 때문이다."]], [["Jones stood for some minutes fixed in one posture, and directing his eyes towards the south; upon which the old gentleman asked, What he was looking at with so much attention?", "존스는 몇 분 동안 한 자세로 꼼짝 않고 서서 두 눈을 남쪽으로 향하고 있었다. 그러자 노신사가 무엇을 그토록 골똘히 바라보고 있느냐고 물었다."], ["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["sir,”", "어르신,\""], ["answered he with a sigh, “I was endeavouring to trace out my own journey hither.", "그가 한숨을 내쉬며 대답했다. \"저는 여기까지 온 제 여정을 더듬어 보려 애쓰고 있었습니다."], ["Good heavens!", "맙소사!"], ["what a distance is Gloucester from us!", "글로스터가 우리에게서 얼마나 먼지요!"], ["What a vast track of land must be between me and my own home!”--“Ay, ay, young gentleman,”", "저와 제 고향 사이에 얼마나 광대한 땅이 가로놓여 있을까요!\" \"그래, 그래, 젊은이,\""], ["cries the other, “and by your sighing, from what you love better than your own home, or I am mistaken.", "노인이 외쳤다. \"게다가 자네의 그 한숨으로 미루어 보건대, 자네 고향보다 더 사랑하는 무언가에서부터 떨어진 거리겠지."], ["I perceive now the object of your contemplation is not within your sight, and yet I fancy you have a pleasure in looking that way.”", "내가 잘못 짚은 게 아니라면 말일세. 이제 보니 자네가 골똘히 생각하는 그 대상은 자네 눈에 들어오지 않는 곳에 있군."], ["Jones answered with a smile, “I find, old friend, you have not yet forgot the sensations of your youth.", "그런데도 자네는 그쪽을 바라보는 데서 즐거움을 느끼는 모양이야.\" 존스가 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["I own my thoughts were employed as you have guessed.”", "\"보아하니 어르신께서는 아직 젊은 날의 그 감정을 잊지 않으셨군요. 제 생각이 어르신께서 짐작하신 그대로였음을 인정합니다.\""]], [["They now walked to that part of the hill which looks to the north-west, and which hangs over a vast and extensive wood.", "이제 두 사람은 북서쪽을 바라보는 언덕 쪽으로 걸어갔는데, 그곳은 광대하게 펼쳐진 숲 위로 비스듬히 내려다보이는 자리였다."], ["Here they were no sooner arrived than they heard at a distance the most violent screams of a woman, proceeding from the wood below them.", "그곳에 막 다다르자마자 그들은 저 아래 숲에서 들려오는, 한 여인의 더없이 격렬한 비명을 멀리서 들었다."], ["Jones listened a moment, and then, without saying a word to his companion (for indeed the occasion seemed sufficiently pressing), ran, or rather slid, down the hill, and, without the least apprehension or concern for his own safety, made directly to the thicket, whence the sound had issued.", "존스는 잠시 귀를 기울이더니, 동행에게 한마디 말도 없이(실로 사정이 충분히 다급해 보였으므로) 언덕을 달려, 아니 차라리 미끄러져 내려갔고, 제 안전에 대한 일말의 두려움이나 걱정도 없이 그 소리가 새어 나온 덤불을 향해 곧장 나아갔다."]], [["He had not entered far into the wood before he beheld a most shocking sight indeed, a woman stript half naked, under the hands of a ruffian, who had put his garter round her neck, and was endeavouring to draw her up to a tree.", "숲속으로 멀리 들어가기도 전에 그는 참으로 끔찍하기 그지없는 광경을 목격했다. 한 여인이 반쯤 벌거벗긴 채 어느 흉한의 손아귀에 잡혀 있었는데, 그자는 자기 대님을 그녀의 목에 둘러 그녀를 나무에 매달아 끌어올리려 하고 있었다."], ["Jones asked no questions at this interval, but fell instantly upon the villain, and made such good use of his trusty oaken stick that he laid him sprawling on the ground before he could defend himself, indeed almost before he knew he was attacked; nor did he cease the prosecution of his blows till the woman herself begged him to forbear, saying, she believed he had sufficiently done his business.", "존스는 이 순간 아무것도 묻지 않고 즉시 그 악당에게 달려들어, 믿음직한 떡갈나무 막대기를 어찌나 잘 휘둘렀던지 상대가 미처 방어하기도 전에, 실로 자신이 공격당하는 줄 알아채기도 전에 그를 땅바닥에 나뒹굴게 만들었다. 그리고 여인이 직접 이제 그만두라고, 할 일은 충분히 다 한 것 같다고 애원할 때까지 그는 매질을 멈추지 않았다."]], [["The poor wretch then fell upon her knees to Jones, and gave him a thousand thanks for her deliverance.", "그러자 그 가련한 여인은 존스 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 자신을 구해 준 데 대해 천 번이나 감사를 올렸다."], ["He presently lifted her up, and told her he was highly pleased with the extraordinary accident which had sent him thither for her relief, where it was so improbable she should find any; adding, that Heaven seemed to have designed him as the happy instrument of her protection.", "그는 곧바로 그녀를 일으켜 세우며, 그녀가 도움을 받으리라곤 도무지 기대할 수 없던 이런 곳으로 자신을 보내어 그녀를 구하게 한 그 기묘한 우연이 무척이나 기쁘다고 말했다. 그러고는 하늘이 자신을 그녀를 지켜 줄 행복한 도구로 점지하신 듯하다고 덧붙였다."], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니에요,\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["answered she, “I could almost conceive you to be some good angel; and, to say the truth, you look more like an angel than a man in my eye.”", "\"저는 당신을 어떤 선한 천사라고 여기고 싶을 정도예요. 사실대로 말하자면, 제 눈에 당신은 사람이라기보다 천사처럼 보이는걸요.\""], ["Indeed he was a charming figure; and if a very fine person, and a most comely set of features, adorned with youth, health, strength, freshness, spirit, and good-nature, can make a man resemble an angel, he certainly had that resemblance.", "실로 그는 매혹적인 모습이었다. 매우 훌륭한 풍채와 더없이 준수한 이목구비에 젊음과 건강, 힘과 생기, 활력과 선한 천성까지 어우러져 사람을 천사처럼 보이게 할 수 있다면, 그는 분명 그러한 닮음을 지니고 있었다."]], [["The redeemed captive had not altogether so much of the human-angelic species: she seemed to be at least of the middle age, nor had her face much appearance of beauty; but her cloaths being torn from all the upper part of her body, her breasts, which were well formed and extremely white, attracted the eyes of her deliverer, and for a few moments they stood silent, and gazing at each other; till the ruffian on the ground beginning to move, Jones took the garter which had been intended for another purpose, and bound both his hands behind him.", "구원받은 포로는 그처럼 인간이면서 천사 같은 부류에 그리 가깝지는 않았다. 그녀는 적어도 중년에 접어든 듯했고, 얼굴에도 미모라 할 만한 것이 그리 많지 않았다."], ["And now, on contemplating his face, he discovered, greatly to his surprize, and perhaps not a little to his satisfaction, this very person to be no other than ensign Northerton.", "그러나 옷이 상체에서 죄다 찢겨 나간 탓에, 모양 좋고 더없이 흰 그녀의 가슴이 구해 준 이의 눈길을 끌었으니, 두 사람은 잠시 말없이 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다. 그러다 땅바닥에 쓰러져 있던 흉한이 움직이기 시작하자, 존스는 본래 다른 용도로 쓰일 뻔했던 그 대님을 집어 그자의 두 손을 등 뒤로 묶었다."], ["Nor had the ensign forgotten his former antagonist, whom he knew the moment he came to himself.", "그러고 나서 그자의 얼굴을 들여다보던 그는, 크게 놀라며, 어쩌면 적잖이 흡족해하며, 바로 이 인물이 다름 아닌 노더턴 소위임을 알아차렸다."], ["His surprize was equal to that of Jones; but I conceive his pleasure was rather less on this occasion.", "그 소위 또한 옛 적수를 잊지 않았으니, 정신이 들자마자 곧바로 그를 알아보았다. 그의 놀라움은 존스의 놀라움에 못지않았으나, 이 경우 그가 느낀 기쁨은 다소 덜했으리라 짐작된다."]], [["Jones helped Northerton upon his legs, and then looking him stedfastly in the face, “I fancy, sir,”", "존스는 노더턴이 다리로 설 수 있게 부축해 일으킨 다음, 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 응시하며 말했다."], ["said he, “you did not expect to meet me any more in this world, and I confess I had as little expectation to find you here.", "\"이보시오, 당신은 이 세상에서 나를 다시 만나리라곤 생각지 못했겠지요. 나 또한 당신을 여기서 마주치리라곤 거의 기대하지 못했음을 고백하오."], ["However, fortune, I see, hath brought us once more together, and hath given me satisfaction for the injury I have received, even without my own knowledge.”", "하지만 보다시피 운명이 우리를 다시 한번 맺어 주었고, 내가 스스로 알지도 못하는 새에 내가 입은 상처에 대한 보상을 내게 안겨 주었구려.\""]], [["“It is very much like a man of honour, indeed,”", "\"등 뒤에서 사람을 때려눕혀 보상을 챙긴다니, 참으로 명예로운 사내다운 짓이오.\" 노더턴이 대꾸했다."], ["answered Northerton, “to take satisfaction by knocking a man down behind his back.", "\"게다가 나는 여기서 당신에게 만족을 줄 처지도 못 되오. 칼이 없으니 말이오."], ["Neither am I capable of giving you satisfaction here, as I have no sword; but if you dare behave like a gentleman, let us go where I can furnish myself with one, and I will do by you as a man of honour ought.”", "하지만 당신이 신사답게 굴 배짱이 있다면, 내가 칼을 구할 수 있는 곳으로 갑시다. 그러면 명예로운 사내라면 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 내 당신을 상대해 주겠소.\""]], [["“Doth it become such a villain as you are,”", "\"당신 같은 악당이,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “to contaminate the name of honour by assuming it? But I shall waste no time in discourse with you.", "\"명예라는 이름을 함부로 입에 올려 그것을 더럽혀도 된단 말이오?"], ["Justice requires satisfaction of you now, and shall have it.”", "그러나 당신과 입씨름하느라 시간을 허비하지는 않겠소."], ["Then turning to the woman, he asked her, if she was near her home; or if not, whether she was acquainted with any house in the neighbourhood, where she might procure herself some decent cloaths, in order to proceed to a justice of the peace.", "정의가 지금 당신에게 응분의 대가를 요구하니, 반드시 받아 낼 것이오.\" 그러고는 여인 쪽으로 돌아서서, 집이 가까운지, 그렇지 않다면 이 근방에 점잖은 옷가지를 마련해 치안판사에게 갈 채비를 할 만한 집을 알고 있는지 물었다."]], [["She answered she was an entire stranger in that part of the world.", "그녀는 자신이 이 지방에서는 전혀 낯선 사람이라고 대답했다. 그러자 존스는 문득 생각이 나서, 가까운 곳에 길을 일러 줄 벗이 한 사람 있다고 말했다."], ["Jones then recollecting himself, said, he had a friend near who would direct them; indeed, he wondered at his not following; but, in fact, the good Man of the Hill, when our heroe departed, sat himself down on the brow, where, though he had a gun in his hand, he with great patience and unconcern had attended the issue.", "실로 그는 그 노인이 따라오지 않은 것을 의아해했다. 그러나 사실인즉, 우리의 주인공이 떠나갈 때 그 선량한 산속 노인은 언덕 비탈에 주저앉아, 손에 총을 쥐고 있으면서도 큰 인내심과 무심함으로 그 일의 결말을 지켜보고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Jones then stepping without the wood, perceived the old man sitting as we have just described him; he presently exerted his utmost agility, and with surprizing expedition ascended the hill.", "이윽고 존스가 숲 밖으로 걸어 나오자, 방금 우리가 묘사한 그대로 앉아 있는 노인이 눈에 들어왔다. 그는 곧 온 힘을 다해 날쌔게 움직여 놀라운 속도로 언덕을 올랐다."]], [["The old man advised him to carry the woman to Upton, which, he said, was the nearest town, and there he would be sure of furnishing her with all manner of conveniencies.", "노인은 그 여인을 업턴으로 데려가라고 일러 주었는데, 그곳이 가장 가까운 마을이니 거기서라면 그녀에게 온갖 편의를 마련해 줄 수 있으리라는 것이었다."], ["Jones having received his direction to the place, took his leave of the Man of the Hill, and, desiring him to direct Partridge the same way, returned hastily to the wood.", "존스는 그곳으로 가는 길을 일러 받고 산속 노인에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 파트리지에게도 같은 길로 오라고 일러 달라 부탁하고는 서둘러 숲으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["Our heroe, at his departure to make this enquiry of his friend, had considered, that as the ruffian's hands were tied behind him, he was incapable of executing any wicked purposes on the poor woman.", "우리의 주인공은 벗에게 이 일을 물으러 떠나면서, 흉한의 두 손이 등 뒤로 묶여 있으니 그 가련한 여인에게 어떤 못된 짓도 저지를 수 없으리라 헤아렸다."], ["Besides, he knew he should not be beyond the reach of her voice, and could return soon enough to prevent any mischief.", "게다가 그는 자신이 그녀의 목소리가 닿는 범위를 벗어나지 않을 것이며, 어떤 해코지든 막을 만큼 충분히 빨리 돌아올 수 있으리라는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["He had moreover declared to the villain, that if he attempted the least insult, he would be himself immediately the executioner of vengeance on him.", "더욱이 그는 그 악당에게, 만일 조금이라도 무례한 짓을 시도한다면 자신이 직접 그자에게 복수의 형 집행자가 되겠노라 선언해 두었다."], ["But Jones unluckily forgot, that though the hands of Northerton were tied, his legs were at liberty; nor did he lay the least injunction on the prisoner that he should not make what use of these he pleased.", "그러나 존스는 불운하게도, 노더턴의 두 손은 묶였어도 두 다리는 자유롭다는 사실을 잊고 말았다. 또한 그 죄수에게 그 다리를 제멋대로 쓰지 말라는 명령은 조금도 내려 두지 않았다."], ["Northerton therefore having given no parole of that kind, thought he might without any breach of honour depart; not being obliged, as he imagined, by any rules, to wait for a formal discharge.", "그리하여 노더턴은 그런 약속을 한 적이 없으니 명예를 조금도 저버림 없이 떠나도 된다고 여겼다. 자기 생각에 어떤 규칙으로도 정식 방면을 기다릴 의무가 없었던 것이다."], ["He therefore took up his legs, which were at liberty, and walked off through the wood, which favoured his retreat; nor did the woman, whose eyes were perhaps rather turned toward her deliverer, once think of his escape, or give herself any concern or trouble to prevent it.", "그래서 그는 자유로운 두 다리를 놀려 숲을 가로질러 걸어 나갔고, 숲은 그의 도주를 도와주었다. 그 여인 또한, 어쩌면 두 눈이 자신을 구해 준 이 쪽으로 더 쏠려 있었던 탓인지, 그자의 도주를 한 번도 떠올리지 않았으며 그것을 막으려 애쓰거나 신경 쓰지도 않았다."]], [["Jones therefore, at his return, found the woman alone.", "그리하여 존스는 돌아왔을 때 여인 혼자만 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 노더턴을 찾는 데 얼마간 시간을 쓰려 했으나, 그녀가 이를 허락하지 않았다."], ["He would have spent some time in searching for Northerton, but she would not permit him; earnestly entreating that he would accompany her to the town whither they had been directed.", "그녀는 두 사람이 일러 받은 그 마을까지 자신과 동행해 달라고 간절히 청했다. \"그자가 달아난 일이라면,\""], ["“As to the fellow's escape,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 조금도 마음이 편치 않을 것이 없어요."], ["said she, “it gives me no uneasiness; for philosophy and Christianity both preach up forgiveness of injuries.", "철학도 기독교도 모두 받은 상처를 용서하라 가르치니까요. 다만 당신께는, 제가 끼치는 수고가 마음에 걸립니다."], ["But for you, sir, I am concerned at the trouble I give you; nay, indeed, my nakedness may well make me ashamed to look you in the face; and if it was not for the sake of your protection, I should wish to go alone.”", "아니, 실로 이 헐벗은 꼴 탓에 당신 얼굴을 마주 보기조차 부끄럽군요. 당신의 보호가 필요한 것만 아니라면 저는 혼자 가고 싶을 지경이에요.\""]], [["Jones offered her his coat; but, I know not for what reason, she absolutely refused the most earnest solicitations to accept it.", "존스는 그녀에게 자기 외투를 내밀었으나, 무슨 까닭에서인지 그녀는 그것을 받아 달라는 더없이 간곡한 청을 한사코 거절했다."], ["He then begged her to forget both the causes of her confusion.", "그러자 그는 그녀의 그 두 가지 거북함을 모두 잊으라고 권했다."], ["“With regard to the former,”", "\"전자에 관해서라면,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “I have done no more than my duty in protecting you; and as for the latter, I will entirely remove it, by walking before you all the way; for I would not have my eyes offend you, and I could not answer for my power of resisting the attractive charms of so much beauty.”", "\"저는 당신을 보호하는 제 본분을 다한 것뿐이고, 후자에 관해서라면 제가 길 내내 당신 앞에서 걸어 그것을 완전히 없애 드리지요. 제 눈이 당신을 불쾌하게 만들기를 원치 않거니와, 이토록 빼어난 아름다움이 지닌 매혹의 마력에 제가 능히 맞설 자신도 없으니 말입니다.\""]], [["Thus our heroe and the redeemed lady walked in the same manner as Orpheus and Eurydice marched heretofore; but though I cannot believe that Jones was designedly tempted by his fair one to look behind him, yet as she frequently wanted his assistance to help her over stiles, and had besides many trips and other accidents, he was often obliged to turn about.", "그리하여 우리의 주인공과 구원받은 여인은 옛날 오르페우스와 에우리디케가 걸어갔던 것과 똑같은 모양새로 걸음을 옮겼다. 그러나 나는 존스가 그 어여쁜 동행에게 일부러 꾐을 받아 뒤를 돌아보았다고는 믿을 수 없지만, 그녀가 계단 울타리를 넘을 때마다 그의 도움을 자주 필요로 했고, 게다가 여러 차례 발을 헛디디는 등 갖가지 일을 겪었던 까닭에, 그는 부득이 자주 뒤를 돌아보아야 했다."], ["However, he had better fortune than what attended poor Orpheus, for he brought his companion, or rather follower, safe into the famous town of Upton.", "그래도 그는 가련한 오르페우스에게 닥쳤던 것보다는 나은 운을 누렸으니, 자기 동행을, 아니 차라리 자기 뒤를 따르던 이를 그 유명한 업턴 마을까지 무사히 데려갔던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["The arrival of Mr Jones with his lady at the inn; with a very full description of the battle of Upton.", "존스 씨가 자신의 동행한 부인과 함께 여관에 도착하다. 그리고 업튼 전투에 관한 매우 상세한 묘사."]], [["Though the reader, we doubt not, is very eager to know who this lady was, and how she fell into the hands of Mr Northerton, we must beg him to suspend his curiosity for a short time, as we are obliged, for some very good reasons which hereafter perhaps he may guess, to delay his satisfaction a little longer.", "독자께서 이 부인이 누구이며 어떻게 노더턴 씨의 손아귀에 떨어지게 되었는지 몹시 알고 싶어 하시리라는 것을 우리는 의심치 않는다. 하지만 잠시만 그 호기심을 눌러 두시기를 청해야겠다. 우리로서는, 나중에 어쩌면 짐작하시게 될 매우 그럴듯한 몇 가지 이유 때문에, 그 만족을 조금 더 미룰 수밖에 없기 때문이다."]], [["Mr Jones and his fair companion no sooner entered the town, than they went directly to that inn which in their eyes presented the fairest appearance to the street.", "존스 씨와 그의 아리따운 동행은 마을에 들어서자마자 거리를 향해 가장 그럴듯한 모습으로 서 있던 여관으로 곧장 향했다."], ["Here Jones, having ordered a servant to show a room above stairs, was ascending, when the dishevelled fair, hastily following, was laid hold on by the master of the house, who cried, “Heyday, where is that beggar wench going?", "여기서 존스는 하인에게 위층 방을 안내하라 이른 뒤 계단을 오르고 있었는데, 머리가 헝클어진 미인이 황급히 그 뒤를 따르자 여관 주인이 그녀를 붙잡으며 소리쳤다. \"이런, 저 거지 계집은 어딜 가는 거야?"], ["Stay below stairs, I desire you.”", "아래층에 좀 있으라고.\""], ["But Jones at that instant thundered from above, “Let the lady come up,”", "그러나 바로 그 순간 존스가 위에서 우레와 같이 호령했다."], ["in so authoritative a voice, that the good man instantly withdrew his hands, and the lady made the best of her way to the chamber.", "\"그 부인을 올라오게 하시오.\" 그 목소리가 어찌나 위엄이 서려 있던지, 착한 주인은 곧바로 손을 놓았고 부인은 서둘러 방으로 향했다."]], [["Here Jones wished her joy of her safe arrival, and then departed, in order, as he promised, to send the landlady up with some cloaths.", "여기서 존스는 그녀가 무사히 도착한 것을 기뻐하며 축하해 준 뒤, 약속한 대로 여관 안주인을 시켜 옷가지를 올려 보내려고 자리를 떴다."], ["The poor woman thanked him heartily for all his kindness, and said, she hoped she should see him again soon, to thank him a thousand times more.", "가엾은 여인은 그의 모든 친절에 진심으로 고마움을 표하며, 곧 다시 그를 만나 천 번이라도 더 감사 인사를 드릴 수 있기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["During this short conversation, she covered her white bosom as well as she could possibly with her arms; for Jones could not avoid stealing a sly peep or two, though he took all imaginable care to avoid giving any offence.", "이 짧은 대화를 나누는 동안 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 두 팔로 자신의 흰 가슴을 가렸다. 존스가 아무리 조금이라도 무례를 범하지 않으려 온갖 신경을 썼다 해도, 슬쩍 한두 번 곁눈질하는 것까지는 어쩔 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Our travellers had happened to take up their residence at a house of exceeding good repute, whither Irish ladies of strict virtue, and many northern lasses of the same predicament, were accustomed to resort in their way to Bath.", "우리의 나그네들은 우연히도 평판이 매우 좋은 집에 묵게 되었는데, 이곳은 정조가 엄격한 아일랜드 부인들과, 같은 처지의 여러 북부 처녀들이 바스로 가는 길에 즐겨 들르던 곳이었다."], ["The landlady therefore would by no means have admitted any conversation of a disreputable kind to pass under her roof.", "그러니 안주인은 자기 지붕 아래에서 평판을 떨어뜨릴 만한 어떤 거래도 결코 용납하려 하지 않았다."], ["Indeed, so foul and contagious are all such proceedings, that they contaminate the very innocent scenes where they are committed, and give the name of a bad house, or of a house of ill repute, to all those where they are suffered to be carried on.", "실로 그런 행위들이란 어찌나 더럽고 전염성이 강한지, 그것이 저질러진 무고한 장소마저 오염시키고, 그런 일이 행해지도록 내버려 둔 모든 집에 '나쁜 집' 혹은 '평판 나쁜 집'이라는 이름을 붙이고 만다."]], [["Not that I would intimate that such strict chastity as was preserved in the temple of Vesta can possibly be maintained at a public inn.", "물론 베스타 신전에서 지켜졌던 만큼의 엄정한 순결이 공공 여관에서 유지될 수 있다고 넌지시 말하려는 것은 아니다."], ["My good landlady did not hope for such a blessing, nor would any of the ladies I have spoken of, or indeed any others of the most rigid note, have expected or insisted on any such thing.", "내 착한 안주인도 그런 복은 바라지 않았고, 앞서 말한 부인들 가운데 누구도, 아니 그 누구보다 가장 엄격한 축에 드는 이들조차 그런 것을 기대하거나 고집하지는 않았을 것이다."], ["But to exclude all vulgar concubinage, and to drive all whores in rags from within the walls, is within the power of every one.", "그러나 모든 천박한 동거를 막고, 누더기를 걸친 창녀들을 담장 안에서 죄다 몰아내는 일은 누구든 할 수 있는 일이다."], ["This my landlady very strictly adhered to, and this her virtuous guests, who did not travel in rags, would very reasonably have expected of her.", "내 안주인은 이 원칙을 아주 엄격히 지켰고, 누더기 차림으로 여행하지 않는 그녀의 정숙한 손님들도 그녀에게 그쯤은 마땅히 기대했을 것이다."]], [["Now it required no very blameable degree of suspicion to imagine that Mr Jones and his ragged companion had certain purposes in their intention, which, though tolerated in some Christian countries, connived at in others, and practised in all, are however as expressly forbidden as murder, or any other horrid vice, by that religion which is universally believed in those countries.", "자, 존스 씨와 그의 누더기 동행이 어떤 속셈을 품고 있다고 상상하는 데에는 그다지 비난받을 만큼의 의심도 필요치 않았다. 그런 일이란 어떤 기독교 국가에서는 용인되고, 다른 곳에서는 눈감아 주며, 모든 곳에서 실제로 행해지지만, 그럼에도 그 나라들에서 두루 믿어지는 종교에 의해서는 살인이나 그 밖의 끔찍한 악덕만큼이나 명백히 금지된 일이었다."], ["The landlady, therefore, had no sooner received an intimation of the entrance of the above-said persons than she began to meditate the most expeditious means for their expulsion.", "그러므로 안주인은 위에서 말한 인물들이 들어왔다는 기별을 받자마자, 그들을 쫓아낼 가장 신속한 방법을 궁리하기 시작했다."], ["In order to this, she had provided herself with a long and deadly instrument, with which, in times of peace, the chambermaid was wont to demolish the labours of the industrious spider.", "이를 위해 그녀는 길고도 무시무시한 도구 하나를 준비했는데, 그것은 평화로운 시절에는 객실 하녀가 부지런한 거미가 애써 지은 집을 허무는 데 쓰던 물건이었다."], ["In vulgar phrase, she had taken up the broomstick, and was just about to sally from the kitchen, when Jones accosted her with a demand of a gown and other vestments, to cover the half-naked woman upstairs.", "쉽게 말하자면, 그녀는 빗자루를 집어 들고 막 부엌에서 출격하려던 참이었는데, 바로 그때 존스가 위층의 반쯤 헐벗은 여인을 가릴 가운과 그 밖의 옷가지를 달라고 청해 왔다."]], [["Nothing can be more provoking to the human temper, nor more dangerous to that cardinal virtue, patience, than solicitations of extraordinary offices of kindness on behalf of those very persons with whom we are highly incensed.", "우리가 몹시 분노하고 있는 바로 그 사람들을 위해 특별한 친절을 베풀어 달라는 부탁만큼 인간의 성미를 돋우는 것도, 인내라는 으뜸가는 미덕에 위태로운 것도 없다."], ["For this reason Shakespear hath artfully introduced his Desdemona soliciting favours for Cassio of her husband, as the means of inflaming, not only his jealousy, but his rage, to the highest pitch of madness; and we find the unfortunate Moor less able to command his passion on this occasion, than even when he beheld his valued present to his wife in the hands of his supposed rival.", "이런 까닭에 셰익스피어는 교묘하게도 그의 데스데모나로 하여금 남편에게 캐시오를 위해 호의를 베풀어 달라 청하게 함으로써, 남편의 질투뿐 아니라 분노까지 광기의 절정에 이르도록 부추기는 수단으로 삼았다. 그리하여 우리는 그 불운한 무어인이, 아내에게 준 소중한 선물을 연적으로 의심하는 자의 손에서 보았을 때보다도, 오히려 이 대목에서 자신의 격정을 다스리지 못함을 보게 된다."], ["In fact, we regard these efforts as insults on our understanding, and to such the pride of man is very difficultly brought to submit.", "사실 우리는 이런 노력을 우리의 분별에 대한 모욕으로 여기며, 인간의 자존심이란 거기에 좀처럼 굴복하려 들지 않는 법이다."]], [["My landlady, though a very good-tempered woman, had, I suppose, some of this pride in her composition, for Jones had scarce ended his request, when she fell upon him with a certain weapon, which, though it be neither long, nor sharp, nor hard, nor indeed threatens from its appearance with either death or wound, hath been however held in great dread and abhorrence by many wise men--nay, by many brave ones; insomuch, that some who have dared to look into the mouth of a loaded cannon, have not dared to look into a mouth where this weapon was brandished; and rather than run the hazard of its execution, have contented themselves with making a most pitiful and sneaking figure in the eyes of all their acquaintance.", "내 안주인은 비록 성품이 매우 너그러운 여인이긴 했으나, 짐작건대 그녀에게도 이런 자존심이 얼마간 섞여 있었던 모양이다. 존스가 부탁을 채 마치기도 전에 그녀는 어떤 무기를 들고 그에게 달려들었던 것이다. 그 무기란 길지도, 날카롭지도, 단단하지도 않으며, 겉모습만 보아서는 죽음이나 상처를 위협하지도 않건만, 그럼에도 수많은 현명한 사람들이, 아니, 수많은 용감한 사람들조차 크게 두려워하고 몹시 꺼려 온 물건이었다. 그리하여 장전된 대포의 아가리를 감히 들여다본 자들 가운데서도, 이 무기가 휘둘러지는 입속만큼은 차마 들여다보지 못하고, 그것이 가져올 결과의 위험을 무릅쓰느니 차라리 자기 지인들 모두의 눈앞에서 더없이 가련하고 비굴한 꼴을 보이는 편을 택한 이들이 있을 정도였다."]], [["To confess the truth, I am afraid Mr Jones was one of these; for though he was attacked and violently belaboured with the aforesaid weapon, he could not be provoked to make any resistance; but in a most cowardly manner applied, with many entreaties, to his antagonist to desist from pursuing her blows; in plain English, he only begged her with the utmost earnestness to hear him; but before he could obtain his request, my landlord himself entered into the fray, and embraced that side of the cause which seemed to stand very little in need of assistance.", "사실대로 고백하자면, 존스 씨도 그런 부류 가운데 하나가 아니었을까 싶다. 앞서 말한 그 무기에 공격을 받고 사정없이 두들겨 맞으면서도, 그는 도무지 저항할 마음이 동하지 않았기 때문이다. 도리어 그는 더없이 비겁한 태도로 거듭 애원하며, 자기 적수에게 매질을 그만두라 청할 뿐이었다. 쉬운 말로 하자면, 그는 그저 자기 말을 들어 달라고 더없이 간절하게 빌기만 했던 것이다. 그러나 그가 그 청을 이루기도 전에, 내 여관 주인이 몸소 싸움판에 끼어들어 도움이 거의 필요 없어 보이는 쪽 편을 들고 나섰다."]], [["There are a sort of heroes who are supposed to be determined in their chusing or avoiding a conflict by the character and behaviour of the person whom they are to engage.", "세상에는 자기가 맞붙어야 할 상대의 성품과 행실을 보고 싸움을 택할지 피할지를 정한다고 하는 영웅들이 있다. 이들은 상대를 잘 알아본다고들 하는데, 존스도 자기 상대 여인을 잘 알아보았던 모양이다."], ["These are said to know their men, and Jones, I believe, knew his woman; for though he had been so submissive to her, he was no sooner attacked by her husband, than he demonstrated an immediate spirit of resentment, and enjoined him silence under a very severe penalty; no less than that, I think, of being converted into fuel for his own fire.", "그녀에게는 그토록 고분고분했으면서도, 그녀의 남편에게 공격당하자마자 그는 곧바로 분개하는 기색을 드러내며, 매우 가혹한 벌을 내걸고 그에게 입을 다물라 명령했던 것이다. 그 벌이란 다름 아니라, 내 생각에는 그 자신의 불 땔감으로 만들어 버리겠다는 정도였다."]], [["The husband, with great indignation, but with a mixture of pity, answered, “You must pray first to be made able.", "그 남편은 크나큰 분노에, 그러나 얼마간의 동정심을 섞어 대답했다. \"그럴 힘부터 갖게 해 달라고 먼저 기도나 하시지."], ["I believe I am a better man than yourself; ay, every way, that I am;”", "내가 댁보다 더 나은 사내라고. 그래, 어느 모로 보나 말이야.\""], ["and presently proceeded to discharge half-a-dozen whores at the lady above stairs, the last of which had scarce issued from his lips, when a swinging blow from the cudgel that Jones carried in his hand assaulted him over the shoulders.", "그러고는 곧장 위층의 부인을 향해 '창녀'라는 욕설을 대여섯 번이나 퍼붓기 시작했는데, 그 마지막 말이 채 입에서 나오기도 전에 존스가 손에 쥐고 있던 몽둥이가 휘둘러져 그의 어깨를 후려쳤다."]], [["It is a question whether the landlord or the landlady was the most expeditious in returning this blow.", "이 일격을 되갚는 데 여관 주인과 안주인 가운데 누가 더 빨랐는지는 가늠하기 어렵다. 손이 비어 있던 내 여관 주인은 주먹으로 덤벼들었고, 착한 안주인은 빗자루를 치켜들어 존스의 머리를 겨냥했으니, 만일 이 빗자루의 낙하가 막히지 않았더라면 아마 싸움도, 더불어 존스도 그 자리에서 끝장이 났을 것이다."], ["My landlord, whose hands were empty, fell to with his fist, and the good wife, uplifting her broom and aiming at the head of Jones, had probably put an immediate end to the fray, and to Jones likewise, had not the descent of this broom been prevented--not by the miraculous intervention of any heathen deity, but by a very natural though fortunate accident, viz., by the arrival of Partridge; who entered the house at that instant (for fear had caused him to run every step from the hill), and who, seeing the danger which threatened his master or companion (which you chuse to call him), prevented so sad a catastrophe, by catching hold of the landlady's arm, as it was brandished aloft in the air.", "그런데 그것을 막은 것은 어느 이교의 신이 기적처럼 끼어든 덕분이 아니라, 지극히 자연스러우면서도 다행스러운 우연, 곧 파트리지의 도착 덕분이었다. 그는 바로 그 순간 집 안으로 들어섰는데(공포에 사로잡혀 언덕에서부터 한 걸음도 쉬지 않고 내달려 온 터였다), 자기 주인 혹은 동행(어느 쪽으로 부르든 좋을 대로)을 위협하는 위험을 보고는, 허공 높이 휘둘러진 안주인의 팔을 붙잡아 그토록 슬픈 참사를 막아 냈다."]], [["The landlady soon perceived the impediment which prevented her blow; and being unable to rescue her arm from the hands of Partridge, she let fall the broom; and then leaving Jones to the discipline of her husband, she fell with the utmost fury on that poor fellow, who had already given some intimation of himself, by crying, “Zounds! do you intend to kill my friend?”", "안주인은 자기 일격을 가로막은 장애물을 곧 알아챘다. 그러나 파트리지의 손아귀에서 팔을 빼낼 수 없자 빗자루를 떨어뜨리고는, 존스는 남편의 손에 맡겨 둔 채 그 가엾은 친구에게 무서운 기세로 달려들었다. 파트리지는 이미 \"젠장! 내 친구를 죽일 셈이오?\" 하고 외치며 자기 존재를 알린 터였다."]], [["Partridge, though not much addicted to battle, would not however stand still when his friend was attacked; nor was he much displeased with that part of the combat which fell to his share; he therefore returned my landlady's blows as soon as he received them: and now the fight was obstinately maintained on all parts, and it seemed doubtful to which side Fortune would incline, when the naked lady, who had listened at the top of the stairs to the dialogue which preceded the engagement, descended suddenly from above, and without weighing the unfair inequality of two to one, fell upon the poor woman who was boxing with Partridge; nor did that great champion desist, but rather redoubled his fury, when he found fresh succours were arrived to his assistance.", "파트리지는 싸움을 그리 즐기는 위인은 아니었으나, 그래도 친구가 공격당하는 마당에 가만히 서 있으려 하지는 않았다. 게다가 자기 몫으로 떨어진 그 싸움 상대에게는 그리 불만도 없었던지라, 그는 안주인의 매를 받자마자 곧바로 되갚아 주었다. 그리하여 이제 싸움은 모든 진영에서 완강하게 이어졌고, 행운의 여신이 어느 편으로 기울지 의심스러운 가운데, 싸움이 시작되기에 앞서 오고 간 대화를 계단 꼭대기에서 엿듣고 있던 그 헐벗은 부인이 위에서 별안간 내려왔다. 그녀는 둘이 하나를 상대하는 불공평한 형세 따위는 따져 보지도 않고, 파트리지와 주먹다짐을 벌이고 있던 가엾은 여인에게 덤벼들었다. 그러자 그 위대한 용사는 손을 멈추기는커녕, 새로운 원군이 자기를 도우러 도착한 것을 보고 도리어 그 기세를 곱절로 키웠다."]], [["Victory must now have fallen to the side of the travellers (for the bravest troops must yield to numbers) had not Susan the chambermaid come luckily to support her mistress.", "이제 승리는 마땅히 나그네들 편으로 떨어졌을 것이다(아무리 용맹한 군대라도 수에는 굴복하는 법이니). 그런데 그때 객실 하녀 수전이 마침맞게 와서 제 주인을 거들었다."], ["This Susan was as two-handed a wench (according to the phrase) as any in the country, and would, I believe, have beat the famed Thalestris herself, or any of her subject Amazons; for her form was robust and man-like, and every way made for such encounters.", "이 수전은 (그 표현 그대로) 이 고장 누구 못지않게 양손잡이로 억센 계집이었으니, 내 짐작으로는 그 이름난 탈레스트리스 자신이나 그 휘하의 어떤 아마존 여전사라도 너끈히 두들겨 팼을 법했다. 그녀의 몸은 사내처럼 건장하여 이런 싸움판에 안성맞춤으로 만들어진 듯했기 때문이다."], ["As her hands and arms were formed to give blows with great mischief to an enemy, so was her face as well contrived to receive blows without any great injury to herself, her nose being already flat to her face; her lips were so large, that no swelling could be perceived in them, and moreover they were so hard, that a fist could hardly make any impression on them.", "그녀의 손과 팔이 적에게 큰 타격을 입히도록 만들어진 것처럼, 그녀의 얼굴 또한 자기는 별로 다치지 않으면서 매를 받아 내기에 알맞게 생겨먹었다. 코는 이미 얼굴에 납작하게 붙어 있었고, 입술은 어찌나 큼지막한지 부어올라도 표가 나지 않았으며, 게다가 어찌나 단단한지 주먹으로 쳐도 좀처럼 자국조차 남길 수 없었다."], ["Lastly, her cheek-bones stood out, as if nature had intended them for two bastions to defend her eyes in those encounters for which she seemed so well calculated, and to which she was most wonderfully well inclined.", "끝으로 그녀의 광대뼈는 불쑥 튀어나와 있었으니, 마치 자연이 그토록 잘 어울리고 또 그토록 놀라우리만치 좋아하는 이 싸움판에서 그녀의 두 눈을 지킬 보루 두 개로 삼으려 한 것만 같았다."]], [["This fair creature entering the field of battle, immediately filed to that wing where her mistress maintained so unequal a fight with one of either sex.", "이 아리따운 피조물은 전장에 들어서자마자 곧장 한쪽 날개로 진격했으니, 바로 그녀의 주인이 남녀 양쪽을 상대로 그토록 불리한 싸움을 벌이던 자리였다."], ["Here she presently challenged Partridge to single combat.", "여기서 그녀는 대뜸 파트리지에게 일대일 결투를 청했다."], ["He accepted the challenge, and a most desperate fight began between them.", "그는 그 도전을 받아들였고, 둘 사이에 더없이 필사적인 싸움이 시작되었다."]], [["Now the dogs of war being let loose, began to lick their bloody lips; now Victory, with golden wings, hung hovering in the air; now Fortune, taking her scales from her shelf, began to weigh the fates of Tom Jones, his female companion, and Partridge, against the landlord, his wife, and maid; all which hung in exact balance before her; when a good-natured accident put suddenly an end to the bloody fray, with which half of the combatants had already sufficiently feasted. This accident was the arrival of a coach and four; upon which my landlord and landlady immediately desisted from fighting, and at their entreaty obtained the same favour of their antagonists: but Susan was not so kind to Partridge; for that Amazonian fair having overthrown and bestrid her enemy, was now cuffing him lustily with both her hands, without any regard to his request of a cessation of arms, or to those loud exclamations of murder which he roared forth.", "이제 전쟁의 사냥개들이 풀려나 그 피투성이 입술을 핥기 시작했다. 이제 승리의 여신이 황금빛 날개를 펼친 채 허공에서 맴돌았다. 이제 운명의 여신이 선반에서 저울을 꺼내 톰 존스와 그의 여인 동행과 파트리지의 운명을, 여관 주인과 그 아내와 하녀의 운명에 맞대어 달기 시작했다. 그 모든 것이 그녀 앞에서 정확히 균형을 이루고 매달려 있었는데, 그때 마음씨 좋은 우연 하나가 그 피비린내 나는 싸움에 별안간 종지부를 찍었다. 싸움꾼들 가운데 절반은 이미 충분히 잔치를 벌인 뒤였다. 그 우연이란 다름 아닌 사두마차의 도착이었다. 그러자 내 여관 주인과 안주인은 즉시 싸움을 멈추었고, 그들의 간청에 적수들도 똑같이 응해 주었다. 그러나 수전은 파트리지에게 그토록 너그럽지 못했다. 그 아마존 같은 미인은 적을 쓰러뜨려 깔고 앉은 채, 무기를 거두자는 그의 청도, 그가 목청껏 질러 대는 \"사람 죽인다\"는 외침도 아랑곳없이 두 손으로 그를 신나게 후려치고 있었다."]], [["No sooner, however, had Jones quitted the landlord, than he flew to the rescue of his defeated companion, from whom he with much difficulty drew off the enraged chambermaid: but Partridge was not immediately sensible of his deliverance, for he still lay flat on the floor, guarding his face with his hands; nor did he cease roaring till Jones had forced him to look up, and to perceive that the battle was at an end.", "그러나 존스는 여관 주인에게서 풀려나기가 무섭게 패배한 동행을 구하러 날아갔고, 갖은 애를 쓴 끝에 격분한 객실 하녀를 그에게서 떼어 냈다. 그런데 파트리지는 자기가 구출되었다는 사실을 곧바로 알아차리지 못했다. 그는 여전히 바닥에 납작 엎드린 채 두 손으로 얼굴을 감싸고 있었던 것이다. 존스가 억지로 그를 고개 들게 하여 싸움이 끝났음을 깨닫게 하기 전까지, 그는 고함치기를 멈추지 않았다."]], [["The landlord, who had no visible hurt, and the landlady, hiding her well-scratched face with her handkerchief, ran both hastily to the door to attend the coach, from which a young lady and her maid now alighted.", "겉으로 보아 다친 데가 없는 여관 주인과, 잔뜩 할퀸 얼굴을 손수건으로 가린 안주인은 둘 다 황급히 문간으로 달려가 마차를 맞이했다. 마차에서는 이제 젊은 부인 하나와 그 하녀가 내렸다."], ["These the landlady presently ushered into that room where Mr Jones had at first deposited his fair prize, as it was the best apartment in the house.", "안주인은 곧 이들을 존스 씨가 처음에 그의 아리따운 전리품을 들여놓았던 바로 그 방으로 안내했는데, 그곳이 집 안에서 가장 좋은 방이었기 때문이다."], ["Hither they were obliged to pass through the field of battle, which they did with the utmost haste, covering their faces with their handkerchiefs, as desirous to avoid the notice of any one.", "그리로 가려면 이들은 전쟁터를 가로질러야 했고, 누구의 눈에도 띄고 싶지 않은 듯 손수건으로 얼굴을 가린 채 더없이 서둘러 그곳을 지났다. 하지만 그들의 그런 조심은 전혀 부질없는 것이었다."], ["Indeed their caution was quite unnecessary; for the poor unfortunate Helen, the fatal cause of all the bloodshed, was entirely taken up in endeavouring to conceal her own face, and Jones was no less occupied in rescuing Partridge from the fury of Susan; which being happily effected, the poor fellow immediately departed to the pump to wash his face, and to stop that bloody torrent which Susan had plentifully set a-flowing from his nostrils.", "가엾고 불운한 헬레네, 그 모든 유혈극의 치명적 빌미가 된 여인은 제 얼굴을 가리느라 온통 정신이 팔려 있었고, 존스 또한 수전의 분노에서 파트리지를 구해 내느라 그에 못지않게 바빴기 때문이다. 그 일이 다행히 마무리되자, 가엾은 친구는 곧장 펌프로 가서 얼굴을 씻고, 수전이 자기 콧구멍에서 콸콸 쏟아지게 만든 그 핏줄기를 막으려 했다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["In which the arrival of a man of war puts a final end to hostilities, and causes the conclusion of a firm and lasting peace between all parties.", "군함 한 척이 도착하여 적대 행위에 마침내 종지부를 찍고, 모든 당사자 사이에 굳고 오래갈 평화의 결말을 이끌어내는 장."]], [["A serjeant and a file of musqueteers, with a deserter in their custody, arrived about this time.", "이 무렵 하사 한 명과 머스킷 총병 한 무리가, 자기들이 잡아 호송하던 탈영병을 데리고 도착했다."], ["The serjeant presently enquired for the principal magistrate of the town, and was informed by my landlord, that he himself was vested in that office.", "하사는 곧바로 이 고을의 으뜸가는 행정관이 누구냐고 물었고, 주인장으로부터 바로 자기 자신이 그 직책을 맡고 있다는 말을 들었다."], ["He then demanded his billets, together with a mug of beer, and complaining it was cold, spread himself before the kitchen fire.", "그러자 하사는 숙박표를 내놓으라고 요구하면서 맥주 한 잔을 청했고, 날이 춥다고 투덜대며 부엌 화롯불 앞에 몸을 쭉 펴고 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Mr Jones was at this time comforting the poor distressed lady, who sat down at a table in the kitchen, and leaning her head upon her arm, was bemoaning her misfortunes; but lest my fair readers should be in pain concerning a particular circumstance, I think proper here to acquaint them, that before she had quitted the room above stairs, she had so well covered herself with a pillowbeer which she there found, that her regard to decency was not in the least violated by the presence of so many men as were now in the room.", "이때 존스는 그 가엾고 곤경에 처한 부인을 위로하고 있었는데, 부인은 부엌의 탁자에 앉아 팔에 머리를 기댄 채 자신의 불운을 한탄하고 있었다. 그런데 나의 어여쁜 여성 독자들이 어떤 한 가지 사정을 두고 마음을 졸이실까 싶어, 여기서 미리 알려드리는 것이 옳겠다. 부인은 위층 방을 떠나기 전에, 그곳에서 찾아낸 베갯잇 하나로 몸을 잘 가렸기에, 지금 그 방에 모여 있는 그 많은 남자들 앞에서도 그녀의 정숙함은 조금도 손상되지 않았다는 사실을 말이다."]], [["One of the soldiers now went up to the serjeant, and whispered something in his ear; upon which he stedfastly fixed his eyes on the lady, and having looked at her for near a minute, he came up to her, saying, “I ask pardon, madam; but I am certain I am not deceived; you can be no other person than Captain Waters's lady?”", "이때 병사들 가운데 하나가 하사에게 다가가 귀에 대고 무언가를 속삭였다. 그러자 하사는 부인에게 시선을 똑바로 박은 채 일 분 가까이 그녀를 바라보더니, 그녀에게 다가가 말했다. \"용서하십시오, 마님. 그러나 저는 제가 잘못 본 게 아니라고 확신합니다. 마님은 다름 아닌 워터스 대위님의 부인이 아니십니까?\""]], [["The poor woman, who in her present distress had very little regarded the face of any person present, no sooner looked at the serjeant than she presently recollected him, and calling him by his name, answered, “That she was indeed the unhappy person he imagined her to be;” but added, “I wonder any one should know me in this disguise.” To which the serjeant replied, “He was very much surprized to see her ladyship in such a dress, and was afraid some accident had happened to her.”--“An accident hath happened to me, indeed,” says she, “and I am highly obliged to this gentleman” (pointing to Jones) “that it was not a fatal one, or that I am now living to mention it.”--“Whatever the gentleman hath done,” cries the serjeant, “I am sure the captain will make him amends for it; and if I can be of any service, your ladyship may command me, and I shall think myself very happy to have it in my power to serve your ladyship; and so indeed may any one, for I know the captain will well reward them for it.”", "지금의 곤경 속에서 그 자리에 있는 누구의 얼굴에도 거의 눈길을 주지 않던 가엾은 여인은, 하사를 바라보자마자 곧 그를 알아보고는 그의 이름을 부르며 대답했다. \"그래요, 나는 정말로 당신이 짐작하는 그 불행한 사람이에요.\" 그러고는 덧붙였다. \"이런 차림을 한 나를 알아보는 사람이 있다니 놀랍군요.\" 이에 하사가 대꾸했다. \"마님께서 이런 옷차림을 하고 계신 것을 보고 무척 놀랐으며, 마님께 무슨 변고라도 생긴 게 아닐까 걱정했습니다.\" \"변고가 생긴 건 사실이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 그것이 치명적인 일로 끝나지 않은 것, 또 내가 지금 살아서 이 일을 이야기할 수 있는 것은 모두 이 신사분 덕분이니, 나는 그분께 큰 신세를 졌어요.\" 하고 존스를 가리켰다. \"이 신사분이 무슨 일을 하셨든,\" 하사가 외쳤다. \"대위님께서 분명 그에 합당한 보답을 해주실 것입니다. 그리고 제가 무슨 도움이라도 될 수 있다면, 마님께서는 무엇이든 분부만 하십시오. 마님을 위해 힘을 쓸 수 있다면 저는 더없이 기쁠 것입니다. 사실 누구에게나 그럴 것입니다. 대위님께서 그 사람에게 후하게 보답해 주실 것을 제가 잘 알고 있으니까요.\""]], [["The landlady, who heard from the stairs all that past between the serjeant and Mrs Waters, came hastily down, and running directly up to her, began to ask pardon for the offences she had committed, begging that all might be imputed to ignorance of her quality: for, “Lud!", "계단에서 하사와 워터스 부인 사이에 오간 말을 모두 엿들은 안주인은 부랴부랴 내려와 곧장 그녀에게로 달려가더니, 자신이 저지른 무례를 용서해 달라고 빌기 시작했다. 모든 것을 마님의 신분을 몰랐던 탓으로 돌려 달라고 애원하면서 말이다."], ["madam,”", "\"어머나, 마님!\""], ["says she, “how should I have imagined that a lady of your fashion would appear in such a dress?", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"마님 같은 지체 높으신 분이 이런 차림으로 나타나실 줄 제가 어찌 상상이나 했겠어요?"], ["I am sure, madam, if I had once suspected that your ladyship was your ladyship, I would sooner have burnt my tongue out, than have said what I have said; and I hope your ladyship will accept of a gown, till you can get your own cloaths.”", "정말이지, 마님, 만약 제가 마님께서 마님이신 줄 조금이라도 짐작했더라면, 제가 한 말을 입에 담느니 차라리 제 혀를 뽑아 태워 버렸을 거예요. 마님께서 마님 옷을 구하실 때까지 부디 제 가운이라도 받아주시면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Prithee, woman,”", "\"여보세요, 아주머니.\""], ["says Mrs Waters, “cease your impertinence: how can you imagine I should concern myself about anything which comes from the lips of such low creatures as yourself?", "하고 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"그 주제넘은 짓 좀 그만둬요."], ["But I am surprized at your assurance in thinking, after what is past, that I will condescend to put on any of your dirty things.", "당신 같은 천한 사람 입에서 나오는 말 따위에 내가 마음을 쓸 거라고 생각하다니? 그보다 더 놀라운 건, 그런 일이 있고도 내가 당신의 그 더러운 물건을 몸에 걸치는 꼴을 보일 거라고 뻔뻔스레 여기는 당신의 배짱이에요."], ["I would have you know, creature, I have a spirit above that.”", "똑똑히 알아두어요, 이 사람아. 나는 그런 것보다 높은 기개를 지닌 사람이니까.\""]], [["Here Jones interfered, and begged Mrs Waters to forgive the landlady, and to accept her gown: “for I must confess,” cries he, “our appearance was a little suspicious when first we came in; and I am well assured all this good woman did was, as she professed, out of regard to the reputation of her house.”", "여기서 존스가 끼어들어, 워터스 부인에게 안주인을 용서하고 그 가운을 받아 달라고 청했다. \"솔직히 말씀드리자면,\" 하고 그가 외쳤다. \"우리가 처음 들어왔을 때 우리 행색이 조금 수상쩍어 보이긴 했습니다. 그리고 이 선량한 아주머니가 한 일은, 본인이 말하듯, 모두 이 집의 평판을 지키려는 마음에서 비롯된 것이라고 저는 확신합니다.\""]], [["“Yes, upon my truly was it,” says she: “the gentleman speaks very much like a gentleman, and I see very plainly is so; and to be certain the house is well known to be a house of as good reputation as any on the road, and though I say it, is frequented by gentry of the best quality, both Irish and English. I defy anybody to say black is my eye, for that matter. And, as I was saying, if I had known your ladyship to be your ladyship, I would as soon have burnt my fingers as have affronted your ladyship; but truly where gentry come and spend their money, I am not willing that they should be scandalized by a set of poor shabby vermin, that, wherever they go, leave more lice than money behind them; such folks never raise my compassion, for to be certain it is foolish to have any for them; and if our justices did as they ought, they would be all whipt out of the kingdom, for to be certain it is what is most fitting for them. But as for your ladyship, I am heartily sorry your ladyship hath had a misfortune, and if your ladyship will do me the honour to wear my cloaths till you can get some of your ladyship's own, to be certain the best I have is at your ladyship's service.”", "\"네, 정말로 그렇답니다.\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"이 신사분은 말씀하시는 게 영락없는 신사분이세요. 척 보아도 그러신 게 분명하고요. 그리고 확실히 이 집으로 말씀드리자면, 이 길에 있는 어느 집 못지않게 평판 좋은 집으로 널리 알려져 있고, 제 입으로 말하긴 뭣하지만, 아일랜드분이든 영국분이든 가장 지체 높은 양반들이 드나드는 곳이지요. 그 점에 대해선 누구라도 제게 트집을 잡아 보라죠. 그리고 아까도 말씀드렸듯이, 만약 제가 마님께서 마님이신 줄 알았더라면, 마님께 무례를 범하느니 차라리 제 손가락을 태워 버렸을 거예요. 하지만 정말이지, 양반들이 와서 돈을 쓰는 자리에서, 가는 곳마다 돈보다 이를 더 많이 남기고 가는 가난뱅이 비렁뱅이 떼거리한테 양반들이 욕을 보는 건 제가 두고 볼 수 없거든요. 그런 작자들은 도무지 제 동정심을 끌어내지 못해요. 확실히 그런 자들에게 동정을 베푸는 건 어리석은 짓이니까요. 그리고 우리 치안판사님들이 마땅히 하셔야 할 일을 하신다면, 그런 작자들은 죄다 매를 맞고 이 나라에서 쫓겨났을 거예요. 확실히 그게 그들에게 가장 어울리는 일이거든요. 하지만 마님으로 말씀드리자면, 마님께서 변고를 당하신 것이 진심으로 안타깝습니다. 그러니 마님께서 마님 옷을 구하실 때까지 제 옷을 입어 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 제가 가진 가장 좋은 것이 마님 분부대로 마련되어 있답니다.\""]], [["Whether cold, shame, or the persuasions of Mr Jones prevailed most on Mrs Waters, I will not determine, but she suffered herself to be pacified by this speech of my landlady, and retired with that good woman, in order to apparel herself in a decent manner.", "추위 탓이었는지, 부끄러움 탓이었는지, 아니면 존스의 설득이 가장 크게 작용했는지 나는 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 워터스 부인은 안주인의 이 말에 마음이 누그러지도록 자신을 내맡겼고, 그 선량한 여인과 함께 점잖은 차림을 갖추려고 물러갔다."]], [["My landlord was likewise beginning his oration to Jones, but was presently interrupted by that generous youth, who shook him heartily by the hand, and assured him of entire forgiveness, saying, “If you are satisfied, my worthy friend, I promise you I am;” and indeed, in one sense, the landlord had the better reason to be satisfied; for he had received a bellyfull of drubbing, whereas Jones had scarce felt a single blow.", "주인장 역시 존스에게 일장 연설을 시작하려 했으나, 이내 그 너그러운 젊은이에게 가로막혔다. 존스는 그의 손을 다정하게 꼭 잡으며 전적으로 용서한다고 단언하고는 말했다. \"그대가 흡족하다면, 나의 갸륵한 친구여, 나도 흡족하다고 약속하겠소.\" 그런데 실은, 어떤 의미에서는 주인장 쪽이 흡족해할 더 그럴듯한 이유가 있었다. 그는 흠씬 두들겨 맞아 배가 가득 찰 지경이었던 반면, 존스는 단 한 대도 거의 맞지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Partridge, who had been all this time washing his bloody nose at the pump, returned into the kitchen at the instant when his master and the landlord were shaking hands with each other.", "이 내내 펌프 앞에서 피투성이가 된 코를 씻고 있던 파트리지는, 자기 주인과 주인장이 서로 손을 맞잡고 있는 바로 그 순간에 부엌으로 돌아왔다."], ["As he was of a peaceable disposition, he was pleased with those symptoms of reconciliation; and though his face bore some marks of Susan's fist, and many more of her nails, he rather chose to be contented with his fortune in the last battle than to endeavour at bettering it in another.", "그는 평화를 사랑하는 성미였던 터라, 이 화해의 조짐들을 보고 기뻐했다. 그리고 비록 그의 얼굴에는 수전의 주먹 자국이 더러 남아 있고 손톱 자국은 그보다 훨씬 많았지만, 그는 또 한바탕 싸워서 형편을 나아지게 해보려 애쓰기보다 지난 전투에서의 자기 운명에 만족하기로 마음먹었다."]], [["The heroic Susan was likewise well contented with her victory, though it had cost her a black eye, which Partridge had given her at the first onset.", "영웅적인 수전 역시 자신의 승리에 흡족해했다. 비록 그 승리의 대가로 첫 접전 때 파트리지에게 얻어맞은 시퍼런 멍 자국이 눈가에 남기는 했지만 말이다."], ["Between these two, therefore, a league was struck, and those hands which had been the instruments of war became now the mediators of peace.", "그리하여 이 두 사람 사이에 동맹이 맺어졌고, 전쟁의 도구였던 그 손들은 이제 평화의 중재자가 되었다."]], [["Matters were thus restored to a perfect calm; at which the serjeant, though it may seem so contrary to the principles of his profession, testified his approbation.", "이렇게 하여 사태는 완전한 고요로 돌아갔는데, 하사는, 비록 그것이 자기 직업의 원칙에 몹시 어긋나 보일지 몰라도, 이를 두고 찬성의 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["“Why now, that's friendly,”", "\"자, 이제 이래야 화목하지.\""], ["said he; “d--n me, I hate to see two people bear ill-will to one another after they have had a tussel.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"젠장, 한바탕 붙고 난 다음에도 두 사람이 서로 앙심을 품고 있는 꼴은 정말 보기 싫어."], ["The only way when friends quarrel is to see it out fairly in a friendly manner, as a man may call it, either with a fist, or sword, or pistol, according as they like, and then let it be all over; for my own part, d--n me if ever I love my friend better than when I am fighting with him!", "친구끼리 다툴 땐, 말하자면 친구다운 방식으로, 주먹이든 칼이든 권총이든 좋을 대로 골라서 정정당당하게 끝장을 보고는, 그걸로 깨끗이 끝내는 게 유일한 길이지. 나로 말하자면, 젠장, 친구하고 싸울 때만큼 그 친구가 더 좋은 적이 없다니까!"], ["To bear malice is more like a Frenchman than an Englishman.”", "앙심을 품는 건 영국 사람보다 프랑스 사람한테나 어울리는 짓이야.\""]], [["He then proposed a libation as a necessary part of the ceremony at all treaties of this kind.", "그러고 나서 그는, 이런 종류의 모든 조약에서 의식의 필수적인 부분이라며 헌주를 제안했다."], ["Perhaps the reader may here conclude that he was well versed in antient history; but this, though highly probable, as he cited no authority to support the custom, I will not affirm with any confidence.", "어쩌면 독자는 여기서 그가 고대 역사에 정통한 사람이라고 결론지을지도 모르겠다. 그러나 그것은, 비록 충분히 그럴 법한 일이긴 해도, 그가 그 관습을 뒷받침할 어떤 전거도 들지 않았으므로, 나는 자신 있게 단언하지는 않겠다."], ["Most likely indeed it is, that he founded his opinion on very good authority, since he confirmed it with many violent oaths.", "사실 그가 매우 든든한 전거 위에 자신의 견해를 세웠을 가능성이 가장 높기는 하다. 그가 그것을 무수한 거친 맹세로 확인해 주었으니 말이다."]], [["Jones no sooner heard the proposal than, immediately agreeing with the learned serjeant, he ordered a bowl, or rather a large mug, filled with the liquor used on these occasions, to be brought in, and then began the ceremony himself.", "존스는 그 제안을 듣자마자 즉시 그 박식한 하사에게 동의하고는, 이런 자리에 쓰이는 술을 가득 채운 사발, 아니 차라리 커다란 잔을 가져오라 일렀고, 이어서 몸소 그 의식을 시작했다."], ["He placed his right hand in that of the landlord, and, seizing the bowl with his left, uttered the usual words, and then made his libation.", "그는 오른손을 주인장의 손에 얹고, 왼손으로는 그 사발을 움켜쥐고서, 으레 하는 말을 읊은 다음, 헌주를 올렸다. 그러고 나서 그 자리에 있는 모든 사람이 똑같이 따라 했다."], ["After which, the same was observed by all present.", "사실 그 의식 전체의 형식을 시시콜콜 묘사할 필요는 거의 없다."], ["Indeed, there is very little need of being particular in describing the whole form, as it differed so little from those libations of which so much is recorded in antient authors and their modern transcribers.", "고대 작가들과 그들을 본뜬 근대의 필사자들이 그토록 많이 기록해 둔 그 헌주들과 별로 다를 게 없었으니 말이다. 주된 차이는 두 가지였다."], ["The principal difference lay in two instances; for, first, the present company poured the liquor only down their throats; and, secondly, the serjeant, who officiated as priest, drank the last; but he preserved, I believe, the antient form, in swallowing much the largest draught of the whole company, and in being the only person present who contributed nothing towards the libation besides his good offices in assisting at the performance.", "첫째, 지금의 이 일행은 술을 오직 자기들 목구멍으로만 들이부었다는 점이고, 둘째, 사제 노릇을 한 하사가 맨 마지막에 마셨다는 점이다. 그러나 내 생각에 그는, 일행 가운데 단연 가장 큰 잔을 들이켰다는 점에서, 그리고 의식을 거드는 자신의 수고 말고는 헌주에 아무것도 보태지 않은 유일한 사람이었다는 점에서, 고대의 형식을 그대로 지킨 셈이었다."]], [["The good people now ranged themselves round the kitchen fire, where good humour seemed to maintain an absolute dominion; and Partridge not only forgot his shameful defeat, but converted hunger into thirst, and soon became extremely facetious.", "이제 이 선량한 사람들은 부엌 화롯불 둘레에 빙 둘러앉았고, 그곳에서는 유쾌한 기분이 완전한 지배권을 쥔 듯 보였다. 파트리지는 자신의 부끄러운 패배를 잊었을 뿐 아니라, 허기를 갈증으로 바꾸어 곧 더없이 익살스러운 사람이 되었다."], ["We must however quit this agreeable assembly for a while, and attend Mr Jones to Mrs Waters's apartment, where the dinner which he had bespoke was now on the table.", "그러나 우리는 이 기분 좋은 모임을 잠시 떠나, 존스를 따라 워터스 부인의 방으로 가야겠다. 그가 미리 주문해 둔 식사가 지금 그곳 탁자 위에 차려져 있으니 말이다."], ["Indeed, it took no long time in preparing, having been all drest three days before, and required nothing more from the cook than to warm it over again.", "사실 그것을 준비하는 데는 그리 오랜 시간이 걸리지 않았다. 사흘 전에 이미 모두 조리해 둔 것이라, 요리사가 할 일이라곤 그저 다시 데우는 것뿐이었다."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["An apology for all heroes who have good stomachs, with a description of a battle of the amorous kind.", "배가 든든한 모든 영웅을 위한 변호, 그리고 사랑의 전투에 관한 묘사라 해 두자."], ["Heroes, notwithstanding the high ideas which, by the means of flatterers, they may entertain of themselves, or the world may conceive of them, have certainly more of mortal than divine about them.", "아첨꾼들 덕분에 영웅들이 스스로를 어떻게 높이 여기든, 혹은 세상이 그들을 어떻게 떠받들든, 그들에게는 분명 신적인 면보다 인간적인 면이 더 많다."], ["However elevated their minds may be, their bodies at least (which is much the major part of most) are liable to the worst infirmities, and subject to the vilest offices of human nature.", "그들의 정신이 아무리 드높다 한들, 적어도 그들의 몸뚱이는 (대부분의 사람에게 이 몸뚱이가 훨씬 더 큰 부분을 차지한다) 가장 고약한 병에 걸리기 쉽고, 인간 본성의 가장 천한 일들에 매여 있다."], ["Among these latter, the act of eating, which hath by several wise men been considered as extremely mean and derogatory from the philosophic dignity, must be in some measure performed by the greatest prince, heroe, or philosopher upon earth; nay, sometimes Nature hath been so frolicsome as to exact of these dignified characters a much more exorbitant share of this office than she hath obliged those of the lowest order to perform.", "그 천한 일들 가운데 먹는 행위는, 여러 현인이 지극히 비천하며 철학자의 품위를 깎아내리는 것이라 여겨 왔음에도, 지상에서 가장 위대한 군주든 영웅이든 철학자든 어느 정도는 반드시 치러야 하는 일이다. 아니, 때로 자연은 어찌나 짓궂은지, 이 고귀한 인물들에게 가장 미천한 부류의 사람들에게 시킨 것보다 훨씬 더 과한 몫의 이 일을 요구하기도 한다."]], [["To say the truth, as no known inhabitant of this globe is really more than man, so none need be ashamed of submitting to what the necessities of man demand; but when those great personages I have just mentioned condescend to aim at confining such low offices to themselves--as when, by hoarding or destroying, they seem desirous to prevent any others from eating--then they surely become very low and despicable.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이 지구에 알려진 어느 거주자도 실은 인간 이상은 아니므로, 인간의 필요가 요구하는 바에 굴복하는 것을 부끄러워할 사람은 아무도 없다. 그러나 내가 방금 언급한 그 위대한 인물들이 이런 천한 일을 자기들끼리만 독차지하려고 몸을 낮출 때—이를테면 쌓아 두거나 망쳐 버림으로써 다른 누구도 먹지 못하게 막으려는 듯이 굴 때—그들은 분명 아주 비천하고 경멸스러운 존재가 되고 만다."]], [["Now, after this short preface, we think it no disparagement to our heroe to mention the immoderate ardour with which he laid about him at this season.", "자, 이 짧은 머리말을 늘어놓았으니, 이제 우리 영웅이 이 무렵 얼마나 절제 없는 열의로 먹어 댔는지를 말한다 해도 그의 명예를 깎는 일은 아니리라 믿는다."], ["Indeed, it may be doubted whether Ulysses, who by the way seems to have had the best stomach of all the heroes in that eating poem of the Odyssey, ever made a better meal.", "실로, 그 먹는 시(詩) 《오디세이아》에 등장하는 모든 영웅 가운데 가장 좋은 위장을 지녔던 듯한 오디세우스조차 이보다 더 잘 먹은 적이 있었을지 의심스러울 정도다."], ["Three pounds at least of that flesh which formerly had contributed to the composition of an ox was now honoured with becoming part of the individual Mr Jones.", "한때 소 한 마리를 이루는 데 보탬이 되었던 그 살코기가 적어도 세 파운드는, 이제 존스 씨라는 개인의 일부가 되는 영예를 누렸다."]], [["This particular we thought ourselves obliged to mention, as it may account for our heroe's temporary neglect of his fair companion, who eat but very little, and was indeed employed in considerations of a very different nature, which passed unobserved by Jones, till he had entirely satisfied that appetite which a fast of twenty-four hours had procured him; but his dinner was no sooner ended than his attention to other matters revived; with these matters therefore we shall now proceed to acquaint the reader.", "이 세세한 사정을 굳이 언급해야겠다고 여긴 것은, 그래야만 우리 영웅이 잠시 그 어여쁜 동반자를 소홀히 한 까닭이 설명되기 때문이다. 그녀는 아주 조금밖에 먹지 않았고, 실은 전혀 다른 종류의 생각에 잠겨 있었는데, 존스는 스물네 시간의 단식이 그에게 안겨 준 그 식욕을 완전히 채울 때까지 그것을 알아차리지 못했다. 그러나 식사가 끝나기가 무섭게 다른 일들에 대한 그의 주의가 되살아났다. 그러니 이제 그 일들을 독자에게 알려 드리기로 하자."]], [["Mr Jones, of whose personal accomplishments we have hitherto said very little, was, in reality, one of the handsomest young fellows in the world.", "존스 씨의 외모에 관해서는 지금껏 거의 말하지 않았으나, 그는 사실 세상에서 가장 잘생긴 젊은이 가운데 하나였다."], ["His face, besides being the picture of health, had in it the most apparent marks of sweetness and good-nature.", "그의 얼굴은 건강 그 자체의 그림일 뿐 아니라, 다정함과 선량함의 가장 또렷한 자취를 담고 있었다."], ["These qualities were indeed so characteristical in his countenance, that, while the spirit and sensibility in his eyes, though they must have been perceived by an accurate observer, might have escaped the notice of the less discerning, so strongly was this good-nature painted in his look, that it was remarked by almost every one who saw him.", "이런 자질들이 어찌나 그의 얼굴에 뚜렷이 새겨져 있던지, 그의 눈에 깃든 생기와 감수성은 세심한 관찰자라면 알아챘겠으나 안목이 덜한 이의 눈은 놓쳤을 법한 데 반해, 그 선량함만큼은 그의 표정에 너무도 강하게 그려져 있어 그를 본 거의 모든 사람이 알아보았다."]], [["It was, perhaps, as much owing to this as to a very fine complexion that his face had a delicacy in it almost inexpressible, and which might have given him an air rather too effeminate, had it not been joined to a most masculine person and mien: which latter had as much in them of the Hercules as the former had of the Adonis.", "어쩌면 바로 이 점, 그리고 더없이 고운 안색 덕분에 그의 얼굴에는 거의 말로 표현하기 힘든 섬세함이 깃들어 있었는데, 이 섬세함은 자칫 그에게 다소 여성스러운 분위기를 줄 뻔했으나, 더없이 남성적인 체격과 풍채가 함께 어우러진 덕에 그러지 않았다. 그 풍채에는 헤라클레스의 면모가 그의 얼굴에 깃든 아도니스의 면모만큼이나 담겨 있었다."], ["He was besides active, genteel, gay, and good-humoured; and had a flow of animal spirits which enlivened every conversation where he was present.", "게다가 그는 활달하고, 품위 있고, 명랑하며, 성품이 좋았다. 또한 넘쳐흐르는 생기가 있어 그가 함께한 자리는 어떤 대화든 생동감이 돌았다."]], [["When the reader hath duly reflected on these many charms which all centered in our heroe, and considers at the same time the fresh obligations which Mrs Waters had to him, it will be a mark more of prudery than candour to entertain a bad opinion of her because she conceived a very good opinion of him.", "독자가 우리 영웅에게 한데 모인 이 숱한 매력을 충분히 헤아리고, 동시에 워터스 부인이 그에게 새로이 진 신세까지 고려한다면, 그녀가 그에 대해 아주 좋은 생각을 품었다 하여 그녀를 나쁘게 보는 것은 솔직함이라기보다 새침함의 표시일 것이다."]], [["But, whatever censures may be passed upon her, it is my business to relate matters of fact with veracity.", "그러나 그녀에게 어떤 비난이 쏟아지든, 사실을 진실하게 전하는 것이 내 일이다."], ["Mrs Waters had, in truth, not only a good opinion of our heroe, but a very great affection for him.", "워터스 부인은 사실 우리 영웅을 좋게 여겼을 뿐 아니라, 그에게 아주 깊은 애정을 품고 있었다."], ["To speak out boldly at once, she was in love, according to the present universally-received sense of that phrase, by which love is applied indiscriminately to the desirable objects of all our passions, appetites, and senses, and is understood to be that preference which we give to one kind of food rather than to another.", "거리낌 없이 단번에 말하자면, 그녀는 사랑에 빠졌다. 오늘날 누구나 받아들이는 그 말의 뜻대로의 사랑인데, 이 뜻에서 사랑이란 우리의 온갖 욕정과 식욕과 감각이 탐내는 대상에 가리지 않고 적용되는 것이며, 우리가 어떤 종류의 음식을 다른 음식보다 더 선호하는 그 마음과 같은 것으로 이해된다."]], [["But though the love to these several objects may possibly be one and the same in all cases, its operations however must be allowed to be different; for, how much soever we may be in love with an excellent surloin of beef, or bottle of Burgundy; with a damask rose, or Cremona fiddle; yet do we never smile, nor ogle, nor dress, nor flatter, nor endeavour by any other arts or tricks to gain the affection of the said beef, &c. Sigh indeed we sometimes may; but it is generally in the absence, not in the presence, of the beloved object. For otherwise we might possibly complain of their ingratitude and deafness, with the same reason as Pasiphae doth of her bull, whom she endeavoured to engage by all the coquetry practised with good success in the drawing-room on the much more sensible as well as tender hearts of the fine gentlemen there.", "그러나 이 여러 대상을 향한 사랑이 어느 경우든 하나이며 똑같은 것일 수 있다 해도, 그 작용만큼은 다르다고 인정해야 한다. 우리가 빼어난 등심 한 점이나 부르고뉴 포도주 한 병에, 혹은 진홍빛 장미나 크레모나산(産) 바이올린에 아무리 깊이 빠져 있다 한들, 그 쇠고기 따위의 애정을 얻으려고 미소 짓거나, 추파를 던지거나, 차려입거나, 아첨하거나, 그 밖의 어떤 재주나 수법을 부리는 일은 결코 없다. 한숨이야 이따금 내쉴지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은 대개 사랑하는 대상이 곁에 있을 때가 아니라 없을 때다. 그러지 않고서야 우리는 어쩌면 그것들의 무정함과 귀먹음을 탓하게 될지도 모른다. 마치 파시파에가 자기 황소를 두고 그랬듯이 말이다. 그녀는 응접실에서 훨씬 더 분별 있고 다감한 멋쟁이 신사들의 마음을 사로잡는 데 곧잘 성공했던 온갖 교태를 부려 그 황소를 꾀어 보려 애썼던 것이다."]], [["The contrary happens in that love which operates between persons of the same species, but of different sexes.", "같은 종(種)이되 성(性)이 다른 사람들 사이에서 작용하는 사랑에서는 그 반대의 일이 벌어진다."], ["Here we are no sooner in love than it becomes our principal care to engage the affection of the object beloved.", "여기서는 우리가 사랑에 빠지기가 무섭게, 사랑하는 대상의 애정을 얻는 일이 무엇보다 중요한 관심사가 된다."], ["For what other purpose indeed are our youth instructed in all the arts of rendering themselves agreeable?", "우리 젊은이들이 스스로를 호감 가게 만드는 온갖 기술을 배우는 것이, 실은 또 다른 무슨 목적에서겠는가?"], ["If it was not with a view to this love, I question whether any of those trades which deal in setting off and adorning the human person would procure a livelihood.", "만일 이 사랑을 염두에 두지 않았다면, 인간의 몸을 돋보이게 하고 꾸며 주는 일을 다루는 그 어떤 직업이 과연 생계를 꾸릴 수 있을지 의문이다."], ["Nay, those great polishers of our manners, who are by some thought to teach what principally distinguishes us from the brute creation, even dancing-masters themselves, might possibly find no place in society.", "아니, 우리의 예법을 다듬는 그 위대한 연마자들, 어떤 이들은 그들이야말로 우리를 짐승과 가장 뚜렷이 구별 짓는 바를 가르친다고 여기는데, 심지어 무용 선생들조차 사회에서 설 자리를 찾지 못할지도 모른다."], ["In short, all the graces which young ladies and young gentlemen too learn from others, and the many improvements which, by the help of a looking-glass, they add of their own, are in reality those very spicula et faces amoris so often mentioned by Ovid; or, as they are sometimes called in our own language, the whole artillery of love.", "요컨대, 젊은 숙녀들과 젊은 신사들이 남에게서 배우는 온갖 우아함, 그리고 거울의 도움을 받아 스스로 보태는 그 숱한 손질은, 실은 오비디우스가 그토록 자주 언급한 바로 그 사랑의 화살과 횃불(spicula et faces amoris)이며, 우리말로는 이따금 사랑의 온갖 화포(火砲)라 불리는 것이다."]], [["Now Mrs Waters and our heroe had no sooner sat down together than the former began to play this artillery upon the latter.", "자, 워터스 부인과 우리 영웅이 마주 앉기가 무섭게, 전자는 후자를 향해 이 화포를 쏘아 대기 시작했다."], ["But here, as we are about to attempt a description hitherto unassayed either in prose or verse, we think proper to invoke the assistance of certain aërial beings, who will, we doubt not, come kindly to our aid on this occasion.", "그런데 여기서 우리는 산문으로든 운문으로든 여태 시도된 바 없는 묘사에 도전하려는 참이니, 어떤 공중의 존재들에게 도움을 청하는 것이 마땅하다고 본다. 그들은 이 자리에서 우리를 친절히 거들어 주러 오리라 믿어 의심치 않는다."]], [["“Say then, ye Graces!", "\"그러니 말해 다오, 그대 우아의 여신들이여! 세라피나의 얼굴이라는 천상의 저택에 깃들어 사는 그대들이여."], ["you that inhabit the heavenly mansions of Seraphina's countenance; for you are truly divine, are always in her presence, and well know all the arts of charming; say, what were the weapons now used to captivate the heart of Mr Jones.”", "그대들은 참으로 신성하며, 늘 그녀 곁에 있고, 매혹의 온갖 기술을 훤히 아는 이들이니. 말해 다오, 이제 존스 씨의 마음을 사로잡으려 동원된 무기들이 무엇이었는지를.\""]], [["“First, from two lovely blue eyes, whose bright orbs flashed lightning at their discharge, flew forth two pointed ogles; but, happily for our heroe, hit only a vast piece of beef which he was then conveying into his plate, and harmless spent their force. The fair warrior perceived their miscarriage, and immediately from her fair bosom drew forth a deadly sigh. A sigh which none could have heard unmoved, and which was sufficient at once to have swept off a dozen beaus; so soft, so sweet, so tender, that the insinuating air must have found its subtle way to the heart of our heroe, had it not luckily been driven from his ears by the coarse bubbling of some bottled ale, which at that time he was pouring forth. Many other weapons did she assay; but the god of eating (if there be any such deity, for I do not confidently assert it) preserved his votary; or perhaps it may not be dignus vindice nodus, and the present security of Jones may be accounted for by natural means; for as love frequently preserves from the attacks of hunger, so may hunger possibly, in some cases, defend us against love.", "\"먼저, 사랑스러운 푸른 두 눈에서—그 빛나는 눈동자는 발사할 때 번개를 번뜩였으니—날카로운 추파 두 발이 쏘아져 나왔다. 그러나 우리 영웅에게는 다행히도, 그것은 그가 마침 접시에 옮겨 담고 있던 큼직한 쇠고기 한 점만 맞히고는 그 힘을 헛되이 소진하고 말았다. 그 어여쁜 전사는 빗나간 것을 알아채고, 즉시 고운 가슴속에서 치명적인 한숨 하나를 꺼내 들었다. 누구라도 흔들리지 않고는 들을 수 없는 한숨, 단번에 멋쟁이 열두 명쯤은 쓸어버리기에 족한 한숨이었다. 어찌나 부드럽고, 어찌나 달콤하고, 어찌나 애틋했던지, 그 파고드는 숨결은 우리 영웅의 마음으로 그 은밀한 길을 기어이 찾아들었으리라. 다행히도 마침 그가 따르고 있던 병맥주의 거친 보글거림이 그것을 그의 귓전에서 몰아내지 않았던들 말이다. 그녀는 그 밖에도 숱한 무기를 시험해 보았다. 그러나 먹기의 신이 (그런 신이 정말 있다면 말인데, 나는 확언하지는 않는다) 자신의 신도를 지켜 주었다. 혹은 어쩌면 이것이 신의 개입이 필요할 만한 매듭(dignus vindice nodus)이 아니어서, 존스의 이번 무사함이 자연스러운 까닭으로 설명될 수도 있겠다. 사랑이 흔히 굶주림의 공격으로부터 우리를 지켜 주듯이, 굶주림 또한 어떤 경우에는 사랑으로부터 우리를 막아 줄지도 모르니 말이다."]], [["“The fair one, enraged at her frequent disappointments, determined on a short cessation of arms.", "\"그 어여쁜 여인은 거듭된 실패에 격분하여, 잠시 무기를 거두기로 결심했다."], ["Which interval she employed in making ready every engine of amorous warfare for the renewing of the attack when dinner should be over.", "그 짬을 그녀는 만찬이 끝나면 다시 공격을 펼칠 사랑의 온갖 병기를 채비하는 데 썼다."]], [["“No sooner then was the cloth removed than she again began her operations.", "\"그리하여 식탁보가 치워지기 무섭게 그녀는 다시 작전을 개시했다."], ["First, having planted her right eye sidewise against Mr Jones, she shot from its corner a most penetrating glance; which, though great part of its force was spent before it reached our heroe, did not vent itself absolutely without effect.", "먼저, 오른쪽 눈을 비스듬히 존스 씨에게 겨눈 다음, 그 눈꼬리에서 더없이 꿰뚫는 눈길 한 줄기를 쏘았다. 비록 그 힘의 대부분이 우리 영웅에게 닿기도 전에 소진되긴 했으나, 아무런 효과도 없이 헛되이 흩어지지는 않았다."], ["This the fair one perceiving, hastily withdrew her eyes, and levelled them downwards, as if she was concerned for what she had done; though by this means she designed only to draw him from his guard, and indeed to open his eyes, through which she intended to surprize his heart.", "이를 알아챈 그 어여쁜 여인은 황급히 눈길을 거두어 아래로 떨구었는데, 마치 자기가 저지른 일을 뉘우치는 듯한 모양이었다. 하지만 이 방법으로 그녀가 노린 것은 다만 그의 경계를 풀고, 나아가 그의 눈을 뜨게 하여 그 눈을 통해 그의 마음을 기습하려는 것이었다."], ["And now, gently lifting up those two bright orbs which had already begun to make an impression on poor Jones, she discharged a volley of small charms at once from her whole countenance in a smile.", "그러고는 이미 가엾은 존스에게 자국을 남기기 시작한 그 빛나는 두 눈동자를 가만히 들어 올리며, 그녀는 얼굴 전체에서 한꺼번에 자잘한 매력의 일제 사격을 미소에 실어 퍼부었다."], ["Not a smile of mirth, nor of joy; but a smile of affection, which most ladies have always ready at their command, and which serves them to show at once their good-humour, their pretty dimples, and their white teeth.", "즐거움의 미소도, 기쁨의 미소도 아니었다. 그것은 애정의 미소였으니, 대부분의 숙녀가 언제든 마음대로 부릴 수 있는 것이며, 그녀들의 좋은 기분과 어여쁜 보조개와 새하얀 이를 한꺼번에 내보이는 데 쓰이는 것이다."]], [["“This smile our heroe received full in his eyes, and was immediately staggered with its force.", "\"이 미소를 우리 영웅은 두 눈에 정통으로 맞고는, 곧장 그 힘에 휘청거렸다."], ["He then began to see the designs of the enemy, and indeed to feel their success.", "이제 그는 적의 의도를 알아채기 시작했고, 실로 그 성공을 몸으로 느끼기 시작했다."], ["A parley now was set on foot between the parties; during which the artful fair so slily and imperceptibly carried on her attack, that she had almost subdued the heart of our heroe before she again repaired to acts of hostility.", "두 진영 사이에 협상이 시작되었는데, 그동안 그 교묘한 미녀는 어찌나 은밀하고 알아챌 수 없게 공격을 이어 갔던지, 다시 적대 행위에 나서기도 전에 우리 영웅의 마음을 거의 굴복시켜 놓았다."], ["To confess the truth, I am afraid Mr Jones maintained a kind of Dutch defence, and treacherously delivered up the garrison, without duly weighing his allegiance to the fair Sophia.", "사실대로 고백하자면, 존스 씨는 일종의 네덜란드식 방어를 취하여, 어여쁜 소피아를 향한 충성을 제대로 가늠하지도 않은 채 배신하듯 요새를 내주고 만 것이 아닌가 싶다."], ["In short, no sooner had the amorous parley ended and the lady had unmasked the royal battery, by carelessly letting her handkerchief drop from her neck, than the heart of Mr Jones was entirely taken, and the fair conqueror enjoyed the usual fruits of her victory.”", "요컨대, 사랑의 협상이 끝나고 그 부인이 목에 두른 손수건을 무심한 듯 떨어뜨려 왕의 포대를 드러내자마자, 존스 씨의 마음은 완전히 함락되었고, 그 어여쁜 정복자는 승리의 통상적인 열매를 누렸다.\""]], [["Here the Graces think proper to end their description, and here we think proper to end the chapter.", "여기서 우아의 여신들은 묘사를 끝내는 것이 마땅하다고 보았고, 우리 또한 여기서 이 장을 끝내는 것이 마땅하다고 본다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["A friendly conversation in the kitchen, which had a very common, though not very friendly, conclusion.", "부엌에서 오간 정겨운 대화. 흔히 볼 수 있는, 그러나 그리 정겹지는 않은 결말로 끝났다."]], [["While our lovers were entertaining themselves in the manner which is partly described in the foregoing chapter, they were likewise furnishing out an entertainment for their good friends in the kitchen.", "우리의 연인들이 앞 장에서 일부 그려진 방식으로 둘만의 시간을 즐기는 동안, 그들은 부엌에 있는 좋은 친구들을 위해서도 즐길 거리를 마련해 주고 있었다."], ["And this in a double sense, by affording them matter for their conversation, and, at the same time, drink to enliven their spirits.", "그것도 두 가지 의미에서였다. 그들에게 대화의 소재를 제공함과 동시에, 흥을 돋울 술까지 베푼 셈이었다."]], [["There were now assembled round the kitchen fire, besides my landlord and landlady, who occasionally went backward and forward, Mr Partridge, the serjeant, and the coachman who drove the young lady and her maid.", "이제 부엌 난롯가에는, 이따금 들락날락하는 주인 부부 말고도 파트리지 씨와 상사, 그리고 젊은 아가씨와 그 하녀를 태우고 온 마부가 둘러앉아 있었다."]], [["Partridge having acquainted the company with what he had learnt from the Man of the Hill concerning the situation in which Mrs Waters had been found by Jones, the serjeant proceeded to that part of her history which was known to him.", "파트리지가 산속 노인에게서 들은 이야기, 곧 존스가 워터스 부인을 발견한 정황을 일행에게 들려주자, 상사는 자신이 아는 부분의 사연을 이어 갔다. 그는 그녀가 워터스 씨의 아내이며, 워터스 씨는 자기네 연대의 대위로서 주둔지에서 자주 함께 지냈다고 했다."], ["He said she was the wife of Mr Waters, who was a captain in their regiment, and had often been with him at quarters.", "\"어떤 사람들은 말이오,\" 그가 말했다, \"두 사람이 교회에서 정식으로 혼인을 올렸는지 어떤지 미심쩍어들 하더이다."], ["“Some folks,” says he, “used indeed to doubt whether they were lawfully married in a church or no.", "하지만 내 입장에서야 그거야 내 알 바 아니지."], ["But, for my part, that's no business of mine: I must own, if I was put to my corporal oath, I believe she is little better than one of us; and I fancy the captain may go to heaven when the sun shines upon a rainy day.", "솔직히 군인의 맹세를 걸고 말하라면, 내 보기엔 저 여자도 우리네와 별반 다를 게 없는 사람이오. 그리고 내 짐작엔 그 대위 양반은 비 오는 날 해 뜰 때나 천국에 가게 될 게요."], ["But if he does, that is neither here nor there; for he won't want company.", "하지만 설령 간다 한들 그건 이러나저러나 마찬가지지. 동행이 없을 리 없으니 말이오."], ["And the lady, to give the devil his due, is a very good sort of lady, and loves the cloth, and is always desirous to do strict justice to it; for she hath begged off many a poor soldier, and, by her good-will, would never have any of them punished.", "그리고 그 부인으로 말하자면, 악마에게도 마땅한 몫을 주듯 공평하게 말하건대, 참으로 좋은 부류의 부인이고, 군복을 사랑하며, 늘 군인들에게 엄정히 의리를 다하려 애쓰는 사람이오. 가엾은 병사들 여럿을 위해 사정해서 벌을 면하게 해 주었고, 자기 뜻대로라면 그 누구도 벌받게 하지 않았을 게요."], ["But yet, to be sure, Ensign Northerton and she were very well acquainted together at our last quarters; that is the very right and truth of the matter.", "하지만 그래도 분명한 건, 노더턴 소위와 그 부인이 우리 지난 주둔지에서 아주 가까운 사이였다는 거요. 그게 바로 옳고도 참된 사정이지."], ["But the captain he knows nothing about it; and as long as there is enough for him too, what does it signify?", "그런데 대위는 그 일을 아무것도 모르오. 그리고 자기 몫이 충분히 있는 한, 그게 무슨 대수겠소?"], ["He loves her not a bit the worse, and I am certain would run any man through the body that was to abuse her; therefore I won't abuse her, for my part.", "그래도 부인을 조금도 덜 사랑하는 게 아니고, 누구든 그 부인을 욕보이는 자가 있으면 그 몸뚱이를 칼로 꿰뚫어 버릴 게 분명하오. 그러니 나로서는 그 부인을 욕하지 않겠소."], ["I only repeat what other folks say; and, to be certain, what everybody says, there must be some truth in.”--“Ay, ay, a great deal of truth, I warrant you,”", "나야 그저 다른 사람들이 하는 말을 옮길 뿐이오. 그리고 분명한 건, 모두가 하는 말에는 어느 정도 진실이 있게 마련이라는 거요.\"—\"암, 암, 분명 진실이 아주 많고말고요.\""], ["cries Partridge; “Veritas odium parit”--“All a parcel of scandalous stuff,”", "파트리지가 외쳤다. \"베리타스 오디움 파리트(진실은 미움을 낳는다).\"—\"죄다 망측한 헛소리들뿐이오.\""], ["answered the mistress of the house.", "안주인이 대꾸했다."], ["“I am sure, now she is drest, she looks like a very good sort of lady, and she behaves herself like one; for she gave me a guinea for the use of my cloaths.”--“A very good lady indeed!”", "\"내 분명히 말하건대, 지금 옷을 갖춰 입은 모습을 보면 아주 좋은 부류의 부인 같고, 처신도 그러한 부인답습디다. 내 옷을 빌려 입은 값으로 1기니를 주었으니 말이오.\"—\"참으로 좋은 부인이고말고!\""], ["cries the landlord; “and if you had not been a little too hasty, you would not have quarrelled with her as you did at first.”--“You need mention that with my truly!”", "주인이 외쳤다. \"당신이 조금만 덜 성급했더라면, 처음에 그렇게 싸우지도 않았을 텐데.\"—\"참, 별소릴 다 하시네!\""], ["answered she: “if it had not been for your nonsense, nothing had happened.", "그녀가 대꾸했다. \"당신의 그 헛소리만 아니었으면 아무 일도 없었을 거요."], ["You must be meddling with what did not belong to you, and throw in your fool's discourse.”--“Well, well,”", "당신은 늘 당신 일도 아닌 데 끼어들어서 그 바보 같은 말참견을 해 대니 탈이지.\"—\"알았어, 알았어.\""], ["answered he; “what's past cannot be mended, so there's an end of the matter.”--“Yes,”", "그가 대답했다. \"지난 일은 돌이킬 수 없으니, 그걸로 그만하자고.\"—\"그래요,\""], ["cries she, “for this once; but will it be mended ever the more hereafter?", "그녀가 외쳤다, \"이번 한 번이야 그렇다 칩시다. 하지만 앞으로는 좀 나아지기나 할까?"], ["This is not the first time I have suffered for your numscull's pate.", "당신의 그 멍청한 대가리 때문에 내가 고생한 게 이번이 처음도 아니오."], ["I wish you would always hold your tongue in the house, and meddle only in matters without doors, which concern you.", "제발 집 안에서는 늘 입을 다물고, 당신과 상관있는 바깥일에만 끼어들었으면 좋겠소."], ["Don't you remember what happened about seven years ago?”--“Nay, my dear,”", "한 칠 년 전에 무슨 일이 있었는지 기억 안 나요?\"—\"이봐, 여보,\""], ["returned he, “don't rip up old stories.", "그가 받아쳤다, \"옛날 얘기를 들추지 좀 마."], ["Come, come, all's well, and I am sorry for what I have done.”", "자, 자, 다 잘됐고, 내가 한 일은 미안하게 됐으니까.\""], ["The landlady was going to reply, but was prevented by the peace-making serjeant, sorely to the displeasure of Partridge, who was a great lover of what is called fun, and a great promoter of those harmless quarrels which tend rather to the production of comical than tragical incidents.", "안주인이 막 대꾸하려는데, 중재에 나선 상사가 가로막았다. 파트리지로서는 몹시 못마땅한 일이었으니, 그는 이른바 재미라는 것을 무척 좋아하는 사람이었고, 비극보다는 희극적인 사건을 빚어내기 십상인 그런 무해한 다툼을 부추기는 데 일가견이 있는 인물이었다."]], [["The serjeant asked Partridge whither he and his master were travelling?", "상사가 파트리지에게 그와 그의 주인은 어디로 여행하는 길이냐고 물었다. \"주인이라니 당치 않소,\""], ["“None of your magisters,”", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"장담컨대 나는 누구의 하인도 아니오."], ["answered Partridge; “I am no man's servant, I assure you; for, though I have had misfortunes in the world, I write gentleman after my name; and, as poor and simple as I may appear now, I have taught grammar-school in my time; sed hei mihi!", "비록 세상살이에서 불운을 겪긴 했어도, 내 이름 뒤에는 '신사'라고 쓰는 사람이오. 그리고 지금이야 이렇게 가난하고 보잘것없어 보여도, 한때는 문법학교에서 가르치던 몸이오."], ["non sum quod fui.”--“No offence, I hope, sir,”", "세드 헤이 미히! 논 숨 쿠오드 푸이(허나 아아, 슬프도다!"], ["said the serjeant; “where, then, if I may venture to be so bold, may you and your friend be travelling?”--“You have now denominated us right,”", "나는 이제 예전의 내가 아니로다).\"—\"기분 상하게 할 뜻은 없소만,\" 상사가 말했다."], ["says Partridge. “Amici sumus.", "\"그럼, 외람되지만 한번 여쭤보겠소."], ["And I promise you my friend is one of the greatest gentlemen in the kingdom”", "당신과 당신 친구분은 어디로 가시는 길이오?\"—\"이제야 우리를 제대로 칭하시는구려.\" 파트리지가 말했다."], ["(at which words both landlord and landlady pricked up their ears).", "\"아미키 수무스(우리는 친구라오). 장담컨대 내 친구로 말하자면 이 왕국에서 손꼽히는 대단한 신사 가운데 한 분이오\"(이 말에 주인과 안주인 둘 다 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다)."], ["“He is the heir of Squire Allworthy.”--“What, the squire who doth so much good all over the country?”", "\"올워디 향사 어른의 상속자이시라오.\"—\"뭐라고요, 온 고장에서 그토록 선행을 많이 베푸는 그 향사 어른 말이오?\" 안주인이 외쳤다."], ["cries my landlady.", "\"바로 그분이오.\""], ["“Even he,”", "파트리지가 대답했다.—\"그렇다면 분명,\""], ["answered Partridge.--“Then I warrant,”", "그녀가 말했다, \"장차 어마어마하게 큰 재산을 물려받으시겠구려.\"—\"틀림없소.\" 파트리지가 대답했다.—\"그것 보오,\""], ["says she, “he'll have a swinging great estate hereafter.”--“Most certainly,”", "안주인이 받았다, \"내 처음 그분을 본 순간부터 좋은 부류의 신사 같다고 했잖소. 그런데 여기 우리 양반은, 참, 누구보다 잘난 줄 알지.\"—\"인정하리다, 여보,\""], ["answered Partridge.--“Well,”", "그가 외쳤다, \"그건 내 실수였소.\"—\"실수고말고요!\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["replied the landlady, “I thought the first moment I saw him he looked like a good sort of gentleman; but my husband here, to be sure, is wiser than anybody.”--“I own, my dear,”", "\"한데 내가 그런 실수를 하는 걸 당신이 언제 본 적이나 있소?\"—\"한데 어찌 된 영문이오, 선생,\" 주인이 외쳤다, \"그런 대단한 신사분이 걸어서 시골을 돌아다니신단 말이오?\"—\"낸들 알겠소,\""], ["cries he, “it was a mistake.”--“A mistake, indeed!”", "파트리지가 받았다. \"대단한 신사들은 때때로 별난 기분이 들곤 하니까."], ["answered she; “but when did you ever know me to make such mistakes?”--“But how comes it, sir,”", "지금 글로스터에 말도 열두 마리쯤 있고 하인들도 거느리고 있다오. 그런데 어젯밤, 날씨가 어찌나 더웠던지, 한사코 저 높은 언덕으로 산책을 가서 몸을 식히겠다지 않겠소."], ["cries the landlord, “that such a great gentleman walks about the country afoot?”--“I don't know,”", "나도 동무 삼아 함께 걸어 올라갔지요. 하지만 다시 한 번 거기로 나를 끌고 갈 수 있는지 어디 두고 보시오."], ["returned Partridge; “great gentlemen have humours sometimes.", "내 평생 그렇게 겁먹은 적이 없었으니. 거기서 어찌나 괴상한 사람을 만났던지.\"—\"내 목을 걸겠소,\""], ["He hath now a dozen horses and servants at Gloucester; and nothing would serve him, but last night, it being very hot weather, he must cool himself with a walk to yon high hill, whither I likewise walked with him to bear him company; but if ever you catch me there again: for I was never so frightened in all my life.", "주인이 외쳤다, \"그게 사람들이 산속 노인이라 부르는 그 양반이 아니라면 말이오. 정말로 그게 사람이라면야."], ["We met with the strangest man there.”--“I'll be hanged,”", "한데 나는 거기 사는 게 악마라고 믿는 사람들을 여럿 알고 있소.\"—\"아니, 아니, 충분히 그럴 법하오.\" 파트리지가 말했다."], ["cries the landlord, “if it was not the Man of the Hill, as they call him; if indeed he be a man; but I know several people who believe it is the devil that lives there.”--“Nay, nay, like enough,”", "\"그러고 보니 당신이 일깨워 주니 하는 말인데, 나는 그게 악마였다고 진실로 진심으로 믿소. 비록 갈라진 발굽은 보지 못했지만 말이오."], ["says Partridge; “and now you put me in the head of it, I verily and sincerely believe it was the devil, though I could not perceive his cloven foot: but perhaps he might have the power given him to hide that, since evil spirits can appear in what shapes they please.”--“And pray, sir,”", "어쩌면 그걸 숨길 능력을 받았는지도 모르지. 악령이란 제멋대로 어떤 모습으로든 나타날 수 있으니 말이오.\"—\"한데 외람되지만, 선생,\""], ["says the serjeant, “no offence, I hope; but pray what sort of a gentleman is the devil?", "상사가 말했다. \"기분 상하게 할 뜻은 없소만, 대체 악마라는 게 어떤 부류의 신사요?"], ["For I have heard some of our officers say there is no such person; and that it is only a trick of the parsons, to prevent their being broke; for, if it was publickly known that there was no devil, the parsons would be of no more use than we are in time of peace.”--“Those officers,”", "우리 장교들 중 몇몇이 그런 자는 없다고 말하는 걸 들은 적이 있어서 말이오. 그건 그저 목사들이 자기들 밥줄 끊기지 않으려고 꾸며낸 수작일 뿐이라고 합디다."], ["says Partridge, “are very great scholars, I suppose.”--“Not much of schollards neither,”", "악마가 없다는 게 세상에 공공연히 알려지면, 목사들이란 평화로운 시절의 우리 군인만큼이나 쓸모없어질 테니 말이오.\"—\"그 장교들로 말하자면,\""], ["answered the serjeant; “they have not half your learning, sir, I believe; and, to be sure, I thought there must be a devil, notwithstanding what they said, though one of them was a captain; for methought, thinks I to myself, if there be no devil, how can wicked people be sent to him?", "파트리지가 말했다, \"필시 아주 대단한 학자들이겠구려.\"—\"학자라고 할 것도 못 되오,\" 상사가 대답했다."], ["and I have read all that upon a book.”--“Some of your officers,”", "\"내 보기엔 그자들 학식이 선생 절반에도 못 미칠 거요. 그리고 분명한 건, 나는 그자들 말에도 불구하고 악마가 있을 수밖에 없다고 생각했다는 거요."], ["quoth the landlord, “will find there is a devil, to their shame, I believe.", "비록 그중 하나가 대위였지만 말이오. 나 혼자 속으로 생각하길, 악마가 없다면 사악한 자들을 대체 어디로 보낸단 말인가 싶었거든."], ["I don't question but he'll pay off some old scores upon my account.", "그리고 나는 그 모든 걸 책에서 읽었소.\"—\"당신네 장교들 가운데 몇몇은,\" 주인이 말했다, \"수치스럽게도 악마가 있다는 걸 알게 될 거요, 내 장담하리다."], ["Here was one quartered upon me half a year, who had the conscience to take up one of my best beds, though he hardly spent a shilling a day in the house, and suffered his men to roast cabbages at the kitchen fire, because I would not give them a dinner on a Sunday.", "그자가 내 몫으로 묵은 빚 몇 가지를 갚아 줄 게 틀림없소. 여기 반년이나 묵었던 자가 하나 있었는데, 글쎄 하루에 1실링도 안 쓰면서 뻔뻔스럽게 내 제일 좋은 침대를 차지하지 않았겠소."], ["Every good Christian must desire there should be a devil for the punishment of such wretches.”--“Harkee, landlord,”", "게다가 내가 일요일에 저녁 한 끼 안 차려 준다고, 부하들을 시켜 부엌 불에 양배추나 구워 먹게 했다오. 선량한 기독교인이라면 누구나 그런 망나니들을 벌하기 위해서라도 악마가 있기를 바랄 게요.\"—\"이보시오, 주인장,\""], ["said the serjeant, “don't abuse the cloth, for I won't take it.”--“D--n the cloth!”", "상사가 말했다, \"군복을 욕하지 마시오. 나는 그건 못 참으니까.\"—\"빌어먹을 군복 같으니!\""], ["answered the landlord, “I have suffered enough by them.”--“Bear witness, gentlemen,”", "주인이 대답했다. \"나는 그자들 때문에 충분히 당했단 말이오.\"—\"증인이 되어 주시오, 여러분,\""], ["says the serjeant, “he curses the king, and that's high treason.”--“I curse the king!", "상사가 말했다, \"이자가 임금님을 저주했소. 그건 대역죄요.\"—\"내가 임금님을 저주했다고!"], ["you villain,”", "이 못된 놈아.\""], ["said the landlord.", "주인이 말했다."], ["“Yes, you did,”", "\"그렇소, 당신이 그랬소,\""], ["cries the serjeant; “you cursed the cloth, and that's cursing the king.", "상사가 외쳤다. \"당신은 군복을 저주했고, 그건 곧 임금님을 저주한 거요."], ["It's all one and the same; for every man who curses the cloth would curse the king if he durst; so for matter o'", "그게 그거니까. 군복을 저주하는 자는 누구든 감히 그럴 수만 있다면 임금님도 저주할 테니, 따져 보면 다 똑같은 일이오.\"—\"그건 좀 봐드릴 수 없소이다, 상사 양반,\""], ["that, it's all one and the same thing.”--“Excuse me there, Mr Serjeant,” quoth Partridge, “that's a non sequitur.”--“None of your outlandish linguo,”", "파트리지가 말했다, \"그건 논 세퀴투르(말이 안 되는 결론)요.\"—\"그 알아먹지도 못할 외국 말 좀 작작하시오.\""], ["answered the serjeant, leaping from his seat; “I will not sit still and hear the cloth abused.”--“You mistake me, friend,”", "상사가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 대답했다. \"나는 가만히 앉아서 군복이 욕먹는 꼴을 두고 볼 수 없소.\"—\"오해하셨소, 친구,\""], ["cries Partridge.", "파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["“I did not mean to abuse the cloth; I only said your conclusion was a non sequitur.[*]”--“You are another,”", "\"나는 군복을 욕할 뜻이 없었소. 그저 당신의 결론이 논 세퀴투르라고 했을 뿐이오.\"—\"당신도 마찬가지요.\""], ["cries the serjeant,”", "상사가 외쳤다."], ["an you come to that.", "\"정 그렇게 나온다면 말이오."], ["No more a sequitur than yourself.", "당신이야말로 세퀴투르고 뭐고 없는 작자지. 당신들은 죄다 악당 무리야."], ["You are a pack of rascals, and I'll prove it; for I will fight the best man of you all for twenty pound.”", "내 그걸 증명해 보이겠소. 20파운드 걸고 당신들 중 제일 센 놈하고 한판 붙어 주지.\""], ["This challenge effectually silenced Partridge, whose stomach for drubbing did not so soon return after the hearty meal which he had lately been treated with; but the coachman, whose bones were less sore, and whose appetite for fighting was somewhat sharper, did not so easily brook the affront, of which he conceived some part at least fell to his share.", "이 도전에 파트리지는 그만 입을 다물고 말았으니, 얼마 전 푸짐하게 대접받은 밥이 아직 꺼지지 않은 터라 두들겨 맞을 식욕이 그리 빨리 돌아오지 않았던 것이다. 하지만 마부는 뼈마디가 덜 쑤셨고 싸움에 대한 식욕은 다소 날카로웠던지라, 적어도 자기 몫 일부는 돌아온다고 여긴 이 모욕을 그리 쉽게 참아 넘기지 못했다."], ["He started therefore from his seat, and, advancing to the serjeant, swore he looked on himself to be as good a man as any in the army, and offered to box for a guinea.", "그리하여 그는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 상사에게 다가가서는, 자기도 군대의 그 누구 못지않은 사내라고 큰소리치며 1기니를 걸고 주먹다짐을 하자고 청했다."], ["The military man accepted the combat, but refused the wager; upon which both immediately stript and engaged, till the driver of horses was so well mauled by the leader of men, that he was obliged to exhaust his small remainder of breath in begging for quarter.", "군인은 대결은 받아들였으나 내기는 거절했다. 그러자 둘은 즉시 웃통을 벗어 던지고 맞붙었는데, 말몰이꾼이 사람들의 지휘자에게 어찌나 흠씬 두들겨 맞았던지, 결국 얼마 남지 않은 숨을 쥐어짜 가며 살려 달라 비는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["[*] This word, which the serjeant unhappily mistook for an affront, is a term in logic, and means that the conclusion does not follow from the premises.", "* 논 세퀴투르: 상사가 불행히도 모욕으로 오해한 이 말은 논리학 용어로, 결론이 전제로부터 도출되지 않는다는 뜻이다."]], [["The young lady was now desirous to depart, and had given orders for her coach to be prepared; but all in vain, for the coachman was disabled from performing his office for that evening.", "젊은 아가씨는 이제 떠나고 싶어 마차를 준비하라고 일러두었다. 그러나 다 헛일이었으니, 마부가 그날 저녁 제 소임을 다할 수 없게 되어 버린 까닭이었다."], ["An antient heathen would perhaps have imputed this disability to the god of drink, no less than to the god of war; for, in reality, both the combatants had sacrificed as well to the former deity as to the latter.", "옛 이교도였다면 아마 이 무능을 전쟁의 신 못지않게 술의 신 탓으로 돌렸으리라. 사실 두 결투자 모두 전자의 신에게도 후자의 신에게도 똑같이 제물을 바친 셈이었으니 말이다."], ["To speak plainly, they were both dead drunk, nor was Partridge in a much better situation.", "솔직히 말하자면 둘 다 곤드레만드레 취해 있었고, 파트리지의 형편도 그리 나을 게 없었다."], ["As for my landlord, drinking was his trade; and the liquor had no more effect on him than it had on any other vessel in his house.", "주인으로 말하자면 술 마시는 게 그의 본업이었으니, 술이 그에게 미치는 효력이란 집 안의 다른 어떤 그릇에 미치는 것과 다를 바 없었다."]], [["The mistress of the inn, being summoned to attend Mr Jones and his companion at their tea, gave a full relation of the latter part of the foregoing scene; and at the same time expressed great concern for the young lady, “who,”", "여관 안주인은 존스 씨와 그 일행이 차를 마시는 자리에 시중을 들러 불려 가서, 앞서 벌어진 장면의 뒷부분을 낱낱이 일러바쳤다. 그러면서 동시에 젊은 아가씨에 대해 큰 걱정을 내비쳤다."], ["she said, “was under the utmost uneasiness at being prevented from pursuing her journey.", "\"그분이,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"여정을 이어 가지 못하게 된 일로 여간 마음이 편치 않으신 게 아니라오."], ["She is a sweet pretty creature,”", "참으로 곱고 사랑스러운 분이에요.\""], ["added she, “and I am certain I have seen her face before.", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그리고 분명히 어디선가 그 얼굴을 본 적이 있는 것 같아요."], ["I fancy she is in love, and running away from her friends.", "내 짐작엔 사랑에 빠져서 식구들한테서 달아나는 길인가 싶어요."], ["Who knows but some young gentleman or other may be expecting her, with a heart as heavy as her own?”", "또 누가 알겠어요, 어디선가 어느 젊은 신사분이 그분 못지않게 무거운 마음을 안고 그분을 기다리고 있을지?\""]], [["Jones fetched a heavy sigh at those words; of which, though Mrs Waters observed it, she took no notice while the landlady continued in the room; but, after the departure of that good woman, she could not forbear giving our heroe certain hints on her suspecting some very dangerous rival in his affections.", "존스는 그 말에 무거운 한숨을 내쉬었다. 워터스 부인은 그것을 알아챘지만, 안주인이 방에 머무는 동안에는 모르는 체했다."], ["The aukward behaviour of Mr Jones on this occasion convinced her of the truth, without his giving her a direct answer to any of her questions; but she was not nice enough in her amours to be greatly concerned at the discovery.", "그러나 그 선량한 여인이 떠난 뒤, 부인은 그의 애정 속에 어떤 몹시 위태로운 연적이 있는 게 아닌가 의심하며 우리 주인공에게 넌지시 운을 떼지 않을 수 없었다. 이때 보인 존스 씨의 어색한 태도는, 그가 부인의 어떤 물음에도 직접 답을 주지 않았는데도 그 진실을 확인시켜 주었다."], ["The beauty of Jones highly charmed her eye; but as she could not see his heart, she gave herself no concern about it.", "하지만 부인은 자기 연애에 그리 까다로운 사람이 아니어서, 그 발견에 크게 마음 쓰지는 않았다. 존스의 미모는 그녀의 눈을 한껏 사로잡았다."], ["She could feast heartily at the table of love, without reflecting that some other already had been, or hereafter might be, feasted with the same repast.", "그러나 그의 마음속까지 들여다볼 수는 없는 노릇이었으므로, 그녀는 그 점에 대해 조금도 개의치 않았다. 그녀는 사랑의 식탁에서 마음껏 포식할 수 있었으니, 이미 다른 누군가가 같은 음식을 즐겼거나 앞으로 즐기게 될지도 모른다는 생각 따위는 떠올리지도 않았다."], ["A sentiment which, if it deals but little in refinement, deals, however, much in substance; and is less capricious, and perhaps less ill-natured and selfish, than the desires of those females who can be contented enough to abstain from the possession of their lovers, provided they are sufficiently satisfied that no one else possesses them.", "이런 마음가짐은, 비록 세련됨과는 거리가 멀지언정 알맹이만큼은 풍부한 것이며, 또 변덕도 덜하고, 어쩌면 심술도 이기심도 덜한 것이리라. 자기 연인을 다른 누구도 차지하지 않았다는 확신만 충분히 선다면 정작 자신이 그 연인을 차지하지 못해도 그럭저럭 만족할 수 있는 그런 여인들의 욕망에 비한다면 말이다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["Containing a fuller account of Mrs Waters, and by what means she came into that distressful situation from which she was rescued by Jones.", "7장. 워터스 부인에 대한 더 자세한 설명, 그리고 그녀가 존스에게 구출된 그 비참한 처지에 어떻게 빠지게 되었는가에 관하여."]], [["Though Nature hath by no means mixed up an equal share either of curiosity or vanity in every human composition, there is perhaps no individual to whom she hath not allotted such a proportion of both as requires much arts, and pains too, to subdue and keep under;--a conquest, however, absolutely necessary to every one who would in any degree deserve the characters of wisdom or good breeding.", "자연이 모든 인간에게 호기심과 허영심을 똑같은 몫으로 섞어 넣은 것은 결코 아니지만, 그 둘을 다스리고 억누르는 데 적잖은 기교와 수고가 필요할 만큼의 분량을 받지 않은 사람은 아마 하나도 없을 것이다. 그런데 이 정복이야말로, 지혜롭다거나 교양 있다는 평을 조금이라도 들을 만한 사람이라면 누구에게나 반드시 필요한 것이다."]], [["As Jones, therefore, might very justly be called a well-bred man, he had stifled all that curiosity which the extraordinary manner in which he had found Mrs Waters must be supposed to have occasioned.", "따라서 존스는 교양 있는 사람이라 불릴 자격이 충분했으므로, 워터스 부인을 그토록 기이한 방식으로 만난 일이 분명 불러일으켰을 그 모든 호기심을 억눌렀다. 사실 그는 처음에는 부인에게 몇 마디 넌지시 물어보기도 했다."], ["He had, indeed, at first thrown out some few hints to the lady; but, when he perceived her industriously avoiding any explanation, he was contented to remain in ignorance, the rather as he was not without suspicion that there were some circumstances which must have raised her blushes, had she related the whole truth.", "그러나 부인이 어떤 설명도 애써 피하는 것을 알아채자, 그는 모르는 채로 머무는 데 만족했다. 더구나 그녀가 모든 진실을 다 털어놓았다면 얼굴을 붉혔을 만한 사정이 얼마쯤 있으리라는 의심도 없지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Now since it is possible that some of our readers may not so easily acquiesce under the same ignorance, and as we are very desirous to satisfy them all, we have taken uncommon pains to inform ourselves of the real fact, with the relation of which we shall conclude this book.", "이제 우리 독자들 가운데 더러는 그처럼 모르는 채로 쉽게 수긍하지 않을지도 모르고, 또 우리는 그들 모두를 만족시키고 싶은 마음이 간절하므로, 우리는 실제 사실을 알아내려고 남다른 수고를 들였다. 그 이야기로 이 권을 마무리하려 한다."]], [["This lady, then, had lived some years with one Captain Waters, who was a captain in the same regiment to which Mr Northerton belonged.", "그러니까 이 부인은 몇 해 동안 워터스 대위라는 사람과 함께 살았는데, 그는 노더턴 씨가 속한 바로 그 연대의 대위였다."], ["She past for that gentleman's wife, and went by his name; and yet, as the serjeant said, there were some doubts concerning the reality of their marriage, which we shall not at present take upon us to resolve.", "그녀는 그 신사의 아내로 통했고 그의 성을 따랐다. 그러나 하사관이 말했듯이, 두 사람이 정말로 혼인한 것인지에는 약간의 의문이 있었는데, 그 문제는 지금 우리가 나서서 풀려 하지 않겠다."]], [["Mrs Waters, I am sorry to say it, had for some time contracted an intimacy with the above-mentioned ensign, which did no great credit to her reputation.", "말하기 안타깝지만, 워터스 부인은 한동안 앞서 말한 그 소위와 친밀한 관계를 맺어 왔고, 이는 그녀의 평판에 그리 좋지 못한 일이었다. 그녀가 그 젊은 친구를 유난히 좋아했다는 것은 거의 틀림없다."], ["That she had a remarkable fondness for that young fellow is most certain; but whether she indulged this to any very criminal lengths is not so extremely clear, unless we will suppose that women never grant every favour to a man but one, without granting him that one also.", "그러나 그녀가 그 애정을 정말로 죄가 될 만한 데까지 마음껏 풀어놓았는지는 그리 분명하지 않다. 여자란 한 가지 호의만 빼고 나머지 모든 호의를 남자에게 베풀면서 그 한 가지마저 함께 베풀지 않는 법이 없다고 우리가 가정하지 않는 한 말이다."]], [["The division of the regiment to which Captain Waters belonged had two days preceded the march of that company to which Mr Northerton was the ensign; so that the former had reached Worcester the very day after the unfortunate re-encounter between Jones and Northerton which we have before recorded.", "워터스 대위가 속한 연대의 부대는 노더턴 씨가 소위로 있던 중대보다 이틀 앞서 행군을 떠났다. 그래서 대위 쪽은 우리가 앞서 기록한 존스와 노더턴의 그 불운한 재회 바로 다음 날에 우스터에 닿았다."]], [["Now, it had been agreed between Mrs Waters and the captain that she would accompany him in his march as far as Worcester, where they were to take their leave of each other, and she was thence to return to Bath, where she was to stay till the end of the winter's campaign against the rebels.", "그런데 워터스 부인과 대위 사이에는, 그녀가 우스터까지 그의 행군에 동행하고 거기서 서로 작별한 뒤, 그녀는 다시 바스로 돌아가 반란군에 맞선 겨울 원정이 끝날 때까지 머무르기로 약속이 되어 있었다."]], [["With this agreement Mr Northerton was made acquainted.", "이 약속을 노더턴 씨도 알고 있었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 부인은 바로 이 장소에서 그와 밀회를 약속하고 그의 부대가 도착할 때까지 우스터에 머물겠노라 약속했던 것이다."], ["To say the truth, the lady had made him an assignation at this very place, and promised to stay at Worcester till his division came thither; with what view, and for what purpose, must be left to the reader's divination; for, though we are obliged to relate facts, we are not obliged to do a violence to our nature by any comments to the disadvantage of the loveliest part of the creation.", "무슨 속셈으로, 무슨 목적으로 그랬는지는 독자의 짐작에 맡길 수밖에 없다. 우리는 사실을 전할 의무는 있으나, 피조물 가운데 가장 사랑스러운 부류에게 불리한 어떤 논평을 달아 우리 본성을 거스르는 짓까지 할 의무는 없으니 말이다."]], [["Northerton no sooner obtained a release from his captivity, as we have seen, than he hasted away to overtake Mrs Waters; which, as he was a very active nimble fellow, he did at the last-mentioned city, some few hours after Captain Waters had left her.", "노더턴은 우리가 본 대로 감금에서 풀려나자마자 워터스 부인을 따라잡으려 서둘러 달려갔다. 그는 무척 날쌔고 민첩한 친구였으므로, 워터스 대위가 그녀와 헤어진 지 몇 시간 지나지 않아 앞서 말한 그 도시에서 그녀를 따라잡았다."], ["At his first arrival he made no scruple of acquainting her with the unfortunate accident; which he made appear very unfortunate indeed, for he totally extracted every particle of what could be called fault, at least in a court of honour, though he left some circumstances which might be questionable in a court of law.", "도착하자마자 그는 거리낌 없이 그 불운한 사고를 그녀에게 알렸는데, 그것을 정말로 몹시 불운한 일로 보이게 꾸몄다. 그는 적어도 명예의 법정에서라면 잘못이라 할 만한 부분은 한 톨도 남김없이 빼버렸으나, 법률의 법정에서라면 시비를 따질 만한 사정 몇 가지는 그대로 남겨두었다."]], [["Women, to their glory be it spoken, are more generally capable of that violent and apparently disinterested passion of love, which seeks only the good of its object, than men.", "여자들은 — 그들의 영예를 위해 하는 말이지만 — 오직 사랑하는 대상의 행복만을 바라는 그 격렬하고 겉보기에 사심 없는 사랑의 열정을, 남자들보다 대체로 더 잘 품을 줄 안다."], ["Mrs Waters, therefore, was no sooner apprized of the danger to which her lover was exposed, than she lost every consideration besides that of his safety; and this being a matter equally agreeable to the gentleman, it became the immediate subject of debate between them.", "그래서 워터스 부인은 자기 연인이 처한 위험을 알게 되자마자, 그의 안전 말고는 다른 모든 생각을 잃어버렸다. 그리고 이것은 그 신사에게도 똑같이 반가운 일이었으므로, 곧 두 사람 사이의 논의 거리가 되었다."]], [["After much consultation on this matter, it was at length agreed that the ensign should go across the country to Hereford, whence he might find some conveyance to one of the sea-ports in Wales, and thence might make his escape abroad.", "이 문제를 한참 의논한 끝에, 마침내 소위가 시골길을 가로질러 헤리퍼드로 가고, 거기서 웨일스의 어느 항구로 가는 교통편을 구해 해외로 달아나기로 합의가 되었다. 이 모든 여정에 워터스 부인은 그와 동행하겠노라 선언했고, 또 그에게 돈도 마련해 줄 수 있었다."], ["In all which expedition Mrs Waters declared she would bear him company; and for which she was able to furnish him with money, a very material article to Mr Northerton, she having then in her pocket three bank-notes to the amount of £90, besides some cash, and a diamond ring of pretty considerable value on her finger.", "노더턴 씨에게는 매우 긴요한 항목이었다. 그녀는 그때 주머니에 90파운드어치의 은행권 석 장과 그 밖의 현금을 지니고 있었고, 손가락에는 제법 값나가는 다이아몬드 반지를 끼고 있었다."], ["All which she, with the utmost confidence, revealed to this wicked man, little suspecting she should by these means inspire him with a design of robbing her.", "그녀는 이 모든 것을 더없는 신뢰로 이 악한에게 털어놓았으니, 이로써 자신이 그에게 강도질의 흉계를 불어넣게 되리라고는 거의 의심하지 못했다."], ["Now, as they must, by taking horses from Worcester, have furnished any pursuers with the means of hereafter discovering their route, the ensign proposed, and the lady presently agreed, to make their first stage on foot; for which purpose the hardness of the frost was very seasonable.", "그런데 우스터에서 말을 빌려 가면, 뒤쫓는 자들에게 훗날 자기들의 경로를 알아낼 단서를 주게 될 것이므로, 소위는 첫 구간을 걸어서 가자고 제안했고 부인도 곧 동의했다. 이를 위해서는 단단히 얼어붙은 서리가 무척 알맞았다."]], [["The main part of the lady's baggage was already at Bath, and she had nothing with her at present besides a very small quantity of linen, which the gallant undertook to carry in his own pockets.", "부인의 짐 대부분은 이미 바스에 가 있었고, 지금 그녀가 지닌 것이라곤 아주 적은 양의 속옷가지뿐이었는데, 그 멋쟁이가 자기 주머니에 넣어 나르겠노라 떠맡았다."], ["All things, therefore, being settled in the evening, they arose early the next morning, and at five o'clock departed from Worcester, it being then above two hours before day, but the moon, which was then at the full, gave them all the light she was capable of affording.", "그리하여 저녁에 모든 것을 정해 두고, 두 사람은 이튿날 아침 일찍 일어나 다섯 시에 우스터를 떠났다. 그때는 동트기까지 두 시간이 넘게 남았으나, 마침 보름이던 달이 베풀어 줄 수 있는 빛을 다 내어 주었다."]], [["Mrs Waters was not of that delicate race of women who are obliged to the invention of vehicles for the capacity of removing themselves from one place to another, and with whom consequently a coach is reckoned among the necessaries of life.", "워터스 부인은 한 곳에서 다른 곳으로 몸을 옮기는 능력을 탈것의 발명에 기대야만 하고, 따라서 마차를 생필품 가운데 하나로 꼽는 그런 가냘픈 부류의 여자가 아니었다."], ["Her limbs were indeed full of strength and agility, and, as her mind was no less animated with spirit, she was perfectly able to keep pace with her nimble lover.", "그녀의 팔다리는 사실 힘과 날렵함으로 가득했고, 마음 또한 그에 못지않게 기운차게 살아 있었으므로, 그녀는 자기의 날쌘 연인과 보조를 맞추는 데 조금도 모자람이 없었다."]], [["Having travelled on for some miles in a high road, which Northerton said he was informed led to Hereford, they came at the break of day to the side of a large wood, where he suddenly stopped, and, affecting to meditate a moment with himself, expressed some apprehensions from travelling any longer in so public a way.", "노더턴이 헤리퍼드로 통한다고 들었다는 큰길을 따라 몇 마일을 가고 나니, 두 사람은 동틀 무렵 어느 큰 숲가에 이르렀다. 거기서 그가 갑자기 걸음을 멈추더니, 혼자 잠시 골똘히 생각하는 척하면서, 이렇게 사람들 눈에 띄는 길로 더 가는 것이 마음에 걸린다고 말했다."], ["Upon which he easily persuaded his fair companion to strike with him into a path which seemed to lead directly through the wood, and which at length brought them both to the bottom of Mazard Hill.", "그러면서 그는 숲을 곧장 가로지르는 듯한 오솔길로 함께 들어서자고 어여쁜 길동무를 어렵잖게 설득했고, 그 길은 마침내 두 사람을 매저드 언덕 기슭으로 데려갔다."]], [["Whether the execrable scheme which he now attempted to execute was the effect of previous deliberation, or whether it now first came into his head, I cannot determine.", "그가 이제 실행에 옮기려 한 그 끔찍한 흉계가 미리 작정해 둔 결과였는지, 아니면 그제야 비로소 머릿속에 떠오른 것인지는 나도 단정할 수 없다."], ["But being arrived in this lonely place, where it was very improbable he should meet with any interruption, he suddenly slipped his garter from his leg, and, laying violent hands on the poor woman, endeavoured to perpetrate that dreadful and detestable fact which we have before commemorated, and which the providential appearance of Jones did so fortunately prevent.", "다만 어떤 방해도 받을 것 같지 않은 이 외딴곳에 이르자, 그는 갑자기 다리에서 양말 대님을 풀어 그 가엾은 여인에게 거칠게 손을 댔고, 우리가 앞서 적은 바 있는, 또 존스의 섭리 같은 등장이 그토록 다행히 막아 준, 그 무섭고 가증스러운 짓을 저지르려 했다."]], [["Happy was it for Mrs Waters that she was not of the weakest order of females; for no sooner did she perceive, by his tying a knot in his garter, and by his declarations, what his hellish intentions were, than she stood stoutly to her defence, and so strongly struggled with her enemy, screaming all the while for assistance, that she delayed the execution of the villain's purpose several minutes, by which means Mr Jones came to her relief at that very instant when her strength failed and she was totally overpowered, and delivered her from the ruffian's hands, with no other loss than that of her cloaths, which were torn from her back, and of the diamond ring, which during the contention either dropped from her finger, or was wrenched from it by Northerton.", "워터스 부인으로서는 자기가 가장 연약한 축의 여자가 아니라는 것이 다행이었다. 그가 대님에 매듭을 짓는 모습과 그의 말을 통해 그 지옥 같은 속셈을 알아채자마자, 그녀는 꿋꿋이 맞서 방어했다. 줄곧 도와달라 비명을 지르며 적과 어찌나 격렬하게 몸싸움을 벌였던지, 그녀는 그 악당의 목적이 실행되는 것을 몇 분이나 늦추었다. 그 덕분에 존스 씨는 그녀의 힘이 다하여 완전히 제압당하는 바로 그 순간에 그녀를 구하러 와, 그 무뢰한의 손아귀에서 그녀를 건져냈다. 잃은 것이라곤 등에서 찢겨 나간 옷과, 다투는 사이 손가락에서 빠졌거나 노더턴에게 비틀려 빼앗긴 다이아몬드 반지뿐이었다."]], [["Thus, reader, we have given thee the fruits of a very painful enquiry which for thy satisfaction we have made into this matter.", "이리하여, 독자여, 우리는 그대의 만족을 위해 이 일을 무척 고생스럽게 캐물어 얻은 결실을 그대에게 내어놓았다."], ["And here we have opened to thee a scene of folly as well as villany, which we could scarce have believed a human creature capable of being guilty of, had we not remembered that this fellow was at that time firmly persuaded that he had already committed a murder, and had forfeited his life to the law.", "그리고 여기서 우리는 흉악함 못지않게 어리석음의 한 장면을 그대 앞에 펼쳐 보였는데, 인간이라는 존재가 이런 짓을 저지를 수 있으리라고는 우리도 좀처럼 믿기 어려웠을 것이다. 다만 이 친구가 그때 이미 살인을 저질러 자기 목숨을 법에 내놓게 되었다고 굳게 믿고 있었음을 우리가 기억하지 않았다면 말이다."], ["As he concluded therefore that his only safety lay in flight, he thought the possessing himself of this poor woman's money and ring would make him amends for the additional burthen he was to lay on his conscience.", "그러니 자기의 유일한 살길은 도주뿐이라 결론짓고 보니, 그는 이 가엾은 여인의 돈과 반지를 차지하는 것이, 양심에 더 얹어야 할 그 짐을 어느 정도 보상해 주리라 여겼던 것이다."]], [["And here, reader, we must strictly caution thee that thou dost not take any occasion, from the misbehaviour of such a wretch as this, to reflect on so worthy and honourable a body of men as are the officers of our army in general.", "그리고 여기서, 독자여, 우리는 그대에게 엄히 당부해야겠다. 이런 비열한 자의 못된 짓을 빌미로, 우리 군대의 장교들이라는 대체로 훌륭하고 명예로운 집단을 함부로 비난하지 말라고 말이다."], ["Thou wilt be pleased to consider that this fellow, as we have already informed thee, had neither the birth nor education of a gentleman, nor was a proper person to be enrolled among the number of such.", "부디 생각해 주기 바란다. 이 친구는, 우리가 이미 알려 준 대로, 신사다운 혈통도 교육도 갖추지 못했고, 그런 사람들 축에 끼일 만한 인물도 아니었다."], ["If, therefore, his baseness can justly reflect on any besides himself, it must be only on those who gave him his commission.", "그러므로 그의 비천함이 그 자신 말고 다른 누구에게라도 정당히 누를 끼칠 수 있다면, 그것은 오로지 그에게 임관장을 내준 자들에게뿐일 것이다."]], [["# BOOK X.", "# BOOK X."]], [["IN WHICH THE HISTORY GOES FORWARD ABOUT TWELVE HOURS.", "독자여, 그대가 어떤 부류의 사람일지 우리로서는 알 길이 없다. 어쩌면 그대는 셰익스피어 그 자신만큼이나 인간 본성에 통달한 사람일 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그의 편집자 몇몇보다 조금도 더 현명하지 않은 사람일 수도 있다."], ["Chapter i.", "자, 후자의 경우일까 염려스러우니, 더 멀리 함께 나아가기 전에 그대에게 몇 마디 유익한 충고를 건네는 것이 마땅하다고 본다."], ["Containing instructions very necessary to be perused by modern critics.", "앞서 말한 편집자 몇몇이 자기네 작가를 오해하고 잘못 전한 것처럼, 그대가 우리를 그토록 심하게 오해하고 잘못 전하는 일이 없도록 말이다."]], [["Reader, it is impossible we should know what sort of person thou wilt be; for, perhaps, thou may'st be as learned in human nature as Shakespear himself was, and, perhaps, thou may'st be no wiser than some of his editors.", "그러면 우선, 이 우리 이야기 속의 어떤 사건이든 그것이 우리의 큰 구상과 무관하고 동떨어진 것이라며 너무 성급히 단죄하지 말라고 그대에게 경고하겠다."], ["Now, lest this latter should be the case, we think proper, before we go any farther together, to give thee a few wholesome admonitions; that thou may'st not as grossly misunderstand and misrepresent us, as some of the said editors have misunderstood and misrepresented their author.", "그저 그 사건이 어떤 식으로 그 구상에 이바지하는지 당장 떠오르지 않는다고 해서 말이다. 사실 이 작품은 우리 자신이 빚어낸 하나의 거대한 창조물로 볼 수 있다."], ["First, then, we warn thee not too hastily to condemn any of the incidents in this our history as impertinent and foreign to our main design, because thou dost not immediately conceive in what manner such incident may conduce to that design.", "그런데 한낱 벌레만 한 비평가가, 전체가 어떻게 연결되어 있는지도 모른 채, 그리고 마지막 파국에 이르기도 전에, 감히 그 일부에 흠을 잡으려 든다면 이는 더없이 주제넘은 어리석음이다."], ["This work may, indeed, be considered as a great creation of our own; and for a little reptile of a critic to presume to find fault with any of its parts, without knowing the manner in which the whole is connected, and before he comes to the final catastrophe, is a most presumptuous absurdity.", "여기서 우리가 끌어 쓴 비유와 은유가 우리 처지에는 한없이 과분하다는 것을 인정해야겠다."], ["The allusion and metaphor we have here made use of, we must acknowledge to be infinitely too great for our occasion; but there is, indeed, no other, which is at all adequate to express the difference between an author of the first rate and a critic of the lowest.", "하지만 실로, 일류 작가와 최하급 비평가 사이의 차이를 표현하기에 이만큼이라도 들어맞는 다른 비유가 달리 없다."]], [["Another caution we would give thee, my good reptile, is, that thou dost not find out too near a resemblance between certain characters here introduced; as, for instance, between the landlady who appears in the seventh book and her in the ninth. Thou art to know, friend, that there are certain characteristics in which most individuals of every profession and occupation agree. To be able to preserve these characteristics, and at the same time to diversify their operations, is one talent of a good writer. Again, to mark the nice distinction between two persons actuated by the same vice or folly is another; and, as this last talent is found in very few writers, so is the true discernment of it found in as few readers; though, I believe, the observation of this forms a very principal pleasure in those who are capable of the discovery; every person, for instance, can distinguish between Sir Epicure Mammon and Sir Fopling Flutter; but to note the difference between Sir Fopling Flutter and Sir Courtly Nice requires a more exquisite judgment: for want of which, vulgar spectators of plays very often do great injustice in the theatre; where I have sometimes known a poet in danger of being convicted as a thief, upon much worse evidence than the resemblance of hands hath been held to be in the law. In reality, I apprehend every amorous widow on the stage would run the hazard of being condemned as a servile imitation of Dido, but that happily very few of our play-house critics understand enough of Latin to read Virgil.", "나의 갸륵한 벌레여, 또 하나 그대에게 건네고 싶은 주의는, 여기 등장하는 어떤 인물들 사이에서 너무 가까운 닮은 점을 찾아내지 말라는 것이다. 이를테면 일곱째 권에 나오는 여관 안주인과 아홉째 권의 안주인 사이에서 말이다. 벗이여, 그대는 알아야 한다. 어느 직업과 생업에서든 대다수 사람이 공유하는 어떤 특성들이 있다는 것을. 이 특성들을 잘 지켜내면서 동시에 그들의 행동을 다채롭게 변주하는 것이 훌륭한 작가의 한 재능이다. 또한, 같은 악덕이나 같은 어리석음에 휘둘리는 두 사람 사이의 미묘한 차이를 짚어내는 것은 또 다른 재능이다. 그리고 이 마지막 재능을 갖춘 작가가 극히 드문 만큼, 그것을 제대로 알아보는 독자 또한 그만큼 드물다. 다만 나는 이를 알아챌 줄 아는 이들에게는 그 관찰이야말로 가장 으뜸가는 즐거움 가운데 하나가 되리라 믿는다. 예컨대 누구든 에피큐어 매먼 경과 포플링 플러터 경은 구별해낼 수 있다. 그러나 포플링 플러터 경과 코틀리 나이스 경 사이의 차이를 알아채려면 한층 더 섬세한 분별력이 필요하다. 그 분별력이 모자란 탓에, 연극을 보는 저속한 관객들은 극장에서 종종 큰 부당함을 저지른다. 나는 더러, 법에서 필적이 닮았다는 것을 증거로 삼는 것보다 훨씬 못한 증거를 가지고 어느 시인이 도둑으로 몰릴 위기에 처하는 것을 본 적이 있다. 실로 내가 우려하건대, 무대 위의 사랑에 빠진 과부란 과부는 모조리 디도를 비굴하게 베껴낸 것이라며 단죄당할 위험에 놓이리라. 다행히도 우리 극장 비평가 가운데 베르길리우스를 읽을 만큼 라틴어를 아는 이가 극히 드물다는 점이 그들을 구해줄 뿐이다."]], [["In the next place, we must admonish thee, my worthy friend (for, perhaps, thy heart may be better than thy head), not to condemn a character as a bad one, because it is not perfectly a good one.", "다음으로, 나의 귀한 벗이여(어쩌면 그대의 가슴이 그대의 머리보다 나을지도 모르니), 우리는 그대에게 일러두어야겠다. 어떤 인물이 완벽하게 선하지 않다고 해서 그를 나쁜 인물이라 단죄하지는 말라고."], ["If thou dost delight in these models of perfection, there are books enow written to gratify thy taste; but, as we have not, in the course of our conversation, ever happened to meet with any such person, we have not chosen to introduce any such here.", "만일 그대가 그런 완벽함의 본보기를 좋아한다면, 그대의 취향을 만족시켜줄 책은 얼마든지 쓰여 있다. 그러나 우리는 사람들과 어울려 살아오는 동안 그런 사람을 단 한 번도 만난 적이 없으니, 여기에 그런 인물을 들여놓지 않기로 했다."], ["To say the truth, I a little question whether mere man ever arrived at this consummate degree of excellence, as well as whether there hath ever existed a monster bad enough to verify that", "사실대로 말하자면, 나는 한낱 인간이 이토록 완전무결한 경지의 탁월함에 이른 적이 과연 있었는지 다소 의심스럽다. 마찬가지로, 유베날리스의 시구"]], [["----nulla virtute redemptum A vitiis----[*]", "\"----nulla virtute redemptum"]], [["[*] Whose vices are not allayed with a single virtue", "A vitiis----\"[*]"]], [["in Juvenal; nor do I, indeed, conceive the good purposes served by inserting characters of such angelic perfection, or such diabolical depravity, in any work of invention; since, from contemplating either, the mind of man is more likely to be overwhelmed with sorrow and shame than to draw any good uses from such patterns; for in the former instance he may be both concerned and ashamed to see a pattern of excellence in his nature, which he may reasonably despair of ever arriving at; and in contemplating the latter he may be no less affected with those uneasy sensations, at seeing the nature of which he is a partaker degraded into so odious and detestable a creature.", "를 입증할 만큼 충분히 사악한 괴물이 과연 존재한 적이 있었는지도 의심스럽다. 또한 실로 나는, 그런 천사 같은 완벽함이나 그런 악마 같은 타락함을 지닌 인물을 어떤 상상의 작품 속에 집어넣어서 이루어지는 유익이 무엇인지 도무지 알 수 없다. 둘 중 어느 쪽을 들여다보든, 사람의 마음은 그런 본보기에서 무언가 좋은 쓸모를 끌어내기보다 슬픔과 부끄러움에 짓눌릴 가능성이 더 크기 때문이다. 전자의 경우, 자기 본성 안에 결코 다다를 수 없으리라 마땅히 단념할 만한 탁월함의 본보기를 보면서 마음이 어지럽고 부끄러워질 수 있다. 그리고 후자를 들여다볼 때에도, 자기도 한몫 나누어 가진 그 본성이 그토록 가증스럽고 혐오스러운 존재로 추락한 것을 보면서 못지않게 괴로운 감정에 사로잡힐 수 있다. [*] 그 악덕이 단 하나의 미덕으로도 누그러지지 않은 자."]], [["In fact, if there be enough of goodness in a character to engage the admiration and affection of a well-disposed mind, though there should appear some of those little blemishes quas humana parum cavit natura, they will raise our compassion rather than our abhorrence.", "사실, 한 인물 안에 선의가 충분히 들어 있어 올바른 마음을 지닌 이의 찬탄과 애정을 끌어낼 정도라면, \"인간의 본성이 미처 다 막아내지 못한\"(quas humana parum cavit natura) 그 자잘한 흠 몇 가지가 드러난다 해도, 그것은 우리의 혐오가 아니라 오히려 우리의 연민을 불러일으킬 것이다."], ["Indeed, nothing can be of more moral use than the imperfections which are seen in examples of this kind; since such form a kind of surprize, more apt to affect and dwell upon our minds than the faults of very vicious and wicked persons.", "실로, 이런 본보기에서 보이는 결점들만큼 도덕적으로 쓸모 있는 것도 없다. 그런 결점은 일종의 놀라움을 자아내어, 몹시 사악하고 못된 사람들의 허물보다 우리 마음에 더 깊이 와닿고 더 오래 머물기 때문이다."], ["The foibles and vices of men, in whom there is great mixture of good, become more glaring objects from the virtues which contrast them and shew their deformity; and when we find such vices attended with their evil consequence to our favourite characters, we are not only taught to shun them for our own sake, but to hate them for the mischiefs they have already brought on those we love.", "선이 크게 뒤섞인 사람들의 약점과 악덕은, 그것과 대비되어 그 일그러짐을 드러내 보이는 미덕들 때문에 한층 더 도드라진 대상이 된다. 그리고 우리가 아끼는 인물들에게 그런 악덕이 좋지 못한 결과를 데리고 따라붙는 것을 볼 때, 우리는 우리 자신을 위해 그것을 멀리하라는 가르침을 얻을 뿐 아니라, 그 악덕이 우리가 사랑하는 이들에게 이미 끼친 해악 때문에 그것을 미워하라는 가르침까지 얻게 된다."]], [["And now, my friend, having given you these few admonitions, we will, if you please, once more set forward with our history.", "자 이제, 나의 벗이여, 그대에게 이 몇 마디 충고를 건넸으니, 괜찮다면 우리 다시 한번 이야기를 가지고 앞으로 나아가 보자."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["Containing the arrival of an Irish gentleman, with very extraordinary adventures which ensued at the inn.", "아일랜드 신사 한 사람의 도착과, 그 뒤를 이어 여관에서 벌어진 매우 기이한 사건들에 관하여."]], [["Now the little trembling hare, which the dread of all her numerous enemies, and chiefly of that cunning, cruel, carnivorous animal, man, had confined all the day to her lurking-place, sports wantonly o'er the lawns; now on some hollow tree the owl, shrill chorister of the night, hoots forth notes which might charm the ears of some modern connoisseurs in music; now, in the imagination of the half-drunk clown, as he staggers through the churchyard, or rather charnelyard, to his home, fear paints the bloody hobgoblin; now thieves and ruffians are awake, and honest watchmen fast asleep; in plain English, it was now midnight; and the company at the inn, as well those who have been already mentioned in this history, as some others who arrived in the evening, were all in bed. Only Susan Chambermaid was now stirring, she being obliged to wash the kitchen before she retired to the arms of the fond expecting hostler.", "이제 저 작고 떨리는 산토끼는, 수많은 적들, 그중에서도 교활하고 잔인하며 고기를 탐하는 짐승인 인간에 대한 두려움 때문에 온종일 숨어 지내던 은신처를 벗어나, 풀밭 위를 마음껏 뛰논다. 이제 어느 텅 빈 나무 위에서는 밤의 째지는 합창단원인 올빼미가, 요즘 음악 애호가 몇몇의 귀를 호릴 만한 가락으로 부엉부엉 운다. 이제 절반쯤 취한 시골뜨기가 비틀거리며 교회 묘지를, 아니 차라리 납골당 마당을 가로질러 집으로 돌아가는 동안, 그의 상상 속에서는 두려움이 피투성이 도깨비를 그려 낸다. 이제 도둑과 불한당들은 깨어나고, 정직한 야경꾼들은 곤히 잠들어 있다. 쉬운 말로 하자면, 때는 이제 한밤중이었다. 여관에 머무는 사람들도, 이 이야기에 이미 언급된 이들이든, 저녁나절에 도착한 다른 몇몇이든, 모두 잠자리에 든 뒤였다. 오직 하녀 수전만이 아직 움직이고 있었으니, 그녀는 자신을 애타게 기다리는 마부의 품에 안기러 가기 전에 부엌을 닦아야 했던 것이다."]], [["In this posture were affairs at the inn when a gentleman arrived there post.", "여관의 사정이 이러할 때, 한 신사가 역마를 타고 그곳에 당도했다."], ["He immediately alighted from his horse, and, coming up to Susan, enquired of her, in a very abrupt and confused manner, being almost out of breath with eagerness, Whether there was any lady in the house?", "그는 곧장 말에서 내려 수전에게 다가오더니, 열의에 차 숨이 거의 넘어갈 듯한 채로, 몹시 다급하고 어수선한 투로, 이 집에 부인이 한 사람 묵고 있느냐고 물었다. 밤늦은 시각과, 그 시간 내내 사납게 눈을 두리번거리는 그 사내의 행동에 수전은 조금 놀란 터라, 무어라 대답하기 전에 잠시 머뭇거렸다."], ["The hour of night, and the behaviour of the man, who stared very wildly all the time, a little surprized Susan, so that she hesitated before she made any answer; upon which the gentleman, with redoubled eagerness, begged her to give him a true information, saying, He had lost his wife, and was come in pursuit of her.", "그러자 신사는 곱절로 다급하게, 부디 제대로 알려 달라고 사정하며 말했다. 자기는 아내를 잃어버려 그녀를 쫓아왔노라고."], ["“Upon my shoul,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["cries he, “I have been near catching her already in two or three places, if I had not found her gone just as I came up with her.", "그가 외쳤다. \"두세 군데서 이미 거의 붙잡을 뻔했는데, 따라잡았다 싶으면 번번이 막 떠난 뒤였소."], ["If she be in the house, do carry me up in the dark and show her to me; and if she be gone away before me, do tell me which way I shall go after her to meet her, and, upon my shoul, I will make you the richest poor woman in the nation.”", "만약 그 여자가 이 집에 있다면, 부디 어둠 속으로 나를 데려가 그 여자를 보여 주시오. 또 그 여자가 나보다 앞서 떠났다면, 어느 길로 쫓아가야 그 여자를 만날 수 있을지 일러 주시오."], ["He then pulled out a handful of guineas, a sight which would have bribed persons of much greater consequence than this poor wench to much worse purposes.", "그러면 맹세코, 내 그대를 이 나라에서 가장 부유한 가난뱅이 여인으로 만들어 주겠소.\" 그러고는 기니 금화를 한 줌 꺼내 보였는데, 그 광경이라면 이 가련한 처녀보다 훨씬 지체 높은 사람들조차 훨씬 더 못된 일을 하도록 매수했을 만했다."]], [["Susan, from the account she had received of Mrs Waters, made not the least doubt but that she was the very identical stray whom the right owner pursued.", "수전은 워터스 부인에 관해 들은 이야기로 미루어, 그 신사가 정당한 주인으로서 쫓아온 바로 그 길 잃은 사람이 틀림없다고 한 치의 의심도 하지 않았다."], ["As she concluded, therefore, with great appearance of reason, that she never could get money in an honester way than by restoring a wife to her husband, she made no scruple of assuring the gentleman that the lady he wanted was then in the house; and was presently afterwards prevailed upon (by very liberal promises, and some earnest paid into her hands) to conduct him to the bedchamber of Mrs Waters.", "그리하여, 남편에게 아내를 되돌려 주는 것보다 더 정직하게 돈을 벌 길은 없으리라고 자못 그럴듯한 이치로 결론을 내린 그녀는, 신사가 찾는 부인이 지금 이 집에 있노라고 망설임 없이 장담했다. 그리고 곧이어 (아주 후한 약속과, 손에 쥐여 준 약간의 선금에 힘입어) 그를 워터스 부인의 침실로 안내하기로 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["It hath been a custom long established in the polite world, and that upon very solid and substantial reasons, that a husband shall never enter his wife's apartment without first knocking at the door.", "상류 사회에서는 아주 단단하고 실질적인 이유에 근거하여 오래전부터 굳어진 관습이 하나 있으니, 남편은 결코 먼저 문을 두드리지 않고서는 아내의 방에 들어가서는 안 된다는 것이다. 이 관습의 숱한 훌륭한 쓰임새는, 세상 물정을 조금이라도 아는 독자에게라면 굳이 귀띔할 필요조차 없으리라."], ["The many excellent uses of this custom need scarce be hinted to a reader who hath any knowledge of the world; for by this means the lady hath time to adjust herself, or to remove any disagreeable object out of the way; for there are some situations in which nice and delicate women would not be discovered by their husbands.", "이 방법 덕분에 부인은 매무새를 다듬거나, 거치적거리는 물건을 미리 치워 둘 시간을 얻기 때문이다. 까다롭고 섬세한 여인이라면 남편에게 들키고 싶지 않은 상황도 더러 있는 법이니까."]], [["To say the truth, there are several ceremonies instituted among the polished part of mankind, which, though they may, to coarser judgments, appear as matters of mere form, are found to have much of substance in them, by the more discerning; and lucky would it have been had the custom above mentioned been observed by our gentleman in the present instance.", "사실대로 말하자면, 세련된 부류의 사람들 사이에 정해진 여러 격식들이 있는데, 거친 판단에는 그저 형식에 불과한 것으로 보일지 몰라도, 더 분별 있는 이들은 그 안에 실질이 적잖이 담겨 있음을 안다. 그러니 만약 우리의 신사가 이번 경우에 앞서 말한 그 관습을 지켰더라면 얼마나 다행이었으랴."], ["Knock, indeed, he did at the door, but not with one of those gentle raps which is usual on such occasions.", "과연 그도 문을 두드리기는 했다. 그러나 그런 자리에서 흔히 쓰이는 점잖은 노크 따위는 아니었다."], ["On the contrary, when he found the door locked, he flew at it with such violence, that the lock immediately gave way, the door burst open, and he fell headlong into the room.", "오히려 문이 잠긴 것을 알자, 그는 어찌나 거칠게 달려들었던지, 자물쇠가 단번에 떨어져 나가고 문이 벌컥 열리며, 그는 머리부터 방 안으로 고꾸라졌다."]], [["He had no sooner recovered his legs than forth from the bed, upon his legs likewise, appeared--with shame and sorrow are we obliged to proceed--our heroe himself, who, with a menacing voice, demanded of the gentleman who he was, and what he meant by daring to burst open his chamber in that outrageous manner.", "그가 미처 두 다리로 몸을 일으키기도 전에, 침대에서 마찬가지로 두 다리로 벌떡 일어선 인물이 나타났으니―부끄럽고 슬픈 마음으로 이 이야기를 이어 갈 수밖에 없거니와―바로 우리의 주인공 그 자신이었다. 그는 위협하는 목소리로 그 신사에게 누구냐고, 또 무슨 까닭으로 감히 그토록 무례하게 자기 방문을 부수고 들어왔느냐고 따져 물었다."]], [["The gentleman at first thought he had committed a mistake, and was going to ask pardon and retreat, when, on a sudden, as the moon shone very bright, he cast his eyes on stays, gowns, petticoats, caps, ribbons, stockings, garters, shoes, clogs, &c., all which lay in a disordered manner on the floor.", "신사는 처음엔 자기가 무언가 착각한 줄로 알고, 용서를 청하며 물러나려 했다. 그런데 별안간, 달빛이 아주 밝게 비치는 통에, 코르셋이며 가운, 페티코트, 모자, 리본, 스타킹, 양말대님, 신발, 나막신 따위가 죄다 바닥에 어지러이 널려 있는 것이 눈에 들어왔다."], ["All these, operating on the natural jealousy of his temper, so enraged him, that he lost all power of speech; and, without returning any answer to Jones, he endeavoured to approach the bed.", "이 모든 것이 그의 천성적인 질투심을 자극하여, 그를 어찌나 격분케 했던지 그는 말할 힘마저 잃고 말았다. 그래서 존스에게 아무 대답도 하지 않은 채, 그는 침대 쪽으로 다가가려 했다."]], [["Jones immediately interposing, a fierce contention arose, which soon proceeded to blows on both sides.", "존스가 즉시 가로막자 격렬한 다툼이 일어났고, 이내 양쪽 모두 주먹다짐으로 번졌다."], ["And now Mrs Waters (for we must confess she was in the same bed), being, I suppose, awakened from her sleep, and seeing two men fighting in her bedchamber, began to scream in the most violent manner, crying out murder!", "이때 워터스 부인이 (실토하건대 그녀가 같은 침대에 있었으니) 짐작건대 잠에서 깨어, 자기 침실에서 두 사내가 싸우는 것을 보고는 더없이 격렬하게 비명을 지르기 시작했다. 살인이야!"], ["robbery!", "강도야!"], ["and more frequently rape!", "그리고 더 자주 외친 것은 겁탈이야!"], ["which last, some, perhaps, may wonder she should mention, who do not consider that these words of exclamation are used by ladies in a fright, as fa, la, la, ra, da, &c., are in music, only as the vehicles of sound, and without any fixed ideas.", "라는 소리였다. 어쩌면 누군가는 그녀가 마지막 말을 입에 올린 것을 의아해할지 모르나, 이는 그 외침의 말들이라는 것이, 겁에 질린 여인들에게는 음악에서의 파, 라, 라, 라, 다 따위와 마찬가지로, 단지 소리를 실어 나르는 도구일 뿐 어떤 정해진 뜻도 담고 있지 않음을 헤아리지 못한 탓이다."]], [["Next to the lady's chamber was deposited the body of an Irish gentleman who arrived too late at the inn to have been mentioned before.", "부인의 방 바로 옆에는 한 아일랜드 신사가 묵고 있었는데, 그는 여관에 너무 늦게 도착한 까닭에 앞서 언급되지 못한 사람이었다."], ["This gentleman was one of those whom the Irish call a calabalaro, or cavalier.", "이 신사는 아일랜드 사람들이 칼라발라로, 곧 기사라 부르는 부류에 속했다."], ["He was a younger brother of a good family, and, having no fortune at home, was obliged to look abroad in order to get one; for which purpose he was proceeding to the Bath, to try his luck with cards and the women.", "그는 괜찮은 집안의 차남으로, 집안에 물려받을 재산이 없는 터라 한밑천 마련하려고 밖으로 눈을 돌릴 수밖에 없었다. 그래서 그 목적으로 카드와 여자들로 운수를 시험해 보고자 배스로 가는 길이었다."]], [["This young fellow lay in bed reading one of Mrs Behn's novels; for he had been instructed by a friend that he would find no more effectual method of recommending himself to the ladies than the improving his understanding, and filling his mind with good literature.", "이 젊은이는 침대에 누워 벤 부인의 소설 한 권을 읽고 있었다. 한 친구로부터, 자기 식견을 갈고닦아 마음을 훌륭한 문학으로 채우는 것보다 숙녀들에게 자신을 더 효과적으로 추천하는 방법은 없다고 일러 받은 터였기 때문이다."], ["He no sooner, therefore, heard the violent uproar in the next room, than he leapt from his bolster, and, taking his sword in one hand, and the candle which burnt by him in the other, he went directly to Mrs Waters's chamber.", "그러니 옆방에서 그 요란한 소동이 들려오자마자, 그는 베개에서 벌떡 뛰어 일어나, 한 손에는 칼을, 다른 손에는 곁에서 타고 있던 촛불을 들고 곧장 워터스 부인의 방으로 향했다. * 벤 부인: 영국 최초의 여성 직업 작가로 꼽히는 애프라 벤(Aphra Behn)을 가리킨다."]], [["If the sight of another man in his shirt at first added some shock to the decency of the lady, it made her presently amends by considerably abating her fears; for no sooner had the calabalaro entered the room than he cried out, “Mr Fitzpatrick, what the devil is the maning of this?” Upon which the other immediately answered, “O, Mr Maclachlan! I am rejoiced you are here.--This villain hath debauched my wife, and is got into bed with her.”--“What wife?” cries Maclachlan; “do not I know Mrs Fitzpatrick very well, and don't I see that the lady, whom the gentleman who stands here in his shirt is lying in bed with, is none of her?”", "셔츠 바람의 또 다른 사내가 등장한 광경이 처음엔 부인의 정숙함에 얼마간 충격을 더했다면, 그것은 곧 그녀의 두려움을 상당히 가라앉혀 주는 것으로 보상을 해 주었다. 칼라발라로가 방에 들어서자마자 이렇게 외쳤기 때문이다. \"피츠패트릭 씨, 도대체 이게 무슨 영문이오?\" 그러자 상대는 즉시 대답했다. \"오, 매클라클런 씨! 당신이 여기 있다니 반갑소.―이 악당이 내 아내를 농락하고는 그 여자와 한 침대에 들어가 있단 말이오.\"―\"무슨 아내요?\" 매클라클런이 외쳤다. \"내가 피츠패트릭 부인을 잘 모를 리가 있소? 그리고 여기 셔츠 바람으로 서 있는 이 신사가 한 침대에 누워 있는 저 부인이, 그분이 전혀 아니라는 게 안 보이오?\""]], [["Fitzpatrick, now perceiving, as well by the glimpse he had of the lady, as by her voice, which might have been distinguished at a greater distance than he now stood from her, that he had made a very unfortunate mistake, began to ask many pardons of the lady; and then, turning to Jones, he said, “I would have you take notice I do not ask your pardon, for you have bate me; for which I am resolved to have your blood in the morning.”", "피츠패트릭은 이제, 부인의 모습을 얼핏 본 것으로도, 또 지금 자기가 서 있는 거리보다 훨씬 멀리서도 알아챌 수 있었을 그녀의 목소리로도, 자기가 아주 불운한 착각을 저질렀음을 깨닫고, 부인에게 거듭 용서를 빌기 시작했다. 그러고는 존스 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"당신한테만은 용서를 빌지 않겠다는 걸 알아 두시오. 당신이 날 때렸으니, 아침에 기어이 당신 피를 보고야 말겠소.\""]], [["Jones treated this menace with much contempt; and Mr Maclachlan answered, “Indeed, Mr Fitzpatrick, you may be ashamed of your own self, to disturb people at this time of night; if all the people in the inn were not asleep, you would have awakened them as you have me.", "존스는 이 협박을 한껏 경멸하며 받아넘겼고, 매클라클런 씨가 대꾸했다. \"정말이지, 피츠패트릭 씨, 이런 한밤중에 사람들을 깨워 놓다니, 부끄러운 줄 아시오."], ["The gentleman has served you very rightly.", "여관 사람들이 죄다 잠들어 있지 않았더라면, 당신이 나를 깨운 것처럼 그들도 다 깨워 놨을 거요."], ["Upon my conscience, though I have no wife, if you had treated her so, I would have cut your throat.”", "이 신사가 당신을 아주 마땅히 다뤘소. 양심을 걸고 말하건대, 나는 아내가 없는 몸이지만, 당신이 내 아내를 그런 식으로 대했다면 나도 당신 목을 베어 버렸을 거요.\""]], [["Jones was so confounded with his fears for his lady's reputation, that he knew neither what to say or do; but the invention of women is, as hath been observed, much readier than that of men.", "존스는 자기 부인의 평판이 걱정되어 어찌나 당황했던지, 무어라 말해야 할지도 어찌해야 할지도 알 수 없었다. 그러나 앞서도 살펴보았듯이, 여자들의 임기응변은 남자들의 그것보다 훨씬 더 재빠른 법이다."], ["She recollected that there was a communication between her chamber and that of Mr Jones; relying, therefore, on his honour and her own assurance, she answered, “I know not what you mean, villains!", "그녀는 자기 방과 존스 씨의 방 사이에 통로가 있음을 떠올렸다. 그래서 그의 신의와 자기 자신의 뻔뻔함에 기대어, 이렇게 대답했다."], ["I am wife to none of you.", "\"무슨 소린지 모르겠어요, 악당들 같으니! 나는 당신들 누구의 아내도 아니에요."], ["Help!", "사람 살려!"], ["Rape!", "겁탈이야! 살인이야!"], ["Murder!", "겁탈이야!\"―그리고 이제 여관 안주인이 방으로 들어오자, 워터스 부인은 더없이 사납게 그녀에게 달려들며 말했다. \"나는 이 집을 점잖은 여관인 줄 알았지 매춘굴인 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["Rape!”--And now, the landlady coming into the room, Mrs Waters fell upon her with the utmost virulence, saying, “She thought herself in a sober inn, and not in a bawdy-house; but that a set of villains had broke into her room, with an intent upon her honour, if not upon her life; and both, she said, were equally dear to her.”", "그런데 한 무리의 악당들이 내 방에 쳐들어와 내 정조를, 어쩌면 내 목숨까지 노렸단 말이에요. 그런데 나한테는 그 둘이 똑같이 소중하다고요.\""]], [["The landlady now began to roar as loudly as the poor woman in bed had done before.", "이제 여관 안주인이 조금 전 침대 속 가련한 여인이 그랬던 것만큼이나 큰 소리로 울부짖기 시작했다. 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["She cried, “She was undone, and that the reputation of her house, which was never blown upon before, was utterly destroyed.”", "\"나는 이제 끝장이에요. 여태껏 한 번도 더럽혀진 적 없던 우리 집 평판이 완전히 망가져 버렸어요.\""], ["Then, turning to the men, she cried, “What, in the devil's name, is the reason of all this disturbance in the lady's room?”", "그러고는 사내들 쪽으로 돌아서서 외쳤다. \"도대체 무슨 까닭으로 부인 방에서 이 난리법석을 떠는 거예요?\""], ["Fitzpatrick, hanging down his head, repeated, “That he had committed a mistake, for which he heartily asked pardon,” and then retired with his countryman.", "피츠패트릭은 고개를 푹 숙인 채, \"자기가 착각을 했으니 진심으로 용서를 빈다\"고 거듭 말하고는, 동향 사람과 함께 물러갔다."], ["Jones, who was too ingenious to have missed the hint given him by his fair one, boldly asserted, “That he had run to her assistance upon hearing the door broke open, with what design he could not conceive, unless of robbing the lady; which, if they intended, he said, he had the good fortune to prevent.”", "존스는 워낙 영리한지라 미인이 던져 준 암시를 놓칠 리 없었으니, 대담하게 주장하기를, \"문이 부서지는 소리를 듣고 그녀를 도우러 달려온 것이며, 부인을 터는 것 말고는 저들이 무슨 속셈이었는지 짐작도 가지 않거니와, 만약 그게 저들의 작정이었다면, 다행히도 자기가 그걸 막아 낸 셈\"이라고 했다. \"내가 이 집을 꾸려 온 이래로 우리 집에서 강도질이 벌어진 적은 한 번도 없어요.\""], ["“I never had a robbery committed in my house since I have kept it,”", "안주인이 외쳤다. \"분명히 말해 두는데요, 선생님, 나는 여기서 노상강도 따위는 절대 들이지 않아요."], ["cries the landlady; “I would have you to know, sir, I harbour no highwaymen here; I scorn the word, thof I say it.", "내 입으로 말하긴 뭣하지만, 그런 말은 입에 담기도 싫어요. 우리 집엔 정직하고 점잖은 양반들만 환영이지요."], ["None but honest, good gentlefolks, are welcome to my house; and, I thank good luck, I have always had enow of such customers; indeed as many as I could entertain.", "그리고 운 좋게도 그런 손님이라면 늘 차고 넘치게 받아 왔답니다. 정말이지 내가 다 모실 수 없을 만큼 많았어요."], ["Here hath been my lord--,”", "여기엔 무슨 무슨 경께서도 묵으셨고―,\""], ["and then she repeated over a catalogue of names and titles, many of which we might, perhaps, be guilty of a breach of privilege by inserting.", "그러고는 이름과 작위를 줄줄이 늘어놓았는데, 그중 상당수는 여기 옮겨 적었다가는 자칫 그분들의 특권을 침해하는 죄를 저지를지도 모르겠다."]], [["Jones, after much patience, at length interrupted her, by making an apology to Mrs Waters, for having appeared before her in his shirt, assuring her “That nothing but a concern for her safety could have prevailed on him to do it.”", "존스는 한참을 참다가, 마침내 워터스 부인에게 셔츠 바람으로 그녀 앞에 나타난 것을 사과하며 그녀의 말을 가로막았으니, \"그녀의 안전을 염려하는 마음이 아니었다면 결코 그런 짓을 하지 않았을 것\"이라고 다짐을 두었다."], ["The reader may inform himself of her answer, and, indeed, of her whole behaviour to the end of the scene, by considering the situation which she affected, it being that of a modest lady, who was awakened out of her sleep by three strange men in her chamber.", "그녀의 대답이 어떠했는지, 아니 실은 이 장면이 끝날 때까지 그녀가 보인 처신 전부가 어떠했는지는, 그녀가 꾸며 낸 처지, 곧 한밤중 낯선 사내 셋에게 잠을 깬 정숙한 부인의 처지를 헤아려 보면 독자 스스로 짐작할 수 있으리라."], ["This was the part which she undertook to perform; and, indeed, she executed it so well, that none of our theatrical actresses could exceed her, in any of their performances, either on or off the stage.", "이것이 그녀가 떠맡아 연기한 배역이었다. 그리고 정말이지 그녀는 그 역을 어찌나 잘 해냈던지, 우리네 극장의 어떤 여배우도 무대 위에서든 무대 밖에서든 그 어떤 연기로도 그녀를 능가하지 못했으리라."]], [["And hence, I think, we may very fairly draw an argument, to prove how extremely natural virtue is to the fair sex; for, though there is not, perhaps, one in ten thousand who is capable of making a good actress, and even among these we rarely see two who are equally able to personate the same character, yet this of virtue they can all admirably well put on; and as well those individuals who have it not, as those who possess it, can all act it to the utmost degree of perfection.", "그리고 여기서, 내 생각에 우리는 정숙함이라는 덕이 여성에게 얼마나 지극히 자연스러운 것인지를 증명할 논거 하나를 꽤 정당하게 끌어낼 수 있을 듯하다. 만 명에 한 명도 훌륭한 여배우가 될 만한 사람은 없을 테고, 그런 이들 가운데서도 같은 배역을 똑같이 잘 연기할 수 있는 둘을 보기란 드문 일이지만, 그럼에도 이 정숙함이라는 덕만큼은 여자들 모두가 감탄스러울 만큼 능숙하게 걸쳐 보일 줄 알기 때문이다. 그것을 지니지 못한 이들도, 그것을 지닌 이들 못지않게, 하나같이 더할 나위 없이 완벽하게 그것을 연기해 낼 수 있는 것이다."]], [["When the men were all departed, Mrs Waters, recovering from her fear, recovered likewise from her anger, and spoke in much gentler accents to the landlady, who did not so readily quit her concern for the reputation of the house, in favour of which she began again to number the many great persons who had slept under her roof; but the lady stopt her short, and having absolutely acquitted her of having had any share in the past disturbance, begged to be left to her repose, which, she said, she hoped to enjoy unmolested during the remainder of the night.", "사내들이 모두 떠나자, 워터스 부인은 두려움에서 벗어남과 동시에 노여움에서도 풀려나, 한결 부드러운 어조로 안주인에게 말을 건넸다. 안주인은 그래도 집안 평판에 대한 걱정을 그리 쉽게 놓지 못하고, 그 평판을 두둔하느라 자기 지붕 아래 묵어 갔던 숱한 고귀한 분들을 다시 헤아리기 시작했다."], ["Upon which the landlady, after much civility and many courtsies, took her leave.", "그러나 부인이 그녀의 말을 짧게 끊고는, 그녀가 지난 소동에 아무런 관련도 없음을 분명히 인정해 준 뒤, 부디 자기를 쉬게 내버려 두기를, 그리고 남은 밤 동안 방해받지 않고 그 쉼을 누리기를 바란다고 청했다. 그러자 안주인은 정중한 인사와 거듭된 무릎 굽힌 절을 한 뒤에 물러갔다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["A dialogue between the landlady and Susan the chamber-maid, proper to be read by all inn-keepers and their servants; with the arrival, and affable behaviour of a beautiful young lady; which may teach persons of condition how they may acquire the love of the whole world.", "여관 안주인과 객실 하녀 수전이 나누는 대화. 모든 여관 주인과 그 하인들이 읽어 둘 만한 내용이다. 더불어 아름다운 젊은 숙녀가 도착하여 보이는 상냥한 처신이 그려지는데, 이는 지체 높은 사람들에게 어떻게 하면 온 세상의 사랑을 얻을 수 있는지를 가르쳐 줄 것이다."]], [["The landlady, remembering that Susan had been the only person out of bed when the door was burst open, resorted presently to her, to enquire into the first occasion of the disturbance, as well as who the strange gentleman was, and when and how he arrived.", "여관 안주인은 문이 부서져 열렸을 때 잠자리에서 일어나 있던 사람이 수전뿐이었음을 떠올리고, 곧장 그녀에게 가서 이 소동이 처음 어떻게 시작되었는지, 그리고 그 낯선 신사는 누구이며 언제 어떻게 도착했는지를 물었다."]], [["Susan related the whole story which the reader knows already, varying the truth only in some circumstances, as she saw convenient, and totally concealing the money which she had received.", "수전은 독자가 이미 아는 이야기를 모조리 들려주었는데, 다만 자기 편할 대로 몇몇 정황만 사실과 달리 둘러댔고, 자기가 받은 돈에 대해서는 완전히 입을 다물었다."], ["But whereas her mistress had, in the preface to her enquiry, spoken much in compassion for the fright which the lady had been in concerning any intended depredations on her virtue, Susan could not help endeavouring to quiet the concern which her mistress seemed to be under on that account, by swearing heartily she saw Jones leap out from her bed.", "그런데 안주인이 질문을 꺼내기에 앞서, 그 숙녀가 자신의 정조를 노린 어떤 침범이 있지 않을까 겁에 질렸던 일을 두고 한참 측은해하며 말했던 터라, 수전은 안주인이 그 일로 품은 듯한 걱정을 가라앉히려는 마음에서, 존스가 그녀의 침대에서 뛰쳐나오는 것을 똑똑히 보았노라고 한껏 힘주어 맹세하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["The landlady fell into a violent rage at these words.", "이 말에 안주인은 불같이 화를 냈다."], ["“A likely story, truly,”", "\"그거 참 그럴듯한 이야기로구나.\""], ["cried she, “that a woman should cry out, and endeavour to expose herself, if that was the case!", "그녀가 소리쳤다. \"정말 그랬다면 여자가 비명을 지르며 제 일을 다 까발리려 들었겠느냐!"], ["I desire to know what better proof any lady can give of her virtue than her crying out, which, I believe, twenty people can witness for her she did?", "어떤 숙녀가 비명을 지르는 것 말고 제 정조를 더 잘 증명할 무슨 방도가 있는지 나는 알고 싶구나. 게다가 그 비명은 스무 사람이라도 증언해 줄 수 있을 게다."], ["I beg, madam, you would spread no such scandal of any of my guests; for it will not only reflect on them, but upon the house; and I am sure no vagabonds, nor wicked beggarly people, come here.”", "부탁이니, 얘야, 내 손님 어느 누구에 대해서도 그런 추문은 퍼뜨리지 말거라. 그건 손님들 체면뿐 아니라 우리 집 체면에까지 먹칠하는 일이고, 분명히 말하지만 여기엔 떠돌이도, 못된 거지 같은 인간도 오지 않는다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["says Susan, “then I must not believe my own eyes.”", "수전이 말했다. \"그럼 제 두 눈으로 본 것도 못 믿어야 한다는 말씀이네요.\""], ["“No, indeed, must you not always,”", "\"그래, 정말이지 늘 제 눈을 믿을 게 못 된단다.\" 안주인이 대답했다."], ["answered her mistress; “I would not have believed my own eyes against such good gentlefolks.", "\"나라도 저런 점잖은 양반들을 두고는 내 눈조차 믿지 않았을 게다. 반년 동안 어젯밤만큼 좋은 저녁상을 주문받은 적이 없었어."], ["I have not had a better supper ordered this half-year than they ordered last night; and so easy and good-humoured were they, that they found no fault with my Worcestershire perry, which I sold them for champagne; and to be sure it is as well tasted and as wholesome as the best champagne in the kingdom, otherwise I would scorn to give it 'em; and they drank me two bottles.", "또 어찌나 너그럽고 기분 좋은 분들이던지, 내가 샴페인이라며 우스터셔산 배술을 팔았는데도 흠 하나 잡지 않으셨다. 하기야 그건 이 나라 최고급 샴페인 못지않게 맛도 좋고 몸에도 이로우니까, 아니라면 내가 부끄러워서 내드리지도 않았겠지."], ["No, no, I will never believe any harm of such sober good sort of people.”", "그분들이 그걸 두 병이나 비우셨어. 아니, 아니야, 나는 저렇게 점잖고 착실한 분들을 두고 무슨 나쁜 일이 있었다고는 절대 믿지 않을 게다.\""]], [["Susan being thus silenced, her mistress proceeded to other matters.", "이렇게 수전의 입을 막은 뒤, 안주인은 다른 일로 화제를 옮겼다. \"그러니까 네 말은,\""], ["“And so you tell me,”", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["continued she, “that the strange gentleman came post, and there is a footman without with the horses; why, then, he is certainly some of your great gentlefolks too.", "\"그 낯선 신사가 역마차를 타고 왔고, 밖에는 말을 끄는 하인이 하나 있다는 거지. 그렇다면 그분도 분명 너희가 말하는 그 지체 높은 양반들 중 하나일 게다."], ["Why did not you ask him whether he'd have any supper?", "왜 저녁을 드시겠느냐고 묻지 않았느냐?"], ["I think he is in the other gentleman's room; go up and ask whether he called.", "그분이 그 신사분 방에 함께 있는 모양인데, 올라가서 부르셨는지 여쭤 보거라."], ["Perhaps he'll order something when he finds anybody stirring in the house to dress it.", "집 안에 누가 깨어 음식을 차릴 수 있다는 걸 알면 뭐라도 시키실지 모르니."], ["Now don't commit any of your usual blunders, by telling him the fire's out, and the fowls alive.", "자, 평소처럼 실수는 하지 말거라. 불이 다 꺼졌다느니, 닭이 아직 살아 있다느니 하는 소리 말이다."], ["And if he should order mutton, don't blab out that we have none.", "혹시 양고기를 시키시거든, 우리한텐 없다고 함부로 떠벌리지도 말고."], ["The butcher, I know, killed a sheep just before I went to bed, and he never refuses to cut it up warm when I desire it.", "푸줏간 주인이 내가 자러 가기 직전에 양 한 마리를 잡은 걸 내가 아는데, 내가 청하면 따뜻할 때 곧장 잘라 주는 걸 한 번도 마다한 적이 없으니까."], ["Go, remember there's all sorts of mutton and fowls; go, open the door with, Gentlemen, d'ye call?", "가거라, 잊지 말고, 양고기든 닭고기든 다 있다고 하거라. 가서 문을 열며 이렇게 말해."], ["and if they say nothing, ask what his honour will be pleased to have for supper?", "'신사 여러분, 부르셨습니까?' 그래도 아무 말씀 없으시거든, 나리께서 저녁으로 무얼 드시면 좋겠느냐고 여쭙거라."], ["Don't forget his honour.", "'나리'라는 말 잊지 말고."], ["Go; if you don't mind all these matters better, you'll never come to anything.”", "어서 가. 이런 일들을 더 잘 챙기지 못하면 너는 평생 변변한 사람이 되지 못할 게다.\""]], [["Susan departed, and soon returned with an account that the two gentlemen were got both into the same bed.", "수전은 떠났다가 이내 돌아와, 두 신사가 둘 다 같은 침대에 들어가 있더라고 전했다. \"신사 둘이,\""], ["“Two gentlemen,”", "안주인이 말했다. \"한 침대에 든다고!"], ["says the landlady, “in the same bed!", "그럴 리가. 분명히 둘 다 형편없는 건달들일 게다."], ["that's impossible; they are two arrant scrubs, I warrant them; and I believe young Squire Allworthy guessed right, that the fellow intended to rob her ladyship; for, if he had broke open the lady's door with any of the wicked designs of a gentleman, he would never have sneaked away to another room to save the expense of a supper and a bed to himself.", "게다가 젊은 올워디 도련님이 옳게 짐작하신 모양이다. 그자가 마님을 털려고 했다는 그 말 말이야."], ["They are certainly thieves, and their searching after a wife is nothing but a pretence.”", "만약 그자가 신사가 품을 법한 못된 속셈으로 그 숙녀의 방문을 부수고 들어간 거라면, 제 저녁값과 침대값을 아끼겠다고 슬그머니 다른 방으로 기어들어 갈 리가 없지 않겠느냐. 그자들은 틀림없이 도둑이고, 아내를 찾는다는 건 그저 핑계일 뿐이야.\""]], [["In these censures my landlady did Mr Fitzpatrick great injustice; for he was really born a gentleman, though not worth a groat; and though, perhaps, he had some few blemishes in his heart as well as in his head, yet being a sneaking or a niggardly fellow was not one of them.", "이런 비난으로 우리 안주인은 피츠패트릭 씨를 크게 부당하게 대한 셈이었다. 그는 사실 한 푼도 없을지언정 분명 신사로 태어난 사람이었고, 머리에든 마음에든 더러 흠이 몇 가지 있었을지는 몰라도, 비굴하다거나 인색하다는 것만은 그 흠 가운데 들지 않았다."], ["In reality, he was so generous a man, that, whereas he had received a very handsome fortune with his wife, he had now spent every penny of it, except some little pittance which was settled upon her; and, in order to possess himself of this, he had used her with such cruelty, that, together with his jealousy, which was of the bitterest kind, it had forced the poor woman to run away from him.", "실은 그는 어찌나 씀씀이가 큰 사람이었던지, 아내와 함께 꽤 두둑한 재산을 받았음에도 이제는 아내 앞으로 묶어 둔 약간의 보잘것없는 몫만 빼고 한 푼도 남김없이 다 써 버린 처지였다. 그런데 그 몫마저 차지하려고 그는 아내를 어찌나 모질게 대했던지, 더없이 지독한 그의 질투까지 겹쳐, 그 가엾은 여인은 끝내 그에게서 달아날 수밖에 없었다."]], [["This gentleman then being well tired with his long journey from Chester in one day, with which, and some good dry blows he had received in the scuffle, his bones were so sore, that, added to the soreness of his mind, it had quite deprived him of any appetite for eating.", "그러니 이 신사는 하루 만에 체스터에서부터 달려온 긴 여정으로 몹시 지쳐 있었고, 거기에다 몸싸움에서 단단히 얻어맞은 매까지 더해져 뼈마디가 어찌나 쑤시던지, 마음의 쓰라림과 합쳐져 음식 생각이 싹 가셔 버린 터였다."], ["And being now so violently disappointed in the woman whom, at the maid's instance, he had mistaken for his wife, it never once entered into his head that she might nevertheless be in the house, though he had erred in the first person he had attacked.", "또한 하녀의 부추김에 자기 아내인 줄로 착각했던 그 여자에게 이토록 격하게 낙담한 나머지, 처음 덮친 사람을 잘못 골랐을 뿐 그녀가 그래도 이 집 안에 있을지 모른다는 생각은 단 한 번도 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다."], ["He therefore yielded to the dissuasions of his friend from searching any farther after her that night, and accepted the kind offer of part of his bed.", "그리하여 그는 그날 밤 더 이상 그녀를 찾지 말라는 친구의 만류에 따르고, 침대 한쪽을 나누어 주겠다는 친절한 제안을 받아들였다."]], [["The footman and post-boy were in a different disposition.", "하인과 마차 소년은 사정이 달랐다."], ["They were more ready to order than the landlady was to provide; however, after being pretty well satisfied by them of the real truth of the case, and that Mr Fitzpatrick was no thief, she was at length prevailed on to set some cold meat before them, which they were devouring with great greediness, when Partridge came into the kitchen.", "그들은 안주인이 내어 줄 마음보다 훨씬 더 거리낌 없이 주문을 했다. 그래도 그들에게서 일이 정말 어찌 된 것인지, 그리고 피츠패트릭 씨가 도둑이 아니라는 것을 충분히 납득한 뒤, 안주인은 마침내 못 이기는 척 그들 앞에 찬 고기를 좀 차려 놓았는데, 그들이 그것을 게걸스럽게 먹어치우고 있을 때 파트리지가 부엌으로 들어왔다."], ["He had been first awaked by the hurry which we have before seen; and while he was endeavouring to compose himself again on his pillow, a screech-owl had given him such a serenade at his window, that he leapt in a most horrible affright from his bed, and, huddling on his cloaths with great expedition, ran down to the protection of the company, whom he heard talking below in the kitchen.", "그는 앞서 우리가 본 그 소란에 처음 잠을 깼다가, 다시 베개에 머리를 누이고 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓰던 차에, 부엉이 한 마리가 창가에서 어찌나 으스스한 세레나데를 불러 대던지 끔찍한 공포에 사로잡혀 침대에서 벌떡 뛰어내렸고, 부랴부랴 옷을 걸치고는 부엌에서 두런두런 이야기 소리가 들려오는 무리의 보호를 찾아 아래층으로 달려 내려온 것이었다."]], [["His arrival detained my landlady from returning to her rest; for she was just about to leave the other two guests to the care of Susan; but the friend of young Squire Allworthy was not to be so neglected, especially as he called for a pint of wine to be mulled. She immediately obeyed, by putting the same quantity of perry to the fire; for this readily answered to the name of every kind of wine.", "그가 온 탓에 우리 안주인은 다시 잠자리로 돌아가지 못했다. 그녀는 마침 다른 두 손님을 수전에게 맡기고 자리를 뜨려던 참이었기 때문이다. 그러나 젊은 올워디 도련님의 친구를 그렇게 소홀히 대할 수는 없었고, 더구나 그가 데운 포도주 한 파인트를 청하는 마당에는 더더욱 그러했다. 그녀는 곧장 응해, 같은 양의 배술을 불에 올렸다. 이 술은 어떤 종류의 포도주 이름에든 척척 둔갑해 주었으니 말이다."]], [["The Irish footman was retired to bed, and the post-boy was going to follow; but Partridge invited him to stay and partake of his wine, which the lad very thankfully accepted. The schoolmaster was indeed afraid to return to bed by himself; and as he did not know how soon he might lose the company of my landlady, he was resolved to secure that of the boy, in whose presence he apprehended no danger from the devil or any of his adherents.", "아일랜드인 하인은 잠자리로 물러갔고, 마차 소년도 따라가려던 참이었다. 그러나 파트리지가 남아서 자기 술을 함께 들자고 권하니, 소년은 감지덕지 받아들였다. 사실 그 교사는 혼자 잠자리로 돌아가기가 무서웠던 것이다. 그리고 우리 안주인의 동행을 얼마나 빨리 잃게 될지 알 수 없었기에, 그는 소년만이라도 곁에 붙들어 두기로 작정했다. 소년이 곁에 있는 한 악마든 그 졸개든 두려울 게 없으리라 여겼던 까닭이다."]], [["And now arrived another post-boy at the gate; upon which Susan, being ordered out, returned, introducing two young women in riding habits, one of which was so very richly laced, that Partridge and the post-boy instantly started from their chairs, and my landlady fell to her courtsies, and her ladyships, with great eagerness.", "바로 그때 또 다른 마차 소년이 대문 앞에 다다랐다. 수전이 나가 보라는 명을 받고 나갔다가 돌아오면서, 승마복을 차려입은 두 젊은 여인을 안내해 들였는데, 그중 한 사람은 옷에 어찌나 화려하게 레이스가 둘려 있던지, 파트리지와 마차 소년이 즉시 의자에서 벌떡 일어났고, 우리 안주인은 더없이 부산스레 절을 하며 \"마님, 마님\" 하고 굽실거렸다."]], [["The lady in the rich habit said, with a smile of great condescension, “If you will give me leave, madam, I will warm myself a few minutes at your kitchen fire, for it is really very cold; but I must insist on disturbing no one from his seat.” This was spoken on account of Partridge, who had retreated to the other end of the room, struck with the utmost awe and astonishment at the splendor of the lady's dress. Indeed, she had a much better title to respect than this; for she was one of the most beautiful creatures in the world.", "화려한 옷차림의 숙녀가 더없이 너그러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"허락해 주신다면, 부인, 부엌 불가에서 잠깐만 몸을 좀 녹였으면 합니다. 정말 무척 춥군요. 다만 누구도 자리에서 일어나게 하는 일만은 없으면 좋겠어요.\" 이 말은 파트리지를 두고 한 것이었다. 그는 숙녀의 눈부신 옷차림에 더없는 경외와 놀라움에 사로잡혀 방 저편 끝으로 물러나 있었다. 사실 그녀는 이 정도의 존경을 받을 자격을 훨씬 웃돌았으니, 세상에서 가장 아름다운 사람 가운데 하나였기 때문이다."]], [["The lady earnestly desired Partridge to return to his seat; but could not prevail.", "숙녀는 파트리지에게 자리로 돌아가라고 간곡히 청했으나, 끝내 그를 움직이지는 못했다."], ["She then pulled off her gloves, and displayed to the fire two hands, which had every property of snow in them, except that of melting.", "그러자 그녀는 장갑을 벗어, 불 앞에 두 손을 내밀어 보였다. 그 손은 녹는다는 한 가지만 빼면 눈(雪)의 온갖 성질을 다 지니고 있었다."], ["Her companion, who was indeed her maid, likewise pulled off her gloves, and discovered what bore an exact resemblance, in cold and colour, to a piece of frozen beef.", "사실 그녀의 하녀였던 동행 또한 장갑을 벗었는데, 차갑기로나 빛깔로나 영락없이 얼어붙은 쇠고기 한 덩이를 빼닮은 것을 드러내 보였다."]], [["“I wish, madam,”", "\"마님,\""], ["quoth the latter, “your ladyship would not think of going any farther to-night.", "하녀가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 더 멀리 가실 생각일랑 마님께서 거두셨으면 합니다."], ["I am terribly afraid your ladyship will not be able to bear the fatigue.”", "마님께서 그 피로를 견디지 못하실까 봐 정말 너무 걱정스럽습니다.\""]], [["“Why sure,”", "\"아무렴요.\""], ["cries the landlady, “her ladyship's honour can never intend it.", "안주인이 외쳤다. \"고귀하신 마님께서 설마 그러실 리가요."], ["O, bless me!", "어머나, 세상에!"], ["farther to-night, indeed!", "오늘 밤에 더 가시다니요, 정말!"], ["let me beseech your ladyship not to think on't----But, to be sure, your ladyship can't.", "부디 그런 생각일랑 마시기를 마님께 간청드립니다. 하지만 정말이지, 마님께서야 그러실 수 없으시지요."], ["What will your honour be pleased to have for supper?", "저녁으로 무얼 드시면 좋겠습니까, 마님?"], ["I have mutton of all kinds, and some nice chicken.”", "온갖 양고기가 다 있고, 맛 좋은 닭도 좀 있습니다.\""]], [["“I think, madam,” said the lady, “it would be rather breakfast than supper; but I can't eat anything; and, if I stay, shall only lie down for an hour or two. However, if you please, madam, you may get me a little sack whey, made very small and thin.”", "\"제 생각엔, 부인,\" 숙녀가 말했다. \"저녁이라기보다는 아침이 되겠군요. 하지만 저는 아무것도 먹을 수 없어요. 또 머문다 해도 한두 시간 누워 있을 뿐일 거예요. 그래도 괜찮으시다면, 부인, 백포도주를 탄 유장(乳漿)을 아주 묽고 연하게 좀 만들어 주시겠어요.\" * 색 위: 백포도주를 데워 우유를 넣어 응고시킨 음료."]], [["“Yes, madam,”", "\"네, 마님,\" 안주인이 외쳤다."], ["cries the mistress of the house, “I have some excellent white wine.”--“You have no sack, then?”", "\"아주 훌륭한 백포도주가 좀 있습지요.\" \"그럼 색 와인은 없는 건가요?\""], ["says the lady.", "숙녀가 말했다. \"있다마다요, 고귀하신 마님, 있고말고요."], ["“Yes, an't please your honour, I have; I may challenge the country for that--but let me beg your ladyship to eat something.”", "그것만은 이 고장 어디와도 견주어 자신 있습니다. 하지만 부디 마님께 청하오니 무어라도 좀 드시지요.\""]], [["“Upon my word, I can't eat a morsel,”", "\"정말이지 저는 한 입도 먹을 수가 없어요.\" 숙녀가 대답했다."], ["answered the lady; “and I shall be much obliged to you if you will please to get my apartment ready as soon as possible; for I am resolved to be on horseback again in three hours.”", "\"그러니 제 방을 되도록 빨리 채비해 주시면 정말 고맙겠어요. 저는 세 시간 안에 다시 말 위에 오르기로 마음먹었으니까요.\""]], [["“Why, Susan,”", "\"이런, 수전아,\""], ["cries the landlady, “is there a fire lit yet in the Wild-goose?", "안주인이 외쳤다. \"기러기 방에는 벌써 불을 지폈느냐?"], ["I am sorry, madam, all my best rooms are full.", "죄송합니다, 마님, 제일 좋은 방들이 죄다 찼습니다."], ["Several people of the first quality are now in bed.", "최상류층 손님 여러 분이 지금 잠자리에 들어 계셔서요."], ["Here's a great young squire, and many other great gentlefolks of quality.”", "여기 대단한 젊은 도련님 한 분에, 그 밖에도 지체 높은 양반들이 많이 계십니다.\""], ["Susan answered, “That the Irish gentlemen were got into the Wild-goose.”", "수전이 대답했다. \"그 아일랜드 신사분들이 기러기 방에 드셨는데요.\""]], [["“Was ever anything like it?”", "\"세상에 이런 일이 다 있나?\" 안주인이 말했다."], ["says the mistress; “why the devil would you not keep some of the best rooms for the quality, when you know scarce a day passes without some calling here?----If they be gentlemen, I am certain, when they know it is for her ladyship, they will get up again.”", "\"아니, 거의 하루도 빠짐없이 누군가 여기 들르는 걸 뻔히 알면서, 어째서 제일 좋은 방 몇 개는 지체 높은 분들을 위해 비워 두지 않았느냐? 그분들이 신사라면, 이게 마님을 위한 일이라는 걸 알면 분명 다시 일어나 주실 게다.\""]], [["“Not upon my account,”", "\"저 때문에는 안 됩니다.\""], ["says the lady; “I will have no person disturbed for me.", "숙녀가 말했다. \"저를 위해 누구도 방해받게 하고 싶지 않아요."], ["If you have a room that is commonly decent, it will serve me very well, though it be never so plain.", "보통 깨끗한 방이라면, 아무리 수수해도 저한테는 더없이 충분해요."], ["I beg, madam, you will not give yourself so much trouble on my account.”", "부디, 부인, 저 때문에 그렇게 애쓰지 마세요.\" \"어머, 마님!\""], ["“O, madam!”", "안주인이 외쳤다."], ["cries the other, “I have several very good rooms for that matter, but none good enough for your honour's ladyship.", "\"그런 거라면야 제법 괜찮은 방이 여럿 있긴 합니다만, 고귀하신 마님께 어울릴 만큼 좋은 방은 하나도 없어서요."], ["However, as you are so condescending to take up with the best I have, do, Susan, get a fire in the Rose this minute.", "하지만 마님께서 이렇게 너그러이 제가 가진 제일 좋은 방으로 만족해 주시겠다니, 수전아, 어서 장미 방에 지금 당장 불을 지피거라."], ["Will your ladyship be pleased to go up now, or stay till the fire is lighted?”", "마님, 지금 올라가시겠습니까, 아니면 불이 지펴질 때까지 기다리시겠습니까?\""], ["“I think I have sufficiently warmed myself,”", "\"몸은 충분히 녹인 것 같군요.\" 숙녀가 대답했다."], ["answered the lady; “so, if you please, I will go now; I am afraid I have kept people, and particularly that gentleman (meaning Partridge), too long in the cold already.", "\"그러니 괜찮으시다면 지금 올라가겠어요. 벌써 사람들을, 특히 저 신사분을(파트리지를 가리키며) 이 추위 속에 너무 오래 붙잡아 둔 것 같아 걱정스럽네요."], ["Indeed, I cannot bear to think of keeping any person from the fire this dreadful weather.”--She then departed with her maid, the landlady marching with two lighted candles before her.", "정말이지 이 끔찍한 날씨에 누구라도 불가에서 떼어 놓는다는 건 생각만 해도 견딜 수가 없어요.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 하녀와 함께 떠났는데, 안주인이 불 켜진 촛대 두 개를 들고 앞장서 걸었다."]], [["When that good woman returned, the conversation in the kitchen was all upon the charms of the young lady.", "그 착한 여인이 돌아왔을 때, 부엌 안의 대화는 온통 젊은 숙녀의 매력에 관한 것이었다."], ["There is indeed in perfect beauty a power which none almost can withstand; for my landlady, though she was not pleased at the negative given to the supper, declared she had never seen so lovely a creature.", "정녕 완벽한 아름다움에는 거의 아무도 거스를 수 없는 힘이 있는 법이다. 우리 안주인만 해도, 저녁 식사를 마다당한 것은 못마땅했으면서도, 그토록 사랑스러운 사람은 본 적이 없노라고 단언했으니까."], ["Partridge ran out into the most extravagant encomiums on her face, though he could not refrain from paying some compliments to the gold lace on her habit; the post-boy sung forth the praises of her goodness, which were likewise echoed by the other post-boy, who was now come in.", "파트리지는 그 얼굴을 두고 더없이 호들갑스러운 찬사를 쏟아 냈는데, 그러면서도 그 옷에 둘린 금빛 레이스에 한두 마디 칭찬을 보태는 것만은 참지 못했다. 마차 소년은 그녀의 착한 마음씨를 소리 높여 칭송했고, 그 칭송은 이제 막 들어온 또 한 사람의 마차 소년에 의해서도 똑같이 되울려졌다."], ["“She's a true good lady, I warrant her,”", "\"그분은 정말 마음씨 고운 마님이세요, 틀림없어요.\""], ["says he; “for she hath mercy upon dumb creatures; for she asked me every now and tan upon the journey, if I did not think she should hurt the horses by riding too fast?", "그가 말했다. \"말 못 하는 짐승한테까지 자비를 베푸시거든요."], ["and when she came in she charged me to give them as much corn as ever they would eat.”", "오시는 내내 이따금 저한테, 너무 빨리 달려서 말들이 상하지 않겠느냐고 물으셨고, 들어오실 때는 말들이 먹고 싶은 만큼 실컷 여물을 주라고 신신당부하셨으니까요.\""]], [["Such charms are there in affability, and so sure is it to attract the praises of all kinds of people.", "상냥함에는 이런 매력이 있어, 온갖 부류의 사람들로부터 칭송을 끌어내는 것이 어김없다."], ["It may indeed be compared to the celebrated Mrs Hussey.[*] It is equally sure to set off every female perfection to the highest advantage, and to palliate and conceal every defect.", "실로 그것은 저 유명한 허시 부인에 견줄 만하다. 상냥함은 여인의 온갖 장점을 더없이 돋보이게 하고, 온갖 결점을 가려 덮어 주는 데에 그만큼 어김이 없다."], ["A short reflection, which we could not forbear making in this place, where my reader hath seen the loveliness of an affable deportment; and truth will now oblige us to contrast it, by showing the reverse.", "이 자리에서 우리는 이 짧은 생각을 적어 두지 않을 수 없었으니, 독자께서는 여기서 상냥한 처신의 사랑스러움을 보셨다. 그러니 이제 진실은, 그 정반대의 모습을 보여 줌으로써 그것과 대비를 이루도록 우리에게 요구할 것이다."]], [["[*] A celebrated mantua-maker in the Strand, famous for setting off the shapes of women.", "* 허시 부인: 스트랜드 가의 이름난 여성복 재단사로, 여인의 몸매를 돋보이게 하는 솜씨로 유명했다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["Containing infallible nostrums for procuring universal disesteem and hatred.", "누구에게나 미움받고 멸시당하는 비법, 그 틀림없는 묘약에 관하여."]], [["The lady had no sooner laid herself on her pillow than the waiting-woman returned to the kitchen to regale with some of those dainties which her mistress had refused.", "부인이 베개에 머리를 채 누이기도 전에 시중드는 하녀는 부엌으로 돌아왔다. 제 마님이 마다한 그 맛난 음식들을 실컷 즐기려는 참이었다."]], [["The company, at her entrance, shewed her the same respect which they had before paid to her mistress, by rising; but she forgot to imitate her, by desiring them to sit down again. Indeed, it was scarce possible they should have done so, for she placed her chair in such a posture as to occupy almost the whole fire. She then ordered a chicken to be broiled that instant, declaring, if it was not ready in a quarter of an hour, she would not stay for it. Now, though the said chicken was then at roost in the stable, and required the several ceremonies of catching, killing, and picking, before it was brought to the gridiron, my landlady would nevertheless have undertaken to do all within the time; but the guest, being unfortunately admitted behind the scenes, must have been witness to the fourberie; the poor woman was therefore obliged to confess that she had none in the house; “but, madam,” said she, “I can get any kind of mutton in an instant from the butcher's.”", "그녀가 들어서자 좌중은 앞서 마님께 보였던 것과 똑같은 예를 갖추어 일제히 자리에서 일어났다. 그러나 그녀는 마님을 본받아 다들 다시 앉으라 권하는 일은 잊고 말았다. 사실 다시 앉기란 거의 불가능했으니, 그녀가 의자를 불 거의 전부를 차지하도록 떡하니 놓아 버렸기 때문이다. 이어 그녀는 당장 닭 한 마리를 구워 내라 명하면서, 십오 분 안에 준비되지 않으면 기다리지 않겠다고 못 박았다. 그런데 정작 그 닭은 그때 마구간에서 홰에 올라 자고 있었고, 석쇠에 오르기까지 잡고 죽이고 털을 뽑는 여러 절차를 거쳐야 했다. 그래도 우리 안주인은 그 시간 안에 다 해 보이겠노라 떠맡았을 것이다. 그러나 손님이 운 나쁘게도 무대 뒤편을 보게 된 처지였으니, 그 속임수를 고스란히 목격할 수밖에 없었으리라. 그리하여 가엾은 안주인은 집에 닭이 없음을 실토할 수밖에 없었다. \"하지만 마님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"정육점에서 양고기라면 어떤 부위든 당장 가져올 수 있습니다요.\""]], [["“Do you think, then,”", "\"그래, 자네는,\" 시중드는 귀부인 행세를 하는 하녀가 대꾸했다."], ["answered the waiting-gentlewoman, “that I have the stomach of a horse, to eat mutton at this time of night?", "\"내가 이 야밤에 양고기나 뜯게 말 같은 위장을 가졌다고 보는 건가? 정말이지 자네들처럼 여관 치는 사람들은 윗사람도 죄다 자기네 같은 줄 아는가 봐."], ["Sure you people that keep inns imagine your betters are like yourselves.", "하긴 이런 누추한 데서 뭘 얻어먹으리라 기대하지도 않았지. 우리 마님이 어쩌자고 여기 멈추셨는지 모르겠어."], ["Indeed, I expected to get nothing at this wretched place.", "여기야 상인이나 가축 장수나 드나드는 데지 누가 또 오겠어.\" 안주인은 제 집을 두고 던진 이 모욕에 발끈했다."], ["I wonder my lady would stop at it.", "그러나 화를 억누르고 이렇게 말하는 것으로 만족했다. \"고맙게도, 아주 점잖은 분들이 자주 찾으신답니다요!\""], ["I suppose none but tradesmen and grasiers ever call here.”", "\"점잖은 분들이라니, 그딴 소리 말게!\" 상대가 소리쳤다."], ["The landlady fired at this indignity offered to her house; however, she suppressed her temper, and contented herself with saying, “Very good quality frequented it, she thanked heaven!”", "\"점잖은 분들로 말하자면 내가 자네 따위보다야 훨씬 더 잘 안다고. 그러니 제발 그 건방진 말로 날 성가시게 하지 말고, 저녁으로 뭘 먹을 수 있는지나 말해 봐."], ["“Don't tell me,” cries the other, “of quality!", "나는 말고기는 못 먹지만, 정말이지 배가 고프단 말이야.\""], ["I believe I know more of people of quality than such as you.--But, prithee, without troubling me with any of your impertinence, do tell me what I can have for supper; for, though I cannot eat horse-flesh, I am really hungry.”", "\"이런, 정말이지 마님,\" 안주인이 대답했다."], ["“Why, truly, madam,”", "\"이번만큼은 또 이렇게 곤란하게 붙드시면 안 됩지요."], ["answered the landlady, “you could not take me again at such a disadvantage; for I must confess I have nothing in the house, unless a cold piece of beef, which indeed a gentleman's footman and the post-boy have almost cleared to the bone.”", "솔직히 집에 아무것도 없습니다요. 차게 식은 쇠고기 한 덩이가 있긴 하나, 실은 어느 신사분 하인하고 마부 녀석이 거진 뼈까지 발라 먹어 치웠고요.\""], ["“Woman,”", "\"여보게,\""], ["said Mrs Abigail (so for shortness we will call her), “I entreat you not to make me sick.", "애비게일 부인이(짧게 부르려고 우리는 그렇게 부르기로 한다) 말했다. \"제발 날 속 메스껍게 만들지 좀 말게."], ["If I had fasted a month, I could not eat what had been touched by the fingers of such fellows.", "한 달을 굶었다 해도 그런 작자들 손가락이 닿은 건 못 먹어. 이 끔찍한 데서 깔끔하고 점잖은 건 하나도 못 구하나?\""], ["Is there nothing neat or decent to be had in this horrid place?”", "\"달걀에 베이컨은 어떠십니까요, 마님?\" 안주인이 말했다."], ["“What think you of some eggs and bacon, madam?”", "\"달걀은 갓 낳은 거야? 오늘 낳은 게 틀림없는가?"], ["said the landlady.", "그리고 베이컨은 아주 곱고 얇게 썰어 줘."], ["“Are your eggs new laid?", "나는 기름지고 투박한 건 도무지 못 견디니까."], ["are you certain they were laid to-day?", "제발 한 번쯤은 그럭저럭 먹을 만하게 해 보고, 집에 농부 마누라나 그런 부류가 있는 줄 알지 좀 마.\" 안주인이 칼을 집어 들려 하자 상대가 그녀를 멈춰 세우며 말했다."], ["and let me have the bacon cut very nice and thin; for I can't endure anything that's gross.--Prithee try if you can do a little tolerably for once, and don't think you have a farmer's wife, or some of those creatures, in the house.”--The landlady began then to handle her knife; but the other stopt her, saying, “Good woman, I must insist upon your first washing your hands; for I am extremely nice, and have been always used from my cradle to have everything in the most elegant manner.”", "\"이봐, 우선 손부터 씻어 줘야겠어. 나는 워낙 까다로운 사람이라, 요람에 있을 적부터 늘 가장 고상한 방식으로만 모든 걸 받아 왔으니까.\""]], [["The landlady, who governed herself with much difficulty, began now the necessary preparations; for as to Susan, she was utterly rejected, and with such disdain, that the poor wench was as hard put to it to restrain her hands from violence as her mistress had been to hold her tongue.", "안주인은 가까스로 자신을 다스리면서 이제 필요한 채비를 시작했다. 수전으로 말하자면 아예 내쳐졌는데, 그 멸시가 어찌나 심했던지 가엾은 계집아이는 손이 거칠게 나가는 것을 참느라, 제 마님이 혀를 붙들고 있느라 애쓴 것 못지않게 안간힘을 써야 했다."], ["This indeed Susan did not entirely; for, though she literally kept it within her teeth, yet there it muttered many “marry-come-ups, as good flesh and blood as yourself;”", "사실 수전은 그것을 온전히 참아내지는 못했다. 말 그대로 이빨 사이에 가두기는 했으나, 그 안에서 \"어이구 잘났네, 너나 나나 똑같은 살덩이 핏덩이지\""], ["with other such indignant phrases.", "따위 분에 받친 말들을 여러 번 중얼거렸으니 말이다."]], [["While the supper was preparing, Mrs Abigail began to lament she had not ordered a fire in the parlour; but, she said, that was now too late. “However,” said she, “I have novelty to recommend a kitchen; for I do not believe I ever eat in one before.” Then, turning to the post-boys, she asked them, “Why they were not in the stable with their horses? If I must eat my hard fare here, madam,” cries she to the landlady, “I beg the kitchen may be kept clear, that I may not be surrounded with all the blackguards in town: as for you, sir,” says she to Partridge, “you look somewhat like a gentleman, and may sit still if you please; I don't desire to disturb anybody but mob.”", "저녁이 준비되는 동안 애비게일 부인은 거실에 불을 피워 두라 시키지 않은 것을 한탄하기 시작했다. 그러나 이제는 너무 늦었다고 했다. \"그래도,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"부엌에서 먹는 것도 새롭다고 봐줄 만하지. 전에 한 번도 부엌에서 먹어 본 적이 없는 것 같으니 말이야.\" 그러고는 마부들을 돌아보며 물었다. \"자네들은 왜 말들하고 마구간에 있지 않고?\" \"여기서 이 험한 끼니를 먹어야 한다면요, 마님,\" 그녀가 안주인에게 외쳤다. \"부디 부엌을 좀 비워 줬으면 좋겠어. 동네 잡것들에게 둘러싸이지 않게 말이야. 그쪽 양반으로 말하자면,\" 그녀가 파트리지에게 말했다. \"신사 비슷해 보이니 원하시면 그대로 앉아 계셔도 괜찮습니다. 내가 성가시게 하려는 건 천한 무리들뿐이니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, madam,”", "\"그럼요, 그렇고말고요, 마님,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["cries Partridge, “I am a gentleman, I do assure you, and I am not so easily to be disturbed.", "\"장담컨대 저는 신사올시다. 그리 쉽게 성가셔하는 사람이 아니지요."], ["Non semper vox casualis est verbo nominativus.”", "Non semper vox casualis est verbo nominativus.\""], ["This Latin she took to be some affront, and answered, “You may be a gentleman, sir; but you don't show yourself as one to talk Latin to a woman.”", "이 라틴어를 그녀는 무슨 모욕으로 여기고 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"신사이실지는 모르겠으나, 여자한테 라틴어나 늘어놓는 걸 보니 신사답게 보이지는 않는군요.\""], ["Partridge made a gentle reply, and concluded with more Latin; upon which she tossed up her nose, and contented herself by abusing him with the name of a great scholar.", "파트리지가 부드럽게 응수하면서 끝에 또 라틴어를 보탰다. 그러자 그녀는 코를 치켜올리고는, 대단한 학자 나리라고 비아냥거리는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["The supper being now on the table, Mrs Abigail eat very heartily for so delicate a person; and, while a second course of the same was by her order preparing, she said, “And so, madam, you tell me your house is frequented by people of great quality?”", "이제 저녁이 식탁에 올랐고, 애비게일 부인은 그토록 까다로운 사람치고는 아주 맛있게 먹어 치웠다. 그리고 자기 명에 따라 같은 음식을 한 차례 더 준비하는 동안 그녀가 말했다. \"그래, 마님, 그쪽 집에 아주 점잖은 분들이 자주 드나든다고 했지?\""]], [["The landlady answered in the affirmative, saying, “There were a great many very good quality and gentlefolks in it now.", "안주인은 그렇다고 답하며 말했다. \"지금도 아주 점잖은 분들하고 양반네가 여럿 묵고 계십지요."], ["There's young Squire Allworthy, as that gentleman there knows.”", "저 젊은 올워디 도련님도 계신데, 저기 저 양반이 잘 아실 겁니다.\""]], [["“And pray who is this young gentleman of quality, this young Squire Allworthy?”", "\"그래, 도대체 그 점잖은 젊은 양반, 그 젊은 올워디 도련님이라는 분은 누구죠?\""], ["said Abigail.", "애비게일이 말했다."]], [["“Who should he be,”", "\"누구긴 누굽니까,\""], ["answered Partridge, “but the son and heir of the great Squire Allworthy, of Somersetshire!”", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"서머싯셔의 그 훌륭하신 올워디 나리의 아들이자 상속자지요!\""]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"어머나, 정말이지,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “you tell me strange news; for I know Mr Allworthy of Somersetshire very well, and I know he hath no son alive.”", "\"이상한 소릴 다 하시네요. 나는 서머싯셔의 올워디 나리를 아주 잘 아는데, 살아 있는 아들이 없다는 것도 압니다.\""]], [["The landlady pricked up her ears at this, and Partridge looked a little confounded.", "이 말에 안주인은 귀를 쫑긋 세웠고, 파트리지는 다소 당황한 기색이었다. 그러나 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤 그가 대답했다."], ["However, after a short hesitation, he answered, “Indeed, madam, it is true, everybody doth not know him to be Squire Allworthy's son; for he was never married to his mother; but his son he certainly is, and will be his heir too, as certainly as his name is Jones.”", "\"사실 그렇습니다, 마님. 누구나 그가 올워디 나리의 아들인 줄 아는 건 아니지요."], ["At that word, Abigail let drop the bacon which she was conveying to her mouth, and cried out, “You surprize me, sir!", "나리가 그 어머니와 결혼한 적이 없으니 말입니다. 하지만 그가 나리의 아들인 건 분명하고, 그 이름이 존스인 것이 분명한 만큼이나 나리의 상속자가 될 것도 분명합니다.\""], ["Is it possible Mr Jones should be now in the house?”", "그 말에 애비게일은 입으로 가져가던 베이컨을 떨어뜨리며 외쳤다. \"놀라운 말이네요, 양반!"], ["“Quare non?”", "존스 씨가 지금 이 집에 있다는 게 정말이에요?\" \"Quare non?\""], ["answered Partridge, “it is possible, and it is certain.”", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"있을 수 있고말고요, 게다가 분명합니다.\""]], [["Abigail now made haste to finish the remainder of her meal, and then repaired back to her mistress, when the conversation passed which may be read in the next chapter.", "애비게일은 이제 서둘러 남은 식사를 마치고는 제 마님에게로 돌아갔다. 그때 오간 대화는 다음 장에서 읽을 수 있다."]], [["Showing who the amiable lady, and her unamiable maid, were.", "사랑스러운 여인과 사랑스럽지 못한 그 하녀가 누구였는지를 밝히는 장."]], [["As in the month of June, the damask rose, which chance hath planted among the lilies, with their candid hue mixes his vermilion; or as some playsome heifer in the pleasant month of May diffuses her odoriferous breath over the flowery meadows; or as, in the blooming month of April, the gentle, constant dove, perched on some fair bough, sits meditating on her mate; so, looking a hundred charms and breathing as many sweets, her thoughts being fixed on her Tommy, with a heart as good and innocent as her face was beautiful, Sophia (for it was she herself) lay reclining her lovely head on her hand, when her maid entered the room, and, running directly to the bed, cried, “Madam--madam--who doth your ladyship think is in the house?”", "6월이 되면, 우연이 백합 사이에 심어 놓은 다마스크 장미가 그 순결한 빛깔 속에 진홍빛을 섞어 넣듯, 또는 즐거운 5월에 장난기 어린 어린 암송아지가 꽃 핀 풀밭 위로 향기로운 숨결을 흩뿌리듯, 또는 꽃 피는 4월에 부드럽고 한결같은 비둘기가 어느 아름다운 가지에 앉아 제 짝을 생각하며 명상에 잠기듯, 백 가지 매력을 머금고 그만큼 많은 향기를 내뿜으며, 제 토미에게 온통 마음을 두고서, 얼굴이 아름다운 만큼이나 착하고 순결한 마음을 지닌 소피아가(바로 그녀 자신이었다) 사랑스러운 머리를 손에 기대고 누워 있을 때, 하녀가 방에 들어와 곧장 침대로 달려오더니 외쳤다. \"아씨, 아씨, 지금 이 집에 누가 와 있는지 아세요?\""], ["Sophia, starting up, cried, “I hope my father hath not overtaken us.”", "소피아가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"설마 아버지께서 우리를 따라잡으신 건 아니겠지.\""], ["“No, madam, it is one worth a hundred fathers; Mr Jones himself is here at this very instant.”", "\"아니에요, 아씨, 아버지 백 분보다도 더 귀한 분이에요. 바로 지금 이 순간, 존스 도련님이 여기 와 계세요.\""], ["“Mr Jones!”", "\"존스 씨라고!\""], ["says Sophia, “it is impossible!", "소피아가 말했다. \"그럴 리가 없어."], ["I cannot be so fortunate.”", "내게 그런 행운이 있을 리 없잖아.\""], ["Her maid averred the fact, and was presently detached by her mistress to order him to be called; for she said she was resolved to see him immediately.", "하녀는 사실이라고 단언했고, 곧 그녀의 주인은 하녀를 보내 그를 불러오게 했다. 당장 그를 만나기로 마음먹었다고 했기 때문이다."]], [["Mrs Honour had no sooner left the kitchen in the manner we have before seen than the landlady fell severely upon her.", "아너 부인이 앞서 우리가 본 그 모양새로 부엌을 나서자마자, 안주인은 그녀에게 호되게 퍼부었다."], ["The poor woman had indeed been loading her heart with foul language for some time, and now it scoured out of her mouth, as filth doth from a mud-cart, when the board which confines it is removed.", "사실 이 가엾은 여인은 한동안 가슴에 험한 말들을 잔뜩 쌓아 두고 있었던 터라, 이제 그것들이, 진흙 수레를 막아 두던 판자가 치워지면 오물이 쏟아져 나오듯 그녀의 입에서 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["Partridge likewise shovelled in his share of calumny, and (what may surprize the reader) not only bespattered the maid, but attempted to sully the lily-white character of Sophia herself.", "파트리지 역시 자기 몫의 비방을 한 삽 거들었는데, (독자를 놀라게 할 만하게도) 하녀만 더럽힌 것이 아니라 소피아 자신의 백합처럼 흰 인품마저 더럽히려 들었다."], ["“Never a barrel the better herring,”", "\"그 통에 그 청어지, 더 나을 게 어디 있나.\""], ["cries he, “Noscitur a socio, is a true saying.", "그가 외쳤다. \"같이 어울리는 자를 보면 그자를 안다(Noscitur a socio)는 말이 참말이야."], ["It must be confessed, indeed, that the lady in the fine garments is the civiller of the two; but I warrant neither of them are a bit better than they should be.", "하긴, 고운 옷 입은 여자 쪽이 둘 중에선 더 점잖다고는 인정해야겠지. 하지만 장담컨대 둘 다 제 처지에 걸맞은 것보다 나을 것이 하나도 없어."], ["A couple of Bath trulls, I'll answer for them; your quality don't ride about at this time o'", "바스에서 굴러온 갈보 한 쌍이라고 내가 책임지지. 점잖은 분들은 이런 야밤에 하인도 없이 말 타고 돌아다니지 않거든.\""], ["night without servants.”", "\"에라, 그것 참 맞는 말이네.\""], ["“Sbodlikins, and that's true,”", "안주인이 외쳤다. \"당신 정말 정곡을 찔렀구먼."], ["cries the landlady, “you have certainly hit upon the very matter; for quality don't come into a house without bespeaking a supper, whether they eat or no.”", "점잖은 분들이라면 먹든 안 먹든 저녁부터 시켜 놓지 않고는 집에 들어서는 법이 없지.\" * Noscitur a socio: \"어울리는 자를 보면 그 사람을 안다\"는 라틴어 격언."]], [["While they were thus discoursing, Mrs Honour returned and discharged her commission, by bidding the landlady immediately wake Mr Jones, and tell him a lady wanted to speak with him.", "이렇게들 이야기를 나누고 있는데, 아너 부인이 돌아와 자신의 임무를 전했다. 안주인에게 당장 존스 씨를 깨워, 한 숙녀가 그와 이야기하고 싶어 한다고 전하라는 것이었다."], ["The landlady referred her to Partridge, saying, “he was the squire's friend: but, for her part, she never called men-folks, especially gentlemen,”", "안주인은 그녀를 파트리지에게 떠넘기며 말했다. \"저 사람이 도련님 친구잖수."], ["and then walked sullenly out of the kitchen.", "나로 말하자면 사내들, 특히 신사 양반들을 부르러 가는 일은 해 본 적이 없으니.\" 그러고는 부루퉁하게 부엌을 걸어 나갔다."], ["Honour applied herself to Partridge; but he refused, “for my friend,”", "아너는 파트리지에게 매달렸지만, 그는 거절했다. \"내 친구는,\""], ["cries he, “went to bed very late, and he would be very angry to be disturbed so soon.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"아주 늦게 잠자리에 들었는데, 이렇게 일찍 깨우면 몹시 화를 낼 거요.\""], ["Mrs Honour insisted still to have him called, saying, “she was sure, instead of being angry, that he would be to the highest degree delighted when he knew the occasion.”", "아너 부인은 그래도 그를 불러야 한다고 우기며 말했다. \"장담컨대, 도련님은 화를 내기는커녕, 무슨 일인지 알면 더없이 기뻐하실 거예요.\""], ["“Another time, perhaps, he might,”", "\"다른 때라면 모를까,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["cries Partridge; “but non omnia possumus omnes.", "\"하지만 우리가 모든 걸 다 할 수는 없는 법(non omnia possumus omnes)이지. 분별 있는 사내한테는 한 번에 여자 하나면 족하거든.\""], ["One woman is enough at once for a reasonable man.”", "\"여자 하나라니, 무슨 소리예요, 이 사람아?\" 아너가 외쳤다."], ["“What do you mean by one woman, fellow?”", "\"'이 사람아'라니, 함부로 그러지 마시오.\""], ["cries Honour.", "파트리지가 대꾸했다."], ["“None of your fellow,” answered Partridge.", "그러고는 존스가 어떤 계집과 한 침대에 누워 있다고 대놓고 일러 주며, 여기 옮기기엔 너무 상스러운 표현까지 써 댔다."], ["He then proceeded to inform her plainly that Jones was in bed with a wench, and made use of an expression too indelicate to be here inserted; which so enraged Mrs Honour, that she called him jackanapes, and returned in a violent hurry to her mistress, whom she acquainted with the success of her errand, and with the account she had received; which, if possible, she exaggerated, being as angry with Jones as if he had pronounced all the words that came from the mouth of Partridge.", "이에 아너 부인은 어찌나 격분했는지 그를 건방진 원숭이라고 불러 대고는, 잔뜩 성이 나 부랴부랴 주인에게 돌아갔다. 그러고는 심부름의 결과와 들은 이야기를 전했는데, 가능한 한 부풀려 전했으니, 마치 파트리지의 입에서 나온 그 모든 말을 존스가 직접 내뱉기라도 한 양 그에게 화가 나 있었던 것이다."], ["She discharged a torrent of abuse on the master, and advised her mistress to quit all thoughts of a man who had never shown himself deserving of her.", "그녀는 그 주인에게 욕설을 폭포처럼 쏟아부었고, 단 한 번도 그녀에게 걸맞은 모습을 보인 적 없는 사내에 대한 생각일랑 모두 접으시라고 주인에게 권했다. 그러고는 몰리 시그림의 이야기를 들춰내고, 그가 예전에 소피아 자신을 떠났던 일에도 더없이 악의적인 해석을 갖다 붙였다."], ["She then ripped up the story of Molly Seagrim, and gave the most malicious turn to his formerly quitting Sophia herself; which, I must confess, the present incident not a little countenanced.", "솔직히 고백하건대, 지금의 이 사건이 그런 해석을 적잖이 거들어 준 셈이었다. * non omnia possumus omnes: \"우리 모두가 모든 것을 다 할 수는 없다\"는 라틴어 구절."]], [["The spirits of Sophia were too much dissipated by concern to enable her to stop the torrent of her maid.", "소피아는 근심으로 마음이 너무나 흩어진 나머지 하녀의 폭포 같은 말을 막을 수가 없었다. 그러나 마침내 그녀는 하녀의 말을 끊으며 말했다."], ["At last, however, she interrupted her, saying, “I never can believe this; some villain hath belied him.", "\"도무지 믿을 수가 없어. 어떤 악당이 그를 모함한 거야."], ["You say you had it from his friend; but surely it is not the office of a friend to betray such secrets.”", "네 말로는 그의 친구한테서 들었다지만, 그런 비밀을 발설하는 게 친구가 할 일은 아니잖니.\" \"제 생각엔,\""], ["“I suppose,”", "아너가 외쳤다."], ["cries Honour, “the fellow is his pimp; for I never saw so ill-looked a villain.", "\"그 사람이 도련님의 뚜쟁이일 거예요. 그렇게 흉악하게 생긴 악당은 처음 봤다니까요."], ["Besides, such profligate rakes as Mr Jones are never ashamed of these matters.”", "게다가 존스 씨 같은 방탕한 난봉꾼들은 그런 일을 부끄러워하는 법이 없거든요.\""]], [["To say the truth, this behaviour of Partridge was a little inexcusable; but he had not slept off the effect of the dose which he swallowed the evening before; which had, in the morning, received the addition of above a pint of wine, or indeed rather of malt spirits; for the perry was by no means pure.", "사실대로 말하자면, 파트리지의 이 행동은 다소 변명의 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그는 전날 저녁에 들이켠 술기운을 아직 다 자고 떨쳐 내지 못한 상태였고, 거기에 아침이 되어 와인을, 아니 실은 맥아주를 한 파인트 넘게 더 보탠 터였다."], ["Now, that part of his head which Nature designed for the reservoir of drink being very shallow, a small quantity of liquor overflowed it, and opened the sluices of his heart; so that all the secrets there deposited run out.", "그 배 술이라는 것도 결코 순수한 것이 아니었으니 말이다. 그런데 자연이 술을 담아 두라고 마련해 준 그의 머릿속 저수지가 워낙 얕은지라, 조금만 술이 들어가도 넘쳐흘러 그의 마음의 수문을 열어 버렸고, 그리하여 거기 담겨 있던 온갖 비밀이 죄다 흘러나오고 말았다."], ["These sluices were indeed, naturally, very ill-secured.", "사실 이 수문이라는 것은 본디 단속이 아주 허술하게 되어 있었다."], ["To give the best-natured turn we can to his disposition, he was a very honest man; for, as he was the most inquisitive of mortals, and eternally prying into the secrets of others, so he very faithfully paid them by communicating, in return, everything within his knowledge.", "그의 기질을 최대한 좋게 해석해 보자면, 그는 매우 정직한 사람이었다. 그는 인간 중에서도 가장 캐묻기 좋아하여 늘 남의 비밀을 들쑤시고 다녔는데, 그 대가로 자기가 아는 것은 무엇이든 죄다 알려 줌으로써 아주 충실하게 빚을 갚았기 때문이다."]], [["While Sophia, tormented with anxiety, knew not what to believe, nor what resolution to take, Susan arrived with the sack-whey.", "소피아가 불안에 시달리며 무엇을 믿어야 할지, 어떤 결단을 내려야 할지 갈피를 못 잡고 있을 때, 수전이 색 휘저음술을 가지고 도착했다. 아너 부인은 즉시 주인에게 귀엣말로, 이 계집을 떠보면 아마 진실을 알아낼 수 있을 거라고 권했다."], ["Mrs Honour immediately advised her mistress, in a whisper, to pump this wench, who probably could inform her of the truth.", "소피아는 그것이 좋겠다 싶어 다음과 같이 말문을 열었다. \"이리 와 보렴, 얘야."], ["Sophia approved it, and began as follows: “Come hither, child; now answer me truly what I am going to ask you, and I promise you I will very well reward you.", "지금 내가 묻는 말에 사실대로 답해 주면, 약속하건대 후하게 보답하마. 이 집에 젊은 신사 한 분, 잘생긴 젊은 신사 한 분이 계시는데, 혹시…….\""], ["Is there a young gentleman in this house, a handsome young gentleman, that----.”", "여기서 소피아는 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 잇지 못했다. \"젊은 신사분 말이다,\""], ["Here Sophia blushed and was confounded.", "아너가 외쳤다. \"지금 부엌에 있는 저 건방진 악당이랑 같이 이 집에 들어온 그 젊은 신사 분 말이야.\""], ["“A young gentleman,”", "수전이 답했다. \"예, 계셨어요.\""], ["cries Honour, “that came hither in company with that saucy rascal who is now in the kitchen?”", "\"그 어떤 부인에 대해서는 아는 게 없니?\" 소피아가 계속 물었다."], ["Susan answered, “There was.”--“Do you know anything of any lady?”", "\"어떤 부인 말이다. 그 여자가 예쁜지 아닌지를 묻는 게 아니야."], ["continues Sophia, “any lady?", "어쩌면 안 예쁠 수도 있겠지."], ["I don't ask you whether she is handsome or no; perhaps she is not; that's nothing to the purpose; but do you know of any lady?”", "그건 중요한 게 아니야. 하지만 혹시 어떤 부인에 대해 아는 게 있느냐 말이다.\""], ["“La, madam,”", "\"아이고, 아씨,\""], ["cries Honour, “you will make a very bad examiner.", "아너가 외쳤다. \"아씨는 심문을 영 못하시겠네요."], ["Hark'ee, child,”", "얘야, 들어 봐라,\""], ["says she, “is not that very young gentleman now in bed with some nasty trull or other?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"바로 그 젊은 신사가 지금 어떤 더러운 갈보랑 한 침대에 누워 있지 않니?\""], ["Here Susan smiled, and was silent.", "여기서 수전은 빙긋 웃기만 할 뿐 입을 다물었다."], ["“Answer the question, child,”", "\"물음에 답하렴, 얘야,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["says Sophia, “and here's a guinea for you.”--“A guinea!", "\"자, 너한테 줄 1기니다.\" \"1기니라고요, 아씨!\""], ["madam,”", "수전이 외쳤다."], ["cries Susan; “la, what's a guinea?", "\"아이고, 1기니가 뭐 대수라고요."], ["If my mistress should know it I shall certainly lose my place that very instant.”", "주인마님이 아시기라도 하면 저는 그 자리에서 당장 일자리를 잃고 말걸요.\""], ["“Here's another for you,”", "\"여기 하나 더 주마,\""], ["says Sophia, “and I promise you faithfully your mistress shall never know it.”", "소피아가 말했다. \"그리고 약속하건대, 네 주인마님은 절대 모를 거야.\""], ["Susan, after a very short hesitation, took the money, and told the whole story, concluding with saying, “If you have any great curiosity, madam, I can steal softly into his room, and see whether he be in his own bed or no.”", "수전은 아주 잠깐 망설인 뒤 돈을 받아들고는 자초지종을 모두 이야기했고, 끝에 가서 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"정 궁금하시다면, 아씨, 제가 살그머니 그분 방에 들어가서 자기 침대에 있는지 없는지 보고 올게요.\""], ["She accordingly did this by Sophia's desire, and returned with an answer in the negative.", "그래서 그녀는 소피아의 청대로 그렇게 했고, 없다는 대답을 가지고 돌아왔다."]], [["Sophia now trembled and turned pale.", "이제 소피아는 부들부들 떨며 얼굴이 창백해졌다."], ["Mrs Honour begged her to be comforted, and not to think any more of so worthless a fellow.", "아너 부인은 부디 마음을 추스르시고, 그런 못난 사내일랑 더는 생각지 마시라고 간청했다. \"저, 아씨,\""], ["“Why there,”", "수전이 말했다."], ["says Susan, “I hope, madam, your ladyship won't be offended; but pray, madam, is not your ladyship's name Madam Sophia Western?”", "\"아씨께서 노여워하지 않으셨으면 좋겠는데, 외람되지만 아씨, 혹시 아씨 성함이 소피아 웨스턴 아씨 아니신가요?\" \"네가 어떻게 나를 안단 말이냐?\""], ["“How is it possible you should know me?”", "소피아가 대답했다. \"저, 부엌에 있는 그 사람, 저 여자분이 말한 그 사람이요, 어젯밤에 아씨에 대해 떠들어 댔거든요."], ["answered Sophia.", "그렇지만 아씨, 제게 화는 안 내셨으면 해요.\" \"정말이지, 얘야,\""], ["“Why that man, that the gentlewoman spoke of, who is in the kitchen, told about you last night.", "그녀가 말했다. \"화 안 났단다."], ["But I hope your ladyship is not angry with me.”", "부디 다 말해 주렴. 약속하건대 보답하마.\""], ["“Indeed, child,”", "\"그러니까요, 아씨,\" 수전이 계속했다."], ["said she, “I am not; pray tell me all, and I promise you I'll reward you.”", "\"그 사람이 부엌에서 우리한테 죄다 떠들기를, 소피아 웨스턴 아씨께서…… 아이고, 정말 어떻게 입에 올려야 할지 모르겠네요.\""], ["“Why, madam,”", "여기서 그녀는 말을 멈췄다가, 소피아에게서 용기를 얻고 아너 부인에게서 거세게 재촉을 받자, 이렇게 이어 갔다. \"그 사람이 그러더라고요, 아씨."], ["continued Susan, “that man told us all in the kitchen that Madam Sophia Western--indeed I don't know how to bring it out.”--Here she stopt, till, having received encouragement from Sophia, and being vehemently pressed by Mrs Honour, she proceeded thus:--“He told us, madam, though to be sure it is all a lie, that your ladyship was dying for love of the young squire, and that he was going to the wars to get rid of you.", "물론 죄다 거짓말이겠지만요. 아씨께서 그 젊은 도련님을 사랑하다 못해 죽을 지경이 되셨고, 그래서 도련님은 아씨를 떨쳐 버리려고 전쟁터에 나가는 거라고요."], ["I thought to myself then he was a false-hearted wretch; but, now, to see such a fine, rich, beautiful lady as you be, forsaken for such an ordinary woman; for to be sure so she is, and another man's wife into the bargain.", "그때 저는 속으로, 참 마음씨 고약한 못난이로구나 했지요. 그런데 이제 아씨처럼 이렇게 곱고 부유하고 아름다운 분이 저런 평범한 여자 때문에 버림받았다니요."], ["It is such a strange unnatural thing, in a manner.”", "그게 정말 그렇거든요, 게다가 남의 아내인 여자고요. 어쩐지 참 이상하고 자연스럽지 못한 일이지 뭐예요.\""]], [["Sophia gave her a third guinea, and, telling her she would certainly be her friend if she mentioned nothing of what had passed, nor informed any one who she was, dismissed the girl, with orders to the post-boy to get the horses ready immediately.", "소피아는 그녀에게 세 번째 기니를 주고는, 지금 있었던 일을 아무에게도 말하지 않고 자신이 누구인지도 알리지 않는다면 반드시 그녀의 편이 되어 주겠노라고 말한 뒤, 그 아이를 내보냈다. 그러면서 마부 소년에게 당장 말을 준비시키라고 일러 두었다."]], [["Being now left alone with her maid, she told her trusty waiting-woman, “That she never was more easy than at present.", "이제 하녀와 단둘이 남게 되자, 그녀는 믿음직한 시녀에게 말했다. \"지금보다 더 마음이 편했던 적이 없구나."], ["I am now convinced,”", "이제 똑똑히 알겠어.\""], ["said she, “he is not only a villain, but a low despicable wretch.", "그녀가 말했다. \"그 사람은 악당일 뿐만 아니라, 비열하고 천박한 못난이야."], ["I can forgive all rather than his exposing my name in so barbarous a manner.", "다른 건 다 용서할 수 있어도, 내 이름을 그토록 야만스럽게 입에 올린 것만은 못 참겠어."], ["That renders him the object of my contempt.", "그 때문에 그는 내 경멸의 대상이 되었어."], ["Yes, Honour, I am now easy; I am indeed; I am very easy;”", "그래, 아너, 이제 마음이 편해. 정말이지 편안하다니까."], ["and then she burst into a violent flood of tears.", "아주 편해.\" 그러더니 그녀는 격렬한 눈물을 왈칵 쏟아 냈다."]], [["After a short interval spent by Sophia, chiefly in crying, and assuring her maid that she was perfectly easy, Susan arrived with an account that the horses were ready, when a very extraordinary thought suggested itself to our young heroine, by which Mr Jones would be acquainted with her having been at the inn, in a way which, if any sparks of affection for her remained in him, would be at least some punishment for his faults.", "소피아가 주로 울면서, 그리고 자기는 더없이 마음이 편하다고 하녀에게 거듭 다짐하면서 잠깐의 시간을 보낸 뒤, 수전이 말이 준비되었다는 소식을 가지고 왔다. 그때 우리의 젊은 여주인공에게 아주 기발한 생각 하나가 떠올랐는데, 그 방법대로라면 존스 씨는 그녀가 그 여관에 다녀갔다는 사실을 알게 될 터였고, 만일 그에게 그녀를 향한 애정의 불씨가 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 그것이 적어도 그의 잘못에 대한 얼마간의 벌은 되어 줄 것이었다."]], [["The reader will be pleased to remember a little muff, which hath had the honour of being more than once remembered already in this history.", "독자께서는 이 이야기에서 이미 한 번 이상 언급되는 영광을 누렸던 작은 토시 하나를 기억해 주시면 좋겠다. 이 토시는 존스 씨가 떠난 이후로 낮에는 소피아의 변함없는 동무였고 밤에는 잠자리의 동무였으며, 바로 지금 이 순간에도 그녀의 팔에 끼워져 있었다."], ["This muff, ever since the departure of Mr Jones, had been the constant companion of Sophia by day, and her bedfellow by night; and this muff she had at this very instant upon her arm; whence she took it off with great indignation, and, having writ her name with her pencil upon a piece of paper which she pinned to it, she bribed the maid to convey it into the empty bed of Mr Jones, in which, if he did not find it, she charged her to take some method of conveying it before his eyes in the morning.", "그녀는 잔뜩 분이 나서 그것을 팔에서 빼더니, 연필로 종이 한 장에 자기 이름을 적어 그 토시에 핀으로 꽂은 뒤, 하녀를 매수해 그것을 존스 씨의 빈 침대에 갖다 두게 했다. 그리고 만일 그가 거기서 그것을 찾지 못한다면, 아침에 어떻게든 그의 눈에 띄게 할 방도를 마련하라고 단단히 일러 두었다."]], [["Then, having paid for what Mrs Honour had eaten, in which bill was included an account for what she herself might have eaten, she mounted her horse, and, once more assuring her companion that she was perfectly easy, continued her journey.", "그런 다음, 아너 부인이 먹은 것에 대한 셈을 치렀는데, 그 계산서에는 그녀 자신이 먹었을 법한 몫에 대한 값까지 포함되어 있었다. 그러고는 말에 올라, 자기는 더없이 마음이 편하다고 동행에게 다시 한번 다짐하면서 여정을 이어 갔다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["Containing, among other things, the ingenuity of Partridge, the madness of Jones, and the folly of Fitzpatrick.", "여러 가지 가운데서도, 파트리지의 잔꾀와 존스의 광기, 그리고 피츠패트릭의 어리석음을 담은 이야기."]], [["It was now past five in the morning, and other company began to rise and come to the kitchen, among whom were the serjeant and the coachman, who, being thoroughly reconciled, made a libation, or, in the English phrase, drank a hearty cup together.", "때는 어느덧 새벽 다섯 시가 넘어 있었고, 다른 손님들도 하나둘 일어나 부엌으로 모여들기 시작했다. 그 가운데에는 그 하사관과 마부도 있었는데, 두 사람은 이제 완전히 화해한 터라 함께 헌주를, 영국식으로 말하자면 한 잔 거나하게 들이켰다."]], [["In this drinking nothing more remarkable happened than the behaviour of Partridge, who, when the serjeant drank a health to King George, repeated only the word King; nor could he be brought to utter more; for though he was going to fight against his own cause, yet he could not be prevailed upon to drink against it.", "이 술자리에서 파트리지의 행동만큼 눈에 띄는 일은 없었다. 하사관이 조지 왕의 만수무강을 위해 잔을 들었을 때, 파트리지는 오직 \"왕\"이라는 한마디만 따라 했을 뿐, 그 이상은 도무지 입 밖에 내려 하지 않았다. 비록 자기 신념과는 반대편에 서서 싸우러 가는 길이었지만, 그렇다고 제 신념을 거스르며 잔을 들이켤 수는 없었던 것이다."]], [["Mr Jones, being now returned to his own bed (but from whence he returned we must beg to be excused from relating), summoned Partridge from this agreeable company, who, after a ceremonious preface, having obtained leave to offer his advice, delivered himself as follows:--", "이제 자기 침대로 돌아온 존스 씨는—다만 그가 어디서 돌아왔는지에 대해서는 굳이 밝히지 않기를 양해해 주기 바란다—이 흥겨운 자리에서 파트리지를 불러올렸다. 파트리지는 격식을 차린 서두를 한참 늘어놓은 끝에 조언을 올려도 좋다는 허락을 받고는 이렇게 말문을 열었다."]], [["“It is, sir, an old saying, and a true one, that a wise man may sometimes learn counsel from a fool; I wish, therefore, I might be so bold as to offer you my advice, which is to return home again, and leave these horrida bella, these bloody wars, to fellows who are contented to swallow gunpowder, because they have nothing else to eat. Now, everybody knows your honour wants for nothing at home; when that's the case, why should any man travel abroad?”", "\"나리, 옛말에, 그리고 참으로 옳은 말에, 현명한 사람도 때로는 어리석은 자에게서 충고를 얻는다는 말이 있습죠. 그러니 외람되지만 제 조언을 한 말씀 올릴까 합니다. 다름이 아니라, 다시 집으로 돌아가시고, 이 '호리다 벨라(horrida bella)', 이 피비린내 나는 전쟁일랑은 달리 먹을 게 없어서 화약이라도 삼키는 걸로 만족하는 자들에게 맡기시라는 겁니다. 자, 나리께서는 집에 계시면 아쉬울 게 하나도 없다는 걸 누구나 다 압니다. 그런 형편인데, 사람이 어찌하여 객지로 떠돌아야 한답니까?\" * 호리다 벨라: 라틴어로 \"끔찍한 전쟁들\"이라는 뜻."]], [["“Partridge,”", "\"파트리지,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “thou art certainly a coward; I wish, therefore, thou wouldst return home thyself, and trouble me no more.”", "\"너는 확실히 겁쟁이로구나. 그러니 너야말로 집으로 돌아가서 나를 더는 성가시게 하지 말았으면 좋겠다.\""]], [["“I ask your honour's pardon,”", "\"나리, 송구하옵니다.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["cries Partridge; “I spoke on your account more than my own; for as to me, Heaven knows my circumstances are bad enough, and I am so far from being afraid, that I value a pistol, or a blunderbuss, or any such thing, no more than a pop-gun.", "\"저는 제 일보다 나리 일을 더 생각해서 드린 말씀입니다. 제 형편으로 말하자면, 하늘도 아시다시피 워낙 딱한 처지라, 무서워하기는커녕 권총이든 나팔총이든 그런 것들을 장난감 딱총만큼도 대수롭게 여기지 않습니다."], ["Every man must die once, and what signifies the manner how?", "사람은 누구나 한 번은 죽는 법, 그 방식이 무슨 대수겠습니까?"], ["besides, perhaps I may come off with the loss only of an arm or a leg.", "게다가 운이 좋으면 팔 하나나 다리 하나쯤 잃는 것으로 끝날지도 모르고요."], ["I assure you, sir, I was never less afraid in my life; and so, if your honour is resolved to go on, I am resolved to follow you.", "정말이지 나리, 제 평생 이토록 겁이 안 났던 적이 없습니다. 그러니 나리께서 기어이 가시겠다면, 저도 기어이 따라가겠습니다."], ["But, in that case, I wish I might give my opinion.", "다만 그렇다면 제 의견을 하나 올렸으면 합니다."], ["To be sure, it is a scandalous way of travelling, for a great gentleman like you to walk afoot.", "나리처럼 훌륭하신 분이 걸어서 길을 가시는 건 정말이지 망신스러운 여행 방식입니다. 그런데 마침 마구간에 쓸 만한 말이 두세 필 있는데, 주인장이 나리께 빌려주는 데 분명 거리낌이 없을 겁니다."], ["Now here are two or three good horses in the stable, which the landlord will certainly make no scruple of trusting you with; but, if he should, I can easily contrive to take them; and, let the worst come to the worst, the king would certainly pardon you, as you are going to fight in his cause.”", "혹시라도 거리낀다면, 제가 쉽게 손을 써서 끌어낼 수도 있고요. 게다가 최악의 경우라 해도, 나리께서는 왕의 대의를 위해 싸우러 가시는 길이니 왕께서 틀림없이 사면해 주실 겁니다.\""]], [["Now, as the honesty of Partridge was equal to his understanding, and both dealt only in small matters, he would never have attempted a roguery of this kind, had he not imagined it altogether safe; for he was one of those who have more consideration of the gallows than of the fitness of things; but, in reality, he thought he might have committed this felony without any danger; for, besides that he doubted not but the name of Mr Allworthy would sufficiently quiet the landlord, he conceived they should be altogether safe, whatever turn affairs might take; as Jones, he imagined, would have friends enough on one side, and as his friends would as well secure him on the other.", "그런데 파트리지의 정직함은 그의 분별력과 엇비슷한 수준이었고, 둘 다 사소한 일에서나 발휘되는 것이었던지라, 그는 이런 도둑질을 완전히 안전하다고 여기지 않았다면 결코 시도하지 않았을 것이다. 그는 사물의 옳고 그름보다 교수대를 더 마음에 두는 부류의 사람이었으니 말이다. 하지만 실은 그는 이 중죄를 아무런 위험 없이 저지를 수 있으리라 믿었다. 올워디 씨의 이름을 대면 주인장을 충분히 잠재울 수 있으리라는 확신은 물론이거니와, 일이 어느 쪽으로 흘러가든 자기들은 안전하리라 여겼던 것이다. 그의 생각으로는, 한쪽으로는 존스에게 든든한 벗들이 있을 테고, 또 다른 쪽으로는 자기 벗들이 자신을 지켜 줄 테니 말이다."]], [["When Mr Jones found that Partridge was in earnest in this proposal, he very severely rebuked him, and that in such bitter terms, that the other attempted to laugh it off, and presently turned the discourse to other matters; saying, he believed they were then in a bawdy house, and that he had with much ado prevented two wenches from disturbing his honour in the middle of the night.", "존스 씨는 파트리지가 이 제안을 진심으로 하고 있다는 것을 알아채자 그를 호되게 꾸짖었는데, 그 말투가 어찌나 신랄했던지, 파트리지는 웃어넘기려 하면서 이내 화제를 다른 데로 돌렸다. 그는 이곳이 아무래도 매음굴인 모양이라며, 한밤중에 두 계집이 나리를 방해하지 못하도록 자기가 진땀을 빼며 막았노라 했다."], ["“Heyday!”", "\"이런 참!\""], ["says he, “I believe they got into your chamber whether I would or no; for here lies the muff of one of them on the ground.”", "그가 말했다. \"제가 막든 말든 기어이 나리 방에 들어갔던 모양입니다."], ["Indeed, as Jones returned to his bed in the dark, he had never perceived the muff on the quilt, and, in leaping into his bed, he had tumbled it on the floor.", "보십쇼, 여기 그중 한 계집의 토시가 바닥에 떨어져 있잖습니까.\" 사실 존스는 어둠 속에서 침대로 돌아오느라 이불 위에 놓인 토시를 미처 보지 못했고, 침대로 뛰어들면서 그것을 바닥으로 떨어뜨린 것이었다."], ["This Partridge now took up, and was going to put into his pocket, when Jones desired to see it.", "파트리지가 이제 그 토시를 집어 들어 제 주머니에 넣으려는데, 존스가 그것을 좀 보자고 했다."], ["The muff was so very remarkable, that our heroe might possibly have recollected it without the information annexed.", "그 토시는 워낙 눈에 띄는 물건이어서, 우리 주인공은 거기 달린 정보가 없었더라도 어쩌면 기억해 냈을지 모른다."], ["But his memory was not put to that hard office; for at the same instant he saw and read the words Sophia Western upon the paper which was pinned to it.", "하지만 그의 기억력이 그런 힘든 일을 감당할 필요는 없었다. 바로 그 순간 그는 토시에 핀으로 꽂힌 종이 위에서 \"소피아 웨스턴\"이라는 글자를 보고 읽었기 때문이다."], ["His looks now grew frantic in a moment, and he eagerly cried out, “Oh Heavens!", "그의 표정은 순식간에 광란에 휩싸였고, 그는 다급하게 외쳤다. \"오, 맙소사!"], ["how came this muff here?”", "이 토시가 어떻게 여기 있단 말이냐?\""], ["“I know no more than your honour,”", "\"저도 나리만큼밖에 모릅니다.\""], ["cried Partridge; “but I saw it upon the arm of one of the women who would have disturbed you, if I would have suffered them.”", "파트리지가 외쳤다. \"다만 제가 막지 않았더라면 나리를 방해했을 그 여인 중 하나의 팔에 끼워져 있던 걸 봤을 뿐입니다.\""], ["“Where are they?”", "\"그들은 어디 있느냐?\""], ["cries Jones, jumping out of bed, and laying hold of his cloaths.", "존스가 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 옷을 움켜쥐며 외쳤다."], ["“Many miles off, I believe, by this time,”", "\"지금쯤이면 몇 마일은 떨어진 곳에 있을 겁니다.\""], ["said Partridge.", "파트리지가 말했다."], ["And now Jones, upon further enquiry, was sufficiently assured that the bearer of this muff was no other than the lovely Sophia herself.", "그리고 이제 존스는 더 캐물은 끝에, 이 토시의 주인이 다름 아닌 사랑스러운 소피아 본인이라는 것을 충분히 확신하게 되었다."]], [["The behaviour of Jones on this occasion, his thoughts, his looks, his words, his actions, were such as beggar all description.", "이 일을 두고 존스가 보인 행동, 그의 생각, 그의 표정, 그의 말, 그의 몸짓은 무어라 형용할 길이 없는 것이었다."], ["After many bitter execrations on Partridge, and not fewer on himself, he ordered the poor fellow, who was frightened out of his wits, to run down and hire him horses at any rate; and a very few minutes afterwards, having shuffled on his clothes, he hastened down-stairs to execute the orders himself, which he had just before given.", "파트리지를 향해 쓰디쓴 저주를 숱하게 퍼붓고, 제 자신에게도 그에 못지않게 욕설을 쏟아 낸 끝에, 그는 혼비백산한 가엾은 사내에게 어서 아래층으로 내려가 값이 얼마가 들든 말을 빌려 오라고 명령했다. 그러고는 불과 몇 분 후, 부랴부랴 옷을 걸치고 방금 자신이 내린 그 명령을 직접 실행하려고 황급히 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["But before we proceed to what passed on his arrival in the kitchen, it will be necessary to recur to what had there happened since Partridge had first left it on his master's summons.", "그러나 그가 부엌에 도착했을 때 벌어진 일로 넘어가기 전에, 파트리지가 주인의 부름을 받고 그곳을 처음 떠난 이후 그 사이에 일어난 일들을 먼저 되짚어 볼 필요가 있겠다."]], [["The serjeant was just marched off with his party, when the two Irish gentlemen arose, and came downstairs; both complaining that they had been so often waked by the noises in the inn, that they had never once been able to close their eyes all night.", "하사관이 막 자기 무리를 거느리고 떠나자, 두 아일랜드 신사가 일어나 아래층으로 내려왔다. 둘 다 여관 안의 소란에 어찌나 자주 깼던지 밤새 한 번도 눈을 붙이지 못했다며 투덜거렸다."]], [["The coach which had brought the young lady and her maid, and which, perhaps, the reader may have hitherto concluded was her own, was, indeed, a returned coach belonging to Mr King, of Bath, one of the worthiest and honestest men that ever dealt in horse-flesh, and whose coaches we heartily recommend to all our readers who travel that road.", "그 젊은 숙녀와 하녀를 태우고 온 마차는—어쩌면 독자는 지금까지 그것을 그녀 소유의 마차라고 여겼을지도 모르지만—실은 바스의 킹 씨에게 속한 회송용 삯마차였다. 킹 씨로 말하자면 말 거래에 종사한 사람 중에서도 가장 훌륭하고 정직한 이로 손꼽히는 분이었으니, 그의 마차를 그 길로 여행하는 모든 독자에게 진심으로 추천하는 바이다."], ["By which means they may, perhaps, have the pleasure of riding in the very coach, and being driven by the very coachman, that is recorded in this history.", "그렇게 하면 독자도 어쩌면 이 이야기에 기록된 바로 그 마차를 타고, 바로 그 마부의 모는 솜씨를 누리는 즐거움을 얻게 될지 모른다."]], [["The coachman, having but two passengers, and hearing Mr Maclachlan was going to Bath, offered to carry him thither at a very moderate price.", "마부는 승객이 둘밖에 없는 데다 매클라클런 씨가 바스로 갈 참이라는 말을 듣고는, 아주 적당한 값에 그를 그곳까지 태워다 주겠노라 제안했다. 그가 이런 제안을 한 데에는 마구간지기의 말이 한몫했다."], ["He was induced to this by the report of the hostler, who said that the horse which Mr Maclachlan had hired from Worcester would be much more pleased with returning to his friends there than to prosecute a long journey; for that the said horse was rather a two-legged than a four-legged animal.", "마구간지기 말이, 매클라클런 씨가 우스터에서 빌린 말은 긴 여정을 이어 가기보다는 거기 제 동무들에게 돌아가는 편을 훨씬 반길 거라는 것이었다. 그 말이라는 게 네 발 달린 짐승이라기보다는 두 발 달린 짐승에 가깝다나."]], [["Mr Maclachlan immediately closed with the proposal of the coachman, and, at the same time, persuaded his friend Fitzpatrick to accept of the fourth place in the coach.", "매클라클런 씨는 즉시 마부의 제안에 응했고, 동시에 자기 벗 피츠패트릭을 설득해 마차의 남은 한 자리를 받아들이게 했다."], ["This conveyance the soreness of his bones made more agreeable to him than a horse; and, being well assured of meeting with his wife at Bath, he thought a little delay would be of no consequence.", "온몸의 뼈마디가 쑤셨던 터라 피츠패트릭에게는 이 이동 수단이 말보다 한결 반가웠다. 게다가 바스에서 아내를 틀림없이 만나리라 확신하고 있었으므로, 조금쯤 늦어진들 대수롭지 않으리라 생각했다."]], [["Maclachlan, who was much the sharper man of the two, no sooner heard that this lady came from Chester, with the other circumstances which he learned from the hostler, than it came into his head that she might possibly be his friend's wife; and presently acquainted him with this suspicion, which had never once occurred to Fitzpatrick himself.", "둘 중 훨씬 영리한 매클라클런은 이 숙녀가 체스터에서 왔다는 말과 더불어 마구간지기에게서 들은 다른 정황들을 듣자마자, 그녀가 어쩌면 제 벗의 아내일지도 모른다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐다. 그리고 이내 이 의심을 피츠패트릭에게 알렸는데, 정작 피츠패트릭 자신은 단 한 번도 그런 생각을 해 본 적이 없었다."], ["To say the truth, he was one of those compositions which nature makes up in too great a hurry, and forgets to put any brains into their head.", "사실대로 말하자면 그는, 자연이 너무 서두른 나머지 머릿속에 뇌라곤 채워 넣는 걸 깜빡한 부류의 인간 가운데 하나였다."]], [["Now it happens to this sort of men, as to bad hounds, who never hit off a fault themselves; but no sooner doth a dog of sagacity open his mouth than they immediately do the same, and, without the guidance of any scent, run directly forwards as fast as they are able.", "그런데 이런 부류의 사람들은 못난 사냥개와 같아서, 제 힘으로는 결코 냄새를 짚어 내지 못한다. 다만 영민한 개 한 마리가 입을 열어 짖기 시작하면, 그제야 똑같이 따라 짖고, 무슨 냄새의 안내도 없이 그저 곧장 앞으로 있는 힘껏 내달릴 뿐이다."], ["In the same manner, the very moment Mr Maclachlan had mentioned his apprehension, Mr Fitzpatrick instantly concurred, and flew directly up-stairs, to surprize his wife, before he knew where she was; and unluckily (as Fortune loves to play tricks with those gentlemen who put themselves entirely under her conduct) ran his head against several doors and posts to no purpose.", "꼭 그처럼, 매클라클런 씨가 자기 의심을 입에 올리기 무섭게 피츠패트릭 씨도 즉각 맞장구를 쳤고, 아내가 어디 있는지도 모르는 채로 곧장 위층으로 날아 올라가 그녀를 덮치려 했다. 그러고는 운수 사납게도—운명의 여신은 자기에게 모든 걸 내맡긴 신사들을 골탕 먹이기 좋아하는 법이라—여러 문짝과 기둥에 머리를 들이받았지만 다 부질없는 짓이었다."], ["Much kinder was she to me, when she suggested that simile of the hounds, just before inserted; since the poor wife may, on these occasions, be so justly compared to a hunted hare.", "운명의 여신은 차라리 내게 훨씬 다정했으니, 방금 끼워 넣은 저 사냥개의 비유를 떠올려 주었던 것이다. 이런 경우의 가엾은 아내야말로 쫓기는 산토끼에 견주기에 더없이 어울리니 말이다."], ["Like that little wretched animal, she pricks up her ears to listen after the voice of her pursuer; like her, flies away trembling when she hears it; and, like her, is generally overtaken and destroyed in the end.", "그 작고 가련한 짐승처럼, 아내는 쫓는 자의 목소리에 귀를 쫑긋 세우고, 그 소리가 들리면 그 짐승처럼 부들부들 떨며 달아나며, 결국에는 그 짐승처럼 대개 따라잡혀 끝장나고 만다."]], [["This was not however the case at present; for after a long fruitless search, Mr Fitzpatrick returned to the kitchen, where, as if this had been a real chace, entered a gentleman hallowing as hunters do when the hounds are at a fault.", "그러나 지금 이 경우는 그렇지 않았다. 한참을 헛되이 뒤진 끝에 피츠패트릭 씨는 부엌으로 돌아왔는데, 마치 이것이 진짜 사냥이라도 되는 양, 사냥개들이 냄새를 놓쳤을 때 사냥꾼들이 하듯 소리를 지르며 한 신사가 들어섰다."], ["He was just alighted from his horse, and had many attendants at his heels.", "그는 막 말에서 내린 참이었고, 그 뒤꽁무니에는 여러 수행원이 따라붙어 있었다."]], [["Here, reader, it may be necessary to acquaint thee with some matters, which, if thou dost know already, thou art wiser than I take thee to be.", "여기서, 독자여, 몇 가지 사정을 그대에게 알려 두는 편이 필요할 듯하다. 만일 그대가 그것을 이미 알고 있다면, 그대는 내가 헤아리는 것보다 더 지혜로운 사람일 터이다."], ["And this information thou shalt receive in the next chapter.", "그리고 이 정보는 다음 장에서 받게 될 것이다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["In which are concluded the adventures that happened at the inn at Upton.", "업튼의 여관에서 막 도착한 이 신사는 다름 아닌 웨스턴 영주 본인이었다. 그는 딸을 뒤쫓아 이곳까지 온 것이었다."], ["In the first place, then, this gentleman just arrived was no other person than Squire Western himself, who was come hither in pursuit of his daughter; and, had he fortunately been two hours earlier, he had not only found her, but his niece into the bargain; for such was the wife of Mr Fitzpatrick, who had run away with her five years before, out of the custody of that sage lady, Madam Western.", "만일 운 좋게도 두 시간만 더 일찍 왔더라면 딸뿐 아니라 조카딸까지 덤으로 찾았을 것이다. 그 조카딸이란 바로 피츠패트릭 씨의 아내였으니, 그녀는 다섯 해 전 저 지혜로운 부인 웨스턴 여사의 보호에서 벗어나 그와 함께 달아났던 것이다."]], [["Now this lady had departed from the inn much about the same time with Sophia; for, having been waked by the voice of her husband, she had sent up for the landlady, and being by her apprized of the matter, had bribed the good woman, at an extravagant price, to furnish her with horses for her escape.", "그런데 이 부인은 소피아와 거의 같은 시각에 여관을 떠났다. 남편의 목소리에 잠이 깬 그녀는 사람을 보내 안주인을 불러올렸고, 사정을 전해 듣자 그 선량한 여인에게 터무니없는 값을 치러 매수하여 달아날 말을 마련하게 했던 것이다."], ["Such prevalence had money in this family; and though the mistress would have turned away her maid for a corrupt hussy, if she had known as much as the reader, yet she was no more proof against corruption herself than poor Susan had been.", "돈이란 이 집안에서 그토록 위력이 컸다. 안주인이 만일 독자만큼 사정을 다 알았더라면 자기 하녀를 타락한 계집이라 내쫓았겠지만, 정작 그녀 자신도 가엾은 수전 못지않게 매수 앞에서 무력했던 것이다."]], [["Mr Western and his nephew were not known to one another; nor indeed would the former have taken any notice of the latter if he had known him; for, this being a stolen match, and consequently an unnatural one in the opinion of the good squire, he had, from the time of her committing it, abandoned the poor young creature, who was then no more than eighteen, as a monster, and had never since suffered her to be named in his presence.", "웨스턴 씨와 그 조카사위는 서로 아는 사이가 아니었다. 설령 알았더라도 웨스턴 씨는 조카사위에게 눈길 한 번 주지 않았을 것이다. 이 결혼은 몰래 이루어진 것이었고, 따라서 선량한 영주의 생각으로는 자연을 거스른 것이었기에, 그는 딸이 그 일을 저지른 그때부터 — 당시 그 가엾은 어린것은 겨우 열여덟에 지나지 않았다 — 그녀를 괴물로 여겨 내버렸고, 그 후로는 자기 앞에서 그녀의 이름이 입에 오르는 것조차 결코 용납하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["The kitchen was now a scene of universal confusion, Western enquiring after his daughter, and Fitzpatrick as eagerly after his wife, when Jones entered the room, unfortunately having Sophia's muff in his hand.", "주방은 이제 온통 아수라장이었다. 웨스턴은 딸을 찾아 묻고, 피츠패트릭은 그 못지않게 다급히 아내를 찾아 물었다. 그때 존스가 방으로 들어섰으니, 불운하게도 그의 손에는 소피아의 토시가 들려 있었다."]], [["As soon as Western saw Jones, he set up the same holla as is used by sportsmen when their game is in view.", "웨스턴은 존스를 보자마자, 사냥꾼들이 사냥감을 눈앞에 두었을 때 내지르는 바로 그 고함을 질렀다."], ["He then immediately run up and laid hold of Jones, crying, “We have got the dog fox, I warrant the bitch is not far off.”", "그러고는 즉시 달려들어 존스를 붙잡으며 외쳤다. \"수여우를 잡았다, 장담컨대 암여우도 멀리 있지 않을 게다.\""], ["The jargon which followed for some minutes, where many spoke different things at the same time, as it would be very difficult to describe, so would it be no less unpleasant to read.", "그 뒤로 몇 분 동안 이어진 알아들을 수 없는 소동에서는 여러 사람이 동시에 제각기 다른 말을 떠들어댔으니, 그것을 묘사하기란 무척 어려운 일이거니와 읽는 것 또한 그에 못지않게 불쾌한 일일 것이다."]], [["Jones having, at length, shaken Mr Western off, and some of the company having interfered between them, our heroe protested his innocence as to knowing anything of the lady; when Parson Supple stepped up, and said, “It is folly to deny it; for why, the marks of guilt are in thy hands.", "마침내 존스가 웨스턴 씨를 떨쳐내고 좌중의 몇 사람이 둘 사이를 가로막자, 우리의 주인공은 그 부인에 관해서는 아무것도 모른다며 자신의 결백을 항변했다. 그때 서플 목사가 나서서 말했다."], ["I will myself asseverate and bind it by an oath, that the muff thou bearest in thy hand belongeth unto Madam Sophia; for I have frequently observed her, of later days, to bear it about her.”", "\"그것을 부인하는 것은 어리석은 짓이오. 어찌하여 그러냐 하면, 죄의 증거가 그대의 손에 있기 때문이오."], ["“My daughter's muff!” cries the squire in a rage.", "그대가 손에 든 그 토시가 소피아 아씨의 것임을, 내 친히 단언하며 맹세로써 보증하리다."], ["“Hath he got my daughter's muff?", "근래 들어 그 아씨가 그것을 지니고 다니는 것을 내 자주 보았으니 말이오.\" \"내 딸의 토시라고!\""], ["bear witness the goods are found upon him.", "영주가 노발대발하며 소리쳤다. \"저자가 내 딸의 토시를 가졌다고?"], ["I'll have him before a justice of peace this instant.", "증인이 되어 주시오, 장물이 저자에게서 나왔소. 당장 저자를 치안판사 앞에 끌고 가겠소."], ["Where is my daughter, villain?”", "내 딸 어디 있느냐, 이 악당아?\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"어르신,\""], ["said Jones, “I beg you would be pacified.", "존스가 말했다. \"부디 진정하시기를 청합니다."], ["The muff, I acknowledge, is the young lady's; but, upon my honour, I have never seen her.”", "이 토시가 그 아씨의 것임은 인정하나, 제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리건대, 저는 그분을 결코 뵌 적이 없습니다.\""], ["At these words Western lost all patience, and grew inarticulate with rage.", "이 말에 웨스턴은 인내심을 완전히 잃고, 분에 차서 말도 제대로 잇지 못했다."]], [["Some of the servants had acquainted Fitzpatrick who Mr Western was.", "하인 몇이 피츠패트릭에게 웨스턴 씨가 누구인지 일러주었다. 그래서 이 선량한 아일랜드 사람은 이제 자기 처삼촌에게 도움을 베풀 기회가 생겼으니, 그렇게 하면 어쩌면 그의 환심을 살 수 있으리라 생각하여 존스에게 다가가 외쳤다."], ["The good Irishman, therefore, thinking he had now an opportunity to do an act of service to his uncle, and by that means might possibly obtain his favour, stept up to Jones, and cried out, “Upon my conscience, sir, you may be ashamed of denying your having seen the gentleman's daughter before my face, when you know I found you there upon the bed together.”", "\"양심을 걸고 말하건대, 선생, 내 면전에서 그 신사분의 따님을 본 적이 없다고 부인하다니 부끄럽지도 않소. 내가 침대 위에서 두 사람이 함께 있는 것을 똑똑히 보았는데 말이오.\""], ["Then, turning to Western, he offered to conduct him immediately to the room where his daughter was; which offer being accepted, he, the squire, the parson, and some others, ascended directly to Mrs Waters's chamber, which they entered with no less violence than Mr Fitzpatrick had done before.", "그러고는 웨스턴을 향해 돌아서서, 따님이 있는 방으로 당장 안내하겠노라 청했다. 그 청이 받아들여지자, 그와 영주, 목사, 그리고 몇몇 사람들이 곧장 워터스 부인의 방으로 올라갔는데, 그들은 앞서 피츠패트릭 씨가 그랬던 것 못지않게 거칠게 방으로 들이닥쳤다."]], [["The poor lady started from her sleep with as much amazement as terror, and beheld at her bedside a figure which might very well be supposed to have escaped out of Bedlam.", "가엾은 부인은 공포 못지않은 경악에 휩싸여 잠에서 화들짝 깨어났고, 침대맡에 베들럼에서 막 뛰쳐나왔다 해도 곧이들을 만한 형상이 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Such wildness and confusion were in the looks of Mr Western; who no sooner saw the lady than he started back, shewing sufficiently by his manner, before he spoke, that this was not the person sought after.", "웨스턴 씨의 얼굴에는 그토록 사납고 어지러운 기색이 서려 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그는 그 부인을 보자마자 흠칫 물러섰으니, 입을 열기도 전에 그 거동만으로도 이 여인이 찾던 사람이 아님을 충분히 드러냈다."]], [["So much more tenderly do women value their reputation than their persons, that, though the latter seemed now in more danger than before, yet, as the former was secure, the lady screamed not with such violence as she had done on the other occasion.", "여자들은 제 몸보다 제 평판을 그토록 더 애틋이 여기는 법이어서, 이제 몸이 전보다 더 위험에 처한 듯했음에도, 평판만큼은 안전했던지라 부인은 앞서 다른 경우에 그랬던 것처럼 격하게 비명을 지르지는 않았다."], ["However, she no sooner found herself alone than she abandoned all thoughts of further repose; and, as she had sufficient reason to be dissatisfied with her present lodging, she dressed herself with all possible expedition.", "그러나 혼자 남게 되자마자 그녀는 더 잘 생각은 모두 접어버렸고, 지금의 거처에 만족하지 못할 충분한 이유가 있었던 만큼, 할 수 있는 한 서둘러 옷을 차려입었다. * 베들럼: 런던의 정신병원으로, 흔히 광인을 가리키는 말로 쓰였다."]], [["Mr Western now proceeded to search the whole house, but to as little purpose as he had disturbed poor Mrs Waters.", "웨스턴 씨는 이제 집안 전체를 뒤지기 시작했으나, 가엾은 워터스 부인을 놀라게 한 것만큼이나 아무 소득도 없었다."], ["He then returned disconsolate into the kitchen, where he found Jones in the custody of his servants.", "그는 낙담하여 주방으로 돌아왔고, 거기서 자기 하인들에게 붙들려 있는 존스를 발견했다."]], [["This violent uproar had raised all the people in the house, though it was yet scarcely daylight.", "이 격렬한 소란은 아직 동도 채 트지 않았는데도 집 안의 모든 사람을 깨워 일으켰다."], ["Among these was a grave gentleman, who had the honour to be in the commission of the peace for the county of Worcester.", "그 가운데에는 우스터 주의 치안판사직을 맡는 영예를 누리던 점잖은 신사 한 명도 있었다."], ["Of which Mr Western was no sooner informed than he offered to lay his complaint before him.", "웨스턴 씨는 그 사실을 알자마자 그 앞에 자신의 고소를 제기하겠노라 했다."], ["The justice declined executing his office, as he said he had no clerk present, nor no book about justice business; and that he could not carry all the law in his head about stealing away daughters, and such sort of things.", "판사는 그 직무를 집행하기를 사양하며, 지금 서기도 곁에 없고 사법 업무에 관한 책도 한 권 가진 게 없으며, 딸을 빼돌리는 일이라든가 그런 따위의 법을 머릿속에 죄다 담아둘 수는 없는 노릇이라고 말했다."]], [["Here Mr Fitzpatrick offered to lend him his assistance, informing the company that he had been himself bred to the law.", "이때 피츠패트릭 씨가 자기 도움을 빌려주겠노라 나서며, 자신이 본디 법조계에서 자랐노라고 좌중에 일러주었다."], ["(And indeed he had served three years as clerk to an attorney in the north of Ireland, when, chusing a genteeler walk in life, he quitted his master, came over to England, and set up that business which requires no apprenticeship, namely, that of a gentleman, in which he had succeeded, as hath been already partly mentioned.)", "(실제로 그는 북아일랜드의 한 변호사 밑에서 삼 년간 서기로 일했었는데, 그 후 좀 더 점잖은 인생행로를 택하여 주인을 떠나 잉글랜드로 건너왔고, 도제 수업 따위가 전혀 필요 없는 직업, 다시 말해 신사라는 직업을 차렸으니, 앞서 일부 언급한 바와 같이 그 일에 성공을 거두었던 것이다.)"]], [["Mr Fitzpatrick declared that the law concerning daughters was out of the present case; that stealing a muff was undoubtedly felony, and the goods being found upon the person, were sufficient evidence of the fact.", "피츠패트릭 씨는 딸에 관한 법은 지금의 사건과는 무관하다고 단언했다. 토시를 훔친 것은 의심할 여지 없는 중죄이며, 그 물건이 당사자에게서 발견되었으니 그 사실을 입증하는 충분한 증거라는 것이었다."]], [["The magistrate, upon the encouragement of so learned a coadjutor, and upon the violent intercession of the squire, was at length prevailed upon to seat himself in the chair of justice, where being placed, upon viewing the muff which Jones still held in his hand, and upon the parson's swearing it to be the property of Mr Western, he desired Mr Fitzpatrick to draw up a commitment, which he said he would sign.", "판사는 이토록 박식한 조력자의 격려에 더하여 영주의 격렬한 간청에 힘입어, 마침내 사법의 의자에 앉도록 설득당했다. 그렇게 자리를 잡고서, 존스가 여전히 손에 들고 있던 토시를 살펴보고, 그것이 웨스턴 씨의 소유물이라는 목사의 맹세를 듣고 나서, 피츠패트릭 씨에게 구금 영장을 작성하라 청하며 자신이 서명하겠노라 말했다."]], [["Jones now desired to be heard, which was at last, with difficulty, granted him.", "이제 존스가 자기 말을 들어달라 청하니, 마침내 어렵사리 허락을 받았다."], ["He then produced the evidence of Mr Partridge, as to the finding it; but, what was still more, Susan deposed that Sophia herself had delivered the muff to her, and had ordered her to convey it into the chamber where Mr Jones had found it.", "그는 토시를 발견한 경위에 관해 파트리지 씨의 증언을 제시했다. 그러나 그보다 더한 것은, 수전이 진술하기를 소피아가 직접 그 토시를 자기에게 건네주며 존스 씨가 그것을 발견한 바로 그 방으로 옮겨다 놓으라 시켰다는 것이었다."]], [["Whether a natural love of justice, or the extraordinary comeliness of Jones, had wrought on Susan to make the discovery, I will not determine; but such were the effects of her evidence, that the magistrate, throwing himself back in his chair, declared that the matter was now altogether as clear on the side of the prisoner as it had before been against him: with which the parson concurred, saying, the Lord forbid he should be instrumental in committing an innocent person to durance.", "타고난 정의감 때문이었는지, 아니면 존스의 유난한 준수함이 수전을 움직여 그 사실을 털어놓게 한 것인지는 내 판단하지 않겠다. 다만 그녀의 증언이 빚어낸 결과인즉, 판사는 의자에 등을 기대고 앉으며, 이 사건은 이제 앞서 피고에게 불리했던 것만큼이나 깡그리 피고에게 유리해졌노라고 선언했다."], ["The justice then arose, acquitted the prisoner, and broke up the court.", "목사도 이에 동의하여, 무고한 사람을 구금하는 데 자신이 도구가 되는 일이 없도록 주께서 막아주시기를 바란다고 말했다. 그러자 판사는 자리에서 일어나 피고를 무죄 방면하고 재판을 파했다."]], [["Mr Western now gave every one present a hearty curse, and, immediately ordering his horses, departed in pursuit of his daughter, without taking the least notice of his nephew Fitzpatrick, or returning any answer to his claim of kindred, notwithstanding all the obligations he had just received from that gentleman.", "웨스턴 씨는 이제 그 자리에 있는 모든 이에게 진심 어린 저주를 퍼붓고는, 즉시 말을 대령시켜 딸을 뒤쫓아 떠났다. 조카사위 피츠패트릭에게는 눈길 한 번 주지 않았고, 그 신사에게서 방금 받은 온갖 신세에도 불구하고 친척이라 주장하는 그의 말에 아무런 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["In the violence, moreover, of his hurry, and of his passion, he luckily forgot to demand the muff of Jones: I say luckily; for he would have died on the spot rather than have parted with it.", "더구나 그토록 다급하고 격분한 와중에, 그는 다행히도 존스에게 토시를 내놓으라 요구하는 것을 잊어버렸다. 다행이라고 말하는 까닭은, 존스라면 그것을 내놓느니 차라리 그 자리에서 죽었을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["Jones likewise, with his friend Partridge, set forward the moment he had paid his reckoning, in quest of his lovely Sophia, whom he now resolved never more to abandon the pursuit of.", "존스 역시 셈을 치르자마자 벗 파트리지와 함께, 사랑스러운 소피아를 찾아 길을 나섰다. 그는 이제 결코 다시는 그 뒤쫓기를 포기하지 않으리라 결심했다."], ["Nor could he bring himself even to take leave of Mrs Waters; of whom he detested the very thoughts, as she had been, though not designedly, the occasion of his missing the happiest interview with Sophia, to whom he now vowed eternal constancy.", "그는 워터스 부인에게 작별 인사를 하는 것조차 견딜 수 없었으니, 그녀를 떠올리는 것만으로도 진저리가 났던 것이다. 비록 의도한 바는 아니었으나 그녀가 소피아와의 더없이 행복했을 만남을 놓치게 한 빌미였기 때문인데, 그는 이제 그 소피아에게 영원한 변함없음을 맹세했다."]], [["As for Mrs Waters, she took the opportunity of the coach which was going to Bath; for which place she set out in company with the two Irish gentlemen, the landlady kindly lending her her cloaths; in return for which she was contented only to receive about double their value, as a recompence for the loan.", "워터스 부인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 마침 배스로 가는 마차를 잡아탈 기회를 얻었다. 그래서 두 아일랜드 신사와 일행이 되어 그곳을 향해 떠났는데, 안주인은 친절하게도 그녀에게 옷을 빌려주었다."], ["Upon the road she was perfectly reconciled to Mr Fitzpatrick, who was a very handsome fellow, and indeed did all she could to console him in the absence of his wife.", "그 대가로 안주인은 옷값의 두 배쯤 되는 돈만 받는 것으로 만족했으니, 빌려준 데 대한 보상인 셈이었다. 길 위에서 그녀는 피츠패트릭 씨와 완전히 화해했는데, 그는 매우 잘생긴 사내였던지라, 그녀는 그가 아내가 곁에 없는 것을 달래주기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다 했다."]], [["Thus ended the many odd adventures which Mr Jones encountered at his inn at Upton, where they talk, to this day, of the beauty and lovely behaviour of the charming Sophia, by the name of the Somersetshire angel.", "이렇게 하여 존스 씨가 업튼의 여관에서 겪은 그 숱한 기이한 모험들이 끝을 맺었다. 그곳에서는 오늘날까지도 매혹적인 소피아의 아름다움과 사랑스러운 거동을, 서머싯셔의 천사라는 이름으로 입에 올린다."]], [["In which the history goes backward.", "이야기가 거꾸로 거슬러 올라가는 장."]], [["Before we proceed any farther in our history, it may be proper to look a little back, in order to account for the extraordinary appearance of Sophia and her father at the inn at Upton.", "우리의 이야기를 더 앞으로 끌고 가기 전에, 소피아와 그녀의 아버지가 업튼의 여관에 그토록 뜻밖에 나타난 까닭을 밝히기 위해, 잠시 뒤를 돌아보는 것이 좋겠다."]], [["The reader may be pleased to remember that, in the ninth chapter of the seventh book of our history, we left Sophia, after a long debate between love and duty, deciding the cause, as it usually, I believe, happens, in favour of the former.", "독자께서는 이 이야기 일곱째 권 아홉째 장에서, 사랑과 의무 사이의 긴 다툼 끝에 소피아가 — 내 생각에 흔히 그렇듯 — 전자의 손을 들어 그 문제를 매듭짓고 떠났던 일을 기억해 주시면 좋겠다."]], [["This debate had arisen, as we have there shown, from a visit which her father had just before made her, in order to force her consent to a marriage with Blifil; and which he had understood to be fully implied in her acknowledgment “that she neither must nor could refuse any absolute command of his.”", "이 다툼은, 거기서 보여드린 대로, 바로 직전에 그녀의 아버지가 그녀를 찾아온 데서 비롯되었다. 그는 블리필과의 혼인에 그녀의 동의를 억지로 받아 내려고 왔고, 그 동의는 \"아버지의 어떤 절대적인 명령도 거절해서는 안 되며 거절할 수도 없다\"는 그녀의 말 속에 온전히 담겨 있다고 그가 받아들인 것이었다."]], [["Now from this visit the squire retired to his evening potation, overjoyed at the success he had gained with his daughter; and, as he was of a social disposition, and willing to have partakers in his happiness, the beer was ordered to flow very liberally into the kitchen; so that before eleven in the evening there was not a single person sober in the house except only Mrs Western herself and the charming Sophia.", "자, 이 면담을 마치고 향사는 딸에게서 거둔 성공에 더없이 기뻐하며 저녁 술자리로 물러났다. 그는 사람들과 어울리기를 좋아하는 성품인지라 자기 행복을 함께 나눌 동무를 두고 싶어 했고, 그리하여 맥주가 부엌으로 아주 넉넉히 흘러가도록 명했다. 그 결과 저녁 열한 시가 되기도 전에 집 안에서 멀쩡히 깨어 있는 사람이라곤 웨스턴 부인 본인과 사랑스러운 소피아뿐이었다."]], [["Early in the morning a messenger was despatched to summon Mr Blifil; for, though the squire imagined that young gentleman had been much less acquainted than he really was with the former aversion of his daughter, as he had not, however, yet received her consent, he longed impatiently to communicate it to him, not doubting but that the intended bride herself would confirm it with her lips.", "이른 아침, 블리필 씨를 불러오라고 심부름꾼이 보내졌다. 비록 향사는 그 젊은 신사가 자기 딸이 전부터 품어 온 거부감을 실제보다 훨씬 모르고 있으리라 짐작했지만, 아직 딸의 동의를 받아 두지는 못한 터라, 한시바삐 그 소식을 그에게 전하고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["As to the wedding, it had the evening before been fixed, by the male parties, to be celebrated on the next morning save one.", "신붓감으로 점찍힌 당사자가 제 입으로 그것을 확인해 주리라는 것을 그는 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 혼례로 말하자면, 그 전날 저녁에 남자 측 사람들끼리 다음다음 날 아침에 치르기로 이미 정해 둔 상태였다."]], [["Breakfast was now set forth in the parlour, where Mr Blifil attended, and where the squire and his sister likewise were assembled; and now Sophia was ordered to be called.", "이제 응접실에 아침 식사가 차려졌고, 거기에는 블리필 씨가 와 있었으며, 향사와 그의 누이도 함께 모여 있었다. 그러고 나서 소피아를 불러오라는 분부가 떨어졌다."]], [["O, Shakespear!", "오, 셰익스피어여!"], ["had I thy pen!", "그대의 펜이 내게 있다면!"], ["O, Hogarth!", "오, 호가스여!"], ["had I thy pencil!", "그대의 화필이 내게 있다면!"], ["then would I draw the picture of the poor serving-man, who, with pale countenance, staring eyes, chattering teeth, faultering tongue, and trembling limbs,", "그렇다면 나는 저 가엾은 하인의 모습을 그려 보이리라. 핏기 없는 얼굴에, 휘둥그레진 눈, 딱딱 부딪치는 이, 더듬거리는 혀, 그리고 떨리는 사지로,"]], [["(E'en such a man, so faint, so spiritless, So dull, so dead in look, so woe-begone, Drew Priam's curtains in the dead of night, And would have told him, half his Troy was burn'd)", "(바로 그런 사내, 그토록 기진하고, 그토록 넋이 빠진,\n그토록 멍하고, 죽은 듯한 낯빛으로, 그토록 비탄에 잠긴 채,"]], [["entered the room, and declared--That Madam Sophia was not to be found.", "깊은 밤중에 프리아모스의 침장을 걷어 젖히고,\n그에게 트로이의 절반이 불탔노라 고하려 했더라)"]], [["“Not to be found!”", "하인은 방으로 들어와 이렇게 알렸다. 소피아 아씨가 보이지 않는다고."], ["cries the squire, starting from his chair; “Zounds and d--nation!", "\"보이지 않는다고!\" 향사가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 소리쳤다."], ["Blood and fury!", "\"이런 빌어먹을, 저주받을!"], ["Where, when, how, what--Not to be found!", "피 튀기고 분통 터지는 노릇이로다! 어디서, 언제, 어떻게, 무슨—보이지 않다니!"], ["Where?”", "어디서?\""]], [["“La!", "\"어머나!"], ["brother,”", "오라버니,\""], ["said Mrs Western, with true political coldness, “you are always throwing yourself into such violent passions for nothing.", "웨스턴 부인이 정치가다운 냉정함으로 말했다. \"오라버니는 늘 아무것도 아닌 일에 그렇게 펄펄 끓어오르시는군요."], ["My niece, I suppose, is only walked out into the garden.", "제 조카는 그저 정원에 산책을 나갔을 뿐일 거예요."], ["I protest you are grown so unreasonable, that it is impossible to live in the house with you.”", "분명히 말하지만, 오라버니가 어찌나 사리 분별을 잃으셨는지 도무지 한집에서 같이 살 수가 없을 지경이에요.\""]], [["“Nay, nay,”", "\"아니, 아니,\""], ["answered the squire, returning as suddenly to himself, as he had gone from himself; “if that be all the matter, it signifies not much; but, upon my soul, my mind misgave me when the fellow said she was not to be found.”", "향사가 정신을 놓았던 것만큼이나 갑작스레 제정신으로 돌아오며 대꾸했다. \"그게 다라면야 별일 아니지."], ["He then gave orders for the bell to be rung in the garden, and sat himself contentedly down.", "하지만 정말이지, 그놈이 애가 안 보인다고 했을 때 내 마음에 불길한 예감이 스쳤단 말이다.\" 그러고는 정원에서 종을 울리라고 명한 뒤, 흡족한 듯 자리에 앉았다."]], [["No two things could be more the reverse of each other than were the brother and sister in most instances; particularly in this, That as the brother never foresaw anything at a distance, but was most sagacious in immediately seeing everything the moment it had happened; so the sister eternally foresaw at a distance, but was not so quick-sighted to objects before her eyes. Of both these the reader may have observed examples: and, indeed, both their several talents were excessive; for, as the sister often foresaw what never came to pass, so the brother often saw much more than was actually the truth.", "이 오누이만큼 거의 모든 면에서 서로 정반대인 두 사람도 없을 것이다. 특히 이 점에서 그러했다. 오라버니는 멀찍이 앞일을 내다보는 법이 결코 없으면서도 일이 벌어진 바로 그 순간에 모든 것을 알아채는 데는 더없이 날카로웠고, 반면에 누이는 영원히 멀리 있는 일을 내다보면서도 눈앞에 놓인 것에는 그리 빠른 눈을 갖지 못했다. 이 둘에 관한 본보기를 독자께서는 이미 보아 왔을 것이다. 게다가 두 사람의 그 재주는 저마다 도를 지나쳤으니, 누이는 끝내 일어나지도 않을 일을 곧잘 내다보았고, 오라버니는 실제 사실보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을 곧잘 보아 버리곤 했다."]], [["This was not however the case at present.", "그러나 이번만큼은 그렇지 않았다."], ["The same report was brought from the garden as before had been brought from the chamber, that Madam Sophia was not to be found.", "정원에서도 앞서 방에서 전해진 것과 똑같은 소식이 들려왔으니, 소피아 아씨가 보이지 않는다는 것이었다."]], [["The squire himself now sallied forth, and began to roar forth the name of Sophia as loudly, and in as hoarse a voice, as whilome did Hercules that of Hylas; and, as the poet tells us that the whole shore echoed back the name of that beautiful youth, so did the house, the garden, and all the neighbouring fields resound nothing but the name of Sophia, in the hoarse voices of the men, and in the shrill pipes of the women; while echo seemed so pleased to repeat the beloved sound, that, if there is really such a person, I believe Ovid hath belied her sex.", "이제 향사가 몸소 나서서, 옛날에 헤라클레스가 힐라스의 이름을 부르짖던 것만큼이나 우렁차고 쉰 목소리로 소피아의 이름을 외쳐 대기 시작했다. 시인이 우리에게 일러 주듯, 온 해안이 그 아름다운 젊은이의 이름을 메아리쳐 돌려보냈던 것처럼, 집과 정원과 이웃한 들판 전체가 오로지 소피아의 이름만을 되울렸다. 사내들의 쉰 목소리로, 또 여인들의 째지는 가락으로 말이다. 그동안 메아리는 그 사랑스러운 소리를 되풀이하는 것이 어찌나 즐거운지, 만일 정말로 그런 존재가 있다면, 내 생각에 오비디우스는 그녀의 성별을 잘못 일러바친 것이리라."]], [["Nothing reigned for a long time but confusion; till at last the squire, having sufficiently spent his breath, returned to the parlour, where he found Mrs Western and Mr Blifil, and threw himself, with the utmost dejection in his countenance, into a great chair.", "오랫동안 어수선함 말고는 아무것도 지배하지 못했다. 마침내 향사가 숨을 다 써 버린 뒤 응접실로 돌아왔고, 거기서 그는 웨스턴 부인과 블리필 씨를 발견하고는, 얼굴 가득 더없는 낙심을 띤 채 커다란 안락의자에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["Here Mrs Western began to apply the following consolation:", "여기서 웨스턴 부인은 다음과 같은 위로의 말을 건네기 시작했다."]], [["“Brother, I am sorry for what hath happened; and that my niece should have behaved herself in a manner so unbecoming her family; but it is all your own doings, and you have nobody to thank but yourself. You know she hath been educated always in a manner directly contrary to my advice, and now you see the consequence. Have I not a thousand times argued with you about giving my niece her own will? But you know I never could prevail upon you; and when I had taken so much pains to eradicate her headstrong opinions, and to rectify your errors in policy, you know she was taken out of my hands; so that I have nothing to answer for. Had I been trusted entirely with the care of her education, no such accident as this had ever befallen you; so that you must comfort yourself by thinking it was all your own doing; and, indeed, what else could be expected from such indulgence?”", "\"오라버니, 벌어진 일은 저도 안타까워요. 제 조카가 제 집안에 그토록 어울리지 않는 짓을 저질렀다는 것도 말이에요. 하지만 이건 모두 오라버니가 자초한 일이고, 탓할 사람은 오라버니 자신밖에 없어요. 그 애가 늘 제 충고와는 정반대로 길러져 왔다는 걸 오라버니도 아시잖아요. 그러니 이제 그 결과를 보시는 거예요. 조카에게 제멋대로 굴게 내버려 두는 일을 두고 제가 천 번도 더 오라버니와 입씨름하지 않았던가요? 하지만 한 번도 오라버니를 꺾을 수 없었던 걸 아시잖아요. 그리고 제가 그토록 애를 써서 그 애의 고집스러운 생각을 뿌리 뽑고 오라버니의 처신상 잘못을 바로잡아 놓았건만, 그 애가 제 손에서 떨어져 나갔다는 것도 아시잖아요. 그러니 제가 책임질 것은 아무것도 없어요. 만약 그 애의 교육이 온전히 제게 맡겨졌더라면, 이런 사고는 결코 오라버니에게 닥치지 않았을 거예요. 그러니 이 모든 게 오라버니 자신이 한 일이라 여기며 스스로를 다독이셔야 해요. 사실 그렇게 오냐오냐했으니 이 말고 무엇을 바랄 수 있었겠어요?\""]], [["“Zounds!", "\"제기랄!"], ["sister,”", "누이야,\""], ["answered he, “you are enough to make one mad.", "그가 대꾸했다. \"너는 사람 미치게 하고도 남겠구나."], ["Have I indulged her?", "내가 그 애를 오냐오냐했다고?"], ["Have I given her her will?----It was no longer ago than last night that I threatened, if she disobeyed me, to confine her to her chamber upon bread and water as long as she lived.----You would provoke the patience of Job.”", "내가 제멋대로 하게 두었다고?——바로 어젯밤에도 나는 그 애가 내 말을 거역하면 평생토록 빵과 물만 먹이며 제 방에 가둬 두겠다고 으름장을 놓았단 말이다.——너는 욥의 인내심마저 돋워 화나게 할 사람이로구나.\""]], [["“Did ever mortal hear the like?”", "\"세상에 이런 소리를 듣는 사람이 또 있을까요?\""], ["replied she.", "그녀가 받아쳤다."], ["“Brother, if I had not the patience of fifty Jobs, you would make me forget all decency and decorum.", "\"오라버니, 제가 욥 쉰 명만큼의 인내심을 갖고 있지 않았더라면, 오라버니 때문에 온갖 체면과 예법을 다 잊어버렸을 거예요."], ["Why would you interfere?", "어째서 끼어드셨어요?"], ["Did I not beg you, did I not intreat you, to leave the whole conduct to me?", "모든 일을 제게 맡겨 달라고 제가 간청하지 않았던가요, 애원하지 않았던가요?"], ["You have defeated all the operations of the campaign by one false step.", "오라버니는 단 한 번의 헛디딤으로 이 전투의 모든 작전을 망쳐 버렸어요."], ["Would any man in his senses have provoked a daughter by such threats as these?", "제정신인 사람이라면 그런 으름장으로 딸을 자극했겠어요?"], ["How often have I told you that English women are not to be treated like Ciracessian[*] slaves.", "영국 여인들을 키르카시아의 노예처럼 다뤄서는 안 된다고 제가 몇 번이나 말했나요?"], ["We have the protection of the world; we are to be won by gentle means only, and not to be hectored, and bullied, and beat into compliance.", "우리에게는 세상의 보호가 있어요. 우리는 오직 다정한 방법으로만 마음을 얻을 수 있을 뿐, 윽박지르고 못살게 굴고 두들겨서 복종시킬 수 있는 존재가 아니에요."], ["I thank Heaven no Salique law governs here.", "다행히도 여기엔 살리카 법이 다스리지 않는답니다."], ["Brother, you have a roughness in your manner which no woman but myself would bear.", "오라버니, 오라버니에게는 저 말고 어떤 여자도 견디지 못할 거친 태도가 있어요."], ["I do not wonder my niece was frightened and terrified into taking this measure; and, to speak honestly, I think my niece will be justified to the world for what she hath done.", "제 조카가 겁에 질리고 무서워서 이런 짓을 저질렀다 해도 저는 조금도 놀랍지 않아요. 솔직히 말하자면, 제 조카가 한 일은 세상에서 정당하다 인정받으리라 생각해요."], ["I repeat it to you again, brother, you must comfort yourself by rememb'ring that it is all your own fault.", "다시 한번 말하지만, 오라버니, 이게 모두 오라버니 자신의 잘못임을 기억하며 스스로를 위로하셔야 해요."], ["How often have I advised--”", "제가 얼마나 자주 충고했던가요——\""], ["Here Western rose hastily from his chair, and, venting two or three horrid imprecations, ran out of the room.", "여기서 웨스턴이 황급히 의자에서 일어나, 두세 마디 끔찍한 욕설을 내뱉고는 방 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 키르카시아: 원문은 'Ciracessian'으로, 아마도 캅카스 지방의 키르카시아(Circassian)를 가리키는 듯하다."]], [["[*] Possibly Circassian. When he was departed, his sister expressed more bitterness (if possible) against him than she had done while he was present; for the truth of which she appealed to Mr Blifil, who, with great complacence, acquiesced entirely in all she said; but excused all the faults of Mr Western, “as they must be considered,”", "그가 떠나자, 그의 누이는 그가 있을 때보다도 (그게 가능하다면) 더 매서운 독설을 그에게 퍼부었다."], ["he said, “to have proceeded from the too inordinate fondness of a father, which must be allowed the name of an amiable weakness.”", "그 말이 옳다는 것에 대해 그녀는 블리필 씨에게 동의를 구했고, 그는 더없이 흡족한 낯으로 그녀가 한 말 전부에 온전히 맞장구를 쳐 주었다. 다만 웨스턴 씨의 모든 잘못만큼은 너그러이 보아 주었으니, 그것은 \"아버지의 지나치게 도를 넘은 애정에서 비롯된 것으로 보아야 마땅하며, 그런 애정에는 사랑스러운 약점이라는 이름을 붙여 줄 만하다\"는 것이 그의 변이었다."], ["“So much the more inexcuseable,”", "\"그러니 더더욱 용서받을 수 없지요.\""], ["answered the lady; “for whom doth he ruin by his fondness but his own child?”", "부인이 대꾸했다. \"그 애정으로 그분이 망치는 사람이 자기 자식 말고 또 누가 있겠어요?\""], ["To which Blifil immediately agreed.", "이 말에 블리필은 즉시 동의했다."]], [["Mrs Western then began to express great confusion on the account of Mr Blifil, and of the usage which he had received from a family to which he intended so much honour. On this subject she treated the folly of her niece with great severity; but concluded with throwing the whole on her brother, who, she said, was inexcuseable to have proceeded so far without better assurances of his daughter's consent: “But he was (says she) always of a violent, headstrong temper; and I can scarce forgive myself for all the advice I have thrown away upon him.”", "그러고 나서 웨스턴 부인은 블리필 씨를 생각하니, 또 그가 그토록 큰 영예를 베풀어 주려던 집안으로부터 받은 대접을 생각하니 몹시 면목이 없다는 뜻을 표하기 시작했다. 이 대목에서 그녀는 조카의 어리석음을 아주 매섭게 다루었으나, 결국에는 모든 것을 제 오라버니에게 돌리며 끝맺었다. 그녀의 말로는, 딸의 동의에 대해 더 확실한 다짐도 받아 두지 않은 채 그렇게까지 일을 밀어붙인 것은 오라버니로서 용서받을 수 없는 일이라는 것이었다. \"하지만 그분은 (그녀가 말하길) 늘 난폭하고 고집스러운 성미였어요. 그분에게 헛되이 쏟아부은 그 모든 충고를 생각하면 저 자신조차 좀처럼 용서가 안 돼요.\""]], [["After much of this kind of conversation, which, perhaps, would not greatly entertain the reader, was it here particularly related, Mr Blifil took his leave and returned home, not highly pleased with his disappointment: which, however, the philosophy which he had acquired from Square, and the religion infused into him by Thwackum, together with somewhat else, taught him to bear rather better than more passionate lovers bear these kinds of evils.", "이런 식의 대화가 한참 더 오갔지만, 그것을 여기 일일이 옮겨 적는다 한들 독자께 큰 즐거움이 되지는 못할 터이니, 그 끝에 블리필 씨는 작별을 고하고 집으로 돌아갔다. 자신의 좌절에 그리 흡족한 기분은 아니었다. 그러나 스퀘어에게서 익힌 철학과 스왁컴이 그에게 불어넣은 신앙심, 게다가 그 밖의 또 다른 무언가가 더해진 덕분에, 그는 더 열정적인 연인들이 이런 종류의 불행을 견디는 것보다는 한결 더 잘 견뎌 낼 수 있었다."]], [["The escape of Sophia.", "소피아의 탈출."]], [["It is now time to look after Sophia; whom the reader, if he loves her half so well as I do, will rejoice to find escaped from the clutches of her passionate father, and from those of her dispassionate lover.", "이제 소피아를 살펴볼 때다. 독자가 그녀를 내가 아끼는 것의 절반만큼이라도 아낀다면, 격정에 사로잡힌 아버지의 손아귀에서, 그리고 냉담하기 그지없는 구혼자의 손아귀에서 그녀가 벗어났음을 알고 기뻐할 것이다."]], [["Twelve times did the iron register of time beat on the sonorous bell-metal, summoning the ghosts to rise and walk their nightly round.----In plainer language, it was twelve o'clock, and all the family, as we have said, lay buried in drink and sleep, except only Mrs Western, who was deeply engaged in reading a political pamphlet, and except our heroine, who now softly stole down-stairs, and, having unbarred and unlocked one of the house-doors, sallied forth, and hastened to the place of appointment.", "시간을 새기는 쇠바늘이 낭랑한 종 쇠를 열두 번 두드려, 유령들에게 일어나 밤마다의 순회를 돌라고 불러냈다. 더 쉽게 말하자면, 자정이었다. 그리고 우리가 앞서 말했듯 온 식구가 술과 잠에 파묻혀 누워 있었다. 다만 정치 소책자를 읽는 데 깊이 빠진 웨스턴 부인만이 예외였고, 또 한 사람, 우리의 여주인공만이 예외였다. 그녀는 이제 살그머니 계단을 내려와, 집의 문 하나를 빗장 풀고 자물쇠를 연 뒤 밖으로 나와, 약속한 장소로 서둘러 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Notwithstanding the many pretty arts which ladies sometimes practise, to display their fears on every little occasion (almost as many as the other sex uses to conceal theirs), certainly there is a degree of courage which not only becomes a woman, but is often necessary to enable her to discharge her duty.", "숙녀들이 더러 부리는 그 갖가지 어여쁜 재주, 곧 사소한 일마다 자신의 두려움을 드러내 보이려는 재주(남성들이 자신의 두려움을 감추려고 쓰는 재주만큼이나 그 수가 많다)에도 불구하고, 분명 어떤 정도의 용기는 여자에게 어울릴 뿐 아니라 흔히 그녀가 본분을 다하는 데 꼭 필요하기도 하다."], ["It is, indeed, the idea of fierceness, and not of bravery, which destroys the female character; for who can read the story of the justly celebrated Arria without conceiving as high an opinion of her gentleness and tenderness as of her fortitude?", "사실 여성의 품격을 망가뜨리는 것은 사나움의 관념이지 용감함의 관념이 아니다. 마땅히 칭송받는 아리아의 이야기를 읽으면서, 그녀의 굳센 기개 못지않게 그녀의 온유함과 다정함에도 높은 평가를 품지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["At the same time, perhaps, many a woman who shrieks at a mouse, or a rat, may be capable of poisoning a husband; or, what is worse, of driving him to poison himself.", "그런가 하면, 생쥐나 들쥐를 보고 비명을 지르는 여자라도 남편을 독살할 수 있고, 더 나쁘게는 남편을 몰아세워 스스로 독을 마시게 만들 수도 있는 법이다."]], [["Sophia, with all the gentleness which a woman can have, had all the spirit which she ought to have. When, therefore, she came to the place of appointment, and, instead of meeting her maid, as was agreed, saw a man ride directly up to her, she neither screamed out nor fainted away: not that her pulse then beat with its usual regularity; for she was, at first, under some surprize and apprehension: but these were relieved almost as soon as raised, when the man, pulling off his hat, asked her, in a very submissive manner, “If her ladyship did not expect to meet another lady?” and then proceeded to inform her that he was sent to conduct her to that lady.", "소피아는 여자가 지닐 수 있는 온갖 온유함을 다 갖추었으면서도, 마땅히 지녀야 할 기개 또한 다 갖추고 있었다. 그래서 그녀가 약속한 장소에 이르렀을 때, 합의했던 대로 하녀를 만나는 대신 한 남자가 곧장 자기 쪽으로 말을 몰아 다가오는 것을 보았으나, 그녀는 비명을 지르지도 기절하지도 않았다. 그렇다고 그때 그녀의 맥박이 평소처럼 고르게 뛰었던 것은 아니다. 처음에는 다소 놀라움과 불안에 휩싸였으니 말이다. 하지만 그 감정들은 솟아오르자마자 거의 곧바로 가라앉았다. 그 남자가 모자를 벗으며 무척 공손한 태도로 \"마님께서 다른 숙녀분을 만나기로 하지 않으셨는지요?\" 하고 물었고, 이어서 자신은 그 숙녀에게 마님을 모셔 가도록 보내진 사람이라고 알려 주었기 때문이다."]], [["Sophia could have no possible suspicion of any falsehood in this account: she therefore mounted resolutely behind the fellow, who conveyed her safe to a town about five miles distant, where she had the satisfaction of finding the good Mrs Honour: for, as the soul of the waiting-woman was wrapt up in those very habiliments which used to enwrap her body, she could by no means bring herself to trust them out of her sight. Upon these, therefore, she kept guard in person, while she detached the aforesaid fellow after her mistress, having given him all proper instructions.", "소피아로서는 이 말에 어떤 거짓이 있으리라고는 도무지 의심할 수 없었다. 그리하여 그녀는 단호히 그 사내 뒤에 올라탔고, 사내는 그녀를 다섯 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 읍내까지 무사히 데려다주었다. 그곳에서 그녀는 마음씨 고운 어너 부인을 만나는 기쁨을 누렸다. 사실 이 시중 드는 여인의 영혼은 늘 자기 몸을 감싸던 바로 그 옷가지들에 온통 휘감겨 있었던 터라, 그 옷들을 도무지 자기 눈 밖으로 내보내 맡길 수가 없었던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 그 옷들을 몸소 지키고 앉아 있는 한편, 앞서 말한 그 사내에게 온갖 적절한 지시를 일러 주어 자기 여주인에게로 떠나보냈던 터였다."]], [["They now debated what course to take, in order to avoid the pursuit of Mr Western, who they knew would send after them in a few hours.", "이제 두 사람은 웨스턴 씨의 추격을 피하려면 어느 길을 잡아야 할지 의논했다. 몇 시간 안에 그가 사람을 풀어 자기들을 뒤쫓으리란 것은 익히 알고 있었다."], ["The London road had such charms for Honour, that she was desirous of going on directly; alleging that, as Sophia could not be missed till eight or nine the next morning, her pursuers would not be able to overtake her, even though they knew which way she had gone.", "런던으로 가는 길은 어너에게 어찌나 매력적이었던지, 그녀는 곧장 그쪽으로 가자고 졸랐다. 소피아가 없어진 것을 이튿날 아침 여덟아홉 시까지는 아무도 알아채지 못할 테니, 설령 추격자들이 그녀가 어느 길로 갔는지 안다 해도 따라잡지 못하리라는 것이었다."], ["But Sophia had too much at stake to venture anything to chance; nor did she dare trust too much to her tender limbs, in a contest which was to be decided only by swiftness.", "그러나 소피아는 걸린 것이 너무 많아 무엇 하나 운에 맡길 수 없었다. 또 오직 빠르기로만 판가름 날 겨루기에서 자신의 가녀린 다리를 너무 믿고 나설 엄두도 나지 않았다."], ["She resolved, therefore, to travel across the country, for at least twenty or thirty miles, and then to take the direct road to London.", "그리하여 그녀는 적어도 이삼십 마일은 들판을 가로질러 간 다음, 거기서부터 런던으로 곧장 가는 길을 잡기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 실은 반대편으로 이십 마일을 갈 작정이면서도, 한쪽으로 이십 마일을 갈 말을 빌렸고, 아버지 집에서 타고 왔던 바로 그 안내인을 다시 뒤에 두고 길을 나섰다."], ["So, having hired horses to go twenty miles one way, when she intended to go twenty miles the other, she set forward with the same guide behind whom she had ridden from her father's house; the guide having now taken up behind him, in the room of Sophia, a much heavier, as well as much less lovely burden; being, indeed, a huge portmanteau, well stuffed with those outside ornaments, by means of which the fair Honour hoped to gain many conquests, and, finally, to make her fortune in London city.", "다만 안내인은 이제 소피아 대신, 훨씬 더 무겁고 또 훨씬 덜 사랑스러운 짐을 뒤에 싣고 있었으니, 그것은 다름 아닌 커다란 여행 가방이었다. 그 안에는 어여쁜 어너가 그것으로 숱한 승리를 거두고 끝내는 런던 시내에서 한밑천 잡으려 마음먹은 그 온갖 겉치레 장식들이 가득 채워져 있었다."]], [["When they had gone about two hundred paces from the inn on the London road, Sophia rode up to the guide, and, with a voice much fuller of honey than was ever that of Plato, though his mouth is supposed to have been a bee-hive, begged him to take the first turning which led towards Bristol.", "런던으로 가는 길로 여관에서 이백 걸음쯤 갔을 때, 소피아는 안내인 곁으로 말을 몰아붙이고는, 입이 벌집이었다고들 하는 플라톤의 목소리조차 그만 못했을 만큼 꿀이 흠뻑 밴 목소리로, 브리스틀로 이어지는 첫 갈림길로 들어서 달라고 청했다."]], [["Reader, I am not superstitious, nor any great believer of modern miracles.", "독자여, 나는 미신을 믿는 사람도 아니고, 요즘의 기적이라는 것을 그리 대단히 믿는 사람도 아니다."], ["I do not, therefore, deliver the following as a certain truth; for, indeed, I can scarce credit it myself: but the fidelity of an historian obliges me to relate what hath been confidently asserted.", "그러니 다음 이야기를 확실한 사실인 양 전하는 것은 아니다. 사실 나 자신도 도무지 믿기 어려운 노릇이니 말이다."], ["The horse, then, on which the guide rode, is reported to have been so charmed by Sophia's voice, that he made a full stop, and expressed an unwillingness to proceed any farther.", "하지만 역사를 적는 자로서의 충실함이, 사람들이 자신 있게 단언해 온 바를 전하지 않을 수 없게 만든다. 그러니까, 안내인이 타고 있던 그 말이 소피아의 목소리에 어찌나 홀렸던지 딱 멈춰 서서는 더 나아가기를 꺼리는 기색을 보였다는 것이다."]], [["Perhaps, however, the fact may be true, and less miraculous than it hath been represented; since the natural cause seems adequate to the effect: for, as the guide at that moment desisted from a constant application of his armed right heel (for, like Hudibras, he wore but one spur), it is more than possible that this omission alone might occasion the beast to stop, especially as this was very frequent with him at other times.", "하지만 어쩌면 그 일은 사실이되, 전해지는 것만큼 기적적인 일은 아닐지도 모른다. 자연스러운 원인만으로도 그 결과를 충분히 설명할 수 있는 듯하니 말이다. 안내인이 바로 그 순간 무장한 오른쪽 뒤꿈치를 끊임없이 박차던 것을 멈췄으니(휴디브라스처럼 그도 박차를 한쪽만 차고 있었다), 어쩌면 이 한 가지 소홀함만으로도 그 짐승이 멈춰 섰으리라는 것은 충분히 있을 법한 일이다. 더구나 이 말로 말하자면 다른 때에도 곧잘 그러기를 잘했던 터다."]], [["But if the voice of Sophia had really an effect on the horse, it had very little on the rider.", "그러나 소피아의 목소리가 정말로 말에게 효험이 있었다 한들, 말 탄 사내에게는 거의 통하지 않았다."], ["He answered somewhat surlily, “That measter had ordered him to go a different way, and that he should lose his place if he went any other than that he was ordered.”", "그는 다소 퉁명스레 대답했다. \"나리께서 다른 길로 가라 분부하셨는데, 분부받은 길 말고 딴 데로 갔다간 일자리를 잃을 거구먼유.\""]], [["Sophia, finding all her persuasions had no effect, began now to add irresistible charms to her voice; charms which, according to the proverb, makes the old mare trot, instead of standing still; charms!", "소피아는 자신의 온갖 설득이 아무 효험이 없음을 알자, 이제 자기 목소리에 거역할 수 없는 마력을 더하기 시작했다."], ["to which modern ages have attributed all that irresistible force which the antients imputed to perfect oratory.", "속담대로 가만히 서 있는 늙은 암말도 종종거리게 만든다는 그 마력, 옛사람들이 완벽한 웅변에 돌렸던 그 거역할 수 없는 힘을 요즘 세상이 죄다 거기에 돌리는 그 마력 말이다."], ["In a word, she promised she would reward him to his utmost expectation.", "한마디로, 그녀는 그가 바랄 수 있는 최대치로 그에게 사례하겠노라 약속했다."]], [["The lad was not totally deaf to these promises; but he disliked their being indefinite; for, though perhaps he had never heard that word, yet that, in fact, was his objection. He said, “Gentlevolks did not consider the case of poor volks; that he had like to have been turned away the other day, for riding about the country with a gentleman from Squire Allworthy's, who did not reward him as he should have done.”", "그 사내아이는 이 약속들에 완전히 귀를 닫고 있지는 않았다. 다만 그 약속이 두루뭉술하다는 점이 마뜩잖았다. 비록 그 '두루뭉술하다'는 말을 들어 본 적은 없었을지 몰라도, 실은 바로 그것이 그가 못마땅해하는 까닭이었다. 그가 말했다. \"양반님네들은 가난뱅이 사정을 통 헤아려 주질 않는다니께유. 요전 날에두 올워디 나리 댁 어떤 양반을 모시구 시골길을 돌아댕기다가 하마터면 쫓겨날 뻔했구먼유. 그 양반이 제대로 사례두 안 해 주구서리.\""]], [["“With whom?”", "\"누구하고?\""], ["says Sophia eagerly.", "소피아가 다급히 물었다."], ["“With a gentleman from Squire Allworthy's,”", "\"올워디 나리 댁 어떤 양반하구유.\" 사내아이가 되풀이했다."], ["repeated the lad; “the squire's son, I think they call 'un.”--“Whither?", "\"나리 아드님이라구들 부르는 것 같던디유.\" \"어디로?"], ["which way did he go?”", "어느 길로 갔지?\" 소피아가 물었다."], ["says Sophia.--“Why, a little o'", "\"그게유, 브리스틀에서 한쪽으로 조금 빠진 데루, 한 이십 마일쯤 떨어진 데였구먼유.\" 사내아이가 대답했다."], ["one side o'", "\"나를 그 자리로 데려다 다오.\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["Bristol, about twenty miles off,”", "\"그러면 너에게 일 기니를 주마. 그것으로 모자라면 이 기니라도.\""], ["answered the lad.--“Guide me,”", "\"암요, 마님께서 제가 무릅쓰는 위험을 헤아려 주시면야 정직하게 따져 이 기니 값은 되구말구유.\" 사내아이가 말했다."], ["says Sophia, “to the same place, and I'll give thee a guinea, or two, if one is not sufficient.”--“To be certain,”", "\"허나 그래두, 마님께서 이 기니를 약속해 주신다면야 한번 무릅써 보겠습니다유. 나리 말을 끌구 돌아댕기는 건 분명 죄될 짓입지요."], ["said the boy, “it is honestly worth two, when your ladyship considers what a risk I run; but, however, if your ladyship will promise me the two guineas, I'll e'en venture: to be certain it is a sinful thing to ride about my measter's horses; but one comfort is, I can only be turned away, and two guineas will partly make me amends.”", "그래두 한 가지 위안이라면, 기껏해야 쫓겨나기밖에 더 하겠습니까. 그러면 이 기니가 어느 정도는 그 손해를 메워 주겠지유.\""]], [["The bargain being thus struck, the lad turned aside into the Bristol road, and Sophia set forward in pursuit of Jones, highly contrary to the remonstrances of Mrs Honour, who had much more desire to see London than to see Mr Jones: for indeed she was not his friend with her mistress, as he had been guilty of some neglect in certain pecuniary civilities, which are by custom due to the waiting-gentlewoman in all love affairs, and more especially in those of a clandestine kind.", "이렇게 흥정이 맺어지자, 사내아이는 브리스틀 길로 방향을 틀었고, 소피아는 존스를 뒤쫓아 길을 나섰다. 이는 어너 부인의 항변과는 한참 어긋나는 일이었으니, 그녀는 존스 씨를 보고 싶은 마음보다 런던을 보고 싶은 마음이 훨씬 더 컸던 것이다."], ["This we impute rather to the carelessness of his temper than to any want of generosity; but perhaps she derived it from the latter motive.", "사실 그녀는 여주인 앞에서 존스의 편이 아니었다. 모든 연애 사건에서, 더구나 은밀한 종류의 연애 사건에서 관례상 시중 드는 여인에게 으레 바쳐야 할 어떤 금전상의 예의를 그가 소홀히 한 죄가 있었기 때문이다."], ["Certain it is that she hated him very bitterly on that account, and resolved to take every opportunity of injuring him with her mistress.", "우리는 이것을 그의 인색함 탓이라기보다 무심한 성미 탓으로 돌리고 싶지만, 어쩌면 그녀는 그것을 인색함 탓으로 여겼을지도 모른다."], ["It was therefore highly unlucky for her, that she had gone to the very same town and inn whence Jones had started, and still more unlucky was she in having stumbled on the same guide, and on this accidental discovery which Sophia had made.", "분명한 것은, 그녀가 그 일로 그를 몹시 모질게 미워했으며, 기회 있을 때마다 여주인 앞에서 그를 깎아내리기로 작정했다는 사실이다. 그러니 그녀로서는, 하필 존스가 출발했던 바로 그 읍내, 그 여관으로 들게 된 것이 무척 운 나쁜 일이었고, 게다가 바로 그 안내인과 마주치고, 또 소피아가 우연히 알아낸 이 사실을 맞닥뜨리게 된 것은 더더욱 운 나쁜 일이었다."]], [["Our travellers arrived at Hambrook[*] at the break of day, where Honour was against her will charged to enquire the route which Mr Jones had taken.", "우리의 여행자들은 날이 밝을 무렵 햄브룩에 도착했고, 거기서 어너는 마지못해 존스 씨가 어느 길로 갔는지 알아보라는 임무를 맡았다."], ["Of this, indeed, the guide himself could have informed them; but Sophia, I know not for what reason, never asked him the question.", "사실 이런 것쯤은 안내인 자신도 일러 줄 수 있었을 테지만, 소피아는 무슨 까닭에서인지 그에게 그것을 묻는 일이 없었다."]], [["[*] This was the village where Jones met the Quaker.", "* 햄브룩: 존스가 퀘이커교도를 만났던 마을이다."]], [["When Mrs Honour had made her report from the landlord, Sophia, with much difficulty, procured some indifferent horses, which brought her to the inn where Jones had been confined rather by the misfortune of meeting with a surgeon than by having met with a broken head.", "어너 부인이 주인장에게서 알아낸 것을 보고하자, 소피아는 꽤 애를 먹은 끝에 시원찮은 말 몇 마리를 구했고, 그 말들이 그녀를 어느 여관까지 데려다주었다. 존스가 머리가 깨져서라기보다 외과의를 만나는 불운 탓에 발이 묶여 있던 바로 그 여관이었다."]], [["Here Honour, being again charged with a commission of enquiry, had no sooner applied herself to the landlady, and had described the person of Mr Jones, than that sagacious woman began, in the vulgar phrase, to smell a rat.", "여기서 어너는 다시 탐문의 임무를 맡았는데, 안주인에게 다가가 존스 씨의 인상착의를 미처 다 늘어놓기도 전에, 그 눈치 빠른 여인은 흔한 말로 수상한 낌새를 챘다. 그리하여 소피아가 방으로 들어서자, 안주인은 하녀에게 대답하는 대신 여주인 쪽을 향해 다음과 같은 말을 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["When Sophia therefore entered the room, instead of answering the maid, the landlady, addressing herself to the mistress, began the following speech: “Good lack-a-day!", "\"아이고 세상에! 이런, 글쎄, 누가 이런 일을 짐작이나 했겠어요?"], ["why there now, who would have thought it?", "단언컨대 이 눈으로 본 중에 가장 어여쁜 한 쌍이로구먼요. 정말이지, 마님, 그 도련님이 마님 얘기로 그렇게나 안달복달하셨던 게 하나도 이상할 게 없네요."], ["I protest the loveliest couple that ever eye beheld.", "사실 그분이 저더러 마님이 세상에서 제일 고운 숙녀라고 하셨거든요. 한데 정말로 그러시네요."], ["I-fackins, madam, it is no wonder the squire run on so about your ladyship.", "가엾어라, 그 도련님, 그 딱한 마음을! 그분이 베개를 끌어안고서 그걸 사랑하는 소피아 아씨라 부르던 걸 보고 제가 어찌나 가여웠는지 몰라요."], ["He told me indeed you was the finest lady in the world, and to be sure so you be.", "전쟁터에 나가지 마시라고 제가 할 수 있는 건 다 했답니다. 그런 고운 숙녀의 사랑도 받지 못하는, 죽으러 가는 것 말고는 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 사내들이 천지에 널렸다고 말씀드렸지요.\""], ["Mercy on him, poor heart!", "\"이 마음씨 좋은 분이 제정신이 아니구나.\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["I bepitied him, so I did, when he used to hug his pillow, and call it his dear Madam Sophia.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 안주인이 소리쳤다."], ["I did all I could to dissuade him from going to the wars: I told him there were men enow that were good for nothing else but to be killed, that had not the love of such fine ladies.”", "\"전 제정신이 아닌 게 아니랍니다. 아니, 마님은 제가 아무것도 모른다고 여기시나요?"], ["“Sure,” says Sophia, “the good woman is distracted.”", "장담컨대 그분이 저한테 죄다 얘기하셨어요.\""], ["“No, no,”", "\"어떤 건방진 작자가\" 어너가 소리쳤다."], ["cries the landlady, “I am not distracted.", "\"우리 아씨에 대해 뭐라도 지껄였단 말이오?\" \"건방진 작자가 아니라\""], ["What, doth your ladyship think I don't know then?", "안주인이 대답했다. \"댁이 찾던 그 젊은 도련님이지요."], ["I assure you he told me all.”", "아주 잘생긴 젊은 분이세요. 게다가 그분은 소피아 웨스턴 아씨를 영혼 밑바닥까지 사랑하신답니다.\""], ["“What saucy fellow,”", "\"그분이 우리 아씨를 사랑한다고!"], ["cries Honour, “told you anything of my lady?”", "이봐요, 잘 들어 두시오. 우리 아씨는 그분의 주인 나리께나 어울리는 분이라우.\""], ["“No saucy fellow,”", "\"이런, 어너.\""], ["answered the landlady, “but the young gentleman you enquired after, and a very pretty young gentleman he is, and he loves Madam Sophia Western to the bottom of his soul.”", "소피아가 그녀의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"이 마음씨 좋은 분에게 화내지 마라."], ["“He love my lady!", "나쁜 뜻이 있는 게 아니잖니.\""], ["I'd have you to know, woman, she is meat for his master.”--“Nay, Honour,”", "\"아무렴요, 정말 그런 뜻이 아니에요.\" 소피아의 부드러운 말투에 용기를 얻은 안주인이 대답했다."], ["said Sophia, interrupting her, “don't be angry with the good woman; she intends no harm.”", "그러고는 여기 다 옮기기엔 너무 지루한 긴 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 그 가운데 몇 마디가 소피아의 기분을 조금 상하게 했고, 그녀의 시중 드는 여인의 기분은 훨씬 더 상하게 했다. 그래서 어너는 두 사람만 남게 되자마자 이를 빌미로 여주인 앞에서 가엾은 존스를 헐뜯었다."], ["“No, marry, don't I,”", "\"그분은 틀림없이 아주 한심한 작자임에 분명해요."], ["answered the landlady, emboldened by the soft accents of Sophia; and then launched into a long narrative too tedious to be here set down, in which some passages dropt that gave a little offence to Sophia, and much more to her waiting-woman, who hence took occasion to abuse poor Jones to her mistress the moment they were alone together, saying, “that he must be a very pitiful fellow, and could have no love for a lady, whose name he would thus prostitute in an ale-house.”", "술집에서 아씨의 이름을 그렇게 함부로 입에 올려 댈 정도라면, 숙녀에게 무슨 사랑이 있을 리 없잖아요.\""]], [["Sophia did not see his behaviour in so very disadvantageous a light, and was perhaps more pleased with the violent raptures of his love (which the landlady exaggerated as much as she had done every other circumstance) than she was offended with the rest; and indeed she imputed the whole to the extravagance, or rather ebullience, of his passion, and to the openness of his heart.", "소피아는 그의 행실을 그렇게까지 나쁘게만 보지는 않았으며, 오히려 그 격렬한 사랑의 황홀경(이것도 안주인은 다른 모든 정황을 부풀린 것 못지않게 부풀려 놓았다)에 기분이 상하기보다 더 흐뭇해했는지도 모른다. 사실 그녀는 그 모든 것을 그의 격정이 지나치게 넘쳐난 탓, 아니 차라리 끓어 넘친 탓, 그리고 그의 솔직한 마음 탓으로 돌렸다."]], [["This incident, however, being afterwards revived in her mind, and placed in the most odious colours by Honour, served to heighten and give credit to those unlucky occurrences at Upton, and assisted the waiting-woman in her endeavours to make her mistress depart from that inn without seeing Jones.", "그러나 이 일은 나중에 그녀의 마음속에서 되살아나, 어너에 의해 더없이 추한 빛깔로 칠해진 채, 업튼에서 벌어진 그 불운한 사건들을 한층 키우고 그럴듯하게 만들어 주었으며, 여주인이 존스를 보지 않고 그 여관을 떠나게 하려는 시중 드는 여인의 노력에 힘을 보태 주었다."]], [["The landlady finding Sophia intended to stay no longer than till her horses were ready, and that without either eating or drinking, soon withdrew; when Honour began to take her mistress to task (for indeed she used great freedom), and after a long harangue, in which she reminded her of her intention to go to London, and gave frequent hints of the impropriety of pursuing a young fellow, she at last concluded with this serious exhortation: “For heaven's sake, madam, consider what you are about, and whither you are going.”", "소피아가 말이 준비될 때까지만, 그것도 먹지도 마시지도 않고 머물 작정임을 알게 된 안주인은 곧 물러갔다. 그러자 어너는 여주인을 나무라기 시작했고(사실 그녀는 무척 거리낌 없이 굴었다), 한참 일장 연설을 늘어놓으며 런던으로 가려던 애초의 뜻을 일깨우고, 젊은 사내를 뒤쫓는 일이 얼마나 온당치 못한지 거듭 넌지시 비춘 끝에, 마침내 이런 진지한 권고로 말을 맺었다. \"제발 부탁이에요, 아씨, 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 계신지, 또 어디로 가고 계신지 좀 생각해 보세요.\""]], [["This advice to a lady who had already rode near forty miles, and in no very agreeable season, may seem foolish enough. It may be supposed she had well considered and resolved this already; nay, Mrs Honour, by the hints she threw out, seemed to think so; and this I doubt not is the opinion of many readers, who have, I make no doubt, been long since well convinced of the purpose of our heroine, and have heartily condemned her for it as a wanton baggage.", "이미 사십 마일 가까이를, 그것도 그리 좋지도 않은 계절에 말을 달려온 숙녀에게 이런 충고는 어지간히 어리석게 들릴 법도 하다. 그녀가 이미 이것을 잘 헤아리고 마음을 굳혔으리라 짐작할 수도 있다. 아니, 어너 부인조차 자기가 흘린 말들로 미루어 그렇게 여기는 듯했다. 그리고 이것이 많은 독자의 생각이리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다. 그들은 진작에 우리 여주인공의 속셈을 훤히 알아채고는, 그 일로 그녀를 바람기 가득한 계집이라고 진심으로 나무라 왔을 테니 말이다."]], [["But in reality this was not the case.", "그러나 실은 그렇지가 않았다."], ["Sophia had been lately so distracted between hope and fear, her duty and love to her father, her hatred to Blifil, her compassion, and (why should we not confess the truth?) her love for Jones; which last the behaviour of her father, of her aunt, of every one else, and more particularly of Jones himself, had blown into a flame, that her mind was in that confused state which may be truly said to make us ignorant of what we do, or whither we go, or rather, indeed, indifferent as to the consequence of either.", "소피아는 요즈음 희망과 두려움 사이에서, 아버지에 대한 본분과 애정 사이에서, 블리필에 대한 미움과, 그 연민과, 그리고 (어찌 사실을 고백하지 않을 수 있으랴?) 존스에 대한 사랑 사이에서 너무도 마음이 어지러웠다. 그 마지막 사랑은 아버지의 행실, 고모의 행실, 그 밖의 모든 사람의 행실, 그리고 무엇보다 존스 자신의 행실로 인해 활활 불길로 타올랐던 터라, 그녀의 마음은 가히 우리가 무엇을 하는지, 어디로 가는지조차 모르게 만든다고, 아니 차라리 그 어느 쪽의 결말에도 무심하게 만든다고 할 만한 그 어지러운 상태에 빠져 있었던 것이다."]], [["The prudent and sage advice of her maid produced, however, some cool reflection; and she at length determined to go to Gloucester, and thence to proceed directly to London.", "그러나 하녀의 신중하고 사려 깊은 충고는 얼마간 냉정한 성찰을 불러일으켰고, 그녀는 마침내 글로스터로 갔다가 거기서 곧장 런던으로 향하기로 마음을 정했다."]], [["But, unluckily, a few miles before she entered that town, she met the hack-attorney, who, as is before mentioned, had dined there with Mr Jones.", "그런데 운수 사납게도, 그 도시로 들어서기 몇 마일 전에 그녀는 그 삯일꾼 변호사와 마주쳤다. 앞서 말했듯이 글로스터에서 존스 씨와 함께 식사를 했던 바로 그 사내였다."], ["This fellow, being well known to Mrs Honour, stopt and spoke to her; of which Sophia at that time took little notice, more than to enquire who he was.", "이 작자는 어너 부인과 잘 아는 사이여서, 멈춰 서서 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 소피아는 그때만 해도 그가 누구인지 물어보는 것 말고는 별로 마음에 두지 않았다."]], [["But, having had a more particular account from Honour of this man afterwards at Gloucester, and hearing of the great expedition he usually made in travelling, for which (as hath been before observed) he was particularly famous; recollecting, likewise, that she had overheard Mrs Honour inform him that they were going to Gloucester, she began to fear lest her father might, by this fellow's means, be able to trace her to that city; wherefore, if she should there strike into the London road, she apprehended he would certainly be able to overtake her. She therefore altered her resolution; and, having hired horses to go a week's journey a way which she did not intend to travel, she again set forward after a light refreshment, contrary to the desire and earnest entreaties of her maid, and to the no less vehement remonstrances of Mrs Whitefield, who, from good breeding, or perhaps from good nature (for the poor young lady appeared much fatigued), pressed her very heartily to stay that evening at Gloucester.", "하지만 나중에 글로스터에서 어너에게서 이 사내에 대한 더 자세한 이야기를 듣고, 또 (앞서 말한 대로) 그가 길을 다니는 데 유난히 빠르기로 이름났다는 말을 듣고, 게다가 어너 부인이 그에게 자기들이 글로스터로 간다고 일러 주는 것을 자기가 엿들었던 일까지 떠올리자, 그녀는 아버지가 이 작자를 통해 자기 자취를 그 도시까지 추적해 낼지도 모른다고 두려워하기 시작했다. 그러니 만일 거기서 런던 길로 들어선다면, 아버지가 틀림없이 자기를 따라잡을 수 있으리라 우려했다. 그래서 그녀는 마음을 바꾸었다. 그리하여 갈 작정도 없는 길로 일주일 거리를 갈 말을 빌린 다음, 가벼운 요기를 마치고 다시 길을 나섰다. 이는 하녀의 바람과 간절한 애원을 거스르는 일이었고, 또한 화이트필드 부인의 못지않게 격렬한 만류를 거스르는 일이기도 했다. 화이트필드 부인은 좋은 교양에서, 어쩌면 좋은 천성에서(이 가엾은 젊은 아씨가 무척 지쳐 보였기에) 그날 저녁만은 글로스터에 머물러 가라고 진심으로 권했던 것이다."]], [["Having refreshed herself only with some tea, and with lying about two hours on the bed, while her horses were getting ready, she resolutely left Mrs Whitefield's about eleven at night, and, striking directly into the Worcester road, within less than four hours arrived at that very inn where we last saw her.", "차 몇 잔으로만 기운을 차리고, 말이 준비되는 동안 두어 시간 침대에 누워 쉰 뒤, 그녀는 밤 열한 시쯤 단호히 화이트필드 부인의 여관을 나섰다. 그러고는 곧장 우스터 길로 들어서서, 네 시간이 채 못 되어 우리가 마지막으로 그녀를 보았던 바로 그 여관에 다다랐다."]], [["Having thus traced our heroine very particularly back from her departure, till her arrival at Upton, we shall in a very few words bring her father to the same place; who, having received the first scent from the post-boy, who conducted his daughter to Hambrook, very easily traced her afterwards to Gloucester; whence he pursued her to Upton, as he had learned Mr Jones had taken that route (for Partridge, to use the squire's expression, left everywhere a strong scent behind him), and he doubted not in the least but Sophia travelled, or, as he phrased it, ran, the same way. He used indeed a very coarse expression, which need not be here inserted; as fox-hunters, who alone will understand it, will easily suggest it to themselves.", "이렇게 우리의 여주인공을 떠나던 순간부터 업튼에 도착하기까지 아주 세세하게 되짚어 보았으니, 이제 몇 마디로 그녀의 아버지를 같은 곳으로 데려가 보자. 그는 딸을 햄브룩까지 데려다준 마부 소년에게서 첫 단서를 얻은 뒤, 아주 손쉽게 그녀의 자취를 글로스터까지 좇았다. 거기서 다시 그는 그녀를 업튼까지 뒤쫓았으니, 존스 씨가 그 길을 잡았다는 것을 알아냈기 때문이다(파트리지로 말하자면, 그 시골 양반의 표현을 빌리건대, 가는 곳마다 짙은 냄새를 뒤에 남기고 다녔던 것이다). 그리고 그는 소피아도, 자기 표현대로 하자면, 같은 길로 달려갔으리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 사실 그는 무척 상스러운 표현을 썼지만, 여기 굳이 옮길 필요는 없겠다. 그 말을 알아들을 사람이라곤 여우 사냥꾼들뿐일 테니, 그들이 알아서 쉬이 떠올릴 것이다."]], [["A crust for the critics.", "사흘 남짓을 담다."]], [["In our last initial chapter we may be supposed to have treated that formidable set of men who are called critics with more freedom than becomes us; since they exact, and indeed generally receive, great condescension from authors.", "지난번 첫머리 장에서 우리는 비평가라 불리는 저 만만찮은 무리를, 우리에게 어울리는 것보다 더 거리낌 없이 다룬 듯하다. 그들은 작가들로부터 큰 굽힘을 요구하고, 실제로도 대개는 그것을 받아 내곤 하니 말이다."], ["We shall in this, therefore, give the reasons of our conduct to this august body; and here we shall, perhaps, place them in a light in which they have not hitherto been seen.", "그러므로 이번 장에서는 이 위엄 있는 무리에게 우리가 그리 행동한 까닭을 밝히겠다. 그리고 여기서 우리는, 어쩌면 그들을 지금까지 한 번도 비추어진 적 없는 새로운 빛 아래 세우게 될지도 모른다."]], [["This word critic is of Greek derivation, and signifies judgment.", "이 비평가(critic)라는 말은 그리스어에서 온 것으로, 판단을 뜻한다."], ["Hence I presume some persons who have not understood the original, and have seen the English translation of the primitive, have concluded that it meant judgment in the legal sense, in which it is frequently used as equivalent to condemnation.", "그래서 나는 짐작하건대, 원어를 제대로 이해하지 못한 채 그 본디 낱말의 영어 번역만 본 어떤 이들은, 그것이 법적 의미에서의 판단을 가리킨다고 결론지었을 것이다. 그 법적 의미에서는 이 말이 흔히 유죄 선고와 같은 뜻으로 쓰인다."]], [["I am the rather inclined to be of that opinion, as the greatest number of critics hath of late years been found amongst the lawyers.", "나는 차라리 그 견해 쪽으로 기우는데, 최근 몇 해 사이 가장 많은 비평가가 법률가들 사이에서 나왔기 때문이다."], ["Many of these gentlemen, from despair, perhaps, of ever rising to the bench in Westminster-hall, have placed themselves on the benches at the playhouse, where they have exerted their judicial capacity, and have given judgment, i.e., condemned without mercy.", "이 신사들 가운데 다수는, 어쩌면 웨스트민스터 홀의 법관석에는 결코 오르지 못하리라는 절망 때문에, 극장의 좌석에 스스로 자리를 잡았다. 거기서 그들은 자신의 사법적 능력을 발휘하여 판결을 내려 왔으니, 다시 말해 인정사정없이 유죄를 선고해 온 것이다."]], [["The gentlemen would, perhaps, be well enough pleased, if we were to leave them thus compared to one of the most important and honourable offices in the commonwealth, and, if we intended to apply to their favour, we would do so; but, as we design to deal very sincerely and plainly too with them, we must remind them of another officer of justice of a much lower rank; to whom, as they not only pronounce, but execute, their own judgment, they bear likewise some remote resemblance.", "이 신사들은, 우리가 그들을 이렇게 이 나라에서 가장 중요하고 명예로운 직책 가운데 하나에 견준 채로 내버려 둔다면 아마 꽤나 흡족해할 것이다. 또 우리가 그들의 호의를 얻으려 했다면 정말 그렇게 했을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 그들을 아주 진솔하게, 게다가 가식 없이 대하려 하므로, 훨씬 낮은 지위의 또 다른 사법 집행관을 그들에게 일깨워 주어야겠다. 그들은 자기네 판결을 선고할 뿐 아니라 스스로 집행하기까지 하므로, 그 집행관과도 어느 정도 먼 닮음이 있는 것이다."]], [["But in reality there is another light, in which these modern critics may, with great justice and propriety, be seen; and this is that of a common slanderer.", "그러나 실은, 이 현대의 비평가들을 아주 정당하고 합당하게 비추어 볼 또 다른 빛이 있다. 바로 흔한 중상꾼의 모습이다."], ["If a person who prys into the characters of others, with no other design but to discover their faults, and to publish them to the world, deserves the title of a slanderer of the reputations of men, why should not a critic, who reads with the same malevolent view, be as properly stiled the slanderer of the reputation of books?", "남의 인격을 들여다보되, 오직 그 허물을 들춰내 세상에 퍼뜨릴 속셈만 품은 사람이 사람들의 명예를 헐뜯는 중상꾼이라는 이름을 받아 마땅하다면, 똑같이 악의에 찬 눈길로 책을 읽는 비평가를 어찌하여 책의 명예를 헐뜯는 중상꾼이라 부르지 못하겠는가?"]], [["Vice hath not, I believe, a more abject slave; society produces not a more odious vermin; nor can the devil receive a guest more worthy of him, nor possibly more welcome to him, than a slanderer. The world, I am afraid, regards not this monster with half the abhorrence which he deserves; and I am more afraid to assign the reason of this criminal lenity shown towards him; yet it is certain that the thief looks innocent in the comparison; nay, the murderer himself can seldom stand in competition with his guilt: for slander is a more cruel weapon than a sword, as the wounds which the former gives are always incurable. One method, indeed, there is of killing, and that the basest and most execrable of all, which bears an exact analogy to the vice here disclaimed against, and that is poison: a means of revenge so base, and yet so horrible, that it was once wisely distinguished by our laws from all other murders, in the peculiar severity of the punishment.", "내가 믿기로, 악덕에는 이보다 더 비천한 종이 없고, 사회에는 이보다 더 가증스러운 해충이 없으며, 악마조차 중상꾼보다 더 자기에게 어울리는 손님을, 아니 더없이 반가운 손님을 맞이할 수는 없을 것이다. 세상은, 안타깝게도 이 괴물을 그가 받아 마땅한 혐오의 절반만큼도 여기지 않는다. 그리고 그에게 베풀어지는 이 죄스러운 관대함의 까닭을 짚어 보기란 나로서는 더욱 두려운 일이다. 그러나 분명한 것은, 이에 견주면 도둑조차 무고해 보인다는 사실이다. 아니, 살인자라 해도 그의 죄와는 좀처럼 견줄 수 없다. 중상이란 칼보다 더 잔인한 무기여서, 그것이 입히는 상처는 언제나 낫지 않기 때문이다. 사람을 죽이는 한 가지 방법, 그것도 온갖 방법 중 가장 비열하고 가장 저주스러운 방법이 있는데, 그것은 여기서 규탄하는 악덕과 정확히 닮은꼴이다. 바로 독이다. 그것은 너무도 비열하면서도 너무도 끔찍한 복수의 수단이라, 한때 우리 법이 그 처벌의 유난한 가혹함으로써 다른 모든 살인과 슬기롭게 구별해 두었을 정도다."]], [["Besides the dreadful mischiefs done by slander, and the baseness of the means by which they are effected, there are other circumstances that highly aggravate its atrocious quality; for it often proceeds from no provocation, and seldom promises itself any reward, unless some black and infernal mind may propose a reward in the thoughts of having procured the ruin and misery of another.", "중상이 저지르는 무시무시한 해악과 그것이 이루어지는 수단의 비열함 말고도, 그 흉악한 성질을 한층 더 무겁게 만드는 다른 정황들이 있다. 중상은 흔히 아무런 도발 없이도 일어나며, 어떤 검고 지옥 같은 마음이 남의 파멸과 비참을 빚어냈다는 생각 속에서 보상을 찾는 경우가 아니라면, 좀처럼 스스로에게 어떤 보상도 약속하지 않기 때문이다."]], [["Shakespear hath nobly touched this vice, when he says--", "셰익스피어는 이 악덕을 고결하게 짚어 냈으니, 그가 이렇게 말할 때다."]], [["“Who steals my purse steals trash; 't is something, nothing; 'Twas mine, 'tis his, and hath been slave to thousands: But he that filches from me my good name Robs me of that WHICH NOT ENRICHES HIM, BUT MAKES ME POOR INDEED.”", "\"내 지갑을 훔치는 자는 쓰레기를 훔치는 것, 그것은 무언가이면서 아무것도 아니라네. 그것은 내 것이었다가 그의 것이 되고, 수천 사람의 종 노릇을 해 온 것. 그러나 내게서 나의 좋은 이름을 슬쩍 가져가는 자는 그를 부유하게 하지도 못하면서 나만을 정녕 가난하게 하는 것을 앗아 가네.\""]], [["With all this my good reader will doubtless agree; but much of it will probably seem too severe, when applied to the slanderer of books.", "이 모든 말에 너그러운 독자는 분명 동의하리라. 그러나 이 가운데 많은 부분은, 책을 헐뜯는 중상꾼에게 적용하면 아마 너무 가혹해 보일 것이다."], ["But let it here be considered that both proceed from the same wicked disposition of mind, and are alike void of the excuse of temptation.", "그러나 여기서 헤아려 보라. 둘 다 똑같이 사악한 마음의 성향에서 나오며, 똑같이 유혹이라는 변명조차 없다는 것을."], ["Nor shall we conclude the injury done this way to be very slight, when we consider a book as the author's offspring, and indeed as the child of his brain.", "또한 책을 작가의 자식으로, 실로 그의 머리에서 난 아이로 여긴다면, 이런 식으로 가해지는 해악이 결코 가볍지 않다고 결론지을 것이다."]], [["The reader who hath suffered his muse to continue hitherto in a virgin state can have but a very inadequate idea of this kind of paternal fondness.", "이제껏 자기 시신(詩神)을 처녀의 상태로 그대로 둔 독자라면, 이런 종류의 부성애를 도무지 제대로 그려 보기 어려울 것이다."], ["To such we may parody the tender exclamation of Macduff, “Alas!", "그런 이에게는 맥더프의 애틋한 탄식을 살짝 바꾸어 이렇게 말해 줄 수 있겠다. \"아아!"], ["Thou hast written no book.”", "그대는 책 한 권 써 본 적이 없구나.\""], ["But the author whose muse hath brought forth will feel the pathetic strain, perhaps will accompany me with tears (especially if his darling be already no more), while I mention the uneasiness with which the big muse bears about her burden, the painful labour with which she produces it, and, lastly, the care, the fondness, with which the tender father nourishes his favourite, till it be brought to maturity, and produced into the world.", "그러나 자기 시신이 무언가를 낳아 본 작가라면 이 애절한 가락을 느낄 것이며, 어쩌면 눈물로 나와 함께해 줄지도 모른다(더구나 그 사랑스러운 아이가 이미 세상에 없다면 더욱 그러하리라). 내가, 만삭의 시신이 그 짐을 안고 다닐 때의 거북함을, 그것을 낳을 때의 고통스러운 진통을, 그리고 마지막으로 다정한 아비가 그 아이를 다 자라 세상에 내보낼 때까지 길러 내는 그 보살핌과 애정을 이야기하는 동안 말이다."]], [["Nor is there any paternal fondness which seems less to savour of absolute instinct, and which may so well be reconciled to worldly wisdom, as this.", "또한 이만큼 순전한 본능의 냄새가 덜 나면서, 동시에 세속의 지혜와도 이토록 잘 들어맞는 부성애도 달리 없을 것이다."], ["These children may most truly be called the riches of their father; and many of them have with true filial piety fed their parent in his old age: so that not only the affection, but the interest, of the author may be highly injured by these slanderers, whose poisonous breath brings his book to an untimely end.", "이 자식들이야말로 참으로 아비의 재산이라 불릴 만하며, 그들 가운데 많은 이가 진정한 효심으로 늙은 어버이를 봉양해 왔다. 그러니 이런 중상꾼들의 독기 어린 입김이 그의 책을 때 이른 죽음으로 몰아갈 때, 작가의 애정뿐 아니라 그의 이익까지도 크게 다칠 수 있는 것이다."]], [["Lastly, the slander of a book is, in truth, the slander of the author: for, as no one can call another bastard, without calling the mother a whore, so neither can any one give the names of sad stuff, horrid nonsense, &c., to a book, without calling the author a blockhead; which, though in a moral sense it is a preferable appellation to that of villain, is perhaps rather more injurious to his worldly interest.", "마지막으로, 책에 대한 중상은 사실상 작가에 대한 중상이다. 누구든 남을 사생아라 부르려면 그 어미를 창녀라 부르지 않을 수 없듯이, 한심한 물건이니 끔찍한 헛소리니 하는 이름을 책에 붙이려면 그 작가를 멍청이라 부르지 않을 수 없기 때문이다. 멍청이라는 호칭은 도덕적 의미에서는 악당이라는 호칭보다 차라리 나을지 몰라도, 그의 세속적 이익에는 어쩌면 더 해로울 것이다."]], [["Now, however ludicrous all this may appear to some, others, I doubt not, will feel and acknowledge the truth of it; nay, may, perhaps, think I have not treated the subject with decent solemnity; but surely a man may speak truth with a smiling countenance. In reality, to depreciate a book maliciously, or even wantonly, is at least a very ill-natured office; and a morose snarling critic may, I believe, be suspected to be a bad man.", "자, 이 모든 것이 어떤 이들에게는 우습게 보일지 몰라도, 다른 이들은 그 진실을 느끼고 인정하리라는 것을 나는 의심하지 않는다. 아니, 어쩌면 내가 이 주제를 마땅한 무게로 다루지 않았다고 여길지도 모르겠다. 그러나 사람은 분명 웃는 낯으로도 진실을 말할 수 있는 법이다. 사실, 악의로, 아니 그저 장난삼아서라도 책을 깎아내리는 것은 적어도 매우 심술궂은 짓이다. 그러니 까칠하게 으르렁대는 비평가는, 내 믿기로, 나쁜 사람이 아닐까 의심받아도 좋을 것이다."]], [["I will therefore endeavour, in the remaining part of this chapter, to explain the marks of this character, and to show what criticism I here intend to obviate: for I can never be understood, unless by the very persons here meant, to insinuate that there are no proper judges of writing, or to endeavour to exclude from the commonwealth of literature any of those noble critics to whose labours the learned world are so greatly indebted.", "그러므로 나는 이 장의 남은 부분에서 이런 부류의 특징들을 밝히고, 내가 여기서 막아 내려는 비평이 어떤 것인지를 보여 주려 한다. 글을 제대로 판단할 자가 아무도 없다고 넌지시 말하거나, 그 노고에 학계가 그토록 큰 빚을 진 저 고결한 비평가들을 문학의 공화국에서 몰아내려 한다는 식으로는, 여기서 지목된 바로 그 사람들이 아니고서는 누구도 나를 오해하지 못할 것이다."], ["Such were Aristotle, Horace, and Longinus, among the antients, Dacier and Bossu among the French, and some perhaps among us; who have certainly been duly authorised to execute at least a judicial authority in foro literario.", "옛사람 가운데는 아리스토텔레스, 호라티우스, 롱기누스가, 프랑스인 가운데는 다시에와 보쉬가 그러했고, 어쩌면 우리들 가운데도 몇 사람 있을 것이다. 이들은 적어도 문학의 법정에서 사법적 권한을 행사하도록 정당하게 인정받은 이들이다."]], [["But without ascertaining all the proper qualifications of a critic, which I have touched on elsewhere, I think I may very boldly object to the censures of any one past upon works which he hath not himself read.", "그러나 비평가의 마땅한 자질을 모두 가려내지 않더라도—그 점은 다른 곳에서 다룬 바 있다—나는 자기가 직접 읽지도 않은 작품에 대해 누군가 내린 혹평에 대해서만큼은 아주 대담하게 이의를 제기할 수 있다고 본다."], ["Such censurers as these, whether they speak from their own guess or suspicion, or from the report and opinion of others, may properly be said to slander the reputation of the book they condemn.", "이런 혹평가들은, 제 짐작이나 의심에서 떠들든 남의 소문과 의견에서 떠들든, 자기가 단죄하는 책의 명예를 헐뜯는 자라고 마땅히 일컬을 수 있다."]], [["Such may likewise be suspected of deserving this character, who, without assigning any particular faults, condemn the whole in general defamatory terms; such as vile, dull, d--d stuff, &c., and particularly by the use of the monosyllable low; a word which becomes the mouth of no critic who is not RIGHT HONOURABLE.", "어떤 구체적인 허물도 짚어 내지 않으면서, 한심하다느니 따분하다느니 빌어먹을 물건이라느니 하는 두루뭉술한 비방의 말로 전체를 통째로 단죄하는 자, 특히 천하다라는 한마디를 즐겨 쓰는 자 또한 이런 부류로 의심받아 마땅하다. 이 천하다라는 말은, 진정으로 고귀한 신분이 아닌 한 어떤 비평가의 입에도 어울리지 않는 말이다."]], [["Again, though there may be some faults justly assigned in the work, yet, if those are not in the most essential parts, or if they are compensated by greater beauties, it will savour rather of the malice of a slanderer than of the judgment of a true critic to pass a severe sentence upon the whole, merely on account of some vicious part.", "다시 말하건대, 작품에 정당하게 짚을 만한 허물이 더러 있다 해도, 그것이 가장 본질적인 부분에 있는 것이 아니거나, 더 큰 아름다움으로 충분히 메워지는 것이라면, 그저 몇몇 흠 있는 대목 때문에 전체에 가혹한 판결을 내리는 것은 참된 비평가의 판단이라기보다 중상꾼의 악의에 가까울 것이다."], ["This is directly contrary to the sentiments of Horace:", "이는 호라티우스의 생각과 정면으로 어긋난다."]], [["Verum ubi plura nitent in carmine, non ego paucis Offendor maculis, quas aut incuria fudit, Aut humana parum cavit natura----", "베룸 우비 플루라 니텐트 인 카르미네, 논 에고 파우키스 오펜도르 마쿨리스, 콰스 아우트 인쿠리아 푸디트, 아우트 후마나 파룸 카비트 나투라——"]], [["But where the beauties, more in number, shine, I am not angry, when a casual line (That with some trivial faults unequal flows) A careless hand or human frailty shows.--MR FRANCIS.", "허나 아름다움이 더 많이 빛나는 곳에서는, 어쩌다 한 줄이(사소한 흠으로 고르지 못하게 흐르는 그 한 줄이) 부주의한 손길이나 인간의 연약함을 드러낸들, 나는 노여워하지 않는다.—프랜시스 씨 옮김."]], [["For, as Martial says, Aliter non fit, Avite, liber.", "마르티알리스가 말했듯, 알리테르 논 피트, 아비테, 리베르."], ["No book can be otherwise composed.", "책이란 달리 지어질 수 없는 법이다."], ["All beauty of character, as well as of countenance, and indeed of everything human, is to be tried in this manner.", "인격의 아름다움이든, 얼굴의 아름다움이든, 실로 인간의 모든 것의 아름다움이란 이런 식으로 가늠해야 한다."], ["Cruel indeed would it be if such a work as this history, which hath employed some thousands of hours in the composing, should be liable to be condemned, because some particular chapter, or perhaps chapters, may be obnoxious to very just and sensible objections.", "이 이야기와 같은 작품이, 짓는 데 수천 시간이 들어간 작품이, 어느 특정한 한 장이, 어쩌면 여러 장이 아주 정당하고 합당한 반론의 표적이 될 수 있다는 이유로 단죄당할 처지에 놓인다면, 그것은 참으로 잔인한 일일 것이다."], ["And yet nothing is more common than the most rigorous sentence upon books supported by such objections, which, if they were rightly taken (and that they are not always), do by no means go to the merit of the whole.", "그런데도 그런 반론에 기대어 책에 가장 가혹한 판결을 내리는 일만큼 흔한 것이 없다. 그 반론들이란, 설령 옳게 짚은 것이라 해도(늘 그런 것도 아니지만) 결코 작품 전체의 가치에까지 미치는 것이 아닌데도 말이다."], ["In the theatre especially, a single expression which doth not coincide with the taste of the audience, or with any individual critic of that audience, is sure to be hissed; and one scene which should be disapproved would hazard the whole piece.", "특히 극장에서는, 관객의 취향에, 또는 그 관객 가운데 어느 한 비평가의 취향에 들어맞지 않는 표현 한마디면 어김없이 야유를 받기 마련이고, 못마땅한 장면 하나가 작품 전체를 위태롭게 한다."], ["To write within such severe rules as these is as impossible as to live up to some splenetic opinions: and if we judge according to the sentiments of some critics, and of some Christians, no author will be saved in this world, and no man in the next.", "이토록 가혹한 규칙에 맞추어 글을 쓴다는 것은, 어떤 심통 사나운 견해에 맞추어 살아가는 것만큼이나 불가능한 일이다. 그리고 만일 우리가 어떤 비평가들의, 또 어떤 기독교인들의 생각대로 판단한다면, 이 세상에서 구원받을 작가도 없고, 저세상에서 구원받을 사람도 없을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["The adventures which Sophia met with after her leaving Upton.", "소피아가 어튼을 떠난 뒤에 겪은 모험들."]], [["Our history, just before it was obliged to turn about and travel backwards, had mentioned the departure of Sophia and her maid from the inn; we shall now therefore pursue the steps of that lovely creature, and leave her unworthy lover a little longer to bemoan his ill-luck, or rather his ill-conduct.", "우리 이야기는 방향을 돌려 거꾸로 거슬러 올라가야만 하기 직전에, 소피아와 그녀의 하녀가 여관을 떠났다는 사실을 언급해 두었다. 그러니 이제 우리는 그 사랑스러운 여인의 발자취를 뒤좇기로 하고, 그녀의 변변찮은 연인은 잠시 더 자신의 불운을, 아니 차라리 자신의 못난 행실을 한탄하도록 내버려 두자."]], [["Sophia having directed her guide to travel through bye-roads, across the country, they now passed the Severn, and had scarce got a mile from the inn, when the young lady, looking behind her, saw several horses coming after on full speed.", "소피아가 안내인에게 시골을 가로질러 샛길로만 가라고 일러 두었던 터라, 그들은 이제 세번강을 건넜고, 여관에서 채 일 마일도 못 갔을 무렵이었다. 그때 젊은 아가씨가 뒤를 돌아보니 여러 마리의 말이 전속력으로 뒤쫓아 오고 있었다."], ["This greatly alarmed her fears, and she called to the guide to put on as fast as possible.", "이에 그녀의 두려움이 크게 솟구쳐, 그녀는 안내인에게 할 수 있는 한 빨리 달리라고 외쳤다."]], [["He immediately obeyed her, and away they rode a full gallop.", "그는 즉시 그녀의 말을 따랐고, 두 사람은 전속력으로 말을 달렸다."], ["But the faster they went, the faster were they followed; and as the horses behind were somewhat swifter than those before, so the former were at length overtaken.", "그러나 그들이 빨리 갈수록, 뒤쫓는 자들도 그만큼 빨리 따라왔다. 그리고 뒤의 말들이 앞의 말들보다 다소 날쌨던 까닭에, 마침내 앞선 이들이 따라잡히고 말았다."], ["A happy circumstance for poor Sophia; whose fears, joined to her fatigue, had almost overpowered her spirits; but she was now instantly relieved by a female voice, that greeted her in the softest manner, and with the utmost civility.", "가엾은 소피아에게는 다행한 일이었다. 두려움에 피로까지 겹쳐 그녀의 기력은 거의 바닥난 참이었으나, 이제 더없이 부드러운 태도로, 더할 나위 없이 정중하게 그녀에게 인사를 건네는 어느 여인의 목소리에 그녀는 단번에 마음이 놓였다."], ["This greeting Sophia, as soon as she could recover her breath, with like civility, and with the highest satisfaction to herself, returned.", "소피아는 숨을 가다듬자마자 똑같이 정중하게, 그리고 스스로도 무척 흡족해하며 그 인사에 답했다."]], [["The travellers who joined Sophia, and who had given her such terror, consisted, like her own company, of two females and a guide.", "소피아 일행에 합류하여 그녀에게 그토록 큰 공포를 안겨 준 길손들은, 그녀 자신의 일행과 마찬가지로 두 여인과 한 안내인으로 이루어져 있었다. 두 무리는 누구도 다시 입을 열려 하지 않은 채 꼬박 삼 마일을 함께 나아갔다."], ["The two parties proceeded three full miles together before any one offered again to open their mouths; when our heroine, having pretty well got the better of her fear (but yet being somewhat surprized that the other still continued to attend her, as she pursued no great road, and had already passed through several turnings), accosted the strange lady in a most obliging tone, and said, “She was very happy to find they were both travelling the same way.”", "그러다 우리의 여주인공은 두려움을 어지간히 가라앉히고서도(다만 자신이 큰길로 가지 않고 이미 여러 갈림길을 지나왔는데도 상대방이 여전히 곁을 따라오는 것에 다소 의아해하며), 더없이 상냥한 어조로 낯선 부인에게 말을 건넸다. \"두 사람이 같은 길을 가게 되어 무척 기쁘다\"고 말이다."], ["The other, who, like a ghost, only wanted to be spoke to, readily answered, “That the happiness was entirely hers; that she was a perfect stranger in that country, and was so overjoyed at meeting a companion of her own sex, that she had perhaps been guilty of an impertinence, which required great apology, in keeping pace with her.”", "마치 유령처럼 그저 말을 걸어 주기만을 기다리던 상대편은 선뜻 대답했다. \"기쁨이라면 온전히 자기 것이며, 자기는 이 고장에 아주 낯선 사람인데 같은 여자 동행을 만나게 되어 어찌나 기쁜지, 어쩌면 큰 사과를 받아야 마땅할 만큼 무례하게 그녀와 보조를 맞추어 따라온 것일지도 모르겠다\"고."], ["More civilities passed between these two ladies; for Mrs Honour had now given place to the fine habit of the stranger, and had fallen into the rear.", "이 두 부인 사이에는 더 많은 인사치레가 오갔다. 그도 그럴 것이, 아너 부인은 이제 낯선 여인의 고운 차림새에 자리를 내주고 뒤로 물러나 있었기 때문이다."], ["But, though Sophia had great curiosity to know why the other lady continued to travel on through the same bye-roads with herself, nay, though this gave her some uneasiness, yet fear, or modesty, or some other consideration, restrained her from asking the question.", "그러나 소피아는 저 부인이 어째서 자기와 똑같은 샛길로 줄곧 따라오는지 무척이나 궁금했고, 아니 그 일이 다소 마음에 걸리기까지 했으나, 두려움 때문인지 수줍음 때문인지 아니면 또 다른 무슨 헤아림 때문인지, 차마 그 질문을 입에 올리지는 못했다."]], [["The strange lady now laboured under a difficulty which appears almost below the dignity of history to mention.", "이제 낯선 부인은 차마 역사의 품위에 어울리지 않을 만큼 사소해 보이는 곤란을 겪고 있었다."], ["Her bonnet had been blown from her head not less than five times within the last mile; nor could she come at any ribbon or handkerchief to tie it under her chin.", "지난 일 마일 사이에 그녀의 모자가 머리에서 적어도 다섯 번이나 바람에 날아갔는데, 그것을 턱 밑에 묶을 리본이나 손수건이라곤 도무지 손에 닿지 않았던 것이다. 이 사정을 알게 된 소피아는 곧바로 그 용도로 쓰라며 손수건 하나를 건네주었다."], ["When Sophia was informed of this, she immediately supplied her with a handkerchief for this purpose; which while she was pulling from her pocket, she perhaps too much neglected the management of her horse, for the beast, now unluckily making a false step, fell upon his fore-legs, and threw his fair rider from his back.", "그런데 손수건을 주머니에서 꺼내느라 아마도 말 다루는 데 너무 소홀했던 모양이다. 마침 불운하게도 짐승이 발을 헛디뎌 앞다리로 고꾸라지면서, 등에 탄 고운 주인을 떨어뜨리고 말았다."]], [["Though Sophia came head foremost to the ground, she happily received not the least damage: and the same circumstances which had perhaps contributed to her fall now preserved her from confusion; for the lane which they were then passing was narrow, and very much overgrown with trees, so that the moon could here afford very little light, and was moreover, at present, so obscured in a cloud, that it was almost perfectly dark.", "소피아는 머리부터 땅에 떨어졌으나 다행히 조금도 다치지 않았다. 그리고 어쩌면 낙마의 원인이 되었을 바로 그 사정이 이번에는 그녀를 망신에서 구해 주었다."], ["By these means the young lady's modesty, which was extremely delicate, escaped as free from injury as her limbs, and she was once more reinstated in her saddle, having received no other harm than a little fright by her fall.", "그들이 지나던 오솔길은 좁은 데다 나무가 빽빽이 우거져, 달빛이 이곳까지는 거의 들지 못했고, 게다가 마침 달이 구름에 가려 거의 완전한 어둠에 잠겨 있었기 때문이다. 이런 까닭에 더없이 섬세했던 이 젊은 아가씨의 정숙함은 그녀의 사지만큼이나 멀쩡히 상처를 면했고, 그녀는 낙마로 인한 약간의 놀라움 외에는 아무런 해도 입지 않은 채 다시 안장에 올랐다."]], [["Daylight at length appeared in its full lustre; and now the two ladies, who were riding over a common side by side, looking stedfastly at each other, at the same moment both their eyes became fixed; both their horses stopt, and, both speaking together, with equal joy pronounced, the one the name of Sophia, the other that of Harriet.", "마침내 날이 환히 밝아 왔다. 이제 두 부인은 들판을 나란히 말을 몰아가다가 서로를 가만히 바라보았는데, 바로 그 순간 두 사람의 눈이 동시에 한 곳에 멎었다. 두 사람의 말도 멈췄고, 두 사람은 함께 입을 열어 똑같은 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다. 한쪽은 소피아의 이름을, 다른 한쪽은 해리엇의 이름을."]], [["This unexpected encounter surprized the ladies much more than I believe it will the sagacious reader, who must have imagined that the strange lady could be no other than Mrs Fitzpatrick, the cousin of Miss Western, whom we before mentioned to have sallied from the inn a few minutes after her.", "이 뜻밖의 만남은 두 부인을 무척이나 놀라게 했으나, 영민한 독자께서는 이만큼 놀라지 않으셨으리라 믿는다. 그 낯선 부인이란 다름 아닌 피츠패트릭 부인, 곧 웨스턴 양의 사촌일 수밖에 없음을 짐작하셨을 테니 말이다. 우리는 앞서 그녀가 소피아보다 몇 분 늦게 여관을 박차고 나섰다고 일러 둔 바 있다."]], [["So great was the surprize and joy which these two cousins conceived at this meeting (for they had formerly been most intimate acquaintance and friends, and had long lived together with their aunt Western), that it is impossible to recount half the congratulations which passed between them, before either asked a very natural question of the other, namely, whither she was going?", "이 두 사촌이 이 만남에서 느낀 놀라움과 기쁨이 어찌나 컸던지(그도 그럴 것이 두 사람은 예전에 더없이 가까운 친지이자 벗이었고, 오랫동안 웨스턴 고모 댁에서 함께 살았던 사이였다), 두 사람 사이에 오간 축하의 말을 절반도 다 옮기기란 불가능할 지경이었다. 그러고 나서야 비로소 한 사람이 다른 사람에게 지극히 자연스러운 질문을 던졌으니, 곧 어디로 가는 길이냐는 것이었다."]], [["This at last, however, came first from Mrs Fitzpatrick; but, easy and natural as the question may seem, Sophia found it difficult to give it a very ready and certain answer. She begged her cousin therefore to suspend all curiosity till they arrived at some inn, “which I suppose,” says she, “can hardly be far distant; and, believe me, Harriet, I suspend as much curiosity on my side; for, indeed, I believe our astonishment is pretty equal.”", "그러나 이 질문은 마침내 피츠패트릭 부인의 입에서 먼저 나왔다. 다만 손쉽고 자연스러워 보이는 질문이었음에도, 소피아는 거기에 선뜻 분명한 답을 내놓기가 어려웠다. 그리하여 그녀는 사촌에게, 어느 여관에 닿을 때까지는 모든 궁금증을 잠시 미뤄 달라고 청했다. \"여관이라면 여기서 멀지 않으리라 싶어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 해리엇, 내 말을 믿어요, 나도 내 쪽 궁금증을 그만큼 미뤄 두고 있으니까요. 사실 우리 둘의 놀라움이 어슷비슷하리라 싶거든요.\""]], [["The conversation which passed between these ladies on the road was, I apprehend, little worth relating; and less certainly was that between the two waiting-women; for they likewise began to pay their compliments to each other.", "이 두 부인이 길에서 나눈 대화는, 내 짐작으로는 굳이 옮길 만한 값어치가 별로 없다. 두 시중드는 여인들 사이에 오간 이야기는 더더욱 그러하다."], ["As for the guides, they were debarred from the pleasure of discourse, the one being placed in the van, and the other obliged to bring up the rear.", "그들 역시 서로에게 인사치레를 차리기 시작했던 것이다. 안내인들로 말하자면, 한 사람은 맨 앞에 서고 다른 한 사람은 맨 뒤에 따라가야 했던 탓에, 이야기를 나누는 즐거움일랑 누리지 못했다."]], [["In this posture they travelled many hours, till they came into a wide and well-beaten road, which, as they turned to the right, soon brought them to a very fair promising inn, where they all alighted: but so fatigued was Sophia, that as she had sat her horse during the last five or six miles with great difficulty, so was she now incapable of dismounting from him without assistance. This the landlord, who had hold of her horse, presently perceiving, offered to lift her in his arms from her saddle; and she too readily accepted the tender of his service. Indeed fortune seems to have resolved to put Sophia to the blush that day, and the second malicious attempt succeeded better than the first; for my landlord had no sooner received the young lady in his arms, than his feet, which the gout had lately very severely handled, gave way, and down he tumbled; but, at the same time, with no less dexterity than gallantry, contrived to throw himself under his charming burden, so that he alone received any bruise from the fall; for the great injury which happened to Sophia was a violent shock given to her modesty by an immoderate grin, which, at her rising from the ground, she observed in the countenances of most of the bye-standers. This made her suspect what had really happened, and what we shall not here relate for the indulgence of those readers who are capable of laughing at the offence given to a young lady's delicacy. Accidents of this kind we have never regarded in a comical light; nor will we scruple to say that he must have a very inadequate idea of the modesty of a beautiful young woman, who would wish to sacrifice it to so paltry a satisfaction as can arise from laughter.", "이런 형국으로 그들은 여러 시간을 달려, 마침내 넓고 잘 다져진 길로 들어섰다. 그 길에서 오른쪽으로 꺾어 들자 곧 보기에도 그럴듯한 여관에 닿았고, 일행은 모두 말에서 내렸다. 그런데 소피아는 어찌나 지쳤던지, 지난 대여섯 마일을 무척 힘겹게 말 위에 버티고 앉아 왔던 터라, 이제는 부축 없이는 도저히 말에서 내릴 수가 없었다. 그녀의 말고삐를 잡고 있던 여관 주인이 이를 곧 알아채고는 자기 팔로 그녀를 안장에서 받아 내리겠노라 청했고, 그녀도 그 친절한 제안을 선뜻 받아들였다. 실로 운명은 그날 소피아의 얼굴을 붉히게 하기로 작정한 듯했다. 두 번째의 짓궂은 시도는 첫 번째보다 더 잘 들어맞았다. 여관 주인이 젊은 아가씨를 두 팔로 받아 안기가 무섭게, 근래 통풍으로 몹시 시달리던 그의 두 발이 풀썩 꺾이며 그가 나동그라지고 만 것이다. 그러나 동시에 그는 늠름함 못지않은 날쌘 솜씨로 자기 몸을 그 어여쁜 짐 밑으로 던져 넣었으니, 떨어지면서 멍이 든 것은 오직 그뿐이었다. 소피아에게 닥친 큰 해라면, 땅에서 일어설 때 곁에 둘러선 사람들 대부분의 얼굴에서 그녀가 본 가당찮은 히죽거림이 그녀의 정숙함에 안긴 격렬한 충격이었다. 이로 인해 그녀는 실제로 무슨 일이 일어났는지를 의심하게 되었는데, 그것이 무엇인지는 젊은 아가씨의 섬세함이 입은 모욕을 두고 웃을 줄 아는 독자들의 흥을 돋우자고 여기 굳이 옮기지는 않겠다. 우리는 이런 종류의 사고를 결코 우스운 일로 본 적이 없다. 그리고 한 아름다운 젊은 여인의 정숙함을, 고작 웃음에서나 얻을 수 있는 그토록 하찮은 만족과 맞바꾸려는 자가 있다면, 그는 그 정숙함이 무엇인지를 지극히 모자라게 헤아리는 사람임에 틀림없다고 우리는 거리낌 없이 말하겠다."]], [["This fright and shock, joined to the violent fatigue which both her mind and body had undergone, almost overcame the excellent constitution of Sophia, and she had scarce strength sufficient to totter into the inn, leaning on the arm of her maid.", "이 놀라움과 충격에, 그녀의 마음과 몸이 함께 겪은 격심한 피로까지 겹치니, 소피아의 빼어난 기력마저 거의 무너질 지경이었다. 그녀는 하녀의 팔에 기댄 채 비틀거리며 가까스로 여관 안으로 들어갈 힘밖에 남아 있지 않았다."], ["Here she was no sooner seated than she called for a glass of water; but Mrs Honour, very judiciously, in my opinion, changed it into a glass of wine.", "자리에 앉기가 무섭게 그녀는 물 한 잔을 청했으나, 내가 보기엔 무척 현명하게도, 아너 부인이 그것을 포도주 한 잔으로 바꿔 놓았다."]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick, hearing from Mrs Honour that Sophia had not been in bed during the two last nights, and observing her to look very pale and wan with her fatigue, earnestly entreated her to refresh herself with some sleep.", "피츠패트릭 부인은 소피아가 지난 이틀 밤을 잠자리에 들지 못했다는 말을 아너 부인에게서 듣고, 또 그녀가 피로로 몹시 창백하고 핼쑥해진 것을 보고는, 한숨 자며 기운을 차리라고 간곡히 권했다. 부인은 아직 소피아의 사연도, 그녀의 근심도 알지 못했다."], ["She was yet a stranger to her history, or her apprehensions; but, had she known both, she would have given the same advice; for rest was visibly necessary for her; and their long journey through bye-roads so entirely removed all danger of pursuit, that she was herself perfectly easy on that account.", "그러나 설령 둘 다 알았더라도 똑같이 권했을 것이다. 소피아에게 휴식이 절실히 필요함은 눈에 뻔히 보였고, 또 그들이 샛길로 그 먼 길을 돌아온 덕분에 추격의 위험이 깡그리 사라져, 그 점에서는 부인 자신도 마음이 더없이 편했기 때문이다."]], [["Sophia was easily prevailed on to follow the counsel of her friend, which was heartily seconded by her maid.", "소피아는 어렵잖게 친구의 권유를 따르기로 했고, 그 권유에는 하녀도 진심으로 거들었다."], ["Mrs Fitzpatrick likewise offered to bear her cousin company, which Sophia, with much complacence, accepted.", "피츠패트릭 부인 역시 사촌의 곁을 지켜 주겠노라 청했으니, 소피아는 무척 흐뭇하게 그 제안을 받아들였다."]], [["The mistress was no sooner in bed than the maid prepared to follow her example.", "여주인이 잠자리에 들기가 무섭게 하녀도 그 본을 따를 채비를 했다."], ["She began to make many apologies to her sister Abigail for leaving her alone in so horrid a place as an inn; but the other stopt her short, being as well inclined to a nap as herself, and desired the honour of being her bedfellow.", "그녀는 여관처럼 끔찍한 곳에 자기 자매뻘 되는 시녀를 홀로 두고 가는 것에 대해 거듭 사과를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그러나 상대방은 자기도 한잠 자고 싶기는 매한가지인지라 그 말을 가로막고는, 함께 자리를 같이하는 영광을 달라고 청했다."], ["Sophia's maid agreed to give her a share of her bed, but put in her claim to all the honour.", "소피아의 하녀는 자기 잠자리를 나눠 주는 데 동의하면서도, 그 영광은 온전히 제 몫이라 우겼다."], ["So, after many courtsies and compliments, to bed together went the waiting-women, as their mistresses had done before them.", "그리하여 숱한 절과 인사치레가 오간 뒤, 시중드는 여인들도 앞서 그들의 여주인들이 그랬듯 함께 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["It was usual with my landlord (as indeed it is with the whole fraternity) to enquire particularly of all coachmen, footmen, postboys, and others, into the names of all his guests; what their estate was, and where it lay.", "우리 여관 주인은(실은 그 업종 사람들이 다 그러하듯) 모든 마부와 하인, 파발꾼 따위에게 손님들의 이름이며, 그들의 재산은 어느 정도이고 어디에 있는지를 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 버릇이 있었다."], ["It cannot therefore be wondered at that the many particular circumstances which attended our travellers, and especially their retiring all to sleep at so extraordinary and unusual an hour as ten in the morning, should excite his curiosity.", "그러니 우리 길손들에게 따라붙은 그 숱한 별스러운 사정들, 특히 아침 열 시라는 유난스럽고 별난 시각에 다들 잠자리에 들어 버린 일이 그의 호기심을 부추긴 것도 이상할 게 없었다."], ["As soon, therefore, as the guides entered the kitchen, he began to examine who the ladies were, and whence they came; but the guides, though they faithfully related all they knew, gave him very little satisfaction.", "그리하여 안내인들이 부엌에 들어서자마자 그는 저 부인들이 누구이며 어디서 왔는지 따져 묻기 시작했다. 그러나 안내인들은 자기들이 아는 바를 충실히 일러 주었음에도, 그의 호기심을 거의 채워 주지 못했다."], ["On the contrary, they rather enflamed his curiosity than extinguished it.", "오히려 그의 호기심을 잠재우기는커녕 더욱 부채질했을 뿐이다."]], [["This landlord had the character, among all his neighbours, of being a very sagacious fellow.", "이 여관 주인은 이웃들 사이에서 무척 영민한 사람이라는 평을 듣고 있었다. 사람들은 그가 교구의 어느 누구보다, 심지어 목사보다도 더 멀리, 더 깊이 사물을 꿰뚫어 본다고 여겼다."], ["He was thought to see farther and deeper into things than any man in the parish, the parson himself not excepted.", "어쩌면 그의 인상이 이런 평판을 얻는 데 적잖이 한몫했을 것이다. 그 인상에는 어딘가 놀랍도록 현명하고 의미심장한 데가 있었는데, 특히 입에 담뱃대를 물고 있을 때 그러했고, 사실 그는 담뱃대를 물지 않은 적이 거의 없었다."], ["Perhaps his look had contributed not a little to procure him this reputation; for there was in this something wonderfully wise and significant, especially when he had a pipe in his mouth; which, indeed, he seldom was without.", "그의 처신 또한 그가 지혜롭다는 평판을 키우는 데 크게 거들었다. 거동에 있어 그는 시무룩하다고까지는 못해도 자못 근엄했고, 좀처럼 말을 하지 않았으되 입을 열 때면 늘 느릿한 목소리로 말했다."], ["His behaviour, likewise, greatly assisted in promoting the opinion of his wisdom.", "게다가 그 문장이 짧으면서도, \"흠\"이며 \"하\" 하는 소리, \"어이\""], ["In his deportment he was solemn, if not sullen; and when he spoke, which was seldom, he always delivered himself in a slow voice; and, though his sentences were short, they were still interrupted with many hums and ha's, ay ays, and other expletives: so that, though he accompanied his words with certain explanatory gestures, such as shaking or nodding the head, or pointing with his fore-finger, he generally left his hearers to understand more than he expressed; nay, he commonly gave them a hint that he knew much more than he thought proper to disclose.", "하는 추임새, 그 밖의 군소리로 거듭 끊기곤 했다. 그래서 비록 그가 머리를 흔들거나 끄덕이는 일, 검지로 가리키는 일 따위의 설명조 몸짓을 말에 곁들이기는 했어도, 그는 대개 자기가 입에 담은 것보다 더 많은 것을 듣는 이들이 알아채도록 내버려 두었다."], ["This last circumstance alone may, indeed, very well account for his character of wisdom; since men are strangely inclined to worship what they do not understand.", "아니, 그는 으레 자기가 적당하다고 여겨 털어놓는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을 알고 있다는 낌새를 풍기곤 했다. 실로 이 마지막 한 가지만으로도 그가 지혜롭다는 평판을 얻은 까닭이 넉넉히 설명될 법하다."], ["A grand secret, upon which several imposers on mankind have totally relied for the success of their frauds.", "사람이란 자기가 이해하지 못하는 것을 떠받들고 싶어 하는 야릇한 성향이 있으니 말이다. 이것이야말로 인류를 속여 온 여러 사기꾼들이 그 사기의 성공을 온전히 기대어 온 어떤 거대한 비결이다."]], [["This polite person, now taking his wife aside, asked her “what she thought of the ladies lately arrived?”", "이 점잖으신 양반은 이제 아내를 한쪽으로 데려가더니, \"근래 도착한 부인들을 어찌 보느냐\"고 물었다. \"그분들을 어찌 보느냐고요?\""], ["“Think of them?”", "아내가 말했다."], ["said the wife, “why, what should I think of them?”", "\"원, 제가 그분들을 어찌 봐야 한단 말이에요?\" \"나는 알지.\""], ["“I know,”", "그가 대답했다."], ["answered he, “what I think.", "\"내가 어찌 보는지는 말이야."], ["The guides tell strange stories.", "안내인들이 묘한 소리를 늘어놓더군."], ["One pretends to be come from Gloucester, and the other from Upton; and neither of them, for what I can find, can tell whither they are going.", "하나는 글로스터에서 왔다 하고, 다른 하나는 어튼에서 왔다는데, 내가 알아낼 수 있는 한, 둘 다 그분들이 어디로 가는지는 말을 못 해. 한데 어튼에서 여기까지, 게다가 런던으로 가겠다고 시골을 가로질러 다니는 사람이 대체 어디 있느냔 말이야."], ["But what people ever travel across the country from Upton hither, especially to London?", "그리고 하녀 하나는 말에서 내리기 전에, 여기가 런던 가는 길이 아니냐고 묻더란 말이지."], ["And one of the maid-servants, before she alighted from her horse, asked if this was not the London road?", "자, 내가 이 모든 사정을 한데 모아 봤는데, 그분들이 누구일 거라 알아냈는지 짐작이나 가나?\" \"원, 참.\""], ["Now I have put all these circumstances together, and whom do you think I have found them out to be?”", "아내가 대답했다. \"제가 당신이 알아내신 걸 함부로 넘겨짚는 법이 없는 줄 잘 아시잖아요.\""], ["“Nay,”", "\"착한 사람이지.\""], ["answered she, “you know I never pretend to guess at your discoveries.”----“It is a good girl,”", "그가 아내의 턱을 살짝 치켜올리며 대꾸했다. \"이런 일에 관한 한 당신이 늘 내 식견에 따라 줬다는 건 나도 인정하지."], ["replied he, chucking her under the chin; “I must own you have always submitted to my knowledge of these matters.", "그러니 단단히 믿어 두라고. 내 말 잘 들어 — 틀림없어, 그분들은 분명 그 반란군 부인들 가운데 일부야."], ["Why, then, depend upon it; mind what I say--depend upon it, they are certainly some of the rebel ladies, who, they say, travel with the young Chevalier; and have taken a roundabout way to escape the duke's army.”", "그 젊은 기사 나리와 함께 다닌다는 사람들 말이야. 공작님 군대를 피하느라 빙 둘러 가는 길을 잡은 거라고.\""]], [["“Husband,” quoth the wife, “you have certainly hit it; for one of them is dressed as fine as any princess; and, to be sure, she looks for all the world like one.----But yet, when I consider one thing”----“When you consider,” cries the landlord contemptuously----“Come, pray let's hear what you consider.”----“Why, it is,” answered the wife, “that she is too humble to be any very great lady: for, while our Betty was warming the bed, she called her nothing but child, and my dear, and sweetheart; and, when Betty offered to pull off her shoes and stockings, she would not suffer her, saying, she would not give her the trouble.”", "\"여보.\" 아내가 말했다. \"당신 분명 제대로 짚으셨어요. 그분들 중 하나는 어느 공주님 못지않게 곱게 차려입었던 데다, 정말이지 영락없는 공주님 같았으니까요. 하지만 그래도 한 가지를 곰곰 생각해 보면요—\" \"당신이 곰곰 생각을 한다.\" 여관 주인이 깔보듯 외쳤다. \"어디 한번, 당신이 곰곰 생각한 걸 들어나 봅시다.\" \"아니, 그게요.\" 아내가 대답했다. \"그분이 그리 대단한 귀부인이라기엔 너무 겸손하시단 말이에요. 우리 베티가 잠자리를 데우는 동안에도, 그분은 베티더러 그저 '얘야' '아가' '예쁜이' 라고만 부르셨고, 베티가 신발이며 양말을 벗겨 드리겠다고 했을 때도 한사코 못 하게 하시면서, 수고를 끼칠 수는 없다고 하셨거든요.\""]], [["“Pugh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["answered the husband, “that is nothing.", "남편이 대꾸했다. \"그까짓 게 무슨 대수라고."], ["Dost think, because you have seen some great ladies rude and uncivil to persons below them, that none of them know how to behave themselves when they come before their inferiors?", "당신이 어떤 귀부인들이 아랫사람들에게 무례하고 막되게 구는 꼴을 좀 봤다고 해서, 귀부인 가운데 아랫사람 앞에서 처신할 줄 아는 이가 하나도 없을 줄 아나?"], ["I think I know people of fashion when I see them--I think I do.", "나는 멋쟁이 양반들을 보면 알아본다고 — 암, 알아보고말고."], ["Did not she call for a glass of water when she came in?", "그분이 들어오자마자 물 한 잔을 청하지 않았나?"], ["Another sort of women would have called for a dram; you know they would.", "딴 부류의 여자들 같았으면 독한 술 한 잔을 청했을 거야 — 당신도 알잖아, 그랬을 거라고."], ["If she be not a woman of very great quality, sell me for a fool; and, I believe, those who buy me will have a bad bargain.", "만약 저분이 더없이 지체 높은 귀부인이 아니라면, 나를 바보로 팔아넘기라지. 한데 나를 사는 사람은 손해 보는 흥정을 한 셈일 거야."], ["Now, would a woman of her quality travel without a footman, unless upon some such extraordinary occasion?”", "자, 저만한 지체의 여자가 이런 별난 사정이 아니고서야 하인 하나 없이 길을 다니겠어?\" \"원, 그야 분명 그렇겠죠, 여보.\""], ["“Nay, to be sure, husband,”", "아내가 외쳤다."], ["cries she, “you know these matters better than I, or most folk.”", "\"당신은 이런 일을 나보다, 또 웬만한 사람들보다 더 잘 아시니까요.\""], ["“I think I do know something,”", "\"내가 뭐 좀 안다 싶긴 하지.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“To be sure,”", "\"정말이지.\""], ["answered the wife, “the poor little heart looked so piteous, when she sat down in the chair, I protest I could not help having a compassion for her almost as much as if she had been a poor body.", "아내가 대답했다. \"그 가엾고 조그만 분이 의자에 앉을 때 어찌나 애처로워 보이던지, 맹세코 그분이 가난한 사람이기라도 한 양 측은한 마음을 누를 수가 없더라고요."], ["But what's to be done, husband?", "한데 어쩌실 거예요, 여보?"], ["If an she be a rebel, I suppose you intend to betray her up to the court.", "만약 그분이 반란군이라면, 당신은 그분을 관아에 넘겨 일러바칠 작정이시겠죠."], ["Well, she's a sweet-tempered, good-humoured lady, be she what she will, and I shall hardly refrain from crying when I hear she is hanged or beheaded.”", "글쎄, 누구든 간에 그분은 천성이 곱고 마음씨 좋은 분이라, 그분이 교수형을 당했다거나 목이 잘렸다는 소리를 들으면 나는 울음을 참기 어려울 거예요.\""], ["“Pooh!”", "\"쳇!\""], ["answered the husband.----“But, as to what's to be done, it is not so easy a matter to determine.", "남편이 대답했다. \"한데 어찌해야 하느냐 하는 문제는 그리 쉽게 정할 일이 아니야."], ["I hope, before she goes away, we shall have the news of a battle; for, if the Chevalier should get the better, she may gain us interest at court, and make our fortunes without betraying her.”", "그분이 떠나기 전에 전투 소식이 들려오면 좋으련만. 그 기사 나리 쪽이 이기기라도 하면, 저분이 우리한테 궁정의 연줄을 마련해 주어, 굳이 일러바치지 않고도 우리 살림을 일으켜 줄지도 모르거든.\""], ["“Why, that's true,”", "\"원, 그건 옳은 말씀이에요.\""], ["replied the wife; “and I heartily hope she will have it in her power.", "아내가 대꾸했다. \"그리고 저분이 그럴 힘을 갖게 되시길 진심으로 바라요."], ["Certainly she's a sweet good lady; it would go horribly against me to have her come to any harm.”", "정말이지 그분은 곱고 착한 부인이라, 그분에게 무슨 해라도 닥치는 건 나로선 끔찍이도 싫거든요.\""], ["“Pooh!”", "\"쳇!\""], ["cries the landlord, “women are always so tender-hearted.", "여관 주인이 외쳤다. \"여편네들이란 늘 마음이 그리 무르다니까."], ["Why, you would not harbour rebels, would you?”", "원, 당신 설마 반란군을 숨겨 줄 작정은 아니겠지?\""], ["“No, certainly,”", "\"아뇨, 그럴 리가요.\""], ["answered the wife; “and as for betraying her, come what will on't, nobody can blame us.", "아내가 대답했다. \"그리고 일러바치는 일이라면, 어찌 되든 누구도 우릴 탓할 수 없을 거예요."], ["It is what anybody would do in our case.”", "우리 처지라면 누구라도 할 만한 일이니까요.\""]], [["While our politic landlord, who had not, we see, undeservedly the reputation of great wisdom among his neighbours, was engaged in debating this matter with himself (for he paid little attention to the opinion of his wife), news arrived that the rebels had given the duke the slip, and had got a day's march towards London; and soon after arrived a famous Jacobite squire, who, with great joy in his countenance, shook the landlord by the hand, saying, “All's our own, boy, ten thousand honest Frenchmen are landed in Suffolk.", "이렇듯 우리의 약삭빠른 여관 주인이—보다시피 이웃들 사이에서 큰 지혜로 이름을 떨친 것이 괜한 일은 아니었으니—이 문제를 홀로 곰곰이 따지는 동안(그는 아내의 의견에는 별 귀를 기울이지 않았으므로), 반란군이 공작을 따돌리고 런던 쪽으로 하루 거리를 앞질러 진군했다는 소식이 당도했다. 그리고 곧이어 이름난 자코바이트 지주 한 사람이 들이닥쳤는데, 그는 얼굴에 큰 기쁨을 띤 채 여관 주인의 손을 부여잡으며 말했다."], ["Old England for ever!", "\"다 우리 차지가 됐네, 이 사람아."], ["ten thousand French, my brave lad!", "정직한 프랑스 사람 만 명이 서퍽에 상륙했다네. 옛 잉글랜드 만세!"], ["I am going to tap away directly.”", "프랑스 사람 만 명일세, 이 용감한 친구야! 나는 곧장 술통을 따러 가는 길일세.\""]], [["This news determined the opinion of the wise man, and he resolved to make his court to the young lady when she arose; for he had now (he said) discovered that she was no other than Madam Jenny Cameron herself.", "이 소식이 그 현명한 양반의 판단을 굳혀 놓았으니, 그는 젊은 아가씨가 일어나거든 그녀의 환심을 사기로 마음먹었다. 이제 그는(스스로 말하기를) 그녀가 다름 아닌 제니 캐머런 마님 바로 그 사람임을 알아냈다고 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["A very short chapter, in which however is a sun, a moon, a star, and an angel.", "아주 짧은 장이지만, 그럼에도 그 안에는 해와 달과 별, 그리고 천사가 들어 있다."]], [["The sun (for he keeps very good hours at this time of the year) had been some time retired to rest when Sophia arose greatly refreshed by her sleep; which, short as it was, nothing but her extreme fatigue could have occasioned; for, though she had told her maid, and perhaps herself too, that she was perfectly easy when she left Upton, yet it is certain her mind was a little affected with that malady which is attended with all the restless symptoms of a fever, and is perhaps the very distemper which physicians mean (if they mean anything) by the fever on the spirits.", "해는(한 해의 이맘때면 무척 규칙적인 시간을 지키는 법이다) 이미 한참 전에 잠자리에 들었을 무렵, 소피아는 잠으로 한결 개운해진 몸을 일으켰다. 그토록 짧은 잠이었으나, 그렇게라도 잠들 수 있었던 것은 오로지 극심한 피로 덕분이었다. 그녀는 어퍼튼을 떠날 때 자신이 더없이 편안하다고 하녀에게, 어쩌면 스스로에게도 말했지만, 사실 그녀의 마음은 그 병에 조금쯤 시달리고 있었던 것이다. 열병에 따라붙는 온갖 안절부절못하는 증세를 동반하는 그 병, 어쩌면 의사들이(그들이 무언가를 뜻하는 것이라면) 정신의 열병이라 부르는 바로 그 병 말이다."]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick likewise left her bed at the same time; and, having summoned her maid, immediately dressed herself.", "피츠패트릭 부인도 같은 시각에 잠자리에서 일어났고, 하녀를 불러 곧장 옷을 차려입었다. 그녀는 정말이지 무척 어여쁜 여인이어서, 소피아만 곁에 없었더라면 아름답다는 소리를 들었을 만했다."], ["She was really a very pretty woman, and, had she been in any other company but that of Sophia, might have been thought beautiful; but when Mrs Honour of her own accord attended (for her mistress would not suffer her to be waked), and had equipped our heroine, the charms of Mrs Fitzpatrick, who had performed the office of the morning-star, and had preceded greater glories, shared the fate of that star, and were totally eclipsed the moment those glories shone forth.", "그러나 어너 부인이 제 발로 시중을 들러 와서는(소피아가 그녀를 깨우지 못하게 한 까닭이었다) 우리의 여주인공을 단장해 놓자, 새벽별 노릇을 하며 더 큰 광채에 앞서 떠올랐던 피츠패트릭 부인의 매력은 그 별의 운명을 그대로 따랐다. 더 큰 광채가 빛을 발하는 순간 완전히 가려져 버린 것이다."]], [["Perhaps Sophia never looked more beautiful than she did at this instant.", "아마 소피아가 이 순간보다 더 아름다워 보인 적은 없었으리라."], ["We ought not, therefore, to condemn the maid of the inn for her hyperbole, who, when she descended, after having lighted the fire, declared, and ratified it with an oath, that if ever there was an angel upon earth, she was now above-stairs.", "그러니 여관의 하녀가 늘어놓은 과장을 두고 우리가 나무랄 일은 아니다. 그녀는 불을 지피고 아래층으로 내려와서는, 만일 이 땅에 천사라는 것이 있다면 바로 지금 위층에 있노라고, 맹세까지 곁들여 단언했던 것이다."]], [["Sophia had acquainted her cousin with her design to go to London; and Mrs Fitzpatrick had agreed to accompany her; for the arrival of her husband at Upton had put an end to her design of going to Bath, or to her aunt Western. They had therefore no sooner finished their tea than Sophia proposed to set out, the moon then shining extremely bright, and as for the frost she defied it; nor had she any of those apprehensions which many young ladies would have felt at travelling by night; for she had, as we have before observed, some little degree of natural courage; and this, her present sensations, which bordered somewhat on despair, greatly encreased. Besides, as she had already travelled twice with safety by the light of the moon, she was the better emboldened to trust to it a third time.", "소피아는 사촌에게 런던으로 갈 작정임을 알린 터였고, 피츠패트릭 부인은 함께 가기로 했다. 남편이 어퍼튼에 나타나는 바람에 배스로, 또는 웨스턴 고모에게로 가려던 그녀의 계획이 무산되었기 때문이다. 그리하여 두 사람은 차를 다 마시기가 무섭게, 소피아가 곧 길을 나서자고 제안했다. 마침 달이 무척 밝게 빛나고 있었고, 서리 따위는 두렵지 않았다. 밤길을 가는 일에 대해 다른 젊은 여인들이라면 느꼈을 그런 불안도 그녀에게는 없었다. 앞서 일러두었듯이 그녀에게는 타고난 용기가 얼마간 있었던 데다, 지금 그녀의 심정이 거의 절망에 가까웠던 터라 그 용기가 한층 북돋워졌기 때문이다. 게다가 그녀는 이미 두 차례나 달빛에 의지해 무사히 길을 다녀 본 적이 있었으므로, 세 번째로 그것에 몸을 맡기는 데 더욱 대담해질 수 있었다."]], [["The disposition of Mrs Fitzpatrick was more timorous; for, though the greater terrors had conquered the less, and the presence of her husband had driven her away at so unseasonable an hour from Upton, yet, being now arrived at a place where she thought herself safe from his pursuit, these lesser terrors of I know not what operated so strongly, that she earnestly entreated her cousin to stay till the next morning, and not expose herself to the dangers of travelling by night.", "피츠패트릭 부인의 기질은 좀 더 겁이 많은 편이었다. 비록 더 큰 두려움이 작은 두려움을 눌렀고, 남편이 나타난 탓에 그토록 때 아닌 시각에 어퍼튼을 떠나기는 했지만, 이제 남편의 추격에서 안전하다 여겨지는 곳에 다다르자, 정체를 알 수 없는 그 작은 두려움들이 어찌나 거세게 일었던지 그녀는 사촌에게 이튿날 아침까지 머물러, 밤길의 위험에 몸을 내맡기지 말자고 간곡히 청했다."]], [["Sophia, who was yielding to an excess, when she could neither laugh nor reason her cousin out of these apprehensions, at last gave way to them. Perhaps, indeed, had she known of her father's arrival at Upton, it might have been more difficult to have persuaded her; for as to Jones, she had, I am afraid, no great horror at the thoughts of being overtaken by him; nay, to confess the truth, I believe she rather wished than feared it; though I might honestly enough have concealed this wish from the reader, as it was one of those secret spontaneous emotions of the soul to which the reason is often a stranger.", "소피아는 어지간하면 양보하는 성품이라, 웃음으로도 이치로도 사촌을 그 불안에서 끌어내지 못하자 결국 그 뜻에 따르고 말았다. 사실, 아버지가 어퍼튼에 와 있다는 사실을 알았더라면 그녀를 설득하기가 더 어려웠을지도 모른다. 존스에 대해서라면, 송구하게도 그녀는 그에게 따라잡힐 생각에 그리 큰 두려움이 없었으니 말이다. 아니, 사실대로 고백하자면, 그녀는 그것을 두려워하기보다 오히려 바라고 있었던 듯하다. 비록 이 바람을 독자에게 솔직히 감출 수도 있었으련만 그러지 않은 까닭은, 그것이 이성으로는 좀처럼 헤아릴 수 없는, 영혼의 은밀하고 절로 솟아나는 감정 가운데 하나였기 때문이다."]], [["When our young ladies had determined to remain all that evening in their inn they were attended by the landlady, who desired to know what their ladyships would be pleased to eat.", "우리의 젊은 두 여인이 그날 저녁 내내 여관에 머물기로 정하자, 안주인이 시중을 들러 와서 두 아씨께서 무엇을 드시고 싶으신지 알고자 했다."], ["Such charms were there in the voice, in the manner, and in the affable deportment of Sophia, that she ravished the landlady to the highest degree; and that good woman, concluding that she had attended Jenny Cameron, became in a moment a stanch Jacobite, and wished heartily well to the young Pretender's cause, from the great sweetness and affability with which she had been treated by his supposed mistress.", "소피아의 목소리며, 몸가짐이며, 상냥한 태도에는 어찌나 매력이 넘쳤던지, 안주인은 더없이 흠뻑 매료되고 말았다. 그 마음씨 고운 여인은 자신이 시중든 이가 제니 캐머런이라고 단정 짓고는, 추정컨대 그의 정부라는 이 여인에게서 받은 더없는 다정함과 상냥함에 감격하여, 순식간에 골수 자코바이트가 되어 젊은 왕위 요구자의 대의가 잘되기를 진심으로 빌었다."]], [["The two cousins began now to impart to each other their reciprocal curiosity to know what extraordinary accidents on both sides occasioned this so strange and unexpected meeting.", "이제 두 사촌은 서로에게, 양쪽 모두 어떤 기이한 사연으로 이토록 야릇하고 뜻밖의 만남에 이르게 되었는지 알고 싶어하는 호기심을 털어놓기 시작했다."], ["At last Mrs Fitzpatrick, having obtained of Sophia a promise of communicating likewise in her turn, began to relate what the reader, if he is desirous to know her history, may read in the ensuing chapter.", "마침내 피츠패트릭 부인은 소피아에게서 자기 차례에도 마찬가지로 이야기를 들려주겠다는 약속을 받아 낸 뒤, 독자가 그녀의 내력을 알고자 한다면 이어지는 장에서 읽을 수 있는 이야기를 들려주기 시작했다."]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick, after a silence of a few moments, fetching a deep sigh, thus began:", "피츠패트릭 부인은 잠시 침묵하다가 깊은 한숨을 한 번 내쉬고는 이렇게 입을 열었다."]], [["“It is natural to the unhappy to feel a secret concern in recollecting those periods of their lives which have been most delightful to them.", "“불행한 사람일수록, 살아오면서 가장 즐거웠던 시절을 떠올릴 때 남모를 아픔을 느끼는 법인가 봐. 지나간 기쁨을 되새기는 일은 우리에게 일종의 애틋한 슬픔을 안기지."], ["The remembrance of past pleasures affects us with a kind of tender grief, like what we suffer for departed friends; and the ideas of both may be said to haunt our imaginations.", "마치 떠나보낸 벗을 그리워할 때 느끼는 그런 슬픔 말이야. 그리고 그 두 가지의 기억이 우리 마음을 떠나지 않고 맴돈다고 해도 좋을 거야."]], [["“For this reason, I never reflect without sorrow on those days (the happiest far of my life) which we spent together when both were under the care of my aunt Western.", "“그래서 나는, 우리 둘이 웨스턴 고모의 보살핌을 받으며 함께 보냈던 그 시절(내 인생에서 단연 가장 행복했던 날들)을 슬픔 없이는 결코 떠올리지 못해."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["why are Miss Graveairs and Miss Giddy no more?", "어째서 새침데기 아가씨와 덤벙이 아가씨는 이제 더 이상 없는 걸까?"], ["You remember, I am sure, when we knew each other by no other names.", "우리가 서로를 다른 이름이 아니라 바로 그 이름으로 불렀던 때를 너도 분명 기억하고 있겠지."], ["Indeed, you gave the latter appellation with too much cause.", "사실 너는 그 뒤쪽 별명을 붙일 만한 이유가 너무도 충분했어."], ["I have since experienced how much I deserved it.", "내가 그 별명을 얼마나 받을 만했는지는 그 후로 몸소 겪어 알게 되었으니까."], ["You, my Sophia, was always my superior in everything, and I heartily hope you will be so in your fortune.", "소피아, 너는 무엇에서든 늘 나보다 나았어. 그리고 운명에서도 네가 나보다 나으리라고 나는 진심으로 바라."], ["I shall never forget the wise and matronly advice you once gave me, when I lamented being disappointed of a ball, though you could not be then fourteen years old.----O my Sophy, how blest must have been my situation, when I could think such a disappointment a misfortune; and when indeed it was the greatest I had ever known!”", "내가 무도회에 못 가게 되었다고 한탄했을 때, 그때 너는 분명 열네 살도 채 안 되었을 텐데, 네가 내게 들려준 그 현명하고도 어른스러운 충고를 나는 결코 잊지 못할 거야.----오 나의 소피, 그런 실망쯤을 불행이라 여길 수 있던 그때 내 처지는 얼마나 복된 것이었을까. 그리고 정말이지 그게 그때까지 내가 겪은 가장 큰 불행이었으니!”"]], [["“And yet, my dear Harriet,”", "“그래도 말이야, 사랑하는 해리엇,”"], ["answered Sophia, “it was then a serious matter with you.", "소피아가 대답했다. “그때 너한테는 그게 정말 심각한 문제였잖아."], ["Comfort yourself therefore with thinking, that whatever you now lament may hereafter appear as trifling and contemptible as a ball would at this time.”", "그러니 이렇게 생각하며 마음을 달래봐. 네가 지금 한탄하는 그 무엇도, 훗날에는 지금 무도회를 떠올리는 만큼이나 사소하고 하찮게 여겨질지 모른다고 말이야.”"]], [["“Alas, my Sophia,”", "“아아, 나의 소피아,” 다른 부인이 대답했다."], ["replied the other lady, “you yourself will think otherwise of my present situation; for greatly must that tender heart be altered if my misfortunes do not draw many a sigh, nay, many a tear, from you.", "“지금 내 처지를 두고서는 너도 그렇게 생각하지 못할 거야. 내 불행이 너에게서 숱한 한숨을, 아니, 숱한 눈물을 자아내지 않는다면, 그 다정한 마음이 어지간히도 변한 게 분명할 테니까."], ["The knowledge of this should perhaps deter me from relating what I am convinced will so much affect you.”", "이런 걸 알면서도, 내가 너를 그토록 아프게 할 거라고 확신하는 이야기를 풀어놓으려 한다는 게, 어쩌면 나를 망설이게 만들어야 마땅하겠지.”"], ["Here Mrs Fitzpatrick stopt, till, at the repeated entreaties of Sophia, she thus proceeded:", "여기서 피츠패트릭 부인은 말을 멈추었다가, 소피아가 거듭 간청하자 다시 이렇게 이어갔다."]], [["“Though you must have heard much of my marriage; yet, as matters may probably have been misrepresented, I will set out from the very commencement of my unfortunate acquaintance with my present husband; which was at Bath, soon after you left my aunt, and returned home to your father.", "“너도 내 결혼에 대해 이런저런 이야기를 많이 들었을 테지만, 어쩌면 사실이 잘못 전해졌을 수도 있으니, 나는 지금 남편과 처음 알게 된 그 불운한 시작부터 짚어 나갈게. 그건 바스에서의 일이었어. 네가 고모 곁을 떠나 아버지 댁으로 돌아가고 얼마 지나지 않아서였지."]], [["“Among the gay young fellows who were at this season at Bath, Mr Fitzpatrick was one.", "“그 무렵 바스에 모여든 멋쟁이 젊은이들 가운데 피츠패트릭 씨도 있었어."], ["He was handsome, dégagé, extremely gallant, and in his dress exceeded most others.", "그는 잘생기고, 거동이 거리낌 없고, 더없이 정중했으며, 옷차림에서는 대부분의 다른 사람들을 능가했지."], ["In short, my dear, if you was unluckily to see him now, I could describe him no better than by telling you he was the very reverse of everything which he is: for he hath rusticated himself so long, that he is become an absolute wild Irishman.", "한마디로, 얘, 만일 네가 운 나쁘게 지금의 그를 본다면, 나는 그가 지금 모습의 정반대였다고 말하는 것밖에는 달리 묘사할 길이 없을 거야. 그는 너무 오래 시골에 처박혀 지내는 바람에 완전히 거친 아일랜드 촌사람이 되고 말았거든."], ["But to proceed in my story: the qualifications which he then possessed so well recommended him, that, though the people of quality at that time lived separate from the rest of the company, and excluded them from all their parties, Mr Fitzpatrick found means to gain admittance.", "그건 그렇고 내 이야기를 계속하자면, 그때 그가 지닌 자질이 어찌나 그를 돋보이게 했던지, 당시 지체 높은 사람들은 나머지 무리와 따로 어울리며 자기네 모임에서 그들을 죄다 배제했는데도, 피츠패트릭 씨만은 끼어들 방도를 찾아냈어. 어쩌면 그를 피하기가 쉽지 않았던 건지도 몰라."], ["It was perhaps no easy matter to avoid him; for he required very little or no invitation; and as, being handsome and genteel, he found it no very difficult matter to ingratiate himself with the ladies, so, he having frequently drawn his sword, the men did not care publickly to affront him.", "그는 초대 같은 건 거의, 아니 전혀 필요로 하지 않았으니까. 게다가 잘생기고 품위 있는 덕에 부인들의 환심을 사는 일이 그리 어렵지 않았고, 또 칼을 자주 빼 든 터라 남자들도 대놓고 그를 모욕하기는 꺼렸어."], ["Had it not been for some such reason, I believe he would have been soon expelled by his own sex; for surely he had no strict title to be preferred to the English gentry; nor did they seem inclined to show him any extraordinary favour.", "그런 이유라도 없었다면, 나는 그가 동성들 손에 금방 쫓겨났으리라 믿어. 그가 영국 신사들보다 우대받을 정당한 권리가 있던 것도 분명 아니었고, 그들 역시 그에게 별다른 호의를 베풀 마음은 없어 보였으니까."], ["They all abused him behind his back, which might probably proceed from envy; for by the women he was well received, and very particularly distinguished by them.", "그들은 모두 그가 없는 자리에서 그를 헐뜯었는데, 아마 시샘에서 나온 일이었을 거야. 여자들에게는 그가 잘 받아들여졌고, 또 유난히 떠받들어졌으니까."]], [["“My aunt, though no person of quality herself, as she had always lived about the court, was enrolled in that party; for, by whatever means you get into the polite circle, when you are once there, it is sufficient merit for you that you are there.", "“우리 고모는 본인이 지체 높은 사람은 아니었지만, 줄곧 궁정 주변에서 지내온 터라 그 무리에 이름을 올리고 있었어. 어떤 수단으로든 일단 상류 사회에 발을 들여놓고 나면, 거기 있다는 사실 자체가 충분한 자격이 되는 법이거든."], ["This observation, young as you was, you could scarce avoid making from my aunt, who was free, or reserved, with all people, just as they had more or less of this merit.", "너도 어렸지만 우리 고모를 보며 이 점만은 알아챌 수밖에 없었을 거야. 고모는 사람들이 이 자격을 더 갖췄느냐 덜 갖췄느냐에 딱 맞춰서, 누구에게는 스스럼없이 대하고 누구에게는 데면데면하게 굴었으니까."]], [["“And this merit, I believe, it was, which principally recommended Mr Fitzpatrick to her favour.", "“그리고 내 생각엔, 피츠패트릭 씨를 고모의 총애로 이끈 것도 주로 바로 이 자격이었던 것 같아."], ["In which he so well succeeded, that he was always one of her private parties.", "거기서 그가 어찌나 성공했던지, 그는 늘 고모의 사적인 모임에 빠지지 않는 일원이 되었지."], ["Nor was he backward in returning such distinction; for he soon grew so very particular in his behaviour to her, that the scandal club first began to take notice of it, and the better-disposed persons made a match between them.", "그도 그런 우대를 되갚는 데 게으르지 않아서, 곧 고모를 대하는 태도가 어찌나 각별해졌던지, 험담패들이 먼저 그걸 입에 올리기 시작했고, 좀 더 호의적인 사람들은 둘을 짝지어 주려 했어."], ["For my own part, I confess, I made no doubt but that his designs were strictly honourable, as the phrase is; that is, to rob a lady of her fortune by way of marriage.", "나로 말하자면, 솔직히 그의 속셈이 흔히 하는 말로 ‘점잖은’ 것이라는 데에는 조금도 의심이 없었어. 다시 말해 결혼을 빌미로 한 부인의 재산을 가로채려는 속셈 말이야."], ["My aunt was, I conceived, neither young enough nor handsome enough to attract much wicked inclination; but she had matrimonial charms in great abundance.", "내가 보기에 고모는 젊지도 곱지도 않아서 그리 못된 욕심을 끌 만한 분은 아니었지만, 혼인이 주는 매력만큼은 차고 넘치게 지니고 있었거든."]], [["“I was the more confirmed in this opinion from the extraordinary respect which he showed to myself from the first moment of our acquaintance. This I understood as an attempt to lessen, if possible, that disinclination which my interest might be supposed to give me towards the match; and I know not but in some measure it had that effect; for, as I was well contented with my own fortune, and of all people the least a slave to interested views, so I could not be violently the enemy of a man with whose behaviour to me I was greatly pleased; and the more so, as I was the only object of such respect; for he behaved at the same time to many women of quality without any respect at all.", "“그가 우리가 처음 알게 된 순간부터 내게 보여 준 유별난 존중 때문에 나는 이 생각을 더욱 굳히게 되었어. 나는 그걸, 그 혼사에 대해 내 이해관계상 품을 법한 거부감을 어떻게든 누그러뜨리려는 시도라고 받아들였지. 그리고 어느 정도는 정말 그런 효과가 있었는지도 몰라. 나는 내 재산에 흡족했고, 누구보다도 이해타산에 매이지 않는 사람이었으니, 나를 대하는 태도가 무척이나 마음에 드는 남자를 거세게 적대할 수는 없었어. 게다가 그런 존중을 받는 대상이 오직 나뿐이었기에 더욱 그랬지. 그는 같은 시간에 지체 높은 다른 여인들에게는 조금의 존중도 없이 굴었으니까."]], [["“Agreeable as this was to me, he soon changed it into another kind of behaviour, which was perhaps more so.", "“이게 내게는 흐뭇한 일이었는데, 그는 곧 그것을 또 다른 태도로 바꾸었어. 어쩌면 그게 더 흐뭇했는지도 몰라."], ["He now put on much softness and tenderness, and languished and sighed abundantly.", "이제 그는 한껏 부드럽고 다정한 태도를 짓고는, 넘치도록 애를 태우며 한숨을 내쉬었지."], ["At times, indeed, whether from art or nature I will not determine, he gave his usual loose to gaiety and mirth; but this was always in general company, and with other women; for even in a country-dance, when he was not my partner, he became grave, and put on the softest look imaginable the moment he approached me.", "물론 이따금은, 그게 꾸민 것인지 타고난 것인지 나는 단정하지 않겠지만, 평소처럼 흥겨움과 명랑함을 마음껏 풀어놓기도 했어. 하지만 그건 늘 여럿이 모인 자리에서, 그것도 다른 여자들을 상대로였지."], ["Indeed he was in all things so very particular towards me, that I must have been blind not to have discovered it.", "시골풍 춤을 출 때조차도, 내 짝이 아닐 때면 그는 진지해졌고, 내게 다가오는 순간엔 상상할 수 있는 가장 부드러운 표정을 지었어. 정말이지 그는 매사에 나를 향해 어찌나 각별했던지, 그걸 알아채지 못했다면 내가 눈먼 사람이었을 거야."], ["And, and, and----”", "그리고, 그리고, 그리고----”"], ["“And you was more pleased still, my dear Harriet,”", "“그리고 너는 그게 더더욱 흐뭇했던 거지, 사랑하는 해리엇.”"], ["cries Sophia; “you need not be ashamed,”", "소피아가 외쳤다. “부끄러워할 것 없어.”"], ["added she, sighing; “for sure there are irresistible charms in tenderness, which too many men are able to affect.”", "그녀는 한숨을 내쉬며 덧붙였다. “다정함에는 거역할 수 없는 매력이 있는데, 너무 많은 남자들이 그걸 꾸며낼 줄 알거든.”"], ["“True,”", "“맞아.”"], ["answered her cousin; “men, who in all other instances want common sense, are very Machiavels in the art of loving.", "사촌이 대답했다. “다른 모든 면에서는 상식조차 없는 남자들이, 사랑을 부리는 기술에서는 어찌나 능구렁이들인지."], ["I wish I did not know an instance.--Well, scandal now began to be as busy with me as it had before been with my aunt; and some good ladies did not scruple to affirm that Mr Fitzpatrick had an intrigue with us both.", "나도 그런 본보기를 몰랐다면 좋았을 텐데.--아무튼, 이제 험담은 전에 고모를 두고 그랬던 것만큼이나 나를 두고도 분주해졌어. 몇몇 점잖은 부인네들은 피츠패트릭 씨가 우리 둘 모두와 정을 통한다고 서슴없이 단언하기까지 했지."]], [["“But, what may seem astonishing, my aunt never saw, nor in the least seemed to suspect, that which was visible enough, I believe, from both our behaviours.", "“그런데 놀라울지 모르겠지만, 우리 고모만은 우리 둘의 행동에서 충분히 드러났을 그걸 전혀 보지 못했고, 조금도 의심하는 기색이 없었어."], ["One would indeed think that love quite puts out the eyes of an old woman.", "정말이지 사랑이란 늙은 여인의 눈을 완전히 멀게 만드는 모양이야."], ["In fact, they so greedily swallow the addresses which are made to them, that, like an outrageous glutton, they are not at leisure to observe what passes amongst others at the same table.", "사실 그들은 자기에게 바쳐지는 구애를 어찌나 게걸스럽게 삼키는지, 마치 사나운 대식가처럼, 같은 식탁에서 다른 이들 사이에 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 살필 겨를이 없거든."], ["This I have observed in more cases than my own; and this was so strongly verified by my aunt, that, though she often found us together at her return from the pump, the least canting word of his, pretending impatience at her absence, effectually smothered all suspicion.", "나는 내 경우 말고도 여러 경우에서 이걸 보아 왔는데, 우리 고모에게서 그게 어찌나 확실히 들어맞던지, 고모가 약수터에서 돌아왔을 때 우리가 함께 있는 걸 종종 보고도, 고모의 부재를 못 견디겠다는 척하는 그의 능청맞은 말 한마디면 모든 의심이 깔끔히 잠재워졌어."], ["One artifice succeeded with her to admiration.", "한 가지 술책이 고모에게는 기막히게 잘 통했지."], ["This was his treating me like a little child, and never calling me by any other name in her presence but that of pretty miss.", "그건 나를 어린아이처럼 다루며, 고모 앞에서는 ‘예쁜 아가씨’ 말고는 결코 다른 이름으로 부르지 않는 거였어."], ["This indeed did him some disservice with your humble servant; but I soon saw through it, especially as in her absence he behaved to me, as I have said, in a different manner.", "사실 이건 너의 변변찮은 친구인 나에게는 오히려 손해를 끼쳤지만, 나는 곧 그 속을 꿰뚫어 보았어. 특히 고모가 없을 때면 그가, 앞서 말했듯이 다른 식으로 나를 대했으니까."], ["However, if I was not greatly disobliged by a conduct of which I had discovered the design, I smarted very severely for it; for my aunt really conceived me to be what her lover (as she thought him) called me, and treated me in all respects as a perfect infant.", "하지만 그 속셈을 간파한 행동에 내가 크게 언짢지는 않았더라도, 나는 그것 때문에 아주 호되게 시달려야 했어. 고모가 정말로 나를, 자기 연인(그렇게 여겼던 사람)이 부르는 그대로의 사람으로 여기고는, 모든 면에서 나를 완전히 갓난아이처럼 대했으니까."], ["To say the truth, I wonder she had not insisted on my again wearing leading-strings.", "솔직히 말해, 고모가 나더러 다시 걸음마 끈을 차고 다니라고 우기지 않은 게 신기할 지경이야."]], [["“At last, my lover (for so he was) thought proper, in a most solemn manner, to disclose a secret which I had known long before.", "“마침내 내 연인은(그는 정말 그랬으니까), 내가 진작부터 알고 있던 비밀을 더없이 엄숙한 태도로 털어놓는 게 좋겠다고 여겼어."], ["He now placed all the love which he had pretended to my aunt to my account.", "그는 이제 고모에게 가장한 척했던 그 사랑을 모조리 내 앞으로 돌렸지."], ["He lamented, in very pathetic terms, the encouragement she had given him, and made a high merit of the tedious hours in which he had undergone her conversation.--What shall I tell you, my dear Sophia?--Then I will confess the truth.", "고모가 자기에게 베푼 호의를 무척이나 애처로운 말투로 한탄했고, 고모와의 대화를 견뎌낸 그 지루한 시간들을 대단한 공로인 양 내세웠어.--내가 너에게 무슨 말을 해야 할까, 사랑하는 소피아?--그래, 솔직히 고백할게."], ["I was pleased with my man.", "나는 그 남자가 마음에 들었어."], ["I was pleased with my conquest.", "내가 거둔 정복이 흐뭇했어."], ["To rival my aunt delighted me; to rival so many other women charmed me.", "고모의 연적이 된다는 게 즐거웠고, 그토록 많은 다른 여자들의 연적이 된다는 게 황홀했어."], ["In short, I am afraid I did not behave as I should do, even upon the very first declaration--I wish I did not almost give him positive encouragement before we parted.", "한마디로, 나는 처음 그가 고백하던 바로 그 순간조차도 마땅히 해야 할 처신을 하지 못했던 것 같아.--우리가 헤어지기 전에 그에게 거의 분명한 호응을 내비치지 않았기를 바랄 뿐이야."]], [["“The Bath now talked loudly--I might almost say, roared against me.", "“이제 바스는 나를 두고 떠들썩하게--거의 으르렁댔다고 해도 좋을 만큼--입방아를 찧기 시작했어."], ["Several young women affected to shun my acquaintance, not so much, perhaps, from any real suspicion, as from a desire of banishing me from a company in which I too much engrossed their favourite man.", "여러 젊은 여자들이 내 곁을 일부러 멀리하는 척했는데, 그건 아마 무슨 진짜 의심에서라기보다는, 자기네가 아끼는 남자를 내가 너무 독차지하고 있던 그 무리에서 나를 쫓아내고 싶은 마음에서였을 거야. 그리고 여기서 내가 빼놓을 수 없는 게, 내게 베풀어 주려 한 내쉬 씨의 호의에 대한 고마움이야."], ["And here I cannot omit expressing my gratitude to the kindness intended me by Mr Nash, who took me one day aside, and gave me advice, which if I had followed, I had been a happy woman. `Child,'", "그분은 어느 날 나를 한쪽으로 데려가더니 충고를 해 주었는데, 내가 그걸 따랐더라면 나는 행복한 여자가 되었을 거야."], ["says he, `I am sorry to see the familiarity which subsists between you and a fellow who is altogether unworthy of you, and I am afraid will prove your ruin.", "‘얘야,’ 그분이 말했어. ‘너와, 너에게 도무지 어울리지 않는 데다 결국 네 파멸이 되고 말 것 같은 그 작자 사이에 오가는 허물없음을 보자니 안타깝구나."], ["As for your old stinking aunt, if it was to be no injury to you and my pretty Sophy Western (I assure you I repeat his words), I should be heartily glad that the fellow was in possession of all that belongs to her.", "네 고약한 늙은 고모로 말하자면, 너와 내 예쁜 소피 웨스턴(분명히 말하지만 나는 그분의 말을 그대로 옮기는 거야)에게 해가 되지만 않는다면, 나는 그 작자가 고모에게 딸린 모든 걸 차지하는 걸 진심으로 기뻐했을 거다. 나는 결코 늙은 여자들에게는 충고하지 않아."], ["I never advise old women: for, if they take it into their heads to go to the devil, it is no more possible than worth while to keep them from him.", "그들이 일단 악마에게 가기로 작정하면, 그를 떼어놓는 건 가능하지도 않거니와 그럴 만한 값어치도 없으니까. 하지만 순결과 젊음과 아름다움은 더 나은 운명을 누릴 자격이 있으니, 나는 그것들을 그 작자의 마수에서 구해 내고 싶구나."], ["Innocence and youth and beauty are worthy a better fate, and I would save them from his clutches.", "그러니 사랑하는 얘야, 내 충고를 들어라. 다시는 그 작자가 너에게 각별히 굴도록 결코 내버려 두지 말거라.’ 그분은 그 밖에도 여러 말을 해 주셨는데, 이제는 다 잊어버렸어."], ["Let me advise you therefore, dear child, never suffer this fellow to be particular with you again.'", "사실 그때도 나는 그 말들에 거의 귀를 기울이지 않았거든. 내 마음이 그분의 모든 말에 어깃장을 놓았으니까."], ["Many more things he said to me, which I have now forgotten, and indeed I attended very little to them at the time; for inclination contradicted all he said; and, besides, I could not be persuaded that women of quality would condescend to familiarity with such a person as he described.", "게다가 나는 지체 높은 여인들이 그분이 묘사한 그런 인물과 허물없이 지낼 만큼 자신을 낮추리라고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었어."]], [["“But I am afraid, my dear, I shall tire you with a detail of so many minute circumstances. To be concise, therefore, imagine me married; imagine me with my husband, at the feet of my aunt; and then imagine the maddest woman in Bedlam, in a raving fit, and your imagination will suggest to you no more than what really happened.", "“하지만 얘, 이렇게 사소한 사정들을 시시콜콜 늘어놓다가는 너를 지치게 만들 것 같아 걱정이야. 그러니 간추려서, 내가 결혼한 모습을 떠올려 봐. 남편과 함께 고모 발치에 엎드려 있는 내 모습을 떠올려 봐. 그리고 베들럼의 가장 미쳐 날뛰는 여인이 광란의 발작을 일으키는 모습을 떠올려 봐. 그러면 네 상상은 실제로 일어난 일 그대로만을 그려 줄 거야."]], [["“The very next day my aunt left the place, partly to avoid seeing Mr Fitzpatrick or myself, and as much perhaps to avoid seeing any one else; for, though I am told she hath since denied everything stoutly, I believe she was then a little confounded at her disappointment. Since that time, I have written to her many letters, but never could obtain an answer, which I must own sits somewhat the heavier, as she herself was, though undesignedly, the occasion of all my sufferings: for, had it not been under the colour of paying his addresses to her, Mr Fitzpatrick would never have found sufficient opportunities to have engaged my heart, which, in other circumstances, I still flatter myself would not have been an easy conquest to such a person. Indeed, I believe I should not have erred so grossly in my choice if I had relied on my own judgment; but I trusted totally to the opinion of others, and very foolishly took the merit of a man for granted whom I saw so universally well received by the women. What is the reason, my dear, that we, who have understandings equal to the wisest and greatest of the other sex, so often make choice of the silliest fellows for companions and favourites? It raises my indignation to the highest pitch to reflect on the numbers of women of sense who have been undone by fools.” Here she paused a moment; but, Sophia making no answer, she proceeded as in the next chapter.", "“바로 그 이튿날 고모는 그곳을 떠났어. 피츠패트릭 씨나 나를 보지 않으려는 마음도 있었겠지만, 어쩌면 다른 그 누구도 마주치지 않으려는 마음이 그만큼 컸을 거야. 듣자 하니 고모는 그 뒤로 모든 걸 한사코 부인해 왔다지만, 그때만은 자신의 어긋난 기대에 적잖이 당황했던 거라고 나는 믿어. 그 후로 나는 고모에게 숱한 편지를 썼지만, 끝내 답장 한 통 받지 못했어. 솔직히 이게 좀 더 무겁게 마음을 짓누르는 건, 본의는 아니었다 해도 내 모든 고통의 빌미가 바로 고모 자신이었기 때문이야. 고모에게 구애한다는 명목이 아니었다면, 피츠패트릭 씨는 내 마음을 사로잡을 만한 기회를 결코 충분히 얻지 못했을 테니까. 다른 상황이었다면, 내 마음이 그런 사람에게 그리 쉬운 정복은 아니었으리라고 나는 여전히 자위하고 있어. 정말이지, 내가 내 판단에만 기댔더라면 그렇게 형편없이 잘못 고르지는 않았을 거야. 그런데 나는 온통 남들의 의견만 믿고서, 여자들에게 그토록 두루 잘 받아들여지는 걸 본 어느 남자의 됨됨이를 어리석게도 당연하게 여겨 버렸지. 얘, 도대체 어찌 된 일일까? 우리는 저 다른 성의 가장 지혜롭고 위대한 이들에 못지않은 분별을 지녔으면서도, 어째서 그토록 자주 가장 어리석은 작자들을 반려이자 총아로 골라 버리는 걸까? 어리석은 자들 때문에 신세를 망친 분별 있는 여자들이 그토록 많다는 걸 떠올리면, 나는 분노가 머리끝까지 치밀어 올라.” 여기서 그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추었지만, 소피아가 아무 대답이 없자 다음 장에서처럼 이야기를 이어갔다."]], [["In which the history of Mrs Fitzpatrick is continued.", "피츠패트릭 부인의 이야기가 이어지는 장."]], [["“We remained at Bath no longer than a fortnight after our wedding; for as to any reconciliation with my aunt, there were no hopes; and of my fortune not one farthing could be touched till I was of age, of which I now wanted more than two years. My husband therefore was resolved to set out for Ireland; against which I remonstrated very earnestly, and insisted on a promise which he had made me before our marriage that I should never take this journey against my consent; and indeed I never intended to consent to it; nor will anybody, I believe, blame me for that resolution; but this, however, I never mentioned to my husband, and petitioned only for the reprieve of a month; but he had fixed the day, and to that day he obstinately adhered.", "\"우리는 결혼식을 올린 뒤로 보름 남짓밖에 바스에 머물지 않았어요. 숙모와 화해할 가망은 조금도 없었고, 제 재산은 성년이 되기 전까지 한 푼도 손댈 수 없었는데, 그때까지는 아직 두 해도 더 남아 있었으니까요. 그래서 남편은 아일랜드로 떠나기로 마음을 굳혔어요. 저는 한사코 반대하며, 결혼 전에 그가 제게 한 약속, 곧 제 동의 없이는 결코 이 여행을 시키지 않겠다던 그 약속을 들어 따졌지요. 사실 저는 결코 동의할 마음이 없었어요. 누구도 그 결심을 두고 저를 탓하지는 않으리라 믿어요. 그렇지만 이 속내만큼은 남편에게 한 번도 말하지 않았고, 다만 한 달만 늦춰 달라고 청했을 뿐이에요. 하지만 그는 날짜를 못 박아 두었고, 그날을 고집스레 밀어붙였습니다."]], [["“The evening before our departure, as we were disputing this point with great eagerness on both sides, he started suddenly from his chair, and left me abruptly, saying he was going to the rooms.", "\"떠나기 전날 저녁, 우리가 양쪽 다 격렬하게 이 문제를 다투고 있을 때, 그는 갑자기 의자에서 벌떡 일어나더니 무도장에 다녀오겠다는 말만 남기고 저를 매정하게 두고 나가 버렸어요."], ["He was hardly out of the house when I saw a paper lying on the floor, which, I suppose, he had carelessly pulled from his pocket, together with his handkerchief.", "그가 집을 채 나서기도 전에, 저는 바닥에 종이 한 장이 떨어져 있는 것을 보았어요. 손수건과 함께 무심코 호주머니에서 빼낸 모양이었지요."], ["This paper I took up, and, finding it to be a letter, I made no scruple to open and read it; and indeed I read it so often that I can repeat it to you almost word for word.", "그 종이를 집어 들어 보니 편지였고, 저는 거리낌 없이 펼쳐 읽었어요. 어찌나 여러 번 읽었던지 거의 한 자 한 자 그대로 당신에게 옮길 수 있을 정도예요."], ["This then was the letter:", "그 편지는 이러했어요."]], [["'To Mr Brian Fitzpatrick.", "'브라이언 피츠패트릭 귀하."]], [["'YOURS received, and am surprized you should use me in this manner, as have never seen any of your cash, unless for one linsey-woolsey coat, and your bill now is upwards of £150.", "'선생님,\n'보내신 편지 받았습니다만, 이런 식으로 저를 대하시니 놀라울 따름입니다. 저는 무명 모직 외투 한 벌 값 외에는 선생님의 현금이라곤 구경도 못 했는데, 지금 외상이 150파운드를 넘었습니다."], ["Consider, sir, how often you have fobbed me off with your being shortly to be married to this lady and t'other lady; but I can neither live on hopes or promises, nor will my woollen-draper take any such in payment.", "생각해 보십시오, 선생님. 곧 이 부인이니 저 부인이니 하는 분과 결혼한다는 말로 저를 몇 번이나 따돌리셨는지요."], ["You tell me you are secure of having either the aunt or the niece, and that you might have married the aunt before this, whose jointure you say is immense, but that you prefer the niece on account of her ready money.", "그러나 저는 희망이나 약속만으로는 살 수 없고, 제 모직 도매상도 그런 걸 대금으로 받아 주지 않습니다. 선생님은 그 숙모든 조카딸이든 둘 중 하나는 손에 넣은 거나 다름없다 하셨고, 또 진작에 숙모와 결혼할 수도 있었는데—그 과부 재산이 어마어마하다 하셨지요—다만 현금이 곧바로 들어오는 조카딸 쪽을 더 좋아하신다 하셨지요."], ["Pray, sir, take a fool's advice for once, and marry the first you can get.", "부디 선생님, 이번 한 번만은 어리석은 자의 충고를 들으시어, 먼저 잡히는 쪽과 어서 결혼하십시오."], ["You will pardon my offering my advice, as you know I sincerely wish you well.", "제가 충고를 드리는 것을 용서하십시오. 제가 선생님을 진심으로 위하는 줄 아시잖습니까."], ["Shall draw on you per next post, in favour of Messieurs John Drugget and company, at fourteen days, which doubt not your honouring, and am,", "다음 우편으로 존 드러깃 상회 앞으로 14일짜리 어음을 발행할 터이니, 부디 인수해 주시리라 믿어 의심치 않으며, 이만 줄입니다."]], [["Sir, your humble servant, 'SAM.", "선생님의 비천한 종, 샘."], ["COSGRAVE.'", "코스그레이브 올림.'"]], [["“This was the letter, word for word.", "\"이것이 한 자도 빠짐없는 그 편지였어요."], ["Guess, my dear girl--guess how this letter affected me.", "짐작해 보세요, 사랑하는 그대—이 편지가 저를 어떻게 뒤흔들어 놓았을지 짐작해 보세요."], ["You prefer the niece on account of her ready money!", "'현금이 곧바로 들어오는 조카딸 쪽을 더 좋아하신다'!"], ["If every one of these words had been a dagger, I could with pleasure have stabbed them into his heart; but I will not recount my frantic behaviour on the occasion.", "만약 이 낱말 하나하나가 단검이었다면, 저는 기꺼이 그것들을 그의 심장에 꽂아 넣었을 거예요. 하지만 그 순간 제가 얼마나 미친 듯이 굴었는지는 늘어놓지 않겠어요."], ["I had pretty well spent my tears before his return home; but sufficient remains of them appeared in my swollen eyes.", "그가 집에 돌아오기 전에 저는 눈물을 거의 다 쏟아 버렸지만, 부어오른 눈에는 그 흔적이 충분히 남아 있었지요. 그는 시무룩하게 의자에 몸을 던졌고, 한참 동안 우리 둘 다 말이 없었어요."], ["He threw himself sullenly into his chair, and for a long time we were both silent.", "마침내 그가 거만한 어조로 말했어요. '부인, 하인들이 짐을 다 꾸렸기를 바라오."], ["At length, in a haughty tone, he said, `I hope, madam, your servants have packed up all your things; for the coach will be ready by six in the morning.'", "마차가 아침 여섯 시에는 준비될 테니.' 이 도발에 제 인내심은 완전히 무너졌고, 저는 이렇게 대답했어요."], ["My patience was totally subdued by this provocation, and I answered, `No, sir, there is a letter still remains unpacked;'", "'아니에요, 선생님. 아직 꾸리지 못한 편지 한 장이 남아 있어요.'"], ["and then throwing it on the table I fell to upbraiding him with the most bitter language I could invent.", "그러고는 그것을 탁자 위에 내던지며, 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 신랄한 말로 그를 몰아붙이기 시작했어요."]], [["“Whether guilt, or shame, or prudence, restrained him I cannot say; but, though he is the most passionate of men, he exerted no rage on this occasion.", "\"죄책감이었는지, 부끄러움이었는지, 아니면 신중함이었는지는 모르겠지만, 그는 누구보다 격정적인 사람인데도 그때만큼은 분노를 터뜨리지 않았어요."], ["He endeavoured, on the contrary, to pacify me by the most gentle means.", "오히려 더없이 부드러운 말로 저를 달래려 애썼지요."], ["He swore the phrase in the letter to which I principally objected was not his, nor had he ever written any such.", "그는 제가 가장 문제 삼은 그 구절은 자기가 쓴 것이 아니며, 그런 글을 쓴 적이 결코 없다고 맹세했어요."], ["He owned, indeed, the having mentioned his marriage, and that preference which he had given to myself, but denied with many oaths the having mentioned any such matter at all on account of the straits he was in for money, arising, he said, from his having too long neglected his estate in Ireland.", "결혼 이야기를 꺼낸 것, 그리고 저를 더 좋아한다고 한 것은 인정했지만, 돈에 쪼들린다는 이유로 그런 일을 입에 올린 적은 절대 없다고 거듭 맹세하며 부인했어요. 그 곤궁은 아일랜드의 영지를 너무 오래 돌보지 않은 데서 비롯된 것이라더군요."], ["And this, he said, which he could not bear to discover to me, was the only reason of his having so strenuously insisted on our journey.", "그리고 차마 제게 털어놓을 수 없었던 바로 이 사정이, 그가 이 여행을 그토록 완강하게 고집한 유일한 이유였다고 했어요."], ["He then used several very endearing expressions, and concluded by a very fond caress, and many violent protestations of love.", "그러고 나서 그는 무척 다정한 말들을 여러 마디 건네더니, 더없이 애틋한 손길과 사랑의 격렬한 맹세로 끝을 맺었어요."]], [["“There was one circumstance which, though he did not appeal to it, had much weight with me in his favour, and that was the word jointure in the taylor's letter, whereas my aunt never had been married, and this Mr Fitzpatrick well knew.----As I imagined, therefore, that the fellow must have inserted this of his own head, or from hearsay, I persuaded myself he might have ventured likewise on that odious line on no better authority. What reasoning was this, my dear? was I not an advocate rather than a judge?--But why do I mention such a circumstance as this, or appeal to it for the justification of my forgiveness?--In short, had he been guilty of twenty times as much, half the tenderness and fondness which he used would have prevailed on me to have forgiven him. I now made no farther objections to our setting out, which we did the next morning, and in a little more than a week arrived at the seat of Mr Fitzpatrick.", "\"그가 들어 따지지는 않았지만, 한 가지 정황이 그에게 유리하게 제 마음을 크게 움직였어요. 바로 재단사의 편지에 나온 '과부 재산'이라는 말이었지요. 제 숙모는 결혼한 적이 한 번도 없었고, 이 사실을 피츠패트릭 씨도 잘 알고 있었거든요. 그러니 저는 그 작자가 제멋대로, 혹은 풍문으로 들은 것을 적어 넣은 게 분명하다고 생각했고, 그렇다면 그 끔찍한 한 줄도 별다른 근거 없이 마음대로 써 갈긴 것일지 모른다고 스스로를 설득했어요. 이게 무슨 추론이었을까요, 그대? 저는 재판관이 아니라 변호인이 아니었을까요? 그런데 제가 어째서 이런 정황을 끌어와 제 용서를 변명하려 드는 걸까요? 한마디로, 설령 그가 그보다 스무 배쯤 더 큰 죄를 지었다 해도, 그가 보인 그 다정함과 애틋함의 절반만으로도 저는 그를 용서하고 말았을 거예요. 저는 더 이상 출발에 토를 달지 않았고, 우리는 이튿날 아침 길을 떠나 일주일 남짓 만에 피츠패트릭 씨의 영지에 닿았어요."]], [["“Your curiosity will excuse me from relating any occurrences which past during our journey; for it would indeed be highly disagreeable to travel it over again, and no less so to you to travel it over with me.", "\"여행 중에 있었던 일들은, 당신의 호기심을 핑계로 그냥 넘어가도 너그러이 봐 주세요. 그 길을 다시 더듬어 가는 일은 제게 몹시 불쾌할 테고, 저와 함께 그 길을 되짚는 일이 당신에게도 못지않게 괴로울 테니까요."]], [["“This seat, then, is an ancient mansion-house: if I was in one of those merry humours in which you have so often seen me, I could describe it to you ridiculously enough.", "\"그 영지는 오래된 저택이에요. 제가 당신이 그토록 자주 보았던 그 유쾌한 기분에 잠겨 있었다면, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 재미있게 그곳을 그려 보일 수도 있었겠지요."], ["It looked as if it had been formerly inhabited by a gentleman.", "그곳은 한때 신사가 살았던 곳처럼 보였어요."], ["Here was room enough, and not the less room on account of the furniture; for indeed there was very little in it.", "방은 넉넉했고, 가구 때문에 좁아질 일도 없었어요. 정말이지 가구라곤 거의 없었으니까요."], ["An old woman, who seemed coeval with the building, and greatly resembled her whom Chamont mentions in the Orphan, received us at the gate, and in a howl scarce human, and to me unintelligible, welcomed her master home.", "그 건물만큼이나 늙어 보이는, 《고아》에서 셔몬트가 언급한 그 노파를 쏙 빼닮은 한 노파가 문간에서 우리를 맞으며, 사람의 소리라고 하기 어렵고 제게는 알아들을 수도 없는 울부짖음으로 제 주인의 귀가를 환영했어요."], ["In short, the whole scene was so gloomy and melancholy, that it threw my spirits into the lowest dejection; which my husband discerning, instead of relieving, encreased by two or three malicious observations.", "한마디로 그 광경 전체가 어찌나 음울하고 우울하던지, 제 기분은 더없이 깊은 낙담 속으로 가라앉았어요. 남편은 그것을 알아채고도 달래기는커녕, 두세 마디 심술궂은 말로 더 부추겼어요."], ["`There are good houses, madam,'", "'부인,' 그가 말했어요."], ["says he, `as you find, in other places besides England; but perhaps you had rather be in a dirty lodgings at Bath.'", "'보다시피 잉글랜드 말고 다른 곳에도 훌륭한 집은 있는 법이오. 하지만 부인은 차라리 바스의 지저분한 셋방에 있는 편이 낫겠지.'\\n\\n* 《고아》: 토머스 오트웨이의 비극 〈The Orphan〉을 가리킨다."]], [["“Happy, my dear, is the woman who, in any state of life, hath a cheerful good-natured companion to support and comfort her!", "\"어떤 처지에 있든, 받쳐 주고 위로해 줄 명랑하고 너그러운 동반자를 둔 여인은 행복한 사람이에요, 그대!"], ["But why do I reflect on happy situations only to aggravate my own misery?", "하지만 제가 어째서 제 비참함을 더 키우려고 행복한 처지를 떠올리는 걸까요?"], ["my companion, far from clearing up the gloom of solitude, soon convinced me that I must have been wretched with him in any place, and in any condition.", "제 동반자는 고독의 음울함을 걷어 주기는커녕, 어디서든 어떤 형편에서든 그와 함께라면 비참할 수밖에 없다는 것을 곧 제게 깨우쳐 주었어요. 한마디로 그는 무뚝뚝하고 심술궂은 사람이었어요."], ["In a word, he was a surly fellow, a character perhaps you have never seen; for, indeed, no woman ever sees it exemplified but in a father, a brother, or a husband; and, though you have a father, he is not of that character.", "당신은 어쩌면 그런 부류를 한 번도 본 적이 없을 거예요. 사실 여자는 아버지든, 오빠든, 남편이든, 그런 사람을 통해서만 그 본보기를 보게 되니까요."], ["This surly fellow had formerly appeared to me the very reverse, and so he did still to every other person.", "게다가 당신에게는 아버지가 계시지만, 그분은 그런 부류가 아니지요. 이 무뚝뚝한 사람은 한때 제게는 정반대로 보였고, 다른 모든 이에게는 지금도 여전히 그렇게 보이지요."], ["Good heaven!", "맙소사!"], ["how is it possible for a man to maintain a constant lie in his appearance abroad and in company, and to content himself with shewing disagreeable truth only at home?", "어떻게 한 사람이 밖에서, 사람들 사이에서는 한결같이 거짓 모습을 꾸미면서, 불쾌한 진실은 오로지 집 안에서만 드러내는 것으로 만족할 수 있을까요?"], ["Here, my dear, they make themselves amends for the uneasy restraint which they put on their tempers in the world; for I have observed, the more merry and gay and good-humoured my husband hath at any time been in company, the more sullen and morose he was sure to become at our next private meeting.", "바로 여기서, 그대, 그들은 세상에서 성미를 억누르느라 받은 불편한 속박을 스스로 보상받는 거예요. 제가 보니, 남편이 사람들 사이에서 더 유쾌하고 밝고 너그럽게 굴수록, 다음번 둘만의 자리에서는 어김없이 더 시무룩하고 까칠해지더군요."], ["How shall I describe his barbarity?", "그의 야만스러움을 어떻게 다 그려 낼까요?"], ["To my fondness he was cold and insensible.", "그는 제 애정에 차갑고 무감각했어요."], ["My little comical ways, which you, my Sophy, and which others, have called so agreeable, he treated with contempt.", "당신, 제 소피아가, 또 다른 이들이 그토록 사랑스럽다 일컬어 주던 제 소소하고 익살스러운 모습들을, 그는 경멸로 대했어요."], ["In my most serious moments he sung and whistled; and whenever I was thoroughly dejected and miserable he was angry, and abused me: for, though he was never pleased with my good-humour, nor ascribed it to my satisfaction in him, yet my low spirits always offended him, and those he imputed to my repentance of having (as he said) married an Irishman.", "제가 더없이 진지한 순간에는 노래를 흥얼대고 휘파람을 불었으며, 제가 완전히 의기소침하고 비참해 있을 때면 화를 내며 저를 모욕했어요. 제 명랑함을 결코 기뻐하지 않았고 그것을 그에게 만족하는 표시로 여기지도 않았으면서, 제 가라앉은 기분만큼은 늘 그의 비위를 거슬렀고, 그것을 (그의 말마따나) 아일랜드 사람과 결혼한 일을 후회하는 탓으로 돌렸으니까요."]], [["“You will easily conceive, my dear Graveairs (I ask your pardon, I really forgot myself), that, when a woman makes an imprudent match in the sense of the world, that is, when she is not an arrant prostitute to pecuniary interest, she must necessarily have some inclination and affection for her man.", "\"쉽게 짐작하겠지요, 사랑하는 그레이베어스—아, 미안해요, 정말 깜빡했네요—세상의 기준으로 경솔한 결혼을 한 여자라 해도, 다시 말해 그 여자가 돈벌이만 좇는 노골적인 매춘부가 아닌 이상, 그 남자에게 얼마간의 끌림과 애정은 반드시 품기 마련이라는 것을요. 그리고 그 애정이 줄어들 수도 있다는 것 또한 쉽게 믿어지겠지요."], ["You will as easily believe that this affection may possibly be lessened; nay, I do assure you, contempt will wholly eradicate it.", "아니, 분명히 말해 두는데, 경멸은 그것을 송두리째 뿌리 뽑아 버려요. 제가 이제 남편에게 품기 시작한 것이 바로 그 경멸이었어요."], ["This contempt I now began to entertain for my husband, whom I now discovered to be--I must use the expression--an arrant blockhead.", "저는 그가—이 표현을 쓸 수밖에 없네요—완전한 멍청이라는 것을 비로소 알아차렸으니까요. 어쩌면 당신은 제가 진작에 이를 알아채지 못한 것을 의아해할지도 몰라요."], ["Perhaps you will wonder I did not make this discovery long before; but women will suggest a thousand excuses to themselves for the folly of those they like: besides, give me leave to tell you, it requires a most penetrating eye to discern a fool through the disguises of gaiety and good breeding.", "하지만 여자란 자기가 좋아하는 사람의 어리석음에 대해서는 천 가지 변명을 스스로 지어내는 법이에요. 게다가 한마디 하자면, 쾌활함과 좋은 교양이라는 가면 너머로 바보를 알아보려면 더없이 날카로운 눈이 필요하답니다."]], [["“It will be easily imagined that, when I once despised my husband, as I confess to you I soon did, I must consequently dislike his company; and indeed I had the happiness of being very little troubled with it; for our house was now most elegantly furnished, our cellars well stocked, and dogs and horses provided in great abundance. As my gentleman therefore entertained his neighbours with great hospitality, so his neighbours resorted to him with great alacrity; and sports and drinking consumed so much of his time, that a small part of his conversation, that is to say, of his ill-humours, fell to my share.", "\"제가 일단 남편을 경멸하게 되자—고백하건대 곧 그렇게 되었지요—결국 그와 함께 있는 것을 싫어하게 되었으리라는 것도 쉽게 짐작할 거예요. 그런데 실은 그와 함께 있어야 하는 일이 거의 없어 다행이었어요. 우리 집은 이제 더없이 우아하게 꾸며졌고, 지하 저장고에는 술이 그득했으며, 개와 말도 넘치도록 갖추어졌으니까요. 그래서 제 신랑은 이웃들을 후하게 대접했고, 이웃들도 기꺼이 그를 찾아왔어요. 사냥과 술자리가 그의 시간을 어찌나 많이 잡아먹던지, 그의 대화—다시 말해 그의 고약한 성미—는 그 작은 일부만이 제 몫으로 떨어졌지요."]], [["“Happy would it have been for me if I could as easily have avoided all other disagreeable company; but, alas!", "\"다른 불쾌한 동무들도 그처럼 쉽게 피할 수 있었다면 제게 얼마나 좋았을까요. 하지만, 아아!"], ["I was confined to some which constantly tormented me; and the more, as I saw no prospect of being relieved from them.", "저는 끊임없이 저를 괴롭히는 어떤 것들에 갇혀 있었어요. 게다가 거기서 벗어날 가망이 보이지 않았기에 더욱 그러했지요."], ["These companions were my own racking thoughts, which plagued and in a manner haunted me night and day.", "그 동무들이란 바로 저를 들볶는 제 자신의 생각들이었어요. 그것들은 밤낮으로 저를 괴롭히며, 말하자면 유령처럼 저를 따라다녔어요."], ["In this situation I past through a scene, the horrors of which can neither be painted nor imagined.", "그런 처지에서 저는 한 장면을 통과했는데, 그 끔찍함은 그림으로도 그려 낼 수 없고 상상으로도 떠올릴 수 없을 거예요."], ["Think, my dear, figure, if you can, to yourself, what I must have undergone.", "생각해 보세요, 그대—할 수 있다면 제가 무엇을 견뎌야 했을지 머릿속에 그려 보세요."], ["I became a mother by the man I scorned, hated, and detested.", "저는 제가 멸시하고 미워하고 혐오하는 남자의 아이를 낳는 어미가 되었어요."], ["I went through all the agonies and miseries of a lying-in (ten times more painful in such a circumstance than the worst labour can be when one endures it for a man one loves) in a desert, or rather, indeed, a scene of riot and revel, without a friend, without a companion, or without any of those agreeable circumstances which often alleviate, and perhaps sometimes more than compensate, the sufferings of our sex at that season.”", "저는 산고의 온갖 고통과 비참함을 다 겪었지요. (그런 처지에서의 산고는, 사랑하는 남자를 위해 겪는 가장 모진 진통보다도 열 배는 더 괴로운 거예요.) 그것도 황량한 사막에서, 아니 오히려 흥청대고 떠들썩한 난장판 한가운데서, 벗 하나 없이, 동무 하나 없이, 또 그 시기에 우리 여자들의 고통을 흔히 덜어 주고 때로는 그 이상으로 메워 주기도 하는 그 어떤 다정한 정경 하나 없이 말이에요.\""]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["In which the mistake of the landlord throws Sophia into a dreadful consternation.", "6장. 여관 주인의 오해로 소피아가 끔찍한 공포에 빠지는 이야기."]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick was proceeding in her narrative when she was interrupted by the entrance of dinner, greatly to the concern of Sophia; for the misfortunes of her friend had raised her anxiety, and left her no appetite but what Mrs Fitzpatrick was to satisfy by her relation.", "피츠패트릭 부인이 한창 이야기를 이어 가던 참에, 저녁 식사가 들어오면서 말이 끊겼다. 소피아로서는 무척 안타까운 일이었다. 벗의 불행이 그녀의 마음을 졸이게 한 터라, 피츠패트릭 부인의 이야기로나 채워질 식욕 말고는 어떤 입맛도 남아 있지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["The landlord now attended with a plate under his arm, and with the same respect in his countenance and address which he would have put on had the ladies arrived in a coach and six.", "이윽고 여관 주인이 접시 하나를 옆구리에 낀 채 나타났다. 그 얼굴과 말투에는, 마치 두 숙녀가 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차를 타고 도착하기라도 한 듯한 정중함이 그대로 깃들어 있었다."]], [["The married lady seemed less affected with her own misfortunes than was her cousin; for the former eat very heartily, whereas the latter could hardly swallow a morsel. Sophia likewise showed more concern and sorrow in her countenance than appeared in the other lady; who, having observed these symptoms in her friend, begged her to be comforted, saying, “Perhaps all may yet end better than either you or I expect.”", "결혼한 쪽의 부인은 사촌보다 자신의 불행에 덜 흔들리는 듯 보였다. 앞쪽은 아주 맛있게 먹었으나, 뒤쪽은 한 입조차 좀처럼 삼키지 못했다. 소피아 역시 다른 부인보다 더 짙은 근심과 슬픔을 얼굴에 드러내고 있었다. 벗에게서 이런 기색을 알아챈 피츠패트릭 부인이 그녀에게 마음을 다잡으라며 말했다. \"어쩌면 모든 일이 너나 나의 예상보다 더 잘 끝날지도 몰라.\""]], [["Our landlord thought he had now an opportunity to open his mouth, and was resolved not to omit it.", "이제 여관 주인은 입을 열 기회가 왔다고 여겼고, 그 기회를 놓치지 않기로 마음먹었다. \"마님,\""], ["“I am sorry, madam,”", "그가 외쳤다. \"마님께서 드시질 못하니 안타깝습니다."], ["cries he, “that your ladyship can't eat; for to be sure you must be hungry after so long fasting.", "이렇게 오래 굶으셨으니 분명 시장하실 텐데요. 부디 무슨 일로 마음 졸이지 않으시길 바랍니다."], ["I hope your ladyship is not uneasy at anything, for, as madam there says, all may end better than anybody expects.", "저기 저 마님 말씀대로, 모든 일이 누구의 예상보다 더 잘 풀릴 수도 있으니까요. 방금 여기 다녀가신 어떤 신사분이 아주 반가운 소식을 전해 주셨습죠."], ["A gentleman who was here just now brought excellent news; and perhaps some folks who have given other folks the slip may get to London before they are overtaken; and if they do, I make no doubt but they will find people who will be very ready to receive them.”", "어쩌면 남을 따돌리고 빠져나간 어떤 분들은 따라잡히기 전에 런던에 닿을 수도 있겠고요. 그렇게만 된다면, 그분들을 기꺼이 맞아 줄 사람들을 분명히 만나게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["All persons under the apprehension of danger convert whatever they see and hear into the objects of that apprehension. Sophia therefore immediately concluded, from the foregoing speech, that she was known, and pursued by her father. She was now struck with the utmost consternation, and for a few minutes deprived of the power of speech; which she no sooner recovered than she desired the landlord to send his servants out of the room, and then, addressing herself to him, said, “I perceive, sir, you know who we are; but I beseech you--nay, I am convinced, if you have any compassion or goodness, you will not betray us.”", "위험을 두려워하는 사람은 누구나 보고 듣는 모든 것을 그 두려움의 대상으로 바꿔 버린다. 그래서 소피아는 앞선 말로 미루어, 자신이 들통났으며 아버지가 뒤쫓고 있다고 곧바로 단정해 버렸다. 그녀는 극심한 공포에 사로잡혀 잠시 말할 힘조차 잃었다. 겨우 정신을 차리자마자, 그녀는 주인에게 하인들을 방에서 내보내 달라고 청한 뒤, 그를 향해 말했다. \"주인장, 보아하니 우리가 누구인지 아시는군요. 하지만 간청합니다—아니, 저는 확신합니다. 주인장께 조금이라도 동정심이나 선한 마음이 있다면, 우리를 배신하지 않으시리라는 걸요.\""]], [["“I betray your ladyship!”", "\"제가 마님을 배신한다고요!\" 주인이 말했다."], ["quoth the landlord; “no (and then he swore several very hearty oaths); I would sooner be cut into ten thousand pieces.", "\"아니지요(그러고는 아주 진심 어린 맹세를 몇 마디 늘어놓았다). 차라리 제 몸이 만 조각으로 잘리는 편이 낫습니다."], ["I hate all treachery.", "저는 배신이라면 질색입니다."], ["I!", "제가요!"], ["I never betrayed any one in my life yet, and I am sure I shall not begin with so sweet a lady as your ladyship.", "저는 평생 누구도 배신해 본 적이 없는데, 하물며 마님처럼 어여쁜 분으로 그 시작을 끊을 리가 있겠습니까. 그랬다간 온 세상이 저를 크게 나무랄 겁니다."], ["All the world would very much blame me if I should, since it will be in your ladyship's power so shortly to reward me.", "머지않아 마님께서 저에게 보답해 주실 수 있는 처지이실 테니까요. 제 아내가 증인입니다."], ["My wife can witness for me, I knew your ladyship the moment you came into the house: I said it was your honour, before I lifted you from your horse, and I shall carry the bruises I got in your ladyship's service to the grave; but what signified that, as long as I saved your ladyship?", "마님께서 이 집에 들어서신 그 순간 저는 마님을 알아보았습니다. 말에서 내려 드리기도 전에 이분이 바로 그 귀하신 분이라고 말했습죠."], ["To be sure some people this morning would have thought of getting a reward; but no such thought ever entered into my head.", "그리고 마님을 모시다 얻은 이 멍은 무덤까지 안고 갈 겁니다. 하지만 마님을 구해 드릴 수만 있었다면 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까?"], ["I would sooner starve than take any reward for betraying your ladyship.”", "분명 오늘 아침 어떤 사람들은 상금 받을 궁리를 했겠지만, 제 머릿속엔 그런 생각이 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없습니다. 마님을 배신하고 상금을 받느니 차라리 굶어 죽고 말겠습니다.\""]], [["“I promise you, sir,”", "\"약속하지요, 주인장,\""], ["says Sophia, “if it be ever in my power to reward you, you shall not lose by your generosity.”", "소피아가 말했다. \"언젠가 제게 보답할 힘이 생긴다면, 당신의 그 너그러움이 헛되지 않게 하겠어요.\""]], [["“Alack-a-day, madam!”", "\"아이고, 마님!\" 여관 주인이 대답했다."], ["answered the landlord; “in your ladyship's power!", "\"마님 손안에 있다니요! 부디 하늘이 그 일을 마님의 뜻에 그만큼 담아 주시기를!"], ["Heaven put it as much into your will!", "다만 저는 마님께서 여관 주인 따위 가난한 사람을 잊으실까 봐 걱정될 뿐입니다."], ["I am only afraid your honour will forget such a poor man as an innkeeper; but, if your ladyship should not, I hope you will remember what reward I refused--refused!", "하오나 마님께서 잊지 않으신다면, 제가 어떤 상금을 마다했는지—마다했다! 그러니까, 마다했을 거라는 말씀이고, 분명 마다했다고 부를 만하지요."], ["that is, I would have refused, and to be sure it may be called refusing, for I might have had it certainly; and to be sure you might have been in some houses;--but, for my part, would not methinks for the world have your ladyship wrong me so much as to imagine I ever thought of betraying you, even before I heard the good news.”", "마음만 먹었으면 틀림없이 받을 수 있었으니까요. 그리고 마님께선 다른 어떤 집에 묵으셨을 수도 있고요—허나 저로 말하자면, 그 반가운 소식을 듣기도 전부터, 제가 마님을 배신할 생각을 단 한 번이라도 품었으리라 여기시며 저를 그토록 오해하시는 일만은 세상 무엇과도 바꾸고 싶지 않습니다.\""]], [["“What news, pray?”", "\"무슨 소식 말인가요?\""], ["says Sophia, something eagerly.", "소피아가 다소 다급하게 물었다."]], [["“Hath not your ladyship heard it, then?” cries the landlord; “nay, like enough, for I heard it only a few minutes ago; and if I had never heard it, may the devil fly away with me this instant if I would have betrayed your honour! no, if I would, may I--” Here he subjoined several dreadful imprecations, which Sophia at last interrupted, and begged to know what he meant by the news.--He was going to answer, when Mrs Honour came running into the room, all pale and breathless, and cried out, “Madam, we are all undone, all ruined, they are come, they are come!” These words almost froze up the blood of Sophia; but Mrs Fitzpatrick asked Honour who were come?--“Who?” answered she, “why, the French; several hundred thousands of them are landed, and we shall be all murdered and ravished.”", "\"그럼 마님께선 아직 못 들으셨군요?\" 여관 주인이 외쳤다. \"하긴 그럴 만도 하지요. 저도 불과 몇 분 전에야 들었으니까요. 게다가 제가 그 소식을 영영 못 들었다 한들, 제가 마님을 배신했다면 당장 이 순간 악마가 저를 채 가도 좋습니다! 아니, 제가 그랬다면, 저는—\" 여기서 그는 끔찍한 저주를 몇 마디 덧붙였고, 마침내 소피아가 그를 가로막으며 그 소식이 도대체 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었다. 그가 막 대답하려는데, 어너 부인이 새파랗게 질려 숨을 헐떡이며 방으로 뛰어 들어와 소리쳤다. \"마님, 우리 모두 끝장났어요, 다 망했어요, 그들이 왔어요, 그들이 왔다고요!\" 이 말에 소피아의 피는 거의 얼어붙을 지경이었다. 그러나 피츠패트릭 부인은 어너에게 누가 왔느냐고 물었다. \"누구라뇨?\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"아니, 프랑스군이요, 수십만이 상륙했답니다. 우리는 다 살해당하고 욕을 당할 거예요.\""]], [["As a miser, who hath, in some well-built city, a cottage, value twenty shillings, when at a distance he is alarmed with the news of a fire, turns pale and trembles at his loss; but when he finds the beautiful palaces only are burnt, and his own cottage remains safe, he comes instantly to himself, and smiles at his good fortunes: or as (for we dislike something in the former simile) the tender mother, when terrified with the apprehension that her darling boy is drowned, is struck senseless and almost dead with consternation; but when she is told that little master is safe, and the Victory only, with twelve hundred brave men, gone to the bottom, life and sense again return, maternal fondness enjoys the sudden relief from all its fears, and the general benevolence which at another time would have deeply felt the dreadful catastrophe, lies fast asleep in her mind;--so Sophia, than whom none was more capable of tenderly feeling the general calamity of her country, found such immediate satisfaction from the relief of those terrors she had of being overtaken by her father, that the arrival of the French scarce made any impression on her. She gently chid her maid for the fright into which she had thrown her, and said “she was glad it was no worse; for that she had feared somebody else was come.”", "잘 지어진 어느 도시에 스무 실링짜리 오두막 한 채를 가진 구두쇠가 멀리서 불이 났다는 소식에 놀라, 자기 손해에 새파래져 부들부들 떨다가도, 막상 화려한 궁전들만 타고 자기 오두막은 멀쩡히 남아 있음을 알게 되면 단숨에 제정신을 되찾고 제 행운에 미소 짓는 것처럼. 아니면(앞의 비유가 어딘가 마음에 들지 않으니), 사랑하는 아들이 물에 빠졌다는 두려움에 휩싸여 정신을 잃고 공포로 거의 죽을 지경이던 자애로운 어머니가, 어린 도련님은 무사하고 다만 빅토리호가 용맹한 천이백 명과 함께 바닷속으로 가라앉았을 뿐이라는 말을 듣자 다시 목숨과 정신이 돌아오고, 모성의 애정은 온갖 두려움에서 풀려난 갑작스러운 안도를 만끽하며, 다른 때 같았으면 그 끔찍한 참사에 깊이 마음 아파했을 그 보편적 인정은 그녀의 마음속에 깊이 잠들어 버리는 것처럼. 그렇게 소피아도—제 나라의 보편적 재앙을 그 누구보다 가슴 아프게 느낄 수 있는 사람이었건만—아버지에게 따라잡힐지 모른다는 그 공포에서 풀려난 안도가 어찌나 즉각적인 만족을 주었던지, 프랑스군이 상륙했다는 소식은 그녀에게 거의 아무런 인상도 남기지 못했다. 그녀는 자신을 그토록 놀라게 한 하녀를 부드럽게 나무라며, \"더 나쁜 일이 아니라 다행이구나. 나는 다른 누가 온 줄 알고 겁이 났단다\" 하고 말했다."]], [["“Ay, ay,”", "\"옳지요, 옳지요,\" 여관 주인이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["quoth the landlord, smiling, “her ladyship knows better things; she knows the French are our very best friends, and come over hither only for our good.", "\"마님께선 사정을 잘 아시는군요. 프랑스인이야말로 우리의 가장 좋은 벗이고, 오직 우리 잘되라고 이리로 건너오는 것이지요."], ["They are the people who are to make Old England flourish again.", "늙은 잉글랜드를 다시 번성하게 해 줄 사람들이 바로 그들입니다. 분명 마님께서는 공작이 오는 줄 아셨겠지요."], ["I warrant her honour thought the duke was coming; and that was enough to put her into a fright.", "그것만으로도 겁이 나실 만하지요. 제가 막 마님께 그 소식을 전하려던 참이었습니다."], ["I was going to tell your ladyship the news.--His honour's majesty, Heaven bless him, hath given the duke the slip, and is marching as fast as he can to London, and ten thousand French are landed to join him on the road.”", "부디 하늘이 보살피시기를, 폐하께서 공작을 따돌리시고 한사코 빠른 걸음으로 런던을 향해 진군 중이시랍니다. 게다가 도중에 합류하려고 프랑스군 만 명이 상륙했고요.\""]], [["Sophia was not greatly pleased with this news, nor with the gentleman who related it; but, as she still imagined he knew her (for she could not possibly have any suspicion of the real truth), she durst not show any dislike.", "소피아는 이 소식도, 그것을 전한 사내도 그다지 반갑지 않았다. 그러나 여전히 그가 자신을 알아보았다고 여겼기에(진짜 진실이야 그녀로서는 도무지 짐작할 수조차 없었으므로), 감히 싫은 기색을 내비치지 못했다."], ["And now the landlord, having removed the cloth from the table, withdrew; but at his departure frequently repeated his hopes of being remembered hereafter.", "이윽고 여관 주인은 식탁보를 걷어 내고 물러갔는데, 떠나면서도 훗날 자신을 기억해 달라는 바람을 거듭 되풀이했다."]], [["The mind of Sophia was not at all easy under the supposition of being known at this house; for she still applied to herself many things which the landlord had addressed to Jenny Cameron; she therefore ordered her maid to pump out of him by what means he had become acquainted with her person, and who had offered him the reward for betraying her; she likewise ordered the horses to be in readiness by four in the morning, at which hour Mrs Fitzpatrick promised to bear her company; and then, composing herself as well as she could, she desired that lady to continue her story.", "이 집에서 자신이 들통났다는 짐작에 소피아의 마음은 조금도 편치 않았다. 여관 주인이 제니 캐머런에게 건넨 여러 말을 그녀는 여전히 자신에게 끌어다 붙이고 있었기 때문이다. 그래서 그녀는 하녀에게 일러, 그가 무슨 수로 자기 신분을 알게 되었는지, 또 누가 자신을 배신하는 대가로 상금을 내걸었는지 그에게서 캐내라고 시켰다. 또한 새벽 네 시까지 말을 준비해 두라고 일렀는데, 그 시각에 피츠패트릭 부인이 함께 길을 나서겠다고 약속한 터였다. 그런 다음 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 마음을 가라앉히고, 그 부인에게 이야기를 계속해 달라고 청했다."]], [["In which Mrs Fitzpatrick concludes her history.", "피츠패트릭 부인이 자신의 이야기를 마무리하는 장."]], [["While Mrs Honour, in pursuance of the commands of her mistress, ordered a bowl of punch, and invited my landlord and landlady to partake of it, Mrs Fitzpatrick thus went on with her relation.", "오너 부인이 안주인의 분부에 따라 펀치 한 사발을 시키고, 집주인 내외를 불러 함께 들자고 권하는 동안, 피츠패트릭 부인은 이렇게 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“Most of the officers who were quartered at a town in our neighbourhood were of my husband's acquaintance.", "\"우리 동네 인근 마을에 주둔한 장교들은 대부분 제 남편과 아는 사이였어요. 그 가운데 중위 한 사람이 있었는데, 아주 멋진 사람이었고, 성품으로 보나 대화로 보나 더없이 상냥한 여인과 결혼한 처지였지요."], ["Among these there was a lieutenant, a very pretty sort of man, and who was married to a woman, so agreeable both in her temper and conversation, that from our first knowing each other, which was soon after my lying-in, we were almost inseparable companions; for I had the good fortune to make myself equally agreeable to her.", "제가 산후조리를 마친 직후 처음 서로 알게 된 그때부터, 우리는 거의 떨어질 수 없는 동무가 되었답니다. 저 또한 그 여인에게 그만큼 마음에 드는 사람이 되는 행운을 누렸으니까요."]], [["“The lieutenant, who was neither a sot nor a sportsman, was frequently of our parties; indeed he was very little with my husband, and no more than good breeding constrained him to be, as he lived almost constantly at our house. My husband often expressed much dissatisfaction at the lieutenant's preferring my company to his; he was very angry with me on that account, and gave me many a hearty curse for drawing away his companions; saying, `I ought to be d--n'd for having spoiled one of the prettiest fellows in the world, by making a milksop of him.'", "\"그 중위는 술꾼도 사냥꾼도 아니었던 터라, 우리 모임에 자주 끼었어요. 사실 제 남편하고는 어울리는 일이 거의 없었고, 그저 예의가 강요하는 만큼만 함께했을 뿐이었지요. 그는 거의 늘 우리 집에서 지내다시피 했으니까요. 제 남편은 중위가 자기보다 제 곁을 더 좋아한다며 자주 큰 불만을 드러냈어요. 그 일로 제게 몹시 화를 냈고, 제가 자기 동무를 빼앗아 간다며 욕설을 마구 퍼부었답니다. 이러더군요. '세상에서 가장 멋진 친구 중 하나를 물러 터진 겁쟁이로 만들어 망쳐 놓았으니, 나는 저주를 받아 마땅하지.'"]], [["“You will be mistaken, my dear Sophia, if you imagine that the anger of my husband arose from my depriving him of a companion; for the lieutenant was not a person with whose society a fool could be pleased; and, if I should admit the possibility of this, so little right had my husband to place the loss of his companion to me, that I am convinced it was my conversation alone which induced him ever to come to the house.", "\"사랑하는 소피아, 제 남편의 분노가 제가 동무를 빼앗아 간 데서 비롯되었다고 여긴다면 그건 오해예요. 그 중위는 어리석은 자가 어울려 즐거울 만한 사람이 아니었거든요."], ["No, child, it was envy, the worst and most rancorous kind of envy, the envy of superiority of understanding.", "설령 그럴 가능성을 인정한다 해도, 제 남편이 동무를 잃은 책임을 제게 돌릴 권리는 거의 없었어요. 애초에 그 사람을 집에 오게 한 것은 오직 저와의 대화뿐이었다고 저는 확신하니까요."], ["The wretch could not bear to see my conversation preferred to his, by a man of whom he could not entertain the least jealousy.", "아니에요, 얘야, 그건 시기였어요. 가장 고약하고 가장 악독한 종류의 시기, 곧 더 뛰어난 지성에 대한 시기 말이에요."], ["O my dear Sophy, you are a woman of sense; if you marry a man, as is most probable you will, of less capacity than yourself, make frequent trials of his temper before marriage, and see whether he can bear to submit to such a superiority.--Promise me, Sophy, you will take this advice; for you will hereafter find its importance.”", "그 한심한 작자는, 조금도 질투할 구석이 없는 남자가 자기보다 저와의 대화를 더 좋아하는 꼴을 견딜 수 없었던 거예요. 오, 사랑하는 소피, 너는 분별 있는 여자야."], ["“It is very likely I shall never marry at all,”", "만일 네가 결혼을 한다면—아마 그러겠지만—너보다 못한 그릇의 남자와 맺어질 가능성이 크니, 결혼 전에 그 사람의 성품을 자주 시험해 보고, 그런 우월함에 굽힐 줄 아는 사람인지 잘 살펴보렴. 약속해 다오, 소피."], ["answered Sophia; “I think, at least, I shall never marry a man in whose understanding I see any defects before marriage; and I promise you I would rather give up my own than see any such afterwards.”", "이 충고를 받아들이겠다고. 나중에 가서야 그 중요함을 깨닫게 될 테니까.\""], ["“Give up your understanding!”", "\"저는 아마 영영 결혼하지 않을 것 같아요.\" 소피아가 대답했다."], ["replied Mrs Fitzpatrick; “oh, fie, child!", "\"적어도, 결혼 전에 그 지성에 어떤 흠이 보이는 남자와는 결코 결혼하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 약속드리는데, 저는 결혼한 뒤에 그런 흠을 보느니 차라리 제 지성을 포기하는 편을 택하겠어요.\""], ["I will not believe so meanly of you.", "\"네 지성을 포기한다고!\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 받아쳤다."], ["Everything else I might myself be brought to give up; but never this.", "\"오, 저런, 얘야! 나는 너를 그토록 하찮게 여기진 않을 거야."], ["Nature would not have allotted this superiority to the wife in so many instances, if she had intended we should all of us have surrendered it to the husband.", "다른 건 다 내놓으라면 나도 못 이겨 내놓을지 모르지만, 이것만은 절대 안 돼. 만일 자연이 우리 모두에게 그 우월함을 남편에게 넘겨주기를 바랐다면, 이토록 많은 경우에 아내에게 그것을 나누어 주지 않았을 거야."], ["This, indeed, men of sense never expect of us; of which the lieutenant I have just mentioned was one notable example; for though he had a very good understanding, he always acknowledged (as was really true) that his wife had a better.", "사실, 분별 있는 남자라면 우리에게 그런 걸 결코 기대하지 않아. 방금 말한 그 중위가 바로 그 두드러진 본보기였지."], ["And this, perhaps, was one reason of the hatred my tyrant bore her.", "그는 무척 좋은 지성을 지녔으면서도, 아내가 자기보다 더 낫다는 걸 늘 인정했거든—실제로도 그게 사실이었고. 그리고 어쩌면 이것이, 그 폭군 같은 내 남편이 그 부인을 미워한 한 가지 이유였을 거야."]], [["“Before he would be so governed by a wife, he said, especially such an ugly b-- (for, indeed, she was not a regular beauty, but very agreeable and extremely genteel), he would see all the women upon earth at the devil, which was a very usual phrase with him. He said, he wondered what I could see in her to be so charmed with her company: since this woman, says he, hath come among us, there is an end of your beloved reading, which you pretended to like so much, that you could not afford time to return the visits of the ladies in this country; and I must confess I had been guilty of a little rudeness this way; for the ladies there are at least no better than the mere country ladies here; and I think I need make no other excuse to you for declining any intimacy with them.", "\"그는 아내에게, 그것도 그렇게 못생긴 계집(사실 그 부인이 반듯한 미인은 아니었지만 무척 상냥하고 더없이 우아했답니다)에게 휘둘릴 바에야 차라리 세상의 모든 여자가 악마에게 끌려가는 꼴을 보겠다더군요—그건 그가 입버릇처럼 쓰던 말이었어요. 그 여자한테 대체 뭘 봤기에 그렇게 함께 있는 걸 즐거워하는지 모르겠다며, 이렇게 말했죠. '이 여자가 우리 사이에 끼어든 뒤로 당신이 그토록 좋아한다던 그 잘난 독서도 끝장이 났군. 어찌나 좋아했으면 이 고장 부인들 답방할 틈조차 못 낸다고 했었나.' 솔직히 고백하자면, 이 점에선 제가 조금 무례했던 게 사실이에요. 거기 부인들이래야 적어도 여기 시골 부인들보다 나을 게 없었거든요. 그러니 그들과 가까이 지내기를 마다한 까닭에 대해서는 너한테 달리 변명할 필요도 없을 것 같구나."]], [["“This correspondence, however, continued a whole year, even all the while the lieutenant was quartered in that town; for which I was contented to pay the tax of being constantly abused in the manner above mentioned by my husband; I mean when he was at home; for he was frequently absent a month at a time at Dublin, and once made a journey of two months to London: in all which journeys I thought it a very singular happiness that he never once desired my company; nay, by his frequent censures on men who could not travel, as he phrased it, without a wife tied up to their tail, he sufficiently intimated that, had I been never so desirous of accompanying him, my wishes would have been in vain; but, Heaven knows, such wishes were very far from my thoughts.", "\"하지만 이 교제는 그 중위가 그 마을에 주둔해 있던 내내, 꼬박 한 해 동안 이어졌어요. 그 대가로 저는 앞서 말한 식으로 남편에게 줄곧 욕먹는 세금을 기꺼이 치렀지요. 물론 남편이 집에 있을 때 말이에요. 그는 자주 한 달씩 더블린에 나가 있었고, 한 번은 두 달이나 런던으로 여행을 떠나기도 했거든요. 그 모든 여행에서 그가 단 한 번도 제 동행을 원치 않았다는 게 저에겐 참으로 유별난 행운으로 여겨졌답니다. 아니, 그는 그가 말하던 식으로 '아내를 꽁무니에 매달지 않고는 여행도 못 하는' 남자들을 자주 헐뜯음으로써, 설령 제가 아무리 따라가고 싶어 한들 그 바람이 헛수고였으리란 걸 충분히 내비쳤어요. 하지만 하늘은 아시지요, 그런 바람은 제 생각에서 한참이나 멀리 있었답니다."]], [["“At length my friend was removed from me, and I was again left to my solitude, to the tormenting conversation with my own reflections, and to apply to books for my only comfort.", "\"마침내 제 벗이 제 곁에서 떠나갔고, 저는 다시 외로움 속에, 제 자신의 생각과 나누는 그 괴로운 대화 속에 홀로 남겨졌어요. 그러고는 오직 책에 기대어 유일한 위안을 찾았지요."], ["I now read almost all day long.", "이제 저는 거의 온종일 책을 읽었어요."], ["How many books do you think I read in three months?”", "제가 석 달 동안 책을 몇 권이나 읽었을 것 같니?\""], ["“I can't guess, indeed, cousin,”", "\"정말 짐작이 안 가요, 언니.\""], ["answered Sophia.", "소피아가 대답했다."], ["“Perhaps half a score.”", "\"어쩌면 열 권쯤?\""], ["“Half a score!", "\"열 권이라니!"], ["half a thousand, child!”", "오백 권이야, 얘야!\""], ["answered the other.", "상대가 답했다."], ["“I read a good deal in Daniel's English History of France; a great deal in Plutarch's Lives, the Atalantis, Pope's Homer, Dryden's Plays, Chillingworth, the Countess D'Aulnois, and Locke's Human Understanding.", "\"대니얼의 『프랑스 영문사』를 꽤 읽었고, 플루타르코스의 『영웅전』, 『아탈란티스』, 포프가 옮긴 호메로스, 드라이든의 희곡들, 칠링워스, 돌누아 백작부인의 글, 그리고 로크의 『인간지성론』도 잔뜩 읽었지."]], [["“During this interval I wrote three very supplicating, and, I thought, moving letters to my aunt; but, as I received no answer to any of them, my disdain would not suffer me to continue my application.” Here she stopt, and, looking earnestly at Sophia, said, “Methinks, my dear, I read something in your eyes which reproaches me of a neglect in another place, where I should have met with a kinder return.” “Indeed, dear Harriet,” answered Sophia, “your story is an apology for any neglect; but, indeed, I feel that I have been guilty of a remissness, without so good an excuse.--Yet pray proceed; for I long, though I tremble, to hear the end.”", "\"이 기간 동안 저는 숙모님께 무척 간절하고, 제 딴엔 마음을 움직일 만한 편지를 세 통이나 썼어요. 하지만 어느 편지에도 답을 받지 못하니, 자존심이 더는 매달리도록 두지 않더군요.\" 여기서 그녀는 말을 멈추고, 소피아를 골똘히 바라보며 말했다. \"얘야, 아무래도 네 눈빛 속에서 무언가를 읽은 것 같구나. 더 다정한 답을 받았어야 마땅한 다른 곳에다 내가 소홀했다고 나무라는 빛 말이야.\" \"정말이지, 사랑하는 해리엇,\" 소피아가 답했다. \"언니의 이야기는 그 어떤 소홀함도 변명해 주는걸요. 하지만 사실, 저야말로 그만한 핑계도 없이 게을렀던 죄가 있다고 느껴요. 그래도 부디 계속해 주세요. 떨리긴 해도, 그 끝을 어서 듣고 싶거든요.\""]], [["Thus, then, Mrs Fitzpatrick resumed her narrative:--“My husband now took a second journey to England, where he continued upwards of three months; during the greater part of this time I led a life which nothing but having led a worse could make me think tolerable; for perfect solitude can never be reconciled to a social mind, like mine, but when it relieves you from the company of those you hate. What added to my wretchedness was the loss of my little infant: not that I pretend to have had for it that extravagant tenderness of which I believe I might have been capable under other circumstances; but I resolved, in every instance, to discharge the duty of the tenderest mother; and this care prevented me from feeling the weight of that heaviest of all things, when it can be at all said to lie heavy on our hands.", "그러자 피츠패트릭 부인은 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다. \"남편은 이제 두 번째로 잉글랜드 여행을 떠나, 거기서 석 달 남짓 머물렀어요. 그 대부분의 시간 동안 저는, 그보다 더한 삶을 겪어 봤기에 비로소 견딜 만하다 여겨지는 그런 삶을 살았답니다. 완전한 고독이란 저처럼 사람을 좋아하는 마음과는 결코 화해할 수 없는 거예요. 다만 미워하는 자들과의 동석에서 벗어나게 해 줄 때만은 예외지요. 제 비참함을 더한 것은 어린 갓난아이를 잃은 일이었어요. 다른 상황이었다면 제가 품을 수도 있었을 그 지나친 애틋함을 그 아이에게 쏟았노라 우길 생각은 없어요. 하지만 저는 매 순간 가장 다정한 어머니의 도리를 다하기로 마음먹었답니다. 그리고 이 보살핌 덕분에, 만일 그것이 우리 손에 무겁게 얹혀 있다고 말할 수 있을 때라면, 세상에서 가장 무거운 그 짐의 무게를 느끼지 않을 수 있었어요."]], [["“I had spent full ten weeks almost entirely by myself, having seen nobody all that time, except my servants and a very few visitors, when a young lady, a relation to my husband, came from a distant part of Ireland to visit me.", "\"저는 꼬박 열 주를 거의 온전히 혼자 보냈어요. 그동안 하인들과 극히 드문 방문객 말고는 아무도 본 적이 없었지요."], ["She had staid once before a week at my house, and then I gave her a pressing invitation to return; for she was a very agreeable woman, and had improved good natural parts by a proper education.", "그러던 차에, 제 남편의 친척인 젊은 부인이 아일랜드의 먼 곳에서 저를 보러 찾아왔답니다. 그녀는 전에도 한 번 우리 집에 일주일을 머문 적이 있었고, 그때 제가 다시 와 달라고 간곡히 청해 두었어요."], ["Indeed, she was to me a welcome guest.", "무척 상냥한 여인인 데다, 타고난 좋은 자질을 알맞은 교육으로 잘 가꾸어 둔 사람이었거든요. 정말이지 저에겐 반가운 손님이었지요."]], [["“A few days after her arrival, perceiving me in very low spirits, without enquiring the cause, which, indeed, she very well knew, the young lady fell to compassionating my case.", "\"그녀가 온 지 며칠 만에, 제가 무척 풀 죽어 있는 걸 알아챈 그 젊은 부인은—사실 그 까닭을 그녀도 잘 알고 있었기에—이유를 묻지도 않고 제 처지를 가엾이 여기기 시작했어요. 그녀가 말하더군요."], ["She said, `Though politeness had prevented me from complaining to my husband's relations of his behaviour, yet they all were very sensible of it, and felt great concern upon that account; but none more than herself.'", "'예의 때문에 제가 남편의 친척들에게 그의 행실을 하소연하지는 않았지만, 그들 모두 그 일을 잘 알고 있었고 그 때문에 무척 마음 아파했어요. 그중에서도 자기가 가장 그러했고요.'"], ["And after some more general discourse on this head, which I own I could not forbear countenancing, at last, after much previous precaution and enjoined concealment, she communicated to me, as a profound secret--that my husband kept a mistress.", "그러고는 이 화제로 좀 더 일반적인 이야기를 주고받은 끝에—사실 저도 맞장구를 치지 않을 수 없었지요—그녀는 마침내 갖은 사전 경계를 거듭하고 비밀로 해 달라 단단히 다짐을 받은 뒤, 더없이 깊은 비밀이라며 제게 알려 주었답니다. 제 남편이 정부를 두고 있다는 사실을요."]], [["“You will certainly imagine I heard this news with the utmost insensibility--Upon my word, if you do, your imagination will mislead you.", "\"너는 분명 내가 이 소식을 더없이 무덤덤하게 들었으리라 상상하겠지. 하지만 맹세컨대, 만일 그렇게 여긴다면 그 상상이 너를 잘못 이끄는 거야."], ["Contempt had not so kept down my anger to my husband, but that hatred rose again on this occasion.", "남편에 대한 경멸이 내 분노를 짓눌러 두긴 했어도, 이 일을 계기로 미움이 다시 솟구쳤거든."], ["What can be the reason of this?", "대체 그 까닭이 무엇일까?"], ["Are we so abominably selfish, that we can be concerned at others having possession even of what we despise?", "우리는 그토록 끔찍이도 이기적이어서, 심지어 우리가 업신여기는 것조차 남이 차지하면 마음이 쓰이는 걸까?"], ["Or are we not rather abominably vain, and is not this the greatest injury done to our vanity?", "아니면 우리는 차라리 끔찍이도 허영심이 많아서, 이것이 우리 허영심에 가해지는 가장 큰 상처인 걸까?"], ["What think you, Sophia?”", "너는 어떻게 생각하니, 소피아?\""]], [["“I don't know, indeed,”", "\"정말 모르겠어요.\" 소피아가 대답했다."], ["answered Sophia; “I have never troubled myself with any of these deep contemplations; but I think the lady did very ill in communicating to you such a secret.”", "\"저는 그런 깊은 생각으로 머리를 썩여 본 적이 없거든요. 다만 그 부인이 언니한테 그런 비밀을 알려 준 건 아주 잘못한 일이라고 생각해요.\""]], [["“And yet, my dear, this conduct is natural,”", "\"그렇지만, 얘야, 그런 행동은 자연스러운 거란다.\""], ["replied Mrs Fitzpatrick; “and, when you have seen and read as much as myself, you will acknowledge it to be so.”", "피츠패트릭 부인이 받았다. \"너도 나만큼 보고 읽어 본다면, 그게 그렇다는 걸 인정하게 될 거야.\""]], [["“I am sorry to hear it is natural,”", "\"그게 자연스러운 일이라니 듣기가 안타깝네요.\" 소피아가 응수했다."], ["returned Sophia; “for I want neither reading nor experience to convince me that it is very dishonourable and very ill-natured: nay, it is surely as ill-bred to tell a husband or wife of the faults of each other as to tell them of their own.”", "\"그게 무척 떳떳지 못하고 무척 심술궂은 짓이라는 건, 책을 읽거나 겪어 보지 않아도 알 수 있으니까요. 아니, 남편이나 아내에게 서로의 허물을 일러바치는 건 분명, 그들 자신의 허물을 일러 주는 것만큼이나 무례한 짓이에요.\""]], [["“Well,” continued Mrs Fitzpatrick, “my husband at last returned; and, if I am thoroughly acquainted with my own thoughts, I hated him now more than ever; but I despised him rather less: for certainly nothing so much weakens our contempt, as an injury done to our pride or our vanity.", "\"그런데,\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 말을 이었다. \"남편이 마침내 돌아왔어. 내 마음을 내가 제대로 안다면, 나는 이제 그를 그 어느 때보다 미워했어. 하지만 업신여기는 마음은 오히려 좀 덜해졌지. 우리의 경멸을 가장 약하게 만드는 건, 분명 우리의 자존심이나 허영심에 가해진 상처거든."]], [["“He now assumed a carriage to me so very different from what he had lately worn, and so nearly resembling his behaviour the first week of our marriage, that, had I now had any spark of love remaining, he might, possibly, have rekindled my fondness for him. But, though hatred may succeed to contempt, and may perhaps get the better of it, love, I believe, cannot. The truth is, the passion of love is too restless to remain contented without the gratification which it receives from its object; and one can no more be inclined to love without loving than we can have eyes without seeing. When a husband, therefore, ceases to be the object of this passion, it is most probable some other man--I say, my dear, if your husband grows indifferent to you--if you once come to despise him--I say--that is--if you have the passion of love in you--Lud! I have bewildered myself so--but one is apt, in these abstracted considerations, to lose the concatenation of ideas, as Mr Locke says:--in short, the truth is--in short, I scarce know what it is; but, as I was saying, my husband returned, and his behaviour, at first, greatly surprized me; but he soon acquainted me with the motive, and taught me to account for it. In a word, then, he had spent and lost all the ready money of my fortune; and, as he could mortgage his own estate no deeper, he was now desirous to supply himself with cash for his extravagance, by selling a little estate of mine, which he could not do without my assistance; and to obtain this favour was the whole and sole motive of all the fondness which he now put on.", "\"그는 이제 최근까지 보이던 것과는 사뭇 다른, 결혼 첫 주의 행동과 거의 흡사한 태도를 내게 취하더구나. 그래서 만일 내게 사랑의 불씨가 한 점이라도 남아 있었다면, 어쩌면 그가 나의 애정을 다시 지펴 놓을 수도 있었을 거야. 하지만 미움은 경멸의 뒤를 이을 수 있고 어쩌면 그것을 이겨 낼 수도 있어도, 사랑은 그러지 못한다고 나는 믿어. 사실인즉, 사랑이라는 정념은 그 대상에게서 받는 만족 없이는 가만히 흡족해할 수 없을 만큼 너무도 들떠 있는 거야. 그래서 눈이 있으면서 보지 않을 수 없듯, 사랑하지 않으면서 사랑하려는 마음을 품을 수도 없는 거지. 그러니 남편이 더 이상 이 정념의 대상이 되지 못하면, 십중팔구 다른 어떤 남자가—그러니까, 얘야, 만일 네 남편이 너한테 무덤덤해진다면—네가 일단 그를 업신여기게 된다면—내 말은—그러니까—네 안에 사랑이라는 정념이 있다면—아이고! 내가 그만 갈피를 잃고 말았네—하지만 사람이 이런 추상적인 생각에 빠지다 보면, 로크 선생 말마따나 관념의 연쇄를 놓치기 쉬운 법이지. 요컨대, 사실인즉—요컨대, 그게 뭔지 나도 잘 모르겠다만—아무튼 말했다시피, 남편이 돌아왔고, 그 행동이 처음엔 나를 무척 놀라게 했어. 하지만 그는 곧 그 속셈을 내게 알려 주었고, 그 까닭을 헤아리게 해 주었지. 한마디로, 그는 내 재산 가운데 당장 쓸 수 있던 돈을 죄다 써서 날려 버렸던 거야. 자기 영지는 더 깊이 저당 잡힐 수도 없으니, 이제 자신의 사치를 위한 현금을 마련하려고 내 작은 영지 하나를 팔고 싶어 했지. 그건 내 도움 없이는 할 수 없는 일이었고. 그가 지금 꾸며 보이던 그 온갖 다정함은, 오로지 이 호의를 얻으려는 것이 전부이자 유일한 속셈이었던 거야."]], [["“With this I peremptorily refused to comply. I told him, and I told him truly, that, had I been possessed of the Indies at our first marriage, he might have commanded it all; for it had been a constant maxim with me, that where a woman disposes of her heart, she should always deposit her fortune; but, as he had been so kind, long ago, to restore the former into my possession, I was resolved likewise to retain what little remained of the latter.", "\"이 일에 대해 나는 단호히 응하지 않겠다고 거절했어. 그에게 말했지, 그것도 진심으로 말이야. 우리가 처음 결혼했을 때 내가 동인도제도를 다 가졌더라도, 그가 그 전부를 마음대로 부릴 수 있었을 거라고. 왜냐하면 여자가 자기 마음을 내어 줄 때는 언제나 재산도 함께 맡겨야 한다는 것이 나의 한결같은 신조였으니까. 하지만 그가 오래전 친절하게도 전자, 곧 내 마음을 도로 내 차지로 돌려주었으니, 나도 후자 가운데 얼마 남지 않은 것만은 똑같이 간직하기로 마음먹었다고 말이야."]], [["“I will not describe to you the passion into which these words, and the resolute air in which they were spoken, threw him: nor will I trouble you with the whole scene which succeeded between us.", "\"이 말과, 그것을 내뱉을 때의 그 단호한 태도가 그를 어떤 광분 속에 몰아넣었는지는 네게 묘사하지 않으마. 그 뒤로 우리 사이에 벌어진 그 모든 장면으로 너를 성가시게 하지도 않을게."], ["Out came, you may be well assured, the story of the mistress; and out it did come, with all the embellishments which anger and disdain could bestow upon it.", "정부 이야기가 튀어나왔다는 건 너도 능히 짐작하겠지. 그리고 그 이야기는 분노와 경멸이 그 위에 덧입힐 수 있는 온갖 치장을 다 걸치고 튀어나왔단다."]], [["“Mr Fitzpatrick seemed a little thunderstruck with this, and more confused than I had seen him, though his ideas are always confused enough, heaven knows.", "\"피츠패트릭 씨는 이 말에 다소 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했고, 내가 일찍이 본 그 어느 때보다 당황한 기색이었어. 뭐, 그의 머릿속이야 하늘이 아시다시피 늘 충분히 뒤죽박죽이지만 말이야."], ["He did not, however, endeavour to exculpate himself; but took a method which almost equally confounded me.", "그래도 그는 자신을 변명하려 들지는 않았어. 대신, 거의 똑같이 나를 어리둥절하게 만드는 수법을 썼지."], ["What was this but recrimination?", "그게 다름 아닌 맞고발이었어. 그가 질투하는 척을 하더구나."], ["He affected to be jealous:--he may, for aught I know, be inclined enough to jealousy in his natural temper; nay, he must have had it from nature, or the devil must have put it into his head; for I defy all the world to cast a just aspersion on my character: nay, the most scandalous tongues have never dared censure my reputation.", "어쩌면 그는 타고난 성품 자체가 질투에 기우는 쪽일지도 몰라. 아니, 그는 그걸 천성으로 타고났거나, 아니면 악마가 그의 머릿속에 집어넣었거나 했을 거야."], ["My fame, I thank heaven, hath been always as spotless as my life; and let falsehood itself accuse that if it dare.", "내 품행에 정당한 오점을 던질 수 있는 자가 있다면 온 세상을 향해 어디 한번 해 보라지. 아니, 아무리 험한 혀라도 감히 내 평판을 헐뜯은 적이 없었어."], ["No, my dear Graveairs, however provoked, however ill-treated, however injured in my love, I have firmly resolved never to give the least room for censure on this account.--And yet, my dear, there are some people so malicious, some tongues so venomous, that no innocence can escape them.", "하늘에 감사하게도, 내 명성은 늘 내 삶만큼이나 티 한 점 없었고, 거짓이 제 입으로 그걸 고발할 배짱이 있거든 어디 해 보라지. 아니야, 사랑하는 점잔 양, 아무리 화가 치밀고, 아무리 학대를 당하고, 아무리 사랑에 상처를 입었어도, 나는 이 일에 관해서만은 비난의 빌미를 조금도 주지 않겠다고 굳게 마음먹었단다."], ["The most undesigned word, the most accidental look, the least familiarity, the most innocent freedom, will be misconstrued, and magnified into I know not what, by some people.", "그렇지만, 얘야, 세상엔 어찌나 악의에 찬 사람들, 어찌나 독을 품은 혀들이 있는지, 어떤 결백도 그들을 피할 수 없는 법이야. 가장 무심코 던진 말, 가장 우연한 눈길, 아주 조그만 허물없음, 더없이 순진한 스스럼없음조차도, 어떤 사람들에 의해 곡해되고 부풀려져 무엇이 되는지 알 수도 없게 된단다."], ["But I despise, my dear Graveairs, I despise all such slander.", "하지만 나는 업신여겨, 사랑하는 점잔 양, 그런 비방 따위는 죄다 업신여긴다고."], ["No such malice, I assure you, ever gave me an uneasy moment.", "분명히 말하지만, 그런 악의가 나를 단 한순간이라도 불편하게 한 적은 없어. 아냐, 아냐, 단언컨대 나는 그런 것쯤은 다 초월한 사람이거든."], ["No, no, I promise you I am above all that.--But where was I?", "그런데 내가 어디까지 말했더라? 아, 그래, 남편이 질투하더라고 말했지."], ["O let me see, I told you my husband was jealous--And of whom, I pray?--Why, of whom but the lieutenant I mentioned to you before!", "그래서, 대체 누구를 두고? 글쎄, 앞서 네게 말한 그 중위 말고 누구겠니!"], ["He was obliged to resort above a year and more back to find any object for this unaccountable passion, if, indeed, he really felt any such, and was not an arrant counterfeit in order to abuse me.", "그 영문 모를 정념의 대상이라는 걸 찾으려고 그는 일 년도 더 거슬러 올라가야 했단다. 정말로 그런 감정을 느끼기나 했다면, 그리고 나를 학대하려고 꾸며 댄 새빨간 거짓이 아니었다면 말이야."]], [["“But I have tired you already with too many particulars.", "\"하지만 내가 벌써 너무 자질구레한 이야기로 너를 지치게 했구나."], ["I will now bring my story to a very speedy conclusion.", "이제 내 이야기를 아주 빨리 매듭지을게."], ["In short, then, after many scenes very unworthy to be repeated, in which my cousin engaged so heartily on my side, that Mr Fitzpatrick at last turned her out of doors; when he found I was neither to be soothed nor bullied into compliance, he took a very violent method indeed.", "요컨대, 다시 옮길 가치도 없는 여러 장면이 지난 뒤에—그 와중에 내 사촌이 어찌나 진심으로 내 편을 들었던지, 피츠패트릭 씨가 끝내 그녀를 문밖으로 내쫓아 버렸지—내가 달래도 윽박질러도 응하지 않으리란 걸 알아챈 그는, 정말이지 아주 난폭한 방법을 택했어."], ["Perhaps you will conclude he beat me; but this, though he hath approached very near to it, he never actually did.", "너는 아마 그가 나를 때렸으리라 결론지을 테지. 하지만 그 짓은, 거의 그 직전까지 가긴 했어도, 실제로 한 적은 결코 없어."], ["He confined me to my room, without suffering me to have either pen, ink, paper, or book: and a servant every day made my bed, and brought me my food.", "그는 나를 방에 가두고, 펜도 잉크도 종이도 책도 일절 갖지 못하게 했지. 그리고 하인이 날마다 내 침대를 정돈하고 음식을 가져다주었단다."]], [["“When I had remained a week under this imprisonment, he made me a visit, and, with the voice of a schoolmaster, or, what is often much the same, of a tyrant, asked me, `If I would yet comply?'", "\"이렇게 갇혀 일주일을 보냈을 때, 그가 나를 찾아왔어. 그러고는 학교 선생 같은—아니, 흔히 그것과 별반 다르지 않은, 폭군 같은 목소리로 묻더구나."], ["I answered, very stoutly, `That I would die first.'", "'이제는 따를 텐가?' 나는 아주 당차게 대답했지."], ["`Then so you shall, and be d--nd!'", "'차라리 죽고 말겠어요.' 그러자 그가 소리치더라."], ["cries he; `for you shall never go alive out of this room.'", "'그렇다면 죽어, 빌어먹을! 너는 살아서는 이 방을 절대 못 나갈 테니까.'"]], [["“Here I remained a fortnight longer; and, to say the truth, my constancy was almost subdued, and I began to think of submission; when, one day, in the absence of my husband, who was gone abroad for some short time, by the greatest good fortune in the world, an accident happened.--I--at a time when I began to give way to the utmost despair----everything would be excusable at such a time--at that very time I received----But it would take up an hour to tell you all particulars.--In one word, then (for I will not tire you with circumstances), gold, the common key to all padlocks, opened my door, and set me at liberty.", "\"나는 여기서 다시 두 주를 더 머물렀어. 그리고 솔직히 말하면, 내 꿋꿋함도 거의 꺾여서, 굴복을 생각하기 시작하던 참이었지. 그런데 어느 날, 잠시 외출하느라 남편이 집을 비운 사이, 세상에서 가장 큰 행운으로 한 가지 일이 일어났단다. 나는—내가 더없는 절망에 빠져들기 시작하던 그때에—그런 때라면 무슨 일이든 용서받을 만하지—바로 그때에 받았어—하지만 그 자초지종을 다 이야기하려면 한 시간은 걸릴 거야. 그러니 한마디로 말하면(자질구레한 사정으로 너를 지치게 하지 않으마), 모든 자물쇠의 공통된 열쇠인 황금이 내 방문을 열어 주었고, 나를 자유롭게 풀어 주었단다."]], [["“I now made haste to Dublin, where I immediately procured a passage to England; and was proceeding to Bath, in order to throw myself into the protection of my aunt, or of your father, or of any relation who would afford it me.", "\"나는 곧장 더블린으로 서둘러 갔고, 거기서 즉시 잉글랜드로 가는 뱃길을 마련했어. 그러고는 숙모님이나 네 아버지, 아니면 보호해 줄 만한 어떤 친척에게든 몸을 의탁하려고 바스로 향하던 중이었지."], ["My husband overtook me last night at the inn where I lay, and which you left a few minutes before me; but I had the good luck to escape him, and to follow you.", "어젯밤 내가 묵던 여관에서 남편이 나를 따라잡았는데, 그 여관은 네가 나보다 몇 분 앞서 떠난 바로 그곳이었어. 하지만 나는 다행히 그를 따돌리고 너를 뒤따라올 수 있었단다."]], [["“And thus, my dear, ends my history: a tragical one, I am sure, it is to myself; but, perhaps, I ought rather to apologize to you for its dullness.”", "\"그리고, 얘야, 이렇게 내 이야기는 끝이 나는구나. 나 자신에게는 분명 비극 같은 이야기지만, 어쩌면 그 따분함에 대해 오히려 네게 사과를 해야 할지도 모르겠다.\""]], [["Sophia heaved a deep sigh, and answered, “Indeed, Harriet, I pity you from my soul!----But what could you expect?", "소피아는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 대답했다. \"정말이지, 해리엇, 저는 영혼 깊은 곳에서부터 언니가 가여워요!"], ["Why, why, would you marry an Irishman?”", "그렇지만 무얼 기대하셨겠어요? 어쩌자고, 어쩌자고 아일랜드 남자와 결혼하셨어요?\""]], [["“Upon my word,” replied her cousin, “your censure is unjust. There are, among the Irish, men of as much worth and honour as any among the English: nay, to speak the truth, generosity of spirit is rather more common among them. I have known some examples there, too, of good husbands; and I believe these are not very plenty in England. Ask me, rather, what I could expect when I married a fool; and I will tell you a solemn truth; I did not know him to be so.”--“Can no man,” said Sophia, in a very low and altered voice, “do you think, make a bad husband, who is not a fool?” “That,” answered the other, “is too general a negative; but none, I believe, is so likely as a fool to prove so. Among my acquaintance, the silliest fellows are the worst husbands; and I will venture to assert, as a fact, that a man of sense rarely behaves very ill to a wife who deserves very well.”", "\"정말이지,\" 사촌이 받았다. \"네 비난은 부당하구나. 아일랜드 사람들 중에도 잉글랜드 사람 못지않게 가치 있고 명예로운 남자들이 있어. 아니, 솔직히 말하면, 너그러운 기상은 오히려 그들에게서 더 흔하지. 거기서도 좋은 남편의 본보기를 몇몇 보았는데, 그런 사람은 잉글랜드에선 그리 흔치 않을 것 같구나. 차라리 내게 물으렴, 어리석은 자와 결혼해 놓고 무얼 기대했느냐고. 그럼 엄숙한 진실을 말해 주마. 나는 그가 그런 자인 줄 몰랐단다.\" \"그럼 언니 생각엔,\" 소피아가 무척 낮고 달라진 목소리로 말했다. \"어리석지 않은 남자라면 나쁜 남편이 될 수 없다는 건가요?\" \"그건,\" 상대가 답했다. \"너무 싸잡은 부정이지. 하지만 어리석은 자만큼 나쁜 남편이 되기 쉬운 사람은 없을 거라고 나는 믿어. 내가 아는 사람들 가운데서도 가장 멍청한 작자들이 가장 못된 남편들이거든. 그리고 나는 하나의 사실로서 감히 단언하겠어. 분별 있는 남자라면, 충분히 잘해 줄 자격이 있는 아내에게 아주 못되게 구는 일이 좀처럼 없다고 말이야.\""]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["A dreadful alarm in the inn, with the arrival of an unexpected friend of Mrs Fitzpatrick.", "소피아는 이제 사촌의 청에 따라 자신의 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["Sophia now, at the desire of her cousin, related--not what follows, but what hath gone before in this history: for which reason the reader will, I suppose, excuse me for not repeating it over again.", "다만 그것은 앞으로 일어날 일이 아니라, 이 이야기에서 이미 지나간 일이었다. 그러니 독자께서는 내가 그것을 다시 되풀이하지 않더라도 너그러이 양해해 주시리라 믿는다."]], [["One remark, however, I cannot forbear making on her narrative, namely, that she made no more mention of Jones, from the beginning to the end, than if there had been no such person alive.", "그러나 그녀의 이야기에 대해 한 가지만큼은 짚지 않고 넘어갈 수가 없다. 그것은 곧, 그녀가 처음부터 끝까지 존스에 대해서는, 마치 이 세상에 그런 사람이 살아 있지도 않은 듯, 단 한 마디도 입에 올리지 않았다는 사실이다."], ["This I will neither endeavour to account for nor to excuse.", "이 점은 내가 설명하려 들지도, 변명하려 들지도 않겠다."], ["Indeed, if this may be called a kind of dishonesty, it seems the more inexcusable, from the apparent openness and explicit sincerity of the other lady.--But so it was.", "사실 이것을 일종의 부정직함이라 부를 수 있다면, 상대편 부인이 보여 준 그 분명한 솔직함과 거리낌 없는 진실함에 견주어 볼 때 더더욱 용서받기 어려워 보인다. 그러나 어쨌든 사정은 그러했다."]], [["Just as Sophia arrived at the conclusion of her story, there arrived in the room where the two ladies were sitting a noise, not unlike, in loudness, to that of a pack of hounds just let out from their kennel; nor, in shrillness, to cats, when caterwauling; or to screech owls; or, indeed, more like (for what animal can resemble a human voice?) to those sounds which, in the pleasant mansions of that gate which seems to derive its name from a duplicity of tongues, issue from the mouths, and sometimes from the nostrils, of those fair river nymphs, ycleped of old the Naïades; in the vulgar tongue translated oyster-wenches; for when, instead of the antient libations of milk and honey and oil, the rich distillation from the juniper-berry, or, perhaps, from malt, hath, by the early devotion of their votaries, been poured forth in great abundance, should any daring tongue with unhallowed license prophane, i.e., depreciate, the delicate fat Milton oyster, the plaice sound and firm, the flounder as much alive as when in the water, the shrimp as big as a prawn, the fine cod alive but a few hours ago, or any other of the various treasures which those water-deities who fish the sea and rivers have committed to the care of the nymphs, the angry Naïades lift up their immortal voices, and the prophane wretch is struck deaf for his impiety.", "소피아가 이야기를 막 끝맺을 무렵, 두 부인이 앉아 있던 방으로 어떤 소리가 들이닥쳤다. 그 소리는 우렁차기로 치자면 막 개집에서 풀려난 사냥개 떼의 짖음과 다르지 않았고, 째지기로 치자면 발정 난 고양이들의 울음이나 부엉이의 비명과도 같았다. 아니, 차라리 (어떤 짐승인들 사람의 목소리를 닮을 수 있겠는가?) 두 갈래 혀에서 그 이름을 따온 듯한 그 관문의 유쾌한 저택에서, 옛적에 나이아데스라 불렸고 속된 말로는 굴 파는 아낙으로 옮겨지는 그 어여쁜 강의 요정들이 입으로, 때로는 콧구멍으로 내뿜는 소리에 더 가까웠다. 옛 제례에서 바치던 우유와 꿀과 기름 대신, 노간주 열매에서 뽑아낸 진한 술, 혹은 보리에서 빚어낸 술이 그 숭배자들의 이른 아침 헌신으로 흥건히 부어진 마당에, 만일 어떤 겁 없는 혀가 불경한 방종으로 신성을 모독한다면, 즉 그 보드랍고 살진 밀턴 굴이며, 단단하고 야무진 가자미며, 물속에 있을 때만큼이나 펄떡이는 넙치며, 보통 새우만 한 작은 새우며, 불과 몇 시간 전까지 살아 있던 싱싱한 대구며, 또 바다와 강을 누비는 그 물의 신들이 요정들의 손에 맡겨 둔 온갖 보물들을 헐뜯기라도 한다면, 노한 나이아데스는 불멸의 목청을 드높이고, 그 불경한 가엾은 자는 제 불경의 죄로 귀가 먹먹해지고 마는 것이다."]], [["Such was the noise which now burst from one of the rooms below; and soon the thunder, which long had rattled at a distance, began to approach nearer and nearer, till, having ascended by degrees upstairs, it at last entered the apartment where the ladies were.", "지금 아래층 방 하나에서 터져 나온 소리가 바로 그러했다. 그리고 곧, 멀리서 한참 동안 우르릉대던 천둥이 점점 가까이 다가오기 시작하더니, 계단을 차츰차츰 올라와 마침내 두 부인이 있던 방으로 들어섰다."], ["In short, to drop all metaphor and figure, Mrs Honour, having scolded violently below-stairs, and continued the same all the way up, came in to her mistress in a most outrageous passion, crying out, “What doth your ladyship think?", "요컨대, 온갖 비유와 수사를 다 걷어 내고 말하자면, 아너 부인이 아래층에서 사납게 악다구니를 퍼붓고는 올라오는 내내 그 기세를 이어 가다가, 가장 노발대발한 모습으로 제 아씨에게 들이닥쳐 이렇게 외쳐 댄 것이다. \"아씨, 이걸 어찌 생각하셔요?"], ["Would you imagine that this impudent villain, the master of this house, hath had the impudence to tell me, nay, to stand it out to my face, that your ladyship is that nasty, stinking wh--re (Jenny Cameron they call her), that runs about the country with the Pretender?", "글쎄, 이 뻔뻔한 악당, 이 집 주인 놈이 저더러 뭐라고 했는지 짐작이나 가셔요? 제 면전에 대고 끝까지 우기더라니까요, 아씨가 그 더럽고 구린내 나는 갈, 갈보(사람들이 제니 캐머런이라 부른다죠)라고요, 그 왕위 참칭자랑 온 나라를 싸돌아다닌다는 그 계집 말이에요!"], ["Nay, the lying, saucy villain had the assurance to tell me that your ladyship had owned yourself to be so; but I have clawed the rascal; I have left the marks of my nails in his impudent face.", "아니, 그 거짓말쟁이에 건방진 악당 놈이 글쎄, 아씨가 스스로 그렇다고 인정했다고까지 뻔뻔하게 지껄이지 뭐예요. 하지만 제가 그 악당 놈을 할퀴어 줬어요."], ["My lady! says I, you saucy scoundrel; my lady is meat for no pretenders.", "그 뻔뻔한 낯짝에 제 손톱 자국을 남겨 놓고 왔다고요."], ["She is a young lady of as good fashion, and family, and fortune, as any in Somersetshire.", "제가 그랬죠, 우리 아씨라고! 이 건방진 망나니야, 우리 아씨는 어떤 참칭자 따위가 넘볼 분이 아니야."], ["Did you never hear of the great Squire Western, sirrah?", "우리 아씨로 말할 것 같으면 서머싯셔의 그 누구 못지않게 가문 좋고 집안 좋고 재산 좋은 젊은 아가씨란 말이야."], ["She is his only daughter; she is----, and heiress to all his great estate.", "이놈아, 그 대단하신 웨스턴 지주님 얘기를 한 번도 못 들어 봤어?"], ["My lady to be called a nasty Scotch wh--re by such a varlet!--To be sure I wish I had knocked his brains out with the punch-bowl.”", "우리 아씨가 바로 그분의 외동따님이시고, 그 어마어마한 재산을 다 물려받으실 상속녀시란 말이야! 우리 아씨가 그런 천한 종놈한테 더러운 스코틀랜드 갈보 소리를 듣다니!—정말이지 펀치 사발로 그놈 골통을 깨 버리지 못한 게 한이에요.\""]], [["The principal uneasiness with which Sophia was affected on this occasion Honour had herself caused, by having in her passion discovered who she was.", "이번 일로 소피아가 느낀 가장 큰 불안은, 다름 아닌 아너 자신이 흥분한 나머지 그녀의 정체를 까발려 버린 데서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["However, as this mistake of the landlord sufficiently accounted for those passages which Sophia had before mistaken, she acquired some ease on that account; nor could she, upon the whole, forbear smiling.", "그러나 여관 주인의 이 착각 덕분에, 소피아가 앞서 잘못 짐작했던 여러 대목이 이제 충분히 설명되었으므로, 그녀는 그 점에서는 어느 정도 마음이 놓였고, 전체적으로 보아 웃음을 참을 수도 없었다."], ["This enraged Honour, and she cried, “Indeed, madam, I did not think your ladyship would have made a laughing matter of it.", "이 모습에 아너는 더욱 발끈하여 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 아씨, 저는 아씨가 이 일을 웃어넘기실 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요."], ["To be called whore by such an impudent low rascal.", "그런 뻔뻔하고 천한 악당 놈한테 갈보 소리를 듣다니요."], ["Your ladyship may be angry with me, for aught I know, for taking your part, since proffered service, they say, stinks; but to be sure I could never bear to hear a lady of mine called whore.--Nor will I bear it.", "아씨야 어쩌면 제가 아씨 편을 든 게 못마땅하실지도 모르죠, 자청한 봉사는 구린내가 난다는 말도 있으니까요. 하지만 정말이지 저는 우리 아씨가 갈보라고 불리는 걸 도저히 못 들어 주겠어요.—참지도 않을 거고요."], ["I am sure your ladyship is as virtuous a lady as ever sat foot on English ground, and I will claw any villain's eyes out who dares for to offer to presume for to say the least word to the contrary.", "우리 아씨는 영국 땅을 밟은 그 어떤 분 못지않게 정숙한 분이시고, 감히 그와 반대되는 말을 한 마디라도 입에 올리려 드는 악당이 있다면 누구든 그 눈깔을 후벼 파 버리겠어요."], ["Nobody ever could say the least ill of the character of any lady that ever I waited upon.”", "제가 모셨던 그 어떤 아씨에 대해서도 누구 하나 흠 잡을 말을 한 적이 없었다니까요.\""]], [["Hinc illae lachrymae; in plain truth, Honour had as much love for her mistress as most servants have, that is to say--But besides this, her pride obliged her to support the character of the lady she waited on; for she thought her own was in a very close manner connected with it. In proportion as the character of her mistress was raised, hers likewise, as she conceived, was raised with it; and, on the contrary, she thought the one could not be lowered without the other.", "여기서 그 눈물이 비롯된 것이다. 솔직히 말하자면, 아너는 여느 하인들이 그러하듯 제 아씨를 사랑했다. 다시 말해—. 그러나 이뿐만 아니라, 그녀의 자존심이 자기가 모시는 아씨의 명예를 떠받들지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 자기 명예가 아씨의 명예와 아주 긴밀하게 얽혀 있다고 여겼기 때문이다. 아씨의 명예가 높아지는 만큼 제 명예도 그에 따라 높아진다고 생각했고, 반대로 한쪽이 낮아지면 다른 쪽도 낮아질 수밖에 없다고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["On this subject, reader, I must stop a moment, to tell thee a story.", "이 대목에서, 독자여, 나는 잠시 멈춰 그대에게 한 가지 이야기를 들려주어야겠다. \"그 유명한 넬 귄이 어느 날 잠시 들렀던 집에서 나와 제 마차에 오르려다가, 큰 무리가 몰려 있고 제 마부가 온통 피투성이에 흙투성이가 된 것을 보았다."], ["“The famous Nell Gwynn, stepping one day, from a house where she had made a short visit, into her coach, saw a great mob assembled, and her footman all bloody and dirty; the fellow, being asked by his mistress the reason of his being in that condition, answered, `I have been fighting, madam, with an impudent rascal who called your ladyship a wh--re.'", "아씨가 어쩌다 그 꼴이 되었느냐고 묻자, 사내는 이렇게 답했다. '아씨, 어떤 뻔뻔한 악당 놈이 아씨를 갈보라고 불러서 제가 그놈하고 싸웠습니다요.'"], ["`You blockhead,'", "'이 멍청아,' 귄 부인이 받아쳤다."], ["replied Mrs Gwynn, `at this rate you must fight every day of your life; why, you fool, all the world knows it.'", "'그런 식이면 너는 평생 날마다 싸워야 할 게다. 이 바보야, 세상이 다 아는 일을.'"], ["`Do they?'", "'그래요?'"], ["cries the fellow, in a muttering voice, after he had shut the coach-door, `they shan't call me a whore's footman for all that.'”", "사내가 마차 문을 닫고 나서 중얼대는 목소리로 외쳤다. '그렇다 해도 그놈들이 날더러 갈보의 마부라고 부르게 둘 순 없죠.'\""]], [["Thus the passion of Mrs Honour appears natural enough, even if it were to be no otherwise accounted for; but, in reality, there was another cause of her anger; for which we must beg leave to remind our reader of a circumstance mentioned in the above simile.", "이렇게 보면, 달리 설명할 길이 없다 하더라도 아너 부인의 격분은 충분히 자연스러워 보인다. 하지만 사실 그녀의 분노에는 또 다른 원인이 있었으니, 이를 위해 우리는 위의 비유에서 언급한 한 가지 정황을 독자께 다시 일깨워 드리고자 한다."], ["There are indeed certain liquors, which, being applied to our passions, or to fire, produce effects the very reverse of those produced by water, as they serve to kindle and inflame, rather than to extinguish.", "실로 어떤 종류의 술은 우리의 격정에, 혹은 불에 닿으면 물이 일으키는 것과는 정반대의 효과를 낸다. 끄기보다는 오히려 불붙이고 타오르게 하는 것이다."], ["Among these, the generous liquor called punch is one.", "그중 하나가 바로 펀치라는 그 후한 술이다."], ["It was not, therefore, without reason, that the learned Dr Cheney used to call drinking punch pouring liquid fire down your throat.", "그러니 학식 높은 체인 박사가 펀치를 마시는 것을 두고 액체로 된 불을 목구멍에 들이붓는 일이라 부르곤 했던 것도 까닭 없는 말은 아니었다."]], [["Now, Mrs Honour had unluckily poured so much of this liquid fire down her throat, that the smoke of it began to ascend into her pericranium and blinded the eyes of Reason, which is there supposed to keep her residence, while the fire itself from the stomach easily reached the heart, and there inflamed the noble passion of pride.", "그런데 아너 부인은 불운하게도 이 액체로 된 불을 어찌나 많이 목구멍에 들이부었던지, 그 연기가 머리뼈 속으로 피어오르기 시작하여, 그곳에 거처를 둔다고 여겨지는 이성의 눈을 멀게 했고, 한편 위장에서 솟은 불 그 자체는 어렵잖게 심장에 이르러 거기서 자존심이라는 고결한 격정을 활활 타오르게 했다."], ["So that, upon the whole, we shall cease to wonder at the violent rage of the waiting-woman; though at first sight we must confess the cause seems inadequate to the effect.", "그러니 전체적으로 보아, 우리는 그 시중드는 여인의 격렬한 분노에 더는 놀라지 않게 될 것이다. 비록 언뜻 보기에는 그 원인이 결과에 견주어 너무 사소해 보인다고 인정할 수밖에 없지만 말이다."]], [["Sophia and her cousin both did all in their power to extinguish these flames which had roared so loudly all over the house.", "소피아와 그녀의 사촌은 온 집 안을 그토록 요란하게 울려 댄 이 불길을 끄려고 둘 다 안간힘을 다했다."], ["They at length prevailed; or, to carry the metaphor one step farther, the fire, having consumed all the fuel which the language affords, to wit, every reproachful term in it, at last went out of its own accord.", "마침내 그들은 뜻을 이루었다. 아니, 비유를 한 걸음 더 밀고 나가자면, 그 불은 언어가 제공할 수 있는 모든 땔감, 곧 거기 담긴 온갖 욕설을 다 태워 버린 끝에, 마침내 제풀에 사그라들고 말았다."]], [["But, though tranquillity was restored above-stairs, it was not so below; where my landlady, highly resenting the injury done to the beauty of her husband by the flesh-spades of Mrs Honour, called aloud for revenge and justice. As to the poor man, who had principally suffered in the engagement, he was perfectly quiet. Perhaps the blood which he lost might have cooled his anger: for the enemy had not only applied her nails to his cheeks, but likewise her fist to his nostrils, which lamented the blow with tears of blood in great abundance. To this we may add reflections on his mistake; but indeed nothing so effectually silenced his resentment as the manner in which he now discovered his error; for as to the behaviour of Mrs Honour, it had the more confirmed him in his opinion; but he was now assured by a person of great figure, and who was attended by a great equipage, that one of the ladies was a woman of fashion, and his intimate acquaintance.", "그러나 위층에는 평온이 돌아왔어도, 아래층은 그렇지 못했다. 그곳에서는 우리의 여관 안주인이 제 남편의 잘생긴 얼굴을 망쳐 놓은 아너 부인의 그 손톱 호미질에 잔뜩 분개하여, 복수와 정의를 큰 소리로 부르짖고 있었다. 정작 이 난투에서 가장 크게 당한 그 가엾은 사내는 더없이 잠잠했다. 어쩌면 그가 흘린 피가 그의 분노를 식혀 주었는지도 모른다. 적수가 그 손톱을 그의 뺨에 박았을 뿐 아니라 주먹을 그의 콧구멍에도 먹였고, 콧구멍은 흥건한 핏물의 눈물로 그 일격을 애도했기 때문이다. 여기에 더해 제 착각에 대한 반성도 한몫했을 것이다. 그러나 사실 그의 분노를 가장 확실히 잠재운 것은, 그가 이제 제 잘못을 깨닫게 된 그 방식이었다. 아너 부인의 행태로 말하자면 도리어 그의 생각을 굳혀 놓기만 했으니 말이다. 그런데 이제 그는 대단한 풍채에다 화려한 마차 행렬을 거느린 한 인물로부터, 그 두 부인 가운데 한 분이 지체 높은 여인이며 자신의 절친한 지인이라는 사실을 확언받게 된 것이다."]], [["By the orders of this person, the landlord now ascended, and acquainted our fair travellers that a great gentleman below desired to do them the honour of waiting on them.", "이 인물의 명에 따라, 여관 주인이 이제 위층으로 올라와 우리의 어여쁜 나그네들에게 아래층의 한 대단한 신사분께서 두 분을 찾아뵙는 영광을 청하신다고 알렸다."], ["Sophia turned pale and trembled at this message, though the reader will conclude it was too civil, notwithstanding the landlord's blunder, to have come from her father; but fear hath the common fault of a justice of peace, and is apt to conclude hastily from every slight circumstance, without examining the evidence on both sides.", "소피아는 이 전갈에 얼굴이 새파래지며 부들부들 떨었다. 비록 독자께서는 여관 주인의 실수가 있긴 했어도 이 전갈이 그녀의 아버지에게서 온 것이라기엔 너무 정중하다고 단정하시겠지만, 두려움이란 치안판사가 흔히 저지르는 잘못을 그대로 닮아서, 양편의 증거를 가려 보지도 않고 아주 사소한 정황 하나만으로도 성급히 결론을 내려 버리기 일쑤인 것이다."]], [["To ease the reader's curiosity, therefore, rather than his apprehensions, we proceed to inform him that an Irish peer had arrived very late that evening at the inn, in his way to London.", "그러니 독자의 불안보다는 호기심을 풀어 드리고자, 우리는 그날 저녁 아주 늦게 한 아일랜드 귀족이 런던으로 가던 길에 그 여관에 당도했음을 알리고자 한다."], ["This nobleman, having sallied from his supper at the hurricane before commemorated, had seen the attendant of Mrs Fitzpatrick, and upon a short enquiry, was informed that her lady, with whom he was very particularly acquainted, was above.", "이 귀족은 앞서 이야기한 그 회오리바람 같은 소동이 일었을 때 저녁 식사 자리에서 뛰쳐나왔다가 피츠패트릭 부인의 하인을 보았고, 잠깐 물어본 끝에 자신과 아주 각별히 아는 사이인 그 부인이 위층에 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["This information he had no sooner received than he addressed himself to the landlord, pacified him, and sent him upstairs with compliments rather civiller than those which were delivered.", "이 소식을 듣자마자 그는 여관 주인에게 다가가 그를 달랜 뒤, 앞서 전해진 것보다는 한결 정중한 인사를 들려 그를 위층으로 올려 보냈다."]], [["It may perhaps be wondered at that the waiting-woman herself was not the messenger employed on this occasion; but we are sorry to say she was not at present qualified for that, or indeed for any other office.", "이 경우에 어째서 시중드는 여인 본인이 그 전갈을 맡지 않았는지 의아해할 분도 계실 것이다. 그러나 유감스럽게도, 그녀는 지금 그 일은 물론이거니와 다른 어떤 일도 감당할 처지가 못 되었음을 말씀드려야겠다."], ["The rum (for so the landlord chose to call the distillation from malt) had basely taken the advantage of the fatigue which the poor woman had undergone, and had made terrible depredations on her noble faculties, at a time when they were very unable to resist the attack.", "럼주(여관 주인은 보리에서 빚은 그 술을 굳이 그렇게 불렀다)가 비열하게도 그 가엾은 여인이 겪은 피로를 틈타, 그녀의 고결한 정신 기능이 그 공격에 도무지 맞설 수 없는 바로 그때, 거기에 끔찍한 노략질을 자행해 놓은 것이다."]], [["We shall not describe this tragical scene too fully; but we thought ourselves obliged, by that historic integrity which we profess, shortly to hint a matter which we would otherwise have been glad to have spared.", "우리는 이 비극적인 장면을 너무 낱낱이 그려 내지는 않겠다. 다만 우리가 표방하는 그 역사가로서의 진실성에 따라, 본디 같으면 기꺼이 덮어 두고 싶었을 이 사안을 간략하게나마 귀띔해 두지 않을 수 없다고 여겼다."], ["Many historians, indeed, for want of this integrity, or of diligence, to say no worse, often leave the reader to find out these little circumstances in the dark, and sometimes to his great confusion and perplexity.", "실로 많은 역사가들이 이 진실성, 혹은 좋게 말해 부지런함이 모자란 탓에, 독자로 하여금 이런 자잘한 정황들을 어둠 속에서 더듬어 찾게 만들고, 때로는 큰 혼란과 당혹 속에 빠뜨리곤 한다."]], [["Sophia was very soon eased of her causeless fright by the entry of the noble peer, who was not only an intimate acquaintance of Mrs Fitzpatrick, but in reality a very particular friend of that lady.", "소피아는 그 고귀한 귀족이 들어서자 까닭 없던 두려움에서 이내 벗어났다. 그는 피츠패트릭 부인의 절친한 지인일 뿐 아니라, 실은 그 부인의 아주 각별한 벗이었던 것이다."], ["To say truth, it was by his assistance that she had been enabled to escape from her husband; for this nobleman had the same gallant disposition with those renowned knights of whom we read in heroic story, and had delivered many an imprisoned nymph from durance.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그녀가 남편에게서 달아날 수 있었던 것도 그의 도움 덕분이었다. 이 귀족은 우리가 영웅 이야기에서 읽는 그 이름난 기사들과 똑같은 의협의 기질을 지녀, 갇혀 있던 수많은 요정을 결박에서 풀어 주었던 것이다."], ["He was indeed as bitter an enemy to the savage authority too often exercised by husbands and fathers, over the young and lovely of the other sex, as ever knight-errant was to the barbarous power of enchanters; nay, to say truth, I have often suspected that those very enchanters with which romance everywhere abounds were in reality no other than the husbands of those days; and matrimony itself was, perhaps, the enchanted castle in which the nymphs were said to be confined.", "실로 그는, 편력 기사가 마법사들의 야만적인 힘을 적대했던 것 못지않게, 남편과 아버지들이 이성異性의 젊고 사랑스러운 이들에게 너무도 자주 휘두르는 그 야만적 권위에 신랄한 적이었다. 아니, 사실대로 말하자면, 나는 로맨스 곳곳에 그득한 바로 그 마법사들이 실은 그 시절의 남편들에 다름 아니었으리라고, 그리고 결혼 그 자체가 어쩌면 요정들이 갇혀 있다고들 하던 그 마법의 성이었으리라고 종종 의심해 왔다."]], [["This nobleman had an estate in the neighbourhood of Fitzpatrick, and had been for some time acquainted with the lady.", "이 귀족은 피츠패트릭의 이웃에 영지를 두고 있어, 한동안 그 부인과 알고 지내던 사이였다. 그러니 그녀가 갇혀 있다는 소식을 듣자마자 그는 열심히 나서서 그녀의 자유를 도모했고, 이를 곧 이루어 냈다."], ["No sooner, therefore, did he hear of her confinement, than he earnestly applied himself to procure her liberty; which he presently effected, not by storming the castle, according to the example of antient heroes, but by corrupting the governor, in conformity with the modern art of war, in which craft is held to be preferable to valour, and gold is found to be more irresistible than either lead or steel.", "다만 옛 영웅들의 본보기를 따라 성을 들이쳐 빼앗은 것이 아니라, 근대의 전술에 걸맞게 그 성주를 매수함으로써였다. 근대의 전술에서는 용맹보다 책략이 낫다고 치고, 황금이 납이나 강철 어느 쪽보다도 더 거역할 수 없는 것으로 통하는 법이다."]], [["This circumstance, however, as the lady did not think it material enough to relate to her friend, we would not at that time impart it to the reader.", "그러나 이 정황은 그 부인이 제 벗에게 들려줄 만큼 중요하다고 여기지 않았던 까닭에, 우리도 그때는 독자께 밝히지 않았다."], ["We rather chose to leave him a while under a supposition that she had found, or coined, or by some very extraordinary, perhaps supernatural means, had possessed herself of the money with which she had bribed her keeper, than to interrupt her narrative by giving a hint of what seemed to her of too little importance to be mentioned.", "우리는 차라리 독자께서, 그녀가 자기를 가둔 자를 매수한 그 돈을 어디서 주웠거나, 직접 찍어 냈거나, 아니면 뭔가 아주 기이한, 어쩌면 초자연적인 수단으로 손에 넣었으리라 짐작하시도록 한동안 내버려 두는 편을 택했다. 그녀가 보기에는 언급할 가치도 없을 만큼 사소해 보였던 일을 귀띔하느라 그녀의 이야기를 끊기보다는 말이다."]], [["The peer, after a short conversation, could not forbear expressing some surprize at meeting the lady in that place; nor could he refrain from telling her he imagined she had been gone to Bath.", "그 귀족은 잠깐의 대화 끝에, 이런 곳에서 그 부인과 마주친 데 대한 다소의 놀라움을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다. 또한 그녀가 배스로 떠난 줄로만 알았노라고 말하지 않을 수도 없었다."], ["Mrs Fitzpatrick very freely answered, “That she had been prevented in her purpose by the arrival of a person she need not mention.", "피츠패트릭 부인은 아주 거리낌 없이 대답했다. \"굳이 입에 올릴 것도 없는 한 사람이 들이닥치는 바람에 제 뜻이 좌절되고 말았답니다."], ["In short,”", "요컨대,\""], ["says she, “I was overtaken by my husband (for I need not affect to conceal what the world knows too well already).", "그녀가 말했다. \"남편에게 따라잡히고 만 거지요(세상이 이미 너무 잘 아는 일을 굳이 감추는 척할 것도 없으니까요)."], ["I had the good fortune to escape in a most surprizing manner, and am now going to London with this young lady, who is a near relation of mine, and who hath escaped from as great a tyrant as my own.”", "다행히 저는 아주 놀라운 방식으로 빠져나왔고, 지금은 이 젊은 아가씨와 함께 런던으로 가는 길이랍니다. 이 아가씨는 저의 가까운 친척으로, 제 남편 못지않은 폭군에게서 달아난 처지지요.\""]], [["His lordship, concluding that this tyrant was likewise a husband, made a speech full of compliments to both the ladies, and as full of invectives against his own sex; nor indeed did he avoid some oblique glances at the matrimonial institution itself, and at the unjust powers given by it to man over the more sensible and more meritorious part of the species.", "그 귀족 나리는 이 폭군 또한 남편이려니 단정하고, 두 부인을 향해 온통 찬사로 가득한 연설을 늘어놓는 한편, 자기 성별을 향해서는 그만큼 온통 비난을 퍼부었다. 그뿐 아니라 결혼 제도 그 자체와, 그것이 남자에게 부여하여 더 분별 있고 더 가치 있는 쪽 인간들 위에 휘두르게 하는 그 부당한 권력을 향해서도 비스듬한 눈길을 던지기를 마다하지 않았다."], ["He ended his oration with an offer of his protection, and of his coach and six, which was instantly accepted by Mrs Fitzpatrick, and at last, upon her persuasions, by Sophia.", "그는 자신의 보호와 더불어 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 자신의 마차를 내어 주겠다는 제안으로 그 일장 연설을 맺었고, 이는 피츠패트릭 부인이 그 자리에서 받아들였으며, 마침내 그녀의 설득으로 소피아도 받아들이게 되었다."]], [["Matters being thus adjusted, his lordship took his leave, and the ladies retired to rest, where Mrs Fitzpatrick entertained her cousin with many high encomiums on the character of the noble peer, and enlarged very particularly on his great fondness for his wife; saying, she believed he was almost the only person of high rank who was entirely constant to the marriage bed.", "이렇게 일이 정해지자 그 귀족 나리는 물러갔고, 두 부인은 잠자리에 들었다. 그곳에서 피츠패트릭 부인은 그 고귀한 귀족의 인품에 대한 온갖 드높은 찬사로 사촌을 즐겁게 해 주었고, 특히 그가 제 아내를 끔찍이 아낀다는 점을 유난히 길게 늘어놓으며, 지체 높은 사람 가운데 결혼의 잠자리에 온전히 한결같은 이는 아마 그가 거의 유일할 것이라고 말했다."], ["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["added she, “my dear Sophy, that is a very rare virtue amongst men of condition.", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"내 사랑하는 소피야, 그건 지체 있는 남자들 사이에서는 참으로 드문 미덕이란다."], ["Never expect it when you marry; for, believe me, if you do, you will certainly be deceived.”", "결혼할 때 그런 걸 절대 기대하지 마라. 정말이지, 그랬다간 너는 틀림없이 속고 말 테니까.\""]], [["A gentle sigh stole from Sophia at these words, which perhaps contributed to form a dream of no very pleasant kind; but, as she never revealed this dream to any one, so the reader cannot expect to see it related here.", "이 말에 소피아의 입에서 가만한 한숨이 새어 나왔고, 어쩌면 이것이 그리 유쾌하지 못한 어떤 꿈을 빚어내는 데 한몫했는지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀가 이 꿈을 누구에게도 털어놓지 않았으니, 독자께서도 그것이 여기 적혀 있기를 기대하실 수는 없겠다."]], [["# Chapter ix.", "# Chapter ix."]], [["The morning introduced in some pretty writing.", "아침이 멋들어진 글솜씨로 소개된다."], ["A stagecoach.", "역마차."], ["The civility of chambermaids.", "하녀들의 친절."], ["The heroic temper of Sophia.", "소피아의 영웅적 기질."], ["Her generosity.", "그녀의 너그러움."], ["The return to it.", "그에 대한 보답."], ["The departure of the company, and their arrival at London; with some remarks for the use of travellers.", "일행의 출발과 런던 도착. 더불어 여행자들에게 도움이 될 몇 가지 소견."]], [["Those members of society who are born to furnish the blessings of life now began to light their candles, in order to pursue their daily labours for the use of those who are born to enjoy these blessings.", "삶의 축복을 마련하도록 태어난 사회 구성원들이, 이제 그 축복을 누리도록 태어난 이들을 위해 하루의 노동을 시작하려 촛불을 밝히기 시작했다."], ["The sturdy hind now attends the levee of his fellow-labourer the ox; the cunning artificer, the diligent mechanic, spring from their hard mattress; and now the bonny housemaid begins to repair the disordered drum-room, while the riotous authors of that disorder, in broken interrupted slumbers, tumble and toss, as if the hardness of down disquieted their repose.", "억센 일꾼은 이제 동료 일꾼인 황소의 기침起寢을 살피러 가고, 영리한 장인과 부지런한 직공은 딱딱한 매트리스에서 몸을 일으킨다. 그리고 어여쁜 하녀는 어질러진 응접실을 정돈하기 시작하는데, 그 난장판을 만든 시끌벅적한 장본인들은 토막토막 끊기는 선잠 속에서 마치 부드러운 깃털이 너무 단단해 잠을 방해하기라도 하듯 이리저리 뒤척이고 있다."]], [["In simple phrase, the clock had no sooner struck seven than the ladies were ready for their journey; and, at their desire, his lordship and his equipage were prepared to attend them.", "쉽게 말하자면, 시계가 일곱 시를 치자마자 부인들은 여행 채비를 마쳤다. 그리고 그들의 청에 따라 그분과 그분의 마차 행렬도 그들을 모실 준비를 갖추었다."]], [["And now a matter of some difficulty arose; and this was how his lordship himself should be conveyed; for though in stage-coaches, where passengers are properly considered as so much luggage, the ingenious coachman stows half a dozen with perfect ease into the place of four; for well he contrives that the fat hostess, or well-fed alderman, may take up no more room than the slim miss, or taper master; it being the nature of guts, when well squeezed, to give way, and to lie in a narrow compass; yet in these vehicles, which are called, for distinction's sake, gentlemen's coaches, though they are often larger than the others, this method of packing is never attempted.", "그런데 이제 다소 까다로운 문제가 하나 생겼으니, 다름 아니라 그분 자신을 어떻게 모실 것인가 하는 것이었다. 역마차에서야 승객을 그저 짐짝쯤으로 치기에, 재간 좋은 마부는 네 사람 자리에 여섯 사람쯤은 거뜬히 욱여넣는다. 뚱뚱한 여관 안주인이나 잘 먹어 살찐 시참사회원이 호리호리한 아가씨나 가냘픈 도련님보다 자리를 더 차지하지 않도록 용케 꾸려 넣는 것이다. 창자란 잘 눌러 두면 물러나서 좁은 데에도 들어앉는 성질이 있으니 말이다. 그러나 구분을 위해 신사용 마차라 부르는 이런 탈것에서는, 비록 다른 마차보다 흔히 더 큰데도, 이런 식으로 짐 싸듯 끼워 넣는 법이 결코 없다."]], [["His lordship would have put a short end to the difficulty, by very gallantly desiring to mount his horse; but Mrs Fitzpatrick would by no means consent to it.", "그분은 매우 정중하게 말을 타겠다고 청하여 이 곤란을 단숨에 해결하려 했으나, 피츠패트릭 부인이 결코 동의하지 않았다."], ["It was therefore concluded that the Abigails should, by turns, relieve each other on one of his lordship's horses, which was presently equipped with a side-saddle for that purpose.", "그리하여 시녀들이 번갈아 가며 그분의 말 가운데 한 마리를 타기로 결론이 났고, 그 말에는 곧 그 용도로 여성용 안장이 채워졌다."]], [["Everything being settled at the inn, the ladies discharged their former guides, and Sophia made a present to the landlord, partly to repair the bruise which he had received under herself, and partly on account of what he had suffered under the hands of her enraged waiting-woman. And now Sophia first discovered a loss which gave her some uneasiness; and this was of the hundred-pound bank-bill which her father had given her at their last meeting; and which, within a very inconsiderable trifle, was all the treasure she was at present worth. She searched everywhere, and shook and tumbled all her things to no purpose, the bill was not to be found: and she was at last fully persuaded that she had lost it from her pocket when she had the misfortune of tumbling from her horse in the dark lane, as before recorded: a fact that seemed the more probable, as she now recollected some discomposure in her pockets which had happened at that time, and the great difficulty with which she had drawn forth her handkerchief the very instant before her fall, in order to relieve the distress of Mrs Fitzpatrick.", "여관에서 모든 것이 정리되자, 부인들은 앞서 길을 안내하던 자들을 돌려보냈고, 소피아는 여관 주인에게 선물을 건넸다. 한편으로는 자기 밑에 깔려 그가 입은 타박상을 위로하기 위함이요, 다른 한편으로는 격분한 자기 시녀의 손에 그가 당한 봉변을 보상하기 위함이었다. 그런데 이때 소피아는 처음으로 한 가지 분실을 알아차리고 적잖이 불안해졌다. 다름 아니라 아버지가 마지막 만남에서 건네준 백 파운드짜리 은행권이었으니, 그것은 아주 하찮은 푼돈을 빼고는 지금 그녀가 지닌 재산의 전부였다. 그녀는 사방을 뒤지고 가진 물건을 죄다 흔들고 헤집어 보았으나 헛수고였다. 은행권은 도무지 보이지 않았다. 그리하여 그녀는 마침내, 앞서 적은 대로 어두운 골목길에서 말에서 떨어지는 불운을 겪었을 때 주머니에서 빠뜨린 것이라고 확신하게 되었다. 그렇게 여길 만한 까닭은, 그때 주머니가 흐트러졌던 것이며, 떨어지기 바로 직전 피츠패트릭 부인의 곤경을 덜어 주려고 손수건을 꺼내는 데 무척 애를 먹었던 일이 새삼 떠올랐기 때문이다."]], [["Misfortunes of this kind, whatever inconveniencies they may be attended with, are incapable of subduing a mind in which there is any strength, without the assistance of avarice.", "이런 종류의 불운은 그것이 어떤 불편을 동반하든, 탐욕의 거듦이 없는 한, 조금이라도 강단이 있는 마음을 꺾지는 못한다. 그러므로 소피아는, 하필 이런 때에 닥친 이 사고만큼 시기가 나쁜 일도 없었으련만, 곧바로 근심을 이겨 내고는 늘 그렇듯 평온하고 밝은 얼굴로 일행에게 돌아왔다."], ["Sophia, therefore, though nothing could be worse timed than this accident at such a season, immediately got the better of her concern, and, with her wonted serenity and cheerfulness of countenance, returned to her company.", "그분은 부인들을 마차로 안내했고, 어너 부인도 마찬가지로 안내했다. 어너 부인은 한참 동안 사양하고 \"어머, 마님\""], ["His lordship conducted the ladies into the vehicle, as he did likewise Mrs Honour, who, after many civilities, and more dear madams, at last yielded to the well-bred importunities of her sister Abigail, and submitted to be complimented with the first ride in the coach; in which indeed she would afterwards have been contented to have pursued her whole journey, had not her mistress, after several fruitless intimations, at length forced her to take her turn on horseback.", "소리를 거듭하다가, 마침내 동료 시녀의 예의 바른 권유에 못 이겨 마차에 먼저 타는 영광을 받아들였다. 사실 그녀로서는 그 후로도 내내 마차를 타고 가는 것으로 만족했을 터이나, 그녀의 여주인이 몇 번이나 헛되이 눈치를 준 끝에 결국 자기 차례에는 말을 타도록 떠밀고 말았다."]], [["The coach, now having received its company, began to move forwards, attended by many servants, and led by two captains, who had before rode with his lordship, and who would have been dismissed from the vehicle upon a much less worthy occasion than was this of accommodating two ladies.", "이제 일행을 다 태운 마차가 앞으로 나아가기 시작했다. 여러 하인들이 따랐고, 앞서 그분과 함께 말을 타고 온 두 대위가 길잡이를 섰는데, 이들은 두 부인을 모시는 이런 가치 있는 일이 아니라 훨씬 시시한 일을 위해서라도 기꺼이 마차에서 내릴 사람들이었다."], ["In this they acted only as gentlemen; but they were ready at any time to have performed the office of a footman, or indeed would have condescended lower, for the honour of his lordship's company, and for the convenience of his table.", "이 일에서 그들은 다만 신사답게 행동했을 뿐이나, 그분의 동행이라는 영예와 그분의 식탁이 주는 편익을 위해서라면 언제든 하인 노릇도 마다하지 않을 자들이었고, 실은 그보다 더 낮은 일도 기꺼이 떠맡았을 위인들이었다."]], [["My landlord was so pleased with the present he had received from Sophia, that he rather rejoiced in than regretted his bruise or his scratches.", "여관 주인은 소피아에게서 받은 선물에 어찌나 흡족했던지, 타박상이나 긁힌 상처를 후회하기는커녕 오히려 기뻐할 지경이었다. 독자는 어쩌면 이 선물의 액수가 얼마인지 궁금할지도 모르겠다."], ["The reader will perhaps be curious to know the quantum of this present; but we cannot satisfy his curiosity.", "그러나 우리는 그 호기심을 채워 줄 수 없다. 액수가 얼마였든, 그것은 주인의 몸이 입은 상처를 보상하기에 충분했다."], ["Whatever it was, it satisfied the landlord for his bodily hurt; but he lamented he had not known before how little the lady valued her money; “For to be sure,”", "다만 그는 그 부인이 돈을 그토록 대수롭지 않게 여긴다는 사실을 진작 몰랐던 것을 한탄했다. \"정말이지,\""], ["says he, “one might have charged every article double, and she would have made no cavil at the reckoning.”", "그가 말했다. \"물건값마다 곱절을 매겼더라도, 셈을 두고 한마디 트집도 잡지 않았을 텐데.\""]], [["His wife, however, was far from drawing this conclusion; whether she really felt any injury done to her husband more than he did himself, I will not say: certain it is, she was much less satisfied with the generosity of Sophia.", "그러나 그의 아내는 이런 결론과는 거리가 멀었다. 그녀가 정말로 자기 남편이 입은 손해를 남편 자신보다 더 절절히 느꼈는지 어떤지는 말하지 않겠다."], ["“Indeed,” cries she, “my dear, the lady knows better how to dispose of her money than you imagine.", "다만 분명한 것은, 그녀가 소피아의 너그러움에 훨씬 덜 흡족해했다는 사실이다."], ["She might very well think we should not put up such a business without some satisfaction, and the law would have cost her an infinite deal more than this poor little matter, which I wonder you would take.”", "\"정말이지,\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“You are always so bloodily wise,”", "\"여보, 그 부인은 당신 생각보다 돈 쓸 줄을 잘 아는 사람이에요."], ["quoth the husband: “it would have cost her more, would it?", "우리가 무슨 보상도 없이 이런 일을 그냥 넘기지 않으리라는 걸 빤히 알았겠죠. 게다가 법으로 갔으면 이 가엾은 푼돈보다 훨씬 더 큰돈이 들었을 텐데, 당신이 그걸 받았다는 게 난 신기하네요.\""], ["dost fancy I don't know that as well as thee?", "\"당신은 늘 그렇게 지긋지긋하게 약아빠졌지.\" 남편이 말했다."], ["but would any of that more, or so much, have come into our pockets?", "\"그쪽이 돈을 더 썼을 거라고? 내가 그걸 당신만큼 모를 줄 알아?"], ["Indeed, if son Tom the lawyer had been alive, I could have been glad to have put such a pretty business into his hands.", "한데 그 더 든다는 돈이, 아니 그만한 돈이라도, 우리 주머니로 들어왔겠냐고? 정말이지, 변호사였던 우리 톰이 살아 있었으면, 이런 짭짤한 건을 그 애 손에 넘겼으면 나도 기뻤겠지."], ["He would have got a good picking out of it; but I have no relation now who is a lawyer, and why should I go to law for the benefit of strangers?”", "그 애라면 거기서 한몫 잘 챙겼을 테니까. 하지만 지금 나한테는 변호사 친척이라곤 없는데, 생판 남들 좋으라고 내가 왜 법정에 나가겠어?\""], ["“Nay, to be sure,”", "\"그래요, 하긴,\""], ["answered she, “you must know best.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"당신이 제일 잘 알겠죠.\""], ["“I believe I do,”", "\"내 생각엔 그래.\""], ["replied he.", "그가 받았다."], ["“I fancy, when money is to be got, I can smell it out as well as another.", "\"돈이 생길 일이라면, 나도 남 못지않게 냄새는 잘 맡는다고."], ["Everybody, let me tell you, would not have talked people out of this.", "누구나 다 이렇게 사람한테서 말로 떼어 낼 수 있는 건 아니야. 그 점을 새겨 둬."], ["Mind that, I say; everybody would not have cajoled this out of her, mind that.”", "그래, 누구나 다 그 여자한테서 이걸 살살 구슬려 받아 낼 수 있는 건 아니라고, 그 점을 새겨 두란 말이야.\""], ["The wife then joined in the applause of her husband's sagacity; and thus ended the short dialogue between them on this occasion.", "그러자 아내도 남편의 영민함을 칭송하는 데 합세했다. 그렇게 해서 이 일을 두고 두 사람 사이에 오간 짧은 대화는 끝이 났다."]], [["We will therefore take our leave of these good people, and attend his lordship and his fair companions, who made such good expedition that they performed a journey of ninety miles in two days, and on the second evening arrived in London, without having encountered any one adventure on the road worthy the dignity of this history to relate.", "그러니 우리도 이 선량한 부부에게 작별을 고하고, 그분과 그 아리따운 동행들을 따라가 보자. 그들은 어찌나 부지런히 길을 재촉했던지, 이틀 만에 구십 마일을 달려 둘째 날 저녁에 런던에 도착했다."], ["Our pen, therefore, shall imitate the expedition which it describes, and our history shall keep pace with the travellers who are its subject.", "길에서 이 이야기의 품격에 어울려 들려줄 만한 모험이라고는 단 하나도 겪지 않은 채 말이다. 그러므로 우리의 붓도 이 묘사하는 신속함을 본받을 것이며, 우리의 이야기도 그 주인공인 여행자들과 발걸음을 맞추어 나아갈 것이다."], ["Good writers will, indeed, do well to imitate the ingenious traveller in this instance, who always proportions his stay at any place to the beauties, elegancies, and curiosities which it affords.", "좋은 작가라면 실로 이 대목에서, 어느 곳에 머무는 시간을 늘 그 고장이 베푸는 아름다움과 우아함과 진기함에 견주어 정하는 재간 있는 여행자를 본받음이 마땅하다."], ["At Eshur, at Stowe, at Wilton, at Eastbury, and at Prior's Park, days are too short for the ravished imagination; while we admire the wondrous power of art in improving nature.", "에셔에서, 스토에서, 윌턴에서, 이스트버리에서, 그리고 프라이어스 파크에서는, 황홀해진 상상력에 하루해가 너무 짧다. 자연을 가다듬는 기예의 경이로운 힘에 우리가 감탄하는 동안에는 말이다."], ["In some of these, art chiefly engages our admiration; in others, nature and art contend for our applause; but, in the last, the former seems to triumph.", "그중 어떤 곳에서는 주로 기예가 우리의 감탄을 자아내고, 어떤 곳에서는 자연과 기예가 우리의 갈채를 다툰다. 그러나 마지막 곳에서는 자연이 승리하는 듯 보인다."], ["Here Nature appears in her richest attire, and Art, dressed with the modestest simplicity, attends her benignant mistress.", "여기서는 자연이 가장 화려한 옷을 차려입고 나타나며, 기예는 더없이 수수한 단순함으로 단장하고서 그 자애로운 여주인을 섬긴다."], ["Here Nature indeed pours forth the choicest treasures which she hath lavished on this world; and here human nature presents you with an object which can be exceeded only in the other.", "여기서야말로 자연은 이 세상에 아낌없이 베푼 보화 가운데 가장 빼어난 것들을 쏟아 놓는다. 그리고 여기서 인간 본성은, 오직 저세상에서만 능가할 수 있을 대상을 그대 앞에 내보인다."]], [["The same taste, the same imagination, which luxuriously riots in these elegant scenes, can be amused with objects of far inferior note.", "이 우아한 풍경 속에서 호사스레 노니는 바로 그 안목, 그 상상력은, 훨씬 격이 낮은 대상으로도 즐거움을 누릴 수 있다. 데번과 도싯의 숲과 강과 풀밭은 재간 있는 여행자의 눈길을 사로잡아 그 걸음을 늦춘다."], ["The woods, the rivers, the lawns of Devon and of Dorset, attract the eye of the ingenious traveller, and retard his pace, which delay he afterwards compensates by swiftly scouring over the gloomy heath of Bagshot, or that pleasant plain which extends itself westward from Stockbridge, where no other object than one single tree only in sixteen miles presents itself to the view, unless the clouds, in compassion to our tired spirits, kindly open their variegated mansions to our prospect.", "그렇게 지체한 시간은, 뒤이어 음울한 백숏 황야나, 스톡브리지에서 서쪽으로 뻗어 나가는 그 흐뭇한 평원을 잽싸게 가로질러 달림으로써 메운다. 그 평원에서는 십육 마일을 가도록 외딴 나무 한 그루 말고는 눈에 띄는 것이 없으니, 지친 우리 마음을 가엾이 여긴 구름이 그 알록달록한 누각을 우리 시야에 다정히 열어 보여 주지 않는 한 그러하다."]], [["Not so travels the money-meditating tradesman, the sagacious justice, the dignified doctor, the warm-clad grazier, with all the numerous offspring of wealth and dulness.", "돈만 궁리하는 장사꾼, 영민한 치안판사, 위엄 부리는 의사, 따뜻이 차려입은 목축업자, 그리고 부유함과 둔함이 낳은 그 수많은 자손들은 이렇게 여행하지 않는다."], ["On they jog, with equal pace, through the verdant meadows or over the barren heath, their horses measuring four miles and a half per hour with the utmost exactness; the eyes of the beast and of his master being alike directed forwards, and employed in contemplating the same objects in the same manner.", "그들은 푸르른 풀밭을 지나든 메마른 황야를 넘든 한결같은 걸음으로 터덜터덜 나아가는데, 그 말들은 시속 사 마일 반을 더없이 정확하게 재며 걷는다. 짐승의 눈도 그 주인의 눈도 똑같이 앞만 바라보며, 똑같은 대상을 똑같은 방식으로 응시하는 데 쓰일 뿐이다."], ["With equal rapture the good rider surveys the proudest boasts of the architect, and those fair buildings with which some unknown name hath adorned the rich cloathing town; where heaps of bricks are piled up as a kind of monument to show that heaps of money have been piled there before.", "그 어진 기수는 건축가의 가장 자랑스러운 걸작들도, 또 어떤 이름 모를 자가 부유한 직물 도시를 꾸며 놓은 그 번듯한 건물들도 똑같은 황홀함으로 바라본다. 그 도시에서는 벽돌 무더기가 쌓여 올라가 있으니, 일찍이 그곳에 돈 무더기가 쌓여 올랐음을 보여 주는 일종의 기념비인 셈이다."]], [["And now, reader, as we are in haste to attend our heroine, we will leave to thy sagacity to apply all this to the Boeotian writers, and to those authors who are their opposites.", "자, 독자여, 이제 우리는 우리의 여주인공을 따라가기에 바쁘니, 이 모든 것을 보이오티아풍의 작가들과 그 정반대편에 선 작가들에게 적용해 보는 일은 그대의 영민함에 맡기겠다. 이 정도는 우리의 도움 없이도 그대가 넉넉히 해낼 수 있으리라."], ["This thou wilt be abundantly able to perform without our aid.", "그러니 이 기회에 그대 스스로 분발하라."], ["Bestir thyself therefore on this occasion; for, though we will always lend thee proper assistance in difficult places, as we do not, like some others, expect thee to use the arts of divination to discover our meaning, yet we shall not indulge thy laziness where nothing but thy own attention is required; for thou art highly mistaken if thou dost imagine that we intended, when we began this great work, to leave thy sagacity nothing to do; or that, without sometimes exercising this talent, thou wilt be able to travel through our pages with any pleasure or profit to thyself.", "까다로운 대목에서는 우리가 늘 알맞은 도움을 빌려주겠지만 — 더러 다른 이들처럼 그대더러 점치는 재주를 부려 우리의 뜻을 알아내라 요구하지는 않으니 — 그대 자신의 주의력만 있으면 될 곳에서까지 그대의 게으름을 받아 주지는 않을 것이다. 우리가 이 위대한 작업을 시작할 때 그대의 영민함이 할 일을 하나도 남겨 두지 않으려 했다고 여긴다면, 혹은 이따금 그 재능을 부려 보지 않고서도 우리의 책장을 헤쳐 가며 스스로 어떤 즐거움이나 유익을 얻을 수 있으리라 여긴다면, 그것은 그대의 큰 착각이기 때문이다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["Containing a hint or two concerning virtue, and a few more concerning suspicion.", "런던에 다다른 우리 일행은 백작 댁 앞에서 내렸다."], ["Our company, being arrived at London, were set down at his lordship's house, where, while they refreshed themselves after the fatigue of their journey, servants were despatched to provide a lodging for the two ladies; for, as her ladyship was not then in town, Mrs Fitzpatrick would by no means consent to accept a bed in the mansion of the peer.", "그곳에서 그들이 여행의 피로를 푸는 동안, 두 부인을 위한 거처를 마련하라는 명을 받은 하인들이 길을 나섰다. 그때 백작 부인이 시내에 없었던 까닭에, 피츠패트릭 부인은 그 귀족의 저택에서 잠자리를 받는 일만큼은 한사코 받아들이려 하지 않았다."]], [["Some readers will, perhaps, condemn this extraordinary delicacy, as I may call it, of virtue, as too nice and scrupulous; but we must make allowances for her situation, which must be owned to have been very ticklish; and, when we consider the malice of censorious tongues, we must allow, if it was a fault, the fault was an excess on the right side, and which every woman who is in the self-same situation will do well to imitate. The most formal appearance of virtue, when it is only an appearance, may, perhaps, in very abstracted considerations, seem to be rather less commendable than virtue itself without this formality; but it will, however, be always more commended; and this, I believe, will be granted by all, that it is necessary, unless in some very particular cases, for every woman to support either the one or the other.", "어떤 독자들은 아마도 이 유별난, 굳이 그렇게 부르자면, 덕의 섬세함을 너무 까탈스럽고 깐깐하다며 나무랄지도 모른다. 그러나 우리는 그녀의 처지를 헤아려 주어야 한다. 그 처지는 누가 보아도 매우 아슬아슬한 것이었음을 인정해야 하니 말이다. 게다가 트집 잡기 좋아하는 입들의 악의를 생각하면, 설령 이것이 잘못이었다 하더라도 그 잘못은 옳은 쪽으로 지나친 것이었음을 인정해야 하며, 똑같은 처지에 놓인 여인이라면 누구나 이를 본받는 편이 좋을 것이다. 덕의 가장 격식 차린 겉모습이란, 그것이 한낱 겉모습에 지나지 않을 때는, 아마도 매우 추상적인 견지에서 본다면 이런 격식 없는 덕 그 자체보다 도리어 조금 덜 칭찬할 만한 것으로 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 그럼에도 그것은 언제나 더 많은 칭찬을 받을 것이다. 그리고 다음 한 가지는 모두가 인정하리라 믿는다. 아주 특별한 몇몇 경우를 빼고는, 모든 여인이 둘 중 어느 하나만큼은 지켜 나가야 한다는 것을 말이다."]], [["A lodging being prepared, Sophia accompanied her cousin for that evening; but resolved early in the morning to enquire after the lady into whose protection, as we have formerly mentioned, she had determined to throw herself when she quitted her father's house.", "거처가 마련되자, 소피아는 그날 저녁만큼은 사촌과 함께 머물렀다. 그러나 이튿날 아침 일찍, 앞서 말한 대로 아버지의 집을 떠날 때 그 보호 아래로 몸을 던지기로 결심했던 그 부인의 행방을 수소문하기로 마음먹었다."], ["And this she was the more eager in doing from some observations she had made during her journey in the coach.", "마차로 오는 동안 그녀가 눈여겨본 몇 가지 일들 때문에, 그녀는 이 일을 더욱 서두르고 싶어졌다."]], [["Now, as we would by no means fix the odious character of suspicion on Sophia, we are almost afraid to open to our reader the conceits which filled her mind concerning Mrs Fitzpatrick; of whom she certainly entertained at present some doubts; which, as they are very apt to enter into the bosoms of the worst of people, we think proper not to mention more plainly till we have first suggested a word or two to our reader touching suspicion in general.", "자, 우리는 소피아에게 의심이라는 그 가증스러운 성격을 결코 덧씌우고 싶지 않은 까닭에, 피츠패트릭 부인을 두고 그녀의 마음을 가득 채운 그 상상들을 독자에게 펼쳐 보이기가 거의 두렵다. 소피아가 지금 그녀에 대해 어떤 의혹을 품고 있었음은 분명하다. 그런데 그런 의혹은 가장 못된 사람들의 가슴속으로도 곧잘 비집고 들어오는 법이니, 우리는 의심이라는 것 자체에 대해 독자에게 먼저 한두 마디 운을 떼기 전에는 그것을 더 또렷이 말하지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 본다."]], [["Of this there have always appeared to me to be two degrees.", "이 의심에는 늘 두 가지 정도가 있는 것으로 내게는 보였다."], ["The first of these I chuse to derive from the heart, as the extreme velocity of its discernment seems to denote some previous inward impulse, and the rather as this superlative degree often forms its own objects; sees what is not, and always more than really exists.", "그 첫 번째 의심은 마음에서 비롯된 것으로 보고 싶다. 그 분별이 지나치게 빠른 것을 보면 어떤 미리 자리한 내면의 충동을 드러내는 듯하고, 더구나 이 극단적인 정도의 의심은 흔히 제 대상마저 스스로 만들어 내어, 있지도 않은 것을 보고, 언제나 실제보다 더 많은 것을 보기 때문이다."], ["This is that quick-sighted penetration whose hawk's eyes no symptom of evil can escape; which observes not only upon the actions, but upon the words and looks, of men; and, as it proceeds from the heart of the observer, so it dives into the heart of the observed, and there espies evil, as it were, in the first embryo; nay, sometimes before it can be said to be conceived.", "이것이 바로 그 재빠른 통찰력이다. 그 매의 눈앞에서는 어떤 악의 징후도 빠져나가지 못하며, 사람들의 행동뿐 아니라 말과 표정까지 살핀다."], ["An admirable faculty, if it were infallible; but, as this degree of perfection is not even claimed by more than one mortal being; so from the fallibility of such acute discernment have arisen many sad mischiefs and most grievous heart-aches to innocence and virtue.", "그리고 그것이 관찰하는 자의 마음에서 나오는 만큼, 관찰당하는 자의 마음속으로도 파고들어, 그 안에서 악을 이를테면 첫 배아 상태로, 아니, 때로는 잉태되었다고 말할 수도 없는 단계에서 찾아낸다. 만일 이것이 결코 틀리는 법이 없다면 참으로 놀라운 재능일 것이다."], ["I cannot help, therefore, regarding this vast quick-sightedness into evil as a vicious excess, and as a very pernicious evil in itself.", "그러나 이 정도의 완벽함은 단 한 분의 존재 말고는 그 누구도 자기 것이라 내세우지 못한다. 그러기에 이토록 날카로운 분별의 그릇됨에서, 죄 없고 덕 있는 이들에게 수많은 슬픈 재앙과 더없이 쓰라린 가슴앓이가 생겨났다."], ["And I am the more inclined to this opinion, as I am afraid it always proceeds from a bad heart, for the reasons I have above mentioned, and for one more, namely, because I never knew it the property of a good one.", "그러므로 나는 악을 향한 이 엄청난 재빠른 시야를 어쩔 수 없이 악덕에 가까운 지나침으로, 또한 그 자체로 매우 해로운 악으로 여기게 된다. 그리고 나는 이 의견 쪽으로 더욱 기울게 되는데, 앞서 말한 이유들 때문에, 그리고 한 가지 더, 곧 나는 그것이 선한 마음의 속성인 적을 한 번도 본 적이 없는 까닭에, 그것이 늘 나쁜 마음에서 나오는 것이 아닐까 두렵기 때문이다."], ["Now, from this degree of suspicion I entirely and absolutely acquit Sophia.", "자, 이 정도의 의심에서 나는 소피아를 온전히, 그리고 절대적으로 결백하다 인정한다."]], [["A second degree of this quality seems to arise from the head.", "이 자질의 두 번째 등급은 머리에서 비롯되는 듯하다."], ["This is, indeed, no other than the faculty of seeing what is before your eyes, and of drawing conclusions from what you see.", "이것은 실로, 눈앞에 있는 것을 보고, 본 것으로부터 결론을 끌어내는 능력에 다름 아니다."], ["The former of these is unavoidable by those who have any eyes, and the latter is perhaps no less certain and necessary a consequence of our having any brains.", "앞의 것은 눈이라는 것을 가진 사람이라면 누구도 피할 수 없고, 뒤의 것은 어쩌면 우리가 뇌라는 것을 가진 데서 비롯되는, 그에 못지않게 확실하고 필연적인 결과일 것이다."], ["This is altogether as bitter an enemy to guilt as the former is to innocence: nor can I see it in an unamiable light, even though, through human fallibility, it should be sometimes mistaken.", "이것은 앞의 자질이 순결함에게 더없이 모진 적이듯, 죄악에게도 꼭 그만큼 모진 적이다. 게다가 나로서는, 인간의 오류 가능성 탓에 그것이 때로 잘못 짚는 일이 있다 하더라도, 그것을 곱지 않은 눈으로 볼 수가 없다."], ["For instance, if a husband should accidentally surprize his wife in the lap or in the embraces of some of those pretty young gentlemen who profess the art of cuckold-making, I should not highly, I think, blame him for concluding something more than what he saw, from the familiarities which he really had seen, and which we are at least favourable enough to when we call them innocent freedoms.", "이를테면, 어떤 남편이 우연히 자기 아내가 오쟁이 지우는 기술을 업으로 삼는 저 잘생긴 젊은 신사들 가운데 누군가의 무릎 위에 있거나 그 품에 안겨 있는 것을 들켰을 때, 그가 실제로 본 그 친밀한 정경으로부터—그리고 우리가 적어도 너그럽게 보아 줄 때는 순진한 장난이라 부르는 그런 정경으로부터—본 것 이상의 무언가를 결론짓는다 하여, 나는 그를 그리 심하게 탓하지는 않으리라 생각한다. 독자라면 스스로 무수한 사례를 쉽게 떠올릴 것이다."], ["The reader will easily suggest great plenty of instances to himself; I shall add but one more, which, however unchristian it may be thought by some, I cannot help esteeming to be strictly justifiable; and this is a suspicion that a man is capable of doing what he hath done already, and that it is possible for one who hath been a villain once to act the same part again.", "나는 다만 하나만 더 보태겠는데, 이는 어떤 이들에게는 비기독교적으로 여겨질지언정, 나로서는 엄격히 따져 정당하다고 여기지 않을 수 없는 것이다. 그것은 바로, 사람이란 이미 저지른 일을 또 저지를 수 있으며, 한 번 악당이었던 자는 같은 짓을 다시 할 수도 있다는 의심이다."], ["And, to confess the truth, of this degree of suspicion I believe Sophia was guilty.", "그리고 진실을 고백하자면, 소피아는 바로 이 등급의 의심에 빠져 있었던 듯하다."], ["From this degree of suspicion she had, in fact, conceived an opinion that her cousin was really not better than she should be.", "실은 이 등급의 의심에서, 그녀는 자기 사촌이 정말로 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 나은 사람은 아니라는 생각을 품게 되었던 것이다."]], [["The case, it seems, was this: Mrs Fitzpatrick wisely considered that the virtue of a young lady is, in the world, in the same situation with a poor hare, which is certain, whenever it ventures abroad, to meet its enemies; for it can hardly meet any other. No sooner therefore was she determined to take the first opportunity of quitting the protection of her husband, than she resolved to cast herself under the protection of some other man; and whom could she so properly choose to be her guardian as a person of quality, of fortune, of honour; and who, besides a gallant disposition which inclines men to knight-errantry, that is, to be the champions of ladies in distress, had often declared a violent attachment to herself, and had already given her all the instances of it in his power?", "사정은 이러했던 것 같다. 피츠패트릭 부인은 현명하게도, 젊은 숙녀의 정조란 이 세상에서 가련한 토끼와 똑같은 처지에 놓여 있다고 여겼다. 토끼는 밖으로 나설 때마다 어김없이 제 적을 만나기 마련이니, 사실 적 말고는 좀처럼 다른 무엇을 만나기 어렵다. 그리하여 그녀는 남편의 보호에서 벗어날 첫 기회를 잡기로 마음먹자마자, 곧바로 다른 어떤 남자의 보호 아래로 몸을 던지기로 작정했다. 그렇다면 신분 있고, 재산 있고, 명예로운 인물, 게다가 남자를 기사도적 모험으로—다시 말해 곤경에 빠진 숙녀의 투사로—이끄는 그 늠름한 기질을 지녔을 뿐 아니라, 그녀 자신에게 격렬한 애정을 거듭 고백했고 이미 제 능력이 닿는 한 온갖 증표를 보여 준 인물보다, 누구를 더 적절한 보호자로 고를 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["But, as the law hath foolishly omitted this office of vice-husband, or guardian to an eloped lady, and as malice is apt to denominate him by a more disagreeable appellation, it was concluded that his lordship should perform all such kind offices to the lady in secret, and without publickly assuming the character of her protector.", "그러나 법이란 어리석게도 이 부부 대리인, 곧 가출한 숙녀의 보호자라는 직책을 빠뜨려 놓았고, 또 악의는 그를 한층 더 거슬리는 호칭으로 부르기 십상이었으므로, 그의 영주께서는 이 모든 친절한 직무를 숙녀에게 은밀히, 그녀의 보호자라는 역할을 공공연히 떠맡는 일 없이 수행하기로 결론지어졌다."], ["Nay, to prevent any other person from seeing him in this light, it was agreed that the lady should proceed directly to Bath, and that his lordship should first go to London, and thence should go down to that place by the advice of his physicians.", "아니, 다른 누군가가 그를 그런 시선으로 보는 일을 막기 위해, 숙녀는 곧장 바스로 향하고, 영주께서는 먼저 런던으로 갔다가 거기서 주치의들의 권고에 따라 그곳으로 내려가기로 합의되었다."]], [["Now all this Sophia very plainly understood, not from the lips or behaviour of Mrs Fitzpatrick, but from the peer, who was infinitely less expert at retaining a secret than was the good lady; and perhaps the exact secrecy which Mrs Fitzpatrick had observed on this head in her narrative served not a little to heighten those suspicions which were now risen in the mind of her cousin.", "그런데 이 모든 것을 소피아는 아주 똑똑히 알아차렸으니, 피츠패트릭 부인의 입이나 거동에서가 아니라, 그 귀족에게서였다. 그는 그 착한 숙녀보다 비밀을 간직하는 데 한없이 서툴렀던 것이다. 그리고 어쩌면 피츠패트릭 부인이 자기 이야기 중에 이 대목에서만은 딱 잘라 비밀을 지켰던 바로 그 점이, 이제 사촌의 마음속에 솟아오른 그 의심을 적잖이 부추기는 데 한몫했을지도 모른다."]], [["Sophia very easily found out the lady she sought; for indeed there was not a chairman in town to whom her house was not perfectly well known; and, as she received, in return of her first message, a most pressing invitation, she immediately accepted it.", "소피아는 찾던 숙녀를 아주 쉽게 찾아냈다. 실로 이 도시에 그녀의 집을 훤히 모르는 가마꾼은 하나도 없었기 때문이다."], ["Mrs Fitzpatrick, indeed, did not desire her cousin to stay with her with more earnestness than civility required.", "그리고 첫 전갈에 대한 답으로 더없이 간곡한 초대를 받자, 그녀는 곧바로 그것을 받아들였다. 사실 피츠패트릭 부인이 사촌에게 함께 머물러 달라고 청한 마음에는 예의가 요구하는 것 이상의 간절함은 없었다."], ["Whether she had discerned and resented the suspicion above-mentioned, or from what other motive it arose, I cannot say; but certain it is, she was full as desirous of parting with Sophia as Sophia herself could be of going.", "그녀가 앞서 말한 그 의심을 알아채고 언짢아한 것인지, 아니면 다른 어떤 동기에서 비롯된 것인지는 내가 말할 수 없다. 다만 분명한 것은, 소피아가 떠나기를 바라는 그만큼이나 부인도 소피아와 헤어지기를 바라고 있었다는 사실이다."]], [["The young lady, when she came to take leave of her cousin, could not avoid giving her a short hint of advice. She begged her, for heaven's sake, to take care of herself, and to consider in how dangerous a situation she stood; adding, she hoped some method would be found of reconciling her to her husband. “You must remember, my dear,” says she, “the maxim which my aunt Western hath so often repeated to us both; That whenever the matrimonial alliance is broke, and war declared between husband and wife, she can hardly make a disadvantageous peace for herself on any conditions. These are my aunt's very words, and she hath had a great deal of experience in the world.” Mrs Fitzpatrick answered, with a contemptuous smile, “Never fear me, child, take care of yourself; for you are younger than I. I will come and visit you in a few days; but, dear Sophy, let me give you one piece of advice: leave the character of Graveairs in the country, for, believe me, it will sit very awkwardly upon you in this town.”", "이 젊은 숙녀는 사촌에게 작별을 고하러 왔을 때, 짤막한 충고 한마디를 건네지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 부인에게 제발 몸을 잘 챙기고, 자신이 얼마나 위태로운 처지에 서 있는지를 생각해 보라고 간청했다. 그러고는 남편과 화해할 어떤 방도가 찾아지기를 바란다고 덧붙였다. \"잊지 말아요, 언니.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"웨스턴 고모가 우리 둘에게 그토록 자주 되풀이하시던 그 격언 말이에요. 혼인의 결속이 깨지고 부부 사이에 전쟁이 선포되고 나면, 아내는 어떤 조건으로도 자신에게 불리하지 않은 화평을 맺기가 좀처럼 어렵다는 말이요. 이건 고모가 하신 바로 그 말씀이고, 고모는 세상 경험이 무척 많은 분이잖아요.\" 피츠패트릭 부인은 경멸 어린 미소를 띠며 대답했다. \"내 걱정은 말고, 얘야, 너나 잘 챙기렴. 넌 나보다 어리잖니. 며칠 안에 너를 보러 갈게. 하지만 사랑하는 소피, 한 가지만 충고해 줄게. 그 새침데기 노릇은 시골에 두고 오렴. 정말이지, 이 도시에서는 그게 너한테 무척 어색하게 들러붙을 테니까.\""]], [["Thus the two cousins parted, and Sophia repaired directly to Lady Bellaston, where she found a most hearty, as well as a most polite, welcome.", "이렇게 두 사촌은 헤어졌고, 소피아는 곧장 벨러스턴 부인에게로 갔다. 거기서 그녀는 더없이 정중하면서도 더없이 진심 어린 환대를 받았다."], ["The lady had taken a great fancy to her when she had seen her formerly with her aunt Western.", "부인은 예전에 웨스턴 고모와 함께 있는 그녀를 보았을 때 그녀를 무척 마음에 들어 했던 터였다."], ["She was indeed extremely glad to see her, and was no sooner acquainted with the reasons which induced her to leave the squire and to fly to London than she highly applauded her sense and resolution; and after expressing the highest satisfaction in the opinion which Sophia had declared she entertained of her ladyship, by chusing her house for an asylum, she promised her all the protection which it was in her power to give.", "부인은 실로 그녀를 보고 몹시 반가워했고, 그녀가 지주를 떠나 런던으로 달아나게 된 사정을 알게 되자마자 그녀의 분별과 결단을 한껏 칭찬했다. 그리고 소피아가 제 집을 피난처로 택함으로써 부인에 대해 품고 있다고 밝힌 그 생각에 더없는 만족을 표한 뒤, 부인은 자기 힘으로 베풀 수 있는 모든 보호를 그녀에게 약속했다."]], [["As we have now brought Sophia into safe hands, the reader will, I apprehend, be contented to deposit her there a while, and to look a little after other personages, and particularly poor Jones, whom we have left long enough to do penance for his past offences, which, as is the nature of vice, brought sufficient punishment upon him themselves.", "이제 우리가 소피아를 안전한 손에 맡겨 두었으니, 독자께서는 잠시 그녀를 그곳에 내려놓고, 다른 인물들을, 특히 가련한 존스를 좀 살펴보는 데 흔쾌히 동의해 주리라 짐작한다. 우리는 그가 지난 잘못을 속죄하도록 충분히 오래 내버려 두었는데, 그 잘못이란 악덕의 본성이 으레 그러하듯, 제 스스로 그에게 넉넉한 벌을 안겨 준 것이었다."]], [["# BOOK XII.", "# BOOK XII."]], [["CONTAINING THE SAME INDIVIDUAL TIME WITH THE FORMER. Chapter i.", "앞 권과 동일한 시간을 다룸."]], [["Showing what is to be deemed plagiarism in a modern author, and what is to be considered as lawful prize.", "현대 작가에게서 무엇을 표절로 보아야 하고, 무엇을 정당한 노획물로 여겨야 하는지 보여 줌."]], [["The learned reader must have observed that in the course of this mighty work, I have often translated passages out of the best antient authors, without quoting the original, or without taking the least notice of the book from whence they were borrowed.", "박식한 독자라면 이 방대한 작업을 진행하는 동안, 내가 가장 뛰어난 고대 작가들의 구절을 자주 옮겨 오면서도 원문을 인용하지 않거나, 그 글을 빌려 온 책에 대해 일말의 언급조차 하지 않았음을 눈치챘을 것이다."]], [["This conduct in writing is placed in a very proper light by the ingenious Abbé Bannier, in his preface to his Mythology, a work of great erudition and of equal judgment.", "글쓰기에서의 이러한 처신은, 박식한 바니에 신부가 그의 《신화》 서문에서—이 책은 대단한 학식과 그에 못지않은 판단력이 어우러진 노작이다—아주 적절하게 밝혀 두었다."], ["“It will be easy,”", "그는 이렇게 말한다."], ["says he, “for the reader to observe that I have frequently had greater regard to him than to my own reputation: for an author certainly pays him a considerable compliment, when, for his sake, he suppresses learned quotations that come in his way, and which would have cost him but the bare trouble of transcribing.”", "\"독자는 내가 종종 내 자신의 평판보다 그를 더 배려해 왔음을 어렵지 않게 알아차릴 것이다. 작가가, 독자를 위하여, 마침 손에 닿아 그저 베껴 적기만 하면 되었을 박식한 인용들을 억눌러 둔다면, 그것은 분명 독자에게 적잖은 경의를 표하는 일이기 때문이다.\""]], [["To fill up a work with these scraps may, indeed, be considered as a downright cheat on the learned world, who are by such means imposed upon to buy a second time, in fragments and by retail, what they have already in gross, if not in their memories, upon their shelves; and it is still more cruel upon the illiterate, who are drawn in to pay for what is of no manner of use to them. A writer who intermixes great quantity of Greek and Latin with his works, deals by the ladies and fine gentlemen in the same paultry manner with which they are treated by the auctioneers, who often endeavour so to confound and mix up their lots, that, in order to purchase the commodity you want, you are obliged at the same time to purchase that which will do you no service.", "이런 자투리들로 한 권의 책을 채우는 일은, 실로 박식한 세상을 노골적으로 속이는 짓이라 볼 수 있다. 그들이 이미 통째로—기억에는 없을지언정 적어도 서가에는—갖고 있는 것을, 그런 수법에 넘어가 조각조각, 그것도 소매로 두 번째로 사들이게 되니 말이다. 게다가 배우지 못한 이들에게는 한층 더 잔인한 일이니, 그들은 자기들에게 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 것을 사도록 꾀임을 당하기 때문이다. 자기 작품에 그리스어와 라틴어를 잔뜩 뒤섞어 넣는 작가는, 경매인이 부인들과 멋쟁이 신사들을 다루는 것과 똑같이 치사한 방식으로 그들을 대하는 셈이다. 경매인은 흔히 물건 묶음을 일부러 헷갈리게 뒤섞어 놓아, 정작 원하는 물건을 사려면 아무 쓸모 없는 것까지 함께 사지 않을 수 없게 만들지 않던가."]], [["And yet, as there is no conduct so fair and disinterested but that it may be misunderstood by ignorance, and misrepresented by malice, I have been sometimes tempted to preserve my own reputation at the expense of my reader, and to transcribe the original, or at least to quote chapter and verse, whenever I have made use either of the thought or expression of another.", "하지만 아무리 공정하고 사심 없는 처신이라 한들 무지에 오해받고 악의에 곡해되지 않는 법이 없으니, 나는 가끔 독자를 희생시켜 내 평판을 지켜 보고 싶은 유혹에 빠지기도 했다. 다른 사람의 생각이나 표현을 쓸 때마다 원문을 베껴 두거나, 적어도 몇 장 몇 절인지 그 출처를 밝혀 두고 싶었던 것이다."], ["I am, indeed, in some doubt that I have often suffered by the contrary method; and that, by suppressing the original author's name, I have been rather suspected of plagiarism than reputed to act from the amiable motive assigned by that justly celebrated Frenchman.", "실로 나는 그 반대의 방식 때문에 종종 손해를 보아 온 것이 아닌가 의심하기도 한다. 원작자의 이름을 숨김으로써, 그 정당하게 이름 높은 프랑스인이 일러 준 갸륵한 동기에서 그리하는 것으로 인정받기보다는 오히려 표절을 의심받아 온 것이 아닌가 하고 말이다."]], [["Now, to obviate all such imputations for the future, I do here confess and justify the fact.", "이제 앞으로 그런 모든 혐의를 미리 막아 두기 위하여, 나는 여기에서 그 사실을 고백하고 또 정당화하고자 한다."], ["The antients may be considered as a rich common, where every person who hath the smallest tenement in Parnassus hath a free right to fatten his muse.", "고대인이란 풍요로운 공유지라 볼 수 있으니, 파르나소스에 손바닥만 한 땅뙈기라도 가진 사람이면 누구나 그곳에서 제 뮤즈를 마음껏 살찌울 자유로운 권리가 있다."], ["Or, to place it in a clearer light, we moderns are to the antients what the poor are to the rich.", "더 또렷하게 말해 보자면, 우리 현대인은 고대인에 대하여 가난한 자가 부자에 대한 관계와 같다."], ["By the poor here I mean that large and venerable body which, in English, we call the mob.", "여기서 가난한 자라 함은, 영어로 우리가 군중이라 부르는 저 거대하고 유서 깊은 무리를 두고 하는 말이다."], ["Now, whoever hath had the honour to be admitted to any degree of intimacy with this mob, must well know that it is one of their established maxims to plunder and pillage their rich neighbours without any reluctance; and that this is held to be neither sin nor shame among them.", "그런데 이 군중과 얼마간이라도 가까이 지내는 영예를 누려 본 사람이라면, 부유한 이웃을 일말의 망설임도 없이 약탈하고 노략질하는 것이 그들의 확립된 신조 가운데 하나임을 잘 알 것이다. 그리고 그것이 그들 사이에서는 죄도 수치도 아닌 것으로 여겨진다는 사실 또한 알 것이다."], ["And so constantly do they abide and act by this maxim, that, in every parish almost in the kingdom, there is a kind of confederacy ever carrying on against a certain person of opulence called the squire, whose property is considered as free-booty by all his poor neighbours; who, as they conclude that there is no manner of guilt in such depredations, look upon it as a point of honour and moral obligation to conceal, and to preserve each other from punishment on all such occasions.", "그들이 이 신조를 어찌나 한결같이 지키고 따르는지, 이 왕국의 거의 모든 교구에는 향사라 불리는 어떤 부유한 인물을 상대로 늘 이어지는 일종의 결탁이 있을 정도다. 그의 재산은 가난한 이웃 모두에게 자유로운 노획물로 여겨지며, 그들은 그런 약탈에 아무런 죄도 없다고 결론짓는 까닭에, 그런 일이 있을 때마다 서로를 숨겨 주고 처벌에서 지켜 주는 것을 명예의 한 점이자 도덕적 의무로 여긴다."]], [["In like manner are the antients, such as Homer, Virgil, Horace, Cicero, and the rest, to be esteemed among us writers, as so many wealthy squires, from whom we, the poor of Parnassus, claim an immemorial custom of taking whatever we can come at.", "이와 마찬가지로, 호메로스, 베르길리우스, 호라티우스, 키케로, 그 밖의 고대인들은 우리 작가들 사이에서 수많은 부유한 향사들로 받들어 마땅하다. 우리, 곧 파르나소스의 가난한 자들은 그들에게서 손에 닿는 것이면 무엇이든 가져가는 것을 까마득한 옛날부터의 관습으로 주장하는 것이다."], ["This liberty I demand, and this I am as ready to allow again to my poor neighbours in their turn.", "나는 이 자유를 요구하며, 또한 가난한 이웃들에게도 그들 차례가 되면 똑같이 기꺼이 허락하려 한다."], ["All I profess, and all I require of my brethren, is to maintain the same strict honesty among ourselves which the mob show to one another.", "내가 공언하는 전부, 그리고 내가 동료들에게 요구하는 전부는, 군중이 서로에게 지키는 것과 똑같이 엄격한 정직함을 우리끼리 지키자는 것이다."], ["To steal from one another is indeed highly criminal and indecent; for this may be strictly stiled defrauding the poor (sometimes perhaps those who are poorer than ourselves), or, to set it under the most opprobrious colours, robbing the spittal.", "서로의 것을 훔치는 일은 실로 지극히 죄스럽고 점잖지 못한 짓이니, 이것이야말로 엄밀히 말해 가난한 자를(어쩌면 때로는 우리 자신보다 더 가난한 자를) 등쳐 먹는 짓이라 부를 만하며, 가장 치욕스러운 빛깔로 칠하자면 자선병원을 터는 짓이라 하겠다."]], [["Since, therefore, upon the strictest examination, my own conscience cannot lay any such pitiful theft to my charge, I am contented to plead guilty to the former accusation; nor shall I ever scruple to take to myself any passage which I shall find in an antient author to my purpose, without setting down the name of the author from whence it was taken.", "그러므로 아무리 엄정하게 따져 보아도 내 양심이 그런 비루한 도둑질을 내게 씌울 수 없는 이상, 나는 앞서의 혐의에 대해서는 기꺼이 유죄를 인정한다. 그리고 어느 고대 작가에게서 내 목적에 맞는 구절을 발견하면, 그것을 가져온 작가의 이름을 적어 두지 않고 내 것으로 삼는 데 결코 거리낌이 없을 것이다."], ["Nay, I absolutely claim a property in all such sentiments the moment they are transcribed into my writings, and I expect all readers henceforwards to regard them as purely and entirely my own.", "아니, 나는 그런 모든 사상이 내 글 속에 옮겨 적히는 순간 그것에 대한 소유권을 절대적으로 주장하며, 앞으로 모든 독자가 그것을 순전히 또 온전히 내 것으로 여겨 주기를 바란다."], ["This claim, however, I desire to be allowed me only on condition that I preserve strict honesty towards my poor brethren, from whom, if ever I borrow any of that little of which they are possessed, I shall never fail to put their mark upon it, that it may be at all times ready to be restored to the right owner.", "다만 이 주장이 내게 허락되기를 청하는 데에는 한 가지 조건이 있으니, 내가 가난한 동료들에게 엄격한 정직함을 지킨다는 것이다. 그들이 가진 그 보잘것없는 것 가운데 무엇이라도 빌려 올 때면, 나는 반드시 그 위에 그들의 표를 찍어 두어, 언제든 정당한 주인에게 돌려줄 수 있도록 할 것이다."]], [["The omission of this was highly blameable in one Mr Moore, who, having formerly borrowed some lines of Pope and company, took the liberty to transcribe six of them into his play of the Rival Modes. Mr Pope, however, very luckily found them in the said play, and, laying violent hands on his own property, transferred it back again into his own works; and, for a further punishment, imprisoned the said Moore in the loathsome dungeon of the Dunciad, where his unhappy memory now remains, and eternally will remain, as a proper punishment for such his unjust dealings in the poetical trade.", "이것을 빠뜨린 일로 무어라는 자는 크게 비난받아 마땅했다. 그는 전에 포프와 그 일행에게서 몇 줄을 빌려 와 놓고, 그중 여섯 줄을 제 희곡 《경쟁하는 유행》에 멋대로 옮겨 적었다. 그러나 포프 씨는 천만다행으로 그 줄들을 그 희곡 안에서 찾아내고는, 제 재산에 거센 손길을 뻗어 다시금 제 작품 속으로 되가져왔다. 그리고 한층 더한 벌로, 그 무어를 《던시어드》라는 역겨운 지하 감옥에 가두어 버렸으니, 그의 가련한 기억은 이제 그곳에 남아 있고, 또 영원히 남아 있을 것이다. 시문학의 거래에서 저지른 그런 부당한 처사에 대한 마땅한 벌로서 말이다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["In which, though the squire doth not find his daughter, something is found which puts an end to his pursuit.", "이 장에서, 지주는 딸을 찾지는 못하지만, 그의 추격에 종지부를 찍는 무언가가 발견된다."]], [["The history now returns to the inn at Upton, whence we shall first trace the footsteps of Squire Western; for, as he will soon arrive at an end of his journey, we shall have then full leisure to attend our heroe.", "이제 이야기는 업튼의 여관으로 되돌아간다. 거기서 우리는 먼저 웨스턴 지주의 발자취를 따라가 보려 한다. 그는 곧 여정의 끝에 다다를 터이니, 그 뒤에는 우리의 주인공에게 충분히 여유롭게 마음을 쏟을 수 있을 것이다."]], [["The reader may be pleased to remember that the said squire departed from the inn in great fury, and in that fury he pursued his daughter.", "앞서 그 지주가 잔뜩 성이 나서 여관을 떠났고, 그 분노에 휩싸인 채 딸을 뒤쫓았다는 것을 독자는 부디 기억해 주기 바란다."], ["The hostler having informed him that she had crossed the Severn, he likewise past that river with his equipage, and rode full speed, vowing the utmost vengeance against poor Sophia, if he should but overtake her.", "마구간지기가 딸이 세번강을 건넜다고 일러 주자, 그 역시 일행을 거느리고 강을 건넜고, 가엾은 소피아를 따라잡기만 한다면 더없이 매서운 복수를 퍼붓겠노라 맹세하며 전속력으로 말을 몰았다."]], [["He had not gone far before he arrived at a crossway.", "얼마 가지 못해 그는 갈림길에 이르렀다."], ["Here he called a short council of war, in which, after hearing different opinions, he at last gave the direction of his pursuit to fortune, and struck directly into the Worcester road.", "여기서 그는 짧은 작전 회의를 열었는데, 여러 의견을 다 들은 끝에 마침내 추격의 방향을 운명에 맡기기로 하고, 곧장 우스터로 가는 길로 들어섰다."]], [["In this road he proceeded about two miles, when he began to bemoan himself most bitterly, frequently crying out, “What pity is it!", "이 길을 한 이 마일쯤 나아갔을 때, 그는 자기 신세를 더없이 쓰라리게 한탄하기 시작했다. \"이런 가엾을 데가!"], ["Sure never was so unlucky a dog as myself!”", "정녕 나만큼 운수 사나운 개 같은 놈은 없었을 게다!\""], ["And then burst forth a volley of oaths and execrations.", "하고 연방 외쳐 대더니, 이내 욕설과 저주를 한바탕 쏟아 냈다."]], [["The parson attempted to administer comfort to him on this occasion.", "목사는 이 자리에서 그를 위로해 보려 했다. \"슬퍼하지 마십시오, 나리.\""], ["“Sorrow not, sir,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “like those without hope.", "\"희망 없는 자들처럼 슬퍼하지는 마십시오."], ["Howbeit we have not yet been able to overtake young madam, we may account it some good fortune that we have hitherto traced her course aright.", "비록 우리가 아직 젊은 아씨를 따라잡지는 못하였으나, 여태껏 그 자취를 바르게 좇아온 것만으로도 어느 정도 다행한 일로 여길 수 있습니다. 어쩌면 아씨께서 곧 여정에 지쳐, 몸의 기력을 되살리려 어느 여관에 머무를지도 모릅니다."], ["Peradventure she will soon be fatigated with her journey, and will tarry in some inn, in order to renovate her corporeal functions; and in that case, in all moral certainty, you will very briefly be compos voti.”", "그리되면, 도덕적으로 거의 확실하게도, 나리께서는 머지않아 소원을 이루실 것입니다.\" * 콤포스 보티(compos voti): 라틴어로 \"소원을 이룬 자\"라는 뜻."]], [["“Pogh!", "\"풋!"], ["d--n the slut!”", "빌어먹을 계집!\""], ["answered the squire, “I am lamenting the loss of so fine a morning for hunting.", "지주가 대꾸했다. \"내가 한탄하는 건 이 좋은 아침을 사냥도 못 하고 날려 버린 일이오."], ["It is confounded hard to lose one of the best scenting days, in all appearance, which hath been this season, and especially after so long a frost.”", "이번 철 들어 보아하니 가장 좋은 냄새 맡기 날 중 하루를, 그것도 그 긴 서리 끝에 이렇게 놓치다니, 분통 터질 노릇이지 뭐요.\""]], [["Whether Fortune, who now and then shows some compassion in her wantonest tricks, might not take pity of the squire; and, as she had determined not to let him overtake his daughter, might not resolve to make him amends some other way, I will not assert; but he had hardly uttered the words just before commemorated, and two or three oaths at their heels, when a pack of hounds began to open their melodious throats at a small distance from them, which the squire's horse and his rider both perceiving, both immediately pricked up their ears, and the squire, crying, “She's gone, she's gone! Damn me if she is not gone!” instantly clapped spurs to the beast, who little needed it, having indeed the same inclination with his master; and now the whole company, crossing into a corn-field, rode directly towards the hounds, with much hallowing and whooping, while the poor parson, blessing himself, brought up the rear.", "이따금 가장 짓궂은 장난 속에서도 얼마간 자비를 베푸는 운명의 여신이, 혹 이 지주를 가엾이 여겼는지, 그리하여 그가 딸을 따라잡지는 못하게 작정해 놓고도 다른 방식으로 그에게 보상해 주기로 마음먹은 것은 아닌지, 나는 단언하지 않겠다. 그러나 그가 방금 적은 그 말을 채 내뱉기가 무섭게, 그 뒤로 두세 마디 욕설을 줄줄이 붙이자마자, 그들로부터 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 한 무리의 사냥개들이 그 가락진 목청을 트기 시작했다. 지주의 말과 그 위에 탄 사람이 둘 다 이를 알아채고는, 둘 다 즉시 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다. 지주는 \"갔다, 갔어! 안 갔으면 내 목을 내놓겠다!\" 하고 외치며, 곧장 짐승에게 박차를 가했다. 짐승은 박차가 거의 필요치도 않았으니, 실로 제 주인과 똑같은 마음이었던 것이다. 이제 일행 전부가 옥수수밭으로 가로질러 들어, 떠들썩하게 고함치고 함성을 지르며 곧장 사냥개들을 향해 말을 몰았고, 그 와중에 가엾은 목사는 성호를 그으며 맨 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Thus fable reports that the fair Grimalkin, whom Venus, at the desire of a passionate lover, converted from a cat into a fine woman, no sooner perceived a mouse than, mindful of her former sport, and still retaining her pristine nature, she leaped from the bed of her husband to pursue the little animal.", "이렇게 우화는 전한다. 어느 열렬한 연인의 청에 따라 비너스가 고양이에서 어여쁜 여인으로 바꾸어 준 아리따운 그리말킨은, 생쥐 한 마리를 보자마자 옛 놀이를 떠올리고 본래의 천성을 그대로 간직한 터라, 남편의 잠자리에서 펄쩍 뛰쳐나와 그 작은 짐승을 뒤쫓았다고."]], [["What are we to understand by this? Not that the bride was displeased with the embraces of her amorous bridegroom; for, though some have remarked that cats are subject to ingratitude, yet women and cats too will be pleased and purr on certain occasions. The truth is, as the sagacious Sir Roger L'Estrange observes, in his deep reflections, that, “if we shut Nature out at the door, she will come in at the window; and that puss, though a madam, will be a mouser still.” In the same manner we are not to arraign the squire of any want of love for his daughter; for in reality he had a great deal; we are only to consider that he was a squire and a sportsman, and then we may apply the fable to him, and the judicious reflections likewise.", "이것을 우리는 어떻게 이해해야 할까? 신부가 사랑에 겨운 신랑의 포옹을 싫어했다는 뜻은 아니다. 더러 고양이가 배은망덕하기 쉽다고들 하지만, 여인도 고양이도 어떤 때에는 흡족해하며 가르랑거리는 법이니까. 진실은, 명민한 로저 레스트레인지 경이 그 깊은 사색 속에서 말한 그대로다. \"자연을 문으로 내쫓으면, 그것은 창으로 들어온다. 그리고 고양이란, 비록 귀부인이 되었어도 여전히 쥐잡이일 것이다.\" 같은 식으로, 우리는 지주가 딸을 향한 사랑이 모자랐다고 책망해서는 안 된다. 사실 그에게는 사랑이 넘치도록 많았으니까. 다만 그가 지주이자 사냥꾼이었음을 헤아리면, 우리는 그 우화를, 그리고 그 사려 깊은 성찰까지도 그에게 적용해 볼 수 있을 것이다."]], [["The hounds ran very hard, as it is called, and the squire pursued over hedge and ditch, with all his usual vociferation and alacrity, and with all his usual pleasure; nor did the thoughts of Sophia ever once intrude themselves to allay the satisfaction he enjoyed in the chace, which, he said, was one of the finest he ever saw, and which he swore was very well worth going fifty miles for. As the squire forgot his daughter, the servants, we may easily believe, forgot their mistress; and the parson, after having expressed much astonishment, in Latin, to himself, at length likewise abandoned all farther thoughts of the young lady, and, jogging on at a distance behind, began to meditate a portion of doctrine for the ensuing Sunday.", "사냥개들은, 그들 말마따나 무척 세차게 내달렸고, 지주는 산울타리와 도랑을 넘으며 여느 때와 다름없는 고함과 민첩함으로, 또 여느 때와 다름없는 즐거움으로 그 뒤를 쫓았다. 소피아에 대한 생각은 그가 사냥에서 누리는 만족을 가시게 하려고 단 한 번도 끼어들지 않았다. 그는 이것이 여태 본 사냥 중에서도 손꼽히게 멋진 것이라 했고, 오십 마일을 달려와도 아깝지 않을 만큼 값진 것이라 맹세했다. 지주가 딸을 잊었으니, 하인들이 안주인을 잊었으리라는 것도 우리는 쉽사리 믿을 수 있다. 목사 또한, 혼자서 라틴어로 한참 경탄을 늘어놓은 끝에 마침내 젊은 아씨에 대한 더 이상의 생각을 죄다 내려놓고, 저만치 뒤에서 느릿느릿 따라가며 다가오는 일요일을 위한 설교 한 토막을 궁리하기 시작했다."]], [["The squire who owned the hounds was highly pleased with the arrival of his brother squire and sportsman; for all men approve merit in their own way, and no man was more expert in the field than Mr Western, nor did any other better know how to encourage the dogs with his voice, and to animate the hunt with his holla.", "그 사냥개들의 주인인 지주는, 같은 지주이자 사냥꾼인 동무가 끼어든 것을 무척 기뻐했다. 사람이란 누구나 제 분야에서 솜씨를 알아보는 법인데, 들판에서 웨스턴 씨보다 노련한 사람은 없었고, 목소리로 개들을 북돋고 고함으로 사냥을 달구는 일에서도 그를 능가할 자가 없었던 것이다."]], [["Sportsmen, in the warmth of a chace, are too much engaged to attend to any manner of ceremony, nay, even to the offices of humanity: for, if any of them meet with an accident by tumbling into a ditch, or into a river, the rest pass on regardless, and generally leave him to his fate: during this time, therefore, the two squires, though often close to each other, interchanged not a single word.", "사냥꾼들은 추격의 열기에 휩싸이면 어떤 예법에도, 아니 사람으로서 마땅히 챙겨야 할 도리에조차 마음 쓸 겨를이 없을 만큼 빠져 든다. 누군가가 도랑이나 강에 곤두박질치는 사고를 당해도, 나머지는 아랑곳없이 지나쳐 가며 대개는 그를 제 운명에 맡겨 둔다."], ["The master of the hunt, however, often saw and approved the great judgment of the stranger in drawing the dogs when they were at a fault, and hence conceived a very high opinion of his understanding, as the number of his attendants inspired no small reverence to his quality.", "그러므로 그동안 두 지주는, 서로 가까이 붙어 있을 때가 많았어도 단 한 마디도 주고받지 않았다. 그래도 사냥의 주인은, 개들이 냄새를 놓쳤을 때 그 낯선 이가 개들을 끌어 모으는 빼어난 판단을 여러 번 보고 감탄하여, 그의 식견을 매우 높이 사게 되었다."], ["As soon, therefore, as the sport was ended by the death of the little animal which had occasioned it, the two squires met, and in all squire-like greeting saluted each other.", "게다가 그를 따르는 수행원의 수는 그의 지체에 대한 적잖은 경의를 불러일으켰다. 그리하여 추격을 일으킨 그 작은 짐승이 죽음으로써 사냥이 끝나자마자, 두 지주는 마주하여 지주다운 격식을 한껏 갖추며 서로 인사를 나누었다."]], [["The conversation was entertaining enough, and what we may perhaps relate in an appendix, or on some other occasion; but as it nowise concerns this history, we cannot prevail on ourselves to give it a place here.", "두 사람의 대화는 제법 재미있었으니, 어쩌면 부록에서나 다른 기회에 옮겨 볼 수도 있겠다. 다만 이 이야기와는 아무 상관이 없으므로, 여기에 그것을 끼워 넣을 마음은 도무지 들지 않는다."], ["It concluded with a second chace, and that with an invitation to dinner.", "대화는 두 번째 사냥으로 마무리되었고, 그것은 다시 저녁 식사 초대로 이어졌다."], ["This being accepted, was followed by a hearty bout of drinking, which ended in as hearty a nap on the part of Squire Western.", "이 초대를 받아들이자 거나한 술자리가 뒤따랐는데, 그 술자리는 웨스턴 지주 쪽에서 똑같이 거나한 낮잠으로 끝이 났다."]], [["Our squire was by no means a match either for his host, or for parson Supple, at his cups that evening; for which the violent fatigue of mind as well as body that he had undergone, may very well account, without the least derogation from his honour. He was indeed, according to the vulgar phrase, whistle drunk; for before he had swallowed the third bottle, he became so entirely overpowered that though he was not carried off to bed till long after, the parson considered him as absent, and having acquainted the other squire with all relating to Sophia, he obtained his promise of seconding those arguments which he intended to urge the next morning for Mr Western's return.", "우리의 지주는 그날 저녁 술잔치에서 주인에게도, 서플 목사에게도 도무지 상대가 되지 못했다. 다만 이는, 그가 겪은 몸과 마음의 극심한 피로로 충분히 설명되는 일이니, 그의 명예에는 조금도 누가 되지 않는다. 그는 실로, 흔한 말로 하자면 휘파람도 못 불 만큼 곯아떨어졌다. 세 병째를 채 비우기도 전에 완전히 인사불성이 된 것이다. 그가 잠자리로 옮겨진 것은 한참 뒤였지만, 목사는 그를 이미 자리에 없는 사람으로 여기고, 다른 지주에게 소피아에 관한 일을 죄다 일러 준 뒤, 이튿날 아침 웨스턴 씨를 집으로 돌려보내려 펼칠 논리에 힘을 보태 주겠다는 약속을 받아 냈다."]], [["No sooner, therefore, had the good squire shaken off his evening, and began to call for his morning draught, and to summon his horses in order to renew his pursuit, than Mr Supple began his dissuasives, which the host so strongly seconded, that they at length prevailed, and Mr Western agreed to return home; being principally moved by one argument, viz., that he knew not which way to go, and might probably be riding farther from his daughter instead of towards her. He then took leave of his brother sportsman, and expressing great joy that the frost was broken (which might perhaps be no small motive to his hastening home), set forwards, or rather backwards, for Somersetshire; but not before he had first despatched part of his retinue in quest of his daughter, after whom he likewise sent a volley of the most bitter execrations which he could invent.", "그리하여 마음씨 좋은 지주가 저녁 술기운을 떨쳐 내고, 아침 해장술을 청하며 추격을 다시 시작하려 말을 대령하라 이르기가 무섭게, 서플 씨가 만류하기 시작했다. 주인 또한 어찌나 거들었던지, 마침내 그 설득이 먹혀들어 웨스턴 씨는 집으로 돌아가기로 했다. 그를 가장 크게 움직인 논거는 한 가지였으니, 곧 그가 어디로 가야 할지 알 수 없는 터라, 딸 쪽으로 가기는커녕 오히려 딸에게서 더 멀어지는 쪽으로 말을 몰고 있는지도 모른다는 것이었다. 이윽고 그는 동료 사냥꾼에게 작별을 고하고, 서리가 풀린 것을 크게 기뻐하며(어쩌면 이것이야말로 그가 서둘러 귀가하게 만든 적잖은 동기였을 것이다) 서머싯셔를 향해 앞으로, 아니 차라리 뒤로 출발했다. 다만 그에 앞서, 먼저 수행원 일부를 딸을 찾아 떠나보냈고, 그 뒤를 좇아 자기가 지어낼 수 있는 가장 쓰디쓴 저주를 한바탕 함께 날려 보냈다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["The departure of Jones from Upton, with what passed between him and Partridge on the road.", "3장. 존스가 업턴을 떠나는 일과, 길 위에서 그와 파트리지 사이에 오간 이야기."]], [["At length we are once more come to our heroe; and, to say truth, we have been obliged to part with him so long, that, considering the condition in which we left him, I apprehend many of our readers have concluded we intended to abandon him for ever; he being at present in that situation in which prudent people usually desist from enquiring any farther after their friends, lest they should be shocked by hearing such friends had hanged themselves.", "마침내 우리는 다시 한번 우리의 주인공에게로 돌아왔다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그와 너무 오래 떨어져 있을 수밖에 없었던 터라, 그를 두고 떠나올 때의 처지를 생각하면, 독자들 가운데 많은 이가 우리가 그를 영영 내버려 둘 작정이었다고 결론지었으리라 짐작된다. 지금 그가 처한 상황이란, 분별 있는 사람들이라면 더 이상 친구의 안부를 캐묻기를 그만두는 그런 상황이기 때문이다. 행여 그런 친구가 목을 매었다는 소식이라도 듣고 충격받을까 두려워서 말이다."]], [["But, in reality, if we have not all the virtues, I will boldly say, neither have we all the vices of a prudent character; and though it is not easy to conceive circumstances much more miserable than those of poor Jones at present, we shall return to him, and attend upon him with the same diligence as if he was wantoning in the brightest beams of fortune.", "그러나 실은, 우리에게 분별 있는 사람의 미덕이 다 갖춰져 있지는 않을지언정, 그 악덕 또한 다 갖춰져 있지는 않다고 나는 당당히 말하겠다. 지금 가엾은 존스의 처지보다 훨씬 더 비참한 상황을 떠올리기란 쉽지 않지만, 그래도 우리는 그에게로 돌아가 그를 살필 것이다. 마치 그가 가장 눈부신 행운의 햇살 속에서 마음껏 노닐고 있기라도 한 것처럼, 똑같은 정성을 다해서 말이다."]], [["Mr Jones, then, and his companion Partridge, left the inn a few minutes after the departure of Squire Western, and pursued the same road on foot, for the hostler told them that no horses were by any means to be at that time procured at Upton. On they marched with heavy hearts; for though their disquiet proceeded from very different reasons, yet displeased they were both; and if Jones sighed bitterly, Partridge grunted altogether as sadly at every step.", "그리하여 존스 군과 그의 동행 파트리지는, 웨스턴 영주가 떠난 지 몇 분 지나지 않아 여관을 나서서 같은 길을 걸어 나아갔다. 마구간지기가 그들에게, 지금 업턴에서는 무슨 수를 써도 말을 구할 수 없다고 일러 준 까닭이었다. 두 사람은 무거운 마음을 안고 걸음을 옮겼다. 비록 그 심란함이 서로 전혀 다른 이유에서 비롯된 것이긴 했어도, 어쨌든 둘 다 언짢기는 매한가지였다. 존스가 쓰디쓴 한숨을 내쉬면, 파트리지는 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 그에 못지않게 구슬프게 끙끙거렸다."]], [["When they came to the cross-roads where the squire had stopt to take counsel, Jones stopt likewise, and turning to Partridge, asked his opinion which track they should pursue.", "영주가 어느 길로 갈지 의논하느라 멈춰 섰던 그 갈림길에 이르자, 존스도 걸음을 멈추고 파트리지를 돌아보며 어느 길로 가야 할지 의견을 물었다. \"아, 나리,\""], ["“Ah, sir,”", "파트리지가 대답했다."], ["answered Partridge, “I wish your honour would follow my advice.”", "\"나리께서 제 충고를 따라 주셨으면 합니다.\" \"내가 따르지 못할 까닭이 무엇이냐?\""], ["“Why should I not?”", "존스가 대꾸했다."], ["replied Jones; “for it is now indifferent to me whither I go, or what becomes of me.”", "\"이제 내가 어디로 가든, 내 신세가 어찌 되든 아무 상관도 없으니 말이다.\" \"그러시다면 제 충고는,\""], ["“My advice, then,”", "파트리지가 말했다."], ["said Partridge, “is, that you immediately face about and return home; for who that hath such a home to return to as your honour, would travel thus about the country like a vagabond?", "\"당장 발길을 돌려 집으로 돌아가시라는 것입니다. 나리처럼 돌아갈 만한 집이 있는 분이라면, 어찌 이렇게 떠돌이처럼 온 나라를 헤매고 다니시겠습니까?"], ["I ask pardon, sed vox ea sola reperta est.”", "용서하십시오, 다만 그 말밖에는 달리 떠오르질 않아서요.\""]], [["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “I have no home to return to;--but if my friend, my father, would receive me, could I bear the country from which Sophia is flown?", "\"나에게는 돌아갈 집이 없다. 설령 내 친구이자 아버지 같은 그분이 나를 받아 주신다 한들, 소피아가 떠나 버린 그 고장을 내가 어찌 견딜 수 있겠느냐?"], ["Cruel Sophia!", "모진 소피아!"], ["Cruel!", "모질구나!"], ["No; let me blame myself!--No; let me blame thee.", "아니, 나 자신을 탓하자! 아니, 너를 탓하자."], ["D--nation seize thee--fool--blockhead!", "빌어먹을 놈, 멍청이, 돌대가리 같으니! 네가 나를 망쳐 놓았다."], ["thou hast undone me, and I will tear thy soul from thy body.”--At which words he laid violent hands on the collar of poor Partridge, and shook him more heartily than an ague-fit, or his own fears had ever done before.", "내 너의 몸뚱이에서 영혼을 뽑아내고 말겠다.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 가엾은 파트리지의 멱살을 거칠게 움켜쥐고는, 학질의 발작이나 그 자신의 공포가 일찍이 그를 떨게 한 것보다도 더 세차게 흔들어 댔다."]], [["Partridge fell trembling on his knees, and begged for mercy, vowing he had meant no harm--when Jones, after staring wildly on him for a moment, quitted his hold, and discharged a rage on himself, that, had it fallen on the other, would certainly have put an end to his being, which indeed the very apprehension of it had almost effected.", "파트리지는 부들부들 떨며 무릎을 꿇고 자비를 구하면서, 결코 해를 끼칠 뜻은 없었노라 맹세했다. 그러자 존스는 잠시 그를 사납게 노려보더니, 움켜쥔 손을 놓고는 자기 자신에게 분노를 쏟아부었다. 만일 그 분노가 상대에게 떨어졌더라면 그의 목숨을 끝장내고도 남았을 정도였으니, 실은 그러리라는 두려움만으로도 파트리지는 거의 숨이 넘어갈 뻔했다."]], [["We would bestow some pains here in minutely describing all the mad pranks which Jones played on this occasion, could we be well assured that the reader would take the same pains in perusing them; but as we are apprehensive that, after all the labour which we should employ in painting this scene, the said reader would be very apt to skip it entirely over, we have saved ourselves that trouble.", "여기서 우리는 이때 존스가 부린 그 온갖 미친 짓을 일일이 세세하게 묘사하느라 적잖은 공을 들일 수도 있겠다. 독자가 그것을 읽는 데도 똑같은 공을 들여 주리라 확신만 할 수 있다면 말이다."], ["To say the truth, we have, from this reason alone, often done great violence to the luxuriance of our genius, and have left many excellent descriptions out of our work, which would otherwise have been in it.", "하지만 우리가 이 장면을 그려 내느라 온갖 노고를 다 기울인들, 그 독자는 그 대목을 통째로 건너뛰어 버리기 십상이리라는 걱정이 들기에, 우리는 아예 그 수고를 덜기로 했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 바로 이 한 가지 이유 때문에 우리는 우리 재능의 무성함을 종종 크게 억눌러 왔고, 그러지 않았더라면 작품 속에 들어갔을 훌륭한 묘사를 여럿 빼 버리곤 했다."], ["And this suspicion, to be honest, arises, as is generally the case, from our own wicked heart; for we have, ourselves, been very often most horridly given to jumping, as we have run through the pages of voluminous historians.", "그리고 솔직히 말해, 이런 의심은 흔히 그렇듯 우리 자신의 못된 마음에서 비롯된 것이다. 우리 또한 방대한 역사가들의 책장을 넘기며, 끔찍하게도 건너뛰기에 빠져들곤 했던 적이 한두 번이 아니기 때문이다."]], [["Suffice it then simply to say, that Jones, after having played the part of a madman for many minutes, came, by degrees, to himself; which no sooner happened, than, turning to Partridge, he very earnestly begged his pardon for the attack he had made on him in the violence of his passion; but concluded, by desiring him never to mention his return again; for he was resolved never to see that country any more.", "그러니 그저 이렇게만 말해 두자. 존스는 여러 분 동안 미치광이 노릇을 한 끝에, 차츰 제정신을 되찾았다. 그러자마자 그는 파트리지를 돌아보며, 격정에 사로잡혀 그에게 손찌검한 일을 진심으로 용서해 달라고 빌었다. 그러나 끝에 가서는, 다시는 집으로 돌아가자는 말을 꺼내지 말라고 당부했다. 그 고장은 두 번 다시 보지 않기로 마음을 굳혔기 때문이었다."]], [["Partridge easily forgave, and faithfully promised to obey the injunction now laid upon him.", "파트리지는 선뜻 용서하고는, 이제 자기에게 내려진 그 분부를 충실히 따르겠노라 굳게 약속했다."], ["And then Jones very briskly cried out, “Since it is absolutely impossible for me to pursue any farther the steps of my angel--I will pursue those of glory.", "그러자 존스가 아주 활기차게 외쳤다. \"내 천사의 발자취를 더는 좇을 길이 도무지 없으니, 이제 나는 영광의 발자취를 좇으리라."], ["Come on, my brave lad, now for the army:--it is a glorious cause, and I would willingly sacrifice my life in it, even though it was worth my preserving.”", "자, 가자, 용감한 친구야, 이제 군대로 가는 거다. 그것은 영광스러운 대의이니, 비록 내 목숨이 지킬 만한 값어치가 있다 해도 나는 기꺼이 그 대의에 그것을 바치겠다.\""], ["And so saying, he immediately struck into the different road from that which the squire had taken, and, by mere chance, pursued the very same through which Sophia had before passed.", "이렇게 말하고 그는 곧장 영주가 택한 길과는 다른 길로 접어들었는데, 순전히 우연으로, 앞서 소피아가 지나간 바로 그 길을 따라가게 되었다."]], [["Our travellers now marched a full mile, without speaking a syllable to each other, though Jones, indeed, muttered many things to himself. As to Partridge, he was profoundly silent; for he was not, perhaps, perfectly recovered from his former fright; besides, he had apprehensions of provoking his friend to a second fit of wrath, especially as he now began to entertain a conceit, which may not, perhaps, create any great wonder in the reader. In short, he began now to suspect that Jones was absolutely out of his senses.", "이제 우리의 나그네들은 서로 한마디도 주고받지 않은 채 꼬박 일 마일을 걸었다. 물론 존스는 혼자서 이런저런 말을 중얼거리기는 했다. 파트리지로 말하자면, 그는 깊은 침묵에 잠겨 있었다. 어쩌면 앞서 받은 놀라움에서 아직 완전히 헤어나지 못한 탓이었으리라. 게다가 그는 친구를 두 번째 분노의 발작으로 몰아넣을까 봐 겁이 났는데, 특히 이제 한 가지 생각을 품기 시작한 터라 더욱 그랬다. 이 생각은 독자에게 그리 큰 놀라움을 자아내지는 않을 것이다. 요컨대 그는 이제 존스가 완전히 정신이 나가 버린 것이 아닌가 의심하기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["At length, Jones, being weary of soliloquy, addressed himself to his companion, and blamed him for his taciturnity; for which the poor man very honestly accounted, from his fear of giving offence.", "마침내 존스는 혼잣말에 싫증이 나서 동행에게 말을 건네며, 그의 과묵함을 나무랐다. 그러자 가엾은 사내는 아주 솔직하게, 노여움을 살까 두려워서 그랬노라고 해명했다."], ["And now this fear being pretty well removed, by the most absolute promises of indemnity, Partridge again took the bridle from his tongue; which, perhaps, rejoiced no less at regaining its liberty, than a young colt, when the bridle is slipt from his neck, and he is turned loose into the pastures.", "이제 결코 책망하지 않겠다는 더없이 확고한 약속으로 그 두려움이 어지간히 가시자, 파트리지는 다시 자기 혀에서 고삐를 풀었다. 그 혀는, 목에서 고삐가 벗겨져 풀밭으로 풀려난 어린 망아지 못지않게, 자유를 되찾은 것을 기뻐했으리라."]], [["As Partridge was inhibited from that topic which would have first suggested itself, he fell upon that which was next uppermost in his mind, namely, the Man of the Hill.", "파트리지는 가장 먼저 떠올랐을 그 화제를 입에 올리지 못하게 된 터라, 마음속에서 그다음으로 떠오른 화제로 옮겨 갔으니, 바로 산속의 사내 이야기였다. \"정말이지요, 나리,\""], ["“Certainly, sir,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “that could never be a man, who dresses himself and lives after such a strange manner, and so unlike other folks.", "\"그렇게 괴상한 차림으로, 그렇게 이상하게, 여느 사람들과는 영 딴판으로 살아가는 자가 사람일 리가 없습니다. 게다가 그 노파가 제게 말하기를, 그자가 먹는 것은 주로 풀이라던데, 그건 기독교인보다 말에게나 어울릴 먹이지요."], ["Besides, his diet, as the old woman told me, is chiefly upon herbs, which is a fitter food for a horse than a Christian: nay, landlord at Upton says that the neighbours thereabouts have very fearful notions about him.", "아니, 업턴의 여관 주인 말로는, 그 근방 이웃들이 그자에 대해 아주 무시무시한 생각을 품고 있답니다. 제 머릿속에는 이상하게도, 그게 어떤 혼령이었음에 틀림없다는 생각이 자꾸만 맴돕니다."], ["It runs strangely in my head that it must have been some spirit, who, perhaps, might be sent to forewarn us: and who knows but all that matter which he told us, of his going to fight, and of his being taken prisoner, and of the great danger he was in of being hanged, might be intended as a warning to us, considering what we are going about?", "어쩌면 우리에게 미리 경고하러 보내진 혼령인지도 모르지요. 그자가 우리에게 들려준 그 모든 이야기, 싸우러 갔다는 것이며 포로로 잡혔다는 것이며 교수형에 처해질 큰 위험에 놓였다는 것이, 우리가 지금 하려는 일을 생각하면 우리에게 주는 경고로 의도된 것인지 누가 알겠습니까?"], ["besides, I dreamt of nothing all last night but of fighting; and methought the blood ran out of my nose, as liquor out of a tap.", "게다가 지난밤 내내 저는 싸우는 꿈만 꾸었습니다. 그것도 제 코에서 술통 꼭지에서 술이 쏟아지듯 피가 흘러나오는 꿈이었지요."], ["Indeed, sir, infandum, regina, jubes renovare dolorem.”", "정말이지요, 나리, 여왕이시여, 차마 입에 담지 못할 슬픔을 되살리라 명하시는군요.\""]], [["“Thy story, Partridge,”", "\"네 이야기는, 파트리지,\""], ["answered Jones, “is almost as ill applied as thy Latin.", "존스가 대답했다. \"네 라틴어만큼이나 영 어울리지 않게 갖다 붙인 거다."], ["Nothing can be more likely to happen than death to men who go into battle.", "전장에 나가는 사람에게 죽음보다 더 일어남 직한 일이 어디 있겠느냐. 어쩌면 우리 둘 다 거기서 쓰러질지도 모르지."], ["Perhaps we shall both fall in it--and what then?”", "그래서 어떻단 말이냐?\" \"그래서 어떻단 말이냐고요?\""], ["“What then?”", "파트리지가 받아쳤다."], ["replied Partridge; “why then there is an end of us, is there not?", "\"그야 그러면 우리는 그걸로 끝이지요, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["when I am gone, all is over with me.", "제가 가 버리면 저로서는 모든 게 끝장입니다."], ["What matters the cause to me, or who gets the victory, if I am killed?", "제가 죽고 나면 그 대의가 저에게 무슨 상관이며, 누가 승리를 거두든 저에게 무슨 상관이겠습니까?"], ["I shall never enjoy any advantage from it.", "저는 거기서 아무런 득도 누리지 못할 텐데요."], ["What are all the ringing of bells, and bonfires, to one that is six foot under ground?", "종을 울리고 모닥불을 피운들, 땅속 여섯 자 아래 묻힌 사람에게 그게 다 무슨 소용입니까? 가엾은 파트리지는 그걸로 끝장이지요.\""], ["there will be an end of poor Partridge.”", "\"가엾은 파트리지도 끝장이 나야겠지.\""], ["“And an end of poor Partridge,”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"언젠가 한 번은."], ["cries Jones, “there must be, one time or other.", "네가 라틴어를 그토록 좋아한다면, 호라티우스의 멋진 시구를 몇 줄 읊어 주마."], ["If you love Latin, I will repeat you some fine lines out of Horace, which would inspire courage into a coward.", "겁쟁이에게도 용기를 불어넣어 줄 시구다. 조국을 위해 죽는 것은 감미롭고 명예로운 일이라."]], [["`Dulce et decorum est pro patria mori Mors et fugacem persequitur virum Nec parcit imbellis juventae Poplitibus, timidoque tergo.'”", "죽음은 달아나는 자도 끝까지 쫓아가나니,\n전쟁을 두려워하는 젊은이의 오금도,"]], [["“I wish you would construe them,”", "겁먹고 돌아선 등짝도 봐주지 않는다.\" \"그걸 좀 풀어 주셨으면 합니다.\""], ["cries Partridge; “for Horace is a hard author, and I cannot understand as you repeat them.”", "파트리지가 외쳤다. \"호라티우스는 어려운 작가라, 나리께서 그렇게 읊으시기만 해서는 도무지 알아들을 수가 없거든요.\""]], [["“I will repeat you a bad imitation, or rather paraphrase, of my own,”", "\"내 솜씨로 서투르게 흉내 낸 것, 아니 풀어 옮긴 것을 읊어 주마.\""], ["said Jones; “for I am but an indifferent poet:", "존스가 말했다. \"나야 변변찮은 시인에 불과하니 말이다."]], [["`Who would not die in his dear country's cause?", "사랑하는 조국의 대의를 위해 죽기를 마다할 자 누구랴?"], ["Since, if base fear his dastard step withdraws, From death he cannot fly:--One common grave Receives, at last, the coward and the brave.'”", "비천한 두려움에 못난 발걸음을 물린다 한들, 죽음으로부터 달아나지는 못하리니. 마침내 하나의 무덤이 겁쟁이와 용사를 똑같이 거두어들이리라.\""]], [["“That's very certain,”", "\"그거야 아주 분명한 사실이지요.\""], ["cries Partridge.", "파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["“Ay, sure, Mors omnibus communis: but there is a great difference between dying in one's bed a great many years hence, like a good Christian, with all our friends crying about us, and being shot to-day or to-morrow, like a mad dog; or, perhaps, hacked in twenty pieces with the sword, and that too before we have repented of all our sins.", "\"암요, 그럼요, 죽음은 누구에게나 공평하지요. 하지만 지금부터 한참 세월이 흐른 뒤에 침대에 누워, 착한 기독교인답게, 온 벗들이 곁에서 울어 주는 가운데 죽는 것과, 오늘내일 미친개처럼 총에 맞아 죽거나, 어쩌면 칼에 스무 토막으로 난도질당해 죽는 것, 그것도 우리가 우리의 온갖 죄를 뉘우치기도 전에 그리되는 것 사이에는 엄청난 차이가 있습니다."], ["O Lord, have mercy upon us!", "오, 주여, 저희를 불쌍히 여기소서!"], ["to be sure the soldiers are a wicked kind of people.", "정말이지 군인이란 사악한 부류의 인간들입니다."], ["I never loved to have anything to do with them.", "저는 그자들과 무슨 상관이라도 얽히는 걸 도무지 좋아한 적이 없습니다."], ["I could hardly bring myself ever to look upon them as Christians.", "그자들을 기독교인이라 여기는 것조차 저로서는 좀처럼 어려운 일이었지요."], ["There is nothing but cursing and swearing among them.", "그자들 사이에는 욕설과 악담밖에 없으니까요. 나리께서 부디 뉘우치셨으면 합니다."], ["I wish your honour would repent: I heartily wish you would repent before it is too late; and not think of going among them.--Evil communication corrupts good manners.", "너무 늦기 전에 부디 뉘우치시고, 그자들 틈에 끼어들 생각일랑 마시기를 진심으로 바랍니다. 나쁜 사귐은 좋은 품행을 망치는 법이니까요."], ["That is my principal reason.", "그게 제 가장 큰 이유입니다."], ["For as for that matter, I am no more afraid than another man, not I; as to matter of that.", "그 점에 대해 말하자면, 저도 다른 사람만큼은 겁이 없습니다, 정말이지요. 그 점에 관해서는요."], ["I know all human flesh must die; but yet a man may live many years, for all that.", "인간의 육신이란 다 죽기 마련임을 저도 압니다. 하지만 그래도 사람이 여러 해를 살 수도 있는 것 아닙니까."], ["Why, I am a middle-aged man now, and yet I may live a great number of years.", "아니, 저도 이제 중년에 접어든 사람이지만, 그래도 앞으로 숱한 세월을 더 살 수 있을지도 모릅니다."], ["I have read of several who have lived to be above a hundred, and some a great deal above a hundred.", "저는 백 살을 넘기도록 산 사람들 이야기를 여럿 읽었고, 더러는 백 살을 훨씬 넘긴 이들도 있었지요. 그렇다고 제가 그런 나이까지 살기를 바란다거나, 저 자신에게 그러리라 장담한다는 건 아닙니다만."], ["Not that I hope, I mean that I promise myself, to live to any such age as that, neither.--But if it be only to eighty or ninety.", "하지만 여든이나 아흔까지만이라도 말이지요. 하늘이여 찬미받으소서, 그건 아직 까마득히 먼 일입니다."], ["Heaven be praised, that is a great ways off yet; and I am not afraid of dying then, no more than another man; but, surely, to tempt death before a man's time is come seems to me downright wickedness and presumption.", "그때 가서 죽는 거라면 저도 다른 사람 못지않게 두렵지 않습니다. 하지만 분명, 자기 때가 오기도 전에 죽음을 자초하는 것은 제가 보기에 그야말로 사악하고 주제넘은 짓입니다."], ["Besides, if it was to do any good indeed; but, let the cause be what it will, what mighty matter of good can two people do?", "게다가 그게 정말로 무슨 보탬이라도 된다면 또 모르지요. 하지만 대의가 무엇이든 간에, 사람 둘이 무슨 대단한 보탬이 될 수 있겠습니까?"], ["and, for my part, I understand nothing of it.", "그리고 저로 말하자면, 그런 일은 아무것도 모릅니다."], ["I never fired off a gun above ten times in my life; and then it was not charged with bullets.", "저는 평생 총을 열 번 넘게 쏴 본 적도 없고, 그나마도 총알을 잰 것은 아니었지요. 칼로 말하자면, 검술이라곤 배워 본 적도 없으니 그 방면은 전혀 모릅니다."], ["And for the sword, I never learned to fence, and know nothing of the matter.", "그리고 또 저 대포라는 게 있는데, 그 앞으로 뛰어드는 건 분명 더없이 주제넘은 짓이라 여길 만하지요. 미치광이가 아니고서야 누가 그런 짓을 하겠습니까."], ["And then there are those cannons, which certainly it must be thought the highest presumption to go in the way of; and nobody but a madman--I ask pardon; upon my soul I meant no harm; I beg I may not throw your honour into another passion.”", "용서하십시오, 맹세코 해를 끼칠 뜻은 없었습니다. 부디 제가 나리를 또다시 분노에 빠뜨리지 않기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Be under no apprehension, Partridge,”", "\"걱정 마라, 파트리지.\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones; “I am now so well convinced of thy cowardice, that thou couldst not provoke me on any account.”", "\"나는 이제 네 비겁함을 어찌나 확실히 알게 되었는지, 네가 무슨 짓을 해도 나를 화나게 할 수는 없을 것이다.\" \"나리,\""], ["“Your honour,”", "그가 대답했다."], ["answered he, “may call me coward, or anything else you please.", "\"저를 겁쟁이라 부르시든, 그 밖에 무엇이라 부르시든 마음대로 하십시오."], ["If loving to sleep in a whole skin makes a man a coward, non immunes ab illis malis sumus.", "성한 살가죽으로 잠자기를 좋아하는 것이 사람을 겁쟁이로 만든다면, 우리는 누구도 그 결점에서 자유롭지 못하지요."], ["I never read in my grammar that a man can't be a good man without fighting.", "저는 문법책 어디에서도 싸우지 않고서는 좋은 사람이 될 수 없다는 구절을 읽어 본 적이 없습니다."], ["Vir bonus est quis?", "누가 좋은 사람인가?"], ["Qui consulta patrum, qui leges juraque servat.", "원로원의 결의와 법과 정의를 지키는 사람이라."], ["Not a word of fighting; and I am sure the scripture is so much against it, that a man shall never persuade me he is a good Christian while he sheds Christian blood.”", "싸움에 대해서는 한마디도 없지 않습니까. 그리고 성경 또한 그것을 어찌나 반대하는지, 어떤 사람이 기독교인의 피를 흘리면서도 자기가 착한 기독교인이라고 저를 설득하려 든들, 저는 결코 넘어가지 않을 것입니다.\""]], [["The adventure of a beggar-man.", "거지를 만난 일."]], [["Just as Partridge had uttered that good and pious doctrine, with which the last chapter concluded, they arrived at another cross-way, when a lame fellow in rags asked them for alms; upon which Partridge gave him a severe rebuke, saying, “Every parish ought to keep their own poor.”", "파트리지가 앞 장을 맺은 그 선하고 경건한 가르침을 막 늘어놓았을 때, 그들은 또 다른 갈림길에 이르렀다. 그곳에서 누더기를 걸친 한 절름발이가 다가와 적선을 청했다."], ["Jones then fell a-laughing, and asked Partridge, “if he was not ashamed, with so much charity in his mouth, to have no charity in his heart.", "그러자 파트리지가 그를 호되게 나무라며 말했다. \"교구마다 제 가난한 사람은 제가 거두어야 마땅하지.\""], ["Your religion,”", "그 말에 존스는 웃음을 터뜨리며 파트리지에게 물었다."], ["says he, “serves you only for an excuse for your faults, but is no incentive to your virtue.", "\"입으로는 그토록 자선을 떠들면서, 정작 마음에는 자선이 없는 것이 부끄럽지 않은가. 자네의 신앙은,\""], ["Can any man who is really a Christian abstain from relieving one of his brethren in such a miserable condition?”", "그가 말했다. \"자네 허물의 핑곗거리로나 쓰일 뿐, 자네의 덕을 북돋우는 데는 아무 쓸모가 없군."], ["And at the same time, putting his hand in his pocket, he gave the poor object a shilling.", "정말로 그리스도인이라 하는 자라면, 이토록 비참한 처지에 놓인 제 형제 하나를 구제하는 일을 어찌 마다할 수 있겠나?\" 그러면서 동시에 호주머니에 손을 넣어, 그 가엾은 이에게 1실링을 건넸다."]], [["“Master,”", "\"나리,\""], ["cries the fellow, after thanking him, “I have a curious thing here in my pocket, which I found about two miles off, if your worship will please to buy it.", "사내가 고맙다고 인사한 뒤 외쳤다. \"제 호주머니에 진기한 물건이 하나 있습니다요."], ["I should not venture to pull it out to every one; but, as you are so good a gentleman, and so kind to the poor, you won't suspect a man of being a thief only because he is poor.”", "한 2마일쯤 떨어진 데서 주운 것인데, 나리께서 사 주실 마음이 있으시다면요. 아무한테나 꺼내 보일 물건은 아닙니다만, 나리께서 이리 훌륭하신 신사이시고, 가난한 사람에게도 이리 친절하시니, 그저 가난하다는 이유만으로 사람을 도둑으로 의심하지는 않으시겠지요.\""], ["He then pulled out a little gilt pocket-book, and delivered it into the hands of Jones.", "그러고는 작은 금박 수첩 하나를 꺼내어 존스의 손에 건넸다."]], [["Jones presently opened it, and (guess, reader, what he felt) saw in the first page the words Sophia Western, written by her own fair hand.", "존스는 곧바로 수첩을 펼쳤고, 첫 장에서 (독자여, 그가 어떤 심정이었을지 짐작해 보라) 소피아 웨스턴이라는 글자가, 바로 그녀의 고운 손으로 쓰인 것을 보았다. 그 이름을 읽자마자 그는 수첩을 입술에 바싹 갖다 댔다."], ["He no sooner read the name than he prest it close to his lips; nor could he avoid falling into some very frantic raptures, notwithstanding his company; but, perhaps, these very raptures made him forget he was not alone.", "일행이 곁에 있는데도 그는 자못 미친 듯한 황홀경에 빠져드는 것을 도무지 어쩌지 못했다. 하지만 어쩌면 그 황홀경 자체가, 자기가 혼자가 아니라는 사실을 잊게 만들었는지도 모른다."]], [["While Jones was kissing and mumbling the book, as if he had an excellent brown buttered crust in his mouth or as if he had really been a book-worm, or an author who had nothing to eat but his own works, a piece of paper fell from its leaves to the ground, which Partridge took up, and delivered to Jones, who presently perceived it to be a bank-bill.", "존스가 마치 입안에 노릇하게 구운 버터 바른 빵 껍질이라도 문 듯이, 혹은 정말로 책벌레라도 된 듯이, 아니면 제 작품 말고는 먹을 것이라곤 없는 작가라도 된 듯이 그 수첩에 입을 맞추고 우물거리는 동안, 종이 한 장이 책장 사이에서 땅으로 떨어졌다. 파트리지가 그것을 주워 존스에게 건넸고, 존스는 곧 그것이 은행권임을 알아보았다."], ["It was, indeed, the very bill which Western had given his daughter the night before her departure; and a Jew would have jumped to purchase it at five shillings less than £100.", "그것은 실로 웨스턴이 딸이 떠나기 전날 밤에 건넨 바로 그 어음이었다. 유대인이라면 100파운드에서 5실링 모자란 값에라도 냉큼 사들이려 덤볐을 물건이었다."]], [["The eyes of Partridge sparkled at this news, which Jones now proclaimed aloud; and so did (though with somewhat a different aspect) those of the poor fellow who had found the book; and who (I hope from a principle of honesty) had never opened it: but we should not deal honestly by the reader if we omitted to inform him of a circumstance which may be here a little material, viz.", "존스가 이제 큰 소리로 알린 이 소식에 파트리지의 눈이 반짝였다. 그리고 (다소 다른 빛깔이긴 했으나) 그 수첩을 주운 가엾은 사내의 눈도 반짝였다."], ["that the fellow could not read.", "그는 (정직함이라는 신조에서였기를 바라거니와) 수첩을 한 번도 펼쳐 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 여기서 다소 중요할 수 있는 한 가지 사정을 독자에게 알리지 않는다면 우리는 독자를 정직하게 대하는 것이 아닐 터이니, 곧 그 사내는 글을 읽을 줄 몰랐다는 사실이다."]], [["Jones, who had felt nothing but pure joy and transport from the finding the book, was affected with a mixture of concern at this new discovery; for his imagination instantly suggested to him that the owner of the bill might possibly want it before he should be able to convey it to her.", "수첩을 찾은 일에서 순전한 기쁨과 환희밖에 느끼지 못했던 존스는, 이 새로운 발견에 한 가닥 근심이 뒤섞이는 것을 느꼈다. 그 어음의 주인이, 자기가 그것을 그녀에게 전해 줄 수 있게 되기도 전에 어쩌면 그것이 필요해질지 모른다는 생각이 곧장 떠올랐기 때문이다."], ["He then acquainted the finder that he knew the lady to whom the book belonged, and would endeavour to find her out as soon as possible, and return it her.", "그래서 그는 수첩을 주운 사내에게, 이 수첩이 누구의 것인지 자기가 안다고, 되도록 빨리 그녀를 찾아내어 돌려주겠다고 일러 주었다."]], [["The pocket-book was a late present from Mrs Western to her niece; it had cost five-and-twenty shillings, having been bought of a celebrated toyman; but the real value of the silver which it contained in its clasp was about eighteen-pence; and that price the said toyman, as it was altogether as good as when it first issued from his shop, would now have given for it. A prudent person would, however, have taken proper advantage of the ignorance of this fellow, and would not have offered more than a shilling, or perhaps sixpence, for it; nay, some perhaps would have given nothing, and left the fellow to his action of trover, which some learned serjeants may doubt whether he could, under these circumstances, have maintained.", "그 수첩은 얼마 전 웨스턴 부인이 조카딸에게 선물한 것이었다. 이름난 장신구 상인에게서 산 것으로 값이 25실링이었으나, 그 죔쇠에 들어간 은의 실제 가치는 1실링 6펜스쯤이었다. 그 장신구 상인이라면, 수첩이 처음 가게에서 나갔을 때와 다름없이 멀쩡하니, 지금도 그 값에는 도로 사들였을 것이다. 그러나 신중한 사람이라면 이 사내의 무지를 적절히 이용하여, 1실링, 어쩌면 6펜스 이상은 내놓지 않았을 것이다. 아니, 더러는 한 푼도 주지 않고 사내에게 동산 횡령 소송이나 걸어 보라고 내버려 두었을지도 모른다. 그런 처지에서 그가 과연 그 소송을 유지할 수 있었을지는, 학식 있는 법관 가운데 일부는 의심할지도 모를 일이지만."]], [["Jones, on the contrary, whose character was on the outside of generosity, and may perhaps not very unjustly have been suspected of extravagance, without any hesitation gave a guinea in exchange for the book.", "이와 반대로, 천성이 너그러움의 도를 넘어서서 어쩌면 헤픔이 지나치다는 의심을 받아도 그리 억울하지 않을 존스는, 망설임 없이 그 수첩 값으로 1기니를 내주었다."], ["The poor man, who had not for a long time before been possessed of so much treasure, gave Mr Jones a thousand thanks, and discovered little less of transport in his muscles than Jones had before shown when he had first read the name of Sophia Western.", "오래도록 그만한 보물을 손에 쥐어 본 적이 없던 그 가엾은 사내는 존스에게 천 번이나 고맙다고 인사했으며, 앞서 존스가 소피아 웨스턴의 이름을 처음 읽었을 때 보였던 환희에 못지않은 기쁨을 제 얼굴 근육에 드러냈다."]], [["The fellow very readily agreed to attend our travellers to the place where he had found the pocket-book.", "사내는 수첩을 주운 그 자리까지 우리 여행자들을 안내하겠다고 선뜻 응했다."], ["Together, therefore, they proceeded directly thither; but not so fast as Mr Jones desired; for his guide unfortunately happened to be lame, and could not possibly travel faster than a mile an hour.", "그리하여 그들은 곧장 그리로 함께 나아갔으나, 존스가 바라는 만큼 빠르지는 못했다. 안내인이 공교롭게도 절름발이여서 도무지 한 시간에 1마일 이상은 걸을 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["As this place, therefore, was at above three miles' distance, though the fellow had said otherwise, the reader need not be acquainted how long they were in walking it.", "그 자리는 사내가 달리 말하긴 했어도 실은 3마일 넘게 떨어진 곳이었으니, 그곳까지 걸어가는 데 시간이 얼마나 걸렸을지는 독자에게 굳이 알릴 필요가 없겠다."]], [["Jones opened the book a hundred times during their walk, kissed it as often, talked much to himself, and very little to his companions.", "존스는 걷는 동안 수첩을 백 번도 더 펼쳤고, 그만큼 자주 입을 맞추었으며, 혼잣말은 많이 하면서 일행에게는 거의 말을 건네지 않았다."], ["At all which the guide exprest some signs of astonishment to Partridge; who more than once shook his head, and cryed, Poor gentleman!", "이 모든 광경에 안내인은 파트리지를 향해 놀라움의 기색을 내비쳤고, 파트리지는 몇 번이고 고개를 저으며 외쳤다. \"딱한 양반 같으니!"], ["orandum est ut sit mens sana in corpore sano.", "건강한 몸에 건강한 정신이 깃들기를 빌어야지.\""]], [["At length they arrived at the very spot where Sophia unhappily dropt the pocket-book, and where the fellow had as happily found it.", "마침내 그들은 소피아가 불행히도 수첩을 떨어뜨린, 그리고 사내가 다행히도 그것을 주운 바로 그 자리에 이르렀다."], ["Here Jones offered to take leave of his guide, and to improve his pace; but the fellow, in whom that violent surprize and joy which the first receipt of the guinea had occasioned was now considerably abated, and who had now had sufficient time to recollect himself, put on a discontented look, and, scratching his head, said, “He hoped his worship would give him something more.", "여기서 존스는 안내인과 작별하고 걸음을 재촉하려 했으나, 1기니를 처음 받았을 때 일었던 그 격렬한 놀라움과 기쁨이 이제 사뭇 가라앉고, 정신을 추스를 만한 시간을 충분히 가진 사내는 못마땅한 낯빛을 지으며 머리를 긁적이고는 말했다. \"나리께서 무언가 더 주셨으면 합니다요."], ["Your worship,”", "나리,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “will, I hope, take it into your consideration that if I had not been honest I might have kept the whole.”", "\"한번 헤아려 주십시오. 제가 정직하지 않았더라면, 통째로 챙겨 버릴 수도 있었을 겁니다요.\""], ["And, indeed, this the reader must confess to have been true.", "그리고 실로 이 말은 독자도 사실임을 인정하지 않을 수 없으리라. \"거기 그 종이가,\""], ["“If the paper there,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “be worth £100, I am sure the finding it deserves more than a guinea.", "\"100파운드짜리라면, 그걸 주운 값으로 1기니는 너무 약소하지요. 게다가 나리께서 그 아씨를 끝내 못 만나서 못 돌려주실 수도 있는 일이고요."], ["Besides, suppose your worship should never see the lady, nor give it her--and, though your worship looks and talks very much like a gentleman, yet I have only your worship's bare word; and, certainly, if the right owner ben't to be found, it all belongs to the first finder.", "나리께서 보기에도 말씀하시는 것도 영락없는 신사이시긴 합니다만, 그래도 제겐 나리의 말씀 한마디밖에 없는 셈이지요. 그리고 진짜 주인을 못 찾으면, 그건 다 처음 주운 사람 차지가 되는 게 분명합니다요."], ["I hope your worship will consider of all these matters: I am but a poor man, and therefore don't desire to have all; but it is but reasonable I should have my share.", "나리께서 이 모든 사정을 헤아려 주셨으면 합니다. 저야 한낱 가난뱅이라 다 갖겠다는 게 아니라, 제 몫만큼은 받는 게 당연하지 않겠습니까."], ["Your worship looks like a good man, and, I hope, will consider my honesty; for I might have kept every farthing, and nobody ever the wiser.”", "나리께서는 좋은 분으로 보이시니, 부디 제 정직함을 생각해 주십시오. 저는 한 푼까지 다 챙기고도 아무도 모르게 할 수 있었으니까요.\""], ["“I promise thee, upon my honour,”", "\"내 명예를 걸고 약속하건대,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “that I know the right owner, and will restore it her.”", "\"나는 진짜 주인을 알고 있고, 그녀에게 돌려줄 것이다.\" \"아닙니다요, 나리,\""], ["“Nay, your worship,”", "사내가 대답했다."], ["answered the fellow, “may do as you please as to that; if you will but give me my share, that is, one-half of the money, your honour may keep the rest yourself if you please;”", "\"그건 나리 좋으실 대로 하시지요. 그저 제 몫만 주시면, 그러니까 돈의 절반만 주시면, 나머지는 나리께서 가지셔도 좋습니다요.\""], ["and concluded with swearing, by a very vehement oath, “that he would never mention a syllable of it to any man living.”", "그러고는 자못 격렬한 맹세를 내뱉으며, \"살아 있는 어느 누구에게도 이 일에 대해 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않겠노라\"고 다짐하며 말을 맺었다."]], [["“Lookee, friend,” cries Jones, “the right owner shall certainly have again all that she lost; and as for any farther gratuity, I really cannot give it you at present; but let me know your name, and where you live, and it is more than possible you may hereafter have further reason to rejoice at this morning's adventure.”", "\"이보게, 친구,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"진짜 주인은 잃은 것을 틀림없이 모두 되찾을 걸세. 그리고 그 이상의 사례로 말하자면, 지금 당장은 정말 줄 수가 없네. 다만 자네 이름과 사는 곳을 일러 주게. 그러면 자네가 오늘 아침의 이 일을 두고 앞으로 더욱 기뻐할 까닭이 생길 가능성이 충분하다네.\""]], [["“I don't know what you mean by venture,” cries the fellow; “it seems I must venture whether you will return the lady her money or no; but I hope your worship will consider--” “Come, come,” said Partridge, “tell his honour your name, and where you may be found; I warrant you will never repent having put the money into his hands.” The fellow, seeing no hopes of recovering the possession of the pocket-book, at last complied in giving in his name and place of abode, which Jones writ upon a piece of paper with the pencil of Sophia; and then, placing the paper in the same page where she had writ her name, he cried out, “There, friend, you are the happiest man alive; I have joined your name to that of an angel.” “I don't know anything about angels,” answered the fellow; “but I wish you would give me a little more money, or else return me the pocket-book.” Partridge now waxed wrath: he called the poor cripple by several vile and opprobrious names, and was absolutely proceeding to beat him, but Jones would not suffer any such thing: and now, telling the fellow he would certainly find some opportunity of serving him, Mr Jones departed as fast as his heels would carry him; and Partridge, into whom the thoughts of the hundred pound had infused new spirits, followed his leader; while the man, who was obliged to stay behind, fell to cursing them both, as well as his parents; “for had they,” says he, “sent me to charity-school to learn to write and read and cast accounts, I should have known the value of these matters as well as other people.”", "\"무슨 일이 생긴다는 건지 저는 통 모르겠습니다요,\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"나리께서 그 아씨한테 돈을 돌려주시든 말든, 제 운수만 내맡기게 생겼군요. 그래도 나리께서 좀 헤아려 주셨으면—\" \"자자,\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"나리께 자네 이름하고 어디로 가면 찾을 수 있는지나 일러 드리게. 돈을 나리 손에 맡긴 걸 결코 후회하는 일은 없을 게야.\" 사내는 수첩을 도로 손에 넣을 가망이 없음을 알고는, 마침내 자기 이름과 사는 곳을 대었다. 존스는 그것을 소피아의 연필로 종이 한 장에 적은 뒤, 그 종이를 그녀가 제 이름을 적어 둔 바로 그 책장에 끼워 넣고는 외쳤다. \"자, 친구, 자네는 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람일세. 내가 자네 이름을 천사의 이름 곁에 나란히 적어 주었으니.\" \"천사니 뭐니 하는 건 저는 모르겠습니다요,\" 사내가 대답했다. \"그저 저는 나리께서 돈을 조금만 더 주시거나, 아니면 수첩을 도로 돌려주셨으면 합니다요.\" 그러자 파트리지가 발끈 성을 냈다. 그는 그 가엾은 절름발이를 갖은 상스럽고 욕된 이름으로 불러 대며, 영락없이 매질이라도 할 기세였으나, 존스가 그런 짓은 결코 용납하지 않았다. 그러고는 사내에게 자기가 반드시 돕게 될 기회를 찾겠노라 이르고서, 존스는 두 발이 닿는 한 빠르게 그곳을 떠났다. 100파운드 생각에 새 기운이 솟은 파트리지도 제 주인을 뒤따랐다. 한편 뒤에 남을 수밖에 없게 된 사내는 그 두 사람은 물론 제 부모까지 싸잡아 저주하기 시작했다. \"그 양반들이,\" 그가 말했다. \"나를 자선 학교에라도 보내 글 읽고 쓰고 셈하는 법을 배우게 했더라면, 나도 남들처럼 이런 물건의 값어치쯤은 알았을 텐데.\" * 동산 횡령 소송(action of trover): 남의 동산을 부당하게 점유한 자를 상대로 그 반환이나 손해배상을 구하던 옛 영국 소송."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["Containing more adventures which Mr Jones and his companion met on the road.", "존스 씨와 그의 일행이 길에서 겪은 더 많은 모험을 담은 장."]], [["Our travellers now walked so fast, that they had very little time or breath for conversation; Jones meditating all the way on Sophia, and Partridge on the bank-bill, which, though it gave him some pleasure, caused him at the same time to repine at fortune, which, in all his walks, had never given him such an opportunity of showing his honesty. They had proceeded above three miles, when Partridge, being unable any longer to keep up with Jones, called to him, and begged him a little to slacken his pace: with this he was the more ready to comply, as he had for some time lost the footsteps of the horses, which the thaw had enabled him to trace for several miles, and he was now upon a wide common, where were several roads.", "이제 우리 여행자들은 어찌나 빨리 걸었던지, 이야기를 나눌 틈도 숨도 거의 없었다. 존스는 내내 소피아를 떠올리며 걸었고, 파트리지는 그 은행권 어음을 생각하며 걸었다. 어음은 그에게 어느 정도 기쁨을 안겨 주면서도, 동시에 운명을 원망하게 만들었다. 그가 평생 걸어 다닌 그 모든 길에서, 운명은 단 한 번도 자신의 정직함을 보일 이런 기회를 준 적이 없었기 때문이다. 두 사람이 삼 마일 남짓 나아갔을 무렵, 더는 존스를 따라잡을 수 없게 된 파트리지가 그를 부르며 부디 걸음을 조금만 늦춰 달라고 사정했다. 존스로서는 그 청을 더욱 선뜻 들어줄 만했다. 얼마 전부터 말발굽 자국을 놓쳤기 때문인데, 눈이 녹은 덕분에 몇 마일이나 따라올 수 있었던 그 자국이 이제는 사라졌고, 그는 여러 갈래 길이 나 있는 너른 공유지 위에 서 있었다."]], [["He here therefore stopt to consider which of these roads he should pursue; when on a sudden they heard the noise of a drum, that seemed at no great distance.", "그래서 그는 이 길들 가운데 어느 쪽을 택해야 할지 헤아리려고 걸음을 멈추었다. 그때 갑자기 두 사람의 귀에 북소리가 들려왔는데, 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 나는 듯했다."], ["This sound presently alarmed the fears of Partridge, and he cried out, “Lord have mercy upon us all; they are certainly a coming!”", "이 소리는 곧장 파트리지의 두려움을 일깨웠고, 그는 외쳤다. \"주여, 우리 모두에게 자비를 베푸소서."], ["“Who is coming?”", "저들이 분명 오고 있습니다!\" \"누가 온단 말인가?\""], ["cries Jones; for fear had long since given place to softer ideas in his mind; and since his adventure with the lame man, he had been totally intent on pursuing Sophia, without entertaining one thought of an enemy.", "존스가 물었다. 그의 마음속에서는 이미 오래전에 두려움이 더 부드러운 생각들에게 자리를 내준 터였다."], ["“Who?”", "절름발이 사내와 겪은 일이 있은 뒤로, 그는 소피아를 뒤쫓는 데에만 온통 마음을 쏟았을 뿐, 적이라는 생각은 한 번도 품지 않았다."], ["cries Partridge, “why, the rebels: but why should I call them rebels?", "\"누구냐고요?\" 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["they may be very honest gentlemen, for anything I know to the contrary.", "\"아니, 반란군 말입니다. 한데 제가 왜 저들을 반란군이라 부르는 걸까요?"], ["The devil take him that affronts them, I say; I am sure, if they have nothing to say to me, I will have nothing to say to them, but in a civil way.", "어쩌면 아주 정직한 신사분들일지도 모르는데 말입니다, 제가 그렇지 않다고 단정할 근거도 없으니까요. 저들에게 모욕을 주는 자는 악마에게나 잡혀가라지요, 저는 그렇게 말하겠습니다."], ["For Heaven's sake, sir, don't affront them if they should come, and perhaps they may do us no harm; but would it not be the wiser way to creep into some of yonder bushes, till they are gone by?", "분명히 말씀드리지만, 저들이 제게 할 말이 없다면 저도 저들에게 할 말이 없습니다, 그저 점잖게 말고는요. 제발 부탁입니다, 나리, 저들이 정말 오거든 모욕을 주지 마십시오."], ["What can two unarmed men do perhaps against fifty thousand?", "그러면 어쩌면 우리에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않을 겁니다. 한데 저들이 다 지나갈 때까지 저쪽 덤불 속에 살그머니 숨어 있는 편이 더 현명하지 않겠습니까?"], ["Certainly nobody but a madman; I hope your honour is not offended; but certainly no man who hath mens sana in corpore sano----”", "무장도 안 한 두 사람이 어쩌면 오만 명을 상대로 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까? 미친 사람이 아니고서야 누가 그러겠습니까."], ["Here Jones interrupted this torrent of eloquence, which fear had inspired, saying, “That by the drum he perceived they were near some town.”", "나리께서 기분 상하지 않으셨으면 합니다만, 분명코 건강한 신체에 건전한 정신을 지닌 사람이라면 누구도——\" 여기서 존스가, 두려움이 불러일으킨 이 도도한 웅변의 물결을 가로막으며 말했다."], ["He then made directly towards the place whence the noise proceeded, bidding Partridge “take courage, for that he would lead him into no danger;”", "\"북소리로 보아 어느 마을 가까이에 온 것 같군.\" 그러고는 곧장 그 소리가 나는 쪽으로 향하면서 파트리지에게 \"용기를 내게, 위험한 곳으로 끌고 가지는 않을 테니\""], ["and adding, “it was impossible the rebels should be so near.”", "하고 일렀고, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"반란군이 이렇게 가까이 있을 리가 없네.\""]], [["Partridge was a little comforted with this last assurance; and though he would more gladly have gone the contrary way, he followed his leader, his heart beating time, but not after the manner of heroes, to the music of the drum, which ceased not till they had traversed the common, and were come into a narrow lane.", "파트리지는 이 마지막 다짐에 조금 마음을 놓았다. 그래도 반대편으로 가는 편이 훨씬 더 좋았겠지만, 그는 가슴을 두근거리며 자기 길잡이를 따라갔다. 다만 그 두근거림은 영웅들이 북의 음악에 맞추어 뛰는 식의 것은 아니었다. 북소리는 두 사람이 공유지를 가로질러 좁은 샛길로 접어들 때까지 그치지 않았다."]], [["And now Partridge, who kept even pace with Jones, discovered something painted flying in the air, a very few yards before him, which fancying to be the colours of the enemy, he fell a bellowing, “Oh Lord, sir, here they are; there is the crown and coffin. Oh Lord! I never saw anything so terrible; and we are within gun-shot of them already.”", "그리고 이제, 존스와 보조를 나란히 맞추어 가던 파트리지가 자기 앞 불과 몇 야드 거리에서 무언가 색칠된 것이 공중에 펄럭이는 것을 발견했다. 그것을 적의 군기라 여긴 그는 고래고래 소리치기 시작했다. \"오 주여, 나리, 저기 저들이 있습니다. 저기 왕관과 관이 보입니다. 오 주여! 저토록 끔찍한 것은 난생처음 봅니다. 게다가 우리는 이미 저들의 총 사정거리 안에 들어와 있습니다.\""]], [["Jones no sooner looked up, than he plainly perceived what it was which Partridge had thus mistaken.", "존스가 눈을 들어 보자마자, 파트리지가 그토록 잘못 본 것이 무엇인지 또렷이 알아차렸다. \"파트리지,\""], ["“Partridge,”", "그가 말했다. \"자네 혼자서도 이 군대 전체를 너끈히 상대할 수 있겠군."], ["says he, “I fancy you will be able to engage this whole army yourself; for by the colours I guess what the drum was which we heard before, and which beats up for recruits to a puppet-show.”", "저 군기를 보니, 아까 우리가 들은 북소리가 무엇이었는지 짐작이 가네. 저건 인형극에 사람을 끌어모으려고 두드리는 북소리일세.\""]], [["“A puppet-show!”", "\"인형극이라고요!\""], ["answered Partridge, with most eager transport.", "파트리지가 더없이 들뜬 환희에 차서 대답했다."], ["“And is it really no more than that?", "\"정말로 그것뿐이란 말입니까?"], ["I love a puppet-show of all the pastimes upon earth.", "저는 이 세상 모든 소일거리 가운데서도 인형극을 제일 좋아합니다."], ["Do, good sir, let us tarry and see it.", "부디, 나리, 잠시 머물러 그것을 봅시다."], ["Besides, I am quite famished to death; for it is now almost dark, and I have not eat a morsel since three o'clock in the morning.”", "게다가 저는 배가 고파 죽을 지경입니다. 이제 거의 어두워졌는데, 새벽 세 시 이후로 한 입도 먹지 못했으니까요.\""]], [["They now arrived at an inn, or indeed an ale-house, where Jones was prevailed upon to stop, the rather as he had no longer any assurance of being in the road he desired. They walked both directly into the kitchen, where Jones began to enquire if no ladies had passed that way in the morning, and Partridge as eagerly examined into the state of their provisions; and indeed his enquiry met with the better success; for Jones could not hear news of Sophia; but Partridge, to his great satisfaction, found good reason to expect very shortly the agreeable sight of an excellent smoaking dish of eggs and bacon.", "이윽고 두 사람은 어느 여관에, 아니 실은 술집에 다다랐고, 존스는 이곳에 멈추자는 설득에 넘어갔다. 더구나 그는 자기가 바라던 길에 들어서 있다는 확신마저 더는 없던 터였다. 두 사람은 곧장 부엌으로 걸어 들어갔고, 거기서 존스는 아침에 이쪽 길로 지나간 부인네는 없었느냐고 묻기 시작했으며, 파트리지는 그에 못지않게 열심히 먹을거리의 형편을 살폈다. 그리고 사실 더 좋은 성과를 거둔 쪽은 파트리지의 탐문이었다. 존스는 소피아의 소식을 들을 수 없었지만, 파트리지는 매우 만족스럽게도 머지않아 김이 모락모락 나는 훌륭한 달걀과 베이컨 요리라는 반가운 광경을 보게 되리라 기대할 만한 충분한 근거를 찾아냈기 때문이다."]], [["In strong and healthy constitutions love hath a very different effect from what it causes in the puny part of the species.", "튼튼하고 건강한 체질에서는, 사랑이 이 종족 가운데 허약한 부류에게 일으키는 것과는 사뭇 다른 효과를 낸다. 허약한 쪽에서는, 사랑이 대개 개체의 보존으로 향하는 모든 식욕을 무너뜨린다."], ["In the latter it generally destroys all that appetite which tends towards the conservation of the individual; but in the former, though it often induces forgetfulness, and a neglect of food, as well as of everything else; yet place a good piece of well-powdered buttock before a hungry lover, and he seldom fails very handsomely to play his part.", "그러나 튼튼한 쪽에서는, 비록 사랑이 음식은 물론 다른 모든 것까지 잊고 소홀히 하게 만드는 일이 잦긴 해도, 배고픈 연인 앞에 밀가루를 잘 뿌린 좋은 살코기 한 점을 놓아 보라, 그는 좀처럼 자기 몫을 멋들어지게 해내지 않는 법이 없다. 지금 이 경우가 바로 그러했다."], ["Thus it happened in the present case; for though Jones perhaps wanted a prompter, and might have travelled much farther, had he been alone, with an empty stomach; yet no sooner did he sit down to the bacon and eggs, than he fell to as heartily and voraciously as Partridge himself.", "존스는 어쩌면 누가 곁에서 부추겨 줄 필요가 있었는지도 모르고, 혼자였다면 빈속으로 훨씬 더 멀리까지 갔을지도 모른다. 그러나 베이컨과 달걀 앞에 앉자마자, 그는 파트리지 못지않게 왕성하고 게걸스럽게 먹어치우기 시작했다."]], [["Before our travellers had finished their dinner, night came on, and as the moon was now past the full, it was extremely dark.", "우리 여행자들이 저녁 식사를 끝내기도 전에 밤이 찾아왔고, 달은 이미 보름을 지난 터라 사위가 몹시 어두웠다."], ["Partridge therefore prevailed on Jones to stay and see the puppet-show, which was just going to begin, and to which they were very eagerly invited by the master of the said show, who declared that his figures were the finest which the world had ever produced, and that they had given great satisfaction to all the quality in every town in England.", "그리하여 파트리지는 막 시작되려는 인형극을 보고 가자고 존스를 설득했다. 그 인형극의 주인은 두 사람을 무척 열심히 불러들였는데, 자기 인형들이야말로 세상이 여태껏 만들어낸 것 중에 가장 빼어난 것이며, 잉글랜드의 어느 마을에서든 모든 지체 높은 분들께 큰 만족을 드렸노라고 큰소리쳤다."]], [["The puppet-show was performed with great regularity and decency.", "인형극은 자못 질서 정연하고 단정하게 펼쳐졌다."], ["It was called the fine and serious part of the Provoked Husband; and it was indeed a very grave and solemn entertainment, without any low wit or humour, or jests; or, to do it no more than justice, without anything which could provoke a laugh.", "그것은 《화난 남편》의 우아하고 진지한 대목이라 불리는 것이었는데, 과연 저속한 재치도 해학도 농담도 전혀 없는, 매우 점잖고 엄숙한 구경거리였다. 아니, 공정을 기하여 말하자면, 웃음을 자아낼 만한 것이라곤 아무것도 없는 구경거리였다."], ["The audience were all highly pleased.", "관객은 모두 크게 흡족해했다."], ["A grave matron told the master she would bring her two daughters the next night, as he did not show any stuff; and an attorney's clerk and an exciseman both declared, that the characters of Lord and Lady Townley were well preserved, and highly in nature.", "어느 점잖은 부인은 주인에게, 당신이 천박한 것은 하나도 보여 주지 않으니 다음 날 밤에는 두 딸을 데려오겠노라고 말했다. 그리고 어느 변호사 서기와 어느 세무 관리는, 타운리 경과 타운리 부인의 인물됨이 잘 살아 있고 매우 자연스럽게 그려졌다고 입을 모았다."], ["Partridge likewise concurred with this opinion.", "파트리지 또한 이 의견에 동의했다."]], [["The master was so highly elated with these encomiums, that he could not refrain from adding some more of his own. He said, “The present age was not improved in anything so much as in their puppet-shows; which, by throwing out Punch and his wife Joan, and such idle trumpery, were at last brought to be a rational entertainment. I remember,” said he, “when I first took to the business, there was a great deal of low stuff that did very well to make folks laugh; but was never calculated to improve the morals of young people, which certainly ought to be principally aimed at in every puppet-show: for why may not good and instructive lessons be conveyed this way, as well as any other? My figures are as big as the life, and they represent the life in every particular; and I question not but people rise from my little drama as much improved as they do from the great.” “I would by no means degrade the ingenuity of your profession,” answered Jones, “but I should have been glad to have seen my old acquaintance master Punch, for all that; and so far from improving, I think, by leaving out him and his merry wife Joan, you have spoiled your puppet-show.”", "주인은 이 찬사들에 어찌나 의기양양해졌던지, 거기에 제 말까지 몇 마디 보태지 않고는 배기지 못했다. 그는 말했다. \"요즘 세상이 인형극만큼 크게 발전한 것이 없습니다. 펀치며 그의 아내 조앤이며 그런 시시껄렁한 것들을 내던져 버린 끝에, 마침내 인형극이 이성적인 구경거리가 되었거든요. 제가 기억하기로는,\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 처음 이 일에 발을 들였을 무렵에는, 사람들을 웃기기에 딱 좋은 저속한 것들이 잔뜩 있었지요. 하지만 그런 것은 결코 젊은이들의 품행을 바로잡는 데에 보탬이 되도록 짜인 적이 없었습니다. 품행을 바로잡는 일이야말로 어느 인형극에서나 마땅히 가장 으뜸으로 삼아야 할 목표인데도 말입니다. 어찌하여 선하고 교훈이 되는 가르침을 다른 어떤 방식 못지않게 이런 방식으로도 전할 수 없겠습니까? 제 인형들은 실물만큼 크고, 모든 세세한 데까지 인생을 고스란히 그려냅니다. 그러니 사람들이 제 작은 연극에서 일어설 때면, 저 거창한 연극에서 그러하듯 제법 품행이 나아져 일어선다고 저는 믿어 의심치 않습니다.\" \"당신 직업의 재간을 결코 깎아내릴 생각은 없소만,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"그래도 나는 옛 친구 펀치 선생을 한 번 봤으면 좋았겠소. 그리고 내 생각엔, 그와 그의 명랑한 아내 조앤을 빼버림으로써 인형극이 나아지기는커녕 도리어 망쳐 놓은 것 같소.\""]], [["The dancer of wires conceived an immediate and high contempt for Jones, from these words. And with much disdain in his countenance, he replied, “Very probably, sir, that may be your opinion; but I have the satisfaction to know the best judges differ from you, and it is impossible to please every taste. I confess, indeed, some of the quality at Bath, two or three years ago, wanted mightily to bring Punch again upon the stage. I believe I lost some money for not agreeing to it; but let others do as they will; a little matter shall never bribe me to degrade my own profession, nor will I ever willingly consent to the spoiling the decency and regularity of my stage, by introducing any such low stuff upon it.”", "인형 줄을 놀리던 사내는 이 말을 듣자 존스를 향해 대뜸 깊은 경멸을 품었다. 그러고는 얼굴 가득 멸시를 띤 채 대꾸했다. \"그게 손님 생각일 수야 있겠지요. 하지만 저는 가장 안목 높은 분들이 손님과 다르게 보신다는 걸 알고 있어 흐뭇할 따름입니다. 어차피 모든 입맛을 다 맞추기란 불가능한 노릇이지요. 솔직히 고백하건대, 두세 해 전에 바스의 지체 높은 분들 가운데 더러는 펀치를 다시 무대에 올리기를 무척이나 바라셨습니다. 그분들 뜻에 따르지 않은 탓에 돈을 좀 잃었을 겁니다. 하지만 남들이야 제멋대로 하라지요. 하찮은 이문 따위가 저를 매수해 제 직업을 욕보이게 하는 일은 결코 없을 것이며, 그런 저속한 것을 끌어들여 제 무대의 단정함과 질서를 망치는 데에 제 발로 동의하는 일도 결코 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Right, friend,”", "\"옳소, 친구,\""], ["cries the clerk, “you are very right.", "서기가 외쳤다. \"참으로 지당한 말이오."], ["Always avoid what is low.", "저속한 것은 언제나 피해야 하는 법이지요."], ["There are several of my acquaintance in London, who are resolved to drive everything which is low from the stage.”", "런던에 내가 아는 사람들 가운데 여럿이, 저속한 것은 무엇이든 무대에서 몰아내기로 단단히 작정하고 있다오.\""], ["“Nothing can be more proper,”", "\"그보다 더 합당한 일은 없지요.\""], ["cries the exciseman, pulling his pipe from his mouth.", "세무 관리가 입에서 파이프를 빼며 외쳤다. \"내가 기억하기로는,\""], ["“I remember,”", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["added he, “(for I then lived with my lord) I was in the footman's gallery, the night when this play of the Provoked Husband was acted first.", "\"(그때 나는 우리 나리 댁에 살고 있었거든) 이 《화난 남편》이라는 연극이 처음 무대에 오른 날 밤, 나는 하인석 관람석에 있었소. 거기엔 의회 의원에 출마하려고 시골 신사 하나가 도시로 올라오는 이야기를 두고 저속한 것이 잔뜩 들어 있었지요."], ["There was a great deal of low stuff in it about a country gentleman come up to town to stand for parliament-man; and there they brought a parcel of his servants upon the stage, his coachman I remember particularly; but the gentlemen in our gallery could not bear anything so low, and they damned it.", "그자의 하인들을 한 무리나 무대에 끌어내었는데, 특히 그의 마부가 기억나는구려. 한데 우리 관람석의 신사들은 그토록 저속한 것을 견디지 못해 그 대목에 야유를 퍼부었다오."], ["I observe, friend, you have left all that matter out, and you are to be commended for it.”", "보아하니, 친구, 당신은 그 모든 대목을 다 빼버렸구려. 그건 칭찬받을 만한 일이오.\""]], [["“Nay, gentlemen,”", "\"아니오, 신사 여러분,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “I can never maintain my opinion against so many; indeed, if the generality of his audience dislike him, the learned gentleman who conducts the show might have done very right in dismissing Punch from his service.”", "\"이렇게 많은 분을 상대로 내 의견을 끝까지 우길 수야 없지요. 정말이지, 관객 대다수가 그를 싫어한다면, 이 극을 이끄시는 학식 높은 신사분께서 펀치를 그 직무에서 내치신 것은 매우 잘하신 일일 수도 있겠소.\""]], [["The master of the show then began a second harangue, and said much of the great force of example, and how much the inferior part of mankind would be deterred from vice, by observing how odious it was in their superiors; when he was unluckily interrupted by an incident, which, though perhaps we might have omitted it at another time, we cannot help relating at present, but not in this chapter.", "그러자 인형극 주인은 두 번째 일장 연설을 시작하여, 본보기가 지닌 큰 힘에 관해, 그리고 못난 부류의 사람들이 윗사람들에게서 악덕이 얼마나 보기 흉한 것인지를 지켜봄으로써 얼마나 악덕을 멀리하게 되는지에 관해 장황하게 늘어놓았다. 그런데 그때 그는 어떤 사건에 그만 운수 사납게 가로막히고 말았다. 그 사건은 다른 때라면 어쩌면 우리가 생략했을지도 모르나, 지금으로서는 이야기하지 않을 수 없다. 다만 이 장에서는 아니다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["From which it may be inferred that the best things are liable to be misunderstood and misinterpreted.", "6장. 이로써 가장 좋은 것들조차 오해받고 곡해되기 쉽다는 사실을 헤아릴 수 있다."]], [["A violent uproar now arose in the entry, where my landlady was well cuffing her maid both with her fist and tongue.", "이제 복도에서 한바탕 거센 소동이 일었다. 안주인이 하녀를 주먹과 입으로 한꺼번에 호되게 두들겨 패고 있었던 것이다."], ["She had indeed missed the wench from her employment, and, after a little search, had found her on the puppet-show stage in company with the Merry Andrew, and in a situation not very proper to be described.", "그녀는 정말로 그 계집아이가 일터에서 사라진 것을 알아챘고, 잠시 찾아 헤맨 끝에 인형극 무대 위에서 어릿광대와 함께, 그리 점잖게 묘사하기 어려운 자세로 있는 것을 발견했다."]], [["Though Grace (for that was her name) had forfeited all title to modesty; yet had she not impudence enough to deny a fact in which she was actually surprized; she, therefore, took another turn, and attempted to mitigate the offence.", "그레이스(이것이 그 아이의 이름이었다)는 정숙함을 주장할 자격을 모조리 잃은 처지였으나, 그렇다고 막상 들킨 사실을 뻔뻔하게 부인할 만큼의 낯짝까지 갖추지는 못했다. 그래서 아이는 방향을 틀어, 그 잘못을 누그러뜨려 보려 했다."], ["“Why do you beat me in this manner, mistress?”", "\"마님, 어째서 저를 이리 때리세요?\""], ["cries the wench.", "계집아이가 외쳤다."], ["“If you don't like my doings, you may turn me away. If I am a w--e”", "\"제가 하는 짓이 마음에 안 드시면, 그냥 내쫓으시면 되잖아요."], ["(for the other had liberally bestowed that appellation on her), “my betters are so as well as I.", "제가 화냥년이라면\"(안주인이 그 호칭을 아이에게 아낌없이 퍼부었던 것이다), \"저보다 잘난 분들도 저랑 매한가지지요."], ["What was the fine lady in the puppet-show just now?", "방금 인형극에 나온 그 귀하신 마님은 뭐였답니까?"], ["I suppose she did not lie all night out from her husband for nothing.”", "그분이 밤새 서방님을 떠나 밖에서 잔 게 괜히 그런 건 아니었겠지요.\""]], [["The landlady now burst into the kitchen, and fell foul on both her husband and the poor puppet-mover.", "이제 안주인이 부엌으로 들이닥쳐, 제 남편과 그 가엾은 인형 부리는 사내에게 한꺼번에 화살을 퍼부었다. \"여보,\""], ["“Here, husband,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"이런 자들을 집에 들이면 어찌 되는지 보라고요."], ["says she, “you see the consequence of harbouring these people in your house.", "저들 덕분에 술이 좀 더 팔린다 한들, 저들이 어질러 놓는 꼴에 비하면 본전도 못 찾아요. 게다가 저런 이투성이 해충 같은 것들 때문에 우리 집이 갈봇집 꼴이 되다니."], ["If one doth draw a little drink the more for them, one is hardly made amends for the litter they make; and then to have one's house made a bawdy-house of by such lousy vermin.", "한마디로, 내일 아침에는 떠나 주셨으면 해요. 더는 이런 짓거리를 봐줄 수 없으니까요."], ["In short, I desire you would be gone to-morrow morning; for I will tolerate no more such doings.", "이런 건 그저 하인들에게 게으름과 헛소리나 가르치는 길일 뿐이에요. 저런 허튼 구경거리에서 무슨 더 나은 걸 배우겠어요."], ["It is only the way to teach our servants idleness and nonsense; for to be sure nothing better can be learned by such idle shows as these.", "인형극이 옛날에 입다의 경솔한 맹세 같은, 그런 좋은 성경 이야기로 만들어지던 시절이 생각나네요. 못된 사람들이 마귀에게 끌려가던 그런 이야기들 말이에요."], ["I remember when puppet-shows were made of good scripture stories, as Jephthah's Rash Vow, and such good things, and when wicked people were carried away by the devil.", "그런 데는 그래도 무슨 뜻이라도 있었지요. 하지만 지난 일요일에 목사님이 말씀하셨듯, 요즘은 아무도 마귀 같은 건 믿지 않아요."], ["There was some sense in those matters; but as the parson told us last Sunday, nobody believes in the devil now-a-days; and here you bring about a parcel of puppets drest up like lords and ladies, only to turn the heads of poor country wenches; and when their heads are once turned topsy-turvy, no wonder everything else is so.”", "그런데 당신은 고작 가엾은 시골 계집애들 머리나 돌려놓으려고, 귀족 나리니 마님이니처럼 차려입힌 인형 떼거리를 끌고 다니는 거예요. 한번 그 머리가 거꾸로 뒤집히고 나면, 나머지도 죄다 그 꼴이 되는 게 당연하지요.\""]], [["Virgil, I think, tells us, that when the mob are assembled in a riotous and tumultuous manner, and all sorts of missile weapons fly about, if a man of gravity and authority appears amongst them, the tumult is presently appeased, and the mob, which when collected into one body, may be well compared to an ass, erect their long ears at the grave man's discourse.", "내 기억으로는 베르길리우스가 이런 이야기를 들려준다. 군중이 난동을 부리며 시끌벅적하게 몰려들어 온갖 던질 무기가 사방으로 날아다닐 때, 위엄과 권위를 갖춘 사람이 그들 사이에 나타나면 소란은 이내 가라앉고, 한 덩어리로 뭉치면 영락없이 나귀에 견줄 만한 그 군중이, 위엄 있는 사람의 말 앞에서 긴 귀를 쫑긋 세운다는 것이다."]], [["On the contrary, when a set of grave men and philosophers are disputing; when wisdom herself may in a manner be considered as present, and administering arguments to the disputants; should a tumult arise among the mob, or should one scold, who is herself equal in noise to a mighty mob, appear among the said philosophers; their disputes cease in a moment, wisdom no longer performs her ministerial office, and the attention of every one is immediately attracted by the scold alone.", "반대로, 한 무리의 위엄 있는 사람들과 철학자들이 논쟁을 벌이고 있을 때, 말하자면 지혜의 여신 그 자신이 자리에 함께하여 논객들에게 논거를 대 준다고 여길 만한 그런 때에, 군중 사이에서 소란이 일거나, 혹은 그 자체로 거대한 군중에 맞먹는 소음을 내는 바가지 긁는 여인 하나가 그 철학자들 틈에 나타난다면, 그들의 논쟁은 순식간에 멎고, 지혜는 더 이상 제 직무를 수행하지 못하며, 모든 이의 주의는 즉시 그 바가지 긁는 여인 하나에게만 쏠려 버린다."]], [["Thus the uproar aforesaid, and the arrival of the landlady, silenced the master of the puppet-show, and put a speedy and final end to that grave and solemn harangue, of which we have given the reader a sufficient taste already.", "이렇듯 앞서 말한 소동과 안주인의 등장은 인형극 주인을 잠잠하게 만들었고, 이미 독자에게 충분히 맛보여 드린 바 있는 그 위엄 있고 엄숙한 일장 연설에 빠르고도 결정적인 종지부를 찍어 버렸다."], ["Nothing indeed could have happened so very inopportune as this accident; the most wanton malice of fortune could not have contrived such another stratagem to confound the poor fellow, while he was so triumphantly descanting on the good morals inculcated by his exhibitions.", "실로 이 사고만큼 더없이 때를 못 맞춘 일도 없었을 것이다. 운명의 가장 짓궂은 악의라 한들, 그 가엾은 사내가 제 공연이 심어 주는 좋은 도덕에 대해 그토록 의기양양하게 늘어놓던 바로 그 순간에 그를 당황케 할 또 다른 술책을 꾸며 낼 수는 없었으리라."], ["His mouth was now as effectually stopt, as that of quack must be, if, in the midst of a declamation on the great virtues of his pills and powders, the corpse of one of his martyrs should be brought forth, and deposited before the stage, as a testimony of his skill.", "이제 그의 입은, 마치 돌팔이 의사가 제 알약과 가루약의 위대한 효능을 일장 연설로 떠벌리던 한복판에, 그 솜씨의 증거랍시고 자기 손에 죽은 희생자의 시신이 들려 나와 무대 앞에 놓일 때 그 입이 막혀 버리는 것만큼이나 확실하게 봉해졌다."]], [["Instead, therefore, of answering my landlady, the puppet-show man ran out to punish his Merry Andrew; and now the moon beginning to put forth her silver light, as the poets call it (though she looked at that time more like a piece of copper), Jones called for his reckoning, and ordered Partridge, whom my landlady had just awaked from a profound nap, to prepare for his journey; but Partridge, having lately carried two points, as my reader hath seen before, was emboldened to attempt a third, which was to prevail with Jones to take up a lodging that evening in the house where he then was. He introduced this with an affected surprize at the intention which Mr Jones declared of removing; and, after urging many excellent arguments against it, he at last insisted strongly that it could be to no manner of purpose whatever; for that, unless Jones knew which way the lady was gone, every step he took might very possibly lead him the farther from her; “for you find, sir,” said he, “by all the people in the house, that she is not gone this way. How much better, therefore, would it be to stay till the morning, when we may expect to meet with somebody to enquire of?”", "그래서 인형극 사내는 안주인에게 대꾸하는 대신, 제 어릿광대를 혼내 주러 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 이제 시인들이 이르는 대로 달이 은빛을 내뿜기 시작했는데(하기야 그때 달은 은보다 구릿빛 한 조각에 더 가까워 보였지만), 존스는 셈을 청한 뒤, 안주인이 막 깊은 낮잠에서 깨워 놓은 파트리지에게 여행을 채비하라 일렀다. 그러나 파트리지는 독자께서 앞서 보셨듯 최근 두 가지를 관철해 낸 터라, 대담해진 김에 세 번째까지 시도해 보려 했다. 그것은 곧 그날 저녁을 지금 머무는 이 집에서 묵도록 존스를 설득하는 일이었다. 그는 먼저 존스 씨가 자리를 옮기겠다고 밝힌 데 대해 짐짓 놀란 척하며 운을 떼고는, 거기에 반대하는 훌륭한 논거를 여럿 늘어놓은 끝에, 마침내 그것이 어떤 목적에도 도무지 닿을 수 없는 일이라고 강하게 주장했다. 존스가 그 부인이 어느 길로 갔는지 알지 못하는 한, 한 발짝 내디딜 때마다 그녀에게서 도리어 더 멀어지게 될지도 모른다는 것이었다. \"나리, 보십시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 집의 누구에게 물어봐도 그분이 이쪽으로 가지 않으셨다는 걸 알 수 있잖습니까. 그러니 날이 밝을 때까지 머무는 편이 얼마나 더 낫겠습니까. 그때라면 물어볼 만한 누군가를 만나리라 기대할 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["This last argument had indeed some effect on Jones, and while he was weighing it the landlord threw all the rhetoric of which he was master into the same scale.", "이 마지막 논거는 정말로 존스에게 얼마쯤 효력이 있었다. 그가 그것을 저울질하고 있는 동안, 주인장이 제가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 수사를 같은 저울판에 보태었다."], ["“Sure, sir,”", "\"암요, 나리,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “your servant gives you most excellent advice; for who would travel by night at this time of the year?”", "\"하인분께서 더없이 훌륭한 조언을 드리는 겁니다. 일 년 중 이런 철에 누가 밤길을 다니겠습니까?\""], ["He then began in the usual stile to trumpet forth the excellent accommodation which his house afforded; and my landlady likewise opened on the occasion----But, not to detain the reader with what is common to every host and hostess, it is sufficient to tell him Jones was at last prevailed on to stay and refresh himself with a few hours'", "그러고는 으레 하는 투로, 제 집이 제공하는 그 훌륭한 잠자리를 나팔 불듯 떠벌리기 시작했고, 안주인 또한 이때다 싶어 입을 열었다. 그러나 어느 집주인이든 안주인이든 다 하는 흔한 소리로 독자를 붙잡아 둘 것까지는 없으니, 결국 존스가 설득에 못 이겨 머무르며 몇 시간의 휴식으로 몸을 풀게 되었다는 것만 일러두면 충분하리라."], ["rest, which indeed he very much wanted; for he had hardly shut his eyes since he had left the inn where the accident of the broken head had happened.", "사실 그에게는 그 휴식이 무척이나 절실했다. 머리가 깨지는 사고가 일어났던 그 여관을 떠난 이후로 거의 눈을 붙이지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["As soon as Jones had taken a resolution to proceed no farther that night, he presently retired to rest, with his two bedfellows, the pocket-book and the muff; but Partridge, who at several times had refreshed himself with several naps, was more inclined to eating than to sleeping, and more to drinking than to either.", "존스는 그날 밤 더 나아가지 않기로 마음을 정하자마자, 곧 두 동침자, 곧 수첩과 토시를 데리고 잠자리에 들었다. 그러나 파트리지는 여러 차례 토막잠으로 몸을 추슬러 온 터라, 잠보다는 먹는 쪽에, 그리고 그 둘 어느 쪽보다도 마시는 쪽에 더 마음이 기울어 있었다."]], [["And now the storm which Grace had raised being at an end, and my landlady being again reconciled to the puppet-man, who on his side forgave the indecent reflections which the good woman in her passion had cast on his performances, a face of perfect peace and tranquillity reigned in the kitchen; where sat assembled round the fire the landlord and landlady of the house, the master of the puppet-show, the attorney's clerk, the exciseman, and the ingenious Mr Partridge; in which company past the agreeable conversation which will be found in the next chapter.", "이제 그레이스가 일으킨 풍파도 가라앉고, 안주인도 인형 부리는 사내와 다시 화해했으며, 그 사내 또한 그 선량한 여인이 화김에 제 공연에 퍼부은 무례한 험담을 용서해 준 터라, 부엌에는 더없이 평화롭고 고요한 기운이 감돌았다. 그곳 난롯가에는 이 집의 주인 내외, 인형극 주인, 변호사 사무원, 소비세 징수원, 그리고 재간둥이 파트리지 씨가 빙 둘러앉아 있었으니, 다음 장에서 보게 될 그 유쾌한 담소가 이 사람들 사이에서 오갔다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["Containing a remark or two of our own and many more of the good company assembled in the kitchen.", "우리 자신의 소견 한두 가지와, 부엌에 모여 앉은 점잖은 무리의 더 많은 소견을 담고 있음."]], [["Though the pride of Partridge did not submit to acknowledge himself a servant, yet he condescended in most particulars to imitate the manners of that rank. One instance of this was, his greatly magnifying the fortune of his companion, as he called Jones: such is a general custom with all servants among strangers, as none of them would willingly be thought the attendant on a beggar: for, the higher the situation of the master is, the higher consequently is that of the man in his own opinion; the truth of which observation appears from the behaviour of all the footmen of the nobility.", "파트리지의 자존심이 자신을 하인이라 인정하는 데까지는 굽히지 않았으나, 그래도 그는 대개의 사소한 점에서 그 신분의 처신을 따라 하는 정도까지는 자신을 낮추었다. 그 한 예가, 자신이 동행이라 부르는 존스의 재산을 크게 부풀려 말한 일이었다. 이는 낯선 이들 사이에서 모든 하인이 흔히 보이는 습성이니, 어느 누구도 거지의 시중꾼으로 여겨지길 바라지 않기 때문이다. 주인의 처지가 높을수록, 그에 따라 자기 자신에 대한 하인의 자부심도 높아지는 법이다. 이 관찰의 진실함은 귀족들을 모시는 모든 종복의 행실에서 고스란히 드러난다."]], [["But, though title and fortune communicate a splendor all around them, and the footmen of men of quality and of estate think themselves entitled to a part of that respect which is paid to the quality and estate of their masters, it is clearly otherwise with regard to virtue and understanding.", "그러나 지위와 재산은 그 둘레의 모든 것에 광채를 나누어 주고, 신분 있고 재산 있는 사람의 종복은 자기 주인의 신분과 재산에 바쳐지는 존경의 일부를 누릴 자격이 있다고 여기지만, 덕과 식견에 관해서는 사정이 분명히 다르다."], ["These advantages are strictly personal, and swallow themselves all the respect which is paid to them.", "이러한 장점은 철저히 그 사람 개인의 것이어서, 거기에 바쳐지는 존경을 자기 안으로 통째로 삼켜 버린다."], ["To say the truth, this is so very little, that they cannot well afford to let any others partake with them.", "사실대로 말하자면, 그 존경이라는 것이 워낙 적어서, 다른 누구와 나눠 가질 여유조차 없다."], ["As these therefore reflect no honour on the domestic, so neither is he at all dishonoured by the most deplorable want of both in his master.", "그러므로 이러한 것들이 하인에게는 아무런 영예도 비추어 주지 않듯, 주인에게 그 둘이 가장 한심할 만큼 결여되어 있다 해도 하인이 조금도 욕될 일은 없다."], ["Indeed it is otherwise in the want of what is called virtue in a mistress, the consequence of which we have before seen: for in this dishonour there is a kind of contagion, which, like that of poverty, communicates itself to all who approach it.", "다만 안주인에게 이른바 덕이라 부르는 것이 결여된 경우라면 사정이 다르니, 그 결과는 앞에서 이미 본 바와 같다. 이 불명예에는 일종의 전염성이 있어, 가난의 그것처럼 그것에 가까이 다가가는 모든 이에게 옮아붙기 때문이다."]], [["Now for these reasons we are not to wonder that servants (I mean among the men only) should have so great regard for the reputation of the wealth of their masters, and little or none at all for their character in other points, and that, though they would be ashamed to be the footman of a beggar, they are not so to attend upon a rogue or a blockhead; and do consequently make no scruple to spread the fame of the iniquities and follies of their said masters as far as possible, and this often with great humour and merriment.", "자, 이러한 이유들로 보아, 하인들이(나는 남자들만을 두고 하는 말이다) 주인의 재산에 대한 평판에는 그토록 큰 관심을 두면서도 다른 점에서의 인품에는 거의, 혹은 전혀 마음을 쓰지 않는다 해도, 또 거지의 종복이 되는 것은 부끄러워하면서도 악당이나 멍청이를 모시는 것은 부끄러워하지 않는다 해도, 그리고 그 결과 그런 주인의 죄악과 어리석음에 대한 소문을 거리낌 없이 가능한 한 멀리 퍼뜨리고, 그것도 흔히 큰 익살과 흥겨움을 곁들여 그리한다 해도, 우리는 놀랄 일이 아니다."], ["In reality, a footman is often a wit as well as a beau, at the expence of the gentleman whose livery he wears.", "실로 종복이란, 자기가 걸친 제복의 주인 양반을 희생양 삼아, 멋쟁이일 뿐 아니라 곧잘 재치꾼이 되기도 하는 법이다."]], [["After Partridge, therefore, had enlarged greatly on the vast fortune to which Mr Jones was heir, he very freely communicated an apprehension, which he had begun to conceive the day before, and for which, as we hinted at that very time, the behaviour of Jones seemed to have furnished a sufficient foundation.", "그리하여 파트리지는 존스 씨가 물려받을 막대한 재산에 대해 한껏 부풀려 늘어놓고 난 뒤, 전날부터 품기 시작한 어떤 우려를 아주 거리낌 없이 털어놓았다. 그 우려에 대해서는, 바로 그때 우리가 넌지시 일러둔 대로, 존스의 행동이 충분한 근거를 마련해 준 듯했다."], ["In short, he was now pretty well confirmed in an opinion that his master was out of his wits, with which opinion he very bluntly acquainted the good company round the fire.", "요컨대 그는 이제 자기 주인이 제정신이 아니라는 견해에 꽤나 굳게 확신을 갖게 되었고, 그 견해를 난롯가에 둘러앉은 점잖은 무리에게 아주 무뚝뚝하게 알려 주었다."]], [["With this sentiment the puppet-show man immediately coincided.", "이 의견에 인형극 사내가 곧바로 맞장구를 쳤다. \"솔직히 말해서,\""], ["“I own,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “the gentleman surprized me very much, when he talked so absurdly about puppet-shows.", "\"그 양반이 인형극에 대해 그렇게 터무니없는 소리를 늘어놓을 때 저는 어찌나 놀랐는지 모릅니다. 정말이지 제정신인 사람이 그토록 크게 잘못 알고 있으리라고는 도무지 믿기지가 않더군요."], ["It is indeed hardly to be conceived that any man in his senses should be so much mistaken; what you say now accounts very well for all his monstrous notions.", "지금 하신 말씀을 들으니 그 양반의 그 괴상한 생각들이 죄다 아주 잘 설명됩니다. 가엾은 양반 같으니!"], ["Poor gentleman!", "저도 그 사람이 진심으로 딱하게 여겨집니다."], ["I am heartily concerned for him; indeed he hath a strange wildness about his eyes, which I took notice of before, though I did not mention it.”", "사실 그 양반 눈에는 뭔가 묘하게 사나운 기운이 있어요. 입 밖에 내진 않았어도 진작 눈여겨봐 두었답니다.\""]], [["The landlord agreed with this last assertion, and likewise claimed the sagacity of having observed it.", "주인장도 이 마지막 단언에 동의했고, 더불어 자기도 그것을 알아챘노라며 그 명민함을 자랑했다. \"그리고 분명히,\""], ["“And certainly,”", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["added he, “it must be so; for no one but a madman would have thought of leaving so good a house to ramble about the country at that time of night.”", "\"그게 틀림없을 겁니다. 미친 사람이 아니고서야 누가 그런 좋은 집을 놔두고 한밤중에 시골을 쏘다닐 생각을 하겠습니까.\""]], [["The exciseman, pulling his pipe from his mouth, said, “He thought the gentleman looked and talked a little wildly;”", "소비세 징수원이 입에서 담뱃대를 빼며 말했다. \"내 보기에도 그 양반이 좀 정신 나간 사람처럼 보이고 그렇게 말하는 것 같더이다.\""], ["and then turning to Partridge, “if he be a madman,”", "그러고는 파트리지를 향해 돌아앉으며 말했다."], ["says he, “he should not be suffered to travel thus about the country; for possibly he may do some mischief.", "\"만약 그 사람이 미치광이라면, 저렇게 시골을 떠돌아다니게 내버려두어선 안 됩니다. 무슨 사고라도 칠지 모르니까요."], ["It is a pity he was not secured and sent home to his relations.”", "붙잡아서 친척들에게 돌려보내지 못한 게 안타깝군요.\""]], [["Now some conceits of this kind were likewise lurking in the mind of Partridge; for, as he was now persuaded that Jones had run away from Mr Allworthy, he promised himself the highest rewards if he could by any means convey him back.", "그런데 이런 종류의 궁리는 파트리지의 마음속에서도 똬리를 틀고 있었다. 그는 이제 존스가 올워디 씨에게서 도망쳐 나왔다고 믿게 되었으므로, 무슨 수를 써서든 그를 도로 데려갈 수만 있다면 최고의 보상을 받으리라 혼자 기대를 부풀리고 있었던 것이다."], ["But fear of Jones, of whose fierceness and strength he had seen, and indeed felt, some instances, had however represented any such scheme as impossible to be executed, and had discouraged him from applying himself to form any regular plan for the purpose.", "그러나 존스의 사나움과 힘에 대해서는 몇 차례 직접 보았고 또 실제로 몸소 겪기까지 했던 터라, 그 두려움 때문에 그런 계획은 도무지 실행 불가능한 일로 여겨졌고, 그래서 그는 그 목적을 위해 무슨 제대로 된 계획을 세워 볼 엄두조차 내지 못하고 있었다."], ["But no sooner did he hear the sentiments of the exciseman than he embraced that opportunity of declaring his own, and expressed a hearty wish that such a matter could be brought about.", "그러나 징수원의 의견을 듣자마자 그는 자기 생각을 밝힐 그 기회를 덥석 붙잡았고, 정말이지 그런 일이 성사되었으면 좋겠다는 간절한 바람을 내비쳤다."]], [["“Could be brought about!”", "\"성사되었으면 좋겠다고요!\""], ["says the exciseman: “why, there is nothing easier.”", "징수원이 말했다. \"아니, 그보다 쉬운 일이 어디 있습니까.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["sir,”", "나리,\""], ["answered Partridge, “you don't know what a devil of a fellow he is.", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"당신은 그 사람이 어떤 악마 같은 작자인지 모르셔서 그럽니다."], ["He can take me up with one hand, and throw me out at window; and he would, too, if he did but imagine--”", "그 사람은 한 손으로 나를 번쩍 들어 창밖으로 내던질 수 있어요. 그리고 만약 그가 조금이라도 눈치챈다면, 정말로 그러고도 남을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Pogh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["says the exciseman, “I believe I am as good a man as he.", "징수원이 말했다. \"내 생각에 나도 그 사람 못지않은 장정이오."], ["Besides, here are five of us.”", "게다가 여기 우리가 다섯이나 되지 않소.\""]], [["“I don't know what five,”", "\"무슨 다섯이라는 거예요,\""], ["cries the landlady, “my husband shall have nothing to do in it.", "안주인이 소리쳤다. \"내 남편은 이 일에 끼어들 일 없어요."], ["Nor shall any violent hands be laid upon anybody in my house.", "내 집에서 그 누구에게도 함부로 손을 댈 수 없고요."], ["The young gentleman is as pretty a young gentleman as ever I saw in my life, and I believe he is no more mad than any of us.", "그 젊은 양반은 내 평생 본 중에 가장 멀쩡한 젊은이고, 내 보기엔 우리 누구보다도 미치지 않았답니다."], ["What do you tell of his having a wild look with his eyes?", "그 사람 눈에 사나운 기운이 있다니, 그게 무슨 소리예요?"], ["they are the prettiest eyes I ever saw, and he hath the prettiest look with them; and a very modest civil young man he is.", "내 평생 본 중에 가장 고운 눈이고, 그 눈으로 짓는 표정도 더없이 곱답니다. 게다가 아주 점잖고 예의 바른 청년이고요."], ["I am sure I have bepitied him heartily ever since the gentleman there in the corner told us he was crost in love.", "저 구석에 있는 양반이 그 사람이 사랑에 어긋났다고 말해 준 뒤로 나는 진심으로 그 사람이 가엾어 죽겠어요."], ["Certainly that is enough to make any man, especially such a sweet young gentleman as he is, to look a little otherwise than he did before.", "그런 일을 겪으면 누구라도, 특히 그처럼 사랑스러운 젊은 양반이라면 전과는 좀 달라 보이기 마련이지요. 귀부인이라니, 참!"], ["Lady, indeed! what the devil would the lady have better than such a handsome man with a great estate?", "그렇게 잘생긴 데다 큰 재산까지 가진 남자보다 그 귀부인이 도대체 더 나은 걸 어디서 바라겠어요?"], ["I suppose she is one of your quality folks, one of your Townly ladies that we saw last night in the puppet-show, who don't know what they would be at.”", "내 짐작에 그 여자는 당신들 말하는 그 지체 높은 양반네 중 하나, 어젯밤 인형극에서 본 그 타운리 부인 같은 여자일 거예요. 자기가 뭘 원하는지도 모르는 그런 부류 말이에요.\""]], [["The attorney's clerk likewise declared he would have no concern in the business without the advice of counsel.", "변호사 사무원도 변호사의 조언 없이는 이 일에 관여하지 않겠노라고 밝혔다."], ["“Suppose,”", "\"가령,\""], ["says he, “an action of false imprisonment should be brought against us, what defence could we make?", "그가 말했다. \"우리를 상대로 불법 감금 소송이 제기되면 우리가 무슨 변명을 할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Who knows what may be sufficient evidence of madness to a jury?", "배심원에게 광기를 입증할 증거로 무엇이 충분할지 누가 알겠어요?"], ["But I only speak upon my own account; for it don't look well for a lawyer to be concerned in these matters, unless it be as a lawyer.", "하지만 이건 어디까지나 제 입장에서만 드리는 말씀입니다. 법률가가 이런 일에 끼어드는 건, 법률가로서가 아니고서는 모양새가 좋지 않거든요."], ["Juries are always less favourable to us than to other people.", "배심원들은 언제나 우리한테 다른 사람보다 덜 호의적이니까요."], ["I don't therefore dissuade you, Mr Thomson (to the exciseman), nor the gentleman, nor anybody else.”", "그러니 톰슨 씨, 당신(징수원에게 한 말이다)이나 저 양반이나 다른 누구든 말리지는 않겠습니다.\""]], [["The exciseman shook his head at this speech, and the puppet-show man said, “Madness was sometimes a difficult matter for a jury to decide: for I remember,” says he, “I was once present at a tryal of madness, where twenty witnesses swore that the person was as mad as a March hare; and twenty others, that he was as much in his senses as any man in England.--And indeed it was the opinion of most people, that it was only a trick of his relations to rob the poor man of his right.”", "징수원은 이 말에 고개를 가로저었고, 인형극 사내가 말했다. \"광기란 때로 배심원이 판단하기 어려운 문제지요. 제가 기억하기로,\" 그가 말했다. \"한번은 어떤 광기 재판에 입회한 적이 있는데, 증인 스무 명이 그 사람은 삼월의 토끼처럼 미쳤다고 맹세했고, 또 다른 스무 명은 그가 영국의 어느 누구 못지않게 멀쩡하다고 맹세하더군요. 그리고 사실 대다수 사람들의 생각은, 그게 그 가엾은 사람의 권리를 가로채려는 친척들의 농간일 뿐이라는 거였답니다.\""]], [["“Very likely!”", "\"충분히 그럴 만하죠!\""], ["cries the landlady.", "안주인이 소리쳤다. \"나도 한 가엾은 양반을 알았는데, 그 사람은 가족 때문에 평생 정신병원에 갇혀 지냈고, 가족들은 그 재산을 누렸지요."], ["“I myself knew a poor gentleman who was kept in a mad-house all his life by his family, and they enjoyed his estate, but it did them no good; for though the law gave it them, it was the right of another.”", "하지만 그게 그들에게 좋은 일이 되지는 못했어요. 법이 그걸 그들에게 주었어도, 그건 본디 다른 사람의 권리였으니까요.\""]], [["“Pogh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["cries the clerk, with great contempt, “who hath any right but what the law gives them?", "사무원이 크게 비웃으며 소리쳤다. \"법이 주는 것 말고 누가 무슨 권리를 가진단 말이오?"], ["If the law gave me the best estate in the country, I should never trouble myself much who had the right.”", "만약 법이 이 고장에서 가장 좋은 재산을 내게 준다면, 나는 누구한테 그 권리가 있는지 따위로 결코 골치를 썩이지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“If it be so,”", "\"그게 그렇다면,\""], ["says Partridge, “Felix quem faciunt aliena pericula cautum.”", "파트리지가 말했다. \"펠릭스 쿠엠 파키운트 알리에나 페리쿨라 카우툼.\"*"]], [["My landlord, who had been called out by the arrival of a horseman at the gate, now returned into the kitchen, and with an affrighted countenance cried out, “What do you think, gentlemen?", "* 라틴어 격언: 남의 위험을 보고 조심하게 되는 사람은 행복하다. 문간에 말 탄 사람이 도착하는 바람에 불려 나갔던 주인장이 이제 부엌으로 돌아오더니, 겁에 질린 얼굴로 소리쳤다."], ["The rebels have given the duke the slip, and are got almost to London.", "\"어떻게들 생각하시오, 여러분? 반란군이 공작을 따돌리고 거의 런던 코앞까지 들이닥쳤답니다."], ["It is certainly true, for a man on horseback just now told me so.”", "분명한 사실이에요. 방금 말 탄 사람이 내게 그렇게 일러 주었으니까.\""]], [["“I am glad of it with all my heart,”", "\"그것참 진심으로 다행이군요,\""], ["cries Partridge; “then there will be no fighting in these parts.”", "파트리지가 소리쳤다. \"그럼 이 근방에서는 싸움이 없겠네요.\""]], [["“I am glad,”", "\"나는 더 나은 이유에서 다행입니다,\""], ["cries the clerk, “for a better reason; for I would always have right take place.”", "사무원이 소리쳤다. \"나는 언제나 정당한 권리가 실현되기를 바라니까요.\""]], [["“Ay, but,”", "\"아, 그렇지만,\""], ["answered the landlord, “I have heard some people say this man hath no right.”", "주인장이 대답했다. \"어떤 사람들은 그 사람에게 아무 권리도 없다고 말하는 걸 들었는데요.\""]], [["“I will prove the contrary in a moment,”", "\"그 반대임을 당장 증명해 드리지요,\" 사무원이 소리쳤다."], ["cries the clerk: “if my father dies seized of a right; do you mind me, seized of a right, I say; doth not that right descend to his son; and doth not one right descend as well as another?”", "\"만약 내 아버지가 어떤 권리를 점유한 채로 죽는다면, 내 말 잘 들으시오, 권리를 점유한 채로라고 했소, 그 권리가 그 아들에게 상속되지 않겠소? 그리고 어느 권리든 다른 권리와 똑같이 상속되는 것 아니겠소?\""]], [["“But how can he have any right to make us papishes?”", "\"하지만 어떻게 그 사람이 우리를 가톨릭교도로 만들 권리를 가질 수 있단 말이오?\""], ["says the landlord.", "주인장이 말했다."]], [["“Never fear that,”", "\"그건 걱정 마시오,\""], ["cries Partridge.", "파트리지가 소리쳤다."], ["“As to the matter of right, the gentleman there hath proved it as clear as the sun; and as to the matter of religion, it is quite out of the case.", "\"권리 문제로 말하자면, 저 양반이 해처럼 환하게 증명해 보였지요. 그리고 종교 문제로 말하자면, 그건 아예 해당되지 않는 일이오."], ["The papists themselves don't expect any such thing.", "가톨릭교도들 자신도 그런 일은 바라지 않으니까요."], ["A popish priest, whom I know very well, and who is a very honest man, told me upon his word and honour they had no such design.”", "내가 아주 잘 아는 어떤 가톨릭 사제는, 더없이 정직한 사람인데, 자기 신앙과 명예를 걸고 그들에게 그런 의도는 없다고 내게 말해 주었소.\""]], [["“And another priest, of my acquaintance,” said the landlady, “hath told me the same thing; but my husband is always so afraid of papishes. I know a great many papishes that are very honest sort of people, and spend their money very freely; and it is always a maxim with me, that one man's money is as good as another's.”", "\"내가 아는 또 다른 사제도,\" 안주인이 말했다. \"내게 똑같은 말을 해 주었어요. 그런데 내 남편은 늘 가톨릭교도들을 그렇게 무서워한답니다. 나는 아주 정직한 부류의 가톨릭교도들을 많이 알아요. 돈도 아주 시원시원하게 쓰는 사람들이지요. 그리고 나한테는 늘 한 가지 격언이 있는데, 이 사람 돈이나 저 사람 돈이나 다 똑같이 좋다는 거예요.\""]], [["“Very true, mistress,” said the puppet-show man, “I don't care what religion comes; provided the Presbyterians are not uppermost; for they are enemies to puppet-shows.”", "\"지당한 말씀입니다, 마님,\" 인형극 사내가 말했다. \"나는 어떤 종교가 들어오든 상관 안 합니다. 다만 장로교도들만 위에 올라서지 않으면 돼요. 그자들은 인형극의 적이거든요.\""]], [["“And so you would sacrifice your religion to your interest,”", "\"그래서 당신은 당신 이익을 위해 종교를 팔아넘기겠다는 거로군요,\""], ["cries the exciseman; “and are desirous to see popery brought in, are you?”", "징수원이 소리쳤다. \"그리고 가톨릭이 들어오는 걸 보고 싶다는 거고, 그런 거요?\""]], [["“Not I, truly,” answered the other; “I hate popery as much as any man; but yet it is a comfort to one, that one should be able to live under it, which I could not do among Presbyterians. To be sure, every man values his livelihood first; that must be granted; and I warrant, if you would confess the truth, you are more afraid of losing your place than anything else; but never fear, friend, there will be an excise under another government as well as under this.”", "\"천만에요, 정말로 아닙니다,\" 사내가 대답했다. \"나도 누구 못지않게 가톨릭을 싫어해요. 하지만 그래도, 그 밑에서나마 먹고살 수 있으리라는 건 위안이 되거든요. 장로교도들 사이에서는 그러지도 못할 테니까요. 확실히 사람은 누구나 제 밥줄을 가장 소중히 여기는 법, 그건 인정해야지요. 그리고 장담컨대, 당신도 솔직히 털어놓는다면 다른 무엇보다도 일자리 잃는 걸 가장 두려워하고 있을 거요. 하지만 걱정 마시오, 친구. 이 정부 아래서나 다른 정부 아래서나 소비세는 똑같이 있을 테니까.\""]], [["“Why, certainly,” replied the exciseman, “I should be a very ill man if I did not honour the king, whose bread I eat. That is no more than natural, as a man may say: for what signifies it to me that there would be an excise-office under another government, since my friends would be out, and I could expect no better than to follow them? No, no, friend, I shall never be bubbled out of my religion in hopes only of keeping my place under another government; for I should certainly be no better, and very probably might be worse.”", "\"아니, 그야 물론,\" 징수원이 대꾸했다. \"내가 그 밥을 먹고 사는 임금님을 받들지 않는다면 나는 아주 못된 인간이겠지요. 그건 그저 당연한 일이오, 사람이 말하자면. 다른 정부 아래서 소비세 관청이 있은들 그게 내게 무슨 소용이겠소? 내 동료들은 다 쫓겨날 테고, 나도 그 뒤를 따라가는 것 말고는 더 나은 걸 기대할 수 없을 텐데 말이오. 아니, 아니지, 친구. 다른 정부 아래서 자리나 지키겠다는 기대 하나로 내 신앙을 속아 넘기는 일은 결코 없을 거요. 그래 봤자 나는 분명 조금도 나아지지 않을 테고, 십중팔구 더 나빠질 테니까.\""]], [["“Why, that is what I say,”", "\"아, 그게 바로 내가 하는 말이오,\""], ["cries the landlord, “whenever folks say who knows what may happen!", "주인장이 소리쳤다. \"사람들이 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 아냐고 떠들어댈 때마다 말이오!"], ["Odsooks!", "맙소사!"], ["should not I be a blockhead to lend my money to I know not who, because mayhap he may return it again?", "누군지도 모르는 사람한테, 어쩌면 도로 갚을지도 모른다는 이유 하나로 내 돈을 빌려준다면 그게 멍청이가 아니고 뭐겠소?"], ["I am sure it is safe in my own bureau, and there I will keep it.”", "내 돈은 내 책상 서랍 안에 있는 게 안전하니, 거기다 그냥 둘 거요.\""]], [["The attorney's clerk had taken a great fancy to the sagacity of Partridge.", "변호사 사무원은 파트리지의 명민함에 크게 반해 버렸다."], ["Whether this proceeded from the great discernment which the former had into men, as well as things, or whether it arose from the sympathy between their minds; for they were both truly Jacobites in principle; they now shook hands heartily, and drank bumpers of strong beer to healths which we think proper to bury in oblivion.", "이것이 사무원이 사물뿐 아니라 사람을 꿰뚫어 보는 그 뛰어난 안목에서 비롯된 것인지, 아니면 두 사람 마음 사이의 공감에서 생겨난 것인지는 모르겠으나, 두 사람 다 신념에서는 진짜배기 자코바이트였던 까닭에, 그들은 이제 진심으로 손을 맞잡고 독한 맥주를 가득 채워 어떤 이들의 건강을 위해 건배했는데, 우리는 그 이름을 망각 속에 묻어 두는 편이 옳다고 본다."]], [["These healths were afterwards pledged by all present, and even by my landlord himself, though reluctantly; but he could not withstand the menaces of the clerk, who swore he would never set his foot within his house again, if he refused.", "이 축배는 뒤이어 그 자리에 있던 모두가, 심지어 주인장 자신조차도 마지못해 따라 들었다. 그러나 그는 만약 거절하면 다시는 이 집에 발을 들여놓지 않겠다고 맹세하는 사무원의 으름장을 도저히 당해 낼 수 없었다."], ["The bumpers which were swallowed on this occasion soon put an end to the conversation.", "이때 들이켠 가득한 잔들이 곧 대화에 종지부를 찍었다."], ["Here, therefore, we will put an end to the chapter.", "그러니 우리도 여기서 이 장을 마치기로 하자."]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["In which fortune seems to have been in a better humour with Jones than we have hitherto seen her.", "8장. 이제껏 우리가 보아 온 것보다 운명의 여신이 존스에게 한결 너그러운 기분이었던 듯한 장."]], [["As there is no wholesomer, so perhaps there are few stronger, sleeping potions than fatigue.", "피로만큼 몸에 좋은 수면제는 없으며, 또한 그만큼 강력한 수면제도 드물 것이다."], ["Of this Jones might be said to have taken a very large dose, and it operated very forcibly upon him.", "존스는 이 약을 아주 큰 분량으로 들이켰다고 할 만했고, 그 효력은 그에게 아주 세차게 작용했다."], ["He had already slept nine hours, and might perhaps have slept longer, had he not been awakened by a most violent noise at his chamber-door, where the sound of many heavy blows was accompanied with many exclamations of murder.", "그는 이미 아홉 시간을 잤고, 어쩌면 더 오래 잤을지도 모른다. 만약 방문 앞에서 터져 나온 더없이 격렬한 소음, 곧 묵직한 매질 소리에 살인이라 외치는 고함이 잇따라 섞이는 그 소동에 깨어나지 않았더라면 말이다."], ["Jones presently leapt from his bed, where he found the master of the puppet-show belabouring the back and ribs of his poor Merry-Andrew, without either mercy or moderation.", "존스는 곧장 침대에서 뛰쳐나왔고, 그곳에서 인형극단의 단장이 자비도 절제도 없이 가엾은 어릿광대의 등과 옆구리를 두들겨 패고 있는 광경을 보았다."]], [["Jones instantly interposed on behalf of the suffering party, and pinned the insulting conqueror up to the wall: for the puppet-show man was no more able to contend with Jones than the poor party-coloured jester had been to contend with this puppet-man.", "존스는 곧바로 매를 맞고 있는 쪽을 위해 끼어들어, 으스대던 그 정복자를 벽에 밀어붙여 꼼짝 못하게 했다. 가엾은 알록달록한 옷차림의 광대가 이 인형극단 단장과 겨룰 처지가 못 되었듯, 이 인형극단 단장 또한 존스와 맞붙을 처지가 못 되었던 것이다."]], [["But though the Merry-Andrew was a little fellow, and not very strong, he had nevertheless some choler about him.", "하지만 그 어릿광대는 작은 사내였고 그리 힘세지도 않았으나, 그래도 나름의 노여움은 품고 있었다."], ["He therefore no sooner found himself delivered from the enemy, than he began to attack him with the only weapon at which he was his equal.", "그래서 그는 적에게서 풀려나자마자, 자신이 그와 호각을 이룰 수 있는 유일한 무기로 그를 공격하기 시작했다."], ["From this he first discharged a volley of general abusive words, and thence proceeded to some particular accusations--“D--n your bl--d, you rascal,”", "우선 그는 두루뭉술한 욕설을 한바탕 퍼붓고, 거기서 나아가 몇 가지 구체적인 비난으로 옮겨 갔다. \"이 빌어먹을 놈아.\""], ["says he, “I have not only supported you (for to me you owe all the money you get), but I have saved you from the gallows.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 너를 먹여 살렸을 뿐 아니라(네가 벌어들이는 돈은 다 내 덕이지) 교수대에서도 구해 줬어."], ["Did you not want to rob the lady of her fine riding-habit, no longer ago than yesterday, in the back-lane here?", "바로 어제, 여기 뒷골목에서 그 부인의 멋진 승마복을 빼앗으려 들지 않았느냐?"], ["Can you deny that you wished to have her alone in a wood to strip her--to strip one of the prettiest ladies that ever was seen in the world?", "그 부인을 숲속에 혼자 끌고 가서 옷을 벗기고 싶어 했던 걸 부인할 수 있어? 세상에 보기 드물게 어여쁜 부인을 말이다!"], ["and here you have fallen upon me, and have almost murdered me, for doing no harm to a girl as willing as myself, only because she likes me better than you.”", "그래 놓고도 너는, 나한테나 똑같이 마음 내켜 한 계집애한테 아무런 해도 끼치지 않은 나를, 그 애가 너보다 나를 더 좋아한다는 이유 하나로 덮쳐서 죽을 만큼 패 놓았어.\""]], [["Jones no sooner heard this than he quitted the master, laying on him at the same time the most violent injunctions of forbearance from any further insult on the Merry-Andrew; and then taking the poor wretch with him into his own apartment, he soon learned tidings of his Sophia, whom the fellow, as he was attending his master with his drum the day before, had seen pass by.", "존스는 이 말을 듣자마자 단장을 놓아주었는데, 그러면서도 어릿광대에게 더는 어떤 모욕도 가하지 말라고 더없이 단호하게 못 박아 두었다. 그러고 나서 그 가엾은 사내를 자기 방으로 데려가서는, 곧 소피아의 소식을 알아냈다."], ["He easily prevailed with the lad to show him the exact place, and then having summoned Partridge, he departed with the utmost expedition.", "그자는 전날 북을 치며 단장을 따라다니다가 그녀가 지나가는 것을 보았던 것이다. 존스는 어렵지 않게 그 청년을 구슬려 정확한 장소를 일러 받았고, 그런 다음 파트리지를 불러 더없이 서둘러 길을 떠났다."]], [["It was almost eight of the clock before all matters could be got ready for his departure: for Partridge was not in any haste, nor could the reckoning be presently adjusted; and when both these were settled and over, Jones would not quit the place before he had perfectly reconciled all differences between the master and the man.", "떠날 채비가 다 갖춰지기까지는 거의 여덟 시가 되었다. 파트리지가 도무지 서두르는 법이 없었고, 셈도 당장 치러지지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 이 두 가지가 다 정리되고 끝났을 때도, 존스는 단장과 그 사내 사이의 모든 다툼을 말끔히 화해시키기 전에는 그곳을 떠나려 하지 않았다."]], [["When this was happily accomplished, he set forwards, and was by the trusty Merry-Andrew conducted to the spot by which Sophia had past; and then having handsomely rewarded his conductor, he again pushed on with the utmost eagerness, being highly delighted with the extraordinary manner in which he received his intelligence. Of this Partridge was no sooner acquainted, than he, with great earnestness, began to prophesy, and assured Jones that he would certainly have good success in the end: for, he said, “two such accidents could never have happened to direct him after his mistress, if Providence had not designed to bring them together at last.” And this was the first time that Jones lent any attention to the superstitious doctrines of his companion.", "이것이 무사히 이루어지자 그는 길을 나섰고, 그 믿음직한 어릿광대의 안내를 받아 소피아가 지나간 지점에 이르렀다. 그러고는 안내인에게 후하게 사례한 뒤, 다시금 더없는 열의로 발걸음을 재촉했다. 소식을 얻게 된 그 남다른 경위에 무척이나 기분이 좋았던 것이다. 파트리지는 이 사정을 듣자마자 자못 진지하게 예언을 늘어놓기 시작하며, 존스에게 결국에는 반드시 좋은 결과가 따를 것이라고 장담했다. 그는 말했다. \"이렇게 두 번씩이나 우연이 일어나 도련님을 마님 뒤로 이끌었으니, 섭리가 끝내 두 분을 맺어 주려 작정한 게 아니라면 어찌 이런 일이 있겠습니까.\" 그리고 존스가 동행인의 미신 같은 소리에 처음으로 귀를 기울인 것이 바로 이때였다."]], [["They had not gone above two miles when a violent storm of rain overtook them; and, as they happened to be at the same time in sight of an ale-house, Partridge, with much earnest entreaty, prevailed with Jones to enter, and weather the storm. Hunger is an enemy (if indeed it may be called one) which partakes more of the English than of the French disposition; for, though you subdue this never so often, it will always rally again in time; and so it did with Partridge, who was no sooner arrived within the kitchen, than he began to ask the same questions which he had asked the night before. The consequence of this was an excellent cold chine being produced upon the table, upon which not only Partridge, but Jones himself, made a very hearty breakfast, though the latter began to grow again uneasy, as the people of the house could give him no fresh information concerning Sophia.", "그들이 채 이 마일도 가기 전에 사나운 빗줄기가 들이닥쳤다. 그리고 마침 같은 순간에 술집 하나가 눈에 들어온 터라, 파트리지는 간곡한 애원 끝에 존스를 안으로 들어가 비를 피하도록 설득해 냈다. 굶주림이란(그것을 적이라 부를 수 있다면 말이지만) 프랑스 사람의 기질보다는 영국 사람의 기질을 더 닮은 적이다. 아무리 자주 물리쳐도 때가 되면 늘 다시 전열을 가다듬으니 말이다. 파트리지도 마찬가지여서, 부엌에 발을 들여놓기가 무섭게 전날 밤 던졌던 것과 똑같은 물음들을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그 결과 훌륭한 차가운 등심 한 덩이가 식탁에 올라왔고, 파트리지뿐 아니라 존스 자신도 무척 든든하게 아침을 들었다. 다만 존스는 집안 사람들이 소피아에 관해 새 소식을 전혀 전해 주지 못하자 다시금 마음이 불안해지기 시작했다."]], [["Their meal being over, Jones was again preparing to sally, notwithstanding the violence of the storm still continued; but Partridge begged heartily for another mug; and at last casting his eyes on a lad at the fire, who had entered into the kitchen, and who at that instant was looking as earnestly at him, he turned suddenly to Jones, and cried, “Master, give me your hand, a single mug shan't serve the turn this bout. Why, here's more news of Madam Sophia come to town. The boy there standing by the fire is the very lad that rode before her. I can swear to my own plaister on his face.”--“Heavens bless you, sir,” cries the boy, “it is your own plaister sure enough; I shall have always reason to remember your goodness; for it hath almost cured me.”", "식사가 끝나자 존스는 여전히 사나운 폭풍이 멎지 않았는데도 다시 길을 나설 채비를 했다. 그러나 파트리지가 한 잔만 더 마시자고 간절히 졸랐다. 그러다가 마침내 부엌에 들어와 난롯가에 서 있는 한 소년에게 눈길이 닿았는데, 그 소년 역시 바로 그 순간 그를 뚫어지게 쳐다보고 있었다. 파트리지는 별안간 존스를 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"도련님, 손 좀 주십시오. 이번 판에는 달랑 한 잔으로는 안 되겠습니다. 아, 소피아 마님의 소식이 또 들어왔지 뭡니까. 저기 난롯가에 서 있는 저 녀석이 바로 마님 앞에서 말을 달리던 그 아이입니다. 제가 저 얼굴에 붙여 준 고약을 두고 맹세할 수도 있습니다.\" \"어르신, 복 받으세요.\" 소년이 외쳤다. \"정말 어르신이 붙여 주신 그 고약이 맞아요. 저는 두고두고 어르신의 은혜를 잊지 못할 겁니다. 덕분에 거의 다 나았거든요.\""]], [["At these words Jones started from his chair, and, bidding the boy follow him immediately, departed from the kitchen into a private apartment; for, so delicate was he with regard to Sophia, that he never willingly mentioned her name in the presence of many people; and, though he had, as it were, from the overflowings of his heart, given Sophia as a toast among the officers, where he thought it was impossible she should be known; yet, even there, the reader may remember how difficultly he was prevailed upon to mention her surname.", "이 말에 존스는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 소년에게 즉시 따라오라 이르고는 부엌을 나서 호젓한 방으로 들어갔다. 소피아에 관한 한 그는 어찌나 조심스러웠던지, 여러 사람 앞에서는 결코 그녀의 이름을 함부로 입에 올리지 않았다. 비록 그가 가슴에서 흘러넘치는 마음을 어쩌지 못해, 결코 그녀가 누군지 알려질 리 없다고 여긴 장교들 앞에서 소피아를 축배의 이름으로 든 적은 있었지만, 그곳에서조차 그가 그녀의 성을 입에 올리도록 설득당하는 데 얼마나 애를 먹었는지는 독자도 기억할 것이다."]], [["Hard therefore was it, and perhaps, in the opinion of many sagacious readers, very absurd and monstrous, that he should principally owe his present misfortune to the supposed want of that delicacy with which he so abounded; for, in reality, Sophia was much more offended at the freedoms which she thought (and not without good reason) he had taken with her name and character, than at any freedoms, in which, under his present circumstances, he had indulged himself with the person of another woman; and to say truth, I believe Honour could never have prevailed on her to leave Upton without her seeing Jones, had it not been for those two strong instances of a levity in his behaviour, so void of respect, and indeed so highly inconsistent with any degree of love and tenderness in great and delicate minds.", "그러니, 그가 그토록 차고 넘치게 지녔던 바로 그 조심스러움이 없었으리라는 오해 때문에 지금의 불행을 주로 겪게 되었다는 것은 참으로 야릇한 일이며, 어쩌면 식견 있는 많은 독자들의 눈에는 무척이나 터무니없고 괴이한 일로 비칠지도 모른다. 실은 소피아는, 지금 같은 처지의 그가 다른 여인의 몸을 두고 어떤 방종을 부렸든 그것보다도, 그가 자신의 이름과 인격을 함부로 다루었다고(그럴 만한 충분한 이유가 있었다) 여긴 그 무례함에 훨씬 더 마음이 상해 있었기 때문이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 그토록 존중이 결여되어 있고, 위대하고 섬세한 마음이 품는 그 어떤 사랑이나 애틋함과도 도무지 어울리지 않는, 그의 처신에 담긴 그 두 가지 명백한 경박함의 본보기가 아니었다면, 어너가 아무리 졸랐어도 소피아가 존스를 만나 보지 않은 채 업튼을 떠나도록 설득해 낼 수는 없었으리라고 나는 믿는다."]], [["But so matters fell out, and so I must relate them; and if any reader is shocked at their appearing unnatural, I cannot help it. I must remind such persons that I am not writing a system, but a history, and I am not obliged to reconcile every matter to the received notions concerning truth and nature. But if this was never so easy to do, perhaps it might be more prudent in me to avoid it. For instance, as the fact at present before us now stands, without any comment of mine upon it, though it may at first sight offend some readers, yet, upon more mature consideration, it must please all; for wise and good men may consider what happened to Jones at Upton as a just punishment for his wickedness with regard to women, of which it was indeed the immediate consequence; and silly and bad persons may comfort themselves in their vices by flattering their own hearts that the characters of men are rather owing to accident than to virtue. Now, perhaps the reflections which we should be here inclined to draw would alike contradict both these conclusions, and would show that these incidents contribute only to confirm the great, useful, and uncommon doctrine, which it is the purpose of this whole work to inculcate, and which we must not fill up our pages by frequently repeating, as an ordinary parson fills his sermon by repeating his text at the end of every paragraph.", "그러나 일이 그렇게 흘러갔고, 그러니 나는 그렇게 전할 수밖에 없다. 그리고 만약 어떤 독자가 이것이 부자연스럽게 보인다 하여 충격을 받는다면, 나로서도 어쩔 도리가 없다. 나는 그런 분들에게 일러두어야겠다. 나는 하나의 체계를 쓰는 것이 아니라 역사를 쓰는 것이며, 모든 사안을 진실과 자연에 관한 통념에 일일이 끼워 맞춰야 할 의무가 없다고. 그러나 설령 그렇게 맞추기가 아무리 쉽다 한들, 아마 나는 그것을 피하는 편이 더 분별 있는 일일 것이다. 이를테면 지금 우리 앞에 놓인 이 사실은, 내가 거기에 아무런 논평도 덧붙이지 않은 지금 이대로일 때, 비록 처음에는 어떤 독자들의 비위를 거스를지 몰라도, 더 무르익은 숙고를 거치고 나면 모두를 흡족하게 할 것이 분명하다. 현명하고 선한 사람들은 업튼에서 존스에게 닥친 일을 여자 문제에서 저지른 그의 못된 짓에 대한 정당한 벌로 여길 수 있는데, 그것은 실로 그 직접적인 결과였기 때문이다. 또 어리석고 못된 자들은 사람의 됨됨이가 덕보다는 우연에 달린 것이라고 제 마음을 다독이며 제 악덕 속에서 위안을 삼을 수도 있다. 그런데 어쩌면 우리가 이 자리에서 끌어내고 싶어질 성찰은 이 두 결론을 똑같이 반박할 것이며, 이 사건들이 다만, 이 작품 전체가 새겨 넣고자 하는 그 위대하고 유익하며 흔치 않은 가르침을 확증하는 데에만 이바지함을 보여 줄 것이다. 그리고 우리는 그 가르침을 자주 되풀이하느라 지면을 채워서는 안 된다. 마치 평범한 목사가 단락마다 끝에서 성경 구절을 되읊어 설교를 채우듯이 해서는 안 되는 것이다."]], [["We are contented that it must appear, however unhappily Sophia had erred in her opinion of Jones, she had sufficient reason for her opinion; since, I believe, every other young lady would, in her situation, have erred in the same manner.", "우리는 이 한 가지가 분명히 드러나는 것으로 만족한다. 소피아가 존스에 대한 자신의 판단에서 아무리 불행하게 잘못 짚었다 해도, 그녀에게는 그렇게 판단할 충분한 이유가 있었다는 사실 말이다."], ["Nay, had she followed her lover at this very time, and had entered this very alehouse the moment he was departed from it, she would have found the landlord as well acquainted with her name and person as the wench at Upton had appeared to be.", "내가 믿기로는, 다른 어떤 젊은 아가씨라도 그녀의 처지였다면 똑같이 잘못 짚었을 것이다. 아니, 만약 그녀가 바로 이때 연인의 뒤를 좇아, 그가 떠난 바로 그 순간에 이 술집에 들어섰더라면, 그녀는 업튼의 계집이 그러했던 것만큼이나 이곳 주인 역시 자신의 이름과 인물을 훤히 알고 있음을 알게 되었으리라."], ["For while Jones was examining his boy in whispers in an inner room, Partridge, who had no such delicacy in his disposition, was in the kitchen very openly catechising the other guide who had attended Mrs Fitzpatrick; by which means the landlord, whose ears were open on all such occasions, became perfectly well acquainted with the tumble of Sophia from her horse, &c., with the mistake concerning Jenny Cameron, with the many consequences of the punch, and, in short, with almost everything which had happened at the inn whence we despatched our ladies in a coach-and-six when we last took our leaves of them.", "존스가 안쪽 방에서 소년을 속삭이며 캐묻는 동안, 그런 조심성이라곤 기질에 없는 파트리지가 부엌에서 피츠패트릭 부인을 모셨던 다른 안내인을 아주 대놓고 문답하듯 추궁하고 있었기 때문이다. 그 바람에, 이런 일에는 늘 귀를 활짝 열어 두는 주인은 소피아가 말에서 굴러떨어진 일이며, 제니 캐머런에 관한 오해며, 펀치주가 빚어낸 갖가지 결말이며, 한마디로, 우리가 두 부인과 마지막으로 작별하며 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차에 태워 떠나보냈던 그 여관에서 일어난 일이라면 거의 모두를 속속들이 알게 되었던 것이다."]], [["Containing little more than a few odd observations.", "9장. 별스러운 소견 몇 가지를 담은 것 외에는 별다른 내용이 없는 장."]], [["Jones had been absent a full half-hour, when he returned into the kitchen in a hurry, desiring the landlord to let him know that instant what was to pay. And now the concern which Partridge felt at being obliged to quit the warm chimney-corner, and a cup of excellent liquor, was somewhat compensated by hearing that he was to proceed no farther on foot, for Jones, by golden arguments, had prevailed with the boy to attend him back to the inn whither he had before conducted Sophia; but to this however the lad consented, upon condition that the other guide would wait for him at the alehouse; because, as the landlord at Upton was an intimate acquaintance of the landlord at Gloucester, it might some time or other come to the ears of the latter that his horses had been let to more than one person; and so the boy might be brought to account for money which he wisely intended to put in his own pocket.", "존스가 자리를 비운 지 꼬박 반 시간이 지났을 무렵, 그는 다급히 주방으로 돌아와 주인에게 당장 계산할 금액을 알려 달라고 청했다. 그리고 이제, 따뜻한 굴뚝 모퉁이와 더없이 좋은 술 한 잔을 두고 일어서야 하는 데서 파트리지가 느낀 서운함은, 더 이상 걸어서 갈 필요가 없다는 말을 듣고 어느 정도 위로를 받았다. 존스가 황금빛 논리로 마부 소년을 설득해, 전에 소피아를 데려다준 그 여관까지 자신을 다시 안내하도록 만들었던 것이다. 그러나 소년은 다른 안내인이 술집에서 자기를 기다려 준다는 조건으로만 이에 응했다. 어퍼턴의 여관 주인이 글로스터의 여관 주인과 친한 사이라, 자기 말들을 한 사람 이상에게 빌려주었다는 사실이 언젠가 후자의 귀에 들어갈 수도 있고, 그러면 소년이 슬쩍 제 주머니에 챙겨 넣을 작정이던 돈에 대해 추궁을 당할 수도 있었기 때문이다."]], [["We were obliged to mention this circumstance, trifling as it may seem, since it retarded Mr Jones a considerable time in his setting out; for the honesty of this latter boy was somewhat high--that is, somewhat high-priced, and would indeed have cost Jones very dear, had not Partridge, who, as we have said, was a very cunning fellow, artfully thrown in half-a-crown to be spent at that very alehouse, while the boy was waiting for his companion. This half-crown the landlord no sooner got scent of, than he opened after it with such vehement and persuasive outcry, that the boy was soon overcome, and consented to take half-a-crown more for his stay. Here we cannot help observing, that as there is so much of policy in the lowest life, great men often overvalue themselves on those refinements in imposture, in which they are frequently excelled by some of the lowest of the human species.", "사소해 보일지 몰라도 우리는 이 사정을 언급하지 않을 수 없었다. 이 일이 존스 씨의 출발을 상당히 지체시켰기 때문이다. 이 두 번째 소년의 정직함은 다소 높았던 것이다. 다시 말해 다소 비쌌고, 실로 존스에게 큰 대가를 치르게 했을 것이다. 앞서 말했듯 매우 약삭빠른 친구였던 파트리지가, 소년이 동료를 기다리는 동안 바로 그 술집에서 쓰라며 슬쩍 반 크라운을 던져 넣지 않았더라면 말이다. 여관 주인은 이 반 크라운의 냄새를 맡자마자, 어찌나 격렬하고 설득력 있는 외침으로 그것을 좇았던지, 소년은 곧 넘어가 자기가 머무는 값으로 반 크라운을 더 받기로 했다. 여기서 우리는 한마디 하지 않을 수 없다. 가장 낮은 삶 속에도 이토록 많은 처세술이 깃들어 있으니, 위대한 인물들은 흔히 자기들이 인간 가운데 가장 미천한 자들에게 도리어 능가당하는 그 속임수의 세련됨을 두고 스스로를 지나치게 높이 평가하곤 한다는 것이다."]], [["The horses being now produced, Jones directly leapt into the side-saddle, on which his dear Sophia had rid. The lad, indeed, very civilly offered him the use of his; but he chose the side-saddle, probably because it was softer. Partridge, however, though full as effeminate as Jones, could not bear the thoughts of degrading his manhood; he therefore accepted the boy's offer: and now, Jones being mounted on the side-saddle of his Sophia, the boy on that of Mrs Honour, and Partridge bestriding the third horse, they set forwards on their journey, and within four hours arrived at the inn where the reader hath already spent so much time. Partridge was in very high spirits during the whole way, and often mentioned to Jones the many good omens of his future success which had lately befriended him; and which the reader, without being the least superstitious, must allow to have been particularly fortunate. Partridge was moreover better pleased with the present pursuit of his companion than he had been with his pursuit of glory; and from these very omens, which assured the pedagogue of success, he likewise first acquired a clear idea of the amour between Jones and Sophia; to which he had before given very little attention, as he had originally taken a wrong scent concerning the reasons of Jones's departure; and as to what happened at Upton, he was too much frightened just before and after his leaving that place to draw any other conclusions from thence than that poor Jones was a downright madman: a conceit which was not at all disagreeable to the opinion he before had of his extraordinary wildness, of which, he thought, his behaviour on their quitting Gloucester so well justified all the accounts he had formerly received. He was now, however, pretty well satisfied with his present expedition, and henceforth began to conceive much worthier sentiments of his friend's understanding.", "이제 말들이 끌려 나오자, 존스는 곧장 사랑하는 소피아가 탔던 옆안장에 뛰어올랐다. 소년은 매우 공손하게 자기 안장을 쓰라고 권했지만, 그는 옆안장을 택했다. 아마도 그쪽이 더 푹신했기 때문이리라. 그러나 존스 못지않게 여성스러운 파트리지는, 제 사내다움을 깎아내린다는 생각만은 견딜 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 소년의 제안을 받아들였다. 이리하여 존스는 소피아의 옆안장에 올라타고, 소년은 어너 부인의 안장에, 파트리지는 세 번째 말에 다리를 벌려 올라탄 채, 그들은 길을 떠나 네 시간 만에 독자가 이미 그토록 많은 시간을 보낸 그 여관에 도착했다. 파트리지는 가는 내내 기분이 무척 들떠 있었고, 최근에 자기를 도와준 앞날의 성공에 대한 여러 좋은 징조를 존스에게 자주 이야기했다. 그것들은 독자가 조금도 미신을 믿지 않는다 해도 유난히 운이 좋았다고 인정할 수밖에 없는 일들이었다. 게다가 파트리지는 동료가 지금 좇고 있는 일이, 전에 좇던 영광보다 더 마음에 들었다. 그리고 이 훈장에게 성공을 확신케 한 바로 그 징조들로부터, 그는 또한 존스와 소피아 사이의 연정에 대해 처음으로 분명한 생각을 얻게 되었다. 그는 전에는 거기에 거의 주의를 기울이지 않았는데, 애초에 존스가 떠난 이유를 두고 엉뚱한 냄새를 좇았던 까닭이었다. 그리고 어퍼턴에서 일어난 일로 말하자면, 그는 그곳을 떠나기 직전과 직후에 너무 겁에 질려 있어, 거기서 가엾은 존스가 영락없는 미치광이라는 것 말고는 다른 결론을 끌어낼 수가 없었다. 이런 짐작은, 그가 전부터 존스의 유별난 광기에 대해 품고 있던 견해와 조금도 어긋나지 않았다. 그는 글로스터를 떠날 때 보인 존스의 행동이야말로, 전에 들었던 온갖 소문을 그대로 입증해 준다고 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 이제 그는 지금의 이 여정에 꽤 만족했고, 이때부터 친구의 분별력에 대해 한결 더 후한 평가를 품기 시작했다."]], [["The clock had just struck three when they arrived, and Jones immediately bespoke post-horses; but unluckily there was not a horse to be procured in the whole place; which the reader will not wonder at when he considers the hurry in which the whole nation, and especially this part of it, was at this time engaged, when expresses were passing and repassing every hour of the day and night.", "시계가 막 세 시를 쳤을 때 그들은 도착했고, 존스는 곧바로 역마를 청했다. 그러나 공교롭게도 그 마을 전체에 구할 수 있는 말이 한 마리도 없었다. 이 시기 온 나라가, 특히 이 지역이 얼마나 분주하게 돌아가고 있었는지, 그리고 밤낮 가리지 않고 매시간 급사들이 오가고 있었는지를 헤아려 본다면, 독자는 이를 조금도 이상하게 여기지 않을 것이다."]], [["Jones endeavoured all he could to prevail with his former guide to escorte him to Coventry; but he was inexorable.", "존스는 전에 안내해 준 소년에게 코번트리까지 호위해 달라고 갖은 애를 썼지만, 소년은 요지부동이었다."], ["While he was arguing with the boy in the inn-yard, a person came up to him, and saluting him by his name, enquired how all the good family did in Somersetshire; and now Jones casting his eyes upon this person, presently discovered him to be Mr Dowling, the lawyer, with whom he had dined at Gloucester, and with much courtesy returned the salutation.", "그가 여관 마당에서 소년과 실랑이를 벌이고 있을 때, 한 사람이 다가와 그의 이름을 부르며 인사하고는 서머싯셔의 그 좋은 가문 식구들이 모두 어떻게 지내느냐고 물었다. 이제 존스가 그 사람에게 눈길을 던지니, 곧 글로스터에서 함께 식사했던 변호사 다울링 씨임을 알아보고는, 매우 정중하게 인사를 되받았다."]], [["Dowling very earnestly pressed Mr Jones to go no further that night; and backed his solicitations with many unanswerable arguments, such as, that it was almost dark, that the roads were very dirty, and that he would be able to travel much better by day-light, with many others equally good, some of which Jones had probably suggested to himself before; but as they were then ineffectual, so they were still: and he continued resolute in his design, even though he should be obliged to set out on foot.", "다울링은 존스 씨에게 그날 밤은 더 가지 말라고 무척 간곡히 권하며, 반박할 수 없는 여러 이유로 그 청을 거들었다. 이를테면 날이 거의 저물었다는 것, 길이 몹시 질척하다는 것, 날이 밝으면 훨씬 수월하게 갈 수 있으리라는 것, 그 밖에도 그에 못지않게 그럴듯한 이유들이 여럿이었는데, 그중 몇몇은 존스가 이미 스스로 떠올렸던 것일 터였다. 그러나 그때도 그 이유들이 소용없었듯, 지금도 마찬가지였다. 그는 설령 걸어서 출발해야 할지라도 자기 계획을 굽히지 않고 단호했다."]], [["When the good attorney found he could not prevail on Jones to stay, he as strenuously applied himself to persuade the guide to accompany him.", "이 선량한 변호사는 존스를 머물게 할 수 없음을 알자, 이번에는 마부 소년을 설득하는 데 그에 못지않게 힘을 쏟았다."], ["He urged many motives to induce him to undertake this short journey, and at last concluded with saying, “Do you think the gentleman won't very well reward you for your trouble?”", "그는 이 짧은 여정을 맡도록 소년을 부추기는 여러 동기를 들이댔고, 마침내 이렇게 말로 끝맺었다. \"저 신사분이 자네 수고에 두둑이 보답하지 않을 성싶은가?\""]], [["Two to one are odds at every other thing as well as at foot-ball.", "둘이 하나를 상대하면 유리한 법인데, 이는 축구뿐 아니라 다른 모든 일에서도 그러하다."], ["But the advantage which this united force hath in persuasion or entreaty must have been visible to a curious observer; for he must have often seen, that when a father, a master, a wife, or any other person in authority, have stoutly adhered to a denial against all the reasons which a single man could produce, they have afterwards yielded to the repetition of the same sentiments by a second or third person, who hath undertaken the cause, without attempting to advance anything new in its behalf.", "그러나 이렇게 힘을 합쳤을 때 설득이나 간청에서 얻는 이점은 눈썰미 있는 관찰자라면 분명히 보아 왔을 것이다. 아버지든 주인이든 아내든, 권위 있는 그 누구든, 한 사람이 내놓을 수 있는 온갖 이유에 맞서 완강히 거절을 고수하다가도, 두 번째나 세 번째 사람이 그 일을 떠맡아 똑같은 말을 되풀이하면 — 그를 위해 새로운 것이라곤 아무것도 보태지 않았음에도 — 끝내 굴복하는 일을 자주 보았을 테니 말이다."], ["And hence, perhaps, proceeds the phrase of seconding an argument or a motion, and the great consequence this is of in all assemblies of public debate.", "그리고 아마 여기서 어떤 주장이나 동의안을 \"재청한다\"는 말이 나왔을 것이며, 이것이 모든 공개 토론의 회합에서 큰 비중을 차지하는 까닭도 여기에 있을 것이다."], ["Hence, likewise, probably it is, that in our courts of law we often hear a learned gentleman (generally a serjeant) repeating for an hour together what another learned gentleman, who spoke just before him, had been saying.", "또한 우리 법정에서, 한 박식한 신사(대개는 변호사)가 바로 앞서 말한 다른 박식한 신사의 말을 한 시간 내내 되풀이하는 것을 우리가 자주 듣게 되는 것도, 아마 이 때문이리라."]], [["Instead of accounting for this, we shall proceed in our usual manner to exemplify it in the conduct of the lad above mentioned, who submitted to the persuasions of Mr Dowling, and promised once more to admit Jones into his side-saddle; but insisted on first giving the poor creatures a good bait, saying, they had travelled a great way, and been rid very hard. Indeed this caution of the boy was needless; for Jones, notwithstanding his hurry and impatience, would have ordered this of himself; for he by no means agreed with the opinion of those who consider animals as mere machines, and when they bury their spurs in the belly of their horse, imagine the spur and the horse to have an equal capacity of feeling pain.", "이를 설명하는 대신, 우리는 늘 하던 방식대로 앞서 언급한 소년의 행동으로 이를 예시해 보겠다. 소년은 다울링 씨의 설득에 넘어가, 다시 한번 존스를 자기 옆안장에 태우기로 약속했다. 다만 먼저 가엾은 짐승들에게 여물을 든든히 먹여야 한다고 우겼다. 녀석들이 먼 길을 왔고 몹시 호되게 몰렸다는 것이었다. 사실 소년의 이 조심성은 군더더기였다. 존스는 그토록 다급하고 안달이 났음에도, 누가 시키지 않아도 제 손으로 그렇게 시켰을 것이기 때문이다. 그는 동물을 한낱 기계로 여기는 자들의 생각에 결코 동의하지 않았으니, 그런 자들은 말의 배에 박차를 깊이 박으면서도 박차와 말이 똑같이 고통을 못 느낀다고 여기는 것이다."]], [["While the beasts were eating their corn, or rather were supposed to eat it (for, as the boy was taking care of himself in the kitchen, the ostler took great care that his corn should not be consumed in the stable), Mr Jones, at the earnest desire of Mr Dowling, accompanied that gentleman into his room, where they sat down together over a bottle of wine.", "짐승들이 옥수수를 먹는 동안, 아니 차라리 먹는다고 여겨지는 동안(소년이 주방에서 제 몸을 챙기는 사이, 마구간지기는 그 옥수수가 마구간에서 축나지 않도록 단단히 챙겼으니 말이다), 존스 씨는 다울링 씨의 간곡한 청에 따라 그 신사를 따라 방으로 들어갔고, 거기서 두 사람은 포도주 한 병을 사이에 두고 마주 앉았다."]], [["In which Mr Jones and Mr Dowling drink a bottle together.", "존스 씨와 다울링 씨가 함께 술 한 병을 나누다."]], [["Mr Dowling, pouring out a glass of wine, named the health of the good Squire Allworthy; adding, “If you please, sir, we will likewise remember his nephew and heir, the young squire: Come, sir, here's Mr Blifil to you, a very pretty young gentleman; and who, I dare swear, will hereafter make a very considerable figure in his country. I have a borough for him myself in my eye.”", "다울링 씨는 포도주를 한 잔 따르더니 선량한 올워디 영주의 건강을 위해 잔을 들자고 했다. 그러고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"괜찮으시다면, 선생, 그분의 조카이자 상속자인 젊은 영주의 건강도 함께 기리지요. 자, 선생, 블리필 씨를 위하여. 참으로 훤칠한 젊은 신사이고, 감히 장담하건대 앞으로 자기 고장에서 상당한 인물이 될 분입니다. 제 마음속에는 이미 그분을 위해 점찍어 둔 선거구까지 있답니다.\""]], [["“Sir,” answered Jones, “I am convinced you don't intend to affront me, so I shall not resent it; but I promise you, you have joined two persons very improperly together; for one is the glory of the human species, and the other is a rascal who dishonours the name of man.”", "\"선생,\" 존스가 대답했다. \"당신이 나를 모욕할 뜻이 없다는 건 알겠으니, 화를 내지는 않겠습니다. 하지만 분명히 말하건대, 당신은 도저히 어울리지 않는 두 사람을 한자리에 묶어 놓았습니다. 한 사람은 인류의 영광이고, 다른 한 사람은 인간이라는 이름을 더럽히는 악당이니까요.\""]], [["Dowling stared at this. He said, “He thought both the gentlemen had a very unexceptionable character. As for Squire Allworthy himself,” says he, “I never had the happiness to see him; but all the world talks of his goodness. And, indeed, as to the young gentleman, I never saw him but once, when I carried him the news of the loss of his mother; and then I was so hurried, and drove, and tore with the multiplicity of business, that I had hardly time to converse with him; but he looked so like a very honest gentleman, and behaved himself so prettily, that I protest I never was more delighted with any gentleman since I was born.”", "다울링은 이 말에 눈이 휘둥그레졌다. 그가 말했다. \"저는 두 분 다 나무랄 데 없는 인품을 지닌 줄로 알았습니다만. 올워디 영주로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 그분을 직접 뵙는 행운을 한 번도 누리지 못했습니다. 그래도 세상 사람들이 모두 그분의 선량함을 입에 올리지요. 그리고 정말이지, 그 젊은 신사분은 딱 한 번 뵈었을 뿐입니다. 그분께 어머님이 돌아가셨다는 소식을 전하러 갔을 때였지요. 그때는 일이 어찌나 많았던지 정신없이 휘몰리고 쫓기고 찢겨서 그분과 이야기할 틈도 거의 없었습니다. 그래도 정말 정직한 신사처럼 보이셨고 행동도 어찌나 반듯하시던지, 단언컨대 저는 태어나서 그 어떤 신사에게서도 그토록 큰 기쁨을 느껴 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“I don't wonder,”", "\"놀랍지도 않군요,\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “that he should impose upon you in so short an acquaintance; for he hath the cunning of the devil himself, and you may live with him many years, without discovering him.", "\"그토록 짧은 만남에서 그가 당신을 속였다는 게 말입니다. 그자는 악마 그 자체의 교활함을 지녔으니, 당신이 그와 여러 해를 함께 산다 해도 그 정체를 알아차리지 못할 겁니다."], ["I was bred up with him from my infancy, and we were hardly ever asunder; but it is very lately only that I have discovered half the villany which is in him.", "나는 어릴 적부터 그와 함께 자랐고, 우리는 거의 떨어져 본 적이 없었습니다. 그런데도 그 안에 든 악행의 절반이나마 깨달은 것은 아주 최근의 일입니다."], ["I own I never greatly liked him.", "솔직히 나는 한 번도 그를 그리 좋아하지 않았습니다. 그에게는 인간 본성의 위대하고 고귀한 모든 것의 든든한 토대가 되는 그 너그러운 정신이 없다고 생각했지요."], ["I thought he wanted that generosity of spirit, which is the sure foundation of all that is great and noble in human nature.", "오래전부터 나는 그에게서 내가 경멸하던 이기심을 보아 왔습니다. 하지만 그가 가장 비열하고 가장 시커먼 음모까지 꾸밀 수 있는 자임을 알게 된 것은 아주, 정말 최근의 일입니다."], ["I saw a selfishness in him long ago which I despised; but it is lately, very lately, that I have found him capable of the basest and blackest designs; for, indeed, I have at last found out, that he hath taken an advantage of the openness of my own temper, and hath concerted the deepest project, by a long train of wicked artifice, to work my ruin, which at last he hath effected.”", "그렇습니다, 나는 마침내 알아냈습니다. 그자가 내 솔직한 성품을 이용해, 오랜 세월 사악한 술책을 한 줄로 엮어 가며 나를 파멸시킬 깊디깊은 계략을 짜 왔고, 결국 그것을 이루어 냈다는 것을요.\""]], [["“Ay!", "\"이런!"], ["ay!”", "이런!\""], ["cries Dowling; “I protest, then, it is a pity such a person should inherit the great estate of your uncle Allworthy.”", "다울링이 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 단언컨대, 그런 자가 당신 백부 올워디 님의 그 큰 재산을 물려받는다는 건 참으로 안타까운 일이군요.\""]], [["“Alas, sir,” cries Jones, “you do me an honour to which I have no title. It is true, indeed, his goodness once allowed me the liberty of calling him by a much nearer name; but as this was only a voluntary act of goodness, I can complain of no injustice when he thinks proper to deprive me of this honour; since the loss cannot be more unmerited than the gift originally was. I assure you, sir, I am no relation of Mr Allworthy; and if the world, who are incapable of setting a true value on his virtue, should think, in his behaviour to me, he hath dealt hardly by a relation, they do an injustice to the best of men: for I--but I ask your pardon, I shall trouble you with no particulars relating to myself; only as you seemed to think me a relation of Mr Allworthy, I thought proper to set you right in a matter that might draw some censures upon him, which I promise you I would rather lose my life than give occasion to.”", "\"아, 선생,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"당신은 제게 어울리지 않는 영예를 안겨 주시는군요. 사실 한때는 그분의 선량함 덕분에 제가 그분을 훨씬 가까운 이름으로 부를 자유를 누리기는 했습니다. 하지만 그것은 오로지 자발적인 선의의 행위였을 뿐이니, 그분이 그 영예를 제게서 거두는 것이 마땅하다 여기신다 해도 저는 어떤 부당함도 호소할 수 없습니다. 그 상실이 부당하다 한들, 애초에 그 선물이 받을 자격 없이 주어진 것보다 더 부당할 수는 없으니까요. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 선생, 저는 올워디 씨와 아무런 혈연도 아닙니다. 그러니 그분의 덕에 참된 가치를 매길 줄 모르는 세상 사람들이, 저를 대하는 그분의 처사를 두고 그분이 친척을 모질게 대했다고 여긴다면, 그것은 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 분께 부당한 짓을 하는 것입니다. 저로 말하자면—아니, 용서하십시오, 제 자신에 관한 자질구레한 사연으로 당신을 번거롭게 하지는 않겠습니다. 다만 당신이 저를 올워디 씨의 친척으로 여기시는 듯하기에, 그분께 비난이 쏟아질 수도 있는 일만큼은 바로잡아 두는 것이 옳다고 여겼을 뿐입니다. 단언컨대 저는 그런 빌미를 드리느니 차라리 제 목숨을 내놓겠습니다.\""]], [["“I protest, sir,”", "\"단언컨대, 선생,\" 다울링이 외쳤다."], ["cried Dowling, “you talk very much like a man of honour; but instead of giving me any trouble, I protest it would give me great pleasure to know how you came to be thought a relation of Mr Allworthy's, if you are not.", "\"당신은 참으로 명예를 아는 분처럼 말씀하시는군요. 하지만 저를 번거롭게 하기는커녕, 정말이지 당신이 그분의 친척이 아니면서 어떻게 친척으로 여겨지게 되었는지 듣는다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다."], ["Your horses won't be ready this half-hour, and as you have sufficient opportunity, I wish you would tell me how all that happened; for I protest it seems very surprizing that you should pass for a relation of a gentleman, without being so.”", "말들이 준비되려면 아직 반 시간은 남았고, 시간도 넉넉하니, 그 모든 일이 어찌 된 것인지 들려주시면 좋겠습니다. 단언컨대 친척도 아니면서 한 신사의 친척으로 통했다는 것이 참으로 놀랍게 여겨지니 말입니다.\""]], [["Jones, who in the compliance of his disposition (though not in his prudence) a little resembled his lovely Sophia, was easily prevailed on to satisfy Mr Dowling's curiosity, by relating the history of his birth and education, which he did, like Othello.", "존스는 (분별력에서는 아니었으나) 사람 좋은 성품에서만큼은 사랑스러운 소피아를 조금 닮은 터라, 어렵지 않게 다울링 씨의 호기심을 채워 주기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 그는 자기 출생과 성장의 내력을 이야기했는데, 마치 오셀로처럼 풀어놓았다."]], [["------Even from his boyish years, To th' very moment he was bad to tell:", "소년 시절부터 바로 그 이야기를 청해 들은 순간에 이르기까지."]], [["the which to hear, Dowling, like Desdemona, did seriously incline;", "다울링은, 마치 데스데모나처럼, 그 이야기에 진지하게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["He swore 'twas strange, 'twas passing strange; 'Twas pitiful, 'twas wonderous pitiful.", "그는 맹세했네, 기이하다고, 더없이 기이하다고. 가엾다고, 한없이 가엾다고."]], [["Mr Dowling was indeed very greatly affected with this relation; for he had not divested himself of humanity by being an attorney.", "다울링 씨는 실로 이 이야기에 크게 마음이 움직였다. 변호사가 되었다고 해서 그가 인간다움을 벗어 버린 것은 아니었기 때문이다."], ["Indeed, nothing is more unjust than to carry our prejudices against a profession into private life, and to borrow our idea of a man from our opinion of his calling.", "사실 어떤 직업에 대한 우리의 편견을 사사로운 삶에까지 끌고 들어가, 그 사람의 생업에 대한 견해로 그 사람 자체를 단정하는 것만큼 부당한 일도 없다. 물론 습관이란 그 직업이 어쩔 수 없이 요구하고, 그래서 몸에 밴 행위들의 끔찍함을 무디게 만들기는 한다."], ["Habit, it is true, lessens the horror of those actions which the profession makes necessary, and consequently habitual; but in all other instances, Nature works in men of all professions alike; nay, perhaps, even more strongly with those who give her, as it were, a holiday, when they are following their ordinary business.", "하지만 그 밖의 모든 경우에는, 자연은 어떤 직업의 사람에게서도 똑같이 작용한다. 아니, 어쩌면 평소 자기 생업을 좇느라 자연에게, 말하자면 휴일을 주는 사람들에게서 한층 더 강하게 작용할지도 모른다."], ["A butcher, I make no doubt, would feel compunction at the slaughter of a fine horse; and though a surgeon can feel no pain in cutting off a limb, I have known him compassionate a man in a fit of the gout.", "푸주한이라도 훌륭한 말을 도살하는 데에는 틀림없이 양심의 가책을 느낄 것이며, 외과의가 팔다리를 잘라 내며 아무 고통도 느끼지 않는다 해도, 나는 그가 통풍 발작으로 괴로워하는 사람을 가엾이 여기는 것을 본 적이 있다."], ["The common hangman, who hath stretched the necks of hundreds, is known to have trembled at his first operation on a head: and the very professors of human blood-shedding, who, in their trade of war, butcher thousands, not only of their fellow-professors, but often of women and children, without remorse; even these, I say, in times of peace, when drums and trumpets are laid aside, often lay aside all their ferocity, and become very gentle members of civil society.", "수백 명의 목을 늘여 놓은 평범한 교수형 집행인도, 처음 한 사람의 머리를 다룰 때에는 떨었다고 전해진다. 그리고 전쟁이라는 자기 생업 속에서 동료 군인은 물론이거니와 흔히 여자와 아이들까지 수천 명을 거리낌 없이 학살하는, 그야말로 사람 피 흘리는 일을 업으로 삼은 자들조차—그런 자들조차, 내 말하건대, 북과 나팔을 거두어 둔 평화로운 시절에는 흔히 그 모든 흉포함을 내려놓고 시민 사회의 더없이 온순한 일원이 된다."], ["In the same manner an attorney may feel all the miseries and distresses of his fellow-creatures, provided he happens not to be concerned against them.", "마찬가지로 변호사도, 자기가 맞서야 할 상대가 아니기만 하다면, 같은 인간이 겪는 온갖 비참과 고통을 고스란히 느낄 수 있는 것이다."]], [["Jones, as the reader knows, was yet unacquainted with the very black colours in which he had been represented to Mr Allworthy; and as to other matters, he did not shew them in the most disadvantageous light; for though he was unwilling to cast any blame on his former friend and patron; yet he was not very desirous of heaping too much upon himself. Dowling therefore observed, and not without reason, that very ill offices must have been done him by somebody: “For certainly,” cries he, “the squire would never have disinherited you only for a few faults, which any young gentleman might have committed. Indeed, I cannot properly say disinherited: for to be sure by law you cannot claim as heir. That's certain; that nobody need go to counsel for. Yet when a gentleman had in a manner adopted you thus as his own son, you might reasonably have expected some very considerable part, if not the whole; nay, if you had expected the whole, I should not have blamed you: for certainly all men are for getting as much as they can, and they are not to be blamed on that account.”", "독자도 알다시피, 존스는 자신이 올워디 씨에게 얼마나 시커먼 빛깔로 그려져 있었는지를 아직 알지 못했다. 그리고 다른 일들에 대해서도 그는 가장 불리한 빛으로 드러내지는 않았다. 옛 벗이자 후원자에게 어떤 허물도 씌우고 싶지 않았던 한편으로, 그렇다고 너무 많은 짐을 제 자신에게 쌓아 올리고 싶지도 않았기 때문이다. 그래서 다울링은, 나름의 근거를 가지고, 누군가가 그에게 아주 못된 짓을 했음에 틀림없다고 보았다. \"분명히,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"영주께서 한낱 몇 가지 잘못만으로, 그것도 어느 젊은 신사라도 저질렀을 법한 잘못만으로 당신의 상속권을 박탈하셨을 리가 없습니다. 사실, 상속권을 박탈했다고 말하는 것도 온당치는 않지요. 법으로 따지면 당신이 상속인으로 권리를 주장할 수는 없으니까요. 그건 분명합니다. 그 점은 누구도 변호사를 찾아가 물어볼 필요조차 없는 일이지요. 그러나 한 신사분이 어떤 의미로든 당신을 친아들처럼 거두어 주었다면, 전부는 아닐지라도 상당히 큰 몫쯤은 기대하는 게 당연했을 겁니다. 아니, 당신이 전부를 기대했다 한들 저는 당신을 탓하지 않겠습니다. 분명 사람이란 누구나 가질 수 있는 만큼 가지려 하는 법이고, 그 때문에 탓할 일은 아니니까요.\""]], [["“Indeed you wrong me,”", "\"정말이지 당신은 저를 오해하고 계십니다,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones; “I should have been contented with very little: I never had any view upon Mr Allworthy's fortune; nay, I believe I may truly say, I never once considered what he could or might give me.", "\"저는 아주 적은 것으로도 만족했을 겁니다. 저는 단 한 번도 올워디 씨의 재산에 눈독을 들인 적이 없습니다."], ["This I solemnly declare, if he had done a prejudice to his nephew in my favour, I would have undone it again.", "아니, 진실로 말할 수 있건대, 그분이 제게 무엇을 줄 수 있고 무엇을 줄지를 단 한 번도 헤아려 본 적이 없습니다."], ["I had rather enjoy my own mind than the fortune of another man.", "엄숙히 단언하건대, 만일 그분이 저를 위해 조카에게 손해를 끼치는 일을 하셨다면, 저는 그것을 도로 되돌려 놓았을 겁니다."], ["What is the poor pride arising from a magnificent house, a numerous equipage, a splendid table, and from all the other advantages or appearances of fortune, compared to the warm, solid content, the swelling satisfaction, the thrilling transports, and the exulting triumphs, which a good mind enjoys, in the contemplation of a generous, virtuous, noble, benevolent action?", "저는 남의 재산을 누리느니 차라리 제 마음을 누리는 편을 택하겠습니다. 웅장한 저택, 수많은 종복과 마차, 호화로운 식탁, 그 밖의 온갖 재산의 이점이나 겉모습에서 비롯되는 그 초라한 자만심이, 너그럽고 덕스럽고 고귀하고 자애로운 행위를 돌이켜 볼 때 선한 마음이 누리는 그 따뜻하고 단단한 만족, 부풀어 오르는 흡족함, 짜릿한 황홀, 의기양양한 승리감에 견줄 수나 있겠습니까?"], ["I envy not Blifil in the prospect of his wealth; nor shall I envy him in the possession of it.", "저는 블리필이 누릴 재산의 전망을 부러워하지 않으며, 그가 그것을 손에 넣은들 부러워하지 않을 겁니다."], ["I would not think myself a rascal half an hour, to exchange situations.", "처지를 맞바꾸자고 단 반 시간이라도 제 자신을 악당이라 여기고 싶지 않습니다."], ["I believe, indeed, Mr Blifil suspected me of the views you mention; and I suppose these suspicions, as they arose from the baseness of his own heart, so they occasioned his baseness to me.", "사실 블리필 씨는 제가 당신이 말한 그런 속셈을 품었다고 의심한 듯합니다. 그리고 짐작건대 그런 의심은 제 마음이 아니라 그 자신의 비열한 마음에서 비롯된 것이니, 그것이 또한 제게 가한 그자의 비열함의 원인이 되었겠지요."], ["But, I thank Heaven, I know, I feel--I feel my innocence, my friend; and I would not part with that feeling for the world.", "하지만 하늘에 감사하게도, 저는 압니다, 저는 느낍니다—나는 내 결백을 느낍니다, 벗이여. 그리고 저는 그 느낌을 온 세상과도 바꾸지 않겠습니다."], ["For as long as I know I have never done, nor even designed, an injury to any being whatever,", "제가 그 어떤 존재에게도 해를 끼친 적이 없고, 끼치려 마음먹은 적조차 없음을 아는 한 말입니다."]], [["_Pone me pigris ubi nulla campis Arbor aestiva recreatur aura, Quod latus mundi nebulae, malusque Jupiter urget.", "포네 메 피그리스 우비 눌라 캄피스 아르보르 아이스티바 레크레아투르 아우라, 쿼드 라투스 문디 네불라이, 말루스퀘 유피테르 우르겟."]], [["Pone sub curru nimium propinqui Solis in terra dominibus negata; Dulce ridentem Lalagen amabo, Dulce loquentem._[*]", "포네 수브 쿠루 니미움 프로핀퀴 솔리스 인 테라 도미니부스 네가타; 둘체 리덴템 랄라겐 아마보, 둘체 로퀜템.[*]"]], [["[*] Place me where never summer breeze Unbinds the glebe, or warms the trees: Where ever-lowering clouds appear, And angry Jove deforms th'", "[*] 여름 산들바람도 흙을 풀어 주지 않고 나무를 데워 주지 않는 곳에 나를 두어라."], ["inclement year.", "언제나 잔뜩 내려앉은 구름이 드리우고 성난 유피테르가 험한 계절을 망쳐 놓는 그곳에."]], [["Place me beneath the burning ray, Where rolls the rapid car of day; Love and the nymph shall charm my toils, The nymph who sweetly speaks, and sweetly smiles.", "타오르는 햇살 아래 나를 두어라, 대낮의 수레가 빠르게 굴러가는 그곳에. 사랑과 그 처녀가 내 노고를 어루만지리니, 달콤하게 말하고 달콤하게 미소 짓는 그 처녀가."], ["MR FRANCIS.", "프랜시스 씨."]], [["He then filled a bumper of wine, and drunk it off to the health of his dear Lalage; and, filling Dowling's glass likewise up to the brim, insisted on his pledging him.", "그러고 나서 그는 포도주를 잔 가득 채워, 사랑하는 랄라게의 건강을 위해 단숨에 들이켰다. 그리고 다울링의 잔도 가장자리까지 가득 채워, 함께 잔을 들이켤 것을 권했다."], ["“Why, then, here's Miss Lalage's health with all my heart,”", "\"그렇다면, 자, 진심으로 랄라게 아가씨의 건강을 위하여,\""], ["cries Dowling.", "다울링이 외쳤다."], ["“I have heard her toasted often, I protest, though I never saw her; but they say she's extremely handsome.”", "\"단언컨대 저는 그분을 위한 건배를 여러 번 들은 적이 있습니다. 직접 뵌 적은 없습니다만, 더없이 아리따운 분이라고들 하더군요.\""]], [["Though the Latin was not the only part of this speech which Dowling did not perfectly understand; yet there was somewhat in it that made a very strong impression upon him.", "이 말 가운데 다울링이 온전히 알아듣지 못한 부분이 라틴어만은 아니었다. 그럼에도 그 안에는 그에게 아주 강한 인상을 남긴 무언가가 있었다."], ["And though he endeavoured by winking, nodding, sneering, and grinning, to hide the impression from Jones (for we are as often ashamed of thinking right as of thinking wrong), it is certain he secretly approved as much of his sentiments as he understood, and really felt a very strong impulse of compassion for him.", "그리고 그는 눈을 찡긋거리고, 고개를 끄덕이고, 비웃는 시늉을 하고, 히죽거리며 그 인상을 존스에게 감추려 애썼지만(우리는 그릇되게 생각할 때만큼이나 바르게 생각할 때에도 자주 부끄러워하는 법이니), 분명한 것은 그가 알아들은 만큼의 그 마음만은 속으로 깊이 수긍했고, 실로 그에게 강한 연민의 충동을 느꼈다는 사실이다. 하지만 이에 대해서는 다른 기회에 논평할 수도 있을 것이다."], ["But we may possibly take some other opportunity of commenting upon this, especially if we should happen to meet Mr Dowling any more in the course of our history.", "특히 우리가 이야기를 풀어 가는 동안 다울링 씨를 다시 만나게 된다면 말이다. 지금으로서는 존스 씨를 본받아, 그 신사와 다소 갑작스럽게 작별할 수밖에 없다."], ["At present we are obliged to take our leave of that gentleman a little abruptly, in imitation of Mr Jones; who was no sooner informed, by Partridge, that his horses were ready, than he deposited his reckoning, wished his companion a good night, mounted, and set forward towards Coventry, though the night was dark, and it just then began to rain very hard.", "존스는 패트리지에게서 말이 준비되었다는 말을 듣자마자 셈을 치르고, 동행에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넨 뒤 말에 올라 코번트리를 향해 길을 나섰다. 밤은 어두웠고, 바로 그때 비가 몹시 거세게 내리기 시작했는데도 말이다."]], [["# Chapter xi.", "# Chapter xi."]], [["The disasters which befel Jones on his departure for Coventry; with the sage remarks of Partridge.", "제일런에게는 코번트리로 가는 길보다 더 분명한 길이 없었다."], ["No road can be plainer than that from the place where they now were to Coventry; and though neither Jones, nor Partridge, nor the guide, had ever travelled it before, it would have been almost impossible to have missed their way, had it not been for the two reasons mentioned in the conclusion of the last chapter.", "존스도, 파트리지도, 안내인도 그 길을 가 본 적은 없었지만, 앞 장의 끝에서 말한 두 가지 이유만 아니었다면 길을 잃기란 거의 불가능했을 것이다."]], [["These two circumstances, however, happening both unfortunately to intervene, our travellers deviated into a much less frequented track; and after riding full six miles, instead of arriving at the stately spires of Coventry, they found themselves still in a very dirty lane, where they saw no symptoms of approaching the suburbs of a large city.", "그러나 불운하게도 이 두 가지 사정이 한꺼번에 끼어드는 바람에, 우리의 여행자들은 훨씬 사람의 발길이 드문 샛길로 빠지고 말았다. 그렇게 꼬박 육 마일을 달렸건만, 그들은 코번트리의 웅장한 첨탑에 다다르기는커녕 여전히 몹시 질척한 좁은 길 위에 있었고, 큰 도시의 변두리로 가까이 가고 있다는 어떤 기미도 보이지 않았다."]], [["Jones now declared that they must certainly have lost their way; but this the guide insisted upon was impossible; a word which, in common conversation, is often used to signify not only improbable, but often what is really very likely, and, sometimes, what hath certainly happened; an hyperbolical violence like that which is so frequently offered to the words infinite and eternal; by the former of which it is usual to express a distance of half a yard, and by the latter, a duration of five minutes. And thus it is as usual to assert the impossibility of losing what is already actually lost. This was, in fact, the case at present; for, notwithstanding all the confident assertions of the lad to the contrary, it is certain they were no more in the right road to Coventry, than the fraudulent, griping, cruel, canting miser is in the right road to heaven.", "이제 존스는 자기들이 분명 길을 잃었다고 단언했다. 그러나 안내인은 그럴 리 없다고 우겼다. \"그럴 리 없다\"는 말은 일상 대화에서 단지 일어날 법하지 않다는 뜻뿐 아니라, 흔히 실제로는 매우 그럼직한 일을, 때로는 이미 확실히 일어난 일까지도 가리키는 데 쓰인다. 이는 \"무한하다\"느니 \"영원하다\"느니 하는 말에 그토록 자주 가해지는 것과 같은 과장된 폭력이다. 앞의 말로는 흔히 반 야드의 거리를 나타내고, 뒤의 말로는 오 분의 길이를 나타내곤 하니 말이다. 그러니 이미 실제로 잃어버린 것을 두고 잃어버리기란 불가능하다고 우기는 것도 그만큼 흔한 일이다. 사실 지금이 바로 그런 경우였다. 소년이 아무리 자신만만하게 그 반대를 주장해도, 그들이 코번트리로 가는 옳은 길에 있는 것은, 사기 잘 치고 인색하며 잔인하고 위선적으로 입에 발린 말만 하는 구두쇠가 천국으로 가는 옳은 길에 있는 것만큼이나 어림없는 일이었다."]], [["It is not, perhaps, easy for a reader, who hath never been in those circumstances, to imagine the horror with which darkness, rain, and wind, fill persons who have lost their way in the night; and who, consequently, have not the pleasant prospect of warm fires, dry cloaths, and other refreshments, to support their minds in struggling with the inclemencies of the weather.", "이런 처지에 한 번도 놓여 본 적 없는 독자가, 밤중에 길을 잃은 사람들을 어둠과 비와 바람이 어떤 공포로 채우는지 상상하기란 아마 쉽지 않을 것이다. 그런 사람들에게는 따뜻한 불, 마른 옷, 그 밖의 위안거리가 마음을 받쳐 주며 모진 날씨와 싸우게 해 줄 흐뭇한 전망조차 없으니 말이다."], ["A very imperfect idea of this horror will, however, serve sufficiently to account for the conceits which now filled the head of Partridge, and which we shall presently be obliged to open.", "그러나 이 공포를 아주 어렴풋이만 떠올려 보아도, 지금 파트리지의 머릿속을 가득 채운 망상들을 설명하기에는 충분할 것이다. 우리는 이제 곧 그 망상을 펼쳐 보일 수밖에 없다."]], [["Jones grew more and more positive that they were out of their road; and the boy himself at last acknowledged he believed they were not in the right road to Coventry; though he affirmed, at the same time, it was impossible they should have mist the way. But Partridge was of a different opinion. He said, “When they first set out he imagined some mischief or other would happen.--Did not you observe, sir,” said he to Jones, “that old woman who stood at the door just as you was taking horse? I wish you had given her a small matter, with all my heart; for she said then you might repent it; and at that very instant it began to rain, and the wind hath continued rising ever since. Whatever some people may think, I am very certain it is in the power of witches to raise the wind whenever they please. I have seen it happen very often in my time: and if ever I saw a witch in all my life, that old woman was certainly one. I thought so to myself at that very time; and if I had had any halfpence in my pocket, I would have given her some; for to be sure it is always good to be charitable to those sort of people, for fear what may happen; and many a person hath lost his cattle by saving a halfpenny.”", "존스는 자기들이 길을 벗어났다고 점점 더 확신했다. 소년 자신도 마침내 코번트리로 가는 옳은 길에 있는 것 같지 않다고 인정했다. 그러면서도 동시에, 자기들이 길을 잘못 들었을 리는 없다고 우겼다. 그러나 파트리지의 생각은 달랐다. 그가 말했다. \"처음 길을 나설 때부터 저는 무슨 화든 닥칠 거라 짐작했습죠. 나리, 못 보셨습니까,\" 그가 존스에게 말했다, \"나리가 말에 오르실 바로 그때 문가에 서 있던 그 늙은 노파 말입니다. 정말이지 진심으로, 나리가 그 노파에게 푼돈이라도 좀 쥐여 주셨더라면 좋았을 것을요. 그때 노파가 후회하실 거라고 하지 않았습니까. 바로 그 순간 비가 내리기 시작했고, 바람은 그때부터 줄곧 거세지기만 했습죠. 어떤 사람들이야 뭐라 생각하든, 저는 마녀들이 마음만 먹으면 언제든 바람을 일으킬 힘이 있다고 굳게 믿습니다. 저는 살아오면서 그런 일을 아주 여러 번 보았습죠. 그리고 제 평생 마녀를 본 적이 있다면, 그 늙은 노파야말로 분명 마녀였습니다. 바로 그때 속으로 그렇게 생각했고, 만일 제 주머니에 반 페니라도 있었다면 좀 줬을 겁니다. 그런 부류의 사람들에게는 무슨 일이 생길지 모르니 늘 자비를 베푸는 게 좋은 법이거든요. 반 페니 아끼려다 가축을 잃은 사람이 한둘이 아닙니다.\""]], [["Jones, though he was horridly vexed at the delay which this mistake was likely to occasion in his journey, could not help smiling at the superstition of his friend, whom an accident now greatly confirmed in his opinion.", "존스는 이 실수가 여정에 가져올 지체에 끔찍이도 속이 상했지만, 친구의 미신을 보고는 웃음을 참을 수 없었다. 그런데 마침 한 사고가 일어나 파트리지의 믿음을 크게 굳혀 주었다."], ["This was a tumble from his horse; by which, however, he received no other injury than what the dirt conferred on his cloaths.", "그것은 그가 말에서 굴러떨어진 일이었는데, 다행히 옷이 진흙을 뒤집어쓴 것 말고는 아무 부상도 입지 않았다."]], [["Partridge had no sooner recovered his legs, than he appealed to his fall, as conclusive evidence of all he had asserted; but Jones finding he was unhurt, answered with a smile: “This witch of yours, Partridge, is a most ungrateful jade, and doth not, I find, distinguish her friends from others in her resentment. If the old lady had been angry with me for neglecting her, I don't see why she should tumble you from your horse, after all the respect you have expressed for her.”", "파트리지는 다리를 추스르기 무섭게, 자기가 넘어진 일이야말로 그동안 자기가 주장한 모든 것의 결정적 증거라며 들이댔다. 그러나 존스는 그가 다치지 않은 것을 보고 웃으며 대답했다. \"파트리지, 자네의 그 마녀란 게 참으로 배은망덕한 계집이로군. 보아하니 원한을 풀 때 제 편과 남을 가리지도 않으니 말이야. 그 노파가 자기를 무시했다고 나한테 화가 났다면, 자네가 그토록 깍듯이 떠받들었는데도 자네를 말에서 떨어뜨릴 까닭이 어디 있겠나.\""]], [["“It is ill jesting,” cries Partridge, “with people who have power to do these things; for they are often very malicious. I remember a farrier, who provoked one of them, by asking her when the time she had bargained with the devil for would be out; and within three months from that very day one of his best cows was drowned. Nor was she satisfied with that; for a little time afterwards he lost a barrel of best-drink: for the old witch pulled out the spigot, and let it run all over the cellar, the very first evening he had tapped it to make merry with some of his neighbours. In short, nothing ever thrived with him afterwards; for she worried the poor man so, that he took to drinking; and in a year or two his stock was seized, and he and his family are now come to the parish.”", "\"이런 일을 할 힘을 가진 사람들을 두고 농을 치는 건 좋지 않습니다,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"그런 자들은 흔히 아주 심술궂거든요. 어떤 편자장이가 마녀 하나를 화나게 한 일이 기억납니다. 악마와 흥정해 둔 기한이 언제 끝나느냐고 물어서 약을 올렸지요. 그런데 바로 그날부터 석 달도 채 안 되어 그의 가장 좋은 암소 한 마리가 물에 빠져 죽었습죠. 마녀는 그것으로도 성에 차지 않았습니다. 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 최고급 술 한 통을 잃었거든요. 그가 이웃 몇과 흥을 돋우려고 술통의 마개를 딴 바로 그 첫날 저녁에, 늙은 마녀가 그 꼭지를 뽑아 버려 술이 지하실 바닥에 죄다 흘러내리게 했으니까요. 한마디로 그 후로는 그에게 잘되는 일이 하나도 없었습죠. 마녀가 그 가엾은 사람을 어찌나 들들 볶았는지 그는 술독에 빠졌고, 한두 해 만에 가산을 압류당해 지금은 그와 그 식구들이 교구 구호 신세를 지고 있답니다.\""]], [["The guide, and perhaps his horse too, were both so attentive to this discourse, that, either through want of care, or by the malice of the witch, they were now both sprawling in the dirt.", "안내인은, 그리고 어쩌면 그의 말까지도 이 이야기에 어찌나 정신이 팔렸던지, 부주의 탓이든 마녀의 심술 탓이든 이제 둘 다 진흙탕 속에 큰대자로 나뒹굴고 말았다."]], [["Partridge entirely imputed this fall, as he had done his own, to the same cause.", "파트리지는 자기가 넘어졌을 때와 마찬가지로, 이 추락도 전적으로 같은 원인 탓으로 돌렸다. 그는 존스에게 말했다."], ["He told Mr Jones, “It would certainly be his turn next; and earnestly entreated him to return back, and find out the old woman, and pacify her.", "\"이제 분명 다음은 나리 차례일 겁니다.\" 그러면서 부디 되돌아가 그 노파를 찾아내 달래자고 간곡히 청했다."], ["We shall very soon,”", "\"곧 여관에 닿을 겁니다,\" 그가 덧붙였다."], ["added he, “reach the inn; for though we have seemed to go forward, I am very certain we are in the identical place in which we were an hour ago; and I dare swear, if it was daylight, we might now see the inn we set out from.”", "\"우리가 앞으로 나아온 것 같지만, 저는 한 시간 전에 있던 바로 그 자리에 우리가 여태 있다고 확신합니다. 감히 맹세하건대, 날이 밝았다면 우리가 출발한 그 여관이 지금 보일 겁니다.\""]], [["Instead of returning any answer to this sage advice, Jones was entirely attentive to what had happened to the boy, who received no other hurt than what had before befallen Partridge, and which his cloaths very easily bore, as they had been for many years inured to the like.", "이 현명한 충고에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않은 채, 존스는 오로지 소년에게 일어난 일에만 정신을 쏟고 있었다. 소년은 앞서 파트리지가 당한 것 말고는 아무 상처도 입지 않았는데, 여러 해 동안 그런 일에 단련되어 온 그의 옷은 그 정도쯤 거뜬히 견뎌 냈다."], ["He soon regained his side-saddle, and by the hearty curses and blows which he bestowed on his horse, quickly satisfied Mr Jones that no harm was done.", "소년은 곧 자기 안장에 다시 올라앉았고, 자기 말에게 호되게 욕설을 퍼붓고 매질을 가하는 것으로, 아무 탈도 없음을 존스에게 금세 납득시켰다."]], [["# Chapter xii.", "# Chapter xii."]], [["Relates that Mr Jones continued his journey, contrary to the advice of Partridge, with what happened on that occasion.", "존스 씨가 파트리지의 충고를 거스르고 여행을 계속한 일과, 그 자리에서 벌어진 일을 이야기하다."]], [["They now discovered a light at some distance, to the great pleasure of Jones, and to the no small terror of Partridge, who firmly believed himself to be bewitched, and that this light was a Jack-with-a-lantern, or somewhat more mischievous.", "이윽고 두 사람은 멀찍이서 불빛 하나를 발견했는데, 이는 존스에게는 더없는 기쁨이었으나 파트리지에게는 적지 않은 공포였다. 그는 자신이 마법에 걸렸다고 굳게 믿고 있었고, 저 불빛이야말로 도깨비불이거나 어쩌면 그보다 더 짓궂은 무언가라고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["But how were these fears increased, when, as they approached nearer to this light (or lights as they now appeared), they heard a confused sound of human voices; of singing, laughing, and hallowing, together with a strange noise that seemed to proceed from some instruments; but could hardly be allowed the name of music!", "그런데 이 불빛(이제는 여러 개의 불빛으로 보였다)에 점점 다가가면서 사람 목소리가 뒤섞인 소리, 노래하고 웃고 떠드는 소리가, 게다가 어떤 악기에서 나는 듯하면서도 도무지 음악이라 부르기 어려운 기묘한 소음까지 들려오자 그의 두려움이 얼마나 더 커졌겠는가!"], ["indeed, to favour a little the opinion of Partridge, it might very well be called music bewitched.", "실로 파트리지의 견해에 조금이나마 힘을 실어 주자면, 그것은 마법에 걸린 음악이라 불러도 좋을 만했다."]], [["It is impossible to conceive a much greater degree of horror than what now seized on Partridge; the contagion of which had reached the post-boy, who had been very attentive to many things that the other had uttered.", "지금 파트리지를 사로잡은 것보다 더한 공포는 상상하기 어려웠다. 그 공포의 전염은 마부에게까지 번졌으니, 마부는 파트리지가 내뱉은 여러 말을 줄곧 귀 기울여 듣고 있던 터였다."], ["He now, therefore, joined in petitioning Jones to return; saying he firmly believed what Partridge had just before said, that though the horses seemed to go on, they had not moved a step forwards during at least the last half-hour.", "이제 그도 존스에게 돌아가자고 함께 간청하며, 조금 전 파트리지가 한 말, 곧 말들이 앞으로 나아가는 듯 보여도 적어도 지난 반 시간 동안 단 한 걸음도 앞으로 움직이지 않았다는 말을 굳게 믿는다고 했다."]], [["Jones could not help smiling in the midst of his vexation, at the fears of these poor fellows.", "존스는 짜증이 치미는 와중에도 이 가엾은 친구들의 두려움에 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었다. \"우리가 저 불빛을 향해 나아가고 있든지,\""], ["“Either we advance,”", "그가 말했다. \"아니면 저 불빛이 우리를 향해 다가왔든지 둘 중 하나일세."], ["says he, “towards the lights, or the lights have advanced towards us; for we are now at a very little distance from them; but how can either of you be afraid of a set of people who appear only to be merry-making?”", "이제 우리는 저것들과 아주 가까운 거리에 있으니 말이야. 그런데 그저 흥겹게 잔치를 벌이는 듯한 사람들을 보고 자네들 둘이 어찌 무서워할 수 있단 말인가?\""]], [["“Merry-making, sir!”", "\"잔치라니요, 나리!\""], ["cries Partridge; “who could be merry-making at this time of night, and in such a place, and such weather?", "파트리지가 외쳤다. \"이런 한밤중에, 이런 곳에서, 이런 날씨에 누가 흥겨운 잔치를 벌이겠습니까?"], ["They can be nothing but ghosts or witches, or some evil spirits or other, that's certain.”", "저것들은 유령이나 마녀, 아니면 무슨 악령 같은 것일 수밖에 없습니다. 그건 분명해요.\""]], [["“Let them be what they will,”", "\"그것들이 무엇이든 간에,\""], ["cries Jones, “I am resolved to go up to them, and enquire the way to Coventry.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"나는 저들에게 다가가 코번트리로 가는 길을 물어보기로 마음먹었네."], ["All witches, Partridge, are not such ill-natured hags as that we had the misfortune to meet with last.”", "파트리지, 모든 마녀가 우리가 불행히도 지난번에 만난 그 노파처럼 심술궂은 것은 아닐세.\""]], [["“O Lord, sir,” cries Partridge, “there is no knowing what humour they will be in; to be sure it is always best to be civil to them; but what if we should meet with something worse than witches, with evil spirits themselves?----Pray, sir, be advised; pray, sir, do. If you had read so many terrible accounts as I have of these matters, you would not be so fool-hardy.----The Lord knows whither we have got already, or whither we are going; for sure such darkness was never seen upon earth, and I question whether it can be darker in the other world.”", "\"오 맙소사, 나리,\" 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"저들이 어떤 기분일지는 알 수가 없습니다. 물론 저들에게 공손하게 구는 것이 언제나 상책이지요. 하지만 만일 우리가 마녀보다 더 고약한 것, 바로 악령 그 자체를 만나기라도 하면 어쩝니까?——제발 나리, 제 말을 들으십시오. 제발요, 나리. 만일 나리께서 제가 이런 일들에 관해 읽은 그 숱한 끔찍한 이야기들을 읽으셨다면, 이렇게 무모하게 굴지는 않으실 겁니다.——주님만이 우리가 이미 어디까지 왔는지, 또 어디로 가고 있는지 아실 겁니다. 정말이지 이 땅 위에 이런 어둠은 일찍이 없었으니, 저세상이라 한들 이보다 더 어두울 수 있을지 의심스럽습니다.\""]], [["Jones put forwards as fast as he could, notwithstanding all these hints and cautions, and poor Partridge was obliged to follow; for though he hardly dared to advance, he dared still less to stay behind by himself.", "이 모든 암시와 경고에도 아랑곳없이 존스는 할 수 있는 한 빨리 앞으로 나아갔고, 가엾은 파트리지도 따라갈 수밖에 없었다. 앞으로 나아가기란 거의 엄두도 못 냈으나, 혼자 뒤에 처지는 것은 그보다 더 엄두가 나지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["At length they arrived at the place whence the lights and different noises had issued.", "마침내 그들은 불빛과 갖가지 소리가 새어 나오던 그곳에 다다랐다."], ["This Jones perceived to be no other than a barn, where a great number of men and women were assembled, and diverting themselves with much apparent jollity.", "존스가 보니 그곳은 다름 아닌 헛간이었는데, 많은 남녀가 모여서 겉보기에도 무척 흥겹게 놀고 있었다."]], [["Jones no sooner appeared before the great doors of the barn, which were open, than a masculine and very rough voice from within demanded, who was there?--To which Jones gently answered, a friend; and immediately asked the road to Coventry.", "존스가 활짝 열린 헛간의 큰 문 앞에 모습을 드러내자마자, 안쪽에서 사내답고도 몹시 거친 목소리가 거기 누구냐고 물었다.——이에 존스는 부드럽게 친구라고 답하고는 곧바로 코번트리로 가는 길을 물었다."]], [["“If you are a friend,”", "\"친구라면,\""], ["cries another of the men in the barn, “you had better alight till the storm is over”", "헛간 안의 다른 사내가 외쳤다. \"폭풍이 그칠 때까지 말에서 내리는 게 좋겠소.\""], ["(for indeed it was now more violent than ever;) “you are very welcome to put up your horse; for there is sufficient room for him at the end of the barn.”", "(실로 폭풍은 그 어느 때보다 거세졌던 것이다.) \"말은 얼마든지 들여놓으셔도 좋소. 헛간 끝에 그 녀석이 들어갈 자리가 넉넉하니 말이오.\""]], [["“You are very obliging,” returned Jones; “and I will accept your offer for a few minutes, whilst the rain continues; and here are two more who will be glad of the same favour.” This was accorded with more good-will than it was accepted: for Partridge would rather have submitted to the utmost inclemency of the weather than have trusted to the clemency of those whom he took for hobgoblins; and the poor post-boy was now infected with the same apprehensions; but they were both obliged to follow the example of Jones; the one because he durst not leave his horse, and the other because he feared nothing so much as being left by himself.", "\"참으로 친절하시군요.\" 존스가 답했다. \"비가 계속되는 동안 잠깐 그 호의를 받아들이겠소. 그리고 여기 같은 호의를 반길 두 사람이 더 있소.\" 이 제안은 받아들여진 것보다 더 흔쾌히 베풀어진 것이었다. 파트리지로서는 자신이 도깨비라고 여긴 자들의 너그러움에 몸을 맡기느니 차라리 날씨의 모진 매서움에 굴복하는 편이 나았을 테고, 가엾은 마부도 이제 같은 불안에 물들어 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 두 사람 모두 존스의 본을 따를 수밖에 없었다. 한쪽은 말을 두고 갈 엄두가 나지 않아서였고, 다른 쪽은 혼자 남겨지는 것만큼 두려운 일이 없어서였다."]], [["Had this history been writ in the days of superstition, I should have had too much compassion for the reader to have left him so long in suspense, whether Beelzebub or Satan was about actually to appear in person, with all his hellish retinue; but as these doctrines are at present very unfortunate, and have but few, if any believers, I have not been much aware of conveying any such terrors.", "이 이야기가 미신이 횡행하던 시절에 쓰였더라면, 나는 독자를 너무도 가엾이 여긴 나머지, 바알세불이나 사탄이 그 온갖 지옥의 무리를 거느리고 정말로 친히 모습을 드러내려는지를 두고 그토록 오래 마음 졸이게 두지는 않았을 것이다. 하지만 이런 교리들은 요즘 들어 무척이나 운이 다하여, 믿는 이가 있다 한들 거의 없는 형편이니, 나는 그런 공포를 전할 마음을 별로 두지 않았다."], ["To say truth, the whole furniture of the infernal regions hath long been appropriated by the managers of playhouses, who seem lately to have laid them by as rubbish, capable only of affecting the upper gallery; a place in which few of our readers ever sit.", "사실대로 말하자면, 지옥 영역의 온갖 소품은 진작에 극장 운영자들이 가져다 써 버렸는데, 그들마저 요즘은 그것들을 위층 맨 꼭대기 관람석이나 흔들어 놓을 만한 쓰레기로 여겨 한쪽에 처박아 둔 듯하다. 그런데 그 자리에는 우리 독자 가운데 앉는 이가 거의 없다."]], [["However, though we do not suspect raising any great terror on this occasion, we have reason to fear some other apprehensions may here arise in our reader, into which we would not willingly betray him; I mean that we are going to take a voyage into fairy-land, and introduce a set of beings into our history, which scarce any one was ever childish enough to believe, though many have been foolish enough to spend their time in writing and reading their adventures.", "그러나 이번 일로 굳이 큰 공포를 불러일으킬 생각은 없다 해도, 우리는 독자에게 다른 어떤 우려가 여기서 생겨날까 염려할 까닭이 있는데, 우리는 독자를 기꺼이 그런 우려에 빠뜨리고 싶지 않다. 다시 말해 우리가 요정 나라로 항해를 떠나, 어느 누구도 그토록 어린아이같이 믿은 적이 거의 없는—그러면서도 그들의 모험담을 쓰고 읽는 데 시간을 허비할 만큼 어리석은 이는 많았던—그런 존재의 무리를 우리 이야기 안에 끌어들이려 한다고 여길까 봐 하는 말이다."]], [["To prevent, therefore, any such suspicions, so prejudicial to the credit of an historian, who professes to draw his materials from nature only, we shall now proceed to acquaint the reader who these people were, whose sudden appearance had struck such terrors into Partridge, had more than half frightened the post-boy, and had a little surprized even Mr Jones himself.", "그러므로 오직 자연에서만 소재를 끌어온다고 표방하는 한 사가의 신용에 그토록 해가 될 이런 의심을 막기 위해, 우리는 이제 그 사람들이 누구였는지 독자에게 알려 주기로 하겠다. 그들이 느닷없이 나타나는 바람에 파트리지는 그토록 큰 공포에 사로잡혔고, 마부는 반 넘게 겁에 질렸으며, 심지어 존스 씨조차 조금은 놀랐던 것이다."]], [["The people then assembled in this barn were no other than a company of Egyptians, or, as they are vulgarly called, gypsies, and they were now celebrating the wedding of one of their society.", "그렇다면 이 헛간에 모인 사람들은 다름 아닌 이집트인 한 무리, 흔히들 집시라 부르는 사람들이었고, 그들은 지금 자기네 무리 가운데 한 사람의 결혼식을 축하하고 있는 참이었다."]], [["It is impossible to conceive a happier set of people than appeared here to be met together.", "여기 한자리에 모인 이들보다 더 행복한 무리는 상상하기 어려웠다."], ["The utmost mirth, indeed, shewed itself in every countenance; nor was their ball totally void of all order and decorum.", "실로 더없는 흥겨움이 얼굴마다 드러나 있었다. 그렇다고 그들의 무도회에 질서와 예법이 통째로 빠져 있는 것도 아니었다."], ["Perhaps it had more than a country assembly is sometimes conducted with: for these people are subject to a formal government and laws of their own, and all pay obedience to one great magistrate, whom they call their king.", "어쩌면 시골 모임이 흔히 치러지는 것보다 더 갖추어져 있었는지도 모른다. 이 사람들은 자기네 나름의 격식 있는 통치와 법에 따르고 있었고, 모두가 한 사람의 위대한 통치자에게 복종을 바쳤는데, 그들은 그를 왕이라 불렀다."]], [["Greater plenty, likewise, was nowhere to be seen than what flourished in this barn.", "또한 이 헛간에 넘쳐나던 만큼 풍성한 광경도 어디서고 보기 어려웠다."], ["Here was indeed no nicety nor elegance, nor did the keen appetite of the guests require any.", "여기에는 실로 정교함도 우아함도 없었으나, 손님들의 날선 식욕은 그런 것을 필요로 하지도 않았다."], ["Here was good store of bacon, fowls, and mutton, to which every one present provided better sauce himself than the best and dearest French cook can prepare.", "여기에는 베이컨과 가금과 양고기가 푸짐하게 갖추어져 있었고, 자리에 있는 누구나 가장 솜씨 좋고 값비싼 프랑스 요리사가 만들 수 있는 것보다 더 나은 양념을 제 스스로 마련해 두고 있었다."]], [["Aeneas is not described under more consternation in the temple of Juno,", "아이네이아스가 유노 신전에서 보였던 것보다 더한 경악에 휩싸여 있다고 묘사되지는 않았으니,"]], [["Dum stupet obtutuque haeret defixus in uno,", "망연자실하여 한곳에 시선을 못 박은 채 멍하니 서 있다,"]], [["than was our heroe at what he saw in this barn.", "우리 주인공이 이 헛간에서 본 것에 사로잡힌 모습이야말로 그러했다."], ["While he was looking everywhere round him with astonishment, a venerable person approached him with many friendly salutations, rather of too hearty a kind to be called courtly.", "그가 놀라움에 사방을 둘러보고 있을 때, 한 위엄 있는 인물이 정중하다 하기엔 다소 지나치게 푸근한 인사를 잔뜩 건네며 그에게 다가왔다. 이 사람이 바로 다름 아닌 집시들의 왕이었다."], ["This was no other than the king of the gypsies himself.", "그는 차림새로는 신하들과 거의 구별되지 않았고, 위엄을 떠받칠 만한 왕권의 표장도 지니지 않았다."], ["He was very little distinguished in dress from his subjects, nor had he any regalia of majesty to support his dignity; and yet there seemed (as Mr Jones said) to be somewhat in his air which denoted authority, and inspired the beholders with an idea of awe and respect; though all this was perhaps imaginary in Jones; and the truth may be, that such ideas are incident to power, and almost inseparable from it.", "그런데도 (존스 씨의 말로는) 그의 풍모에는 권위를 나타내고 보는 이에게 경외와 존경의 관념을 불어넣는 무언가가 있는 듯했다. 물론 이 모든 것이 어쩌면 존스의 상상에 지나지 않았을 수도 있고, 실은 그런 관념이란 권력에 으레 따라붙는 것이어서 권력과 거의 떼어 놓을 수 없는 것인지도 모른다."]], [["There was somewhat in the open countenance and courteous behaviour of Jones which, being accompanied with much comeliness of person, greatly recommended him at first sight to every beholder.", "존스의 트인 얼굴빛과 정중한 몸가짐에는 무언가가 있어서, 거기에 빼어난 풍채까지 어우러지니 첫눈에 보는 이마다 그에게 크게 호감을 품게 만들었다."], ["These were, perhaps, a little heightened in the present instance, by that profound respect which he paid to the king of the gypsies, the moment he was acquainted with his dignity, and which was the sweeter to his gypseian majesty, as he was not used to receive such homage from any but his own subjects.", "이번 경우에는 이런 점들이 어쩌면 조금 더 돋보였을 텐데, 그가 집시들의 왕에게 그 신분을 알게 된 순간 바친 깊은 존경 덕분이었다. 또한 그 존경은 집시 왕에게 한결 더 달콤했으니, 자기 신하 말고는 누구에게서도 그런 경의를 받아 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."]], [["The king ordered a table to be spread with the choicest of their provisions for his accommodation; and, having placed himself at his right hand, his majesty began to discourse with our heroe in the following manner:--", "왕은 그를 대접하려고 자기네 음식 가운데 가장 좋은 것으로 식탁을 차리라 명했다. 그러고는 그의 오른편에 자리를 잡고서, 폐하께서 우리 주인공에게 다음과 같이 이야기를 건네기 시작했다.——"]], [["“Me doubt not, sir, but you have often seen some of my people, who are what you call de parties detache: for dey go about everywhere; but me fancy you imagine not we be so considrable body as we be; and may be you will be surprize more when you hear de gypsy be as orderly and well govern people as any upon face of de earth.", "\"손님, 내 백성 가운데 몇몇을 자주 보셨을 게요, 그대들 말로 척후대라 부르는 자들 말이오. 그들은 어디든 돌아다니니까 말이오. 하지만 우리가 이만큼 대단한 무리인 줄은 미처 상상 못 하셨을 게요. 그리고 집시가 이 땅 위의 어느 백성 못지않게 질서 있고 잘 다스려지는 백성이라는 걸 들으면 더욱 놀라실지도 모르겠소."]], [["“Me have honour, as me say, to be deir king, and no monarch can do boast of more dutiful subject, ne no more affectionate.", "\"내가 말했듯, 나는 그들의 왕인 영예를 누리고 있는데, 어떤 군주도 이보다 더 충실하고 더 정 깊은 백성을 자랑할 수는 없을 게요. 내가 그들의 호의를 얼마나 받을 만한지는 말하지 않겠소."], ["How far me deserve deir good-will, me no say; but dis me can say, dat me never design anyting but to do dem good.", "다만 이 한 가지는 말할 수 있소. 나는 그들에게 좋은 일을 해 주는 것 말고는 결코 다른 무엇도 꾀한 적이 없다는 거요."], ["Me sall no do boast of dat neider: for what can me do oderwise dan consider of de good of dose poor people who go about all day to give me always de best of what dey get.", "하지만 그것조차 자랑하지는 않겠소. 종일 돌아다니며 자기들이 얻은 것 가운데 늘 가장 좋은 것을 내게 가져다주는 저 가엾은 백성의 행복을 헤아리는 것 말고, 내가 달리 무엇을 할 수 있겠소?"], ["Dey love and honour me darefore, because me do love and take care of dem; dat is all, me know no oder reason.", "그러니 그들은 나를 사랑하고 받드는 거요, 내가 그들을 사랑하고 보살피기 때문에 말이오. 그게 다요, 나는 다른 까닭을 알지 못하오."]], [["“About a tousand or two tousand year ago, me cannot tell to a year or two, as can neider write nor read, dere was a great what you call--a volution among de gypsy; for dere was de lord gypsy in dose days; and dese lord did quarrel vid one anoder about de place; but de king of de gypsy did demolish dem all, and made all his subject equal vid each oder; and since dat time dey have agree very well; for dey no tink of being king, and may be it be better for dem as dey be; for me assure you it be ver troublesome ting to be king, and always to do justice; me have often wish to be de private gypsy when me have been forced to punish my dear friend and relation; for dough we never put to death, our punishments be ver severe. Dey make de gypsy ashamed of demselves, and dat be ver terrible punishment; me ave scarce ever known de gypsy so punish do harm any more.”", "\"한 천 년인지 이천 년인지 전에—나는 글을 쓸 줄도 읽을 줄도 모르니 한두 해까지는 가릴 수 없소만—집시들 사이에 그대들 말로 큰 무어라 하던가, 그래 혁명이 있었소. 그 시절엔 집시 영주라는 게 있었거든. 그 영주들이 자리를 두고 서로 다투었지. 그런데 집시 왕이 그들을 모조리 무너뜨리고 모든 백성을 서로 평등하게 만들었소. 그 뒤로 그들은 아주 화목하게 지냈소. 왕이 될 생각을 하지 않으니 말이오. 어쩌면 지금 이대로가 그들에게 더 나을 게요. 내 장담하건대 왕 노릇을 하며 늘 공정함을 베푼다는 건 무척 골치 아픈 일이오. 내 사랑하는 벗이며 친척을 어쩔 수 없이 벌해야 할 때면, 나는 차라리 한낱 평범한 집시였으면 하고 자주 바랐소. 우리는 결코 사형에 처하지는 않지만, 우리 형벌은 무척 가혹하거든. 그것은 집시로 하여금 제 자신을 부끄럽게 만드는데, 그게 바로 끔찍한 형벌이오. 그렇게 벌받은 집시가 다시 잘못을 저지르는 건 내 일찍이 거의 본 적이 없소.\""]], [["The king then proceeded to express some wonder that there was no such punishment as shame in other governments.", "이어 왕은 다른 나라의 통치에는 수치 같은 형벌이 없다는 데에 자못 놀라움을 드러냈다. 이에 존스는 그렇지 않다고 그를 일깨워 주었다."], ["Upon which Jones assured him to the contrary; for that there were many crimes for which shame was inflicted by the English laws, and that it was indeed one consequence of all punishment.", "영국 법으로도 수치를 형벌로 내리는 죄가 많고, 실로 수치는 모든 형벌에 따르는 한 가지 결과라는 것이었다. \"그것참 이상하구려,\""], ["“Dat be ver strange,”", "왕이 말했다."], ["said the king; “for me know and hears good deal of your people, dough me no live among dem; and me have often hear dat sham is de consequence and de cause too of many of your rewards.", "\"나는 그대 백성들 틈에 살지는 않지만 그들에 대해 잘 알고 또 많이 듣소. 그런데 수치란 그대들이 내리는 숱한 상의 결과이자 또한 원인이기도 하다는 말을 자주 들었거든."], ["Are your rewards and punishments den de same ting?”", "그렇다면 그대들의 상과 벌은 같은 것이오?\""]], [["While his majesty was thus discoursing with Jones, a sudden uproar arose in the barn, and as it seems upon this occasion:--the courtesy of these people had by degrees removed all the apprehensions of Partridge, and he was prevailed upon not only to stuff himself with their food, but to taste some of their liquors, which by degrees entirely expelled all fear from his composition, and in its stead introduced much more agreeable sensations.", "폐하께서 이렇게 존스와 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 헛간 안에서 별안간 소동이 일었으니, 어찌 된 영문인고 하니 이러했다.——이 사람들의 친절함이 차츰 파트리지의 모든 불안을 걷어 내어, 그는 그들의 음식을 배불리 먹었을 뿐만 아니라 그들의 술까지 조금 맛보도록 구슬려졌던 것이다. 그 술은 차츰 그의 마음에서 두려움을 깡그리 몰아내고, 그 자리에 한결 더 흐뭇한 기분을 들여놓았다."]], [["A young female gypsy, more remarkable for her wit than her beauty, had decoyed the honest fellow aside, pretending to tell his fortune.", "미모보다는 재치로 더 눈에 띄는 한 젊은 집시 여자가 점을 봐 주겠다는 핑계로 이 순진한 사내를 한쪽으로 꾀어냈다."], ["Now, when they were alone together in a remote part of the barn, whether it proceeded from the strong liquor, which is never so apt to inflame inordinate desire as after moderate fatigue; or whether the fair gypsy herself threw aside the delicacy and decency of her sex, and tempted the youth Partridge with express solicitations; but they were discovered in a very improper manner by the husband of the gypsy, who, from jealousy it seems, had kept a watchful eye over his wife, and had dogged her to the place, where he found her in the arms of her gallant.", "그런데 그들이 헛간의 외진 구석에 단둘이 있게 되자—그것이 적당한 피로 뒤에 부절제한 욕정을 부추기는 데 그토록 잘 듣는 독한 술 탓이었는지, 아니면 그 어여쁜 집시 여자 스스로 제 성의 곱고 정숙한 태도를 내던지고 노골적인 수작으로 젊은 파트리지를 꾀어낸 것인지—아무튼 둘은 그 집시 여자의 남편에게 무척이나 망측한 꼴로 발각되고 말았다. 그 남편은 질투심에서였는지 제 아내를 줄곧 눈여겨 지켜보다가 그 자리까지 뒤쫓아 와서, 정부의 품에 안긴 아내를 찾아낸 것이었다."]], [["To the great confusion of Jones, Partridge was now hurried before the king; who heard the accusation, and likewise the culprit's defence, which was indeed very trifling; for the poor fellow was confounded by the plain evidence which appeared against him, and had very little to say for himself. His majesty, then turning towards Jones, said, “Sir, you have hear what dey say; what punishment do you tink your man deserve?”", "존스가 크게 당황한 가운데, 파트리지는 이제 왕 앞으로 황급히 끌려갔다. 왕은 고발을 듣고, 또한 죄인의 변명도 들었는데, 그 변명이란 실로 보잘것없는 것이었다. 가엾은 사내는 제게 불리하게 드러난 명백한 증거에 어쩔 줄 몰라 했고, 스스로 변호할 말이 거의 없었던 것이다. 그러자 폐하께서 존스를 향해 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"손님, 저들이 하는 말을 들으셨을 게요. 그대 사람이 어떤 벌을 받아 마땅하다고 생각하시오?\""]], [["Jones answered, “He was sorry for what had happened, and that Partridge should make the husband all the amends in his power: he said, he had very little money about him at that time;”", "존스가 답하기를, \"일어난 일이 유감스럽고, 파트리지가 그 남편에게 할 수 있는 한 모든 보상을 해야 마땅하다\"고 했다."], ["and, putting his hand into his pocket, offered the fellow a guinea.", "그러면서 \"지금 가진 돈이 무척 적다\"고 말하고는 주머니에 손을 넣어 그 사내에게 1기니를 내밀었다."], ["To which he immediately answered, “He hoped his honour would not think of giving him less than five.”", "이에 사내는 곧바로 \"나리께서 설마 5기니보다 적게 주실 생각은 아니시겠지요\" 하고 답했다."]], [["This sum, after some altercation, was reduced to two; and Jones, having stipulated for the full forgiveness of both Partridge and the wife, was going to pay the money; when his majesty, restraining his hand, turned to the witness and asked him, “At what time he had discovered the criminals?”", "이 액수는 얼마간 실랑이를 거쳐 2기니로 깎였다. 그래서 존스가 파트리지와 그 아내 둘 다에게 온전한 용서를 베푸는 조건을 내걸고 막 돈을 치르려 할 때, 폐하께서 그의 손을 막고는 증인을 향해 돌아서서 물었다."], ["To which he answered, “That he had been desired by the husband to watch the motions of his wife from her first speaking to the stranger, and that he had never lost sight of her afterwards till the crime had been committed.”", "\"그대는 언제 이 죄인들을 발견했소?\" 이에 그는 답하기를, \"남편의 부탁을 받아, 아내가 그 낯선 자에게 처음 말을 걸 때부터 그 거동을 살폈고, 그 뒤로 죄가 저질러질 때까지 줄곧 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다\"고 했다."], ["The king then asked, “if the husband was with him all that time in his lurking-place?”", "왕이 또 물었다. \"그 내내 남편도 그대와 함께 숨어 있었소?\""], ["To which he answered in the affirmative.", "이에 그는 그렇다고 답했다. 그러자 이집트의 폐하께서는 남편을 향해 다음과 같이 말했다."], ["His Egyptian majesty then addressed himself to the husband as follows: “Me be sorry to see any gypsy dat have no more honour dan to sell de honour of his wife for money.", "\"제 아내의 명예를 돈에 팔 만큼 명예심이 없는 집시를 보다니 참으로 딱하오. 그대가 아내를 사랑했다면 이 일을 막았어야지, 아내를 들춰내려고 그녀를 창녀로 만들려 들어서는 안 되었소."], ["If you had de love for your wife, you would have prevented dis matter, and not endeavour to make her de whore dat you might discover her.", "나는 그대에게 한 푼도 주지 말라 명하오. 그대는 상이 아니라 벌을 받아 마땅하니 말이오."], ["Me do order dat you have no money given you, for you deserve punishment, not reward; me do order derefore, dat you be de infamous gypsy, and do wear pair of horns upon your forehead for one month, and dat your wife be called de whore, and pointed at all dat time; for you be de infamous gypsy, but she be no less de infamous whore.”", "그러므로 나는 명하노니, 그대는 수치스러운 집시가 되어 한 달 동안 이마에 뿔 한 쌍을 달고 다니고, 그대 아내는 창녀라 불리며 그동안 내내 손가락질을 받을지어다. 그대는 수치스러운 집시이고, 그녀도 그에 못지않게 수치스러운 창녀이니 말이오.\""]], [["The gypsies immediately proceeded to execute the sentence, and left Jones and Partridge alone with his majesty.", "집시들은 곧바로 그 판결을 집행하러 나섰고, 존스와 파트리지만 폐하와 함께 남겨 두었다."]], [["Jones greatly applauded the justice of the sentence: upon which the king, turning to him, said, “Me believe you be surprize: for me suppose you have ver bad opinion of my people; me suppose you tink us all de tieves.”", "존스는 그 판결의 공정함을 크게 칭송했다. 이에 왕이 그를 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"그대가 놀랐으리라 믿소. 그대는 내 백성을 무척 나쁘게 여길 테니 말이오. 그대는 우리를 모두 도둑이라 여길 테지요.\""]], [["“I must confess, sir,”", "\"솔직히 고백하건대, 폐하,\""], ["said Jones, “I have not heard so favourable an account of them as they seem to deserve.”", "존스가 말했다. \"저는 그들이 받아 마땅해 보이는 것만큼 호의적인 평판을 들어 본 적은 없습니다.\""]], [["“Me vil tell you,”", "\"내 말해 주겠소,\""], ["said the king, “how the difference is between you and us.", "왕이 말했다. \"그대들과 우리가 어떻게 다른지를 말이오."], ["My people rob your people, and your people rob one anoder.”", "내 백성은 그대 백성을 털지만, 그대 백성은 자기들끼리 서로를 터니 말이오.\""]], [["Jones afterwards proceeded very gravely to sing forth the happiness of those subjects who live under such a magistrate.", "그 후 존스는 자못 진지하게, 그런 통치자 아래 사는 백성의 행복을 소리 높여 찬미하기 시작했다."]], [["Indeed their happiness appears to have been so compleat, that we are aware lest some advocate for arbitrary power should hereafter quote the case of those people, as an instance of the great advantages which attend that government above all others.", "실로 그들의 행복이 어찌나 완전해 보였던지, 우리는 훗날 전제 권력을 옹호하는 누군가가 이 사람들의 경우를 끌어다, 그 통치가 다른 무엇보다 우월하게 따라붙는 크나큰 이점의 한 사례로 들지나 않을까 염려스럽다."]], [["And here we will make a concession, which would not perhaps have been expected from us, that no limited form of government is capable of rising to the same degree of perfection, or of producing the same benefits to society, with this.", "그래서 여기서 우리는, 어쩌면 우리에게서 기대하지 못했을 한 가지를 인정하고자 한다. 곧 어떤 제한된 형태의 통치도 이만큼의 완전함에 이르거나 사회에 이만큼의 이로움을 가져다줄 수는 없다는 것이다."], ["Mankind have never been so happy, as when the greatest part of the then known world was under the dominion of a single master; and this state of their felicity continued during the reigns of five successive princes.[*] This was the true aera of the golden age, and the only golden age which ever had any existence, unless in the warm imaginations of the poets, from the expulsion from Eden down to this day.", "인류가 당시 알려진 세계의 가장 큰 부분이 단 한 사람의 주인 아래 있었을 때만큼 행복했던 적은 없었다. 그리고 그 복된 상태는 다섯 군주가 잇따라 다스리는 동안 줄곧 이어졌다.[*] 이때야말로 황금시대의 참된 연대였으니, 에덴에서 쫓겨난 이래 오늘에 이르기까지, 시인들의 따뜻한 상상 속에서가 아니고서는 실제로 존재한 적이 있었던 유일한 황금시대였다."]], [["[*] Nerva, Trajan, Adrian, and the two Antonini.", "[*] 네르바, 트라야누스, 하드리아누스, 그리고 두 안토니누스 황제."]], [["In reality, I know but of one solid objection to absolute monarchy.", "사실 나는 절대 군주제에 대해 단 하나의 묵직한 반론밖에 알지 못한다."], ["The only defect in which excellent constitution seems to be, the difficulty of finding any man adequate to the office of an absolute monarch: for this indispensably requires three qualities very difficult, as it appears from history, to be found in princely natures: first, a sufficient quantity of moderation in the prince, to be contented with all the power which is possible for him to have.", "이 빼어난 정체에 있는 유일한 결함이란, 절대 군주의 직책에 걸맞은 사람을 도무지 찾기 어렵다는 점인 듯하다. 이 직책은 세 가지 자질을 반드시 요구하는데, 역사가 보여 주듯 그것은 군주의 천성에서 좀처럼 찾아보기 어려운 것들이다."], ["2ndly, Enough of wisdom to know his own happiness.", "첫째, 군주에게 자신이 가질 수 있는 온갖 권력으로 만족할 만큼의 충분한 절제."], ["And, 3rdly, Goodness sufficient to support the happiness of others, when not only compatible with, but instrumental to his own.", "둘째, 자기 자신의 행복을 알아볼 만큼의 충분한 지혜. 셋째, 남의 행복이 제 행복과 양립할 뿐 아니라 제 행복에 보탬이 될 때 그것을 떠받쳐 줄 만큼의 충분한 선함이다."]], [["Now if an absolute monarch, with all these great and rare qualifications, should be allowed capable of conferring the greatest good on society; it must be surely granted, on the contrary, that absolute power, vested in the hands of one who is deficient in them all, is likely to be attended with no less a degree of evil.", "자, 만일 이 모든 위대하고 드문 자질을 갖춘 절대 군주가 사회에 더없이 큰 선을 베풀 수 있다고 인정한다면, 반대로 그 자질을 죄다 갖추지 못한 자의 손에 쥐어진 절대 권력은 그에 못지않게 큰 악을 동반하기 쉽다는 것 또한 분명 인정해야 하리라."]], [["In short, our own religion furnishes us with adequate ideas of the blessing, as well as curse, which may attend absolute power.", "요컨대, 우리 자신의 종교가 우리에게 절대 권력에 따를 수 있는 축복과 저주에 대한 충분한 관념을 마련해 준다."], ["The pictures of heaven and of hell will place a very lively image of both before our eyes; for though the prince of the latter can have no power but what he originally derives from the omnipotent Sovereign in the former, yet it plainly appears from Scripture that absolute power in his infernal dominions is granted to their diabolical ruler.", "천국과 지옥의 그림은 그 둘을 무척 생생한 영상으로 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐 보여 준다. 후자의 군주는 전자에 있는 전능하신 주권자에게서 본디 받은 것 말고는 아무런 권력도 가질 수 없지만, 그래도 성경에 분명히 나타나 있듯이 그 지옥의 영토 안에서의 절대 권력만큼은 그 악마 같은 통치자에게 허락되어 있는 것이다."], ["This is indeed the only absolute power which can by Scripture be derived from heaven.", "실로 이것이 성경에 따라 하늘로부터 받을 수 있는 유일한 절대 권력이다."], ["If, therefore, the several tyrannies upon earth can prove any title to a Divine authority, it must be derived from this original grant to the prince of darkness; and these subordinate deputations must consequently come immediately from him whose stamp they so expressly bear.", "그러므로 만일 이 땅 위의 여러 폭정이 신성한 권위에 대한 무슨 자격이라도 입증할 수 있다면, 그것은 어둠의 군주에게 내려진 이 본래의 허락에서 비롯된 것일 수밖에 없다. 따라서 이 하급의 대리권들은 결국, 그것들이 그토록 또렷이 새겨 지니고 있는 그 도장의 주인에게서 곧바로 나온 것일 수밖에 없다."]], [["To conclude, as the examples of all ages shew us that mankind in general desire power only to do harm, and, when they obtain it, use it for no other purpose; it is not consonant with even the least degree of prudence to hazard an alteration, where our hopes are poorly kept in countenance by only two or three exceptions out of a thousand instances to alarm our fears.", "끝으로, 모든 시대의 사례가 우리에게 보여 주듯 인류는 대개 오로지 해를 끼치기 위해서만 권력을 바라며, 그것을 손에 넣으면 다른 어떤 목적에도 쓰지 않으니, 우리 두려움을 부추기는 천 가지 사례 가운데 고작 두셋의 예외로나 우리 희망이 가까스로 체면치레를 하는 마당에, 변혁을 무릅쓴다는 것은 일말의 분별에도 들어맞지 않는다."], ["In this case it will be much wiser to submit to a few inconveniencies arising from the dispassionate deafness of laws, than to remedy them by applying to the passionate open ears of a tyrant.", "이런 경우에는, 격정에 휩쓸려 활짝 열린 폭군의 귀에 호소하여 폐단을 바로잡으려 하기보다, 차라리 냉정하게 귀먹은 법에서 비롯되는 몇 가지 불편에 감수하는 편이 훨씬 더 지혜로울 것이다."]], [["Nor can the example of the gypsies, though possibly they may have long been happy under this form of government, be here urged; since we must remember the very material respect in which they differ from all other people, and to which perhaps this their happiness is entirely owing, namely, that they have no false honours among them, and that they look on shame as the most grievous punishment in the world.", "또한 집시들의 사례는, 비록 그들이 이 통치 형태 아래에서 오래도록 행복했을지 모르나 여기에 끌어다 댈 수 없다. 우리는 그들이 다른 모든 사람과 다른 매우 중대한 점을, 어쩌면 이 행복이 전적으로 거기에 기대고 있을 그 점을 기억해야 하기 때문이다. 곧 그들 사이에는 거짓된 명예가 없으며, 그들은 수치를 이 세상에서 가장 견디기 힘든 형벌로 여긴다는 것이다."]], [["A dialogue between Jones and Partridge.", "정직하게 자유를 사랑하는 이들이라면, 지난 장 끝머리에서 우리가 빠져들었던 그 긴 여담을 너그러이 보아 주리라 믿어 의심치 않는다."], ["The honest lovers of liberty will, we doubt not, pardon that long digression into which we were led at the close of the last chapter, to prevent our history from being applied to the use of the most pernicious doctrine which priestcraft had ever the wickedness or the impudence to preach.", "그것은 다름 아니라, 사제의 술수가 일찍이 사악함이나 뻔뻔함을 무릅쓰고 설파했던 그 가장 해로운 교리에 우리 이야기가 악용되는 것을 막기 위함이었다."]], [["We will now proceed with Mr Jones, who, when the storm was over, took leave of his Egyptian majesty, after many thanks for his courteous behaviour and kind entertainment, and set out for Coventry; to which place (for it was still dark) a gypsy was ordered to conduct him.", "이제 우리는 다시 존스 씨에게로 돌아가자. 그는 소동이 가라앉자 집시 폐하께 정중한 대접과 친절한 환대에 거듭 감사를 표한 뒤 작별을 고하고, 코번트리를 향해 길을 나섰다. 아직 어두웠던 까닭에, 집시 한 사람이 그를 그곳까지 안내하라는 명을 받았다."]], [["Jones having, by reason of his deviation, travelled eleven miles instead of six, and most of those through very execrable roads, where no expedition could have been made in quest of a midwife, did not arrive at Coventry till near twelve.", "존스는 길을 벗어난 탓에 육 마일이면 될 것을 열한 마일이나 돌아왔고, 그 길 대부분이 산파를 부르러 가는 길이라 해도 도무지 서두를 수 없을 만큼 끔찍한 길이었던 터라, 코번트리에 다다른 것은 거의 열두 시가 다 되어서였다. 게다가 다시 안장에 오른 것도 두 시가 지나서였다."], ["Nor could he possibly get again into the saddle till past two; for post-horses were now not easy to get; nor were the hostler or post-boy in half so great a hurry as himself, but chose rather to imitate the tranquil disposition of Partridge; who, being denied the nourishment of sleep, took all opportunities to supply its place with every other kind of nourishment, and was never better pleased than when he arrived at an inn, nor ever more dissatisfied than when he was again forced to leave it.", "역마는 이제 구하기가 쉽지 않았고, 마부나 역참 소년이나 존스의 절반만큼도 서두르지 않았으며, 오히려 파트리지의 느긋한 성미를 본받는 편을 택했다. 파트리지로 말하자면, 잠이라는 자양분을 빼앗긴 처지였으므로 틈만 나면 그 자리를 온갖 다른 종류의 자양분으로 메우려 들었고, 여관에 도착했을 때만큼 기분 좋은 적이 없었으며, 다시 그곳을 떠나야만 할 때만큼 불만스러운 적도 없었다."]], [["Jones now travelled post; we will follow him, therefore, according to our custom, and to the rules of Longinus, in the same manner. From Coventry he arrived at Daventry, from Daventry at Stratford, and from Stratford at Dunstable, whither he came the next day a little after noon, and within a few hours after Sophia had left it; and though he was obliged to stay here longer than he wished, while a smith, with great deliberation, shoed the post-horse he was to ride, he doubted not but to overtake his Sophia before she should set out from St Albans; at which place he concluded, and very reasonably, that his lordship would stop and dine.", "이제 존스는 역마를 갈아타며 달렸다. 그러니 우리도 관례대로, 그리고 롱기누스의 법칙에 따라 같은 방식으로 그를 뒤따르자. 그는 코번트리에서 데이번트리에, 데이번트리에서 스트랫퍼드에, 스트랫퍼드에서 던스터블에 이르렀고, 그 이튿날 정오를 조금 지나, 소피아가 그곳을 떠난 지 몇 시간 뒤에 던스터블에 닿았다. 비록 그가 탈 역마에 대장장이가 자못 신중을 기하며 편자를 박는 동안 바라던 것보다 오래 머물러야 했지만, 그는 소피아가 세인트올번스를 떠나기 전에는 반드시 따라잡으리라 믿어 의심치 않았다. 그는 그곳에서 그 귀족 나리께서 멈춰 식사를 하리라고, 더없이 그럴듯하게 결론지었던 것이다."]], [["And had he been right in this conjecture, he most probably would have overtaken his angel at the aforesaid place; but unluckily my lord had appointed a dinner to be prepared for him at his own house in London, and, in order to enable him to reach that place in proper time, he had ordered a relay of horses to meet him at St Albans.", "만일 이 짐작이 옳았다면, 그는 십중팔구 앞서 말한 그곳에서 자신의 천사를 따라잡았을 것이다. 그러나 불운하게도 나리께서는 런던 자택에 자신을 위한 만찬을 차려 두라 일러두었고, 제때에 그곳에 닿기 위해 세인트올번스에서 갈아탈 말을 미리 대기시켜 두라 명해 둔 터였다."], ["When Jones therefore arrived there, he was informed that the coach-and-six had set out two hours before.", "그리하여 존스가 그곳에 도착했을 때, 그는 여섯 마리가 끄는 마차가 두 시간 전에 이미 떠났다는 말을 들었다."]], [["If fresh post-horses had been now ready, as they were not, it seemed so apparently impossible to overtake the coach before it reached London, that Partridge thought he had now a proper opportunity to remind his friend of a matter which he seemed entirely to have forgotten; what this was the reader will guess, when we inform him that Jones had eat nothing more than one poached egg since he had left the alehouse where he had first met the guide returning from Sophia; for with the gypsies he had feasted only his understanding.", "지금 당장 새 역마가 준비되어 있었다면 모를까—실은 그렇지 못했다—마차가 런던에 닿기 전에 따라잡는다는 것은 누가 보아도 불가능해 보였으므로, 파트리지는 자기 벗이 까맣게 잊은 듯한 한 가지 문제를 일깨워 줄 마침맞은 기회가 왔다고 여겼다. 그것이 무엇인지는 독자도 짐작하리라. 존스가 소피아에게서 돌아오던 안내인을 처음 만났던 그 선술집을 떠난 이후로 수란 한 알밖에 먹지 않았다는 사실을 알려 드린다면 말이다. 집시들과 함께 있는 동안 그가 호사를 누린 것은 오직 그의 지성뿐이었으니까."]], [["The landlord so entirely agreed with the opinion of Mr Partridge, that he no sooner heard the latter desire his friend to stay and dine, than he very readily put in his word, and retracting his promise before given of furnishing the horses immediately, he assured Mr Jones he would lose no time in bespeaking a dinner, which, he said, could be got ready sooner than it was possible to get the horses up from grass, and to prepare them for their journey by a feed of corn.", "여관 주인 또한 파트리지 씨의 의견에 어찌나 전적으로 동의했던지, 파트리지가 자기 벗에게 머물러 식사를 하라고 청하는 말을 듣자마자 선뜻 한마디 거들었다. 그러고는 즉시 말을 대령하겠노라던 앞선 약속을 슬쩍 거두며, 식사부터 차리는 데 한시도 지체하지 않겠노라 존스 씨에게 장담했다. 풀밭에 있는 말을 끌어와 곡물 사료를 먹여 여정에 대비시키는 것보다, 식사를 차리는 편이 더 빨리 될 수 있다는 것이었다."]], [["Jones was at length prevailed on, chiefly by the latter argument of the landlord; and now a joint of mutton was put down to the fire.", "존스는 마침내 설득당했는데, 주로 여관 주인의 그 마지막 논거 때문이었다. 그리하여 양고기 한 덩이가 불 위에 올랐다."], ["While this was preparing, Partridge, being admitted into the same apartment with his friend or master, began to harangue in the following manner.", "그것이 익는 동안, 파트리지는 자기 벗 또는 주인과 같은 방에 들도록 허락받자 다음과 같이 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“Certainly, sir, if ever man deserved a young lady, you deserve young Madam Western; for what a vast quantity of love must a man have, to be able to live upon it without any other food, as you do?", "\"확실히 나리, 만일 어떤 사내가 젊은 아가씨를 누릴 자격이 있다면, 나리야말로 젊은 웨스턴 아씨를 누릴 자격이 있습니다요. 나리처럼 다른 음식이라곤 한 입도 없이 오로지 사랑만으로 살아갈 수 있으려면 도대체 얼마나 어마어마한 양의 사랑을 품어야 한단 말입니까?"], ["I am positive I have eat thirty times as much within these last twenty-four hours as your honour, and yet I am almost famished; for nothing makes a man so hungry as travelling, especially in this cold raw weather.", "분명 저는 지난 스물네 시간 동안 나리보다 서른 배는 더 먹었는데도 거의 굶어 죽을 지경입니다. 사람을 그토록 허기지게 하는 건 여행만 한 게 없거든요."], ["And yet I can't tell how it is, but your honour is seemingly in perfect good health, and you never looked better nor fresher in your life.", "더구나 이렇게 으슬으슬 추운 날씨에는요. 헌데 어찌 된 영문인지 나리께선 겉보기에 더없이 정정하시고, 평생에 이보다 더 좋아 보이고 생기 넘쳐 보인 적이 없으십니다요."], ["It must be certainly love that you live upon.”", "나리께서 양식으로 삼고 사는 건 틀림없이 사랑일 겝니다.\""]], [["“And a very rich diet too, Partridge,”", "\"그것도 아주 기름진 음식이지, 파트리지.\""], ["answered Jones.", "존스가 대답했다."], ["“But did not fortune send me an excellent dainty yesterday?", "\"한데 어제 운명의 여신이 내게 더없이 훌륭한 진미를 보내 주지 않았더냐?"], ["Dost thou imagine I cannot live more than twenty-four hours on this dear pocket-book?”", "내가 이 소중한 수첩 한 권으로 스물네 시간 넘게 못 버틸 성싶으냐?\""]], [["“Undoubtedly,”", "\"두말할 것도 없습죠.\""], ["cries Partridge, “there is enough in that pocket-book to purchase many a good meal.", "파트리지가 외쳤다. \"그 수첩 안에는 좋은 끼니를 여러 번 사고도 남을 만큼 들어 있으니까요."], ["Fortune sent it to your honour very opportunely for present use, as your honour's money must be almost out by this time.”", "운명의 여신이 그걸 나리께 보내 주신 게 지금 당장 쓰기에 참으로 때맞춤이었습니다. 나리 돈도 이맘때면 거의 바닥났을 테니 말입죠.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 소리냐?\""], ["answered Jones; “I hope you don't imagine that I should be dishonest enough, even if it belonged to any other person, besides Miss Western----”", "존스가 대꾸했다. \"설마 내가, 설령 그게 웨스턴 양 말고 다른 누구의 것이라 한들, 그토록 부정직한 짓을 하리라고 여기는 건 아니겠지——\""]], [["“Dishonest!” replied Partridge, “heaven forbid I should wrong your honour so much! but where's the dishonesty in borrowing a little for present spending, since you will be so well able to pay the lady hereafter? No, indeed, I would have your honour pay it again, as soon as it is convenient, by all means; but where can be the harm in making use of it now you want it? Indeed, if it belonged to a poor body, it would be another thing; but so great a lady, to be sure, can never want it, especially now as she is along with a lord, who, it can't be doubted, will let her have whatever she hath need of. Besides, if she should want a little, she can't want the whole, therefore I would give her a little; but I would be hanged before I mentioned the having found it at first, and before I got some money of my own; for London, I have heard, is the very worst of places to be in without money. Indeed, if I had not known to whom it belonged, I might have thought it was the devil's money, and have been afraid to use it; but as you know otherwise, and came honestly by it, it would be an affront to fortune to part with it all again, at the very time when you want it most; you can hardly expect she should ever do you such another good turn; for fortuna nunquam perpetuo est bona. You will do as you please, notwithstanding all I say; but for my part, I would be hanged before I mentioned a word of the matter.”", "\"부정직이라뇨!\" 파트리지가 응수했다. \"맙소사, 제가 나리께 그렇게까지 못된 누명을 씌울 리가요! 하지만 당장 쓰려고 조금 빌리는 게 무슨 부정직이란 말입니까. 어차피 나중에 아가씨께 갚을 능력이 넉넉하실 텐데요? 아니죠, 정말이지 형편 닿는 대로 어떻게든 다시 갚으셔야지요. 하지만 지금 필요하실 때 그걸 좀 쓰는 게 무슨 해가 된단 말입니까? 하기야 그게 가난한 사람의 것이라면 사정이 다르겠지만, 그토록 지체 높은 아가씨께서야 분명 그게 아쉬울 리 없지요. 더구나 지금은 어떤 나리와 함께 계시니, 그분이 아가씨께 필요한 건 무엇이든 대 주시리란 건 의심할 여지가 없고요. 게다가 아가씨께서 설사 조금 아쉬우시더라도 전부가 아쉬울 리는 없을 테니, 저라면 조금만 드리겠습니다요. 다만 처음부터 그걸 주웠다는 말을 입에 올리느니, 그리고 제 손에 돈이 좀 들어오기 전에 그러느니, 차라리 목매달리는 편을 택하겠습니다. 듣자 하니 런던이란 곳은 돈 없이 지내기엔 세상에서 가장 고약한 데라니까요. 사실 그게 누구 것인지 몰랐더라면, 저는 그걸 악마의 돈인 줄 알고 무서워서 못 썼을지도 모르지요. 하지만 나리께선 사정을 아시고, 떳떳하게 손에 넣으셨으니, 정작 가장 절실히 필요한 바로 이때에 그걸 도로 다 내주신다면 그거야말로 운명의 여신을 모욕하는 일입죠. 여신께서 또다시 그런 호의를 베푸시리라곤 좀처럼 기대하기 어려울 테니까요. 포르투나 눈콴 페르페투오 에스트 보나(운명의 여신은 결코 영원토록 좋게만 굴지 않는다)라 하지 않습니까. 제가 무슨 말을 하든 나리께선 마음대로 하시겠지요. 하지만 저로서는 그 문제를 한마디라도 입에 올리느니 차라리 목매달리는 편을 택하겠습니다.\""]], [["“By what I can see, Partridge,” cries Jones, “hanging is a matter non longe alienum a Scaevolae studiis.” “You should say alienus,” says Partridge,--“I remember the passage; it is an example under communis, alienus, immunis, variis casibus serviunt.” “If you do remember it,” cries Jones, “I find you don't understand it; but I tell thee, friend, in plain English, that he who finds another's property, and wilfully detains it from the known owner, deserves, in foro conscientiae, to be hanged, no less than if he had stolen it. And as for this very identical bill, which is the property of my angel, and was once in her dear possession, I will not deliver it into any hands but her own, upon any consideration whatever, no, though I was as hungry as thou art, and had no other means to satisfy my craving appetite; this I hope to do before I sleep; but if it should happen otherwise, I charge thee, if thou would'st not incur my displeasure for ever, not to shock me any more by the bare mention of such detestable baseness.”", "\"내가 보기에 말이다, 파트리지,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"목매다는 일이야말로 논 롱게 알리에눔 아 스카이볼라이 스투디이스(스카이볼라의 연구와 그리 동떨어진 것이 아닌)로구나.\" \"알리에누스라고 하셔야지요.\" 파트리지가 말했다. \"그 구절을 기억합니다. 코무니스, 알리에누스, 임무니스, 바리이스 카시부스 세르비운트(communis, alienus, immunis는 여러 격을 취한다) 아래에 나오는 예문이지요.\" \"자네가 정녕 그걸 기억한다면,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"정작 그 뜻은 이해하지 못하는 모양일세. 허나 알기 쉬운 영어로 말해 주지. 남의 재물을 주워 놓고 그 주인이 누군지 뻔히 알면서도 짐짓 돌려주지 않고 쥐고 있는 자는, 포로 콘스키엔티아이(양심의 법정에서) 그것을 훔친 자와 다름없이 목매달릴 만하다는 걸세. 그리고 바로 이 어음으로 말하자면, 이건 내 천사의 재물이요 한때 그분의 소중한 손에 들려 있던 것이니, 나는 어떤 사정이 있더라도 그분 본인의 손 외에 다른 어떤 손에도 이걸 넘기지 않겠네. 그래, 설령 내가 자네처럼 굶주리고 이 들끓는 식욕을 채울 다른 방도가 전혀 없다 한들 말일세. 잠들기 전에 이 일을 해내고 싶네만, 혹 일이 그리되지 않더라도, 내 노여움을 영영 사고 싶지 않거든 그런 가증스러운 비열함을 입에 올려 다시는 나를 진저리치게 하지 말게.\""]], [["“I should not have mentioned it now,”", "\"제 눈에 그렇게 보였다면 지금 그 말을 꺼내지도 않았을 겝니다.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["cries Partridge, “if it had appeared so to me; for I'm sure I scorn any wickedness as much as another; but perhaps you know better; and yet I might have imagined that I should not have lived so many years, and have taught school so long, without being able to distinguish between fas et nefas; but it seems we are all to live and learn.", "\"저도 어떤 못된 짓이든 남 못지않게 경멸한다고 자부하니까요. 헌데 어쩌면 나리께서 더 잘 아시겠지요."], ["I remember my old schoolmaster, who was a prodigious great scholar, used often to say, Polly matete cry town is my daskalon.", "그래도 제가 이 나이 먹도록 살고 그토록 오래 학교에서 가르쳐 왔으면서 파스 에트 네파스(옳음과 그름)도 가릴 줄 모를 리야 있겠습니까. 하지만 사람은 누구나 죽을 때까지 배워야 하나 봅니다."], ["The English of which, he told us, was, That a child may sometimes teach his grandmother to suck eggs.", "굉장한 대학자셨던 제 옛 스승께서 곧잘 이르시던 말씀이 떠오르는군요. 폴리 마테테 크라이 타운 이스 마이 다스칼론."], ["I have lived to a fine purpose, truly, if I am to be taught my grammar at this time of day.", "그분이 일러 주시기로 그 영어 뜻은, 아이가 더러는 제 할미한테 달걀 빠는 법을 가르치려 든다는 것이었지요. 이 나이에 새삼 문법을 배워야 한다면, 참으로 제가 헛산 셈입니다요."], ["Perhaps, young gentleman, you may change your opinion, if you live to my years: for I remember I thought myself as wise when I was a stripling of one or two and twenty as I am now.", "어쩌면, 젊은 도련님, 도련님도 제 나이쯤 사시면 생각이 바뀔지 모릅니다. 저도 스물한둘 풋내기 시절엔 지금만큼이나 제가 똑똑한 줄 알았던 게 기억나거든요."], ["I am sure I always taught alienus, and my master read it so before me.”", "분명 저는 늘 알리에누스라고 가르쳤고, 제 스승께서도 제 앞에서 그렇게 읽으셨습니다요.\""]], [["There were not many instances in which Partridge could provoke Jones, nor were there many in which Partridge himself could have been hurried out of his respect.", "파트리지가 존스를 격분시킬 수 있는 경우는 그리 많지 않았고, 또 파트리지 자신이 존스를 향한 공경심을 잃고 발끈하게 되는 경우도 그리 많지 않았다."], ["Unluckily, however, they had both hit on one of these.", "그러나 불운하게도, 두 사람은 그 드문 경우 가운데 하나를 동시에 건드리고 만 것이다."], ["We have already seen Partridge could not bear to have his learning attacked, nor could Jones bear some passage or other in the foregoing speech.", "우리는 이미 파트리지가 자기 학식이 공격당하는 것을 견디지 못한다는 것을 보았고, 존스 또한 앞선 연설 속 이런저런 대목을 참아 넘길 수 없었다."], ["And now, looking upon his companion with a contemptuous and disdainful air (a thing not usual with him), he cried, “Partridge, I see thou art a conceited old fool, and I wish thou art not likewise an old rogue.", "그리하여 그는 자기 길동무를 경멸과 멸시 어린 낯빛으로(그로서는 좀처럼 없는 일이었다) 바라보며 외쳤다. \"파트리지, 보아하니 자넨 자만에 빠진 늙은 바보로군."], ["Indeed, if I was as well convinced of the latter as I am of the former, thou should'st travel no farther in my company.”", "게다가 늙은 악당까지는 아니기를 바라네. 정말이지, 내가 후자를 전자만큼이나 확신하게 된다면, 자넨 더는 나와 동행하지 못할 걸세.\""]], [["The sage pedagogue was contented with the vent which he had already given to his indignation; and, as the vulgar phrase is, immediately drew in his horns.", "이 현명한 훈장 나리는 이미 자기 분풀이를 어느 정도 쏟아낸 것으로 만족하고는, 흔한 속담대로, 곧장 뿔을 거두어들였다."], ["He said, he was sorry he had uttered anything which might give offence, for that he had never intended it; but Nemo omnibus horis sapit.", "그는 노여움을 살 만한 말을 내뱉어 미안하다고, 결코 그럴 뜻은 아니었다고 했다. 그러나 네모 옴니부스 호리스 사피트(어느 누구도 늘 현명할 수는 없는 법)라는 것이었다."]], [["As Jones had the vices of a warm disposition, he was entirely free from those of a cold one; and if his friends must have confest his temper to have been a little too easily ruffled, his enemies must at the same time have confest, that it as soon subsided; nor did it at all resemble the sea, whose swelling is more violent and dangerous after a storm is over than while the storm itself subsists.", "존스는 뜨거운 기질에서 비롯되는 악덕은 지녔으되, 차가운 기질에서 비롯되는 악덕에서는 전적으로 자유로웠다. 그의 벗들이라면 그의 성미가 좀 지나치게 쉬이 발끈한다는 점은 인정해야 했겠지만, 그의 적들이라도 동시에 그것이 또한 그만큼 빨리 가라앉는다는 점은 인정해야 했을 것이다."], ["He instantly accepted the submission of Partridge, shook him by the hand, and with the most benign aspect imaginable, said twenty kind things, and at the same time very severely condemned himself, though not half so severely as he will most probably be condemned by many of our good readers.", "그것은 폭풍이 잦아든 뒤의 일렁임이 폭풍 그 자체가 몰아칠 때보다 더 사납고 위태로운 바다와는 조금도 닮지 않았다. 그는 즉시 파트리지의 사과를 받아들이고 그의 손을 잡아 흔들며, 상상할 수 있는 가장 자애로운 낯빛으로 다정한 말을 스무 마디나 건넸고, 동시에 자신을 매우 호되게 나무랐다—비록 우리의 점잖은 독자 여러 분이 그를 나무랄 만한 정도의 절반에도 미치지 못했지만 말이다."]], [["Partridge was now highly comforted, as his fears of having offended were at once abolished, and his pride completely satisfied by Jones having owned himself in the wrong, which submission he instantly applied to what had principally nettled him, and repeated in a muttering voice, “To be sure, sir, your knowledge may be superior to mine in some things; but as to the grammar, I think I may challenge any man living. I think, at least, I have that at my finger's end.”", "이제 파트리지는 크게 위로받았다. 노여움을 샀을지 모른다는 두려움이 단숨에 사라졌고, 존스가 스스로 잘못을 인정한 덕분에 그의 자존심도 완전히 충족되었기 때문이다. 그는 그 사과를 당장 자신을 무엇보다 거슬리게 했던 대목에 갖다 붙이고는, 중얼거리는 목소리로 되뇌었다. \"그야 물론입죠, 나리, 어떤 일에서는 나리의 지식이 제 것보다 위에 계시겠지요. 하지만 문법으로 말할 것 같으면, 저는 살아 있는 어느 누구에게라도 한판 겨뤄 보자고 할 수 있을 듯합니다요. 적어도 그것만큼은 손끝에 훤히 꿰고 있다고 생각합니다.\""]], [["If anything could add to the satisfaction which the poor man now enjoyed, he received this addition by the arrival of an excellent shoulder of mutton, that at this instant came smoaking to the table.", "이 가련한 사내가 지금 누리는 만족에 무언가를 더 보탤 수 있는 것이 있었다면, 그는 바로 그것을 얻었다. 바로 그 순간 김을 모락모락 피우며 식탁에 오른 훌륭한 양 어깨살 덕분이었다."], ["On which, having both plentifully feasted, they again mounted their horses, and set forward for London.", "두 사람은 그것으로 한껏 배불리 먹고는 다시 말에 올라 런던을 향해 길을 나섰다."]], [["What happened to Mr Jones in his journey from St Albans.", "세인트올번스를 떠난 길에서 존스 씨에게 일어난 일."]], [["They were got about two miles beyond Barnet, and it was now the dusk of the evening, when a genteel-looking man, but upon a very shabby horse, rode up to Jones, and asked him whether he was going to London; to which Jones answered in the affirmative. The gentleman replied, “I should be obliged to you, sir, if you will accept of my company; for it is very late, and I am a stranger to the road.” Jones readily complied with the request; and on they travelled together, holding that sort of discourse which is usual on such occasions.", "일행이 바넷을 지나 두 마일쯤 갔을 무렵, 어느덧 저녁 어스름이 깔리고 있었다. 그때 점잖아 보이는 차림새의 한 남자가, 하지만 무척 초라한 말을 타고서 존스에게 다가와 런던으로 가는 길이냐고 물었다. 존스는 그렇다고 대답했다. 그 신사는 이렇게 말했다. \"선생, 동행을 받아주신다면 고맙겠습니다. 시간도 무척 늦었고, 저는 이 길이 낯선 사람이라서요.\" 존스는 흔쾌히 그 청을 받아들였고, 두 사람은 이런 경우에 흔히 나누는 그런 종류의 이야기를 주고받으며 함께 길을 갔다."]], [["Of this, indeed, robbery was the principal topic: upon which subject the stranger expressed great apprehensions; but Jones declared he had very little to lose, and consequently as little to fear.", "실로 그중에서도 노상강도가 주된 화제였다. 그 이야기를 두고 낯선 남자는 큰 두려움을 드러냈으나, 존스는 자신은 잃을 것이 거의 없으니 두려워할 것도 그만큼 없다고 잘라 말했다."], ["Here Partridge could not forbear putting in his word.", "이때 파트리지가 한마디 거들지 않을 수 없었다. \"나리께서야 별것 아니라 여기실지 모르지만요,\""], ["“Your honour,”", "그가 말했다. \"제 생각엔, 만약 나리처럼 제 주머니에 백 파운드짜리 은행권이 있다면 그걸 잃는 게 무척 속상할 겁니다."], ["said he, “may think it a little, but I am sure, if I had a hundred-pound bank-note in my pocket, as you have, I should be very sorry to lose it; but, for my part, I never was less afraid in my life; for we are four of us, and if we all stand by one another, the best man in England can't rob us.", "그래도 저로 말하자면 평생 이보다 덜 무서웠던 적이 없습니다. 우리가 넷이나 되고, 우리 모두가 서로 등을 맞대고 버티면 잉글랜드에서 제일가는 장사라도 우릴 털진 못할 테니까요."], ["Suppose he should have a pistol, he can kill but one of us, and a man can die but once.--That's my comfort, a man can die but once.”", "설사 그자가 권총을 가졌대도, 그래봐야 우리 중 하나밖에 못 죽이고, 사람은 한 번밖에 못 죽으니까요. 그게 제 위안이지요, 사람은 한 번밖에 못 죽는다는 게.\""]], [["Besides the reliance on superior numbers, a kind of valour which hath raised a certain nation among the moderns to a high pitch of glory, there was another reason for the extraordinary courage which Partridge now discovered; for he had at present as much of that quality as was in the power of liquor to bestow.", "수적 우세에 대한 믿음—근대에 이르러 어느 한 민족을 드높은 영광의 경지로 끌어올린 그런 종류의 용맹—외에도, 파트리지가 지금 드러낸 이 유별난 담력에는 또 다른 이유가 있었다. 그는 지금 술기운이 베풀 수 있는 만큼의 그 담력을 한껏 지니고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Our company were now arrived within a mile of Highgate, when the stranger turned short upon Jones, and pulling out a pistol, demanded that little bank-note which Partridge had mentioned.", "일행이 이제 하이게이트에서 한 마일 안쪽까지 다다랐을 때, 그 낯선 남자가 별안간 존스에게 몸을 홱 돌리더니 권총을 꺼내 들고, 파트리지가 말했던 그 자그마한 은행권을 내놓으라고 요구했다."]], [["Jones was at first somewhat shocked at this unexpected demand; however, he presently recollected himself, and told the highwayman, all the money he had in his pocket was entirely at his service; and so saying, he pulled out upwards of three guineas, and offered to deliver it; but the other answered with an oath, That would not do.", "존스는 처음엔 이 뜻밖의 요구에 다소 충격을 받았다. 하지만 곧 정신을 가다듬고 노상강도에게, 주머니에 든 돈은 모두 기꺼이 그에게 내주겠다고 말했다."], ["Jones answered coolly, he was very sorry for it, and returned the money into his pocket.", "그러면서 삼 기니가 넘는 돈을 꺼내 건네려 했으나, 상대는 욕설을 내뱉으며 그걸론 안 된다고 대답했다. 존스는 차분하게, 그것 참 안됐다고 말하고는 돈을 도로 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["The highwayman then threatened, if he did not deliver the bank-note that moment, he must shoot him; holding his pistol at the same time very near to his breast.", "그러자 노상강도는, 당장 은행권을 내놓지 않으면 쏘아 죽이겠다고 으름장을 놓으며 권총을 그의 가슴 바로 앞에 들이댔다."], ["Jones instantly caught hold of the fellow's hand, which trembled so that he could scarce hold the pistol in it, and turned the muzzle from him.", "존스는 즉시 그자의 손을 붙잡았는데, 그 손이 어찌나 떨리던지 권총을 제대로 쥐고 있기도 힘들 정도였다. 존스는 총구를 자기 쪽에서 돌려놓았다."], ["A struggle then ensued, in which the former wrested the pistol from the hand of his antagonist, and both came from their horses on the ground together, the highwayman upon his back, and the victorious Jones upon him.", "곧 몸싸움이 벌어졌고, 그 와중에 존스가 상대의 손에서 권총을 비틀어 빼앗았으며, 두 사람은 함께 말에서 땅바닥으로 굴러떨어졌다. 노상강도는 등을 대고 자빠졌고, 승리한 존스가 그 위에 올라탔다."]], [["The poor fellow now began to implore mercy of the conqueror: for, to say the truth, he was in strength by no means a match for Jones.", "이 가련한 자는 이제 정복자에게 자비를 빌기 시작했다. 사실대로 말하자면, 힘으로는 도무지 존스의 상대가 되지 못했던 것이다."], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"정말입니다, 선생,\""], ["says he, “I could have had no intention to shoot you; for you will find the pistol was not loaded.", "그가 말했다. \"전 선생을 쏠 생각이 조금도 없었습니다."], ["This is the first robbery I ever attempted, and I have been driven by distress to this.”", "보시면 아시겠지만 권총엔 총알도 들어 있지 않았어요. 강도질이라곤 이게 난생처음이고, 저는 궁핍에 떠밀려 여기까지 온 겁니다.\""]], [["At this instant, at about a hundred and fifty yards' distance, lay another person on the ground, roaring for mercy in a much louder voice than the highwayman.", "바로 이 순간, 백오십 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서 또 다른 사람이 땅바닥에 엎드린 채, 노상강도보다 훨씬 더 큰 소리로 자비를 부르짖고 있었다."], ["This was no other than Partridge himself, who, endeavouring to make his escape from the engagement, had been thrown from his horse, and lay flat on his face, not daring to look up, and expecting every minute to be shot.", "다름 아닌 파트리지였다. 그는 이 싸움에서 달아나려다 말에서 떨어져 얼굴을 땅에 처박은 채 납작 엎드려, 감히 고개를 들지도 못하고 시시각각 총에 맞을까 봐 떨고 있었다."]], [["In this posture he lay, till the guide, who was no otherwise concerned than for his horses, having secured the stumbling beast, came up to him, and told him his master had got the better of the highwayman.", "그는 그런 자세로 엎드려 있었다. 마침내, 오로지 말들 걱정뿐이던 안내인이 비틀거리던 짐승을 붙잡아 둔 뒤 그에게 다가와, 주인 양반이 노상강도를 제압했다고 일러주었다."]], [["Partridge leapt up at this news, and ran back to the place where Jones stood with his sword drawn in his hand to guard the poor fellow; which Partridge no sooner saw than he cried out, “Kill the villain, sir, run him through the body, kill him this instant!”", "이 소식에 파트리지는 벌떡 일어나, 존스가 칼을 빼 들고 그 가련한 자를 지키고 서 있는 자리로 달려갔다. 그 광경을 보자마자 파트리지는 소리쳤다. \"그 악당을 죽이세요, 나리, 몸을 꿰뚫어 버리세요, 당장 죽이세요!\""]], [["Luckily, however, for the poor wretch, he had fallen into more merciful hands; for Jones having examined the pistol, and found it to be really unloaded, began to believe all the man had told him, before Partridge came up: namely, that he was a novice in the trade, and that he had been driven to it by the distress he mentioned, the greatest indeed imaginable, that of five hungry children, and a wife lying in of the sixth, in the utmost want and misery. The truth of all which the highwayman most vehemently asserted, and offered to convince Mr Jones of it, if he would take the trouble to go to his house, which was not above two miles off; saying, “That he desired no favour, but upon condition of proving all he had all alledged.”", "하지만 다행히도 이 불쌍한 자는 좀 더 자비로운 손에 떨어진 셈이었다. 존스는 권총을 살펴보고 정말로 총알이 들어 있지 않음을 확인한 뒤, 파트리지가 오기 전에 그자가 했던 말을 모두 믿기 시작했던 것이다. 곧 그가 이 일에 풋내기이며, 자신이 말한 궁핍에 떠밀려 이 짓을 하게 되었다는 것이었다. 그 궁핍이란 실로 상상할 수 있는 가장 극심한 것으로, 배곯는 다섯 아이와, 더없는 궁색과 비참 속에서 여섯째를 낳고 누워 있는 아내가 있다는 것이었다. 노상강도는 이 모든 것이 사실이라고 더없이 격렬하게 주장하며, 수고스럽지만 자기 집까지 가주신다면—그곳은 두 마일도 채 안 되는 거리라며—그것을 존스 씨에게 납득시켜 드리겠노라 했다. 그는 \"다른 호의는 바라지 않으며, 다만 제가 한 말을 모두 증명한다는 조건에서만 바랄 뿐\"이라고 말했다."]], [["Jones at first pretended that he would take the fellow at his word, and go with him, declaring that his fate should depend entirely on the truth of his story.", "존스는 처음엔 그자의 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들여 함께 가겠노라 하는 체하며, 그의 운명은 전적으로 그 이야기의 진위에 달려 있노라고 못박았다."], ["Upon this the poor fellow immediately expressed so much alacrity, that Jones was perfectly satisfied with his veracity, and began now to entertain sentiments of compassion for him.", "이 말에 그 가련한 자가 즉시 어찌나 선뜻 응하는 기색을 보이던지, 존스는 그 진실함에 완전히 만족하여 이제 그에게 측은한 마음을 품기 시작했다."], ["He returned the fellow his empty pistol, advised him to think of honester means of relieving his distress, and gave him a couple of guineas for the immediate support of his wife and his family; adding, “he wished he had more for his sake, for the hundred pound that had been mentioned was not his own.”", "그는 빈 권총을 그자에게 돌려주고, 궁핍을 면할 더 정직한 방도를 생각해 보라고 충고했으며, 아내와 식구를 당장 건사하라고 두 기니를 건넸다. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, \"자네를 위해 더 줄 수 있었으면 좋겠네만, 아까 말이 나온 그 백 파운드는 내 것이 아니라네\"라고 했다."]], [["Our readers will probably be divided in their opinions concerning this action; some may applaud it perhaps as an act of extraordinary humanity, while those of a more saturnine temper will consider it as a want of regard to that justice which every man owes his country.", "아마 우리 독자들은 이 행동을 두고 의견이 갈릴 것이다. 어떤 이들은 더없이 인정 넘치는 행위라며 갈채를 보낼 테고, 좀 더 음울한 기질을 지닌 이들은 모든 사람이 제 나라에 마땅히 갚아야 할 정의를 소홀히 한 것이라 여길 것이다."], ["Partridge certainly saw it in that light; for he testified much dissatisfaction on the occasion, quoted an old proverb, and said, he should not wonder if the rogue attacked them again before they reached London.", "파트리지는 분명 그렇게 보았다. 그는 이 일에 큰 불만을 드러내며 옛 속담을 들먹였고, 그 악당이 런던에 닿기도 전에 다시 우리를 덮친다 해도 놀랄 일이 아니라고 말했다."]], [["The highwayman was full of expressions of thankfulness and gratitude.", "노상강도는 고마움과 감사의 말을 쏟아냈다."], ["He actually dropt tears, or pretended so to do.", "그는 실제로 눈물을 떨구거나, 그러는 척했다."], ["He vowed he would immediately return home, and would never afterwards commit such a transgression: whether he kept his word or no, perhaps may appear hereafter.", "그는 곧장 집으로 돌아가 다시는 그런 죄를 저지르지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 그가 그 약속을 지켰는지 아닌지는 아마 나중에 드러날 것이다."]], [["Our travellers having remounted their horses, arrived in town without encountering any new mishap. On the road much pleasant discourse passed between Jones and Partridge, on the subject of their last adventure: in which Jones exprest a great compassion for those highwaymen who are, by unavoidable distress, driven, as it were, to such illegal courses, as generally bring them to a shameful death: “I mean,” said he, “those only whose highest guilt extends no farther than to robbery, and who are never guilty of cruelty nor insult to any person, which is a circumstance that, I must say, to the honour of our country, distinguishes the robbers of England from those of all other nations; for murder is, amongst those, almost inseparably incident to robbery.”", "우리의 여행자들은 다시 말에 올라, 새로운 변고 하나 없이 도시에 다다랐다. 길 위에서 존스와 파트리지 사이에는 방금 겪은 모험을 두고 유쾌한 이야기가 오갔다. 그 가운데 존스는, 어쩔 수 없는 궁핍에 떠밀려 말하자면 그런 불법의 길로 내몰리고, 그로 인해 대개 수치스러운 죽음에 이르고 마는 노상강도들에게 깊은 연민을 드러냈다. \"내가 말하는 건,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 죄가 강도질을 넘지 않는 자들, 그 누구에게도 잔혹하거나 모욕을 가하는 일이 없는 자들만을 두고 하는 말일세. 이건 우리나라의 명예를 위해 꼭 말해 두어야겠는데, 바로 그 점이 잉글랜드의 강도들을 다른 모든 나라의 강도들과 구별 짓는 점이지. 다른 나라에선 살인이 강도질과 거의 떼려야 뗄 수 없이 붙어 다니거든.\""]], [["“No doubt,”", "\"두말할 것 없이,\""], ["answered Partridge, “it is better to take away one's money than one's life; and yet it is very hard upon honest men, that they can't travel about their business without being in danger of these villains.", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"남의 목숨을 빼앗는 것보다야 돈을 빼앗는 편이 낫지요."], ["And to be sure it would be better that all rogues were hanged out of the way, than that one honest man should suffer.", "그래도 정직한 사람들로선, 그런 악당들 때문에 위험에 처하지 않고서는 제 볼일 보러 다니지도 못한다는 게 무척 고달픈 노릇입니다."], ["For my own part, indeed, I should not care to have the blood of any of them on my own hands; but it is very proper for the law to hang them all.", "그러니 정직한 사람 하나가 화를 입느니, 그런 악당들이 죄다 교수형에 처해져 사라지는 편이 분명 나을 겁니다. 저로 말하자면, 그자들 누구의 피든 제 손에 묻히고 싶진 않지만, 법이 그놈들을 다 매달아 버리는 건 지극히 마땅한 일이지요."], ["What right hath any man to take sixpence from me, unless I give it him?", "제가 주지도 않은 육 펜스를 대체 무슨 권리로 빼앗는단 말입니까?"], ["Is there any honesty in such a man?”", "그런 자에게 무슨 정직함이 있겠어요?\""]], [["“No, surely,”", "\"그렇고말고,\""], ["cries Jones, “no more than there is in him who takes the horses out of another man's stable, or who applies to his own use the money which he finds, when he knows the right owner.”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"그건 남의 마구간에서 말을 끌어내는 자나, 주운 돈의 임자가 누군지 뻔히 알면서도 제 것인 양 써버리는 자에게 정직함이 없는 것과 마찬가지지.\""]], [["These hints stopt the mouth of Partridge; nor did he open it again till Jones, having thrown some sarcastical jokes on his cowardice, he offered to excuse himself on the inequality of fire-arms, saying, “A thousand naked men are nothing to one pistol; for though it is true it will kill but one at a single discharge, yet who can tell but that one may be himself?”", "이 말에 파트리지는 입을 다물었다. 그러고는 존스가 그의 비겁함을 두고 빈정대는 농담을 몇 마디 던지기 전까지 다시 입을 열지 않았다. 그러자 그는 화기(火器)가 공평치 못했다는 핑계로 자신을 변명하려 들며 이렇게 말했다. \"맨손인 사람 천 명도 권총 하나엔 아무것도 아니죠. 한 번 쏘면 한 명밖에 못 죽이는 게 사실이라지만, 그 한 명이 바로 저 자신이 아니라고 누가 장담하겠습니까?\""]], [["An Invocation.", "열이틀의 기간을 담은 권."]], [["Come, bright love of fame, inspire my glowing breast: not thee I will call, who, over swelling tides of blood and tears, dost bear the heroe on to glory, while sighs of millions waft his spreading sails; but thee, fair, gentle maid, whom Mnesis, happy nymph, first on the banks of Hebrus did produce.", "영감을 청함. 오라, 명예를 향한 빛나는 사랑이여, 달아오른 내 가슴에 영감을 불어넣어다오."], ["Thee, whom Maeonia educated, whom Mantua charmed, and who, on that fair hill which overlooks the proud metropolis of Britain, sat'st, with thy Milton, sweetly tuning the heroic lyre; fill my ravished fancy with the hopes of charming ages yet to come.", "다만 내가 부르려는 것은, 피와 눈물의 부풀어 오르는 물결 위로 영웅을 영광으로 실어 나르며, 수백만의 한숨이 그 펼쳐진 돛을 밀어 주는 그런 사랑은 아니다. 오히려 내가 부르려는 것은, 행복한 님프 므네시스가 헤브루스 강가에서 처음 낳은, 아름답고 온화한 처녀 그대다."], ["Foretel me that some tender maid, whose grandmother is yet unborn, hereafter, when, under the fictitious name of Sophia, she reads the real worth which once existed in my Charlotte, shall from her sympathetic breast send forth the heaving sigh.", "마이오니아가 길렀고, 만투아가 매혹했으며, 브리튼의 도도한 수도를 굽어보는 저 아름다운 언덕 위에 그대의 밀턴과 더불어 앉아 영웅의 칠현금을 감미롭게 조율하던 그대여, 다가올 매혹의 시대에 대한 희망으로 황홀해진 내 상상을 가득 채워다오. 내게 예언해다오."], ["Do thou teach me not only to foresee, but to enjoy, nay, even to feed on future praise.", "그 할머니가 아직 태어나지도 않은 어느 다정한 처녀가 훗날, 소피아라는 지어낸 이름 아래 한때 나의 샬럿에게 실재했던 참된 가치를 읽을 때, 그 공감 어린 가슴에서 들썩이는 한숨을 토해 내리라고. 그대는 내게 미래의 칭송을 내다보는 법뿐 아니라, 그것을 누리는 법, 아니 그것을 양식 삼아 살아가는 법까지 가르쳐다오."], ["Comfort me by a solemn assurance, that when the little parlour in which I sit at this instant shall be reduced to a worse furnished box, I shall be read with honour by those who never knew nor saw me, and whom I shall neither know nor see.", "엄숙한 확언으로 나를 위로해다오. 지금 이 순간 내가 앉아 있는 이 작은 거실이 더 초라하게 꾸며진 관(棺) 한 칸으로 줄어들 때에도, 나를 알지도 보지도 못했고, 나 또한 알지도 보지도 못할 사람들이 영예로이 나를 읽으리라고."]], [["And thou, much plumper dame, whom no airy forms nor phantoms of imagination cloathe; whom the well-seasoned beef, and pudding richly stained with plums, delight: thee I call: of whom in a treckschuyte, in some Dutch canal, the fat ufrow gelt, impregnated by a jolly merchant of Amsterdam, was delivered: in Grub-street school didst thou suck in the elements of thy erudition.", "그리고 그대, 한층 더 통통한 부인이여, 어떤 공기 같은 형상도 상상의 환영도 그대를 감싸지 못하고, 잘 양념된 소고기와 자두로 푸짐하게 물든 푸딩이 그대를 기쁘게 하는 그대여, 그대를 부른다. 어느 네덜란드 운하의 거룻배 안에서, 암스테르담의 유쾌한 상인에게 잉태된 살진 우프로 겔트가 낳은 그대여."], ["Here hast thou, in thy maturer age, taught poetry to tickle not the fancy, but the pride of the patron.", "그럽 가(街)의 학교에서 그대는 학식의 기초를 빨아들였다. 여기서 그대는, 한층 무르익은 나이에 이르러, 시(詩)에게 상상이 아니라 후원자의 자만심을 간질이는 법을 가르쳤다."], ["Comedy from thee learns a grave and solemn air; while tragedy storms aloud, and rends th' affrighted theatres with its thunders.", "희극은 그대에게서 근엄하고 엄숙한 태도를 배우고, 비극은 큰 소리로 폭풍처럼 휘몰아치며 그 천둥으로 겁먹은 극장들을 찢어 놓는다."], ["To soothe thy wearied limbs in slumber, Alderman History tells his tedious tale; and, again, to awaken thee, Monsieur Romance performs his surprizing tricks of dexterity.", "그대의 지친 사지를 잠으로 달래려, 시의원 역사 선생이 지루한 이야기를 늘어놓고, 다시 그대를 깨우려, 로맨스 선생이 놀라운 손재주의 묘기를 부린다."], ["Nor less thy well-fed bookseller obeys thy influence.", "잘 먹고 자란 그대의 서적상도 그대의 영향력에 못지않게 복종한다."], ["By thy advice the heavy, unread, folio lump, which long had dozed on the dusty shelf, piecemealed into numbers, runs nimbly through the nation.", "그대의 조언에 따라, 먼지 쌓인 선반 위에서 오래 졸고 있던, 읽히지 않은 그 묵직한 2절판 덩어리가 여러 분책으로 쪼개져 온 나라를 날렵하게 누빈다."], ["Instructed by thee, some books, like quacks, impose on the world by promising wonders; while others turn beaus, and trust all their merits to a gilded outside.", "그대의 가르침을 받아, 어떤 책들은 돌팔이처럼 경이로움을 약속하며 세상을 속이고, 또 어떤 책들은 멋쟁이로 변신하여 제 모든 가치를 금박 입힌 겉모습에 내맡긴다."], ["Come, thou jolly substance, with thy shining face, keep back thy inspiration, but hold forth thy tempting rewards; thy shining, chinking heap; thy quickly convertible bank-bill, big with unseen riches; thy often-varying stock; the warm, the comfortable house; and, lastly, a fair portion of that bounteous mother, whose flowing breasts yield redundant sustenance for all her numerous offspring, did not some too greedily and wantonly drive their brethren from the teat.", "오라, 빛나는 얼굴을 한 그대 유쾌한 실체여, 그대의 영감일랑 거두어 두고, 대신 그대의 솔깃한 보상을 내밀어 보여다오. 그대의 번쩍이며 짤랑대는 무더기, 눈에 보이지 않는 부로 잔뜩 부풀어 금세 현금으로 바꿀 수 있는 그대의 은행 어음, 끊임없이 값이 오르내리는 그대의 주식, 따뜻하고 안락한 집, 그리고 마지막으로, 흐르는 젖가슴으로 그 수많은 자식 모두에게 넘치도록 양식을 베푸는 그 너그러운 어머니의 — 다만 더러 너무 탐욕스럽고 방자하게 제 형제들을 젖꼭지에서 밀쳐 내지만 않았더라면 — 정당한 몫 한 자락을."], ["Come thou, and if I am too tasteless of thy valuable treasures, warm my heart with the transporting thought of conveying them to others.", "오라 그대여, 그리고 만일 내가 그대의 값진 보물을 너무도 분별없이 대한다면, 그것을 남들에게 전하리라는 황홀한 생각으로 내 마음을 데워다오."], ["Tell me, that through thy bounty, the pratling babes, whose innocent play hath often been interrupted by my labours, may one time be amply rewarded for them.", "내게 말해다오. 그대의 너그러움 덕분에, 그 천진한 놀이가 나의 노고에 자주 가로막혔던 종알대는 아기들이 언젠가 그 노고에 대해 넉넉히 보답받게 되리라고."]], [["And now, this ill-yoked pair, this lean shadow and this fat substance, have prompted me to write, whose assistance shall I invoke to direct my pen?", "그런데 이제, 이 어울리지 않게 한데 묶인 한 쌍, 이 야윈 그림자와 이 살진 실체가 나로 하여금 글을 쓰도록 부추겼으니, 내 펜을 이끌어 달라고 누구의 도움을 청해야 할까?"]], [["First, Genius; thou gift of Heaven; without whose aid in vain we struggle against the stream of nature.", "먼저, 천재(天才)여, 그대 하늘의 선물이여. 그대의 도움 없이는 우리가 자연의 물줄기를 거슬러 헛되이 발버둥칠 따름이다."], ["Thou who dost sow the generous seeds which art nourishes, and brings to perfection.", "그대는 너그러운 씨앗을 뿌리고, 기예가 그것을 길러 완성으로 이끈다."], ["Do thou kindly take me by the hand, and lead me through all the mazes, the winding labyrinths of nature.", "부디 다정하게 내 손을 잡고, 자연의 온갖 미로, 그 구불구불한 미궁을 두루 지나도록 이끌어다오."], ["Initiate me into all those mysteries which profane eyes never beheld.", "불경한 눈이 결코 본 적 없는 그 모든 신비 속으로 나를 들여보내다오."], ["Teach me, which to thee is no difficult task, to know mankind better than they know themselves.", "그대에게는 조금도 어렵지 않을 일이니, 인류를 그들 자신이 아는 것보다 더 잘 아는 법을 내게 가르쳐다오."], ["Remove that mist which dims the intellects of mortals, and causes them to adore men for their art, or to detest them for their cunning, in deceiving others, when they are, in reality, the objects only of ridicule, for deceiving themselves.", "인간의 지성을 흐리게 하는 저 안개를 걷어다오. 그 안개는 사람들로 하여금, 남을 속이는 자를 그 재주 때문에 떠받들거나 그 교활함 때문에 혐오하게 만들지만, 정작 그들은 제 자신을 속였다는 점에서 비웃음의 대상일 뿐이다."], ["Strip off the thin disguise of wisdom from self-conceit, of plenty from avarice, and of glory from ambition.", "자만심에서 지혜라는 얄팍한 가면을, 탐욕에서 풍요라는 가면을, 야망에서 영광이라는 가면을 벗겨다오."], ["Come, thou that hast inspired thy Aristophanes, thy Lucian, thy Cervantes, thy Rabelais, thy Molière, thy Shakespear, thy Swift, thy Marivaux, fill my pages with humour; till mankind learn the good-nature to laugh only at the follies of others, and the humility to grieve at their own.", "오라, 그대의 아리스토파네스, 그대의 루키아노스, 그대의 세르반테스, 그대의 라블레, 그대의 몰리에르, 그대의 셰익스피어, 그대의 스위프트, 그대의 마리보에게 영감을 불어넣었던 그대여, 내 페이지를 해학으로 가득 채워다오. 마침내 인류가, 오직 남의 어리석음에만 웃을 만큼의 선한 마음과, 제 자신의 어리석음에는 슬퍼할 만큼의 겸손을 배우게 될 때까지."]], [["And thou, almost the constant attendant on true genius, Humanity, bring all thy tender sensations.", "그리고 그대, 참된 천재에게 거의 변함없이 따라붙는 동반자, 인간애(人間愛)여, 그대의 온갖 다정한 감정을 모두 가져와다오."], ["If thou hast already disposed of them all between thy Allen and thy Lyttleton, steal them a little while from their bosoms.", "만일 그대가 이미 그 감정들을 그대의 앨런과 그대의 리틀턴에게 죄다 나누어 주었다면, 잠시만 그들의 품에서 슬쩍 빌려와다오."], ["Not without these the tender scene is painted.", "이런 감정 없이는 애틋한 장면이 그려지지 않는다."], ["From these alone proceed the noble, disinterested friendship, the melting love, the generous sentiment, the ardent gratitude, the soft compassion, the candid opinion; and all those strong energies of a good mind, which fill the moistened eyes with tears, the glowing cheeks with blood, and swell the heart with tides of grief, joy, and benevolence.", "이 감정에서만 고귀하고 사심 없는 우정, 녹아드는 사랑, 너그러운 마음씨, 뜨거운 감사, 부드러운 연민, 솔직한 견해가 우러나며, 또 촉촉이 젖은 눈에 눈물을 채우고, 달아오른 뺨에 피를 돌게 하며, 가슴을 슬픔과 기쁨과 자애의 물결로 부풀어 오르게 하는, 선한 마음의 그 모든 강렬한 힘이 솟아난다."]], [["And thou, O Learning!", "그리고 그대, 오 학식(學識)이여!"], ["(for without thy assistance nothing pure, nothing correct, can genius produce) do thou guide my pen.", "(그대의 도움 없이는 천재도 순수한 것, 올바른 것을 결코 만들어 내지 못하므로) 부디 내 펜을 인도해다오."], ["Thee in thy favourite fields, where the limpid, gently-rolling Thames washes thy Etonian banks, in early youth I have worshipped.", "맑고 잔잔히 흐르는 템스강이 그대의 이튼 강기슭을 씻어 내리는 그대가 가장 아끼는 들판에서, 나는 어린 시절에 그대를 경배했다."], ["To thee, at thy birchen altar, with true Spartan devotion, I have sacrificed my blood.", "그대의 자작나무 제단 앞에서, 참된 스파르타식 헌신으로 나는 내 피를 바쳤다."], ["Come then, and from thy vast, luxuriant stores, in long antiquity piled up, pour forth the rich profusion.", "그러니 오라, 그리고 아득한 옛적부터 쌓아 올린 그대의 광대하고 풍요로운 곳간에서 그 풍성함을 쏟아부어다오."], ["Open thy Maeonian and thy Mantuan coffers, with whatever else includes thy philosophic, thy poetic, and thy historical treasures, whether with Greek or Roman characters thou hast chosen to inscribe the ponderous chests: give me a while that key to all thy treasures, which to thy Warburton thou hast entrusted.", "그대의 마이오니아 궤와 만투아 궤를, 또 그리스 글자로든 로마 글자로든 그대가 새겨 넣기로 택한 그 묵직한 상자들이 품은, 그대의 철학적·시적·역사적 보물 무엇이든 모두 열어다오. 그대가 그대의 워버튼에게 맡겨 둔, 그대의 온갖 보물로 통하는 그 열쇠를 잠시만 내게 빌려다오."]], [["Lastly, come Experience, long conversant with the wise, the good, the learned, and the polite.", "끝으로, 오라 경험이여, 지혜로운 자, 선한 자, 학식 있는 자, 세련된 자와 오래 어울려 온 그대여."], ["Nor with them only, but with every kind of character, from the minister at his levee, to the bailiff in his spunging-house; from the dutchess at her drum, to the landlady behind her bar.", "아니, 그들뿐 아니라 온갖 부류의 인물과도 어울려 온 그대여. 접견실의 대신에서부터 채무자 가둔 집의 집행관까지, 무도회의 공작 부인에서부터 술집 카운터 뒤의 여주인까지."], ["From thee only can the manners of mankind be known; to which the recluse pedant, however great his parts or extensive his learning may be, hath ever been a stranger.", "인류의 풍속은 오직 그대에게서만 알 수 있으니, 아무리 재능이 뛰어나고 학식이 넓다 한들, 세상과 등진 학자는 그것에 늘 낯선 이방인이었다."]], [["Come all these, and more, if possible; for arduous is the task I have undertaken; and, without all your assistance, will, I find, be too heavy for me to support.", "이들 모두 오라, 그리고 가능하다면 더 많이도 오라. 내가 떠맡은 일은 고된 것이어서, 그대들 모두의 도움 없이는 내가 떠받치기에 너무 무거우리라는 것을 나는 안다."], ["But if you all smile on my labours I hope still to bring them to a happy conclusion.", "그러나 그대들 모두가 나의 노고에 미소 지어 준다면, 나는 그래도 이 일을 행복한 결말로 이끌어 가기를 바란다."]], [["What befel Mr Jones on his arrival in London.", "런던에 도착한 존스에게 일어난 일."]], [["The learned Dr Misaubin used to say, that the proper direction to him was To Dr Misaubin, in the World; intimating that there were few people in it to whom his great reputation was not known.", "학식 깊은 미소뱅 박사는 자기 앞으로 오는 편지의 올바른 수신처는 \"세상에 계신 미소뱅 박사께\"라고 말하곤 했다. 세상에 자신의 대단한 명성을 모르는 사람이 거의 없다는 뜻이었다."], ["And, perhaps, upon a very nice examination into the matter, we shall find that this circumstance bears no inconsiderable part among the many blessings of grandeur.", "그리고 어쩌면, 이 문제를 아주 면밀히 따져 본다면, 우리는 이 사정이 위세가 누리는 그 숱한 축복 가운데서도 결코 작지 않은 몫을 차지함을 알게 될 것이다."]], [["The great happiness of being known to posterity, with the hopes of which we so delighted ourselves in the preceding chapter, is the portion of few.", "후세에 이름이 알려지는 그 크나큰 행복, 앞 장에서 우리가 그 희망에 그토록 들떠 마지않던 그 행복은 오직 소수만이 누리는 몫이다."], ["To have the several elements which compose our names, as Sydenham expresses it, repeated a thousand years hence, is a gift beyond the power of title and wealth; and is scarce to be purchased, unless by the sword and the pen.", "시드넘이 표현한 대로, 우리 이름을 이루는 여러 낱낱의 글자들이 천 년 뒤에까지 되풀이되어 불리는 일이란, 지위와 부의 힘으로도 미치지 못하는 선물이며, 칼과 펜이 아니고서는 좀처럼 사들일 수 없는 것이다."], ["But to avoid the scandalous imputation, while we yet live, of being one whom nobody knows (a scandal, by the bye, as old as the days of Homer[*]) will always be the envied portion of those, who have a legal title either to honour or estate.", "그러나 우리가 아직 살아 있는 동안에 \"아무도 모르는 자\"라는 수치스러운 낙인을 면하는 일은(덧붙이자면, 이는 호메로스 시절만큼이나 오래된 수치다) 명예든 재산이든 합법적 권리를 지닌 이들이 늘 부러움을 사며 누리는 몫이다."]], [["[*] See the 2d Odyssey, ver. 175.", "[*] 〈오디세이아〉 제2권, 175행을 보라."]], [["From that figure, therefore, which the Irish peer, who brought Sophia to town, hath already made in this history, the reader will conclude, doubtless, it must have been an easy matter to have discovered his house in London without knowing the particular street or square which he inhabited, since he must have been one whom everybody knows. To say the truth, so it would have been to any of those tradesmen who are accustomed to attend the regions of the great; for the doors of the great are generally no less easy to find than it is difficult to get entrance into them. But Jones, as well as Partridge, was an entire stranger in London; and as he happened to arrive first in a quarter of the town, the inhabitants of which have very little intercourse with the householders of Hanover or Grosvenor-square (for he entered through Gray's-inn-lane), so he rambled about some time before he could even find his way to those happy mansions where fortune segregates from the vulgar those magnanimous heroes, the descendants of antient Britons, Saxons, or Danes, whose ancestors, being born in better days, by sundry kinds of merit, have entailed riches and honour on their posterity.", "그러므로 소피아를 도시로 데려온 그 아일랜드 귀족이 이 이야기 속에서 이미 보여 준 그 위세로 미루어 보아, 독자는 분명 이렇게 결론지을 것이다. 그가 사는 거리나 광장을 따로 몰라도 런던에서 그의 저택을 찾아내기란 쉬운 일이었으리라고. 그야말로 \"누구나 아는 사람\" 중 하나였을 테니 말이다. 사실대로 말하자면, 위세 높은 이들의 영역을 드나드는 데 익숙한 상인들에게라면 정말 그러했을 것이다. 위세 높은 이들의 문이란, 들어가기가 어려운 만큼이나 찾아내기는 대체로 쉬운 법이니까. 그러나 존스는 파트리지와 마찬가지로 런던에서는 완전한 이방인이었다. 게다가 그는 하필 그 도시에서, 하노버 광장이나 그로스베너 광장의 저택 주인들과는 거의 왕래가 없는 사람들이 사는 구역에 먼저 들어서고 말았던 터라(그는 그레이스인 길을 통해 들어왔다), 운명이 미천한 무리로부터 떼어 따로 자리 잡게 해 놓은 그 행복한 저택들로 가는 길조차 찾기 전에 한참을 헤매고 돌아다녀야 했다. 그 저택의 주인들이란 고대 브리튼인, 색슨인, 데인인의 후예인 도량 넓은 영웅들이니, 그 조상들은 더 좋은 시절에 태어나 갖가지 공덕으로 후손에게 부와 명예를 물려준 이들이었다."]], [["Jones, being at length arrived at those terrestrial Elysian fields, would now soon have discovered his lordship's mansion; but the peer unluckily quitted his former house when he went for Ireland; and as he was just entered into a new one, the fame of his equipage had not yet sufficiently blazed in the neighbourhood; so that, after a successless enquiry till the clock had struck eleven, Jones at last yielded to the advice of Partridge, and retreated to the Bull and Gate in Holborn, that being the inn where he had first alighted, and where he retired to enjoy that kind of repose which usually attends persons in his circumstances.", "마침내 그 지상의 엘리시온 들판에 다다른 존스는 이제 곧 귀족의 저택을 찾아낼 듯했다. 그러나 그 귀족은 운 나쁘게도 아일랜드로 떠날 때 옛집을 비웠고, 막 새집으로 들어선 참이어서 그 마차 행렬의 명성이 아직 이웃에 충분히 떨치지 못한 상태였다. 그리하여 시계가 열한 시를 칠 때까지 헛되이 수소문하던 존스는 끝내 파트리지의 권유에 따라 홀번에 있는 불 앤드 게이트로 물러났다. 그곳은 그가 처음 내렸던 여관이었고, 그는 그곳으로 물러나 자신과 같은 처지의 사람에게 으레 찾아드는 그런 종류의 휴식을 누리고자 했다."]], [["Early in the morning he again set forth in pursuit of Sophia; and many a weary step he took to no better purpose than before.", "이튿날 이른 아침 그는 다시 소피아를 찾아 나섰고, 전과 다를 바 없이 헛수고로 끝나는 지친 걸음을 숱하게 옮겼다."], ["At last, whether it was that Fortune relented, or whether it was no longer in her power to disappoint him, he came into the very street which was honoured by his lordship's residence; and, being directed to the house, he gave one gentle rap at the door.", "그러다 마침내, 운명이 마음을 누그러뜨렸는지, 아니면 더는 그를 실망시킬 힘이 남지 않았는지, 그는 바로 그 귀족의 저택이 영광을 더하고 있는 그 거리로 들어섰다. 그리고 그 집을 안내받아, 문을 한 번 가볍게 두드렸다."]], [["The porter, who, from the modesty of the knock, had conceived no high idea of the person approaching, conceived but little better from the appearance of Mr Jones, who was drest in a suit of fustian, and had by his side the weapon formerly purchased of the serjeant; of which, though the blade might be composed of well-tempered steel, the handle was composed only of brass, and that none of the brightest. When Jones, therefore, enquired after the young lady who had come to town with his lordship, this fellow answered surlily, “That there were no ladies there.” Jones then desired to see the master of the house; but was informed that his lordship would see nobody that morning. And upon growing more pressing the porter said, “he had positive orders to let no person in; but if you think proper,” said he, “to leave your name, I will acquaint his lordship; and if you call another time you shall know when he will see you.”", "문지기는 그 조심스러운 노크 소리에서 다가온 사람을 그리 대단하게 여기지 않았는데, 존스 씨의 행색을 보고도 별반 나은 인상을 받지 못했다. 존스는 거친 무명천 옷을 입고 있었고, 옆구리에는 전에 하사관에게서 사들인 무기를 차고 있었는데, 칼날은 잘 벼린 강철로 되어 있었을지 몰라도 손잡이는 그저 놋쇠로, 그것도 그리 번쩍이지 않는 놋쇠로 만들어진 것이었다. 그래서 존스가 귀족과 함께 도시로 온 젊은 아가씨에 대해 묻자, 이 사내는 무뚝뚝하게 \"여기에는 아가씨 같은 분 안 계시오\" 하고 대답했다. 그러자 존스는 집주인을 만나기를 청했으나, 귀족께서는 오늘 아침 아무도 만나지 않으신다는 답을 들었다. 그래도 더 끈질기게 졸라대자 문지기가 말했다. \"아무도 들이지 말라는 분명한 분부를 받았소. 하지만 정 그러시다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"이름을 남겨 두시오. 내가 귀족께 여쭐 테니. 그리고 다음에 또 들르시면 언제 뵐 수 있을지 알려 드리리다.\""]], [["Jones now declared, “that he had very particular business with the young lady, and could not depart without seeing her.” Upon which the porter, with no very agreeable voice or aspect, affirmed, “that there was no young lady in that house, and consequently none could he see;” adding, “sure you are the strangest man I ever met with, for you will not take an answer.”", "이제 존스는 이렇게 잘라 말했다. \"그 젊은 아가씨께 아주 긴한 용무가 있어서, 그분을 뵙지 않고는 떠날 수가 없소.\" 그러자 문지기는 그리 곱지 않은 목소리와 표정으로 잘라 말했다. \"이 집에는 젊은 아가씨 같은 분이 없으니, 당연히 만날 분도 없다지 않소.\" 그러고는 한마디 덧붙였다. \"참, 당신처럼 별난 사람은 내 평생 처음 봤소. 도무지 대답을 곧이듣지를 않으니 말이오.\""]], [["I have often thought that, by the particular description of Cerberus, the porter of hell, in the 6th Aeneid, Virgil might possibly intend to satirize the porters of the great men in his time; the picture, at least, resembles those who have the honour to attend at the doors of our great men.", "나는 종종 이런 생각을 해 보았다. 베르길리우스가 〈아이네이스〉 제6권에서 지옥의 문지기 케르베로스를 그토록 자세히 묘사한 것은, 어쩌면 자기 시대 권세가들의 문지기를 풍자하려는 뜻이었을지 모른다고."], ["The porter in his lodge answers exactly to Cerberus in his den, and, like him, must be appeased by a sop before access can be gained to his master.", "적어도 그 묘사는 우리네 권세가의 문 앞을 지키는 영광을 누리는 자들과 꼭 닮았다. 자기 수위실에 앉은 문지기는 굴속의 케르베로스와 정확히 들어맞으며, 그처럼 미끼 한 점으로 달래야만 그 주인에게 다가갈 길이 열린다."], ["Perhaps Jones might have seen him in that light, and have recollected the passage where the Sibyl, in order to procure an entrance for Aeneas, presents the keeper of the Stygian avenue with such a sop.", "어쩌면 존스도 그를 그런 눈으로 보고서, 무녀가 아이네이아스를 들여보내려고 스틱스 강 길목의 문지기에게 그런 미끼를 바치는 대목을 떠올렸는지도 모른다."], ["Jones, in like manner, now began to offer a bribe to the human Cerberus, which a footman, overhearing, instantly advanced, and declared, “if Mr Jones would give him the sum proposed, he would conduct him to the lady.”", "존스 역시 그와 마찬가지로 이제 이 인간 케르베로스에게 뇌물을 내밀기 시작했는데, 이를 엿들은 하인 하나가 대뜸 나서서 이렇게 말했다. \"존스 씨가 제게 그 돈을 주신다면, 제가 아가씨께 안내해 드리겠습니다.\""], ["Jones instantly agreed, and was forthwith conducted to the lodging of Mrs Fitzpatrick by the very fellow who had attended the ladies thither the day before.", "존스는 즉시 응했고, 곧바로 피츠패트릭 부인의 거처로 안내되었으니, 바로 전날 그 아가씨들을 그리로 모셔다 준 바로 그 사내가 안내자였다."]], [["Nothing more aggravates ill success than the near approach to good.", "바라던 것에 가까이 다가갔다 놓치는 것만큼 실패의 쓰라림을 더하는 것은 없다."], ["The gamester, who loses his party at piquet by a single point, laments his bad luck ten times as much as he who never came within a prospect of the game.", "피케 노름에서 단 한 점 차로 판을 내준 노름꾼은, 애초에 이길 가망조차 보이지 않던 자보다 열 배는 더 제 불운을 한탄하는 법이다."], ["So in a lottery, the proprietors of the next numbers to that which wins the great prize are apt to account themselves much more unfortunate than their fellow-sufferers.", "복권도 마찬가지여서, 대박이 터진 번호의 바로 옆 번호를 쥐고 있던 자는 함께 떨어진 다른 이들보다 자신이 훨씬 더 불운하다고 여기기 쉽다."], ["In short, these kind of hairbreadth missings of happiness look like the insults of Fortune, who may be considered as thus playing tricks with us, and wantonly diverting herself at our expense.", "요컨대 이렇게 행복을 종이 한 장 차이로 놓치는 일은 운명의 모욕처럼 보인다. 운명이란 이렇듯 우리를 가지고 장난을 치며, 우리를 희생양 삼아 제멋대로 즐기는 존재라 여길 만하다."]], [["Jones, who more than once already had experienced this frolicsome disposition of the heathen goddess, was now again doomed to be tantalized in the like manner; for he arrived at the door of Mrs Fitzpatrick about ten minutes after the departure of Sophia.", "이미 한 번 이상 이 이교 여신의 짓궂은 변덕을 겪어 본 존스는, 이제 또다시 같은 식으로 애만 태우는 신세가 될 운명이었다. 그가 피츠패트릭 부인의 문 앞에 다다른 것은 소피아가 떠난 지 십 분쯤 지난 뒤였기 때문이다."], ["He now addressed himself to the waiting-woman belonging to Mrs Fitzpatrick; who told him the disagreeable news that the lady was gone, but could not tell him whither; and the same answer he afterwards received from Mrs Fitzpatrick herself.", "그는 이제 피츠패트릭 부인의 시중드는 여인에게 말을 건넸는데, 여인은 아가씨가 떠나셨다는 달갑지 않은 소식을 전하면서도 어디로 갔는지는 말해 주지 못했다. 그리고 나중에 피츠패트릭 부인 본인에게서도 똑같은 답을 들었다."], ["For as that lady made no doubt but that Mr Jones was a person detached from her uncle Western, in pursuit of his daughter, so she was too generous to betray her.", "그 부인은 존스가 숙부 웨스턴이 딸을 쫓으라고 보낸 사람임이 틀림없다고 여겼던 터라, 너무도 너그러운 마음에 소피아를 팔아넘길 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["Though Jones had never seen Mrs Fitzpatrick, yet he had heard that a cousin of Sophia was married to a gentleman of that name. This, however, in the present tumult of his mind, never once recurred to his memory; but when the footman, who had conducted him from his lordship's, acquainted him with the great intimacy between the ladies, and with their calling each other cousin, he then recollected the story of the marriage which he had formerly heard; and as he was presently convinced that this was the same woman, he became more surprized at the answer which he had received, and very earnestly desired leave to wait on the lady herself; but she as positively refused him that honour.", "존스는 피츠패트릭 부인을 한 번도 본 적이 없었지만, 소피아의 사촌이 그 성을 쓰는 신사와 결혼했다는 이야기는 들은 적이 있었다. 그러나 지금처럼 마음이 들끓는 와중에는 그 일이 단 한 번도 기억에 떠오르지 않았다. 그러다 귀족의 집에서 그를 안내해 온 하인이 두 여인 사이가 무척 가깝고 서로를 사촌이라 부른다고 일러 주자, 그제야 그는 전에 들었던 그 결혼 이야기를 떠올렸다. 그리고 이 여인이 바로 그 여인임을 곧바로 확신하게 되자, 자신이 들은 대답에 더욱 놀라며 그 부인을 직접 뵙게 해 달라고 간곡히 청했다. 그러나 부인은 그 영예를 단호히 거절했다."]], [["Jones, who, though he had never seen a court, was better bred than most who frequent it, was incapable of any rude or abrupt behaviour to a lady.", "존스는 궁정이라고는 한 번도 본 적 없었지만, 그곳을 드나드는 대다수보다 더 잘 자란 사람이어서, 숙녀에게 무례하거나 퉁명스럽게 구는 짓 따위는 할 줄 몰랐다. 그래서 단호한 거절을 받자, 그는 일단 물러나며 시중드는 여인에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["When he had received, therefore, a peremptory denial, he retired for the present, saying to the waiting-woman, “That if this was an improper hour to wait on her lady, he would return in the afternoon; and that he then hoped to have the honour of seeing her.”", "\"지금이 마님을 뵙기에 적절치 않은 시각이라면, 오후에 다시 오겠소. 그때는 부디 뵙는 영광을 누렸으면 하오.\""], ["The civility with which he uttered this, added to the great comeliness of his person, made an impression on the waiting-woman, and she could not help answering; “Perhaps, sir, you may;”", "그가 이 말을 건넨 정중함에, 더없이 준수한 그의 용모까지 더해져 시중드는 여인에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 여인은 저도 모르게 대답하고 말았다."], ["and, indeed, she afterwards said everything to her mistress, which she thought most likely to prevail on her to admit a visit from the handsome young gentleman; for so she called him.", "\"그러실 수도 있겠지요, 선생님.\" 그리고 실제로 그녀는 나중에 마님에게, 그 잘생긴 젊은 신사의(그녀는 그를 그렇게 불렀다) 방문을 받아들이도록 설득할 만하다 싶은 말은 죄다 늘어놓았다."]], [["Jones very shrewdly suspected that Sophia herself was now with her cousin, and was denied to him; which he imputed to her resentment of what had happened at Upton. Having, therefore, dispatched Partridge to procure him lodgings, he remained all day in the street, watching the door where he thought his angel lay concealed; but no person did he see issue forth, except a servant of the house, and in the evening he returned to pay his visit to Mrs Fitzpatrick, which that good lady at last condescended to admit.", "존스는 사촌과 함께 있는 사람이 다름 아닌 소피아 자신이며, 그녀가 자기를 만나기를 거절한 것이라고 제법 날카롭게 짐작했다. 그는 그것을 업튼에서 벌어진 일에 대한 그녀의 노여움 탓으로 돌렸다. 그래서 파트리지에게는 묵을 곳을 구해 오라 보내 놓고, 자신은 종일 거리에 남아 자기의 천사가 숨어 있으리라 여긴 그 문을 지켜보았다. 그러나 그 집 하인 하나 말고는 누구도 나오는 것을 보지 못했고, 저녁이 되자 그는 다시 피츠패트릭 부인을 찾아갔다. 그제야 그 마음씨 고운 부인은 마침내 면회를 허락하는 아량을 베풀었다."]], [["There is a certain air of natural gentility, which it is neither in the power of dress to give, nor to conceal.", "타고난 기품이라는 것이 있어서, 그것은 옷차림으로 부여할 수도 없거니와 가릴 수도 없다."], ["Mr Jones, as hath been before hinted, was possessed of this in a very eminent degree.", "앞서 넌지시 비친 대로, 존스 씨는 이 기품을 아주 두드러지게 지니고 있었다."], ["He met, therefore, with a reception from the lady somewhat different from what his apparel seemed to demand; and after he had paid her his proper respects, was desired to sit down.", "그래서 그는 그 부인에게서, 자기 옷차림이 마땅히 받아야 할 듯한 대접과는 사뭇 다른 응대를 받았다. 그리고 마땅한 예를 갖춰 인사를 올린 뒤, 자리에 앉으라는 권유를 받았다."]], [["The reader will not, I believe, be desirous of knowing all the particulars of this conversation, which ended very little to the satisfaction of poor Jones.", "독자는, 내 생각에, 이 대화의 시시콜콜한 내용을 다 알고 싶어 하지는 않을 것이다. 그것은 가엾은 존스에게는 거의 만족스럽지 못한 결말로 끝났으니 말이다."], ["For though Mrs Fitzpatrick soon discovered the lover (as all women have the eyes of hawks in those matters), yet she still thought it was such a lover, as a generous friend of the lady should not betray her to.", "피츠패트릭 부인은 이내 그가 연인임을 알아차렸지만(이런 문제에서는 모든 여인이 매의 눈을 지니고 있으니), 그래도 여전히 그가 마음 너그러운 벗이라면 아가씨를 팔아넘겨선 안 될 그런 부류의 연인이라고 여겼다."], ["In short, she suspected this was the very Mr Blifil, from whom Sophia had flown; and all the answers which she artfully drew from Jones, concerning Mr Allworthy's family, confirmed her in this opinion.", "요컨대 그녀는 이 사람이 바로 소피아가 달아나 온 그 블리필 씨라고 의심했다. 그리고 그녀가 올워디 씨 집안에 관해 존스에게서 교묘히 끌어낸 모든 대답이 이 생각을 더욱 굳혀 주었다."], ["She therefore strictly denied any knowledge concerning the place whither Sophia was gone; nor could Jones obtain more than a permission to wait on her again the next evening.", "그래서 그녀는 소피아가 어디로 갔는지에 대해 아는 바가 전혀 없다고 딱 잡아뗐다. 존스가 얻어 낸 것이라곤 이튿날 저녁에 다시 찾아와도 좋다는 허락뿐이었다."]], [["When Jones was departed Mrs Fitzpatrick communicated her suspicion concerning Mr Blifil to her maid; who answered, “Sure, madam, he is too pretty a man, in my opinion, for any woman in the world to run away from. I had rather fancy it is Mr Jones.”--“Mr Jones!” said the lady, “what Jones?” For Sophia had not given the least hint of any such person in all their conversation; but Mrs Honour had been much more communicative, and had acquainted her sister Abigail with the whole history of Jones, which this now again related to her mistress.", "존스가 떠나자 피츠패트릭 부인은 블리필 씨에 대한 자신의 의심을 하녀에게 털어놓았다. 그러자 하녀가 대답했다. \"어머, 마님, 제가 보기엔 그분은 세상 어떤 여자라도 도망쳐 나오기엔 너무 잘생긴 분인걸요. 저라면 그분이 존스 씨라고 여기겠어요.\" \"존스 씨라고!\" 부인이 말했다. \"존스가 누구냐?\" 소피아는 두 사람이 나눈 모든 대화에서 그런 사람에 대해 털끝만큼도 내비친 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 어너 부인은 훨씬 입이 가벼워서, 자기 동무인 애비게일에게 존스의 사연을 죄다 들려준 터였고, 이제 하녀가 그것을 다시 마님에게 풀어놓은 것이었다."]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick no sooner received this information, than she immediately agreed with the opinion of her maid; and, what is very unaccountable, saw charms in the gallant, happy lover, which she had overlooked in the slighted squire. “Betty,” says she, “you are certainly in the right: he is a very pretty fellow, and I don't wonder that my cousin's maid should tell you so many women are fond of him. I am sorry now I did not inform him where my cousin was; and yet, if he be so terrible a rake as you tell me, it is a pity she should ever see him any more; for what but her ruin can happen from marrying a rake and a beggar against her father's consent? I protest, if he be such a man as the wench described him to you, it is but an office of charity to keep her from him; and I am sure it would be unpardonable in me to do otherwise, who have tasted so bitterly of the misfortunes attending such marriages.”", "피츠패트릭 부인은 이 이야기를 듣자마자 곧바로 하녀의 의견에 동의했다. 그리고 참으로 알 수 없는 일이지만, 박대받은 그 시골 지주에게서는 못 본 척 지나쳤던 매력을, 이 늠름하고 행복한 연인에게서는 발견하고 말았다. \"베티야,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네 말이 분명 옳다. 정말이지 아주 잘생긴 사내로구나. 내 사촌의 하녀가 너에게 많은 여자가 그에게 빠져 있다고 한 것도 놀랄 일이 아니다. 이제 와 보니 내 사촌이 어디 있는지 그에게 알려 주지 않은 것이 후회스럽구나. 그렇지만 네 말대로 그가 그토록 지독한 난봉꾼이라면, 사촌이 다시는 그를 보지 않는 편이 차라리 낫겠지. 아버지의 허락도 없이 난봉꾼에다 빈털터리와 결혼해서 무엇이 따르겠느냐, 신세를 망치는 일밖에 더 있겠느냐? 단언컨대, 그가 그 계집애가 네게 묘사한 대로의 사람이라면, 사촌을 그에게서 떼어 놓는 것이야말로 자비를 베푸는 일이다. 그리고 나로서는 그렇게 하지 않는다면 결코 용서받지 못할 일이지. 그런 결혼에 따르는 불행을 이토록 쓰라리게 맛본 내가 아니냐.\""]], [["Here she was interrupted by the arrival of a visitor, which was no other than his lordship; and as nothing passed at this visit either new or extraordinary, or any ways material to this history, we shall here put an end to this chapter.", "이때 한 방문객이 찾아드는 바람에 그녀의 말은 끊기고 말았는데, 그가 바로 다름 아닌 그 귀족이었다. 그리고 이 방문에서는 새롭다거나 특별하다 할 일도, 이 이야기에 어떻게든 긴요한 일도 전혀 일어나지 않았으므로, 우리는 여기서 이 장을 끝맺기로 한다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["A project of Mrs Fitzpatrick, and her visit to Lady Bellaston. When Mrs Fitzpatrick retired to rest, her thoughts were entirely taken up by her cousin Sophia and Mr Jones.", "피츠패트릭 부인이 잠자리에 들었을 때, 그녀의 머릿속은 온통 사촌 소피아와 존스 씨로 가득 차 있었다."], ["She was, indeed, a little offended with the former, for the disingenuity which she now discovered.", "사실 그녀는 소피아에게 조금 서운한 마음이 들었는데, 이제야 그 솔직하지 못한 면을 알아차렸기 때문이다."], ["In which meditation she had not long exercised her imagination before the following conceit suggested itself; that could she possibly become the means of preserving Sophia from this man, and of restoring her to her father, she should, in all human probability, by so great a service to the family, reconcile to herself both her uncle and her aunt Western.", "그렇게 한참 생각에 잠겨 상상을 펼치던 차에, 문득 다음과 같은 생각이 떠올랐다. 만일 자신이 소피아를 이 남자에게서 지켜내어 아버지 품으로 돌려보내는 길잡이가 될 수 있다면, 그토록 큰 봉사를 집안에 베푼 셈이니, 인간의 짐작이 닿는 한, 자신은 외숙부와 외숙모 웨스턴 두 분 모두와 화해하게 되리라는 것이었다."]], [["As this was one of her most favourite wishes, so the hope of success seemed so reasonable, that nothing remained but to consider of proper methods to accomplish her scheme.", "이것은 그녀가 가장 바라 마지않던 소망 가운데 하나였고, 성공의 희망 또한 무척이나 그럴듯해 보였기에, 이제 남은 것이라곤 그 계획을 이룰 적당한 방법을 궁리하는 일뿐이었다."], ["To attempt to reason the case with Sophia did not appear to her one of those methods: for as Betty had reported from Mrs Honour, that Sophia had a violent inclination to Jones, she conceived that to dissuade her from the match was an endeavour of the same kind, as it would be very heartily and earnestly to entreat a moth not to fly into a candle.", "소피아와 직접 이치를 따져 설득해 보는 것은 그런 방법 가운데 하나로 보이지 않았다. 베티가 어너 부인에게서 전해 들은 바로는 소피아가 존스에게 격렬한 마음을 품고 있다 했으니, 이 혼사에서 그녀를 떼어 놓으려 애쓰는 것은, 나방에게 촛불 속으로 날아들지 말라고 진심으로 간곡히 애원하는 것과 다를 바 없는 노릇이라 여겼던 것이다."]], [["If the reader will please to remember that the acquaintance which Sophia had with Lady Bellaston was contracted at the house of Mrs Western, and must have grown at the very time when Mrs Fitzpatrick lived with this latter lady, he will want no information, that Mrs Fitzpatrick must have been acquainted with her likewise.", "소피아가 벨라스턴 부인과 알게 된 것이 웨스턴 부인의 집에서였고, 그 친분이 피츠패트릭 부인이 바로 이 웨스턴 부인과 함께 지내던 그 무렵에 깊어졌으리라는 사실을 독자가 부디 기억해 준다면, 피츠패트릭 부인 역시 그 부인과 알고 지냈으리라는 점은 굳이 일러 줄 필요도 없을 것이다."], ["They were, besides, both equally her distant relations.", "게다가 두 사람 모두 똑같이 그 부인의 먼 친척이기도 했다."]], [["After much consideration, therefore, she resolved to go early in the morning to that lady, and endeavour to see her, unknown to Sophia, and to acquaint her with the whole affair.", "그리하여 많은 궁리 끝에 그녀는 이튿날 아침 일찍 그 부인을 찾아가, 소피아 모르게 그녀를 만나 이 모든 사정을 알리기로 결심했다."], ["For she did not in the least doubt, but that the prudent lady, who had often ridiculed romantic love, and indiscreet marriages, in her conversation, would very readily concur in her sentiments concerning this match, and would lend her utmost assistance to prevent it.", "평소 대화에서 낭만적인 사랑과 분별없는 혼인을 자주 비웃던 그 신중한 부인이라면, 이 혼사에 관한 자신의 생각에 흔쾌히 동의하고, 그것을 막는 데 온 힘을 보태 주리라는 것을 그녀는 조금도 의심하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["This resolution she accordingly executed; and the next morning before the sun, she huddled on her cloaths, and at a very unfashionable, unseasonable, unvisitable hour, went to Lady Bellaston, to whom she got access, without the least knowledge or suspicion of Sophia, who, though not asleep, lay at that time awake in her bed, with Honour snoring by her side.", "이 결심을 그녀는 곧바로 실행에 옮겼다. 이튿날 아침 해가 뜨기도 전에 부랴부랴 옷을 걸치고는, 격식에도 맞지 않고 철도 맞지 않으며 방문하기에도 마땅찮은 시각에 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아갔다. 그리하여 소피아에게는 조금도 알려지거나 의심받는 일 없이 그 부인을 만날 수 있었으니, 소피아는 그때 잠들지는 않았으나 어너가 곁에서 코를 골며 자는 가운데 침대에 누워 깨어 있었다."]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick made many apologies for an early, abrupt visit, at an hour when, she said, “she should not have thought of disturbing her ladyship, but upon business of the utmost consequence.”", "피츠패트릭 부인은 이른 시각에 불쑥 찾아온 것을 두고 거듭 사과하며, 이런 시각에는 \"더없이 중대한 일이 아니고서야 부인께 폐를 끼칠 생각조차 하지 않았을 것\"이라고 말했다."], ["She then opened the whole affair, told all she had heard from Betty; and did not forget the visit which Jones had paid to herself the preceding evening.", "그러고는 이 모든 사정을 털어놓으며 베티에게서 들은 것을 죄다 전했고, 전날 저녁 존스가 자신을 찾아왔던 일도 빠뜨리지 않았다."]], [["Lady Bellaston answered with a smile, “Then you have seen this terrible man, madam; pray, is he so very fine a figure as he is represented?", "벨라스턴 부인은 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"그러니까 부인께서 그 무서운 사내를 보셨다는 거군요."], ["for Etoff entertained me last night almost two hours with him.", "그런데, 그 사람이 정말 소문대로 그토록 멋들어진 인물인가요? 에토프가 어젯밤에 거의 두 시간이나 그 사람 이야기로 저를 즐겁게 해 주었답니다."], ["The wench I believe is in love with him by reputation.”", "그 계집애는 소문만 듣고도 그 사람한테 반한 것 같아요.\" 여기서 독자는 의아해할 법하다."], ["Here the reader will be apt to wonder; but the truth is, that Mrs Etoff, who had the honour to pin and unpin the Lady Bellaston, had received compleat information concerning the said Mr Jones, and had faithfully conveyed the same to her lady last night (or rather that morning) while she was undressing; on which accounts she had been detained in her office above the space of an hour and a half.", "하지만 사실인즉, 벨라스턴 부인의 옷을 핀으로 여미고 풀어 주는 영예를 누리던 에토프 부인이 그 존스 씨에 관한 정보를 빠짐없이 입수해 두었다가, 어젯밤(아니, 정확히는 그날 새벽이라 해야겠다) 부인이 옷을 벗는 동안 그 이야기를 고스란히 전했던 것이다. 그런 까닭에 그녀는 한 시간 반이 넘도록 그 자리에서 붙들려 있었다."]], [["The lady indeed, though generally well enough pleased with the narratives of Mrs Etoff at those seasons, gave an extraordinary attention to her account of Jones; for Honour had described him as a very handsome fellow, and Mrs Etoff, in her hurry, added so much to the beauty of his person to her report, that Lady Bellaston began to conceive him to be a kind of miracle in nature.", "사실 그 부인은 그런 시각에 에토프 부인이 들려주는 이야기에 으레 흡족해하기는 했으나, 이번 존스에 관한 이야기에는 유난히 귀를 기울였다. 어너가 그를 무척 잘생긴 사내라고 묘사해 둔 데다, 에토프 부인이 흥에 겨워 자기 이야기 속에 그의 외모를 한껏 부풀려 보탰던 터라, 벨라스턴 부인은 그를 자연이 빚은 일종의 기적쯤으로 여기기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["The curiosity which her woman had inspired was now greatly increased by Mrs Fitzpatrick, who spoke as much in favour of the person of Jones as she had before spoken in dispraise of his birth, character, and fortune.", "자기 하녀가 불러일으킨 그 호기심은 이제 피츠패트릭 부인 덕분에 한층 더 부풀어 올랐다. 그녀는 앞서 존스의 출신과 인품과 재산을 헐뜯었던 만큼이나, 그의 사람됨에 대해서는 한껏 좋게 말했기 때문이다."]], [["When Lady Bellaston had heard the whole, she answered gravely, “Indeed, madam, this is a matter of great consequence.", "벨라스턴 부인은 모든 이야기를 다 듣고 나서 진지하게 대답했다. \"정말이지, 부인, 이건 대단히 중대한 일이군요."], ["Nothing can certainly be more commendable than the part you act; and I shall be very glad to have my share in the preservation of a young lady of so much merit, and for whom I have so much esteem.”", "부인께서 하시는 일만큼 칭찬받아 마땅한 것도 없겠지요. 저 또한 이토록 훌륭하고, 제가 그토록 존중해 마지않는 젊은 아가씨를 지켜내는 데 한몫을 거들 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다.\""]], [["“Doth not your ladyship think,”", "\"부인께서는 생각하지 않으세요.\""], ["says Mrs Fitzpatrick eagerly, “that it would be the best way to write immediately to my uncle, and acquaint him where my cousin is?”", "피츠패트릭 부인이 간절하게 말했다. \"당장 외숙부께 편지를 써서, 제 사촌이 어디 있는지 알려 드리는 것이 가장 좋은 방법이라고요?\""]], [["The lady pondered a little upon this, and thus answered--“Why, no, madam, I think not. Di Western hath described her brother to me to be such a brute, that I cannot consent to put any woman under his power who hath escaped from it. I have heard he behaved like a monster to his own wife, for he is one of those wretches who think they have a right to tyrannise over us, and from such I shall ever esteem it the cause of my sex to rescue any woman who is so unfortunate to be under their power.--The business, dear cousin, will be only to keep Miss Western from seeing this young fellow, till the good company, which she will have an opportunity of meeting here, give her a properer turn.”", "부인은 이 말을 잠시 곰곰이 헤아리더니 이렇게 대답했다. \"글쎄요, 부인, 저는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요. 디 웨스턴이 자기 오라비를 어찌나 짐승 같은 사람으로 묘사하던지, 일단 그 손아귀에서 벗어난 여자를 다시 그 밑으로 들여보내는 데에는 도저히 찬성할 수가 없군요. 듣자 하니 그 사람은 제 아내한테도 괴물처럼 굴었다더군요. 우리 여자들을 짓밟을 권리라도 있는 줄 아는 그런 못된 작자 가운데 하나이니 말이에요. 그런 자들의 손아귀에 들어갈 불운에 처한 여자라면 누구든 구해 내는 것을, 저는 언제나 우리 여성 모두의 대의라고 여길 거예요. 여보세요, 사촌, 할 일이란 그저 웨스턴 양이 이 젊은 사내를 만나지 못하게 막는 것뿐이에요. 여기서 좋은 사람들을 만날 기회가 생길 테니, 그러다 보면 그 애의 마음도 더 올바른 쪽으로 돌아설 거예요.\""]], [["“If he should find her out, madam,”", "\"만일 그 사람이 그 애를 찾아낸다면요, 부인.\""], ["answered the other, “your ladyship may be assured he will leave nothing unattempted to come at her.”", "다른 쪽이 대답했다. \"장담하건대 그 사람은 그 애한테 다가가려고 무슨 짓이든 마다하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“But, madam,” replied the lady, “it is impossible he should come here--though indeed it is possible he may get some intelligence where she is, and then may lurk about the house--I wish therefore I knew his person.", "\"하지만, 부인.\" 부인이 응수했다. \"그 사람이 이 집에 들어오기란 불가능한 일이지요. 다만 그 애가 어디 있는지 어떻게든 정보를 얻어 내, 집 주변을 어슬렁거릴 수는 있겠군요. 그러니 저로서는 그 사람 얼굴을 알아 두었으면 싶어요."]], [["“Is there no way, madam, by which I could have a sight of him? for, otherwise, you know, cousin, she may contrive to see him here without my knowledge.” Mrs Fitzpatrick answered, “That he had threatened her with another visit that afternoon, and that, if her ladyship pleased to do her the honour of calling upon her then, she would hardly fail of seeing him between six and seven; and if he came earlier she would, by some means or other, detain him till her ladyship's arrival.”--Lady Bellaston replied, “She would come the moment she could get from dinner, which she supposed would be by seven at farthest; for that it was absolutely necessary she should be acquainted with his person. Upon my word, madam,” says she, “it was very good to take this care of Miss Western; but common humanity, as well as regard to our family, requires it of us both; for it would be a dreadful match indeed.”", "부인, 제가 그 사람을 한번 볼 방법이 없을까요? 그렇지 않으면, 아시다시피, 사촌, 그 애가 제 모르게 여기서 그 사람을 만날 궁리를 할 수도 있으니 말이에요.\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 대답하기를, \"그 사람이 오늘 오후에 다시 찾아오겠다고 자기를 으름장 놓았으니, 만일 부인께서 그때 자기를 찾아 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 여섯 시에서 일곱 시 사이에 그 사람을 보시기 어렵지 않을 거예요. 그리고 그 사람이 더 일찍 온다면, 어떻게든 부인이 오실 때까지 그를 붙들어 두겠어요.\" 벨라스턴 부인이 답했다. \"식사 자리에서 빠져나오는 대로 곧장 오겠어요. 아무리 늦어도 일곱 시까지는 일어날 수 있을 테니까요. 그 사람 얼굴을 알아 두는 것이 무엇보다 꼭 필요한 일이거든요. 정말이지, 부인.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이렇게 웨스턴 양을 돌봐 주시다니 참으로 갸륵한 일이에요. 하지만 우리 집안에 대한 마음은 둘째 치고, 사람으로서의 도리만 따져도 우리 둘 다 마땅히 해야 할 일이지요. 그건 정말이지 끔찍한 혼사가 될 테니까요.\""]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick failed not to make a proper return to the compliment which Lady Bellaston had bestowed on her cousin, and, after some little immaterial conversation, withdrew; and, getting as fast as she could into her chair, unseen by Sophia or Honour, returned home.", "피츠패트릭 부인은 벨라스턴 부인이 자기 사촌에게 베푼 그 칭찬에 어김없이 합당한 답례를 올렸고, 이런저런 대수롭지 않은 이야기를 나눈 뒤 물러났다. 그러고는 소피아나 어너의 눈에 띄지 않게 부랴부랴 가마에 올라 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Which consists of visiting.", "방문으로 이루어진 장."]], [["Mr Jones had walked within sight of a certain door during the whole day, which, though one of the shortest, appeared to him to be one of the longest in the whole year. At length, the clock having struck five, he returned to Mrs Fitzpatrick, who, though it was a full hour earlier than the decent time of visiting, received him very civilly; but still persisted in her ignorance concerning Sophia.", "존스는 온종일 어떤 문이 보이는 거리 안에서 서성거렸다. 그날은 일 년 중 가장 짧은 날 가운데 하나였건만, 그에게는 가장 긴 날 가운데 하나로 여겨졌다. 마침내 시계가 다섯 시를 알리자, 그는 피츠패트릭 부인에게로 돌아갔다. 점잖은 방문 시각보다 한 시간이나 이른 때였지만, 부인은 그를 매우 정중하게 맞이했다. 그러면서도 소피아에 관해서는 여전히 아무것도 모른다고 우겼다."]], [["Jones, in asking for his angel, had dropped the word cousin, upon which Mrs Fitzpatrick said, “Then, sir, you know we are related: and, as we are, you will permit me the right of enquiring into the particulars of your business with my cousin.”", "존스는 자신의 천사를 찾으면서 사촌이라는 말을 무심코 흘렸고, 그러자 피츠패트릭 부인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 선생, 우리가 친척 사이라는 걸 아시겠군요."], ["Here Jones hesitated a good while, and at last answered, “He had a considerable sum of money of hers in his hands, which he desired to deliver to her.”", "사정이 그러하니, 제 사촌과 어떤 용건이 있으신지 그 세세한 내막을 캐물을 권리를 제게 허락하셔야겠어요.\" 여기서 존스는 한참을 머뭇거리다가 마침내 대답하기를, \"그녀의 상당한 액수의 돈을 자신이 맡아 가지고 있으며, 그것을 그녀에게 전해 주고 싶다\"고 했다."], ["He then produced the pocket-book, and acquainted Mrs Fitzpatrick with the contents, and with the method in which they came into his hands.", "그러고는 지갑을 꺼내어, 그 안에 든 것과 그것이 어떤 경위로 자신의 손에 들어오게 되었는지를 피츠패트릭 부인에게 알렸다."], ["He had scarce finished his story, when a most violent noise shook the whole house.", "그가 이야기를 채 끝내기도 전에, 더없이 사나운 소음이 온 집안을 뒤흔들었다."], ["To attempt to describe this noise to those who have heard it would be in vain; and to aim at giving any idea of it to those who have never heard the like, would be still more vain: for it may be truly said--\n--Non acuta Sic geminant Corybantes aera.", "그 소리를 들어 본 적 있는 사람들에게 그것을 묘사하려 드는 일은 부질없을 것이며, 그런 소리를 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 사람들에게 어떤 관념이나마 전하려 애쓰는 일은 더더욱 부질없을 것이다. 참으로 이렇게 말할 수 있으니—"]], [["The priests of Cybele do not so rattle their sounding brass.", "—날카롭게 울리는 키벨레 사제들의 청동도 이처럼 거듭 울리지는 않는다."]], [["In short, a footman knocked, or rather thundered, at the door.", "요컨대, 하인 하나가 문을 두드렸다, 아니 천둥처럼 내리쳤다. 존스는 그 소리에 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["Jones was a little surprized at the sound, having never heard it before; but Mrs Fitzpatrick very calmly said, that, as some company were coming, she could not make him any answer now; but if he pleased to stay till they were gone, she intimated she had something to say to him.", "전에는 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 피츠패트릭 부인은 매우 차분하게, 손님이 오는 중이라 지금은 그에게 어떤 답도 해 줄 수 없으나, 만약 그들이 돌아갈 때까지 기다려 주겠다면 그에게 할 말이 있다고 넌지시 일렀다."]], [["The door of the room now flew open, and, after pushing in her hoop sideways before her, entered Lady Bellaston, who having first made a very low courtesy to Mrs Fitzpatrick, and as low a one to Mr Jones, was ushered to the upper end of the room.", "이제 방문이 활짝 열리더니, 후프 치마를 옆으로 밀어 넣으며 벨라스턴 부인이 들어왔다. 그녀는 먼저 피츠패트릭 부인에게 매우 깊이 절을 하고, 존스에게도 그만큼 깊이 절을 한 뒤, 방의 상석으로 안내받았다."]], [["We mention these minute matters for the sake of some country ladies of our acquaintance, who think it contrary to the rules of modesty to bend their knees to a man.", "이런 사소한 일들을 굳이 적어 두는 것은, 우리가 아는 어떤 시골 부인들을 위해서다. 그들은 남자에게 무릎을 굽히는 것이 정숙함의 규칙에 어긋난다고 여기는 사람들이다."]], [["The company were hardly well settled, before the arrival of the peer lately mentioned, caused a fresh disturbance, and a repetition of ceremonials.", "손님들이 채 자리를 제대로 잡기도 전에, 앞서 언급한 그 귀족의 도착이 새로운 소동을 일으켰고, 인사 절차가 다시 한 번 되풀이되었다."]], [["These being over, the conversation began to be (as the phrase is) extremely brilliant.", "이것이 다 끝나자, 대화는 (흔히 말하듯) 더없이 빛나기 시작했다."], ["However, as nothing past in it which can be thought material to this history, or, indeed, very material in itself, I shall omit the relation; the rather, as I have known some very fine polite conversation grow extremely dull, when transcribed into books, or repeated on the stage.", "그러나 그 안에서는 이 이야기에 중요하다 할 만한 것도, 사실 그 자체로 그리 대단한 것도 오가지 않았으므로, 나는 그 내용을 옮기는 일을 생략하겠다. 더구나 나는 어떤 아주 멋지고 세련된 대화가 책에 옮겨지거나 무대 위에서 되풀이될 때 지독히도 따분해지는 것을 보아 왔기 때문이다."], ["Indeed, this mental repast is a dainty, of which those who are excluded from polite assemblies must be contented to remain as ignorant as they must of the several dainties of French cookery, which are served only at the tables of the great.", "사실 이런 정신의 만찬이란 하나의 진미여서, 세련된 모임에서 배제된 사람들은 그것을 모르는 채로 만족할 수밖에 없으니, 마치 오직 귀인의 식탁에만 오르는 프랑스 요리의 여러 진미를 모르는 채 지내야 하는 것과 같다."], ["To say the truth, as neither of these are adapted to every taste, they might both be often thrown away on the vulgar.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이 둘 다 누구의 입맛에나 맞는 것은 아니어서, 천한 사람들에게는 둘 다 종종 헛되이 버려지기 십상일 것이다."]], [["Poor Jones was rather a spectator of this elegant scene, than an actor in it; for though, in the short interval before the peer's arrival, Lady Bellaston first, and afterwards Mrs Fitzpatrick, had addressed some of their discourse to him; yet no sooner was the noble lord entered, than he engrossed the whole attention of the two ladies to himself; and as he took no more notice of Jones than if no such person had been present, unless by now and then staring at him, the ladies followed his example.", "가엾은 존스는 이 우아한 장면 속에서 배우라기보다 차라리 구경꾼이었다. 귀족이 도착하기 전의 짧은 틈에는 처음에는 벨라스턴 부인이, 그다음에는 피츠패트릭 부인이 대화의 일부를 그에게 건네기도 했으나, 그 고귀한 귀족이 들어서자마자 두 부인의 모든 관심을 자기 혼자 독차지해 버렸기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 이따금 존스를 빤히 쳐다보는 것 말고는 마치 그런 사람이 그 자리에 없기라도 한 듯 그에게 더 이상 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않자, 두 부인도 그의 본을 따랐다."]], [["The company had now staid so long, that Mrs Fitzpatrick plainly perceived they all designed to stay out each other.", "손님들이 이제 너무 오래 머무는 바람에, 피츠패트릭 부인은 그들 모두가 서로 끝까지 버티며 누가 더 오래 남나 겨루려 한다는 것을 빤히 알아차렸다."], ["She therefore resolved to rid herself of Jones, he being the visitant to whom she thought the least ceremony was due.", "그래서 부인은 존스를 떼어 버리기로 마음먹었으니, 그가 가장 격식을 차릴 필요가 적다고 여겨지는 방문객이었기 때문이다. 그리하여 잡담이 잠시 멎은 틈을 타, 부인은 그에게 진지하게 말을 건넸다."], ["Taking therefore an opportunity of a cessation of chat, she addressed herself gravely to him, and said, “Sir, I shall not possibly be able to give you an answer to-night as to that business; but if you please to leave word where I may send to you to-morrow---”", "\"선생, 그 일에 관해서는 오늘 밤 도저히 답을 드릴 수가 없겠어요. 하지만 괜찮으시다면, 내일 어디로 사람을 보내면 될지 말을 남겨 주시겠어요?\""]], [["Jones had natural, but not artificial good-breeding.", "존스는 타고난 좋은 예법은 갖추었으나, 꾸며 낸 예법은 없었다."], ["Instead therefore of communicating the secret of his lodgings to a servant, he acquainted the lady herself with it particularly, and soon after very ceremoniously withdrew.", "그래서 자기 숙소의 비밀을 하인에게 알리는 대신, 부인에게 직접 그것을 자세히 일러 주고는, 곧이어 매우 격식 있게 물러났다."]], [["He was no sooner gone than the great personages, who had taken no notice of him present, began to take much notice of him in his absence; but if the reader hath already excused us from relating the more brilliant part of this conversation, he will surely be very ready to excuse the repetition of what may be called vulgar abuse; though, perhaps, it may be material to our history to mention an observation of Lady Bellaston, who took her leave in a few minutes after him, and then said to Mrs Fitzpatrick, at her departure, “I am satisfied on the account of my cousin; she can be in no danger from this fellow.”", "그가 떠나기가 무섭게, 그가 있을 때는 그에게 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않던 그 거물들은 그가 없는 자리에서 그를 한껏 입에 올리기 시작했다. 그러나 독자가 이미 이 대화의 더 빛나는 부분을 옮기는 일을 우리에게 면해 주었다면, 천한 험담이라 할 만한 것을 되풀이하는 일은 분명 기꺼이 면해 줄 것이다. 다만 어쩌면 우리 이야기에 중요할지 모를 한 가지, 곧 벨라스턴 부인의 한마디만은 언급해 둘 만하다. 부인은 존스가 떠나고 몇 분 뒤 자리를 뜨면서, 떠나는 길에 피츠패트릭 부인에게 이렇게 말했다. \"내 사촌 일은 이제 마음이 놓이는군요. 그 애가 저런 자에게서 위험에 처할 일은 없겠어요.\""]], [["Our history shall follow the example of Lady Bellaston, and take leave of the present company, which was now reduced to two persons; between whom, as nothing passed, which in the least concerns us or our reader, we shall not suffer ourselves to be diverted by it from matters which must seem of more consequence to all those who are at all interested in the affairs of our heroe.", "우리의 이야기도 벨라스턴 부인의 본을 따라, 이제 두 사람으로 줄어든 지금의 일행에게 작별을 고하겠다. 두 사람 사이에는 우리나 우리 독자와 조금이라도 관계되는 일이 전혀 오가지 않았으니, 우리는 그것에 마음을 빼앗겨, 우리 주인공의 일에 조금이라도 관심을 둔 모든 이에게 더 중요하게 여겨질 만한 일들로부터 멀어지는 일이 없도록 하겠다."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["An adventure which happened to Mr Jones at his lodgings, with some account of a young gentleman who lodged there, and of the mistress of the house, and her two daughters.", "존스가 자기 숙소에서 겪은 모험, 그리고 그 집에 묵고 있던 한 젊은 신사와 집주인 여인, 그 두 딸에 관한 약간의 이야기."]], [["The next morning, as early as it was decent, Jones attended at Mrs Fitzpatrick's door, where he was answered that the lady was not at home; an answer which surprized him the more, as he had walked backwards and forwards in the street from break of day; and if she had gone out, he must have seen her.", "이튿날 아침, 예의에 어긋나지 않을 만큼 이른 시각이 되자 존스는 피츠패트릭 부인의 문 앞을 찾았으나, 부인은 집에 안 계신다는 대답을 들었다. 이 대답이 그를 더욱 놀라게 한 것은, 그가 동틀 무렵부터 그 거리를 이리저리 오가고 있었던 터라, 만일 부인이 외출했다면 분명 그 모습을 보았을 것이기 때문이었다."], ["This answer, however, he was obliged to receive, and not only now, but to five several visits which he made her that day.", "그러나 그는 이 대답을 받아들일 수밖에 없었으니, 그것도 이번 한 번만이 아니라 그날 하루 부인을 다섯 차례나 찾아갔을 때마다 그러했다."]], [["To be plain with the reader, the noble peer had from some reason or other, perhaps from a regard for the lady's honour, insisted that she should not see Mr Jones, whom he looked on as a scrub, any more; and the lady had complied in making that promise to which we now see her so strictly adhere.", "독자에게 솔직히 말하자면, 그 고귀한 귀족은 어떤 이유에서인지, 어쩌면 부인의 명예를 염려해서인지, 부인이 천한 작자로밖에 보지 않던 존스 씨를 다시는 만나서는 안 된다고 강하게 못 박았던 것이다. 그리고 부인은 그 약속을 받아들였으니, 지금 우리가 보는 대로 그토록 철저히 지키고 있는 것이다."]], [["But as our gentle reader may possibly have a better opinion of the young gentleman than her ladyship, and may even have some concern, should it be apprehended that, during this unhappy separation from Sophia, he took up his residence either at an inn, or in the street; we shall now give an account of his lodging, which was indeed in a very reputable house, and in a very good part of the town.", "그러나 우리의 점잖은 독자께서는 어쩌면 부인보다 이 젊은 신사를 더 좋게 보고 계실지도 모르고, 또 그가 소피아와 이렇게 불행하게 떨어져 지내는 동안 여관이나 길거리에서 거처를 정한 것은 아닐까 하는 걱정마저 품으실지 모르니, 이제 그의 숙소에 관해 이야기해 보겠다. 그곳은 사실 매우 평판 좋은 집이었고, 시내에서도 아주 괜찮은 동네에 자리하고 있었다."]], [["Mr Jones, then, had often heard Mr Allworthy mention the gentlewoman at whose house he used to lodge when he was in town.", "존스 씨는 올워디 씨가 시내에 머물 때 늘 묵곤 하던 어느 부인에 관해 자주 들어 두었던 터였다."], ["This person, who, as Jones likewise knew, lived in Bond-street, was the widow of a clergyman, and was left by him, at his decease, in possession of two daughters, and of a compleat set of manuscript sermons.", "존스 역시 알고 있었듯이 본드 거리에 사는 이 사람은 어느 성직자의 미망인으로, 남편이 세상을 떠나면서 그녀에게 두 딸과 직접 쓴 설교문 원고 한 묶음을 고스란히 남겼던 것이다."]], [["Of these two daughters, Nancy, the elder, was now arrived at the age of seventeen, and Betty, the younger, at that of ten.", "이 두 딸 가운데 언니 낸시는 이제 열일곱 살이 되었고, 동생 베티는 열 살이 되었다."]], [["Hither Jones had despatched Partridge, and in this house he was provided with a room for himself in the second floor, and with one for Partridge in the fourth.", "존스는 이리로 파트리지를 먼저 보냈고, 이 집에서 자신은 삼층에 방 하나를, 파트리지는 오층에 방 하나를 마련했다."]], [["The first floor was inhabited by one of those young gentlemen, who, in the last age, were called men of wit and pleasure about town, and properly enough; for as men are usually denominated from their business or profession, so pleasure may be said to have been the only business or profession of those gentlemen to whom fortune had made all useful occupations unnecessary.", "이층에는 지난 시대에 도시의 재치와 향락을 좇는 사람들이라 불리던 젊은 신사 가운데 하나가 살고 있었는데, 그 호칭은 꽤나 적절했다. 사람을 흔히 그가 하는 일이나 직업으로 일컫듯이, 운명이 모든 쓸모 있는 일거리를 불필요하게 만들어 준 이런 신사들에게는 향락이야말로 유일한 일이자 직업이었다고 말할 수 있기 때문이다."], ["Playhouses, coffeehouses, and taverns were the scenes of their rendezvous.", "극장과 커피점과 선술집이 그들이 만나는 무대였다."], ["Wit and humour were the entertainment of their looser hours, and love was the business of their more serious moments.", "재치와 해학은 한가로운 시간의 즐길 거리였고, 사랑은 좀 더 진지한 순간의 일거리였다."], ["Wine and the muses conspired to kindle the brightest flames in their breasts; nor did they only admire, but some were able to celebrate the beauty they admired, and all to judge of the merit of such compositions.", "술과 시의 여신들이 한데 어우러져 그들의 가슴속에 가장 환한 불꽃을 지폈으니, 그들은 단지 아름다움을 흠모하기만 한 것이 아니라, 더러는 흠모하는 아름다움을 노래로 기릴 줄도 알았고, 또 다들 그런 작품의 가치를 판단할 줄도 알았다."]], [["Such, therefore, were properly called the men of wit and pleasure; but I question whether the same appellation may, with the same propriety, be given to those young gentlemen of our times, who have the same ambition to be distinguished for parts.", "그러므로 그런 이들이야말로 마땅히 재치와 향락을 좇는 사람들이라 불릴 만했다. 그러나 우리 시대의 젊은 신사들, 곧 재능으로 두드러지려는 같은 야망을 품은 자들에게 똑같은 호칭을 똑같이 적절하게 붙일 수 있을지는 의문이다."], ["Wit certainly they have nothing to do with.", "재치로 말하자면 그들은 분명 아무런 관계가 없다."], ["To give them their due, they soar a step higher than their predecessors, and may be called men of wisdom and vertù (take heed you do not read virtue).", "공정하게 말하자면, 그들은 선배들보다 한 단계 더 높이 날아올라 지혜와 베르튀(부디 미덕이라고 읽지 마시기를)를 좇는 사람들이라 불릴 만하다."], ["Thus at an age when the gentlemen above mentioned employ their time in toasting the charms of a woman, or in making sonnets in her praise; in giving their opinion of a play at the theatre, or of a poem at Will's or Button's; these gentlemen are considering the methods to bribe a corporation, or meditating speeches for the House of Commons, or rather for the magazines.", "그리하여 앞서 말한 신사들이 한 여인의 매력에 술잔을 기울이거나 그녀를 기리는 소네트를 짓는 데, 극장의 연극이나 윌스나 버튼스에서 어느 시에 관한 의견을 내놓는 데 시간을 쓰던 그 나이에, 이 신사들은 어느 자치단체를 매수할 방법을 궁리하거나 하원에서 할 연설을, 아니 차라리 잡지에 실을 연설을 골똘히 생각한다."], ["But the science of gaming is that which above all others employs their thoughts.", "그러나 무엇보다도 그들의 머릿속을 차지하는 것은 도박의 학문이다."], ["These are the studies of their graver hours, while for their amusements they have the vast circle of connoisseurship, painting, music, statuary, and natural philosophy, or rather unnatural, which deals in the wonderful, and knows nothing of Nature, except her monsters and imperfections.", "이것이 그들의 좀 더 진지한 시간에 매달리는 공부이고, 한편 그들이 즐기는 것으로는 감식안이라는 드넓은 세계, 곧 회화, 음악, 조각, 그리고 자연철학—아니 차라리 부자연철학이라 해야 할 것이니, 그것은 경이로운 것만을 다루고 자연에 대해서는 그 괴물과 결함밖에 알지 못한다—이 있다."]], [["When Jones had spent the whole day in vain enquiries after Mrs Fitzpatrick, he returned at last disconsolate to his apartment.", "존스가 하루 종일 피츠패트릭 부인을 헛되이 수소문하며 보낸 뒤, 마침내 풀이 죽은 채 자기 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["Here, while he was venting his grief in private, he heard a violent uproar below-stairs; and soon after a female voice begged him for heaven's sake to come and prevent murder.", "이곳에서 홀로 슬픔을 토해 내고 있는데, 아래층에서 요란한 소동이 들려왔다. 곧이어 한 여인의 목소리가 제발 내려와서 살인을 막아 달라고 그에게 애원했다."], ["Jones, who was never backward on any occasion to help the distressed, immediately ran down-stairs; when stepping into the dining-room, whence all the noise issued, he beheld the young gentleman of wisdom and vertù just before mentioned, pinned close to the wall by his footman, and a young woman standing by, wringing her hands, and crying out, “He will be murdered!", "어떤 경우에도 어려움에 빠진 사람을 돕는 일에 결코 뒤로 물러서는 법이 없는 존스는 즉시 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다. 그 모든 소리가 흘러나오던 식당으로 발을 들여놓자, 그는 앞서 말한 그 지혜와 베르튀를 좇는 젊은 신사가 하인에게 벽에 바짝 짓눌려 있는 것을 보았고, 그 곁에 한 젊은 여인이 서서 두 손을 비틀며 \"이 사람이 죽겠어요!"], ["he will be murdered!”", "죽겠어요!\""], ["and, indeed, the poor gentleman seemed in some danger of being choaked, when Jones flew hastily to his assistance, and rescued him, just as he was breathing his last, from the unmerciful clutches of the enemy.", "하고 외치고 있는 것을 보았다. 그리고 실로 그 가엾은 신사는 목이 졸려 죽을 위험에 처한 듯 보였는데, 존스가 급히 그를 도우러 날아들어, 그가 막 마지막 숨을 내쉬려는 찰나에 그 무자비한 적의 손아귀에서 그를 구해 냈다."]], [["Though the fellow had received several kicks and cuffs from the little gentleman, who had more spirit than strength, he had made it a kind of scruple of conscience to strike his master, and would have contented himself with only choaking him; but towards Jones he bore no such respect; he no sooner therefore found himself a little roughly handled by his new antagonist, than he gave him one of those punches in the guts which, though the spectators at Broughton's amphitheatre have such exquisite delight in seeing them, convey but very little pleasure in the feeling.", "이 작자는 힘보다 기개가 앞서던 그 작은 신사에게서 발길질과 주먹질을 여러 차례 당했음에도, 주인을 때리는 것만큼은 일종의 양심의 거리낌으로 삼아 그저 목을 조르는 것으로 만족하려던 참이었다. 그러나 존스에게는 그런 존중심을 품지 않았다. 그래서 새로 나타난 적수에게 다소 거칠게 다뤄지자마자, 그는 존스의 명치에 한 방을 먹였다. 그런 주먹질은 브로턴 원형 경기장의 구경꾼들에게는 더없는 기쁨을 안겨 주지만, 정작 맞는 사람에게는 즐거움이라곤 거의 전해 주지 못하는 법이다."]], [["The lusty youth had no sooner received this blow, than he meditated a most grateful return; and now ensued a combat between Jones and the footman, which was very fierce, but short; for this fellow was no more able to contend with Jones than his master had before been to contend with him.", "이 한 방을 맞자마자 우리의 건장한 젊은이는 더없이 고마운 답례를 궁리했다. 이제 존스와 하인 사이에 한바탕 격투가 벌어졌는데, 그것은 매우 사납기는 했으나 짧았다. 이 작자는 앞서 그의 주인이 그를 당해 내지 못했던 것처럼, 존스를 도저히 당해 낼 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["And now, Fortune, according to her usual custom, reversed the face of affairs.", "이제 운명의 여신은 늘 그러하듯 형세를 뒤집어 놓았다."], ["The former victor lay breathless on the ground, and the vanquished gentleman had recovered breath enough to thank Mr Jones for his seasonable assistance; he received likewise the hearty thanks of the young woman present, who was indeed no other than Miss Nancy, the eldest daughter of the house.", "좀 전의 승자는 숨도 못 쉬고 바닥에 나뒹굴었고, 패배했던 신사는 숨을 충분히 되찾아 때맞춰 도와준 존스 씨에게 고마움을 전했다. 그는 또한 그 자리에 있던 젊은 여인의 진심 어린 감사도 받았는데, 그 여인은 다름 아닌 그 집 맏딸 낸시 양이었다."]], [["The footman, having now recovered his legs, shook his head at Jones, and, with a sagacious look, cried--“O d--n me, I'll have nothing more to do with you; you have been upon the stage, or I'm d--nably mistaken.” And indeed we may forgive this his suspicion; for such was the agility and strength of our heroe, that he was, perhaps, a match for one of the first-rate boxers, and could, with great ease, have beaten all the muffled[*] graduates of Mr Broughton's school.", "이제 다리를 되찾은 하인은 존스를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 무언가 알겠다는 표정으로 외쳤다. \"에잇, 빌어먹을, 난 당신하곤 더 상대 안 하겠소. 당신 무대에 서 본 적이 있는 게 분명해, 아니면 내가 지독히 잘못 본 거고.\" 그리고 실로 우리는 그의 이 의심을 용서해 줄 만하다. 우리 주인공의 날렵함과 힘이 어찌나 대단했던지, 그는 어쩌면 일류 권투 선수와도 맞붙을 만했고, 브로턴 씨의 학교를 거친 솜장갑 낀 졸업생들쯤은 아주 손쉽게 모조리 때려눕힐 수 있었을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["[*] Lest posterity should be puzzled by this epithet, I think proper to explain it by an advertisement which was published Feb.", "* 솜장갑: 후세 사람들이 이 표현에 어리둥절해할까 싶어, 1747년 2월 1일에 실린 광고로 설명해 두는 것이 좋겠다."], ["1, 1747.", "주의—브로턴 씨는 적절한 도움을 받아, 헤이마켓에 있는 자기 집에 권투의 비법에 입문하고자 하는 이들을 가르칠 학교를 열고자 한다."], ["N.B.--Mr Broughton proposes, with proper assistance, to open an academy at his house in the Haymarket, for the instruction of those who are willing to be initiated in the mystery of boxing: where the whole theory and practice of that truly British art, with all the various stops, blows, cross-buttocks, &c., incident to combatants, will be fully taught and explained; and that persons of quality and distinction may not be deterred from entering into A course of those lectures, they will be given with the utmost tenderness and regard to the delicacy of the frame and constitution of the pupil, for which reason muffles are provided, that will effectually secure them from the inconveniency of black eyes, broken jaws, and bloody noses.", "이곳에서는 그 진정한 영국식 기술의 이론과 실기 전반이, 격투에 따르는 온갖 막기, 치기, 엇걸어 메치기 등과 더불어 빠짐없이 가르쳐지고 설명될 것이다. 또한 지체 높고 명망 있는 분들이 이 강좌를 듣기를 꺼리지 않도록, 수강생의 몸과 체질의 섬세함을 최대한 부드럽게 배려하여 강의가 진행될 것이며, 그런 까닭에 솜장갑이 마련되어 있어, 멍든 눈과 부러진 턱과 코피의 불편함으로부터 그들을 확실히 지켜 줄 것이다."]], [["The master, foaming with wrath, ordered his man immediately to strip, to which the latter very readily agreed, on condition of receiving his wages.", "주인은 분노로 입에 거품을 물고 하인에게 당장 옷을 벗으라고 명했고, 하인은 밀린 품삯을 받는다는 조건으로 선뜻 응했다."], ["This condition was presently complied with, and the fellow was discharged.", "이 조건은 곧바로 받아들여졌고, 그 작자는 해고되었다."]], [["And now the young gentleman, whose name was Nightingale, very strenuously insisted that his deliverer should take part of a bottle of wine with him; to which Jones, after much entreaty, consented, though more out of complacence than inclination; for the uneasiness of his mind fitted him very little for conversation at this time.", "이제 나이팅게일이라는 이름의 젊은 신사는 자기를 구해 준 사람에게 함께 포도주 한 병을 나누자고 한사코 권했다. 존스는 여러 차례 청을 받고서야 응했는데, 마음이 내켜서라기보다는 그저 사양하기 어려워서였다."], ["Miss Nancy likewise, who was the only female then in the house, her mamma and sister being both gone to the play, condescended to favour them with her company.", "이때 그의 마음이 너무도 편치 않아 대화를 나눌 기분이라곤 거의 들지 않았기 때문이다. 낸시 양도, 마침 어머니와 동생이 둘 다 연극을 보러 간 터라 그때 집 안에 있던 유일한 여자였는데, 기꺼이 함께 자리해 주는 호의를 베풀었다."]], [["When the bottle and glasses were on the table the gentleman began to relate the occasion of the preceding disturbance.", "술병과 잔이 식탁에 놓이자 신사는 앞서 벌어진 소동의 사연을 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“I hope, sir,” said he to Jones, “you will not from this accident conclude, that I make a custom of striking my servants, for I assure you this is the first time I have been guilty of it in my remembrance, and I have passed by many provoking faults in this very fellow, before he could provoke me to it; but when you hear what hath happened this evening, you will, I believe, think me excusable. I happened to come home several hours before my usual time, when I found four gentlemen of the cloth at whist by my fire;--and my Hoyle, sir--my best Hoyle, which cost me a guinea, lying open on the table, with a quantity of porter spilt on one of the most material leaves of the whole book. This, you will allow, was provoking; but I said nothing till the rest of the honest company were gone, and then gave the fellow a gentle rebuke, who, instead of expressing any concern, made me a pert answer, `That servants must have their diversions as well as other people; that he was sorry for the accident which had happened to the book, but that several of his acquaintance had bought the same for a shilling, and that I might stop as much in his wages, if I pleased.' I now gave him a severer reprimand than before, when the rascal had the insolence to---In short, he imputed my early coming home to----In short, he cast a reflection----He mentioned the name of a young lady, in a manner--in such a manner that incensed me beyond all patience, and, in my passion, I struck him.”", "\"이런 일이 있었다고 해서,\" 그가 존스에게 말했다. \"선생, 부디 제가 하인을 때리는 것을 버릇처럼 한다고 여기지는 마십시오. 제 기억으로 이런 죄를 저지른 건 이번이 처음이라고 분명히 말씀드리며, 그 작자가 저를 이 지경까지 부추기기 전에 그가 저지른 화나는 잘못을 여러 번 그냥 넘겨주었답니다. 그러나 오늘 저녁 무슨 일이 있었는지 들으시면, 아마 저를 용서해 줄 만하다 여기실 겁니다. 제가 평소보다 몇 시간이나 일찍 집에 돌아왔더니, 성직자 옷을 입은 신사 네 명이 제 난롯가에서 휘스트를 치고 있더군요. 게다가 제 호일 책이, 선생, 일 기니나 주고 산 제일 좋은 호일 책이 펼쳐진 채 식탁에 놓여 있고, 그 책 전체에서 가장 중요한 책장 가운데 한 장에 흑맥주가 잔뜩 쏟아져 있지 뭡니까. 이건 화날 만했다고 인정하시겠지요. 그래도 저는 그 점잖은 일행이 다 갈 때까지 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 그 작자에게 부드럽게 꾸짖었습니다. 그런데 그자는 미안한 기색은커녕 건방진 대답을 내놓더군요. '하인도 남들처럼 즐길 거리가 있어야 하는 법이다, 책에 생긴 일은 안됐지만 자기 아는 사람 여럿이 똑같은 책을 일 실링이면 샀더라, 그러니 원하시면 품삯에서 그만큼 떼어 가시라'고요. 제가 이번엔 전보다 더 호되게 나무랐더니, 그 무뢰한이 뻔뻔하게도—요컨대, 제가 일찍 집에 온 것을 두고—요컨대, 비아냥거리는 말을 하더군요—그자가 어떤 젊은 숙녀의 이름을 입에 올리는데, 그 말투가—그 말투가 어찌나 저를 더는 참을 수 없게 만들던지, 그만 화가 치밀어 제가 그를 때리고 말았습니다.\""]], [["Jones answered, “That he believed no person living would blame him; for my part,” said he, “I confess I should, on the last-mentioned provocation, have done the same thing.”", "존스가 대답했다. \"살아 있는 누구도 선생을 탓하지 않으리라 믿습니다. 저로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다. \"솔직히 방금 말씀하신 그런 도발을 당했더라면 저도 똑같이 했을 겁니다.\""]], [["Our company had not sat long before they were joined by the mother and daughter, at their return from the play.", "우리 일행이 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 연극을 보고 돌아온 어머니와 딸이 합류했다."], ["And now they all spent a very chearful evening together; for all but Jones were heartily merry, and even he put on as much constrained mirth as possible.", "이제 그들은 다 함께 매우 즐거운 저녁을 보냈으니, 존스를 빼고는 모두 진심으로 흥겨워했고, 그조차도 애써 할 수 있는 한 즐거운 척했다."], ["Indeed, half his natural flow of animal spirits, joined to the sweetness of his temper, was sufficient to make a most amiable companion; and notwithstanding the heaviness of his heart, so agreeable did he make himself on the present occasion, that, at their breaking up, the young gentleman earnestly desired his further acquaintance.", "사실 그의 타고난 활기와 쾌활함이 절반쯤만 살아나도, 그 부드러운 성품과 어우러져 더없이 사랑스러운 길동무가 되기에 충분했다. 마음이 무거웠음에도 그는 이날 자리에서 어찌나 유쾌하게 굴었던지, 자리가 파할 무렵 젊은 신사는 그와 더 가까이 사귀기를 간곡히 청했다."], ["Miss Nancy was well pleased with him; and the widow, quite charmed with her new lodger, invited him, with the other, next morning to breakfast.", "낸시 양도 그를 무척 마음에 들어 했고, 새 하숙인에게 완전히 반한 미망인은 그와 또 다른 신사를 이튿날 아침 식사에 초대했다."]], [["Jones on his part was no less satisfied.", "존스 쪽에서도 못지않게 흡족했다."], ["As for Miss Nancy, though a very little creature, she was extremely pretty, and the widow had all the charms which can adorn a woman near fifty.", "낸시 양으로 말하자면, 몸집은 아주 작아도 더없이 예뻤고, 미망인은 쉰을 바라보는 여인을 꾸며 줄 수 있는 온갖 매력을 다 지니고 있었다."], ["As she was one of the most innocent creatures in the world, so she was one of the most chearful.", "그녀는 세상에서 가장 순진한 사람 가운데 하나인 만큼, 또한 가장 쾌활한 사람 가운데 하나였다."], ["She never thought, nor spoke, nor wished any ill, and had constantly that desire of pleasing, which may be called the happiest of all desires in this, that it scarce ever fails of attaining its ends, when not disgraced by affectation.", "그녀는 결코 나쁜 것을 생각하지도, 말하지도, 바라지도 않았으며, 늘 남을 기쁘게 하려는 마음을 품고 있었다. 그 마음은 모든 바람 가운데 가장 행복한 것이라 할 만한데, 가식으로 흠집 나지만 않는다면 그 목적을 이루지 못하는 일이 거의 없기 때문이다."], ["In short, though her power was very small, she was in her heart one of the warmest friends.", "요컨대 그녀의 힘은 보잘것없었으나, 그 마음만큼은 더없이 따뜻한 벗 가운데 하나였다."], ["She had been a most affectionate wife, and was a most fond and tender mother.", "그녀는 더없이 다정한 아내였고, 더없이 자상하고 살뜰한 어머니였다."], ["As our history doth not, like a newspaper, give great characters to people who never were heard of before, nor will ever be heard of again, the reader may hence conclude, that this excellent woman will hereafter appear to be of some importance in our history.", "우리 이야기는 신문처럼 전에 들어 본 적도 없고 앞으로도 들을 일 없는 사람들에게 거창한 인물됨을 부여하지는 않으니, 독자께서는 이로써 이 훌륭한 여인이 앞으로 우리 이야기에서 어느 정도 중요한 자리를 차지하리라 짐작하셔도 좋다."]], [["Nor was Jones a little pleased with the young gentleman himself, whose wine he had been drinking.", "존스는 또한 자기가 그 포도주를 얻어 마신 그 젊은 신사 자신에게도 적잖이 호감을 느꼈다."], ["He thought he discerned in him much good sense, though a little too much tainted with town-foppery; but what recommended him most to Jones were some sentiments of great generosity and humanity, which occasionally dropt from him; and particularly many expressions of the highest disinterestedness in the affair of love.", "그는 그에게서 비록 도시풍의 멋부림에 다소 물들어 있긴 해도 상당한 분별력을 엿볼 수 있다고 여겼다. 그러나 무엇보다 존스의 마음을 끈 것은, 그에게서 이따금 흘러나오는 크나큰 너그러움과 인정 어린 마음씨였고, 특히 사랑의 문제에서 더없이 사심 없는 태도를 드러내는 여러 표현이었다."], ["On which subject the young gentleman delivered himself in a language which might have very well become an Arcadian shepherd of old, and which appeared very extraordinary when proceeding from the lips of a modern fine gentleman; but he was only one by imitation, and meant by nature for a much better character.", "이 주제에 관해 젊은 신사는 옛 아르카디아의 목동에게나 썩 어울릴 법한 말로 자신을 털어놓았는데, 그런 말이 현대의 멋쟁이 신사 입에서 나오니 무척이나 별스럽게 들렸다. 그러나 그는 그저 흉내로만 멋쟁이 신사였을 뿐, 본바탕은 훨씬 더 나은 인물로 타고난 사람이었다."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["What arrived while the company were at breakfast, with some hints concerning the government of daughters.", "일행은 어제 저녁 헤어질 때 서로에게 품었던 그 살가운 마음을 그대로 간직한 채 아침에 다시 모였다."], ["Our company brought together in the morning the same good inclinations towards each other, with which they had separated the evening before; but poor Jones was extremely disconsolate; for he had just received information from Partridge, that Mrs Fitzpatrick had left her lodging, and that he could not learn whither she was gone.", "그러나 가엾은 존스만큼은 더없이 풀이 죽어 있었다. 방금 파트리지에게서 피츠패트릭 부인이 묵던 거처를 떠났으며, 그녀가 어디로 갔는지는 도무지 알아낼 수 없었다는 소식을 전해 들었기 때문이다."], ["This news highly afflicted him, and his countenance, as well as his behaviour, in defiance of all his endeavours to the contrary, betrayed manifest indications of a disordered mind.", "이 소식은 그를 크게 괴롭혔고, 아무리 애써 감추려 해도 그의 표정이며 거동에는 마음이 헝클어졌다는 기색이 역력히 드러나고 말았다."]], [["The discourse turned at present, as before, on love; and Mr Nightingale again expressed many of those warm, generous, and disinterested sentiments upon this subject, which wise and sober men call romantic, but which wise and sober women generally regard in a better light. Mrs Miller (for so the mistress of the house was called) greatly approved these sentiments; but when the young gentleman appealed to Miss Nancy, she answered only, “That she believed the gentleman who had spoke the least was capable of feeling most.”", "지금 화제는 전과 마찬가지로 사랑으로 흘러갔다. 나이팅게일 씨는 이 주제를 두고 또다시 그 따뜻하고 너그럽고 사심 없는 감정을 여럿 늘어놓았는데, 지혜롭고 분별 있는 남자들은 이런 것을 두고 낭만적이라 부르지만, 지혜롭고 분별 있는 여자들은 대개 더 곱게 보아 준다. 밀러 부인(이 집 안주인의 이름이 그러했다)은 이런 감정에 크게 동의했다. 그러나 이 젊은 신사가 낸시 양에게 의견을 물었을 때, 그녀는 다만 이렇게만 답했다. \"가장 말을 적게 한 분이야말로 가장 깊이 느낄 줄 아는 분이라고 생각해요.\""]], [["This compliment was so apparently directed to Jones, that we should have been sorry had he passed it by unregarded.", "이 찬사는 워낙 대놓고 존스를 겨눈 것이어서, 만일 그가 그것을 흘려보냈다면 우리로서는 무척 안타까웠을 것이다."], ["He made her indeed a very polite answer, and concluded with an oblique hint, that her own silence subjected her to a suspicion of the same kind: for indeed she had scarce opened her lips either now or the last evening.", "그는 실로 그녀에게 매우 정중하게 답했고, 끝에 가서는 그녀 자신의 침묵 역시 똑같은 의심을 살 만하다는 점을 에둘러 일러 주었다. 사실 그녀는 지금도, 어젯밤에도 거의 입을 열지 않았으니 말이다."]], [["“I am glad, Nanny,”", "\"잘됐구나, 내니.\""], ["says Mrs Miller, “the gentleman hath made the observation; I protest I am almost of his opinion.", "밀러 부인이 말했다. \"이 신사분이 그 점을 짚어 주셨으니 말이다."], ["What can be the matter with you, child?", "정말이지 나도 거의 같은 생각이란다. 대체 무슨 일이니, 얘야?"], ["I never saw such an alteration.", "이렇게 변한 모습은 처음 본다."], ["What is become of all your gaiety?", "그 명랑하던 기운은 다 어디로 갔니?"], ["Would you think, sir, I used to call her my little prattler?", "선생님, 믿어지세요? 제가 이 아이를 제 작은 수다쟁이라고 부르곤 했답니다."], ["She hath not spoke twenty words this week.”", "그런데 이번 주에는 스무 마디도 안 했어요.\""]], [["Here their conversation was interrupted by the entrance of a maid-servant, who brought a bundle in her hand, which, she said, “was delivered by a porter for Mr Jones.”", "여기서 둘의 대화는 한 하녀가 들어오는 바람에 끊겼다. 하녀는 손에 꾸러미 하나를 들고 와서, \"짐꾼이 존스 씨께 전하라며 두고 갔습니다\"라고 말했다."], ["She added, “That the man immediately went away, saying, it required no answer.”", "그러고는 \"그 사람이 답장은 필요 없다는 말만 남기고 곧장 가 버렸어요\"라고 덧붙였다."]], [["Jones expressed some surprize on this occasion, and declared it must be some mistake; but the maid persisting that she was certain of the name, all the women were desirous of having the bundle immediately opened; which operation was at length performed by little Betsy, with the consent of Mr Jones: and the contents were found to be a domino, a mask, and a masquerade ticket.", "존스는 이 일에 다소 놀라움을 드러내며 무슨 착오일 것이라고 단언했다. 그러나 하녀가 이름만큼은 분명하다고 거듭 우기는 통에, 여자들은 모두 어서 그 꾸러미를 풀어 보고 싶어 했다. 마침내 존스 씨의 허락을 받아 어린 베치가 그 일을 해냈고, 안에서 나온 것은 도미노 한 벌과 가면 하나, 그리고 가면무도회 입장권 한 장이었다."]], [["Jones was now more positive than ever in asserting, that these things must have been delivered by mistake; and Mrs Miller herself expressed some doubt, and said, “She knew not what to think.”", "존스는 이제 그 어느 때보다도 단호하게, 이 물건들이 착오로 전해진 게 틀림없다고 주장했다. 밀러 부인 자신도 다소 미심쩍어하며 \"어찌 생각해야 할지 모르겠다\"고 말했다."], ["But when Mr Nightingale was asked, he delivered a very different opinion.", "그러나 나이팅게일 씨에게 물으니, 그는 사뭇 다른 견해를 내놓았다. \"선생, 제가 여기서 끌어낼 수 있는 결론은 단 하나, 선생이 무척 운 좋은 사람이라는 것뿐입니다.\""], ["“All I can conclude from it, sir,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “is, that you are a very happy man; for I make no doubt but these were sent you by some lady whom you will have the happiness of meeting at the masquerade.”", "\"이건 분명 어느 부인이 보낸 것이고, 선생은 그 가면무도회에서 그분을 만나는 행복을 누리게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["Jones had not a sufficient degree of vanity to entertain any such flattering imagination; nor did Mrs Miller herself give much assent to what Mr Nightingale had said, till Miss Nancy having lifted up the domino, a card dropt from the sleeve, in which was written as follows:--", "존스에게는 그런 우쭐한 상상을 품을 만큼의 허영심이 없었다. 밀러 부인 자신도 나이팅게일 씨의 말에 그다지 동의하지 않았다. 그런데 낸시 양이 도미노를 들어 올리자 소매에서 카드 한 장이 떨어졌고, 거기에는 다음과 같이 적혀 있었다. 존스 씨께."]], [["The queen of the fairies sends you this; Use her favours not amiss.", "요정의 여왕이 그대에게 이것을 보내니, 그 호의를 함부로 쓰지 마시기를."]], [["Mrs Miller and Miss Nancy now both agreed with Mr Nightingale; nay, Jones himself was almost persuaded to be of the same opinion.", "이제 밀러 부인과 낸시 양도 둘 다 나이팅게일 씨의 말에 동의했다. 아니, 존스 자신마저 거의 같은 생각으로 기울 뻔했다."], ["And as no other lady but Mrs Fitzpatrick, he thought, knew his lodging, he began to flatter himself with some hopes, that it came from her, and that he might possibly see his Sophia.", "그리고 자기 거처를 아는 부인은 피츠패트릭 부인밖에 없다고 여긴 그는, 이것이 그녀에게서 온 것이며 어쩌면 자신의 소피아를 만나게 될지도 모른다는 희망에 부풀기 시작했다. 이런 희망에는 분명 별다른 근거가 없었다."], ["These hopes had surely very little foundation; but as the conduct of Mrs Fitzpatrick, in not seeing him according to her promise, and in quitting her lodgings, had been very odd and unaccountable, he conceived some faint hopes, that she (of whom he had formerly heard a very whimsical character) might possibly intend to do him that service in a strange manner, which she declined doing by more ordinary methods.", "그러나 피츠패트릭 부인이 약속대로 그를 만나 주지도 않고 거처마저 떠나 버린 그 행동이 워낙 기이하고 영문 모를 일이었던지라, 그는 어렴풋한 희망 하나를 품게 되었다. 곧, 예전부터 무척 변덕스럽다는 평을 들어 온 그녀가, 흔한 방식으로는 마다한 그 도움을 어쩌면 이런 별난 방식으로 베풀려는 것인지도 모른다는 희망이었다."], ["To say the truth, as nothing certain could be concluded from so odd and uncommon an incident, he had the greater latitude to draw what imaginary conclusions from it he pleased.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이토록 기이하고 흔치 않은 일에서는 무엇 하나 확실한 결론을 끌어낼 수 없었으니, 그는 도리어 마음 가는 대로 어떤 상상의 결론이든 그려 볼 더 넓은 여지를 얻은 셈이었다."], ["As his temper therefore was naturally sanguine, he indulged it on this occasion, and his imagination worked up a thousand conceits, to favour and support his expectations of meeting his dear Sophia in the evening.", "그리하여 천성이 낙천적이던 그는 이번 기회에 그 기질을 한껏 풀어놓았고, 그의 상상은 그날 저녁 사랑하는 소피아를 만나리라는 기대를 떠받치고 부풀리려 천 가지 망상을 지어냈다."]], [["Reader, if thou hast any good wishes towards me, I will fully repay them by wishing thee to be possessed of this sanguine disposition of mind; since, after having read much and considered long on that subject of happiness which hath employed so many great pens, I am almost inclined to fix it in the possession of this temper; which puts us, in a manner, out of the reach of Fortune, and makes us happy without her assistance.", "독자여, 그대가 내게 무언가 좋은 마음을 품고 있다면, 나는 그대 또한 이런 낙천적 기질을 지니기를 바라는 것으로 그 마음을 온전히 갚으려 한다. 그토록 많은 위대한 펜들이 매달려 온 행복이라는 주제를 오래 읽고 길이 헤아려 본 끝에, 나는 행복이란 거의 이 기질을 지니는 데 있다고 여기는 쪽으로 기울었기 때문이다."], ["Indeed, the sensations of pleasure it gives are much more constant as well as much keener, than those which that blind lady bestows; nature having wisely contrived, that some satiety and languor should be annexed to all our real enjoyments, lest we should be so taken up by them, as to be stopt from further pursuits.", "이 기질은 우리를 말하자면 운명의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 데려다 놓으며, 그녀의 도움 없이도 우리를 행복하게 해 준다. 실로 이 기질이 안기는 즐거움의 느낌은, 저 눈먼 여신이 베푸는 것보다 훨씬 한결같을 뿐 아니라 훨씬 더 날카롭다."], ["I make no manner of doubt but that, in this light, we may see the imaginary future chancellor just called to the bar, the archbishop in crape, and the prime minister at the tail of an opposition, more truly happy than those who are invested with all the power and profit of those respective offices.", "자연은 지혜롭게도, 우리가 누리는 모든 실제 즐거움에는 어느 정도의 물림과 나른함을 함께 붙여 두었으니, 우리가 그것에 너무 사로잡혀 더 나아갈 길을 멈추는 일이 없게 하려 함이다. 이런 눈으로 보자면, 이제 막 변호사로 불려 나온 상상 속의 미래 대법관이나, 검은 상복 같은 옷을 걸친 대주교나, 야당의 꽁무니에 붙은 수상이, 그 각각의 자리에 따르는 온갖 권세와 이익을 거머쥔 자들보다 도리어 더 참되게 행복하다는 것을 나는 조금도 의심하지 않는다."]], [["Mr Jones having now determined to go to the masquerade that evening, Mr Nightingale offered to conduct him thither.", "이제 존스 씨가 그날 저녁 가면무도회에 가기로 마음을 굳히자, 나이팅게일 씨는 자신이 그를 데려다주겠노라 나섰다. 그러면서 이 젊은 신사는 낸시 양과 그 어머니에게도 입장권을 권했다."], ["The young gentleman, at the same time, offered tickets to Miss Nancy and her mother; but the good woman would not accept them.", "그러나 마음씨 고운 부인은 이를 받지 않았다. 그녀는 이렇게 말했다."], ["She said, “she did not conceive the harm which some people imagined in a masquerade; but that such extravagant diversions were proper only for persons of quality and fortune, and not for young women who were to get their living, and could, at best, hope to be married to a good tradesman.”----“A tradesman!”", "\"어떤 이들이 가면무도회에 있다고 상상하는 그 해악을 저는 잘 모르겠어요. 다만 그렇게 분에 넘치는 놀이는 지체 높고 재산 있는 분들에게나 어울리지, 제 손으로 먹고살아야 하고 잘해야 착실한 장사꾼에게 시집가기를 바랄 수밖에 없는 처녀들에게 맞는 것은 아니지요.\"—\"장사꾼이라뇨!\""], ["cries Nightingale, “you shan't undervalue my Nancy.", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"제 낸시를 그렇게 낮춰 보시면 안 됩니다."], ["There is not a nobleman upon earth above her merit.”", "이 세상에 그녀의 됨됨이보다 나은 귀족은 없습니다.\""], ["“O fie! Mr Nightingale,”", "\"어머, 무슨 말씀을, 나이팅게일 씨.\""], ["answered Mrs Miller, “you must not fill the girl's head with such fancies: but if it was her good luck”", "밀러 부인이 답했다. \"그런 헛된 공상으로 아이 머릿속을 채우시면 안 돼요."], ["(says the mother with a simper) “to find a gentleman of your generous way of thinking, I hope she would make a better return to his generosity than to give her mind up to extravagant pleasures.", "하지만\"—어머니는 빙긋 웃으며 말을 이었다—\"이 아이가 운이 좋아 선생님처럼 너그러운 마음을 지닌 신사를 만난다면, 분에 넘치는 즐거움에 마음을 내주는 것보다 그 너그러움에 더 나은 보답을 할 줄 알기를 바라요. 정말이지, 처녀가 제 손으로 큰 재산을 가져오는 경우라면 제 것을 쓰겠다고 고집할 권리쯤은 있겠지요."], ["Indeed, where young ladies bring great fortunes themselves, they have some right to insist on spending what is their own; and on that account I have heard the gentlemen say, a man has sometimes a better bargain with a poor wife, than with a rich one.----But let my daughters marry whom they will, I shall endeavour to make them blessings to their husbands:----I beg, therefore, I may hear of no more masquerades.", "그래서 신사분들이 하는 말을 들어 보면, 남자에게는 부유한 아내보다 가난한 아내가 때로 더 남는 흥정이라더군요.—하지만 제 딸들이 누구와 혼인하든, 저는 그 아이들이 남편에게 복이 되도록 힘쓸 거예요.—그러니 부디 가면무도회 이야기는 더 듣지 않았으면 합니다. 낸시는 분명 가고 싶다고 조를 만큼 철없는 아이가 아니에요."], ["Nancy is, I am certain, too good a girl to desire to go; for she must remember when you carried her thither last year, it almost turned her head; and she did not return to herself, or to her needle, in a month afterwards.”", "작년에 선생님이 그 애를 거기 데려가셨을 때, 하마터면 정신이 다 나갈 뻔했던 일을 그 아이도 기억할 테니까요. 그 뒤로 한 달이 지나서야 겨우 제정신으로, 또 제 바느질거리로 돌아왔답니다.\""]], [["Though a gentle sigh, which stole from the bosom of Nancy, seemed to argue some secret disapprobation of these sentiments, she did not dare openly to oppose them.", "낸시의 가슴에서 가만히 새어 나온 부드러운 한숨이 이 말에 대한 어떤 은밀한 못마땅함을 드러내는 듯했으나, 그녀는 감히 드러내 놓고 맞서지는 못했다."], ["For as this good woman had all the tenderness, so she had preserved all the authority of a parent; and as her indulgence to the desires of her children was restrained only by her fears for their safety and future welfare, so she never suffered those commands which proceeded from such fears to be either disobeyed or disputed.", "이 마음씨 고운 부인은 부모로서의 온갖 다정함을 지녔으되, 부모로서의 온갖 권위 또한 고스란히 지키고 있었기 때문이다. 자식들의 바람을 너그러이 받아 주는 그녀의 마음은 오직 그들의 안전과 앞날의 행복을 염려하는 마음에만 제동이 걸렸으니, 그런 염려에서 나온 분부만큼은 결코 거역당하거나 따져지는 일을 용납하지 않았다."], ["And this the young gentleman, who had lodged two years in the house, knew so well, that he presently acquiesced in the refusal.", "그리고 이 집에서 두 해를 묵어 온 이 젊은 신사는 그것을 너무도 잘 알았기에, 그 거절에 곧바로 수긍하고 말았다."]], [["Mr Nightingale, who grew every minute fonder of Jones, was very desirous of his company that day to dinner at the tavern, where he offered to introduce him to some of his acquaintance; but Jones begged to be excused, “as his cloaths,”", "나이팅게일 씨는 시시각각 존스를 더욱 아끼게 되어, 그날 선술집에서 함께 저녁을 들기를 몹시 바랐다. 거기서 그는 자기 지인 몇몇에게 존스를 소개하겠다고 했다."], ["he said, “were not yet come to town.”", "그러나 존스는 \"아직 옷가지가 시내에 도착하지 않았다\"는 핑계로 양해를 구했다."]], [["To confess the truth, Mr Jones was now in a situation, which sometimes happens to be the case of young gentlemen of much better figure than himself.", "사실대로 털어놓자면, 존스 씨는 이제 자기보다 훨씬 행색이 번듯한 젊은 신사들에게도 더러 닥치는 그런 처지에 놓여 있었다."], ["In short, he had not one penny in his pocket; a situation in much greater credit among the antient philosophers than among the modern wise men who live in Lombard-street, or those who frequent White's chocolate-house.", "한마디로, 그의 주머니에는 단 한 푼도 없었다. 이런 처지는 롬바드 거리에 사는 현대의 현인들이나 화이트 초콜릿 하우스를 들락거리는 자들 사이에서보다, 옛 철학자들 사이에서 훨씬 더 높은 평판을 누렸다."], ["And, perhaps, the great honours which those philosophers have ascribed to an empty pocket may be one of the reasons of that high contempt in which they are held in the aforesaid street and chocolate-house.", "그리고 어쩌면, 저 철학자들이 빈 주머니에 돌린 그 큰 영예야말로, 앞서 말한 그 거리와 초콜릿 하우스에서 그들이 받는 그 깊은 멸시의 한 가지 까닭일는지도 모른다."]], [["Now if the antient opinion, that men might live very comfortably on virtue only, be, as the modern wise men just above-mentioned pretend to have discovered, a notorious error; no less false is, I apprehend, that position of some writers of romance, that a man can live altogether on love; for however delicious repasts this may afford to some of our senses or appetites, it is most certain it can afford none to others.", "자, 사람이 오직 덕만으로도 무척 편안히 살 수 있다는 옛 견해가, 방금 말한 현대의 현인들이 발견했노라 떠벌리듯, 명백한 오류라고 치자. 그렇다면 내가 보기에 그에 못지않게 거짓된 것이 바로, 사람이 오로지 사랑만으로 살 수 있다는 어떤 연애소설 작가들의 주장이다."], ["Those, therefore, who have placed too great a confidence in such writers, have experienced their error when it was too late; and have found that love was no more capable of allaying hunger, than a rose is capable of delighting the ear, or a violin of gratifying the smell.", "사랑이 우리의 어떤 감각이나 욕구에는 더없이 달콤한 만찬을 차려 줄지 몰라도, 다른 감각에는 아무것도 차려 주지 못한다는 것만큼은 가장 확실하기 때문이다. 그러니 그런 작가들을 지나치게 믿어 버린 이들은 너무 늦어서야 그 오류를 몸으로 겪었으며, 사랑이 허기를 달래 주는 데는, 장미가 귀를 즐겁게 하거나 바이올린이 코를 만족시키는 것만큼이나 무력하다는 것을 깨닫고 말았다."]], [["Notwithstanding, therefore, all the delicacies which love had set before him, namely, the hopes of seeing Sophia at the masquerade; on which, however ill-founded his imagination might be, he had voluptuously feasted during the whole day, the evening no sooner came than Mr Jones began to languish for some food of a grosser kind.", "그리하여 사랑이 그의 앞에 차려 놓은 온갖 진미에도—다시 말해, 가면무도회에서 소피아를 만나리라는 희망, 그 상상이 아무리 근거가 빈약했을지언정 그날 하루 내내 그가 황홀히 누린 그 향연에도—저녁이 찾아오기 무섭게 존스 씨는 좀 더 거친 종류의 양식을 갈망하기 시작했다."], ["Partridge discovered this by intuition, and took the occasion to give some oblique hints concerning the bank-bill; and, when these were rejected with disdain, he collected courage enough once more to mention a return to Mr Allworthy.", "파트리지는 직감으로 이를 알아챘고, 이때를 틈타 은행권에 관해 몇 마디 에두른 암시를 던졌다. 그것이 경멸 속에 물리쳐지자, 그는 다시 한번 용기를 그러모아 올워디 씨에게로 돌아가는 일을 입에 올렸다."]], [["“Partridge,”", "\"파트리지.\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “you cannot see my fortune in a more desperate light than I see it myself; and I begin heartily to repent that I suffered you to leave a place where you was settled, and to follow me.", "\"내 처지를 자네가 나보다 더 절망적으로 볼 수는 없을 거야. 나도 그렇게 보고 있으니까."], ["However, I insist now on your returning home; and for the expense and trouble which you have so kindly put yourself to on my account, all the cloaths I left behind in your care I desire you would take as your own.", "그리고 자네가 자리 잡고 있던 곳을 떠나 나를 따라오도록 내버려 둔 것을, 나는 진심으로 뉘우치기 시작했네. 그러니 이제 자네에게 집으로 돌아가라고 강권하겠네."], ["I am sorry I can make you no other acknowledgment.”", "그리고 자네가 나 때문에 그토록 고맙게도 들인 비용과 수고에 대해서는, 내가 자네에게 맡겨 두고 온 그 옷가지를 모두 자네 것으로 삼아 주게. 그 외에 달리 보답할 길이 없어 미안하네.\""]], [["He spoke these words with so pathetic an accent, that Partridge, among whose vices ill-nature or hardness of heart were not numbered, burst into tears; and after swearing he would not quit him in his distress, he began with the most earnest entreaties to urge his return home.", "그가 어찌나 애절한 어조로 이 말을 했던지, 그 악덕 가운데 심술이나 모진 마음만큼은 끼어 있지 않던 파트리지가 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다. 그는 어려움에 처한 그를 결코 떠나지 않겠다고 맹세한 뒤, 더없이 간절한 애원으로 집으로 돌아가시라고 채근하기 시작했다."], ["“For heaven's sake, sir,”", "\"제발 부탁입니다, 도련님.\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “do but consider; what can your honour do?--how is it possible you can live in this town without money?", "\"한번 생각해 보세요. 도련님이 대체 무엇을 하실 수 있겠습니까?—돈 한 푼 없이 이 도시에서 어떻게 사실 수 있단 말입니까?"], ["Do what you will, sir, or go wherever you please, I am resolved not to desert you.", "뭘 하시든, 어디로 가시든, 저는 도련님을 버리지 않기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["But pray, sir, consider--do pray, sir, for your own sake, take it into your consideration; and I'm sure,”", "하지만 제발, 도련님, 생각해 보세요.—제발, 도련님, 도련님 자신을 위해서, 부디 헤아려 보세요. 도련님의 그 분별력이라면 분명,\""], ["says he, “that your own good sense will bid you return home.”", "그가 말했다. \"집으로 돌아가라고 이르실 겁니다.\""]], [["“How often shall I tell thee,”", "\"내가 몇 번이나 말해야 알아듣겠나.\""], ["answered Jones, “that I have no home to return to?", "존스가 답했다. \"내게는 돌아갈 집이 없다고."], ["Had I any hopes that Mr Allworthy's doors would be open to receive me, I want no distress to urge me--nay, there is no other cause upon earth, which could detain me a moment from flying to his presence; but, alas!", "올워디 씨의 문이 나를 맞아 주려 열려 있으리란 희망이 조금이라도 있다면, 나를 떠밀 어떤 곤경도 필요치 않을 걸세.—아니, 이 세상에 그분 앞으로 날아가는 나를 한순간이라도 붙들어 둘 다른 이유란 없을 거야. 하지만, 아아!"], ["that I am for ever banished from.", "나는 그곳에서 영원히 추방당한 몸일세."], ["His last words were--O, Partridge, they still ring in my ears--his last words were, when he gave me a sum of money--what it was I know not, but considerable I'm sure it was--his last words were--`I am resolved from this day forward, on no account to converse with you any more.'”", "그분의 마지막 말씀은—오, 파트리지, 그 말씀이 아직도 내 귓가에 울리네—그분의 마지막 말씀은, 내게 얼마간의 돈을 주시면서—그게 얼마였는지는 모르나 적지 않은 액수였던 건 분명하네—그 마지막 말씀은 이러했네. '나는 오늘 이후로 어떤 일이 있어도 다시는 너와 말을 섞지 않기로 굳게 마음먹었다.'\""]], [["Here passion stopt the mouth of Jones, as surprize for a moment did that of Partridge; but he soon recovered the use of speech, and after a short preface, in which he declared he had no inquisitiveness in his temper, enquired what Jones meant by a considerable sum--he knew not how much--and what was become of the money.", "여기서 격한 감정이 존스의 입을 막았고, 놀라움이 한순간 파트리지의 입도 막았다. 그러나 그는 곧 말문을 되찾았고, 자신은 본디 캐묻기 좋아하는 성미가 아니라고 짤막하게 운을 뗀 뒤, 존스가 말한 적지 않은 액수란 게 무슨 뜻이냐고—얼마인지는 모른다지만—그리고 그 돈은 어찌 되었느냐고 물었다."]], [["In both these points he now received full satisfaction; on which he was proceeding to comment, when he was interrupted by a message from Mr Nightingale, who desired his master's company in his apartment.", "이 두 가지 점에 대해 그는 이제 충분한 답을 들었다. 그가 한참 거기에 토를 달려던 참에, 나이팅게일 씨가 보낸 전갈이 끼어들어 그를 막았다. 자기 방으로 주인을 모셨으면 한다는 청이었다."]], [["When the two gentlemen were both attired for the masquerade, and Mr Nightingale had given orders for chairs to be sent for, a circumstance of distress occurred to Jones, which will appear very ridiculous to many of my readers.", "두 신사가 모두 가면무도회 차림을 갖추고, 나이팅게일 씨가 가마를 불러오라 일러 둔 뒤, 존스에게는 곤혹스러운 사정 하나가 떠올랐다. 내 독자들 가운데 많은 이에게는 이것이 무척 우스워 보일 것이다."], ["This was how to procure a shilling; but if such readers will reflect a little on what they have themselves felt from the want of a thousand pounds, or, perhaps, of ten or twenty, to execute a favourite scheme, they will have a perfect idea of what Mr Jones felt on this occasion.", "다름 아니라 어떻게 1실링을 마련하느냐 하는 것이었다. 그러나 그런 독자들이라도, 마음에 둔 어떤 계획을 이루려는데 1천 파운드가, 혹은 10파운드나 20파운드쯤이 없어 자신이 겪었던 심정을 잠시 떠올려 본다면, 이때 존스 씨가 느낀 바를 고스란히 헤아릴 수 있으리라."], ["For this sum, therefore, he applied to Partridge, which was the first he had permitted him to advance, and was the last he intended that poor fellow should advance in his service.", "그리하여 그는 이 돈을 파트리지에게 청했다. 그것은 그가 파트리지에게 내어놓도록 허락한 첫 번째 돈이었고, 또한 이 가엾은 사내가 자기를 위해 내어놓는 마지막 돈이 되게 하겠다고 마음먹은 것이었다."], ["To say the truth, Partridge had lately made no offer of this kind.", "사실대로 말하자면, 파트리지는 근래 들어 이런 식의 제안을 한 적이 없었다."], ["Whether it was that he desired to see the bank-bill broke in upon, or that distress should prevail on Jones to return home, or from what other motive it proceeded, I will not determine.", "그것이 은행권을 헐어 쓰는 꼴을 보고 싶어서였는지, 아니면 곤궁함이 존스를 설득해 집으로 돌아가게 만들기를 바라서였는지, 그도 아니면 또 다른 어떤 동기에서 비롯된 것이었는지는 내가 단정하지 않겠다."]], [["Containing the whole humours of a masquerade.", "가장무도회의 온갖 익살을 담은 장."]], [["Our cavaliers now arrived at that temple, where Heydegger, the great Arbiter Deliciarum, the great high-priest of pleasure, presides; and, like other heathen priests, imposes on his votaries by the pretended presence of the deity, when in reality no such deity is there.", "우리의 기사들은 마침내 그 신전에 다다랐다. 위대한 쾌락의 중재자이자 환락의 대제사장인 하이데거가 주재하는 곳이었다. 그리고 그는 다른 이교의 사제들이 그러하듯, 실제로는 그곳에 어떤 신도 없건만 신이 임재한 척 꾸며 자신의 숭배자들을 속였다."]], [["Mr Nightingale, having taken a turn or two with his companion, soon left him, and walked off with a female, saying, “Now you are here, sir, you must beat about for your own game.”", "나이팅게일 씨는 일행과 한두 바퀴를 돈 뒤 곧 그를 떠나 어떤 여인과 함께 걸어가 버리며 이렇게 말했다. \"이제 여기까지 왔으니, 자네는 알아서 자기 사냥감을 찾아 헤매 보게나.\""]], [["Jones began to entertain strong hopes that his Sophia was present; and these hopes gave him more spirits than the lights, the music, and the company; though these are pretty strong antidotes against the spleen.", "존스는 자신의 소피아가 그 자리에 와 있으리라는 강한 희망을 품기 시작했다. 그리고 이 희망은 불빛이나 음악이나 사람들보다 그에게 더 큰 활기를 불어넣었다."], ["He now accosted every woman he saw, whose stature, shape, or air, bore any resemblance to his angel.", "물론 그런 것들도 우울을 다스리는 꽤 강력한 해독제이긴 했지만. 그는 이제 키나 몸매, 분위기가 자기 천사를 조금이라도 닮은 여인이 보일 때마다 말을 걸었다."], ["To all of whom he endeavoured to say something smart, in order to engage an answer, by which he might discover that voice which he thought it impossible he should mistake.", "그런 여인들 모두에게 그는 어떻게든 재치 있는 말을 건네려 애썼는데, 대답을 끌어내어 결코 잘못 들을 리 없다고 여기는 그 목소리를 알아내려는 속셈이었다. 그중 몇몇은 새된 목소리로 되물었다."], ["Some of these answered by a question, in a squeaking voice, Do you know me?", "\"나 알아요?\" 훨씬 많은 수는 \"당신을 모르겠어요, 선생님\""], ["Much the greater number said, I don't know you, sir, and nothing more.", "하고는 그뿐이었다. 어떤 이는 그를 무례한 작자라 불렀고, 어떤 이는 아예 대꾸도 하지 않았으며, 어떤 이는 \"정말이지 당신 목소리를 모르겠고, 당신과는 할 말이 없어요\""], ["Some called him an impertinent fellow; some made him no answer at all; some said, Indeed I don't know your voice, and I shall have nothing to say to you; and many gave him as kind answers as he could wish, but not in the voice he desired to hear.", "하고 말했다. 또 많은 여인들이 그가 바랄 만큼 다정한 대답을 주었지만, 정작 그가 듣고 싶어 하는 그 목소리는 아니었다."]], [["Whilst he was talking with one of these last (who was in the habit of a shepherdess) a lady in a domino came up to him, and slapping him on the shoulder, whispered him, at the same time, in the ear, “If you talk any longer with that trollop, I will acquaint Miss Western.”", "그가 이 마지막 부류의 한 사람과(양치기 처녀 차림을 한 여인이었다) 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 도미노 차림의 한 부인이 그에게 다가와 어깨를 툭 치며 동시에 귓속말로 속삭였다. \"저 잡것과 좀 더 이야기를 나누신다면, 웨스턴 양에게 일러바치겠어요.\""]], [["Jones no sooner heard that name, than, immediately quitting his former companion, he applied to the domino, begging and entreating her to show him the lady she had mentioned, if she was then in the room.", "존스는 그 이름을 듣자마자 곧장 앞서의 상대를 버려두고 도미노 차림의 부인에게 매달려, 만일 그분이 지금 이 방 안에 있다면 자신에게 보여 달라고 빌고 또 애원했다."]], [["The mask walked hastily to the upper end of the innermost apartment before she spoke; and then, instead of answering him, sat down, and declared she was tired.", "가면을 쓴 여인은 말을 꺼내기 전에 가장 안쪽 방의 위편 끝으로 황급히 걸어갔다. 그러고는 그의 물음에 답하는 대신 자리에 앉아 피곤하다고 했다."], ["Jones sat down by her, and still persisted in his entreaties; at last the lady coldly answered, “I imagined Mr Jones had been a more discerning lover, than to suffer any disguise to conceal his mistress from him.”", "존스도 그 곁에 앉아 여전히 애원을 그치지 않았다. 마침내 부인이 차갑게 대답했다."], ["“Is she here, then, madam?”", "\"나는 존스 씨가 좀 더 안목 있는 연인인 줄 알았어요."], ["replied Jones, with some vehemence.", "변장 따위가 자기 연인을 가리도록 내버려 둘 분은 아닌 줄로요.\" \"그렇다면 그분이 여기 계십니까, 부인?\""], ["Upon which the lady cried--“Hush, sir, you will be observed.", "존스가 다소 격하게 응했다. 그러자 부인이 외쳤다."], ["I promise you, upon my honour, Miss Western is not here.”", "\"쉿, 선생님, 남들 눈에 띄겠어요. 내 명예를 걸고 약속하건대, 웨스턴 양은 여기 없어요.\""]], [["Jones, now taking the mask by the hand, fell to entreating her in the most earnest manner, to acquaint him where he might find Sophia; and when he could obtain no direct answer, he began to upbraid her gently for having disappointed him the day before; and concluded, saying, “Indeed, my good fairy queen, I know your majesty very well, notwithstanding the affected disguise of your voice. Indeed, Mrs Fitzpatrick, it is a little cruel to divert yourself at the expense of my torments.”", "존스는 이제 가면 쓴 여인의 손을 잡고, 더없이 간곡한 태도로 소피아를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 알려 달라고 애원하기 시작했다. 그리고 어떤 직접적인 답도 얻을 수 없자, 어제 자기를 바람맞힌 일을 두고 부드럽게 책망하기 시작했다. 그러고는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"정말이지, 친애하는 요정의 여왕이시여, 목소리를 짐짓 꾸미셨지만 저는 폐하를 아주 잘 알아봅니다. 정말이지, 피츠패트릭 부인, 제 고통을 즐길 거리로 삼으시다니 좀 잔인하시군요.\""]], [["The mask answered, “Though you have so ingeniously discovered me, I must still speak in the same voice, lest I should be known by others.", "가면 쓴 여인이 대답했다. \"당신이 그렇게 영리하게 나를 알아보았다 해도, 나는 여전히 같은 목소리로 말해야 해요."], ["And do you think, good sir, that I have no greater regard for my cousin, than to assist in carrying on an affair between you two, which must end in her ruin, as well as your own?", "다른 사람들에게 들킬까 봐서요. 그리고 친애하는 선생님, 당신은 내가 사촌을 그토록 하찮게 여겨, 두 분의 정사를 거들기라도 하리라 생각하나요?"], ["Besides, I promise you, my cousin is not mad enough to consent to her own destruction, if you are so much her enemy as to tempt her to it.”", "그 끝이 그 애의 파멸이면서 동시에 당신 자신의 파멸이 될 일인데요? 게다가 분명히 말하지만, 당신이 그토록 그 애의 적이 되어 부추긴다 한들, 내 사촌은 제 손으로 제 파멸에 동의할 만큼 미치지 않았어요.\""]], [["“Alas, madam!”", "\"아아, 부인!\""], ["said Jones, “you little know my heart, when you call me an enemy of Sophia.”", "존스가 말했다. \"저를 소피아의 적이라 부르시다니, 부인은 제 마음을 너무도 모르십니다.\""]], [["“And yet to ruin any one,” cries the other, “you will allow, is the act of an enemy; and when by the same act you must knowingly and certainly bring ruin on yourself, is it not folly or madness, as well as guilt? Now, sir, my cousin hath very little more than her father will please to give her; very little for one of her fashion--you know him, and you know your own situation.”", "\"하지만 누군가를 파멸시키는 일이란,\" 상대가 외쳤다. \"당신도 인정하겠지만 적이 하는 짓이에요. 그리고 같은 행동으로 당신 자신에게도 틀림없이, 그것도 알면서 파멸을 불러온다면, 그건 죄일 뿐 아니라 어리석음이나 광기 아닌가요? 자, 선생님, 내 사촌이 가진 것이라곤 그 애 아버지가 기꺼이 내주려는 것 말고는 거의 없어요. 그 애 신분의 여인치고는 너무도 적지요. 당신은 그분을 알고, 또 당신 자신의 처지도 알잖아요.\""]], [["Jones vowed he had no such design on Sophia, “That he would rather suffer the most violent of deaths than sacrifice her interest to his desires.”", "존스는 소피아에게 그런 의도가 전혀 없노라 맹세했다. \"차라리 가장 끔찍한 죽음이라도 견딜지언정, 제 욕망을 위해 그분의 이익을 희생시키지는 않겠습니다.\""], ["He said, “he knew how unworthy he was of her, every way, that he had long ago resolved to quit all such aspiring thoughts, but that some strange accidents had made him desirous to see her once more, when he promised he would take leave of her for ever.", "그는 말했다. \"제가 어느 모로 보나 그분에게 어울리지 않는 사람임을 잘 알기에, 진작부터 그런 야심 어린 생각은 모두 버리기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["No, madam,”", "다만 어떤 이상한 사정들로 인해 그분을 단 한 번만 더 보고 싶어졌을 뿐이며, 그러고 나면 영영 작별하겠다고 약속드립니다. 아닙니다, 부인,\""], ["concluded he, “my love is not of that base kind which seeks its own satisfaction at the expense of what is most dear to its object.", "그가 끝맺었다. \"제 사랑은 사랑하는 대상에게 가장 소중한 것을 희생시키면서 제 만족을 구하는 그런 비천한 종류가 아닙니다."], ["I would sacrifice everything to the possession of my Sophia, but Sophia herself.”", "저는 제 소피아를 얻기 위해서라면 모든 것을 희생할 수 있습니다. 다만 소피아 자신만은 예외입니다.\""]], [["Though the reader may have already conceived no very sublime idea of the virtue of the lady in the mask; and though possibly she may hereafter appear not to deserve one of the first characters of her sex; yet, it is certain, these generous sentiments made a strong impression upon her, and greatly added to the affection she had before conceived for our young heroe.", "독자는 이미 가면 쓴 부인의 덕성에 대해 그다지 고상한 관념을 품지 못했을 것이며, 어쩌면 그녀가 나중에 자기 성별 가운데 으뜸가는 인물에 걸맞지 않은 사람으로 드러날 수도 있다. 그럼에도 이 너그러운 마음씨가 그녀에게 강한 인상을 남겼고, 그녀가 우리의 젊은 주인공에게 전부터 품어 온 애정을 한층 더 깊게 했음은 분명하다."]], [["The lady now, after silence of a few moments, said, “She did not see his pretensions to Sophia so much in the light of presumption, as of imprudence.", "이제 부인은 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말했다. \"소피아를 향한 당신의 마음을 주제넘은 짓이라기보다는 분별없는 짓으로 본다\"고 했다."], ["Young fellows,”", "\"젊은 사내들은,\""], ["says she, “can never have too aspiring thoughts.", "그녀가 말했다. \"아무리 야심 찬 생각을 품어도 지나칠 게 없어요."], ["I love ambition in a young man, and I would have you cultivate it as much as possible.", "나는 젊은 남자의 야심을 좋아하고, 당신도 그것을 될 수 있는 한 키워 나가길 바라요. 어쩌면 당신은 재산이 한없이 더 나은 여인들과도 잘될지 몰라요."], ["Perhaps you may succeed with those who are infinitely superior in fortune; nay, I am convinced there are women----but don't you think me a strange creature, Mr Jones, to be thus giving advice to a man with whom I am so little acquainted, and one with whose behaviour to me I have so little reason to be pleased?”", "아니, 나는 그런 여자들이 분명 있다고 확신해요... 하지만 존스 씨, 이렇게 거의 알지도 못하는 남자에게, 게다가 나를 대하는 태도로 보아 도무지 흡족할 까닭이 없는 남자에게 이런 충고를 늘어놓고 있으니, 나를 참 이상한 사람이라 여기지 않으세요?\""]], [["Here Jones began to apologize, and to hope he had not offended in anything he had said of her cousin.--To which the mask answered, “And are you so little versed in the sex, to imagine you can well affront a lady more than by entertaining her with your passion for another woman?", "여기서 존스는 사과를 시작하며, 그녀의 사촌에 대해 자신이 한 말 가운데 무엇으로도 그녀의 기분을 상하게 하지 않았기를 바란다고 했다. 그러자 가면 쓴 여인이 대답했다."], ["If the fairy queen had conceived no better opinion of your gallantry, she would scarce have appointed you to meet her at the masquerade.”", "\"당신은 여인이라는 성별에 그토록 어두워서, 다른 여자를 향한 정열로 한 부인을 즐겁게 하는 것보다 더 큰 모욕을 안길 수 있다고 생각하나요? 만약 요정의 여왕이 당신의 정중함을 더 낫게 보지 않았다면, 가장무도회에서 자기를 만나자고 당신을 부르지도 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["Jones had never less inclination to an amour than at present; but gallantry to the ladies was among his principles of honour; and he held it as much incumbent on him to accept a challenge to love, as if it had been a challenge to fight.", "존스는 지금만큼 정사에 마음이 없던 적이 없었다. 그러나 부인들에 대한 정중함은 그의 명예 원칙 가운데 하나였고, 그는 결투 신청을 받아들이는 것만큼이나 사랑의 도전을 받아들이는 것 또한 자기 의무라 여겼다."], ["Nay, his very love to Sophia made it necessary for him to keep well with the lady, as he made no doubt but she was capable of bringing him into the presence of the other.", "아니, 소피아를 향한 바로 그 사랑 때문에라도 이 부인과 잘 지낼 필요가 있었으니, 그녀가 자신을 그 다른 여인의 앞으로 데려다줄 수 있으리라 조금도 의심하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["He began therefore to make a very warm answer to her last speech, when a mask, in the character of an old woman, joined them.", "그리하여 그가 그녀의 마지막 말에 아주 뜨거운 답을 막 건네려는 참에, 노파로 분장한 가면 하나가 두 사람에게 끼어들었다."], ["This mask was one of those ladies who go to a masquerade only to vent ill-nature, by telling people rude truths, and by endeavouring, as the phrase is, to spoil as much sport as they are able.", "이 가면은 가장무도회에 오로지 심술을 풀려고 오는 부류의 여인들 가운데 하나였다. 사람들에게 무례한 진실을 들이대고, 흔히들 말하듯 가능한 한 흥을 깨려 애쓰는 부류였다."], ["This good lady, therefore, having observed Jones, and his friend, whom she well knew, in close consultation together in a corner of the room, concluded she could nowhere satisfy her spleen better than by interrupting them.", "그래서 이 갸륵한 부인은 존스와, 그녀가 잘 아는 그의 친구가 방 구석에서 은밀히 이야기를 나누는 것을 보고는, 그들을 방해하는 것보다 자기 심술을 풀기에 더 좋은 데가 없으리라 결론지었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그들을 공격해 곧 그 은신처에서 몰아냈다."], ["She attacked them, therefore, and soon drove them from their retirement; nor was she contented with this, but pursued them to every place which they shifted to avoid her; till Mr Nightingale, seeing the distress of his friend, at last relieved him, and engaged the old woman in another pursuit.", "그것으로도 만족하지 못하고, 그들이 그녀를 피해 옮겨 가는 곳마다 쫓아다녔다. 마침내 나이팅게일 씨가 친구의 곤경을 보고 그를 구해 주었으니, 그 노파를 다른 추격거리에 붙들어 매어 놓았던 것이다."]], [["While Jones and his mask were walking together about the room, to rid themselves of the teazer, he observed his lady speak to several masks, with the same freedom of acquaintance as if they had been barefaced. He could not help expressing his surprize at this; saying, “Sure, madam, you must have infinite discernment, to know people in all disguises.” To which the lady answered, “You cannot conceive anything more insipid and childish than a masquerade to the people of fashion, who in general know one another as well here as when they meet in an assembly or a drawing-room; nor will any woman of condition converse with a person with whom she is not acquainted. In short, the generality of persons whom you see here may more properly be said to kill time in this place than in any other; and generally retire from hence more tired than from the longest sermon. To say the truth, I begin to be in that situation myself; and if I have any faculty at guessing, you are not much better pleased. I protest it would be almost charity in me to go home for your sake.” “I know but one charity equal to it,” cries Jones, “and that is to suffer me to wait on you home.” “Sure,” answered the lady, “you have a strange opinion of me, to imagine, that upon such an acquaintance, I would let you into my doors at this time of night. I fancy you impute the friendship I have shown my cousin to some other motive. Confess honestly; don't you consider this contrived interview as little better than a downright assignation? Are you used, Mr Jones, to make these sudden conquests?” “I am not used, madam,” said Jones, “to submit to such sudden conquests; but as you have taken my heart by surprize, the rest of my body hath a right to follow; so you must pardon me if I resolve to attend you wherever you go.” He accompanied these words with some proper actions; upon which the lady, after a gentle rebuke, and saying their familiarity would be observed, told him, “She was going to sup with an acquaintance, whither she hoped he would not follow her; for if you should,” said she, “I shall be thought an unaccountable creature, though my friend indeed is not censorious: yet I hope you won't follow me; I protest I shall not know what to say if you do.”", "존스와 그의 가면 쓴 부인이 그 성가신 자를 떨쳐 내려고 함께 방을 거니는 동안, 그는 자기 부인이 여러 가면들에게 마치 맨얼굴로 만난 사이인 양 거리낌 없이 말을 거는 것을 보았다. 그는 이에 놀라움을 표하지 않을 수 없었다. \"정말이지, 부인, 온갖 변장 속에서도 사람을 알아보시다니, 한없는 안목을 지니셨나 봅니다.\" 그러자 부인이 대답했다. \"상류층 사람들에게는 가장무도회만큼 시시하고 유치한 것이 없답니다. 그들은 대개 회합이나 응접실에서 만날 때만큼이나 여기서도 서로를 잘 알아보거든요. 또 어떤 지체 있는 여인도 모르는 사람과는 대화하지 않아요. 한마디로, 여기 보이는 사람들 대부분은 다른 어느 곳에서보다 바로 이곳에서 시간을 죽인다고 하는 편이 더 옳을 거예요. 그리고 대개는 더없이 긴 설교를 듣고 나올 때보다 더 지친 채 여기서 물러간답니다. 사실대로 말하자면, 나 자신도 그런 처지가 되어 가기 시작했어요. 그리고 내게 눈치챌 재주가 조금이라도 있다면, 당신도 별로 더 즐거워 보이지 않는군요. 솔직히 당신을 위해서 집에 가 주는 게 거의 자선이 될 거예요.\" \"저는 그에 맞먹는 자선을 딱 하나밖에 모릅니다.\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"그건 제가 부인을 댁까지 모셔다 드리도록 허락해 주시는 겁니다.\" \"정말이지,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"당신은 나에 대해 참 이상한 생각을 품고 있군요. 이 정도 면식으로 이 야밤에 당신을 내 집 문 안으로 들여보내 주리라 상상하다니. 짐작건대 당신은 내가 사촌에게 보인 우정을 무슨 다른 속셈으로 돌리는 모양이군요. 솔직히 고백해 봐요. 당신은 이렇게 꾸며진 만남을 노골적인 밀회나 다름없는 것으로 여기는 거 아닌가요? 존스 씨, 당신은 이렇게 느닷없는 정복을 일삼는 사람인가요?\" \"저는, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"이런 느닷없는 정복에 굴복하는 데에는 익숙하지 않습니다. 그러나 부인께서 제 마음을 불시에 사로잡으셨으니, 제 몸의 나머지도 따라갈 권리가 있지요. 그러니 부인이 어디로 가시든 따르기로 마음먹은 저를 용서하셔야 합니다.\" 그는 이 말에 적절한 행동을 곁들였다. 그러자 부인은 가볍게 나무라고 두 사람의 친밀함이 남들 눈에 띌 거라고 말하고는 그에게 일렀다. \"지인과 함께 야식을 들러 가는 길인데, 거기까지는 따라오지 않기를 바라요. 만약 따라온다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 영문 모를 여자로 여겨질 거예요. 하긴 내 친구가 그리 까다롭게 흠잡는 사람은 아니지만요. 그래도 따라오지 않기를 바라요. 정말이지 당신이 따라오면 나는 뭐라 말해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["The lady presently after quitted the masquerade, and Jones, notwithstanding the severe prohibition he had received, presumed to attend her.", "부인은 곧이어 가장무도회장을 떠났고, 존스는 그토록 엄한 금지령을 받았음에도 감히 그녀를 따라나섰다."], ["He was now reduced to the same dilemma we have mentioned before, namely, the want of a shilling, and could not relieve it by borrowing as before.", "그는 이제 앞서 말한 것과 똑같은 곤경에 빠져 있었다. 다름 아니라 일 실링이 없다는 것이었는데, 이번에는 전처럼 빌려서 해결할 수도 없었다."], ["He therefore walked boldly on after the chair in which his lady rode, pursued by a grand huzza, from all the chairmen present, who wisely take the best care they can to discountenance all walking afoot by their betters.", "그래서 그는 자기 부인이 탄 가마를 뒤따라 당당히 걸어갔고, 그 자리에 있던 모든 가마꾼들의 요란한 야유가 그를 쫓았다. 그들은 윗사람들이 두 발로 걷는 일이라면 무엇이든 못마땅하게 만들려고 영리하게도 갖은 애를 쓰는 자들이었다."], ["Luckily, however, the gentry who attend at the Opera-house were too busy to quit their stations, and as the lateness of the hour prevented him from meeting many of their brethren in the street, he proceeded without molestation, in a dress, which, at another season, would have certainly raised a mob at his heels.", "그러나 다행히도 오페라 극장에 대기하던 가마꾼들은 너무 바빠 제자리를 뜨지 못했고, 시간이 늦은 덕에 거리에서 그 동료들과 많이 마주치지도 않았다. 그리하여 그는 아무런 시달림 없이 나아갔으니, 다른 철이었다면 그 차림새가 틀림없이 그의 뒤꽁무니에 군중을 불러 모았을 터였다."]], [["The lady was set down in a street not far from Hanover-square, where the door being presently opened, she was carried in, and the gentleman, without any ceremony, walked in after her.", "부인은 하노버 광장에서 멀지 않은 어느 거리에서 내렸고, 그곳에서 문이 곧 열리자 안으로 옮겨졌다. 그리고 신사는 아무런 격식도 차리지 않고 그녀를 따라 안으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["Jones and his companion were now together in a very well-furnished and well-warmed room; when the female, still speaking in her masquerade voice, said she was surprized at her friend, who must absolutely have forgot her appointment; at which, after venting much resentment, she suddenly exprest some apprehension from Jones, and asked him what the world would think of their having been alone together in a house at that time of night? But instead of a direct answer to so important a question, Jones began to be very importunate with the lady to unmask; and at length having prevailed, there appeared not Mrs Fitzpatrick, but the Lady Bellaston herself.", "이제 존스와 그의 동행은 가구가 아주 잘 갖춰지고 따뜻하게 데워진 방에 함께 있었다. 그때 여인이 여전히 가장무도회 때의 목소리로, 자기 친구에게 놀랐다며, 그가 약속을 까맣게 잊은 게 틀림없다고 말했다. 그러고는 한참 분을 터뜨린 끝에, 갑자기 존스를 두고 어떤 걱정을 내비치며, 이런 야밤에 한 집에 단둘이 있었다는 것을 세상이 어찌 여기겠느냐고 물었다. 그러나 그토록 중대한 물음에 곧장 답하는 대신, 존스는 부인에게 가면을 벗으라고 몹시 끈질기게 조르기 시작했다. 마침내 뜻을 이루고 보니, 거기 나타난 것은 피츠패트릭 부인이 아니라 다름 아닌 벨라스턴 부인 자신이었다."]], [["It would be tedious to give the particular conversation, which consisted of very common and ordinary occurrences, and which lasted from two till six o'clock in the morning.", "새벽 두 시부터 여섯 시까지 이어진 그 구체적인 대화를 일일이 옮기는 것은 따분할 것이다. 그것은 아주 흔하고 평범한 일들로 이루어져 있었으니 말이다."], ["It is sufficient to mention all of it that is anywise material to this history.", "이 이야기에 어떻게든 의미가 있는 부분만 들어 두는 것으로 충분하다."], ["And this was a promise that the lady would endeavour to find out Sophia, and in a few days bring him to an interview with her, on condition that he would then take his leave of her.", "그것은 부인이 소피아를 찾아내도록 애써 보겠으며, 며칠 안에 그를 그녀와 만나게 해 주겠다는 약속이었다. 단, 그때 그가 그녀와 작별한다는 조건이었다."], ["When this was thoroughly settled, and a second meeting in the evening appointed at the same place, they separated; the lady returned to her house, and Jones to his lodgings.", "이것이 완전히 매듭지어지고, 저녁에 같은 장소에서 두 번째 만남이 정해지자, 둘은 헤어졌다. 부인은 자기 집으로 돌아갔고, 존스는 자기 숙소로 돌아갔다."]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["Containing a scene of distress, which will appear very extraordinary to most of our readers.", "존스는 몇 시간 눈을 붙여 몸을 추스른 뒤 파트리지를 불러들였다."], ["Jones having refreshed himself with a few hours' sleep, summoned Partridge to his presence; and delivering him a bank-note of fifty pounds, ordered him to go and change it.", "그러고는 50파운드짜리 은행권 한 장을 건네며 그것을 가서 잔돈으로 바꿔 오라고 일렀다."], ["Partridge received this with sparkling eyes, though, when he came to reflect farther, it raised in him some suspicions not very advantageous to the honour of his master: to these the dreadful idea he had of the masquerade, the disguise in which his master had gone out and returned, and his having been abroad all night, contributed.", "파트리지는 두 눈을 반짝이며 그것을 받아 들었지만, 막상 더 곰곰이 생각해 보니 주인의 명예에 그리 이롭지 않은 의심이 슬그머니 고개를 들었다. 가면무도회에 대해 품고 있던 무시무시한 상상, 주인이 변장을 하고 나갔다가 변장한 채로 돌아온 일, 그리고 밤새 바깥에 나가 있었던 일이 그 의심을 부채질했다."], ["In plain language, the only way he could possibly find to account for the possession of this note, was by robbery: and, to confess the truth, the reader, unless he should suspect it was owing to the generosity of Lady Bellaston, can hardly imagine any other.", "쉽게 말해, 이 은행권을 손에 넣은 경위를 그가 도무지 달리 설명할 길이 없었던 까닭은 강도질밖에 떠오르지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 솔직히 말하자면, 독자 역시 이것이 벨라스턴 부인의 너그러움 덕분이었으리라 짐작하지 않는 한, 다른 어떤 까닭도 떠올리기 어려울 것이다."]], [["To clear, therefore, the honour of Mr Jones, and to do justice to the liberality of the lady, he had really received this present from her, who, though she did not give much into the hackney charities of the age, such as building hospitals, &c., was not, however, entirely void of that Christian virtue; and conceived (very rightly I think) that a young fellow of merit, without a shilling in the world, was no improper object of this virtue.", "그러니 존스의 명예를 깨끗이 해 두고, 또 그 부인의 후한 인심에 정당한 몫을 돌려주기 위해 밝혀 두자면, 그는 정말로 이 선물을 그녀에게서 받은 것이었다. 그녀는 병원 짓기 따위 그 시절의 흔해 빠진 자선에는 그다지 마음을 쓰지 않았으나, 그렇다고 그러한 기독교적 미덕이 아주 없는 사람은 아니었다. 그리고 그녀는 (내 생각에 아주 옳게도) 한 푼도 가진 것 없는 됨됨이 훌륭한 젊은이야말로 이 미덕을 베풀기에 부적절한 대상이 아니라고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Mr Jones and Mr Nightingale had been invited to dine this day with Mrs Miller.", "이날 존스와 나이팅게일은 밀러 부인의 집에서 함께 점심을 들기로 초대받은 터였다."], ["At the appointed hour, therefore, the two young gentlemen, with the two girls, attended in the parlour, where they waited from three till almost five before the good woman appeared.", "그리하여 약속한 시각에 두 젊은 신사는 두 아가씨와 더불어 거실에 모였고, 그곳에서 세 시부터 거의 다섯 시가 될 때까지 마음씨 고운 그 부인이 나타나기를 기다렸다."], ["She had been out of town to visit a relation, of whom, at her return, she gave the following account.", "그녀는 어느 친척을 보러 시외에 다녀온 길이었는데, 돌아와서는 다음과 같은 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["“I hope, gentlemen, you will pardon my making you wait; I am sure if you knew the occasion--I have been to see a cousin of mine, about six miles off, who now lies in.--It should be a warning to all persons (says she, looking at her daughters) how they marry indiscreetly. There is no happiness in this world without a competency. O Nancy! how shall I describe the wretched condition in which I found your poor cousin? she hath scarce lain in a week, and there was she, this dreadful weather, in a cold room, without any curtains to her bed, and not a bushel of coals in her house to supply her with fire; her second son, that sweet little fellow, lies ill of a quinzy in the same bed with his mother; for there is no other bed in the house. Poor little Tommy! I believe, Nancy, you will never see your favourite any more; for he is really very ill. The rest of the children are in pretty good health: but Molly, I am afraid, will do herself an injury: she is but thirteen years old, Mr Nightingale, and yet, in my life, I never saw a better nurse: she tends both her mother and her brother; and, what is wonderful in a creature so young, she shows all the chearfulness in the world to her mother; and yet I saw her--I saw the poor child, Mr Nightingale, turn about, and privately wipe the tears from her eyes.” Here Mrs Miller was prevented, by her own tears, from going on, and there was not, I believe, a person present who did not accompany her in them; at length she a little recovered herself, and proceeded thus: “In all this distress the mother supports her spirits in a surprizing manner. The danger of her son sits heaviest upon her, and yet she endeavours as much as possible to conceal even this concern, on her husband's account. Her grief, however, sometimes gets the better of all her endeavours; for she was always extravagantly fond of this boy, and a most sensible, sweet-tempered creature it is. I protest I was never more affected in my life than when I heard the little wretch, who is hardly yet seven years old, while his mother was wetting him with her tears, beg her to be comforted. `Indeed, mamma,' cried the child, `I shan't die; God Almighty, I'm sure, won't take Tommy away; let heaven be ever so fine a place, I had rather stay here and starve with you and my papa than go to it.' Pardon me, gentlemen, I can't help it” (says she, wiping her eyes), “such sensibility and affection in a child.--And yet, perhaps, he is least the object of pity; for a day or two will, most probably, place him beyond the reach of all human evils. The father is, indeed, most worthy of compassion. Poor man, his countenance is the very picture of horror, and he looks like one rather dead than alive. Oh heavens! what a scene did I behold at my first coming into the room! The good creature was lying behind the bolster, supporting at once both his child and his wife. He had nothing on but a thin waistcoat; for his coat was spread over the bed, to supply the want of blankets.--When he rose up at my entrance, I scarce knew him. As comely a man, Mr Jones, within this fortnight, as you ever beheld; Mr Nightingale hath seen him. His eyes sunk, his face pale, with a long beard. His body shivering with cold, and worn with hunger too; for my cousin says she can hardly prevail upon him to eat.--He told me himself in a whisper--he told me--I can't repeat it--he said he could not bear to eat the bread his children wanted. And yet, can you believe it, gentlemen? in all this misery his wife has as good caudle as if she lay in the midst of the greatest affluence; I tasted it, and I scarce ever tasted better.--The means of procuring her this, he said, he believed was sent him by an angel from heaven. I know not what he meant; for I had not spirits enough to ask a single question.", "\"여러분, 이렇게 기다리시게 해서 정말 죄송해요. 사정을 아신다면 분명히 — 한 6마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 사는 사촌을 보러 다녀왔답니다. 지금 산후 조리 중이거든요. 이건 모든 사람에게 (그녀는 딸들을 바라보며 말했다) 경솔하게 결혼하는 일이 어떤 것인지 경고가 되어야 할 일이에요. 이 세상에 살림의 여유 없이는 행복이란 없어요. 오, 낸시! 네 가엾은 사촌이 처한 그 비참한 형편을 내가 어떻게 다 말로 그릴 수 있을까? 산후 일주일도 채 안 됐는데, 이 끔찍한 날씨에 침대에 휘장 하나 없는 차가운 방에 누워 있고, 불을 지필 석탄 한 가마니조차 집에 없더구나. 둘째 아들, 그 사랑스러운 어린 녀석은 후두염으로 어미와 같은 침대에 앓아 누워 있단다. 집에 다른 침대라곤 없으니까. 가엾은 어린 토미! 낸시야, 아무래도 네가 그토록 아끼던 그 아이를 다시는 못 볼 것 같구나. 정말이지 몹시 위중하거든. 나머지 아이들은 꽤 건강한 편이다만, 몰리는 제 몸을 상하게 할까 봐 걱정이야. 나이팅게일 씨, 그 애가 이제 겨우 열세 살인데, 내 평생 그렇게 잘 돌보는 간병인은 본 적이 없어요. 제 어미와 동생을 둘 다 보살피거든요. 게다가 그렇게 어린아이로서는 놀랍게도, 어미 앞에서는 세상 그 누구보다 밝은 얼굴을 보인답니다. 그런데 내가 봤어요 — 나이팅게일 씨, 그 가엾은 아이가 고개를 돌려 몰래 제 눈에서 눈물을 훔치는 걸 봤지 뭐예요.\" 여기서 밀러 부인은 북받치는 눈물에 말을 잇지 못했고, 그 자리에 있던 사람치고 함께 눈물짓지 않은 이는 아마 한 사람도 없었으리라. 이윽고 그녀는 조금 진정을 되찾고 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"이 모든 고생 속에서도 그 어미는 놀라우리만치 꿋꿋하게 마음을 다잡고 있어요. 아들이 위태로운 것이 제일 무겁게 그 가슴을 짓누르는데도, 남편을 생각해서 그 걱정마저 되도록 감추려 애쓴답니다. 그래도 슬픔이 이따금 그 모든 애씀을 이기고 마는 건, 그녀가 늘 이 아이를 끔찍이도 사랑했기 때문이에요. 정말이지 영민하고 마음씨 고운 아이거든요. 단언컨대 내 평생 그토록 가슴이 미어진 적이 없어요. 이제 겨우 일곱 살밖에 안 된 그 가엾은 어린것이, 제 어미가 눈물로 제 몸을 적시는 동안 어미더러 마음을 추스르라고 달래는 소리를 들었을 때 말이에요. '정말이에요, 엄마,' 그 아이가 외치더군요. '저는 안 죽어요. 전능하신 하느님께서 분명 토미를 데려가지 않으실 거예요. 천국이 아무리 좋은 곳이라도, 저는 거기 가느니 차라리 여기서 엄마랑 아빠랑 함께 굶는 편이 좋아요.' 여러분, 용서하세요. 어쩔 수가 없네요\" (그녀는 눈물을 닦으며 말했다). \"어린아이가 그토록 여린 마음과 정을 지녔으니. — 그래도 어쩌면 그 아이는 가장 덜 가엾은 처지일지도 몰라요. 하루이틀이면 십중팔구 인간 세상의 온갖 불행이 미치지 못하는 곳에 가 있을 테니까요. 정작 가장 가엾은 건 그 아비예요. 가엾은 사람, 그 얼굴이야말로 공포 그 자체의 그림 같아서, 산 사람이라기보다 죽은 사람처럼 보인답니다. 오, 맙소사! 내가 그 방에 처음 들어섰을 때 어떤 광경을 보았는지! 그 착한 사람이 베개받침 뒤에 누워서 제 아이와 아내를 한꺼번에 받쳐 안고 있더군요. 몸에는 얇은 조끼 하나밖에 걸치지 않았어요. 외투는 담요 대신 침대 위에 펼쳐 놓았거든요. — 내가 들어서자 그가 몸을 일으켰는데, 나는 그를 알아보기도 힘들었어요. 존스 씨, 보름 전만 해도 당신이 보신 어떤 이 못지않게 잘생긴 사람이었답니다. 나이팅게일 씨는 그를 본 적이 있지요. 두 눈은 움푹 꺼지고, 얼굴은 창백하고, 수염은 길게 자라 있었어요. 몸은 추위에 떨고, 게다가 굶주림에 야위어 있었지요. 사촌 말로는 그에게 도무지 무얼 먹게 할 수가 없다더군요. — 그가 직접 내게 속삭이듯 말했어요 — 그가 내게 말하기를 — 차마 옮길 수가 없네요 — 제 아이들에게 부족한 빵을 자기가 먹는 일을 견딜 수가 없다고 하더군요. 그런데 여러분, 믿어지세요? 이 모든 비참함 속에서도 그 아내만큼은 마치 더없이 풍족한 가운데 산후 조리를 하는 것처럼 좋은 caudle을 먹고 있더라니까요. 내가 맛을 봤는데, 그보다 나은 것을 맛본 적이 거의 없을 정도였어요. — 그가 말하길, 아내에게 이걸 마련해 줄 방도가 하늘에서 보낸 천사로부터 자기에게 온 것 같다고 하더군요. 무슨 뜻인지는 모르겠어요. 한 마디라도 물어볼 만한 기운이 내겐 없었으니까요."]], [["“This was a love-match, as they call it, on both sides; that is, a match between two beggars.", "* caudle: 산모나 환자에게 먹이던, 포도주나 맥주에 달걀·곡물·향신료를 섞어 데운 따뜻한 음료. \"그 둘은 흔히들 말하는 연애결혼을 했어요."], ["I must, indeed, say, I never saw a fonder couple; but what is their fondness good for, but to torment each other?”", "양쪽 다 말이죠. 그러니까 거지 둘이 맺어진 셈이지요."], ["“Indeed, mamma,”", "정말이지 그렇게 정다운 부부는 본 적이 없다고 말해야겠어요. 그런데 그 정다움이라는 게 서로를 괴롭히는 것 말고 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?\""], ["cries Nancy, “I have always looked on my cousin Anderson”", "\"정말이에요, 엄마,\" 낸시가 외쳤다."], ["(for that was her name) “as one of the happiest of women.”", "\"저는 늘 앤더슨 사촌 언니를\" (그것이 그녀의 이름이었다) \"세상에서 가장 행복한 여자 중 하나라고 여겼는데.\""], ["“I am sure,”", "\"분명한 건,\" 밀러 부인이 말했다."], ["says Mrs Miller, “the case at present is much otherwise; for any one might have discerned that the tender consideration of each other's sufferings makes the most intolerable part of their calamity, both to the husband and wife.", "\"지금 형편은 그것과는 영 딴판이라는 거다. 서로의 고통을 애틋하게 헤아리는 마음이야말로 그 재난 가운데서도 남편에게나 아내에게나 가장 견디기 힘든 부분이라는 걸 누구라도 알아챘을 거야."], ["Compared to which, hunger and cold, as they affect their own persons only, are scarce evils.", "그에 비하면 굶주림과 추위는 제 한 몸에만 닥치는 것이니 고생 축에도 들지 못하지."], ["Nay, the very children, the youngest, which is not two years old, excepted, feel in the same manner; for they are a most loving family, and, if they had but a bare competency, would be the happiest people in the world.”", "아니, 두 살도 안 된 막내만 빼면, 아이들조차 똑같이 그 마음을 느낀단다. 그 집은 더없이 정 많은 가족이라서, 살림에 최소한의 여유만 있었어도 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람들이었을 거야.\""], ["“I never saw the least sign of misery at her house,”", "\"저는 그 집에서 비참함의 기색이라곤 조금도 본 적이 없어요.\" 낸시가 대꾸했다."], ["replied Nancy; “I am sure my heart bleeds for what you now tell me.”--“O child,”", "\"지금 엄마가 들려주신 이야기에 정말 가슴이 미어져요.\" — \"오, 얘야,\""], ["answered the mother, “she hath always endeavoured to make the best of everything.", "어미가 답했다. \"그 애는 늘 무슨 일이든 좋게 꾸려 보려고 애썼단다."], ["They have always been in great distress; but, indeed, this absolute ruin hath been brought upon them by others.", "그들은 늘 큰 곤궁에 시달려 왔지. 하지만 정말이지, 이번의 이 완전한 파멸만큼은 남들이 그들에게 끼친 거란다."], ["The poor man was bail for the villain his brother; and about a week ago, the very day before her lying-in, their goods were all carried away, and sold by an execution.", "그 가엾은 사람이 제 형제라는 악당의 보증을 섰거든. 그러다 한 일주일쯤 전, 바로 그 애가 몸을 풀기 전날, 그 집 살림살이가 모조리 차압당해 강제 집행으로 팔려 나갔지 뭐냐."], ["He sent a letter to me of it by one of the bailiffs, which the villain never delivered.--What must he think of my suffering a week to pass before he heard of me?”", "그가 집행관 한 사람 편에 그 일을 알리는 편지를 내게 보냈다는데, 그 악당이 그걸 끝내 전하지 않았던 거야. — 자기 소식을 듣기까지 내가 일주일이나 흘려보냈으니, 그가 나를 어떻게 생각했겠니?\""]], [["It was not with dry eyes that Jones heard this narrative; when it was ended he took Mrs Miller apart with him into another room, and, delivering her his purse, in which was the sum of £50, desired her to send as much of it as she thought proper to these poor people.", "존스는 마른 눈으로 이 이야기를 들은 것이 아니었다. 이야기가 끝나자 그는 밀러 부인을 다른 방으로 따로 데려가, 50파운드가 든 자기 지갑을 건네며 그중 적당하다고 여겨지는 만큼을 그 가엾은 사람들에게 보내 달라고 청했다."], ["The look which Mrs Miller gave Jones, on this occasion, is not easy to be described.", "이때 밀러 부인이 존스에게 보낸 그 눈빛은 말로 그려 내기 쉽지 않다. 그녀는 일종의 벅찬 환희의 발작에 휩싸여 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["She burst into a kind of agony of transport, and cryed out--“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사! 세상에 이런 사람이 다 있다니!\""], ["is there such a man in the world?”--But recollecting herself, she said, “Indeed I know one such; but can there be another?”", "— 그러나 곧 정신을 추스르고 그녀는 말했다. \"사실 그런 분을 한 분 알고는 있어요."], ["“I hope, madam,”", "하지만 또 한 분이 있을 수 있을까요?\" \"부인,\""], ["cries Jones, “there are many who have common humanity; for to relieve such distresses in our fellow-creatures, can hardly be called more.”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"흔한 인정을 지닌 사람이라면 많으리라 믿습니다."], ["Mrs Miller then took ten guineas, which were the utmost he could prevail with her to accept, and said, “She would find some means of conveying them early the next morning;”", "동포가 겪는 그런 고통을 덜어 주는 것을 그 이상이라 부르기는 어려우니까요.\" 그러자 밀러 부인은 10기니를 받았는데, 그것이 그가 그녀에게 받게 할 수 있었던 최대한이었다."], ["adding, “that she had herself done some little matter for the poor people, and had not left them in quite so much misery as she found them.”", "그러고는 \"내일 아침 일찍 그것을 전할 방도를 찾아보겠노라\"고 말하며, \"자기도 그 가엾은 사람들을 위해 얼마간 손을 써 두어서, 처음 본 만큼 그렇게까지 비참한 처지에 그들을 내버려 두지는 않았다\"고 덧붙였다."]], [["They then returned to the parlour, where Nightingale expressed much concern at the dreadful situation of these wretches, whom indeed he knew; for he had seen them more than once at Mrs Miller's.", "그런 뒤 그들은 거실로 돌아왔고, 그곳에서 나이팅게일은 이 가엾은 이들의 끔찍한 형편에 큰 근심을 드러냈다. 실은 그도 그들을 알고 있었으니, 밀러 부인의 집에서 한두 번 이상 그들을 본 적이 있었던 것이다."], ["He inveighed against the folly of making oneself liable for the debts of others; vented many bitter execrations against the brother; and concluded with wishing something could be done for the unfortunate family.", "그는 남의 빚에 제 몸을 걸어 책임지는 어리석음을 강하게 성토했고, 그 형제를 향해 신랄한 저주를 숱하게 퍼부었으며, 그 불행한 가족을 위해 무언가 할 수 있으면 좋겠다는 바람으로 말을 맺었다. \"부인,\""], ["“Suppose, madam,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “you should recommend them to Mr Allworthy?", "\"그들을 올워디 씨께 천거해 보시면 어떨까요?"], ["Or what think you of a collection?", "아니면 모금을 하는 건 어떻게 생각하세요?"], ["I will give them a guinea with all my heart.”", "저는 기꺼이 1기니를 내놓겠습니다.\""]], [["Mrs Miller made no answer; and Nancy, to whom her mother had whispered the generosity of Jones, turned pale upon the occasion; though, if either of them was angry with Nightingale, it was surely without reason. For the liberality of Jones, if he had known it, was not an example which he had any obligation to follow; and there are thousands who would not have contributed a single halfpenny, as indeed he did not in effect, for he made no tender of anything; and therefore, as the others thought proper to make no demand, he kept his money in his pocket.", "밀러 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그리고 어미가 존스의 너그러움을 귀엣말로 일러 준 낸시는 그 말에 얼굴이 창백해졌다. 하기야 두 사람 중 누구든 나이팅게일에게 화가 났다면, 그것은 분명 까닭 없는 노여움이었으리라. 설령 그가 존스의 후한 인심을 알았다 한들, 그것은 그가 따를 의무가 있는 본보기는 아니었으니 말이다. 게다가 반 푼도 내놓지 않을 사람이 수천 명은 될 것이며, 실은 그도 사실상 그러했다. 그는 무엇 하나 내밀지 않았고, 따라서 다른 이들이 굳이 청하지 않기로 한 이상, 그는 제 돈을 주머니에 그대로 간직했던 것이다."]], [["I have, in truth, observed, and shall never have a better opportunity than at present to communicate my observation, that the world are in general divided into two opinions concerning charity, which are the very reverse of each other.", "사실 나는 한 가지를 줄곧 눈여겨봐 왔는데, 지금보다 그 생각을 전하기에 더 좋은 기회는 다시없을 듯하다. 그것은 자선을 두고 세상이 대체로 정반대되는 두 가지 견해로 나뉜다는 점이다."], ["One party seems to hold, that all acts of this kind are to be esteemed as voluntary gifts, and, however little you give (if indeed no more than your good wishes), you acquire a great degree of merit in so doing.", "한쪽은 이런 종류의 행위는 모두 자발적인 선물로 여겨야 하며, 아무리 적게 준다 해도 (설령 그저 좋은 마음 하나뿐일지라도) 그렇게 함으로써 큰 공덕을 쌓는 것이라고 여기는 듯하다."], ["Others, on the contrary, appear to be as firmly persuaded, that beneficence is a positive duty, and that whenever the rich fall greatly short of their ability in relieving the distresses of the poor, their pitiful largesses are so far from being meritorious, that they have only performed their duty by halves, and are in some sense more contemptible than those who have entirely neglected it.", "반대로 다른 쪽은, 선행이란 마땅히 해야 할 의무이며, 부유한 자가 가난한 이의 고통을 덜어 줄 자기 능력에 한참 못 미치게 베풀 때 그 알량한 베풂은 공덕은커녕 제 의무를 절반밖에 행하지 못한 것이고, 어떤 의미에서는 그 일을 아예 저버린 자들보다 더 경멸받을 만하다고 굳게 믿는 듯하다."]], [["To reconcile these different opinions is not in my power.", "이 서로 다른 견해를 화해시키는 일은 내 힘으로 될 바가 아니다."], ["I shall only add, that the givers are generally of the former sentiment, and the receivers are almost universally inclined to the latter.", "다만 한 가지만 덧붙이자면, 베푸는 자들은 대체로 앞쪽 생각을 지니고, 받는 자들은 거의 예외 없이 뒤쪽으로 기운다는 것이다."]], [["# Chapter ix.", "# Chapter ix."]], [["Which treats of matters of a very different kind from those in the preceding chapter.", "앞 장에서 다룬 것과는 전혀 다른 종류의 문제들을 다루는 장."]], [["In the evening Jones met his lady again, and a long conversation again ensued between them: but as it consisted only of the same ordinary occurrences as before, we shall avoid mentioning particulars, which we despair of rendering agreeable to the reader; unless he is one whose devotion to the fair sex, like that of the papists to their saints, wants to be raised by the help of pictures. But I am so far from desiring to exhibit such pictures to the public, that I would wish to draw a curtain over those that have been lately set forth in certain French novels; very bungling copies of which have been presented us here under the name of translations.", "저녁에 존스는 다시 그 귀부인을 만났고, 두 사람 사이에는 또 한 차례 긴 대화가 이어졌다. 그러나 그 대화라는 것이 앞서와 똑같이 평범한 이야기들로만 이루어져 있었으므로, 우리는 그 세세한 내용을 언급하지 않으려 한다. 독자에게 즐거움을 줄 가망이 없다고 보기 때문이다. 다만 그 독자가, 가톨릭교도들이 성인들을 향해 그러하듯, 여성에 대한 헌신을 그림의 도움으로 북돋워야 하는 부류라면 모를까. 그러나 나는 그런 그림을 대중 앞에 내보이기를 바라기는커녕, 최근 어떤 프랑스 소설들에 펼쳐진 그런 장면들 위에 차라리 장막을 드리우고 싶다. 그것들의 솜씨 서툰 모작이 번역이라는 이름으로 이곳 우리에게 소개되어 왔으니 말이다."]], [["Jones grew still more and more impatient to see Sophia; and finding, after repeated interviews with Lady Bellaston, no likelihood of obtaining this by her means (for, on the contrary, the lady began to treat even the mention of the name of Sophia with resentment), he resolved to try some other method.", "존스는 소피아를 보고 싶은 마음에 점점 더 안달이 났다. 그러나 벨라스턴 부인과 거듭 만남을 가져 보아도 그녀를 통해 그 뜻을 이룰 가망이 보이지 않자(오히려 부인은 소피아의 이름을 입에 올리는 것조차 노여움으로 대하기 시작했다), 그는 다른 방법을 시도해 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["He made no doubt but that Lady Bellaston knew where his angel was, so he thought it most likely that some of her servants should be acquainted with the same secret.", "그는 벨라스턴 부인이 자신의 천사가 어디 있는지 알고 있으리라고 의심치 않았고, 따라서 그녀의 하인들 가운데 누군가도 같은 비밀을 알고 있을 가능성이 가장 높다고 생각했다."], ["Partridge therefore was employed to get acquainted with those servants, in order to fish this secret out of them.", "그리하여 파트리지가 그 하인들과 가까워져 그들에게서 이 비밀을 캐내는 임무를 맡게 되었다."]], [["Few situations can be imagined more uneasy than that to which his poor master was at present reduced; for besides the difficulties he met with in discovering Sophia, besides the fears he had of having disobliged her, and the assurances he had received from Lady Bellaston of the resolution which Sophia had taken against him, and of her having purposely concealed herself from him, which he had sufficient reason to believe might be true; he had still a difficulty to combat which it was not in the power of his mistress to remove, however kind her inclination might have been. This was the exposing of her to be disinherited of all her father's estate, the almost inevitable consequence of their coming together without a consent, which he had no hopes of ever obtaining.", "그의 가엾은 주인이 지금 처한 처지보다 더 불편한 상황은 좀처럼 상상하기 어려울 것이다. 소피아를 찾아내는 데서 마주친 어려움 외에도, 자신이 그녀의 비위를 거슬렀을지 모른다는 두려움, 그리고 소피아가 그를 거부하기로 결심했으며 일부러 그에게서 몸을 숨겼다고 벨라스턴 부인에게서 들은 단언이 있었으니 말이다. 게다가 그 말이 사실일지도 모른다고 믿을 만한 충분한 이유가 그에게는 있었다. 그뿐만 아니라 그에게는 여전히 맞서 싸워야 할 또 하나의 난관이 있었는데, 그것은 그의 연인이 아무리 다정한 마음을 품었다 한들 그녀의 힘으로는 도저히 없앨 수 없는 것이었다. 바로 그녀를 아버지의 전 재산에서 상속받지 못하게 만드는 일이었다. 두 사람이 동의 없이 맺어지면 거의 피할 수 없는 결과가 그것이었고, 그 동의를 얻을 가망이라곤 그에게 전혀 없었다."]], [["Add to all these the many obligations which Lady Bellaston, whose violent fondness we can no longer conceal, had heaped upon him; so that by her means he was now become one of the best-dressed men about town; and was not only relieved from those ridiculous distresses we have before mentioned, but was actually raised to a state of affluence beyond what he had ever known.", "여기에다, 이제 더는 감출 수 없게 된 그 격렬한 애정의 주인공 벨라스턴 부인이 그에게 베풀어 쌓아 올린 수많은 은혜까지 더해 보라. 그녀의 도움으로 그는 이제 런던에서 가장 옷을 잘 입는 남자 가운데 하나가 되었고, 앞서 언급한 그 우스꽝스러운 곤궁에서 벗어났을 뿐 아니라, 실로 일찍이 경험해 본 적 없는 풍족한 처지로까지 올라섰다."]], [["Now, though there are many gentlemen who very well reconcile it to their consciences to possess themselves of the whole fortune of a woman, without making her any kind of return; yet to a mind, the proprietor of which doth not deserved to be hanged, nothing is, I believe, more irksome than to support love with gratitude only; especially where inclination pulls the heart a contrary way. Such was the unhappy case of Jones; for though the virtuous love he bore to Sophia, and which left very little affection for any other woman, had been entirely out of the question, he could never have been able to have made any adequate return to the generous passion of this lady, who had indeed been once an object of desire, but was now entered at least into the autumn of life, though she wore all the gaiety of youth, both in her dress and manner; nay, she contrived still to maintain the roses in her cheeks; but these, like flowers forced out of season by art, had none of that lively blooming freshness with which Nature, at the proper time, bedecks her own productions. She had, besides, a certain imperfection, which renders some flowers, though very beautiful to the eye, very improper to be placed in a wilderness of sweets, and what above all others is most disagreeable to the breath of love.", "이제, 한 여인의 전 재산을 차지하고도 그녀에게 아무런 보답도 하지 않은 채 그것을 양심과 곧잘 화해시키는 신사들이 많기는 하다. 그러나 교수형에 처해 마땅하지 않을 정도의 양심을 지닌 사람에게는, 사랑을 오직 감사만으로 떠받치는 것보다 더 괴로운 일은 없으리라고 나는 믿는다. 더구나 마음이 정반대 쪽으로 끌릴 때는 말이다. 존스의 불행한 처지가 바로 그러했다. 그가 소피아에게 품은 그 정결한 사랑, 다른 어떤 여인을 향한 애정도 거의 남겨 두지 않은 그 사랑을 아예 제쳐 놓더라도, 그는 이 귀부인의 너그러운 정열에 결코 합당한 보답을 해 줄 수 없었을 것이다. 그녀는 한때 욕망의 대상이었던 것이 사실이나, 이제는 적어도 인생의 가을로 접어든 사람이었다. 비록 옷차림에서나 몸가짐에서나 여전히 젊음의 발랄함을 모두 걸치고 있었고, 심지어 두 뺨의 장밋빛까지 용케 유지하고 있었지만 말이다. 그러나 그 장밋빛은, 기술로 철 이르게 피워 낸 꽃처럼, 자연이 제때에 자기 작품을 단장할 때 입혀 주는 그 생기 있고 만발한 싱그러움을 조금도 지니지 못했다. 게다가 그녀에게는 어떤 결함이 하나 있었는데, 그것은 어떤 꽃들이 눈으로 보기에는 무척 아름다울지언정 향기로운 꽃밭 한가운데 두기에는 몹시 부적절하게, 그리고 무엇보다 사랑의 숨결에 가장 거슬리게 만드는 그런 종류의 것이었다."]], [["Though Jones saw all these discouragements on the one side, he felt his obligations full as strongly on the other; nor did he less plainly discern the ardent passion whence those obligations proceeded, the extreme violence of which if he failed to equal, he well knew the lady would think him ungrateful; and, what is worse, he would have thought himself so. He knew the tacit consideration upon which all her favours were conferred; and as his necessity obliged him to accept them, so his honour, he concluded, forced him to pay the price. This therefore he resolved to do, whatever misery it cost him, and to devote himself to her, from that great principle of justice, by which the laws of some countries oblige a debtor, who is no otherwise capable of discharging his debt, to become the slave of his creditor.", "존스는 한편으로 이 모든 의기를 꺾는 사정들을 보면서도, 다른 한편으로는 자신이 진 은혜를 그에 못지않게 절절히 느꼈다. 또한 그 은혜가 흘러나온 그 뜨거운 정열을 그는 똑똑히 알아보았으니, 그 극도의 격렬함에 자신이 부응하지 못한다면 부인이 그를 배은망덕한 자로 여기리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 더 나쁜 것은, 그 자신도 스스로를 그렇게 여기게 되리라는 점이었다. 그는 그녀의 온갖 호의가 어떤 무언의 대가를 전제로 베풀어진 것인지 알고 있었다. 그리고 자신의 궁핍이 그것들을 받아들이도록 그를 몰아붙였듯이, 자신의 명예가 그 대가를 치르도록 자신을 강요한다고 결론지었다. 그리하여 그는 그 일을 하기로 마음먹었다. 그것이 자신에게 어떤 비참함을 안기든, 어떤 나라의 법이 달리 빚을 갚을 길이 없는 채무자로 하여금 채권자의 노예가 되도록 강제하는 그 위대한 정의의 원리에 따라, 그녀에게 자신을 바치기로 한 것이다."]], [["While he was meditating on these matters, he received the following note from the lady:--", "이런 문제들을 곰곰이 생각하고 있을 때, 그는 부인에게서 다음과 같은 쪽지를 받았다."]], [["“A very foolish, but a very perverse accident hath happened since our last meeting, which makes it improper I should see you any more at the usual place.", "\"우리가 마지막으로 만난 이후로 아주 어리석으면서도 아주 고약한 일이 하나 생겼는데, 그 때문에 늘 만나던 그곳에서 더는 당신을 보는 것이 마땅치 않게 되었습니다."], ["I will, if possible, contrive some other place by to-morrow.", "가능하다면 내일까지 다른 장소를 마련해 보겠어요."], ["In the meantime, adieu.”", "그동안, 안녕히.\""]], [["This disappointment, perhaps, the reader may conclude was not very great; but if it was, he was quickly relieved; for in less than an hour afterwards another note was brought him from the same hand, which contained as follows:--", "이 실망이 그리 크지 않았으리라고 독자는 어쩌면 짐작할지도 모른다. 그러나 설령 컸다 한들 그는 곧 위안을 얻었다. 한 시간도 채 지나지 않아 같은 손에서 온 또 다른 쪽지가 그에게 전해졌는데, 그 내용은 이러했다."]], [["“I have altered my mind since I wrote; a change which, if you are no stranger to the tenderest of all passions, you will not wonder at.", "\"편지를 쓰고 나서 마음이 바뀌었습니다. 만일 당신이 모든 정열 가운데 가장 애틋한 그것을 모르는 사람이 아니라면, 이런 변덕에 놀라지 않으시겠지요."], ["I am now resolved to see you this evening at my own house, whatever may be the consequence.", "이제 나는 무슨 일이 생기든 오늘 저녁 내 집에서 당신을 만나기로 결심했습니다."], ["Come to me exactly at seven; I dine abroad, but will be at home by that time.", "정확히 일곱 시에 와 주세요. 나는 밖에서 저녁을 먹지만 그 시각까지는 집에 와 있을 거예요."], ["A day, I find, to those that sincerely love, seems longer than I imagined.", "진심으로 사랑하는 이들에게는, 알고 보니 하루가 내 생각보다 더 길게 느껴지는군요."]], [["“If you should accidentally be a few moments before me, bid them show you into the drawing-room.”", "혹시라도 나보다 몇 분 일찍 도착하거든, 응접실로 안내해 달라고 이르세요.\""]], [["To confess the truth, Jones was less pleased with this last epistle than he had been with the former, as he was prevented by it from complying with the earnest entreaties of Mr Nightingale, with whom he had now contracted much intimacy and friendship. These entreaties were to go with that young gentleman and his company to a new play, which was to be acted that evening, and which a very large party had agreed to damn, from some dislike they had taken to the author, who was a friend to one of Mr Nightingale's acquaintance. And this sort of fun, our heroe, we are ashamed to confess, would willingly have preferred to the above kind appointment; but his honour got the better of his inclination.", "사실대로 고백하자면, 존스는 이 마지막 편지가 앞선 편지보다 덜 반가웠다. 이 편지 때문에 그는 나이팅게일 군의 간곡한 청을 들어줄 수 없게 되었기 때문이다. 그는 이제 나이팅게일과 꽤 깊은 친분과 우정을 맺은 사이였다. 그 청이란, 그 젊은 신사와 그 일행과 함께 그날 저녁 상연될 어느 새 연극을 보러 가자는 것이었다. 많은 무리가 그 연극을 야유로 망쳐 버리기로 합의해 두었는데, 작가에게 어떤 반감을 품었기 때문이었다. 그 작가는 나이팅게일 군의 지인 가운데 한 사람과 친구 사이였다. 그리고 이런 종류의 장난을, 부끄럽지만 고백하건대 우리의 주인공은 위에 말한 그 다정한 약속보다 기꺼이 더 좋아했을 것이다. 그러나 그의 명예가 그 마음을 눌러 이겼다."]], [["Before we attend him to this intended interview with the lady, we think proper to account for both the preceding notes, as the reader may possibly be not a little surprized at the imprudence of Lady Bellaston, in bringing her lover to the very house where her rival was lodged.", "우리가 그를 따라 이 예정된 부인과의 만남으로 가기 전에, 앞의 두 쪽지에 대해 모두 설명을 해 두는 것이 마땅하리라 본다. 독자가 어쩌면 벨라스턴 부인의 경솔함에, 즉 자기 연인을 하필 자기 연적이 묵고 있는 바로 그 집으로 불러들인 일에 적잖이 놀랄지도 모르기 때문이다."]], [["First, then, the mistress of the house where these lovers had hitherto met, and who had been for some years a pensioner to that lady, was now become a methodist, and had that very morning waited upon her ladyship, and after rebuking her very severely for her past life, had positively declared that she would, on no account, be instrumental in carrying on any of her affairs for the future.", "먼저, 이 연인들이 지금까지 만나 왔던 그 집의 안주인은 여러 해 동안 그 부인에게서 연금을 받아 온 사람이었는데, 이제 감리교도가 되어 바로 그날 아침 부인을 찾아와 지난날의 삶을 몹시 호되게 꾸짖고는, 앞으로는 어떤 일이 있어도 부인의 어떤 정사든 거드는 도구가 되지 않겠다고 단호히 선언해 버린 것이다."]], [["The hurry of spirits into which this accident threw the lady made her despair of possibly finding any other convenience to meet Jones that evening; but as she began a little to recover from her uneasiness at the disappointment, she set her thoughts to work, when luckily it came into her head to propose to Sophia to go to the play, which was immediately consented to, and a proper lady provided for her companion. Mrs Honour was likewise despatched with Mrs Etoff on the same errand of pleasure; and thus her own house was left free for the safe reception of Mr Jones, with whom she promised herself two or three hours of uninterrupted conversation after her return from the place where she dined, which was at a friend's house in a pretty distant part of the town, near her old place of assignation, where she had engaged herself before she was well apprized of the revolution that had happened in the mind and morals of her late confidante.", "이 사건이 부인을 몰아넣은 마음의 동요는, 그날 저녁 존스를 만날 다른 편의 장소를 도저히 찾을 수 없으리라는 절망에 빠지게 했다. 그러나 그 실망에서 비롯된 불안에서 조금씩 회복되기 시작하면서 그녀는 머리를 굴리기 시작했는데, 다행히도 소피아에게 연극을 보러 가자고 제안하자는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그 제안은 곧바로 받아들여졌고, 그녀의 동행으로 적당한 부인이 한 사람 마련되었다. 어너 부인 또한 같은 유흥의 심부름을 위해 에토프 부인과 함께 보내졌다. 이렇게 하여 그녀 자신의 집은 존스 군을 안전하게 맞아들이기에 마침맞게 비워졌고, 그녀는 저녁 식사를 한 곳에서 돌아온 뒤 그와 두세 시간 방해받지 않는 대화를 나누기를 기약했다. 저녁을 먹은 곳은 런던에서 꽤 멀리 떨어진 동네에 있는 한 친구의 집이었는데, 그곳은 예전의 밀회 장소 근처였다. 그녀는 옛 심복의 마음과 도덕에 일어난 그 변혁을 채 제대로 알기도 전에 그 약속을 잡아 두었던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["A chapter which, though short, may draw tears from some eyes.", "존스는 마침 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아뵙기 위해 옷을 다 차려입은 참이었는데, 그때 밀러 부인이 그의 방문을 두드렸다."], ["Mr Jones was just dressed to wait on Lady Bellaston, when Mrs Miller rapped at his door; and, being admitted, very earnestly desired his company below-stairs, to drink tea in the parlour.", "안으로 들어선 그녀는 아래층 응접실에서 함께 차를 마시자며 그의 동석을 간곡히 청했다."]], [["Upon his entrance into the room, she presently introduced a person to him, saying, “This, sir, is my cousin, who hath been so greatly beholden to your goodness, for which he begs to return you his sincerest thanks.”", "그가 방에 들어서자 그녀는 곧바로 한 사람을 그에게 소개하며 말했다. \"이 사람은 제 사촌이랍니다, 선생님. 선생님의 너그러우심에 큰 신세를 진 사람이라, 진심 어린 감사를 드리고 싶어 한답니다.\""]], [["The man had scarce entered upon that speech which Mrs Miller had so kindly prefaced, when both Jones and he, looking stedfastly at each other, showed at once the utmost tokens of surprize.", "밀러 부인이 그토록 다정하게 서두를 떼어 준 그 인사말을 그 사내가 채 시작하기도 전에, 존스와 그는 서로를 뚫어지게 바라보다가 동시에 더없는 놀라움의 기색을 드러냈다."], ["The voice of the latter began instantly to faulter; and, instead of finishing his speech, he sunk down into a chair, crying, “It is so, I am convinced it is so!”", "사내의 목소리는 당장에 떨리기 시작했고, 인사말을 끝맺기는커녕 의자에 털썩 주저앉으며 외쳤다. \"맞아, 틀림없어, 분명히 그래!\""]], [["“Bless me!", "\"이런!"], ["what's the meaning of this?”", "이게 다 무슨 영문이람?\""], ["cries Mrs Miller; “you are not ill, I hope, cousin?", "밀러 부인이 소리쳤다. \"어디 아픈 건 아니지요, 사촌?"], ["Some water, a dram this instant.”", "물 좀, 어서 술 한 잔.\""]], [["“Be not frighted, madam,”", "\"놀라지 마십시오, 부인.\""], ["cries Jones, “I have almost as much need of a dram as your cousin.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"저도 댁의 사촌 못지않게 술 한 잔이 절실합니다."], ["We are equally surprized at this unexpected meeting.", "이 뜻밖의 만남에 우리 둘 다 똑같이 놀란 것이지요."], ["Your cousin is an acquaintance of mine, Mrs Miller.”", "밀러 부인, 댁의 사촌은 제가 아는 사람입니다.\""]], [["“An acquaintance!”", "\"아는 사람이라니!\""], ["cries the man.--“Oh, heaven!”", "사내가 외쳤다. \"오, 하느님!\""]], [["“Ay, an acquaintance,”", "\"그렇소, 아는 사람이지요.\""], ["repeated Jones, “and an honoured acquaintance too.", "존스가 거듭 말했다. \"게다가 존경하는 사람이기도 합니다."], ["When I do not love and honour the man who dares venture everything to preserve his wife and children from instant destruction, may I have a friend capable of disowning me in adversity!”", "아내와 자식들을 당장의 파멸에서 지켜 내려 모든 것을 무릅쓰는 사람을 제가 사랑하고 존경하지 않는다면, 저 역시 역경 속에서 저를 모른 체할 수 있는 벗을 두어 마땅하겠지요!\""]], [["“Oh, you are an excellent young man,”", "\"오, 선생님은 참으로 훌륭한 청년이세요.\""], ["cries Mrs Miller:--“Yes, indeed, poor creature!", "밀러 부인이 외쳤다. \"그래요, 정말이지 가엾은 사람이랍니다!"], ["he hath ventured everything.--If he had not had one of the best of constitutions, it must have killed him.”", "이 사람은 모든 것을 무릅썼어요. 타고난 체질이 워낙 튼튼하지 않았더라면 그 일로 목숨을 잃었을 거예요.\""]], [["“Cousin,”", "\"사촌,\""], ["cries the man, who had now pretty well recovered himself, “this is the angel from heaven whom I meant.", "이제 어지간히 정신을 차린 사내가 외쳤다. \"제가 말씀드린 하늘에서 내려온 천사가 바로 이분이에요."], ["This is he to whom, before I saw you, I owed the preservation of my Peggy.", "제가 누이를 뵙기도 전에, 제 페기의 목숨을 지켜 준 분이 바로 이분입니다."], ["He it was to whose generosity every comfort, every support which I have procured for her, was owing.", "제가 그 사람을 위해 마련할 수 있었던 온갖 위안과 온갖 도움이 다 이분의 너그러움 덕분이었어요."], ["He is, indeed, the worthiest, bravest, noblest; of all human beings.", "이분이야말로 정말이지 모든 인간 가운데 가장 가치 있고, 가장 용감하며, 가장 고귀한 분입니다."], ["O cousin, I have obligations to this gentleman of such a nature!”", "오, 사촌, 제가 이분께 진 신세가 어떤 것이냐면!\""]], [["“Mention nothing of obligations,”", "\"신세 이야기는 입에 올리지 마십시오.\""], ["cries Jones eagerly; “not a word, I insist upon it, not a word”", "존스가 다급히 외쳤다. \"한마디도, 부탁드리니 한마디도 마십시오.\""], ["(meaning, I suppose, that he would not have him betray the affair of the robbery to any person). “If, by the trifle you have received from me, I have preserved a whole family, sure pleasure was never bought so cheap.”", "(강도 사건을 아무에게도 발설하지 않게 하려는 뜻이었으리라.) \"제게서 받은 그 하찮은 것으로 한 가정 전체를 지켜 낸 것이라면, 이토록 값싸게 기쁨을 산 적은 일찍이 없었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Oh, sir!” cries the man, “I wish you could this instant see my house. If any person had ever a right to the pleasure you mention, I am convinced it is yourself. My cousin tells me she acquainted you with the distress in which she found us. That, sir, is all greatly removed, and chiefly by your goodness.----My children have now a bed to lie on----and they have----they have----eternal blessings reward you for it!----they have bread to eat. My little boy is recovered; my wife is out of danger, and I am happy. All, all owing to you, sir, and to my cousin here, one of the best of women. Indeed, sir, I must see you at my house.--Indeed my wife must see you, and thank you.--My children too must express their gratitude.----Indeed, sir, they are not without a sense of their obligation; but what is my feeling when I reflect to whom I owe that they are now capable of expressing their gratitude.----Oh, sir, the little hearts which you have warmed had now been cold as ice without your assistance.”", "\"오, 선생님!\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"지금 당장 제 집을 보실 수 있다면 좋으련만. 선생님께서 말씀하신 그 기쁨을 누릴 자격이 있는 사람이 있다면, 그건 바로 선생님이라고 저는 확신합니다. 제 사촌이 그러더군요, 우리가 처한 그 곤경을 선생님께 알려 드렸다고요. 그 곤경이, 선생님, 이제는 거의 다 사라졌습니다. 무엇보다 선생님의 너그러우심 덕분에요. 이제 제 아이들은 누울 잠자리가 생겼고, 또 그 아이들은, 그 아이들은, 그 일로 영원한 축복이 선생님께 보답하기를! 그 아이들은 먹을 빵이 있습니다. 어린 아들은 다 나았고, 아내도 고비를 넘겼으며, 저는 행복합니다. 다, 다 선생님 덕분이에요, 선생님, 그리고 여기 계신 제 사촌, 더없이 훌륭한 여인 가운데 한 분인 이분 덕분입니다. 정말이지, 선생님, 저는 선생님을 제 집에서 꼭 뵙고 싶습니다. 제 아내도 꼭 선생님을 뵙고 감사드려야 합니다. 제 아이들도 그 고마움을 표해야 하고요. 정말이지, 선생님, 그 아이들도 자기들이 진 신세를 모르지 않습니다. 하지만 그 아이들이 지금 그 고마움을 표할 수 있게 된 것이 누구 덕분인지를 떠올리면, 제 가슴이 어떠하겠습니까. 오, 선생님, 선생님이 따뜻이 데워 주신 그 작은 가슴들이, 선생님의 도움이 없었더라면 지금쯤 얼음장처럼 차갑게 식어 있었을 겁니다.\""]], [["Here Jones attempted to prevent the poor man from proceeding; but indeed the overflowing of his own heart would of itself have stopped his words. And now Mrs Miller likewise began to pour forth thanksgivings, as well in her own name, as in that of her cousin, and concluded with saying, “She doubted not but such goodness would meet a glorious reward.”", "여기서 존스는 가엾은 사내가 더 말을 잇지 못하게 막으려 했다. 하지만 정작 사내 자신의 벅차오르는 가슴이 저절로 그 말문을 막아 버렸다. 그러자 이번에는 밀러 부인까지 감사의 말을 쏟아 내기 시작했으니, 자기 자신의 이름으로도, 또 사촌의 이름으로도 그러했다. 그리고 그녀는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"이런 선한 마음에는 틀림없이 영광스러운 보답이 따를 거예요.\""]], [["Jones answered, “He had been sufficiently rewarded already.", "존스가 대답했다. \"저는 이미 충분히 보답받았습니다."], ["Your cousin's account, madam,”", "부인, 댁의 사촌이 들려준 이야기는,\""], ["said he, “hath given me a sensation more pleasing than I have ever known.", "그가 말했다. \"제가 일찍이 알지 못했던 더없이 흐뭇한 감정을 제게 안겨 주었습니다."], ["He must be a wretch who is unmoved at hearing such a story; how transporting then must be the thought of having happily acted a part in this scene!", "이런 이야기를 듣고도 마음이 움직이지 않는 자라면 비참한 인간일 테지요. 그러니 이 장면에서 다행히도 한몫을 해냈다는 생각이 얼마나 가슴 벅찬 것이겠습니까!"], ["If there are men who cannot feel the delight of giving happiness to others, I sincerely pity them, as they are incapable of tasting what is, in my opinion, a greater honour, a higher interest, and a sweeter pleasure than the ambitious, the avaricious, or the voluptuous man can ever obtain.”", "만일 남에게 행복을 베푸는 그 기쁨을 느끼지 못하는 사람이 있다면, 저는 진심으로 그들을 가엾게 여깁니다. 제 생각에 그것은 야심가도, 탐욕가도, 향락가도 결코 얻을 수 없는 더 큰 영예이자 더 높은 이익이며 더 달콤한 기쁨인데, 그들은 그 맛을 볼 수 없는 사람들이니까요.\""]], [["The hour of appointment being now come, Jones was forced to take a hasty leave, but not before he had heartily shaken his friend by the hand, and desired to see him again as soon as possible; promising that he would himself take the first opportunity of visiting him at his own house.", "이제 약속한 시각이 되어 존스는 서둘러 자리를 떠야 했지만, 그 전에 진심으로 친구의 손을 굳게 잡고 되도록 빨리 다시 만나기를 청했다. 그리고 자기가 직접 첫 기회를 보아 그의 집을 찾아가겠노라 약속했다."], ["He then stept into his chair, and proceeded to Lady Bellaston's, greatly exulting in the happiness which he had procured to this poor family; nor could he forbear reflecting, without horror, on the dreadful consequences which must have attended them, had he listened rather to the voice of strict justice than to that of mercy, when he was attacked on the high road.", "그런 다음 그는 가마에 올라타 벨라스턴 부인의 댁으로 향했는데, 이 가난한 가정에 마련해 준 행복에 크게 마음이 들떠 있었다. 또한 그는 큰길에서 습격을 당했을 때 자비의 목소리가 아니라 엄정한 정의의 목소리에 귀를 기울였더라면 그들에게 닥쳤을 끔찍한 결말을, 소름 끼치지 않고는 떠올릴 수 없었다."]], [["Mrs Miller sung forth the praises of Jones during the whole evening, in which Mr Anderson, while he stayed, so passionately accompanied her, that he was often on the very point of mentioning the circumstance of the robbery.", "밀러 부인은 저녁 내내 존스를 찬양해 마지않았는데, 앤더슨 씨도 머무는 동안 어찌나 열렬히 그녀에게 맞장구를 쳤던지, 강도 사건의 정황을 입 밖에 낼 뻔한 적이 한두 번이 아니었다."], ["However, he luckily recollected himself, and avoided an indiscretion which would have been so much the greater, as he knew Mrs Miller to be extremely strict and nice in her principles.", "그러나 그는 다행히 정신을 차려, 그러잖아도 더 큰 경솔이 될 일을 피했다. 밀러 부인이 자기 원칙에 대해 지극히 엄격하고 까다로운 사람임을 그가 알고 있었던 만큼 더욱 그러했다."], ["He was likewise well apprized of the loquacity of this lady; and yet such was his gratitude, that it had almost got the better both of discretion and shame, and made him publish that which would have defamed his own character, rather than omit any circumstances which might do the fullest honour to his benefactor.", "또한 그는 이 부인이 말이 많다는 것도 익히 알고 있었다. 그런데도 그의 감사하는 마음이 어찌나 컸던지, 그것이 분별심과 수치심을 모두 거의 눌러 버려, 자기 은인에게 더없는 영예를 돌릴 수 있는 어떤 정황이라도 빠뜨리느니 차라리 제 평판을 더럽힐 그 일을 떠벌릴 뻔하게 만들었던 것이다."]], [["In which the reader will be surprized.", "독자가 깜짝 놀라게 될 이야기."]], [["Mr Jones was rather earlier than the time appointed, and earlier than the lady; whose arrival was hindered, not only by the distance of the place where she dined, but by some other cross accidents very vexatious to one in her situation of mind. He was accordingly shown into the drawing-room, where he had not been many minutes before the door opened, and in came----no other than Sophia herself, who had left the play before the end of the first act; for this, as we have already said, being, a new play, at which two large parties met, the one to damn, and the other to applaud, a violent uproar, and an engagement between the two parties, had so terrified our heroine, that she was glad to put herself under the protection of a young gentleman who safely conveyed her to her chair.", "존스는 약속된 시각보다 다소 일찍 도착했고, 부인보다도 일찍 와 있었다. 부인의 도착이 늦어진 것은 그녀가 저녁을 먹은 곳이 멀어서이기도 했지만, 그녀처럼 심란한 처지에 놓인 사람에게는 몹시 성가신 다른 자잘한 사고들이 겹쳤기 때문이기도 했다. 그리하여 존스는 응접실로 안내되었는데, 그곳에 든 지 몇 분 지나지 않아 문이 열리고 들어선 사람은—다름 아닌 소피아 자신이었다. 그녀는 첫 막이 끝나기도 전에 연극을 떠나온 터였다. 앞서 이미 말했듯이 이 작품은 새로 올린 연극이어서, 두 패거리가 한쪽은 야유를 퍼붓고 다른 한쪽은 갈채를 보내려고 몰려들었던 것이다. 두 패 사이에 격렬한 소란과 충돌이 벌어지자 우리의 여주인공은 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지, 한 젊은 신사의 보호를 받으며 무사히 가마까지 데려다 받게 된 것을 다행으로 여겼다."]], [["As Lady Bellaston had acquainted her that she should not be at home till late, Sophia, expecting to find no one in the room, came hastily in, and went directly to a glass which almost fronted her, without once looking towards the upper end of the room, where the statue of Jones now stood motionless.---In this glass it was, after contemplating her own lovely face, that she first discovered the said statue; when, instantly turning about, she perceived the reality of the vision: upon which she gave a violent scream, and scarce preserved herself from fainting, till Jones was able to move to her, and support her in his arms.", "벨라스턴 부인이 늦게야 돌아올 것이라 일러두었던 터라, 소피아는 방에 아무도 없으리라 여기고 급히 들어와, 거의 정면에 놓인 거울로 곧장 다가갔다. 방 위쪽 끝, 지금 존스가 조각상처럼 꼼짝 않고 서 있는 그곳은 한 번도 돌아보지 않은 채였다. 그녀가 자신의 사랑스러운 얼굴을 들여다보다가 바로 그 거울 속에서 그 조각상을 처음 발견하고는, 곧장 몸을 돌려 그 환영이 실제임을 알아차렸다. 그 순간 그녀는 날카로운 비명을 지르며 거의 까무러칠 뻔했으나, 존스가 그녀에게 다가가 두 팔로 부축할 때까지 가까스로 정신을 붙들었다."]], [["To paint the looks or thoughts of either of these lovers, is beyond my power.", "이 두 연인의 표정이나 생각을 그려내는 일은 내 능력 밖이다."], ["As their sensations, from their mutual silence, may be judged to have been too big for their own utterance, it cannot be supposed that I should be able to express them: and the misfortune is, that few of my readers have been enough in love to feel by their own hearts what past at this time in theirs.", "둘 다 말이 없었던 것으로 보아 그들의 감정이 스스로 입에 담기엔 너무 벅찼으리라 짐작되니, 내가 그것을 표현할 수 있으리라고는 도저히 생각할 수 없다. 게다가 안타까운 일은, 내 독자들 가운데 이때 그들의 가슴속에 오간 것을 자기 마음으로 느껴볼 만큼 사랑에 빠져본 이가 드물다는 점이다."]], [["After a short pause, Jones, with faultering accents, said--“I see, madam, you are surprized.”--“Surprized!”", "잠깐의 침묵이 지난 뒤, 존스가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"보아하니, 아가씨, 놀라신 듯합니다.\""], ["answered she; “Oh heavens!", "\"놀라다니요!\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["Indeed, I am surprized.", "\"오, 맙소사! 정말이지, 저는 놀랐습니다."], ["I almost doubt whether you are the person you seem.”--“Indeed,”", "당신이 정말 그 사람인지조차 의심스러울 지경이에요.\" \"정말입니다.\""], ["cries he, “my Sophia, pardon me, madam, for this once calling you so, I am that very wretched Jones, whom fortune, after so many disappointments, hath, at last, kindly conducted to you.", "그가 외쳤다. \"나의 소피아—아가씨, 이번 한 번만은 그렇게 불러도 용서해 주십시오—저는 바로 그 가엾은 존스입니다."], ["Oh! my Sophia, did you know the thousand torments I have suffered in this long, fruitless pursuit.”--“Pursuit of whom?”", "그토록 많은 좌절을 겪은 끝에, 운명이 마침내 자애롭게도 저를 당신께로 이끌어 준 것입니다."], ["said Sophia, a little recollecting herself, and assuming a reserved air.--“Can you be so cruel to ask that question?”", "오! 나의 소피아, 이 길고 헛된 추적 속에서 제가 견뎌온 그 수천 가지 고통을 당신이 아신다면.\""], ["cries Jones; “Need I say, of you?”", "\"누구를 추적했단 말인가요?\" 소피아는 다소 정신을 가다듬으며 새침한 태도를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Of me!”", "\"어찌 그리 모질게 그런 질문을 하실 수 있습니까?\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["answered Sophia: “Hath Mr Jones, then, any such important business with me?”--“To some, madam,”", "\"당신을, 이라고 말할 필요가 있겠습니까?\" \"저를요!\""], ["cries Jones, “this might seem an important business”", "소피아가 대답했다. \"그렇다면 존스 씨에게 저와 관련된 그토록 중대한 볼일이라도 있는 건가요?\""], ["(giving her the pocket-book).", "\"어떤 이들에게는, 아가씨,\" 존스가 외치며 그녀에게 수첩을 건넸다."], ["“I hope, madam, you will find it of the same value as when it was lost.”", "\"이것이 중대한 볼일로 보일 수도 있겠지요. 부디, 아가씨, 잃어버렸을 때와 같은 값어치 그대로 들어 있기를 바랍니다.\""], ["Sophia took the pocket-book, and was going to speak, when he interrupted her thus:--“Let us not, I beseech you, lose one of these precious moments which fortune hath so kindly sent us.", "소피아는 수첩을 받아 들고 막 입을 열려 했으나, 그가 이렇게 말을 가로막았다. \"부탁이니, 운명이 이토록 자애롭게 우리에게 보내준 이 귀한 순간을 단 한 순간도 헛되이 흘려보내지 맙시다."], ["O, my Sophia!", "오, 나의 소피아!"], ["I have business of a much superior kind.", "제게는 훨씬 더 중대한 볼일이 있습니다. 이렇게 무릎을 꿇고, 당신의 용서를 구하게 해 주십시오.\""], ["Thus, on my knees, let me ask your pardon.”--“My pardon!”", "\"용서를요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["cries she; “Sure, sir, after what is past, you cannot expect, after what I have heard.”--“I scarce know what I say,”", "\"분명, 선생님, 그간 있었던 일을 생각하면, 그리고 제가 들은 바를 생각하면, 그것을 바라실 수는 없겠지요.\" \"제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모르겠습니다.\""], ["answered Jones.", "존스가 대답했다."], ["“By heavens!", "\"하늘에 맹세코!"], ["I scarce wish you should pardon me.", "당신이 저를 용서해 주기를 바라지도 못하겠습니다."], ["O my Sophia!", "오, 나의 소피아!"], ["henceforth never cast away a thought on such a wretch as I am.", "이제부터 저 같은 가엾은 인간에게는 다시는 한 가닥 생각도 던지지 마십시오."], ["If any remembrance of me should ever intrude to give a moment's uneasiness to that tender bosom, think of my unworthiness; and let the remembrance of what passed at Upton blot me for ever from your mind.”", "만약 저에 대한 어떤 기억이라도 끼어들어 그 여린 가슴에 한순간의 불편함이라도 안겨준다면, 제가 그럴 자격 없는 인간임을 떠올려 주십시오. 그리고 어프턴에서 있었던 일의 기억으로, 당신의 마음에서 저를 영원히 지워 버려 주십시오.\""]], [["Sophia stood trembling all this while.", "소피아는 그동안 내내 떨며 서 있었다."], ["Her face was whiter than snow, and her heart was throbbing through her stays.", "그녀의 얼굴은 눈보다 희었고, 가슴은 코르셋 너머로 두근거리고 있었다."], ["But, at the mention of Upton, a blush arose in her cheeks, and her eyes, which before she had scarce lifted up, were turned upon Jones with a glance of disdain.", "그러나 어프턴이 언급되자 두 뺨에 홍조가 솟았고, 그때까지 거의 들지도 않았던 두 눈이 경멸의 눈빛을 띤 채 존스를 향했다."], ["He understood this silent reproach, and replied to it thus: “O my Sophia!", "그는 이 말 없는 질책을 알아차리고 이렇게 응답했다. \"오, 나의 소피아!"], ["my only love!", "나의 유일한 사랑!"], ["you cannot hate or despise me more for what happened there than I do myself; but yet do me the justice to think that my heart was never unfaithful to you.", "그곳에서 일어난 일에 대해, 당신이 저를 미워하고 경멸하는 것보다 제가 저 자신을 더 미워하고 경멸합니다. 하지만 그래도 제게 한 가지 정당한 평가만은 내려 주십시오."], ["That had no share in the folly I was guilty of; it was even then unalterably yours.", "제 마음은 결코 당신께 불충하지 않았다는 것을요. 제가 저지른 그 어리석음에 마음만은 조금도 가담하지 않았습니다."], ["Though I despaired of possessing you, nay, almost of ever seeing you more, I doated still on your charming idea, and could seriously love no other woman.", "그때조차 제 마음은 변함없이 당신의 것이었습니다. 비록 당신을 차지하리라는 희망을, 아니, 다시 뵙는 일조차 거의 단념했지만, 저는 여전히 당신의 매혹적인 모습을 마음에 품고 그리워했으며, 다른 어떤 여인도 진심으로 사랑할 수 없었습니다."], ["But if my heart had not been engaged, she, into whose company I accidently fell at that cursed place, was not an object of serious love.", "그러나 설령 제 마음이 매여 있지 않았다 해도, 그 저주받은 곳에서 우연히 함께하게 된 그 여인은 진지한 사랑의 대상이 될 만한 사람이 아니었습니다."], ["Believe me, my angel, I never have seen her from that day to this; and never intend or desire to see her again.”", "믿어 주십시오, 나의 천사여, 저는 그날 이후 오늘까지 그녀를 한 번도 보지 않았으며, 다시 볼 생각도 바람도 없습니다.\""], ["Sophia, in her heart, was very glad to hear this; but forcing into her face an air of more coldness than she had yet assumed, “Why,”", "소피아는 속으로 이 말을 듣고 무척 기뻤다. 그러나 지금까지 지어 보였던 것보다 더 차가운 기색을 억지로 얼굴에 띄우며 말했다."], ["said she, “Mr Jones, do you take the trouble to make a defence where you are not accused?", "\"그런데, 존스 씨, 어찌하여 고발당하지도 않은 일에 대해 굳이 변명하는 수고를 하시나요?"], ["If I thought it worth while to accuse you, I have a charge of unpardonable nature indeed.”--“What is it, for heaven's sake?”", "만약 제가 당신을 고발할 만하다고 여겼다면, 정말이지 용서받지 못할 성질의 죄목이 하나 있긴 합니다만.\" \"그게 대체 무엇입니까, 제발?\""], ["answered Jones, trembling and pale, expecting to hear of his amour with Lady Bellaston.", "존스는 떨며 창백해진 채 대답했다. 벨라스턴 부인과의 정사 이야기가 나올까 봐서였다."], ["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said she, “how is it possible!", "그녀가 말했다. \"어찌 그럴 수가!"], ["can everything noble and everything base be lodged together in the same bosom?”", "모든 고귀한 것과 모든 비천한 것이 한 가슴속에 함께 깃들 수 있단 말인가요?\""], ["Lady Bellaston, and the ignominious circumstance of having been kept, rose again in his mind, and stopt his mouth from any reply.", "벨라스턴 부인과, 첩처럼 얹혀살았다는 그 치욕스러운 사정이 다시 그의 마음속에 떠올라, 어떤 대꾸도 못하도록 입을 막아 버렸다. \"제가,\""], ["“Could I have expected,”", "소피아가 말을 이었다."], ["proceeded Sophia, “such treatment from you?", "\"당신에게서 이런 처사를 예상할 수 있었겠어요?"], ["Nay, from any gentleman, from any man of honour?", "아니, 어떤 신사에게서, 명예를 아는 어떤 남자에게서라도요?"], ["To have my name traduced in public; in inns, among the meanest vulgar!", "제 이름이 공개적으로, 여관에서, 가장 천한 무리들 사이에서 더럽혀지다니요!"], ["to have any little favours that my unguarded heart may have too lightly betrayed me to grant, boasted of there!", "경계심 없는 제 마음이 너무 가볍게 베풀어 버린 작은 호의들이 그런 데서 자랑거리가 되다니요!"], ["nay, even to hear that you had been forced to fly from my love!”", "아니, 심지어 당신이 제 사랑으로부터 도망쳐야만 했다는 말까지 듣다니요!\""]], [["Nothing could equal Jones's surprize at these words of Sophia; but yet, not being guilty, he was much less embarrassed how to defend himself than if she had touched that tender string at which his conscience had been alarmed. By some examination he presently found, that her supposing him guilty of so shocking an outrage against his love, and her reputation, was entirely owing to Partridge's talk at the inns before landlords and servants; for Sophia confessed to him it was from them that she received her intelligence. He had no very great difficulty to make her believe that he was entirely innocent of an offence so foreign to his character; but she had a great deal to hinder him from going instantly home, and putting Partridge to death, which he more than once swore he would do. This point being cleared up, they soon found themselves so well pleased with each other, that Jones quite forgot he had begun the conversation with conjuring her to give up all thoughts of him; and she was in a temper to have given ear to a petition of a very different nature; for before they were aware they had both gone so far, that he let fall some words that sounded like a proposal of marriage. To which she replied, “That, did not her duty to her father forbid her to follow her own inclinations, ruin with him would be more welcome to her than the most affluent fortune with another man.” At the mention of the word ruin, he started, let drop her hand, which he had held for some time, and striking his breast with his own, cried out, “Oh, Sophia! can I then ruin thee? No; by heavens, no! I never will act so base a part. Dearest Sophia, whatever it costs me, I will renounce you; I will give you up; I will tear all such hopes from my heart as are inconsistent with your real good. My love I will ever retain, but it shall be in silence; it shall be at a distance from you; it shall be in some foreign land; from whence no voice, no sigh of my despair, shall ever reach and disturb your ears. And when I am dead”--He would have gone on, but was stopt by a flood of tears which Sophia let fall in his bosom, upon which she leaned, without being able to speak one word. He kissed them off, which, for some moments, she allowed him to do without any resistance; but then recollecting herself, gently withdrew out of his arms; and, to turn the discourse from a subject too tender, and which she found she could not support, bethought herself to ask him a question she never had time to put to him before, “How he came into that room?” He began to stammer, and would, in all probability, have raised her suspicions by the answer he was going to give, when, at once, the door opened, and in came Lady Bellaston.", "소피아의 이 말에 존스가 느낀 놀라움은 무엇과도 견줄 수 없었다. 그러나 죄가 없는 그였기에, 만약 그녀가 그의 양심이 켕기던 그 여린 줄을 건드렸을 때보다 훨씬 덜 곤혹스럽게 자신을 변호할 수 있었다. 몇 마디 따져 물어본 끝에 그는, 그녀가 자신을 두고 사랑과 명예에 대한 그토록 충격적인 만행을 저질렀다고 여기게 된 것이, 전적으로 파트리지가 여관에서 주인장들과 하인들 앞에서 떠벌린 말 때문임을 곧 알게 되었다. 소피아가 바로 그들에게서 그 정보를 들었다고 그에게 털어놓았기 때문이다. 자신의 성품과는 동떨어진 그런 죄에 자신이 전혀 무고하다는 것을 그녀가 믿게 만드는 일은 그리 어렵지 않았다. 그러나 그가 당장 집으로 달려가 파트리지를 죽여 버리겠다고 한두 번이 아니게 맹세하는 것을 그녀가 말리느라 무척이나 애를 먹어야 했다. 이 점이 풀리고 나자, 두 사람은 이내 서로가 너무도 흡족해진 나머지, 존스는 자신을 향한 모든 생각을 단념해 달라고 간청하며 대화를 시작했던 것조차 까맣게 잊어버렸다. 그리고 그녀 역시 전혀 다른 성격의 청원에라도 귀를 기울일 만한 마음 상태였다. 미처 깨닫기도 전에 두 사람은 이미 너무 멀리 나아가, 그가 청혼처럼 들리는 몇 마디를 흘리고 말았기 때문이다. 이에 그녀는 이렇게 대답했다. \"아버지께 대한 도리가 제 마음 가는 대로 따르는 것을 막지만 않는다면, 다른 남자와 더없이 풍족한 재산을 누리는 것보다 당신과 함께 파멸하는 편이 제게는 더 반가울 거예요.\" 파멸이라는 말이 나오자 그는 흠칫하더니, 한동안 잡고 있던 그녀의 손을 놓고, 제 손으로 제 가슴을 치며 외쳤다. \"오, 소피아! 그렇다면 제가 당신을 파멸시킬 수 있겠습니까? 아니요, 하늘에 맹세코, 안 됩니다! 저는 결코 그런 비열한 짓은 하지 않겠습니다. 사랑하는 소피아, 어떤 대가를 치르더라도, 저는 당신을 단념하겠습니다. 당신을 놓아 드리겠습니다. 당신의 진정한 행복과 어긋나는 그런 온갖 희망을 제 가슴에서 모조리 뜯어내겠습니다. 제 사랑만은 영원히 간직하겠으나, 그것은 침묵 속에 있을 것입니다. 당신에게서 멀리 떨어진 곳에 있을 것이며, 어느 낯선 이국 땅에 있을 것입니다. 그곳에서는 제 절망의 어떤 목소리도, 어떤 한숨도 결코 당신의 귀에 닿아 당신을 어지럽히지 못할 것입니다. 그리고 제가 죽고 나면—\" 그는 말을 더 이으려 했으나, 소피아가 그의 가슴에 쏟아낸 눈물의 물결에 가로막혔다. 그녀는 그의 가슴에 기댄 채 한마디도 하지 못했다. 그는 그 눈물에 입을 맞춰 닦아냈고, 그녀는 한동안 아무 저항 없이 그가 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두었다. 그러나 이내 정신을 차리고 그의 두 팔에서 부드럽게 빠져나왔다. 그리고 너무도 여려서 도저히 감당할 수 없을 것 같은 화제에서 벗어나려고, 전에는 미처 물어볼 겨를이 없었던 질문 하나를 그에게 던질 생각을 해냈다. \"어떻게 이 방에 들어오게 되셨어요?\" 그가 더듬거리기 시작했고, 십중팔구 그가 막 내놓으려던 대답으로 그녀의 의심을 자아냈을 바로 그때, 별안간 문이 열리며 벨라스턴 부인이 들어왔다."]], [["Having advanced a few steps, and seeing Jones and Sophia together, she suddenly stopt; when, after a pause of a few moments, recollecting herself with admirable presence of mind, she said--though with sufficient indications of surprize both in voice and countenance--“I thought, Miss Western, you had been at the play?”", "몇 걸음 들어서다가 존스와 소피아가 함께 있는 것을 보고 그녀는 문득 멈춰 섰다. 그러더니 몇 순간의 침묵 끝에, 감탄할 만한 침착함으로 정신을 가다듬고 말했다—목소리와 표정 양쪽에 놀라움의 기색이 충분히 드러나 있긴 했지만. \"웨스턴 양, 연극을 보러 가 있는 줄 알았는데요?\""]], [["Though Sophia had no opportunity of learning of Jones by what means he had discovered her, yet, as she had not the least suspicion of the real truth, or that Jones and Lady Bellaston were acquainted, so she was very little confounded; and the less, as the lady had, in all their conversations on the subject, entirely taken her side against her father.", "소피아는 존스가 어떤 방법으로 자신을 찾아냈는지 그에게서 들을 기회가 없었지만, 그 진짜 진상을, 혹은 존스와 벨라스턴 부인이 아는 사이라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았으므로 거의 당황하지 않았다. 더욱이 이 부인은 그 문제에 관한 모든 대화에서 그녀의 아버지에 맞서 전적으로 그녀의 편을 들어 왔던 터라 더욱 그러했다."], ["With very little hesitation, therefore, she went through the whole story of what had happened at the play-house, and the cause of her hasty return.", "그래서 그녀는 별 망설임 없이 극장에서 일어난 일의 자초지종과 자신이 서둘러 돌아온 까닭을 죽 이야기했다."]], [["The length of this narrative gave Lady Bellaston an opportunity of rallying her spirits, and of considering in what manner to act.", "이 이야기가 긴 덕분에 벨라스턴 부인은 마음을 추스르고 어떻게 처신할지 궁리할 틈을 얻었다."], ["And as the behaviour of Sophia gave her hopes that Jones had not betrayed her, she put on an air of good humour, and said, “I should not have broke in so abruptly upon you, Miss Western, if I had known you had company.”", "그리고 소피아의 태도로 보아 존스가 자신을 발설하지 않은 듯 희망이 생기자, 그녀는 쾌활한 기색을 띠며 말했다. \"웨스턴 양, 손님이 와 계신 줄 알았더라면 이렇게 불쑥 끼어들지 않았을 텐데요.\""]], [["Lady Bellaston fixed her eyes on Sophia whilst she spoke these words.", "벨라스턴 부인은 이 말을 하는 동안 소피아에게 시선을 고정하고 있었다."], ["To which that poor young lady, having her face overspread with blushes and confusion, answered, in a stammering voice, “I am sure, madam, I shall always think the honour of your ladyship's company----”", "그 가엾은 젊은 아가씨는 얼굴이 온통 홍조와 당혹으로 뒤덮인 채 더듬거리는 목소리로 대답했다. \"정말이지, 부인, 저는 부인의 자리에 함께해 주시는 영광을 언제나—\""], ["“I hope, at least,”", "\"적어도 제가,\" 벨라스턴 부인이 외쳤다."], ["cries Lady Bellaston, “I interrupt no business.”--“No, madam,”", "\"무슨 볼일을 방해하는 건 아니길 바라요.\" \"아니에요, 부인,\""], ["answered Sophia, “our business was at an end.", "소피아가 대답했다. \"저희 볼일은 끝났어요."], ["Your ladyship may be pleased to remember I have often mentioned the loss of my pocket-book, which this gentleman, having very luckily found, was so kind to return it to me with the bill in it.”", "부인께서도 기억하시겠지만, 제가 수첩을 잃어버렸다는 얘기를 여러 번 했었지요. 이분이 운 좋게도 그걸 주워서, 친절하게도 안에 든 어음과 함께 돌려주러 오신 거예요.\""]], [["Jones, ever since the arrival of Lady Bellaston, had been ready to sink with fear.", "존스는 벨라스턴 부인이 들어선 그 순간부터 두려움에 푹 가라앉을 지경이었다."], ["He sat kicking his heels, playing with his fingers, and looking more like a fool, if it be possible, than a young booby squire, when he is first introduced into a polite assembly.", "그는 발뒤꿈치를 까딱거리고 손가락을 만지작거리며, 가능하다면, 세련된 모임에 처음 끌려 나온 풋내기 시골 도령보다도 더 얼간이 같은 꼴로 앉아 있었다."], ["He began, however, now to recover himself; and taking a hint from the behaviour of Lady Bellaston, who he saw did not intend to claim any acquaintance with him, he resolved as entirely to affect the stranger on his part.", "그러나 이제 그도 차츰 정신을 추스르기 시작했다. 그리고 벨라스턴 부인이 자기와 아는 사이임을 내세울 생각이 없음을 알아채고, 그 태도에서 단서를 얻어, 자기도 똑같이 처음 보는 사람인 척하기로 마음먹었다."], ["He said, “Ever since he had the pocket-book in his possession, he had used great diligence in enquiring out the lady whose name was writ in it; but never till that day could be so fortunate to discover her.”", "그는 말했다. \"수첩을 손에 넣은 뒤로, 그 안에 이름이 적힌 부인을 수소문하느라 무던히도 애를 썼습니다만, 오늘에야 비로소 운 좋게 그분을 찾아낼 수 있었습니다.\""]], [["Sophia had indeed mentioned the loss of her pocket-book to Lady Bellaston; but as Jones, for some reason or other, had never once hinted to her that it was in his possession, she believed not one syllable of what Sophia now said, and wonderfully admired the extreme quickness of the young lady in inventing such an excuse.", "소피아는 과연 수첩을 잃어버린 일을 벨라스턴 부인에게 말한 적이 있었다. 그러나 존스가 무슨 까닭에서인지 그것이 자기 손에 있다는 것을 그녀에게 한 번도 내비친 적이 없었으므로, 그녀는 지금 소피아가 하는 말을 한 음절도 믿지 않았고, 그토록 재빠르게 그런 핑계를 지어내는 이 젊은 아가씨의 비상한 기지에 그저 놀랄 따름이었다."], ["The reason of Sophia's leaving the playhouse met with no better credit; and though she could not account for the meeting between these two lovers, she was firmly persuaded it was not accidental.", "소피아가 극장을 떠난 이유 역시 그보다 나을 것 없는 신빙성을 얻었다. 비록 이 두 연인 사이의 만남을 설명할 길은 없었지만, 그녀는 그것이 우연이 아니라고 굳게 확신했다."]], [["With an affected smile, therefore, she said, “Indeed, Miss Western, you have had very good luck in recovering your money.", "그래서 그녀는 짐짓 꾸민 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"정말이지, 웨스턴 양, 돈을 되찾다니 운이 무척 좋았네요."], ["Not only as it fell into the hands of a gentleman of honour, but as he happened to discover to whom it belonged.", "명예를 아는 신사분 손에 들어간 데다, 그분이 마침 그게 누구 것인지까지 알아내셨으니 말이에요."], ["I think you would not consent to have it advertised.--It was great good fortune, sir, that you found out to whom the note belonged.”", "양은 그걸 광고로 내는 데는 동의하지 않으셨을 것 같은데요.—선생님, 그 어음이 누구 것인지 알아내신 건 대단한 행운이었어요.\""]], [["“Oh, madam,”", "\"오, 부인,\""], ["cries Jones, “it was enclosed in a pocket-book, in which the young lady's name was written.”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"그건 수첩 안에 들어 있었고, 거기에 이 아가씨의 이름이 적혀 있었거든요.\""]], [["“That was very fortunate, indeed,”", "\"그거야말로 정말 다행이었네요.\" 부인이 외쳤다."], ["cries the lady:--“And it was no less so, that you heard Miss Western was at my house; for she is very little known.”", "\"그리고 웨스턴 양이 제 집에 있다는 걸 들으신 것도 그에 못지않게 다행이었고요. 이 사람은 아는 이가 거의 없거든요.\""]], [["Jones had at length perfectly recovered his spirits; and as he conceived he had now an opportunity of satisfying Sophia as to the question she had asked him just before Lady Bellaston came in, he proceeded thus: “Why, madam,” answered he, “it was by the luckiest chance imaginable I made this discovery. I was mentioning what I had found, and the name of the owner, the other night to a lady at the masquerade, who told me she believed she knew where I might see Miss Western; and if I would come to her house the next morning she would inform me, I went according to her appointment, but she was not at home; nor could I ever meet with her till this morning, when she directed me to your ladyship's house. I came accordingly, and did myself the honour to ask for your ladyship; and upon my saying that I had very particular business, a servant showed me into this room; where I had not been long before the young lady returned from the play.”", "존스는 마침내 완전히 평정을 되찾았다. 그리고 벨라스턴 부인이 들어오기 직전에 소피아가 던졌던 질문에 대해 그녀에게 해명할 기회가 지금 생겼다고 여기고는, 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"저, 부인,\" 그가 대답했다. \"상상할 수 있는 가장 운 좋은 우연으로 이걸 알아냈습니다. 며칠 전 밤 가면무도회에서 어느 부인께 제가 주운 물건과 그 주인의 이름을 말씀드렸더니, 그분이 웨스턴 양을 어디서 뵐 수 있을지 아실 것 같다며, 이튿날 아침 자기 집으로 오면 알려 주겠다고 하셨지요. 약속대로 갔습니다만, 그분은 집에 안 계셨습니다. 그 뒤로도 도무지 만날 수가 없다가, 오늘 아침에야 비로소 만나 부인 댁으로 가 보라고 일러 주셨습니다. 그래서 이리로 와서 부인을 뵙고자 청하는 영광을 누렸고, 제가 아주 특별한 볼일이 있다고 하자, 하인이 저를 이 방으로 안내했습니다. 그리고 그리 오래지 않아 이 아가씨가 연극에서 돌아오신 겁니다.\""]], [["Upon his mentioning the masquerade, he looked very slily at Lady Bellaston, without any fear of being remarked by Sophia; for she was visibly too much confounded to make any observations. This hint a little alarmed the lady, and she was silent; when Jones, who saw the agitation of Sophia's mind, resolved to take the only method of relieving her, which was by retiring; but, before he did this, he said, “I believe, madam, it is customary to give some reward on these occasions;--I must insist on a very high one for my honesty;--it is, madam, no less than the honour of being permitted to pay another visit here.”", "가면무도회 이야기를 꺼내면서 그는 벨라스턴 부인을 아주 슬쩍 곁눈질했는데, 소피아에게 들킬까 봐 걱정할 필요는 전혀 없었다. 그녀는 보아하니 너무도 당황한 나머지 무엇 하나 눈여겨볼 처지가 아니었던 것이다. 이 암시에 부인은 다소 놀라 입을 다물었다. 그때 소피아의 마음이 동요하는 것을 본 존스는, 그녀를 풀어 줄 유일한 방법, 곧 물러나는 길을 택하기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 그렇게 하기 전에 그가 말했다. \"부인, 이런 경우에는 으레 얼마간 사례를 하는 것이 관례라고 알고 있습니다.—저는 제 정직함에 대해 아주 후한 보답을 한사코 청해야겠습니다.—그것은, 부인, 다름이 아니라, 이곳을 다시 한 번 방문하도록 허락받는 영광입니다.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["replied the lady, “I make no doubt that you are a gentleman, and my doors are never shut to people of fashion.”", "부인이 대답했다. \"당신이 신사라는 점은 조금도 의심치 않으며, 제 집 문은 지체 있는 분들께는 결코 닫혀 있지 않답니다.\""]], [["Jones then, after proper ceremonials, departed, highly to his own satisfaction, and no less to that of Sophia; who was terribly alarmed lest Lady Bellaston should discover what she knew already but too well.", "그리하여 존스는 격식에 맞는 예를 갖춘 뒤 떠났는데, 자신도 무척 만족스러웠고 소피아 또한 그에 못지않게 만족스러웠다. 그녀는 자신이 이미 너무도 잘 알고 있는 사실을 벨라스턴 부인이 알아챌까 봐 몹시 마음 졸이던 참이었던 것이다."]], [["Upon the stairs Jones met his old acquaintance, Mrs Honour, who, notwithstanding all she had said against him, was now so well bred to behave with great civility.", "계단에서 존스는 오랜 지인인 어너 부인과 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 그토록 그를 험담했으면서도 이제는 아주 예의 바르게 굴 만큼 처신이 능숙했다."], ["This meeting proved indeed a lucky circumstance, as he communicated to her the house where he lodged, with which Sophia was unacquainted.", "이 만남은 실로 운 좋은 일로 드러났는데, 존스가 자신이 묵고 있는 집을 그녀에게 알려 주었기 때문이다. 그 집을 소피아는 모르고 있었다."]], [["In which the thirteenth book is concluded.", "우아한 섀프츠베리 경은 어딘가에서 진실을 지나치게 많이 말하는 것에 반대한다."], ["The elegant Lord Shaftesbury somewhere objects to telling too much truth: by which it may be fairly inferred, that, in some cases, to lie is not only excusable but commendable.", "이로 미루어 보면, 어떤 경우에는 거짓말이 단지 용서받을 만한 일일 뿐 아니라 칭찬받을 만한 일이기도 하다고 충분히 짐작할 수 있다."]], [["And surely there are no persons who may so properly challenge a right to this commendable deviation from truth, as young women in the affair of love; for which they may plead precept, education, and above all, the sanction, nay, I may say the necessity of custom, by which they are restrained, not from submitting to the honest impulses of nature (for that would be a foolish prohibition), but from owning them.", "그리고 분명, 이처럼 칭찬받을 만한 진실로부터의 일탈을 누릴 권리를 가장 정당하게 주장할 수 있는 이들이라면, 사랑 문제에 놓인 젊은 여인들만 한 사람이 없을 것이다. 그들은 이를 위해 가르침과 교육을, 그리고 무엇보다 관습의 승인, 아니 관습의 필연성까지 내세울 수 있기 때문이다. 그 관습은 그들을 자연의 정직한 충동에 순응하지 못하도록 막는 것이 아니라(그런 금지란 어리석은 일일 테니), 그 충동을 인정하지 못하도록 막는 것이다."]], [["We are not, therefore, ashamed to say, that our heroine now pursued the dictates of the above-mentioned right honourable philosopher.", "그러므로 우리는 이렇게 말하는 것을 부끄러워하지 않는다. 우리의 여주인공이 이제 앞서 말한 그 존경하는 철학자의 가르침을 좇았노라고."], ["As she was perfectly satisfied then, that Lady Bellaston was ignorant of the person of Jones, so she determined to keep her in that ignorance, though at the expense of a little fibbing.", "그녀는 벨라스턴 부인이 존스라는 사람을 알아보지 못한다는 사실에 그때 완전히 안심하고 있었으므로, 약간의 거짓말을 치르더라도 부인을 그 무지 속에 그대로 두기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Jones had not been long gone, before Lady Bellaston cryed, “Upon my word, a good pretty young fellow; I wonder who he is; for I don't remember ever to have seen his face before.”", "존스가 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않아 벨라스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 제법 잘생긴 젊은이로군요. 대체 누구일까요. 저 얼굴을 전에 본 기억이 도무지 없으니 말이에요.\""]], [["“Nor I neither, madam,”", "\"저도 그래요, 부인.\""], ["cries Sophia.", "소피아가 말했다."], ["“I must say he behaved very handsomely in relation to my note.”", "\"제 쪽지 일에 관해서는 참 점잖게 처신했다고 해야겠어요.\""]], [["“Yes; and he is a very handsome fellow,”", "\"그래요. 게다가 아주 잘생긴 청년이지요.\""], ["said the lady: “don't you think so?”", "부인이 말했다. \"그렇게 생각하지 않나요?\""]], [["“I did not take much notice of him,”", "\"별로 눈여겨보지 않았어요.\""], ["answered Sophia, “but I thought he seemed rather awkward, and ungenteel than otherwise.”", "소피아가 대답했다. \"다만 어딘가 어색하고 품위가 없어 보이던걸요.\""]], [["“You are extremely right,” cries Lady Bellaston: “you may see, by his manner, that he hath not kept good company. Nay, notwithstanding his returning your note, and refusing the reward, I almost question whether he is a gentleman.----I have always observed there is a something in persons well born, which others can never acquire.----I think I will give orders not to be at home to him.”", "\"정말 옳은 말이에요.\" 벨라스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"그 태도만 봐도 좋은 사람들과 어울려 지내지 못했다는 걸 알 수 있지요. 아니, 쪽지를 돌려주고 사례를 거절했다고는 하지만, 저 사람이 과연 신사인지조차 의심스러워요. 저는 늘 보아 왔답니다. 잘 태어난 사람에게는 다른 이들이 결코 얻을 수 없는 무언가가 있다는 걸 말이에요. 저 사람에게는 집에 없다고 일러두라 명해야겠어요.\""]], [["“Nay, sure, madam,” answered Sophia, “one can't suspect after what he hath done;--besides, if your ladyship observed him, there was an elegance in his discourse, a delicacy, a prettiness of expression that, that----”", "\"아니에요, 부인, 정말이지.\" 소피아가 대답했다. \"저 사람이 한 일을 보고서야 그렇게 의심할 수는 없지요. 게다가 부인께서 눈여겨보셨다면, 그의 말에는 우아함이, 섬세함이, 표현의 어떤 고운 맛이 있었어요. 그게, 그게……\""]], [["“I confess,” said Lady Bellaston, “the fellow hath words----And indeed, Sophia, you must forgive me, indeed you must.”", "\"인정해요.\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"저 사람에게 말솜씨가 있긴 하지요. 그런데 정말이지, 소피아, 나를 용서해 줘야겠어요. 정말로요.\""]], [["“I forgive your ladyship!”", "\"제가 부인을 용서하다니요!\""], ["said Sophia.", "소피아가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, indeed you must,” answered she, laughing; “for I had a horrible suspicion when I first came into the room----I vow you must forgive it; but I suspected it was Mr Jones himself.”", "\"그래요, 정말 그래 줘야 해요.\" 부인이 웃으며 대답했다. \"방에 처음 들어섰을 때 끔찍한 의심이 들었거든요. 정말이지 용서해 줘야 해요. 나는 저 사람이 바로 존스 씨 본인이 아닌가 의심했답니다.\""]], [["“Did your ladyship, indeed?”", "\"정말로요, 부인?\""], ["cries Sophia, blushing, and affecting a laugh.", "소피아가 얼굴을 붉히며, 짐짓 웃음을 지으며 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, I vow I did,”", "\"그래요, 정말 그랬다니까요.\" 부인이 대답했다."], ["answered she.", "\"어쩌다 그런 생각이 머릿속에 들어왔는지 모르겠어요. 저 사람의 공은 인정해 줘야지요."], ["“I can't imagine what put it into my head: for, give the fellow his due, he was genteely drest; which, I think, dear Sophy, is not commonly the case with your friend.”", "옷차림이 점잖았으니까요. 그런데 사랑하는 소피, 그건 네 친구분께는 흔한 일이 아닐 것 같구나.\""]], [["“This raillery,”", "\"이런 놀림은요, 부인.\""], ["cries Sophia, “is a little cruel, Lady Bellaston, after my promise to your ladyship.”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"제가 부인께 약속을 드린 마당에 좀 매정하세요.\""]], [["“Not at all, child,”", "\"전혀 그렇지 않단다, 얘야.\" 부인이 말했다."], ["said the lady;----“It would have been cruel before; but after you have promised me never to marry without your father's consent, in which you know is implied your giving up Jones, sure you can bear a little raillery on a passion which was pardonable enough in a young girl in the country, and of which you tell me you have so entirely got the better.", "\"그 전이었다면 매정했겠지. 하지만 아버지의 동의 없이는 결코 결혼하지 않겠다고 내게 약속한 다음이잖니."], ["What must I think, my dear Sophy, if you cannot bear a little ridicule even on his dress?", "너도 알다시피 거기엔 존스를 단념한다는 뜻이 담겨 있고 말이야. 시골의 어린 처녀에게는 충분히 용서받을 만했던 그 연정에 대해, 그리고 네가 완전히 이겨 냈다고 내게 말한 그 연정에 대해, 가벼운 놀림쯤은 견딜 수 있겠지."], ["I shall begin to fear you are very far gone indeed; and almost question whether you have dealt ingenuously with me.”", "사랑하는 소피, 그 사람 옷차림에 대한 작은 우스갯소리조차 견디지 못한다면 내가 무슨 생각을 해야겠니? 네가 정말로 깊이 빠져 있는 게 아닌가 두려워지기 시작하고, 네가 내게 솔직하게 대했는지조차 의심스러워질 거야.\""]], [["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"정말이지, 부인.\""], ["cries Sophia, “your ladyship mistakes me, if you imagine I had any concern on his account.”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"제가 그 사람 때문에 무슨 마음이라도 쓴다고 여기신다면, 부인께서 저를 오해하시는 거예요.\""]], [["“On his account!” answered the lady: “You must have mistaken me; I went no farther than his dress;----for I would not injure your taste by any other comparison--I don't imagine, my dear Sophy, if your Mr Jones had been such a fellow as this--”", "\"그 사람 때문이라니!\" 부인이 대답했다. \"네가 나를 오해한 게 분명하구나. 나는 옷차림 이상은 말하지도 않았어. 그 밖의 어떤 비교로도 네 안목을 깎아내릴 생각은 없으니까. 사랑하는 소피, 네 존스 씨가 만일 이 사람 같은 자였다면, 나는 상상도……\""]], [["“I thought,”", "\"전 부인께서,\""], ["says Sophia, “your ladyship had allowed him to be handsome”----", "소피아가 말했다. \"그분을 잘생겼다고 인정하신 줄 알았는데요……\""]], [["“Whom, pray?”", "\"누구를 말이냐?\""], ["cried the lady hastily.", "부인이 다급히 외쳤다."]], [["“Mr Jones,” answered Sophia;--and immediately recollecting herself, “Mr Jones!--no, no; I ask your pardon;--I mean the gentleman who was just now here.”", "\"존스 씨요.\" 소피아가 대답했다. 그러고는 곧바로 정신을 차리며, \"존스 씨라니요! 아니, 아니에요. 죄송해요. 방금 여기 있던 그 신사분을 말한 거예요.\""]], [["“O Sophy!", "\"오, 소피!"], ["Sophy!”", "소피!\""], ["cries the lady; “this Mr Jones, I am afraid, still runs in your head.”", "부인이 외쳤다. \"이 존스 씨가, 아무래도 여전히 네 머릿속을 맴돌고 있는 모양이구나.\""]], [["“Then, upon my honour, madam,”", "\"그러면, 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데요, 부인.\""], ["said Sophia, “Mr Jones is as entirely indifferent to me, as the gentleman who just now left us.”", "소피아가 말했다. \"존스 씨는 방금 우리 곁을 떠난 그 신사분만큼이나 제게는 아무런 의미가 없는 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Upon my honour,”", "\"내 명예를 걸고,\""], ["said Lady Bellaston, “I believe it.", "벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"그 말을 믿으마."], ["Forgive me, therefore, a little innocent raillery; but I promise you I will never mention his name any more.”", "그러니 작은 악의 없는 놀림은 용서해 다오. 다시는 그 이름을 입에 올리지 않겠다고 약속할게.\""]], [["And now the two ladies separated, infinitely more to the delight of Sophia than of Lady Bellaston, who would willingly have tormented her rival a little longer, had not business of more importance called her away.", "이제 두 여인은 헤어졌는데, 그것은 벨라스턴 부인보다 소피아에게 한없이 더 큰 기쁨이었다. 부인으로서는 더 중요한 볼일이 그녀를 불러내지만 않았더라면 기꺼이 연적을 좀 더 오래 괴롭혔을 터였다."], ["As for Sophia, her mind was not perfectly easy under this first practice of deceit; upon which, when she retired to her chamber, she reflected with the highest uneasiness and conscious shame.", "소피아로 말하자면, 처음으로 거짓을 행한 뒤 마음이 결코 편치 못했다. 방으로 물러난 그녀는 그 일을 더없는 불안과 부끄러운 양심으로 곱씹었다."], ["Nor could the peculiar hardship of her situation, and the necessity of the case, at all reconcile her mind to her conduct; for the frame of her mind was too delicate to bear the thought of having been guilty of a falsehood, however qualified by circumstances.", "처지의 유난한 고달픔도, 그럴 수밖에 없었던 사정도, 자신의 행동을 마음과 화해시켜 주지는 못했다. 그녀의 마음은 너무도 섬세해서, 비록 상황 때문에 어쩔 수 없었다 해도 거짓을 저질렀다는 생각만큼은 견디지 못했던 것이다."], ["Nor did this thought once suffer her to close her eyes during the whole succeeding night.", "그리고 이 생각은 그날 밤 내내 한 번도 그녀가 눈을 붙이도록 내버려 두지 않았다."]], [["An essay to prove that an author will write the better for having some knowledge of the subject on which he writes.", "이틀 동안의 이야기를 담음. 글을 쓰려는 주제에 대해 얼마간의 지식을 갖춘 작가가 더 잘 쓴다는 것을 증명하려는 시론."]], [["As several gentlemen in these times, by the wonderful force of genius only, without the least assistance of learning, perhaps, without being well able to read, have made a considerable figure in the republic of letters; the modern critics, I am told, have lately begun to assert, that all kind of learning is entirely useless to a writer; and, indeed, no other than a kind of fetters on the natural sprightliness and activity of the imagination, which is thus weighed down, and prevented from soaring to those high flights which otherwise it would be able to reach.", "요즈음 여러 신사가 오로지 타고난 재능의 놀라운 힘만으로, 학문의 도움이라곤 조금도 받지 않고, 어쩌면 제대로 글을 읽을 줄도 모르면서도 문단에서 제법 이름을 떨치는지라, 들리는 바로는 근래의 비평가들이 이렇게 주장하기 시작했다고 한다. 온갖 종류의 학문은 작가에게 전혀 쓸모가 없으며, 실은 상상력의 타고난 발랄함과 활기를 옭아매는 일종의 족쇄에 지나지 않아서, 그로 인해 상상력이 짓눌려 본래라면 닿을 수 있었을 그 드높은 비상을 펼치지 못하게 된다는 것이다."]], [["This doctrine, I am afraid, is at present carried much too far: for why should writing differ so much from all other arts?", "이 학설은 지금 너무 멀리까지 끌고 간 것이 아닐까 싶다. 어찌하여 글쓰기만이 다른 모든 기예와 그토록 달라야 하는가?"], ["The nimbleness of a dancing-master is not at all prejudiced by being taught to move; nor doth any mechanic, I believe, exercise his tools the worse by having learnt to use them.", "춤 선생의 날렵함은 몸 놀리는 법을 배운다 해서 조금도 손상되지 않으며, 어떤 장인이든 연장 쓰는 법을 익혔다고 해서 그 연장을 더 서투르게 다루지는 않으리라 믿는다."], ["For my own part, I cannot conceive that Homer or Virgil would have writ with more fire, if instead of being masters of all the learning of their times, they had been as ignorant as most of the authors of the present age.", "나로서는, 호메로스나 베르길리우스가 자기 시대의 온갖 학문에 통달한 대가가 아니라 오늘날의 작가 대부분처럼 무지했더라면 더 뜨거운 불꽃으로 글을 썼으리라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없다."], ["Nor do I believe that all the imagination, fire, and judgment of Pitt, could have produced those orations that have made the senate of England, in these our times, a rival in eloquence to Greece and Rome, if he had not been so well read in the writings of Demosthenes and Cicero, as to have transferred their whole spirit into his speeches, and, with their spirit, their knowledge too.", "또한 피트의 그 모든 상상력과 열정과 판단력이라 한들, 만일 그가 데모스테네스와 키케로의 글을 깊이 읽어 그들의 정신을 통째로 자신의 연설 속에 옮겨 담고, 그 정신과 함께 그들의 지식까지 옮겨 오지 않았더라면, 우리 시대에 영국 의회를 웅변에서 그리스와 로마에 견줄 만한 곳으로 만든 그 연설들을 만들어낼 수 있었으리라고는 믿지 않는다."]], [["I would not here be understood to insist on the same fund of learning in any of my brethren, as Cicero persuades us is necessary to the composition of an orator.", "여기서 내가 동료 문인들 누구에게나 키케로가 웅변가를 길러내는 데 반드시 필요하다고 우리에게 설득하는 그만한 학식의 밑천을 요구한다고는 부디 오해하지 말기 바란다."], ["On the contrary, very little reading is, I conceive, necessary to the poet, less to the critic, and the least of all to the politician.", "오히려 시인에게는 아주 약간의 독서면 충분하고, 비평가에게는 그보다 더 적게, 정치가에게는 그중에서도 가장 적게 필요하다고 나는 생각한다."], ["For the first, perhaps, Byshe's Art of Poetry, and a few of our modern poets, may suffice; for the second, a moderate heap of plays; and, for the last, an indifferent collection of political journals.", "첫째 부류에게는 어쩌면 바이시의 《작시법》과 우리 현대 시인 몇 사람이면 족할 것이고, 둘째 부류에게는 그저 그런 희곡 한 무더기면 되며, 마지막 부류에게는 시원찮은 정치 잡지 모음 정도면 될 것이다."]], [["To say the truth, I require no more than that a man should have some little knowledge of the subject on which he treats, according to the old maxim of law, Quam quisque nôrit artem in eâ se exerceat.", "사실대로 말하자면, 나는 사람이 다루는 주제에 대해 얼마간의 작은 지식을 갖추기만을 바랄 뿐이니, 이는 \"각자 자신이 아는 기예 안에서 일하라\"는 옛 법언에 따른 것이다."], ["With this alone a writer may sometimes do tolerably well; and, indeed, without this, all the other learning in the world will stand him in little stead.", "이것 하나만으로도 작가는 때때로 그런대로 잘해낼 수 있으며, 실로 이것이 없다면 세상의 다른 모든 학문도 그에게 별 도움이 되지 못할 것이다."]], [["For instance, let us suppose that Homer and Virgil, Aristotle and Cicero, Thucydides and Livy, could have met all together, and have clubbed their several talents to have composed a treatise on the art of dancing: I believe it will be readily agreed they could not have equalled the excellent treatise which Mr Essex hath given us on that subject, entitled, The Rudiments of Genteel Education.", "이를테면 호메로스와 베르길리우스, 아리스토텔레스와 키케로, 투키디데스와 리비우스가 한자리에 모여 저마다의 재능을 한데 모아 춤의 기예에 관한 논고를 지었다고 가정해 보자. 그래도 그들이 에식스 씨가 그 주제에 관해 우리에게 내놓은 《품위 있는 교육의 기초》라는 저 빼어난 논고에는 미치지 못하리라는 데에 쉽게 동의하리라 믿는다."], ["And, indeed, should the excellent Mr Broughton be prevailed on to set fist to paper, and to complete the above-said rudiments, by delivering down the true principles of athletics, I question whether the world will have any cause to lament, that none of the great writers, either antient or modern, have ever treated about that noble and useful art.", "그리고 만일 탁월한 브로턴 씨가 마음을 돌려 펜을 잡고, 위에서 말한 그 기초에 운동의 참된 원리를 덧붙여 완성해 준다면, 고금의 위대한 작가 가운데 누구도 그 고귀하고 유익한 기예를 다룬 적이 없음을 세상이 한탄할 까닭이 과연 있을지 의문이다."]], [["To avoid a multiplicity of examples in so plain a case, and to come at once to my point, I am apt to conceive, that one reason why many English writers have totally failed in describing the manners of upper life, may possibly be, that in reality they know nothing of it.", "이토록 명백한 사안에 예를 잔뜩 늘어놓는 것을 피하고 단숨에 요점으로 가자면, 나는 많은 영국 작가가 상류 생활의 풍속을 묘사하는 데 완전히 실패한 한 가지 이유가, 실은 그들이 그것에 관해 아무것도 모르기 때문일 수 있다고 생각하는 편이다."]], [["This is a knowledge unhappily not in the power of many authors to arrive at.", "이것은 불행히도 많은 작가가 얻을 수 있는 능력 밖의 지식이다."], ["Books will give us a very imperfect idea of it; nor will the stage a much better: the fine gentleman formed upon reading the former will almost always turn out a pedant, and he who forms himself upon the latter, a coxcomb.", "책은 그것에 관해 매우 불완전한 관념밖에 주지 못하며, 무대도 그보다 그다지 나을 것이 없다. 앞의 것을 읽어 빚어진 멋진 신사는 거의 언제나 현학자로 드러나고, 뒤의 것을 본떠 자신을 빚은 자는 멋쟁이 허풍선이로 드러나기 마련이다."]], [["Nor are the characters drawn from these models better supported.", "이러한 본보기에서 그려낸 인물들도 더 잘 떠받쳐지는 것은 아니다."], ["Vanbrugh and Congreve copied nature; but they who copy them draw as unlike the present age as Hogarth would do if he was to paint a rout or a drum in the dresses of Titian and of Vandyke.", "밴브루와 콩그리브는 자연을 본떴다. 그러나 그들을 본뜨는 자들은 마치 호가스가 야회나 무도회를 티치아노와 반다이크 시대의 의상으로 그린다면 그러할 만큼이나 오늘날의 모습과 동떨어진 그림을 그린다."], ["In short, imitation here will not do the business.", "한마디로, 여기서는 모방이 일을 해내지 못한다."], ["The picture must be after Nature herself.", "그림은 반드시 자연 그 자체를 본떠야 한다."], ["A true knowledge of the world is gained only by conversation, and the manners of every rank must be seen in order to be known.", "세상에 대한 참된 앎은 오직 교제를 통해서만 얻어지며, 모든 계층의 풍속은 직접 보아야만 알 수 있는 법이다."]], [["Now it happens that this higher order of mortals is not to be seen, like all the rest of the human species, for nothing, in the streets, shops, and coffee-houses; nor are they shown, like the upper rank of animals, for so much a-piece.", "그런데 마침 이 인류의 더 높은 계급은, 나머지 모든 인간 부류처럼 거리며 가게며 커피하우스에서 공짜로 볼 수 있는 것이 아니요, 짐승 중 상등품처럼 한 마리에 얼마씩 받고 구경시켜 주는 것도 아니다."], ["In short, this is a sight to which no persons are admitted without one or other of these qualifications, viz., either birth or fortune, or, what is equivalent to both, the honourable profession of a gamester.", "한마디로 이것은 다음 자격 가운데 하나라도 갖추지 못하면 아무도 들여보내 주지 않는 구경거리이니, 곧 가문이나 재산, 혹은 그 둘에 맞먹는 것으로서 노름꾼이라는 명예로운 직업이 그것이다."], ["And, very unluckily for the world, persons so qualified very seldom care to take upon themselves the bad trade of writing; which is generally entered upon by the lower and poorer sort, as it is a trade which many think requires no kind of stock to set up with.", "그런데 세상으로서는 참으로 불운하게도, 그런 자격을 갖춘 사람들은 글쓰기라는 못난 장사를 스스로 떠맡으려 하는 일이 좀처럼 없다. 글쓰기란 흔히 더 낮고 가난한 부류가 발을 들이는 일인데, 밑천이라곤 한 푼도 들지 않고 차릴 수 있는 장사라고 많은 이가 여기기 때문이다."]], [["Hence those strange monsters in lace and embroidery, in silks and brocades, with vast wigs and hoops; which, under the name of lords and ladies, strut the stage, to the great delight of attorneys and their clerks in the pit, and of the citizens and their apprentices in the galleries; and which are no more to be found in real life than the centaur, the chimera, or any other creature of mere fiction.", "바로 여기서 저 기이한 괴물들이 나온다. 레이스와 수, 비단과 금란을 두르고 거대한 가발과 후프 치마를 걸친 채, 귀족과 귀부인이라는 이름으로 무대 위를 으스대며 거니는 자들 말이다."], ["But to let my reader into a secret, this knowledge of upper life, though very necessary for preventing mistakes, is no very great resource to a writer whose province is comedy, or that kind of novels which, like this I am writing, is of the comic class.", "이들은 일층 입석에 앉은 변호사와 그 서기들을, 위층 관람석에 앉은 시민과 그 도제들을 크게 기쁘게 하지만, 실제 삶에서는 켄타우로스나 키마이라, 혹은 순전히 지어낸 다른 어떤 피조물만큼이나 찾아볼 수 없는 것들이다. 그러나 독자에게 한 가지 비밀을 일러두자면, 상류 생활에 관한 이 지식은 실수를 막는 데는 매우 요긴하지만, 희극이나 혹은 지금 내가 쓰고 있는 이것처럼 희극 부류에 속하는 소설을 영역으로 삼은 작가에게는 그리 대단한 밑천은 못 된다."]], [["What Mr Pope says of women is very applicable to most in this station, who are, indeed, so entirely made up of form and affectation, that they have no character at all, at least none which appears.", "포프 씨가 여자들에 관해 한 말은 이 계급에 속한 대다수에게 그대로 들어맞는데, 그들은 실로 형식과 가식으로만 통째로 이루어져 있어 도무지 성격이라는 것이 없으며, 적어도 겉으로 드러나는 성격은 없다."], ["I will venture to say the highest life is much the dullest, and affords very little humour or entertainment.", "감히 말하건대 최상류 생활이야말로 단연 가장 따분하고, 해학이나 즐길 거리를 거의 내놓지 못한다."], ["The various callings in lower spheres produce the great variety of humorous characters; whereas here, except among the few who are engaged in the pursuit of ambition, and the fewer still who have a relish for pleasure, all is vanity and servile imitation.", "더 낮은 영역의 갖가지 직업은 해학적인 인물의 큰 다양성을 빚어내지만, 이곳에서는 야심을 좇는 데 매달린 소수와 쾌락을 즐길 줄 아는 더더욱 적은 소수를 빼고 나면, 모든 것이 허영과 비굴한 모방뿐이다."], ["Dressing and cards, eating and drinking, bowing and courtesying, make up the business of their lives.", "차려입기와 카드놀이, 먹고 마시기, 절하고 인사하기가 그들 삶의 일과를 이룬다."]], [["Some there are, however, of this rank upon whom passion exercises its tyranny, and hurries them far beyond the bounds which decorum prescribes; of these the ladies are as much distinguished by their noble intrepidity, and a certain superior contempt of reputation, from the frail ones of meaner degree, as a virtuous woman of quality is by the elegance and delicacy of her sentiments from the honest wife of a yeoman and shopkeeper.", "그러나 이 계급에도 더러는 정념이 폭정을 휘둘러, 예법이 정해 놓은 한계를 한참 넘어 그들을 내모는 자들이 있다. 그런 부류의 부인들은 그 당당한 대담함과, 평판에 대한 어떤 우월한 경멸로 인해 더 낮은 신분의 연약한 여자들과 뚜렷이 구별되는데, 이는 마치 신분 있는 정숙한 여인이 자영농이나 가게 주인의 정직한 아내와 그 정서의 우아함과 섬세함으로 구별되는 것과 같다."], ["Lady Bellaston was of this intrepid character; but let not my country readers conclude from her, that this is the general conduct of women of fashion, or that we mean to represent them as such.", "벨라스턴 부인이 바로 이런 대담한 성격이었다. 그러나 시골의 독자들은 그녀를 보고 이것이 멋쟁이 여자들의 일반적인 행실이라거나, 우리가 그들을 그렇게 그리려 한다고 결론짓지 않기를 바란다."], ["They might as well suppose that every clergyman was represented by Thwackum, or every soldier by ensign Northerton.", "그것은 마치 모든 성직자가 스왁컴으로 그려지고, 모든 군인이 노더턴 소위로 그려진다고 짐작하는 것이나 매한가지일 터이다."]], [["There is not, indeed, a greater error than that which universally prevails among the vulgar, who, borrowing their opinion from some ignorant satirists, have affixed the character of lewdness to these times.", "실로 어떤 무지한 풍자가들에게서 의견을 빌려 와 이 시대에 음탕함의 성격을 갖다 붙이는, 속된 무리 사이에 널리 퍼진 저 통념보다 더 큰 오류는 없다."], ["On the contrary, I am convinced there never was less of love intrigue carried on among persons of condition than now.", "오히려 나는 신분 있는 사람들 사이에서 지금만큼 연애 음모가 적게 벌어진 적은 없었다고 확신한다."], ["Our present women have been taught by their mothers to fix their thoughts only on ambition and vanity, and to despise the pleasures of love as unworthy their regard; and being afterwards, by the care of such mothers, married without having husbands, they seem pretty well confirmed in the justness of those sentiments; whence they content themselves, for the dull remainder of life, with the pursuit of more innocent, but I am afraid more childish amusements, the bare mention of which would ill suit with the dignity of this history.", "오늘날의 여자들은 어머니에게서 오직 야심과 허영에만 마음을 두고 사랑의 즐거움은 거들떠볼 가치도 없는 것으로 멸시하도록 배웠으며, 그 후 그런 어머니들의 보살핌으로 남편이랄 것도 없이 결혼한 까닭에, 그러한 정서가 옳다는 확신을 꽤 단단히 굳힌 듯하다. 그리하여 그들은 따분하게 남은 평생을 좀 더 순진하지만 유감스럽게도 좀 더 유치한 오락을 좇는 일로 만족하니, 그것을 입에 올리는 것조차 이 이야기의 품위에 어울리지 않을 정도이다."], ["In my humble opinion, the true characteristic of the present beau monde is rather folly than vice, and the only epithet which it deserves is that of frivolous.", "변변찮은 내 소견으로는, 오늘날 상류 사교계의 참된 특징은 악덕이라기보다 어리석음이며, 그것이 받아 마땅한 유일한 수식어는 경박함이라는 말뿐이다."]], [["Containing letters and other matters which attend amours.", "연애에 따라붙기 마련인 편지와 그 밖의 일들에 관하여."]], [["Jones had not been long at home before he received the following letter:--", "존스가 집에 돌아온 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 그는 다음과 같은 편지 한 통을 받았다."]], [["“I was never more surprized than when I found you was gone. When you left the room I little imagined you intended to have left the house without seeing me again. Your behaviour is all of a piece, and convinces me how much I ought to despise a heart which can doat upon an idiot; though I know not whether I should not admire her cunning more than her simplicity: wonderful both! For though she understood not a word of what passed between us, yet she had the skill, the assurance, the----what shall I call it? to deny to my face that she knows you, or ever saw you before.----Was this a scheme laid between you, and have you been base enough to betray me?----O how I despise her, you, and all the world, but chiefly myself! for----I dare not write what I should afterwards run mad to read; but remember, I can detest as violently as I have loved.”", "“당신이 가버렸다는 걸 알았을 때만큼 놀란 적은 없었어요. 방을 나설 때, 나를 다시 보지도 않고 그 집을 떠나려 했으리라고는 조금도 짐작 못 했지요. 당신의 행동은 처음부터 끝까지 한결같아서, 백치 같은 여자에게 빠질 수 있는 마음을 내가 얼마나 경멸해야 마땅한지 새삼 일깨워 주는군요. 하기야 그 여자의 단순함보다는 차라리 그 교활함에 감탄해야 할지도 모르겠어요. 둘 다 놀랍긴 매한가지니까요! 우리 사이에 오간 말은 한마디도 못 알아들으면서도, 내 면전에서 당신을 안다는 것도, 전에 본 적이 있다는 것도 딱 잡아떼는 그 솜씨며 배짱이며, 그것을—뭐라고 불러야 할까요? 이건 둘이서 짜고 한 일인가요? 당신은 그토록 비열하게 나를 배신한 건가요? 오, 그 여자도, 당신도, 온 세상도 다 경멸스러워요. 무엇보다 내 자신이요! 왜냐하면—나중에 읽으면 미쳐버릴 말은 차마 쓰지 못하겠네요. 하지만 기억해 두세요. 나는 사랑했던 만큼이나 격렬하게 증오할 수도 있다는 걸요.”"]], [["Jones had but little time given him to reflect on this letter, before a second was brought him from the same hand; and this, likewise, we shall set down in the precise words.", "존스가 이 편지를 곱씹어볼 겨를도 거의 없이, 같은 손에서 온 두 번째 편지가 그에게 전해졌다. 이것 역시 우리는 그 글자 그대로 옮겨 적기로 한다."]], [["“When you consider the hurry of spirits in which I must have writ, you cannot be surprized at any expressions in my former note.--Yet, perhaps, on reflection, they were rather too warm.", "“내가 어떤 마음의 소용돌이 속에서 그 글을 썼을지 헤아려 본다면, 앞선 쪽지의 어떤 표현에도 당신은 놀라지 않을 거예요. 그래도 다시 생각해 보니, 좀 지나치게 격했던 듯싶네요."], ["At least I would, if possible, think all owing to the odious playhouse, and to the impertinence of a fool, which detained me beyond my appointment.----How easy is it to think well of those we love!----Perhaps you desire I should think so.", "적어도 나는, 가능하다면 모든 걸 그 끔찍한 극장 탓으로, 그리고 약속 시간을 넘기도록 나를 붙잡아 둔 어떤 어리석은 자의 뻔뻔함 탓으로 돌리고 싶어요. 사랑하는 사람에 대해 좋게 생각하기란 얼마나 쉬운지요!"], ["I have resolved to see you to-night; so come to me immediately.", "어쩌면 당신은 내가 그렇게 생각하길 바라겠지요. 오늘 밤 당신을 보기로 마음먹었으니, 곧장 내게로 와요."]], [["“P.S.--I have ordered to be at home to none but yourself.", "추신. 당신 말고는 누구도 들이지 말라고 일러 두었어요."]], [["“P.S.--Mr Jones will imagine I shall assist him in his defence; for I believe he cannot desire to impose on me more than I desire to impose on myself.", "추신. 존스 씨는 내가 그의 변명을 거들어 주리라 짐작하겠지요. 내가 내 자신을 속이고 싶어 하는 만큼이나, 그가 나를 속이고 싶어 할 리는 없을 테니까요."]], [["“P.S.--Come immediately.”", "추신. 어서 와요.”"]], [["To the men of intrigue I refer the determination, whether the angry or the tender letter gave the greatest uneasiness to Jones. Certain it is, he had no violent inclination to pay any more visits that evening, unless to one single person. However, he thought his honour engaged, and had not this been motive sufficient, he would not have ventured to blow the temper of Lady Bellaston into that flame of which he had reason to think it susceptible, and of which he feared the consequence might be a discovery to Sophia, which he dreaded. After some discontented walks therefore about the room, he was preparing to depart, when the lady kindly prevented him, not by another letter, but by her own presence. She entered the room very disordered in her dress, and very discomposed in her looks, and threw herself into a chair, where, having recovered her breath, she said--“You see, sir, when women have gone one length too far, they will stop at none. If any person would have sworn this to me a week ago, I would not have believed it of myself.” “I hope, madam,” said Jones, “my charming Lady Bellaston will be as difficult to believe anything against one who is so sensible of the many obligations she hath conferred upon him.” “Indeed!” says she, “sensible of obligations! Did I expect to hear such cold language from Mr Jones?” “Pardon me, my dear angel,” said he, “if, after the letters I have received, the terrors of your anger, though I know not how I have deserved it.”--“And have I then,” says she, with a smile, “so angry a countenance?--Have I really brought a chiding face with me?”--“If there be honour in man,” said he, “I have done nothing to merit your anger.--You remember the appointment you sent me; I went in pursuance.”--“I beseech you,” cried she, “do not run through the odious recital.--Answer me but one question, and I shall be easy. Have you not betrayed my honour to her?”--Jones fell upon his knees, and began to utter the most violent protestations, when Partridge came dancing and capering into the room, like one drunk with joy, crying out, “She's found! she's found!--Here, sir, here, she's here--Mrs Honour is upon the stairs.” “Stop her a moment,” cries Jones--“Here, madam, step behind the bed, I have no other room nor closet, nor place on earth to hide you in; sure never was so damned an accident.”--“D--n'd indeed!” said the lady, as she went to her place of concealment; and presently afterwards in came Mrs Honour. “Hey-day!” says she, “Mr Jones, what's the matter?--That impudent rascal your servant would scarce let me come upstairs. I hope he hath not the same reason to keep me from you as he had at Upton.--I suppose you hardly expected to see me; but you have certainly bewitched my lady. Poor dear young lady! To be sure, I loves her as tenderly as if she was my own sister. Lord have mercy upon you, if you don't make her a good husband! and to be sure, if you do not, nothing can be bad enough for you.” Jones begged her only to whisper, for that there was a lady dying in the next room. “A lady!” cries she; “ay, I suppose one of your ladies.--O Mr Jones, there are too many of them in the world; I believe we are got into the house of one, for my Lady Bellaston I darst to say is no better than she should be.”--“Hush! hush!” cries Jones, “every word is overheard in the next room.” “I don't care a farthing,” cries Honour, “I speaks no scandal of any one; but to be sure the servants make no scruple of saying as how her ladyship meets men at another place--where the house goes under the name of a poor gentlewoman; but her ladyship pays the rent, and many's the good thing besides, they say, she hath of her.”--Here Jones, after expressing the utmost uneasiness, offered to stop her mouth:--“Hey-day! why sure, Mr Jones, you will let me speak; I speaks no scandal, for I only says what I heard from others--and thinks I to myself, much good may it do the gentlewoman with her riches, if she comes by it in such a wicked manner. To be sure it is better to be poor and honest.” “The servants are villains,” cries Jones, “and abuse their lady unjustly.”--“Ay, to be sure, servants are always villains, and so my lady says, and won't hear a word of it.”--“No, I am convinced,” says Jones, “my Sophia is above listening to such base scandal.” “Nay, I believe it is no scandal, neither,” cries Honour, “for why should she meet men at another house?--It can never be for any good: for if she had a lawful design of being courted, as to be sure any lady may lawfully give her company to men upon that account: why, where can be the sense?”--“I protest,” cries Jones, “I can't hear all this of a lady of such honour, and a relation of Sophia; besides, you will distract the poor lady in the next room.--Let me entreat you to walk with me down stairs.”--“Nay, sir, if you won't let me speak, I have done.--Here, sir, is a letter from my young lady--what would some men give to have this? But, Mr Jones, I think you are not over and above generous, and yet I have heard some servants say----but I am sure you will do me the justice to own I never saw the colour of your money.” Here Jones hastily took the letter, and presently after slipped five pieces into her hand. He then returned a thousand thanks to his dear Sophia in a whisper, and begged her to leave him to read her letter: she presently departed, not without expressing much grateful sense of his generosity.", "성난 편지와 다정한 편지 가운데 어느 쪽이 존스에게 더 큰 불편을 안겼는지는, 그 판단을 밀회에 능한 남자들에게 맡기겠다. 분명한 것은, 그가 그날 저녁 더 이상의 방문을 하고픈 마음이 조금도 없었다는 사실이다. 단 한 사람만은 예외였지만. 그렇지만 그는 자신의 명예가 걸려 있다고 여겼고, 설령 이것만으로는 충분한 동기가 못 되었더라도, 벨라스턴 부인의 성미를 자칫 불붙기 쉽다고 짐작되는 그 불길로 부채질하는 모험은 감히 하지 못했을 것이다. 그 결과가 소피아에게 들통나는 일로 이어질까 두려웠기 때문이다. 그래서 방 안을 못마땅한 듯 몇 차례 서성인 끝에 막 떠나려던 참에, 부인이 친절하게도 그를 가로막았다. 또 한 통의 편지가 아니라, 몸소 나타나서 말이다. 그녀는 옷매무새가 몹시 흐트러지고 낯빛도 매우 어지러운 채로 방에 들어와 의자에 몸을 던졌다. 그러고는 숨을 고른 뒤 입을 열었다. “보세요, 선생님. 여자란 한번 도를 넘어 멀리 가버리면, 어디서도 멈추지 못하는 법이지요. 일주일 전에 누군가가 이런 짓을 두고 내게 맹세했더라도, 나 자신이 그러리라고는 믿지 못했을 거예요.” “바라건대, 부인,” 존스가 말했다. “저의 매혹적인 벨라스턴 부인께서는, 자신이 베푼 그 많은 은혜를 이토록 잘 헤아리는 사람을 헐뜯는 어떤 말도 좀처럼 믿지 않으시겠지요.” “세상에!” 그녀가 말했다. “은혜를 헤아린다고요! 내가 존스 씨에게서 이런 싸늘한 말을 들으리라 기대했을까요?” “용서하세요, 나의 사랑스러운 천사여.” 그가 말했다. “받은 편지들 뒤로, 당신의 분노가 주는 두려움이라니요. 제가 어찌하여 그것을 받을 짓을 했는지는 모르겠습니다만.” “그러면 내가,” 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다. “그렇게나 성난 얼굴을 하고 있나요? 내가 정말로 꾸짖는 표정을 하고 왔던가요?” “남자에게 명예라는 것이 있다면,” 그가 말했다. “저는 당신의 분노를 살 만한 짓은 아무것도 하지 않았습니다. 당신이 보내신 약속을 기억하시지요. 저는 그에 따라 갔던 것입니다.” “제발,” 그녀가 외쳤다. “그 끔찍한 이야기를 처음부터 늘어놓지는 마세요. 한 가지 물음에만 답해 주면, 나는 마음을 놓겠어요. 당신은 그 여자에게 내 명예를 팔아넘기지 않았지요?” 존스가 무릎을 꿇고 더없이 격렬한 맹세를 쏟아내려는 순간, 파트리지가 기쁨에 취한 사람처럼 깡충거리고 까불며 방으로 뛰어들어 외쳤다. “찾았습니다요! 찾았어요! 여기, 도련님, 여기, 그분이 와 있어요. 어너 부인이 계단에 올라와 있습죠.” “잠깐 그녀를 붙잡아 둬라.” 존스가 외쳤다. “여기, 부인, 침대 뒤로 들어가세요. 당신을 숨길 다른 방도, 벽장도, 이 세상 어디에도 마땅한 자리가 없으니. 이렇게 빌어먹을 사달이 또 있을까요.” “정말 빌어먹을 일이군요!” 부인은 숨을 곳으로 가면서 말했다. 그리고 곧이어 어너 부인이 들어왔다. “어머나!” 그녀가 말했다. “존스 씨, 무슨 일이래요? 저 뻔뻔한 도련님 하인 녀석이 좀처럼 위층으로 올려 보내 주질 않으려 하지 뭐예요. 어퍼튼에서처럼 나를 도련님께 못 가게 막을 까닭이 또 있는 건 아니겠죠. 저를 보리라곤 별로 기대 안 하셨겠지만요. 도련님이 우리 아씨한테 분명 무슨 마법을 거셨나 봐요. 가엾고 사랑스러운 우리 아씨! 정말이지 친동생처럼 애틋하게 아끼는 분이거든요. 아씨한테 좋은 남편이 되어 주지 않으신다면, 하느님께서 자비를 베푸시기를요! 정말이지, 그러지 않으신다면 도련님한텐 아무리 끔찍한 벌이 내려도 모자랄 거예요.” 존스는 옆방에 죽어가는 부인이 한 분 계시니 제발 목소리를 낮춰 달라고 빌었다. “부인이라고요!” 그녀가 외쳤다. “옳거니, 도련님의 그 부인들 중 하나겠지요. 오, 존스 씨, 세상엔 그런 여자들이 너무 많다니까요. 우리가 그런 여자의 집에 들어온 게 아닌가 싶네요. 감히 말하건대 벨라스턴 부인이란 분도 행실이 그리 곱지는 않거든요.” “쉿! 쉿!” 존스가 외쳤다. “옆방에서 한마디도 빠짐없이 다 들린다.” “한 푼어치도 상관없어요.” 어너가 외쳤다. “저는 누구 험담도 안 해요. 하지만 정말이지 하인들은 거리낌도 없이들 말하던걸요. 그 마님이 딴 데서 남자들을 만난다고요. 어느 가난한 부인네 이름으로 되어 있는 집인데, 집세는 마님이 내고, 그 밖에도 그 부인네한테 좋은 것을 잔뜩 받는다고들 하더라고요.” 여기서 존스는 더없는 불편함을 드러내며 그녀의 입을 막으려 했다. “아니 이런! 아니 글쎄, 존스 씨, 말은 좀 하게 해 주세요. 저는 험담하는 게 아니에요. 그저 남들한테 들은 걸 옮길 뿐이라고요. 그러면서 속으로 생각하지요. 그 부인네가 그토록 사악한 방법으로 손에 넣은 재물이라면, 그게 그분한테 좋은 일이 될 게 뭐람, 하고요. 정말이지 가난해도 정직한 편이 낫지요.” “하인들은 악당이야.” 존스가 외쳤다. “자기네 마님을 부당하게 헐뜯는 게야.” “옳지요, 정말이지 하인이란 늘 악당이니까요. 우리 아씨도 그렇게 말씀하시면서 그런 소리는 한마디도 들으려 하지 않으셔요.” “그래, 나는 확신한다.” 존스가 말했다. “나의 소피아는 그런 비열한 험담에 귀 기울일 분이 아니지.” “아니 글쎄, 저도 그게 험담은 아니라고 봐요.” 어너가 외쳤다. “안 그러면 왜 딴 집에서 남자들을 만나겠어요? 절대 좋은 일일 리 없잖아요. 정식으로 구애를 받으려는 떳떳한 뜻이라면야—물론 어느 부인이든 그런 까닭으로 남자와 자리를 함께하는 거야 떳떳한 일이지만요—그렇다면 대체 무슨 까닭이겠어요?” “단언컨대,” 존스가 외쳤다. “그토록 명예로운 부인이자 소피아의 친척에 대해 이런 말을 다 듣고 있을 수는 없네. 게다가 자네는 옆방의 가엾은 부인을 미치게 만들 작정이군. 부디 나와 함께 아래층으로 내려가 주게.” “아니, 선생님, 말을 못 하게 하신다면 그만하지요. 여기, 우리 아씨가 보낸 편지가 있어요. 이걸 가지려고 어떤 남자들은 얼마를 내놓을까요? 그런데 존스 씨, 제가 보기에 도련님은 그다지 후한 편은 아니신 것 같네요. 게다가 어떤 하인들은 그러던데요—아무튼 분명 도련님은 제가 도련님 돈 빛깔이라곤 구경해 본 적도 없다는 걸 인정해 주시겠지요.” 여기서 존스는 황급히 편지를 받아 들고, 곧이어 그녀의 손에 다섯 닢을 슬쩍 쥐여 주었다. 그러고는 사랑하는 소피아에게 속삭이듯 천 번이고 감사를 보낸 뒤, 그 편지를 읽게 자기를 혼자 두고 가 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 그의 후한 인심에 깊은 고마움을 한껏 드러내며 곧 자리를 떴다."]], [["Lady Bellaston now came from behind the curtain.", "이제 벨라스턴 부인이 휘장 뒤에서 나왔다. 그녀의 격분을 내가 어찌 그려낼 수 있을까?"], ["How shall I describe her rage?", "처음에는 혀가 말을 이루지 못했다. 그러나 두 눈에서는 불줄기가 쏘아져 나왔으니, 실로 그럴 만도 했다."], ["Her tongue was at first incapable of utterance; but streams of fire darted from her eyes, and well indeed they might, for her heart was all in a flame.", "그녀의 가슴이 온통 불길에 휩싸여 있었으니 말이다. 그리고 마침내 목소리가 길을 찾자, 그녀는 어너나 자기 하인들을 향해 분노를 터뜨리는 대신, 가엾은 존스를 공격하기 시작했다."], ["And now as soon as her voice found way, instead of expressing any indignation against Honour or her own servants, she began to attack poor Jones.", "“보세요,” 그녀가 말했다."], ["“You see,”", "“내가 당신을 위해 무엇을 희생했는지를요. 내 평판도, 내 명예도—영영 사라져버렸어요!"], ["said she, “what I have sacrificed to you; my reputation, my honour--gone for ever!", "그런데 내가 받은 보답이 뭐죠? 시골 계집애 따위, 백치 같은 여자 따위 때문에 외면당하고 멸시당하는 거잖아요.”"], ["And what return have I found?", "“제가 무슨 외면을, 혹은 무슨 멸시를,” 존스가 외쳤다."], ["Neglected, slighted for a country girl, for an idiot.”--“What neglect, madam, or what slight,”", "“저질렀단 말입니까?” “존스 씨,”"], ["cries Jones, “have I been guilty of?”--“Mr Jones,”", "그녀가 말했다. “시치미를 떼봐야 소용없어요."], ["said she, “it is in vain to dissemble; if you will make me easy, you must entirely give her up; and as a proof of your intention, show me the letter.”--“What letter, madam?”", "나를 안심시키고 싶다면, 그 여자를 완전히 단념해야 해요. 그리고 당신의 진심을 보이는 증거로, 그 편지를 내게 보여 주세요.”"], ["said Jones.", "“무슨 편지 말입니까, 부인?” 존스가 말했다."], ["“Nay, surely,”", "“아니 정말,” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “you cannot have the confidence to deny your having received a letter by the hands of that trollop.”--“And can your ladyship,”", "“설마 그 갈보 같은 계집 손에서 편지를 받은 걸 부인할 만큼 뻔뻔하지는 않겠지요.” “그러면 당신께서는,”"], ["cries he, “ask of me what I must part with my honour before I grant?", "그가 외쳤다. “제가 명예를 내던지지 않고서는 허락할 수 없는 것을 제게 요구하시는 겁니까?"], ["Have I acted in such a manner by your ladyship?", "제가 당신께 그런 식으로 행동한 적이 있던가요? 제가 이 가엾고 죄 없는 처녀를 당신께 팔아넘기는 짓을 할 수 있다면, 제가 당신께도 똑같이 굴지 않으리라는 보장을 무엇으로 받으시겠습니까?"], ["Could I be guilty of betraying this poor innocent girl to you, what security could you have that I should not act the same part by yourself?", "잠깐만 생각해 보시면, 한 부인의 비밀이 안전하게 지켜지지 못하는 남자야말로 가장 경멸스러운 자임을 분명 깨달으실 겁니다.” “좋아요,”"], ["A moment's reflection will, I am sure, convince you that a man with whom the secrets of a lady are not safe must be the most contemptible of wretches.”--“Very well,”", "그녀가 말했다. “당신이 스스로의 눈에 그런 경멸스러운 자가 되라고 굳이 우기지는 않겠어요."], ["said she--“I need not insist on your becoming this contemptible wretch in your own opinion; for the inside of the letter could inform me of nothing more than I know already.", "그 편지의 속내라야 내가 이미 아는 것 이상은 알려 주지 못할 테니까요. 당신이 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지 다 보여요.”"], ["I see the footing you are upon.”--Here ensued a long conversation, which the reader, who is not too curious, will thank me for not inserting at length.", "여기서 긴 대화가 이어졌으나, 너무 꼬치꼬치 캐묻지 않는 독자라면 그것을 길게 늘어놓지 않은 데 대해 내게 고마워할 것이다. 그러니 이쯤만 일러두는 것으로 충분하겠다."], ["It shall suffice, therefore, to inform him, that Lady Bellaston grew more and more pacified, and at length believed, or affected to believe, his protestations, that his meeting with Sophia that evening was merely accidental, and every other matter which the reader already knows, and which, as Jones set before her in the strongest light, it is plain that she had in reality no reason to be angry with him.", "벨라스턴 부인은 점점 누그러져, 마침내 그날 저녁 소피아와 마주친 것은 그저 우연일 뿐이라는 그의 항변과, 독자가 이미 아는 그 밖의 모든 사정을 믿게 되었거나, 적어도 믿는 척했다. 존스가 그 일들을 더없이 또렷하게 늘어놓고 보니, 그녀로서는 실상 그에게 화낼 까닭이 전혀 없었음이 분명해진 것이다."]], [["She was not, however, in her heart perfectly satisfied with his refusal to show her the letter; so deaf are we to the clearest reason, when it argues against our prevailing passions. She was, indeed, well convinced that Sophia possessed the first place in Jones's affections; and yet, haughty and amorous as this lady was, she submitted at last to bear the second place; or, to express it more properly in a legal phrase, was contented with the possession of that of which another woman had the reversion.", "그렇지만 그녀의 마음속은, 편지를 보여 주기를 거절한 그의 태도에 온전히 만족하지는 못했다. 우리는 가장 명백한 이치라 해도 그것이 우리를 사로잡은 정념을 거스를 때면 그토록 귀를 닫아버리는 법이다. 그녀는 실로 소피아가 존스의 애정에서 첫 자리를 차지하고 있음을 잘 알고 있었다. 그런데도 거만하고 정욕에 들뜬 이 부인은, 끝내 두 번째 자리를 감수하기로 굽혔다. 좀 더 적절하게 법률 용어로 표현하자면, 다른 여자가 그 복귀권을 쥐고 있는 것을, 자신은 그 점유로 만족했던 것이다."]], [["It was at length agreed that Jones should for the future visit at the house: for that Sophia, her maid, and all the servants, would place these visits to the account of Sophia; and that she herself would be considered as the person imposed upon.", "마침내 존스가 앞으로는 그 집을 드나들기로 합의가 이루어졌다. 소피아도, 그녀의 하녀도, 모든 하인들도 이 방문을 소피아를 보러 온 것으로 여길 테고, 부인 자신은 속아 넘어가는 사람으로 비칠 터였기 때문이다."]], [["This scheme was contrived by the lady, and highly relished by Jones, who was indeed glad to have a prospect of seeing his Sophia at any rate; and the lady herself was not a little pleased with the imposition on Sophia, which Jones, she thought, could not possibly discover to her for his own sake.", "이 계략은 부인이 꾸민 것이었고, 존스도 크게 흡족해했다. 어떻게든 자신의 소피아를 볼 수 있는 가망이 생긴 것이 그로서는 진실로 반가웠던 까닭이다. 부인 또한 소피아를 속이는 일이 적잖이 즐거웠다. 존스가 자기 자신을 위해서라도 그 속임수를 소피아에게 발설할 리는 결코 없으리라 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["The next day was appointed for the first visit, and then, after proper ceremonials, the Lady Bellaston returned home.", "이튿날이 첫 방문 날로 정해졌고, 그런 다음 적절한 인사를 나눈 뒤 벨라스턴 부인은 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Containing various matters.", "여러 가지 일들을 담음."]], [["Jones was no sooner alone than he eagerly broke open his letter, and read as follows:--", "존스는 혼자 남게 되자마자 다급히 편지를 뜯어 다음과 같은 내용을 읽었다."]], [["“Sir, it is impossible to express what I have suffered since you left this house; and as I have reason to think you intend coming here again, I have sent Honour, though so late at night, as she tells me she knows your lodgings, to prevent you. I charge you, by all the regard you have for me, not to think of visiting here; for it will certainly be discovered; nay, I almost doubt, from some things which have dropt from her ladyship, that she is not already without some suspicion. Something favourable perhaps may happen; we must wait with patience; but I once more entreat you, if you have any concern for my ease, do not think of returning hither.”", "\"선생님, 당신이 이 집을 떠난 뒤로 제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지는 말로 다 표현할 수가 없습니다. 당신이 다시 이곳에 오실 생각이라고 짐작할 만한 까닭이 있기에, 밤이 이렇게 늦었음에도 어너를 보냅니다. 그 아이가 당신의 거처를 안다고 하니, 당신을 막으려는 것입니다. 저를 아끼는 그 마음을 다해 당부드리오니, 부디 이곳을 찾아오실 생각은 하지 말아 주세요. 그러면 틀림없이 발각될 것입니다. 아니, 마님께서 흘리신 몇 마디로 미루어 보면, 그분이 벌써 어느 정도 의심을 품고 계신 것은 아닐까 싶기까지 합니다. 어쩌면 형편이 풀릴 일이 생길지도 모릅니다. 우리는 참고 기다려야 합니다. 그러나 다시 한 번 간청하오니, 제 마음의 평안을 조금이라도 헤아리신다면, 이곳으로 돌아오실 생각은 하지 말아 주세요.\""]], [["This letter administered the same kind of consolation to poor Jones, which Job formerly received from his friends. Besides disappointing all the hopes which he promised to himself from seeing Sophia, he was reduced to an unhappy dilemma, with regard to Lady Bellaston; for there are some certain engagements, which, as he well knew, do very difficultly admit of any excuse for the failure; and to go, after the strict prohibition from Sophia, he was not to be forced by any human power. At length, after much deliberation, which during that night supplied the place of sleep, he determined to feign himself sick: for this suggested itself as the only means of failing the appointed visit, without incensing Lady Bellaston, which he had more than one reason of desiring to avoid.", "이 편지는 가엾은 존스에게, 옛날 욥이 친구들에게서 받았던 것과 똑같은 종류의 위로를 안겨 주었다. 소피아를 만나리라 스스로 약속했던 모든 희망이 무너진 것에 더하여, 그는 레이디 벨라스턴 문제에 관해서도 난처한 진퇴양난에 빠지고 말았다. 그가 잘 알고 있다시피, 세상에는 어겼을 때 어떤 변명도 좀처럼 통하지 않는 약속이라는 것이 있기 때문이다. 그렇다고 소피아의 그 단호한 금지가 있은 뒤에 그곳으로 가는 일은, 어떤 인간의 힘으로도 그를 떠밀 수 없는 일이었다. 마침내 그는, 그날 밤 잠을 대신했던 오랜 궁리 끝에, 자신이 병이 난 척하기로 마음을 정했다. 이것이야말로 레이디 벨라스턴의 노여움을 사지 않으면서 약속된 방문을 어길 수 있는 유일한 방편으로 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그가 그녀의 노여움을 피하고자 하는 데에는 한 가지 이상의 까닭이 있었다."]], [["The first thing, however, which he did in the morning, was, to write an answer to Sophia, which he inclosed in one to Honour.", "그러나 아침에 그가 가장 먼저 한 일은 소피아에게 보낼 답장을 쓰는 것이었고, 그것을 어너에게 보내는 편지 안에 넣었다."], ["He then despatched another to Lady Bellaston, containing the above-mentioned excuse; and to this he soon received the following answer:--", "그런 다음 그는 앞서 말한 변명을 담은 또 다른 편지를 레이디 벨라스턴에게 부쳤다. 그리고 이에 대해 곧 다음과 같은 답을 받았다."]], [["“I am vexed that I cannot see you here this afternoon, but more concerned for the occasion; take great care of yourself, and have the best advice, and I hope there will be no danger.--I am so tormented all this morning with fools, that I have scarce a moment's time to write to you. Adieu.", "\"오늘 오후에 여기서 당신을 볼 수 없다니 속상하지만, 그 사정이 더 마음에 걸립니다. 부디 몸을 잘 보살피시고 가장 좋은 의사의 진찰을 받으세요. 위험한 일은 없으리라 믿습니다. 저는 오늘 아침 내내 어리석은 자들에게 시달리느라 당신에게 편지 쓸 짬조차 거의 없습니다. 안녕히."]], [["“P.S.--I will endeavour to call on you this evening, at nine.--Be sure to be alone.”", "추신. 오늘 저녁 아홉 시에 당신에게 들르도록 애써 보겠습니다. 반드시 혼자 계세요.\""]], [["Mr Jones now received a visit from Mrs Miller, who, after some formal introduction, began the following speech:--“I am very sorry, sir, to wait upon you on such an occasion; but I hope you will consider the ill consequence which it must be to the reputation of my poor girls, if my house should once be talked of as a house of ill-fame. I hope you won't think me, therefore, guilty of impertinence, if I beg you not to bring any more ladies in at that time of night. The clock had struck two before one of them went away.”--“I do assure you, madam,” said Jones, “the lady who was here last night, and who staid the latest (for the other only brought me a letter), is a woman of very great fashion, and my near relation.”--“I don't know what fashion she is of,” answered Mrs Miller; “but I am sure no woman of virtue, unless a very near relation indeed, would visit a young gentleman at ten at night, and stay four hours in his room with him alone; besides, sir, the behaviour of her chairmen shows what she was; for they did nothing but make jests all the evening in the entry, and asked Mr Partridge, in the hearing of my own maid, if madam intended to stay with his master all night; with a great deal of stuff not proper to be repeated. I have really a great respect for you, Mr Jones, upon your own account; nay, I have a very high obligation to you for your generosity to my cousin. Indeed, I did not know how very good you had been till lately. Little did I imagine to what dreadful courses the poor man's distress had driven him. Little did I think, when you gave me the ten guineas, that you had given them to a highwayman! O heavens! what goodness have you shown! How have you preserved this family!--The character which Mr Allworthy hath formerly given me of you was, I find, strictly true.--And indeed, if I had no obligation to you, my obligations to him are such, that, on his account, I should shew you the utmost respect in my power.--Nay, believe me, dear Mr Jones, if my daughters' and my own reputation were out of the case, I should, for your own sake, be sorry that so pretty a young gentleman should converse with these women; but if you are resolved to do it, I must beg you to take another lodging; for I do not myself like to have such things carried on under my roof; but more especially upon the account of my girls, who have little, heaven knows, besides their characters, to recommend them.” Jones started and changed colour at the name of Allworthy. “Indeed, Mrs Miller,” answered he, a little warmly, “I do not take this at all kind. I will never bring any slander on your house; but I must insist on seeing what company I please in my own room; and if that gives you any offence, I shall, as soon as I am able, look for another lodging.”--“I am sorry we must part then, sir,” said she; “but I am convinced Mr Allworthy himself would never come within my doors, if he had the least suspicion of my keeping an ill house.”--“Very well, madam,” said Jones.--“I hope, sir,” said she, “you are not angry; for I would not for the world offend any of Mr Allworthy's family. I have not slept a wink all night about this matter.”--“I am sorry I have disturbed your rest, madam,” said Jones, “but I beg you will send Partridge up to me immediately;” which she promised to do, and then with a very low courtesy retired.", "이제 존스는 밀러 부인의 방문을 받았는데, 그녀는 격식 차린 인사를 몇 마디 건넨 뒤 다음과 같은 말을 시작했다. \"선생님, 이런 일로 찾아뵙게 되어 참으로 죄송합니다. 그렇지만 제 집이 한번이라도 평판 나쁜 집으로 입에 오르내리게 된다면, 제 가엾은 딸들의 명예에 얼마나 못된 결과가 미칠지 헤아려 주시기 바랍니다. 그러니 한밤중 그런 시각에 더 이상 부인들을 데려오지 말아 달라고 청하더라도, 제가 주제넘다고 여기지 말아 주세요. 어젯밤 한 분이 떠난 것이 시계가 두 시를 친 뒤였습니다.\" \"장담하건대, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다. \"어젯밤 여기 와서 가장 늦게까지 머문 부인은—다른 한 사람은 제게 편지만 전했을 뿐입니다만—신분이 아주 높은 분이고, 저와 가까운 친척입니다.\" \"그분이 어떤 신분인지는 모릅니다.\" 밀러 부인이 대답했다. \"그렇지만 정말로 아주 가까운 친척이 아니고서야, 정숙한 여인이라면 밤 열 시에 젊은 신사를 찾아와 그 방에서 단둘이 네 시간이나 머무르지는 않을 겁니다. 게다가, 선생님, 그 부인의 가마꾼들이 보인 행실만 봐도 그분이 어떤 사람인지 알 만합니다. 그자들은 저녁 내내 현관에서 농지거리만 늘어놓더니, 제 하녀가 듣는 데서 파트리지 씨에게, 마님이 제 주인과 밤을 새울 작정이냐고 묻고는, 차마 옮길 수 없는 온갖 잡소리를 지껄였답니다. 저는 선생님을 정말로 존경합니다, 존스 씨, 선생님 그 자체로요. 아니, 제 사촌에게 베푸신 그 너그러움에 대해서는 더없이 큰 신세를 졌지요. 사실 저는 선생님이 그토록 선한 분인 줄을 최근에야 알았습니다. 그 가엾은 사람이 곤궁에 몰려 얼마나 끔찍한 길로 들어섰는지는 짐작도 못 했어요. 선생님이 제게 십 기니를 주셨을 때, 그것을 노상강도에게 건네주시는 거라고는 꿈에도 생각 못 했습니다! 오, 하느님! 선생님은 얼마나 큰 선을 베푸셨는지요! 이 집안을 어떻게 지켜 주셨는지요! 예전에 올워디 씨가 선생님에 대해 제게 들려주신 말씀이, 알고 보니 한 치도 틀림이 없었습니다. 그리고 정말이지, 설령 제가 선생님께 진 신세가 없다 하더라도, 그분께 진 신세가 워낙 크니, 그분을 보아서라도 제 힘닿는 한 최대의 존경을 선생님께 표해야 마땅하지요. 아니, 제 말을 믿어 주세요, 친애하는 존스 씨, 제 딸들과 제 자신의 평판이 걸린 문제가 아니라 하더라도, 선생님을 위해서라도 저는 이토록 잘생긴 젊은 신사가 그런 여자들과 어울리는 것이 안타까울 겁니다. 그러나 선생님이 기어이 그러시겠다면, 부디 다른 거처를 구해 달라고 청할 수밖에 없습니다. 저로서는 제 지붕 아래서 그런 일이 벌어지는 것을 좋아하지 않을뿐더러, 무엇보다 제 딸들 때문입니다. 하느님은 아시지만, 그 아이들에게는 명예 말고는 내세울 것이 거의 없으니까요.\" 존스는 올워디라는 이름에 흠칫 놀라며 안색이 변했다. \"정말이지, 밀러 부인,\" 그가 다소 격앙된 어조로 대답했다. \"이건 도무지 곱게 받아들일 수가 없군요. 저는 결코 부인의 집에 어떤 누명도 씌우지 않을 겁니다. 그렇지만 제 방에서 제가 원하는 손님을 만나는 것만큼은 양보할 수 없습니다. 그것이 부인의 비위를 거스른다면, 저는 형편이 되는 대로 다른 거처를 찾아보겠습니다.\" \"그러시다면 우리가 헤어져야 한다니 유감입니다, 선생님.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 올워디 씨께서도, 제가 평판 나쁜 집을 꾸린다는 의심을 조금이라도 품으셨다면 결코 제 집 문턱을 넘지 않으셨으리라 확신합니다.\" \"아주 좋습니다, 부인.\" 존스가 말했다. \"선생님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"부디 노여워하지는 마세요. 저는 세상 무엇과도 바꿔서 올워디 씨 댁의 어느 누구에게도 결코 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않으니까요. 저는 이 일로 밤새 한숨도 못 잤답니다.\" \"부인의 안식을 어지럽혀 드려 죄송합니다, 부인.\" 존스가 말했다. \"하지만 파트리지를 즉시 제게 올려 보내 주시기를 청합니다.\" 그녀는 그러겠노라 약속한 뒤, 깍듯이 절을 하고 물러갔다."]], [["As soon as Partridge arrived, Jones fell upon him in the most outrageous manner.", "파트리지가 올라오자마자 존스는 더없이 사납게 그를 몰아세웠다. \"도대체,\""], ["“How often,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “am I to suffer for your folly, or rather for my own in keeping you?", "\"내가 네 어리석음 때문에, 아니 그런 너를 데리고 있는 내 어리석음 때문에 몇 번이나 더 곤욕을 치러야 하느냐? 그 혓바닥이 기어이 나를 파멸시키기로 작정했느냐?\""], ["is that tongue of yours resolved upon my destruction?”", "\"제가 무엇을 했다고요, 나리?\" 겁에 질린 파트리지가 대답했다."], ["“What have I done, sir?”", "\"그 강도 이야기를 입에 올리라고, 또 네가 여기서 본 사람이 바로 그자라고 떠벌리라고 누가 너에게 권한을 주더냐?\" \"제가요, 나리?\""], ["answered affrighted Partridge.", "파트리지가 외쳤다. \"그걸 부인하는 거짓말까지 보태지는 마라.\""], ["“Who was it gave you authority to mention the story of the robbery, or that the man you saw here was the person?”", "존스가 말했다. \"제가 그런 이야기를 입에 올렸다 한들,\""], ["“I, sir?”", "파트리지가 대답했다."], ["cries Partridge.", "\"맹세코 나쁜 뜻은 없었습니다."], ["“Now don't be guilty of a falsehood in denying it,” said Jones.", "그분의 벗이며 친척 되는 분들에게가 아니었다면 입도 뻥긋하지 않았을 텐데, 그분들이라면 그 이야기를 더 멀리 옮기지는 않으리라 여겼던 겁니다.\""], ["“If I did mention such a matter,”", "\"하지만 그보다 훨씬 무거운 죄목이 너에게 있다.\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["answers Partridge, “I am sure I thought no harm; for I should not have opened my lips, if it had not been to his own friends and relations, who, I imagined, would have let it go no farther.”", "\"내가 그토록 거듭 조심하라 일렀거늘, 이 집에서 감히 올워디 씨의 이름을 어떻게 입에 올렸느냐?\" 파트리지는 온갖 맹세를 늘어놓으며 결코 그런 적이 없다고 잡아뗐다."], ["“But I have a much heavier charge against you,”", "\"그게 아니라면,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["cries Jones, “than this.", "\"밀러 부인이 그분과 나 사이에 어떤 연줄이 있다는 것을 어떻게 알았겠느냐?"], ["How durst you, after all the precautions I gave you, mention the name of Mr Allworthy in this house?”", "게다가 바로 방금 그녀가 나에게, 그분을 보아 나를 존경한다고 말하던데.\" \"오, 맙소사, 나리.\""], ["Partridge denied that he ever had, with many oaths.", "파트리지가 말했다. \"제발 끝까지 들어만 주십시오."], ["“How else,”", "정말이지, 이렇게 불운한 일은 없었습니다."], ["said Jones, “should Mrs Miller be acquainted that there was any connexion between him and me?", "끝까지 들어 보시면, 나리께서 저를 얼마나 억울하게 몰아세우셨는지 인정하실 겁니다. 어젯밤 어너 부인이 아래층으로 내려오다가 현관에서 저를 만나, 제 주인이 올워디 씨에게서 소식을 들은 게 언제냐고 물었습니다."], ["And it is but this moment she told me she respected me on his account.”", "그리고 분명 밀러 부인이 그 말을 고스란히 들은 겁니다. 어너 마님이 가자마자, 그녀가 저를 거실로 불러들이더군요."], ["“O Lord, sir,”", "'파트리지 씨,' 그녀가 말했습니다."], ["said Partridge, “I desire only to be heard out; and to be sure, never was anything so unfortunate: hear me but out, and you will own how wrongfully you have accused me.", "'그 부인이 말한 올워디 씨가 어느 분이지요? 서머싯셔의 그 대단한 올워디 씨 말인가요?'"], ["When Mrs Honour came downstairs last night she met me in the entry, and asked me when my master had heard from Mr Allworthy; and to be sure Mrs Miller heard the very words; and the moment Madam Honour was gone, she called me into the parlour to her.", "'맹세코, 부인,' 제가 말했습니다."], ["`Mr Partridge,'", "'저는 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다.' '설마,'"], ["says she, `what Mr Allworthy is it that the gentlewoman mentioned?", "그녀가 말했습니다. '당신 주인이 올워디 씨가 이야기하시던 그 존스 씨는 아니겠지요?'"], ["is it the great Mr Allworthy of Somersetshire?'", "'맹세코, 부인,' 제가 말했습니다."], ["`Upon my word, madam,'", "'저는 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다.' '그렇다면,'"], ["says I, `I know nothing of the matter.'", "그녀가 딸 낸시 쪽으로 돌아서며 말하더군요. '십 펜스를 걸어도 좋아, 바로 그 젊은 신사가 틀림없어."], ["`Sure,'", "영주님이 묘사하신 것과 한 치도 어김없이 들어맞잖니.'"], ["says she, `your master is not the Mr Jones I have heard Mr Allworthy talk of?'", "도대체 누가 그녀에게 일러바쳤는지는 하늘이 아실 일입니다. 그게 제 입에서 나온 것이라면 저는 두 다리로 걸어 다니는 더없이 지독한 악당일 겁니다."], ["`Upon my word, madam,'", "약속드리지만, 나리, 저는 비밀을 지키라는 부탁을 받으면 지킬 줄 압니다. 아니, 나리, 저는 그녀에게 올워디 씨에 관해 무언가를 일러 주기는커녕, 정반대로 말해 주었습니다."], ["says I, `I know nothing of the matter.' `Then,'", "그 순간에는 부인하지 않았지만, 흔히들 말하듯 두 번째 생각이 가장 낫다고 하니, 누군가가 그녀에게 알려 준 게 틀림없다는 데까지 생각이 미치자, 속으로 이 이야기에 끝을 내야겠다 마음먹었지요."], ["says she, turning to her daughter Nancy, says she, `as sure as tenpence this is the very young gentleman, and he agrees exactly with the squire's description.'", "그래서 얼마 뒤에 거실로 다시 들어가 이렇게 말했습니다. 맹세코, 제가 말하기를, 이 신사가 존스 씨라고, 그러니까 이 존스 씨가 그 존스 씨라고 누가 부인께 일렀든 간에, 그건 새빨간 거짓말이라고 했습니다."], ["The Lord above knows who it was told her: for I am the arrantest villain that ever walked upon two legs if ever it came out of my mouth.", "그리고 제가 청하기를, 부디 다시는 그런 이야기를 입에 올리지 말아 달라고 했습니다. 제 주인이, 제가 그렇게 일러바친 줄로 알 게 분명하기 때문이라고요."], ["I promise you, sir, I can keep a secret when I am desired.", "그리고 이 집안의 누구든 제가 그런 말을 한 마디라도 했다고 말할 수 있으면 어디 해 보라고 했습니다. 정말이지, 나리, 참으로 신기한 일이라, 저는 그때부터 줄곧 도대체 그녀가 어떻게 알게 되었는지 속으로 곰곰이 생각해 왔습니다."], ["Nay, sir, so far was I from telling her anything about Mr Allworthy, that I told her the very direct contrary; for, though I did not contradict it at that moment, yet, as second thoughts, they say, are best, so when I came to consider that somebody must have informed her, thinks I to myself, I will put an end to the story; and so I went back again into the parlour some time afterwards, and says I, upon my word, says I, whoever, says I, told you that this gentleman was Mr Jones; that is, says I, that this Mr Jones was that Mr Jones, told you a confounded lie: and I beg, says I, you will never mention any such matter, says I; for my master, says I, will think I must have told you so; and I defy anybody in the house ever to say I mentioned any such word.", "다만 일전에 여기 문간에서 동냥하는 노파를 하나 보았는데, 워릭셔에서 우리에게 그 모든 화를 불러왔던 그 노파와 어찌나 닮았던지요. 정말이지, 노파를 그냥 지나치면서 무언가를 쥐여 주지 않는 것은 결코 좋은 일이 아닙니다."], ["To be certain, sir, it is a wonderful thing, and I have been thinking with myself ever since, how it was she came to know it; not but I saw an old woman here t'other day a begging at the door, who looked as like her we saw in Warwickshire, that caused all that mischief to us.", "더구나 그 노파가 당신을 빤히 쳐다본다면 말이지요. 온 세상이 뭐라 해도, 저는 그들에게 화를 부르는 대단한 힘이 있다는 믿음을 절대 꺾지 않을 겁니다."], ["To be sure it is never good to pass by an old woman without giving her something, especially if she looks at you; for all the world shall never persuade me but that they have a great power to do mischief, and to be sure I shall never see an old woman again, but I shall think to myself, Infandum, regina, jubes renovare dolorem.”", "그리고 분명 저는 앞으로 노파를 볼 때마다 속으로 이렇게 되뇌겠지요. 인판둠, 레기나, 유베스 레노바레 돌로렘.\"*\n* 인판둠, 레기나, 유베스 레노바레 돌로렘: 베르길리우스의 〈아이네이스〉에 나오는 구절로, \"여왕이시여, 차마 말 못 할 슬픔을 다시 들추라 명하시는군요\"라는 뜻이다."]], [["The simplicity of Partridge set Jones a laughing, and put a final end to his anger, which had indeed seldom any long duration in his mind; and, instead of commenting on his defence, he told him he intended presently to leave those lodgings, and ordered him to go and endeavour to get him others.", "파트리지의 그 순박함에 존스는 그만 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 그의 노여움도 끝내 사그라들고 말았다. 실은 그의 마음속에서 노여움이 오래 머무는 일은 좀처럼 없었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 파트리지의 변명을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하는 대신, 곧 이 거처를 떠날 작정이라 일러 주고는, 나가서 다른 거처를 구해 보라고 그에게 명했다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["Which we hope will be very attentively perused by young people of both sexes.", "파트리지가 존스 씨를 떠나기가 무섭게, 그동안 그와 무척 가까운 사이가 된 나이팅게일 씨가 그에게 다가왔다."], ["Partridge had no sooner left Mr Jones than Mr Nightingale, with whom he had now contracted a great intimacy, came to him, and, after a short salutation, said, “So, Tom, I hear you had company very late last night.", "짧게 인사를 나눈 뒤 그가 말했다. \"그래, 톰, 어젯밤엔 꽤 늦게까지 손님이 있었다지."], ["Upon my soul you are a happy fellow, who have not been in town above a fortnight, and can keep chairs waiting at your door till two in the morning.”", "정말이지 자넨 운 좋은 친구야. 이 도시에 온 지 보름도 안 됐는데, 새벽 두 시까지 문 앞에 가마를 세워 둘 수 있다니.\""], ["He then ran on with much commonplace raillery of the same kind, till Jones at last interrupted him, saying, “I suppose you have received all this information from Mrs Miller, who hath been up here a little while ago to give me warning.", "그러고는 그런 식의 흔해 빠진 농담을 한참 늘어놓았고, 마침내 존스가 말을 가로막으며 입을 열었다. \"그 얘긴 다 밀러 부인한테서 들은 모양이군."], ["The good woman is afraid, it seems, of the reputation of her daughters.”", "조금 전에 여기 올라와서 내게 주의를 주고 갔거든. 그 착한 부인은 아무래도 딸들의 평판이 걱정인 모양이야.\""], ["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["she is wonderfully nice,”", "그 점에 대해선 정말이지 깐깐하지.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다."], ["says Nightingale, “upon that account; if you remember, she would not let Nancy go with us to the masquerade.”", "\"기억나나? 부인은 낸시를 우리와 함께 가면무도회에 보내려 하지 않았잖아.\""], ["“Nay, upon my honour, I think she's in the right of it,”", "\"아니, 정말이지, 내 생각엔 부인이 옳아.\" 존스가 말했다."], ["says Jones: “however, I have taken her at her word, and have sent Partridge to look for another lodging.”", "\"어쨌든 나는 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들여서, 파트리지를 보내 다른 하숙을 알아보게 했네.\" \"자네만 좋다면,\""], ["“If you will,”", "나이팅게일이 말했다."], ["says Nightingale, “we may, I believe, be again together; for, to tell you a secret, which I desire you won't mention in the family, I intend to quit the house to-day.”", "\"우리 또 함께 지낼 수도 있겠군. 사실 비밀을 하나 털어놓자면 — 이 집 식구들한테는 말하지 말아 주게 — 나도 오늘 이 집을 떠날 작정이거든.\""], ["“What, hath Mrs Miller given you warning too, my friend?”", "\"아니, 친구, 자네한테도 밀러 부인이 주의를 줬단 말인가?\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones. “No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["answered the other; “but the rooms are not convenient enough.", "상대가 대답했다. \"다만 방이 영 편치가 않아서 말이지."], ["Besides, I am grown weary of this part of the town.", "게다가 이 동네에도 슬슬 싫증이 났어."], ["I want to be nearer the places of diversion; so I am going to Pall-mall.”", "좀 더 놀거리 가까이에 있고 싶어서, 펠멜로 옮길 생각이네.\""], ["“And do you intend to make a secret of your going away?”", "\"그래서 떠나는 걸 비밀로 할 작정인가?\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones.", "\"약속하지.\""], ["“I promise you,”", "나이팅게일이 대답했다."], ["answered Nightingale, “I don't intend to bilk my lodgings; but I have a private reason for not taking a formal leave.”", "\"하숙비를 떼먹으려는 건 아니야. 다만 정식으로 작별 인사를 하지 않으려는 데에는 나름의 사사로운 이유가 있어.\""], ["“Not so private,”", "\"그리 사사로운 이유도 아니지.\""], ["answered Jones; “I promise you, I have seen it ever since the second day of my coming to the house.", "존스가 대답했다. \"분명히 말하는데, 나는 이 집에 온 둘째 날부터 그걸 다 보고 있었어."], ["Here will be some wet eyes on your departure.", "자네가 떠나면 여기 눈물 적실 사람이 생기겠지."], ["Poor Nancy, I pity her, faith!", "가엾은 낸시, 정말이지 안됐어!"], ["Indeed, Jack, you have played the fool with that girl.", "정말로, 잭, 자넨 그 처녀를 데리고 바보짓을 했어."], ["You have given her a longing, which I am afraid nothing will ever cure her of.”", "자네가 그 아이한테 갈망을 심어 놓았으니, 그건 아무것도 고쳐 주지 못할까 봐 두려워.\""], ["Nightingale answered, “What the devil would you have me do?", "나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"대체 나더러 어쩌란 말인가?"], ["would you have me marry her to cure her?”", "그 아이를 고쳐 주겠다고 결혼이라도 하란 말이야?\""], ["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["answered Jones, “I would not have had you make love to her, as you have often done in my presence.", "존스가 대답했다. \"자네가 내 눈앞에서 그토록 자주 했던 것처럼 그 아이한테 사랑을 속삭이지 말았어야 한다는 거야."], ["I have been astonished at the blindness of her mother in never seeing it.”", "나는 그 어머니가 그걸 한 번도 못 보다니, 그 눈먼 짓에 어이가 없었네.\""], ["“Pugh, see it!”", "\"흥, 그걸 봐?\""], ["cries Nightingale.", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다."], ["“What, the devil should she see?”", "\"대체 뭘 본단 말인가?\" \"뭘 보긴.\""], ["“Why, see,”", "존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “that you have made her daughter distractedly in love with you.", "\"자네가 그 딸을 자네한테 정신없이 빠지게 만들어 놓은 걸 본단 말이지."], ["The poor girl cannot conceal it a moment; her eyes are never off from you, and she always colours every time you come into the room.", "그 가엾은 처녀는 단 한순간도 그걸 숨기질 못해. 그 눈은 자네한테서 떠날 줄을 모르고, 자네가 방에 들어올 때마다 매번 얼굴을 붉히지."], ["Indeed, I pity her heartily; for she seems to be one of the best-natured and honestest of human creatures.”", "정말이지 나는 그 아이가 진심으로 가엾어. 사람들 중에서도 가장 마음씨 곱고 정직한 축으로 보이거든.\""], ["“And so,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["answered Nightingale, “according to your doctrine, one must not amuse oneself by any common gallantries with women, for fear they should fall in love with us.”", "나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"자네 말대로라면, 여자들이 우리한테 반할까 봐 두려워서 그저 흔한 가벼운 추파조차 즐겨서는 안 된다는 거로군.\""], ["“Indeed, Jack,”", "\"정말이지, 잭,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “you wilfully misunderstand me; I do not fancy women are so apt to fall in love; but you have gone far beyond common gallantries.”", "\"자넨 일부러 내 말을 잘못 알아듣는군. 나는 여자들이 그리 쉽게 사랑에 빠진다고 생각하진 않아."], ["“What, do you suppose,”", "다만 자네는 흔한 추파의 선을 한참 넘어섰단 말이지.\""], ["says Nightingale, “that we have been a-bed together?”", "\"뭐, 자넨 우리가 한 잠자리에 들기라도 했다고 생각하는 건가?\" 나이팅게일이 말했다."], ["“No, upon my honour,”", "\"아니, 정말이지,\" 존스가 자못 진지하게 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, very seriously, “I do not suppose so ill of you; nay, I will go farther, I do not imagine you have laid a regular premeditated scheme for the destruction of the quiet of a poor little creature, or have even foreseen the consequence: for I am sure thou art a very good-natured fellow; and such a one can never be guilty of a cruelty of that kind; but at the same time you have pleased your own vanity, without considering that this poor girl was made a sacrifice to it; and while you have had no design but of amusing an idle hour, you have actually given her reason to flatter herself that you had the most serious designs in her favour.", "\"자넬 그렇게까지 나쁘게 보진 않아. 아니, 한 걸음 더 나아가서 말하지."], ["Prithee, Jack, answer me honestly; to what have tended all those elegant and luscious descriptions of happiness arising from violent and mutual fondness?", "나는 자네가 그 가엾은 어린것의 평온을 무너뜨리려고 미리 짜 놓은 계획을 세웠다거나, 그 결과를 내다보고 있었다고도 생각하지 않아. 자네가 무척 마음씨 좋은 친구라는 걸 잘 아니까."], ["all those warm professions of tenderness, and generous disinterested love?", "그런 사람은 그런 식의 잔인한 짓을 저지를 리 없지. 하지만 그와 동시에, 자네는 이 가엾은 처녀가 그 제물로 바쳐지고 있다는 걸 헤아리지 않은 채 자기 허영심을 채운 거야."], ["Did you imagine she would not apply them?", "그리고 그저 무료한 시간을 달래려는 마음밖에 없었으면서도, 실은 그 아이로 하여금 자네가 자기한테 더없이 진지한 뜻을 품고 있다고 자만하게 만들 빌미를 준 거지. 자, 잭, 솔직하게 대답해 보게."], ["or, speak ingenuously, did not you intend she should?”", "격렬하고도 서로를 향한 애정에서 솟아나는 행복을, 그토록 우아하고 달콤하게 늘어놓은 그 모든 묘사가 다 무엇을 향한 거였나? 그 모든 따뜻한 다정함의 고백과, 너그럽고 사심 없는 사랑의 맹세는 다 무엇이었나?"], ["“Upon my soul, Tom,”", "자넨 그 아이가 그걸 제게 끌어다 새기지 않으리라고 생각했나?"], ["cries Nightingale, “I did not think this was in thee.", "아니면, 솔직히 말해 보게, 자넨 그 아이가 그러기를 바라지 않았던가?\" \"정말이지, 톰,\""], ["Thou wilt make an admirable parson.", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"자네한테 이런 면이 있는 줄은 몰랐네."], ["So I suppose you would not go to bed to Nancy now, if she would let you?”", "자넨 훌륭한 목사가 되겠어. 그러니까 자네라면 이제 낸시가 허락한다 해도 그 아이와 잠자리에 들지 않겠다는 거지?\""], ["“No,”", "\"안 들지.\""], ["cries Jones, “may I be d--n'd if I would.”", "존스가 외쳤다. \"들면 내가 천벌을 받겠네.\""], ["“Tom, Tom,”", "\"톰, 톰,\""], ["answered Nightingale, “last night; remember last night----", "나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"어젯밤 말이야, 어젯밤을 떠올려 보게——"]], [["When every eye was closed, and the pale moon, And silent stars, shone conscious of the theft.”", "모든 눈이 감기고, 창백한 달과\n말 없는 별들만이, 그 은밀한 도둑질을 알고 빛나던 그때를.\""]], [["“Lookee, Mr Nightingale,”", "\"이보게, 나이팅게일 씨.\""], ["said Jones, “I am no canting hypocrite, nor do I pretend to the gift of chastity, more than my neighbours.", "존스가 말했다. \"나는 입에 발린 위선자가 아니고, 이웃들보다 더 정결한 척하지도 않아."], ["I have been guilty with women, I own it; but am not conscious that I have ever injured any.--Nor would I, to procure pleasure to myself, be knowingly the cause of misery to any human being.”", "나도 여자들과 죄를 지은 적이 있고, 그건 인정하네. 하지만 내가 누구를 해쳤다는 자각은 없어.—그리고 내 쾌락을 얻자고, 어떤 사람에게든 알면서도 불행의 원인이 되지는 않겠네.\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"알았어, 알았어.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다."], ["said Nightingale, “I believe you, and I am convinced you acquit me of any such thing.”", "\"자네 말을 믿네. 그리고 자네가 나를 그런 짓에서만큼은 무죄로 봐 준다고 확신하네.\""]], [["“I do, from my heart,”", "\"진심으로 그러네.\""], ["answered Jones, “of having debauched the girl, but not from having gained her affections.”", "존스가 대답했다. \"그 아이를 농락했다는 점에서는 무죄로 보지만, 그 아이의 마음을 얻어 버린 일에 대해서는 아니야.\""]], [["“If I have,” said Nightingale, “I am sorry for it; but time and absence will soon wear off such impressions. It is a receipt I must take myself; for, to confess the truth to you--I never liked any girl half so much in my whole life; but I must let you into the whole secret, Tom. My father hath provided a match for me with a woman I never saw; and she is now coming to town, in order for me to make my addresses to her.”", "\"내가 그랬다면,\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"그건 미안한 일이지. 하지만 시간과 떨어져 있음이 그런 인상쯤은 곧 지워 줄 거야. 그건 나 스스로도 받아들여야 할 처방이거든. 사실대로 털어놓자면—내 평생 어떤 처녀도 그 절반만큼도 좋아해 본 적이 없으니까. 하지만 톰, 자네한테 비밀을 전부 알려 줘야겠어. 우리 아버지가 나는 본 적도 없는 여자와 혼처를 정해 두셨어. 그 여자가 지금 도시로 올라오고 있는데, 내가 가서 구애를 하라는 거야.\""]], [["At these words Jones burst into a loud fit of laughter; when Nightingale cried--“Nay, prithee, don't turn me into ridicule.", "이 말에 존스가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리자, 나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"아니, 제발, 날 비웃지 말게."], ["The devil take me if I am not half mad about this matter!", "이 일로 내가 반쯤 미쳐 있지 않다면 내가 악마한테 끌려가도 좋아!"], ["my poor Nancy!", "가엾은 내 낸시!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["Jones, Jones, I wish I had a fortune in my own possession.”", "존스, 존스, 내 손에 쥔 재산이 있었으면 좋겠네.\""]], [["“I heartily wish you had,”", "\"나도 진심으로 자네한테 그런 게 있었으면 싶네.\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones; “for, if this be the case, I sincerely pity you both; but surely you don't intend to go away without taking your leave of her?”", "\"사정이 그렇다면, 나는 자네들 둘 다 진심으로 가엾게 여기네. 하지만 설마 그 아이한테 작별 인사도 없이 떠날 작정은 아니겠지?\""]], [["“I would not,”", "\"나는,\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다."], ["answered Nightingale, “undergo the pain of taking leave, for ten thousand pounds; besides, I am convinced, instead of answering any good purpose, it would only serve to inflame my poor Nancy the more.", "\"만 파운드를 준다 해도 작별 인사의 고통은 견디지 못하겠네. 게다가 나는, 그게 무슨 좋은 목적에 보탬이 되기는커녕 가엾은 내 낸시를 더욱 달아오르게 만들 뿐이라고 확신해."], ["I beg, therefore, you would not mention a word of it to-day, and in the evening, or to-morrow morning, I intend to depart.”", "그러니 부탁인데 오늘은 그 일에 대해 한마디도 꺼내지 말아 주게. 나는 저녁에, 아니면 내일 아침에 떠날 작정이야.\""]], [["Jones promised he would not; and said, upon reflection, he thought, as he had determined and was obliged to leave her, he took the most prudent method.", "존스는 그러지 않겠다고 약속했다. 그러고는 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 자네가 이미 마음을 정했고 또 그 아이를 떠나야만 하는 처지라면, 가장 신중한 방법을 택한 것 같다고 말했다."], ["He then told Nightingale he should be very glad to lodge in the same house with him; and it was accordingly agreed between them, that Nightingale should procure him either the ground floor, or the two pair of stairs; for the young gentleman himself was to occupy that which was between them.", "이어 그는 나이팅게일에게 그와 같은 집에서 묵게 된다면 무척 기쁘겠다고 했고, 그리하여 두 사람 사이에서 나이팅게일이 그에게 일층이나 삼층 방 중 하나를 구해 주기로 합의가 이루어졌다. 그 사이 층은 이 젊은 신사 자신이 쓰기로 되어 있었기 때문이다."]], [["This Nightingale, of whom we shall be presently obliged to say a little more, was in the ordinary transactions of life a man of strict honour, and, what is more rare among young gentlemen of the town, one of strict honesty too; yet in affairs of love he was somewhat loose in his morals; not that he was even here as void of principle as gentlemen sometimes are, and oftener affect to be; but it is certain he had been guilty of some indefensible treachery to women, and had, in a certain mystery, called making love, practised many deceits, which, if he had used in trade, he would have been counted the greatest villain upon earth.", "이 나이팅게일은, 우리가 곧 그에 대해 조금 더 말하지 않을 수 없게 될 인물인데, 일상적인 거래에서는 엄격하게 명예를 지키는 사람이었고, 도시의 젊은 신사들 사이에서 더욱 드문 일로, 엄격하게 정직하기까지 한 사람이었다. 그럼에도 연애 문제에서는 그 도덕관념이 다소 헐거웠다. 그렇다고 이 점에서 그가 신사들이 더러 그러하고 더 자주 그런 척하는 것처럼 아예 원칙이라곤 없는 자였다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그가 여자들에게 변명의 여지가 없는 어떤 배신을 저질러 온 것만은 분명했고, 사랑을 한다고 불리는 어떤 알 수 없는 술수 속에서 숱한 기만을 부려 왔는데, 만약 그것을 장사에서 써먹었더라면 그는 세상에서 가장 지독한 악당으로 손꼽혔을 것이다."]], [["But as the world, I know not well for what reason, agree to see this treachery in a better light, he was so far from being ashamed of his iniquities of this kind, that he gloried in them, and would often boast of his skill in gaining of women, and his triumphs over their hearts, for which he had before this time received some rebukes from Jones, who always exprest great bitterness against any misbehaviour to the fair part of the species, who, if considered, he said, as they ought to be, in the light of the dearest friends, were to be cultivated, honoured, and caressed with the utmost love and tenderness; but, if regarded as enemies, were a conquest of which a man ought rather to be ashamed than to value himself upon it.", "하지만 세상은, 무슨 까닭에서인지 나로서는 잘 모르겠으나, 이런 배신을 너그러운 눈으로 보아 주기로 합의해 둔 터라, 그는 이런 종류의 죄악을 부끄러워하기는커녕 도리어 자랑스러워했고, 여자를 손에 넣는 자신의 솜씨와 그 마음을 정복한 승리를 곧잘 떠벌리곤 했다. 이 때문에 그는 그 전에도 존스에게서 몇 차례 꾸지람을 들은 적이 있었으니, 존스는 이 종족의 아리따운 절반에게 가하는 어떤 못된 행실에 대해서도 늘 큰 쓴소리를 쏟아 냈다. 그의 말로는, 여자들이란 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 가장 소중한 벗이라는 관점에서 본다면 더없는 사랑과 다정함으로 보살피고 떠받들며 애지중지해야 할 존재이지만, 만약 적으로 여긴다면 그 정복이란 한 사내가 그것을 자랑으로 삼기보다 도리어 부끄러워해야 마땅한 것이라고 했다."]], [["A short account of the history of Mrs Miller.", "밀러 부인의 내력에 관한 짧은 이야기."]], [["Jones this day eat a pretty good dinner for a sick man, that is to say, the larger half of a shoulder of mutton.", "존스는 이날, 병자치고는 꽤 푸짐한 저녁을 먹었다. 다시 말해 양 어깨살 한 덩이의 절반이 넘는 양을 먹어치웠다."], ["In the afternoon he received an invitation from Mrs Miller to drink tea; for that good woman, having learnt, either by means of Partridge, or by some other means natural or supernatural, that he had a connexion with Mr Allworthy, could not endure the thoughts of parting with him in an angry manner.", "오후에는 밀러 부인에게서 차를 함께 마시자는 청을 받았다. 마음씨 고운 이 부인은 파트리지를 통해서였는지, 아니면 자연스럽든 초자연적이든 다른 어떤 경로를 통해서였는지, 그가 올워디 씨와 인연이 있다는 사실을 알게 되자, 그를 불편한 마음으로 떠나보낸다는 생각을 도무지 견딜 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["Jones accepted the invitation; and no sooner was the tea-kettle removed, and the girls sent out of the room, than the widow, without much preface, began as follows: “Well, there are very surprizing things happen in this world; but certainly it is a wonderful business that I should have a relation of Mr Allworthy in my house, and never know anything of the matter.", "존스는 그 청을 받아들였다. 찻주전자가 치워지고 딸들이 방에서 물러나자마자, 미망인은 별다른 서두도 없이 다음과 같이 이야기를 시작했다."], ["Alas!", "\"글쎄요, 이 세상에는 참으로 놀라운 일들이 많이도 일어나지요. 하지만 올워디 씨의 친척분을 제 집에 모시고도 그 사실을 까맣게 모르고 있었다니, 정말이지 기막힌 노릇이 아닐 수 없어요."], ["sir, you little imagine what a friend that best of gentlemen hath been to me and mine.", "아아! 선생님, 그 누구보다 훌륭한 그 신사분이 저와 제 식구들에게 얼마나 큰 은인이셨는지 선생님은 짐작도 못 하실 거예요."], ["Yes, sir, I am not ashamed to own it; it is owing to his goodness that I did not long since perish for want, and leave my poor little wretches, two destitute, helpless, friendless orphans, to the care, or rather to the cruelty, of the world.", "그래요, 선생님, 저는 부끄러움 없이 인정하겠어요. 제가 오래전에 굶주려 죽지 않고, 의지할 데 없고 가엾고 친구 하나 없는 두 고아 자식을 세상의 보살핌이라기보다는 차라리 세상의 잔혹함에 내맡기고 떠나지 않을 수 있었던 것은, 모두가 그분의 자비 덕분이랍니다."]], [["“You must know, sir, though I am now reduced to get my living by letting lodgings, I was born and bred a gentlewoman.", "\"선생님, 아셔야 할 것이, 제가 지금은 셋방을 놓아 입에 풀칠하는 처지로 전락했지만, 본래는 양갓집 규수로 나고 자랐답니다."], ["My father was an officer of the army, and died in a considerable rank: but he lived up to his pay; and, as that expired with him, his family, at his death, became beggars.", "제 아버지는 군 장교셨고, 상당한 계급에 오른 채 돌아가셨어요. 하지만 봉급을 한 푼도 남김없이 다 쓰며 사셨고, 그 봉급이 아버지와 함께 끊어지자 그분이 돌아가셨을 때 우리 식구는 거지 신세가 되고 말았지요."], ["We were three sisters.", "우리는 세 자매였어요."], ["One of us had the good luck to die soon after of the small-pox; a lady was so kind as to take the second out of charity, as she said, to wait upon her.", "그중 하나는 운 좋게도 얼마 안 가 천연두로 세상을 떠났고, 둘째는 어느 부인이 자비를 베푼다며 시중을 들이려고 데려갔지요."], ["The mother of this lady had been a servant to my grand-mother; and, having inherited a vast fortune from her father, which he had got by pawnbroking, was married to a gentleman of great estate and fashion.", "그 부인의 어머니가 제 할머니의 하녀였는데, 전당포로 돈을 모은 자기 아버지에게서 막대한 재산을 물려받아 큰 영지를 가진 멋쟁이 신사와 결혼했더랬어요. 그 부인이 제 동생을 어찌나 야만스럽게 부려먹었는지, 걸핏하면 태생과 가난을 들먹이며 비웃듯 '양갓집 아가씨'라 불러댔으니, 결국 그 가엾은 아이의 가슴을 찢어놓고 만 거라고 저는 믿어요."], ["She used my sister so barbarously, often upbraiding her with her birth and poverty, calling her in derision a gentlewoman, that I believe she at length broke the heart of the poor girl.", "요컨대 그 애 역시 아버지가 돌아가신 지 한 해도 못 되어 세상을 떠났답니다. 운명은 제게는 더 나은 길을 마련해 주는 것이 옳다고 여겼는지, 아버지가 돌아가신 지 한 달 만에 저는 어느 목사님과 결혼했어요."], ["In short, she likewise died within a twelvemonth after my father.", "그분은 오래전부터 저를 사랑했고, 그 때문에 제 아버지에게 몹시 박대를 받았던 분이지요."], ["Fortune thought proper to provide better for me, and within a month from his decease I was married to a clergyman, who had been my lover a long time before, and who had been very ill used by my father on that account: for though my poor father could not give any of us a shilling, yet he bred us up as delicately, considered us, and would have had us consider ourselves, as highly as if we had been the richest heiresses.", "제 가엾은 아버지는 우리에게 단돈 한 푼 물려주지 못하면서도, 우리를 어찌나 곱게 길렀던지, 마치 우리가 세상에서 가장 부유한 상속녀라도 되는 양 우리를 대했고 우리 자신도 그렇게 여기기를 바라셨거든요. 하지만 사랑하는 제 남편은 그런 박대를 모두 잊고, 우리가 아버지를 여읜 그 순간 곧장 제게 어찌나 따뜻하게 다시 구애해 왔던지, 늘 그분을 좋아했고 이제는 그 어느 때보다 그분을 존경하게 된 저는 이내 마음을 허락했지요."], ["But my dear husband forgot all this usage, and the moment we were become fatherless he immediately renewed his addresses to me so warmly, that I, who always liked, and now more than ever esteemed him, soon complied.", "저는 그 더없이 훌륭한 분과 더없는 행복 속에서 오 년을 살았어요, 마침내, 그만—아아! 잔혹하고, 잔혹한 운명, 우리를 갈라놓고, 제게서 더없이 다정한 남편을, 제 가엾은 딸들에게서 더없이 자상한 아버지를 앗아간 그 운명—오, 가엾은 내 딸들아!"], ["Five years did I live in a state of perfect happiness with that best of men, till at last--Oh! cruel!", "너희는 너희가 잃은 그 축복이 무엇인지조차 알지 못하는구나—존스 씨, 이런 여자다운 나약함이 부끄럽습니다만, 저는 그분을 입에 올릴 때마다 눈물 없이는 못 한답니다.\""], ["cruel fortune, that ever separated us, that deprived me of the kindest of husbands and my poor girls of the tenderest parent.--O my poor girls!", "\"오히려 제가 부끄러워해야지요, 부인.\" 존스가 말했다."], ["you never knew the blessing which ye lost.--I am ashamed, Mr Jones, of this womanish weakness; but I shall never mention him without tears.”", "\"제가 부인과 함께 눈물 흘리지 못하니 말입니다.\" \"그런데 선생님,\""], ["“I ought rather, madam,”", "부인이 말을 이었다."], ["said Jones, “to be ashamed that I do not accompany you.” “Well, sir,”", "\"저는 이제 두 번째로, 이전보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 처지에 놓이게 되었어요."], ["continued she, “I was now left a second time in a much worse condition than before; besides the terrible affliction I was to encounter, I had now two children to provide for; and was, if possible, more pennyless than ever; when that great, that good, that glorious man, Mr Allworthy, who had some little acquaintance with my husband, accidentally heard of my distress, and immediately writ this letter to me.", "견뎌내야 할 그 끔찍한 슬픔에 더해, 이번에는 길러야 할 두 아이까지 있었고, 그러면서도 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 그 어느 때보다 무일푼이었으니까요. 바로 그때 제 남편과 약간의 친분이 있던 그 위대하고, 선량하고, 영광스러운 분, 올워디 씨가 우연히 제 곤경을 전해 듣고는 곧장 제게 이런 편지를 써 보내셨어요."], ["Here, sir, here it is; I put it into my pocket to shew it you.", "여기 있어요, 선생님, 여기. 선생님께 보여드리려고 주머니에 넣어 왔답니다."], ["This is the letter, sir; I must and will read it to you.", "이게 그 편지예요, 선생님. 꼭 읽어드려야겠어요, 그리고 그러고 싶어요."]], [["“'Madam,", "\"'부인께,"]], [["“'I heartily condole with you on your late grievous loss, which your own good sense, and the excellent lessons you must have learnt from the worthiest of men, will better enable you to bear than any advice which I am capable of giving.", "\"'근래 겪으신 비통한 상실에 대해 진심으로 애도를 표합니다. 부인 자신의 훌륭한 분별력과, 더없이 훌륭하셨던 분께 배우셨을 그 귀한 가르침이 있으니, 제가 드릴 수 있는 그 어떤 충고보다도 부인께서 그 슬픔을 더 잘 견디게 해 줄 것입니다."], ["Nor have I any doubt that you, whom I have heard to be the tenderest of mothers, will suffer any immoderate indulgence of grief to prevent you from discharging your duty to those poor infants, who now alone stand in need of your tenderness.", "또한 더없이 다정한 어머니라고 익히 들어온 부인께서, 지나친 비탄에 빠진 나머지, 이제 부인의 다정함만을 오롯이 필요로 하는 그 가엾은 어린것들에 대한 의무를 저버리시는 일은 결코 없으리라 믿어 의심치 않습니다."]], [["“`However, as you must be supposed at present to be incapable of much worldly consideration, you will pardon my having ordered a person to wait on you, and to pay you twenty guineas, which I beg you will accept till I have the pleasure of seeing you, and believe me to be, madam, &c.'", "\"'다만 지금 부인께서는 세상일을 두루 헤아릴 형편이 못 되시리라 짐작되니, 제가 사람을 시켜 부인을 찾아뵙고 이십 기니를 전해 드리도록 한 것을 너그러이 용서해 주십시오. 부디 받아주시기를 청하며, 부인을 직접 뵙는 기쁨을 누릴 때까지 기다리겠습니다. 부디 저를, 부인, 이러이러한 사람으로 여겨 주십시오.'"]], [["“This letter, sir, I received within a fortnight after the irreparable loss I have mentioned; and within a fortnight afterwards, Mr Allworthy--the blessed Mr Allworthy, came to pay me a visit, when he placed me in the house where you now see me, gave me a large sum of money to furnish it, and settled an annuity of £50 a-year upon me, which I have constantly received ever since.", "\"이 편지를 저는, 앞서 말씀드린 그 돌이킬 수 없는 상실을 겪은 지 보름 만에 받았어요, 선생님. 그리고 또 보름 뒤에는 올워디 씨가—그 복되신 올워디 씨가 친히 저를 찾아오셔서, 선생님이 지금 저를 보고 계신 바로 이 집에 저를 들여앉히시고, 살림을 갖추라며 큰돈을 주시고, 해마다 오십 파운드의 연금을 정해 주셨답니다."], ["Judge, then, Mr Jones, in what regard I must hold a benefactor, to whom I owe the preservation of my life, and of those dear children, for whose sake alone my life is valuable.", "그 연금을 저는 그 후로 줄곧 한 번도 거르지 않고 받아왔지요. 그러니 헤아려 보세요, 존스 씨, 제 목숨과, 또 그 아이들이 있기에 비로소 제 목숨이 값진 것이 되는 그 사랑하는 자식들의 목숨까지 보전해 준 은인을 제가 어떤 마음으로 여겨야 하는지를요."], ["Do not, therefore, think me impertinent, Mr Jones (since I must esteem one for whom I know Mr Allworthy hath so much value), if I beg you not to converse with these wicked women.", "그러니 존스 씨, 제가 이 말씀을 드린다고 주제넘다 여기지 말아주세요(올워디 씨가 그토록 귀히 여기시는 분이니 저 또한 선생님을 귀히 여길 수밖에 없는지라). 부디 그 사악한 여자들과는 가까이하지 말아주시기를 청합니다."], ["You are a young gentleman, and do not know half their artful wiles.", "선생님은 젊은 신사라, 그들의 교묘한 술책을 절반도 모르시잖아요."], ["Do not be angry with me, sir, for what I said upon account of my house; you must be sensible it would be the ruin of my poor dear girls.", "제 집을 두고 제가 드린 말씀에 노여워하지 마세요, 선생님. 그것이 제 가엾고 사랑하는 딸들의 파멸이 되리라는 걸 선생님도 분명 아실 거예요."], ["Besides, sir, you cannot but be acquainted that Mr Allworthy himself would never forgive my conniving at such matters, and particularly with you.”", "게다가 선생님, 올워디 씨 본인께서도 제가 그런 일들을, 더구나 선생님과 관련하여 눈감아 주는 것을 결코 용서치 않으시리라는 걸 모르실 리 없잖아요.\""]], [["“Upon my word, madam,” said Jones, “you need make no farther apology; nor do I in the least take anything ill you have said; but give me leave, as no one can have more value than myself for Mr Allworthy, to deliver you from one mistake, which, perhaps, would not be altogether for his honour; I do assure you, I am no relation of his.”", "\"정말이지요, 부인.\" 존스가 말했다. \"더는 사과하실 것 없습니다. 또 부인께서 하신 말씀을 조금도 언짢게 여기지도 않고요. 다만, 올워디 씨를 저보다 더 귀히 여길 사람은 없을 터이니, 어쩌면 그분의 명예에 썩 좋지만은 않을 한 가지 오해에서 부인을 풀어드리고자 합니다. 단언컨대 저는 그분의 친척이 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Alas!", "\"아아! 선생님,\""], ["sir,”", "부인이 대답했다."], ["answered she, “I know you are not, I know very well who you are; for Mr Allworthy hath told me all; but I do assure you, had you been twenty times his son, he could not have expressed more regard for you than he hath often expressed in my presence.", "\"친척이 아니라는 것, 저도 압니다. 선생님이 누구신지 아주 잘 알아요."], ["You need not be ashamed, sir, of what you are; I promise you no good person will esteem you the less on that account.", "올워디 씨가 제게 다 말씀해 주셨거든요. 하지만 분명히 말씀드리지만, 선생님이 설령 스무 번 그분의 친아들이었다 한들, 제 앞에서 그분이 누차 보이신 그 마음보다 더한 애정을 보이실 수는 없었을 거예요."], ["No, Mr Jones, the words `dishonourable birth'", "선생님의 처지를 부끄러워하실 것 없어요."], ["are nonsense, as my dear, dear husband used to say, unless the word `dishonourable'", "약속하건대 어떤 선량한 사람도 그 때문에 선생님을 덜 귀하게 여기지는 않을 테니까요. 아니지요, 존스 씨, '떳떳지 못한 출생'이라는 말은, 제 사랑하고 사랑하는 남편이 늘 말했듯이, 헛소리일 뿐이에요."], ["be applied to the parents; for the children can derive no real dishonour from an act of which they are intirely innocent.”", "그 '떳떳지 못한'이라는 말이 부모에게 붙는 것이라면 모를까요. 아이들이야, 자기들과는 전혀 무관한, 자기들이 아무 죄 없는 어떤 행위로부터 진짜 불명예를 떠안을 까닭이 없으니까요.\""]], [["Here Jones heaved a deep sigh, and then said, “Since I perceive, madam, you really do know me, and Mr Allworthy hath thought proper to mention my name to you; and since you have been so explicit with me as to your own affairs, I will acquaint you with some more circumstances concerning myself.”", "여기서 존스는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 말했다. \"부인께서 저를 정말로 알고 계시고, 올워디 씨가 제 이름을 부인께 말씀하시는 것이 옳다고 여기셨음을 이제 알았으니, 또 부인께서 당신 자신의 사정을 제게 그토록 솔직히 털어놓으셨으니, 저도 제 자신에 관한 몇 가지 사정을 더 말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["And these Mrs Miller having expressed great desire and curiosity to hear, he began and related to her his whole history, without once mentioning the name of Sophia.", "밀러 부인이 그 이야기를 듣고 싶다는 큰 바람과 호기심을 드러내자, 그는 이야기를 시작하여 자신의 내력을 처음부터 끝까지 들려주었다. 다만 소피아의 이름만은 단 한 번도 입에 올리지 않았다."]], [["There is a kind of sympathy in honest minds, by means of which they give an easy credit to each other.", "정직한 마음들 사이에는 일종의 공감이 있어서, 그것을 통해 서로의 말을 쉽게 믿어준다. 밀러 부인은 존스가 들려준 이야기를 모두 사실로 믿었고, 그를 향해 깊은 연민과 걱정을 드러냈다."], ["Mrs Miller believed all which Jones told her to be true, and exprest much pity and concern for him.", "그녀가 그 이야기에 대해 이런저런 말을 보태려는 참이었으나 존스가 그 말을 가로막았다. 약속한 시각이 이제 가까워졌기에, 그는 그날 저녁 그 부인과 한 번 더 만나게 해달라고 청하기 시작했던 것이다."], ["She was beginning to comment on the story, but Jones interrupted her; for, as the hour of assignation now drew nigh, he began to stipulate for a second interview with the lady that evening, which he promised should be the last at her house; swearing, at the same time, that she was one of great distinction, and that nothing but what was intirely innocent was to pass between them; and I do firmly believe he intended to keep his word.", "그러면서 이번이 그녀의 집에서 갖는 마지막 만남이 될 것이라 약속했고, 동시에 그 부인은 지체 높은 분이며 둘 사이에는 더없이 결백한 일 외에는 아무것도 오가지 않으리라고 맹세했다. 그리고 나는 그가 그 말을 지킬 작정이었음을 굳게 믿는다."]], [["Mrs Miller was at length prevailed on, and Jones departed to his chamber, where he sat alone till twelve o'clock, but no Lady Bellaston appeared.", "밀러 부인은 마침내 설득되었고, 존스는 자기 방으로 물러갔다. 그곳에서 그는 열두 시까지 홀로 앉아 있었으나, 벨라스턴 부인은 끝내 나타나지 않았다."]], [["As we have said that this lady had a great affection for Jones, and as it must have appeared that she really had so, the reader may perhaps wonder at the first failure of her appointment, as she apprehended him to be confined by sickness, a season when friendship seems most to require such visits. This behaviour, therefore, in the lady, may, by some, be condemned as unnatural; but that is not our fault; for our business is only to record truth.", "앞서 이 부인이 존스에게 깊은 애정을 품고 있다고 말했고, 또 실제로 그러했음이 분명히 드러났을 터이니, 독자는 어쩌면 그녀가 첫 약속부터 어긴 것을 의아하게 여길지도 모르겠다. 더구나 그녀는 그가 병으로 발이 묶여 있다고 짐작하고 있었는데, 그런 때야말로 우정이 그런 방문을 가장 절실히 요구하는 법이니 말이다. 그러므로 이 부인의 처신을 두고 더러는 부자연스럽다 탓할 수도 있겠다. 하지만 그것은 우리 잘못이 아니다. 우리의 소임은 다만 진실을 기록하는 것뿐이니까."]], [["# Chapter vi.", "# Chapter vi."]], [["Containing a scene which we doubt not will affect all our readers.", "모든 독자의 마음을 움직이리라 믿어 의심치 않는 한 장면을 담은 이야기."]], [["Mr Jones closed not his eyes during all the former part of the night; not owing to any uneasiness which he conceived at being disappointed by Lady Bellaston; nor was Sophia herself, though most of his waking hours were justly to be charged to her account, the present cause of dispelling his slumbers. In fact, poor Jones was one of the best-natured fellows alive, and had all that weakness which is called compassion, and which distinguishes this imperfect character from that noble firmness of mind, which rolls a man, as it were, within himself, and like a polished bowl, enables him to run through the world without being once stopped by the calamities which happen to others. He could not help, therefore, compassionating the situation of poor Nancy, whose love for Mr Nightingale seemed to him so apparent, that he was astonished at the blindness of her mother, who had more than once, the preceding evening, remarked to him the great change in the temper of her daughter, “who from being,” she said, “one of the liveliest, merriest girls in the world, was, on a sudden, become all gloom and melancholy.”", "존스 씨는 그날 밤 초저녁 내내 한순간도 눈을 붙이지 못했다. 그러나 그것은 벨라스턴 부인에게 바람을 맞아 생긴 불편함 때문이 아니었다. 깨어 있던 시간의 대부분을 마땅히 그녀 몫으로 돌려야 할 소피아조차도, 이번만큼은 그의 잠을 쫓아낸 원인이 아니었다. 사실 가엾은 존스는 이 세상에 살아 있는 사람 가운데 가장 천성이 따뜻한 축에 드는 인물이었고, 연민이라 불리는 그 모든 나약함을 지니고 있었다. 그 나약함이야말로 이 불완전한 성품을, 마치 사람을 제 안으로 둥글게 말아 넣어 잘 닦인 공처럼 굴려, 남에게 닥치는 재앙에 단 한 번도 발이 묶이지 않은 채 세상을 굴러가게 하는 저 고결한 마음의 단단함과 구별 짓는 것이었다. 그러니 그는 가엾은 낸시의 처지를 동정하지 않을 수 없었다. 나이팅게일 씨를 향한 그녀의 사랑이 그에게는 너무도 뚜렷이 보였기에, 그는 그 어미의 눈먼 둔함에 놀랐다. 그 어미는 바로 전날 저녁만 해도 그에게 딸의 성정이 크게 변했다고 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 말했던 것이다. \"세상에서 가장 활기차고 명랑한 아이들 가운데 하나였던 애가,\" 그녀의 말로는, \"갑자기 온통 우울하고 침울한 아이가 되어 버렸다\"는 것이었다."]], [["Sleep, however, at length got the better of all resistance; and now, as if he had already been a deity, as the antients imagined, and an offended one too, he seemed to enjoy his dear-bought conquest.--To speak simply, and without any metaphor, Mr Jones slept till eleven the next morning, and would, perhaps, have continued in the same quiet situation much longer, had not a violent uproar awakened him.", "그러나 마침내 잠은 모든 저항을 이겨 냈다. 그리고 이제, 옛사람들이 상상했듯 마치 그 자신이 이미 하나의 신, 그것도 노여워하는 신이라도 된 양, 그는 비싼 값을 치르고 얻은 그 승리를 음미하는 듯했다. 비유 없이 단순하게 말하자면, 존스 씨는 이튿날 아침 열한 시까지 잤고, 만일 격렬한 소란이 그를 깨우지 않았더라면 어쩌면 그 고요한 상태를 훨씬 더 오래 이어 갔을 것이다."]], [["Partridge was now summoned, who, being asked what was the matter, answered, “That there was a dreadful hurricane below-stairs; that Miss Nancy was in fits; and that the other sister, and the mother, were both crying and lamenting over her.” Jones expressed much concern at this news; which Partridge endeavoured to relieve, by saying, with a smile, “he fancied the young lady was in no danger of death; for that Susan” (which was the name of the maid) “had given him to understand, it was nothing more than a common affair. In short,” said he, “Miss Nancy hath had a mind to be as wise as her mother; that's all; she was a little hungry, it seems, and so sat down to dinner before grace was said; and so there is a child coming for the Foundling Hospital.”----“Prithee, leave thy stupid jesting,” cries Jones. “Is the misery of these poor wretches a subject of mirth? Go immediately to Mrs Miller, and tell her I beg leave--Stay, you will make some blunder; I will go myself; for she desired me to breakfast with her.” He then rose and dressed himself as fast as he could; and while he was dressing, Partridge, notwithstanding many severe rebukes, could not avoid throwing forth certain pieces of brutality, commonly called jests, on this occasion. Jones was no sooner dressed than he walked downstairs, and knocking at the door, was presently admitted by the maid, into the outward parlour, which was as empty of company as it was of any apparatus for eating. Mrs Miller was in the inner room with her daughter, whence the maid presently brought a message to Mr Jones, “That her mistress hoped he would excuse the disappointment, but an accident had happened, which made it impossible for her to have the pleasure of his company at breakfast that day; and begged his pardon for not sending him up notice sooner.” Jones desired, “She would give herself no trouble about anything so trifling as his disappointment; that he was heartily sorry for the occasion; and that if he could be of any service to her, she might command him.”", "이내 파트리지가 불려 왔고, 무슨 일이냐는 물음에 그는 이렇게 답했다. \"아래층에서 무시무시한 난리가 났습니다. 낸시 양은 발작을 일으켰고, 다른 자매와 어머니는 둘 다 그 곁에서 울며 슬퍼하고 있습니다.\" 존스는 이 소식에 깊은 걱정을 드러냈다. 파트리지는 그 걱정을 덜어 주려는 듯 빙그레 웃으며 말했다. \"그 아가씨가 죽을 위험에 처한 것은 아닌 듯합니다. 수전이\"—이것이 하녀의 이름이었다—\"제게 말해 준 바로는, 그저 흔해 빠진 일에 지나지 않는다고 하더군요. 한마디로,\" 그가 말했다, \"낸시 양은 제 어머니만큼이나 영리해지고 싶었던 모양입니다. 그게 다입니다. 보아하니 좀 시장하셨던지, 식전 기도도 올리기 전에 자리에 앉아 저녁을 잡수신 거지요. 그래서 업둥이 보호원으로 갈 아이가 하나 생기게 된 겁니다.\" \"제발 그 어리석은 농담 좀 그만두게,\" 존스가 외쳤다. \"이 가엾은 사람들의 비참함이 우스갯거리란 말인가? 당장 밀러 부인께 가서 내가 청을 드린다고 전하게—아니, 기다리게, 자네는 또 무슨 실수를 저지를 게야. 내가 직접 가겠네. 부인께서 함께 아침을 들자고 청하셨으니.\" 그러고 나서 그는 일어나 할 수 있는 한 서둘러 옷을 입었다. 그가 옷을 입는 동안 파트리지는 호된 꾸지람을 여러 번 듣고도, 이런 판에 흔히 농담이라 불리는 어떤 야비한 말들을 던지는 것을 끝내 참지 못했다. 존스는 옷을 입자마자 아래층으로 내려가 문을 두드렸고, 곧 하녀의 안내를 받아 바깥 응접실로 들었다. 그곳은 손님이 없는 만큼이나 식사 채비라곤 아무것도 없이 텅 비어 있었다. 밀러 부인은 안쪽 방에서 딸과 함께 있었는데, 이내 하녀가 거기서 존스 씨에게 전갈을 가져왔다. \"마님께서 약속을 못 지키게 된 것을 부디 너그러이 용서해 주십사 하십니다. 뜻밖의 일이 생겨 오늘 아침은 함께 식사하는 기쁨을 누릴 수 없게 되었고, 더 일찍 기별을 올리지 못한 것을 거듭 사죄드린다 하십니다.\" 존스는 부탁했다. \"약속을 못 지킨 것 같은 사소한 일로는 조금도 마음 쓰지 마시라고 전해 주게. 나는 그 사연이 진심으로 안타깝네. 그리고 내가 부인께 무슨 도움이라도 될 수 있다면, 무엇이든 분부만 하시라 하게.\""]], [["He had scarce spoke these words, when Mrs Miller, who heard them all, suddenly threw open the door, and coming out to him, in a flood of tears, said, “O Mr Jones!", "그가 이 말을 채 마치기도 전에, 그 말을 모두 듣고 있던 밀러 부인이 갑자기 문을 활짝 열고 눈물을 쏟으며 그에게로 나와 말했다. \"오, 존스 씨!"], ["you are certainly one of the best young men alive.", "당신은 분명 세상에 살아 있는 가장 훌륭한 젊은이 가운데 한 분이세요."], ["I give you a thousand thanks for your kind offer of your service; but, alas!", "도움을 베풀어 주시겠다는 그 다정한 말씀에 천 번이라도 감사드립니다. 하지만, 아아!"], ["sir, it is out of your power to preserve my poor girl.--O my child!", "선생님, 제 가엾은 딸을 구하는 것만은 선생님의 힘으로도 어쩌실 수가 없답니다. 오, 내 아이!"], ["my child!", "내 아이!"], ["she is undone, she is ruined for ever!”", "그 애는 끝장났어요, 영영 망가지고 말았어요!\""], ["“I hope, madam,”", "\"부디, 부인,\""], ["said Jones, “no villain”----“O Mr Jones!”", "존스가 말했다, \"어떤 악당이……\" \"오, 존스 씨!\""], ["said she, “that villain who yesterday left my lodgings, hath betrayed my poor girl; hath destroyed her.--I know you are a man of honour.", "그녀가 말했다, \"어제 제 하숙을 떠난 그 악당이 제 가엾은 딸을 배신했어요. 그 애를 망쳐 놓았답니다."], ["You have a good--a noble heart, Mr Jones.", "저는 선생님이 명예를 아는 분임을 압니다."], ["The actions to which I have been myself a witness, could proceed from no other.", "선생님께는 선한, 고결한 마음이 있으세요, 존스 씨. 제가 직접 목격한 그 행동들은 다른 어떤 마음에서도 나올 수 없는 것이었지요."], ["I will tell you all: nay, indeed, it is impossible, after what hath happened, to keep it a secret.", "모두 말씀드리겠어요. 아니, 사실 이렇게 일이 터진 마당에 비밀로 묻어 둘 수도 없는 노릇이지요."], ["That Nightingale, that barbarous villain, hath undone my daughter.", "그 나이팅게일, 그 잔인한 악당이 제 딸을 망쳐 놓았어요."], ["She is--she is--oh!", "그 애는—그 애는—오!"], ["Mr Jones, my girl is with child by him; and in that condition he hath deserted her.", "존스 씨, 제 딸이 그자의 아이를 가졌답니다. 그래 놓고 그자는 그런 처지에 놓인 그 애를 내버린 거예요."], ["Here!", "여기!"], ["here, sir, is his cruel letter: read it, Mr Jones, and tell me if such another monster lives.”", "여기 있어요, 선생님, 이게 그자의 모진 편지예요. 읽어 보세요, 존스 씨, 그리고 이런 괴물이 또 어디 있을지 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["The letter was as follows:", "편지는 다음과 같았다."]], [["“As I found it impossible to mention to you what, I am afraid, will be no less shocking to you, than it is to me, I have taken this method to inform you, that my father insists upon my immediately paying my addresses to a young lady of fortune, whom he hath provided for my--I need not write the detested word. Your own good understanding will make you sensible, how entirely I am obliged to an obedience, by which I shall be for ever excluded from your dear arms. The fondness of your mother may encourage you to trust her with the unhappy consequence of our love, which may be easily kept a secret from the world, and for which I will take care to provide, as I will for you. I wish you may feel less on this account than I have suffered; but summon all your fortitude to your assistance, and forgive and forget the man, whom nothing but the prospect of certain ruin could have forced to write this letter. I bid you forget me, I mean only as a lover; but the best of friends you shall ever find in your faithful, though unhappy,", "\"사랑하는 낸시,\n나로서는 도저히 입에 올릴 수 없는 일을 — 그대에게도 나 못지않게 충격일 것이 두려운 그 일을 — 전하기 위해 이 방법을 택했소. 내 아버지께서, 나를 위해 마련해 두신 재산 있는 어느 젊은 처자에게 내가 당장 구애에 나서야 한다고 고집하시오. 그 가증스러운 말을 굳이 적을 필요는 없겠지. 그대의 뛰어난 분별력이라면, 내가 이 복종에 얼마나 송두리째 매여 있는지 절감하게 될 거요. 그 복종으로 인해 나는 그대의 그리운 품에서 영영 떨어져 나가게 될 것이오. 그대 어머니의 다정함이라면, 우리 사랑의 그 불행한 결과를 그분께 털어놓도록 그대를 북돋워 줄지도 모르겠소. 그 일은 세상에 숨기기도 어렵지 않을 것이고, 나는 그대를 보살피듯 그 일도 책임지고 마련해 두겠소. 부디 이 일로 그대가 겪는 고통이 내가 겪은 것보다 덜하기를 바라오. 그러나 가진 모든 의연함을 끌어모아 그대 자신을 추스르고, 닥쳐올 파멸이 뻔히 보이지 않았던들 결코 이 편지를 쓰지 않았을 이 사내를 용서하고 잊어 주오. 나를 잊으라 하지만, 어디까지나 연인으로서만 잊으라는 뜻이오. 그러나 변함없는, 비록 불행한 그대의 벗으로서는, 그대가 언제까지나 가장 좋은 벗을 내게서 찾게 될 것이오."]], [["When Jones had read this letter, they both stood silent during a minute, looking at each other; at last he began thus: “I cannot express, madam, how much I am shocked at what I have read; yet let me beg you, in one particular, to take the writer's advice.", "J. N.\""], ["Consider the reputation of your daughter.”----“It is gone, it is lost, Mr Jones,”", "존스가 이 편지를 다 읽고 나자, 두 사람은 잠시 동안 말없이 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다. 마침내 그가 입을 열었다."], ["cryed she, “as well as her innocence.", "\"부인, 제가 읽은 것에 얼마나 큰 충격을 받았는지 이루 말로 다 할 수가 없습니다."], ["She received the letter in a room full of company, and immediately swooning away upon opening it, the contents were known to every one present.", "그래도 한 가지만은, 부디 이 편지를 쓴 자의 조언을 따르시기를 간청드립니다. 따님의 평판을 생각하셔야 합니다.\""], ["But the loss of her reputation, bad as it is, is not the worst; I shall lose my child; she hath attempted twice to destroy herself already; and though she hath been hitherto prevented, vows she will not outlive it; nor could I myself outlive any accident of that nature.--What then will become of my little Betsy, a helpless infant orphan?", "\"그건 이미 떠나 버렸어요, 사라져 버렸답니다, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 외쳤다, \"그 애의 순결과 마찬가지로요."], ["and the poor little wretch will, I believe, break her heart at the miseries with which she sees her sister and myself distracted, while she is ignorant of the cause.", "그 애는 손님들로 가득 찬 방에서 그 편지를 받았고, 봉투를 뜯자마자 그 자리에서 까무러쳐서, 그 안에 담긴 내용이 거기 있던 사람들 모두에게 알려지고 말았어요. 하지만 평판을 잃은 것이, 그것이 아무리 끔찍해도, 가장 나쁜 일은 아니에요."], ["O 'tis the most sensible, and best-natured little thing!", "저는 제 아이를 잃게 될 거예요. 그 애는 벌써 두 번이나 스스로 목숨을 끊으려 했어요."], ["The barbarous, cruel----hath destroyed us all.", "지금까지는 가까스로 막았지만, 이 일을 안고서는 살아남지 않겠다고 맹세하고 있답니다. 그리고 저 자신도 그런 일을 당하고서는 살아남을 수가 없을 거예요."], ["O my poor children! Is this the reward of all my cares?", "그러면 제 어린 베치는, 의지할 데 없는 그 핏덩이 고아는 어찌 되겠어요?"], ["Is this the fruit of all my prospects?", "그 가엾은 어린것은, 까닭도 모른 채 제 언니와 제가 슬픔에 미쳐 가는 그 비참한 꼴을 보다가, 제 생각엔, 가슴이 미어져 버릴 거예요."], ["Have I so chearfully undergone all the labours and duties of a mother?", "오, 그 애는 더없이 영민하고 마음씨 고운 작은 아이랍니다! 그 야만스럽고 잔인한 자가 우리 모두를 망쳐 놓았어요."], ["Have I been so tender of their infancy, so careful of their education?", "오, 내 가엾은 아이들! 이것이 제 그 모든 정성의 보답인가요?"], ["Have I been toiling so many years, denying myself even the conveniences of life, to provide some little sustenance for them, to lose one or both in such a manner?”", "이것이 제 모든 바람의 열매인가요? 제가 어미의 그 모든 수고와 의무를 그토록 기꺼이 감당해 온 것이, 그 애들의 어린 시절을 그토록 애틋이 보살피고, 그 애들의 교육을 그토록 정성껏 챙긴 것이, 그 애들에게 변변찮으나마 먹을거리라도 마련해 주려고 제 한 몸 누릴 편의마저 마다하며 그 숱한 세월을 애써 온 것이, 결국 그 가운데 하나를, 아니 둘 다를 이런 식으로 잃기 위해서였단 말인가요?\""], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"참으로, 부인,\""], ["said Jones, with tears in his eyes, “I pity you from my soul.”--“O!", "존스가 두 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 말했다, \"저는 영혼 깊은 곳에서 부인을 가엾이 여깁니다.\" \"오!"], ["Mr Jones,”", "존스 씨,\""], ["answered she, “even you, though I know the goodness of your heart, can have no idea of what I feel.", "그녀가 대답했다, \"저는 선생님의 마음이 얼마나 선한지 알지만, 선생님조차도 제가 지금 느끼는 것이 어떤 것인지는 짐작도 못 하실 거예요."], ["The best, the kindest, the most dutiful of children!", "가장 착하고, 가장 다정하고, 가장 효성스러운 아이였답니다!"], ["O my poor Nancy, the darling of my soul!", "오, 내 가엾은 낸시, 내 영혼의 사랑이여!"], ["the delight of my eyes!", "내 두 눈의 기쁨이여!"], ["the pride of my heart!", "내 가슴의 자랑이여!"], ["too much, indeed, my pride; for to those foolish, ambitious hopes, arising from her beauty, I owe her ruin.", "정말이지 너무나 큰 제 자랑거리였어요. 그 애의 아름다움에서 비롯된 그 어리석고 욕심 사나운 기대들 때문에 제가 그 애를 망친 것이니까요."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["I saw with pleasure the liking which this young man had for her.", "저는 이 젊은이가 그 애에게 보이는 호감을 기쁘게 바라보았어요."], ["I thought it an honourable affection; and flattered my foolish vanity with the thoughts of seeing her married to one so much her superior.", "저는 그것이 떳떳한 애정이라 여겼고, 그 애가 그토록 격이 높은 사람과 혼인하는 것을 보게 되리라는 생각으로 제 어리석은 허영을 부풀렸지요. 그자는 제 앞에서 천 번도 넘게, 아니 선생님 앞에서도 종종, 사심 없는 사랑의 더없이 너그러운 말들로 이 기대를 어르고 북돋우려 했어요."], ["And a thousand times in my presence, nay, often in yours, he hath endeavoured to soothe and encourage these hopes by the most generous expressions of disinterested love, which he hath always directed to my poor girl, and which I, as well as she, believed to be real.", "그 말들은 늘 제 가엾은 딸을 향한 것이었고, 그 애도 저도 그것을 진심이라 믿었답니다. 이것이 그저 제 아이의 순결을 배신하고 우리 모두를 파멸시키려고 놓아 둔 덫일 뿐이었다고, 제가 어찌 믿을 수 있었겠어요?\""], ["Could I have believed that these were only snares laid to betray the innocence of my child, and for the ruin of us all?”--At these words little Betsy came running into the room, crying, “Dear mamma, for heaven's sake come to my sister; for she is in another fit, and my cousin can't hold her.”", "이 말이 끝날 즈음 어린 베치가 방으로 달려 들어오며 외쳤다. \"엄마, 제발 언니한테 와 주세요."], ["Mrs Miller immediately obeyed the summons; but first ordered Betsy to stay with Mr Jones, and begged him to entertain her a few minutes, saying, in the most pathetic voice, “Good heaven!", "또 발작을 일으켜서 사촌 언니가 붙잡지를 못해요.\" 밀러 부인은 곧장 그 부름에 따랐으나, 먼저 베치에게는 존스 씨와 함께 있으라 이르고, 그에게 몇 분만 아이를 돌봐 달라 부탁하며 더없이 애처로운 목소리로 말했다."], ["let me preserve one of my children at least.”", "\"오, 하느님! 적어도 제 아이 하나만이라도 지켜 주소서.\""]], [["Jones, in compliance with this request, did all he could to comfort the little girl, though he was, in reality, himself very highly affected with Mrs Miller's story.", "존스는 이 부탁에 따라, 비록 그 자신도 사실 밀러 부인의 사연에 무척이나 마음이 흔들리고 있었지만, 어린 소녀를 달래기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다했다. 그는 아이에게 말했다."], ["He told her “Her sister would be soon very well again; that by taking on in that manner she would not only make her sister worse, but make her mother ill too.”", "\"네 언니는 곧 다시 좋아질 거란다. 네가 그렇게 슬퍼하면 언니를 더 나쁘게 할 뿐 아니라, 어머니까지 병나게 할 거야.\""], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"정말이에요, 아저씨,\""], ["says she, “I would not do anything to hurt them for the world.", "아이가 말했다, \"저는 무슨 일이 있어도 그분들을 아프게 할 짓은 하지 않을 거예요. 제가 우는 걸 그분들이 보느니 차라리 가슴이 터지는 게 나아요."], ["I would burst my heart rather than they should see me cry.--But my poor sister can't see me cry.--I am afraid she will never be able to see me cry any more.", "하지만 가엾은 우리 언니는 제가 우는 걸 볼 수가 없어요. 언니가 다시는 제가 우는 걸 볼 수 없게 될까 봐 무서워요."], ["Indeed, I can't part with her; indeed, I can't.--And then poor mamma too, what will become of her?--She says she will die too, and leave me: but I am resolved I won't be left behind.”", "정말이지 저는 언니랑 헤어질 수 없어요. 정말 그럴 수 없어요."], ["“And are you not afraid to die, my little Betsy?”", "그리고 가엾은 엄마는 또 어찌 되시려고요? 엄마도 죽어서 저를 두고 떠나겠다고 하시는데, 저는 결코 혼자 남지 않을 거예요.\""], ["said Jones.", "\"그럼 너는 죽는 게 두렵지 않니, 우리 어린 베치?\" 존스가 말했다."], ["“Yes,”", "\"두려워요,\" 아이가 대답했다, \"저는 늘 죽는 게 두려웠어요."], ["answered she, “I was always afraid to die; because I must have left my mamma, and my sister; but I am not afraid of going anywhere with those I love.”", "죽으면 엄마랑 언니를 두고 떠나야 하니까요. 하지만 제가 사랑하는 사람들과 함께라면 어디로 가든 두렵지 않아요.\""]], [["Jones was so pleased with this answer, that he eagerly kissed the child; and soon after Mrs Miller returned, saying, “She thanked heaven Nancy was now come to herself.", "존스는 이 대답이 어찌나 흐뭇했던지 아이에게 열렬히 입을 맞추었다. 곧이어 밀러 부인이 돌아오며 말했다."], ["And now, Betsy,”", "\"이제 낸시가 정신을 차렸으니 하느님께 감사할 따름이에요. 자, 베치,\""], ["says she, “you may go in, for your sister is better, and longs to see you.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"너는 들어가 보렴. 언니가 좀 나아져서 너를 몹시 보고 싶어 하니까.\""], ["She then turned to Jones, and began to renew her apologies for having disappointed him of his breakfast.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 존스에게로 돌아서서, 그에게 아침 식사를 대접하기로 한 약속을 못 지킨 것에 대해 다시금 사죄의 말을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“I hope, madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Jones, “I shall have a more exquisite repast than any you could have provided for me.", "존스가 말했다, \"저는 부인께서 차려 주셨을 그 어떤 것보다 더 진귀한 한 끼를 누리게 될 듯합니다."], ["This, I assure you, will be the case, if I can do any service to this little family of love.", "제가 이 작고 사랑스러운 가족에게 조금이라도 도움이 될 수 있다면, 분명 그렇게 되리라 단언합니다."], ["But whatever success may attend my endeavours, I am resolved to attempt it.", "하지만 제 노력에 어떤 결과가 따르든, 저는 그 일을 시도해 보기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["I am very much deceived in Mr Nightingale, if, notwithstanding what hath happened, he hath not much goodness of heart at the bottom, as well as a very violent affection for your daughter.", "만일, 이런 일이 벌어졌음에도, 나이팅게일 씨가 마음속 깊은 곳에 따뜻한 선의를, 그리고 따님을 향한 매우 격렬한 애정을 품고 있지 않다면, 그것은 제가 그 사람을 크게 잘못 본 것이겠지요."], ["If this be the case, I think the picture which I shall lay before him will affect him.", "만일 그렇다면, 제가 그 앞에 펼쳐 보일 이 그림이 그의 마음을 움직이리라 생각합니다."], ["Endeavour, madam, to comfort yourself, and Miss Nancy, as well as you can.", "부인, 부디 부인 자신과 낸시 양을 할 수 있는 한 추스르도록 애써 보십시오."], ["I will go instantly in quest of Mr Nightingale; and I hope to bring you good news.”", "저는 당장 나이팅게일 씨를 찾아 나서겠습니다. 그리고 부인께 좋은 소식을 가져다드리기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Mrs Miller fell upon her knees and invoked all the blessings of heaven upon Mr Jones; to which she afterwards added the most passionate expressions of gratitude.", "밀러 부인은 무릎을 꿇고 하늘의 온갖 축복이 존스 씨에게 내리기를 빌었으며, 거기에 더해 더없이 간절한 감사의 말을 쏟아 냈다."], ["He then departed to find Mr Nightingale, and the good woman returned to comfort her daughter, who was somewhat cheared at what her mother told her; and both joined in resounding the praises of Mr Jones.", "그러고 나서 그는 나이팅게일 씨를 찾으러 떠났고, 그 착한 부인은 딸을 위로하러 돌아갔다. 딸은 어머니가 전해 준 말에 다소 기운을 차렸고, 두 사람은 함께 존스 씨에 대한 칭송을 거듭거듭 울려 퍼뜨렸다."]], [["The interview between Mr Jones and Mr Nightingale. The good or evil we confer on others very often, I believe, recoils on ourselves.", "우리가 남에게 베푸는 선이든 악이든, 그것은 흔히 우리 자신에게로 되돌아오는 법이라고 나는 믿는다."], ["For as men of a benign disposition enjoy their own acts of beneficence equally with those to whom they are done, so there are scarce any natures so entirely diabolical, as to be capable of doing injuries, without paying themselves some pangs for the ruin which they bring on their fellow-creatures.", "너그러운 성품을 지닌 사람들이 선행의 대상이 되는 이들 못지않게 자신의 선행을 즐기는 것과 마찬가지로, 자기 동료에게 끼친 파멸에 대해 스스로 아무런 아픔도 치르지 않은 채 남을 해칠 수 있을 만큼 완전히 악마 같은 천성을 지닌 사람은 거의 없기 때문이다."]], [["Mr Nightingale, at least, was not such a person.", "적어도 나이팅게일은 그런 사람이 아니었다."], ["On the contrary, Jones found him in his new lodgings, sitting melancholy by the fire, and silently lamenting the unhappy situation in which he had placed poor Nancy.", "오히려 존스는 그가 새 거처에서 난롯가에 우울하게 앉아, 가엾은 낸시를 자신이 몰아넣은 그 불행한 처지를 말없이 슬퍼하고 있는 모습을 발견했다. 그는 친구가 나타난 것을 보자마자 황급히 일어나 맞이했고, 한참 반가운 인사를 나눈 뒤 이렇게 말했다."], ["He no sooner saw his friend appear than he arose hastily to meet him; and after much congratulation said, “Nothing could be more opportune than this kind visit; for I was never more in the spleen in my life.”", "\"이렇게 고마운 방문이 이보다 더 때맞춰 올 수는 없었을 걸세. 평생 이렇게까지 울적했던 적이 없었거든.\""]], [["“I am sorry,”", "\"안타깝게도,\""], ["answered Jones, “that I bring news very unlikely to relieve you: nay, what I am convinced must, of all other, shock you the most.", "존스가 대답했다. \"내가 가져온 소식은 자네를 풀어 주기는커녕, 다른 무엇보다도 자네에게 가장 큰 충격을 줄 거라고 확신하는 그런 소식일세."], ["However, it is necessary you should know it.", "그래도 자네가 알아야 할 일이지."], ["Without further preface, then, I come to you, Mr Nightingale, from a worthy family, which you have involved in misery and ruin.”", "그러니 더 길게 서두를 것 없이 말하겠네, 나이팅게일. 나는 자네가 비참과 파멸로 몰아넣은 어느 훌륭한 가족을 대신해 자네를 찾아온 걸세.\""], ["Mr Nightingale changed colour at these words; but Jones, without regarding it, proceeded, in the liveliest manner, to paint the tragical story with which the reader was acquainted in the last chapter.", "나이팅게일은 이 말에 안색이 변했다. 그러나 존스는 그것을 아랑곳하지 않고, 독자가 앞 장에서 익히 알게 된 그 비극적인 이야기를 더없이 생생한 어조로 그려 나갔다."]], [["Nightingale never once interrupted the narration, though he discovered violent emotions at many parts of it.", "나이팅게일은 여러 대목에서 격렬한 동요를 드러내면서도 한 번도 이야기를 끊지 않았다. 그러나 이야기가 끝나자 깊은 한숨을 내쉰 뒤 이렇게 말했다."], ["But when it was concluded, after fetching a deep sigh, he said, “What you tell me, my friend, affects me in the tenderest manner.", "\"자네가 들려준 이야기는, 친구여, 내 마음 가장 여린 곳을 찌르는군. 그 가엾은 아이가 내 편지를 들킨 것보다 더 저주스러운 사고는 없었을 걸세."], ["Sure there never was so cursed an accident as the poor girl's betraying my letter.", "그것만 아니었다면 아이의 평판은 무사했을 테고, 이 일도 깊은 비밀로 남았을 텐데. 그랬다면 아이도 조금도 손해 보지 않고 시집갈 수 있었을 거야."], ["Her reputation might otherwise have been safe, and the affair might have remained a profound secret; and then the girl might have gone off never the worse; for many such things happen in this town: and if the husband should suspect a little, when it is too late, it will be his wiser conduct to conceal his suspicion both from his wife and the world.”", "이 도시에선 그런 일이 흔하거든. 설령 남편이 너무 늦게라도 조금 의심을 품는다 해도, 그 의심을 아내한테나 세상한테나 감추는 게 그로선 더 현명한 처신일 테고.\""]], [["“Indeed, my friend,”", "\"아닐세, 친구여,\""], ["answered Jones, “this could not have been the case with your poor Nancy.", "존스가 대답했다. \"자네의 가엾은 낸시는 그럴 수 없는 처지일세."], ["You have so entirely gained her affections, that it is the loss of you, and not of her reputation, which afflicts her, and will end in the destruction of her and her family.”", "자네가 그 아이의 마음을 너무도 완전히 사로잡아 버려서, 그 아이를 괴롭히는 건 평판을 잃는 것이 아니라 자네를 잃는 것이고, 그것이 결국 그 아이와 가족 모두의 파멸로 끝나고 말 거야.\""], ["“Nay, for that matter, I promise you,”", "\"아니, 그 점이라면 내 장담하지,\""], ["cries Nightingale, “she hath my affections so absolutely, that my wife, whoever she is to be, will have very little share in them.”", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"그 아이가 내 마음을 어찌나 송두리째 차지하고 있는지, 장차 내 아내가 될 사람이 누구든 거기서 가질 몫은 아주 보잘것없을 걸세.\""], ["“And is it possible then,”", "\"그렇다면 어떻게,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “you can think of deserting her?”", "\"그 아이를 저버릴 생각을 할 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["“Why, what can I do?”", "\"그럼 내가 어쩌겠나?\""], ["answered the other.", "상대가 대답했다."], ["“Ask Miss Nancy,”", "\"낸시 양에게 물어보게.\""], ["replied Jones warmly.", "존스가 열을 띠며 대꾸했다."], ["“In the condition to which you have reduced her, I sincerely think she ought to determine what reparation you shall make her.", "\"자네가 그 아이를 몰아넣은 처지를 생각하면, 나는 진심으로 자네가 어떤 보상을 해야 할지 그 아이가 정해야 한다고 보네."], ["Her interest alone, and not yours, ought to be your sole consideration.", "자네의 이익이 아니라 오직 그 아이의 이익만이 자네가 고려해야 할 전부일세."], ["But if you ask me what you shall do, what can you do less,”", "하지만 자네가 어떻게 해야 하느냐고 내게 묻는다면,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “than fulfil the expectations of her family, and her own?", "\"그 아이 가족의 기대와 그 아이 자신의 기대를 채워 주는 것보다 더 못한 일을 어떻게 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Nay, I sincerely tell you, they were mine too, ever since I first saw you together.", "아니, 솔직히 말하지. 자네 두 사람이 함께 있는 걸 처음 본 그 순간부터 그건 내 기대이기도 했네."], ["You will pardon me if I presume on the friendship you have favoured me with, moved as I am with compassion for those poor creatures.", "저 가엾은 사람들에게 측은한 마음이 동한 나머지, 자네가 베풀어 준 우정을 빌미로 주제넘게 구는 걸 용서해 주게."], ["But your own heart will best suggest to you, whether you have never intended, by your conduct, to persuade the mother, as well as the daughter, into an opinion, that you designed honourably: and if so, though there may have been no direct promise of marriage in the case, I will leave to your own good understanding, how far you are bound to proceed.”", "그러나 자네 행실로 어머니까지, 딸까지 자네가 떳떳한 뜻을 품고 있다고 믿게 만들 작정은 결코 없었는지, 그건 자네 마음이 가장 잘 일러 줄 걸세. 만약 그랬다면, 비록 이 일에 결혼을 직접 약속한 적은 없다 하더라도, 자네가 어디까지 책임을 다해야 하는지는 자네의 분별에 맡기겠네.\""]], [["“Nay, I must not only confess what you have hinted,”", "\"아니, 자네가 넌지시 비춘 것을 인정해야 할 뿐 아니라,\""], ["said Nightingale; “but I am afraid even that very promise you mention I have given.”", "나이팅게일이 말했다. \"자네가 말한 바로 그 약속마저 내가 한 것 같아 두렵네.\""], ["“And can you, after owning that,”", "\"그걸 시인하고서도,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “hesitate a moment?”", "\"자네가 한순간이라도 망설일 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["“Consider, my friend,”", "\"생각해 보게, 친구여,\" 상대가 대답했다."], ["answered the other; “I know you are a man of honour, and would advise no one to act contrary to its rules; if there were no other objection, can I, after this publication of her disgrace, think of such an alliance with honour?”", "\"나는 자네가 명예를 아는 사람이며, 누구에게도 명예의 규칙에 어긋나게 행동하라 권하지 않을 사람임을 아네. 다른 반대 이유가 전혀 없다 해도, 그 아이의 수치가 이렇게 세상에 알려진 마당에, 내가 그런 결합을 명예롭게 여기며 받아들일 수 있겠나?\""], ["“Undoubtedly,”", "\"물론이지,\""], ["replied Jones, “and the very best and truest honour, which is goodness, requires it of you.", "존스가 대답했다. \"그리고 가장 훌륭하고 참된 명예, 곧 선함이야말로 자네에게 그것을 요구하네."], ["As you mention a scruple of this kind, you will give me leave to examine it.", "자네가 이런 종류의 거리낌을 입에 올렸으니, 그것을 한번 따져 보게 해 주게."], ["Can you with honour be guilty of having under false pretences deceived a young woman and her family, and of having by these means treacherously robbed her of her innocence?", "거짓된 구실로 한 젊은 여인과 그 가족을 속이고, 그 수법으로 배신스럽게 그녀의 순결을 앗아 가고도 명예로울 수 있겠나?"], ["Can you, with honour, be the knowing, the wilful occasion, nay, the artful contriver of the ruin of a human being?", "한 인간의 파멸을, 알면서, 일부러, 아니 교묘히 꾸며 낸 장본인이고도 명예로울 수 있겠나?"], ["Can you, with honour, destroy the fame, the peace, nay, probably, both the life and soul too, of this creature?", "이 가여운 사람의 명예와 평안을, 아니 어쩌면 목숨과 영혼까지 무너뜨리고도 명예로울 수 있겠나?"], ["Can honour bear the thought, that this creature is a tender, helpless, defenceless, young woman?", "이 가여운 사람이 여리고, 의지할 데 없고, 무방비한 젊은 여인이라는 생각을 명예가 견딜 수 있겠나?"], ["A young woman, who loves, who doats on you, who dies for you; who hath placed the utmost confidence in your promises; and to that confidence hath sacrificed everything which is dear to her?", "자네를 사랑하고, 자네에게 빠져 있고, 자네 때문에 죽어 가며, 자네의 약속에 더할 나위 없는 신뢰를 걸고, 그 신뢰를 위해 자기에게 소중한 모든 것을 바친 젊은 여인 말일세."], ["Can honour support such contemplations as these a moment?”", "명예가 이런 생각들을 한순간이라도 떠받칠 수 있겠나?\""]], [["“Common sense, indeed,” said Nightingale, “warrants all you say; but yet you well know the opinion of the world is so contrary to it, that, was I to marry a whore, though my own, I should be ashamed of ever showing my face again.”", "\"상식으로 따지면,\" 나이팅게일이 말했다. \"자네 말이 다 옳네. 하지만 세상의 통념이 그와는 정반대라는 것 또한 자네가 잘 알지 않나. 내가 비록 내 손으로 그렇게 만든 여자라 해도 화냥년을 아내로 맞는다면, 다시는 얼굴을 들고 다니기가 부끄러울 걸세.\""]], [["“Fie upon it, Mr Nightingale!”", "\"이런, 나이팅게일!\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “do not call her by so ungenerous a name: when you promised to marry her she became your wife; and she hath sinned more against prudence than virtue.", "\"그 아이를 그렇게 야박한 이름으로 부르지 말게. 자네가 결혼을 약속한 순간 그 아이는 자네의 아내가 된 걸세."], ["And what is this world which you would be ashamed to face but the vile, the foolish, and the profligate?", "그리고 그 아이가 죄를 지었다면 그것은 덕에 대해서라기보다 분별에 대해서네."], ["Forgive me if I say such a shame must proceed from false modesty, which always attends false honour as its shadow.--But I am well assured there is not a man of real sense and goodness in the world who would not honour and applaud the action.", "게다가 자네가 얼굴 들기 부끄러워하는 그 세상이라는 게 비열하고 어리석고 방탕한 자들 말고 또 무엇이란 말인가? 내 말을 용서하게만, 그런 부끄러움이란 거짓 겸손에서 나오는 것이 틀림없고, 그 거짓 겸손은 거짓 명예를 그림자처럼 늘 따라다니는 법일세."], ["But, admit no other would, would not your own heart, my friend, applaud it?", "그러나 나는 단언하네. 이 세상에 참된 분별과 선함을 지닌 사람치고 자네의 그 행동을 기리고 갈채하지 않을 자는 없을 걸세."], ["And do not the warm, rapturous sensations, which we feel from the consciousness of an honest, noble, generous, benevolent action, convey more delight to the mind than the undeserved praise of millions?", "설령 다른 누구도 그러지 않는다 해도, 자네 자신의 마음만은, 친구여, 그것을 기리지 않겠나? 정직하고 고결하고 너그럽고 자애로운 행동을 했다는 의식에서 우러나는 그 뜨겁고 황홀한 감정이, 수백만 명의 받을 자격 없는 칭찬보다 우리 마음에 더 큰 기쁨을 가져다주지 않겠나?"], ["Set the alternative fairly before your eyes.", "두 갈래 길을 자네 눈앞에 공평하게 펼쳐 놓게."], ["On the one side, see this poor, unhappy, tender, believing girl, in the arms of her wretched mother, breathing her last.", "한쪽에는 이 가엾고 불행하고 여리고 잘 믿는 아이가 비참한 어머니의 품에 안겨 마지막 숨을 거두는 모습이 있네."], ["Hear her breaking heart in agonies, sighing out your name; and lamenting, rather than accusing, the cruelty which weighs her down to destruction.", "단장의 고통 속에 자네 이름을 한숨으로 토해 내며, 자기를 파멸로 짓누르는 그 잔인함을 원망하기보다 슬퍼하는 그 무너지는 가슴 소리를 들어 보게."], ["Paint to your imagination the circumstances of her fond despairing parent, driven to madness, or, perhaps, to death, by the loss of her lovely daughter.", "사랑스러운 딸을 잃고 미쳐 버리거나, 어쩌면 죽음에 이르고 마는 그 애틋하고 절망에 빠진 어머니의 처지를 자네 상상 속에 그려 보게."], ["View the poor, helpless, orphan infant; and when your mind hath dwelt a moment only on such ideas, consider yourself as the cause of all the ruin of this poor, little, worthy, defenceless family.", "가엾고 의지할 데 없는 고아 아기를 떠올려 보게. 그리고 그런 광경에 자네 마음이 단 한순간만이라도 머물거든, 이 가엾고 작고 갸륵하고 무방비한 가족이 송두리째 무너진 그 모든 원인이 바로 자네임을 헤아려 보게."], ["On the other side, consider yourself as relieving them from their temporary sufferings.", "다른 한쪽에는, 자네가 그들을 당장의 고통에서 건져 내는 모습을 떠올려 보게."], ["Think with what joy, with what transports that lovely creature will fly to your arms.", "그 사랑스러운 아이가 얼마나 큰 기쁨과 환희로 자네 품에 달려들지 생각해 보게."], ["See her blood returning to her pale cheeks, her fire to her languid eyes, and raptures to her tortured breast.", "핏기가 그 창백한 뺨에 다시 돌고, 생기가 그 시든 눈에 다시 살아나며, 황홀함이 그 괴로워하던 가슴에 차오르는 모습을 보게."], ["Consider the exultations of her mother, the happiness of all.", "그 어머니의 환희를, 모두의 행복을 헤아려 보게."], ["Think of this little family made by one act of yours completely happy.", "자네의 단 한 번의 행동으로 이 작은 가족이 더없이 행복해지는 광경을 그려 보게. 이 두 갈래 길을 떠올려 보게."], ["Think of this alternative, and sure I am mistaken in my friend if it requires any long deliberation whether he will sink these wretches down for ever, or, by one generous, noble resolution, raise them all from the brink of misery and despair to the highest pitch of human happiness.", "이 가엾은 이들을 영원히 가라앉혀 버릴지, 아니면 단 한 번의 너그럽고 고결한 결심으로 그들 모두를 비참과 절망의 벼랑 끝에서 인간 행복의 가장 높은 자리로 끌어올릴지, 그것을 정하는 데 오랜 숙고가 필요하다면 나는 내 친구를 잘못 본 것이 분명하네. 여기에 한 가지만 더 생각해 보태게."], ["Add to this but one consideration more; the consideration that it is your duty so to do--That the misery from which you will relieve these poor people is the misery which you yourself have wilfully brought upon them.”", "그렇게 하는 것이 자네의 의무라는 사실 말일세. 자네가 이 가엾은 사람들을 건져 줄 그 비참함이란, 바로 자네 자신이 그들에게 일부러 안겨 준 비참함이라는 사실 말일세.\""]], [["“O, my dear friend!”", "\"오, 사랑하는 친구여!\""], ["cries Nightingale, “I wanted not your eloquence to rouse me.", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"나를 일깨우는 데 자네의 그 웅변까지는 필요치 않았네."], ["I pity poor Nancy from my soul, and would willingly give anything in my power that no familiarities had ever passed between us.", "나는 가엾은 낸시를 진심으로 가엾이 여기며, 우리 사이에 그런 친밀한 일이 결코 없었던 일이 될 수만 있다면 내 힘닿는 무엇이라도 기꺼이 내놓겠네."], ["Nay, believe me, I had many struggles with my passion before I could prevail with myself to write that cruel letter, which hath caused all the misery in that unhappy family.", "아니, 내 말을 믿게. 저 불행한 가족에 온갖 비참을 불러온 그 모진 편지를 쓰자고 스스로를 다잡기까지, 나는 내 욕정과 숱하게 싸웠네."], ["If I had no inclinations to consult but my own, I would marry her to-morrow morning: I would, by heaven!", "만약 내가 헤아려야 할 마음이 오직 내 마음뿐이라면, 당장 내일 아침에라도 그 아이와 결혼하겠네. 하늘에 맹세코 그러겠어!"], ["but you will easily imagine how impossible it would be to prevail on my father to consent to such a match; besides, he hath provided another for me; and to-morrow, by his express command, I am to wait on the lady.”", "하지만 우리 아버지가 그런 혼사에 동의하시도록 설득한다는 게 얼마나 불가능한 일인지는 자네도 쉽게 짐작할 걸세. 게다가 아버지는 나를 위해 다른 짝을 정해 두셨고, 내일은 그분의 엄한 분부에 따라 그 아가씨를 찾아뵙기로 되어 있네.\""]], [["“I have not the honour to know your father,”", "\"나는 자네 부친을 알 영광을 갖지 못했네만,\""], ["said Jones; “but, suppose he could be persuaded, would you yourself consent to the only means of preserving these poor people?”", "존스가 말했다. \"혹 그분을 설득할 수 있다고 치면, 자네 자신은 이 가엾은 사람들을 구할 그 유일한 길에 동의하겠나?\""], ["“As eagerly as I would pursue my happiness,”", "\"내 행복을 좇듯 간절히 그러겠네,\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다."], ["answered Nightingale: “for I never shall find it in any other woman.--O, my dear friend!", "\"다른 어떤 여자한테서도 나는 결코 그 행복을 찾지 못할 테니까. 오, 사랑하는 친구여!"], ["could you imagine what I have felt within these twelve hours for my poor girl, I am convinced she would not engross all your pity.", "지난 열두 시간 동안 내가 그 가엾은 아이를 두고 어떤 심정이었는지 자네가 상상할 수만 있다면, 자네의 동정이 온통 그 아이한테만 쏠리지는 않으리라 확신하네. 내 마음은 오직 그 아이만을 향해 있어."], ["Passion leads me only to her; and, if I had any foolish scruples of honour, you have fully satisfied them: could my father be induced to comply with my desires, nothing would be wanting to compleat my own happiness or that of my Nancy.”", "그리고 내게 명예에 대한 어리석은 거리낌이 조금이라도 있었다면, 자네가 그것을 말끔히 풀어 주었네. 아버지를 내 뜻에 따르도록 이끌 수만 있다면, 내 자신의 행복도, 내 낸시의 행복도 부족할 것이 없을 걸세.\""]], [["“Then I am resolved to undertake it,”", "\"그렇다면 내가 그 일을 맡기로 마음먹었네,\""], ["said Jones.", "존스가 말했다."], ["“You must not be angry with me, in whatever light it may be necessary to set this affair, which, you may depend on it, could not otherwise be long hid from him: for things of this nature make a quick progress when once they get abroad, as this unhappily hath already.", "\"이 일을 어떤 식으로 펼쳐 보일 필요가 생기든, 나한테 화를 내선 안 되네. 장담컨대 그렇게라도 하지 않으면 이 일이 그분께 오래도록 감춰질 수는 없으니까."], ["Besides, should any fatal accident follow, as upon my soul I am afraid will, unless immediately prevented, the public would ring of your name in a manner which, if your father hath common humanity, must offend him.", "이런 종류의 일은 한번 밖으로 새어 나가면 순식간에 퍼지는 법이고, 안타깝게도 이미 그렇게 되어 버렸네. 게다가 당장 막지 않으면, 내 영혼을 걸고 두렵네만, 어떤 끔찍한 사고라도 뒤따를 경우 세상이 자네 이름을 입에 올려 떠들어 댈 텐데, 자네 부친께 사람으로서의 보통 인정이라도 있다면 그것이 그분을 분명 노엽게 할 걸세."], ["If you will therefore tell me where I may find the old gentleman, I will not lose a moment in the business; which, while I pursue, you cannot do a more generous action than by paying a visit to the poor girl.", "그러니 그 노신사를 어디서 뵐 수 있는지 일러 주면, 나는 한순간도 지체 않고 이 일에 나서겠네. 내가 그 일을 좇는 동안, 자네는 그 가엾은 아이를 찾아가 주는 것보다 더 너그러운 일을 할 수 없을 걸세."], ["You will find I have not exaggerated in the account I have given of the wretchedness of the family.”", "그 가족의 비참함에 대해 내가 들려준 이야기에 조금도 과장이 없었음을 자네도 알게 될 거야.\""]], [["Nightingale immediately consented to the proposal; and now, having acquainted Jones with his father's lodging, and the coffee-house where he would most probably find him, he hesitated a moment, and then said, “My dear Tom, you are going to undertake an impossibility. If you knew my father you would never think of obtaining his consent.----Stay, there is one way--suppose you told him I was already married, it might be easier to reconcile him to the fact after it was done; and, upon my honour, I am so affected with what you have said, and I love my Nancy so passionately, I almost wish it was done, whatever might be the consequence.”", "나이팅게일은 그 제안에 즉시 동의했다. 그러고는 존스에게 아버지의 거처와, 그를 가장 만나기 쉬울 커피점을 일러 준 뒤, 한순간 머뭇거리다가 이렇게 말했다. \"이보게 톰, 자네는 불가능한 일을 맡으려는 걸세. 우리 아버지를 알면 그분의 동의를 얻겠다는 생각은 아예 하지도 않을 거야. 잠깐, 한 가지 방법이 있네. 자네가 내가 이미 결혼했다고 그분께 말하면 어떻겠나. 일이 다 벌어진 뒤라면 그 사실을 받아들이도록 화해시키기가 더 쉬울지도 모르지. 그리고 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 나는 자네가 한 말에 어찌나 마음이 움직였는지, 그리고 내 낸시를 어찌나 뜨겁게 사랑하는지, 결과야 어찌 되든 거의 그 일이 다 벌어져 버렸으면 싶을 지경이네.\""]], [["Jones greatly approved the hint, and promised to pursue it.", "존스는 그 묘안을 크게 반기며 그대로 따르겠노라 약속했다."], ["They then separated, Nightingale to visit his Nancy, and Jones in quest of the old gentleman.", "그런 다음 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 나이팅게일은 자신의 낸시를 찾아가러, 존스는 그 노신사를 찾으러."]], [["# Chapter viii.", "# Chapter viii."]], [["What passed between Jones and old Mr Nightingale; with the arrival of a person not yet mentioned in this history.", "8장. 존스와 늙은 나이팅게일 씨 사이에 오간 일과, 이 이야기에 아직 등장하지 않은 한 인물의 도래에 관하여."]], [["Notwithstanding the sentiment of the Roman satirist, which denies the divinity of fortune, and the opinion of Seneca to the same purpose; Cicero, who was, I believe, a wiser man than either of them, expressly holds the contrary; and certain it is, there are some incidents in life so very strange and unaccountable, that it seems to require more than human skill and foresight in producing them.", "운명의 신성함을 부정한 로마 풍자시인의 견해, 그리고 같은 취지의 세네카의 의견에도 불구하고, 내 생각에 두 사람보다 더 현명한 인물이었던 키케로는 정반대의 입장을 분명히 견지한다. 또한 인생에는 너무도 기이하고 설명할 길 없는 사건들이 있어서, 그것을 빚어내는 데에 인간의 솜씨와 예지 이상의 무언가가 필요해 보인다는 것만큼은 분명한 사실이다."]], [["Of this kind was what now happened to Jones, who found Mr Nightingale the elder in so critical a minute, that Fortune, if she was really worthy all the worship she received at Rome, could not have contrived such another.", "이번에 존스에게 일어난 일이 바로 그런 부류였다. 그는 늙은 나이팅게일 씨를 너무도 절묘한 순간에 만났으니, 만일 운명이 정말로 로마에서 받았던 그 모든 숭배를 받을 자격이 있었다 한들, 이런 또 하나의 상황을 꾸며낼 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["In short, the old gentleman, and the father of the young lady whom he intended for his son, had been hard at it for many hours; and the latter was just now gone, and had left the former delighted with the thoughts that he had succeeded in a long contention, which had been between the two fathers of the future bride and bridegroom; in which both endeavoured to overreach the other, and, as it not rarely happens in such cases, both had retreated fully satisfied of having obtained the victory.", "요컨대 이 노신사와, 그가 아들의 짝으로 점찍어 둔 젊은 처녀의 아버지는 여러 시간 동안 격렬하게 흥정을 벌였고, 후자는 방금 막 자리를 떴으며, 전자는 오랜 다툼에서 자신이 이겼다는 생각에 흡족해하며 남아 있었다. 그 다툼이란 장차 신부와 신랑이 될 두 집안의 두 아버지 사이에 벌어진 것으로, 둘 다 상대를 한 수 앞지르려 애썼고, 그런 경우 드물지 않게 일어나듯 양쪽 모두 승리를 거두었다고 더없이 만족하며 물러난 터였다."]], [["This gentleman, whom Mr Jones now visited, was what they call a man of the world; that is to say, a man who directs his conduct in this world as one who, being fully persuaded there is no other, is resolved to make the most of this. In his early years he had been bred to trade; but, having acquired a very good fortune, he had lately declined his business; or, to speak more properly, had changed it from dealing in goods, to dealing only in money, of which he had always a plentiful fund at command, and of which he knew very well how to make a very plentiful advantage, sometimes of the necessities of private men, and sometimes of those of the public. He had indeed conversed so entirely with money, that it may be almost doubted whether he imagined there was any other thing really existing in the world; this at least may be certainly averred, that he firmly believed nothing else to have any real value.", "존스 씨가 이번에 방문한 이 신사는 사람들이 흔히 말하는 세상물정에 밝은 사람이었다. 다시 말해 이 세상 말고 다른 세상은 없다고 철석같이 믿고서, 이 세상을 최대한 활용하기로 작정한 사람처럼 처신하는 인물이었다. 젊은 시절 그는 장사를 배웠으나, 꽤 넉넉한 재산을 모은 뒤로는 최근 사업에서 손을 떼었다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하자면 물건을 거래하던 일을 오로지 돈만 거래하는 일로 바꾼 것이다. 그는 늘 손안에 풍족한 자금을 굴렸고, 그것으로 풍족한 이득을 챙기는 법을 아주 잘 알았다. 때로는 개인의 곤궁을 이용했고, 때로는 공공의 곤궁을 이용했다. 실로 그는 오로지 돈하고만 대화하며 살아온 터라, 이 세상에 정말로 존재하는 다른 무엇이 있다고 그가 상상이나 했을지 의심스러울 지경이다. 적어도 한 가지만큼은 확실히 단언할 수 있으니, 그는 돈 말고는 그 무엇에도 진정한 가치가 있다고는 굳게 믿지 않았다는 점이다."]], [["The reader will, I fancy, allow that Fortune could not have culled out a more improper person for Mr Jones to attack with any probability of success; nor could the whimsical lady have directed this attack at a more unseasonable time.", "독자께서는, 운명이 존스 씨가 성공의 가망을 품고 들이대기에 이보다 더 부적절한 인물을 골라낼 수 없었으며, 이 변덕스러운 여신이 이 공략을 이보다 더 때 나쁜 시기로 잡을 수도 없었으리라는 데 동의하시리라 생각한다."]], [["As money then was always uppermost in this gentleman's thoughts, so the moment he saw a stranger within his doors it immediately occurred to his imagination, that such stranger was either come to bring him money, or to fetch it from him.", "이 신사의 머릿속에는 늘 돈이 가장 위에 있었던 만큼, 자기 집 문 안에 낯선 사람이 들어선 순간 곧바로 그의 상상에 떠오르는 생각은, 그 낯선 자가 돈을 가져다주러 왔거나 아니면 돈을 받아 가려고 왔다는 것이었다."], ["And according as one or other of these thoughts prevailed, he conceived a favourable or unfavourable idea of the person who approached him.", "그리고 이 두 생각 가운데 어느 쪽이 우세하느냐에 따라, 그는 다가오는 인물에 대해 호의적인 인상 또는 비호의적인 인상을 품었다."]], [["Unluckily for Jones, the latter of these was the ascendant at present; for as a young gentleman had visited him the day before, with a bill from his son for a play debt, he apprehended, at the first sight of Jones, that he was come on such another errand.", "존스에게는 불운하게도, 지금은 후자의 생각이 우세한 참이었다. 바로 전날 어떤 젊은 신사가 그의 아들이 진 노름빚 청구서를 들고 찾아왔던 터라, 그는 존스를 처음 본 순간 또 그런 용건으로 온 줄 알고 지레 짐작했다."], ["Jones therefore had no sooner told him that he was come on his son's account than the old gentleman, being confirmed in his suspicion, burst forth into an exclamation, “That he would lose his labour.”", "그리하여 존스가 아들 일로 찾아왔다고 말하기가 무섭게, 노신사는 자신의 의심이 확인된 양 버럭 소리를 질렀다. \"헛수고하러 오셨구먼.\""], ["“Is it then possible, sir,”", "\"그렇다면, 선생님,\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “that you can guess my business?”", "\"제 용건을 짐작하실 수 있단 말씀입니까?\" \"내가 짐작한다면 말이지,\""], ["“If I do guess it,”", "상대가 대꾸했다."], ["replied the other, “I repeat again to you, you will lose your labour.", "\"다시 한 번 말하지만, 헛수고하시는 거요. 뭐, 짐작건대 당신도 내 아들을 그 모든 난동과 방탕의 자리로 끌어들여 결국 그 애를 파멸시킬 그 한량들 중 하나겠지."], ["What, I suppose you are one of those sparks who lead my son into all those scenes of riot and debauchery, which will be his destruction?", "하지만 분명히 말해 두는데, 나는 그 애 빚을 더는 한 푼도 갚지 않을 거요. 앞으로 그 애가 그런 무리와는 완전히 손을 끊기를 바라오."], ["but I shall pay no more of his bills, I promise you.", "그렇지 않을 거라 짐작했더라면 그 애 아내를 마련해 주지도 않았을 테니까. 나는 누구의 파멸에도 손을 보태고 싶지 않으니 말이오.\""], ["I expect he will quit all such company for the future.", "\"어떻게 된 일입니까, 선생님,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["If I had imagined otherwise, I should not have provided a wife for him; for I would be instrumental in the ruin of nobody.”", "\"그러면 그 아가씨는 선생님께서 마련해 주신 짝입니까?\" \"그게 말이오, 선생,\""], ["“How, sir,”", "노신사가 대답했다."], ["said Jones, “and was this lady of your providing?”", "\"어째서 그게 당신이 상관할 일이 된단 말이오?\" \"아니, 선생님,\""], ["“Pray, sir,”", "존스가 받았다."], ["answered the old gentleman, “how comes it to be any concern of yours?”--“Nay, dear sir,”", "\"제가 그토록 크게 존경하고 아끼는 분, 곧 선생님 아드님의 행복에 관한 일에 관심을 두는 것을 노여워하지 말아 주십시오. 바로 그 일 때문에 선생님을 뵈러 온 것입니다."], ["replied Jones, “be not offended that I interest myself in what regards your son's happiness, for whom I have so great an honour and value.", "방금 하신 말씀이 제게 얼마나 큰 만족을 주었는지 이루 다 말할 수 없습니다. 정말이지 선생님 아드님은 제가 더없이 높이 존경하는 분입니다."], ["It was upon that very account I came to wait upon you.", "아니, 선생님, 선생님께 품은 제 존경 또한 쉬이 표현하기 어렵습니다."], ["I can't express the satisfaction you have given me by what you say; for I do assure you your son is a person for whom I have the highest honour.--Nay, sir, it is not easy to express the esteem I have for you; who could be so generous, so good, so kind, so indulgent to provide such a match for your son; a woman, who, I dare swear, will make him one of the happiest men upon earth.”", "어쩌면 그토록 너그럽고, 그토록 선량하며, 그토록 다정하고, 그토록 아낌없이 아드님께 이런 짝을 마련해 주실 수 있단 말입니까. 제가 감히 맹세컨대, 그 여인은 아드님을 이 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람 중 하나로 만들어 줄 것입니다.\""]], [["There is scarce anything which so happily introduces men to our good liking, as having conceived some alarm at their first appearance; when once those apprehensions begin to vanish we soon forget the fears which they occasioned, and look on ourselves as indebted for our present ease to those very persons who at first raised our fears.", "사람을 우리 호감 속으로 그토록 행복하게 끌어들이는 것은, 처음 마주쳤을 때 그에게서 어떤 경계심을 품었던 경우만 한 게 거의 없다. 일단 그 불안이 사라지기 시작하면 우리는 곧 그것이 불러일으킨 두려움을 잊고, 처음에 우리의 두려움을 자아냈던 바로 그 사람들에게 지금의 안도를 빚지고 있다고 여기게 된다."]], [["Thus it happened to Nightingale, who no sooner found that Jones had no demand on him, as he suspected, than he began to be pleased with his presence.", "나이팅게일에게도 바로 그런 일이 벌어졌다. 그는 존스가 자신이 의심했던 것처럼 자신에게 받아낼 게 없다는 것을 알아차리자마자 그의 존재가 반갑게 느껴지기 시작했다."], ["“Pray, good sir,”", "\"자, 어서,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “be pleased to sit down.", "\"앉으시구려. 전에 당신을 뵌 적은 없는 것 같소만, 당신이 내 아들의 친구이고 이 아가씨에 관해 할 말이 있다면 기꺼이 듣겠소."], ["I do not remember to have ever had the pleasure of seeing you before; but if you are a friend of my son, and have anything to say concerning this young lady, I shall be glad to hear you.", "그 아가씨가 그 애를 행복하게 해 줄지 어떨지로 말하자면, 그렇게 못 된다면 그건 순전히 그 애 잘못일 거요. 나는 가장 중요한 항목을 챙겨 둠으로써 내 도리를 다했소."], ["As to her making him happy, it will be his own fault if she doth not.", "그 아가씨가 가져올 재산은 분별 있고 신중하며 절제할 줄 아는 사람이라면 누구라도 행복하게 만들기에 충분하니 말이오.\" \"틀림없습니다,\""], ["I have discharged my duty, in taking care of the main article.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"그 아가씨 자체가 하나의 재산이니까요."], ["She will bring him a fortune capable of making any reasonable, prudent, sober man, happy.”", "그토록 아름답고, 그토록 기품 있고, 그토록 상냥하며, 그토록 잘 교육받았으니, 정말이지 더없이 교양 있는 아가씨입니다. 노래도 감탄스러우리만치 잘 부르고, 하프시코드를 다루는 손길도 더없이 섬세합니다.\""], ["“Undoubtedly,”", "\"그런 건 하나도 몰랐소이다,\" 노신사가 대답했다."], ["cries Jones, “for she is in herself a fortune; so beautiful, so genteel, so sweet-tempered, and so well-educated; she is indeed a most accomplished young lady; sings admirably well, and hath a most delicate hand at the harpsichord.”", "\"그 아가씨를 본 적이 없으니 말이오. 하지만 당신이 들려준 이야기 때문에 그 아가씨가 더 마음에 안 드는 것은 아니오."], ["“I did not know any of these matters,”", "그리고 그 아버지가 우리 흥정에서 그런 자질들을 조금도 내세우지 않은 점이 오히려 더 마음에 들었소. 나는 늘 그것을 그가 사리에 밝다는 증거로 여길 거요."], ["answered the old gentleman, “for I never saw the lady: but I do not like her the worse for what you tell me; and I am the better pleased with her father for not laying any stress on these qualifications in our bargain.", "어리석은 자였다면 그런 항목들을 재산에 보태는 것으로 들먹였을 테지만, 공정하게 말하자면 그는 그런 따위는 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았소. 하기야 그런 게 여자에게 흠이 되는 일이야 분명 아니지만 말이오.\""], ["I shall always think it a proof of his understanding.", "\"정말이지, 선생님,\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["A silly fellow would have brought in these articles as an addition to her fortune; but, to give him his due, he never mentioned any such matter; though to be sure they are no disparagements to a woman.”", "\"그 아가씨는 그 모든 자질을 더없이 빼어난 정도로 갖추고 있습니다. 솔직히 저는 선생님께서 혹시 좀 미적지근하시지 않을까, 이 혼사에 좀 덜 마음이 기우시지 않을까 걱정했습니다."], ["“I do assure you, sir,”", "아드님이 제게 말하기를 선생님께서 그 아가씨를 본 적이 없다고 하셨거든요."], ["cries Jones, “she hath them all in the most eminent degree: for my part, I own I was afraid you might have been a little backward, a little less inclined to the match; for your son told me you had never seen the lady; therefore I came, sir, in that case, to entreat you, to conjure you, as you value the happiness of your son, not to be averse to his match with a woman who hath not only all the good qualities I have mentioned, but many more.”--“If that was your business, sir,”", "그래서 선생님, 그런 경우라면 제가 간청드리러, 아니 호소드리러 온 것이었습니다."], ["said the old gentleman, “we are both obliged to you; and you may be perfectly easy; for I give you my word I was very well satisfied with her fortune.”", "아드님의 행복을 소중히 여기신다면, 제가 말씀드린 그 모든 미덕뿐 아니라 그 이상을 갖춘 여인과의 혼사를 부디 마다하지 말아 달라고 말입니다.\" \"그게 당신 용건이었다면, 선생,\""], ["“Sir,”", "노신사가 말했다."], ["answered Jones, “I honour you every moment more and more.", "\"우리 둘 다 당신에게 신세를 진 셈이오. 그리고 마음 푹 놓으시오."], ["To be so easily satisfied, so very moderate on that account, is a proof of the soundness of your understanding, as well as the nobleness of your mind.”----“Not so very moderate, young gentleman, not so very moderate,”", "내 장담컨대 나는 그 아가씨의 재산에 아주 흡족했으니까.\" \"선생님,\""], ["answered the father.--“Still more and more noble,”", "존스가 대답했다. \"저는 매 순간 선생님을 점점 더 존경하게 됩니다."], ["replied Jones; “and give me leave to add, sensible: for sure it is little less than madness to consider money as the sole foundation of happiness.", "그토록 쉽게 만족하시고, 그 점에 대해 그토록 절도를 지키시는 것은 선생님의 분별이 건전하다는 증거인 동시에 마음이 고귀하다는 증거입니다.\" \"그리 절도 있는 건 아니오, 젊은이, 그리 절도 있는 건 아니라니까,\""], ["Such a woman as this with her little, her nothing of a fortune”--“I find,”", "아버지가 대답했다. \"점점 더 고귀하십니다,\""], ["cries the old gentleman, “you have a pretty just opinion of money, my friend, or else you are better acquainted with the person of the lady than with her circumstances.", "존스가 받았다. \"그리고 한마디 덧붙이자면, 사려 깊으시기도 합니다."], ["Why, pray, what fortune do you imagine this lady to have?”", "돈을 행복의 유일한 토대로 여기는 것이야말로 거의 광기에 가까운 일이니까요. 이런 여인이라면, 그 보잘것없는, 그 있으나 마나 한 재산을 가졌다 해도—\""], ["“What fortune?”", "\"보아하니,\" 노신사가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “why, too contemptible a one to be named for your son.”--“Well, well, well,”", "\"여보게, 자네는 돈에 대해 꽤 올바른 생각을 가졌거나, 아니면 그 아가씨의 형편보다 사람됨을 더 잘 아는 모양이군. 그래, 묻겠는데, 자네는 그 아가씨가 재산을 얼마나 가졌다고 생각하나?\""], ["said the other, “perhaps he might have done better.”--“That I deny,”", "\"재산이요?\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["said Jones, “for she is one of the best of women.”--“Ay, ay, but in point of fortune I mean,”", "\"글쎄요, 선생님 아드님 짝으로 입에 올리기엔 너무도 하찮은 액수지요.\" \"글쎄, 글쎄, 글쎄,\""], ["answered the other.", "상대가 말했다. \"어쩌면 그 애가 더 나은 짝을 찾을 수도 있었겠지.\""], ["“And yet, as to that now, how much do you imagine your friend is to have?”--“How much?”", "\"그건 부정합니다,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["cries Jones, “how much?", "\"그 아가씨는 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 여인 중 하나니까요.\" \"그래, 그래, 하지만 내 말은 재산 면에서 말이오,\""], ["Why, at the utmost, perhaps £200.”", "상대가 대답했다. \"그건 그렇고, 자, 자네 친구가 얼마나 가졌으리라 생각하나?\""], ["“Do you mean to banter me, young gentleman?”", "\"얼마라뇨?\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["said the father, a little angry.", "\"얼마요? 글쎄, 많아 봐야 아마 이백 파운드쯤이겠지요.\""], ["“No, upon my soul,”", "\"지금 나를 놀리는 게요, 젊은이?\" 아버지가 약간 화를 내며 말했다."], ["answered Jones, “I am in earnest: nay, I believe I have gone to the utmost farthing.", "\"아닙니다, 맹세코,\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["If I do the lady an injury, I ask her pardon.”", "\"진심입니다. 아니, 오히려 한 푼까지 다 쳐서 최대한으로 말씀드린 것 같습니다."], ["“Indeed you do,”", "제가 그 아가씨에게 누를 끼쳤다면 용서를 빌겠습니다.\" \"정말이지 누를 끼쳤소,\""], ["cries the father; “I am certain she hath fifty times that sum, and she shall produce fifty to that before I consent that she shall marry my son.”", "아버지가 외쳤다. \"내 분명히 아는데 그 아가씨는 그 액수의 쉰 배는 가졌고, 내 아들과 결혼하도록 내가 동의하기 전에 거기에 또 쉰 배를 내놓아야 할 거요.\""], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “it is too late to talk of consent now; if she had not fifty farthings your son is married.”--“My son married!”", "\"이제 와서 동의를 운운하기엔 너무 늦었습니다. 설령 그 아가씨가 쉰 파딩도 없다 한들, 선생님 아드님은 이미 결혼했으니까요.\""], ["answered the old gentleman, with surprize.", "\"내 아들이 결혼을 해?\" 노신사가 놀라며 대답했다."], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Jones, “I thought you was unacquainted with it.”", "존스가 말했다. \"저는 선생님께서 알고 계신 줄 알았습니다.\""], ["“My son married to Miss Harris!”", "\"내 아들이 해리스 양과 결혼을 해?\" 그가 다시 말했다."], ["answered he again.", "\"해리스 양이라뇨?\" 존스가 말했다."], ["“To Miss Harris!”", "\"아닙니다, 선생님. 낸시 밀러 양과 결혼했습니다."], ["said Jones; “no, sir; to Miss Nancy Miller, the daughter of Mrs Miller, at whose house he lodged; a young lady, who, though her mother is reduced to let lodgings--“--“Are you bantering, or are you in earnest?”", "밀러 부인의 딸인데, 아드님이 그 댁에 하숙하고 있었지요. 그 아가씨는, 비록 그 어머니가 형편이 기울어 하숙을 치기는 하지만—\""], ["cries the father, with a most solemn voice.", "\"지금 나를 놀리는 게요, 아니면 진심이오?\" 아버지가 더없이 엄숙한 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 선생님,\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “I scorn the character of a banterer.", "\"저는 남을 놀리는 자라는 평판을 경멸합니다. 저는 더없이 진지하게 진심으로 선생님을 찾아온 것입니다."], ["I came to you in most serious earnest, imagining, as I find true, that your son had never dared acquaint you with a match so much inferior to him in point of fortune, though the reputation of the lady will suffer it no longer to remain a secret.”", "제 짐작이—그리고 그게 사실임을 알게 되었습니다만—아드님이 재산 면에서 자기보다 훨씬 못한 짝을 감히 선생님께 알리지 못했을 거라 여겼거든요. 다만 그 아가씨의 평판 때문에 이 일은 더 이상 비밀로 남아 있을 수 없게 되었습니다.\""]], [["While the father stood like one struck suddenly dumb at this news, a gentleman came into the room, and saluted him by the name of brother.", "아버지가 이 소식에 갑자기 벙어리라도 된 듯 멍하니 서 있는 동안, 한 신사가 방으로 들어와 그를 형제라 부르며 인사했다."]], [["But though these two were in consanguinity so nearly related, they were in their dispositions almost the opposites to each other.", "하지만 이 두 사람은 혈연으로는 그토록 가까이 맺어졌어도, 기질에서는 거의 정반대였다."], ["The brother who now arrived had likewise been bred to trade, in which he no sooner saw himself worth £6000 than he purchased a small estate with the greatest part of it, and retired into the country; where he married the daughter of an unbeneficed clergyman; a young lady, who, though she had neither beauty nor fortune, had recommended herself to his choice entirely by her good humour, of which she possessed a very large share.", "방금 도착한 형제 역시 장사를 배워 자랐는데, 자기 재산이 육천 파운드에 이른 것을 보자마자 그 대부분으로 작은 영지를 사들이고 시골로 물러났다. 그곳에서 그는 봉록 없는 한 성직자의 딸과 결혼했는데, 그 아가씨는 미모도 재산도 없었으나, 자기가 매우 넉넉히 지닌 그 좋은 성품 하나만으로 온전히 그의 선택을 받기에 이르렀다."]], [["With this woman he had, during twenty-five years, lived a life more resembling the model which certain poets ascribe to the golden age, than any of those patterns which are furnished by the present times.", "그는 이 여인과 더불어 이십오 년 동안, 오늘날의 풍속이 보여 주는 어떤 본보기보다도 어떤 시인들이 황금시대에 돌렸던 그 모형에 더 가까운 삶을 살았다. 그는 그녀에게서 네 아이를 두었으나, 딸 하나만 빼고는 누구도 성년에 이르지 못했다."], ["By her he had four children, but none of them arrived at maturity, except only one daughter, whom, in vulgar language, he and his wife had spoiled; that is, had educated with the utmost tenderness and fondness, which she returned to such a degree, that she had actually refused a very extraordinary match with a gentleman a little turned of forty, because she could not bring herself to part with her parents.", "그 딸을, 속된 말로 하자면 그와 그의 아내는 응석받이로 키웠다. 다시 말해 더없는 다정함과 애정으로 길렀고, 딸 또한 그 사랑에 어찌나 크게 보답했던지, 부모와 떨어지는 것을 차마 견딜 수 없다는 이유로 마흔을 조금 넘긴 어느 신사와의 더없이 좋은 혼처를 실제로 거절하기까지 했다."]], [["The young lady whom Mr Nightingale had intended for his son was a near neighbour of his brother, and an acquaintance of his niece; and in reality it was upon the account of his projected match that he was now come to town; not, indeed, to forward, but to dissuade his brother from a purpose which he conceived would inevitably ruin his nephew; for he foresaw no other event from a union with Miss Harris, notwithstanding the largeness of her fortune, as neither her person nor mind seemed to him to promise any kind of matrimonial felicity: for she was very tall, very thin, very ugly, very affected, very silly, and very ill-natured.", "나이팅게일 씨가 아들의 짝으로 점찍어 둔 그 처녀는 그의 형제와 가까운 이웃이자 그의 조카딸과 아는 사이였다. 사실 그가 지금 이 도시에 온 것도 바로 그 추진 중인 혼사 때문이었다. 다만 혼사를 거들기 위해서가 아니라, 자기 조카를 틀림없이 파멸시키리라 여기는 그 계획에서 형제를 만류하기 위해서였다. 그는 해리스 양과의 결합에서, 그녀의 막대한 재산에도 불구하고 다른 어떤 결말도 내다볼 수 없었다. 그녀의 외모도 마음씨도 그가 보기엔 그 어떤 부부의 행복도 약속해 주지 못할 것 같았기 때문이다. 그녀는 키가 무척 컸고, 무척 말랐으며, 무척 못생겼고, 무척 가식적이며, 무척 어리석고, 또 무척 심술궂었다."]], [["His brother, therefore, no sooner mentioned the marriage of his nephew with Miss Miller, than he exprest the utmost satisfaction; and when the father had very bitterly reviled his son, and pronounced sentence of beggary upon him, the uncle began in the following manner:", "그러므로 그의 형제가 조카가 밀러 양과 결혼했다고 입에 올리기가 무섭게, 그는 더없는 만족을 드러냈다. 그리고 아버지가 아들을 호되게 욕하고 거지 신세가 될 거라며 선고를 내리자, 삼촌은 다음과 같이 입을 열었다."]], [["“If you was a little cooler, brother, I would ask you whether you love your son for his sake or for your own. You would answer, I suppose, and so I suppose you think, for his sake; and doubtless it is his happiness which you intended in the marriage you proposed for him.", "\"형님, 좀 진정하셨다면 저는 형님께 묻고 싶습니다. 형님이 아드님을 사랑하시는 건 그 애를 위해서입니까, 아니면 형님 자신을 위해서입니까? 그 애를 위해서라고 대답하시겠지요. 또 그렇게 여기고 계시리라 짐작합니다. 그리고 의심할 바 없이, 형님이 그 애에게 권하신 혼사에서 의도하신 것도 그 애의 행복이었을 겁니다."]], [["“Now, brother, to prescribe rules of happiness to others hath always appeared to me very absurd, and to insist on doing this, very tyrannical.", "자, 형님, 남에게 행복의 규칙을 정해 주는 것은 제게 늘 무척 터무니없어 보였고, 그것을 기어이 강요하는 것은 무척 폭압적으로 여겨졌습니다."], ["It is a vulgar error, I know; but it is, nevertheless, an error.", "흔한 착각이라는 건 압니다. 하지만 그래도 그것은 착각입니다."], ["And if this be absurd in other things, it is mostly so in the affair of marriage, the happiness of which depends entirely on the affection which subsists between the parties.", "그리고 다른 일에서도 그것이 터무니없다면, 결혼이라는 일에서는 더더욱 그렇습니다. 결혼의 행복은 두 당사자 사이에 깃든 애정에 전적으로 달려 있으니까요."]], [["“I have therefore always thought it unreasonable in parents to desire to chuse for their children on this occasion; since to force affection is an impossible attempt; nay, so much doth love abhor force, that I know not whether, through an unfortunate but uncurable perverseness in our natures, it may not be even impatient of persuasion.", "그래서 저는 부모가 이런 일에서 자식을 대신해 짝을 고르려 드는 것이 늘 이치에 맞지 않다고 생각해 왔습니다. 애정을 억지로 강요하는 것은 불가능한 시도이니까요. 아니, 사랑이란 강요를 어찌나 질색하는지, 우리 본성에 깃든 어떤 불행하지만 고칠 수 없는 비뚤어짐 탓에, 사랑이 설득조차 견디지 못하는 건 아닌지 저로서는 모르겠습니다."]], [["“It is, however, true that, though a parent will not, I think, wisely prescribe, he ought to be consulted on this occasion; and, in strictness, perhaps, should at least have a negative voice.", "하지만 부모가 현명하게 처신한다면 짝을 정해 주지는 않을지언정, 이런 일에서 의논 상대로는 마땅히 삼아야 한다는 것은 사실입니다. 그리고 엄밀히 말하자면, 아마 적어도 거부할 권리쯤은 가져야 할 것입니다."], ["My nephew, therefore, I own, in marrying, without asking your advice, hath been guilty of a fault.", "그러니 제 조카가 형님의 조언을 구하지 않고 결혼한 것은, 인정컨대 잘못을 저지른 셈입니다."], ["But, honestly speaking, brother, have you not a little promoted this fault?", "하지만 솔직히 말해서, 형님, 형님께서 이 잘못을 조금은 부추기신 게 아닐까요?"], ["Have not your frequent declarations on this subject given him a moral certainty of your refusal, where there was any deficiency in point of fortune?", "이 문제에 관해 형님이 거듭 단언하신 말씀들이, 재산 면에서 조금이라도 부족함이 있는 경우엔 형님이 반드시 거절하시리라는 것을 그 애에게 거의 확신시켜 준 게 아닐까요? 아니, 지금 형님의 노여움도 오로지 그 부족함에서 비롯된 게 아닙니까?"], ["Nay, doth not your present anger arise solely from that deficiency?", "그리고 그 애가 여기서 자기 도리를 다하지 못했다면, 형님 역시 권한을 그만큼 넘어선 게 아닙니까?"], ["And if he hath failed in his duty here, did you not as much exceed that authority when you absolutely bargained with him for a woman, without his knowledge, whom you yourself never saw, and whom, if you had seen and known as well as I, it must have been madness in you to have ever thought of bringing her into your family?", "형님 자신도 본 적 없는 여자를, 그 애도 모르게 그 애를 두고 흥정까지 벌이셨으니 말입니다. 게다가 그 여자를 형님이 저만큼 보고 알았더라면, 그 여자를 가문에 들일 생각을 한 것 자체가 형님으로선 미친 짓이었을 겁니다."]], [["“Still I own my nephew in a fault; but surely it is not an unpardonable fault.", "그래도 제 조카가 잘못을 저지른 건 인정합니다. 하지만 그것이 결코 용서 못 할 잘못은 아니지요."], ["He hath acted indeed without your consent, in a matter in which he ought to have asked it, but it is in a matter in which his interest is principally concerned; you yourself must and will acknowledge that you consulted his interest only, and if he unfortunately differed from you, and hath been mistaken in his notion of happiness, will you, brother, if you love your son, carry him still wider from the point?", "그 애는 분명 형님의 동의를 구했어야 할 일에서 동의 없이 행동했습니다. 하지만 그건 무엇보다 그 애 자신의 이해가 걸린 일입니다."], ["Will you increase the ill consequences of his simple choice?", "형님 자신도 형님이 오로지 그 애의 이익만을 헤아렸노라고 인정하실 수밖에 없을 테고 또 인정하실 겁니다."], ["Will you endeavour to make an event certain misery to him, which may accidentally prove so?", "그런데 그 애가 불행히도 형님과 의견이 갈렸고, 행복에 대한 자기 생각에서 잘못 짚었다 한들, 형님, 만일 아드님을 사랑하신다면, 그 애를 핵심에서 더 멀리 끌고 가실 작정입니까? 그 애의 단순한 선택이 빚을 나쁜 결과를 더 키우시겠습니까?"], ["In a word, brother, because he hath put it out of your power to make his circumstances as affluent as you would, will you distress them as much as you can?”", "어쩌다 비참한 일이 될지도 모를 그 결말을, 기어이 확실한 비참으로 만들려 애쓰시겠습니까? 한마디로, 형님, 그 애가 자기 형편을 형님이 바라시는 만큼 넉넉하게 만들 권한을 형님에게서 앗아갔다고 해서, 그 형편을 형님이 할 수 있는 한 최대로 곤궁하게 만드시겠습니까?\""]], [["By the force of the true Catholic faith St Anthony won upon the fishes.", "참된 가톨릭 신앙의 힘으로 성 안토니우스는 물고기들의 마음을 얻었다."], ["Orpheus and Amphion went a little farther, and by the charms of music enchanted things merely inanimate. Wonderful, both!", "오르페우스와 암피온은 한 걸음 더 나아가, 음악의 매력으로 한낱 생명 없는 것들마저 홀렸다."], ["but neither history nor fable have ever yet ventured to record an instance of any one, who, by force of argument and reason, hath triumphed over habitual avarice.", "둘 다 놀랍기 그지없다! 하지만 역사도 우화도, 논증과 이성의 힘으로 몸에 밴 탐욕을 이겨낸 사례만큼은 아직 감히 기록한 적이 없다."]], [["Mr Nightingale, the father, instead of attempting to answer his brother, contented himself with only observing, that they had always differed in their sentiments concerning the education of their children.", "아버지 나이팅게일 씨는 형제에게 답하려 들기는커녕, 그저 자기네 둘이 자식 교육 문제에서 늘 생각이 달랐다는 점만 짚고 만족했다. \"내 바라건대,\""], ["“I wish,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “brother, you would have confined your care to your own daughter, and never have troubled yourself with my son, who hath, I believe, as little profited by your precepts, as by your example.”", "\"동생, 자네는 자네 딸이나 신경 쓸 것이지, 내 아들 일로는 결코 수고하지 말았어야 했네. 그 애는 자네의 가르침으로도, 자네의 본보기로도 거의 덕을 못 본 것 같으니 말일세.\""], ["For young Nightingale was his uncle's godson, and had lived more with him than with his father.", "젊은 나이팅게일은 삼촌의 대자였고, 아버지보다 삼촌과 더 많이 어울려 살았기 때문이다."], ["So that the uncle had often declared he loved his nephew almost equally with his own child.", "그래서 삼촌은 조카를 자기 친자식과 거의 똑같이 사랑한다고 곧잘 단언하곤 했다."]], [["Jones fell into raptures with this good gentleman; and when, after much persuasion, they found the father grew still more and more irritated, instead of appeased, Jones conducted the uncle to his nephew at the house of Mrs Miller.", "존스는 이 선량한 신사에게 그만 마음을 빼앗겨 황홀해졌다. 그리고 한참을 설득했는데도 아버지가 누그러지기는커녕 점점 더 노여워하는 것을 알게 되자, 존스는 삼촌을 밀러 부인의 집에 있는 조카에게로 데려갔다."]], [["Containing strange matters.", "서로 다른 기이한 일들이 담긴 이야기."]], [["At his return to his lodgings, Jones found the situation of affairs greatly altered from what they had been in at his departure.", "존스는 숙소로 돌아와, 자신이 떠날 때와는 상황이 크게 달라져 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["The mother, the two daughters, and young Mr Nightingale, were now sat down to supper together, when the uncle was, at his own desire, introduced without any ceremony into the company, to all of whom he was well known; for he had several times visited his nephew at that house.", "어머니와 두 딸, 그리고 젊은 나이팅게일 씨가 이제 함께 둘러앉아 저녁을 들고 있었는데, 그때 삼촌이 자신의 청에 따라 별다른 격식 없이 그 자리에 안내되어 들어왔다. 그곳에 있던 사람들은 모두 그를 잘 알고 있었으니, 그가 여러 차례 이 집으로 조카를 찾아온 적이 있었기 때문이다."]], [["The old gentleman immediately walked up to Miss Nancy, saluted and wished her joy, as he did afterwards the mother and the other sister; and lastly, he paid the proper compliments to his nephew, with the same good humour and courtesy, as if his nephew had married his equal or superior in fortune, with all the previous requisites first performed.", "노신사는 곧장 낸시 양에게 다가가 인사를 건네며 축하의 말을 전했고, 이어서 어머니와 다른 자매에게도 같은 인사를 건넸다. 그리고 마지막으로 조카에게도 합당한 덕담을 건넸는데, 마치 조카가 모든 사전 절차를 빠짐없이 마친 뒤 자신과 대등하거나 그보다 나은 재산을 가진 사람과 혼인한 것처럼, 더없이 너그럽고 정중한 태도였다."]], [["Miss Nancy and her supposed husband both turned pale, and looked rather foolish than otherwise upon the occasion; but Mrs Miller took the first opportunity of withdrawing; and, having sent for Jones into the dining-room, she threw herself at his feet, and in a most passionate flood of tears, called him her good angel, the preserver of her poor little family, with many other respectful and endearing appellations, and made him every acknowledgment which the highest benefit can extract from the most grateful heart.", "낸시 양과 그녀의 남편이라는 사람은 둘 다 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 이 자리에서 어쩐지 머쓱하기 짝이 없는 모습이었다. 그러나 밀러 부인은 기회를 보아 가장 먼저 자리를 빠져나왔고, 존스를 식당으로 불러들인 뒤 그의 발치에 몸을 던지고는 더없이 격정적인 눈물을 쏟으며 그를 자신의 착한 천사라, 가엾은 어린 가족의 구원자라 불렀다. 그 밖에도 온갖 정중하고도 애틋한 호칭으로 그를 부르며, 더없이 감사한 마음이 가장 큰 은혜로부터 끌어낼 수 있는 모든 감사의 표현을 그에게 바쳤다."]], [["After the first gust of her passion was a little over, which she declared, if she had not vented, would have burst her, she proceeded to inform Mr Jones that all matters were settled between Mr Nightingale and her daughter, and that they were to be married the next morning; at which Mr Jones having expressed much pleasure, the poor woman fell again into a fit of joy and thanksgiving, which he at length with difficulty silenced, and prevailed on her to return with him back to the company, whom they found in the same good humour in which they had left them.", "격정의 첫 물결이 조금 가라앉은 뒤에, 그녀는 만일 이 감정을 쏟아내지 못했더라면 가슴이 터져버렸을 것이라고 말하고는, 나이팅게일 씨와 자기 딸 사이의 모든 일이 매듭지어졌으며 두 사람이 이튿날 아침에 혼인하기로 했다고 존스 씨에게 알렸다. 존스 씨가 이에 큰 기쁨을 드러내자, 가엾은 부인은 다시금 기쁨과 감사의 발작에 빠져들었고, 그는 가까스로 그녀를 진정시킨 끝에 자신과 함께 일행에게로 돌아가자고 설득했다. 두 사람이 돌아가 보니 일행은 떠날 때와 다름없이 화기애애한 분위기였다."]], [["This little society now past two or three very agreeable hours together, in which the uncle, who was a very great lover of his bottle, had so well plyed his nephew, that this latter, though not drunk, began to be somewhat flustered; and now Mr Nightingale, taking the old gentleman with him upstairs into the apartment he had lately occupied, unbosomed himself as follows:--", "이 작은 모임은 이제 두세 시간을 함께 아주 흐뭇하게 보냈는데, 그동안 술병을 무척이나 사랑하는 삼촌이 조카에게 어찌나 부지런히 술을 권했던지, 조카는 취하지는 않았으되 어느새 다소 얼근하게 달아오르기 시작했다. 그러자 나이팅게일 씨는 노신사를 데리고 이층으로 올라가 자신이 얼마 전까지 쓰던 방으로 안내한 뒤, 다음과 같이 속내를 털어놓았다."]], [["“As you have been always the best and kindest of uncles to me, and as you have shown such unparalleled goodness in forgiving this match, which to be sure may be thought a little improvident, I should never forgive myself if I attempted to deceive you in anything.”", "\"삼촌은 언제나 제게 더없이 좋고 다정한 분이셨고, 누가 보아도 다소 경솔하다 여길 만한 이 혼인을 용서해 주시는 데서도 비길 데 없는 너그러움을 보여 주셨으니, 만일 제가 무슨 일로든 삼촌을 속이려 한다면 저 자신을 결코 용서하지 못할 것입니다.\""], ["He then confessed the truth, and opened the whole affair.", "그러고 나서 그는 진실을 고백하며 사건의 전모를 털어놓았다."]], [["“How, Jack?”", "\"뭐라고, 잭?\""], ["said the old gentleman, “and are you really then not married to this young woman?”", "노신사가 말했다. \"그러면 너는 정말로 이 젊은 여자와 혼인한 게 아니란 말이냐?\""], ["“No, upon my honour,”", "\"네, 제 명예를 걸고요.\""], ["answered Nightingale, “I have told you the simple truth.”", "나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"있는 그대로의 진실을 말씀드린 겁니다.\""], ["“My dear boy,”", "\"이 사랑스러운 녀석아.\""], ["cries the uncle, kissing him, “I am heartily glad to hear it.", "삼촌이 그에게 입을 맞추며 외쳤다. \"그 말을 들으니 진심으로 기쁘구나."], ["I never was better pleased in my life.", "내 평생 이보다 기뻤던 적이 없다."], ["If you had been married I should have assisted you as much as was in my power to have made the best of a bad matter; but there is a great difference between considering a thing which is already done and irrecoverable, and that which is yet to do.", "만일 네가 이미 혼인을 했더라면, 나는 이 나쁜 일에서나마 최선을 끌어내려 내가 할 수 있는 힘껏 너를 도왔을 것이다. 하지만 이미 저질러져 돌이킬 수 없는 일을 따져보는 것과, 아직 하지 않은 일을 두고 따져보는 것 사이에는 큰 차이가 있는 법이지."], ["Let your reason have fair play, Jack, and you will see this match in so foolish and preposterous a light, that there will be no need of any dissuasive arguments.”", "이성에게 공정한 기회를 주어 보거라, 잭. 그러면 너도 이 혼인이 얼마나 어리석고 터무니없는 것인지 알게 될 테니, 굳이 만류하는 말을 늘어놓을 필요조차 없을 게다.\""], ["“How, sir?”", "\"어째서요, 삼촌?\""], ["replies young Nightingale, “is there this difference between having already done an act, and being in honour engaged to do it?”", "젊은 나이팅게일이 대꾸했다. \"어떤 일을 이미 저지른 것과, 명예를 걸고 그 일을 하기로 약속한 것 사이에 그런 차이가 있다는 말씀입니까?\""], ["“Pugh!”", "\"쳇!\""], ["said the uncle, “honour is a creature of the world's making, and the world hath the power of a creator over it, and may govern and direct it as they please.", "삼촌이 말했다. \"명예라는 건 세상이 만들어낸 피조물이고, 세상은 그 위에 창조주와 같은 권능을 지녀서 제 마음대로 다스리고 휘두를 수 있다."], ["Now you well know how trivial these breaches of contract are thought; even the grossest make but the wonder and conversation of a day.", "그런데 너도 잘 알다시피, 이런 약혼 파기쯤은 얼마나 사소하게 여겨지더냐. 가장 심한 경우라 해도 기껏 하루치 화젯거리나 놀라움이 될 뿐이지."], ["Is there a man who afterwards will be more backward in giving you his sister, or daughter?", "그 일이 있은 뒤에 제 누이나 딸을 너에게 주기를 더 꺼릴 남자가 있겠느냐? 아니면 너를 받아들이기를 더 꺼릴 누이나 딸이 있겠느냐?"], ["or is there any sister or daughter who would be more backward to receive you?", "명예는 이런 약속 따위와는 아무 상관이 없다.\" \"용서하십시오, 삼촌.\""], ["Honour is not concerned in these engagements.”", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"저는 결코 그렇게 생각할 수 없습니다."], ["“Pardon me, dear sir,”", "명예뿐만 아니라 양심과 인정도 여기에 걸려 있습니다. 저는 분명히 확신합니다."], ["cries Nightingale, “I can never think so; and not only honour, but conscience and humanity, are concerned.", "만일 제가 지금 저 젊은 사람을 저버린다면 그 끝은 그녀의 죽음일 것이고, 저는 저 자신을 그녀를 죽인 살인자로, 그것도 모든 방법 가운데 가장 잔인한 방법, 곧 그녀의 가슴을 찢어 죽인 살인자로 여기게 될 것입니다.\" \"가슴을 찢어 죽인다니, 원!"], ["I am well satisfied, that, was I now to disappoint the young creature, her death would be the consequence, and I should look upon myself as her murderer; nay, as her murderer by the cruellest of all methods, by breaking her heart.”", "아니다, 아니야, 잭.\" 삼촌이 외쳤다."], ["“Break her heart, indeed!", "\"여자들의 가슴이란 그리 쉽게 찢어지는 게 아니다. 그것들은 질기고도 질기단다, 얘야, 질기고말고.\""], ["no, no, Jack,”", "\"하지만 삼촌.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다."], ["cries the uncle, “the hearts of women are not so soon broke; they are tough, boy, they are tough.”", "\"제 마음이 이미 그녀에게 매여 있어서, 다른 어떤 여자와도 결코 행복할 수가 없습니다. 자식들은 늘 제 짝을 스스로 고르게 해야 하며, 사촌 해리엇에게도 그리하도록 하겠노라고 삼촌께서 얼마나 자주 말씀하셨습니까?\""], ["“But, sir,”", "\"그래, 맞다.\""], ["answered Nightingale, “my own affections are engaged, and I never could be happy with any other woman.", "노신사가 대꾸했다. \"그래, 나도 그러길 바란다."], ["How often have I heard you say, that children should be always suffered to chuse for themselves, and that you would let my cousin Harriet do so?”", "다만 현명하게 고르길 바라는 게지. 정말이지, 잭, 너는 그 여자를 반드시 떠나야 하고, 떠나게 될 것이다.\""], ["“Why, ay,”", "\"정말이지, 삼촌.\""], ["replied the old gentleman, “so I would have them; but then I would have them chuse wisely.--Indeed, Jack, you must and shall leave the girl.”----“Indeed, uncle,”", "다른 쪽이 외쳤다. \"저는 반드시 그녀를 맞이해야 하고, 맞이하고야 말겠습니다.\""], ["cries the other, “I must and will have her.”", "\"그러시겠다, 젊은 양반?\" 삼촌이 말했다."], ["“You will, young gentleman;”", "\"너에게서 그런 말을 들을 줄은 몰랐다. 너를 늘 개처럼 대하고, 폭군이 신하에게 두는 만큼의 거리를 두어 온 네 아비에게라면 그런 말투를 썼다 해도 이상할 게 없겠지."], ["said the uncle; “I did not expect such a word from you.", "하지만 너와 대등하게 지내 온 나로서는, 분명히 더 나은 대접을 기대할 만하지 않으냐. 허나 나는 이 모든 까닭을 안다."], ["I should not wonder if you had used such language to your father, who hath always treated you like a dog, and kept you at the distance which a tyrant preserves over his subjects; but I, who have lived with you upon an equal footing, might surely expect better usage: but I know how to account for it all: it is all owing to your preposterous education, in which I have had too little share.", "죄다 너의 그 터무니없는 교육 탓이지. 그 교육에 내 몫이 너무 적었던 게야."], ["There is my daughter, now, whom I have brought up as my friend, never doth anything without my advice, nor ever refuses to take it when I give it her.”", "저기 내 딸을 보아라. 나는 그 아이를 벗처럼 길러서, 내 조언 없이는 아무 일도 하지 않고, 내가 조언을 줄 때 그것을 받아들이기를 마다하는 법도 없다.\""], ["“You have never yet given her advice in an affair of this kind,”", "\"삼촌은 아직 이런 종류의 일에 대해서는 사촌에게 조언을 주신 적이 없으시지요.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다."], ["said Nightingale; “for I am greatly mistaken in my cousin, if she would be very ready to obey even your most positive commands in abandoning her inclinations.”", "\"제가 사촌을 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 사촌은 제 마음을 버리는 일에서만큼은 삼촌의 가장 단호한 명령조차 선뜻 따르려 하지 않을 테니까요.\" \"내 딸을 함부로 말하지 마라.\""], ["“Don't abuse my girl,”", "노신사가 다소 격해진 어조로 대답했다. \"내 해리엇을 함부로 말하지 마."], ["answered the old gentleman with some emotion; “don't abuse my Harriet.", "나는 그 아이를 내 뜻과 어긋나는 마음을 품지 않도록 길러 왔다. 그 아이가 하고 싶은 대로 무엇이든 하게 내버려둠으로써, 나는 그 아이가 내가 원하는 것을 기꺼이 하는 버릇이 몸에 배도록 길들여 온 것이다.\""], ["I have brought her up to have no inclinations contrary to my own.", "\"용서하십시오, 삼촌.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다."], ["By suffering her to do whatever she pleases, I have enured her to a habit of being pleased to do whatever I like.”", "\"저는 사촌을 두고 무어라 헐뜯을 생각은 조금도 없습니다. 저는 사촌을 더없이 존경하니까요."], ["“Pardon, me, sir,”", "게다가 저는 확신합니다. 삼촌께서는 사촌을 그렇게 가혹한 시험에 들게 하시거나, 제게 내리시는 것 같은 그런 모진 명령을 그 아이에게 내리시는 일은 결코 없으리라고요."], ["said Nightingale, “I have not the least design to reflect on my cousin, for whom I have the greatest esteem; and indeed I am convinced you will never put her to so severe a tryal, or lay such hard commands on her as you would do on me.--But, dear sir, let us return to the company; for they will begin to be uneasy at our long absence.", "하지만 삼촌, 이제 일행에게로 돌아가시지요. 우리가 너무 오래 자리를 비우면 다들 불안해하기 시작할 테니까요."], ["I must beg one favour of my dear uncle, which is that he would not say anything to shock the poor girl or her mother.”", "사랑하는 삼촌께 한 가지 청을 드려야겠습니다. 부디 저 가엾은 아가씨나 그 어머니에게 충격이 될 만한 말씀은 하지 말아 주십시오.\""], ["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you need not fear me,”", "그건 걱정하지 않아도 된다.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["answered he, “I understand myself too well to affront women; so I will readily grant you that favour; and in return I must expect another of you.”", "\"나는 여자에게 모욕을 줄 만큼 처신을 모르는 사람이 아니다. 그러니 그 청은 기꺼이 들어주마."], ["“There are but few of your commands, sir,”", "그 대신 나도 네게 한 가지를 청해야겠다.\" \"삼촌의 분부 가운데 제가 아주 기꺼이 따르지 않을 것은 별로 없습니다, 삼촌.\""], ["said Nightingale, “which I shall not very chearfully obey.”", "나이팅게일이 말했다. \"아니다, 나는 별것을 청하는 게 아니다.\""], ["“Nay, sir, I ask nothing,”", "삼촌이 말했다. \"다만 네가 내 숙소까지 함께 가 주는 영광을 베풀어 주기를 바랄 뿐이다."], ["said the uncle, “but the honour of your company home to my lodging, that I may reason the case a little more fully with you; for I would, if possible, have the satisfaction of preserving my family, notwithstanding the headstrong folly of my brother, who, in his own opinion, is the wisest man in the world.”", "그곳에서 이 일을 좀 더 충분히 너와 따져보고 싶구나. 가능하다면 나는, 제 딴에는 세상에서 가장 현명한 사람이라 여기는 내 동생의 고집스러운 어리석음에도 불구하고, 우리 가문을 지켜냈다는 만족을 얻고 싶으니까.\""]], [["Nightingale, who well knew his uncle to be as headstrong as his father, submitted to attend him home, and then they both returned back into the room, where the old gentleman promised to carry himself with the same decorum which he had before maintained.", "나이팅게일은 삼촌이 제 아비만큼이나 고집이 센 사람임을 익히 알고 있었기에, 그를 집까지 모시는 데 따르기로 했다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 함께 방으로 돌아갔고, 그곳에서 노신사는 앞서 지켜오던 것과 똑같은 예의를 갖추겠노라 약속했다."]], [["A short chapter, which concludes the book.", "본권을 매듭짓는 짧은 장."]], [["The long absence of the uncle and nephew had occasioned some disquiet in the minds of all whom they had left behind them; and the more, as, during the preceding dialogue, the uncle had more than once elevated his voice, so as to be heard downstairs; which, though they could not distinguish what he said, had caused some evil foreboding in Nancy and her mother, and, indeed, even in Jones himself.", "삼촌과 조카가 그토록 오래 자리를 비운 탓에, 뒤에 남겨진 모든 이의 마음에는 적잖은 불안이 일었다. 더구나 앞선 대화가 오가는 동안 삼촌이 한두 번 목소리를 높여 아래층까지 들릴 정도였으니, 그 불안은 한층 커질 수밖에 없었다. 무슨 말을 하는지 또렷이 알아들을 수는 없었지만, 그 소리는 낸시와 그녀의 어머니에게, 그리고 실은 존스 자신에게까지 어떤 불길한 예감을 불러일으켰다."]], [["When the good company, therefore, again assembled, there was a visible alteration in all their faces; and the good-humour which, at their last meeting, universally shone forth in every countenance, was now changed into a much less agreeable aspect.", "그리하여 이 정겨운 사람들이 다시 한자리에 모였을 때, 모두의 얼굴에는 눈에 띄는 변화가 있었다. 지난번 모임에서는 누구의 얼굴에나 한결같이 환하게 빛나던 그 좋은 기분이, 이제는 훨씬 덜 유쾌한 빛으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["It was a change, indeed, common enough to the weather in this climate, from sunshine to clouds, from June to December.", "사실 그것은 이 나라의 날씨에 흔히 있는 변화였다. 햇살에서 구름으로, 유월에서 십이월로 옮겨 가는 그런 변화 말이다."]], [["This alteration was not, however, greatly remarked by any present; for as they were all now endeavouring to conceal their own thoughts, and to act a part, they became all too busily engaged in the scene to be spectators of it.", "그러나 이 변화를 자리에 있던 누구도 크게 알아차리지는 못했다. 모두가 저마다 제 속마음을 감추고 어떤 역할을 연기하느라, 그 장면에 너무 깊이 빠져들어 정작 그것을 지켜보는 구경꾼이 되지는 못했기 때문이다."], ["Thus neither the uncle nor nephew saw any symptoms of suspicion in the mother or daughter; nor did the mother or daughter remark the overacted complacence of the old man, nor the counterfeit satisfaction which grinned in the features of the young one.", "그래서 삼촌도 조카도 어머니나 딸에게서 의심의 기미를 알아채지 못했고, 어머니나 딸 역시 노인의 지나치게 과장된 다정함도, 젊은이의 얼굴에 어색하게 번지던 거짓 만족의 미소도 눈치채지 못했다."]], [["Something like this, I believe, frequently happens, where the whole attention of two friends being engaged in the part which each is to act, in order to impose on the other, neither sees nor suspects the arts practised against himself; and thus the thrust of both (to borrow no improper metaphor on the occasion) alike takes place.", "내 생각엔 이런 일이 종종 일어나는 듯하다. 두 친구의 온 신경이 서로를 속이려고 저마다 맡은 배역에 쏠려 있을 때, 누구도 자신을 겨눈 술책을 보지도 의심하지도 못하는 것이다. 그리하여 (이 자리에 어울리지 않을 리 없는 비유를 빌리자면) 두 사람의 찌르기가 똑같이 빗나가고 만다."]], [["From the same reason it is no unusual thing for both parties to be overreached in a bargain, though the one must be always the greater loser; as was he who sold a blind horse, and received a bad note in payment.", "같은 이유로, 거래에서 양쪽이 모두 속아 넘어가는 일도 드물지 않다. 물론 그 가운데 한쪽이 늘 더 크게 손해를 보기 마련이지만 말이다. 마치 눈먼 말을 팔고서 그 값으로 가짜 어음을 받은 사람처럼."]], [["Our company in about half an hour broke up, and the uncle carried off his nephew; but not before the latter had assured Miss Nancy, in a whisper, that he would attend her early in the morning, and fulfil all his engagements.", "우리의 일행은 반 시간쯤 지나 자리를 파했고, 삼촌은 조카를 데리고 떠났다. 하지만 그 전에 조카는 낸시 양에게 속삭이듯, 내일 아침 일찍 찾아와 약속한 모든 것을 지키겠노라고 다짐해 두었다."]], [["Jones, who was the least concerned in this scene, saw the most.", "이 장면에서 가장 마음 쓸 일이 적었던 존스가, 정작 가장 많은 것을 보았다. 그는 사실의 진상을 의심하기까지 했다."], ["He did indeed suspect the very fact; for, besides observing the great alteration in the behaviour of the uncle, the distance he assumed, and his overstrained civility to Miss Nancy; the carrying off a bridegroom from his bride at that time of night was so extraordinary a proceeding that it could be accounted for only by imagining that young Nightingale had revealed the whole truth, which the apparent openness of his temper, and his being flustered with liquor, made too probable.", "삼촌의 태도에 일어난 큰 변화, 그가 두른 거리감, 낸시 양에게 보인 지나치게 빳빳한 예의를 눈여겨본 데다, 한밤중 그 시각에 신랑을 신부에게서 떼어 데려가는 것은 너무도 별난 처사여서, 젊은 나이팅게일이 진실을 모조리 털어놓았다고밖에는 달리 설명할 길이 없었기 때문이다. 게다가 겉보기에 솔직한 그의 성미와 술기운에 들떠 있던 정황을 떠올리면, 그럴 가능성은 더욱 짙어 보였다."]], [["While he was reasoning with himself, whether he should acquaint these poor people with his suspicion, the maid of the house informed him that a gentlewoman desired to speak with him.----He went immediately out, and, taking the candle from the maid, ushered his visitant upstairs, who, in the person of Mrs Honour, acquainted him with such dreadful news concerning his Sophia, that he immediately lost all consideration for every other person; and his whole stock of compassion was entirely swallowed up in reflections on his own misery, and on that of his unfortunate angel.", "존스가 이 가엾은 사람들에게 자신의 의심을 알려야 할지 혼자 마음속으로 따져 보고 있을 때, 집안의 하녀가 어떤 부인이 그와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 알려 왔다. 그는 곧장 밖으로 나가 하녀에게서 촛불을 받아 든 뒤, 손님을 위층으로 안내했다. 그런데 그 손님은 다름 아닌 어너 부인이었고, 그녀가 그의 소피아에 관해 전한 끔찍한 소식에 그는 순식간에 다른 어떤 사람에 대한 배려도 모두 잊고 말았다. 그가 지녔던 동정심은 죄다, 제 자신의 비참함과 불운한 천사의 처지를 향한 상념 속으로 송두리째 삼켜져 버렸다."]], [["What this dreadful matter was, the reader will be informed, after we have first related the many preceding steps which produced it, and those will be the subject of the following book.", "그 끔찍한 일이 대체 무엇이었는지는, 그 일을 빚어낸 앞선 여러 단계를 먼저 이야기한 뒤에야 독자에게 알려 드리겠다. 그리고 그 이야기야말로 다음 권에서 다룰 내용이 될 것이다."]], [["Too short to need a preface.", "역사가 이틀쯤 나아가는 권."]], [["There are a set of religious, or rather moral writers, who teach that virtue is the certain road to happiness, and vice to misery, in this world. A very wholesome and comfortable doctrine, and to which we have but one objection, namely, that it is not true.", "서문이 필요 없을 만큼 짧은 장. 종교적인, 아니 좀 더 정확히 말하자면 도덕적인 글을 쓰는 한 무리의 작가들이 있다. 이들은 덕이 이 세상에서 행복으로 가는 확실한 길이며, 악덕은 불행으로 가는 길이라고 가르친다. 참으로 건전하고 위안이 되는 가르침이다. 다만 우리에게는 여기에 한 가지 반론이 있을 뿐이다. 그것이 사실이 아니라는 점 말이다."]], [["Indeed, if by virtue these writers mean the exercise of those cardinal virtues, which like good housewives stay at home, and mind only the business of their own family, I shall very readily concede the point; for so surely do all these contribute and lead to happiness, that I could almost wish, in violation of all the antient and modern sages, to call them rather by the name of wisdom, than by that of virtue; for, with regard to this life, no system, I conceive, was ever wiser than that of the antient Epicureans, who held this wisdom to constitute the chief good; nor foolisher than that of their opposites, those modern epicures, who place all felicity in the abundant gratification of every sensual appetite.", "사실, 만일 이 작가들이 덕이라는 말로, 마치 알뜰한 주부처럼 집 안에 머물며 제 가족의 일만 챙기는 그 으뜸가는 덕목들의 실천을 뜻한다면, 나는 기꺼이 그 점을 인정하겠다. 그런 덕목들은 어김없이 행복에 보탬이 되고 행복으로 이끌기에, 나는 옛 현인과 오늘의 현인 모두의 뜻을 어기면서까지, 그것들을 차라리 덕이 아니라 지혜라는 이름으로 부르고 싶을 지경이다. 내가 보기에, 이 세상의 삶에 관한 한, 이 지혜야말로 으뜸가는 선을 이룬다고 여겼던 옛 에피쿠로스학파보다 더 현명한 사상은 일찍이 없었으며, 그와 정반대로 온갖 감각적 욕구를 마음껏 채우는 데 모든 행복이 있다고 보는 오늘날의 향락가들보다 더 어리석은 사상도 없었으니 말이다."]], [["But if by virtue is meant (as I almost think it ought) a certain relative quality, which is always busying itself without-doors, and seems as much interested in pursuing the good of others as its own; I cannot so easily agree that this is the surest way to human happiness; because I am afraid we must then include poverty and contempt, with all the mischiefs which backbiting, envy, and ingratitude, can bring on mankind, in our idea of happiness; nay, sometimes perhaps we shall be obliged to wait upon the said happiness to a jail; since many by the above virtue have brought themselves thither.", "그러나 만일 덕이라는 말이 (나는 거의 그래야 마땅하다고 생각하는데) 늘 바깥을 분주히 오가며, 제 이익만큼이나 남의 이익을 좇는 데에도 깊은 관심을 쏟는 듯한 어떤 관계적 성질을 뜻한다면, 나는 이것이 인간의 행복으로 가는 가장 확실한 길이라고 그리 쉽게 동의할 수가 없다. 그렇게 되면 우리는 행복이라는 관념 안에 가난과 멸시를, 그리고 험담과 시기와 배은망덕이 인류에게 끼칠 수 있는 온갖 해악까지 함께 넣어야 할까 봐 두렵기 때문이다. 아니, 때로는 어쩌면 그 행복을 모시고 감옥까지 따라 들어가야 할지도 모른다. 앞서 말한 그 덕 때문에 스스로 그곳에 이른 이들이 적지 않으니 말이다."]], [["I have not now leisure to enter upon so large a field of speculation, as here seems opening upon me; my design was to wipe off a doctrine that lay in my way; since, while Mr Jones was acting the most virtuous part imaginable in labouring to preserve his fellow-creatures from destruction, the devil, or some other evil spirit, one perhaps cloathed in human flesh, was hard at work to make him completely miserable in the ruin of his Sophia.", "지금 내게는 여기서 내 앞에 펼쳐지는 듯한 이토록 넓은 사색의 들판으로 들어설 여유가 없다. 내 뜻은 다만 내 길목에 놓인 한 가지 가르침을 닦아 내려는 것뿐이었다. 존스 씨가 동료 인간들을 파멸에서 구하려 애쓰며 상상할 수 있는 가장 덕스러운 역할을 하고 있는 동안, 악마가, 아니면 다른 어떤 악령이—어쩌면 인간의 살을 뒤집어쓴 자가—그를 그의 소피아의 파멸 속에서 완전히 비참하게 만들려고 한창 애쓰고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["This therefore would seem an exception to the above rule, if indeed it was a rule; but as we have in our voyage through life seen so many other exceptions to it, we chuse to dispute the doctrine on which it is founded, which we don't apprehend to be Christian, which we are convinced is not true, and which is indeed destructive of one of the noblest arguments that reason alone can furnish for the belief of immortality.", "따라서 이것은 앞서 말한 규칙에 대한 예외처럼 보일 것이다. 만일 그것이 정말 규칙이라면 말이다. 그러나 우리는 인생이라는 항해를 거치며 그에 대한 다른 예외들을 너무도 많이 보아 왔기에, 그 규칙이 딛고 선 가르침 자체를 따져 보기로 한다. 우리가 보기에 그것은 기독교적이지 않으며, 우리가 확신하건대 사실이 아니고, 실로 이성만으로 불멸을 믿게 해 줄 수 있는 가장 고귀한 논거 가운데 하나를 무너뜨리는 가르침이기 때문이다."]], [["But as the reader's curiosity (if he hath any) must be now awake, and hungry, we shall provide to feed it as fast as we can.", "그러나 이제 독자의 호기심이 (혹 그에게 호기심이 있다면) 깨어나 허기져 있을 터이니, 우리는 그것을 되도록 빨리 먹여 채울 채비를 하겠다."]], [["In which is opened a very black design against Sophia.", "소피아를 겨눈 매우 음흉한 계략이 펼쳐지다."]], [["I remember a wise old gentleman who used to say, “When children are doing nothing, they are doing mischief.” I will not enlarge this quaint saying to the most beautiful part of the creation in general; but so far I may be allowed, that when the effects of female jealousy do not appear openly in their proper colours of rage and fury, we may suspect that mischievous passion to be at work privately, and attempting to undermine, what it doth not attack above-ground.", "언젠가 한 지혜로운 노신사가 입버릇처럼 하던 말이 떠오른다. \"아이들이 아무것도 안 하고 있을 때, 그때가 바로 사고를 치고 있는 때다.\" 나는 이 익살스러운 말을 창조물 가운데 가장 아름다운 절반 전체에까지 넓혀 적용할 생각은 없다. 다만 이만큼은 허락해 주기를 바란다. 여자의 질투가 분노와 격노라는 제 본래의 빛깔로 드러내 놓고 나타나지 않을 때, 우리는 그 사악한 정념이 은밀히 일하고 있다고, 즉 지상에서 정면으로 공격하지 않는 것을 땅 밑에서 허물어뜨리려 시도하고 있다고 의심해도 좋다는 것이다."]], [["This was exemplified in the conduct of Lady Bellaston, who, under all the smiles which she wore in her countenance, concealed much indignation against Sophia; and as she plainly saw that this young lady stood between her and the full indulgence of her desires, she resolved to get rid of her by some means or other; nor was it long before a very favourable opportunity of accomplishing this presented itself to her.", "이런 모습이 벨라스턴 부인의 행동에서 그대로 드러났다. 그녀는 얼굴에 띤 온갖 미소 아래로 소피아를 향한 깊은 분노를 감추고 있었다. 그리고 이 젊은 아가씨가 자신과 욕망의 온전한 충족 사이를 가로막고 있음을 똑똑히 보았기에, 무슨 수를 써서라도 그녀를 없애 버리기로 마음먹었다. 그리고 이를 이룰 더없이 좋은 기회가 그녀에게 찾아오기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다."]], [["The reader may be pleased to remember, that when Sophia was thrown into that consternation at the playhouse, by the wit and humour of a set of young gentlemen who call themselves the town, we informed him, that she had put herself under the protection of a young nobleman, who had very safely conducted her to her chair.", "독자께서는 기억해 주시기 바란다. 소피아가 극장에서, 스스로를 '도회의 멋쟁이'라 칭하는 한 무리의 젊은 신사들이 부리는 재담과 익살에 그토록 큰 낭패와 당황에 빠졌을 때, 우리는 그녀가 어느 젊은 귀족의 보호 아래 몸을 맡겼고, 그 귀족이 그녀를 가마까지 무사히 데려다주었다고 알려 드린 바 있다."]], [["This nobleman, who frequently visited Lady Bellaston, had more than once seen Sophia there, since her arrival in town, and had conceived a very great liking to her; which liking, as beauty never looks more amiable than in distress, Sophia had in this fright so encreased, that he might now, without any great impropriety, be said to be actually in love with her.", "벨라스턴 부인을 자주 찾아오던 이 귀족은, 소피아가 상경한 이후로 그곳에서 그녀를 한두 번 본 적이 있었고, 그녀에게 큰 호감을 품게 되었다. 그런데 곤경에 처한 아름다움만큼 더없이 사랑스러워 보이는 것이 없는 법이라, 소피아는 이 소동 속에서 그 호감을 어찌나 키워 놓았던지, 이제 그는 큰 무리 없이 그녀에게 실제로 사랑에 빠졌다고 말해도 좋을 정도였다."]], [["It may easily be believed, that he would not suffer so handsome an occasion of improving his acquaintance with the beloved object as now offered itself to elapse, when even good breeding alone might have prompted him to pay her a visit.", "사랑하는 대상과의 친분을 돈독히 할, 지금 눈앞에 펼쳐진 이토록 좋은 기회를, 그가 그냥 흘려보내지 않으리라는 것은 쉽게 믿을 수 있다. 설령 사랑이 아니라 단순한 예의범절만으로도 그녀를 찾아가 보라고 그를 부추겼을 만한 상황이었으니 말이다."]], [["The next morning therefore, after this accident, he waited on Sophia, with the usual compliments, and hopes that she had received no harm from her last night's adventure.", "그리하여 이 사고가 있은 다음 날 아침, 그는 소피아를 찾아가 으레 갖추는 인사를 건네며, 어젯밤의 소동으로 다친 데는 없으신지 바란다고 안부를 물었다."]], [["As love, like fire, when once thoroughly kindled, is soon blown into a flame, Sophia in a very short time compleated her conquest.", "사랑은 불과 같아서 한번 완전히 불붙으면 이내 활활 타오르는 법이라, 소피아는 아주 짧은 시간 만에 그를 완전히 사로잡았다. 이제 시간은 알아차릴 새도 없이 흘러갔고, 이 고귀한 경께서는 이 아가씨와 함께한 지 두 시간이 지나서야 비로소 자신이 너무 오래 머물렀다는 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["Time now flew away unperceived, and the noble lord had been two hours in company with the lady, before it entered into his head that he had made too long a visit.", "이 사정 하나만으로도 소피아는 경계심을 품었을 터인데, 지금은 셈에 한결 밝아진 처지였다. 게다가 그녀는 연모하는 이의 눈빛으로부터 그의 가슴속에 무엇이 오가는지를 알려 주는 훨씬 더 분명한 증거를 얻었다."], ["Though this circumstance alone would have alarmed Sophia, who was somewhat more a mistress of computation at present; she had indeed much more pregnant evidence from the eyes of her lover of what past within his bosom; nay, though he did not make any open declaration of his passion, yet many of his expressions were rather too warm, and too tender, to have been imputed to complacence, even in the age when such complacence was in fashion; the very reverse of which is well known to be the reigning mode at present.", "아니, 그가 자신의 정념을 드러내 놓고 고백하지는 않았으나, 그의 말들 가운데 상당수는 단순한 정중함으로 돌리기에는 너무 뜨겁고 너무 다정했다. 그런 정중함이 유행하던 시절에 견주어도 그러했으니, 지금은 그와 정반대가 판을 치는 풍조임은 익히 알려진 바다."]], [["Lady Bellaston had been apprized of his lordship's visit at his first arrival; and the length of it very well satisfied her, that things went as she wished, and as indeed she had suspected the second time she saw this young couple together. This business, she rightly I think concluded, that she should by no means forward by mixing in the company while they were together; she therefore ordered her servants, that when my lord was going, they should tell him she desired to speak with him; and employed the intermediate time in meditating how best to accomplish a scheme, which she made no doubt but his lordship would very readily embrace the execution of.", "벨라스턴 부인은 경께서 도착하자마자 그의 방문을 전해 들었다. 그리고 그 방문이 길어지는 것을 보고, 일이 자기 바람대로, 아니 실은 이 젊은 두 사람이 함께 있는 것을 두 번째 보았을 때 이미 짐작한 대로 흘러가고 있음을 흡족히 확인했다. 이 일은, 내 생각에 그녀가 옳게 결론지었듯이, 둘이 함께 있을 때 자신이 끼어들어서는 결코 진전시킬 수 없는 노릇이었다. 그래서 그녀는 하인들에게, 경께서 떠나려 하시거든 자신이 한마디 나누고 싶어 한다고 전하라 일러 두었다. 그러고는 그사이의 시간을, 경께서 틀림없이 기꺼이 실행에 옮기리라 의심치 않는 어떤 계략을 어찌하면 가장 잘 이룰 수 있을지 궁리하는 데 썼다."]], [["Lord Fellamar (for that was the title of this young nobleman) was no sooner introduced to her ladyship, than she attacked him in the following strain: “Bless me, my lord, are you here yet?", "펠라마 경(이것이 이 젊은 귀족의 작위였다)이 부인 앞으로 안내되자마자, 그녀는 이런 식으로 그를 공략하기 시작했다. \"어머나, 경, 아직 여기 계셨어요?"], ["I thought my servants had made a mistake, and let you go away; and I wanted to see you about an affair of some importance.”----“Indeed, Lady Bellaston,”", "저는 제 하인들이 실수를 해서 경을 그냥 보내 드린 줄로만 알았어요. 제가 좀 중요한 일로 경을 뵙고 싶었거든요.\"—— \"정말이지, 벨라스턴 부인,\""], ["said he, “I don't wonder you are astonished at the length of my visit; for I have staid above two hours, and I did not think I had staid above half-a-one.”----“What am I to conclude from thence, my lord?”", "그가 말했다. \"제 방문이 길어진 데 놀라시는 것도 무리가 아닙니다."], ["said she. “The company must be very agreeable which can make time slide away so very deceitfully.”----“Upon my honour,”", "제가 두 시간 넘게 머물렀으면서도, 반 시간도 채 못 머문 줄로만 알았으니까요.\"—— \"그렇다면 저는 그걸 어떻게 받아들여야 할까요, 경?\""], ["said he, “the most agreeable I ever saw.", "그녀가 말했다. \"시간을 그토록 감쪽같이 흘려보내게 만들 만큼, 그 자리가 어지간히 즐거웠던 모양이지요.\"—— \"맹세코,\""], ["Pray tell me, Lady Bellaston, who is this blazing star which you have produced among us all of a sudden?”----“What blazing star, my lord?”", "그가 말했다. \"제 평생 본 가장 즐거운 자리였습니다."], ["said she, affecting a surprize.", "부디 말씀해 주십시오, 벨라스턴 부인. 갑자기 우리 모두 가운데 띄워 놓으신 이 눈부신 별은 대체 누구입니까?\"—— \"눈부신 별이라니요, 경?\""], ["“I mean,”", "그녀가 짐짓 놀란 척하며 말했다. \"제 말은,\""], ["said he, “the lady I saw here the other day, whom I had last night in my arms at the playhouse, and to whom I have been making that unreasonable visit.”----“O, my cousin Western!”", "그가 말했다. \"며칠 전 여기서 뵙고, 어젯밤 극장에서 제 품에 안았으며, 방금 그토록 분별없이 긴 방문을 한 그 아가씨 말입니다.\"—— \"오, 제 사촌 웨스턴 양 말이군요!\""], ["said she; “why, that blazing star, my lord, is the daughter of a country booby squire, and hath been in town about a fortnight, for the first time.”----“Upon my soul,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"글쎄요, 경, 그 눈부신 별은 시골 촌뜨기 지주의 딸이고, 처음으로 상경한 지 보름쯤 된 처녀랍니다.\"—— \"맹세코,\""], ["said he, “I should swear she had been bred up in a court; for besides her beauty, I never saw anything so genteel, so sensible, so polite.”----“O brave!”", "그가 말했다. \"저라면 그녀가 궁정에서 자란 사람이라고 단언했을 겁니다."], ["cries the lady, “my cousin hath you, I find.”----“Upon my honour,”", "미모는 말할 것도 없고, 그토록 우아하고, 그토록 분별 있고, 그토록 세련된 사람은 본 적이 없으니까요.\"—— \"어머, 대단하셔라!\" 부인이 외쳤다."], ["answered he, “I wish she had; for I am in love with her to distraction.”----“Nay, my lord,”", "\"보아하니 제 사촌이 경을 사로잡았군요.\"—— \"맹세코,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["said she, “it is not wishing yourself very ill neither, for she is a very great fortune: I assure you she is an only child, and her father's estate is a good £3000 a-year.”", "\"그녀가 정말 그래 주었으면 합니다. 저는 정신을 잃을 만큼 그녀에게 빠져 있으니까요.\"—— \"어머, 경,\""], ["“Then I can assure you, madam,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그건 경 자신에게 그리 나쁜 바람도 아니에요."], ["answered the lord, “I think her the best match in England.”", "그 처녀는 어마어마한 재산가거든요. 분명히 말씀드리는데 외동딸이고, 아버지의 영지는 연 3000파운드는 너끈히 되는 좋은 자리랍니다.\""], ["“Indeed, my lord,”", "\"그렇다면 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있겠습니다, 부인,\""], ["replied she, “if you like her, I heartily wish you had her.”", "경이 대답했다. \"제 생각에 그녀는 잉글랜드에서 가장 좋은 혼처입니다.\""], ["“If you think so kindly of me, madam,”", "\"정말이지, 경,\" 그녀가 응했다."], ["said he, “as she is a relation of yours, will you do me the honour to propose it to her father?”", "\"경께서 그녀를 마음에 들어 하신다면, 저는 진심으로 경께서 그녀를 차지하시기를 바랍니다.\" \"부인께서 저를 그토록 후하게 여겨 주신다면,\""], ["“And are you really then in earnest?”", "그가 말했다. \"그녀가 부인의 친척이시니, 부디 그녀의 아버지께 이 혼담을 꺼내 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\""], ["cries the lady, with an affected gravity.", "\"그럼 정말로 진심이신 건가요?\" 부인이 짐짓 근엄한 척하며 외쳤다."], ["“I hope, madam,”", "\"부인,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["answered he, “you have a better opinion of me, than to imagine I would jest with your ladyship in an affair of this kind.”", "\"이런 종류의 일을 두고 제가 부인을 상대로 농을 칠 사람이라고 여기실 만큼, 저를 낮게 보시지는 않으시리라 믿습니다.\" \"그렇다면 정말로,\""], ["“Indeed, then,”", "부인이 말했다."], ["said the lady, “I will most readily propose your lordship to her father; and I can, I believe, assure you of his joyful acceptance of the proposal; but there is a bar, which I am almost ashamed to mention; and yet it is one you will never be able to conquer.", "\"저는 더없이 기꺼이 그녀의 아버지께 경을 천거해 드리겠어요. 그리고 제가 보기에 그분이 이 혼담을 기쁘게 받아들이시리라는 것도 장담할 수 있어요."], ["You have a rival, my lord, and a rival who, though I blush to name him, neither you, nor all the world, will ever be able to conquer.”", "다만 한 가지 걸림돌이 있는데, 입에 올리기조차 부끄럽군요. 게다가 그건 경께서 결코 이겨 낼 수 없는 종류의 것이에요."], ["“Upon my word, Lady Bellaston,”", "경께는 연적이 있어요."], ["cries he, “you have struck a damp to my heart, which hath almost deprived me of being.”", "그 연적은, 이름을 대기조차 낯이 뜨겁지만, 경도, 온 세상 누구도 결코 이겨 낼 수 없는 자랍니다.\" \"맹세코, 벨라스턴 부인,\""], ["“Fie, my lord,”", "그가 외쳤다. \"부인께서 제 가슴에 찬물을 끼얹으셨습니다."], ["said she, “I should rather hope I had struck fire into you.", "저를 거의 숨도 못 쉬게 만드실 만큼이요.\" \"이런, 경,\""], ["A lover, and talk of damps in your heart!", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 오히려 경께 불을 지폈기를 바라는데요."], ["I rather imagined you would have asked your rival's name, that you might have immediately entered the lists with him.”", "사랑하는 사람이, 가슴이 찬물에 식었다는 말씀을 하시다니요! 저는 경께서 당장 연적과 겨룰 채비를 하시려고 그자의 이름부터 물으실 줄 알았는데요.\""], ["“I promise you, madam,”", "\"장담합니다, 부인,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["answered he, “there are very few things I would not undertake for your charming cousin; but pray, who is this happy man?”--“Why, he is,”", "\"부인의 그 매혹적인 사촌을 위해서라면 제가 떠맡지 못할 일은 거의 없습니다. 한데 대체 그 행복한 사내가 누구입니까?\"—— \"글쎄요, 그자는,\""], ["said she, “what I am sorry to say most happy men with us are, one of the lowest fellows in the world.", "그녀가 말했다. \"안타깝게도 우리네 가장 행복한 사내들이 흔히 그렇듯, 세상에서 가장 천한 부류의 인간이랍니다."], ["He is a beggar, a bastard, a foundling, a fellow in meaner circumstances than one of your lordship's footmen.”", "거지에다, 사생아에다, 업둥이에다, 경의 하인들 가운데 하나보다도 못한 처지의 사내예요.\" \"그게 가당키나 한 일입니까,\""], ["“And is it possible,”", "그가 외쳤다."], ["cried he, “that a young creature with such perfections should think of bestowing herself so unworthily?”", "\"그토록 완벽한 젊은 처녀가 자신을 그토록 합당치 못한 곳에 내던질 생각을 한다는 것이 말입니다.\" \"아아!"], ["“Alas!", "경,\""], ["my lord,”", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["answered she, “consider the country--the bane of all young women is the country.", "\"시골을 생각해 보세요. 모든 젊은 여자를 망치는 독이 바로 시골이랍니다."], ["There they learn a set of romantic notions of love, and I know not what folly, which this town and good company can scarce eradicate in a whole winter.”", "거기서 그들은 사랑이니 뭐니 하는 온갖 낭만적인 관념과, 또 무슨 어리석은 생각들을 배워 오는데, 이 도회와 좋은 사교계가 겨우내 매달려도 좀처럼 뿌리 뽑지 못할 정도예요.\" \"정말이지, 부인,\""], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "경이 응했다."], ["replied my lord, “your cousin is of too immense a value to be thrown away; such ruin as this must be prevented.”", "\"부인의 사촌은 너무도 귀한 보배라 그렇게 내버려져서는 안 됩니다. 이런 파멸만큼은 막아야 합니다.\""], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["cries she, “my lord, how can it be prevented?", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"경, 어떻게 막을 수 있겠어요?"], ["The family have already done all in their power; but the girl is, I think, intoxicated, and nothing less than ruin will content her.", "집안에서는 이미 할 수 있는 일을 다 했어요. 하지만 그 처녀는, 제가 보기엔 완전히 취해 버렸고, 파멸이 아니고서는 아무것도 그녀를 만족시키지 못할 거예요."], ["And to deal more openly with you, I expect every day to hear she is run away with him.”", "더 터놓고 말씀드리자면, 저는 날마다 그녀가 그자와 달아났다는 소식을 들을까 봐 마음을 졸이고 있답니다.\" \"벨라스턴 부인, 지금 하신 말씀은,\""], ["“What you tell me, Lady Bellaston,”", "경께서 대답했다. \"제 마음을 더없이 아프게 합니다."], ["answered his lordship, “affects me most tenderly, and only raises my compassion, instead of lessening my adoration of your cousin.", "그리고 부인의 사촌에 대한 제 흠모를 줄이기는커녕 오히려 제 연민만 키우는군요. 그토록 값을 매길 수 없는 보석을 지킬 방도를 반드시 찾아야 합니다."], ["Some means must be found to preserve so inestimable a jewel.", "부인께서는 그녀를 타일러 보려 애써 보셨습니까?\" 여기서 부인은 짐짓 웃음을 지으며 외쳤다."], ["Hath your ladyship endeavoured to reason with her?”", "\"친애하는 경, 설마 젊은 여자를 타일러서 그 마음을 돌려놓을 수 있다고 말씀하실 만큼, 우리 여자들을 모르시지는 않으시겠지요?"], ["Here the lady affected a laugh, and cried, “My dear lord, sure you know us better than to talk of reasoning a young woman out of her inclinations?", "이 값진 보석들은 자기네가 걸친 보석만큼이나 귀가 먹었답니다. 시간이에요, 경, 시간만이 그들의 어리석음을 고칠 유일한 약이지요."], ["These inestimable jewels are as deaf as the jewels they wear: time, my lord, time is the only medicine to cure their folly; but this is a medicine which I am certain she will not take; nay, I live in hourly horrors on her account.", "하지만 이 약을, 그 처녀는 결코 받아들이지 않을 것이 분명해요. 아니, 저는 그녀 때문에 시시각각 끔찍한 두려움 속에 살고 있답니다."], ["In short, nothing but violent methods will do.”", "한마디로, 격렬한 수단이 아니고서는 안 되겠어요.\""], ["“What is to be done?”", "\"어찌하면 좋겠습니까?\""], ["cries my lord; “what methods are to be taken?--Is there any method upon earth?--Oh!", "경께서 외쳤다. \"어떤 수단을 써야 합니까?—이 세상에 무슨 방도가 있기는 합니까?—오!"], ["Lady Bellaston!", "벨라스턴 부인!"], ["there is nothing which I would not undertake for such a reward.”----“I really know not,”", "그런 보상을 위해서라면 제가 떠맡지 못할 일은 아무것도 없습니다.\"—— \"저도 정말 모르겠어요,\" 부인이 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 대답했다."], ["answered the lady, after a pause; and then pausing again, she cried out--“Upon my soul, I am at my wit's end on this girl's account.--If she can be preserved, something must be done immediately; and, as I say, nothing but violent methods will do.----If your lordship hath really this attachment to my cousin (and to do her justice, except in this silly inclination, of which she will soon see her folly, she is every way deserving), I think there may be one way, indeed, it is a very disagreeable one, and what I am almost afraid to think of.--It requires a great spirit, I promise you.”", "그러고는 다시 뜸을 들이다가 외쳤다.—— \"맹세코, 저는 이 처녀 일로 정말 어찌할 바를 모르겠어요.—그녀를 구할 수 있다면, 지금 당장 무언가를 해야 해요. 그리고 제가 말했듯, 격렬한 수단이 아니고서는 안 될 거예요.—만일 경께서 제 사촌에게 정말로 이만한 애착을 품고 계신다면(그리고 공정하게 말하자면, 머지않아 스스로 어리석음을 깨달을 이 한심한 마음만 빼면, 그녀는 어느 모로 보나 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 사람이에요), 한 가지 길이 있을 수도 있겠어요."], ["“I am not conscious, madam,”", "다만 정말이지 몹시 거북한 길이라, 떠올리기조차 거의 두려운 것이지요.—분명히 말씀드리는데, 그건 대단한 기개가 필요한 일이에요.\" \"부인,\""], ["said he, “of any defect there; nor am I, I hope, suspected of any such.", "그가 말했다. \"그 점에서라면 저는 제게 어떤 부족함도 없다고 자신합니다."], ["It must be an egregious defect indeed, which could make me backward on this occasion.”", "또 그런 의심을 받을 사람이라고도 생각지 않습니다. 이런 자리에서 저를 머뭇거리게 만들 수 있는 부족함이라면, 정말이지 어지간히 큰 것이어야 할 겁니다.\""], ["“Nay, my lord,”", "\"아니에요, 경,\""], ["answered she, “I am so far from doubting you, I am much more inclined to doubt my own courage; for I must run a monstrous risque.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"저는 경을 의심하기는커녕, 오히려 제 자신의 용기를 의심하는 쪽이에요."], ["In short, I must place such a confidence in your honour as a wise woman will scarce ever place in a man on any consideration.”", "저야말로 엄청난 위험을 무릅써야 하니까요. 한마디로, 저는 경의 명예에 대해, 어떤 사정을 두고서도 현명한 여자라면 좀처럼 남자에게 두지 않을 만한 신뢰를 두어야 하는 거예요.\""], ["In this point likewise my lord very well satisfied her; for his reputation was extremely clear, and common fame did him no more than justice, in speaking well of him.", "이 점에서도 경께서는 그녀를 흡족히 안심시켰다. 그의 평판은 더없이 깨끗했고, 세간의 소문도 그를 좋게 말하는 데 그저 마땅한 정도였으니 말이다."], ["“Well, then,”", "\"그럼, 좋아요,\""], ["said she, “my lord,--I--I vow, I can't bear the apprehension of it.--No, it must not be.----At least every other method shall be tried.", "그녀가 말했다. \"경,—저는—맹세코, 그 일을 떠올리기만 해도 견딜 수가 없군요.—아니에요, 그래선 안 돼요.—적어도 다른 모든 방법을 먼저 시도해 봐야 해요."], ["Can you get rid of your engagements, and dine here to-day?", "약속들을 미루고, 오늘 여기서 식사하실 수 있겠어요?"], ["Your lordship will have an opportunity of seeing a little more of Miss Western.--I promise you we have no time to lose.", "경께서는 웨스턴 양을 조금 더 가까이서 뵐 기회를 얻으실 거예요.—분명히 말씀드리는데 우리에게는 허비할 시간이 없어요."], ["Here will be nobody but Lady Betty, and Miss Eagle, and Colonel Hampsted, and Tom Edwards; they will all go soon--and I shall be at home to nobody.", "여기 베티 부인과, 이글 양과, 햄프스테드 대령과, 톰 에드워즈 말고는 아무도 없을 거예요. 그 사람들도 곧 다 갈 테고요—그러면 저는 누구에게도 자리를 비운 걸로 하겠어요."], ["Then your lordship may be a little more explicit.", "그때 경께서는 조금 더 속내를 털어놓으실 수 있을 거예요."], ["Nay, I will contrive some method to convince you of her attachment to this fellow.”", "아니, 제가 무슨 수를 써서라도 그녀가 이 사내에게 빠져 있다는 것을 경께 똑똑히 보여 드릴게요.\" 경께서는 적절한 인사를 건네고 초대를 받아들였다."], ["My lord made proper compliments, accepted the invitation, and then they parted to dress, it being now past three in the morning, or to reckon by the old style, in the afternoon.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 옷을 갈아입으러 헤어졌다. 때는 이미 새벽 세 시가 지난 시각, 옛 셈법으로 따지자면 오후였다."]], [["A further explanation of the foregoing design.", "비록 독자께서는 진작부터 벨라스턴 부인을 상류 사회의 일원으로, 그것도 결코 하찮지 않은 일원으로 여겨 왔을지 모르나, 그녀는 사실 작은 사회의 매우 비중 있는 일원이었다."], ["Though the reader may have long since concluded Lady Bellaston to be a member (and no inconsiderable one) of the great world; she was in reality a very considerable member of the little world; by which appellation was distinguished a very worthy and honourable society which not long since flourished in this kingdom.", "이 작은 사회라는 이름으로 불리던 것은, 얼마 전까지 이 왕국에서 융성했던 어느 매우 훌륭하고 명예로운 모임을 가리킨다."]], [["Among other good principles upon which this society was founded, there was one very remarkable; for, as it was a rule of an honourable club of heroes, who assembled at the close of the late war, that all the members should every day fight once at least; so 'twas in this, that every member should, within the twenty-four hours, tell at least one merry fib, which was to be propagated by all the brethren and sisterhood.", "이 모임이 세워진 여러 좋은 원칙 가운데, 유독 주목할 만한 것이 하나 있었다. 지난 전쟁이 끝날 무렵 모였던 어느 명예로운 영웅들의 클럽에 모든 회원은 날마다 적어도 한 번은 결투를 해야 한다는 규칙이 있었던 것처럼, 이 모임에는 모든 회원이 스물네 시간 안에 적어도 한 가지 유쾌한 거짓말을 지어내야 하며, 그것을 형제자매 회원 모두가 퍼뜨려야 한다는 규칙이 있었다."]], [["Many idle stories were told about this society, which from a certain quality may be, perhaps not unjustly, supposed to have come from the society themselves.", "이 모임을 두고 한가한 이야기들이 숱하게 떠돌았는데, 그 이야기들이 지닌 어떤 성질로 미루어 보면 어쩌면 그 모임 사람들 자신에게서 나온 것이라 짐작해도 그리 부당하지는 않을 것이다."], ["As, that the devil was the president; and that he sat in person in an elbow-chair at the upper end of the table; but, upon very strict enquiry, I find there is not the least truth in any of those tales, and that the assembly consisted in reality of a set of very good sort of people, and the fibs which they propagated were of a harmless kind, and tended only to produce mirth and good humour.", "이를테면 악마가 그 모임의 회장이라느니, 그가 몸소 식탁 상석의 팔걸이의자에 앉아 있다느니 하는 이야기들이다. 그러나 매우 엄밀히 알아본 결과, 나는 이런 이야기들 어디에도 티끌만 한 진실조차 없으며, 그 모임은 실은 매우 점잖은 부류의 사람들로 이루어졌고, 그들이 퍼뜨리던 거짓말도 해롭지 않은 종류여서 오로지 웃음과 좋은 기분을 자아내는 데 그쳤음을 알게 되었다."]], [["Edwards was likewise a member of this comical society.", "에드워즈 또한 이 익살스러운 모임의 일원이었다. 그래서 벨라스턴 부인은 그를 자기 목적에 알맞은 도구로 삼아, 그에게 거짓말 하나를 일러 주고는 자신이 신호를 보낼 때마다 그것을 풀어놓도록 했다."], ["To him therefore Lady Bellaston applied as a proper instrument for her purpose, and furnished him with a fib, which he was to vent whenever the lady gave him her cue; and this was not to be till the evening, when all the company but Lord Fellamar and himself were gone, and while they were engaged in a rubber at whist.", "그 신호는 저녁이 되기 전에는 보내지 않기로 했는데, 펠라마 경과 그를 제외한 손님들이 모두 돌아가고, 일행이 휘스트 한 판에 빠져 있을 때를 노린 것이었다. * 휘스트: 네 사람이 두 편으로 나뉘어 하는 카드놀이."]], [["To this time then, which was between seven and eight in the evening, we will convey our reader; when Lady Bellaston, Lord Fellamar, Miss Western, and Tom, being engaged at whist, and in the last game of their rubbers, Tom received his cue from Lady Bellaston, which was, “I protest, Tom, you are grown intolerable lately; you used to tell us all the news of the town, and now you know no more of the world than if you lived out of it.”", "그렇다면 이제 우리는 독자를 그 시각, 곧 저녁 일곱 시에서 여덟 시 사이로 모셔 가 보자. 벨라스턴 부인과 펠라마 경, 웨스턴 양과 톰이 휘스트에 빠져 마지막 판을 치르고 있을 때, 톰은 벨라스턴 부인에게서 신호를 받았다. 그 신호란 이런 말이었다. \"정말이지, 톰, 자네 요즘 못 봐주겠네그려. 예전엔 시내의 온갖 소식을 죄다 전해 주더니, 이제는 마치 세상 밖에 사는 사람처럼 아무것도 모르는구먼.\""]], [["Mr Edwards then began as follows: “The fault is not mine, madam: it lies in the dulness of the age, that doth nothing worth talking of.----O la! though now I think on't there hath a terrible accident befallen poor Colonel Wilcox.----Poor Ned.----You know him, my lord, everybody knows him; faith! I am very much concerned for him.”", "그러자 에드워즈가 이렇게 입을 열었다. \"제 탓이 아닙니다, 부인. 그건 이 시대가 따분해서 입에 올릴 만한 일을 하나도 만들어 내지 못하는 탓이지요. 아, 참! 그러고 보니 가엾은 윌콕스 대령에게 끔찍한 일이 벌어졌답니다. 불쌍한 네드 말입니다. 경께서도 그를 아시지요, 다들 아는 사람 아닙니까. 정말이지! 저는 그가 너무도 안쓰럽습니다.\""]], [["“What is it, pray?”", "\"무슨 일인데 그래요?\""], ["says Lady Bellaston.", "벨라스턴 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Why, he hath killed a man this morning in a duel, that's all.”", "\"그게, 오늘 아침에 결투로 사람을 하나 죽였답니다. 그게 다예요.\""]], [["His lordship, who was not in the secret, asked gravely, whom he had killed? To which Edwards answered, “A young fellow we none of us know; a Somersetshire lad just came to town, one Jones his name is; a near relation of one Mr Allworthy, of whom your lordship I believe hath heard. I saw the lad lie dead in a coffee-house.--Upon my soul, he is one of the finest corpses I ever saw in my life!”", "속사정을 모르는 경은 자못 엄숙하게 누구를 죽였느냐고 물었다. 이에 에드워즈가 대답했다. \"우리 가운데 아무도 모르는 젊은이입니다. 서머싯셔에서 막 상경한 청년인데, 이름이 존스라던가요. 경께서도 들어 보셨을 올워디라는 분의 가까운 친척이라더군요. 제가 그 청년이 어느 커피하우스에 죽어 누워 있는 걸 봤습니다. 맹세코, 제 평생 본 시신 중에 가장 잘생긴 시신이었어요!\" 톰이 사람이 죽었다는 말을 꺼냈을 때 마침 카드를 돌리기 시작했던 소피아는 손을 멈추고 귀를 기울였다.(그런 종류의 이야기는 무엇이든 그녀의 마음을 흔들었으므로) 그러나 그가 이야기의 뒷부분에 이르자 그녀는 다시 카드를 돌리기 시작했고, 한 사람에게 석 장, 다른 사람에게 일곱 장, 또 한 사람에게 열 장을 돌리다가 끝내 남은 카드를 손에서 떨어뜨리고는 의자에 쓰러지듯 기대고 말았다."]], [["Sophia, who had just began to deal as Tom had mentioned that a man was killed, stopt her hand, and listened with attention (for all stories of that kind affected her), but no sooner had he arrived at the latter part of the story than she began to deal again; and having dealt three cards to one, and seven to another, and ten to a third, at last dropt the rest from her hand, and fell back in her chair.", "좌중은 이런 경우에 흔히 그러듯이 행동했다. 으레 있을 법한 소동이 뒤따랐고, 으레 부르는 도움이 불려 왔으며, 소피아는 마침내, 으레 그렇듯이 다시 정신을 차렸다. 그러고는 곧 자신의 간절한 청에 따라 자기 방으로 이끌려 갔다. 그곳에서 경의 부탁으로 벨라스턴 부인은 그녀에게 진실을 알려 주었는데, 그것을 자기가 꾸민 농담으로 둘러대려 애쓰면서, 경도 톰도—비록 그 이야기를 자기가 톰에게 일러 준 것이긴 하나—이 일의 진짜 속내는 모른다고 거듭 다짐하며 그녀를 달랬다."]], [["The company behaved as usually on these occasions.", "벨라스턴 부인이 이 사건을 자신에게 얼마나 정확히 일러 주었는지 펠라마 경을 납득시키는 데에는 더 이상의 증거가 필요하지 않았다."], ["The usual disturbance ensued, the usual assistance was summoned, and Sophia at last, as it is usual, returned again to life, and was soon after, at her earnest desire, led to her own apartment; where, at my lord's request, Lady Bellaston acquainted her with the truth, attempted to carry it off as a jest of her own, and comforted her with repeated assurances, that neither his lordship nor Tom, though she had taught him the story, were in the true secret of the affair.", "그리하여 그녀가 방으로 돌아오자, 이 두 고귀한 인물 사이에 한 가지 계략이 짜였다. 그것이 경의 눈에는 그리 흉악한 일로 비치지 않았으나(그는 훗날 결혼으로 그 부인에게 자기 힘이 닿는 모든 보상을 해 주겠노라 진심으로 약속했고, 또 진심으로 그리 마음먹었으므로) 우리 독자들 가운데 많은 이는 분명 마땅한 혐오감으로 이 일을 바라보게 되리라는 것을 우리는 의심치 않는다."]], [["There was no farther evidence necessary to convince Lord Fellamar how justly the case had been represented to him by Lady Bellaston; and now, at her return into the room, a scheme was laid between these two noble persons, which, though it appeared in no very heinous light to his lordship (as he faithfully promised, and faithfully resolved too, to make the lady all the subsequent amends in his power by marriage), yet many of our readers, we doubt not, will see with just detestation.", "그 끔찍한 목적을 위해 이튿날 저녁 일곱 시가 정해졌다. 벨라스턴 부인은 그때 소피아를 혼자 있게 하고 경을 그녀에게 들여보내겠노라 맡았다. 온 집안이 그 목적에 맞게 단속될 것이며, 하인 대부분은 집 밖으로 내보낼 작정이었다. 그리고 의심을 사지 않도록 경이 올 때까지 안주인 곁에 남겨 둘 아너 부인은, 벨라스턴 부인이 몸소 나서서 그 못된 일이 벌어질 무대에서 되도록 멀리 떨어진 방으로, 소피아의 귀에 닿지 않는 곳으로 붙들어 두기로 했다."]], [["The next evening at seven was appointed for the fatal purpose, when Lady Bellaston undertook that Sophia should be alone, and his lordship should be introduced to her. The whole family were to be regulated for the purpose, most of the servants despatched out of the house; and for Mrs Honour, who, to prevent suspicion, was to be left with her mistress till his lordship's arrival, Lady Bellaston herself was to engage her in an apartment as distant as possible from the scene of the intended mischief, and out of the hearing of Sophia.", "이렇게 일이 합의되자 경은 작별을 고했고, 부인은 쉬러 물러갔다. 그녀는 성공을 의심할 까닭이 조금도 없는 이 계획에 무척 흡족해했다. 이 계획은 소피아가 존스와의 자기 정사에 더는 걸림돌이 되지 못하도록 그토록 확실히 치워 줄 것을 약속했으며, 설령 그 일이 세상에 드러난다 해도 자신은 결코 죄를 지은 사람으로 보이지 않을 방도였다. 게다가 그것마저도, 그녀는 능욕당한 소피아가 쉽사리 동의하게 될 것이고 나머지 식구들 모두가 기뻐할 결혼을 서둘러 치러 버림으로써 막을 수 있으리라 믿어 의심치 않았다. 그러나 또 한 사람의 공모자의 가슴속에서는 일이 그토록 잔잔하지가 못했다. 그의 마음은 셰익스피어가 그토록 고귀하게 그려 낸 그 어지러운 불안 속에 내던져져 있었다."]], [["Matters being thus agreed on, his lordship took his leave, and her ladyship retired to rest, highly pleased with a project, of which she had no reason to doubt the success, and which promised so effectually to remove Sophia from being any further obstruction to her amour with Jones, by a means of which she should never appear to be guilty, even if the fact appeared to the world; but this she made no doubt of preventing by huddling up a marriage, to which she thought the ravished Sophia would easily be brought to consent, and at which all the rest of her family would rejoice.", "\"끔찍한 일을 저지르기까지,\n그 첫 충동과 실행 사이의 모든 틈은"]], [["But affairs were not in so quiet a situation in the bosom of the other conspirator; his mind was tost in all the distracting anxiety so nobly described by Shakespear--", "허깨비 같고, 흉측한 꿈만 같아라. 혼과 죽을 운명의 도구들이"]], [["“Between the acting of a dreadful thing, And the first motion, all the interim is Like a phantasma, or a hideous dream; The genius and the mortal instruments Are then in council; and the state of man, Like to a little kingdom, suffers then The nature of an insurrection.”----", "그때 회의를 열고, 사람의 처지란\n작은 왕국과도 같아, 그때 한바탕"]], [["Though the violence of his passion had made him eagerly embrace the first hint of this design, especially as it came from a relation of the lady, yet when that friend to reflection, a pillow, had placed the action itself in all its natural black colours before his eyes, with all the consequences which must, and those which might probably attend it, his resolution began to abate, or rather indeed to go over to the other side; and after a long conflict, which lasted a whole night, between honour and appetite, the former at length prevailed, and he determined to wait on Lady Bellaston, and to relinquish the design.", "반란의 형국을 겪느니라.\" 그의 격렬한 정념은 이 계획의 첫마디를 덥석 끌어안게 했고, 더욱이 그것이 그 부인의 친척에게서 나온 것이었기에 그러했다. 그러나 사색의 벗인 베개가 그 행위 자체를, 본디 지녔던 시커먼 빛깔 그대로, 반드시 뒤따를 결과와 어쩌면 뒤따를지도 모를 결과를 모두 그의 눈앞에 펼쳐 보이자, 그의 결심은 누그러지기 시작했다. 아니, 차라리 정반대 편으로 넘어가 버렸다. 그리고 명예와 욕망 사이에 밤새도록 이어진 기나긴 다툼 끝에 마침내 명예가 이겼고, 그는 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아가 그 계획을 단념하겠노라 마음먹었다."]], [["Lady Bellaston was in bed, though very late in the morning, and Sophia sitting by her bed-side, when the servant acquainted her that Lord Fellamar was below in the parlour; upon which her ladyship desired him to stay, and that she would see him presently; but the servant was no sooner departed than poor Sophia began to intreat her cousin not to encourage the visits of that odious lord (so she called him, though a little unjustly) upon her account. “I see his design,” said she; “for he made downright love to me yesterday morning; but as I am resolved never to admit it, I beg your ladyship not to leave us alone together any more, and to order the servants that, if he enquires for me, I may be always denied to him.”", "아침이 꽤 늦은 시각이었는데도 벨라스턴 부인은 아직 침대에 누워 있었고, 소피아는 그 머리맡에 앉아 있었다. 그때 하인이 펠라마 경이 아래층 응접실에 와 있다고 알렸다. 이에 부인은 그더러 기다리라 하고는 곧 만나러 가겠노라 했다. 그러나 하인이 물러가기가 무섭게 가엾은 소피아는 사촌에게 자기 때문에 그 가증스러운 경(그녀는 그를 그렇게 불렀으나, 조금은 억울한 표현이었다)의 방문을 부추기지 말아 달라고 애원하기 시작했다. \"저는 그 사람의 속셈을 알아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어제 아침에 저한테 대놓고 구애를 했거든요. 하지만 저는 절대로 그걸 받아들이지 않기로 마음먹었으니, 부디 다시는 저희 둘만 남겨 두지 말아 주시고, 하인들에게도 일러 두세요. 그가 저를 찾거든 늘 만날 수 없다고 돌려보내라고요.\""]], [["“La!", "\"어머!"], ["child,”", "얘야.\""], ["says Lady Bellaston, “you country girls have nothing but sweethearts in your head; you fancy every man who is civil to you is making love.", "벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"너희 시골 처녀들은 머릿속에 사내 생각밖에 없구나."], ["He is one of the most gallant young fellows about town, and I am convinced means no more than a little gallantry.", "너한테 친절하게 구는 남자는 죄다 구애를 하는 줄 아는구나. 그분은 이 도시에서 둘째가라면 서러운 멋쟁이 청년인데, 나는 그가 그저 가벼운 호의를 베푼 것에 지나지 않는다고 믿는다."], ["Make love to you indeed!", "너한테 구애를 하다니!"], ["I wish with all my heart he would, and you must be an arrant mad woman to refuse him.”", "진심으로 나야 그분이 그래 주기를 바란다만, 그분을 마다하다니 너는 영락없이 미친 여자임에 틀림없겠구나.\""]], [["“But as I shall certainly be that mad woman,”", "\"하지만 저는 분명 그 미친 여자가 될 테니,\""], ["cries Sophia, “I hope his visits shall not be intruded upon me.”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"그분의 방문이 제게 밀고 들어오지 않기를 바랄 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“O child!”", "\"오, 얘야!\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다."], ["said Lady Bellaston, “you need not be so fearful; if you resolve to run away with that Jones, I know no person who can hinder you.”", "\"그렇게 겁낼 것까지는 없단다. 네가 그 존스라는 사람과 달아나기로 작정한 거라면, 너를 막을 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없을 테니 말이다.\""]], [["“Upon my honour, madam,”", "\"제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리지만, 부인,\""], ["cries Sophia, “your ladyship injures me.", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"부인께서는 저를 모욕하고 계세요."], ["I will never run away with any man; nor will I ever marry contrary to my father's inclinations.”", "저는 결코 어떤 남자와도 달아나지 않을 것이며, 아버지의 뜻을 거슬러 혼인하지도 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, Miss Western,”", "\"좋다, 웨스턴 양.\" 부인이 말했다."], ["said the lady, “if you are not in a humour to see company this morning, you may retire to your own apartment; for I am not frightened at his lordship, and must send for him up into my dressing-room.”", "\"오늘 아침 손님을 맞을 기분이 아니라면, 네 방으로 물러가 있으렴. 나는 경이 두렵지 않으니, 그를 내 화장실로 불러올려야겠다.\""]], [["Sophia thanked her ladyship, and withdrew; and presently afterwards Fellamar was admitted upstairs.", "소피아는 부인에게 고마움을 표하고 물러갔다. 그리고 곧이어 펠라마 경이 위층으로 안내되어 들어왔다."]], [["# Chapter iv.", "# Chapter iv."]], [["By which it will appear how dangerous an advocate a lady is when she applies her eloquence to an ill purpose.", "어느 부인이 자신의 능변을 나쁜 목적에 쏟을 때 그 변호가 얼마나 위험한 것이 되는지를 보여 주는 장."]], [["When Lady Bellaston heard the young lord's scruples, she treated them with the same disdain with which one of those sages of the law, called Newgate solicitors, treats the qualms of conscience in a young witness.", "벨라스턴 부인은 젊은 귀족이 망설이는 것을 듣자, 뉴게이트 변호사라 불리는 법조계의 그 현인들이 젊은 증인의 양심의 가책을 다루는 것과 똑같은 경멸로 그 망설임을 다루었다. \"친애하는 경이시여,\""], ["“My dear lord,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “you certainly want a cordial.", "\"분명 강심제가 필요하신 모양이군요."], ["I must send to Lady Edgely for one of her best drams. Fie upon it!", "에지리 부인에게 사람을 보내 그 댁의 가장 좋은 술 한 잔을 가져오게 해야겠어요."], ["have more resolution.", "원, 부끄러운 줄 아셔야지요!"], ["Are you frightened by the word rape?", "좀 더 결단력을 가지세요. 능욕이라는 말에 겁이 나시는 건가요?"], ["Or are you apprehensive----?", "아니면 무언가 걱정이 되시는——?"], ["Well!", "자!"], ["if the story of Helen was modern, I should think it unnatural.", "헬레네의 이야기가 만일 요즘 일이었다면, 저는 그것을 부자연스럽다 여겼을 거예요."], ["I mean the behaviour of Paris, not the fondness of the lady; for all women love a man of spirit.", "제 말은 파리스의 행동을 두고 하는 말이지, 그 부인의 애틋한 마음을 두고 하는 말이 아니랍니다. 여자란 모두 기개 있는 남자를 사랑하니까요."], ["There is another story of the Sabine ladies--and that too, I thank heaven, is very antient.", "사비니 여인들에 관한 또 다른 이야기도 있지요——그리고 그것 또한, 다행히도, 아주 오래된 이야기예요. 경께서는 어쩌면 제 독서량에 감탄하실지 모르겠습니다만, 후크 씨가 우리에게 일러 주기로는, 그 여인들이 그 뒤로 제법 괜찮은 아내가 되었다더군요."], ["Your lordship, perhaps, will admire my reading; but I think Mr Hook tells us, they made tolerable good wives afterwards.", "제가 아는 결혼한 부인들 가운데 자기 남편에게 능욕당한 이는 거의 없는 듯합니다.\" \"아니, 친애하는 벨라스턴 부인,\""], ["I fancy few of my married acquaintance were ravished by their husbands.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"저를 이런 식으로 조롱하지 마십시오.\""], ["“Nay, dear Lady Bellaston,”", "\"어머, 친절하신 경이시여,\""], ["cried he, “don't ridicule me in this manner.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"영국의 어떤 여자라도, 겉으로는 아무리 정숙한 척을 하더라도, 속으로는 경을 비웃지 않으리라 생각하시나요?——경께서는 저로 하여금 이상한 말투를 쓰게 만드시고, 제 성(性)을 가장 고약하게 배신하게 만드시는군요."], ["“Why, my good lord,”", "하지만 저는 제 의도가 선하다는 것을, 그리고 제가 제 사촌을 도우려 애쓰고 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로 만족합니다."], ["answered she, “do you think any woman in England would not laugh at you in her heart, whatever prudery she might wear in her countenance?----You force me to use a strange kind of language, and to betray my sex most abominably; but I am contented with knowing my intentions are good, and that I am endeavouring to serve my cousin; for I think you will make her a husband notwithstanding this; or, upon my soul, I would not even persuade her to fling herself away upon an empty title.", "이런 일에도 불구하고 경께서 그 아이를 아내로 맞아 좋은 남편이 되어 주시리라 생각하기 때문이지요. 그렇지 않았다면, 맹세코, 저는 그 아이에게 속 빈 작위 하나에 자신을 내던지라고 권하지도 않았을 거예요."], ["She should not upbraid me hereafter with having lost a man of spirit; for that his enemies allow this poor young fellow to be.”", "그 아이가 훗날 저더러 기개 있는 남자를 놓치게 했다고 원망하는 일은 없어야 하니까요. 적들조차 이 가엾은 젊은이가 그런 사람임은 인정한답니다.\""]], [["Let those who have had the satisfaction of hearing reflections of this kind from a wife or a mistress, declare whether they are at all sweetened by coming from a female tongue.", "아내나 정부의 입에서 이런 식의 빈정거림을 들어 본 만족을 누려 본 이들이라면, 그것이 여자의 혀에서 나온다 하여 조금이라도 달콤해지는지 어디 한번 말해 보라."], ["Certain it is, they sunk deeper into his lordship than anything which Demosthenes or Cicero could have said on the occasion.", "분명한 것은, 그 말이 데모스테네스나 키케로가 그 자리에서 무슨 말을 했더라도 그보다 더 깊이 그 귀족의 가슴속에 박혔다는 사실이다."]], [["Lady Bellaston, perceiving she had fired the young lord's pride, began now, like a true orator, to rouse other passions to its assistance. “My lord,” says she, in a graver voice, “you will be pleased to remember, you mentioned this matter to me first; for I would not appear to you in the light of one who is endeavouring to put off my cousin upon you. Fourscore thousand pounds do not stand in need of an advocate to recommend them.” “Nor doth Miss Western,” said he, “require any recommendation from her fortune; for, in my opinion, no woman ever had half her charms.” “Yes, yes, my lord,” replied the lady, looking in the glass, “there have been women with more than half her charms, I assure you; not that I need lessen her on that account: she is a most delicious girl, that's certain; and within these few hours she will be in the arms of one, who surely doth not deserve her, though I will give him his due, I believe he is truly a man of spirit.”", "벨라스턴 부인은 자신이 젊은 귀족의 자존심에 불을 붙였음을 알아채자, 이제 참된 웅변가답게 그 불길을 돕도록 다른 정념들을 일깨우기 시작했다. \"경이시여,\" 그녀가 한결 진중한 목소리로 말했다. \"이 일을 제게 먼저 꺼내신 분이 경이라는 사실을 부디 기억해 주세요. 저는 경께 제 사촌을 떠넘기려 애쓰는 사람처럼 비치고 싶지 않으니까요. 팔만 파운드라는 재산은 그것을 천거해 줄 변호인이 필요한 물건이 아니랍니다.\" \"웨스턴 양 또한,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 재산이 천거해 줄 필요가 없는 사람입니다. 제 생각으로는, 그 절반의 매력이라도 지녔던 여자조차 일찍이 없었으니까요.\" \"그럼요, 그럼요, 경이시여,\" 부인이 거울을 들여다보며 대답했다. \"그 아이의 매력의 절반 이상을 지녔던 여자들도 있었답니다, 정말이에요. 그렇다고 제가 그 때문에 그 아이를 깎아내릴 필요야 없겠지요. 더없이 사랑스러운 아이인 것만은 분명하니까요. 그리고 앞으로 몇 시간 안에 그 아이는 어느 남자의 품에 안기게 될 거예요. 그 남자가 그 아이를 받을 자격이 있다고는 결코 할 수 없겠지만, 그래도 공정하게 말하자면, 저는 그가 참으로 기개 있는 남자라고 믿는답니다.\""]], [["“I hope so, madam,”", "\"그렇기를 바랍니다, 부인,\" 귀족이 말했다."], ["said my lord; “though I must own he doth not deserve her; for, unless heaven or your ladyship disappoint me, she shall within that time be in mine.”", "\"비록 그가 그녀를 받을 자격이 없다는 것은 저도 인정해야 하겠지만 말입니다. 하늘이나 부인께서 저를 실망시키지 않으신다면, 그 시간 안에 그녀는 제 품에 안기게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["“Well spoken, my lord,”", "\"훌륭한 말씀이세요, 경이시여,\" 부인이 대답했다."], ["answered the lady; “I promise you no disappointment shall happen from my side; and within this week I am convinced I shall call your lordship my cousin in public.”", "\"제 쪽에서는 어떤 실망도 일어나지 않으리라 약속드리지요. 그리고 이번 주 안에 저는 사람들 앞에서 경을 제 사촌이라 부르게 되리라 확신합니다.\""]], [["The remainder of this scene consisted entirely of raptures, excuses, and compliments, very pleasant to have heard from the parties; but rather dull when related at second hand.", "이 장면의 나머지는 온통 황홀한 찬사와 변명과 인사치레로 이루어졌으니, 당사자들에게서 직접 듣기에는 무척 즐거웠을 테지만, 남의 입을 거쳐 전해 들으니 어쩐지 따분하다."], ["Here, therefore, we shall put an end to this dialogue, and hasten to the fatal hour when everything was prepared for the destruction of poor Sophia.", "그러니 우리는 여기서 이 대화를 끝맺고, 가엾은 소피아를 파멸시킬 모든 채비가 갖추어진 그 운명의 시각으로 서둘러 나아가기로 하자."]], [["But this being the most tragical matter in our whole history, we shall treat it in a chapter by itself.", "다만 이것이 우리의 이야기 전체를 통틀어 가장 비극적인 사건인 만큼, 우리는 따로 한 장을 마련하여 다루기로 하겠다."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["Containing some matters which may affect, and others which may surprize, the reader.", "독자의 마음을 흔들 만한 일들과, 독자를 놀라게 할 만한 일들을 담은 장."]], [["The clock had now struck seven, and poor Sophia, alone and melancholy, sat reading a tragedy.", "시계가 막 일곱 시를 알렸고, 가엾은 소피아는 홀로 우울에 잠긴 채 비극 한 편을 읽고 있었다."], ["It was the Fatal Marriage; and she was now come to that part where the poor distrest Isabella disposes of her wedding-ring.", "그것은 《치명적인 결혼》이었고, 그녀는 마침 가엾고 비탄에 잠긴 이사벨라가 결혼반지를 처분하는 대목에 이르러 있었다."]], [["Here the book dropt from her hand, and a shower of tears ran down into her bosom.", "바로 그 순간 책이 손에서 미끄러져 떨어졌고, 한 줄기 눈물이 가슴께로 흘러내렸다. 그렇게 일 분쯤 있었을 때 문이 열리며 펠라마 경이 들어왔다."], ["In this situation she had continued a minute, when the door opened, and in came Lord Fellamar.", "소피아는 그가 들어서자 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰고, 그는 앞으로 나서며 깊이 허리를 숙여 인사하고는 말했다. \"웨스턴 양, 이렇게 갑작스레 찾아와 무례를 범하는 게 아닌가 두렵습니다.\""], ["Sophia started from her chair at his entrance; and his lordship advancing forwards, and making a low bow, said, “I am afraid, Miss Western, I break in upon you abruptly.”", "\"정말이지, 경께서,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Indeed, my lord,”", "\"이 뜻밖의 방문에 제가 조금 놀랐음을 고백하지 않을 수 없군요.\" \"이 방문이 뜻밖이라면, 아가씨,\""], ["says she, “I must own myself a little surprized at this unexpected visit.”", "펠라마 경이 답했다. \"지난번 영광스럽게도 당신을 뵈었을 때, 제 두 눈이 제 마음을 너무도 미덥지 못하게 전한 셈이겠지요."], ["“If this visit be unexpected, madam,”", "그렇지 않고서야 당신이, 제 마음을 당신 손안에 붙잡아 두고도 그 주인의 방문을 기대하지 않으셨을 리가 없으니 말입니다.\" 소피아는 당황한 와중에도 이 허풍에 (내 생각엔 매우 마땅하게도) 상상조차 할 수 없을 만큼 멸시 어린 눈빛으로 답했다."], ["answered Lord Fellamar, “my eyes must have been very faithless interpreters of my heart, when last I had the honour of seeing you; for surely you could not otherwise have hoped to detain my heart in your possession, without receiving a visit from its owner.”", "그러자 그는 똑같은 투의, 더 긴 연설을 한 차례 더 늘어놓았다. 그 말에 소피아는 떨면서 말했다."], ["Sophia, confused as she was, answered this bombast (and very properly I think) with a look of inconceivable disdain.", "\"정말로 경께서 제정신이 아니시라고 여겨야 하는 건가요? 분명, 경이시여, 그런 행동에는 달리 변명의 여지가 없을 텐데요.\""], ["My lord then made another and a longer speech of the same sort.", "\"정말로, 아가씨, 저는 당신이 짐작하시는 그 상태에 빠져 있습니다.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["Upon which Sophia, trembling, said, “Am I really to conceive your lordship to be out of your senses?", "\"그리고 분명 당신은, 바로 당신 자신이 불러일으킨 이 광기의 결과를 용서해 주시겠지요. 사랑이 제게서 이성을 완전히 앗아가 버려, 저는 제 어떤 행동에도 거의 책임을 질 수 없을 지경이니까요.\""], ["Sure, my lord, there is no other excuse for such behaviour.”", "\"맹세코 말씀드리지만, 경이시여,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["“I am, indeed, madam, in the situation you suppose,”", "\"저는 당신의 말도 당신의 행동도 도무지 이해할 수 없어요.\" \"그렇다면 아가씨, 부디 허락해 주십시오.\""], ["cries his lordship; “and sure you will pardon the effects of a frenzy which you yourself have occasioned; for love hath so totally deprived me of reason, that I am scarce accountable for any of my actions.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"당신의 발치에서 그 둘을 다 설명하게 해 주십시오."], ["“Upon my word, my lord,”", "제 영혼을 당신께 활짝 열어 보이며, 제가 미칠 듯한 극한에 이르도록 당신을 흠모하고 있음을 고백하게 해 주십시오. 오, 더없이 사랑스럽고, 더없이 거룩한 분이시여!"], ["said Sophia, “I neither understand your words nor your behaviour.”", "어떤 말로 제 마음의 심정을 다 표현할 수 있겠습니까?\" \"분명히 말씀드리지만, 경이시여,\""], ["“Suffer me then, madam,”", "소피아가 말했다. \"저는 이런 말을 더는 듣고 있지 않겠어요.\""], ["cries he, “at your feet to explain both, by laying open my soul to you, and declaring that I doat on you to the highest degree of distraction.", "\"그러지 마십시오.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["O most adorable, most divine creature!", "\"그렇게 잔인하게 저를 떠날 생각은 마십시오. 제가 느끼는 고통의 절반만이라도 아신다면, 그 부드러운 가슴이 저 두 눈이 불러온 일을 가엾이 여기실 텐데요.\""], ["what language can express the sentiments of my heart?”", "그러더니 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 그녀의 손을 붙잡고는, 몇 분 동안 어떤 어조로 계속 말을 쏟아냈는데, 그것은 그 숙녀에게 못지않게 독자에게도 그리 즐겁지 않을 것이다. 그리고 마침내 이렇게 단언하며 끝을 맺었다."], ["“I do assure you, my lord,”", "\"만일 제가 온 세상의 주인이라면, 그것을 당신 발치에 바치겠습니다.\" 그러자 소피아는 그의 손에서 자기 손을 억지로 빼내며 자못 기세 있게 답했다."], ["said Sophia, “I shall not stay to hear any more of this.”", "\"분명히 말해 두지만, 선생님, 당신의 세상이든 그 주인이든, 저는 똑같이 경멸하며 내쳐 버릴 거예요.\" 그러고는 가려고 하자, 펠라마 경은 다시 그녀의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["“Do not,”", "\"용서하십시오, 사랑하는 나의 천사여, 절망이 아니었다면 결코 감히 저지르지 못했을 이 무례를."], ["cries he, “think of leaving me thus cruelly; could you know half the torments which I feel, that tender bosom must pity what those eyes have caused.”", "믿어 주십시오, 만일 제 작위와 재산이 — 당신의 가치에 견주지 않는 한 둘 다 결코 하찮지 않은 것들이지만 — 받아들여지리라는 한 가닥 희망이라도 품을 수 있었다면, 저는 더없이 겸손하게 그것들을 당신 앞에 바쳤을 것입니다. 하지만 저는 당신을 잃을 수가 없습니다."], ["Then fetching a deep sigh, and laying hold of her hand, he ran on for some minutes in a strain which would be little more pleasing to the reader than it was to the lady; and at last concluded with a declaration, “That if he was master of the world, he would lay it at her feet.”", "하늘에 맹세코, 차라리 제 영혼을 내놓겠습니다! 당신은, 당신은 반드시, 오직 저만의 사람이어야 합니다.\""], ["Sophia then, forcibly pulling away her hand from his, answered with much spirit, “I promise you, sir, your world and its master I should spurn from me with equal contempt.”", "\"경이시여,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["She then offered to go; and Lord Fellamar, again laying hold of her hand, said, “Pardon me, my beloved angel, freedoms which nothing but despair could have tempted me to take.----Believe me, could I have had any hope that my title and fortune, neither of them inconsiderable, unless when compared with your worth, would have been accepted, I had, in the humblest manner, presented them to your acceptance.----But I cannot lose you.--By heaven, I will sooner part with my soul!--You are, you must, you shall be only mine.”", "\"부디 헛된 추구를 그만두시기를 간청합니다. 제 명예를 걸고, 저는 이 일에 관해 결코 당신의 말을 듣지 않겠어요."], ["“My lord,”", "제 손을 놓으세요, 경이시여. 저는 지금 이 순간 당신에게서 떠나기로 결심했으니까요."], ["says she, “I intreat you to desist from a vain pursuit; for, upon my honour, I will never hear you on this subject.", "그리고 다시는 당신을 보지 않겠어요.\" \"그렇다면, 아가씨,\""], ["Let go my hand, my lord; for I am resolved to go from you this moment; nor will I ever see you more.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"저는 이 순간을 최대한 활용해야겠습니다."], ["“Then, madam,”", "저는 당신 없이는 살 수 없고, 살지도 않을 테니까요.\" \"무슨 뜻이세요, 경이시여?\""], ["cries his lordship, “I must make the best use of this moment; for I cannot live, nor will I live without you.”----“What do you mean, my lord?”", "소피아가 말했다. \"제가 온 집안 사람을 불러 모으겠어요.\""], ["said Sophia; “I will raise the family.”", "\"저는 아무것도 두렵지 않습니다, 아가씨.\" 그가 답했다."], ["“I have no fear, madam,”", "\"당신을 잃는 것 말고는요. 그리고 그것만큼은, 절망이 제게 가리키는 단 하나의 방법으로라도 막기로 결심했습니다.\""], ["answered he, “but of losing you, and that I am resolved to prevent, the only way which despair points to me.”--He then caught her in his arms: upon which she screamed so loud, that she must have alarmed some one to her assistance, had not Lady Bellaston taken care to remove all ears.", "그러더니 그는 그녀를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안았다. 그 순간 그녀가 어찌나 크게 비명을 질렀던지, 벨라스턴 부인이 모든 귀를 미리 치워 두지 않았더라면 분명 누군가가 그녀를 도우러 달려왔을 것이다."]], [["But a more lucky circumstance happened for poor Sophia; another noise now broke forth, which almost drowned her cries; for now the whole house rang with, “Where is she?", "그러나 가엾은 소피아에게는 더 다행스러운 일이 벌어졌다. 이제 또 다른 소란이 터져 나와 그녀의 비명을 거의 묻어 버릴 지경이었던 것이다."], ["D--n me, I'll unkennel her this instant.", "온 집 안이 이런 고함으로 울렸다. \"그 애가 어디 있어?"], ["Show me her chamber, I say.", "빌어먹을, 당장 그 애를 굴에서 끌어내고 말겠다. 그 애 방을 보여 달란 말이야."], ["Where is my daughter?", "내 딸이 어디 있어?"], ["I know she's in the house, and I'll see her if she's above-ground.", "그 애가 이 집에 있는 걸 다 알아. 땅 위에 살아 있다면 내가 기어이 보고 말겠어."], ["Show me where she is.”--At which last words the door flew open, and in came Squire Western, with his parson and a set of myrmidons at his heels.", "어디 있는지 보여 달라니까.\" 마지막 말과 함께 문이 벌컥 열리며, 웨스턴 영주가 자기 목사와 한 무리의 졸개들을 뒤에 거느리고 들어왔다."]], [["How miserable must have been the condition of poor Sophia, when the enraged voice of her father was welcome to her ears!", "가엾은 소피아의 처지가 얼마나 비참했으면, 분노에 찬 아버지의 목소리가 그녀의 귀에 반갑게 들렸겠는가!"], ["Welcome indeed it was, and luckily did he come; for it was the only accident upon earth which could have preserved the peace of her mind from being for ever destroyed.", "정말로 반가웠고, 그가 온 것은 더없이 다행스러운 일이었다. 그것이야말로, 그녀의 마음의 평화가 영영 부서져 버리는 것을 막아 줄 수 있었던, 이 세상 단 하나의 우연이었으니 말이다."]], [["Sophia, notwithstanding her fright, presently knew her father's voice; and his lordship, notwithstanding his passion, knew the voice of reason, which peremptorily assured him, it was not now a time for the perpetration of his villany.", "소피아는 그 놀라움 속에서도 곧바로 아버지의 목소리를 알아챘고, 펠라마 경 역시 그 격정 속에서도 이성의 목소리를 알아들었으니, 그것은 지금이 그 악행을 저지를 때가 아님을 단호하게 그에게 일러 주었다."], ["Hearing, therefore, the voice approach, and hearing likewise whose it was (for as the squire more than once roared forth the word daughter, so Sophia, in the midst of her struggling, cried out upon her father), he thought proper to relinquish his prey, having only disordered her handkerchief, and with his rude lips committed violence on her lovely neck.", "그리하여 그 목소리가 가까이 다가오는 것을 듣고, 또 그것이 누구의 목소리인지도 듣고 나서 (영주가 딸이라는 말을 한 번 이상 고래고래 외쳐 댔듯, 소피아 역시 몸부림치는 와중에 아버지를 부르며 소리쳤으므로), 그는 자기 먹이를 놓아주는 것이 옳다고 여겼다. 다만 그녀의 손수건을 흐트러뜨리고, 그 무례한 입술로 그녀의 사랑스러운 목에 폭력을 가했을 뿐이었다."]], [["If the reader's imagination doth not assist me, I shall never be able to describe the situation of these two persons when Western came into the room.", "독자의 상상력이 나를 거들어 주지 않는다면, 웨스턴이 방 안으로 들어왔을 때 이 두 사람이 처한 상황을 나는 도저히 그려낼 수 없을 것이다."], ["Sophia tottered into a chair, where she sat disordered, pale, breathless, bursting with indignation at Lord Fellamar; affrighted, and yet more rejoiced, at the arrival of her father.", "소피아는 비틀거리며 의자에 주저앉았고, 거기서 흐트러진 채, 창백하고, 숨이 가쁘고, 펠라마 경에 대한 분노로 터질 듯한 모습으로 앉아 있었다. 아버지의 등장에 겁을 먹었으면서도, 그보다 더 기뻐하면서."]], [["His lordship sat down near her, with the bag of his wig hanging over one of his shoulders, the rest of his dress being somewhat disordered, and rather a greater proportion of linen than is usual appearing at his bosom.", "펠라마 경은 그녀 가까이에 앉았는데, 가발의 머리 주머니가 한쪽 어깨 위로 늘어져 있었고, 나머지 차림새도 다소 흐트러진 데다, 평소보다 좀 많은 양의 속옷이 가슴께로 드러나 보였다."], ["As to the rest, he was amazed, affrighted, vexed, and ashamed.", "그 밖의 점으로 말하자면, 그는 어리둥절하고, 겁먹고, 짜증나고, 부끄러워하고 있었다."]], [["As to Squire Western, he happened at this time to be overtaken by an enemy, which very frequently pursues, and seldom fails to overtake, most of the country gentlemen in this kingdom. He was, literally speaking, drunk; which circumstance, together with his natural impetuosity, could produce no other effect than his running immediately up to his daughter, upon whom he fell foul with his tongue in the most inveterate manner; nay, he had probably committed violence with his hands, had not the parson interposed, saying, “For heaven's sake, sir, animadvert that you are in the house of a great lady. Let me beg you to mitigate your wrath; it should minister a fulness of satisfaction that you have found your daughter; for as to revenge, it belongeth not unto us. I discern great contrition in the countenance of the young lady. I stand assured, if you will forgive her, she will repent her of all past offences, and return unto her duty.”", "웨스턴 영주로 말하자면, 그는 마침 이때 한 적에게 따라잡혀 있었는데, 그 적이란 이 왕국 시골 향신들 대부분을 매우 자주 쫓아다니며 좀처럼 따라잡는 데 실패하는 법이 없는 놈이었다. 그는 말 그대로 취해 있었다. 이 사정에 그의 타고난 불같은 성미까지 더해지자, 다른 어떤 결과도 나올 수 없었으니, 그는 곧장 딸에게 달려가 가장 끈질긴 방식으로 입으로 욕을 퍼부어 댔다. 아니, 목사가 끼어들지 않았더라면 아마 손으로도 폭력을 휘둘렀을 것이다. 목사는 이렇게 말했다. \"제발 부탁드립니다, 나리, 여기가 어느 지체 높은 부인의 댁임을 헤아리십시오. 분노를 누그러뜨리시기를 간청드립니다. 따님을 찾으셨으니 마땅히 그것으로 가득한 만족을 누리셔야 합니다. 복수로 말하자면, 그것은 우리에게 속한 것이 아니니까요. 저 젊은 아가씨의 얼굴에서 깊은 뉘우침이 보입니다. 확신컨대, 나리께서 용서해 주시면, 아가씨는 지난 모든 잘못을 회개하고 본분으로 돌아올 것입니다.\""]], [["The strength of the parson's arms had at first been of more service than the strength of his rhetoric.", "목사의 팔 힘은 애초에 그의 수사학적 힘보다 더 쓸모가 있었다. 그래도 그의 마지막 말이 어느 정도 효과를 냈는지, 영주가 답했다."], ["However, his last words wrought some effect, and the squire answered, “I'll forgee her if she wull ha un.", "\"그놈만 받아들이면 용서해 주마. 그놈만 받으면, 소피, 다 용서해 줄게."], ["If wot ha un, Sophy, I'll forgee thee all.", "왜 말이 없어? 그놈을 받아야 해!"], ["Why dost unt speak? Shat ha un!", "빌어먹을, 받아야 한다고!"], ["d--n me, shat ha un!", "왜 대답이 없어?"], ["Why dost unt answer? Was ever such a stubborn tuoad?”", "이렇게 고집 센 두꺼비 같은 게 또 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Let me intreat you, sir, to be a little more moderate,”", "\"부디 간청드립니다, 나리, 좀 더 누그러지십시오.\""], ["said the parson; “you frighten the young lady so, that you deprive her of all power of utterance.”", "목사가 말했다. \"아가씨를 어찌나 겁주시는지, 입을 뗄 기력조차 다 앗아가고 계십니다.\""]], [["“Power of mine a--,”", "\"기력은 무슨 빌어먹을 기력.\""], ["answered the squire.", "영주가 답했다."], ["“You take her part then, you do?", "\"그럼 자네는 저 애 편을 드는 거야, 응?"], ["A pretty parson, truly, to side with an undutiful child!", "참 잘난 목사로군, 불효막심한 자식 편을 들다니!"], ["Yes, yes, I will gee you a living with a pox.", "그래, 그래, 빌어먹을 성직록을 자네한테 주지."], ["I'll gee un to the devil sooner.”", "차라리 악마한테 주고 말지.\""]], [["“I humbly crave your pardon,”", "\"황송하오나 용서를 빕니다.\""], ["said the parson; “I assure your worship I meant no such matter.”", "목사가 말했다. \"나리께 맹세컨대 저는 그런 뜻이 전혀 아니었습니다.\""]], [["My Lady Bellaston now entered the room, and came up to the squire, who no sooner saw her, than, resolving to follow the instructions of his sister, he made her a very civil bow, in the rural manner, and paid her some of his best compliments. He then immediately proceeded to his complaints, and said, “There, my lady cousin; there stands the most undutiful child in the world; she hankers after a beggarly rascal, and won't marry one of the greatest matches in all England, that we have provided for her.”", "이제 벨라스턴 부인이 방으로 들어와 영주에게 다가왔다. 영주는 그녀를 보자마자, 누이의 당부를 따르기로 마음먹고 시골식으로 매우 정중하게 허리를 숙여 인사하고는, 자기가 할 수 있는 가장 그럴듯한 인사말 몇 마디를 건넸다. 그러고는 곧장 자기 하소연으로 넘어가 이렇게 말했다. \"자, 사촌 부인, 여기 세상에서 가장 불효막심한 자식이 서 있습니다. 거지 같은 불한당 놈을 못 잊어 안달하면서, 우리가 마련해 준 온 잉글랜드에서 손꼽히는 혼처 하나는 마다하지 뭡니까.\""]], [["“Indeed, cousin Western,”", "\"정말이지, 웨스턴 사촌,\""], ["answered the lady, “I am persuaded you wrong my cousin.", "부인이 답했다. \"당신이 제 조카를 잘못 보신 게 분명해요."], ["I am sure she hath a better understanding.", "분명 이 아이는 더 분별이 있답니다."], ["I am convinced she will not refuse what she must be sensible is so much to her advantage.”", "자기에게 그토록 큰 이득이 되는 일임을 알면서 거절할 리 없다고 저는 확신해요.\""]], [["This was a wilful mistake in Lady Bellaston, for she well knew whom Mr Western meant; though perhaps she thought he would easily be reconciled to his lordship's proposals.", "이것은 벨라스턴 부인의 고의적인 착각이었다. 그녀는 웨스턴 씨가 누구를 가리키는지 잘 알고 있었으니 말이다. 다만 아마도 그녀는, 그가 펠라마 경의 청혼에 쉽게 마음을 돌리리라 여겼을 것이다."]], [["“Do you hear there,”", "\"저기 좀 들어 봐라.\""], ["quoth the squire, “what her ladyship says?", "영주가 말했다. \"부인께서 하시는 말씀을 말이야."], ["All your family are for the match.", "네 집안 사람이 다 그 혼처를 찬성한다잖니."], ["Come, Sophy, be a good girl, and be dutiful, and make your father happy.”", "자, 소피, 착한 딸이 되어 본분을 지키고, 네 아비를 기쁘게 해 다오.\""]], [["“If my death will make you happy, sir,”", "\"제 죽음이 아버지를 기쁘게 한다면, 아버지,\""], ["answered Sophia, “you will shortly be so.”", "소피아가 답했다. \"곧 그렇게 되시겠지요.\""]], [["“It's a lye, Sophy; it's a d--n'd lye, and you know it,”", "\"그건 거짓말이야, 소피. 빌어먹을 거짓말이고, 너도 그걸 알잖아.\""], ["said the squire.", "영주가 말했다."]], [["“Indeed, Miss Western,” said Lady Bellaston, “you injure your father; he hath nothing in view but your interest in this match; and I and all your friends must acknowledge the highest honour done to your family in the proposal.”", "\"정말이에요, 웨스턴 양,\" 벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"당신은 아버지께 못할 짓을 하는 거예요. 이 혼사에서 아버지는 오직 당신의 이익만 바라보고 계세요. 그리고 저도, 당신의 모든 벗들도, 이 청혼이 당신 집안에 더없는 영광을 안겨 준 것임을 인정하지 않을 수 없답니다.\""]], [["“Ay, all of us,”", "\"그래, 우리 모두가 말이지.\""], ["quoth the squire; “nay, it was no proposal of mine.", "영주가 말했다. \"아니, 그건 내가 꺼낸 청혼도 아니었어."], ["She knows it was her aunt proposed it to me first.--Come, Sophy, once more let me beg you to be a good girl, and gee me your consent before your cousin.”", "처음에 네 고모가 나한테 제안한 거라는 걸 저 애도 알아. 자, 소피, 다시 한번 부탁한다, 착한 딸이 되어서, 네 사촌 앞에서 승낙해 다오.\""]], [["“Let me give him your hand, cousin,”", "\"제가 이 아이의 손을 그분께 건네드릴게요, 사촌.\""], ["said the lady.", "부인이 말했다."], ["“It is the fashion now-a-days to dispense with time and long courtships.”", "\"요즘은 시간을 들이고 길게 구애하는 걸 생략하는 게 유행이랍니다.\""]], [["“Pugh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["said the squire, “what signifies time; won't they have time enough to court afterwards?", "영주가 말했다. \"시간이 무슨 대수라고."], ["People may court very well after they have been a-bed together.”", "나중에 구애할 시간이야 얼마든지 있지 않겠어? 한 침대에 든 다음에도 사람들은 얼마든지 잘만 구애하는걸.\""]], [["As Lord Fellamar was very well assured that he was meant by Lady Bellaston, so, never having heard nor suspected a word of Blifil, he made no doubt of his being meant by the father.", "펠라마 경은 벨라스턴 부인이 자기를 가리킨다는 것을 확실히 알았으므로, 또 블리필에 관해서는 한마디도 듣거나 의심해 본 적이 없었으므로, 아버지가 가리키는 사람도 자기라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 그리하여 영주에게 다가가 말했다."], ["Coming up, therefore, to the squire, he said, “Though I have not the honour, sir, of being personally known to you, yet, as I find I have the happiness to have my proposals accepted, let me intercede, sir, in behalf of the young lady, that she may not be more solicited at this time.”", "\"비록 나리, 제가 직접 인사를 드릴 영광은 갖지 못했으나, 제 청혼이 받아들여지는 행운을 얻은 듯하니, 나리, 저 젊은 아가씨를 위해 한마디 청을 드리겠습니다. 지금 이 순간만큼은 아가씨를 더 다그치지 마시기를요.\""]], [["“You intercede, sir!”", "\"자네가 청을 드린다고, 선생?\""], ["said the squire; “why, who the devil are you?”", "영주가 말했다. \"아니, 자넨 대체 누구야?\""]], [["“Sir, I am Lord Fellamar,”", "\"나리, 저는 펠라마 경입니다.\""], ["answered he, “and am the happy man whom I hope you have done the honour of accepting for a son-in-law.”", "그가 답했다. \"그리고 나리께서 영광스럽게도 사위로 받아들여 주셨기를 바라는, 그 행복한 사내입니다.\""]], [["“You are a son of a b----,”", "\"넌 개자식이야.\" 영주가 받아쳤다."], ["replied the squire, “for all your laced coat.", "\"그 화려한 레이스 옷을 다 입었어도 말이지."], ["You my son-in-law, and be d--n'd to you!”", "네가 내 사위라고, 빌어먹을 놈 같으니!\""]], [["“I shall take more from you, sir, than from any man,”", "\"나리, 다른 어떤 사람한테서보다 나리한테서는 더 많이 참겠습니다.\" 경이 답했다."], ["answered the lord; “but I must inform you that I am not used to hear such language without resentment.”", "\"하지만 분명히 말씀드려야겠습니다. 저는 그런 말을 듣고도 분개하지 않는 데에는 익숙하지 못합니다.\""]], [["“Resent my a--,” quoth the squire.", "\"분개는 무슨 빌어먹을 분개.\""], ["“Don't think I am afraid of such a fellow as thee art!", "영주가 말했다. \"내가 너 따위를 무서워할 것 같으냐!"], ["because hast got a spit there dangling at thy side.", "옆구리에 그 꼬챙이 하나 매달고 다닌다고 말이지."], ["Lay by your spit, and I'll give thee enough of meddling with what doth not belong to thee.", "그 꼬챙이일랑 치워 둬라, 그럼 네놈 일도 아닌 일에 끼어든 죗값을 내가 톡톡히 치르게 해 주마."], ["I'll teach you to father-in-law me.", "어디 내 장인 노릇을 하겠다고?"], ["I'll lick thy jacket.”", "네 저고리를 흠씬 두들겨 주마.\""]], [["“It's very well, sir,”", "\"좋습니다, 나리.\""], ["said my lord, “I shall make no disturbance before the ladies.", "경이 말했다. \"저는 숙녀분들 앞에서 소동을 일으키지 않겠습니다."], ["I am very well satisfied.", "저는 아주 만족합니다."], ["Your humble servant, sir; Lady Bellaston, your most obedient.”", "그럼 이만 물러가겠습니다, 나리. 벨라스턴 부인, 더없이 충실한 종이 인사 올립니다.\""]], [["His lordship was no sooner gone, than Lady Bellaston, coming up to Mr Western, said, “Bless me, sir, what have you done? You know not whom you have affronted; he is a nobleman of the first rank and fortune, and yesterday made proposals to your daughter; and such as I am sure you must accept with the highest pleasure.”", "경이 떠나기가 무섭게 벨라스턴 부인이 웨스턴 씨에게 다가와 말했다. \"맙소사, 사촌, 대체 무슨 짓을 하신 거예요? 당신이 누구한테 모욕을 준 건지 모르시는군요. 그분은 일등가는 지체와 재산을 가진 귀족인 데다, 어제 당신 따님께 청혼을 한 분이에요. 게다가 그 청혼이라는 게, 당신이 분명 더없는 기쁨으로 받아들여야 할 그런 것이라니까요.\""]], [["“Answer for yourself, lady cousin,”", "\"부인 일은 부인이 알아서 하시구려, 사촌 부인.\""], ["said the squire, “I will have nothing to do with any of your lords.", "영주가 말했다. \"나는 당신네 귀족 나리들하고는 아무 상관도 하고 싶지 않소."], ["My daughter shall have an honest country gentleman; I have pitched upon one for her--and she shall ha'", "내 딸은 정직한 시골 향신과 짝지어 줄 거요. 내가 점찍어 둔 사람이 하나 있으니, 그 애는 그놈을 받아들일 거요."], ["un.--I am sorry for the trouble she hath given your ladyship with all my heart.”", "부인께 끼친 폐는 진심으로 미안하게 생각하오.\""], ["Lady Bellaston made a civil speech upon the word trouble; to which the squire answered--“Why, that's kind--and I would do as much for your ladyship.", "벨라스턴 부인이 그 폐라는 말을 두고 정중한 인사말을 건네자, 영주가 답했다. \"허허, 친절하시구려."], ["To be sure relations should do for one another.", "나도 부인을 위해서라면 그만큼은 하지요. 암, 친척끼리는 서로 도와야지요."], ["So I wish your ladyship a good night.--Come, madam, you must go along with me by fair means, or I'll have you carried down to the coach.”", "그럼 부인께 편안한 밤 되시기를 비오. 자, 얘야, 너는 순순히 나하고 같이 가든지, 아니면 마차까지 들려 가든지 해라.\""]], [["Sophia said she would attend him without force; but begged to go in a chair, for she said she should not be able to ride any other way.", "소피아는 억지로 끌려가지 않고 따르겠노라 말했지만, 가마를 타고 가게 해 달라고 청했다. 다른 방법으로는 도저히 갈 수 없을 것 같다는 것이었다."]], [["“Prithee,”", "\"이런,\""], ["cries the squire, “wout unt persuade me canst not ride in a coach, wouldst?", "영주가 외쳤다. \"설마 네가 마차를 못 탄다고 나를 구슬릴 셈은 아니겠지, 응?"], ["That's a pretty thing surely!", "그것참 가관이로구나!"], ["No, no, I'll never let thee out of my sight any more till art married, that I promise thee.”", "안 돼, 안 돼, 네가 시집갈 때까지는 다시는 너를 내 눈앞에서 놓치지 않을 거다, 약속하마.\""], ["Sophia told him, she saw he was resolved to break her heart.", "소피아는 아버지가 자기 가슴을 찢어 놓기로 작정하신 게 보인다고 말했다."], ["“O break thy heart and be d--n'd,”", "\"오, 찢어지든 말든, 빌어먹을,\""], ["quoth he, “if a good husband will break it.", "그가 말했다. \"좋은 남편이 네 가슴을 찢어 놓는다면야."], ["I don't value a brass varden, not a halfpenny, of any undutiful b-- upon earth.”", "이 세상 어떤 불효막심한 계집이라도, 나는 놋쇠 동전 한 닢, 반 페니어치도 쳐주지 않는다.\""], ["He then took violent hold of her hand; upon which the parson once more interfered, begging him to use gentle methods.", "그러더니 그는 그녀의 손을 거칠게 와락 붙잡았다. 그 순간 목사가 다시 한번 끼어들어, 부드러운 방법을 쓰시라고 그에게 간청했다."], ["At that the squire thundered out a curse, and bid the parson hold his tongue, saying, “At'nt in pulpit now?", "그러자 영주는 욕설을 한바탕 우레처럼 퍼붓고는 목사에게 입을 다물라 명하며 말했다. \"지금 설교단에 서 있는 줄 아나?"], ["when art a got up there I never mind what dost say; but I won't be priest-ridden, nor taught how to behave myself by thee.", "자네가 거기 올라가 있을 땐 무슨 말을 하든 내가 신경도 안 쓰지. 하지만 나는 목사한테 끌려다니지도 않을 거고, 자네한테 처신하는 법을 배우지도 않을 거야."], ["I wish your ladyship a good-night.", "부인께 편안한 밤 되시길 비오. 자, 가자, 소피."], ["Come along, Sophy; be a good girl, and all shall be well. Shat ha'", "착한 딸이 되면 다 잘될 거다."], ["un, d--n me, shat ha' un!”", "그놈을 받아야 해, 빌어먹을, 받아야 한다고!\""]], [["Mrs Honour appeared below-stairs, and with a low curtesy to the squire offered to attend her mistress; but he pushed her away, saying, “Hold, madam, hold, you come no more near my house.”", "아너 부인이 아래층에 나타나 영주에게 공손히 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 자기 여주인을 모시겠다고 나섰다. 그러나 영주는 그녀를 밀쳐 내며 말했다."], ["“And will you take my maid away from me?”", "\"그만, 이 사람아, 그만둬. 다시는 내 집 근처에 얼씬도 하지 마.\""], ["said Sophia.", "\"제 하녀까지 빼앗아 가실 거예요?\""], ["“Yes, indeed, madam, will I,”", "소피아가 말했다. \"그럼, 정말이지, 그럴 거다.\""], ["cries the squire: “you need not fear being without a servant; I will get you another maid, and a better maid than this, who, I'd lay five pounds to a crown, is no more a maid than my grannum.", "영주가 외쳤다. \"하인 없이 지낼까 봐 걱정할 건 없다."], ["No, no, Sophy, she shall contrive no more escapes, I promise you.”", "다른 하녀를 구해 줄 테니까. 이 계집보다 더 나은 하녀로 말이야."], ["He then packed up his daughter and the parson into the hackney coach, after which he mounted himself, and ordered it to drive to his lodgings.", "이 계집은, 내 우리 할멈만큼도 처녀가 아니라는 데 5파운드 대 1크라운을 걸겠다. 안 돼, 안 돼, 소피, 이 계집이 더는 도망칠 궁리를 못 하게 하마, 약속하지.\""], ["In the way thither he suffered Sophia to be quiet, and entertained himself with reading a lecture to the parson on good manners, and a proper behaviour to his betters.", "그러고는 딸과 목사를 삯마차에 욱여넣고, 그 뒤에 자기도 올라타고는 자기 숙소로 몰라고 명했다. 그리로 가는 길에 그는 소피아를 가만 내버려 두었고, 목사에게 예절과 윗사람에 대한 올바른 처신에 관해 일장 훈계를 늘어놓으며 시간을 보냈다."]], [["It is possible he might not so easily have carried off his daughter from Lady Bellaston, had that good lady desired to have detained her; but, in reality, she was not a little pleased with the confinement into which Sophia was going; and as her project with Lord Fellamar had failed of success, she was well contented that other violent methods were now going to be used in favour of another man.", "만일 그 어진 부인이 소피아를 붙잡아 두고 싶어 했더라면, 영주가 벨라스턴 부인의 집에서 그토록 쉽게 딸을 데려가지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 실은, 부인은 소피아가 앞으로 들어가게 될 그 감금 신세가 적잖이 흡족했다. 게다가 펠라마 경과 함께 꾸민 자기 계략이 실패로 돌아간 마당이라, 이제 또 다른 사내를 위해 다른 거친 방법들이 동원되려는 것이 그녀로서는 못내 만족스러웠던 것이다."]], [["By what means the squire came to discover his daughter.", "시골 지주가 어떤 경로로 딸을 찾아내게 되었는가."]], [["Though the reader, in many histories, is obliged to digest much more unaccountable appearances than this of Mr Western, without any satisfaction at all; yet, as we dearly love to oblige him whenever it is in our power, we shall now proceed to shew by what method the squire discovered where his daughter was.", "여러 이야기책에서 독자는 웨스턴 씨의 이번 경우보다 훨씬 더 설명하기 어려운 일들조차 아무런 해명 없이 그대로 삼켜야 할 때가 많다. 그러나 우리는 할 수 있는 한 언제든 독자를 기쁘게 해 드리는 것을 진심으로 좋아하므로, 이제 그 지주가 어떤 방법으로 딸이 있는 곳을 알아냈는지 보여 드리려 한다."]], [["In the third chapter, then, of the preceding book, we gave a hint (for it is not our custom to unfold at any time more than is necessary for the occasion) that Mrs Fitzpatrick, who was very desirous of reconciling her uncle and aunt Western, thought she had a probable opportunity, by the service of preserving Sophia from committing the same crime which had drawn on herself the anger of her family.", "그러니 앞선 권의 제3장에서 우리는 한 가지 단서를 흘려 두었다(우리는 어느 때든 그 상황에 꼭 필요한 것 이상은 펼쳐 보이지 않는 것이 관례이기 때문이다). 자기 외삼촌과 외숙모 웨스턴을 화해시키고 싶어 안달이 난 피츠패트릭 부인이, 소피아가 자신을 가족의 분노 속으로 끌어들였던 바로 그 죄를 저지르지 못하게 막아 주는 봉사를 통해 그럴듯한 기회를 잡았다고 여겼다는 단서 말이다."], ["After much deliberation, therefore, she resolved to inform her aunt Western where her cousin was, and accordingly she writ the following letter, which we shall give the reader at length, for more reasons than one.", "그리하여 그녀는 오랜 고심 끝에 자기 외숙모 웨스턴에게 사촌이 어디 있는지 알리기로 결심했고, 그에 따라 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다. 우리는 한두 가지가 아닌 여러 이유로 이 편지를 독자에게 전문 그대로 보여 드리려 한다."]], [["“The occasion of my writing this will perhaps make a letter of mine agreeable to my dear aunt, for the sake of one of her nieces, though I have little reason to hope it will be so on the account of another.", "“존경하는 외숙모님께,\n“이 글을 쓰게 된 사연이, 비록 다른 한 조카 때문에는 그러기를 바랄 만한 이유가 거의 없다 하더라도, 또 다른 한 조카를 위해서라면 어쩌면 제 편지를 사랑하는 외숙모님께 반갑게 만들어 드릴지도 모르겠습니다."]], [["“Without more apology, as I was coming to throw my unhappy self at your feet, I met, by the strangest accident in the world, my cousin Sophy, whose history you are better acquainted with than myself, though, alas! I know infinitely too much; enough indeed to satisfy me, that unless she is immediately prevented, she is in danger of running into the same fatal mischief, which, by foolishly and ignorantly refusing your most wise and prudent advice, I have unfortunately brought on myself.", "“더 긴 사죄는 접어 두고 말씀드리겠습니다. 이 가엾은 몸을 외숙모님 발치에 던지러 가던 길에, 세상에서 가장 기이한 우연으로 저는 사촌 소피를 만났습니다. 그 아이의 사정은 저보다 외숙모님께서 더 잘 알고 계시지요. 그래도, 아, 저 역시 너무나 많은 것을 알고 있습니다. 정말이지 충분히 알고 있어서, 만약 지금 당장 막지 않으면 그 아이가 제가 어리석고 무지하게도 외숙모님의 더없이 현명하고 신중한 충고를 거절하여 불행히도 제 몸에 끌어들인 바로 그 치명적인 화 속으로 뛰어들 위험에 처해 있다고 확신하기에 족할 만큼 말입니다."]], [["“In short, I have seen the man, nay, I was most part of yesterday in his company, and a charming young fellow I promise you he is. By what accident he came acquainted with me is too tedious to tell you now; but I have this morning changed my lodgings to avoid him, lest he should by my means discover my cousin; for he doth not yet know where she is, and it is adviseable he should not, till my uncle hath secured her.----No time therefore is to be lost; and I need only inform you, that she is now with Lady Bellaston, whom I have seen, and who hath, I find, a design of concealing her from her family. You know, madam, she is a strange woman; but nothing could misbecome me more than to presume to give any hint to one of your great understanding and great knowledge of the world, besides barely informing you of the matter of fact.", "“간단히 말씀드리면, 저는 그 남자를 보았습니다. 아니, 어제 거의 온종일 그 사람과 함께 있었답니다. 장담컨대 매력적인 젊은이더군요. 어떤 우연으로 그가 저와 알게 되었는지는 지금 말씀드리기엔 너무 장황합니다. 다만 저는 오늘 아침 그를 피하려고 거처를 옮겼습니다. 혹시라도 저를 통해 그가 제 사촌이 있는 곳을 알아낼까 두려워서지요. 그는 아직 그 아이가 어디 있는지 모르고, 외삼촌께서 그 아이를 단단히 붙드시기 전까지는 모르는 편이 좋습니다.——그러니 한시도 지체해서는 안 됩니다. 저는 다만 그 아이가 지금 벨라스턴 부인과 함께 있다는 것만 알려 드리면 됩니다. 저는 그분을 만나 보았는데, 제가 보기에 그분은 그 아이를 가족에게서 숨겨 둘 속셈을 품고 있는 듯합니다. 외숙모님께서도 아시다시피, 그분은 별난 여자입니다. 그러나 사실관계를 그저 알려 드리는 것 외에, 외숙모님처럼 큰 식견과 세상 물정에 밝으신 분께 감히 무언가를 넌지시 일러 드리려 드는 것보다 제게 더 어울리지 않는 일은 없을 것입니다."]], [["“I hope, madam, the care which I have shewn on this occasion for the good of my family will recommend me again to the favour of a lady who hath always exerted so much zeal for the honour and true interest of us all; and that it may be a means of restoring me to your friendship, which hath made so great a part of my former, and is so necessary to my future happiness.", "“외숙모님, 이번 일에서 제가 우리 가문의 안녕을 위해 보여 드린 정성이, 늘 우리 모두의 명예와 진정한 이익을 위해 그토록 열심을 다해 오신 부인의 호의를 제게 다시 얻게 해 주기를 바랍니다. 그리고 그것이, 제 지난날 행복의 그토록 큰 부분을 이루었고 앞으로의 행복에도 그토록 꼭 필요한 외숙모님의 우정을 제게 되돌려 주는 길이 되기를 바랍니다. “더없는 존경을 담아,"]], [["“I am, with the utmost respect, honoured madam, your most dutiful obliged niece, and most obedient humble servant, HARRIET FITZPATRICK.”", "존경하는 외숙모님의\n더없이 효심 깊고 신세 진 조카이자"]], [["Mrs Western was now at her brother's house, where she had resided ever since the flight of Sophia, in order to administer comfort to the poor squire in his affliction. Of this comfort, which she doled out to him in daily portions, we have formerly given a specimen.", "더없이 순종하는 미천한\n종,"]], [["She was now standing with her back to the fire, and, with a pinch of snuff in her hand, was dealing forth this daily allowance of comfort to the squire, while he smoaked his afternoon pipe, when she received the above letter; which she had no sooner read than she delivered it to him, saying, “There, sir, there is an account of your lost sheep.", "해리엇 피츠패트릭 올림.” 웨스턴 부인은 이때 오라버니의 집에 머물고 있었는데, 소피아가 달아난 이후로 줄곧 그곳에 지내며 슬픔에 잠긴 가엾은 지주를 위로해 주고 있었다."], ["Fortune hath again restored her to you, and if you will be governed by my advice, it is possible you may yet preserve her.”", "날마다 조금씩 배급하듯 나눠 주던 그 위로의 견본을 우리는 앞서 보여 드린 바 있다."]], [["The squire had no sooner read the letter than he leaped from his chair, threw his pipe into the fire, and gave a loud huzza for joy.", "그녀는 지금 난롯불을 등지고 서서, 손에 코담배 한 자밤을 쥔 채, 지주가 오후 담배를 피우는 동안 이 일일 위로 배급분을 풀어 놓고 있었다."], ["He then summoned his servants, called for his boots, and ordered the Chevalier and several other horses to be saddled, and that parson Supple should be immediately sent for.", "바로 그때 위의 편지를 받았는데, 다 읽기가 무섭게 그것을 오라버니에게 건네며 말했다. “자, 오라버니, 여기 잃어버린 양에 관한 소식이 있어요."], ["Having done this, he turned to his sister, caught her in his arms, and gave her a close embrace, saying, “Zounds! you don't seem pleased; one would imagine you was sorry I have found the girl.”", "운명이 그 아이를 다시 오라버니께 돌려보내 주었으니, 제 충고에 따라 주시기만 한다면 어쩌면 아직 그 아이를 지켜 낼 수 있을지도 몰라요.”"]], [["“Brother,”", "지주는 편지를 읽기가 무섭게 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 담뱃대를 불 속에 던져 넣고는 기쁨에 겨워 큰 소리로 만세를 외쳤다."], ["answered she, “the deepest politicians, who see to the bottom, discover often a very different aspect of affairs, from what swims on the surface.", "그러고는 하인들을 불러 장화를 가져오게 하고, 슈발리에와 다른 말 몇 필에 안장을 얹으라 명하며, 서플 목사를 즉시 불러오라 일렀다."], ["It is true, indeed, things do look rather less desperate than they did formerly in Holland, when Lewis the Fourteenth was at the gates of Amsterdam; but there is a delicacy required in this matter, which you will pardon me, brother, if I suspect you want.", "이 일을 마치고 그는 누이에게 돌아서서 그녀를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안고는 바짝 껴안으며 말했다. “이런!"], ["There is a decorum to be used with a woman of figure, such as Lady Bellaston, brother, which requires a knowledge of the world, superior, I am afraid, to yours.”", "너는 기뻐 보이질 않는구나. 내가 그 애를 찾은 걸 못마땅해하는 사람처럼 보일 지경이야.”"]], [["“Sister,”", "“오라버니,”"], ["cries the squire, “I know you have no opinion of my parts; but I'll shew you on this occasion who is a fool.", "그녀가 대답했다. “바닥까지 꿰뚫어 보는 가장 깊은 책략가들은, 수면 위에 떠 있는 것과는 사뭇 다른 일의 양상을 곧잘 알아채는 법이에요."], ["Knowledge, quotha! I have not been in the country so long without having some knowledge of warrants and the law of the land.", "사실, 지금 사정이 예전 네덜란드에서 루이 14세가 암스테르담 성문 앞까지 닥쳐왔을 때보다는 그리 절망적으로 보이지 않는 건 맞아요."], ["I know I may take my own wherever I can find it.", "그렇지만 이 일에는 섬세함이 필요한데, 오라버니, 용서하세요, 제가 보기엔 오라버니께 바로 그게 모자란 듯해요."], ["Shew me my own daughter, and if I don't know how to come at her, I'll suffer you to call me a fool as long as I live.", "벨라스턴 부인 같은 지체 높은 여인을 대할 때는 마땅히 지켜야 할 예법이 있는데, 그건 세상 물정에 대한 지식을 요한답니다."], ["There be justices of peace in London, as well as in other places.”", "안타깝게도 오라버니의 것을 훨씬 웃도는 지식 말이에요.”"]], [["“I protest,” cries she, “you make me tremble for the event of this matter, which, if you will proceed by my advice, you may bring to so good an issue. Do you really imagine, brother, that the house of a woman of figure is to be attacked by warrants and brutal justices of the peace? I will inform you how to proceed. As soon as you arrive in town, and have got yourself into a decent dress (for indeed, brother, you have none at present fit to appear in), you must send your compliments to Lady Bellaston, and desire leave to wait on her. When you are admitted to her presence, as you certainly will be, and have told her your story, and have made proper use of my name (for I think you just know one another only by sight, though you are relations), I am confident she will withdraw her protection from my niece, who hath certainly imposed upon her. This is the only method.--Justices of peace, indeed! do you imagine any such event can arrive to a woman of figure in a civilised nation?”", "“누이야,” 지주가 외쳤다. “네가 내 머리를 우습게 본다는 걸 잘 안다. 그렇지만 이번 일에서는 누가 바보인지 내가 보여 주마. 지식이라고, 흥! 내가 이렇게 오래 시골에 살면서 영장이며 이 나라 법에 대해 아무것도 모를 줄 아느냐. 내 것은 어디서 찾든 내가 가질 수 있다는 걸 안다. 내 친딸을 내 앞에 보여만 주면, 그 애한테 다가갈 방법을 내가 모른다면, 죽을 때까지 네가 나를 바보라 불러도 좋다. 런던에도 다른 곳들과 마찬가지로 치안판사들이 있단 말이다.” “정말이지,” 그녀가 외쳤다. “오라버니 때문에 이 일의 결말이 떨려요. 제 충고대로만 하시면 아주 좋은 결말로 이끌 수 있는 일인데 말이에요. 오라버니, 정말로 지체 높은 여인의 집을 영장과 무지막지한 치안판사들로 쳐들어갈 수 있다고 생각하세요? 어찌해야 하는지 제가 일러 드리지요. 도시에 도착하시는 대로, 우선 단정한 옷차림부터 갖추셔야 해요(정말이지 오라버니, 지금은 사람 앞에 나설 만한 옷이 한 벌도 없으니까요). 그런 다음 벨라스턴 부인께 인사를 전하고 찾아뵙기를 청하셔야 합니다. 그분 앞에 들게 되면—틀림없이 그리 될 텐데—오라버니의 사정을 말씀드리고 제 이름을 적절히 활용하세요(두 분은 친척이긴 해도 그저 얼굴만 아는 사이일 테니까요). 그러면 제 확신컨대 그분은 분명 자신을 속였을 제 조카에게서 보호를 거두실 거예요. 이것이 유일한 방법입니다.——치안판사라니, 원! 문명국에서 지체 높은 여인에게 그런 일이 일어날 수 있다고 생각하세요?”"]], [["“D--n their figures,”", "“그놈의 지체고 뭐고 다 빌어먹으라지,”"], ["cries the squire; “a pretty civilised nation, truly, where women are above the law.", "지주가 외쳤다. “참 대단한 문명국이로구나, 여자들이 법 위에 있다니."], ["And what must I stand sending a parcel of compliments to a confounded whore, that keeps away a daughter from her own natural father?", "그리고 친아비한테서 딸을 떼어 놓는 그 빌어먹을 화냥년한테 내가 인사치레 한 보따리를 보내며 굽실거려야 한다고?"], ["I tell you, sister, I am not so ignorant as you think me----I know you would have women above the law, but it is all a lye; I heard his lordship say at size, that no one is above the law.", "누이야, 내가 네 생각만큼 그렇게 무지하진 않아.——나는 네가 여자들을 법 위에 두고 싶어 하는 걸 알지만, 그건 죄다 거짓말이야. 순회재판소에서 그분께서 말씀하시는 걸 내가 들었는데, 그 누구도 법 위에 있지 않다고 하셨다."], ["But this of yours is Hanover law, I suppose.”", "하지만 네 말은, 내 짐작에, 하노버 법이렷다.”"]], [["“Mr Western,”", "“웨스턴 씨,”"], ["said she, “I think you daily improve in ignorance.----I protest you are grown an arrant bear.”", "그녀가 말했다. “오라버니는 날마다 무지가 늘어 가는 것 같군요.——정말이지 오라버니는 영락없는 곰이 다 되셨어요.”"]], [["“No more a bear than yourself, sister Western,”", "“너야말로 곰이지 나는 아니다, 누이 웨스턴,”"], ["said the squire.--“Pox!", "지주가 말했다.——“제기랄!"], ["you may talk of your civility an you will, I am sure you never shew any to me.", "네 예의범절 타령은 마음껏 해라만, 내겐 한 번도 그런 걸 보여 준 적이 없잖느냐."], ["I am no bear, no, nor no dog neither, though I know somebody, that is something that begins with a b; but pox!", "나는 곰이 아니야, 아니고말고, 개도 아니다. 그래도 ‘ㅁ’ 자로 시작하는 뭔가에 해당하는 누군가는 내가 알지만 말이다."], ["I will show you I have got more good manners than some folks.”", "흥! 어쨌든 나도 어떤 사람들보다는 예의범절을 더 갖췄다는 걸 너한테 보여 주마.”"]], [["“Mr Western,”", "* ‘ㅁ’ 자: 원문에서는 'b'로 시작하는 단어, 곧 'bitch(암캐)'를 빗댄 말장난이다. “웨스턴 씨,”"], ["answered the lady, “you may say what you please, je vous mesprise de tout mon coeur.", "부인이 대답했다. “하고 싶은 말씀 마음껏 하세요."], ["I shall not therefore be angry.----Besides, as my cousin, with that odious Irish name, justly says, I have that regard for the honour and true interest of my family, and that concern for my niece, who is a part of it, that I have resolved to go to town myself upon this occasion; for indeed, indeed, brother, you are not a fit minister to be employed at a polite court.--Greenland--Greenland should always be the scene of the tramontane negociation.”", "저는 진심으로 당신을 경멸하니까요.* 그러니 저는 화내지 않겠어요.——게다가, 그 끔찍한 아일랜드식 성을 가진 제 조카가 옳게 말했듯이, 저는 우리 가문의 명예와 진정한 이익을 그토록 소중히 여기고, 그 가문의 일부인 제 조카를 그토록 염려하므로, 이번 일에 직접 도시로 올라가기로 결심했어요. 정말이지, 정말이지 오라버니, 오라버니는 세련된 궁정에 보낼 만한 적임의 사신이 못 됩니다.——그린란드, 그린란드야말로 그 산 너머 야만의 협상이 벌어질 무대로나 어울리지요.”"]], [["“I thank Heaven,”", "* je vous mesprise de tout mon coeur: “저는 진심으로 당신을 경멸합니다”라는 뜻의 프랑스어. “하느님께 감사하지,”"], ["cries the squire, “I don't understand you now.", "지주가 외쳤다. “이번엔 네 말을 도통 알아듣지 못하겠으니 말이다."], ["You are got to your Hanoverian linguo.", "또 그 하노버 말투가 나왔구나. 그래도 나도 예의에서 네게 뒤처지는 건 질색이라는 걸 보여 주마."], ["However, I'll shew you I scorn to be behind-hand in civility with you; and as you are not angry for what I have said, so I am not angry for what you have said.", "네가 내 말에 화내지 않았으니, 나도 네 말에 화내지 않으마. 정말이지 나는 늘 친척끼리 다투는 건 어리석은 짓이라 여겨 왔다."], ["Indeed I have always thought it a folly for relations to quarrel; and if they do now and then give a hasty word, why, people should give and take; for my part, I never bear malice; and I take it very kind of you to go up to London; for I never was there but twice in my life, and then I did not stay above a fortnight at a time, and to be sure I can't be expected to know much of the streets and the folks in that time.", "어쩌다 한 번씩 성난 말이 오가더라도, 사람이란 주고받을 줄 알아야 하는 법이지. 나로 말하자면 결코 앙심을 품는 법이 없다."], ["I never denied that you know'd all these matters better than I.", "그리고 네가 런던까지 올라가 준다니 참 고맙구나. 나는 평생 그곳에 두 번밖에 가 보질 못했고, 그때도 한 번에 보름 넘게 머문 적이 없으니, 그 짧은 동안에 그곳 거리며 사람들을 많이 알았을 리가 없지."], ["For me to dispute that would be all as one as for you to dispute the management of a pack of dogs, or the finding a hare sitting, with me.”--“Which I promise you,”", "나는 네가 이런 일들을 나보다 더 잘 안다는 걸 한 번도 부인한 적이 없다. 내가 그걸 두고 따지는 건, 네가 나한테 개 떼 다루는 법이나 웅크린 토끼 찾아내는 법을 두고 따지는 거나 매한가지일 테니까.”——“그건,”"], ["says she, “I never will.”--“Well, and I promise you,”", "그녀가 말했다. “장담컨대 제가 결코 하지 않을 일이지요.”——“그래, 그럼 나도 장담하마,”"], ["returned he, “that I never will dispute the t'other.”", "그가 응수했다. “나도 다른 쪽 일은 결코 따지지 않으마.”"]], [["Here then a league was struck (to borrow a phrase from the lady) between the contending parties; and now the parson arriving, and the horses being ready, the squire departed, having promised his sister to follow her advice, and she prepared to follow him the next day.", "이로써 다투던 양편 사이에 (부인의 말투를 빌리자면) 동맹이 맺어졌다. 그러는 사이 목사가 도착하고 말들이 준비되자, 지주는 누이의 충고를 따르겠노라 약속하고 길을 떠났으며, 누이는 이튿날 그를 뒤따를 채비를 했다."]], [["But having communicated these matters to the parson on the road, they both agreed that the prescribed formalities might very well be dispensed with; and the squire, having changed his mind, proceeded in the manner we have already seen.", "그러나 길에서 이 일들을 목사에게 털어놓고 보니, 두 사람은 정해 놓은 격식 따위는 얼마든지 생략해도 좋겠다는 데 의견이 일치했다. 그리하여 지주는 마음을 바꾸어, 우리가 이미 본 그대로의 방식으로 일을 밀고 나갔다."]], [["In which various misfortunes befel poor Jones.", "가엾은 존스에게 갖가지 불행이 닥치는 이야기."]], [["Affairs were in the aforesaid situation when Mrs Honour arrived at Mrs Miller's, and called Jones out from the company, as we have before seen, with whom, when she found herself alone, she began as follows:--", "앞서 묘사한 그런 상황에 일이 놓여 있을 때, 아너 부인이 밀러 부인의 집에 도착해, 우리가 앞서 본 대로 존스를 일행에게서 불러냈다. 그와 단둘이 있게 되자 부인은 이렇게 입을 열었다."]], [["“O, my dear sir!", "\"오, 도련님!"], ["how shall I get spirits to tell you; you are undone, sir, and my poor lady's undone, and I am undone.”", "무슨 기운으로 이 말을 전해야 할지 모르겠어요. 도련님은 끝장나셨고, 가엾은 우리 아가씨도 끝장나셨고, 저도 끝장났어요.\""], ["“Hath anything happened to Sophia?”", "\"소피아에게 무슨 일이라도 생긴 거요?\" 존스가 미친 사람처럼 눈을 부릅뜨며 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, staring like a madman.", "\"나쁜 일이란 나쁜 일은 다요.\" 아너가 외쳤다."], ["“All that is bad,”", "\"오, 다시는 그런 아가씨를 못 모실 거예요! 오, 내가 살아서 이런 날을 보게 되다니!\""], ["cries Honour: “Oh, I shall never get such another lady!", "이 말에 존스는 잿빛으로 새파랗게 질려 떨며 더듬거렸지만, 아너는 계속했다. \"오!"], ["Oh that I should ever live to see this day!”", "존스 도련님, 저는 아가씨를 영영 잃었어요.\" \"어떻게요?"], ["At these words Jones turned pale as ashes, trembled, and stammered; but Honour went on--“O!", "뭐라고요! 제발, 말해줘요."], ["Mr Jones, I have lost my lady for ever.”", "오, 내 사랑 소피아!\" \"그렇게 부르실 만하죠.\""], ["“How? what!", "아너가 말했다."], ["for Heaven's sake, tell me.", "\"제게도 더없이 소중한 아가씨였으니까요. 다시는 그런 자리를 못 얻을 거예요.\""], ["O, my dear Sophia!”", "\"당신 자리 따위 빌어먹을!\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["“You may well call her so,”", "\"어디 있어요, 무슨, 내 소피아가 어떻게 됐냐고요?\" \"그럼요, 그러시겠죠.\""], ["said Honour; “she was the dearest lady to me.", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"하인 따위야 빌어먹어도 되겠죠."], ["I shall never have such another place.”----“D--n your place!”", "내쫓겨서 아주 신세를 망쳐도, 그들이 어떻게 되든 아무 상관없겠죠. 하긴 그들은 다른 사람들처럼 피와 살을 가진 존재가 아니니까요."], ["cries Jones; “where is--what--what is become of my Sophia?”", "아무렴, 그들이 어떻게 되든 아무 상관없겠죠.\" \"조금이라도 가엾은 마음, 동정심이 있거든,\""], ["“Ay, to be sure,”", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries she, “servants may be d--n'd.", "\"제발 부탁이니 소피아에게 무슨 일이 생겼는지 당장 말해줘요.\" \"그럼요, 도련님이 저를 가엾어하시는 것보다야 제가 도련님을 더 가엾어하죠.\""], ["It signifies nothing what becomes of them, though they are turned away, and ruined ever so much.", "아너가 대답했다. \"저는 도련님이 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨를 잃었다고 해서 빌어먹으라고는 안 하잖아요."], ["To be sure they are not flesh and blood like other people.", "정말이지 도련님은 동정받을 만하고, 저도 동정받을 만하죠. 왜냐하면, 정말이지, 세상에 좋은 안주인이 있었다면 바로—\""], ["No, to be sure, it signifies nothing what becomes of them.”", "\"무슨 일이 생겼냐고요?\" 존스가 거의 발광하듯 외쳤다."], ["“If you have any pity, any compassion,”", "\"뭐냐고요? 뭐냐고요?\""], ["cries Jones, “I beg you will instantly tell me what hath happened to Sophia?”", "아너가 말했다. \"그게요, 도련님께도 제게도 일어날 수 있는 가장 나쁜 일이죠."], ["“To be sure, I have more pity for you than you have for me,”", "아버님이 도시로 올라오셔서, 우리 둘 모두에게서 아가씨를 데려가 버리셨어요.\" 여기서 존스는 그보다 더 나쁜 일이 아니라는 데 감사하며 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["answered Honour; “I don't d--n you because you have lost the sweetest lady in the world.", "\"더 나쁜 일이 아니라뇨!\" 아너가 되뇌었다."], ["To be sure you are worthy to be pitied, and I am worthy to be pitied too: for, to be sure, if ever there was a good mistress----”", "\"우리 둘 중 누구에게 이보다 더 나쁜 일이 있을 수 있겠어요? 그분은 아가씨를 블리필 도련님과 결혼시키겠다고 맹세하면서 데려가셨어요."], ["“What hath happened?”", "그게 도련님께 위로가 되겠네요."], ["cries Jones, in almost a raving fit.", "그리고 가엾은 저는 문밖으로 쫓겨났고요.\" \"정말이지, 아너 부인,\""], ["“What?--What?”", "존스가 대답했다."], ["said Honour: “Why, the worst that could have happened both for you and for me.--Her father is come to town, and hath carried her away from us both.”", "\"당신은 나를 정신이 나갈 만큼 겁먹게 했소. 나는 소피아에게 무슨 끔찍한 사고라도 갑자기 닥친 줄 알았소."], ["Here Jones fell on his knees in thanksgiving that it was no worse. “No worse!”", "그에 비하면 그녀가 블리필과 결혼하는 걸 보는 것조차 사소한 일일 그런 사고 말이오."], ["repeated Honour; “what could be worse for either of us?", "하지만 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 희망은 있는 법이오, 아너."], ["He carried her off, swearing she should marry Mr Blifil; that's for your comfort; and, for poor me, I am turned out of doors.”", "이 자유의 나라에서는 여자가 실제로 짐승 같은 완력으로 결혼당할 수는 없으니까.\" \"그럼요, 도련님,\""], ["“Indeed, Mrs Honour,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그건 맞아요."], ["answered Jones, “you frightened me out of my wits.", "도련님께는 어떤 희망이 있을지도 모르죠. 하지만 어이쿠 맙소사!"], ["I imagined some most dreadful sudden accident had happened to Sophia; something, compared to which, even seeing her married to Blifil would be a trifle; but while there is life there are hopes, my dear Honour.", "가엾은 저에게는 무슨 희망이 있겠어요? 그리고 정말이지, 도련님, 제가 이 모든 걸 도련님 때문에 겪고 있다는 걸 아셔야 해요."], ["Women in this land of liberty, cannot be married by actual brutal force.”", "나리께서 제게 품으신 노여움이라는 게 다, 제가 그랬듯이 블리필 도련님에 맞서 도련님 편을 들었기 때문이거든요.\" \"정말이지, 아너 부인,\""], ["“To be sure, sir,”", "그가 대답했다."], ["said she, “that's true. There may be some hopes for you; but alack-a-day!", "\"나는 당신에게 진 빚을 잘 알고 있으며, 내 힘이 닿는 한 무엇이든 다 해서 갚으리다.\""], ["what hopes are there for poor me?", "\"어이쿠, 도련님,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["And to be sure, sir, you must be sensible I suffer all this upon your account.", "\"하인에게 한 자리를 잃은 것을 갚아줄 수 있는 게 똑같이 좋은 다른 자리를 얻는 것 말고 뭐가 있겠어요?\" \"절망하지 마시오, 아너 부인,\""], ["All the quarrel the squire hath to me is for taking your part, as I have done, against Mr Blifil.”", "존스가 말했다. \"당신을 다시 그 자리에 복직시켜 줄 수 있기를 바라오.\""], ["“Indeed, Mrs Honour,”", "\"어이쿠 맙소사, 도련님,\""], ["answered he, “I am sensible of my obligations to you, and will leave nothing in my power undone to make you amends.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그게 불가능한 일인 줄 뻔히 아는데 어떻게 그런 희망으로 제 마음을 부풀리겠어요?"], ["“Alas! sir,”", "나리께서 저를 그토록 미워하시는데 말예요."], ["said she, “what can make a servant amends for the loss of one place but the getting another altogether as good?”", "하지만, 만일 도련님이 언젠가 우리 아가씨를 차지하신다면—정말이지 이제 저는 진심으로 그러시길 바라요. 도련님은 너그럽고 마음씨 고운 신사분이시고, 분명 아가씨를 사랑하시잖아요."], ["“Do not despair, Mrs Honour,”", "그리고 아가씨도 제 영혼처럼 도련님을 끔찍이 사랑하시는 게 분명해요. 부인해봤자 헛일이죠."], ["said Jones, “I hope to reinstate you again in the same.”", "왜냐하면 우리 아가씨를 조금이라도 아는 사람이라면 누구나 알 수밖에 없으니까요. 가엾고 고운 아가씨는 속을 감추질 못하시거든요."], ["“Alack-a-day, sir,”", "서로 사랑하는 두 사람이 행복하지 못하다면, 대체 누가 행복해야 하겠어요?"], ["said she, “how can I flatter myself with such hopes when I know it is a thing impossible?", "행복이란 게 늘 가진 것에 달린 건 아니죠. 게다가 우리 아가씨는 두 분이 쓰고도 남을 만큼 가지셨고요."], ["for the squire is so set against me: and yet, if you should ever have my lady, as to be sure I now hopes heartily you will; for you are a generous, good-natured gentleman; and I am sure you loves her, and to be sure she loves you as dearly as her own soul; it is a matter in vain to deny it; because as why, everybody, that is in the least acquainted with my lady, must see it; for, poor dear lady, she can't dissemble: and if two people who loves one another a'n't happy, why who should be so?", "그러니 정말이지, 말하자면, 그렇게 사랑하는 두 분을 떼어놓는다는 건 세상에 그보다 안타까운 일이 없을 거예요. 아니, 제 생각으로는, 결국 두 분이 만나게 되실 거라고 확신해요."], ["Happiness don't always depend upon what people has; besides, my lady has enough for both.", "될 일이라면 막을 수가 없으니까요. 하늘에서 맺어진 혼인이라면, 땅 위의 어떤 치안판사도 그걸 깨뜨릴 수 없죠."], ["To be sure, therefore, as one may say, it would be all the pity in the world to keep two such loviers asunder; nay, I am convinced, for my part, you will meet together at last; for, if it is to be, there is no preventing it.", "정말이지 저는 서플 목사님이 좀 더 기개가 있어서, 딸을 그 뜻과 어긋나게 억지로 시집보내려는 게 얼마나 못된 짓인지 나리께 말씀드렸으면 싶어요. 하지만 그분은 모든 게 나리께 매여 있으니까요."], ["If a marriage is made in heaven, all the justices of peace upon earth can't break it off.", "그래서 그 가엾은 분은, 신앙심 깊고 좋은 분이라서 나리 등 뒤에서는 그런 짓이 나쁘다고 말씀하시면서도, 면전에서는 제 영혼이 제 것이라고 감히 말하지 못하시죠. 정말이지 저는 그분이 방금 전처럼 대담하게 구는 걸 한 번도 본 적이 없어요."], ["To be sure I wishes that parson Supple had but a little more spirit, to tell the squire of his wickedness in endeavouring to force his daughter contrary to her liking; but then his whole dependance is on the squire; and so the poor gentleman, though he is a very religious good sort of man, and talks of the badness of such doings behind the squire's back, yet he dares not say his soul is his own to his face.", "저는 나리께서 그분을 때리시는 줄 알았다니까요. 도련님, 우울해하시거나 절망하지 마세요."], ["To be sure I never saw him make so bold as just now; I was afeard the squire would have struck him.", "아가씨를 확신하실 수 있는 한 일은 더 잘 풀릴 거예요. 그리고 그건 제가 분명히 장담하는데, 아가씨는 결코 다른 어떤 남자와 결혼하는 데 동의하지 않으실 테니까요."], ["I would not have your honour be melancholy, sir, nor despair; things may go better, as long as you are sure of my lady, and that I am certain you may be; for she never will be brought to consent to marry any other man.", "정말이지 저는 나리께서 화김에 아가씨를 해코지하실까 봐 무섭기 짝이 없어요. 나리는 어마어마하게 격정적인 분이시거든요."], ["Indeed I am terribly afeared the squire will do her a mischief in his passion, for he is a prodigious passionate gentleman; and I am afeared too the poor lady will be brought to break her heart, for she is as tender-hearted as a chicken.", "그리고 가엾은 아가씨가 상심으로 가슴이 찢어지실까 봐도 무서워요. 아가씨는 병아리처럼 여린 마음을 지니셨으니까요."], ["It is pity, methinks, she had not a little of my courage.", "제 생각엔, 아가씨가 제 용기를 조금만 가지셨더라면 좋았을 텐데 안타까워요. 만일 제가 어떤 젊은 남자를 사랑하는데 아버지가 저를 가두려 든다면, 저는 그분 눈알을 후벼파서라도 그 남자에게 갈 거예요."], ["If I was in love with a young man, and my father offered to lock me up, I'd tear his eyes out but I'd come at him; but then there's a great fortune in the case, which it is in her father's power either to give her or not; that, to be sure, may make some difference.”", "하지만 이 경우엔 큰 재산이 걸려 있고, 그걸 주고 안 주고는 아버님 손에 달려 있죠. 그게 정말이지 좀 사정을 다르게 만들 수도 있겠네요.\""]], [["Whether Jones gave strict attention to all the foregoing harangue, or whether it was for want of any vacancy in the discourse, I cannot determine; but he never once attempted to answer, nor did she once stop till Partridge came running into the room, and informed him that the great lady was upon the stairs.", "존스가 앞서의 이 긴 사설에 줄곧 귀를 기울였는지, 아니면 그저 말 사이에 끼어들 틈이 없었던 탓인지는 내가 가늠할 수 없다. 다만 그는 단 한 번도 대꾸하려 들지 않았고, 그녀 역시 패트리지가 방으로 뛰어들어와 그 대단한 귀부인이 계단에 와 있다고 알릴 때까지 단 한 번도 말을 멈추지 않았다."]], [["Nothing could equal the dilemma to which Jones was now reduced.", "이제 존스가 빠진 진퇴양난에 견줄 만한 것은 없었다."], ["Honour knew nothing of any acquaintance that subsisted between him and Lady Bellaston, and she was almost the last person in the world to whom he would have communicated it.", "아너는 그와 벨라스턴 귀부인 사이에 어떤 친분이 있는지 전혀 몰랐고, 그가 그 사실을 알리고 싶은 사람 가운데 그녀는 세상에서 거의 가장 마지막에 꼽힐 사람이었다. 이 다급함과 곤경 속에서 그는 (흔히 그렇듯이) 가장 나쁜 길을 택했다."], ["In this hurry and distress, he took (as is common enough) the worst course, and, instead of exposing her to the lady, which would have been of little consequence, he chose to expose the lady to her; he therefore resolved to hide Honour, whom he had but just time to convey behind the bed, and to draw the curtains.", "그녀를 귀부인에게 드러내는 편이—그래봤자 별일도 아니었을 텐데—나았으련만, 그는 귀부인을 그녀에게 드러내는 쪽을 골랐다. 그리하여 그는 아너를 숨기기로 마음먹고, 겨우 침대 뒤로 그녀를 밀어 넣어 휘장을 칠 시간밖에 없었다."]], [["The hurry in which Jones had been all day engaged on account of his poor landlady and her family, the terrors occasioned by Mrs Honour, and the confusion into which he was thrown by the sudden arrival of Lady Bellaston, had altogether driven former thoughts out of his head; so that it never once occurred to his memory to act the part of a sick man; which, indeed, neither the gaiety of his dress, nor the freshness of his countenance, would have at all supported.", "가엾은 집주인 여자와 그 가족 때문에 존스가 하루 종일 분주했던 일, 아너 부인이 불러일으킨 공포, 그리고 벨라스턴 귀부인의 갑작스러운 도착으로 그가 빠진 혼란이, 그 모든 것이 합쳐져 이전의 생각들을 그의 머릿속에서 몰아내 버렸다. 그래서 병자 노릇을 해야 한다는 것이 그의 기억에 단 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다. 사실 그의 화사한 옷차림도, 생기 도는 얼굴빛도 그런 연기를 조금도 받쳐주지 못했을 테지만."]], [["He received her ladyship therefore rather agreeably to her desires than to her expectations, with all the good humour he could muster in his countenance, and without any real or affected appearance of the least disorder.", "그리하여 그는 그녀가 기대했던 것보다는 오히려 바랐던 모습에 더 가깝게 부인을 맞이했다. 얼굴에 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 좋은 기분을 띠고서, 조금도 흐트러진 기색을—실제로든 꾸며서든—드러내지 않은 채로 말이다."]], [["Lady Bellaston no sooner entered the room, than she squatted herself down on the bed: “So, my dear Jones,” said she, “you find nothing can detain me long from you. Perhaps I ought to be angry with you, that I have neither seen nor heard from you all day; for I perceive your distemper would have suffered you to come abroad: nay, I suppose you have not sat in your chamber all day drest up like a fine lady to see company after a lying-in; but, however, don't think I intend to scold you; for I never will give you an excuse for the cold behaviour of a husband, by putting on the ill-humour of a wife.”", "벨라스턴 귀부인은 방에 들어서기가 무섭게 침대에 털썩 주저앉았다. \"자, 내 사랑 존스,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이렇게 무엇도 나를 당신에게서 오래 떼어놓지 못한다는 걸 알겠죠. 어쩌면 당신에게 화를 내야 할지도 몰라요. 하루 종일 당신을 보지도, 소식을 듣지도 못했으니까요. 보아하니 당신의 병환쯤은 외출을 허락했을 텐데 말이에요. 아니, 출산 뒤 손님맞이하는 귀부인처럼 곱게 차려입고 하루 종일 방 안에 앉아 있지는 않았겠죠. 하지만, 그래도 내가 당신을 나무라려는 거라고는 생각 마세요. 나는 결코 아내의 심술을 부려서 남편의 차가운 행실에 핑계를 만들어주지는 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Nay, Lady Bellaston,”", "\"아니요, 벨라스턴 부인,\""], ["said Jones, “I am sure your ladyship will not upbraid me with neglect of duty, when I only waited for orders.", "존스가 말했다. \"부인께서 제가 그저 명령을 기다리고 있었을 뿐인데 직무를 게을리했다고 책망하지는 않으시리라 믿습니다."], ["Who, my dear creature, hath reason to complain?", "내 사랑하는 그대여, 불평할 이유가 있는 쪽이 누구입니까?"], ["Who missed an appointment last night, and left an unhappy man to expect, and wish, and sigh, and languish?”", "어젯밤 약속을 어겨, 가엾은 사내를 기다리게 하고, 바라게 하고, 한숨짓게 하고, 애태우게 한 사람이 누구입니까?\""]], [["“Do not mention it, my dear Mr Jones,”", "\"그 얘기는 꺼내지 마세요, 내 사랑 존스,\""], ["cried she.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“If you knew the occasion, you would pity me.", "\"그 사정을 안다면 당신은 나를 가엾어할 거예요."], ["In short, it is impossible to conceive what women of condition are obliged to suffer from the impertinence of fools, in order to keep up the farce of the world.", "한마디로, 신분 있는 여자들이 세상이라는 익살극을 이어가느라 어리석은 자들의 무례를 얼마나 견뎌야 하는지는 상상도 못 할 거예요."], ["I am glad, however, all your languishing and wishing have done you no harm; for you never looked better in your life.", "하지만 당신이 그렇게 애태우고 바라느라 아무 탈도 나지 않아서 기뻐요. 당신은 평생 이보다 더 좋아 보인 적이 없으니까요."], ["Upon my faith!", "정말이지!"], ["Jones, you might at this instant sit for the picture of Adonis.”", "존스, 당신은 지금 이 순간 아도니스의 초상화 모델로 앉아도 되겠어요.\""]], [["There are certain words of provocation which men of honour hold can properly be answered only by a blow.", "명예를 아는 사내들이 보기에, 어떤 도발의 말들은 오직 한 대 후려치는 것으로만 제대로 답할 수 있다고들 한다."], ["Among lovers possibly there may be some expressions which can be answered only by a kiss.", "연인들 사이에서는 아마도 오직 입맞춤으로만 답할 수 있는 표현들이 있을 것이다."], ["Now the compliment which Lady Bellaston now made Jones seems to be of this kind, especially as it was attended with a look, in which the lady conveyed more soft ideas than it was possible to express with her tongue.", "그런데 지금 벨라스턴 귀부인이 존스에게 건넨 찬사가 바로 이런 부류에 속하는 듯했다. 더구나 그것은, 부인이 혀로 표현할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 부드러운 마음을 담은 눈길과 함께였으니 말이다."]], [["Jones was certainly at this instant in one of the most disagreeable and distressed situations imaginable; for, to carry on the comparison we made use of before, though the provocation was given by the lady, Jones could not receive satisfaction, nor so much as offer to ask it, in the presence of a third person; seconds in this kind of duels not being according to the law of arms.", "존스는 분명 이 순간 상상할 수 있는 가장 거북하고 곤혹스러운 처지 가운데 하나에 놓여 있었다. 앞서 우리가 썼던 비유를 이어가자면, 도발은 부인이 했으되, 존스는 제삼자가 있는 자리에서 그 만족을 받을 수도, 그것을 청하려 들 수조차 없었다."], ["As this objection did not occur to Lady Bellaston, who was ignorant of any other woman being there but herself, she waited some time in great astonishment for an answer from Jones, who, conscious of the ridiculous figure he made, stood at a distance, and, not daring to give the proper answer, gave none at all.", "이런 종류의 결투에서는 입회인을 두는 것이 무기의 법도에 어긋나기 때문이다. 자기 말고 다른 여자가 거기 있다는 걸 모르는 벨라스턴 귀부인에게는 이런 사정이 떠오르지 않았으므로, 그녀는 큰 놀라움 속에 한동안 존스의 대답을 기다렸다."], ["Nothing can be imagined more comic, nor yet more tragical, than this scene would have been if it had lasted much longer.", "한편 존스는 자기가 우스꽝스러운 꼴이 되었음을 알고서 멀찍이 서 있었고, 마땅한 답을 감히 내놓지 못해 아예 아무 답도 하지 못했다. 만일 이 장면이 훨씬 더 오래 이어졌더라면, 이보다 더 희극적이면서도 동시에 더 비극적인 광경은 상상하기 어려웠을 것이다."], ["The lady had already changed colour two or three times; had got up from the bed and sat down again, while Jones was wishing the ground to sink under him, or the house to fall on his head, when an odd accident freed him from an embarrassment out of which neither the eloquence of a Cicero, nor the politics of a Machiavel, could have delivered him, without utter disgrace.", "부인은 이미 두세 번 낯빛이 변했고, 침대에서 일어났다가 다시 앉기를 거듭했으며, 존스는 땅이 자기 발밑으로 꺼지거나 집이 머리 위로 무너지기를 바라고 있었다. 그때 묘한 사고가 일어나, 키케로의 웅변으로도 마키아벨리의 책략으로도 완전한 망신 없이는 그를 끌어낼 수 없었을 그 곤경에서 그를 구해 주었다."]], [["This was no other than the arrival of young Nightingale, dead drunk; or rather in that state of drunkenness which deprives men of the use of their reason without depriving them of the use of their limbs.", "그것은 다름 아니라, 곤드레만드레 취한 젊은 나이팅게일의 등장이었다. 아니, 정확히 말하면, 이성은 못 쓰게 만들면서도 사지는 멀쩡히 쓰게 내버려 두는 그런 취기 상태로 말이다."]], [["Mrs Miller and her daughters were in bed, and Partridge was smoaking his pipe by the kitchen fire; so that he arrived at Mr Jones's chamber-door without any interruption.", "밀러 부인과 딸들은 침대에 들어 있었고, 패트리지는 부엌 난롯가에서 담뱃대를 피우고 있었다. 그래서 그는 아무 제지도 받지 않고 존스의 방문 앞에 다다랐다."], ["This he burst open, and was entering without any ceremony, when Jones started from his seat and ran to oppose him, which he did so effectually, that Nightingale never came far enough within the door to see who was sitting on the bed.", "그는 그 문을 박차고 열어젖히고는 거리낌 없이 들어오려 했는데, 그때 존스가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 그를 막으러 달려갔다. 어찌나 효과적으로 막았던지, 나이팅게일은 침대에 누가 앉아 있는지 볼 수 있을 만큼 문 안으로 들어오지조차 못했다."]], [["Nightingale had in reality mistaken Jones's apartment for that in which himself had lodged; he therefore strongly insisted on coming in, often swearing that he would not be kept from his own bed.", "사실 나이팅게일은 존스의 방을 자기가 묵던 방으로 착각한 것이었다. 그래서 그는 들어가겠다고 한사코 우기며, 제 침대에서 자기를 떼어놓지 못한다고 거듭 맹세했다."], ["Jones, however, prevailed over him, and delivered him into the hands of Partridge, whom the noise on the stairs soon summoned to his master's assistance.", "하지만 존스가 그를 이겨내, 계단의 소란에 곧 주인을 도우러 올라온 패트리지의 손에 그를 넘겼다."]], [["And now Jones was unwillingly obliged to return to his own apartment, where at the very instant of his entrance he heard Lady Bellaston venting an exclamation, though not a very loud one; and at the same time saw her flinging herself into a chair in a vast agitation, which in a lady of a tender constitution would have been an hysteric fit.", "이제 존스는 마지못해 자기 방으로 돌아가야 했는데, 들어서는 바로 그 순간 그는 벨라스턴 귀부인이 그리 크지는 않은 탄성을 토해내는 소리를 들었다. 동시에 그녀가 몹시 동요한 채 의자에 몸을 내던지는 것을 보았으니, 여린 체질의 부인이었다면 그것은 히스테리 발작이었을 것이다."]], [["In reality the lady, frightened with the struggle between the two men, of which she did not know what would be the issue, as she heard Nightingale swear many oaths he would come to his own bed, attempted to retire to her known place of hiding, which to her great confusion she found already occupied by another.", "사실 부인은 두 사내의 몸싸움에 겁을 먹고서—그 결말이 어찌 될지 알 수 없었던 데다, 나이팅게일이 제 침대로 오겠다고 여러 번 맹세하는 것을 들은 터라—제가 아는 숨을 자리로 물러나려 했던 것이다. 그런데 거기가 이미 다른 사람에게 차지되어 있는 것을 발견하고는 크게 당황하고 말았다."]], [["“Is this usage to be borne, Mr Jones?” cries the lady.--“Basest of men?----What wretch is this to whom you have exposed me?” “Wretch!” cries Honour, bursting in a violent rage from her place of concealment--“Marry come up!----Wretch forsooth?----as poor a wretch as I am, I am honest; this is more than some folks who are richer can say.”", "\"존스 씨, 이런 대접을 참으란 말인가요?\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"사내 중에서도 가장 비열한 자여! 당신이 나를 드러내 보인 이 천한 것은 대체 누구죠?\" \"천한 것이라고요!\" 아너가 숨은 자리에서 격한 분노로 튀어나오며 외쳤다. \"원 참 어처구니가 없네! 천한 것이라뇨? 제가 아무리 가난한 천것이라도, 저는 정직해요. 이건 저보다 부유한 어떤 사람들은 못 하는 말이죠.\""]], [["Jones, instead of applying himself directly to take off the edge of Mrs Honour's resentment, as a more experienced gallant would have done, fell to cursing his stars, and lamenting himself as the most unfortunate man in the world; and presently after, addressing himself to Lady Bellaston, he fell to some very absurd protestations of innocence. By this time the lady, having recovered the use of her reason, which she had as ready as any woman in the world, especially on such occasions, calmly replied: “Sir, you need make no apologies, I see now who the person is; I did not at first know Mrs Honour: but now I do, I can suspect nothing wrong between her and you; and I am sure she is a woman of too good sense to put any wrong constructions upon my visit to you; I have been always her friend, and it may be in my power to be much more hereafter.”", "존스는, 더 노련한 바람둥이였다면 그랬을 법하게 아너 부인의 분노를 누그러뜨리는 데 곧장 매달리는 대신, 제 운명의 별을 저주하며 자기야말로 세상에서 가장 불운한 사내라고 한탄하기 시작했다. 그러고는 이내 벨라스턴 귀부인을 향해, 자기는 결백하다는 매우 터무니없는 항변을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 이 무렵 부인은 이성의 쓸모를 되찾았는데—그녀는 세상 어느 여자 못지않게, 특히 이런 경우에 이성을 곧잘 부려 쓰는 사람이었다—차분히 대답했다. \"이봐요, 변명할 것 없어요. 이제 저 사람이 누군지 알겠으니까요. 처음엔 아너 부인을 못 알아봤어요. 하지만 이제 알겠으니, 그녀와 당신 사이에 잘못된 일이 있으리라고는 조금도 의심하지 않아요. 그리고 저 여자는 내가 당신을 찾아온 것을 두고 그릇된 해석을 할 만큼 분별없는 사람이 아닐 거예요. 나는 늘 저 여자의 친구였고, 앞으로는 훨씬 더 큰 힘이 되어줄 수 있을지도 모르죠.\""]], [["Mrs Honour was altogether as placable as she was passionate. Hearing, therefore, Lady Bellaston assume the soft tone, she likewise softened hers.----“I'm sure, madam,” says she, “I have been always ready to acknowledge your ladyship's friendships to me; sure I never had so good a friend as your ladyship----and to be sure, now I see it is your ladyship that I spoke to, I could almost bite my tongue off for very mad.--I constructions upon your ladyship--to be sure it doth not become a servant as I am to think about such a great lady--I mean I was a servant: for indeed I am nobody's servant now, the more miserable wretch is me.--I have lost the best mistress----” Here Honour thought fit to produce a shower of tears.--“Don't cry, child,” says the good lady; “ways perhaps may be found to make you amends. Come to me to-morrow morning.” She then took up her fan which lay on the ground, and without even looking at Jones walked very majestically out of the room; there being a kind of dignity in the impudence of women of quality, which their inferiors vainly aspire to attain to in circumstances of this nature.", "아너 부인은 성을 잘 내는 만큼이나 또 쉽게 풀어지는 사람이었다. 그래서 벨라스턴 귀부인이 부드러운 어조를 띠는 것을 듣자, 그녀 역시 제 어조를 누그러뜨렸다. \"정말이지, 마님,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 늘 마님이 제게 베푸신 우정을 기꺼이 인정해 왔답니다. 정말이지 저는 마님 같은 좋은 친구를 가져본 적이 없어요. 그리고 정말이지, 제가 말씀드린 상대가 마님이라는 걸 이제 알고 나니, 너무 화가 나서 혀를 깨물고 싶을 지경이에요. 제가 마님을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 해석을 하다니요. 정말이지 저 같은 하인이 그런 대단한 귀부인에 대해 이러니저러니 생각하는 건 어울리지 않는 일이죠. 아니, 하인이었다는 말이에요. 사실 저는 이제 아무의 하인도 아니니까요. 그러니 제가 더 비참한 천것이죠. 저는 더없이 좋은 안주인을 잃었어요—\" 여기서 아너는 한바탕 눈물을 쏟아내는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼다. \"울지 마라, 얘야,\" 마음씨 좋은 귀부인이 말했다. \"어쩌면 네게 보상해 줄 방도를 찾을 수도 있을 거야. 내일 아침에 나를 찾아오너라.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 바닥에 떨어져 있던 부채를 집어 들고, 존스는 쳐다보지도 않은 채 매우 위엄 있게 방을 걸어 나갔다. 신분 있는 여자들의 그 뻔뻔함에는 일종의 위엄이 깃들어 있어서, 그보다 못한 자들은 이런 처지에서 그것을 흉내 내려 해봐야 헛수고일 뿐이다."]], [["Jones followed her downstairs, often offering her his hand, which she absolutely refused him, and got into her chair without taking any notice of him as he stood bowing before her.", "존스는 계단을 내려가는 그녀를 뒤따르며 거듭 손을 내밀었으나, 그녀는 한사코 그 손을 마다했고, 그가 그녀 앞에 허리 굽혀 인사하며 서 있는 것도 아랑곳없이 가마에 올라탔다."]], [["At his return upstairs, a long dialogue past between him and Mrs Honour, while she was adjusting herself after the discomposure she had undergone.", "그가 위층으로 돌아오자, 그와 아너 부인 사이에 긴 대화가 오갔다. 그녀가 겪은 소동 뒤 옷매무새를 가다듬는 동안에 말이다."], ["The subject of this was his infidelity to her young lady; on which she enlarged with great bitterness; but Jones at last found means to reconcile her, and not only so, but to obtain a promise of most inviolable secrecy, and that she would the next morning endeavour to find out Sophia, and bring him a further account of the proceedings of the squire.", "이 대화의 주제는 그가 젊은 아가씨에게 보인 부정(不貞)이었고, 그녀는 그것을 두고 몹시 신랄하게 늘어놓았다. 그러나 존스는 마침내 그녀를 달랠 방도를 찾아냈고, 그뿐만 아니라 결코 깨뜨리지 않겠다는 비밀 엄수의 약속과, 이튿날 아침 소피아를 찾아 나서서 나리의 동태에 관한 더 자세한 소식을 그에게 가져다주겠다는 약속까지 얻어냈다."]], [["Thus ended this unfortunate adventure to the satisfaction only of Mrs Honour; for a secret (as some of my readers will perhaps acknowledge from experience) is often a very valuable possession: and that not only to those who faithfully keep it, but sometimes to such as whisper it about till it come to the ears of every one except the ignorant person who pays for the supposed concealing of what is publickly known.", "이리하여 이 불운한 소동은 오직 아너 부인만 만족시킨 채 끝이 났다. 비밀이란 (내 독자 가운데 몇몇은 아마 경험으로 인정하겠지만) 흔히 매우 값진 재산이기 때문이다. 그리고 그것은 비밀을 충실히 지키는 사람에게만이 아니라, 때로는 그것을 여기저기 속삭이고 다녀 마침내, 누구나 다 아는 사실을 감춰준다는 명목으로 대가를 치르는 그 어수룩한 당사자만 빼고 모든 사람의 귀에 들어가게 만드는 사람에게도 그러하다."]], [["Short and sweet.", "짧고도 흐뭇한 이야기."]], [["Notwithstanding all the obligations she had received from Jones, Mrs Miller could not forbear in the morning some gentle remonstrances for the hurricane which had happened the preceding night in his chamber.", "존스에게서 그토록 많은 은혜를 입었음에도, 밀러 부인은 이튿날 아침 전날 밤 그의 방에서 벌어진 한바탕 소동을 두고 부드러운 충고 몇 마디를 건네지 않을 수 없었다."], ["These were, however, so gentle and so friendly, professing, and indeed truly, to aim at nothing more than the real good of Mr Jones himself, that he, far from being offended, thankfully received the admonition of the good woman, expressed much concern for what had past, excused it as well as he could, and promised never more to bring the same disturbances into the house.", "그러나 그 말은 어찌나 부드럽고 다정한지, 오직 존스 씨 자신의 진정한 행복만을 바라는 마음에서 나온 것임을 — 실제로도 그러했다 — 분명히 드러내고 있어서, 그는 기분이 상하기는커녕 그 선한 부인의 훈계를 고맙게 받아들였고, 지난 일에 깊은 유감을 표했으며, 할 수 있는 한 변명을 한 뒤, 다시는 그런 소란을 이 집에 끌어들이지 않겠노라 약속했다."]], [["But though Mrs Miller did not refrain from a short expostulation in private at their first meeting, yet the occasion of his being summoned downstairs that morning was of a much more agreeable kind, being indeed to perform the office of a father to Miss Nancy, and to give her in wedlock to Mr Nightingale, who was now ready drest, and full as sober as many of my readers will think a man ought to be who receives a wife in so imprudent a manner.", "하지만 밀러 부인이 처음 마주쳤을 때 단둘이서 짤막한 잔소리를 삼가지는 않았어도, 그날 아침 그가 아래층으로 불려 내려간 까닭은 훨씬 더 즐거운 종류의 것이었다. 다름 아니라 낸시 양에게 아버지 노릇을 대신 해 주고, 그녀를 나이팅게일 씨에게 시집보내는 일이었다. 나이팅게일 씨는 이제 옷을 말끔히 차려입은 채로, 이토록 분별없는 방식으로 아내를 맞아들이는 사내라면 응당 이래야 한다고 여길 만큼 — 내 독자들 가운데 상당수가 그렇게 생각하겠지만 — 충분히 말짱한 정신이었다."]], [["And here perhaps it may be proper to account for the escape which this young gentleman had made from his uncle, and for his appearance in the condition in which we have seen him the night before.", "그리고 여기서 어쩌면, 이 젊은 신사가 어떻게 큰아버지에게서 빠져나왔는지, 또 우리가 전날 밤에 본 그 꼴로 나타나게 되었는지를 설명해 두는 것이 마땅하겠다."]], [["Now when the uncle had arrived at his lodgings with his nephew, partly to indulge his own inclinations (for he dearly loved his bottle), and partly to disqualify his nephew from the immediate execution of his purpose, he ordered wine to be set on the table; with which he so briskly plyed the young gentleman, that this latter, who, though not much used to drinking, did not detest it so as to be guilty of disobedience or want of complacence by refusing, was soon completely finished.", "큰아버지가 조카를 데리고 자기 숙소에 이르렀을 때, 그는 한편으로는 제 취향을 마음껏 채우려고(그는 술병을 끔찍이도 사랑했으므로), 또 한편으로는 조카가 곧장 제 뜻을 실행에 옮기지 못하도록 만들려고, 식탁에 술을 내오라 일렀다. 그러고는 그것으로 어찌나 부지런히 젊은 신사를 들이켜게 했던지, 술에 그리 익숙하지는 않으나 거절함으로써 불복종이나 무례를 저지를 만큼 술을 싫어하지도 않던 조카는 이내 완전히 뻗어 버리고 말았다."]], [["Just as the uncle had obtained this victory, and was preparing a bed for his nephew, a messenger arrived with a piece of news, which so entirely disconcerted and shocked him, that he in a moment lost all consideration for his nephew, and his whole mind became entirely taken up with his own concerns.", "큰아버지가 막 이 승리를 거두고 조카에게 잠자리를 마련해 주려던 참에, 한 전령이 도착해 어떤 소식을 전했는데, 그것이 어찌나 그를 당황시키고 충격에 빠뜨렸던지, 그는 순식간에 조카에 대한 온갖 신경을 잊어버렸고, 그의 온 마음은 오직 제 자신의 일로만 가득 차 버렸다."]], [["This sudden and afflicting news was no less than that his daughter had taken the opportunity of almost the first moment of his absence, and had gone off with a neighbouring young clergyman; against whom, though her father could have had but one objection, namely, that he was worth nothing, yet she had never thought proper to communicate her amour even to that father; and so artfully had she managed, that it had never been once suspected by any, till now that it was consummated.", "이 느닷없고도 비통한 소식이란 다름 아니라, 그의 딸이 거의 그가 자리를 비운 첫 순간을 틈타 이웃의 한 젊은 성직자와 달아나 버렸다는 것이었다. 그 사내에 대해 아버지가 내세울 만한 흠이라곤 단 하나, 곧 그가 빈털터리라는 것뿐이었음에도, 그녀는 그 아버지에게조차 자신의 연애를 한 번도 털어놓지 않는 편이 옳다고 여겼다. 게다가 어찌나 교묘하게 일을 꾸몄던지, 그 일이 이렇게 끝장을 보기 전까지는 누구도 단 한 번 의심해 본 적이 없었다."]], [["Old Mr Nightingale no sooner received this account, than in the utmost confusion he ordered a post-chaise to be instantly got ready, and, having recommended his nephew to the care of a servant, he directly left the house, scarce knowing what he did, nor whither he went.", "늙은 나이팅게일 씨는 이 소식을 듣자마자 극도의 혼란 속에서 곧장 역마차를 준비시키라 일렀고, 조카를 하인에게 맡겨 둔 채, 제가 무엇을 하는지도 어디로 가는지도 거의 알지 못한 채로 집을 나서 버렸다."]], [["The uncle thus departed, when the servant came to attend the nephew to bed, had waked him for that purpose, and had at last made him sensible that his uncle was gone, he, instead of accepting the kind offices tendered him, insisted on a chair being called; with this the servant, who had received no strict orders to the contrary, readily complied; and, thus being conducted back to the house of Mrs Miller, he had staggered up to Mr Jones's chamber, as hath been before recounted.", "이렇게 큰아버지가 떠나고, 하인이 조카를 잠자리로 모시러 와서 그 목적으로 그를 깨워, 마침내 큰아버지가 가 버렸음을 알아듣게 하자, 그는 자기에게 베풀어진 친절한 시중을 받아들이기는커녕 가마를 불러 달라고 우겼다. 그러자 그러지 말라는 엄한 지시를 받은 바 없던 하인은 선뜻 그 말을 따랐고, 그리하여 다시 밀러 부인의 집으로 인도된 그는, 앞서 이야기한 대로 비틀거리며 존스 씨의 방으로 올라갔던 것이다."]], [["This bar of the uncle being now removed (though young Nightingale knew not as yet in what manner), and all parties being quickly ready, the mother, Mr Jones, Mr Nightingale, and his love, stept into a hackney-coach, which conveyed them to Doctors'", "이제 큰아버지라는 걸림돌이 치워졌으니(젊은 나이팅게일은 아직 그것이 어떤 식으로 그리되었는지는 몰랐지만), 그리고 모두가 금세 채비를 마치자, 어머니와 존스 씨, 나이팅게일 씨와 그의 연인은 삯마차에 올라 닥터스 코먼스로 향했다."], ["Commons; where Miss Nancy was, in vulgar language, soon made an honest woman, and the poor mother became, in the purest sense of the word, one of the happiest of all human beings.", "그곳에서 낸시 양은, 흔한 말로 하자면, 이내 정숙한 여인이 되었고, 가엾은 어머니는 그 말의 가장 순수한 뜻 그대로 온 인류 가운데 가장 행복한 사람 중 하나가 되었다."]], [["And now Mr Jones, having seen his good offices to that poor woman and her family brought to a happy conclusion, began to apply himself to his own concerns; but here, lest many of my readers should censure his folly for thus troubling himself with the affairs of others, and lest some few should think he acted more disinterestedly than indeed he did, we think proper to assure our reader, that he was so far from being unconcerned in this matter, that he had indeed a very considerable interest in bringing it to that final consummation.", "* 닥터스 코먼스: 당시 런던에서 혼인 허가증 등을 발급하던 교회법 관련 법조 기관. 이제 존스 씨는 그 가엾은 여인과 그 가족에게 베푼 선행이 행복한 결말에 이른 것을 보고 나서, 자신의 일에 마음을 쏟기 시작했다. 그런데 여기서, 내 독자들 가운데 많은 이가 이렇게 남의 일로 제 몸을 수고롭게 한 그의 어리석음을 나무랄까 봐, 또 몇몇은 그가 실제보다 더 사심 없이 행동했다고 여길까 봐, 우리는 독자에게 이렇게 일러 두는 것이 마땅하다고 본다. 그는 이 일에 무심하기는커녕, 사실 그것을 이 마지막 결말로 이끄는 데 상당히 큰 이해관계를 지니고 있었다고 말이다."]], [["To explain this seeming paradox at once, he was one who could truly say with him in Terence, Homo sum: humani nihil a me alienum puto. He was never an indifferent spectator of the misery or happiness of any one; and he felt either the one or the other in great proportion as he himself contributed to either. He could not, therefore, be the instrument of raising a whole family from the lowest state of wretchedness to the highest pitch of joy without conveying great felicity to himself; more perhaps than worldly men often purchase to themselves by undergoing the most severe labour, and often by wading through the deepest iniquity.", "이 그럴듯한 역설을 단번에 풀어 보자면, 그는 테렌티우스의 작중 인물처럼 진심으로 이렇게 말할 수 있는 사람이었다. \"나는 인간이다. 인간의 일이라면 그 무엇도 나와 무관하다 여기지 않는다.\" 그는 그 누구의 불행이나 행복에 대해서도 결코 무심한 구경꾼인 적이 없었다. 그리고 그는 자기 자신이 그 어느 쪽에 보탬이 된 만큼 그 둘 중 하나를 크게 느꼈다. 그러므로 그는 한 가족 전체를 가장 비참한 밑바닥에서 가장 드높은 기쁨의 정점으로 끌어올리는 도구가 되면서, 제 자신에게도 크나큰 행복을 안기지 않을 수 없었다. 어쩌면 세상 사람들이 더없이 혹독한 수고를 견디고, 더러는 가장 깊은 죄악을 헤쳐 나가며 스스로 사들이는 것보다 더 큰 행복을 말이다."]], [["Those readers who are of the same complexion with him will perhaps think this short chapter contains abundance of matter; while others may probably wish, short as it is, that it had been totally spared as impertinent to the main design, which I suppose they conclude is to bring Mr Jones to the gallows, or, if possible, to a more deplorable catastrophe.", "그와 같은 기질을 지닌 독자라면 어쩌면 이 짧은 장에 풍성한 내용이 담겨 있다고 여길 것이다. 반면 다른 이들은 아마, 이토록 짧긴 해도, 이 장이 본래의 큰 목적과는 무관하니 차라리 통째로 덜어 내는 편이 나았으리라 바랄지도 모른다. 내 짐작에 그들이 단정 짓는 그 목적이란, 존스 씨를 교수대로, 아니 될 수만 있다면 더 한심한 파국으로 끌고 가는 것일 테니 말이다."]], [["Containing love-letters of several sorts.", "9장. 여러 종류의 연애편지를 담은 장."]], [["Mr Jones, at his return home, found the following letters lying on his table, which he luckily opened in the order they were sent.", "존스는 집에 돌아와 책상 위에 놓인 다음의 편지들을 발견했는데, 다행히도 그것들을 보내진 순서대로 열어 보았다."], ["LETTER I.", "첫째 편지."]], [["“Surely I am under some strange infatuation; I cannot keep my resolutions a moment, however strongly made or justly founded.", "\"분명 저는 어떤 야릇한 홀림에 빠져 있나 봅니다. 아무리 굳게 다지고 아무리 옳게 세운 결심이라 해도 한순간도 지키지를 못하니 말이에요."], ["Last night I resolved never to see you more; this morning I am willing to hear if you can, as you say, clear up this affair.", "어젯밤 저는 다시는 당신을 보지 않겠다고 마음먹었습니다. 그런데 오늘 아침이 되니, 당신 말대로 이 일을 정말 해명할 수 있다면 그 말을 들어 보고 싶어집니다."], ["And yet I know that to be impossible.", "그러면서도 저는 그것이 불가능하다는 걸 알아요."], ["I have said everything to myself which you can invent.----Perhaps not.", "당신이 꾸며 낼 수 있는 말이라면 저는 이미 제 자신에게 죄다 해 보았으니까요.----어쩌면 아닐지도 모르죠."], ["Perhaps your invention is stronger.", "어쩌면 당신의 둘러대는 재간이 더 셀지도 모르겠어요."], ["Come to me, therefore, the moment you receive this.", "그러니 이 편지를 받는 순간 곧장 제게로 오세요."], ["If you can forge an excuse I almost promise you to believe it.", "당신이 변명을 지어낼 수 있다면, 저는 그것을 믿겠다고 거의 약속이라도 할 것 같아요."], ["Betrayed too----I will think no more.----Come to me directly.----This is the third letter I have writ, the two former are burnt----I am almost inclined to burn this too----I wish I may preserve my senses.----Come to me presently.”", "배신까지 당하고도----더는 생각하지 않겠어요.----곧바로 제게로 오세요.----이것이 제가 쓴 세 번째 편지예요. 앞의 두 통은 태워 버렸어요----이것마저 태워 버리고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같아요----부디 제가 제정신을 잃지 않기를 바랄 뿐이에요.----어서 제게로 오세요.\""]], [["# LETTER II.", "# LETTER II."]], [["“If you ever expect to be forgiven, or even suffered within my doors, come to me this instant.”", "편지 2."], ["LETTER III.", "\"용서받기를, 아니 내 집 문 안에 한 발이라도 들이기를 바란다면, 지금 이 순간 당장 내게로 오시오.\""]], [["“I now find you was not at home when my notes came to your lodgings.", "편지 3. \"이제야 알았습니다."], ["The moment you receive this let me see you;--I shall not stir out; nor shall anybody be let in but yourself.", "제 쪽지들이 당신 숙소에 닿았을 때 당신은 집에 없었더군요. 이 편지를 받는 즉시 나를 만나러 오세요."], ["Sure nothing can detain you long.”", "나는 한 발짝도 밖으로 나가지 않을 것이며, 당신 말고는 그 누구도 들이지 않겠습니다. 분명 당신을 오래 붙들어 둘 일이란 없을 테지요.\""]], [["Jones had just read over these three billets when Mr Nightingale came into the room.", "존스가 이 세 통의 짧은 편지를 막 다 읽었을 때, 나이팅게일 씨가 방으로 들어왔다. \"이보게, 톰.\""], ["“Well, Tom,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “any news from Lady Bellaston, after last night's adventure?”", "\"어젯밤 그 소동을 치르고 나서 벨라스턴 부인에게서 무슨 소식이라도 왔나?\""], ["(for it was now no secret to any one in that house who the lady was). “The Lady Bellaston?”", "(이제 그 집 안에서는 그 부인이 누구인지 누구에게도 비밀이 아니었던 것이다.) \"벨라스턴 부인이라니?\""], ["answered Jones very gravely.----“Nay, dear Tom,”", "존스가 자못 진지하게 되물었다. \"이런, 톰.\""], ["cries Nightingale, “don't be so reserved to your friends.", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"친구한테 그렇게 숨길 것까진 없잖나."], ["Though I was too drunk to see her last night, I saw her at the masquerade.", "내가 어젯밤엔 너무 취해서 그분을 못 봤지만, 가면무도회에서는 봤다네."], ["Do you think I am ignorant who the queen of the fairies is?”", "자네는 내가 그 요정 나라의 여왕이 누구인지 모를 거라고 생각하나?\""], ["“And did you really then know the lady at the masquerade?”", "\"그래서 자네는 정말로 가면무도회에서 그 부인을 알아본 건가?\""], ["said Jones.", "존스가 말했다."], ["“Yes, upon my soul, did I,”", "\"그렇다네, 내 영혼을 걸고 알아봤지.\""], ["said Nightingale, “and have given you twenty hints of it since, though you seemed always so tender on that point, that I would not speak plainly.", "나이팅게일이 말했다. \"그 후로도 자네에게 스무 번은 넌지시 운을 띄웠어."], ["I fancy, my friend, by your extreme nicety in this matter, you are not so well acquainted with the character of the lady as with her person.", "다만 자네가 늘 그 대목에서 어찌나 예민하게 굴던지, 대놓고 말을 못 했을 뿐이야. 친구, 이 일에 대한 자네의 그 지나친 조심성을 보아하니, 자네는 그 부인의 사람됨보다는 외모 쪽을 더 잘 아는 모양일세."], ["Don't be angry, Tom, but upon my honour, you are not the first young fellow she hath debauched.", "화내지 말게, 톰. 하지만 내 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 자네가 그 부인이 망쳐 놓은 첫 번째 젊은이는 아니라네."], ["Her reputation is in no danger, believe me.”", "그분의 평판은 조금도 위태롭지 않으니, 내 말을 믿게.\""]], [["Though Jones had no reason to imagine the lady to have been of the vestal kind when his amour began; yet, as he was thoroughly ignorant of the town, and had very little acquaintance in it, he had no knowledge of that character which is vulgarly called a demirep; that is to say, a woman who intrigues with every man she likes, under the name and appearance of virtue; and who, though some over-nice ladies will not be seen with her, is visited (as they term it) by the whole town, in short, whom everybody knows to be what nobody calls her.", "존스는 자신의 연애가 시작될 때 그 부인을 베스타의 처녀 같은 부류라고 상상할 만한 근거가 없었다. 그러나 그는 이 도시의 물정에 완전히 어두웠고 아는 이도 거의 없었던 터라, 흔히 데미렙이라 불리는 그런 사람됨에 대해서는 알지 못했다. 그것은 곧, 덕이라는 이름과 겉모습을 두르고서 마음에 드는 남자라면 누구와든 정을 통하는 여자를 일컫는 말이다. 지나치게 깔끔을 떠는 일부 부인들은 그런 여자와 함께 있는 모습을 보이려 하지 않지만, 그래도 (그들 표현을 빌리자면) 온 도시 사람들이 다 그 집을 드나든다. 한마디로, 누구나 그 정체를 알면서도 아무도 그 이름으로는 부르지 않는 여자다."]], [["When he found, therefore, that Nightingale was perfectly acquainted with his intrigue, and began to suspect that so scrupulous a delicacy as he had hitherto observed was not quite necessary on the occasion, he gave a latitude to his friend's tongue, and desired him to speak plainly what he knew, or had ever heard of the lady.", "* 베스타의 처녀: 고대 로마에서 화로의 여신 베스타를 섬기던, 순결을 지킨 여사제. 정숙한 처녀를 빗대는 표현. 그리하여 존스는 나이팅게일이 자신의 정사를 속속들이 알고 있음을 알아차렸고, 지금껏 자신이 지켜 온 그 까다로운 조심성이 이 경우에는 그리 필요치 않았던 게 아닌가 의심하기 시작했다. 그래서 그는 친구의 혀에 자유를 허락하고, 그 부인에 대해 아는 것이나 들은 것이 있으면 숨김없이 말해 달라고 청했다."]], [["Nightingale, who, in many other instances, was rather too effeminate in his disposition, had a pretty strong inclination to tittle-tattle.", "나이팅게일은 다른 여러 면에서 기질이 다소 여성스러운 데가 있었지만, 수다를 떠는 데에는 제법 강한 끌림을 지니고 있었다."], ["He had no sooner, therefore, received a full liberty of speaking from Jones, than he entered upon a long narrative concerning the lady; which, as it contained many particulars highly to her dishonour, we have too great a tenderness for all women of condition to repeat.", "그리하여 존스에게서 마음껏 말해도 좋다는 허락을 받기가 무섭게, 그는 그 부인에 관한 긴 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그 이야기에는 그녀의 명예를 크게 떨어뜨릴 시시콜콜한 내용이 워낙 많았던지라, 우리는 지체 높은 모든 여인에 대해 너무나 큰 동정심을 품고 있는 터라 차마 그것을 옮기지 못하겠다."], ["We would cautiously avoid giving an opportunity to the future commentators on our works, of making any malicious application and of forcing us to be, against our will, the author of scandal, which never entered into our head.", "우리는 훗날 우리 작품에 주석을 달 이들에게, 무언가 악의적인 해석을 갖다 붙이거나, 우리 머릿속에는 떠오른 적도 없는 추문의 작가로 우리를 본의 아니게 만들어 버릴 빌미를 주는 일을 조심스레 피하고자 한다."]], [["Jones, having very attentively heard all that Nightingale had to say, fetched a deep sigh; which the other, observing, cried, “Heyday!", "존스는 나이팅게일이 하는 말을 모두 아주 주의 깊게 듣고 나서 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그것을 본 나이팅게일이 외쳤다."], ["why, thou art not in love, I hope!", "\"아니 이런! 설마 자네 사랑에 빠진 건 아니겠지!"], ["Had I imagined my stories would have affected you, I promise you should never have heard them.”", "내 이야기들이 자네 마음을 이렇게 흔들 줄 알았더라면, 맹세코 자네는 결코 그것을 듣지 못했을 걸세.\""], ["“O my dear friend!”", "\"오, 친구!\""], ["cries Jones, “I am so entangled with this woman, that I know not how to extricate myself.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"나는 그 여자에게 너무 얽혀 버려서, 어떻게 빠져나와야 할지 모르겠네."], ["In love, indeed!", "사랑이라니, 그건 아닐세, 친구."], ["no, my friend, but I am under obligations to her, and very great ones.", "다만 나는 그분께 신세를 졌어. 그것도 아주 큰 신세를."], ["Since you know so much, I will be very explicit with you.", "자네가 이미 그렇게 많이 아니, 나도 자네에게 아주 솔직히 털어놓겠네."], ["It is owing, perhaps, solely to her, that I have not, before this, wanted a bit of bread.", "어쩌면 내가 지금까지 빵 한 조각 굶지 않은 것도 오로지 그분 덕분일지 몰라. 그런 여자를 내가 어떻게 저버릴 수 있겠나?"], ["How can I possibly desert such a woman?", "그러나 나는 그녀를 버려야만 하네."], ["and yet I must desert her, or be guilty of the blackest treachery to one who deserves infinitely better of me than she can; a woman, my Nightingale, for whom I have a passion which few can have an idea of.", "안 그러면, 그녀보다 나에게 한없이 더 값진 사람에게 더없이 시커먼 배신을 저지르는 꼴이 되고 마네. 나이팅게일, 그건 좀처럼 누구도 상상하기 어려운 격정을 내가 품고 있는 여인일세."], ["I am half distracted with doubts how to act.”", "나는 어찌 처신해야 할지 갈피를 못 잡고 반쯤 정신이 나가 있다네.\""], ["“And is this other, pray, an honourable mistress?”", "\"그런데 말이지, 그 다른 여인은 떳떳한 정인인가?\""], ["cries Nightingale.", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다."], ["“Honourable!”", "\"떳떳하냐고!\""], ["answered Jones; “no breath ever yet durst sully her reputation.", "존스가 답했다. \"지금껏 그 어떤 입김도 감히 그녀의 평판을 더럽히지 못했네."], ["The sweetest air is not purer, the limpid stream not clearer, than her honour.", "가장 향기로운 바람도 그녀의 명예만큼 맑지 못하고, 가장 투명한 시냇물도 그만큼 깨끗하지 못해."], ["She is all over, both in mind and body, consummate perfection.", "그녀는 마음에서나 몸에서나 온통 완벽함 그 자체일세."], ["She is the most beautiful creature in the universe: and yet she is mistress of such noble elevated qualities, that, though she is never from my thoughts, I scarce ever think of her beauty but when I see it.”--“And can you, my good friend,”", "이 우주에서 가장 아름다운 존재지. 그러면서도 그녀는 그토록 고귀하고 드높은 자질을 갖추고 있어서, 비록 그녀가 한순간도 내 생각에서 떠나지 않건만, 나는 그녀의 아름다움을 직접 눈으로 볼 때가 아니고서는 그 아름다움을 거의 떠올리지조차 않는다네.\""], ["cries Nightingale, “with such an engagement as this upon your hands, hesitate a moment about quitting such a--”", "\"그렇다면 친구, 자네는 그런 인연을 손에 쥐고 있으면서, 그런 여자를 떠나는 일에 한순간이라도 망설일 수 있단 말인가, 그런—\" \"그만.\""], ["“Hold,”", "존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “no more abuse of her: I detest the thought of ingratitude.”", "\"더는 그녀를 헐뜯지 말게. 나는 배은망덕하다는 생각만으로도 진저리가 나네.\""], ["“Pooh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["answered the other, “you are not the first upon whom she hath conferred obligations of this kind.", "상대가 대꾸했다. \"그녀가 이런 식의 은혜를 베푼 사람이 자네가 처음은 아닐세."], ["She is remarkably liberal where she likes; though, let me tell you, her favours are so prudently bestowed, that they should rather raise a man's vanity than his gratitude.”", "그녀는 마음에 드는 사람에게는 놀랄 만큼 후하지. 하지만 내 말 좀 들어 보게, 그녀의 호의란 워낙 약삭빠르게 베풀어지는 것이라, 사람의 고마움이 아니라 차라리 허영심을 부추기는 쪽이라네.\""], ["In short, Nightingale proceeded so far on this head, and told his friend so many stories of the lady, which he swore to the truth of, that he entirely removed all esteem for her from the breast of Jones; and his gratitude was lessened in proportion.", "요컨대 나이팅게일은 이 대목으로 한참을 더 나아가며, 자기가 진실이라 맹세하는 그 부인의 이야기를 친구에게 어찌나 많이 늘어놓았던지, 존스의 가슴에서 그녀에 대한 모든 존경심을 깡그리 걷어내고 말았다. 그리고 그의 고마움도 그에 비례하여 줄어들었다."], ["Indeed, he began to look on all the favours he had received rather as wages than benefits, which depreciated not only her, but himself too in his own conceit, and put him quite out of humour with both.", "실로 그는 자기가 받은 모든 호의를 은혜라기보다는 차라리 품삯으로 여기기 시작했고, 이는 그녀뿐 아니라 자기 자신마저도 스스로의 눈에 깎아내리는 일이어서, 그는 두 사람 모두에 대해 잔뜩 기분이 상해 버렸다. 이 역겨움에서 그의 마음은 자연스러운 흐름을 타고 소피아에게로 향했다."], ["From this disgust, his mind, by a natural transition, turned towards Sophia; her virtue, her purity, her love to him, her sufferings on his account, filled all his thoughts, and made his commerce with Lady Bellaston appear still more odious.", "그녀의 덕성, 그녀의 순결함, 자신을 향한 그녀의 사랑, 자신 때문에 그녀가 겪은 고초가 온통 그의 생각을 가득 채웠고, 벨라스턴 부인과의 관계는 한층 더 혐오스럽게만 보이게 만들었다. 이 모든 것의 결말은 이러했다."], ["The result of all was, that, though his turning himself out of her service, in which light he now saw his affair with her, would be the loss of his bread; yet he determined to quit her, if he could but find a handsome pretence: which being communicated to his friend, Nightingale considered a little, and then said, “I have it, my boy!", "이제 그는 그녀와의 일을 일종의 고용살이로 보게 되었는데, 그 고용살이에서 스스로 발을 빼는 일은 곧 자기 빵줄을 잃는 일이었음에도, 그는 그럴듯한 구실만 찾을 수 있다면 그녀를 떠나기로 마음을 굳혔다. 이를 친구에게 털어놓자, 나이팅게일은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 이내 말했다."], ["I have found out a sure method; propose marriage to her, and I would venture hanging upon the success.”", "\"찾았네, 이 친구야! 확실한 방법을 알아냈어."], ["“Marriage?”", "그녀에게 청혼을 하게."], ["cries Jones.", "그 성공에 내 목을 걸어도 좋네.\""], ["“Ay, propose marriage,”", "\"청혼이라고?\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["answered Nightingale, “and she will declare off in a moment.", "\"그래, 청혼을 하란 말일세.\" 나이팅게일이 답했다."], ["I knew a young fellow whom she kept formerly, who made the offer to her in earnest, and was presently turned off for his pains.”", "\"그러면 그녀는 단번에 발을 뺄 거야. 예전에 그녀가 데리고 있던 젊은이를 하나 알았는데, 그가 그녀에게 진심으로 청혼을 했다가, 그 수고의 대가로 곧장 쫓겨났다네.\""]], [["Jones declared he could not venture the experiment.", "존스는 그런 시험을 감히 해 볼 수 없노라고 잘라 말했다. \"어쩌면,\""], ["“Perhaps,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “she may be less shocked at this proposal from one man than from another.", "\"그녀가 다른 남자보다 내 입에서 나온 이 청혼에는 덜 충격을 받을지도 모르지. 그러다 그녀가 정말로 내 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들이면, 그땐 나는 어찌 되는 건가?"], ["And if she should take me at my word, where am I then?", "제 덫에 제가 걸려 영영 끝장나는 게 아닌가.\" \"아닐세.\""], ["caught, in my own trap, and undone for ever.” “No;”", "나이팅게일이 답했다."], ["answered Nightingale, “not if I can give you an expedient by which you may at any time get out of the trap.”----“What expedient can that be?”", "\"내가 자네에게 언제든 그 덫에서 빠져나올 방책 하나를 일러 줄 수만 있다면 그럴 일은 없지.\" \"그게 대체 무슨 방책이란 말인가?\""], ["replied Jones.", "존스가 받아쳤다. \"이런 거라네.\""], ["“This,”", "나이팅게일이 답했다."], ["answered Nightingale.", "\"내가 말한 그 젊은이는 세상에서 나와 가장 가까운 친구들 가운데 하나인데, 그 후로 그녀가 자기에게 못된 짓을 몇 가지 저지른 일로 단단히 화가 나 있어서, 분명 아무 어려움 없이 자네에게 그녀의 편지를 보여 줄 걸세. 그것을 빌미로 자네는 점잖게 그녀와 갈라설 수 있고, 만약 그녀가 정말로 그 매듭을 묶고 싶어 한다면—나는 그럴 리 없다고 확신하지만—그 매듭이 묶이기 전에 발을 뺄 수 있지.\""], ["“The young fellow I mentioned, who is one of the most intimate acquaintances I have in the world, is so angry with her for some ill offices she hath since done him, that I am sure he would, without any difficulty, give you a sight of her letters; upon which you may decently break with her; and declare off before the knot is tyed, if she should really be willing to tie it, which I am convinced she will not.”", "얼마간 망설인 끝에, 존스는 이 장담에 힘입어 동의했다. 그러나 그는 면전에서 그 일을 꺼낼 만한 배짱이 없다고 맹세했으므로, 나이팅게일이 불러 주는 대로 다음과 같은 편지를 썼다."]], [["After some hesitation, Jones, upon the strength of this assurance, consented; but, as he swore he wanted the confidence to propose the matter to her face, he wrote the following letter, which Nightingale dictated:--", "\"부인,\n불행히도 밖에서 약속에 매여 있던 탓에, 부인의 분부가 당도한 바로 그 순간에 그 영광을 받들지 못한 것이 못내 마음에 걸립니다. 이제 와 부인께 제 처지를 변명해야 하는 이 지체가 그 불운을 더욱 무겁게 합니다. 오, 벨라스턴 부인! 이 짓궂은 사고들로 인해 부인의 평판이 행여 드러날까 제가 얼마나 두려움에 떨었는지요! 그것을 지킬 길은 오직 하나뿐입니다. 그것이 무엇인지 굳이 입에 올릴 필요는 없겠지요. 다만 이 말만은 하게 해 주십시오. 부인의 명예가 제 명예만큼이나 제게 소중하듯, 제 유일한 야심은 제 자유를 부인의 발치에 바치는 영광을 얻는 것뿐입니다. 그리고 제 말을 믿어 주십시오. 부인을 영원히 제 사람이라 부를 법적 권리를 부인께서 너그러이 베풀어 주시지 않는 한, 저는 결코 온전한 행복을 누릴 수 없으리라는 것을. 저는,"]], [["“I am extremely concerned, that, by an unfortunate engagement abroad, I should have missed receiving the honour of your ladyship's commands the moment they came; and the delay which I must now suffer of vindicating myself to your ladyship greatly adds to this misfortune. O, Lady Bellaston! what a terror have I been in for fear your reputation should be exposed by these perverse accidents! There is one only way to secure it. I need not name what that is. Only permit me to say, that as your honour is as dear to me as my own, so my sole ambition is to have the glory of laying my liberty at your feet; and believe me when I assure you, I can never be made completely happy without you generously bestow on me a legal right of calling you mine for ever.--I am,", "부인의,\n더없이 깊은 존경을 담아,"]], [["madam, with most profound respect, your ladyship's most obliged, obedient, humble servant, THOMAS JONES.”", "부인께 더없이 큰 신세를 진,\n순종하는 미천한 종,"]], [["To this she presently returned the following answer:", "토머스 존스 올림.\" 이에 그녀는 곧장 다음과 같은 답장을 보내왔다."]], [["“When I read over your serious epistle, I could, from its coldness and formality, have sworn that you already had the legal right you mention; nay, that we had for many years composed that monstrous animal a husband and wife.", "\"선생,\n당신의 그 점잖은 편지를 다 읽고 나니, 그 냉랭함과 격식으로 미루어, 당신이 이미 말씀하신 그 법적 권리를 손에 쥐고 있다고, 아니 우리가 여러 해 동안 부부라는 그 괴물 같은 짐승을 이루고 살아왔다고 맹세라도 할 뻔했습니다."], ["Do you really then imagine me a fool?", "그래, 당신은 정말로 나를 바보로 여기는 것입니까?"], ["or do you fancy yourself capable of so entirely persuading me out of my senses, that I should deliver my whole fortune into your power, in order to enable you to support your pleasures at my expense?", "아니면 당신이 내 정신을 송두리째 홀려, 내가 내 전 재산을 당신 손아귀에 고스란히 넘겨, 당신이 내 돈으로 당신 쾌락이나 떠받치도록 거들게 만들 수 있다고 자신하는 것입니까?"], ["Are these the proofs of love which I expected?", "이런 것이 내가 기대했던 사랑의 증거란 말입니까?"], ["Is this the return for--?", "이것이 그 보답입니까—?"], ["but I scorn to upbraid you, and am in great admiration of your profound respect.", "그러나 나는 당신을 나무라는 것조차 떳떳지 못하다 여기며, 당신의 그 깊디깊은 존경에는 그저 감탄해 마지않습니다."]], [["“P.S.", "추신. 다시 고쳐 볼 겨를이 없습니다."], ["I am prevented from revising:----Perhaps I have said more than I meant.----Come to me at eight this evening.”", "어쩌면 제 본심보다 더 많은 말을 했는지도 모르겠습니다. 오늘 저녁 여덟 시에 내게로 오세요.\""]], [["Jones, by the advice of his privy-council, replied:", "존스는 자신의 밀실 자문단의 조언을 받아 이렇게 답했다."]], [["“It is impossible to express how much I am shocked at the suspicion you entertain of me.", "\"부인,\n부인께서 저를 의심하신다는 사실에 제가 얼마나 큰 충격을 받았는지는 이루 말로 다 할 수 없습니다."], ["Can Lady Bellaston have conferred favours on a man whom she could believe capable of so base a design?", "벨라스턴 부인께서 그토록 비열한 흉계를 품을 만한 사람이라 믿을 수 있는 자에게 은혜를 베푸셨겠습니까?"], ["or can she treat the most solemn tie of love with contempt?", "아니면 부인께서 사랑의 가장 엄숙한 맹세를 한낱 경멸로 대하실 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Can you imagine, madam, that if the violence of my passion, in an unguarded moment, overcame the tenderness which I have for your honour, I would think of indulging myself in the continuance of an intercourse which could not possibly escape long the notice of the world; and which, when discovered, must prove so fatal to your reputation?", "부인, 한번 생각해 보십시오. 설령 방심한 한순간에 제 격정이 거세어져, 부인의 명예를 향한 제 애틋함을 이겨 버렸다 한들, 머지않아 세상의 이목을 결코 피할 수 없고, 발각되는 날에는 부인의 평판에 그토록 치명적일 수밖에 없는 그런 관계를 계속 이어 가며 제 욕심을 채울 생각을 하겠습니까?"], ["If such be your opinion of me, I must pray for a sudden opportunity of returning those pecuniary obligations, which I have been so unfortunate to receive at your hands; and for those of a more tender kind, I shall ever remain, &c.”", "만일 저를 그런 사람으로 보신다면, 저는 제가 불운하게도 부인의 손에서 받게 된 그 금전적 신세를 하루빨리 갚을 기회가 오기를 빌 따름입니다. 그리고 그보다 더 애틋한 종류의 신세에 대해서는, 저는 언제까지나 운운.\""], ["And so concluded in the very words with which he had concluded the former letter.", "그러고는 앞서의 편지를 맺을 때 썼던 바로 그 말 그대로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["The lady answered as follows:", "부인은 다음과 같이 답했다."]], [["“I see you are a villain!", "\"이제 보니 당신은 악당이군요!"], ["and I despise you from my soul.", "나는 영혼 깊은 곳에서부터 당신을 경멸합니다."], ["If you come here I shall not be at home.”", "당신이 여기 온대도, 나는 집에 없을 것입니다.\""]], [["Though Jones was well satisfied with his deliverance from a thraldom which those who have ever experienced it will, I apprehend, allow to be none of the lightest, he was not, however, perfectly easy in his mind. There was in this scheme too much of fallacy to satisfy one who utterly detested every species of falshood or dishonesty: nor would he, indeed, have submitted to put it in practice, had he not been involved in a distressful situation, where he was obliged to be guilty of some dishonour, either to the one lady or the other; and surely the reader will allow, that every good principle, as well as love, pleaded strongly in favour of Sophia.", "이런 속박에서 벗어난 것에 존스는 무척 흡족했지만—그것을 한 번이라도 겪어 본 사람이라면, 짐작건대 그 속박이 결코 가벼운 축에 들지 않음을 인정하리라—그렇다고 그의 마음이 온전히 편안했던 것은 아니었다. 이 계략 속에는 거짓이나 부정직함이라면 그 어떤 종류든 진저리 치게 싫어하는 사람을 흡족하게 하기에는 속임수가 너무 많이 들어 있었다. 그리고 실로 그가 이를 실행에 옮기는 데 응한 것도, 한쪽 부인이든 다른 쪽 여인이든, 어느 한 사람에게는 어떤 불명예를 저지를 수밖에 없는 괴로운 처지에 얽혀 있지 않았더라면 결코 없었을 일이다. 그리고 분명 독자도 인정하리라. 모든 선한 원칙이, 사랑과 더불어, 소피아 편에 서서 강력하게 호소하고 있었음을."]], [["Nightingale highly exulted in the success of his stratagem, upon which he received many thanks and much applause from his friend.", "나이팅게일은 자기 책략이 성공한 것에 크게 우쭐했고, 그 일로 친구에게서 많은 감사와 갈채를 받았다."], ["He answered, “Dear Tom, we have conferred very different obligations on each other.", "그가 대답했다. \"이보게 톰, 우리는 서로에게 사뭇 다른 신세를 진 셈일세."], ["To me you owe the regaining your liberty; to you I owe the loss of mine.", "자네는 자유를 되찾은 일을 내게 빚졌고, 나는 자유를 잃은 일을 자네에게 빚졌으니."], ["But if you are as happy in the one instance as I am in the other, I promise you we are the two happiest fellows in England.”", "하지만 자네가 그 한쪽에서 내가 다른 쪽에서 누리는 만큼 행복하다면, 장담컨대 우리 둘은 잉글랜드에서 가장 행복한 사내들일세.\""]], [["The two gentlemen were now summoned down to dinner, where Mrs Miller, who performed herself the office of cook, had exerted her best talents to celebrate the wedding of her daughter.", "이제 두 신사는 식사 자리로 불려 내려갔다. 그곳에서는 밀러 부인이 손수 요리사 노릇을 맡아, 딸의 혼례를 축하하느라 자신의 온 솜씨를 발휘하고 있었다."], ["This joyful circumstance she ascribed principally to the friendly behaviour of Jones, her whole soul was fired with gratitude towards him, and all her looks, words, and actions, were so busied in expressing it, that her daughter, and even her new son-in-law, were very little objects of her consideration.", "그녀는 이 기쁜 경사를 무엇보다 존스의 다정한 행실 덕으로 돌렸고, 온 영혼이 그를 향한 고마움으로 불타올라, 그 눈빛이며 말이며 행동이 죄다 그것을 드러내느라 분주한 나머지, 정작 딸은, 심지어 새 사위마저도 그녀의 안중에는 거의 들어오지 않았다."]], [["Dinner was just ended when Mrs Miller received a letter; but as we have had letters enow in this chapter, we shall communicate its contents in our next.", "식사가 막 끝났을 때 밀러 부인은 편지 한 통을 받았다. 그러나 이 장에서는 이미 편지가 차고도 넘쳤으니, 그 내용은 다음 장에서 전하기로 하겠다."]], [["# Chapter x.", "# Chapter x."]], [["Consisting partly of facts, and partly of observations upon them.", "앞 장 끝머리에 도착한 그 편지는 올워디 씨가 보낸 것이었다."], ["The letter then which arrived at the end of the preceding chapter was from Mr Allworthy, and the purport of it was, his intention to come immediately to town, with his nephew Blifil, and a desire to be accommodated with his usual lodgings, which were the first floor for himself, and the second for his nephew.", "내용인즉, 곧 조카 블리필을 데리고 런던으로 올라올 작정이니 늘 쓰던 숙소를 마련해 두기를 바란다는 것이었다. 그 숙소란 자신이 묵을 이 층과 조카가 묵을 삼 층을 말함이었다."]], [["The chearfulness which had before displayed itself in the countenance of the poor woman was a little clouded on this occasion.", "이 가엾은 여인의 얼굴에 그전까지 떠올라 있던 밝은 빛이 이번 일로 조금 흐려졌다. 이 소식이 그녀를 적잖이 당황하게 만든 것은 사실이었다."], ["This news did indeed a good deal disconcert her.", "한편으로는, 딸과의 그토록 사심 없는 혼인을 갚는 대가로 이제 막 사위가 된 사람을 당장 문밖으로 내쫓는다는 것이 그녀에게는 도무지 정당화될 수 없는 일로 보였다. 다른 한편으로는, 올워디 씨에게서 그토록 많은 은혜를 입고서도 엄밀히 따지면 마땅히 그의 몫인 숙소를 빼앗는 일에 무슨 변명을 늘어놓을 생각을 차마 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["To requite so disinterested a match with her daughter, by presently turning her new son-in-law out of doors, appeared to her very unjustifiable on the one hand; and on the other, she could scarce bear the thoughts of making any excuse to Mr Allworthy, after all the obligations received from him, for depriving him of lodgings which were indeed strictly his due; for that gentleman, in conferring all his numberless benefits on others, acted by a rule diametrically opposite to what is practised by most generous people.", "이 신사는 남에게 그 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 은혜를 베풀 때, 대다수의 너그러운 사람들이 행하는 방식과는 정반대의 원칙을 따랐기 때문이다. 그는 어떤 경우에든 자신의 선행을 세상으로부터는 물론이고 그 선행을 받는 당사자에게서까지 숨기려 들었다."], ["He contrived, on all occasions, to hide his beneficence, not only from the world, but even from the object of it.", "그는 \"주다\"라는 말 대신 한결같이 \"빌려주다\", \"갚다\"라는 말을 썼다. 또한 그가 생각해 낼 수 있는 온갖 다른 방법을 동원해, 두 손으로는 은혜를 산더미처럼 쌓아 올리면서도 입으로는 늘 그 은혜를 깎아내렸다."], ["He constantly used the words Lend and Pay, instead of Give; and by every other method he could invent, always lessened with his tongue the favours he conferred, while he was heaping them with both his hands.", "그래서 밀러 부인에게 연 50파운드의 연금을 정해 줄 때에도, 그는 \"이건 내가 런던에 올라올 때 항상 그대의 이 층을 쓰는 값으로 치는 것이오(사실 그는 그럴 작정이 거의 없었다). 다만 다른 때에는 얼마든지 세를 놓아도 좋소."], ["When he settled the annuity of £50 a year therefore on Mrs Miller, he told her, “it was in consideration of always having her first-floor when he was in town (which he scarce ever intended to be), but that she might let it at any other time, for that he would always send her a month's warning.”", "내가 늘 한 달 전에 미리 기별을 보낼 테니 말이오\"라고 말했다. 그러나 이번에는 너무도 갑작스럽게 런던행을 서두르는 바람에 그런 기별을 보낼 틈이 없었다."], ["He was now, however, hurried to town so suddenly, that he had no opportunity of giving such notice; and this hurry probably prevented him, when he wrote for his lodgings, adding, if they were then empty; for he would most certainly have been well satisfied to have relinquished them, on a less sufficient excuse than what Mrs Miller could now have made.", "그리고 아마도 이 황망함 때문에, 숙소를 청하는 편지를 쓰면서 \"그때 비어 있다면\"이라는 단서를 덧붙이는 것을 잊었으리라. 그도 그럴 것이, 밀러 부인이 지금 내놓을 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 부족한 변명에도 그는 틀림없이 흔쾌히 그 숙소를 양보했을 사람이었으니 말이다."]], [["But there are a sort of persons, who, as Prior excellently well remarks, direct their conduct by something", "그러나 프라이어가 더없이 절묘하게 짚어 냈듯, 어떤 부류의 사람들은 자신의 행실을 무언가에 따라 다스린다."]], [["Beyond the fix'd and settled rules Of vice and virtue in the schools, Beyond the letter of the law.", "학교에서 가르치는 악덕과 미덕의 정해지고 굳어진 규칙을 넘어, 법의 문구를 넘어선 그 무언가에."]], [["To these it is so far from being sufficient that their defence would acquit them at the Old Bailey, that they are not even contented, though conscience, the severest of all judges, should discharge them.", "이런 이들에게는, 자신의 변명이 올드 베일리 법정에서 무죄를 받아 낼 정도로는 결코 충분하지 않다. 그뿐 아니라 모든 재판관 가운데 가장 엄혹한 양심이 자신을 방면해 준다 해도 만족하지 못한다."], ["Nothing short of the fair and honourable will satisfy the delicacy of their minds; and if any of their actions fall short of this mark, they mope and pine, are as uneasy and restless as a murderer, who is afraid of a ghost, or of the hangman.", "오직 공정하고 떳떳한 것만이 그 섬세한 마음을 흡족하게 할 수 있으니, 만일 그들의 어떤 행위가 이 기준에 조금이라도 못 미치면, 그들은 풀이 죽어 시들시들 앓으며, 유령이나 교수형 집행인을 두려워하는 살인자만큼이나 불안하고 안절부절못한다."]], [["Mrs Miller was one of these.", "밀러 부인이 바로 그런 사람 가운데 하나였다."], ["She could not conceal her uneasiness at this letter; with the contents of which she had no sooner acquainted the company, and given some hints of her distress, than Jones, her good angel, presently relieved her anxiety.", "그녀는 이 편지를 받고 솟아오른 불안을 감추지 못했다. 좌중에게 편지 내용을 알리고 자신의 곤혹스러움을 넌지시 비치자마자, 그녀의 수호천사 같은 존스가 곧바로 그 근심을 덜어 주었다."], ["“As for myself, madam,”", "\"저로 말하자면, 부인,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “my lodging is at your service at a moment's warning; and Mr Nightingale, I am sure, as he cannot yet prepare a house fit to receive his lady, will consent to return to his new lodging, whither Mrs Nightingale will certainly consent to go.”", "\"제 숙소는 한마디 기별만 주시면 언제든 내드리겠습니다. 그리고 나이팅게일 씨도, 아직 부인을 맞이할 만한 집을 마련하지 못했으니, 분명 새 숙소로 돌아가는 데 동의할 것입니다."], ["With which proposal both husband and wife instantly agreed.", "나이팅게일 부인께서도 틀림없이 그곳으로 가는 데 응하실 테고요.\" 이 제안에 부부는 그 자리에서 모두 찬성했다."]], [["The reader will easily believe, that the cheeks of Mrs Miller began again to glow with additional gratitude to Jones; but, perhaps, it may be more difficult to persuade him, that Mr Jones having in his last speech called her daughter Mrs Nightingale (it being the first time that agreeable sound had ever reached her ears), gave the fond mother more satisfaction, and warmed her heart more towards Jones, than his having dissipated her present anxiety.", "밀러 부인의 두 뺨이 존스를 향한 한층 깊어진 고마움으로 다시금 붉게 달아오르기 시작했으리라는 것은 독자도 쉬이 믿을 것이다. 그러나 존스 씨가 방금 한 말에서 그녀의 딸을 \"나이팅게일 부인\"이라 부른 일이(그 듣기 좋은 소리가 그녀의 귀에 닿은 것은 그때가 처음이었다), 눈앞의 근심을 풀어 준 것보다도 이 자애로운 어머니에게 더 큰 만족을 안기고 존스를 향한 그녀의 마음을 더 따뜻하게 데웠다는 사실은, 어쩌면 독자를 설득하기가 한결 더 어려울지도 모르겠다."]], [["The next day was then appointed for the removal of the new-married couple, and of Mr Jones, who was likewise to be provided for in the same house with his friend. And now the serenity of the company was again restored, and they past the day in the utmost chearfulness, all except Jones, who, though he outwardly accompanied the rest in their mirth, felt many a bitter pang on the account of his Sophia, which were not a little heightened by the news of Mr Blifil's coming to town (for he clearly saw the intention of his journey); and what greatly aggravated his concern was, that Mrs Honour, who had promised to inquire after Sophia, and to make her report to him early the next evening, had disappointed him.", "그리하여 신혼부부와 존스 씨가 거처를 옮길 날로 이튿날이 정해졌다. 존스 역시 친구와 같은 집에 머물 수 있도록 마련될 참이었다. 이제 좌중의 평온함이 다시 회복되었고, 그들은 더없이 명랑하게 그 하루를 보냈다. 다만 존스만은 예외였다. 그는 겉으로는 나머지 사람들과 어울려 흥겨워했지만, 소피아를 생각하며 속으로 숱한 쓰라린 아픔을 느꼈다. 그 아픔은 블리필 씨가 런던에 온다는 소식에(그는 그 여행의 의도를 똑똑히 꿰뚫어 보았기에) 적잖이 더해졌다. 게다가 그의 근심을 한층 더 무겁게 한 것은, 소피아의 소식을 알아보고 이튿날 저녁 일찍 보고해 주겠다 약속했던 어너 부인이 그를 실망시켰다는 점이었다."]], [["In the situation that he and his mistress were in at this time, there were scarce any grounds for him to hope that he should hear any good news; yet he was as impatient to see Mrs Honour as if he had expected she would bring him a letter with an assignation in it from Sophia, and bore the disappointment as ill.", "이 무렵 그와 그의 연인이 처한 상황에서는, 그가 무슨 좋은 소식을 들으리라 바랄 만한 근거가 거의 없었다. 그런데도 그는 마치 어너 부인이 소피아가 보낸 밀회 약속이 담긴 편지라도 가져다줄 것처럼 그녀를 애타게 기다렸고, 그 기다림이 어긋난 것을 그만큼 견디기 힘들어했다."], ["Whether this impatience arose from that natural weakness of the human mind, which makes it desirous to know the worst, and renders uncertainty the most intolerable of pains; or whether he still flattered himself with some secret hopes, we will not determine.", "이 조바심이, 인간의 마음에 본디 깃든 그 약점—최악을 알고 싶어 하고 불확실함을 가장 견디기 힘든 고통으로 여기게 만드는—에서 비롯된 것인지, 아니면 그가 여전히 어떤 은밀한 희망으로 스스로를 달래고 있었기 때문인지, 우리는 단정하지 않겠다."], ["But that it might be the last, whoever has loved cannot but know.", "그러나 그것이 후자일 수도 있다는 것은, 사랑해 본 사람이라면 누구나 모를 리 없다."], ["For of all the powers exercised by this passion over our minds, one of the most wonderful is that of supporting hope in the midst of despair.", "이 정념이 우리 마음에 휘두르는 온갖 힘 가운데, 가장 놀라운 것 하나가 바로 절망의 한가운데서도 희망을 떠받치는 힘이기 때문이다."], ["Difficulties, improbabilities, nay, impossibilities, are quite overlooked by it; so that to any man extremely in love, may be applied what Addison says of Caesar,", "곤경도, 가망 없음도, 아니 불가능함조차도 이 정념 앞에서는 깡그리 무시되고 만다. 그리하여 사랑에 흠뻑 빠진 사람에게는, 애디슨이 카이사르를 두고 한 말을 그대로 갖다 붙일 수 있을 것이다."]], [["“The Alps, and Pyrenaeans, sink before him!”", "\"알프스도, 피레네도, 그의 앞에서는 무너져 내린다!\""]], [["Yet it is equally true, that the same passion will sometimes make mountains of molehills, and produce despair in the midst of hope; but these cold fits last not long in good constitutions. Which temper Jones was now in, we leave the reader to guess, having no exact information about it; but this is certain, that he had spent two hours in expectation, when, being unable any longer to conceal his uneasiness, he retired to his room; where his anxiety had almost made him frantick, when the following letter was brought him from Mrs Honour, with which we shall present the reader verbatim et literatim.", "그러나 이와 똑같이 참인 것은, 바로 그 정념이 때로는 두더지가 파 놓은 흙더미를 산으로 부풀려 놓고, 희망의 한복판에서 절망을 빚어내기도 한다는 사실이다. 다만 건강한 기질을 지닌 사람에게는 이런 싸늘한 발작이 오래가지 않는다. 존스가 지금 어느 쪽 기분에 잠겨 있었는지는, 정확한 정보가 없으니 독자의 짐작에 맡기겠다. 그러나 한 가지만은 분명하다. 그는 두 시간을 기다림 속에 보냈고, 마침내 더는 그 불안을 숨길 수 없게 되자 자기 방으로 물러났다. 그곳에서 근심이 그를 거의 미칠 지경으로 몰아갔을 무렵, 어너 부인에게서 다음과 같은 편지가 전해졌다. 우리는 그것을 독자 앞에 글자 그대로, 철자 그대로 옮겨 보이겠다. \"나으리,"]], [["“I shud sartenly haf kaled on you a cordin too mi prommiss haddunt itt bin that hur lashipp prevent mee; for to bee sur, Sir, you nose very well that evere persun must luk furst at ome, and sartenly such anuther offar mite not have ever hapned, so as I shud ave bin justly to blam, had I not excepted of it when her lashipp was so veri kind as to offar to mak mee hur one uman without mi ever askin any such thing, to be sur shee is won of thee best ladis in thee wurld, and pepil who sase to the kontrari must bee veri wiket pepil in thare harts. To bee sur if ever I ave sad any thing of that kine it as bin thru ignorens, and I am hartili sorri for it. I nose your onur to be a genteelman of more onur and onesty, if I ever said ani such thing, to repete it to hurt a pore servant that as alwais add thee gratest respect in thee wurld for ure onur. To be sur won shud kepe wons tung within wons teeth, for no boddi nose what may hapen; and to bee sur if ani boddi ad tolde mee yesterday, that I shud haf bin in so gud a plase to day, I shud not haf beleeved it; for to be sur I never was a dremd of any such thing, nor shud I ever have soft after ani other bodi's plase; but as her lashipp wass so kine of her one a cord too give it mee without askin, to be sur Mrs Etoff herself, nor no other boddi can blam mee for exceptin such a thing when it fals in mi waye. I beg ure Onur not to menshion ani thing of what I haf sad, for I wish ure Onur all thee gud luk in the wurld; and I don't cuestion butt thatt u will haf Madam Sofia in the end; butt ass to miself ure onur nose I kant bee of ani farder sarvis to u in that matar, nou bein under thee cumand off anuther parson, and nott mi one mistress, I begg ure Onur to say nothing of what past, and belive me to be, sir, ure Onur's umble servant to cumand till deth,", "진작에 약속대로 차자 뵈었을 텐데 마님이 못 가게 부뜨신 탓이옵니다. 나으리도 잘 아시다십이, 사람은 누구나 제 앞가림부터 해야 하는 법이고, 또 이런 자리가 다시 올 리 만무하니, 마님이 그처럼 친절하시게도 제가 청하지도 안엇는데 당신 시녀로 삼아 주시겠다 하셨을 때 그걸 마다햇다면 저야말로 마땅이 욕을 먹을 일이엇겟지요. 마님은 참말로 세상에서 손꼽이는 좋은 마님이시고, 그와 반대로 말하는 사람들은 속마음이 아주 고약한 자들임에 틀임없습니다. 만일 제가 그런 말을 한 적이 있다면 그건 다 무지한 탓이엇고, 진심으로 뉘우치고 있사옵니다. 저는 나으리께서 더없는 명예와 정직을 갖추신 신사이심을 아오니, 설사 제가 그런 말을 한 적이 있더라도, 늘 나으리께 세상 무엇보다 큰 존경을 품어 온 가엾은 하인을 해치려고 그 말을 옴기실 분이 아니실 줄로 믿습니다. 참말로 사람은 제 혀를 이빨 안에 단단이 가둬 두어야 하는 법이옵니다. 앞으로 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겟습니까. 어제 누가 제게, 네가 내일 이처럼 좋은 자리에 있게 되리라 일러 주었다면 저는 믿지 안엇을 겁니다. 참말로 그런 일은 꿈에도 생각지 못햇고, 남의 자리를 노린 적도 결코 없었으니까요. 다만 마님이 그처럼 친절하시게 청하지도 안엇는데 손수 그 자리를 주시겟다 하니, 참말로 이토프 부인이든 그 누구든, 굴러든 복을 제가 받앗다고 저를 탓할 수는 없을 것이옵니다. 제가 한 말은 부디 한마디도 입에 올리지 마시기를 나으리께 비옵니다. 저는 나으리께 세상 모든 행운이 있기를 바라오며, 끝내 소피아 아씨를 차지하실 것을 조금도 의심치 안사옵니다. 다만 저로 말하자면, 나으리도 아시다십이 이제 다른 분의 명을 받는 처지이고 제 본 마님을 모시는 몸이 아니니, 그 일에 더는 도움이 되어 드릴 수가 없게 되엇습니다. 그러니 부디 지난 일은 아무 말씀도 마시기를 비오며, 죽는 날까지 나으리를 받드는 미천한 종임을 믿어 주시옵소서."]], [["Various were the conjectures which Jones entertained on this step of Lady Bellaston; who, in reality, had little farther design than to secure within her own house the repository of a secret, which she chose should make no farther progress than it had made already; but mostly, she desired to keep it from the ears of Sophia; for though that young lady was almost the only one who would never have repeated it again, her ladyship could not persuade herself of this; since, as she now hated poor Sophia with most implacable hatred, she conceived a reciprocal hatred to herself to be lodged in the tender breast of our heroine, where no such passion had ever yet found an entrance.", "어너 블랙모어 올림.\" 존스는 벨라스턴 부인의 이 처사를 두고 갖가지 추측을 떠올렸다. 그러나 사실 그녀의 속셈은, 이미 진행된 것 이상으로는 결코 더 퍼지지 않기를 바라는 어떤 비밀의 저장고를 제 집 안에 단단히 가둬 두려는 것에 지나지 않았다. 무엇보다 그녀는 그 비밀이 소피아의 귀에 들어가지 않기를 바랐다. 그 젊은 아씨야말로 그 일을 결코 두 번 다시 입에 올리지 않을 거의 유일한 사람이었건만, 부인은 도무지 그렇게 믿을 수가 없었다. 이제 가엾은 소피아를 더없이 풀 길 없는 증오로 미워하게 된 터라, 우리 여주인공의 그 보드라운 가슴속에도 자신을 향한 똑같은 증오가 깃들어 있으리라 짐작했던 것이다. 정작 그곳에는 그런 정념이 단 한 번도 발을 들인 적이 없었는데도 말이다."]], [["While Jones was terrifying himself with the apprehension of a thousand dreadful machinations, and deep political designs, which he imagined to be at the bottom of the promotion of Honour, Fortune, who hitherto seems to have been an utter enemy to his match with Sophia, tried a new method to put a final end to it, by throwing a temptation in his way, which in his present desperate situation it seemed unlikely he should be able to resist.", "존스가 어너의 출세 뒤에 깔려 있으리라 상상한 천 가지 무시무시한 음모와 깊은 책략을 떠올리며 스스로를 공포에 떨게 하고 있던 그때, 지금껏 그와 소피아의 혼인에 더할 나위 없는 적이었던 것만 같은 운명의 여신은, 그 혼인에 마지막 종지부를 찍으려 새로운 수를 시도했다. 곧, 그의 앞길에 하나의 유혹을 던져 놓은 것이다. 지금 그가 처한 그 절망적인 처지로 보아, 도저히 뿌리치지 못할 것만 같은 유혹을."]], [["Containing curious, but not unprecedented matter.", "기이하지만 전례가 없지는 않은 일에 관하여."]], [["There was a lady, one Mrs Hunt, who had often seen Jones at the house where he lodged, being intimately acquainted with the women there, and indeed a very great friend to Mrs Miller.", "헌트 부인이라 불리는 한 부인이 있었는데, 그녀는 존스가 묵고 있는 집에서 그를 여러 차례 본 적이 있었다. 그 집 여자들과 가깝게 지냈으며, 실로 밀러 부인과는 둘도 없는 벗이었기 때문이다."], ["Her age was about thirty, for she owned six-and-twenty; her face and person very good, only inclining a little too much to be fat.", "그녀의 나이는 서른쯤이었으니, 본인은 스물여섯이라 주장했다. 얼굴과 몸매는 매우 훌륭했으나, 다만 살집이 조금 지나치게 붙는 쪽으로 기우는 것이 흠이라면 흠이었다."], ["She had been married young by her relations to an old Turkey merchant, who, having got a great fortune, had left off trade.", "그녀는 젊은 나이에 친척들의 손에 이끌려 나이 든 터키 무역상에게 시집갔는데, 그는 큰 재산을 모은 뒤 장사에서 손을 뗀 사람이었다."], ["With him she lived without reproach, but not without pain, in a state of great self-denial, for about twelve years; and her virtue was rewarded by his dying and leaving her very rich.", "그녀는 그와 함께 흠잡힐 일 없이, 그러나 고통이 없지는 않게, 큰 인내를 견디는 처지로 십이 년쯤을 살았다. 그리고 그녀의 정숙함은 그가 죽으면서 그녀에게 막대한 재산을 남겨 준 것으로 보답받았다."], ["The first year of her widowhood was just at an end, and she had past it in a good deal of retirement, seeing only a few particular friends, and dividing her time between her devotions and novels, of which she was always extremely fond.", "과부살이의 첫해가 막 끝나려는 참이었는데, 그녀는 그 한 해를 상당히 은둔하며 보냈다. 가까운 벗 몇 사람만 만나고, 시간을 신앙 생활과 소설에 나누어 썼는데, 그녀는 늘 소설을 무척이나 좋아했다."], ["Very good health, a very warm constitution, and a good deal of religion, made it absolutely necessary for her to marry again; and she resolved to please herself in her second husband, as she had done her friends in the first.", "아주 좋은 건강과 매우 뜨거운 기질, 그리고 적지 않은 신앙심이 그녀로 하여금 다시 결혼하지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 그래서 그녀는 첫 번째 남편으로 친척들의 마음에 들었던 것처럼, 두 번째 남편으로는 자신의 마음에 드는 사람을 고르기로 결심했다."], ["From her the following billet was brought to Jones:--", "그녀로부터 다음의 짧은 편지가 존스에게 전해졌다. “선생님께,"]], [["“From the first day I saw you, I doubt my eyes have told you too plainly that you were not indifferent to me; but neither my tongue nor my hand should have ever avowed it, had not the ladies of the family where you are lodged given me such a character of you, and told me such proofs of your virtue and goodness, as convince me you are not only the most agreeable, but the most worthy of men.", "“당신을 처음 본 그날부터, 제 눈이 당신께 너무도 또렷이 말해 버린 것은 아닐까 염려됩니다. 제가 당신께 무심하지 않다는 것을 말입니다."], ["I have also the satisfaction to hear from them, that neither my person, understanding, or character, are disagreeable to you.", "그러나 만일 당신이 묵고 계신 집의 부인들이 제게 당신에 관해 그토록 좋은 평을 들려주지 않았더라면, 그리고 당신이 가장 호감 가는 사람일 뿐 아니라 가장 그럴 자격이 있는 사람임을 제게 확신시켜 줄 만큼 당신의 덕과 선함을 증명하는 이야기를 들려주지 않았더라면, 제 혀도 제 손도 결코 그것을 고백하지는 않았을 것입니다. 또한 저는 그분들로부터, 제 사람됨도, 식견도, 인품도 당신께 거슬리지 않는다는 말을 듣는 기쁨을 누렸습니다."], ["I have a fortune sufficient to make us both happy, but which cannot make me so without you.", "제게는 우리 두 사람을 다 행복하게 해 줄 만한 재산이 있으나, 그것도 당신 없이는 저를 행복하게 만들지 못합니다."], ["In thus disposing of myself, I know I shall incur the censure of the world; but if I did not love you more than I fear the world, I should not be worthy of you.", "이렇게 제 한 몸을 내맡기는 일로 세상의 비난을 사게 되리라는 것을 저도 압니다. 그러나 만일 제가 세상을 두려워하는 것보다 당신을 더 사랑하지 않는다면, 저는 당신께 어울리는 사람이 못 될 것입니다."], ["One only difficulty stops me: I am informed you are engaged in a commerce of gallantry with a woman of fashion.", "다만 한 가지 어려움이 저를 멈춰 세웁니다. 듣자 하니 당신은 어느 지체 높은 여인과 연애 관계에 얽혀 있다고 하더군요."], ["If you think it worth while to sacrifice that to the possession of me, I am yours; if not, forget my weakness, and let this remain an eternal secret between you and", "만일 당신이 저를 차지하기 위해 그것을 희생할 만하다고 여기신다면, 저는 당신의 것입니다. 그렇지 않다면, 저의 약한 마음을 잊으시고, 이 일이 당신과 저 사이에 영원한 비밀로 남게 해 주십시오."]], [["At the reading of this, Jones was put into a violent flutter.", "“아라벨라 헌트 올림.” 이 편지를 읽고 존스는 격한 동요에 휩싸였다."], ["His fortune was then at a very low ebb, the source being stopt from which hitherto he had been supplied.", "그의 형편은 그때 몹시 바닥나 있었으니, 지금껏 그를 받쳐 주던 돈줄이 끊겨 버린 까닭이었다."], ["Of all he had received from Lady Bellaston, not above five guineas remained; and that very morning he had been dunned by a tradesman for twice that sum.", "벨라스턴 부인에게서 받은 것 가운데 다섯 기니 남짓밖에 남지 않았는데, 바로 그날 아침에 한 상인이 그 두 배에 해당하는 금액을 갚으라며 그를 졸라 댔다."], ["His honourable mistress was in the hands of her father, and he had scarce any hopes ever to get her out of them again.", "그가 명예롭게 여기는 연인은 그녀의 아버지 손아귀에 들어가 있었고, 그녀를 다시 거기서 빼낼 가망은 거의 없었다."], ["To be subsisted at her expense, from that little fortune she had independent of her father, went much against the delicacy both of his pride and his love.", "그녀가 아버지와 별개로 지닌 그 작은 재산으로 그녀의 비용을 빌려 살아간다는 것은, 그의 자존심과 사랑의 섬세함 양쪽 모두에 크게 거슬리는 일이었다."], ["This lady's fortune would have been exceeding convenient to him, and he could have no objection to her in any respect.", "이 부인의 재산은 그에게 더없이 요긴했을 터였고, 그녀에 대해서는 어느 면으로 보아도 흠잡을 데가 없었다. 도리어 그는 소피아를 제외하면 어떤 여자 못지않게 그녀를 좋게 여겼다."], ["On the contrary, he liked her as well as he did any woman except Sophia.", "그러나 소피아를 저버리고 다른 여자와 결혼한다는 것, 그것은 불가능했다."], ["But to abandon Sophia, and marry another, that was impossible; he could not think of it upon any account, Yet why should he not, since it was plain she could not be his?", "그는 어떤 이유로도 그것을 떠올릴 수가 없었다. 하지만 그녀가 자신의 사람이 될 수 없음이 분명한데, 어찌하여 그래서는 안 된단 말인가?"], ["Would it not be kinder to her, than to continue her longer engaged in a hopeless passion for him?", "그녀를 가망 없는 사랑에 더 오래 묶어 두는 것보다는, 그렇게 하는 편이 오히려 그녀를 위하는 길이 아닐까?"], ["Ought he not to do so in friendship to her?", "그녀를 향한 우정으로 보더라도 마땅히 그렇게 해야 하는 것이 아닐까?"], ["This notion prevailed some moments, and he had almost determined to be false to her from a high point of honour: but that refinement was not able to stand very long against the voice of nature, which cried in his heart that such friendship was treason to love.", "이런 생각이 한동안 그를 사로잡았고, 그는 드높은 명예심에서 그녀에게 거짓된 사람이 되기로 거의 마음을 굳힐 뻔했다. 그러나 그 고상한 논리는, 그러한 우정이야말로 사랑에 대한 배신이라고 그의 가슴속에서 외치는 본성의 목소리 앞에서 그리 오래 버티지 못했다."], ["At last he called for pen, ink, and paper, and writ as follows to Mrs Hunt:--", "마침내 그는 펜과 잉크와 종이를 가져오라 하여, 헌트 부인에게 다음과 같이 써 보냈다."]], [["“It would be but a poor return to the favour you have done me to sacrifice any gallantry to the possession of you, and I would certainly do it, though I were not disengaged, as at present I am, from any affair of that kind.", "“부인께,\n“당신을 차지하기 위해 어떤 연애를 희생한다 한들, 그것은 당신이 제게 베푸신 호의에 대한 변변찮은 보답에 지나지 않을 것입니다. 그리고 지금의 저처럼 그런 일에서 자유롭지 않은 처지였더라도, 저는 분명 그렇게 했을 것입니다."], ["But I should not be the honest man you think me, if I did not tell you that my affections are engaged to another, who is a woman of virtue, and one that I never can leave, though it is probable I shall never possess her.", "하지만 만일 제 마음이 다른 이에게 매여 있다는 사실을 당신께 말씀드리지 않는다면, 저는 당신이 여기시는 그 정직한 사람이 못 될 것입니다. 그분은 덕 있는 여인이며, 비록 제가 그녀를 영영 차지하지 못할 가능성이 크다 해도, 결코 떠날 수 없는 사람입니다."], ["God forbid that, in return of your kindness to me, I should do you such an injury as to give you my hand when I cannot give my heart.", "당신의 친절에 보답한답시고, 제 마음을 드릴 수 없으면서 제 손을 내드려 당신께 그런 해를 끼치는 일은 결단코 없어야 할 것입니다. 아닙니다."], ["No; I had much rather starve than be guilty of that.", "그런 죄를 짓느니 저는 차라리 굶어 죽는 편을 택하겠습니다."], ["Even though my mistress were married to another, I would not marry you unless my heart had entirely effaced all impressions of her.", "설령 제 연인이 다른 사람과 결혼한다 할지라도, 제 마음에서 그녀의 모든 흔적이 온전히 지워지지 않는 한 저는 당신과 결혼하지 않을 것입니다. 부디 믿어 주십시오."], ["Be assured that your secret was not more safe in your own breast, than in that of your most obliged, and grateful humble servant,", "당신의 비밀은 당신 자신의 가슴속에서보다, 당신께 더없는 은혜를 입고 깊이 감사하는 미천한 종의 가슴속에서 결코 덜 안전하지 않으리라는 것을요."]], [["When our heroe had finished and sent this letter, he went to his scrutore, took out Miss Western's muff, kissed it several times, and then strutted some turns about his room, with more satisfaction of mind than ever any Irishman felt in carrying off a fortune of fifty thousand pounds.", "“T. 존스 올림.” 우리의 주인공은 이 편지를 다 쓰고 부친 뒤, 책상으로 가서 웨스턴 양의 토시를 꺼내어 여러 번 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 방 안을 몇 바퀴 거닐며 의기양양해했는데, 그 마음의 흡족함은 어떤 아일랜드 사람이 오만 파운드의 재산을 거머쥐고 달아날 때 느꼈을 것보다도 더한 것이었다."]], [["A discovery made by Partridge.", "파트리지가 한 발견."]], [["While Jones was exulting in the consciousness of his integrity, Partridge came capering into the room, as was his custom when he brought, or fancied he brought, any good tidings.", "존스가 자신의 결백을 의식하며 흐뭇해하고 있는 동안, 파트리지가 깡충거리며 방으로 들어왔다. 좋은 소식을 가져오거나, 가져왔다고 스스로 믿을 때면 늘 그러던 버릇이었다."], ["He had been despatched that morning by his master, with orders to endeavour, by the servants of Lady Bellaston, or by any other means, to discover whither Sophia had been conveyed; and he now returned, and with a joyful countenance told our heroe that he had found the lost bird.", "그날 아침 그는 주인의 명을 받고, 벨라스턴 부인의 하인들을 통해서든 다른 어떤 방법으로든 소피아가 어디로 옮겨졌는지 알아내려 길을 나섰던 터였다. 그리고 이제 돌아와, 기쁨에 찬 얼굴로 우리의 주인공에게 잃어버린 새를 찾았노라고 일러주었다."], ["“I have seen, sir,”", "\"나리, 제가 사냥터지기인 블랙 조지를 보았습니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “Black George, the gamekeeper, who is one of the servants whom the squire hath brought with him to town.", "\"그자는 지주 어른께서 함께 도시로 데려오신 하인들 가운데 하나입니다요. 몇 해째 보지 못했는데도 단번에 알아보았지요."], ["I knew him presently, though I have not seen him these several years; but you know, sir, he is a very remarkable man, or, to use a purer phrase, he hath a most remarkable beard, the largest and blackest I ever saw.", "하지만 나리도 아시다시피 그자는 아주 눈에 띄는 사람입니다요. 아니, 더 고상하게 말하자면, 더없이 눈에 띄는 수염을 가졌지요."], ["It was some time, however, before Black George could recollect me.”", "제가 여태 본 가운데 가장 크고 가장 검은 수염입니다요. 다만 블랙 조지가 저를 알아보기까지는 한참이 걸렸습니다요.\""], ["“Well, but what is your good news?”", "\"그래, 그런데 자네 좋은 소식이란 게 뭔가?\" 존스가 소리쳤다."], ["cries Jones; “what do you know of my Sophia?”", "\"내 소피아에 대해 뭘 알아냈느냔 말이야?\" \"곧 아시게 됩니다요, 나리.\""], ["“You shall know presently, sir,”", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"최대한 빨리 본론으로 가는 중입니다요."], ["answered Partridge, “I am coming to it as fast as I can.", "나리께서는 어찌나 성급하신지, 명령법에 닿기도 전에 부정법부터 오시려 하시는군요. 말씀드렸다시피, 나리, 그자가 제 얼굴을 알아보기까지는 한참이 걸렸지요.\"—\"자네 얼굴 따위는 집어치우게!\""], ["You are so impatient, sir, you would come at the infinitive mood before you can get to the imperative.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"내 소피아는 어찌 됐단 말인가?\""], ["As I was saying, sir, it was some time before he recollected my face.”--“Confound your face!”", "\"아니, 나리.\" 파트리지가 대답했다."], ["cries Jones, “what of my Sophia?” “Nay, sir,”", "\"소피아 아씨에 대해서는 지금 말씀드리려는 것 말고는 아무것도 모릅니다요."], ["answered Partridge, “I know nothing more of Madam Sophia than what I am going to tell you; and I should have told you all before this if you had not interrupted me; but if you look so angry at me you will frighten all of it out of my head, or, to use a purer phrase, out of my memory.", "나리께서 말을 끊지만 않으셨어도 진작 다 말씀드렸을 겁니다요. 그런데 그렇게 화난 얼굴로 저를 노려보시면 죄다 제 머리에서, 아니 더 고상하게 말하자면 제 기억에서 놀라 달아나 버린답니다요."], ["I never saw you look so angry since the day we left Upton, which I shall remember if I was to live a thousand years.”--“Well, pray go on your own way,”", "업튼을 떠나던 날 이후로 나리께서 이토록 화난 얼굴을 하신 건 처음 봅니다요. 그날을 저는 천 년을 산다 해도 잊지 못할 겝니다요.\"—\"그래, 그럼 자네 좋을 대로 계속하게.\""], ["said Jones: “you are resolved to make me mad I find.”", "존스가 말했다. \"자네는 기어이 나를 미치게 만들 작정인 모양이군.\""], ["“Not for the world,”", "\"천만의 말씀입니다요.\" 파트리지가 대답했다."], ["answered Partridge, “I have suffered enough for that already; which, as I said, I shall bear in my remembrance the longest day I have to live.”", "\"그 일로는 이미 충분히 혼났는걸요. 말씀드렸듯이 살아 있는 동안 가장 긴 날까지도 두고두고 기억할 일이지요.\""], ["“Well, but Black George?”", "\"그래, 그건 그렇고 블랙 조지는?\""], ["cries Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["“Well, sir, as I was saying, it was a long time before he could recollect me; for, indeed, I am very much altered since I saw him.", "\"예, 나리, 말씀드렸다시피 그자가 저를 알아보기까지 한참이 걸렸지요. 사실 그자를 본 이후로 저도 무척 변했으니까요."], ["Non sum qualis eram.", "옛날의 제가 아니지요. 세상살이에서 갖은 풍파를 겪었는데, 슬픔만큼 사람을 변하게 하는 건 없습니다요."], ["I have had troubles in the world, and nothing alters a man so much as grief.", "슬픔이 하룻밤 새 사람 머리칼 빛깔을 바꿔놓는다는 말도 들었습니다요. 어쨌든 끝내 그자가 저를 알아본 건 확실합니다요."], ["I have heard it will change the colour of a man's hair in a night.", "저희 둘은 동갑이고 같은 자선학교를 다녔거든요. 조지는 지독한 둔재였지만, 뭐 그건 상관없지요."], ["However, at last, know me he did, that's sure enough; for we are both of an age, and were at the same charity school.", "사람이 다 배운 만큼 세상에서 잘되는 건 아니니까요. 저야말로 그리 말할 만한 처지지요."], ["George was a great dunce, but no matter for that; all men do not thrive in the world according to their learning.", "하지만 천 년 뒤면 다 매한가지일 겝니다요. 그래, 나리, 제가 어디까지 말했더라?—아, 그렇지—저희가 서로 알아보자마자, 손을 몇 번이고 정답게 맞잡고 나서 술집에 가 한잔하기로 했지요."], ["I am sure I have reason to say so; but it will be all one a thousand years hence.", "운 좋게도 그 맥주는 제가 도시에 온 이래 맛본 것 중에 으뜸가는 것이었답니다요. 자, 나리, 이제 본론으로 들어갑니다요."], ["Well, sir, where was I?--O--well, we no sooner knew each other, than, after many hearty shakes by the hand, we agreed to go to an alehouse and take a pot, and by good luck the beer was some of the best I have met with since I have been in town.", "제가 나리 성함을 입에 올리고, 나리와 제가 함께 도시로 와 그 뒤로 줄곧 같이 지내왔다고 말하자마자, 그자는 한 잔 더 가져오라 하고는 나리의 건강을 위해 마시겠다며 맹세했지요. 어찌나 진심으로 나리의 건강을 빌며 마시던지, 세상에 아직 그만한 고마움이 남아 있구나 싶어 저는 더없이 기뻤습니다요."], ["Now, sir, I am coming to the point; for no sooner did I name you, and told him that you and I came to town together, and had lived together ever since, than he called for another pot, and swore he would drink to your health; and indeed he drank your health so heartily that I was overjoyed to see there was so much gratitude left in the world; and after we had emptied that pot I said I would buy my pot too, and so we drank another to your health; and then I made haste home to tell you the news.”", "그 잔을 비우고 나서 저도 한잔 사겠다고 해서, 또 나리의 건강을 위해 한 잔을 마셨지요. 그러고는 이 소식을 전하려 부랴부랴 집으로 돌아온 겝니다요.\""]], [["“What news?”", "\"무슨 소식 말인가?\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["cries Jones, “you have not mentioned a word of my Sophia!”", "\"자네는 내 소피아에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았네!\" \"이런 맙소사!"], ["“Bless me!", "그걸 잊을 뻔했군요. 사실 저희는 젊은 웨스턴 아씨에 대해서 꽤 많은 이야기를 나누었고, 조지가 죄다 일러주었습니다요."], ["I had like to have forgot that.", "블리필 도련님이 아씨와 혼인하러 도시로 올라온다는 겝니다요. 그럼 서둘러야겠구먼, 안 그러면 오기도 전에 누군가 아씨를 채갈 테니, 하고 제가 말했지요."], ["Indeed, we mentioned a great deal about young Madam Western, and George told me all; that Mr Blifil is coming to town in order to be married to her.", "그러고는, 시그림 씨, 누군가 아씨를 차지하지 못한다면 그야말로 천만 번 안타까운 일일세, 하고 말했습죠. 그분이야말로 세상 어떤 여인보다 아씨를 사랑하시니까요."], ["He had best make haste then, says I, or somebody will have her before he comes; and, indeed, says I, Mr Seagrim, it is a thousand pities somebody should not have her; for he certainly loves her above all the women in the world.", "저는 나리도 아씨도 알아주셨으면 합니다요. 그분이 아씨를 따르는 건 결코 재산 때문이 아니라는 걸 말입니다요."], ["I would have both you and she know, that it is not for her fortune he follows her; for I can assure you, as to matter of that, there is another lady, one of much greater quality and fortune than she can pretend to, who is so fond of somebody that she comes after him day and night.”", "그 점에 관해서라면 제가 장담할 수 있습죠. 아씨가 감히 견줄 수 없을 만큼 훨씬 높은 신분과 재산을 지닌 또 다른 부인이 계신데, 그 부인이 어떤 분에게 어찌나 푹 빠져 있는지 밤낮으로 그분을 쫓아다닐 정도이니 말입니다요.\""]], [["Here Jones fell into a passion with Partridge, for having, as he said, betrayed him; but the poor fellow answered, he had mentioned no name: “Besides, sir,” said he, “I can assure you George is sincerely your friend, and wished Mr Blifil at the devil more than once; nay, he said he would do anything in his power upon earth to serve you; and so I am convinced he will. Betray you, indeed! why, I question whether you have a better friend than George upon earth, except myself, or one that would go farther to serve you.”", "여기서 존스는 파트리지가 자기를 배신했다며 그에게 발끈 화를 냈다. 그러나 가엾은 친구는 자기는 이름을 입에 올린 적이 없다고 대답했다. \"게다가 나리.\" 그가 말했다. \"장담컨대 조지는 진심으로 나리의 벗입니다요. 한두 번이 아니라 블리필 도련님을 악마에게나 가버리라고 빌었는걸요. 아니, 나리를 위해서라면 이 땅에서 제 힘닿는 무슨 일이든 하겠다고까지 했습니다요. 그러니 저는 그자가 정말 그러리라 믿습니다요. 배신이라니, 당치도 않습니다요! 저를 빼면 이 땅에 조지보다 나은 벗이 나리께 또 있을까 싶고, 나리를 위해 그자보다 더 멀리 나설 사람이 또 있을까 싶은걸요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["says Jones, a little pacified, “you say this fellow, who, I believe, indeed, is enough inclined to be my friend, lives in the same house with Sophia?”", "다소 누그러진 존스가 말했다. \"자네 말로는, 정말이지 내 벗이 되어줄 마음이 충분한 듯한 그 친구가, 소피아와 같은 집에 산다는 게로군?\""]], [["“In the same house!” answered Partridge; “why, sir, he is one of the servants of the family, and very well drest I promise you he is; if it was not for his black beard you would hardly know him.”", "\"같은 집이라니요!\" 파트리지가 대답했다. \"아니 나리, 그자는 그 댁 하인들 가운데 하나입니다요. 옷차림이 아주 번듯하다고 장담합니다요. 검은 수염만 아니라면 나리도 알아보기 어려우실 겁니다요.\""]], [["“One service then at least he may do me,”", "\"그렇다면 적어도 한 가지 일은 나를 위해 해줄 수 있겠군.\""], ["says Jones: “sure he can certainly convey a letter to my Sophia.”", "존스가 말했다. \"분명 그 친구라면 내 소피아에게 편지 한 통쯤은 틀림없이 전해줄 수 있을 테지.\""]], [["“You have hit the nail ad unguem”", "\"바로 정곡을 짚으셨습니다요, 한 치도 어김없이요!\""], ["cries Partridge; “how came I not to think of it?", "파트리지가 외쳤다. \"어찌 제가 그 생각을 못 했을까요?"], ["I will engage he shall do it upon the very first mentioning.”", "제가 한 번만 말을 꺼내면 그자가 그 일을 해내리라 장담합니다요.\""]], [["“Well, then,” said Jones, “do you leave me at present, and I will write a letter, which you shall deliver to him to-morrow morning; for I suppose you know where to find him.”", "\"그럼 됐네.\" 존스가 말했다. \"지금은 자네는 나를 좀 혼자 두게. 내가 편지를 한 통 쓸 테니, 내일 아침 그 친구에게 전해주게. 자네는 그를 어디서 찾을지 알 테지.\""]], [["“O yes, sir,”", "\"아, 그럼요, 나리.\""], ["answered Partridge, “I shall certainly find him again; there is no fear of that.", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"다시 찾고말고요."], ["The liquor is too good for him to stay away long.", "그 점은 염려 마십시오. 술맛이 어찌나 좋은지 그자가 오래 발길을 끊을 리가 없습니다요."], ["I make no doubt but he will be there every day he stays in town.”", "그자가 도시에 머무는 동안에는 날마다 거기 나타나리라는 데 의심이 없습니다요.\""]], [["“So you don't know the street then where my Sophia is lodged?”", "\"그러니까 자네는 내 소피아가 묵고 있는 거리도 모른단 말이로군?\""], ["cries Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."]], [["“Indeed, sir, I do,”", "\"천만에요, 나리, 압니다요.\""], ["says Partridge.", "파트리지가 말했다."]], [["“What is the name of the street?”", "\"그 거리 이름이 뭔가?\""], ["cries Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."]], [["“The name, sir?", "\"이름 말입니까요, 나리?"], ["why, here, sir, just by,”", "아니, 여기서, 나리, 바로 코앞입니다요.\""], ["answered Partridge, “not above a street or two off.", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"한두 골목 떨어졌을까 한 곳이지요."], ["I don't, indeed, know the very name; for, as he never told me, if I had asked, you know, it might have put some suspicion into his head.", "사실 정확한 이름은 모릅니다요. 그자가 일러주지도 않았고, 제가 물었더라면, 아시다시피, 그자 머릿속에 의심을 심어줄 수도 있었을 테니까요."], ["No, no, sir, let me alone for that.", "아니지요, 아닙니다요, 나리, 그건 제게 맡겨두십시오."], ["I am too cunning for that, I promise you.”", "그런 일에는 제가 너무 약아빠져서요, 장담합니다요.\""]], [["“Thou art most wonderfully cunning, indeed,”", "\"자네는 정말이지 놀랍도록 약아빠진 사람일세.\""], ["replied Jones; “however, I will write to my charmer, since I believe you will be cunning enough to find him to-morrow at the alehouse.”", "존스가 받아쳤다. \"어쨌든 내일 술집에서 그를 찾아낼 만큼은 자네가 영악하리라 믿으니, 나는 내 연인에게 편지나 쓰겠네.\""]], [["And now, having dismissed the sagacious Partridge, Mr Jones sat himself down to write, in which employment we shall leave him for a time.", "이제 영민한 파트리지를 내보낸 뒤, 존스 씨는 자리에 앉아 편지를 쓰기 시작했다. 그가 그 일에 매달려 있도록 잠시 그를 남겨두기로 하자."], ["And here we put an end to the fifteenth book.", "그리고 여기서 우리는 제15권을 끝맺는다."]], [["Of prologues.", "닷새 동안의 일을 담은 권. 1장."]], [["I have heard of a dramatic writer who used to say, he would rather write a play than a prologue; in like manner, I think, I can with less pains write one of the books of this history than the prefatory chapter to each of them.", "머리말에 관하여. 어느 극작가가 이런 말을 하곤 했다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있다. 자신은 머리말 하나를 쓰느니 차라리 희곡 한 편을 쓰는 편이 낫겠다고 말이다. 나 역시 그와 비슷하게, 이 이야기의 본문 한 권을 쓰는 것이 각 권 앞에 붙이는 서두의 장 하나를 쓰는 것보다 수고가 덜하다고 느낀다."]], [["To say the truth, I believe many a hearty curse hath been devoted on the head of that author who first instituted the method of prefixing to his play that portion of matter which is called the prologue; and which at first was part of the piece itself, but of latter years hath had usually so little connexion with the drama before which it stands, that the prologue to one play might as well serve for any other.", "사실대로 말하자면, 처음으로 희곡 앞에 머리말이라 불리는 부분을 붙이는 방식을 만들어낸 그 작가의 머리 위로 진심 어린 저주가 적잖이 쏟아졌으리라 믿는다. 머리말이란 본래 작품 그 자체의 일부였으나, 근래에 이르러서는 그 앞에 놓인 극과 좀처럼 연결되는 데가 없어져서, 한 희곡의 머리말이 다른 어떤 희곡에 붙어도 무방할 지경이 되었다."], ["Those indeed of more modern date, seem all to be written on the same three topics, viz., an abuse of the taste of the town, a condemnation of all contemporary authors, and an eulogium on the performance just about to be represented.", "실로 더 근래에 쓰인 머리말들은 하나같이 똑같은 세 가지 주제 위에 쓰인 듯하다. 곧 도시 사람들의 취향에 대한 비난, 동시대 모든 작가에 대한 규탄, 그리고 막 상연될 작품에 대한 찬사가 그것이다."], ["The sentiments in all these are very little varied, nor is it possible they should; and indeed I have often wondered at the great invention of authors, who have been capable of finding such various phrases to express the same thing.", "이 모든 것에 담긴 생각은 거의 변하지 않으며, 또 변할 수도 없다. 같은 것을 표현하려고 그토록 다양한 어구를 찾아낼 줄 아는 작가들의 대단한 창의력에는 나도 종종 놀라곤 했다."]], [["In like manner I apprehend, some future historian (if any one shall do me the honour of imitating my manner) will, after much scratching his pate, bestow some good wishes on my memory, for having first established these several initial chapters; most of which, like modern prologues, may as properly be prefixed to any other book in this history as to that which they introduce, or indeed to any other history as to this.", "이와 마찬가지로 나는 이렇게 짐작한다. 미래의 어느 역사가가 (혹 누군가 내 방식을 본떠 주는 영예를 베푼다면) 한참 머리를 긁적인 뒤, 이러한 여러 서두의 장을 처음으로 자리 잡게 한 나의 기억에 대고 몇 마디 좋은 축원을 보내리라고 말이다. 그 대부분은 근래의 머리말들과 같아서, 이 이야기의 다른 어느 권에 붙여도, 아니 다른 어떤 이야기에 붙여도, 정작 자기가 소개하는 권에 붙은 것만큼이나 잘 어울릴 것이다."]], [["But however authors may suffer by either of these inventions, the reader will find sufficient emolument in the one as the spectator hath long found in the other.", "하지만 작가들이 이 두 가지 발명 중 어느 쪽으로든 고생을 겪는다 해도, 관객이 오래전부터 머리말에서 충분한 이득을 누려 왔듯이, 독자도 이런 서두의 장에서 넉넉한 이득을 발견하게 될 것이다."]], [["First, it is well known that the prologue serves the critic for an opportunity to try his faculty of hissing, and to tune his cat-call to the best advantage; by which means, I have known those musical instruments so well prepared, that they have been able to play in full concert at the first rising of the curtain.", "첫째로, 머리말이 비평가에게 야유하는 솜씨를 시험하고 자기가 쓸 호각을 가장 유리하게 조율할 기회를 제공한다는 것은 익히 알려진 사실이다. 그 덕분에 나는 그런 악기들이 어찌나 잘 준비되어 있던지, 막이 처음 오르는 그 순간에 곧장 합주를 시작할 수 있는 광경까지 본 적이 있다."]], [["The same advantages may be drawn from these chapters, in which the critic will be always sure of meeting with something that may serve as a whetstone to his noble spirit; so that he may fall with a more hungry appetite for censure on the history itself.", "이런 서두의 장들에서도 똑같은 이점을 끌어낼 수 있다. 비평가는 여기서 자신의 고귀한 기개를 갈아 세울 숫돌이 되어 줄 무언가를 어김없이 만나게 될 테고, 그리하여 더 굶주린 식욕으로 이야기 자체를 향해 비난을 퍼부으며 달려들 수 있을 것이다."], ["And here his sagacity must make it needless to observe how artfully these chapters are calculated for that excellent purpose; for in these we have always taken care to intersperse somewhat of the sour or acid kind, in order to sharpen and stimulate the said spirit of criticism.", "게다가 그의 빈틈없는 통찰이 있으니, 이 장들이 그 훌륭한 목적을 위해 얼마나 교묘하게 짜였는지를 굳이 짚어 줄 필요도 없을 것이다. 왜냐하면 여기에는 그 비평의 기개를 날카롭게 벼리고 부추기려고, 우리가 늘 시큼하거나 신맛 나는 무언가를 군데군데 섞어 두었기 때문이다."]], [["Again, the indolent reader, as well as spectator, finds great advantage from both these; for, as they are not obliged either to see the one or read the others, and both the play and the book are thus protracted, by the former they have a quarter of an hour longer allowed them to sit at dinner, and by the latter they have the advantage of beginning to read at the fourth or fifth page instead of the first, a matter by no means of trivial consequence to persons who read books with no other view than to say they have read them, a more general motive to reading than is commonly imagined; and from which not only law books, and good books, but the pages of Homer and Virgil, of Swift and Cervantes, have been often turned over.", "또한 게으른 독자도 게으른 관객 못지않게 이 둘에서 큰 이득을 본다. 어느 쪽이든 머리말을 볼 의무도, 서두의 장을 읽을 의무도 없으니 말이다. 더구나 이렇게 해서 희곡도 책도 분량이 늘어난 덕분에, 관객은 머리말 덕에 저녁 식탁에 십오 분쯤 더 앉아 있을 여유를 얻고, 독자는 서두의 장 덕에 첫 쪽이 아니라 넷째나 다섯째 쪽에서부터 읽기 시작하는 이점을 누린다. 이것은 그저 읽었다고 말하기 위한 목적 말고는 다른 뜻 없이 책을 펼치는 사람들에게는 결코 사소한 일이 아니다. 그렇게 읽는 동기는 흔히 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 널리 퍼져 있으며, 그 동기 탓에 법률서나 좋은 책뿐 아니라 호메로스와 베르길리우스, 스위프트와 세르반테스의 책장도 자주 그저 넘겨지곤 한다."]], [["Many other are the emoluments which arise from both these, but they are for the most part so obvious, that we shall not at present stay to enumerate them; especially since it occurs to us that the principal merit of both the prologue and the preface is that they be short.", "이 둘에서 비롯되는 이득은 그 밖에도 여럿이지만, 대부분 너무나 빤한 것이라 지금 일일이 늘어놓느라 머무르지는 않겠다. 더구나 머리말이든 서문이든 그 으뜸가는 미덕은 짧다는 데 있다는 사실이 마침 떠오르니 말이다."]], [["# Chapter ii.", "# Chapter ii."]], [["A whimsical adventure which befel the squire, with the distressed situation of Sophia.", "시골 향사에게 닥친 별난 소동, 그리고 소피아의 딱한 처지."]], [["We must now convey the reader to Mr Western's lodgings, which were in Piccadilly, where he was placed by the recommendation of the landlord at the Hercules Pillars at Hyde Park Corner; for at the inn, which was the first he saw on his arrival in town, he placed his horses, and in those lodgings, which were the first he heard of, he deposited himself.", "이제 우리는 독자를 웨스턴 씨의 숙소로 안내해야겠다. 그곳은 피커딜리에 있었는데, 하이드파크 코너의 헤라클레스 기둥 여관 주인이 천거해 준 곳이었다. 그가 도시에 도착해 처음 본 그 여관에 말들을 맡겼고, 처음 소문을 들은 그 숙소에 자신의 몸을 부려 놓았기 때문이다."]], [["Here, when Sophia alighted from the hackney-coach, which brought her from the house of Lady Bellaston, she desired to retire to the apartment provided for her; to which her father very readily agreed, and whither he attended her himself.", "여기서, 소피아가 벨라스턴 부인의 집에서 그녀를 태우고 온 삯마차에서 내렸을 때, 그녀는 자기를 위해 마련된 방으로 물러가기를 청했다. 아버지는 선뜻 그러라 했고, 몸소 그녀를 데려다주었다."], ["A short dialogue, neither very material nor pleasant to relate minutely, then passed between them, in which he pressed her vehemently to give her consent to the marriage with Blifil, who, as he acquainted her, was to be in town in a few days; but, instead of complying, she gave a more peremptory and resolute refusal than she had ever done before.", "그러고 나서 두 사람 사이에 짧은 대화가 오갔는데, 그리 중요하지도, 자세히 옮길 만큼 유쾌하지도 않은 내용이었다. 그는 며칠 안에 도시로 올 거라고 일러 둔 블리필과의 혼인에 동의하라고 그녀를 격렬하게 다그쳤다."], ["This so incensed her father, that after many bitter vows, that he would force her to have him whether she would or no, he departed from her with many hard words and curses, locked the door, and put the key into his pocket.", "그러나 그녀는 따르기는커녕, 그 어느 때보다 단호하고 결연한 거절을 내놓았다. 이에 아버지는 어찌나 격노했던지, 네가 원하든 원치 않든 기어이 그놈과 짝지어 주겠노라고 모진 맹세를 거듭 퍼붓고는, 험한 말과 저주를 한가득 쏟아 내며 그녀 곁을 떠나 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["While Sophia was left with no other company than what attend the closest state prisoner, namely, fire and candle, the squire sat down to regale himself over a bottle of wine, with his parson and the landlord of the Hercules Pillars, who, as the squire said, would make an excellent third man, and could inform them of the news of the town, and how affairs went; for to be sure, says he, he knows a great deal, since the horses of many of the quality stand at his house.", "소피아가, 가장 엄중히 갇힌 죄수에게나 딸린 그 동무, 곧 난롯불과 촛불 말고는 아무 벗도 없이 남겨진 동안, 향사는 자기 목사와 헤라클레스 기둥 여관 주인을 데리고 포도주 한 병을 앞에 놓고 흥겹게 자리를 잡았다. 향사의 말로는, 여관 주인이야말로 셋째 사람으로 더없이 훌륭한 짝이며, 도시의 소식과 세상 돌아가는 형편을 일러 줄 수 있는 자였다. \"암, 그야 많이 알고말고.\" 그가 말했다. \"지체 높은 양반들 말이 숱하게 그자네 마구간에 묵으니까 말이야.\""]], [["In this agreeable society Mr Western past that evening and great part of the succeeding day, during which period nothing happened of sufficient consequence to find a place in this history.", "이 즐거운 무리 속에서 웨스턴 씨는 그날 저녁과 이튿날 한나절을 보냈는데, 그동안 이 이야기에 자리를 차지할 만큼 중요한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 그 내내 소피아는 홀로 지냈다."], ["All this time Sophia past by herself; for her father swore she should never come out of her chamber alive, unless she first consented to marry Blifil; nor did he ever suffer the door to be unlocked, unless to convey her food, on which occasions he always attended himself.", "아버지가, 먼저 블리필과 혼인하기로 승낙하지 않는 한 살아서는 결코 그 방을 나오지 못하리라고 으름장을 놓았기 때문이다. 또한 음식을 들여보낼 때를 빼고는 문이 열리는 것을 결코 허락하지 않았고, 그럴 때면 늘 몸소 곁을 지켰다."]], [["The second morning after his arrival, while he and the parson were at breakfast together on a toast and tankard, he was informed that a gentleman was below to wait on him.", "도시에 도착한 지 이틀째 되는 아침, 그가 목사와 함께 토스트와 한 잔의 맥주로 아침을 들고 있는데, 어떤 신사가 아래층에서 그를 뵙고자 기다린다는 전갈이 왔다."]], [["“A gentleman!”", "\"신사라고?\""], ["quoth the squire, “who the devil can he be?", "향사가 말했다. \"대체 누구지?"], ["Do, doctor, go down and see who 'tis.", "박사 양반, 어디 내려가서 누군지 좀 보고 와요."], ["Mr Blifil can hardly be come to town yet.--Go down, do, and know what his business is.”", "블리필 씨가 벌써 도시에 왔을 리는 없을 텐데.—내려가서 무슨 볼일인지 알아봐 줘요.\""]], [["The doctor returned with an account that it was a very well-drest man, and by the ribbon in his hat he took him for an officer of the army; that he said he had some particular business, which he could deliver to none but Mr Western himself.", "목사가 돌아와 전하기를, 매우 잘 차려입은 사람인데 모자에 단 리본으로 보아 군 장교인 듯하며, 어떤 특별한 볼일이 있어 웨스턴 씨 본인 말고는 누구에게도 전할 수 없다고 했다는 것이었다."]], [["“An officer!”", "\"장교라!\""], ["cries the squire; “what can any such fellow have to do with me?", "향사가 외쳤다. \"그런 작자가 나랑 무슨 볼일이람?"], ["If he wants an order for baggage-waggons, I am no justice of peace here, nor can I grant a warrant.--Let un come up then, if he must speak to me.”", "짐수레 차출 명령서가 필요한 거라면, 난 여기 치안판사도 아니고 영장을 내줄 수도 없는데.—기어이 나한테 할 말이 있다면야 올려보내요.\""]], [["A very genteel man now entered the room; who, having made his compliments to the squire, and desired the favour of being alone with him, delivered himself as follows:--", "이윽고 아주 점잖은 사내가 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 향사에게 인사를 차린 뒤, 단둘이 있게 해 달라고 청하고는 이렇게 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Sir, I come to wait upon you by the command of my Lord Fellamar; but with a very different message from what I suppose you expect, after what past the other night.”", "\"선생, 저는 펠라머 경의 명을 받들어 찾아뵈었습니다. 다만, 일전 그날 밤 일이 있은 뒤라 선생께서 짐작하실 법한 것과는 사뭇 다른 전갈을 가져왔습니다.\""]], [["“My lord who?”", "\"무슨 경이라고?\""], ["cries the squire; “I never heard the name o'un.”", "향사가 외쳤다. \"그런 이름은 들어 본 적도 없는데.\""]], [["“His lordship,” said the gentleman, “is willing to impute everything to the effect of liquor, and the most trifling acknowledgment of that kind will set everything right; for as he hath the most violent attachment to your daughter, you, sir, are the last person upon earth from whom he would resent an affront; and happy is it for you both that he hath given such public demonstrations of his courage as to be able to put up an affair of this kind without danger of any imputation on his honour. All he desires, therefore, is, that you will before me make some acknowledgment; the slightest in the world will be sufficient; and he intends this afternoon to pay his respects to you, in order to obtain your leave of visiting the young lady on the footing of a lover.”", "\"경께서는,\" 사내가 말했다. \"모든 것을 술기운 탓으로 돌리실 뜻이시며, 그 정도의 사소한 인정 한마디면 모든 일이 바로잡힐 것입니다. 그분은 따님께 더없이 격렬한 애정을 품고 계셔서, 선생, 이 세상에서 모욕을 받고도 가장 노여워하지 않으실 마지막 분이 바로 선생이십니다. 또한 두 분께 다행스러운 것은, 그분이 이런 종류의 일을 명예에 한 점 흠 잡힐 위험 없이 덮어 둘 수 있을 만큼 용기를 이미 만천하에 증명해 보이셨다는 점입니다. 그러니 그분이 바라시는 것은 다만, 선생께서 제 앞에서 어떤 인정의 말을 해 주시는 것뿐입니다. 세상에서 가장 가벼운 한마디면 충분합니다. 그분은 오늘 오후 선생을 찾아뵙고, 연인의 자격으로 따님을 방문할 허락을 얻고자 하십니다.\""]], [["“I don't understand much of what you say, sir,”", "\"무슨 말인지 도통 모르겠소, 선생.\" 향사가 말했다."], ["said the squire; “but I suppose, by what you talk about my daughter, that this is the lord which my cousin, Lady Bellaston, mentioned to me, and said something about his courting my daughter.", "\"허나 내 딸 얘기를 하는 걸 보니, 이게 바로 내 사촌 벨라스턴 부인이 나한테 말했던 그 경인 모양이오. 부인이 내 딸한테 구애를 한다나 어쩐다나 하더군."], ["If so be that how that be the case--you may give my service to his lordship, and tell un the girl is disposed of already.”", "사정이 그렇다면야—경께 내 인사나 전하고, 그 애는 이미 임자가 정해졌다고 일러 주시오.\""]], [["“Perhaps, sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said the gentleman, “you are not sufficiently apprized of the greatness of this offer.", "사내가 말했다. \"아마 이 제안이 얼마나 대단한 것인지 충분히 헤아리지 못하신 듯합니다."], ["I believe such a person, title, and fortune would be nowhere refused.”", "저런 인물에, 작위에, 재산이라면 어디서도 거절당할 리 없을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Lookee, sir,” answered the squire; “to be very plain, my daughter is bespoke already; but if she was not, I would not marry her to a lord upon any account; I hate all lords; they are a parcel of courtiers and Hanoverians, and I will have nothing to do with them.”", "\"이보시오, 선생.\" 향사가 답했다. \"아주 까놓고 말하리다. 내 딸은 벌써 정해진 데가 있소. 허나 설령 그렇지 않더라도, 난 무슨 일이 있어도 그 애를 경 따위한테 시집보내지 않을 거요. 난 경이란 작자들을 다 싫어해. 죄다 아첨꾼에 하노버 패거리들이고, 난 그런 자들하곤 아무 상관도 하기 싫소.\""]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"그렇군요, 선생.\" 사내가 말했다."], ["said the gentleman, “if that is your resolution, the message I am to deliver to you is that my lord desires the favour of your company this morning in Hyde Park.”", "\"그것이 선생의 결심이라면, 제가 전해 드릴 전갈은 이렇습니다. 경께서 오늘 아침 하이드파크에서 선생과 한자리하기를 청하십니다.\""]], [["“You may tell my lord,”", "\"경한테 전하시오.\""], ["answered the squire, “that I am busy and cannot come.", "향사가 답했다. \"난 바빠서 갈 수가 없다고."], ["I have enough to look after at home, and can't stir abroad on any account.”", "집안에 챙길 일이 산더미라 무슨 일이 있어도 밖에 나설 수가 없소.\""]], [["“I am sure, sir,”", "\"선생,\" 상대가 말했다."], ["quoth the other, “you are too much a gentleman to send such a message; you will not, I am convinced, have it said of you, that, after having affronted a noble peer, you refuse him satisfaction.", "\"선생께서는 그런 전갈을 보낼 만큼 모자란 신사가 결코 아니실 겁니다. 고귀한 경 한 분을 모욕해 놓고 그분께 결투의 만족을 거절했다는 말이 선생을 두고 나돌게 두지는 않으시리라 확신합니다."], ["His lordship would have been willing, from his great regard to the young lady, to have made up matters in another way; but unless he is to look on you as a father, his honour will not suffer his putting up such an indignity as you must be sensible you offered him.”", "경께서는 따님을 어찌나 존중하시는지, 다른 방식으로 일을 매듭짓고자 하셨습니다. 그러나 선생을 장인으로 우러러보게 될 처지가 아니라면, 선생께서 분명 의식하셨을 그런 모욕을 그분의 명예가 덮어 두고 참아 줄 수는 없습니다.\""]], [["“I offered him!”", "\"내가 모욕을 줬다고!\""], ["cries the squire; “it is a d--n'd lie!", "향사가 외쳤다. \"그건 빌어먹을 거짓말이오!"], ["I never offered him anything.”", "난 그자한테 아무것도 한 게 없소.\""]], [["Upon these words the gentleman returned a very short verbal rebuke, and this he accompanied at the same time with some manual remonstrances, which no sooner reached the ears of Mr Western, than that worthy squire began to caper very briskly about the room, bellowing at the same time with all his might, as if desirous to summon a greater number of spectators to behold his agility.", "이 말에 사내는 아주 짧은 말로 한마디 꾸짖었고, 동시에 손으로도 몇 차례 항의를 더했다. 그것이 웨스턴 씨의 귀에—아니 몸에—닿기가 무섭게, 그 점잖은 향사는 자기 날렵함을 구경하라고 더 많은 구경꾼이라도 불러 모으려는 듯, 있는 힘껏 고래고래 소리치며 방 안을 아주 활기차게 껑충껑충 뛰어다니기 시작했다."]], [["The parson, who had left great part of the tankard unfinished, was not retired far; he immediately attended therefore on the squire's vociferation, crying, “Bless me!", "맥주잔을 절반이나 비우지 못한 채 자리를 뜬 목사는 멀리 가지 않은 터였다. 그래서 그는 향사의 고함 소리에 곧장 달려와 외쳤다."], ["sir, what's the matter?”--“Matter!”", "\"이런! 선생, 무슨 일입니까?\"—\"무슨 일이냐고!\""], ["quoth the squire, “here's a highwayman, I believe, who wants to rob and murder me--for he hath fallen upon me with that stick there in his hand, when I wish I may be d--n'd if I gid un the least provocation.”", "향사가 말했다. \"여기 노상강도가 하나 있는데, 날 털고 죽이려 드는 모양이오.—내가 빌어먹을 놈이 되어도 좋으니, 손톱만큼도 자극한 적 없는데 손에 든 저 막대기로 날 덮쳤단 말이오.\""]], [["“How, sir,”", "\"무슨 소립니까, 선생.\""], ["said the captain, “did you not tell me I lyed?”", "대위가 말했다. \"당신이 나한테 거짓말한다고 하지 않았소?\""]], [["“No, as I hope to be saved,”", "\"아니, 내가 구원받기를 바라는 마음으로 맹세코,\" 향사가 답했다."], ["answered the squire, “--I believe I might say, 'Twas a lie that I had offered any affront to my lord--but I never said the word, `you lie.'--I understand myself better, and you might have understood yourself better than to fall upon a naked man.", "\"—내가 경한테 무슨 모욕을 줬다는 게 거짓말이라고는 했을지 몰라도—'당신이 거짓말한다'라는 말은 결코 입에 올린 적 없소. 난 내 분수를 더 잘 아는 사람이고, 당신도 맨손인 사람한테 덤벼들지 않을 만큼은 제 분수를 알았어야지."], ["If I had a stick in my hand, you would not have dared strike me.", "내 손에 막대기 하나만 있었어도 당신 따위 감히 날 못 쳤을 거요."], ["I'd have knocked thy lantern jaws about thy ears.", "그 턱주가리를 귓등까지 날려 버렸을 텐데."], ["Come down into yard this minute, and I'll take a bout with thee at single stick for a broken head, that I will; or I will go into naked room and box thee for a belly-full.", "당장 마당으로 내려와, 막대기 한 대씩 주고받으며 머리통이 깨질 때까지 붙어 보자고, 정말이야. 아니면 빈방으로 들어가서 실컷 주먹다짐이라도 해 보든가."], ["At unt half a man, at unt, I'm sure.”", "당신은 절반쯤도 사내가 못 되는 작자야, 그래, 분명히.\""]], [["The captain, with some indignation, replied, “I see, sir, you are below my notice, and I shall inform his lordship you are below his. I am sorry I have dirtied my fingers with you.” At which words he withdrew, the parson interposing to prevent the squire from stopping him, in which he easily prevailed, as the other, though he made some efforts for the purpose, did not seem very violently bent on success. However, when the captain was departed, the squire sent many curses and some menaces after him; but as these did not set out from his lips till the officer was at the bottom of the stairs, and grew louder and louder as he was more and more remote, they did not reach his ears, or at least did not retard his departure.", "대위는 다소 분개하여 대꾸했다. \"보아하니, 선생, 당신은 내가 상대할 가치도 없는 사람이군. 경께도 당신이 그분 안중에 둘 가치도 없는 자라고 아뢰겠소. 당신 따위에 손가락을 더럽힌 게 유감이오.\" 이 말과 함께 그는 물러갔다. 목사가 끼어들어 향사가 그를 막지 못하게 했는데, 그 일은 쉽게 성공했다. 향사 쪽에서 막아 보려고 몇 번 애쓰는 시늉은 했어도, 정작 성공할 마음이 그리 간절해 보이지는 않았기 때문이다. 그래도 대위가 떠나자, 향사는 그 뒤에 대고 저주를 한가득, 으름장도 몇 마디 날렸다. 그러나 이 말들이 입에서 터져 나온 것은 장교가 이미 계단을 다 내려간 뒤였고, 그가 멀어질수록 점점 더 커졌으므로, 그의 귀에는 닿지도 않았고, 적어도 그의 발걸음을 늦추지는 못했다."]], [["Poor Sophia, however, who, in her prison, heard all her father's outcries from first to last, began now first to thunder with her foot, and afterwards to scream as loudly as the old gentleman himself had done before, though in a much sweeter voice. These screams soon silenced the squire, and turned all his consideration towards his daughter, whom he loved so tenderly, that the least apprehension of any harm happening to her, threw him presently into agonies; for, except in that single instance in which the whole future happiness of her life was concerned, she was sovereign mistress of his inclinations.", "그런데 갇혀 있던 가엾은 소피아는 처음부터 끝까지 아버지의 고함을 죄다 듣고 있었다. 그녀는 이제 처음으로 발을 쿵쿵 굴렀고, 이어 조금 전 노인이 했던 것만큼이나 크게—다만 훨씬 고운 목소리로—비명을 지르기 시작했다. 이 비명은 이내 향사를 잠잠하게 만들었고, 그의 온 마음을 딸에게로 돌려놓았다. 그는 딸을 어찌나 애틋이 사랑했던지, 그녀에게 무슨 해라도 닥칠까 하는 작은 두려움만으로도 곧장 고통 속에 빠져들었다. 그녀의 평생 행복이 걸린 그 단 하나의 일을 제외하면, 그녀는 그의 마음을 다스리는 더없는 여주인이었던 것이다."]], [["Having ended his rage against the captain, with swearing he would take the law of him, the squire now mounted upstairs to Sophia, whom, as soon as he had unlocked and opened the door, he found all pale and breathless.", "대위를 향한 분노를, 법으로 그를 손봐 주겠다는 맹세로 마무리한 향사는 이제 소피아에게로 계단을 올라갔다. 문을 열고 들어서자, 그는 딸이 새파랗게 질려 숨을 헐떡이는 것을 보았다."], ["The moment, however, that she saw her father, she collected all her spirits, and, catching him hold by the hand, she cryed passionately, “O my dear sir, I am almost frightened to death!", "그러나 아버지를 보는 순간, 그녀는 온 정신을 그러모아 그의 손을 붙잡고 격정에 차서 부르짖었다. \"오, 아버지, 무서워서 죽을 것만 같았어요!"], ["I hope to heaven no harm hath happened to you.”", "부디 아버지께 아무 해도 없었기를 하늘에 빌어요.\" \"아니, 아니다.\""], ["“No, no,”", "향사가 외쳤다."], ["cries the squire, “no great harm.", "\"큰 해는 없다. 그 못된 놈이 날 크게 다치게 하진 않았어."], ["The rascal hath not hurt me much, but rat me if I don't ha the la o'", "허나 그놈을 법으로 가만두지 않으면 내가 사람이 아니다.\" \"제발 아버지,\""], ["un.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"무슨 일인지 말씀해 주세요."], ["“Pray, dear sir,”", "아버지를 모욕한 게 누구예요?\" \"그자 이름은 모른다.\""], ["says she, “tell me what's the matter; who is it that hath insulted you?”", "웨스턴이 답했다. \"어떤 장교 나부랭이겠지."], ["“I don't know the name o' un,”", "우리한테 매를 맞고도 도리어 우리가 값을 치러야 하는 그런 부류 말이다."], ["answered Western; “some officer fellow, I suppose, that we are to pay for beating us; but I'll make him pay this bout, if the rascal hath got anything, which I suppose he hath not.", "허나 이번엔 내가 그놈한테 단단히 치르게 해 줄 테다, 그 못된 놈한테 뭐라도 있다면 말이지. 뭐 없을 것 같다만."], ["For thof he was drest out so vine, I question whether he had got a voot of land in the world.”", "그렇게 곱게 차려입긴 했어도, 세상에 땅 한 뼘이나 가졌는지 의심스럽단 말이야.\" \"그런데 아버지,\""], ["“But, dear sir,”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"다툰 까닭이 뭐였어요?\""], ["cries she, “what was the occasion of your quarrel?”", "\"그게 뭐겠느냐, 소피.\" 향사가 답했다."], ["“What should it be, Sophy,”", "\"다 너 때문이지, 소피! 내 모든 불행이 다 너 탓이다."], ["answered the squire, “but about you, Sophy?", "넌 결국 이 가엾은 아비를 죽이고 말 게야. 여기 어디 사는지도 모를 경이라는 작자가, 참 어이없게도!"], ["All my misfortunes are about you; you will be the death of your poor father at last.", "너를 마음에 들어 한답시고, 내가 승낙을 안 해 줬다고 나한테 결투장을 보내왔지 뭐냐. 자, 착한 딸이 되어 주렴, 소피."], ["Here's a varlet of a lord, the Lord knows who, forsooth!", "이 아비의 온갖 골칫거리를 끝내 다오. 자, 그놈한테 시집가겠다고 해 주렴."], ["who hath a taan a liking to you, and because I would not gi un my consent, he sent me a kallenge.", "그놈이 하루 이틀 안에 도시로 올 게다. 오는 대로 혼인하겠다고 이 아비한테 약속만 해 주면, 넌 날 세상에서 가장 행복한 사내로 만들어 주는 거고, 나도 널 가장 행복한 여인으로 만들어 주마."], ["Come, do be a good girl, Sophy, and put an end to all your father's troubles; come, do consent to ha un; he will be in town within this day or two; do but promise me to marry un as soon as he comes, and you will make me the happiest man in the world, and I will make you the happiest woman; you shall have the finest cloaths in London, and the finest jewels, and a coach and six at your command.", "런던에서 가장 고운 옷에, 가장 좋은 보석에, 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차까지 네 마음대로 부리게 해 주마. 난 벌써 올워디한테 내 재산 절반을 내주겠다고 약속했다—에라, 까짓것!"], ["I promised Allworthy already to give up half my estate--od rabbet it!", "통째로 내준대도 그리 아깝지 않을 것 같구나.\" \"아버지께서는,\""], ["I should hardly stick at giving up the whole.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"제 말도 좀 들어 주실 만큼 자상하신가요?\"—\"어찌 그런 걸 묻느냐, 소피?\""], ["“Will my papa be so kind,”", "그가 외쳤다. \"내가 네 목소리를 영국에서 제일 좋은 사냥개 무리의 노랫소리보다도 더 듣고 싶어 한다는 걸 알면서.—네 말을 듣고말고, 내 어여쁜 딸아!"], ["says she, “as to hear me speak?”--“Why wout ask, Sophy?”", "살아 있는 한 네 목소리를 들었으면 좋겠다. 만일 그 기쁨을 잃어버린다면, 난 단 한순간을 더 살자고 동전 한 닢도 내놓지 않을 게다."], ["cries he, “when dost know I had rather hear thy voice than the musick of the best pack of dogs in England.--Hear thee, my dear little girl!", "정말이지, 소피, 넌 내가 널 얼마나 사랑하는지 모른다, 정말 몰라. 알았다면 결코 도망쳐서 이 가엾은 아비를 버려 두지 못했을 게야."], ["I hope I shall hear thee as long as I live; for if I was ever to lose that pleasure, I would not gee a brass varden to live a moment longer.", "이 세상에 어린 소피 말고는 아무 기쁨도, 아무 위안도 없는 이 아비를 말이다.\" 이 말에 그의 눈에는 눈물이 고였다."], ["Indeed, Sophy, you do not know how I love you, indeed you don't, or you never could have run away and left your poor father, who hath no other joy, no other comfort upon earth, but his little Sophy.”", "그러자 소피아도 (눈에서 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며) 답했다. \"정말이에요, 사랑하는 아버지."], ["At these words the tears stood in his eyes; and Sophia (with the tears streaming from hers) answered, “Indeed, my dear papa, I know you have loved me tenderly, and heaven is my witness how sincerely I have returned your affection; nor could anything but an apprehension of being forced into the arms of this man have driven me to run from a father whom I love so passionately, that I would, with pleasure, sacrifice my life to his happiness; nay, I have endeavoured to reason myself into doing more, and had almost worked up a resolution to endure the most miserable of all lives, to comply with your inclination.", "아버지께서 저를 애틋이 사랑하신 걸 잘 알아요. 제가 그 사랑을 얼마나 진심으로 되돌려 드렸는지는 하늘이 증인이시고요."], ["It was that resolution alone to which I could not force my mind; nor can I ever.”", "이 사람의 품에 억지로 떠밀려 갈지 모른다는 두려움이 아니었다면, 그 어떤 것도 제가 이토록 격렬히 사랑하는 아버지에게서 달아나도록 몰아세우지 못했을 거예요. 아버지의 행복을 위해서라면 저는 기꺼이 제 목숨도 바칠 거예요."], ["Here the squire began to look wild, and the foam appeared at his lips, which Sophia, observing, begged to be heard out, and then proceeded: “If my father's life, his health, or any real happiness of his was at stake, here stands your resolved daughter; may heaven blast me if there is a misery I would not suffer to preserve you!--No, that most detested, most loathsome of all lots would I embrace.", "아니, 저는 그보다 더한 일까지 하려고 스스로를 다잡으려 애썼고, 아버지의 뜻을 따르기 위해 세상에서 가장 비참한 삶을 견디겠다는 결심을 거의 세울 뻔했어요. 다만 제 마음을 도저히 끌고 갈 수 없었던 것이 바로 그 한 가지 결심이었어요."], ["I would give my hand to Blifil for your sake.”--“I tell thee, it will preserve me,”", "앞으로도 결코 그러지 못할 거고요.\" 여기서 향사는 사나운 눈빛을 띠기 시작했고, 그 입가에는 거품이 일었다."], ["answers the father; “it will give me health, happiness, life, everything.--Upon my soul I shall die if dost refuse me; I shall break my heart, I shall, upon my soul.”--“Is it possible,”", "소피아는 그것을 보고 끝까지 들어 달라고 청한 뒤 말을 이었다. \"만일 아버지의 목숨이나 건강, 혹은 아버지의 어떤 진정한 행복이 걸린 일이라면, 여기 결심을 굳힌 따님이 있어요."], ["says she, “you can have such a desire to make me miserable?”--“I tell thee noa,”", "아버지를 지킬 수만 있다면 제가 못 견딜 비참함이 어디 있겠어요, 그렇지 못하다면 하늘이 저를 쳐 버려도 좋아요!—아니, 그 가장 혐오스럽고 가장 역겨운 운명까지도 저는 끌어안을 거예요. 아버지를 위해서라면 블리필에게 이 손을 내어 드리겠어요.\"—\"그게 바로 나를 지키는 거라니까.\""], ["answered he loudly, “d--n me if there is a thing upon earth I would not do to see thee happy.”--“And will not my dear papa allow me to have the least knowledge of what will make me so?", "아버지가 답했다. \"그게 나한테 건강과 행복과 목숨, 그 모든 걸 줄 게다.—맹세코, 네가 날 거절하면 난 죽고 말 게야."], ["If it be true that happiness consists in opinion, what must be my condition, when I shall think myself the most miserable of all the wretches upon earth?”", "가슴이 미어질 게다, 정말로, 맹세코.\" —\"그럴 수가 있나요,\""], ["“Better think yourself so,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"절 비참하게 만들고 싶은 마음이 그토록 크실 수가.\""], ["said he, “than know it by being married to a poor bastardly vagabond.”", "—\"아니라니까.\" 그가 큰 소리로 답했다."], ["“If it will content you, sir,”", "\"널 행복하게 만드는 일이라면 이 세상에서 내가 못 할 일은 없다, 빌어먹을!\" —\"그런데도 사랑하는 아버지께서는 무엇이 절 행복하게 할지에 대해 제가 조금도 알도록 허락해 주지 않으시는 거예요?"], ["said Sophia, “I will give you the most solemn promise never to marry him, nor any other, while my papa lives, without his consent.", "행복이 마음먹기에 달렸다는 게 참말이라면, 제가 스스로를 이 세상 모든 가련한 이들 중 가장 비참하다고 여기게 될 때, 제 처지는 대체 어떻게 되겠어요?\" \"그렇게 여기는 편이 낫지.\""], ["Let me dedicate my whole life to your service; let me be again your poor Sophy, and my whole business and pleasure be, as it hath been, to please and divert you.”", "그가 말했다. \"가난뱅이 사생아 뜨내기 따위와 혼인해서 진짜로 그렇게 되는 것보다는.\""], ["“Lookee, Sophy,”", "\"아버지께서 그것으로 흡족하시다면,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["answered the squire, “I am not to be choused in this manner.", "\"더없이 엄숙한 약속을 드리겠어요. 아버지께서 살아 계신 동안에는 그 사람도, 다른 누구도, 아버지의 승낙 없이는 결코 혼인하지 않겠다고요."], ["Your aunt Western would then have reason to think me the fool she doth.", "제 평생을 아버지를 모시는 데 바치게 해 주세요. 다시 아버지의 가엾은 소피가 되게 해 주시고, 예전처럼 아버지를 기쁘게 하고 즐겁게 해 드리는 것을 제 온 일이자 기쁨으로 삼게 해 주세요.\""], ["No, no, Sophy, I'd have you to know I have a got more wisdom, and know more of the world, than to take the word of a woman in a matter where a man is concerned.”", "\"이봐라, 소피.\" 향사가 답했다."], ["“How, sir, have I deserved this want of confidence?”", "\"난 이런 식으로 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니다. 그랬다간 네 웨스턴 고모가 날 자기 생각대로 바보 천치라고 여길 빌미만 생기지."], ["said she; “have I ever broke a single promise to you?", "안 돼, 안 돼, 소피, 똑똑히 알아 둬라. 난 사내가 걸린 일에서 여자의 말 따위를 곧이듣지 않을 만큼은 더 지혜롭고 세상 물정도 더 잘 안다.\""], ["or have I ever been found guilty of a falsehood from my cradle?”", "\"아버지, 제가 어떻게 이런 불신을 받을 짓을 했나요?\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Lookee, Sophy,”", "\"제가 아버지께 한 약속을 단 하나라도 어긴 적 있나요? 아니면 요람에 있을 적부터 거짓말로 죄지은 적이 한 번이라도 있었나요?\""], ["cries he; “that's neither here nor there.", "\"이봐라, 소피.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["I am determined upon this match, and have him you shall, d--n me if shat unt.", "\"그건 이러쿵저러쿵할 일이 아니다. 난 이 혼인을 단단히 작정했고, 넌 그놈을 맞이하게 될 거다, 안 그러면 내가 사람이 아니다."], ["D--n me if shat unt, though dost hang thyself the next morning.”", "빌어먹을, 안 그러면 내가 사람이 아니라니까. 네가 그 이튿날 아침에 목을 매단다 해도 말이다.\""], ["At repeating which words he clinched his fist, knit his brows, bit his lips, and thundered so loud, that the poor afflicted, terrified Sophia sunk trembling into her chair, and, had not a flood of tears come immediately to her relief, perhaps worse had followed.", "이 말을 되뇌며 그는 주먹을 불끈 쥐고, 눈썹을 찌푸리고, 입술을 깨물며 어찌나 우렁차게 호통을 쳤던지, 가엾고 괴롭고 겁에 질린 소피아는 부들부들 떨며 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다. 그리고 눈물이 곧장 쏟아져 그녀를 풀어 주지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 더 나쁜 일이 뒤따랐을지도 모른다."]], [["Western beheld the deplorable condition of his daughter with no more contrition or remorse than the turnkey of Newgate feels at viewing the agonies of a tender wife, when taking her last farewel of her condemned husband; or rather he looked down on her with the same emotions which arise in an honest fair tradesman, who sees his debtor dragged to prison for £10, which, though a just debt, the wretch is wickedly unable to pay.", "웨스턴은 딸의 그 가련한 모습을 보면서도, 사형 선고를 받은 남편에게 마지막 작별을 고하는 다정한 아내의 고통을 바라보는 뉴게이트 간수가 느끼는 것만큼의 가책이나 회한조차 느끼지 않았다. 아니, 오히려 그는 정직하고 점잖은 상인이, 비록 정당한 빚이긴 하나 그 가련한 자가 차마 갚지 못하는 10파운드 때문에 채무자가 감옥으로 끌려가는 꼴을 바라볼 때 일어나는 그런 감정으로 딸을 내려다보았다."], ["Or, to hit the case still more nearly, he felt the same compunction with a bawd, when some poor innocent, whom she hath ensnared into her hands, falls into fits at the first proposal of what is called seeing company.", "아니, 더 꼭 들어맞게 말하자면, 그는 자기 손아귀에 옭아 넣은 어떤 가엾은 순진한 처녀가 이른바 손님맞이라는 것을 처음 제안받고 발작을 일으킬 때 뚜쟁이가 느끼는 것과 똑같은 양심의 가책을 느꼈다."], ["Indeed this resemblance would be exact, was it not that the bawd hath an interest in what she doth, and the father, though perhaps he may blindly think otherwise, can, in reality, have none in urging his daughter to almost an equal prostitution.", "사실 이 비유는 꼭 들어맞을 뻔했으나, 다만 한 가지 차이가 있다. 뚜쟁이는 제가 하는 짓에서 이익을 챙기지만, 아버지는—비록 눈이 멀어 달리 여길지 몰라도—실은 딸을 거의 그에 맞먹는 매음으로 떠미는 데서 아무런 이익도 챙길 수 없다는 점이다."]], [["In this condition he left his poor Sophia, and, departing with a very vulgar observation on the effect of tears, he locked the room, and returned to the parson, who said everything he durst in behalf of the young lady, which, though perhaps it was not quite so much as his duty required, yet was it sufficient to throw the squire into a violent rage, and into many indecent reflections on the whole body of the clergy, which we have too great an honour for that sacred function to commit to paper.", "이런 상태로 그는 가엾은 소피아를 남겨 두고, 눈물의 효험에 대해 아주 천박한 한마디를 내뱉으며 방을 잠그고 떠나 목사에게로 돌아왔다. 목사는 그 처녀를 위해 차마 입에 담을 수 있는 말은 다 했는데, 비록 그것이 그의 본분이 요구하는 만큼에는 미치지 못했을지 모르나, 그래도 향사를 격렬한 분노에 빠뜨리고 성직자 전체에 대해 온갖 무례한 험담을 퍼붓게 만들기에는 충분했다. 그 험담들은 우리가 그 거룩한 직분을 너무도 존경하는 까닭에 차마 종이에 옮기지 않겠다."]], [["What happened to Sophia during her confinement.", "3장. 소피아가 갇혀 지내는 동안 일어난 일."]], [["The landlady of the house where the squire lodged had begun very early to entertain a strange opinion of her guests. However, as she was informed that the squire was a man of vast fortune, and as she had taken care to exact a very extraordinary price for her rooms, she did not think proper to give any offence; for, though she was not without some concern for the confinement of poor Sophia, of whose great sweetness of temper and affability the maid of the house had made so favourable a report, which was confirmed by all the squire's servants, yet she had much more concern for her own interest than to provoke one, whom, as she said, she perceived to be a very hastish kind of a gentleman.", "향사가 묵고 있던 집의 안주인은 일찌감치 자기 손님들에 대해 묘한 생각을 품기 시작했다. 그러나 그 향사가 어마어마한 재산을 가진 사람이라는 말을 들은 데다, 자기 방값을 유난히 비싸게 받아 두었던 터라, 굳이 비위를 거스를 마음은 없었다. 가엾은 소피아가 갇혀 있는 처지에 대해 다소 마음이 쓰이긴 했다. 집안의 하녀가 소피아의 더없이 다정한 성품과 상냥함을 좋게 전했고, 향사의 하인들도 모두 그 말을 거들었기 때문이다. 그래도 그녀는 자기 입으로 보기에 몹시 성마른 양반이라 여겨지는 사람을 자극하기에는, 제 잇속을 챙기는 마음이 훨씬 더 컸다."]], [["Though Sophia eat but little, yet she was regularly served with her meals; indeed, I believe, if she had liked any one rarity, that the squire, however angry, would have spared neither pains nor cost to have procured it for her; since, however strange it may appear to some of my readers, he really doated on his daughter, and to give her any kind of pleasure was the highest satisfaction of his life.", "소피아는 거의 먹지 않았으나, 끼니는 빠짐없이 차려졌다. 사실 내 생각엔, 소피아가 무엇이든 귀한 음식을 먹고 싶어 했다면, 향사가 아무리 화가 나 있더라도 수고와 비용을 아끼지 않고 그것을 구해다 주었으리라. 일부 독자에게는 이상하게 보일지 모르나, 그는 진심으로 딸을 끔찍이 아꼈고, 딸에게 어떤 즐거움이든 주는 것이야말로 그의 인생에서 가장 큰 만족이었기 때문이다."]], [["The dinner-hour being arrived, Black George carried her up a pullet, the squire himself (for he had sworn not to part with the key) attending the door.", "저녁 식사 시간이 되자 검은 조지가 영계 한 마리를 올려다 갔고, 향사 자신은 (열쇠를 결코 내주지 않겠다고 맹세했으므로) 문간을 지켰다."], ["As George deposited the dish, some compliments passed between him and Sophia (for he had not seen her since she left the country, and she treated every servant with more respect than some persons shew to those who are in a very slight degree their inferiors).", "조지가 접시를 내려놓는 동안, 그와 소피아 사이에 몇 마디 인사가 오갔다 (그는 소피아가 시골을 떠난 뒤로 그녀를 본 적이 없었고, 소피아는 어떤 하인이든 어떤 이들이 조금 아랫사람을 대하는 것보다 훨씬 더 정중하게 대했다)."], ["Sophia would have had him take the pullet back, saying, she could not eat; but George begged her to try, and particularly recommended to her the eggs, of which he said it was full.", "소피아는 영계를 도로 가져가라며, 먹을 수가 없다고 했다. 그러나 조지는 한번 들어 보시라고 청했고, 특히 그 안에 가득하다며 알을 권했다."]], [["All this time the squire was waiting at the door; but George was a great favourite with his master, as his employment was in concerns of the highest nature, namely, about the game, and was accustomed to take many liberties. He had officiously carried up the dinner, being, as he said, very desirous to see his young lady; he made therefore no scruple of keeping his master standing above ten minutes, while civilities were passing between him and Sophia, for which he received only a good-humoured rebuke at the door when he returned.", "이 모든 시간 동안 향사는 문간에서 기다리고 있었다. 그러나 조지는 주인의 큰 총애를 받는 자였다. 그의 일이라는 게 가장 중대한 일, 곧 사냥감을 다루는 일이었던지라, 그는 여러모로 제멋대로 굴어도 괜찮은 데 익숙해져 있었다. 그는 자기 아가씨를 몹시 보고 싶었노라며 일부러 나서서 식사를 올려다 갔고, 그래서 소피아와 정중한 말을 주고받느라 주인을 십 분 넘게 세워 두고도 거리낌이 없었다. 그가 돌아왔을 때 문간에서 받은 것이라곤 기분 좋은 가벼운 핀잔뿐이었다."]], [["The eggs of pullets, partridges, pheasants, &c., were, as George well knew, the most favourite dainties of Sophia.", "영계며 자고새며 꿩 따위의 알이 소피아가 가장 좋아하는 별미라는 것을 조지는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["It was therefore no wonder that he, who was a very good-natured fellow, should take care to supply her with this kind of delicacy, at a time when all the servants in the house were afraid she would be starved; for she had scarce swallowed a single morsel in the last forty hours.", "그러니 천성이 무척 선량한 그가, 집안의 하인들이 모두 아가씨가 굶어 죽지나 않을까 두려워하던 그 무렵에, 이런 종류의 진미를 그녀에게 챙겨다 준 것은 조금도 이상한 일이 아니었다. 소피아는 지난 마흔 시간 동안 음식 한 입 거의 삼키지 못했으니 말이다."]], [["Though vexation hath not the same effect on all persons as it usually hath on a widow, whose appetite it often renders sharper than it can be rendered by the air on Bansted Downs, or Salisbury Plain; yet the sublimest grief, notwithstanding what some people may say to the contrary, will eat at last.", "번민이 모든 사람에게, 흔히 과부에게 미치는 것과 똑같은 작용을 하는 것은 아니다. 과부에게는 번민이 밴스테드 다운스나 솔즈베리 평원의 바람으로도 그렇게까지 돋울 수 없을 만큼 식욕을 날카롭게 만드는 일이 잦지만 말이다."], ["And Sophia, herself, after some little consideration, began to dissect the fowl, which she found to be as full of eggs as George had reported it.", "그래도 누가 뭐라 반대로 말하든, 가장 숭고한 슬픔이라 해도 결국에는 먹기 마련이다. 그리하여 소피아 자신도 잠시 망설인 끝에 그 새를 발라내기 시작했고, 조지가 전한 대로 정말 알이 가득 차 있음을 발견했다."]], [["But, if she was pleased with these, it contained something which would have delighted the Royal Society much more; for if a fowl with three legs be so invaluable a curiosity, when perhaps time hath produced a thousand such, at what price shall we esteem a bird which so totally contradicts all the laws of animal oeconomy, as to contain a letter in its belly?", "그러나 그녀가 그 알에 기뻐했다 한들, 그 안에는 왕립학회를 훨씬 더 기쁘게 했을 무언가가 들어 있었다. 다리가 셋 달린 새가 — 어쩌면 세월이 그런 것을 천 마리쯤 만들어 냈을 텐데도 — 그토록 값을 매길 수 없는 진귀한 것이라면, 동물의 생리 법칙을 송두리째 거스르며 뱃속에 편지 한 장을 품은 새는 대체 얼마짜리로 쳐 주어야 할까?"], ["Ovid tells us of a flower into which Hyacinthus was metamorphosed, that bears letters on its leaves, which Virgil recommended as a miracle to the Royal Society of his day; but no age nor nation hath ever recorded a bird with a letter in its maw.", "오비디우스는 히아킨토스가 변신하여 된 어느 꽃에 대해, 그 잎에 글자가 적혀 있다고 전하며, 베르길리우스는 그것을 당대의 왕립학회에 기적이라 천거했다. 그러나 어느 시대 어느 나라도 모이주머니에 편지를 품은 새를 기록한 적은 없었다."]], [["But though a miracle of this kind might have engaged all the Académies des Sciences in Europe, and perhaps in a fruitless enquiry; yet the reader, by barely recollecting the last dialogue which passed between Messieurs Jones and Partridge, will be very easily satisfied from whence this letter came, and how it found its passage into the fowl.", "그러나 이런 종류의 기적이라면 유럽의 모든 과학 아카데미(Académies des Sciences)를 끌어들였을 법하고, 어쩌면 아무 소득 없는 탐구로 이어졌을 법하지만, 독자는 존스 씨와 파트리지 씨 사이에 오간 지난번 대화를 떠올리기만 해도, 이 편지가 어디서 왔고 어떻게 그 새 속으로 들어가게 되었는지 아주 쉽게 납득할 것이다."]], [["Sophia, notwithstanding her long fast, and notwithstanding her favourite dish was there before her, no sooner saw the letter than she immediately snatched it up, tore it open, and read as follows:--", "소피아는 그토록 오래 굶었음에도, 또 가장 좋아하는 음식이 눈앞에 있었음에도, 편지를 보자마자 곧장 그것을 낚아채 뜯어 열고는 다음과 같이 읽었다. “아가씨,"]], [["“Was I not sensible to whom I have the honour of writing, I should endeavour, however difficult, to paint the horrors of my mind at the account brought me by Mrs Honour; but as tenderness alone can have any true idea of the pangs which tenderness is capable of feeling, so can this most amiable quality, which my Sophia possesses in the most eminent degree, sufficiently inform her what her Jones must have suffered on this melancholy occasion.", "제가 누구에게 글을 쓰는 영광을 누리는지 모르는 사람이었다면, 아무리 어렵더라도 아너 부인이 제게 전한 소식에 제 마음이 얼마나 끔찍한 지경에 빠졌는지를 그려 보려 애썼을 것입니다. 그러나 오직 다정한 마음만이 다정한 마음이 느낄 수 있는 그 고통을 참되이 헤아릴 수 있는 법이니, 제 소피아가 누구보다도 가장 빼어나게 지닌 이 더없이 사랑스러운 자질이야말로, 이 슬픈 일을 두고 그대의 존스가 얼마나 괴로워했을지 그대에게 충분히 일러 줄 것입니다."], ["Is there a circumstance in the world which can heighten my agonies, when I hear of any misfortune which hath befallen you?", "그대에게 닥친 어떤 불행을 전해 들을 때, 제 고뇌를 더욱 키울 만한 사정이 이 세상에 있을까요?"], ["Surely there is one only, and with that I am accursed.", "분명히 단 하나가 있고, 저는 바로 그것으로 저주받았습니다."], ["It is, my Sophia, the dreadful consideration that I am myself the wretched cause.", "소피아여, 그것은 제 자신이 이 모든 비참함의 원인이라는 그 무서운 생각입니다."], ["Perhaps I here do myself too much honour, but none will envy me an honour which costs me so extremely dear.", "어쩌면 제가 여기서 제게 너무 과한 영예를 돌리는지도 모르나, 이토록 값비싸게 치르는 영예라면 누구도 제게서 그것을 시샘하지는 않을 것입니다. 이 주제넘음을 용서해 주시고, 더 큰 주제넘음 또한 용서해 주십시오."], ["Pardon me this presumption, and pardon me a greater still, if I ask you, whether my advice, my assistance, my presence, my absence, my death, or my tortures can bring you any relief?", "제 충고나, 제 도움이나, 제가 곁에 있는 것이나, 제가 떠나 있는 것이나, 제 죽음이나, 제 고통이 그대에게 조금이라도 위안이 될 수 있겠는지 여쭙는 이 일을 말입니다. 더없이 완벽한 흠모, 더없이 빈틈없는 보살핌, 더없이 뜨거운 사랑, 더없이 녹아드는 다정함, 그대의 뜻에 대한 더없이 순순한 복종이, 그대가 제 행복을 위해 희생하려는 것에 보답이 될 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Can the most perfect admiration, the most watchful observance, the most ardent love, the most melting tenderness, the most resigned submission to your will, make you amends for what you are to sacrifice to my happiness?", "만일 그럴 수 있다면, 제 사랑스러운 천사여, 언제나 그대를 받아 안고 지켜 주려 열려 있는 이 두 팔로 날아오십시오. 그대가 그대 자신만을 데려오든, 세상의 온갖 재물을 함께 가져오든, 제 생각에는 따져 볼 가치도 없는 차이일 뿐입니다."], ["If they can, fly, my lovely angel, to those arms which are ever open to receive and protect you; and to which, whether you bring yourself alone, or the riches of the world with you, is, in my opinion, an alternative not worth regarding.", "반대로, 만일 지혜가 우세하여, 가장 무르익은 숙고 끝에 그대에게 이 희생이 너무 크다고 일러 준다면, 그리고 저를 버리는 것 말고는 아버님과 화해하고 그대의 소중한 마음에 평안을 되찾아 줄 길이 달리 없다면, 부디 저를 영영 그대의 생각에서 몰아내고, 그대의 결단을 떨치십시오. 제 고통에 대한 어떤 연민도 그 다정한 가슴속에서 조금의 무게도 갖지 못하게 하십시오."], ["If, on the contrary, wisdom shall predominate, and, on the most mature reflection, inform you, that the sacrifice is too great; and if there be no way left to reconcile your father, and restore the peace of your dear mind, but by abandoning me, I conjure you drive me for ever from your thoughts, exert your resolution, and let no compassion for my sufferings bear the least weight in that tender bosom.", "아가씨, 믿어 주십시오. 저는 제 자신보다 그대를 더 진심으로 사랑하기에, 저의 크고 으뜸가는 목적은 그대의 행복입니다."], ["Believe me, madam, I so sincerely love you better than myself, that my great and principal end is your happiness.", "저의 첫 번째 소망은 (어찌하여 운명은 제게 그것을 허락하지 않았을까요?) 그대가 매 순간 세상에서 가장 행복한 여인이 되는 모습을 보는 것이었고, 외람되나 말씀드리면 지금도 그러합니다. 저의 두 번째 소망은 그대가 그러하다는 소식을 듣는 것입니다."], ["My first wish (why would not fortune indulge me in it?) was, and pardon me if I say, still is, to see you every moment the happiest of women; my second wish is, to hear you are so; but no misery on earth can equal mine, while I think you owe an uneasy moment to him who is,", "그러나 그대가, 다음과 같은 사람에게 단 한 순간의 괴로움이라도 빚지고 있다고 제가 생각하는 한, 이 세상 어떤 비참함도 제 것에 견줄 수 없을 것입니다. 아가씨,"]], [["Madam, in every sense, and to every purpose, your devoted, THOMAS JONES.”", "모든 의미에서, 그리고 모든 뜻에서,\n그대에게 헌신하는,"]], [["What Sophia said, or did, or thought, upon this letter, how often she read it, or whether more than once, shall all be left to our reader's imagination.", "토머스 존스 올림.” 이 편지를 두고 소피아가 무어라 말했는지, 무엇을 했는지, 무슨 생각을 했는지, 그것을 몇 번이나 읽었는지, 한 번 넘게 읽었는지 어떤지는 모두 독자의 상상에 맡기겠다."], ["The answer to it he may perhaps see hereafter, but not at present: for this reason, among others, that she did not now write any, and that for several good causes, one of which was this, she had no paper, pen, nor ink.", "이 편지에 대한 답장은 어쩌면 나중에 보게 될지도 모르나, 지금은 아니다. 여러 까닭이 있지만 그중 하나는, 그녀가 지금은 답장을 쓰지 않았다는 것이며, 거기에는 몇 가지 그럴 만한 이유가 있는데, 그중 하나가 바로 그녀에게 종이도, 펜도, 잉크도 없었다는 것이다."]], [["In the evening, while Sophia was meditating on the letter she had received, or on something else, a violent noise from below disturbed her meditations.", "저녁 무렵, 소피아가 받은 편지에 대해, 혹은 다른 무언가에 대해 골똘히 생각하고 있을 때, 아래층에서 들려온 요란한 소리가 그녀의 사색을 흩뜨렸다."], ["This noise was no other than a round bout at altercation between two persons.", "이 소리는 다름 아닌 두 사람 사이에 벌어진 한바탕 언쟁이었다."], ["One of the combatants, by his voice, she immediately distinguished to be her father; but she did not so soon discover the shriller pipes to belong to the organ of her aunt Western, who was just arrived in town, where having, by means of one of her servants, who stopt at the Hercules Pillars, learned where her brother lodged, she drove directly to his lodgings.", "다투는 이들 중 한 사람은 목소리로 보아 곧장 자기 아버지임을 알아차렸으나, 그보다 더 날카로운 음색이 자기 고모 웨스턴의 목청에서 나오는 것임은 그리 빨리 알아채지 못했다. 고모는 막 도시에 도착한 참이었는데, 헤라클레스 기둥 여관에 들른 자기 하인 하나를 통해 오라버니가 묵는 곳을 알아내고는, 곧장 그의 숙소로 마차를 몰아온 것이었다."]], [["We shall therefore take our leave at present of Sophia, and, with our usual good-breeding, attend her ladyship.", "그러므로 우리는 지금은 소피아와 작별하고, 여느 때의 예의 바른 태도로 그 마님을 모시기로 하자."]], [["In which Sophia is delivered from her confinement.", "소피아가 갇힘에서 풀려나는 이야기."]], [["The squire and the parson (for the landlord was now otherwise engaged) were smoaking their pipes together, when the arrival of the lady was first signified.", "지주와 목사는(여관 주인은 이제 다른 일에 매여 있었으므로) 함께 담배를 피우고 있었는데, 그때 부인이 도착했다는 소식이 처음 전해졌다. 지주는 누이의 이름을 듣자마자 곧장 아래로 달려 내려가 그녀를 위층으로 안내했다."], ["The squire no sooner heard her name, than he immediately ran down to usher her upstairs; for he was a great observer of such ceremonials, especially to his sister, of whom he stood more in awe than of any other human creature, though he never would own this, nor did he perhaps know it himself.", "그는 이런 격식을 무척 따지는 사람이었고, 특히 누이에게는 더욱 그러했다. 그는 다른 어떤 사람보다도 누이를 어려워했으니, 본인은 결코 그것을 인정하려 들지 않았고 어쩌면 스스로도 알지 못했을 것이다."]], [["Mrs Western, on her arrival in the dining-room, having flung herself into a chair, began thus to harangue: “Well, surely, no one ever had such an intolerable journey.", "웨스턴 부인은 식당에 들어서자마자 의자에 몸을 던지고는 이렇게 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했다. \"어휴, 정말이지 이렇게 견디기 힘든 여정을 겪은 사람은 없을 거예요."], ["I think the roads, since so many turnpike acts, are grown worse than ever.", "통행세 법령이 그렇게 많이 생겼는데도 길은 전보다 더 나빠진 것 같아요."], ["La, brother, how could you get into this odious place?", "어머나, 오라버니, 어쩌자고 이런 끔찍한 곳에 드신 거예요?"], ["no person of condition, I dare swear, ever set foot here before.”", "분명히 장담컨대, 지체 있는 사람치고 여기에 발을 들인 이는 없었을 거예요.\" \"글쎄다,\""], ["“I don't know,”", "지주가 말했다."], ["cries the squire, “I think they do well enough; it was landlord recommended them.", "\"내 보기엔 그럭저럭 괜찮은데. 여관 주인이 추천한 거다."], ["I thought, as he knew most of the quality, he could best shew me where to get among um.”", "그자가 상류층 사람들을 거의 다 안다기에, 그자라면 그런 사람들 틈에 끼는 법을 제일 잘 일러줄 거라고 생각했지.\""], ["“Well, and where's my niece?”", "\"그래서, 내 조카는 어디 있죠?\" 부인이 말했다."], ["says the lady; “have you been to wait upon Lady Bellaston yet?”", "\"벌써 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아뵙기는 했어요?\" \"그럼, 그럼,\""], ["“Ay, ay,”", "지주가 말했다."], ["cries the squire, “your niece is safe enough; she is upstairs in chamber.”", "\"네 조카는 아주 안전해. 위층 방에 있다.\""], ["“How!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["answered the lady, “is my niece in this house, and does she not know of my being here?”", "부인이 답했다. \"제 조카가 이 집에 있는데, 제가 여기 온 줄도 모른단 말이에요?\""], ["“No, nobody can well get to her,”", "\"아니, 아무도 그 애한테 함부로 못 가.\""], ["says the squire, “for she is under lock and key.", "지주가 말했다. \"자물쇠로 채워 가뒀거든."], ["I have her safe; I vetched her from my lady cousin the first night I came to town, and I have taken care o'", "안전하게 데리고 있어. 내가 도시에 온 첫날 밤 사촌 마님한테서 데려와서, 그 뒤로 줄곧 잘 지켜왔다."], ["her ever since; she is as secure as a fox in a bag, I promise you.”", "자루 속에 든 여우만큼이나 단단히 가둬뒀으니 걱정 마라.\""], ["“Good heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["returned Mrs Western, “what do I hear?", "웨스턴 부인이 되받았다. \"이게 무슨 소리예요?"], ["I thought what a fine piece of work would be the consequence of my consent to your coming to town yourself; nay, it was indeed your own headstrong will, nor can I charge myself with having ever consented to it.", "오라버니가 직접 도시로 올라오는 걸 제가 허락하면 무슨 꼴이 날지 짐작은 했지만요. 아니, 사실 그건 오라버니의 고집불통 뜻이었지, 제가 거기에 동의했다는 책임은 질 수 없어요."], ["Did not you promise me, brother, that you would take none of these headstrong measures?", "오라버니, 이런 막무가내 수단은 쓰지 않겠다고 제게 약속하지 않았어요?"], ["Was it not by these headstrong measures that you forced my niece to run away from you in the country?", "바로 그런 막무가내 수단 때문에 시골에서 조카가 오라버니한테서 도망친 거잖아요?"], ["Have you a mind to oblige her to take such another step?”", "또 그런 짓을 하게 만들 작정이세요?\""], ["“Z--ds and the devil!”", "\"빌어먹을, 제기랄!\""], ["cries the squire, dashing his pipe on the ground; “did ever mortal hear the like?", "지주가 담뱃대를 바닥에 내동댕이치며 소리쳤다. \"이런 소리를 들은 사람이 또 있겠나?"], ["when I expected you would have commended me for all I have done, to be fallen upon in this manner!”", "내가 한 일을 죄다 칭찬해 줄 줄 알았더니, 이런 식으로 몰아세우다니!\" \"아니, 오라버니!\""], ["“How, brother!”", "부인이 말했다."], ["said the lady, “have I ever given you the least reason to imagine I should commend you for locking up your daughter?", "\"제가 언제 딸을 가둔 일로 오라버니를 칭찬할 거라고 짐작할 만한 빌미를 조금이라도 준 적 있어요?"], ["Have I not often told you that women in a free country are not to be treated with such arbitrary power?", "자유로운 나라에서 여자를 그런 제멋대로의 권력으로 다뤄선 안 된다고 제가 누차 말씀드리지 않았어요? 우리도 남자만큼 자유로워요."], ["We are as free as the men, and I heartily wish I could not say we deserve that freedom better.", "그리고 진심으로 바라건대, 우리가 그 자유를 더 누릴 자격이 있다고 말하지 않을 수 있었으면 좋겠네요."], ["If you expect I should stay a moment longer in this wretched house, or that I should ever own you again as my relation, or that I should ever trouble myself again with the affairs of your family, I insist upon it that my niece be set at liberty this instant.”", "만일 제가 이 끔찍한 집에 한순간이라도 더 머물기를, 또 제가 오라버니를 다시 친척으로 인정하기를, 또 제가 오라버니 가족의 일에 다시 신경 쓰기를 바라신다면, 제 조카를 당장 이 자리에서 풀어줄 것을 요구해요.\" 그녀가 이렇게 말할 때, 등을 난로 쪽으로 두고 한 손은 뒤로 한 채 다른 손에는 코담배 한 줌을 쥐고 선 그 위풍당당한 기세란, 아마존 부대를 이끄는 탈레스트리스조차도 이보다 더 무시무시한 모습을 보인 적이 있었을까 싶을 정도였다."], ["This she spoke with so commanding an air, standing with her back to the fire, with one hand behind her, and a pinch of snuff in the other, that I question whether Thalestris, at the head of her Amazons, ever made a more tremendous figure.", "그러니 가엾은 지주가 그녀가 자아내는 위압감을 당해내지 못한 것도 이상한 일이 아니었다. \"옜다,\""], ["It is no wonder, therefore, that the poor squire was not proof against the awe which she inspired.", "그가 열쇠를 내던지며 소리쳤다. \"여기 있다, 마음대로 해라."], ["“There,” he cried, throwing down the key, “there it is, do whatever you please.", "나는 그저 블리필이 도시에 올 때까지만 가둬둘 작정이었어."], ["I intended only to have kept her up till Blifil came to town, which can't be long; and now if any harm happens in the mean time, remember who is to be blamed for it.”", "그것도 곧이지. 그동안에 무슨 해라도 생기거든, 누구를 탓해야 하는지나 기억해 둬라.\""]], [["“I will answer it with my life,”", "\"제 목숨을 걸고 책임지겠어요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 소리쳤다."], ["cried Mrs Western, “but I shall not intermeddle at all, unless upon one condition, and that is, that you will commit the whole entirely to my care, without taking any one measure yourself, unless I shall eventually appoint you to act.", "\"하지만 한 가지 조건이 아니라면 저는 일절 끼어들지 않겠어요. 그 조건이란, 오라버니가 모든 일을 완전히 제 손에 맡기고, 제가 결국 오라버니더러 나서라고 정해주지 않는 한, 어떤 수단도 직접 취하지 않는 거예요."], ["If you ratify these preliminaries, brother, I yet will endeavour to preserve the honour of your family; if not, I shall continue in a neutral state.”", "오라버니, 만일 이 사전 조건을 받아들이신다면, 저는 그래도 오라버니 가문의 명예를 지키려 애써보겠어요. 그러지 않으신다면, 저는 중립을 지키겠어요.\""]], [["“I pray you, good sir,”", "\"부탁드립니다, 어르신,\" 목사가 말했다."], ["said the parson, “permit yourself this once to be admonished by her ladyship: peradventure, by communing with young Madam Sophia, she will effect more than you have been able to perpetrate by more rigorous measures.”", "\"이번 한 번만은 부인의 충고를 받아들이도록 자신을 허락하십시오. 어쩌면 소피아 아가씨와 마음을 나누어, 더 엄한 수단으로 어르신께서 이루지 못하신 것보다 더 많은 것을 부인께서 해내실지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“What, dost thee open upon me?”", "\"뭐야, 네놈까지 나한테 입을 놀려?\""], ["cries the squire: “if thee dost begin to babble, I shall whip thee in presently.”", "지주가 소리쳤다. \"그렇게 주절대기 시작하면 당장 채찍질을 해줄 테다.\""]], [["“Fie, brother,”", "\"어머, 오라버니,\" 부인이 답했다."], ["answered the lady, “is this language to a clergyman?", "\"성직자한테 그게 무슨 말투예요? 서플 목사님은 분별 있는 분이고, 오라버니에게 가장 좋은 충고를 드리는 거예요."], ["Mr Supple is a man of sense, and gives you the best advice; and the whole world, I believe, will concur in his opinion; but I must tell you I expect an immediate answer to my categorical proposals.", "온 세상이 그분의 의견에 동의할 거라고 저는 믿어요. 하지만 말해두는데, 저는 제 단도직입적인 제안에 즉답을 기대해요."], ["Either cede your daughter to my disposal, or take her wholly to your own surprizing discretion, and then I here, before Mr Supple, evacuate the garrison, and renounce you and your family for ever.”", "따님을 제 처분에 맡기시든지, 아니면 따님을 온전히 오라버니의 그 놀라운 분별에 맡기시든지요. 후자라면 저는 여기서 서플 목사님 앞에서 이 진지를 떠나, 오라버니와 오라버니 가족을 영영 등지겠어요.\""]], [["“I pray you let me be a mediator,”", "\"부디 제가 중재자가 되게 해주십시오.\""], ["cries the parson, “let me supplicate you.”", "목사가 소리쳤다. \"간청드립니다.\""]], [["“Why, there lies the key on the table,”", "\"이봐, 열쇠는 저기 탁자 위에 있다.\""], ["cries the squire.", "지주가 소리쳤다."], ["“She may take un up, if she pleases: who hinders her?”", "\"원하면 집어가면 될 거 아냐. 누가 막기라도 한대?\""]], [["“No, brother,”", "\"아니에요, 오라버니,\""], ["answered the lady, “I insist on the formality of its being delivered me, with a full ratification of all the concessions stipulated.”", "부인이 답했다. \"저는 정식으로 그것이 제게 건네지기를, 그리고 약정된 모든 양보가 온전히 비준되기를 요구해요.\""]], [["“Why then I will deliver it to you.--There 'tis,”", "\"그럼 내가 너한테 건네주마. 옜다.\""], ["cries the squire.", "지주가 소리쳤다."], ["“I am sure, sister, you can't accuse me of ever denying to trust my daughter to you. She hath a-lived wi'", "\"정말이지, 누이야, 내가 딸을 너한테 맡기길 거절한 적 있다고는 못 할 거다."], ["you a whole year and muore to a time, without my ever zeeing her.”", "그 애는 너랑 꼬박 한 해도 넘게 살았어. 내가 코빼기도 못 본 채 말이야.\""]], [["“And it would have been happy for her,”", "\"그 애가 늘 저와 함께 살았더라면,\""], ["answered the lady, “if she had always lived with me.", "부인이 답했다. \"그 애에게는 행복한 일이었을 거예요."], ["Nothing of this kind would have happened under my eye.”", "제 눈앞에서라면 이런 일은 일어나지 않았을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Ay, certainly,”", "\"그래, 그렇겠지.\""], ["cries he, “I only am to blame.”", "그가 소리쳤다. \"잘못한 건 오로지 나뿐이지.\""]], [["“Why, you are to blame, brother,”", "\"그래요, 오라버니 잘못이에요.\""], ["answered she.", "그녀가 답했다."], ["“I have been often obliged to tell you so, and shall always be obliged to tell you so.", "\"제가 누차 그렇게 말씀드릴 수밖에 없었고, 앞으로도 늘 그렇게 말씀드릴 수밖에 없을 거예요."], ["However, I hope you will now amend, and gather so much experience from past errors, as not to defeat my wisest machinations by your blunders.", "하지만 이제는 고치시길 바라요. 그리고 지난 잘못에서 충분한 교훈을 얻어, 오라버니의 실수로 제 가장 현명한 계략을 망치지나 마세요."], ["Indeed, brother, you are not qualified for these negociations.", "정말이지, 오라버니, 오라버니는 이런 교섭에 어울리는 사람이 아니에요."], ["All your whole scheme of politics is wrong.", "오라버니의 책략이라는 게 통째로 틀려먹었어요."], ["I once more, therefore, insist, that you do not intermeddle.", "그러니 다시 한번 요구해요. 끼어들지 마세요."], ["Remember only what is past.”----", "지난 일만 기억하세요.\""]], [["“Z--ds and bl--d, sister,”", "\"제기랄, 빌어먹을, 누이야,\""], ["cries the squire, “what would you have me say?", "지주가 소리쳤다. \"나더러 무슨 말을 하란 말이냐?"], ["You are enough to provoke the devil.”", "너는 악마라도 화나게 할 사람이다.\""]], [["“There, now,”", "\"거 봐요, 또 저러시네.\""], ["said she, “just according to the old custom.", "그녀가 말했다. \"딱 옛날 버릇 그대로예요."], ["I see, brother, there is no talking to you.", "오라버니, 내가 보니 오라버니랑은 말이 안 통해요."], ["I will appeal to Mr Supple, who is a man of sense, if I said anything which could put any human creature into a passion; but you are so wrongheaded every way.”", "제가 사람을 화나게 할 만한 말을 한 마디라도 했는지, 분별 있는 분이신 서플 목사님께 여쭤보겠어요. 오라버니는 무슨 일에서나 그렇게 외고집이라니까요.\""]], [["“Let me beg you, madam,”", "\"제발 부탁드립니다, 부인,\""], ["said the parson, “not to irritate his worship.”", "목사가 말했다. \"어르신을 자극하지 말아 주십시오.\""]], [["“Irritate him?”", "\"자극이라뇨?\""], ["said the lady; “sure, you are as great a fool as himself.", "부인이 말했다. \"당신도 저 양반만큼이나 바보로군요."], ["Well, brother, since you have promised not to interfere, I will once more undertake the management of my niece.", "좋아요, 오라버니, 끼어들지 않겠다고 약속하셨으니, 제가 다시 한번 조카 일을 맡겠어요."], ["Lord have mercy upon all affairs which are under the directions of men!", "주여, 남자들 손에 맡겨진 모든 일에 자비를 베푸소서!"], ["The head of one woman is worth a thousand of yours.”", "여자 하나의 머리가 오라버니 같은 사람 천 명보다 나아요.\""], ["And now having summoned a servant to show her to Sophia, she departed, bearing the key with her.", "그러고는 하인을 불러 소피아에게 안내하게 한 뒤, 그녀는 열쇠를 챙겨 들고 나갔다."]], [["She was no sooner gone, than the squire (having first shut the door) ejaculated twenty bitches, and as many hearty curses against her, not sparing himself for having ever thought of her estate; but added, “Now one hath been a slave so long, it would be pity to lose it at last, for want of holding out a little longer.", "그녀가 나가자마자 지주는(먼저 문을 닫고는) 그녀를 향해 스무 번쯤 욕을 퍼붓고 그만큼 진심 어린 저주를 쏟아냈으며, 누이의 재산 따위에 마음을 두었던 자신도 봐주지 않았다. 그러나 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["The bitch can't live for ever, and I know I am down for it upon the will.”", "\"이렇게 오래 노예 노릇을 했는데, 이제 와서 조금만 더 버티지 못하고 놓쳐버린다면 아까운 노릇이지. 저 망할 것이 영원히 살 수도 없는 노릇이고, 유언장에 내 몫으로 적혀 있는 걸 내가 아니까 말이야.\""]], [["The parson greatly commended this resolution: and now the squire having ordered in another bottle, which was his usual method when anything either pleased or vexed him, did, by drinking plentifully of this medicinal julap, so totally wash away his choler, that his temper was become perfectly placid and serene, when Mrs Western returned with Sophia into the room. The young lady had on her hat and capuchin, and the aunt acquainted Mr Western, “that she intended to take her niece with her to her own lodgings; for, indeed, brother,” says she, “these rooms are not fit to receive a Christian soul in.”", "목사는 이 결심을 크게 칭찬했다. 이윽고 지주는 술을 한 병 더 들이라 명했는데, 이는 기쁘거나 언짢은 일이 있을 때면 늘 쓰는 그의 방식이었다. 그러고는 이 약효 좋은 청량제를 넉넉히 들이켜 노여움을 깡그리 씻어냈으니, 웨스턴 부인이 소피아를 데리고 방으로 돌아왔을 때 그의 기분은 더없이 평온하고 잔잔해져 있었다. 젊은 아가씨는 모자와 두건 달린 외투를 걸치고 있었고, 고모는 웨스턴 씨에게 알렸다. \"조카를 제 거처로 데려갈 작정이에요. 정말이지, 오라버니,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이런 방들은 멀쩡한 기독교인이 머물 만한 곳이 못 돼요.\""]], [["“Very well, madam,”", "\"좋습니다, 부인,\" 웨스턴이 말했다."], ["quoth Western, “whatever you please.", "\"당신 뜻대로 하시구려. 그 애가 당신 손보다 더 좋은 손에 맡겨질 데가 어디 있겠소."], ["The girl can never be in better hands than yours; and the parson here can do me the justice to say, that I have said fifty times behind your back, that you was one of the most sensible women in the world.”", "여기 목사도 증언해 줄 거요. 내가 당신 안 보는 데서 쉰 번도 넘게, 당신이 세상에서 가장 분별 있는 여자 중 하나라고 말했다는 걸 말이오.\""]], [["“To this,”", "\"그것에 대해서는,\""], ["cries the parson, “I am ready to bear testimony.”", "목사가 소리쳤다. \"제가 기꺼이 증언하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Nay, brother,”", "\"아니에요, 오라버니,\""], ["says Mrs Western, “I have always, I'm sure, given you as favourable a character.", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"저도 늘 오라버니를 그만큼 좋게 평했던 게 분명해요."], ["You must own you have a little too much hastiness in your temper; but when you will allow yourself time to reflect I never knew a man more reasonable.”", "오라버니, 성미가 좀 지나치게 급한 건 인정하셔야 해요. 하지만 시간을 두고 곰곰이 생각하실 때면, 저는 오라버니보다 더 사리 밝은 분을 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“Why then, sister, if you think so,”", "\"그렇다면, 누이야, 네가 그리 생각한다면,\""], ["said the squire, “here's your good health with all my heart.", "지주가 말했다. \"온 마음을 다해 네 건강을 위해 한잔하마."], ["I am a little passionate sometimes, but I scorn to bear any malice.", "나도 가끔은 좀 욱하긴 해도, 앙심을 품는 건 질색이다."], ["Sophy, do you be a good girl, and do everything your aunt orders you.”", "소피, 착한 아이가 되어, 고모가 시키는 건 뭐든지 하거라.\""]], [["“I have not the least doubt of her,”", "\"그 애는 조금도 의심하지 않아요.\""], ["answered Mrs Western.", "웨스턴 부인이 답했다."], ["“She hath had already an example before her eyes in the behaviour of that wretch her cousin Harriet, who ruined herself by neglecting my advice.", "\"이미 그 애 눈앞에 본보기가 있었으니까요. 제 충고를 무시하다가 신세를 망친 그 못된 사촌 해리엇의 행실 말이에요."], ["O brother, what think you?", "아, 오라버니, 어떻게 생각하세요?"], ["You was hardly gone out of hearing, when you set out for London, when who should arrive but that impudent fellow with the odious Irish name--that Fitzpatrick.", "오라버니가 런던으로 떠나고서 소리가 채 닿지 않을 만큼 멀어졌나 싶을 때, 누가 들이닥쳤는지 아세요? 글쎄 그 뻔뻔한 작자, 그 끔찍한 아일랜드 이름을 가진—피츠패트릭 말이에요."], ["He broke in abruptly upon me without notice, or I would not have seen him.", "그자가 기별도 없이 불쑥 들이닥치는 바람에, 안 그랬으면 만나지도 않았을 거예요."], ["He ran on a long, unintelligible story about his wife, to which he forced me to give him a hearing; but I made him very little answer, and delivered him the letter from his wife, which I bid him answer himself.", "그자는 제 아내 얘기를 길게, 알아들을 수도 없게 늘어놓으면서 억지로 제게 들으라 강요하더군요. 하지만 저는 거의 대꾸도 하지 않고, 아내가 보낸 편지를 그자에게 건네면서 직접 답하라고 일렀어요."], ["I suppose the wretch will endeavour to find us out, but I beg you will not see her, for I am determined I will not.”", "그 못된 작자가 우리를 찾아내려 들겠지만, 부탁이니 그 애는 만나지 마세요. 저는 절대 만나지 않기로 마음먹었으니까요.\""]], [["“I zee her!”", "\"내가 그 애를 만나!\""], ["answered the squire; “you need not fear me.", "지주가 답했다. \"그건 걱정 마라."], ["I'll ge no encouragement to such undutiful wenches.", "그런 불효막심한 계집애한테 부추길 일은 하지 않을 거다."], ["It is well for the fellow, her husband, I was not at huome.", "그 작자, 그 애 남편 말이다, 내가 집에 없었으니 그자한텐 다행이지."], ["Od rabbit it, he should have taken a dance thru the horse-pond, I promise un.", "빌어먹을, 못 연못에서 한바탕 헤엄을 치게 해줬을 텐데."], ["You zee, Sophy, what undutifulness brings volks to.", "봐라, 소피, 불효가 사람들을 어떤 꼴로 만드는지."], ["You have an example in your own family.”", "네 집안에 본보기가 있잖느냐.\""]], [["“Brother,”", "\"오라버니,\""], ["cries the aunt, “you need not shock my niece by such odious repetitions.", "고모가 소리쳤다. \"그렇게 끔찍한 얘기를 되풀이해서 조카에게 충격 줄 거 없어요."], ["Why will you not leave everything entirely to me?”", "어째서 모든 걸 전적으로 제게 맡기지 않으시는 거예요?\""], ["“Well, well, I wull, I wull,”", "\"알았다, 알았어. 그러마, 그러마.\""], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["And now Mrs Western, luckily for Sophia, put an end to the conversation by ordering chairs to be called.", "그리고 이제 웨스턴 부인이, 소피아에게는 다행스럽게도, 가마를 불러오라 명하면서 대화를 끝맺었다. 다행이라고 한 까닭은, 만일 대화가 훨씬 더 길게 이어졌다면 오누이 사이에 십중팔구 새로운 다툼거리가 생겼을 것이기 때문이다."], ["I say luckily, for had it continued much longer, fresh matter of dissension would, most probably, have arisen between the brother and sister; between whom education and sex made the only difference; for both were equally violent and equally positive: they had both a vast affection for Sophia, and both a sovereign contempt for each other.", "둘 사이의 유일한 차이라곤 교육과 성별뿐이었다. 둘 다 똑같이 격렬하고 똑같이 막무가내였으며, 둘 다 소피아를 끔찍이 아끼면서도, 둘 다 서로를 더없이 경멸했으니 말이다."]], [["# Chapter v.", "# Chapter v."]], [["In which Jones receives a letter from Sophia, and goes to a play with Mrs Miller and Partridge.", "존스가 소피아에게서 편지를 받고, 밀러 부인과 파트리지와 함께 연극을 보러 가다."]], [["The arrival of Black George in town, and the good offices which that grateful fellow had promised to do for his old benefactor, greatly comforted Jones in the midst of all the anxiety and uneasiness which he had suffered on the account of Sophia; from whom, by the means of the said George, he received the following answer to his letter, which Sophia, to whom the use of pen, ink, and paper was restored with her liberty, wrote the very evening when she departed from her confinement:", "블랙 조지가 도시에 도착했다는 소식, 그리고 그 고마운 사내가 옛 은인을 위해 해 주겠다고 약속한 호의는, 소피아 때문에 겪어 온 온갖 불안과 초조 한가운데 있던 존스에게 큰 위로가 되었다. 바로 그 조지를 통해 존스는 자기 편지에 대한 다음과 같은 답장을 받았다. 자유를 되찾으면서 펜과 잉크와 종이의 사용까지 함께 되돌려 받은 소피아가, 감금에서 풀려나던 바로 그날 저녁에 쓴 편지였다."]], [["“Sir,", "\"선생님,"]], [["“As I do not doubt your sincerity in what you write, you will be pleased to hear that some of my afflictions are at an end, by the arrival of my aunt Western, with whom I am at present, and with whom I enjoy all the liberty I can desire.", "당신이 쓰신 말씀의 진심을 의심하지 않기에, 제 고통 가운데 일부가 끝났다는 소식을 들으시면 기뻐하시리라 믿습니다. 웨스턴 고모님이 오신 덕분인데, 저는 지금 그분과 함께 지내며 제가 바랄 수 있는 모든 자유를 누리고 있습니다."], ["One promise my aunt hath insisted on my making, which is, that I will not see or converse with any person without her knowledge and consent.", "다만 고모님이 제게 한 가지 약속만은 꼭 하라고 고집하셨는데, 그분이 알지 못하고 동의하지 않은 어떤 사람도 만나거나 이야기를 나누지 않겠다는 것입니다."], ["This promise I have most solemnly given, and shall most inviolably keep: and though she hath not expressly forbidden me writing, yet that must be an omission from forgetfulness; or this, perhaps, is included in the word conversing.", "이 약속을 저는 더없이 엄숙하게 드렸고, 더없이 어김없이 지킬 것입니다. 비록 고모님이 편지 쓰는 것을 분명히 금하지는 않으셨지만, 그것은 깜빡 잊으신 데서 온 누락일 뿐이거나, 어쩌면 '이야기를 나눈다'는 말 속에 이미 포함되어 있을 것입니다."], ["However, as I cannot but consider this as a breach of her generous confidence in my honour, you cannot expect that I shall, after this, continue to write myself or to receive letters, without her knowledge.", "그러나 저로서는 이것을 제 명예에 대한 고모님의 너그러운 신뢰를 저버리는 일로 여기지 않을 수 없으니, 이후로 제가 그분 모르게 편지를 계속 쓰거나 편지를 받으리라고는 기대하지 마십시오."], ["A promise is with me a very sacred thing, and to be extended to everything understood from it, as well as to what is expressed by it; and this consideration may, perhaps, on reflection, afford you some comfort.", "제게 약속이란 매우 거룩한 것이어서, 말로 드러난 것뿐 아니라 거기서 미루어 짐작되는 모든 것에까지 미쳐야 합니다. 이 점을 곰곰이 생각해 보시면, 어쩌면 당신께 어떤 위안이 될지도 모르겠습니다."], ["But why should I mention a comfort to you of this kind; for though there is one thing in which I can never comply with the best of fathers, yet am I firmly resolved never to act in defiance of him, or to take any step of consequence without his consent.", "그런데 제가 어찌하여 당신께 이런 종류의 위안을 말씀드릴까요. 비록 더없이 훌륭하신 아버지의 뜻을 결코 따를 수 없는 한 가지 일이 있긴 하나, 저는 결코 아버지를 거스르며 행동하거나, 그분의 동의 없이 중대한 일에 한 걸음도 내딛지 않으리라 굳게 결심했기 때문입니다."], ["A firm persuasion of this must teach you to divert your thoughts from what fortune hath (perhaps) made impossible.", "이 점을 굳게 믿으신다면, 운명이 (어쩌면) 불가능하게 만들어 버린 것에서 마음을 거두는 법을 배우셔야 할 것입니다."], ["This your own interest persuades you.", "이것이 당신 자신의 이익이 권하는 바입니다."], ["This may reconcile, I hope, Mr Allworthy to you; and if it will, you have my injunctions to pursue it.", "또 이것이, 바라건대, 올워디 씨로 하여금 당신과 화해하시게 할 수 있습니다. 만일 그리되겠다면, 부디 그 길을 좇으시기를 제가 당부드립니다."], ["Accidents have laid some obligations on me, and your good intentions probably more.", "여러 일들이 제게 어떤 빚을 지웠고, 당신의 선한 마음은 아마도 더 많은 빚을 지웠습니다."], ["Fortune may, perhaps, be some time kinder to us both than at present.", "운명이 언젠가는 우리 두 사람 모두에게 지금보다 더 다정해질지도 모릅니다. 이것만은 믿어 주십시오."], ["Believe this, that I shall always think of you as I think you deserve, and am,", "저는 언제나 당신을, 당신이 마땅히 받으실 만하다고 제가 여기는 그대로 생각할 것이며, 또한 다음과 같은 사람임을. 선생님,"]], [["Sir, your obliged humble servant, Sophia Western.", "당신께 신세 진 미천한 종,\n소피아 웨스턴."]], [["“I charge you write to me no more--at present at least; and accept this, which is now of no service to me, which I know you must want, and think you owe the trifle only to that fortune by which you found it.”[*]", "당신께 부탁드리니 더는 제게 편지하지 마십시오. 적어도 지금은 말입니다. 그리고 이것을 받아 주십시오. 이제 제게는 아무 쓸모가 없으나 당신께는 분명 필요하리라 아는 것이며, 이 사소한 것은 다만 당신이 그것을 발견하게 한 그 운명에 빚진 것이라 여겨 주십시오.\"[*]"]], [["[*] Meaning, perhaps, the bank-bill for £100.", "[*] 아마 100파운드짜리 은행 어음을 말하는 듯하다."]], [["A child who hath just learnt his letters would have spelt this letter out in less time than Jones took in reading it. The sensations it occasioned were a mixture of joy and grief; somewhat like what divide the mind of a good man when he peruses the will of his deceased friend, in which a large legacy, which his distresses make the more welcome, is bequeathed to him. Upon the whole, however, he was more pleased than displeased; and, indeed, the reader may probably wonder that he was displeased at all; but the reader is not quite so much in love as was poor Jones; and love is a disease which, though it may, in some instances, resemble a consumption (which it sometimes causes), in others proceeds in direct opposition to it, and particularly in this, that it never flatters itself, or sees any one symptom in a favourable light.", "이제 막 글자를 깨친 아이라도, 존스가 이 편지를 읽는 데 들인 시간보다는 짧게 한 자 한 자 더듬어 읽었을 것이다. 이 편지가 불러일으킨 감정은 기쁨과 슬픔이 뒤섞인 것이었다. 마치 어떤 선한 사람이 세상을 떠난 벗의 유언장을 들여다보다가, 자기 곤궁함 때문에 더없이 반가운 큰 유산이 자신에게 남겨져 있음을 발견했을 때 그 마음을 갈라놓는 것과 비슷했다. 그러나 전체로 보면 그는 언짢기보다는 기뻤다. 사실 독자는 그가 조금이라도 언짢아했다는 사실에 의아해할지도 모른다. 하지만 독자는 가엾은 존스만큼 깊이 사랑에 빠져 있지는 않다. 사랑이란 하나의 병이어서, 어떤 경우에는 폐병(때로는 사랑이 폐병을 일으키기도 한다)과 닮았지만, 또 어떤 경우에는 그와 정반대로 나아간다. 특히 이 점에서 그러한데, 사랑은 결코 스스로를 다독여 위로하지 않으며, 어떤 징후도 좋게 보는 법이 없다."]], [["One thing gave him complete satisfaction, which was, that his mistress had regained her liberty, and was now with a lady where she might at least assure herself of a decent treatment. Another comfortable circumstance was the reference which she made to her promise of never marrying any other man; for however disinterested he might imagine his passion, and notwithstanding all the generous overtures made in his letter, I very much question whether he could have heard a more afflicting piece of news than that Sophia was married to another, though the match had been never so great, and never so likely to end in making her completely happy. That refined degree of Platonic affection which is absolutely detached from the flesh, and is, indeed, entirely and purely spiritual, is a gift confined to the female part of the creation; many of whom I have heard declare (and, doubtless, with great truth), that they would, with the utmost readiness, resign a lover to a rival, when such resignation was proved to be necessary for the temporal interest of such lover. Hence, therefore, I conclude that this affection is in nature, though I cannot pretend to say I have ever seen an instance of it.", "한 가지 점만은 그를 완전히 흡족하게 했으니, 곧 그의 연인이 자유를 되찾았고, 이제 적어도 점잖은 대접만은 스스로 확신할 수 있는 어느 부인과 함께 있다는 사실이었다. 또 하나 위로가 되는 정황은, 다른 어떤 남자와도 결코 결혼하지 않겠다는 그녀의 약속을 그녀가 언급했다는 점이었다. 자신의 연정이 아무리 사심 없는 것이라 여기더라도, 또 편지에서 베푼 그 온갖 너그러운 제안에도 불구하고, 그가 소피아가 다른 남자와 결혼했다는 소식보다 더 마음을 찢는 소식을 들을 수 있었을지 나는 몹시 의심스럽다. 설령 그 혼사가 더없이 훌륭하고, 그녀를 더없이 완전한 행복으로 이끌 가능성이 높았다 해도 말이다. 살에서 완전히 떨어져 나와, 실로 온전히 그리고 순수하게 정신적인, 그 세련된 경지의 플라톤적 애정이란 피조물 가운데 여성에게만 한정된 선물이다. 나는 그들 가운데 여럿이 (그리고 분명 매우 진실하게) 이렇게 단언하는 것을 들었다. 연인의 현세적 이익을 위해 그런 포기가 꼭 필요하다고 증명되면, 자신들은 더없이 기꺼이 연인을 연적에게 양보하겠노라고. 그러므로 나는 이런 애정이 자연 속에 실재한다고 결론짓는다. 다만 내가 그 실례를 본 적이 있다고는 감히 말하지 못하겠다."]], [["Mr Jones having spent three hours in reading and kissing the aforesaid letter, and being, at last, in a state of good spirits, from the last-mentioned considerations, he agreed to carry an appointment, which he had before made, into execution.", "존스 씨는 앞서 말한 편지를 읽고 입 맞추는 데 세 시간을 보낸 뒤, 마침내 방금 말한 그 생각들 덕분에 기분이 좋아진 상태가 되자, 전에 잡아 두었던 약속 하나를 실행에 옮기기로 했다."], ["This was, to attend Mrs Miller, and her younger daughter, into the gallery at the play-house, and to admit Mr Partridge as one of the company.", "그것은 밀러 부인과 그 작은딸을 극장 일층 관람석으로 모시고 가는 일이었으며, 파트리지도 일행으로 끼워 주기로 한 것이었다."], ["For as Jones had really that taste for humour which many affect, he expected to enjoy much entertainment in the criticisms of Partridge, from whom he expected the simple dictates of nature, unimproved, indeed, but likewise unadulterated, by art.", "사실 존스는 많은 사람들이 짐짓 흉내만 내는 그 해학에 대한 진짜 취향을 지니고 있었던 터라, 파트리지의 평을 들으며 큰 재미를 누리리라 기대했다. 그에게서 자연 그대로의 소박한 반응을 기대했던 것인데, 그것은 비록 기예로 다듬어지지 않았으나 또한 기예로 변질되지도 않은 것이었다."]], [["In the first row then of the first gallery did Mr Jones, Mrs Miller, her youngest daughter, and Partridge, take their places.", "그리하여 일층 관람석 첫 줄에 존스 씨와 밀러 부인, 그 작은딸과 파트리지가 자리를 잡았다."], ["Partridge immediately declared it was the finest place he had ever been in.", "파트리지는 대뜸 이곳이 지금껏 와 본 곳 가운데 가장 멋진 곳이라고 선언했다."], ["When the first music was played, he said, “It was a wonder how so many fiddlers could play at one time, without putting one another out.”", "첫 음악이 연주되자 그는 말했다. \"저렇게 많은 바이올린 연주자가 한꺼번에 켜는데 서로 헷갈리지 않다니, 참 신기한 노릇이오.\""], ["While the fellow was lighting the upper candles, he cried out to Mrs Miller, “Look, look, madam, the very picture of the man in the end of the common-prayer book before the gunpowder-treason service.”", "한 사내가 위쪽 촛불에 불을 붙이는 동안, 그는 밀러 부인에게 외쳤다. \"보세요, 보세요, 부인, 꼭 기도서 끝, 화약 음모 예배 앞에 나오는 그 사람 그림 같소.\""], ["Nor could he help observing, with a sigh, when all the candles were lighted, “That here were candles enough burnt in one night, to keep an honest poor family for a whole twelvemonth.”", "또 촛불이 모두 켜지자, 그는 한숨을 내쉬며 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다. \"여기서 하룻밤에 태우는 촛불이면, 정직한 가난한 집안 하나가 일 년 내내 살 만하겠소.\""]], [["As soon as the play, which was Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, began, Partridge was all attention, nor did he break silence till the entrance of the ghost; upon which he asked Jones, “What man that was in the strange dress; something,”", "연극이 시작되자—그것은 《덴마크 왕자 햄릿》이었다—파트리지는 온통 집중했고, 유령이 등장할 때까지 침묵을 깨지 않았다. 유령이 나오자 그는 존스에게 물었다."], ["said he, “like what I have seen in a picture.", "\"저 이상한 옷차림을 한 사람은 누구요? 어쩐지,\""], ["Sure it is not armour, is it?”", "그가 말했다, \"그림에서 본 것과 비슷한데."], ["Jones answered, “That is the ghost.”", "설마 저게 갑옷은 아니겠지요?\" 존스가 답했다."], ["To which Partridge replied with a smile, “Persuade me to that, sir, if you can.", "\"저게 유령이오.\" 그 말에 파트리지는 빙긋 웃으며 대꾸했다."], ["Though I can't say I ever actually saw a ghost in my life, yet I am certain I should know one, if I saw him, better than that comes to.", "\"그렇게 믿게 해 보시구려, 선생, 할 수 있다면. 평생 유령을 실제로 본 적은 없다고 해도, 막상 보면 그게 유령인지 아닌지 그 정도는 똑똑히 알아볼 자신이 있소."], ["No, no, sir, ghosts don't appear in such dresses as that, neither.”", "아니, 아니오, 선생, 유령은 저런 옷차림으로 나타나지 않는 법이오.\""], ["In this mistake, which caused much laughter in the neighbourhood of Partridge, he was suffered to continue, till the scene between the ghost and Hamlet, when Partridge gave that credit to Mr Garrick, which he had denied to Jones, and fell into so violent a trembling, that his knees knocked against each other.", "파트리지 주변에 큰 웃음을 일으킨 이 착각을, 그는 유령과 햄릿 사이의 장면이 나올 때까지 그대로 붙들고 있었다. 그 장면에서 파트리지는 존스에게는 인정하지 않았던 신뢰를 개릭 씨에게는 보냈으니, 어찌나 심하게 부들부들 떨었던지 무릎이 서로 맞부딪칠 지경이었다."], ["Jones asked him what was the matter, and whether he was afraid of the warrior upon the stage? “O la!", "존스가 무슨 일이냐고, 무대 위의 저 전사가 무섭냐고 묻자, 그가 말했다."], ["sir,” said he, “I perceive now it is what you told me.", "\"아이고, 선생, 이제 보니 선생이 말한 그대로구려."], ["I am not afraid of anything; for I know it is but a play.", "나는 아무것도 무섭지 않소. 그저 연극인 줄 아니까."], ["And if it was really a ghost, it could do one no harm at such a distance, and in so much company; and yet if I was frightened, I am not the only person.”", "설령 진짜 유령이라 해도, 이렇게 멀고 사람도 이리 많은데 사람한테 해를 끼칠 리 없지요. 그래도 내가 겁먹었다면, 겁먹은 게 나 혼자만은 아니오.\""], ["“Why, who,”", "\"아니,\""], ["cries Jones, “dost thou take to be such a coward here besides thyself?”", "존스가 외쳤다, \"여기서 너 말고 또 누가 그런 겁쟁이라는 거냐?\""], ["“Nay, you may call me coward if you will; but if that little man there upon the stage is not frightened, I never saw any man frightened in my life.", "\"아니, 날 겁쟁이라 부르고 싶으면 그러시구려. 하지만 저기 무대 위 저 작은 사람이 겁먹은 게 아니라면, 나는 평생 겁먹은 사람을 본 적이 없는 거요."], ["Ay, ay: go along with you: Ay, to be sure!", "그래, 그래, 어서 가 보시구려. 그래, 정말이지!"], ["Who's fool then?", "그럼 누가 바보요?"], ["Will you?", "가시겠다고?"], ["Lud have mercy upon such fool-hardiness!--Whatever happens, it is good enough for you.----Follow you?", "저런 무모한 짓에 주여 자비를 베푸소서!—무슨 일이 닥치든, 그래도 싸오.—따라가라고?"], ["I'd follow the devil as soon.", "차라리 악마를 따라가지."], ["Nay, perhaps it is the devil----for they say he can put on what likeness he pleases.--Oh!", "아니, 어쩌면 저게 악마일지도 모르오—듣자 하니 악마는 제멋대로 아무 모습이나 꾸며 낼 수 있다고 하니까.—아!"], ["here he is again.----No farther!", "또 나왔다.—더는 안 돼!"], ["No, you have gone far enough already; farther than I'd have gone for all the king's dominions.”", "안 돼, 이미 충분히 멀리 갔소. 임금님의 온 영토를 다 준대도 내가 갈 만한 데보다 더 멀리 갔단 말이오.\""], ["Jones offered to speak, but Partridge cried “Hush, hush!", "존스가 무슨 말을 하려 하자, 파트리지가 외쳤다. \"쉿, 쉿!"], ["dear sir, don't you hear him?”", "선생, 저 소리 안 들리오?\""], ["And during the whole speech of the ghost, he sat with his eyes fixed partly on the ghost and partly on Hamlet, and with his mouth open; the same passions which succeeded each other in Hamlet, succeeding likewise in him.", "그러고는 유령이 말하는 내내, 눈은 반쯤 유령에, 반쯤 햄릿에 고정한 채 입을 떡 벌리고 앉아 있었다. 햄릿에게서 차례로 일어나는 그 감정들이 그에게서도 똑같이 일어났던 것이다."]], [["When the scene was over Jones said, “Why, Partridge, you exceed my expectations.", "그 장면이 끝나자 존스가 말했다. \"이런, 파트리지, 자네는 내 기대를 뛰어넘는군."], ["You enjoy the play more than I conceived possible.”", "내가 가능하리라 여긴 것보다 훨씬 더 연극을 즐기고 있어.\" \"아니, 선생,\""], ["“Nay, sir,”", "파트리지가 답했다, \"선생이 악마가 무섭지 않다면, 그거야 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이지요. 하지만 분명 그런 것들에 놀라는 건 사람으로서 자연스러운 일이오."], ["answered Partridge, “if you are not afraid of the devil, I can't help it; but to be sure, it is natural to be surprized at such things, though I know there is nothing in them: not that it was the ghost that surprized me, neither; for I should have known that to have been only a man in a strange dress; but when I saw the little man so frightened himself, it was that which took hold of me.”", "그 안에 아무것도 없다는 걸 알면서도 말이오. 사실 나를 놀라게 한 건 유령이 아니었소."], ["“And dost thou imagine, then, Partridge,”", "그거야 그저 이상한 옷차림을 한 사람일 뿐이라고 알아챘을 테니까. 다만 저 작은 사람이 그토록 겁먹는 걸 보니, 바로 그게 나를 사로잡은 거요.\""], ["cries Jones, “that he was really frightened?”", "\"그럼 자네는,\" 존스가 외쳤다, \"저 사람이 정말로 겁먹었다고 생각하나, 파트리지?\""], ["“Nay, sir,”", "\"아니, 선생,\" 파트리지가 말했다, \"선생도 나중에 보지 않았소?"], ["said Partridge, “did not you yourself observe afterwards, when he found it was his own father's spirit, and how he was murdered in the garden, how his fear forsook him by degrees, and he was struck dumb with sorrow, as it were, just as I should have been, had it been my own case?--But hush!", "그게 자기 아버지의 혼령이고, 정원에서 어떻게 살해당했는지 알게 되자, 두려움이 차츰 가시고 슬픔에 그만 말문이 막혀 버린 것을. 꼭 내가 그 처지였다면 그랬을 것처럼 말이오.—그런데 쉿!"], ["O la!", "아이고!"], ["what noise is that?", "저 소리는 뭐요?"], ["There he is again.----Well, to be certain, though I know there is nothing at all in it, I am glad I am not down yonder, where those men are.”", "또 나왔다.—거참, 그 안에 아무것도 없는 줄 알면서도, 저 사람들 있는 저 아래쪽에 내가 있지 않아서 다행이오.\""], ["Then turning his eyes again upon Hamlet, “Ay, you may draw your sword; what signifies a sword against the power of the devil?”", "그러고는 다시 눈을 햄릿에게 돌리며, \"그래, 칼을 뽑으시구려. 악마의 힘 앞에 칼이 무슨 소용이람?\""]], [["During the second act, Partridge made very few remarks.", "2막 동안 파트리지는 별다른 말을 거의 하지 않았다."], ["He greatly admired the fineness of the dresses; nor could he help observing upon the king's countenance.", "의상의 화려함에는 크게 감탄했고, 왕의 표정에 대해서도 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“Well,”", "\"거참,\""], ["said he, “how people may be deceived by faces!", "그가 말했다, \"사람이 얼굴에 얼마나 잘 속는지!"], ["Nulla fides fronti is, I find, a true saying.", "'얼굴은 믿을 게 못 된다'(Nulla fides fronti)는 말이 참말이구려."], ["Who would think, by looking in the king's face, that he had ever committed a murder?”", "저 왕의 얼굴을 보고서, 저 사람이 살인을 저질렀으리라고 누가 생각하겠소?\""], ["He then enquired after the ghost; but Jones, who intended he should be surprized, gave him no other satisfaction, than, “that he might possibly see him again soon, and in a flash of fire.”", "그러고는 유령에 관해 물었으나, 그를 놀라게 할 작정이었던 존스는 다른 만족은 주지 않고 다만, \"어쩌면 곧 다시, 그것도 불길 속에서 그를 보게 될지도 모른다\"고만 했다."]], [["Partridge sat in a fearful expectation of this; and now, when the ghost made his next appearance, Partridge cried out, “There, sir, now; what say you now?", "파트리지는 이것을 겁에 질려 기다리며 앉아 있었다. 그러다 유령이 다음번에 등장하자, 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["is he frightened now or no?", "\"저기, 선생, 자 이제, 뭐라 하시겠소?"], ["As much frightened as you think me, and, to be sure, nobody can help some fears.", "저 사람이 이제 겁먹은 거요, 아니오? 선생이 나더러 겁먹었다 한 만큼이나 겁먹었구려."], ["I would not be in so bad a condition as what's his name, squire Hamlet, is there, for all the world.", "그래, 정말이지 사람이 좀 무서운 거야 어쩔 수 없지요. 나는 저기 저, 이름이 뭐더라, 햄릿 도령이 처한 저 딱한 꼴이 되느니 차라리 온 세상을 마다하겠소."], ["Bless me!", "아이고!"], ["what's become of the spirit?", "혼령은 어디로 갔담?"], ["As I am a living soul, I thought I saw him sink into the earth.”", "살아 있는 내 영혼을 두고 맹세컨대, 저게 땅속으로 가라앉는 걸 본 것 같소.\" \"정말로 제대로 봤소,\""], ["“Indeed, you saw right,”", "존스가 답했다. \"그래, 그래,\""], ["answered Jones. “Well, well,”", "파트리지가 외쳤다, \"그저 연극인 줄 아오."], ["cries Partridge, “I know it is only a play: and besides, if there was anything in all this, Madam Miller would not laugh so; for as to you, sir, you would not be afraid, I believe, if the devil was here in person.--There, there--Ay, no wonder you are in such a passion, shake the vile wicked wretch to pieces.", "게다가 이 모든 게 정말 무슨 일이라면 밀러 부인이 저렇게 웃으실 리 없지. 선생으로 말하자면, 악마가 직접 여기 와 있다 해도 무서워하지 않으리라 믿소만.—저기, 저기—그래, 저렇게 길길이 날뛰는 것도 무리가 아니지, 저 더럽고 사악한 계집을 박살을 내시구려."], ["If she was my own mother, I would serve her so.", "저게 내 친어미라 해도 나는 똑같이 다루겠소."], ["To be sure all duty to a mother is forfeited by such wicked doings.----Ay, go about your business, I hate the sight of you.”", "그래, 저런 사악한 짓을 저질렀으면 어미에 대한 효도고 뭐고 다 끝장난 거지.—그래, 어서 네 볼일이나 보러 가라, 네 꼴도 보기 싫다.\""]], [["Our critic was now pretty silent till the play, which Hamlet introduces before the king.", "우리의 비평가는 이제 햄릿이 왕 앞에서 선보이는 극중극이 나올 때까지 꽤 잠잠했다."], ["This he did not at first understand, till Jones explained it to him; but he no sooner entered into the spirit of it, than he began to bless himself that he had never committed murder.", "그는 처음에는 이것을 이해하지 못했으나 존스가 설명해 주었고, 그 의미를 알아차리자마자 자기는 평생 살인을 저지른 적이 없다며 가슴을 쓸어내리기 시작했다."], ["Then turning to Mrs Miller, he asked her, “If she did not imagine the king looked as if he was touched; though he is,”", "그러고는 밀러 부인을 돌아보며 물었다. \"저 왕이 마음에 찔린 사람처럼 보이지 않으셨소?"], ["said he, “a good actor, and doth all he can to hide it.", "비록,\" 그가 말했다, \"훌륭한 배우라 그걸 감추려고 갖은 애를 다 쓰긴 하지만 말이오."], ["Well, I would not have so much to answer for, as that wicked man there hath, to sit upon a much higher chair than he sits upon.", "거참, 나라면 저기 저 사악한 사람이 짊어진 만큼의 죄책을 짊어지느니, 차라리 그가 앉은 자리보다 훨씬 높은 자리에 앉기를 마다하겠소."], ["No wonder he ran away; for your sake I'll never trust an innocent face again.”", "저 사람이 달아난 것도 무리가 아니지. 선생 덕분에 나는 다시는 순진한 얼굴은 믿지 않겠소.\""]], [["The grave-digging scene next engaged the attention of Partridge, who expressed much surprize at the number of skulls thrown upon the stage.", "다음으로 무덤 파는 장면이 파트리지의 주의를 끌었는데, 그는 무대 위로 던져지는 해골의 수효에 크게 놀랐다."], ["To which Jones answered, “That it was one of the most famous burial-places about town.”", "그 말에 존스가 답했다. \"여기가 이 도시에서 가장 이름난 묘지 가운데 하나라오.\""], ["“No wonder then,”", "\"그러면 그렇지,\""], ["cries Partridge, “that the place is haunted.", "파트리지가 외쳤다, \"그러니 이 자리에 귀신이 드는 게지."], ["But I never saw in my life a worse grave-digger.", "그런데 저렇게 서툰 무덤지기는 평생 본 적이 없소."], ["I had a sexton, when I was clerk, that should have dug three graves while he is digging one.", "내가 교회 서기였을 적에 데리고 있던 묘지기라면, 저 사람이 하나 파는 동안 셋은 팠을 거요."], ["The fellow handles a spade as if it was the first time he had ever had one in his hand.", "저 사람이 삽을 다루는 꼴이라니, 꼭 평생 처음 삽을 손에 쥐어 본 사람 같구려."], ["Ay, ay, you may sing.", "그래, 그래, 노래나 부르시게."], ["You had rather sing than work, I believe.”--Upon Hamlet's taking up the skull, he cried out, “Well!", "일하느니 노래가 낫다, 이거겠지.\"—햄릿이 해골을 집어 들자, 그가 외쳤다. \"거참!"], ["it is strange to see how fearless some men are: I never could bring myself to touch anything belonging to a dead man, on any account.--He seemed frightened enough too at the ghost, I thought.", "어떤 사람들은 어쩌면 저리도 겁이 없는지 신기하오. 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 죽은 사람한테 딸린 건 도무지 손도 못 댄단 말이오.—그런데 아까 유령 앞에서는 저 사람도 꽤 겁먹은 것 같던데."], ["Nemo omnibus horis sapit.”", "'아무도 매 순간 현명하지는 못한 법'(Nemo omnibus horis sapit)이지.\""]], [["Little more worth remembering occurred during the play, at the end of which Jones asked him, “Which of the players he had liked best?”", "연극이 진행되는 동안 더 기억할 만한 일은 별로 없었고, 연극이 끝나자 존스가 그에게 물었다. \"배우들 가운데 누가 제일 마음에 들었나?\""], ["To this he answered, with some appearance of indignation at the question, “The king, without doubt.”", "이 물음에 그는 질문을 다소 못마땅해하는 기색으로 답했다. \"두말할 것 없이 왕이지요.\""], ["“Indeed, Mr Partridge,”", "\"이런, 파트리지 씨,\""], ["says Mrs Miller, “you are not of the same opinion with the town; for they are all agreed, that Hamlet is acted by the best player who ever was on the stage.”", "밀러 부인이 말했다, \"당신은 세상 사람들과 의견이 다르군요. 다들 햄릿을 연기한 이가 지금껏 무대에 선 배우 가운데 최고라는 데 의견이 일치하는데 말이에요.\""], ["“He the best player!”", "\"그 사람이 최고 배우라고요!\""], ["cries Partridge, with a contemptuous sneer, “why, I could act as well as he myself.", "파트리지가 멸시 어린 비웃음을 띠며 외쳤다, \"원, 그 정도야 나도 그만큼은 하겠소."], ["I am sure, if I had seen a ghost, I should have looked in the very same manner, and done just as he did.", "분명 나도 유령을 봤다면 꼭 그와 똑같은 표정을 짓고, 그가 한 그대로 했을 거요. 그리고 또, 부인이 말한 그 장면, 그가 어머니와 함께 있던, 그가 그토록 멋지게 연기했다던 그 장면 말이오."], ["And then, to be sure, in that scene, as you called it, between him and his mother, where you told me he acted so fine, why, Lord help me, any man, that is, any good man, that had such a mother, would have done exactly the same.", "원, 맙소사, 어떤 사람이든, 그러니까 어떤 선량한 사람이든, 그런 어머니를 두었다면 누구나 똑같이 했을 거요. 부인이 나를 놀리는 줄 알지만, 정말이지, 부인, 비록 런던에서 연극을 본 적은 없어도 시골에서 연기라는 걸 본 적은 있소."], ["I know you are only joking with me; but indeed, madam, though I was never at a play in London, yet I have seen acting before in the country; and the king for my money; he speaks all his words distinctly, half as loud again as the other.--Anybody may see he is an actor.”", "그러니 내 돈을 걸자면 왕이오. 그는 말을 죄다 또박또박하게, 다른 사람보다 곱절은 크게 하지 않소.—누가 봐도 저 사람이 진짜 배우인 게지.\""]], [["While Mrs Miller was thus engaged in conversation with Partridge, a lady came up to Mr Jones, whom he immediately knew to be Mrs Fitzpatrick.", "밀러 부인이 이렇게 파트리지와 이야기를 나누는 동안, 한 부인이 존스 씨에게 다가왔는데, 그는 곧 그 부인이 피츠패트릭 부인임을 알아보았다."], ["She said, she had seen him from the other part of the gallery, and had taken that opportunity of speaking to him, as she had something to say, which might be of great service to himself.", "그녀는 관람석 다른 편에서 그를 보았으며, 그에게 큰 도움이 될 만한 할 이야기가 있어 이 기회에 말을 걸게 되었다고 했다."], ["She then acquainted him with her lodgings, and made him an appointment the next day in the morning; which, upon recollection, she presently changed to the afternoon; at which time Jones promised to attend her.", "그러고는 그에게 자기 숙소를 알려 주고 이튿날 아침에 만나기로 약속을 잡았는데, 생각해 보더니 곧 그것을 오후로 바꾸었고, 존스는 그때 찾아뵙겠다고 약속했다."]], [["Thus ended the adventure at the playhouse; where Partridge had afforded great mirth, not only to Jones and Mrs Miller, but to all who sat within hearing, who were more attentive to what he said, than to anything that passed on the stage.", "이렇게 하여 극장에서의 모험은 끝났다. 그곳에서 파트리지는 존스와 밀러 부인뿐 아니라, 그의 말이 들리는 자리에 앉은 모든 이에게 큰 웃음을 안겨 주었으니, 그들은 무대에서 벌어지는 그 어떤 일보다 그가 하는 말에 더 귀를 기울였던 것이다."]], [["He durst not go to bed all that night, for fear of the ghost; and for many nights after sweated two or three hours before he went to sleep, with the same apprehensions, and waked several times in great horrors, crying out, “Lord have mercy upon us!", "그날 밤 그는 유령이 무서워 도무지 잠자리에 들지 못했고, 그 뒤로도 여러 밤 동안 똑같은 두려움에 잠들기 전 두세 시간씩 식은땀을 흘렸으며, 몇 번이나 큰 공포에 휩싸여 잠에서 깨어 외쳤다. \"주여, 우리에게 자비를 베푸소서!"], ["there it is.”", "저기 있다.\""]], [["In which the history is obliged to look back.", "아무리 훌륭한 부모라도 제 자식들에게 한 치의 치우침 없는 공평함을 지키기란 거의 불가능한 일이다."], ["It is almost impossible for the best parent to observe an exact impartiality to his children, even though no superior merit should bias his affection; but sure a parent can hardly be blamed, when that superiority determines his preference.", "어느 한쪽이 더 뛰어나서 애정이 기울 까닭이 전혀 없을 때조차 그러하다. 그러나 바로 그 뛰어남 때문에 부모가 한 자식을 더 아끼게 되는 것이라면, 그 부모를 탓하기란 어려운 노릇이다."]], [["As I regard all the personages of this history in the light of my children; so I must confess the same inclination of partiality to Sophia; and for that I hope the reader will allow me the same excuse, from the superiority of her character.", "나는 이 이야기에 등장하는 모든 인물을 내 자식처럼 여기고 있으니, 소피아에게 똑같은 치우침의 마음이 있음을 고백하지 않을 수 없다. 그리고 그 점에 대해서는, 그녀의 인품이 남달리 뛰어나다는 이유로 독자께서도 내게 똑같은 변명을 허락해 주시리라 믿는다."]], [["This extraordinary tenderness which I have for my heroine never suffers me to quit her any long time without the utmost reluctance.", "내 여주인공을 향한 이 유별난 애틋함은, 그녀 곁을 오래 떠나 있는 것을 도무지 견디지 못하게 만든다."], ["I could now, therefore, return impatiently to enquire what hath happened to this lovely creature since her departure from her father's, but that I am obliged first to pay a short visit to Mr Blifil.", "그러니 나는 지금이라도 조바심을 내며 그녀에게로 돌아가, 아버지의 집을 떠난 뒤로 이 사랑스러운 사람에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알아보고 싶다. 다만 그 전에 블리필 군을 잠깐 찾아가 보지 않을 수 없다."]], [["Mr Western, in the first confusion into which his mind was cast upon the sudden news he received of his daughter, and in the first hurry to go after her, had not once thought of sending any account of the discovery to Blifil.", "웨스턴 씨는 딸에 관한 갑작스러운 소식을 듣고 마음이 처음 그 혼란에 빠졌을 때, 또 그녀를 뒤쫓아 가려는 첫 황급함 속에서, 그 사실을 블리필에게 알릴 생각은 한 번도 하지 못했다."], ["He had not gone far, however, before he recollected himself, and accordingly stopt at the very first inn he came to, and dispatched away a messenger to acquaint Blifil with his having found Sophia, and with his firm resolution to marry her to him immediately, if he would come up after him to town.", "그러나 그리 멀리 가지 못해 정신을 차렸고, 가장 먼저 눈에 띈 여관에 멈추어 블리필에게 사람을 보냈다. 소피아를 찾았다는 것과, 그가 자기를 따라 시내로 올라온다면 즉시 그녀를 그에게 시집보내겠다는 굳은 결심을 전하기 위해서였다."]], [["As the love which Blifil had for Sophia was of that violent kind, which nothing but the loss of her fortune, or some such accident, could lessen, his inclination to the match was not at all altered by her having run away, though he was obliged to lay this to his own account.", "블리필이 소피아에게 품은 사랑이란, 그녀의 재산을 잃는다든가 그런 종류의 사고가 아니고서는 결코 줄어들지 않는 그 격렬한 종류의 것이었으므로, 그녀가 달아났다 하여 이 혼사에 대한 그의 마음은 조금도 변하지 않았다. 비록 그 일을 제 탓으로 돌릴 수밖에 없는 처지였는데도 말이다."], ["He very readily, therefore, embraced this offer.", "그래서 그는 이 제안을 아주 기꺼이 받아들였다. 사실 그는 이제 이 아가씨와 혼인함으로써 탐욕 말고도 또 하나의 매우 강한 욕정을 채우려 했으니, 그것은 바로 증오였다."], ["Indeed, he now proposed the gratification of a very strong passion besides avarice, by marrying this young lady, and this was hatred; for he concluded that matrimony afforded an equal opportunity of satisfying either hatred or love; and this opinion is very probably verified by much experience.", "혼인이란 증오든 사랑이든 어느 쪽이든 똑같이 만족시킬 기회를 준다고 그는 결론지었던 것이다. 그리고 이 견해는 숱한 경험으로 충분히 입증되는 듯하다."], ["To say the truth, if we are to judge by the ordinary behaviour of married persons to each other, we shall perhaps be apt to conclude that the generality seek the indulgence of the former passion only, in their union of everything but of hearts.", "솔직히 말해, 혼인한 사람들이 서로에게 보이는 흔한 행태로 미루어 판단한다면, 우리는 아마 이렇게 결론짓고 싶어질 것이다. 대다수가 마음만 빼고 모든 것을 하나로 합치는 그 결합 속에서, 오직 앞엣것의 욕정을 채우려 들 뿐이라고."]], [["There was one difficulty, however, in his way, and this arose from Mr Allworthy.", "그러나 그의 앞길에는 한 가지 어려움이 놓여 있었으니, 그것은 올워디 씨에게서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["That good man, when he found by the departure of Sophia (for neither that, nor the cause of it, could be concealed from him), the great aversion which she had for his nephew, began to be seriously concerned that he had been deceived into carrying matters so far.", "그 선량한 사람은 소피아의 출분으로(그 일도, 그 까닭도 그에게 숨길 수는 없었으므로) 그녀가 자기 조카를 몹시 싫어한다는 것을 알게 되자, 자신이 속아 일을 이토록 멀리까지 끌고 왔다는 데 진심으로 마음이 무거워지기 시작했다."], ["He by no means concurred with the opinion of those parents, who think it as immaterial to consult the inclinations of their children in the affair of marriage, as to solicit the good pleasure of their servants when they intend to take a journey; and who are by law, or decency at least, withheld often from using absolute force.", "그는 결코 그런 부모들의 견해에 동의하지 않았다. 그런 부모들은 혼인 문제에서 자식의 마음을 묻는 일을, 여행을 떠나려 할 때 하인의 기분을 살피는 것만큼이나 하찮게 여기며, 법이나 적어도 체면 때문에 절대적인 강요만은 못 하는 경우가 많은 자들이다."], ["On the contrary, as he esteemed the institution to be of the most sacred kind, he thought every preparatory caution necessary to preserve it holy and inviolate; and very wisely concluded, that the surest way to effect this was by laying the foundation in previous affection.", "오히려 그는 이 제도를 더없이 신성한 종류의 것으로 존중했기에, 그것을 거룩하고 침범할 수 없는 것으로 지키기 위해서는 사전의 온갖 신중함이 필요하다고 여겼다. 그리고 매우 현명하게도, 그것을 이루는 가장 확실한 길은 미리 싹튼 애정 위에 그 토대를 놓는 것이라고 결론지었다."]], [["Blifil indeed soon cured his uncle of all anger on the score of deceit, by many vows and protestations that he had been deceived himself, with which the many declarations of Western very well tallied; but now to persuade Allworthy to consent to the renewing his addresses was a matter of such apparent difficulty, that the very appearance was sufficient to have deterred a less enterprizing genius; but this young gentleman so well knew his own talents, that nothing within the province of cunning seemed to him hard to be achieved.", "블리필은 자기 또한 속았다는 숱한 맹세와 항변으로, 속임수 문제에 관한 한 삼촌의 노여움을 곧 말끔히 풀어 주었다. 웨스턴의 여러 진술도 그 말과 잘 들어맞았다. 그러나 이제 다시 구애를 시작하는 것에 올워디가 동의하도록 설득하는 일은 겉보기에도 워낙 어려워 보여서, 그 겉모습만으로도 덜 진취적인 사람이라면 단념하기에 충분했을 것이다. 그러나 이 젊은 신사는 제 재주를 너무도 잘 알았기에, 교활함의 영역에 속하는 일이라면 무엇이든 이루기 어렵게 보이지 않았다."]], [["Here then he represented the violence of his own affection, and the hopes of subduing aversion in the lady by perseverance.", "그리하여 여기서 그는 제 애정의 격렬함과, 끈기로 아가씨의 반감을 누그러뜨리리라는 희망을 늘어놓았다."], ["He begged that, in an affair on which depended all his future repose, he might at least be at liberty to try all fair means for success.", "그는 자신의 앞날의 평온이 모두 걸린 이 일에서, 적어도 성공을 위한 온갖 떳떳한 수단만은 자유로이 시도할 수 있게 해 달라고 간청했다."], ["Heaven forbid, he said, that he should ever think of prevailing by any other than the most gentle methods!", "그는 가장 부드러운 방법이 아닌 다른 수단으로 그녀를 차지할 생각은 결코 하지 않겠노라고, 하늘이 그것을 막아 주시기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["“Besides, sir,”", "\"게다가 삼촌,\""], ["said he, “if they fail, you may then (which will be surely time enough) deny your consent.”", "그가 말했다. \"그 방법들이 실패하면, 그때 가서(분명 그래도 늦지 않을 겁니다) 동의를 거두시면 되지 않겠습니까.\""], ["He urged the great and eager desire which Mr Western had for the match; and lastly, he made great use of the name of Jones, to whom he imputed all that had happened; and from whom, he said, to preserve so valuable a young lady was even an act of charity.", "그는 웨스턴 씨가 이 혼사를 얼마나 크고 간절하게 바라는지를 역설했다. 그리고 마지막으로 그는 존스의 이름을 크게 활용하여, 일어난 모든 일을 그에게 뒤집어씌웠고, 이토록 귀한 아가씨를 그자에게서 지키는 것이야말로 자선의 행위라고까지 말했다."]], [["All these arguments were well seconded by Thwackum, who dwelt a little stronger on the authority of parents than Mr Blifil himself had done.", "이 모든 논변은 스왜컴이 든든히 거들어 주었는데, 그는 블리필 본인보다도 부모의 권위를 한층 강하게 강조했다."], ["He ascribed the measures which Mr Blifil was desirous to take to Christian motives; “and though,”", "그는 블리필이 취하고자 하는 조치들을 기독교적 동기로 돌렸다. \"그리고 비록,\""], ["says he, “the good young gentleman hath mentioned charity last, I am almost convinced it is his first and principal consideration.”", "그가 말했다. \"이 착한 젊은 신사가 자선을 맨 마지막에 들먹이긴 했습니다만, 저는 그것이야말로 그의 첫째가는 으뜸 고려라고 거의 확신합니다.\""]], [["Square, possibly, had he been present, would have sung to the same tune, though in a different key, and would have discovered much moral fitness in the proceeding: but he was now gone to Bath for the recovery of his health.", "스퀘어도, 만일 그 자리에 있었더라면, 가락은 다르되 같은 곡조를 노래했을 것이고, 이 일에서 도덕적으로 합당한 점을 많이 찾아냈을 것이다. 그러나 그는 이제 건강을 회복하러 바스로 가 있었다."]], [["Allworthy, though not without reluctance, at last yielded to the desires of his nephew.", "올워디는 내키지 않으면서도 마침내 조카의 바람에 굴복했다. 그는 조카와 함께 런던으로 가겠다고, 그곳에서 조카가 아가씨의 마음을 얻기 위해 온갖 정직한 노력을 자유로이 기울여도 좋다고 말했다."], ["He said he would accompany him to London, where he might be at liberty to use every honest endeavour to gain the lady: “But I declare,”", "\"허나 분명히 말해 두마.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I will never give my consent to any absolute force being put on her inclinations, nor shall you ever have her, unless she can be brought freely to compliance.”", "\"나는 그 아이의 마음에 어떤 절대적인 강요를 가하는 데에는 결코 동의하지 않을 것이다. 그 아이가 스스로 우러나 응하지 않는 한, 너는 결코 그 아이를 얻지 못할 것이다.\""]], [["Thus did the affection of Allworthy for his nephew betray the superior understanding to be triumphed over by the inferior; and thus is the prudence of the best of heads often defeated by the tenderness of the best of hearts.", "이렇게 하여 조카를 향한 올워디의 애정은, 뛰어난 분별이 못한 분별에게 압도당하도록 내어 주고 말았다. 그리고 이렇게, 가장 훌륭한 머리의 신중함은 흔히 가장 훌륭한 가슴의 다정함에게 패하고 마는 것이다."]], [["Blifil, having obtained this unhoped-for acquiescence in his uncle, rested not till he carried his purpose into execution.", "삼촌에게서 이 뜻밖의 묵인을 얻어 낸 블리필은, 제 목적을 실행에 옮기기 전까지는 한시도 가만히 있지 않았다."], ["And as no immediate business required Mr Allworthy's presence in the country, and little preparation is necessary to men for a journey, they set out the very next day, and arrived in town that evening, when Mr Jones, as we have seen, was diverting himself with Partridge at the play.", "올워디 씨가 시골에 있어야 할 급한 용무도 없었고, 사람이 여행을 떠나는 데에는 채비할 것도 별로 없었으므로, 두 사람은 바로 이튿날 길을 나서 그날 저녁 시내에 닿았다. 우리가 앞서 본 대로, 존스 씨가 파트리지와 함께 연극을 보며 즐기고 있던 바로 그 무렵이었다."]], [["The morning after his arrival Mr Blifil waited on Mr Western, by whom he was most kindly and graciously received, and from whom he had every possible assurance (perhaps more than was possible) that he should very shortly be as happy as Sophia could make him; nor would the squire suffer the young gentleman to return to his uncle till he had, almost against his will, carried him to his sister.", "블리필 씨는 도착한 다음 날 아침 웨스턴 씨를 찾아갔고, 그에게서 더없이 친절하고 자애로운 환대를 받았다. 또한 그에게서, 머지않아 소피아가 안겨 줄 수 있는 만큼 행복해지리라는 온갖 가능한 다짐을(어쩌면 가능한 것 이상의 다짐을) 받았다. 게다가 이 지주는 젊은 신사를 거의 그의 뜻을 거스르다시피 하여 제 누이에게로 데려가기 전까지는, 그를 삼촌에게 돌려보내려 하지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter vii.", "# Chapter vii."]], [["In which Mr Western pays a visit to his sister, in company with Mr Blifil.", "웨스턴 부인은 조카에게 신중함과 결혼의 정략에 관한 일장 훈계를 늘어놓고 있었다."], ["Mrs Western was reading a lecture on prudence, and matrimonial politics, to her niece, when her brother and Blifil broke in with less ceremony than the laws of visiting require.", "그때 오라비와 블리필이, 방문의 예법이 요구하는 것보다 한참 격식을 덜 차린 채 불쑥 들이닥쳤다."], ["Sophia no sooner saw Blifil than she turned pale, and almost lost the use of all her faculties; but her aunt, on the contrary, waxed red, and, having all her faculties at command, began to exert her tongue on the squire.", "소피아는 블리필을 보자마자 얼굴이 창백해지더니 거의 모든 감각을 잃을 지경이 되었다. 반면에 그녀의 고모는 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아올랐고, 자신의 모든 감각을 여전히 또렷이 부리며 향사를 향해 혀를 놀리기 시작했다."]], [["“Brother,”", "\"오라버니,\""], ["said she, “I am astonished at your behaviour; will you never learn any regard to decorum?", "그녀가 말했다. \"오라버니 행실에 정말이지 기가 막힙니다."], ["Will you still look upon every apartment as your own, or as belonging to one of your country tenants?", "끝내 예의범절이라곤 배우지 못하실 작정인가요? 아직도 모든 방을 제 것으로, 아니면 시골 소작인 하나의 것쯤으로 여기시는 건가요?"], ["Do you think yourself at liberty to invade the privacies of women of condition, without the least decency or notice?”----“Why, what a pox is the matter now?”", "지체 있는 여인들의 사적인 공간을 일말의 예의도, 기별도 없이 함부로 침범해도 된다고 생각하시는 건가요?\"——\"아니, 도대체 이번엔 또 무슨 난리야?\" 향사가 말했다."], ["quoth the squire; “one would think I had caught you at--“--“None of your brutality, sir, I beseech you,”", "\"마치 내가 너를 무슨 현장에서 잡기라도——\"——\"제발 그 막돼먹은 소리 좀 그만두세요, 오라버니.\""], ["answered she.----“You have surprized my poor niece so, that she can hardly, I see, support herself.----Go, my dear, retire, and endeavour to recruit your spirits; for I see you have occasion.”", "그녀가 대답했다.——\"오라버니가 가엾은 제 조카를 어찌나 놀라게 했는지, 저 애가 제 몸 하나 가누기도 힘들어하는 게 보이지 않나요.——가거라, 얘야, 물러가서 마음을 좀 추슬러 보렴. 너에게 그게 필요한 게 보이는구나.\""], ["At which words Sophia, who never received a more welcome command, hastily withdrew.", "이 말이 떨어지자, 이보다 더 반가운 명령을 받아 본 적 없던 소피아는 황급히 자리를 떴다."]], [["“To be sure, sister,”", "\"이거 참, 누이,\""], ["cries the squire, “you are mad, when I have brought Mr Blifil here to court her, to force her away.”", "향사가 외쳤다. \"내가 저 애한테 청혼하라고 블리필 군을 여기까지 데려왔는데, 저 애를 쫓아내다니, 너 제정신이 아니구나.\""]], [["“Sure, brother,”", "\"제정신이 아닌 건 오라버니죠,\""], ["says she, “you are worse than mad, when you know in what situation affairs are, to----I am sure I ask Mr Blifil's pardon, but he knows very well to whom to impute so disagreeable a reception.", "그녀가 말했다. \"일이 지금 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지 뻔히 알면서도——블리필 군께는 정말 죄송합니다만, 그분도 이렇게 불쾌한 대접을 누구 탓으로 돌려야 할지 잘 아실 겁니다."], ["For my own part, I am sure I shall always be very glad to see Mr Blifil; but his own good sense would not have suffered him to proceed so abruptly, had you not compelled him to it.”", "저로 말하자면, 블리필 군을 뵙는 것은 언제든 더없이 기쁜 일이지요. 하지만 오라버니가 그분을 억지로 떠밀지만 않았던들, 그분의 분별력으로 이렇게 불쑥 일을 밀어붙이는 짓은 하지 않으셨을 거예요.\""]], [["Blifil bowed and stammered, and looked like a fool; but Western, without giving him time to form a speech for the purpose, answered, “Well, well, I am to blame, if you will, I always am, certainly; but come, let the girl be fetched back again, or let Mr Blifil go to her.----He's come up on purpose, and there is no time to be lost.”", "블리필은 허리를 굽혀 절하고 더듬거리며 바보처럼 서 있었다. 그러나 웨스턴은 그가 그럴듯한 말을 꾸며 낼 틈도 주지 않고 대답했다. \"그래, 그래, 정 그렇다면 내 탓이지, 늘 내 탓이고말고, 암. 그건 그렇고, 어서 그 애를 다시 데려오게 하든지, 아니면 블리필 군이 그 애한테 가게 하든지 하자고.——그가 일부러 올라온 마당에, 한시도 허비할 시간이 없단 말이다.\""]], [["“Brother,”", "\"오라버니,\""], ["cries Mrs Western, “Mr Blifil, I am confident, understands himself better than to think of seeing my niece any more this morning, after what hath happened.", "웨스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"블리필 군은 분별 있는 분이니, 이런 일이 있은 마당에 오늘 아침에 제 조카를 다시 만날 생각 따위는 하지 않으실 거예요."], ["Women are of a nice contexture; and our spirits, when disordered, are not to be recomposed in a moment.", "여자란 섬세한 결로 짜여 있는 존재라, 한번 마음이 흐트러지면 한순간에 다시 추슬러지는 게 아니랍니다."], ["Had you suffered Mr Blifil to have sent his compliments to my niece, and to have desired the favour of waiting on her in the afternoon, I should possibly have prevailed on her to have seen him; but now I despair of bringing about any such matter.”", "만약 오라버니가 블리필 군더러 제 조카에게 안부 인사만 전하고 오후에 찾아뵐 영광을 청하도록 두셨더라면, 어쩌면 제가 그 애를 설득해 그분을 만나게 했을지도 모르지요. 하지만 이제는 그런 일을 성사시키기란 틀렸다고 봅니다.\""]], [["“I am very sorry, madam,”", "\"정말 죄송합니다, 부인,\" 블리필이 외쳤다."], ["cried Blifil, “that Mr Western's extraordinary kindness to me, which I can never enough acknowledge, should have occasioned--”", "\"결코 다 갚을 수 없는 웨스턴 씨의 각별한 호의가 도리어 이런 일을 빚어내고 말았으니——\" \"정말이지, 선생,\""], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "그녀가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다."], ["said she, interrupting him, “you need make no apologies, we all know my brother so well.”", "\"사과하실 필요 없습니다. 우리 모두 제 오라버니를 워낙 잘 아니까요.\""]], [["“I don't care what anybody knows of me,”", "\"누가 나를 어찌 알든 난 상관없어,\""], ["answered the squire;----“but when must he come to see her?", "향사가 대답했다.——\"하지만 그가 언제 그 애를 보러 와야 하느냐 말이다."], ["for, consider, I tell you, he is come up on purpose, and so is Allworthy.”--“Brother,”", "생각 좀 해 봐라, 내 말하지 않느냐, 그가 일부러 올라왔고, 올워디도 마찬가지란 말이다.\"——\"오라버니,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “whatever message Mr Blifil thinks proper to send to my niece shall be delivered to her; and I suppose she will want no instructions to make a proper answer.", "\"블리필 군이 제 조카에게 보내는 게 마땅하다고 여기는 전갈이라면 그게 무엇이든 그 애에게 전해질 거예요. 또 그 애가 합당한 답을 하는 데 무슨 지시 따위는 필요 없으리라 생각합니다."], ["I am convinced she will not refuse to see Mr Blifil at a proper time.”--“The devil she won't!”", "적당한 때가 되면 그 애가 블리필 군 만나기를 거절하지 않으리라고 저는 확신해요.\"——\"거절하지 않는다고, 흥!\""], ["answered the squire.--“Odsbud!--Don't we know--I say nothing, but some volk are wiser than all the world.----If I might have had my will, she had not run away before: and now I expect to hear every moment she is guone again.", "향사가 대답했다.——\"맙소사!——우리가 모를 줄 아느냐——나야 아무 말 안 하지만, 어떤 사람들은 온 세상 사람보다 더 똑똑하단 말이지.——내 뜻대로 할 수 있었다면 그 애가 진작에 도망치지도 않았을 게다. 그런데 이제는 그 애가 또 달아났다는 소리를 시시각각 듣게 되리라 싶구나."], ["For as great a fool as some volk think me, I know very well she hates----”", "어떤 사람들이 나를 천하의 바보로 여기지만, 나도 저 애가 누굴 끔찍이 싫어하는지는——\""], ["“No matter, brother,”", "\"그만하세요, 오라버니,\""], ["replied Mrs Western, “I will not hear my niece abused.", "웨스턴 부인이 받아쳤다. \"제 조카가 헐뜯기는 소리는 듣지 않겠어요."], ["It is a reflection on my family.", "그건 우리 집안에 대한 모욕이에요."], ["She is an honour to it; and she will be an honour to it, I promise you.", "그 애는 집안의 자랑이고, 앞으로도 그러할 거예요, 제가 장담합니다. 그 애의 처신에 제 세상 명예를 통째로 걸겠어요.——오후에 다시 뵙는 건 반갑겠어요, 오라버니."], ["I will pawn my whole reputation in the world on her conduct.----I shall be glad to see you, brother, in the afternoon; for I have somewhat of importance to mention to you.--At present, Mr Blifil, as well as you, must excuse me; for I am in haste to dress.”", "긴히 말씀드릴 일이 좀 있으니까요.——지금은, 블리필 군도 그렇고 오라버니도, 양해해 주셔야겠어요. 옷매무새를 서둘러 갖춰야 해서요.\""], ["“Well, but,”", "\"그래, 그건 그렇고,\""], ["said the squire, “do appoint a time.”", "향사가 말했다. \"시간을 좀 정하란 말이다.\""], ["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said she, “I can appoint no time.", "그녀가 말했다. \"시간은 못 정해요."], ["I tell you I will see you in the afternoon.”--“What the devil would you have me do?”", "오후에 뵙겠다고 말씀드렸잖아요.\"——\"대체 날더러 어쩌란 말이냐?\" 향사가 블리필 쪽으로 돌아서며 외쳤다."], ["cries the squire, turning to Blifil; “I can no more turn her, than a beagle can turn an old hare.", "\"비글이 늙은 산토끼를 돌려세우지 못하듯, 나도 저 애를 도무지 돌려세울 수가 없네."], ["Perhaps she will be in a better humour in the afternoon.”--“I am condemned, I see, sir, to misfortune,”", "오후가 되면 어쩌면 기분이 좀 나아지겠지.\"——\"제가 보아하니, 선생, 저는 불운을 타고난 모양입니다,\""], ["answered Blifil; “but I shall always own my obligations to you.”", "블리필이 대답했다. \"하지만 선생께 진 신세만큼은 언제까지나 잊지 않겠습니다.\""], ["He then took a ceremonious leave of Mrs Western, who was altogether as ceremonious on her part; and then they departed, the squire muttering to himself with an oath, that Blifil should see his daughter in the afternoon.", "그러고 나서 그는 격식을 갖춰 웨스턴 부인에게 작별을 고했고, 부인 역시 자기 쪽에서 못지않게 격식을 차렸다. 그리하여 두 사람은 떠났는데, 향사는 오후에는 블리필이 제 딸을 보고야 말리라고 욕설을 섞어 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["If Mr Western was little pleased with this interview, Blifil was less.", "웨스턴 씨가 이 만남을 별로 달가워하지 않았다면, 블리필은 더더욱 그러했다."], ["As to the former, he imputed the whole behaviour of his sister to her humour only, and to her dissatisfaction at the omission of ceremony in the visit; but Blifil saw a little deeper into things.", "전자로 말하자면, 그는 누이의 그 모든 행동을 한낱 그녀의 변덕 탓으로, 그리고 방문의 예법이 빠진 것에 대한 불만 탓으로만 돌렸다. 그러나 블리필은 사정을 조금 더 깊이 꿰뚫어 보았다."], ["He suspected somewhat of more consequence, from two or three words which dropt from the lady; and, to say the truth, he suspected right, as will appear when I have unfolded the several matters which will be contained in the following chapter.", "그는 부인의 입에서 흘러나온 두세 마디 말에서 무언가 더 중대한 낌새를 의심했다. 그리고 사실대로 말하자면, 그의 의심은 옳았으니, 이는 다음 장에 담길 여러 사정을 내가 펼쳐 보일 때 드러날 것이다."]], [["Schemes of Lady Bellaston for the ruin of Jones.", "소피아를 파멸시키려는 벨라스턴 부인의 계략."]], [["Love had taken too deep a root in the mind of Lord Fellamar to be plucked up by the rude hands of Mr Western.", "펠라마 경의 마음속에 사랑은 너무 깊이 뿌리내려, 웨스턴 씨의 거친 손으로는 도저히 뽑아낼 수 없을 지경이었다. 분노가 끓어오르던 와중에 그는 실로 에글레인 대위에게 어떤 임무를 맡겼는데, 대위는 그 임무를 실행하면서 한참을 지나치게 넘어서고 말았다."], ["In the heat of resentment he had, indeed, given a commission to Captain Egglane, which the captain had far exceeded in the execution; nor had it been executed at all, had his lordship been able to find the captain after he had seen Lady Bellaston, which was in the afternoon of the day after he had received the affront; but so industrious was the captain in the discharge of his duty, that, having after long enquiry found out the squire's lodgings very late in the evening, he sat up all night at a tavern, that he might not miss the squire in the morning, and by that means missed the revocation which my lord had sent to his lodgings.", "게다가 경께서 벨라스턴 부인을 만난 뒤—그것은 모욕을 당한 다음 날 오후의 일이었다—대위를 찾을 수만 있었더라면, 그 임무는 아예 실행되지조차 않았을 것이다. 그러나 대위는 자기 임무를 수행하는 데 어찌나 부지런했던지, 오랜 수소문 끝에 그날 저녁 매우 늦게야 향사의 숙소를 알아내고는, 아침에 향사를 놓치지 않으려고 어느 선술집에서 밤을 꼬박 새웠고, 그 바람에 경께서 자기 숙소로 보내신 명령 철회를 받아 보지 못했던 것이다."]], [["In the afternoon then next after the intended rape of Sophia, his lordship, as we have said, made a visit to Lady Bellaston, who laid open so much of the character of the squire, that his lordship plainly saw the absurdity he had been guilty of in taking any offence at his words, especially as he had those honourable designs on his daughter.", "앞서 말했듯이, 소피아를 겁탈하려던 그 계획이 있었던 다음 날 오후, 경께서는 벨라스턴 부인을 찾아갔다. 부인은 향사의 성품을 어찌나 낱낱이 펼쳐 보였던지, 경은 그의 말 한마디에 노여움을 품었던 것이 얼마나 어처구니없는 짓이었는지를 분명히 깨달았다."], ["He then unbosomed the violence of his passion to Lady Bellaston, who readily undertook the cause, and encouraged him with certain assurance of a most favourable reception from all the elders of the family, and from the father himself when he should be sober, and should be made acquainted with the nature of the offer made to his daughter.", "더구나 그 딸에게 그토록 떳떳한 뜻을 품고 있는 처지였으니 말이다. 그는 자신의 격렬한 연정을 벨라스턴 부인에게 털어놓았고, 부인은 선뜻 그 일을 떠맡았다."], ["The only danger, she said, lay in the fellow she had formerly mentioned, who, though a beggar and a vagabond, had, by some means or other, she knew not what, procured himself tolerable cloaths, and past for a gentleman.", "그러고는 그에게, 집안의 모든 어른은 물론이요 술이 깨고 자기 딸에게 들어온 청혼이 어떤 것인지를 알게 되면 아버지 본인에게서도 더없이 호의적인 환대를 받으리라는 확실한 보장으로 그를 북돋아 주었다. 다만 한 가지 위험은, 전에 언급한 적 있는 그 사내에게 있다고 부인은 말했다."], ["“Now,”", "거지에다 떠돌이 신세이면서도, 무슨 수를 썼는지 알 수 없으나 어쨌든 그럴듯한 옷을 마련해 신사 행세를 하고 다니는 자였다. \"그래서요,\""], ["says she, “as I have, for the sake of my cousin, made it my business to enquire after this fellow, I have luckily found out his lodgings;”", "부인이 말했다. \"제 사촌을 생각해서 이 사내의 뒤를 캐는 일을 떠맡았더니, 다행히 그자의 숙소까지 알아냈답니다.\""], ["with which she then acquainted his lordship.", "그러고는 그 위치를 경께 일러 주었다. \"제 생각엔 말이죠, 경이시여,\""], ["“I am thinking, my lord,”", "부인이 덧붙였다. \"(이런 사내는 경께서 몸소 노여워하시기엔 너무 천한 자이니) 경께서 무슨 수를 짜내어 그자를 강제 징집해 배에 태워 보낼 수는 없을까요."], ["added she “(for this fellow is too mean for your personal resentment), whether it would not be possible for your lordship to contrive some method of having him pressed and sent on board a ship.", "이 계획은 법으로도 양심으로도 막을 것이 없습니다. 단언컨대 그 사내는 아무리 잘 차려입었다 한들 떠돌이에 지나지 않으니, 거리의 어느 사내 못지않게 강제로 군역에 끌려갈 만한 자이지요."], ["Neither law nor conscience forbid this project: for the fellow, I promise you, however well drest, is but a vagabond, and as proper as any fellow in the streets to be pressed into the service; and as for the conscientious part, surely the preservation of a young lady from such ruin is a most meritorious act; nay, with regard to the fellow himself, unless he could succeed (which Heaven forbid) with my cousin, it may probably be the means of preserving him from the gallows, and perhaps may make his fortune in an honest way.”", "양심에 관한 문제로 말하자면, 한 젊은 숙녀를 그런 파멸에서 지켜 내는 일이야말로 더없이 갸륵한 행위가 아니겠습니까. 아니, 그 사내 자신을 두고 보더라도, 제 사촌과 일이 잘 풀리지만 않는다면(하늘이 막아 주시기를) 이는 어쩌면 그자를 교수대에서 구해 주는 길이 될 수도 있고, 어쩌면 정직한 방식으로 그의 앞날을 열어 주는 수도 될 것입니다.\""]], [["Lord Fellamar very heartily thanked her ladyship for the part which she was pleased to take in the affair, upon the success of which his whole future happiness entirely depended.", "펠라마 경은 부인이 이 일에 기꺼이 나서 준 것에 대해 진심으로 감사를 표했다. 그 성패에 자신의 앞날 행복이 송두리째 달려 있었기 때문이다."], ["He said, he saw at present no objection to the pressing scheme, and would consider of putting it in execution.", "그는 지금으로서는 강제 징집 계획에 아무런 이의도 없으며, 그것을 실행에 옮기는 일을 고려해 보겠다고 말했다."], ["He then most earnestly recommended to her ladyship to do him the honour of immediately mentioning his proposals to the family; to whom he said he offered a carte blanche, and would settle his fortune in almost any manner they should require.", "그러고는 부인에게, 부디 곧바로 자기 청혼을 그 집안에 전해 주는 영예를 베풀어 달라고 더없이 간절히 청했다. 그는 그 집안에 백지 위임을 내놓겠으며, 그들이 요구하는 거의 어떤 방식으로든 자기 재산을 정해 주겠노라고 말했다."], ["And after uttering many ecstasies and raptures concerning Sophia, he took his leave and departed, but not before he had received the strongest charge to beware of Jones, and to lose no time in securing his person, where he should no longer be in a capacity of making any attempts to the ruin of the young lady.", "그리고 소피아에 관해 온갖 황홀과 환희의 말을 쏟아 낸 뒤, 작별을 고하고 떠났다. 다만 떠나기 전에, 존스를 조심하고 한시도 지체 말고 그자의 신병을 확보하여, 다시는 그 젊은 숙녀를 파멸로 몰아넣을 어떤 시도도 할 수 없게 만들라는 더없이 단단한 당부를 받고서였다."]], [["The moment Mrs Western was arrived at her lodgings, a card was despatched with her compliments to Lady Bellaston; who no sooner received it than, with the impatience of a lover, she flew to her cousin, rejoiced at this fair opportunity, which beyond her hopes offered itself, for she was much better pleased with the prospect of making the proposals to a woman of sense, and who knew the world, than to a gentleman whom she honoured with the appellation of Hottentot; though, indeed, from him she apprehended no danger of a refusal.", "웨스턴 부인이 숙소에 당도하자마자, 벨라스턴 부인에게 안부를 전하는 명함이 발송되었다. 그것을 받자마자 벨라스턴 부인은 연인의 조바심으로 사촌에게 한달음에 날아갔으니, 바라던 것 이상으로 찾아든 이 좋은 기회를 반긴 것이었다. 이 청혼을, 그가 호텐토트라는 별칭으로 영예롭게 불러 주던 신사에게 들이미는 것보다는, 세상 물정에 밝고 분별 있는 여인에게 들이미는 편이 훨씬 더 마음에 들었기 때문이다. 물론 그 신사에게서야 거절당할 염려는 전혀 하지 않았지만 말이다."]], [["The two ladies being met, after very short previous ceremonials, fell to business, which was indeed almost as soon concluded as begun; for Mrs Western no sooner heard the name of Lord Fellamar than her cheeks glowed with pleasure; but when she was acquainted with the eagerness of his passion, the earnestness of his proposals, and the generosity of his offer, she declared her full satisfaction in the most explicit terms.", "두 부인이 만나, 짤막한 인사치레를 마치자 곧장 본론으로 들어갔으니, 그 본론은 시작되기가 무섭게 마무리되었다 해도 좋을 만했다. 웨스턴 부인은 펠라마 경의 이름을 듣자마자 두 뺨이 기쁨으로 달아올랐고, 그의 연정이 얼마나 간절한지, 그 청혼이 얼마나 진지한지, 그 제안이 얼마나 후한지를 듣고 나서는 더없이 또렷한 말로 흡족함을 가득 드러냈다."]], [["In the progress of their conversation their discourse turned to Jones, and both cousins very pathetically lamented the unfortunate attachment which both agreed Sophia had to that young fellow; and Mrs Western entirely attributed it to the folly of her brother's management. She concluded, however, at last, with declaring her confidence in the good understanding of her niece, who, though she would not give up her affection in favour of Blifil, will, I doubt not, says she, soon be prevailed upon to sacrifice a simple inclination to the addresses of a fine gentleman, who brings her both a title and a large estate: “For, indeed,” added she, “I must do Sophy the justice to confess this Blifil is but a hideous kind of fellow, as you know, Bellaston, all country gentlemen are, and hath nothing but his fortune to recommend him.”", "이야기가 흘러가는 가운데 화제는 존스에게로 돌아갔고, 두 사촌은 소피아가 그 젊은이에게 품은 불운한 애정을—둘 다 그렇다고 의견을 모았다—더없이 애처롭게 한탄했다. 웨스턴 부인은 그 일을 전적으로 오라비의 어리석은 처사 탓으로 돌렸다. 그러나 끝내는, 조카딸의 좋은 분별력에 대한 믿음을 밝히는 것으로 말을 맺었다. 그 아이가 블리필을 위해 자기 애정을 내놓지는 않으려 할지언정, \"내 장담하건대,\" 부인이 말했다. \"작위와 큰 영지를 함께 안겨 주는 훌륭한 신사의 구애 앞에서는 머지않아 그 한낱 끌림 따위는 희생하도록 설득될 거예요. 사실,\" 부인이 덧붙였다. \"소피에게 공정하게 말하자면, 이 블리필이라는 자가 보기 흉한 부류의 사내라는 점은 인정해야겠어요. 벨라스턴, 당신도 알다시피 시골 신사들이란 다 그렇잖아요. 게다가 그자는 재산 말고는 내세울 게 아무것도 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["said Lady Bellaston, “I don't then so much wonder at my cousin; for I promise you this Jones is a very agreeable fellow, and hath one virtue, which the men say is a great recommendation to us.", "벨라스턴 부인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 내 사촌이 그러는 것도 그리 놀랍지 않네요."], ["What do you think, Mrs Western--I shall certainly make you laugh; nay, I can hardly tell you myself for laughing--will you believe that the fellow hath had the assurance to make love to me?", "단언컨대 이 존스라는 자는 아주 호감 가는 사내이고, 남자들 말로는 우리 여자들에게 큰 호평거리가 된다는 한 가지 미덕도 갖추었거든요. 어떻게 생각하세요, 웨스턴 부인—분명 당신을 웃게 만들 거예요."], ["But if you should be inclined to disbelieve it, here is evidence enough, his own handwriting, I assure you.”", "아니, 나조차 웃음을 참느라 말을 제대로 못 할 지경인데—그 사내가 뻔뻔스럽게도 내게 사랑을 고백했다면 믿으시겠어요? 혹시 못 믿겠다 싶으면, 여기 충분한 증거가 있답니다."], ["She then delivered her cousin the letter with the proposals of marriage, which, if the reader hath a desire to see, he will find already on record in the XVth book of this history.", "그자가 손수 쓴 글씨예요, 정말이라니까요.\" 그러고는 청혼이 담긴 그 편지를 사촌에게 건넸으니, 독자께서 그것을 보고 싶으시다면 이 이야기의 제15권에 이미 기록되어 있음을 확인하실 수 있을 것이다."]], [["“Upon my word I am astonished,”", "\"맙소사, 정말 놀랍군요.\""], ["said Mrs Western; “this is, indeed, a masterpiece of assurance.", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"이거야말로 뻔뻔함의 걸작이로군요."], ["With your leave I may possibly make some use of this letter.”", "괜찮으시다면 이 편지를 어쩌면 좀 써먹을 수 있겠어요.\""], ["“You have my full liberty,”", "\"얼마든지 마음대로,\""], ["cries Lady Bellaston, “to apply it to what purpose you please.", "벨라스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"당신 좋으실 대로 어떤 용도에든 쓰세요."], ["However, I would not have it shewn to any but Miss Western, nor to her unless you find occasion.”", "다만 웨스턴 양 말고는 누구에게도 보이지 마시고, 그 아이에게도 꼭 필요한 계기가 생기지 않는 한은 보이지 마세요.\""], ["“Well, and how did you use the fellow?”", "\"그래, 그 사내는 어떻게 대하셨어요?\""], ["returned Mrs Western.", "웨스턴 부인이 되물었다."], ["“Not as a husband,”", "\"남편으로 대하진 않았지요.\""], ["said the lady; “I am not married, I promise you, my dear.", "부인이 말했다. \"단언컨대 난 재혼하지 않았어요, 이봐요."], ["You know, Bell, I have tried the comforts once already; and once, I think, is enough for any reasonable woman.”", "벨, 당신도 알다시피 난 이미 한 번 그 위안이라는 걸 겪어 봤잖아요. 분별 있는 여자라면 한 번이면 족하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["This letter Lady Bellaston thought would certainly turn the balance against Jones in the mind of Sophia, and she was emboldened to give it up, partly by her hopes of having him instantly dispatched out of the way, and partly by having secured the evidence of Honour, who, upon sounding her, she saw sufficient reason to imagine was prepared to testify whatever she pleased.", "벨라스턴 부인은 이 편지가 소피아의 마음속에서 존스에게 불리한 쪽으로 저울추를 확실히 기울게 하리라 여겼다. 그리고 그것을 선뜻 내준 것은, 한편으로는 존스가 곧장 처치되어 길에서 사라지리라는 기대 때문이었고, 다른 한편으로는 어너의 증언을 미리 확보해 둔 덕분이었다. 어너의 의중을 떠본 결과, 그녀가 무엇이든 원하는 대로 증언할 채비가 되어 있다고 짐작할 만한 충분한 근거를 보았던 것이다."]], [["But perhaps the reader may wonder why Lady Bellaston, who in her heart hated Sophia, should be so desirous of promoting a match which was so much to the interest of the young lady. Now, I would desire such readers to look carefully into human nature, page almost the last, and there he will find, in scarce legible characters, that women, notwithstanding the preposterous behaviour of mothers, aunts, &c., in matrimonial matters, do in reality think it so great a misfortune to have their inclinations in love thwarted, that they imagine they ought never to carry enmity higher than upon these disappointments; again, he will find it written much about the same place, that a woman who hath once been pleased with the possession of a man, will go above halfway to the devil, to prevent any other woman from enjoying the same.", "그런데 어쩌면 독자께서는 의아해하실지도 모르겠다. 속으로는 소피아를 미워하는 벨라스턴 부인이 어째서 그 젊은 숙녀에게 그토록 이로운 혼사를 그리도 열심히 밀어붙이려 했을까 하고 말이다. 자, 나는 그런 독자께 인간 본성이라는 책을 거의 맨 마지막 쪽까지 찬찬히 들여다보시기를 권하고 싶다. 그러면 거기, 알아보기도 힘든 글씨로 이렇게 적혀 있음을 발견하시리라. 혼사를 두고 어머니와 이모 따위가 그토록 터무니없이 굴기는 해도, 여자들은 실상 자기 사랑의 끌림이 가로막히는 것을 너무도 큰 불행으로 여긴 나머지, 그런 좌절에 대해서만큼은 적의를 그 이상으로 끌어올려서는 결코 안 된다고 여긴다는 것을. 또한 그분은 거의 같은 자리에서 이렇게 적힌 것도 발견하시리라. 한 번이라도 어떤 남자를 차지하는 기쁨을 맛본 여자는, 다른 어떤 여자가 똑같은 기쁨을 누리지 못하도록 막기 위해서라면 지옥까지 가는 길의 절반은 넘어서까지 기꺼이 가리라는 것을."]], [["If he will not be contented with these reasons, I freely confess I see no other motive to the actions of that lady, unless we will conceive she was bribed by Lord Fellamar, which for my own part I see no cause to suspect.", "만일 그분이 이런 이유들로 만족하지 못하신다면, 솔직히 고백하건대 나로서는 그 부인의 행동에 다른 어떤 동기도 떠오르지 않는다. 펠라마 경에게 매수당했다고 여기지 않는 한에는 말이다. 하지만 나로서는 그것을 의심할 까닭이 도무지 보이지 않는다."]], [["Now this was the affair which Mrs Western was preparing to introduce to Sophia, by some prefatory discourse on the folly of love, and on the wisdom of legal prostitution for hire, when her brother and Blifil broke abruptly in upon her; and hence arose all that coldness in her behaviour to Blifil, which, though the squire, as was usual with him, imputed to a wrong cause, infused into Blifil himself (he being a much more cunning man) a suspicion of the real truth.", "바로 이것이 웨스턴 부인이 소피아에게 꺼내려고 준비하던 일이었다. 사랑의 어리석음과, 돈을 위해 법의 테두리 안에서 몸을 파는 일의 지혜로움에 관한 몇 마디 서두를 깔아 가며 말이다. 그런데 바로 그때 오라비와 블리필이 불쑥 들이닥쳤다. 그리하여 블리필을 대하는 부인의 그 모든 냉랭함이 생겨난 것이니, 향사는 평소 버릇대로 그것을 엉뚱한 원인 탓으로 돌렸으나, 블리필 본인은(그가 훨씬 더 약삭빠른 사람이었기에) 그 진짜 속내를 의심하게 되었던 것이다."]], [["In which Jones pays a visit to Mrs Fitzpatrick.", "존스가 피츠패트릭 부인을 방문하는 이야기."]], [["The reader may now, perhaps, be pleased to return with us to Mr Jones, who, at the appointed hour, attended on Mrs Fitzpatrick; but before we relate the conversation which now past it may be proper, according to our method, to return a little back, and to account for so great an alteration of behaviour in this lady, that from changing her lodging principally to avoid Mr Jones, she had now industriously, as hath been seen, sought this interview.", "이제 독자께서는 우리와 함께 존스 씨에게로 돌아가기를 바라실지도 모르겠다. 그는 약속한 시각에 피츠패트릭 부인을 찾아갔다. 그러나 거기서 오간 대화를 전하기에 앞서, 우리가 늘 따라온 방식대로 잠시 뒤로 돌아가, 이 부인의 행동에 이토록 큰 변화가 생긴 까닭을 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠다. 처음에는 주로 존스 씨를 피하려고 거처를 옮겼던 그가, 이제는 앞서 본 대로 부지런히 이 만남을 청해 온 것이니 말이다."]], [["And here we shall need only to resort to what happened the preceding day, when, hearing from Lady Bellaston that Mr Western was arrived in town, she went to pay her duty to him, at his lodgings at Piccadilly, where she was received with many scurvy compellations too coarse to be repeated, and was even threatened to be kicked out of doors. From hence, an old servant of her aunt Western, with whom she was well acquainted, conducted her to the lodgings of that lady, who treated her not more kindly, but more politely; or, to say the truth, with rudeness in another way. In short, she returned from both, plainly convinced, not only that her scheme of reconciliation had proved abortive, but that she must for ever give over all thoughts of bringing it about by any means whatever. From this moment desire of revenge only filled her mind; and in this temper meeting Jones at the play, an opportunity seemed to her to occur of effecting this purpose.", "여기서는 다만 그 전날 일어난 일만 떠올리면 충분하다. 벨라스턴 부인에게서 웨스턴 씨가 도시에 도착했다는 소식을 들은 그는, 피카딜리에 있는 그의 거처로 문안을 드리러 갔다. 그러나 거기서 그는 차마 다시 옮기기 민망할 만큼 거칠고 천한 호칭을 잔뜩 들었고, 심지어 문밖으로 차내겠다는 위협까지 받았다. 그곳에서, 자기와 잘 아는 사이인 고모 웨스턴 댁의 늙은 하인이 그를 그 부인의 거처로 데려다주었다. 그러나 그 부인은 그를 더 친절하게 대한 것이 아니라 단지 더 정중하게 대했을 뿐이었다. 아니, 사실대로 말하자면 다른 방식으로 무례하게 대했다고 해야겠다. 요컨대 그는 양쪽 모두에게서 돌아오면서, 자신의 화해 계획이 수포로 돌아갔을 뿐 아니라 어떤 수단으로든 그것을 이루려는 생각은 영영 단념해야 한다는 것을 분명히 깨달았다. 이 순간부터 복수의 욕망만이 그의 마음을 가득 채웠다. 그리고 이런 심정으로 극장에서 존스를 마주쳤을 때, 그에게는 이 목적을 이룰 기회가 눈앞에 찾아온 것처럼 보였다."]], [["The reader must remember that he was acquainted by Mrs Fitzpatrick, in the account she gave of her own story, with the fondness Mrs Western had formerly shewn for Mr Fitzpatrick at Bath, from the disappointment of which Mrs Fitzpatrick derived the great bitterness her aunt had expressed toward her. She had, therefore, no doubt but that the good lady would as easily listen to the addresses of Mr Jones as she had before done to the other; for the superiority of charms was clearly on the side of Mr Jones; and the advance which her aunt had since made in age, she concluded (how justly I will not say), was an argument rather in favour of her project than against it.", "독자께서는 기억하실 것이다. 피츠패트릭 부인이 자기 사연을 이야기하며, 예전에 웨스턴 부인이 배스에서 피츠패트릭 씨에게 보였던 호감을 존스에게 들려준 것을 말이다. 그리고 그 호감이 어그러진 데서, 고모가 자신을 향해 드러낸 그 깊은 원한이 비롯되었음도 함께였다. 그러므로 그는, 그 인자한 부인이 예전에 다른 이의 구애에 그토록 쉽게 귀를 기울였던 것처럼, 존스 씨의 구애에도 그만큼 쉽게 응하리라고 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 매력의 우위로 말하자면 분명 존스 씨 쪽에 있었고, 게다가 그 사이 고모의 나이가 더 들었다는 사실 또한 (얼마나 정당한 판단인지는 말하지 않겠다) 자신의 계획에 불리하기보다 오히려 유리한 근거라고 그는 결론지었다."]], [["Therefore, when Jones attended, after a previous declaration of her desire of serving him, arising, as she said, from a firm assurance how much she should by so doing oblige Sophia; and after some excuses for her former disappointment, and after acquainting Mr Jones in whose custody his mistress was, of which she thought him ignorant; she very explicitly mentioned her scheme to him, and advised him to make sham addresses to the older lady, in order to procure an easy access to the younger, informing him at the same time of the success which Mr Fitzpatrick had formerly owed to the very same stratagem.", "그리하여 존스가 찾아왔을 때, 그는 먼저 그를 돕고 싶다는 뜻을 밝혔는데, 그렇게 함으로써 소피아에게 얼마나 큰 도움이 될지 굳게 확신한다는 것이 그 이유였다. 그러고는 지난번 약속을 어긴 일에 대해 몇 마디 변명을 한 뒤, 존스 씨가 모르고 있으리라 여겨, 그의 연인이 누구의 보호 아래 있는지를 일러 주었다. 그런 다음 그는 자신의 계획을 아주 분명하게 그에게 털어놓으며, 어린 쪽에게 쉽게 다가가기 위해 늙은 부인에게 짐짓 구애하는 척하라고 권했다. 동시에 예전에 피츠패트릭 씨가 바로 이 똑같은 책략 덕분에 성공을 거두었던 일도 함께 알려 주었다."]], [["Mr Jones expressed great gratitude to the lady for the kind intentions towards him which she had expressed, and indeed testified, by this proposal; but, besides intimating some diffidence of success from the lady's knowledge of his love to her niece, which had not been her case in regard to Mr Fitzpatrick, he said, he was afraid Miss Western would never agree to an imposition of this kind, as well from her utter detestation of all fallacy as from her avowed duty to her aunt.", "존스 씨는 부인이 자기를 향해 드러내고, 또 이 제안으로 실제로 입증해 보인 그 친절한 뜻에 깊은 감사를 표했다. 그러나 그는, 피츠패트릭 씨의 경우와는 달리 그 부인이 자신의 조카를 향한 자기 사랑을 이미 알고 있으니 성공이 어렵지 않겠느냐는 우려를 넌지시 비치면서, 웨스턴 양은 결코 이런 속임수에 응하지 않을 것 같다고 말했다. 모든 거짓을 철저히 혐오하는 마음에서도 그러하거니와, 고모를 향해 스스로 떳떳이 밝히는 효심 때문에도 그러하리라는 것이었다."]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick was a little nettled at this; and indeed, if it may not be called a lapse of the tongue, it was a small deviation from politeness in Jones, and into which he scarce would have fallen, had not the delight he felt in praising Sophia hurried him out of all reflection; for this commendation of one cousin was more than a tacit rebuke on the other.", "피츠패트릭 부인은 이 말에 조금 발끈했다. 사실 이것은, 말이 헛나간 것이라 부를 수 없다면, 존스로서는 예법에서 살짝 벗어난 실수였다. 그리고 소피아를 칭찬하는 데서 느낀 기쁨이 그를 온갖 분별 밖으로 떠밀지만 않았던들, 그가 좀처럼 저지르지 않았을 실수이기도 했다. 한쪽 사촌을 칭찬하는 이 말이, 다른 쪽 사촌에 대한 은근한 책망 이상이 되어 버렸으니 말이다."]], [["“Indeed, sir,” answered the lady, with some warmth, “I cannot think there is anything easier than to cheat an old woman with a profession of love, when her complexion is amorous; and, though she is my aunt, I must say there never was a more liquorish one than her ladyship. Can't you pretend that the despair of possessing her niece, from her being promised to Blifil, has made you turn your thoughts towards her? As to my cousin Sophia, I can't imagine her to be such a simpleton as to have the least scruple on such an account, or to conceive any harm in punishing one of these haggs for the many mischiefs they bring upon families by their tragi-comic passions; for which I think it is a pity they are not punishable by law. I had no such scruple myself; and yet I hope my cousin Sophia will not think it an affront when I say she cannot detest every real species of falsehood more than her cousin Fitzpatrick. To my aunt, indeed, I pretend no duty, nor doth she deserve any. However, sir, I have given you my advice; and if you decline pursuing it, I shall have the less opinion of your understanding--that's all.”", "\"정말이지, 선생님,\" 부인이 약간 열을 올리며 대답했다. \"사랑한다고 떠벌리며 늙은 여자를 속이는 것보다 더 쉬운 일은 없다고 생각해요. 그 여자의 기질이 음탕하기만 하다면요. 그분이 제 고모이긴 하지만, 그분만큼 색을 밝히는 분도 없었다고 말씀드려야겠네요. 조카가 블리필과 약혼했기 때문에 조카를 차지하리란 절망이 당신 마음을 그분에게로 돌려놓았다고 둘러댈 수도 있잖아요? 제 사촌 소피아로 말하자면, 그 애가 이런 일에 조금이라도 거리낄 만큼, 또 이런 노파를 벌하는 데서 무슨 해악이라도 느낄 만큼 그렇게 어리석은 애라고는 도무지 생각할 수 없어요. 이런 노파들은 희비극적인 정념으로 집안에 온갖 풍파를 불러오는데, 그게 법으로 처벌되지 않는 게 안타까울 따름이지요. 저 자신은 그런 거리낌이 전혀 없었어요. 그래도 제 사촌 소피아가, 제가 이렇게 말한다고 해서 모욕으로 여기지 않기를 바라요. 그 애가 온갖 종류의 진짜 거짓을 혐오한다 해도, 사촌인 이 피츠패트릭보다 더 혐오하지는 못하리라는 거요. 고모에게는, 솔직히 저는 아무런 효심도 가장하지 않아요. 그분이 그럴 자격도 없고요. 어쨌든 선생님, 저는 충고를 드렸어요. 그걸 따르지 않으시겠다면, 저는 당신의 분별력을 그만큼 낮춰 볼 따름이에요. 그뿐이지요.\""]], [["Jones now clearly saw the error he had committed, and exerted his utmost power to rectify it; but he only faultered and stuttered into nonsense and contradiction. To say the truth, it is often safer to abide by the consequences of the first blunder than to endeavour to rectify it; for by such endeavours we generally plunge deeper instead of extricating ourselves; and few persons will on such occasions have the good-nature which Mrs Fitzpatrick displayed to Jones, by saying, with a smile, “You need attempt no more excuses; for I can easily forgive a real lover, whatever is the effect of fondness for his mistress.”", "이제 존스는 자기가 저지른 실수를 똑똑히 깨닫고, 그것을 바로잡으려 온 힘을 다했다. 그러나 그는 그저 더듬거리고 말을 흐리다가 헛소리와 모순 속으로 빠져들 뿐이었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 첫 실수를 바로잡으려 애쓰기보다 그 결과를 그냥 감내하는 편이 더 안전할 때가 많다. 그렇게 애쓰다 보면 대개 빠져나오기는커녕 더 깊이 가라앉게 마련이니 말이다. 또한 이런 경우에, 피츠패트릭 부인이 존스에게 보여 준 만큼의 너그러움을 지닌 이도 드물 것이다. 그는 미소 지으며 이렇게 말했다. \"더는 변명하실 것 없어요. 저는 진정한 연인이라면, 연인을 향한 애정에서 비롯된 일이라면 무엇이든 쉽게 용서할 수 있으니까요.\""]], [["She then renewed her proposal, and very fervently recommended it, omitting no argument which her invention could suggest on the subject; for she was so violently incensed against her aunt, that scarce anything was capable of affording her equal pleasure with exposing her; and, like a true woman, she would see no difficulties in the execution of a favourite scheme.", "그러고는 다시 자신의 제안을 꺼내 아주 열렬하게 권했다. 이 주제에 관해 자신의 머리가 떠올릴 수 있는 어떤 논거도 빠뜨리지 않았다. 그는 고모에게 어찌나 격렬하게 분이 치밀어 있던지, 그 고모의 약점을 들춰내는 일만큼 그에게 똑같은 기쁨을 줄 수 있는 것은 거의 없었다. 그리고 천생 여자답게, 그는 자기가 아끼는 계획을 실행하는 데 어떤 어려움도 보려 하지 않았다."]], [["Jones, however, persisted in declining the undertaking, which had not, indeed, the least probability of success.", "그러나 존스는 그 일을 맡기를 한사코 거절했다. 사실 그 일은 성공할 가망이 조금도 없었다."], ["He easily perceived the motives which induced Mrs Fitzpatrick to be so eager in pressing her advice.", "그는 피츠패트릭 부인이 자기 충고를 이토록 다급하게 들이미는 동기를 쉽게 알아챘다. 그는 소피아를 향한 자신의 다정하고 열렬한 마음을 부인하지는 않겠다고 말했다."], ["He said he would not deny the tender and passionate regard he had for Sophia; but was so conscious of the inequality of their situations, that he could never flatter himself so far as to hope that so divine a young lady would condescend to think on so unworthy a man; nay, he protested, he could scarce bring himself to wish she should.", "그러나 두 사람의 처지가 너무도 차이 난다는 것을 잘 알기에, 그토록 거룩한 아가씨가 이토록 보잘것없는 사내를 마음에 두어 줄 거라는 헛된 기대는 결코 품을 수 없다고 했다. 아니, 그는 그가 그러기를 바라는 마음조차 좀처럼 일으킬 수 없다고 단언했다."], ["He concluded with a profession of generous sentiments, which we have not at present leisure to insert.", "그는 너그러운 심정을 토로하며 말을 맺었는데, 그 내용을 여기 옮길 겨를은 지금 우리에게 없다."]], [["There are some fine women (for I dare not here speak in too general terms) with whom self is so predominant, that they never detach it from any subject; and, as vanity is with them a ruling principle, they are apt to lay hold of whatever praise they meet with; and, though the property of others, convey it to their own use. In the company of these ladies it is impossible to say anything handsome of another woman which they will not apply to themselves; nay, they often improve the praise they seize; as, for instance, if her beauty, her wit, her gentility, her good humour deserve so much commendation, what do I deserve, who possess those qualities in so much more eminent a degree?", "세상에는 어떤 아름다운 여자들이 있는데(여기서 너무 일반화해 말할 엄두는 내지 못하겠다), 자아가 어찌나 압도적인지 어떤 화제에서도 그것을 떼어 놓지 못한다. 그들에게는 허영이 으뜸가는 원리여서, 마주치는 칭찬이라면 무엇이든 움켜쥐려 들고, 그것이 남의 것일지라도 제 것으로 가져다 쓴다. 이런 부인들과 함께 있을 때는, 다른 여자를 두고 무슨 멋진 말을 하든 그들이 그것을 자기 자신에게 갖다 붙이지 않을 수가 없다. 아니, 그들은 흔히 자기가 낚아챈 칭찬을 한술 더 떠 부풀린다. 이를테면, 저 여자의 아름다움과 재치, 우아함과 좋은 성품이 그만한 칭찬을 받을 만하다면, 그런 자질들을 훨씬 더 빼어나게 지닌 나는 대체 무엇을 받아 마땅하단 말인가, 하는 식으로."]], [["To these ladies a man often recommends himself while he is commending another woman; and, while he is expressing ardour and generous sentiments for his mistress, they are considering what a charming lover this man would make to them, who can feel all this tenderness for an inferior degree of merit. Of this, strange as it may seem, I have seen many instances besides Mrs Fitzpatrick, to whom all this really happened, and who now began to feel a somewhat for Mr Jones, the symptoms of which she much sooner understood than poor Sophia had formerly done.", "이런 부인들에게는, 사내가 다른 여자를 칭찬하는 동안에 도리어 자기 자신을 천거하게 되는 일이 흔하다. 그가 자기 연인을 향한 뜨거움과 너그러운 마음을 토로하는 동안, 그들은 이런 생각을 한다. 더 못한 정도의 가치를 지닌 여자에게도 이토록 다정할 수 있는 이 사내라면, 우리에게는 얼마나 매력적인 연인이 되어 줄까 하고. 이상하게 들릴지 모르나, 나는 이런 사례를 피츠패트릭 부인 말고도 여럿 보아 왔다. 그리고 이 모든 일이 실제로 일어난 피츠패트릭 부인은, 이제 존스 씨에게 어떤 감정을 느끼기 시작했는데, 그 징후를 그는 예전의 가엾은 소피아보다 훨씬 빨리 알아차렸다."]], [["To say the truth, perfect beauty in both sexes is a more irresistible object than it is generally thought; for, notwithstanding some of us are contented with more homely lots, and learn by rote (as children to repeat what gives them no idea) to despise outside, and to value more solid charms; yet I have always observed, at the approach of consummate beauty, that these more solid charms only shine with that kind of lustre which the stars have after the rising of the sun.", "사실대로 말하자면, 남녀 양쪽 모두에게 완벽한 아름다움이란 흔히들 여기는 것보다 훨씬 거역하기 어려운 대상이다. 우리 가운데 더러는 더 수수한 운명에 만족하며, (어린아이가 아무 뜻도 모르는 말을 따라 외우듯이) 겉모습을 멸시하고 더 단단한 매력을 더 높이 치라고 입에 익도록 배우지만, 그럼에도 나는 늘 보아 왔다. 더없이 빼어난 아름다움이 다가올 때면, 그 더 단단한 매력이란 것은 해가 뜬 뒤의 별들이 내는 그런 빛으로밖에 빛나지 못한다는 것을."]], [["When Jones had finished his exclamations, many of which would have become the mouth of Oroöndates himself, Mrs Fitzpatrick heaved a deep sigh, and, taking her eyes off from Jones, on whom they had been some time fixed, and dropping them on the ground, she cried, “Indeed, Mr Jones, I pity you; but it is the curse of such tenderness to be thrown away on those who are insensible of it. I know my cousin better than you, Mr Jones, and I must say, any woman who makes no return to such a passion, and such a person, is unworthy of both.”", "존스가 그 감격에 찬 말들을 끝마쳤을 때(그 가운데 상당수는 오론다테스의 입에서 나왔어도 어울렸을 만한 것이었다), 피츠패트릭 부인은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러고는 한동안 존스에게 못 박혀 있던 눈길을 거두어 바닥으로 떨구며 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 존스 씨, 저는 당신이 가엾어요. 하지만 그런 다정함을 그것을 알아주지도 못하는 이들에게 헛되이 던져 버리는 것이야말로 그 다정함의 저주랍니다. 저는 당신보다 제 사촌을 더 잘 알아요, 존스 씨. 그래서 말씀드리는데, 이런 정념과 이런 사람에게 아무런 보답도 하지 않는 여자라면, 그 둘 어느 쪽에도 어울리지 않는 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Sure, madam,”", "\"설마, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “you can't mean----”", "\"그 말씀은 설마……\" \"설마라뇨!\""], ["“Mean!”", "피츠패트릭 부인이 외쳤다."], ["cries Mrs Fitzpatrick, “I know not what I mean; there is something, I think, in true tenderness bewitching; few women ever meet with it in men, and fewer still know how to value it when they do.", "\"저도 제가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 모르겠어요. 진정한 다정함에는 무언가 사람을 홀리는 게 있는 것 같아요."], ["I never heard such truly noble sentiments, and I can't tell how it is, but you force one to believe you.", "사내에게서 그런 걸 만나는 여자는 드물고, 만난다 해도 그 값을 알아볼 줄 아는 여자는 더더욱 드물지요. 저는 이토록 진실로 고귀한 마음을 들어 본 적이 없어요."], ["Sure she must be the most contemptible of women who can overlook such merit.”", "어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만, 당신은 사람을 믿지 않을 수 없게 만드는군요. 이런 가치를 알아보지 못하는 여자라면, 분명 세상에서 가장 경멸할 만한 여자임에 틀림없어요.\""]], [["The manner and look with which all this was spoke infused a suspicion into Jones which we don't care to convey in direct words to the reader.", "이 모든 말을 내뱉는 태도와 눈빛은 존스에게 어떤 의심을 불어넣었는데, 우리는 그것을 독자께 곧이곧대로 말씀드리고 싶지는 않다."], ["Instead of making any answer, he said, “I am afraid, madam, I have made too tiresome a visit;”", "그는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않은 채 이렇게 말했다. \"부인, 제가 너무 지루하게 머물렀나 봅니다.\""], ["and offered to take his leave.", "그러고는 작별을 고하려 했다."]], [["“Not at all, sir,” answered Mrs Fitzpatrick.--“Indeed I pity you, Mr Jones; indeed I do: but if you are going, consider of the scheme I have mentioned--I am convinced you will approve it--and let me see you again as soon as you can.--To-morrow morning if you will, or at least some time to-morrow. I shall be at home all day.”", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아요, 선생님.\" 피츠패트릭 부인이 대답했다. \"정말이지 당신이 가엾어요, 존스 씨. 정말이에요. 하지만 가시려거든, 제가 말씀드린 계획을 한번 곰곰이 생각해 보세요. 분명 마음에 드실 거예요. 그리고 되도록 빨리 다시 뵙도록 해요. 원하신다면 내일 아침에요, 아니면 적어도 내일 어느 때라도요. 저는 하루 종일 집에 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["Jones, then, after many expressions of thanks, very respectfully retired; nor could Mrs Fitzpatrick forbear making him a present of a look at parting, by which if he had understood nothing, he must have had no understanding in the language of the eyes.", "그리하여 존스는 여러 차례 감사의 말을 한 뒤, 아주 공손하게 물러났다. 헤어질 때 피츠패트릭 부인은 그에게 눈길 하나를 선물하지 않을 수 없었는데, 만일 그가 그 눈길에서 아무것도 알아채지 못했다면, 그는 눈빛이라는 언어를 도무지 모르는 사람이었을 것이다."], ["In reality, it confirmed his resolution of returning to her no more; for, faulty as he hath hitherto appeared in this history, his whole thoughts were now so confined to his Sophia, that I believe no woman upon earth could have now drawn him into an act of inconstancy.", "사실 그 눈길은 다시는 그에게 돌아가지 않으리라는 그의 결심을 굳혀 주었다. 이 이야기에서 지금껏 허물 많은 사람으로 보였을지언정, 이제 그의 모든 생각은 오직 자신의 소피아에게만 매여 있어서, 세상 어떤 여자도 이제 그를 변심의 행위로 끌어들일 수는 없으리라고 나는 믿는다."]], [["Fortune, however, who was not his friend, resolved, as he intended to give her no second opportunity, to make the best of this; and accordingly produced the tragical incident which we are now in sorrowful notes to record.", "그러나 그의 편이 아니었던 운명의 여신은, 그가 두 번째 기회를 주지 않으려 마음먹은 것을 알고는, 이 기회를 한껏 살려 보기로 작정했다. 그리하여 우리가 이제 슬픈 가락으로 적어 내려가야 할 그 비극적인 사건을 빚어내고 말았다."]], [["The consequence of the preceding visit.", "앞선 방문이 불러온 결과."]], [["Mr Fitzpatrick having received the letter before mentioned from Mrs Western, and being by that means acquainted with the place to which his wife was retired, returned directly to Bath, and thence the day after set forward to London.", "피츠패트릭 씨는 앞서 말한 웨스턴 부인의 편지를 받고, 그 덕분에 아내가 몸을 피해 있는 장소를 알게 되자 곧장 배스로 돌아갔으며, 그 이튿날 거기서 런던으로 출발했다."]], [["The reader hath been already often informed of the jealous temper of this gentleman.", "독자는 이 신사의 질투심 많은 성미에 대해 이미 여러 차례 들어 알고 있을 것이다."], ["He may likewise be pleased to remember the suspicion which he had conceived of Jones at Upton, upon his finding him in the room with Mrs Waters; and, though sufficient reasons had afterwards appeared entirely to clear up that suspicion, yet now the reading so handsome a character of Mr Jones from his wife, caused him to reflect that she likewise was in the inn at the same time, and jumbled together such a confusion of circumstances in a head which was naturally none of the clearest, that the whole produced that green-eyed monster mentioned by Shakespear in his tragedy of Othello.", "또한 그가 업튼에서 워터스 부인과 한 방에 있는 존스를 발견했을 때 품었던 의심도 기억해 주리라. 그 의심을 말끔히 풀어 줄 충분한 이유가 나중에 드러났음에도, 지금 아내가 쓴 편지에서 존스 씨에 대한 그토록 그럴듯한 평판을 읽고 나니, 그는 그녀 역시 같은 시간에 그 여관에 있었음을 떠올리게 되었고, 본래 그리 맑지도 못한 머릿속에서 온갖 정황을 뒤죽박죽 뒤섞어 버린 끝에, 그 전체가 셰익스피어가 비극 「오셀로」에서 언급한 그 초록 눈의 괴물을 빚어내고 말았다."]], [["And now, as he was enquiring in the street after his wife, and had just received directions to the door, unfortunately Mr Jones was issuing from it.", "그리고 지금, 그가 거리에서 아내의 거처를 물어 막 그 집 문까지 안내를 받은 참인데, 공교롭게도 존스 씨가 바로 그 문에서 나오고 있었다."]], [["Fitzpatrick did not yet recollect the face of Jones; however, seeing a young well-dressed fellow coming from his wife, he made directly up to him, and asked him what he had been doing in that house?", "피츠패트릭은 아직 존스의 얼굴을 기억해 내지 못했다. 그러나 잘 차려입은 젊은 사내가 자기 아내의 집에서 나오는 것을 보자 곧장 그에게로 다가가, 저 집에서 무슨 일을 하고 왔느냐고 물었다."], ["“for I am sure,”", "\"분명히,\""], ["said he, “you must have been in it, as I saw you come out of it.”", "그가 말했다, \"당신은 저 안에 있었던 게 틀림없소. 거기서 나오는 걸 내가 봤으니 말이오.\""]], [["Jones answered very modestly, “That he had been visiting a lady there.”", "존스는 아주 겸손하게 대답했다. \"거기 있는 어느 부인을 찾아뵙고 오는 길입니다.\""], ["To which Fitzpatrick replied, “What business have you with the lady?”", "이에 피츠패트릭이 받아쳤다. \"그 부인과 당신이 무슨 볼일이 있단 말이오?\""], ["Upon which Jones, who now perfectly remembered the voice, features, and indeed coat, of the gentleman, cried out----“Ha, my good friend!", "그러자 이제는 그 신사의 목소리와 이목구비, 실은 외투까지도 또렷이 기억해 낸 존스가 외쳤다. \"아니, 이 사람아!"], ["give me your hand; I hope there is no ill blood remaining between us, upon a small mistake which happened so long ago.”", "손 좀 줘 보게. 그토록 오래전에 일어난 사소한 오해를 두고 우리 사이에 아직 나쁜 감정이 남아 있지는 않기를 바라네.\""]], [["“Upon my soul, sir,”", "\"맹세코, 선생,\" 피츠패트릭이 말했다, \"나는 당신 이름도 얼굴도 모르오.\""], ["said Fitzpatrick, “I don't know your name nor your face.”", "\"참으로, 선생,\" 존스가 말했다, \"저 역시 선생의 성함을 알게 되는 기쁨은 누리지 못했습니다만, 얼굴만큼은 전에 업튼에서 뵌 적이 있는 걸로 잘 기억하고 있습니다."], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "거기서 우리 사이에 어리석은 다툼이 있었지요."], ["said Jones, “neither have I the pleasure of knowing your name, but your face I very well remember to have seen before at Upton, where a foolish quarrel happened between us, which, if it is not made up yet, we will now make up over a bottle.”", "만약 그 일이 아직 풀리지 않았다면, 이제 한잔 기울이며 풀어 봅시다.\""]], [["“At Upton!”", "\"업튼에서라고!\""], ["cried the other;----“Ha!", "상대가 외쳤다. \"하!"], ["upon my soul, I believe your name is Jones?”", "맹세코, 당신 이름이 존스 아니오?\" \"그렇습니다,\""], ["“Indeed,” answered he, “it is.”--“O!", "그가 대답했다, \"맞습니다.\""], ["upon my soul,”", "\"오! 맹세코,\""], ["cries Fitzpatrick, “you are the very man I wanted to meet.--Upon my soul I will drink a bottle with you presently; but first I will give you a great knock over the pate.", "피츠패트릭이 외쳤다, \"당신이야말로 내가 만나고 싶던 바로 그 작자로군."], ["There is for you, you rascal.", "맹세코 곧 당신과 한 병 비우겠소."], ["Upon my soul, if you do not give me satisfaction for that blow, I will give you another.”", "하지만 먼저 당신 정수리에 한 방 크게 먹여 주지. 자, 받아라, 이 악당아."], ["And then, drawing his sword, put himself in a posture of defence, which was the only science he understood.", "맹세코 당신이 그 일격에 대해 내게 명예를 회복시켜 주지 않으면, 한 방 더 먹여 줄 테다.\" 그러더니 그는 칼을 뽑아 방어 자세를 취했는데, 그것이 그가 아는 유일한 기술이었다."]], [["Jones was a little staggered by the blow, which came somewhat unexpectedly; but presently recovering himself he also drew, and though he understood nothing of fencing, prest on so boldly upon Fitzpatrick, that he beat down his guard, and sheathed one half of his sword in the body of the said gentleman, who had no sooner received it than he stept backwards, dropped the point of his sword, and leaning upon it, cried, “I have satisfaction enough: I am a dead man.”", "존스는 다소 뜻밖에 날아든 그 일격에 잠시 비틀거렸으나, 곧 정신을 차리고 자기도 칼을 뽑았다. 비록 검술이라곤 아무것도 몰랐지만, 어찌나 대담하게 피츠패트릭에게 밀어붙였던지 그의 방어를 뚫고 칼날의 절반을 그 신사의 몸에 박아 넣고 말았다. 그 신사는 칼을 받자마자 뒤로 한 걸음 물러나 칼끝을 떨어뜨리고는, 그 위에 몸을 기대며 외쳤다. \"이만하면 충분히 만족하오. 나는 죽은 목숨이오.\""]], [["“I hope not,”", "\"그러지 않기를 바랍니다,\""], ["cries Jones, “but whatever be the consequence, you must be sensible you have drawn it upon yourself.”", "존스가 외쳤다, \"그러나 결과가 어찌 되든, 당신 스스로 자초한 일임은 분명히 아셔야 합니다.\""], ["At this instant a number of fellows rushed in and seized Jones, who told them he should make no resistance, and begged some of them at least would take care of the wounded gentleman.", "바로 그 순간 한 무리의 사내들이 들이닥쳐 존스를 붙잡았고, 존스는 저항하지 않겠노라 말하며, 적어도 그들 중 몇은 부상당한 신사를 보살펴 달라고 청했다."]], [["“Ay,”", "\"아무렴,\""], ["cries one of the fellows, “the wounded gentleman will be taken care enough of; for I suppose he hath not many hours to live.", "사내들 중 하나가 외쳤다, \"부상당한 신사는 충분히 보살핌을 받을 거요. 살 시간이 몇 시간도 안 남은 것 같으니 말이오."], ["As for you, sir, you have a month at least good yet.”", "당신으로 말하자면, 선생, 적어도 한 달은 멀쩡히 더 살겠지.\""], ["“D--n me, Jack,”", "\"젠장, 잭,\""], ["said another, “he hath prevented his voyage; he's bound to another port now;”", "다른 하나가 말했다, \"저 양반 항해는 끝났구먼. 이제 다른 항구로 가게 됐어.\""], ["and many other such jests was our poor Jones made the subject of by these fellows, who were indeed the gang employed by Lord Fellamar, and had dogged him into the house of Mrs Fitzpatrick, waiting for him at the corner of the street when this unfortunate accident happened.", "이렇게 우리 가엾은 존스는 이 사내들의 온갖 농지거리의 대상이 되었으니, 사실 이들은 펠라마 경에게 고용된 패거리로, 존스를 미행하여 피츠패트릭 부인의 집까지 따라붙었고, 이 불운한 사고가 벌어졌을 때 길모퉁이에서 그를 기다리고 있던 것이었다."]], [["The officer who commanded this gang very wisely concluded that his business was now to deliver his prisoner into the hands of the civil magistrate.", "이 패거리를 지휘하던 자는 이제 자신이 할 일이 죄수를 민간 치안관의 손에 넘기는 것임을 매우 현명하게 판단했다."], ["He ordered him, therefore, to be carried to a public-house, where, having sent for a constable, he delivered him to his custody.", "그리하여 그는 존스를 어느 선술집으로 데려가게 한 뒤, 거기서 순경을 불러 그에게 신병을 인계했다."]], [["The constable, seeing Mr Jones very well drest, and hearing that the accident had happened in a duel, treated his prisoner with great civility, and at his request dispatched a messenger to enquire after the wounded gentleman, who was now at a tavern under the surgeon's hands.", "순경은 존스 씨가 아주 잘 차려입은 것을 보고, 또 그 사고가 결투에서 벌어진 일이라는 말을 듣고는, 죄수를 매우 정중하게 대했다. 그리고 존스의 청에 따라 부상당한 신사의 소식을 알아보러 사람을 보냈는데, 그 신사는 지금 어느 선술집에서 외과의의 손에 맡겨져 있었다."], ["The report brought back was, that the wound was certainly mortal, and there were no hopes of life.", "돌아온 보고는, 상처가 분명 치명적이며 살아날 가망이 없다는 것이었다. 이에 순경은 존스에게 치안판사 앞으로 가야 한다고 알렸다."], ["Upon which the constable informed Jones that he must go before a justice.", "그가 대답했다. \"어디든 좋을 대로 하시오."], ["He answered, “Wherever you please; I am indifferent as to what happens to me; for though I am convinced I am not guilty of murder in the eye of the law, yet the weight of blood I find intolerable upon my mind.”", "내게 무슨 일이 닥치든 나는 개의치 않소. 법의 눈으로 보면 내가 살인죄를 저지른 것이 아님을 확신하지만, 그래도 피의 무게가 내 마음을 견딜 수 없이 짓누르는구려.\""]], [["Jones was now conducted before the justice, where the surgeon who dressed Mr Fitzpatrick appeared, and deposed that he believed the wound to be mortal; upon which the prisoner was committed to the Gatehouse.", "이제 존스는 치안판사 앞으로 끌려갔고, 거기서 피츠패트릭 씨를 치료한 외과의가 나와 그 상처가 치명적이라고 믿는다고 진술했다. 이에 죄수는 게이트하우스 감옥에 수감되었다."], ["It was very late at night, so that Jones would not send for Partridge till the next morning; and, as he never shut his eyes till seven, so it was near twelve before the poor fellow, who was greatly frightened at not hearing from his master so long, received a message which almost deprived him of his being when he heard it.", "밤이 매우 깊은 시각이라 존스는 이튿날 아침까지는 파트리지를 부르지 않았다. 그리고 존스는 일곱 시까지 눈을 붙이지 못했으므로, 그토록 오래도록 주인의 소식을 듣지 못해 잔뜩 겁에 질려 있던 가엾은 파트리지가 그 소식을 듣자마자 거의 넋이 나갈 뻔한 전갈을 받은 것은 정오가 다 되어서였다."]], [["He went to the Gatehouse with trembling knees and a beating heart, and was no sooner arrived in the presence of Jones than he lamented the misfortune that had befallen him with many tears, looking all the while frequently about him in great terror; for as the news now arrived that Mr Fitzpatrick was dead, the poor fellow apprehended every minute that his ghost would enter the room.", "그는 무릎을 덜덜 떨고 가슴을 두근거리며 게이트하우스로 갔고, 존스 앞에 다다르자마자 그에게 닥친 불행을 두고 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 한탄했다. 그러면서도 내내 몹시 겁에 질려 사방을 두리번거렸으니, 피츠패트릭 씨가 죽었다는 소식이 이제 막 도착한 터라, 이 가엾은 사내는 그의 유령이 방 안으로 들어올까 봐 시시각각 두려워했던 것이다."], ["At last he delivered him a letter, which he had like to have forgot, and which came from Sophia by the hands of Black George.", "마침내 그는 하마터면 잊을 뻔했던 편지 한 통을 존스에게 건넸는데, 그것은 검둥이 조지의 손을 거쳐 소피아에게서 온 것이었다."]], [["Jones presently dispatched every one out of the room, and, having eagerly broke open the letter, read as follows:--", "존스는 즉시 모두를 방에서 내보낸 뒤, 다급히 편지를 뜯어 다음과 같이 읽었다."]], [["“You owe the hearing from me again to an accident which I own surprizes me.", "\"제가 당신에게서 다시 소식을 듣게 된 것은, 솔직히 저로서도 놀라운 어떤 우연 덕분입니다."], ["My aunt hath just now shown me a letter from you to Lady Bellaston, which contains a proposal of marriage.", "방금 숙모께서 당신이 벨라스턴 부인에게 보낸 편지를 제게 보여 주셨는데, 거기에는 청혼이 담겨 있더군요. 그것이 당신의 친필임을 저는 확신합니다."], ["I am convinced it is your own hand; and what more surprizes me is, that it is dated at the very time when you would have me imagine you was under such concern on my account.--I leave you to comment on this fact.", "더욱 놀라운 것은, 그 편지의 날짜가 바로 당신이 저를 위해 그토록 애태우고 있다고 제게 믿게 하려던 그 시기로 되어 있다는 점입니다. 이 사실에 대한 해명은 당신께 맡기겠습니다."], ["All I desire is, that your name may never more be mentioned to", "제가 바라는 것은 오직 하나, 당신의 이름이 다시는 제 앞에서 거론되지 않는 것뿐입니다."]], [["Of the present situation of Mr Jones's mind, and of the pangs with which he was now tormented, we cannot give the reader a better idea than by saying, his misery was such that even Thwackum would almost have pitied him.", "S. W.\""], ["But, bad as it is, we shall at present leave him in it, as his good genius (if he really had any) seems to have done.", "존스 씨의 지금 심정과, 그가 이제 시달리고 있는 고통에 대해서는, 그의 비참함이 어찌나 컸던지 저 스왁컴조차 거의 그를 가엾이 여겼을 정도라고 말하는 것보다 독자에게 더 잘 전할 길이 없다. 그러나 그 처지가 아무리 가혹할지라도, 우리는 지금으로서는 그를 그 속에 그대로 두려 한다."], ["And here we put an end to the sixteenth book of our history.", "그의 수호신마저도 (정말 그런 것이 그에게 있었다면) 그리한 듯하니 말이다. 그리고 여기서 우리는 이 이야기의 열여섯 번째 권을 마친다."]], [["Containing a portion of introductory writing.", "사흘간의 이야기. 서두를 여는 글 한 토막."]], [["When a comic writer hath made his principal characters as happy as he can, or when a tragic writer hath brought them to the highest pitch of human misery, they both conclude their business to be done, and that their work is come to a period.", "희극을 쓰는 작가가 주요 인물들을 더없이 행복하게 만들어 놓았을 때, 혹은 비극을 쓰는 작가가 그들을 인간이 겪을 수 있는 가장 비참한 밑바닥까지 끌고 내려갔을 때, 두 사람은 모두 제 할 일이 끝났으며 작품이 마침표에 이르렀다고 여긴다."]], [["Had we been of the tragic complexion, the reader must now allow we were very nearly arrived at this period, since it would be difficult for the devil, or any of his representatives on earth, to have contrived much greater torments for poor Jones than those in which we left him in the last chapter; and as for Sophia, a good-natured woman would hardly wish more uneasiness to a rival than what she must at present be supposed to feel.", "만일 우리가 비극을 쓰는 사람이었다면, 독자는 이제 우리가 그 마침표에 거의 다다랐음을 인정해야 할 것이다. 악마든, 이 땅에 있는 그 어떤 악마의 대리인이든, 가엾은 존스를 위해 지난 장에서 우리가 그를 남겨 둔 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 고통을 꾸며 내기란 어려울 테니 말이다."], ["What then remains to complete the tragedy but a murder or two and a few moral sentences!", "소피아로 말하자면, 마음씨 고운 여인이라면 연적에게라도 지금 그녀가 느끼고 있으리라 짐작되는 것 이상의 괴로움을 바라지는 않을 것이다. 그렇다면 이 비극을 완성하기 위해 남은 것이 살인 한두 건과 교훈적인 문장 몇 줄 말고 또 무엇이 있겠는가!"]], [["But to bring our favourites out of their present anguish and distress, and to land them at last on the shore of happiness, seems a much harder task; a task indeed so hard that we do not undertake to execute it. In regard to Sophia, it is more than probable that we shall somewhere or other provide a good husband for her in the end--either Blifil, or my lord, or somebody else; but as to poor Jones, such are the calamities in which he is at present involved, owing to his imprudence, by which if a man doth not become felon to the world, he is at least a felo de se; so destitute is he now of friends, and so persecuted by enemies, that we almost despair of bringing him to any good; and if our reader delights in seeing executions, I think he ought not to lose any time in taking a first row at Tyburn.", "그러나 우리의 사랑하는 인물들을 지금의 고뇌와 곤경에서 끌어내어, 마침내 행복의 기슭에 내려 주는 일은 훨씬 더 힘든 과업으로 보인다. 실로 어찌나 힘든 과업인지, 우리는 그것을 해내겠다고 떠맡지조차 못하겠다. 소피아에 관해서라면, 우리가 끝에 가서 어딘가에서 그녀에게 좋은 남편을 마련해 주리라는 것이 십중팔구일 것이다. 블리필이든, 그분 나리든, 아니면 다른 누군가든 말이다. 그러나 가엾은 존스로 말하면, 그가 제 경솔함 탓에 지금 휘말려 있는 재앙이 어찌나 깊은지, 그런 경솔함 때문에 사람이 세상을 향한 중죄인까지는 되지 않더라도 적어도 스스로를 해치는 자가 되고 만다. 지금 그는 벗 하나 없이 외롭고 적들에게 시달리고 있어서, 우리는 그를 어떤 좋은 결말로 이끄는 일을 거의 단념할 지경이다. 그러니 우리 독자가 사형 집행 구경을 즐기는 분이라면, 한시바삐 타이번으로 가서 맨 앞줄을 차지하는 편이 좋으리라 생각한다."]], [["This I faithfully promise, that, notwithstanding any affection which we may be supposed to have for this rogue, whom we have unfortunately made our heroe, we will lend him none of that supernatural assistance with which we are entrusted, upon condition that we use it only on very important occasions. If he doth not therefore find some natural means of fairly extricating himself from all his distresses, we will do no violence to the truth and dignity of history for his sake; for we had rather relate that he was hanged at Tyburn (which may very probably be the case) than forfeit our integrity, or shock the faith of our reader.", "* 타이번: 당시 런던의 공개 처형장. 다만 이것만은 진심으로 약속하겠다. 불행히도 우리가 주인공으로 삼고 만 이 망나니에게 우리가 품고 있으리라 짐작되는 어떤 애정에도 불구하고, 우리는 그에게 그 초자연적인 도움의 손길을 조금도 빌려주지 않을 것이다. 그런 힘은 오직 매우 중대한 경우에만 쓰겠다는 조건 아래 우리에게 맡겨진 것이기 때문이다. 그러므로 그가 자신의 모든 곤경에서 정정당당하게 벗어날 어떤 자연스러운 방도를 찾아내지 못한다면, 우리는 그를 위해 역사의 진실과 품위를 함부로 해치지 않을 것이다. 우리로서는 우리의 진실성을 저버리거나 독자의 믿음에 충격을 주느니, 차라리 그가 타이번에서 교수형을 당했다고 적는 편이 낫겠다. 그리고 그렇게 될 공산이 다분하기도 하다."]], [["In this the antients had a great advantage over the moderns.", "이 점에서는 옛사람들이 요즘 사람들보다 큰 이점을 누렸다."], ["Their mythology, which was at that time more firmly believed by the vulgar than any religion is at present, gave them always an opportunity of delivering a favourite heroe.", "그 시절 일반 백성들에게는 그들의 신화가 오늘날 어떤 종교보다도 더 굳게 믿어졌고, 그 신화 덕분에 작가들은 언제든 좋아하는 주인공을 구해 낼 기회를 가질 수 있었다."], ["Their deities were always ready at the writer's elbow, to execute any of his purposes; and the more extraordinary the invention was, the greater was the surprize and delight of the credulous reader.", "그들의 신들은 작가가 어떤 목적이든 이루어 줄 채비를 갖추고 늘 작가 곁에 대기하고 있었으며, 그 꾸며 낸 이야기가 기상천외할수록 잘 속아 넘어가는 독자의 놀라움과 즐거움도 그만큼 컸다."], ["Those writers could with greater ease have conveyed a heroe from one country to another, nay from one world to another, and have brought him back again, than a poor circumscribed modern can deliver him from a jail.", "그런 작가들은 가엾은 처지에 매여 있는 오늘날의 작가가 주인공을 감옥에서 빼내는 것보다 훨씬 손쉽게, 주인공을 한 나라에서 다른 나라로, 아니 한 세계에서 다른 세계로 데려갔다가 다시 돌아오게 할 수 있었다."]], [["The Arabians and Persians had an equal advantage in writing their tales from the genii and fairies, which they believe in as an article of their faith, upon the authority of the Koran itself.", "아랍인과 페르시아인들도 그들의 이야기를 쓸 때 정령과 요정 덕분에 똑같은 이점을 누렸다. 그들은 코란 그 자체의 권위에 기대어 이 정령과 요정을 신앙의 한 조목으로 믿었던 것이다."], ["But we have none of these helps.", "그러나 우리에게는 이런 도움이 하나도 없다. 우리는 오로지 자연스러운 방도에만 매여 있다."], ["To natural means alone we are confined; let us try therefore what, by these means, may be done for poor Jones; though to confess the truth, something whispers me in the ear that he doth not yet know the worst of his fortune; and that a more shocking piece of news than any he hath yet heard remains for him in the unopened leaves of fate.", "그러니 이 방도로 가엾은 존스를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 한번 시험해 보자. 솔직히 털어놓자면, 무언가가 내 귀에 속삭이기를, 그는 아직 제 운명의 가장 나쁜 대목을 알지 못하며, 지금껏 들은 그 어떤 소식보다 더 충격적인 소식이 아직 펼쳐지지 않은 운명의 책장 속에 그를 기다리고 있다고 한다."]], [["Mr Allworthy and Mrs Miller were just sat down to breakfast, when Blifil, who had gone out very early that morning, returned to make one of the company.", "올워디 씨와 밀러 부인이 막 아침 식사를 하려고 자리에 앉았을 때, 그날 아침 아주 일찍 외출했던 블리필이 돌아와 그 자리에 합석했다."]], [["He had not been long seated before he began as follows: “Good Lord!", "그는 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아 다음과 같이 말문을 열었다. \"맙소사!"], ["my dear uncle, what do you think hath happened?", "사랑하는 외삼촌, 무슨 일이 있었는지 아십니까?"], ["I vow I am afraid of telling it you, for fear of shocking you with the remembrance of ever having shewn any kindness to such a villain.”", "정말이지 이걸 말씀드리기가 두렵습니다. 그런 악당에게 친절을 베푸셨던 일을 떠올리시며 충격받으실까 봐서요.\""], ["“What is the matter, child?”", "\"무슨 일이냐, 얘야?\""], ["said the uncle.", "외삼촌이 말했다."], ["“I fear I have shewn kindness in my life to the unworthy more than once.", "\"나도 살면서 한 번 이상은 자격 없는 자에게 친절을 베풀었을 게다."], ["But charity doth not adopt the vices of its objects.”", "그러나 자선이란 그 대상의 악덕까지 받아들이는 것은 아니지.\""], ["“O, sir!”", "\"오, 외삼촌!\""], ["returned Blifil, “it is not without the secret direction of Providence that you mention the word adoption.", "블리필이 대답했다. \"받아들인다는 말씀을 하시는 것도 섭리의 은밀한 인도가 없지 않은가 봅니다."], ["Your adopted son, sir, that Jones, that wretch whom you nourished in your bosom, hath proved one of the greatest villains upon earth.”", "외삼촌이 받아들이신 그 아들, 그 존스라는 자, 외삼촌이 품에 안아 길러 주신 그 비열한 놈이 이 세상에서 가장 큰 악당 중 하나임이 드러났습니다.\" \"신성한 모든 것을 걸고 말하건대, 그건 거짓이에요.\""], ["“By all that's sacred 'tis false,”", "밀러 부인이 외쳤다. \"존스 씨는 악당이 아닙니다."], ["cries Mrs Miller. “Mr Jones is no villain.", "그는 숨 쉬는 사람 중에 가장 훌륭한 분이에요."], ["He is one of the worthiest creatures breathing; and if any other person had called him villain, I would have thrown all this boiling water in his face.”", "다른 사람이 그분을 악당이라 불렀다면, 저는 이 끓는 물을 죄다 그자 얼굴에 끼얹었을 거예요.\" 올워디 씨는 이런 행동에 몹시 놀란 표정을 지었다."], ["Mr Allworthy looked very much amazed at this behaviour.", "그러나 부인은 그가 말할 틈도 주지 않고 그를 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"제게 화내지 않으시기를 바랍니다."], ["But she did not give him leave to speak, before, turning to him, she cried, “I hope you will not be angry with me; I would not offend you, sir, for the world; but, indeed, I could not bear to hear him called so.”", "세상을 다 준다 해도 어르신의 기분을 상하게 하고 싶지는 않아요. 하지만 정말이지 그분이 그렇게 불리는 것을 차마 들을 수가 없었어요.\""], ["“I must own, madam,”", "\"솔직히 말씀드리는데, 부인,\""], ["said Allworthy, very gravely, “I am a little surprized to hear you so warmly defend a fellow you do not know.”", "올워디가 아주 진지하게 말했다. \"잘 알지도 못하는 사내를 그토록 열을 올려 변호하시는 것을 들으니 조금 놀랍군요.\""], ["“O!", "\"오!"], ["I do know him, Mr Allworthy,”", "저는 그분을 알아요, 올워디 씨.\" 부인이 말했다."], ["said she, “indeed I do; I should be the most ungrateful of all wretches if I denied it.", "\"정말로 압니다. 그걸 부인한다면 저는 모든 사람 중에 가장 배은망덕한 자가 될 거예요."], ["O!", "오!"], ["he hath preserved me and my little family; we have all reason to bless him while we live.--And I pray Heaven to bless him, and turn the hearts of his malicious enemies.", "그분은 저와 제 어린 가족을 지켜 주셨어요. 우리 모두 살아 있는 동안 그분을 축복할 이유가 있답니다."], ["I know, I find, I see, he hath such.”", "그리고 저는 하늘이 그분을 축복하시고 그분을 악의로 미워하는 적들의 마음을 돌이켜 주시기를 기도합니다."], ["“You surprize me, madam, still more,”", "그분에게 그런 적들이 있다는 걸 저는 알고, 느끼고, 또 봅니다.\" \"부인, 더더욱 놀랍군요.\""], ["said Allworthy; “sure you must mean some other.", "올워디가 말했다. \"분명 다른 사람을 말씀하시는 게 틀림없습니다."], ["It is impossible you should have any such obligations to the man my nephew mentions.”", "제 조카가 말한 그 사람에게 부인이 그런 은혜를 입었을 리가 없어요.\" \"너무도 분명히,\""], ["“Too surely,”", "부인이 대답했다."], ["answered she, “I have obligations to him of the greatest and tenderest kind.", "\"저는 그분에게 가장 크고 가장 애틋한 은혜를 입었습니다. 그분은 저와 제 식구들을 지켜 주신 분이에요."], ["He hath been the preserver of me and mine.", "믿어 주세요, 어르신, 그분은 어르신께 모함을 당했어요, 지독한 모함을요. 저는 그렇다는 걸 압니다."], ["Believe me, sir, he hath been abused, grossly abused to you; I know he hath, or you, whom I know to be all goodness and honour, would not, after the many kind and tender things I have heard you say of this poor helpless child, have so disdainfully called him fellow.--Indeed, my best of friends, he deserves a kinder appellation from you, had you heard the good, the kind, the grateful things which I have heard him utter of you.", "그렇지 않고서야, 온통 선함과 명예로 이루어진 분이신 어르신께서, 이 가엾고 의지가지없는 아이를 두고 그토록 다정하고 애틋하게 말씀하시던 것을 제가 여러 번 들었는데, 어떻게 그를 그렇게 멸시하듯 '사내'라 부르셨겠어요. 정말이지, 제 가장 좋은 벗이여, 어르신께서 그분이 어르신에 대해 하던 그 착하고 다정하고 고마워하는 말들을 들으셨다면, 그분은 어르신께 더 다정한 호칭을 받을 자격이 있는 분이에요."], ["He never mentions your name but with a sort of adoration.", "그분은 어르신의 이름을 일종의 흠모 없이는 결코 입에 올리지 않으셨어요."], ["In this very room I have seen him on his knees, imploring all the blessings of heaven upon your head.", "바로 이 방에서, 저는 그분이 무릎을 꿇고 하늘의 온갖 축복이 어르신의 머리 위에 내리기를 간청하는 모습을 보았답니다."], ["I do not love that child there better than he loves you.”", "저기 저 아이를 사랑하는 마음도, 그분이 어르신을 사랑하는 마음만큼은 못 됩니다.\""]], [["“I see, sir, now,”", "\"이제 알겠습니다, 외삼촌.\""], ["said Blifil, with one of those grinning sneers with which the devil marks his best beloved, “Mrs Miller really doth know him.", "블리필이, 악마가 자기가 가장 아끼는 자에게 새겨 넣는 그런 이죽거리는 비웃음 가운데 하나를 지으며 말했다. \"밀러 부인이 정말로 그를 아는군요."], ["I suppose you will find she is not the only one of your acquaintance to whom he hath exposed you.", "짐작건대 그가 외삼촌의 험담을 늘어놓은 상대가 부인뿐만은 아니라는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다."], ["As for my character, I perceive, by some hints she hath thrown out, he hath been very free with it, but I forgive him.”", "제 평판으로 말하자면, 부인이 흘린 몇 마디 말로 보아 그가 그것을 아주 거리낌 없이 다룬 모양입니다만, 저는 그를 용서합니다.\""], ["“And the Lord forgive you, sir!”", "\"그리고 주님께서 당신을 용서하시기를, 도련님!\""], ["said Mrs Miller; “we have all sins enough to stand in need of his forgiveness.”", "밀러 부인이 말했다. \"우리 모두 그분의 용서가 필요할 만큼 죄가 많으니까요.\""]], [["“Upon my word, Mrs Miller,”", "\"솔직히 말하건대, 밀러 부인,\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “I do not take this behaviour of yours to my nephew kindly; and I do assure you, as any reflections which you cast upon him must come only from that wickedest of men, they would only serve, if that were possible, to heighten my resentment against him: for I must tell you, Mrs Miller, the young man who now stands before you hath ever been the warmest advocate for the ungrateful wretch whose cause you espouse.", "\"제 조카에 대한 당신의 이런 태도를 곱게 받아들일 수가 없군요. 그리고 분명히 말씀드리는데, 당신이 그에게 던지는 어떤 비난도 오직 그 가장 사악한 인간에게서 나온 것일 수밖에 없으니, 그런 비난은 그저, 만일 그게 가능하다면, 그에 대한 제 분노를 더 키우는 데나 쓰일 따름입니다."], ["This, I think, when you hear it from my own mouth, will make you wonder at so much baseness and ingratitude.”", "말씀드려야겠는데, 밀러 부인, 지금 당신 앞에 서 있는 이 젊은이야말로 당신이 편드는 그 배은망덕한 놈을 위해 늘 가장 열렬히 변호해 온 사람입니다. 이걸 제 입에서 직접 들으시면, 당신은 그토록 비열하고 배은망덕한 짓에 놀라시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“You are deceived, sir,” answered Mrs Miller; “if they were the last words which were to issue from my lips, I would say you were deceived; and I once more repeat it, the Lord forgive those who have deceived you! I do not pretend to say the young man is without faults; but they are all the faults of wildness and of youth; faults which he may, nay, which I am certain he will, relinquish, and, if he should not, they are vastly overbalanced by one of the most humane, tender, honest hearts that ever man was blest with.”", "\"속으셨어요, 어르신.\" 밀러 부인이 대답했다. \"이게 제 입에서 나오는 마지막 말이 된다 해도, 저는 어르신이 속으셨다고 말하겠어요. 그리고 다시 한 번 말씀드립니다. 어르신을 속인 자들을 주님께서 용서하시기를! 그 젊은이가 허물이 없다고 우기는 게 아닙니다. 하지만 그것들은 모두 혈기와 젊음에서 나온 허물이에요. 그분이 버릴 수 있는, 아니, 제가 확신하건대 반드시 버리게 될 허물입니다. 설령 그러지 못한다 해도, 그 허물들은 사람이 복으로 받은 것 중 가장 인정 많고 다정하고 정직한 마음 하나에 의해 한참 상쇄되고도 남아요.\""]], [["“Indeed, Mrs Miller,” said Allworthy, “had this been related of you, I should not have believed it.” “Indeed, sir,” answered she, “you will believe everything I have said, I am sure you will: and when you have heard the story which I shall tell you (for I will tell you all), you will be so far from being offended, that you will own (I know your justice so well), that I must have been the most despicable and most ungrateful of wretches if I had acted any other part than I have.”", "\"정말이지, 밀러 부인,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"이게 당신에 관한 이야기였다면, 저는 믿지 않았을 겁니다.\" \"정말이지, 어르신,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"어르신은 제가 한 모든 말을 믿게 되실 거예요. 분명히 그러시리라 믿습니다. 그리고 제가 들려드릴 이야기를 다 들으시고 나면 (전부 다 말씀드릴 테니까요), 어르신은 기분이 상하기는커녕, 오히려 인정하시게 될 거예요. (저는 어르신의 공정함을 잘 아니까요.) 제가 한 것과 다른 식으로 처신했다면 저야말로 가장 비열하고 가장 배은망덕한 자가 되었을 거라고요.\""]], [["“Well, madam,”", "\"좋습니다, 부인.\""], ["said Allworthy, “I shall be very glad to hear any good excuse for a behaviour which, I must confess, I think wants an excuse.", "올워디가 말했다. \"솔직히 변명이 필요하다고 생각되는 그 태도에 대해, 무엇이든 그럴듯한 변명을 듣게 된다면 매우 기쁘겠습니다."], ["And now, madam, will you be pleased to let my nephew proceed in his story without interruption.", "그런데 부인, 부디 제 조카가 끼어듦 없이 이야기를 계속하게 해 주시겠습니까."], ["He would not have introduced a matter of slight consequence with such a preface.", "그 아이가 사소한 일을 이런 서두로 꺼내지는 않았을 테니까요."], ["Perhaps even this story will cure you of your mistake.”", "어쩌면 이 이야기가 당신의 오해를 고쳐 줄지도 모르겠군요.\""]], [["Mrs Miller gave tokens of submission, and then Mr Blifil began thus: “I am sure, sir, if you don't think proper to resent the ill-usage of Mrs Miller, I shall easily forgive what affects me only.", "밀러 부인이 따르겠다는 뜻을 내비치자, 블리필 씨가 이렇게 말을 시작했다. \"분명히 말씀드리는데, 외삼촌, 밀러 부인의 무례한 처사에 분개하지 않으시겠다면, 저는 저 자신에게만 관계된 일이라면 쉽게 용서하겠습니다."], ["I think your goodness hath not deserved this indignity at her hands.”", "외삼촌의 선하심이 부인의 손에서 이런 모욕을 받을 만하지는 않다고 생각합니다만.\" \"좋다, 얘야.\""], ["“Well, child,”", "올워디가 말했다. \"그런데 이 새로운 사례라는 게 무엇이냐?"], ["said Allworthy, “but what is this new instance?", "그가 최근에 무슨 짓을 했단 말이냐?\" \"무슨 짓이냐고요.\""], ["What hath he done of late?”", "블리필이 외쳤다. \"밀러 부인이 그토록 말씀하셨음에도, 말씀드리기가 참으로 송구스럽습니다."], ["“What,”", "그리고 온 세상에 도저히 감출 수 없는 일이 아니었다면 제 입에서는 결코 들으실 일이 없었을 겁니다."], ["cries Blifil, “notwithstanding all Mrs Miller hath said, I am very sorry to relate, and what you should never have heard from me, had it not been a matter impossible to conceal from the whole world.", "한마디로, 그가 사람을 죽였습니다. 살해했다고는 하지 않겠어요."], ["In short he hath killed a man; I will not say murdered--for perhaps it may not be so construed in law, and I hope the best for his sake.”", "어쩌면 법적으로는 그렇게 해석되지 않을 수도 있으니까요. 그를 위해서라도 저는 최선의 결과를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Allworthy looked shocked, and blessed himself; and then, turning to Mrs Miller, he cried, “Well, madam, what say you now?”", "올워디는 충격을 받은 표정으로 성호를 그었다. 그러고는 밀러 부인을 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"자, 부인, 이제 무슨 말씀을 하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Why, I say, sir,”", "\"글쎄요, 저는 이렇게 말하겠어요, 어르신.\""], ["answered she, “that I never was more concerned at anything in my life; but, if the fact be true, I am convinced the man, whoever he is, was in fault.", "부인이 대답했다. \"살면서 이보다 더 마음 아픈 일은 없었습니다."], ["Heaven knows there are many villains in this town who make it their business to provoke young gentlemen.", "하지만 그게 사실이라면, 저는 그 상대가 누구든 간에 잘못은 그쪽에 있었다고 확신해요. 하늘은 아십니다, 이 도시에는 젊은 신사들을 도발하는 것을 일삼는 악당들이 많다는 걸요."], ["Nothing but the greatest provocation could have tempted him; for of all the gentlemen I ever had in my house, I never saw one so gentle or so sweet-tempered.", "더없이 큰 도발이 아니고서는 결코 그분을 자극할 수 없었을 거예요. 제 집에 묵었던 모든 신사 중에서, 저는 그분만큼 점잖고 다정한 성품을 가진 분을 본 적이 없으니까요."], ["He was beloved by every one in the house, and every one who came near it.”", "그분은 집안의 모든 사람에게, 그리고 그 근처에 오는 모든 사람에게 사랑받았답니다.\""]], [["While she was thus running on, a violent knocking at the door interrupted their conversation, and prevented her from proceeding further, or from receiving any answer; for, as she concluded this was a visitor to Mr Allworthy, she hastily retired, taking with her her little girl, whose eyes were all over blubbered at the melancholy news she heard of Jones, who used to call her his little wife, and not only gave her many playthings, but spent whole hours in playing with her himself.", "부인이 이렇게 거침없이 이야기를 이어 가는 동안, 문을 세차게 두드리는 소리가 그들의 대화를 가로막았고, 부인이 더 말을 잇거나 어떤 답을 듣는 것도 막아 버렸다. 부인은 이것이 올워디 씨를 찾아온 손님이리라 짐작하고는, 어린 딸을 데리고 황급히 물러났다. 그 아이의 두 눈은 존스에 관해 들은 서글픈 소식에 온통 눈물로 범벅이 되어 있었다. 존스는 그 아이를 자기의 어린 아내라 부르곤 했고, 여러 장난감을 주었을 뿐 아니라 몇 시간씩이나 손수 그 아이와 놀아 주던 사람이었다."]], [["Some readers may, perhaps, be pleased with these minute circumstances, in relating of which we follow the example of Plutarch, one of the best of our brother historians; and others, to whom they may appear trivial, will, we hope, at least pardon them, as we are never prolix on such occasions.", "어떤 독자들은 어쩌면 이런 자질구레한 정황을 즐거워할지도 모르겠다. 이런 것을 서술함에 있어 우리는 우리의 가장 훌륭한 동료 역사가 중 한 사람인 플루타르코스의 본을 따른다. 또 다른 독자들에게는 이런 것이 하찮아 보일지도 모르나, 우리는 이런 경우에 결코 장황하게 늘어놓는 법이 없으니, 부디 적어도 너그러이 보아 주기를 바란다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["The arrival of Mr Western, with some matters concerning the paternal authority.", "웨스턴 씨가 도착했을 때 밀러 부인이 방을 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않았다. 다만 그가 방에 들기 전, 그와 그의 가마꾼들 사이에 작은 실랑이 한바탕이 벌어졌다."], ["Mrs Miller had not long left the room when Mr Western entered; but not before a small wrangling bout had passed between him and his chairmen; for the fellows, who had taken up their burden at the Hercules Pillars, had conceived no hopes of having any future good customer in the squire; and they were moreover farther encouraged by his generosity (for he had given them of his own accord sixpence more than their fare); they therefore very boldly demanded another shilling, which so provoked the squire, that he not only bestowed many hearty curses on them at the door, but retained his anger after he came into the room; swearing that all the Londoners were like the court, and thought of nothing but plundering country gentlemen.", "헤라클레스 기둥에서 짐을 떠맡았던 이 사내들은 이 지주에게서 앞으로 좋은 단골이 되어 줄 가망을 조금도 품지 못한 데다, 그의 후한 인심에 한층 더 부추겨졌던 터였다(그가 제 손으로 삯보다 육 펜스를 더 얹어 주었으니 말이다). 그래서 그들은 아주 뻔뻔스럽게 일 실링을 더 내놓으라 요구했고, 이것이 지주를 어찌나 발끈하게 만들었던지, 그는 문간에서 그들에게 진심 어린 욕설을 한바탕 퍼부었을 뿐 아니라, 방에 들어온 뒤까지도 그 화를 거두지 못했다."], ["“D--n me,”", "런던 사람들은 죄다 궁정 인간들과 똑같아서 시골 양반들 등쳐 먹을 궁리밖에 할 줄 모른다고 욕을 해댔다. \"제기랄,\""], ["says he, “if I won't walk in the rain rather than get into one of their hand-barrows again.", "그가 말했다. \"또다시 저놈들 손수레짝에 올라타느니 차라리 빗속을 걸어가고 말겠소."], ["They have jolted me more in a mile than Brown Bess would in a long fox-chase.”", "저놈들이 일 마일 가는 동안 나를 흔들어댄 것이, 브라운 베스가 긴 여우 사냥 내내 흔드는 것보다 더하더이다.\""]], [["When his wrath on this occasion was a little appeased, he resumed the same passionate tone on another.", "이 일로 인한 노여움이 조금 가라앉자, 그는 또 다른 일을 두고 똑같이 격앙된 어조를 다시 꺼냈다."], ["“There,”", "\"이보시오,\""], ["says he, “there is fine business forwards now.", "그가 말했다. \"이제 아주 볼만한 일이 벌어지고 있소."], ["The hounds have changed at last; and when we imagined we had a fox to deal with, od-rat it, it turns out to be a badger at last!”", "사냥개들이 마침내 냄새를 바꿔 버렸지 뭐요. 우리가 여우를 상대하는 줄로만 알았더니, 빌어먹을, 끝에 가서 오소리로 드러났단 말이오!\""]], [["“Pray, my good neighbour,”", "\"이보시오, 친애하는 이웃,\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “drop your metaphors, and speak a little plainer.”", "\"그 비유 좀 그만두고, 좀 더 알아듣게 말해 보시오.\" \"그럼,\""], ["“Why, then,”", "지주가 말했다."], ["says the squire, “to tell you plainly, we have been all this time afraid of a son of a whore of a bastard of somebody's, I don't know whose, not I.", "\"솔직히 말해서, 우리는 이제껏 누군지도 모를 어떤 놈의 사생아, 그 매춘부 자식 놈을 두고 겁을 먹고 있었던 게요. 누구 자식인지 나야 알 게 뭐요."], ["And now here's a confounded son of a whore of a lord, who may be a bastard too for what I know or care, for he shall never have a daughter of mine by my consent.", "그런데 이제 와서 여기 빌어먹을 귀족 나리 매춘부 자식 놈이 나타났단 말이오. 내가 알기로도 신경 쓰기로도 그놈 역시 사생아일지 모르오."], ["They have beggared the nation, but they shall never beggar me.", "어쨌든 내 동의 아래 내 딸을 줄 일은 결코 없을 테니까. 그자들이 나라를 거덜 내긴 했어도, 나만은 결코 거덜 내지 못할 거요."], ["My land shall never be sent over to Hanover.”", "내 땅이 하노버로 넘어가는 일은 결코 없을 거란 말이오.\""]], [["“You surprize me much, my good friend,”", "\"참으로 놀랍구려, 친애하는 벗이여,\""], ["said Allworthy.", "올워디가 말했다."], ["“Why, zounds!", "\"허허, 이런!"], ["I am surprized myself,”", "나 자신도 놀랐소이다,\""], ["answered the squire.", "지주가 대답했다."], ["“I went to zee sister Western last night, according to her own appointment, and there I was had into a whole room full of women.", "\"어젯밤에 누이 웨스턴이 직접 잡아 둔 약속대로 그 사람을 보러 갔더니, 글쎄 여자들로 가득 찬 방으로 끌려 들어갔지 뭐요. 사촌 벨라스턴 마님이 계셨고, 베티 마님, 캐서린 마님, 그리고 누군지도 모를 마님까지 있더이다."], ["There was my lady cousin Bellaston, and my Lady Betty, and my Lady Catherine, and my lady I don't know who; d--n me, if ever you catch me among such a kennel of hoop-petticoat b--s!", "제기랄, 그 후프 페티코트 입은 암캐들 소굴에 또 나를 끼워 넣을 생각일랑 마시오! 빌어먹을, 차라리 내 사냥개들한테 쫓기는 편이 낫겠소."], ["D--n me, I'd rather be run by my own dogs, as one Acton was, that the story-book says was turned into a hare, and his own dogs killed un and eat un.", "옛날이야기 책에 나오는 그 액턴인가 하는 자처럼 말이오. 그자가 산토끼로 변해서 제 사냥개들한테 물려 죽고 잡아먹혔다지."], ["Od-rabbit it, no mortal was ever run in such a manner; if I dodged one way, one had me; if I offered to clap back, another snapped me.", "빌어먹을, 어느 인간도 그런 식으로 쫓겨 본 적은 없을 게요. 한쪽으로 피하면 한 여자가 나를 붙잡고, 되받아치려 들면 또 다른 여자가 나를 물어뜯었소."], ["`O!", "'오!"], ["certainly one of the greatest matches in England,'", "분명 영국에서 손꼽힐 만한 혼처지요.'"], ["says one cousin (here he attempted to mimic them); `A very advantageous offer indeed,'", "한 사촌이 그러더이다(여기서 그는 그들의 흉내를 내려 들었다). '참으로 더없이 유리한 제안이군요.'"], ["cries another cousin (for you must know they be all my cousins, thof I never zeed half o'", "또 다른 사촌이 소리치고요(그들이 죄다 내 사촌인 걸 알아 둬야 하오, 전엔 절반도 본 적 없는 자들인데도 말이오)."], ["um before). `Surely,'", "'확실히 말씀드리지만,'"], ["says that fat a--se b--, my Lady Bellaston, `cousin, you must be out of your wits to think of refusing such an offer.'”", "그 살집 두둑한 엉덩짝 암캐, 벨라스턴 마님이 그러더이다. '사촌, 그런 제안을 거절할 생각을 하다니, 제정신이 아니신 게 분명해요.'\""]], [["“Now I begin to understand,”", "\"이제 좀 알겠소,\""], ["says Allworthy; “some person hath made proposals to Miss Western, which the ladies of the family approve, but is not to your liking.”", "올워디가 말했다. \"누군가 웨스턴 양에게 청혼을 했고, 집안 부인들은 그것을 찬성하는데, 정작 그대 마음에는 들지 않는다는 거구려.\""]], [["“My liking!”", "\"내 마음이라!\""], ["said Western, “how the devil should it?", "웨스턴이 말했다. \"그게 어떻게 마음에 들겠소?"], ["I tell you it is a lord, and those are always volks whom you know I always resolved to have nothing to do with.", "내 말하지 않았소, 그자는 귀족이라고. 그리고 귀족이란, 내가 평생 상종하지 않기로 작정한 족속들임을 당신도 알지 않소."], ["Did unt I refuse a matter of vorty years' purchase now for a bit of land, which one o'", "한 귀족 놈이 제 사냥터로 삼고 싶어 했던 땅뙈기 하나를 두고, 사십 년치 임대료에 맞먹는 값을 내가 마다하지 않았소?"], ["um had a mind to put into a park, only because I would have no dealings with lords, and dost think I would marry my daughter zu?", "오로지 귀족들과는 거래를 안 하겠다는 이유 하나로 말이오. 그런데 내가 내 딸을 그런 놈에게 시집보낼 성싶소?"], ["Besides, ben't I engaged to you, and did I ever go off any bargain when I had promised?”", "게다가 나는 이미 당신과 약조를 맺지 않았소? 내가 한번 약속한 흥정에서 발을 뺀 적이 있기나 하오?\""]], [["“As to that point, neighbour,”", "\"그 점에 관해서라면, 이웃이여,\""], ["said Allworthy, “I entirely release you from any engagement.", "올워디가 말했다. \"나는 그대를 어떤 약속에서도 온전히 풀어 드리겠소."], ["No contract can be binding between parties who have not a full power to make it at the time, nor ever afterwards acquire the power of fulfilling it.”", "어떤 계약도, 맺을 당시에 그것을 맺을 온전한 권한이 없고, 그 후로도 그것을 이행할 권한을 얻지 못하는 당사자들 사이에서는 구속력을 가질 수 없는 법이오.\""]], [["“Slud! then,”", "\"이런!\""], ["answered Western, “I tell you I have power, and I will fulfil it.", "웨스턴이 대답했다. \"내 말하건대, 나에게는 권한이 있고, 나는 그것을 이행하겠소."], ["Come along with me directly to Doctors'", "당장 나와 함께 닥터스 코먼스로 갑시다. 내가 허가증을 받아 오겠소."], ["Commons, I will get a licence; and I will go to sister and take away the wench by force, and she shall ha un, or I will lock her up, and keep her upon bread and water as long as she lives.”", "그리고 누이한테 가서 그 계집애를 힘으로 끌어내 오겠소. 그 애는 그자와 혼인하든가, 아니면 내가 그 애를 가둬 놓고 죽을 때까지 빵과 물만 먹이며 살게 하겠소.\""]], [["“Mr Western,” said Allworthy, “shall I beg you will hear my full sentiments on this matter?”--“Hear thee; ay, to be sure I will,” answered he. “Why, then, sir,” cries Allworthy, “I can truly say, without a compliment either to you or the young lady, that when this match was proposed, I embraced it very readily and heartily, from my regard to you both. An alliance between two families so nearly neighbours, and between whom there had always existed so mutual an intercourse and good harmony, I thought a most desirable event; and with regard to the young lady, not only the concurrent opinion of all who knew her, but my own observation assured me that she would be an inestimable treasure to a good husband. I shall say nothing of her personal qualifications, which certainly are admirable; her good nature, her charitable disposition, her modesty, are too well known to need any panegyric: but she hath one quality which existed in a high degree in that best of women, who is now one of the first of angels, which, as it is not of a glaring kind, more commonly escapes observation; so little indeed is it remarked, that I want a word to express it. I must use negatives on this occasion. I never heard anything of pertness, or what is called repartee, out of her mouth; no pretence to wit, much less to that kind of wisdom which is the result only of great learning and experience, the affectation of which, in a young woman, is as absurd as any of the affectations of an ape. No dictatorial sentiments, no judicial opinions, no profound criticisms. Whenever I have seen her in the company of men, she hath been all attention, with the modesty of a learner, not the forwardness of a teacher. You'll pardon me for it, but I once, to try her only, desired her opinion on a point which was controverted between Mr Thwackum and Mr Square. To which she answered, with much sweetness, `You will pardon me, good Mr Allworthy; I am sure you cannot in earnest think me capable of deciding any point in which two such gentlemen disagree.' Thwackum and Square, who both alike thought themselves sure of a favourable decision, seconded my request. She answered with the same good humour, `I must absolutely be excused: for I will affront neither so much as to give my judgment on his side.' Indeed, she always shewed the highest deference to the understandings of men; a quality absolutely essential to the making a good wife. I shall only add, that as she is most apparently void of all affectation, this deference must be certainly real.”", "\"웨스턴 씨,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"이 일에 대한 나의 온전한 생각을 들어 봐 주시겠소?\"—\"들어 보라고? 아무렴, 듣고말고.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그럼, 그러지요.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"나는 그대에게도 그 젊은 아가씨에게도 빈말을 보태지 않고, 진심으로 말할 수 있소. 이 혼담이 처음 나왔을 때, 나는 두 분 모두에 대한 존중에서 그것을 아주 기꺼이, 진심으로 받아들였소. 이토록 가까운 이웃인 두 집안, 그리고 늘 서로 왕래하며 좋은 화목을 나누어 온 두 집안 사이의 결합이라니, 더없이 바람직한 일이라 여겼소. 그리고 그 아가씨로 말하자면, 그녀를 아는 모든 이의 한결같은 평판뿐 아니라 내 자신의 관찰만으로도, 그녀가 좋은 남편에게 더없이 값진 보배가 되리라 확신했소. 그녀의 빼어난 용모에 대해서는 더 말하지 않겠소. 그것은 분명 감탄할 만하니 말이오. 그녀의 선한 천성, 자애로운 마음씨, 정숙함은 너무도 잘 알려져 있어 따로 찬사가 필요치 않소. 그러나 그녀에게는 한 가지 자질이 있는데, 그것은 지금 천사들 중에서도 으뜸가는 자리에 있는 그 더없이 훌륭했던 여인에게 높은 정도로 깃들어 있던 것이오. 그것은 화려한 종류의 것이 아니어서, 흔히 사람들의 눈에 띄지 않고 지나치곤 하오. 실로 어찌나 드러나지 않는지, 나는 그것을 표현할 말조차 찾지 못하겠소. 이 자리에서는 부정의 방식으로 말할 수밖에 없겠소. 나는 그녀의 입에서 건방진 말이나, 이른바 재치 있는 응수라는 것을 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없소. 재기를 뽐내는 일도 없거니와, 오로지 깊은 학식과 경험에서만 나오는 그런 종류의 지혜를 내세우는 일은 더더욱 없소. 젊은 여인이 그런 것을 꾸며 보이는 일이란, 원숭이의 온갖 꾸밈만큼이나 우스꽝스러운 것이오. 단정 짓는 견해도, 재판관 같은 의견도, 심오한 비평도 없소. 내가 그녀를 남자들과 어울리는 자리에서 볼 때마다, 그녀는 늘 귀를 기울이는 모습이었소. 가르치려 드는 자의 주제넘음이 아니라, 배우는 자의 겸손함을 지니고 말이오. 이런 말을 하는 걸 용서하시오만, 나는 한번 그녀를 그저 시험해 보려고, 스웨컴 씨와 스퀘어 씨 사이에서 논쟁이 된 한 가지 문제에 대해 그녀의 의견을 청한 적이 있소. 그러자 그녀는 더없이 상냥하게 대답하더이다. '용서하세요, 친애하는 올워디 어른. 두 분 같은 신사분들이 의견을 달리하는 문제를 제가 가려낼 수 있으리라고는 진심으로 여기지 않으실 거예요.' 둘 다 똑같이 자기 쪽에 유리한 판정이 내려지리라 믿고 있던 스웨컴과 스퀘어가 내 청을 거들었소. 그녀는 똑같이 좋은 기색으로 대답하더이다. '저는 한사코 빠지겠어요. 어느 한 분 편을 들어 제 판단을 내놓아 다른 분을 모욕하는 일은 결코 하지 않겠어요.' 실로 그녀는 언제나 남자들의 식견에 더없는 경의를 보였소. 좋은 아내가 되는 데 절대적으로 필요한 자질이지요. 한 가지만 덧붙이겠소. 그녀에게는 어떤 꾸밈도 전혀 없음이 너무도 분명하니, 이 경의 역시 분명 진심에서 우러난 것임에 틀림없소.\""]], [["Here Blifil sighed bitterly; upon which Western, whose eyes were full of tears at the praise of Sophia, blubbered out, “Don't be chicken-hearted, for shat ha her, d--n me, shat ha her, if she was twenty times as good.”", "여기서 블리필이 비통하게 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그러자 소피아에 대한 칭찬에 두 눈 가득 눈물이 고여 있던 웨스턴이 엉엉 울먹이며 내뱉었다. \"기죽지 말게, 자네가 그 애를 차지할 테니까, 빌어먹을, 그 애가 지금보다 스무 배 더 훌륭하더라도 자네가 차지할 거야.\""]], [["“Remember your promise, sir,”", "\"약속을 잊지 마시오, 선생.\""], ["cried Allworthy, “I was not to be interrupted.”", "올워디가 소리쳤다. \"내 말을 중간에 끊지 않기로 했잖소.\""], ["“Well, shat unt,”", "\"알았소, 안 끊으리다.\""], ["answered the squire; “I won't speak another word.”", "지주가 대답했다. \"한마디도 더 안 하겠소.\""]], [["“Now, my good friend,”", "\"자, 친애하는 벗이여,\""], ["continued Allworthy, “I have dwelt so long on the merit of this young lady, partly as I really am in love with her character, and partly that fortune (for the match in that light is really advantageous on my nephew's side) might not be imagined to be my principal view in having so eagerly embraced the proposal.", "올워디가 말을 이었다. \"내가 이 아가씨의 미덕을 이토록 오래 늘어놓은 까닭은, 한편으로는 내가 정말로 그녀의 인품에 반했기 때문이고, 다른 한편으로는 내가 이 제안을 그토록 열렬히 받아들인 데에 재산이 주된 속셈이었다고 여겨지지 않게 하기 위함이오(그런 관점에서 보자면 이 혼담은 사실 내 조카 쪽에 유리한 것이니 말이오)."], ["Indeed, I heartily wished to receive so great a jewel into my family; but though I may wish for many good things, I would not, therefore, steal them, or be guilty of any violence or injustice to possess myself of them.", "실로 나는 그토록 귀한 보석을 내 집안에 들이기를 진심으로 바랐소. 그러나 나는 좋은 것을 많이 바랄 수는 있어도, 그렇다고 그것을 훔치거나, 그것을 차지하기 위해 어떤 폭력이나 불의를 저지르지는 않겠소."], ["Now to force a woman into a marriage contrary to her consent or approbation, is an act of such injustice and oppression, that I wish the laws of our country could restrain it; but a good conscience is never lawless in the worst regulated state, and will provide those laws for itself, which the neglect of legislators hath forgotten to supply.", "한 여인을 그 동의나 승낙에 어긋나게 혼인으로 몰아넣는 것은, 우리 나라 법이 그것을 막아 줄 수 있었으면 싶을 만큼 부당하고 억압적인 행위요. 그러나 선한 양심은 가장 형편없이 다스려지는 나라에서도 결코 무법이 아니어서, 입법자들의 태만이 미처 마련하지 못한 그 법들을 스스로 자기 안에 갖추는 법이오."], ["This is surely a case of that kind; for, is it not cruel, nay, impious, to force a woman into that state against her will; for her behaviour in which she is to be accountable to the highest and most dreadful court of judicature, and to answer at the peril of her soul?", "이것은 분명 그런 부류의 경우요. 한 여인을 제 뜻에 어긋나게 그 상태로 몰아넣는 것이, 어찌 잔인한 일이 아니겠소, 아니 불경한 일이 아니겠소?"], ["To discharge the matrimonial duties in an adequate manner is no easy task; and shall we lay this burthen upon a woman, while we at the same time deprive her of all that assistance which may enable her to undergo it?", "그 상태에서의 처신에 대해 그녀는 가장 높고 가장 두려운 심판의 법정 앞에서 책임을 져야 하고, 제 영혼을 걸고 답해야 하거늘 말이오. 부부의 의무를 마땅한 방식으로 다해 내는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니오."], ["Shall we tear her very heart from her, while we enjoin her duties to which a whole heart is scarce equal?", "그런데 우리가 이 짐을 한 여인에게 지우면서, 동시에 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 있게 해 줄 모든 도움을 그녀에게서 빼앗는단 말이오? 온 마음을 다해도 감당하기 힘든 의무를 그녀에게 지우면서, 정작 그녀의 바로 그 마음을 가슴에서 뜯어낸단 말이오?"], ["I must speak very plainly here.", "여기서는 아주 솔직히 말해야겠소."], ["I think parents who act in this manner are accessories to all the guilt which their children afterwards incur, and of course must, before a just judge, expect to partake of their punishment; but if they could avoid this, good heaven!", "이런 식으로 행동하는 부모들은, 그 자식들이 훗날 짓게 되는 모든 죄의 공범이며, 따라서 정의로운 심판자 앞에서 그 벌을 함께 나눠 받게 되리라 각오해야 마땅하오. 그러나 설령 그 벌을 피할 수 있다 한들, 오 하느님!"], ["is there a soul who can bear the thought of having contributed to the damnation of his child?", "제 자식의 영원한 파멸에 일조했다는 생각을 견뎌 낼 수 있는 영혼이 어디 있겠소?"]], [["“For these reasons, my best neighbour, as I see the inclinations of this young lady are most unhappily averse to my nephew, I must decline any further thoughts of the honour you intended him, though I assure you I shall always retain the most grateful sense of it.”", "\"이런 까닭으로, 더없이 좋은 이웃이여, 나는 이 아가씨의 마음이 더없이 불행하게도 내 조카에게서 등을 돌리고 있음을 보았기에, 그대가 그 애에게 베풀려 했던 영예에 대한 더 이상의 생각은 접어야겠소. 다만 그 영예에 대해서는 내가 언제나 더없이 고마운 마음을 간직하리라는 것만은 분명히 말해 두겠소.\""]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"좋소, 선생.\""], ["said Western (the froth bursting forth from his lips the moment they were uncorked), “you cannot say but I have heard you out, and now I expect you'll hear me; and if I don't answer every word on't, why then I'll consent to gee the matter up.", "웨스턴이 말했다(마개를 뽑자마자 거품이 터져 나오듯 말이 쏟아졌다). \"내가 당신 말을 끝까지 들어 줬다는 것만은 부인 못 할 거요."], ["First then, I desire you to answer me one question--Did not I beget her?", "이제 당신이 내 말을 들어 줄 차례요. 만일 내가 그 말 한 마디 한 마디에 답하지 못한다면, 그땐 이 일을 깨끗이 포기하는 데 동의하리다."], ["did not I beget her?", "우선 한 가지만 대답해 보시오—내가 그 애를 낳지 않았소? 내가 그 애를 낳지 않았느냐 말이오?"], ["answer me that.", "그것부터 답해 보시오."], ["They say, indeed, it is a wise father that knows his own child; but I am sure I have the best title to her, for I bred her up.", "하긴 제 자식을 아는 아비야말로 현명한 아비라고들 하지요. 허나 나야말로 그 애에 대한 가장 떳떳한 권리를 가진 사람이오."], ["But I believe you will allow me to be her father, and if I be, am I not to govern my own child?", "내가 그 애를 길러 냈으니까. 어쨌든 당신도 내가 그 애 아비라는 건 인정할 거요."], ["I ask you that, am I not to govern my own child?", "그럼 내가 아비라면, 내 자식을 내가 다스려야 하지 않겠소?"], ["and if I am to govern her in other matters, surely I am to govern her in this, which concerns her most.", "그걸 묻는 거요, 내 자식을 내가 다스리면 안 되는 거요? 다른 일들에서 그 애를 다스려야 한다면, 그 애에게 가장 중대한 이 일에서도 마땅히 내가 다스려야 하는 것 아니오?"], ["And what am I desiring all this while?", "게다가 그동안 내내 내가 바라는 게 대체 무엇이오?"], ["Am I desiring her to do anything for me?", "내가 그 애더러 나를 위해 뭘 하라 하오? 나에게 뭘 달라 하오?—오히려 정반대요."], ["to give me anything?--Zu much on t'other side, that I am only desiring her to take away half my estate now, and t'other half when I die.", "나는 그저 그 애더러 지금 내 재산의 절반을 가져가고, 나머지 절반은 내가 죽으면 가져가라는 것뿐이오. 그래, 이게 다 무엇을 위해서겠소?"], ["Well, and what is it all vor?", "다 그 애를 행복하게 해 주려는 것 아니오?"], ["Why, is unt it to make her happy?", "사람들 떠드는 소리를 듣고 있자면 미칠 노릇이오."], ["It's enough to make one mad to hear volks talk; if I was going to marry myself, then she would ha reason to cry and to blubber; but, on the contrary, han't I offered to bind down my land in such a manner, that I could not marry if I would, seeing as narro'", "내가 만약 나 자신이 장가들려는 거라면, 그 애가 울고불고할 까닭이 있겠지. 허나 오히려 정반대로, 나는 내 땅을 묶어 놓아서 설령 내가 장가들고 싶어도 못 들게 하겠다고까지 했소."], ["woman upon earth would ha me.", "이 땅 위에 나를 데려갈 여자가 어디 한 명이라도 있겠소만."], ["What the devil in hell can I do more?", "빌어먹을, 내가 그 이상 뭘 더 할 수 있단 말이오?"], ["I contribute to her damnation!--Zounds!", "내가 그 애를 파멸로 몰아넣는다고!—이런 제기랄!"], ["I'd zee all the world d--n'd bevore her little vinger should be hurt.", "그 애의 새끼손가락 하나라도 다치게 하느니, 나는 차라리 온 세상이 다 망하는 꼴을 보겠소."], ["Indeed, Mr Allworthy, you must excuse me, but I am surprized to hear you talk in zuch a manner, and I must say, take it how you will, that I thought you had more sense.”", "정말이지, 올워디 씨, 미안하오만, 당신이 그런 식으로 말하는 걸 들으니 놀랍기 그지없소. 그리고 어떻게 받아들이든 한마디 해야겠는데, 나는 당신이 좀 더 분별이 있는 사람인 줄 알았소.\""]], [["Allworthy resented this reflection only with a smile; nor could he, if he would have endeavoured it, have conveyed into that smile any mixture of malice or contempt.", "올워디는 이 빈정거림을 그저 미소로 받아넘겼다. 설령 그가 애를 썼다 한들, 그 미소에 악의나 멸시의 기색을 조금이라도 담아낼 수는 없었으리라."], ["His smiles at folly were indeed such as we may suppose the angels bestow on the absurdities of mankind.", "어리석음을 향한 그의 미소란, 천사들이 인류의 터무니없는 짓들에 보낼 법한 바로 그런 미소였다."]], [["Blifil now desired to be permitted to speak a few words. “As to using any violence on the young lady, I am sure I shall never consent to it. My conscience will not permit me to use violence on any one, much less on a lady for whom, however cruel she is to me, I shall always preserve the purest and sincerest affection; but yet I have read that women are seldom proof against perseverance. Why may I not hope then by such perseverance at last to gain those inclinations, in which for the future I shall, perhaps, have no rival; for as for this lord, Mr Western is so kind to prefer me to him; and sure, sir, you will not deny but that a parent hath at least a negative voice in these matters; nay, I have heard this very young lady herself say so more than once, and declare that she thought children inexcusable who married in direct opposition to the will of their parents. Besides, though the other ladies of the family seem to favour the pretensions of my lord, I do not find the lady herself is inclined to give him any countenance; alas! I am too well assured she is not; I am too sensible that wickedest of men remains uppermost in her heart.”", "이제 블리필이 몇 마디 하게 해 달라고 청했다. \"그 아가씨에게 어떤 폭력을 쓰는 일이라면, 저는 결코 동의하지 않을 것입니다. 제 양심은 그 누구에게도 폭력을 쓰는 것을 허락하지 않으니, 하물며 한 아가씨에게야 더 말할 것도 없습니다. 그녀가 제게 아무리 모질게 군다 해도, 저는 그녀를 향해 언제나 더없이 순수하고 진실한 애정을 간직할 것입니다. 그렇지만 저는 여자들이 꾸준한 인내 앞에서는 좀처럼 버티지 못한다는 글을 읽은 적이 있습니다. 그러니 그런 꾸준함으로 마침내 그녀의 마음을 얻으리라 바라면 안 될 까닭이 무엇이겠습니까? 더욱이 앞으로는 제게 경쟁자가 없을지도 모르는 일이고요. 이 귀족으로 말하자면, 웨스턴 씨께서 저를 그자보다 더 아껴 주시니 말입니다. 그리고 분명, 어른, 이런 일에서 부모가 적어도 거부할 발언권만큼은 가진다는 것을 어른께서도 부인하지 않으실 겁니다. 아니, 저는 바로 그 아가씨 자신이 한두 번이 아니라 그렇게 말하는 것을 들은 적이 있습니다. 부모의 뜻에 정면으로 맞서 혼인하는 자식은 변명의 여지가 없다고 그녀가 단언했지요. 게다가 집안의 다른 부인들은 그 귀족의 청혼을 두둔하는 듯하나, 정작 그 아가씨 자신은 그자에게 조금의 호의도 보일 마음이 없어 보입니다. 아아! 저는 그녀가 그렇지 않음을 너무도 잘 알고 있습니다. 저 가장 사악한 사내가 그녀의 마음속에 여전히 가장 높은 자리를 차지하고 있음을, 저는 너무도 절절히 느끼고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, so he does,”", "\"그래, 그래, 정말 그렇다네.\""], ["cries Western.", "웨스턴이 소리쳤다."]], [["“But surely,”", "\"하지만 분명,\""], ["says Blifil, “when she hears of this murder which he hath committed, if the law should spare his life----”", "블리필이 말했다. \"그자가 저지른 이 살인 소식을 그녀가 듣게 되면, 설령 법이 그자의 목숨만은 살려 준다 하더라도——\""]], [["“What's that?”", "\"그게 무슨 소리냐?\""], ["cries Western.", "웨스턴이 소리쳤다."], ["“Murder!", "\"살인이라!"], ["hath he committed a murder, and is there any hopes of seeing him hanged?--Tol de rol, tol lol de rol.”", "그놈이 살인을 저질렀단 말이냐, 그래서 그놈이 교수형 당하는 꼴을 볼 가망이 있단 말이냐?—툴 드 롤, 툴 롤 드 롤.\""], ["Here he fell a singing and capering about the room.", "여기서 그는 노래를 부르며 방 안을 깡충깡충 뛰어다니기 시작했다."]], [["“Child,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["says Allworthy, “this unhappy passion of yours distresses me beyond measure.", "올워디가 말했다. \"너의 이 불행한 연정이 나를 더없이 괴롭게 하는구나."], ["I heartily pity you, and would do every fair thing to promote your success.”", "나는 진심으로 너를 가엾이 여기고, 네가 뜻을 이루도록 정당한 일이라면 무엇이든 해 주고 싶다.\""]], [["“I desire no more,”", "\"그 이상은 바라지 않습니다.\""], ["cries Blifil; “I am convinced my dear uncle hath a better opinion of me than to think that I myself would accept of more.”", "블리필이 소리쳤다. \"친애하는 외숙께서 저를 좋게 보아 주시니, 제가 그 이상을 받아들이려 들 사람이라고는 여기지 않으실 줄 압니다.\""]], [["“Lookee,”", "\"이보게,\""], ["says Allworthy, “you have my leave to write, to visit, if she will permit it--but I insist on no thoughts of violence.", "올워디가 말했다. \"그녀가 허락한다면 편지를 쓰고 찾아가는 것은 내가 허락하마—허나 폭력은 결코 생각도 말아야 한다."], ["I will have no confinement, nothing of that kind attempted.”", "가둬 두는 일도, 그런 종류의 어떤 시도도 나는 결단코 용납하지 않겠다.\""]], [["“Well, well,” cries the squire, “nothing of that kind shall be attempted; we will try a little longer what fair means will effect; and if this fellow be but hanged out of the way--Tol lol de rol! I never heard better news in my life--I warrant everything goes to my mind.--Do, prithee, dear Allworthy, come and dine with me at the Hercules Pillars: I have bespoke a shoulder of mutton roasted, and a spare-rib of pork, and a fowl and egg-sauce. There will be nobody but ourselves, unless we have a mind to have the landlord; for I have sent Parson Supple down to Basingstoke after my tobacco-box, which I left at an inn there, and I would not lose it for the world; for it is an old acquaintance of above twenty years' standing. I can tell you landlord is a vast comical bitch, you will like un hugely.”", "\"좋소, 좋아.\" 지주가 소리쳤다. \"그런 짓은 결코 하지 않으리다. 좀 더 오래 점잖은 방법으로 어찌 되나 한번 해 봅시다. 그리고 이놈만 교수형으로 깨끗이 치워진다면—툴 롤 드 롤! 내 평생 이보다 더 반가운 소식은 들어 본 적이 없소—만사가 내 뜻대로 풀릴 게 분명하오.—자, 부디, 친애하는 올워디, 헤라클레스 기둥으로 가서 나와 함께 식사합시다. 양 어깨살 구이에다, 돼지 갈비, 닭고기에 달걀 소스까지 시켜 두었소. 우리 둘 말고는 아무도 없을 거요. 정 마음 내키면 주막 주인이나 불러도 좋고. 서플 목사는 내가 베이징스토크로 내려보냈소. 거기 어느 여관에 두고 온 내 담뱃갑을 가지러 말이오. 그건 무슨 일이 있어도 잃을 수 없소. 이십 년 넘게 함께한 오랜 벗이거든. 그 주막 주인이 아주 익살맞은 친구라, 당신도 그자를 무척 마음에 들어 할 거요.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy at last agreed to this invitation, and soon after the squire went off, singing and capering at the hopes of seeing the speedy tragical end of poor Jones.", "올워디 씨는 마침내 이 초대에 응했고, 곧이어 지주는 가엾은 존스의 빠른 비극적 최후를 보게 되리라는 희망에 노래를 부르고 깡충거리며 떠나갔다."]], [["When he was gone, Mr Allworthy resumed the aforesaid subject with much gravity.", "그가 떠난 뒤, 올워디 씨는 앞서의 그 화제를 자못 진지하게 다시 꺼냈다. 그는 조카에게 말했다."], ["He told his nephew, “He wished with all his heart he would endeavour to conquer a passion, in which I cannot,”", "\"나는 진심으로, 네가 이 연정을 이겨 내려 애써 주기를 바란다. 그것에 대해서는,\""], ["says he, “flatter you with any hopes of succeeding.", "그가 말했다. \"내가 너에게 성공할 어떤 희망도 빈말로 안겨 줄 수가 없구나."], ["It is certainly a vulgar error, that aversion in a woman may be conquered by perseverance.", "꾸준함으로 여자의 반감을 이겨 낼 수 있다는 것은 분명 흔한 착각이다. 무관심이라면 어쩌면 이따금 거기에 굴복할지도 모르지."], ["Indifference may, perhaps, sometimes yield to it; but the usual triumphs gained by perseverance in a lover are over caprice, prudence, affectation, and often an exorbitant degree of levity, which excites women not over-warm in their constitutions to indulge their vanity by prolonging the time of courtship, even when they are well enough pleased with the object, and resolve (if they ever resolve at all) to make him a very pitiful amends in the end.", "그러나 연인이 꾸준함으로 거두는 통상의 승리란, 변덕이나 신중함, 꾸밈, 그리고 흔히는 도를 넘은 가벼움을 상대로 한 것이다. 천성이 그리 정열적이지 않은 여자들은 그런 가벼움 탓에, 설령 그 상대가 제법 마음에 들고 (애초에 마음을 정하기나 한다면) 끝내 그에게 보상해 주기로 마음먹은 경우에도, 구애의 기간을 질질 끌어 가며 제 허영을 부리려 들거든."], ["But a fixed dislike, as I am afraid this is, will rather gather strength than be conquered by time.", "그러나 확고한 혐오는—이것이 바로 그런 경우일까 봐 나는 두렵다만—시간이 흐를수록 이겨지기는커녕 오히려 힘을 더해 가는 법이다."], ["Besides, my dear, I have another apprehension which you must excuse.", "게다가, 얘야, 나에게는 또 다른 염려가 있는데, 이건 부디 양해해 다오."], ["I am afraid this passion which you have for this fine young creature hath her beautiful person too much for its object, and is unworthy of the name of that love which is the only foundation of matrimonial felicity.", "나는 네가 이 어여쁜 아가씨에게 품은 이 연정이 그녀의 아름다운 외모를 지나치게 그 대상으로 삼고 있어서, 부부의 행복의 유일한 토대인 그 사랑의 이름에 걸맞지 않은 것은 아닐까 두렵다. 아름다운 여인을 향해, 그녀가 우리에게 품은 마음 따위는 아랑곳없이, 감탄하고 좋아하고 그녀를 차지하기를 갈망하는 것—그것은 너무도 자연스러운 일일까 봐 나는 두렵구나."], ["To admire, to like, and to long for the possession of a beautiful woman, without any regard to her sentiments towards us, is, I am afraid, too natural; but love, I believe, is the child of love only; at least, I am pretty confident that to love the creature who we are assured hates us is not in human nature.", "그러나 사랑이란, 내가 믿기로는, 오직 사랑에서만 태어나는 자식이다. 적어도 우리를 미워한다고 확신되는 사람을 사랑하는 것은 인간의 본성에 없는 일이라고, 나는 꽤 확신한다."], ["Examine your heart, therefore, thoroughly, my good boy, and if, upon examination, you have but the least suspicion of this kind, I am sure your own virtue and religion will impel you to drive so vicious a passion from your heart, and your good sense will soon enable you to do it without pain.”", "그러니, 착한 아이야, 네 마음을 샅샅이 살펴보아라. 그리하여 살펴본 끝에 이런 종류의 의심이 조금이라도 든다면, 분명 너 자신의 덕과 신앙심이 그토록 사악한 연정을 네 가슴에서 몰아내도록 너를 떠밀 것이고, 너의 좋은 분별력은 머지않아 네가 그것을 고통 없이 해내게 해 줄 것이다.\""]], [["The reader may pretty well guess Blifil's answer; but, if he should be at a loss, we are not at present at leisure to satisfy him, as our history now hastens on to matters of higher importance, and we can no longer bear to be absent from Sophia.", "블리필의 대답은 독자도 어느 정도 짐작할 수 있으리라. 그러나 만일 독자가 짐작이 잘 안 되더라도, 지금 우리는 그것을 채워 줄 겨를이 없다. 우리 이야기는 이제 한층 더 중요한 문제들을 향해 서둘러 나아가고 있으며, 우리는 더 이상 소피아 곁을 떠나 있는 것을 견딜 수가 없기 때문이다."]], [["An extraordinary scene between Sophia and her aunt.", "소피아와 그녀의 고모 사이에 벌어진 기이한 한 장면."]], [["The lowing heifer and the bleating ewe, in herds and flocks, may ramble safe and unregarded through the pastures.", "음매 우는 암송아지와 매애 우는 암양은 떼를 지어 무리 속에서 안전하게, 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채로 목초지를 거닐 수 있다."], ["These are, indeed, hereafter doomed to be the prey of man; yet many years are they suffered to enjoy their liberty undisturbed.", "사실 이들도 훗날에는 인간의 먹잇감이 될 운명이지만, 그래도 여러 해 동안은 방해받지 않고 자유를 누리도록 허락받는다. 그러나 살집 좋은 암사슴 한 마리가 숲에서 빠져나와 어느 들판이나 작은 숲에서 쉬고 있는 것이 발각되면, 온 교구가 당장 발칵 뒤집힌다."], ["But if a plump doe be discovered to have escaped from the forest, and to repose herself in some field or grove, the whole parish is presently alarmed, every man is ready to set his dogs after her; and, if she is preserved from the rest by the good squire, it is only that he may secure her for his own eating.", "너 나 할 것 없이 모두가 사슴을 쫓아 개를 풀 준비를 한다. 그리고 만일 인심 좋은 지주가 그 사슴을 다른 이들로부터 지켜 준다면, 그것은 오직 자기가 잡아먹기 위해 따로 확보해 두려는 것일 뿐이다."]], [["I have often considered a very fine young woman of fortune and fashion, when first found strayed from the pale of her nursery, to be in pretty much the same situation with this doe. The town is immediately in an uproar; she is hunted from park to play, from court to assembly, from assembly to her own chamber, and rarely escapes a single season from the jaws of some devourer or other; for, if her friends protect her from some, it is only to deliver her over to one of their own chusing, often more disagreeable to her than any of the rest; while whole herds or flocks of other women securely, and scarce regarded, traverse the park, the play, the opera, and the assembly; and though, for the most part at least, they are at last devoured, yet for a long time do they wanton in liberty, without disturbance or controul.", "나는 재산과 지체를 갖춘 매우 아리따운 젊은 여성이 양육이라는 울타리 밖으로 처음 길을 벗어난 모습을 볼 때면, 그녀가 이 암사슴과 거의 똑같은 처지에 놓여 있다고 자주 생각해 왔다. 도시는 곧장 떠들썩해진다. 그녀는 공원에서 연극장으로, 궁정에서 모임으로, 모임에서 다시 제 방으로 쫓겨 다니며, 어느 한 철도 이런저런 포식자의 아가리에서 좀처럼 벗어나지 못한다. 친구들이 어떤 자에게서 그녀를 지켜 준다 해도, 그것은 다만 그들 자신이 골라 둔 누군가에게 그녀를 넘겨주기 위함일 뿐이며, 그 상대는 흔히 다른 누구보다도 그녀에게 더 못마땅한 인물이다. 그러는 동안 다른 여자들은 떼를 지어 무리째 안전하게, 거의 눈길도 받지 않은 채 공원과 연극장과 오페라와 모임을 누비고 다닌다. 비록 적어도 대개는 그들도 결국에는 잡아먹히고 말지만, 그래도 오랫동안은 방해도 통제도 없이 자유를 마음껏 즐긴다."]], [["Of all these paragons none ever tasted more of this persecution than poor Sophia.", "이 모든 본보기 같은 여인들 가운데, 가엾은 소피아만큼 이 박해를 더 톡톡히 맛본 이는 일찍이 없었다."], ["Her ill stars were not contented with all that she had suffered on account of Blifil, they now raised her another pursuer, who seemed likely to torment her no less than the other had done.", "그녀의 불운한 별은 블리필 때문에 그녀가 겪은 그 모든 고초로도 만족하지 못하고, 이제 또 다른 추격자를 일으켜 세웠으니, 그자는 앞서의 인물 못지않게 그녀를 괴롭힐 듯이 보였다."], ["For though her aunt was less violent, she was no less assiduous in teizing her, than her father had been before.", "비록 고모는 덜 격렬했지만, 그녀를 들들 볶는 데 있어서만큼은 앞서 아버지가 그랬던 것에 조금도 뒤지지 않았다."]], [["The servants were no sooner departed after dinner than Mrs Western, who had opened the matter to Sophia, informed her, “That she expected his lordship that very afternoon, and intended to take the first opportunity of leaving her alone with him.”", "저녁 식사가 끝나고 하인들이 물러가자마자, 이미 소피아에게 이 일을 꺼낸 적 있는 웨스턴 부인이 그녀에게 알렸다. \"바로 그날 오후에 백작께서 오시기를 기대하고 있으며, 첫 기회가 오는 대로 너를 그분과 단둘이 남겨 둘 작정이다.\""], ["“If you do, madam,”", "\"그러시면, 고모님,\""], ["answered Sophia, with some spirit, “I shall take the first opportunity of leaving him by himself.”", "소피아가 다소 기개 있게 대답했다. \"저는 첫 기회가 오는 대로 그분을 홀로 남겨 두고 자리를 뜨겠어요.\""], ["“How!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["madam!”", "얘야!\""], ["cries the aunt; “is this the return you make me for my kindness in relieving you from your confinement at your father's?”", "고모가 외쳤다. \"내가 네 아버지 집에서 갇혀 지내던 너를 풀어 준 그 친절에 대해, 이게 네가 갚는 보답이냐?\""], ["“You know, madam,”", "\"고모님도 아시잖아요,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["said Sophia, “the cause of that confinement was a refusal to comply with my father in accepting a man I detested; and will my dear aunt, who hath relieved me from that distress, involve me in another equally bad?”", "\"그렇게 갇혀 지낸 까닭은, 제가 진저리 치는 남자를 받아들이라는 아버지의 뜻을 따르지 못하겠다고 거절했기 때문이에요. 그런데 그 괴로움에서 저를 구해 주신 사랑하는 고모님께서, 똑같이 나쁜 또 다른 괴로움 속으로 저를 밀어 넣으시려는 건가요?\""], ["“And do you think then, madam,”", "\"그러면 너는, 얘야,\""], ["answered Mrs Western, “that there is no difference between my Lord Fellamar and Mr Blifil?”", "웨스턴 부인이 대답했다. \"펠라마 백작님과 블리필 씨 사이에 아무 차이가 없다고 생각하는 거냐?\""], ["“Very little, in my opinion,”", "\"제 생각엔 차이가 거의 없어요,\" 소피아가 외쳤다."], ["cries Sophia; “and, if I must be condemned to one, I would certainly have the merit of sacrificing myself to my father's pleasure.”", "\"그리고 둘 중 하나를 받아들이도록 단죄받아야 한다면, 저는 분명 제 자신을 아버지의 만족을 위해 희생한다는 떳떳함이라도 가지겠어요.\" \"그러니까 내 만족 따위는,\""], ["“Then my pleasure, I find,”", "고모가 말했다. \"네게 거의 무게가 없다는 거로구나."], ["said the aunt, “hath very little weight with you; but that consideration shall not move me.", "하지만 그런 따위 생각은 나를 움직이지 못한다. 나는 더 고귀한 동기로 움직이는 사람이야."], ["I act from nobler motives.", "우리 가문을 드높이고, 너 자신을 귀하게 만드는 것, 그것이 내가 나아가는 방향이다."], ["The view of aggrandizing my family, of ennobling yourself, is what I proceed upon.", "너는 야심이라는 감각이 전혀 없느냐? 네 마차 위에 백작 관(冠)을 얹는다는 생각에 아무런 매력도 느끼지 못한단 말이냐?\""], ["Have you no sense of ambition?", "\"조금도요, 제 명예를 걸고요,\""], ["Are there no charms in the thoughts of having a coronet on your coach?”", "소피아가 말했다. \"제 마차 위에는 바늘꽂이를 얹는 편이 차라리 똑같이 흡족하겠어요.\""], ["“None, upon my honour,”", "\"명예 따위는 입에 올리지도 마라,\""], ["said Sophia.", "고모가 외쳤다."], ["“A pincushion upon my coach would please me just as well.”", "\"너 같은 한심한 것의 입에는 어울리지 않는 말이다. 얘야, 네가 나로 하여금 이런 말을 쓰게 만드니 안타깝구나만, 나는 네 비굴한 성미를 도무지 참을 수가 없다."], ["“Never mention honour,” cries the aunt.", "네 안에는 웨스턴 가문의 피가 한 방울도 흐르지 않아."], ["“It becomes not the mouth of such a wretch.", "하지만 네 생각이 아무리 천하고 비루하다 해도, 너 때문에 내 이름에 오점을 묻힐 수는 없다. 내가 잉글랜드에서 손꼽히는 최고의 혼처를 거절하도록 너를 부추겼다는 말이 세상에 돌게 둘 수는 없어."], ["I am sorry, niece, you force me to use these words, but I cannot bear your groveling temper; you have none of the blood of the Westerns in you.", "그 혼처는 재산상의 이점은 말할 것도 없고, 거의 어느 가문에든 명예가 될 것이며, 작위에 있어서는 실로 우리 가문보다 한 수 위니 말이다.\" \"분명,\""], ["But, however mean and base your own ideas are, you shall bring no imputation on mine.", "소피아가 말했다. \"저는 부족하게 태어난 모양이에요."], ["I will never suffer the world to say of me that I encouraged you in refusing one of the best matches in England; a match which, besides its advantage in fortune, would do honour to almost any family, and hath, indeed, in title, the advantage of ours.”", "다른 사람들이 복으로 받은 그 감각을 저는 갖지 못한 거예요. 소리와 겉치레의 기쁨을 맛볼 줄 아는 어떤 감각이 분명 따로 있는데, 제게는 그게 없는 거지요."], ["“Surely,” says Sophia, “I am born deficient, and have not the senses with which other people are blessed; there must be certainly some sense which can relish the delights of sound and show, which I have not; for surely mankind would not labour so much, nor sacrifice so much for the obtaining, nor would they be so elate and proud with possessing, what appeared to them, as it doth to me, the most insignificant of all trifles.”", "그렇지 않고서야, 제 눈에 보이는 것처럼 그것이 모든 하찮은 것 가운데서도 가장 하찮게 보인다면, 사람들이 그것을 얻으려 그토록 애쓰고 그토록 많은 것을 희생하지도, 또 그것을 손에 넣었다고 그토록 우쭐대며 자랑스러워하지도 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“No, no, miss,”", "\"아니다, 아니야, 아가씨,\""], ["cries the aunt; “you are born with as many senses as other people; but I assure you you are not born with a sufficient understanding to make a fool of me, or to expose my conduct to the world; so I declare this to you, upon my word, and you know, I believe, how fixed my resolutions are, unless you agree to see his lordship this afternoon, I will, with my own hands, deliver you to-morrow morning to my brother, and will never henceforth interfere with you, nor see your face again.”", "고모가 외쳤다. \"너는 다른 사람들만큼이나 많은 감각을 갖고 태어났어."], ["Sophia stood a few moments silent after this speech, which was uttered in a most angry and peremptory tone; and then, bursting into tears, she cryed, “Do with me, madam, whatever you please; I am the most miserable undone wretch upon earth; if my dear aunt forsakes me where shall I look for a protector?”", "다만 분명히 말해 두는데, 너는 나를 바보로 만들거나 내 처신을 세상에 웃음거리로 내놓을 만큼의 분별력을 갖고 태어나지는 못했다. 그러니 내 말로써 이것을 네게 선언하마."], ["“My dear niece,”", "그리고 내 결심이 얼마나 확고한지는 너도 알 거라 믿는다."], ["cries she, “you will have a very good protector in his lordship; a protector whom nothing but a hankering after that vile fellow Jones can make you decline.”", "네가 오늘 오후에 백작님을 만나기로 동의하지 않는다면, 나는 내일 아침 내 손으로 직접 너를 내 오라비에게 넘겨줄 것이며, 이후로는 결코 네 일에 관여하지도, 다시는 네 얼굴을 보지도 않겠다.\" 이 말은 더없이 노엽고 단호한 어조로 내뱉어진 것이었는데, 소피아는 그 뒤로 잠시 말없이 서 있었다."], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "그러다 와락 눈물을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"고모님, 저를 마음대로 하세요."], ["said Sophia, “you wrong me.", "저는 이 땅에서 가장 비참하고 망가진 신세예요. 사랑하는 고모님마저 저를 버리신다면, 저는 어디서 보호자를 찾아야 하나요?\""], ["How can you imagine, after what you have shewn me, if I had ever any such thoughts, that I should not banish them for ever?", "\"사랑하는 조카야,\" 고모가 외쳤다."], ["If it will satisfy you, I will receive the sacrament upon it never to see his face again.”", "\"너는 백작님이라는 더없이 훌륭한 보호자를 얻게 될 거다. 저 비열한 작자 존스에 대한 미련이 아니고서는, 결코 마다할 까닭이 없는 보호자란 말이다.\""], ["“But, child, dear child,”", "\"정말이지, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["said the aunt, “be reasonable; can you invent a single objection?”", "\"고모님은 저를 잘못 보고 계세요. 고모님께서 제게 보여 주신 것들을 겪고도, 설령 제가 한때 그런 마음을 품었더라도 그것을 영영 떨쳐 버리지 않으리라고 어찌 짐작하실 수 있나요?"], ["“I have already, I think, told you a sufficient objection,”", "고모님께서 흡족하시다면, 다시는 그의 얼굴을 보지 않겠노라고 성찬을 받들어 맹세하겠어요.\" \"하지만 얘야, 사랑하는 얘야,\""], ["answered Sophia.", "고모가 말했다."], ["“What?”", "\"이치를 따져 보렴."], ["cries the aunt; “I remember none.”", "단 하나라도 반대할 까닭을 댈 수 있겠니?\" \"이미 충분한 반대 이유를 말씀드린 것 같은데요,\""], ["“Sure, madam,”", "소피아가 대답했다. \"뭐라고?\""], ["said Sophia, “I told you he had used me in the rudest and vilest manner.”", "고모가 외쳤다. \"나는 아무것도 기억나지 않는다.\""], ["“Indeed, child,”", "\"분명히요, 고모님,\""], ["answered she, “I never heard you, or did not understand you:--but what do you mean by this rude, vile manner?”", "소피아가 말했다. \"그가 저를 가장 무례하고 비열한 방식으로 다뤘다고 말씀드렸잖아요.\""], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"정말이지, 얘야,\""], ["said Sophia, “I am almost ashamed to tell you.", "고모가 대답했다. \"나는 들은 적이 없거나, 아니면 못 알아들었구나."], ["He caught me in his arms, pulled me down upon the settee, and thrust his hand into my bosom, and kissed it with such violence that I have the mark upon my left breast at this moment.”", "그런데 이 무례하고 비열한 방식이라는 게 무슨 뜻이냐?\" \"정말이지, 고모님,\""], ["“Indeed!”", "소피아가 말했다."], ["said Mrs Western. “Yes, indeed, madam,”", "\"말씀드리기가 거의 부끄러울 지경이에요."], ["answered Sophia; “my father luckily came in at that instant, or Heaven knows what rudeness he intended to have proceeded to.”", "그가 저를 팔로 붙잡아 긴 의자 위로 끌어 내리고는, 제 가슴팍에 손을 들이밀고 어찌나 거칠게 입을 맞췄던지, 지금 이 순간에도 왼쪽 가슴에 그 자국이 남아 있어요.\" \"정말이냐!\""], ["“I am astonished and confounded,”", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"네, 정말이에요, 고모님,\""], ["cries the aunt.", "소피아가 대답했다."], ["“No woman of the name of Western hath been ever treated so since we were a family.", "\"마침 그 순간에 아버지가 들어오셨기에 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 그가 어디까지 무례하게 굴 작정이었는지는 하늘만이 아실 거예요.\" \"기가 막히고 어이가 없구나,\""], ["I would have torn the eyes of a prince out, if he had attempted such freedoms with me.", "고모가 외쳤다. \"우리가 한 가문을 이룬 이래로 웨스턴이라는 이름을 가진 여자가 그런 취급을 받은 적은 없었다."], ["It is impossible! sure, Sophia, you must invent this to raise my indignation against him.”", "어떤 왕족이 내게 그런 무례를 시도했더라면, 나는 그자의 두 눈알을 뽑아 버렸을 거야."], ["“I hope, madam,”", "그럴 리가 없어!"], ["said Sophia, “you have too good an opinion of me to imagine me capable of telling an untruth.", "분명, 소피아, 네가 그자에 대한 내 분노를 돋우려고 이 일을 지어낸 게 틀림없다.\" \"고모님,\""], ["Upon my soul it is true.”", "소피아가 말했다. \"저를 거짓말이나 할 수 있는 아이로 여기실 만큼 저를 나쁘게 보지는 않으시리라 믿어요."], ["“I should have stabbed him to the heart, had I been present,”", "제 영혼을 걸고 사실이에요.\" \"내가 그 자리에 있었더라면, 나는 그자의 심장을 칼로 찔렀을 게다,\""], ["returned the aunt.", "고모가 받아쳤다."], ["“Yet surely he could have no dishonourable design; it is impossible!", "\"그렇지만 분명 그에게 부정한 속셈은 있을 수 없어. 그럴 리가 없다!"], ["he durst not: besides, his proposals shew he hath not; for they are not only honourable, but generous.", "그가 감히 그러진 못할 거야. 게다가 그의 청혼만 봐도 그렇지 않다는 게 드러나잖니."], ["I don't know; the age allows too great freedoms.", "그 청혼은 명예로울 뿐 아니라 너그럽기까지 하니 말이다."], ["A distant salute is all I would have allowed before the ceremony.", "모르겠구나. 요즘 세상은 너무 큰 방종을 허용하지."], ["I have had lovers formerly, not so long ago neither; several lovers, though I never would consent to marriage, and I never encouraged the least freedom.", "나라면 예식 전에는 멀찍이서 인사하는 정도밖에 허락하지 않았을 거야. 나도 예전에 구애하는 이들이 있었단다, 그리 오래전 일도 아니야."], ["It is a foolish custom, and what I never would agree to.", "여럿 있었지. 비록 나는 결혼에는 결코 동의하지 않았고, 조금의 방종도 부추긴 적이 없지만 말이다."], ["No man kissed more of me than my cheek.", "그건 어리석은 풍습이고, 나라면 결코 받아들이지 않았을 일이야. 어떤 남자도 내 뺨 이상으로 입 맞춘 적은 없었다."], ["It is as much as one can bring oneself to give lips up to a husband; and, indeed, could I ever have been persuaded to marry, I believe I should not have soon been brought to endure so much.”", "남편에게 입술을 내어 주는 것조차 사람이 스스로를 다잡아 겨우 할 수 있는 일이야. 그리고 사실, 내가 설령 결혼하도록 설득당했다 한들, 그렇게까지 견디는 일에 그리 쉽게 이르지는 못했을 게다.\""], ["“You will pardon me, dear madam,”", "\"용서해 주세요, 사랑하는 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["said Sophia, “if I make one observation: you own you have had many lovers, and the world knows it, even if you should deny it.", "\"한 가지만 짚어 보고 싶어요. 고모님께서는 구애하는 이들이 많았다고 스스로 인정하셨고, 설령 부인하셔도 세상이 다 아는 사실이지요."], ["You refused them all, and, I am convinced, one coronet at least among them.”", "고모님은 그들을 모두 거절하셨고, 저는 그중에 적어도 백작 관 하나쯤은 있었으리라 확신해요.\""], ["“You say true, dear Sophy,”", "\"맞는 말이다, 사랑하는 소피야,\" 고모가 대답했다."], ["answered she; “I had once the offer of a title.”", "\"내게도 한때 작위를 주겠다는 제안이 있었지.\" \"그렇다면, 고모님,\""], ["“Why, then,”", "소피아가 말했다."], ["said Sophia, “will you not suffer me to refuse this once?”", "\"어째서 저에게는 이번 한 번 거절하는 것을 허락하지 않으시나요?\" \"그건 그렇다, 얘야,\""], ["“It is true, child,”", "고모가 말했다. \"나는 작위 제안을 거절한 적이 있어."], ["said she, “I have refused the offer of a title; but it was not so good an offer; that is, not so very, very good an offer.”--“Yes, madam,”", "하지만 그건 그리 좋은 제안이 아니었어. 그러니까, 그렇게나 아주, 아주 좋은 제안은 아니었단 말이다.\""], ["said Sophia; “but you have had very great proposals from men of vast fortunes.", "\"그렇지요, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["It was not the first, nor the second, nor the third advantageous match that offered itself.”", "\"하지만 고모님께는 어마어마한 재산을 가진 남자들로부터 굉장한 청혼들이 들어왔잖아요. 첫 번째도, 두 번째도, 세 번째 좋은 혼처도 처음으로 나타난 게 아니었고요.\""], ["“I own it was not,”", "\"그건 인정한다,\""], ["said she.", "고모가 말했다."], ["“Well, madam,”", "\"그러면, 고모님,\""], ["continued Sophia, “and why may not I expect to have a second, perhaps, better than this?", "소피아가 이어 갔다. \"어째서 저는 두 번째, 어쩌면 이번보다 더 나은 청혼을 기대하면 안 되나요?"], ["You are now but a young woman, and I am convinced would not promise to yield to the first lover of fortune, nay, or of title too.", "고모님은 지금도 아직 젊은 여인이시고, 처음 나타난, 재산을 가진, 아니, 작위를 가진 구애자에게 굴복하겠다고 약속하지 않으실 거라고 저는 확신해요."], ["I am a very young woman, and sure I need not despair.”", "저는 아주 젊은 여자인데, 절망할 까닭이 없는 게 분명하잖아요.\""], ["“Well, my dear, dear Sophy,”", "\"그래, 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 소피야,\""], ["cries the aunt, “what would you have me say?”", "고모가 외쳤다. \"그러면 나더러 무슨 말을 하란 말이냐?\""], ["“Why, I only beg that I may not be left alone, at least this evening; grant me that, and I will submit, if you think, after what is past, I ought to see him in your company.”", "\"그저, 적어도 오늘 저녁만큼은 제가 홀로 남겨지지 않게 해 주시기를 바랄 뿐이에요. 그것만 허락해 주시면, 지나간 일을 헤아려 보시고 제가 고모님과 함께 있는 자리에서 그분을 만나야 한다고 여기신다면, 저는 따르겠어요.\""], ["“Well, I will grant it,”", "\"그래, 그건 허락하마,\""], ["cries the aunt.", "고모가 외쳤다."], ["“Sophy, you know I love you, and can deny you nothing.", "\"소피야, 너도 알다시피 나는 너를 사랑하니, 네게 아무것도 거절할 수가 없구나."], ["You know the easiness of my nature; I have not always been so easy.", "내 천성이 무던하다는 건 너도 알지. 늘 이렇게 무던했던 건 아니야."], ["I have been formerly thought cruel; by the men, I mean.", "예전엔 모질다는 소리를 들었단다, 남자들한테서 말이야. 사람들은 나를 '모진 파르테니사'라고 불렀어."], ["I was called the cruel Parthenissa.", "나는 '모진 파르테니사'에게 바치는 시구가 적힌 창문을 여럿 깨뜨려 봤단다."], ["I have broke many a window that has had verses to the cruel Parthenissa in it.", "소피야, 나는 너만큼 어여뻤던 적은 없지만, 그래도 예전엔 네 모습 같은 데가 좀 있었어. 지금은 조금 변했다만."], ["Sophy, I was never so handsome as you, and yet I had something of you formerly. I am a little altered.", "키케로가 그의 서한에서 말했듯, 왕국이며 나라도 변화를 겪는 법이니, 인간의 모습이라고 변하지 않을 수 있겠니.\""], ["Kingdoms and states, as Tully Cicero says in his epistles, undergo alterations, and so must the human form.”", "이렇게 그녀는 거의 반 시간 가까이 자기 자신과, 자신이 거둔 정복들과, 자신의 모짊에 대해 떠들어 댔다. 그러다 마침내 백작이 도착했는데, 그 더없이 지루한 방문 동안 웨스턴 부인은 단 한 번도 방을 비켜 줄 기색을 보이지 않았다."], ["Thus run she on for near half an hour upon herself, and her conquests, and her cruelty, till the arrival of my lord, who, after a most tedious visit, during which Mrs Western never once offered to leave the room, retired, not much more satisfied with the aunt than with the niece; for Sophia had brought her aunt into so excellent a temper, that she consented to almost everything her niece said; and agreed that a little distant behaviour might not be improper to so forward a lover.", "그렇게 백작은 물러갔으니, 조카에게 그러했던 것 못지않게 고모에게도 그리 흡족하지는 못했다. 소피아가 고모를 어찌나 더없이 좋은 기분으로 돌려놓았던지, 고모는 조카가 하는 말이라면 거의 무엇이든 동의해 주었고, 그처럼 들이대는 구애자에게는 다소 거리를 두는 처신이 어쩌면 부적절하지 않으리라는 데에도 수긍해 주었던 것이다."]], [["Thus Sophia, by a little well-directed flattery, for which surely none will blame her, obtained a little ease for herself, and, at least, put off the evil day.", "이렇게 소피아는 조금의 잘 겨냥된 아첨으로—이를 두고 누구도 그녀를 탓하지는 않으리라—스스로에게 약간의 안도를 얻어 냈고, 적어도 그 끔찍한 날을 미루어 두었다."], ["And now we have seen our heroine in a better situation than she hath been for a long time before, we will look a little after Mr Jones, whom we left in the most deplorable situation that can be well imagined.", "자, 이제 우리는 우리의 여주인공이 오랫동안 겪어 온 처지보다 한결 나은 형편에 놓인 것을 보았으니, 우리가 가히 상상할 수 있는 가장 비참한 처지에 남겨 두고 떠나왔던 존스 씨를 잠시 살펴보기로 하자."]], [["Mrs Miller and Mr Nightingale visit Jones in the prison.", "밀러 부인과 나이팅게일 씨가 감옥으로 존스를 찾아오다."]], [["When Mr Allworthy and his nephew went to meet Mr Western, Mrs Miller set forwards to her son-in-law's lodgings, in order to acquaint him with the accident which had befallen his friend Jones; but he had known it long before from Partridge (for Jones, when he left Mrs Miller, had been furnished with a room in the same house with Mr Nightingale). The good woman found her daughter under great affliction on account of Mr Jones, whom having comforted as well as she could, she set forwards to the Gatehouse, where she heard he was, and where Mr Nightingale was arrived before her.", "올워디 씨와 그 조카가 웨스턴 씨를 만나러 간 사이, 밀러 부인은 사위의 거처로 향했다. 친구 존스에게 닥친 사고를 사위에게 알리려는 것이었다. 그러나 사위는 이미 오래전에 파트리지에게서 그 소식을 들어 알고 있었다(존스는 밀러 부인의 집을 떠난 뒤 나이팅게일 씨와 같은 집에 방을 하나 얻어 머물고 있었기 때문이다). 이 선량한 부인은 딸이 존스 씨 일로 크게 상심해 있는 것을 보고, 할 수 있는 한 위로를 건넨 다음, 존스가 있다고 들은 게이트하우스로 길을 나섰다. 그곳에는 나이팅게일 씨가 부인보다 먼저 와 있었다."]], [["The firmness and constancy of a true friend is a circumstance so extremely delightful to persons in any kind of distress, that the distress itself, if it be only temporary, and admits of relief, is more than compensated by bringing this comfort with it.", "참된 벗의 굳건함과 한결같음은 어떤 종류의 고난에 빠진 사람에게든 더없이 큰 기쁨이어서, 그 고난이 단지 일시적이고 또 누그러질 여지가 있는 것이라면, 고난 그 자체가 이 위로를 함께 데려옴으로써 충분히 보상되고도 남는다."], ["Nor are instances of this kind so rare as some superficial and inaccurate observers have reported.", "그리고 이런 사례는, 어떤 피상적이고 부정확한 관찰자들이 전한 것만큼 그리 드물지 않다."], ["To say the truth, want of compassion is not to be numbered among our general faults.", "사실대로 말하자면, 동정심의 결핍은 우리의 일반적인 결점에 끼지 않는다."], ["The black ingredient which fouls our disposition is envy.", "우리의 성품을 더럽히는 검은 성분은 바로 시기심이다."], ["Hence our eye is seldom, I am afraid, turned upward to those who are manifestly greater, better, wiser, or happier than ourselves, without some degree of malignity; while we commonly look downwards on the mean and miserable with sufficient benevolence and pity.", "그래서 우리의 눈길은, 안타깝게도, 우리보다 분명히 더 위대하고 더 선하고 더 지혜롭고 더 행복한 이들을 향해 위로 들어 올려질 때면 으레 어느 정도의 악의를 품기 마련이다. 반면에 우리는 비천하고 가련한 이들을 내려다볼 때에는 흔히 넉넉한 자비와 연민을 보낸다."], ["In fact, I have remarked, that most of the defects which have discovered themselves in the friendships within my observation have arisen from envy only: a hellish vice; and yet one from which I have known very few absolutely exempt.", "실제로 나는, 내가 지켜본 우정들 속에서 드러난 결함의 대부분이 오로지 시기심에서 비롯되었음을 알아차렸다. 그것은 지옥에서 온 악덕이건만, 거기서 완전히 벗어난 사람은 내가 아는 한 극히 드물었다."], ["But enough of a subject which, if pursued, would lead me too far.", "그러나 이 주제는 이쯤 해 두자. 더 파고들었다가는 너무 멀리 나가게 될 테니."]], [["Whether it was that Fortune was apprehensive lest Jones should sink under the weight of his adversity, and that she might thus lose any future opportunity of tormenting him, or whether she really abated somewhat of her severity towards him, she seemed a little to relax her persecution, by sending him the company of two such faithful friends, and what is perhaps more rare, a faithful servant.", "운명의 여신은 존스가 역경의 무게에 눌려 가라앉아 버릴까, 그리하여 앞으로 그를 괴롭힐 기회를 잃을까 걱정했던 것인지, 아니면 정말로 그를 향한 가혹함을 다소 누그러뜨린 것인지 알 수 없지만, 그토록 충실한 두 벗의 동행을, 그리고 어쩌면 더더욱 드문 일이지만 충실한 하인 하나를 함께 보내 줌으로써 그 박해를 조금은 늦추어 준 듯 보였다. 파트리지로 말하자면, 흠은 많았어도 충직함만큼은 모자라지 않았다."], ["For Partridge, though he had many imperfections, wanted not fidelity; and though fear would not suffer him to be hanged for his master, yet the world, I believe, could not have bribed him to desert his cause.", "비록 두려움이 그로 하여금 주인을 위해 교수대에 오르게 할 만큼은 아니었으나, 온 세상이 뇌물을 준다 한들 그가 주인의 편을 저버리게 만들 수는 없었으리라고 나는 믿는다."]], [["While Jones was expressing great satisfaction in the presence of his friends, Partridge brought an account that Mr Fitzpatrick was still alive, though the surgeon declared that he had very little hopes.", "존스가 벗들이 곁에 있어 큰 위안이 된다고 말하고 있을 때, 파트리지가 들어와 피츠패트릭 씨가 아직 살아 있다는 소식을 전했다. 다만 외과의는 가망이 거의 없다고 단언했다는 것이었다."], ["Upon which, Jones fetching a deep sigh, Nightingale said to him, “My dear Tom, why should you afflict yourself so upon an accident, which, whatever be the consequence, can be attended with no danger to you, and in which your conscience cannot accuse you of having been the least to blame?", "이 말에 존스가 깊은 한숨을 내쉬자, 나이팅게일이 그에게 말했다. \"여보게, 톰, 어째서 이런 사고로 그렇게 스스로를 괴롭히는가."], ["If the fellow should die, what have you done more than taken away the life of a ruffian in your own defence?", "그 결과가 어떻든 자네에게는 아무런 위험도 닥치지 않을 일이고, 자네 양심으로도 조금의 책임조차 없다고 떳떳하지 않은가? 그자가 죽는다 한들, 자네가 한 일이라곤 자기 방어를 위해 한 불한당의 목숨을 거둔 것뿐일세."], ["So will the coroner's inquest certainly find it; and then you will be easily admitted to bail; and, though you must undergo the form of a trial, yet it is a trial which many men would stand for you for a shilling.”", "검시 배심도 분명 그렇게 판정할 걸세. 그러면 자네는 쉽게 보석으로 풀려날 테고, 비록 재판이라는 형식은 거쳐야겠지만, 그건 많은 사람이 단돈 한 실링이면 기꺼이 자네 대신 받아 줄 만한 재판이라네.\""], ["“Come, come, Mr Jones,”", "\"자, 자, 존스 씨,\""], ["says Mrs Miller, “chear yourself up.", "밀러 부인이 말했다. \"기운을 내요."], ["I knew you could not be the aggressor, and so I told Mr Allworthy, and so he shall acknowledge too, before I have done with him.”", "나는 당신이 먼저 시비를 건 사람일 리 없다는 걸 잘 알아요. 그래서 올워디 씨께도 그렇게 말씀드렸고, 내가 그분과 이야기를 끝내기 전에 그분도 반드시 그걸 인정하시게 만들 거예요.\""]], [["Jones gravely answered, “That whatever might be his fate, he should always lament the having shed the blood of one of his fellow-creatures, as one of the highest misfortunes which could have befallen him.", "존스는 무겁게 대답했다. \"제 운명이 어떻게 되든, 같은 인간의 피를 흘리게 했다는 사실만큼은 제게 닥칠 수 있는 가장 큰 불행 가운데 하나로 여기며 늘 한탄할 겁니다."], ["But I have another misfortune of the tenderest kind----O!", "하지만 제게는 가장 애틋한 종류의 또 다른 불행이 있습니다——아!"], ["Mrs Miller, I have lost what I held most dear upon earth.”", "밀러 부인, 저는 이 세상에서 가장 소중히 여기던 것을 잃고 말았습니다.\""], ["“That must be a mistress,”", "\"그건 분명 사랑하는 사람 얘기겠지요,\""], ["said Mrs Miller; “but come, come; I know more than you imagine”", "밀러 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 자, 자, 나는 당신이 짐작하는 것보다 더 많이 알고 있어요\"(사실 파트리지가 죄다 떠벌려 놓았던 것이다)."], ["(for indeed Partridge had blabbed all); “and I have heard more than you know.", "\"그리고 당신이 아는 것보다 더 많은 이야기를 들었답니다."], ["Matters go better, I promise you, than you think; and I would not give Blifil sixpence for all the chance which he hath of the lady.”", "정말이지 일은 당신 생각보다 잘 풀려 가고 있어요. 블리필이 그 아가씨를 얻을 가망이라고는, 내가 보기엔 육 펜스 값어치도 안 돼요.\""]], [["“Indeed, my dear friend, indeed,”", "\"정말이지, 부인,\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones, “you are an entire stranger to the cause of my grief.", "\"정말이지 부인께서는 제 슬픔의 까닭을 전혀 모르십니다."], ["If you was acquainted with the story, you would allow my case admitted of no comfort.", "그 사연을 아신다면, 제 처지에 어떤 위로도 통하지 않는다는 걸 인정하실 겁니다. 저는 블리필 따위는 조금도 두렵지 않아요."], ["I apprehend no danger from Blifil.", "제가 제 손으로 저를 망쳐 놓았습니다.\""], ["I have undone myself.”", "\"절망하지 말아요,\""], ["“Don't despair,”", "밀러 부인이 받았다."], ["replied Mrs Miller; “you know not what a woman can do; and if anything be in my power, I promise you I will do it to serve you.", "\"여자가 무엇을 해낼 수 있는지 당신은 모를 거예요. 그러니 내 힘으로 할 수 있는 일이라면, 무엇이든 당신을 위해 하겠다고 약속할게요."], ["It is my duty.", "그게 내 도리니까요."], ["My son, my dear Mr Nightingale, who is so kind to tell me he hath obligations to you on the same account, knows it is my duty.", "내 아들, 그러니까 사랑하는 나이팅게일도 같은 이유로 당신께 신세를 졌다고 친절히 말해 주는데, 그 아이도 이게 내 도리라는 걸 알아요."], ["Shall I go to the lady myself?", "내가 직접 그 아가씨를 찾아갈까요?"], ["I will say anything to her you would have me say.”", "당신이 전하라는 말이라면 무엇이든 그대로 전해 주겠어요.\""]], [["“Thou best of women,”", "\"세상에서 가장 훌륭한 부인이여,\""], ["cries Jones, taking her by the hand, “talk not of obligations to me;--but as you have been so kind to mention it, there is a favour which, perhaps, may be in your power.", "존스가 부인의 손을 잡으며 외쳤다. \"제게 신세 졌다는 말씀은 마십시오.——하지만 부인께서 그렇게 친절히 말씀을 꺼내 주셨으니, 어쩌면 부인의 힘이 닿을지도 모를 부탁이 하나 있습니다."], ["I see you are acquainted with the lady (how you came by your information I know not), who sits, indeed, very near my heart.", "보아하니 부인께서는 그 아가씨를 알고 계신 듯합니다(어떻게 그 소식을 들으셨는지는 저도 모르겠습니다만). 그분은 참으로 제 마음 깊은 곳에 자리한 분입니다."], ["If you could contrive to deliver this (giving her a paper from his pocket), I shall for ever acknowledge your goodness.”", "이것을 그분께 전해 주실 방도를 마련해 주신다면(주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내 부인께 건네며), 저는 부인의 그 은혜를 영원히 잊지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Give it me,”", "\"이리 주세요,\""], ["said Mrs Miller.", "밀러 부인이 말했다."], ["“If I see it not in her own possession before I sleep, may my next sleep be my last!", "\"잠들기 전에 이것이 그분 손에 들린 걸 보지 못한다면, 내 다음 잠이 마지막 잠이 되어도 좋아요! 마음을 편히 가져요, 착한 젊은이!"], ["Comfort yourself, my good young man!", "지난날의 어리석음에서 교훈을 얻을 만큼 현명해져요. 그러면 모든 일이 잘 풀릴 거라고 내가 장담하지요."], ["be wise enough to take warning from past follies, and I warrant all shall be well, and I shall yet see you happy with the most charming young lady in the world; for I so hear from every one she is.”", "나는 머지않아 당신이 이 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨와 행복하게 지내는 모습을 보게 될 거예요. 누구에게 물어봐도 그분이 그런 분이라고 하니까요.\""]], [["“Believe me, madam,”", "\"부인, 제 말을 믿어 주십시오,\""], ["said he, “I do not speak the common cant of one in my unhappy situation.", "그가 말했다. \"저는 저처럼 불행한 처지에 놓인 사람이 흔히 늘어놓는 빈말을 하는 게 아닙니다."], ["Before this dreadful accident happened, I had resolved to quit a life of which I was become sensible of the wickedness as well as folly.", "이 끔찍한 사고가 일어나기 전부터, 저는 그 사악함과 어리석음을 절감하게 된 그런 삶을 끊어 버리기로 결심했습니다."], ["I do assure you, notwithstanding the disturbances I have unfortunately occasioned in your house, for which I heartily ask your pardon, I am not an abandoned profligate.", "진심으로 말씀드리지만, 불행히도 제가 부인 댁에서 일으킨 소동들에 대해서는 진정으로 용서를 빌면서도, 저는 자포자기한 난봉꾼은 아닙니다."], ["Though I have been hurried into vices, I do not approve a vicious character, nor will I ever, from this moment, deserve it.”", "비록 악덕에 휩쓸려 떠밀려 오긴 했어도, 저는 악한 인간됨을 옳다고 여기지 않으며, 이 순간부터는 결코 그런 자라는 소리를 들을 짓을 하지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["Mrs Miller expressed great satisfaction in these declarations, in the sincerity of which she averred she had an entire faith; and now the remainder of the conversation past in the joint attempts of that good woman and Mr Nightingale to cheer the dejected spirits of Mr Jones, in which they so far succeeded as to leave him much better comforted and satisfied than they found him; to which happy alteration nothing so much contributed as the kind undertaking of Mrs Miller to deliver his letter to Sophia, which he despaired of finding any means to accomplish; for when Black George produced the last from Sophia, he informed Partridge that she had strictly charged him, on pain of having it communicated to her father, not to bring her any answer. He was, moreover, not a little pleased to find he had so warm an advocate to Mr Allworthy himself in this good woman, who was, in reality, one of the worthiest creatures in the world.", "밀러 부인은 이 다짐들에 크게 흡족해하며, 그 진실함을 온전히 믿는다고 단언했다. 이제 남은 대화는 그 선량한 부인과 나이팅게일 씨가 풀이 죽은 존스 씨의 기운을 북돋우려 함께 애쓰는 것으로 흘러갔다. 두 사람은 그를 어지간히 성공적으로 다독여, 처음 보았을 때보다 훨씬 위로받고 마음 편해진 상태로 그를 두고 나올 수 있었다. 이 기쁜 변화에 무엇보다 크게 보탬이 된 것은, 그가 도무지 이룰 방도를 찾지 못해 절망하던 일, 곧 그의 편지를 소피아에게 전해 주겠다는 밀러 부인의 다정한 약속이었다. 앞서 블랙 조지가 소피아의 마지막 편지를 가져왔을 때, 그는 파트리지에게, 아가씨가 답장을 가져오지 말라고, 만일 어긴다면 그 사실을 아버지께 알리겠다며 엄히 일러두었다고 전했던 터였다. 게다가 존스는, 이 선량한 부인이라는, 사실 세상에서 가장 가치 있는 사람 가운데 하나가 올워디 씨에게 직접 자기를 위해 그토록 열렬히 변호해 줄 사람이 되어 준다는 것을 알고 적잖이 기뻤다."]], [["After about an hour's visit from the lady (for Nightingale had been with him much longer), they both took their leave, promising to return to him soon; during which Mrs Miller said she hoped to bring him some good news from his mistress, and Mr Nightingale promised to enquire into the state of Mr Fitzpatrick's wound, and likewise to find out some of the persons who were present at the rencounter.", "부인이 한 시간쯤 머물다(나이팅게일은 그보다 훨씬 오래 함께 있었다) 두 사람은 곧 다시 오겠노라 약속하며 작별했다. 그 자리에서 밀러 부인은 그의 연인에게서 좋은 소식을 가져올 수 있기를 바란다고 했고, 나이팅게일 씨는 피츠패트릭 씨의 상처가 어떤 상태인지 알아보고, 아울러 그 싸움 자리에 있었던 사람들 가운데 몇몇을 찾아내 보겠다고 약속했다."]], [["The former of these went directly in quest of Sophia, whither we likewise shall now attend her.", "이 가운데 앞엣사람, 곧 밀러 부인은 곧장 소피아를 찾아 나섰다. 이제 우리도 그 길을 따라 그녀와 함께 가 보기로 하자."]], [["In which Mrs Miller pays a visit to Sophia.", "밀러 부인이 소피아를 찾아간다."]], [["Access to the young lady was by no means difficult; for, as she lived now on a perfect friendly footing with her aunt, she was at full liberty to receive what visitants she pleased.", "그 젊은 아가씨를 만나는 일은 결코 어렵지 않았다. 이제 소피아는 고모와 더없이 다정한 사이로 지내고 있었으므로, 마음에 드는 손님이라면 누구든 마음껏 맞이할 자유가 있었던 것이다."]], [["Sophia was dressing when she was acquainted that there was a gentlewoman below to wait on her.", "소피아가 한창 몸단장을 하고 있을 때, 아래층에 한 부인이 그녀를 뵙고자 기다린다는 전갈이 왔다."], ["As she was neither afraid, nor ashamed, to see any of her own sex, Mrs Miller was immediately admitted.", "같은 여인이라면 누구를 만나든 두려울 것도 부끄러울 것도 없었으므로, 밀러 부인은 곧바로 안으로 안내되었다."]], [["Curtsies and the usual ceremonials between women who are strangers to each other, being past, Sophia said, “I have not the pleasure to know you, madam.”", "서로 낯선 여인들 사이에 오가는 인사와 으레 갖추는 예의가 끝나자, 소피아가 말했다. \"부인을 알게 되는 기쁨을 아직 누리지 못했군요.\""], ["“No, madam,”", "\"그렇습니다, 아가씨.\""], ["answered Mrs Miller, “and I must beg pardon for intruding upon you.", "밀러 부인이 대답했다. \"이렇게 불쑥 찾아뵌 것을 용서해 주셔야겠습니다."], ["But when you know what has induced me to give you this trouble, I hope----”", "다만 무엇이 저로 하여금 이런 폐를 끼치게 했는지 아신다면, 부디……\""], ["“Pray, what is your business, madam?”", "\"그런데 무슨 용건이신지요, 부인?\""], ["said Sophia, with a little emotion.", "소피아가 약간 마음이 흔들린 채 물었다."], ["“Madam, we are not alone,”", "\"아가씨, 우리 둘만 있는 것이 아니라서요.\""], ["replied Mrs Miller, in a low voice.", "밀러 부인이 나직한 목소리로 대꾸했다."], ["“Go out, Betty,”", "\"베티, 나가 있거라.\""], ["said Sophia.", "소피아가 말했다."]], [["When Betty was departed, Mrs Miller said, “I was desired, madam, by a very unhappy young gentleman, to deliver you this letter.”", "베티가 물러가자 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"아가씨, 무척 불행한 한 젊은 신사분의 간곡한 부탁으로 이 편지를 전해 드리러 왔습니다.\""], ["Sophia changed colour when she saw the direction, well knowing the hand, and after some hesitation, said--“I could not conceive, madam, from your appearance, that your business had been of such a nature.--Whomever you brought this letter from, I shall not open it.", "소피아는 겉봉을 보고 그 필체를 단번에 알아채고는 얼굴빛이 변했고, 잠시 머뭇거리다가 말했다. \"부인, 부인의 모습으로 보아서는 용건이 그런 종류의 것이라고는 생각지도 못했습니다."], ["I should be sorry to entertain an unjust suspicion of any one; but you are an utter stranger to me.”", "그 편지를 누가 보냈든, 저는 열어 보지 않겠습니다. 누구에게든 부당한 의심을 품는 것은 저로서도 괴로운 일입니다만, 부인은 제게 전혀 낯선 분이니까요.\""]], [["“If you will have patience, madam,”", "\"아가씨, 조금만 참고 들어 주신다면,\""], ["answered Mrs Miller, “I will acquaint you who I am, and how I came by that letter.”", "밀러 부인이 대답했다. \"제가 누구인지, 또 그 편지를 어떻게 맡게 되었는지 말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["“I have no curiosity, madam, to know anything,”", "\"부인, 저는 무엇을 알고 싶은 호기심이 전혀 없습니다.\""], ["cries Sophia; “but I must insist on your delivering that letter back to the person who gave it you.”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"다만 그 편지를 준 사람에게 도로 돌려주시기를 거듭 청합니다.\""]], [["Mrs Miller then fell upon her knees, and in the most passionate terms implored her compassion; to which Sophia answered: “Sure, madam, it is surprizing you should be so very strongly interested in the behalf of this person. I would not think, madam”--“No, madam,” says Mrs Miller, “you shall not think anything but the truth. I will tell you all, and you will not wonder that I am interested. He is the best-natured creature that ever was born.”--She then began and related the story of Mr Anderson.--After this she cried, “This, madam, this is his goodness; but I have much more tender obligations to him. He hath preserved my child.”--Here, after shedding some tears, she related everything concerning that fact, suppressing only those circumstances which would have most reflected on her daughter, and concluded with saying, “Now, madam, you shall judge whether I can ever do enough for so kind, so good, so generous a young man; and sure he is the best and worthiest of all human beings.”", "그러자 밀러 부인은 무릎을 꿇고 더없이 간절한 말로 그녀의 자비를 빌었다. 소피아가 대답했다. \"정말이지, 부인, 부인께서 이 사람을 위해 이토록 강하게 마음을 쓰시는 것이 놀랍군요. 설마 부인이……\" \"아니요, 아가씨.\" 밀러 부인이 말했다. \"진실 말고는 어떤 것도 생각지 말아 주십시오. 제가 다 말씀드리겠으니, 제가 이렇게 마음을 쓰는 것을 이상히 여기지 않으실 겁니다. 그분은 세상에 태어난 누구보다도 천성이 착한 분이에요.\" 그러고는 앤더슨 씨의 이야기를 들려주기 시작했다. 그 이야기를 마치고 나서 그녀가 외쳤다. \"아가씨, 이것이, 이것이 바로 그분의 선함입니다. 하지만 저는 그분께 그보다 훨씬 더 애틋한 은혜를 입었습니다. 그분이 제 아이를 구해 주셨거든요.\" 그러고는 몇 방울 눈물을 흘린 뒤, 그 일에 관한 모든 것을 들려주었으되, 자기 딸에게 가장 누가 될 만한 정황만은 감추었다. 그리고 이렇게 말로 끝맺었다. \"자, 아가씨, 이토록 친절하고, 이토록 선하고, 이토록 너그러운 젊은이를 위해 제가 아무리 한들 지나치겠습니까. 정말이지 그분은 온 인류 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 값진 분입니다.\""]], [["The alterations in the countenance of Sophia had hitherto been chiefly to her disadvantage, and had inclined her complexion to too great paleness; but she now waxed redder, if possible, than vermilion, and cried, “I know not what to say; certainly what arises from gratitude cannot be blamed--But what service can my reading this letter do your friend, since I am resolved never----” Mrs Miller fell again to her entreaties, and begged to be forgiven, but she could not, she said, carry it back. “Well, madam,” says Sophia, “I cannot help it, if you will force it upon me.--Certainly you may leave it whether I will or no.” What Sophia meant, or whether she meant anything, I will not presume to determine; but Mrs Miller actually understood this as a hint, and presently laying the letter down on the table, took her leave, having first begged permission to wait again on Sophia; which request had neither assent nor denial.", "여태까지 소피아의 얼굴빛 변화는 주로 그녀에게 불리한 쪽이었고, 안색이 너무 창백한 쪽으로 기울었었다. 그러나 이제는, 그럴 수만 있다면, 주홍보다도 더 붉어졌고, 그녀가 외쳤다. \"무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르겠군요. 감사하는 마음에서 우러난 일이라면 분명 나무랄 수 없겠지요. 하지만 제가 이 편지를 읽는 것이 부인의 벗에게 무슨 도움이 되겠습니까. 저는 결코……\" 밀러 부인이 다시금 애원하기 시작했고, 부디 용서해 달라고, 그러나 이것을 도로 가지고 갈 수는 없노라고 말했다. \"그렇다면, 부인,\" 소피아가 말했다. \"부인이 굳이 제게 떠맡기시겠다면 저로서도 어쩔 수 없지요. 제가 받든 안 받든, 부인이 두고 가실 수야 있겠지요.\" 소피아가 무슨 뜻으로 그리 말했는지, 아니 무슨 뜻이 있기나 했는지, 나로서는 감히 단정하지 않겠다. 다만 밀러 부인은 이 말을 하나의 암시로 알아듣고, 곧 편지를 탁자 위에 내려놓은 뒤 작별을 고했으며, 그에 앞서 다시 한번 소피아를 찾아뵙도록 허락해 달라 청했다. 그 청에는 승낙도 거절도 돌아오지 않았다."]], [["The letter lay upon the table no longer than till Mrs Miller was out of sight; for then Sophia opened and read it.", "편지는 밀러 부인이 시야에서 사라질 때까지만 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다. 부인이 사라지자 소피아는 곧 그것을 열어 읽었으니 말이다."]], [["This letter did very little service to his cause; for it consisted of little more than confessions of his own unworthiness, and bitter lamentations of despair, together with the most solemn protestations of his unalterable fidelity to Sophia, of which, he said, he hoped to convince her, if he had ever more the honour of being admitted to her presence; and that he could account for the letter to Lady Bellaston in such a manner, that, though it would not entitle him to her forgiveness, he hoped at least to obtain it from her mercy.", "이 편지는 그의 처지에 그다지 도움이 되지 못했다. 거의 자신이 얼마나 보잘것없는 사람인지에 대한 고백과, 절망에 찬 쓰라린 한탄, 그리고 소피아를 향한 자신의 변치 않는 충정을 더없이 엄숙하게 다짐하는 말로 채워져 있을 뿐이었기 때문이다."], ["And concluded with vowing that nothing was ever less in his thoughts than to marry Lady Bellaston.", "그는 만일 다시 한번 그녀 앞에 나설 영광을 얻는다면 그 충정을 그녀에게 납득시키기를 바란다고 적었고, 벨라스턴 부인에게 보낸 편지에 대해서도 비록 그것이 그녀의 용서를 받을 자격을 그에게 주지는 못하겠으나, 적어도 그녀의 자비로부터 용서만은 얻기를 바란다고 해명할 수 있다고 했다. 그리고 벨라스턴 부인과 결혼하는 것만큼 자기 생각에서 멀리 있는 일은 결코 없었노라 맹세하며 끝맺었다."]], [["Though Sophia read the letter twice over with great attention, his meaning still remained a riddle to her; nor could her invention suggest to her any means to excuse Jones.", "소피아는 그 편지를 두 번이나 골똘히 읽었지만, 그의 뜻은 여전히 그녀에게 수수께끼로 남았다. 그녀의 머리로는 존스를 변명해 줄 어떤 방도도 떠오르지 않았다."], ["She certainly remained very angry with him, though indeed Lady Bellaston took up so much of her resentment, that her gentle mind had but little left to bestow on any other person.", "그녀는 분명 그에게 몹시 화가 나 있었으나, 실은 벨라스턴 부인이 그녀의 분노를 너무도 크게 차지하고 있어서, 그 온화한 마음에는 다른 사람에게 나눠 줄 분노가 거의 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["That lady was most unluckily to dine this very day with her aunt Western, and in the afternoon they were all three, by appointment, to go together to the opera, and thence to Lady Thomas Hatchet's drum. Sophia would have gladly been excused from all, but would not disoblige her aunt; and as to the arts of counterfeiting illness, she was so entirely a stranger to them, that it never once entered into her head. When she was drest, therefore, down she went, resolved to encounter all the horrors of the day, and a most disagreeable one it proved; for Lady Bellaston took every opportunity very civilly and slily to insult her; to all which her dejection of spirits disabled her from making any return; and, indeed, to confess the truth, she was at the very best but an indifferent mistress of repartee.", "그 부인은 하필이면 바로 이날 웨스턴 고모와 함께 식사를 하기로 되어 있었고, 오후에는 약속대로 세 사람이 함께 오페라에 갔다가 거기서 다시 토머스 해칫 부인의 드럼 모임으로 가기로 되어 있었다. 소피아는 이 모든 것에서 기꺼이 빠지고 싶었으나 고모의 비위를 거스르려 하지 않았다. 그리고 병을 꾸며내는 재주로 말하자면, 그녀는 그런 것에 어찌나 생판 낯선 사람이었던지, 그런 생각이 머릿속에 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그리하여 단장을 마치자 그녀는 아래층으로 내려갔으니, 그날의 온갖 끔찍한 일들을 맞닥뜨리기로 마음먹은 것이었다. 그리고 그날은 과연 더없이 불쾌한 하루로 드러났다. 벨라스턴 부인이 기회 있을 때마다 매우 정중하면서도 교활하게 그녀를 모욕했기 때문이다. 그 모든 것에 대해 그녀는 가라앉은 마음 탓에 아무런 대꾸도 하지 못했다. 그리고 사실대로 고백하자면, 그녀는 아무리 잘해야 재치 있는 응수에는 그저 그런 솜씨밖에 지니지 못한 사람이었다."]], [["Another misfortune which befel poor Sophia was the company of Lord Fellamar, whom she met at the opera, and who attended her to the drum.", "가엾은 소피아에게 닥친 또 하나의 불운은 펠라마 경과 한자리에 있게 된 것이었다. 그녀는 오페라에서 그를 만났고, 그는 드럼 모임까지 그녀를 따라왔다."], ["And though both places were too publick to admit of any particularities, and she was farther relieved by the musick at the one place, and by the cards at the other, she could not, however, enjoy herself in his company; for there is something of delicacy in women, which will not suffer them to be even easy in the presence of a man whom they know to have pretensions to them which they are disinclined to favour.", "두 곳 다 너무 공개된 자리여서 어떤 사사로운 수작도 끼어들 틈이 없었고, 또 한 곳에서는 음악이, 다른 곳에서는 카드놀이가 그녀를 한결 덜어 주었지만, 그래도 그녀는 그와 함께 있는 자리를 즐길 수가 없었다. 여인에게는 어떤 섬세한 데가 있어서, 자기에게 마음이 있되 그 마음을 받아 줄 뜻이 없는 남자가 곁에 있으면 도무지 편안할 수가 없는 법이니 말이다."]], [["Having in this chapter twice mentioned a drum, a word which our posterity, it is hoped, will not understand in the sense it is here applied, we shall, notwithstanding our present haste, stop a moment to describe the entertainment here meant, and the rather as we can in a moment describe it.", "이 장에서 드럼이라는 말을 두 번이나 꺼냈는데, 우리 후손들은 이 말을 여기 쓰인 뜻으로는 부디 이해하지 않게 되기를 바라는 바이므로, 지금 우리가 서두르는 처지이긴 하나 잠시 멈추어 여기서 말하는 그 모임을 설명하고자 한다. 더구나 그것은 순식간에 설명할 수 있는 것이기에 더욱 그렇다."]], [["A drum, then, is an assembly of well-dressed persons of both sexes, most of whom play at cards, and the rest do nothing at all; while the mistress of the house performs the part of the landlady at an inn, and like the landlady of an inn prides herself in the number of her guests, though she doth not always, like her, get anything by it.", "드럼이란, 그러니까, 잘 차려입은 남녀가 한데 모인 자리로, 그들 대부분은 카드놀이를 하고 나머지는 아무것도 하지 않으며, 그동안 그 집 안주인은 여관 여주인 노릇을 한다. 그리고 여관 여주인이 그러하듯, 손님 수가 많은 것을 자랑으로 여기되, 다만 여관 여주인과 달리 그것으로 늘 무언가를 벌어들이지는 못한다."]], [["No wonder then, as so much spirits must be required to support any vivacity in these scenes of dulness, that we hear persons of fashion eternally complaining of the want of them; a complaint confined entirely to upper life.", "그러니 이렇듯 따분한 자리에서 조금이나마 활기를 떠받치려면 그토록 많은 기운이 필요할 수밖에 없는 터라, 멋쟁이들이 기운이 없다고 끝없이 푸념하는 소리를 듣는 것도 놀랄 일이 아니다. 이 푸념은 오로지 상류 사회에만 국한된 것이다."], ["How insupportable must we imagine this round of impertinence to have been to Sophia at this time; how difficult must she have found it to force the appearance of gaiety into her looks, when her mind dictated nothing but the tenderest sorrow, and when every thought was charged with tormenting ideas!", "이 무렵 소피아에게 이 진절머리 나는 따분함의 순환이 얼마나 견디기 힘들었겠는가. 마음속에서는 더없이 애틋한 슬픔밖에 일러 주는 것이 없고, 온갖 생각마다 괴로운 상념으로 가득할 때, 즐거운 표정을 억지로 지어내기가 그녀에게 얼마나 어려웠겠는가!"]], [["Night, however, at last restored her to her pillow, where we will leave her to soothe her melancholy at least, though incapable we fear of rest, and shall pursue our history, which, something whispers us, is now arrived at the eve of some great event.", "그러나 마침내 밤이 그녀를 베개로 돌려보내 주었으니, 그곳에 그녀를 남겨 두어 적어도 그 우수만은 달래게 하자. 비록 쉬기는 어려우리라 염려되지만 말이다. 우리는 우리의 이야기를 계속 좇아가려 하거니와, 무언가가 우리에게 속삭이기를, 이제 그 이야기는 어떤 커다란 사건의 전야에 다다랐다고 한다."]], [["A pathetic scene between Mr Allworthy and Mrs Miller.", "올워디 씨와 밀러 부인 사이의 가슴 저린 한 장면."]], [["Mrs Miller had a long discourse with Mr Allworthy, at his return from dinner, in which she acquainted him with Jones's having unfortunately lost all which he was pleased to bestow on him at their separation; and with the distresses to which that loss had subjected him; of all which she had received a full account from the faithful retailer Partridge. She then explained the obligations she had to Jones; not that she was entirely explicit with regard to her daughter; for though she had the utmost confidence in Mr Allworthy, and though there could be no hopes of keeping an affair secret which was unhappily known to more than half a dozen, yet she could not prevail with herself to mention those circumstances which reflected most on the chastity of poor Nancy, but smothered that part of her evidence as cautiously as if she had been before a judge, and the girl was now on her trial for the murder of a bastard.", "올워디 씨가 저녁 식사를 마치고 돌아왔을 때, 밀러 부인은 그와 긴 이야기를 나누었다. 그 자리에서 부인은 존스가 헤어질 무렵 올워디 씨가 기꺼이 베풀어 준 그 모든 것을 불운하게도 잃어버린 일이며, 그 상실 때문에 그가 빠지게 된 곤경을 그에게 알렸다. 이 모든 사정은 충실한 소식 전달자 파트리지에게서 낱낱이 전해 들은 것이었다. 이어서 부인은 자신이 존스에게 진 신세를 설명했다. 다만 자기 딸과 관련해서는 모든 것을 다 털어놓지는 않았다. 부인은 올워디 씨를 더없이 신뢰하고 있었고, 또 이미 여섯 사람도 넘는 이들이 안타깝게도 알고 있는 일을 비밀로 지킬 가망은 없었지만, 그래도 가엾은 낸시의 정절에 가장 누가 될 만한 그 정황만큼은 차마 입에 올릴 수가 없었던 것이다. 부인은 마치 판사 앞에 선 양, 그래서 그 딸이 지금 사생아 살해죄로 재판을 받고 있기라도 한 양, 자기 증언의 그 부분을 조심스레 덮어 두었다."]], [["Allworthy said, there were few characters so absolutely vicious as not to have the least mixture of good in them. “However,” says he, “I cannot deny but that you have some obligations to the fellow, bad as he is, and I shall therefore excuse what hath past already, but must insist you never mention his name to me more; for, I promise you, it was upon the fullest and plainest evidence that I resolved to take the measures I have taken.” “Well, sir,” says she, “I make not the least doubt but time will shew all matters in their true and natural colours, and that you will be convinced this poor young man deserves better of you than some other folks that shall be nameless.”", "올워디는 말하기를, 인간의 성품 가운데 선이라곤 티끌만큼도 섞이지 않은, 그토록 철저하게 악한 성품은 좀처럼 없다고 했다. \"하지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"그자가 아무리 나쁜 사람이라 해도, 부인이 그에게 어느 정도 신세를 졌다는 것만은 부인할 수 없겠지요. 그러니 이미 지나간 일은 너그러이 보아 드리겠습니다. 다만 다시는 내 앞에서 그의 이름을 입에 올리지 않으시기를 분명히 청합니다. 내가 지금까지 취한 조치들을 단행하기로 마음먹은 것은, 정말이지 더없이 충분하고 명백한 증거에 따른 것이었으니까요.\" \"글쎄요, 어르신,\" 부인이 말했다. \"저는 조금도 의심하지 않습니다. 세월이 흐르면 모든 일이 본디의 참된 빛깔을 드러낼 것이고, 이 가엾은 젊은이가 이름을 밝히지 않겠습니다만 어떤 다른 사람들보다 어르신께 훨씬 더 어울리는 사람이었음을 어르신께서도 납득하시게 되리라고요.\""]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\" 올워디가 약간 언짢아진 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["cries Allworthy, a little ruffled, “I will not hear any reflections on my nephew; and if ever you say a word more of that kind, I will depart from your house that instant.", "\"내 조카를 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하는 말은 듣지 않겠습니다. 그런 말을 한 마디라도 더 하신다면, 나는 그 즉시 이 집을 떠나겠습니다."], ["He is the worthiest and best of men; and I once more repeat it to you, he hath carried his friendship to this man to a blameable length, by too long concealing facts of the blackest die.", "그 아이는 더없이 훌륭하고 가장 선량한 사람입니다. 다시 한번 말씀드리지만, 그 아이는 가장 시커먼 사실들을 너무 오래 숨김으로써, 이 사내에 대한 우정을 나무랄 만한 지경까지 끌고 갔습니다."], ["The ingratitude of the wretch to this good young man is what I most resent; for, madam, I have the greatest reason to imagine he had laid a plot to supplant my nephew in my favour, and to have disinherited him.”", "이 못난 자가 그 착한 젊은이에게 보인 배은망덕이야말로 내가 가장 분개하는 바입니다. 부인, 나로서는 그자가 내 호의 속에서 조카의 자리를 빼앗고 그 아이의 상속권마저 박탈하려는 음모를 꾸몄다고 짐작할 만한 더없이 큰 이유가 있으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“I am sure, sir,”", "\"확실히 말씀드리지만, 어르신,\""], ["answered Mrs Miller, a little frightened (for, though Mr Allworthy had the utmost sweetness and benevolence in his smiles, he had great terror in his frowns), “I shall never speak against any gentleman you are pleased to think well of.", "밀러 부인이 조금 겁먹은 채 대답했다(올워디 씨는 미소 지을 때면 더없이 다정하고 자애로웠으나, 얼굴을 찌푸릴 때면 그 안에 큰 위엄이 서려 있었기 때문이다). \"어르신께서 좋게 보아 주시는 어느 신사분께든 저는 결코 거스르는 말을 하지 않을 것입니다."], ["I am sure, sir, such behaviour would very little become me, especially when the gentleman is your nearest relation; but, sir, you must not be angry with me, you must not indeed, for my good wishes to this poor wretch.", "정말이지, 어르신, 그런 처신은 제게 도무지 어울리지 않을 테지요. 더구나 그 신사분이 어르신의 가장 가까운 친척이시니 말입니다."], ["Sure I may call him so now, though once you would have been angry with me if I had spoke of him with the least disrespect.", "하지만, 어르신, 이 가엾은 못난이에게 제가 잘되기를 비는 마음만으로 저를 노여워하시면 안 됩니다. 정말로 그러시면 안 됩니다."], ["How often have I heard you call him your son?", "한때는 제가 그를 조금이라도 함부로 말하면 어르신께서 노여워하셨겠지만, 이제는 그렇게 불러도 되겠지요."], ["How often have you prattled to me of him with all the fondness of a parent?", "어르신께서 그를 아드님이라 부르시는 것을 제가 얼마나 자주 들었던가요? 어버이의 온갖 애틋함을 담아 그에 대해 제게 조잘조잘 이야기하시던 적이 또 얼마나 잦았던가요?"], ["Nay, sir, I cannot forget the many tender expressions, the many good things you have told me of his beauty, and his parts, and his virtues; of his good-nature and generosity.", "아니, 어르신, 저는 잊을 수가 없습니다. 그의 고운 용모며 재주며 덕에 대해, 또 그의 선한 성품과 너그러움에 대해 어르신께서 제게 들려주신 그 숱한 다정한 말씀과 그 많은 좋은 이야기들을 말입니다."], ["I am sure, sir, I cannot forget them, for I find them all true.", "정말이지, 어르신, 저는 그것들을 잊을 수가 없습니다. 그 모두가 사실임을 제가 깨달았으니까요."], ["I have experienced them in my own cause.", "저는 제 일을 통해 그것들을 직접 겪었습니다."], ["They have preserved my family.", "그가 제 가족을 지켜 주었습니다."], ["You must pardon my tears, sir, indeed you must.", "제 눈물을 용서해 주십시오, 어르신, 정말로 용서해 주셔야 합니다."], ["When I consider the cruel reverse of fortune which this poor youth, to whom I am so much obliged, hath suffered; when I consider the loss of your favour, which I know he valued more than his life, I must, I must lament him.", "제가 그토록 큰 신세를 진 이 가엾은 젊은이가 겪은 그 모진 운명의 뒤바뀜을 생각하면, 또 그가 제 목숨보다 더 소중히 여겼던 어르신의 호의를 잃은 것을 생각하면, 저는, 저는 그를 안타까워하지 않을 수가 없습니다."], ["If you had a dagger in your hand, ready to plunge into my heart, I must lament the misery of one whom you have loved, and I shall ever love.”", "설령 어르신께서 손에 단검을 쥐고 제 심장을 찌르려 하신다 해도, 저는 어르신께서 한때 사랑하셨고 제가 앞으로도 늘 사랑할 그 사람의 불행을 안타까워하지 않을 수 없을 것입니다.\""]], [["Allworthy was pretty much moved with this speech, but it seemed not to be with anger; for, after a short silence, taking Mrs Miller by the hand, he said very affectionately to her, “Come, madam, let us consider a little about your daughter.", "올워디는 이 말에 자못 마음이 움직였으나, 그것은 노여움 때문은 아닌 듯했다. 잠시 침묵하던 그는 밀러 부인의 손을 잡으며 더없이 다정하게 말했다."], ["I cannot blame you for rejoicing in a match which promises to be advantageous to her, but you know this advantage, in a great measure, depends on the father's reconciliation.", "\"자, 부인, 우리 따님 일을 조금 헤아려 봅시다. 따님에게 이로울 듯한 혼사를 두고 부인이 기뻐하시는 것을 나무랄 수야 없지요."], ["I know Mr Nightingale very well, and have formerly had concerns with him; I will make him a visit, and endeavour to serve you in this matter.", "하지만 부인도 아시다시피, 그 이로움은 상당 부분 아버지가 마음을 풀고 받아들이느냐에 달려 있습니다. 나는 나이팅게일 씨를 아주 잘 알고, 예전에 그와 거래한 일도 있습니다."], ["I believe he is a worldly man; but as this is an only son, and the thing is now irretrievable, perhaps he may in time be brought to reason.", "내가 그를 한번 찾아가, 이 일에서 부인을 도와 드리도록 애써 보겠습니다. 내 생각에 그는 세속적인 사람입니다."], ["I promise you I will do all I can for you.”", "그러나 외아들인 데다 이제는 돌이킬 수 없게 된 일이니, 어쩌면 시간이 지나면 이치를 받아들이게 될지도 모르지요. 부인을 위해 내가 할 수 있는 일은 다 하겠다고 약속드립니다.\""]], [["Many were the acknowledgments which the poor woman made to Allworthy for this kind and generous offer, nor could she refrain from taking this occasion again to express her gratitude towards Jones, “to whom,” said she, “I owe the opportunity of giving you, sir, this present trouble.” Allworthy gently stopped her; but he was too good a man to be really offended with the effects of so noble a principle as now actuated Mrs Miller; and indeed, had not this new affair inflamed his former anger against Jones, it is possible he might have been a little softened towards him, by the report of an action which malice itself could not have derived from an evil motive.", "이 친절하고 너그러운 제안에 가엾은 부인이 올워디에게 건넨 감사의 말은 한두 마디가 아니었다. 또한 부인은 이 기회를 빌려 다시금 존스에 대한 고마움을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다. \"그분 덕분에,\" 부인이 말했다. \"지금 어르신께 이런 폐를 끼칠 기회를 얻은 것입니다.\" 올워디는 부드럽게 그 말을 막았다. 그러나 그는 지금 밀러 부인을 움직이는 그토록 고귀한 마음씨의 발로에 정말로 노여워하기에는 너무도 선량한 사람이었다. 사실, 이번의 새로운 일이 존스를 향한 그의 묵은 노여움에 다시 불을 지피지만 않았던들, 아무리 악의라 해도 결코 나쁜 동기에서 비롯되었다고 둘러댈 수 없는 그 선행의 이야기를 듣고, 그는 어쩌면 그에게 마음이 조금은 누그러졌을지도 모른다."]], [["Mr Allworthy and Mrs Miller had been above an hour together, when their conversation was put an end to by the arrival of Blifil and another person, which other person was no less than Mr Dowling, the attorney, who was now become a great favourite with Mr Blifil, and whom Mr Allworthy, at the desire of his nephew, had made his steward; and had likewise recommended him to Mr Western, from whom the attorney received a promise of being promoted to the same office upon the first vacancy; and, in the meantime, was employed in transacting some affairs which the squire then had in London in relation to a mortgage.", "올워디 씨와 밀러 부인이 한 시간 넘게 함께한 무렵, 그들의 대화는 블리필과 또 한 사람이 들이닥치는 바람에 끝나 버렸다. 그 또 한 사람이란 다름 아닌 변호사 다울링 씨였다. 그는 이제 블리필 씨의 큰 총애를 받는 인물이 되어 있었고, 올워디 씨가 조카의 바람대로 그를 자기 집사로 삼은 터였다. 또한 올워디 씨는 그를 웨스턴 씨에게도 천거하였는데, 다울링은 웨스턴 씨로부터 첫 자리가 비는 대로 같은 직책에 올려 주겠다는 약속을 받아 둔 상태였다. 그 사이에 그는 향사가 당시 런던에서 안고 있던 어떤 저당 관련 일을 처리하는 데 부려지고 있었다."]], [["This was the principal affair which then brought Mr Dowling to town; therefore he took the same opportunity to charge himself with some money for Mr Allworthy, and to make a report to him of some other business; in all which, as it was of much too dull a nature to find any place in this history, we will leave the uncle, nephew, and their lawyer concerned, and resort to other matters.", "이것이 바로 그때 다울링 씨를 런던으로 불러올린 주된 용건이었다. 그래서 그는 이 기회에 올워디 씨에게 전할 돈도 맡아 오고, 또 몇 가지 다른 일을 그에게 보고하기로 했다. 그 모든 일은 이 이야기에 자리할 만한 곳을 찾기에는 너무도 따분한 성격의 것이니, 우리는 이 일에 매인 숙부와 조카, 그리고 그들의 변호사를 그대로 두고 다른 이야기로 옮겨 가기로 하자."]], [["Containing various matters.", "여러 가지 일들을 담은 장."]], [["Before we return to Mr Jones, we will take one more view of Sophia.", "존스 씨에게로 돌아가기에 앞서, 우리는 소피아를 한 번 더 들여다보기로 하자."]], [["Though that young lady had brought her aunt into great good humour by those soothing methods which we have before related, she had not brought her in the least to abate of her zeal for the match with Lord Fellamar.", "비록 이 젊은 아가씨가 앞서 이야기한 그 부드러운 방법들로 고모의 기분을 무척 좋게 만들어 놓기는 했으나, 펠라마 경과의 혼사에 대한 고모의 열의만큼은 조금도 누그러뜨리지 못했다. 이 열의는 이제 벨라스턴 부인에 의해 더욱 활활 타올랐다."], ["This zeal was now inflamed by Lady Bellaston, who had told her the preceding evening, that she was well satisfied from the conduct of Sophia, and from her carriage to his lordship, that all delays would be dangerous, and that the only way to succeed was to press the match forward with such rapidity that the young lady should have no time to reflect, and be obliged to consent while she scarce knew what she did; in which manner, she said, one-half of the marriages among people of condition were brought about.", "부인은 그 전날 저녁 고모에게, 소피아의 행동과 그녀가 경을 대하는 태도를 보건대 어떤 지체도 위험할 것이라 충분히 확신한다고, 그리고 성공할 유일한 길은 그 젊은 아가씨가 곰곰이 생각할 틈조차 없도록 혼사를 빠르게 밀어붙여, 자기가 무슨 일을 하는지도 거의 모르는 사이에 승낙하지 않을 수 없게 만드는 것이라고 말했던 것이다. 부인의 말로는, 지체 있는 사람들 사이에서 이루어지는 혼인의 절반이 이런 식으로 성사된다고 했다."], ["A fact very probably true, and to which, I suppose, is owing the mutual tenderness which afterwards exists among so many happy couples.", "아마도 사실일 법한 이야기이며, 짐작건대 그토록 많은 행복한 부부 사이에 뒷날 깃드는 그 서로에 대한 애틋함도 여기서 비롯되는 것이리라."]], [["A hint of the same kind was given by the same lady to Lord Fellamar; and both these so readily embraced the advice that the very next day was, at his lordship's request, appointed by Mrs Western for a private interview between the young parties.", "같은 부류의 귀띔이 같은 부인에게서 펠라마 경에게도 건네졌다. 그리고 두 사람 모두 이 조언을 어찌나 기꺼이 받아들였던지, 경의 청에 따라 웨스턴 부인은 바로 다음 날을 두 젊은이의 단둘이만의 만남을 위한 날로 정했다."], ["This was communicated to Sophia by her aunt, and insisted upon in such high terms, that, after having urged everything she possibly could invent against it without the least effect, she at last agreed to give the highest instance of complacence which any young lady can give, and consented to see his lordship.", "이 일은 고모를 통해 소피아에게 전해졌고, 어찌나 단호한 말투로 강요되었던지, 소피아는 자기가 지어낼 수 있는 온갖 핑계를 다 들이대고도 조금의 효과조차 보지 못한 끝에, 결국 어떤 젊은 아가씨라도 보일 수 있는 최고의 순응을 보이는 데 동의하여, 경을 만나는 것을 승낙하고 말았다."]], [["As conversations of this kind afford no great entertainment, we shall be excused from reciting the whole that past at this interview; in which, after his lordship had made many declarations of the most pure and ardent passion to the silent blushing Sophia, she at last collected all the spirits she could raise, and with a trembling low voice said, “My lord, you must be yourself conscious whether your former behaviour to me hath been consistent with the professions you now make.”", "이런 종류의 대화는 그리 큰 즐거움을 주지 못하니, 이 만남에서 오간 말을 전부 옮기는 일은 부디 면하게 해 주기를 바란다. 그 자리에서 경이 말없이 얼굴을 붉히는 소피아에게 더없이 순수하고 뜨거운 열정을 거듭 고백한 끝에, 소피아는 마침내 끌어올릴 수 있는 모든 기운을 그러모아 떨리는 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Is there,” answered he, “no way by which I can atone for madness?", "\"경이시여, 예전에 저를 대하시던 행동이 지금 하시는 그 고백과 과연 부합하는지는 경 스스로가 잘 아시겠지요.\""], ["what I did I am afraid must have too plainly convinced you, that the violence of love had deprived me of my senses.”", "\"제 광기를 속죄할 길이 정녕 없겠습니까?\" 그가 대답했다."], ["“Indeed, my lord,”", "\"제가 한 짓은, 사랑의 격정이 저에게서 분별을 앗아 갔음을 너무도 명백히 증명해 보였을까 두렵습니다.\" \"참으로, 경이시여,\""], ["said she, “it is in your power to give me a proof of an affection which I much rather wish to encourage, and to which I should think myself more beholden.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"제가 훨씬 더 북돋우고 싶고, 또 거기에 더 큰 신세를 졌다고 여기고 싶은 그런 애정의 증거를 주시는 것은 경의 능력 안에 있습니다.\""], ["“Name it, madam,”", "\"말씀해 주십시오, 아가씨.\""], ["said my lord, very warmly.", "경이 무척 뜨겁게 말했다."], ["“My lord,”", "\"경이시여,\""], ["says she, looking down upon her fan, “I know you must be sensible how uneasy this pretended passion of yours hath made me.”", "그녀가 부채를 내려다보며 말했다. \"경께서도 이 거짓 열정이 저를 얼마나 괴롭혀 왔는지 틀림없이 느끼고 계실 것입니다.\""], ["“Can you be so cruel to call it pretended?”", "\"어찌 그토록 모질게 그것을 거짓이라 부르실 수 있습니까?\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he. “Yes, my lord,”", "\"그렇습니다, 경이시여,\""], ["answered Sophia, “all professions of love to those whom we persecute are most insulting pretences.", "소피아가 대답했다. \"우리가 괴롭히는 이에게 바치는 사랑의 고백이란 모두 더없이 모욕적인 거짓일 뿐입니다."], ["This pursuit of yours is to me a most cruel persecution: nay, it is taking a most ungenerous advantage of my unhappy situation.”", "경의 이 추적은 제게 더없이 잔인한 박해입니다. 아니, 그것은 저의 불행한 처지를 더없이 야박하게 이용하는 일입니다.\""], ["“Most lovely, most adorable charmer, do not accuse me,”", "\"더없이 사랑스럽고, 더없이 흠모스러운 매혹의 그대여, 저를 탓하지 마십시오.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["cries he, “of taking an ungenerous advantage, while I have no thoughts but what are directed to your honour and interest, and while I have no view, no hope, no ambition, but to throw myself, honour, fortune, everything at your feet.”", "\"제 마음에는 오로지 그대의 명예와 이익으로만 향한 생각뿐이고, 저 자신과 명예와 재산과 모든 것을 그대의 발치에 던지는 것 외에는 어떤 속셈도, 어떤 희망도, 어떤 야심도 없는데, 제가 야박하게 이용한다고 탓하시다니요.\" \"경이시여,\""], ["“My lord,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “it is that fortune and those honours which gave you the advantage of which I complain.", "\"제가 한탄하는 그 우월함을 경께 안겨 준 것이 바로 그 재산과 그 명예입니다. 그것들은 제 친척들을 홀린 매력일 뿐, 제게는 아무래도 좋은 것들입니다."], ["These are the charms which have seduced my relations, but to me they are things indifferent.", "경께서 정녕 제 감사를 받을 자격을 얻고자 하신다면, 길은 단 하나뿐입니다.\" \"용서하십시오, 신성한 그대여,\""], ["If your lordship will merit my gratitude, there is but one way.”", "그가 말했다. \"그런 길은 있을 수 없습니다."], ["“Pardon me, divine creature,”", "제가 그대를 위해 할 수 있는 모든 일은 마땅히 그대가 받아야 할 것이며, 제게는 너무도 큰 기쁨을 주는 일이니, 거기에 그대의 감사가 끼어들 자리란 없습니다.\""], ["said he, “there can be none.", "\"참으로, 경이시여,\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["All I can do for you is so much your due, and will give me so much pleasure, that there is no room for your gratitude.”", "\"경께서는 제 감사를, 제 호의를, 제가 베풀 수 있는 온갖 다정한 생각과 바람을 얻으실 수 있습니다. 아니, 손쉽게 얻으실 수 있습니다."], ["“Indeed, my lord,”", "너그러운 마음을 지닌 분이라면 제 청을 들어주는 일쯤은 분명 쉬울 테니까요."], ["answered she, “you may obtain my gratitude, my good opinion, every kind thought and wish which it is in my power to bestow; nay, you may obtain them with ease, for sure to a generous mind it must be easy to grant my request.", "그러니 부디 간청하건대, 결코 성공할 수 없는 이 추적을 그만두어 주십시오. 경 자신을 위해서도, 또 저를 위해서도 저는 이 호의를 애원합니다."], ["Let me beseech you, then, to cease a pursuit in which you can never have any success.", "분명 경께서는 너무도 고귀하신 분이라, 한 불행한 사람을 괴롭히는 데서 어떤 기쁨도 느끼지 않으실 테니까요. 경께서 저를 어떤 곤경으로 내모신다 해도, 제 명예를 걸고, 제 영혼을 걸고, 경의 뜻이 저를 움직일 수 없고, 또 움직이게 두지도 않겠다는데, 이런 고집으로 경께서 스스로의 괴로움 말고 무엇을 얻으려 하십니까?\""], ["For your own sake as well as mine I entreat this favour; for sure you are too noble to have any pleasure in tormenting an unhappy creature.", "여기서 경은 깊은 한숨을 내쉬더니 말했다. \"그렇다면, 아가씨, 제가 그토록 불행하게도 그대의 혐오와 경멸의 대상이 되었다는 말씀입니까."], ["What can your lordship propose but uneasiness to yourself, by a perseverance, which, upon my honour, upon my soul, cannot, shall not prevail with me, whatever distresses you may drive me to.”", "아니면 다른 누군가가 있지 않은가 의심하더라도 용서해 주시겠습니까?\" 여기서 그가 머뭇거리자, 소피아는 다소 기개 있게 대답했다."], ["Here my lord fetched a deep sigh, and then said--“Is it then, madam, that I am so unhappy to be the object of your dislike and scorn; or will you pardon me if I suspect there is some other?”", "\"경이시여, 저는 제 행동의 까닭을 경께 일일이 해명할 의무가 없습니다. 경께서 베풀어 주신 너그러운 청혼에 대해서는 감사드립니다."], ["Here he hesitated, and Sophia answered with some spirit, “My lord, I shall not be accountable to you for the reasons of my conduct.", "그것이 제 자격이나 기대를 넘어서는 것임은 인정합니다. 그러나 경이시여, 제가 받아들일 수 없노라 밝히는 마당에, 부디 그 까닭만은 캐묻지 말아 주시기를 바랍니다.\""], ["I am obliged to your lordship for the generous offer you have made; I own it is beyond either my deserts or expectations; yet I hope, my lord, you will not insist on my reasons, when I declare I cannot accept it.”", "펠라마 경은 이에 많은 말을 되돌려 주었으나, 우리는 그것을 온전히 이해하지 못하겠고, 어쩌면 그 전부가 사리에든 문법에든 엄밀히 들어맞지도 않았을 것이다. 다만 그는 장광설을 늘어놓은 끝에 이렇게 맺었다."], ["Lord Fellamar returned much to this, which we do not perfectly understand, and perhaps it could not all be strictly reconciled either to sense or grammar; but he concluded his ranting speech with saying, “That if she had pre-engaged herself to any gentleman, however unhappy it would make him, he should think himself bound in honour to desist.”", "\"만일 그대가 어느 신사분께 이미 마음을 약속해 두었다면, 그것이 저를 아무리 불행하게 만든다 해도, 저는 명예를 걸고 물러나는 것이 마땅하다 여기겠습니다.\" 어쩌면 경이 신사라는 말에 지나치게 힘을 주었는지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps my lord laid too much emphasis on the word gentleman; for we cannot else well account for the indignation with which he inspired Sophia, who, in her answer, seemed greatly to resent some affront he had given her.", "그렇지 않고서는 그가 소피아에게 불러일으킨 분노를 달리 설명하기 어렵기 때문이다. 소피아는 그 대답에서, 그가 자신에게 안긴 어떤 모욕에 크게 분개하는 듯이 보였다."]], [["While she was speaking, with her voice more raised than usual, Mrs Western came into the room, the fire glaring in her cheeks, and the flames bursting from her eyes.", "그녀가 평소보다 높아진 목소리로 말하고 있을 때, 웨스턴 부인이 두 뺨에 불길이 이글거리고 두 눈에서 불꽃이 터져 나오는 듯한 모습으로 방으로 들어왔다. \"경이시여,\""], ["“I am ashamed,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “my lord, of the reception which you have met with.", "\"경께서 받으신 이 대접이 부끄럽기 짝이 없습니다."], ["I assure your lordship we are all sensible of the honour done us; and I must tell you, Miss Western, the family expect a different behaviour from you.”", "분명히 말씀드리지만 저희 모두 경께서 베풀어 주신 영광을 잘 알고 있습니다. 그리고 너에게 말해 두겠는데, 웨스턴 양, 집안은 너에게 이와는 다른 행동을 기대하고 있다.\""], ["Here my lord interfered on behalf of the young lady, but to no purpose; the aunt proceeded till Sophia pulled out her handkerchief, threw herself into a chair, and burst into a violent fit of tears.", "여기서 경이 아가씨를 위해 끼어들었으나 아무 소용이 없었다. 고모는 계속 몰아붙였고, 마침내 소피아는 손수건을 꺼내 들고 의자에 몸을 던지더니 격렬한 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다."]], [["The remainder of the conversation between Mrs Western and his lordship, till the latter withdrew, consisted of bitter lamentations on his side, and on hers of the strongest assurances that her niece should and would consent to all he wished.", "웨스턴 부인과 경 사이에 오간 나머지 대화는, 경이 물러갈 때까지, 그의 쪽에서는 쓰라린 한탄으로, 부인의 쪽에서는 조카딸이 그가 바라는 모든 것에 반드시 동의할 것이고 또 동의하게 되리라는 더없이 강한 다짐으로 이루어졌다. \"참으로, 경이시여,\""], ["“Indeed, my lord,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “the girl hath had a foolish education, neither adapted to her fortune nor her family.", "\"저 애는 어리석은 교육을 받았답니다. 제 재산에도, 제 집안에도 어울리지 않는 교육이지요."], ["Her father, I am sorry to say it, is to blame for everything.", "유감스럽게도 그 애 아비가 모든 일의 잘못입니다."], ["The girl hath silly country notions of bashfulness.", "그 애는 수줍음에 관한 어리석은 시골뜨기 생각을 품고 있어요."], ["Nothing else, my lord, upon my honour; I am convinced she hath a good understanding at the bottom, and will be brought to reason.”", "다른 건 없습니다, 경이시여, 제 명예를 걸고요. 저는 그 애가 속으로는 좋은 분별을 지니고 있어서 곧 이치를 깨닫게 되리라 확신합니다.\""]], [["This last speech was made in the absence of Sophia; for she had some time before left the room, with more appearance of passion than she had ever shown on any occasion; and now his lordship, after many expressions of thanks to Mrs Western, many ardent professions of passion which nothing could conquer, and many assurances of perseverance, which Mrs Western highly encouraged, took his leave for this time.", "이 마지막 말은 소피아가 없는 자리에서 한 것이었다. 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 격한 감정을 드러내며 얼마 전에 방을 나가 버렸기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 경은 웨스턴 부인에게 거듭 감사의 말을 전하고, 무엇으로도 꺾을 수 없는 열정을 거듭 뜨겁게 고백하며, 끝까지 밀고 나가겠노라 거듭 다짐한 뒤―웨스턴 부인은 이를 한껏 부추겼다―이번에는 작별을 고했다."]], [["Before we relate what now passed between Mrs Western and Sophia, it may be proper to mention an unfortunate accident which had happened, and which had occasioned the return of Mrs Western with so much fury, as we have seen.", "이제 웨스턴 부인과 소피아 사이에 오간 일을 이야기하기에 앞서, 한 가지 불운한 사건이 일어났음을 짚고 넘어가는 것이 마땅하겠다. 바로 그 일 때문에 우리가 본 대로 웨스턴 부인이 그토록 노발대발하며 돌아왔던 것이다."]], [["The reader then must know that the maid who at present attended on Sophia was recommended by Lady Bellaston, with whom she had lived for some time in the capacity of a comb-brush: she was a very sensible girl, and had received the strictest instructions to watch her young lady very carefully. These instructions, we are sorry to say, were communicated to her by Mrs Honour, into whose favour Lady Bellaston had now so ingratiated herself, that the violent affection which the good waiting-woman had formerly borne to Sophia was entirely obliterated by that great attachment which she had to her new mistress.", "그러니 독자는 알아 두어야 한다. 지금 소피아의 시중을 들고 있는 하녀는 벨라스턴 부인이 천거한 사람으로, 한동안 부인의 곁에서 머리 손질하는 일을 맡아 살았던 처녀였다. 그녀는 무척 영리한 처녀였고, 자기가 모시는 젊은 아가씨를 아주 면밀히 감시하라는 더없이 엄한 지시를 받은 터였다. 유감스럽게도 이 지시는 어너 부인을 통해 그녀에게 전해진 것이었다. 벨라스턴 부인이 이제 어너 부인의 환심을 어찌나 잘 사 두었던지, 이 충직한 시녀가 예전에 소피아에게 품었던 그 격렬한 애정은 새 마님에게 쏟는 그 큰 애착에 완전히 지워져 버렸다."]], [["Now, when Mrs Miller was departed, Betty (for that was the name of the girl), returning to her young lady, found her very attentively engaged in reading a long letter, and the visible emotions which she betrayed on that occasion might have well accounted for some suspicions which the girl entertained; but indeed they had yet a stronger foundation, for she had overheard the whole scene which passed between Sophia and Mrs Miller.", "자, 밀러 부인이 떠나고 나자, 베티(그 처녀의 이름이 그러했다)가 자기 아가씨에게 돌아와 보니, 아가씨는 긴 편지 한 통을 무척 골똘히 읽고 있었다. 그때 아가씨가 내비친 뚜렷한 동요는 이 처녀가 품은 어떤 의심을 충분히 설명해 줄 만한 것이었으나, 실은 그 의심에는 더 단단한 근거가 있었다. 베티는 소피아와 밀러 부인 사이에 오간 장면 전부를 엿들었던 것이다."]], [["Mrs Western was acquainted with all this matter by Betty, who, after receiving many commendations and some rewards for her fidelity, was ordered, that, if the woman who brought the letter came again, she should introduce her to Mrs Western herself.", "웨스턴 부인은 베티에게서 이 모든 일을 전해 들었다. 베티는 그 충직함에 대해 많은 칭찬과 약간의 보상을 받은 뒤, 만일 편지를 가져온 그 여자가 다시 오거든 웨스턴 부인 본인에게 직접 데려오라는 명을 받았다."]], [["Unluckily, Mrs Miller returned at the very time when Sophia was engaged with his lordship.", "공교롭게도 밀러 부인은 소피아가 경과 함께 있던 바로 그때 다시 찾아왔다."], ["Betty, according to order, sent her directly to the aunt; who, being mistress of so many circumstances relating to what had past the day before, easily imposed upon the poor woman to believe that Sophia had communicated the whole affair; and so pumped everything out of her which she knew relating to the letter and relating to Jones.", "베티는 명령대로 그녀를 곧장 고모에게 안내했다. 전날 일어난 일에 관한 여러 정황을 꿰고 있던 고모는, 소피아가 그 일을 전부 자기에게 털어놓았다고 이 가엾은 여자가 믿도록 손쉽게 속여 넘겼고, 그리하여 편지에 관해서나 존스에 관해서나 그녀가 아는 모든 것을 죄다 캐냈다."]], [["This poor creature might, indeed, be called simplicity itself.", "이 가엾은 여자는 실로 순진함 그 자체라 불러도 좋을 만했다."], ["She was one of that order of mortals who are apt to believe everything which is said to them; to whom nature hath neither indulged the offensive nor defensive weapons of deceit, and who are consequently liable to be imposed upon by any one who will only be at the expense of a little falshood for that purpose.", "그녀는 남이 하는 말이라면 무엇이든 곧이곧대로 믿는 부류의 인간이었고, 자연이 기만의 공격용 무기도 방어용 무기도 베풀어 주지 않은 사람이었으며, 따라서 그 목적을 위해 약간의 거짓말을 들이는 수고만 마다하지 않는 자라면 누구에게든 쉽게 속아 넘어가기 마련인 사람이었다. 웨스턴 부인은 밀러 부인이 아는 것을 죄다 짜냈는데, 그것은 실로 얼마 되지 않았으나 고모로 하여금 많은 것을 의심하게 만들기에는 충분했다."], ["Mrs Western, having drained Mrs Miller of all she knew, which, indeed, was but little, but which was sufficient to make the aunt suspect a great deal, dismissed her with assurances that Sophia would not see her, that she would send no answer to the letter, nor ever receive another; nor did she suffer her to depart without a handsome lecture on the merits of an office to which she could afford no better name than that of procuress.--This discovery had greatly discomposed her temper, when, coming into the apartment next to that in which the lovers were, she overheard Sophia very warmly protesting against his lordship's addresses.", "부인은 소피아가 그녀를 만나지 않을 것이며, 편지에 아무 답장도 보내지 않을 것이고, 앞으로도 결코 다른 편지를 받지 않을 것이라 단언하며 그녀를 돌려보냈다. 게다가 그녀를 그냥 보내지도 않고, 뚜쟁이라는 말 외에는 달리 더 나은 이름을 붙여 줄 수 없는 그 노릇의 됨됨이에 대해 한바탕 그럴듯한 훈계를 늘어놓았다."], ["At which the rage already kindled burst forth, and she rushed in upon her niece in a most furious manner, as we have already described, together with what past at that time till his lordship's departure.", "이 발각은 부인의 심기를 크게 어지럽혀 놓았는데, 그러던 차에 연인들이 있는 방의 바로 옆방으로 들어서다가, 소피아가 경의 구애에 무척 격렬하게 항의하는 소리를 엿듣게 되었던 것이다. 그러자 이미 지펴진 분노가 활활 타올랐고, 부인은 우리가 이미 묘사한 대로 더없이 노발대발한 기세로 조카딸에게 달려들었으며, 그 뒤로 경이 떠날 때까지 벌어진 일도 앞서 본 그대로였다."]], [["No sooner was Lord Fellamar gone than Mrs Western returned to Sophia, whom she upbraided in the most bitter terms for the ill use she had made of the confidence reposed in her; and for her treachery in conversing with a man with whom she had offered but the day before to bind herself in the most solemn oath never more to have any conversation.", "펠라마 경이 떠나기가 무섭게 웨스턴 부인은 소피아에게로 돌아왔다. 부인은 자기가 베푼 신뢰를 그 애가 못되게 써먹었다며, 또 바로 전날 결코 다시는 어떤 말도 섞지 않겠노라 가장 엄숙한 맹세로 스스로를 묶겠다고까지 했던 그 사내와 이야기를 나눈 배신을 두고 더없이 쓰라린 말로 그녀를 나무랐다."], ["Sophia protested she had maintained no such conversation.", "소피아는 그런 대화를 나눈 적이 없다고 항변했다. \"뭐라고, 뭐라고!"], ["“How, how!", "웨스턴 양,\""], ["Miss Western,”", "고모가 말했다."], ["said the aunt; “will you deny your receiving a letter from him yesterday?”", "\"어제 그자에게서 편지를 받은 일을 부인하겠다는 거냐?\" \"편지라뇨, 고모님!\""], ["“A letter, madam!”", "소피아가 다소 놀라며 대답했다."], ["answered Sophia, somewhat surprized.", "\"내 말을 따라 되뇌는 건 그리 점잖은 짓이 아니다, 얘야.\""], ["“It is not very well bred, miss,”", "고모가 받아쳤다. \"편지라고 했다."], ["replies the aunt, “to repeat my words.", "그리고 당장 그걸 내게 보여 주기를 요구한다.\""], ["I say a letter, and I insist upon your showing it me immediately.”", "\"저는 거짓말을 경멸합니다, 고모님.\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["“I scorn a lie, madam,”", "\"편지를 받은 것은 사실입니다."], ["said Sophia; “I did receive a letter, but it was without my desire, and, indeed, I may say, against my consent.”", "하지만 그건 제가 바란 일도 아니었고, 정말이지 제 뜻을 거슬러 받은 것이라 해도 좋을 것입니다.\" \"정말이지, 정말이지, 얘야,\""], ["“Indeed, indeed, miss,”", "고모가 외쳤다."], ["cries the aunt, “you ought to be ashamed of owning you had received it at all; but where is the letter?", "\"그걸 받았다는 사실을 인정하는 것만으로도 부끄러워해야 마땅하다. 한데 그 편지는 어디 있느냐?"], ["for I will see it.”", "내가 봐야겠다.\""]], [["To this peremptory demand, Sophia paused some time before she returned an answer; and at last only excused herself by declaring she had not the letter in her pocket, which was, indeed, true; upon which her aunt, losing all manner of patience, asked her niece this short question, whether she would resolve to marry Lord Fellamar, or no?", "이 단호한 요구에 소피아는 한동안 말을 멈추었다가 대답을 내놓았는데, 결국에는 편지를 주머니에 지니고 있지 않다고 밝히는 것으로 변명을 대신했다. 사실 그것은 참말이었다."], ["to which she received the strongest negative.", "그러자 고모는 온갖 인내심을 잃고 조카딸에게 이 짤막한 질문을 던졌다. 펠라마 경과 혼인할 결심이 섰느냐, 아니냐?"], ["Mrs Western then replied with an oath, or something very like one, that she would early the next morning deliver her back into her father's hand.", "이에 부인은 더없이 단호한 부정의 대답을 들었다. 그러자 웨스턴 부인은 맹세, 혹은 거기에 무척 가까운 무엇과 함께, 다음 날 아침 일찍 너를 도로 네 아비 손에 넘겨주겠다고 받아쳤다."]], [["Sophia then began to reason with her aunt in the following manner:--“Why, madam, must I of necessity be forced to marry at all?", "그러자 소피아는 다음과 같이 고모에게 따지기 시작했다. \"어찌하여, 고모님, 저는 기어이 혼인을 강요당해야만 하는 건가요?"], ["Consider how cruel you would have thought it in your own case, and how much kinder your parents were in leaving you to your liberty.", "고모님 자신의 일이었다면 그것을 얼마나 잔인하다 여기셨을지, 또 고모님을 자유롭게 내버려 두셨던 부모님이 얼마나 더 다정하셨을지 생각해 보세요. 제가 이 자유를 잃을 만한 무슨 짓을 했나요?"], ["What have I done to forfeit this liberty?", "저는 결코 아버지의 승낙을 거슬러 혼인하지 않을 것이고, 고모님의 승낙을 구하지 않고서도 하지 않을 거예요."], ["I will never marry contrary to my father's consent, nor without asking yours----And when I ask the consent of either improperly, it will be then time enough to force some other marriage upon me.”", "그리고 제가 어느 한 분의 승낙이라도 부당하게 구하게 되거든, 그때 가서 제게 다른 혼인을 강요하셔도 늦지 않을 거예요.\" \"지금 주머니에 살인자가 보낸 편지를 지닌 계집애한테서,\""], ["“Can I bear to hear this,”", "웨스턴 부인이 외쳤다. \"내가 이런 소릴 들어야 한단 말이냐?\""], ["cries Mrs Western, “from a girl who hath now a letter from a murderer in her pocket?”", "\"그런 편지는 지니고 있지 않아요, 약속드려요.\" 소피아가 대답했다."], ["“I have no such letter, I promise you,”", "\"그리고 그분이 살인자라면, 곧 고모님께 더 이상의 성가심을 끼칠 처지가 못 될 거예요.\" \"뭐라고, 웨스턴 양!\""], ["answered Sophia; “and, if he be a murderer, he will soon be in no condition to give you any further disturbance.”", "고모가 말했다. \"네가 감히 그자를 두고 이런 식으로 말하다니, 내 면전에서 그런 악당에게 품은 애정을 인정하다니?\""], ["“How, Miss Western!”", "\"분명, 고모님,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["said the aunt, “have you the assurance to speak of him in this manner; to own your affection for such a villain to my face?”", "\"고모님께서는 제 말을 무척 이상하게 해석하시는군요.\" \"정말이지, 웨스턴 양,\""], ["“Sure, madam,”", "부인이 외쳤다."], ["said Sophia, “you put a very strange construction on my words.”", "\"나는 이런 대접을 참지 않겠다."], ["“Indeed, Miss Western,”", "너는 네 아비한테서 나를 이렇게 다루는 법을 배웠어."], ["cries the lady, “I shall not bear this usage; you have learnt of your father this manner of treating me; he hath taught you to give me the lie.", "그자가 너에게 나더러 거짓말쟁이라 하는 법을 가르친 게다. 그자는 이 그릇된 교육 방식으로 너를 완전히 망쳐 놓았어."], ["He hath totally ruined you by this false system of education; and, please heaven, he shall have the comfort of its fruits; for once more I declare to you, that to-morrow morning I will carry you back.", "하느님의 뜻이라면, 그자는 그 열매가 주는 위안을 톡톡히 맛보게 될 게다. 다시 한 번 너에게 선언하건대, 내일 아침 나는 너를 도로 데려가겠다."], ["I will withdraw all my forces from the field, and remain henceforth, like the wise king of Prussia, in a state of perfect neutrality.", "나는 전장에서 내 모든 병력을 거두어, 이제부터는 저 현명한 프로이센 왕처럼 완전한 중립 상태에 머물겠다. 너희 둘 다 내 방책에 따르기엔 너무도 영리하니 말이다."], ["You are both too wise to be regulated by my measures; so prepare yourself, for to-morrow morning you shall evacuate this house.”", "그러니 채비를 해 두어라. 내일 아침 너는 이 집을 비워야 할 테니까.\""]], [["Sophia remonstrated all she could; but her aunt was deaf to all she said.", "소피아는 할 수 있는 한 온갖 항변을 했지만, 고모는 그녀가 하는 말에 도무지 귀를 기울이지 않았다."], ["In this resolution therefore we must at present leave her, as there seems to be no hopes of bringing her to change it.", "따라서 우리는 지금으로서는 그녀를 이 결심 속에 그대로 남겨 둘 수밖에 없다. 그 결심을 바꾸게 할 가망이라곤 없어 보이니 말이다."]], [["What happened to Mr Jones in the prison.", "감옥에서 존스에게 일어난 일."]], [["Mr Jones passed about twenty-four melancholy hours by himself, unless when relieved by the company of Partridge, before Mr Nightingale returned; not that this worthy young man had deserted or forgot his friend; for, indeed, he had been much the greatest part of the time employed in his service.", "존스는 나이팅게일이 돌아오기 전까지 스물네 시간 남짓을 홀로 우울하게 보냈다. 그나마 파트리지가 곁에 있어 줄 때만 그 외로움을 덜 수 있었다. 그렇다고 이 갸륵한 젊은이가 친구를 저버렸거나 잊은 것은 아니었다. 사실 그는 그 시간의 거의 대부분을 친구를 위한 일에 쏟고 있었다."]], [["He had heard, upon enquiry, that the only persons who had seen the beginning of the unfortunate rencounter were a crew belonging to a man-of-war which then lay at Deptford.", "그는 수소문 끝에, 그 불행한 충돌의 시작을 목격한 유일한 사람들이 그때 뎁트퍼드에 정박해 있던 군함 소속 선원들이라는 사실을 알아냈다."], ["To Deptford therefore he went in search of this crew, where he was informed that the men he sought after were all gone ashore.", "그리하여 그는 그 선원들을 찾아 뎁트퍼드로 갔으나, 거기서 찾는 자들이 모두 뭍으로 올라갔다는 말을 들었다."], ["He then traced them from place to place, till at last he found two of them drinking together, with a third person, at a hedge-tavern near Aldersgate.", "그는 이곳저곳으로 그들의 자취를 좇았고, 마침내 올더스게이트 근처의 어느 허름한 선술집에서 그들 가운데 둘이 또 다른 사람 하나와 함께 술을 마시고 있는 것을 찾아냈다."]], [["Nightingale desired to speak with Jones by himself (for Partridge was in the room when he came in).", "나이팅게일은 존스와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶다고 했다(그가 들어왔을 때 방 안에 파트리지가 있었기 때문이다). 둘만 남게 되자 나이팅게일은 존스의 손을 잡고 외쳤다."], ["As soon as they were alone, Nightingale, taking Jones by the hand, cried, “Come, my brave friend, be not too much dejected at what I am going to tell you----I am sorry I am the messenger of bad news; but I think it my duty to tell you.”", "\"이보게, 나의 용감한 친구, 내가 이제부터 하려는 말에 너무 낙담하지 말게. 나쁜 소식을 전하는 사람이 나라는 게 미안하네."], ["“I guess already what that bad news is,”", "그러나 자네에게 알리는 것이 내 도리라고 생각하네.\" \"그 나쁜 소식이 뭔지 벌써 짐작이 가네.\""], ["cries Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["“The poor gentleman then is dead.”--“I hope not,”", "\"그 가엾은 신사가 죽었군.\" \"아니길 바라네.\""], ["answered Nightingale.", "나이팅게일이 대답했다. \"오늘 아침까지는 살아 있었네."], ["“He was alive this morning; though I will not flatter you; I fear, from the accounts I could get, that his wound is mortal.", "하지만 자네에게 듣기 좋은 말만 하지는 않겠네. 내가 알아낸 바로는 그의 상처가 치명적인 것 같아 두렵네."], ["But if the affair be exactly as you told it, your own remorse would be all you would have reason to apprehend, let what would happen; but forgive me, my dear Tom, if I entreat you to make the worst of your story to your friends.", "그러나 일이 자네가 말한 그대로라면, 무슨 일이 일어나든 자네가 걱정해야 할 것은 자네 자신의 양심의 가책뿐일 걸세. 다만 용서하게, 나의 친애하는 톰, 친구들에게는 자네 이야기를 가장 나쁜 쪽으로 들려주라고 간청하는 것을 말일세."], ["If you disguise anything to us, you will only be an enemy to yourself.”", "만일 자네가 우리에게 무언가를 숨긴다면, 그것은 결국 자네 자신의 적이 되는 길일 뿐이라네.\""]], [["“What reason, my dear Jack, have I ever given you,” said Jones, “to stab me with so cruel a suspicion?”", "\"나의 친애하는 잭, 내가 언제 자네에게 그토록 잔인한 의심으로 나를 찌를 빌미를 준 적이 있던가?\""], ["“Have patience,”", "존스가 말했다. \"참아 보게.\""], ["cries Nightingale, “and I will tell you all.", "나이팅게일이 외쳤다. \"그러면 내가 다 말해 주겠네."], ["After the most diligent enquiry I could make, I at last met with two of the fellows who were present at this unhappy accident, and I am sorry to say, they do not relate the story so much in your favour as you yourself have told it.”", "내가 할 수 있는 가장 부지런한 수소문 끝에, 마침내 이 불행한 사건 현장에 있었던 자들 가운데 둘을 만났네. 그런데 말하기 안타깝지만, 그들은 자네가 직접 들려준 것만큼 자네에게 유리하게 이야기를 전하지 않더군.\""], ["“Why, what do they say?”", "\"아니, 그들이 뭐라고 하던가?\""], ["cries Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["“Indeed what I am sorry to repeat, as I am afraid of the consequence of it to you.", "\"사실, 자네에게 미칠 결과가 두려워 되풀이하기가 안타까운 말이네. 그들은 자기들이 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어서 자네들 사이에 오간 말은 한마디도 듣지 못했다고 하네."], ["They say that they were at too great a distance to overhear any words that passed between you: but they both agree that the first blow was given by you.”", "그러나 둘 다 첫 일격을 가한 사람은 자네라고 입을 모으더군.\" \"그렇다면, 내 영혼을 걸고 말하지.\""], ["“Then, upon my soul,”", "존스가 대답했다. \"그들은 나를 모함하는 걸세."], ["answered Jones, “they injure me.", "그는 나를 먼저 쳤을 뿐 아니라, 조금의 도발도 없는데 나를 친 거야. 무엇이 그 악당들로 하여금 나를 거짓으로 고발하게 만든단 말인가?\""], ["He not only struck me first, but struck me without the least provocation.", "\"글쎄, 그건 나도 짐작이 안 가네.\" 나이팅게일이 말했다."], ["What should induce those villains to accuse me falsely?” “Nay, that I cannot guess,”", "\"그리고 자네 자신도, 그리고 진심으로 자네 편인 나도 그들이 왜 자네를 거짓으로 헐뜯어야 하는지 그 이유를 떠올리지 못한다면, 이해관계 없는 법정인들 그들의 말을 믿지 말아야 할 이유를 무엇으로 댈 수 있겠나?"], ["said Nightingale, “and if you yourself, and I, who am so heartily your friend, cannot conceive a reason why they should belie you, what reason will an indifferent court of justice be able to assign why they should not believe them?", "나는 그 질문을 그들에게 여러 차례 되풀이했고, 그 자리에 있던 다른 신사 한 사람도 그렇게 했네. 내가 보기에 그는 뱃사람인 듯한데, 정말로 자네에게 더없이 우호적으로 행동해 주었네."], ["I repeated the question to them several times, and so did another gentleman who was present, who, I believe, is a seafaring man, and who really acted a very friendly part by you; for he begged them often to consider that there was the life of a man in the case; and asked them over and over, if they were certain; to which they both answered, that they were, and would abide by their evidence upon oath.", "그는 이 일에 한 사람의 목숨이 걸려 있다는 것을 거듭 생각해 보라고 그들에게 간청했고, 확실하냐고 몇 번이나 물었네. 그런데 둘 다 확실하다고, 맹세하고서라도 자기들 증언을 지키겠다고 대답하더군."], ["For heaven's sake, my dear friend, recollect yourself; for, if this should appear to be the fact, it will be your business to think in time of making the best of your interest.", "제발, 나의 친애하는 친구여, 정신을 가다듬게. 만일 이것이 사실로 드러난다면, 자네는 때를 놓치지 말고 자네에게 이로운 방도를 최대한 마련할 궁리를 해야 할 걸세."], ["I would not shock you; but you know, I believe, the severity of the law, whatever verbal provocations may have been given you.”", "자네를 충격에 빠뜨리고 싶지는 않네. 그러나 자네에게 어떤 말로 된 도발이 있었든 간에, 법이 얼마나 가혹한지는 자네도 알고 있으리라 믿네.\""], ["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["my friend,”", "나의 친구여,\""], ["cries Jones, “what interest hath such a wretch as I?", "존스가 외쳤다. \"나 같은 가련한 자에게 무슨 이로운 방도가 있겠나?"], ["Besides, do you think I would even wish to live with the reputation of a murderer?", "게다가, 내가 살인자라는 평판을 안고 살기를 바라기라도 할 것 같은가?"], ["If I had any friends (as, alas!", "설령 내게 친구가 있다 한들(아아!"], ["I have none), could I have the confidence to solicit them to speak in the behalf of a man condemned for the blackest crime in human nature?", "내겐 아무도 없지만), 인간 본성 가운데 가장 시커먼 죄로 단죄받은 사람을 위해 변호해 달라고 그들에게 청할 염치가 내게 있겠나? 믿어 주게, 내겐 그런 희망이 없네."], ["Believe me, I have no such hope; but I have some reliance on a throne still greatly superior; which will, I am certain, afford me all the protection I merit.”", "다만 나는 훨씬 더 높은 어떤 보좌에 약간의 의지를 두고 있을 뿐일세. 그분께서는, 분명히, 내가 받아 마땅한 모든 보호를 베풀어 주실 걸세.\""]], [["He then concluded with many solemn and vehement protestations of the truth of what he had at first asserted.", "그러고 나서 그는 처음에 단언했던 것이 진실이라며, 엄숙하고 격렬한 항변을 거듭하는 것으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["The faith of Nightingale was now again staggered, and began to incline to credit his friend, when Mrs Miller appeared, and made a sorrowful report of the success of her embassy; which when Jones had heard, he cried out most heroically, “Well, my friend, I am now indifferent as to what shall happen, at least with regard to my life; and if it be the will of Heaven that I shall make an atonement with that for the blood I have spilt, I hope the Divine Goodness will one day suffer my honour to be cleared, and that the words of a dying man, at least, will be believed, so far as to justify his character.”", "흔들렸던 나이팅게일의 믿음은 이제 다시 흔들려, 친구의 말을 믿는 쪽으로 기울기 시작했다. 그때 밀러 부인이 나타나 자신이 맡았던 임무의 결과를 슬픈 어조로 전했다. 그 말을 들은 존스는 더없이 비장하게 외쳤다. \"좋소, 나의 친구여, 이제 나는 무슨 일이 일어나든 개의치 않겠소. 적어도 내 목숨에 관해서는 말이오. 그리고 내가 흘린 피를 내 목숨으로 속죄하는 것이 하늘의 뜻이라면, 신의 선하심이 언젠가 내 명예가 씻기는 것을 허락해 주시기를, 그리하여 적어도 죽어 가는 자의 말이, 그의 인품을 정당하게 밝혀 줄 만큼은 믿어지기를 바라오.\""]], [["A very mournful scene now past between the prisoner and his friends, at which, as few readers would have been pleased to be present, so few, I believe, will desire to hear it particularly related.", "이제 죄수와 그의 친구들 사이에 매우 애통한 장면이 펼쳐졌다. 그 자리에 함께 있고 싶어할 독자가 거의 없을 것이듯, 그것을 자세히 듣고 싶어할 독자도 거의 없으리라 믿는다."], ["We will, therefore, pass on to the entrance of the turnkey, who acquainted Jones that there was a lady without who desired to speak with him when he was at leisure.", "그러니 우리는 간수가 들어오는 대목으로 넘어가기로 하자. 그는 존스에게, 밖에 한 부인이 와 있는데 그가 한가할 때 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 알렸다."]], [["Jones declared his surprize at this message.", "존스는 이 전갈에 놀라움을 드러냈다."], ["He said, “He knew no lady in the world whom he could possibly expect to see there.”", "그는 말했다. \"세상에 이런 곳에서 만날 거라고 기대할 만한 부인은 아무도 없는데.\""], ["However, as he saw no reason to decline seeing any person, Mrs Miller and Mr Nightingale presently took their leave, and he gave orders to have the lady admitted.", "그러나 어떤 사람이든 만나기를 거절할 이유가 없었으므로, 밀러 부인과 나이팅게일은 곧 작별을 고했고, 그는 그 부인을 들여보내라고 일렀다."]], [["If Jones was surprized at the news of a visit from a lady, how greatly was he astonished when he discovered this lady to be no other than Mrs Waters!", "존스가 한 부인의 방문 소식에 놀랐다면, 그 부인이 다름 아닌 워터스 부인임을 알았을 때 그가 얼마나 경악했겠는가!"], ["In this astonishment then we shall leave him awhile, in order to cure the surprize of the reader, who will likewise, probably, not a little wonder at the arrival of this lady.", "그러니 우리는 그를 잠시 그 경악 속에 남겨 두고, 이 부인의 등장에 마찬가지로 적잖이 의아해할 독자의 놀라움부터 풀어 주기로 하자."]], [["Who this Mrs Waters was, the reader pretty well knows; what she was, he must be perfectly satisfied.", "이 워터스 부인이 누구인지는 독자가 제법 잘 알고 있다. 그녀가 어떤 사람인지에 대해서도 독자는 충분히 알고 있을 것이다."], ["He will therefore be pleased to remember that this lady departed from Upton in the same coach with Mr Fitzpatrick and the other Irish gentleman, and in their company travelled to Bath.", "그러니 부디 기억해 주기 바란다. 이 부인은 피츠패트릭 씨와 또 다른 아일랜드 신사와 같은 마차를 타고 업튼을 떠났고, 그들과 함께 배스까지 여행했다는 것을."]], [["Now there was a certain office in the gift of Mr Fitzpatrick at that time vacant, namely that of a wife: for the lady who had lately filled that office had resigned, or at least deserted her duty.", "그런데 그 무렵 피츠패트릭 씨가 내려 줄 수 있는 어떤 자리 하나가 비어 있었으니, 바로 아내라는 자리였다. 얼마 전까지 그 자리를 채우고 있던 부인이 사임했거나, 적어도 자신의 임무를 저버렸기 때문이다."], ["Mr Fitzpatrick therefore, having thoroughly examined Mrs Waters on the road, found her extremely fit for the place, which, on their arrival at Bath, he presently conferred upon her, and she without any scruple accepted.", "그리하여 피츠패트릭 씨는 길에서 워터스 부인을 철저히 살펴본 끝에 그녀가 그 자리에 더없이 적합하다는 것을 알게 되었고, 배스에 도착하자마자 곧 그 자리를 그녀에게 내주었으며, 그녀도 아무 거리낌 없이 받아들였다."], ["As husband and wife this gentleman and lady continued together all the time they stayed at Bath, and as husband and wife they arrived together in town.", "이 신사와 부인은 배스에 머무는 내내 부부로서 함께 지냈고, 부부로서 함께 도시에 도착했다."]], [["Whether Mr Fitzpatrick was so wise a man as not to part with one good thing till he had secured another, which he had at present only a prospect of regaining; or whether Mrs Waters had so well discharged her office, that he intended still to retain her as principal, and to make his wife (as is often the case) only her deputy, I will not say; but certain it is, he never mentioned his wife to her, never communicated to her the letter given him by Mrs Western, nor ever once hinted his purpose of repossessing his wife; much less did he ever mention the name of Jones.", "피츠패트릭 씨가 다른 하나를 확보하기 전에는 좋은 것 하나를 놓지 않을 만큼 영리한 사람이어서—지금으로서는 그 다른 하나를 되찾을 가망만 있었으니—그런 것인지, 아니면 워터스 부인이 그 직무를 어찌나 잘 수행했는지 그가 여전히 그녀를 정처로 두고, (흔히 그러하듯) 자기 아내는 그저 그녀의 대리쯤으로 삼으려 했던 것인지는 내가 말하지 않겠다. 그러나 분명한 것은, 그가 그녀에게 아내 이야기를 한 번도 꺼내지 않았고, 웨스턴 부인이 그에게 준 편지를 그녀에게 알리지도 않았으며, 아내를 되찾으려는 자신의 속셈을 단 한 번도 내비치지 않았다는 사실이다."], ["For, though he intended to fight with him wherever he met him, he did not imitate those prudent persons who think a wife, a mother, a sister, or sometimes a whole family, the safest seconds on these occasions.", "하물며 존스의 이름은 입에 올린 적조차 없었다. 왜냐하면 그는 어디서든 존스를 만나면 결투를 벌일 작정이었지만, 아내든 어머니든 누이든, 때로는 온 가족이든 이런 경우에 가장 안전한 결투 입회인이라고 여기는 그런 신중한 사람들을 본받지는 않았기 때문이다."], ["The first account therefore which she had of all this was delivered to her from his lips, after he was brought home from the tavern where his wound had been drest.", "그러므로 그녀가 이 모든 일에 대해 처음 들은 이야기는, 그가 상처를 치료받았던 선술집에서 집으로 실려 온 뒤 그의 입에서 나온 것이었다."]], [["As Mr Fitzpatrick, however, had not the clearest way of telling a story at any time, and was now, perhaps, a little more confused than usual, it was some time before she discovered that the gentleman who had given him this wound was the very same person from whom her heart had received a wound, which, though not of a mortal kind, was yet so deep that it had left a considerable scar behind it.", "그러나 피츠패트릭 씨는 평소에도 이야기를 그다지 또렷하게 풀어내는 사람이 아니었고, 이번에는 어쩌면 평소보다 조금 더 정신이 혼미했던 터라, 그에게 이 상처를 입힌 신사가 다름 아닌 그녀의 마음에 상처를 입힌 바로 그 사람이라는 것을 그녀가 알아차리기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 그 상처는 치명적인 종류는 아니었으나 꽤 깊어서 상당한 흉터를 남겨 두고 간 것이었다."], ["But no sooner was she acquainted that Mr Jones himself was the man who had been committed to the Gatehouse for this supposed murder, than she took the first opportunity of committing Mr Fitzpatrick to the care of his nurse, and hastened away to visit the conqueror.", "그러나 이 추정상의 살인으로 게이트하우스에 수감된 사람이 바로 존스 씨라는 것을 알게 되자마자, 그녀는 첫 기회를 잡아 피츠패트릭 씨를 간병인의 손에 맡겨 두고, 그 승리자를 찾아 서둘러 길을 나섰다."]], [["She now entered the room with an air of gaiety, which received an immediate check from the melancholy aspect of poor Jones, who started and blessed himself when he saw her.", "그녀는 이제 명랑한 기색으로 방에 들어섰으나, 그 기색은 가엾은 존스의 우울한 모습에 곧장 꺾이고 말았다. 존스는 그녀를 보자 깜짝 놀라 성호를 그었다."], ["Upon which she said, “Nay, I do not wonder at your surprize; I believe you did not expect to see me; for few gentlemen are troubled here with visits from any lady, unless a wife.", "그러자 그녀가 말했다. \"아니, 당신이 놀라는 것도 무리는 아니죠."], ["You see the power you have over me, Mr Jones.", "당신은 저를 보리라고는 기대하지 못했을 테니까요. 이런 곳에서 어느 부인의 방문으로 신경 쓰이는 신사는 거의 없잖아요, 아내라면 모를까."], ["Indeed, I little thought, when we parted at Upton, that our next meeting would have been in such a place.”", "당신이 저에게 얼마나 큰 힘을 가졌는지 보세요, 존스 씨. 정말이지, 우리가 업튼에서 헤어질 때만 해도 다음번 만남이 이런 곳에서 이루어질 줄은 거의 생각지도 못했어요.\""], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"정말이지, 부인,\" 존스가 말했다."], ["says Jones, “I must look upon this visit as kind; few will follow the miserable, especially to such dismal habitations.”", "\"이 방문을 친절로 여길 수밖에 없군요. 비참한 자를 따라와 주는 사람은 거의 없으니까요, 더구나 이런 음울한 거처까지는 말입니다.\""], ["“I protest, Mr Jones,”", "\"맹세컨대, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “I can hardly persuade myself you are the same agreeable fellow I saw at Upton.", "\"당신이 업튼에서 보았던 그 유쾌한 사람과 같은 사람이라고는 도무지 믿기지 않네요. 아니, 당신 얼굴이 세상 어느 지하 감옥보다 더 비참해 보이는걸요."], ["Why, your face is more miserable than any dungeon in the universe.", "대체 무슨 일이 있는 거예요?\" \"부인,\""], ["What can be the matter with you?”", "존스가 말했다. \"제가 여기 있는 걸 아셨으니, 그 불행한 사연도 알고 계시리라 생각했습니다.\""], ["“I thought, madam,”", "\"쳇!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said Jones, “as you knew of my being here, you knew the unhappy reason.”", "\"결투에서 사람 하나 찔렀을 뿐이잖아요, 그게 다죠.\" 존스는 이런 가벼운 태도에 약간의 분개를 드러내며, 일어난 일에 대해 더없는 회한을 담아 말했다."], ["“Pugh!”", "그러자 그녀가 대답했다."], ["says she, “you have pinked a man in a duel, that's all.”", "\"그럼, 좋아요, 선생님, 그 일을 그토록 마음에 두신다면 제가 시름을 덜어 드리죠. 그 신사는 죽지 않았어요."], ["Jones exprest some indignation at this levity, and spoke with the utmost contrition for what had happened.", "그리고 저는 그가 죽을 위험은 없다고 꽤 확신해요. 사실 처음 그를 치료한 외과의는 젊은 친구였는데, 그를 고쳐서 더 큰 명예를 얻으려고 그의 상태를 되도록 나쁘게 보이려는 듯했어요."], ["To which she answered, “Well, then, sir, if you take it so much to heart, I will relieve you; the gentleman is not dead, and, I am pretty confident, is in no danger of dying.", "하지만 그 후 왕실 외과의가 그를 진찰했고, 지금으로선 아무 증상도 없는 열병이라도 생기지 않는 한 목숨에 조금의 위험도 없다고 했답니다.\" 존스는 이 소식에 얼굴 가득 큰 만족을 드러냈다."], ["The surgeon, indeed, who first dressed him was a young fellow, and seemed desirous of representing his case to be as bad as possible, that he might have the more honour from curing him: but the king's surgeon hath seen him since, and says, unless from a fever, of which there are at present no symptoms, he apprehends not the least danger of life.”", "그러자 그녀는 그것이 사실임을 거듭 확언하며 덧붙였다. \"세상에서 가장 기막힌 우연으로, 제가 같은 집에 묵고 있어요."], ["Jones shewed great satisfaction in his countenance at this report; upon which she affirmed the truth of it, adding, “By the most extraordinary accident in the world I lodge at the same house; and have seen the gentleman, and I promise you he doth you justice, and says, whatever be the consequence, that he was entirely the aggressor, and that you was not in the least to blame.”", "그 신사도 만나 봤는데, 장담컨대 그는 당신에게 공정하게 말하더군요. 결과가 어찌 되든, 자기가 전적으로 먼저 덤빈 쪽이었고 당신에게는 조금도 잘못이 없다고 말이에요.\""]], [["Jones expressed the utmost satisfaction at the account which Mrs Waters brought him.", "존스는 워터스 부인이 전해 준 이야기에 더없는 만족을 드러냈다."], ["He then informed her of many things which she well knew before, as who Mr Fitzpatrick was, the occasion of his resentment, &c.", "그러고 나서 그는 그녀가 이미 잘 알고 있는 여러 가지를 알려 주었으니, 피츠패트릭 씨가 누구인지, 그의 원한의 까닭이 무엇인지 따위였다."], ["He likewise told her several facts of which she was ignorant, as the adventure of the muff, and other particulars, concealing only the name of Sophia.", "또한 그녀가 모르고 있던 여러 사실도 들려주었는데, 토시 사건이라든가 그 밖의 자잘한 일들이었고, 다만 소피아의 이름만은 감추었다."], ["He then lamented the follies and vices of which he had been guilty; every one of which, he said, had been attended with such ill consequences, that he should be unpardonable if he did not take warning, and quit those vicious courses for the future.", "그런 다음 그는 자신이 저질러 온 어리석음과 악덕을 한탄했다. 그 하나하나가 모두 어찌나 나쁜 결과를 불러왔던지, 만일 그것을 교훈 삼아 앞으로 그 사악한 길을 버리지 않는다면 자신은 용서받지 못할 자라고 말했다."], ["He lastly concluded with assuring her of his resolution to sin no more, lest a worse thing should happen to him.", "끝으로 그는, 더 나쁜 일이 자신에게 닥치지 않도록 다시는 죄를 짓지 않겠다는 결심을 그녀에게 다짐하는 것으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["Mrs Waters with great pleasantry ridiculed all this, as the effects of low spirits and confinement.", "워터스 부인은 이 모든 것을 기분 가라앉음과 갇혀 있는 처지가 빚어낸 결과라며 더없이 익살스럽게 비웃었다. 그녀는 악마도 병들면 어쩌고 하는 우스갯소리 몇 마디를 늘어놓고는 그에게 말했다."], ["She repeated some witticisms about the devil when he was sick, and told him, “She doubted not but shortly to see him at liberty, and as lively a fellow as ever; and then,”", "\"당신이 머지않아 풀려나 예전처럼 활기찬 사람이 되는 걸 분명히 보게 될 거예요. 그리고 그때가 되면,\""], ["says she, “I don't question but your conscience will be safely delivered of all these qualms that it is now so sick in breeding.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"지금 그렇게 앓으며 품고 있는 그 모든 양심의 가책도 무사히 다 떨쳐 버릴 게 틀림없어요.\""]], [["Many more things of this kind she uttered, some of which it would do her no great honour, in the opinion of some readers, to remember; nor are we quite certain but that the answers made by Jones would be treated with ridicule by others.", "그녀는 이런 식의 말을 훨씬 더 많이 늘어놓았는데, 그중 어떤 것들은 일부 독자가 보기에 기억해 둔다 해서 그녀에게 그리 명예로울 게 없을 것이다. 또한 존스가 한 대답들이 다른 독자들에게 조롱거리로 다루어지지 않으리라고 우리도 썩 자신하지는 못하겠다."], ["We shall therefore suppress the rest of this conversation, and only observe that it ended at last with perfect innocence, and much more to the satisfaction of Jones than of the lady; for the former was greatly transported with the news she had brought him; but the latter was not altogether so pleased with the penitential behaviour of a man whom she had, at her first interview, conceived a very different opinion of from what she now entertained of him.", "그러므로 우리는 이 대화의 나머지는 덮어 두기로 하고, 다만 그것이 끝내 완전무결한 순결함 속에서 마무리되었으며, 부인보다는 존스에게 훨씬 더 만족스럽게 끝났다는 점만 짚어 두겠다. 전자는 그녀가 가져온 소식에 크게 들떴지만, 후자는 처음 만났을 때 품었던 것과는 사뭇 다른 생각을 지금 품게 된 그 남자의 뉘우치는 행동이 그다지 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Thus the melancholy occasioned by the report of Mr Nightingale was pretty well effaced; but the dejection into which Mrs Miller had thrown him still continued.", "이리하여 나이팅게일의 전언이 불러온 우울은 제법 말끔히 지워졌다. 그러나 밀러 부인이 그를 빠뜨려 놓은 낙담만은 여전히 가시지 않았다."], ["The account she gave so well tallied with the words of Sophia herself in her letter, that he made not the least doubt but that she had disclosed his letter to her aunt, and had taken a fixed resolution to abandon him.", "그녀가 전한 이야기는 편지 속 소피아 자신의 말과 어찌나 잘 들어맞던지, 그는 그녀가 자기 편지를 숙모에게 털어놓았고 자신을 버리기로 굳게 마음먹었다는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았다."], ["The torments this thought gave him were to be equalled only by a piece of news which fortune had yet in store for him, and which we shall communicate in the second chapter of the ensuing book.", "이 생각이 그에게 안긴 고통에 견줄 수 있는 것은 오직, 운명이 아직 그를 위해 마련해 둔 한 가지 소식뿐이었으니, 그것은 다음 권의 두 번째 장에서 전하기로 하겠다."]], [["A farewel to the reader.", "엿새 남짓의 이야기를 담다."]], [["We are now, reader, arrived at the last stage of our long journey. As we have, therefore, travelled together through so many pages, let us behave to one another like fellow-travellers in a stage coach, who have passed several days in the company of each other; and who, notwithstanding any bickerings or little animosities which may have occurred on the road, generally make all up at last, and mount, for the last time, into their vehicle with chearfulness and good humour; since after this one stage, it may possibly happen to us, as it commonly happens to them, never to meet more.", "독자에게 고하는 작별. 독자여, 우리는 이제 이 긴 여정의 마지막 구간에 다다랐다. 이렇게 그토록 많은 페이지를 함께 지나왔으니, 며칠을 한 마차 안에서 같이 보낸 역마차 길동무들처럼 서로를 대해 보자. 그들은 길 위에서 어떤 다툼이나 사소한 앙금이 있었더라도 결국에는 대개 다 풀어 버리고, 마지막으로 다시 한 번 명랑하고 흐뭇한 기분으로 마차에 오른다. 이 한 구간만 지나고 나면, 그들에게 흔히 그러하듯 우리에게도 다시는 만나지 못할 일이 일어날지 모르기 때문이다."]], [["As I have here taken up this simile, give me leave to carry it a little farther.", "이렇게 비유를 끄집어낸 김에, 그것을 조금만 더 끌고 가도록 허락해 다오."], ["I intend, then, in this last book, to imitate the good company I have mentioned in their last journey.", "그러니까 나는 이 마지막 권에서, 앞서 말한 그 좋은 길동무들이 마지막 여정에서 보이는 모습을 본떠 보려 한다."], ["Now, it is well known that all jokes and raillery are at this time laid aside; whatever characters any of the passengers have for the jest-sake personated on the road are now thrown off, and the conversation is usually plain and serious.", "자, 잘 알려져 있듯이 이때쯤이면 온갖 농담과 익살은 모두 거두어진다. 길 위에서 승객들 가운데 누군가가 우스개 삼아 꾸며 보이던 역할은 이제 다 벗어 던지고, 대화는 으레 담백하고 진지해진다."]], [["In the same manner, if I have now and then, in the course of this work, indulged any pleasantry for thy entertainment, I shall here lay it down.", "같은 방식으로, 만일 내가 이 작품을 써 오는 동안 때때로 너의 즐거움을 위해 어떤 익살을 부렸다면, 여기서는 그것을 내려놓겠다."], ["The variety of matter, indeed, which I shall be obliged to cram into this book, will afford no room for any of those ludicrous observations which I have elsewhere made, and which may sometimes, perhaps, have prevented thee from taking a nap when it was beginning to steal upon thee.", "실로 이 권에 욱여넣어야 할 사건들이 워낙 다양하다 보니, 내가 다른 데서 늘어놓았던 그런 우스꽝스러운 논평들을 끼워 넣을 자리가 없을 것이다. 어쩌면 그런 논평들이, 너에게 슬그머니 졸음이 밀려들기 시작할 때 한숨 자려는 너를 막아 주기도 했을 텐데 말이다."], ["In this last book thou wilt find nothing (or at most very little) of that nature.", "이 마지막 권에서 너는 그런 종류의 것을 전혀(많아야 아주 조금밖에) 찾지 못할 것이다."], ["All will be plain narrative only; and, indeed, when thou hast perused the many great events which this book will produce, thou wilt think the number of pages contained in it scarce sufficient to tell the story.", "모든 것이 오직 담백한 서술뿐이리라. 그리고 실로, 이 권이 펼쳐 놓을 그 숱한 큰 사건들을 다 읽고 나면, 너는 그 이야기를 다 들려주기에 여기 담긴 페이지 수가 도무지 모자라다고 여기게 될 것이다."]], [["And now, my friend, I take this opportunity (as I shall have no other) of heartily wishing thee well.", "그리고 이제, 나의 벗이여, 나는 이 기회를 빌려(달리 기회가 없을 터이니) 진심으로 너의 안녕을 빈다. 만일 내가 너에게 즐거운 길동무였다면, 분명히 말하건대 그것이야말로 내가 바라던 바였다."], ["If I have been an entertaining companion to thee, I promise thee it is what I have desired.", "만일 무엇으로든 내가 너의 기분을 상하게 했다면, 그것은 정말이지 아무 의도도 없이 그리된 것이다."], ["If in anything I have offended, it was really without any intention.", "여기 쓰인 어떤 것들이 어쩌면 너나 너의 벗들을 찔렀을지도 모르겠다."], ["Some things, perhaps, here said, may have hit thee or thy friends; but I do most solemnly declare they were not pointed at thee or them.", "그러나 나는 더없이 엄숙하게 단언하건대, 그것들은 너나 그들을 겨냥한 것이 아니었다. 나에 관한 다른 여러 이야기 가운데, 네가 아주 입이 험한 자와 함께 여행하게 될 거라는 말을 들었을 법도 하다."], ["I question not but thou hast been told, among other stories of me, that thou wast to travel with a very scurrilous fellow; but whoever told thee so did me an injury.", "그러나 너에게 그리 말한 자가 누구든, 그는 나에게 해를 끼친 것이다. 나만큼 막말을 혐오하고 경멸하는 사람도 없으며, 그럴 만한 이유를 나보다 더 많이 가진 사람도 없다."], ["No man detests and despises scurrility more than myself; nor hath any man more reason; for none hath ever been treated with more; and what is a very severe fate, I have had some of the abusive writings of those very men fathered upon me, who, in other of their works, have abused me themselves with the utmost virulence.", "나만큼 그런 막말에 시달려 본 사람이 없기 때문이다. 게다가 참으로 가혹한 운명이라 할 것은, 바로 그자들이 다른 작품들에서 더없이 모질게 나를 욕해 놓고도, 그들이 쓴 욕설 가득한 글 가운데 일부가 도리어 내 것인 양 내게 떠넘겨졌다는 사실이다."]], [["All these works, however, I am well convinced, will be dead long before this page shall offer itself to thy perusal; for however short the period may be of my own performances, they will most probably outlive their own infirm author, and the weakly productions of his abusive contemporaries.", "그러나 이 모든 글들은, 이 페이지가 너의 눈앞에 펼쳐지기 훨씬 전에 이미 죽어 사라질 것이라고 나는 굳게 확신한다. 내 자신의 작품들이 누릴 수명이 아무리 짧다 한들, 그 글들은 십중팔구 그 병약한 저자보다도, 또 나를 헐뜯던 동시대인들의 그 가냘픈 작품들보다도 더 오래 살아남을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["Containing a very tragical incident.", "매우 비극적인 사건을 담은 장."]], [["While Jones was employed in those unpleasant meditations, with which we left him tormenting himself, Partridge came stumbling into the room with his face paler than ashes, his eyes fixed in his head, his hair standing an end, and every limb trembling.", "존스가 앞서 우리가 그를 두고 떠나올 때처럼 그 괴로운 상념에 빠져 스스로를 들볶고 있을 때, 파트리지가 비틀거리며 방으로 들어왔다. 얼굴은 잿빛보다 더 창백했고, 두 눈은 머리에 못 박힌 듯 굳어 있었으며, 머리카락은 곤두서고, 사지가 하나하나 떨리고 있었다."], ["In short, he looked as he would have done had he seen a spectre, or had he, indeed, been a spectre himself.", "한마디로, 유령을 본 사람, 아니 그 자신이 유령이라도 된 듯한 몰골이었다."]], [["Jones, who was little subject to fear, could not avoid being somewhat shocked at this sudden appearance.", "좀처럼 겁을 먹는 법이 없는 존스도 이 갑작스러운 출현 앞에서는 다소 충격을 받지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He did, indeed, himself change colour, and his voice a little faultered while he asked him, What was the matter?", "실로 그 자신도 낯빛이 변했고, 무슨 일이냐고 물을 때 목소리가 조금 떨렸다."]], [["“I hope, sir,”", "\"나리,\""], ["said Partridge, “you will not be angry with me.", "파트리지가 말했다. \"부디 저에게 노하지 말아 주십시오."], ["Indeed I did not listen, but I was obliged to stay in the outward room.", "정말로 엿들은 것은 아닙니다만, 바깥방에 있을 수밖에 없었습니다."], ["I am sure I wish I had been a hundred miles off, rather than have heard what I have heard.”", "제가 들은 그것을 듣느니, 차라리 백 마일은 떨어진 곳에 있었더라면 좋았을 것을, 정말이지 그렇게 바랐습니다.\""], ["“Why, what is the matter?”", "\"아니, 대체 무슨 일이냐?\""], ["said Jones.", "존스가 말했다."], ["“The matter, sir?", "\"무슨 일이냐고요, 나리?"], ["O good Heaven!”", "오, 자비로운 하늘이시여!\""], ["answered Partridge, “was that woman who is just gone out the woman who was with you at Upton?”", "파트리지가 대답했다. \"방금 나간 저 여자가 어퍼튼에서 나리와 함께 있던 그 여자입니까?\""], ["“She was, Partridge,”", "\"그렇다, 파트리지.\""], ["cried Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["“And did you really, sir, go to bed with that woman?”", "\"그러면 나리, 정말로 저 여자와 동침을 하셨습니까?\""], ["said he, trembling.--“I am afraid what past between us is no secret,”", "그가 떨면서 물었다. \"우리 사이에 있었던 일은 이제 비밀도 아닌 모양이로구나.\""], ["said Jones.--“Nay, but pray, sir, for Heaven's sake, sir, answer me,”", "존스가 말했다. \"아니, 그래도 제발, 나리, 하늘에 맹세코, 나리, 대답해 주십시오.\""], ["cries Partridge.", "파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["“You know I did,”", "\"너도 알지 않느냐, 그렇다.\""], ["cries Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["“Why then, the Lord have mercy upon your soul, and forgive you,”", "\"그렇다면, 주님께서 나리의 영혼에 자비를 베푸시고 용서해 주시기를.\" 파트리지가 외쳤다."], ["cries Partridge; “but as sure as I stand here alive, you have been a-bed with your own mother.”", "\"허나 제가 여기 이렇게 살아 서 있는 것만큼이나 분명하게, 나리께서는 친어머니와 잠자리를 같이하신 겁니다.\""]], [["Upon these words Jones became in a moment a greater picture of horror than Partridge himself.", "이 말을 듣자 존스는 순식간에 파트리지보다도 더한 공포의 화신이 되고 말았다."], ["He was, indeed, for some time struck dumb with amazement, and both stood staring wildly at each other.", "실로 그는 한동안 경악에 사로잡혀 입을 떼지 못했고, 두 사람은 서로를 미친 듯이 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["At last his words found way, and in an interrupted voice he said, “How!", "마침내 그의 입에서 말이 새어 나왔고, 끊어지는 목소리로 그가 말했다. \"뭐라고!"], ["how!", "뭐라고!"], ["what's this you tell me?”", "그게 대체 무슨 소리냐?\""], ["“Nay, sir,”", "\"아니, 나리,\""], ["cries Partridge, “I have not breath enough left to tell you now, but what I have said is most certainly true.--That woman who now went out is your own mother.", "파트리지가 외쳤다. \"지금은 다 말씀드릴 숨조차 남아 있지 않습니다만, 제가 한 말은 틀림없는 사실입니다."], ["How unlucky was it for you, sir, that I did not happen to see her at that time, to have prevented it!", "방금 나간 저 여자가 나리의 친어머니입니다. 나리, 그때 제가 마침 그 여자를 보고 막아 드리지 못한 것이 얼마나 불운한 일이었는지요!"], ["Sure the devil himself must have contrived to bring about this wickedness.”", "분명 악마가 직접 이 사악한 일을 꾸며 낸 게 틀림없습니다.\""]], [["“Sure,”", "\"분명,\""], ["cries Jones, “Fortune will never have done with me till she hath driven me to distraction.", "존스가 외쳤다. \"운명은 나를 미치게 몰아넣을 때까지 결코 나를 놓아주지 않을 것이다."], ["But why do I blame Fortune?", "하지만 어찌하여 내가 운명을 탓하랴?"], ["I am myself the cause of all my misery.", "이 모든 비참함의 원인은 바로 나 자신이다."], ["All the dreadful mischiefs which have befallen me are the consequences only of my own folly and vice.", "내게 닥친 그 끔찍한 재앙들은 모두 나 자신의 어리석음과 악덕이 빚은 결과일 뿐이다. 파트리지, 네가 내게 한 말은 거의 나에게서 제정신을 앗아 가 버렸다!"], ["What thou hast told me, Partridge, hath almost deprived me of my senses!", "그렇다면 워터스 부인이 정말로... 하지만 내가 어찌 묻겠는가?"], ["And was Mrs Waters, then--but why do I ask?", "너는 분명 그 여자를 알고 있을 테니."], ["for thou must certainly know her--If thou hast any affection for me, nay, if thou hast any pity, let me beseech thee to fetch this miserable woman back again to me.", "만일 네게 나를 위하는 마음이 조금이라도 있다면, 아니, 네게 조금이라도 연민이 있다면, 부디 간청하노니 그 불쌍한 여자를 다시 내게 데려와 다오. 오, 자비로운 하늘이시여!"], ["O good Heavens!", "근친상간이라니..."], ["incest----with a mother!", "어머니와! 나는 대체 무엇을 위해 남겨진 존재란 말인가!\""], ["To what am I reserved!”", "그러고 나서 그는 더없이 격렬하고 광적인 슬픔과 절망의 고통 속으로 빠져들었고, 그 모습에 파트리지는 그를 결코 떠나지 않겠다고 다짐했다."], ["He then fell into the most violent and frantic agonies of grief and despair, in which Partridge declared he would not leave him; but at last, having vented the first torrent of passion, he came a little to himself; and then, having acquainted Partridge that he would find this wretched woman in the same house where the wounded gentleman was lodged, he despatched him in quest of her.", "그러나 마침내 격정의 첫 격류를 다 쏟아 내고 나자 그는 조금씩 정신을 차렸다. 그러고는 파트리지에게, 상처 입은 신사가 묵고 있는 바로 그 집에서 이 가엾은 여자를 찾을 수 있을 것이라 일러 주고는 그녀를 찾으러 보냈다."]], [["If the reader will please to refresh his memory, by turning to the scene at Upton, in the ninth book, he will be apt to admire the many strange accidents which unfortunately prevented any interview between Partridge and Mrs Waters, when she spent a whole day there with Mr Jones.", "독자께서 부디 제9권의 어퍼튼 장면으로 되돌아가 기억을 새롭게 해 주신다면, 워터스 부인이 존스 씨와 함께 그곳에서 하루를 온전히 보냈음에도 파트리지와 그녀의 만남을 공교롭게 가로막은 그 숱한 기묘한 우연들에 감탄하게 될 것이다. 이런 종류의 사례는 우리 삶에서도 흔히 볼 수 있다."], ["Instances of this kind we may frequently observe in life, where the greatest events are produced by a nice train of little circumstances; and more than one example of this may be discovered by the accurate eye, in this our history.", "가장 큰 사건들이 자잘한 정황의 절묘한 연쇄로 빚어지는 경우 말이다. 그리고 이런 예는 우리의 이 이야기 속에서도 눈 밝은 이라면 한둘 넘게 찾아낼 수 있을 것이다."]], [["After a fruitless search of two or three hours, Partridge returned back to his master, without having seen Mrs Waters.", "두세 시간을 헛되이 헤맨 끝에, 파트리지는 워터스 부인을 만나지 못한 채 주인에게로 돌아왔다."], ["Jones, who was in a state of desperation at his delay, was almost raving mad when he brought him his account.", "그의 지체에 절망 상태에 빠져 있던 존스는, 그가 와서 사정을 전하자 거의 미쳐 날뛸 지경이었다."], ["He was not long, however, in this condition before he received the following letter:", "그러나 그 상태가 오래가기 전에 그는 다음과 같은 편지를 받았다. \"나리께,"]], [["“Since I left you I have seen a gentleman, from whom I have learned something concerning you which greatly surprizes and affects me; but as I have not at present leisure to communicate a matter of such high importance, you must suspend your curiosity till our next meeting, which shall be the first moment I am able to see you. O, Mr Jones, little did I think, when I past that happy day at Upton, the reflection upon which is like to embitter all my future life, who it was to whom I owed such perfect happiness. Believe me to be ever sincerely your unfortunate", "당신과 헤어진 뒤 저는 한 신사를 만났고, 그에게서 당신에 관한 무언가를 알게 되었습니다. 그것은 저를 몹시 놀라게 하고 또 깊이 동요케 합니다. 그러나 지금은 그토록 중대한 일을 전할 겨를이 없으니, 당신의 궁금증은 우리가 다음에 만날 때까지 미루어 두셔야겠습니다. 그 만남은 제가 당신을 뵐 수 있게 되는 첫 순간이 될 것입니다. 오, 존스 씨, 제가 어퍼튼에서 그 행복한 하루를 보낼 때만 해도, 그토록 완전한 행복을 누가 제게 베풀어 준 것인지 저는 조금도 알지 못했습니다. 그날을 떠올리는 것이 앞으로의 제 모든 삶을 쓰디쓰게 만들 것만 같습니다. 저를 언제까지나 진심으로 당신의 불행한 사람으로 믿어 주십시오."]], [["“P.S. I would have you comfort yourself as much as possible, for Mr Fitzpatrick is in no manner of danger; so that whatever other grievous crimes you may have to repent of, the guilt of blood is not among the number.”", "J. 워터스 올림.\" \"추신. 부디 되도록 마음을 위로하시기 바랍니다. 피츠패트릭 씨는 조금도 위험하지 않으니까요. 그러니 당신이 뉘우쳐야 할 다른 어떤 무거운 죄가 있다 하더라도, 피의 죄만큼은 그 가운데 들어 있지 않습니다.\""]], [["Jones having read the letter, let it drop (for he was unable to hold it, and indeed had scarce the use of any one of his faculties).", "편지를 다 읽은 존스는 그것을 떨어뜨리고 말았다(편지를 쥐고 있을 수도 없었고, 실로 어느 한 가지 기능도 제대로 쓸 수 없는 지경이었으니까)."], ["Partridge took it up, and having received consent by silence, read it likewise; nor had it upon him a less sensible effect.", "파트리지가 그것을 집어 들었고, 침묵으로 허락을 얻은 뒤 자신도 그것을 읽었다. 그 편지가 그에게 미친 충격 또한 결코 덜하지 않았다."], ["The pencil, and not the pen, should describe the horrors which appeared in both their countenances.", "두 사람의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 공포는 펜이 아니라 화가의 붓으로나 그려 낼 수 있을 것이었다."], ["While they both remained speechless the turnkey entered the room, and, without taking any notice of what sufficiently discovered itself in the faces of them both, acquainted Jones that a man without desired to speak with him.", "두 사람이 모두 말을 잃고 있을 때, 간수가 방으로 들어왔다. 그리고 두 사람의 얼굴에 충분히 드러나 있는 것을 조금도 알아채지 못한 채, 밖에 한 남자가 와서 그와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 존스에게 전했다."], ["This person was presently introduced, and was no other than Black George.", "그 사람이 곧 안으로 안내되어 들어왔는데, 다름 아닌 검둥이 조지였다."]], [["As sights of horror were not so usual to George as they were to the turnkey, he instantly saw the great disorder which appeared in the face of Jones.", "* 검둥이 조지: 'Black George'의 옮김으로, 사냥터지기 조지 시그림의 별명. 공포의 광경이 간수에게는 익숙해도 조지에게는 그만큼 익숙한 것이 아니었던 터라, 그는 존스의 얼굴에 떠오른 큰 동요를 단번에 알아보았다."], ["This he imputed to the accident that had happened, which was reported in the very worst light in Mr Western's family; he concluded, therefore, that the gentleman was dead, and that Mr Jones was in a fair way of coming to a shameful end.", "그는 그것을, 웨스턴 씨 댁에서 더없이 나쁜 쪽으로 전해진 그 사건 탓으로 돌렸다. 그래서 그는 그 신사가 죽었으며, 존스 씨가 수치스러운 최후를 맞을 처지에 놓였다고 단정했다."], ["A thought which gave him much uneasiness; for George was of a compassionate disposition, and notwithstanding a small breach of friendship which he had been over-tempted to commit, was, in the main, not insensible of the obligations he had formerly received from Mr Jones.", "이 생각은 그를 몹시 불편하게 했다. 조지는 본디 인정 많은 성품이었던 데다, 비록 지나친 유혹에 넘어가 우정에 작은 금을 낸 일이 있긴 했어도, 근본은 예전에 존스 씨에게서 받은 은혜를 잊지 못하는 사람이었기 때문이다."]], [["The poor fellow, therefore, scarce refrained from a tear at the present sight.", "그래서 그 가엾은 사내는 지금의 광경에 눈물을 가까스로 참았다."], ["He told Jones he was heartily sorry for his misfortunes, and begged him to consider if he could be of any manner of service.", "그는 존스에게 그의 불운이 진심으로 안타깝다고 말하면서, 자신이 무슨 일로든 도움이 될 수 있을지 헤아려 봐 달라고 청했다. \"어쩌면 나리,\""], ["“Perhaps, sir,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “you may want a little matter of money upon this occasion; if you do, sir, what little I have is heartily at your service.”", "\"이런 마당에 약간의 돈이 필요하실지도 모르겠습니다. 그러시다면, 나리, 제게 있는 얼마 안 되는 것이나마 진심으로 나리께 쓰십시오.\""]], [["Jones shook him very heartily by the hand, and gave him many thanks for the kind offer he had made; but answered, “He had not the least want of that kind.”", "존스는 그의 손을 더없이 정겹게 잡고는, 그가 베풀어 준 친절한 제안에 거듭 고마움을 표했다. 그러나 그런 종류의 도움은 조금도 필요하지 않다고 대답했다."], ["Upon which George began to press his services more eagerly than before.", "그러자 조지는 전보다 더 간곡하게 자기 도움을 받아 달라고 졸랐다."], ["Jones again thanked him, with assurances that he wanted nothing which was in the power of any man living to give.", "존스는 다시 고마움을 표하면서, 살아 있는 그 누구의 힘으로도 줄 수 있는 것이라면 아무것도 필요하지 않다고 거듭 확언했다."], ["“Come, come, my good master,”", "\"자, 자, 우리 좋으신 주인 나리,\""], ["answered George, “do not take the matter so much to heart.", "조지가 대답했다. \"그 일을 너무 가슴에 담아 두지 마십시오."], ["Things may end better than you imagine; to be sure you an't the first gentleman who hath killed a man, and yet come off.”", "일이 생각하시는 것보다 잘 풀릴 수도 있습니다. 정말이지, 사람을 죽이고도 무사히 빠져나온 신사가 나리가 처음은 아니니까요.\""], ["“You are wide of the matter, George,”", "\"조지, 자네는 핵심을 한참 비껴갔네.\""], ["said Partridge, “the gentleman is not dead, nor like to die.", "파트리지가 말했다. \"그 신사는 죽지도 않았고, 죽을 것 같지도 않아."], ["Don't disturb my master, at present, for he is troubled about a matter in which it is not in your power to do him any good.”", "지금은 우리 주인 나리를 어지럽히지 말게. 나리는 자네 힘으로는 조금도 도울 수 없는 일로 괴로워하고 계시니까.\""], ["“You don't know what I may be able to do, Mr Partridge,”", "\"파트리지 씨, 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지는 당신이 모르는 일이지요.\""], ["answered George; “if his concern is about my young lady, I have some news to tell my master.”", "조지가 대답했다. \"나리의 걱정이 우리 아가씨에 관한 것이라면, 제게 주인 나리께 전할 소식이 좀 있습니다.\""], ["“What do you say, Mr George?”", "\"무슨 말인가, 조지?\""], ["cried Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["“Hath anything lately happened in which my Sophia is concerned?", "\"최근에 나의 소피아와 관련된 무슨 일이라도 있었단 말인가? 나의 소피아라니!"], ["My Sophia! how dares such a wretch as I mention her so profanely.”", "나 같은 못난 자가 어찌 감히 그분의 이름을 이리도 함부로 입에 올리는가.\""], ["“I hope she will be yours yet,”", "\"그분은 아직 나리의 사람이 될 것이라 저는 믿습니다.\""], ["answered George.", "조지가 대답했다."], ["“Why yes, sir, I have something to tell you about her.", "\"그렇고말고요, 나리, 그분에 관해 말씀드릴 게 좀 있습니다."], ["Madam Western hath just brought Madam Sophia home, and there hath been a terrible to do.", "웨스턴 마님이 방금 소피아 아가씨를 집으로 데려오셨는데, 한바탕 끔찍한 소동이 벌어졌습니다. 그 자초지종을 제대로 알아내지는 못했습니다만, 우리 주인 나리는 굉장히 큰 노발대발이셨고, 웨스턴 마님도 그러셨습니다."], ["I could not possibly learn the very right of it; but my master he hath been in a vast big passion, and so was Madam Western, and I heard her say, as she went out of doors into her chair, that she would never set her foot in master's house again.", "마님이 문밖으로 나가 가마에 오르시면서 다시는 주인 나리 댁에 발을 들이지 않겠다고 하시는 걸 제가 들었습니다. 무슨 영문인지는 저로서는 모르겠습니다만, 제가 나올 때는 모든 것이 아주 잠잠했습니다."], ["I don't know what's the matter, not I, but everything was very quiet when I came out; but Robin, who waited at supper, said he had never seen the squire for a long while in such good humour with young madam; that he kissed her several times, and swore she should be her own mistress, and he never would think of confining her any more.", "그런데 저녁 시중을 들던 로빈이 말하기를, 영주님이 그토록 오랫동안 젊은 아가씨에게 그렇게 기분 좋아하시는 모습은 처음 본다고 했습니다. 아가씨에게 여러 번 입을 맞추셨고, 아가씨더러 자기 자신의 주인이 되라 맹세하셨으며, 다시는 아가씨를 가두어 둘 생각을 하지 않겠다고 하셨답니다."], ["I thought this news would please you, and so I slipped out, though it was so late, to inform you of it.”", "이 소식을 들으시면 나리께서 기뻐하시리라 생각해서, 이렇게 늦은 시각인데도 몰래 빠져나와 나리께 알려 드리러 온 것입니다.\""], ["Mr Jones assured George that it did greatly please him; for though he should never more presume to lift his eyes toward that incomparable creature, nothing could so much relieve his misery as the satisfaction he should always have in hearing of her welfare.", "존스는 조지에게, 그 소식이 자신을 참으로 기쁘게 한다고 확언했다. 비록 그 비할 데 없는 사람을 향해 다시는 감히 눈을 들지 못할 처지였지만, 그녀가 평안하다는 소식을 들으며 늘 누리게 될 그 만족만큼 그의 비참함을 덜어 줄 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없었기 때문이다."]], [["The rest of the conversation which passed at the visit is not important enough to be here related.", "이 방문에서 오간 나머지 대화는 여기에 옮길 만큼 중요하지 않다."], ["The reader will, therefore, forgive us this abrupt breaking off, and be pleased to hear how this great good-will of the squire towards his daughter was brought about.", "따라서 독자께서는 우리가 이렇게 불쑥 이야기를 끊는 것을 너그러이 보아 주시고, 부디 영주가 딸에게 품은 이 큰 호의가 어떻게 빚어지게 되었는지 들어 주시기 바란다."]], [["Mrs Western, on her first arrival at her brother's lodging, began to set forth the great honours and advantages which would accrue to the family by the match with Lord Fellamar, which her niece had absolutely refused; in which refusal, when the squire took the part of his daughter, she fell immediately into the most violent passion, and so irritated and provoked the squire, that neither his patience nor his prudence could bear it any longer; upon which there ensued between them both so warm a bout at altercation, that perhaps the regions of Billingsgate never equalled it. In the heat of this scolding Mrs Western departed, and had consequently no leisure to acquaint her brother with the letter which Sophia received, which might have possibly produced ill effects; but, to say truth, I believe it never once occurred to her memory at this time.", "웨스턴 부인은 오라비의 처소에 막 도착하자마자, 펠라마 경과의 혼사가 가문에 가져다줄 크나큰 영예와 이익을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그것은 조카딸이 단호히 거절한 혼사였다. 그 거절을 두고 영주가 딸의 편을 들자, 부인은 즉시 더없이 격렬한 분노에 빠져들었고, 영주를 어찌나 약 올리고 들쑤셨는지, 영주의 인내심도 분별력도 더는 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 없었다. 그리하여 두 사람 사이에 어찌나 뜨거운 말다툼 한판이 벌어졌던지, 어쩌면 빌링스게이트 어시장의 욕설판도 그에 견줄 수 없었을 것이다. 이렇게 욕설이 한창 달아오른 와중에 웨스턴 부인이 떠나 버렸고, 그 바람에 그녀는 소피아가 받은 편지에 대해 오라비에게 알릴 겨를이 없었다. 그 편지를 알렸더라면 어쩌면 나쁜 결과를 낳았을지도 모른다. 그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 나는 그 일이 이때만큼은 단 한 번도 그녀의 기억에 떠오르지 않았으리라고 믿는다. * 빌링스게이트: 런던의 생선 시장으로, 상스러운 욕설로 악명이 높았다."]], [["When Mrs Western was gone, Sophia, who had been hitherto silent, as well indeed from necessity as inclination, began to return the compliment which her father had made her, in taking her part against her aunt, by taking his likewise against the lady. This was the first time of her so doing, and it was in the highest degree acceptable to the squire. Again, he remembered that Mr Allworthy had insisted on an entire relinquishment of all violent means; and, indeed, as he made no doubt but that Jones would be hanged, he did not in the least question succeeding with his daughter by fair means; he now, therefore, once more gave a loose to his natural fondness for her, which had such an effect on the dutiful, grateful, tender, and affectionate heart of Sophia, that had her honour, given to Jones, and something else, perhaps, in which he was concerned, been removed, I much doubt whether she would not have sacrificed herself to a man she did not like, to have obliged her father. She promised him she would make it the whole business of her life to oblige him, and would never marry any man against his consent; which brought the old man so near to his highest happiness, that he was resolved to take the other step, and went to bed completely drunk.", "웨스턴 부인이 떠나자, 그때까지 필요 때문이기도 하고 마음 때문이기도 하여 줄곧 입을 다물고 있던 소피아가, 아버지가 고모에 맞서 자기 편을 들어 준 그 호의에 보답하기 시작했다. 이번에는 그녀가 아버지 편을 들어 고모에게 맞선 것이다. 그녀가 그렇게 한 것은 이번이 처음이었고, 그것은 영주에게 더없이 흡족한 일이었다. 게다가 영주는, 올워디 씨가 모든 강압적인 수단을 완전히 포기하라고 강력히 요구했던 것을 떠올렸다. 그리고 실로 그는 존스가 교수형에 처해질 것을 조금도 의심하지 않았던 터라, 점잖은 방법으로 딸을 손에 넣는 데 성공하리라는 것 또한 추호도 의심하지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 이제 다시 한번 딸을 향한 타고난 애정을 마음껏 풀어놓았다. 그것이 효성스럽고 고마움을 아는, 다정하고 살가운 소피아의 마음에 어찌나 큰 영향을 미쳤던지, 만일 존스에게 바친 그녀의 정절과, 또 어쩌면 그와 관련된 다른 무언가가 제거되었더라면, 그녀가 아버지를 흡족하게 해 드리려고 마음에도 없는 남자에게 자신을 희생해 버리지 않았을지 나로서는 몹시 의심스럽다. 그녀는 아버지를 흡족하게 해 드리는 것을 평생의 유일한 과업으로 삼겠다고, 그의 동의 없이는 결코 어떤 남자와도 혼인하지 않겠다고 그에게 약속했다. 이 말은 노인을 더없는 행복의 문턱에까지 바짝 데려다 놓았고, 그리하여 그는 다음 단계로 나아가기로 작정하고는 완전히 곤드레만드레 취한 채 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["# Chapter iii.", "# Chapter iii."]], [["Allworthy visits old Nightingale; with a strange discovery that he made on that occasion.", "올워디가 나이팅게일 노인을 찾아가다. 그리고 그 자리에서 이루어진 기이한 발견에 관하여."]], [["The morning after these things had happened, Mr Allworthy went, according to his promise, to visit old Nightingale, with whom his authority was so great, that, after having sat with him three hours, he at last prevailed with him to consent to see his son.", "이 모든 일이 벌어진 다음 날 아침, 올워디 씨는 약속대로 나이팅게일 노인을 찾아갔다. 그에 대한 올워디 씨의 영향력은 어찌나 컸던지, 세 시간을 함께 앉아 있은 끝에 마침내 그가 아들을 만나 보겠다고 동의하도록 설득해 냈다."]], [["Here an accident happened of a very extraordinary kind; one indeed of those strange chances whence very good and grave men have concluded that Providence often interposes in the discovery of the most secret villany, in order to caution men from quitting the paths of honesty, however warily they tread in those of vice.", "여기서 참으로 기이한 종류의 사건이 하나 일어났다. 실로, 매우 선하고 진중한 사람들이, 신의 섭리가 가장 은밀한 악행을 들추어내는 데 종종 끼어든다고 결론짓게 한 그 이상한 우연들 가운데 하나였다. 사람들이 아무리 조심스레 악덕의 길을 디딘다 해도, 정직의 길에서 벗어나지 말라고 경계시키기 위함이라는 것이다."]], [["Mr Allworthy, at his entrance into Mr Nightingale's, saw Black George; he took no notice of him, nor did Black George imagine he had perceived him.", "올워디 씨는 나이팅게일 씨의 집에 들어서면서 블랙 조지를 보았다. 그는 그를 아는 체하지 않았고, 블랙 조지 역시 그가 자신을 알아보았으리라고는 생각지 못했다."]], [["However, when their conversation on the principal point was over, Allworthy asked Nightingale, Whether he knew one George Seagrim, and upon what business he came to his house?", "그러나 본론에 관한 대화가 끝나자, 올워디는 나이팅게일에게 조지 시그림이라는 자를 아는지, 또 무슨 일로 그가 집을 찾아왔는지 물었다. \"예.\""], ["“Yes,”", "나이팅게일이 대답했다."], ["answered Nightingale, “I know him very well, and a most extraordinary fellow he is, who, in these days, hath been able to hoard up £500 from renting a very small estate of £30 a year.”", "\"그자를 아주 잘 압니다. 정말이지 보통내기가 아닌 친구지요."], ["“And is this the story which he hath told you?”", "요즘 세상에, 일 년에 30파운드밖에 안 되는 아주 작은 땅을 부쳐서 500파운드나 모아 두었다니 말입니다.\" \"그게 그자가 당신에게 들려준 이야기입니까?\""], ["cries Allworthy.", "올워디가 외쳤다."], ["“Nay, it is true, I promise you,”", "\"아니, 사실입니다. 장담하지요.\""], ["said Nightingale, “for I have the money now in my own hands, in five bank-bills, which I am to lay out either in a mortgage, or in some purchase in the north of England.”", "나이팅게일이 말했다. \"지금 그 돈이 내 손에 있으니까요."], ["The bank-bills were no sooner produced at Allworthy's desire than he blessed himself at the strangeness of the discovery.", "은행 어음 다섯 장으로 말입니다. 그것을 저당에 넣든지, 아니면 잉글랜드 북부에 무언가를 사들이는 데 쓰려고 합니다.\""], ["He presently told Nightingale that these bank-bills were formerly his, and then acquainted him with the whole affair.", "올워디의 청에 따라 그 은행 어음이 꺼내지자마자, 그는 그 발견의 기이함에 절로 성호를 그었다. 그는 곧 나이팅게일에게 이 은행 어음이 본래 자기 것이었다고 말하고는, 일의 전말을 모두 알려 주었다."], ["As there are no men who complain more of the frauds of business than highwaymen, gamesters, and other thieves of that kind, so there are none who so bitterly exclaim against the frauds of gamesters, &c., as usurers, brokers, and other thieves of this kind; whether it be that the one way of cheating is a discountenance or reflection upon the other, or that money, which is the common mistress of all cheats, makes them regard each other in the light of rivals; but Nightingale no sooner heard the story than he exclaimed against the fellow in terms much severer than the justice and honesty of Allworthy had bestowed on him.", "노상강도나 노름꾼, 그 밖의 그런 부류의 도둑들만큼 장사의 사기를 두고 불평하는 자들이 없듯이, 고리대금업자나 거간꾼, 그 밖의 이런 부류의 도둑들만큼 노름꾼 따위의 사기를 두고 모질게 떠들어 대는 자들도 없다. 한쪽의 속임수가 다른 쪽에게 불명예이거나 자기 모습을 비추는 거울이 되기 때문인지, 아니면 모든 사기꾼의 공통된 정부(情婦)인 돈이 그들로 하여금 서로를 경쟁자로 여기게 만들기 때문인지는 모르겠으나, 나이팅게일은 이 이야기를 듣자마자, 올워디의 정의로움과 정직함이 그자에게 베푼 것보다 훨씬 더 가혹한 말로 그를 욕했다."]], [["Allworthy desired Nightingale to retain both the money and the secret till he should hear farther from him; and, if he should in the meantime see the fellow, that he would not take the least notice to him of the discovery which he had made.", "올워디는 나이팅게일에게, 다시 연락이 갈 때까지 그 돈도 비밀도 그대로 간직해 달라고 청했다. 또한 그사이에 그자를 보더라도, 자신이 알아낸 사실을 조금도 내색하지 말아 달라고 부탁했다."], ["He then returned to his lodgings, where he found Mrs Miller in a very dejected condition, on account of the information she had received from her son-in-law.", "그러고 나서 그는 자신의 숙소로 돌아왔는데, 그곳에서 밀러 부인이 사위에게서 들은 소식 때문에 몹시 풀이 죽어 있는 것을 보았다. 올워디 씨는 아주 밝은 얼굴로, 전해 줄 좋은 소식이 많다고 말했다."], ["Mr Allworthy, with great chearfulness, told her that he had much good news to communicate; and, with little further preface, acquainted her that he had brought Mr Nightingale to consent to see his son, and did not in the least doubt to effect a perfect reconciliation between them; though he found the father more sowered by another accident of the same kind which had happened in his family.", "그리고 별다른 서두 없이, 나이팅게일 씨가 아들을 만나 보기로 동의했으며 두 사람을 완전히 화해시킬 수 있으리라는 데 조금도 의심하지 않는다고 알렸다. 다만 그 집안에서 같은 종류의 또 다른 사건이 일어나는 바람에 아버지의 마음이 한층 더 틀어져 있더라는 말도 덧붙였다."], ["He then mentioned the running away of the uncle's daughter, which he had been told by the old gentleman, and which Mrs Miller and her son-in-law did not yet know.", "그러고는 삼촌의 딸이 달아난 일을 언급했는데, 이는 그 노신사에게서 들은 것으로, 밀러 부인과 그 사위는 아직 알지 못하는 일이었다."]], [["The reader may suppose Mrs Miller received this account with great thankfulness, and no less pleasure; but so uncommon was her friendship to Jones, that I am not certain whether the uneasiness she suffered for his sake did not overbalance her satisfaction at hearing a piece of news tending so much to the happiness of her own family; nor whether even this very news, as it reminded her of the obligations she had to Jones, did not hurt as well as please her; when her grateful heart said to her, “While my own family is happy, how miserable is the poor creature to whose generosity we owe the beginning of all this happiness!”", "독자는 밀러 부인이 이 소식을 큰 감사와, 그에 못지않은 기쁨으로 받아들였으리라 짐작할 것이다. 그러나 존스를 향한 그녀의 우정이 어찌나 남다른지, 그를 위해 마음 아파하는 괴로움이 제 가족의 행복에 그토록 보탬이 되는 소식을 들은 기쁨을 능가하지 않았는지 나로서는 장담할 수 없다. 또한 이 소식이 그녀로 하여금 존스에게 진 빚을 떠올리게 한 만큼, 바로 이 소식조차 그녀를 기쁘게 한 동시에 아프게 하지 않았는지도 알 수 없다. 그녀의 고마운 마음이 그녀에게 이렇게 속삭였기 때문이다. \"내 가족은 행복한데, 우리가 이 모든 행복의 첫걸음을 빚진 그 너그러운 가엾은 사람은 얼마나 비참한가!\""]], [["Allworthy, having left her a little while to chew the cud (if I may use that expression) on these first tidings, told her he had still something more to impart, which he believed would give her pleasure.", "올워디는 그녀가 이 첫 소식을 잠시 곱씹도록(이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 내버려 둔 뒤, 아직 전할 것이 더 남았으며 그녀를 기쁘게 하리라 믿는다고 말했다. \"내 생각에는,\""], ["“I think,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I have discovered a pretty considerable treasure belonging to the young gentleman, your friend; but perhaps, indeed, his present situation may be such that it will be of no service to him.”", "\"부인의 친구인 그 젊은이에게 속한 꽤 상당한 재산을 찾아낸 것 같습니다. 하지만 어쩌면, 사실, 그의 지금 처지가 그것이 그에게 아무 쓸모도 없을 그런 형편일지도 모르겠군요.\""], ["The latter part of the speech gave Mrs Miller to understand who was meant, and she answered with a sigh, “I hope not, sir.”", "이 말의 뒷부분에서 밀러 부인은 그가 누구를 가리키는지 알아차렸고, 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다. \"그렇지 않기를 바랍니다, 어른.\""], ["“I hope so too,”", "\"나도 그러기를 바랍니다.\" 올워디가 외쳤다."], ["cries Allworthy, “with all my heart; but my nephew told me this morning he had heard a very bad account of the affair.”----“Good Heaven!", "\"진심으로요. 하지만 내 조카가 오늘 아침 그 일에 관해 아주 나쁜 이야기를 들었다고 하더군요.\""], ["sir,”", "\"맙소사!"], ["said she--“Well, I must not speak, and yet it is certainly very hard to be obliged to hold one's tongue when one hears.”--“Madam,”", "어른.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “you may say whatever you please, you know me too well to think I have a prejudice against any one; and as for that young man, I assure you I should be heartily pleased to find he could acquit himself of everything, and particularly of this sad affair.", "\"글쎄, 제가 입을 다물어야겠지만, 듣고서도 혀를 묶어 두어야 한다는 건 정말이지 견디기 힘든 일이에요.\" \"부인,\""], ["You can testify the affection I have formerly borne him.", "올워디가 말했다. \"하고 싶은 말은 무엇이든 하셔도 됩니다."], ["The world, I know, censured me for loving him so much.", "부인은 나를 잘 알지 않습니까. 내가 누구에게든 편견을 품을 사람이 아니라는 것을요."], ["I did not withdraw that affection from him without thinking I had the justest cause.", "그리고 그 젊은이로 말하자면, 그가 모든 일에서, 특히 이 슬픈 일에서 결백을 밝힐 수 있다면 진심으로 기쁘겠다고 분명히 말합니다. 부인은 내가 예전에 그를 얼마나 아꼈는지 증언해 줄 수 있을 겁니다."], ["Believe me, Mrs Miller, I should be glad to find I have been mistaken.”", "세상이 내가 그를 너무 사랑한다고 나무란 것을 압니다. 나도 가장 정당한 이유가 있다고 여기지 않고서는 그 애정을 거두지 않았습니다."], ["Mrs Miller was going eagerly to reply, when a servant acquainted her that a gentleman without desired to speak with her immediately.", "나를 믿어 주십시오, 밀러 부인, 내가 잘못 알았던 것이기를 나도 바랍니다.\" 밀러 부인이 간절히 대답하려는데, 하인이 들어와 어떤 신사가 밖에서 그녀와 당장 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다고 알렸다."], ["Allworthy then enquired for his nephew, and was told that he had been for some time in his room with the gentleman who used to come to him, and whom Mr Allworthy guessing rightly to be Mr Dowling, he desired presently to speak with him.", "그러자 올워디는 자기 조카를 찾았고, 조카가 늘 그를 찾아오던 신사와 함께 한동안 방에 있다는 말을 들었다. 올워디 씨는 그 신사를 다울링 씨라고 옳게 짐작하고는, 당장 그와 이야기하고 싶다고 청했다."]], [["When Dowling attended, Allworthy put the case of the bank-notes to him, without mentioning any name, and asked in what manner such a person might be punished.", "다울링이 들어오자, 올워디는 이름은 일절 대지 않은 채 은행권에 관한 사정을 그에게 들려주고, 그런 자가 어떤 식으로 처벌받을 수 있느냐고 물었다. 이에 다울링이 대답했다."], ["To which Dowling answered, “He thought he might be indicted on the Black Act; but said, as it was a matter of some nicety, it would be proper to go to counsel.", "\"블랙 액트로 기소될 수 있을 듯합니다만, 다소 미묘한 문제이니 법률 자문을 구하는 것이 마땅하겠습니다. 마침 웨스턴 씨의 일로 곧 자문 변호인들을 만날 참인데, 올워디 씨께서 괜찮으시다면 이 사정도 그들 앞에 내놓아 보겠습니다.\""], ["He said he was to attend counsel presently upon an affair of Mr Western's, and if Mr Allworthy pleased he would lay the case before them.”", "그렇게 하기로 합의를 보았다. 그때 밀러 부인이 문을 열고 외쳤다."], ["This was agreed to; and then Mrs Miller, opening the door, cried, “I ask pardon, I did not know you had company;”", "\"용서하세요, 손님이 계신 줄 몰랐어요.\" 그러나 올워디는 일이 끝났다며 그녀에게 들어오라고 청했다."], ["but Allworthy desired her to come in, saying he had finished his business.", "이에 다울링 씨가 물러나고, 밀러 부인은 올워디에게 베풂받은 큰 친절에 감사를 전하려는 젊은 나이팅게일 씨를 소개했다."], ["Upon which Mr Dowling withdrew, and Mrs Miller introduced Mr Nightingale the younger, to return thanks for the great kindness done him by Allworthy: but she had scarce patience to let the young gentleman finish his speech before she interrupted him, saying, “O sir!", "그러나 그녀는 그 젊은 신사가 인사를 마치도록 참아 줄 인내심도 없이 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"오, 어른!"], ["Mr Nightingale brings great news about poor Mr Jones: he hath been to see the wounded gentleman, who is out of all danger of death, and, what is more, declares he fell upon poor Mr Jones himself, and beat him.", "나이팅게일 씨가 가엾은 존스 씨에 관한 굉장한 소식을 가져왔답니다. 그 다친 신사를 만나고 왔는데, 죽을 위험은 전혀 없고, 게다가 자기가 먼저 가엾은 존스 씨에게 달려들어 때렸노라고 밝혔다는군요."], ["I am sure, sir, you would not have Mr Jones be a coward.", "분명히, 어른, 어른께서도 존스 씨가 겁쟁이이기를 바라지는 않으실 거예요."], ["If I was a man myself, I am sure, if any man was to strike me, I should draw my sword.", "제가 만일 남자였다면, 누군가 저를 친다면 분명 칼을 뽑았을 거예요."], ["Do pray, my dear, tell Mr Allworthy, tell him all yourself.”", "자, 부탁이니, 이보게, 올워디 씨께 말씀드려요. 전부 자네가 직접 말씀드려요.\""], ["Nightingale then confirmed what Mrs Miller had said; and concluded with many handsome things of Jones, who was, he said, one of the best-natured fellows in the world, and not in the least inclined to be quarrelsome.", "그러자 나이팅게일은 밀러 부인이 한 말을 확인해 주고, 존스에 관한 훌륭한 이야기를 잔뜩 늘어놓으며 끝맺었다. 그의 말에 따르면 존스는 세상에서 가장 천성이 착한 사람 가운데 하나이며, 다투기를 좋아하는 기색이라곤 조금도 없는 사람이라는 것이었다."], ["Here Nightingale was going to cease, when Mrs Miller again begged him to relate all the many dutiful expressions he had heard him make use of towards Mr Allworthy.", "나이팅게일이 여기서 말을 그치려 하는데, 밀러 부인이 다시금, 그가 올워디 씨를 향해 쓰는 것을 들었다는 그 갖은 공손한 표현들을 모두 들려 달라고 청했다."], ["“To say the utmost good of Mr Allworthy,”", "\"올워디 씨에 관해 더없이 좋은 말을 한다는 건,\" 나이팅게일이 외쳤다."], ["cries Nightingale, “is doing no more than strict justice, and can have no merit in it: but indeed, I must say, no man can be more sensible of the obligations he hath to so good a man than is poor Jones.", "\"그저 엄정한 정의를 행하는 것일 뿐, 거기에 무슨 공이 있을 수는 없지요. 하지만 정말이지, 가엾은 존스만큼 이토록 훌륭한 분께 진 은혜를 절절히 느끼는 사람도 없으리라고 말하지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["Indeed, sir, I am convinced the weight of your displeasure is the heaviest burthen he lies under.", "정말로, 어른, 저는 어른의 노여움이라는 무게야말로 그가 짊어진 가장 무거운 짐이라고 확신합니다."], ["He hath often lamented it to me, and hath as often protested in the most solemn manner he hath never been intentionally guilty of any offence towards you; nay, he hath sworn he would rather die a thousand deaths than he would have his conscience upbraid him with one disrespectful, ungrateful, or undutiful thought towards you.", "그는 자주 제게 그것을 한탄했고, 또 그만큼 자주, 더없이 엄숙한 태도로, 어른께 어떤 잘못도 고의로 저지른 적이 없노라고 단언했습니다. 아니, 그는 어른을 향해 무례하거나 배은망덕하거나 불충한 생각 하나라도 양심에 책잡히느니 차라리 천 번이라도 죽겠노라고 맹세했습니다."], ["But I ask pardon, sir, I am afraid I presume to intermeddle too far in so tender a point.”", "하지만 용서하십시오, 어른, 이토록 민감한 대목에 제가 너무 깊이 끼어드는 것은 아닌지 두렵습니다.\" \"그리스도인으로서 마땅히 할 말 그 이상은 하지 않으셨어요.\""], ["“You have spoke no more than what a Christian ought,”", "밀러 부인이 외쳤다. \"참으로, 나이팅게일 씨.\""], ["cries Mrs Miller.", "올워디가 대답했다."], ["“Indeed, Mr Nightingale,”", "\"당신의 그 너그러운 우정에 박수를 보냅니다."], ["answered Allworthy, “I applaud your generous friendship, and I wish he may merit it of you.", "부디 그가 당신에게 그런 우정을 받을 만한 사람이기를 바랍니다. 솔직히 이 불운한 젊은이에 관해 당신이 가져온 소식을 들으니 기쁩니다."], ["I confess I am glad to hear the report you bring from this unfortunate gentleman; and, if that matter should turn out to be as you represent it (and, indeed, I doubt nothing of what you say), I may, perhaps, in time, be brought to think better than lately I have of this young man; for this good gentlewoman here, nay, all who know me, can witness that I loved him as dearly as if he had been my own son.", "그리고 그 일이 당신이 그린 그대로 드러난다면(실로 당신의 말을 조금도 의심하지 않습니다만), 어쩌면 시간이 지나면 이 젊은이를 두고 요즘 품었던 것보다 더 좋게 생각하게 될지도 모르겠습니다. 여기 이 선량한 부인도, 아니, 나를 아는 모든 이가 증언해 줄 수 있듯이, 나는 그를 친아들이라도 되는 것처럼 끔찍이 사랑했으니까요."], ["Indeed, I have considered him as a child sent by fortune to my care.", "정말로 나는 그를 운명이 내 보살핌에 맡긴 아이로 여겼습니다."], ["I still remember the innocent, the helpless situation in which I found him.", "나는 그를 처음 발견했던 그 순진하고 무력한 모습을 아직도 기억합니다."], ["I feel the tender pressure of his little hands at this moment.", "그 작은 손이 다정하게 쥐던 감촉이 이 순간에도 느껴집니다."], ["He was my darling, indeed he was.”", "그는 내 귀염둥이였습니다, 정말로 그랬습니다.\""], ["At which words he ceased, and the tears stood in his eyes.", "이 말과 함께 그는 말을 멈추었고, 눈에는 눈물이 고였다."]], [["As the answer which Mrs Miller made may lead us into fresh matters, we will here stop to account for the visible alteration in Mr Allworthy's mind, and the abatement of his anger to Jones.", "밀러 부인이 한 대답이 우리를 새로운 이야기로 이끌 수 있으니, 여기서 잠시 멈추어 올워디 씨의 마음에 눈에 띄게 일어난 변화와, 존스를 향한 노여움이 누그러진 까닭을 짚어 보기로 하자. 사실 이런 종류의 급변은 역사가나 극작가들의 글에 흔히 나타나는데, 다른 이유에서가 아니라 그저 그 역사나 극이 결말로 향하기 때문이며, 작가의 권위로 정당화되곤 한다."], ["Revolutions of this kind, it is true, do frequently occur in histories and dramatic writers, for no other reason than because the history or play draws to a conclusion, and are justified by authority of authors; yet, though we insist upon as much authority as any author whatever, we shall use this power very sparingly, and never but when we are driven to it by necessity, which we do not at present foresee will happen in this work.", "그러나 우리는 어떤 작가 못지않은 권위를 주장하면서도, 이 권한만큼은 아주 아껴 쓸 것이며, 필요에 떠밀릴 때가 아니고서는 결코 휘두르지 않을 것이다. 그리고 이 작품에서 그런 일이 일어나리라고는 지금으로선 내다보지 않는다."]], [["This alteration then in the mind of Mr Allworthy was occasioned by a letter he had just received from Mr Square, and which we shall give the reader in the beginning of the next chapter.", "그러니 올워디 씨의 마음에 일어난 이 변화는, 그가 방금 스퀘어 씨에게서 받은 한 통의 편지에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 편지를 우리는 다음 장의 첫머리에서 독자에게 보여 주려 한다."]], [["Containing two letters in very different stiles.", "매우 다른 두 편지를 담은 장."]], [["“MY WORTHY FRIEND,--I informed you in my last that I was forbidden the use of the waters, as they were found by experience rather to increase than lessen the symptoms of my distemper.", "존경하는 벗에게. 지난번 편지에서 저는, 광천수가 제 병의 증세를 덜어 주기는커녕 오히려 더 키운다는 사실이 경험으로 드러나, 그 사용이 금지되었음을 알려 드렸습니다."], ["I must now acquaint you with a piece of news, which, I believe, will afflict my friends more than it hath afflicted me.", "이제 저는 한 가지 소식을 전하지 않을 수 없는데, 제 생각에 이 소식은 저를 괴롭히는 것보다 제 벗들을 더 괴롭게 할 것입니다."], ["Dr Harrington and Dr Brewster have informed me that there is no hopes of my recovery.", "해링턴 박사와 브루스터 박사가 제게, 제가 회복할 가망은 전혀 없다고 알려 주었습니다."]], [["“I have somewhere read, that the great use of philosophy is to learn to die.", "저는 어디선가, 철학의 큰 쓸모란 죽는 법을 배우는 데 있다고 읽은 적이 있습니다."], ["I will not therefore so far disgrace mine as to shew any surprize at receiving a lesson which I must be thought to have so long studied.", "그러니 제가 그토록 오래 익혀 온 것으로 여겨질 법한 가르침을 받으면서 새삼 놀라는 모습을 보여, 제 철학을 욕되게 하지는 않겠습니다."], ["Yet, to say the truth, one page of the Gospel teaches this lesson better than all the volumes of antient or modern philosophers.", "그러나 사실대로 말하자면, 복음서의 한 페이지가 고금 철학자들의 모든 저서를 합친 것보다 이 가르침을 더 잘 일러 줍니다."], ["The assurance it gives us of another life is a much stronger support to a good mind than all the consolations that are drawn from the necessity of nature, the emptiness or satiety of our enjoyments here, or any other topic of those declamations which are sometimes capable of arming our minds with a stubborn patience in bearing the thoughts of death, but never of raising them to a real contempt of it, and much less of making us think it is a real good.", "복음서가 우리에게 주는 또 다른 삶에 대한 확신은, 자연의 필연성에서, 이승에서 누리는 즐거움의 덧없음이나 물림에서, 혹은 그 밖의 온갖 변설의 화제에서 끌어낸 모든 위안보다, 선한 마음에 훨씬 더 든든한 버팀목이 됩니다. 그런 변설들은 때로 우리 마음을 무장시켜 죽음의 생각을 끈질긴 인내로 견디게 할 수는 있어도, 결코 그 마음을 들어 올려 죽음을 참으로 경멸하게 하지는 못하며, 하물며 죽음을 진정한 선으로 여기게 하는 일은 더더욱 못합니다."], ["I would not here be understood to throw the horrid censure of atheism, or even the absolute denial of immortality, on all who are called philosophers.", "여기서 저는 철학자라 불리는 모든 이에게 무신론이라는 끔찍한 비난을, 아니 불멸에 대한 절대적 부정이라는 비난을 던지려는 것으로 오해받고 싶지는 않습니다."], ["Many of that sect, as well antient as modern, have, from the light of reason, discovered some hopes of a future state; but in reality, that light was so faint and glimmering, and the hopes were so incertain and precarious, that it may be justly doubted on which side their belief turned.", "그 학파 가운데 고대와 근대를 막론하고 많은 이들이 이성의 빛으로부터 내세에 대한 어떤 희망을 발견하기도 했습니다. 그러나 실은 그 빛이 너무 희미하고 가물거렸으며 그 희망 또한 너무 불확실하고 위태로워, 그들의 믿음이 어느 쪽으로 기울었는지를 마땅히 의심할 만합니다."], ["Plato himself concludes his Phaedon with declaring that his best arguments amount only to raise a probability; and Cicero himself seems rather to profess an inclination to believe, than any actual belief in the doctrines of immortality.", "플라톤조차 자신의 《파이돈》을 마무리하면서, 자기 최선의 논증이라야 겨우 개연성을 세우는 데 그친다고 선언했습니다. 키케로 또한 불멸의 교리에 대한 실제 믿음이라기보다는 믿고자 하는 마음의 기울임을 고백하는 데 가까워 보입니다."], ["As to myself, to be very sincere with you, I never was much in earnest in this faith till I was in earnest a Christian.", "저 자신으로 말하자면, 경께 아주 솔직히 털어놓건대, 제가 참된 그리스도인이 되기 전에는 이 믿음에 진지하게 매달린 적이 한 번도 없었습니다."]], [["“You will perhaps wonder at the latter expression; but I assure you it hath not been till very lately that I could, with truth, call myself so.", "경께서는 어쩌면 이 마지막 표현을 의아하게 여기실지도 모릅니다. 그러나 분명히 말씀드리건대, 제가 진실로 저 자신을 그리스도인이라 부를 수 있게 된 것은 아주 최근에 이르러서입니다."], ["The pride of philosophy had intoxicated my reason, and the sublimest of all wisdom appeared to me, as it did to the Greeks of old, to be foolishness.", "철학의 오만이 제 이성을 취하게 했고, 모든 지혜 가운데 가장 숭고한 그 지혜가, 옛 그리스인들에게 그러했듯 제게도 어리석음으로만 보였습니다."], ["God hath, however, been so gracious to shew me my error in time, and to bring me into the way of truth, before I sunk into utter darkness forever.", "그러나 하느님께서는 너그러이도, 제가 영원한 어둠 속으로 완전히 가라앉기 전에 제 잘못을 제때 깨닫게 하시고 저를 진리의 길로 이끌어 주셨습니다."]], [["“I find myself beginning to grow weak, I shall therefore hasten to the main purpose of this letter.", "제 기운이 점점 빠지기 시작함을 느낍니다. 그러니 이 편지의 본래 목적으로 서둘러 들어가겠습니다."]], [["“When I reflect on the actions of my past life, I know of nothing which sits heavier upon my conscience than the injustice I have been guilty of to that poor wretch your adopted son.", "지난 제 삶의 행적들을 돌이켜 볼 때, 경의 양자인 그 가엾은 청년에게 제가 저지른 부당함만큼 제 양심을 무겁게 짓누르는 것이 없습니다."], ["I have, indeed, not only connived at the villany of others, but been myself active in injustice towards him.", "저는 실로 남들의 악행을 못 본 척 눈감았을 뿐 아니라, 저 스스로도 그에게 부당한 일을 적극 행했습니다."], ["Believe me, my dear friend, when I tell you, on the word of a dying man, he hath been basely injured.", "친애하는 벗이여, 죽어 가는 사람의 말로 제가 전하니 부디 믿어 주십시오. 그는 비열하게 해를 입었습니다."], ["As to the principal fact, upon the misrepresentation of which you discarded him, I solemnly assure you he is innocent.", "그 잘못된 전언 때문에 경께서 그를 내치셨던 그 핵심 사건에 대하여, 저는 엄숙히 단언합니다. 그는 결백합니다."], ["When you lay upon your supposed deathbed, he was the only person in the house who testified any real concern; and what happened afterwards arose from the wildness of his joy on your recovery; and, I am sorry to say it, from the baseness of another person (but it is my desire to justify the innocent, and to accuse none).", "경께서 임종하시리라 여겨졌던 그 자리에 누워 계셨을 때, 집 안에서 참된 근심을 드러낸 사람은 그가 유일했습니다. 그 뒤에 일어난 일은 경의 회복에 그가 미친 듯이 기뻐한 데서 비롯된 것이며, 말씀드리기 송구하오나, 또 다른 사람의 비열함에서 비롯된 것이기도 합니다(그러나 제 바람은 결백한 이를 변호하는 것이지, 누구를 고발하려는 것이 아닙니다)."], ["Believe me, my friend, this young man hath the noblest generosity of heart, the most perfect capacity for friendship, the highest integrity, and indeed every virtue which can ennoble a man.", "벗이여, 부디 믿어 주십시오. 이 젊은이는 더없이 고결한 너그러움을, 더없이 완전한 우정의 자질을, 더없이 드높은 성실함을, 그리고 실로 사람을 고귀하게 만드는 온갖 덕을 지니고 있습니다."], ["He hath some faults, but among them is not to be numbered the least want of duty or gratitude towards you.", "그에게 허물이 없지는 않으나, 경께 대한 도리나 감사의 마음이 조금이라도 모자란 점만큼은 그 허물에 들지 않습니다."], ["On the contrary, I am satisfied, when you dismissed him from your house, his heart bled for you more than for himself.", "오히려 저는 확신합니다. 경께서 그를 집에서 내치셨을 때, 그의 가슴은 자신을 위해서보다 경을 위해 더 피 흘렸으리라고요."]], [["“Worldly motives were the wicked and base reasons of my concealing this from you so long; to reveal it now I can have no inducement but the desire of serving the cause of truth, of doing right to the innocent, and of making all the amends in my power for a past offence.", "세속적인 동기야말로 제가 이 사실을 그토록 오래 경께 숨겨 온 사악하고 비열한 이유였습니다. 이제 와 그것을 밝히는 데에는, 진리의 대의에 봉사하고, 결백한 이에게 정당함을 돌려주며, 지난날의 잘못을 제 힘이 닿는 한 모두 갚으려는 바람 외에 다른 어떤 동기도 있을 수 없습니다."], ["I hope this declaration, therefore, will have the effect desired, and will restore this deserving young man to your favour; the hearing of which, while I am yet alive, will afford the utmost consolation to,", "그러므로 저는 이 고백이 바라던 효과를 거두어, 이 마땅한 대접을 받아야 할 청년을 경의 총애 속으로 되돌려 놓기를 바랍니다. 제가 아직 살아 있는 동안 그 소식을 듣는 것이야말로, 다음과 같은 사람에게 더없는 위안이 될 것입니다."]], [["Sir, Your most obliged, obedient humble servant, THOMAS SQUARE.”", "경이시여, 경께 더없이 큰 신세를 진, 순종하는 미천한 종, 토머스 스퀘어 올림."]], [["The reader will, after this, scarce wonder at the revolution so visibly appearing in Mr Allworthy, notwithstanding he received from Thwackum, by the same post, another letter of a very different kind, which we shall here add, as it may possibly be the last time we shall have occasion to mention the name of that gentleman.", "이 편지를 읽고 나면, 올워디 씨에게 그토록 뚜렷이 나타난 마음의 변화에 독자는 거의 놀라지 않을 것이다. 비록 그가 같은 우편으로 스왝컴에게서 사뭇 다른 종류의 또 다른 편지를 받았다 해도 말이다. 이제 그 편지를 여기 덧붙이는데, 어쩌면 우리가 그 신사의 이름을 입에 올릴 마지막 기회가 될지도 모르기 때문이다."]], [["“I am not at all surprized at hearing from your worthy nephew a fresh instance of the villany of Mr Square the atheist's young pupil.", "경이시여,\n경의 훌륭하신 조카로부터, 무신론자 스퀘어 씨의 어린 제자가 저지른 악행의 새로운 사례를 전해 듣고도 저는 조금도 놀라지 않았습니다."], ["I shall not wonder at any murders he may commit; and I heartily pray that your own blood may not seal up his final commitment to the place of wailing and gnashing of teeth.", "그가 앞으로 어떤 살인을 저지른다 해도 저는 놀라지 않을 것입니다. 그리고 경 자신의 핏줄이, 그를 통곡과 이 갈리는 곳으로 최종적으로 보내는 봉인이 되지 않기를 저는 진심으로 기도합니다."]], [["“Though you cannot want sufficient calls to repentance for the many unwarrantable weaknesses exemplified in your behaviour to this wretch, so much to the prejudice of your own lawful family, and of your character; I say, though these may sufficiently be supposed to prick and goad your conscience at this season, I should yet be wanting to my duty, if I spared to give you some admonition in order to bring you to a due sense of your errors.", "경께서 이 가련한 자에게 보이신 행동에서 드러난, 변명의 여지 없는 그 숱한 나약함들, 경 자신의 정당한 가문과 경의 명성에 그토록 해를 끼친 그 나약함들에 대해 회개할 충분한 이유가 없으실 리 없습니다. 다시 말해, 그것만으로도 지금 이 시기에 경의 양심을 찌르고 다그치기에 충분하다고 여길 만하나, 그럼에도 제가 경께 약간의 훈계를 베풀어 경으로 하여금 자신의 잘못을 마땅히 깨닫게 하기를 아낀다면, 저는 제 본분을 저버리는 셈이 될 것입니다."], ["I therefore pray you seriously to consider the judgment which is likely to overtake this wicked villain; and let it serve at least as a warning to you, that you may not for the future despise the advice of one who is so indefatigable in his prayers for your welfare.", "그러므로 저는 경께 간곡히 청하오니, 이 사악한 악당에게 닥칠 법한 심판을 진지하게 헤아려 보십시오. 그리고 적어도 그것이 경께 하나의 경고가 되어, 경의 안녕을 위해 그토록 지칠 줄 모르고 기도하는 자의 충고를 앞으로는 업신여기지 않으시도록 하십시오."]], [["“Had not my hand been withheld from due correction, I had scourged much of this diabolical spirit out of a boy, of whom from his infancy I discovered the devil had taken such entire possession.", "마땅한 징계를 가하려던 제 손이 묶이지만 않았던들, 저는 그 아이에게서 이 악마 같은 영혼을 매질하여 상당 부분 몰아냈을 것입니다. 어려서부터 저는 그 아이를 악마가 그토록 완전히 사로잡고 있음을 알아보았으니까요."], ["But reflections of this kind now come too late.", "그러나 이런 회한도 이제는 너무 늦게 찾아왔습니다."]], [["“I am sorry you have given away the living of Westerton so hastily.", "경께서 웨스터턴의 성직록을 그토록 성급히 내어 주신 것이 안타깝습니다."], ["I should have applied on that occasion earlier, had I thought you would not have acquainted me previous to the disposition.----Your objection to pluralities is being righteous over-much.", "경께서 그것을 처분하기에 앞서 제게 알려 주지 않으시리라 알았더라면, 저는 그 기회에 진작 청을 넣었을 것입니다.―성직 겸임에 대한 경의 반대는 지나치게 의로운 처사입니다."], ["If there were any crime in the practice, so many godly men would not agree to it.", "만일 그 관행에 무슨 죄가 있다면, 그토록 많은 경건한 분들이 그것에 동의하지는 않았을 것입니다."], ["If the vicar of Aldergrove should die (as we hear he is in a declining way), I hope you will think of me, since I am certain you must be convinced of my most sincere attachment to your highest welfare--a welfare to which all worldly considerations are as trifling as the small tithes mentioned in Scripture are, when compared to the weighty matters of the law.", "올더그로브의 교구 목사가 죽기라도 한다면(듣자 하니 그가 쇠약해져 가고 있다 하니), 부디 저를 생각해 주시기 바랍니다. 경의 더없는 안녕에 대한 저의 더없이 진실한 헌신을 경께서 확신하시리라 저는 믿기 때문입니다.―그 안녕에 비하면 모든 세속적 고려란, 성경에 나오는 그 자잘한 십일조가 율법의 더 중대한 일들에 견주어 그러하듯, 하찮은 것에 지나지 않습니다."]], [["I am, sir, Your faithful humble servant, ROGER THWACKUM.”", "경이시여, 저는, 경의 충실하고 미천한 종, 로저 스왝컴 올림."]], [["This was the first time Thwackum ever wrote in this authoritative stile to Allworthy, and of this he had afterwards sufficient reason to repent, as in the case of those who mistake the highest degree of goodness for the lowest degree of weakness.", "스왝컴이 올워디에게 이렇게 권위적인 어투로 글을 쓴 것은 이번이 처음이었으며, 이에 대해 그는 뒷날 충분히 후회할 만한 이유를 갖게 되었으니, 이는 더없이 높은 정도의 선함을 더없이 낮은 정도의 나약함으로 착각한 자들의 경우와 같았다. 올워디는 사실 이 사내를 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없었다."], ["Allworthy had indeed never liked this man.", "그는 이 사내가 오만하고 심술궂음을 알고 있었다."], ["He knew him to be proud and ill-natured; he also knew that his divinity itself was tinctured with his temper, and such as in many respects he himself did by no means approve; but he was at the same time an excellent scholar, and most indefatigable in teaching the two lads.", "또한 그의 신학 자체가 그의 성미에 물들어 있어, 여러 면에서 올워디 자신이 결코 인정할 수 없는 것임도 알고 있었다. 그러나 동시에 그는 뛰어난 학자였고, 두 소년을 가르치는 데 더없이 지칠 줄을 몰랐다."], ["Add to this, the strict severity of his life and manners, an unimpeached honesty, and a most devout attachment to religion.", "거기에 더하여 그의 엄격한 생활과 몸가짐, 흠잡을 데 없는 정직함, 그리고 종교에 대한 더없이 경건한 헌신도 있었다."], ["So that, upon the whole, though Allworthy did not esteem nor love the man, yet he could never bring himself to part with a tutor to the boys, who was, both by learning and industry, extremely well qualified for his office; and he hoped, that as they were bred up in his own house, and under his own eye, he should be able to correct whatever was wrong in Thwackum's instructions.", "그리하여 전체적으로 보아, 올워디는 이 사내를 존경하지도 사랑하지도 않았지만, 학식으로 보나 근면함으로 보나 그 직분에 더없이 합당한 소년들의 가정교사를 차마 내보낼 수 없었다. 그리고 그는, 소년들이 자기 집에서 자기 눈 아래 길러지고 있으니, 스왝컴의 가르침에서 잘못된 것은 무엇이든 자신이 바로잡을 수 있으리라 기대했다."]], [["In which the history is continued.", "올워디 씨는 방금 한 말 가운데 존스를 떠올리게 하는 어떤 애틋한 생각들이 되살아나, 이 선량한 사람의 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 그것을 알아챈 밀러 부인이 말했다."], ["Mr Allworthy, in his last speech, had recollected some tender ideas concerning Jones, which had brought tears into the good man's eyes.", "\"그래요, 그래요, 어른. 이 가엾은 청년에게 베푸신 어른의 자비는 아무리 감추려 애쓰셔도 다 알려져 있답니다."], ["This Mrs Miller observing, said, “Yes, yes, sir, your goodness to this poor young man is known, notwithstanding all your care to conceal it; but there is not a single syllable of truth in what those villains said.", "하지만 그 악당들이 한 말에는 단 한 마디의 진실도 없어요. 나이팅게일 씨가 이제 일의 전모를 밝혀냈답니다."], ["Mr Nightingale hath now discovered the whole matter.", "보아하니 그 작자들은 가엾은 존스 씨의 연적인 어느 귀족에게 고용되어, 그를 배에 강제로 끌어다 태우려 했던 거예요."], ["It seems these fellows were employed by a lord, who is a rival of poor Mr Jones, to have pressed him on board a ship.--I assure them I don't know who they will press next.", "정말이지, 그자들이 다음엔 또 누구를 잡아갈지 알 수가 없어요. 여기 이 나이팅게일 씨가 그 장교를 직접 만나 보았는데, 아주 점잖은 신사더래요."], ["Mr Nightingale here hath seen the officer himself, who is a very pretty gentleman, and hath told him all, and is very sorry for what he undertook, which he would never have done, had he known Mr Jones to have been a gentleman; but he was told that he was a common strolling vagabond.”", "그가 모든 걸 털어놓고는 자기가 맡았던 일을 몹시 뉘우치더랍니다. 존스 씨가 신사인 줄 알았더라면 결코 그런 일을 하지 않았을 텐데, 그가 흔해빠진 떠돌이 부랑자라는 말을 들었다지 뭐예요.\""]], [["Allworthy stared at all this, and declared he was a stranger to every word she said.", "올워디는 이 모든 말을 멍하니 바라보다가, 그녀가 한 말 한 마디 한 마디가 도무지 처음 듣는 이야기라고 잘라 말했다. \"그렇겠지요, 어른.\""], ["“Yes, sir,”", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["answered she, “I believe you are.----It is a very different story, I believe, from what those fellows told this lawyer.”", "\"그러실 줄 알았어요. 아마 그 작자들이 그 변호사에게 한 이야기와는 사뭇 다른 이야기겠지요.\""]], [["“What lawyer, madam?", "\"무슨 변호사 말이오, 부인?"], ["what is it you mean?”", "대체 무슨 소리요?\" 올워디가 물었다."], ["said Allworthy.", "\"어머나, 어머나,\""], ["“Nay, nay,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “this is so like you to deny your own goodness: but Mr Nightingale here saw him.”", "\"이렇게 자기 자비를 한사코 부인하시는 게 꼭 어른답네요. 하지만 여기 나이팅게일 씨가 그를 봤다니까요.\""], ["“Saw whom, madam?”", "\"누구를 봤다는 거요, 부인?\""], ["answered he.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Why, your lawyer, sir,”", "\"아니, 어른의 변호사 말이에요.\""], ["said she, “that you so kindly sent to enquire into the affair.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"어른께서 그 일을 알아보라고 친절히 보내신 그 변호사 말이지요.\""], ["“I am still in the dark, upon my honour,”", "\"맹세코, 나는 아직도 영문을 모르겠소.\""], ["said Allworthy.", "올워디가 말했다."], ["“Why then do you tell him, my dear sir,”", "\"그럼 어른, 직접 말씀해 보세요.\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["cries she.", "\"사실은, 어른,\""], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "나이팅게일이 말했다."], ["said Nightingale, “I did see that very lawyer who went from you when I came into the room, at an alehouse in Aldersgate, in company with two of the fellows who were employed by Lord Fellamar to press Mr Jones, and who were by that means present at the unhappy rencounter between him and Mr Fitzpatrick.”", "\"제가 이 방에 들어왔을 때 어른 곁에서 막 나가던 바로 그 변호사를, 올더스게이트의 어느 선술집에서 보았습니다. 펠라마 경에게 고용되어 존스 씨를 강제로 끌어가려 했던, 그래서 그와 피츠패트릭 씨 사이의 그 불행한 충돌 현장에 있었던 두 작자와 함께 있더군요.\""], ["“I own, sir,”", "\"사실 어른,\""], ["said Mrs Miller, “when I saw this gentleman come into the room to you, I told Mr Nightingale that I apprehended you had sent him thither to inquire into the affair.”", "밀러 부인이 말했다. \"이 신사분이 어른께 다가오는 걸 봤을 때, 저는 나이팅게일 씨에게 어른께서 일을 알아보라고 저 사람을 거기 보내신 모양이라고 말했답니다.\""], ["Allworthy shewed marks of astonishment in his countenance at this news, and was indeed for two or three minutes struck dumb by it.", "올워디는 이 소식에 얼굴 가득 놀라움을 드러냈고, 정말이지 이삼 분 동안은 그 충격에 말문이 막혔다. 마침내 그는 나이팅게일 씨를 향해 말했다."], ["At last, addressing himself to Mr Nightingale, he said, “I must confess myself, sir, more surprized at what you tell me than I have ever been before at anything in my whole life.", "\"솔직히 말하건대, 선생, 나는 당신이 들려준 이야기에 내 평생 어떤 일을 두고도 느껴 본 적 없을 만큼 놀랐소. 그게 정말 그 신사가 틀림없소?\""], ["Are you certain this was the gentleman?”", "\"틀림없습니다.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다."], ["“I am most certain,”", "\"올더스게이트에서 말이오?\" 올워디가 외쳤다."], ["answered Nightingale.", "\"그리고 당신이 그 변호사와 두 작자와 함께 있었단 말이오?\" \"그렇습니다, 어른.\""], ["“At Aldersgate?”", "그가 말했다. \"거의 반 시간 가까이 있었지요.\""], ["cries Allworthy.", "\"그래, 선생,\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["“And was you in company with this lawyer and the two fellows?”--“I was, sir,”", "\"그래서 그 변호사는 어찌 행동했소? 그와 작자들 사이에 오간 말을 다 들었소?\""], ["said the other, “very near half an hour.”", "\"아닙니다, 어른.\" 나이팅게일이 대답했다."], ["“Well, sir,”", "\"제가 가기 전에 이미 함께 있었던 모양입니다."], ["said Allworthy, “and in what manner did the lawyer behave?", "제 앞에서 변호사는 별말이 없었지요. 하지만 제가 그 작자들을 여러 차례 캐물어도, 그들이 존스 씨에게 들은 것과 정반대인 이야기를, 그러니까 피츠패트릭 씨 말로는 새빨간 거짓말인 이야기를 끝까지 고집하자, 그제야 변호사가 작자들에게 진실 아닌 것은 아무 말도 하지 말라고 당부하더군요."], ["did you hear all that past between him and the fellows?”", "그러면서 존스 씨를 어찌나 두둔하던지, 어른 곁에 있던 같은 사람을 봤을 때 저는 어른의 자비로운 마음이 그를 거기 보내신 모양이라고 결론지었던 겁니다.\" \"그럼 어른께서 그를 거기 보내신 게 아닌가요?\""], ["“No, sir,”", "밀러 부인이 말했다. \"정말로 아니오.\""], ["answered Nightingale, “they had been together before I came.--In my presence the lawyer said little; but, after I had several times examined the fellows, who persisted in a story directly contrary to what I had heard from Mr Jones, and which I find by Mr Fitzpatrick was a rank falshood, the lawyer then desired the fellows to say nothing but what was the truth, and seemed to speak so much in favour of Mr Jones, that, when I saw the same person with you, I concluded your goodness had prompted you to send him thither.”--“And did you not send him thither?”", "올워디가 대답했다. \"이 순간까지 그가 그런 심부름을 갔다는 것조차 몰랐소.\""], ["says Mrs Miller.--“Indeed I did not,”", "\"이제 다 알겠어요!\" 밀러 부인이 말했다."], ["answered Allworthy; “nor did I know he had gone on such an errand till this moment.”--“I see it all!”", "\"맹세코, 다 알겠다고요! 어쩐지 요즘 그렇게 둘이 바짝 붙어 쑥덕거리더라니."], ["said Mrs Miller, “upon my soul, I see it all!", "나이팅게일, 부디 지금 당장 그 작자들을 잡으러 달려가 줘요."], ["No wonder they have been closeted so close lately.", "땅 위에 있기만 하다면 꼭 찾아내요."], ["Son Nightingale, let me beg you run for these fellows immediately----find them out if they are above-ground.", "나도 직접 가겠어요.\" \"부인,\""], ["I will go myself”--“Dear madam,”", "올워디가 말했다. \"진정하시오."], ["said Allworthy, “be patient, and do me the favour to send a servant upstairs to call Mr Dowling hither, if he be in the house, or, if not, Mr Blifil.”", "그리고 부디 하인을 위층에 올려 보내, 다울링 씨가 집 안에 있거든 이리 불러 주시오. 없거든 블리필 씨라도.\""], ["Mrs Miller went out muttering something to herself, and presently returned with an answer, “That Mr Dowling was gone; but that the t'other,”", "밀러 부인은 혼자 무어라 중얼거리며 나가더니, 이내 대답을 가지고 돌아왔다. \"다울링 씨는 떠났고, 그 사람은,\""], ["as she called him, “was coming.”", "그녀는 블리필을 그렇게 불렀다. \"오는 중이라네요.\""]], [["Allworthy was of a cooler disposition than the good woman, whose spirits were all up in arms in the cause of her friend.", "올워디는 친구를 위해 온통 분기충천한 이 선량한 부인보다 한결 차분한 기질이었다."], ["He was not however without some suspicions which were near akin to hers.", "그러나 그에게도 그녀의 것과 사촌쯤 되는 의심이 없지는 않았다."], ["When Blifil came into the room, he asked him with a very serious countenance, and with a less friendly look than he had ever before given him, “Whether he knew anything of Mr Dowling's having seen any of the persons who were present at the duel between Jones and another gentleman?”", "블리필이 방에 들어오자, 그는 더없이 엄숙한 얼굴로, 그리고 일찍이 그에게 보낸 적 없는 덜 다정한 눈빛으로 물었다. \"다울링 씨가 존스와 어느 신사 사이의 결투 현장에 있었던 사람들 가운데 누구든 만났다는 일에 대해, 자네가 뭔가 아는 게 있는가?\""]], [["There is nothing so dangerous as a question which comes by surprize on a man whose business it is to conceal truth, or to defend falshood.", "진실을 숨기거나 거짓을 변호하는 것이 본분인 사람에게 불시에 날아드는 질문만큼 위험한 것은 없다."], ["For which reason those worthy personages, whose noble office it is to save the lives of their fellow-creatures at the Old Bailey, take the utmost care, by frequent previous examination, to divine every question which may be asked their clients on the day of tryal, that they may be supplyed with proper and ready answers, which the most fertile invention cannot supply in an instant.", "그런 까닭에, 올드베일리 법정에서 동족의 목숨을 구하는 고귀한 직무를 맡은 그 훌륭한 인물들은, 사전에 거듭 검토하여 재판 당일 의뢰인에게 던져질 만한 모든 질문을 미리 헤아려 두려 온갖 정성을 기울인다. 그래야만 적절하고 즉각적인 답변을 갖추어 둘 수 있으니, 아무리 비옥한 재간이라도 그 자리에서 즉석에 만들어 낼 수 없는 것이기 때문이다."], ["Besides, the sudden and violent impulse on the blood, occasioned by these surprizes, causes frequently such an alteration in the countenance, that the man is obliged to give evidence against himself.", "게다가 이런 불시의 충격이 피에 일으키는 갑작스럽고 격렬한 동요는, 흔히 얼굴빛을 크게 바꾸어 놓아, 그 사람이 자기에게 불리한 증거를 스스로 내놓게 만든다."], ["And such indeed were the alterations which the countenance of Blifil underwent from this sudden question, that we can scarce blame the eagerness of Mrs Miller, who immediately cryed out, “Guilty, upon my honour!", "실로 이 갑작스러운 질문에 블리필의 얼굴빛이 겪은 변화가 어찌나 컸던지, 우리는 당장 \"맹세코 유죄예요! 영혼을 걸고 유죄라고요!\""], ["guilty, upon my soul!”", "하고 외친 밀러 부인의 성급함을 차마 탓할 수 없을 정도다."]], [["Mr Allworthy sharply rebuked her for this impetuosity; and then turning to Blifil, who seemed sinking into the earth, he said, “Why do you hesitate, sir, at giving me an answer? You certainly must have employed him; for he would not, of his own accord, I believe, have undertaken such an errand, and especially without acquainting me.”", "올워디 씨는 그 성마름을 두고 그녀를 날카롭게 나무랐다. 그러고는 땅속으로 꺼져 들어갈 것만 같은 블리필을 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"왜 내게 대답하기를 망설이는가? 자네가 분명 그를 부린 게 틀림없네. 그가 제 발로, 더구나 내게 알리지도 않고 그런 심부름을 떠맡았을 리는 없으니 말일세.\""]], [["Blifil then answered, “I own, sir, I have been guilty of an offence, yet may I hope your pardon?”--“My pardon,” said Allworthy, very angrily.--“Nay, sir,” answered Blifil, “I knew you would be offended; yet surely my dear uncle will forgive the effects of the most amiable of human weaknesses. Compassion for those who do not deserve it, I own is a crime; and yet it is a crime from which you yourself are not entirely free. I know I have been guilty of it in more than one instance to this very person; and I will own I did send Mr Dowling, not on a vain and fruitless enquiry, but to discover the witnesses, and to endeavour to soften their evidence. This, sir, is the truth; which, though I intended to conceal from you, I will not deny.”", "그러자 블리필이 대답했다. \"솔직히 말씀드리면, 어른, 제가 잘못을 저질렀습니다. 그래도 용서를 바랄 수 있을까요?\" \"용서라니.\" 올워디가 몹시 화를 내며 말했다. \"아니, 어른,\" 블리필이 대답했다. \"노하실 줄 알았습니다. 하지만 분명 사랑하는 외숙께서는 인간의 가장 사랑스러운 약점이 빚어낸 결과를 용서해 주시리라 믿습니다. 받을 자격 없는 자에 대한 동정이 죄임은 저도 압니다. 그러나 그것은 어른 자신도 온전히 벗어나 있지 못한 죄이기도 하지요. 저 역시 바로 이 사람에게 한두 번 그 죄를 저질렀음을 압니다. 그리고 솔직히 고백하건대, 제가 다울링 씨를 보낸 것은 헛되고 부질없는 조사를 위해서가 아니라, 증인들을 찾아내어 그 증언을 누그러뜨려 보려는 것이었습니다. 이것이, 어른, 진실입니다. 어른께 감추려 했던 것이지만, 부인하지는 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“I confess,”", "\"솔직히 말씀드리면,\""], ["said Nightingale, “this is the light in which it appeared to me from the gentleman's behaviour.”", "나이팅게일이 말했다. \"그 신사분의 행동으로 보아 제게도 그렇게 비쳤습니다.\""]], [["“Now, madam,”", "\"자, 부인,\""], ["said Allworthy, “I believe you will once in your life own you have entertained a wrong suspicion, and are not so angry with my nephew as you was.”", "올워디가 말했다. \"이제 부인도 평생에 한 번쯤은 자신이 잘못된 의심을 품었음을 인정하고, 전처럼 내 조카에게 화내지 않게 되리라 믿소.\""]], [["Mrs Miller was silent; for, though she could not so hastily be pleased with Blifil, whom she looked upon to have been the ruin of Jones, yet in this particular instance he had imposed upon her as well as upon the rest; so entirely had the devil stood his friend.", "밀러 부인은 입을 다물었다. 존스를 파멸시킨 장본인으로 여겨 온 블리필이 그리 선뜻 마음에 들 리야 없었지만, 적어도 이 일에서만큼은 그가 나머지 사람들과 더불어 그녀까지도 감쪽같이 속였던 것이다."], ["And, indeed, I look upon the vulgar observation, “That the devil often deserts his friends, and leaves them in the lurch,”", "그토록 철저히 악마가 그의 편을 들어 주었던 셈이다. 사실 나는 \"악마는 흔히 제 친구를 저버리고 곤경에 빠뜨린다\"는 저 흔한 말이야말로, 그 신사의 인품을 크게 욕보이는 것이라 본다."], ["to be a great abuse on that gentleman's character.", "어쩌면 그는 한낱 술친구에 지나지 않는 자들이나, 기껏해야 절반쯤만 제 사람인 자들은 이따금 저버릴지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps he may sometimes desert those who are only his cup acquaintance; or who, at most, are but half his; but he generally stands by those who are thoroughly his servants, and helps them off in all extremities, till their bargain expires.", "그러나 철두철미하게 제 종이 된 자들에게는 대개 끝까지 곁을 지키며, 그 거래의 기한이 다할 때까지 어떤 위기에서도 빠져나오도록 도와주는 법이다."]], [["As a conquered rebellion strengthens a government, or as health is more perfectly established by recovery from some diseases; so anger, when removed, often gives new life to affection.", "진압된 반란이 정부를 더욱 굳건히 하듯, 또 어떤 병에서 회복되고 나면 건강이 한층 더 완전하게 자리 잡듯, 노여움도 가시고 나면 흔히 애정에 새 생명을 불어넣는다. 올워디 씨의 경우가 바로 그러했다."], ["This was the case of Mr Allworthy; for Blifil having wiped off the greater suspicion, the lesser, which had been raised by Square's letter, sunk of course, and was forgotten; and Thwackum, with whom he was greatly offended, bore alone all the reflections which Square had cast on the enemies of Jones.", "블리필이 더 큰 의심을 씻어 내자, 스퀘어의 편지가 불러일으켰던 작은 의심은 당연히 가라앉아 잊히고 말았다. 그래서 그가 몹시 노여워하던 스왝컴이, 스퀘어가 존스의 적들에게 퍼부었던 온갖 비난을 혼자 고스란히 뒤집어쓰게 되었다."]], [["As for that young man, the resentment of Mr Allworthy began more and more to abate towards him.", "그 청년으로 말하자면, 올워디 씨의 원망은 점점 더 누그러져 갔다."], ["He told Blifil, “He did not only forgive the extraordinary efforts of his good-nature, but would give him the pleasure of following his example.”", "그는 블리필에게 말했다. \"자네의 그 유별난 선의의 노력을 용서할 뿐만 아니라, 자네에게 그 본을 따르는 기쁨까지 안겨 주겠네.\""], ["Then, turning to Mrs Miller with a smile which would have become an angel, he cryed, “What say you, madam?", "그러고는 천사에게나 어울릴 법한 미소를 띠고 밀러 부인을 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"어떻소, 부인?"], ["shall we take a hackney-coach, and all of us together pay a visit to your friend?", "우리 모두 삯마차를 잡아타고 함께 부인의 친구를 찾아가 보지 않겠소?"], ["I promise you it is not the first visit I have made in a prison.”", "장담컨대, 내가 감옥을 찾아가는 것이 이번이 처음은 아니라오.\""]], [["Every reader, I believe, will be able to answer for the worthy woman; but they must have a great deal of good-nature, and be well acquainted with friendship, who can feel what she felt on this occasion. Few, I hope, are capable of feeling what now passed in the mind of Blifil; but those who are will acknowledge that it was impossible for him to raise any objection to this visit. Fortune, however, or the gentleman lately mentioned above, stood his friend, and prevented his undergoing so great a shock; for at the very instant when the coach was sent for, Partridge arrived, and, having called Mrs Miller from the company, acquainted her with the dreadful accident lately come to light; and hearing Mr Allworthy's intention, begged her to find some means of stopping him: “For,” says he, “the matter must at all hazards be kept a secret from him; and if he should now go, he will find Mr Jones and his mother, who arrived just as I left him, lamenting over one another the horrid crime they have ignorantly committed.”", "독자라면 누구나 이 갸륵한 부인의 마음을 능히 헤아릴 수 있으리라 믿는다. 다만 이 자리에서 그녀가 느낀 바를 온전히 느끼려면, 더없이 너그러운 천성을 지니고 우정이 무엇인지를 깊이 아는 사람이라야 할 것이다. 한편 이제 블리필의 마음속을 스쳐 간 것을 느낄 수 있는 사람은 거의 없기를 바란다. 그러나 그것을 느낄 줄 아는 사람이라면, 그가 이 방문에 어떤 이의도 제기할 수 없었으리라는 점을 인정할 것이다. 그렇지만 운명이, 아니 앞서 말한 그 신사가 그의 편을 들어, 그가 그토록 큰 충격을 겪는 일을 막아 주었다. 바로 마차를 부르러 보낸 그 순간 파트리지가 도착하여, 밀러 부인을 사람들에게서 따로 불러내고는 최근에 드러난 그 끔찍한 사건을 알려 준 것이다. 그러고는 올워디 씨의 뜻을 듣자, 무슨 수를 써서라도 그를 말려 달라고 그녀에게 간청했다. \"왜냐하면,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 일은 무슨 일이 있어도 그분께는 비밀로 해야 합니다. 만일 지금 가신다면, 존스 씨와 그 어머니가, 제가 그를 떠날 때 막 도착한 그 어머니가, 자기들이 모르고 저지른 그 끔찍한 죄를 두고 서로 부둥켜안고 탄식하는 꼴을 보시게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["The poor woman, who was almost deprived of her senses at his dreadful news, was never less capable of invention than at present. However, as women are much readier at this than men, she bethought herself of an excuse, and, returning to Allworthy, said, “I am sure, sir, you will be surprized at hearing any objection from me to the kind proposal you just now made; and yet I am afraid of the consequence of it, if carried immediately into execution. You must imagine, sir, that all the calamities which have lately befallen this poor young fellow must have thrown him into the lowest dejection of spirits; and now, sir, should we all on a sudden fling him into such a violent fit of joy, as I know your presence will occasion, it may, I am afraid, produce some fatal mischief, especially as his servant, who is without, tells me he is very far from being well.”", "이 무시무시한 소식에 정신이 거의 나갈 지경이 된 가엾은 부인은, 지금만큼 꾀를 짜낼 형편이 못 되는 때가 없었다. 그러나 여자는 남자보다 이런 데 훨씬 재빠른 법이라, 그녀는 핑곗거리 하나를 떠올리고는 올워디에게 돌아가 말했다. \"어른, 방금 친절히 내주신 제안에 제가 이의를 다는 걸 들으시면 분명 놀라시겠지요. 그래도 그것을 당장 실행에 옮기면 그 결과가 어찌 될지 두렵습니다. 어른, 생각해 보세요. 요즈음 이 가엾은 청년에게 닥친 그 모든 재앙이 그를 더없이 깊은 실의에 빠뜨려 놓았을 게 틀림없습니다. 그런 마당에 우리가 느닷없이, 어른의 행차가 불러일으킬 것이 분명한 그토록 격렬한 기쁨의 발작 속으로 그를 내던진다면, 무슨 치명적인 화를 부를까 두렵습니다. 더구나 밖에 있는 그의 하인 말로는, 그가 도무지 성치 못하다고 하니 말이지요.\""]], [["“Is his servant without?”", "\"그의 하인이 밖에 있소?\""], ["cries Allworthy; “pray call him hither.", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"어서 이리 불러 주시오."], ["I will ask him some questions concerning his master.”", "그 주인에 관해 몇 가지 물어봐야겠소.\""]], [["Partridge was at first afraid to appear before Mr Allworthy; but was at length persuaded, after Mrs Miller, who had often heard his whole story from his own mouth, had promised to introduce him.", "파트리지는 처음에는 올워디 씨 앞에 나서기를 두려워했다. 그러나 그의 입에서 그 사연 전부를 여러 차례 들어 온 밀러 부인이 그를 소개해 주겠노라 약속하자, 마침내 마음을 돌렸다."]], [["Allworthy recollected Partridge the moment he came into the room, though many years had passed since he had seen him. Mrs Miller, therefore, might have spared here a formal oration, in which, indeed, she was something prolix; for the reader, I believe, may have observed already that the good woman, among other things, had a tongue always ready for the service of her friends.", "올워디는 그가 방에 들어서는 순간 파트리지를 알아보았다. 그를 본 지 여러 해가 지났는데도 그러했다. 그러니 밀러 부인은 여기서 격식 차린 일장 연설을 굳이 늘어놓지 않아도 좋았을 것이다. 실은 그 연설이 다소 장황하기도 했다. 독자라면 이미 알아챘으리라 믿거니와, 이 선량한 부인은 다른 여러 재주와 더불어, 친구를 위해서라면 언제든 술술 놀려 줄 혀를 지니고 있었으니 말이다."]], [["“And are you,”", "\"그래, 자네가,\""], ["said Allworthy to Partridge, “the servant of Mr Jones?”", "올워디가 파트리지에게 말했다. \"존스 씨의 하인인가?\""], ["“I can't say, sir,”", "\"글쎄요, 어른,\""], ["answered he, “that I am regularly a servant, but I live with him, an't please your honour, at present.", "그가 대답했다. \"제가 어엿한 하인이라고는 못 하겠습니다만, 황송하게도 지금은 그분과 함께 지내고 있습죠."], ["Non sum qualis eram, as your honour very well knows.”", "논 숨 콸리스 에람, 어른께서도 익히 아시는 대로지요.\""]], [["Mr Allworthy then asked him many questions concerning Jones, as to his health, and other matters; to all which Partridge answered, without having the least regard to what was, but considered only what he would have things appear; for a strict adherence to truth was not among the articles of this honest fellow's morality or his religion.", "* 논 숨 콸리스 에람(Non sum qualis eram): \"나는 예전의 내가 아니다\"라는 뜻의 라틴어. 이어서 올워디 씨는 존스에 관해, 그의 건강이며 그 밖의 여러 일에 대해 그에게 숱한 질문을 던졌다. 파트리지는 그 모든 물음에 사실이 어떠한지는 조금도 개의치 않고, 오직 일들이 어찌 보이기를 바라는지만 헤아려 대답했다. 진실을 곧이곧대로 지키는 것은 이 정직한 친구의 도덕이나 신앙의 조목에 들어 있지 않았던 것이다."]], [["During this dialogue Mr Nightingale took his leave, and presently after Mrs Miller left the room, when Allworthy likewise despatched Blifil; for he imagined that Partridge when alone with him would be more explicit than before company.", "이 문답이 오가는 동안 나이팅게일 씨는 작별을 고했고, 곧이어 밀러 부인도 방을 나갔다. 그러자 올워디 역시 블리필을 내보냈다."], ["They were no sooner left in private together than Allworthy began, as in the following chapter.", "파트리지가 자기와 단둘이 있으면 여럿 앞에서보다 더 솔직히 털어놓으리라 짐작했기 때문이다. 둘만 따로 남게 되자마자 올워디는 다음 장에서와 같이 입을 열었다."]], [["In which the history is farther continued\n“Sure, friend,”", "“정말이지, 친구여,” 선한 노인이 말했다."], ["said the good man, “you are the strangest of all human beings.", "“자네는 인간 가운데 가장 알 수 없는 사람일세."], ["Not only to have suffered as you have formerly for obstinately persisting in a falshood, but to persist in it thus to the last, and to pass thus upon the world for a servant of your own son!", "예전에 거짓을 끝끝내 고집하다가 그렇게 고초를 겪고도, 지금까지도 그것을 끝까지 우기다니. 게다가 자기 친아들의 하인 행세를 하며 세상을 속이다니!"], ["What interest can you have in all this?", "이 모든 일에서 자네가 얻을 이익이 무엇인가?"], ["What can be your motive?”", "도대체 무슨 속셈인가?”"]], [["“I see, sir,”", "“보아하니, 나리,”"], ["said Partridge, falling down upon his knees, “that your honour is prepossessed against me, and resolved not to believe anything I say, and, therefore, what signifies my protestations?", "파트리지가 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. “나리께서는 저를 미리 못 믿기로 작정하시고, 제가 무슨 말을 해도 곧이듣지 않으려 하시니, 제 항변이 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?"], ["but yet there is one above who knows that I am not the father of this young man.”", "그래도 위에 한 분 계시니, 그분만은 제가 이 청년의 아비가 아님을 아십니다.”"]], [["“How!”", "“뭐라고!”"], ["said Allworthy, “will you yet deny what you was formerly convicted of upon such unanswerable, such manifest evidence?", "올워디가 말했다. “그토록 반박할 수 없고 그토록 명백한 증거로 예전에 유죄가 입증된 일을, 아직도 부인하려는가?"], ["Nay, what a confirmation is your being now found with this very man, of all which twenty years ago appeared against you!", "아니, 자네가 지금 바로 이 사람과 함께 있는 것 자체가, 이십 년 전 자네에게 불리하게 드러났던 모든 것을 다시금 확증하는 셈이 아닌가!"], ["I thought you had left the country!", "나는 자네가 이 고장을 떠난 줄로만 알았네!"], ["nay, I thought you had been long since dead.--In what manner did you know anything of this young man?", "아니, 오래전에 죽은 줄로만 여겼네. 그래, 자네가 이 청년에 대해 어떻게 알게 되었는가?"], ["Where did you meet with him, unless you had kept some correspondence together?", "둘이 무슨 연락을 주고받지 않았다면, 어디서 그를 만났단 말인가?"], ["Do not deny this; for I promise you it will greatly raise your son in my opinion, to find that he hath such a sense of filial duty as privately to support his father for so many years.”", "그것을 부인하지 말게. 약속하건대, 자네 아들이 그토록 여러 해 동안 남몰래 제 아비를 보살펴 온 효심을 지녔다는 사실을 알게 되면, 그 아이에 대한 내 평가가 크게 올라갈 테니 말일세.”"]], [["“If your honour will have patience to hear me,” said Partridge, “I will tell you all.”--Being bid go on, he proceeded thus: “When your honour conceived that displeasure against me, it ended in my ruin soon after; for I lost my little school; and the minister, thinking I suppose it would be agreeable to your honour, turned me out from the office of clerk; so that I had nothing to trust to but the barber's shop, which, in a country place like that, is a poor livelihood; and when my wife died (for till that time I received a pension of £12 a year from an unknown hand, which indeed I believe was your honour's own, for nobody that ever I heard of doth these things besides)--but, as I was saying, when she died, this pension forsook me; so that now, as I owed two or three small debts, which began to be troublesome to me, particularly one[*] which an attorney brought up by law-charges from 15s. to near £30, and as I found all my usual means of living had forsook me, I packed up my little all as well as I could, and went off.", "“나리께서 인내심을 갖고 제 말을 들어주신다면,” 파트리지가 말했다. “모두 말씀드리겠습니다.” 그리고 계속하라는 분부를 받고 이렇게 이어 갔다. “나리께서 저에게 그 노여움을 품으셨을 때, 그 일은 머지않아 저의 파멸로 끝났습니다. 저는 작은 학교를 잃었고, 목사님께서는, 제 짐작에 그것이 나리의 뜻에 맞으리라 여기셨던지, 저를 서기 자리에서도 내치셨습니다. 그래서 제게 기댈 것이라곤 이발소밖에 남지 않았는데, 그런 시골 동네에서야 그것으로는 입에 풀칠하기도 어려웠지요. 그러다 아내가 죽었습니다(그때까지는 어느 알 수 없는 손길에서 해마다 12파운드의 연금을 받았는데, 실은 그것이 나리께서 손수 베푸신 것이라 저는 믿습니다. 제가 들어 본 바로는 그런 일을 하는 분이 나리 말고는 달리 없으니까요). 어쨌든, 말씀드린 대로 아내가 죽자 그 연금도 끊겼습니다. 그래서 이제, 자질구레한 빚이 두세 건 있었는데 그것이 점점 골칫거리가 되기 시작했고, 특히 한 건은 어느 변호사가 소송 비용을 붙여 15실링짜리를 거의 30파운드 가까이로 불려 놓았으며, 게다가 늘 의지하던 생계 수단이 죄다 저를 저버린 것을 알게 되자, 저는 가진 보잘것없는 살림을 할 수 있는 만큼 챙겨 떠나 버렸습니다."]], [["[*] This is a fact which I knew happen to a poor clergyman in Dorsetshire, by the villany of an attorney who, not contented with the exorbitant costs to which the poor man was put by a single action, brought afterwards another action on the judgment, as it was called.", "* 이것은 내가 실제로 도싯셔의 어느 가난한 성직자에게 일어난 줄로 아는 사건이다. 어느 변호사의 악행 때문이었는데, 그는 단 한 건의 소송으로 그 가엾은 사람에게 터무니없는 비용을 물리고도 만족하지 못하고, 나중에는 이른바 판결을 빌미로 또 다른 소송을 걸었다."], ["A method frequently used to oppress the poor, and bring money into the pockets of attorneys, to the great scandal of the law, of the nation, of Christianity, and even of human nature itself.", "이는 가난한 자를 억누르고 변호사들의 주머니를 채우는 데 흔히 쓰이는 수법으로, 법과 나라와 기독교에, 나아가 인간 본성 그 자체에까지 크나큰 수치를 안기는 일이다."]], [["“The first place I came to was Salisbury, where I got into the service of a gentleman belonging to the law, and one of the best gentlemen that ever I knew, for he was not only good to me, but I know a thousand good and charitable acts which he did while I staid with him; and I have known him often refuse business because it was paultry and oppressive.” “You need not be so particular,” said Allworthy; “I know this gentleman, and a very worthy man he is, and an honour to his profession.”--“Well, sir,” continued Partridge, “from hence I removed to Lymington, where I was above three years in the service of another lawyer, who was likewise a very good sort of a man, and to be sure one of the merriest gentlemen in England. Well, sir, at the end of the three years I set up a little school, and was likely to do well again, had it not been for a most unlucky accident. Here I kept a pig; and one day, as ill fortune would have it, this pig broke out, and did a trespass, I think they call it, in a garden belonging to one of my neighbours, who was a proud, revengeful man, and employed a lawyer, one--one--I can't think of his name; but he sent for a writ against me, and had me to size. When I came there, Lord have mercy upon me--to hear what the counsellors said! There was one that told my lord a parcel of the confoundedest lies about me; he said that I used to drive my hogs into other folk's gardens, and a great deal more; and at last he said, he hoped I had at last brought my hogs to a fair market. To be sure, one would have thought that, instead of being owner only of one poor little pig, I had been the greatest hog-merchant in England. Well--” “Pray,” said Allworthy, “do not be so particular, I have heard nothing of your son yet.” “O it was a great many years,” answered Partridge, “before I saw my son, as you are pleased to call him.----I went over to Ireland after this, and taught school at Cork (for that one suit ruined me again, and I lay seven years in Winchester jail).”--“Well,” said Allworthy, “pass that over till your return to England.”--“Then, sir,” said he, “it was about half a year ago that I landed at Bristol, where I staid some time, and not finding it do there, and hearing of a place between that and Gloucester where the barber was just dead, I went thither, and there I had been about two months when Mr Jones came thither.” He then gave Allworthy a very particular account of their first meeting, and of everything, as well as he could remember, which had happened from that day to this; frequently interlarding his story with panegyrics on Jones, and not forgetting to insinuate the great love and respect which he had for Allworthy. He concluded with saying, “Now, sir, I have told your honour the whole truth.” And then repeated a most solemn protestation, “That he was no more the father of Jones than of the Pope of Rome;” and imprecated the most bitter curses on his head, if he did not speak truth.", "“제가 처음 다다른 곳은 솔즈베리였습니다. 거기서 법조계에 몸담은 어느 신사의 시중을 들게 되었는데, 제가 아는 한 가장 훌륭한 신사 가운데 한 분이었습니다. 저에게 잘해 주셨을 뿐 아니라, 제가 모시는 동안 그분이 베푸신 선하고 자비로운 일을 천 가지는 알고 있으니까요. 더구나 일감이 옹졸하고 가혹한 것이면 마다하시는 모습도 여러 번 보았습니다.” “그렇게 시시콜콜 말할 것 없네,” 올워디가 말했다. “나도 그 신사를 알거니와, 참으로 훌륭한 사람이요 그 직업의 영예라 할 만하지.” “그렇습니다, 나리,” 파트리지가 계속했다. “거기서 저는 리밍턴으로 옮겨, 또 다른 변호사 밑에서 삼 년 남짓 일했습니다. 그분 역시 무척 좋은 분이었고, 확실히 잉글랜드에서 가장 유쾌한 신사 가운데 한 분이었지요. 자, 나리, 삼 년이 끝날 무렵 저는 작은 학교를 열었고, 더없이 불운한 사고만 아니었다면 다시 잘 풀릴 참이었습니다. 그때 저는 돼지를 한 마리 길렀는데, 어느 날 하필 운이 나쁘려고 그 돼지가 우리를 뛰쳐나가, 이웃 가운데 한 사람의 정원에 들어가 무슨 침입이라던가 하는 짓을 저질렀습니다. 그 이웃이라는 자가 거만하고 앙심 깊은 사람이라, 변호사를 한 명 고용했는데, 그자가, 그자가, 이름이 도무지 떠오르지 않습니다만, 어쨌든 그자가 저를 상대로 영장을 청구해 저를 순회 재판소에 끌어냈습니다. 제가 거기 갔을 때, 주여 저를 불쌍히 여기소서, 그 변호사들이 늘어놓는 말을 들어 보셨어야 합니다! 그중 하나는 판사 나리께 저에 대해 가장 어처구니없는 거짓말 보따리를 풀어 놓았습니다. 제가 늘 남의 정원으로 돼지를 몰아넣는다는 둥, 그밖에도 잔뜩 늘어놓더니, 마침내는 제가 드디어 돼지를 제값에 팔게 되기를 바란다고까지 했습니다. 정말이지, 그 말만 들으면 제가 가엾고 조그만 돼지 한 마리의 주인이 아니라 잉글랜드에서 가장 큰 돼지 상인이라도 되는 줄 알았을 겁니다. 그래서—” “제발,” 올워디가 말했다. “그렇게 시시콜콜 늘어놓지 말게. 나는 아직 자네 아들 이야기는 한마디도 듣지 못했네.” “아, 그건 아주 여러 해가 지난 뒤였습니다,” 파트리지가 대답했다. “나리께서 굳이 제 아들이라 부르시는 그 사람을 제가 본 것은요. 이 일이 있은 뒤 저는 아일랜드로 건너가 코크에서 학교를 가르쳤습니다(그 한 건의 소송이 저를 또 망쳐 놓아, 저는 윈체스터 감옥에서 칠 년을 살았으니까요).” “그래,” 올워디가 말했다. “그 대목은 건너뛰고 자네가 잉글랜드로 돌아온 때부터 말하게.” “그러면, 나리,” 그가 말했다. “반년쯤 전에 저는 브리스틀에 닿았고, 거기서 얼마간 머물렀습니다만, 그곳에서 일이 잘 풀리지 않았습니다. 그러다 그곳과 글로스터 사이 어느 마을에서 이발사가 막 죽었다는 소식을 듣고 그리로 갔지요. 거기서 두 달쯤 지냈을 무렵 존스 도련님이 그곳에 오신 것입니다.” 그러고 나서 그는 올워디에게 둘이 처음 만난 일과, 기억나는 한 그날부터 오늘까지 일어난 모든 일을 아주 시시콜콜 들려주었다. 이야기 사이사이 존스에 대한 찬사를 자주 끼워 넣었고, 자신이 올워디를 향해 품은 크나큰 사랑과 존경을 넌지시 비치는 것도 잊지 않았다. 그는 이렇게 끝맺었다. “자, 나리, 저는 나리께 모든 진실을 말씀드렸습니다.” 그러고는 더없이 엄숙한 항변을 되풀이했다. “제가 존스의 아비라면 로마 교황의 아비이기도 하다”고. 그리고 자기가 진실을 말하는 것이 아니라면 가장 모진 저주가 제 머리 위에 떨어지기를 빌었다."]], [["“What am I to think of this matter?”", "“이 일을 어떻게 받아들여야 한단 말인가?”"], ["cries Allworthy.", "올워디가 외쳤다."], ["“For what purpose should you so strongly deny a fact which I think it would be rather your interest to own?”", "“인정하는 편이 오히려 자네에게 이로울 듯한 사실을, 무슨 까닭으로 그토록 한사코 부인하는가?”"], ["“Nay, sir,”", "“아니올시다, 나리,”"], ["answered Partridge (for he could hold no longer), “if your honour will not believe me, you are like soon to have satisfaction enough.", "파트리지가 대답했다(더는 참을 수 없었기 때문이다). “나리께서 저를 믿지 않으시겠다면, 머잖아 충분히 납득하시게 될 것입니다."], ["I wish you had mistaken the mother of this young man, as well as you have his father.”--And now being asked what he meant, with all the symptoms of horror, both in his voice and countenance, he told Allworthy the whole story, which he had a little before expressed such desire to Mrs Miller to conceal from him.", "나리께서 이 청년의 아비를 잘못 아신 것처럼, 그 어미도 잘못 아신 것이라면 좋으련만.” 그러자 무슨 뜻이냐는 물음을 받고, 그는 목소리에도 얼굴에도 온갖 공포의 빛을 띤 채, 조금 전 밀러 부인에게 나리께는 부디 숨겨 달라고 그토록 간곡히 청했던 그 이야기를 통째로 올워디에게 털어놓았다."]], [["Allworthy was almost as much shocked at this discovery as Partridge himself had been while he related it.", "올워디는 이 폭로에 거의, 파트리지가 그것을 이야기하는 동안 스스로 받았던 만큼이나 큰 충격을 받았다."], ["“Good heavens!”", "“하느님 맙소사!”"], ["says he, “in what miserable distresses do vice and imprudence involve men!", "그가 말했다. “악덕과 경솔함이 사람을 어떤 비참한 곤경으로 몰아넣는단 말인가!"], ["How much beyond our designs are the effects of wickedness sometimes carried!”", "사악함의 결과가 때로는 우리가 꾀한 것을 얼마나 멀리 넘어서 치닫는단 말인가!”"], ["He had scarce uttered these words, when Mrs Waters came hastily and abruptly into the room.", "이 말을 채 마치기도 전에, 워터스 부인이 황급히, 그리고 느닷없이 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Partridge no sooner saw her than he cried, “Here, sir, here is the very woman herself.", "파트리지는 그녀를 보자마자 외쳤다. “여기, 나리, 바로 그 여인이 여기 있습니다."], ["This is the unfortunate mother of Mr Jones.", "이분이 바로 존스 도련님의 불행한 어머니입니다."], ["I am sure she will acquit me before your honour.", "분명 이분이 나리 앞에서 저의 결백을 밝혀 주실 겁니다."], ["Pray, madam----”", "부탁입니다, 부인—”"]], [["Mrs Waters, without paying any regard to what Partridge said, and almost without taking any notice of him, advanced to Mr Allworthy.", "워터스 부인은 파트리지의 말에는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고, 거의 그를 거들떠보지도 않은 채 올워디 씨에게 다가갔다."], ["“I believe, sir, it is so long since I had the honour of seeing you, that you do not recollect me.”", "“나리, 제가 나리를 뵙는 영광을 누린 지 너무 오래되어, 저를 기억하지 못하시리라 생각합니다.”"], ["“Indeed,”", "“사실이오,”"], ["answered Allworthy, “you are so very much altered, on many accounts, that had not this man already acquainted me who you are, I should not have immediately called you to my remembrance.", "올워디가 대답했다. “부인은 여러모로 너무나 변하셔서, 만일 이 사람이 부인이 누구신지 이미 일러 주지 않았더라면 곧바로 떠올리지는 못했을 것이오."], ["Have you, madam, any particular business which brings you to me?”", "부인, 무슨 특별한 용무가 있어 나를 찾아오셨소?”"], ["Allworthy spoke this with great reserve; for the reader may easily believe he was not well pleased with the conduct of this lady; neither with what he had formerly heard, nor with what Partridge had now delivered.", "올워디는 이 말을 무척 조심스럽게 했다. 독자도 능히 짐작하겠지만, 그는 이 부인의 행실이 — 예전에 들었던 것이든 지금 파트리지가 전한 것이든 — 도무지 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Mrs Waters answered--“Indeed, sir, I have very particular business with you; and it is such as I can impart only to yourself. I must desire, therefore, the favour of a word with you alone: for I assure you what I have to tell you is of the utmost importance.”", "워터스 부인이 대답했다. “나리, 실은 나리께 아주 특별한 용무가 있습니다. 그것도 오직 나리께만 전할 수 있는 일이지요. 그러니 청하건대, 나리와 단둘이 한마디 나눌 수 있는 은혜를 베풀어 주십시오. 제가 말씀드릴 것은 더없이 중대한 일임을 분명히 말씀드립니다.”"]], [["Partridge was then ordered to withdraw, but before he went, he begged the lady to satisfy Mr Allworthy that he was perfectly innocent.", "이에 파트리지는 물러가라는 분부를 받았으나, 나가기 전에 그는 부인에게 자기가 완전히 결백하다는 것을 올워디 씨께 밝혀 달라고 간청했다. 그러자 그녀가 대답했다."], ["To which she answered, “You need be under no apprehension, sir; I shall satisfy Mr Allworthy very perfectly of that matter.”", "“걱정하실 것 없습니다. 그 일에 관해서는 제가 올워디 나리께 아주 완벽하게 밝혀 드리겠습니다.”"]], [["Then Partridge withdrew, and that past between Mr Allworthy and Mrs Waters which is written in the next chapter.", "그러고 나서 파트리지가 물러갔고, 올워디 씨와 워터스 부인 사이에 오간 이야기는 다음 장에 적혀 있다."]], [["Continuation of the history.", "이야기의 계속."]], [["Mrs Waters remaining a few moments silent, Mr Allworthy could not refrain from saying, “I am sorry, madam, to perceive, by what I have since heard, that you have made so very ill a use----” “Mr Allworthy,” says she, interrupting him, “I know I have faults, but ingratitude to you is not one of them. I never can nor shall forget your goodness, which I own I have very little deserved; but be pleased to wave all upbraiding me at present, as I have so important an affair to communicate to you concerning this young man, to whom you have given my maiden name of Jones.”", "워터스 부인이 잠시 말없이 있자, 올워디 씨는 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"부인, 그 뒤로 제가 들은 바로 미루어, 부인께서 그토록 좋지 못한 데에 쓰셨다는 것을 알게 되어 유감스럽습니다——\" \"올워디 씨.\" 그녀가 그의 말을 가로채며 말했다. \"저도 제게 허물이 있음을 압니다. 하지만 당신께 은혜를 모르는 것만큼은 그 허물에 들지 않습니다. 저는 당신의 자비를 결코 잊을 수 없고, 잊지도 않을 것입니다. 그것을 받을 자격이 거의 없었음은 인정합니다. 다만 지금은 저를 나무라는 일은 모두 미뤄 두시기를 청합니다. 당신께서 제 처녀 적 이름인 존스를 붙여 주신 이 젊은이에 관하여, 너무도 중대한 일을 알려 드려야 하기 때문입니다.\""]], [["“Have I then,”", "\"그렇다면 제가.\""], ["said Allworthy, “ignorantly punished an innocent man, in the person of him who hath just left us?", "올워디가 말했다. \"방금 우리 곁을 떠난 그 사람을 두고, 죄 없는 사람을 모르고 벌한 것입니까?"], ["Was he not the father of the child?”", "그가 그 아이의 아버지가 아니었단 말입니까?\""], ["“Indeed he was not,”", "\"정말이지 그는 아니었습니다.\""], ["said Mrs Waters.", "워터스 부인이 말했다."], ["“You may be pleased to remember, sir, I formerly told you, you should one day know; and I acknowledge myself to have been guilty of a cruel neglect, in not having discovered it to you before.", "\"기억하실지 모르겠습니다만, 선생님, 제가 예전에 언젠가는 아시게 될 것이라고 말씀드린 적이 있지요. 그리고 그것을 진작 밝히지 않은 잔인한 태만의 죄를 제가 저질렀음을 인정합니다."], ["Indeed, I little knew how necessary it was.”", "사실 저는 그것이 얼마나 필요한 일인지 거의 알지 못했습니다.\" \"그럼, 부인.\""], ["“Well, madam,”", "올워디가 말했다. \"어서 계속하시지요.\""], ["said Allworthy, “be pleased to proceed.”", "\"기억하셔야 합니다, 선생님.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“You must remember, sir,”", "\"서머라는 이름의 젊은이를 말입니다.\" \"잘 압니다.\""], ["said she, “a young fellow, whose name was Summer.”", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"그는 학식과 덕이 깊은 어느 성직자의 아들이었고, 저는 그분께 더없는 우정을 품고 있었지요.\""], ["“Very well,”", "\"그러셨던 모양입니다, 선생님.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["cries Allworthy, “he was the son of a clergyman of great learning and virtue, for whom I had the highest friendship.”", "\"당신께서 그 젊은이를 길러 주시고 대학까지 학비를 대 주신 것으로 압니다. 그가 당신 댁에 와서 지내게 되었을 무렵에는, 제 생각으로는 학업을 마쳤던 것 같습니다."], ["“So it appeared, sir,”", "정말이지 그보다 더 잘난 사내에게 해가 비친 적은 없었다고 말해야겠습니다."], ["answered she; “for I believe you bred the young man up, and maintained him at the university; where, I think, he had finished his studies, when he came to reside at your house; a finer man, I must say, the sun never shone upon; for, besides the handsomest person I ever saw, he was so genteel, and had so much wit and good breeding.”", "제가 본 가장 잘생긴 사람이었을 뿐 아니라, 어찌나 기품이 있고, 또 재치와 좋은 교양을 갖추었던지요.\" \"가엾은 사람.\""], ["“Poor gentleman,”", "올워디가 말했다. \"그는 정말이지 때 이르게 빼앗기고 말았지요."], ["said Allworthy, “he was indeed untimely snatched away; and little did I think he had any sins of this kind to answer for; for I plainly perceive you are going to tell me he was the father of your child.”", "그가 이런 종류의 죄를 책임져야 하리라고는 조금도 생각지 못했습니다. 부인께서 그가 당신 아이의 아버지였다고 말하려 하심을 분명히 알겠으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 선생님.\""], ["answered she, “he was not.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"그는 아니었습니다.\""], ["“How!”", "\"어찌된 일입니까!\""], ["said Allworthy, “to what then tends all this preface?”", "올워디가 말했다. \"그렇다면 이 모든 서두는 다 무엇을 향한 것입니까?\""], ["“To a story,”", "\"한 이야기입니다.\""], ["said she, “which I am concerned falls to my lot to unfold to you.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신께 풀어 놓는 일이 제 몫으로 떨어진 것이 마음 아픈 그런 이야기지요."], ["O, sir!", "오, 선생님!"], ["prepare to hear something which will surprize you, will grieve you.”", "당신을 놀라게 하고, 또 슬프게 할 무언가를 들으실 마음의 준비를 하십시오.\""], ["“Speak,”", "\"말하시오.\""], ["said Allworthy, “I am conscious of no crime, and cannot be afraid to hear.”", "올워디가 말했다. \"나는 어떤 죄도 짊어진 것이 없으니, 듣기를 두려워할 까닭이 없습니다.\""], ["“Sir,”", "\"선생님.\""], ["said she, “that Mr Summer, the son of your friend, educated at your expense, who, after living a year in the house as if he had been your own son, died there of the small-pox, was tenderly lamented by you, and buried as if he had been your own; that Summer, sir, was the father of this child.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신 벗의 아들이요 당신의 비용으로 교육받았던 그 서머 씨, 마치 당신의 친아들처럼 일 년을 그 댁에서 살다가 그곳에서 천연두로 죽고, 당신께서 따뜻이 애도하시며 친아들처럼 묻어 주신 그 서머 씨, 그 사람이, 선생님, 바로 이 아이의 아버지였습니다.\""], ["“How!”", "\"어찌된 일이오!\""], ["said Allworthy; “you contradict yourself.”", "올워디가 말했다. \"당신은 스스로 모순되는 말을 하고 있소.\""], ["“That I do not,”", "\"그렇지 않습니다.\""], ["answered she; “he was indeed the father of this child, but not by me.”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"그는 정말로 이 아이의 아버지였으나, 저를 통해서가 아니었습니다.\""], ["“Take care, madam,”", "\"조심하시오, 부인.\""], ["said Allworthy, “do not, to shun the imputation of any crime, be guilty of falshood.", "올워디가 말했다. \"어떤 죄의 혐의를 피하려고 거짓을 짓는 죄를 범하지 마시오."], ["Remember there is One from whom you can conceal nothing, and before whose tribunal falshood will only aggravate your guilt.”", "당신이 아무것도 숨길 수 없는 한 분이 계심을, 그리고 그분의 법정 앞에서는 거짓이 당신의 죄를 더 무겁게 할 뿐임을 기억하시오.\" \"정말이지, 선생님.\""], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “I am not his mother; nor would I now think myself so for the world.”", "\"저는 그의 어머니가 아닙니다. 또한 이제 와서 무슨 일이 있어도 제 자신을 그렇게 여기지는 않겠습니다.\""], ["“I know your reason,”", "\"당신의 이유를 압니다.\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “and shall rejoice as much as you to find it otherwise; yet you must remember, you yourself confest it before me.”", "\"그리고 사실이 다르다는 것을 알게 된다면 나도 당신만큼이나 기뻐할 것입니다. 그러나 당신 스스로 내 앞에서 그것을 인정했음을 기억해야 합니다.\""], ["“So far what I confest,”", "\"제가 인정한 것 가운데서 이만큼은 사실이었습니다.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “was true, that these hands conveyed the infant to your bed; conveyed it thither at the command of its mother; at her commands I afterwards owned it, and thought myself, by her generosity, nobly rewarded, both for my secrecy and my shame.”", "\"바로 이 두 손이 그 갓난아이를 당신의 침상으로 옮겼다는 것 말입니다. 그 아이의 어머니가 명한 대로 그곳에 옮겨 놓았고, 그 명령에 따라 나중에 제가 그 아이를 제 아이라 인정했으며, 그분의 너그러움 덕분에 제 침묵과 제 수치에 대해 모두 후하게 보상받았다고 여겼습니다.\""], ["“Who could this woman be?”", "\"그 여인이 대체 누구였소?\""], ["said Allworthy.", "올워디가 말했다."], ["“Indeed, I tremble to name her,”", "\"정말이지, 그분의 이름을 입에 올리기가 떨립니다.\""], ["answered Mrs Waters.", "워터스 부인이 대답했다."], ["“By all this preparation I am to guess that she was a relation of mine,”", "\"이 모든 준비를 보아하니, 그 여인이 내 친척이었다고 짐작해야겠군요.\""], ["cried he.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“Indeed she was a near one.”", "\"정말로 가까운 분이셨습니다.\""], ["At which words Allworthy started, and she continued--“You had a sister, sir.”", "그 말에 올워디가 흠칫 놀랐고, 그녀는 말을 이었다——\"당신께는 누이가 한 분 계셨지요, 선생님.\""], ["“A sister!”", "\"누이라!\""], ["repeated he, looking aghast.--“As there is truth in heaven,”", "그가 아연실색한 얼굴로 되뇌었다.——\"하늘에 진실이 있는 것이 분명하듯이,\""], ["cries she, “your sister was the mother of that child you found between your sheets.”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"당신의 누이가 바로 당신이 침상 시트 사이에서 발견하신 그 아이의 어머니였습니다.\""], ["“Can it be possible?”", "\"그게 가능한 일이오?\""], ["cries he, “Good heavens!”", "그가 외쳤다. \"맙소사!\""], ["“Have patience, sir,”", "\"참으십시오, 선생님.\""], ["said Mrs Waters, “and I will unfold to you the whole story.", "워터스 부인이 말했다. \"제가 그 모든 이야기를 풀어 드리겠습니다."], ["Just after your departure for London, Miss Bridget came one day to the house of my mother.", "당신께서 런던으로 떠나신 직후, 어느 날 브리짓 아씨가 제 어머니의 집으로 오셨습니다."], ["She was pleased to say she had heard an extraordinary character of me, for my learning and superior understanding to all the young women there, so she was pleased to say.", "그분은 제가 학식과, 그곳의 어느 젊은 여인들보다도 뛰어난 이해력으로 비범한 평판을 얻고 있다는 말을 들으셨다고, 친절히 말씀해 주셨습니다."], ["She then bid me come to her to the great house; where, when I attended, she employed me to read to her.", "그러고는 큰 저택으로 자기를 찾아오라 하셨고, 제가 거기 들자 책을 읽어 달라 시키셨습니다."], ["She expressed great satisfaction in my reading, shewed great kindness to me, and made me many presents.", "그분은 제 낭독에 큰 만족을 보이시고, 제게 많은 친절을 베푸시며 여러 선물을 주셨습니다."], ["At last she began to catechise me on the subject of secrecy, to which I gave her such satisfactory answers, that, at last, having locked the door of her room, she took me into her closet, and then locking that door likewise, she said she should convince me of the vast reliance she had on my integrity, by communicating a secret in which her honour, and consequently her life, was concerned.", "마침내 그분은 비밀을 지키는 일에 관해 저를 시험하듯 캐물으셨고, 제가 어찌나 흡족한 대답을 드렸던지, 결국에는 방문을 잠그시더니 저를 자신의 골방으로 데려가셨습니다. 그 방문마저 잠그신 다음, 그분은 자신의 명예가, 따라서 자신의 목숨이 걸린 비밀 하나를 털어놓음으로써 제 정직함에 얼마나 큰 의지를 두고 계신지 보여 주겠노라 하셨습니다."], ["She then stopt, and after a silence of a few minutes, during which she often wiped her eyes, she enquired of me if I thought my mother might safely be confided in.", "그러고는 멈추셨고, 몇 분 동안의 침묵 끝에—그동안 자주 눈물을 훔치셨지요—제 어머니를 안심하고 믿어도 되겠느냐고 제게 물으셨습니다."], ["I answered, I would stake my life on her fidelity.", "저는 어머니의 충직함에 제 목숨을 걸겠노라고 대답했습니다."], ["She then imparted to me the great secret which laboured in her breast, and which, I believe, was delivered with more pains than she afterwards suffered in child-birth.", "그러자 그분은 가슴속에서 진통처럼 끓던 그 크나큰 비밀을 제게 털어놓으셨는데, 제 생각에 그것은 그분이 나중에 해산할 때 겪으신 것보다 더한 고통과 함께 쏟아져 나온 것이었습니다. 그리하여 그 일이 있을 때에는 오직 제 어머니와 저만이 곁을 지키고, 윌킨스 부인은 멀리 떨어진 곳으로 보내기로 짜였습니다."], ["It was then contrived that my mother and myself only should attend at the time, and that Mrs Wilkins should be sent out of the way, as she accordingly was, to the very furthest part of Dorsetshire, to enquire the character of a servant; for the lady had turned away her own maid near three months before; during all which time I officiated about her person upon trial, as she said, though, as she afterwards declared, I was not sufficiently handy for the place.", "실제로 그분은 도싯셔의 가장 먼 곳까지, 어느 하인의 평판을 알아보라며 윌킨스 부인을 보내셨지요. 그 아씨는 석 달쯤 전에 자기 시녀를 내보내셨고, 그동안 내내, 아씨 말씀으로는 시험 삼아, 제가 그분의 시중을 들었습니다."], ["This, and many other such things which she used to say of me, were all thrown out to prevent any suspicion which Wilkins might hereafter have, when I was to own the child; for she thought it could never be believed she would venture to hurt a young woman with whom she had intrusted such a secret.", "비록 나중에 그분께서는 제가 그 자리에 충분히 손이 야무지지 못하다고 말씀하셨지만요. 이 말이며, 그분이 저에 관해 늘 하시던 다른 여러 말들은, 나중에 제가 그 아이를 제 아이라 인정할 때 윌킨스가 품을지도 모를 의심을 미리 막으려고 던지신 것이었습니다."], ["You may be assured, sir, I was well paid for all these affronts, which, together with being informed with the occasion of them, very well contented me.", "그런 비밀을 맡긴 젊은 여인에게 굳이 해를 끼치려 들리라고는 누구도 결코 믿지 않으리라 여기셨기 때문이지요. 선생님, 분명히 말씀드리지만, 저는 이 모든 모욕에 대해 후하게 보상받았고, 그 모욕의 까닭까지 들어 알게 되었으니 매우 만족스러웠습니다."], ["Indeed, the lady had a greater suspicion of Mrs Wilkins than of any other person; not that she had the least aversion to the gentlewoman, but she thought her incapable of keeping a secret, especially from you, sir; for I have often heard Miss Bridget say, that, if Mrs Wilkins had committed a murder, she believed she would acquaint you with it.", "사실 그 아씨는 다른 어느 누구보다 윌킨스 부인을 더 의심하셨습니다. 그 부인을 조금이라도 싫어하셔서가 아니라, 그 부인이 비밀을 지키지 못하는 사람, 특히 당신께는 더욱 그러한 사람이라고 여기셨기 때문입니다."], ["At last the expected day came, and Mrs Wilkins, who had been kept a week in readiness, and put off from time to time, upon some pretence or other, that she might not return too soon, was dispatched.", "브리짓 아씨께서 자주 하시던 말씀이, 윌킨스 부인이 살인을 저질렀다 해도 결국 당신께 그 일을 일러바칠 것이라 하셨으니까요. 마침내 기다리던 날이 왔고, 일주일 동안 채비를 갖춘 채 대기하던 윌킨스 부인은, 너무 일찍 돌아오지 못하도록 이런저런 핑계로 거듭 출발이 미뤄지다가, 드디어 보내졌습니다."], ["Then the child was born, in the presence only of myself and my mother, and was by my mother conveyed to her own house, where it was privately kept by her till the evening of your return, when I, by the command of Miss Bridget, conveyed it into the bed where you found it.", "그러고서 아이가 태어났는데, 오직 저와 제 어머니만이 곁에 있었고, 제 어머니가 그 아이를 자기 집으로 데려가 당신께서 돌아오시는 날 저녁까지 몰래 두었습니다. 그날 저녁 저는 브리짓 아씨의 명에 따라 그 아이를, 당신께서 발견하신 그 침상으로 옮겨 놓았습니다."], ["And all suspicions were afterwards laid asleep by the artful conduct of your sister, in pretending ill-will to the boy, and that any regard she shewed him was out of meer complacence to you.”", "그리고 그 뒤로 모든 의심은, 당신 누이의 교묘한 처신으로 잠재워졌지요. 그 사내아이에게 짐짓 미운 마음을 품은 척하시고, 그 아이에게 보이신 어떤 보살핌도 다만 당신을 기쁘게 해 드리려는 것일 뿐인 양 꾸미셨으니까요.\""]], [["Mrs Waters then made many protestations of the truth of this story, and concluded by saying, “Thus, sir, you have at last discovered your nephew; for so I am sure you will hereafter think him, and I question not but he will be both an honour and a comfort to you under that appellation.”", "워터스 부인은 이어서 이 이야기가 진실임을 여러 차례 다짐했고, 이렇게 맺었다. \"이리하여, 선생님, 당신께서는 마침내 당신의 조카를 찾으신 것입니다. 분명 앞으로는 그를 그렇게 여기시게 되리라 믿으며, 그가 그 호칭 아래에서 당신께 명예이자 위안이 되리라는 것을 저는 의심치 않습니다.\""]], [["“I need not, madam,”", "\"부인, 굳이,\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “express my astonishment at what you have told me; and yet surely you would not, and could not, have put together so many circumstances to evidence an untruth.", "\"당신께서 들려주신 이야기에 대한 제 놀라움을 표현할 필요는 없겠지요. 그렇지만 분명히, 거짓을 증명하려고 이토록 많은 정황을 엮어 내려 하지도, 또 엮어 낼 수도 없었을 것입니다."], ["I confess I recollect some passages relating to that Summer, which formerly gave me a conceit that my sister had some liking to him.", "솔직히 말해, 그 서머에 관한 몇 가지 일들이 떠오릅니다. 예전에 제 누이가 그에게 호감을 품고 있는 것이 아닌가 하는 생각을 갖게 한 일들 말입니다."], ["I mentioned it to her; for I had such a regard to the young man, as well on his own account as on his father's, that I should willingly have consented to a match between them; but she exprest the highest disdain of my unkind suspicion, as she called it; so that I never spoke more on the subject.", "저는 그것을 누이에게 말했습니다. 저는 그 젊은이를, 그 자신을 위해서나 그의 아버지를 위해서나 무척 아꼈으므로, 둘의 혼인을 기꺼이 허락했을 것이기 때문이지요."], ["Good heavens!", "그러나 누이는 자기가 말하는 그 '매정한 의심'에 대해 더없는 경멸을 드러냈고, 그래서 저는 그 이야기를 다시는 입에 올리지 않았습니다."], ["Well!", "맙소사!"], ["the Lord disposeth all things.--Yet sure it was a most unjustifiable conduct in my sister to carry this secret with her out of the world.”", "아! 주께서 모든 일을 정하시는구나.——그렇더라도, 누이가 이 비밀을 안고 세상을 떠난 것은 도무지 변명할 수 없는 처사였습니다.\""], ["“I promise you, sir,”", "\"맹세코 말씀드립니다만, 선생님.\""], ["said Mrs Waters, “she always profest a contrary intention, and frequently told me she intended one day to communicate it to you.", "워터스 부인이 말했다. \"그분은 늘 그와 반대되는 뜻을 밝히셨고, 언젠가는 당신께 그것을 알려 드릴 작정이라고 제게 자주 말씀하셨습니다."], ["She said, indeed, she was highly rejoiced that her plot had succeeded so well, and that you had of your own accord taken such a fancy to the child, that it was yet unnecessary to make any express declaration.", "사실 그분은 자신의 계략이 그토록 잘 들어맞았다는 것과, 당신께서 스스로 그 아이를 그토록 어여삐 여기게 되셨다는 것이 무척 기쁘다고 하셨지요. 그러니 아직은 굳이 분명한 선언을 할 필요가 없다고 말입니다."], ["Oh!", "오! 선생님, 그 아씨가 살아 계셔서 이 가엾은 젊은이가 부랑자처럼 당신 댁에서 쫓겨나는 꼴을 보셨더라면."], ["sir, had that lady lived to have seen this poor young man turned like a vagabond from your house: nay, sir, could she have lived to hear that you had yourself employed a lawyer to prosecute him for a murder of which he was not guilty----Forgive me, Mr Allworthy, I must say it was unkind.--Indeed, you have been abused, he never deserved it of you.”", "아니, 선생님, 그가 저지르지도 않은 살인죄로 그를 기소하라고 당신께서 손수 변호사를 부리셨다는 말을 들으실 때까지 살아 계셨더라면——용서하십시오, 올워디 씨, 그것은 매정한 일이었다고 말해야겠습니다.——정말이지 당신께서는 속으셨던 것입니다. 그는 결코 당신께 그런 대접을 받을 사람이 아니었습니다.\""], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"정말이지, 부인.\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “I have been abused by the person, whoever he was, that told you so.”", "\"그렇게 당신께 말한 사람이 누구든, 저는 그자에게 속은 것입니다.\" \"아니요, 선생님.\""], ["“Nay, sir,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"오해를 사고 싶지 않습니다."], ["said she, “I would not be mistaken, I did not presume to say you were guilty of any wrong.", "당신께서 무슨 그릇된 짓을 저지르셨다고 감히 말씀드린 것은 아닙니다. 저를 찾아온 그 신사는 그런 식의 말은 하지 않았습니다."], ["The gentleman who came to me proposed no such matter; he only said, taking me for Mr Fitzpatrick's wife, that, if Mr Jones had murdered my husband, I should be assisted with any money I wanted to carry on the prosecution, by a very worthy gentleman, who, he said, was well apprized what a villain I had to deal with.", "그는 다만, 저를 피츠패트릭 씨의 부인으로 알고서, 만일 존스 씨가 제 남편을 살해했다면, 매우 훌륭한 어느 신사분의 도움으로 제가 기소를 진행하는 데 필요한 돈을 얼마든지 받게 될 것이라고 했을 뿐입니다. 그분은 제가 상대해야 할 자가 얼마나 악당인지 잘 알고 계시다고 그가 말하더군요."], ["It was by this man I found out who Mr Jones was; and this man, whose name is Dowling, Mr Jones tells me is your steward.", "제가 존스 씨가 누구인지 알아낸 것도 바로 이 사람을 통해서였습니다. 그리고 이 사람, 이름이 다울링이라는데, 존스 씨 말로는 당신의 집사라더군요."], ["I discovered his name by a very odd accident; for he himself refused to tell it me; but Partridge, who met him at my lodgings the second time he came, knew him formerly at Salisbury.”", "저는 아주 묘한 우연으로 그의 이름을 알게 되었습니다. 그 자신은 제게 이름 밝히기를 거부했으나, 그가 제 숙소에 두 번째로 왔을 때 마주친 파트리지가 예전에 솔즈베리에서 그를 알고 있었기 때문이지요.\""]], [["“And did this Mr Dowling,”", "\"그래서 이 다울링이라는 자가,\""], ["says Allworthy, with great astonishment in his countenance, “tell you that I would assist in the prosecution?”--“No, sir,”", "올워디가 얼굴에 큰 놀라움을 띤 채 말했다. \"내가 그 기소를 돕겠다고 당신께 말했단 말입니까?\"——\"아니요, 선생님.\""], ["answered she, “I will not charge him wrongfully.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"그분께 부당한 혐의를 씌우지는 않겠습니다."], ["He said I should be assisted, but he mentioned no name.", "그는 제가 도움을 받게 될 것이라고만 했지, 어떤 이름도 입에 올리지 않았습니다."], ["Yet you must pardon me, sir, if from circumstances I thought it could be no other.”--“Indeed, madam,”", "그러나 용서하십시오, 선생님. 정황으로 미루어 다른 사람일 리 없다고 생각한 것이니까요.\"——\"정말이지, 부인.\""], ["says Allworthy, “from circumstances I am too well convinced it was another.", "올워디가 말했다. \"저야말로 정황으로 미루어, 그것이 다른 자였음을 너무도 확실히 믿게 되었습니다."], ["Good Heaven!", "맙소사!"], ["by what wonderful means is the blackest and deepest villany sometimes discovered!--Shall I beg you, madam, to stay till the person you have mentioned comes, for I expect him every minute?", "가장 검고 깊은 악행이 때로는 얼마나 놀라운 방법으로 드러나는가!——부인, 당신께서 말씀하신 그 사람이 올 때까지 머물러 주시기를 청해도 되겠습니까? 그를 시시각각 기다리고 있으니까요."], ["nay, he may be, perhaps, already in the house.”", "아니, 어쩌면 그가 이미 이 집 안에 와 있을지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["Allworthy then stept to the door, in order to call a servant, when in came, not Mr Dowling, but the gentleman who will be seen in the next chapter.", "그러고서 올워디는 하인을 부르려고 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼는데, 그때 들어온 사람은 다울링 씨가 아니라, 다음 장에서 보게 될 그 신사였다."]], [["Further continuation. The gentleman who now arrived was no other than Mr Western.", "지금 도착한 신사는 다름 아닌 웨스턴 씨였다."], ["He no sooner saw Allworthy, than, without considering in the least the presence of Mrs Waters, he began to vociferate in the following manner: “Fine doings at my house!", "그는 올워디를 보자마자, 워터스 부인이 그 자리에 있다는 것은 조금도 개의치 않고 다음과 같이 고래고래 소리치기 시작했다. \"내 집에서 아주 볼 만한 일이 벌어졌네!"], ["A rare kettle of fish I have discovered at last!", "마침내 기막힌 꼴을 찾아냈어! 도대체 어떤 작자가 딸년 때문에 이렇게 시달려야 한단 말인가?\""], ["who the devil would be plagued with a daughter?”", "\"무슨 일이오, 이웃 양반?\" 올워디가 물었다."], ["“What's the matter, neighbour?” said Allworthy.", "\"무슨 일이라니, 일이 차고 넘치오.\""], ["“Matter enough,”", "웨스턴이 대답했다."], ["answered Western: “when I thought she was just a coming to; nay, when she had in a manner promised me to do as I would ha her, and when I was a hoped to have had nothing more to do than to have sent for the lawyer, and finished all; what do you think I have found out?", "\"이제 막 그년이 마음을 돌리는가 싶었을 때, 아니, 사실상 내 뜻대로 하겠노라 약속까지 한 마당에, 변호사를 불러 모든 걸 끝맺기만 하면 더 할 일이 없겠거니 바라고 있던 그 순간에, 내가 무얼 알아냈는지 아시오? 그 어린 계집년이 그동안 내내 나를 속여먹고, 당신네 그 사생아 놈과 편지질을 하고 있었다지 뭐요."], ["that the little b-- hath bin playing tricks with me all the while, and carrying on a correspondence with that bastard of yours.", "그년 때문에 다투기까지 한 웨스턴 누이가 내게 그 사실을 일러주었고, 나는 그년이 잠든 사이 호주머니를 뒤지게 했더니, 여기 그 매춘부 자식 놈 이름이 떡하니 서명된 편지를 손에 넣었소. 절반도 채 읽을 인내심이 없었는데, 서플 목사의 설교보다 더 길더란 말이오."], ["Sister Western, whom I have quarrelled with upon her account, sent me word o't, and I ordered her pockets to be searched when she was asleep, and here I have got un signed with the son of a whore's own name.", "하지만 죄다 사랑 타령인 건 분명히 알겠더이다. 사실 그것 말고 뭐가 더 있겠소?"], ["I have not had patience to read half o't, for 'tis longer than one of parson Supple's sermons; but I find plainly it is all about love; and indeed what should it be else?", "그년을 다시 방에 가둬놨고, 내일 아침엔 곧장 시골로 내려보낼 작정이오. 당장 결혼하겠다고 동의하지 않는 한 말이오."], ["I have packed her up in chamber again, and to-morrow morning down she goes into the country, unless she consents to be married directly, and there she shall live in a garret upon bread and water all her days; and the sooner such a b-- breaks her heart the better, though, d--n her, that I believe is too tough.", "거기서 평생 다락방에 갇혀 빵과 물만 먹고 살게 할 거요. 그런 계집년이 가슴이 미어져 일찍 죽으면 죽을수록 좋겠지만, 빌어먹을, 그년은 너무 질겨서 그럴 것 같지도 않소."], ["She will live long enough to plague me.”", "나를 괴롭히려고 아주 오래 살 게요.\" \"웨스턴 씨,\""], ["“Mr Western,”", "올워디가 대답했다."], ["answered Allworthy, “you know I have always protested against force, and you yourself consented that none should be used.”", "\"내가 늘 강압에는 반대해왔다는 걸 당신도 알지 않소. 그리고 당신 스스로도 어떤 강압도 쓰지 않겠다 동의했소.\""], ["“Ay,”", "\"그야,\""], ["cries he, “that was only upon condition that she would consent without.", "그가 외쳤다. \"그건 그년이 강압 없이도 동의한다는 조건에서나 그랬던 거지."], ["What the devil and doctor Faustus!", "빌어먹을 파우스트 박사를 두고 맹세하건대, 내 딸을 내 마음대로 못한단 말이오?"], ["shan't I do what I will with my own daughter, especially when I desire nothing but her own good?”", "그것도 그년 자신의 행복 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않는 마당에?\" \"그럼, 이웃 양반,\""], ["“Well, neighbour,”", "올워디가 대답했다."], ["answered Allworthy, “if you will give me leave, I will undertake once to argue with the young lady.”", "\"내게 허락해주신다면, 내가 한번 그 아가씨와 이야기를 나눠보겠소.\" \"그래주시겠소?\""], ["“Will you?”", "웨스턴이 말했다. \"그거 참 친절하고 이웃다운 일이오."], ["said Western; “why that is kind now, and neighbourly, and mayhap you will do more than I have been able to do with her; for I promise you she hath a very good opinion of you.”", "어쩌면 당신이 내가 그년한테 못한 일을 해낼지도 모르겠구려. 장담컨대 그년은 당신을 무척 좋게 여기니까.\""], ["“Well, sir,”", "\"좋소, 그럼.\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “if you will go home, and release the young lady from her captivity, I will wait upon her within this half-hour.”", "\"댁으로 돌아가셔서 그 아가씨를 감금에서 풀어주시오. 내가 반 시간 안에 찾아뵙겠소.\""], ["“But suppose,”", "\"한데 그사이,\" 웨스턴이 말했다."], ["said Western, “she should run away with un in the meantime?", "\"그년이 그놈과 도망쳐버리면 어쩐단 말이오? 다울링 변호사 말로는 결국 그놈을 교수형에 처할 가망은 없다는데."], ["For lawyer Dowling tells me there is no hopes of hanging the fellow at last; for that the man is alive, and like to do well, and that he thinks Jones will be out of prison again presently.”", "그 사내가 살아 있고, 호전될 것 같다고 하더이다. 그러니 존스가 곧 다시 감옥에서 풀려날 거라는 게요.\""], ["“How!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["said Allworthy; “what, did you employ him then to enquire or to do anything in that matter?”", "올워디가 말했다. \"그럼 당신이 그 사람을 시켜 그 문제를 알아보거나 무슨 일을 하게 했단 말이오?\""], ["“Not I,”", "\"내가 그런 게 아니오.\""], ["answered Western, “he mentioned it to me just now of his own accord.”", "웨스턴이 대답했다. \"방금 그 사람이 제 발로 내게 그 얘기를 꺼냈소.\""], ["“Just now!”", "\"방금이라니!\""], ["cries Allworthy, “why, where did you see him then?", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"아니, 그럼 그 사람을 어디서 보셨소?"], ["I want much to see Mr Dowling.”", "나는 다울링 씨를 몹시 만나고 싶소만.\""], ["“Why, you may see un an you will presently at my lodgings; for there is to be a meeting of lawyers there this morning about a mortgage.", "\"원한다면 지금 당장 내 숙소에서 만날 수 있소. 오늘 아침 거기서 저당 잡힌 일로 변호사들 모임이 있을 참이니."], ["'Icod!", "빌어먹을!"], ["I shall lose two or dree thousand pounds, I believe, by that honest gentleman, Mr Nightingale.”", "그 정직하신 신사 나이팅게일 씨 덕분에, 내 보아하니 이삼천 파운드는 날리게 생겼소이다.\" \"좋소, 그럼.\""], ["“Well, sir,”", "올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “I will be with you within the half-hour.”", "\"반 시간 안에 댁으로 가겠소.\" \"그리고 이번 한 번만,\""], ["“And do for once,”", "향사가 외쳤다. \"어리석은 사람 충고를 들으시오."], ["cries the squire, “take a fool's advice; never think of dealing with her by gentle methods, take my word for it those will never do.", "그년을 부드러운 방법으로 다룰 생각일랑 절대 마시오. 내 말 믿으시오, 그런 건 절대 안 통하오."], ["I have tried 'um long enough.", "나는 그걸 충분히 오래 시도해봤소."], ["She must be frightened into it, there is no other way.", "겁을 줘서 몰아넣어야 하오. 다른 길은 없소."], ["Tell her I'm her father; and of the horrid sin of disobedience, and of the dreadful punishment of it in t'other world, and then tell her about being locked up all her life in a garret in this, and being kept only on bread and water.”", "내가 제 아비라는 걸 일러주고, 불효라는 끔찍한 죄와 저세상에서 그 죄에 따를 무서운 벌을 말해주시오. 그러고 나서 이 세상에서는 평생 다락방에 갇혀 빵과 물만 먹고 산다는 것도 일러주시오.\""], ["“I will do all I can,”", "\"내 할 수 있는 건 다 하겠소.\""], ["said Allworthy; “for I promise you there is nothing I wish for more than an alliance with this amiable creature.”", "올워디가 말했다. \"장담컨대, 이 사랑스러운 아이와 사돈을 맺는 것보다 내가 더 바라는 일은 없으니 말이오.\""], ["“Nay, the girl is well enough for matter o' that,”", "\"원, 뭐 그런 거라면 그 아이도 그만하면 괜찮지.\""], ["cries the squire; “a man may go farther and meet with worse meat; that I may declare o'her, thof she be my own daughter.", "향사가 외쳤다. \"더 멀리 찾아가봤자 더 못한 고기나 만날 뿐이오."], ["And if she will but be obedient to me, there is narrow a father within a hundred miles o'", "내 딸이긴 하다만 그 정도는 내 단언할 수 있소. 그년이 내게 순종하기만 한다면, 이 일대 백 마일 안에 나만큼 딸을 사랑하는 아비는 없을 게요."], ["the place, that loves a daughter better than I do; but I see you are busy with the lady here, so I will go huome and expect you; and so your humble servant.”", "한데 보아하니 당신은 여기 이 부인과 볼일이 있는 듯하니, 나는 집으로 가서 당신을 기다리리다. 그럼, 이만 실례하오.\""]], [["As soon as Mr Western was gone Mrs Waters said, “I see, sir, the squire hath not the least remembrance of my face.", "웨스턴 씨가 떠나자마자 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"보아하니, 어르신, 저 향사께서는 제 얼굴을 조금도 기억하지 못하시는군요."], ["I believe, Mr Allworthy, you would not have known me neither.", "제 생각엔, 올워디 어르신, 어르신께서도 저를 못 알아보셨을 거예요. 어르신께서 그토록 친절하게 충고를 해주셨던 그날 이후로 저는 무척이나 많이 변했답니다."], ["I am very considerably altered since that day when you so kindly gave me that advice, which I had been happy had I followed.”", "그 충고를 따랐더라면 저는 행복했을 텐데요.\" \"정말이지, 부인.\""], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"그 반대였다는 걸 처음 들었을 때 나는 무척 마음이 아팠소.\""], ["cries Allworthy, “it gave me great concern when I first heard the contrary.”", "\"사실은요, 어르신.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"저는 아주 깊은 악당의 계략에 걸려 신세를 망쳤답니다. 그 내막을 아신다면, 비록 그것이 어르신의 눈에 저를 떳떳하게 해주리라고는 감히 생각지 않지만, 적어도 제 잘못을 덜어주고, 어르신께서 저를 가엾이 여기시도록 만들 거예요."], ["says she, “I was ruined by a very deep scheme of villany, which if you knew, though I pretend not to think it would justify me in your opinion, it would at least mitigate my offence, and induce you to pity me: you are not now at leisure to hear my whole story; but this I assure you, I was betrayed by the most solemn promises of marriage; nay, in the eye of heaven I was married to him; for, after much reading on the subject, I am convinced that particular ceremonies are only requisite to give a legal sanction to marriage, and have only a worldly use in giving a woman the privileges of a wife; but that she who lives constant to one man, after a solemn private affiance, whatever the world may call her, hath little to charge on her own conscience.”", "지금은 제 이야기를 전부 들으실 겨를이 없으시겠지요. 하지만 이것만은 분명히 말씀드릴게요."], ["“I am sorry, madam,”", "저는 더없이 엄숙한 결혼 약속에 속아 넘어갔습니다. 아니, 하늘의 눈으로 보자면 저는 그와 결혼한 몸이었어요."], ["said Allworthy, “you made so ill a use of your learning.", "그 주제에 관해 많은 책을 읽고 나서, 저는 특정한 의식이란 결혼에 법적 효력을 부여하기 위해서만 필요한 것이며, 여자에게 아내의 권리를 주는 세속적인 쓸모만 있을 뿐이라고 확신하게 되었거든요. 엄숙한 사적 약혼을 한 뒤 한 남자에게 변함없이 지조를 지킨 여자라면, 세상이 그녀를 무어라 부르든, 제 양심에 떳떳지 못할 게 거의 없다고요.\""], ["Indeed, it would have been well that you had been possessed of much more, or had remained in a state of ignorance.", "\"유감이오, 부인.\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["And yet, madam, I am afraid you have more than this sin to answer for.”", "\"당신이 학식을 그토록 그릇되게 썼다는 것이 말이오. 차라리 훨씬 더 많이 배웠거나, 아니면 아무것도 모르는 상태로 남아 있었더라면 좋았을 거요."], ["“During his life,”", "하지만 부인, 안타깝게도 당신에겐 이 죄말고도 책임져야 할 죄가 더 있는 것 같소만.\" \"그가 살아 있던 동안에는,\""], ["answered she, “which was above a dozen years, I most solemnly assure you I had not.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"십이 년 남짓 되는 그 세월 동안에는, 정말이지 엄숙하게 맹세하건대 그런 일이 없었어요."], ["And consider, sir, on my behalf, what is in the power of a woman stript of her reputation and left destitute; whether the good-natured world will suffer such a stray sheep to return to the road of virtue, even if she was never so desirous.", "그리고 어르신, 제 처지에서 헤아려주세요. 명예를 빼앗기고 빈털터리로 버려진 여자에게 무슨 힘이 있겠어요."], ["I protest, then, I would have chose it had it been in my power; but necessity drove me into the arms of Captain Waters, with whom, though still unmarried, I lived as a wife for many years, and went by his name.", "인심 좋은 세상이 그런 길 잃은 양 한 마리를, 설령 그녀가 아무리 간절히 바란다 한들, 덕의 길로 되돌아오게 내버려두겠어요? 그러니 단언컨대, 제게 그럴 힘이 있었다면 저는 그 길을 택했을 거예요."], ["I parted with this gentleman at Worcester, on his march against the rebels, and it was then I accidentally met with Mr Jones, who rescued me from the hands of a villain.", "하지만 궁핍이 저를 워터스 대위의 품으로 내몰았고, 그와는 여전히 정식으로 혼인하지 않은 채로 여러 해를 아내처럼 살며 그의 이름으로 통했답니다. 저는 우스터에서 그 신사와 헤어졌어요."], ["Indeed, he is the worthiest of men.", "그가 반란군을 치러 진군하던 때였지요. 그리고 바로 그때 우연히 존스 씨를 만나게 되었는데, 그분이 어느 악당의 손아귀에서 저를 구해주셨어요."], ["No young gentleman of his age is, I believe, freer from vice, and few have the twentieth part of his virtues; nay, whatever vices he hath had, I am firmly persuaded he hath now taken a resolution to abandon them.”", "정말이지 그분은 더없이 훌륭한 분이에요. 제 생각에 그 나이 또래의 젊은 신사 가운데 그분만큼 악덕에서 자유로운 이는 없고, 그분이 지닌 미덕의 이십 분의 일이라도 가진 이는 드물 거예요."], ["“I hope he hath,”", "아니, 그분에게 어떤 악덕이 있었든 간에, 저는 그분이 이제 그것들을 버리기로 결심하셨다고 굳게 믿어요.\" \"나도 그러기를 바라오.\""], ["cries Allworthy, “and I hope he will preserve that resolution.", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그 결심을 지켜가기를 바라오."], ["I must say, I have still the same hopes with regard to yourself.", "말해두건대, 나는 당신 자신에 대해서도 여전히 같은 희망을 품고 있소. 세상이 이런 경우에 너무 매정하게 구는 경향이 있다는 데는 나도 동의하오."], ["The world, I do agree, are apt to be too unmerciful on these occasions; yet time and perseverance will get the better of this their disinclination, as I may call it, to pity; for though they are not, like heaven, ready to receive a penitent sinner; yet a continued repentance will at length obtain mercy even with the world.", "하지만 세월과 끈기는 결국 동정을 꺼리는, 말하자면 그 인색한 마음을 이겨낼 거요. 세상은 하늘처럼 뉘우치는 죄인을 선뜻 받아들이지는 않지만, 그래도 끊임없는 참회는 마침내 세상에서조차 자비를 얻어내게 될 테니 말이오."], ["This you may be assured of, Mrs Waters, that whenever I find you are sincere in such good intentions, you shall want no assistance in my power to make them effectual.”", "이것만은 믿어도 좋소, 워터스 부인. 당신이 그러한 선한 뜻에 진실하다는 것을 내가 확인하는 날에는, 그것을 이루는 데 내 힘이 닿는 도움이라면 무엇 하나 부족함이 없을 거요.\""]], [["Mrs Waters fell now upon her knees before him, and, in a flood of tears, made him many most passionate acknowledgments of his goodness, which, as she truly said, savoured more of the divine than human nature.", "워터스 부인은 이제 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 그의 선량함에 대해 더없이 격정적인 감사의 말을 거듭 쏟아냈다. 그녀가 진심으로 말했듯이, 그 선량함은 인간의 본성보다는 신의 본성에 더 가까운 것이었다."]], [["Allworthy raised her up, and spoke in the most tender manner, making use of every expression which his invention could suggest to comfort her, when he was interrupted by the arrival of Mr Dowling, who, upon his first entrance, seeing Mrs Waters, started, and appeared in some confusion; from which he soon recovered himself as well as he could, and then said he was in the utmost haste to attend counsel at Mr Western's lodgings; but, however, thought it his duty to call and acquaint him with the opinion of counsel upon the case which he had before told him, which was that the conversion of the moneys in that case could not be questioned in a criminal cause, but that an action of trover might be brought, and if it appeared to the jury to be the moneys of plaintiff, that plaintiff would recover a verdict for the value.", "올워디는 그녀를 일으켜 세우고는, 더없이 다정한 태도로 말하며, 그녀를 위로하기 위해 자신의 머리가 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 표현을 다 동원했다. 그때 다울링 씨가 도착하는 바람에 그의 말이 끊겼다. 다울링은 들어서자마자 워터스 부인을 보고 흠칫 놀라며 다소 당황한 기색을 보였으나, 곧 되도록 침착하게 자세를 가다듬고는, 웨스턴 씨의 숙소에서 변호사들과 만나야 해서 몹시 바쁘지만, 그래도 들러서 이전에 말씀드린 그 사건에 대한 법률 자문의 견해를 알려드리는 것이 자신의 도리라 여겼다고 말했다. 그 견해란, 그 경우 돈의 횡령은 형사 사건으로는 문제 삼을 수 없으나, 동산 점유 회복 소송을 제기할 수는 있으며, 만일 그 돈이 원고의 것임이 배심원에게 명백히 드러난다면, 원고가 그 가액에 대한 평결을 받아낼 수 있으리라는 것이었다."]], [["Allworthy, without making any answer to this, bolted the door, and then, advancing with a stern look to Dowling, he said, “Whatever be your haste, sir, I must first receive an answer to some questions. Do you know this lady?”--“That lady, sir!” answered Dowling, with great hesitation. Allworthy then, with the most solemn voice, said, “Look you, Mr Dowling, as you value my favour, or your continuance a moment longer in my service, do not hesitate nor prevaricate; but answer faithfully and truly to every question I ask.----Do you know this lady?”--“Yes, sir,” said Dowling, “I have seen the lady.” “Where, sir?” “At her own lodgings.”--“Upon what business did you go thither, sir; and who sent you?” “I went, sir, to enquire, sir, about Mr Jones.” “And who sent you to enquire about him?” “Who, sir? why, sir, Mr Blifil sent me.” “And what did you say to the lady concerning that matter?” “Nay, sir, it is impossible to recollect every word.” “Will you please, madam, to assist the gentleman's memory?” “He told me, sir,” said Mrs Waters, “that if Mr Jones had murdered my husband, I should be assisted by any money I wanted to carry on the prosecution, by a very worthy gentleman, who was well apprized what a villain I had to deal with. These, I can safely swear, were the very words he spoke.”--“Were these the words, sir?” said Allworthy. “I cannot charge my memory exactly,” cries Dowling, “but I believe I did speak to that purpose.”--“And did Mr Blifil order you to say so?” “I am sure, sir, I should not have gone on my own accord, nor have willingly exceeded my authority in matters of this kind. If I said so, I must have so understood Mr Blifil's instructions.” “Look you, Mr Dowling,” said Allworthy; “I promise you before this lady, that whatever you have done in this affair by Mr Blifil's order I will forgive, provided you now tell me strictly the truth; for I believe what you say, that you would not have acted of your own accord and without authority in this matter.----Mr Blifil then likewise sent you to examine the two fellows at Aldersgate?”--“He did, sir.” “Well, and what instructions did he then give you? Recollect as well as you can, and tell me, as near as possible, the very words he used.”--“Why, sir, Mr Blifil sent me to find out the persons who were eye-witnesses of this fight. He said, he feared they might be tampered with by Mr Jones, or some of his friends. He said, blood required blood; and that not only all who concealed a murderer, but those who omitted anything in their power to bring him to justice, were sharers in his guilt. He said, he found you was very desirous of having the villain brought to justice, though it was not proper you should appear in it.” “He did so?” says Allworthy.--“Yes, sir,” cries Dowling; “I should not, I am sure, have proceeded such lengths for the sake of any other person living but your worship.”--“What lengths, sir?” said Allworthy.--“Nay, sir,” cries Dowling, “I would not have your worship think I would, on any account, be guilty of subornation of perjury; but there are two ways of delivering evidence. I told them, therefore, that if any offers should be made them on the other side, they should refuse them, and that they might be assured they should lose nothing by being honest men, and telling the truth. I said, we were told that Mr Jones had assaulted the gentleman first, and that, if that was the truth, they should declare it; and I did give them some hints that they should be no losers.”--“I think you went lengths indeed,” cries Allworthy.--“Nay, sir,” answered Dowling, “I am sure I did not desire them to tell an untruth;----nor should I have said what I did, unless it had been to oblige you.”--“You would not have thought, I believe,” says Allworthy, “to have obliged me, had you known that this Mr Jones was my own nephew.”--“I am sure, sir,” answered he, “it did not become me to take any notice of what I thought you desired to conceal.”--“How!” cries Allworthy, “and did you know it then?”--“Nay, sir,” answered Dowling, “if your worship bids me speak the truth, I am sure I shall do it.--Indeed, sir, I did know it; for they were almost the last words which Madam Blifil ever spoke, which she mentioned to me as I stood alone by her bedside, when she delivered me the letter I brought your worship from her.”--“What letter?” cries Allworthy.--“The letter, sir,” answered Dowling, “which I brought from Salisbury, and which I delivered into the hands of Mr Blifil.”--“O heavens!” cries Allworthy: “Well, and what were the words? What did my sister say to you?”--“She took me by the hand,” answered he, “and, as she delivered me the letter, said, `I scarce know what I have written. Tell my brother, Mr Jones is his nephew--He is my son.--Bless him,' says she, and then fell backward, as if dying away. I presently called in the people, and she never spoke more to me, and died within a few minutes afterwards.”--Allworthy stood a minute silent, lifting up his eyes; and then, turning to Dowling, said, “How came you, sir, not to deliver me this message?” “Your worship,” answered he, “must remember that you was at that time ill in bed; and, being in a violent hurry, as indeed I always am, I delivered the letter and message to Mr Blifil, who told me he would carry them both to you, which he hath since told me he did, and that your worship, partly out of friendship to Mr Jones, and partly out of regard to your sister, would never have it mentioned, and did intend to conceal it from the world; and therefore, sir, if you had not mentioned it to me first, I am certain I should never have thought it belonged to me to say anything of the matter, either to your worship or any other person.”", "올워디는 이에 아무런 대답도 하지 않고 문을 걸어 잠그더니, 다울링에게 엄한 표정으로 다가서며 말했다. \"당신이 아무리 바쁘다 한들, 나는 먼저 몇 가지 질문에 대한 답을 들어야겠소. 이 부인을 아시오?\" \"그 부인 말씀입니까, 어르신?\" 다울링이 몹시 머뭇거리며 대답했다. 그러자 올워디는 더없이 엄숙한 목소리로 말했다. \"이보시오, 다울링 씨. 나의 호의를 소중히 여긴다면, 또 내 밑에서 단 한순간이라도 더 일하고 싶다면, 머뭇거리지도 둘러대지도 마시오. 내가 묻는 모든 질문에 거짓 없이 진실하게 답하시오. 이 부인을 아시오?\" \"압니다, 어르신.\" 다울링이 말했다. \"이 부인을 뵌 적이 있습니다.\" \"어디서 말이오?\" \"이분의 거처에서요.\" \"무슨 용건으로 거기 갔소? 그리고 누가 보냈소?\" \"저는, 어르신, 존스 씨에 관해 알아보러 갔습니다.\" \"그럼 누가 당신을 그 사람에 관해 알아보라고 보냈소?\" \"누구냐고요, 어르신? 그게, 어르신, 블리필 씨가 저를 보냈습니다.\" \"그럼 당신은 그 문제에 관해 이 부인에게 뭐라고 말했소?\" \"원, 어르신, 한마디 한마디 다 기억해내기란 불가능합니다.\" \"부인, 이분의 기억을 거들어주시겠소?\" \"그는 제게 이렇게 말했어요, 어르신.\" 워터스 부인이 말했다. \"만일 존스 씨가 제 남편을 살해했다면, 매우 훌륭한 어느 신사분이 소송을 진행하는 데 필요한 돈을 얼마든지 대주실 것이며, 그분은 제가 상대하는 자가 얼마나 악당인지 잘 알고 계신다고요. 이것이 그가 한 바로 그 말이었음을 저는 떳떳이 맹세할 수 있어요.\" \"이것이 당신이 한 말이오?\" 올워디가 말했다. \"정확히 기억해낼 수는 없습니다만,\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"그런 취지로 말한 것 같긴 합니다.\" \"그럼 블리필 씨가 그렇게 말하라고 시켰소?\" \"분명한 것은, 어르신, 제가 제 발로 가지는 않았을 것이며, 이런 종류의 일에서 제 권한을 일부러 넘어서지도 않았으리라는 점입니다. 제가 그렇게 말했다면, 블리필 씨의 지시를 그렇게 이해했음이 틀림없습니다.\" \"이보시오, 다울링 씨.\" 올워디가 말했다. \"이 부인 앞에서 약속하건대, 당신이 이 일에서 블리필 씨의 지시에 따라 한 것이라면 무엇이든 용서하겠소. 단, 지금 내게 엄정하게 진실을 말한다는 조건에서요. 나는 당신 말을 믿소. 당신이 이 일에서 제 발로, 그리고 권한도 없이 행동하지는 않았으리라는 말을 말이오. 그럼 블리필이 마찬가지로 당신을 보내 올더스게이트에 있는 두 사내를 심문하게 했소?\" \"그랬습니다, 어르신.\" \"좋소, 그럼 그가 그때 당신에게 어떤 지시를 내렸소? 되도록 잘 기억해내서, 그가 쓴 바로 그 말에 최대한 가깝게 내게 일러주시오.\" \"그게요, 어르신, 블리필 씨는 저를 보내 이 싸움을 목격한 사람들을 찾아내게 했습니다. 그는 그자들이 존스 씨나 그 친구들 가운데 누군가에게 매수당할까 봐 염려된다고 했습니다. 또 피는 피를 부른다고, 살인자를 숨기는 자들뿐 아니라 그를 법의 심판대에 세우기 위해 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 일을 빠뜨리는 자들도 모두 그 죄를 나눠 진다고 했습니다. 그는 어르신께서 그 악당이 법의 심판을 받기를 무척 바라시되, 다만 어르신께서 거기에 나서는 것은 온당치 않다고 여기시는 걸 알게 되었다고 말했습니다.\" \"그가 그렇게 말했단 말이오?\" 올워디가 말했다. \"그렇습니다, 어르신.\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"분명히 말씀드리건대, 저는 어르신이 아닌 다른 어떤 살아 있는 사람을 위해서라면 그렇게까지 멀리 나아가지 않았을 겁니다.\" \"그렇게까지라니, 무슨 말이오?\" 올워디가 말했다. \"아니, 어르신,\" 다울링이 외쳤다. \"어르신께서 제가 무슨 일이 있어도 위증 교사 죄를 저지를 사람이라 여기시지는 않기를 바랍니다. 하지만 증언을 끌어내는 데는 두 가지 방법이 있지요. 그래서 저는 그자들에게, 만일 상대편에서 어떤 제안이 들어오더라도 거절하라고, 정직한 사람으로서 진실을 말한다고 해서 손해 볼 일은 결코 없을 거라고 일러주었습니다. 또 저는, 듣자 하니 존스 씨가 그 신사를 먼저 공격했다는데, 만일 그게 사실이라면 그렇게 진술하라고 말했고, 그자들에게 손해 보는 일은 없으리라는 언질을 좀 주었습니다.\" \"내 보기엔 당신이 정말로 멀리까지 나아갔구려.\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"아니, 어르신,\" 다울링이 대답했다. \"저는 분명 그자들에게 거짓을 말하라고 요구하지는 않았습니다. 그리고 어르신을 위하는 일이 아니었다면 제가 한 그 말도 하지 않았을 겁니다.\" \"내 생각엔, 만일 이 존스 씨가 내 친조카라는 것을 당신이 알았더라면,\" 올워디가 말했다. \"나를 위하는 일이라고는 여기지 않았을 것 같소만.\" \"분명한 것은, 어르신,\" 그가 대답했다. \"어르신께서 감추고 싶어 하신다고 제가 여긴 일을 함부로 입에 올리는 것은 제 도리가 아니었다는 점입니다.\" \"뭐라고!\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그럼 그때 당신이 그 사실을 알고 있었단 말이오?\" \"아니, 어르신,\" 다울링이 대답했다. \"어르신께서 진실을 말하라 명하신다면, 저는 분명 그리하겠습니다. 사실 저는 그것을 알고 있었습니다. 그것은 블리필 마님께서 생전에 마지막으로 하신 말씀에 가까운 것이었는데, 제가 그분의 침상 곁에 홀로 서 있을 때 제게 일러주신 것입니다. 그분이 어르신께 전해 올리라며 제게 편지를 건네주실 때 말입니다.\" \"무슨 편지 말이오?\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그 편지요, 어르신.\" 다울링이 대답했다. \"제가 솔즈베리에서 가져와 블리필 씨의 손에 직접 건넨 그 편지 말입니다.\" \"오, 하늘이시여!\" 올워디가 외쳤다. \"그래, 그래서 무슨 말이었소? 내 누이가 당신에게 뭐라 했소?\" \"그분은 제 손을 잡으셨습니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그러고는 편지를 건네시며 말씀하셨습니다. '내가 무얼 썼는지 나도 잘 모르겠네. 내 오라비에게 전해주게. 존스 씨는 그의 조카라고. 그 아이는 내 아들일세. 그 애를 축복해주세요.' 그분은 이렇게 말씀하시고는, 마치 숨이 끊어지듯 뒤로 쓰러지셨습니다. 저는 곧장 사람들을 불러들였고, 그분은 제게 더는 아무 말씀도 못 하시고 몇 분 뒤에 숨을 거두셨습니다.\" 올워디는 잠시 말없이 선 채 눈을 들어 올렸다. 그러더니 다울링을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"어찌하여, 당신은 이 전언을 내게 전하지 않았소?\" \"어르신께서는,\" 그가 대답했다. \"그 무렵 병석에 누워 계셨음을 기억하실 겁니다. 그리고 저는 몹시 서두르던 참이라, 사실 늘 그렇긴 합니다만, 편지와 전언을 블리필 씨께 넘겼습니다. 그분은 두 가지 모두 어르신께 가져다드리겠다고 하셨고, 그 뒤에 제게 말하기를 정말로 그리했노라고, 그리고 어르신께서는 한편으로는 존스 씨에 대한 정 때문에, 또 한편으로는 누이분에 대한 정 때문에, 그 일이 결코 입에 오르내리는 것을 원치 않으시며 세상에 감추실 작정이라고 하셨습니다. 그러니, 어르신, 어르신께서 먼저 제게 이 일을 꺼내지 않으셨다면, 저는 분명 어르신께든 다른 누구에게든 이 일에 대해 무어라 말하는 것이 제 소관이라고는 결코 여기지 않았을 것입니다.\""]], [["We have remarked somewhere already, that it is possible for a man to convey a lie in the words of truth; this was the case at present; for Blifil had, in fact, told Dowling what he now related, but had not imposed upon him, nor indeed had imagined he was able so to do. In reality, the promises which Blifil had made to Dowling were the motives which had induced him to secrecy; and, as he now very plainly saw Blifil would not be able to keep them, he thought proper now to make this confession, which the promises of forgiveness, joined to the threats, the voice, the looks of Allworthy, and the discoveries he had made before, extorted from him, who was besides taken unawares, and had no time to consider of evasions.", "우리는 이미 어딘가에서, 사람이 진실의 말로 거짓을 전할 수도 있다고 짚어둔 바 있다. 지금이 바로 그런 경우였다. 블리필은 실제로 다울링에게 그가 방금 전한 그대로 말했으나, 그를 속인 것은 아니었으며, 사실 자신에게 그럴 능력이 있다고 상상하지도 않았다. 실은 블리필이 다울링에게 했던 약속들이야말로 그를 입 다물게 만든 동기였다. 그리고 이제 블리필이 그 약속을 지킬 수 없으리라는 것을 아주 명백히 알아차린 다울링은, 지금이야말로 이렇게 털어놓을 때라고 여겼다. 용서해주겠다는 약속에 더해, 올워디의 위협과 목소리와 표정, 그리고 그가 앞서 밝혀낸 사실들이 다울링에게서 이 고백을 쥐어짜낸 것이었다. 게다가 그는 불시에 허를 찔린 터라, 빠져나갈 구실을 궁리할 겨를도 없었다."]], [["Allworthy appeared well satisfied with this relation, and, having enjoined on Dowling strict silence as to what had past, conducted that gentleman himself to the door, lest he should see Blifil, who was returned to his chamber, where he exulted in the thoughts of his last deceit on his uncle, and little suspected what had since passed below-stairs.", "올워디는 이 진술에 꽤 만족한 듯 보였다. 그는 다울링에게 지나간 일에 대해 철저히 함구할 것을 단단히 일러둔 뒤, 그가 블리필과 마주치지 않도록 직접 그 신사를 문까지 배웅했다. 블리필은 자기 방으로 돌아가 있었는데, 거기서 숙부를 또 한 번 감쪽같이 속였다는 생각에 우쭐거리며, 그사이 아래층에서 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지는 조금도 의심하지 못하고 있었다."]], [["As Allworthy was returning to his room he met Mrs Miller in the entry, who, with a face all pale and full of terror, said to him, “O! sir, I find this wicked woman hath been with you, and you know all; yet do not on this account abandon the poor young man. Consider, sir, he was ignorant it was his own mother; and the discovery itself will most probably break his heart, without your unkindness.”", "올워디가 자기 방으로 돌아가던 길에 현관에서 밀러 부인과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 새파랗게 질려 공포에 사로잡힌 얼굴로 그에게 말했다. \"오! 어르신, 보아하니 그 사악한 여자가 어르신과 함께 있었고, 어르신께서 모든 것을 알게 되신 모양이군요. 하지만 그렇다고 그 가엾은 젊은이를 버리지는 말아주세요. 헤아려주세요, 어르신. 그 사람은 자신의 친어머니인 줄 몰랐던 거예요. 그리고 그 사실을 알게 되는 것만으로도, 어르신의 매정함이 없더라도, 십중팔구 그의 가슴은 미어질 거예요.\""]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["says Allworthy, “I am under such an astonishment at what I have heard, that I am really unable to satisfy you; but come with me into my room.", "올워디가 말했다. \"내가 들은 것에 너무도 놀란 나머지, 정말이지 당신에게 속 시원히 답해줄 수가 없구려."], ["Indeed, Mrs Miller, I have made surprizing discoveries, and you shall soon know them.”", "그러니 나와 함께 내 방으로 갑시다. 정말이지, 밀러 부인, 나는 놀라운 사실들을 알아냈고, 당신도 곧 그것을 알게 될 거요.\""]], [["The poor woman followed him trembling; and now Allworthy, going up to Mrs Waters, took her by the hand, and then, turning to Mrs Miller, said, “What reward shall I bestow upon this gentlewoman, for the services she hath done me?--O! Mrs Miller, you have a thousand times heard me call the young man to whom you are so faithful a friend, my son. Little did I then think he was indeed related to me at all.--Your friend, madam, is my nephew; he is the brother of that wicked viper which I have so long nourished in my bosom.--She will herself tell you the whole story, and how the youth came to pass for her son. Indeed, Mrs Miller, I am convinced that he hath been wronged, and that I have been abused; abused by one whom you too justly suspected of being a villain. He is, in truth, the worst of villains.”", "그 가엾은 여인은 떨면서 그를 따라갔다. 이제 올워디는 워터스 부인에게로 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡고는, 밀러 부인을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"이분이 내게 베푼 도움에 대해, 내가 무슨 보답을 해드려야 하겠소? 오! 밀러 부인, 당신은 내가 그 젊은이를, 당신이 그토록 충실한 벗이 되어주는 그 젊은이를, 내 아들이라 부르는 것을 수천 번도 더 들었지요. 그때 나는 그가 정말로 나와 핏줄로 이어져 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했소. 부인, 당신의 그 벗은 내 조카요. 그는 내가 그토록 오래 가슴에 품어 길러온 그 사악한 독사의 형제라오. 이분이 직접 당신에게 그 전말을, 그리고 어찌하여 그 젊은이가 자기 아들로 통하게 되었는지를 다 일러줄 거요. 정말이지, 밀러 부인, 나는 그가 부당한 일을 당했고, 내가 속아왔음을 확신하오. 당신이 너무도 정당하게 악당이라 의심했던 그자에게 속아온 것이오. 그자는 실로 악당 중에서도 가장 못된 악당이오.\""]], [["The joy which Mrs Miller now felt bereft her of the power of speech, and might perhaps have deprived her of her senses, if not of life, had not a friendly shower of tears come seasonably to her relief.", "이제 밀러 부인이 느낀 기쁨은 그녀에게서 말할 힘을 앗아갔고, 만일 정다운 눈물이 때맞춰 흘러내려 그녀를 구해주지 않았더라면 어쩌면 그녀에게서 정신을, 아니 어쩌면 목숨까지 앗아갔을지도 모른다."], ["At length, recovering so far from her transport as to be able to speak, she cried, “And is my dear Mr Jones then your nephew, sir, and not the son of this lady?", "마침내 그녀는 황홀경에서 어느 정도 헤어나와 말할 수 있게 되자 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 제가 아끼는 존스 씨가 어르신의 조카이고, 이 부인의 아들이 아니란 말씀이세요?"], ["And are your eyes opened to him at last?", "그리고 어르신께서 마침내 그 사람을 향해 눈을 뜨신 거예요?"], ["And shall I live to see him as happy as he deserves?”", "그리고 제가 그 사람이 마땅히 누릴 만큼 행복해지는 것을 살아서 보게 되는 건가요?\""], ["“He certainly is my nephew,”", "\"그는 분명 내 조카요.\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["says Allworthy, “and I hope all the rest.”--“And is this the dear good woman, the person,”", "\"그리고 나머지도 다 그러기를 바라오.\" \"그럼 이 부인이 바로 그 다정하고 선량하신 분, 이 모든 것이 밝혀진 게 다 그분 덕분이라는 그분이세요?\""], ["cries she, “to whom all this discovery is owing?”--“She is indeed,”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"정말 그렇소.\""], ["says Allworthy.--“Why, then,”", "올워디가 말했다. \"그렇다면,\""], ["cried Mrs Miller, upon her knees, “may Heaven shower down its choicest blessings upon her head, and for this one good action forgive her all her sins, be they never so many!”", "밀러 부인이 무릎을 꿇고 외쳤다. \"하늘이시여, 이분의 머리 위에 가장 귀한 축복을 쏟아부어 주시고, 이 한 가지 선행으로 이분의 모든 죄를, 그것이 아무리 많을지라도 용서해주옵소서!\""]], [["Mrs Waters then informed them that she believed Jones would very shortly be released; for that the surgeon was gone, in company with a nobleman, to the justice who committed him, in order to certify that Mr Fitzpatrick was out of all manner of danger, and to procure his prisoner his liberty.", "그러자 워터스 부인은 존스가 머지않아 풀려나리라 믿는다고 그들에게 알려주었다. 외과의가 어느 귀족과 함께, 존스를 수감한 치안판사에게 가서, 피츠패트릭 씨가 온갖 위험에서 완전히 벗어났음을 증명하고 그 죄수에게 자유를 얻어주려 한다는 것이었다."]], [["Allworthy said he should be glad to find his nephew there at his return home; but that he was then obliged to go on some business of consequence.", "올워디는 집에 돌아갔을 때 조카가 그곳에 와 있는 것을 보게 된다면 기쁘겠다고 말했다. 다만 자신은 지금 중요한 볼일이 있어 나가봐야 한다고 했다."], ["He then called to a servant to fetch him a chair, and presently left the two ladies together.", "그러고는 하인을 불러 가마를 가져오게 하고, 곧 두 부인을 함께 남겨둔 채 자리를 떴다."]], [["Mr Blifil, hearing the chair ordered, came downstairs to attend upon his uncle; for he never was deficient in such acts of duty. He asked his uncle if he was going out, which is a civil way of asking a man whither he is going: to which the other making no answer, he again desired to know when he would be pleased to return?--Allworthy made no answer to this neither, till he was just going into his chair, and then, turning about, he said--“Harkee, sir, do you find out, before my return, the letter which your mother sent me on her death-bed.” Allworthy then departed, and left Blifil in a situation to be envied only by a man who is just going to be hanged.", "블리필은 가마를 부르라는 소리를 듣고, 숙부의 시중을 들러 아래층으로 내려왔다. 그는 이런 의무를 다하는 일에는 결코 소홀한 적이 없었으니까. 그는 숙부에게 외출하시느냐고 물었는데, 이는 어디 가시느냐고 묻는 점잖은 방식이었다. 이에 상대가 아무 대답이 없자, 그는 다시 언제쯤 돌아오실 작정이시냐고 알려달라고 청했다. 올워디는 이 물음에도 아무 대답을 하지 않다가, 막 가마에 오르려는 순간 몸을 돌려 말했다. \"이보게, 자네는 내가 돌아오기 전에, 자네 어머니가 임종 자리에서 내게 보낸 그 편지를 찾아내게.\" 그러고 나서 올워디는 떠나갔고, 블리필은 막 교수대에 오르려는 사람이나 부러워할 만한 처지에 남겨졌다."]], [["A further continuation. Allworthy took an opportunity, whilst he was in the chair, of reading the letter from Jones to Sophia, which Western delivered him; and there were some expressions in it concerning himself which drew tears from his eyes.", "올워디는 가마에 타고 있는 동안 틈을 내어, 웨스턴이 그에게 건넨 존스가 소피아에게 보낸 편지를 읽었다."], ["At length he arrived at Mr Western's, and was introduced to Sophia.", "그 안에는 그 자신에 관한 몇몇 표현이 들어 있어 그의 눈에서 눈물이 흘러나오게 했다. 마침내 그는 웨스턴 씨의 집에 이르렀고, 소피아에게 안내되었다."]], [["When the first ceremonies were past, and the gentleman and lady had taken their chairs, a silence of some minutes ensued; during which the latter, who had been prepared for the visit by her father, sat playing with her fan, and had every mark of confusion both in her countenance and behaviour.", "처음의 인사치레가 끝나고 신사와 숙녀가 각자 의자에 자리를 잡자, 몇 분간 침묵이 이어졌다. 그동안 아버지로부터 이 방문에 대해 미리 들어 마음의 준비를 해 둔 소피아는 부채를 만지작거리며 앉아 있었고, 그녀의 표정과 몸짓 모두에 당혹스러움의 기색이 역력했다."], ["At length Allworthy, who was himself a little disconcerted, began thus: “I am afraid, Miss Western, my family hath been the occasion of giving you some uneasiness; to which, I fear, I have innocently become more instrumental than I intended.", "마침내 올워디도 자신 또한 다소 마음이 편치 않은 채로 이렇게 입을 열었다. \"웨스턴 양, 제 집안이 양에게 어떤 불편함을 끼친 빌미가 된 듯하여 송구합니다."], ["Be assured, madam, had I at first known how disagreeable the proposals had been, I should not have suffered you to have been so long persecuted.", "게다가 저 자신도, 본의는 아니었으나 의도했던 것보다 더 그 일에 한몫을 한 듯하여 두렵습니다. 부디 믿어 주십시오, 양."], ["I hope, therefore, you will not think the design of this visit is to trouble you with any further solicitations of that kind, but entirely to relieve you from them.”", "처음부터 그 청혼이 얼마나 거슬리는 일인지 알았더라면, 양이 그토록 오래 시달리도록 두지 않았을 것입니다. 그러니 이 방문의 목적이 그런 종류의 간청으로 양을 더 괴롭히려는 것이 아니라, 오로지 그로부터 양을 풀어 드리려는 것임을 믿어 주시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said Sophia, with a little modest hesitation, “this behaviour is most kind and generous, and such as I could expect only from Mr Allworthy; but as you have been so kind to mention this matter, you will pardon me for saying it hath, indeed, given me great uneasiness, and hath been the occasion of my suffering much cruel treatment from a father who was, till that unhappy affair, the tenderest and fondest of all parents.", "소피아가 약간의 겸손한 망설임과 함께 말했다. \"이런 태도는 더없이 친절하고 너그러우시며, 오직 올워디 선생님께만 기대할 수 있는 것입니다."], ["I am convinced, sir, you are too good and generous to resent my refusal of your nephew.", "다만 선생님께서 친히 이 일을 언급해 주셨으니, 솔직히 말씀드림을 용서해 주세요. 그것은 정말로 제게 큰 괴로움을 주었고, 그 불행한 일이 있기 전까지는 모든 부모 가운데 가장 다정하고 인자하셨던 아버지로부터 모진 대우를 받게 된 빌미가 되었습니다."], ["Our inclinations are not in our own power; and whatever may be his merit, I cannot force them in his favour.”", "선생님, 저는 선생님께서 제가 조카분을 거절한 것을 노여워하시기에는 너무도 선하고 너그러운 분이심을 확신합니다. 마음이란 우리 뜻대로 되는 것이 아니며, 그분의 자질이 어떠하든 저는 제 마음을 그분께로 억지로 돌릴 수가 없습니다.\""], ["“I assure you, most amiable young lady,”", "\"장담하건대, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨,\" 올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “I am capable of no such resentment, had the person been my own son, and had I entertained the highest esteem for him.", "\"설령 그 사람이 제 친아들이었고 제가 그에게 더없이 깊은 존경을 품고 있었다 해도, 저는 그런 노여움을 품을 수 없는 사람입니다. 양의 말씀이 옳으니까요."], ["For you say truly, madam, we cannot force our inclinations, much less can they be directed by another.”", "우리는 우리 마음을 억지로 돌릴 수 없으며, 하물며 남이 그것을 좌우할 수는 더더욱 없는 법입니다.\" \"오!"], ["“Oh! sir,”", "선생님,\""], ["answered Sophia, “every word you speak proves you deserve that good, that great, that benevolent character the whole world allows you.", "소피아가 대답했다. \"선생님께서 하시는 말씀 한마디 한마디가, 온 세상이 인정하는 그 선하고 위대하며 자애로운 인품을 선생님께서 받으실 자격이 있음을 증명합니다."], ["I assure you, sir, nothing less than the certain prospect of future misery could have made me resist the commands of my father.”", "장담하건대, 선생님, 장차 닥칠 불행이 확실히 내다보이지 않았다면 저는 결코 아버지의 명을 거역하지 못했을 것입니다.\" \"진심으로 양의 말을 믿습니다, 양.\""], ["“I sincerely believe you, madam,”", "올워디가 대답했다."], ["replied Allworthy, “and I heartily congratulate you on your prudent foresight, since by so justifiable a resistance you have avoided misery indeed!”", "\"그리고 양의 신중한 선견지명에 진심으로 축하를 드립니다. 그토록 정당한 거역으로 양은 참으로 불행을 피하셨으니까요!\""], ["“You speak now, Mr Allworthy,”", "\"올워디 선생님,\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["cries she, “with a delicacy which few men are capable of feeling!", "\"선생님께서는 이제 좀처럼 느끼지 못하는 사람이 많은 그 섬세함으로 말씀하시는군요!"], ["but surely, in my opinion, to lead our lives with one to whom we are indifferent must be a state of wretchedness.----Perhaps that wretchedness would be even increased by a sense of the merits of an object to whom we cannot give our affections.", "하지만 분명, 제 생각으로는, 우리가 무심한 사람과 평생을 함께하는 것은 비참한 처지일 수밖에 없습니다.——어쩌면 그 비참함은, 우리가 마음을 줄 수 없는 상대의 미덕을 느끼는 만큼 오히려 더 커질지도 모릅니다. 만일 제가 블리필 씨와 결혼했더라면——\""], ["If I had married Mr Blifil--”", "\"말씀을 끊어 죄송합니다, 양,\""], ["“Pardon my interrupting you, madam,”", "올워디가 대답했다."], ["answered Allworthy, “but I cannot bear the supposition.--Believe me, Miss Western, I rejoice from my heart, I rejoice in your escape.--I have discovered the wretch for whom you have suffered all this cruel violence from your father to be a villain.”", "\"하지만 그런 가정은 견딜 수가 없군요.——믿어 주십시오, 웨스턴 양, 저는 진심으로 기뻐하고 있습니다. 양이 벗어나신 것을 기뻐하고 있어요.——저는 양이 아버지로부터 이 모든 모진 횡포를 견디게 만든 그 사람이 악당임을 알아냈습니다.\""], ["“How, sir!”", "\"어떻게요, 선생님!\" 소피아가 외쳤다."], ["cries Sophia--“you must believe this surprizes me.”--“It hath surprized me, madam,”", "\"이 일이 저를 얼마나 놀라게 하는지 믿어 주셔야 합니다.\" \"그것은 저도 놀라게 했습니다, 양.\""], ["answered Allworthy, “and so it will the world.----But I have acquainted you with the real truth.”", "올워디가 대답했다. \"그리고 세상도 놀라게 할 것입니다.——하지만 저는 양께 있는 그대로의 진실을 알려 드린 것입니다.\""], ["“Nothing but truth,”", "\"올워디 선생님의 입술에서는,\" 소피아가 말했다."], ["says Sophia, “can, I am convinced, come from the lips of Mr Allworthy.----Yet, sir, such sudden, such unexpected news.----Discovered, you say----may villany be ever so!”--“You will soon enough hear the story,”", "\"진실 외에는 아무것도 나올 수 없음을 저는 확신합니다.——하지만 선생님, 이렇게 갑작스럽고 뜻밖의 소식이라니.——알아내셨다고요——악행이란 언제나 그런 것이겠지요!\" \"그 이야기는 머지않아 충분히 듣게 되실 겁니다.\""], ["cries Allworthy;--“at present let us not mention so detested a name.--I have another matter of a very serious nature to propose.--O!", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"지금은 그토록 혐오스러운 이름은 입에 올리지 맙시다.——제게는 매우 중대한 또 다른 문제를 제안드릴 것이 있습니다.——오!"], ["Miss Western, I know your vast worth, nor can I so easily part with the ambition of being allied to it.--I have a near relation, madam, a young man whose character is, I am convinced, the very opposite to that of this wretch, and whose fortune I will make equal to what his was to have been.", "웨스턴 양, 저는 양의 크나큰 가치를 알기에, 그것과 인연을 맺고자 하는 바람을 그리 쉽게 접을 수가 없습니다.——제게는 가까운 친척이 하나 있습니다, 양. 그 젊은이의 됨됨이는, 제가 확신하건대, 그 악당과는 정반대이며, 저는 그의 재산을 본래 그 사람이 받았을 것과 똑같이 만들어 줄 것입니다."], ["Could I, madam, hope you would admit a visit from him?”", "양, 제가 그 젊은이의 방문을 양께서 허락해 주시리라 바라도 되겠습니까?\""], ["Sophia, after a minute's silence, answered, “I will deal with the utmost sincerity with Mr Allworthy.", "소피아는 잠시 침묵한 뒤 대답했다. \"올워디 선생님께는 더없이 진실하게 대하겠습니다."], ["His character, and the obligation I have just received from him, demand it.", "선생님의 인품과, 방금 제가 선생님께 입은 은혜가 그것을 요구하니까요."], ["I have determined at present to listen to no such proposals from any person.", "저는 지금으로서는 그 누구로부터도 그런 청혼에 귀 기울이지 않기로 결심했습니다."], ["My only desire is to be restored to the affection of my father, and to be again the mistress of his family.", "제 유일한 바람은 아버지의 사랑을 되찾고, 다시 그 집안의 안주인이 되는 것입니다."], ["This, sir, I hope to owe to your good offices.", "선생님, 이것을 선생님의 선의 어린 주선 덕에 이루기를 바랍니다."], ["Let me beseech you, let me conjure you, by all the goodness which I, and all who know you, have experienced, do not, the very moment when you have released me from one persecution, do not engage me in another as miserable and as fruitless.”", "부디 간청드립니다, 부디 빌건대, 저와 선생님을 아는 모든 이가 경험한 그 모든 선하심에 걸고, 저를 한 박해에서 풀어 주신 바로 그 순간에, 저를 또 다른 박해, 그만큼 비참하고 그만큼 헛된 박해에 옭아매지는 말아 주십시오.\" \"정말로, 웨스턴 양,\""], ["“Indeed, Miss Western,”", "올워디가 대답했다."], ["replied Allworthy, “I am capable of no such conduct; and if this be your resolution, he must submit to the disappointment, whatever torments he may suffer under it.”", "\"저는 그런 행동을 할 수 없는 사람입니다. 그리고 이것이 양의 결심이라면, 그가 그 아래에서 어떤 고통을 겪든 그 실망을 감수해야겠지요.\""], ["“I must smile now, Mr Allworthy,”", "\"이제는 웃을 수밖에 없군요, 올워디 선생님,\""], ["answered Sophia, “when you mention the torments of a man whom I do not know, and who can consequently have so little acquaintance with me.”", "소피아가 대답했다. \"선생님께서 제가 알지도 못하는 사람, 따라서 저와는 거의 면식이 없는 사람의 고통을 말씀하시니 말입니다.\""], ["“Pardon me, dear young lady,”", "\"용서하십시오, 사랑스러운 아가씨,\" 올워디가 외쳤다."], ["cries Allworthy, “I begin now to be afraid he hath had too much acquaintance for the repose of his future days; since, if ever man was capable of a sincere, violent, and noble passion, such, I am convinced, is my unhappy nephew's for Miss Western.”", "\"이제 저는 그가 자신의 앞날의 평안을 위해서는 너무 많은 면식을 가진 것이 아닐까 두려워지기 시작합니다. 만일 어떤 사람이 진실하고 격렬하며 고귀한 정열을 품을 수 있다면, 저는 제 불행한 조카가 웨스턴 양께 품은 정열이 바로 그러하다고 확신하니까요.\""], ["“A nephew of your's, Mr Allworthy!”", "\"선생님의 조카라고요, 올워디 선생님!\""], ["answered Sophia.", "소피아가 대답했다."], ["“It is surely strange.", "\"정말 이상한 일이군요."], ["I never heard of him before.”", "저는 그분에 대해 이전에 들어 본 적이 없습니다.\" \"사실, 양,\""], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "올워디가 외쳤다."], ["cries Allworthy, “it is only the circumstance of his being my nephew to which you are a stranger, and which, till this day, was a secret to me.--Mr Jones, who has long loved you, he!", "\"양께서 모르시는 것은 다만 그가 제 조카라는 사정뿐이며, 그것은 오늘까지 제게도 비밀이었습니다.——오래도록 양을 사랑해 온 존스 씨, 그가, 그가 제 조카입니다!\" \"존스 씨가 선생님의 조카라고요, 선생님!\""], ["he is my nephew!”", "소피아가 외쳤다."], ["“Mr Jones your nephew, sir!”", "\"그게 가능한 일인가요?\""], ["cries Sophia, “can it be possible?”--“He is, indeed, madam,”", "\"정말로 그렇습니다, 양.\" 올워디가 대답했다."], ["answered Allworthy; “he is my own sister's son--as such I shall always own him; nor am I ashamed of owning him.", "\"그는 제 친누이의 아들입니다.——그런 사람으로서 저는 그를 언제까지나 인정할 것이며, 그를 인정하는 것이 부끄럽지 않습니다. 저는 오히려 지난날 그에게 했던 제 처사가 훨씬 더 부끄럽습니다."], ["I am much more ashamed of my past behaviour to him; but I was as ignorant of his merit as of his birth.", "하지만 저는 그의 출생을 몰랐던 것만큼이나 그의 자질도 알지 못했습니다. 정말로, 웨스턴 양, 저는 그를 모질게 대했습니다.——정말 그랬습니다.\""], ["Indeed, Miss Western, I have used him cruelly----Indeed I have.”--Here the good man wiped his eyes, and after a short pause proceeded--“I never shall be able to reward him for his sufferings without your assistance.----Believe me, most amiable young lady, I must have a great esteem of that offering which I make to your worth.", "여기서 이 선한 사람은 눈물을 닦았고, 잠시 멈춘 뒤 말을 이었다. \"양의 도움 없이는 저는 결코 그가 겪은 고통을 보상해 줄 수 없을 것입니다.——믿어 주십시오, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨, 제가 양의 가치 앞에 바치는 이 제안을 저는 매우 귀하게 여기고 있습니다."], ["I know he hath been guilty of faults; but there is great goodness of heart at the bottom.", "저는 그가 잘못을 저질러 온 것을 압니다. 하지만 그 밑바닥에는 크나큰 선한 마음이 있습니다."], ["Believe me, madam, there is.”", "믿어 주십시오, 양, 정말 그렇습니다.\""], ["Here he stopped, seeming to expect an answer, which he presently received from Sophia, after she had a little recovered herself from the hurry of spirits into which so strange and sudden information had thrown her: “I sincerely wish you joy, sir, of a discovery in which you seem to have such satisfaction.", "여기서 그는 답을 기다리는 듯 말을 멈추었고, 이내 소피아로부터 답을 들었다. 그토록 이상하고 갑작스러운 소식에 휘말렸던 마음의 동요에서 조금 벗어난 뒤였다."], ["I doubt not but you will have all the comfort you can promise yourself from it.", "\"선생님, 선생님께서 그토록 큰 만족을 느끼시는 것 같은 그 발견에 대해 진심으로 기쁘게 여깁니다. 선생님께서 그로부터 스스로 약속하실 수 있는 온갖 위안을 모두 누리시리라 믿어 의심치 않습니다."], ["The young gentleman hath certainly a thousand good qualities, which makes it impossible he should not behave well to such an uncle.”--“I hope, madam,”", "그 젊은 신사분은 분명 수천 가지 좋은 자질을 지녔으니, 그런 큰아버지께 잘 처신하지 않을 수가 없겠지요.\" \"양,\""], ["said Allworthy, “he hath those good qualities which must make him a good husband.--He must, I am sure, be of all men the most abandoned, if a lady of your merit should condescend--”", "올워디가 말했다. \"저는 그가 좋은 남편이 될 만한 그런 좋은 자질들을 지녔기를 바랍니다.——양처럼 가치 있는 숙녀께서 몸을 낮추어 주신다면, 그가 만일 잘못 처신한다면 그는 분명 모든 사람 가운데 가장 타락한 자일 것입니다——\""], ["“You must pardon me, Mr Allworthy,”", "\"용서해 주셔야겠습니다, 올워디 선생님,\" 소피아가 대답했다."], ["answered Sophia; “I cannot listen to a proposal of this kind.", "\"저는 이런 종류의 제안에는 귀 기울일 수가 없습니다. 존스 씨가 많은 자질을 지녔음을 저는 확신합니다."], ["Mr Jones, I am convinced, hath much merit; but I shall never receive Mr Jones as one who is to be my husband--Upon my honour I never will.”--“Pardon me, madam,”", "하지만 저는 결코 존스 씨를 제 남편이 될 사람으로 받아들이지 않을 것입니다——제 명예를 걸고, 결코 그러지 않을 것입니다.\" \"용서하십시오, 양,\""], ["cries Allworthy, “if I am a little surprized, after what I have heard from Mr Western--I hope the unhappy young man hath done nothing to forfeit your good opinion, if he had ever the honour to enjoy it.--Perhaps, he may have been misrepresented to you, as he was to me.", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"제가 웨스턴 씨에게 들은 바가 있어서, 조금 놀랍기는 합니다.——그 불행한 젊은이가, 혹 한때 양의 호의를 누릴 영광을 가졌었다면, 그 호의를 잃을 만한 어떤 짓도 하지 않았기를 바랍니다.——어쩌면 그가 제게 그러했듯 양께도 잘못 전해졌을지 모릅니다."], ["The same villany may have injured him everywhere.--He is no murderer, I assure you; as he hath been called.”--“Mr Allworthy,”", "같은 악행이 어디서나 그를 해쳤을 수 있으니까요.——장담하건대 그는 사람들이 일컫던 그런 살인자가 아닙니다.\" \"올워디 선생님,\""], ["answered Sophia, “I have told you my resolution.", "소피아가 대답했다. \"저는 제 결심을 말씀드렸습니다."], ["I wonder not at what my father hath told you; but, whatever his apprehensions or fears have been, if I know my heart, I have given no occasion for them; since it hath always been a fixed principle with me, never to have married without his consent.", "아버지께서 선생님께 무슨 말씀을 하셨든 저는 놀라지 않습니다. 다만, 그분의 염려나 두려움이 무엇이었든, 제 마음을 제가 안다면, 저는 그럴 빌미를 드린 적이 없습니다."], ["This is, I think, the duty of a child to a parent; and this, I hope, nothing could ever have prevailed with me to swerve from.", "아버지의 동의 없이는 결코 결혼하지 않겠다는 것이 늘 제 확고한 신조였으니까요. 이것이야말로 자식이 부모께 마땅히 지켜야 할 도리라고 저는 생각하며, 어떤 것도 결코 저를 거기서 벗어나게 하지 못하리라 바랍니다."], ["I do not indeed conceive that the authority of any parent can oblige us to marry in direct opposition to our inclinations.", "다만 저는, 어떤 부모의 권위라 해도 우리의 마음에 정면으로 거스르는 결혼을 강요할 수는 없다고 생각합니다."], ["To avoid a force of this kind, which I had reason to suspect, I left my father's house, and sought protection elsewhere.", "제가 의심할 만한 이유가 있었던 그런 강요를 피하기 위해 저는 아버지의 집을 떠나 다른 곳에서 보호를 구했습니다."], ["This is the truth of my story; and if the world, or my father, carry my intentions any farther, my own conscience will acquit me.”", "이것이 제 이야기의 진실이며, 만일 세상이나 아버지가 제 의도를 그 이상으로 몰아간다 해도, 제 양심만큼은 저를 무죄로 여길 것입니다.\""], ["“I hear you, Miss Western,”", "\"웨스턴 양,\" 올워디가 외쳤다."], ["cries Allworthy, “with admiration.", "\"저는 감탄하며 양의 말씀을 듣고 있습니다."], ["I admire the justness of your sentiments; but surely there is more in this.", "양의 감정이 지닌 올바름에 저는 감탄합니다. 하지만 분명 여기에는 그 이상의 무언가가 있습니다."], ["I am cautious of offending you, young lady; but am I to look on all which I have hitherto heard or seen as a dream only?", "양을 거스르지 않으려 조심하고 있습니다만, 아가씨, 제가 지금까지 듣고 본 모든 것을 한낱 꿈으로만 여겨야 하는 것입니까?"], ["And have you suffered so much cruelty from your father on the account of a man to whom you have been always absolutely indifferent?”", "그리고 양께서는, 늘 전혀 무심하기만 했던 한 남자 때문에 아버지로부터 그토록 모진 처사를 견디셨다는 말씀입니까?\" \"올워디 선생님, 부디,\""], ["“I beg, Mr Allworthy,”", "소피아가 대답했다."], ["answered Sophia, “you will not insist on my reasons;--yes, I have suffered indeed; I will not, Mr Allworthy, conceal----I will be very sincere with you--I own I had a great opinion of Mr Jones--I believe--I know I have suffered for my opinion--I have been treated cruelly by my aunt, as well as by my father; but that is now past--I beg I may not be farther pressed; for, whatever hath been, my resolution is now fixed.", "\"제 이유를 캐묻지는 말아 주세요.——그래요, 저는 정말로 고통받았습니다. 올워디 선생님, 숨기지 않겠습니다——선생님께 아주 솔직하게 말씀드리겠어요——저는 존스 씨를 매우 높이 평가했음을 인정합니다——제 생각으로는——아니, 저는 분명 그 평가 때문에 고통받았습니다——저는 아버지뿐 아니라 고모로부터도 모진 대우를 받았습니다."], ["Your nephew, sir, hath many virtues--he hath great virtues, Mr Allworthy.", "하지만 그것은 이제 지난 일입니다——더는 다그치지 말아 주시기를 청합니다. 무슨 일이 있었든, 제 결심은 이제 확고하니까요."], ["I question not but he will do you honour in the world, and make you happy.”--“I wish I could make him so, madam,”", "선생님, 선생님의 조카분은 많은 미덕을 지녔습니다——크나큰 미덕을 지녔어요, 올워디 선생님. 저는 그분이 세상에서 선생님께 영예를 안기고, 선생님을 행복하게 해 드릴 것을 의심치 않습니다.\""], ["replied Allworthy; “but that I am convinced is only in your power.", "\"그를 행복하게 해 줄 수 있다면 좋겠습니다, 양.\" 올워디가 대답했다."], ["It is that conviction which hath made me so earnest a solicitor in his favour.”", "\"하지만 그것은 오직 양의 손에 달려 있음을 저는 확신합니다. 바로 그 확신이 저로 하여금 그를 위해 이토록 간곡히 청하게 만든 것입니다.\""], ["“You are deceived indeed, sir; you are deceived,”", "\"선생님은 정말로 속고 계십니다."], ["said Sophia.", "속고 계세요.\""], ["“I hope not by him.", "소피아가 말했다."], ["It is sufficient to have deceived me.", "\"그에게 속으신 것이 아니기를 바랍니다."], ["Mr Allworthy, I must insist on being pressed no farther on this subject.", "저 한 사람을 속인 것으로 충분합니다. 올워디 선생님, 이 일에 대해서는 더 이상 다그치지 말아 주시기를 간곡히 청합니다."], ["I should be sorry--nay, I will not injure him in your favour.", "저는 안타까워해야겠지요——아니, 선생님 앞에서 그를 깎아내리지는 않겠습니다."], ["I wish Mr Jones very well.", "저는 존스 씨가 잘되기를 진심으로 바랍니다."], ["I sincerely wish him well; and I repeat it again to you, whatever demerit he may have to me, I am certain he hath many good qualities.", "진심으로 그가 잘되기를 바랍니다. 그리고 다시 한번 말씀드리지만, 그가 제게 어떤 허물을 졌든, 저는 그가 많은 좋은 자질을 지녔음을 확신합니다."], ["I do not disown my former thoughts; but nothing can ever recal them.", "저는 지난날의 제 생각을 부인하지는 않습니다. 하지만 어떤 것도 그것을 되돌릴 수는 없습니다."], ["At present there is not a man upon earth whom I would more resolutely reject than Mr Jones; nor would the addresses of Mr Blifil himself be less agreeable to me.”", "지금 이 땅에서, 제가 존스 씨보다 더 단호하게 거절할 사람은 없으며, 블리필 씨 본인의 구애라 해도 제게 그보다 덜 달갑지는 않을 것입니다.\""]], [["Western had been long impatient for the event of this conference, and was just now arrived at the door to listen; when, having heard the last sentiments of his daughter's heart, he lost all temper, and, bursting open the door in a rage, cried out--“It is a lie!", "웨스턴은 이 회담의 결과를 오래도록 애타게 기다리던 터라, 마침 그 순간 문 앞에 이르러 엿듣고 있었다. 그러다 딸의 마음속 마지막 심정을 듣고는 완전히 이성을 잃고, 화가 치밀어 문을 박차고 들어오며 외쳤다."], ["It is a d--n'd lie!", "\"거짓말이다! 빌어먹을 거짓말이야!"], ["It is all owing to that d--n'd rascal Jones; and if she could get at un, she'd ha un any hour of the day.”", "이게 다 그 빌어먹을 악당 존스 놈 때문이라고. 그놈한테 닿을 수만 있다면, 저 애는 하루 중 어느 때라도 그놈을 가지려 들 거란 말이다.\""], ["Here Allworthy interposed, and addressing himself to the squire with some anger in his look, he said, “Mr Western, you have not kept your word with me.", "여기서 올워디가 끼어들어, 표정에 다소 노기를 띤 채 지주에게 말했다. \"웨스턴 씨, 당신은 제게 한 약속을 지키지 않으셨습니다."], ["You promised to abstain from all violence.”--“Why, so I did,”", "모든 폭력적인 행동을 삼가겠다고 약속하셨잖습니까.\" \"아니, 나도 그랬소,\""], ["cries Western, “as long as it was possible; but to hear a wench telling such confounded lies----Zounds!", "웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"할 수 있는 데까지는 말이오."], ["doth she think, if she can make vools of other volk, she can make one of me?--No, no, I know her better than thee dost.”", "하지만 계집애가 저런 어처구니없는 거짓말을 늘어놓는 걸 듣고서야——제기랄! 다른 사람들은 다 바보로 만들 수 있다고, 나까지 바보로 만들 수 있다고 저 애가 생각하는 거요?——아니, 아니, 나는 당신보다 저 애를 더 잘 안단 말이오.\""], ["“I am sorry to tell you, sir,”", "\"선생, 말씀드리기 송구하나,\""], ["answered Allworthy, “it doth not appear, by your behaviour to this young lady, that you know her at all.", "올워디가 대답했다. \"이 아가씨를 대하는 당신의 태도로 보아서는, 당신이 따님을 조금도 알지 못하시는 것 같습니다."], ["I ask pardon for what I say: but I think our intimacy, your own desires, and the occasion justify me.", "제 말을 용서해 주십시오. 하지만 우리의 친분, 당신 자신의 바람, 그리고 이 상황이 제게 그리 말할 자격을 준다고 봅니다."], ["She is your daughter, Mr Western, and I think she doth honour to your name.", "그녀는 당신의 딸이며, 웨스턴 씨, 저는 그녀가 당신의 이름에 영예를 안긴다고 생각합니다."], ["If I was capable of envy, I should sooner envy you on this account than any other man whatever.”--“Odrabbit it!”", "만일 제가 시기심을 품을 줄 아는 사람이라면, 저는 이 일로 다른 그 누구보다 당신을 먼저 부러워할 것입니다.\" \"젠장맞을!\""], ["cries the squire, “I wish she was thine, with all my heart--wouldst soon be glad to be rid of the trouble o'", "지주가 외쳤다. \"저 애가 진심으로 당신 것이었으면 좋겠소——그러면 저 애를 떠맡은 골칫거리에서 벗어나 곧 기뻐하게 될 테니.\""], ["her.” “Indeed, my good friend,”", "\"정말이지, 나의 좋은 벗이여,\""], ["answered Allworthy, “you yourself are the cause of all the trouble you complain of.", "올워디가 대답했다. \"당신이 한탄하는 그 모든 골칫거리의 원인은 바로 당신 자신입니다."], ["Place that confidence in the young lady which she so well deserves, and I am certain you will be the happiest father on earth.”--“I confidence in her?”", "저 아가씨가 충분히 받을 자격이 있는 그 신뢰를 그녀에게 두십시오. 그러면 분명 당신은 이 땅에서 가장 행복한 아버지가 될 것입니다.\""], ["cries the squire. “'Sblood!", "\"내가 저 애를 신뢰하라고?\""], ["what confidence can I place in her, when she won't do as I would ha' her?", "지주가 외쳤다."], ["Let her gi'", "\"제기랄!"], ["but her consent to marry as I would ha'", "저 애가 내가 바라는 대로 하지 않는데, 내가 무슨 신뢰를 둘 수 있단 말이오?"], ["her, and I'll place as much confidence in her as wouldst ha'", "내가 바라는 대로 결혼하겠다고 동의만 하면, 당신이 바라는 만큼 얼마든지 저 애를 신뢰하리다.\""], ["me.”--“You have no right, neighbour,”", "\"이웃이여, 당신께는,\" 올워디가 대답했다."], ["answered Allworthy, “to insist on any such consent.", "\"그런 동의를 강요할 권리가 없습니다."], ["A negative voice your daughter allows you, and God and nature have thought proper to allow you no more.”--“A negative voice!”", "따님은 당신께 거부권만은 허락하고 있으며, 하느님과 자연은 그 이상은 당신께 허락하는 것이 옳지 않다고 여기셨습니다.\" \"거부권이라!\""], ["cries the squire, “Ay!", "지주가 외쳤다. \"그래!"], ["ay!", "그래!"], ["I'll show you what a negative voice I ha.--Go along, go into your chamber, go, you stubborn----.”", "내 거부권이 어떤 건지 보여 주리다.——가거라, 네 방으로 들어가, 가, 이 고집불통——\""], ["“Indeed, Mr Western,”", "\"정말이지, 웨스턴 씨,\""], ["said Allworthy, “indeed you use her cruelly--I cannot bear to see this--you shall, you must behave to her in a kinder manner.", "올워디가 말했다. \"정말이지 당신은 따님을 모질게 대하고 계십니다——저는 이 광경을 차마 볼 수가 없습니다——당신은 그녀에게 더 다정하게 대하셔야 하고, 또 그러셔야만 합니다."], ["She deserves the best of treatment.”", "그녀는 더없이 좋은 대우를 받을 자격이 있습니다.\""], ["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래,\""], ["said the squire, “I know what she deserves: now she's gone, I'll shew you what she deserves.", "지주가 말했다. \"저 애가 무슨 대우를 받아 마땅한지는 내가 알고 있소."], ["See here, sir, here is a letter from my cousin, my Lady Bellaston, in which she is so kind to gi'", "자, 이제 저 애가 갔으니, 저 애가 무슨 대우를 받아 마땅한지 보여 드리리다. 여기 보시오, 선생, 여기 내 사촌 벨라스턴 부인에게서 온 편지가 있소."], ["me to understand that the fellow is got out of prison again; and here she advises me to take all the care I can o'", "그 안에서 그녀는 친절하게도, 그 녀석이 다시 감옥에서 빠져나왔다는 걸 내게 알려 주었소. 그리고 여기서 저 계집애를 할 수 있는 한 단단히 단속하라고 충고하고 있단 말이오."], ["the wench.", "빌어먹을!"], ["Odzookers! neighbour Allworthy, you don't know what it is to govern a daughter.”", "이웃 올워디, 당신은 딸자식을 다스린다는 게 어떤 건지 모르오.\""]], [["The squire ended his speech with some compliments to his own sagacity; and then Allworthy, after a formal preface, acquainted him with the whole discovery which he had made concerning Jones, with his anger to Blifil, and with every particular which hath been disclosed to the reader in the preceding chapters.", "지주는 자신의 명민함에 대한 몇 마디 자화자찬으로 말을 맺었다. 그러자 올워디는 격식을 갖춘 서두를 단 뒤, 자신이 존스에 관해 알아낸 모든 사실과, 블리필에 대한 자신의 분노, 그리고 앞선 여러 장에서 독자에게 밝혀진 낱낱의 사정을 그에게 알려 주었다."]], [["Men over-violent in their dispositions are, for the most part, as changeable in them.", "기질이 지나치게 격한 사람들은 대개 그 기질만큼이나 변덕도 심한 법이다."], ["No sooner then was Western informed of Mr Allworthy's intention to make Jones his heir, than he joined heartily with the uncle in every commendation of the nephew, and became as eager for her marriage with Jones as he had before been to couple her to Blifil.", "그리하여 웨스턴은 올워디 씨가 존스를 자신의 상속자로 삼으려 한다는 것을 알게 되자마자, 큰아버지에 못지않게 진심으로 조카에 대한 온갖 칭찬에 가담했고, 전에 딸을 블리필과 맺어 주려 안달했던 만큼이나 이제는 딸을 존스와 결혼시키려 열을 올렸다."]], [["Here Mr Allworthy was again forced to interpose, and to relate what had passed between him and Sophia, at which he testified great surprize.", "여기서 올워디 씨는 다시금 끼어들어, 자신과 소피아 사이에 오간 일을 이야기해야 했고, 지주는 그 이야기에 크게 놀라움을 드러냈다."]], [["The squire was silent a moment, and looked wild with astonishment at this account.--At last he cried out, “Why, what can be the meaning of this, neighbour Allworthy?", "지주는 잠시 말이 없었고, 이 이야기에 어리둥절하여 멍하니 놀란 표정을 지었다.——마침내 그는 외쳤다. \"아니, 이게 도대체 무슨 영문이오, 이웃 올워디?"], ["Vond o'un she was, that I'll be sworn to.----Odzookers!", "저 애가 그놈한테 푹 빠져 있었다는 건, 내가 맹세코 단언할 수 있소.——제기랄!"], ["I have hit o't.", "알아챘소."], ["As sure as a gun I have hit o' the very right o't.", "총알처럼 확실하게, 내가 바로 그 진상을 알아챘단 말이오."], ["It's all along o'", "다 누이 때문이오."], ["zister. The girl hath got a hankering after this son of a whore of a lord.", "저 계집애가 그 화냥년의 자식 같은 귀족 놈한테 마음을 두고 있는 거요."], ["I vound 'em together at my cousin my Lady Bellaston's.", "내 사촌 벨라스턴 부인 댁에서 그 둘이 함께 있는 걸 내가 봤단 말이오."], ["He hath turned the head o' her, that's certain--but d--n me if he shall ha her--I'll ha no lords nor courtiers in my vamily.”", "그놈이 저 애 머리를 돌려놓은 게야, 그건 확실하오——하지만 빌어먹을, 그놈이 저 애를 갖게 두진 않겠소——내 집안에는 귀족이든 궁정 신하든 들이지 않을 거요.\""]], [["Allworthy now made a long speech, in which he repeated his resolution to avoid all violent measures, and very earnestly recommended gentle methods to Mr Western, as those by which he might be assured of succeeding best with his daughter. He then took his leave, and returned back to Mrs Miller, but was forced to comply with the earnest entreaties of the squire, in promising to bring Mr Jones to visit him that afternoon, that he might, as he said, “make all matters up with the young gentleman.” At Mr Allworthy's departure, Western promised to follow his advice in his behaviour to Sophia, saying, “I don't know how 'tis, but d--n me, Allworthy, if you don't make me always do just as you please; and yet I have as good an estate as you, and am in the commission of the peace as well as yourself.”", "이제 올워디는 긴 연설을 했는데, 그 안에서 모든 폭력적인 수단을 피하겠다는 자신의 결심을 거듭 밝혔고, 따님에게 가장 잘 통할 수 있는 방법으로서 부드러운 방식을 웨스턴 씨에게 매우 간곡히 권했다. 그러고는 작별을 고하고 밀러 부인에게로 돌아갔으나, 그날 오후에 존스 씨를 데리고 와 그를 방문하게 하겠다는 약속을 하라는 지주의 간곡한 부탁에는 응할 수밖에 없었다. 지주의 말로는, \"그 젊은 신사와 모든 일을 매듭짓기\" 위해서였다. 올워디 씨가 떠날 때, 웨스턴은 소피아를 대하는 태도에서 그의 충고를 따르겠다고 약속하며 이렇게 말했다. \"어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠소만, 빌어먹을, 올워디, 당신은 늘 나를 당신 마음대로 하게 만든단 말이오. 그래도 나는 당신만큼이나 좋은 재산을 가졌고, 당신 못지않게 치안판사직도 맡고 있는 사람인데 말이오.\""]], [["Wherein the history begins to draw towards a conclusion.", "역사가 결말을 향해 다가서기 시작하는 대목."]], [["When Allworthy returned to his lodgings, he heard Mr Jones was just arrived before him.", "올워디가 숙소로 돌아왔을 때, 그는 존스 씨가 자기보다 막 먼저 도착했다는 소식을 들었다."], ["He hurried therefore instantly into an empty chamber, whither he ordered Mr Jones to be brought to him alone.", "그래서 그는 곧장 빈 방으로 서둘러 들어가, 존스 씨를 자기 앞에 홀로 데려오라고 일렀다."]], [["It is impossible to conceive a more tender or moving scene than the meeting between the uncle and nephew (for Mrs Waters, as the reader may well suppose, had at her last visit discovered to him the secret of his birth).", "숙부와 조카 사이의 그 만남보다 더 다정하고 가슴 뭉클한 장면은 도무지 상상할 수 없을 것이다(독자도 능히 짐작하겠지만, 워터스 부인이 마지막 방문에서 그에게 출생의 비밀을 밝혀 주었던 것이다). 양쪽 모두가 느낀 그 첫 기쁨의 격정은 실로 내 필력으로 그려낼 수 있는 범위를 넘어선다."], ["The first agonies of joy which were felt on both sides are indeed beyond my power to describe: I shall not therefore attempt it.", "그러니 나는 그것을 시도하지 않겠다. 올워디는 무릎 꿇고 엎드려 있던 존스를 일으켜 두 팔로 안고 나서 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["After Allworthy had raised Jones from his feet, where he had prostrated himself, and received him into his arms, “O my child!”", "\"오, 내 아들아! 내가 얼마나 잘못했더냐!"], ["he cried, “how have I been to blame!", "내가 너를 얼마나 해쳤더냐! 내가 품었던 그 매정하고 부당한 의심들, 그리고 그것이 네게 안긴 그 모든 고통에 대해, 내가 무엇으로 보상할 수 있겠느냐?\""], ["how have I injured you!", "\"이미 보상받지 않았습니까?\" 존스가 외쳤다."], ["What amends can I ever make you for those unkind, those unjust suspicions which I have entertained, and for all the sufferings they have occasioned to you?”", "\"제 고통이 설령 열 배는 더 컸더라도, 지금 이렇게 넘치도록 보답받지 않았겠습니까? 오, 그리운 숙부님, 이 자애로움, 이 다정함이 저를 압도하고, 무너뜨리고, 사라지게 합니다."], ["“Am I not now made amends?”", "이토록 빠르게 밀려드는 환희를 저는 견뎌낼 수가 없습니다. 다시 숙부님 곁으로, 숙부님의 총애 안으로 돌아오다니."], ["cries Jones.", "위대하고 고귀하며 너그러우신 저의 은인께 이렇게 다시 한번 따뜻하게 받아들여지다니.\" \"정말이지, 아들아.\""], ["“Would not my sufferings, if they had been ten times greater, have been now richly repaid?", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"나는 너를 모질게 대했구나.\""], ["O my dear uncle, this goodness, this tenderness overpowers, unmans, destroys me.", "그러고는 블리필의 모든 배신을 그에게 낱낱이 설명하고, 그 배신에 이끌려 그를 그토록 못되게 대한 것에 대해 더없는 비통함의 말을 거듭 되풀이했다. \"오, 그런 말씀 마십시오!\""], ["I cannot bear the transports which flow so fast upon me.", "존스가 대답했다. \"정말이지, 숙부님, 숙부님은 저를 고결하게 대하셨습니다."], ["To be again restored to your presence, to your favour; to be once more thus kindly received by my great, my noble, my generous benefactor.”--“Indeed, child,”", "아무리 지혜로운 사람이라도 숙부님처럼 속을 수 있었을 것이며, 그런 기만 아래서라면 가장 훌륭한 사람도 꼭 숙부님처럼 행동했을 것입니다. 숙부님의 자애로움은 분노의 한복판에서도, 바로 그때조차 그렇게 보였듯 그 모습을 드러냈습니다."], ["cries Allworthy, “I have used you cruelly.”----He then explained to him all the treachery of Blifil, and again repeated expressions of the utmost concern, for having been induced by that treachery to use him so ill.", "저는 그 자애로움에 모든 것을 빚졌으며, 그러면서도 저는 그것을 누릴 자격이 가장 없는 사람이었습니다. 너그러운 마음을 너무 멀리까지 밀고 나가시어 저를 자책에 빠뜨리지 말아 주십시오."], ["“O, talk not so!”", "아아!"], ["answered Jones; “indeed, sir, you have used me nobly.", "숙부님, 저는 마땅히 받아야 할 것보다 더 큰 벌을 받은 것이 아니며, 앞으로 제 남은 삶의 모든 일은 숙부님께서 지금 베풀어 주시는 그 행복을 누릴 자격을 갖추는 데 바칠 것입니다. 그리운 숙부님, 믿어 주십시오."], ["The wisest man might be deceived as you were; and, under such a deception, the best must have acted just as you did.", "제가 받은 벌은 헛되이 흘려보내지 않았습니다. 저는 큰 죄인이긴 했어도, 마음이 굳어버린 죄인은 아닙니다."], ["Your goodness displayed itself in the midst of your anger, just as it then seemed.", "하늘에 감사하게도, 저는 지난 삶을 돌아볼 시간을 가졌습니다. 거기서 저는 비록 어떤 큰 악행으로 스스로를 책할 수는 없으나, 뉘우치고 부끄러워하기에 차고도 넘칠 만큼 많은 어리석음과 악덕을 분명히 알아보았습니다."], ["I owe everything to that goodness, of which I have been most unworthy.", "그 어리석음은 제 자신에게 끔찍한 결과를 가져왔고, 저를 파멸의 벼랑 끝까지 몰아갔습니다.\" \"기쁘구나, 내 아들아.\""], ["Do not put me on self-accusation, by carrying your generous sentiments too far.", "올워디가 대답했다. \"네가 이렇게 분별 있게 말하는 것을 들으니 기쁘다."], ["Alas!", "위선(맙소사!"], ["sir, I have not been punished more than I have deserved; and it shall be the whole business of my future life to deserve that happiness you now bestow on me; for, believe me, my dear uncle, my punishment hath not been thrown away upon me: though I have been a great, I am not a hardened sinner; I thank Heaven, I have had time to reflect on my past life, where, though I cannot charge myself with any gross villany, yet I can discern follies and vices more than enough to repent and to be ashamed of; follies which have been attended with dreadful consequences to myself, and have brought me to the brink of destruction.”", "내가 남들의 위선에 얼마나 속아 넘어갔던가!)은 결코 네 결점 가운데 있지 않았다고 나는 확신하기에, 네가 하는 말을 모두 기꺼이 믿을 수 있구나. 톰, 너는 이제 보았겠지."], ["“I am rejoiced, my dear child,”", "경솔함 하나만으로도 덕이 어떤 위험에 놓일 수 있는지를(이제 나는 네가 덕을 크게 사랑한다고 확신한다). 신중함이란 실로 우리가 우리 자신에게 마땅히 져야 할 의무다."], ["answered Allworthy, “to hear you talk thus sensibly; for as I am convinced hypocrisy (good Heaven!", "우리가 그것을 소홀히 할 만큼 스스로의 적이 된다면, 세상이 우리에 대한 의무를 다하지 못한다 한들 놀랄 일이 아니지. 사람이 제 파멸의 기초를 스스로 놓으면, 안타깝게도 남들은 그 위에 너무도 쉽게 무언가를 쌓아 올리려 들기 마련이니까."], ["how have I been imposed on by it in others!) was never among your faults, so I can readily believe all you say.", "그러나 너는 네 잘못을 깨달았고, 그것을 고치겠노라 말하는구나. 나는 너를 굳게 믿는다, 내 아들아."], ["You now see, Tom, to what dangers imprudence alone may subject virtue (for virtue, I am now convinced, you love in a great degree).", "그러니 이 순간부터 나는 결코 그 잘못들을 다시 들먹여 네게 일깨우지 않겠다. 다만 너 스스로는, 앞으로 그것들을 더 잘 피하도록 일러줄 만큼만 기억해 두어라."], ["Prudence is indeed the duty which we owe to ourselves; and if we will be so much our own enemies as to neglect it, we are not to wonder if the world is deficient in discharging their duty to us; for when a man lays the foundation of his own ruin, others will, I am afraid, be too apt to build upon it.", "그러나 위안 삼아 이것만은 기억하여라. 너그러운 마음으로 보아 경솔함이라 해석될 수 있는 결점과, 오로지 악행에서 비롯되었다고밖에 볼 수 없는 결점 사이에는 이 큰 차이가 있다는 것을."], ["You say, however, you have seen your errors, and will reform them.", "앞엣것은 어쩌면 사람을 파멸로 몰아넣는 데 더 쉬울지도 모른다."], ["I firmly believe you, my dear child; and therefore, from this moment, you shall never be reminded of them by me.", "그러나 그가 잘못을 고치면, 그의 명예는 마침내 온전히 회복될 것이다. 세상은 당장은 아닐지라도 시간이 지나면 그와 화해할 것이며, 그는 자신이 벗어난 위험을 돌아보며 어느 정도 즐거움이 섞인 회상을 할 수도 있을 것이다."], ["Remember them only yourself so far as for the future to teach you the better to avoid them; but still remember, for your comfort, that there is this great difference between those faults which candor may construe into imprudence, and those which can be deduced from villany only.", "그러나 악행이란, 내 아이야, 일단 드러나면 돌이킬 수 없다. 그것이 남기는 얼룩은 어떤 세월로도 씻어낼 수 없다."], ["The former, perhaps, are even more apt to subject a man to ruin; but if he reform, his character will, at length, be totally retrieved; the world, though not immediately, will in time be reconciled to him; and he may reflect, not without some mixture of pleasure, on the dangers he hath escaped; but villany, my boy, when once discovered is irretrievable; the stains which this leaves behind, no time will wash away.", "인류의 비난이 그 가련한 자를 쫓을 것이며, 그들의 경멸이 그를 사람들 앞에서 부끄럽게 할 것이다. 그리고 수치심이 그를 은둔으로 내몰면, 그는 도깨비를 무서워하는 지친 아이가 사람들 곁을 떠나 홀로 잠자리에 들 때 느끼는 그 모든 공포를 안고 그 은둔으로 들어갈 것이다."], ["The censures of mankind will pursue the wretch, their scorn will abash him in publick; and if shame drives him into retirement, he will go to it with all those terrors with which a weary child, who is afraid of hobgoblins, retreats from company to go to bed alone.", "그곳에서 그가 죽여버린 양심이 그를 따라다니며 괴롭힐 것이다. 안식은 거짓된 벗처럼 그에게서 달아날 것이다."], ["Here his murdered conscience will haunt him.--Repose, like a false friend, will fly from him.", "어디로 눈을 돌리든 공포가 그 앞에 나타날 것이다. 뒤를 돌아보면 아무 소용없는 후회가 발꿈치를 밟고 따라오고, 앞을 보면 고칠 길 없는 절망이 얼굴을 노려본다."], ["Wherever he turns his eyes, horror presents itself; if he looks backward, unavailable repentance treads on his heels; if forward, incurable despair stares him in the face, till, like a condemned prisoner confined in a dungeon, he detests his present condition, and yet dreads the consequence of that hour which is to relieve him from it.", "그리하여 그는 지하 감옥에 갇힌 사형수처럼 지금의 처지를 혐오하면서도, 그 처지에서 자신을 풀어줄 그 시간이 가져올 결말을 두려워하게 되지. 다시 말하건대, 내 아들아, 이것이 네 경우가 아님을 위안으로 삼아라."], ["Comfort yourself, I say, my child, that this is not your case; and rejoice with thankfulness to him who hath suffered you to see your errors, before they have brought on you that destruction to which a persistance in even those errors must have led you.", "그리고 네 잘못이, 그 잘못을 고집했다면 반드시 너를 이끌고 갔을 그 파멸을 가져오기 전에, 너로 하여금 잘못을 깨닫게 해주신 그분께 감사함으로 기뻐하여라. 너는 그 잘못들을 버렸고, 이제 네 앞에 펼쳐진 전망은 행복이 마치 네 손안에 있는 듯하구나.\""], ["You have deserted them; and the prospect now before you is such, that happiness seems in your own power.”", "이 말에 존스는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 올워디가 그것을 나무라자 그가 말했다."], ["At these words Jones fetched a deep sigh; upon which, when Allworthy remonstrated, he said, “Sir, I will conceal nothing from you: I fear there is one consequence of my vices I shall never be able to retrieve.", "\"숙부님, 저는 숙부님께 아무것도 숨기지 않겠습니다. 제 악덕이 빚은 한 가지 결과만은 결코 되돌릴 수 없을까 두렵습니다."], ["O, my dear uncle!", "오, 그리운 숙부님!"], ["I have lost a treasure.”", "저는 보물을 잃었습니다.\" \"더 말할 것 없다.\""], ["“You need say no more,”", "올워디가 대답했다. \"내가 솔직히 말하마."], ["answered Allworthy; “I will be explicit with you; I know what you lament; I have seen the young lady, and have discoursed with her concerning you.", "나는 네가 무엇을 한탄하는지 안다. 나는 그 젊은 아가씨를 만났고, 너에 관해 그녀와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["This I must insist on, as an earnest of your sincerity in all you have said, and of the stedfastness of your resolution, that you obey me in one instance.", "네가 한 모든 말의 진심과 네 결심의 굳건함을 증명하는 표시로서, 한 가지만은 나를 따라야 한다고 나는 단단히 일러두겠다. 그 결과가 네게 유리하든 아니든, 전적으로 그 젊은 아가씨의 결정에 따르는 것이다."], ["To abide intirely by the determination of the young lady, whether it shall be in your favour or no.", "그녀는 이미 내가 생각하기조차 싫은 그 끈질긴 구애로 충분히 고통받았다. 그녀는 더 이상 내 집안 탓에 어떤 속박도 짊어져서는 안 된다."], ["She hath already suffered enough from solicitations which I hate to think of; she shall owe no further constraint to my family: I know her father will be as ready to torment her now on your account as he hath formerly been on another's; but I am determined she shall suffer no more confinement, no more violence, no more uneasy hours.”", "나는 그녀의 아버지가 예전에 다른 사람을 두고 그러했듯, 이제는 너를 두고 그녀를 괴롭히려 들리라는 것을 안다. 그러나 나는 그녀가 더 이상 어떤 감금도, 어떤 폭력도, 어떤 괴로운 시간도 겪지 않게 하기로 굳게 마음먹었다.\""], ["“O, my dear uncle!”", "\"오, 그리운 숙부님!\""], ["answered Jones, “lay, I beseech you, some command on me, in which I shall have some merit in obedience.", "존스가 대답했다. \"부디 청하오니, 따르는 데 제게 어떤 공이 돌아갈 만한 명령을 내려 주십시오."], ["Believe me, sir, the only instance in which I could disobey you would be to give an uneasy moment to my Sophia.", "믿어 주십시오, 숙부님, 제가 숙부님께 거역할 수 있는 유일한 경우란, 제 소피아에게 괴로운 한순간이라도 안기는 일뿐일 것입니다."], ["No, sir, if I am so miserable to have incurred her displeasure beyond all hope of forgiveness, that alone, with the dreadful reflection of causing her misery, will be sufficient to overpower me.", "아닙니다, 숙부님, 만일 제가 너무도 비참하여 용서의 희망조차 모두 넘어설 만큼 그녀의 노여움을 샀다면, 그것 하나만으로도, 그리고 제가 그녀의 불행의 원인이 되었다는 그 끔찍한 생각만으로도 저를 무너뜨리기에 충분할 것입니다. 소피아를 제 사람이라 부르는 것이야말로 하늘이 베풀 수 있는 가장 크고, 이제는 유일하게 남은 추가의 축복입니다."], ["To call Sophia mine is the greatest, and now the only additional blessing which heaven can bestow; but it is a blessing which I must owe to her alone.”", "그러나 그것은 오로지 그녀에게서만 받을 수 있는 축복입니다.\" \"너에게 듣기 좋은 말은 하지 않으마, 아들아.\""], ["“I will not flatter you, child,”", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"네 처지가 절망적일까 두렵구나."], ["cries Allworthy; “I fear your case is desperate: I never saw stronger marks of an unalterable resolution in any person than appeared in her vehement declarations against receiving your addresses; for which, perhaps, you can account better than myself.”", "네 구애를 받아들이지 않겠다는 그녀의 격렬한 선언에서 드러난 것만큼, 그 어떤 사람에게서도 흔들림 없는 결심의 강한 표시를 본 적이 없다. 그 까닭은 어쩌면 나보다 네가 더 잘 설명할 수 있겠지.\""], ["“Oh, sir!", "\"오, 숙부님!"], ["I can account too well,”", "저는 너무도 잘 설명할 수 있습니다.\" 존스가 대답했다."], ["answered Jones; “I have sinned against her beyond all hope of pardon; and guilty as I am, my guilt unfortunately appears to her in ten times blacker than the real colours.", "\"저는 그녀에게 용서받을 희망을 모두 넘어설 만큼 죄를 지었습니다. 게다가 죄인인 제 죄가, 불행히도 그녀의 눈에는 실제 빛깔보다 열 배는 더 시커멓게 비치고 있습니다."], ["O, my dear uncle!", "오, 그리운 숙부님!"], ["I find my follies are irretrievable; and all your goodness cannot save me from perdition.”", "제 어리석음은 돌이킬 수 없는 것임을 깨닫습니다. 숙부님의 그 모든 자애로움으로도 저를 파멸에서 구해낼 수 없습니다.\""]], [["A servant now acquainted them that Mr Western was below-stairs; for his eagerness to see Jones could not wait till the afternoon.", "이때 하인이 웨스턴 씨가 아래층에 와 있다고 두 사람에게 알렸다. 존스를 보고 싶은 그의 조바심이 오후까지 기다릴 수 없었던 것이다."], ["Upon which Jones, whose eyes were full of tears, begged his uncle to entertain Western a few minutes, till he a little recovered himself; to which the good man consented, and, having ordered Mr Western to be shewn into a parlour, went down to him.", "그러자 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 존스는 자기가 조금 마음을 추스를 때까지 몇 분만 웨스턴을 상대해 달라고 숙부에게 청했다. 마음씨 좋은 그는 이에 응하여, 웨스턴 씨를 응접실로 안내하라 이른 뒤 그에게로 내려갔다."]], [["Mrs Miller no sooner heard that Jones was alone (for she had not yet seen him since his release from prison) than she came eagerly into the room, and, advancing towards Jones, wished him heartily joy of his new-found uncle and his happy reconciliation; adding, “I wish I could give you joy on another account, my dear child; but anything so inexorable I never saw.”", "밀러 부인은 존스가 혼자 있다는 말을 듣자마자(그가 감옥에서 풀려난 뒤로 그녀는 아직 그를 만나지 못한 터였다) 다급히 방으로 들어와, 존스에게 다가가며 새로 찾은 숙부와 그 행복한 화해를 진심으로 축하해 주었다. 그러고는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"다른 일로도 축하를 드릴 수 있으면 좋으련만, 내 사랑하는 도련님, 저토록 요지부동인 사람은 정말이지 본 적이 없답니다.\""]], [["Jones, with some appearance of surprize, asked her what she meant.", "존스는 다소 놀란 기색으로 그것이 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었다. \"그러니까요.\""], ["“Why then,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"제가 도련님의 아가씨를 찾아가서, 제 사위 나이팅게일이 제게 들려준 그대로 모든 일을 그녀에게 설명했답니다."], ["says she, “I have been with your young lady, and have explained all matters to her, as they were told to me by my son Nightingale.", "그녀는 이제 그 편지에 대해 더 이상 의심할 수 없을 거예요. 그건 제가 확신해요."], ["She can have no longer any doubt about the letter; of that I am certain; for I told her my son Nightingale was ready to take his oath, if she pleased, that it was all his own invention, and the letter of his inditing.", "그녀가 원한다면 제 사위 나이팅게일이 그것이 모두 자기가 꾸며낸 것이며 편지도 자기가 지어낸 것이라고 맹세할 준비가 되어 있다고 제가 그녀에게 말했으니까요. 그 편지를 보낸 바로 그 이유야말로 도련님을 그녀에게 더 좋게 천거해 주어야 마땅하다고 제가 말했지요."], ["I told her the very reason of sending the letter ought to recommend you to her the more, as it was all upon her account, and a plain proof that you was resolved to quit all your profligacy for the future; that you had never been guilty of a single instance of infidelity to her since your seeing her in town: I am afraid I went too far there; but Heaven forgive me!", "그것은 모두 그녀를 위한 일이었고, 도련님이 앞으로 모든 방탕을 끊겠다고 결심했다는 분명한 증거이며, 도련님이 시내에서 그녀를 만난 뒤로 단 한 번도 그녀에게 불충했던 적이 없다고 말이에요. 그 대목에서는 제가 너무 멀리 나갔나 봐요."], ["I hope your future behaviour will be my justification.", "하지만 하늘이 용서하시기를! 앞으로의 도련님 행실이 저의 변명이 되어 주기를 바라요."], ["I am sure I have said all I can; but all to no purpose.", "정말이지 저는 할 수 있는 말은 다 했답니다. 그러나 모두 헛수고였어요."], ["She remains inflexible.", "그녀는 요지부동이에요. 그녀가 말하길, 젊음을 봐서 여러 잘못은 용서했지만, 난봉꾼의 성품에 대해서는 어찌나 혐오를 드러내던지 저를 완전히 입 다물게 만들더군요."], ["She says, she had forgiven many faults on account of youth; but expressed such detestation of the character of a libertine, that she absolutely silenced me.", "저는 몇 번이나 도련님을 변호하려 했지만, 그녀의 비난이 너무도 정당해서 제 얼굴을 후려치는 듯했어요. 맹세코, 그녀는 사랑스러운 여인이고, 제가 본 가장 다정하고 분별 있는 사람 가운데 하나예요."], ["I often attempted to excuse you; but the justness of her accusation flew in my face.", "그녀가 한 어떤 말에는 거의 입을 맞추고 싶을 지경이었답니다. 세네카나 주교에게나 어울릴 만한 통찰이었거든요."], ["Upon my honour, she is a lovely woman, and one of the sweetest and most sensible creatures I ever saw.", "제가 '저는 한때 생각했습니다, 아가씨' 하고 말하니, 그녀가 이러더군요."], ["I could have almost kissed her for one expression she made use of.", "'저는 존스 씨에게서 큰 마음의 선함을 발견했고, 그 점에 대해서는 진심으로 존경했음을 인정합니다. 그러나 행실이 송두리째 방탕에 빠지면 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 마음도 썩어버리는 법이며, 천성이 선한 난봉꾼이 바랄 수 있는 것이라고는 우리가 경멸과 혐오에 한 줌의 연민을 섞어 주는 정도뿐이지요.'"], ["It was a sentiment worthy of Seneca, or of a bishop.", "그녀는 천사 같은 사람이에요, 그게 진실이랍니다.\" \"오, 밀러 부인!\""], ["`I once fancied madam.'", "존스가 대답했다. \"제가 그런 천사를 잃었다는 생각을 어찌 견딜 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["and she, `I had discovered great goodness of heart in Mr Jones; and for that I own I had a sincere esteem; but an entire profligacy of manners will corrupt the best heart in the world; and all which a good-natured libertine can expect is, that we should mix some grains of pity with our contempt and abhorrence.'", "\"잃었다니요! 아니에요.\""], ["She is an angelic creature, that is the truth on't.”", "밀러 부인이 외쳤다. \"아직 그녀를 잃은 건 아니길 바라요."], ["“O, Mrs Miller!”", "그런 사악한 길을 떠나기로 결심하세요. 그러면 아직 희망이 있을 거예요."], ["answered Jones, “can I bear to think that I have lost such an angel?”", "아니, 설령 그녀가 끝내 요지부동이라 해도, 또 다른 젊은 아가씨가 있어요. 다정하고 어여쁜 데다 어마어마한 재산을 가진 아가씨가, 도련님을 향한 사랑에 완전히 애가 타고 있답니다."], ["“Lost! no,”", "바로 오늘 아침에 그 소식을 듣고 웨스턴 아가씨에게 일러주었어요."], ["cries Mrs Miller; “I hope you have not lost her yet.", "아니, 제가 또 진실에서 조금 벗어났네요. 도련님이 그녀를 거절했다고 말했거든요."], ["Resolve to leave such vicious courses, and you may yet have hopes, nay, if she would remain inexorable, there is another young lady, a sweet pretty young lady, and a swinging fortune, who is absolutely dying for love of you.", "하지만 도련님이 그녀를 거절하리라는 걸 저는 정말 알고 있었어요. 그리고 여기서 도련님에게 조금 위안을 드려야겠어요."], ["I heard of it this very morning, and I told it to Miss Western; nay, I went a little beyond the truth again; for I told her you had refused her; but indeed I knew you would refuse her.", "제가 그 젊은 아가씨의 이름을 댔을 때, 그 사람은 다름 아닌 어여쁜 과부 헌트였는데, 그녀의 얼굴이 창백해지는 것 같았어요. 그런데 도련님이 그녀를 거절했다고 하니, 맹세컨대 그녀의 얼굴이 순식간에 온통 진홍빛으로 물들더군요."], ["And here I must give you a little comfort; when I mentioned the young lady's name, who is no other than the pretty widow Hunt, I thought she turned pale; but when I said you had refused her, I will be sworn her face was all over scarlet in an instant; and these were her very words: `I will not deny but that I believe he has some affection for me.'”", "그리고 그녀가 한 말이 바로 이거예요. '그분이 저에게 어느 정도 애정을 품고 있다는 것은 부인하지 않겠어요.'\""]], [["Here the conversation was interrupted by the arrival of Western, who could no longer be kept out of the room even by the authority of Allworthy himself; though this, as we have often seen, had a wonderful power over him.", "여기서 대화는 웨스턴이 들이닥치는 바람에 끊기고 말았다. 그는 올워디 본인의 권위로도 더 이상 방 밖에 붙들어 둘 수가 없었다. 우리가 여러 번 보아 왔듯, 그 권위가 그에게는 놀라운 힘을 발휘했음에도 말이다."]], [["Western immediately went up to Jones, crying out, “My old friend Tom, I am glad to see thee with all my heart! all past must be forgotten; I could not intend any affront to thee, because, as Allworthy here knows, nay, dost know it thyself, I took thee for another person; and where a body means no harm, what signifies a hasty word or two? One Christian must forget and forgive another.” “I hope, sir,” said Jones, “I shall never forget the many obligations I have had to you; but as for any offence towards me, I declare I am an utter stranger.” “A't,” says Western, “then give me thy fist; a't as hearty an honest cock as any in the kingdom. Come along with me; I'll carry thee to thy mistress this moment.” Here Allworthy interposed; and the squire being unable to prevail either with the uncle or nephew, was, after some litigation, obliged to consent to delay introducing Jones to Sophia till the afternoon; at which time Allworthy, as well in compassion to Jones as in compliance with the eager desires of Western, was prevailed upon to promise to attend at the tea-table.", "웨스턴은 곧장 존스에게 다가가며 외쳤다. \"내 오랜 친구 톰, 자넬 보니 진심으로 기쁘이! 지나간 일은 모두 잊어야지. 내가 자네한테 어떤 모욕을 줄 뜻은 있을 수 없었네. 여기 올워디도 알다시피, 아니, 자네 자신도 알다시피, 나는 자넬 딴 사람으로 여겼으니 말일세. 게다가 해칠 뜻이 없는데, 성급한 말 한두 마디가 무슨 대수란 말인가? 한 기독교도는 다른 기독교도를 잊고 또 용서해야 하는 법이지.\" \"어르신,\" 존스가 말했다. \"제가 어르신께 진 그 많은 은혜를 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다. 하지만 저를 향한 모욕에 대해서라면, 저는 전혀 모르는 일이라고 분명히 말씀드립니다.\" \"그렇구먼.\" 웨스턴이 말했다. \"그럼 자네 주먹을 이리 내게. 자넨 이 왕국의 어느 누구 못지않게 씩씩하고 정직한 사내일세. 나랑 같이 가세. 지금 당장 자넬 자네 아가씨한테 데려다주겠네.\" 이때 올워디가 끼어들었다. 향사는 숙부에게도 조카에게도 뜻을 관철하지 못한 채, 옥신각신한 끝에 존스를 소피아에게 소개하는 일을 오후로 미루는 데 동의할 수밖에 없었다. 그 시각에 올워디는, 존스에 대한 연민에서나 웨스턴의 간절한 바람에 응하는 뜻에서나, 다과 자리에 함께하겠노라 약속하도록 설득당했다."]], [["The conversation which now ensued was pleasant enough; and with which, had it happened earlier in our history, we would have entertained our reader; but as we have now leisure only to attend to what is very material, it shall suffice to say that matters being entirely adjusted as to the afternoon visit Mr Western again returned home.", "뒤이어 오간 대화는 제법 유쾌했다. 만일 그것이 우리 이야기의 더 앞쪽에서 일어났더라면 우리는 그것으로 독자를 즐겁게 해드렸을 것이다. 그러나 이제는 정말로 중요한 일만 살필 여유밖에 없으니, 오후 방문에 관한 일이 모두 매듭지어진 뒤 웨스턴 씨가 다시 집으로 돌아갔다고만 말해 두면 충분하겠다."]], [["The history draws nearer to a conclusion. When Mr Western was departed, Jones began to inform Mr Allworthy and Mrs Miller that his liberty had been procured by two noble lords, who, together with two surgeons and a friend of Mr Nightingale's, had attended the magistrate by whom he had been committed, and by whom, on the surgeons'", "웨스턴 씨가 떠나자, 존스는 올워디 씨와 밀러 부인에게 자신의 석방이 두 귀족의 힘으로 이루어졌음을 알리기 시작했다."], ["oaths, that the wounded person was out of all manner of danger from his wound, he was discharged.", "그 두 귀족이 외과의 두 사람과 나이팅게일 씨의 친구 한 사람을 데리고, 자신을 수감했던 치안판사를 찾아갔으며, 외과의들이 다친 자가 그 상처로 인해 결코 어떤 위험에도 처해 있지 않다고 선서하자, 그 판사에게서 풀려나게 되었다는 것이다."]], [["One only of these lords, he said, he had ever seen before, and that no more than once; but the other had greatly surprized him by asking his pardon for an offence he had been guilty of towards him, occasioned, he said, entirely by his ignorance who he was.", "그가 말하기를, 그 두 귀족 가운데 한 사람은 전에 본 적이 있는데 그것도 단 한 번뿐이었고, 다른 한 사람은 자신에게 저지른 잘못을 사과하며 자신을 크게 놀라게 했다는 것이었다. 그 잘못이란, 그 귀족의 말로는 자신이 누구인지를 전혀 몰랐던 데서 비롯된 것이라 했다."]], [["Now the reality of the case, with which Jones was not acquainted till afterwards, was this:--The lieutenant whom Lord Fellamar had employed, according to the advice of Lady Bellaston, to press Jones as a vagabond into the sea-service, when he came to report to his lordship the event which we have before seen, spoke very favourably of the behaviour of Mr Jones on all accounts, and strongly assured that lord that he must have mistaken the person, for that Jones was certainly a gentleman; insomuch that his lordship, who was strictly a man of honour, and would by no means have been guilty of an action which the world in general would have condemned, began to be much concerned for the advice which he had taken.", "존스가 나중에야 알게 된 이 사건의 진상은 이러했다. 펠라마 경이 벨라스턴 부인의 조언에 따라 존스를 부랑자로 몰아 강제로 해군에 끌어넣으려고 부린 중위는, 우리가 앞서 본 그 사건의 결말을 보고하러 경에게 왔을 때, 모든 면에서 존스 씨의 처신을 매우 호의적으로 이야기했다. 그리고 자신이 사람을 잘못 보았던 게 틀림없다고, 존스는 분명 신사라고 그 경에게 단호히 장담했다. 그리하여 엄격하게 명예를 지키는 사람이며 세상이 두루 비난할 만한 행동은 결코 저지르려 하지 않았던 경은, 자신이 받아들인 그 조언을 두고 크게 마음을 졸이기 시작했다."]], [["Within a day or two after this, Lord Fellamar happened to dine with the Irish peer, who, in a conversation upon the duel, acquainted his company with the character of Fitzpatrick; to which, indeed, he did not do strict justice, especially in what related to his lady. He said she was the most innocent, the most injured woman alive, and that from compassion alone he had undertaken her cause. He then declared an intention of going the next morning to Fitzpatrick's lodgings, in order to prevail with him, if possible, to consent to a separation from his wife, who, the peer said, was in apprehensions for her life, if she should ever return to be under the power of her husband. Lord Fellamar agreed to go with him, that he might satisfy himself more concerning Jones and the circumstances of the duel; for he was by no means easy concerning the part he had acted. The moment his lordship gave a hint of his readiness to assist in the delivery of the lady, it was eagerly embraced by the other nobleman, who depended much on the authority of Lord Fellamar, as he thought it would greatly contribute to awe Fitzpatrick into a compliance; and perhaps he was in the right; for the poor Irishman no sooner saw these noble peers had undertaken the cause of his wife than he submitted, and articles of separation were soon drawn up and signed between the parties.", "이 일이 있은 지 하루 이틀 뒤, 펠라마 경은 마침 그 아일랜드 귀족과 함께 식사를 하게 되었는데, 그가 결투에 관한 이야기를 나누던 중 자리에 함께 있던 사람들에게 피츠패트릭의 인물됨을 알려 주었다. 다만 그는, 특히 그 부인에 관한 일에서는 사실 엄정한 평가를 내리지 못했다. 그는 그 부인이 살아 있는 여인 가운데 가장 결백하고 가장 부당하게 상처받은 사람이며, 자신은 오로지 연민에서 그녀의 편을 들기로 했다고 말했다. 그러고는 다음 날 아침 피츠패트릭의 거처를 찾아가, 가능하다면 그를 설득해 아내와의 별거에 동의하게 만들 생각이라고 밝혔다. 그 귀족의 말로는, 부인이 다시 남편의 손아귀에 들어가게 된다면 목숨을 잃을까 두려워하고 있다는 것이었다. 펠라마 경은 그와 함께 가기로 했다. 존스와 결투의 정황에 대해 좀 더 확실히 마음을 가다듬고 싶었기 때문이었다. 그는 자신이 한 짓을 두고 결코 마음이 편치 않았던 것이다. 경이 부인을 구해 내는 일을 돕겠다는 뜻을 슬쩍 비치는 순간, 다른 귀족은 그것을 덥석 받아들였다. 그는 펠라마 경의 권위에 크게 기대고 있었으니, 그것이 피츠패트릭을 위협해 순순히 따르게 하는 데 큰 보탬이 되리라 여겼던 것이다. 어쩌면 그 생각이 옳았다. 가엾은 아일랜드 사내는 이 고귀한 귀족들이 자기 아내의 편을 들고 나선 것을 보자마자 굴복했고, 별거 약정서가 곧 작성되어 양쪽이 서명을 마쳤기 때문이다."]], [["Fitzpatrick, who had been so well satisfied by Mrs Waters concerning the innocence of his wife with Jones at Upton, or perhaps, from some other reasons, was now become so indifferent to that matter, that he spoke highly in favour of Jones to Lord Fellamar, took all the blame upon himself, and said the other had behaved very much like a gentleman and a man of honour; and upon that lord's further enquiry concerning Mr Jones, Fitzpatrick told him he was nephew to a gentleman of very great fashion and fortune, which was the account he had just received from Mrs Waters after her interview with Dowling.", "피츠패트릭은 어퍼튼에서 존스와 자기 아내 사이에 아무 일도 없었다는 점에 대해 워터스 부인에게서 충분히 만족스러운 해명을 들은 터였고, 아니면 어쩌면 다른 어떤 이유에서였는지, 이제는 그 문제에 너무도 무관심해져서 펠라마 경에게 존스를 한껏 두둔하며 모든 잘못을 자기 탓으로 돌렸다. 그러면서 상대방은 신사답게, 또 명예를 아는 사람답게 행동했다고 말했다. 그 경이 존스 씨에 관해 더 캐묻자, 피츠패트릭은 그가 대단히 지체 높고 재산도 많은 어느 신사의 조카라고 일러 주었는데, 이는 워터스 부인이 다울링과 만난 뒤 그에게 막 전해 준 이야기였다."]], [["Lord Fellamar now thought it behoved him to do everything in his power to make satisfaction to a gentleman whom he had so grossly injured, and without any consideration of rivalship (for he had now given over all thoughts of Sophia), determined to procure Mr Jones's liberty, being satisfied, as well from Fitzpatrick as his surgeon, that the wound was not mortal.", "이제 펠라마 경은 자신이 그토록 심하게 해를 끼친 신사에게 마땅히 할 수 있는 모든 일로 보상을 해야 한다고 여겼다. 그리고 연적 따위는 조금도 염두에 두지 않은 채(이제는 소피아에 대한 생각을 모두 접은 터였으므로), 피츠패트릭에게서나 그 외과의에게서나 상처가 치명적이지 않다는 확신을 얻고는 존스 씨의 석방을 도모하기로 마음먹었다."], ["He therefore prevailed with the Irish peer to accompany him to the place where Jones was confined, to whom he behaved as we have already related.", "그리하여 그는 아일랜드 귀족을 설득해 존스가 갇혀 있는 곳까지 함께 가게 되었고, 그곳에서 우리가 이미 이야기한 대로 존스를 대했던 것이다."]], [["When Allworthy returned to his lodgings, he immediately carried Jones into his room, and then acquainted him with the whole matter, as well what he had heard from Mrs Waters as what he had discovered from Mr Dowling.", "올워디는 자신의 거처로 돌아오자마자 존스를 곧장 자기 방으로 데리고 들어가, 워터스 부인에게서 들은 것이며 다울링 씨에게서 알아낸 것이며 모든 일을 그에게 일러 주었다."]], [["Jones expressed great astonishment and no less concern at this account, but without making any comment or observation upon it.", "존스는 이 이야기에 크게 놀라고 그에 못지않게 마음 쓰는 기색을 보였으나, 거기에 대해 아무 토도, 아무 견해도 달지 않았다."], ["And now a message was brought from Mr Blifil, desiring to know if his uncle was at leisure that he might wait upon him.", "그때 블리필 씨로부터, 외삼촌이 한가하신지 알고 싶다는, 찾아뵙고자 한다는 전갈이 왔다."], ["Allworthy started and turned pale, and then in a more passionate tone than I believe he had ever used before, bid the servant tell Blifil he knew him not.", "올워디는 흠칫하며 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 내가 짐작하기로는 전에 그가 한 번도 쓴 적 없을 만큼 격앙된 어조로, 하인에게 블리필에게 가서 자기는 그런 사람을 모른다고 전하라 일렀다."], ["“Consider, dear sir,”", "\"고정하십시오, 외삼촌.\""], ["cries Jones, in a trembling voice.", "존스가 떨리는 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“I have considered,”", "\"이미 고심해 보았다.\""], ["answered Allworthy, “and you yourself shall carry my message to the villain.", "올워디가 답했다. \"그러니 네가 직접 그 악당에게 내 전갈을 전해야겠다."], ["No one can carry him the sentence of his own ruin so properly as the man whose ruin he hath so villanously contrived.”", "그자가 그토록 악랄하게 꾸민 파멸을 당할 뻔한 바로 그 사람만큼, 그 파멸의 선고를 마땅히 그에게 전할 자가 어디 있겠느냐.\""], ["“Pardon me, dear sir,”", "\"용서하십시오, 외삼촌.\""], ["said Jones; “a moment's reflection will, I am sure, convince you of the contrary.", "존스가 말했다. \"잠깐만 곱씹어 보셔도 그 반대임을 분명 깨달으실 겁니다."], ["What might perhaps be but justice from another tongue, would from mine be insult; and to whom?--my own brother and your nephew.", "다른 사람의 입에서 나온다면 어쩌면 정당한 말일지라도, 제 입에서 나온다면 그것은 모욕이 될 것입니다. 게다가 누구에게 말입니까?"], ["Nor did he use me so barbarously--indeed, that would have been more inexcusable than anything he hath done.", "제 친형제이자 외삼촌의 조카에게요. 그가 저를 그토록 모질게 대한 것도 아니었습니다."], ["Fortune may tempt men of no very bad dispositions to injustice; but insults proceed only from black and rancorous minds, and have no temptations to excuse them.", "사실 그랬다면, 그것이야말로 그가 한 어떤 짓보다도 용서받기 힘든 일이었을 겁니다. 운명은 그리 나쁘지 않은 성품의 사람조차 불의로 꾈 수 있습니다."], ["Let me beseech you, sir, to do nothing by him in the present height of your anger.", "하지만 모욕이란 오로지 검고 원한에 찬 마음에서만 나오며, 그것을 변명해 줄 유혹 같은 것도 없습니다."], ["Consider, my dear uncle, I was not myself condemned unheard.”", "부디 간청하건대, 지금 노여움이 한껏 솟구친 이때에는 그를 두고 아무 일도 하지 말아 주십시오. 헤아려 주십시오, 외삼촌."], ["Allworthy stood silent a moment, and then, embracing Jones, he said, with tears gushing from his eyes, “O my child!", "저 역시 변명 한마디 듣지 못한 채 단죄당하지는 않았으니까요.\" 올워디는 한순간 말없이 서 있다가, 존스를 끌어안으며 두 눈에서 눈물을 쏟아 내며 말했다."], ["to what goodness have I been so long blind!”", "\"오, 내 아이야! 내가 그토록 오래 어떤 선함에 눈이 멀어 있었단 말이냐!\""]], [["Mrs Miller entering the room at that moment, after a gentle rap which was not perceived, and seeing Jones in the arms of his uncle, the poor woman in an agony of joy fell upon her knees, and burst forth into the most ecstatic thanksgivings to heaven for what had happened; then, running to Jones, she embraced him eagerly, crying, “My dearest friend, I wish you joy a thousand and a thousand times of this blest day.” And next Mr Allworthy himself received the same congratulations. To which he answered, “Indeed, indeed, Mrs Miller, I am beyond expression happy.” Some few more raptures having passed on all sides, Mrs Miller desired them both to walk down to dinner in the parlour, where she said there were a very happy set of people assembled--being indeed no other than Mr Nightingale and his bride, and his cousin Harriet with her bridegroom.", "바로 그 순간 밀러 부인이, 미처 들리지 않은 가벼운 노크 끝에 방으로 들어와, 존스가 외삼촌의 품에 안겨 있는 것을 보았다. 가엾은 부인은 기쁨에 겨워 무릎을 꿇고는, 일어난 일에 대해 하늘을 향해 더없이 황홀한 감사의 기도를 터뜨렸다. 그러고는 존스에게 달려가 그를 와락 끌어안으며 외쳤다. \"내 더없이 소중한 벗이여, 이 복된 날을 천 번이고 만 번이고 축하드려요.\" 다음으로 올워디 씨 자신이 똑같은 축하를 받았다. 그는 이렇게 답했다. \"정말이지, 정말이지, 밀러 부인, 나는 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 행복합니다.\" 모두가 더 몇 마디 환희를 주고받은 뒤, 밀러 부인은 두 사람에게 응접실로 내려가 함께 식사하자고 청했다. 그곳에는 매우 행복한 사람들이 모여 있다는 것이었는데, 사실 그들은 다름 아닌 나이팅게일 씨와 그 신부, 그리고 그의 사촌 해리엇과 그녀의 신랑이었다."]], [["Allworthy excused himself from dining with the company, saying he had ordered some little thing for him and his nephew in his own apartment, for that they had much private business to discourse of; but would not resist promising the good woman that both he and Jones would make part of her society at supper.", "올워디는 함께 식사하는 것을 양해해 달라고 했다. 자기와 조카가 따로 의논할 사사로운 일이 많아, 자기 방에 두 사람을 위한 간단한 음식을 준비해 두라 일렀다는 것이었다. 다만 자신과 존스가 저녁 식사 자리만큼은 부인의 모임에 함께하겠노라는 약속만은 마다하지 않았다."]], [["Mrs Miller then asked what was to be done with Blifil?", "그러자 밀러 부인은 블리필을 어찌할 작정이냐고 물었다. \"정말이지,\""], ["“for indeed,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저런 악당이 제 집에 있는 한 저는 마음이 편할 수가 없어요.\""], ["says she, “I cannot be easy while such a villain is in my house.”--Allworthy answered, “He was as uneasy as herself on the same account.”", "올워디가 답했다. \"나 역시 같은 이유로 부인 못지않게 마음이 편치 않소.\""], ["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["cries she, “if that be the case, leave the matter to me, I'll soon show him the outside out of my doors, I warrant you.", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 그 일은 저에게 맡겨 주세요."], ["Here are two or three lusty fellows below-stairs.”", "제가 그자를 당장 문밖으로 내쫓아 보이겠어요, 장담합니다. 아래층에 건장한 사내가 두셋은 있으니까요.\""], ["“There will be no need of any violence,”", "\"폭력 같은 건 전혀 필요 없을 거요.\""], ["cries Allworthy; “if you will carry him a message from me, he will, I am convinced, depart of his own accord.”", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"내 전갈만 그에게 전해 주면, 그는 분명 제 발로 떠날 거라 확신하오.\""], ["“Will I?”", "\"제가요?\""], ["said Mrs Miller; “I never did anything in my life with a better will.”", "밀러 부인이 말했다. \"이보다 더 기꺼운 마음으로 한 일은 제 평생 없었어요.\""], ["Here Jones interfered, and said, “He had considered the matter better, and would, if Mr Allworthy pleased, be himself the messenger.", "여기서 존스가 끼어들어 말했다. \"그 문제를 좀 더 곰곰이 생각해 보았는데, 올워디 외삼촌만 허락하신다면 제가 직접 전령이 되고 싶습니다."], ["I know,”", "외삼촌의 뜻은,\""], ["says he, “already enough of your pleasure, sir, and I beg leave to acquaint him with it by my own words.", "그가 말했다. \"이미 충분히 알고 있으니, 제 말로써 그에게 그 뜻을 전하게 해 주십시오."], ["Let me beseech you, sir,”", "부디 간청하건대, 외삼촌.\""], ["added he, “to reflect on the dreadful consequences of driving him to violent and sudden despair.", "그가 덧붙였다. \"그를 격렬하고 갑작스러운 절망으로 몰아넣었을 때의 끔찍한 결과를 헤아려 주십시오."], ["How unfit, alas! is this poor man to die in his present situation.”", "아아, 지금 같은 처지에서 죽음을 맞기에 저 가엾은 사람은 얼마나 준비가 안 되어 있겠습니까.\""], ["This suggestion had not the least effect on Mrs Miller.", "이 말은 밀러 부인에게는 조금도 효력을 미치지 못했다."], ["She left the room, crying, “You are too good, Mr Jones, infinitely too good to live in this world.”", "그녀는 방을 나서며 외쳤다. \"당신은 너무 착해요, 존스 씨, 이 세상에 살기엔 더없이 지나치게 착하시다고요.\""], ["But it made a deeper impression on Allworthy.", "하지만 그 말은 올워디에게는 더 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["“My good child,”", "\"착한 내 아이야.\""], ["said he, “I am equally astonished at the goodness of your heart, and the quickness of your understanding.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 네 마음의 선함과 네 분별의 민첩함에 똑같이 놀랐다."], ["Heaven indeed forbid that this wretch should be deprived of any means or time for repentance!", "정말이지 하늘이 막아 주시기를, 이 가엾은 자가 회개할 어떤 수단이나 시간도 빼앗기지 않기를! 그것은 참으로 충격적인 일일 테니 말이다."], ["That would be a shocking consideration indeed.", "그러니 그에게 가서 네 재량껏 하여라."], ["Go to him, therefore, and use your own discretion; yet do not flatter him with any hopes of my forgiveness; for I shall never forgive villany farther than my religion obliges me, and that extends not either to our bounty or our conversation.”", "다만 내 용서를 바랄 어떤 희망으로도 그를 부추기지는 말아라. 나는 내 신앙이 명하는 그 이상으로는 결코 악행을 용서하지 않을 것이며, 그 신앙이라는 것도 우리의 베풂이나 교류에까지 미치지는 않으니까.\""]], [["Jones went up to Blifil's room, whom he found in a situation which moved his pity, though it would have raised a less amiable passion in many beholders. He cast himself on his bed, where he lay abandoning himself to despair, and drowned in tears; not in such tears as flow from contrition, and wash away guilt from minds which have been seduced or surprized into it unawares, against the bent of their natural dispositions, as will sometimes happen from human frailty, even to the good; no, these tears were such as the frighted thief sheds in his cart, and are indeed the effects of that concern which the most savage natures are seldom deficient in feeling for themselves.", "존스는 블리필의 방으로 올라갔는데, 그곳에서 본 그의 모습은 자신의 연민을 자아냈으나, 많은 사람들의 눈에는 그보다 덜 너그러운 감정을 불러일으킬 만한 것이었다. 그는 침대에 몸을 던진 채 절망에 자신을 내맡기고 눈물에 잠겨 누워 있었다. 그러나 그것은 뉘우침에서 흘러나와, 타고난 성정을 거슬러 자기도 모르게 죄에 꾀이거나 휩쓸린 마음에서 죄를 씻어 내는 그런 눈물은 아니었다. 그런 눈물이라면 인간의 연약함 탓에 때로는 선한 사람에게서조차 흐르기도 하건만. 아니, 이 눈물은 겁에 질린 도둑이 호송 수레 위에서 흘리는 그런 눈물이었으니, 실은 아무리 야만적인 본성이라도 좀처럼 모자라는 법이 없는, 제 자신을 향한 그 근심의 결과일 뿐이었다."]], [["It would be unpleasant and tedious to paint this scene in full length.", "이 장면을 낱낱이 그려 내는 것은 불쾌하고도 지루한 일이 될 것이다."], ["Let it suffice to say, that the behaviour of Jones was kind to excess.", "다만 존스의 처신이 지나칠 만큼 다정했다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하리라."], ["He omitted nothing which his invention could supply, to raise and comfort the drooping spirits of Blifil, before he communicated to him the resolution of his uncle that he must quit the house that evening.", "그는 외삼촌의 결심, 곧 블리필이 그날 저녁 이 집을 떠나야 한다는 그 결정을 그에게 전하기에 앞서, 풀이 죽은 블리필의 기운을 북돋고 위로하기 위해 자신의 기지로 떠올릴 수 있는 모든 것을 빠짐없이 동원했다."], ["He offered to furnish him with any money he wanted, assured him of his hearty forgiveness of all he had done against him, that he would endeavour to live with him hereafter as a brother, and would leave nothing unattempted to effectuate a reconciliation with his uncle.", "그는 그가 필요로 하는 돈은 얼마든 대 주겠노라 제안했고, 자신에게 저지른 모든 일을 진심으로 용서한다고 확언했으며, 앞으로는 그와 형제처럼 지내도록 애쓰겠고, 외삼촌과의 화해를 이루기 위해서라면 무엇이든 다 해 보겠다고 말했다."]], [["Blifil was at first sullen and silent, balancing in his mind whether he should yet deny all; but, finding at last the evidence too strong against him, he betook himself at last to confession.", "블리필은 처음에는 뚱하니 입을 다문 채, 아직도 모든 것을 부인할까 마음속으로 저울질했다. 그러나 마침내 자신에게 불리한 증거가 너무 확고함을 깨닫고는, 결국 고백에 의지하고 말았다."], ["He then asked pardon of his brother in the most vehement manner, prostrated himself on the ground, and kissed his feet; in short he was now as remarkably mean as he had been before remarkably wicked.", "그러고는 더없이 격렬한 태도로 형에게 용서를 빌며 땅바닥에 엎드려 그의 발에 입을 맞추었다. 요컨대 그는, 앞서 두드러지게 사악했던 만큼이나 이제는 두드러지게 비굴해졌다."]], [["Jones could not so far check his disdain, but that it a little discovered itself in his countenance at this extreme servility. He raised his brother the moment he could from the ground, and advised him to bear his afflictions more like a man; repeating, at the same time, his promises, that he would do all in his power to lessen them; for which Blifil, making many professions of his unworthiness, poured forth a profusion of thanks; and then, he having declared he would immediately depart to another lodging, Jones returned to his uncle.", "존스는 이 극도의 비굴함 앞에서 자신의 경멸을 다 억누르지는 못해, 그것이 얼굴에 조금 드러나고 말았다. 그는 형제를 되도록 빨리 땅바닥에서 일으켜 세우고는, 좀 더 사내답게 고난을 견디라고 충고했다. 그러면서 동시에, 그 고난을 덜어 주기 위해 자신이 할 수 있는 모든 일을 하겠다는 약속을 거듭했다. 이에 블리필은 자신이 그럴 자격이 없노라고 여러 번 되뇌며 넘치도록 감사를 쏟아 냈다. 그러고는 그가 곧장 다른 거처로 떠나겠다고 밝히자, 존스는 외삼촌에게로 돌아갔다."]], [["Among other matters, Allworthy now acquainted Jones with the discovery which he had made concerning the £500 bank-notes.", "다른 여러 일과 더불어, 올워디는 이제 5백 파운드짜리 은행권에 관해 자신이 알아낸 사실을 존스에게 일러 주었다."], ["“I have,”", "\"나는,\""], ["said he, “already consulted a lawyer, who tells me, to my great astonishment, that there is no punishment for a fraud of this kind.", "그가 말했다. \"이미 한 법률가에게 자문을 구했는데, 그자의 말로는 이런 종류의 사기에는 처벌이 없다는구나."], ["Indeed, when I consider the black ingratitude of this fellow toward you, I think a highwayman, compared to him, is an innocent person.”", "참으로 놀라운 일이지. 정말이지, 너를 향한 이 작자의 시커먼 배은망덕을 생각하면, 노상강도조차 그에 비하면 결백한 사람이라 여겨지는구나.\""]], [["“Good Heaven!” says Jones, “is it possible?--I am shocked beyond measure at this news. I thought there was not an honester fellow in the world.----The temptation of such a sum was too great for him to withstand; for smaller matters have come safe to me through his hand. Indeed, my dear uncle, you must suffer me to call it weakness rather than ingratitude; for I am convinced the poor fellow loves me, and hath done me some kindnesses, which I can never forget; nay, I believe he hath repented of this very act; for it is not above a day or two ago, when my affairs seemed in the most desperate situation, that he visited me in my confinement, and offered me any money I wanted. Consider, sir, what a temptation to a man who hath tasted such bitter distress, it must be, to have a sum in his possession which must put him and his family beyond any future possibility of suffering the like.”", "\"맙소사!\" 존스가 말했다. \"그게 가능한 일입니까? 이 소식에 저는 한량없이 충격을 받았습니다. 저는 세상에 그보다 더 정직한 사람은 없을 거라 여겼는데요. 그만한 액수의 유혹은 그가 버텨 내기에 너무 컸던 거지요. 더 적은 액수는 그의 손을 거쳐 무사히 제게 닿곤 했으니까요. 정말이지, 외삼촌, 저는 그것을 배은망덕이라기보다 약함이라 부르도록 허락해 주셔야겠습니다. 저 가엾은 사람이 저를 사랑하며, 제가 결코 잊을 수 없는 친절을 여러 번 베풀었다고 저는 확신하니까요. 아니, 저는 그가 바로 이 짓을 두고 뉘우쳤으리라 믿습니다. 불과 하루 이틀 전, 제 형편이 가장 절망적으로 보이던 그때에도, 그는 갇혀 있던 저를 찾아와 제가 필요한 돈은 얼마든 대 주겠다고 했으니까요. 헤아려 주십시오, 외삼촌. 그토록 쓰라린 곤궁을 맛본 사람이, 자기와 가족을 앞으로 그런 고통을 다시 겪을 어떤 가능성에서도 영영 벗어나게 해 줄 큰돈을 손에 쥐고 있다는 것이, 그에게 얼마나 큰 유혹이었겠습니까.\""]], [["“Child,”", "\"얘야.\" 올워디가 외쳤다."], ["cries Allworthy, “you carry this forgiving temper too far.", "\"너는 이 용서하는 마음을 너무 멀리까지 끌고 가는구나. 그렇게 그릇된 자비는 약함일 뿐 아니라 불의에 맞닿아 있으며, 악을 부추기는 만큼 사회에 매우 해롭다."], ["Such mistaken mercy is not only weakness, but borders on injustice, and is very pernicious to society, as it encourages vice.", "이 작자의 부정직함이라면 어쩌면 내가 용서했을지도 모르겠다만, 그 배은망덕만큼은 결코 용서할 수 없다. 그리고 한마디 하게 해 다오."], ["The dishonesty of this fellow I might, perhaps, have pardoned, but never his ingratitude.", "어떤 유혹이 부정직함 자체를 갚아 주도록 우리가 받아들인다면, 그것은 우리가 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 공정하고 자비로운 것이다. 사실 나도 그만큼은 가 보았다."], ["And give me leave to say, when we suffer any temptation to atone for dishonesty itself, we are as candid and merciful as we ought to be; and so far I confess I have gone; for I have often pitied the fate of a highwayman, when I have been on the grand jury; and have more than once applied to the judge on the behalf of such as have had any mitigating circumstances in their case; but when dishonesty is attended with any blacker crime, such as cruelty, murder, ingratitude, or the like, compassion and forgiveness then become faults.", "나는 대배심에 앉아 있을 때 노상강도의 운명을 가엾이 여기곤 했고, 그 사정에 정상을 참작할 여지가 있는 자들을 위해서는 한두 번 이상 판사에게 청을 넣기도 했으니까. 하지만 부정직함이 잔혹함이나 살인, 배은망덕 따위의 더 시커먼 죄와 한데 얽힐 때면, 연민과 용서는 그때 잘못이 되고 만다."], ["I am convinced the fellow is a villain, and he shall be punished; at least as far as I can punish him.”", "나는 그 작자가 악당임을 확신하니, 그는 벌을 받을 것이다. 적어도 내가 벌할 수 있는 한도까지는 말이다.\""]], [["This was spoken with so stern a voice, that Jones did not think proper to make any reply; besides, the hour appointed by Mr Western now drew so near, that he had barely time left to dress himself.", "이 말은 너무도 준엄한 목소리로 내뱉어진지라, 존스는 어떤 대꾸도 하는 것이 마땅치 않다고 여겼다. 게다가 웨스턴 씨가 정해 둔 시각이 어느덧 가까워져, 옷을 차려입을 시간조차 빠듯하게 남아 있었다."], ["Here therefore ended the present dialogue, and Jones retired to another room, where Partridge attended, according to order, with his cloaths.", "그리하여 지금의 대화는 여기서 끝났고, 존스는 다른 방으로 물러났다. 그곳에는 분부받은 대로 파트리지가 그의 옷가지를 들고 시중을 들고 있었다."]], [["Partridge had scarce seen his master since the happy discovery.", "파트리지는 그 행복한 발견 이후로 주인을 거의 보지 못한 터였다."], ["The poor fellow was unable either to contain or express his transports.", "가엾은 사내는 자신의 환희를 가눌 수도, 다 드러낼 수도 없었다."], ["He behaved like one frantic, and made almost as many mistakes while he was dressing Jones as I have seen made by Harlequin in dressing himself on the stage.", "그는 미쳐 날뛰는 사람처럼 굴었고, 존스에게 옷을 입히면서 어찌나 많은 실수를 저질렀던지, 무대 위에서 제 옷을 입던 어릿광대가 저지르던 실수를 내가 본 것에 견줄 만했다."]], [["His memory, however, was not in the least deficient. He recollected now many omens and presages of this happy event, some of which he had remarked at the time, but many more he now remembered; nor did he omit the dreams he had dreamt the evening before his meeting with Jones; and concluded with saying, “I always told your honour something boded in my mind that you would one time or other have it in your power to make my fortune.” Jones assured him that this boding should as certainly be verified with regard to him as all the other omens had been to himself; which did not a little add to all the raptures which the poor fellow had already conceived on account of his master.", "그러나 그의 기억력만큼은 조금도 부족함이 없었다. 그는 이제 이 행복한 사건의 수많은 전조와 징조를 떠올렸는데, 그중 몇 가지는 그때 그가 눈여겨봐 두었던 것이었고, 더 많은 것들은 이제야 기억해 낸 것이었다. 또한 존스를 만나기 전날 저녁에 꾸었던 꿈들도 빼놓지 않았다. 그러고는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"제가 늘 나리께 말씀드렸지요. 나리께서 언젠가 한 번쯤은 제 운을 틔워 주실 힘을 갖게 되리라는 무언가가 제 마음에 점쳐진다고 말입니다.\" 존스는 그에게, 다른 모든 전조가 자신에게 들어맞았던 것만큼이나 이 점괘도 그에게 틀림없이 이루어질 것이라고 확언해 주었다. 이 말은 가엾은 사내가 이미 주인을 두고 품고 있던 그 모든 환희에 적잖이 보탬이 되었다."]], [["Approaching still nearer to the end.", "결말에 한층 더 가까이 다가가다."]], [["Jones, being now completely dressed, attended his uncle to Mr Western's.", "이제 옷차림을 완전히 갖춘 존스는 숙부를 모시고 웨스턴 씨 댁으로 향했다. 그는 실로 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 빼어난 풍채를 지닌 사내였으며, 그 외모만으로도 세상 여인 대부분의 마음을 사로잡았을 것이다."], ["He was, indeed, one of the finest figures ever beheld, and his person alone would have charmed the greater part of womankind; but we hope it hath already appeared in this history that Nature, when she formed him, did not totally rely, as she sometimes doth, on this merit only, to recommend her work.", "그러나 우리는 이 이야기를 통해 이미 드러났기를 바란다. 자연이 그를 빚을 때, 가끔 그러하듯 이 한 가지 장점에만 온전히 기대어 자신의 작품을 내세우려 하지는 않았다는 사실을 말이다."]], [["Sophia, who, angry as she was, was likewise set forth to the best advantage, for which I leave my female readers to account, appeared so extremely beautiful, that even Allworthy, when he saw her, could not forbear whispering Western, that he believed she was the finest creature in the world.", "소피아는, 화가 났으면서도 그녀대로 가장 돋보이는 차림을 하고 있었는데—그 까닭은 여성 독자들의 헤아림에 맡기겠다—어찌나 곱고 아름다웠던지, 올워디조차 그녀를 보고는 웨스턴에게 그녀가 세상에서 가장 아리따운 사람이라고 속삭이지 않을 수 없었다. 이에 웨스턴은 속삭이듯 대답했으나 그 자리에 있던 모두가 다 들을 만한 소리로 말했다."], ["To which Western answered, in a whisper, overheard by all present, “So much the better for Tom;--for d--n me if he shan't ha the tousling her.”", "\"그러니 톰한테는 더 잘된 일 아닌가.—제기랄, 저 녀석이 저 애를 끌어안고 뒹굴지 못하면 내가 사람이 아니지.\""], ["Sophia was all over scarlet at these words, while Tom's countenance was altogether as pale, and he was almost ready to sink from his chair.", "이 말에 소피아는 온통 새빨개졌고, 톰의 낯빛은 그와 반대로 온통 핏기가 가셔 의자에서 까무러칠 지경이었다."]], [["The tea-table was scarce removed before Western lugged Allworthy out of the room, telling him he had business of consequence to impart, and must speak to him that instant in private, before he forgot it.", "찻상이 채 치워지기도 전에 웨스턴은 올워디를 방 밖으로 끌어냈다. 긴히 전할 일이 있어 잊어버리기 전에 지금 당장 단둘이 이야기해야겠다는 것이었다."]], [["The lovers were now alone, and it will, I question not, appear strange to many readers, that those who had so much to say to one another when danger and difficulty attended their conversation, and who seemed so eager to rush into each other's arms when so many bars lay in their way, now that with safety they were at liberty to say or do whatever they pleased, should both remain for some time silent and motionless; insomuch that a stranger of moderate sagacity might have well concluded they were mutually indifferent; but so it was, however strange it may seem; both sat with their eyes cast downwards on the ground, and for some minutes continued in perfect silence.", "이제 두 연인만 남게 되었다. 위험과 어려움이 그들의 대화를 따라다닐 때에는 서로에게 그토록 할 말이 많았고, 그토록 많은 장애물이 가로놓여 있을 때에는 서로의 품으로 뛰어들고 싶어 그토록 안달하던 두 사람이, 이제 마음 놓고 무슨 말이든 무슨 일이든 자유로이 할 수 있게 되자 한동안 둘 다 말없이 꼼짝도 않고 앉아 있었다는 사실은—내 의심치 않건대—많은 독자에게 기이하게 비칠 것이다. 어찌나 그러했던지, 어지간한 눈치를 지닌 낯선 사람이라면 둘이 서로에게 무심하다고 결론지었을 법도 했다. 그러나 아무리 기이해 보여도 사정은 그러했다. 둘 다 눈을 내리깔고 바닥만 바라본 채 몇 분 동안 완전한 침묵을 이어갔다."]], [["Mr Jones during this interval attempted once or twice to speak, but was absolutely incapable, muttering only, or rather sighing out, some broken words; when Sophia at length, partly out of pity to him, and partly to turn the discourse from the subject which she knew well enough he was endeavouring to open, said--", "그 사이 존스 씨는 한두 번 입을 열려 했으나 도무지 말이 나오지 않아, 그저 몇 마디 끊긴 말을 웅얼거리거나, 차라리 한숨처럼 내뱉을 뿐이었다. 그러다 마침내 소피아가, 한편으로는 그가 가여워서, 또 한편으로는 그가 꺼내려 애쓰고 있음을 빤히 아는 그 화제에서 이야기를 돌리려고 입을 열었다."]], [["“Sure, sir, you are the most fortunate man in the world in this discovery.”", "\"분명히, 선생님, 이번에 모든 사실이 밝혀졌으니 선생님은 세상에서 가장 운이 좋은 분이시네요.\""], ["“And can you really, madam, think me so fortunate,”", "\"그런데 정말로, 아가씨, 저를 그토록 운이 좋다고 여기실 수 있겠습니까,\" 존스가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["said Jones, sighing, “while I have incurred your displeasure?”--“Nay, sir,”", "\"제가 당신의 노여움을 산 마당에 말입니다.\" \"글쎄요, 선생님,\""], ["says she, “as to that you best know whether you have deserved it.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그 점이라면 그럴 만한 짓을 하셨는지 아닌지는 선생님이 가장 잘 아시겠지요.\""], ["“Indeed, madam,”", "\"정말이지, 아가씨,\""], ["answered he, “you yourself are as well apprized of all my demerits.", "그가 대답했다. \"제 허물이라면 당신께서도 죄다 알고 계시지 않습니까."], ["Mrs Miller hath acquainted you with the whole truth.", "밀러 부인이 모든 진실을 다 일러드렸으니까요."], ["O!", "오!"], ["my Sophia, am I never to hope for forgiveness?”--“I think, Mr Jones,”", "나의 소피아, 저는 영영 용서를 바랄 수 없는 것입니까?\" \"제 생각엔, 존스 씨,\""], ["said she, “I may almost depend on your own justice, and leave it to yourself to pass sentence on your own conduct.”--“Alas!", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 거의 선생님 자신의 공정함에 기댈 수 있을 듯하니, 선생님 자신의 행실에 대한 판결은 선생님 스스로에게 맡기겠어요.\""], ["madam,”", "\"아아!"], ["answered he, “it is mercy, and not justice, which I implore at your hands.", "아가씨,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["Justice I know must condemn me.--Yet not for the letter I sent to Lady Bellaston.", "\"제가 당신 손에 비는 것은 공정함이 아니라 자비입니다."], ["Of that I most solemnly declare you have had a true account.”", "공정함이라면 분명 저를 단죄할 수밖에 없음을 압니다.—그러나 제가 벨라스턴 부인에게 보낸 그 편지 때문은 아닙니다."], ["He then insisted much on the security given him by Nightingale of a fair pretence for breaking off, if, contrary to their expectations, her ladyship should have accepted his offer; but confest that he had been guilty of a great indiscretion to put such a letter as that into her power, “which,”", "그 일에 관해서라면 제가 진실한 해명을 드렸음을 더없이 엄숙히 단언합니다.\" 그러고는 그는, 기대와 달리 부인이 자신의 청혼을 받아들였을 경우 깨끗이 발을 뺄 그럴듯한 구실을 나이팅게일이 보장해 주었음을 거듭 강조했다."], ["said he, “I have dearly paid for, in the effect it has upon you.”", "다만 그런 편지를 그녀의 손에 쥐여준 것은 자신의 큰 경솔함이었음을 인정하며 말했다."], ["“I do not, I cannot,”", "\"그 경솔함에 대해서는,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says she, “believe otherwise of that letter than you would have me.", "\"당신에게 미친 그 결과로 톡톡히 대가를 치르고 있습니다.\" \"저는 그 편지를 두고 선생님이 바라시는 것과 다르게 믿지 않아요, 아니, 믿을 수가 없어요,\""], ["My conduct, I think, shews you clearly I do not believe there is much in that.", "그녀가 말했다. \"제 행동을 보시면 제가 거기에 별것 없다고 여긴다는 걸 분명히 아실 거예요."], ["And yet, Mr Jones, have I not enough to resent?", "그렇지만, 존스 씨, 제가 분개할 까닭이 그뿐이겠어요?"], ["After what past at Upton, so soon to engage in a new amour with another woman, while I fancied, and you pretended, your heart was bleeding for me?", "어프턴에서 그 일이 있은 뒤, 그토록 금세 다른 여자와 새로운 연애에 빠지다니요. 그러는 동안에도 저는 선생님 가슴이 저 때문에 피 흘리고 있다 여겼고, 선생님은 그런 척하셨잖아요."], ["Indeed, you have acted strangely.", "정말이지, 선생님은 이상하게 처신하셨어요."], ["Can I believe the passion you have profest to me to be sincere?", "제게 고백하신 그 열정이 진심이라고 제가 믿을 수 있겠어요?"], ["Or, if I can, what happiness can I assure myself of with a man capable of so much inconstancy?”", "설령 믿는다 한들, 그토록 변덕스러운 사람과 무슨 행복을 장담할 수 있겠어요?\" \"오!"], ["“O! my Sophia,”", "나의 소피아,\""], ["cries he, “do not doubt the sincerity of the purest passion that ever inflamed a human breast.", "그가 외쳤다. \"일찍이 인간의 가슴을 불태운 가장 순결한 열정의 진실함을 의심하지 마십시오."], ["Think, most adorable creature, of my unhappy situation, of my despair.", "가장 사랑스러운 분이시여, 제 불행한 처지를, 제 절망을 헤아려 주십시오."], ["Could I, my Sophia, have flattered myself with the most distant hopes of being ever permitted to throw myself at your feet in the manner I do now, it would not have been in the power of any other woman to have inspired a thought which the severest chastity could have condemned.", "나의 소피아여, 지금처럼 당신 발치에 이 몸을 던지도록 허락받으리라는 아주 먼 희망이라도 제가 품을 수 있었더라면, 그 어떤 다른 여인도 가장 엄격한 정절조차 단죄할 만한 생각을 제 안에 불러일으키지 못했을 것입니다."], ["Inconstancy to you!", "당신에게 변심이라니요!"], ["O Sophia!", "오 소피아!"], ["if you can have goodness enough to pardon what is past, do not let any cruel future apprehensions shut your mercy against me.", "지나간 일을 너그러이 용서해 주실 만큼 선량하시거든, 부디 잔인한 미래의 두려움 따위로 제게 베푸실 자비를 닫지 말아 주십시오."], ["No repentance was ever more sincere. O!", "이보다 더 진실한 뉘우침은 일찍이 없었습니다."], ["let it reconcile me to my heaven in this dear bosom.”", "오! 이 사랑스러운 가슴 안에서 그 뉘우침이 저를 제 천국과 화해하게 해 주기를.\""], ["“Sincere repentance, Mr Jones,”", "\"진실한 뉘우침은, 존스 씨,\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["answered she, “will obtain the pardon of a sinner, but it is from one who is a perfect judge of that sincerity.", "\"죄인의 용서를 얻어내겠지요. 그러나 그것은 그 진실함을 완벽하게 판별할 줄 아는 분에게서나 그러합니다."], ["A human mind may be imposed on; nor is there any infallible method to prevent it.", "인간의 마음은 속을 수 있으며, 그것을 막을 확실한 방법도 없으니까요. 그러니 알아 두세요."], ["You must expect, however, that if I can be prevailed on by your repentance to pardon you, I will at least insist on the strongest proof of its sincerity.”", "제가 선생님의 뉘우침에 마음이 움직여 용서하게 되더라도, 적어도 그 진실함에 대한 가장 강력한 증거만큼은 반드시 요구하겠어요.\" \"제 힘으로 댈 수 있는 증거라면 무엇이든 말씀하십시오,\""], ["“Name any proof in my power,”", "존스가 간절히 대답했다. \"시간이에요,\""], ["answered Jones eagerly. “Time,”", "그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["replied she; “time alone, Mr Jones, can convince me that you are a true penitent, and have resolved to abandon these vicious courses, which I should detest you for, if I imagined you capable of persevering in them.”", "\"오직 시간만이, 존스 씨, 선생님이 참되이 뉘우치는 사람이며, 그 못된 행실을 끊어버리기로 결심했음을 제게 납득시켜 줄 수 있어요. 만약 선생님이 그런 행실을 계속할 수 있는 사람이라 여겨진다면, 저는 그 때문에 선생님을 혐오하게 될 거예요.\""], ["“Do not imagine it,”", "\"그리 여기지 마십시오,\""], ["cries Jones.", "존스가 외쳤다."], ["“On my knees I intreat, I implore your confidence, a confidence which it shall be the business of my life to deserve.”", "\"무릎 꿇고 간청합니다, 부디 저를 믿어 주십시오. 그 믿음을 얻을 자격을 갖추는 것이야말로 제 평생의 과업이 되게 하겠습니다.\""], ["“Let it then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said she, “be the business of some part of your life to shew me you deserve it.", "그녀가 말했다. \"선생님 생애의 얼마간을, 그 믿음을 받을 자격이 있음을 제게 보여 주는 일에 바치세요."], ["I think I have been explicit enough in assuring you, that, when I see you merit my confidence, you will obtain it.", "제가 충분히 분명하게 말씀드린 것 같은데요. 선생님이 제 믿음을 받을 만하다는 것을 제가 보게 되면, 그 믿음을 얻게 되실 거예요."], ["After what is past, sir, can you expect I should take you upon your word?”", "지나간 일이 있은 뒤에, 선생님, 제가 선생님 말만 곧이듣기를 바라실 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["He replied, “Don't believe me upon my word; I have a better security, a pledge for my constancy, which it is impossible to see and to doubt.”", "그가 대답했다. \"제 말만으로 저를 믿지는 마십시오."], ["“What is that?”", "제겐 더 나은 담보가 있습니다."], ["said Sophia, a little surprized.", "제 한결같음에 대한 증표가요. 그것은 보고도 의심할 수 없는 것입니다.\""], ["“I will show you, my charming angel,”", "\"그게 무엇인데요?\" 소피아가 조금 놀라며 말했다."], ["cried Jones, seizing her hand and carrying her to the glass.", "\"보여 드리지요, 나의 매혹적인 천사여,\" 존스가 외치며 그녀의 손을 잡아 거울 앞으로 데려갔다."], ["“There, behold it there in that lovely figure, in that face, that shape, those eyes, that mind which shines through these eyes; can the man who shall be in possession of these be inconstant?", "\"자, 저기, 저 거울 속을 보십시오. 저 사랑스러운 자태를, 저 얼굴을, 저 몸매를, 저 눈을, 그리고 저 눈을 통해 빛나는 저 마음을 말입니다."], ["Impossible! my Sophia; they would fix a Dorimant, a Lord Rochester.", "이것들을 차지하게 될 사내가 변심할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["You could not doubt it, if you could see yourself with any eyes but your own.”", "어림없습니다! 나의 소피아여, 저것들은 도리먼트도, 로체스터 경도 붙들어 매어 둘 것입니다."], ["Sophia blushed and half smiled; but, forcing again her brow into a frown--“If I am to judge,”", "당신 자신의 눈이 아닌 다른 눈으로 당신을 볼 수 있다면, 결코 그것을 의심하지 못하실 겁니다.\" 소피아는 얼굴을 붉히며 반쯤 미소를 지었다."], ["said she, “of the future by the past, my image will no more remain in your heart when I am out of your sight, than it will in this glass when I am out of the room.”", "그러나 다시 억지로 이마를 찌푸리며 말했다. \"지난 일로 앞날을 가늠해야 한다면, 제가 방을 나가면 이 거울 속에 제 모습이 남지 않듯, 제가 선생님 눈에서 사라지면 제 모습도 더는 선생님 가슴에 남아 있지 않겠지요.\""], ["“By heaven, by all that is sacred!”", "\"하늘에 두고, 신성한 모든 것에 두고 맹세하건대!\""], ["said Jones, “it never was out of my heart.", "존스가 말했다. \"당신은 단 한 번도 제 가슴을 떠난 적이 없습니다."], ["The delicacy of your sex cannot conceive the grossness of ours, nor how little one sort of amour has to do with the heart.”", "당신네 여성의 섬세함으로는 우리 남성의 거친 면모를, 또 한 종류의 연애가 마음과 얼마나 무관한지를 헤아릴 수 없을 것입니다.\" \"저는 결코,\""], ["“I will never marry a man,”", "소피아가 자못 진지하게 대답했다."], ["replied Sophia, very gravely, “who shall not learn refinement enough to be as incapable as I am myself of making such a distinction.”", "\"그런 구별 따위는 저처럼 도무지 할 줄 모를 만큼 충분히 고상함을 익히지 못한 사람과는 결혼하지 않겠어요.\""], ["“I will learn it,”", "\"그것을 배우겠습니다,\""], ["said Jones.", "존스가 말했다."], ["“I have learnt it already.", "\"이미 배웠습니다."], ["The first moment of hope that my Sophia might be my wife taught it me at once; and all the rest of her sex from that moment became as little the objects of desire to my sense as of passion to my heart.”", "나의 소피아가 제 아내가 될지도 모른다는 희망이 싹튼 그 첫 순간이 단번에 그것을 제게 가르쳐 주었습니다. 그 순간부터 나머지 모든 여성은 제 감각에 욕망의 대상이 되지도, 제 가슴에 정열의 대상이 되지도 못하게 되었습니다.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"좋아요,\""], ["says Sophia, “the proof of this must be from time.", "소피아가 말했다. \"그건 시간이 증명해 주어야겠지요."], ["Your situation, Mr Jones, is now altered, and I assure you I have great satisfaction in the alteration.", "선생님의 처지는, 존스 씨, 이제 바뀌었어요. 솔직히 저는 그 변화가 무척 흡족하답니다."], ["You will now want no opportunity of being near me, and convincing me that your mind is altered too.”", "이제 선생님은 제 곁에 머물며 선생님 마음도 바뀌었음을 제게 납득시킬 기회가 부족하진 않으실 거예요.\" \"오!"], ["“O! my angel,”", "나의 천사여,\""], ["cries Jones, “how shall I thank thy goodness!", "존스가 외쳤다. \"당신의 선량함에 제가 어찌 감사를 다 드릴 수 있겠습니까!"], ["And are you so good to own that you have a satisfaction in my prosperity?----Believe me, believe me, madam, it is you alone have given a relish to that prosperity, since I owe to it the dear hope----O!", "그리고 제가 잘되는 것이 흡족하다고 인정해 주실 만큼 그토록 너그러우신 것입니까?——믿어 주십시오, 믿어 주십시오, 아가씨, 그 행운에 운치를 더해 준 것은 오직 당신뿐입니다. 제가 그 덕에 그 소중한 희망을 품게 된 까닭이니까요——오!"], ["my Sophia, let it not be a distant one.--I will be all obedience to your commands.", "나의 소피아여, 부디 그것이 먼 훗날의 희망이 되지 않게 해 주십시오.—저는 당신의 분부에 온전히 따르겠습니다."], ["I will not dare to press anything further than you permit me.", "당신이 허락하시는 것 이상으로는 감히 아무것도 재촉하지 않겠습니다."], ["Yet let me intreat you to appoint a short trial. O!", "그래도 부디 간청하건대, 짧은 시험 기간을 정해 주십시오."], ["tell me when I may expect you will be convinced of what is most solemnly true.”", "오! 가장 엄숙하게 진실인 그것을 당신이 언제쯤 납득하시게 될지, 제게 일러 주십시오.\""], ["“When I have gone voluntarily thus far, Mr Jones,”", "\"제가 자진하여 여기까지 온 마당에, 존스 씨,\""], ["said she, “I expect not to be pressed.", "그녀가 말했다. \"재촉받기를 바라진 않아요."], ["Nay, I will not.”--“O!", "아니, 재촉당하지 않겠어요.\"—\"오!"], ["don't look unkindly thus, my Sophia,”", "그렇게 매정하게 보지 마십시오, 나의 소피아여,\""], ["cries he.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“I do not, I dare not press you.--Yet permit me at least once more to beg you would fix the period.", "\"재촉하지 않겠습니다, 감히 재촉하지 못합니다.—그래도 적어도 한 번만 더, 그 기한을 정해 주시기를 청하게 해 주십시오."], ["O!", "오!"], ["consider the impatience of love.”--“A twelvemonth, perhaps,”", "사랑의 조바심을 헤아려 주십시오.\"—\"한 일 년쯤일까요,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she.", "\"오!"], ["“O! my Sophia,”", "나의 소피아여,\""], ["cries he, “you have named an eternity.”--“Perhaps it may be something sooner,”", "그가 외쳤다. \"당신은 영원이나 다름없는 세월을 일러 주셨군요.\"—\"어쩌면 좀 더 빠를 수도 있겠지요,\""], ["says she; “I will not be teazed.", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 졸림을 당하고 싶지 않아요."], ["If your passion for me be what I would have it, I think you may now be easy.”--“Easy!", "저를 향한 선생님의 열정이 제가 바라는 그대로라면, 이제는 마음을 놓으셔도 될 듯한데요.\"—\"마음을 놓으라니요!"], ["Sophia, call not such an exulting happiness as mine by so cold a name.----O!", "소피아, 저처럼 환희에 찬 행복을 그토록 차가운 말로 부르지 마십시오.——오!"], ["transporting thought!", "가슴 벅찬 생각이여!"], ["am I not assured that the blessed day will come, when I shall call you mine; when fears shall be no more; when I shall have that dear, that vast, that exquisite, ecstatic delight of making my Sophia happy?”--“Indeed, sir,”", "그 복된 날이 오리라는 것을, 당신을 제 사람이라 부를 그날이, 두려움이라곤 더는 없을 그날이, 나의 소피아를 행복하게 해 줄 그 소중하고도 광대하며 더없이 황홀한 환희를 누릴 그날이 오리라는 것을 제가 확신하지 않습니까?\"—\"정말이지, 선생님,\""], ["said she, “that day is in your own power.”--“O!", "그녀가 말했다. \"그날은 선생님 손에 달려 있는걸요.\"—\"오!"], ["my dear, my divine angel,”", "나의 사랑스러운, 나의 거룩한 천사여,\""], ["cried he, “these words have made me mad with joy.----But I must, I will thank those dear lips which have so sweetly pronounced my bliss.”", "그가 외쳤다. \"그 말씀에 기뻐서 미칠 것만 같습니다.——그러나 제 더없는 복을 그토록 달콤하게 일러 준 그 사랑스러운 입술에 감사를 드려야겠습니다, 아니 드리겠습니다.\""], ["He then caught her in his arms, and kissed her with an ardour he had never ventured before.", "그러고는 그녀를 와락 품에 안고, 일찍이 한 번도 감히 보이지 못했던 뜨거움으로 입을 맞추었다."]], [["At this instant Western, who had stood some time listening, burst into the room, and, with his hunting voice and phrase, cried out, “To her, boy, to her, go to her.----That's it, little honeys, O that's it!", "바로 그 순간, 한동안 문밖에서 엿듣고 서 있던 웨스턴이 방으로 뛰어들며 사냥터에서 쓰는 목청과 말투로 외쳤다. \"그 애한테 가, 이 녀석, 그 애한테!"], ["Well! what, is it all over?", "가서 안아!——옳지, 요 귀여운 것들, 오 바로 그거야!"], ["Hath she appointed the day, boy?", "자! 어떻게 됐어, 다 끝난 거냐?"], ["What, shall it be to-morrow or next day?", "날은 잡았어, 이 녀석? 어때, 내일로 할까 모레로 할까?"], ["It shan't be put off a minute longer than next day, I am resolved.”", "모레보다 단 한순간도 미루는 일은 없을 거다, 내 그리 작정했으니.\""], ["“Let me beseech you, sir,”", "\"부디 청하건대, 어르신,\""], ["says Jones, “don't let me be the occasion”----“Beseech mine a----,”", "존스가 말했다. \"제가 빌미가 되어\"——\"청하긴 무슨 얼어죽을,\""], ["cries Western.", "웨스턴이 외쳤다."], ["“I thought thou hadst been a lad of higher mettle than to give way to a parcel of maidenish tricks.----I tell thee 'tis all flimflam.", "\"나는 네놈을 처녀애 같은 잔꾀 따위에 굴복하는 위인보다는 좀 더 기개 있는 녀석으로 봤다.——내 말하건대 그건 죄다 헛수작이야."], ["Zoodikers!", "빌어먹을!"], ["she'd have the wedding to-night with all her heart.", "저 애는 진심으로 오늘 밤에라도 혼례를 치르고 싶을 게다."], ["Would'st not, Sophy?", "안 그러냐, 소피?"], ["Come, confess, and be an honest girl for once.", "자, 솔직히 털어놓고 한 번쯤은 정직한 계집애가 되어 보거라."], ["What, art dumb?", "뭐냐, 꿀먹은 벙어리냐?"], ["Why dost not speak?”", "어째서 말을 안 해?\""], ["“Why should I confess, sir,”", "\"어째서 제가 털어놓아야 하나요, 아버지,\""], ["says Sophia, “since it seems you are so well acquainted with my thoughts?”----“That's a good girl,”", "소피아가 말했다. \"아버지가 제 속마음을 그토록 잘 알고 계신 듯한데요?\"——\"착하다, 우리 딸,\""], ["cries he, “and dost consent then?”", "그가 외쳤다. \"그럼 승낙하는 거지?\""], ["“No, indeed, sir,”", "\"아니에요, 정말로, 아버지,\""], ["says Sophia, “I have given no such consent.”---“And wunt not ha un then to-morrow, nor next day?”", "소피아가 말했다. \"저는 그런 승낙은 한 적 없어요.\"——\"그럼 내일도 모레도 저 녀석을 맞을 생각이 없단 말이냐?\""], ["says Western.--“Indeed, sir,”", "웨스턴이 말했다.—\"정말로, 아버지,\""], ["says she, “I have no such intention.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 그럴 뜻이 없어요.\""], ["“But I can tell thee,”", "\"허나 내 말해 주마,\" 그가 대꾸했다."], ["replied he, “why hast nut; only because thou dost love to be disobedient, and to plague and vex thy father.”", "\"어째서 그런지를. 그저 네가 거역하기를, 네 아비를 괴롭히고 속 썩이기를 좋아해서 그러는 게야.\""], ["“Pray, sir,”", "\"제발, 어르신,\""], ["said Jones, interfering----“I tell thee thou art a puppy,”", "존스가 끼어들며 말했다——\"내 말하건대 너는 풋내기다,\""], ["cries he.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“When I vorbid her, then it was all nothing but sighing and whining, and languishing and writing; now I am vor thee, she is against thee.", "\"내가 저 애한테 안 된다 했을 때는 죄다 한숨에 우는소리에 애태우고 편지질이더니, 이제 내가 네 편을 드니까 저 애가 네 반대편으로 돌아서는구나."], ["All the spirit of contrary, that's all.", "죄다 청개구리 심보지, 그뿐이야."], ["She is above being guided and governed by her father, that is the whole truth on't.", "제 아비한테 이끌리고 다스려지는 걸 못 견디는 게다, 그게 사실의 전부야."], ["It is only to disoblige and contradict me.”", "그저 나를 거스르고 거역하려는 게지.\""], ["“What would my papa have me do?”", "\"아버지는 제가 어쩌기를 바라세요?\""], ["cries Sophia.", "소피아가 외쳤다."], ["“What would I ha thee do?”", "\"내가 너더러 어쩌라겠느냐?\""], ["says he, “why, gi'", "그가 말했다."], ["un thy hand this moment.”--“Well, sir,”", "\"아니, 당장 저 녀석한테 네 손을 내주란 말이다.\"—\"좋아요, 아버지,\""], ["says Sophia, “I will obey you.--There is my hand, Mr Jones.”", "소피아가 말했다. \"분부에 따르겠어요.—여기 제 손이에요, 존스 씨.\""], ["“Well, and will you consent to ha un to-morrow morning?”", "\"그래, 그럼 내일 아침에 저 녀석을 맞아들이는 데 승낙하는 거냐?\""], ["says Western.--“I will be obedient to you, sir,”", "웨스턴이 말했다.—\"아버지께 순종하겠어요,\""], ["cries she.--“Why then to-morrow morning be the day,”", "그녀가 외쳤다.—\"그럼 내일 아침을 그날로 잡자꾸나,\""], ["cries he.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“Why then to-morrow morning shall be the day, papa, since you will have it so,”", "\"그럼 내일 아침을 그날로 하지요, 아버지, 아버지가 그토록 원하시니,\""], ["says Sophia.", "소피아가 말했다."], ["Jones then fell upon his knees, and kissed her hand in an agony of joy, while Western began to caper and dance about the room, presently crying out--“Where the devil is Allworthy?", "그러자 존스는 무릎을 꿇고 환희의 격정에 휩싸여 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었고, 그동안 웨스턴은 방 안을 폴짝폴짝 뛰며 춤을 추다가 이내 외쳤다.—\"대체 올워디는 어디 있는 거야?"], ["He is without now, a talking with that d--d lawyer Dowling, when he should be minding other matters.”", "다른 일에나 마음을 써야 할 판에, 지금 밖에서 그 빌어먹을 변호사 다울링이랑 노닥거리고 있구먼.\""], ["He then sallied out in quest of him, and very opportunely left the lovers to enjoy a few tender minutes alone.", "그러고는 그를 찾으러 뛰쳐나가, 마침맞게도 두 연인이 단둘이 몇 분간 다정한 시간을 누리도록 자리를 비워 주었다."]], [["But he soon returned with Allworthy, saying, “If you won't believe me, you may ask her yourself.", "그러나 그는 곧 올워디를 데리고 돌아와 말했다. \"내 말을 못 믿겠거든 자네가 직접 저 애한테 물어보게."], ["Hast nut gin thy consent, Sophy, to be married to-morrow?”", "안 그러냐, 소피, 내일 혼인하는 데 승낙했지?\""], ["“Such are your commands, sir,”", "\"그것이 아버지의 분부이니,\""], ["cries Sophia, “and I dare not be guilty of disobedience.”", "소피아가 외쳤다. \"감히 거역하는 죄를 지을 수가 없지요.\""], ["“I hope, madam,”", "\"바라건대, 아가씨,\""], ["cries Allworthy, “my nephew will merit so much goodness, and will be always as sensible as myself of the great honour you have done my family.", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"제 조카가 그토록 큰 호의를 받을 자격이 있는 사람이 되어, 아가씨가 우리 집안에 베푸신 크나큰 영광을 저처럼 늘 깊이 새기기를 바랍니다."], ["An alliance with so charming and so excellent a young lady would indeed be an honour to the greatest in England.”", "이토록 매혹적이고 이토록 훌륭한 아가씨와 인연을 맺는 것은 영국에서 가장 지체 높은 이에게도 참으로 영광일 것입니다.\" \"그렇지,\""], ["“Yes,”", "웨스턴이 외쳤다."], ["cries Western, “but if I had suffered her to stand shill I shall I, dilly dally, you might not have had that honour yet a while; I was forced to use a little fatherly authority to bring her to.”", "\"허나 내가 저 애더러 이리 갈까 저리 갈까, 우물쭈물 미적거리도록 내버려 뒀더라면, 자네는 그 영광을 한동안 누리지 못했을 게야. 내가 부득이 아비로서의 권위를 좀 부려서 저 애를 끌어온 거라네.\""], ["“I hope not, sir,”", "\"그러지 않았기를 바랍니다, 어르신,\""], ["cries Allworthy, “I hope there is not the least constraint.”", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"조금의 강요도 없었기를 바랍니다.\""], ["“Why, there,”", "\"이런, 그렇다면,\""], ["cries Western, “you may bid her unsay all again if you will.", "웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"원한다면 저 애더러 한 말을 죄다 도로 무르라고 해 보게나."], ["Dost repent heartily of thy promise, dost not, Sophia?”", "네 약속을 진심으로 후회하느냐, 안 그러냐, 소피아?\" \"정말이지, 아버지,\""], ["“Indeed, papa,”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"저는 후회하지 않아요."], ["cries she, “I do not repent, nor do I believe I ever shall, of any promise in favour of Mr Jones.”", "존스 씨를 위한 약속이라면 그 어떤 것도 앞으로도 결코 후회하지 않으리라 믿어요.\" \"그렇다면, 조카야,\""], ["“Then, nephew,”", "올워디가 외쳤다. \"진심으로 축하한다."], ["cries Allworthy, “I felicitate you most heartily; for I think you are the happiest of men.", "너야말로 세상에서 가장 행복한 사내라 여겨지는구나. 그리고 아가씨, 이 기쁜 자리에서 제가 축하의 말을 올리도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["And, madam, you will give me leave to congratulate you on this joyful occasion: indeed, I am convinced you have bestowed yourself on one who will be sensible of your great merit, and who will at least use his best endeavours to deserve it.”", "참으로, 저는 확신합니다. 아가씨는 당신의 크나큰 미덕을 알아볼 사람에게, 적어도 그 미덕을 받을 자격을 갖추고자 최선을 다할 사람에게 자신을 내어 주신 것이라고요.\""], ["“His best endeavours!”", "\"최선을 다한다고!\" 웨스턴이 외쳤다."], ["cries Western, “that he will, I warrant un.----Harkee, Allworthy, I'll bet thee five pounds to a crown we have a boy to-morrow nine months; but prithee tell me what wut ha!", "\"암, 그러고말고, 내 장담하지.——이보게, 올워디, 자네한테 5파운드 대 1크라운을 걸겠네. 오늘로부터 아홉 달 뒤엔 우리한테 사내아이가 생길 거야."], ["Wut ha Burgundy, Champaigne, or what?", "한데 어디 말해 보게, 뭘로 하려나! 부르고뉴로 할 텐가, 샹파뉴로 할 텐가, 아니면 뭘로?"], ["for, please Jupiter, we'll make a night on't.”", "주피터 신께 비노니, 우리 오늘 밤 진탕 마셔 보세나.\" \"참으로, 어르신,\""], ["“Indeed, sir,”", "올워디가 말했다."], ["said Allworthy, “you must excuse me; both my nephew and I were engaged before I suspected this near approach of his happiness.”--“Engaged!”", "\"용서를 구해야겠습니다. 조카의 행복이 이토록 가까이 다가온 줄 짐작도 하기 전에, 저와 조카는 이미 다른 약속이 있었답니다.\"—\"약속이라!\""], ["quoth the squire, “never tell me.--I won't part with thee to-night upon any occasion.", "지주가 말했다. \"그런 소리 말게.—오늘 밤만큼은 무슨 일이 있어도 자네를 보낼 수 없네."], ["Shalt sup here, please the lord Harry.”", "여기서 저녁을 들어야지, 해리 영주님께 맹세코.\""], ["“You must pardon me, my dear neighbour!”", "\"용서해 주셔야겠습니다, 이웃 양반!\""], ["answered Allworthy; “I have given a solemn promise, and that you know I never break.”", "올워디가 대답했다. \"제가 엄숙히 약속한 일인데, 제가 약속을 깨는 법이 없음은 잘 아시지 않습니까.\""], ["“Why, prithee, who art engaged to?”", "\"이런, 어디, 대체 누구와 약속했단 말인가?\""], ["cries the squire.----Allworthy then informed him, as likewise of the company.----“Odzookers!”", "지주가 외쳤다.——그러자 올워디는 그에게 사정을 알리고, 함께할 일행에 대해서도 일러 주었다.——\"이런 빌어먹을!\""], ["answered the squire, “I will go with thee, and so shall Sophy!", "지주가 대답했다. \"내가 자네랑 함께 가지, 소피도 같이 가고!"], ["for I won't part with thee to-night; and it would be barbarous to part Tom and the girl.”", "오늘 밤만큼은 자네를 보낼 수 없으니 말이야. 게다가 톰이랑 저 애를 떼어 놓는 건 너무 야박한 짓이지.\""], ["This offer was presently embraced by Allworthy, and Sophia consented, having first obtained a private promise from her father that he would not mention a syllable concerning her marriage.", "이 제안을 올워디는 곧바로 받아들였고, 소피아도 동의했다. 다만 그녀는 먼저, 자신의 결혼에 관해서는 한 마디도 입에 올리지 않겠다는 약속을 아버지에게서 은밀히 받아 두었다."]], [["In which the history is concluded. Young Nightingale had been that afternoon, by appointment, to wait on his father, who received him much more kindly than he expected.", "젊은 나이팅게일은 그날 오후 약속대로 아버지를 찾아뵈었는데, 아버지는 그가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 따뜻하게 그를 맞아 주었다."], ["There likewise he met his uncle, who was returned to town in quest of his new-married daughter.", "그곳에서 그는 새로 결혼한 딸을 찾아 도시로 돌아온 숙부와도 마주쳤다."]], [["This marriage was the luckiest incident which could have happened to the young gentleman; for these brothers lived in a constant state of contention about the government of their children, both heartily despising the method which each other took.", "이 결혼은 이 젊은 신사에게 일어날 수 있었던 일 가운데 가장 운 좋은 사건이었다. 이 두 형제는 자식 교육 방식을 두고 늘 다투며 지냈으니, 서로가 택한 방법을 진심으로 경멸했던 것이다."], ["Each of them therefore now endeavoured, as much as he could, to palliate the offence which his own child had committed, and to aggravate the match of the other.", "그러므로 이제 두 사람은 저마다 제 자식이 저지른 잘못은 되도록 감싸고, 상대 자식의 혼사는 되도록 깎아내리려 애썼다."], ["This desire of triumphing over his brother, added to the many arguments which Allworthy had used, so strongly operated on the old gentleman that he met his son with a smiling countenance, and actually agreed to sup with him that evening at Mrs Miller's.", "형제를 이겨 보고 싶은 이 욕망에, 올워디가 펼친 여러 논리까지 더해지자, 그 효과는 노신사에게 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 그는 미소 띤 얼굴로 아들을 맞이했고, 실제로 그날 저녁 밀러 부인 댁에서 아들과 함께 저녁을 들기로 응낙했다."]], [["As for the other, who really loved his daughter with the most immoderate affection, there was little difficulty in inclining him to a reconciliation.", "다른 한쪽으로 말하자면, 딸을 더없이 지극한 애정으로 진심으로 사랑하던 그를 화해 쪽으로 기울게 하는 데에는 별 어려움이 없었다."], ["He was no sooner informed by his nephew where his daughter and her husband were, than he declared he would instantly go to her.", "조카에게서 딸과 사위가 어디 있는지 듣자마자, 그는 당장 딸에게 가겠다고 선언했다."], ["And when he arrived there he scarce suffered her to fall upon her knees before he took her up, and embraced her with a tenderness which affected all who saw him; and in less than a quarter of an hour was as well reconciled to both her and her husband as if he had himself joined their hands.", "그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 딸이 자기 앞에 무릎을 꿇기도 전에 얼른 일으켜 세워, 보는 이 모두를 감동시킬 만큼 다정하게 끌어안았다. 그리고 채 십오 분도 지나지 않아, 마치 제 손으로 두 사람의 손을 맞잡아 준 것처럼 딸과 사위 모두와 깨끗이 화해해 버렸다."]], [["In this situation were affairs when Mr Allworthy and his company arrived to complete the happiness of Mrs Miller, who no sooner saw Sophia than she guessed everything that had happened; and so great was her friendship to Jones, that it added not a few transports to those she felt on the happiness of her own daughter.", "이런 상황일 때 올워디 씨와 그 일행이 도착해 밀러 부인의 행복을 완성해 주었다. 부인은 소피아를 보자마자 그동안 일어난 모든 일을 짐작했다. 존스를 향한 그녀의 우정은 어찌나 깊었던지, 제 딸의 행복에서 느끼던 환희에 적지 않은 기쁨이 더해졌다."]], [["There have not, I believe, been many instances of a number of people met together, where every one was so perfectly happy as in this company.", "내가 믿기로, 여러 사람이 한자리에 모여 저마다 이 일행만큼 완벽하게 행복했던 예는 그리 많지 않았을 것이다. 그 가운데 젊은 나이팅게일의 아버지가 가장 덜 완전한 만족을 누렸다."], ["Amongst whom the father of young Nightingale enjoyed the least perfect content; for, notwithstanding his affection for his son, notwithstanding the authority and the arguments of Allworthy, together with the other motive mentioned before, he could not so entirely be satisfied with his son's choice; and, perhaps, the presence of Sophia herself tended a little to aggravate and heighten his concern, as a thought now and then suggested itself that his son might have had that lady, or some other such.", "아들을 향한 애정에도, 올워디의 권위와 논리에도, 앞서 말한 다른 동기까지 보태졌음에도, 그는 아들의 선택에 그토록 온전히 만족할 수는 없었던 것이다. 게다가 소피아가 그 자리에 있다는 사실 자체가 그의 근심을 조금쯤 키우고 돋우는 데 한몫했을지도 모른다."], ["Not that any of the charms which adorned either the person or mind of Sophia created the uneasiness; it was the contents of her father's coffers which set his heart a longing.", "아들이 저런 아가씨를, 혹은 그와 비슷한 어떤 아가씨를 아내로 맞을 수도 있었으련만 하는 생각이 이따금 고개를 들었기 때문이다. 소피아의 외모나 마음을 꾸미는 그 어떤 매력이 그를 불편하게 한 것은 아니었다."], ["These were the charms which he could not bear to think his son had sacrificed to the daughter of Mrs Miller.", "그의 마음을 갈망으로 들끓게 한 것은 그녀 아버지의 금고에 담긴 내용물이었다. 아들이 밀러 부인의 딸을 위해 희생해 버린 것이 바로 그 매력이라고 생각하니, 그는 도무지 견딜 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["The brides were both very pretty women; but so totally were they eclipsed by the beauty of Sophia, that, had they not been two of the best-tempered girls in the world, it would have raised some envy in their breasts; for neither of their husbands could long keep his eyes from Sophia, who sat at the table like a queen receiving homage, or, rather, like a superior being receiving adoration from all around her. But it was an adoration which they gave, not which she exacted; for she was as much distinguished by her modesty and affability as by all her other perfections.", "두 신부 모두 무척 예쁜 여인들이었다. 그러나 소피아의 아름다움에 너무도 완전히 빛을 잃어버린 탓에, 만약 이들이 세상에서 가장 마음씨 고운 처녀들 축에 들지 않았더라면 그들의 가슴속에 적잖은 질투가 일었을 법했다. 두 신랑 가운데 누구도 소피아에게서 오래 눈을 떼지 못했으니 말이다. 소피아는 신하의 경배를 받는 여왕처럼, 아니 차라리 주위 모두에게서 흠모를 받는 더 높은 존재처럼 식탁에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그것은 그들이 자진해 바치는 흠모였지, 그녀가 요구한 흠모가 아니었다. 그녀는 다른 모든 완벽함만큼이나 그 겸손과 상냥함으로도 돋보였기 때문이다."]], [["The evening was spent in much true mirth.", "저녁 시간은 참된 흥겨움 속에서 흘러갔다."], ["All were happy, but those the most who had been most unhappy before.", "모두가 행복했으나, 그 가운데서도 전에 가장 불행했던 이들이 가장 행복했다."], ["Their former sufferings and fears gave such a relish to their felicity as even love and fortune, in their fullest flow, could not have given without the advantage of such a comparison.", "지난날의 고통과 두려움은 그들의 행복에 그런 깊은 맛을 더해 주었으니, 사랑과 행운이 아무리 가득 흘러넘친들 그런 대비의 도움 없이는 결코 줄 수 없는 맛이었다."], ["Yet, as great joy, especially after a sudden change and revolution of circumstances, is apt to be silent, and dwells rather in the heart than on the tongue, Jones and Sophia appeared the least merry of the whole company; which Western observed with great impatience, often crying out to them, “Why dost not talk, boy?", "그러나 큰 기쁨이란, 특히 처지가 갑작스레 바뀌고 뒤집힌 뒤에는 으레 말이 없어지고, 혀보다는 가슴속에 머무는 법이라, 존스와 소피아는 모인 사람들 가운데 가장 덜 들떠 보였다. 웨스턴은 이를 몹시 답답해하며 두 사람에게 자주 외쳤다."], ["Why dost look so grave?", "\"이놈아, 왜 말이 없느냐?"], ["Hast lost thy tongue, girl?", "어찌 그리 심각한 얼굴이냐?"], ["Drink another glass of wine; sha't drink another glass.”", "얘야, 혀라도 잃어버렸느냐? 포도주 한 잔 더 들이켜라."], ["And, the more to enliven her, he would sometimes sing a merry song, which bore some relation to matrimony and the loss of a maidenhead.", "한 잔 더 마셔야지.\" 그리고 딸의 흥을 더 돋우려고 이따금 흥겨운 노래를 불렀는데, 결혼과 처녀성을 잃는 일에 얼마쯤 관련된 노래였다."], ["Nay, he would have proceeded so far on that topic as to have driven her out of the room, if Mr Allworthy had not checkt him, sometimes by looks, and once or twice by a “Fie!", "아니, 그는 그 주제를 어찌나 멀리 끌고 갔던지, 올워디 씨가 때로는 눈짓으로, 또 한두 번은 \"이런! 웨스턴 씨!\""], ["Mr Western!”", "하고 제지하지 않았더라면 딸을 방에서 쫓아낼 뻔했다."], ["He began, indeed, once to debate the matter, and assert his right to talk to his own daughter as he thought fit; but, as nobody seconded him, he was soon reduced to order.", "실제로 그는 한번은 그 문제를 따지고 들며, 제 딸에게 제 마음대로 말할 권리가 있노라 주장하기 시작했다. 그러나 아무도 그를 거들지 않았으므로, 그는 이내 잠잠해졌다."]], [["Notwithstanding this little restraint, he was so pleased with the chearfulness and good-humour of the company, that he insisted on their meeting the next day at his lodgings.", "이런 자그마한 제지에도, 그는 일행의 쾌활함과 좋은 기분에 어찌나 흡족했던지 이튿날 자기 숙소에서 다시 모이자고 고집했다."], ["They all did so; and the lovely Sophia, who was now in private become a bride too, officiated as the mistress of the ceremonies, or, in the polite phrase, did the honours of the table.", "모두가 그렇게 했다. 그리고 이제 남몰래 신부가 된 사랑스러운 소피아가 의식의 안주인 노릇을, 점잖은 표현을 빌리자면 식탁의 접대를 맡았다."], ["She had that morning given her hand to Jones, in the chapel at Doctors'-Commons, where Mr Allworthy, Mr Western, and Mrs Miller, were the only persons present.", "그날 아침 그녀는 닥터스 커먼스의 예배당에서 존스에게 손을 내주었는데, 그 자리에 함께한 이는 올워디 씨와 웨스턴 씨, 그리고 밀러 부인뿐이었다."]], [["Sophia had earnestly desired her father that no others of the company, who were that day to dine with him, should be acquainted with her marriage.", "소피아는 그날 아버지와 함께 식사하기로 한 다른 일행에게는 자기 결혼을 알리지 말아 달라고 아버지에게 간곡히 청했다."], ["The same secrecy was enjoined to Mrs Miller, and Jones undertook for Allworthy.", "밀러 부인에게도 같은 비밀을 당부했고, 올워디 쪽은 존스가 맡았다."], ["This somewhat reconciled the delicacy of Sophia to the public entertainment which, in compliance with her father's will, she was obliged to go to, greatly against her own inclinations.", "이 덕분에, 제 뜻과는 크게 어긋났음에도 아버지의 바람을 좇아 어쩔 수 없이 나가야 했던 그 공개적인 잔치에 소피아의 까다로운 마음도 어느 정도 누그러졌다."], ["In confidence of this secrecy she went through the day pretty well, till the squire, who was now advanced into the second bottle, could contain his joy no longer, but, filling out a bumper, drank a health to the bride.", "비밀이 지켜지리라는 믿음에 그녀는 하루를 그런대로 잘 버텼다. 그러나 이제 두 번째 병으로 넘어간 지주가 더는 기쁨을 누르지 못하고 잔을 가득 채워 신부의 건강을 위해 건배를 들고 말았다."], ["The health was immediately pledged by all present, to the great confusion of our poor blushing Sophia, and the great concern of Jones upon her account.", "자리에 있던 모두가 곧장 그 건배에 응했고, 가엾게도 얼굴을 붉힌 우리 소피아는 크게 당황했으며, 존스도 그녀 때문에 크게 마음을 졸였다."], ["To say truth, there was not a person present made wiser by this discovery; for Mrs Miller had whispered it to her daughter, her daughter to her husband, her husband to his sister, and she to all the rest.", "사실대로 말하자면, 이 폭로로 새로 알게 된 사람은 한 명도 없었다. 밀러 부인이 딸에게 귀띔했고, 딸은 남편에게, 남편은 누이에게, 누이는 나머지 모두에게 이미 전했기 때문이다."]], [["Sophia now took the first opportunity of withdrawing with the ladies, and the squire sat in to his cups, in which he was, by degrees, deserted by all the company except the uncle of young Nightingale, who loved his bottle as well as Western himself.", "소피아는 이제 부인들과 함께 자리를 뜰 첫 기회를 잡았고, 지주는 술잔을 앞에 두고 눌러앉았다. 그러는 동안 일행은 하나둘 그를 떠났고, 끝까지 남은 이는 웨스턴 못지않게 술병을 사랑하는 젊은 나이팅게일의 숙부뿐이었다."], ["These two, therefore, sat stoutly to it during the whole evening, and long after that happy hour which had surrendered the charming Sophia to the eager arms of her enraptured Jones.", "그리하여 이 두 사람은 저녁 내내 굳건히 술잔을 기울였으니, 매혹적인 소피아가 황홀해진 존스의 애타는 두 팔에 안기는 그 행복한 순간이 한참 지난 뒤까지 그러했다."]], [["Thus, reader, we have at length brought our history to a conclusion, in which, to our great pleasure, though contrary, perhaps, to thy expectation, Mr Jones appears to be the happiest of all humankind; for what happiness this world affords equal to the possession of such a woman as Sophia, I sincerely own I have never yet discovered.", "이리하여 독자여, 우리는 마침내 우리 이야기를 결말에 이르게 했다. 어쩌면 그대의 기대와는 어긋날지 모르나, 우리로서는 더없이 기쁘게도, 그 결말에서 존스 씨는 온 인류 가운데 가장 행복한 사람으로 나타난다. 소피아 같은 여인을 차지하는 것에 견줄 만한 행복을 이 세상이 베푸는지, 나는 솔직히 고백하건대 아직껏 발견하지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["As to the other persons who have made any considerable figure in this history, as some may desire to know a little more concerning them, we will proceed, in as few words as possible, to satisfy their curiosity.", "이 이야기에서 어느 정도 비중 있는 인물로 등장한 다른 이들에 대해서는, 그들에 관해 조금 더 알고 싶어 하는 분들이 있을지 모르니, 되도록 짧은 말로 그 궁금증을 풀어 드리고자 한다."]], [["Allworthy hath never yet been prevailed upon to see Blifil, but he hath yielded to the importunity of Jones, backed by Sophia, to settle £200 a-year upon him; to which Jones hath privately added a third.", "올워디는 아직껏 블리필을 다시 만나 보라는 설득에 응하지 않았으나, 소피아의 지지를 등에 업은 존스의 끈질긴 간청에는 못 이겨 그에게 연 200파운드를 주기로 정했다."], ["Upon this income he lives in one of the northern counties, about 200 miles distant from London, and lays up £200 a-year out of it, in order to purchase a seat in the next parliament from a neighbouring borough, which he has bargained for with an attourney there.", "거기에 존스는 남몰래 그 삼분의 일을 더 보탰다. 이 수입으로 그는 런던에서 약 200마일 떨어진 북부 어느 주에서 살며, 그 가운데 연 200파운드를 모아 두는데, 이는 이웃 자치구에서 다음 의회의 의석을 사들이려는 것으로, 그곳 변호사와 이미 흥정을 마쳐 두었다."], ["He is also lately turned Methodist, in hopes of marrying a very rich widow of that sect, whose estate lies in that part of the kingdom.", "또 그는 최근 메소디스트로 개종했는데, 그 종파에 속한, 영지가 그 지방에 있는 어느 무척 부유한 과부와 결혼하려는 속셈에서였다."]], [["Square died soon after he writ the before-mentioned letter; and as to Thwackum, he continues at his vicarage.", "스퀘어는 앞서 말한 편지를 쓴 직후에 세상을 떠났다. 스왜컴으로 말하자면, 그는 여전히 자기 교구 목사 자리를 지키고 있다."], ["He hath made many fruitless attempts to regain the confidence of Allworthy, or to ingratiate himself with Jones, both of whom he flatters to their faces, and abuses behind their backs.", "그는 올워디의 신임을 되찾거나 존스의 환심을 사려고 여러 번 헛된 시도를 했으니, 두 사람 모두에게 면전에서는 아첨하고 등 뒤에서는 욕을 해 댄다."], ["But in his stead, Mr Allworthy hath lately taken Mr Abraham Adams into his house, of whom Sophia is grown immoderately fond, and declares he shall have the tuition of her children.", "그러나 그 대신 올워디 씨는 최근 에이브러햄 애덤스 씨를 자기 집에 들였는데, 소피아는 그를 더없이 좋아하게 되어, 제 아이들의 교육을 그에게 맡기겠노라 선언한다."]], [["Mrs Fitzpatrick is separated from her husband, and retains the little remains of her fortune.", "피츠패트릭 부인은 남편과 갈라서서 얼마 남지 않은 재산의 잔여분을 지니고 있다."], ["She lives in reputation at the polite end of the town, and is so good an economist, that she spends three times the income of her fortune, without running into debt.", "그녀는 도시의 점잖은 구역에서 평판을 누리며 사는데, 어찌나 살림 솜씨가 좋은지 빚 한 푼 지지 않으면서도 제 재산 수입의 세 배를 써 댄다."], ["She maintains a perfect intimacy with the lady of the Irish peer; and in acts of friendship to her repays all obligations she owes her husband.", "그녀는 아일랜드 귀족의 부인과 더없이 친밀한 관계를 유지하며, 그 부인에게 우정을 베푸는 행위로써 제 남편에게 진 온갖 빚을 갚는다."]], [["Mrs Western was soon reconciled to her niece Sophia, and hath spent two months together with her in the country.", "웨스턴 부인은 이내 조카딸 소피아와 화해하여, 시골에서 함께 두 달을 보냈다."], ["Lady Bellaston made the latter a formal visit at her return to town, where she behaved to Jones as a perfect stranger, and, with great civility, wished him joy on his marriage.", "벨라스턴 부인은 소피아가 도시로 돌아오자 그녀를 격식 차려 방문했는데, 그 자리에서 존스에게는 생판 낯선 사람을 대하듯 굴며, 더없이 예의 바르게 그의 결혼을 축하했다."]], [["Mr Nightingale hath purchased an estate for his son in the neighbourhood of Jones, where the young gentleman, his lady, Mrs Miller, and her little daughter reside, and the most agreeable intercourse subsists between the two families.", "나이팅게일 씨는 아들을 위해 존스의 이웃에 영지를 사들였고, 그곳에서 젊은 신사와 그의 부인, 밀러 부인, 그리고 그 어린 딸이 함께 산다. 두 집안 사이에는 더없이 흐뭇한 왕래가 이어지고 있다."]], [["As to those of lower account, Mrs Waters returned into the country, had a pension of £60 a-year settled upon her by Mr Allworthy, and is married to Parson Supple, on whom, at the instance of Sophia, Western hath bestowed a considerable living.", "좀 더 미천한 이들로 말하자면, 워터스 부인은 시골로 돌아갔는데, 올워디 씨에게서 연 60파운드의 연금을 받게 되었고, 서플 목사와 결혼했다. 소피아의 청에 따라 웨스턴은 이 목사에게 제법 괜찮은 성직록을 베풀어 주었다."]], [["Black George, hearing the discovery that had been made, ran away, and was never since heard of; and Jones bestowed the money on his family, but not in equal proportions, for Molly had much the greatest share.", "블랙 조지는 모든 일이 들통난 것을 알고 달아나, 그 뒤로는 소식을 들은 이가 없다. 존스는 그 돈을 그의 가족에게 나누어 주었으나 똑같이 나누지는 않았으니, 몰리가 단연 가장 많은 몫을 차지했다."]], [["As for Partridge, Jones hath settled £50 a-year on him; and he hath again set up a school, in which he meets with much better encouragement than formerly, and there is now a treaty of marriage on foot between him and Miss Molly Seagrim, which, through the mediation of Sophia, is likely to take effect.", "파트리지로 말하자면, 존스는 그에게 연 50파운드를 정해 주었다. 그는 다시 학교를 열었는데, 예전보다 훨씬 나은 호응을 얻고 있다. 그리고 지금 그와 몰리 시그림 양 사이에 혼담이 오가고 있는데, 소피아의 중재로 성사될 듯하다."]], [["We now return to take leave of Mr Jones and Sophia, who, within two days after their marriage, attended Mr Western and Mr Allworthy into the country.", "이제 우리는 존스 씨와 소피아에게 작별을 고하고자 한다. 두 사람은 결혼한 지 이틀 만에 웨스턴 씨와 올워디 씨를 모시고 시골로 내려갔다."], ["Western hath resigned his family seat, and the greater part of his estate, to his son-in-law, and hath retired to a lesser house of his in another part of the country, which is better for hunting.", "웨스턴은 집안의 본가와 영지의 대부분을 사위에게 물려주고, 사냥하기에 더 좋은, 그 고장 다른 곳에 있는 자기의 작은 집으로 물러났다. 실로 그는 존스 씨를 자주 찾아오는데, 존스는 딸과 마찬가지로 그를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 할 수 있는 모든 일을 다 하는 데서 더없는 즐거움을 느낀다."], ["Indeed, he is often as a visitant with Mr Jones, who, as well as his daughter, hath an infinite delight in doing everything in their power to please him.", "그리고 이 두 사람의 정성은 어찌나 잘 통했던지, 노신사는 평생 이때만큼 행복한 적이 없었노라 선언한다."], ["And this desire of theirs is attended with such success, that the old gentleman declares he was never happy in his life till now.", "그는 이곳에 거실 하나와 곁방 하나를 따로 두고, 마음에 드는 이 누구와든 그곳에서 술을 마신다."], ["He hath here a parlour and ante-chamber to himself, where he gets drunk with whom he pleases: and his daughter is still as ready as formerly to play to him whenever he desires it; for Jones hath assured her that, as, next to pleasing her, one of his highest satisfactions is to contribute to the happiness of the old man; so, the great duty which she expresses and performs to her father, renders her almost equally dear to him with the love which she bestows on himself.", "또 딸은 예전과 다름없이 그가 바랄 때면 언제든 연주를 해 주는데, 존스가 그녀에게 이렇게 일러두었기 때문이다. 그녀를 기쁘게 하는 일 다음으로 자신의 가장 큰 만족 가운데 하나가 노인의 행복에 보탬이 되는 일이니, 그녀가 아버지에게 드러내고 또 다하는 그 갸륵한 효성이야말로, 자신에게 쏟는 사랑에 거의 맞먹을 만큼 그녀를 노인에게 소중한 사람으로 만들어 준다고."]], [["Sophia hath already produced him two fine children, a boy and a girl, of whom the old gentleman is so fond, that he spends much of his time in the nursery, where he declares the tattling of his little grand-daughter, who is above a year and a half old, is sweeter music than the finest cry of dogs in England.", "소피아는 벌써 그에게 잘생긴 아이 둘, 사내아이와 계집아이를 안겨 주었는데, 노신사는 이 아이들을 어찌나 사랑하는지 많은 시간을 아기방에서 보낸다. 그곳에서 그는, 한 살 반이 넘은 어린 손녀가 옹알이하는 소리야말로 잉글랜드에서 가장 훌륭한 사냥개들이 짖는 소리보다 더 감미로운 음악이라고 선언한다."]], [["Allworthy was likewise greatly liberal to Jones on the marriage, and hath omitted no instance of shewing his affection to him and his lady, who love him as a father.", "올워디 역시 결혼에 즈음하여 존스에게 무척 후하게 베풀었으며, 그와 그의 부인에게 애정을 보일 기회를 하나도 빠뜨리지 않았으니, 두 사람은 그를 아버지처럼 사랑한다."], ["Whatever in the nature of Jones had a tendency to vice, has been corrected by continual conversation with this good man, and by his union with the lovely and virtuous Sophia.", "존스의 천성 가운데 악덕으로 기울 만한 점이 무엇이든, 이 선량한 분과 끊임없이 나눈 대화로, 그리고 사랑스럽고 정숙한 소피아와의 결합으로 바로잡혔다."], ["He hath also, by reflection on his past follies, acquired a discretion and prudence very uncommon in one of his lively parts.", "또한 그는 지난날의 어리석음을 돌이켜 봄으로써, 그처럼 활달한 기질을 지닌 사람에게서는 좀처럼 보기 드문 분별과 신중함을 갖추게 되었다."]], [["To conclude, as there are not to be found a worthier man and woman, than this fond couple, so neither can any be imagined more happy.", "끝으로, 이 다정한 부부보다 더 훌륭한 남자와 여자를 찾아볼 수 없듯, 이들보다 더 행복한 이들 또한 상상할 수 없다."], ["They preserve the purest and tenderest affection for each other, an affection daily encreased and confirmed by mutual endearments and mutual esteem.", "그들은 서로를 향해 더없이 순수하고 다정한 애정을 간직하고 있으니, 그 애정은 서로를 아끼고 서로를 존중하는 가운데 날마다 자라고 굳어 간다."], ["Nor is their conduct towards their relations and friends less amiable than towards one another.", "친척과 벗을 대하는 그들의 태도 또한 서로를 대하는 태도 못지않게 사랑스럽다."], ["And such is their condescension, their indulgence, and their beneficence to those below them, that there is not a neighbour, a tenant, or a servant, who doth not most gratefully bless the day when Mr Jones was married to his Sophia.", "그리고 아랫사람들을 향한 그들의 겸손함과 너그러움, 그리고 자비로움이 어찌나 큰지, 이웃이든 소작인이든 하인이든, 존스 씨가 그의 소피아와 결혼한 그날을 더없이 감사하는 마음으로 축복하지 않는 이가 하나도 없다. 끝."]]];
